(120712,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name for the compound in the given diagram(1283)','4-Ethyl-1-fluoro-2-nitrobenzene','Alphabetical order is preferred here as all of them are substituents. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 348; Problem 12.10)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','1-Ethyl-3-fluoro-4-nitrobenzene','5-Ethyl-2-fluoro-21-nitrobenzene','4-Ethyl-1-fluoro-2-nitrobenzene','None of these','NEW','11-348','11-270','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120712q.png'),
(120801,'chemistry','There are two major types of isomerism:','Structural and stereoisomerism','The phenomenon of existence of two or more compounds possessing the same molecular formula but different properties is known as isomerism. Such compounds are called as isomers. Isomerism can structural and stereoisomerism. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 348; Paragraph No. 1 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Isomerism',NULL,'1','mcq','Structural and stereoisomerism','Structural and Optical isomerism','Geometrical and Functional isomerism','Geometrical and Stereoisomerism','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(120802,'chemistry','Compounds having the same molecular formula but different structures are classified as __________ isomers.','Structural','Compounds having the same molecular formula but different structures (manners in which atoms are linked) are classified as structural isomers. Chain isomerism, position isomerism, functional group isomerism and metamerism are type of structural isomerism. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 348; Paragraph No. 2 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Isomerism',NULL,'2','mcq','Functional','Structural','Geometrical','Chain','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(120803,'chemistry','When two or more compounds have similar molecular formula but different carbon skeletons, these are referred to as __________ isomers.','Chain','When two or more compounds have similar molecular formula but different carbon skeletons, these are referred to as chain isomers and the phenomenon is termed as chain isomerism. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 348; Paragraph No. 2 and 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Isomerism',NULL,'2','mcq','Position','Optical','Chain','Geometrical','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(120804,'chemistry','Two or more compounds having the same molecular formula but different functional groups are called __________isomers.','Functional','Two or more compounds having the same molecular formula but different functional groups are called functional isomers and this phenomenon is termed as functional group isomerism. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 348; Paragraph No. 3 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Isomerism',NULL,'2','mcq','Functional','Optical','Chain','Geometrical','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(120805,'chemistry','What type of structural isomerism is found in pentane and isopentane as show in given diagram(12101)','Chain isomerism','Pentane and isopentane are chain isomers of each other. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 348; Paragraph No. 2 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Isomerism',NULL,'3','mcq','Chain isomerism','Positional isomerism','Functional group isomerism','Geometrical isomer','NEW','11-348','11-270','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120805q.png'),
(120806,'chemistry','What type of structural isomerism is found in the given diagram(12102)','Positional isomerism','Propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol are positional isomers of each other as OH is present at different positions here. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 348; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Isomerism',NULL,'3','mcq','Chain isomerism','Positional isomerism','Functional group isomerism','Geometrical isomer','NEW','11-348','11-270','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120806q.png'),
(120807,'chemistry','What type of structural isomerism is found in the given diagram(12103)','Functional group isomerism','Propanone and propanal are functional group isomers of each other as both have the same formula but different functional group. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 348; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Isomerism',NULL,'3','mcq','Metamerism','Positional isomerism','Functional group isomerism','Chain isomerism','NEW','11-348','11-270','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120807q.png'),
(120808,'chemistry','Which of the following pair shows metamerism?','Methoxypropane and Ethoxyethane','Methoxypropane and Ethoxyethane are metamers as arrangement of carbon atoms around oxygen here (part of ether functional group) is different. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 1 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Isomerism',NULL,'2','mcq','Pentane and isopentane','Methoxypropane and Ethoxyethane','Propanone and propanal','None of these','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(120809,'chemistry','Geometrical and optical isomerism are types of _________isomerism.','Stereo','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 2 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Isomerism',NULL,'2','mcq','Stereo','Functional','Structural','Chain','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(120810,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect option regarding Isomerism:\n a. They differ in both physical and chemical properties.\n b. They have the different molecular formula.\n c. There are two types of Isomerism: Structural and Stereo Isomerism\n d. Geometrical and optical isomerism are two types of Stereo Isomerism.','Only b','Isomers have same molecular formula. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 348; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Isomerism',NULL,'3','mcq','Only a and b','Only b','Only c and d','All of these','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(120811,'chemistry','How many chain isomers are possible with C₆H₁₄ ?','5','Chain isomers have same formula but different length of parent carbon chain There are 5 chain isomers possible with this formula i.e., one linear, two monosubstituted, two disubstituted. As it is shown in digram(e1207) Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 348; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Isomerism',NULL,'3','mcq','3','4','5','6','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(120812,'chemistry','Which of the following is an isomer of ethanol ?','Dimethyl ether','Only Dimetheyl ether has the same formula as ethanol and it is a metamer of ethanol. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 1 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Isomerism',NULL,'2','mcq','Dimethyl ether','Diethyl ether','Methanol','Acetone','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(120813,'chemistry','What type of structural isomers are the two compounds in the given diagram(12109)','Position','In structure one -CO group is present at 3rd position and in second the -CO group is present at second position, therefore they are positional isomers. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 348; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Isomerism',NULL,'3','mcq','Geometrical isomer','Chain','Position','Optical','NEW','11-348','11-270','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120813q.png'),
(120814,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n \n Assertion: Alkanes having more than 3 carbons exhibit chain isomerism.\n \n Reason: In alkanes, all carbon atoms are sp³ hybridised.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Minimum 4 carbon atoms should be in an alkane to exhibit chain isomerism. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 348; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Isomerism',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(120901,'chemistry','Substrate is that reactant which supplies carbon to the new bond and the other reactant is called ________.','Reagent','Substrate is that reactant which supplies carbon to the new bond and the other reactant is called reagent. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 3 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Reagent','Reactant','Product','Catalyst','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(120902,'chemistry','In an organic reaction a ________ bond between two carbon atoms or a carbon and some other atom is broken and a new bond is formed.','Covalent','In an organic reaction a covalent bond between two carbon atoms or a carbon and some other atom is broken and a new bond is formed.Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 4 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','Covalent','Coordinate Covalent','Metallic','Ionic','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(120903,'chemistry','In ________ cleavage, the bond breaks in such a fashion that the shared pair of electrons remains with one of the fragments.','Heterolytic','Heterolytic cleavage results in making of charged species. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 6)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','Homolytic','Heterolytic','Covelnt Bond','Single Bond','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(120904,'chemistry','A species having a carbon atom possessing sextext of electrons and a positive charge is called a _________.','Carbocation','Carbonium ion stability is more when more alkyl groups are attached to it. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 7)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','Catenation','Carbocation','Carbanion','Carbonium ion','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(120905,'chemistry','What is the shape of carbocation?','Trigonal planar','Carbocation is sp² hybridized and therefore has trigonal planar shape. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 8)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','Linear','Pyramidal','Trigonal planar','None of these','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(120906,'chemistry','Carbocation and anion are produced in __________ cleavage.','Heterolytic','Carbocation and anion are produced in heterolytic cleavage. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 350; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','Homolytic','Heterolytic','Covalent Bond','Single Bond','NEW','11-350','11-272',NULL),
(120907,'chemistry','In __________ cleavage, one of the electrons of the shared pair in a covalent bond goes with each of the bonded atoms.','Homolytic','In homolytic cleavage, one of the electrons of the shared pair in a covalent bond goes with each of the bonded atoms. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 350; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','Homolytic','Heterolytic','Covalent Bond','Pi Bond','NEW','11-350','11-272',NULL),
(120908,'chemistry','Homolytic cleavage results in the formation of neutral species which contains an unpaired electron called __________.','Free Radical','Homolytic cleavage results in the formation of neutral species (atom or group) which contains an unpaired electron called a free radical. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 350; Paragraph No. 5)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','Cation','Anion','Free Radical','Neutral atom','NEW','11-350','11-272',NULL),
(121001,'chemistry','Movement of _________ electron is indicated by a single barbed ‘fish hooks’.','Single','Movement of single electron is indicated by a single barbed ‘fish hooks’ (i.e. half headed curved arrow). Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 352; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'2','mcq','Double','Single','Radical','Paired','NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(121002,'chemistry','Match the following effects with their correct characteristics:','Inductive-Permanent,Electromeric-Temporary','The electron displacements due to the influence of an atom or a substituent group present in the molecule cause permanent polarization of the bond. Inductive effect and resonance effects are examples of this type of electron displacements. Temporary electron displacement effects are seen in a molecule when a reagent approaches to attack it. This type of electron displacement is called electromeric effect or polarisability effect. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 352; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'3','match','Inductive,Permanent','Electromeric,Temporary',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(121003,'chemistry','When ____ bond is formed between atoms of different electronegativity, the electron density is more towards the more electronegative atom.','Covalent','When a covalent bond is formed between atoms of different electronegativity, the electron density is more towards the more electronegative atom of the bond. Such a shift of electron density results in a polar covalent bond. Bond polarity leads to various electronic effects in organic compounds. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 352; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','Covalent','Coordinate Covalent','Ionic','Metallic','NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(121004,'chemistry','Polarisation of sigma- bond caused by the polarisation of adjacent sigma-bond is referred to as the ___ effect.','Inductive','Polarisation of sigma- bond caused by the polarisation of adjacent sigma-bond is referred to as the inductive effect. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 352; Paragraph No. 5)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','Inductive','Resonance','Mesomeric','Hyperconjugation','NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(121005,'chemistry','What is the relation of inductive effect with distance?','Inversely Proportion','This effect is passed on to the subsequent bonds also but the effect decreases rapidly as the number of intervening bonds increases and becomes vanishingly small after three bonds. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 352; Paragraph No. 6)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'2','mcq','Remains Constant','Direclty Proportion','Inversely Proportion','Not applicable for bonds.','NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(121006,'chemistry','Which of the following is an electron withdrawing group?','Halogen','Halogen are electron withdrawing group. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 352; Paragraph No. 6)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','-CH₃','Halogen','Ethyl','-OH','NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(121007,'chemistry','Which of the following is an electron donating group?','CH₃','Alkyls are electron donating groups. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 352; Paragraph No. 6)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','-CN','-COOH','COOR','CH₃','NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(121008,'chemistry','Inductive effect involves ____ of sigma electrons.','Displacement','Inductive effect involves displacement of sigma electrons towards more electronegative atom. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 352; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','Displacement','Substitute','Delocalization','None of these','NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(121009,'chemistry','Inductive effect of which of the following element is zero as compared to other elements?','H','Hydrogen has comparatively negligible inductive effect. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 352; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','O','H','Cl','C','NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(121010,'chemistry','Which bond is more polar between the following atoms?','C-F','F is more electronegative than O,N,H therefore C-F bond is most polar among them.Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 352; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'2','mcq','C-O','C-F','C-N','C-H','NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(121011,'chemistry','Which of the following pairs has only polar molecules ?','H₃C-NH₂ and H₃C-OH','C-N and C-O are both have polar bonds , therefore H₃C-NH₂ and H₃C-OH both are polar. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 352; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'2','mcq','H₃C-H and H₃C-Br','H₃C-NH₂ and H₃C-OH','H₃C-H and H₃C-SH','H₃C-NH₂ and H₃C-CH₃','NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(121012,'chemistry','Which effect can explain the stability of benzene ?','Resonance','The difference in energy between the actual structure and the lowest energy resonance structure is called the resonance stabilisation energy or simply the resonance energy. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 353; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','Inductive','Resonance','Electromeric','Mesomeric','NEW','11-353','11-275',NULL),
(121013,'chemistry','What is the bond length between two carbon atoms in benzene ?','139 pm','Benzene has a uniform C–C bond distances of 139 pm, a value intermediate between the C–C single(154pm) and C=C double (134 pm) bonds. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 353; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','139 pm','135 pm','154 pm','134 pm','NEW','11-353','11-275',NULL),
(121014,'chemistry','The resonating structures contribute to the actual structure in proportion to their ________.','Stability','The resonating structures contribute to the actual structure in proportion to their stability. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 353; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'2','mcq','Visibilty','Stability','Spinning','Energy','NEW','11-353','11-275',NULL),
(121015,'chemistry','The energy of actual structure of the molecule (the resonance hybrid) is lower than that of any of the ________ structures.','Canonical','The energy of actual structure of the molecule (the resonance hybrid) is lower than that of any of the canonical structures. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 353; Paragraph No. 6)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'2','mcq','Identical','Different','Hexagon','Canonical','NEW','11-353','11-275',NULL),
(121016,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct ?\n a. The more the number of important contributing structures, the less the resonance energy.\n b. Resonance is particularly important when the contributing structures are equivalent in energy.','Only b','More resonating structures means more resonance energy and therefore more stability. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 353; Paragraph No. 6)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Both','None of these','NEW','11-353','11-275',NULL),
(121017,'chemistry','Resonating structures have the same number of ______ electrons.','Unpaired','Resonating structures have the same number of unpaired electrons. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 353; Paragraph No. 7)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'2','mcq','Shared','Unpaired','Paired','Lone Paired','NEW','11-353','11-275',NULL),
(121018,'chemistry','Among the resonance structures, the one which has more number of covalent bonds is more _____than other.','Stable','Among the resonance structures, the one which has more number of covalent bonds is more stable than other. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 353; Paragraph No. 7)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'2','mcq','Unstable','Stable','Stronger','Weaker','NEW','11-353','11-275',NULL),
(121019,'chemistry','What type of effect is in place in the given diagram(12105)','+ R','In +R effect, the transfer of electrons is away from an atom or substituent group attached to the conjugated system. This electron displacement makes certain positions in the molecule of high electron densities. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 354; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'3','mcq','- R','+ R','+ I','- I','NEW','11-354','11-276','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121019q.png'),
(121020,'chemistry','What type of effect is in place in the given diagram(12106)','- R','-R effect is observed when the transfer of electrons is towards the atom or substituent group attached to the conjugated system. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 354; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'3','mcq','- R','+ R','+ I','- I','NEW','11-354','11-276','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121020q.png'),
(121021,'chemistry','The +R effect ___ electron density in the conjugated system.','Increases','+R effect, the transfer of electrons is away from an atom or substituent group attached to the conjugated system. This electron displacement makes certain positions in the molecule of high electron densities. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 354; Paragraph No. 2)"','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','fillup','In,cr,e,as,es,De',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-354','11-276',NULL),
(121022,'chemistry','-R effect ___ electron density in the conjugated system.','Decreases','-R effect is observed when the transfer of electrons is towards the atom or substituent group attached to the conjugated system. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 354; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','fillup','De,crea,ses,In',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-354','11-276',NULL),
(121023,'chemistry','Which of the following shows +R effect ?','OH','-OH donates electron pair towards the conjugated system. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 354; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','CN','OH','COOH','CHO','NEW','11-354','11-276',NULL),
(121024,'chemistry','Which of the following shows -R effect ?','All of these','-CN, -COOH, -CHO all withdraw electrons away from the conjugated system. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 354; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','COOH','CN','CHO','All of these','NEW','11-354','11-276',NULL),
(121025,'chemistry','Which among the following is an example of conjugated system?','All of these','The presence of alternate single and double bonds in an open chain or cyclic system is termed as a conjugated system. These systems often show abnormal behaviour. The examples are 1,3- butadiene, aniline and nitrobenzene etc. In such systems, the pie-electrons are delocalised and the system develops polarity. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 355; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','1,3- butadiene','Aniline','Nitrobenzene','All of these','NEW','11-355','11-277',NULL),
(121101,'chemistry','Which of the following is a method to purify organic compound ?','All of these','The common techniques used for purification are as follows : (i) Sublimation (ii) Crystallisation (iii) Distillation (iv) Differential extraction and (v) Chromatography. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 356; Paragraph No. 4 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Sublimation','Distillation','Crystallisation','All of these','NEW','11-356','11-278',NULL);
\nA(s) ⇌B(g) + C(g)  ;Kₚ₁= x atm\n\nD (s) ⇌ C(g) + E (g) ;Kₚ₂= y atm\n\n\nThe total pressure when both the solids dissociate\n simultaneously is [JEE (Main)-2019]','2(√x+y) atm','A(s) ⇌B(g) + C(g)\n          P₁       P₂        Kₚ₁ = P₁(P₁ + P₂) = x\n\nD (s) ⇌ C(g) + E (g)\n           P₂+P₁   P₂     Kₚ₂ = P₂(P₁ + P₂) = y\n\n→P₁(P₁ + P₂) + P₂(P₁ + P₂) = x + y\n→(P₁ + P₂)² = x + y  \n→ P₁ + P₂ = √x+y\n\nTotal pressure = 2(P₁ + P₂) = 2 √x+y ) atm at\n equilibrium','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','x² + y² atm','x + y atm','√ x + y atm','2(√x+y) atm','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71414,'chemistry','For the equilibrium\n2H₂O ⇌ H₃O + OH  , the value of △Gº at 298\n K is approximately [JEE (Main)-2019]','80 kJ/mol','△G = △G° + RT lnQ\nAt equilibrium\n△G = 0 and Q = Keq\n→ △G° = – 2.303 RT log Kw\n            = –2.303 × 8.314 × 298 log\n10⁻¹⁴\n≈ 80 kJ/mol','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','80 kJ/mol','-80 kJ/mol','100 kJ/mol','-100 kJ/mol','NEW','11-208','11-184',NULL),
(71415,'chemistry','Consider the reaction\n \nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)\nThe equilibrium constant of the above reaction is Kₚ\n\nIf pure ammonia is left to dissociate, the partial  pressure of ammonia at equilibrium is given by  (Assume that PNH₃ << Ptotal at equilibrium) [JEE (Main)-2019]','3³/² x² Kₚ½/16','2NH₃(g) ⇌N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) , K = 1/Kₚ\n   P₀ - 2x       x            3x\n    → P = P₀ + 2x and x = P₀/2 ; 4x = p\n\nK = 1/Kₚ = x(3x)³/P_(NH₃)²\nP_(NH₃)² = 3³x⁴Kₚ\nP_NH₃     = 3³/² x² Kₚ½/16','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','3³/² x² Kₚ½/16','Kₚp²/4','3³/² x² Kₚ½/4','Kₚp²/16','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71416,'chemistry','5.1 g NH₄SH is introduced in 3.0 L evacuated flask  at 327ºC. 30% of the solid NH₄SH decomposed to  NH₃ and H₂S as gases. The Kₚ  of the reaction at  327ºC is (R = 0.082 L atm /mol K, Molar mass of  S = 32 g /mol, molar mass of N = 14 g /mol)  [JEE (Main)-2019]','0.242 atm²','NH₄SH → NH₃(g) + H₂S(g)\nInitial moles 5.1/51 = 0.1 mol\n\n                                      NH₄SH → NH₃(g) + H₂S(g)\nMoles at equilibrium      0.1(1-0.3)        0.1×0.3          0.1×0.3 \n\n→ K꜀\n = [NH₃][H₂S] = (0.03/3)² = 10⁻⁴\n\nKₚ = K꜀(RT)△ng = 10⁻⁴ × (0.082 × 600)² = 242 atm²','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','4.9 × 10⁻³ atm²','0.242 atm²','1 × 10⁻⁴ atm²','0.242 × 10⁻⁴ atm²','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71417,'chemistry','A mixture of 100 mmol of Ca(OH)₂ and 2 g of sodium sulphate was dissolved in water and the  volume was made up to 100 mL. The mass of  calcium sulphate formed and the concentration of  OH⁻ in resulting solution, respectively, are (Molar  mass of Ca(OH)₂, Na₂SO₄ and CaSO₄ are 74, 143  and 136 g /mol, respectively; Ksp of Ca(OH)₂ is  5.5 × 10⁻⁶) [JEE (Main)-2019]','1.9 g, 0.28 mol /L','Na₂SO₄ + Ca(OH)₂ → CaSO₄ + NaOH\n\nm mol of Na₂SO₄ = 2 × 1000/143 = 13.98 m mol\nm mol of CaSO₄ formed = 13.98 m mol\n\nMass of CaSO₄ formed = 13.9L × 10⁻³ × 136 = 1.90 g\n\nm mol of NaOH = 28 m mol\n\nCa(OH)₂ ⇌Ca²⁺ + 2OH⁻\nValue of ‘S’ will be negligible so\n\n[OH⁻] = 0.028/0.1 = 0.28 mol/L','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','1.9 g, 0.28 mol /L','13.6 g, 0.28 mol /L','13.6 g, 0.14 mol /L','1.5g, 0.30 mol /L','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71418,'chemistry','The values of Kₚ /K꜀  for the following reactions at  300 K are, respectively (At 300 K, RT = 24.62 dm³ atm /mol)\nN₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2NO(g)\nN₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g)\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g) [JEE (Main)-2019]','1, 24.62 dm³ atm /mol, 1.65 × 10⁻³ mol² /dm⁶ atm²','Kₚ\n/K꜀\n = RT^△ng \nN₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2NO(g) , △ng = 0\nKₚ\n/K꜀\n = 1\n\nN₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g) , △ng = 1\nKₚ\n/K꜀\n = 24.62 \n\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g) , △ng = -2\nKₚ\n/K꜀\n = 1/(24.62)² = 1.65 × 10⁻³','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','24.62 dm³ atm /mol, 606.0 dm⁶ atm²/ mol²,\n 1.65 × 10⁻³ mol² /dm⁶ atm²','1, 24.62 dm³ atm /mol, 1.65 × 10⁻³ mol² /dm⁶ atm²','1, 24.62 dm³ atm /mol, 606.0 dm⁶ atm² /mol²','1, 4.1 × 10⁻² /dm³ atm mol, 606 dm⁶ atm²/mol²','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71419,'chemistry','Consider the following reversible chemical reactions\n \nA₂(g) + B₂(g) ⇌K₁⇌ 2AB(g)  ------>(1)\n\n6AB(g) ⇌K₂⇌3A₂(g)+ 3B₂(g) ------>(2)\n\nThe relation between K₁ and K₂ is  [JEE (Main)-2019','K₂ = (1/K₁)³','A₂(g) + B₂(g) ⇌K₁⇌ 2AB(g)\n2AB ⇌A₂ + B₂ ; K = 1/K₁\n6AB(g) ⇌K₂⇌3A₂(g)+ 3B₂(g) ; K₂ = (1/K₁)³','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','K₁³ = K₂','K₁K₂ = 1/3','K₂ = (1/K₁)³','K₁K₂ = 3','NEW','11-201','11-177',NULL),
(71420,'chemistry','20 ml of 0.1 M H₂SO₄ solution is added to 30 mL  of 0.2 M NH₄OH solution. The pH of the resultant  mixture is : [pKb of NH₄OH = 4.7]  [JEE (Main)-2019]','9','H₂SO₄ + 2NH₄OH\n  20 ml        30ml\n  0.1M          0.2M\n               ↓\n(NH₄)₂SO₄ + NH₄OH\n     20 ml         10ml\n     0.1M           0.2M\n\npOH = pK_b + log(salt/base)\n         = 4.7 + log ( 2×20× 0.1 / 10 × 2 )\n         = 4.7 + log2 = 5\n    pH = 14 – pOH = 9','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','9','9.2','5','5.2','NEW','11-227','11-203',NULL),
(71510,'chemistry','Acidity of BF₃ can be explained on the basis of which of the following concepts?','Lewis concept','According to Lewis concept, a positively charged or an electron deficient species acts as Lewis acid. BF₃ is an electron deficient compound with B having 6 electrons only.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Arrhenius concept','Bronsted Lowry concept','Lewis concept','Bronsted Lowry as well as Lewis concept','NEW','11-216','11-192',NULL),
(71421,'chemistry','An alkali is titrated against an acid with methyl  orange as indicator, which of the following is a  correct combination? [JEE (Main)-2018] Refer diagram(n0705)','3','The pH range of methyl orange is\n\n←Pinkesh red — 3.9—4.5—Yellow→\n\nWeak base is having pH greater than 7. When\n methyl orange is added to weak base solution, the\n solution becomes yellow. This solution is titrated by\n strong acid and at the end point pH will be less than\n 3.1. Therefore solution becomes pinkish red.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/71421q.png'),
(71422,'chemistry','Which of the following salts is the most basic in  aqueous solution? [JEE (Main)-2018]','CH₃COOK','CH₃COOK + H₂O → CH₃COOH + KOH\n                                                           Basic\nFeCl₃ → Acidic solution\nAl(CN)₃ → Salt of weak acid and weak base\nPb(CH₃COO)₂ → Salt of weak acid and weak base\nCH₃COOK → is salt of weak acid and strong base.\nHence solution of CH₃COOK is basic.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','Al(CN)₃','CH₃COOK','FeCl₃','Pb(CH₃COO)₂','NEW','11-226','11-202',NULL),
(71423,'chemistry','An aqueous solution contains an unknown  concentration of Ba²⁺. When 50 mL of a 1 M  solution of Na₂SO₄ is added, BaSO₄ just begins to precipitate. The final volume is 500 mL. The  solubility product of BaSO₄ is 1 × 10⁻¹⁰. What is  original concentration of Ba²⁺? [JEE (Main)-2018]','1.1 × 10⁻⁹ M','Final concentration of [SO₄²⁻] = [ 50 × 1 ] / [ 500 ] = 0.1 M\n\nKₛₚ of BaSO₄ = [Ba²⁺][SO₄²⁻] = 1 × 10⁻¹⁰\n\n[Ba²⁺][0.1] = 10⁻¹⁰/0.1 = 10⁻⁹ M\n\nConcentration of Ba²⁺ in final solution = 10⁻⁹ M\nConcentration of Ba²⁺ in the original solution.\n\nM₁V₁ = M₂V₂\nM₁(500-50) = 10⁻⁹(500)\nM₁ = 1.1 × 10⁻⁹ M','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','1.1 × 10⁻⁹ M','1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ M','2 × 10⁻⁹ M','5 × 10⁻⁹ M','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71424,'chemistry','An aqueous solution contains 0.10 M H₂S and  0.20 M HCl. If the equilibrium constant for the  formation of HS⁻ from H₂S is 1.0 × 10⁻⁷ and that  of S²⁻ from HS⁻ ions is 1.2 × 10⁻¹³ then the  concentration of S²⁻ ions in aqueous solution is  [JEE (Main)-2018].','3 × 10⁻²⁰','In presence of external H⁺,\nH₂S  ⇌ 2H⁺ + S²⁻ , Kₐ₁. Kₐ₂ = Keq\n\n[H⁺]²[S²⁻]/[H₂S] = 1 × 10⁻⁷ × 1.2 × 10⁻¹³\n\n[0.2]²[S²⁻]/[0.1] = 1.2 × 10⁻²⁰\n[S²⁻] = 3 × 10⁻²⁰','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','3 × 10⁻²⁰','6 × 10⁻²¹','5 × 10⁻¹⁹','5 × 10⁻⁸','NEW','11-225','11-201',NULL),
(71425,'chemistry','Which of the following lines correctly show the  temperature dependence of equilibrium constant K,  for an exothermic reaction? [JEE (Main)-2018] Refer diagram(n0706)','A and B','Equilibrium constant K = (Aբ/A_b) e^△H°/RT\n                            lnK = ln(Aբ/A_b) - △H°/RT\n                           y      =  C + mx\nComparing with equation of straight line,\n             Slope = -△H°/R\nSince, reaction is exothermic, △H° = –ve, therefore,\n slope = +ve. \nHere A and B showing positive slope.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','A and B','B and C','C and D','A and D','NEW','11-211','11-187',NULL),
(71426,'chemistry','pKₐ of a weak acid (HA) and pK_b of a weak base  (BOH) are 3.2 and 3.4, respectively, The pH of their  salt (AB) solution is [JEE (Main)-2017]','6.9','A + B ⇌ C + D\nInitially:   1     1      11         [D]eq = 1.818 M\nQ = 1\nQ < Keq\n→ Equilibrium is forward shifted.\n                    A + B ⇌ C + D\nEquilibrium :1–x 1–x   1+x 1+x\n     (1+x)²/(1-x)² = 10²\n     (1+x)/(1-x)   = 10\n              1 + x = 10 – 10x\n                 11x = 9\n                   x = 9/11 = 0.818\nSo, equilibrium concentration of \'D\' = 1.818 M.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','7','6.9','7.2','1','NEW','11-226','11-202',NULL),
(71427,'chemistry','The equilibrium constant at 298 K for a reaction  A + B ⇌ C + D is 100. If the initial concentration  of all the four species were 1 M each, then  equilibrium concentration of D (in mol /L) will be  [JEE (Main)-2016]','1.818',NULL,'Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','0.818','1.818','1.182','0.182','NEW','11-199','11-175',NULL),
(71428,'chemistry','The standard Gibbs energy change at 300 K for the  reaction 2A ⇌ B + C is 2494.2 J. At a given\n time, the composition of the reaction mixture is  [A] = 1/2 , [B] = 2 and [C] = 1/2 . The reaction proceeds  in the : [R = 8.314 J/K/mol, e = 2.718] [JEE (Main)-2015','Reverse direction because Q > K꜀','2A ⇌ B + C ,△G° = 2494.2 J\nAs we know △G° = –2.303 RT logK꜀\n2494.2 = –2.303 × 8.314 × 300 log K꜀\n–0.434 = log K꜀\nK꜀ = anti log (–0.434)\n \nK꜀ = 0.367\n[A] = 1/2 , [B] = 2 and [C] = 1/2\n\nNow, Q꜀ = [ C ] [ B] / [ A ] ² = (1/2)2 / (1/2)² = 4\nas Q꜀ > K꜀, hence reaction will shift in backward\n direction.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','Forward direction because Q > K꜀','Reverse direction because Q > K꜀','Forward direction because Q < K꜀','Reverse direction because Q < K꜀','NEW','11-208','11-184',NULL),
(71429,'chemistry','For the reaction SO₂ + 1/2O ₂  ⇌ SO₃(g),\nif Kₚ = K꜀(RT)x where the symbols have usual  meaning then the value of x is (assuming ideality)\n [JEE (Main)-2014]','-1/2','SO₂ + 1/2O\n₂\n ⇌ SO₃(g)\nKₚ = K꜀(RT)ˣ\nx =△ng = no. of gaseous moles in product\n – no. of gaseous moles in reactant\n   = 1- (1 +(1/2)) = 1-3/2 = -1/2','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','1/2','-1/2','1','-1','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71430,'chemistry','How many litres of water must be added to 1 litre of an aqueous solution of HCl with a pH of 1 to  create an aqueous solution with pH of 2?  [JEE (Main)-2013]','9 L','[H⁺] of the given HCl solution = 0.1 M\nLet x litre of water be added to 1 litre of the given HCl\n solution to get pH = 2 or [H⁺] = 10⁻² M\n\n0.1/ 1+x = 0.01\nx = 9 litres','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','9 L','0.9 L','2 L','0.1 L','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL);
respectively are\n Cr in [Cr(H₂O)₆]Cl₃ , [Cr(C₆H₆)₂] and K₂[Cr(CN)₂(O₂)(O₂)(NH₃)] [JEE (Main)-2018]','+3, 0 and +6','[Cr(H₂O)₆]Cl₃ → X + (0×6) - (1×3) = 0 so, X = +3\n[Cr(C₆H₆)₂] → X + 2×0 = 0 so X = 0\nK₂[Cr(CN)₂(O₂)(O₂)(NH₃)] → 1×2 + (x-1)2 - 2×2 - 2×1 = 0 , so X = +6','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','+3, +4 and +6','+3, +2 and +4','+3, 0 and +6','+3, 0 and +4','NEW','12-248','12-122',NULL),
