(231601,'chemistry','Which of the following complexes formed by Cu²⁺ ions is most stable?','Cu²⁺ + 4CN⁻\n ⇌ [Cu(CN)₄]\n²⁻, logK = 27.3','Higher the value of stability constant K, greater is the stability. In the given examples, the reaction with the highest value of log K which in turn corresponds to the highest value of K.So [Cu(CN)₄ ]²⁻  is most stable out of given Cu²⁺ complexes.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','Cu²⁺ + 4NH₃⇌ [Cu(NH₃)\n₄]\n²⁺, logK = 11.6','Cu²⁺ + 4CN⁻\n ⇌ [Cu(CN)₄]\n²⁻, logK = 27.3','Cu²⁺ + 2en ⇌ [Cu(en)₂]\n²⁺, logK = 15.4','Cu²⁺ + 4H₂O ⇌ [Cu(H₂O)4]\n²⁺, logK = 8.9','NEW','12-246','12-120',NULL),
(181323,'chemistry','In any uni molecular reaction,\n(a) only one reacting species is involved in the rate determining step\n(b) the order and the molecularity of slowest step are equal to one\n(c) the molecularity of the reaction is one and order is zero\n(d) both molecularity and order of the reaction','a,b','For any unimolecular reaction, only one reactant is involved in rate determining step and the order and molecularity of rate determining or slowest step are one.\nA→ B , rate = k[A]','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c,d','a,b,c','c,d','NEW','12-104','12-070',NULL),
(231602,'chemistry','The colour of the coordination compounds depends on the crystal field\n splitting. What will be the correct order of absorption of wavelength of light in\n the visible region, for the complexes, [Co(NH₃)₆]\n³⁺ , [Co(CN)₆]\n³⁻ , [Co(H₂O)₆]\n³⁺','[Co(H₂O)₆]\n³⁺ > \n[Co(NH₃)₆]\n³⁺\n >[Co(CN)₆]\n³⁻','In strong field ligand, there is more energy separation than weak field ligand it means that as the strength of ligand increases crystal field splitting energy increases.\nΔE=hc/λ or Δα1/λ\nAs ΔE increases , wavelength of light absorbed decreases.\nFurther , the colour of coordination compounds depends on nature and the magnitude of crystal field splitting of the ligans bonded with central atom. a stronger ligand has higher splitting power than a weak ligand amongst the given ligands in Co-complexes the order of splitting power is : H₂O < NH₃ < CN⁻ ; since CN⁻ has higher splitting power it would absorb more.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','[Co(CN)₆]\n³⁻ > Co(NH₃)₆]\n³⁺> [Co(H₂O)₆]\n³⁺','Co(NH₃)₆]\n³⁺ > [Co(H₂O)₆]\n³⁺ > [Co(CN)₆]\n³⁻','[Co(H₂O)₆]\n³⁺ > \n[Co(NH₃)₆]\n³⁺\n >[Co(CN)₆]\n³⁻','[Co(CN)₆]\n³⁻ > Co(NH₃)₆]\n³⁺ > [Co(H₂O)₆]\n³⁺','NEW','12-257','12-131',NULL),
(231603,'chemistry','When 0.1 mol CoCl₃(NH₃)\n₅ is treated with excess of AgNO₃, 0.2 mol of AgCl\n are obtained. The conductivity of solution will correspond to','1:2 electrolyte','When 0.1 mol CoCl₃(NH₃)₅  is treated with excess of AgNO₃ , 0.2 mol of AgCl are obtained. When 1 mol CoCl  (NH₃)₅  is treated with excess of AgNO₃ , 2 mol of AgCl will be obtained. \nHence, one molecule of CoCl₃(NH₃)₅ dissociates to form two chloride ions. CoCl₃(NH₃)₅ can be represented as [Co(NH₃)₅ Cl]Cl₂ .\n[Co(NH₃)₅Cl]Cl₂  →[Co(NH₃)₅Cl]²⁺\n+2Cl⁻.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','1:3 electrolyte','1:2 electrolyte','1:1 electrolyte','3:1 electrolyte','NEW','12-246','12-120',NULL),
(231604,'chemistry','When 1 mol CrCl₃⋅6H₂O is treated with excess of AgNO₃, 3 mol of AgCl are\n obtained. The formula of the complex is :','[Cr(H₂O)₆]Cl₃','When 1 mol CrCl₃.6H₂O is treated with excess of AgNO₃ , 3 mol of AgCl are obtained. It means that when CrCl₃.6H₂O molecule dissociates in aqueous solution, all three chloride ions comes in solution. The formula of the complex is \n[Cr(H₂O)₆]Cl₃ .CrCl₃ .6H₂O  or [Cr(H₂O)₆]Cl₃ →[Cr(H₂O)₆]³⁺ +3Cl−3Cl⁻ +3AgNO₃ →3AgCl↓+3NO₃⁻','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','[CrCl₃(H₂O)₃]⋅3H₂O','[CrCl₂(H₂O)₄]Cl⋅2H₂O','[CrCl(H₂O)₅]Cl₂⋅H₂O','[Cr(H₂O)₆]Cl₃','NEW','12-246','12-120',NULL),
(231605,'chemistry','The correct IUPAC name of [Pt(NH₃)\n2Cl₂] is','Diamminedichloridoplatinum (II)','The correct IUPAC name of the compound is diammine dichlorido platinum (II).As here Pt in +2 oxidation state.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Diamminedichloridoplatinum (II)','Diamminedichloridoplatinum (IV)','Diamminedichloridoplatinum (0)','Dichloridodiammineplatinum (IV)','NEW','12-249','12-123',NULL),
(231606,'chemistry','The stabilisation of coordination compounds due to chelation is called the\n chelate effect. Which of the following is the most stable complex species?','[Fe(C₂O₄)\n₃]\n³⁻','Formation of a cycle by linkage between a metal ion and ligand is known as chelation. Chelation stabilizes the coordinate compound. The ligand which chelates the metal ion are known as chelating ligand. Here, only [Fe(C₂O₄)₃]³⁻ is a coordination compound which contains oxalate ion as a chelating ligand. Hence, it stabilizes coordinate compound by chelating Fe³⁺  ion.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','[Fe(CO)₅]','[Fe(CN)₆]\n³⁻','[Fe(C₂O₄)\n₃]\n³⁻','[Fe(H₂O)₆]\n³⁺','NEW','12-250','12-124',NULL),
(231607,'chemistry','Indicate the complex ion which shows geometrical isomerism.','[Cr(H₂O)₄Cl₂]⁺','[Cr(H₂O)₄Cl₂ ]⁺ , it is MA₄B₂  type complex compound which shows geometrical isomerism.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','[Cr(H₂O)₄Cl₂]⁺','[Pt(NH₃)\n₃Cl]','[Co(NH₃)₆]\n³⁺','[Co(CN)₅(NC)]³⁻','NEW','12-252','12-124',NULL),
(231608,'chemistry','The CFSE for octahedral [CoCl₆]\n⁴⁻ is 18,000 /cm. The CFSE for tetrahedral\n[CoCl₄]\n²⁻ will be','8,000 /cm','∆ₜ = (4/9)∆₀ \nCFSE for tetrahedral complexe is ∆ₜ = (4/9)∆₀  = 4/9 × 18,000 = 8,000 /cm','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','18,000 /cm','16,000 /cm','8,000 /cm','20,000 /cm','NEW','12-257','12-131',NULL),
(231609,'chemistry','Due to the presence of ambidentate ligands coordination compounds show\n isomerism. Palladium complexes of the type [Pd(C₆H₅)₂(SCN)₂] and\n [Pd(C₆H₅)\n₂(NCS)₂] are','linkage isomers','The ligands having two different bonding sites are known as ambident ligands e.g., NCS, NO₂, etc.\nHere, NCS has two binding sites at N and S.\nHence, NCS (thiocyanate) can bind to the metal ion in two ways M <- NCS or M —> SNC\nThus, coordination compounds containing NCS as a ligand can show linkage isomerism i.e., [Pd(C₆H₅)₂(SCN)₂] and\n [Pd(C₆H₅)\n₂(NCS)₂] are linkage isomers.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','linkage isomers','coordination isomers','ionisation isomers','geometrical isomers','NEW','12-253','12-125',NULL),
(231610,'chemistry','The compounds [Co(SO₄)(NH₃)\n₅]Br and [Co(SO₄)(NH₃)\n₅]Cl represent','no isomerism','[Co(SO₄)(NH₃)\n₅]Br and [Co(SO₄)(NH₃)\n₅]Cl  represent no isomerism because they are different compounds.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','linkage isomerism','ionisation isomerism','coordination isomerism','no isomerism','NEW','12-252','12-124',NULL),
(231611,'chemistry','A chelating agent has two or more than two donor atoms to bind to a single\n metal ion. Which of the following is not a chelating agent?','thiosulphato','Thiosulphate or S₂O₃⁻ is not a chelating agent since it is a monodentate ligand.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','thiosulphato','oxalato','glycinato','ethane-1,2-diamine','NEW','12-250','12-124',NULL),
(231612,'chemistry','Which of the following species is not expected to be a ligand?','NH₄⁺','Ligand must donate a pair of electrons or loosely held electron pair to metal and form a M – L bond. Among all NH₄⁺ does not have any pair of electrons. Hence NH₄⁺ is not a ligand.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','NO','NH₄⁺','NH₂CH₂CH₂NH₂','CO','NEW','12-248','12-122',NULL),
