(50106,'chemistry','The type of interaction between the two molecules in the given diagram is?\n(n502)','Dipole - Induced dipole','Dipole-Induced dipole forces operate between the polar molecules having permanent dipole and the molecules lacking permanent dipole.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 138; Paragraph No.3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Introduction, intermolecular forces and thermal energy',NULL,'3','mcq','Dipole - Induced dipole','Dipole - Dipole','Dispersion forces','Ion-dipole interaction','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/50106q.png'),
(50107,'chemistry','________  are a  powerful force in determining the structure and properties of many compounds, for example proteins and nucleic acids.','Hydrogen bonds','Energy of hydrogen bond varies between 10 to 100 kJ mol–1. \n\nThis is quite a significant amount of energy; therefore, hydrogen bonds are a powerful force in determining the structure and properties of many compounds, for example proteins and nucleic acids.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 139; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Introduction, intermolecular forces and thermal energy',NULL,'1','mcq','Hydrogen bonds','Dipole - Dipole forces','London forces','Dipole - Induced Dipole forces','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50108,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement\n\na. Thermal energy is directly proportional to the temperature of a substance.\n\nb. Thermal energy of molecules tends to keep them apart. \n\nc. Three states of matter are the result of balance between intermolecular forces and  the thermal energy of the molecules.','All of these','Thermal energy is the energy of a body arising from motion of its atoms or molecules. \n\nIt is directly proportional to the temperature of the substance. \n\nIt is the measure of average kinetic energy of the particles of matter and is thus responsible for movement of particles.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 139; Paragraph No. 3 and 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Introduction, intermolecular forces and thermal energy',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c','All of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50109,'chemistry','The correct order of predominance of intermolecular interactions in the three states of matter is','Solid > Liquid > Gas','Intermolecular interactions are more dominant in solid then in liquids and least in gases.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 139; Paragraph No.5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Introduction, intermolecular forces and thermal energy',NULL,'3','mcq','Liquid > Gas > Solid','Solid > Liquid > Gas','Gas > Liquid > Solid','Solid > Gas > Liquid','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50110,'chemistry','The correct order of predominance of thermal energy in the three states of matter is','Gas > Liquid > Solid','Thermal energy is maximum for gases.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 139; Paragraph No.5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Introduction, intermolecular forces and thermal energy',NULL,'3','mcq','Solid > Liquid > Gas','Gas > Liquid > Solid','Liquid > Solid > Gas','Liquid > Gas > Solid','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50111,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion: Dipole-dipole forces acting between the molecules possessing permanent dipole are weaker than ion-ion interactions.\n\nReason: The attractive forces decrease with increase in distance between the dipoles','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Ends of the dipoles possess “partial charges” and these partial charges are always less than the unit electronic charge (1.6×10⁻¹⁹ C), that is why dipole-dipole forces are weaker than ion-ion forces.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 138; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Introduction, intermolecular forces and thermal energy',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50112,'chemistry','Bulk properties of matter are the properties associated with','Collection of a large number of atoms, ions or molecules','Most of the observable characteristics of chemical systems with which we are familiar represent bulk properties of matter, i.e., the properties associated with a collection of a large number of atoms, ions or molecules.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 136; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Introduction, intermolecular forces and thermal energy',NULL,'1','mcq','One atom','One molecule','One ion','Collection of a large number of atoms, ions or molecules','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50113,'chemistry','The characteristics of the three states of water depends on','energies of the molecules and on the manner in which water molecules aggregate','Characteristics of the three states of water depend on the energies of molecules and\non the manner in which water molecules aggregate. Same is true for other substances also.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 136; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Introduction, intermolecular forces and thermal energy',NULL,'1','mcq','energies of molecules only','the manner in which water molecules aggregate only','energies of the molecules and on the manner in which water molecules aggregate','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50114,'chemistry','Intermolecular forces are the forces of','attraction and repulsion','Intermolecular forces are the forces of attraction and repulsion between interacting particles (atoms and molecules).\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 137; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Introduction, intermolecular forces and thermal energy',NULL,'1','mcq','attraction only','repulsion only','attraction and repulsion','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50115,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\n\na. Only a few elements can participate in hydrogen bond formation, therefore it is treated as a separate category\n\nb. Atoms and nonpolar molecules are electrically symmetrical and have no dipole moment because their electronic charge cloud is\nsymmetrically distributed. But a dipole may develop momentarily even in such atoms and molecules.','Both a and b','Only a few elements can participate in hydrogen bond formation, they include Nitrogen, Oxygen, Fluorine and Chlorine.  \n\nAtoms and nonpolar molecules are electrically symmetrical and have no dipole moment because their electronic charge cloud is symmetrically distributed. \n\nBut a dipole may develop momentarily even in such atoms and molecules. This statement is true.    \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 137; Paragraph No.1 and 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Introduction, intermolecular forces and thermal energy',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Both a and b','None of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50116,'chemistry','London forces were first proposed by Fritz London who was a','German physicist','Fritz London is was a German Physicist. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 137; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Introduction, intermolecular forces and thermal energy',NULL,'1','mcq','English physicist','Dutch physicist','German physicist','Greek physicist','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50117,'chemistry','Another name for London forces is','Dispersion forces','London forces are also referred to as dispersion forces.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 138; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Introduction, intermolecular forces and thermal energy',NULL,'1','mcq','Dispersion forces','Distorted forces','Refractive forces','Reflective forces','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50118,'chemistry','London forces are always attractive and interaction energy is inversely proportional to the','sixth power of the distance between two interacting particles','London forces are always attractive and interaction energy is inversely proportional to the sixth power of the distance between two interacting particles (i.e., 1/r⁶ where r is the distance between two particles). \n\nThese forces are important only at short distances (~500 pm) and their magnitude depends on the polarisability of the particle.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 138; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Introduction, intermolecular forces and thermal energy',NULL,'1','mcq','fourth power of the distance between two interacting particles','third power of the distance between two interacting particles','sixth power of the distance between two interacting particles','eight power of the distance between two interacting particles','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50119,'chemistry','Intermolecular forces doesn\'t include','electrostatic forces','Intermolecular forces does not include the electrostatic forces that exist between the two oppositely charged ions and the forces that hold atoms of a molecule together i.e., covalent bonds.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 137; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Introduction, intermolecular forces and thermal energy',NULL,'1','mcq','electrostatic forces','magnetic force','electromagnetic forces','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50120,'chemistry','Dipole-dipole interaction energy between stationary polar molecules (as in solids) is proportional to distance r as','1/r³','Dipole-dipole interaction energy between stationary polar molecules (as in solids) is proportional to 1/r³.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 138; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Introduction, intermolecular forces and thermal energy',NULL,'1','mcq','1/r²','1/r³','1/r⁴','1/r⁵','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50121,'chemistry','High polarisability ____ the strength of attractive interactions.','increases','Induced dipole moment depends upon the dipole moment present in the permanent dipole and the polarisability of the electrically neutral molecule. \n\nHigh polarisability increases the strength of attractive interactions.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 138; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Introduction, intermolecular forces and thermal energy',NULL,'1','mcq','increases','decreases','removes','equals','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50122,'chemistry','What type of highly polar bonds should be present for hydrogen bonding?','All of these','Hydrogen bonding is found in the molecules in which highly polar N–H, O–H or H–F bonds are present.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 139; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Introduction, intermolecular forces and thermal energy',NULL,'1','mcq','N-H only','O-H only','H-F only','All of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50616,'chemistry','Critical volume for He is','0.0577 dm³ mol⁻¹','Critical volume for He is 0.0577 dm³ mol⁻¹ \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Table No. 5.4 )','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','0.577 dm³ mol⁻¹','5.77 dm³ mol⁻¹','57.7 dm³ mol⁻¹','0.0577 dm³ mol⁻¹','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50617,'chemistry','Critical temperature for N₂ is','126 K','Critical temperature for N₂ is 126 K\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Table No. 5.4 )','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','126 K','100 K','222 k','273 K','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50618,'chemistry','Critical pressure for N₂ is','33.9 bar','Critical pressure for N₂ is 33.9 bar\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Table No. 5.4 )','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','27.5 bar','1 bar','33.9 bar','2 bar','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
