(50626,'chemistry','Critical temperature for H₂O is','647.1 K','Critical temperature for H₂O is 647.1 K\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Table No. 5.4 )','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','373 K','647.1 K','273 K','100 K','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50627,'chemistry','Critical pressure for H₂O is','220.6 bar','Critical pressure for H₂O is 220.6 bar\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Table No. 5.4 )','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','22.0 bar','220.6 bar','1 bar','100 bar','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50628,'chemistry','Critical volume for H₂O is','0.0450 dm³ mol⁻¹','Critical volume for H₂O is 0.0450 dm³ mol⁻¹.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Table No. 5.4 )','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','0.0450 dm³ mol⁻¹','0.45 dm³ mol⁻¹','4.5 dm³ mol⁻¹','450 dm³ mol⁻¹','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50629,'chemistry','Critical temperature for NH₃ is','405.5 K','Critical temperature for NH₃ is 405.5 K\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Table No. 5.4 )','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','405.5 K','40 K','4.0 K','0.4 K','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50630,'chemistry','Critical pressure for NH₃ is','113 bar','Critical pressure for NH₃ is 113 bar\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Table No. 5.4 )','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','123 bar','1 bar','100 bar','113 bar','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50631,'chemistry','Critical volume for NH₃ is','0.0723 dm³ mol⁻¹','Critical volume for NH₃ is\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Table No. 5.4 )','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','0.723 dm³ mol⁻¹','1.23 dm³ mol⁻¹','7.23 dm³ mol⁻¹','0.0723 dm³ mol⁻¹','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50855,'chemistry','An ideal gas, obeying kinetic theory of gases cannot be liquefied, because;','forces acting between its molecules are negligible','An ideal gas, obeying kinetic theory of gases cannot be liquefied, because forces acting between its molecules are negligible\n\nNEET (1995)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','it solidifies before becoming a liquid','forces acting between its molecules are negligible','its critical temperature is above 0°C','its molecules are relatively small in size','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50856,'chemistry','For real gases van der Waals’ equation is written as (p+ an²/v²)(V- nb)=nRT where a and b are van der Waals’ constants. \n\nTwo sets of gases are\n\n(I) O₂, CO₂, H₂ and He\n\n(II) CH₄, O₂ and H₂\n\nThe gases given in set-I in increasing order of b and gases given in set-II in decreasing order of a, are arranged below. \n\nSelect the correct order from the following :','(I) H₂ < He < O₂ < CO₂ (II) CH₄ > O₂ > H₂','For real gases van der Waals’ equation is written as (p+  an²/v²)(V- nb)=nRT\nwhere a and b are van der Waals’ constants. \n\nTwo sets of gases are\n\n(I) O₂, CO₂, H₂ and He\n\n(II) CH₄, O₂ and H₂\n\nthe correct order is (I) H₂ < He < O₂ < CO₂ (II) CH₄ > O₂ > H₂\n\n(NEET 2012)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(I) He < H₂ < CO₂ < O₂ (II) CH₄ > H₂ > O₂','(I) O₂ < He < H₂ < CO₂ (II) H₂ > O₂ > CH₄','(I) H₂ < He < O₂ < CO₂ (II) CH₄ > O₂ > H₂','(I) H₂ < O₂ < He < CO₂ (II) O₂ > CH4 > H₂','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50957,'chemistry','Which of these statements are a consequence of two atoms ‘A’ and ‘B’ in the being in close vicinity of each other.\n\na) Momentarily, an electronic charge distribution occur in one of the atoms, say ‘A’, becomes unsymmetrical.\n\nb) A dipole is induced in the atom ‘B’.\n\nc) The temporary dipoles of atom ‘A’ and ‘B’ attract each other and temporary dipoles are induced in molecules.','all of these','Suppose we have two atoms ‘A’ and ‘B’ in the close vicinity of each other, It may so happen that momentarily electronic charge distribution in one of the atoms, say ‘A’, becomes unsymmetrical i.e., the charge cloud is more on one side than the other.\n\nThis results in the development of instantaneous dipole on the atom ‘A’ for a very short time. \n\nThis instantaneous or transientdipole distorts the electron density of the other atom ‘B’, which is close to it and as a consequence a dipole is induced in the atom ‘B’.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 137; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b and c only','a and b only','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50958,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true?\n\na) Dipole-dipole interaction energy between rotating polar molecules is proportional to 1/r ⁶, where r is the distance between polar molecules.\n\nb) Dipole-dipole interaction energy between stationary polar molecules (as in solids) is proportional to 1/r³.','both a and b','Dipole-dipole interaction energy between stationary polar molecules (as in solids) is proportional to 1/r³ and that between rotating polar molecules is is proportional to 1/r ⁶, where r is the distance between polar molecules.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 138; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50959,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true.\n\na) High polarisability increases the strength of attractive interactions.\n\nb) Molecules of larger sizes can easily be polarized.','both a and b','Molecules of larger size can be easily polarized. \n\nHigh polarisability increases the strength of attractive interactions.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 138; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50960,'chemistry','The correct formation of hydrogen bond is;\n\na) ᵟ+H-Fᵟ-... Hᵟ+- Fᵟ-\n\nb) ᵟ-H-Fᵟ+... Hᵟ-- Fᵟ+','a only','The correct formation of hydrogen bond is a) ᵟ+H-Fᵟ-... Hᵟ+- Fᵟ-\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 138; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a only','b only','none of these','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50962,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true.\n\na) Although molecules come very close to each other and intermolecular forces operate to the maximum, gases do not liquify on compression only.\n\nb) Intermolecular forces discussed are attractive forces.','both a and b','Intermolecular forces discussed so far are all attractive.\n\nGases do not liquify on compression only, although molecules come very close to each other and intermolecular forces operate to the maximum.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 139; Paragraph No. 2 and 5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50963,'chemistry','_________ is the simplest state of matter.','gas','The simplest state of matter is the gaseous state.\n\nThe gaseous state is the easiest to study. Any change in temperature results in evident changes in other quantities like volume and pressure. \n\nThe properties can be explained easily on the basis of the Kinetic theory of gases. Therefore these facts make gases the easiest state to study.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 139; Paragraph No. 6)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','solid','liquid','gas','vapour','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50964,'chemistry','A look at the periodic table shows that only _________  elements exist as gases under normal conditions.','eleven','A look at the periodic table shows that only eleven elements exist as gases under normal conditions\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 139; Paragraph No. 7)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','ten','eleven','twelve','thirteen','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50965,'chemistry','Which of these statements are correct?\n\na) Gases are highly incompressible.\n\nb) Gases have much higher density than the solids and liquids.','none of these','Gases are highly compressible.\n\nGases have much lower density than the solids and liquids.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 140; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50966,'chemistry','Which of these statements are correct?\n\na) The volume and the shape of gases are not fixed. They assume volume and shape of the container.\n\nb) Gases mix evenly and completely in all proportions without any mechanical aid.','both a and b','The volume and the shape of gases are not fixed. They assume volume and shape of the container.\n\nGases mix evenly and completely in all proportions without any mechanical aid.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 140; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50968,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true.\n\na) Interdependence of the variables (temperature, volume and pressure) leads to the formulation of gas laws.\n\nb) Gases exert pressure equally in all directions.','both a and b','Interdependence of the variables (temperature, volume and pressure) leads to the formulation of gas laws.\n\nGases exert pressure equally in all directions.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 140; Paragraph No. 2 and 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50969,'chemistry','Which of these statements are incorrect?\n\n1) A constant temperature, pressure is inversely proportional to the density of a fixed mass of the gas.\n\n2) The Kelvin temperature scale is also referred to as the Absolute temperature scale.','a only','A constant temperature, pressure is directly proportional to the density of a fixed mass of the gas.\n\nThe Kelvin temperature scale is also referred to as the Absolute temperature scale.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 142; Paragraph No. 1 and 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50970,'chemistry','Investigations of two scientists named _______ and _______ showed that for a fixed mass of a gas at constant pressure, volume of a gas increases on increasing temperature and decreases on cooling.','Charles and Gay Lussac','Charles and Gay Lussac performed several experiments on gases independently to improve upon hot air balloon technology.\n\nTheir investigations showed that for a fixed mass of a gas at constant pressure, volume of a gas increases on increasing temperature and decreases on cooling.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 142; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Charles and Gay Lussac','Charles and Boyle','Gay Lussac and Boyle','Gay Lussac and Charles','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50972,'chemistry','Number of moles(n) of a gas can be calculated as _________ .    \nWhere m = mass of the gas under investigation and M = molar mass','n = m/M','Number of moles of a gas can be calculated as n = m/M\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 144; Paragraph No. 5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','n = m/M','n = M/n','n = n/M','n = m/n','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50973,'chemistry','At STP conditions used, (0°C and 1 atm pressure), value of R is ____________.','8.20578 × 10–² L atm K–¹ mol–¹','At STP conditions used earlier (0 °C and 1 atm pressure), value of R is 8.20578 × 10–2 L atm K–1 mol–1.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 145; Paragraph No. 6)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','8.20578 × 10–² L atm K–¹ mol–¹','82.0578 × 10–² L atm K–¹ mol–¹','820.578 × 10–² L atm K–¹ mol–¹','0.820578 × 10–² L atm K–¹ mol–¹','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50975,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true?\n\na) At any given temperature, nitrogen molecules have higher value of most probable speed than the chlorine molecules.\n \nb) Speed distribution also depends upon mass of molecules.','both a and b','At any given temperature, nitrogen molecules have higher value of most probable speed than the chlorine molecules. \n\nSpeed distribution also depends upon mass of molecules.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 148; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50976,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason. \n\nAssertion: Conducting careful experiments is an important aspect of scientific method. \n\nReason: It tells us how the particular system is behaving under different conditions.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Conducting careful experiments is an important aspect of scientific method and it tells us how the particular system is behaving under different conditions.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 149; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50977,'chemistry','In the curves for dihydrogen and helium, as the pressure increases the value of pV __________','increases','In the curves for dihydrogen and helium, as the pressure increases the value of pV increases.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 150; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','decreases','increases','remains constant','is negligible','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50978,'chemistry','On heating, glass melts and the surface of the liquid tends to take the rounded shape at the edges, which makes the edges smooth. \nThis is called ___________','fire polishing of glass','On heating, the glass melts and the surface of the liquid tends to take the rounded shape at the edges, which makes the edges smooth. \n\nThis is called fire polishing of glass.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 156; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','fire polishing of glass','fire modelling of glass','fire profiling of glass','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50979,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true.\n\na) Viscosity is one of the characteristic properties of liquids.\n\nb) The molecules on the surface of a liquid experience a net downward force and have more energy than the molecules in the bulk, which do not experience any net force.','both a and b','Viscosity is one of the characteristic properties of liquids.  \n\nThe molecules on the surface of a liquid  experience a net downward force and have more energy than the molecules in the bulk, which do not experience any net force.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 155; Paragraph No. 4 and Page No. 156; Paragraph No. 7)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50980,'chemistry','For the expression of force in velocity gradation, F ∝ A du/dz \ndu/dz stands for','velocity gradient','For the expression of force in velocity gradation, F ∝ A du/dz  du/dz stands for\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 156; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','coefficient of viscosity','velocity gradient','resistance to flow','area of contact','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50981,'chemistry','For the expression of force in velocity gradation, F ∝ A du/dz \nA is _____________','area of contact','"For the expression of force in velocity gradation, F ∝ A du/dz \nA is area of contact\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 156; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','area of contact','velocity gradient','resistance to flow','coefficient of viscosity','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50982,'chemistry','For the expression of force in velocity gradation, F = 𝝶A du/dz, 𝝶 is a constant of proportionality called','coefficient of viscosity','For the expression of force in velocity gradation,, F = 𝝶A du/dz, 𝝶 is a constant of proportionality called coefficient of viscosity\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 156; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','area of contact','velocity gradient','resistance to flow','coefficient of viscosity','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50983,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true.\n\na) During boiling, at first a clear boundary is visible between liquid and vapour phase because liquid is more dense than vapour.\n\nb) As the temperature increases however, more and more molecules go to vapour phase and density of vapours rises.','both a and b','During boiling, at first a clear boundary is visible between liquid and vapour phase because liquid is more dense than vapour. \n\nAs the temperature increases however, more and more molecules go to vapour phase and density of vapours rises.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 156; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50984,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true.\n\na) Liquid gets the shape of the container it occupies.\n\nb) Small drops of mercury form spherical bead instead of spreading on the surface due to a phenomena called surface tension.','both a and b','Liquid assume the shape of the container it occupies.\n\nSmall drops of mercury form spherical bead instead of spreading on the surface due to a phenomena called surface tension.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 155; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50985,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true.\n\na) Soil at the bottom of river remain separated but they stick together when taken out as a result of a phenomenon called surface tension.\n\nb) Surface tension decreases as  temperature decreases.','a only','Soil at the bottom of river remain separated but they stick together when taken out as a result of a phenomenon called surface tension.\n\nSurface tension decreases as  temperature decreases.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 156; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50986,'chemistry','For the molecules on the surface of liquid, net attractive force is towards the interior of the liquid due to ________','the molecules below it','For the molecules on the surface of liquid, net attractive force is towards the interior of the liquid due to the molecules below it.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 155; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','the molecules below it','the molecules above it','the molecules in the atmosphere','the molecules in space','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50987,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason. \n\nAssertion: Liquids at high altitudes boil at lower temperatures in comparison to that at sea level.\n \nReason:  At high altitudes atmospheric pressure is low.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Liquids at high altitudes boil at lower temperatures in comparison to that at sea level.\n  \nTherefore, at high altitudes atmospheric pressure is low.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 155; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50988,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true.\n\na) Molecules in liquids are so close that there is very little empty space between them and under normal conditions liquids are denser than gases.\n\nb) Molecules of liquids are held together by attractive intermolecular forces.','both a and b','Molecules in liquids are so close that there is very little empty space between them and under normal conditions liquids are denser than gases.\nMolecules of liquids are held together by attractive intermolecular forces.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50989,'chemistry','Which of these statements are incorrect?\n\na) A gas below the critical temperature can be liquified by applying pressure, and is called vapour of the substance.\n \nb) Carbon dioxide gas below its critical temperature is called carbon dioxide vapour.','none of these','A gas below the critical temperature can be liquified by applying pressure, and is called vapour of the substance. \n\nCarbon dioxide gas below its critical temperature is called carbon dioxide vapour.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51101,'chemistry','A person living in Shimla observed that cooking food without using pressure cooker takes more time. The reason for this observation is that at high altitude\n \n a)pressure and temperature increases\n \n b)pressure and temperture decreases\n \n c)only temperature increases\n \n d)only pressure decreases\n \n e)volume is constant','only d','Pressure at the top of a mountain [or at high altitude] is low. This suggests that boiling takes place at lower temperature, due to which things take more time to boil. However, in a pressure cooker, pressure is increased and hence, boiling point increases. Thus, things comes to boil in a pressure cooker in a less period of time.\n \n (Reference NCERT chemistry class 11 page 155 paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a and e','b and e','c and e','only d','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51134,'chemistry','Name two phenomena that can be explained on the basis of surface tension\n \n a) Rise or fall of the liquid in a capillary-(capillary action). \n \n b) Spherical shape of small liquid drops.\n \n c)liquids tend to have minimum number of molecules at their surface.','a,b','Two phenomena that can be explained on the basis of surface tension are as following\n \n (i) Rise or fall of the liquid in a capillary-(capillary action). \n \n (ii) Spherical shape of small liquid drops. \n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 155 paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','a,b','b,c','all of them','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51135,'chemistry','For real gases the relation between p V, and T is given by van der Waals’ equation\n (p+an²/V²)(V-nb)=nRT . For the gases: CH₄,O₂,H₂ , Which of the following is correct for the value of \'a\'\n \n a) CH₄>O₂\n \n b)O₂>H₂\n \n c)O₂<H₂\n \n d)CH₄>O₂','a,b','Van der Waals’ constant ‘ a ’ is the measure of magnitude of intermolecular attraction. The magnitude of intermolecular attractions increases with increase in size of electron cloud in a molecule. Hence, for the given gases magnitude of ‘ a ’ decreases in the following order CH₄>O₂>H₂\n \n Greater the size of electron cloud, greater is the polarisability of the molecule and greater is the dispersion forces or London forces\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 151 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','a,c','b,d','none of them are true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51136,'chemistry','Viscosity of a liquid arises due to strong intermolecular forces existing between the molecules. Stronger the intermolecular forces, greater is the viscosity. Name the intermolecular forces existing in the following liquids: water,hexane,glycerin:\n \n a)hydrogen bond\n \n b)dipole dipole \n \n c)London forces\n \n d)all of them','all of them','In water and glycerine - Hydrogen bonding and dipole-dipole interactions exists as both are polar molecules,hexane - Dispersion forces/London forces are present due ts non-polar nature.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 137 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','a,c','b,d','all of them','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51137,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement t of increasing the temperature of a liquid effect, on  intermolecular forces operating between its particles:\n \n a)As the temperature of a liquid increases, kinetic energy of the molecules increases \n \n b)the liquid can flow more easily\n \n c)kinetic energy of the molecules can overcome intermolecular forces.','all of them are correct','As the temperature of a liquid increases, kinetic energy of the molecules increases which can overcome intermolecular forces. So, the liquid can flow more easily, this results in decrease in viscosity of the liquid.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 154 paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','a,c','b,d','all of them are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51138,'chemistry','In the following question a statement of Assertion (A) followed by a statement of Reason (R) is given. Choose the correct option out of the choices given below in each\n \n \n Assertion (A) Three states of matter are the result of balance between intermolecular forces and thermal energy of the molecules.\n \n Reason (R) Intermolecular forces tend to keep the molecules together but thermal energy of molecules tends to keep them apart.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion. Intermolecular forces and thermal energy decide the state of matter because the former kept the molecules together but later keep them apart\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 137 paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51139,'chemistry','In the following question a statement of Assertion (A) followed by a statement of Reason (R) is given. Choose the correct option out of the choices given below in each\n \n \n Assertion (A) At constant temperature, pV vs V plot for real gases is not a straight line.\n \n Reason (R) At high pressure all gases have Z > 1 but at intermediate pressure most gases have Z < 1.','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion. At constant temperature, pV vs V for real gases is not a straight line because real gases have intermolecular forces of attraction.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 153 paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51140,'chemistry','In the following question a statement of Assertion (A) followed by a statement of Reason (R) is given. Choose the correct option out of the choices given below in each\n \n \n Assertion (A) The temperature at which vapour pressure of a liquid is equal to the external pressure is called boiling temperature.\n \n Reason (R) At high altitude atmospheric pressure is high.','A is true but R is false','Assertion is true but reason is false. At high altitude, atmospheric pressure is low.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 155 paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51141,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Gases do not liquefy above their critical temperature, even on applying high pressure. \n \n Reason (R) Above critical temperature, the molecular speed is high and intermolecular attractions cannot hold the molecules together because they escape because of high speed','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion. Gases do not liquefy above their critical temperature, even an applying high pressure because above critical temperature, the molecular speed is high and intermolecular attractions cannot hold the molecules together.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 148 paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51142,'chemistry','In the following question a statement of Assertion (A) followed by a statement of Reason (R) is given. Choose the correct option out of the choices given below in each\n \n \n Assertion (A) At critical temperature liquid passes into gaseous state imperceptibly and continuously.\n \n Reason (R) The density of liquid and gaseous phase is equal to critical temperature.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion. At critical temperature, density of liquid and gaseous phase becomes equal. Due to which liquid passes into gaseous state imperceptibly and continuously\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 153 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51143,'chemistry','In the following question a statement of Assertion (A) followed by a statement of Reason (R) is given. Choose the correct option out of the choices given below in each\n \n Assertion (A) Liquids tend to have maximum number of molecules at their surface. \n \n Reason (R) Small liquid drops have spherical shape.','A is false but R is true.','Assertion is false but reason is true. Liquid tend to reduce number of molecules or surface tension at there surface, that’s why small liquid drops have spherical shape.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 155 paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51401,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about London dispersion forces is/are correct?\n \n a. London dispersion forces are the weakest intermolecular forces. \n \n b. London dispersion forces occur in all molecules and atoms. \n \n c. London dispersion forces increase with increasing atomic or molecular size.','b,c','London dispersion forces are the weakest intermolecular forces, but they occur in all molecules and atoms. These forces arise due to the temporary fluctuations in electron distribution around atoms and molecules, which create temporary dipoles. Larger atoms and molecules have more electrons and thus exhibit stronger London dispersion forces.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 137 paragraph 5,6)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','all of them','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51402,'chemistry','Which of the following substances exhibit hydrogen bonding? \n \n a. Water \n \n b. Methane \n \n c. Ethanol \n \n d. Carbon dioxide','a,c','Hydrogen bonding occurs when a hydrogen atom is covalently bonded to an electronegative atom, such as oxygen, nitrogen, or fluorine, and is attracted to the lone pair of electrons on another electronegative atom in a nearby molecule. Water and ethanol both have OH functional groups that can participate in hydrogen bonding.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 139 paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','all of them','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51403,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about dipole-dipole interactions is/are correct? \n \n a. Dipole-dipole interactions occur between polar molecules. \n \n b. Dipole-dipole interactions are weaker than London dispersion forces. \n \n c. Dipole-dipole interactions are stronger in substances with larger dipole moments.','a,c','Dipole-dipole interactions occur between polar molecules that have permanent dipoles. These interactions are stronger in substances with larger dipole moments, which arise from differences in electronegativity between atoms in the molecule. Dipole-dipole interactions are typically stronger than London dispersion forces.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 138 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','all of them','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51404,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about intermolecular forces and boiling points is/are correct? \n \n a. The stronger the intermolecular forces, the higher the boiling point. \n \n b. The boiling point of a substance is dependent only on its molar mass. \n \n c. A substance with hydrogen bonding will have a higher boiling point than a substance with only London dispersion forces.','a,c','The stronger the intermolecular forces, the higher the boiling point, because more energy is required to overcome those forces and vaporize the substance. Boiling point is not dependent only on molar mass, as intermolecular forces play a critical role. A substance with hydrogen bonding will have a higher boiling point than a substance with only London dispersion forces because hydrogen bonding is a stronger intermolecular force.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 137 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','all of them','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51405,'chemistry','Which of the following substances exhibit dipole-induced dipole interactions?\n \n a. Methane \n \n b. Water \n \n c. Carbon dioxide \n \n d. Nitrogen','b,c','Dipole-induced dipole interactions occur when a polar molecule induces a dipole in a nonpolar molecule. Water and carbon dioxide are polar molecules that can induce dipoles in nonpolar molecules, such as methane and nitrogen.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 138 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','all of them','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51406,'chemistry','Which of the following substances exhibit ion-dipole interactions? \n \n a. Sodium chloride dissolved in water \n \n b. Methane \n \n c. Carbon dioxide \n \n d. Nitrogen','only a','Ion-dipole interactions occur between an ion and a polar molecule. Sodium chloride dissolved in water is an example of a substance that exhibits ion-dipole interactions.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 137 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only a','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51406q.png'),
(2000032031,'physics','Calculate the focal length of a reading glass of a person  if  his distance of distinct  vision is $75\\,cm$.','A','The distance of normal vision is $25\\,cm$. If a book to be read is at $u =  - 25\\,cm$, its image should be formed at $v =  - 75\\,cm$. Therefore, the required focal length ($f$) is given by,<br>$\\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{{ - 75\\,cm}} - \\frac{1}{{ - 25\\,cm}} = \\frac{2}{{75\\,cm}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow f = \\frac{{75\\,cm}}{2} = 37.5{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{cm}}$',' Optical Instruments','Ray Optics And Optical Instruments','Medium',NULL,'$37.5\\,cm$','$100.4\\,cm$','$25.6\\,cm$','$75.2\\,cm$',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51407,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about van der Waals forces is/are correct?\n \n a. Van der Waals forces are the sum of all intermolecular forces.\n \n b. Van der Waals forces are stronger in substances with larger surface areas.\n \n c. Van der Waals forces include London dispersion forces','only c','Attractive intermolecular forces are known as van der Waals forces, in honour of Dutch scientist Johannes van der Waals (1837- 1923), who explained the deviation of real gases from the ideal behaviour through these forces. We will learn about this later in this unit. van der Waals forces vary considerably in magnitude and include dispersion forces or London forces, dipole-dipole forces, and dipole-induced dipole forces. A particularly strong type of dipole-dipole interaction is hydrogen bonding.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 137 paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only c','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51408,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about thermal energy are correct? \n \n a. Thermal energy is the energy associated with the motion of atoms and molecules. \n \n b. Thermal energy increases with increasing temperature. \n \n c. Thermal energy is the same for all substances at the same temperature.','a,b','Thermal energy is the energy associated with the motion of atoms and molecules. It increases with increasing temperature because higher temperatures correspond to faster molecular motion. The amount of thermal energy a substance has depends on its mass, temperature, and specific heat capacity, among other factors.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 139 paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only c','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51409,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about the relationship between intermolecular forces and melting point is/are correct? \n \n a. The stronger the intermolecular forces, the higher the melting point. \n \n b. Melting point is the temperature at which a substance changes from a solid to a liquid. \n \n c. Substances with only London dispersion forces will have higher melting points than substances with hydrogen bonding.','a,b','The stronger the intermolecular forces, the higher the melting point, because more energy is required to break those forces and convert the substance from a solid to a liquid. Melting point is the temperature at which a substance changes from a solid to a liquid. Substances with hydrogen bonding will have higher melting points than substances with only London dispersion forces, because hydrogen bonding is a stronger intermolecular force.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 139 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only c','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51410,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about Boyle\'s law is/are correct? \n \n a. Boyle\'s law states that at constant temperature, the pressure of a gas is inversely proportional to its volume. \n \n b. Boyle\'s law applies to all gases, regardless of their composition or intermolecular forces. \n \n c. Boyle\'s law is expressed mathematically as P1V1 = P2V2.','a,c','Boyle\'s law states that at constant temperature, the pressure of a gas is inversely proportional to its volume. It applies to all gases, regardless of their composition or intermolecular forces. Boyle\'s law is expressed mathematically as P1V1 = P2V2, where P1 and V1 are the initial pressure and volume of the gas, and P2 and V2 are the final pressure and volume of the gas.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 140 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only c','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51411,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about Charles\'s law is/are correct? \n \n a. Charles\'s law states that at constant pressure, the volume of a gas is directly proportional to its temperature. \n \n b. Charles\'s law applies to all gases, regardless of their composition or intermolecular forces. \n \n c. Charles\'s law is expressed mathematically as V1/T1 = V2/T2.','a,c','Charles\'s law states that at constant pressure, the volume of a gas is directly proportional to its temperature. It applies to all gases, regardless of their composition or intermolecular forces. Charles\'s law is expressed mathematically as V1/T1 = V2/T2, where V1 and T1 are the initial volume and temperature of the gas, and V2 and T2 are the final volume and temperature of the gas.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 142 paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only c','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51412,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about Avogadro\'s law is/are correct? \n \n a. Avogadro\'s law states that equal volumes of gases at the same temperature and pressure contain equal numbers of molecules.\n \n b. Avogadro\'s law applies to all gases, regardless of their composition or intermolecular forces. \n \n c. Avogadro\'s law is expressed mathematically as V1/n1 = V2/n2.','a,b','Avogadro\'s law states that equal volumes of gases at the same temperature and pressure contain equal numbers of molecules. It applies to all gases, regardless of their composition or intermolecular forces. Avogadro\'s law is expressed mathematically as V1/n1 = V2/n2, where V1 and n1 are the initial volume and number of molecules of the gas, and V2 and n2 are the final volume and number of molecules of the gas.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 144 paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only c','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51413,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about the kinetic-molecular theory is/are correct? \n \n a. The kinetic-molecular theory assumes that gas molecules are in constant random motion. \n \n b. The kinetic-molecular theory assumes that gas molecules have no volume and do not interact with each other. \n \n c. The kinetic-molecular theory explains the behavior of real gases at low temperatures and high pressures.','only a','The kinetic-molecular theory assumes that gas molecules are in constant random motion. It also assumes that gas molecules have volume and do interact with each other, though to a lesser degree than in liquids or solids. The kinetic-molecular theory is used to explain the behavior of real gases at a wide range of temperatures and pressures.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 149 paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only a','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51414,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about the average kinetic energy of gas molecules is/are correct? \n \n a. The average kinetic energy of gas molecules is directly proportional to the temperature of the gas. \n \n b. The average kinetic energy of gas molecules is inversely proportional to the molar mass of the gas. \n \n c. The average kinetic energy of gas molecules is independent of the pressure of the gas.','a,b','The average kinetic energy of gas molecules is directly proportional to the temperature of the gas, as stated by the kinetic theory of gases. The average kinetic energy of gas molecules is also inversely proportional to the molar mass of the gas, since lighter molecules have higher average speeds and kinetic energies. The average kinetic energy of gas molecules is not affected by the pressure of the gas, since pressure only affects the frequency and force of molecular collisions, not their kinetic energy.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 148 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only c','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL);
After losing two electron electronic configuration will be like this Mn²⁺(3d⁵)\nand this is most stable configuration due to half filled orbitals hence third ionization enthalpy will be highest in this case.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Vanadium (Z = 23)','Manganese (Z = 25)','Chromium (Z = 24)','Iron (Z = 26)','NEW','12-221','12-094',NULL),
(80614,'chemistry','The oxidation state for d-block elements differ each other by ___.','unity','The oxidation state for d-block elements differ each other by unity.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 223; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxidation states',NULL,'1','mcq','two','three','unity','four','NEW','12-223','12-098',NULL),
(80615,'chemistry','The variability of oxidation states of non transition elements  oxidation states normally differ by a unit of ____.','2','The variability of oxidation states of non transition elements where oxidation states normally differ by a unit of two.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 223; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxidation states',NULL,'1','mcq','2','3','4','1','NEW','12-223','12-098',NULL),
(80616,'chemistry','Assertion: In p-block, the lower oxidation states are favoured by the heavier members.\n\nReason: MoO₃ and WO₃ are more stable against oxidation as compared to Cr(VI).\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','An interesting feature in the variability of oxidation states of the d–block elements is noticed among the groups (groups 4 through 10). Although in the p–block the lower oxidation states are favoured by the heavier members (due to inert pair effect), the opposite is true in the groups of d-block. In group 6, Mo(VI) and W(VI) are found to be more stable than Cr(VI). Thus Cr(VI) in the form of dichromate in acidic medium is a strong oxidising agent, whereas WO₃ and MO₃ are not.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 223; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxidation states',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-223','12-098',NULL),
(80617,'chemistry','What is oxidation state of Ni in Ni(CO)₄?','0','Low oxidation states are found when a complex compound has ligands capable of p-acceptor character in addition to the s-bonding. For example, in Ni(CO)₄ and Fe(CO)₅, the oxidation state of nickel and iron is zero.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 223; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxidation states',NULL,'2','mcq','2+','4+','0','-2','NEW','12-223','12-098',NULL),
(80618,'chemistry','With which ligand lower oxidation state can be found in a complex for 3d metals?','CO','Low oxidation states are found when a complex compound has ligands capable of p-acceptor character in addition to the s-bonding. For example, in Ni(CO)₄ and Fe(CO)₅, the oxidation state of nickel and iron is zero.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 223; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxidation states',NULL,'2','mcq','Cl⁻','CO','NH₃','H₂O','NEW','12-223','12-098',NULL),
(81301,'chemistry','Assertion: The transition metals and their compounds are known for their catalytic activity.\n\nReason: Transition metals can adopt multiple oxidations states and form complexes.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','The transition metals and their compounds are known for their catalytic activity. This activity is ascribed to their ability to adopt multiple oxidation states and to form complexes.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 230; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Catalytic properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-230','12-104',NULL),
(81302,'chemistry','Which one of the following catalyst is used in Contact process?','Vanadium(V) oxide','Vanadium(V) oxide (in Contact Process), finely divided iron (in Haber’s Process), and nickel (in Catalytic Hydrogenation) are some of the examples.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 230; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Catalytic properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Finely divided iron','Vanadium(V) oxide','Nickel','TiCl₄','NEW','12-230','12-104',NULL),
(81303,'chemistry','Which one of the following catalyst is used in Catalytic hyrogenation?','Nickel','Vanadium(V) oxide (in Contact Process), finely divided iron (in Haber’s Process), and nickel (in Catalytic Hydrogenation) are some of the examples.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 230; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Catalytic properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Finely divided iron','Vanadium(V) oxide','Nickel','TiCl₄','NEW','12-230','12-104',NULL),
(81304,'chemistry','Which one of the following catalyst is used in Haber\'s process?','Finely divided iron','Vanadium(V) oxide (in Contact Process), finely divided iron (in Haber’s Process), and nickel (in Catalytic Hydrogenation) are some of the examples.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 230; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Catalytic properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Finely divided iron','Vanadium(V) oxide','Nickel','Charcoal','NEW','12-230','12-104',NULL),
(81305,'chemistry','Which orbitals are utilised by the first row transiton elements to bond with the catalyst at the solid surface?','4s and 3d','Catalysts at a solid surface involve the formation of bonds between reactant molecules and atoms of the surface of the catalyst (first row transition metals utilise 3d and 4s electrons for bonding).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 230; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Catalytic properties',NULL,'2','mcq','4s and 4d','3s and 4d','4s and 3d','4s and 4p','NEW','12-230','12-104',NULL),
(81306,'chemistry','Which of the following is statement is false?','Formation of bonds between catalyst and reactant decreases the concentration of reactants.','Catalysts at a solid surface involve the formation of bonds between reactant molecules and atoms of the surface of the catalyst (first row transition metals utilise 3d and 4s electrons for bonding). This has the effect of increasing the concentration of the reactants at the catalyst surface and also weakening of the bonds in the reacting molecules (the activation energy is lowering). Also because the transition metal ions can change their oxidation states, they become more effective as catalysts.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 230; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Catalytic properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Catalyst lowers the activation energy.','Formation of bonds between catalyst and reactant decreases the concentration of reactants.','Formation of bonds between catalyst and reactant weakens the bonds in the reactant.','As transition metals can change oxidation states, they can behave more effectively as catalysts','NEW','12-230','12-104',NULL),
(81307,'chemistry','The reaction between persulphate and iodide ions is catalysed by which metal ion?','Fe(III)','Fe(III) catalyses reaction between iodide and persuphate ions. \n2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂\n2Fe²⁺ + S₂O₈²⁻ → 2Fe³⁺ + 2SO₄²⁻\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 230; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Catalytic properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Fe(III)','Fe(II)','Zn(II)','Au(I)','NEW','12-230','12-104',NULL),
(81308,'chemistry','In the reaction between persulphate and iodide ions, persulphate is reduced to which sulphur species?','SO₄²⁻','Persulphate is reduced to SO₄²⁻, in the reaction between persulphate and iodide ions.\n2I⁻ + S₂O₈²⁻ → I₂ + SO₄²⁻\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 230; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Catalytic properties',NULL,'2','mcq','SO₃','SO₂','SO₄²⁻','S','NEW','12-230','12-104','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/81308q.png'),
(81401,'chemistry','______ compounds are those which are formed when small atoms like H, C or N are trapped inside the crystal lattices of metals.','Interstitial','Interstitial compounds are those which are formed when small atoms like H, C or N are trapped inside the crystal lattices of metals.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 230; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Interstitial Compounds and Alloy formation',NULL,'1','mcq','Ionic','Interstitial','Covalent','Molecular','NEW','12-230','12-104',NULL),
(81402,'chemistry','Choose the odd one with respect to interstitial compounds.','NiCl₂','Only NiCl₂ is not interstital compound while all the others are.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 230; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Interstitial Compounds and Alloy formation',NULL,'2','mcq','TiC','Mn₄N','NiCl₂','Fe₃H','NEW','12-230','12-104',NULL),
(81403,'chemistry','Which statements are incorrect for interstitial compounds?\nA. They are non-stoichiometric.\nB. The formulas correspond to normal oxidation state of the metal.\nC. They are neither typicaly covalent nor ionic.\nD. They are chemically reactive.','B and D','They are usually non stoichiometric and are neither typically ionic nor covalent,The formulas quoted do not, of course, correspond to any normal oxidation state of the metal. Because of the nature of their composition, these compounds are referred to as interstitial compounds.They are chemically inert. Therefore, statements B and D are incorrect.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 230; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Interstitial Compounds and Alloy formation',NULL,'2','mcq','A, B and D','B, C and D','B and C','B and D','NEW','12-230','12-104',NULL),
(81404,'chemistry','Choose the odd with repect to properties of interstitial compounds.','They do not retain metallic conductivity.','The principal physical and chemical characteristics of the interstitial compounds are as follows: \n(i) They have high melting points, higher than those of pure metals. \n(ii) They are very hard, some borides approach diamond in hardness. \n(iii) They retain metallic conductivity. \n(iv) They are chemically inert.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 230; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Interstitial Compounds and Alloy formation',NULL,'2','mcq','They have high melting points, higher than those of pure metals.','They do not retain metallic conductivity.','They are chemically inert.','They are very hard, some borides approach diamond in hardness.','NEW','12-230','12-104',NULL),
(81405,'chemistry','Alloys may be ______solid solutions in which the atoms of one metal are distributed ______ among the atoms of the other.','homogeneous, randomly','An alloy is a blend of metals prepared by mixing the components.Alloys may be homogeneous solid solutions in which the atoms of one metal are distributed randomly among the atoms of the other.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 230; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Interstitial Compounds and Alloy formation',NULL,'1','mcq','heterogeneous, randomly','homogeneous, systematically','heterogeneous, systematically','homogeneous, randomly','NEW','12-230','12-104',NULL),
(81406,'chemistry','Alloys are formed by atoms with metallic radii that are within about __ percent of each other.','15','Alloys are formed by atoms with metallic radii that are within about 15 percent of each other.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 230; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Interstitial Compounds and Alloy formation',NULL,'1','mcq','15','50','0.1','99','NEW','12-230','12-104',NULL),
(81407,'chemistry','Which metals constitute brass?','Cu and Zn','Alloys of transition metals with non transition metals such as brass (copper-zinc) and bronze (copper-tin), are also of considerable industrial importance.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 230; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Interstitial Compounds and Alloy formation',NULL,'1','mcq','Cu and Zn','Cu and Sn','Sn and Zn','Sn and Pb','NEW','12-230','12-104',NULL),
(81408,'chemistry','Which of the following elements is not used for production of variety of steels and stainless steel?','Ti','The best known are ferrous alloys: chromium, vanadium, tungsten, molybdenum and manganese are used for the production of a variety of steels and stainless steel.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 230; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Interstitial Compounds and Alloy formation',NULL,'1','mcq','Cr','Mo','W','Ti','NEW','12-230','12-104',NULL),
(81409,'chemistry','Mn(VI) disproportionates to give two different oxidation states of Mn in acidic medium. What are the two oxidation states?','VII and IV','When a particular oxidation state becomes less stable relative to other oxidation states, one lower, one higher, it is said to undergo disproportionation. For example, manganese (VI) becomes unstable relative to manganese(VII) and manganese (IV) in acidic solution.\n3MnⱽᴵO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺ → 2MnⱽᴵᴵO₄⁻ + MnᴵⱽO₂ + H₂O\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Example 8.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Interstitial Compounds and Alloy formation',NULL,'2','mcq','VII and V','V and III','VII and II','VII and IV','NEW','12-231','12-104',NULL),
(81501,'chemistry','Transition metals oxides are generally formed by the reaction of metals with ____ at ___ temperatures.','Oxygen, high','Transition metal oxides are generally formed by the reaction of metals with oxygen at high temperatures.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','Oxygen, high','Oxygen, low','Ozone, high','Ozone, low','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81502,'chemistry','Which of the following metal do not form MO type of oxide?','Sc','All the metals except scandium form MO oxides which are ionic.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Mn','Zn','Cu','Sc','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81503,'chemistry','The highest oxidation number in the oxides, coincides with the group number. This statement is not true for-','Cu','The highest oxidation number in the oxides, coincides with the group number and is attained in Sc₂O₃ to Mn₂O₇\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Mn','Sc','V','Cu','NEW','12-231','12-106','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/81503q.png'),
(81504,'chemistry','What is the highest oxidation state achieved by iron in its oxide form?','3+','Beyond group 7, no higher oxides of iron above Fe₂O₃ are known.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','2+','4+','6+','3+','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81505,'chemistry','Which of the following is false for Mn₂O₇?','In this oxide, basic character is predominant.','As the oxidation number of a metal increases, ionic character decreases. In the case of Mn, Mn₂O₇ is a covalent green oil.In these higher oxides, the acidic character is predominant.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','It is a covalent compound.','It is green oil.','It has Mn-O-Mn bridge present in its structure.','In this oxide, basic character is predominant.','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81506,'chemistry','Which is true about melting point of CrO₃ and V₂O₅?','It is low for both','Even CrO₃ and V₂O₅ have low melting points.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','It is high for both','It is low for both','It is same for both','Both donot melt','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81507,'chemistry','Assertion: Higher oxides have acidic character predominant.\n\nReason: On increasing oxidation number, ionic character of compound increases.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','As the oxidation number of a metal increases, ionic character decreases. In the case of Mn, Mn₂O₇ is a covalent green oil.In these higher oxides, the acidic character is predominant.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81508,'chemistry','The chromium oxide CrO₃  gives which type of acids?\nA. H₂CrO₄\nB. HCrO₂ \nC. H₂Cr₂O₇','Both A and C','The chromium oxide CrO₃  gives  H₂CrO₄ and H₂Cr₂O₇ type of acids.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and B','Both B and C','Both A and C','Only B','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81509,'chemistry','Which option describes the nature of V₂O₅ correctly?','Amphoteric though mainly acidic','The nature of V₂O₅ is Amphoteric though mainly acidic.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Amphoteric though mainly basic','Basic','Amphoteric though mainly acidic','Neutral','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81510,'chemistry','What is the nature of CrO?','Basic','The nature of CrO is Basic\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Acidic','Neutral','Amphoteric','Basic','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81511,'chemistry','What is the nature of oxide Cr₂O₃?','Amphoteric','The nature of oxide Cr₂O₃ is Amphoteric \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Acidic','Neutral','Amphoteric','Basic','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81512,'chemistry','Assertion: V₂O₄ is more basic than V₂O₃.\n\nReason: Higher oxides have basic character predominant.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are false.','In vanadium there is gradual change from the basic V₂O₃ to less basic V₂O₄  and to amphoteric V₂O₅.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81513,'chemistry','Which oxide among the following gives two type of salt?','V₂O₅','V₂O₅ gives two type of salts - VO₄³⁻⁺ and VO₂⁺\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','V₂O₃','V₂O₅','CrO','Mn₂O₇','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81514,'chemistry','V₂O₅ reacts with alkalies to give which type of ion?','VO₄³⁻','V₂O₅ reacts with alkalies to give VO₄³⁻ and with acids to give VO₄⁺\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','VO₂⁺','VO₃⁴⁻','VO₄⁻','VO₄³⁻','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81515,'chemistry','Which among the following is not the use of potassium dichromate?','Bleaching of wool,','Potassium dichromate is a very important chemical used in leather industry and as an oxidant for preparation of many azo compounds.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','Used in leather industry.','Oxidant for preparation of azo-cpmpounds.','Used in analytical chemistry.','Bleaching of wool,','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81516,'chemistry','What is the correct formula of chromite ore?','FeCrO₄','FeCrO₄ is the correct formula of chromite ore\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','Fe₂CrO','FeCrO₄','FeCrO₂','FeCr₂O₇','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81517,'chemistry','Which of the following is incorrect for preparation dichromates?','Chromate are prepared by fusion of chromite ore with sodium carbonate in absence of air.','Dichromates are generally prepared from chromate, which in turn are obtained by the fusion of chromite ore (FeCr₂O₄) with sodium or potassium carbonate in free access of air.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Dichromates are generally prepared from chromate.','Chromate are prepared by fusion of chromite ore with sodium carbonate in absence of air.','Carbon dioxide is obtained as by product,','The yellow solution of sodium chromate is filtered and acidified with sulphuric acid','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81518,'chemistry','Fusion of chromite ore with sodium carbonate gives three products A, B and C.B is oxide of iron. What is the oxidation state of Fe in B?','3+','Dichromates are generally prepared from chromate, which in turn are obtained by the fusion of chromite ore (FeCr₂O₄) with sodium or potassium carbonate in free access of air. The reaction with sodium carbonate occurs as follows:\n4FeCr₂O₄ + 8Na₂CO₃ + 7O₂ → 8Na₂CrO₄ + 2Fe₂O₃ + 8CO₂\nB is Fe₂O₃. Oxidation state of Fe in Fe₂O₃ is +3\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','3+','2+','4+','6+','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81519,'chemistry','Fusion of chromite ore with sodium carbonate gives three products A, B and C. A is yellow colored. What is A?','Sodium chromate','Dichromates are generally prepared from chromate, which in turn are obtained by the fusion of chromite ore (FeCr₂O₄) with sodium or potassium carbonate in free access of air. The reaction with sodium carbonate occurs as follows:\n4FeCr₂O₄ + 8Na₂CO₃ + 7O₂ → 8Na₂CrO₄ + 2Fe₂O₃ + 8CO₂\nA is Na₂CrO₄. The colour of Na₂CrO₄ is yellow.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Sodium chromate','Iron chromate','Chromium carbonate','Sodium oxide','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81520,'chemistry','What is the color of sodium dichromate crystals?','Orange','The color of sodium dichromate crystals Is Orange.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','Yellow','Blue','Orange','Green','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL);
