(80348,'biology','Cellwall of fungi is made up of','Chitin and polysaccharides','Cellwall of fungi is made up of chitin and polysaccharides. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 22; Paragraph No. 5)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Cell Membrane And Cell Wall',NULL,'2','mcq','Pellicle','Chitin and polysaccharides','Flagellin','Calcium','NEW','11-022','11-022',NULL),
(80401,'chemistry','In general, ions of the same charge in a given series show progressive _____ in radius with ________ atomic number.','decrease, increasing','In general, ions of the same charge in a given series show progressive decrease in radius with increasing atomic number.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 219; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','increase, increasing','decrease, increasing','increase, decreasing','decrease, decreasing','NEW','12-219','12-093',NULL),
(80402,'chemistry','Assertion: In general, ions of the same charge in a given series show progressive decrease in radius with increasing atomic number.\n\nReason: The new electron enters a d-orbital each time the nuclear charge increases by unity.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','In general, ions of the same charge in a given series show progressive decrease in radius with increasing atomic number. This is because the new electron enters a d-orbital each time the nuclear charge increases by unity. It may be recalled that the shielding effect of a d-electron is not that effective, hence the net electrostatic attraction between the nuclear charge and the outermost electron increases and the ionic radius decreases.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 219; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-219','12-093',NULL),
(80403,'chemistry','Which series have virtually similar atomic size as those of the corresponding elements in other series.?','4d and 5d','An interesting point emerges when atomic sizes of one series are compared with those of the corresponding elements in the other series. The curves in Figure show an increase from the first (3d) to the second (4d) series of the elements but the radii of the third (5d) series are virtually the same as those of the corresponding members of the second series. The filling of 4f before 5d orbital results in a regular decrease in atomic radii called Lanthanoid contraction which essentially compensates for the expected increase in atomic size with increasing atomic number. The net result of the lanthanoid contraction is that the second and the third d-series exhibit similar radii \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 219; Paragraph No. 3)\ndiagram(ne0803)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','3d and 4d','3d and 5d','4d and 5d','5d and 6f','NEW','12-219','12-093',NULL),
(80404,'chemistry','Assertion: Zr and Hf have similar radius and similar physical and chemical properties.\n\nReason: There is filling of 4f before 5d orbital results in a regular decrease in atomic radii.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','The filling of 4f before 5d orbital results in a regular decrease in atomic radii called Lanthanoid contraction which essentially compensates for the expected increase in atomic size with increasing atomic number. The net result of the lanthanoid contraction is that the second and the third d-series exhibit similar radii (e.g., Zr 160 pm, Hf 159 pm) and have very similar physical and chemical properties.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 219; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-219','12-093',NULL),
(80405,'chemistry','Which among the following is expected to have similar atomic radius?','Pt and Pd','Pt and Pd are expected to have similar atomic radius. The filling of 4f before 5d orbital results in a regular decrease in atomic radii called Lanthanoid contraction which essentially compensates for the expected increase in atomic size with increasing atomic number. The net result of the lanthanoid contraction is that the second and the third d-series exhibit similar radii \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 220; Figure 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Hf and Os','Nb and V','Fe and Ru','Pt and Pd','NEW','12-220','12-093',NULL),
(80406,'chemistry','Which among the following is expected to have similar atomic radius?','Mo and W','Mo and W are expected to have similar atomic radii. The filling of 4f before 5d orbital results in a regular decrease in atomic radii called Lanthanoid contraction which essentially compensates for the expected increase in atomic size with increasing atomic number. The net result of the lanthanoid contraction is that the second and the third d series exhibit similar radii \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 220; Figure 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Cr and Mo','Co and Rh','Ni and Re','Mo and W','NEW','12-220','12-093',NULL),
(80407,'chemistry','Which among the following do not have similar atomic sizes?','Y and La','Y and La donot have similar atomic radii. This is because Y and La elements occur before the elements of f-block. Lanthanoid contraction is the result of impperfect shielding of f-orbitals. Hence these elements follow regular trend of atomic radii i.e La>Y\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 220; Figure 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Hf and Zr','Mo and W','Ru and Os','Y and La','NEW','12-220','12-093',NULL),
(80408,'chemistry','Which element for 4d has largest atomic radius?','Y','Y has the largest atomic radius in 4d series.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 220; Figure 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','Sc','Y','La','Cd','NEW','12-220','12-093',NULL),
(80409,'chemistry','Assertion: From Ti to Cu, significant increase in density may be noted.\n\nReason: The decrease in metallic radius coupled with increase in atomic mass results in the general increase in the density of these elements.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','The decrease in metallic radius coupled with increase in atomic mass results in a general increase in the density of these elements. Thus, from titanium (Z = 22) to copper (Z = 29) the significant increase in the density may be noted.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 220; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-220','12-093',NULL),
(80410,'chemistry','Which among the following is incorrect?','Shielding of one 4f electron by another is more than that of one d electron by another.','The factor responsible for the lanthanoid contraction is somewhat similar to that observed in an ordinary transition series and is attributed to similar cause, i.e., the imperfect shielding of one electron by another in the same set of orbitals. However, the shielding of one 4f electron by another is less than that of one d electron by another, and as the nuclear charge increases along the series, there is fairly regular decrease in the size of the entire 4fⁿ orbitals.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 220; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Factor responsible for lanthanoid contraction is imperfect shielding of one f electron by another.','Shielding of one 4f electron by another is more than that of one d electron by another.','As nuclear charge increases, there is fairly regular decrease in the size of the entire 4fⁿ orbitals.','All of these','NEW','12-220','12-093',NULL),
