(80501,'chemistry','There is an ____ in ionisation enthalpy along each series of the transition elements from ________.','increase, left to right','There is an increase in ionisation enthalpy along each series of the transition elements from left to right due to an increase in nuclear charge which accompanies the filling of the inner d orbitals.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 221: Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ionisation Enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','increase, left to right','decrease, left to right','increase, top to bottom','decrease, top to bottom','NEW','12-221','12-095',NULL),
(80502,'chemistry','The variation in ionisation enthalpy along a series of ________ elements is much ______ in comparison to the variation along a period of _______ elements.','transition, less, non-transition.','The variation in ionisation enthalpy along a series of transition elements is much less in comparison to the variation along a period of non-transition elements.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 221: Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ionisation Enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','transition, more, non-transition.','transition, less, non-transition.','transition, more, inner-transition.','inner-transition, less, transition.','NEW','12-221','12-095',NULL),
(80503,'chemistry','How many of the following statements are true?\nA. The first ionisation enthalpy, in general, increases, but the magnitude of the increase in the second and third ionisation enthalpies for the successive elements, is much higher along a series.\nB. The irregular trend in the first ionisation enthalpy of the metals of 3d series can be accounted for by considering that the removal of one electron alters the relative energies of 4s and 3d orbitals.\nC. 3d electrons shield the 4s electrons from the increasing nuclear charge somewhat less effectively than the outer shell electrons can shield one another.\nD. Ionisation energy increases only slightly along 3d series.','3','The first ionisation enthalpy, in general, increases, but the magnitude of the increase in the second and third ionisation enthalpies for the successive elements, is much higher along a series. The irregular trend in the first ionisation enthalpy of the metals of 3d series, though of little chemical significance, can be accounted for by considering that the removal of one electron alters the relative energies of 4s and 3d orbitals. When d-block elements form ions, ns electrons are lost before (n – 1) d electrons. As we move along the period in 3d series, we see that nuclear charge increases from scandium to zinc but electrons are added to the orbital of inner subshell, i.e., 3d orbitals. These 3d electrons shield the 4s electrons from the increasing nuclear charge somewhat more effectively than the outer shell electrons can shield one another.\nTherefore, only statement C is incorrect.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 221: Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ionisation Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','2','1','3','4','NEW','12-221','12-095',NULL),
(80504,'chemistry','The trend of steady increase in second ionisation enthaly breaks for formation which ion?','Mn⁺²','The trend of steady increase in second ionisation enthalpy breaks for the formation of Mn⁺² . In this case, ion has d⁵ configuration. Similar breaks occur at corresponding elements in the later transition series.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 221: Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ionisation Enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','Mn⁺²','Cu⁺²','V⁺²','Ti⁺²','NEW','12-221','12-095',NULL),
(80505,'chemistry','The trend of steady increase in third ionisation enthalpy breaks for formation of which ion?','Fe⁺³','The trend of steady increase in second and third ionisation enthalpy breaks for the formation of Mn⁺² and Fe⁺³ respectively. In both the cases, ions have d⁵ configuration. Similar breaks occur at corresponding elements in the later transition series.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 221: Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ionisation Enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','Mn⁺³','Fe⁺³','Sc⁺³','Ti⁺³','NEW','12-221','12-095',NULL),
(80506,'chemistry','Which of the following term is not responsible for value of ionisation enthalpy?','Repulsion between the protons.','The three terms responsible for the value of ionisation enthalpy are attraction of each electron towards nucleus, repulsion between the electrons and the exchange energy.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 221: Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ionisation Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','Attraction of each electron towards nucleus','Repulsion between the electrons','Exchange energy','Repulsion between the protons.','NEW','12-221','12-095',NULL),
(80507,'chemistry','___ energy is approximately proportional to the total number of possible pairs of parallel spins in the degenerate orbitals.','Exchange','Exchange energy is responsible for the stabilisation of energy state. Exchange energy is approximately proportional to the total number of possible pairs of parallel spins in the degenerate orbitals.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 222: Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ionisation Enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','Ionisation','Exchange','Excitation','Atomisation','NEW','12-221','12-095',NULL),
(80508,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is not true for exchange energy?','The loss of exchange energy decreases the stability.','Exchange energy is responsible for the stabilisation of energy state. When several electrons occupy a set of degenerate orbitals, the lowest energy state corresponds to the maximum possible extent of single occupation of orbital and parallel spins (Hunds rule). The loss of exchange energy increases the stability. As the stability increases, the ionisation becomes more difficult. There is no loss of exchange energy at d⁶ configuration.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 222: Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ionisation Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','Exchange energy is responsible for the stabilisation of energy state.','The loss of exchange energy decreases the stability.','There is no loss of exchange energy at d⁶ configuration.','As the stability increases, the ionisation becomes more difficult.','NEW','12-222','12-096',NULL),
(80509,'chemistry','Assertion : Ionisation enthalpy of Cr⁺ is lower than that of Mn⁺.\n\nReason: Mn⁺ has d⁵ configuration while Cr+ has 3d⁵ 4s¹.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are false.','Mn⁺ has 3d⁵4s¹ configuration and configuration of Cr⁺ is d⁵. During ionisation of Mn⁺, the electron is removed from s-orbiital and for Cr⁺ electron is removed from d orbital. It is easier to remove electron from half-filled s-orbital than from half-filled d-orbital because exhange energy of former is less than that of latter. Therefore, ionisation enthalpy of Mn⁺ is lower than Cr⁺\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 222: Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ionisation Enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','NEW','12-222','12-096',NULL),
(80510,'chemistry','What is the lowest common oxidation state for the 3d metals?','2+','The lowest common oxidation state for the 3d metals is +2.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 222: Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ionisation Enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','1+','2+','4+','6+','NEW','12-222','12-096',NULL),
(80511,'chemistry','Which of the following terms are required to form M⁺² from gaseous atoms?','Sum of first and second ionisation enthalpy along with enthalpy of atomisation','To form the M⁺² ions from the gaseous atoms, the sum of the first and second ionisation enthalpy is required in addition to the enthalpy of atomisation.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 222: Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ionisation Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','Sum of first, second and third ionisation enthalpy','Sum of first and second ionisation enthalpy along with enthalpy of atomisation','Sum of first and second ionisation enthalpy only','Sum of all three ionisation enthalpy along with atomisation enthalpy','NEW','12-222','12-096',NULL),
(80512,'chemistry','For which two elements second ionisation is unsually high?','Cu and Cr','The dominant term is the second ionisation enthalpy which shows unusually high values for Cr and Cu where M⁺ ions have the d⁵ and d¹⁰ configurations respectively.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 222: Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ionisation Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','Cu and Cr','Cu and Zn','Fe and Co','Mn and Zn','NEW','12-222','12-096',NULL),
(80513,'chemistry','The trend in the ___ ionisation enthalpies is not complicated by the 4s orbital factor.','third','The trend in the third ionisation enthalpies is not complicated by the 4s orbital factor and shows the greater difficulty of removing an electron from the d⁵ and d¹⁰ configurations.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 222: Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ionisation Enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','first','second','third','fourth','NEW','12-222','12-096',NULL),
(80514,'chemistry','Assertion: Value of second ionisation enthalpy is correspondingly low for Zn.\n\nReason: Ionisation causes removal of electron from 4s orbital and leads to formation of stable d¹⁰ configuration.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','The value for 2nd IE for Zn is correspondingly low as the ionisation causes the removal of one 4s electron which results in the formation of stable d¹⁰ configuration.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 222: Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ionisation Enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-222','12-096',NULL),
(80515,'chemistry','Why it is difficult to obtain oxidation state greater than two for Zinc?','High third ionisaton value','In general, the third ionisation enthalpies are quite high. After removing 2 elecetrons Zn acquires d¹⁰ confiiguration. Fully filled d-orbital makes it difficult to remove electron thus 3rd IE for Zn is high\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 222: Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ionisation Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','High third ionisaton value','Poor hydration of Zn⁺² in water','DIsintegration of nucleus','Gain of exchange energy','NEW','12-222','12-096',NULL),
(80516,'chemistry','For the four successive transition elements (Cr, Mn, Fe and Co), the stability of +2 oxidation state will be there in which of the following order? At. No Cr= 24, Mn= 25, Fe = 26, Co= 27)','Mn>Fe>Cr>Co','This can be understood on the basis of E⁰ values for M²⁺/M.\n\nE⁰/V         Cr         Mn      Fe        Co\nM²⁺/M    – 0.90 – 1.18 – 0.44 – 0.28\nDue to the extra stability of the half-filled Mn²⁺ ion, the E⁰ value for the Mn is more negative than expected from the general trend. Due to this, the correct order is \nMn > Cr >  Fe > Co\nFurthermore, it has been seen from the E⁰ values for redox couple M³⁺/M²⁺ that the Cr²⁺ is a strong reducing agent (E⁰ M³⁺/M²⁺= 0.41 V) and liberates H2 when reacted with dilute acids.\n\n2Cr²⁺ (aq) + 2H⁺(aq) 2Cr³⁺(aq)+ H² (g)\nThe correct order is Mn> Fe> Cr> Co','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ionisation Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','Fe>Mn>Co>Cr','Co>Mn>Fe>Cr','Cr<Mn>Co>Fe','Mn>Fe>Cr>Co','NEW','12-222','12-096',NULL),
(80517,'biology','Which of the following is correct about the outer and inner membrane of chloroplast?','More permeable and less permeable respectively','Like mitochondria, the chloroplasts are also double membrane bound. Of the two, the inner chloroplast membrane is relatively less permeable. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:136, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >>  Cell Organelles',NULL,'2','mcq','Less permeable and more permeable respectively','More permeable and less permeable respectively','Both are equally permeable','Both are equally impermeable','NEW','11-136','11-098',NULL),
(31203,'chemistry','In OF₂ molecule, fluorine is given oxidation state - _____','-1','Each of the atoms of fluorine, with outer electronic configuration 2s²2p⁵, shares one electron with oxygen in the OF molecule. Being highest electronegative element, fluorine is given oxidation state –1.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 92; Paragraph no. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Oxidation states',NULL,'1','fillup','-1,-2,+2,0,+1',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-092','11-092',NULL),
(80518,'biology','Grana of the chloroplast are interconnected by -','Stromal lamellae','Like mitochondria, the chloroplasts are also double membrane bound. Of the two, the inner chloroplast membrane is relatively less permeable. The space limited by the inner membrane of the chloroplast is called the stroma. A number of organised flattened membranous sacs called the thylakoids, are present in the stroma. Thylakoids are arranged in stacks like the piles of coins called grana (singular: granum) or the intergranal thylakoids. In addition, there are flat membranous tubules called the stroma lamellae connecting the thylakoids of the different grana. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:136, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >>  Cell Organelles',NULL,'2','mcq','Granai thylakoids','Granai lamellae','Stromal lamellae','Strama','NEW','11-136','11-098',NULL),
(80519,'biology','Which of the following is not membraneless?','Nucleus','All are membraneless. Ribosomes are the granular structures first observed under the electron microscope as dense particles by George Palade (1953). They are composed of ribonucleic acid (RNA) and proteins and are not surrounded by any membrane. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:136, paragraph no:2)\nThe nucleoli are spherical structures present in the nucleoplasm. The content of nucleolus is continuous with the rest of the nucleoplasm as it is not a membrane bound structure. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:138, paragraph no:3)\nElectron microscopy has revealed that the nuclear envelope, which consists of two parallel membranes with a space between (10 to 50 nm) called the perinuclear space, forms a barrier between the materials present inside the nucleus and that of the cytoplasm. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:138, paragraph no:2) diagram (e0814)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >>  Cell Organelles',NULL,'1','mcq','Ribosomes','Nucleolus','Centriole','Nucleus','NEW','11-138','11-100',NULL),
(80520,'biology','The stroma of the chloroplast contains enzymes required for the synthesis of','Carbohydrates and proteins','The stroma of the chloroplast contains enzymes required for the synthesis of carbohydrates and proteins.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:136, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >>  Cell Organelles',NULL,'3','mcq','Lipids','Vesicles','Carbohydrates and proteins','Oxysomes','NEW','11-136','11-098',NULL),
(80521,'biology','Who discovered ribosomes as dense particles under the electron microscope?','George Palade','Ribosomes are the granular structures first observed under the electron microscope as dense particles by George Palade (1953).(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:136, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >>  Cell Organelles',NULL,'2','mcq','George Palade','Kolliker','Boveri','Strasburger','NEW','11-136','11-098',NULL),
(80522,'biology','Golgi apparatus consist of many flat, disc-shaped sacs or cisternae of ____________ diameter.','0.5μm to 1.0μm','Golgi apparatus consist of many flat, disc-shaped sacs or cisternae of 0.5μm to 1.0μm diameter.  (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:133, paragraph no:6)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >>  Cell Organelles',NULL,'3','mcq','0.1μm to 0.5μm','0.5μm to 1.0μm','1μm to 3μm','0.5nm to 1.0nm','NEW','11-133','11-095',NULL),
(80523,'biology','"These are lens-shaped, oval, spherical, discoid or even ribbon-like organelles having variable length (5-10μm) and width (2-4μm)" is a statement about which of the following?','Chloroplast','Majority of the chloroplasts of the green plants are found in the mesophyll cells of the leaves. These are lens-shaped, oval, spherical, discoid or even ribbon like organelles having variable length (5-10μm) and width (2-4μm). Their number varies from 1 per cell of the Chlamydomonas, a green alga to 20-40 per cell in the mesophyll. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:136, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >>  Cell Organelles',NULL,'2','mcq','Golgi bodies','Chlorophyll','Chloroplast','Mitochondria','NEW','11-136','11-098',NULL),
(80524,'biology','The central tubules in the axoneme of cilia and flagella are connected by bridges and is also enclosed by a central sheath, which is connected to one of the tubules of each peripheral doublets by a  _____________. Fill in the blank.','Radial spoke','The central tubules in the axoneme of cilia and flagella are connected by bridges and is also enclosed by a central sheath, which is connected to one of the tubules of each peripheral doublets by a radial spoke. Thus, there are nine radial spokes. The peripheral doublets are also interconnected by linkers. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:137, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >>  Cell Organelles',NULL,'2','mcq','Radial spoke','Linkers.','Bridges','Tubules','NEW','11-137','11-099',NULL),
(80525,'biology','Both the centrioles in a centrosome lie___________ to each other in which each has an organisation like the cartwheel.','Perpendicular','Centrosome is an organelle usually containing two cylindrical structures called centrioles. They are surrounded by amorphous pericentriolar materials. Both the centrioles in a centrosome lie perpendicular to each other in which each has an organisation like the cartwheel. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:137, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >>  Cell Organelles',NULL,'2','mcq','Parallel','Perpendicular','Oblique','End to end','NEW','11-137','11-099',NULL),
(81053,'biology','Golgi apparatus consist of many flat, disc-shaped sacs or cisternae of ____________ diameter.','0.5μm to 1.0μm','Golgi apparatus consist of many flat, disc-shaped sacs or cisternae of 0.5μm to 1.0μm diameter.  (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:133, paragraph no:6)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >>  Cell Organelles',NULL,'3','mcq','0.1μm to 0.5μm','0.5μm to 1.0μm','1μm to 3μm','0.5nm to 1.0nm','NEW','11-133','11-095',NULL),
(81054,'biology','"These are lens-shaped, oval, spherical, discoid or even ribbon-like organelles having variable length (5-10μm) and width (2-4μm)" is a statement about which of the following?','Chloroplast','Majority of the chloroplasts of the green plants are found in the mesophyll cells of the leaves. These are lens-shaped, oval, spherical, discoid or even ribbon like organelles having variable length (5-10μm) and width (2-4μm). Their number varies from 1 per cell of the Chlamydomonas, a green alga to 20-40 per cell in the mesophyll. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:136, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >>  Cell Organelles',NULL,'2','mcq','Golgi bodies','Chlorophyll','Chloroplast','Mitochondria','NEW','11-136','11-098',NULL),
(81055,'biology','The central tubules in the axoneme of cilia and flagella are connected by bridges and is also enclosed by a central sheath, which is connected to one of the tubules of each peripheral doublets by a  _____________. Fill in the blank.','Radial spoke','The central tubules in the axoneme of cilia and flagella are connected by bridges and is also enclosed by a central sheath, which is connected to one of the tubules of each peripheral doublets by a radial spoke. Thus, there are nine radial spokes. The peripheral doublets are also interconnected by linkers. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:137, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >>  Cell Organelles',NULL,'2','mcq','Radial spoke','Linkers.','Bridges','Tubules','NEW','11-137','11-099',NULL),
(81056,'biology','Both the centrioles in a centrosome lie___________ to each other in which each has an organisation like the cartwheel.','Perpendicular','Centrosome is an organelle usually containing two cylindrical structures called centrioles. They are surrounded by amorphous pericentriolar materials. Both the centrioles in a centrosome lie perpendicular to each other in which each has an organisation like the cartwheel. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:137, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >>  Cell Organelles',NULL,'2','mcq','Parallel','Perpendicular','Oblique','End to end','NEW','11-137','11-099',NULL),
(80601,'chemistry','Which of the following is the most common oxidation state for Nickel?(At. No Cr= 24, Mn= 25, Fe= 26, Co= 27)','2+','The most common oxidation state for Ni is +2.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 222; Table 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxidation states',NULL,'1','mcq','4+','2+','3+','5+','NEW','12-223','12-097',NULL),
(80602,'chemistry','Which metal exhibits only +3 oxidation state?','Sc','Sc exhibits only +3 oxidation state.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 222; Table 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxidation states',NULL,'1','mcq','Mn','Zn','Ni','Sc','NEW','12-223','12-097',NULL),
(80603,'chemistry','Match the following with their respective most common oxidation state.','Cr-+3 and +6, V-+5, Ti-+4, Co-+2 and +3','The correct match is Cr - +3 +6, V - +5, Ti - +4, Co - +2 +3\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 222; Table 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxidation states',NULL,'3','match','Cr,+3 and +6','V,+5','Ti,+4','Co,+2 and +3','NEW','12-223','12-097',NULL),
(80604,'chemistry','Which of following is/are the most common oxidation state/s for Mn?','2+ and 7+','The most common oxidation states for Mn are +2 and +7\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 222; Table 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxidation states',NULL,'1','mcq','2+ and 5+','7+ only','2+ and 7+','2+ only','NEW','12-223','12-097',NULL),
(80605,'chemistry','What is the maximum possible oxidation state of Fe?','6+','The the maximum possible oxidation state for Fe is +6\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 222; Table 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxidation states',NULL,'1','mcq','4+','8+','6+','3+','NEW','12-223','12-097',NULL),
(80606,'biology','The cellular structures that are almost like centrioles are -','Basal bodies','Both the cilium and flagellum emerge from centriole-like structure called the basal bodies.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:137, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Filaments For Locomotion And Support',NULL,'2','mcq','Basal bodies','Microfilaments','Microtubules','Centromeres','NEW','11-137','11-099',NULL),
(80607,'chemistry','For which of the 3d metal maximum number of oxidation states are possible?','Mn','Mn has the maximum possible oxidation states which are 2+, 3+, 4+, 5+, 6+, 7+, with +2 and +7 being common oxidation states.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 222; Table 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxidation states',NULL,'2','mcq','Fe','Mn','Cr','V','NEW','12-223','12-097',NULL),
(80608,'chemistry','In which of the following element +5 oxidation state does not exist?','Fe','5+ oxidation state doesn\'t exist in Fe.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 222; Table 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxidation states',NULL,'1','mcq','Fe','Mn','Cr','V','NEW','12-223','12-097',NULL),
(80609,'chemistry','Which among the following is not the reason for lesser no. of oxidation states in the extreme ends of 3d series?','There are too many electrons to lose','The lesser number of oxidation states at the extreme ends stems from either too few electrons to lose or share (Sc, Ti) or too many d electrons (hence fewer orbitals available in which to share electrons with others) for higher valence (Cu, Zn).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 223; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxidation states',NULL,'1','mcq','There are too many electrons to lose','There are too many d electrons for higher valence','There are too few electrons to share','There are too few electrons to lose','NEW','12-223','12-098',NULL),
(80610,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statements among the following.\nA. Sc(II) is virtually unknown and Sc(III) is observable.\nB. Ti(II) is more stable than Ti(IV).\nC. Only oxidation state of Zn is +2.\nD. The maximum oxidation states of reasonable stability correspond in value to the sum of the s and d electrons upto iron.','B and D','Early in the series scandium(II) is virtually unknown and titanium (IV) is more stable than Ti(III) or Ti(II). At the other end, the only oxidation state of zinc is +2 (no d electrons are involved). The maximum oxidation states of reasonable stability correspond in value to the sum of the s and d electrons upto manganese.\nThus, statements 2 and 4 are incorrect\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 223; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxidation states',NULL,'2','mcq','A and C','C and D','B and C','B and D','NEW','12-223','12-098',NULL),
(80611,'chemistry','Upto which element the maximum oxidation states of reasonable stability correspond in value to the sum of the s and d electrons ?','Mn','The maximum oxidation states of reasonable stability correspond in value to the sum of the s and d electrons upto manganese.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 223; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxidation states',NULL,'2','mcq','Fe','Cr','V','Mn','NEW','12-223','12-098','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/80611q.png'),
(80612,'chemistry','Which transition element does not exhibit variable oxidation states?','Sc','Sc exibits only +3 oxidation state.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 223; Intext Question 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxidation states',NULL,'2','mcq','Sc','Zn','Fe','Cu','NEW','12-223','12-098',NULL),
(80613,'chemistry','Which among the following is strong oxidising agent?','CrO₃','In group 6, Mo(VI) and W(VI) are found to be more stable than Cr(VI). Thus Cr(VI) in the form of dichromate in acidic medium is a strong oxidising agent, whereas WO₃ and MO₃ are not.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 223; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxidation states',NULL,'2','mcq','CrO₃','WO₃','MO₃','Mn₂O₇','NEW','12-223','12-098',NULL),
(80701,'chemistry','Assertion: Cu cannot release dihydrogen gas from acids.\n\nReason: The high energy to tranform from Cu to Cu⁺² is not balanced by its hydration enthalpy.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','The unique behaviour of Cu, having a positive SRP accounts for its inability to liberate H₂ from acids. Only oxidising acids (nitric and hot concentrated sulphuric) react with Cu, the acids being reduced. The high energy to transform Cu(s) to Cu²⁺(aq) is not balanced by its hydration enthalpy.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 224; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Standard Electrode Potentials',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-224','12-099',NULL),
(80702,'chemistry','For which of the following elements E° is more negative than expected?','Mn, Ni, Zn','It is interesting to note that the value of E° for Mn, Ni and Zn are more negative than expected from the trend.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 224; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Standard Electrode Potentials',NULL,'2','mcq','Mn, Zn, Fe','Fe, Ni, Co','Cu, Sc, V','Mn, Ni, Zn','NEW','12-224','12-099',NULL),
(80703,'chemistry','Assertion: Cr⁺² is reducing in nature and Mn⁺³ is oxidising in nature.\n\nReason: Cr⁺² configuration changes to stable half-filled t₂g level, while Mn⁺³ configuration changes to stable half-filled d-orbital.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Cr²⁺ is reducing as its configuration changes from d⁴ to d³, the latter having a half-filled t₂g level . On the other hand, the change from Mn³⁺ to Mn²⁺ results in the half-filled (d⁵) configuration which has extra stability.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 224; Example 8.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Standard Electrode Potentials',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-224','12-099',NULL),
(80704,'chemistry','Arrange the following in the increasing order of their negative hydration enthalpy of M⁺² form.','Mn<Ti<V<Cr','Mn < Ti < V < Cr is the increasing order of their negative hydration enthalpy of M⁺² form. The values are -1862 kJ/mol, -1866 kJ/mol, -1895 kJ/mol and -1925 kJ/mol for Mn , Ti , V and Cr respectively\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 225; Table 8.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Standard Electrode Potentials',NULL,'3','order','1,Mn','2,Ti','3,V','4,Cr','NEW','12-225','12-099',NULL),
(80705,'chemistry','Arrange the following in the increasing order of their negative hydration enthalpy of M⁺² form.','Fe<Zn<Co<Cu','Fe < Zn < Co < Cu is the increasing order of their negative hydration enthalpy of M⁺² form. The values are -1998  kJ/mol, -2059  kJ/mol, -2079  kJ/mol and -2121  kJ/mol for Fe , Zn , Co and Cu respectively\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 225; Table 8.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Standard Electrode Potentials',NULL,'3','order','1,Fe','2,Zn','3,Co','4,Cu','NEW','12-225','12-099',NULL),
(80706,'chemistry','Assertion: For Ni value of standard electrode potential is more negative than expected.\n\nReason: Ni has highest negative value of enthalpy of hydration.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','The stability of the half-filled d sub-shell in Mn²⁺ and the completely filled d¹⁰ configuration in Zn²⁺ are related to their E values, whereas E for Ni is related to the highest negative hydration enthalpy.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 225; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Standard Electrode Potentials',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-225','12-099',NULL),
(80707,'chemistry','Choose the correct statements among the following.\nA. The low value for Sc reflects the stability of Sc⁺³ which has a noble gas configuration.\nB. The lowest value for Zn is due to the removal of an electron from the stable d¹⁰ configuration of Zn⁺².\nC. The comparatively low value for V is related to the stability of V⁺².','Only A and C','The low value for Sc reflects the stability of Sc⁺³ which has a noble gas configuration.The highest value for Zn is due to the removal of an electron from the stable d¹⁰ configuration of Zn⁺². The comparatively low value for V is related to the stability of V⁺².\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 225; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Standard Electrode Potentials',NULL,'2','mcq','Only A and B','Only B and C','Only A and C','All A, B, C','NEW','12-225','12-099',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(80708,'biology','Disc shaped structures called ______ are present on side of centromere.','Kinetochores','Every chromosome (visible only in dividing cells) essentially has a primary constriction or the centromere on the sides of which disc shaped structures called kinetochores are present (Figure 8.12). (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:139, paragraph no:2) diagram (e0816)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Nucleus',NULL,'3','mcq','Kinetochores','Histones','Centriole','Centrosome','NEW','11-139','11-101',NULL),
(80709,'biology','Cells actively carrying out protein synthesis have -','Large and more numerous nucleoli','Nucleoli is a site for active ribosomal RNA synthesis. Larger and more numerous nucleoli are present in cells actively carrying out protein synthesis (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:138, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Nucleus',NULL,'2','mcq','Smaller and single nucleolus','Smaller and more numerous nucleoli','Large and more numerous nucleoli','Larger and single nucleolus','NEW','11-138','11-100',NULL),
(80710,'biology','Chromatin consists of -','DNA+ RNA+ histones + non-histones','Chromatin contains DNA and some basic proteins called histones, some non-histone proteins and also RNA(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:139, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Nucleus',NULL,'2','mcq','DNA only','DNA+ histones','DNA+ RNA+ histones + non-histones','Ribonucleoproteins only','NEW','11-139','11-101',NULL),
(80711,'biology','The total length of DNA molecules of 46 chromosomes in a human cell is about _____ , where as a-typical is 10 m in length -','Two meter','A single human cell has approximately two metre long thread of DNA distributed among its forty six (twenty three pairs) chromosomes(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:139, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Nucleus',NULL,'2','mcq','Two mm','Two cm','One fifth of  mm','Two meter','NEW','11-139','11-101',NULL),
(80712,'biology','Chromosomes have non-staining secondary constrictions at a constant location. This gives the appearance of a small fragment called_______','Satellite','Sometimes a few chromosomes have non-staining secondary constrictions at a constant location. This gives the appearance of a small fragment called the satellite. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:140, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Nucleus',NULL,'2','mcq','Chromomere','Telomere','Satellite','Primary constriction','NEW','11-140','11-102',NULL),
(31204,'chemistry','Match the groups with their respective valence:','15-3 and 5,16-2 and 6,2-2,18-0 and 8','Group with their no. of valence electrons are shown in the given diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 92; diagram(ne0315)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Oxidation states',NULL,'3','match','15,3 and 5','16,2 and 6','2,2','18,0 and 8','NEW','11-092','11-092',NULL),
(80713,'biology','Structure which provides the shape to chromosomes is called -','Centromere','Every chromosome (visible only in dividing cells) essentially has a primary constriction or the centromere on the sides of which disc shaped structures called kinetochores are present . Centromere holds two chromatids of a chromosome. Based on the position of the centromere, the chromosomes can be classified into four types. The metacentric chromosome has middle centromere forming two equal arms of the chromosome. The sub metacentric chromosome has centromere slightly away from the middle of the chromosome resulting into one shorter arm and one longer arm. In case of acrocentric chromosome the centromere is situated close to its end forming one extremely short and one very long arm, whereas the telocentric chromosome has a terminal centromere. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:139, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Nucleus',NULL,'2','mcq','Centromere','Centriole','Satellite','Chromomere','NEW','11-139','11-101',NULL),
(80714,'biology','Which among the following is incorrect?','Microbodies are present only in plant cell.','Many membrane bound minute vesicles called microbodies that contain various enzymes, are present in both plant and animal cells(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:140, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Nucleus',NULL,'2','mcq','Membrane bound minute vesicles called microbodies','Microbodies contain various enzymes','Microbodies are present in both plant and animal cells','Microbodies are present only in plant cell.','NEW','11-140','11-102',NULL),
(80715,'biology','Mark the parts in the chromosome diagram (0811)','a-Satellite, b-Secondary constriction, c-Centromere','a-Satellite, \nb-Secondary constriction,\nc-Centromere. \nNCERT class 11 Page 139 Figure 8.13','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Nucleus',NULL,'3','match','a,Satellite','b,Secondary constriction','c,Centromere',NULL,'NEW','11-139','11-101','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/80715q.png'),
(80716,'biology','The term "nuclear envelope" is more correct than the term "nuclear membrane" because','The enclosure is made up of two membranes','Electron microscopy has revealed that the nuclear envelope, which consists of two parallel membranes with a space between (10 to 50 nm) called the perinuclear space, forms a barrier between the materials present inside the nucleus and that of the cytoplasm. The outer membrane usually remains continuous with the endoplasmic reticulum and also bears ribosomes on it. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:138 paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Nucleus',NULL,'2','mcq','The enclosure has pores which membranes do not','The enclosure is made up of two membranes','The chemical composition is inconsistent with cellular membranes','All of them','NEW','11-138','11-100',NULL),
(80717,'biology','_________ chromosome has middle centromere forming two equal arms of the chromosome.','Metacentric','The metacentric chromosome has middle centromere forming two equal arms of the chromosome. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:139, paragraph no:2) diagram (e0817)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Nucleus',NULL,'2','fillup','Me,ta,cen,tric',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-139','11-101',NULL),
(80718,'biology','_________ chromosome has centromere slightly away from the middle of the chromosome resulting into one shorter arm and one longer arm.','sub-metacentric','The sub-metacentric chromosome has centromere slightly away from the middle of the chromosome resulting into one shorter arm and one longer arm. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:139, paragraph no:2)  diagram (e0817)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Nucleus',NULL,'2','mcq','sub-metacentric','metacentric','telocentric','acrocentric','NEW','11-139','11-101',NULL),
(80719,'biology','________ chromosome the centromere is situated close to its end forming one extremely short and one very long arm.','acrocentric','In case of acrocentric chromosome the centromere is situated close to its end forming one extremely short and one very long arm. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:139, paragraph no:2)  diagram (e0817)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Nucleus',NULL,'2','mcq','metacentric','sub-metacentric','acrocentric','telocentric','NEW','11-139','11-101',NULL),
(80720,'biology','_________ chromosome has a terminal centromere.','Telocentric','The telocentric chromosome has a terminal centromere. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:139, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Nucleus',NULL,'2','fillup','Te,lo,cen,tric',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-139','11-101',NULL),
(80721,'biology','The __________nucleus has a loose and indistinct network of nucleoprotein fibres called chromatin.','Interphase','You may recall that the interphase nucleus has a loose and indistinct network of nucleoprotein fibres called chromatin. But during different stages of cell division, cells show structured chromosomes in place of the nucleus. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:139, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Nucleus',NULL,'2','mcq','Interphase','Prophase','Metaphase','Anaphase','NEW','11-139','11-101',NULL),
(80722,'biology','Some mature cells even lack nucleus, e.g., ______ of many mammals.','Erythrocytes','Some mature cells even lack nucleus, e.g., erythrocytes of many mammals and sieve tube cells of vascular plants.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Nucleus',NULL,'1','mcq','Erythrocytes','WBC','Platlets','None of them','NEW','11-139','11-101',NULL),
(80801,'chemistry','What is highest oxidation of Ti with halides?','4','The highest oxidation numbers are achieved in TiX₄ (tetrahalides),\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 225; Table 8.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'1','mcq','4','2','6','0','NEW','12-225','12-099',NULL),
(80802,'chemistry','Which one of the following halide does not exist?','MnF₇','MnF₇ doesn\'t exist since the +7 state for Mn is not represented in simple halides.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 225; Table 8.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','MnF₂','MnF₇','MnF₄','MnF₃','NEW','12-225','12-099',NULL),
(80803,'chemistry','Which element among the following form halide in its +1 oxidation state?','Cu','Cu forms halides in +1 oxidation state however they are unstable.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 225; Table 8.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','Co','Ni','Ti','Cu','NEW','12-225','12-099',NULL),
(80804,'chemistry','Which of the following flouride does not exist?','TiF₂','Only TiCl₂ and TiI₂ exists in 2+ oxidation state of Ti\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 225; Table 8.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','VF₂','TiF₂','NiF₂','ZnF₂','NEW','12-225','12-099',NULL),
(80805,'chemistry','What is highest possible oxidation state of Mn with halides?','4+','4+ is the highest oxidation state of Mn in halides.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry  Class 12; Page 225; Table 8.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'1','mcq','3+','6+','4+','7+','NEW','12-225','12-099',NULL),
(80806,'chemistry','Which of the following copper halide will be unstable?','CuF','In 1+ oxidation state of Cu only CuCl, CuBr and CuI are known.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 225; Table 8.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'1','mcq','CuCl','CuF','CuI','CuBr','NEW','12-225','12-099',NULL),
(80807,'chemistry','Which halide of Cobalt exists?','CoF₃','Co forms only CoF₃ in its 3+ oxidation state. Cobalt tetrahalides donot exist. Co forms only triflouride and dihalides\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 225; Table 8.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','CoF₃','CoF₄','CoCl','CoCl₃','NEW','12-225','12-099',NULL),
(80808,'chemistry','Which among the following halides exist?','NiF₂','Only NiF₂ exists. Rest halides are inexistent.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 225; Table 8.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','TiF₂','VI₄','MnCl₃','NiF₂','NEW','12-225','12-099',NULL),
(80809,'chemistry','The ability of fluorine to stabilise the highest oxidation state is due to higher lattice energy as in the case of which flouride?','CoF₃','The ability of fluorine to stabilise the highest oxidation state is due to either higher lattice energy as in the case of CoF₃, or higher bondenthalpy terms for the higher covalent compounds, e.g., VF₅ and CrF₆. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 225; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','TiF₂','CoF₄','CoF₃','VF₅','NEW','12-225','12-099',NULL),
(80810,'chemistry','The ability of fluorine to stabilise the highest oxidation state is due to higher bond enthalpy as in the case of which fluoride?','CrF₆','The ability of fluorine to stabilise the highest oxidation state is due to either higher lattice energy as in the case of CoF₃, or higher bondenthalpy terms for the higher covalent compounds, e.g., VF₅ and CrF₆. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 225; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','TiF₂','CrF₆','CoF₃','CoF','NEW','12-225','12-099',NULL),
(80811,'chemistry','What do VX₅ type of halides upon hydrolysis give?','VOX₃','Although V⁺⁵ is represented only by VF₅, the other halides, however, undergo hydrolysis to give oxohalides, VOX₅.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 225; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','VO₂X','V₂O₅','VOX₃','VO₃','NEW','12-225','12-099',NULL),
(80812,'chemistry','Cu⁺² oxidises the I⁻ to two products X and Y. X is used for quantitative estimation along with hypo solution. Here X is-','I₂','All Cu (II) halides are known except the iodide. In this case, Cu⁺² oxidises I⁻ to I₂. I₂ is used for quantitative estimation along with hypo solution.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 226; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'3','mcq','I₂','Cu₂I₂','CuI₂','I₂O₅','NEW','12-226','12-099',NULL),
(80813,'chemistry','Statement I : Cu(I) compounds are unstable in aqueous solution and undergo disproportionation.\n\nStatement II : The stability of Cu²⁺ (aq) rather than Cu⁺(aq) is due to the more negative value of hydration of Cu²⁺.','Both statements are true.','Cu(I) compounds are unstable in aqueous solution and undergo disproportionation.The stability of Cu²⁺ (aq) rather than Cu⁺(aq) is due to the much more negative value of hydration of Cu²⁺.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 226; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','Only statement I is true.','Only statement II is true.','Both statements are true.','Both statements are false.','NEW','12-226','12-099',NULL),
(80814,'chemistry','What is the highest oxidation number attained by Mn in its oxides?','7+','The highest oxidation number in the oxides coincides with the group number and is attained in Mn₂O₇ as+7\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 226; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'1','mcq','4+','3+','6+','7+','NEW','12-226','12-099',NULL),
(80815,'chemistry','Iron forms ferrate(VI) compound which media?','Alkaline','Beyond Group 7, no higher oxides of Fe above Fe₂O₃, are known, although ferrates (VI)  FeO₄²⁻, are formed in alkaline media but they readily decompose to Fe₂O₃ and O₂\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 226; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'1','mcq','Alkaline','Acidic','Neutral','Aqueous','NEW','12-226','12-099',NULL),
(80816,'chemistry','The compound FeO₄²⁻ formed in alkaline media, readily decomposes to give paramagnetic gas and metal oxide. What is this gas?','O₂','The compound FeO₄²⁻ formed in alkaline media, readily decomposes to gives Fe₂O₂ and O₂ . O₂  is a paramagnetic gas.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 226; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','O₂','Fe₂O₃','NO₂','O₃','NEW','12-226','12-099',NULL),
(80817,'chemistry','The ability of oxygen to stabilise the high oxidation states exceeds that of ______.','Flourine','The ability of oxygen to stabilise these high oxidation states exceeds that of fluorine.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 226; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'1','mcq','Chlorine','Flourine','Iodine','Bromine','NEW','12-226','12-099',NULL),
(80818,'chemistry','Assertion: The ability of oxygen to stabilise these high oxidation states exceeds that of fluorine.\n\nReason: Oxygen has ability to form multiple bonds with metals.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','The ability of oxygen to stabilise these high oxidation states exceeds that of fluorine. Thus the highest Mn fluoride is MnF₄ whereas the highest oxide is Mn₂O₇. The ability of oxygen to form multiple bonds to metals explains its superiority.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 226; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-227','12-100',NULL),
(80819,'chemistry','Oxygen stabilises oxocation Vᵛ as-','VO₂⁺','Besides the oxides, oxocations stabilise V as VO²⁺, V IV as VO²⁺ and Ti IV as TiO²⁺.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 226; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','VO₂⁺','VO⁺','VO₂²⁻','VO²⁺','NEW','12-227','12-100',NULL),
(31205,'chemistry','Group 18 has __ valence electrons.','8','Group 18 has 8 valence electrons.As shown in the given diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 92; diagram(ne0315)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Oxidation states',NULL,'1','fillup','10,4,6,8',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-092','11-092',NULL),
(80820,'chemistry','Which of the following is true about Mn₂O₇?','There is the presence of Mn-O-Mn bridge.','In the covalent oxide Mn₂O₇., each Mn is tetrahedrally surrounded by O’s including a Mn–O–Mn bridge.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 226; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','It is a ionic oxide.','Each Mn is octahedrally surrounded by O.','There is the presence of Mn-O-Mn bridge.','Each Mn is trigonaly surrounded by O’s','NEW','12-227','12-100',NULL),
(80821,'chemistry','Which one of the following does not exist as [MO₄]ⁿ⁻ tetrahedral ion?','V(IV)','Tetrahedral ions [MO₄]ⁿ⁻ are known for V(V), CrVl, Mn(V), Mn(Vl) and Mn(VII)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 226; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','V(V)','Cr(VI)','Mn(VII)','V(IV)','NEW','12-227','12-100',NULL),
(80822,'chemistry','How many types of oxides are formed by titanium?','3','TiO₂, Ti₂O₃, and TiO are the 3 types of oxides are formed by titanium\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 226; Table 8.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'1','mcq','2','3','5','4','NEW','12-227','12-100',NULL),
(80823,'chemistry','Which among the following oxides does not exist?','Cr₂O₅','Cr₂O₅ does not exist. Only CrO₃, CrO₂, Cr₂O₃ and CrO exist.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 226; Table 8.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','CrO₂','V₂O₅','Cr₂O₅','VO','NEW','12-227','12-100',NULL),
(80824,'chemistry','What is the highest possible oxidation state of chromium in its oxides?','6','6+ is the highest possible oxidation state of chromium in its oxides.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 226; Table 8.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'1','mcq','4','6','7','5','NEW','12-227','12-100',NULL),
(80825,'chemistry','Which element among the following does not form mixed oxides?','Sc','Sc does not form mixed oxides. It only forms Sc₂O₃.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 226; Table 8.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','Mn','Fe','Co','Sc','NEW','12-227','12-100',NULL),
(80826,'chemistry','Which element do not exist in oxide form in its +2 oxidation state?','Sc','Sc only forms Sc₂O₃. In this oxide Sc exists in +3 oxidation state.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 226; Table 8.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','Sc','Ti','Cu','Mn','NEW','12-227','12-100',NULL),
(80827,'chemistry','Which 3d metal forms oxide in its +1 oxidation state?','Cu','Cu forms oxide in its +1 oxidation state.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 226; Table 8.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'1','mcq','Sc','V','Co','Cu','NEW','12-227','12-100',NULL),
(80828,'chemistry','Which of the following oxides of Mn do not exists?','MnO₄','MnO₄ does not exist.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 226; Table 8.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','MnO₂','Mn₂O₇','MnO','MnO₄','NEW','12-227','12-100',NULL),
(80829,'chemistry','Select the correct order for oxidising power?','MnO₄⁻ > VO₂⁺ > Cr₂O₇²⁻','The correct order is MnO₄⁻ > VO₂⁺ > Cr₂O₇²⁻. This is due to the increasing stability of the lower species to which the are reduced.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 226; Example 8.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','VO₂⁺ > MnO₄⁻ > Cr₂O₇²⁻','Cr₂O₇²⁻ > MnO₄⁻ > VO₂⁺','MnO₄⁻ > VO₂⁺ > Cr₂O₇²⁻','Cr₂O₇²⁻ > VO₂⁺ > MnO₄⁻','NEW','12-227','12-100',NULL),
(80830,'biology','Important site for formation of glycoproteins and glycolipids is [AIPMT 2011]','Golgi apparatus','Eukaryotic cells contain a unique cluster of membrane vesicles known as Golgi apparatus. It principally performs the function of packaging materials. The newly synthesized proteins are handed over to the Golgi apparatus which is catalysed by the addition of carbohydrates, lipid or sulphates moieties to the proteins. Golgi apparatus is the important site of formation of glycoproteins and glycolipids. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:134, paragraph no:1).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Vacuole','Golgi apparatus','Plastid','Lysosome','NEW','11-134','11-096',NULL),
(80831,'biology','Which one of the following is not considered as a part of the endomembrane system? [AIPMTMains 2011]','Peroxisome','While each of the membranous organelles is distinct in terms of its structure and function, many of these are considered together as an endomembrane system because their functions are coordinated. The endomembrane system include endoplasmic reticulum (ER), Golgi complex, lysosomes and vacuoles. Since the functions of the mitochondria, chloroplast and peroxisomes are not coordinated with the above components, these are not considered as part of the endomembrane system(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:133, paragraph no:1).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Golgi complex','Peroxisome','Vacuole','Lysosome','NEW','11-133','11-095',NULL),
(80832,'biology','The plasma membrane consists mainly of [AIPMT 2010]','Proteins embedded in a phospholipid bilayer','Plasma membrane consists of lipids, proteins, carbohydrates and water. Lipid molecules possess both hydrophobic and hydrophilic ends and are thus arranged in the form of lipid bilayer. Most common lipid of the bilayer is phospholipid. Protein molecules occur at places both inside (intrinsic proteins) and on the outer side (extrinsic proteins) of the phospholipid bilayer.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:131, paragraph no:1).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Phospholipids embedded in a protein bilayer','Proteins embedded in a phospholipid bilayer','Proteins embedded in a polymer of glucose molecules','Proteins embedded in a carbohydrate bilayer','NEW','11-131','11-093',NULL),
(80833,'biology','The main area of various types of activities of a cell is [AIPMT 2010]','Cytoplasm','Cytoplasm is granular, crystallocolloidal complex that forms the living protoplasm of a cell excluding its nucleus. It consists of proteins, nucleic acids, fats, carbohydrates, vitamins, minerals, waste metabolites, and all the organelles. It is the main area for various types of activities of a cell like respiration, nutrition, storage, etc.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Plasma membrane','Mitochondrion','Cytoplasm','Nucleus','NEW','11-126','11-088',NULL),
(80834,'biology','Which one of the following has its own dna? [AIPMT 2010]','Mitochondria','Mitochondria are the sites of aerobic respiration. They produce cellular energy in the form of ATP, hence they are called ‘power houses’ of the cell. The matrix also possesses single circular DNA molecule, a few RNA molecules, ribosomes (70S) and the components required for the synthesis of proteins. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:135, paragraph no:1).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Mitochondria','Dictyosome','Lysosome','Peroxisome','NEW','11-135','11-097',NULL),
(80835,'biology','Which one of the following structures between two adjacent cells is an effective transport pathway? [AIPMT 2010]','Plasmodesmata','Plasmodesmata are fine cytoplasmic strands that connect the protoplasts of adjacent plant cells by passing through their cell walls. Plasmodesmata are cylindrical in shape(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:132, paragraph no:3).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Plasmodesmata','Plastoquinones','Endoplasmic reticulum','Plasmalemma','NEW','11-132','11-094',NULL),
(80836,'biology','An elaborate network of filamentous proteinaceous structures present in the cytoplasm which helps in the maintenance of cell shape is called','Cytoskeleton','An elaborate network of filamentous proteinaceous structures consisting of microtubules, microfilaments and intermediate filaments present in the cytoplasm is collectively referred to as the cytoskeleton. The cytoskeleton in a cell are involved in many functions such as mechanical support, motility, maintenance of the shape of the cell. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:136, paragraph no:3).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Thylakoid','Endoplasmic reticulum','Plasmalemma','Cytoskeleton','NEW','11-136','11-098',NULL),
(80837,'biology','Middle lamella is composed mainly of [AIPMT 2009]','Calcium pectate','Middle lamella of cell wall is composed of calcium and magnesium pectate. It joins the adjoining cells together and thus acts as a cementing layer. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:132, paragraph no:3).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Muramic acid','Calcium pectate','Phosphoglycerides','Hemicellulose','NEW','11-132','11-094',NULL),
(80838,'biology','Cytoskeleton is made up of [AIPMT 2009]','Proteinaceous filaments','The ability of eukaryotic cells to adopt a variety of shapes and to carry out coordinated and directed movements depends on the cytoskeleton. The main proteins that are present in the cytoskeleton are tubulin (in the microtubules), actin, myosin, tropomyosin and other (in the microfilaments) and keratins, vimentin, desmin,lamin and other (in intermediate filaments)(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:136, paragraph no:4).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Callose deposits','Cellulosic microfibrils','Proteinaceous filaments','Calcium carbonate granules','NEW','11-136','11-098',NULL),
(80839,'biology','Plasmodesmata are [AIPMT 2009]','Connections between adjacent cells','The cell wall and middle lamellae may be traversed by plasmodesmata which connect the cytoplasm of neighbouring cells. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:132, paragraph no:5).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Locomotary structures','Membranes connecting the nucleus with plasmalemma','Connections between adjacent cells','Lignified cemented layers between cells','NEW','11-132','11-094',NULL),
(80840,'biology','In germinating seeds fatty acids are degraded exclusively in the [AIPMT 2008]','Glyoxysomes','Glyoxysomes are small, spherical vesicles enclosed by a single unit membrane. Glyoxysomes are generally found in cells of yeast, Neurospora and in germinating fatty seeds where fat is being converted into carbohydrates, i.e., glyoxylate cycle (gluconeogenesis).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Peroxisomes','Mitochondria','Proplastids','Glyoxysomes','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80841,'biology','Vacuole in a plant cell [AIPMT 2008]','Is membranebound and contains water and excretory substances','In a plant cell, vacuole may be defined as a nonliving reservoir, bounded by a differentially or selectively permeable membrane, the tonoplast. It is filled with a highly concentrated solution called vascular sap or cell sap which contains many dissolved solutes such as organic acids, soluble carbohydrates, soluble nitrogenous compounds as nitrates, enzyme, tannins, chlorides, phosphates, amino acids, alkaloids and anthocyanin pigments.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:134, paragraph no:3).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Lacks membrane and contains air','Lacks membrane and contains water and excretory substances','Is membranebound and contains storage proteins and lipids','Is membranebound and contains water and excretory substances','NEW','11-134','11-096',NULL),
(80842,'biology','The two subunits of ribosome remain united at a critical ion level of [AIPMT 2008]','Magnesium','Ribosomes are very small organelles having a diameter in nanometers range','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Magnesium','Calcium','Copper','Manganese','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80843,'biology','Keeping in view the ‘fluid mosaic model‘ for the structure of cell membrane, which one of he following statement is correct with respect to the movements of lipids and proteins from one lipid monolayer to the other (described as flipflop movement)? [AIPMT 2008]','While lipids can rarely flipflop, proteins cannot','According to fluid mosaic model there is rapid internal motion involving flexing within each lipid molecule a rapid lateral diffusion of the lipids is possible and a slow ‘flipflop’ motion, i.e., a transfer of lipid molecules from one side of the bilayer to the other, is also possible. The lipid molecules might also rotate about their axes. The proteins of the membrane are concerned with the enzymatic activity of the membrane, with transport of molecules, and with a receptor function whereas, the lipid bilayer provides the permeability barrier','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','While proteins can flipflop, lipids can not','Neither lipids, nor proteins can flipflop','Both lipids and proteins can flipflop','While lipids can rarely flipflop, proteins cannot','NEW','11-132','11-094',NULL),
(80844,'biology','Which one of the following is not a constituent of cell membrane? [AIPMT 2007]','Proline','Studies showed that the cell membrane is mainly composed of lipids and proteins. The major lipids are phospholipids that are arranged in a bilayer. Also, the lipids are arranged within the membrane with the polar head towards the outer sides and the hydrophobic tails towards the inner part.This ensures that the nonpolar tail of saturated hydrocarbons is protected from the aqueous environment (Figure 8.4). In addition to phospholipids membrane also contains cholesterol. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:131, paragraph no:2).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Glycolipids','Proline','Phospholipids','Cholesterol','NEW','11-131','11-093',NULL),
(80845,'biology','Select the wrong statement from the following. [AIPMT 2007]','Both chloroplasts and mitochondria have an internal compartment, the thylakoid space bounded by the thylakoid membrane.','both chloroplasts and mitochondria have an intrernal compartmentr but thylakoid space is present only in chloroplast whereas mitochondria contains cristae','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Both chloroplasts and mitochondria have an internal compartment, the thylakoid space bounded by the thylakoid membrane.','Both chloroplasts and mitochondria contain dna.','The chloroplasts are generally much larger than mitochondria.','Both chloroplasts and mitochondria contain an inner and an outer membrane','NEW','11-136','11-098',NULL),
(80846,'biology','Biological organisation starts with [AIPMT 2007]','Submicroscopic molecular level','Molecular assemblies are large organised sets of molecular units that make up parts of organelles. For example, one common macromolecular assembly is the microtubule which is important in forming structure in the cell related to maintaining the cell structure or related to cell movement. The cell (plasma) membrane that surrounds many organelles and the cell is a highly organised molecular assembly.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Cellular level','Organismic level','Atomic level','Submicroscopic molecular level','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80847,'biology','Which of the following statements regarding mitochondrial membrane is not correct ? [AIPMT 2006]','The enzymes of the electron transfer chain are embedded in the outer membrane.','The outer membrane of mitochondrion is smooth, freely permeable to most small molecules, contains fewer enzymes and is poor in proteins. It has porin proteins which form channels for the passage of molecules through it. It allows uptake of substrates and release of ATP. The inner membrane is semipermeable and regulates the passage of materials into and out of the mitochondrion. It is rich in enzymes and carrier proteins (permeases). It is usually produced into numerous infolds called cristae (singular crista). It bears minute regularly spaced lollipopshaped particles known as oxysomes. The rest of the inner membrane contains the electron carrier molecules of the electron transport chain.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:135, paragraph no:1).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','The outer membrane resembles a sieve.','The outer membrane is permeable to all kinds of molecules.','The enzymes of the electron transfer chain are embedded in the outer membrane.','The inner membrane is highly convoluted forming a series of infoldings','NEW','11-135','11-097',NULL),
(80848,'biology','Which of the following statements regarding cilia is not correct ? [AIPMT 2006]','The organized beating of cilia is controlled by fluxes of ca 2+ across the membrane.','Cilia are fine hair like vibratile, cytoplasmic processes borne by certain cell types. Their movement either propel the organism or move the medium past a fixed cell. The cilia are enclosed by a unit membrane which is an extension of the plasma membrane of the cell. Within the membrane, is a fluid matrix having a supporting axial shaft, or axoneme. The microtubules, single as well as double, are composed of the globular units of the protein tubulin. The arms of A microtubules contain a protein dynein. The latter is ATPase enzyme which catalyzes hydrolysis of ATP to ADP, and transfers the free energy released direct to ciliary work. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:137, paragraph no:1-2).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Cilia contain an outer ring of nine doublet microtubules surrounding two singlet microtubules.','The organized beating of cilia is controlled by fluxes of ca 2+ across the membrane.','Cilia are hairlike cellular appendages.','Microtubules of cilia are composed of tubulin','NEW','11-137','11-099',NULL),
(80849,'biology','A major breakthrough in the studies of cells came with the development of electron microscope. this is because [AIPMT 2006]','The resolving power of the electron microscope is much higher than that of the light microscope','Microscopes are used for studying cellular structures. They are used to magnify small objects. In electron microscopes, a high energy beam of electrons is focused through electromagnetic lenses. It can magnify very small details with high resolving power. The increased resolution results from the shorter wavelength of the electron beam.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','The electron microscope is more powerful than the light microscope as it uses a beam of electrons which has wavelength much longer than that of photons','The resolving power of the electron microscope is much higher than that of the light microscope','The resolving power of the electron microscope is 200 350 nm as compared to0.1 0.2 nm for the light microscope','Electron beam can pass through thick materials, whereas light microscopy requires thin sections','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80850,'biology','Chlorophyll in chloroplasts is located in [AIPMT 2005]','Grana','Like mitochondria, the chloroplasts are also double membrane bound. Of the two, the inner chloroplast membrane is relatively less permeable. The space limited by the inner membrane of the chloroplast is called the stroma. A number of organised flattened membranous sacs called the thylakoids, are present in the stroma (Figure 8.8). Thylakoids are arranged in stacks like the piles of coins called grana (singular: granum) or the intergranal thylakoids. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:136, paragraph no:1).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Grana','Pyrenoid','Stroma','Both grana and stroma','NEW','11-136','11-098',NULL),
(80851,'biology','A student wishes to study the cell structure under a light microscope having 10x eyepiece and 45x objective. he should illuminate the object by which one of the following colours of light so as to get the best possible resolution? [AIPMT 2005]','Blue','Resolution of microscope is inversely proportional to wavelength of light used. Out of four options given, blue light has minimum wavelength and hence maximum resolution.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Blue','Green','Yellow','Red','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80852,'biology','According to widely accepted “fluid mosaic model” cell membranes are semifluid, where lipids and integral proteins can diffuse randomly. in recent years, this model has been modified in several respects. in this regard, which of the following statements is incorrect? [AIPMT 2005]','Proteins can also undergo flipflop movements in the lipid bilayer.','According to this, the quasi-fluid nature of lipid enables lateral movement of proteins within the overall bilayer. This ability to move within the membrane is measured as its fluidity.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:132, paragraph no:1).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Proteins in cell membranes can travel within the lipid bilayer.','Proteins can also undergo flipflop movements in the lipid bilayer.','Proteins can remain confined within certain domains of the membrane.','Many proteins remain completely embedded within the lipid bilayer','NEW','11-132','11-094',NULL),
(80853,'biology','Centromere is required for [AIPMT 2005]','Movement of chromosomes towards poles','Centromere is the point at which the two chromatids of a chromosome are held together. During movement of chromosomes, the spindle fibres (on which the chromatids move) are attached to the centromere. Crossing over involves physical exchange of genetic material between nonsister chromatids of homologous chromosomes. Cytoplasmic cleavage is the division of cytoplasm. Transcription is the process in which the genetic information of DNA is transferred to mRNA as the first step in protein synthesis. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:138, paragraph no:1).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Movement of chromosomes towards poles','Cytoplasmic cleavage','Crossing over','Transcription','NEW','11-138','11-100',NULL),
(80854,'biology','Chemiosmotic theory of ATP synthesis in the chloroplasts and mitochondria is based on [AIPMT 2005]','Proton gradient','Chemiosmotic coupling hypothesis is the most widely accepted explanation for oxidative phosphorylation in mitochondria and photophosphorylation in thylakoid membranes. Mitchell proposed the idea of chemiosmotic coupling. He suggested that a concentration gradient of protons is established across the mitochondrial membrane because there is an accumulation of hydrogen ions on one side of the mitochondrial membrane. The proton accumulation is necessary for energy transfer to the endergonic ADP phosphorylation process.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Membrane potential','Accumulation of na ions','Accumulation of k ions','Proton gradient','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80855,'biology','The main organelle involved in modification and outing of newly synthesized proteins to their destinations is [AIPMT 2005]','Endoplasmic reticulum','The proteins formed on ribosomes pass into the ER lumen where they are modified. Then the modified proteins move on into the transitional area, where the ER buds off membranous sacs, the transport vesicles, carrying the proteins to the Golgi apparatus. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:133, paragraph no:2).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Chloroplast','Mitochondria','Lysosome','Endoplasmic reticulum','NEW','11-133','11-095',NULL),
(80856,'biology','The telomeres of eukaryotic chromosomes consist of short sequences of [AIPMT 2004]','Guanine rich repeats','Telomeres are highly conserved element throughout the eukaryotes both in structure and function. Telomeric DNA has been shown to consist of simple randomly repeated sequences, characterised by clusters of G residues in one strand and C residues in other strand.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Thymine rich repeats','Cytosine rich repeats','Adenine rich repeats','Guanine rich repeats','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80857,'biology','In chloroplasts, chlorophyll is present in the [AIPMT 2004]','Thylakoids','The space limited by the inner membrane of the chloroplast is called the stroma. A number of organised flattened membranous sacs called the thylakoids, are present in the stroma (Figure 8.8). (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:136, paragraph no:1).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Outer membrane','Inner membrane','Thylakoids','Stroma','NEW','11-136','11-098',NULL),
(80858,'biology','Cellular totipotency is demonstrated by [AIPMT 2003]','All plant cells','Totipotency is the ability of a living somatic plant cell to develop into a complete plant. It was first demonstrated by Steward et. al','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only gymnosperm cells','All plant cells','All eukaryotic cells','Only bacterial cells','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80901,'chemistry','Many of transition metals are sufficiently ____ to dissolve in mineral acids.','electropositive','Transition metals vary widely in their chemical reactivity. Many of them are sufficiently electropositive to dissolve in mineral acids, although a few are ‘noble’—that is, they are unaffected by single acids.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 227; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Chemical Reactivity',NULL,'1','mcq','electronegative','electropositive','noble','active','NEW','12-227','12-101',NULL),
(80902,'chemistry','Which of the following elements are passive to dilute non-oxidising acids at room temperature?','Ti and V','The metals of the first series with the exception of copper are relatively more reactive and are oxidised by 1M H⁺, though the actual rate at which these metals react with oxidising agents like hydrogen ion (H⁺) is sometimes slow. For example, titanium and vanadium, in practice, are passive to dilute non oxidising acids at room temperature.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 227; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Chemical Reactivity',NULL,'2','mcq','Ti and Co','V and Cr','V and Fe','Ti and V','NEW','12-227','12-101',NULL),
(80903,'chemistry','Which among the following are strongest oxidising agents in aqueous solutions?\nA. Mn⁺³, B. Co⁺³, C. Cr⁺³','Both A and B','An examination of the E values for the redox couple M⁺³/M⁺² shows that Mn⁺³ and Co⁺³ ions are the strongest oxidising agents\nin aqueous solutions.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 227; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Chemical Reactivity',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and C','Both B and C','Both A and B','Only C','NEW','12-227','12-101',NULL),
(80904,'chemistry','Which of the following ions will not liberate hydrogen from dilute acid solution?','Cu⁺','Cu⁺ doesn\'t release hydrogen from dilute acid solution. This is because it has positive standard reduction potential for Cu⁺/Cu.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 227; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Chemical Reactivity',NULL,'2','mcq','Ti⁺²','V⁺²','Cr⁺²','Cu⁺','NEW','12-227','12-101',NULL),
(80905,'chemistry','Asserion: There is irregularity in the values for  E°(M⁺²/M) in 3d series.\n\nReason: There is irregular variation of ionisation enthapy and sublimation enthalpies which are relatively much less for V and Mn.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','E°(M⁺²/M) values are not regular which can be explained from the irregular variation of sum of first and second ionisation enthalpies and also the sublimation enthalpies which are relatively much less for manganese and vanadium.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 227; Example 8.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Chemical Reactivity',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-227','12-101',NULL),
(80906,'chemistry','Assertion:  E° value for Mn⁺³/Mn⁺² couple is much less positive than that for Cr⁺³/Cr⁺² couple.\n\nReason: Third ionisation energy of Cr is much higher than that of Mn.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are false.','The correct assertion is-  E° value for Mn⁺³/Mn⁺² couple is much more positive than that for Cr⁺³/Cr⁺² couple and the correct reason is- Third ionisation energy of Mn is much higher than that of Cr. Much larger third ionisation energy of Mn (where the required change is d⁵ to d⁴) is mainly responsible for this. This also explains why the +3 state of Mn is of little importance.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 227; Example 8.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Chemical Reactivity',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-227','12-101',NULL),
(80907,'chemistry','Which is stronger oxidizing agent among the following?','Mn⁺²','Mn⁺² is the strongest reducing agent among the following with E° value -1.18 which is the lowest among the following.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 227; Intext Question 8.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Chemical Reactivity',NULL,'2','mcq','Fe⁺²','Cr⁺²','Mn⁺²','Co⁺²','NEW','12-227','12-101',NULL),
(80908,'chemistry','Assertion:The highest oxidation state of a metal exhibited in its oxide or fluoride only.\n\nReason: Oxygen and Flourine can oxidise metal to its highest oxidation state because high electronegativity.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Because of small size and high electronegativity oxygen or fluorine can oxidise the metal to its highest oxidation state.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 227; Intext Question 8.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Chemical Reactivity',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-227','12-101',NULL),
(80909,'biology','Which cell organelle is concerned with glycosylation of protein? [AIPMT 2000]','Endoplasmic reticulum','Glycolysation of protein means linking of sugars to proteins which starts in rough endoplasmic reticulum and completed in golgi complex.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Ribosome','Peroxisome','Endoplasmic reticulum','Mitochondria','NEW','11-133','11-095',NULL),
(80910,'biology','Which of the following organelles has single membrane? [AIPMT 1999]','Spherosomes','The spherosomes are, spherical bodies, which has a single membrane','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Mitochondria','Spherosomes','Nucleus','Cell wall','NEW','11-138','11-100',NULL),
(80911,'biology','The proteins are synthesized at [AIPMT 1999]','Ribosomes','The ribosomes provide space for the synthesis of proteins in the cell. Hence, they are known as the “protein factories” of the cell. The ribosomes bound to the membranes generally synthesize proteins for export as secretions by exocytosis, or for incorporation into membranes, or for inclusion into lysosomes. The free ribosomes generally produce enzymic proteins for use in the cell itself.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:136, paragraph no:2).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Centrosomes','Golgi bodies','Ribosomes','Mitochondria','NEW','11-136','11-098',NULL),
(80912,'biology','Which of the following structures will not be common to mitotic cell of a higher plant? [AIPMT 1997]','Centriole','The centrioles occur in nearly all animal cells and in motile plant cells, such as zoospores of algae, sperm cells of ferns, and motile algae. They are absent is amoebae, prokaryotic cells, higher gymnosperms and all angiosperms. An interphase (undividing) cell has a pair of centrioles (diplosome) usually near the nucleus. They lie in a small mass of specialized, distinctly staining cytoplasm that lacks other cell organelles. The centrioles and the centrosphere are together referred to as centrosome. Before cell division, the centrioles duplicate so that a dividing cell has a pair of centrioles at each pole of the spindle. Spindle fibre, cell plate and centromere are present in all plant cells. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:138, paragraph no:1).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Centriole','Spindle fibre','Cell plate','Centromere','NEW','11-138','11-100',NULL),
(80913,'biology','The mechanism of ATP formation both in chloroplast and mitochondria is explained by [AIPMT 1997]','Chemiosmotic theory','The mechanism of ATP formation both in chloroplast and mitochondria is explained by Chemiosmotic Hypothesis based on the proton gradient and proton pump with ATPase activity','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Chemiosmotic theory','Munch’s hypothesis (mass flow model)','Relay pump theory of godlewski','Cholodnywont’s model','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80914,'biology','Protein synthesis in an animal cell, takes place [AIPMT 1997]','Only on ribosomes present in the cytoplasm as well as mitochondria','Ribosomes are the robust organelles, present in the cytoplasm, composed of rRNA and a set of specific proteins. They are present free in cytosol or on the surface of the endoplasmic reticulum. The spatial separation depends on the presence of ER-targeting signal sequence on the protein being synthesized, thus, a particular ribosome might be ER-bound while synthesizing one protein and free in the cytosol when translating another protein\nSo, the correct answer is \'On ribosomes present in cytoplasm as well as in mitochondria\'','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Only on ribosomes present in the cytoplasm','Only on ribosomes present in the cytoplasm as well as mitochondria','Only in the ribosomes attached to nucleolus','Only on ribosomes attached to ER and nucleus','NEW','11-133','11-095',NULL),
(80915,'biology','Lysosomes are rich in [AIPMT 1996]','Hydrolytic enzymes','The isolated lysosomal vesicles have been found to be very rich in almost all types of hydrolytic enzymes (hydrolases – lipases, proteases, carbohydrases) optimally active at the acidic pH. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:134, paragraph no:2).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Nucleic acids','Hydrolytic enzymes','Carbohydrates','Hormones','NEW','11-134','11-096',NULL),
(80916,'biology','Colchicine is an inhibitory chemical, which [AIPMT 1996]','Prevents the spindle formation in mitosis','Colchicine is an alkaloid derived from the autumn crocus, Colchicum autumnale. It inhibits spindle formation in cells during mitosis so that chromosomes cannot separate during anaphase, thus inducing multiple sets of chromosomes. Colchicine is used in genetics, cytology, and plant breeding research and also in cancer therapy to inhibit cell division.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Stops the functioning of centriole','Prevents attaching of centromeres with rays','Prevents the spindle formation in mitosis','Prevents the formation of equatorial plane','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80917,'biology','The prokaryotic flagella possess [AIPMT 1995]','Helically arranged protein molecule','Prokaryotic flagellum is not surrounded by any membrane. It consists of a single thread. The thread is made of numerous identical spherical protein subunits called, flagellin.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Helically arranged protein molecule','“9 + 2” membrane enclosed structure','Unit membrane enclosed fibre','Protein membrane enclosed fibre','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80918,'biology','The desmosomes are concerned with [AIPMT 1995]','Cell adherence','In desmosomes circular patches of cell membranes are held together by interaction of proteins that extend through each membrane into the space between cells. The cell membrane has on the inner side a dense plate of protein for mechanical support and bears fine filaments, the tonofibrils, radiating into the cell. The desmosomes act as “spot welds” and keep the cells firmly together.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Cell division','Cell adherence','Cytolysis','Cellular excretion','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80919,'biology','Which of the following organelles contain enzymes that have digestive action? [AIPMT 1994]','Lysosomes','Lysosomes are membrane bound vesicular structures formed by the process of packaging in the golgi apparatus. The isolated lysosomal vesicles have been found to be very rich in almost all types of hydrolytic enzymes (hydrolases – lipases, proteases, carbohydrases) optimally active at the acidic pH. These enzymes are capable of digesting carbohydrates, proteins, lipids and nucleic acids. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:134, paragraph no:2).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Ribosomes','Polysomes','Plastids','Lysosomes','NEW','11-134','11-096',NULL),
(80920,'biology','In mitochondria, cristae act as sites for [AIPMT 1994]','Oxidationreduction reaction','Respiratory chain is located in the inner membrane (cristae) of mitochondria. It consists of a series of proteins containing oxidationreduction groups. Chemical treatment of the mitochondrial membrane results in the isolation of five complexes which have been designated as complexes I, II, III, IV and V. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:135, paragraph no:1).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Protein synthesis','Phosphorylation of flavoproteins','Breakdown of macromolecules','Oxidationreduction reaction','NEW','11-135','11-097',NULL),
(80921,'biology','Centromere is required for [AIPMT 1994]','Poleward movement of chromosomes','Poleward movement of duplicate centrosomes is perfromed by the centromere.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Replication of dna','Chromosome segregation','Poleward movement of chromosomes','Cytoplasmic cleavage','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80922,'biology','Which one of the following organelles is located near the nucleus and contains a collection of flattened membrane bound cisternae? [AIPMT 1994]','Golgi apparatus','Golgi apparatus is a stack of parallel, flattened, intercommunicating sacs or cisternae and many peripheral tubules and vesicles(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:134, paragraph no:4).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Nucleolus','Mitochondrion','Centriole','Golgi apparatus','NEW','11-134','11-096',NULL),
(80923,'biology','The inner membrane of the mitochondria is, usually, highly convoluted forming a series of infoldings known as [AIPMT 1994]','Cristae','The inner membrane in mitochondria forms a number of infoldings called the cristae (sing.: crista) towards the matrix. The cristae increase the surface area.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:135, paragraph no:1). diagram (e0813)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Thylakoids','Lamellae','Cristae','Grana','NEW','11-135','11-097',NULL),
(80924,'biology','Besides giving out secretory vesicles, the golgi apparatus is also concerned with the formation of [AIPMT 1994]','Lysosomes','The two poles of a Golgi apparatus are called cis face and trans face, which act respectively as the receiving and shipping departments. The vesicles lie near the ends and concave surface of the Golgi complex. They are pinched off from the tubules of the cisternae. They are of two types: smooth or secretory vesicles, which have a smooth surface and contain secretions of the cell; and coated vesicles, that have rough surface and elaborate membrane proteins. They carry materials to or from the cisternae. The Golgi complex gives rise to primary lysosomes by budding from the trans face of cisternae. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:134, paragraph no:2).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Lysosomes','Plastids','Grana of chloroplasts','Cell plates after cell division in plants','NEW','11-134','11-096',NULL),
(80925,'biology','Active and passive transports across cell membrane differ in [AIPMT 1993]','Passive transport is along the concentration gradient while active transport is due to metabolic energy','Many molecules can move briefly across the membrane without any requirement of energy and this is called the passive transport. Neutral solutes may move across the membrane by the process of simple diffusion along the concentration gradient, i.e., from higher concentration to the lower. Water may also move across this membrane from higher to lower concentration. Movement of water by diffusion is called osmosis. As the polar molecules cannot pass through the nonpolar lipid bilayer, they require a carrier protein of the membrane to facilitate their transport across the membrane. A few ions or molecules are transported across the membrane against their concentration gradient, i.e., from lower to the higher concentration. Such a transport is an energy dependent process, in which ATP is utilised and is called active transport, e.g., Na+/K+ Pump. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:132, paragraph no:3).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Passive transport is nonselective','Passive transport is along the concentration gradient while active transport is due to metabolic energy','Active transport is more rapid','Passive transport is confined to anions while active transport in confined to cations','NEW','11-132','11-094',NULL),
(80926,'biology','Balbiani rings (puffs) are sites of [AIPMT 1993]','RNA and protein synthesis','Balbiani rings (puffs) are site of RNA and protein synthesis. These chromosomes show distinct dark and light bands. Euchromatin is present in dark bands and heterochromatin is present in light bands. These bands helps in mapping of chromosomes in cytogenetic studies. These chromosomes form puffs or loops (in region of dark bands) which are called Balbiani puffs or Balbiani rings where synthesis of mRNA occurs.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','DNA replication','RNA and protein synthesis','Synthesis of polysaccharides','Synthesis of lipids','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80927,'biology','In plant cells, peroxisomes are associated with [AIPMT 1993]','Photorespiration','In plant cells, peroxisomes are associated with photorespiration. Found in C4 plants','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Photorespiration','Phototropism','Photoperiodism','Photosynthesis','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80928,'biology','Membranous bag with hydrolytic enzymes which is used for controlling intracellular digestion of macromolecules is [AIPMT 1993]','Lysosome','Lysosomes are membrane bound vesicular structures formed by the process of packaging in the golgi apparatus. The isolated lysosomal vesicles have been found to be very rich in almost all types of hydrolytic enzymes (hydrolases – lipases, proteases, carbohydrases) optimally active at the acidic pH. These enzymes are capable of digesting carbohydrates, proteins, lipids and nucleic acids. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:134, paragraph no:2).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Endoplasmic reticulum','Nucleosome','Lysosome','Phagosome','NEW','11-134','11-096',NULL),
(80929,'biology','Golgi apparatus is absent in [AIPMT 1993]','Bacteria and bluegreen algae','Golgi apparatus is absent in bacteria and blue green algae. Golgi bodies are absent in prokaryotic cells and present in eukaryotic cells except in male gametes of bryophytes and pteridophytes, mammalian RBCs, sieve tubes of plants and in cells of fungi. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:126, paragraph no:4).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Higher plants','Yeast','Bacteria and bluegreen algae','None','NEW','11-126','11-088',NULL),
(80930,'biology','Cell recognition and adhesion occur due to biochemicals of cell membranes named [AIPMT 1993]','Glycoproteins and glycolipids','Cell recognition and adhesion occur due to biochemicals of cell membranes named glycoproteins and glycolipids. These are formed due to small carbohydrate molecules present on lipids and extrinsic proteins.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Proteins','Lipids','Proteins and lipids','Glycoproteins and glycolipids','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80931,'biology','In salivary gland chromosomes/polytene chromosomes, pairing is [AIPMT 1993]','Formed between homologous chromosomes','In salivary gland chromosomes/polytene chromosomes, pairing is formed between homologous chromosomes. A characteristics feature of these chromosomes is that somatic pairing occurs in them and hence their number appears half of normal somatic cells.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Absent','Occasional','Formed between nonhomologous chromosomes','Formed between homologous chromosomes','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80932,'biology','Which is correct about cell theory in view of current status of our knowledge about cell structure? [AIPMT 1993]','Cell theory does not hold good because all living beings (e.g., viruses) do not have cellular organisation.','Viruses are an exceptions to the cell theory as they are obligate parasites (subcellular in nature). Paramecium, Rhizopus, Vaucheria are some examples which may or may not be exceptions to the theory. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:126, paragraph no:1).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','It needs modification due to discovery of subcellular structures like chloroplasts and mitochondria.','Modified cell theory means that all living being are composed of cells capable of reproducing.','Cell theory does not hold good because all living beings (e.g., viruses) do not have cellular organisation.','Cell theory means that all living objects consists of cells whether or not capable of reproducing','NEW','11-126','11-088',NULL),
(80933,'biology','Names of schleiden and schwann are associated with [AIPMT 1993]','Cell theory','Names of Schleiden and Schwann are associated with cell theory(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:126, paragraph no:1).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Protoplasm as the physical basis of life','Cell theory','Theory of cell lineage','Nucleus functions as control centre of cell','NEW','11-126','11-088',NULL),
(80934,'biology','Binding of specific protein on regulatory DNA sequency can be studied by means of [AIPMT 1993]','Xrays crystallography','Xrays crystallography is a technique which is used to study of binding protein on regulatory DNA sequence. In this technique, Xrays pass through a crystal of a substance and form a diffraction pattern. With the help of this technique, the arrangement of atoms in the molecular structure of enzymes, proteins, DNA, etc. can be studied. Wilkins, Watson and Crick used this technique to determine the molecular configuration of double helix of DNA.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Ultra centrifugation','Electron microscope','Light microscope','Xrays crystallography','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80935,'biology','All types of plastids possess essentially the same structure because they [AIPMT 1992]','Can transform from one form to another','All the plastids have a common origin and one type of plastid can change into another.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Perform the same function','Store food materials like starch, fat and protein','Occur in aerial parts','Can transform from one form to another','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80936,'biology','Experiments on acetabularia by hammerling proved the role of [AIPMT 1992]','Nucleus in heredity','Acetabularia used in Hammerling’s nucleocytoplasmic experiment is unicellular uninucleate green algae. Hammerling’s experiment on Acetabularia involved exchanging rhizoid and stalk. Presence of hereditary information in the nucleous was proved by the work of Hammerling on single celled alga Acetabularia.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Cytoplasm in controlling differentiation','Nucleus in heredity','Chromosomes in heredity','Nucleocytoplasmic ratio','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80937,'biology','Which one is apparato reticolare? [AIPMT 1992]','Golgi apparatus','Apparato reticolare are Golgi apparatus. The Golgi apparatus was named after Camillo Golgi','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Golgi apparatus','Endoplasmic reticulum','Microfilaments','Microtubules','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80938,'biology','An outer covering membrane is absent over [AIPMT 1992]','Nucleolus','An outer covering membrane is absent over nucleolus. Nucleolus is a dense, spherical, colloidal body which remains attached with nucleolar organizing chromosomes.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:138, paragraph no:2).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Nucleolus','Lysosome','Mitochondrion','Plastid','NEW','11-138','11-100',NULL),
(80939,'biology','All plastids have similar structure because they can [AIPM T1992]','Get transformed from one type to another','All plastids are transformable from one type to another based on the need for function and gene expression.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Store starch, lipids and proteins','Get transformed from one type to another','Perform same function','Be present together','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80940,'biology','Oxysomes or f 0 – f 1 particles occur on [AIPMT 1992]','Inner mitochondrial membrane','Oxysomes or F1 particles are embedded on the inner mitochondrial membrane and helps in transportiong of H⁺ ions','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Thylakoids','Mitochondrial surface','Inner mitochondrial membrane','Chloroplast surface','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80941,'biology','Ribosomes are the centre for [AIPMT 1992]','Protein synthesis','Ribosomes are the site of protein synthesis. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:129, paragraph no:4).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Respiration','Photosynthesis','Protein synthesis','Fat synthesis','NEW','11-129','11-091',NULL),
(80942,'biology','Angstrom (å) is equal to [AIPMT 1992]','0.0001 micro m','An angstrom (symbol Å) is a nonSI unit of length that is internationally recognised, equal to 0.0001micro m.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','0.01 mm','0.001 mm','0.0001 micro m','000001 mm','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80943,'biology','Electron microscope has a high resolution power. this is due to [AIPMT 1992, 1990]','Very low wavelength of electron beam','electrons have much shorter wavelength than visible light and this allows electron microscope to produce higher resolution image and have higher range of magnificatiom','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Electromagnetic lenses','Very low wavelength of electron beam','Low wavelength of light source used','High numerical aperture of glass lenses used','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80944,'biology','Addition of new cell wall particles amongst the existing ones is [AIPMT 1991]','Intussusception','Addition of new cell wall particles amongst the existing one is intussusception. Growth of cell wall occurs by two methods by intussusception and by apposition. By intussusception, the primary wall is stretched and materials of secondary wall are deposited. By apposition, material of secondary wall are deposited in the form of thin layers.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Deposition','Apposition','Intussusception','Aggregation','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80945,'biology','Cell wall shows [AIPMT 1991]','Complete permeability','Cell wall shows complete permeability because it helps in the transport of substances into and out of the cell. The main function of cell wall is to provide mechanical support.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Complete permeability','Semi-permeability','Differential permeability','Impermeability','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80946,'biology','Ribosomes were discovered by [AIPMT 1991]','Palade','Ribosomes are the granular structures first observed under the electron microscope as dense particles by George Palade (1953).(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:136, paragraph no:2).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Golgi','Porter','De robertis','Palade','NEW','11-136','11-098',NULL),
(80947,'biology','Fluid mosaic model of cell membrane was put forward by [AIPMT 1991]','Singer and nicolson','An improved model of the structure of cell membrane was proposed by Singer and Nicolson. (1972) widely accepted as fluid mosaic model(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:132, paragraph no:1).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Danielli and davson','Singer and nicolson','Garner and allard','Watson and crick','NEW','11-132','11-094',NULL),
(80948,'biology','Resolution power is the ability to [AIPMT 1991]','Distinguish two close objects','Resolution power is the ability to distinguish two close objects. It is the ability of a system to distinguish two close points as two separate points. Resolving power of microscope depends upon the numerical aperature of the objective lens system and its wavelength.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Distinguish two close points','Distinguish two close objects','Distinguish amongst organelles','Magnify image','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80949,'biology','Hammerling’s experiments of acetabularia involved exchanging [AIPMT 1990]','Rhizoid and stalk','Acetabularia cell is composed of three segments: the "rhizoid" or base which contains the nucleus, the "stalk," and the "cap." \nIn his experiments, Hammerling grafted the stalk of one species of Acetabularia onto the rhizoid of another species. In all cases, the cap that eventually developed on the grafted cell matched the species of the rhizoid rather than that of the stalk.\nThis experiment shows that the base is responsible for the type of cap that grows. The nucleus that contains genetic information is in the base, so the nucleus directs cellular development.\nSo, the correct option is \'Rhizoid and stalk\'.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Cytoplasm','Nucleus','Rhizoid and stalk','Gametes','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80950,'biology','The latest model for plasma membrane is [AIPMT 1990]','Fluid mosaic model','An improved model of the structure of cell membrane was proposed by Singer and Nicolson. (1972) widely accepted as fluid mosaic model(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:132, paragraph no:1).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Lamellar model','Unit membrane model','Fluid mosaic model','Molecular lipid model','NEW','11-132','11-094',NULL),
(80951,'biology','Magnification of compound microscope is not connected with (1990)','Numerical aperture','Magnification of compound microscope is not connected with numerical aperature. The magnifying power is represented by the symbol ‘X’. The total magnification of a microscope is obtained by multiplying the objective magnification and ocular lens magnification.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Numerical aperture','Focal length of objective','Focal length of eye piece','Tube length','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80952,'biology','Magnification of compound microscope is not connected with [AIPMT 1990]','Four chromatids and two centromeres','A bivalent consist of four chromatids and two centromeres. Bivalent is a pair of homologous chromosome lying together in the zygotene stage of prophase I of first meiotic division.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Two chromatids and one centromere','Two chromatids and two centromeres','Four chromatids and two centromeres','Four chromatids and four centromeres','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80953,'biology','Nucleoproteins are synthesised in [AIPMT 1989]','Cytoplasm','Nucleoproteins are compounds present in cells of living organisms that consist of nucleic acids with proteins. Nucleoproteins are synthesized in cytoplasm. These are conjugated proteins. They are of two types Deoxyribonucleoproteins and ribonucleoproteins.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Nucleoplasm','Nuclear envelope','Nucleolus','Cytoplasm','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80954,'biology','Polyribosomes are aggregates of [AIPMT  1989]','Several ribosomes held together by string of mrna','Polyribosomes are aggregates of several ribosomes held together by string of mRNA.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Ribosomes and rrna','Only rrna','Peroxisomes','Several ribosomes held together by string of mrna','NEW','11-130','11-092',NULL),
(80955,'biology','Plasma membrane is made of [AIPMT 1989]','Proteins, lipids and carbohydrates','Studies showed that the cell membrane is mainly composed of lipids and proteins. The major lipids are phospholipids that are arranged in a bilayer. Also, the lipids are arranged within the membrane with the polar head towards the outer sides and the hydrophobic tails towards the inner part.This ensures that the nonpolar tail of saturated hydrocarbons is protected from the aqueous environment. In addition to phospholipids membrane also contains cholesterol. Later, biochemical investigation clearly revealed that the cell membranes also possess protein and carbohydrate. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:131, paragraph no:1-2).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Proteins and carbohydrates','Proteins and lipids','Proteins, lipids and carbohydrates','Proteins, some nucleic acid and lipids','NEW','11-131','11-093',NULL),
(80956,'biology','Organelles can be separated from cell homogenate through [AIPMT 1989]','Differential centrifugation','Organelles can be separated from cell homogenate through differential centrifugation. The basic principle involved here is sedimentation of particles in a suspension by centrifugal force. In a centrifuge, the particles sediment at different rates when an accelerating force is subjected. The rate of sedimentation depends upon the size of the particles, its shape and density.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Chromatography','Xrays diffraction','Differential centrifugation','Autoradiography','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80957,'biology','Acetabularia used in hammerling’s nucleocytoplasmic experiments is [AIPMT 1988]','Unicellular uninucleate green algae','this experiment demonstrated that the nucleus contains the genetic information by exchanging the stalk and rhizoid of acetabularia as the nucleus is always present in the rhizoid','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Unicellular fungus','Multicellular fungus','Unicellular uninucleate green algae','Unicellular multinucleate green algae','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80958,'biology','According to fluid mosaic model, plasma membrane is composed of [AIPMT 1988]','Phospholipids, extrinsic proteins and intrinsic proteins','These studies showed that the cell membrane is mainly composed of lipids and proteins. The major lipids are phospholipids that are arranged in a bilayer. Also, the lipids are arranged within the membrane with the polar head towards the outer sides and the hydrophobic tails towards the inner part.This ensures that the nonpolar tail of saturated hydrocarbons is protected from the aqueous environment. In addition to phospholipids membrane also contains cholesterol. Later, biochemical investigation clearly revealed that the cell membranes also possess protein and carbohydrate. The ratio of protein and lipid varies considerably in different cell types. In human beings, the membrane of the erythrocyte has approximately 52 % protein and 40 % lipids. Depending on the ease of extraction, membrane proteins can be classified as integral and peripheral. Peripheral proteins lie on the surface of membrane while the integral proteins are partially or totally buried in the membrane.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:131, paragraph no:1-2-3).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Phospholipids and oligosaccharides','Phospholipids and hemicellulose','Phospholipids and integral proteins','Phospholipids, extrinsic proteins and intrinsic proteins','NEW','11-131','11-093',NULL),
(80959,'biology','Match the following [NEET 2021]','Cristae-infoldings in mitochondria, Thylakoids-flattened membranous sacs in stroma of plastids, Centromere-primary constrictions in chromosome, cisternae-Discs shaped sacs in golgi apparatus','Cristae-infoldings in mitochondria, Thylakoids-flattened membranous sacs in stroma of plastids, Centromere-primary constrictions in chromosome, cisternae-Discs shaped sacs in golgi apparatus. The inner membrane forms a number of infoldings called the cristae (sing.: crista) towards the matrix. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:135, paragraph no:1)\nThe space limited by the inner membrane of the chloroplast is called the stroma. A number of organised flattened membranous sacs called the thylakoids, are present in the stroma.  (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:136, paragraph no:1)\nEvery chromosome (visible only in dividing cells) essentially has a primary constriction or the centromere on the sides of which disc shaped structures called kinetochores are present. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:139, paragraph no:2)\nCamillo Golgi (1898) first observed densely stained reticular structures near the nucleus. These were later named Golgi bodies after him. They consist of many flat, disc-shaped sacs or cisternae of 0.5μm to 1.0μm diameter.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:133, paragraph no:6)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','match','Cristae,infoldings in mitochondria','Thylakoids,flattened membranous sacs in stroma of plastids','Centromere,primary constrictions in chromosome','cisternae,Discs shaped sacs in golgi apparatus','NEW','11-133','11-095',NULL),
(80960,'biology','Plasma membrane is made of [AIPMT 1989]','Protein and Lipid','Plasma membrane is the outermost covering of the cell which is made up of phospholipids bilayer and proteins.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:139, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Protein and Lipid','Protein','Lipid','Carbohydrate','NEW','11-139','11-101',NULL),
(80961,'biology','The organelles that are included in the endomembrane system are [NEET 2021]','Endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi complex, Lysosomes and Vacuoles','The endomembrane system include endoplasmic reticulum (ER), golgi complex, lysosomes and vacuoles. Since the functions of the mitochondria, chloroplast and peroxisomes are not coordinated with the above components, these are not considered as part of the endomembrane system. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:133, paragraph no:1)..','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Golgi complex, Endoplasmic reticulum, Mitochondria and Lysosomes','Endoplasmic reticulum, Mitochondria, Ribosomes and Lysosomes','Endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi complex, Lysosomes and Vacuoles','Golgi complex, Mitochondria, Ribosomes and Lysosomes','NEW','11-133','11-095',NULL),
(81058,'biology','Match the following [NEET 2021]','Cristae-infoldings in mitochondria, Thylakoids-flattened membranous sacs in stroma of plastids, Centromere-primary constrictions in chromosome, cisternae-Discs shaped sacs in golgi apparatus','Cristae-infoldings in mitochondria, Thylakoids-flattened membranous sacs in stroma of plastids, Centromere-primary constrictions in chromosome, cisternae-Discs shaped sacs in golgi apparatus. The inner membrane forms a number of infoldings called the cristae (sing.: crista) towards the matrix. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:135, paragraph no:1)\nThe space limited by the inner membrane of the chloroplast is called the stroma. A number of organised flattened membranous sacs called the thylakoids, are present in the stroma.  (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:136, paragraph no:1)\nEvery chromosome (visible only in dividing cells) essentially has a primary constriction or the centromere on the sides of which disc shaped structures called kinetochores are present. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:139, paragraph no:2)\nCamillo Golgi (1898) first observed densely stained reticular structures near the nucleus. These were later named Golgi bodies after him. They consist of many flat, disc-shaped sacs or cisternae of 0.5μm to 1.0μm diameter.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:133, paragraph no:6)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','match','Cristae,infoldings in mitochondria','Thylakoids,flattened membranous sacs in stroma of plastids','Centromere,primary constrictions in chromosome','cisternae,Discs shaped sacs in golgi apparatus','NEW','11-139','11-101',NULL),
(81059,'biology','The organelles that are included in the endomembrane system are [NEET 2021]','Endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi complex, Lysosomes and Vacuoles','The endomembrane system include endoplasmic reticulum (ER), golgi complex, lysosomes and vacuoles. Since the functions of the mitochondria, chloroplast and peroxisomes are not coordinated with the above components, these are not considered as part of the endomembrane system. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:133, paragraph no:1)..','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Golgi complex, Endoplasmic reticulum, Mitochondria and Lysosomes','Endoplasmic reticulum, Mitochondria, Ribosomes and Lysosomes','Endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi complex, Lysosomes and Vacuoles','Golgi complex, Mitochondria, Ribosomes and Lysosomes','NEW','11-133','11-095',NULL),
(81060,'biology','When the centromere is situated in the middle of two equal arms of chromosomes, the chromosome is referred as :[NEET 2021]','Metacentric','The metacentric chromosome has middle centromere forming two equal arms of the chromosome. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:139, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Acrocentric','Metacentric','Telocentric','Sub-metacentric','NEW','11-139','11-101',NULL),
(81062,'biology','Which of the following statements with respect to Endoplasmic Reticulum is incorrect?[NEET 2022]','In prokaryotes only RER are present','Prokaryotes lack membrane bound organelles, and thus lack Nucleus, Mitochondria, Lysosome and Endoplasmic Reticullum; The Cell membrane is very complex in Prokaryotes and compensates for the function of most eukaryotic cell organelles','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','RER has ribosomes attached to ER','SER is devoid of ribosomes','In prokaryotes only RER are present','SER are the sites for lipid synthesis','NEW','11-133','11-095',NULL),
(81063,'biology','Which of the following statements are correct with \nrespect of Golgi apparatus ? \n (A) It is the important site of formation of \nglycoprotein and glycolipids. \n (B) It produces cellular energy in the form of ATP. \n (C) It modifies the protein synthesized by ribosomes \non ER. \n (D) It facilitates the transport of ions. \n (E) It provides mechanical support. \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(2) (A) and (C) only','Golgi bodies are the important site of the formation of glycoproteins and glycolipids in Eukaryotic cells.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(2) (A) and (C) only','(1) (B) and (C) only','(3) (A) and (D) only','(4) (D) and (E) only','NEW','11-134','11-096',NULL),
(81064,'biology','Given below are two statements: \n Statement I :-\n In bacteria, the mesosomes are formed by the \nextensions of plasma membrane. \nStatement II :-\n The mesosomes, in bacteria, help in DNA \nreplication and cell wall formation. \n In the light of the above statements, choose the \nmost appropriate answer from the options given \nbelow: [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(3) Both Statement I and Statement II are \ncorrect.','Mesosomes are specific structures found in bacteria.\nThey are caused by plasma membrane invagination.\nThey contribute to DNA replication and respiration.\nThe primary function is to increase the surface area of the plasma membrane.\nThe membrane\'s surface area increases, which aids in cellular respiration.\nMesosomes aid in cell division as well as cell wall and DNA synthesis.\nMesosomes aid in the formation of crosswalks and improve the binding of bacterial DNA to the cell membrane.\nIt facilitates the separation of bacterial DNA into daughter cells.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(3) Both Statement I and Statement II are \ncorrect.','(1) Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.','(2) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is \ncorrect.','(4) Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.','NEW','11-128','11-090',NULL),
(81065,'biology','Which of the following functions is carried out by cytoskeleton in a cell? [NEET 2023]','(2) Motility','The cytoskeleton maintains the shape, size and position of the various cellular components in eukaryotic and prokaryotic cells. It is also responsible for transport of various substances throughout the cell.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Protein synthesis','(2) Motility','(3) Transportation','(4) Nuclear division','NEW','11-136','11-098',NULL),
(81066,'biology','Which of the following are NOT considered as the part of endomembrane system?\nA. Mitochondria\nB. Endoplasmic reticulum\nC. Chloroplasts\nD. Golgi complex\nE. Peroxisomes\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:[NEET 2023]','(1) A, C and E only','The endomembrane system includes endoplasmic reticulum (ER), Golgi complex, lysosomes and vacuoles. Reason: Mitochondria, chloroplast and peroxisomes are not the part of endomembrane system because their function are not coordinated with the same.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) A, C and E only','(2) A and D only','(3) A, D and E only','(4) B and D only','NEW','11-133','11-095',NULL),
