(81001,'chemistry','Which substances are repelled by magnetic field?','Diamagnetic','Diamagnetic substances are repelled by the applied field.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 227; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Magnetic Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Paramagnetic','Diamagnetic','Ferromagnetic','Anti-ferromagnetic','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(81002,'chemistry','Which substances are slightly attracted by magnetic field?','Paramagnetic','Diamagnetic substances are repelled by the applied field while the paramagnetic substances are attracted.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 227; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Magnetic Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Paramagnetic','Diamagnetic','Ferromagnetic','Anti-ferromagnetic','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(81003,'chemistry','Which substances are strongly attracted by magnetic field?','Ferromagnetic','Substances that are attracted very strongly are said to be ferromagnetic.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 227; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Magnetic Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Paramagnetic','Diamagnetic','Ferromagnetic','Anti-ferromagnetic','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(81004,'chemistry','Ferromagnetism is extreme form of ____.','Paramagnetism','Ferromagnetism is extreme form of paramagnetism.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 228; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Magnetic Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Paramagnetism','Diamagnetism','Anti-ferromagnetism','Ferrimagnetism','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(81005,'chemistry','______ arises from the presence of unpaired electrons, each such electron having a magnetic moment.','Paramagnetism','Paramagnetism arises from the presence of unpaired electrons, each such electron having a magnetic moment associated with its spin angular momentum and orbital angular momentum.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 228; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Magnetic Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Paramagnetism','Diamagnetism','Ferromagnetism','Anti-ferromagnetism','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(81006,'chemistry','Magnetic moment of electron is associated with-\nA. Orbital angular momentum \nB. Spin angular momentum\nC. Nature of charge on electron','Both A and B','Paramagnetism arises from the presence of unpaired electrons, each such electron having a magnetic moment associated with its spin angular momentum and orbital angular momentum.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 228; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Magnetic Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and B','Both B and C','Both A and C','Only C','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(81007,'chemistry','How many of the following statements are true?\nA. For the compounds of the first series of transition metals, the contribution of the orbital angular momentum is effectively quenched.\nB. The orbital angular momentum is of no significance for the first series of transition metals.\nC. Magnetic moment is determined by the number of unpaired electrons.\nD. A single unpaired electron has a magnetic moment of 1 Bohr magnetons.','3','For the compounds of the first series of transition metals, the contribution of the orbital angular momentum is effectively quenched and hence is of no significance. For these, the magnetic moment is determined by the number of unpaired electrons and is calculated by using the ‘spin-only’ formula,  where n is the number of unpaired electrons and μ is the magnetic moment in units of Bohr magneton (BM). A single unpaired electron has a magnetic moment of 1.73 Bohr magnetons (BM). Only D is incorrect.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 228; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Magnetic Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','2','1','3','4','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(81008,'chemistry','The magnetic moment ____ with the increasing number of unpaired electrons.','increases','The magnetic moment increases with the increasing number of unpaired electrons.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 228; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Magnetic Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','increases','decreases','changes slightly','doesn\'t change','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(81009,'chemistry','Match the following ions with their spin only magnetic moments.','Sc⁺³-0, Co⁺²-3.87, Cu⁺²-1.73, Fe⁺²-4.90','The correct match is Sc⁺³-0, Co⁺²-3.87, Cu⁺²-1.73, Fe⁺²-4.90.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 228; Table 8.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Magnetic Properties',NULL,'3','match','Sc⁺³,0','Co⁺²,3.87','Cu⁺²,1.73','Fe⁺²,4.90','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(81010,'chemistry','Which ions among the following have same spin only magnetic moment?','Ni⁺² and Ti⁺²','Ni⁺² and Ti⁺² both have 2.84 BM of spin only magnetic moment.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 228; Table 8.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Magnetic Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Cu⁺² and Ni⁺²','Ni⁺² and Ti⁺²','Cu⁺² and Co⁺²','Ti⁺³ and Fe⁺³','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(81011,'chemistry','Which ion has highest spin only magnetic moment?','Mn⁺²','Mn⁺² has highest spin only magnetic moment. which is 5.92 BM. Cu⁺². Zn⁺², V⁺² have 1.73 BM , 0 BM and 3.87 BM respectively\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 228; Table 8.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Magnetic Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Mn⁺²','Cu⁺²','Zn⁺²','V⁺²','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(81012,'chemistry','Which ion has least magnetic moment?','Zn⁺²','Zn⁺² has the least spin only magnetic moment of value 0. This is because the no. of unpaired electrons is 0 in Zn⁺².\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 228; Table 8.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Magnetic Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Co⁺²','Fe⁺²','V⁺²','Zn⁺²','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(81013,'chemistry','Arrange the following in the increasing order of their magnetic moment(spin-only).','Zn⁺²<Ti⁺³<V⁺²<Fe⁺²','Zn⁺² (0 BM) < Ti⁺³ (1.73 BM) < V⁺² (3.87 BM) < Fe⁺² (4.90 BM) is the correct increasing order of their magnetic moment(spin-only).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 228; Table 8.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Magnetic Properties',NULL,'3','order','1,Zn⁺²','2,Ti⁺³','3,V⁺²','4,Fe⁺²','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(81014,'chemistry','Calculate the spin-only magnetic moment for Cr⁺².','4.9','The spin only formula is ʋ = √n(n+2) where n is the no. of unpaired electrons in d orbital.\nFor  Cr²⁺ (Z=24),  n = 4 i.e √4(4+2) = 4.9 \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 228; Table 8.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Magnetic Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','4.9','0','3.87','5.92','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(81015,'chemistry','Which of the following has least number of unpaired electrons?','Ti⁺²','V⁺² has 3 unpaired electrons, Co⁺² has 3 unpaired electrons, Fe⁺² has 4 unpaired electrons and Ti⁺² has 2 unpaired electrons which is the least among the following.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 228; Table 8.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Magnetic Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','V⁺²','Co⁺²','Fe⁺²','Ti⁺²','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(81016,'chemistry','The observed magnetic moment gives a useful indication about the number of __________ present in the atom,\nmolecule or ion.','unpaired electrons','The observed magnetic moment gives a useful indication about the number of unpaired electrons present in the atom, molecule or ion.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 228; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Magnetic Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','paired electrons','unpaired electrons','half-filled orbitals','full-filled orbitals','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(81017,'chemistry','Which ion from the following is expected to repel the applied magnetic field?','Zn⁺²','Zn⁺² being diamagnetic in nature repels the applied magnetic field. The diamagnetic nature is attributed to the fully filled d orbitals in Zn⁺² ions.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 228; Table 8.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Magnetic Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Ti⁺²','Co⁺²','Cu⁺²','Zn⁺²','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(81018,'chemistry','Calculate the magnetic moment of a trivalent ion in aqueous solution if its atomic number is 25.','4.9 B.M.','The spin only formula is ʋ = √n(n+2) where n is the no. of unpaired electrons in d orbital.\nFor  M³⁺ (Z=25),  n = 4 i.e √4(4+2) = 4.9  B.M.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 228; Example 8.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Magnetic Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','3.87 B.M.','4.9 B.M.','1.73 B.M.','0 B.M.','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(81019,'chemistry','Calculate the spin only magnetic moment of M⁺²(aq) ion. (Z= 29)','1.73 B.M.','The spin only formula is ʋ = √n(n+2) where n is the no. of unpaired electrons in d orbital.\nFor  M⁺² (Z=29) n=1 i.e √1(1+2) = 1.73  B.M.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 229; Intext Question: 8.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Magnetic Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','3.87 B.M.','4.86 B.M.','1.73 B.M.','2.84 B.M.','NEW','12-229','12-102',NULL),
(81020,'biology','What is common between chloroplasts, chromoplasts and leucoplasts? [AIPMT 2006]','Ability to multiply by a fission-like process.','Chloroplast, chromoplast, and leucoplast are all plastids that divide by binary fission process.Leucoplasts lack pigments and store starch, proteins and lipids,while Chloroplasts and chromoplasts contain pigments, Chloroplasts contain Grana and thylakoid structure storing chlorophyll pigment, which is absent in the other two;','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Presence of pigments.','Possession of thylakoids and grana.','Storage of starch, proteins and lipids.','Ability to multiply by a fission-like process.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(81021,'biology','In prokaryotes, chromatophores are [AIPMT 2006]','Internal membrane systems that may become extensive and complex in photosynthetic bacteria.','In prokaryotes, chromatophores are internal membrane system that may become extensive and complex in photosynthetic bacteria. It is structurally and functionally similar to eukaryotic chloroplast.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Specialized granules responsible for colouration of cells.','Structures responsible for organizing the shape of the organism.','Inclusion bodies lying free inside the cells for carrying out various metabolic activities.','Internal membrane systems that may become extensive and complex in photosynthetic bacteria.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(81022,'biology','Which of the following is responsible for the mechanical support, protein synthesis and enzyme transport? [AIPMT 2007]','Endoplasmic reticulum','Endoplasmic reticulum (ER) is a system of flattened membranes running through the cytoplasm. Rough ER containing ribosomes is mainly concerned with protein synthesis and transport of enzymes. It also functions as cytoskeleton by giving mechanical support to the cytoplasm.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cell membrane','Mitochondria','Dictyosome','Endoplasmic reticulum','NEW','11-133','11-095',NULL),
(81023,'biology','“omnis-cellula-e-cellula” was given by[AIPMT 2007]','Virchow','Rudolf Virchow (1855) first explained that cells divided and new cells are formed from pre-existing cells (Omnis cellula-e cellula). He modified the hypothesis of Schleiden and Schwann to give the cell theory a final shape. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:126, paragraph no:2).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Virchow','Hooke','Leeuwenhoek','Brown','NEW','11-126','11-088',NULL),
(81024,'biology','Genes present in the cytoplasm of eukaryotic cells, are found in [AIPMT 2005, 2008]','Mitochondria and inherited via egg cytoplasm','Genes present in the cytoplasm of eukaryotic cells are found in mitochondria and inherited via egg cytoplasm.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Mitochondria and inherited via egg cytoplasm','Lysosomes and peroxisomes','Golgi bodies and\' smooth endoplasmic reticulum','Plastids and inherited via male gamete','NEW','11-135','11-097',NULL),
(81025,'biology','What is common between chloroplasts, chromoplasts and leucoplasts? [AIPMT 2008]','Ability to multiply by a fission-like process.','Chromoplasts are plastids responsible for pigment synthesis and storage. They, like all other plastids (including chloroplasts and leucoplasts), are organelles found in specific photosynthetic eukaryotic species. Chloroplasts conduct photosynthesis. Chloroplasts absorb light and use it in conjunction with water and  CO₂  to produce sugars. Leucoplasts are non-pigmented, in contrast to other plastids such as the chloroplast. Lacking pigments, leucoplasts are not green, so they are predictably located in roots and non-photosynthetic tissues of plants. They may become specialized for bulk storage of starch, lipid or protein and are then known as amyloplasts, elaioplasts, or proteinoplasts respectively.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Presence of pigments.','Possession of thylakoids and grana.','Storage of starch, proteins and lipids.','Ability to multiply by a fission-like process.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(81026,'biology','Molecules that are too large to pass through the pores of a cell membrane may enter the cell by a process known as [AIPMT 2009]','Pinocytosis','Pinocytosis, or pinching in of the cell membrane, allows cells, such as the Paramecium, to capture larger food molecules. Hydrolysis, is the process of using water to split molecules apart. Cyclosis is a mechanism for transporting materials within a cell, by the cytoplasm swirling. Synthesis is the process of building up molecules within the cell.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Hydrolysis','Pinocytosis','Cyclosis','Synthesis','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(81027,'biology','Three morphological forms of golgi complex are','Cisternae, tubules and vesicles','Camillo Golgi (1898) first observed densely stained reticular structures near the nucleus. These were later named Golgi bodies after him. They consist of many flat, disc-shaped sacs or cisternae of 0.5μm to 1.0μm diameter (Figure 8.6). These are stacked parallel to each other. Varied number of cisternae are present in a Golgi complex. The Golgi cisternae are concentrically arranged near the nucleus with distinct convex cis or the forming face and concave trans or the maturing face. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:133, paragraph no:5).\nThe cis and the trans faces of the organelle are entirely different, but interconnected. The golgi apparatus principally performs the function of packaging materials, to be delivered either to the intra-cellular targets or secreted outside the cell. Materials to be packaged in the form of vesicles from the ER fuse with the cis face of the golgi apparatus and move towards the maturing face. This explains, why the golgi apparatus remains in close association with the endoplasmic reticulum. A number of proteins synthesised by ribosomes on the endoplasmic reticulum are modified in the cisternae of the golgi apparatus before they are released from its trans face. Golgi apparatus is the important site of formation of glycoproteins and glycolipids. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:134, paragraph no:1).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Lamellae, tubules and vesicles [2012]','Cisternae, tubules and vesicles','Cisternae, tubules and lamellae','Granum, thalykoids and vesicles','NEW','11-134','11-096',NULL),
(81028,'biology','Which chromosome may lost during cell division? [AIPMT 2012]','Acentric chromosome','Acentric chromosome may be lost during cell division as centromere is absent in them. During metaphase and anaphase, spindle fibres are not attached with them and so they are not able to reach to the poles.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:139, paragraph no:2).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Giant chromosome','Acentric chromosome','Polycentric chromosome','Telocentric chromosome','NEW','11-139','11-101',NULL),
(81029,'biology','Which of the following statements are correct?\n(i) In prokaryotic cells, a special membranous structure formed by the extension of the plasma membrane into the cell is known as polysome.\n(ii) The smooth endoplasmic reticulum is the major site for synthesis of glycoproteins.\n(iii) RuBisco is the most abundant protein in the whole biosphere.\n(iv) Mitochondria, chloroplasts and peroxisomes are not considered as part of endomembrane system.\n[NEET 2016]','(iii) and (iv)','The special membranous structure formed by the extension of prokaryotic plasma membrane is known as mesosome while polysome is structure formed by combination of many ribosomes. SER is the major site of synthesis of lipids. The site of protein synthesis is RER.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(iii) and (iv)','(i) and (ii)','(ii) and (iii)','(i) and (iv)','NEW','11-128','11-090',NULL),
(81030,'biology','Consider the following statements[AIIMS 2015]\n\ni. The shape of the cells may vary with the function they perform\n\nii. Human RBC is about 7.0 micro m in diameter \n\niii.Cytoplasm is the main arena of cellular activities\n\niv. Various chemical reactions occur in cytoplasm to keep the cell in the living state','All are correct','All the given statements are correct.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','All are correct','Only i and ii are correct','Only iv is correct','All are wrong','NEW','11-126','11-088',NULL),
(81101,'chemistry','When an electron from a lower energy d orbital is excited to a higher energy d orbital, the energy of excitation corresponds to-','the frequency of light absorbed.','When an electron from a lower energy d orbital is excited to a higher energy d orbital, the energy of excitation corresponds to the frequency of light absorbed.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 229; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Formation of Coloured Ions',NULL,'1','mcq','the frequency of light emitted.','the frequency of light absorbed.','the wavelength of light absorbed.','the wavelength of light emitted.','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(81102,'chemistry','The colour observed corresponds to the ____ colour of the light absorbed.','complementary','The colour observed corresponds to the complementary colour of the light absorbed.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 229; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Formation of Coloured Ions',NULL,'1','mcq','complementary','lighter shade','same','darker shade','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(81103,'chemistry','The frequency of light absorbed by the transition metal complex is dependent on-','Nature of ligands','The frequency of the light absorbed is determined by the nature of the ligand.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 229; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Formation of Coloured Ions',NULL,'2','mcq','Frequency of light falling on them.','Method used to produce the light','Nature of ligands','Source of light used','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(81104,'chemistry','Match the ions with their respective colors in the aqueous solution.','Sc⁺³-colorless, Mn⁺³-violet, Fe⁺³-yellow, Co⁺³-bluepink','Sc⁺³-colorless, Mn⁺³-violet, Fe⁺³-yellow, Co⁺³-bluepink is the correct match of ions and their colors. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 229; Table 8.8','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Formation of Coloured Ions',NULL,'3','match','Sc⁺³,colorless','Mn⁺³,violet','Fe⁺³,yellow','Co⁺³,bluepink','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(81105,'chemistry','Match the ions with their respective colors in the aqueous solution.','Mn⁺²-pink, Fe⁺²-green, V⁺²-violet, Cu⁺²-blue','Mn⁺²-pink, Fe⁺²-green, V⁺²-violet, Cu⁺²-blue is the correct match of ions with their colors. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 229; Table 8.8','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Formation of Coloured Ions',NULL,'3','match','Mn⁺²,pink','Fe⁺²,green','V⁺²,violet','Cu⁺²,blue','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(81106,'chemistry','Which one the following is expected to be colorless?','Zn⁺²','Zn⁺² is expected to be colourless because d-orbital is fully filled and there are no unpaired electrons in it.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 229; Table 8.8','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Formation of Coloured Ions',NULL,'2','mcq','Mn⁺²','Zn⁺²','Cr⁺²','Co⁺²','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(81107,'chemistry','Which metal solution is green in color?','Ni⁺²','Ni⁺² gives green colour in the aqueous solution.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 229; Table 8.8','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Formation of Coloured Ions',NULL,'1','mcq','Mn⁺²','Cu⁺²','V⁺²','Ni⁺²','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(81108,'chemistry','Which of the following ions solution is expected to be colorless?','Ti⁺⁴','Ti⁺⁴ is colourless because it has empty d-orbital.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 229; Table 8.8','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Formation of Coloured Ions',NULL,'2','mcq','Mn⁺³','Ti⁺⁴','Cr⁺²','V⁺⁴','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(81109,'chemistry','Select the pair of ions in which both ions are colored?','V⁺⁴, V⁺³','In Mn⁺³ and Zn⁺², Zn⁺² is colourless. In Ti⁺⁴ and V⁺⁴,Ti⁺⁴ is colourless. In  Sc⁺³ and Fe⁺³, Sc⁺³ is colourless. Therefore, V⁺⁴, V⁺³ is the only pair where both the ions are coloured. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 229; Table 8.8','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Formation of Coloured Ions',NULL,'2','mcq','Mn⁺³, Zn⁺²','V⁺⁴, V⁺³','Ti⁺⁴, V⁺⁴','Sc⁺³, Fe⁺³','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(81110,'chemistry','Which among the following salt might be present in water, if after dissolving the salt water turned blue?','CuSO₄','CuSO₄ might be present in water, if after dissolving the salt water turned blue. This is because of Cu²⁺ ions. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 229; Table 8.8','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Formation of Coloured Ions',NULL,'2','mcq','NaCl','TiCl₄','CuSO₄','KClO₄','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(81111,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding cell theory and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Schleiden and Schwann together formulated the cell theory. \n\n2. Schleiden and Schwann together explained as to how new cells were formed. \n\n3. Rudolf Virchow (1855) first explained that cells divided and new cells are formed from pre-existing cells (Omnis cellula-e cellula). \n\n4. The modified Cell theory concludes: all living organisms are composed of cells and products of cells only','both 1 and 3 are correct','Schleiden and Schwann together formulated the cell theory. This theory\nhowever, did not explain as to how new cells were formed. Rudolf Virchow\n(1855) first explained that cells divided and new cells are formed from\npre-existing cells (Omnis cellula-e cellula). He modified the hypothesis of\nSchleiden and Schwann to give the cell theory a final shape. Cell theory\nas understood today is:\n(i) all living organisms are composed of cells and products of cells.\n(ii) all cells arise from pre-existing cells.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:126, paragraph no: 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','only 3 is correct','both 2 and 3 are correct','only 1 is incorrect','both 1 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-126','11-088',NULL),
(81112,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding cell and choose the correct option:\n\n1. The onion cell which is a typical plant cell, has a distinct outermost boundary called cell membrane. \n\n2. The cells of the human cheek have an outer membrane as the delimiting structure of the cell.\n\n3. Inside each cell is a dense membrane bound structure called ribosome that helps in protien synthesis.\n\n4. The nucleus contains the chromosomes which in turn contain the genetic material, DNA.','both 1 and 3 are incorrect','The onion cell which is a typical plant cell, has a distinct cell wall as its outer boundary and just within it is the cell membrane.  (ribosomes are membraneless structure)The cells of the human cheek have an outer membrane as the delimiting structure of the cell. Inside each cell is a dense membrane bound structure called nucleus. This nucleus contains the chromosomes which in turn contain the genetic material, DNA.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:126, paragraph no: 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','both 1 and 3 are incorrect','both 1 and 2 are correct','both 2 and 3 are correct','only 3 is correct','NEW','11-126','11-088',NULL),
(81113,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding cell and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Cells that have membrane bound nuclei are called eukaryotic cells\n\n2. Cells that lack a membrane bound nucleus are prokaryotic cells \n\n3. Only eukaryotic cells have a semi-fluid matrix called cytoplasm.\n\n4. The cytoplasm is the main arena of cellular activities in both the plant and animal cells.','only 3 is incorrect','Cells that have membrane bound nuclei are called eukaryotic whereas cells that lack a membrane bound nucleus are prokaryotic. In both prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells, a semi-fluid matrix\ncalled cytoplasm occupies the volume of the cell. The cytoplasm is the main arena of cellular activities in both the plant and animal cells.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:126, paragraph no: 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','only 3 is incorrect','only 2 is incorrect','only 3 is correct','both 2 and 3 is incorrect','NEW','11-126','11-088',NULL),
(81114,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding cell and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Various chemical reactions occur in the cytoplasm to keep the cell in the ‘living state’.\n\n2. The eukaryotic cells have other membrane bound organelles \n\n3. Organelles that are membrane bound are endoplasmic reticulum (ER), the golgi complex, lysosomes, ribosomes, mitochondria, microbodies and vacuoles.\n\n4. Some prokaryotic cells have membrane bound organelles like lysosomes and vacuole','both 1 and 2 are correct','Various chemical reactions occur in cytoplasm to keep the cell in the ‘living state’.\nBesides the nucleus, the eukaryotic cells have other membrane bound distinct structures called organelles like the endoplasmic reticulum (ER), the golgi complex, lysosomes, mitochondria, microbodies and vacuoles.\nThe prokaryotic cells lack such membrane bound organelles. Ribosomes are non-membrane bound organelles found in all cells – both eukaryotic as well as prokaryotic.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:126, paragraph no: 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','both 2 and 3 are correct','both 1 and 4 are correct','both 1 and 2 are correct','only 4 is correct','NEW','11-126','11-088',NULL),
(81115,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding cell and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Ribosomes are non-membrane bound organelles found in all cells –both eukaryotic as well as prokaryotic. \n\n2. Within the cell, ribosomes are found not only in the cytoplasm but also within the two organelles – chloroplasts (in plants) and mitochondria and on rough ER.\n\n3. Centrosome is present in plant cell only \n\n4. Centrosomes help in protein and enzyme synthesis that is vital for a cell to perform its functions','both 3 and 4 are incorrect','Ribosomes are non-membrane bound organelles found in all cells –both eukaryotic as well as prokaryotic. Within the cell, ribosomes are found not only in the cytoplasm but also within the two organelles – chloroplasts (in plants) and mitochondria and on rough ER.\nAnimal cells contain another non-membrane bound organelle called centrosome which helps in cell division.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:126, paragraph no: 4)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','only 4 is correct','only 1 is incorrect','both 1 and 4 are incorrect','both 3 and 4 are incorrect','NEW','11-126','11-088',NULL),
(81116,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding cell and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Mycoplasmas, the smallest cells, are only 1.3 μm in length. \n\n2. The largest isolated single cell is the egg of an ostrich.\n\n3. Human red blood cells are about 7.0 μm in diameter. \n\n4. Nerve cells are some of the longest cells.','only 1 is incorrect','Mycoplasmas, the smallest cells, are only 0.3 μm in length while bacteria could be 3 to 5 μm. The largest isolated single cell is the egg of an ostrich. Among multicellular organisms, human red blood cells are about 7.0 μm in diameter. Nerve cells are some of the longest cells. Cells also vary greatly in their shape.\n\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:127, paragraph no: 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','only 1 is correct','only 3 is incorrect','only 1 is incorrect','only 4 is incorrect','NEW','11-127','11-089',NULL),
(81117,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding prokaryotic cells and choose the correct option:\n\n1. The prokaryotic cells are represented by bacteria, blue-green algae, mycoplasma and PPLO (Pleuro Pneumonia Like Organisms). \n\n2. They are generally smaller and multiply slower than the eukaryotic cells\n\n3. They may vary greatly in shape and size. \n\n4. The two basic shapes of bacteria are bacillus (rod like) and  coccus (spherical) only','both 2 and 4 are incorrect','The prokaryotic cells are represented by bacteria, blue-green algae, mycoplasma and PPLO (Pleuro Pneumonia Like Organisms). They are\ngenerally smaller and multiply more rapidly than the eukaryotic cells (Figure 8.2). They may vary greatly in shape and size. The four basic\nshapes of bacteria are bacillus (rod like), coccus (spherical), vibrio (comma shaped) and spirillum (spiral).\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:127, paragraph no: 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','both 2 and 4 are incorrect','only 2 is correct','both 2 and 3 are incorrect','only 4 is incorrect','NEW','11-127','11-089',NULL),
(81118,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding prokaryotic cells and choose the correct option:\n\n1. All the prokaryotic cells including mycoplasma have a cell wall surrounding the cell membrane.\n\n2. The fluid matrix filling the cell is the cytoplasm.\n\n3. There is well-defined nucleus. \n\n4. The genetic material is basically naked, not enveloped by a nuclear membrane.','both 2 and 4 are correct','All prokaryotes have a cell wall surrounding the cell membrane except in mycoplasma. The fluid\nmatrix filling the cell is the cytoplasm. There is no well-defined nucleus. The genetic material is\nbasically naked, not enveloped by a nuclear membrane.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:128, paragraph no: 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','both 2 and 4 are correct','both 1 and 4 are correct','both 3 and 4 are correct','only 1 is incorrect','NEW','11-128','11-090',NULL),
(81119,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding prokaryotic cells and choose the correct option:\n\n1. In the genomic DNA there are two chromosomes\n\n2. In prokaryotes there is presence of circular DNA in genome\n\n3. Many bacteria have small circular DNA outside the genomic DNA called plasmids. \n\n4. The plasmid DNA confers certain unique phenotypic characters to such bacteria like resistance to antibiotics.','2, 3 and 4 are correct','The genetic material is basically naked, not enveloped by a nuclearnmembrane. In addition to the genomic DNA (the single chromosome/circular DNA), many bacteria have small circular DNA outside the genomic DNA. These smaller DNA are called plasmids. The plasmid DNA confers certain unique phenotypic characters to such bacteria. One such character is resistance to antibiotics.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:128, paragraph no: 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','2, 3 and 4 are correct','only 1 is correct','both 2 and 3 are correct','only 4 is incorrect','NEW','11-128','11-090',NULL),
(81120,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding prokaryotic cells and choose the correct option:\n\n1. The plasmid DNA is used to monitor bacterial transformation with foreign DNA. \n\n2. No organelles, like the ones in eukaryotes, are found in prokaryotic cells except for mesosomes. \n\n3. Prokaryotes have something unique in the form of inclusions. \n\n4. Mesosomes are essentially infoldings of cell membrane.','only 2 is incorrect','The plasmid DNA is used to monitor bacterial transformation with foreign DNA. Nuclear membrane is found in eukaryotes. No organelles, like the ones in eukaryotes, are found in prokaryotic cells except for ribosomes. Prokaryotes have something unique in the form of inclusions. A specialised differentiated form of cell membrane called mesosome is the characteristic of prokaryotes. They are essentially infoldings of cell membrane.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:128, paragraph no: 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','only 2 is correct','both 3 and 4 are incorrect','only 3 is incorrect','only 2 is incorrect','NEW','11-128','11-090',NULL),
(81121,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option:\n\nAssertion: Most prokaryotic cells, particularly the bacterial cells, have a chemically complex cell envelope.\n\nReason:  The cell envelope consists of a tightly bound three layered structure i.e., the outermost glycocalyx followed by the cell wall and then the plasma membrane.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Most prokaryotic cells, particularly the bacterial cells, have a chemically\ncomplex cell envelope. The cell envelope consists of a tightly bound three\nlayered structure i.e., the outermost glycocalyx followed by the cell wall and\nthen the plasma membrane.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:128, paragraph no: 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-128','11-090',NULL),
(81122,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding prokaryotic cells and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Most prokaryotic cells, particularly the bacterial cells, have a chemically complex cell envelope.\n\n2. The cell envelope consists of a tightly bound three layered structure i.e., the outermost cell wall followed by the glycocalyx layer and then the plasma membrane.\n\n3. Bacteria can be classified on the basis of the differences in the cell membrane\n\n4. Gram positive and Gram negative bacteria on the basis of gram staining','both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Most prokaryotic cells, particularly the bacterial cells, have a chemically\ncomplex cell envelope. The cell envelope consists of a tightly bound three\nlayered structure i.e., the outermost glycocalyx followed by the cell wall and\nthen the plasma membrane. Although each layer of the envelope performs\ndistinct function, they act together as a single protective unit. Bacteria can\nbe classified into two groups on the basis of the differences in the cell envelopes\nand the manner in which they respond to the staining procedure developed\nby Gram viz., those that take up the gram stain are Gram positive and the\nothers that do not are called Gram negative bacteria.\n\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:128, paragraph no: 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','both 2 and 3 are incorrect','both 2 and 3 are correct','both 2 and 4 are incorrect','only 1 is incorrect','NEW','11-128','11-090',NULL),
(81123,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding prokaryotic cells and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Glycocalyx differs in composition and thickness among different bacteria. \n\n2. It could be a loose sheath called the slime layer in some, while in others it may be thick and tough, called the capsule. \n\n3. The cell wall determines the shape of the cell and provides a strong structural support to prevent the bacterium from bursting or collapsing.\n\n4. The plasma membrane is different in structure to that of the eukaryotes.','1, 2 and 3 are correct','Glycocalyx differs in composition and thickness among different\nbacteria. It could be a loose sheath called the slime layer in some, while\nin others it may be thick and tough, called the capsule. The cell wall\ndetermines the shape of the cell and provides a strong structural support\nto prevent the bacterium from bursting or collapsing.\nThe plasma membrane is selectively permeable in nature and interacts\nwith the outside world. This membrane is similar structurally to that of\nthe eukaryotes.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:128, paragraph no: 2, 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','only 3 is correct','only 1 is correct','both 1 and 4 are correct','1, 2 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-128','11-090',NULL),
(81124,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding prokaryotic cells and choose the CORRECT option:\n\n1. The plasma membrane isn\'t an selectively permeable in nature and interacts with the outside world. \n\n2. This membrane is similar structurally to that of the eukaryotes.\n\n3. A special membranous structure is the mesosome which is formed by the extensions of the cell wall into the cell. \n\n4. These extensions of mesosomes are in the form of vesicles only','only 2 is correct','The plasma membrane is selectively permeable in nature and interacts\nwith the outside world. This membrane is similar structurally to that of\nthe eukaryotes.\nA special membranous structure is the mesosome which is formed\nby the extensions of plasma membrane into the cell. These extensions are\nin the form of vesicles, tubules and lamellae. \n\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:128, paragraph no: 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','both 1 and 4 are correct','only 2 is correct','both1 and 3 are correct','only 3 is correct','NEW','11-128','11-090',NULL),
(81125,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding prokaryotic cells and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Mesosomes helps in cell wall formation, DNA replication and distribution to daughter cells.\n\n2. These extensions of mesosmes are in the form of vesicles, tubules and lamellae.\n\n3. In some prokaryotes like mycoplasma, there are other membranous extensions into the cytoplasm called chromatophores which contain pigments.\n\n4. Mesosomes also help in respiration, secretion processes, to increase the surface area of the plasma membrane and enzymatic content.','only 3 is incorrect','mesosomes helps in cell wall formation, DNA replication and distribution to daughter cells.\nThey also help in respiration, secretion processes, to increase the surface area of the plasma membrane and enzymatic content. In some prokaryotes like cyanobacteria, there are other membranous extensions into the cytoplasm\ncalled chromatophores which contain pigments.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:129, paragraph no: 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','only 2 is correct','only 3 is incorrect','only 1 is incorrect','both 1 and 3 are incorrect','NEW','11-129','11-091',NULL),
(81126,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option:\n\nAssertion: Bacterial cells can be motile.\n\nReason: If motile, they have thin filamentous extensions from their cell wall called flagella.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Bacterial cells may be motile or non-motile. If motile, they have thin\nfilamentous extensions from their cell wall called flagella. Bacteria show a\nrange in the number and arrangement of flagella.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:129, paragraph no: 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-129','11-091',NULL),
(81127,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding motility of bacteria and choose the correct option:\n\n1. All Bacterial cells are motile.\n\n2. Motile bacteria have thin filamentous extensions from their cytoplasm called flagella. \n\n3. Bacterial flagellum is composed of three parts – filament, hook and basal body. \n\n4. The hook is the longest portion and extends from the cell surface to the outside.','only 3 is correct','Bacterial cells may be motile or non-motile. If motile, they have thin filamentous extensions from their cell wall called flagella. Bacteria show a range in the number and arrangement of flagella. Bacterial flagellum is composed of three parts – filament, hook and basal body. The filament is the longest portion and extends from the cell surface to the outside.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:129, paragraph no: 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','only 3 is correct','only 1 is correct','only 2 is correct','both 2 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-129','11-091',NULL),
(81128,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding bacteria and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Besides flagella, Pili and Fimbriae are also surface structures of the bacteria that also plays a role in motility. \n\n2. The pili are elongated tubular structures made of a special protein. \n\n3. The fimbriae are long filamentous like fibres sprouting out of the cell. \n\n4. In some bacteria, they are known to help attach the bacteria to rocks in streams and also to the host tissues.','both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Besides flagella, Pili and Fimbriae are also surface structures of the bacteria but do not play a role in motility. The pili are elongated tubular structures made of a special protein. The fimbriae are small bristle like fibres sprouting out of the cell. In some bacteria, they are known to help attach the bacteria to rocks in streams and also to the host tissues.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:129, paragraph no: 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','both 1 and 2 are incorrect','only 1 is incorrect','both 1 and 3 are incorrect','both 2 and 3 are incorrect','NEW','11-129','11-091',NULL),
(81129,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding prokaryotic cells and choose the correct option:\n\n1. In prokaryotes, ribosomes are associated with the plasma membrane of the cell. \n\n2. They are made of two subunits - 60S and 40S units which when present together form 80S prokaryotic ribosomes. \n\n3. Ribosomes are the site of protein synthesis. \n\n4. Several ribosomes may attach to a single tRNA and form a chain called polyribosomes or polysome.','both 2 and 4 are incorrect','In prokaryotes, ribosomes are associated with the plasma membrane of\nthe cell. They are about 15 nm by 20 nm in size and are made of two\nsubunits - 50S and 30S units which when present together form 70S\nprokaryotic ribosomes. Ribosomes are the site of protein synthesis. Several\nribosomes may attach to a single mRNA and form a chain called\npolyribosomes or polysome. \n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:129, paragraph no: 4)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','both 1 and 3 are incorrect','both 3 and 4 are correct','both 2 and 4 are incorrect','only 1 is correct','NEW','11-129','11-091',NULL),
(81130,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding prokaryotic cells and choose the correct option:\n\n1. The ribosomes of a polysome translate the mRNA into proteins.\n\n2. Reserve material in prokaryotic cells are stored in the cytoplasm in the form of inclusion bodies. \n\n3. inclusion bodies are bounded membrane system.\n\n4. examples of inclusion bodies are: phosphate granules, cyanadian granules and glycogen granules. \n\n5. Gas vacuoles are found in blue green and purple and green photosynthetic bacteria.','both 3 and 4 are incorrect','The ribosomes of a polysome translate the mRNA into proteins.\nInclusion bodies: Reserve material in prokaryotic cells are stored in\nthe cytoplasm in the form of inclusion bodies.\nThese are not bound by any membrane system and lie free in the cytoplasm, e.g., phosphate granules, cyanophycean granules and glycogen granules. Gas vacuoles are found in blue green and purple and green photosynthetic bacteria.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:129, paragraph no: 5)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','only 1 is incorrect','only 3 is correct','only 3 is incorrect','both 3 and 4 are incorrect','NEW','11-129','11-091',NULL),
(81131,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Eukaryotic cells and choose the correct option:\n\n1. The eukaryotes include all the protists, plants and animals except fungi.\n\n2. In eukaryotic cells there is an extensive compartmentalisation of cytoplasm through the presence of membrane bound organelles. \n\n3. Eukaryotic cells possess an organised nucleus with a nuclear envelope.\n\n4. Eukaryotic cells do not have any locomotory structures.','both 2 and 3 are correct','The eukaryotes include all the protists, plants, animals and fungi. \nIn eukaryotic cells there is an extensive compartmentalisation of cytoplasm through the presence of membrane bound organelles. \nEukaryotic cells possess an organised nucleus with a nuclear envelope. In addition, eukaryotic cells have a variety of complex locomotory and cytoskeletal structures.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:129, paragraph no: 6)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','both 2 and 3 are correct','both 2 and 3 are incorrect','both 3 and 4 are incorrect','both 1 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-129','11-091',NULL),
(81132,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Eukaryotic cells and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Their genetic material is organised into chromosomes.\n\n2. All eukaryotic cells are identical.\n\n3. Plant and animal cells are different as the former possess cell walls, plastids and a large central vacuole which are absent in animal cells.\n\n4. On the other hand, plant cells have centrioles which are absent in almost all animal cells','both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Their genetic material is organised into chromosomes.\nAll eukaryotic cells are not identical. Plant and animal cells are different as the former possess cell walls, plastids and a large central vacuole which are absent in animal cells. \nOn the other hand, animal cells have centrioles which are absent in almost all plant cells\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:129, paragraph no: 6)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','both 2 and 4 are incorrect','both 3 and 4 are incorrect','both 1 and 4 are incorrect','only 1 is correct','NEW','11-129','11-091',NULL),
(81133,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding cell membrane and choose the correct option:\n\n1. The detailed structure of the membrane was studied only after the advent of the simple microscope in the 1980s. \n\n2. Chemical studies in human red blood cells (RBCs), enabled the scientists to deduce the possible structure of chromosomes.\n\n3. studies showed that the cell membrane is mainly composed of lipids and proteins.\n\n4. In cell membrane, major lipids are phospholipids that are arranged in a single layer.','1, 2 and 4 are incorrect','The detailed structure of the membrane was studied only after the advent\nof the electron microscope in the 1950s. Meanwhile, chemical studies on\nthe cell membrane, especially in human red blood cells (RBCs), enabled\nthe scientists to deduce the possible structure of plasma membrane.\nThese studies showed that the cell membrane is mainly composed of\nlipids and proteins. The major lipids are phospholipids that are arranged\nin a bilayer.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:131, paragraph no: 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','only 1 is correct','both 3 and 4 are incorrect','both 1 and 4 are incorrect','1, 2 and 4 are incorrect','NEW','11-131','11-093',NULL),
(31206,'chemistry','There are many elements which exhibit variable valence. This is particularly characteristic of ____ and ____ .','Transition metals, actinoids','There are many elements which exhibit variable valence. This is particularly characteristic of transition elements and actinoids. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 93; Paragraph no. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Oxidation states',NULL,'1','mcq','Representative element, Transition elements','Transition metals, actinoids','D block elements, S block elements','Lanthanoids, Representative elements','NEW','11-093','11-093',NULL),
(81134,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the cell membrane and choose the correct option:\n\n1. The lipids are arranged within the membrane with the polar head towards the outer sides and the hydrophobic tails towards the inner part.\n\n2. The nonpolar tail of saturated hydrocarbons is protected from the aqueous environment.\n\n3. Phospholipids membrane also contains cholesterol.\n\n4. Biochemical investigations clearly revealed that the cell membranes also possess protein and carbohydrate.','All are correct','In cell membrane, the major lipids are phospholipids that are arranged\nin a bilayer. Also, the lipids are arranged within the membrane with the\npolar head towards the outer sides and the hydrophobic tails towards\nthe inner part. This ensures that the nonpolar tail of saturated\nhydrocarbons is protected from the aqueous environment.\nIn addition to phospholipids membrane also contains cholesterol.\nLater, biochemical investigation clearly revealed that the cell membranes\nalso possess protein and carbohydrate.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:131, paragraph no: 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','All are correct','only 1 is correct','only 3 is correct','only 2 is incorrect','NEW','11-131','11-093',NULL),
(81135,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the Eukaryotic cell membrane and choose the correct option:\n\n1. The ratio of protein and lipid is similar in all cell types. \n\n2. In human beings, the membrane of the erythrocyte has approximately 40 % protein and 52 % lipids.\n\n3. Depending on the ease of extraction, membrane proteins can be classified as integral and peripheral. \n\n4. Peripheral proteins lie on the surface of membrane while the integral proteins are partially or totally buried in the membrane.','both 3 and 4 are correct','The ratio of protein and lipid varies considerably in different cell types. In human beings, the membrane of the\nerythrocyte has approximately 52 % protein and 40 % lipids.\nDepending on the ease of extraction, membrane proteins can be classified as integral and peripheral. Peripheral proteins lie on the surface of membrane while the integral proteins are partially or totally buried in\nthe membrane.\n\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:131, paragraph no: 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','both 3 and 4 are correct','both 1 and 3 are correct','both 1 and 2 are correct','only 1 is correct','NEW','11-131','11-093',NULL),
(81136,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option:\n\nAssertion: the lipids are arranged within the membrane with the polar head towards the outer sides and the hydrophobic tails towards the inner part.\n\nReason: This ensures that the nonpolar tail of saturated hydrocarbons is protected from the aqueous environment.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','In cell membrane, the major lipids are phospholipids that are arranged\nin a bilayer. Also, the lipids are arranged within the membrane with the\npolar head towards the outer sides and the hydrophobic tails towards\nthe inner part.This ensures that the nonpolar tail of saturated\nhydrocarbons is protected from the aqueous environment.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:131, paragraph no: 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-131','11-093',NULL),
(81137,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option:\n\nAssertion: In human beings, the membrane of the erythrocyte has approximately 52 % protein and 40 % lipids.\n\nReason: The ratio of protein and lipid varies considerably in different cell types','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The ratio of protein and lipid varies considerably in different cell types. In human beings, the membrane of the erythrocyte has approximately 52 % protein and 40 % lipids.\n\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:131, paragraph no: 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-131','11-093',NULL),
(81138,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the cell membrane and choose the correct option:\n\n1. An improved model of the structure of cell membrane was proposed by Singer and Nicolson in 1942.\n\n2. This model was widely accepted as fluid mosaic model\n\n3. According to this, the quasi-fluid nature of lipid enables lateral movement of proteins within the overall bilayer. \n\n4. This ability to move within the membrane is measured as its viscosity.','both 2 and 3 are correct','An improved model of the structure of cell membrane was proposed\nby Singer and Nicolson (1972) widely accepted as fluid mosaic model\n(Figure 8.4). According to this, the quasi-fluid nature of lipid enables\nlateral movement of proteins within the overall bilayer. This ability to move\nwithin the membrane is measured as its fluidity.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:132, paragraph no: 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','both 2 and 3 are correct','only 1 is correct','only 2 is correct','both 1 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-132','11-094',NULL),
(81139,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the cell membrane and choose the correct option:\n\n1. This ability to move within the membrane is measured as its fluidity.\n\n2. The fluid nature of the membrane is also important from the point of view of functions like cell growth and cell division only.\n\n3. One of the most important functions of the plasma membrane is the transport of the molecules across it.\n\n4. The membrane is selectively permeable to some molecules present on either side of it.','only 2 is incorrect','The fluid nature of the membrane is also important from the point of\nview of functions like cell growth, formation of intercellular junctions,\nsecretion, endocytosis, cell division etc.\nOne of the most important functions of the plasma membrane is the\ntransport of the molecules across it. The membrane is selectively permeable\nto some molecules present on either side of it. The membrane is selectively permeable\nto some molecules present on either side of it.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:132, paragraph no: 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','only 2 is incorrect','only 3 is incorrect','only 1 is incorrect','only 2 is correct','NEW','11-132','11-094',NULL),
(31207,'chemistry','Which among the following hydrides has doubtful existance?','InH₃','Periodic trends in valence of elements as shown by the formulas of their compounds are given in diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 93; diagram(ne0316)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Oxidation states',NULL,'2','mcq','B₂H₆','AlH₃','InH₃','CaH₂','NEW','11-093','11-093',NULL),
(81140,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the cell membrane and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Many molecules can move briefly across the membrane without any requirement of energy and this is called the active transport. \n\n2. Neutral solutes may move across the membrane by the process of simple diffusion along the concentration gradient, i.e., from lower concentration to the higher.\n\n3. Water may also move across this membrane from higher to lower concentration.\n\n4. Movement of water by diffusion is called osmosis.','both 3 and 4 are correct','Many molecules can move briefly across the membrane without any requirement of energy and this\nis called the passive transport. Neutral solutes may move across the\nmembrane by the process of simple diffusion along the concentration\ngradient, i.e., from higher concentration to the lower. Water may also move\nacross this membrane from higher to lower concentration. Movement of\nwater by diffusion is called osmosis.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:132, paragraph no: 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','only 1 is correct','both 3 and 4 are correct','only 2 is correct','only 4 is incorrect','NEW','11-132','11-094',NULL),
(81141,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the cell membrane and choose the correct option:\n\n1. As the polar molecules cannot pass through the nonpolar lipid bilayer, they require a carrier protein of the membrane to facilitate their transport across the membrane.\n\n2. A few ions or molecules are transported across the membrane against their concentration gradient, i.e., from lower to the higher concentration.\n\n3. When a  few ions or molecules are transported across the membrane against their concentration gradient a transport is an energy dependent process. \n\n4. Na+/K+ Pump is an example of a passive transport','only 4 is incorrect','As the polar molecules cannot pass through the nonpolar lipid bilayer, they require a carrier protein of the membrane to facilitate their transport across the membrane. A few ions\nor molecules are transported across the membrane against their\nconcentration gradient, i.e., from lower to the higher concentration. Such\na transport is an energy dependent process, in which ATP is utilised and\nis called active transport, e.g., Na+/K+ Pump.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:132, paragraph no: 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','only 2 is incorrect','only 4 is correct','only 1 is correct','only 4 is incorrect','NEW','11-132','11-094',NULL),
(81142,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option:\n\nAssertion:  Polar molecule require a carrier protein of the membrane to facilitate their transport across the membrane. \n\nReason: As the polar molecules cannot pass through the nonpolar lipid bilayer cell membrane.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','As the polar molecules cannot pass through the nonpolar lipid bilayer, they require a carrier protein of the membrane to facilitate their transport across the membrane. \n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:132, paragraph no: 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-132','11-094',NULL),
(81143,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the cell wall and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Cell wall is a living rigid structure \n\n2. Cell wall forms an outer covering for the plasma membrane of fungi and plants. \n\n3. Cell wall provides shape to the cell and protects the cell from mechanical damage and infection.\n\n4. It  helps in cell-to-membrane interaction','both 2 and 3 are correct','cell wall is a non-living rigid structure that forms an outer covering for the plasma membrane of fungi and plants. Cell wall not only gives shape to the cell and protects the cell from mechanical damage and infection, it also helps in cell-to-cell interaction and provides barrier to undesirable macromolecules. \n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:132, paragraph no: 4)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','both 2 and 4 are correct','onlly 2 is incorrect','only 1 is correct','both 2 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-132','11-094',NULL),
(81144,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the cell wall and choose the correct option:\n\n1. In the cell wall of a young plant cell, the primary wall is capable of growth, which gradually expands as the cell matures.\n\n2. The secondary wall is formed on the inner (towards membrane) side of the cell.\n\n3. The middle lamella is a layer mainly of calcium carbonate which holds or glues the different neighbouring cells together.\n\n4. The cell wall and middle lamellae may be traversed by plasmodesmata which connect the cytoplasm of neighbouring cells.','both 1 and 3 are incorrect','The cell wall of a young plant cell, the primary wall is capable of growth,\nwhich gradually diminishes as the cell matures and the secondary wall is\nformed on the inner (towards membrane) side of the cell.\nThe middle lamella is a layer mainly of calcium pectate which holds\nor glues the different neighbouring cells together. The cell wall and middle\nlamellae may be traversed by plasmodesmata which connect the cytoplasm\nof neighbouring cells.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:132, paragraph no: 4)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','both 1 and 3 are incorrect','both 1 and 4 are incorrect','both 1 and 2 are incorrect','only 1 is correct','NEW','11-132','11-094',NULL),
(81145,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the cell wall and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Cell wall gives shape to the cell and protects the cell from mechanical damage and infection.\n\n2. It also helps in cell-to-cell interaction \n\n3. It provides passage to undesirable macromolecules. \n\n4. Algae have cell wall, made of cellulose, chitin, mannans and minerals like calcium pectate.','both 1 and 2 are correct','Cell wall not only gives shape to the cell and protects the cell from mechanical\ndamage and infection, it also helps in cell-to-cell interaction and provides\nbarrier to undesirable macromolecules. Algae have cell wall, made of\ncellulose, galactans, mannans and minerals like calcium carbonate, while\nin other plants it consists of cellulose, hemicellulose, pectins and proteins.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:132, paragraph no: 4)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','both 1 and 3 are correct','both 1 and 2 are correct','only 4 is correct','both 2 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-132','11-094',NULL),
(31208,'chemistry','Which of the following is formula of group 16 oxides?','EO₃','Periodic trends in valence of elements as shown by the formulas of their compounds are given in diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 93; diagram(ne0316)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Oxidation states',NULL,'1','mcq','EO₄','EO₃','EO','E₂O₅','NEW','11-093','11-093',NULL),
(81146,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option:\n\nAssertion: The middle lamella is a layer mainly of calcium pectate \n\nReason: it holds or glues the different part of a cell together.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Middle lemella is the outermost layer of the cell wall in between cells. The middle lamella is a layer mainly of calcium pectate which holds or glues the different neighbouring cells together.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:132, paragraph no: 4)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-132','11-094',NULL),
(81147,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding endomembrane system and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Many of these membranous organelles are considered together as an endomembrane system because their functions are coordinated. \n\n2. The endomembrane system include endoplasmic reticulum (ER), golgi complex, mitochondria and vacuoles. \n\n3. Mitochondria is a part of endomembrane system because its function coordinates with other organelles \n\n4. Chloroplast is not considered as part of the endomembrane system.','both 1 and 4 are correct','While each of the membranous organelles is distinct in terms of its structure and function, many of these are considered together as an endomembrane system because their functions are coordinated. The endomembrane system include endoplasmic reticulum (ER), golgi complex, lysosomes and\nvacuoles. Since the functions of the mitochondria, chloroplast and peroxisomes are not coordinated with the above components, these are not considered as part of the endomembrane system.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:133, paragraph no: 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','both 1 and 4 are correct','only 2 is correct','only 1 is incorrect','both 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-133','11-095',NULL),
(81148,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option:\n\nAssertion: Vacuoles are a part of endomembrane system\n\nReason:  Structure and functions of  endoplasmic reticulum (ER), golgi complex, lysosomes and vacuoles are coordinated','Assertion is true but Reason is false','While each of the membranous organelles is distinct in terms of its structure and function, many of these are considered together as an endomembrane system because their functions are coordinated. The endomembrane system include endoplasmic reticulum (ER), golgi complex, lysosomes and\nvacuoles. Since the functions of the mitochondria, chloroplast and peroxisomes are not coordinated with the above components, these are not considered as part of the endomembrane system.\n\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:133, paragraph no: 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-133','11-095',NULL),
(81149,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option:\n\nAssertion: Mitochondria is a part of endomembrane system\n\nReason: Mitochondria does not have its own genetic material and its sole purpose is to provide energy to the cell','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','The endomembrane system include endoplasmic reticulum (ER), golgi complex, lysosomes and\nvacuoles. Since the functions of the mitochondria, chloroplast and peroxisomes are not coordinated with the above components, these are not considered as part of the endomembrane system.\n\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:133, paragraph no: 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-133','11-095',NULL),
(81150,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding endoplasmic reticulum and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Electron microscopic studies of eukaryotic cells reveal the presence of a network or reticulum of tiny tubular structures scattered in the cytoplasm that is called the endoplasmic reticulum (ER).\n\n2. ER divides the intracellular space into two distinct compartments, i.e., luminal  and intra luminal compartments.\n\n3. The ER often shows ribosomes attached to their outer surface. \n\n4. The endoplasmic reticulum bearing ribosomes on their surface is called rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER).','only 2 is incorrect','Electron microscopic studies of eukaryotic cells reveal the presence of a network or reticulum of tiny tubular structures scattered in the cytoplasm that is called the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) (Figure 8.5). Hence, ER divides the intracellular space into two distinct compartments, i.e., luminal (inside ER) and extra luminal (cytoplasm) compartments.\nThe ER often shows ribosomes attached to their outer surface. The endoplasmic reticulum bearing ribosomes on their surface is called rough\nendoplasmic reticulum (RER).\n\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:133, paragraph no: 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','only 2 is incorrect','only 4 is incorrect','only 1 is correct','only 3 is correct','NEW','11-133','11-095',NULL),
(81151,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding endoplasmic reticulum and choose the correct option:\n\n1. The endoplasmic reticulum bearing ribosomes on their surface is called rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER). \n\n2. In the absence of ribosomes they appear smooth and are called smooth endoplasmic reticulum (SER). \n\n3. RER is frequently observed in the cells actively involved in lipid synthesis and secretion.\n\n4. RER are extensive and continuous with the outer membrane of the lysosomes.','both 1 and 2 are correct','The endoplasmic reticulum bearing ribosomes on their surface is called rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER). In the absence of ribosomes they appear smooth and are called smooth endoplasmic reticulum (SER). RER is frequently observed in the cells actively involved in protein synthesis and secretion. They are extensive and continuous with the outer membrane of the nucleus.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:133, paragraph no: 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','only 2 is incorrect','both 1 and 2 are correct','both 2 and 3 are correct','only 1 is correct','NEW','11-133','11-095',NULL),
(31209,'chemistry','Maximum covalency of first member of each group is ____ .','4','In addition, the first member of group has only four valence orbitals (2s and 2p) available for bonding, whereas the second member of the groups have nine valence orbitals (3s, 3p, 3d). \n\nAs a consequence of this, the maximum covalency of the first member of each group is 4. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 93; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Oxidation states',NULL,'1','fillup','4,6,2,1',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-093','11-093',NULL),
(81152,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option:\n\nAssertion: Steroidal hormones are synthesised in SER.\n\nReason:  SER have smooth outer surface','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','In the absence of ribosomes they appear smooth and are called smooth endoplasmic reticulum (SER).\nThe smooth endoplasmic reticulum is the major site for synthesis of lipid. In animal cells lipid-like\nsteroidal hormones are synthesised in SER.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:133, paragraph no: 5)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-133','11-095',NULL),
(81153,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Golgi bodies the and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Camillo Golgi first observed lightly stained reticular structures near the nucleus. \n\n2. Camillo Golgi named those densely stained reticular structures near the nucleus, Golgi bodies \n\n3. Golgi bodies consist of many spiral, comma-shaped sacs or cisternae of 0.5μm to 1.0μm diameter \n\n4.  Varied number of cisternae are present in a Golgi complex.','both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Camillo Golgi (1898) first observed densely stained reticular structures near the nucleus. These were\nlater named Golgi bodies after him. They consist of many flat, disc-shaped sacs or cisternae of\n0.5μm to 1.0μm diameter (Figure 8.6). These are stacked parallel to each other. Varied number of\ncisternae are present in a Golgi complex.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:133, paragraph no: 6)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','both 1 and 3 are incorrect','both 1 and 4 are incorrect','both 1 and 2 are incorrect','only 2 is correct','NEW','11-133','11-095',NULL),
(81154,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Golgi bodies and choose the correct option:\n\n1. The Golgi cisternae are concentrically arranged near the nucleus.\n\n2. Golgi cisternae has a convex cis or the maturing face and a concave trans or the forming face.\n\n3. The cis and the trans faces of the organelle are entirely different, but interconnected.\n\n4. Materials to be packaged in the form of vesicles from the ER fuse with the trans face of the golgi apparatus','both 1 and 3 are correct','The Golgi cisternae are concentrically arranged near the nucleus with distinct convex cis or the forming face and concave trans or the maturing face. The cis and the trans faces of the organelle are entirely different, but interconnected.\nThe golgi apparatus principally performs the function of packaging materials, to be delivered either to the intra-cellular targets or secreted outside the cell. Materials to be packaged in the form of vesicles from the ER fuse with the cis face of the golgi apparatus and move towards the maturing face.\n\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:133, paragraph no: 6)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','both 1 and 3 are incorrect','only 4 is correct','both 1 and 3 are correct','both 1 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-133','11-095',NULL),
(81155,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Golgi bodies and choose the correct option:\n\n1. The golgi apparatus principally performs the function of packaging materials.\n\n2. These packaged materials are delivered to only intra-cellular targets \n\n3. Materials to be packaged in the form of vesicles from the ER fuse with the cis face of the golgi apparatus and move towards the maturing face.\n\n4. The golgi apparatus and endoplasmic reticulum are at different sites apart from each other.','both 2 and 4 are incorrect','The golgi apparatus principally performs the function of packaging materials, to be delivered either to the intra-cellular targets or secreted outside the cell. Materials to be packaged in the form of vesicles from the ER fuse with the cis face of the golgi apparatus and move towards the maturing face.\n\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:134, paragraph no: 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','both 2 and 3 are incorrect','both 2 and 4 are incorrect','only 3 is incorrect','only 4 is correct','NEW','11-134','11-096',NULL),
(81156,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option:\n\nAssertion: Golgi apparatus remains in close association with the endoplasmic reticulum.\n\nReason: A number of proteins synthesised by ribosomes on the endoplasmic reticulum are modified in the cisternae of the golgi apparatus before they are released from its trans face.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','the golgi apparatus remains in close association with the endoplasmic reticulum. A number of proteins synthesised by ribosomes on the endoplasmic reticulum are modified in the cisternae of the golgi apparatus before they are released from its trans face.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:134, paragraph no: 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-134','11-096',NULL),
(81157,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Golgi bodies and choose the correct option:\n\n1. The golgi apparatus remains in close association with the endoplasmic reticulum. \n\n2. A number of proteins synthesised by ribosomes on the endoplasmic reticulum are modified in the cisternae of the golgi apparatus.\n\n3. The packaged material from the golgi apparatus is released from its cis face. \n\n4. Golgi apparatus is the important site of formation of cholesterol and lipids.','both 1 and 2 are correct','the golgi apparatus remains in close association with the endoplasmic reticulum. A number of proteins synthesised by ribosomes on the endoplasmic reticulum are modified in the cisternae of the golgi apparatus before they are released from its trans face. Golgi apparatus is the important site of formation of glycoproteins and glycolipids.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:134, paragraph no: 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','both 1 and 4 are correct','only 3 is correct','only 2 is correct','both 1 and 2 are correct','NEW','11-134','11-096',NULL),
(81201,'chemistry','Which is not the favourable factor for formation of complex by transition metals?','Low ionic charges','The transition metals form a large number of complex compounds. This is due to the comparatively smaller sizes of the metal ions, their high ionic charges and the availability of d orbitals for bond formation.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 230; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Formation of Complex Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','Smaller size of metal ions','High ionic charges','Availability of d-orbitals','Low ionic charges','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(81202,'chemistry','Find the odd one with respect to complex compounds.','CaOCl₂','Complex compounds are those in which the metal ions bind a number of anions or neutral molecules giving complex species with characteristic properties.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 229; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Formation of Complex Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','Ni(CO)₄','[Cu(NH₃)₄]⁺²','CaOCl₂','[Pt(Cl)₄]²⁻','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(81203,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding vacuoles and choose the correct option:\n\n1. In plants, the tonoplast facilitates the transport of a number of ions and other materials against concentration gradients into the vacuole.\n\n2. the concentration is significantly lower in the vacuole than in the cytoplasm.\n\n3. In Amoeba the contractile vacuole is important for osmoregulation and excretion.\n\n4. In many cells, as in protists, food vacuoles are formed by engulfing the food particles.','only 2 is incorrect','In plants, the tonoplast facilitates the transport of a number of ions and other materials against concentration gradients into the vacuole, hence their concentration is significantly higher in the vacuole than in the cytoplasm.\nIn Amoeba the contractile vacuole is important for osmoregulation and excretion. In many cells, as in protists, food vacuoles are formed by engulfing the food particles.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:134, paragraph no: 4, 5)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','both 3 and 4 are incorrect','only 2 is incorrect','only 1 is correct','both 2 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-134','11-096',NULL),
(81204,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding mitochondria and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Mitochondria is easily stained, and are easily visible under the microscope.\n\n2.  The number of mitochondria per cell is variable depending on the physiological activity of the cells. \n\n3. They are similar in terms of shape and size.\n\n4. Typically it is sausage-shaped or cylindrical having a diameter of 0.2-1.0μm (average 0.5μm) and length 1.0-4.1μm.','both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Mitochondria (sing.: mitochondrion), unless specifically stained, are not\neasily visible under the microscope. The number of mitochondria per cell\nis variable depending on the physiological activity of the cells. In terms of\nshape and size also, considerable degree of variability is observed. Typically\nit is sausage-shaped or cylindrical having a diameter of 0.2-1.0μm (average\n0.5μm) and length 1.0-4.1μm.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:134, paragraph no: 6)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','both 1 and 3 are incorrect','both 1 and 2 are incorrect','both 1 and 4 are incorrect','only 3 is correct','NEW','11-134','11-096',NULL),
(81205,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding mitochondria and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Each mitochondrion is a double membrane-bound structure with the outer membrane and the inner membrane.\n\n2. The inner compartment is filled with a dense homogeneous substance called the matrix.\n\n3. The outer membrane forms the continuous limiting boundary of the organelle. \n\n4. The inner membrane forms a number of infoldings called the cisternae towards the matrix.','only 4 is incorrect','Each mitochondrion is a double membrane-bound structure with the outer membrane and the inner\nmembrane dividing its lumen distinctly into two aqueous compartments,\ni.e., the outer compartment and the inner compartment. The inner\ncompartment is filled with a dense homogeneous substance called the\nmatrix. The outer membrane forms the continuous limiting boundary of\nthe organelle. The inner membrane forms a number of infoldings called\nthe cristae (sing.: crista) towards the matrix. (cistermae are present in golgi bodies)\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:135, paragraph no: 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','only 1 is incorrect','both 1 and 3 are correct','only 4 is incorrect','1, 2 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-135','11-097',NULL),
(81206,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding mitochondria and choose the correct option:\n\n1. The cristae increase the surface area. \n\n2. The two membranes have their own specific enzymes associated with the mitochondrial function. \n\n3. Mitochondria are the sites of aerobic respiration as well as anaerobic respiration.\n\n4. They produce cellular energy in the form of ATP, hence they are called ‘power houses’ of the cell.','only 3 is incorrect','The cristae increase the surface area. The two membranes have their own specific enzymes associated with the mitochondrial function. Mitochondria are the sites of aerobic respiration. They produce cellular energy in the form of ATP, hence they are called ‘power houses’ of the cell. The matrix also possesses single circular DNA molecule, a few RNA molecules, ribosomes (70S) and the components required for the synthesis of proteins. The mitochondria divide by fission.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:135, paragraph no: 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','only 3 is incorrect','only 2 is incorrect','both 2 and 3 are incorrect','only 4 is correct','NEW','11-135','11-097',NULL),
(81207,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding mitochondria and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Mitochondria are the sites of aerobic respiration.\n\n2. The matrix possesses double circular DNA molecule\n\n3. The matrix possesses a few RNA molecules, ribosomes (80S) and the components required for the synthesis of proteins.\n\n4. The mitochondria divide by fission.','both 1 and 4 are correct','Mitochondria are the sites of aerobic respiration.They produce cellular energy in the form of ATP, hence they are called ‘power houses’ of the cell. \nThe matrix also possesses single circular DNA molecule, a few RNA molecules, ribosomes (70S) and the components required for the synthesis of proteins. The mitochondria divide by fission.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:135, paragraph no: 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','both 1 and 4 are correct','both 1 and 2 are correct','both 1 and 4 are incorrect','only 2 is correct','NEW','11-135','11-097',NULL),
(81208,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding plastid and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Plastids are found in all plant cells but not in euglenoides. \n\n2. These are easily observed under the microscope as they are large.\n\n3. They bear some specific pigments, thus imparting specific colours to the plants. \n\n4. Based on the specific functions, plastids can be classified into chloroplasts, chromoplasts and leucoplasts.','both 2 and 3 are correct','Plastids are found in all plant cells and in euglenoides. These are easily\nobserved under the microscope as they are large. They bear some specific\npigments, thus imparting specific colours to the plants. Based on the\ntype of pigments plastids can be classified into chloroplasts,\nchromoplasts and leucoplasts.\n\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:135, paragraph no: 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','both 2 and 3 are correct','both 2 and 4 are correct','both 2 and 3 are incorrect','only 1 is correct','NEW','11-135','11-097',NULL),
(81209,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding plastid and choose the correct option:\n\n1. The chloroplasts contain chlorophyll and carotenoid pigments which are responsible for trapping light energy essential for photosynthesis. \n\n2. In the chromoplasts fat soluble carotenoid pigments like carotene, xanthophylls and others are present. \n\n3. The leucoplasts are the red in colour and are present in varied shapes and sizes with stored nutrients. \n\n4. Amyloplasts store oils and fats.','both 3 and 4 are incorrect','The chloroplasts contain chlorophyll and carotenoid pigments which are responsible for trapping light energy essential for photosynthesis. In the chromoplasts fat soluble carotenoid pigments like carotene, xanthophylls and others are present. This gives the part of the plant a yellow, orange or red colour. The leucoplasts are the colourless plastids of varied shapes and sizes with stored nutrients: Amyloplasts store carbohydrates (starch), e.g., potato; elaioplasts store oils and fats whereas the aleuroplasts store proteins.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:135, paragraph no: 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','both 3 and 4 are correct','both 1 and 4 are incorrect','both 3 and 4 are incorrect','only 4 is correct','NEW','11-135','11-097',NULL),
(81210,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding plastid and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Majority of the chloroplasts of the green plants are found in the mesophyll cells of the leaves.\n\n2. These are only lens-shaped organelles having variable length (5-10μm) and width (2-4μm).\n\n3. The number of chloroplasts in Chlamydomonas is 10 per cell\n\n4. The number of chloroplasts in green alga is 20-40 per cell in the mesophyll.','both 1 and 4 are correct','Majority of the chloroplasts of the green plants are found in the mesophyll cells of the leaves. These are lens-shaped, oval, spherical, discoid or even ribbon-like organelles having variable length (5-10μm) and width (2-4μm). Their number varies from 1 per cell of the Chlamydomonas, a\ngreen alga to 20-40 per cell in the mesophyll.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:136, paragraph no: 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','both 1 and 3 are correct','both 1 and 2 are correct','both 1 and 4 are correct','only 4 is incorrect','NEW','11-136','11-098',NULL),
(81211,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding plastid and choose the correct option:\n\n1. The chloroplasts are also double membrane bound. \n\n2. The inner membrane of the chloroplast membrane is relatively more permeable.\n\n3. The space limited by the inner membrane of the chloroplast is called the matrix.\n\n4. Thylakoids are arranged in stacks like the piles of coins called grana','both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Like mitochondria, the chloroplasts are also double membrane bound. Of the two, the inner chloroplast membrane is relatively less permeable. The space limited by the inner membrane of the chloroplast is called the stroma. A number of organised flattened membranous sacs called the thylakoids, are present in the stroma. Thylakoids are arranged in stacks like the piles of coins called\ngrana (singular: granum) or the intergranal thylakoids.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:136, paragraph no: 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','both 2 and 4 are incorrect','only 4 is correct','both 2 and 3 are correct','both 2 and 3 are incorrect','NEW','11-136','11-098',NULL),
(81212,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding plastid and choose the correct option:\n\n1. There are flat membranous tubules called the stroma lamellae connecting the thylakoids of the different grana.\n\n2. The membrane of the thylakoids enclose a space called a stroma. \n\n3. The stroma of the chloroplast contains enzymes required for the synthesis of carbohydrates and proteins. \n\n4. It also contains small, single-stranded circular DNA molecules and ribosomes.','both 1 and 3 are correct','there are flat membranous tubules called the stroma lamellae connecting the thylakoids of the different grana. The membrane of the thylakoids enclose a space called a lumen. The stroma of the chloroplast contains enzymes required for the synthesis of carbohydrates and proteins. It also contains small, double- stranded circular DNA molecules and ribosomes. Chlorophyll pigments are present in the thylakoids. The ribosomes of the chloroplasts are smaller (70S) than the cytoplasmic ribosomes (80S).\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:136, paragraph no: 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','both 2 and 4 are correct','only 2 is incorrect','both 1 and 3 are correct','only 1 is correct','NEW','11-136','11-098',NULL),
(81213,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding ribosomes and choose the correct option:\n\n1. They are composed of ribonucleic acid (RNA) and proteins \n\n2. They are surrounded by a membrane. \n\n3. The eukaryotic ribosomes are 80S while the prokaryotic ribosomes are 70S. \n\n4. The two subunits of 80S ribosomes are 60S and 30S while that of 70S ribosomes are 50S and 20S.','both 1 and 3 are correct','Ribosomes are the granular structures first observed under the electron microscope as dense particles by George Palade (1953). They are composed of ribonucleic acid (RNA) and proteins and\nare not surrounded by any membrane. The eukaryotic ribosomes are 80S while the\nprokaryotic ribosomes are 70S. Each ribosome has two subunits, larger and smaller subunits. The two subunits of 80S ribosomes are 60S and 40S while that of 70S ribosomes are 50S and 30S. Here ‘S’ (Svedberg’s Unit) stands for the sedimentation coefficient; it is indirectly a measure of density and size. Both 70S and 80S ribosomes are composed of two subunits.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:136, paragraph no: 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','both 2 and 4 are correct','only 2 is incorrect','both 1 and 3 are correct','only 1 is correct','NEW','11-136','11-098',NULL),
(81214,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the cytoskeleton of a cell and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Cytoskeleton are filamentous proteinaceous structures.\n\n2. It consists of microtubules, microfilaments and intermediate filaments present in the cytoplasm which are collectively referred to as the cytoskeleton. \n\n3. The cytoskeleton in a cell provides mechanical support to a cell\n\n4. It does not have any role in motility but maintains the shape of the cell.','only 4 is incorrect','An elaborate network of filamentous proteinaceous structures consisting of microtubules, microfilaments and intermediate filaments present in the cytoplasm is collectively referred to as the cytoskeleton. The cytoskeleton in a cell are involved in many functions such as mechanical\nsupport, motility, maintenance of the shape of the cell.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:136, paragraph no: 4)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','only 1 is incorrect','both 2 and 4 are correct','only 4 is incorrect','only 2 is incorrect','NEW','11-136','11-098',NULL),
(2000038205,'physics','Critical temperature of $C{O_2}$ is $31.2^\\circ C$. In summer, the room temperature is $40^\\circ C$, then','A','Gases cannot be liquified above critical temperature, however large the pressure may be hence $C{O_2}$ cannot be liquified.','Behaviour of Gases','Kinetic Theory Of Gases','Hard',NULL,'$C{O_2}$ cannot be liquified','$C{O_2}$ can be liquified with increase of pressure','$C{O_2}$ can be liquified with decrease of pressure','$C{O_2}$ can be liquified, if temperature of $C{O_2}$ is decreased below $31.2^\\circ C$',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(81215,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding cilli and flagella, and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Cilia and flagella are hair-like outgrowths of the endoplasmic reticulum.\n\n2. Cilia are small structures that work like oars, causing the movement of either the cell or the surrounding fluid.\n\n3. Flagella are comparatively longer and responsible for cell movement. \n\n4. The prokaryotic bacteria also possess flagella that are structurally similar to that of the eukaryotic flagella.','both 2 and 3 are correct','Cilia (sing.: cilium) and flagella (sing.: flagellum) are hair-like outgrowths of the cell membrane. Cilia are small structures which work like oars, causing the movement of either the cell or the surrounding fluid. Flagella are comparatively longer and responsible for cell movement. The prokaryotic bacteria also possess flagella but these are structurally different from that of the eukaryotic flagella.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:137, paragraph no: 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','both 2 and 3 are correct','both 2 and 1 are correct','Both 1 and 4 are correct','only 2 is incorrect','NEW','11-137','11-099',NULL),
(81216,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding cilli and flagella, and choose the correct option:\n\n1. A cilium or the flagellum are uncovered, ie. that they are not covered with any membrane or sheath. \n\n2. Their core called the axoneme, possesses a number of microtubules running parallel to the long axis. \n\n3. The axoneme usually has nine doublets of radially arranged peripheral microtubules, and a pair of centrally located microtubules. \n\n4. Both the cilium and flagellum emerge from centriole-like structure called spindle fibre.','both 1 and 4 are incorrect','The electron microscopic study of a cilium or the flagellum show that they are covered with plasma membrane. Their core called the axoneme, possesses a number of microtubules running parallel to the long axis. The axoneme usually has nine doublets of radially arranged peripheral microtubules, and a pair of centrally located microtubules. Such an arrangement of axonemal microtubules is referred to as the 9+2 array. Both the cilium and flagellum emerge from centriole-like structure called the basal bodies.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:137, paragraph no: 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','both 1 and 2 are incorrect','both 1 and 4 are incorrect','both 1 and 3 are incorrect','both 1 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-137','11-099',NULL),
(81217,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding cilli and flagella, and choose the correct option:\n\n1. The arrangement of axonemal microtubules is referred to as the 9+2 array. \n\n2. The central tubules are connected by bridges and is also enclosed by a central sheath, which is connected to one of the tubules of each peripheral doublets by a radial spoke. \n\n3. There are 4 radial spokes. \n\n4. The peripheral doublets are also interconnected by radial spokes.','both 3 and 4 are incorrect','The axoneme usually has nine doublets of radially arranged peripheral microtubules, and a pair of centrally located microtubules. Such an arrangement of axonemal microtubules is referred to as the 9+2 array. The central tubules are connected by bridges and is also enclosed by a central sheath, which is connected to one of the tubules of each peripheral doublets by a radial spoke. Thus, there are nine radial spokes. The peripheral doublets are also interconnected by linkers. Both the cilium and flagellum emerge from centriole-like structure called the basal bodies.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:137, paragraph no: 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','both 2 and 4 are correct','both 1 and 4 are incorrect','both 3 and 4 are correct','both 3 and 4 are incorrect','NEW','11-137','11-099',NULL),
(81218,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding centrosome, and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Centrosome is an organelle usually containing two cylindrical structures called centrioles. \n\n2. They are surrounded by amorphous pericentriolar materials. \n\n3. Both the centrioles in a centrosome lie parallel to each other in which each has an organisation like the cartwheel.\n\n4. They are made up of eight evenly spaced peripheral fibrils of tubulin protein.','both 1 and 2 are correct','Centrosome is an organelle usually containing two cylindrical structures called centrioles. They are surrounded by amorphous pericentriolar materials. Both the centrioles in a centrosome lie perpendicular to each other in which each has an organisation like the cartwheel. They are made up of nine evenly spaced peripheral fibrils of tubulin protein.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:137, paragraph no: 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','both 1 and 2 are incorrect','both 1 and 3 are correct','both 1 and 2 are correct','only 2 is correct','NEW','11-137','11-099',NULL),
(81219,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding centrosome and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Each of the peripheral fibril is a doublet.\n\n2. The adjacent triplets are also linked.\n\n3. The central part of the proximal region of the centriole is also proteinaceous and called central tubule.\n\n4. The centrioles form the basal body of cilia or flagella, and spindle fibres that give rise to spindle apparatus during cell division in animal cells.','both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Each of the peripheral fibril is a triplet.The adjacent triplets are also linked.\nThe central part of the proximal region of the centriole is also proteinaceous and called the hub, which is connected with tubules of the peripheral triplets by radial spokes made of protein. The centrioles form the basal body of cilia or flagella, and spindle fibres that give rise to spindle\napparatus during cell division in animal cells.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:138, paragraph no: 5)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','both 1 and 2 are incorrect','both 1 and 3 are incorrect','only 1 is correct','both 1 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-138','11-100',NULL),
(81220,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding nucleus and choose the correct option:\n\n1. Nucleus as a cell organelle was first described by Robert Whitteker as early as 1831. \n\n2. Later the material of the nucleus stained by the basic dyes was given the name chromatin by Flemming.\n\n3. The interphase nucleus is the phase where nucleus of a cell when it is dividing\n\n4. In interphase, the nucleus has highly extended and elaborate nucleoprotein fibres called chromatin, nuclear matrix and one or more spherical bodies called nucleoli','both 2 and 4 are correct','Nucleus as a cell organelle was first described by Robert Brown as early\nas 1831. Later the material of the nucleus stained by the basic dyes was\ngiven the name chromatin by Flemming.\nThe interphase nucleus (nucleus of a cell when it is not dividing) has highly\nextended and elaborate nucleoprotein fibres called chromatin, nuclear matrix\nand one or more spherical bodies called nucleoli\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:138, paragraph no: 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','both 2 and 4 are correct','both 2 and 3 are correct','both 2 and 4 are incorrect','only 3 is correct','NEW','11-138','11-100',NULL),
(2000038299,'physics','The venturi-meter works on:','A','Venturimeter works on Bernoulli\'s principle. Correct option is (1).','Bernoulli’s Principle','Mechanical Properties of Fluids','Easy',NULL,'Bernoulli\'s principle','The principle of parallel axes','The principle of perpendicular axes','Huygen\'s principle',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(81221,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding nucleus and choose the correct option:\n\n1. The nuclear envelope consists of two parallel membranes with a space between called the subnuclear space.\n\n2. Nuclear envelop forms a barrier between the materials present inside the nucleus and that of the cytoplasm. \n\n3. The outer membrane usually remains continuous with the endoplasmic reticulum and also bears ribosomes on it.\n\n4.  At a number of places the nuclear envelope do not have any kind of pores.','both 1 and 4 are incorrect','Electron microscopy has revealed that the nuclear envelope, which consists of two parallel membranes with a space between (10 to 50 nm) called the perinuclear space, forms a barrier between the materials present inside the nucleus and that of the cytoplasm. The outer membrane usually remains continuous with the endoplasmic reticulum and also bears ribosomes on it. At a number of places the nuclear envelope is interrupted by minute pores, which are formed by the fusion of its two membranes. These nuclear pores are the passages through which movement of RNA and protein molecules takes place in both directions between the nucleus and the cytoplasm.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:138, paragraph no: 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','both 1 and 4 are incorrect','both 2 and 4 are correct','both 2 and 4 are incorrect','only 3 is correct','NEW','11-138','11-100',NULL),
(81222,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding nucleus and choose the correct option:\n\n1. The nuclear matrix or the nucleoplasm contains nucleolus and chromatin. \n\n2. The nucleoli are spherical structures present in the cytoplasm. \n\n3. The content of nucleolus is continuous with the rest of the nucleoplasm as it is a membrane bound structure. \n\n4. It is a site for active ribosomal RNA synthesis.','both 2 and 3 are incorrect','The nuclear matrix or the nucleoplasm contains nucleolus and chromatin. The nucleoli are spherical structures present in the nucleoplasm. The content of nucleolus is continuous with the rest of the nucleoplasm as it is not a membrane bound structure. It is a site for active ribosomal RNA synthesis. Larger and more numerous nucleoli are present in cells actively carrying out protein synthesis.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:138, paragraph no: 5)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','only 2 is correct','both 2 and 4 are incorrect','both 2 and 3 are correct','both 2 and 3 are incorrect','NEW','11-138','11-100',NULL),
(81223,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding nucleus and choose the correct option:\n\n1. The interphase nucleus has a loose and indistinct network of nucleoprotein fibres called chromatin.\n\n2. Chromatin contains DNA and some basic proteins called histones, some non-histone proteins and also RNA. \n\n3. A single human cell has approximately two centimeter long thread of DNA.\n\n4. There are forty six (twenty three pairs) chromosomes in human cell.','only 3 is incorrect','You may recall that the interphase nucleus has a loose\nand indistinct network of nucleoprotein fibres called\nchromatin. But during different stages of cell division, cells\nshow structured chromosomes in place of the nucleus.\nChromatin contains DNA and some basic proteins called\nhistones, some non-histone proteins and also RNA. A\nsingle human cell has approximately two metre long\nthread of DNA distributed among its forty six (twenty three\npairs) chromosomes.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:139, paragraph no: 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','only 3 is incorrect','only 4 is incorrect','only 1 is incorrect','both 1 and 2 are incorrect','NEW','11-139','11-101',NULL),
(81224,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding nucleus and choose the correct option:\n\n1. In a dividing cell, each chromosome has a primary constriction or the centromere on the sides of which disc shaped structures called kinetochores are present. \n\n2. Centromere holds two chromatids of a chromosome. \n\n3. Based on the position of the centromere, the chromosomes can be classified into three types\n\n4. In metacentric chromosome, centromere is situated close to its end forming one extremely short and one very long arm,','both 1 and 2 are correct','Every chromosome (visible only in dividing cells)\nessentially has a primary constriction or the centromere\non the sides of which disc shaped structures called\nkinetochores are present (Figure 8.12). Centromere holds\ntwo chromatids of a chromosome. Based on the position\nof the centromere, the chromosomes can be classified into\nfour types (Figure 8.13). The metacentric chromosome\nhas middle centromere forming two equal arms of the\nchromosome.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:139, paragraph no: 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','both 1 and 2 are correct','only 3 is incorrect','only 4 is incorrect','both 1 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-139','11-101',NULL),
(81225,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the nucleus and choose the correct option:\n\n1. The metacentric chromosome has middle centromere forming two equal arms of the chromosome. \n\n2. The sub-metacentric chromosome has centromere situated close to its end forming one extremely short and one very long arm, whereas the telocentric chromosome has a terminal  centromere. \n\n3. In acrocentric chromosome the centromere is slightly away from the middle of the chromosome resulting into one shorter arm and one longer arm. \n\n4. Sometimes a few chromosomes have non-staining secondary constrictions at a constant location, that gives the appearance of a small fragment called the telomere.','only 1 is correct','The metacentric chromosome\nhas middle centromere forming two equal arms of the\nchromosome. The sub-metacentric chromosome has\ncentromere slightly away from the middle of the\nchromosome resulting into one shorter arm and one\nlonger arm. In case of acrocentric chromosome the\ncentromere is situated close to its end forming one\nextremely short and one very long arm, whereas the\ntelocentric chromosome has a terminal centromere. Sometimes a few chromosomes have non-staining secondary\nconstrictions at a constant location. This gives the appearance of a small\nfragment called the satellite.\n\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:139, paragraph no: 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Cell : The Unit Of Life >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','only 1 is incorrect','only 2 is incorrect','only 1 is correct','1,2 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-139','11-101',NULL),
(110101,'chemistry','The last electron in p-block enters the outermost','p-orbital','Last shell of p-block elements is L-shell ,which have two sub-\n shell(s,p) ,after completion of s-subshell,electrons are filled in p\n subshell.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 315; Para-\n graph No.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','d-orbital','p-orbital','s-orbital','f-orbital','NEW','11-315',NULL,NULL),
(110102,'chemistry','Maximum accomodated electron in p-subshell','six','p-subshell is divided into three orbitals and one orbital can have\n maximum two electrons,so p-subshell will have six electrons. \n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class11; Page 315; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','six','two','ten','fourteen','NEW','11-315',NULL,NULL),
(110103,'chemistry','Valence shell electronic configuration of p-block is','ns²np¹⁻⁶','last shell of p-block elements is L-shell ,which have two sub-\n shell(s,p) ,after completion of s-subshell,electrons are filled in p\n subshell. n stands for period no.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 315; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','ns²np¹','ns²np¹⁻⁶','ns²np¹','ns²np¹⁻³','NEW','11-315',NULL,NULL),
(110104,'chemistry','Inert pair effect increase','along group','In p-block, last subshell is p,and having electronic configuration\n ns2np1-6, as we move down the group, number of due to involment of d\n or f subshell which are diffused orbitals,it is difficult to remove electron \n from ns that\'s why this pair of electrons become inert and not easy to \n remove. Inert pair effect starts from third period and increase down the\n group.\n Reference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11;Page 315; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','along period','along group','bottom to top in a group','right to left in aperiod','NEW','11-315',NULL,NULL),
(110105,'chemistry','The number of oxidation state in p-block increase','along period','as we move left to right in periodic table,group No.increase,oxida-\n tion No. is related to group No.so by moving along period oxida-\n tion state increase.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 315; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','along period','along group','bottom to top in a group','right to left in aperiod','NEW','11-315',NULL,NULL),
(110106,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R)\n \n (A)-Oxidation state two unit less than the group oxidation state becomes stable for the heavier elements in the group\n \n (R)-inert pair effect prevents ns electrons to be removed','1-Both assertion and reason are true ,and reason is correct explanation of assertion','The inert pair effect is the reluctance of \'s\' electrons for participating in bonding.the oxidation state two unit less than the group oxidation state becomes progressively more stable for the heavier elements in each group\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 315; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Introduction',NULL,'3','mcq','1-Both assertion and reason are true ,and reason is correct explanation of assertion','2-Both assertion and reason are true ,but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','3-Assertion is true but reason is false','4-Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-315',NULL,NULL),
(110107,'chemistry','The non-metallic character of p-block elements decrease _________','along group','Non-metallic character depends on the ease of removal of electron\n more tightly the electrons are bound more will be the non-metallic \n character,as we move down the group,size of atoms increase,that\'s why\n hold of nucleus on outermost electrons decrease,electrons are losely\n bound and easy to remove,metallic character increase and non metallic\n character decrease.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 316; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','along group','along period','right to left in aperiod','bottom to top in a group','NEW','11-316',NULL,NULL),
(110109,'chemistry','The compounds formed by the highly reactive metals and highly reactive non-metals are','Ionic','Greater the electronegative difference ,greater will be the ionic charac-\n ter, non-metals are higly electronegative and metals are highly electro-\n positive and least electronegative,thier difference of electronegativity is\n highest.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 316; Paragraph No.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Ionic','Covalent','Metallic','Non-polar','NEW','11-316',NULL,NULL),
(110110,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R)\n (A)-Ionization energy is directly linked with electronegativity\n (R)-Smaller size ,difficult to remove electrons ,more electronegative','Both assertion and reason are true ,but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 316; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Introduction',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true ,and reason is correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true ,but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-316',NULL,NULL),
(110111,'chemistry','Non-metals mostly forms','Acidic oxide','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 316; Paragraph No.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Basic oxide','Acidic oxide','Amphoteric oxide','Neutral oxide','NEW','11-316',NULL,NULL),
(110112,'chemistry','The first member of p-block differs from remaining members of of group in','Both a and b','Down the group size increase as well as shell no.increase,which leads to\n increase in no.of subshell in higher members ,first member only have s or\n p subshell,while all others have s,p,d,or f,subshells\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 316; Paragraph No.4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Size','Absence of d-subshell','Both a and b','No.of electrons in last shell','NEW','11-316',NULL,NULL),
(110113,'chemistry','The most common multiple bonding in second period is','pㄫ-pㄫ','The first member of group forms stable multiple bonds to itself, this is \n due to ability of the elements to satisfy their valancy by itself, these \n elements have ability to hybridize and form equal energy orbitals to form \n sigma bond, the type of hybridization which they exibit is sp3,sp2,sp \n Reference :(NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 316; Paragraph No.4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','pㄫ-pㄫ','dㄫ-dㄫ','pㄫ-dㄫ','sㄫ-dㄫ','NEW','11-316',NULL,NULL),
(110201,'chemistry','All members of group 13 are metals except','B','Boron is typical non-metal while all other members are metal, Boron \n being small in size and hard packing favours to add electron which is \n property of non-metal. As we move down size increase due to increase in\n shell number, so metallic character increase ,removal of electron is easy,\n because nucleus hold on outer electrons decrease,so they are losely \n bound, \n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 317; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> group 13- elements-The Boron Family',NULL,'2','mcq','In','B','Ga','Al','NEW','11-317',NULL,NULL),
(110202,'chemistry','Consider the following statements:\n 1- In india Borax occurs in Puga Valley\n 2-The abundance of B in earth crust is less than 0.0001%\n 3- There are two isotopes of B (10,11)\n 4- B is always found in combined state with oxygen as oxyborate ions\n All of the above statements are true except','All statements are true','Boron is a fairly rare element, mainly occurs as orthoboric acid, (H3 BO3 ), borax, Na2 B4 O7 ·10H2 O, and kernite, Na2 B4 O7 ·4H2 O. In India borax occurs in Puga Valley (Ladakh) and Sambhar Lake (Rajasthan). The abundance of boron in earth crust is less than 0.0001% by mass. There are two isotopic forms of boron 10B (19%) and 11B (81%). .Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 317; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> group 13- elements-The Boron Family',NULL,'3','mcq','2,3','2,4','1,3','All statements are true','NEW','11-317',NULL,NULL),
(110203,'chemistry','Consider the following statements:\n\n 1- In India Boron occurs in Puga Valley\n\n 2-The abundance of B in earth crust is 8.3%\n\n 3- There are two isotopes of B (10,11)\n\n All of the above statements are correct except','2','Boron is a fairly rare element, mainly occurs as orthoboric acid, (H3 BO3 ), borax, Na2 B4 O7 ·10H2 O, and kernite, Na2 B4 O7 ·4H2 O. In India borax occurs in Puga Valley (Ladakh) and Sambhar Lake (Rajasthan). The abundance of boron in earth crust is less than 0.0001% by mass. There are two isotopic forms of boron 10B (19%) and 11B (81%). .Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 317; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> group 13- elements-The Boron Family',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','None of the above.','NEW','11-317',NULL,NULL),
(110204,'chemistry','Consider the following statements:\n\n 1- In india Alumimium found as Mica\n\n 2-The abundance of Al in earth crust is 8.3%\n\n 3- Fluorspur and Cryolite are important minerals of Al\n\n All of the above statements are correct except','3','Aluminium is the most abundant metal and the third most abundant element in the earth’s crust (8.3% by mass) after oxygen (45.5%) and Si (27.7%). Bauxite, Al2O3 . 2H2 O and cryolite, Na3 AlF6 are the important minerals of aluminium. In India it is found as mica in Madhya Pradesh, Karnataka, Orissa and Jammu. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 317; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> group 13- elements-The Boron Family',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-317',NULL,NULL),
(110205,'chemistry','The half life of Nh is','20 seconds','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 317; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> group 13- elements-The Boron Family',NULL,'1','mcq','30 seconds','20 seconds','10 seconds','15 seconds','NEW','11-317',NULL,NULL),
(110206,'biology','Diffusion is very important to plants since it is ?','Only means for gaseous movement within the plant body','Diffusion is very important to plants since it is the only means for gaseous movement within the plant body\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 176','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Diffusion',NULL,'2','mcq','Control total metabolic output','Correlated with energy utilization','Only means for gaseous movement within the plant body','None of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110207,'biology','Diffusion rates are affected by ? \n\n1. Temperature. \n\n2. Permeability. \n\n3. Gradient. \n\n4. Pressure','1,2,3 and 4','Diffusion rates are affected by the gradient of concentration, the permeability of the membrane separating them, temperature and pressure.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 176','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Diffusion',NULL,'2','mcq','1,2,3 and 4','1, 2, 3 only','1, 2, 4 only','only 1 and 2','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110208,'biology','The diffusion rate depends on the ?','Size of the substances','Always diffusion rate depends on the size of the substances; obviously smaller substances diffuse faster.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 176','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Diffusion',NULL,'2','mcq','Quality of air present around','Weight of the substances','Volume of the substances','Size of the substances','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110209,'biology','The diffusion of any substance across a membrane  depends  upon ?','Solubility in lipids','The diffusion of any substance across a membrane also depends on its solubility in lipids, the major constituent of the membrane.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 176','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Diffusion',NULL,'2','mcq','Solubility in acetic acid','Solubility in lipids','Proteins present across the membrane','none of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110210,'biology','True about hydrophilic substances.','Their movement has to be facilitated','Substances that have a hydrophilic moiety, find it difficult to pass through the membrane; their movement has to be facilitated.Reference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 176','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Diffusion',NULL,'2','mcq','Difficult to go through facilitated diffusion','Easy to pass through the membrane','Their movement has to be facilitated','Diffuse through cytoplasmic membrane','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110211,'biology','Membrane proteins  do not set up a concentration gradient','TRUE','Membrane proteins provide sites at which such molecules cross the membrane. They do not set up a concentration gradient: a concentration gradient must already be present for molecules to diffuse even if facilitated by the proteins. This process is called facilitated diffusion.Reference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 176','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Diffusion',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110212,'biology','How facilitated diffusion occurs?','Special proteins help move substances across membranes without expenditure of ATP energy','In facilitated diffusion special proteins help move substances across membranes without expenditure of ATP energy\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 176','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Diffusion',NULL,'2','mcq','Special proteins help move substances across membranes with expenditure of ATP energy','Special proteins help move substances across membranes without expenditure of ATP energy','Metal ions help in the movement across the membrane','All the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110213,'biology','Incorrect about the facilitated diffusion ?','Larger substances diffuse faster','Facilitated diffusion cannot cause net transport of molecules from a low to a high concentration – this would require input of energy.The diffusion rate depends on the size of the substances; obviously smaller substances diffuse faster\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 176','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Diffusion',NULL,'2','mcq','A concentration gradient must be present','Cannot cause net transport of molecules from a low to a high concentration which requires input of energy','Both a and b','Larger substances diffuse faster','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110214,'biology','When transport rate reaches peak in facilitated diffusion ?','Transport rate reaches maximum when all of the protein transporters are being used (saturation).','Facilitated diffusion cannot cause net transport of molecules from a low to a high concentration – this would require input of energy. Transport rate reaches a maximum when all of the protein transporters are being used (saturation).\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 176','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Diffusion',NULL,'2','mcq','When concentration gradient completely established','Transport rate reaches maximum when all of the protein transporters are being used (saturation).','When ATP energy is used completely','All the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110215,'biology','The ________ are proteins that form large pores in the outer membranes of the plastids, mitochondria and some bacteria allowing molecules up to the size of small proteins to pass through.','Porins','The porins are proteins that form large pores in the outer membranes of the plastids, mitochondria and some bacteria allowing molecules up to the size of small proteins to pass through.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 177','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Diffusion',NULL,'2','mcq','Porins','Spores','Aquaporins','Hormones','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110216,'biology','Water channels made up of eight different types of ________','Aquaporins','The transport protein then rotates and releases the molecule inside the cell, e.g., water channels – made up of eight different types of aquaporinsReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 177','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Diffusion',NULL,'2','mcq','Porins','Aquaporins','Atoms','all the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
