(110403,'biology','Terrestrial plants take up huge amount water daily but most of it is lost to the air through evaporation from the leaves which is called as','Transpiration','Transpiration is the process of water movement through a plant and its evaporation from aerial parts, such as leaves, stems and flowers. Water is necessary for plants but only a small amount of water taken up by the roots is used for growth and metabolism\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Plant water relations  and  Water Potential',NULL,'3','mcq','Photosynthesis','Respiration','Translocation','Transpiration','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110404,'biology','Watermelon has over ______ water','92%','A watermelon has over 92 percent water; most herbaceous plants have only about 10 to 15 percent of its fresh weight as dry matter. Of course, distribution of water within a plant varies\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Plant water relations  and  Water Potential',NULL,'2','mcq','92%','50%','75%','20%','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110405,'biology','Distribution of water within a plant varies match the following with they appropriate content of water','Woody parts of plant-little water,soft parts of plant-more water','Of course, distribution of water within a plant varies – woody parts have relatively very little water, while soft parts mostly contain water. A seed may appear dry but it still has water – otherwise it would not be alive and respiring!\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Plant water relations  and  Water Potential',NULL,'3','match','Woody parts of plant,little water','Soft parts of plant,more water',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110406,'biology','What is water potential?','It is a concept fundamental to understanding water movement','Water potential (Ψw) is a concept fundamental to understanding water movement.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Plant water relations  and  Water Potential',NULL,'3','mcq','It is a concept fundamental to understanding water movement','Measure of breakdown of energy in water molecules','Calculates energy','None of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110407,'biology','Which among the following is incorrect?','The greater the concentration of water in a system, the lower is its kinetic energy or ‘water potential','Water molecules possess kinetic energy. In liquid and gaseous form they are in random motion that is both rapid and constant. The greater the concentration of water in a system, the greater is its kinetic energy or ‘water potential’\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Plant water relations  and  Water Potential',NULL,'3','mcq','Water molecules possess kinetic energy','Water molecules In liquid and gaseous form they are in random motion that is both rapid and constant','The greater the concentration of water in a system, the lower is its kinetic energy or ‘water potential','None of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110408,'biology','________ will have the greatest water potential','Pure water','Water molecules possess kinetic energy. In liquid and gaseous form they are in random motion that is both rapid and constant. The greater the concentration of water in a system, the greater is its kinetic energy or ‘water potential’. Hence, it is obvious that pure water will have the greatest water potential.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Plant water relations  and  Water Potential',NULL,'3','mcq','Solvent','Solute solution','Pure water','Acetic acid','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110409,'biology','Water move from the system containing water at higher water potential to the one having low water potential.This process of movement of substances down a gradient of free energy is called as','Diffusion','Thus water will move from the system containing water at higher water potential to the one having low water potential. This process of movement of substances down a gradient of free energy is called diffusion. Water potential is denoted by the Greek symbol Psi or Ψ. NCERT class 11th, Pg., 179.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Plant water relations  and  Water Potential',NULL,'3','mcq','Osmosis','Active transport','Diffusion','Passive absorption','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110410,'biology','Water potential is expressed in','Pressure units such as pascals (Pa)','Water potential is expressed in pressure units such as Pascal.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Plant water relations  and  Water Potential',NULL,'2','mcq','Mass(kilogram)','Pressure units such as pascals (Pa)','Temperature (kelvin)','Length(meter)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110411,'biology','By convention, the water potential of pure water at standard temperatures, which is not under any pressure, is taken as','Zero','By convention, the water potential of pure water at standard temperatures, which is not under any pressure, is taken to be zero.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Plant water relations  and  Water Potential',NULL,'2','mcq','Hundred','One','Zero','Two','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110412,'biology','If some solute is dissolved in pure water, the solution has fewer free water molecules and the concentration (free energy) of water_______ , reducing its _________','Decreases,water potential','If some solute is dissolved in pure water, the solution has fewer free water molecules and the concentration (free energy) of water decreases, reducing its water potential\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Plant water relations  and  Water Potential',NULL,'2','mcq','Increases,water potential','Decreases,water potential','Increases,solute potential','Decreases,solute potential','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110413,'biology','All solutions have a higher water potential than pure water','FALSE','Every solution have a lower water potential than pure water\n\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Plant water relations  and  Water Potential',NULL,'3','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110414,'biology','The magnitude of lowering water potential due to dissolution of a solute is called','Solute potential or Ψs','All solutions have a lower water potential than pure water; the magnitude of this lowering due to dissolution of a solute is called solute potential or Ψs.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Plant water relations  and  Water Potential',NULL,'3','mcq','Pressure potential or Ψp','Solute potential or Ψs','Water potential or Ψw','none of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110415,'biology','Solute potential Ψs is always','Negative','Hence, all solutions have a lower water potential than pure water; the magnitude of this lowering due to dissolution of a solute is called solute potential or Ψ s. Ψ s is always negative.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Plant water relations  and  Water Potential',NULL,'2','mcq','Positive','Negative','Zero','Random','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110416,'biology','Which among the following is incorrect?','If a pressure greater than atmospheric pressure is applied to pure water or a solution, its water potential decreases','If a pressure greater than atmospheric pressure is applied to pure water or a solution, its water potential increases. It is equivalent to pumping water from one place to another.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Plant water relations  and  Water Potential',NULL,'2','mcq','The more the solute molecules, the lower (more negative) is the Ψs','For a solution at atmospheric pressure (water potential) Ψw = (solute potential) Ψs','If a pressure greater than atmospheric pressure is applied to pure water or a solution, its water potential decreases','All the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110417,'biology','Pressure can build up in a plant system when water enters a plant cell due to diffusion causing a pressure built up against the cell wall, it makes the cell _______','Turgid','Pressure can build up in a plant system when water enters a plant cell due to diffusion causing a pressure built up against the cell wall, it makes the cell turgid\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Plant water relations  and  Water Potential',NULL,'2','mcq','Turgid','Flaccid','Isotonic','None of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110418,'biology','Turgid cells ________ the pressure potential','Increases','Turgid cell increases the pressure potential.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Plant water relations  and  Water Potential',NULL,'2','mcq','Increases','Decreases','Neutral','None of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110419,'biology','Pressure potential is usually positive,but negative in _______','Xylem','Pressure potential is usually positive, though in plants negative potential or tension in the water column in the xylem plays a major role in water transport up a stem\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Plant water relations  and  Water Potential',NULL,'3','mcq','Phloem','Xylem','Trachoids','Root','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110420,'biology','Water potential of a cell is affected by','Pressure and solute potential','Water potential of a cell is affected by both solute and pressure potential\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Plant water relations  and  Water Potential',NULL,'3','mcq','Pressure potential','Solute potential','Pressure and solute potential','Photosynthesis','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110421,'biology','What is the relationship between water potential and amount of water','Directly proportional','The greater the concentration of water in a system, the greater is its kinetic energy or ‘water potential’\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Plant water relations  and  Water Potential',NULL,'2','mcq','Directly proportional','Inversely proportional','Equal to','None of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110422,'biology','As the concentration of solute in the water increases,water concentration','Decreases','As the concentration of solute in the water increases,water concentration decreases there by decreasing its water potential.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Plant water relations  and  Water Potential',NULL,'3','mcq','Increases','Decreases','First increases then decreases','None of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110423,'biology','A cell has solute potential -2.5 and another cell has solute potential -2.8.They are kept in contact find the direction of flow of water.','It will be from -2.5 to -2.8','Water will move from the system containing water at higher water potential to the one having low water potential\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Plant water relations  and  Water Potential',NULL,'3','mcq','It will be from -2.5 to -2.8','It will be from -2.8 to -2.5','no movement of water will take place','water will move out of both the cells','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110425,'biology','Relation between water concentration and kinetic energy?','The greater the concentration of water in a system, the greater is its kinetic energy or ‘water potential’.','Water molecules possess kinetic energy. In liquid and gaseous form they are in random motion that is both rapid and constant. The greater the concentration of water in a system, the greater is its kinetic energy or ‘water potential’\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Plant water relations  and  Water Potential',NULL,'3','mcq','The greater the concentration of water in a system, the greater is its kinetic energy or ‘water potential’.','Not related','Inversely proportional','Both a and c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110426,'biology','Mechanism of water potential is water will move from its region of higher chemical potential (or concentration) to its region of lower chemical potential until equilibrium is reached. At equilibrium the two chambers should have nearly have the same water potential.','TRUE','Water will move from its region of higher chemical potential (or concentration) to its region of lower chemical potential until equilibrium is reached. At equilibrium the two chambers should have nearly the same water potential. NCERT Class 11th Pg., 180.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Plant water relations  and  Water Potential',NULL,'3','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
