(110502,'chemistry','The order of ionization enthalpies is','I.E₁<I.E₂<I.E₃','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 317; Paragraph 7)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Ionization energy',NULL,'2','mcq','I.E₁>I.E₂>I.E₃','I.E₁<I.E₂<I.E₃','I.E₁<I.E₂>I.E₃','I.E₁>I.E₂<I.E₃','NEW','11-317',NULL,NULL),
(110503,'biology','Among the following which are important determinants of movement of molecules in or out of the plant cell.','Tonoplast ,the membrane of the vacuole','In plant cells, the cell membrane and the membrane of the vacuole, the tonoplast together are important determinants of movement of molecules in or out of the cell.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 180','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'2','mcq','Cell wall','Tonoplast ,the membrane of the vacuole','Both a and b','Mitochondria','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110504,'biology','What is osmosis?','Diffusion of water across a differentially- or selectively permeable membrane','Osmosis is the term used to refer specifically to the diffusion of water across a differentially- or selectively permeable membrane\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 180','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','Diffusion of water across non-permeable membrane','Diffusion of water across a differentially- or selectively permeable membrane','Movement through protein channels','all the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110505,'biology','How osmosis occurs?','Occurs spontaneously in response to a driving force','Osmosis occurs spontaneously in response to a driving force.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 180','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','On stimulation of signals','Occurs spontaneously in response to a driving force','Requires ATP energy','None of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110506,'biology','The net direction and rate of osmosis depends on ?','Pressure and Concentration gradient','The net direction and rate of osmosis depends on both the pressure gradient and concentration gradient\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 180','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','Solute potential','Pressure gradient','Pressure and Concentration gradient','Water potential','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110507,'biology','In osmosis flow of water is?','High water Potential to Lower water potential','In osmosis water will move from its region of higher chemical potential (or concentration) to its region of lower chemical potential until equilibrium is reached\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 180','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','Not definite','Low water Potential to higher water potential','High water Potential to Lower water potential','all the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110508,'biology','The pressure required to prevent water from diffusing is called as','Osmotic pressure','The pressure required to prevent water from diffusing is called as Osmotic pressure.\nNumerically osmotic pressure is equivalent to the osmotic potential, but the sign is opposite.Osmotic pressure is the positive pressure applied, while osmotic potential is negative\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 180','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'2','mcq','Solute potential','Osmotic pressure','Water potential','Concentration gradient','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110509,'biology','Osmotic pressure is','Directly proportional to solute concentration','More the solute concentration, greater will be the pressure required to prevent water from diffusing in.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 180','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'2','mcq','Directly proportional to solute concentration','Directly proportional to pressure','Directly proportional to volume','Inversely proportional to solute concentration','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110510,'biology','Which among the following is correct?','Numerically osmotic pressure is equivalent to the osmotic potential, but the sign is opposite','Numerically osmotic pressure is equivalent to the osmotic potential, but the sign is opposite.Osmotic pressure is the positive pressure applied, while osmotic potential is negative\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 181','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','Numerically osmotic pressure is equivalent to the osmotic potential, but the sign is opposite','Osmotic pressure is the negative pressure applied, while osmotic potential is negative','More the solute concentration, lower will be the pressure required to prevent water from diffusing in.','All the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110511,'biology','If the osmotic pressure, of the external solution on the other side of cell membrane,balances the osmotic pressure of the cytoplasm, the condition is said to be_______','isotonic','If the external solution balances the osmotic pressure of the cytoplasm, it is said to be isotonic.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 181','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'2','fillup','iso,to,nic,hypo',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110512,'biology','If the external solution is more dilute than the cytoplasm, it is called as _______','Hypotonic','If the external solution is more dilute than the cytoplasm, it is hypotonic and if the external solution is more concentrated, it is hypertonic.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 181','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','Hypotonic','Hypertonic','Isotonic','None of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110513,'biology','If the external solution is more concentrated, it is called as ______','Hypertonic','If the external solution is more dilute than the cytoplasm, it is hypotonic and if the external solution is more concentrated, it is hypertonic.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 181','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','Hypotonic','Hypertonic','Isotonic','None of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110514,'biology','Match the following','In hypotonic solutions-Cells swell,in hypertonic solutions-Cells shrink','In hypotonic solutions-Cells swell,in hypertonic solutions-Cells shrink\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 181','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'2','match','In hypotonic solutions,Cells swell','In hypertonic solutions,Cells shrink',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110515,'biology','Plasmolysis is related to','When water moves out of the cell and the cell membrane of a plant cell shrinks away from its cell wall.','Plasmolysis occurs when water moves out of the cell and the cell membrane of a plant cell shrinks away from its cell wall.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 181','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'2','mcq','When water moves out of the cell and the cell membrane of a plant cell shrinks away from its cell wall.','Swelling of cell','Full accomplishment of gradient','All the Above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110516,'biology','When the cell (or tissue) is placed in a solution that is hypertonic (has more solutes) to the protoplasm.Water moves out; it is first lost from the cytoplasm and then from the vacuole.','TRUE','When the cell (or tissue) is placed in a solution that is hypertonic (has more solutes) to the protoplasm.Water moves out; it is first lost from the cytoplasm and then from the vacuole. NCERT Class 11th Pg., 181.','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110517,'biology','The water when drawn out of the cell through diffusion into the________ fluid causes the protoplast to shrink away from the walls.','Extracellular','The water when drawn out of the cell through diffusion into the extracellular (outside cell) fluid causes the protoplast to shrink away from the walls. The cell is said to be plasmolysed.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 182','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','Extracellular','Intracellular','Intra-organelle','Intra-nuclear','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110518,'biology','The movement of water in plasmolysis is','High water potential to an area of lower water potential','The movement of water occurs across the membrane moving from an area of high water potential (i.e., the cell) to an area of lower water potential outside.therefore the cell causing shrinkage of cell\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 182','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'2','mcq','High water potential to an area of lower water potential','Low water potential to an area of high water potential','Opposite to osmosis','No movement','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110519,'biology','Plasmolysis is strictly related to organisms having cell wall or mitochondria?','Cell wall, It\'s not for animals.','Cell wall and vacuole is present in plants cell\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 182','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','Cell wall, It\'s not for animals.','Related to animal cells only','Related to Rbc','All the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110520,'biology','Which among the following is true for isotonic?','If the external solution balances the osmotic pressure of the cytoplasm it is said to be isotonic','When the cell (or tissue) is placed in an isotonic solution, there is no net flow of water towards the inside or outside. If the external solution balances the osmotic pressure of the cytoplasm it is said to be isotonic.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 182','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'2','mcq','When the cell (or tissue) is placed in an isotonic solution, there is net flow of water towards the inside.','If the external solution balances the osmotic pressure of the cytoplasm it is said to be isotonic','Both a and b','Internal solution balances the osmotic pressure','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110521,'biology','When water flows into the cell and out of the cell and are in equilibrium, the cells are said to be','Flaccid','When water flows into the cell and out of the cell and are in equilibrium, the cells are said to be flaccid\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 182','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','Flaccid','Turgid','Hypotonic','none of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110522,'biology','When the cells are placed in a hypotonic solution,water diffuses into the cell causing the cytoplasm to build up a pressure against the wall, that is called','Turgor pressure','When the cells are placed in a hypotonic solution (higher water potential or dilute solution as compared to the cytoplasm), water diffuses into the cell causing the cytoplasm to build up a pressure against the wall, that is called turgor pressure\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 182','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'2','mcq','Turgor pressure','Pressure gradient','Pressure potential','Concentration gradient','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110523,'biology','The pressure exerted by the protoplasts due to entry of water against the rigid walls is called','Turgor pressure','The pressure exerted by the protoplasts due to entry of water against the rigid walls is called  turgor pressure\nReference :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 182','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','Turgor pressure','pressure gradient','pressure potential','concentration gradient','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110524,'biology','The process of plasmolysis is usually irreversible','FALSE','The process of plasmolysis is usually reversible.when the cells are placed in a hypotonic solution (higher water potential or dilute solution as compared to the cytoplasm), water diffuses into the cell causing the cytoplasm to build up a pressure against the wall and in most cases the cell doesnt burst, and can recover when the water concentration decreases in the surrounding solution\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 182','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110525,'biology','_______ is a special type of diffusion when water is absorbed by solids , colloids ,causing them to increase in volume','Imbibition','Imbibition is a special type of diffusion when water is absorbed by solids – colloids – causing them to increase in volume\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 183','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'2','mcq','Inhibition','Diffusion','Translocation','Imbibition','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110526,'biology','The classical examples of imbibition are','Absorption of water by seeds and dry wood','The classical examples of imbibition are absorption of water by seeds and dry wood.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 183','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'2','mcq','Shrinking of bark','Drawing of cell','Absorption of water by seeds and dry wood','none of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110527,'biology','Which among the following is incorrect?','Imbibition is also diffusion since water movement is against a concentration gradient','The pressure that is produced by the swelling of wood had been used by prehistoric man to split rocks and boulders. If it were not for the pressure due to imbibition, seedlings would not have been able to emerge out of the soil into the open; they probably would not have been able to establish.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 183','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'2','mcq','The pressure that is produced by the swelling of wood had been used by prehistoric man to split rocks and boulders','If it were not for the pressure due to imbibition, seedlings would not have been able to emerge out of the soil into the open','Imbibition is also diffusion since water movement is against a concentration gradient','All the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110528,'biology','Water potential gradient between the absorbent and the liquid imbibed is essential for imbibition. In addition, for any substance to imbibe any liquid, affinity between the adsorbent and the liquid is also a prerequisite.','TRUE','Water potential gradient between the absorbent and the liquid imbibed is essential for imbibition. In addition, for any substance to imbibe any liquid, affinity between the adsorbent and the liquid is also a prerequisite. NCERT Class 11th Pg., 183.','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110529,'biology','By which difference we can differentiate imbibition from osmosis.?','Imbibition is a surface phenomenon','Osmosis is a biophysical phenomenon occurring commonly in biologic systems, in which cells of fluid compartments are separated by semipermeable membranes. Whereas, Imbibition is Absorption of water by surface contact and water gets transported by cell membrane into cell junctions of adjacent cell membranes but does not enter the cell like in osmosis.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 183','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','no difference at all','Imbibition is a surface phenomenon','quality of membrane','based on cell','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110530,'biology','A cell when immersed in a solution, increases in volume, so the external solution is','Hypotonic','High concentration of water as a result endo-osmosis occurs, water moves from external medium into the cell\n  as a result volume of cell increases\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 183','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','Hypotonic','Isotonic','Either hypertonic or hypotonic','Hypertonic','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110534,'biology','Select the incorrect statement w.r.t. imbibition','Affinity between the adsorbent and the liquid is not a prerequisite','Affinity between adsorbent and the liquid is a prerequisite i.e., contact must be there.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 183','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','Affinity between the adsorbent and the liquid is not a prerequisite','It is diffusion process','It involves both capillary action and adsorption','Phycocolloids are best imbibants','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110535,'biology','Concentration of water molecules in a system determines','Water potential of the system','Water potential is kinetic energy of the water molecules.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 183','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','Water potential of the system','Uphill transport rate','Number of carrier proteins','Membrane permeability','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110536,'biology','Osmotic potential is always ----- for a solution.','Negative','Osmotic potential is always negative for a solution.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 183','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','Positive','Negative','Neutral','Both positive and negative','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110537,'biology','When solute potential increases, then the magnitude of water potential would?','Increase','For a solution at atmospheric pressure (water potential) Ψw = (solute potential) Ψs. \n\nIf there is a pure solution without any solute then Ψw =0, Ψs=0.\nHowever on adding the solute ,suppose Ψs becomes -10, then Ψw = -10.\n\nBut the magnitude of Ψw is +10. So the magnitude of Ψw has increased by 10.\n\n(Reference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179)','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','Remain same','First increase then decrease','Increase','Decrease','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110538,'biology','The hydrostatic pressure which develops due to entry of water into a plant cell is----','Positive pressure','The hydrostatic pressure which develops due to entry of water into a plant cell is positive.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 181','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','Negative pressure','Neutral pressure','Positive pressure','Can be positive or negative','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110539,'biology','When a cell is placed in a solution whose osmotic concentration is equal to cell sap then','No net movement of water occurs','Concentration inside cell and outside cell is same.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 182','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','Water moves inside the cell','Cell will be plasmolysed','Water moves outside the cell','No net movement of water occurs','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110540,'biology','Turgor pressure is the','Positive pressure','TP is exerted by the cell on to the external environment.','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','Negative pressure','Atmospheric pressure','Imbibition pressure','Positive pressure','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110541,'biology','Plant seeds when sown in soil, germinate and come out of it; due to','Imbibition pressure','As seed absorbs moisture it exerts pressure and seed coat breaks.','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','Osmotic pressure','Atmospheric pressure','Imbibition pressure','Turgor pressure','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110542,'biology','In mass flow, various substances move independently according to their','Concentration gradient','In mass flow various substances move from high concentration to low concentration according to the\n  concentration gradient and pressure gradient.','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','Size','Concentration gradient','Root pressure','Carrier proteins','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110543,'biology','Correct expression for water potential of plasmolysed cell will be','ψw = ψs + (– ψp )','In a plasmolysed cell, Turgor pressure will be negative.\n  TP = ψp = –ve\n  Therefore the relationship ψw = ψs+ ψp in the case ψp is negative becomes\n  ψw = ψs+ (–ψp)','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','ψw = ψs','ψs = ψp','ψw = 0','ψw = ψs + (– ψp )','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110544,'biology','Which is not a characteristic of imbibition?','It is a property of hydrophobic and lyophobic colloids','It is a property of hydrophilic and lyophilic colloids.','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Osmosis,Plasmolysis  and  Imbibition',NULL,'3','mcq','Loss of water','It is a property of hydrophobic and lyophobic colloids','It is a reversible phenomenon','Heat is generated','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110601,'chemistry','Electronegativity decrease in a group','top to down','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 318; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Electronegativity',NULL,'2','mcq','Left to right','down to up','top to down','Right to left','NEW','11-318',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(110602,'chemistry','The trend in the Electronegativity of group 13 elements','first decreases from B to Al and then increases marginally down the group','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 318; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Electronegativity',NULL,'2','mcq','first decreases from B to Al and then increases marginally down the group','First increase then decrease','Gradually decrease','First decrease than increase','NEW','11-318',NULL,NULL),
(110603,'biology','Water and minerals, and food are generally moved by ?','Mass or bulk flow system','Water and minerals, and food are generally moved by a mass or bulk flow system\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 183','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Long Distance Transport Of Water',NULL,'2','mcq','Diffusion','Mass or bulk flow system','Along water movement','all the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110604,'biology','What is mass flow?','The movement of substances in bulk or en masse from one point to another as a result of pressure differences between the two points','Water and minerals, and food are generally moved by a mass or bulk flow system. Mass flow is the movement of substances in bulk or en masse from one point to another as a result of pressure differences between the two point\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 183','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Long Distance Transport Of Water',NULL,'2','mcq','The movement of substances in bulk or en masse from one point to another as a result of pressure differences between the two points','Surely a movement of substances in low amount','Movement of substances in masses due difference in distances','Movement of liquid in plants','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110605,'biology','A characteristic of mass flow is ?','Substances,whether in solution or in suspension, are swept along at the same pace,as in a flowing river.','It is a characteristic of mass flow that substances,whether in solution or in suspension, are swept along at the same pace,as in a flowing river.This is unlike diffusion where different substances move independently depending on their concentration gradients.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 183','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Long Distance Transport Of Water',NULL,'2','mcq','Substances with different masses are moved at different range of time','Substances,whether in solution or in suspension, are swept along at the same pace,as in a flowing river.','Movement in opposite direction','none of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110606,'biology','How is Bulk flow achieved?','Both positive and negative hydrostatic gradient','Bulk flow can be achieved either through a positive hydrostatic pressure gradient (e.g., a garden hose) or a negative hydrostatic pressure gradient (e.g., suction through a straw).\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 183','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Long Distance Transport Of Water',NULL,'2','mcq','Positive hydrostatic pressure gradient','Negative hydrostatic pressure gradient','Both positive and negative hydrostatic gradient','Neutral hydrostatic pressure','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110607,'biology','The bulk movement of substances through the conducting or vascular tissues of plants is called _________','Translocation','The bulk movement of substances through the conducting or vascular tissues of plants is called translocation.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 183','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Long Distance Transport Of Water',NULL,'2','mcq','Translocation','Diffusion','Facilitation','Imbibition','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110608,'biology','Xylem is associated with translocation from roots to the aerial parts of the plants which mainly includes ?','Hormones, Water, Organic Nitrogen','Xylem is associated with translocation of mainly water, mineral salts, some organic nitrogen and hormones, from roots to the aerial parts of the plants\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 183','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Long Distance Transport Of Water',NULL,'2','mcq','Water,mineral salts, Oxygen','Some organic nitrogen, Sugar and oxygen','Hormones, Water, Organic Nitrogen','All the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110609,'biology','The phloem translocates mainly from the leaves to other parts of the plants which includes a variety of ?','Organic and inorganic solutes','The phloem translocates a variety of organic and inorganic solutes, mainly from the leaves to other parts of the plants.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 184','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Long Distance Transport Of Water',NULL,'2','mcq','Water','Hormones','Organic and inorganic solutes','all the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110701,'chemistry','Screening effect of group 13 elements increase as we move','Top to bottom','Screening effect increases down the group as shells increases from top to bottom . \n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 318; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Physical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Top to bottom','Bottom to top','Left to right','Right to left','NEW','11-318',NULL,NULL),
(110702,'chemistry','Match the following','Boron-High melting point,Ga-low melting point,Tl-High Ionization Enthalpy,Al-Conductor','Boron-High melting point,Ga-low melting point,Tl-High Ionization Enthalpy,Al-Conductor;Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 318; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Physical properties',NULL,'3','match','Boron,High melting point','Ga,low melting point','Tl,High Ionization Enthalpy','Al,Conductor','NEW','11-318',NULL,NULL),
(110703,'chemistry','Melting points of group 13 elements is in order','B>Al>Tl>In>Ga','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 318; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Physical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','B>Al>Ga>In>Tl','B>Al>Tl>In>Ga','B>Al<Tl>In<Ga','B<Al>Tl>In>Ga','NEW','11-318',NULL,NULL),
(110704,'chemistry','Boiling points of group 13 elements is in order','B>Al>Ga>In>Tl','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 318; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Physical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','B>Al>Ga>In>Tl','B<Al<Ga<In<Tl','Al>Ga>In>B>Tl','Al>B<Ga>In>Tl','NEW','11-318',NULL,NULL),
(110705,'chemistry','The trend in the densities of the elements of group 13 is','A gradual increase','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 318; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Physical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','A gradual increase','A gradual decrease','First decrease then increase','First increase then decrease','NEW','11-318',NULL,NULL),
(110706,'chemistry','Gallium due to its unusual high boiling point is used to measure _______','Temperature','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 318; Paragraph No.2) HCl','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Physical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Temperature','pressure','reactivity','acidic nature','NEW','11-318',NULL,NULL),
(110707,'biology','Movement through the apoplast does not involve crossing the?','Cell membrane','Movement through the apoplast does not involve crossing the cell membrane.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 184','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> How do Plants Absorb Water?',NULL,'2','mcq','Cell wall','Cell membrane','Endodermis','all the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110921,'biology','Which among the following is incorrect ?','Because of higher concentration of water vapour in the atmosphere as compared to the substomatal cavity and intercellular spaces, water diffuses into the surrounding air. This creates a ‘pull','The process of photosynthesis requires water. The system of xylem vessels from the root to the leaf vein can supply the needed water. But what force does a plant use to move water molecules into the leaf parenchyma cells where they are needed? As water evaporates through the stomata, since the thin film of water over the cells is continuous, it results in pulling of water, molecule by molecule, into the leaf from the xylem. Also, because of lower concentration of water vapour in the atmosphere as compared to the substomatal cavity and intercellular spaces, water diffuses into the surrounding air. This creates a ‘pull\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 188','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Transpiration',NULL,'3','mcq','The process of photosynthesis requires water','As water evaporates through the stomata, since the thin film of water over the cells is continuous, it results in pulling of water, molecule by molecule, into the leaf from the xylem','Because of higher concentration of water vapour in the atmosphere as compared to the substomatal cavity and intercellular spaces, water diffuses into the surrounding air. This creates a ‘pull','None of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110922,'biology','Measurements reveal that the forces generated by transpiration can create pressures sufficient to lift a xylem sized column of water over 130 metres high','TRUE','Measurements reveal that the forces generated by transpiration can create pressures sufficient to lift a xylem sized column of water over 130 metres high.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 188','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Transpiration',NULL,'3','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110923,'biology','The purpose of transpiration is?','Transports minerals from the soil to all parts of the plant','Transpiration has more than one purpose; it creates transpiration pull for absorption and transport of plants,supplies water for photosynthesis, transports minerals from the soil to all parts of the plant,cools leaf surfaces, sometimes 10 to 15 degrees, by evaporative cooling,maintains the shape and structure of the plants by keeping cells turgid.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 189','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Transpiration',NULL,'3','mcq','Supplies water for evaporation','Transports minerals from the soil to all parts of the plant','Maintains the shape and structure of the plants by keeping cells flaccid','All the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110924,'biology','The humidity of rainforests is largely due to','Vast cycling of water from root to leaf to atmosphere and back to the soil','An actively photosynthesising plant has an insatiable need for water. Photosynthesis is limited by available water which can be swiftly depleted by transpiration. The humidity of rainforests is largely due to this vast cycling of water from root to leaf to atmosphere and back to the soil\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 189','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Transpiration',NULL,'3','mcq','Vast cycling of water from root to leaf to atmosphere and back to the soil','Over clouds accumulation','Due to heavy rain','Due to more sun exposure','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110925,'biology','C4 plants are twice as efficient as C3 plants in terms of ?','CO₂  fixing','C4 plants are twice as efficient as C3 plants in terms of fixing  CO₂  (making sugar)\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 189','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Transpiration',NULL,'3','mcq','CO₂  fixing','Carrying out photosynthesis','Transpiration','Productivity','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110926,'biology','Match the following','Cohesion-Mutual attraction among water molecules,Adhesion-Attraction towards polar surfaces,Guttation-Water loss in liquid phase,Surface Tension-More attraction in liquid phase','"• Surface Tension – water molecules are attracted to each other in the liquid phase more than to water in the gas phase.\n"Adhesion – attraction of water molecules to polar surfaces (such as the surface of tracheary elements).\n"These physical properties(cohesion,adhesion,surface tension) give water high tensile strength, i.e., an ability to resist a pulling force, and high capillarity, i.e., the ability to rise in thin tubes\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 188"','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Transpiration',NULL,'3','match','Cohesion,Mutual attraction among water molecules','Adhesion,Attraction towards polar surfaces','Guttation,Water loss in liquid phase','Surface Tension,More attraction in liquid phase','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110927,'biology','Correct about C3 and C4 plants is','C4 plants are twice as efficient as C3 plants in terms of fixing  CO₂  (making sugar)','The evolution of the C4 photosynthetic system is probably one of the strategies for maximising the availability of CO₂ while minimising water loss and C4 plant loses only half as much water as a C3 plant for the same amount of CO₂ fixed.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 189','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Transpiration',NULL,'3','mcq','The evolution of the C3 photosynthetic system is probably one of the strategies for maximising the availability of CO₂ while minimising water loss','C4 plants are twice as efficient as C3 plants in terms of fixing  CO₂  (making sugar)','C3 plant loses only half as much water as a C4 plant for the same amount of CO₂ fixed','All the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111001,'chemistry','Boron is inert at room temperature towards','acids and bases','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 319; Paragraph No.6)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Reactivity towards acids and bases',NULL,'1','mcq','water','air','acids and bases','nitrogen','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
(111002,'chemistry','Al on reaction with acids librates','H₂','Al being good metal readily donate electrons to hydrogen from acids ,\n which librates in form of hydrogen gas \n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 319; Paragraph No.6)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Reactivity towards acids and bases',NULL,'2','mcq','N₂','O₂','H₂','O₃','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
(111003,'chemistry','Concentrated nitric acid renders Al passive by forming','Oxide','Oxide layer covers the metal ,which prevent the exposure of electrons \n from metal to any reagent, so oxide layer decrease the reactivity of Al.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 319; Paragraph No.6)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Reactivity towards acids and bases',NULL,'2','mcq','Nitride','Nitrates','Nitrile','Oxide','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
(111004,'chemistry','Al on reaction with base evolve','H₂','OH ion from base and water reacts with Al to form tetrahydroxyaluminate\n electrons from Al are accepted by hydrogen of water to form hydrgen gas\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 319; Paragraph No.6)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Reactivity towards acids and bases',NULL,'2','mcq','O₂','H₂','N₂','O₃','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
(111005,'biology','__________ in the membranes of root hair cells actively pump ions from the soil into the cytoplasms of the epidermal cells','Specific proteins','Specific proteins in the membranes of root hair cells actively pump ions from the soil into the cytoplasms of the epidermal cells.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 189','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Uptake of Mineral Ions',NULL,'2','mcq','Pores','Lipid channel','Specific proteins','Plasmodesmata','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111006,'biology','The endodermal cells have many?','Transport proteins embedded in their plasma membrane','Endodermal cells have many transport proteins embedded in their plasma membrane; they let some solutes cross the membrane, but not others.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 189','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Uptake of Mineral Ions',NULL,'2','mcq','Mitochondria count','Lipid channel present in cell membrane','Transport proteins embedded in their plasma membrane','Pores','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111007,'biology','Transport proteins of _________ are control points, where a plant adjusts the quantity and types of solutes that reach the xylem','Endodermal cells','Transport proteins of endodermal cells are control points, where a plant adjusts the quantity and types of solutes that reach the xylem.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 189','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Uptake of Mineral Ions',NULL,'2','mcq','Epidermal cells','Hypodermal cells','Vascular cells','Endodermal cells','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111008,'biology','Unloading of mineral ions occurs at the fine vein endings through _______ and _____ uptake by these cells','Diffusion,active','Unloading of mineral ions occurs at the fine vein endings through diffusion and active uptake by these cells\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 189','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Uptake of Mineral Ions',NULL,'2','mcq','Diffusion,active','Diffusion,passive','Active,passive','Passive,active','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111009,'biology','Remobilised minerals become available to','Young leaves','Major sink of mineral elements is young and growing organs.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 190','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Uptake of Mineral Ions',NULL,'2','mcq','Chlorotic leaves','Dried leaves','Older parts of plant','Young leaves','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111010,'biology','An analysis of the xylem exudates shows that much of the nitrogen travels as','Organic form (amino acids and amides)','Much of nitrogen travels through xylem as organic compounds.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 190','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Uptake of Mineral Ions',NULL,'2','mcq','Organic form (amino acids and amides)','Inorganic form (Nitrate and nitrites)','Molecular nitrogen','Inorganic ions','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111011,'biology','Find odd one w.r.t. chief sink for mineral elements.','Mature or older leaves','Sink are the plant parts where concentration of food is low, requirement is very high. So, Apical and lateral meristem, young leaves and fruits and seeds are growing parts of plants.These regions require food, therefore they serve as sink while mature and old leaves are about to fall, they don\'t require high food as their metabolic rate decreases.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 190','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Uptake of Mineral Ions',NULL,'2','mcq','Fruits and seeds','Mature or older leaves','Young leaves','Apical and lateral meristem','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111012,'biology','Select incorrect statement w.r.t. translocation of mineral ions.','Most of the nitrogen travels as inorganic ions through xylem.','An analysis of the xylem exudates shows that though some of the nitrogen travels as inorganic ions, much of it is carried in the organic form as amino acids and related compounds\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 190','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Uptake of Mineral Ions',NULL,'2','mcq','Most of the nitrogen travels as inorganic ions through xylem.','The chief sinks for the mineral elements are the apical and lateral meristems.','Elements most readily mobilised are phosphorus, sulphur, nitrogen and potassium','Small amount of P and S are carried as organic compounds through xylem.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111013,'biology','In Xylem small amounts of P and S are carried as organic compounds.','TRUE','Similarly, small amounts of P and S are carried as organic compounds. In addition, small amount of exchange of materials does take place between xylem and phloem.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 190','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Uptake of Mineral Ions',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111014,'biology','Mineral uptake is ------ process.','Active','Mineral uptake is an active process.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 190','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Uptake of Mineral Ions',NULL,'2','mcq','Passive','Active','Facilitated','Diffusion','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111015,'biology','Match the following','Major sink of mineral-young and growing organs\n nitrogen travel-xylem\n water and minerals-Unidirectional \n organic and mineral-Multidirectional','Major sink of mineral-young and growing organs\n nitrogen travel-xylem\n water and minerals-Unidirectional \n organic and mineral-Multidirectional\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 190','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Uptake of Mineral Ions',NULL,'2','match','Major sink of mineral,young and growing organs','Nitrogen travel,xylem','Water and minerals,Unidirectional','Organic and mineral,Multidirectional','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
