(111101,'chemistry','B family react with halogens and form (E=B,Al,Ga,In,Tl),(X=F,Cl,Br,I)','EX₃','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 320; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Reactivity towards halogens',NULL,'1','mcq','EX₄','EX₂','EX₃','EX','NEW','11-320',NULL,NULL),
(111102,'chemistry','White Fumes appear around the bottle of anhydrous Aluminium Chloride due to','HCl gas','Anhydrous Aluminium Chloride is partially hydrolyzed by atmosphere to\n form white HCl gas.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 320; Problem 11,2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Reactivity towards halogens',NULL,'2','mcq','HCl gas','O₂ gas','H₂ gas','O₃ gas','NEW','11-320',NULL,NULL),
(111103,'biology','When statement supports sugar as a source of energy?','Sugar stored in roots may be mobilised to become a source of food in the early spring when the buds of trees, act as sink; they need energy for growth and development of the photosynthetic apparatus','Sugar stored in roots may be mobilised to become a source of food in the early spring when the buds of trees, act as sink; they need energy for growth and development of the photosynthetic apparatus. NCERT Class 11th pg., 190.','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Phloem Transport',NULL,'3','mcq','As starch is obtained from xylem','Sugar stored in roots may be mobilised to become a source of food in the early spring when the buds of trees, act as sink; they need energy for growth and development of the photosynthetic apparatus','Sugar is helpful for mineral observation','all the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111104,'biology','The direction of movement in the phloem is','Bi-directional','Since the source-sink relationship is variable, the direction of movement in the phloem can be upwards or downwards, i.e., bi-directional. NCERT Class 11th pg., 190.','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Phloem Transport',NULL,'3','mcq','Uni-directional','Bi-directional','Multi-directional','both a and b','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111105,'biology','Match the following respective with their function','Xylem-unidirectional,phloem-bidirectional','Xylem-unidirectional,phloem-bidirectional\n\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 190','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Phloem Transport',NULL,'3','match','Xylem,unidirectional','Phloem,bidirectional',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111106,'biology','Unlike one-way flow of water in transpiration, food in phloem sap can be transported in any required direction so long as there is a source of sugar and a sink able to use, store or remove the sugar','TRUE','This contrasts with that of the xylem where the movement is always unidirectional, i.e., upwards. Hence, unlike one-way flow of water in transpiration, food in phloem sap can be transported in any required direction so long as there is a source of sugar and a sink able to use, store or remove the sugar.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 191','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Phloem Transport',NULL,'3','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111107,'biology','Phloem sap is mainly water and sucrose, but other sugars, hormones and amino acids are also transported or ________ through phloem','Translocated','Phloem sap is mainly water and sucrose, but other sugars, hormones and amino acids are also transported or translocated through phloem\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 191','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Phloem Transport',NULL,'3','mcq','Translocated','Transfused','Diffused','Facilitated','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111108,'biology','The accepted mechanism used for the translocation of sugars from source to sink is called','Pressure flow hypothesis','The accepted mechanism used for the translocation of sugars from source to sink is called the pressure flow hypothesis. NCERT Class 11th pg., 191.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 191','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Phloem Transport',NULL,'3','mcq','Root to leaves concept','Pressure flow hypothesis','Transporting mechanism','none of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111109,'biology','In translocation of sugars glucose is prepared at the source (by photosynthesis) it is converted to sucrose at _______','Source','As glucose is prepared at the source (by photosynthesis) it is converted to sucrose (a disaccharide) at the source and then get moved to other parts.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 191','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Phloem Transport',NULL,'3','mcq','Source','Sink','Hypodermis','Stroma','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111110,'biology','The sugar from source is moved in the form of sucrose into the companion cells and then into the living phloem sieve tube cells by which transport?','Active transport','The sugar from source is moved in the form of sucrose into the companion cells and then into the living phloem sieve tube cells by active transport.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 191','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Phloem Transport',NULL,'3','mcq','Active transport','Passive transport','Diffusion','Osmosis','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111111,'biology','Process of loading sucrose at the source produces a ________ condition in the phloem','Hypertonic','As glucose is prepared at the source (by photosynthesis) it is converted to sucrose (a disaccharide). The sugar is then moved in the form of sucrose into the companion cells and then into the living phloem sieve tube cells by active transport. This process of loading at the source produces a hypertonic condition in the phloem. Water in the adjacent xylem moves into the phloem by osmosis. As osmotic pressure builds up the phloem sap will move to areas of lower pressure\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 191','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Phloem Transport',NULL,'3','mcq','Isotonic','Hypotonic','Hypertonic','Turgid','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111112,'biology','Mechanism used for the translocation of sugars occurs as','Loading of the phloem sets up a water potential gradient that facilitates the mass movement in the phloem','The correct statement are- 1. The movement of sugars in the phloem begins at the source. 2. Sugars are loaded (actively) into a sieve tube\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 191','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Phloem Transport',NULL,'3','mcq','The movement of sugars in the xylem begins at the source','Sugars are loaded (diffused) into a sieve tube','Loading of the phloem sets up a water potential gradient that facilitates the mass movement in the phloem','All the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111113,'biology','Phloem tissue is composed of sieve tube cells, which form long columns with holes in their end walls called','Sieve plates','Phloem tissue is composed of sieve tube cells, which form long columns with holes in their end walls called sieve plates. Cytoplasmic strands pass through the holes in the sieve plates, so forming continuous filaments\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 191','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Phloem Transport',NULL,'3','mcq','Sieve tubes','Sieve plates','Sieve cells','Dead cells','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111114,'biology','At the sink, incoming sugars are actively transported out of the phloem and removed as','Complex carbohydrates','At the sink, incoming sugars are actively transported out of the phloem and removed and the loss of solute produces a high water potential in the phloem, and water passes out, returning eventually to xylem\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 191','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Phloem Transport',NULL,'3','mcq','Fats','Complex proteins','Complex carbohydrates','Wastage','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111115,'biology','True about girdling is','It\'s a simple experiment used to identify the tissues through which food is transported','A simple experiment, called girdling, was used to identify the tissues through which food is transported. This simple experiment shows that phloem is the tissue responsible for translocation of food; and that transport takes place in one direction, i.e., towards the roots. \nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 191','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Phloem Transport',NULL,'3','mcq','It\'s a process to test content of phloem','It\'s a simple experiment used to identify the tissues through which food is transported','Experimental procedure for movement in plant tissue','All the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111116,'biology','Phloem loading and unloading process are respectively','Active, Active','Both loading i.e., movement of sugar from mesophyll cells to sieve elements of phloem require energy as well as unloading i.e., movement of sugar from sieve element to other part of plant requiring food also require energy\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 191','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Phloem Transport',NULL,'3','mcq','Active, Passive','Passive, Active','Passive, Passive','Active, Active','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111117,'biology','The given diagram represents (1105)','Mechanism of translocation of sugars','NCERT 11th Page no-191 figure 11.10','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Phloem Transport',NULL,'3','mcq','Mechanism of translocation of sugars','Mechanism of movement of water','Mechanism of all movements in plants','Synthesis of food in plants','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/111117q.png'),
