(111207,'chemistry','Boron is unable to form BF₆³⁻ due to','absence of d-subshell','due to absence of d-subshell ,Boron is unable to expand its octet, or \n coordination no.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 320; Problem no.11.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-elements-Important trends and anomalous properties of boron',NULL,'2','mcq','absence of p-subshell','absence of d-subshell','presence of p-subshell','presence of d-subshell','NEW','11-320',NULL,NULL),
(111209,'biology','Guard cells help in: [AIPMT (Prelims)-2009','Transpiration','Transpiration occurs due to opening of guard cell.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 187','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Transpiration','Guttation','Fighting against infection','Protection against grazing','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111210,'biology','The translocation of organic solutes in sieve tube members is supported by : [AIPMT (Prelims)-2006]','Mass flow involving a carrier and ATP','At sink the incoming sugars are actively transported out of the phloem and removed as complex carbohydrates. The loss of solute produces a high water potential in the phloem, and water passes out, returning eventually to xylem.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 191','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Mass flow involving a carrier and ATP','Root pressure and transpiration pull','P-proteins','Cytoplasmic streaming','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111211,'biology','In a ring girdled plant [AIPMT-2015]','The root dies first','In the ring-girdling process, the phloem is removed which  results in the death of the plant. This tree death occurs due to the inability of the plant leaves of transporting\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 191','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Neither root nor shoot will die','The root dies first','The shoot dies first','The shoot and root die together','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111212,'biology','When a cell is fully turgid, which of the following will be zero? [1997]','Water potential','In a fully turgid cell, DPD = 0 because it has T.P. = O.P. It means that the cell has no further capacity to absorb any water. Water potential is equal but opposite in sign to DPD. So in a fully turgid cell the water potential is zero','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Turgor pressure','Water potential','Wall pressure','Osmotic pressure','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111213,'biology','The process responsible for facilitating loss of water in liquid form from the tip of grass blades at night and in early morning is[NEET 2020]','Root pressure','Transportation of ions from the soil into the vascular tissues of the roots, increases the pressure inside the xylem, known as root pressure. Effects of root pressure is observable at night and early morning when evaporation is low and excess water collects in the form of droplets around special openings of veins near the tip of grass blades and leaves of many herbaceous parts\n\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 186','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Transpiration','Root pressure','Imbibition','Plasmolysis','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111301,'chemistry','Borax is a mineral of','B','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 320; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13- elements-Borax',NULL,'1','mcq','B','Al','Si','C','NEW','11-320',NULL,NULL),
(111303,'chemistry','The compound which forms color in Borax bead test is','Metal meta borate','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 320; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13- elements-Borax',NULL,'3','mcq','Metal oxide','Metal boride','Metal borate','Metal meta borate','NEW','11-320',NULL,NULL),
(111306,'chemistry','On Excessive Heating Borax Convert to','B₂O₃','On Heating Borax first lose water and swells up,Further heating turns\n it into transparent liquid which after sometimes solidifies to glass like\n material called Borax bead.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 320; Paragraph 6)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13- elements-Borax',NULL,'2','mcq','H₃BO₃','H₂O','HCl','B₂O₃','NEW','11-320',NULL,NULL),
(111307,'chemistry','Borax Bead test is used to identify','Transition Metals','Transition metals show characteristics colour when heat with borax on\n platinum wire, that\'s why it is used in qualitative analysis of transition \n metals,Transition metals show colour because on heating ,their d-orbitals\n splits into two energy groups one is higher in energy other is lower in \n energy,on heating electrons from lower enrgy orbitals go to higher E \n orbital when comes back to lower energy orbital evolve wavlength of \n partucular colour,this is called d-d transitions,which is only property of \n transition metals.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 320; Paragraph 7)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13- elements-Borax',NULL,'2','mcq','Transition Metals','Alkali metals','Coinage metals','Alkaline earth metals','NEW','11-320',NULL,NULL),
(111309,'biology','Transpiration is least in[1988]','High atmospheric humidity','Transpiration is least in high atmospheric humidity. The rate of transpiration is directly proportional to the saturation deficit. In other words, transpiration rate depends upon the gradient of vapour pressure. Hence, at high atmospheric humidity transpiration rate is low.\n\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 188','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Good soil moisture','High wind velocity','Dry environment','High atmospheric humidity','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111312,'biology','Opening and closing of stomata is due to the[2002]','Change in turgor pressure of guard cells','When turgidity in the cell increases the outer thin membrane swells up leading to opening of the stomata in a crescent shape and the stomata closes when the cell is flaccid.\n\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 187','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Hormonal change in guard cells','Change in turgor pressure of guard cells','Gaseous exchange','Respiration.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111313,'biology','Glycolate induces opening of stomata in[2001]','Low CO₂ concentration','Zelitch (1963) suggested that glycolic acid is formed in the guard cells. This acid is formed under low concentration of CO₂ . Glycolate formed gives rise to carbohydrates. Under this condition, osmotically active material is produced and ATP synthesis also takes place. ATP is produced during glyoxylate- glycolate shuttle. This ATP helps in the active pumping of water in the guard cells and stomata open. Stomata close when this process is reversed','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Presence of oxygen','Low CO₂ concentration','High CO₂','High nitrogen','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111314,'biology','Which of the following is not a feature of active transport of solutes in plants ?[Odisha NEET 2019]','Non-selective','Active transport is highly selective process','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Occurs against concentration gradient','Non-selective','Occurs through membranes','High nitrogen','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111315,'biology','What will be the direction of flow of water when a plant cell is placed in a hypotonic solution ?[Odisha NEET 2019]','Water will flow into the cell','Water will flow from its high concentration (that is hypotonic solution) to its low concentration that is cell.','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Water will flow in both directions','Water will flow out of the cell','Water will flow into the cell','No flow of water in any direction','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111316,'biology','When water enters in roots due to diffusion, is termed as(1996)','Passive absorption','Water is absorbed from soil by root system and mainly by root tips. There are two independent mechanisms of water absorption in plants-active water absorption and passive water absorption. The pull or tension is removed only when water is absorbed through root hair and this is passive water absorption. Thus transpiration pull is responsible for passive water absorption.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 187','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Osmosis','Passive absorption','Endocytosis','Active absorption','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111317,'biology','Which of the following is not a feature of active transport of solutes in plants ? [Odisha NEET 2019]','Non-selective','Active transport is highly selective process','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Occurs against concentration gradient','Non-selective','Occurs through membranes','Requires ATP','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111318,'biology','What will be the direction of flow of water when a plant cell is placed in a hypotonic solution ? [Odisha NEET 2019]','Water will flow into the cell.','Water will flow from its high concentration (that is hypotonic solution) to its low concentration that is cell.','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Water will flow in both directions','Water will flow out of the cell','Water will flow into the cell.','No flow of water in any direction','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111319,'biology','The process responsible for facilitating loss of water in liquid form from the tip of grass blades at night and in early morning is[NEET 2020]','Root pressure','Transportation of ions from the soil into the vascular tissues of the roots, increases the pressure inside the xylem, known as root pressure. Effects of root pressure is observable at night and early morning when evaporation is low and excess water collects in the form of droplets around special openings of veins near the tip of grass blades and leaves of many herbaceous parts','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Transpiration','Root pressure','Imbibition','Plasmolysis','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111320,'biology','Xylem translocates[NEET 2019]','Water, mineral salts, some organic nitrogen and hormones','Xylem translocates water, mineral salts, some organic nitrogen and hormones\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 190','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Water, mineral salts, some organic nitrogen and hormones','Water only','Water and mineral salts only','Water, mineral salts and some organic nitrogen only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111321,'biology','Stomatal movement is not affected by[NEET 2018]','O₂ concentration','High temperature, light and CO₂ concentration affect opening and closing of stomata while O₂ concentration has negligible effect on stomatal opening and closing.\n\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 187','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Temperature','Light','O₂ concentration','CO₂ concentration','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111322,'biology','What is the direction of movement of sugars in phloem? [NEET 2019]','Bi-directional','Food is transported by vascular tissue phloem from a source to sink. Source is a part that synthesises food and sink is a part that stores or needs the food. Since source and sink can be reversed depending on plant’s need, therefore direction of movement of sugar in phloem can be bidirectional, i.e., both upwards or downwards.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 190','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Bi-directional','Non-multidirectional','Upward','Downward','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111323,'biology','"Girdling Experiment" was performed by Plant Physiologists to identify the plant tissue through which:\n[NEET 2022]','food is transported','A simple experiment, called girdling, was used to identify the tissues through which food is transported. This simple experiment shows that phloem is the tissue responsible for translocation of food; and that transport takes place in one direction, i.e., towards the roots.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 192','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','water is transported','food is transported','for both water and food transportation','osmosis is observed','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111324,'biology','Which of the following is not observed during apoplastic pathway?\n[NEET 2022]','The movement is aided by cytoplasmic streaming','The symplastic system is the system of interconnected protoplasts.Neighbouring cells are connected through cytoplasmic strands that extend through plasmodesmata. The symplastic movement is aided by cytoplasmic streaming.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 185','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Movement of water occurs through intercellular spaces and wall of the cells','The movement does not involve crossing of cell membrane','The movement is aided by cytoplasmic streaming','Apoplast is continuous and does not provide any barrier to water movement','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111325,'biology','Addition of more solutes in a given solution will : \n[NEET 2022]','lower its water potential','If some solute is dissolved in pure water, the solution has fewer free water molecules and the concentration (free energy) of water decreases, reducing its water potential\nHence, all solutions have a lower water potential than pure water; the magnitude of this lowering due to dissolution of a solute is called solute potential or Ψ s. Ψ s is always negative.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','raise its water potential','lower its water potential','make its water potential zero','not affect the water potential at all','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111326,'biology','When a carrier protein facilitates the movement of \ntwo molecules across the membrane in same \ndirection, it is called : [ RE NEET 2022]','Symport','In symport, the same carrier protein transports two substances in the same direction. In antiport, a carrier protein transports two substances in opposite directions.','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Symport','Uniport','Transport','Antiport','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2000044209,'physics','Which of the following statement does not hold good for thermal radiation?','B','When radiation travels from one medium to another, its wavelength and speed changes whereas frequency remains unchanged.','Electromagnetic Spectrum','Electromagnetic Waves','Easy',NULL,'The wavelength changes when it travels from one medium to another.','The frequency changes when it travels from one medium to another.','The speed changes when it travels from one medium to antother.','They travel in straight line in a given medium',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111327,'biology','The ascent of xylem sap in plants is mainly accomplished by the : [ RE NEET 2022]','(3) cohesion and adhesion between water molecules','Transpirational pull is responsible for the ascent of water in the xylem. This ascent of water is dependent on the following physical factors: Cohesion – Mutual attraction between water molecules. Surface tension – Responsible for the greater attraction between water molecules in liquid phase than in gaseous phase.','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) size of the stomatal aperture','(2) distribution of stomata on the upper and lower epidermis','(3) cohesion and adhesion between water molecules','(4) root pressure','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111328,'biology','Given below are two statements : \n Statement I : \n The process of translocation through phloem is unidirectional but through xylem, it is bidirectional. \n Statement II : \n The most readily mobilized elements are phosphorus, sulphur, nitrogen and potassium. \n In the light of the above statements, choose the \nmost appropriate answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(2) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.','The correct option is 2: Statement I is incorrect, but Statement II is correct.\n\nExplanation: Statement I: The process of translocation through phloem is bidirectional, not unidirectional. Phloem transports sugars and other organic compounds both upward (source to sink) and downward (sink to source) in plants.\n\nStatement II: The statement is correct. Phosphorus, sulfur, nitrogen, and potassium are highly mobile elements in plants and are readily transported and remobilized within the plant for various metabolic processes.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is that Statement I is incorrect, but Statement II is correct.','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.','(2) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.','(3) Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','(4) Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111329,'biology','Match List I with List II :\nList I List II\nA. Cohesion I. More attraction in liquid phase\nB. Adhesion II. Mutual attraction among water molecules\nC. Surface tension III. Water loss in liquid phase\nD. Guttation IV. Attraction towards polar surfaces\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023]','(4) A – II, B – IV, C – I, D – III','A – II, B – IV, C – I, D – III','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) A – IV, B – III, C – II, D – I','(2) A – III, B – I, C – IV, D – II','(3) A – II, B – I, C – IV, D – III','(4) A – II, B – IV, C – I, D – III','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111330,'biology','Identify the correct statements regarding Mass flow hypothesis.\n(A) Mass flow is faster than diffusion.\n(B) Mass flow is the result of pressure difference between the end points.\n(C) Different substances involved in mass flow move at different paces.\n(D) Mass flow can result through either a positive or a negative hydrostatic pressure gradient.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below: [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(2) (A), (B), (D) only','Long distance transport of substances within a plant cannot be by diffusion alone as it is a slow process. \nThus, special long distance transport system (mass flow system) become necessary so as to move substances across long distance and at a much faster rate. Thus statement (A) is correct.\nMass flow is the movement of substances in bulk from one point to another as a result of pressure differences between the two points. Thus, statement (B) is correct.\nIt is a characteristic of mass flow that substances, whether in solution or in suspension, are swept along at the same pace. Thus, statement (C) is incorrect.\nBulk flow can be achieved either through a positive hydrostatic pressure gradient or a negative hydrostatic pressure gradient. Thus, statement (D) is correct.\nThus, option (2) is correct as it is mentioning all the correct statements','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) (A), (B), (C) only','(2) (A), (B), (D) only','(3) (A), (C), (D) only','(4) (B), (C), (D) only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
