(111404,'chemistry','Boric acid on heating above 370K finally forms','B₂O₃','B2O3 can also be obtained by the thermal decomposition of boric acid or borax.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 321; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13- elements-Boric acid',NULL,'1','mcq','H₃BO₃','B₂O₃','HBO₂','H₂O only','NEW','11-321',NULL,NULL),
(111408,'biology','Water potential of pure water and its solution are [2013]','0 and less than 1','The water potential of pure water is zero and the potential of the solutions is always negative.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 179','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0 and 1','0 and 0','0 and more than one','0 and less than 1','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111409,'biology','In which method of transport in plasma membrane does not require carrier molecule? [2014]','Simple diffusion','Simple diffusion does not require any protein channel or energy to transport the substance.','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Active Transport','Facilitated diffusion','Simple diffusion','Na+ – K+ pump','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111410,'biology','Seed increase in its volume by the adsorption of water through [2014]','Imbibition','Imbibition is the process of adsorption of water by hydrophilic surfaces of a substance without forming a solution. It is a type of diffusion by which movement of water takes place along a diffusion gradient.The solid particles which adsorb water or any other liquid are called Imbibants. The liquid which is imbibed is known as Imbibate. Examples are absorption of water by seeds and dry wood\n\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 183','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Osmosis','Plasmolysis','Imbibition','Diffusion','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31210,'chemistry','The second member of the groups of representative elements have __ valence orbitals','9','In addition, the first member of group has only four valence orbitals (2s and 2p) available for bonding, whereas the second member of the groups have nine valence orbitals (3s, 3p, 3d). \n\nAs a consequence of this, the maximum covalency of the first member of each group is 4. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 93; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Oxidation states',NULL,'1','fillup','9,10,5,7',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-093','11-093',NULL),
(111411,'biology','A boy is studying transport of a certain type of molecules into cell. He finds that transport slows down when the cells are poisoned with a chemical that inhibits energy production. Under normal circumstances, the molecules studied by the boy is probably transported by [2016]','Active transport','Active transport uses energy (ATP) to pump molecules against a concentration gradient. Cells undergoing active transport bear abundant mitochondria to provide ATP, needed to power active transport. So, the production of ATP is blocked or decreased and active transport is blocked or slow down\n\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 178','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Simple diffusion','Osmosis','Active transport','Facilitated diffusion','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111412,'biology','Assertion : Long distance flow of photoassimilates in plants occurs through sieve tubes. \n\nReason : Mature sieve tubes have parietal cytoplasm and perforated sieve plates [2012, 2015]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The parietal cytoplasm and perforated sieve plates help in the transport of photoassimilates that are required for photosynthesis. The parietal cytoplasm is the streaming of cytoplasm. The streaming cytoplasm moves throughout the cell, thus, helping in the transport of photoassimilates from one cell to another through the sieve plates','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111413,'biology','Assertion : Light is very important factor in transpiration. \n\nReason : Light induces stomatal opening and darkness closing of stomata.  [1999, 2015]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Light is an important factor in transpiration. The stomata opens well on days when light is brighter. It is also evident on cloudy days that the stomata does not open well. Hence, light induces stomatal opening and darkness closing','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
