(2000044213,'physics','Electromagnetic radiation used to sterilise milk is','C',NULL,'Application of EM Waves','Electromagnetic Waves','Easy',NULL,'$X$-rays','$$\\gamma$$ - rays','$UV$ rays','Radiowaves',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111517,'chemistry','LiAlH₄ and NaBH₄ are','Reducing agents','These compounds contain boron as central atom,which is less electro-\n negative than hydrogen,so it will get partial positive charge,and hydrogen\n gets partial negative charge, so hydrogen can act as base or nucleophile\n and attack at any electrophilic centre(Electron Deficient), addition of \n hydrogen is called reduction so these compounds act as reducing agent.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 322; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13- elements-Diboranes',NULL,'2','mcq','Reducing agents','Oxidizing agents','Boronating agents','metalating agents','NEW','11-322',NULL,NULL),
(111518,'chemistry','Other metal borohydrides can be prepared by using ____________as starting material','NaBH₄','Reference: NaBH4 is the most widely commercially produced borohydride and is used in the paper and textile industries, and as a reducing agent in organic synthesis. It is also a commonly used starting material for synthesis of other metal borohydrides.(NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 322; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13- elements-Diboranes',NULL,'2','mcq','LiAlH₄','NaBH₄','NaAlH₄','both a and b','NEW','11-322',NULL,NULL),
(111519,'biology','Assertion: Osmosis occurs spontaneously.\n \n Reason: This happens when equilibrium is reached.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Osmosis occurs spontaneously in response to a driving force. Water will move from its region of higher chemical potential (or concentration) to its region of lower chemical potential until equilibrium is reached. (Reference  s: NCERT, page 180, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111520,'biology','Assertion: The behaviour of the plant cells (or tissues) with regard to water movement depends on the plant species.\n \n Reason: Plant species have different cell wall properties that behave differently.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','The behaviour of the plant cells (or tissues) with regard to water movement depends on the surrounding solution. If the external solution balances the osmotic pressure of the cytoplasm, it is said to be isotonic. If the external solution is more dilute than the cytoplasm, it is hypotonic and if the external solution is more concentrated, it is hypertonic. (Reference  : NCERT, page 181, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111521,'biology','Assertion: Plants can be hypotonic.\n \n Reason: If the external solution is more dilute than the cytoplasm, it is hypotonic.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The behaviour of the plant cells (or tissues) with regard to water movement depends on the surrounding solution. If the external solution is more dilute than the cytoplasm, it is hypotonic (Reference  : NCERT, page 181, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111522,'biology','Assertion: The cell does not rupture due to turgor pressure.\n \n Reason: Water diffuses into the cell causing the cytoplasm to build up a pressure against the wall, and that is called turgor pressure.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Water diffuses into the cell causing the cytoplasm to build up a pressure against the wall, that is called turgor pressure. The pressure exerted by the protoplasts due to entry of water against the rigid walls is called pressure potential. Because of the rigidity of the cell wall, the cell does not rupture. (Reference  : NCERT, page 182, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111523,'biology','Assertion: When the cell (or tissue) is placed in an isotonic solution, there is no net flow of water towards the inside or outside.\n \n Reason: When water flows into the cell and out of the cell and are in equilibrium, the cells are flaccid.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','When the cell (or tissue) is placed in an isotonic solution, there is no net flow of water towards the inside or outside. If the external solution balances the osmotic pressure of the cytoplasm it is said to be isotonic. (Reference  : NCERT, page 182, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111524,'biology','Assertion: Seedlings are able to emerge out of the soil into the open.\n \n Reason: Imbibition is diffusion since water movement is along a concentration gradient.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','If it were not for the pressure due to imbibition, seedlings would not have been able to emerge out of the soil into the open; they probably would not have been able to establish! (Reference  : NCERT, page 183, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111525,'biology','Assertion: Diffusion can account for only short distance movement of molecules.\n \n Reason: Diffusion is a fast process.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Diffusion is a slow process. It can account for only short distance movement of molecules. (Reference  : NCERT, page 183, paragraph 4).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111526,'biology','Assertion: The responsibility of absorption of water and minerals is more specifically the function of the root hairs.\n \n Reason: Root hairs are thin-walled slender extensions of root epidermal cells that greatly increase the surface area for absorption.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The responsibility of absorption of water and minerals is more specifically the function of the root hairs that are present in millions at the tips of the roots. Root hairs are thin-walled slender extensions of root epidermal cells that greatly increase the surface area for absorption. (Reference  : NCERT, page 184, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111527,'biology','Assertion: Water is not absorbed along with mineral solutes, by the root hairs. \n \n Reason: This is because diffusion cannot take place at this level.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','Water is absorbed along with mineral solutes, by the root hairs, purely by diffusion (Reference  : NCERT, page 184, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111528,'biology','Assertion: In symplastic pathway, the movement of water is faster.\n \n Reason: Water has to enter the cells through the cell walls.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','During symplastic movement, the water travels through the cells – their cytoplasm; intercellular movement is through the plasmodesmata. Water has to enter the cells through the cell membrane, hence the movement is relatively slower. (Reference  : NCERT, page 185, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111529,'biology','Assertion: The inner boundary of the cortex, the endodermis, is impervious to water. \n \n Reason: Water molecules are absorbed by the proteins present on the boundary.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Most of the water flow in the roots occurs via the apoplast since the cortical cells are loosely packed, and hence offer no resistance to water movement. However, the inner boundary of the cortex, the endodermis, is impervious to water because of a band of suberised matrix called the casparian strip. Water molecules are unable to penetrate the layer, so they are directed to wall regions that are not suberised, into the cells proper through the membranes. (Reference  : NCERT, page 185, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111530,'biology','Assertion: A mycorrhiza is a symbiotic association of a fungus with a root system.\n \n Reason: The roots provides minerals and water to the mycorrhizae, in turn the fungus provide sugars and N-containing compounds to the roots.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','The fungus provides minerals and water to the roots, in turn the roots provide sugars and N-containing compounds to the mycorrhizae. (Reference  : NCERT, page 185  and  186, paragraph 5  and  1, respectively).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111531,'biology','Assertion: Pinus seeds cannot germinate and establish without the presence of mycorrhizae.\n \n Reason: Some plants have a voluntary association with mycorrhizae.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Some plants have an obligate association with the mycorrhizae. For example, Pinus seeds cannot germinate and establish without the presence of mycorrhizae. (Reference  : NCERT, page 186, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111532,'biology','Assertion: More than 10 % of the water reaching the leaves is used in photosynthesis and plant growth.\n \n Reason: Leaves have great ability to hold water.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','Less than 1 % of the water reaching the leaves is used in photosynthesis and plant growth. Most of it is lost through the stomata in the leaves. This water loss is known as transpiration. (Reference  : NCERT, page 187, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111533,'biology','Assertion: Most researchers agree that water is mainly ‘pulled’ through the plant. \n \n Reason: It is believed that the driving force for this process is transpiration from the leaves.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Most researchers agree that water is mainly ‘pulled’ through the plant, and that the driving force for this process is transpiration from the leaves. This is referred to as the cohesion-tension-transpiration pull model of water transport. (Reference  : NCERT, page 187, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31211,'chemistry','Give oxidation state and covalency respective of Al in [AlCl(H2O)₅]²⁺','3 and 6','The oxidation state of Al is +3 and the covalency is 6. \n\nPeriodic trends in valence of elements as shown by the formulas of their compounds are given in diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 93; diagram(ne0316)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Oxidation states',NULL,'1','mcq','3 and 3','6 and 6','6 and 3','3 and 6','NEW','11-093','11-093',NULL),
(111534,'biology','Assertion: Water evaporation through the stomata results in pulling of water.\n \n Reason: The thin film of water over the cells is continuous, it results in pulling of water','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','As water evaporates through the stomata, since the thin film of water over the cells is continuous, it results in pulling of water, molecule by molecule, into the leaf from the xylem. (Reference  : NCERT, page 188, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111535,'biology','Assertion: Water diffuses from the surrounding air into the plant.\n \n Reason: This happens because of higher concentration of water vapour in the atmosphere as compared to the substomatal cavity and intercellular spaces.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','Because of lower concentration of water vapour in the atmosphere as compared to the substomatal cavity and intercellular spaces, water diffuses into the surrounding air. (Reference  : NCERT, page: 188, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111536,'biology','Assertion: Photosynthesis is not limited by available water.\n \n Reason: Water can be stored indefinitely in plant cells.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','The process of photosynthesis requires water. The system of xylem\nvessels from the root to the leaf vein can supply the needed water.\n(Reference  : NCERT, page: 188, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111537,'biology','Assertion: All minerals can be passively absorbed by the roots.\n \n Reason: Minerals are uncharged and their concentration in soil is higher than inside the root.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','Unlike water, all minerals cannot be passively absorbed by the roots. Two factors account for this: (i) minerals are present in the soil as charged particles (ions) which cannot move across cell membranes and (ii) the concentration of minerals in the soil is usually lower than the concentration of minerals in the root. Therefore, (Reference  : NCERT, page 189, paragraph 5).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111538,'biology','Assertion: Apical and lateral meristems are growing regions of a plant.\n \n Reason: They are chief sinks for water.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','The chief sinks for the mineral elements are the growing regions of the plant, such as the apical and lateral meristems, young leaves, developing flowers, fruits and seeds, and the storage organs. (Reference  : NCERT, page 190, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111539,'biology','Assertion: Older dying leaves export much of their mineral content to younger leaves.\n \n Reason: Mineral ions are frequently remobilised, particularly from older, senescing parts.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Mineral ions are frequently remobilised, particularly from older, senescing parts. Older dying leaves export much of their mineral content to younger leaves. (Reference  : NCERT, page 190, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111540,'biology','Assertion: The phloem sap moves to areas of higher pressure.\n \n Reason: It is conventional for this to happen as osmotic pressure decreases.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','As osmotic pressure builds up the phloem sap will move to areas of lower pressure. (Reference  : NCERT, page 191, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111541,'biology','Assertion: Water in the adjacent xylem moves into the phloem.\n \n Reason: Active transport mechanism facilitates this movement.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Water in the adjacent xylem moves into the phloem by osmosis. (Reference  : NCERT, page 191, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111542,'biology','Assertion: In active transport, energy in the form of ATP is utilized.\n \n Reason: It is used to pump molecules along the concentration gradient.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','In active transport, energy in the form of ATP is utilised to pump molecules against a concentration gradient across membranes. (Reference  : NCERT, page 192, paragraph 4).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31212,'chemistry','The ___ is the most characteristic property of the elements.','Valence electron','The valence is the most characteristic property of the elements. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 92; Paragraph no. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Oxidation states',NULL,'1','mcq','Valence electron','Electron','Proton','Neutron','NEW','11-092','11-092',NULL),
(111543,'biology','Assertion: Water minerals and food are transported by a mass flow system.\n \n Reason: Diffusion is inadequate for the transportation of water minerals and food within the body of the plant.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Water minerals and food cannot be moved within the body of a plant by diffusion alone. They are therefore, transported by a mass flow system – movement of substance in bulk from one point to another as a result of pressure differences between the two points. (Reference  : NCERT, page 192, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111544,'biology','Consider the following statements about Melvin Calvin, and choose the correct option.','Calvin and co-workers put radio-activity to beneficial use.','Calvin and co-workers put radio-activity to beneficial use. Melvin Calvin was born in Minnesota in April, 1911, received his Ph.D. in Chemistry from the University of Minnesota. He served as Professor of Chemistry at the University of California, Berkeley. (Reference  : NCERT, page 174, paragraph 1  and  2).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Calvin and co-workers put radio-activity to beneficial use.','Melvin Calvin received his Ph.D. in Biology from the University of Minnesota.','Calvin served as a Professor of Biology at the University of California, Berkeley.','A  and  C both are correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111545,'biology','Consider the following statements about Melvin Calvin, and choose the incorrect option.','Calvin, along with J.A. Bassham studied reactions in green plants forming fruits and  CO₂ .','Calvin and co-workers put radioactivity to beneficial use. He along with J.A. Bassham studied reactions in green plants forming sugar and other substances from raw materials like  CO₂ , water and minerals. The mapping of the pathway of carbon assimilation in photosynthesis earned him Nobel Prize in 1961. (Reference  : NCERT, page 174, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Calvin earned a Nobel Prize in 1961.','Calvin and co-workers put radioactivity to beneficial use.','Calvin, along with J.A. Bassham studied reactions in green plants forming fruits and  CO₂ .','A  and  B both are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111546,'biology','Consider the following statements about Melvin Calvin: \n \n i. Melvin Calvin was born in Minnesota in April, 1941.\n \n ii. Calvin received his Ph.D. in Chemistry. \n \n iii. Calvin served as Professor of Chemistry at the University of California, Berkeley.\n \n Select the correct answer using the code below:','Only ii and iii are correct.','Melvin Calvin was born in Minnesota in April, 1911, received his Ph.D. in Chemistry from the University of Minnesota. He served as Professor of Chemistry at the University of California, Berkeley. (Reference  s: NCERT, page 174, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct.','Only i and ii are correct.','Only ii and iii are correct.','Only iii is correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111547,'biology','Consider the following statements about Melvin Calvin:\n \n i. Calvin proposed that plants change light energy to chemical energy by transferring an electron in an organised array of pigment molecules and other substances.\n \n ii. The mapping of the pathway of carbon assimilation in photosynthesis earned Calvin a Nobel Prize in 1961.\n \n iii. Calvin along with Pasteur studied reactions in green plants forming sugar and other substances from raw materials.\n \n Select the correct answer using the code below:','Only i and ii are correct.','Calvin along with J.A. Bassham studied reactions in green plants forming sugar and other substances from raw materials like  CO₂ , water and minerals by labelling the  CO₂  with C¹⁴. Calvin proposed that plants change light energy to chemical energy by transferring an electron in an organised array of pigment molecules and other substances. The mapping of the pathway of carbon assimilation in photosynthesis earned him Nobel Prize in 1961. (Reference  : NCERT, page 174, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct.','Only i and ii are correct.','Only ii and iii are correct.','Only iii is correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111548,'biology','Consider the following statements about transportation in plants and choose the correct option.','Water taken up by the roots has to reach all parts of the plant.','Plants need to move molecules over very long distances, much more than animals do; they also do not have a circulatory system in place. Water taken up by the roots has to reach all parts of the plant, up to the very tip of the growing stem. (Reference  : NCERT, page 175, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Plants need to move molecules over very small distances, much lesser than animals.','Water taken up by the roots has to reach all parts of the plant.','Plants have their own circulatory system in place.','A  and  B both are correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111549,'biology','Consider the following statements about transportation in plants and select the incorrect option.','Movement from cell to cell within the tissue, involves a long distance transportation system.','Movement across short distances, say within the cell, across the membranes and from cell to cell within the tissue has also to take place. Transport over longer distances proceeds through the vascular system (the xylem and the phloem) and is called translocation. In a flowering plant the substances that would need to be transported are water, mineral nutrients, organic nutrients and plant growth regulators. (Reference  : NCERT, page 175, paragraph 1  and  2).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','In a flowering plant the substances that would need to be transported are water, mineral nutrients, organic nutrients and plant growth regulators.','Transport over longer distances proceeds through the vascular system.','Movement from cell to cell within the tissue, involves a long distance transportation system.','Transport over longer distances proceeds through xylem and phloem','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111550,'biology','Given below are a few statements about Diffusion. Choose the option that is factually correct.','No energy expenditure takes place during diffusion.','No energy expenditure takes place in diffusion. Diffusion is a slow process and is not dependent on a ‘living system’. Diffusion is obvious in gases and liquids, but diffusion in solids is more likely rather than of solids. Diffusion is very important to plants since it is the only means for gaseous movement within the plant body. (Reference  : NCERT, page 176, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Diffusion is a fast process.','No energy expenditure takes place during diffusion.','Diffusion of gases, liquids and solids is really obvious.','Diffusion is very important to plants since it is the only means for solid movement.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111551,'biology','Consider the following statements about Facilitated Diffusion: \n \n i. Substances soluble in lipids diffuse through the membrane faster.\n \n ii. Substances that have a hydrophilic moiety do not need their to be facilitated.\n \n iii. Membrane proteins provide sites at which molecules are stopped from entering the membrane.\n \n Select the correct answer using the code below:','Only i is correct.','Substances soluble in lipids diffuse through the membrane faster. Substances that have a hydrophilic moiety, find it difficult to pass through the membrane; their movement has to be facilitated. Membrane proteins provide sites at which such molecules cross the membrane. (Reference  : NCERT, page 176, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct.','Only i  and  ii are correct.','Only ii and iii are correct.','Only iii is correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111552,'biology','Consider the following statements about Facilitated Diffusion, and choose the correct option.','Some channels are large, allowing a variety of molecules to cross.','The proteins form channels in the membrane for molecules to pass through. Some channels are always open; others can be controlled. Some are large, allowing a variety of molecules to cross. (Reference  : NCERT, page 177, paragraph 2)','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The fatty acid molecules form channels in the membrane for molecules to pass through.','All channels can be controlled.','Both A  and  B are correct.','Some channels are large, allowing a variety of molecules to cross.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111553,'biology','Consider the following statements about the Passive symports and antiports. Choose the incorrect option.','In an antiport, both molecules move in same directions to cross the membrane.','Some carrier or transport proteins allow diffusion only if two types of molecules move together. In a symport, both molecules cross the membrane in the same direction; in an antiport, they move in opposite directions (Reference  : NCERT, page 177, paragraph 4).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','In a symport, both molecules cross the membrane in the same direction.','In an antiport, both molecules move in same directions to cross the membrane.','In an antiport, both molecules move in opposite directions.','Both A  and  B are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111554,'biology','Consider the following statements about Active Transport: \n \n i. Different proteins in the membrane play a major role in active transport only.\n \n ii. Transport rate can easily reach a maximum when all the protein transporters are unsaturated.\n \n iii. The carrier protein is very specific in what it carries across the membrane.\n \n Choose the correct option, using the code below.','Only iii is correct.','Different proteins in the membrane play a major role in both active as well as passive transport. Transport rate reaches a maximum when all the protein transporters are being used or are saturated. Like enzymes the carrier protein is very specific in what it carries across the membrane (Reference  : NCERT, page 178, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct.','Only i  and  ii are correct.','Only ii and iii are correct.','Only iii is correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111555,'biology','Consider the following statements about the Comparison of Different Transport Processes: \n \n i. Simple diffusion requires special membrane proteins.\n \n ii. Facilitated transport requires ATP energy.\n \n iii. Uphill transport occurs in Active Transport only.\n \n Choose the correct option, using the code below.','Only iii is correct.','Simple diffusion does not require special membrane proteins. Facilitated transport does not require ATP energy. Uphill transport occurs in Active Transport only. (Reference  : NCERT, page 178, paragraph 3, Table 11.1).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct.','Only i  and  ii are correct.','Only ii and iii are correct.','Only iii is correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111556,'biology','Consider the following statements about Plant-water relations and choose the correct option.','Water is often the limiting factor for plant growth and productivity in both agricultural and natural environments.','Terrestrial plants take up huge amount water daily but most of it is lost to the air through evaporation from the leaves, i.e., transpiration. A mustard plant absorbs water equal to its own weight in about 5 hours. Because of this high demand for water, it is not surprising that water is often the limiting factor for plant growth and productivity in both agricultural and natural environments. (Reference  : NCERT, page 179, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A mustard plant absorbs water equal to its own weight in about 5 days.','Terrestrial plants take up lesser amounts of water on a daily basis.','Water is often the limiting factor for plant growth and productivity in both agricultural and natural environments.','Both A  and  B are correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111557,'biology','Consider the following statements about water potential and choose the incorrect option.','By convention, the water potential of pure water at standard temperatures, which is not under any pressure, is taken to be one.','If some solute is dissolved in pure water, the solution has fewer free water molecules and the concentration (free energy) of water decreases, reducing its water potential. Hence, all solutions have a lower water potential than pure water. By convention, the water potential of pure water at standard temperatures, which is not under any pressure, is taken to be zero. If a pressure greater than atmospheric pressure is applied to pure water or a solution, its water potential increases. (Reference  : NCERT, page 179, paragraph 4,5  and  6).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','By convention, the water potential of pure water at standard temperatures, which is not under any pressure, is taken to be one.','If a pressure greater than atmospheric pressure is applied to pure water or a solution, its water potential increases.','All solutions have a lower water potential than pure water.','Both A  and  C are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111558,'biology','Consider the following statements about Osmosis:\n \n i. The plant cell is surrounded by a cell membrane and a cell wall.\n \n ii. The cell wall is not a barrier to movement for water and substances in solution.\n \n iii. In plants, the cells usually contain many small, floating vacuoles. \n \n Choose the correct option, using the code below.','Only i  and  ii are correct.','The plant cell is surrounded by a cell membrane and a cell wall. The cell wall is freely permeable to water and substances in solution hence is not a barrier to movement. In plants the cells usually contain a large central vacuole, whose contents, the vacuolar sap, contribute to the solute potential of the cell. (Reference  : NCERT, page 180, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct.','Only i  and  ii are correct.','Only ii and iii are correct.','Only iii is correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111559,'biology','Consider the following statements about Osmosis and choose the incorrect option.','The net direction and rate of osmosis depends on water’s temperature.','Osmosis occurs spontaneously in response to a driving force.The net direction and rate of osmosis depends on both the pressure gradient and concentration gradient. Water will move from its region of higher chemical potential (or concentration) to its region of lower chemical potential until equilibrium is reached. (Reference  : NCERT, page 180, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Osmosis occurs spontaneously in response to a driving force.','The net direction and rate of osmosis depends on water’s temperature.','Water will move from its region of higher chemical potential (or concentration) to its region of lower chemical potential until equilibrium is reached.','Both A  and  B are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111560,'biology','Consider the following statements about Plasmolysis below:\n \n i. Plasmolysis occurs when water moves out of the cell and the cell membrane of a plant cell shrinks away from its cell wall.\n \n ii. Cells swell in hypertonic solutions.\n \n iii. Cells shrink in hypotonic solutions. \n \n Using the code below, choose the correct option.','Only ii and iii are incorrect.','Plasmolysis occurs when water moves out of the cell and the cell membrane of a plant cell shrinks away from its cell wall.Cells swell in hypotonic solutions and shrink in hypertonic ones. (Reference  : NCERT, page 181, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is incorrect.','Only i  and  ii are incorrect.','Only ii and iii are incorrect.','Only iii is incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111561,'biology','Consider the following statements about Plasmolysis and choose the correct option.','If the external solution balances the osmotic pressure of the cytoplasm, it is said to be isotonic.','If the external solution balances the osmotic pressure of the cytoplasm, it is said to be isotonic. If the external solution is more dilute than the cytoplasm, it is hypotonic (Reference  : NCERT, page 181, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','If the external solution is more dilute than the cytoplasm, it is hypertonic.','If the external solution balances the osmotic pressure of the cytoplasm, it is said to be isotonic.','If the external solution balances the osmotic pressure of the cytoplasm, it is said to be monotonic.','Both A  and  B are correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111562,'biology','Consider the following statements about the Plasmolysis and choose the correct option.','This turgor pressure is ultimately responsible for enlargement and extension growth of cells.','The process of plasmolysis is usually reversible. The pressure exerted by the protoplasts due to entry of water against the rigid walls is called pressure potential. This turgor pressure is ultimately responsible for enlargement and extension growth of cells.(Reference  : NCERT, page 182, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The process of plasmolysis is usually irreversible.','The pressure exerted on the protoplasts due to entry of solutes against the fragile walls is called pressure potential.','A  and  B both are correct','This turgor pressure is ultimately responsible for enlargement and extension growth of cells.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111563,'biology','Consider the following statements about Plasmolysis below: \n \n i. When water flows into the cell and out of the cell and are in equilibrium, the cells are said to be turgid.\n \n ii. When the cell (or tissue) is placed in a hypertonic solution, there is no net flow of water towards the inside or outside.\n \n iii. Because of the rigidity of the cell wall, the cell does not rupture because of turgor pressure. \n \n Using the code below, choose the correct option.','Only iii is correct.','When the cell (or tissue) is placed in an isotonic solution, there is no net flow of water towards the inside or outside. When water flows into the cell and out of the cell and are in equilibrium, the cells are said to be flaccid. Water diffuses into the cell causing the cytoplasm to build up a pressure against the wall, that is called turgor pressure. The pressure exerted by the protoplasts due to entry of water against the rigid walls is called pressure potential. Because of the rigidity of the cell wall, the cell does not rupture. (Reference  : NCERT, page 182, paragraph 2  and  3).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct.','Only i  and  ii are correct.','Only ii  and  iii are correct.','Only iii is correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111564,'biology','Consider the following statements about long distance transport of water and choose the correct option.','Bulk flow can be achieved either through a positive hydrostatic pressure gradient or a negative hydrostatic pressure gradient.','Long distance transport of substances within a plant cannot be by diffusion alone. Diffusion is a slow process. It can account for only short distance movement of molecules. Bulk flow can be achieved either through a positive hydrostatic pressure gradient (e.g., a garden hose) or a negative hydrostatic pressure gradient (Reference  : NCERT, page: 183, paragraph 4  and  5).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Bulk flow can be achieved either through a positive hydrostatic pressure gradient or a negative hydrostatic pressure gradient.','Diffusion is a fast process.','Diffusion alone is sufficient for long distance transport of plants.','Both A  and  B are correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111565,'biology','Consider the following statements about Imbibition and choose the correct option.','Water potential gradient between the absorbent and the liquid imbibed is essential for imbibition.','Water potential gradient between the absorbent and the liquid imbibed is essential for imbibition. For any substance to imbibe any liquid, affinity between the adsorbant and the liquid is also a pre-requisite. Imbibition is also diffusion since water movement is along a concentration gradient; (Reference  : NCERT, page 183, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Imbibition is also diffusion since water movement is against a concentration gradient.','For any substance to imbibe any liquid, affinity between the adsorbant and the liquid is not required.','Water potential gradient between the absorbent and the liquid imbibed is essential for imbibition.','Both A  and  B are correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121412,'chemistry','Electrophilic addition reactions proceed in two steps. The first step involves the addition of an electrophile. Name the type of intermediate formed in the first step of the following addition reaction.\n\nCH₃CH=CH₂ + H⁺ →','2ᵒ carbocation','The electrophilic addition reaction of H+ with CH₃CH=CH₂ proceeds in two steps.\n\nIn the first step, the H+ ion adds to the alkene double bond, resulting in the formation of a carbocation intermediate:\n\nCH₃CH=CH₂ + H⁺ → CH₃CH⁺-CH=CH₂\n\nThis intermediate is a carbocation, which is a positively charged species with an empty p-orbital on the carbon atom. The carbocation is a reactive intermediate that can undergo further reactions in the presence of nucleophiles or other electron-rich species.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2ᵒ carbanion','1ᵒ carbocation','2ᵒ carbocation','1ᵒ carbanion','NEW','11-356','11-278',NULL),
(111566,'biology','Consider the following statements about Absorption of water in plants and choose the incorrect option.','Root hairs are thick-walled extensions of root endodermal cells that greatly decrease the surface area for absorption.','Root hairs are thin-walled slender extensions of root epidermal cells that greatly increase the surface area for absorption. Water is absorbed along with mineral solutes, by the root hairs, purely by diffusion. Once water is absorbed by the root hairs, it can move deeper into root layers by two distinct pathways: apoplast pathway and symplast pathway (Reference  : NCERT, page 184, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Root hairs are thick-walled extensions of root endodermal cells that greatly decrease the surface area for absorption.','Water is absorbed along with mineral solutes, by the root hairs, purely by diffusion.','Once water is absorbed by the root hairs, it can move deeper into root layers by two distinct pathways.','Both B  and  C are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111567,'biology','Consider the following statement about transportation in a plant.\n \n i. Xylem is not associated with translocation of hormones.\n \n ii. The phloem translocates a variety of organic and inorganic solutes.\n \n iii. Xylem is associated with translocation from roots to the aerial parts of the plant. \n \n Using the code below, choose the incorrect option.','Only i is incorrect.','Xylem is associated with translocation of mainly water, mineral salts, some organic nitrogen and hormones, from roots to the aerial parts of the plants. The phloem translocates a variety of organic and inorganic solutes, mainly from the leaves to other parts of the plants. (Reference  : NCERT, page 184, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is incorrect.','Only i  and  ii are incorrect.','Only ii  and  iii are incorrect.','Only iii is incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111568,'biology','Consider the following statement about Absorption of water in plants:\n \n i. The symplastic system is the system of interconnected protoplasts.\n \n ii. In symplastic system, neighbouring cells are connected through cytoplasmic strands.\n \n iii. Movement is upwards a potential gradient.\n \n Using the code below, choose the correct option.','Only i  and  ii are correct.','The symplastic system is the system of interconnected protoplasts. Neighbouring cells are connected through cytoplasmic strands that extend through plasmodesmata. Movement is again down a potential gradient. (Reference  : NCERT, page 185, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct.','Only i  and  ii are correct.','Only ii  and  iii are correct.','Only iii is correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111569,'biology','Consider the following statements about the absorption of water in plants and choose the correct option.','The inner boundary of the cortex, the endodermis, is impervious to water.','The inner boundary of the cortex, the endodermis, is impervious to water because of a band of suberised matrix called the casparian strip. In young roots, water enters directly into the xylem vessels and/or tracheids. The movement of water through the root layers is ultimately symplastic in the endodermis. (Reference  : NCERT, page 185, paragraph 3  and  4).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','In young roots, water enters indirectly into the phloem vessels.','The movement of water through the root layers is ultimately apoplastic in the endodermis.','The inner boundary of the cortex, the endodermis, is impervious to water.','Both B  and  C are correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111570,'biology','Consider the following statements about the root pressure and choose the correct option.','Root pressure can be responsible for pushing up water to small heights in the stem.','Root pressure, and can be responsible for pushing up water to small heights in the stem. Effects of root pressure is also observable at night and early morning when evaporation is low, and excess water collects in the form of droplets around special openings of veins near the tip of grass blades, and leaves of many herbaceous parts. Such water loss in its liquid phase is known as guttation. (Reference  : NCERT, page 186, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Effects of root pressure is also observable at day time when evaporation is high.','Water loss in its gaseous phase is known as guttation.','Root pressure can be responsible for pushing up water to small heights in the stem.','Both A  and  B are correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111571,'biology','Consider the following statements about the root pressure choose the incorrect option.','Root pressure plays a major role in water movement up tall trees.','Root pressure can, at best, only provide a modest push in the overall process of water transport. They obviously do not play a major role in water movement up tall trees. The greatest contribution of root pressure may be to re-establish the continuous chains of water molecules in the xylem which often break under the enormous tensions created by transpiration. Root pressure does not account for the majority of water transport; most plants meet their need by transpiratory pull. (Reference  : NCERT, page 186, paragraph 4).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Root pressure does not account for the majority of water transport.','The greatest contribution of root pressure may be to re-establish the continuous chains of water molecules in the xylem.','Root pressure plays a major role in water movement up tall trees.','Both A  and  B are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111572,'biology','Consider the following statements about Transpiration and choose the option that is incorrect.','When the guard cells lose turgor, due to water loss (or water stress) the elastic inner walls lose their original shape.','The opening of the stoma is also aided due to the orientation of the microfibrils in the cell walls of the guard cells. Cellulose microfibrils are oriented radially rather than longitudinally making it easier for the stoma to open. When the guard cells lose turgor, due to water loss (or water stress) the elastic inner walls regain their original shape, the guard cells become flaccid and the stoma closes (Reference  : NCERT, page 187, paragraph 4).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','When the guard cells lose turgor, due to water loss (or water stress) the elastic inner walls lose their original shape.','The opening of the stoma is also aided due to the orientation of the microfibrils in the cell walls of the guard cells.','Cellulose microfibrils are oriented radially rather than longitudinally.','Both B  and  C are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111573,'biology','Consider the following statements about Transpiration and choose the incorrect option.','The inner wall of each guard cell, towards the pore or stomatal aperture, is thin and inelastic.','Normally stomata are open in the day time and close during the night. Transpiration occurs mainly through stomata. The inner wall of each guard cell, towards the pore or stomatal aperture, is thick and elastic. (Reference  : NCERT, page 187, paragraph 4).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Transpiration occurs mainly through stomata.','Normally stomata are open in the day time and close during the night.','The inner wall of each guard cell, towards the pore or stomatal aperture, is thin and inelastic.','Both A  and  B are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111574,'biology','Consider the following statements about Transpiration:\n \n i. The system of phloem vessels from the root to the leaf vein can supply the needed water.\n \n ii. As water evaporates through the stomata, it results in pulling of water.\n \n iii. Measurements reveal that the forces generated by transpiration can create pressures sufficient to lift a xylem sized column of water over 130 metres high.\n \n Select the incorrect answer using the code below:','Only i is incorrect.','The system of xylem vessels from the root to the leaf vein can supply the needed water. As water evaporates through the stomata, since the thin film of water over the cells is continuous, it results in pulling of water, molecule by molecule, into the leaf from the  xylem. Measurements reveal that the forces generated by transpiration can create pressures sufficient to lift a xylem sized column of water over 130 metres high. (Reference  : NCERT, page 188, paragraph 3  and  4).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is incorrect.','Only i  and  ii are incorrect.','Only ii  and  iii are incorrect.','Only iii is incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111575,'biology','Consider the following statements about Transpiration:\n \n i. Water molecules are attracted to each other in the gas phase more than to water in the liquid phase.\n \n ii. Cohesion is the mutual attraction between water molecules.\n \n iii. Adhesion is the attraction of water molecules to polar surfaces (such as the surface of tracheary elements).\n \n Select the incorrect answer using the code below:','Only i is incorrect.','Cohesion is mutual attraction between water molecules. Adhesion is attraction of water molecules to polar surfaces (such as the surface of tracheary element). Water molecules are attracted to each other in the liquid phase more than to water in the gas phase. (Reference  s: NCERT, page 188, paragraph 1  and  2).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is incorrect.','Only i  and  ii are incorrect.','Only ii  and  iii are incorrect.','Only iii is incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111576,'biology','Consider the following statements about the Transpiration and Photosynthesis and choose the correct option.','Transpiration transports minerals from the soil to all parts of the plant.','Transpiration supplies water for photosynthesis. It transports minerals from the soil to all parts of the plant. It maintains the shape and structure of the plants by keeping cells turgid (Reference  : NCERT, page 189, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Transpiration does not facilitate photosynthesis in any way.','Transpiration transports minerals from the soil to all parts of the plant.','Transpiration plays no role in the plant’s shape maintenance.','A  and  C both are correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111577,'biology','Consider the following statements about Transpiration and Photosynthesis and select the incorrect option.','An actively photosynthesising plant has less need for water.','The humidity of rainforests is largely due to this vast cycling of water from root to leaf to atmosphere and back to the soil. Photosynthesis is limited by available water which can be swiftly depleted by transpiration. An actively photosynthesising plant has an insatiable need for water (Reference  : NCERT, page 189, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','An actively photosynthesising plant has less need for water.','Photosynthesis is limited by available water which can be swiftly depleted\nby transpiration.','The humidity of rainforests is largely due to this vast cycling of water from root to leaf to atmosphere and back to the soil.','A  and  B both are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111578,'biology','Given below are a few statements about Translocation of Mineral Ions. Choose the option that is factually correct.','Before leaf fall in decidous plants; minerals are removed to other parts.','The chief sinks for the mineral elements are the growing regions of the plant. Mineral ions are frequently remobilised, particularly from older, senescing parts. Older dying leaves export much of their mineral content to younger leaves. Before leaf fall in decidous plants, minerals are removed to other parts. (Reference  : NCERT, page 190, paragraph 2  and  3).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The chief sinks for the mineral elements are the mature regions of the plant.','Before leaf fall in decidous plants; minerals are removed to other parts.','Mineral ions are frequently remobilised, particularly from younger parts.','Younger leaves export much of their mineral content to dying leaves.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111579,'biology','Consider the following statements about the Translocation of Mineral Ions: \n \n i. Small amount of exchange of materials does take place between xylem and phloem.\n \n ii. Some elements that are structural components like calcium are not remobilised.\n \n iii. Small amounts of K+ and H⁺ are carried as organic compounds.\n \n Select the correct answer using the code below:','Only i  and  ii are correct.','Similarly, small amounts of P and S are carried as organic compounds. In addition, small amount of exchange of materials does take place between xylem and phloem. (Reference  : NCERT, page 190, paragraph 3  and  4).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct.','Only i  and  ii are correct.','Only ii  and  iii are correct.','Only iii is correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111580,'biology','Consider the following statements about the Pressure Flow or Mass Flow Hypothesis and choose the correct option.','The process of loading at the source produces a hypertonic condition in the phloem.','The accepted mechanism used for the translocation of sugars from source to sink is called the pressure flow hypothesis. As glucose is prepared at the source (by photosynthesis) it is converted to sucrose (a dissacharide). The sugar is then moved in the form of sucrose into the companion cells and then into the living phloem sieve tube cells by active transport. This process of loading at the source produces a hypertonic condition in the phloem. (Reference  : NCERT, page 191, paragraph 1)','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The accepted mechanism used for the translocation of sugars from sink to source is called the pressure flow hypothesis.','As glucose is prepared at the source (by photosynthesis) it is converted to a trisaccharide.','Both A  and  B are correct.','The process of loading at the source produces a hypertonic condition in the phloem.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121413,'chemistry','Covalent bond can undergo fission in two different ways. The correct representation involving a heterolytic fission of CH₃-Br is: Refer diagram(12148)','b','A covalent bond can get cleaved either by : (i) heterolytic cleavage, or by (ii) homolytic cleavage.\nAfter heterolysis, one atom has a sextet electronic structure and a positive charge and the other, a valence octet with at least one lone pair and a negative charge. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 7)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-349','11-271','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121413q.png'),
(111581,'biology','Consider the following statements about the Pressure Flow or Mass Flow Hypothesis and Choose the incorrect option.','The movement of sugars in the phloem begins at the source, where sugars are passively transported.','As sugars are removed, the osmotic pressure decreases and water moves out of the phloem. To summarise, the movement of sugars in the phloem begins at the source, where sugars are loaded (actively transported) into a sieve tube. Loading of the phloem sets up a water potential gradient that facilitates the mass movement in the phloem. (Reference  : NCERT, page 191, paragraph 1  and  2).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Loading of the phloem sets up a water potential gradient that facilitates the mass movement in the phloem.','As sugars are removed, the osmotic pressure decreases and water moves out of the phloem.','The movement of sugars in the phloem begins at the source, where sugars are passively transported.','Both A  and  B are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111582,'biology','Consider the following statements about the transportation in plants: \n \n i. Transport across cell membrane can be through diffusion.\n \n ii. Transport across cell membrane can be through facilitated transport or active transport.\n \n iii. Water and minerals absorbed by roots are transported by phloem.\n \n Choose the correct option, using the code below.','Only i  and  ii are correct.','Transport across cell membrane can be through diffusion, facilitated transport or active transport. Water and minerals absorbed by roots are transported by xylem and the organic material synthesised in the leaves is transported to other parts of plant through phloem. (Reference  : NCERT, page 192, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct.','Only i  and  ii are correct.','Only ii  and  iii are correct.','Only iii is correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(111583,'biology','Consider the following statements about the transportation in plants: \n \n i. Water absorbed by root hairs moves into the root tissue by two distinct pathways.\n \n ii. Transpiration pull model is the most acceptable to explain the transport of water.\n \n iii. Various ions, and water from soil can be transported upto some large heights in stems by root pressure.\n \n Choose the correct option, using the code below.','Only iii is incorrect.','Water absorbed by root hairs moves into the root tissue by two distinct pathways, i.e., apoplast and symplast. Various ions, and water from soil can be transported upto a small height in stems by root pressure. The transpiration pull model is the most acceptable to explain the transport of water. (Reference  : NCERT, page 192, paragraph 5).','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is incorrect.','Only i  and  ii are incorrect.','Only ii  and  iii are incorrect.','Only iii is incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(60101,'chemistry','Which of the following types of reaction is responsible for the release of heat during the combustion of a fuel like methane?','Chemical reactions','Chemical energy stored by molecules can be released as heat during chemical\nreactions when a fuel like methane,\ncooking gas or coal burns in air.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 154; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Physical reaction','Chemical reactions','Redox reaction','Chemical and physical reaction','NEW','11-160','11-136',NULL),
(60102,'chemistry','The chemical energy may also be used to provide_________ energy through a galvanic cell like dry cell.','electrical','The chemical energy may also be used to do mechanical work when a fuel burn in an engine or to provide electrical energy through a galvanic cell like dry cell.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 154; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Introduction',NULL,'2','fillup','el,ect,ric,al,no,nel,ect,ric,al',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-160','11-136',NULL),
(60103,'chemistry','Which deals with the study of different forms of energy and the quantitative relationship between them?','Thermodynamics','Thermodynamics deals with the study of different forms of energy and the quantitative relationship between them.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 154; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Thermodynamics','Heat capacity','Enthalpy','Thermodynamics and enthalpy','NEW','11-160','11-136',NULL),
(60104,'chemistry','Which macroscopic properties do not change with time for a system in equilibrium state?','Pressure and temperature','Macroscopic properties like pressure and temperature do not change with time for a system in equilibrium state.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 154; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Only pressure','Pressure and temperature','Only temprature','Volume and temperature','NEW','11-162','11-138',NULL),
(60105,'chemistry','Consider the following assertion and reason:\n \n Assertion : Variables like p, V, T are called state variables or state functions\n \n Reason : These Values depend only on the state of the system and not on how it is reached\n \n Choose the correct statement.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Variables like p, V, T are called state variables or state functions because their values depend only on the state of the system and not on how it is reached.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 162; Paragraph No.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','11-162','11-138',NULL),
(60106,'chemistry','Which of the following is a correct statement.\n \n i. Laws of thermodynamics apply only when a system is in equilibrium state.\n \n ii. Laws of thermodynamics apply only when a system moves from one equilibrium state to another equilibrium state.\n \n iii. Laws of thermodynamics deal with energy changes of macroscopic system only\n \n iv. First law of thermodynamics, ∆U = q + w','Statement iv','i. This statement is incorrect because the laws of thermodynamics are applicable to both equilibrium and non-equilibrium systems.\n\nii. This statement is also incorrect because the laws of thermodynamics are applicable to all processes, not just those that involve a system moving from one equilibrium state to another.\n\niii. This statement is partially correct. The laws of thermodynamics deal with energy changes of both microscopic and macroscopic systems. However, they are mainly concerned with macroscopic systems because the behavior of microscopic systems is described by statistical mechanics.\n\niv. This statement is correct. The first law of thermodynamics, also known as the law of conservation of \nenergy, states that the change in the internal energy (∆U) of a system is equal to the heat added to the system (q) plus the work done on the system (w).\n\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 154; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Statement i','Statement ii','Statement iii','Statement iv','NEW','11-162','11-138',NULL),
(60107,'chemistry','A thermodynamic state function is an quantity used to determine ------------ volume work','pressure','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 154; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Introduction',NULL,'1','fillup','pr,ess,ure,te,mpr,atu,re',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-162','11-138',NULL),
(60108,'chemistry','Choose the correct answer: A thermodynamic state function is a quantity \n \n (i) used to determine heat changes \n (ii) whose value is independent of path \n (iii) used to determine pressure volume work \n (iv) whose value depends on temperature only','Whose value is independent of path','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 154; Paragraph No.1) bn','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Used to determine heat changes','Used to determine pressure volume work','Whose value is independent of path','Whose value depends on temperature only','NEW','11-162','11-138',NULL),
(60109,'biology','Which one is correct about intercalary meristem?\n\ni. It occurs between mature tissues.\n\nii. It occurs in grasses and regenerate parts removed by grazing herbivores.\n\niii. It is a primary meristem.','i, ii  and  iii','The meristem which occurs between mature tissues is known as intercalary meristem. They occur in grasses and regenerate parts removed by the grazing herbivores. Both apical meristems and intercalary meristems are primary meristems because they appear early in life of a plant and contribute to the formation of the primary plant body (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:85, paragraph no:1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Meristematic Tissues',NULL,'2','mcq','Only i','Only i  and  ii','Only i  and  iii','i, ii  and  iii','NEW','11-085',NULL,NULL),
(60110,'biology','All among the following are lateral meristems except -','Apical and intercalary meristem','The meristem that occurs in the mature regions of roots and shoots of many plants, particularly those that produce woody axis and appear later than primary meristem is called the secondary or lateral meristem. They are cylindrical meristems. Fascicular vascular cambium, interfascicular cambium and cork-cambium are examples of lateral meristems. These are responsible for producing the secondary tissues.Apical meristem and intercalary meristem are primary meristem(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:85, paragraph no:1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Meristematic Tissues',NULL,'1','mcq','Fascicular or vascular cambium','Interfascicular cambium','Apical and intercalary meristem','Phellogen','NEW','11-085',NULL,NULL),
(60111,'biology','Secondary tissues are produced by all except -','Apical meristem','The meristem that occurs in the mature regions of roots and shoots of many plants, particularly those that produce woody axis and appear later than primary meristem is called the secondary or lateral meristem. They are cylindrical meristems. Fascicular vascular cambium, interfascicular cambium and cork-cambium are examples of lateral meristems. These are responsible for producing the secondary tissues.Apical meristem and intercalary meristem are primary meristem(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:85, paragraph no:1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Meristematic Tissues',NULL,'1','mcq','Fascicular cambium','Interfascicular cambium','Apical meristem','Phellogen','NEW','11-085',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(60112,'biology','During the formation of the primary plant body, specific regions of the apical meristem\nproduce which of the following tissues?\n\ni. Dermal Tissues\n\nii. Ground Tissues\n\niii. Vascular Tissues','i, ii  and  iii','During the formation of the primary plant body, specific regions of the apical meristem produce dermal tissues, ground tissues and vascular tissues (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:85, paragraph no:3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Meristematic Tissues',NULL,'2','mcq','i only','Only i  and  ii','Only i  and  iii','i, ii  and  iii','NEW','11-085',NULL,NULL),
(60113,'biology','Which meristem gives rise to axillary buds?','Shoot apical meristem','During the formation of leaves and elongation of stem, some cells ‘left behind’ from shoot apical meristem, constitute the axillary bud.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:85, paragraph no:1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Meristematic Tissues',NULL,'1','mcq','Shoot apical meristem','Root apical meristem','Secondary meristems','Both a and c','NEW','11-085',NULL,NULL),
(60114,'biology','Which of them is not true for apical meristem?','Lost the ability of cell division','Meristematic cells gives rise to permanent tissues. Growth in plants is largely restricted to specialised regions of active cell division called meristems (Gk. meristos: divided). Plants have different kinds of meristems. The meristems which occur at the tips of roots and shoots and produce primary tissues are called apical meristems (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:84, paragraph no:3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Meristematic Tissues',NULL,'1','mcq','Occurs at tip of root','Produce primary tissue','Occur at tip of stem','Lost the ability of cell division','NEW','11-084',NULL,NULL),
(60115,'biology','During the formation of the _______, specific regions of the apical meristem produce dermal tissues, ground tissues and vascular tissues.','Primary plant body','During the\nformation of the primary plant body, specific regions of the apical meristem\nproduce dermal tissues, ground tissues and vascular tissues (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:85, paragraph no:3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Meristematic Tissues',NULL,'2','mcq','Primary plant body','Secondary plant body','Cork cambium','All of these','NEW','11-085',NULL,NULL),
(60116,'biology','Which is true for secondary tissues of plant?','Add to the diameter of existing roots and shoots','The secondary growth occurs in most of the dicotyledonous roots and stems and it increases the girth (diameter) of the organs by the activity of the vascular cambium and the cork cambium(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:99, paragraph no:1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Meristematic Tissues',NULL,'2','mcq','Add to the length of roots and shoots','Add to the diameter of existing roots and shoots','Are found only in the embryo','Are found only in the seedling','NEW','11-099',NULL,NULL),
(60117,'biology','Identify the missing part in the given diagram (0601)','Root apical meristem','Figure 6.1 NCERT class 11 page no-85 diagram (e0620)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Meristematic Tissues',NULL,'2','mcq','Root apical meristem','Shoot apical meristem','Permanent tissues','Complex tissue','NEW','11-085',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/60117q.png'),
(60118,'biology','Identify the missing part in the given diagram (0602)','Shoot apical meristem,Leaf primordium','Figure 6.1 NCERT class 11 page no-85 diagram (e0621)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Meristematic Tissues',NULL,'2','mcq','Shoot apical meristem,Leaf primordium','Root apical meristem,auxillary bud','Shoot apical meristem,leaflet','Root apical meristem,leaflets','NEW','11-085',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/60118q.png'),
(60119,'biology','How are cells of permanent tissues specialised?','They are specialised both structurally and functionally.','Permanent tissues have specific structure and functions.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:85, paragraph no:3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Meristematic Tissues',NULL,'1','mcq','They are specialised structurally only.','They are specialised functionally.','They are specialised both structurally and functionally.','To undego mitosis.','NEW','11-085',NULL,NULL),
(60120,'biology','During the formation of the primary plant body, specific regions of the apical meristem produce all of the following except-','Cork','During the formation of the primary plant body, specific regions of the apical meristem produce dermal tissues, ground tissues and vascular tissues (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:85, paragraph no:3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Meristematic Tissues',NULL,'2','mcq','Dermal tissues','Ground tissues','Cork','All of these','NEW','11-085',NULL,NULL),
(60121,'biology','Apical meristems and intercalary meristem are primary meristem because they-','Appear early in life of a plant and contribute to the formation of the primary plant body','Both apical meristems and intercalary meristems are primary meristems because they appear early in life of a plant and\ncontribute to the formation of the primary plant body (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:85, paragraph no:1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Meristematic Tissues',NULL,'2','mcq','Are disintegrated after primary growth of plant','Are disintegrated after secondary growth of plant','Appear early in life of a plant and contribute to the formation of the primary plant body','None of these','NEW','11-085',NULL,NULL),
(60122,'biology','What is the role of meristem?','Growth of plants','Growth in plants is largely restricted to specialised regions of active cell division called meristems(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:84, paragraph no:3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Meristematic Tissues',NULL,'1','mcq','Absorption of water','Growth of plants','Increasing the flower producing capacity','Transpiration','NEW','11-084',NULL,NULL),
(60123,'biology','Grass stem elongates after initial growth due to -','Intercalary meristem','Intercalary meristem regenerate the plant parts removed by grazing herbivores(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:85, paragraph no:1).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Meristematic Tissues',NULL,'2','mcq','Lateral meristem','Secondary meristem','Intercalary meristem','Apical meristem','NEW','11-085',NULL,NULL),
(60124,'biology','Where does meristematic activity occurs?','Stem apex','The meristems which occur at the tips of roots and shoots and produce primary tissues are called apical meristems (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:84, paragraph no:3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Meristematic Tissues',NULL,'1','mcq','Vascular tissue','Stem apex','Leaf','Root hair','NEW','11-084',NULL,NULL),
(60125,'biology','Why is root apex subterminal?','Covered with root cap','(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:85, figure 6.1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Meristematic Tissues',NULL,'2','mcq','Covered with root hair','Covered with root cap','Covered with epidermis','Under the soil','NEW','11-085',NULL,NULL),
(60126,'biology','Axillary buds develop by activity of -','Apical meristem','The meristems which occur at the tips of roots and shoots and produce primary tissues are called apical meristems\n\nDuring the formation of leaves and elongation of stem, some cells ‘left behind’ from shoot apical meristem, constitute the axillary bud\n(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:84, paragraph no:3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Meristematic Tissues',NULL,'1','mcq','Lateral meristem','Intercalary meristem','Apical meristem','Parenchyma','NEW','11-084',NULL,NULL),
(60127,'biology','Which one is secondary lateral meristem?','Both Cork cambium and Interfascicular cambium','Cork cambium and interfascicular cambium  are secondary meristems.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:85, paragraph no:2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Meristematic Tissues',NULL,'2','mcq','Intercalary','Cork cambium','Interfascicular cambium','Both Cork cambium and Interfascicular cambium','NEW','11-085',NULL,NULL),
(60128,'biology','Axillary buds are present in ____.','Axils of leaves','During the formation of leaves and elongation of stem, some cells ‘left behind’ from shoot apical meristem, constitute the axillary bud. Such buds are present in the axils of leaves and are capable of forming a branch or a flower.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Meristematic Tissues',NULL,'1','mcq','Axils of leaves','Root hairs','Base of the stem','Tip of leaves','NEW','11-085',NULL,NULL),
(60201,'chemistry','A system in the thermodynamics refers to that part of universe in which observation are made and remaining universe constitutes the ___________.','surrounding','A system in the thermodynamics refers to that part of universe in which observation are made and remaining universe constitutes the surrounding.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 155; Paragraph No.6.1.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'2','fillup','su,rro,di,ng,un,iver,se',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(60202,'chemistry','The universe is equal to','The system+The surroundings','"A system in the thermodynamics refers to that part of universe in which observation are made and remaining universe constitutes the surrounding.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 155; Paragraph No.6.1.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'1','mcq','The system+ The universe','The surroundings - The system','The system- The surroundings','The system+The surroundings','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(60203,'chemistry','The system may simply be defined by a set of ?','Cartesian coordinates','The system may simply be defined by a set of Cartesian coordinates specifying a particular volume in space.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 155; Paragraph No.6.1.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'1','mcq','Surroundings','Molecular coordinates','Cartesian coordinates','Physical coordinates','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(60204,'chemistry','The wall that separates the system and the surroundings is called?','Boundary','"The wall that separates the system and the surroundings is called Boundary.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 155; Paragraph No.6.1.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'2','mcq','Boundary','Wall','Boundary + wall','Physical boundaries','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(60205,'chemistry','The wall that separates the system and the surroundings which may be ?','Real or imaginary','The wall that separates the system and the surroundings which may be Real or imaginary.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 155; Paragraph No.6.1.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'1','mcq','Real or imaginary','Erect or imaginary','Virtual or imaginary','Virtual or real','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(60207,'chemistry','Consider the following assertion and reason: \n \n Assertion : The presence of reactants in a closed vessel made of conducting material is an example of a closed system. \n \n Reason : In a closed system, there is no exchange of matter but exchange of energy is possible between the system and the surroundings. Choose the correct Statements','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 155; Paragraph No.6.1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(60208,'chemistry','For a process to occur under adiabatic conditions,the condition is','q = 0','NCERT Exercise problem No: 6.2 (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 155; Paragraph No.6.1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'1','mcq','∆T = 0','∆P = 0','q = 0','w = 0','NEW','11-162','11-138',NULL),
(60209,'chemistry','The presence of reactants in a thermoflask or any other closed insulated vessels is an example of an?','Isolated system','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 156; Paragraph No.6.1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'1','mcq','Closed system','Isolated system','Open system','Both a and b','NEW','11-161','11-137','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/60209q.png'),
(60210,'chemistry','System in which there is no exchange of matter, work or energy from surroundings is','Isolated','In isolated system, work, energy or heat are not exchanged with the surroundings.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 155; Paragraph No.6.1.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'2','mcq','Closed','Isothermal','Isolated','Adiabatic','NEW','11-161','11-137','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/60210q.png'),
(60211,'chemistry','∆U =q+w, is the mathematical expression for','First law of thermodynamics','The first law of thermodynamics: ∆U= q+w.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 158; Paragraph No.6.1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'1','mcq','First law of thermodynamics','Second law of thermodynamics','Zeroth law of thermodynamics','Third law of thermodynamics','NEW','11-164','11-140','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/60211q.png'),
(60212,'chemistry','When the system does not exchange heat with the surroundings, the process is','Adiabatic','In adiabatic process, system does not exchange heat with surroundings and temperature keeps increasing.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 155; Paragraph No.6.1.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'2','mcq','Adiabatic','Closed','Isochoric','Thermal','NEW','11-161','11-137','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/60212q.png'),
(60213,'chemistry','Which of the following are not state functions but path functions?','Both q and w','Heat (q) and work (W) are not state functions being path dependent. A state function is the property of the system whose value depends only on the initial and final state of the system and is independent of the path.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'1','mcq','q','w','H','Both q and w','NEW','11-162','11-138',NULL),
(60214,'chemistry','Which of the following is a correct equation?','∆U = ΔQ + ΔW','This mathemetical relation of the first law of  thermodynamics, ∆U = change in internal energy,∆Q = heat absorbed by the system, W = work done by the system. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 158; Paragraph No.6.1.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'1','mcq','∆U = ΔQ + ΔW','∆U = ∆Q - W','ΔW = ΔU + ΔQ','None of these','NEW','11-164','11-140',NULL),
(60215,'chemistry','The state of a gas can be described by quoting the relationship between----------','Pressure, volume, temprature, amount','.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 156; Paragraph No.6.1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'1','mcq','Pressure, volume, temprature','Temprature, amount, pressure','Amount, volume, temprature','Pressure, volume, temprature, amount','NEW','11-162','11-138',NULL),
(60216,'chemistry','Which is the states of the system?','State functions or State variables','We specify the state of the system by State functions or state variables.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 156; Paragraph No.6.1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'1','mcq','Physical state or chemical state','State functions or State variables','Physical state or state variables','Chemical state or state variables','NEW','11-162','11-138',NULL),
(60301,'chemistry','Work is the product of','Pressure - volume','For a gas, work is the product of the pressure p and the volume V during a change of volume.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 158; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'1','mcq','Temprature - volume','Temprature- pressure','Pressure - volume','Temprature- pressure','NEW','11-165','11-141',NULL),
(60302,'chemistry','Pressure is equal to','Force/area','Pressure is the force per unit perpendicular area over which the force is applied, p=F/A.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 159; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'1','mcq','Force/area','Area/force','Volume / Area','Area / Volume','NEW','11-165','11-141',NULL),
(60303,'chemistry','What is the work done when expansion/ compression of a gas?','W = Pₑₓ ∆V','The work done when expansion/ compression of a gas is W = Pₑₓ ∆V.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 159; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'1','mcq','W = Pin ∆V','W = Pₑₓ ∆V','W = Pₑₓ Pin','W = Pₑₓ ∆H','NEW','11-165','11-141',NULL),
(60304,'chemistry','If the presssure is not constant but changes during the process such that it is always infinitesimally __________ than the presssure of the gas.','greater','"If the presssure is not constant but changes during the process such that it i-s always infinitesimally greater than the presssure of the gas, then, at it stage of compression, the volume decreased by an infinitesimal amount,dv.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 159; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'2','fillup','gr,eat,er,sm,all,er',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-165','11-141',NULL),
(60305,'chemistry','In case of compression Pₑₓ = ?','Pₑₓ = (Pᵢₙ + dp)','Pex at each stage is equal to (Pin + dp) in case of compression.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 159; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'1','mcq','Pₑₓ = (Pᵢₙ - dp)','Pₑₓ = (Pᵢₙ + dp)','Pₑₓ = (Pᵢₙ ± dp)','Pₑₓ = (Pᵢₙ - dv)','NEW','11-165','11-141',NULL),
(60306,'chemistry','In case of expansion Pₑₓ = ?','Pₑₓ = (Pᵢₙ - dp)','In an. expansion process under similar conditions, the external pressure is always less than the pressure of the system i.e; Pex = (Pin - dp).(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 159; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'1','mcq','Pₑₓ = (Pᵢₙ - dp)','Pₑₓ = (Pᵢₙ + dp)','Pₑₓ = (Pᵢₙ - dv)','Pₑₓ = (Pᵢₙ ± dp)','NEW','11-165','11-141',NULL),
(60307,'chemistry','Which process proceeds infinitely by a series of equilibrium states such that system and the surroundings are always in near equilibrium with each other?','Reversible','A reversible process proceeds infinitely by a series of equilibrium states such that system and the surroundings are always in near equilibrium with each other.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 159; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'2','mcq','Regular','Irregular','Irreversible','Reversible','NEW','11-165','11-141',NULL),
(60308,'chemistry','What is not applicable for the work done for isothermal reversible expansion of an ideal gas ?','Infinity work is needed to bring about the process','When an ideal gas is subjected to isothermal expansion (∆T = 0) in vacuum the work done w = 0 as p(external)=0. As determined by Joule experimentally q =0, thus ∆U = 0. For isothermal reversible and irreversible changes; equation 1 can be expressed as: Isothermal reversible change: q = -w = p(Vբ-Vᵢ)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'2','mcq','Zero','W = -2.303nRT log(Vf/Vi)','W = -2.303nRT log(Pi/Pf)','Infinity work is needed to bring about the process','NEW','11-165','11-141',NULL),
(60309,'chemistry','The work done during the expansion of a gas from 8dm³ to 12dm³ against a constant external pressure of 4 atm is ( 1L atm = 101.32 J)','-1621.12','W = - Pₑₓ ∆v = - Pₑₓ (Vբ-Vᵢ) = -4 (12 - 8) dm³ = -4 (4)dm³ = -16dm³ = -16× 101.32 = 1621.12.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 159; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'2','mcq','-1701.21','-1621.12','-160.21','-1624.31','NEW','11-165','11-141',NULL),
(60310,'chemistry','What will be the work done when 1 mole of a gas expands isothermally from 25 L to 250 L against a constant pressure of 1 atm and a temperature of 300 K?','−5744J','The work done for an isothermal process is given by: \nW = -nRT ln(V₂/V₁) = -1 × 8314 × 300× ln(250/25) = −5744J','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'2','mcq','-320.6J','−5744J','-3202.5528J','-302.55J','NEW','11-165','11-141',NULL),
(60311,'chemistry','What is the external pressure when the work done is 600 J during the expansion of a gas from 5dm³ to 9dm³ ?','150 atm','W = -Pₑₓ (Vբ-Vᵢ) = -600 = -Pₑₓ (9-5)dm³ = -Pₑₓ =600/4 = 150 atm.Work done is taken as -600J as expansion happens and is spontaneous .(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 159; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'1','mcq','150 atm','160 atm','- 150 atm','200 atm','NEW','11-165','11-141',NULL),
(60312,'chemistry','According to the first law of thermodynamics,∆U= q+w. In special cases the statement can be expressed in different ways. Which of the following is not correct expression?','When work is done by the system: ∆U= q + w','When work is done by the system: ∆U= q - w.  By conventions of IUPAC in chemical\nthermodynamics. The positive sign expresses\nthat wad is positive when work is done on the\nsystem and the internal energy of system\nincreases. Similarly, if the work is done by the\nsystem,wad will be negative because internal\nenergy of the system decreases.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 157; Paragraph No.6.1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'3','mcq','At constant temperature: q = -w','When no work is done: ∆U = q','When work is done by the system: ∆U= q + w','In gaseous system: ∆U + P∆V','NEW','11-164','11-140',NULL),
(60313,'chemistry','Consider the following assertion and reason: \n \n Assertion : In adiabatic system, ∆U = W(ad). \n \n Reason : In adiabatic system, no transfer of heat takes place.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 157; Paragraph No.6.1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','11-164','11-140',NULL),
(60314,'chemistry','If a process is carried out at constant volume, then ∆U = qᵥ , when heat is _____','Supplied','If a process is carried out at constant volume,  then ∆U = qᵥ , when heat is supplied.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'1','mcq','Supplied','Absorbed','Supplied at constant pressure not constant volume','Absorbed at constant temperature not constant volume','NEW','11-164','11-140','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/60314q.png'),
(60315,'chemistry','The pressure, volume work for an ideal gas can be calculated by using the expression W = -∫ Pex∆V , where V from Vբ to Vᵢ. The work can also be calculated from the PV - plot by using the area under the curve within the specified limits.when an ideal gas is compressed (i) reversibly or (ii) irreversibly from volume Vᵢ- Vբ. choose the correct option.','Wreversible < Wirreversible','pV-plot when pressure is not constant\nand changes in finite steps during\ncompression from initial volume, Vᵢ to\nfinal volume, Vբ . Work done on the gas\nis represented by the shaded area.                                                                             \npV-plot when pressure is not constant\nand changes in infinite steps\n(reversible conditions) during\ncompression from initial volume, Vᵢ to\nfinal volume, Vբ . Work done on the gas\nis represented by the shaded area\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 159; Paragraph No.6.1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'3','mcq','Wreversible = Wirreversible +Pex∆V','Wreversible < Wirreversible','Wreversible > Wirreversible','Wreversible = Wirreversible','NEW','11-165','11-141','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/60315q.png'),
(60316,'chemistry','Under isothermal condition, a gas at 300K expands from 0.1 L to 0.25 L against a constant external pressure of 2 bar.Work done by the gas is [ Given that 1L bar = 100 J]','-30 J','Expansion of a gas against a constant external pressure is an irreversible process.The work done in a free expansion process. \n-Pₑₓ∆V = -Pₑₓ (V₂ - V₁) = -2(0.25 - 0.1) = -2 × 0.15 L bar = - 0.30 × 100 J = -30 J\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'2','mcq','-30 J','30 J','-0.30 J','0.30 J','NEW','11-165','11-141','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/60316q.png'),
(60317,'chemistry','The work done during the expansion of a gas from a volume 4dm³ to 10dm³ at a constant external pressure of 6 atm is ( 1Latm = 101.32 J)','-3647.52 J','W = -Pₑₓ∆V = -6 × (10 - 4) × 101.32 = -3647.52 j .(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'2','mcq','-3647.52 J','-3547.52 J','+3647.52 J','+ 3547.52 J','NEW','11-165','11-141',NULL),
(60318,'chemistry','When 2 mol of gas is heated at constant volume temprature is raised from 298 k to 408 k . Heat supplied to the gas is 600 j. Then which statement is correct?','q = ∆U = 600 J, w = 0','∆H = ∆U + P∆V, when ∆V = 0, w = 0, ∆H = ∆U + 0, ∆H = ∆U, \nAs ∆U = q + w, ∆U = q, in the p=resent problem: ∆H = 600 j, ∆H = ∆U = 600 j , q = 600 j, w = 0  .(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'3','mcq','q = w = 600 J, ∆U = 0','q = ∆U =0, w = 600 J','q = ∆U = 600 J, w = 0','∆U = 0, q = w = -600 J','NEW','11-164','11-140',NULL),
(60319,'chemistry','Work done on an ideal gas in a cylinder when it compressed by a constant external pressure, Pex( in a single step). Then What is the work done on the gas can be calculated?','W = Pₑₓ.L.A','The work done on the gas can be calculated from P, V graph .work done is equal to the area ABVᵢVբ.Work done on an ideal gas in a cylinder when it compressed by a constant external pressure, Pex is equal to the shaded area .(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 158; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'2','mcq','W = Pₑₓ.A','W = Pᵢₙ.L.A','W = Pₑₓ.L','W = Pₑₓ.L.A','NEW','11-165','11-141',NULL),
(60320,'chemistry','Two litres of an ideal gas at a presssure of 10 atm expands isothermally at 25 °C into a vacuum until its total volume is 10 litres. How much heat is absorbed and how much work is done in the expansion ?','q = 0, No work is done; no heat is absorbed','We have q = -w = Pₑₓ (10 -2) = 0(8) = 0. No work is done; no heat is absorbed. \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.1) ( NCERT Problem 6.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'2','mcq','q = 0, No work is done; no heat is absorbed','q = 8, work is done on the system; heat is absorbed','q = -8, work is done by the system; heat is absorbed','q = -8, work is done by the system; heat is evolved.','NEW','11-165','11-141',NULL),
(60321,'chemistry','10 mole of an ideal gas expanded spontaneously into vacuum.The work done will be','Zero','Since the ideal gas expands spontaneously into vacuum Pex = 0, hence work done is also zero .(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'1','mcq','Zero','Infinite','4 J','-4 J','NEW','11-165','11-141',NULL),
(60322,'chemistry','In a process, 701 J of heat is absorbed by a system and 394 J of work is done by the system. What is the change in internal energy for the process?','307J','According to first law of thermodynamics, ∆U = q + w\n∆U = 701 - 394 = 307 J.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 158; Paragraph No.6.1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'1','mcq','307J','-307J','1095J','-1095J','NEW','11-164','11-140',NULL),
(60323,'chemistry','A gas is contained in a cylinder with a moveable piston on which a heavy block is placed. Suppose the region outside the chamber is evacuated and the total mass of the block and the movable piston is 150 kg. When 2000J of heat flows into the gas, the internal energy of the gas increases by 1680 J. What is the distance through which the piston rises? use g = 9.8 m/s²','0.21 m','W = q - U = 2000 - 1680 = 320 J\nWork done by gas = Work done by piston mass\n320 J = mgx\nx = 320/150 × 9.81 = 0.21m (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 158; Paragraph No.6.1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'2','mcq','0.21 m','0.31 m','2.01m','3.01m','NEW','11-164','11-140',NULL),
(60324,'chemistry','Expansion of a gas in vacuum (Pₑₓ=0) is called ?','Free expansion','Expansion of a gas in vacuum (Pₑₓ=0) is called free expansion.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'1','mcq','Free expansion','Zeroth expansion','Free expansion and zero expansion','Both Free expansion and zero expansion & Zeroth expansion','NEW','11-166','11-142',NULL),
(60325,'chemistry','No work is done during ------------- expansion of an ideal gas.','free','No work is done during free expansion of an ideal gas whether the process is reversible or irreversible.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'1','fillup','fr,ee,ze,ro',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-166','11-142',NULL),
(60326,'chemistry','In a constant volume, ∆U = ?','∆U = qv','If a process is carried out at constant volume, (∆V = 0), then ∆U = qv the subscript v in qv denotes. that heat is supplied at constant volume.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'1','mcq','∆U = qv','∆U = 0','∆U = qp','∆U = ∆H','NEW','11-164','11-140',NULL),
(60327,'chemistry','Consider the following assertion and reason: \n \n Assertion : Work done during free expansion of an ideal gas whether reversible or irreversible is positive. \n \n Reason : During free expansion, external pressure is always less than the pressure of the system.','Both assertion and reason are false.','During free expansion, in vacuum Pex = 0. Hence, no work is done whether the process is reversible or irreversible.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a\n correct explanation of the\nassertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a\ncorrect explanation of the\nassertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','11-164','11-140',NULL),
(60328,'chemistry','Two litres of an ideal gas at a pressure of 10 atm expands isothermally at 25 °C into a vacuum until its total volume is 10 litres. How much heat is absorbed and how much work is done in the expansion ?','0','q = -w = Pₑₓ(10 - 2) = 0(8) = 0, no work is done; no heat is absorbed. \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.1; prob 6.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'1','mcq','0','8','-8','10','NEW','11-166','11-142',NULL),
(60401,'chemistry','What is the internal energy at constant pressure?','∆U = qₚ - P∆V','Internal energy at constant pressure is ∆U = qₚ - P∆V  , where qₚ is heat absorbed by the system and -P∆V represent expansion work done by the system.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','∆U = qᵥ + P∆V','∆U = qₚ - P∆V','∆U = qᵥ - P∆V','∆U = qₚ + P∆V','NEW','11-166','11-142',NULL),
(60402,'chemistry','Enthalpy is a','Both a and b','Enthalpy is a state function because it depends on U, V and P all of which are state functions.\nTherefore, ∆H is independent of path.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','State function','Independent of path','Both a and b','Dependent on path','NEW','11-167','11-143',NULL),
(60403,'chemistry','Which of the following is correct: i. ∆H = ∆U + ∆ngRT\n \n ii. ∆H = ∆U + P∆V\n \n iii. ∆H = qₚ\n \n iv. ∆H = ∆U =qᵥ','All of the above','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','Both i and ii','Both ii and iv','Both i and iii','All of the above','NEW','11-167','11-143',NULL),
(60404,'chemistry','If a process is carried out at constant pressure, then ∆H = qp when heat is ____','Absorbed by the system','If a process is carried out at constant pressure,  then ∆H = qₚ, when heat is absorbed by the system.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','Supplied by the system','Absorbed by the system','Absorbed by the surroundings','Supplied by the surroundings','NEW','11-167','11-143',NULL),
(60405,'chemistry','For exothermic reaction, ∆H should be','Negative','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','Negative','Positive','Negative, when heat is involved','Positive, when heat is evolved','NEW','11-167','11-143',NULL),
(60406,'chemistry','For endothermic reaction, ∆H should be','Positive','A system of reactants that absorbs heat from the surroundings in an endothermic reaction has a positive ΔH, because the enthalpy of the products is higher than the enthalpy of the reactants of the system. \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','Negative','Positive','Positive, when heat is involved','Negative, when heat is evolved','NEW','11-167','11-143',NULL),
(60407,'chemistry','∆ng refers to','Number of moles of gaseous product - Number of moles of gaseous reactants','P∆V = ∆ngRT , where ∆ng refers to number of moles of gaseous product minus number of moles of gaseous reactants. \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','Number of moles of gaseous reactant + Number of moles of gaseous product','Number of moles of gaseous product + Number of moles of gaseous reactants','Number of moles of gaseous reactant - Number of moles of gaseous product','Number of moles of gaseous product - Number of moles of gaseous reactants','NEW','11-167','11-143',NULL),
(2000044217,'physics','The source of electromagnetic wave can be a charge.','B','Accelerated charges can produce electromagnetic waves. Since a charge moving in a circular orbit is accelerating continuously, it will emit $EM$ waves.','Electromagnetic Waves','Electromagnetic Waves','Easy',NULL,'Moving with a constant velocity','Moving in a circular orbit','At rest','Moving parallel to the magnetic field',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(60408,'chemistry','Which of the following relationship is not correct for the relation between ∆H and ∆U?','When ∆ngRT = 0 then ∆H > ∆U','When ∆ngRT = 0 then ∆H = ∆U (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','When ∆ng = 0 then ∆H = ∆U','When ∆ngRT = 0 then ∆H = ∆U','When ∆ngRT = 0 then ∆H > ∆U','When ∆ng < 0 then ∆H < ∆U','NEW','11-167','11-143',NULL),
(60409,'chemistry','The enthalpy change of a reaction depend upon','State of reactants and product, nature of reactant and product, initial and final enthalpy change of a reaction.','The enthalpy change of a reaction depend upon State of reactants and product, nature of reactant and product, initial and final enthalpy and change of a reaction. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','State of reactants and product, nature of reactant and product, initial and final enthalpy change of a reaction.','Different intermediate reaction','Initial and final state of reactant','Both a and c','NEW','11-167','11-143','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/60409q.png'),
(60410,'chemistry','Consider the following reaction: OF₂ (g) +H₂0(g) ---- O₂(g) +2HF(g) . How are ∆U and ∆H related for the reaction?','∆H = ∆U + 1RT','∆H = ∆U + ∆ng RT \n∆ng = 3-2 = 1 so ∆H = ∆U + 1RT  \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','∆H = ∆U - 1RT','∆H = ∆U + 1.5RT','∆H = ∆U + 1RT','∆H = ∆U - 3RT','NEW','11-167','11-143','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/60410q.png'),
(60411,'chemistry','Match the following','ΔH-ΔU+ΔngRT, \nPΔV-ΔngRT, at constant pressure-ΔH=qₚ, at constant volume- ΔU=qᵥ','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'3','match','ΔH,ΔU+ΔngRT','PΔV,ΔngRT','At constant pressure,ΔΗ=qp','At constant volume,ΔU=qv','NEW','11-167','11-143','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/60411q.png'),
(60412,'chemistry','Two reaction are given below: \n(i) CO(g) + 1/2 O₂(g) → CO₂(g) \n(ii) Ag₂O(s) → 2Ag(s) + 1/2 O₂(g) \nWhich of the following statement is true?','For (i) ∆H < ∆U and for (ii). ∆H > ∆U','For (i) ∆ng = 1 - 3/2 = -1/2  and for (ii) = ∆ng = 1/2 - 0 = 1/2','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','For both (i) and (ii). ∆H < ∆U','For both (i) and (ii) ∆H > ∆U','For (i) ∆H < ∆U and for (ii). ∆H > ∆U','For (i) ∆H > ∆U and for (ii). ∆H < ∆U','NEW','11-167','11-143',NULL),
(60413,'chemistry','For combustion of 1 mole of benzene at 27°c, the heat of reaction at constant pressure is - 700 Kcal.What will be the heat of reaction at constant volume C₆H₆(l) + 7/2 O₂(g) → 6CO₂(g) + 3H₂O(l)','-698.5 Kcal','qₚ = qᵥ +∆ngRT, qᵥ = qₚ - ∆ngRT (∆H =∆U) \nqᵥ = -700 - (-2.5 × 2 × 300 ×10⁻³) = -698.5 Kcal \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','-698.5 Kcal','+698.5 Kcal','700.9 Kcal','-700.9 Kcal','NEW','11-167','11-143',NULL),
(60414,'chemistry','If water vapour is assumed to be a perfect gas, molar enthalpy change for vapourisation of 1 mol of water at 1bar and 100°C is 41kJ /mol.Calculate the internal energy change, when 1 mol of water is vapourised at 1 bar pressure and 100°C. and 1 mol of water is converted into ice.','37.904 kJ/mol, 41.00KJ/mol','The change H2O (l) → H2O(g), ∆H =∆U + ∆ngRT or ∆U =∆H - ∆ngRT, Substituting the values we get\n∆U = 41.00 kJ/mol -1 × 8.3 J/mol/k ×373 K = 41.00 kJ/mol - 3.096kJ/mol = 37.904kJ/mol\nand The change H2O(l) -----H2O(s), there is negligible change in volume\nso we can put P∆V = ∆ngRT ≌ 0 in this case, ∆H ≌ ∆U so\n∆U = 41.00 kJ/mol (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 162; Paragraph No.6.2.2) (NCERT Problem no:6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','37.904 kJ/mol, 0','37.904 kJ/mol, 41.00KJ/mol','-37.904 kJ/mol, 41.00KJ/mol','-37.904 kJ/mol, -41.00KJ/mol','NEW','11-167','11-143',NULL),
(60415,'chemistry','What will be the standard internal energy change for the reaction at 500K? for reaction: PCl₅(g) → PCl₃(g) + Cl₂(g) ; ∆H = - 600KJ','-604157kj','∆U = ∆H - ∆ngRT  where ∆H = -600 × 10³ , ∆ng = 2 -1 = 1, R = 8.314 J /k mol, T = 500K  \n∆U = -600×10³ -(1× 8.314 ×500) = -604157kJ\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','-604157kj','-504976kj','604185kj','+604160kj','NEW','11-167','11-143',NULL),
(60416,'chemistry','The value for ∆U for the reversible isothermal evaporation of 126g water at 273K will be (∆H(evap) of water = 40.8 kJ /mol , R = 8.314J /k mol )','-15.888KJ','∆H for 18 g of water = 40.8 KJ for 126g water = 40.8/18× 126 = 285.6KJ, n for 126g water = 126/18 = 7, ∆ng = 7-0 = 7\n∆U = ∆H - ∆ngRT = 0.2856 - (7 × 8.314 × 273) = -15887.7684J = -15.888KJ \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','+15.888KJ','-15.888 J','-15.888KJ','-1.587KJ','NEW','11-167','11-143',NULL),
(60417,'chemistry','Consider the following assertion and reason: \n \n Assertion : The difference between ∆H and ∆U is not significant for systems consisting of only solids and/or liquids. \n \n Reason : Solids and liquids do not suffer any significant volume changes upon heating.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','11-167','11-143',NULL),
(60418,'chemistry','Consider the following assertion and reason: \n \n Assertion : For the change, H₂O(l) → H₂O(s), ∆H = ∆U. \n \n Reason : No enthalpy change is involved in this process.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','For the change, H₂O(l) → H₂O(s), there is a negligible change in volume. \nP∆V = ∆ngRT = 0, Now substituting the above value in eqn\n∆H = ∆U + ∆ngRT,  we get, ∆H = ∆U. \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a\n correct explanation of the\nassertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a\ncorrect explanation of the\nassertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','11-167','11-143',NULL),
(121414,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds contains all the carbon atoms in the same hybridisation state?Refer diagram(12149)','a,d','Hybridisation influences the bond length and bond enthalpy (strength) in compounds. The sp hybrid orbital contains more s character and hence it is closer to its nucleus. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 335; Paragraph No.2) Refer diagram(e1215)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','NEW','11-335','11-257','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121414q.png'),
(60419,'chemistry','Consider the following assertion and reason: \n \n Assertion : The enthalpy change for the reaction CaO(s) + CO₂(g) → CaCO₃(s) is called enthalpy of formation of calcium carbonate. \n \n Reason : The reaction involves formation of 1mole of CaCO₃ from its constituent elements.','Both assertion and reason are false.','The enthalpy change for the given reaction is not an enthalpy of formation of calcium carbonate, since CaCO₃ has been formed from other compounds,and not from its constituent elements.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a\n correct explanation of the\nassertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a\ncorrect explanation of the\nassertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','11-167','11-143','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/60419q.png'),
(60420,'chemistry','For the reaction C₂H₅OH(l) + 3O₂(g) → 2CO₂(g) + 3H₂O(l): at constant Temperature: ∆H - ∆E is','-1RT','C₂H₅OH(l) + 3O₂(g) → 2CO₂(g) + 3H₂O(l)\n∆ng = 2 - 3 = -1, ∆H = ∆E +P∆V or ∆H - ∆E \n∆H - ∆E = P∆V, ∆H - ∆E = ∆ngRT = -1RT  \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','-1RT','+RT','2RT','-2RT','NEW','11-167','11-143','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/60420q.png'),
(60421,'chemistry','For the reaction C₂H₅OH(l) + 3O₂(g) → 2CO₂(g) + 3H₂O(l): Which one is true?','∆H = ∆E - RT','∆H = ∆E + P∆V also P∆V = ∆ngRT,   ∆H = ∆E + ∆ngRT, \n∆ng = change in number of gaseous moles \n∆ng = 2 - 3 = -1 , ∆H = ∆E - RT (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','∆H = ∆E + 2RT','∆H = ∆E - 2RT','∆H = ∆E - RT','∆H = ∆E + RT','NEW','11-167','11-143','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/60421q.png'),
(60422,'chemistry','Calculate the enthalpy change on freezing of 1.0 mol of water at 10.0°C to ice at –10.0°C. ∆fusH = 6.03 kJ /mol at 0°C. Cₚ [H₂O(l)] = 75.3 J /mol K , Cₚ [H₂O(s)] = 36.8 J /mol K','-7.151 kJ/mol','Total enthalpy change involved in the transformation is the sum of the following changes\n(a) Energy change involved in the transformation of 1 mol of water at 10°C to 1 mol of water at 0°C.\n(b) Energy change involved in the transformation of 1 mol of water at 0° to 1 mol of ice at 0°C.\n(c) Energy change involved in the transformation of 1 mol of ice at 0°C to 1 mol of ice at â€“10°C.\nTotal ∆H = cp[H2OCl]∆T +∆Hfreezing + cp[H2O(s)]∆T\n(75.3 J mol\nâ€“1 K\nâ€“1) (0 â€“ 10)K + (â€“6.03 × 10\n3J molâ€“1) + (36.8 J molâ€“1 K\nâ€“1) (â€“10 â€“ 0)K\nâ€“753 J molâ€“1â€“ 6030 J molâ€“1â€“ 368 J mol\nâ€“1 \nâ€“7151 J molâ€“1 = â€“7.151 kJ mol\nâ€“1 \nHence, the enthalpy change involved in the transformation is â€“7.151 kJ mol\nâ€“1\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','+7.151 J/K/mol','-7.151 J/K/mol','+7.151 kJ/mol','-7.151 kJ/mol','NEW','11-167','11-143',NULL),
(60423,'chemistry','Under What conditions, ΔH of a process is equal to ΔU?','At constant temperature and constant volume.','At constant volume (∆V = 0), ∆U = qV\n\n∆H = ∆U = qv\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','At constant temperature and constant pressure','At constant volume.','At constant temperature and constant volume.','At constant temperature','NEW','11-167','11-143',NULL),
(60424,'chemistry','Is weight extensive or intensive property?','Extensive property','An extensive property is a property whose value depends on the quantity or size of matter present\n in the system. for example, mass, volume, internal energy, enthalpy, etc (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 162; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','Intensive property','Extensive property','Extensive in high temperature','Intensive in low temperature','NEW','11-168','11-144',NULL),
(60426,'chemistry','In extensive properties, the value depends on','The quantity or size of matter present in the\n system','Extensive properties is the properties whose value depends on the quantity or size of the matter\npresent in the system e.g., mass, volume, internal energy, enthalpy, enthalpy, heat capacity, etc (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 162; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','The quality or size of matter present in the\n system','The quality or volume of matter present in the\n system','The quantity or size of matter present in the\n system','The quantity or size of matter present in the\n surroundings','NEW','11-168','11-144',NULL),
(60427,'chemistry','In thermodynamics, which one of the following properties is not an extensive properties?','Pressure','Extensive properties is the properties whose value depends on the quantity or size of the matter\npresent in the system e.g., mass, volume, internal energy, enthalpy, enthalpy, heat capacity, etc" (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 162; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','Mass','Volume','Pressure','heat capacity','NEW','11-168','11-144','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/60427q.png'),
(60428,'chemistry','In intensive properties, the value does not depends on','The quantity or size of matter present in the\n system','Intensive properties is the properties whose value does not depends on the quantity or size of the\nmatter present in the system e.g., temprature, density, pressure, molar volume, molar heat capacity, etc (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 162; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','The quality or size of matter present in the\nsurroundings','The quantity or size of matter present in the\n system','The quality or temprature of matter present in the\n system','The quantity and temperature of matter present\nin the system','NEW','11-168','11-144','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/60428q.png'),
(60429,'chemistry','In thermodynamics, which one of the following properties is not an intensive properties?','Enthalpy','Intensive properties is the properties whose value does not depends on the quantity or size of the\nmatter present in the system e.g., temprature, density, pressure, molar volume, molar heat capacity, etc (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 162; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','Temprature','Enthalpy','Molar heat capacity','Molar volume','NEW','11-168','11-144',NULL),
(60430,'chemistry','What is the molar property Xm, when n is the amount of matter?','Xm = X/n','A molar property Xm, is the value of an extensive property X of the system for 1 mol of the\nsubstance. If n is the amount of matter, Xm = X/n is independent of amount of matter. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 162; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','Xm = n/X','Xm = X/n','Xm = - X/n','Xm= - n/X','NEW','11-168','11-144',NULL),
(60431,'chemistry','A molar property Xm, is the value of an','extensive property','A molar property Xm, is the value of an extensive property.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 162; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','molar volume','extensive property','intensive properties','both b and a','NEW','11-168','11-144',NULL),
(60432,'chemistry','The increase of temprature is proportional to the','Heat transferred','The increase of temprature is proportional to the heat transferred. (q =coeff × T) .(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 162; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','Heat absorbed','Heat capacity','Heat transferred','Heat','NEW','11-168','11-144',NULL),
(60433,'chemistry','C is called ?','Heat capacity','The coefficient, C is called the heat capacity. .(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 162; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','Heat','Heat absorbed','Heat transferred','Heat capacity','NEW','11-168','11-144',NULL),
(60434,'chemistry','The magnitude of the coefficient depends on the','All of the above','The magnitude of the coefficient depends on the size , composition and nature of the\n system . .(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 162; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','The size of the system','Composition of the system','Nature of the system','All of the above','NEW','11-168','11-144',NULL),
(60435,'chemistry','What is the formula of heat capacity (C)?','C = q/∆T','The increase of temperature is proportional to the heat transferred. q = coeff × ∆T\nThe magnitude of the coefficient depends  to the heat transferred on the size, composition and nature\n of the system. We can also write it as q = C ∆T  The coefficient, C is called the heat capacity.\nso heat capacity C = q/∆T .(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 162; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','C = q/∆T','C = -q/ ∆T','C = ∆T/q','C = -∆T/q','NEW','11-168','11-144',NULL),
(60436,'chemistry','What is the value of q with heat capacity?','q= coeff × ∆T, q = C∆T , q = c × m × ∆T','The increase of temperature is proportional to the heat transferred. q = coeff × ∆T\nThe magnitude of the coefficient depends  to the heat transferred on the size, composition and nature\n of the system. We can also write it as q = C ∆T  The coefficient, C is called the heat capacity.\nso heat capacity C = q/∆T . For finding out the heat, q, required\nto raise the temperatures of a sample, we\nmultiply the specific heat of the substance,\nc, by the mass m, and temperatures change,\n∆T as\n q = c × m × ∆T = C∆T  .(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 162 and 163 ; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','q= coeff × ∆T, q = C∆T , q = c × m × ∆C','q= coeff × ∆T, q = C∆V , q = c × m × ∆T','q= coeff × ∆T, q = C∆T , q = c × m × ∆T','q= coeff × ∆V, q = C∆T , q = c × m × ∆T','NEW','11-168','11-144',NULL),
(60437,'chemistry','What is the amount of heat required to raise the temprature of the system in heat capacity?','1°C or 1°K','Heat capacity(C) of a system is the amount of heat required to raise the temprature of the system \nthrough 1°C or 1K . (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 163; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','1°C or 1°F','1°K or 1°F','1°C or 1°K','1°C or 100°C','NEW','11-168','11-144',NULL),
(60438,'chemistry','Specific heat capacity or heat capacity of a substance is the amount of heat required to raise the temperature of ?','1 gram of the substance through 1°C or 1°K','Specific heat, also called specific heat\ncapacity is the quantity of heat required to\nraise the temperature of one unit mass of a substance by one degree celsius (or one\nkelvin).  The molar heat capacity of a\nsubstance, Cm =\n(C/n),  is the heat capacity for\none mole of the substance and is the quantity\nof heat needed to raise the temperature of\none mole by one degree celsius (or one\nkelvin.  (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 163; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','1 gram of the substance through 1°C or 1°K','5 gram of the substance through 1°C or 1°K','10 gram of the substance through 1°C or 1°K','100 gram of the substance through 1°C or 1°K','NEW','11-168','11-144',NULL),
(60439,'chemistry','Molar heat capacity of a substance is the amount of heat required to raise the temprature of?','1 mol of substance through 1°C or 1°K','The molar heat capacity of a\nsubstance, Cm =\nCm = (C/n) is the heat capacity for\none mole of the substance and is the quantity\nof heat needed to raise the temperature of\none mole by one degree celsius (or one\nkelvin).  (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 163; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','10 mol of substance through 1°C or 1°K','100 mole of substance through 1°C or 1°K','1 gram of substance through 1°C or 1°K','1 mol of substance through 1°C or 1°K','NEW','11-168','11-144',NULL),
(60440,'chemistry','Consider the following statement: \n \n i.The magnitude of the coefficient depends on the size, composition and nature of the system in heat capacity. \n \n ii. when heat capacity is large, a given amount of heat results in only a small temprature rise. \n \n iii. Formula of heat capacity is C = q/∆V. \n \n iv. formula of heat capacity is C = q/∆T. which of the above statements are true?','i, ii, iv','The magnitude of the coefficient depends\non the size, composition and nature of the\nsystem. We can also write it as q = C ∆T.\nThe coefficient, C is called the heat capacity.\nThus, we can measure the heat supplied\nby monitoring the temperature rise, provided\nwe know the heat capacity.\nWhen C is large, a given amount of heat\nresults in only a small temperature rise. Water\nhas a large heat capacity i.e., a lot of energy is\nneeded to raise its temperature.\n.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 162; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','i, ii,iii','ii, iii, iv','only i and iv','i, ii, iv','NEW','11-168','11-144',NULL),
(60441,'chemistry','What is specific heat capacity of water?','1 cal g^-1 k^-1 or 4.18 J g^-1 k^-1','Specific heat capacity of water = 1 calg^-1k^-1 or 4.18 Jg^-1k^-1','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','1 cal g^-1 k^-1 or 4.50 J g°-1 k^-1','1 cal g^-1 k^-1 or 4.18 J g^-1 k^-1','1 cal g k^-1 or 4.18 J g k^-1','1 cal g^-1 k^-1 or 4.18 J g-1^k','NEW','11-168','11-144',NULL),
(60501,'chemistry','Bond dissociation energies of Cl2, PCl3 and PCl5 are 200, 150 and 128 kcal/mol respectively. calculate the enthalpy of formation of PCl5.','122 kcal','PCl3 + Cl2 ------- PCl5, ∆H = 150 + 200 - 128= 122 kcal. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 166; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','-122 kcal','350 kcal','-128 kcal','122 kcal','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60502,'chemistry','The enthalpy of formation of HCl gas is -22 kcal. Bond dissociation energies of H2 and Cl2 are 104, 58 kcal/mol, then calculate the bond dissociation energy of HCl(g).','103 kcal','let the Bond  dissociation energy of HCl  is (x), 1/2 H2 + 1/2 Cl2 ------HCl, \n\n∆H of formation = 1/2 × 104 + 1/2 × 58 - x, \n\n-22= (52+29)-x\nx= 103 kcal\n\n (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 166; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','-103 kcal','-150 kcal','-152 kcal','103 kcal','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60503,'chemistry','What is the enthalpy change for the given reaction, if enthalpies of formation of Al2O3 and Fe2O3 are -1850 kj/mol and -545 kj/ mol respectively? \n \n Fe2O3(s) + 3H2(g) ------ 2Fe(s) + 3H2O(l)','-1305 kj/mol','ΔH = ∑ΔHp - ∑ΔHr = -1850 - (- 545) = -1305 kj/mol (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 165; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','+1305 kj/mol','1300 kj/mol','-1389 kj/mol','-1305 kj/mol','NEW','11-169','11-145','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60503q.png'),
(60504,'chemistry','What will be the heat of reaction for the following reaction? Will the reaction be exothermic or endothermic? Fe₂O₃(s) + 3H₂(g) ------ 2Fe(s) + 3H₂O(l), ∆fH°(H₂O,l) = -595.83 kj/mol, ∆fH°(Fe₂O₃,s) = -365.2 kj/mol','-1422.29 kj/mol , exothermic','∆fH°(Fe,s) = 0, ∆fH°(H₂,g) = 0, ∆rH° = 3( -595.83) - 1(-365.2) = -1787.49 + 365.2 = -1422.29 kj/mol (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 166; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','-1422 kj/mol, exothermic','1322.28 kj/mol, endothermic','-1422.29 kj/mol , exothermic','-1322.28 kj/mol, exothermic','NEW','11-169','11-145','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60504q.png'),
(60505,'chemistry','For a reaction, CaCO₃(s) ------ CaO(s) + CO₂(g), ∆fH°(CaO) = -635.1 kj/mol, ∆fH°(CO₂) = -393.5 kj/mol and ∆fH°(CaCO₃) = -1206.9 kj/mol. Which of the following statement is correct?','Decomposition of CaCO₃ is an endothermic process and heat is provided for decomposition.','∆rH = ∑fHp - ∑fHr = [-635.1 + (-393.5)] - [-1206.9] = + 178.4 kj. so, Decomposition of CaCO₃ is an endothermic process\nand heat is provided for decomposition. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 166; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','A large amount of heat is evolved during the decomposition of CaCO₃.','Decomposition of CaCO₃ is an endothermic process and heat is provided for decomposition.','The amount of heat evolved cannot be calculated from the data provided.','∆rH = ∆Hr - ∆Hp','NEW','11-169','11-145','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60505q.png'),
(60506,'chemistry','Which of the following relationships is not correct?','∆H°r = ΣH°f(reactants) - ΣH°f(products)','∆H°r = ΣH°f(products) - ΣH°f(reaction) ,(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 165; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','∆H = ∆U +∆ngRT','∆H sub = ∆Hfusion + ∆Hvap','∆H°r = ΣH°f(reactants) - ΣH°f(products)','∆H°r = ΣB.E of reactants - ΣB.E of products','NEW','11-169','11-145','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60506q.png'),
(60507,'chemistry','The magnitude of the enthalpy change depends on the strength of the ----------------- interactions in the substance undergoing the phase transformations.','intermolecular','The magnitude of the enthalpy change depends on the strength\nof the intermolecular interactions in the substance undergoing the\n phase transformations. ,(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 165; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'1','fillup','in,ter,mol,ecul,ar,in,tra,mol,ecul,ar',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-169','11-145','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60507q.png'),
(60508,'chemistry','Which organic liquid has the intermolecular dipole-dipole interaction are significantly weaker?','Acetone','For an organic liquid, such as acetone, the intermolecular dipole-dipole interaction\nare significantly weaker. ,(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 165; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','Acetone','Ketone','Butane','Ethylene','NEW','11-169','11-145','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60508q.png'),
(60509,'chemistry','What is the change in enthalpy of sublimation of dry ice at 195K?','25.2 kJ/mol','The change in enthalpy of sublimation of dry ice at 195K is 25.5 kJ/mol. ,(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 165; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','26.6 kJ/mol','-26.6 kJ/mol','25.2 kJ/mol','-25.5 kJ/mol','NEW','11-169','11-145','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60509q.png'),
(60510,'chemistry','What is the change in enthalpy of sublimation of naphthalene?','73.0 kJ/mol','The change in enthalpy of sublimation of naphthalene is 73.0 kJ/mol. ,(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 165; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','70 kJ/mol','73.0 kJ/mol','25.5 kJ/mol','-73.0 kJ/mol','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60511,'chemistry','A swimmer coming out from a pool is covered with a film of water weighing about 18g. How much heat must be supplied to evaporate this water at 298 K ? Calculate the internal energy of vaporisation at 298K. ∆vap H for water at 298K= 44.01kJ mol–1','37.56 kJ/mol','We can represent the process of\nevaporation as 18gH2O(l) --------18gH2O(g) \nNo. of moles in 18g H2O(l) is = 18/18g/mol = 1mol \n∆vapU = ∆vapHΘ − P∆V = ∆vapHΘ − ∆ngRT (assuming steam behaving as an ideal\ngas).\n∆vapHΘ - ∆ngRT =(1)(8.314 J/K/mol)(373K)(10*3 kJ/J) = 40.66 kJ/mol\n∆vapUΘ = (40.66 - 3.10) kJ/mol = 37.56 kJ/mol\n. ,(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 166; Paragraph No.6.4)(NCERT Problem 6.7)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','37.56 kJ/mol','-37.56 kJ/mol','40 kJ/mol','39.97 kJ/mol','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60512,'chemistry','The enthalpy change accompanying a reaction is called --------- enthalpy.','reaction','The enthalpy change accompanying a reaction is called reaction enthalpy. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 164; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'1','fillup','re,ac,ti,on,pr,odu,cti,on',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60513,'chemistry','In a reaction enthalpy, ∆rH = ?','∆rH° = (sum of enthalpies of products) – (sum  of enthalpies of reactants)','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 165; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','∆rH° = (sum of enthalpies of exothermic reaction ) - (sum  of enthalpies of endothermic reaction)','∆rH° = (sum of enthalpies of reactants) – (sum  of enthalpies of products)','∆rH° = (sum of enthalpies of products) – (sum  of enthalpies of reactants)','∆rH° = (sum of enthalpies of products) + (sum  of enthalpies of reactants)','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60514,'chemistry','If the product is formed from element of 2 moles, instead of 1 mole then What is the standard enthalpy of formation?','∆rHΘ = 2∆fHΘ','Here two moles, instead of one mole of the product is formed from the elements, i.e., ∆rHΘ = 2∆fHΘ (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 167; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','∆rHΘ = 2∆fHΘ','∆rHΘ = 2/2∆fHΘ','∆rHΘ = 1/2∆fHΘ','∆rHΘ = ∆fHΘ','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60515,'chemistry','Which of the following expression is correct to calculate enthalpy of a reaction?','ΔHreaction = ΣB.E.reactants -∑ΔB.E.products','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 171; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','ΔHreaction = ∑ΔfHreactants - ∑ΔfHproducts','ΔHreaction = ∑ΔB.E.products - ∑ΔB.E.reactants','ΔHreaction = ΔH1 × ΔH2  × ΔH3  .....','ΔHreaction = ΣB.E.reactants -∑ΔB.E.products','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60516,'chemistry','The standard enthalpy of reaction is the enthalpy change for a reaction when all the participating substances are in their ---------------- states.','standard','The\nstandard enthalpy of reaction is the\nenthalpy change for a reaction when all\nthe participating substances are in their standard states. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 165; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'2','fillup','st,anda,rd,no,nst,and,ard',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60517,'chemistry','The standard state of a substance at a specified temperature is its pure form at ------ bar.','one','The standard state of a substance at a specified temperature is\n its pure form at one bar. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 165; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'1','fillup','on,e,te,n',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-169','11-145','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60517q.png'),
(60518,'chemistry','Choose the wrong correct Statements.','Ethanol boils at -78°C','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 168; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','Ethanol boils at 78°C','Ethanol melts at -114°C','Ethanol is fuel source','Ethanol boils at -78°C','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60519,'chemistry','What is the standard state of ethanol?','Liquid','The standard state can be defined at any temperature, generally used by the chemist is 25°C. Ethanol\n melts at −114 ∘C and boils at 78 ∘C, so ethanol is a liquid in its standard state. so ethanol is a fuel source. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 165; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','Solid','Liquid','vapour','pure','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60520,'chemistry','Sublimation is direct conversion of a solid into its?','vapour','sublimation is direct conversion of a solid into vapour. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 165; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','liquid','solid','vapour','both a and c','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60521,'chemistry','The standard enthalpy of reaction is related to ?','bond enthalpies of reactants and products in gas phase','The standard enthalpy of reaction is related to bond enthalpies of reactants and products in gas phase.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','bond enthalpies of reactants and products in gas phase','bond enthalpies of reactants and products in solid phase','reaction enthalpies of reactants and products in gas phase','bond enthalpies of products and reactants in gas phase','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60522,'chemistry','When a balanced chemical equation together with the value of its ∆rH is called?','thermochemical equation','A balanced chemical equation together with the value of\nits ∆rH is called thermochemical equation. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 168; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','thermochemical equation','unbalanced reaction enthalpy','balanced reaction enthalpy','thermochemical reaction','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60523,'chemistry','Consider the following statements of Thermochemical equations: \n \n i. The coefficients in a balanced thermo- chemical equation refer to the number of moles (never molecules) of reactants and products involved in the reaction. \n \n ii. The numerical value of ∆rHΘ refers to the number of moles of substances specified by an equation. Standard enthalpy change ∆rHΘ will have units as kJ mol–1. \n \n iii. A balanced chemical equations together with the value of its ∆rH is is called thermochemical equation. \n \n iv. A balanced chemical equations together with the value of its ∆rH is called balanced reaction enthalpy. which of the following statement is wrong.','only iv','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 168; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','both i and iv','only iv','both iv and ii','both i and ii','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60524,'chemistry','If enthalpy of an overall reaction X ----Υ along one route is ΔrH and ∆rH1, ∆rH2, ∆rH3 ...... representing enthalpies of reactions leading to same product Y and then ∆rH is','∆rH = ∆rH1 + ∆rH2 + ∆rH3.....','If a reaction takes place in several steps then its standard reaction enthalpy is the sum of the standard\n enthalpies of the intermediate reactions into which the overall reaction may be divided at the same  temperature. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 169; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','∆rH = ∆rH1 - ∆rH2 + ∆rH3.....','∆rH = ∆rH1 + ∆rH2 - ∆rH3.....','∆rH = ∆rH1 + ∆rH2 + ∆rH3.....','∆rH = ∆rH1 × ∆rH2 × ∆rH3.....','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60525,'chemistry','Which of the following does not represent enthalpy change during phase transformation?','standard enthalpy of formation','There is no phase change in formation. A new product is formed during the reaction. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 166; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','standard enthalpy of formation','standard enthalpy of sublimation','standard enthalpy of fusion','standard enthalpy of vaporisation','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(60526,'chemistry','In the reaction: S + 3/2 O₂ ----- SO₃ + 2x kcal and SO₂ + 1/2O₂ ---- SO₃ + y kcal, heat of formation of SO₂ is _','(2x- y)','S + 3/2 O₂ -------- SO₃ +2x kcal     (i)    ,  SO₂ +1/2 O₂ ------ SO₃ +y kcal  (ii)\nNow, substract eq(ii) from (i), we get,  S + O₂ ----- SO₂ + (2x + y) kcal (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 168; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','(x + y)','(2x- y)','(2x + y)','(x - y)','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(60527,'chemistry','Identify the correct increasing order of melting point in NaCl,C₆H₆,H₂O,CCl₄','NaCl>C₆H₆>H₂O>CCl₄','melting point of NaCl - 1081.0 \nmelting point of C₆H₆ - 278.65 \nmelting point of H₂O - 273.15 \nmelting point of CCl₄ - 250.16 \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 166; Paragraph No.6.4) Table 6.1','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','NaCl<C₆H₆>H₂O>CCl₄','NaCl>C₆H₆>H₂O<CCl₄','NaCl<C₆H₆<H₂O<CCl₄','NaCl>C₆H₆>H₂O>CCl₄','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(60528,'chemistry','Identify the correct decreasing order of standard enthalpy changes of fusion in N₂,HCl, CCl₄ and NH₃','N₂<HCl<CCl₄<NH₃','∆fusHΘ of N₂ - 0.72 \n∆fusHΘ of HCl - 1.992\n∆fusHΘ of CCl₄ - 2.5 \n∆fusHΘ of NH₃ - 5.65 \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 166; Paragraph No.6.4) Table 6.1','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','N₂<HCl<CCl₄>NH₃','N₂<HCl<CCl₄>NH₃','N₂<HCl<CCl₄<NH₃','N₂>HCl>CCl₄>NH₃','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(60529,'chemistry','What Is the standard molar enthalpy of formation of C (graphite)?','0','The standard molar enthalpy of formation of C (graphite) is zero. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 167; Paragraph No.6.4) Table 6.2','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','0','-84.68','1.89','-1.89','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(60530,'chemistry','Consider the following statements of standard enthalpies of formation: \n \n i.The standard molar enthalpy of formation of C (graphite) is zero. \n \n ii.The standard molar enthalpy of formation of Br2 is zero. \n \n iii.The standard molar enthalpy of formation of Cl2 is zero. \n \n iv.The standard molar enthalpy of formation of H2 is zero. \n \n Which of the following statements is correct?','All of the above','The standard molar enthalpy of formation of C (graphite) is zero.\nThe standard molar enthalpy of formation of Br2 is zero.\nThe standard molar enthalpy of formation of Cl2 is zero.\nThe standard molar enthalpy of formation of H2 is zero.\n.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 167; Paragraph No.6.4) Table 6.2','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','i, ii and iv','ii, iii and iv','i, ii, iii','All of the above','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(60531,'chemistry','What Is the standard molar enthalpy of formation of C (diamond)?','1.89','The standard molar enthalpy of formation of C (diamond) is +1.89.  (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 167; Paragraph No.6.4) Table 6.2','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','-1.89','0','1.89','49','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(60532,'chemistry','Identify the correct increasing order of standard molar enthalpy changes of formation in NO,NO₂,PCl₃,PCl₅','NO>NO₂>PCl₃>PCl₅','The standard molar enthalpy of formation of NO - +90.25\nThe standard molar enthalpy of formation of NO₂ - +33.18\nThe standard molar enthalpy of formation of PCl₃ - (-319.70)\nThe standard molar enthalpy of formation of PCl₅ - (-443.5)\n.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 167; Paragraph No.6.4) Table 6.2','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','NO>NO₂<PCl₃>PCl₅','NO<NO₂<PCl₃<PCl₅','NO>NO₂>PCl₃>PCl₅','NO>NO₂>PCl₃<PCl₅','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(60533,'chemistry','What is the standard enthalpy change for decomposition of calcium carbonate ,where the standard enthalpy of formation of CaO, CO₂ and CaCO₃ is -635.1 kJ/mol , -393.5 kJ/mol and -1206.9 kJ/mol.','178.3 kJ/mol','∆rHΘ = ∆fHΘ [CaO(s)]+ ∆fHΘ [CO₂\n(g)]\n– ∆fHΘ [CaCO₃(s)] \n[1(-635.1) + 1(-393.5) - 1(-1206.9)] kJ/mol = 178.3 kJ/mol\n.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 168; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','180.73 kJ/mol','-178.3 kJ/mol','178.3 kJ/mol','-180.73 kJ/mol','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(60534,'chemistry','The decomposition of CaCO₃(s) is an endothermic process or endothermic process?','Endothermic process','The decomposition of CaCO₃(s) is an endothermic process. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 168; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','Exothermic process','Endothermic process','Exothermic process and heat is involved','Endothermic process and heat is evolved','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(60535,'chemistry','Identify the correct order of standard enthalpy changes of fusion in Fe, H₂O, H₂','Fe = H₂','The standard molar enthalpy of formation of Fe is zero.\nThe standard molar enthalpy of formation of H₂ is zero.\n.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 167; Paragraph No.6.4) Table 6.2','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','Fe > H₂','Fe < H₂','Fe = H₂','H₂O > Fe','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(60536,'chemistry','Calculate the standard enthalpy change for the following reaction: \n \n Fe₂O₃(s) + 3H₂(g) -------- 2Fe(s) + 3H₂O(l), \n \n where ∆fHΘ (H₂O,l) = –285.83 kJ mol–1; \n ∆fHΘ (Fe₂O₃ ,s) = – 824.2 kJ mol–1','-33.3 kJ/mol','Fe₂O₃(s) + 3H₂(g) -------- 2Fe(s) + 3H₂O(l), in this reaction \n∆fHΘ (H₂O,l) = –285.83 kJ mol–1, ∆fHΘ (Fe₂O₃\n,s) = – 824.2 kJ mol–1 Also ∆fHΘ (Fe,s) =0 and ∆fHΘ (H2,g) = 0 so\n∆rHΘ = ∆fHΘ [H₂O\n(l)]\n– ∆fHΘ [Fe₂O₃(s)]  \n∆rHΘ = 3(-285.83 kJ/mol) - (-824.2 kJ/mol) = -33.3 kJ/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 168; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','39.3 kJ/mol','-33.3 kJ/mol','-39.3 kJ/mol','33.3 kJ/mol','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(60537,'chemistry','N₂(g) + 3H₂ (g) → 2NH₃ (g); ∆rHΘ = – 91.8 kJ. mol–1 \n \n What is the value of ∆rHΘ when this chemical equation is reversed?','91.8 kJ/mol','N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g);\n∆rHΘ = – 91.8 kJ. mol–1 \nfor this reaction, 2NH₃(g) → N₂(g) + 3H₂(g);  ∆rHΘ = +91.8 kJ. mol–1 \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 169; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','8.19 kJ/mol','-8.19 kJ/mol','91.8 kJ/mol','No change in ∆rHΘ','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(60538,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement: \n \n i. When a chemical equation is reversed, the value of ∆rHΘis reversed in sign \n \n ii.When a chemical equation is reversed, the value of ∆rHΘis reversed from positive to negative sign then,the process is exothermic. \n \n iii. When a chemical equation is reversed, the value of ∆rHΘis reversed from negative to positive sign then,the process is endothermic. \n \n iv. When a chemical equation is reversed, the value of ∆rHΘis reversed from positive to negative sign then,the process is endothermic.','i, ii, iii','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 169; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, iii','ii, iii, iv','i,ii, iv','i, iii, iv','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(60539,'chemistry','What is the unit of standard enthalpy change, ∆rHΘ ?','kJmol⁻¹','The unit of standard enthalpy change, ∆rHΘ is kJmol⁻¹\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 168; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','kJmol','J/k/mol','kJmol⁻¹','Jk⁻¹','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(60540,'chemistry','The statement, "the change in enthalpy of a chemical reaction is same whether the reaction takes place in one or several steps" is','Hess\'s law','If a reaction takes place in several steps then its standard enthalpy is the sum of the standard enthalpies of\nthe intermediate reactions into which the overall reaction may be divided at the same temperature. gg\n.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 169; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','Le Chatelier\'s law','Van\'t Hoff\'s law','Fisrt law of thermodynamics','Hess\'s law','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(60541,'chemistry','What Is the standard molar enthalpy of formation of Br₂ (l)?','0','The standard molar enthalpy of formation of Br₂(l) is zero. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 167; Paragraph No.6.4) Table 6.2','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','30.91','0','-30.91','None of the above','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(60542,'chemistry','What Is the standard molar enthalpy of formation of Cl₂ (g)?','0','The standard molar enthalpy of formation of Cl₂ (g) is zero. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 167; Paragraph No.6.4) Table 6.2','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','0','-110.53','110.53','-84.68','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(60543,'chemistry','What Is the standard molar enthalpy of formation of H₂(g)?','0','The standard molar enthalpy of formation of H₂ (g) is zero. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 167; Paragraph No.6.4) Table 6.2','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','217.97','-271.1','0','-217.97','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(60544,'chemistry','The enthalpy of formation of HCl is -92.3 kJ/mol. The enthalpy for the reaction 2HCl ------ H₂ + Cl₂.','-184.6 kJ/mol','H₂ + Cl₂ ---- 2HCl,  1/2 H₂ + 1/2 Cl₂ ---- HCl  \n2HCl ------ H₂ + Cl₂\n2 × (- 92.3) = 184.6 kJ/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 166; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','-184.6 kJ/mol','-150.3 kJ/mol','-203.3 kJ/mol','-88.6 kJ/mol','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(121415,'chemistry','Electrophiles are electron seeking species. Which of the following groups contain only electrophiles?  Refer diagram(12150)','b,c','A reagent that takes away an electron pair from reactive site is called electrophile (E+) i.e., electron seeking and the reaction is called electrophilic. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','NEW','11-351','11-273','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121415q.png'),
(60545,'chemistry','Why enthalpy of neutralization is less if either the acid or the base or both are weak?','A part of the heat is used up for dissociation of the weak acid or weak base or both.','The magnitude of the enthalpy change\ndepends on the strength of the intermolecular\ninteractions in the substance undergoing the\nphase transfomations. For example, the strong\nhydrogen bonds between water molecules hold\nthem tightly in liquid phase. For an organic\nliquid, such as acetone, the intermolecular\ndipole-dipole interactions are significantly\nweaker. Thus, it requires less heat to vaporise\n1 mol of acetone than it does to vaporize 1 mol\nof water.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 165; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','A part of the heat is used up for dissociation of the strong acid or strong base or both.','A part of the heat is used up for dissociation of the weak acid or weak base or both.','A part of the heat is used up for dissociation of the weak acid or strong base or both.','A part of the heat is used up for dissociation of the strong acid or weak base or both.','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(60546,'chemistry','The enthalpy of formation of methane at constant pressure and 300 K is – 78.84 kJ. What will be the enthalpy of formation at constant volume?','-81.35 kJ','C(s) + 2H2(g) ------ CH4(g) , ∆H = -78.84 kJ\n∆H = 78.84 kJ, ∆ng = 1 - 2 = -1mol\nR = 8.314 × 10^-3 kJ/K/mol, T = 300 K\nAccording to the question\n∆H = ∆U + ∆ngRT \n∆U = ∆H - ∆ngRT = (-78.84kJ) - (1 mol) × (8.314 × 10^-3 kJ/K/mol) × 300 K\n= - 81.35 kJ','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','-81.38 kJ','-81.35 kJ','-81.40kJ','-80.35 kJ','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(60547,'chemistry','Water requires heat for ?','evaporation','Water requires heat for evaporation.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 165; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','evaporation','boiling','enhance reactant','enhance product','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(60548,'chemistry','If water freezes, then process is ?','reversed and equal amount of heat released','If water freezes, then process is reversed and equal amount of heat is given off to the\nsurroundings.                  (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 165; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Reaction enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','irreversible and equal amount of heat released','reversed and equal amount of heat released','irreversible and unequal amount of heat is released','reversed and unequal amount of heat is released','NEW','11-171','11-147',NULL),
(60549,'biology','In dicot stem, medullary rays are','few layers of radially placed parenchymatous cells','In between the vascular bundles there are few layers of radially placed parenchymatous cells, which constitute medullary rays.\n\n(Reference  s: NCERT Chapter 6; Page 93; Paragraph 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Anatomy of Dicots and Monocots',NULL,'2','mcq','tangentially placed parenchymatous cells','few layers of radially placed parenchymatous cells','present above vascular bundles','present just above the phloem','NEW','11-093','11-076',NULL),
(60550,'biology','Ring arrangement of vascular bundles is a characteristics of','Dicot stem','A large no. of vascular bundles are arranged in ring: the ring arrangement of vascular bundles is a characteristics of dicot stem.\n\n(Reference  s: NCERT Chapter 6; Page 93; Paragraph 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Anatomy of Dicots and Monocots',NULL,'2','mcq','Dicot root','Dicot stem','Monocot stem','Monocot root','NEW','11-093','11-076',NULL),
(60551,'biology','Which of them is not true about pith of dicot stem?','parenchymatous cells without intercellular spaces','A large no. of rounded, parenchymatous cells with large intercellular spaces which occupy the central portion of the stem constiute the pith.\n\n(Reference  s: NCERT Chapter 6; Page 93; Paragraph 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Anatomy of Dicots and Monocots',NULL,'2','mcq','rounded parenchymatous cells','parenchymatous cells without intercellular spaces','parenchymatous cells with intercellular spaces','occupies the central portion','NEW','11-093','11-076',NULL),
(60552,'biology','Sclerenchymatous hypodermis can be found in','Monocot stem','The monocot stem has a sclerenchymatous hypodermis, a large no. of scattered vascular bundles, each surrounded by sclerenchymatous bundle sheath and a large conspicous ground tissue.\n\n(Reference  s: NCERT Chapter 6; Page 93; Paragraph 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Anatomy of Dicots and Monocots',NULL,'2','mcq','Dicot root','Dicot stem','Monocot stem','Monocot root','NEW','11-093','11-076',NULL),
(60553,'biology','In monocot stem, each vascular bundle is surrounded by','sclerenchymatous bundle sheath cells','The monocot stem has a sclerenchymatous hypodermis, a large no. of scattered vascular bundles, each surrounded by sclerenchymatous bundle sheath and a large conspicous ground tissue.\n\n(Reference  s: NCERT Chapter 6; Page 93; Paragraph 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Anatomy of Dicots and Monocots',NULL,'2','mcq','parenchymatous cells with intercellular spaces','sclerenchymatous bundle sheath cells','parenchymatous cells in the form of semi lunar patches','conspicous ground tissue','NEW','11-093','11-076',NULL),
(60554,'biology','In Monocotyledonous stem, central vascular bundles are','larger than peripheral vascular bundles','Peripheral vascular bundles are generally smaller than centrally located ones.\n\n(Reference  s: NCERT Chapter 6; Page 93; Paragraph 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Anatomy of Dicots and Monocots',NULL,'2','mcq','larger than peripheral vascular bundles','smaller than peripheral vascular bundles','same as that of centrally located ones','none of the above','NEW','11-093','11-076',NULL),
(60555,'biology','Water containing cavities are present in','vascular bundles','The phloem parenchyma is absent, and water containing cavities are present within vascular bundles.\n\n(Reference  s: NCERT Chapter 6; Page 93; Paragraph 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Anatomy of Dicots and Monocots',NULL,'2','mcq','phloem parenchyma','vascular bundles','hypodermis','bundle sheath','NEW','11-093','11-076',NULL),
(60556,'biology','Which of them is not present in Dicot leaf','Cortex','Dorsiventral leaf shows three main parts, namely epidermis, mesophyll and vascular systems.\n\n(Reference  s: NCERT Chapter 6; Page 93; Paragraph 3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Anatomy of Dicots and Monocots',NULL,'2','mcq','Epidermis','Cortex','Mesophyll','Vascular system','NEW','11-093','11-076',NULL),
(60557,'biology','In dicot leaf, cuticle can be seen on','abaxial epidermis','The epidermis which covers both the upper surface(adaxial epidermis)\n and lower surface(abaxial epidermis) of the leaf has a conspicous cuticle.\n\n(Reference  s: NCERT Chapter 6; Page 93; Paragraph 3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Anatomy of Dicots and Monocots',NULL,'2','mcq','abaxial epidermis','mesophyll','palisade parenchyma','bundle sheath cells','NEW','11-093','11-076',NULL),
(60558,'biology','Vascular bundles of Dicot leaf is surrounded by','Thick walled bundle sheath cells','The vascular bundles are surrounded by layer of thick walled bundle sheath cells.\n(References: NCERT Chapter 6; Page 93; Paragraph 3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Anatomy of Dicots and Monocots',NULL,'2','mcq','loosely arranged spongy parenchyma','Thick walled bundle sheath cells','thin walled bundle sheath cells','none of the above','NEW','11-093','11-076',NULL),
(60559,'biology','Bulliform cells are','large cells along the veins','In grasses, certain adaxial epidermal cells along the veins modify themselves into large, empty colorless cells, These are called bulliform cells.\n\n(Reference  s: NCERT Chapter 6; Page 94; Paragraph 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Anatomy of Dicots and Monocots',NULL,'1','mcq','small, colored cells','empty cells along the abaxial epidermal cells','large cells along the veins','found in dorsiventral leaf','NEW','11-094','11-077',NULL),
(60560,'biology','What is the innermost layer of cortex?','Endodermis','The innermost layer of the cortex is called endodermis. It comprises a single layer of barrel-shape cells without any intercellular spaces(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:91, paragraph no:1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Anatomy of Dicots and Monocots',NULL,'2','mcq','Pericycle','Conjunctive tissue','Endodermis','Exodermis','NEW','11-091','11-074',NULL),
(60601,'chemistry','Combustion reactions are ----------- in nature.','exothermic','Combustion reactions are exothermic in nature. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 170; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'1','fillup','ex,oth,erm,ic,en,dot,herm,ic',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-172','11-148',NULL),
(60602,'chemistry','Combustion reactions is important in?','all of the above','Combustion reactions is important in industry, rocketry and other walks of life.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','In industry','Rocketry','Generate electricity','all of the above','NEW','11-172','11-148',NULL),
(60603,'chemistry','The enthalpy change per mole ( or per unit amount) of a substance, when it undergoes combustion and all reactants and products being in their standard states at the specified temperature is called','standard enthalpy of combustion','The enthalpy change per mole ( or per unit amount) of a substance, when it undergoes\ncombustion and all reactants and products being in their standard\nstates at the specified temperature is called standard enthalpy of combustion\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 168; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','standard enthalpy of combustion','standard enthalpy of formation','standard enthalpy of fusion','Enthalpy of atomization','NEW','11-172','11-148',NULL),
(60604,'chemistry','Consider the following statements of standard enthalpies of combustion; \n \n i. Standard enthalpy of combustion are important in industry, rocketry and other walks of life \n \n ii.Combustion reactions are endothermic in nature. \n \n iii.Standard enthalpy of combustion is defined as the enthalpy change per mole of a substance. \n \n iv. Combustion reactions are exothermic in nature. \n \n Which of the following statement is right?','i, iii,iv','Combustion reactions are exothermic in\nnature. These are important in industry,\nrocketry, and other\nwalks of life. Standard\nenthalpy of combustion is defined as the\nenthalpy change per mole (or per unit amount)\nof a substance, when it undergoes combustion\nand all the reactants and products being in\ntheir standard states at the specified\ntemperature.  (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 168; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','i , ii iii','ii, iii, iv','i, iii,iv','only ii and iii','NEW','11-172','11-148',NULL),
(60605,'chemistry','Which gas is filled in cylinders?','Butane','Cooking gas in cylinders contains mostly butane. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 170; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Butane','Butyne','Methane','Butene','NEW','11-173','11-149',NULL),
(60606,'chemistry','How many heat is released during complete combustion of one mole of butane?','2658 kJ','During complete combustion\nof one mole of butane, 2658 kJ of heat is\nreleased. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 170; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','-2658 J','2658 kJ','2658 J','-2658 kJ','NEW','11-173','11-149','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60606q.png'),
(60607,'chemistry','The heat of combustion of methane and propane is 890 and 2220 kJ/mol respectively.Which of the two has higher efficiency as fuel per gram and the amount of heat produced per gram?','Methane, 55.62 kJ/g','Heat produced by methane = 890/16 = 55.625 kJ/g\nHeat produced by propane = 2220/44 = 50.45 kJ/g\nso, methane has higher efficiency as fuel per gram and the amount of heat produced per gram.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 170; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Propane, 50.45 kJ/g','Methane, 55.62 kJ/g','Propane, 44 kJ/g','Methane, 16 kJ/g','NEW','11-173','11-149',NULL),
(60608,'chemistry','What will be the amount of heat evolved by burning 20 L of graphite under standard conditions? (Given heat of formation of C, O2 and CO2 are -393.5, 0 and 0 kJ/mol respectively)','351.33 kJ','C + O2 ----- CO2\n∆H = ∆P - ∆R , ∆H°f(CO2) - ∆H°f(C) + ∆H°f(O2) \n-393.5 - 0+0 = -393.5 kJ/mol\n22.4 L of C (1mole) gives 393.5 kJ\n20 L of C will give 393.5/22.4× 20 = 351.33 kJ\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 170; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','393.5 kJ','0 kJ','351.33 kJ','-351.33 kJ','NEW','11-173','11-149','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60608q.png'),
(60609,'chemistry','What is the enthalpy of combustion of H2? (Given heat of formation of H2O(l), O2(g),H2(g) are -285.8, 0 and 0 respectively)','-571.6 kJ/mol','2H2 + O2 ----- 2HO2\n∆H = ∆P - ∆R , ∆H°f(2HO2) - ∆H°f(2H2) + ∆H°f(O2) \n2 × (- 285.8) - 2 ×0+0 = -571.6 kJ/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 170; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','571.6 kJ','-571.6 kJ/mol','-571.6 kJ','+571.6 kJ/mol','NEW','11-173','11-149',NULL),
(60610,'chemistry','For a reaction: C(s) + O₂(g) --------- CO₂(g) What is the relation between enthalpy of reaction(∆Hr), enthalpy of formation of CO₂ (∆Hf) and enthalpy of combustion of carbon(∆Hcomb)?','∆Hr = ∆Hf = ∆Hcomb','The reaction shows combustion of one mole of carbon, ∆Hreaction is\nenthalpy of combustion since 1 mole of CO₂ is formed.∆Hreaction\nis also the enthalpy of formation of CO₂.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 170; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','∆Hr = ∆Hf < ∆Hcomb','∆Hr = ∆Hf + ∆Hcomb','∆Hr = ∆Hf = ∆Hcomb','∆Hr > ∆Hf = ∆Hcomb','NEW','11-173','11-149',NULL),
(60611,'chemistry','The heat of combustion of C, S and CS2 are -393.3 kJ, -293.7 kJ and -1108.76 kJ. What will be the heat of formation of CS2?','-128.02 kJ','C + S2 ---- CS2\n∆H = ∑ΔHp - ∑ΔHr = -1108.76 -[ -393.3 + 2(-293.7)]  \n= -1108. 76 + 393.3 + 587.4 = -128.02 kJ (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 170; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','-128.02 kJ','128.02 kJ','321.32 kJ','231.21 kJ','NEW','11-173','11-149',NULL),
(60612,'chemistry','How much heat is evolved if 46.8 g of benzene is burnt and if the heat of combustion of benzene is -3264 kJ/mol?','1958.4 kJ','78 g of C6H6 gives 3264 kJ of heat\n46.8 g C6H6 will give 3264/78 × 46.8 = 1958.4 kJ\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 170; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','2054.4 kJ','1958.4 kJ','158.4 kJ','199.4 kJ','NEW','11-173','11-149',NULL),
(60613,'chemistry','The heat of combustion of C, O and CO are -393.3 kJ, 0 kJ and - 283.5 kJ. What will be the heat of formation of CO?','110 kJ','C + 1/2O2 ---- CO\n∆H = ∑ΔHp - ∑ΔHr = -283.5 -[(-393.5 - 0)]  \n= -283.5 + 393.5 = -110 kJ (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 170; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','-110 kJ','85 kJ','110 kJ','95 kJ','NEW','11-173','11-149',NULL),
(60614,'chemistry','Enthalpy of combustion of carbon to CO2is -393.5 kJ mol-1. Calculate the heat released upon formation of 35.2 g of CO2 from carbon and dioxygen gas.','-314.8 kJ/mol','C(s) + O2(g) ------ CO2(g) , ∆Hf = -393.5 kJ/mol\n(1 mol = 44g), heat released on formation of 44g CO2 = -393.5 kJ/mol\nHeat released on formation of 35.2 g of CO2\n= -393.5/44 ×35.2 g = -314.8 kJ/mol (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 170; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','324.68 kJ/mol','-321.5 kJ/mol','-314.8 kJ/mol','314.8 kJ/mol','NEW','11-173','11-149',NULL),
(60615,'chemistry','When 100 L of a gas mixture of propane and pentane is perfectly combusted at 0°c and 1 atmosphere, 18 L of Oxygen at the same temperature and pressure is consumed.The amount of heat released from the combustion in kJ, ∆H combustion of C₃H₈ = 2220 kJ/mol and ∆H combustion of C₄H₁₀ = 119.9 kJ/mol','1987.66 kJ','C₃H₈ + 5O₂ -------- 3CO₂ + 4H₂O \nC₅H₁₂ + 8O₂ -------- 5CO₂ + 6H₂O \nNo. of moles in gaseous mixture\nC₃H₈ + C₅H₁₂ = 100/22.4 = 0.466 mole\nNo. of mole of O₂ = 18/22.4 = 0.803 mole\nLet x mole of C₃H₈ is their in gaseous mixture so, the number of mole of C5H12 would be  0.466 - x\nThen mole of O₂ consumed, \n5x + (0.446 - x)8 = 0.803 \n3x = 3.568 - 0.803, x = 2.765/3 = 0.921 \nTotal amount of heat liberated \n0.921 × 2220 - 0.475 × 119.9     ( 0.446 - 0.921 = 0.475) \n2044.62 - 56.9525 = 1987.66 kJ (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 170; Paragraph No.6.5)⁻','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','2101.57 kJ','- 56.9525 kJ','1987.66 kJ','2044.62 kJ','NEW','11-173','11-149',NULL),
(60616,'chemistry','Two reactions are given below: \n \n C(graphite) + O₂(g) ---------> CO₂(g) ; ∆H = -393.7 kJ \n C(diamond) ----------> C(graphite) ; ∆H = -2.1 kJ \n \n What quantity of diamond will give 1600 kJ of heat on burning?','48.5 g','C(diamond) + O₂(g) ------ CO₂(g) \n-393.7 + (-2.1) = -395.8 \n12 g of diamond gives 395.8 kJ\n1600 kJ will be given by 12/395.8 × 1600 = 48.5 g\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 170; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','48.5 g','50 g','45.5. g','35.6 g','NEW','11-174','11-150',NULL),
(60617,'chemistry','Consider the following statements of enthalpy of atomization \n \n i. In enthalpy change of atomization is the change on breaking one mole of bonds complety to obtain atoms in the gas phase. \n \n ii. In diatomic molecules, the enthalpy of atomization is also the bond dissociation enthalpy. \n \n iii. In enthalpy of atomization, the product are only atoms of C and H. \n \n iv. In enthalpy of atomization, the product are only atoms of O and H. \n \n Which of the following statement are wrong?','only iv','H₂(g) → 2H(g); ∆aHΘ\n = 435.0 kJ mol–1\nYou can see that H atoms are formed by\nbreaking H–H bonds in dihydrogen. The\nenthalpy change in this process is known as\nenthalpy of atomization, ∆aHΘ. It is the\nenthalpy change on breaking one mole of\nbonds completely to obtain atoms in the gas phase.\nIn case of diatomic molecules, like\ndihydrogen (given above), the enthalpy of\natomization is also the bond dissociationenthalpy. The other examples of enthalpy of\natomization can be\nCH₄\n(g) → C(g) + 4H(g); ∆aHΘ = 1665 kJ mol–1 .\nNote that the products are only atoms of C\nand H in gaseous phase. \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 171; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','only iii','both i and iv','only iv','both i and ii','NEW','11-174','11-150',NULL),
(60618,'chemistry','In the reaction: Na(s) -------- Na(g) The enthalpy of atomization is same the ?','Enthalpy of sublimation','In this case, the enthalpy of atomization is\nsame as the enthalpy of sublimation.\nIn this reaction solid is directly change into gaseous state. \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 171; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Enthalpy of atomization','Enthalpy of vaporisation','Bond enthalpy','Enthalpy of sublimation','NEW','11-174','11-150','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/60618q.png'),
(60619,'chemistry','Chemical reactions involve the breaking ang making of ---------- bonds.','Chemical','Chemical reactions involve the breaking ang making of chemical bonds. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 171; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'1','fillup','ch,emic,al,ph,ysic,al',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-174','11-150',NULL),
(60620,'chemistry','Energy is required to ------------ a bond','Break','Energy is required to break a bond (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 171; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'1','fillup','Br,ea,k,ma,ke',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-174','11-150',NULL),
(60621,'chemistry','Energy is released when a bond is ------------- .','formed','Energy is released when a bond is formed.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 171; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'1','fillup','fo,rm,ed,de,for,med',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-174','11-150',NULL),
(121416,'chemistry','Which of the following pairs are position isomers. Refer diagram(12151)','II and III','Position isomers are compounds that have the same molecular formula and functional groups but differ in the position of the functional group(s) or substituent(s) on the carbon chain.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','I and II','II and III','II and IV','III and IV','NEW','11-348','11-270','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121416q.png'),
(2000111115,'physics','If $\\chi_{m}$ is the susceptibility and $\\mu_{r}$ is the relative permeability of a ferromagnetic substance, then','D','Ferromagnetic substance are those substance, when placed in magnetic field it can get highly magnetised. <br>So, susceptibility of ferromagnetic substance is much greater than 1. <br>$\\chi_{m} \\gg 1$<br>Hence, the correct option is (4).','Magnetic Properties of Materials','Magnetism and Matter','Easy',NULL,'${\\chi _m} <  < 1$','${\\mu _r} <  < 1$','${\\mu _r} = 0$','${\\chi _m} >  > 1$',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(60622,'chemistry','Consider the following statements of bond enthalpy: \n \n i. Chemical reactions involve the breaking and making of chemical bonds. \n \n ii. Physical reactions involve the breaking and making of chemical bonds. \n \n iii. In diatomic molecules, the enthalpy of atomization is also the bond dissociation enthalpy. \n \n iv. The bond dissociation enthalpy is the change in enthalpy when one mole of covalent bonds of a gaseous covalent compound is broken to form products in the solid phase. \n \n Which of the following statements is correct?','i, iii, and iv','Chemical reactions involve the breaking and\nmaking of chemical bonds. Energy is required\nto break a bond and energy is released when\na bond is formed. It is possible to relate heat\nof reaction to changes in energy associated\nwith breaking and making of chemical bonds. \n In case of diatomic molecules, like\ndihydrogen the enthalpy of change involved in this process is the\n bond dissociation\nenthalpy.  \nThe bond dissociation enthalpy is the change\nin enthalpy when one mole of covalent bonds\nof a gaseous covalent compound is broken to\nform products in the gas phase.i. This statement is correct. Chemical reactions typically involve the breaking of existing chemical bonds in reactants and the formation of new chemical bonds in products.\n\niii. This statement is correct. In diatomic molecules, the bond dissociation enthalpy is equal to the enthalpy of atomization. Bond dissociation enthalpy refers to the energy required to break a particular bond in one mole of a gaseous substance, and in diatomic molecules, breaking the bond also results in the formation of two separate atoms.\n\niv. This statement is correct. The bond dissociation enthalpy is defined as the enthalpy change when one mole of covalent bonds in a gaseous covalent compound is broken to form products in the solid phase. It is a measure of the strength of a particular covalent bond.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 171; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','both ii and iv','only ii','i, iii, and iv','only iv','NEW','11-174','11-150',NULL),
(60623,'chemistry','Calculate the mean bond enthalpy of C - H bond by using this reaction: \n \n CH₄ (g) → CH₃ (g)+H(g); ∆bondHΘ = +427 kJ mol–1 \n \n CH₃ (g) → CH₂ (g)+H(g); ∆bondHΘ = +439 kJ mol–1 \n \n CH₂ (g) → CH(g)+H(g); ∆bondHΘ= +452 kJ mol–1 \n \n CH (g) → C(g)+H(g); ∆bondHΘ = +347 kJ mol–1','416 kJ/mol','Using Hess\' law to determine mean bond enthalpy \n∆aHΘ = ∆bondHΘ1 + ∆bondHΘ2 + ∆bondHΘ3 + ∆bondHΘ4 \n∆aHΘ = 427 kJ/mol + 439 kJ/mol + 452 kJ/mol + 347 kJ/mol \nTherefore, \nCH₄(g) ----- C(g) + 4H(g); ∆aHΘ = 1665 kJ/mol\n∆C-H HΘ = 1/4(∆aHΘ) = 1/4(1665 kJ/mol) = 416 kJ/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 171; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','347 kJ/mol','416 kJ/mol','452 kJ/mol','-1665 kJ/mol','NEW','11-174','11-150',NULL),
(60624,'chemistry','We use this reaction enthalpy ∆r HΘ = ∑bond enthalpies reactants - ∑bond enthalpies products when all substances (reactants and products) in the reaction are in?','Gaseous state','We use this reaction enthalpy\n∆r HΘ =\n\n∑bond enthalpies\nreactants - \n∑bond enthalpies\nproducts\nwhen all substances (reactants and products) in the reaction are in\ngaseous state. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 172; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Solid state','Vapour state','Gaseous state','Liquid state','NEW','11-174','11-150',NULL),
(60625,'chemistry','Consider the three oxygen containing compounds: OF2 ,ClO2 and H2O. The increasing order of mean single bond enthalpies of the four elements involved in these compounds is:','F > O > H > Cl','Mean bond enthalpy of F - 569 kJ/mol\nMean bond enthalpy of O - 464 kJ/mol\nMean bond enthalpy of H - 435.8 kJ/mol\nMean bond enthalpy of Cl - 431 kJ/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 172; Paragraph No.6.5) Table 6.3(a)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','F > O > H < Cl','F > O < H > Cl','F < O < H < Cl','F > O > H > Cl','NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(60626,'chemistry','What is the correct order of mean bond enthalpy of single, double and triple bonds between carbon atoms?','C ≡ C > C = C > C - C','Mean bond enthalpy of C ≡ C - 837 kJ/mol\nMean bond enthalpy of C = C - 611 kJ/mol\nMean bond enthalpy of C - C - 414 kJ/mol\n\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 172; Paragraph No.6.5) Table 6.3(b)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','C ≡ C > C = C < C - C','C ≡ C > C = C > C - C','C - C > C ≡ C > C = C >','C ≡ C < C = C < C - C','NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(60627,'chemistry','Bond energies of C - C and O - O are 347 kJ/mol and 351 kJ/mol respectively.∆Hf for CO is 110.5 kJ/mol.What will be the bond energy of C - O?','412 kJ','C + 1/2O2 ------- CO\n∆Hf(CO) = 110.5 kJ/mol, C - C =347 kJ/mol, O - O = 351 kJ/mol\n110.5 = 347 + 1/2 × 351-B.E(C -- O)\n110.5 = 522.5 - B.E( C -- O) \nB.E(C -- O) = 522.5 - 110.5 = 412 kJ\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 172; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','412 kJ','421 kJ','512 kJ','410kJ','NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(60628,'chemistry','Bond energies of N = N, O = O and N = O are 418 kJ/mol and 489 kJ/mol 607 kJ/mol respectively. What will be the ∆Hf of N = O?','-203.5 kJ/mol','1/2N2 + 1/2O2 ------- NO\nN=N= 418 kJ/mol, O=O = 489 kJ/mol, N=O = 607 kJ/mol\n∆Hf(N=O) = 1/2 × 418 + 1/2 × 489 - 607\n∆Hf(N=O) = -203.5 kJ/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 172; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','-203.5 kJ/mol','-1010.5 kJ/mol','203.5 kJ/mol','1010.5 kJ/mol','NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(60629,'chemistry','Bond energies of si - si and Cl - Cl are 293 kJ/mol and 431 kJ/mol respectively.∆Hf for SiCl₄ is 657 kJ/mol.What will be the bond energy of Si - Cl₄?','498 kJ','si + 2Cl₂ ------- SiCl₄\n∆Hf(SiCl₄) = 657 kJ/mol, Si - Si = 293  kJ/mol, Cl - Cl = 431 kJ/mol\n657 = 293 + 2 × 431 -B.E(Si -- cl₄)\n657 = 1155 - B.E( C -- O) \nB.E(C -- O) = 1155 -  657 = 498 kJ\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 172; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','398 kJ','450kJ','498 kJ','500 kJ','NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(60630,'chemistry','What will be the ∆H for the reaction, CH₂Cl₂ --------- C + 2H + 2Cl B.E of C-H and C-Cl bonds are 416 kJ/mol and 325 kJ/mol respectively)','1482 kJ','In CH₂Cl₂ , C makes two single bond with H and Cl\n∆H = ∑ B.E.r - ∑B.E.p\n     = 2 × B.E(C-H) + 2 × B.E(C-Cl) \n     = 2 × 416 + 2 × 325 = 1482 kJ\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 172; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','1482 kJ','1000 kJ','1523 kJ','1628 kJ','NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(60631,'chemistry','Dissociation of CH₂ takes place in two step \n \n CH₂ (g) → CH(g)+H(g); ∆bondHΘ= +452 kJ mol–1 \n \n CH(g) → C(g)+H(g); ∆bondHΘ = +347 kJ mol–1 \n \n What is the mean bond enthalpy of C ---- H bond?','399.5 kJ','Using Hess\' law to determine mean bond enthalpy \n∆aHΘ = ∆bondHΘ1 + ∆bondHΘ2 \n∆aHΘ = 452 kJ/mol + 347 kJ/mol \nTherefore, \nCH₂(g) ----- C(g) + 2H(g); ∆aHΘ = 799 kJ/mol\n∆C-H HΘ = 1/2(∆aHΘ) = 1/2(799 kJ/mol) = 399.5 kJ/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 171; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','395.5 kJ','399.9 kJ','499.5 kJ','399.5 kJ','NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(60632,'chemistry','Bond energies of few bonds are given below: \n \n H-H = 435 kJ/mol, Br-Br = 368 kJ/mol, H-Br = 362 \n \n Using the B.E., calculate ∆H for the following reaction, H₂ + Br₂ ----- 2HBr','79 kJ/mol','H₂ + Br₂ ---- 2HBr, ∆H = B.E(H-H) + (Br-Br) - (2 × H-Br)\n435 + 368 - 2 × 362 = 435 + 368 - 724 = 803 - 724 = 79 kJ/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 171; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','79 kJ/mol','48 kJ/mol','80 kJ/mol','53 kJ/mol','NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(60633,'chemistry','Match the following','H₂(g) → 2H(g)-∆aHΘ\n=435.0 kJ/mol,\nCH₄(g) → C(g) + 4H(g)- ∆aHΘ = 1665 kJ/mol,Na(s) → Na(g) - ∆aHΘ = 108.4 kJ/mol,H₂(g) → 2H(g) -∆H–HHΘ = 435.0 kJ/mol','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 171; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','match','H₂(g) → 2H(g),∆aHΘ=435.0 kJ/mol','CH₄(g) → C(g) + 4H(g),∆aHΘ = 1665 kJ/mol','Na(s) → Na(g),∆aHΘ = 108.4 kJ/mol','H₂(g) → 2H(g),∆H–HHΘ = 435.0 kJ/mol','NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(60634,'chemistry','Match the following','1Latm -101.32500J, 1Latm -24.21calorie, 1calorie -4.184J, R -2×10*3kcal','1Latm = 101.32500J 1Latm = 24.21calorie, 1calorie = 4.184J, R = 2×10*3kcal','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','match','1Latm,101.32500J','1Latm,24.21calorie','1calorie,4.184J','R,2×10*3','NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(60635,'chemistry','Match the following','H₂(g) → 2H(g)-∆H–HHΘ=435.0 kJ mol–1,Cl₂(g) → 2Cl(g)-∆Cl–ClHΘ=242 kJ mol–1,O₂(g) → 2O(g)-∆O=OHΘ= 428 kJ mol–1,N₂(g) → 2N(g)-∆N=NHΘ= 418 kJ mol–1','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 171; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','match','H₂(g) → 2H(g),∆H–HHΘ=435.0 kJ mol–1','Cl₂(g) → 2Cl(g),∆Cl–ClHΘ=242 kJ mol–1','O₂(g) → 2O(g),∆O=OHΘ= 428 kJ mol–1','N₂(g) → 2N(g),∆N=NHΘ= 418 kJ mol–1','NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(60636,'chemistry','Given N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₂(g) ; ∆rHΘ= –92.4 kJ mol–1 \n \n What is the standard enthalpy of formation of NH₃ gas?','-46.2 kJ/mol','∆HΘ = 1/2 ∆rHΘ = 1/2 ×(-92.4 kJ/mol) = -46.2 kJ/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 171; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','-46.2 kJ/mol','-184.8 kJ/mol','-46 kJ/mol','46.2 kJ/mol','NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(60637,'chemistry','Bond energies of PCl₅, PCl₃ and Cl₂ are 260 kJ/mol, 322 kJ/mol and 240 kJ/mol. Calculate ∆H for the following reaction, PCl₅ = PCl₃ + Cl₂','-302 kJ/mol','∆H = B.E of PCl₅ - B.E of PCl₃ + B.E of Cl₂\n∆H = 260 - 322 + 240 = 260 - 562 = -302 kJ/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 171; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','-302 kJ/mol','-322 kJ/mol','302 kJ/mol','822 kJ/mol','NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(60638,'chemistry','Which is the correct order of bond energy of double and triple bonds between phosphorus and nitrogen?','N ≡ N>P≡P>P-P>N-N','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 171; Paragraph No.6.5) Table 6.3 (a) and 6.3(b)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','N ≡ N>P≡P>P-P<N-N','N ≡ N<P≡P<P-P<N-N','N ≡ N>P≡P<P-P<N-N','N ≡ N>P≡P>P-P>N-N','NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(60639,'chemistry','Bond energies of P-P and Cl-Cl are 490 kJ/mol and 255 kJ/mol respectively. \n \n ∆Hf for PCl₃ is -306 kJ/mol.What will be the bond energy of P-Cl?','1025.5 kJ/mol','The reaction is here\n2P + 3Cl₂ ----- 2PCl₃\n∆Hf = ∑ B.E.r - ∑B.E.p\n-306 = 2(B.E. P-P) + 3(B.E. Cl- Cl) - 2(B.E. P-Cl)\n-306 = 2×490 + 3×325  - 2(B.E. P-Cl) \n-306 = 1745  - 2(B.E. P-Cl) \n2(B.E. P-Cl) = 2051\n(B.E. P-Cl) = 2051/2 = 1025.5 kJ/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 172; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','1025.5 kJ/mol','1125.5 kJ/mol','2015 kJ/mol','1745 kJ/mol','NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(60640,'chemistry','Enthalpy of solution of a substance is the enthalpy change when one mole of it dissolves in a specified amount of ------------.','solvent','Enthalpy of solution of a substance is the\nenthalpy change when one mole of it dissolves\nin a specified amount of solvent. The enthalpy\nof solution at infinite dilution is the enthalpy\nchange observed on dissolving the substance\nin an infinite amount of solvent when the\ninteractions between the ions (or solute molecules) are negligible.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 172; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','fillup','so,lve,nt,so,luti,on',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(60641,'chemistry','When an ionic compound dissolves in a solvent, the ions leave their ordered positions on the ----------- lattice.','crystal','When an ionic compound dissolves in a\nsolvent, the ions leave their ordered positions on\nthe crystal lattice. These are now more free in\nsolution. But solvation of these ions (hydration\nin case solvent is water) also occurs at the same\ntime.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 173; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','fillup','cr,yst,al,no,ncr,yst,al',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(60642,'chemistry','Consider the following statements of enthalpy of solution: \n \n i. Enthalpy of solution of a substance is the enthalpy change when one mole of it dissolves in a specified amount of solvent. \n \n ii.When an ionic compound dissolves in a solvent, the ions leave their ordered positions on the crystal lattice. \n \n iii.For most of the ionic compounds, ∆sol HΘ is positive and the dissociation process is exodothermic. \n \n iv. For most of the ionic compounds, ∆sol HΘ is positive and the dissociation process is endothermic. \n \n Which of the following statements is correct?','i, ii and iv','Enthalpy of solution of a substance is the\nenthalpy change when one mole of it dissolves\nin a specified amount of solvent. The enthalpy\nof solution at infinite dilution is the enthalpy\nchange observed on dissolving the substance\nin an infinite amount of solvent when the\ninteractions between the ions (or solute molecules) are negligible.\nWhen an ionic compound dissolves in a\nsolvent, the ions leave their ordered positions on\nthe crystal lattice. These are now more free in\nsolution. But solvation of these ions (hydration\nin case solvent is water) also occurs at the same\ntime.\nFor most of the ionic compounds, ∆sol HΘ\n is\npositive and the dissociation process is\nendothermic. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 173; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','i, ii and iii','only ii and iii','i, ii and iv','only iv and ii','NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(60643,'chemistry','Match the following','∆solHΘ- ∆latticeHΘ+ΔhydΗΘ, ΔΗsub-ΔΗfusion+ΔΗvap,ΔΗ-ΔU+ΔngRT,ΔΗ°=∑B.E. of reactants - ∑B.E. of products','∆solHΘ = ∆latticeHΘ+ΔhydΗΘ\nΔΗsub = ΔΗfusion+ΔΗvap                                  \nΔΗ = ΔU+ΔngRT\nΔΗ°=∑B.E. of reactants - ∑B.E. of products\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 163,172,173; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','match','∆solHΘ,∆latticeHΘ+ΔhydΗΘ','ΔΗsub,ΔΗfusion+ΔΗvap','ΔΗ,ΔU+ΔngRT','ΔΗ°,∑B.E. of reactants - ∑B.E. of products','NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(60644,'chemistry','Consider the following statements of enthalpy of solution: \n \n i. If the lattice enthalpy is very high, the dissolution of compounds become very easy. \n \n ii. Enthalpy of solution is determined by the selective values of the lattice enthalpy and hydration enthalpy. \n \n iii.For most ionic compounds, ∆H°soln. is positive and the dissociation process is endothermic. \n \n iv. Solubility of the most salts increases with increase in temperature. \n \n Which of the following statements is not correct?','only i','Enthalpy of solution of a substance is the\nenthalpy change when one mole of it dissolves\nin a specified amount of solvent. The enthalpy\nof solution at infinite dilution is the enthalpy\nchange observed on dissolving the substance\nin an infinite amount of solvent when the\ninteractions between the ions (or solute molecules) are negligible.\nWhen an ionic compound dissolves in a\nsolvent, the ions leave their ordered positions on\nthe crystal lattice. These are now more free in\nsolution. But solvation of these ions (hydration\nin case solvent is water) also occurs at the same\ntime.\nFor most of the ionic compounds, ∆sol HΘ\n is\npositive and the dissociation process is\nendothermic.Therefore the solubility of most\nsalts in water increases with rise of\ntemperature. If the lattice enthalpy is very\nhigh, the dissolution of the compound may not\ntake place at all (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 173; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','only i','Both i and ii','i, ii and iii','only iv','NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(60645,'chemistry','Match the following','CH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) --- CO₂(g) + 2H₂O-∆cH°, H₂(g) --- 2H(g)-∆bondH°, NaCl(s) --- Na+(g) + CL-(g)-∆latticeH°, NaCl(s) --- Na+(aq) + Cl-(aq)-∆solH°','CH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) --- CO₂(g) + 2H₂O = ∆cH°                                                                           \nH₂(g) --- 2H(g) = ∆bondH°                                                                                                    \nNaCl(s) --- Na+(g) + CL-(g) = ∆latticeH°                                                                               \nNaCl(s) --- Na+(aq) + Cl-(aq) = ∆solH°(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 173; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','match','CH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) --- CO₂(g) + 2H₂O,∆cH°','H₂(g) --- 2H(g),∆bondH°','NaCl(s) --- Na+(g) + CL-(g),∆latticeH°','NaCl(s) --- Na+(aq) + Cl-(aq),∆solH°','NEW','11-177','11-153',NULL),
(60646,'chemistry','The amount of heat evolved when 0.80 mole of HCl is mixed with 0.50 mole of NaOH solution is','28.55 kJ','0.80 mol of HCl = 0.80 mol of H+ \n0.50 mol of NaOH = 0.50 mol of OH-\nH*+ + OH*- ------ H2O ( Neutralization)\n0.50 mol of HCl = 0.50 mol of H+\nor 0.50 mol of H+ = 0.50 mol of OH-\nHeat evolved with 1 mol H+ = 57.1 kJ\nHeat evolved with 0.50 mol H+ = 5.71×0.50 = 28.55 kJ\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 173; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','28.55 kJ','17.13 kJ','73.25 kJ','11.42 kJ','NEW','11-177','11-153',NULL),
(60647,'chemistry','Which of the following reactions will have the value of enthalpy of neutralization as -57.1 kJ/mol?','HCl + NaOH ---- NaCl + H2O','Enthalpy of neutralization is -57.1 kJ/mol for a strong acid and strong base','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','CH3COOH + NaOH ----- CH3COONa + H2O','HCl + NH4OH ---- NH4Cl + H2O','HCOOH + NaOH ---- HCOONa + H2O','HCl + NaOH ---- NaCl + H2O','NEW','11-177','11-153',NULL),
(60648,'chemistry','Consider the following assertion and reason: \n \n Assertion : The solubility of most salts in water increases with rise of temperature. \n \n Reason : For most ionic compounds, ∆H°soln. is positive and the dissociation process is endothermic .','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 173; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','11-177','11-153',NULL),
(60649,'chemistry','Consider the following assertion and reason: \n \n Assertion : Heat of neutralization of HNO₃ and NaOH is same as that of HCl and KOH \n \n Reason : Both NHO₃ and HCl are strong acids and NaOH and KOH are strong bases.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 173; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','11-177','11-153',NULL),
(60650,'chemistry','Which of the following is correct for enthalpy of solution?','∆H°soln. = ∆H°lattice + ∆H°Hydration','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 173; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','∆H°lattice = ∆H°hyd + ∆H°soln','∆H°soln. = ∆H°lattice × ∆H°hyd','∆H°hyd= ∆H°lattice × ∆H°soln','∆H°soln. = ∆H°lattice + ∆H°Hydration','NEW','11-178','11-154',NULL),
(60701,'chemistry','The flow of heat is --------------.','unidirectional','The flow of heat is unidirectional\nfrom higher temperature to lower\ntemperature.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 174; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','fillup','un,idir,ecti,on,al,bi,dir,ect,ion,al',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(60702,'chemistry','In spontaneous reaction','reaction occuring in extremely slow rate','Although the reaction is taking place between them, it is at an extremely slow rate.\nIt is still called spontaneous reaction\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 174; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','mcq','reaction occuring in slow rate','reaction occuring in high rate','reaction occuring in extremely slow rate','reaction occuring in extremely high rate','NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(60703,'chemistry','Spontaneity means ‘having the potential to proceed ---------------- the assistance of external agency.','without','Spontaneity means ‘having the potential to\nproceed without the assistance of external\nagency.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 175; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','fillup','wi,tho,ut,wi,th',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(121501,'chemistry','Geometrical isomerism is not shown by','1,1-dichloro-1-pentene','1,1-dichloro-1-pentene has two identical chlorine atoms attached to the same carbon atom of the double bond, and a methyl group attached to the other carbon atom. Since the two substituents on the carbon atoms of the double bond are identical, there is no possibility of geometrical isomerism.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is that geometrical isomerism is not shown by 1,1-dichloro-1-pentene.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','1,1-dichloro-1-pentene','1,2-dichloro-1-pentene','1,3-dichloro-2-pentene','1,4-dichloro-2-pentene','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(60704,'chemistry','Consider the following statements of spontaneity: \n \n i. A spontaneous process is an irreversible process and may only be reversed by some external agency. \n \n ii. A spontaneous process is an reversible process and may only be reversed by some external agency. \n \n iii. All naturally occurring processes whether chemical or physical will tend to proceed spontaneously in one direction only. \n \n iv. In spontaneous reaction, reaction occuring in extremely slow rate. \n \n Which of the following statements is correct?','i, iii and iv','Take the case of combination of\nhydrogen and oxygen. These gases may be\nmixed at room temperature and left for many\nyears without observing any perceptible\nchange. Although the reaction is taking place between them, it is at an extremely slow rate.\nIt is still called spontaneous reaction. So\nspontaneity means ‘having the potential to\nproceed without the assistance of external\nagency’. However, it does not tell about the\nrate of the reaction or process. \nA spontaneous process is an irreversible process and may only  be reversed by some external agency.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 175; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'3','mcq','ii, iii and iv','iii and iv','i, iii and iv','only ii','NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(60705,'chemistry','The decrease in enthalpy in passing from reactants to products for any -------- reaction.','exothermic','The decrease in enthalpy in passing from\nreactants to products  for any exothermic reaction.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 175; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','fillup','ex,oth,erm,ic,en,dot,herm,ic',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(60706,'chemistry','In endothermic reactions,','reactants have less energy than products','In endothermic reactions,reactants have less energy than products.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 175; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','mcq','reactants have lower temperature than products','reactants and products have same energy','reactants have more energy than products','reactants have less energy than products','NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(60707,'chemistry','The greater the disorder in an isolated system, the higher is the','entropy','Entropy as a measure\nof the degree of randomness or disorder in the\nsystem. The greater the disorder in an isolated\nsystem, the higher is the entropy.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 176; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','mcq','Spontaneity','enthalpy','entropy','All of the above','NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(60708,'chemistry','If the structure of the products is very much disordered than that of the reactants, there will be a resultant ---------- in entropy.','increase','The greater the disorder in an isolated\nsystem, the higher is the entropy. As far as a\nchemical reaction is concerned, this entropy\nchange can be attributed to rearrangement of\natoms or ions from one pattern in the reactants\nto another (in the products). If the structure\nof the products\n is very much disordered than\nthat of the reactants, there will be a resultant\nincrease in entropy.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 176; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'2','fillup','in,crea,se,de,crea,se',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(60709,'chemistry','The gaseous state is state of ---------- entropy.','highest','The gaseous state is state of highest entropy. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 176; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','fillup','hi,ghe,st,lo,we,st',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(60710,'chemistry','Decrease of regularity in structure would mean \n \n i. increase in entropy \n \n ii. decrease in entropy \n \n iii. decrease in temperature \n \n iv. increase in temperature \n \n Which of the following statements is correct?','i and iv','Decrease of regularity in structure\nwould mean increase in entropy. For a given\nsubstance, the crystalline solid state is the\nstate of lowest entropy (most ordered), The\ngaseous state is state of highest entropy.\nWhenever heat is added to the system, it\nincreases molecular motions causing\nincreased randomness in the system.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 176; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'3','mcq','i and iv','only i','i and iii','ii and iv','NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(60711,'chemistry','Increase of regularity in structure would mean\n \n i. increase in entropy\n \n ii. decrease in entropy\n \n iii. decrease in temperature\n \n iv. increase in temperature which of the following statements is correct?','ii and iii','Increase of regularity in structure\nwould mean decrease in entropy. For a given\nsubstance, the crystalline solid state is the\nstate of lowest entropy (most ordered), The\ngaseous state is state of highest entropy.Whenever heat is added to the system, it\nincreases molecular motions causing\nincreased randomness in the system.\nTherefore, decrease in temperature, the entropy is deceased. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 176; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'3','mcq','i and ii','ii and iii','iii and iv','i and iv','NEW','11-181','11-157','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60711q.png'),
(60712,'chemistry','Heat added to a system at lower temperature causes ----------- randomness.','greater','Heat added to a system at lower temperature\ncauses greater randomness than when the\nsame quantity of heat is added to it at higher\ntemperature. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 176; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','fillup','gr,eat,er,lo,wer',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(60713,'chemistry','The total entropy change (∆Stotal) for the system and the surrounding of a spontaneous process is given by','∆Stotal = ∆Ssystem + ∆Ssurr > 0','For a spontaneous process, total change in entropy (system + surrounding) should be positive.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 177; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','mcq','∆Stotal = ∆Ssystem + ∆Ssurr < 0','∆Ssystem = ∆Stotal + ∆Ssurr > 0','∆Stotal = ∆Ssystem + ∆Ssurr > 0','∆Ssurr = ∆Ssystem + ∆Ssurr < 0','NEW','11-181','11-157','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60713q.png'),
(60714,'chemistry','The entropy change is inversely proportional to the ?','temperature','The entropy change is inversely proportional to the temprature.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 177; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','mcq','temperature','pressure','heat','enthalpy','NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(60715,'chemistry','Which of the following expressions regarding entropy is not correct?','∆Ssystem = ∆Stotal + ∆Ssurrounding','For a spontaneous process, total change in entropy (system + surrounding) should be positive.\n∆Stotal = ∆Ssystem + ∆Ssurr > 0 (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 177; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','mcq','∆Stotal= ∆Ssystem + ∆Ssurrounding','∆S= Sfinal + Sinitial','∆Ssystem = q/T','∆Ssystem = ∆Stotal + ∆Ssurrounding','NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(60716,'chemistry','When a system is in equilibrium, the entropy is maximum, and the change in entropy, ∆S = ?','zero','When a system is in equilibrium, the\nentropy is maximum, and the change in\nentropy, ∆S = 0.\nWe can say that entropy for a spontaneous\nprocess increases till it reaches maximum and\nat equilibrium the change in entropy is zero. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 177; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','mcq','T/q','zero','∆S > 0','∆S < 0','NEW','11-181','11-157','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60716q.png'),
(60717,'chemistry','Which is the correct expression of Gibbs energy?','both a and b','we define a new\nthermodynamic function the Gibbs energy or\nGibbs function, G, as\nG = H – TS  and\nThe change in Gibbs energy for the system,\n∆Gsys, can be written as ∆Gsys = ∆Hsys - T∆Ssys \nAt constant temperature, ∆T = 0 \n∴ ∆Gsys = ∆Hsys -  T∆Ssys \nUsually the subscript ‘system’ is dropped\nand we simply write this equation as\n∆G =  ∆H -  T∆S (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','mcq','G = H – TS','∆G = ∆H – T∆S','G = H + TS','both a and b','NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(60718,'chemistry','Gibbs energy is an ------------ property and a State function.','extensive','Gibbs energy is an extensive property and a State function. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','fillup','ex,ten,si,ve,in,ten,si,ve',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-182','11-158',NULL),
(60719,'chemistry','Which is the correct expression for entropy change of surroundings?','∆Ssurr = -∆Hsys/T','If the system is in thermal equilibrium with\nthe surrounding, then the temperature of the\nsurrounding is same as that of the system.\nAlso, increase in enthalpy of the surrounding\nis equal to decrease in the enthalpy of the\nsystem.Therefore, entropy change of surroundings\n∆Ssurr = ∆Hsurr/T = -∆Hsys/T. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'3','mcq','∆Ssurr = ∆T/∆Hsys','∆Ssurr = T/∆Hsys','∆Ssurr = -∆Hsys/T','∆Ssurr = ∆Hsys/T','NEW','11-182','11-158','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60719q.png'),
(60720,'chemistry','For a reaction, P + Q ------- R + S. The value of H° is -30 kJ/mol and ∆S is -100 J/k/mol. At What temperature the reaction wil be at equilibrium?','27°C','∆G = ∆H - T∆S, \nAt equilibrium, ∆G = 0, ∆H = T∆S,  30000 = T × 100\nT = 300 K or 27°C (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'2','mcq','30°C','27°C','30°C','45°C','NEW','11-182','11-158',NULL),
(60721,'chemistry','Which of the following reactions will have the value of ∆S with a negative sign?','2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) --------- 2SO₃(g)','2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) -------- 2SO₃(g)\n∆ng = 2 - 3 = -1, ∆S = -ve\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','mcq','CaCO₃(s) --------- CaO(s) + CO₂(g)','H₂O(l) ---------- H₂O(g)','2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) --------- 2SO₃(g)','Cl₂(g) ---------- 2Cl(g)','NEW','11-182','11-158',NULL),
(60722,'chemistry','A reaction is at equilibrium at 100°C and the enthalpy change for the reaction is 50.0 kJ/mol.What will be the value of ∆S in J/k/mol ?','134.04 J/k/mol','∆G = ∆H - T∆S, \nAt equilibrium, ∆G = 0, ∆H = T∆S\nT = 100 + 273 = 373K \n∆S = ∆S/T = 50000 Jmol-1/373 = 134.04 J/k/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'2','mcq','150.04 J/k/mol','134.04 J/k/mol','135.04 J/k/mol','124.04 J/k/mol','NEW','11-182','11-158',NULL),
(60723,'chemistry','At What temperature liquid water wil be in equilibrium with water vapour? ∆Hvap = 73.37 kJ/mol, ∆Svap = 0.18 kJ/k/mol','407.61 k','∆G = ∆H - T∆S, \nAt equilibrium, ∆G = 0, ∆H = T∆S\nT = ∆H/∆S = 73.37/0.18 = 407.61 k\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'2','mcq','407.61 k','500.61 k','410.61 k','407 k','NEW','11-182','11-158',NULL),
(60724,'chemistry','Which is the correct expression for standard free energy of a reaction:','∆rG° = ∑ΔfG°products - ∑ΔfG°reactants','∆rG° = ∑ΔfG°products - ∑ΔfG°reactants. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 179; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','mcq','∆rG° = ∑ΔfG°products + ∑ΔfG°reactants','∆rG° = ∑ΔfG°products - ∑ΔfG°reactants','∆rG° = ∑ΔfG°reactants - ∑ΔfG°products','∆rG° = ∑ΔfG°reactants + ∑ΔfG°products','NEW','11-182','11-158',NULL),
(60725,'chemistry','Which is the correct expression for standard entropy:','∆S° = ΣS°p - ΣS°r','∆S° = ΣS°p - ΣS°r,   (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 179; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','mcq','∆S° = ΣS°r + ΣS°p','∆S° = ΣS°r - ΣS°p','∆S° = ΣS°p - ΣS°r','∆S° = ΣS°p + ΣS°r','NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(60726,'chemistry','For a reaction: X ----- Y + Z Absolute entropies are X = 150 J/k/mol Y = 321.42 J/k/mol and Z = 764.34 J/k/mol What will be the entropy change at 298 k and 1 atm?','935.76 J/k','∆S° = ΣS°p - ΣS°r \n       = (321.42 +  764.34) - 150\n       = 1085.76 - 150 \n       = 935.76 J/k\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 179; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'2','mcq','215.76 J/k','925.76 J/k','935 J/k','935.76 J/k','NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(60727,'chemistry','What will be the melting point of KCl if enthalpy change for the reaction is 92.8 J/mol and entropy change is 1.006 J/k/mol?','92.24 K','T = ∆H/∆S = 92.8/1.006 = 92.24 K\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','mcq','0.010K','98.24 K','92.24 K','92.34 K','NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(60728,'chemistry','Match the following','State function-entropy, ∆H = q - at constant pressure, ∆U = q - at constant volume, Intensive property - specific heat','State function = entropy\n∆H = q = at constant pressure\n∆U = q = at constant volume\nIntensive property = specific heat\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 175, 160 and 161; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'3','match','State function,entropy','∆H = q,at constant pressure','∆U = q,at constant volume','Intensive property,specific heat','NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(60729,'chemistry','Read the following statements regarding spontaneity of a process and mark the appropriate choice.\n \n i. When enthalpy factor is absent that randomness factor decides spontaneity of a process.\n \n ii. When randomness factor is absent then enthalpy factors decides spontaneity of a process.\n \n iii. When both the factors take place simultaneously, the magnitude of both the factors decides spontaneity of a process.','i, ii and iii are correct','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 175, 176; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii are correct and iii is incorrect','iii is correct and i and ii is incorrect','i, ii and iii are correct','i, ii and iii are incorrect','NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(60730,'chemistry','Which of the following processes is a non-spontaneous process?','Flow of heat from a cold body to a hot body in contact','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 175; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'2','mcq','Flow of heat from a cold body to a hot body in contact','Dissolution of salt or sugar in water','Mixing of different gases through diffusion','Precipitation of copper when zinc rod is dipped\nin an aqueous solution of copper sulphate','NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(60731,'chemistry','Which of the following statements regarding Gibbs energy change is correct?','If ∆G is negative (< 0), the process is spontaneous','∆G gives a criteria for spontaneity at\nconstant pressure and temperature.\n(i) If ∆G is negative (< 0), the process is\nspontaneous.\n(ii) If ∆G is positive (> 0), the process is non\nspontaneous.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'2','mcq','If ∆G is positive (> 0), the process is in equilibrium','If ∆G is positive (> 0), the process is spontaneous','If ∆G is negative (< 0), the process is  non-spontaneous','If ∆G is negative (< 0), the process is spontaneous','NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(60732,'chemistry','Gibbs free energy(G) is the energy available to do ----------- work.','useful','Gibbs free energy(G) is the energy available to do useful work.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','fillup','us,ef,ul,no,tus,ef,ul',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(60733,'chemistry','Increase in enthalpy of the surrounding is equal to','decrease in the enthalpy of the system','Increase in enthalpy of the surrounding is equal to decrease in the enthalpy of the system\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','mcq','increase in the enthalpy of the surrounding','decrease in the enthalpy of the system','decrease in the enthalpy of the surrounding','increase in the enthalpy of the system','NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(60734,'chemistry','For a reaction to be spontaneous at any temperature, the conditions are','∆H = -ve, ∆S = +ve','If a reaction has a positive enthalpy\nchange and positive entropy change, it can be\nspontaneous when T∆S is large enough to\noutweigh ∆H. This can happen in two ways;\n(a) The positive entropy change of the system can be ‘small’ in which case T must be large.\n(b) The positive entropy change of the system\ncan be ’large’, in which case T may be small.\nThe former is one of the reasons why reactions\nare often carried out at high temperature.\n\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178, 179; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','mcq','∆H = -ve, ∆S = +ve','∆H = +ve, ∆S = -ve','∆H = +ve, ∆S = +ve','∆H = -ve, ∆S = -ve','NEW','11-183','11-159',NULL),
(60735,'chemistry','In a reaction M + N ----- O + P, there is no change in entropy. Enthalpy change for the reaction (∆H) is 50 kJ/mol. Under What conditions, reaction will have negative value of free energy change?','If ∆H is negative','∆G = ∆H - T∆S, ∆S = 0 or T∆S = 0, ∆G = ∆H (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','mcq','If ∆H is positive','If ∆H is 100 kJ/mol','If ∆H is negative','If temprature of reaction is high','NEW','11-183','11-159',NULL),
(60736,'chemistry','For a reaction, 4K(g) + L(g) ------- 7M(g); ∆U° = -18.5 kJ and ∆S° = -20.5 J/k. \n\nCalculate ∆G° for the reaction and predict whether the reaction will be spontaneous or non-spontaneous?','60.95 kJ, non-spontaneous','4K(g) + L(g) ------- 7M(g), ∆ng = 7 - 5 = 2 \n∆H = ∆U + ∆ngRT = -18.5 × 1000 + 2 × 8.314 × 298 = -13.54 kJ\n∆G° = ∆H° - T∆S° = -13.54 - 298 (-20.5) \n        = -13.54 + 6109 = 60.95 kJ\nsince ∆G° is +ve hence it is non-spontaneous (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'2','mcq','13.54 kJ, non-spontaneous','-13.54 kJ, non-spontaneous','-60.95 kJ, spontaneous','60.95 kJ, non-spontaneous','NEW','11-183','11-159',NULL),
(60737,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is not correct?\n \n i. For a spontaneous process, ∆G must be negative.\n \n ii. Enthalpy, entropy,free energy etc,are state variables.\n \n iii. A spontaneous process is reversible in nature.\n \n iv. Total of all possible kinds of energy of a system is called its internal energy.','only iii','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'2','mcq','only iv','only iii','ii and iii','ii and iv','NEW','11-183','11-159',NULL),
(60738,'chemistry','∆G is the net energy available to do useful work and is a measure of free energy.If a reaction has positive entropy change,under What conditions will the reaction be spontaneous?','∆G is Negative at high temperature hence reaction is spontaneous at high temperature','∆G = ∆H - T∆S, For a spontaneous reaction if ∆H is +ve and ∆S is +ve, ∆G will be negative\nwhen T∆S>∆H            (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 179; Paragraph No.6.6) Table 6.4','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'2','mcq','∆G is Negative at high temperature hence reaction is spontaneous at high temperature','∆G will be positive at low temperature hence reaction is spontaneous at low temperature','∆G is Negative at low temperature hence reaction is spontaneous at low temperature','∆G is Negative at all temperature hence reaction is spontaneous at all temperature','NEW','11-183','11-159',NULL),
(60739,'chemistry','Dissolution of ammonium chloride in water is an endothermic reaction yet it is a spontaneous process.This is due to the fact that','∆H is +ve, ∆S is +ve, and ∆H<T∆S','∆G is negative under these conditions.\n∆G = ∆H - T∆S, For a spontaneous reaction if ∆H is +ve and ∆S is +ve, ∆G will be negative\nwhen T∆S>∆H            (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'2','mcq','∆H is +ve, ∆S is +ve, and ∆H<T∆S','∆H is +ve, ∆S is -ve','∆H is -ve, ∆S is +ve','∆H is +ve, ∆H>T∆S','NEW','11-183','11-159',NULL),
(60740,'chemistry','What will be the signs of ∆H when KOH is dissolved in water?','Negative','When KOH dissolved in water, heat is evolved. so ∆H is negative\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','mcq','Negative','Positive','Negative at low temperature','Postive at high temperature','NEW','11-183','11-159',NULL),
(60741,'chemistry','Consider the following assertion and reason: \n \n Assertion : When salt dissolves in water, ΔS for the process is positive. \n \n Reason : Aqueous ions have a greater entropy than ions in a solid.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the\ncorrect explanation of the\nassertion.','The entropies of gases are much larger than those of liquids, which are larger than those of solids. When salt dissolves in water, ΔSΔS for the process is positive because salt transfer from solid phase to liquid phase,which have higher entropy.\nAqueous ions have a greater entropy than ions in a solid\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 176; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the\ncorrect explanation of the\nassertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a\ncorrect explanation of the\nassertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','11-183','11-159',NULL),
(60742,'chemistry','What will be the signs of ∆S, when H₂O(s) change to H₂O(l)?','+ve, entropy increases','When a substance goes from a solid to a gas (sublimation) or from a liquid to a gas (evaporation), entropy increases.\ṅ(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 176; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','mcq','+ve, entropy increases','-ve, entropy increases','+ve, entropy decreases','-ve, entropy decreases','NEW','11-182','11-158',NULL),
(60743,'chemistry','At absolute zero, the entropy of a pure crystal is zero. This is','third law of thermodynamics','At absolute zero, the entropy of a pure crystal is zero. This is third law of thermodynamics','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','mcq','second law of thermodynamics','first law of thermodynamics','zeroth law of thermodynamics','third law of thermodynamics','NEW','11-182','11-158',NULL),
(60744,'chemistry','For the reaction 2Cl(g) ------ Cl₂(g), What are the signs of ∆H and ∆S?','∆H = -ve and ∆S = -ve','∆H  is negative ( bond energy is released) and ∆S is negative (there is less randomness among the\nmolecules than among the atoms)\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 176; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','mcq','∆H = +ve and ∆S = +ve','∆H = +ve and ∆S = -ve','∆H = -ve and ∆S = -ve','∆H = -ve and ∆S = +ve','NEW','11-182','11-158',NULL),
(60745,'chemistry','For an isolated system, ∆U = 0, What will be ∆S?','∆S > 0','ΔS will be positive i.e., greater than zero\nSince ΔU= 0, ΔSwill be positive and the reaction will be spontaneous.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','mcq','∆S < 1','∆S = 0','∆S > 0','∆S < 0','NEW','11-182','11-158',NULL),
(60746,'chemistry','Unit of entropy is','J/K/mol','Entropy change (∆S) is given by ∆S = qrev/T\nUnit of entropy = J/K/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','mcq','J/K/mol','JK/mol','J/mol','J/mol/K','NEW','11-182','11-158',NULL),
(60747,'chemistry','Why the entropy of a diamond is less than that of graphite?','Diamond is more compact than graphite.','Decrease of regularity in structure\nwould mean increase in entropy. For a given\nsubstance, the crystalline solid state is the\nstate of lowest entropy (most ordered), The\ngaseous state is state of highest entropy.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 176; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'2','mcq','Graphite is less compact than diamond','Diamond is more compact than graphite.','Graphite is more compact than diamond','Diamond is less compact than graphite.','NEW','11-182','11-158',NULL),
(60748,'chemistry','Entropy decreases during:','Crystallization of sucrose from solution','Decrease of regularity in structure\nwould mean increase in entropy. For a given\nsubstance, the crystalline solid state is the\nstate of lowest entropy (most ordered), The\ngaseous state is state of highest entropy.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 176; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Spontaneity',NULL,'1','mcq','Rusting of iron','Melting of ice','Crystallization of sucrose from solution','Vaporisation of camphor','NEW','11-182','11-158',NULL),
(60749,'biology','Which of the following statements is true? [NEET 2002]','Tracheids are unicellular with wide lumen','Xylem is the principal water conducting tissue of the plant. It consists of four types of cellstracheids, vessels, xylem fibres and xylem parenchyma. The tracheids and vessels together are known as tracheary elements. Tracheids are characteristic of all vascular plant. Tracheids originate from single cells. These are single elongated cells with tapering ends. The end walls are without perforations. Their length varies from one to three mm. Tracheids are devoid of protoplast, hence dead; fairly large cavity of these cells is without any contents. The wall of tracheids is moderately thick and usually lignified.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Vessels are multicellular with wide lumen.','Tracheids are multicellular with narrow lumen.','Vessels are unicellular with narrow lumen.','Tracheids are unicellular with wide lumen','NEW','11-087',NULL,NULL),
(60750,'biology','Axillary bud and terminal bud derived from the activity of [NEET 2002]','Apical meristem','Apical meristems are situated at the tips of the root and shoot. They take part in initial growth. Plants elongate and increase in height as a result of divisions in this meristem. Promeristem and primary meristem (root and shoot apices) are included in this type of meristem. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:85, paragraph no:1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Lateral meristem','Intercalary meristem','Apical meristem','Parenchyma','NEW','11-084',NULL,NULL),
(60801,'chemistry','Enthalpy change for the process, H₂O(ice) -------- H₂O (water) is 6 kJ/mol. The entropy change of 1 mol of ice at its melting point will be','22 J/K/mol','∆Sfusion = ∆Hfusion/∆Tfusion = 6 × 1000/273 = 22 J/K/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 179; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Gibbs energy change and Equilibrium',NULL,'2','mcq','22 J/K/mol','12 J/K/mol','100 J/K/mol','30J/K/mol','NEW','11-184','11-160',NULL),
(60802,'chemistry','What is the entropy change when 2 mole oxygen gas expands isothermally and reversibly from an initial volume of 20 L to 200 L at 300 K?','38.29 J/K','∆S = 2.033 nRlogV2-V1\n      = 2.303 × 1 × 8.314 log 200/20 = 38.29 J/K\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 179; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Gibbs energy change and Equilibrium',NULL,'2','mcq','28.29 J/K','20 J/K','38.29 J/K','308.29 J/K','NEW','11-184','11-160',NULL),
(60803,'chemistry','Match the following','Exothermic-∆H is negative, Spontaneous-∆G is negative, Cyclic process-∆H=0 and ∆U=0, Equilibrium-∆G=0',NULL,'Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Gibbs energy change and Equilibrium',NULL,'3','match','Exothermic,∆H is negative','Spontaneous,∆G is negative','Cyclic process,∆H=0 and ∆U=0','Equilibrium,∆G=0','NEW','11-184','11-160',NULL),
(60804,'chemistry','Mark the one which is incorrect.','The process in which pressure remains constant is called isochoric.','The process in which temperature remains constant is called isothermal.\nThe process in which volume remains constant is called isochoric.\nThe process in which pressure remains constant is called Isobaric.\nThe relation between ∆H and ∆U when all the reactants and products are solid : ∆H = ∆U. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 179; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Gibbs energy change and Equilibrium',NULL,'2','mcq','The process in which temperature remains constant is called isothermal.','The process in which volume remains constant is called isochoric.','The process in which pressure remains constant is called isochoric.','The relation between ∆H and ∆U when all the reactants and products are solid : ∆H = ∆U.','NEW','11-184','11-160','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60804q.png'),
(60805,'chemistry','Consider the following assertion and reason: \n \n Assertion : Heat added to a system at lower temperature causes greater randomness than when the same quantity of heat is added to it at higher temperature. \n \n Reason : Entropy is a measure of the degree of randomness or disorder in the system.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','∆S is related with q and T fir a reversible reaction: =∆S = qrev/T\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 179; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Gibbs energy change and Equilibrium',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','11-184','11-160',NULL),
(60806,'chemistry','Consider the following assertion and reason: \n \n Assertion : An exothermic process which is non spontaneous at high temperature may become spontaneous at low temperature. \n \n Reason : Spontaneous process is an irreversible process and may be reversed by some external agency.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','∆G = ∆H - T∆S, for exothermic process ∆H = -ve. For ∆G to be negative ( for spontaneous process), ∆H > T∆S which is\npossible only at low temperature. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 179; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Gibbs energy change and Equilibrium',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','11-184','11-160',NULL),
(60807,'chemistry','Consider the following assertion and reason: \n \n Assertion : If both ∆H° and ∆S° are positive then reaction will be spontaneous at high temperature. \n \n Reason : All processes with positive entropy change are spontaneous.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Spontaneity of a process depends upon ∆G° which combines both factors ∆H° and ∆S°.. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 179; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Gibbs energy change and Equilibrium',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the\ncorrect explanation of the\nassertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a\ncorrect explanation of the\nassertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','11-184','11-160',NULL),
(60808,'chemistry','Consider the following assertion and reason: \n \n Assertion : Third law of thermodynamics is confined to pure crystalline solids. \n \n Reason : Theoretical. arguments and practical evedence have shown that entropy of solutions and super cooled liquids is not zero at 0K.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the\ncorrect explanation of the\nassertion.','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 179; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Gibbs energy change and Equilibrium',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the\ncorrect explanation of the\nassertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a\ncorrect explanation of the\nassertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','11-185','11-161',NULL),
(60809,'chemistry','Which is the correct relationship between ∆G° and equilibrium constant Kp?','Kp = e*-∆G/RT','The correct relationship between ∆G° and equilibrium constant Kp is\nKp = e*-∆G/RT\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 179; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Gibbs energy change and Equilibrium',NULL,'1','mcq','Kp = -RTlog∆G°','Kp = e*-∆G/RT','Kp = [e/RT]*∆G°','Kp = -∆G°/RT','NEW','11-185','11-161',NULL),
(60810,'chemistry','A reaction attains equilibrium state under standard conditions, then What is incorrect for this?','Equilibrium constant at k = 0','∆G° = 0 at equilibrium under standard state.Also at equilibrium ∆G = 0, ∴ ΔH° = TΔS° = 0 \nAlso ∆G° = 2.303RTlogk\n∴ k = 1 (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 179; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Gibbs energy change and Equilibrium',NULL,'1','mcq','Equilibrium constant at k = 0','Equilibrium constant at k = 1','∆G° = 0 and ∆H° = T∆S°','∆G = 0 and ∆H = T∆S','NEW','11-185','11-161',NULL),
(60811,'chemistry','The equilibrium constant for a reaction is 20, What will be the value of ∆G° at 400 K?','-9.96 kJ','∆G° = -2.303RTlogk\n        = -2.303 × 8.314 × 400 log 20 = -9964.4 J = -9.96 kJ\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 179; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Gibbs energy change and Equilibrium',NULL,'2','mcq','-11.96 kJ','-9.96 kJ','+9964.4kJ','-9964.4 kJ','NEW','11-185','11-161',NULL),
(60812,'chemistry','Calculate ∆rGΘ for conversion of oxygen to ozone, 3/2 O₂ (g) → O₃ (g) at 298 K. if Kp for this conversion is 2.47 × 10*–29 .','163kJ/mol','We know ∆rGΘ\n= – 2.303 RT log Kp\n and\nR = 8.314 JK–1 mol–1 \nTherefore, ∆rGΘ\n=\n– 2.303 (8.314 J K–1 mol–1)\n × (298 K) (log 2.47 × 10*–29)\n= 163000 J mol–1\n= 163 kJ mol–1\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 180; Paragraph No.6.6)( NCERT Problem 6.11)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Gibbs energy change and Equilibrium',NULL,'2','mcq','163kJ/mol','163000 kJ/mol','-16300 kJ/mol','162 kJ/mol','NEW','11-186','11-162',NULL),
(60813,'chemistry','For a reaction 2 A(g) + B(g) ----- 2D(g) ∆U° = -10.5 kJ and ∆S° = -44.1 J/k Calculate ∆GΘ for the reaction, and predict whether the reaction may occur spontaneously.','0.164 kJ, non-spontaneous','For the given reaction,\n2 A(g) + B(g) → 2D(g)\nΔng = 2 - (3)\n= -1 mole\nSubstituting the value of ΔU\nin the expression of ΔH:\nΔH\n= ∆U + ΔngRT\n= (-10.5 kJ) - (-1) (8.314 x 10*-3\nkJ K\n-1 mol\n-1\n) (298 K)\n= -10.5 kJ - 2.48 kJ\nΔH\n= -12.98 kJ\nSubstituting the values of ΔH\nand ΔS in the expression of ΔG:\nΔG = ΔH - TΔS\n= -12.98 kJ - (298 K) (-44.1 J K\n-1)\n= -12.98 kJ + 13.14 kJ\nΔG\n= + 0.16 kJ\nSince ΔG\nfor the reaction is positive, the reaction will not occur spontaneously\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 179; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Gibbs energy change and Equilibrium',NULL,'2','mcq','2.164 kJ, non-spontaneous','-0.164 kJ, non-spontaneous','0.164 kJ, non-spontaneous','0.164 kJ, spontaneous','NEW','11-186','11-162',NULL),
(60814,'chemistry','The equilibrium constant for a reaction is 10. What will be the valueof ΔG? R = 8.314 JK -1mol -1 , T= 300 K.','-5.744 kJ mol\n-1','ΔG\n= -2.303 RTlogK\n= (2.303) (8.314 JK\n-1mol\n-1\n) (300 K) log10\n= -5744.14 Jmol-1\n= -5.744 kJ mol-1\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 179; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Gibbs energy change and Equilibrium',NULL,'2','mcq','+6.744 kJ mol\n-1','+5.744 kJ mol\n-1','-5.744 kJ mol\n-1','-6.744 kJ mol\n-1','NEW','11-186','11-162',NULL),
(60815,'chemistry','For the reaction at 298 K, 2A + B → C ΔH = 400 kJ mol -1and ΔS = 0.2 kJ K-1mol-1 At What temperature will the reaction become spontaneous Considering ΔH and ΔS to be constant over the temperature range?','2000K','ΔG= ΔH - TΔS\n(∆G = 0, at equilibrium) so\nT = ∆H/∆S = 400kJmol-1/0.2 kJk-1mol-1\nT = 2000K \nFor the reaction to be spontaneous, ΔGmust be negative. Hence, for the given reaction to be spontaneous, Tshould \nbe greater than 2000 K \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Gibbs energy change and Equilibrium',NULL,'2','mcq','2000K','1500K','1000K','200K','NEW','11-186','11-162',NULL),
(60816,'biology','Which exposed wood will decay faster? [NEET 1993]','Sapwood','Sap wood will decay faster. Sap wood is less durable because it is susceptible to attack by pathogen and insects(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:96, paragraph no:3).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Sapwood','Softwood','Wood with lot of fibres','Heartwood','NEW','11-096',NULL,NULL),
(60817,'biology','Abnormal/anomalous secondary growth occurs in [NEET 1993]','Dracaena','Monocot trees such as palms grow in thickness by primary thickening meristem situated at the base of the leaf. Plants like Dracaena show secondary growth by a special cambium. It develops in the form of strips just outside the vascular region. This cambial strips produce secondary vascular bundles which is amphivasal in Dracaena.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Dracaena','Ginger','Wheat','Sunflower','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60817q.png'),
(60818,'biology','Bordered pits are found in [NEET 1993]','Vessel wall','Bordered pits are found in vessel wall. In bordered pits, the thickening material over arches the pit cavity in such a way that a pit chamber opens to the interior by a pit aperture.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Sieve cells','Vessel wall','Companion cells','Sieve tube wall','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(60819,'biology','A bicollateral vascular bundle is characterized by [NEET 1992]','Xylem being sandwitched between phloem','A bicollateral vascular bundles is characterised by xylem being sandwiched between phloem. Here there are two cambuim rings e.g., Cucurbita.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Phloem being sandwitched between xylem','Transverse splitting of vascular bundle','Longitudinal splitting of vascular bundle','Xylem being sandwitched between phloem','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(60820,'biology','Vascular cambium produces [NEET 1992, 1990]','Secondary xylem and secondary phloem','Vascular cambium produces secondary xylem and secondary phloem. It is develop from the procambium which is an embryonic tissue, hence it is primary in origin. It is secondary in function for it forms the secondary tissues like secondary xylem, secondary phloem and secondary medullary rays. The cambium is a radially one cell thick zone of meristematic cells. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:94, paragraph no:4)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Primary xylem and primary phloem','Secondary xylem and secondary phloem','Primary xylem and secondary phloem','Secondary xylem and primary phloem','NEW','11-094','11-077','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60820q.png'),
(60821,'biology','What is true about a monocot leaf? [NEET 1992, 1990]','Mesophyll not differentiated into palisade and spongy tissues','In monocot leaf, mesophyll cells are not differentiated into palisade and spongy tissues. But there is well differentiate mesophyll cells in dicot stem. Also in the upper epidermis, there are some large cells found in groups which are called bulliform cells. The venation pattern in monocot is parallel. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:94, paragraph no1-:2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Reticulate venation','Absence of bulliform cells from epidermis','Mesophyll not differentiated into palisade and spongy tissues','Well differentiated mesophyll','NEW','11-094','11-077',NULL),
(60822,'biology','Which is correct about transport of conduction of substances? [NEET 1991]','Organic food moves upwardly and downwardly through phloem','Phloem is the food conducting tissue of plants. The sieve tubes are food conducting elements of the plants. It is proposed that food is translocated by mass flow or by streaming currents of protoplasm. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:88, paragraph no:1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Organic food moves up through phloem','Organic food moves up through xylem','Inorganic food moves upwardly and downwardly through xylem','Organic food moves upwardly and downwardly through phloem','NEW','11-088','11-071',NULL),
(60823,'biology','An organised and differentiated cellular structure having cytoplasm but no nucleus is [NEET 1991]','Sieve tubes','An organised and differentiated cellular structure having cytoplasm but no nucleus are sieve tubes. The sieve tubes are living cells. Their walls are thicker than surrounding parenchyma cells. Sometimes they have a special, shining nacreous thickening. Cytoplasm occurs in the form of thin lining enclosing a big central vacuole.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:88, paragraph no:1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Vessels','Xylem parenchyma','Sieve tubes','Tracheids','NEW','11-088','11-071',NULL),
(60824,'biology','Angular collenchyma occurs in [NEET 1991]','Cucurbita','Angular collenchyma occurs in, Cucurbita. It has thickening at the angles and there are no intercellular spaces. It is generally found in leaf petioles.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Cucurbita','Helianthus','Althaea','Salvia','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(60825,'biology','For union between stock and scion in grafting which one is the first to occur [NEET 1990]','Formation of callus','Grafting is a techinque in which cambium bearing shoot (scion = graft) of one plant is joined to cambium bearing stump (root system = stock) of a related plant through different unions like tongue grafting, wedge grafting etc. In grafting union between stock and scion produces undifferentiated mass of cells called callus. Therefore, for union between stock and scion in grafting, first to occur is the formation of callus. Callus is more or less corky secondary tissue developed lay woody plants over a wound. It is derived from cambium.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Formation of callus','Production of plasmodesmata','Differentiation of new vascular tissues','Regeneration of cortex and epidermis','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(60826,'biology','Pericycle of roots produces [NEET 1990]','Lateral roots','Pericycle of root produces lateral roots. Endodermis is followed by pericycle. Usually it is a continuous layer but in some monocots it is interrupted by xylem and phloem. It is the site of origin of lateral roots and cork cambium. The root branches are, therefore described as endogenous in origin. (Reference  : NCERT, page 91, paragraph 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Mechanical support','Lateral roots','Vascular bundles','Adventitious buds','NEW','11-091','11-074',NULL),
(60827,'biology','Collenchyma occurs is [NEET 1990]','Climbing stems','Collenchyma occurs in climbing stems. Collenchyma occurs in the stem and petioles of dicot herbs. Due to deposition of pectin, it has high water retaining capacity. Since pectin appears at the angles, it becomes a spongy tissues. The collenchyma is a mechanical tissue which gives tensile strength to the plant.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Herbaceous climbers','Woody climbers','Climbing stems','Water plants','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(60828,'biology','Collenchyma occurs in the stem and petioles of [NEET 1990]','Dicot herbs','Collenchyma occurs in climbing stems. Collenchyma occurs in the stem and petioles of dicot herbs. Due to deposition of pectin, it has high water retaining capacity. Since pectin appears at the angles, it becomes a spongy tissues. The collenchyma is a mechanical tissue which gives tensile strength to the plant.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Xerophytes','Monocots','Dicot herbs','Hydrophytes','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(60829,'biology','Monocot leaves possess [NEET 1990]','Intercalary meristem','Monocot leaves possess intercalary meristem. Intercalary meristem are responsible for localised growth. Perhaps they have been separated or detached from the mother meristem e.g., meristem present at the base of leaves in many monocots, in the internode of grasses, at the top of peduncles of Plantago and Taraxacum etc.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Intercalary meristem','Lateral meristem','Apical meristem','Mass meristem','NEW','11-085',NULL,NULL),
(60830,'biology','Cork cambium and vascular cambium are [NEET 1990]','Lateral meristem','Cork cambium and vascular cambium are lateral meristems. Both are responsible for the secondary growth of stem. It also increases the girth of stem. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:85, paragraph no:2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Parts of secondary xylem and phloem','Parts of pericycle','Lateral meristem','Apical meristem','NEW','11-085',NULL,NULL),
(60831,'biology','Out of diffuse porous and ring porous woods, which is correct? [NEET 1989]','Ring porous wood carries more water when need is higher.','Ring porous wood carries more water when need is higher. Ring porous wood provides better translocation when requirement of plant is more. Hence, it is very advanced than diffuse porous wood.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Ring porous wood carries more water for short period.','Diffuse porous wood carries more water.','Ring porous wood carries more water when need is higher.','Diffuse porous wood is less specialised but conducts water rapidly throughout','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(60832,'biology','Sieve tubes are suited for translocation of food because they possess [NEET 1989]','Broader lumen and perforated cross walls','Sieve tubes are suited for translocation of food because they possess broader lumen and perforated cross walls. Sieve tubes are elongated tubular conducting channels of phloem. The end wall possess many small pores and have thin cellulosic wall.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:88, paragraph no:1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Bordered pits','No ends walls','Broader lumen and perforated cross walls','No protoplasm','NEW','11-088','11-071',NULL),
(60833,'biology','Death of protoplasm is a prerequisite for a vital function like [NEET 1989]','Transport of sap','Death of protoplasm is a prerequisite for a vital functions like transport of sap. Xylem is a dead tissue and do not have protoplasm, xylem performs the function of transport of water or sap inside the plant from roots to leaves','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Transport of sap','Transport of food','Absorption of water','Gaseous exchange','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(60834,'biology','Organisation of stem apex into corpus and tunica is determined mainly by [NEET 1988]','Planes of cell division','The tunica corpus concept was given by Schmidt','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Planes of cell division','Regions of meristematic activity','Rate of cell growth','Rate of shoot tip growth','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(60835,'biology','Pith and cortex do not differentiate in [NEET 1988]','Monocot stem','Pith and cortex do not differentiate in monocot stem. Since numerous vascular bundles lie scattered, the ground tissue system in a monocot stem is distinguishable into hypodermis and ground parenchyma.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Monocot stem','Dicot stem','Monocot root','Dicot root','NEW','11-093','11-076',NULL),
(60836,'biology','Cork is formed from [NEET 1988]','Cork cambium (phellogen)','In hypodermis or outer cortical cells, a layer becomes meristematic which is known as cork cambium or phellogen. This phellogen also cuts off cells both on its outer side and inner side. The cells cut off on outer side are phellem or cork cells and cells cut off on inner side are phelloderm or secondary cortex. The phellem or cork cells are dead and have deposition of a fatty substance called suberin (i.e., cork cells are suberized). Suberin is impervious to water and thus cork cells are buoyant (i.e., float on water). Phellem, phellogen and phelloderm collectively constitute periderm. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:96, paragraph no:4)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Cork cambium (phellogen)','Vascular cambium','Phloem','Xylem','NEW','11-096',NULL,NULL),
(60837,'biology','Which meristem helps in increasing girth? [NEET 1988]','Lateral meristem','The meristem that helps in increasing girth is lateral meristem. The lateral meristem is responsible for lateral growth of the plant i.e., growth in thickness e.g., cambium and cork cambium. It divides only periclinally or radially and is responsible for increase in girth or diameter. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:85, paragraph no:2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Lateral meristem','Intercalary meristem','Primary meristem','Apical meristem','NEW','11-085',NULL,NULL),
(60838,'biology','Which of the following is an incorrect statement?[NEET 2021]','Mature sieve tube elements possess a conspicuous nucleus and usual cytoplasmic organelles','Sieve elements are thin-walled cells that are alive at maturity, although the protoplast is greatly changed, and they generally lack nuclei.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:88, paragraph no:1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Nuclear pores act as passages for proteins and RNA molecules in both directions between nucleus and cytoplasm','Mature sieve tube elements possess a conspicuous nucleus and usual cytoplasmic organelles','Microbodies are present both in plant and animal cells','The perinuclear space forms a barrier between the materials present inside the nucleus and that of the cytoplasm','NEW','11-088','11-071',NULL),
(60839,'biology','Match List-I with List-II according to the given diagram [NEET 2021] (0618)','3','During secondary growth, where epidermis is replaced by tough and hard periderm, the cracked/ruptured epidermis forms the small holes called lenticels which help in gaseous exchange.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:97, paragraph no:2)\n\nCork cambium or phellogen develops, usually in the cortex region. It provides a new protective cell layer. . It is made of narrow, thin-walled and nearly rectangular cells. The outer cells cut by the phallogen differentiate into cork or phellem while the inner cells differentiate into secondary cortex or phelloderm. The cork is impermiable to water due to suberin deposition in the cell wall. (Reference  s: NCERT Chapter 6; Page 97; Paragraph 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-097',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/60839q.png'),
(60840,'biology','Select the correct pair.[NEET 2021]','Cells of medullary rays  that form part of  cambial ring- Interfascicular cambium','The cells of medullary rays, adjoining these intrafascicular cambium become meristematic and form the interfascicular cambium. (Reference  : NCERT, page 95, paragraph 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Loose parenchyma cells rupturing the epidermis and forming a lens shaped opening in bark- Spongy parenchyma','Large colorless empty cells in the epidermis of grass leaves- Subsidiary cells','In dicot leaves, vascular  bundles are surrounded by large thick-walled cells- Conjunctive tissue','Cells of medullary rays  that form part of  cambial ring- Interfascicular cambium','NEW','11-095',NULL,NULL),
(60841,'biology','Match the following [NEET 2021]','Cells with active cell division capacity-Meristamatic tissue,Tissues having all cells similar in structure and functions-Simple tissue,Tissues having different types of cells-Vascular tissue,Dead cells with highly thickened walls and narrow lumen-Scelerids','Meristems are specialised regions of active cell division. Growth in plants is largely restricted to meristems. Plants have different parts as meristems. The meristems which occur at the tips of roots and shoots and produce primary tissues are called apical meristems. (Reference  s: NCERT Chapter 6; Page 84; Paragraph 3)\n\nA tissue is a group of cells with a common origin and performing a common function. Meristematic tissues actively divide further but permanent tissues do not. (Reference  s: NCERT Chapter 6; Page 84; Paragraph 2 )','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','match','Cells with active cell division capacity,Meristamatic tissue','Tissues having all cells similar in structure and functions,Simple tissue','Tissues having different types of cells,Vascular tissue','Dead cells with highly thickened walls and narrow lumen,Scelerids','NEW','11-084',NULL,NULL),
(60842,'biology','In old trees the greater part of the secondary xylem is dark brown and resistant to insect attack due to:\n \n (a) secretion of secondary metabolites and their deposition in the lumen of vessels\n \n (b) deposition of organic compounds like tannins and resins in the central layers of stems\n \n (c) deposition of suberin and aromatic substances in the outer layer of stem\n \n (d) deposition of tannins, gum, resin and aromatic substances in the peripheral layers of stem\n \n (e) presence of parenchyma cells, functionally active xylem elements and essential oils\n \n Choose the correct answer from the following options given below:\n [NEET 2022]','a  and  b only','In old trees, the greater part of secondary xylem is dark brown due to deposition of organic compounds like tannins, resins, oils, gums, aromatic\nsubstances and essential oils in the central or innermost layers of the stem. These substances make it hard, durable and resistant to the attacks of micro-\norganisms and insects. (Reference  : NCERT, page: 96, paragraph 3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','a  and  b only','c  and  d only','d  and  e only','b  and  d only','NEW','11-096',NULL,NULL),
(60843,'biology','Read the following statements about the vascular bundle:\n\na. In roots, xylem and phloem in a vascular bundle are arranged in an alternate manner along the different radii.\n\nb. Conjoint closed vascular bundles do not possess cambium.\n\nc. In open vascular bundles, cambium is present in between xylem and phloem.\n\nd. The vascular bundles of dicotyledonous stem possess endarch protoxylem. \n\ne. In monocotyledonous root, usually there are more than six xylem bundles present.\n \n Choose the correct answer from the following options given below:\n [NEET 2022]','a, c, d  and  e only','Each vascular bundle is conjoint, open, and with endarch protoxylem. In dicotyledonous stems, cambium is present between phloem and xylem. Such vascular bundles because of the presence of\ncambium possess the ability to form secondary xylem and phloem tissues, and hence are called open vascular bundles. As compared to the dicot root which have fewer xylem bundles, there are usually more than six (polyarch) xylem bundles in the monocot root. When xylem and phloem within a vascular bundle are arranged in an alternate manner along the different radii, the arrangement is called radial\nsuch as in roots. (refernece: NCERT, page 90, paragraph 1).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','a, b  and  d only','b, c, d  and  e only','a,b ,c  and  d only','a, c, d  and  e only','NEW','11-090','11-073',NULL),
(60844,'biology','The anatomy of springwood shows some peculiar features. Identify the correct set of statements about springwood: [NEET 2022]\n\n(a) It is also called as the earlywood\n\n\n(b) In spring season cambium produces xylem elements with narrow vessels \n\n(c) It is lighter in colour\n\n(d) The springwood along  with autumnwood shows alternate concentric rings forming annual rings\n\n(e) It is lower density\n\n\nChoose the correct answer from the following options given below:','a, c, d  and  e only','In the spring season, cambium is very active and produces a large number of xylary elements having vessels with wider cavities. The wood formed during this season is called spring\nwood or early wood. The spring wood is lighter in colour and has a lower density whereas the autumn wood is darker and has a higher density. The two kinds of woods that appear as alternate concentric rings, constitute an annual ring.(Reference  : NCERT, page 96, paragraph 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','a, b, d  and  e only','a, c, d  and  e only','a, b  and  d only','c, d  and  e only','NEW','11-096',NULL,NULL),
(60845,'biology','Initiation of lateral roots and vascular cambium\n during secondary growth takes place in cells of :  [ RE NEET 2022]','(4) Pericycle','Secondary growth is an increase in the thickness of the shoots and roots of a vascular plant as a result of the activity of the two lateral meristems, the cork cambium and vascular cambium.\nThe pericycle is a cylinder of cells that lies just inside the endodermis and phloem in plant roots while in stems, it is situated around the ring of vascular bundles. It has the capacity to regulate the formation of lateral roots by rapidly dividing near the xylem elements of the root. It strengthens the roots and provides protection for the vascular bundles.\nIn plants undergoing secondary growth, the pericycle contributes to the vascular cambium often diverging into a cork cambium.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(1) Epiblema','(2) Cortex','(3) Endodermis','(4) Pericycle','NEW','11-091','11-074',NULL),
(60846,'biology','The Floral diagram represents which one of the \nfollowing families ? (0620)','(2) Brassicaceae','Brassicaceae family','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','(1) Fabaceae','(2) Brassicaceae','(3) Solanaceae','(4) Liliaceae','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/60846q.png'),
(60847,'biology','The type of tissue commonly found in the fruit wall of nuts is :','(4) Sclereid','The spherical, oval or cylindrical cells with very narrow lumen; in sclerenchyma are called sclereids. Sclereids are usually found in the fruit walls of nuts, pulp of fruits like guava, pear and sapota; seed coats of legumes and leaves of tea. The gritty texture of pear or guava is because of sclereids.\nSo, the correct answer is \'Sclereids.\'','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Parenchyma','(2) Collenchyma','(3) Sclerenchyma','(4) Sclereid','NEW','11-087',NULL,NULL),
(60848,'biology','Interfascicular cambium is present between : [ RE NEET 2022]','(3) Two vascular bundles','In a dicotyledonous stem, the cells present between the primary xylem and primary phloem are called intrafascicular cambium.\nThe intrafascicular cambium is also known as the vascular cambium','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Primary xylem and primary phloem','(2) Pericycle and endodermis','(3) Two vascular bundles','(4) Secondary xylem and secondary phloem','NEW','11-085',NULL,NULL),
(60849,'biology','Consider the following tissues in the stelar region of a stem showing secondary growth.\n(A) Primary xylem\n(B) Secondary xylem\n(C) Primary phloem\n(D) Secondary phloem\nArrange these in the correct sequence of their position from pith towards cortex.[ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(3) (A), (B), (D), (C)','The correct sequence of tissues in the stelar region of the stem showing secondary growth from pith towards cortex is (A), (B), (D), (C)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) (A), (B), (C), (D)','(2) (B), (A), (D), (C)','(3) (A), (B), (D), (C)','(4) (B), (A), (C), (D)','NEW','11-095',NULL,NULL),
(60901,'chemistry','Following reaction occuring in an automobile \n2C₈H₁₈(g) + 25O₂(g) -------> 16CO₂(g) + 18H₂O(g) \n\nThe sign of ∆H, ∆S and ∆G would be','-, +, -','(i) The given reaction is a combustion reaction, therefore ∆H is less than 0. Hence, ∆H is negative.\n(ii) Since there is increase in the number of moles of gaseous products, therefore ∆S is positive.\n(iii) Since reaction is spontaneous, therefore ∆G is negative.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6) ( NEET 1994)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','+, -, -','-, -, +','-, +, -','+, -, +','NEW','11-178','11-154',NULL),
(60902,'chemistry','Cell reaction spontaneous when','∆G° is negative','For a cell reaction to be spontaneous, ∆G° should be negative. As ∆G° = -nfE°cell, so the value will\nbe -ve only E°cell is +ve.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6) ( NEET 2000)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','∆G° is negative','∆G° is positive','∆Ered° is positive','∆Ered° is negative','NEW','11-178','11-154',NULL),
(60903,'chemistry','PbO₂ ----->PbO; ∆G 298 < 0 \nSnO₂ ------>SnO; ∆G298 > 0 \n\nMost probable oxidation state of Pb and Sn will be','Pb⁺², Sn⁺⁴','The sign and magnitude of Gibbs free energy is a criterion of spontaneity for a process.\nWhen ∆G > 0 or +ve, it means Gproduct > G reactants\nas ∆G = Gproduct - G reactant\nthe reaction will not take place simultaneously; to the reaction should be spontaneous in reverse direction\nSnO₂⁺⁴ (more favourable) -------- SnO⁺²; ∆G > 0 \n∆G < 0 or -ve, the reaction or change occur spontaneously.\nPbO₂⁺⁴ ------- PbO⁺² (more favourable) ; ∆G < 0 \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6) ( NEET 2001)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','Pb⁺⁴, Sn⁺²','Pb⁺², Sn⁺²','Pb⁺², Sn⁺⁴','Pb⁺⁴, Sn⁺⁴','NEW','11-178','11-154',NULL),
(60904,'chemistry','The standard enthalpy and standard entropy changes for the oxidation of ammonia at 298 K are 382.64 kJ mol −1and 145.6 J K −1mol −1, respectively. Standard Gibb\'s energy change for the same reaction at 298 K is:','-339.3 kJ/mol','∆G = ∆H - T∆S \n      = -382.64 - 298(-145.6/1000) = -382.64 + 43.38 = -339.3 kJ/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6) ( NEET 2004)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','-339.3 kJ/mol','-221.1 kJ/mol','-523.2 kJ/mol','-439.3 kJ/mol','NEW','11-178','11-154',NULL),
(60905,'chemistry','For the reaction, X₂O₄(l)→2XO₂(g), ΔU=2.1 Kcal, ΔS=20 cal K−1 at 300 K.Hence, ΔG is:','-2.7 cal','ΔH=ΔU+ΔngRT\nΔng is the (number of gaseous moles in product) - (number of gaseous moles in reactant)\n∆ng = 2 - 0 = 2, R is the gas constant =2 cal\nBut,\nΔH=(2.1×10³)+(2×2×300)=3300cal\nHence,\nΔG=ΔH=TΔS\nΔG=3300−(300×20)\nΔG=−2700cal=−2.7cal\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2) ( NEET 2014)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','-2.7 cal','2.7 cal','-9.3 cal','9.3 cal','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(60906,'chemistry','An ideal gas expands from 1×10⁻³m³ to 1×10⁻²m³ at 300K against a constant pressure of 1×10⁵Nm⁻².The work done is','-900 j','W=−PΔV\nW=−1×10⁵(10⁻² - 10⁻³)\n⇒W=−10⁵ ×(9×10⁻³)\n⇒W=−9×10²\n =−900J\nHence the work done is −900J.    (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 159; Paragraph No.6.2.1) ( Odisha NEET 2019)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','900 j','270 j','-900 j','300 j','NEW','11-159','11-135',NULL),
(60907,'chemistry','A gas is allowed to expand in a well insulated container against constant external pressure of 2.5 atm from an initial volume of 2.50 L to a final volume of 4.50 L. The change in internal energy △U of the gas in joules will be','-505 j','ΔU=q+W\nSince container is well insulated, q=0.\nHence, ΔU=W=−PΔV\nΔU=W=−2.5atm×(4.50L−2.50L)\nΔU=W=−5.0Latm\nConvert the unit to J\nΔU=W=−5.0Latm×101.33J​/1Latm =−505J\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 158; Paragraph No.6.1.4) ( NEET 2017)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','-500 j','500 j','1136.25 j','-505 j','NEW','11-158','11-134',NULL),
(60908,'chemistry','Equal volumes of two monoatomic gases, A and B, at same temperature and pressure are mixed. The ratio of specific heats (Cp /Cv) of the mixture will be:','1.67','Cp for monoatomic gas is 5/2R and Cv is 3/2R \nCp/Cv = 5/2R/3/2R = 5/3 = 1.67 \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 163; Paragraph No.6.2.2) ( NEET 2012)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','0.83','1.5','3.3','1.67','NEW','11-163','11-137',NULL),
(60909,'chemistry','Which of the following is correct option for free expansion of an ideal gas under adiabatic condition is','q = 0, ∆T = 0, w = 0','For free expansion of an ideal gas under adiabatic condition q = 0, ∆T = 0, w = 0 \nFor free expansion w = 0, adiabatic process q = 0,  ∆U = q + w = 0 \nInternal energy remain constant means ∆T = 0 \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 158; Paragraph No.6.1.4) ( NEET 2011)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','q = 0, ∆T < 0, w ≠0','q = 0, ∆T = 0, w = 0','q = 0, ∆T ≠0, w = 0','q ≠ 0, ∆T = 0, w = 0','NEW','11-158','11-134',NULL),
(60910,'chemistry','Three mole of an ideal gas expanded spontaneously into vacuum The work done will be','zero','Since the ideal gas expands spontaneously into vacuum, Pex = 0. Hence work done is also zero.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 159; Paragraph No.6.2.1) ( Mains 2010)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','zero','infinite','3 j','9 j','NEW','11-159','11-135',NULL),
(60911,'chemistry','Assume each reaction is carried out in an open container.For which reaction will ΔH = ΔE ?','H₂(g) + Br₂(g) ------ 2HBr(g)','We know that, ∆H=∆U+∆PV\nBut for a physical or chemical change, △H=△U+△ngRT\nwhere, ng= No. of gaseous moles of product- Reactant\nFor ∆H=∆U or ∆H=∆E\n∆ng has to =0 ∆ng=2−2=0 ; Therefore, ∆H=∆E. \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2) ( NEET 2006)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','2CO(g) + O₂(g) ------ 2CO₂(g)','PCl₅(g) ------- PCl₃(g) + Cl₂(g)','H₂(g) + Br₂(g) ------ 2HBr(g)','C(s) + 2H₂O(g) ------ 2H₂(g) + CO₂(g)','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(60912,'chemistry','The work done during the expansion of a gas from a volume of 4 dm^3 to 6 dm^3 against a constant external pressure of 3 atm is:','-608 j','W=−P∆V\nw = -P( Vfinal - Vinitial)\nW=−3(6−4) = -6L−atm = −6×101.32 = ≈ −608J          (∵1dm^3 =1L) \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 159; Paragraph No.6.2) ( NEET 2004)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','-6 j','-608 j','-304 j','304 j','NEW','11-159','11-135',NULL),
(60913,'chemistry','For a reaction \nC₃H₈(g) + 5O₂(g) ------> 3CO₂(g) + 4H₂O(l) At constant temperature, ∆H - ∆E is','-3RT','C₃H₈(g) + 5O₂(g) → 3CO₂(g) + 4H₂O(l)\n∆ng = 3 - 6 = -3  \n∆H = ∆E + P∆V  or ∆H - ∆E = P∆V  \n∆H - ∆E = ∆ngRT = -3RT\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2) ( NEET 2003)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','RT','-2RT','-3RT','3RT','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(60914,'chemistry','The molar heat capacity of water at constant pressure P, is 75 JK−1mol−1. When 1.0 kJ of heat is supplied to 100 g of water which is free to expand, the increase in temperature of water is:','2.4 k','q=m⋅CpΔT, q=1kJ=1000J\nNo of mole = m/M = 100/18 \nSubstitute values in the above expression.\n1000 = 100/18 × 75 × ∆T\n∆T = 10 × 18 /  75 = 2.4 k\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 163; Paragraph No.6.2.2) ( NEET 2003)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','2.4 k','-2.4 k','4.4 k','6.6 k','NEW','11-163','11-137',NULL),
(60915,'chemistry','One mole of an ideal gas at 300 K is expanded isothermally from an initial volume of 1 litre to 10 litres. The value of ΔE for this process is (R=2 cal mol −1K−1)','zero','Change in internal energy depends upon temprature.\nAt constant temperature, the internal energy of a gas remain constant\nso ∆E = 0 \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2) ( NEET 1998)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','9 L atm','163.7 cal','zero','1381.1 cal','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(60916,'chemistry','For a reaction N₂ + 3H₂ ----- 2NH₃, ∆H = ?','∆E - 2RT','∆ng = 2 - 4 = -2 so ∆H = ∆E  -2RT\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2) ( NEET 1991)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','∆E  + 2RT','∆H = RT','∆E - 2RT','∆E = RT','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(60917,'chemistry','If ΔH is the change in enthalpy and ΔE the change in internal energy accompanying a gaseous reaction, then','∆H < ∆E only if the number of moles of the product is less than the number of moles of the reactants','As ∆H =∆E + ∆ngRT and If  ∆nP < ∆ nR: ∆ng = nP - nR = -ve . Hence ∆H < ∆E \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2) ( NEET 1990)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','∆H < ∆E only if the number of moles of the product is less than the number of moles of the reactants','∆H is always less than ∆E','∆H  is always greater than ∆E','∆H < ∆E only if the number of moles of the product is greater than the number of moles of the reactants','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(60918,'chemistry','Three thermochemical equations are given below: \n\n(i) C(graphite) + O₂(g) → CO₂(g); ∆ᵣH° = x kJ mol⁻¹ \n\n(ii) C(graphite) + 1/2O₂(g) → CO(g); ∆ᵣH° = y kJ mol⁻¹ \n\n(iii) CO(g) + 1/2 O₂(g) → CO₂(g); ∆ᵣH° = z kJ mol⁻¹ \n\nBased on the above equations, find out which of the relationship given below is correct?','x = y + z','According to Hess\'s law, equation(i) is equal to equation (ii) + (iii)\ni.e., x = y + z\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 169; Paragraph No.6.4) ( Karnataka NEET 2013)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','z = x + y','x = y + z','y = 2z - x','x = y - z','NEW','11-169','11-143','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60918q.png'),
(60919,'chemistry','Standard enthalpy of vaporisation ∆VapH° for water at 100°C is 40.66 kJ/mol. The internal energy of vaporisation of water at 100°C ( in kJ/mol) is','37.56','∆vapH° = 40.66 kJ/mol\nT = 100 + 273 = 373K \n∆H =  ∆E + ∆ngRT or ∆E = ∆H - ∆ngRT \nH2O(l) ----- H2O(g)\n∆ng = 1\n∆E = ∆H - ∆ngRT \n∆E = (40.66 ×10^3) - ( 8.314 - 373) = 37559 j/mol or 37.56 kJ/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.2) (NEET 2012)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','37.56','-43.76','40.66','43.76','NEW','11-160','11-136',NULL),
(60920,'chemistry','Consider the following process: ∆H ( kJ/mol) \n\n1/2 A = B +150 3B ------ 2C + D -125 \n\nE + A ------ 2D +350 \n\nFor B + D ------- E + 2C, ∆H will be','-175 kJ/mol','2(i) - (iii) + (ii)\n∆H = 2(150) - 350 - 125 = -175 kJ/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.2) (Mains 2011)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','525 kJ/mol','-175 kJ/mol','-325 kJ/mol','325 kJ/mol','NEW','11-160','11-136',NULL),
(60921,'chemistry','The following two reactions are known Fe₂O₃(s)+3CO(g)→2Fe(s)+3CO₂(g) ; \n\nΔ H=−26.8 kJ FeO(s)+CO(g)→Fe(s)+CO₂(g) ; ΔH=−16.5 kJ \n\nThe value of Δ H for the following reaction is: \nFe₂O₃(s)+CO(g)→2FeO(s)+CO₂(g)','+ 6.2 kJ','Fe₂O₃(s)+3CO(g)→2Fe(s)+3CO₂(g) ; Δ H=−26.8 kJ (i)\nFeO(s)+CO(g)→Fe(s)+CO₂(g) ; ΔH=−16.5 kJ (ii)\nFe₂O₃(s)+CO(g)→2FeO(s)+CO₂(g), ∆H = ?  (iii)\nEq. (iii) can be obtained as\n(i) - 2(ii)\n= -26.8 - 2(-16.5) = 6.2 kJ\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.2) (Mains 2010)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','+ 6.2 kJ','-10.3 kJ','-43.3 kJ','10.3 kJ','NEW','11-160','11-136','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60921q.png'),
(60922,'chemistry','Change in enthalpy for reaction : 2H₂O₂(ℓ)⟶ 2H₂O(ℓ) + O₂(g) If heat of formation of H₂O₂(l) and H₂O(l) are -188 and -286 kJ/mol respectively:','-196 kJ/mol','∆H°f = ∑H°f(product) - ∑H°f(reactant)\nfor the given reaction\n2H₂O₂(l) ------- 2H₂O(l) + O₂(g)\nH°f = 2 × ∆H°f(H₂O) - 2 × ∆H°f(H₂O2)\n2 × -286 kJ/mol - 2 × (-188) kJ/mol\n-196 kJ/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.2) (NEET 2001)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','+196 kJ/mol','-196 kJ/mol','+948 kJ/mol','-948 kJ/mol','NEW','11-160','11-136','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60922q.png'),
(60923,'chemistry','The bond dissociation: energies of X2,YY and XY are in the ratio of 1:0.5:1. ΔH for the formation of XY is −200 kJ mol −1. The bond dissociation energy of X2 will be','800 kJ/mol','Let B.E of X2, Y2 and XY is x kJ/mol, 0.5 kJ/mol and x kJ/mol respectively\n1/2 X + 1/2Y ------- XY\nΔH (reaction )=ΣΔH (reactant bonds )−ΣΔH ( product bonds)\nΔH (reaction) = 1/2 ∆H(X-X) + 1/2 ∆H(Y-Y) - ∆H(X-Y)\n-200 = 1/2(x) + 0.5(x) - 1(x)\n− 200=−0.25 (x)\nx = -200/-0.25 = 800 kJ/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.2) (NEET 2018)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','200 kJ/mol','800 kJ/mol','100 kJ/mol','300 kJ/mol','NEW','11-160','11-136','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60923q.png'),
(2000044511,'physics','When an electron in hydrogen atom revolves in stationary orbit, it','A','According to Bohr’s quantisation principle, if an electron revolves in a stationary orbit which has fixed energy, will not radiate light. Since,the direction of velocity changes, so we can say its velocity changes','Bohr Model of the Hydrogen Atom ','Atoms','Easy',NULL,'Does not radiate light through its velocity changes','Does not radiate light and velocity remains unchanged','Radiates light but its velocity is unchanged','Radiates light with the change of energy',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(60924,'chemistry','The absolute enthalpy of neutralisation of the reaction will be: MgO(s)+2HCl(aq)→MgCl₂(aq)+H₂O(l)','less than -57.33 kJ/mol','MgO is the oxide of weak and we know that heat of neutralization\nof 1 eq  of strong acid with strong base is -57.33 kJ/mol.\nWith weak base some heat is absorbed in dissociation of weak base.\nHeat of neutralization of weak base with strong acid will be less than\n-57.33 kJ/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 165; Paragraph No.6.4) (NEET 2005)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','-57.33 kJ/mol','greater than -57.33 kJ/mol','less than -57.33 kJ/mol','57.33 kJ/mol','NEW','11-165','11-139','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60924q.png'),
(60925,'chemistry','Consider the following liquid - vapour equilibrium Liquid ⇌ Vapour. Which of the following relations is correct?','d lnP/dT = ∆Hv/RT2','According to Clausius - Clapeyron\'s equation\nd lnP/dT = ∆Hv/RT2\nAccording to this equation, the rate at which the natural logarithm of the vapor pressure of a liquid\nchanges with temperature is determined by the molar enthalpy of vaporization of the liquid, the ideal\ngas constant, and the temperature of the system. (NEET - I 2016)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','d lnP/dT = -∆Hv/RT^2','d lnP/dT = -∆Hv/T^2','d lnG/dT^2= -∆Hv/RT^2','d lnP/dT = ∆Hv/RT2','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60925q.png'),
