(60854,'biology','Given below is the diagram of a stomatal apparatus.\nIn which of the following all the four parts labelled\nas A, B, C and D are correctly identified?','Epidermal cell - Subsidiary cell - Stomatal aperture - Guard cell','NCERT XII : 89','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Epidermal cell - Subsidiary cell - Stomatal aperture - Guard cell','Guard cell - Stomatal aperture - Subsidiary cell - Epidermal cell','Epidermal cell - Guard cell -Stomatal apperture - Subsidiary cell','Epidermal cell - Guard cell - Stomatal apperture - Subsidiary cell','NEW','11-089','11-072','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/60854q.png'),
(60855,'biology','Match it:\n(a) Cells with active cell division capacity - (i) Vascular tissues\n(b) Tissue having all cells similar in structure and function - (ii) Meristematic tissue \n(c) Tissue having different types of cells - (iii) Sclereids\n(d) Dead cells with highly thickenend  walls and narrow lumen - (iv)Simple tissue','ii,iv,i,iii','NCERT XII : 84-90','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','i,ii,iii,iv','iii,ii,iv,i','ii,iv,i,iii','iv,iii,ii,i','NEW','11-090','11-073',NULL),
(60856,'biology','Read the different components from (i) to (iv) in\nthe list given below and tell the correct order of the\ncomponents with reference to their arrangement\nfrom outer side to inner side in a woody dicot stem.\n(i) Secondary cortex (ii) Wood\n(iii)Secondary phloem (iv) Phellem\nThe correct order is\n\n(2015)','(a) (iv), (i), (iii), (ii)','NCERT XII : 92','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(a) (iv), (i), (iii), (ii)','(b) (iv), (iii), (i), (ii)','(c) (iii), (iv), (ii), (i)','(d) (i), (ii), (iv), (iii)','NEW','11-092','11-075',NULL),
(60857,'biology','Given below are two statements\nstatement I : Endarch and exarch are the term often used for describing the position of secondary xylem in the plant body\nstatement II : Exarch condition is the most common feature of the root system \nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from options given below:','Statement I is incorrect but statement II is true','NCERT XII : 92','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Statement I is incorrect but statement II is true','Both statement I and Statement II are true','Both statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but statement II is false','NEW','11-092','11-075',NULL),
(60858,'biology','The transverse section of a plant shows following anatomical features\na) Large number of scattered vascular bundles surrounded by bundle sheath\nb) Large conspicuous parenchymatous ground tissue\nc) Vascular bundles conjoint and closed\nd) Phloem parenchyma absent \nIdentify the category of plant and it\'s part:','Monocotyledonous stem','NCERT XII : 93','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Monocotyledonous root','Dicotyledonous stem','Dicotyledonous root','Monocotyledonous stem','NEW','11-093','11-076',NULL),
(60859,'biology','Large , empty colourless cells of the adaxial epidermis along the veins of grass leaves are','Bulliform cells','NCERT XII : 94','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Lenticles','Guard cells','Bundle sheath cells','Bulliform cells','NEW','11-094','11-077',NULL),
(60860,'biology','Grass leaves curl inwards during very dry weather.\nSelect the most appropriate reason from the\nfollowing.(NEET 2019)','(c) Flaccidity of bulliform cells','NCERT XII : 94','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','(a) Tyloses in vessels','(b) Closure of stomata','(c) Flaccidity of bulliform cells','(d) Shrinkage of air spaces in spongy mesophyll','NEW','11-094','11-077',NULL),
(60861,'biology','Secondary xylem and phloem in dicot stem are produced by\n(NEET 2018)','(b) vascular cambium','NCERT XII : 94','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','(a) apical meristems','(b) vascular cambium','(c) phellogen','(d) axillary meristems','NEW','11-094','11-077',NULL),
(60862,'biology','The vascular cambium normally gives rise to\n\n(NEET 2017)','(b) secondary xylem','NCERT XII : 94-95','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(a) primary phloem','(b) secondary xylem','(c) periderm','(d) phelloderm','NEW','11-095',NULL,NULL),
(60863,'biology','Identify the correct statement:\nA. Lenticles are the lens-shaped openings permitting the exchange of gases\nB. Bark formed early in the season is called hard bark\nC. Bark refers to periderm and secondary Phloem\nD. Phellogen is single layered in thickness.\nchoose the correct answer from the options given below:','A and D only','NCERT XII : 96','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Anatomy of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','B and C only','B,C and E only','A and D only','A,B and D only','NEW','11-096',NULL,NULL),
(61001,'chemistry','The second law of thermodynamics states that any spontaneously occurring process will always lead to?\n \n a)de-escalation in the entropy\n \n b) increase in disorder of surroundings\n \n c) overall positive entropy\n \n d)none of the above','b,c','The second law ofthermodynamics explains why spontaneous exothermic reactions are so common. In exothermic reactions heat released by the reaction increases the disorder of the surroundings and overall entropy change is positive which makes the reaction spontaneous.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 185 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,c','b,c','only a','only c','NEW','11-185','11-161',NULL),
(61002,'chemistry','Which of them act as a closed thermodynamic system?\n \n a) Human body\n \n b) Bonfire\n \n c) Sun\n \n d) Planet Earth','c,d','In an open system, there is exchange of energy and matter between system and surroundings.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 161 paragraph 6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,c','b,c','c,d','a,d','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(61003,'chemistry','We specify the state of the system by?\n \n a)state variables\n \n b)state coordinates\n \n c)Cartesian coordinates\n \n d)Temperature, pressure, volume','a,d','The state of a thermodynamic system is described by its measurable or macroscopic (bulk) properties. We can describe the state of a gas by quoting its pressure (p), volume (V), temperature (T ), amount (n) etc. Variables like p, V, T are called state variables or state functions because their values depend only on the state of the system and not on how it is reached.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 162 paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-162','11-138',NULL),
(61004,'chemistry','Which of these properties does not belong to the state variables\n \n a)internal energy \n \n b)temperature\n \n c)heat\n \n d)pressure','only c','The state of a thermodynamic system is described by its measurable or macroscopic (bulk) properties. We can describe the state of a gas by quoting its pressure (p), volume (V), temperature (T ), amount (n) etc. Variables like p, V, T are called state variables or state functions because their values depend only on the state of the system and not on how it is reached.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 162 paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','only c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-162','11-138',NULL),
(61005,'chemistry','What is free expansion?\n \n a)Expansion of a gas in vacuum\n \n b)expansion of a gas in infinite pressure\n \n c) unlimitted expansion of a gas\n \n d) Work done =0','a,d','Expansion of a gas in vacuum (pex= 0) is called free expansion. No work is done during free expansion of an ideal gas whether the process is reversible or irreversible.\n \n (Reference : NCERT class 11 page 166 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-166','11-140',NULL),
(61006,'chemistry','What is not extensive property ?\n \n a) volume\n \n b) internal energy \n \n c) temperature\n \n d) pressure','c,d','An extensive property is a property whose value depends on the quantity or size of matter present in the system. For example, mass, volume, internal energy, enthalpy, heat capacity, etc. are extensive properties.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 168 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-168','11-142',NULL),
(61007,'chemistry','When work is done on the system , then work done is:','positive','By conventions of IUPAC in chemical thermodynamics. The positive sign expresses that wad is positive when work is done on the system and the internal energy of system increases. Similarly, if the work is done by the system,wad will be negative because internal energy of the system decreases.\n \n (Reference : NCERT class 11 page 163 paragraph 5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','positive','negative','constant','zero','NEW','11-163','11-137',NULL),
(61008,'chemistry','Amount of heat required to vaporize one mole of a liquid at constant temperature and under standard pressure (1bar) is called?\n \n a)standard enthalpy of vaporation\n \n b)molar enthalpy of vaporation\n \n c)relative enthalpy\n \n d)general enthalpy','a,d','By conventions of IUPAC in chemical thermodynamics. The positive sign expresses that wad is positive when work is done on the system and the internal energy of system increases. Similarly, if the work is done by the system,W will be negative because internal energy of the system decreases.\n \n (Reference : NCERT class 11 page 163 paragraph 5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-163','11-137',NULL),
(61009,'chemistry','What is free expansion?\n \n a)Expansion of a gas in vacuum\n \n b)expansion of a gas in infinite pressure\n \n c) unlimitted expansion of a gas\n \n d) Work done =0','a,d','Expansion of a gas in vacuum (pex= 0) is called free expansion. No work is done during free expansion of an ideal gas whether the process is reversible or irreversible.\n \n (Reference : NCERT class 11 page 166 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-166','11-140',NULL),
(61010,'chemistry','What is not extensive property ?\n \n a) volume\n \n b) internal energy \n \n c) temperature\n \n d) pressure','c,d','An extensive property is a property whose value depends on the quantity or size of matter present in the system. For example, mass, volume, internal energy, enthalpy, heat capacity, etc. are extensive properties.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 168 paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-168','11-142',NULL),
(61011,'chemistry','When work is done on the system ,the internal energy of system:','increases','By conventions of IUPAC in chemical thermodynamics. The positive sign expresses that W is positive when work is done on the system and the internal energy of system increases. Similarly, if the work is done by the system,W will be negative because internal energy of the system decreases.\n \n (Reference : NCERT class 11 page 163 paragraph 5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','increases','decreases','remains constant','zero','NEW','11-163','11-137',NULL),
(61012,'chemistry','Amount of heat required to vaporize one mole of a liquid at constant temperature and under standard pressure (1bar) is called?\n \n a)standard enthalpy of vaporation\n \n b)molar enthalpy of vaporation\n \n c)relative enthalpy\n \n d)general enthalpy','a,b','Amount of heat required to vaporize one mole of a liquid at constant temperature and under standard pressure (1bar) is called its standard enthalpy of vaporization or molar enthalpy of vaporization, ∆vapH°.\n \n (Reference: NCERT class 11 page 172 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-172','11-148',NULL),
(61013,'chemistry','write equation for heat, q at constant volume\n \n a) q= Cv ∆ T\n \n b)Cv ∆ T=∆U\n \n c)Cv/∆ T=∆U\n \n d)1/q= Cv ∆ T','a,b','At constant volume, the heat capacity, C is denoted by Cv and at constant pressure, this is denoted by Cp. Let us find the relationship between the two.\n \n We can write equation for heat, qat constant volume as qv= Cv ∆T=∆U at constant pressure as qp= Cp∆T= ∆H\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 169 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-169','11-143',NULL),
(61014,'chemistry','By conventions of IUPAC in chemical thermodynamics. The q is positive, when?\n \n a)heat is transfered from the surroundings to the system\n \n b)the internal energy of the system increases\n \n c)the internal energy of the system decreases\n \n d)heat is transferred from system to the surroundings','a,b','By conventions of IUPAC in chemical thermodynamics. The positive sign expresses that wad is positive when work is done on the system and the internal energy of system increases. Similarly, if the work is done by the system,wad will be negative because internal energy of the system decreases.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 163 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-163','11-137',NULL),
(61015,'chemistry','Express the change in internal energy of a system when No work is done on the system, but q amount of heat is taken out from the system and given to the surroundings.\n \n a)∆ U = – q\n \n b)∆ U = q\n \n c)wall is adiabatic\n \n d)thermally conducting walls','a,d','No work is done on the system, but q amount of heat is taken out from the system and given to the surroundings:\n \n ∆ U = – q, thermally conducting walls\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 164 Problem 6.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-164','11-138',NULL),
(61016,'chemistry','At constant pressure for the ideal gas the heat capacity is equal ?\n \n a)qp= Cp∆T\n \n b)enthalpy change\n \n c)internal energy change\n \n d)qp=-Cp∆T','a,b','At constant volume, the heat capacity, C is denoted by CV and at constant pressure, this is denoted by Cp. Let us find the relationship between the two.\n \n We can write equation for heat, \n \n at constant volume as qV:\n \n qv= Cv∆T= ∆U\n \n at constant pressure as qp\n \n qp= Cp∆T =∆H\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 169 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-169','11-143',NULL),
(61017,'chemistry','At constant volume for the ideal gas the heat capacity is equal ?\n \n a)qp= Cv∆T \n \n b)enthalpy change \n \n c)internal energy change \n \n d)qp=-Cv∆T','a,c','At constant volume, the heat capacity, C is denoted by Cv and at constant pressure, this is denoted by Cp. Let us find the relationship between the two.\n \n We can write equation for heat, \n \n at constant volume as qV:\n \n qv= Cv∆T= ∆U\n \n at constant pressure as qp\n \n qp= Cp∆T =∆H\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 169 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-169','11-143',NULL),
(61018,'chemistry','In calorimetry,in which way we can determine the heat involved in the process?\n \n a)by measuring temperature changes at constant volume\n \n b)by measuring temperature changes at constant pressure \n \n c)by measuring velocity od temperature changes.\n \n d)by measuring pressure changes.','a,b','Knowing the heat capacity of the liquid in which calorimeter is immersed and the heat capacity of calorimeter, it is possible to determine the heat evolved in the process by measuring temperature changes. Measurements are made under two different conditions:\n \n i) at constant volume, qv \n \n ii) at constant pressure, qp\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 169 paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-169','11-143',NULL),
(61019,'chemistry','Bomb calorimeter is used for? \n \n a)measuring heat absorbed in constant volume\n \n b)measuring temperature change in constant volume\n \n c)measuring heat absorbed in constant pressure \n \n d)measuring temperature change in constant pressure','a,b','"For chemical reactions, heat absorbed at constant volume, is measured in a bomb calorimeter.Temperature change of the calorimeter produced by the completed\n \n reaction is then converted to qv, by using the known heat capacity of the calorimeter with the help of equation q =c*m*∆T=C ∆T \n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page170 problem 6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-170','11-144',NULL),
(61020,'chemistry','In bomb calorimeter...?\n \n a) reaction work is 0\n \n b)∆V is constant\n \n c) reaction work is increasing\n \n d)∆V=V2-V1','a,b','Since the bomb calorimeter is sealed, its volume does not change i.e., the energy changes associated with reactions are measured at constant volume. Under these conditions, no work is done as the reaction is carried out at constant volume in the bomb calorimeter.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 169 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-169','11-143',NULL),
(61021,'chemistry','The bomb in bomb calorimeter is?\n \n a)immersed in water\n \n b)made of steel\n \n c)immersed in gas\n \n d)made of glass','a,b','In bomb calorimeter a steel vessel (the bomb) is immersed in a water bath.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 169 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-169','11-143',NULL),
(61022,'chemistry','Measurement of heat change at constant pressure is also?\n \n a)∆H Measurements\n \n b)Measurement of heat change at atmospheric pressure\n \n c)∆U Measurements\n \n d)Measurement of heat change at vacuum','a,b','Measurement of heat change at constant pressure (generally under atmospheric pressure) can be done in a calorimeter shown in Fig. 6.8.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 170 paragraph first)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-170','11-144',NULL),
(61023,'chemistry','The standard state of a substance ..?\n \n a)at specified temperature \n \n b)is its pure form at 1 bar.\n \n c)is its pure form at atmosphere pressure\n \n d)at 273,15K','a,b','The standard state of a substance at a specified temperature is its pure form at 1 bar. For example, the standard state of liquid ethanol at 298 K is pure liquid ethanol at 1 bar; standard state of solid iron at 500 K is pure iron at 1 bar.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 171 paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-171','11-145',NULL),
(61024,'chemistry','Standard state of solid iron at .... is .....?\n \n a)500K\n \n b)600K\n \n c)pure iron\n \n d)liquid iron','a,c','The standard state of a substance at a specified temperature is its pure form at 1 bar. For example, the standard state of liquid ethanol at 298 K is pure liquid ethanol at 1 bar; standard state of solid iron at 500 K is pure iron at 1 bar.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 171 paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-171','11-145',NULL),
(61025,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\n \n A:For an organic liquid, such as acetone, the intermolecular dipole-dipole interactions are significantly weaker.\n \n R:it requires less heat to vaporise 1 mol of acetone than it does to vaporize 1 mol of water.','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','The magnitude of the enthalpy change depends on the strength of the intermolecular interactions in the substance undergoing the phase transfomations. For example, the strong hydrogen bonds between water molecules hold them tightly in liquid phase.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 172 paragraph 5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','11-172','11-148',NULL),
(61026,'chemistry','The standard enthalpy change of formation is:\n \n a)standard enthalpy of formation of 1 mole of a compound\n \n b)standard enthalpy of formation of a compound from it elements in their most stable states of agregation\n \n c)standard enthalpy change of formation of a compound at 273 K\n \n d)Standard enthalpy of formation of a compound at an atmospheric pressure','a,b','The standard enthalpy change for the formation of one mole of a compound from its elements in their most stable states of aggregation (also known as reference states) is called Standard Molar Enthalpy of Formation. Its symbol is ∆fH °.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 173paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-173','11-149',NULL),
(61027,'chemistry','which reaction is endothermic?\n \n a)CaCO3(s) → CaO(s) + CO2(g)\n \n b)2NH3 (g) → N2 (g) + 3H2 (g);\n \n c)1⁄2H2(g) + 1⁄2Br2(1) → HBr(g)\n \n d)H2(g) + Br2(l) → 2HBr(g)','a,b','∆H is negative for exothermic reactions which evolve heat during the reaction and ∆H is positive for endothermic reactions which absorb heat from the surroundings.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 167 paragraph 5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-167','11-141',NULL),
(61028,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true? about thermochemical reactions?\n \n a)The coefficients in a balanced thermo-chemical equation refer to the number of moles ) of reactants and products involved in the reaction.\n \n b)The numerical value of ∆rH° refers to the number of moles of substances specified by an equation.\n \n c)Standard enthalpy change ∆rH° will have units as kJ mol–1 .\n \n d)Standard enthalpy change ∆rH° will have units as J','a,b,c','It would be necessary to remember the following conventions regarding thermo-chemical equations. \n \n \n \n 1. The coefficients in a balanced thermo-chemical equation refer to the number ofmoles (never molecules) of reactants and products involved in the reaction. \n \n 2. The numerical value of ∆rH° refers to the number of moles of substances specified by an equation. Standard enthalpy change ∆rH° will have units as kJ mol–1.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 174 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b,c','b,d,a','c,b,d','a,c,d','NEW','11-174','11-150',NULL),
(61029,'chemistry','If a reaction takes place in several steps then its standard reaction enthalpy?\n \n a)is the sum of the standard enthalpies of the intermediate reactions\n \n b)is equal to the enthalpy of last step \n \n c)follows the Hesse\'s law\n \n d)is always positive','a,c','Hess law:\nIf a reaction takes place in several steps then its standard reaction enthalpy is the sum of the standard enthalpies of the intermediate reactions into which the overall reaction may be divided at the same temperature.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 175 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,c','c,d','b,c','a,d','NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(61030,'chemistry','Which reaction is exothermic?\n \n a)CH4(g) → C(g) + 4H(g)\n \n b)Cl2(g) → 2Cl(g)\n \n c)CaO(s) + CO2(g) → CaCO3(s);\n \n d)H2 (g) + Br2(l) → 2HBr(g);','c,d','∆H is negative for exothermic reactions which evolve heat during the reaction and ∆H is positive for endothermic reactions which absorb heat from the surroundings.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 167 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,c','c,d','b,c','a,d','NEW','11-167','11-141',NULL),
(61031,'chemistry','for Ionization enthalpy :\n \n a)Cp = -5/2RT\n \n b)Cv=3/2RT\n \n c)(ionization enthalpy)= E0 (ionization energy) + 5/2 RT\n \n d)(ionization enthalpy)0= E0 (ionization energy) - 5/2 RT','b,c','The value of Cp for each species (ionization and electron gain) is 5/2 R (CV = 3/2R) \n \n So, ∆rCp°= + 5/2 R (for ionization) \n \n ∆rCp° = – 5/2 R (for electron gain) \n \n Therefore, \n \n ∆rH° (ionization enthalpy)= E0 (ionization energy) + 5/2 RT \n \n ∆rH° (electron gain enthalpy) = – A( electron affinity) – 5/2 RT\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 179 paragraph 5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','c,d','b,c','a,d','NEW','11-179','11-155',NULL),
(61101,'chemistry','The enthalpy change for a reaction does not depend upon the [ JEE 2003 ]','Nature of intermediate reaction steps','The enthalpy change for a reaction does not depend upon the nature of intermediate reaction steps. This is according to Hess’s law.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Physical states of reactants and products','Use of different reactants for the same product','Nature of intermediate reaction steps','Difference in initial or final temperatures of involved substances.','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(61102,'chemistry','On the basis of the following thermochemical data\n\nH₂O(l) → H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq); ΔH = 57.32 kJ\n\nH₂(g) + 1/2 O₂(g) →  H₂O(l); ΔH = –286.20 kJ\n\nThe value of enthalpy of formation of OH⁻ ion at 25ºC is [AIEEE-2009]','–228.88 kJ','I. H₂O(l) → H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq); ΔH = 57.32 kJ\n\nII. H₂\n(g) + \n1\n/2 O₂(g) →  H₂\nO(l); ΔH = –286.20 kJ\n\nAdding I & II we get,\n\nH₂\n(g) + \n1\n/2 O₂\n(g) → H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq)\n\nΔH = 57.32 – 286.2\n = –228.88 kJ','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','–228.88 kJ','228.88 kJ','–343.52 kJ','–22.88 kJ','NEW','11-163','11-137',NULL),
(61103,'chemistry','In a fuel cell methanol is used as fuel and oxygen\n gas is used as an oxidizer. The reaction is\n\nCH₃OH(l) + 3/2O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(l)\n\nAt 298 K standard Gibb\'s energies of formation for CH₃OH(l), H₂O(l) and CO₂(g) are –166.2, –237.2 and  –394.4 kJ/mol respectively. If standard enthalpy of combustion of methanol is –726 kJ/mol , efficiency of the fuel cell will be','97%','CH₃OH(l) + \n3/2 O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(l)\n\nΔG(reaction) = ΔG(product) - ΔG(reactant)\n= [–394.4 – 2 × 237.2] – [–166.2]\n= –702.6 kJ\nWe know, efficiency of a fuel cell,\n\nη = ΔG/ΔH × 100 = –702.6×100\n/–726 ≈ 97%','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'3','mcq','87%','90%','97%','80%','NEW','11-164','11-138',NULL),
(61104,'chemistry','For a particular reversible reaction at temperature T, ∆H and ∆S were found to be both +ve. If Tₑ is the temperature at equilibrium, the reaction would be spontaneous when','T > Te','ΔG = ΔH – TΔS\nFor equilibrium ΔG = 0\nFor spontaneous reaction, ΔG < 0\n→ T > Te','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','T = Te','Te\n > T','T > Te','Te\n is 5 times T','NEW','11-166','11-140',NULL),
(61105,'chemistry','The standard enthalpy of formation of NH₃ is –46.0 kJ/mol. If the enthalpy of formation of H₂ from its atoms is –436 kJ/mol and that of N₂ is – 712 kJ/mol, the average bond enthalpy of N — H bond is NH₃ is','352 KJ/mol','1/2N₂\n + 3/2 H₂\n → NH₃\n\nΔH(NH₃) = -46 kJ\n\nNH₃ → 1/2N₂\n + 3/2 H₂\n\n46 = 3ΔH(N-H) -1/2(712) + (3/2)436\n\nΔH(N-H) = 352 KJ/mol','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','–1102 kJ/mol','–964 kJ/mol','352 kJ/mol','1056 kJ/mol','NEW','11-166','11-140',NULL),
(61106,'chemistry','Consider the reaction :\n\n4NO₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2N₂O₅(g), ∆ᵣH = –111 kJ.\n\nIf N₂O₅(s) is formed instead of N₂O₅(g) in the above reaction, the ∆ᵣH value will be: (given, ∆H of sublimation for 2N₂O₅  is 54 kJ/mol) [AIEEE-2011]','–219 kJ','∆ᵣH = –111 – (2 × 54) = –219 kJ','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','–219 kJ','–165 kJ','54 kJ','219 kJ','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(61107,'chemistry','In view of the signs of ∆ᵣG° for the following reactions\n\nPbO₂ + Pb → 2 PbO, ∆ᵣG° < 0\nSnO₂ + Sn → 2 SnO, ∆ᵣG° > 0,\nwhich oxidation states are more characteristic for lead and tin?','For lead +2, for tin +4','From the data, it is clear that Pb⁺² is more stable\n than Pb⁺⁴ and Sn⁺² is less stable than Sn⁺⁴.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','For lead +2, for tin +4','For lead +4, for tin +2','For lead +2, for tin +2','For lead +4, for tin +4','NEW','11-163','11-137',NULL),
(61108,'chemistry','The value of enthalpy change (∆H) for the reaction\n\nC₂H₅OOH(l) + 3O₂(g) → 2CO₂(g) + 3H₂O(1) at 27ºC is –1366.5 kJ/mol. The value of internal energy change for the above reaction at this temperature will be','–1364.0 kJ','∆H =∆E +∆ngRT\n–1366.5 = ∆E + (–1 × 8.314 × 300 × 10⁻³)\n∆E = –1364 kJ','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','–1361.5 kJ','–1371.5 kJ','–1369.0 kJ','–1364.0 kJ','NEW','11-162','11-138',NULL),
(61109,'chemistry','The incorrect expression among the following is\n[AIEEE-2012]','lnK = ∆H° - T∆S° / RT','lnK = ∆H° - T∆S° / RT','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','In isothermal process, W(reversible) = -nRT ln Vₜ/Vᵢ','lnK = ∆H° - T∆S° / RT','K = e ᴳ°/ᴿ∆ᵀ','∆G(system)/∆S(total) = 1','NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(61110,'chemistry','A piston filled with 0.04 mol of an ideal gas expands reversibly from 50.0 mL to 375 mL at a  constant temperature of 37.0°C. As it does so, it  absorbs 208 J of heat. The values of q and w for  the process will be [JEE (Main)-2013]  (R = 8.314 J/mol K) (ln 7.5 = 2.01)','q = + 208 J, w = – 208 J','For isothermal reversible expansion of an ideal gas,\n the work done is given by\n\nW(rev\n) = - 0.04 × 8.314 × 310 ln 375/50\n   = –207.22 = -208 J \nq = –W(rev) =  208J','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','q = + 208 J, w = – 208 J','q = – 208 J, w = – 208 J','q = – 208 J, w = + 208 J','q = + 208 J, w = + 208 J','NEW','11-173','11-149',NULL),
(61111,'chemistry','For complete combustion of ethanol,\n\nC₂H₅OH(l) + 3O₂(g) → 2CO₂(g) + 3H₂O(l),\nthe amount of heat produced as measured in bomb calorimeter, is 1364.47 kJ/mol at 25°C. Assuming  ideality the enthalpy of combustion, ∆꜀H, for the  reaction will be (R = 8.314 kJ/mol) [JEE (Main)-2014]','–1366.95 kJ/mol','C₂\nH₅OH(l) + 3O₂\n\n(g) → 2CO₂\n\n(g) + 3H₂\nO(l)\n\nBomb calorimeter gives ∆U of the reaction\n ,So, as per question\n\n∆U = –1364.47 kJ/mol\n∆ng = –1\n∆H = ∆U + ∆ngRT\nBy solving, –1366.93 kJ/mol','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','–1366.95 kJ/mol','–1361.95 kJ/mol','–1460.50 kJ/mol','–1350.50 kJ/mol','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(61112,'chemistry','The following reaction is performed at 298 K.\n2NO(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g)\n\nThe standard free energy of formation of NO(g) is 86.6 kJ/mol at 298 K. What is the standard free  energy of formation of NO₂(g) at 298 K?(Kp  = 1.6 × 10¹²) [JEE (Main)-2015]','0.5 [ 2×86,600 – R(298)ln1.6×10¹²]','2NO(g) + O₂\n\n(g) ⇌ 2NO₂\n\n(g)\n\n∆G°(Reaction) = [(∆G°) Formation] product -  [(∆G°) Formation] reactant\n\n = -RT ln Kₚ = 2(∆G°)NO₂\n - 2(∆G°)NO\n\n→(∆G°)NO₂\n = 2(∆G°)NO - RT lnKₚ\n\n→(∆G°)NO₂\n = 2 × 86600 - R(298)lnKₚ\n\n=2× 86600 - R×298 ×ln1.6 10¹²/2\n\n=0.5× 2 86,600 - R(298)ln1.6 10¹²','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','R(298) ln(1.6 × 10¹²) – 86600','86600 + R(298) ln(1.6 × 10¹²)','86600 - ln 1.6 × 10¹²/R(298)','0.5 [ 2×86,600 – R(298)ln1.6×10¹²]','NEW','11-184','11-160',NULL),
(61113,'chemistry','The heats of combustion of carbon and carbon monoxide are –393.5 and –283.5 kJ/mol, respectively. The heat of formation (in kJ) of carbon monoxide per mole is [JEE (Main)-2016]','-110.5','C(s) + O₂ (g) → CO₂ (g) , ∆H = – 393.5 kJ/mol\n \nCO(g) + 1/2O (g) → CO₂(g) ,   ∆H = – 283.5 kJ/mol\n-           -                     -                   +\n____________________________________________\nC(s) + 1/2O₂ (g) → CO₂ (g) , ∆H = – 110.5 kJ/mol','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','676.5','-676.5','-110.5','110.5','NEW','11-185','11-161',NULL),
(61114,'chemistry','Given\nC(graphite) + O₂(g) → CO₂(g);∆ᵣH° = –393.5 kJ/mol \n\nH₂(g) + 1/2O₂(g) → H₂O(l); ∆ᵣHº = –285.8 kJ/mol\n\nCO₂(g) + 2H₂O(l) → CH₄(g) + 2O₂(g); ∆ᵣHº = +890.3 kJ/mol\n\nBased on the above thermochemical equations, theb value of ∆ᵣHº at 298 K for the reaction\n \nC(graphite) + 2H₂(g) → CH₄(g) will be [JEE (Main)-2017]','–74.8 kJ/mol','C(graphite) + O₂\n\n(g) → CO₂\n\n(g);\n∆ᵣ\nH° = –393.5 kJ/mol.  ------(1)\n\nH₂\n\n(g) + 1/2\nO₂\n\n(g) → H₂\nO(l); ∆ᵣHº = –285.8 kJ/mol\n------------(2)\n\nCO₂\n\n(g) + 2H₂\nO(l) → CH₄\n(g) + 2O\n₂\n\n(g);\n ∆ᵣHº = +890.3 kJ/mol----(3)\n\nBy applying the operation\n(1) + 2 × (2) + (3), we get\n\nC(graphite) + 2H₂\n\n(g) → CH₄\n\n(g) \n             ∆ᵣHº = –393.5 –285.8 × 2 + 890 .3 = –74.8 kJ/mol','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'3','mcq','–74.8 kJ/mol','–144.0 kJ/mol','74.8 kJ/mol','144.0 kJ/mol','NEW','11-164','11-138',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(61115,'chemistry','∆U is equal to','Adiabatic work','For adiabatic process, q = 0\n \nAs per 1st law of thermodynamics,\n→U = W','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Adiabatic work','Isothermal work','Isochoric work','Isobaric work','NEW','11-166','11-140',NULL),
(61116,'chemistry','The combustion of benzene (l) gives CO₂(g) and  H₂O(l). Given that heat of combustion of benzene  at constant volume is –3263.9 kJ/mol at 25° C;  heat of combustion (in kJ/mol) of benzene at  constant pressure will be (R = 8.314 J/Kmol)  [JEE (Main)-2018]','-3267.6','C₆H₆ (l) +  (15/2)O₂ (g) → 6CO₂ (g) + 3H ₂O(l)\n\n∆ng = 6 -15/2 = -3/2\n        = -3263.9 + (-3/2) 8.314 × 298 × 10⁻³\n        = -3263.9 + (-3.71)\n        = –3267.6 kJ/mol','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','4152.6','-452.46','3260','-3267.6','NEW','11-166','11-140',NULL),
(61117,'chemistry','Consider the reversible isothermal expansion of an  ideal gas in a closed system at two different  temperatures T1  and T2  (T1< T2). The correct  graphical depiction of the dependence of work  done (w) on the final volume (V) is. Refer diagram(n0601)  [JEE (Main)-2019]','3','Work done in an isothermal reversible expansion is\n given by\nW = -2.303 × nRTlog(vբ/vᵢ)\nT₂ > T₁\nSo, Vi(T₂) > Vi(T₁)\nHence, slope at T₂ will be greater but intercept at T₁\n will be lesser.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-168','11-142',NULL),
(61118,'chemistry','The entropy change associated with the conversion  of 1 kg of ice at 273 K to water vapours at 383 K is  (Specific heat of water liquid and water vapour are  4.2 kJ /Kkg and 2.0 kJ /K kg; heat of liquid  fusion and vapourisation of water are 334 kJ/kg and 2491 kJ/kg, respectively). (log 273 = 2.436, log 373 = 2.572, log 383 = 2.583)  [JEE (Main)-2019]','9.26 kJ / kg K','∆S(fus) = ∆H(fus)/ 273  = 334/273 = 1.22\n\n∆S(Vap) = ∆H(vap)/373 = 2491/373 = 6.67\n\n∆S(water) = mCdT/T = mCIn(T2/T1)\n\n               = 4.2×ln(383/372) = 0.05\nTotal entropy change ,∆S = 9.26 kJ / kg K','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','8.49 kJ/kgK','7.90 kJ / kg K','9.26 kJ / kg K','2.64 kJ/ kgK','NEW','11-178','11-154',NULL),
(61119,'chemistry','A process had ∆H = 200 J/moland ∆S = 40 J/K mol. Out of the values given below,  choose the minimum temperature above which the  process will be spontaneous. [JEE (Main)-2019]','5 K','∆H = 200 J/mol\n∆S = 40 J/K mol\nFor spontaneous reaction,\n\nT ≥ ∆H/∆S\nT ≥ 200/40 ≥ 5k\nSo, minimum temperature is 5 K','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','4 k','12 K','5 K','20 K','NEW','11-173','11-149',NULL),
(61120,'chemistry','An ideal gas undergoes isothermal compression  from 5 m³ to 1 m³  against a constant external  pressure of 4 N/m². Heat released in this process  is used to increase the temperature of 1 mole of  Al. If molar heat capacity of Al is 24 J /molK , the  temperature of Al increases by [JEE (Main)-2019]','2/3 K','w = –Pext(Vf\n – Vi\n)\n   = –4 N/m²(1 – 5)m³\n   = 16 Nm → 16 J\nFor isothermal process\n∆U = q + w → q = –w = –16 J\n(∆U = 0 for isothermal process)\nFrom calorimetry\nHeat given = nC∆T \n16 = 1 × 24 × ∆T / molK\n∆T = 2/3','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'3','mcq','3/2K','1 K','2 K','2/3 K','NEW','11-172','11-148',NULL),
(61121,'chemistry','For the chemical reaction X ⇌ Y, the standard  reaction Gibbs energy depends on temperature T (in  K) as  3 ∆ᵣG° (in kJ/mol ) = 120 – (3/8)T .The major component of the reaction mixture at T is  [JEE (Main)-2019]','X if T = 315 K','If ∆G° is positive then Keq < 1.\nSo, [y]/[x] <1\n\nIf ∆G° is negative then Keq > 1\nSo, [y]/[x] > 1\n\n∆G° = 120-3T/8\nAt 315 K,∆G° = 120 – 118.125 = positive\nso, [X] > [Y]','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','Y if T = 280 K','X if T = 315 K','Y if T = 300 K','X if T = 350 K','NEW','11-173','11-149',NULL),
(61122,'chemistry','Two blocks of the same metal having same mass  and at temperature T1  and T2  , respectively, are  brought in contact with each other and allowed to  attain thermal equilibrium at constant pressure. The  change in entropy, ∆ᵣS, for this process is  [JEE (Main)-2019]','2Cₚ ln ( T1 + T2/4T1T2)','Final temperature = \nT1+ T2/2 , let T2 > T1\n\ndS = dq/T = Cₚln(Tf/Ti)\n\n∆S = Cₚln ( T1 + T2/2T1) + Cₚ ln ( T1 + T2/2T2)\n\n      = 2Cₚ ln ( T1 + T2/4T1T2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','2Cₚ ln ( T1 + T2/4T1T2)','Cₚ ln ( (T1 + T2)²/4T1T2)','2Cₚ ln ( T1 + T2/2T1T2)','2Cₚ ln ( (T1 + T2)½/4T1T2)','NEW','11-169','11-143',NULL),
(61123,'chemistry','The reaction\n\nMgO(s) + C(s) → Mg(s) + CO(g), for which  ∆ᵣH° = +491.1 kJ/mol and ∆ᵣS° = 198.0 J/Kmol , is not feasible at 298 K. Temperature above which  reaction will be feasible is [JEE (Main)-2019]','2480.3 K','MgO(s) + C(s) → Mg(s) + CO(g)\nFor reaction to be spontaneous\n\n∆ᵣ\nH° - T∆ᵣ\nS° < 0\nT > ∆ᵣ\nH°/∆ᵣ\nS°\nT > 491.1 ×1000\n/198\nT > 2480.3 K','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','2040.5 K','1890.0 K','2480.3 K','2380.5 K','NEW','11-170','11-144',NULL),
(61124,'chemistry','The standard reaction Gibbs energy for a chemical  reaction at an absolute temperature T is given by  ∆ᵣG° = A – BT  .Where A and B are non-zero constants. Which of  the following is true about this reaction?  [JEE (Main)-2019]','Endothermic if A > 0','∆ᵣG° = A – BT\nA and B are non-zero constants\n∆G° = ∆H° – T∆ᵣ\nS° = A – BT\n→ Reaction will be endothermic if A > 0.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','Exothermic if B < 0','Endothermic if A > 0','Endothermic if A < 0 and B > 0','Exothermic if A > 0 and B < 0','NEW','11-169','11-143',NULL),
(61125,'chemistry','For a diatomic ideal gas in a closed system, which of the following plots does not correctly describe  the relation between various thermodynamic  quantities?Refer diagram(n0602) [JEE (Main)-2019]','2','Cp and Cv for ideal gases are dependant on\ntemperature only. So, Cp will not change with\npressure.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(61126,'chemistry','Given\n(i) C(graphite) + O₂(g) → CO₂(g);∆ᵣH° = xkJ/mol\n\n(ii) C(graphite) + 1/2O₂(g) → CO₂(g);∆ᵣH° = ykJ/mol\n\n(iii) CO(g) +(1/2) O₂ (g) → CO₂ (g); ∆ᵣH° = zKJ/mol\n\nBased on the above thermochemical equations, find out which one of the following algebraic relationships is correct? [JEE (Main)-2019]','x = y + z','According to Hess’s law, the enthalpy change of a\n reaction does not depend on the number of steps\n involved in the reaction\n\nC(graphite) + 1/2O₂\n\n(g) → CO₂\n\n(g);\n∆ᵣ\nH° =\n ykJ/mol\n\nCO(g) +(1/2) O₂\n\n (g) → CO₂\n\n (g); ∆ᵣ\nH° =\n zKJ/mol\n_____________________________________________\nC(graphite) + O₂\n\n(g) → CO₂\n\n(g);\n∆ᵣ\nH° =\n xkJ/mol\n\n∆H°₃ = ∆H°₁ + ∆H°₂\nx = y + z\n** in reaction ii, Product should be CO (gas) instead\nof CO2 (gas).','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','x = y – z','x = y + z','y = 2z – x','z = x + y','NEW','11-171','11-145',NULL),
(61127,'chemistry','The combination of plots which does not represent  isothermal expansion of an ideal gas is . Refer diagram(n0603)','(B) and (D)','(B) and (D) are not correct representation for\nisothermal expansion of ideal gas.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','(A) and (C)','(A) and (D)','(B) and (C)','(B) and (D)','NEW','11-164','11-138','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/61127q.png'),
(61128,'chemistry','Which one of the following equations does not  correctly represent the first law of thermodynamics  for the given processes involving an ideal gas?  (Assume non-expansion work is zero)  [JEE (Main)-2019]','Adiabatic process : ΔU = –w','∆U = q + W\nAdiabatic process q = 0\n∆U = W\nFor isothermal,∆U = 0\nFor cyclic, ∆U = 0\nFor isochoric, W = 0','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','Isothermal process : q = –w','Cyclic process : q = –w','Isochoric process : ΔU = q','Adiabatic process : ΔU = –w','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(61129,'chemistry','For silver, Cp (J/Kmol) = 23 + 0.01T. If the  temperature (T) of 3 moles of silver is raised from  300 K to 1000 K at 1 atm pressure, the value of  ∆H will be close to [JEE (Main)-2019]','62 kJ','Refer diagram(ne0601)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','21 kJ','13 kJ','62 kJ','16 kJ','NEW','11-162','11-138',NULL),
(61130,'chemistry','5 moles of an ideal gas at 100 K are allowed to  undergo reversible compression till its temperature  becomes 200 K. If Cᵥ  = 28 J/Kmol, calculate  ∆U and ∆pV for this process. (R = 8.0 J/Kmol) [JEE (Main)-2019]','ΔU = 14 kJ; Δ(pV) = 4 kJ','∆U = nC∆T = 5 × 28 × 100 = 14kJ\n∆PV = nR (T2- T1) INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(121102,'chemistry','Most of the _______ compounds have sharp melting points and boiling points.','Pure','Most of the pure compounds have sharp melting points and boiling points. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 357; Paragraph No. 1 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','Impure','Pure','Several','Chemical','NEW','11-357','11-279',NULL),
(121103,'chemistry','Crystallisation is based on the ____ in the solubilities of the compound and the impurities in a suitable solvent.','Difference','Crystallisation is based on the difference in the solubilities of the compound and the impurities in a suitable solvent.Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 357; Paragraph No. 2 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'1','fillup','Diff,ere,nce,Simi,lar,it,ies',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-357','11-279',NULL),
(121104,'chemistry','Repeated crystallisation becomes necessary for the purification of compounds containing _____ of comparable solubilities.','Impurities','Repeated crystallisation becomes necessary for the purification of compounds containing impurities of comparable solubilities. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 357; Paragraph No. 2 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Impurities','Crystals','Precipitates','Mixture','NEW','11-357','11-279',NULL),
(121105,'chemistry','Which method is used to separate volatile liquids from nonvolatile impurities and the liquids having sufficient difference in their boiling points?','Distillation','Distillation important method is used to separate volatile liquids from nonvolatile impurities and the liquids having sufficient difference in their boiling points.If the difference in boiling points of two liquids is not much, simple distillation cannot be used to separate them and therefore fractional distillation is used. In fractional distillation vapours of the liquid with higher boiling point condense before the vapours of the liquid with lower boiling point. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 357; Paragraph No. 5 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','Distillation','Chromatography','Sublimation','Crystallisation','NEW','11-357','11-279',NULL),
(121106,'chemistry','Each successive condensation and vaporisation unit in the fractionating column is called a _____ plate.','Theoretical','Each successive condensation and vaporisation unit in the fractionating column is called a theoretical plate. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 358; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Hypothetical','Theoretical','Practical','Scientific','NEW','11-358','11-280',NULL),
(121107,'chemistry','Which of the following methods is mainly used to separate fractions of crude oil in petroleum industry ?','Fractional distillation','One of the technological applications of fractional distillation is to separate different fractions of crude oil in petroleum industry. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 358; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Chromatography','Fractional distillation','Sublimation','Crystallisation','NEW','11-358','11-280',NULL),
(121108,'chemistry','Which method is used to purify liquids having very high boiling points and those, which decompose at or below their boiling points ?','Distillation under reduced pressure','This method is used to purify liquids having very high boiling points and those, which decompose at or below their boiling points. Such liquids are made to boil at a temperature lower than their normal boiling points by reducing the pressure on their surface. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 358; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Chromatography','Distillation under reduced pressure','Fractional distillation','Crystallisation','NEW','11-358','11-280',NULL),
(121201,'chemistry','Carbon and hydrogen are detected by heating the compound with ____ oxide.','Copper','Detection of Carbon and Hydrogen Carbon and hydrogen are detected by heating the compound with copper(II) oxide. Carbon present in the compound is oxidised to carbon dioxide (tested with lime-water, which develops turbidity) and hydrogen to water (tested with anhydrous copper sulphate, which turns blue. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 362; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Aluminum','Copper','Ferric','Silicon','NEW','11-362','11-284',NULL),
(121202,'chemistry','____ test is used to detect Nitrogen, sulphur, halogens and phosphorus present in an organic compound.','Lassaigne','Nitrogen, sulphur, halogens and phosphorus present in an organic compound are detected by “Lassaigne’s test”. The elements present in the compound are converted from covalent form into the ionic form by fusing the compound with sodium metal. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 362; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Grignard','Lassaigne','Dumas','Culture','NEW','11-362','11-284',NULL),
(121203,'chemistry','Which of the following gives prussian blue color in lassaigne test ?','Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃','In lassaigne test, iron(III) hexacyanidoferrate(II) (ferriferrocyanide) gives Prussian blue colour. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 363; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Fe(CN)₃','Na₃[Fe(CN)₆]','Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃','Fe₄[Fe(CN)₃]₆','NEW','11-363','11-285',NULL),
(121204,'chemistry','Which of the following are present if the acidified lassaigne solution gives blood red color on addition of FeCl₃?','S and N','In case, nitrogen and sulphur both are present in an organic compound, sodium thiocyanate is formed. It gives blood red colour and no Prussian blue since there are no free cyanide ions. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 363; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','N','S','S and N','S and Cl','NEW','11-363','11-285',NULL),
(121205,'chemistry','If the sodium fusion extract is acidified with nitric acid and then treated with silver nitrate, a ____ precipitate, sparingly soluble in ammonium hydroxide shows the presence of bromine.','Yellowish','The sodium fusion extract is acidified with nitric acid and then treated with silver nitrate. A white precipitate, soluble in ammonium hydroxide shows the presence of chlorine, a yellowish precipitate, sparingly soluble in ammonium hydroxide shows the presence of bromine. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 363; Paragraph No. 5)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Yellowish','White','Reddish','Blue','NEW','11-363','11-285',NULL),
(121206,'chemistry','If the sodium fusion extract is acidified with nitric acid and then treated with silver nitrate, a ____ precipitate, soluble in ammonium hydroxide shows the presence of chlorine.','White','The sodium fusion extract is acidified with nitric acid and then treated with silver nitrate. A white precipitate, soluble in ammonium hydroxide shows the presence of chlorine, a yellowish precipitate, sparingly soluble in ammonium hydroxide shows the presence of bromine. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 363; Paragraph No. 5)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Yellowish','White','Reddish','Blue','NEW','11-363','11-285',NULL),
(121207,'chemistry','If the sodium fusion extract is acidified with nitric acid and then treated with silver nitrate, a yellow precipitate ___ in ammonium hydroxide shows the presence of iodine.','Insoluble','The sodium fusion extract is acidified with nitric acid and then treated with silver nitrate. A white precipitate, soluble in ammonium hydroxide shows the presence of chlorine, a yellowish precipitate, sparingly soluble in ammonium hydroxide shows the presence of bromine and a yellow precipitate, insoluble in ammonium hydroxide shows the presence of iodine. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 363; Paragraph No. 5)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'1','fillup','in,so,lu,ble,sol,ub,le',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-363','11-285',NULL),
(121208,'chemistry','If a compound is heated with an oxidising agent and then boiled with nitric acid and then treated with ammonium molybdate. A yellow colouration or precipitate indicates the presence of:','Phosphorus','The compound is heated with an oxidising agent (sodium peroxide). The phosphorus present in the compound is oxidised to phosphate. The solution is boiled with nitric acid and then treated with ammonium molybdate. A yellow colouration or precipitate indicates the presence of phosphorus. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 363; Paragraph No. 7)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Phosphorus','Potassium','Nitrogen','Chlorine','NEW','11-363','11-285',NULL),
(121209,'chemistry','Which of the following wil not give a white ppt. when AgNO₃ is added to its solution ?','CCl₄','CCl₄ being covalent wouldn\'t furnish ions to form ppt. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 363; Paragraph No. 5)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','MgCl₂','CCl₄','KCl','NaCl','NEW','11-363','11-285',NULL),
(121210,'chemistry','On complete combustion, 0.246g of an organic compound gave 0.198g of carbon dioxide and 0.1014g of water. What is the percentage composition of carbon and hydrogen respectively in the compound ?','21.95% and 4.58%','Percentage of carbon = 12 x 0.198 x100/44 x 0.246 = 21.95% \n Percentage of hydrogen = 2 x 0.1014 x100/18 x 0.246 = 4.58%\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 364; Problem No. 12.20)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','21.95% and 4.58%','4.58% and 21.95%','27.95% and 4.58%','none of these','NEW','11-364','11-286',NULL),
(121301,'chemistry','The number of sigma (s) and pi (p) bonds in pent-2-en-4-yne is:','10 s bonds and 3 p bonds','From the structure of HC≣C-CH=CH-CH₃ it has 10 s bonds and 3 p bonds. (NEET 2019)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','13 s bonds and no p bond','10 s bonds and 3 p bonds','8 s bonds and 5 p bonds',') 11 s bonds and 2 p bonds','NEW','11-335','11-257',NULL),
(121302,'chemistry','Which of the following molecules represents the order of hybridisation sp², sp², sp, sp from left to right atoms?','CH₂=CH-C≣CH','A carbon having 4 bonds but with 3 atoms only is sp² hybrid and carbon with 4 bonds but with 2 atoms only is sp hybrid (from the concept of hybridization). (NEET 2018)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','HC≣C-C≣CH','CH₂=CH-C≣CH','CH₂=CH-CH=CH₂','CH₃-CH=CH-CH₃','NEW','11-335','11-257',NULL),
(121303,'chemistry','What are the total number of π-bond electrons in the structure in the given diagram(12110)','8','There are four double bonds. Hence, no. of π-electrons = 2 × 4 = 8. (NEET 2015)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','12','16','8','4','NEW','11-335','11-257','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121303q.png'),
(121304,'chemistry','What is the sequence of state of hybridisation of C₂, C₃, C₅ and C₆ of the hydrocarbon in the given diagram(12111)','sp, sp³ , sp² and sp³','A carbon having 4 bonds with 4 atoms is sp³ hybrid, carbon having 4 bonds but with 3 atoms only is sp² hybrid and carbon with 4 bonds but with 2 atoms only is sp hybrid (from the concept of hybridization). (NEET 2009)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','sp³, sp², sp² and sp','sp, sp² , sp² and sp³','sp, sp³ , sp³ and sp²','sp, sp³ , sp² and sp³','NEW','11-335','11-257','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121304q.png'),
(121305,'chemistry','What is the sequence of state of hybridisation of Carbon 1,3 and 5 of the hydrocarbon in the given diagram(12112)','sp, sp³ and sp²','A carbon having 4 bonds with 4 atoms is sp³ hybrid, carbon having 4 bonds but with 3 atoms only is sp² hybrid and carbon with 4 bonds but with 2 atoms only is sp hybrid (from the concept of hybridization). (NEET 2008)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','sp,sp² and sp³','sp³, sp², sp','sp², sp and sp³','sp, sp³ and sp²','NEW','11-335','11-257','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121305q.png'),
(121306,'chemistry','In which of the following compounds there is more than one kind of hybridisation carbon?','CH₃-CH=CH-CH₃','Only in this option carbon has different hybridization diagram (e12113)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CH₂=CH-CH=CH₂','H-C≣C-H','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃','CH₃-CH=CH-CH₃','NEW','11-335','11-257',NULL),
(121307,'chemistry','Which of the following possesses a sp-carbon in its structure?','CH₂=C=CH₂','Carbon with 4 bonds but with 2 atoms only is sp hybrid (from the concept of hybridization). (NEET 1989)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','CH₂=CCl-CH=CH₂','CCl₂=CCl₂','CH₂=C=CH₂','CH₂=CH-CH=CH₂','NEW','11-335','11-257',NULL),
(121308,'chemistry','What is the Cl–C–Cl angle in 1, 1, 2, 2-tetrachloro-ethene and tetrachloromethane respectively ?','120° and 109.5°','Tetrachloroethene being an alkene has sp² -hybridised C-atoms and hence the angle Cl – C – Cl is 120° while in tetrachloromethane, carbon is sp³ hybridised, therefore the angle Cl – C – Cl is 109°28′. (NEET 1988)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','120° and 109.5°','90° and 109.5°','109.5° and 90°','109.5° and 120°','NEW','11-335','11-257',NULL),
(121309,'chemistry','An organic compound X (molecular formula (C₆H₇O₂N) has six carbon atoms in a ring system, two double bonds and a nitro group as substituent, X is:','Homocyclic but not aromatic','Hence, it is homocyclic (as the ring system is made of one type of atoms, i.e., carbon) but not aromatic. (NEET 1990)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Homocyclic but not aromatic','Aromatic but not homocyclic','Homocyclic and aromatic','Heterocyclic and aromatic.','NEW','11-339','11-261',NULL),
(121503,'chemistry','Following types of compounds are studied in terms of isomerism in: \nI. CH₃CH=CHCH₃\n\nII. CH₃-CH(C₂H₅)(OH)','stereoisomerism','The types of isomerism studied for the given compounds are:\n\nI. CH₃CH=CHCH₃\n\nGeometrical isomerism (also called cis-trans isomerism) is a sterioisomer due to the presence of a carbon-carbon double bond with two different substituents attached to each carbon atom.\nII. CH₃-CH(C₂H₅)(OH)\n\nChain isomerism due to the presence of a branched chain.\nPosition isomerism due to the different positions of the -OH group on the carbon chain.\nStereoisomerism due to the presence of a chiral center (the carbon atom attached to -OH group), which can give rise to enantiomers and diastereomers.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','chain isomerism','position isomerism','conformers','stereoisomerism','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(121504,'chemistry','Following reactions is an example of :\n\n(CH₃)₃CBr + H₂O → (CH₃)₃COH + HBr','nucleophillic substitution','The given reaction is an example of nucleophilic substitution reaction.\n\nIn this reaction, the nucleophile (water molecule) attacks the electrophilic carbon atom in the carbon-bromine bond of the tert-butyl bromide ((CH₃)₃CBr) molecule, leading to the formation of an intermediate species (an alcohol) and the expulsion of a leaving group (hydrogen bromide, HBr). The intermediate then gains a proton from the solvent (water) to form the final product, tert-butyl alcohol ((CH₃)₃COH). [AIEEE 2002]','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','elimination reaction','free radical substitution','nucleophillic substitution','electrophillic subsitution','NEW','11-356','11-278',NULL),
(121505,'chemistry','Underlined carbon is sp3 hybridised in : Refer diagram(12152)','b','A carbon having 4 bonds but with 3 atoms only is sp² hybrid and carbon with 4 bonds but with 2 atoms only is sp hybrid (from the concept of hybridization). [AIEEE 2002]','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-335','11-257','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121505q.png'),
(121506,'chemistry','In the anion, HCOO⁻ the two carbon-oxygen bonds are formed to be of equal length. What is the reason for it?','The anion HCOO⁻ has two resonating structures','The reason for the two carbon-oxygen bonds in HCOO⁻ (formate ion) being of equal length is due to resonance.\n\nIn the formate ion, the negative charge is delocalized over both oxygen atoms and the carbon atom. This is because the formate ion can exist in two resonance structures, where the negative charge is located on each of the two oxygen atoms in turn.\n\nThe delocalization of the negative charge through resonance means that the carbon-oxygen bonds have partial double bond character. The double bond character results in the two carbon-oxygen bonds being of equal length and having an intermediate length between a typical single bond and a typical double bond.\n\nTherefore, the reason for the two carbon-oxygen bonds in HCOO⁻ being of equal length is due to resonance, which delocalizes the negative charge and results in partial double bond character.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','Electronic orbits of carbon atom are hybridised','The C=O bond is weaker than C-O bond','The anion HCOO⁻ has two resonating structures','The anion is obtained by removal of a proton from the acid molecule','NEW','11-353','11-275',NULL),
(121507,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is true for the given structures: Refer diagram(12153)','only I and II are chiral compounds','A molecule is chiral if it is not superimposable on its mirror image. This means that the molecule must have an asymmetric carbon atom (also called a chiral center) or a plane of symmetry.\n\nIn structure I and II, each have an asymmetric carbon atom , which means they are chiral compounds.\n\nIn contrast, structure III has a plane of symmetry, which means it is not chiral. Structure IV has two identical substituents on the asymmetric carbon atom, making it an achiral meso compound, which is not chiral.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','all four are chiral compounds','only I and II are chiral compounds','only III is a chiral compound','only II and IV are chiral compounds','NEW','11-348','11-270','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121507q.png'),
(121509,'chemistry','Which of the following will have a meso isomer also?','2,3 dichlorobutane','The compound that will have a meso isomer is 2,3-dichlorobutane.\n\nA meso compound is a molecule with two or more identical chiral centers that also contains an internal plane of symmetry, which makes the molecule achiral as a whole.\n[AIEEE 2004]','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','2-chlorobutane','2,3 dichlorobutane','2,3-dichloropentane','2-hydroxypropanoic acid','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(121510,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds is not chiral?','1-chloropentane','To be chiral, a molecule must have a non-superimposable mirror image, which requires the presence of at least one chiral center. A chiral center is an atom, typically carbon, that is bonded to four different substituents.\n\nIn 2-chloropentane, the chlorine atom is attached to the second carbon, which also has two other identical substituents (two methyl groups) and one ethyl group. This carbon is not a chiral center because the two methyl groups are identical, making the molecule symmetrical, and its mirror image would be superimposable on itself. Therefore, 2-chloropentane is not chiral. \n[AIEEE 2004]','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','1-chloropentane','2-chloropentane','1-chloro-2-methyl pentane','3-chloro-2-methyl pentane','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(121511,'chemistry','Which one of the following does not have sp2 hybridised carbon?','Acetonitrile','Acetonitrile does not have a sp2 hybridized carbon atom.\n\nAcetonitrile has a linear structure with a carbon triple-bonded to a nitrogen atom (C≡N). Both carbon and nitrogen atoms are sp hybridized, which is necessary to form the triple bond.\n\nAcetone, acetic acid, and acetamide all have sp2 hybridized carbon atoms in their structures, but not acetonitrile. \n[AIEEE 2004]','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','Acetone','Acetic acid','Acetonitrile','Acetamide','NEW','11-335','11-257',NULL),
(121512,'chemistry','Which type of isomerism is shown by 2,3-dichlorobutane? [AIEEE 2005]','Optical','2,3-dichlorobutane has two chiral centers, and therefore, it can exist in four different stereoisomers. Among these stereoisomers, two are enantiomeric pairs that are non-superimposable mirror images of each other. Hence, 2,3-dichlorobutane shows optical isomerism.\n\nOptical isomers or enantiomers have identical physical and chemical properties except for their ability to rotate plane-polarized light in opposite directions. The direction of rotation depends on the configuration of the chiral centers in the molecule.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','Structural','Geometric','Optical','Diasterisomers','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(121513,'chemistry','3-Methylpent-2-ene on reaction with HBr in the presence of peroxide forms an additional product. The number of possible stereoisomers for the product is','Four','Since the product includes two chiral centres it is assymetrical.Hence the total stereoisomers 2^2=4','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','six','three','two','four','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(121514,'chemistry','The compound formed in the positive test for nitrogen with the Lassaigne solution of an organic compound is','Fe4[Fe(CN)6]3','The compound formed in the positive test for nitrogen with the Lassaigne solution of an organic compound is prussian blue coloured complex compound ferric ferrocyanide.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','Fe4[Fe(CN)6]3','Na4[Fe(CN)5NOS]','Fe(CN)3','Na3[Fe(CN)6]','NEW','11-362','11-284',NULL),
(121515,'chemistry','Which types of isomerism is shown by 2,3-dichlorobutane?','Optical','In 2, 3 dichlorobutane has 2 chiral centres.\n\nThe 2nd and 3rd carbons are chiral. They are not superimposable. So it shows optical isomerism.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','diastereo','optical','geometrical','structural','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(121516,'chemistry','The number and type of bonds between two carbon atoms in calcium carbide are','one sigma, two pi','The number and type of bonds between two carbon atoms in calcium carbide are one sigma bond and two pi bonds.\n\nCalcium carbide has the chemical formula CaC2, and it is made up of one calcium atom and two carbon atoms. The two carbon atoms are bonded to each other by a triple bond, which consists of one sigma bond and two pi bonds. Refer diagram(e1216)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','two sigma, two pi','two sigma, one pi','one sigma, two pi','one sigma, one pi','NEW','11-335','11-257',NULL),
(121517,'chemistry','The alkene that exhibits geometrical isomerism is: [AIEEE 2009]','2-butene','The compounds which have the same molecular formula but differ in the relative spatial arrangement of atoms or groups in space are known as geometrical isomers. This phenomenon is called geometrical isomerism. 2-butene exists as cis or trans. It exhibits geometric isomerism because of restricted rotation around the double bond.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','2-butene','2-methyl-2-butene','propene','2-methylpropene','NEW','11-348','11-271',NULL),
(121518,'chemistry','Increasing order of stability among the eclipse, anti, gauche of 2-fluoroethanol is :[AIEEE 2006]','Eclipse,Anti, Gauche','Gauche is most stable because of H- bonding between F and OH group. Refer diagram(e1217) .','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','Ecipse,Gauche, Anti','Gauche,Eclipse,Anti','Eclipse,Anti, Gauche','Anti, Gauche, Eclipse','NEW','11-348','11-271',NULL),
(121519,'chemistry','The IUPAC name of the compound shown below is: Refer diagram(12155)','3-bromo-1-chlorocyclohexene','In this case, the functional group is the double bond between carbons 1 and 2 of the cyclohexene ring.\n\nSince the compound contains both bromine and chlorine atoms, these substituents must be indicated in the name as prefixes. In alphabetical order, bromine comes before chlorine, so "bromo" is written before "chloro" in the name. The bromine atom is attached to carbon 3 of the cyclohexene ring, so the prefix "3-bromo" is used. Similarly, the chlorine atom is attached to carbon 1 of the cyclohexene ring, so the prefix "1-chloro" is used.\n\nFinally, since the double bond in the cyclohexene ring is between carbons 1 and 2, the stereochemistry of the double bond must be indicated. The compound is named as (E)-3-bromo-1-chlorocyclohexene to indicate that the two highest-priority substituents on opposite sides of the double bond are on the same side of the ring','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','2-bromo-6 chlorocyclohexene','6-bromo 2-chlorocyclohexene','3-bromo-1-chlorocyclohexene','1-bromo-3-chlorocyclohexene','NEW','11-341','11-263','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121519q.png'),
(121521,'chemistry','The correct decreasing order of priority for the functional groups of organic compounds in the IUPAC system of nomenclature is','-COOH, -SO3H, -CONH2, -CHO','The correct decreasing order of priority for the functional groups of organic compounds in the IUPAC system of nomenclature is:\n\n-COOH, -SO3H, -CONH2, -CHO.\n\nThis is because carboxylic acids (-COOH) have the highest priority, followed by sulfonic acids (-SO3H), amides (-CONH2), and aldehydes (-CHO). [AIEEE 2008]','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'3','mcq','-COOH, -SO3H, -CONH2, -CHO','-SO3H, -COOH, -CONH2, -CHO','-CHO, -COOH, -SO3H, -CONH2','-CONH2, -CHO, -SO3H, -COOH','NEW','11-341','11-263',NULL),
(121522,'chemistry','The IUPAC name of neopentane is [AIEEE 2008]','2,2-dimethylpropane','The IUPAC name for neopentane is 2,2-dimethylpropane. It has a branched structure, with four carbon atoms arranged in a "T" shape, and each of the three terminal carbon atoms is bonded to a single hydrogen atom. The central carbon atom is bonded to two methyl groups (CH3). Refer diagram(e1219)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','2-methylbutane','2,2-dimethylpropane','2-methylpropane','2,2-dimethylbutane','NEW','11-341','11-263',NULL),
(121601,'chemistry','Assertion: Organic compounds are essential for sustaining life on earth.\n\nReason: They include complex molecules like DNA and proteins that are necessary for the formation of essential compounds in our body.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Organic compounds are vital for sustaining life on earth and include complex molecules like genetic information bearing deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) and proteins that constitute essential compounds of our blood, muscles and skin.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Assertion- Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-334','11-256',NULL),
(121602,'chemistry','Assertion: The notion of a \'vital force\' being responsible for the formation of organic compounds was rejected in the 19th century.\n\nReason: F. Wohler\'s synthesised  an inorganic compound from an organic compound.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Berzilius, a Swedish chemist\nproposed that a ‘vital force’ was responsible for the\nformation of organic compounds. However, this notion was rejected in 1828 when F. Wohler synthesised an organic compound, urea from an inorganic compound, ammonium cyanate. \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 334; Paragraph No.3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Assertion- Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-334','11-256',NULL),
(71513,'chemistry','What will be the value of pH of 0.01 mol /dm³ CH₃COOH (Kₐ = 1.74 x 10⁻⁵)?','3.4','We know that the ionization of CH₃COOH occurs as follows:-\n\n\nCH₃COOH + H₂O ⇌ H₃O⁺ + CH₃COO⁻\nNow , we know that the initial concentration : 0.01\n\nWe also know that the equilibrium concentration: 0.01-x \n        Kₐ = [H₃O⁺][CH₃COO⁻]/[CH₃COOH]\n             = x²/0.01-x\nNow the ionisation constant \nAs , x >> 0.01. Therefore , 0.01 -x ≈ 0.01\nX² = 1.74 × 10⁻⁵ × 0.01\nX = 4.2 × 10⁻⁴\nWhich can also written as , pH = -log(4.2 × 10⁻⁴) = 3.4','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','3.4','3.6','3.9','3','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(121603,'chemistry','Assertion: In a CH2=CH2 molecule, all the atoms must be in the different plane.\n\nReason: The p orbitals are mutually parallel to each other','Both Assertion and reason are false','In CH₂=CH₂ molecule all the atoms must be in the same plane. The p orbitals are mutually parallel and both the p orbitals are perpendicular to the plane of the molecule. Rotation of one CH2 fragment with respect to other interferes with maximum overlap of p orbitals and, therefore, such rotation about carbon-carbon double bond (C=C) is restricted. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 335; Paragraph No.3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Assertion- Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-335','11-257',NULL),
(121604,'chemistry','Assertion: Rotation of one CH2 fragment with respect to the other is restricted in a molecule with a carbon-carbon double bond (C=C).\n\nReason: Such rotation interferes with the maximum overlap of p orbitals','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','In CH₂=CH₂ molecule all the atoms must be in the same plane. The p orbitals are mutually parallel and both the p orbitals are perpendicular to the plane of the molecule. Rotation of one CH2 fragment with respect to other interferes with maximum overlap of p orbitals and, therefore, such rotation about carbon-carbon double bond (C=C) is restricted. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 335; Paragraph No.3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Assertion- Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-335','11-257',NULL),
(121605,'chemistry','Assertion:  Distribution of electron charge in π bond makes the electrons easily available to attacking reagents, resulting in highly reactive centres in the molecule.\n\nReason: The electron charge cloud of the π bond is located above and below the plane of bonding atoms in a molecule with a double bond.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','In a pi bond formation, parallel orientation of the two p orbitals on adjacent atoms is necessary for a proper sideways overlap. The electron charge cloud of the pi bond is located above and below the plane of bonding atoms. This distribution of electron charge makes the electrons easily available to attacking reagents, resulting in highly reactive centres in the molecule.Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 335; Paragraph No.3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Assertion- Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-335','11-257',NULL),
(121606,'chemistry','Assertion:  (π) bonds provide the most reactive centres in molecules containing multiple bonds.\n\nReason: The electron charge cloud of the π bond is highly reactive due to its availability to attacking reagents.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','In a pi bond formation, parallel orientation of the two p orbitals on adjacent atoms is necessary for a proper sideways overlap. The electron charge cloud of the pi bond is located above and below the plane of bonding atoms. This distribution of electron charge makes the electrons easily available to attacking reagents, resulting in highly reactive centres in the molecule.Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 335; Paragraph No.3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Assertion- Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-335','11-257',NULL),
(121607,'chemistry','Assertion: A solid wedge in a molecule\'s 3-D representation is used to indicate a bond projecting towards the observer.\n\nReason: A dashed wedge is used to depict a bond projecting out of the plane of the paper and away from the observer.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The three-dimensional (3-D) structure of organic molecules can be represented on paper by using certain conventions.For example, by using solid and dashed wedge formula, the 3-D image of a molecule from a two-dimensional picture can be perceived. In these formulas the solid-wedge is used to indicate a bond projecting out of the plane of paper, towards the observer. The dashed-wedge is used to depict the bond projecting out of the plane of the paper and away from the observer. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 338; Paragraph No.1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Assertion- Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-338','11-260',NULL),
(121610,'chemistry','Assertion: The ball-and-stick model represents both atoms and bonds in a molecule.\n\nReason: In this model, balls represent single bonds and sticks represent double bond','Assertion is true but reason is false','In the ball-and-stick model, both the atoms and the bonds are shown. Balls represent atoms and the stick denotes a bond. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 338; Paragraph No.1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Assertion- Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false and reason is true','NEW','11-338','11-260',NULL),
(121611,'chemistry','Assertion: The space-filling model emphasizes the relative size of each atom based on its van der Waals radius and does not show bonds. \n\nReason: This model conveys the volume occupied by each atom in the molecule.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','The space-filling model emphasises the relative size of each atom based on its van der Waals radius. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 338; Paragraph No.1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Assertion- Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false and reason is true','NEW','11-338','11-260',NULL),
(121612,'chemistry','Assertion: A covalent bond can get cleaved either by heterolytic cleavage, or by homolytic cleavage.\n\nReason: In heterolytic cleavage, the bond breaks in such a fashion that the shared pair of electrons remains with one of the fragments.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','In an organic reaction a covalent bond between two carbon atoms or a carbon and some other atom is broken and a new bond is formed.\nIn heterolytic cleavage, the bond breaks in such a fashion that the shared pair of electrons remains with one of the fragments. After heterolysis, one atom has a sextet electronic structure and a positive charge and the other, a valence octet with at least one lone pair and a negative charge.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 4 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Assertion- Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false and reason is true','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(121613,'biology','During biological nitrogen fixation, inactivation of nitrogenase by O₂ poisoning is prevented by [AIPMT 2015]','leghaemoglobin','Leghaemoglobin is a pinkish colour pigment present in the root nodules of leguminous plants. It acts as O₂ scavenger and prevents the inactivation of nitrogenase enzyme by O₂ poisoning.','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','carotene','cytochrome','leghaemoglobin','xanthophyll','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121614,'biology','The first stable product of fixation of atmospheric nitrogen in leguminous plants is [NEET 2013]','ammonia','The nodule of leguminous plants contains all the necessary biochemical components, such as the enzyme nitrogenase and leghaemoglobin, for nitrogen fixation. The enzyme nitrogenase is a Mo-Fe protein and catalyses the conversion of atmospheric nitrogen to ammonia, the first stable product of nitrogen fixation','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','NO₃⁻','glutamate','NO₂⁻','ammonia','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121615,'biology','Which two distinct microbial processes are responsible for the release of fixed nitrogen as dinitrogen gas (N₂) to the atmosphere  [Karnataka NEET 2013]','Anaerobic ammonium oxidation and denitrification','Denitrification is a chemical process in which nitrates in the soil are reduced to molecular nitrogen (N2) which is released into the atmosphere. It is done by denitrifying bacteria like Pseudomonas denitrificans. Anaerobic oxidation of ammonium (NH4) also releases nitrogen in the atmosphere.','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Aerobic nitrate oxidation and nitrite reduction','Decomposition of organic nitrogen and conversion of dinitrogen to ammonium compounds','Enteric fermentation in cattle and nitrogen fixation by Rhizobium in root nodules of legumes','Anaerobic ammonium oxidation and denitrification','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121616,'biology','Which one of the following is wrong statement (2012)','Phosphorus is a constituent of cell membranes, certain nucleic acids and all proteins','Phosphorus is present in plasma membrane in the form of phospholipid bilayer. It is an essential component of all nucleic acids not ‘certain’ nucleic acids','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Anabaena and Nostoc are capable of fixing nitrogen in free-living state also.','Root nodule forming nitrogen fixers live as aerobes under free-living conditions.','Phosphorus is a constituent of cell membranes, certain nucleic acids and all proteins','Nitrosomonas and Nitrobacter are chemoautotrophs','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121617,'biology','For its action, nitrogenase requires [Mains 2012]','high input of energy','Nitrogenase enzyme is present in prokaryotic nitrogen fixers. The enzyme nitrogenase requires a high input of energy to carry out biological nitrogen fixation','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','high input of energy','light','Mn²⁺','super O₂ radicals','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121618,'biology','Nitrifying bacteria [AIPMT 2011]','oxidise ammonia to nitrates','Nitrifying bacteria involves the oxidation of ammonia to nitrates through nitrites called nitrification.Nitrite bacteria (Nitrosomonas and Nitrococcus) convert ammonia to nitrites whereas, nitrate bacteria (Nitrobacter and Nitrocystis) convert nitrite to soluble nitrates','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','oxidise ammonia to nitrates','convert free nitrogen to nitrogen compounds','convert proteins into ammonia','reduce nitrates to free nitrogen','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121619,'biology','The function of leghaemoglobin in the root nodules of legumes is [AIPMT 2011]','O₂ removal','The root nodule of legume contains enzyme nitrogenase and leghaemoglobin. Nitrogenase catalyses the conversion of atmospheric nitrogen to ammonia. It is highly sensitive to the molecular O₂ and requires anaerobic conditions. The nodules have adaptations to ensure that the enzyme is protected from oxygen. To protect these enzymes, the nodule contains an O₂ scavenger called leghaemoglobin.','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','inhibition of nitrogenase activity','O₂ removal','nodule differentiation','expression of nif gene','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121620,'biology','Leguminous plants are able to fix atmospheric nitrogen through the process of symbiotic nitrogen fixation. Which one of the following statements is not correct during this process of nitrogen fixation? [Mains 2010]','Nitrogenase is insensitive to oxygen','The enzyme nitrogenase is highly sensitive to the molecular oxygen.','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Leghaemoglobin scavenges O₂ and is pinkish in colour','Nodules act as sites for nitrogen fixation.','The enzyme nitrogenase catalyses the conversion of atmospheric N₂ to NH₃','Nitrogenase is insensitive to oxygen','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121621,'biology','One of the free-living, anaerobic nitrogen-fixeris [AIPMT 2010]','Rhodospirillum','Many free living bacteria and blue green algae are capable to fix atmospheric nitrogen Rhodospirillum is a free living photosynthetic anaerobic nitrogen fixing non-sulphur bacteria. It is capable of synthesizing its organic food in presence of light and in absence of O₂ by a process known as bacterial photosynthesis. Beijerinckia and Azotobacter are free living but aerobic nitrogen fixing bacteria. Rhizobium is a symbiotic nitrogen fixing bacteria','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Beijerinckia','Rhodospirillum','Rhizobium','Azotobacter','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121627,'biology','Enzyme first used for nitrogen fixation [AIPMT 2001]','nitrogenase','Nitrogen fixation involves conversion of atmospheric nitrogen to ammonia. It is done with the help of nitrogenase enzyme which occurs inside thick walled heterocysts of the blue green algae. These provide suitable anaerobic environment for nitrogenase activityeven in aerobic conditions.','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','nitrogenase','nitroreductase','transferase','transaminase','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121628,'biology','A non-photosynthetic aerobic nitrogen fixing soil bacterium is [AIPMT 1997]','Azotobacter','All plants need nitrogen to synthesize proteins, but for this purpose they are unable to utilize atmospheric nitrogen. Nitrogen fixation is brought about by two types of bacteria which are known as nitrogen fixing bacteria. One type is symbiotic nitrogen fixers that are associated with plants, e.g., Rhizobium and Azospirillum. The other type of these bacteria are free living in the soil, e.g., Azotobacter and Nostoc. Nostoc is photosynthetic and Azotobacter is non-photosynthetic. So that, the free living aerobic non-photosynthetic nitrogen fixing bacterium is Azotobacter','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Nostoc','Azospirillum','Rhizobium','Azotobacter','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121629,'biology','Which of the following is free-living aerobic non-photosynthetic nitrogen-fixing bacterium [AIPMT 1994, 1990]','Azotobacter','All plants need nitrogen to synthesize proteins, but for this purpose they are unable to utilize atmospheric nitrogen. Nitrogen fixation is brought about by two types of bacteria which are known as nitrogen fixing bacteria. One type is symbiotic nitrogen fixers that are associated with plants, e.g., Rhizobium and Azospirillum. The other type of these bacteria are free living in the soil, e.g., Azotobacter and Nostoc. Nostoc is photosynthetic and Azotobacter is non-photosynthetic. So that, the free living aerobic non-photosynthetic nitrogen fixing bacterium is Azotobacter','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Rhizobium','Clostridium','Azotobacter','Klebsiella','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121630,'biology','Match the following concerning essential elements and their functions in plants. [NEET2020]\nColumn-I        Column-II\n(A)Iron        (i)Photolysis of water\n(B)Zinc        (ii)Pollen germination\n(C)Boron       (iii) Requiredforchlorophyll biosynthesis\n(D)Manganese (iv)IAA biosynthesis\nSelect the correct option.','A(iii)B(iv)C(ii)D(i)','Iron is required for chlorophyll biosynthesis\nzinc is required for IAA biosynthesis\nboron is essential for pollen germination\nmanganese is requred for photoysis of water','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','A(ii)B(i)C(iv) D(iii)','A(iv) B(iii) C(ii) D(i)','A(iii)B(iv)C(ii)D(i)','A(iv)B(i)C(ii)D(iii)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121631,'biology','Which of the following elements is responsible for maintaining turgor in cells? [NEET2018]','Potassium','Potassium helps in the maintenance of cell turgidity and opening and closing of stomata','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Magnesium','Sodium','Potassium','Calcium','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121632,'biology','In which of the following forms is iron absorbed by plants? [NEET2018]','Ferric','Iron is absorbed by plants in the form of ferric ions.','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Ferric','Ferrous','Free element','Both ferric and ferrous','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121633,'biology','Match Column-I with Column-II. [NEET2021]\nColumn-I                Column-II   \n(a)Nitrococcus     (i)Denitrification\n(b) Rhizobium     (ii)Conversion of ammonia to nitrite\n(c) Thiobacillus   (iii)Conversion of nitrite to nitrate\n(d) Nitrobacter     (iv)Conversion of atmospheric nitrogen to ammonia\nChoose the correct answer from options given\nbelow.\n      (a) (b) (c) (d)\n(1) (iv) (iii) (ii) (i)\n(2) (ii) (iv) (i) (iii)\n(3) (i) (ii) (iii) (iv)\n(4) (iii) (i) (iv) (ii)','2','Nitrococcus -Conversion of ammonia to nitrite.\nRhizobium -Conversion of atmospheric nitrogen to ammonia \nThiobacillus- Denitrification\nNitrobacter -Conversion of nitrite to nitrate','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121634,'biology','Which one of the following produces nitrogen fixing nodules on the root of alnus','Frankia','the microbe frankia, produces nitrogen fixing nodules on the root of alnus','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Rhizobium','Frankia','Rhodospirillum','Beijerinckia','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121635,'biology','Choose the correct answer from the options given below : [RE NEET 2022] diagram (1207)','(2) (a) - (iv), (b) - (i), (c) - (ii), (d) - (iii)','(a) - (iv), (b) - (i), (c) - (ii), (d) - (iii)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) (a) - (ii), (b) - (i), (c) - (iv), (d) - (iii)','(2) (a) - (iv), (b) - (i), (c) - (ii), (d) - (iii)','(3) (a) - (iii), (b) - (iv), (c) - (ii), (d) - (i)','(4) (a) - (ii), (b) - (iv), (c) - (i), (d) - (iii)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121636,'biology','Which of the following mineral ion is not \nremobilized in plants ? [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(2) Cyclic photophosphorylation involves both PS I and PS II','Calcium is not remobilized from the leaves to the fruits, like potassium, phosphorus and sulphur. It occurs abundantly in a non-exchangeable form such as anorthite (CaAl2Si2O8).','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Grana lamellae have both PS I and PS II','(2) Cyclic photophosphorylation involves both PS I and PS II','(4) Stroma lamellae lack PS II and NADP reductase','(3) Both ATP and NADPH + H+\n are synthesised during non-cyclic photophosphorylation.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121637,'biology','Match List I with List II:\nList I List II\nA. Iron I. Synthesis of auxin\nB. Zinc II. Component of nitrate reductase\nC. Boron III. Activator of catalase\nD. Molybdenum IV. Cell elongation and differentiation\nChoos the correct answer from the options given below: [ NEET 2023]','(2) A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II','A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I','(2) A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II','(3) A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III','(4) A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121701,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion-The basic needs of all living organisms are essentially different. \nReason- Living organisms require micro-molecules, such as carbohydrates, proteins and fats.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','The basic needs of all living organisms are essentially the same. They require macromolecules, such as carbohydrates, proteins and fats, and water and minerals for their growth and development. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 194, Para 1 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51041,'chemistry','At the same temperature, molecular speed distribution curves are given below for Nitrogen and Chlorine molecules.\n\nChoose the most appropriate match for "A" and "B" curves from the molecular speed distribution curve shown below diagram(n516)','A-nitrogen, B-chlorine','At the same temperature lighter nitrogen molecules move faster than heavier chlorine molecules.\n\nHence, at any given temperature, nitrogen molecules have higher value of most probable speed than the chlorine molecules. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 148; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A-nitrogen, B-chlorine','A-chlorine, B-nitrogen','Both curves should be at same molecular speed','Both curves should be straight lines','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121702,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Julius von Sachs demonstrated, for the first time, that plants could be grown to maturity in a define nutrient solution in complete absence of soil. \nReason- In hydroponics, soil is required.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','In 1860, Julius von Sachs, a prominent German botanist, demonstrated, for the first time, that plants could be grown to maturity in a defined nutrient solution in complete absence of soil. This technique of growing plants in a nutrient solution is known as hydroponics. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 194, Para 2 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121703,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Juilus Von Sachs was a British Botanist.\nReason- Juilus Von Sachs discovered hydropnics in 1960.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','In 1860, Julius von Sachs, a prominent German botanist, demonstrated,\nfor the first time, that plants could be grown to maturity in a defined\nnutrient solution in complete absence of soil. This technique of growing\nplants in a nutrient solution is known as hydroponics. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 194, Para 2 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121704,'biology','Consider these statements regarding hydroponics and choose the correct option.\n1. Hydroponics involves the culture of plants in a soil-free medium.\n2. It requires purified water.\n3. Mineral salts are also needed.\n4. A defined mineral solution is not required.','Only 4 is incorrect','A number of improvised methods have been employed to try and determine the mineral nutrients essential for plants. The essence of all these methods\ninvolves the culture of plants in a soil-free, defined mineral solution. These methods require purified water and mineral nutrient salts. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 194, Para 2 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','All the options are correct','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Only is 1 is correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121705,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Hydroponics has been successfully employed as a technique for the commercial production of vegetables such as seedless tomato, cucumber and lettuce. \nReason- After a series of experiments in which the soil of the plants were immersed in nutrient solutions and wherein an element was added / substituted / removed or given in varied concentration, a mineral solution suitable for the plant growth was obtained.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','After a series of experiments in which the roots of the plants were immersed in nutrient solutions and wherein an element was added / substituted / removed or given in varied concentration, a mineral solution suitable for the plant growth was obtained. By this method, essential elements were identified and their deficiency symptoms discovered. Hydroponics has been successfully employed as a technique for the commercial production of vegetables such as tomato, seedless cucumber and lettuce. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 195, Para 1 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121706,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- The nutrient solutions must be adequately aerated to obtain the optimum growth.\nReason- Roots will die if not provided with enough oxygen.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','When the cells in the roots do not have access to oxygen, they will die and this can cause the entire plant to die as well. Thus, the nutrient solutions must be adequately aerated to obtain the optimum growth. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 195, Para 1 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121707,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- More than seventy elements of the 105 discovered so far are found in different plants. \nReason- Most of the minerals present can enter plants through leaves.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Most of the minerals present in soil can enter plants through roots. In fact, more than sixty elements of the 105 discovered so far are found in different plants. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 195, Para 2 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121708,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- There are techniques that are able to detect the minerals even at a very low concentration.\nReason- Some plants growing near nuclear test sites take up radioactive selenium.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Some plants growing near nuclear test sites take up radioactive strontium. There are techniques that are able to detect the minerals even at a very low concentration (10 ⁻⁸ g/mL). ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 195, Para 2 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(71514,'chemistry','Kₐ for CH₃COOH is 1.8 x 10⁻⁵ and K_b for NH₄OH is 1.8 x 10⁻⁵. The pH of ammonium acetate will be','7','Ammonium acetate is a salt of weak acid and weak base.\n\npH = 1/2[ pK_w + pKₐ + pK_b ] = 1/2 [ 14- log(1.8 x 10⁻⁵) + log(1.8 x 10⁻⁵) ] = 7.0','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','7.005','4.75','7','Between 6 and 7','NEW','11-226','11-202',NULL),
(121709,'biology','Consider these statements regarding criteria for essentiality and choose the correct option.\n1. The element must be absolutely necessary for supporting normal growth and reproduction.\n2. In the absence of the element the plants can complete their life cycle or set the seeds.\n3. The requirement of the element must be specific and not replaceable by another element. \n4. The element must not be directly involved in the metabolism of the plant.','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','The criteria for essentiality of an element are given below: (a) The element must be absolutely necessary for supporting normal growth and reproduction. In the absence of the element the plants do not complete their life cycle or set the seeds. (b) The requirement of the element must be specific and not replaceable by another element. In other words, deficiency of any one element cannot be met by supplying some other element. (c) The element must be directly involved in the metabolism of the plant. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 195, Para 3 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 2 is incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Only 1 is correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121710,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Hydroponic plant production and choose the correct option.\n1. A pump circulates a nutrient solution from a reservoir to the elevated end of the tube. \n2. Plants are grown in a tube or trough placed on a straight angle.\n3. The solution flows down the tube and returns to the reservoir due to gravity.\n4. Roots are continuously bathed in aerated nutrient solution.','Only 2 is incorrect','Hydroponic plant production. Plants are grown in a tube or trough placed on a slight incline. A pump circulates a nutrient solution from a reservoir to the elevated end of the tube. The solution flows down the tube and returns to the reservoir due to gravity. Roots are continuously bathed in aerated nutrient solution. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 195, Last Para )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 2 is incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Only 1 is correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121711,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- The macronutrients include carbon, hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen, phosphorous, sulphur, potassium, calcium and magnesium.\nReason- Carbon, hydrogen and water are absorbed from the soil as mineral nutrition. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 196, Para 2)','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The macronutrients include carbon, hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen, phosphorous, sulphur, potassium, calcium and magnesium. Of these, carbon, hydrogen and O₂ are mainly obtained from  CO₂  and water, while the others are absorbed from the soil as mineral nutrition. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 196, Para 2)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121712,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Macronutrients are generally present in plant tissues in large amounts (in excess of 10 mole/kg of dry matter). \nReason- Micronutrients or trace elements, are needed in very small amounts (less than 10 mole/kg of dry matter).','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Micronutrients or trace elements, are needed in very small amounts (less than 10 mmole/Kg  of dry matter). Macronutrients are generally present in plant tissues in large amounts (in excess of 10 mmole /Kg of dry matter). ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 196, Para 2)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121713,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Sodium, silicon, cobalt and strontium are beneficial elements.\nReason- Beneficial elements are required by lower plants.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','There are some beneficial elements such as sodium, silicon, cobalt and selenium. They are required by higher plants. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 196, Para 3)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121714,'biology','Consider these statements regarding essential elements and choose the correct option.\n1. Essential elements are used as components of biomolecules.\n2. Minerals are used as solvents in determining the water potential of a cell.\n3. Phosphorus plays an important role in the opening and closing of stomata. \n4. Essential elements can alter the osmotic potential of a cell.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Some essential elements can alter the osmotic potential of a cell. Potassium plays an important role in the opening and closing of stomata. You may recall the role of minerals as solutes in determining the water potential of a cell. Essential elements are used as components of biomolecules. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 196, Para 5 and 6)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Only 2 is correct','Only 1 is correct','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121715,'biology','Consider these statements regarding essential elements and choose the correct option.\n1. Carbon, hydrogen, O₂ and nitrogen are structural elements of the cell.\n2. Magnesium is energy related compound found in ATP.\n3. Essential elements activate or inhibit enzymes.\n4. Phosphorus is energy related compound found in chlorophyll.','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Essential elements as components of biomolecules and hence structural elements of cells (e.g., carbon, hydrogen, O₂ and nitrogen). Essential elements can activate or inhibit enzymes. Essential elements that are components of energy-related chemical compounds in plants (e.g., magnesium in chlorophyll and phosphorous in ATP). ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 196, Para 6 and 7)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Only 4 is incorrect','All the options are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(71515,'chemistry','Which of the following options will be correct for the stage of half completion of the reaction A ⇌B.','ΔG° = 0','A ⇌B\nΔG° = -RT In K\nAt the stage of half completion of reaction [A] = [B],\nTherefore, K = 1. Thus, ΔG° = 0.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','ΔG° = 0','ΔG° > 0','ΔG° < 0','ΔG° = -RT In 2','NEW','11-208','11-184',NULL),
(121716,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Micronutrients include iron, manganese, copper, molybdenum, zinc, boron, chlorine and nickel.\nReason- There are 18 essential elements.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','There are 17 essential elements. Micronutrients include iron, manganese, copper, molybdenum, zinc, boron, chlorine and nickel. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 196, Para 3)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121717,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Carbon is mainly obtained from  CO₂ .\nReason- Micronutrients are also known as trace elements.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Micronutrients or trace elements, are needed in very small amounts. Carbon, hydrogen and O₂ are mainly obtained from  CO₂  and water. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 196, Para 2)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121718,'biology','Consider these statements regarding essential elements and choose the correct option.\n1. Mo activates nitrogenase during nitrogen metabolism. \n2. Zn²⁺ is an activator of alcohol dehydrogenase.\n3. Ribulose bisphosphate carboxylase-oxygenase and phosphoenol pyruvate oxygenase, both are critical enzymes in photosynthetic carbon fixation.\n4. Mg²⁺ is an activator for only ribulose bisphosphate carboxylase-oxygenase.','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','Essential elements that activate or inhibit enzymes, for example Mg²⁺ is an activator for both ribulose bisphosphate carboxylase-oxygenase and phosphoenol pyruvate carboxylase, both of which are critical enzymes in photosynthetic carbon fixation; Zn²⁺ is an activator of alcohol dehydrogenase and Mo of nitrogenase during nitrogen metabolism. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 196, Para 6 and 7)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','Statements 2, 3 and 4 are correct','All the statements are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121719,'biology','Consider these statements regarding essential elements and choose the correct option.\n1. They act as major constituents of macromolecules and co-enzymes.\n2. They maintain osmotic concentration of cell sap.\n3. They are used in electron-transport systems and buffering action.\n4. They maintain permeability of cell membrane.','All the statements are correct','Essential elements perform several functions. They participate in various metabolic processes in the plant cells such as permeability of cell membrane, maintenance of osmotic concentration of cell sap, electron-transport systems, buffering action, enzymatic activity and act as major constituents of macromolecules and co-enzymes. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 197, Para 1)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','Statements 2, 3 and 4 are correct','All the statements are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121720,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Nitrogen is an essential nutrient element required by plants in the greatest amount.\nReason- Nitrogen is mainly absorbed as NO₂.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Nitrogen is the essential nutrient element required by plants in the greatest amount. It is absorbed mainly as NO₃⁻. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 197, Para 2)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121721,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Nitrogen is one of the major constituents of proteins, nucleic acids, vitamins and hormones.\nReason- Nitrogen is required by all parts of a plant, particularly the meristematic tissues and the metabolically inactive cells.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Nitrogen is required by all parts of a plant, particularly the meristematic tissues and the metabolically active cells. Nitrogen is one of the major constituents of proteins, nucleic acids, vitamins and hormones. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 197, Para 2)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121722,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Phosphorus is a constituent of cell walls.\nReason- Phosphorus is absorbed by the plants from soil in the form of PO₄⁻ ions.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Phosphorus is absorbed by the plants from soil in the form of phosphate ions as H₂PO₄⁻ or HPO₄²⁻. Phosphorus is a constituent of cell membranes. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 197, Para 3)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121723,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Phosphorus is required for all phosphorylation reactions.\nReason- All nucleic acids and nucleotides contain Phosphorus.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Phosphorus is a constituent of cell membranes, certain proteins, all nucleic acids and nucleotides, and is required for all phosphorylation reactions. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 197, Para 3)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121724,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Potassium and choose the correct option.\n1. It is absorbed as potassium ion K⁺.\n2. Potassium helps to maintain an anion-cation balance in cells.\n3. It is required in more abundant quantities in the older tissues.\n4.  It is involved in vitamin synthesis.','Both 1 and 2 are correct','It is absorbed as potassium ion K⁺. In plants, this is required in more abundant quantities in the meristematic tissues, buds, leaves and root tips. Potassium helps to maintain an anion-cation balance in cells and is involved in protein synthesis, opening and closing of stomata, activation of enzymes and in the maintenance of the turgidity of cells. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 197, Para 4)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Statements 2, 3 and 4 are correct','All the statements are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121725,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Potassium and choose the correct option.\n1. It guards opening and closing of stomata.\n2. Potassium helps in deactivation of enzymes.\n3. It is required in more abundant quantities in the older tissues.\n4. It is used in the buds, leaves and root tips.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','In plants, this is required in more abundant quantities in the meristematic tissues, buds, leaves and root tips. Potassium helps to maintain an anion-cation balance in cells and is involved in protein synthesis, opening and closing of stomata, activation of enzymes and in the maintenance of the turgidity of cells. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 197, Para 4)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Only 3 is correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121726,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option\n.\nAssertion- Calcium accumulates in younger leaves.\n\nReason- Calcium does not play an important role in regulating metabolic activities.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Calcium accumulates in older leaves and plays an important role in regulating metabolic activities. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 197, Para 5)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121727,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Plant absorbs calcium from the soil in the form of calcium ions (Ca²⁺). \nReason- Calcium is needed during the formation of mitotic spindle.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Plant absorbs calcium from the soil in the form of calcium ions (Ca²⁺). It is needed during the formation of mitotic spindle. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 197, Para 5)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121728,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Calcium is involved in the normal functioning of the cell walls. \nReason- During cell division Calcium is used in the synthesis of cell membrane.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Calcium is required by meristematic and differentiating tissues. During cell division it is used in the synthesis of cell wall, particularly as calcium pectate in the middle lamella. It is involved in the normal functioning of the cell membranes. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 197, Para 5)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121729,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Calcium is found as calcium pectate in the middle lamella. \nReason- Calcium cannot be used to activate enzymes.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','During cell division it is used in the synthesis of cell wall, particularly as calcium pectate in the middle lamella. It activates certain enzymes and plays an important role in regulating metabolic activities. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 197, Para 5)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121730,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Magnesium and choose the correct option.\n1. Magnesium is a constituent of the ring structure of chlorophyll.\n2. Magnesium maintains the ribosome structure.\n3. Magnesium is involved in the synthesis of DNA and RNA. \n4. Magnesium activates the enzymes of respiration and photosynthesis.','All the statements are correct','Magnesium activates the enzymes of respiration, photosynthesis and are involved in the synthesis of DNA and RNA. Magnesium is a constituent of the ring structure of chlorophyll and helps to maintain the ribosome structure. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 197, Para 6)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Statements 2, 3 and 4 are correct','All the statements are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121731,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Sulphur is present in ferredoxin.\nReason- Sulphur is present in two amino acids – histidine and methionine','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Sulphur is present in two amino acids – cysteine and methionine. It is the main constituent of ferredoxin. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 197, Para 7)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121732,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Plants obtain iron in the form of ferrous ions.\nReason- It is required in lesser amounts in comparison to other micronutrients.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Plants obtain iron in the form of ferric ions. It is required in larger amounts in comparison to other micronutrients. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 197, Para 8)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121733,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Iron is involved in the transfer of electrons like ferredoxin and cytochromes. \nReason- Iron is reversibly oxidised from Fe⁺³ to Fe²⁺ during electron transfer.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Iron is an important constituent of proteins involved in the transfer of electrons like ferredoxin and cytochromes. It is reversibly oxidised from Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺ during electron transfer. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 197, Para 8)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121734,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Iron is essential for the formation of chlorophyll.\nReason- Iron can activate the catalase enzyme.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Iron activates catalase enzyme, and is essential for the formation of chlorophyll. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 197, Para 8)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121735,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Manganese split the water to liberate O₂ during photosynthesis.\nReason- Manganese is absorbed in the form of manganous ions (Mn³⁺).','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Manganese is absorbed in the form of manganous ions (Mn²⁺).The best defined function of manganese is in the splitting of water to liberate O₂ during photosynthesis. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 198, Para 1)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121736,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Zinc activates carboxylases.\nReason- Zinc is needed in the synthesis of auxin and cytokinin.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Zinc activates various enzymes, especially carboxylases. It is also needed in the synthesis of auxin. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 198, Para 2)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121737,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Zinc is essential for the overall metabolism in plants.\nReason- Copper is involved in redox reactions and is reversibly oxidised from Cu²⁺ to Cu⁺.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Copper is essential for the overall metabolism in plants. Like iron, it is associated with certain enzymes involved in redox reactions and is reversibly oxidised from Cu⁺ to Cu²⁺. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 198, Para 3)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121738,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Boron and choose the correct option.\n1. It is required for membrane functioning and pollen germination.\n2. Boron is required for uptake and utilisation of Cu²⁺.\n3. It is absorbed as B₄O₇⁻.\n4. It is required for cell differentiation and carbohydrate metabolism.','Only 1 is correct','It is absorbed as BO₃³⁻ or B₄O₇²⁻. Boron is required for uptake and utilisation of Ca²⁺, membrane functioning, pollen germination, cell elongation, cell differentiation and carbohydrate translocation. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 198, Para 4)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Statements 2, 3 and 4 are correct','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','All the statements are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121739,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Molybdenum participates in nitrogen metabolism.\nReason- Molybdenum is a component of several enzymes, including nitrogenase and nitrate reductase.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Molybdenum is a component of several enzymes, including nitrogenase and nitrate reductase both of which participate in nitrogen metabolism. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 198, Para 5)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51042,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true?\n\na) Liquid tends to rise (or fall) in the capillary because of surface tension. \n\nb) Liquids wet the things because they spread across their surfaces as thin film.','both a and b','Liquid tends to rise (or fall) in the capillary because of surface tension. \n\nLiquids wet the things because they spread across their surfaces as thin film.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 156; Paragraph  No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121740,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Plants obtain Molybdenum in the form of molybdate ions (MoO₂⁺)\nReason- Molybdenum helps in determining the solute concentration.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Molybdate ions (MoO₂²⁺) is absorbed through plants. Chlorine helps in determining the solute concentration. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 198, Para 5)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121741,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Chlorine helps in O₂ evolution.\nReason- Chlorine is essential for the water-splitting reaction in photosynthesis.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Chlorine is essential for the water-splitting reaction in photosynthesis, a reaction that leads to O₂ evolution. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 198, Para 6)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121742,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Chlorine helps in anion-cation balance in cells. \nReason- Chlorine is absorbed in form of Cl₂.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Chlorine is absorbed in the form of chloride anion (Cl⁻). It helps in determining the solute concentration and the anion-cation balance in cells. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 198, Para 6)','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121743,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- In the absence of any particular element, plants show certain morphological changes. \nReason- Each element has one or more specific structural or functional role in plants.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Since each element has one or more specific structural or functional role in plants, in the absence of any particular element, plants show certain morphological changes. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 198, Last Para )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121744,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- The concentration of the essential element above which plant growth is retarded is termed as critical concentration. \nReason- The deficiency symptoms are similar for most elements.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','The concentration of the essential element below which plant growth is retarded is termed as critical concentration. The deficiency symptoms vary from element to element. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 198, Last Para )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121745,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Deficiency symptoms disappear when the deficient mineral nutrient is provided to the plant. \nReason- Whenever the supply of an essential element becomes unlimited, plant growth is retarded.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Whenever the supply of an essential element becomes limited, plant growth is retarded. The deficiency symptoms vary from element to element and they disappear when the deficient mineral nutrient is provided to the plant. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 198, Last Para )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121746,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Deficiency symptoms of nitrogen, potassium and magnesium are visible first in the young leaves. \nReason- For elements that are actively mobilised within the plants and exported to old tissues, the deficiency symptoms tend to appear first in the younger tissues.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Deficiency symptoms of nitrogen, potassium and magnesium are visible first in the senescent leaves. For elements that are actively mobilised within the plants and exported to young developing tissues, the deficiency symptoms tend to appear first in the older tissues. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 198, Last Para )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121747,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Mobile elements are available for mobilising to younger leaves.\nReason- In the younger leaves, biomolecules containing mobile elements are broken down.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','In the older leaves, biomolecules containing these elements are broken down, making these elements available for mobilising to younger leaves. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 198, Last Para )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121748,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- The deficiency symptoms tend to appear first in the young tissues whenever the elements are relatively immobile.\nReason- Sodium and calcium are a part of the structural component of the cell and hence are not easily released.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The deficiency symptoms tend to appear first in the young tissues whenever the elements are relatively immobile and are not transported out of the mature organs, for example, element like sulphur and calcium are a part of the structural component of the cell and hence are not easily released. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 198, Last Para )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121749,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Chlorosis is the loss of chlorophyll leading to yellowing in leaves.\nReason- different plants also respond differently to the deficiency of the same element.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Different plants respond differently to the deficiency of the same element. Chlorosis is the loss of chlorophyll leading to yellowing in leaves. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 198, Last Para )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121750,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Same symptoms may be caused by the deficiency of one of several different elements. \nReason- The deficiency of any element can cause multiple symptoms.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The deficiency of any element can cause multiple symptoms and that the same symptoms may be caused by the deficiency of one of several different elements. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 198, Last Para )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121751,'biology','Consider these statements regarding deficiency symptoms and choose the correct option.\n1. Necrosis is caused due to the deficiency of C, Mg, Cu, K. \n2. N, S, Mo delay flowering.\n3. Low level of N, K, S, Mg causes an inhibition of cell division.\n4. Chlorosis is caused by the deficiency of elements Na, K, Mg, S, Fe, Mn, Zn and Mo.','Only 2 is correct','The kind of deficiency symptoms shown in plants include chlorosis, necrosis, stunted plant growth, premature fall of leaves and buds, and inhibition of cell division. Chlorosis is the loss of chlorophyll leading to yellowing in leaves. This symptom is caused by the deficiency of elements N, K, Mg, S, Fe, Mn, Zn and Mo. Likewise, necrosis, or death of tissue, particularly leaf tissue, is due to the deficiency of Ca, Mg, Cu, K. Lack or low level of N, K, S, Mo causes an inhibition of cell division. Some elements like N, S, Mo delay flowering if their concentration in plants is low. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 199, Para 1 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Statements 2, 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 is correct','Only 2 is correct','All the statements are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121752,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- There is a narrow range of concentration at which the elements are optimum. \nReason- The requirement of micronutrients is always in low amounts while their moderate decrease causes the deficiency symptoms and a moderate increase causes toxicity.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The requirement of micronutrients is always in low amounts while their moderate decrease causes the deficiency symptoms and a moderate increase causes toxicity. In other words, there is a narrow range of concentration at which the elements are optimum. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 199, Para 2 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121753,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion-  What appears as symptoms of manganese toxicity may actually be the deficiency symptoms of iron, magnesium and calcium. \nReason-  Excess of manganese induce deficiencies of iron, magnesium and calcium.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Excess of manganese may, in fact, induce deficiencies of iron, magnesium and calcium. Thus, what appears as symptoms of manganese toxicity may actually be the deficiency symptoms of iron, magnesium and calcium. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 199, Para 2 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121754,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- The prominent symptom of manganese toxicity is the appearance of brown spots surrounded by chlorotic veins. \nReason- Manganese competes with calcium and magnesium for uptake and with magnesium for binding with enzymes.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The prominent symptom of manganese toxicity is the appearance of brown spots surrounded by chlorotic veins. manganese competes with iron and magnesium for uptake and with magnesium for binding with enzymes. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 199, Para 2 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121755,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- The movement of ions is called flux.\nReason- The inward movement into the cells is efflux and the outward movement, influx.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The movement of ions is usually called flux; the inward movement into the cells is influx and the outward movement, efflux. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 200, Para 1 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121756,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- The active movement of ions into the apoplast usually occurs through ion-channels, the trans-membrane proteins that function as selective pores.\nReason- The entry or exit of ions to and from the symplast requires the expenditure of metabolic energy, which is a passive process.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','The passive movement of ions into the apoplast usually occurs through ion-channels, the trans-membrane proteins that function as selective pores. The entry or exit of ions to and from the symplast requires the expenditure of metabolic energy, which is an active process. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 200, Para 1 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121757,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Majority of the nutrients become available to the roots due to weathering and breakdown of rocks.\nReason- Nutrients are derived from the rock minerals, their role in plant nutrition is referred to as mineral nutrition.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Majority of the nutrients that are essential for the growth and development of plants become available to the roots due to weathering and breakdown of rocks. Nutrients are derived from the rock minerals, their role in plant nutrition is referred to as mineral nutrition. (Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 200, Para 3 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121758,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Nitrogen is a limiting nutrient for both natural and agricultural eco-systems.\nReason- Plants compete with microbes for the limited nitrogen that is available in soil.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Plants compete with microbes for the limited nitrogen that is available in soil. Thus, nitrogen is a limiting nutrient for both natural and agricultural eco-systems. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 201, Para 1 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121759,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- The process of conversion of nitrogen to ammonia is termed as ammonification.\nReason- In nature, Industrial combustions, forest fires, automobile exhausts and power-generating stations provide enough energy to convert nitrogen to nitrogen oxides.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','The process of conversion of nitrogen  to ammonia is termed as nitrogen-fixation. In nature, lightning and ultraviolet radiation provide enough energy to convert nitrogen to nitrogen oxides. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 201, Para 1 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121760,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Industrial combustions, forest fires, automobile exhausts and power -generating stations are sources of atmospheric nitrogen oxides.\nReason- Decomposition of organic nitrogen of dead plants and animals into ammonia is called ammonification.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Industrial combustions, forest fires, automobile exhausts and power-generating stations are also sources of atmospheric nitrogen oxides. Decomposition of organic nitrogen of dead plants and animals into ammonia is called ammonification. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 201, Para 1 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121761,'biology','Consider these statements regarding nitrogen cycle and choose the correct option.\n1. Ammonia is oxidised to nitrite by the bacteria Nitrobacter.\n2. Process of conversion of ammonia into nitrates and nitrites is called Nitrification.\n3. Nitrosomonas is a chemoautotrophic bacteria.\n4. The nitrite is oxidised to nitrate with the help of the bacterium Nitrococcus.','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','Ammonia is first oxidised to nitrite by the bacteria Nitrosomonas and/or Nitrococcus. The nitrite is further oxidised to nitrate with the help of the bacterium Nitrobacter. These steps are called nitrification. These nitrifying bacteria are chemoautotrophs. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 201, Para 2 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','Only 2 is correct','Only 4 is incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51043,'chemistry','The compressibility factor (Z) for a real gas at a high pressure is:','1+(Pb/RT)','(P+a/V2)(V-b) = RT [Real gas equation]\na/V2 can be neglected at high pressure.\nPV-Pb = RT\nPV/RT = (RT/RT) + (Pb/RT)\nPV/RT = 1 + (Pb/RT)  > (1)\nZ = PV/RT > (2)\nEquating (1) and (2)\nZ = 1 + (Pb/RT) So d is the answer  \nReference: (JEE Main 2021 Exam)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1-(Pb/RT)','1+ (RT/Pb)','1','1+(Pb/RT)','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121762,'biology','Consider these statements regarding nitrogen cycle and choose the correct option.\n1. Nitrate formed is absorbed by plants and is transported to the roots.\n2. In leaves, nitrate is reduced to form ammonia.\n3. Denitrification is carried by bacteria Pseudomonas and Thiobacillus.\n4. Nitrate present in the soil is reduced to nitrogen by the process of Nitrification.','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','The nitrate thus formed is absorbed by plants and is transported to the leaves. In leaves, it is reduced to form ammonia that finally forms the amine group of amino acids. Nitrate present in the soil is also reduced to nitrogen by the process of denitrification. Denitrification is carried by bacteria Pseudomonas and Thiobacillus. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 201, Para 2 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','Only 2 is correct','Only 4 is incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121763,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Biological Nitrogen Fixation and choose the correct option.\n1. Nitrogenase is capable of nitrogen reduction and is called N₂- fixer.\n2. Azotobacter and Beijerinckia are free living and anaerobic.\n3. Rhodospirillum is anaerobic and free-living. \n4. Cyanobacteria such as Anabaena and Nostoc are free-living nitrogen-fixers.','Only 2 is incorrect','The nitrogen-fixing microbes could be free-living or symbiotic. Examples of free-living nitrogen-fixing aerobic microbes are Azotobacter and Beijerinckia while Rhodospirillum is anaerobic and free-living. In addition, a number of cyanobacteria such as Anabaena and Nostoc are also free-living nitrogen-fixers. The enzyme, nitrogenase which is capable of nitrogen reduction is present exclusively in prokaryotes. Such microbes are called Nitrogen-fixers. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 202, Para 2 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 2 is incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121764,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- The central portion of root nodule is red or pink. \nReason- Presence of leguminous haemoglobin or leg-haemoglobin makes the nodules pink.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Uproot any one plant of a common pulse, just before flowering. You will see near-spherical outgrowths on the roots. These are nodules. If you cut through them you will notice that the central portion is red or pink. What makes the nodules pink? This is due to the presence of leguminous haemoglobin or leg-haemoglobin. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 202, Para 4 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121765,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Symbiotic Biological Nitrogen Fixation and choose the correct option.\n1. Alfalfa, sweet clover, sweet pea, lentils, garden pea,broad bean and clover beans are legumes.\n2. Both Rhizobium and Frankia as symbionts, can fix atmospheric nitrogen.\n3. Both Rhizobium and Frankia are free-living in soil\n4. Frankia, produces nitrogen-fixing nodules on the roots of leguminous plants.','Only 4 is incorrect','Species of rod-shaped Rhizobium has such relationship with the roots of several legumes such as alfalfa, sweet clover, sweet pea, lentils, garden pea, broad bean, clover beans, etc. The most common association on roots is as nodules. These nodules are small outgrowths on the roots. The microbe, Frankia, also produces nitrogen-fixing nodules on the roots of non-leguminous plants (e.g., Alnus). Both Rhizobium and Frankia are free-living in soil, but as symbionts, can fix atmospheric nitrogen. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 202, Para 3 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 2 is incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121766,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- The root nodule contains all the necessary biochemical components, such as the enzyme nitrobacter and leghaemoglobin. \nReason- The enzyme nitrobacter is a Mo-Fe protein and catalyses the conversion of atmospheric nitrogen to ammonia','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','The nodule contains all the necessary biochemical components, such as the enzyme nitrogenase and leghaemoglobin. The enzyme nitrogenase is a Mo-Fe protein and catalyses the conversion of atmospheric nitrogen to ammonia. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 202, Last Para )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121767,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- The root nodules have adaptations that ensure that the enzyme is protected from oxygen.\nReason- The enzyme nitrogenase is highly sensitive to the molecular oxygen; it requires anaerobic conditions.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The enzyme nitrogenase is highly sensitive to the molecular oxygen; it requires anaerobic conditions. The nodules have adaptations that ensure that the enzyme is protected from oxygen. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 203, Para 1 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121768,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Leg-haemoglobin is an O₂ scavenger.\nReason- Ammonia synthesis by nitrogenease requires a very high input of energy.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','To protect enzymes, the nodule contains an O₂ scavenger called leg-haemoglobin. The ammonia synthesis by nitrogenease requires a very high input of energy (8 ATP for each ammonia produced). ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 203, Para 1 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121769,'biology','Consider these statements regarding nodule formation and choose the correct option.\n1. Rhizobia multiply and colonise the surroundings of roots and get attached to epidermal and root hair cells. \n2. The nodule formed, establishes a direct vascular connection with the host for exchange of nutrients.\n3. An infection thread is produced carrying the bacteria into the endodermis of the root.\n4. Bacteria are released from the thread into the cells which leads to the differentiation of specialised nitrogen fixing cells.','Only 3 is incorrect','Rhizobia multiply and colonise the surroundings of roots and get attached to epidermal and root hair cells. The root-hairs curl and the bacteria invade the root-hair. An infection thread is produced carrying the bacteria into the cortex of the root, where they initiate the nodule formation in the cortex of the root. Then the bacteria are released from the thread into the cells which leads to the differentiation of specialised nitrogen fixing cells. The nodule thus formed, establishes a direct vascular connection with the host for exchange of nutrients. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 202, Last Para )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 3 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Statements 2, 3 and 4 are correct','All the statements are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121770,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- At physiological pH, the ammonia is protonated to form ammonium.\nReason- Ammonium ions are quite toxic to plants and hence cannot accumulate in them.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','At physiological pH, the ammonia is protonated to form ammonium ion. While most of the plants can assimilate nitrate as well as ammonium ions, the latter is quite toxic to plants and hence cannot accumulate in them. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 204, Para 2 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121771,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Amides are transported to other parts of the plant via xylem vessels. \nReason- Amides contain more nitrogen than the amino acids.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Since amides contain more nitrogen than the amino acids, they are transported to other parts of the plant via xylem vessels. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 204, Last Para )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121772,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Ureides have a particularly high carbon to nitrogen ratio.\nReason- Asparagine and glutamine are a functional part of proteins.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Along with the transpiration stream the nodules of some plants (e.g., soyabean) export the fixed nitrogen as ureides. These compounds also have a particularly high nitrogen to carbon ratio. The two most important amides – asparagine and glutamine – found in plants are a structural part of proteins. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 204, Last Para )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121773,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- In reductive amination, ammonia reacts with α-ketoglutaric acid and forms glutamic acid.\nReason- Glutamic acid is the main amino acid in transamination.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Reductive amination : In these processes, ammonia reacts with α-ketoglutaric acid and forms glutamic acid. Transamination : It involves the transfer of amino group from one amino acid to the keto group of a keto acid. Glutamic acid is the main amino acid from which the transfer of NH₂ , the amino group takes place and other amino acids are formed through transamination. ( Reference  : Biology NCERTClass 11, Mineral Nutrition, Page No. 204, Para 2 )','Plant Physiology >> Mineral Nutrition >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360101,'biology','_______ is the science dealing with the study of organisms and their relations to the environment.','Ecology','Ecology is the study of the relationships between living organisms, including humans, and their physical environment; it seeks to understand the vital connections between plants and animals and the world around them.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'2','mcq','Ecology','Physiology','Biology','Environmental Science','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360102,'biology','An ecosystem can be visualised as a _________ unit of nature.','Functional','An ecosystem can be visualised as a functional unit of nature, where living organisms interact among themselves and also with the surrounding physical environment.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'1','mcq','Functional','Anatomical','Biochemical','Physical','NEW','12-241','12-205',NULL),
(360103,'biology','In an ecosystem living organisms interact among themselves and also with the surrounding ______ environment.','Physical','Ecosystem is where living organisms interact among themselves and also with the surrounding physical environment.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'1','mcq','Physical','Non-physical','Physiological','Non-physiological','NEW','12-241','12-205',NULL),
(360104,'biology','Biosphere is regarded as','Global ecosystem','Ecosystem varies greatly in size from a small pond to a large forest or a sea. Many ecologists regard the entire biosphere as a global ecosystem, as a composite of all local ecosystems on earth','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'1','mcq','Local ecosytem','Global population','Global ecosystem','Atmosphere + Ecosystem','NEW','12-241','12-205',NULL),
(360105,'biology','Find the incorrect statement .','Ecosystem Is Too Small And Easy To Be Studied At One Time','An ecosystem can be visualised as a functional unit of nature, where living organisms interact among themselves and also with the surrounding physical environment. Ecosystem varies greatly in size from a small pond to a large forest or a sea. Many ecologists regard the entire biosphere as a global ecosystem, as a composite of all local ecosystems on earth. Since this system is too much big and complex to be studied at one time.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'1','mcq','Ecologists Regard The Entire Biosphere As A Global Ecosystem','Ecosystem Is Too Small And Easy To Be Studied At One Time','Ecosystem Is A Functional Unit Of Nature','Ecosystem Varies Greatly In Size','NEW','12-241','12-205',NULL),
(360106,'biology','In How many categories ecosystem is divided?','2','Since this system is too big and complex to be studied at one time, it is convenient to divide it into two basic categories, namely terrestrial and the aquatic.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'1','mcq','2','3','4','5','NEW','12-241','12-205',NULL),
(360107,'biology','What are the basic categories of ecosystem , namely ?','Terrestrial And Aquatic','Two basic categories, namely the terrestrial and the aquatic.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'2','mcq','Terrestrial And Aquatic','Aquatic And Tropical','Tropical','Both A And B','NEW','12-241','12-205',NULL),
(360108,'biology','What are some examples of terrestrial ecosystems\ni) Forest\n\nii)Grasslands\n\niii)Aquarium','ii, i','Forest, grassland and desert are some examples of terrestrial ecosystems','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'2','mcq','i, iii','ii, iii','i','ii, i','NEW','12-241','12-205',NULL),
(360109,'biology','Abiotic components include','All of the above.','Abiotic factors refer to non-living physical and chemical elements in the ecosystem. Abiotic resources are usually obtained from the lithosphere, atmosphere, and hydrosphere. Examples of abiotic factors are water, air, soil, sunlight, and minerals. Biotic factors are living or once-living organisms in the ecosystem.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'2','mcq','Physical Components','Inorganic Matter','Organic Matter','All of the above.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360110,'biology','The broadly divided three categories of _______ components of ecosystem are producers, consumers and decomposers.','Biotic','Biotic describes a living component of an ecosystem; for example organisms, such as plants and animals. Examples water, light, wind, soil, humidity, minerals, gases. All living things — autotrophs and heterotrophs — plants, animals, fungi, bacteria.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'1','mcq','Biotic','Abiotic','Terrestrial','Aquatic','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360111,'biology','______ and ______ may also be considered as man-made ecosystems.','Crop Fields  and Aquarium','Cropfield and an aquarium may also be considered as man-made ecosystems.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'2','mcq','Crop Fields and Desert','Aquarium and Mangroove Forest','Crop Fields  and Aquarium','Grassland','NEW','12-241','12-205',NULL),
(360112,'biology','Interaction of ______  and ________ components result in a physical structure that is characteristic for each type of ecosystem','Biotic and Abiotic','Interaction of biotic and abiotic components result in a physical structure that is characteristic for each type of ecosystem','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'2','mcq','Biotic and Abiotic','Real and Atificial','Inorganic and Salts','O₂ and Metals','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(360113,'biology','Identification and enumeration of plant and animal species of an ecosystem gives its _______ composition.','Species','Identification and enumeration of plant and animal species of an ecosystem gives its species composition.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'2','mcq','Genetic','Species','Ecological','Community','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(360114,'biology','Vertical distribution of different species occupying different levels is called ?','Stratification','Vertical distribution of different species occupying different levels is called stratification.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'2','mcq','Stratification','Fortification','Biofortification','Succession','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(360115,'biology','Choose the correct one based on the position occupied in the forest','Trees - Top , Shurbs - Second , Grasses - Bottom.','Trees occupy top vertical strata or layer of a forest, shrubs the second and herbs and grasses occupy the bottom layers.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'2','mcq','Shurbs - Top , Grasses- Second , Trees - Bottom.','Shurbs - Top , Trees - Second , Grasses - Bottom.','Grasses- Top , Shurbs - Second , Trees - Bottom.','Trees - Top , Shurbs - Second , Grasses - Bottom.','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(360116,'biology','The autotrophic components of pond include ?','Phytoplankton and Submerged Plants','The autotrophic components include the phytoplankton, some algae and the floating, submerged and marginal plants found at the edges','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'1','mcq','Phytoplankton and Zooplankton','Fungi','Submerged and Marginal Plants Only','Phytoplankton and Submerged Plants','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(360117,'biology','Which is / are true regarding ecosystem?-\n A. self sufficient unit\n\n B. cyclic exchange of materials between living beings and environment; \n\nC. only requirement is input of energy\n\nD.unidirectional movement of energy','A,B,C,D','Given following are the statements true about ecosystem- \na. Self sufficient unit\n\nb. Cyclic exchange of materials between living beings and environment\n\nc. Only requirement is input of energy.\n\nd.unidirectional movement of energy\n\nReference  : NCERT Class 12; Page no. 241','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'2','mcq','A,C','A,D','C only','A,B,C,D','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360118,'biology','Conversion of inorganic into organic material occurs with the help of the _____ energy.','Radiant','Conversion of inorganic into organic material with the help of the radiant energy of Sun by the autotrophs;','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'2','mcq','Electric','Radiant','Mechanical','Heat','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(360119,'biology','The consumers of pond include?','Zooplankton','The consumers are represented by the zooplankton, the free swimming and bottom dwelling forms','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'1','mcq','Zooplankton','Algea','Fungi','Phytoplankton','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(360120,'biology','The decomposers in pond are the?','Fungi','The decomposers are the fungi, bacteria and flagellates especially abundant in the bottom of the pond.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'2','mcq','Fungi','Phytoplankton','Algae','Frogs','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(360121,'biology','Who performs all the functions of any ecosystem and of the biosphere as a whole?','Both a and b','Autotrophs,consumers,decomposers performs all the functions of any ecosystem and of the biosphere as a whole.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'2','mcq','Decomposers','Consumers','Both a and b','Phytoplankton','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360122,'biology','Ecosystem has two components, which are they?','Biotic and Abiotic','Ecosystem consists of two types of components that are biotic and abiotic components.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'1','mcq','Plants and Animals','Weeds and Trees','Biotic and Abiotic','Frogs and Man.','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(360123,'biology','There is ___________  movement of energy towards the higher trophic levels and its dissipation and loss as heat to the environment.','Unidirectional','There is unidirectional movement of energy towards the higher trophic levels and its dissipation and loss as heat to the environment.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'1','mcq','Unidirectional','Bidirectional','Multidirectional','Straight','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(360124,'biology','What are the four major aspects of the ecosystem?','Productivity,Energy Flow, Decomposition, Nutrient Cycling','The components of the ecosystem are seen to function as a unit when you consider the following aspects: (i) Productivity; (ii) Decomposition; (iii) Energy flow; and (iv) Nutrient cycling.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'2','mcq','Productivity,Energy Flow, Decomposition, Nutrient Cycling','Productivity, Energy Pyramids, Nutrient Cycling, Consumption','Decomposition, Ecological Succession, Energy Flow, Consumption','Energy Flow, Biomass, Ecological Succession, Productivity','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(360125,'biology','Which of the following are abiotic components in a pond ecosystem?','Both A and B','The abiotic component is the water with all the dissolved inorganic and organic substances and the rich soil deposit at the bottom of the pond.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and B','Water','Soil deposits','Atmosphere','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(360126,'biology','Which of the following statements is true with respect to the pond ecosystem?','The solar input, the cycle of temperature, day-length and other climatic conditions regulate the rate of function of the entire pond.','The abiotic component is the water with all the dissolved inorganic and organic substances and the rich soil deposit at the bottom of the pond. The solar input, the cycle of temperature, day-length and other climatic conditions regulate the rate of function of the entire pond.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'2','mcq','The solar input, the cycle of temperature, day-length and other climatic conditions regulate the rate of function of the entire pond.','The solar input, the cycle of temperature, day-length and other climatic conditions cannot regulate the rate of function of the entire pond alone.','Only climatic conditions regulate the rate of function of the entire pond.','The rate of function of the pond is directly proportional to the amount of biotic components present in it.','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(360127,'biology','The correct sequence of structure in an ecosystem is ?','Productivity>Nutrient Cycle>Degradation','We will first look at the structure of the ecosystem, in order to appreciate the input (productivity), transfer of energy (food chain/web, nutrient cycling) and the output (degradation and energy loss).','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'2','mcq','Productivity>Nutrient Cycle>Degradation','Nutrient Cycle>Productivity>Degradation','Degradation>Nutrient cycle>Productivity','Nutrient Cycle>Degradation>productivity','NEW','12-241','12-205',NULL),
(360128,'biology','What is the range of sizes that ecosystems can vary between?','Small pond to a large forest','Ecosystem varies greatly in size from a small pond to a large forest or a sea.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'2','mcq','Small pond to a large forest','Small garden to a tall building','A mountain to a desert','A river to an ocean','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(360129,'biology','Which of the following is an example of an aquatic ecosystem?','Lake','Pond, lake, wetland, river and estuary are some examples of aquatic ecosystems.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'2','mcq','desert','grassland','tropical rainforest','Lake','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(360130,'biology','Why is a pond considered a valuable example for explaining complex interactions in aquatic ecosystems?','Because it is a self-sustainable unit and illustrates complex interactions','Pond is fairly a self-sustainable unit and rather simple example that explains even the complex interactions that exist in an aquatic ecosystem.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecosystem- Introduction, Structure and Function',NULL,'2','mcq','Because it lacks diversity of species','Because it is too small to support any interactions','Because it is a self-sustainable unit and illustrates complex interactions','Because it is too complex to study interactions','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(360201,'biology','A constant input of _____ energy is the basic requirement for any ecosystem to function and sustain.','Solar','The vast majority of energy that exists in food webs originates from the sun and is converted (transformed) into chemical energy by the process of photosynthesis in plants. A small proportion of this chemical energy is transformed directly into heat when compounds are broken down during resp​iration in plants.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Productivity',NULL,'1','mcq','Solar','Energy from Carbon Fuel','Wind','Soil','NEW','12-242','12-206','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/360201q.png'),
(360202,'biology','The rate of biomass production is called __________ .','Productivity','In ecology, the term productivity refers to the rate of generation of biomass in an ecosystem, usually expressed in units of mass per volume (unit surface) per unit of time, such as grams per square metre per day (g m−2 d−1). The unit of mass can relate to dry matter or to the mass of generated carbon.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Productivity',NULL,'2','mcq','Productivity','Consistency','Biomass Stability','Utility','NEW','12-243','12-207','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/360202q.png'),
(360203,'biology','________ production is defined as the amount of biomass or organic matter produced per unit area over a time period by plants during photosynthesis.','Primary','In ecology, primary production is the synthesis of organic compounds from atmospheric or aqueous  CO₂ .','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Productivity',NULL,'2','mcq','Primary','Secondary','Tertiary','Quarternary','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360204,'biology','Primary production is expressed in terms of?','Both A and B','Primary production is defined as the amount of biomass or organic matter produced per unit area over a time period by\nplants during photosynthesis. It is expressed in terms of weight (gm–2) orenergy (kcal m–2).','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Productivity',NULL,'2','mcq','gm⁻²','kcal m⁻²','Both A and B','gm⁻¹','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(360205,'biology','Productivity is expressed in terms of?','gm⁻² yr⁻¹ and (kcal m⁻² )yr⁻¹','In ecology, the term productivity refers to the rate of generation of biomass in an ecosystem, usually expressed in units of mass per volume (unit surface) per unit of time, such as grams per square metre per day (g m−2 d−1) or (kcalm^-2yr^-1).','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Productivity',NULL,'2','mcq','gm⁻²yr⁻¹','kcal m⁻¹yr⁻²','(kcal m⁻² )yr⁻¹','gm⁻² yr⁻¹ and (kcal m⁻² )yr⁻¹','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360206,'biology','_________ of an ecosystem is the rate of production of organic matter during photosynthesis.','Gross Primary Productivity','Gross primary productivity, or gpp, is the rate at which solar energy is captured in sugar molecules during photosynthesis (energy captured per unit area per unit time).','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Productivity',NULL,'2','mcq','Gross Primary Productivity','Net Primary Productivity','Secondary Productivity','Tertiary Productivity','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360207,'biology','Net primary productivity (npp) =_______?','Net primary productivity (npp) = the Gross primary productivity (gpp) - Respiration (r).','Your net productivity is equal to your gross productivity minus respiration, which is the same as the equation above that states the net primary productivity (npp) = the gross primary productivity (gpp) minus respiration (r).','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Productivity',NULL,'2','mcq','Net primary productivity (npp) = the Gross primary productivity (gpp) +  Respiration (r).','Net primary productivity (npp) = the Gross primary productivity (gpp) - Respiration (r).','Net primary productivity (npp)','Net primary productivity (npp) = Respiration (r) - Gross primary productivity (gpp)','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360208,'biology','_________ is the available biomass for the consumption to heterotrophs (herbivores and decomposers).','Net Primary Productivity','Net primary productivity, or npp, is gross primary productivity minus the rate of energy loss to metabolism and maintenance. In other words, it\'s the rate at which energy is stored as biomass by plants or other primary producers and made available to the consumers in the ecosystem.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Productivity',NULL,'2','mcq','Gross Primary Productivity','Respiration Losses','Net Primary Productivity','secondary productivity','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360209,'biology','_________ is defined as the rate of formation of new organic matter by consumers.','Secondary Productivity','Secondary productivity is the rate at which the consumers convert the chemical energy of their food into their own biomass is called as secondary productivity. The efficiency at which energy is transferred from one trophic level to another is called as ecological efficiency.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Productivity',NULL,'2','mcq','Secondary Productivity','Primary Productivity','Net Primary Productivity','Gross Primary Productivity','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360210,'biology','Primary Productivity depends on?','both a and b','Primary productivity depends on species inhabiting particular area, variety of environmental factors, and availability of nutrients and photosynthetic capacity of plants.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Productivity',NULL,'2','mcq','Species inhabiting particular area.','Variety of environmental factors.','both a and b','Human residing nearby','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360211,'biology','Primary productivity varies in different types of ecosystem.Given statement is true or false?','TRUE','Primary productivity varies in different types of ecosystem.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Productivity',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360212,'biology','The annual Net Primary Productivity of the whole biosphere  is approximately ____ billion tons (dry weight) of organic matter.','170','The annual net primary productivity of the whole biosphere  is approximately 170 billion tons (dry weight) of organic matter.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Productivity',NULL,'1','mcq','270','170','180','160','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360213,'biology','Ocean covers approximately ____ of the earth\'s surface.','70%','The area of the world ocean is about 361.9 million square kilometers (139.7 million square miles), which covers about 70.9% of earth\'s surface, and its volume is approximately 1.335 billion cubic kilometers (320.3 million cubic miles).','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Productivity',NULL,'1','mcq','70%','50%','30%','97%','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360214,'biology','The annual Net Primary Productivity of oceans is approximately','55 Billion Tons','The annual net primary productivity of oceans is approximately 55 billion tons (dry weight) of organic matter.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Productivity',NULL,'2','mcq','55 Billion Tons','170 Billion Tons','115 Billion Tons','60 Billion Tons','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360215,'biology','What are the types of productivity ?','Primary and secondary','Productivity can be divided into 2 types i.e primary and secondary.Primary productivity, in ecology, the rate at which energy is converted to organic substances by photosynthetic producers (photoautotrophs), which obtain energy and nutrients by harnessing sunlight, and chemosynthetic producers (chemoautotrophs), which obtain chemical energy through oxidation.\nSecondary productivity is rate of formation of new organic matter by consumers.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Productivity',NULL,'2','mcq','Primary and secondary','Simple and complex','Basic and complex','Net and Gross','NEW','12-243','12-207','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/360215q.png'),
(360216,'biology','Secondary productivity is rate of formation of new organic matter by','Consumer','Secondary productivity is rate of formation of new organic matter by consumers.Secondary productivity is the rate at which the consumers convert the chemical energy of their food into their own biomass. The efficiency at which energy is transferred from one trophic level to another is called as ecological efficiency.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Productivity',NULL,'2','mcq','Parasite','Consumer','Decomposer','Producer','NEW','12-243','12-207','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/360216q.png'),
(360217,'biology','Primary Productivity can be divided into namely?','gross primary productivity,net primary productivity.','Primary productivity productivity can be divided into gross primary productivity (gpp) and net primary productivity (npp).Primary productivity, in ecology, the rate at which energy is converted to organic substances by photosynthetic producers (photoautotrophs), which obtain energy and nutrients by harnessing sunlight, and chemosynthetic producers (chemoautotrophs), which obtain chemical energy through oxidation.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Productivity',NULL,'2','mcq','Gross primary productivity,secondary productivity','net primary productivity,respiration loss','gross primary productivity,net primary productivity.','net primary productivity,secondary productivity','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(360218,'biology','What is Primary productivity?','Is the rate of conversion of light to chemical energy in an ecosystem.','Primary productivity, in ecology, the rate at which energy is converted to organic substances by photosynthetic producers (photoautotrophs), which obtain energy and nutrients by harnessing sunlight, and chemosynthetic producers (chemoautotrophs), which obtain chemical energy through oxidation.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Productivity',NULL,'2','mcq','Is equal to the standing crop of an ecosystem.','Is greatest in freshwater ecosystems.','Is the rate of conversion of light to chemical energy in an ecosystem.','Is inverted in some aquatic ecosystems.','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360219,'biology','What is Secondary productivity?','Is the rate of biomass production in consumers.','Secondary productivity is the rate at which the consumers convert the chemical energy of their food into their own biomass. The efficiency at which energy is transferred from one trophic level to another is called as ecological efficiency.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Productivity',NULL,'2','mcq','Is measured by the standing crop.','Is the rate of biomass production in consumers.','Is greater than primary productivity.','Is 10% less than primary productivity.','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360220,'biology','Which of the following changes would not result in an increase in net primary production?','Increased latitude (moving from the equator toward the poles)','Increased latitude (moving from the equator toward the poles) would not result in an increase in net primary production.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Productivity',NULL,'3','mcq','Increased precipitation in an area','Increased soil fertility','Increased latitude (moving from the equator toward the poles)','Moving down a mountain to warmer temperatures','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360221,'biology','The amount of energy that is left to build bodies is termed as','Net productivity','Food produced by photosynthesis forms the base of most ecosystem food chains. Gross primary production (GPP) is the total rate at which material is produced and net primary production (NPP) is the rate at which material is accumulated in excess of respiration. In other words,NPP is GPP minus respiration. The amount of energy that is left to build bodies is termed as net productivity.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Productivity',NULL,'2','mcq','Gross productivity','Net productivity','Total productivity','Respiratory loss','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360222,'biology','Gross primary productivity plus respiration losses (R), is the net primary productivity (NPP).Given statement is true or false:','FALSE','Gross primary productivity minus respiration losses (r), is the net primary productivity (npp).','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Productivity',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360223,'biology','The rate of formation of new organic matter by rabbit in a grassland, is called :','Secondary productivity','Secondary productivity is the rate at which the consumers convert the chemical energy of their food into their own biomass is called as secondary productivity. The efficiency at which energy is transferred from one trophic level to another is called as ecological efficiency.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Productivity',NULL,'2','mcq','Net productivity','Secondary productivity','Net primary productivity','Gross primary productivity','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360224,'biology','What happens to a significant portion of Gross Primary Productivity (GPP) in ecosystems ?','It is utilized by plants in respiration','A considerable amount of GPP is utilized by plants in respiration.','Ecology>>Ecosystem>>Productivity',NULL,'1','mcq','It is lost as waste material','it is consumed by herbivores','It is utilized by plants in respiration','It is converted into secondary productivity','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360301,'biology','_________ break down complex organic matter into inorganic substances.','Decomposers','Decomposers break down dead, organic materials, detritivores—like millipedes, earthworms, and termites—eat dead organisms and wastes. ... Decomposers feed on dead things: dead plant materials such as leaf litter and wood, animal carcasses, and feces.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Decomposition',NULL,'2','mcq','Decomposers','Consumers','Heterotrophs','autotrophs','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360302,'biology','Decomposers break down complex organic matter into inorganic substances like  CO₂ , water and nutrients and this process is called ________ .','Decomposition','Decomposition is the process by which dead organic substances are broken down into simpler organic or inorganic matter such as  CO₂ , water, simple sugars and mineral salts.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Decomposition',NULL,'2','mcq','Decomposition','composition','consumption','mineralisation','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360303,'biology','________ is the raw material of decomposition.','Detritus','Detritus, in ecology, matter composed of leaves and other plant parts, animal remains, waste products, and other organic debris that falls onto the soil or into bodies of water from surrounding terrestrial communities.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Decomposition',NULL,'2','mcq','Detritus','Dead plant only','Dead animals only','fossil','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360304,'biology','Which of the following constitutes detritus?\ni)Dead plant remains such as leaves, bark, flowers etc.\n\nii)Dead remains of animals.\n\niii) Phytoplankton \n\niv)Zooplankton','i and ii','Dead plant remains such as leaves, bark, flowers etc., dead remains of animals and faecal matter constitutes detritus.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Decomposition',NULL,'2','mcq','i and iii','iii and iv','i and ii','ii and iv','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360305,'biology','The important steps in the process of decomposition is/are','both a and b','Detritivores (e.g, earthworm) break down detritus into smaller particles.\nThis process is called fragmentation. By the process of leaching, water-\nsoluble inorganic nutrients go down into the soil horizon and get precipitated\nas unavailable salts. Bacterial and fungal enzymes degrade detritus into\nsimpler inorganic substances. This process is called as catabolism','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Decomposition',NULL,'2','mcq','Fragmentation','Leaching and catabolism','both a and b','dehydration','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360306,'biology','_________ (e.g. earthworm) break down detritus into smaller particles.','Detritivores','Detritivores are heterotrophs that obtain nutrients by consuming detritus. There are many kinds of invertebrates, vertebrates and plants that carry out coprophagy. Detritivores (e.G. Earthworm) break down detritus into smaller particles.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Decomposition',NULL,'2','mcq','Decomposers','Detritivores','Consumers','parasites','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360307,'biology','Detritivores break down detritus into smaller particles. This process is called _________ .','Fragmentation','Fragmentation. It is the initial stage of decomposition. Fragmentation means the breakdown of detritus into smaller pieces by the detritivores.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Decomposition',NULL,'3','mcq','Fragmentation','Leaching','Humification','Mineralisation','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360308,'biology','By the process of ________, water soluble inorganic nutrients go down into the soil horizon and get precipitated as unavailable salts.','Leaching','The process of movement of water soluble inorganic nutrients into the soil horizon and get precipitated as unavailable salts is called leaching.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Decomposition',NULL,'3','mcq','Fragmentation','Leaching','Humification','Mineralisation','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360309,'biology','Bacterial and fungal enzymes degrade detritus into simple inorganic substances. This process is called ________.','Catabolism','Once the complex material is broken down into smaller particles and the inorganic nutrients are removed, it is time to convert the detritus into simpler inorganic compounds. This process is carried out by various fungal and bacterial enzymes by the process of catabolism.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Decomposition',NULL,'3','mcq','Catabolism','Fragmentation','Leaching','Mineralisation','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360310,'biology','_________ is a dark coloured amorphous substance, highly resistant to microbial action and undergoes decomposition at an extremely slow rate.','Humus','Humus is dark, organic material that forms in soil when plant and animal matter decays. When plants drop leaves, twigs, and other material to the ground, it piles up.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Decomposition',NULL,'3','mcq','Humus','Dentritus','Fragments','Minerals','NEW','12-244','12-208',NULL),
(360311,'biology','_________ leads to accumulation of a dark coloured amorphous substance called humus.','Humification','Humification is a process of formation of humic substances (organic matter that has reached maturity) decomposed from plant remains.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Decomposition',NULL,'2','mcq','Humification','Mineralisation','Decomposition','Fragmentation','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360312,'biology','The humus is further degraded by some microbes and release of inorganic nutrients occur by the process known as','Mineralisation','Mineralization in soil science is the decomposition of the chemical compounds in organic matter, by which the nutrients in those compounds are released in soluble inorganic forms that may be available to plants. Mineralization is the opposite of immobilization.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Decomposition',NULL,'2','mcq','Humification','Mineralisation','Fragmentation','Catabolism','NEW','12-244','12-208',NULL),
(360313,'biology','The rate of decomposition is controlled by','Both a and b','The rate of decomposition is controlled by chemical composition of detritus, climatic factors and the O₂ present in atmosphere.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Decomposition',NULL,'2','mcq','Chemical composition of detritus.','Climatic factors.','Both a and b','Nitrogen presence','NEW','12-244','12-208',NULL),
(360314,'biology','In a particular climatic condition, decomposition rate is slower if detritus is rich in','Lignin and chitin','In a particular climatic condition, decomposition rate is slower if detritus is rich in ligni and chitin.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Decomposition',NULL,'3','mcq','Lignin and chitin','Nitrogen','Water soluble substances like sugars','Both B and C.','NEW','12-244','12-208',NULL),
(360315,'biology','In a particular climatic condition, decomposition rate is quicker if detritus is rich in','Both Nitrogen and Water soluble substances like sugars.','In a particular climatic condition, decomposition rate is quicker if detritus is rich in nitrogen and water soluble substances like sugars.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Decomposition',NULL,'3','mcq','Lignin and chitin','Nitrogen','Water soluble substances like sugars','Both Nitrogen and Water soluble substances like sugars.','NEW','12-244','12-208','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/360315q.png'),
(360316,'biology','What is the effect of warm and moist environment on decomposition?','Favour decomposition.','Warm and moist environment favour decomposition.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Decomposition',NULL,'3','mcq','Favour decomposition.','Inhibit decomposition.','Does not affect decomposition.','Either B or C.','NEW','12-244','12-208',NULL),
(360317,'biology','In recycling of mineral elements within an ecosystem, the responsible direct causal organism are called','Decomposers','A decomposer is an organism that breaks down organic materials from dead organisms to obtain energy. These organisms are basically living recycling plants. Fungi, worms, and bacteria are all examples.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Decomposition',NULL,'2','mcq','Decomposers','Producers','Primary consumers','Secondary consumers','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360318,'biology','Which of the following is called as a detritivore?','An animal feeding on decaying organic matter.','Detritivores are heterotrophs that obtain nutrients by consuming detritus. There are many kinds of invertebrates, vertebrates and plants that carry out coprophagy. By doing so, all these detritivores contribute to decomposition and the nutrient cycles.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Decomposition',NULL,'2','mcq','An animal feeding on decaying organic matter.','An animal feeding on a plant.','A plant feeding on an animal.','An animal feeding on another animal.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360319,'biology','Selects the incorrect statement(s) - \nA. The factors affecting decomposition are chemical nature of detritus and the climatic factors.\n\nB. Detritus is rich in lignin and chitin decomposition is very fast\n\nC. Decomposition is very slow if the detritus is rich in nitrogen\n\nD. Detritus is the raw material for decomposition','B,C','Dead plant remains such as leaves, bark, flowers and dead remains of animals, including fecal matter, constitute detritus, which is the rawmaterial for decomposition. The rate of decomposition is controlled by chemical composition of detritus and climatic factors. In a particular climatic condition, decomposition rate is slower if detritus is rich in lignin and chitin, and quicker, if detritus is rich in nitrogen and water-soluble substances like sugars.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Decomposition',NULL,'3','mcq','B,C','A,D','A,B,C','A,B,C,D','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360320,'biology','Which worm is being referred to as the farmer’s ‘friend’?','earthworm','Earthworm being referred to as the farmer’s ‘friend’.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Decomposition',NULL,'3','mcq','tape worm','earthworm','silkworm','round worm','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360321,'biology','Low temperature and anaerobiosis','Both A and B.','Low temperature and anaerobiosis inhibit decomposition and result in build up of organic materials.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Decomposition',NULL,'3','mcq','Inhibit decomposition.','Result in build up of organic materials.','Does not affect decomposition.','Both A and B.','NEW','12-244','12-208',NULL),
(360322,'biology','Earthworms help in the ________?','both a and b','This is so because they help in the breakdown of complex organic matter as well as in loosening of the soil.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Decomposition',NULL,'3','mcq','breakdown of complex organic matter.','loosening of the soil.','both a and b','carbon cycling','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
