(360418,'biology','The amount of energy reaching an upper trophic level is determined by','Gross primary productivity and the efficiencies with which food energy is converted to biomass.','Organisms at each trophic level depend on those at the lower trophic level for their energy demands. Only 10 % of the energy is transferred to each trophic level from the lower trophic level. Gross primary productivity of an ecosystem is the rate of production of organic matter during photosynthesis. A considerable amount of GPP is utilised by plants in respiration. Gross primary productivity minus respiration losses (R), is the net primary productivity (NPP). GPP – R = NPP. Net primary productivity is the available biomass for the consumption\nto heterotrophs (herbiviores and decomposers).','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Energy Flow',NULL,'3','mcq','Net primary productivity and respiratory losses','Net primary productivity and the efficiencies with which food energy is converted to biomass.','Gross primary productivity only','Gross primary productivity and the efficiencies with which food energy is converted to biomass.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360419,'biology','In a terrestrial ecosystem, major producers are ?','both a and b','In a terrestrial ecosystem, major producers are herbaceous and woody plants.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Energy Flow',NULL,'2','mcq','woody plants','herbaceous','herbivores','both a and b','NEW','12-245','12-209',NULL),
