(360452,'biology','In terrestrial ecosystems, what characterizes the flow of energy in comparison to aquatic ecosystems?','A larger fraction of energy flows through the detritus food chain (DFC) than through the grazing food chain (GFC).','As against this, in a terrestrial ecosystem, a much larger fraction of energy flows through the detritus food chain than through the GFC.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Energy Flow',NULL,'2','mcq','More energy flows through the grazing food chain (GFC) than the detritus food chain (DFC).','The energy flow is similar in both terrestrial and aquatic ecosystems.','A larger fraction of energy flows through the detritus food chain (DFC) than through the grazing food chain (GFC).','Terrestrial ecosystems do not have a detritus food chain.','NEW','12-246','12-210',NULL),
(360453,'biology','What term is used to describe the natural interconnection of multiple food chains within an ecosystem?','Food web','The natural interconnection of food chains make it a food web.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Energy Flow',NULL,'2','mcq','Food circle','Food web','Food cycle','Food pyramid','NEW','12-246','12-210',NULL),
(360454,'biology','What happens to an organism when it dies in an ecosystem, providing an energy source for decomposers?','It is converted to detritus or dead biomass.','When any organism dies it is converted to detritus or dead    biomass that serves as an energy source for decomposers.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Energy Flow',NULL,'2','mcq','It becomes a primary producer.','It remains intact and unaltered.','It is converted to detritus or dead biomass.','It becomes a top predator in the food chain.','NEW','12-247','12-211',NULL),
(360455,'biology','In ecology, how is the biomass of a species typically expressed, and which measurement is considered more accurate?','Biomass is expressed in terms of weight, and dry weight is more accurate.','The biomass of a species is expressed in terms of fresh or dry weight. Measurement of biomass in terms of dry weight is more accurate.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Energy Flow',NULL,'2','mcq','Biomass is expressed in terms of volume, and fresh weight is more accurate.','Biomass is expressed in terms of height, and dry weight is more accurate.','Biomass is expressed in terms of weight, and fresh weight is more accurate.','Biomass is expressed in terms of weight, and dry weight is more accurate.','NEW','12-247','12-211',NULL),
(360456,'biology','In a grazing food chain, how many distinct levels or trophic positions can exist in nature?','Five levels: producer, herbivore, primary carnivore, secondary carnivore, and tertiary carnivore','In nature, it is possible to have so many levels – producer, herbivore, primary carnivore, secondary carnivore in the grazing food chain.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Energy Flow',NULL,'2','mcq','Three levels: producer, herbivore, and primary carnivore','Four levels: producer, herbivore, primary carnivore, and secondary carnivore','Five levels: producer, herbivore, primary carnivore, secondary carnivore, and tertiary carnivore','Two levels: producer and herbivore','NEW','12-247','12-211',NULL),
(360457,'biology','In ecological food chains and webs, what is the fundamental pattern where an organism feeds on another organism, which in turn becomes food for another?','Predator-prey interactions','Starting from the plants (or producers) food chains or rather webs are formed such that an animal feeds on a plant or on another animal and in turn is food for another. Therefore, Predator-Prey interaction is the fundamental pattern of food chains or webs.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Energy Flow',NULL,'2','mcq','Mutualism','Predator-prey interactions','Commensalism','Symbiosis','NEW','12-245','12-209',NULL),
