(360517,'biology','Energy at a lower trophic level is always_______ at a higher level.','more than','Energy at a lower trophic level is always more than at a higher level.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Pyramids',NULL,'2','mcq','more than','less than','remains constant','fluctuate','NEW','12-249','12-213',NULL),
(360518,'biology','Which of the following trophic levels would have the largest numbers of individuals?','Primary producers','Trophic levels of primary producers would have the largest numbers of individuals.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Pyramids',NULL,'1','mcq','Primary producers','Omnivores','Primary consumers','Opportunistic feeders','NEW','12-248','12-212',NULL),
(360519,'biology','Which of the following always has a "pyramidal" shape, that is, decreasing values at higher trophic levels?','Pyramids of energy only','Pyramids of energy only always has a "pyramidal" shape, that is, decreasing values at higher trophic levels.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Pyramids',NULL,'1','mcq','Pyramids of numbers only','Pyramids of biomass only','Pyramids of energy only','Both pyramids of biomass and pyramids of energy','NEW','12-249','12-213',NULL),
(360520,'biology','An inverted pyramid of _______ may occasionally be observed in ________ communities.','Biomass, Marine','An inverted pyramid of biomass may occasionally be observed in marine communities.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Pyramids',NULL,'2','mcq','Energy, Grassland','Energy, Forest','Biomass, Marine','Biomass, Grassland','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360521,'biology','The pyramid of energy is always upright for any ecosystem. This situation indicates the fact that','Herbivores have a better energy conversion efficiency than carnivores','The pyramid of energy is always upright for any ecosystem. This situation indicates the fact that herbivores have a better energy conversion efficiency than carnivores','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Pyramids',NULL,'2','mcq','Producers have the lowest energy conversion efficiency','Carnivores have a better energy conversion efficiency than herbivores','Energy conversion efficiency is the same in all trophic','Herbivores have a better energy conversion efficiency than carnivores','NEW','12-249','12-213',NULL),
(360522,'biology','In a food chain, the maximum population is that of','Producers','In a food chain, the maximum population is that of producers.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Pyramids',NULL,'1','mcq','Producers','Primary Consumers','Secondary consumers','Tertiary consumers','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360523,'biology','Graphic representation of biomass relationship between the producer and the consumers is called','Ecological pyramid','An ecological pyramid (also trophic pyramid, eltonian pyramid, energy pyramid, or sometimes food pyramid) is a graphical representation designed to show the biomass or bioproductivity at each trophic level in a given ecosystem.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Pyramids',NULL,'2','mcq','Ecological system','Ecological niche','Ecological pyramid','Trophic level','NEW','12-248','12-212',NULL),
(360524,'biology','The base of each pyramid represents the _______','producers','The base of each pyramid represents the producers','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Pyramids',NULL,'2','mcq','primary consumers','producers','teritiary consumers','decomposers','NEW','12-248','12-212',NULL),
(360525,'biology','The apex of each pyramid represents __________','teritiary consumers','The apex of each pyramid represents tertiary consumers','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Pyramids',NULL,'2','mcq','primary consumers','secondary consumers','teritiary consumers','producers','NEW','12-248','12-212',NULL),
(360526,'biology','How many types of ecological pyramids are present in ecosystem?','3','There are three types of ecological pyramids in ecosystem','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Pyramids',NULL,'2','mcq','2','3','4','5','NEW','12-248','12-212',NULL),
(360527,'biology','Which of the following ecological pyramids are usually studied??','all the above','The three types of ecological pyramids that are usually studied are \n(a) pyramid of number; \n\n(b) pyramid of biomass and\n\n (c) pyramid of energy.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Pyramids',NULL,'2','mcq','pyramid of number','pyramid of biomass','pyramid of energy.','all the above','NEW','12-248','12-212',NULL),
(360528,'biology','Can you work out how many trophic levels human beings function at in a food chain?','3','Organisms, especially the consumers can be placed on the food chain based on their food habits. Humans are omnivores,\nIf they feed on plants, they become primary consumersIf they feed on animals, they become secondary or tertiary consumers.\nTherefore, humans can function at a minimum of three trophic levels.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Pyramids',NULL,'2','mcq','2','3','4','1','NEW','12-249','12-213',NULL),
(360529,'biology','Primary producers convert only ___ per cent of the energy available to them into NPP.','1 %','In pyramid of biomass, there is a decrease in biomass at higher trophic levels. An ideal pyramid of energy. Observe that primary producers convert only 1% of the energy in the sunlight available to them into NPP','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Pyramids',NULL,'2','mcq','10 %','1 %','5 %','15 %','NEW','12-249','12-213',NULL),
(360530,'biology','What is true about expressing the food or energy relationship between organisms at different trophic levels?','The shape is similar, whether expressing food or energy relationships.','One gets a similar shape, whether you express the food or energy relationship between organisms at different trophic levels.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Pyramids',NULL,'2','mcq','The shape varies significantly based on ecosystem type.','The shape is always linear.','The shape is similar, whether expressing food or energy relationships.','The shape is unpredictable','NEW','12-247','12-211',NULL),
(360531,'biology','How many top carnivores can typically be supported in an ecosystem based on the production of nearly 6 million plants?','Three','Only three top-carnivores are supported in an ecosystem based on production of nearly 6 millions plants','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Pyramids',NULL,'2','mcq','One','Two','Three','Four','NEW','12-248','12-212',NULL),
(360532,'biology','What does the pyramid of biomass typically show regarding trophic levels?','It shows a sharp decrease in biomass at higher trophic levels.','Pyramid of biomass shows a sharp decrease in biomass at higher trophic levels','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Pyramids',NULL,'2','mcq','It remains constant across all levels.','It exhibits a sharp increase in biomass at higher trophic levels.','It shows a sharp decrease in biomass at higher trophic levels.','It has an inverted shape.','NEW','12-248','12-212',NULL),
(360533,'biology','In an ecosystem, why is it important to include all organisms at a particular trophic level in calculations of energy content, biomass, or numbers?','Generalizations may not be true if only a few individuals are considered.','Any calculations of energy content, biomass or numbers, has to include all organisms at that trophic level. No generalisations we make will be true if we take only a few individuals at any trophic level into account.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Pyramids',NULL,'2','mcq','Generalizations are more accurate with a small sample.','It simplifies ecological analysis.','Generalizations may not be true if only a few individuals are considered.','Generalizations are accurate even with a few individuals considered.','NEW','12-249','12-213',NULL),
(360534,'biology','What does a trophic level represent in an ecosystem?','A functional level','One must remember that the trophic level represents a functional level, not a species as such. A given species may occupy more than one trophic level in the same ecosystem at the same time; for example, a sparrow','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Pyramids',NULL,'2','mcq','A specific species','The position of an organism in the food chain','A functional level','The geographic location of an organism','NEW','12-249','12-213',NULL),
(360535,'biology','In most ecosystems, what is the relationship between producers, herbivores, and carnivores in terms of numbers and biomass?','Producers are more in number and biomass than herbivores, and herbivores are more in number and biomass than carnivores.','In most ecosystems, all the pyramids, of number, of energy and biomass are upright, i.e., producers are more in number and biomass than the herbivores, and herbivores are more in number and biomass than the carnivores.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Pyramids',NULL,'2','mcq','Carnivores outnumber herbivores','Herbivores outnumber producers','Producers have the highest biomass','Producers are more in number and biomass than herbivores, and herbivores are more in number and biomass than carnivores.','NEW','12-249','12-213',NULL),
(360536,'biology','When counting the number of insects feeding on a big tree in an ecosystem, what kind of pyramid would you typically get?','Inverted pyramid of biomass','There are exceptions to this generalisation: If you were to count the number of insects feeding on a big tree what kind of pyramid would you get? Now add an estimate of the number of small birds depending on the insects, as also the number of larger birds eating the smaller.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Pyramids',NULL,'2','mcq','Upright pyramid of numbers','Inverted pyramid of numbers','Upright pyramid of biomass','Inverted pyramid of biomass','NEW','12-249','12-213',NULL),
(360537,'biology','Why is the pyramid of energy always upright in ecosystems?','Energy is always lost as heat at each trophic level','Pyramid of energy is always upright, and can never be inverted, because when energy flows from a particular trophic level to the next trophic level, some energy is always lost as heat at each step.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Pyramids',NULL,'2','mcq','Energy is always lost as heat at each trophic level.','Energy is evenly distributed among trophic levels.','Energy is transferred efficiently from one trophic level to the next.','Energy remains cinstant at each trophic level','NEW','12-249','12-213',NULL),
(360601,'biology','Causes of succession include- \nA. climatic change\n\nB. one species altering the environment for the next species\n\nC. different species dispersal mechanism','A,B,C','Listed following are the causes of succession- a. Climatic change; b. One species altering the environment for the next species; c. Different species dispersal mechanism.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Succession',NULL,'2','mcq','A,C','A,B','A,B,C','B only','OLD',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360602,'biology','The gradual and fairly predictable change in the species composition of a given area is called :','ecological succession','The gradual and fairly predictable change in the species composition of a given area is called ecological succession.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Succession',NULL,'2','mcq','ecological succession','environmental succession','physical succession','chemical succession','OLD','12-250',NULL,NULL),
(360603,'biology','On reaching the climax community, the net productivity','Becomes stable','The climax community remains stable as long as the environment remains unchanged. So, on reaching climax, the net productivity becomes stable. Ecological succession is the appearance and disappearance of communities in regular succession in a particular area until a final stable community emerges.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Succession',NULL,'3','mcq','Continues to increase','Becomes halved','Becomes stable','Becomes zero','OLD','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(360604,'biology','Choose the correct order of succession levels in xerarch?','Lichen, moss stage, annual herb stage, perennial herb stage, shrub stage, forest','The order of succession levels in xerarch is lichen, moss stage, annual herb stage, perennial herb stage, shrub stage, forest.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Succession',NULL,'2','mcq','Lichen, moss stage, annual herb stage, perennial herb stage, shrub stage, forest','Annual herb stage, perennial herb stage, lichen, moss stage, shrub stage, forest','Perennial herb stage, annual herb stage, lichen, moss stage, shrub stage, forest','Shrub stage, forest, annual herb stage, perennial herb stage, lichen, moss stage.','OLD',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360605,'biology','Which creatures are direct or indirect food of all creatures on the ocean\'s surface?','Phytoplanktons','Phytoplanktons are direct or indirect food of all creatures on the ocean\'s surface.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Succession',NULL,'2','mcq','Protozoans','Phytoplanktons','Fish','Aquatic insects.','OLD',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360606,'biology','Both, hydrarch and xerarch successions lead to?','Medium water conditions','Hydrarch succession takes place in wet areas and the successional series progress from hydric to the mesic conditions. As against this, xerarch succession takes place in dry areas and the series progress from xeric to mesic conditions.Both, hydrarch and xerarch successions lead to medium water conditions.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Succession',NULL,'3','mcq','Medium water conditions','Xeric conditions','Highly dry conditions','Excessive wet conditions','OLD','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(360607,'biology','Which one of the following statements is correct for secondary succession?','It occurs on a deforested site.','Secondary succession begins in areas where natural biotic communities have been destroyed such as in abandoned farm lands, burned or cut forests, lands that have been flooded. Since some soil or sediment is present, succession is faster than primary succession.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Succession',NULL,'2','mcq','It is similar to primary succession except that it has a relatively fast pace.','It begins on a bare rock.','It occurs on a deforested site.','It follows primary succession.','OLD','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(360608,'biology','Based on the nature of the _____  succession of plants can be hydrarch or xerarch.','habitat','Based on the nature of the habitat – whether it is water (or very wet areas) or it is on very dry areas – succession of plants is called hydrarch or xerarch, respectively.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Succession',NULL,'3','mcq','habitat','growth','morphology','conditions','OLD','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(360609,'biology','In succession,_________is a crucial factor.','Time','In succession, time is a crucial factor.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Succession',NULL,'2','mcq','Time','Direction','Height','Space','OLD','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(360610,'biology','Which succession takes place in wet areas and the successional series progress from hydric to the mesic conditions.','Hydrarch succession','Hydrarch succession takes place in wet areas and the successional series progress from hydric to the mesic conditions.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Succession',NULL,'1','mcq','cyclic succession','Hydrarch succession','Xerarch succession','secondary succession','OLD','12-251',NULL,NULL),
