(360638,'biology','In which type of succession is the rate faster due to the presence of some soil or sediment?','Secondary succession','Since some soil or sediment is present, secondary succession is faster than primary succession','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Succession',NULL,'1','mcq','Primary succession','Secondary succession','Tertiary succession','Quaternary succession','OLD','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(360639,'biology','How do vegetational changes during succession affect animals in an ecosystem?','They decrease the food and shelter available to animals.','However, these vegetational changes in turn affect food and shelter for various types of animals.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Succession',NULL,'2','mcq','They have no impact on animals.','They decrease the food and shelter available to animals.','They increase the food and shelter available to animals.','They result in the extinction of animal species.','OLD','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(360640,'biology','What can convert a particular seral stage of succession to an earlier stage during primary or secondary succession?','Human-induced disturbances','At any time during primary or secondary succession, natural or human induced disturbances (fire, deforestation, etc.), can convert a particular seral stage of succession to an earlier stage.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Succession',NULL,'2','mcq','Climate change','Human-induced disturbances','Evolutionary processes','Animal predation','OLD','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(360641,'biology','In ecological succession, what type of community is ultimately formed after several stages?','Climax community','They are, with time, succeeded by higher plants, and after several more stages, ultimately a stable climax forest community is formed.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Succession',NULL,'2','mcq','Seral community','climax community','Pioneer community','Transitional community','OLD','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(360642,'biology','Under what conditions does a climax community remain stable in ecological succession?','When the environment remains unchanged','The climax community remains stable as long as the environment remains unchanged.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Succession',NULL,'2','mcq','When the environment remains unchanged','When frequent disturbances occur','When invasive species are introduced','When natural selection is active','OLD','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(360643,'biology','In what direction does a xerophytic habitat typically transform with time during ecological succession?','It becomes mesophytic.','With time the xerophytic habitat gets converted into a mesophytic one.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Succession',NULL,'1','mcq','It becomes more arid.','It remains unchanged','It becomes mesophytic','It becomes hydrophytic','OLD','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(360644,'biology','During primary succession, what happens to a water body over time?','It transforms into land.','With time the water body is converted into land during primary succession.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Succession',NULL,'2','mcq','It remains unchanged.','It becomes a desert.','It transforms into land.','It becomes an ocean','OLD','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(360645,'biology','In secondary succession, how does the presence of soil impact the rate of succession and reaching climax?','It speeds up succession, reaching climax more quickly.','Since soil is already there, the rate of succession is much faster and hence, climax is also reached more quickly.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Succession',NULL,'2','mcq','It slows down succession.','It has no impact on succession.','It speeds up succession, reaching climax more quickly.','It leads to the formation of a unique climax community.','OLD','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(360646,'biology','How would you describe the pace of primary succession, especially in reaching the climax stage?','It is very slow, possibly taking thousands of years.','What is important to understand is that succession, particularly primary succession, is a very slow process, taking maybe thousands of years for the climax to be reached.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Succession',NULL,'2','mcq','It is rapid, taking only a few years.','It is relatively quick, taking several decades.','It is very slow, possibly taking thousands of years.','It is unpredictable and varies greatly.','OLD','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(360647,'biology','What is a common feature of all forms of ecological succession, whether in water or on land?','They ultimately progress to a similar mesic climax community.','Another important fact is to understand that all succession whether taking place in water or on land, proceeds to a similar climax community – the mesic.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Ecological Succession',NULL,'2','mcq','They lead to desert formation.','They all end with a hydrophytic community.','They ultimately progress to a similar mesic climax community.','They all result in a decline in biodiversity.','OLD','12-252',NULL,NULL),
(360701,'biology','The phosphates remain outside the natural cycle for a long time','Both A and B.','The phosphates remain outside the natural cycle for a long time when they form compounds with metals and when they are incorporated in bone and teeth.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','When they form compounds with metals','When they are incorporated in bone and teeth','When the bodies of the organisms excrete and decompose','Both A and B.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360702,'biology','Organisms need a constant supply of nutrients to _____  various body functions.','both a and b','Organisms need a constant supply of nutrients to grow, reproduce and regulate various body functions.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','grow','regulate','both a and b','live','NEW','12-253',NULL,NULL),
(360703,'biology','The amount of nutrients, such as carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus, calcium, etc., present in the soil at any given time, is referred to as?','standing state','The amount of nutrients, such as carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus, calcium, etc., present in the soil at any given time, is referred to as the standing state.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','standing state','standing time','standing phase','standing crop','NEW','12-253',NULL,NULL),
(360704,'biology','The movement of nutrient elements through the various components of an ecosystem is called ?','nutrient cycling','The movement of nutrient elements through the various components of an ecosystem is called nutrient cycling.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','nutrient cycling','carbon cycling','phosphorus cycling','sedimentary cycling','NEW','12-253','12-214',NULL),
(360705,'biology','Another name of nutrient cycling is _________ cycles','biogeochemical cycles','Another name of nutrient cycling is biogeochemical cycles','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','biocycle','geocycle','biogeocycle','biogeochemical cycles','NEW','12-253',NULL,NULL),
(360706,'biology','Nutrient cycles are of how many types?','2','Nutrient cycles are of two types','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','2','3','4','5','NEW','12-253','12-214',NULL),
(360707,'biology','Nutrient cycles are of two types what are they?','gaseous,sedimentary','Nutrient cycles are of two types: (a) gaseous and (b) sedimentary.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','gaseous,hydrous','sedimentary,hydrous','gaseous,sedimentary','terrestrial,hydrous','NEW','12-253','12-214',NULL),
(360708,'biology','Reservoir for gaseous type of nutrient cycle exists in the ?','atmosphere','Reservoir for gaseous type of nutrient cycle (e.G., nitrogen, carbon cycle) exists in the atmosphere','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','earths crust','earths core','atmosphere','Ocean','NEW','12-253','12-214',NULL),
(360709,'biology','Reservior for sedimentary cycle is located in :','Earths crust','For the sedimentary cycle (e.g., sulphur and phosphorus cycle), the reservoir is located in earth’s crust.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','Earths core','Earths crust','atmosphere','Ocean','NEW','12-253',NULL,NULL),
(360710,'biology','Reservoir for gaseous type of nutrient cycle exists in the atmosphere it consists which cycles?\n A.Sulphur cycle \n\nB.Phosphorous cycle \n\nC.Nitrogen cycle \n\nD.Carbon cycle','C,D','Reservoir for gaseous type of nutrient cycle exists in the atmosphere it consists of nitrogen and carbon cycle','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','A,B','A,B,C,D','A,D','C,D','NEW','12-253','12-214',NULL),
(360711,'biology','For the sedimentary cycle, the reservoir is located in Earth’s crust. It consists of which cycles? \nA.Sulphur cycle \n\nB.Phosphorous cycle \n\nC.Nitrogen cycle \n\nD.Carbon cycle \n\nchoose the correct pair of option','A,B','For the sedimentary cycle, the reservoir is located in earth’s crust. It consists of phosphorous and sulphur cycle.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','A,B,C,D','A,C','A,B','A,D','NEW','12-253',NULL,NULL),
(360712,'biology','Which of the following biogeochemical cycles has a major reservoir in sedimentary rock?','Phosphorus / Sulfur cycle','In biogeochemical cycles, the major reservoir of carbon cycle is earth’s crust, oceans and living organisms whereas for nitrogen cycles, it  is the earth’s atmosphere. The major reservoir of phosphorus cycle as well as sulfur cycle is sedimentary rocks where phosphorus exists in the form of phosphate minerals in rocks.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','Carbon cycle','Nitrogen cycle','Phosphorus / Sulfur cycle','Includes More than one cycle given above','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360713,'biology','In biogeochemical cycles, elements that cycle fastest','Have a gaseous phase.','In biogeochemical cycles, elements that cycle fastest have a gaseous phase.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','Are found in organisms.','Are scarce.','Have a gaseous phase.','Do not become fixed into sediment.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360714,'biology','The function of the reservoir is to meet with the deficit which occurs due to imbalance in the rate of influx and efflux. Given the statement is true or false?','TRUE','The function of the reservoir is to meet with the deficit which occurs due to imbalance in the rate of influx and efflux.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL),
(360715,'biology','According to the composition of living organisms, _____  constitutes __  % of dry weight of organisms and is next only to ________?','carbon,49,water','According to the composition of living organisms, carbon constitutes 49 % of dry weight of organisms and is next only to water','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','carbon,79,water','carbon,69,soil','carbon,49,water','sulphur,50,soil','NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL),
(360716,'biology','Studying the total quantity of global carbon, we find how much % carbon is found dissolved in oceans?','71','Studying the total quantity of global carbon, we find that 71 % carbon is found dissolved in oceans.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','49','79','71','50','NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL),
(360717,'biology','________ also represent a reservoir of carbon.','fossil fuel','Fossil fuel also represent a reservoir of carbon.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','ocean','fossil fuel','atmosphere','earth crust','NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL),
(360718,'biology','Which of the environment is richest in free oxygen?','Atmosphere','Atmosphere is the environment richest in free oxygen.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','Salt water','Atmosphere','Warm fresh water','Cold Fresh water','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360719,'biology','The phosphorus cycle differs from the carbon cycle in that','The phosphorus cycle does not include a gaseous phase, whereas the carbon cycle does.','The phosphorus cycle differs from the carbon cycle in that the phosphorus cycle does not include a gaseous phase, whereas the carbon cycle does.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','Phosphorus does not enter living organisms, whereas carbon does.','The phosphorus cycle does not include a gaseous phase, whereas the carbon cycle does.','The phosphorus cycle includes a solid phase, whereas the carbon cycle does not.','The primary reservoir of the phosphorus cycle is the atmosphere, whereas the primary reservoir for the carbon cycle is in rock.','NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL),
(360720,'biology','Which of the following biogeochemical cycles is characterized by a form which is a major greenhouse gas?','Carbon cycle','Carbon has a gaseous phase as part of its cycle, occurring in the atmosphere as  CO₂  (CO₂ ) and methane (ch4), both greenhouse gases. Carbon enters the atmosphere through the respiration of living things, through fires that burn organic compounds, and by diffusion from the ocean.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','Carbon cycle','Nitrogen cycle','Phosphorus cycle','Sulfur cycle','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360721,'biology','Which of the following biogeochemical cycles lacks a gaseous phase?','Phosphorus cycle','The phosphorus cycle does not include a gaseous phase, resulting in no significant quantities of atmospheric phosphorus. Explanation: the phosphorus cycle does not contain an atmospheric phase, while both the sulfur and nitrogen cycles do.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','Carbon cycle','Nitrogen cycle','Phosphorus cycle','Sulfur cycle','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360722,'biology','Carbon cycling occurs through?','both a and b','Carbon cycling occurs through atmosphere, ocean and through living and dead organisms.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','atmosphere','ocean','rocks','both a and b','NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL),
(360723,'biology','According to one estimate 4 × 10¹³ kg of carbon is fixed annually in the biosphere through:','photosynthesis','According to one estimate  4 × 10¹³  kg of carbon is fixed annually in the biosphere through photosynthesis','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','respiration','decomposition','nutrient cycle','photosynthesis','NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL),
(360724,'biology','A considerable amount of carbon returns to the atmosphere as CO₂  through respiratory activities of the ?','both a and b','A considerable amount of carbon returns to the atmosphere as CO₂  through respiratory activities of the producers and consumers.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','producers','consumers','both a and b','carnivores','NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL),
(360725,'biology','________ contribute substantially to CO₂  pool by their processing of waste materials and dead organic matter of land or oceans.','decomposers','Decomposers also contribute substantially to CO₂  pool by their processing of waste materials and dead organic matter of land or oceans.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'1','mcq','producers','consumers','decomposers','carnivores','NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL);
