(360744,'biology','Which environmental factors regulate the rate of release of nutrients into the atmosphere?','All of the above','Environmental factors, e.g., soil, moisture, pH, temperature, etc., regulate the rate of release of nutrients into the atmosphere.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'2','mcq','Soil','pH','Moisture','All of the above','NEW','12-253',NULL,NULL),
(360745,'biology','How much carbon is fixed annually in the biosphere through photosynthesis?','4000 kg','According to one estimate 4 × 1013 kg of carbon is fixed annually in the biosphere through photosynthesis.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'2','mcq','4000 kg','400 kg','40,000 kg','40 kg','NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL),
(360746,'biology','In ecosystems, what varies in different kinds of ecosystems and on a seasonal basis?','Standing state','Standing state varies in different kinds of ecosystems and also on a seasonal basis','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'2','mcq','Standing state','Nutrient recycling','Environmental factors','Carbon dioxide levels','NEW','12-253',NULL,NULL),
(360747,'biology','What happens to nutrients in ecosystems, and how are they treated?','Nutrients are recycled indefinitely within ecosystems.','What is important is to appreciate that nutrients which are never lost from the ecosystems, rather they are recycled time and again indefinitely.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'2','mcq','Nutrients are lost from ecosystems and never returned.','Nutrients are recycled indefinitely within ecosystem','Nutrients are continuously exported from ecosystems','Nutrients are converted into enrgy and lost','NEW','12-253',NULL,NULL),
(360748,'biology','Which of the following environmental factors regulate the rate of nutrient release into the atmosphere?','Soil','Environmental factors, e.g., soil, moisture, pH, temperature, etc., regulate the rate of release of nutrients into the atmosphere.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'2','mcq','Atmospheric pressure','Soil','Ocean currents','Wind speed','NEW','12-253',NULL,NULL),
(360749,'biology','Which reservoir plays a role in regulating the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere?','Oceanic reservoir','This oceanic reservoir regulates the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'2','mcq','Underground aquifers','Forest canopies','Oceanic reservoir','Desert dunes','NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL),
(360750,'biology','What happens to some of the fixed carbon in the carbon cycle?','It is lost to sediments and removed from circulation.','Some amount of the fixed carbon is lost to sediments and removed from circulation.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'2','mcq','It is permanently stored in plants.','It is lost to sediments and removed from circulation.','It is released into the atmosphere through respiration.','it is absorbed by the roots of trees.','NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL),
(360751,'biology','How have human activities influenced the carbon cycle?','They have significantly increased the release of carbon dioxide into the atmosphere.','Human activities have significantly influenced the carbon cycle. Rapid deforestation and massive burning of fossil fuel for energy and transport have significantly increased the rate of release of carbon dioxide into the atmosphere','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'2','mcq','They have reduced the carbon dioxide levels in the atmosphere.','They have no impact on the carbon cycle.','They have significantly increased the release of carbon dioxide into the atmosphere.','They have slowed down the carbon cycle.','NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL),
(360752,'biology','What do many animals need large quantities of to make shells, bones, and teeth?','Phosphorus','Many animals also need large quantities of Phosphorus to make shells, bones and teeth.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'2','mcq','Nitrogen','Oxygen','Phosphorus','Hydrogen','NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL),
(360753,'biology','How do plants obtain phosphorus for growth?','By absorbing it through their roots','When rocks are weathered, minute amounts of these phosphates dissolve in soil solution and are absorbed by the roots of the plants','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'2','mcq','Through respiration','By absorbing it through their leaves','By consuming other plants','By absorbing it through their roots','NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL),
(360754,'biology','What is unique about the release of phosphorus into the atmosphere?','There is no respiratory release of phosphorus into the atmosphere.','Unlike the carbon cycle, there is no respiratory release of phosphorus into the atmosphere.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'2','mcq','It is released through plant respiration.','It is released through volcanic eruptions.','There is no respiratory release of phosphorus into the atmosphere.','It is released through oceanic processes.','NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL),
(360755,'biology','Approximately how much is the global gross national product (GNP)?','US $ 18 trillion','Researchers have put an average price tag of US $ 33 trillion a year on these fundamental ecosystems services, which are largely taken for granted because they are free. This is nearly twice the value of the global gross national product GNP which is (US $ 18 trillion).','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'2','mcq','US $ 6 trillion','US $ 12 trillion','US $ 18 trillion','US $ 24 trillion','NEW','12-255',NULL,NULL),
(360756,'biology','What is the approximate value of ecosystem services, relative to the global gross national product (GNP)?','It is nearly twice the GNP.','Researchers have put an average price tag of US $ 33 trillion a year on these fundamental ecosystems services, which are largely taken for granted because they are free. This is nearly twice the value of the global gross national product GNP which is (US $ 18 trillion).','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Nutrient Cycle and Ecosystem Services',NULL,'2','mcq','It is less than the GNP.','It is equal to GNP','It is nearly twice the GNP.','It is impossible to determine its value','NEW','12-255',NULL,NULL),
(360801,'biology','The term ecosystem was coined by','A. G. Tansley.','The term ecosystem was coined by A.G. Tansley in 1935.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','E. Haeckel','E.Warming','E.P. Odum','A. G. Tansley.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360802,'biology','The amount of nutrients, such as carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus and calcium present in the soil at any given time, is referred as :[NEET 2021]','Standing State','Standing state is the amount of inorganic material found in the environment. It can be carbon, phosphorous, nitrogen, etc. The non-living matter is represented by a standing state.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Standing crop','Standing State','Climax community','Climax','NEW','12-253',NULL,NULL),
(360803,'biology','Which one of the following is a characteristic feature of cropland ecosystem? \n[NEET1 2016]','Least genetic diversity','Cropland ecosystem is an artificial or man-made terrestrial ecosystem which is created and maintained by human beings for their maximum benefits. Therefore, they will have least genetic diversity.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Absence of weeds','Ecological succession','Absence of soil organisms','Least genetic diversity','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360804,'biology','Vertical distribution of different species occupying different levels in a biotic community,is known as?','Stratification.','Vertical distribution of different species occupying different levels is called stratification. For example, trees occupy top vertical strata or layer of a forest, shrubs the second and herbs and grasses occupy the bottom layers.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Zonation','Pyramid','Divergence','Stratification.','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(360805,'biology','In relation to Gross primary productivity and Net primary productivity of an ecosystem, which one of the following statements is correct? [NEET 2020]','Gross primary productivity is always more than Net primary productivity.','Gross primary productivity (gpp) is the rate of production of organic matter during photosynthesis. A considerable amount of gpp is utilised by plants in respiration. Gpp minus respiration losses (r) is the net primary productivity gpp – r = npp. Therefore, gpp is always more than npp.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Gross primary productivity is always less than Net primary productivity.','Gross primary productivity is always more than Net primary productivity.','Gross primary productivity and Net primary productivity are one and same.','There is no relationship between Gross primary productivity and Net primary productivity.','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360806,'biology','The mass of living material at a trophic level at a particular time is called [2015 Cancelled]','Standing crop','Standing crop is the total amount of living material in a specified population at a particular time, expressed as biomass (standing biomass) or its equivalent in terms of energy. The standing crop may vary at different times of the year; for example, in a population of deciduous trees between summer and winter.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Net primary productivity','Standing crop','Gross primary productivity','Standing state.','NEW','12-247','12-211',NULL),
(360807,'biology','In an ecosystem the rate of production of organic matter during photosynthesis is termed as [2015 Cancelled]','Gross primary productivity.','The amount of energy accumulation in green plants as biomass or organic matter per unit area over a time period is known as primary productivity. The rate of total capture of energy, or the rate of total production of organic material (biomass), is known as gross primary productivity.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Secondary productivity','Net productivity','Net primary productivity','Gross primary productivity.','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360808,'biology','Secondary productivity is rate of formation of new organic matter by [NEET 2013]','Consumers','Secondary productivity is rate of formation of new organic matter by consumers.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Consumers','Decomposers','Producers','Parasites.','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(311626,'biology','Select the incorrect statement with respect to Multiple Ovulation Embryo Transfer (MOET) Technology. [ Neet 2023(phase 2)]','(1) Fertilised eggs at 4 to 6 cells - stages are recovered non-surgically from super-ovulating cow and transferred to surrogate mother.','MOET or multiple ovulation and embryo transfer is a technique aimed at increasing the reproductive rate of animals and hence, increasing the herd size in a short time. In this technique, a female is administered with hormones having FSH-like activity. The higher number of eggs produced are fertilized via insemination and are recovered non-surgically and transferred to another female. It has been tried in animals like cattle, camel, rabbit, etc.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Fertilised eggs at 4 to 6 cells - stages are recovered non-surgically from super-ovulating cow and transferred to surrogate mother.','(2) It is used to increase herd size in a short time','(3) Cow is administered with hormones to induce \nsuper-ovulation','(4) Super-ovulating cow is either mated with elite bull or is artificially inseminated.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360809,'biology','Which one of the following processes during decomposition is correctly described? [NEET 2013]','Fragmentation – Carried out by organisms such as earthworm','Decomposition is the process in which decomposers break down complex organic matter into inorganic substances like  CO₂ , water and nutrients. The important steps in the process of decomposition are fragmentation, leaching, catabolism, humification and mineralisation. Detritivores (e.G., earthworm) break down detritus into smaller particles. This process is called fragmentation. By the process of leaching, water-soluble inorganic nutrients go down into the soil horizon and get precipitated as unavailable salts. Bacterial and fungal enzymes degrade detritus into simpler inorganic substances. This process is called as catabolism. Humification and mineralisation occur during decomposition in the soil. Humification leads to accumulation of a dark coloured amorphous substance called humus that is highly resistant to microbial action and undergoes decomposition at an extremely slow rate. The humus is further degraded by some microbes and release of inorganic nutrients occur by the process known as mineralisation.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Catabolism – Last step in the decomposition under fully anaerobic condition','Leaching – Water soluble inorganic nutrients rise to the top layers of soil','Fragmentation – Carried out by organisms such as earthworm','Humification – Leads to the accumulation of a dark coloured substance humus which undergoes microbial action at a very fast rate.','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360810,'biology','The primary producers of the deep-sea hydrothermal vent ecosystem are [NEET2 2016]','Chemosynthetic bacteria','Hydrothermal vents are cracks in the ocean floor that emit jets of hot water loaded with minerals and chemosynthetic bacteria. These bacteria are autotrophs that oxidise hydrogen sulphide in vent water to obtain energy which is used to produce organic material. These chemosynthetic bacteria are the primary producers and form the base of vent food webs. All vent animals ultimately depend on bacteria for food.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Green algae','Chemosynthetic bacteria','Blue-green algae','Coral reefs','NEW','12-245','12-209',NULL),
(360811,'biology','Most animals that live in deep oceanic waters are [2015]','Detritivores','Benthos is the community of organisms which live on, in, or near the sea bed, also known as the benthic zone. As no light is available at this zone of sea, the energy source for deep benthic ecosystems is often organic matter from higher up in the water column which drifts down to the depths. This dead and decaying matter sustains the benthic organisms, and therefore, most organisms in benthic zone, i.E., in deep oceanic waters, are scavengers or detritivores.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Tertiary consumers','Detritivores','Primary consumers','Secondary consumers','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360812,'biology','If 20 J of energy is trapped at producer level, then how much energy will be available to peacock as food in the following chain? Plant → Mice → Snake → Peacock [2014]','0.02 J','Herbivores are eaten by primary carnivores. Only 10% of the herbivores productivity is utilized for raising productivity of primary carnivores. The rest is consumed in ingestion, respiration, maintenance of body heat and other activities. Higher carnivores similarly are able to retain only 10% of energy present in primary carnivores. It is called 10% law which was proposed by lindemann. Accordingly, if plant trapped 20 j of energy, mice will have 2 j, snake will have 0.2 j and hence, peacock will have 0.02 j of energy.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','0.02 J','0.002 J','0.2 J','0.0002 J','NEW','12-245','12-209',NULL),
(360813,'biology','Which of the following is a primary consumer in maize field ecosystem? [Karnataka NEET 2013]','Grasshopper','Primary consumers are herbivorous organisms that feed on producers. Carnivores are termed secondary, tertiary, etc., consumers depending upon their position in food chain. In food chain on land, grasshopper is a herbivore (primary consumer) while wolf and lion are carnivores. Phytoplanktons are producers in aquatic food chains.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Grasshopper','Wolf','Phytoplankton','Lion','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360814,'biology','When man eats fish which feeds on zooplanktons which have eaten small plants, the producer in this chain is [Karnataka NEET 2013]','Small plants','The transfer of food energy from producers to consumer through a series of organisms with repeated eating and being eaten is known as food chain. Green plants are always the first link of food chain because they alone are capable of synthesising organic food by using light energy by photosynthesis. The logical sequence of a food chain is : producer → consumers → decomposer i.E. Small plants(primary producer) → zooplanktons(primary consumer) → fish(secondary consumer) → man(tertiary consumer).','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Small plants','Fish','Man','Zooplankton','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360815,'biology','Match the trophic levels with their correct species examples in grassland ecosystem. [NEET 2020]','Fourth trophic level-Vulture,Second trophic level-Rabbit,First trophic level-Grass,Third trophic level-Crow','Fourth trophic level-vulture,second trophic level-rabbit,first trophic level-grass,third trophic level-crow','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','match','Fourth trophic level,Vulture','Second trophic level,Rabbit','First trophic level,Grass','Third trophic level,Crow','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360816,'biology','Which of the following ecological pyramids is generally inverted? [NEET 2019]','Pyramid of biomass in a sea','Pyramid of biomass in an aquatic ecosystem (e.G., pond, sea) is usually inverted because the biomass of fishes exceeds the biomass of phytoplanktons.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Pyramid of biomass in a sea','Pyramid of numbers in grassland','Pyramid of energy','Pyramid of biomass in a forest','NEW','12-249','12-213',NULL),
(360901,'biology','What type of ecological pyramid would be obtained with the following data? [NEET 2018]\n\nSecondary consumer : 120 g; \n\nPrimary consumer : 60 g; \n\nPrimary producer : 10 g','Inverted pyramid of biomass','The given data depicts the inverted pyramid of biomass, usually found in aquatic ecosystem. This can be understood by taking an example of a pond in which phytoplankton (10g) represents the producer. Small fishes (60g) feeding upon these phytoplankton represent primary consumers which are further eaten by the big fishes (120g) representing secondary consumers.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Inverted pyramid of biomass','Pyramid of energy','Upright pyramid of numbers','Upright pyramid of biomass','NEW','12-249','12-213',NULL),
(360902,'biology','Which ecosystem has the maximum biomass? [NEET 2017]','Forest ecosystem','Forest ecosystem has the maximum biomass than grassland ecosystem, pond ecosystem, lake ecosystem','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Grassland ecosystem','Pond ecosystem','Lake ecosystem','Forest ecosystem','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360903,'biology','Which of the following would appear as the pioneer organisms on bare rocks? [NEET1 2016]','Lichens','The bare rocky habitat is extremely hostile to living beings. There is no water, as the substratum does not absorb rain water. There is no nutrient holding mechanism. Plants cannot grow on these rocks. The first inhabitants or pioneers of such a habitat are usually lichens as they are resistant to desiccation and extreme temperature.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Mosses','Green algae','Lichens','Liverworts','NEW','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(360904,'biology','During ecological succession [NEET 2015]','The gradual and predictable change in species composition occurs in a given area','Biotic or ecological succession is the natural development of a series of biotic communities at the same site, one after the other till a climax community develops which does not change further because it is in perfect harmony with the environment of the area. The change is orderly and sequential. There is a parallel change in the physical environment.During an ecological succession, the number and types of animals goes on increasing with time. The community that is in near equilibrium with the environment is called climax community. The establishment of a new biotic community is slow in its primary phase but gradually becomes fast in its secondary phase.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','The numbers and types of animals remain constant','The changes lead to a community that is in near equilibrium with the environment and is called pioneer community','The gradual and predictable change in species composition occurs in a given area','The establishment of a new biotic community is very fast in its primary phase.','NEW','12-250',NULL,NULL),
(360905,'biology','Secondary succession takes place on/in [NEET 2015 Cancelled]','Degraded forest.','Secondary succession (subsere) is the biotic succession that occurs in an area which become secondarily bare due to the destruction of community previously present there. Secondary succession starts from previously built up substrata with already existing living matter. The action of any external force, such as a sudden change in climatic factors, biotic intervention, fire, etc., had resulted in the destruction of previous community. Thus, area became devoid of living matter but its substratum, is built up. It has organic matter, so is biologically fertile and thus the successions are comparatively more rapid.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Newly created pond','Newly cooled lava','Bare rock','Degraded forest.','NEW','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(360906,'biology','In which of the following both pairs have correct combination? [2015]','Gaseous nutrient cycle, Sedimentary nutrient cycle - Carbon and Nitrogen, Sulphur and Phosphorus','Biogeochemical cycles are of two types: gaseous and sedimentary. In gaseous nutrient cycles, the materials involved in circulation between biotic and abiotic components of biosphere are gases or vapours and the reservoir pool is atmosphere or hydrosphere, e.G., carbon, hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen, water. In sedimentary nutrient cycles, materials involved in circulation between biotic and abiotic components of biosphere are non-gaseous and the reservoir pool is lithosphere, e.G., phosphorus, calcium, magnesium. Sulphur has both sedimentary and gaseous nutrient cycles.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Gaseous nutrient cycle, Sedimentary nutrient cycle - Nitrogen and Sulphur, Carbon and Phosphorus','Gaseous nutrient cycle, Sedimentary nutrient cycle - Sulphur and Phosphorus, Carbon and Nitrogen','Gaseous nutrient cycle, Sedimentary nutrient cycle - Carbon and Nitrogen, Sulphur and Phosphorus','Gaseous nutrient cycle, Sedimentary nutrient cycle - Carbon and Sulphur, Nitrogen and Phosphorus','NEW','12-253','12-214',NULL),
(360907,'biology','Given below is a simplified model of phosphorus cycling in a terrestrial ecosystem with four blanks (A-D). Identify the blanks. Refer to the diagram (3601)','A-Detritus,B-Rock Minerals,C-Producers,D-Litter Fall','When rocks are weathered, minute amounts of these phosphates dissolve in soil solution and are absorbed by the roots of the plants . Herbivores and other animals obtain this element from plants. The waste products and the dead organisms are decomposed by phosphate-solubilising bacteria releasing phosphorus.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','match','A,Detritus','B,Rock Minerals','C,Producers','D,Litter Fall','NEW','12-254',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/360907q.png'),
(360908,'biology','Natural reservoir of phosphorus is [NEET 2013]','Rock','The reservoir pool of phosphorus is in phosphate rocks while the cycling pool is soil and water for terrestrial and aquatic ecosystems respectively. Small amount of phosphate is always being added to the cycling pool through weathering of rocks. Phosphate is generally found in soil in combination with calcium, iron and aluminium. Atmosphere or gaseous cycle is absent. Phosphate circulates in the abiotic environment in lithosphere as well as hydrosphere.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Rock','Fossils','Sea water','Animal bones.','NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL),
(360909,'biology','Match the following and select the correct option. [2014]','Earthworm-Detritivore,Succession-Pioneer species,Ecosystem service-Pollination,Population growth-Natality','Detrivores are heterotrophs that obtain nutrients by consuming detritus or decomposing plant and animal parts  as well as faeces. Earthworm is a detrivore.\n In succession, pioneer species like lichen, algae, and fungi, as well as other abiotic factors  like wind and water start to \'normalize\' the habitat.\n Pollination is a type of ecosystem service which enhances the service provided by insects/birds through behavioral interaction.\n Natality is the crude birth rate or specific birth rate and is the greatest influence on a population\'s increase.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','match','Earthworm,Detritivore','Succession,Pioneer species','Ecosystem service,Pollination','Population growth,Natality','NEW','12-246','12-210',NULL),
(360910,'biology','In the equation GPP – R = NPP (R) represents : [NEET 2021]','Respiration losses','The overall productivity of a system can be found in an equation where the net primary production, or npp, is equal to the gross primary production, or gpp, minus the respiration, or r. The formula is the npp = gpp - r. The npp is the overall efficiency of the plants in the ecosystem.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Respiration losses','Radiant energy','Retardation factor','Environmental factor','NEW','12-243','12--207',NULL),
(360911,'biology','The amount of nutrients, such as carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus and calcium present in the soil at any given time, is referred as :[NEET 2021]','Standing state','The weight or quantity of living matter in an area at a given moment is the standing crop. The amount of inorganic nutrients like carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus, etc., present in soil at that time is called standing state.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Standing crop','Climax','Climax community','Standing state','NEW','12-253',NULL,NULL),
(360912,'biology','Which of the following statements is not correct?[NEET 2021]','Pyramid of biomass in sea is generally upright.','The pyramid of biomass in the sea is inverted because the amount of biomass is least at the base of the pyramid and the amount of biomass is maximum at the apex of the pyramid.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Pyramid of numbers in a grassland ecosystem is upright','Pyramid of biomass in sea is generally inverted.','Pyramid of biomass in sea is generally upright.','Pyramid of energy is always upright.','NEW','12-249','12-213',NULL),
(311627,'biology','Select the incorrect statement with respect to inbreeding of animals. [ RE NEET 2022]','(3) It decreases homozygosity.','The incorrect statement regarding inbreeding is inbreeding selects harmful recessive genes that reduce fertility and productivity.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) It is used for evolving pure lines in cattle.','(2) It helps in accumulation of superior genes and elimination of less desirable genes.','(3) It decreases homozygosity.','(4) It exposes harmful recessive genes that are eliminated by selection.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360913,'biology','Given below are two statements:\n\nStatement I: Decomposition is a process in which the detritus is degraded into simpler substances by\nmicrobes.\n\nStatement II: Decomposition is faster if the detritus is rich in lignin and chitin.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below; \n[NEET 2022]','Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect','Decomposition is the process by which decomposers breakdown complex organic matter into inorganic\nsubstances.\nThe rate of decomposition is controlled by chemical composition of detritus and climatic factors.\nDecomposition is slower if detritus is rich in lignin and chitin and quicker, if detritus is rich in nitrogen\nand water soluble substances like sugars.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect','Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360914,'biology','Which one of the following will accelerate phosphorus cycle? [NEET 2022]','Weathering of rocks','Phosphorus cycle is a sedimentary cycle. Reservoir pool of phosphorus in ecosystem is the earth’s crust or lithosphere. Weathering of rocks accelerate phosphorus cycle','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Burning of fossil fuels','Volcanic activity','Weathering of rocks','Rain fall and storms','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360915,'biology','Which one of the following is not a function of an ecosystem? [2013]','Stratification','Four important functional aspects of the ecosystem are \n(i) productivity \n\n(ii) decomposition, \n\n(iii) energy flow and \n\n(iv) nutrient cycling.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Energy flow','Decomposition','Productivity','Stratification','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(360916,'biology','How much portion of the Photosynthetically Active Radiation (PAR) is captured by the plants? [AIIMS 2016]','2 – 10%','The main source of energy for an ecosystem is the radiant energy or light energy derived from the sun. 50% of the total solar radiation that falls on earth is photosynthetically active radiation (par). The light energy is converted into chemical energy in the form of sugar by photosynthesis. 6H₂O + 6CO₂  + light o 6c6h12o6  + 6O₂ plants utilize 2-10% of par in photosynthesis.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','5 – 10%','7 – 10%','8 – 10%','2 – 10%','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(360917,'biology','Arrange the following ecosystems in increasing order of mean NPP (Tonnes / ha / year); \nA. Tropical deciduous forest; \n\nB. Temperate coniferous forest; \n\nC. Tropical rain forest; \n\nD. Temperate deciduous forest [AIIMS 2017]','B < D < A < C','Net primary productivity (npp) is the biomass or storage of energy by green plants. It is equal to the gross primary productivity minus loss due to respiration. The productivity generally increases from polar regions toward the tropics, because of the increasing sunlight and temperature.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','B < A < D < C','D < B < A < C','A < C < D < B','B < D < A < C','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360918,'biology','Assertion : Net primary productivity is gross primary productivity minus respiration. \n\nReason : Secondary productivity is produced by heterotrophs. [AIIMS 2013]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Net primary productivity is the rate of organic matter built up or stored by producers in their bodies per unit time and area. Net productivity is equal to gross primary productivity minus loss due to respiration and other reasons. Rate of increase in energy containing organic matter or biomass by heterotrophs or consumers per unit time and area is known as secondary productivity.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360919,'biology','Assertion : Net primary productivity is gross primary productivity minus respiration. \n\nReason : Secondary productivity is produced by heterotrophs. [AIIMS 2016]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Net primary productivity is the rate of organic matter build up or stored by producers in their bodies per unit time and area. Net productivity is equal to gross primary productivity minus loss due to respiration and other reasons. Rate of increase in energy containing organic matter or biomass by heterotrophs or consumers per unit time and area is known as secondary productivity.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(360920,'biology','Assertion : In a food chain, members of successive higher levels are fewer in number. \n\nReason : Number of organisms at any trophic level depends upon the availability of organisms which serve as food at the lower level. [2003, 2017]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','When food is made available, automatically the next higher level of organism in the hierarchy should increase. This is because when the forest cover got depleted it led to the increase in the number of endangered species. If the deer population is more, it automatically leads to an increase in the tiger population.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(360921,'biology','The pioneer species in a hydrarch succession are : [ RE NEET 2022]','(3) Phytoplanktons','Xerarch succession takes place in dry areas and the series progress from xeric to mesic conditions. Lichens are the pioneer species in xerarch succession, which secrete acids that disslove rocks. It helps in weathering and soil formation.\nHydrarch succession takes place in shallow waters. The pioneer species in hydrarch succession are phytoplanktons','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Free-floating angiosperms','(2) Submerged rooted plants','(3) Phytoplanktons','(4) Filamentous algae','NEW','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(360922,'biology','The species that come to appear in bare area are called  [ RE NEET 2022]','(1) Pioneer species','Pioneer community is the first biotic community on a bare area. Plants that are first to invade the bare area are called pioneer species. Lichens are the pioneers in lithosere and phytoplanktons are the pioneers in hydrosere.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Pioneer species','(2) Invasive species','(3) Competitive species','(4) Species of seral community','NEW','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(360923,'biology','All successions irrespective of the habitat proceed to which type of climax community ? [ RE NEET -2022]','(2) Mesic','1. Succession in plants occur as xerarch or hydrarch.\n2. Xerarch succession takes place in dry areas and the series then progresses from xeric to mesic conditions. The hydrarch succession takes place in water bodies and the series then progress from hydric to mesic conditions. \n3. Both these conditions lead to medium water conditions (mesic).','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Xeric','(2) Mesic','(3) Hydrophytic','(4) Edaphic','NEW','12-252',NULL,NULL),
(360924,'biology','The amount of nutrients such as carbon, nitrogen, potassium and calcium present in the soil at any given time is referred to as : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(3) Standing state','The amount of nutrients such as carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus, calcium, etc., present in the soil at any \ngiven time is referred to as the standing state. Standing crop is the amount of living material present in different trophic levels at a given time.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Humus','(2) Detritus','(3) Standing state','(4) Standing crop','NEW','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(360925,'biology','Match List-I with List-II. \nList-I List-II\n(A) Hydrarch succession (I) Gradual change in\nthe species \ncomposition \n(B) Xerarch succession (II) Faster and climax\nreached quickly\n(C) Ecological succession (III) Lichens to mesic\nconditions \n(D) Secondary succession (IV) Phytoplankton to\nmesic conditions \nChoose the correct answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(4) (A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)','(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)','Ecology >> Ecosystem >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) (A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(I)','(2) (A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)','(3) (A)-(I), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)','(4) (A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(361001,'biology','Which of the following statement is correct regarding Ecosystem','An ecosystem can be visualised as a physiological unit of nature, where living organisms interact among themselves and also with the surrounding physical environmen','An ecosystem can be visualised as a functional( it can be said as physiological )unit of nature, where living organisms interact among themselves and also with the surrounding physical environment. Ecosystem varies greatly in size from a small pond to a large forest or a sea. Many ecologists regard the entire biosphere as a global ecosystem, as a composite of all local ecosystems on earth. Since this system is too much big and complex to be studied at one time, it is convenient to divide it into two basic categories, namely the terrestrial and the aquatic. Forest, grassland and desert are some examples of terrestrial ecosystems; pond, lake, wetland, river and estuary are some examples of aquatic ecosystems. Crop fields and an aquarium may also be considered as man-made ecosystems.\nReference   NCERT class 12 biology chapter 14 page 241','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','An ecosystem can be visualised as a physiological unit of nature, where living organisms interact among themselves and also with the surrounding physical environmen','Ecosystem is always a small unit of land constituting the larger biosphere','Since, it is easy to study the biosphere as a single unit, we need not study them as water bodies and terrestrial compartments','Forest, grassland and desert are some examples of aquatic ecosystems; pond, lake, wetland, river and estuary are some examples of terrestrial ecosystems,Crop fields and an aquarium may also be considered under natural ecosystems.','NEW','12-241','12-205',NULL),
(361002,'biology','Which of the following statement is incorrect regarding Structure of an ecosystem','Trees occupy bottom vertical strata or layer of a forest, shrubs the second and herbs and grasses occupy the topmost layers','Interaction of biotic and abiotic components result in a physical structure that is characteristic for each type of ecosystem. Identification and enumeration of plant and animal species of an ecosystem gives its species composition. Vertical distribution of different species occupying different levels is called stratification. For example, trees occupy top vertical strata or layer of a forest, shrubs the second and herbs and grasses occupy the bottom layers.\nReference   NCERT class 12 biology chapter 14 page 241','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Interaction of biotic and abiotic components result in a physical structure that is characteristic for each type of ecosystem','Identification and enumeration of plant and animal species of an ecosystem gives its species composition.','Vertical distribution of different species occupying different levels is called stratification.','Trees occupy bottom vertical strata or layer of a forest, shrubs the second and herbs and grasses occupy the topmost layers','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(361003,'biology','Which of the following statement is incorrect regarding Structure of an ecosystem','A pond is not a fairly self-sustainable unit and rather simple example that cannot explain the complex interactions that exist in an aquatic ecosystem.','The components of the ecosystem are seen to function as a unit when you consider the following aspects:\n(i) productivity;\n(ii) decomposition;\n(iii) energy flow; and\n(iv) nutrient cycling.\nTo understand the ethos of an aquatic ecosystem let us take a small pond as an example. This is fairly a self-sustainable unit and rather simple example that explain even the complex interactions that exist in an aquatic ecosystem. A pond is a shallow water body in which all the above mentioned four basic components of an ecosystem are well exhibited. The abiotic component is the water with all the dissolved inorganic and organic substances and the rich soil deposit at the bottom of the pond.\nReference   NCERT class 12 biology chapter 14 page 242','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','The components of the ecosystem are seen to function as a unit when you consider  productivity,decomposition, energy flow and nutrient cycling.','A pond is not a fairly self-sustainable unit and rather simple example that cannot explain the complex interactions that exist in an aquatic ecosystem.','A pond is a shallow water body in which productivity, nutrient cycling, decomposition and energy flow are well exhibited.','The abiotic component of a pond is the water with all the dissolved inorganic and\norganic substances and the rich soil deposit at the bottom of the pond.','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(311628,'biology','Match List-I with List-II. \nList-I List-II \n(A) Auxin (I) Promotes female flower formation in cucumber \n(B) Gibberellin (II) Overcoming apical dominance \n(C) Cytokinin (III) Increase in the length of grape stalks \n(D) Ethylene (IV) Promotes flowering in \npineapple \nChoose the correct answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(2) (A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)','(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(1) (A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(III)','(2) (A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)','(3) (A)-(I), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)','(4) (A)-(III), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311701,'biology','Which of the following is the pair of biofertilizers?\n[2014]','Azolla and BGA','Azolla and BGA are biofertilizer which\nincreases the fertility of soil.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Azolla and BGA','Nostoc and legume','Rhizobium and grasses','Salmonella and E.coli','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(361004,'biology','Assertion : A pond is a self-sustainable unit and can explain the complex interactions that exist in an aquatic ecosystem.\n\nReason :  A pond is a water body exhibiting all four basic components of an ecosystem and has both biotic and abiotic components.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','To understand the ethos of an aquatic ecosystem let us take a small pond as an example. This is fairly a self-sustainable unit and rather simple example that explain even the complex interactions that exist in an aquatic ecosystem. A pond is a shallow water body in which all the above mentioned four basic components of an ecosystem are well exhibited. The abiotic component is the water with all the dissolved inorganic and organic substances and the rich soil deposit at the bottom of the pond. Reference   NCERT class 12 biology chapter 14 page 242','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(361005,'biology','Which of the following statement is incorrect regarding Pond as an ecosystem','The autotrophic components include the zooplankton, the free swimming and bottom dwelling forms ,while the consumers are represented by the phytoplankton, some algae and the floating, submerged and marginal plants found at the edges.','The solar input, the cycle of temperature, day-length and other climatic conditions regulate the rate of function of the entire pond. The autotrophic components include the phytoplankton, some algae and the floating, submerged and marginal plants found at the edges. The consumers are represented by the zooplankton, the free swimming and bottom dwelling forms. The decomposers are the fungi, bacteria and flagellates especially abundant in the bottom of the pond. This system performs all the functions of any ecosystem and of the biosphere as a whole, i.E., conversion of inorganic into organic material with the help of the radiant energy of the sun by the autotrophs; consumption of the autotrophs by heterotrophs; decomposition and mineralisation of the dead matter to release them back for reuse by the autotrophs, these event are repeated over and over again. There is unidirectional movement of energy towards the higher trophic levels and its dissipation and loss as heat to the environment.\nReference   NCERT class 12 biology chapter 14 page 242','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','The solar input, the cycle of temperature, day-length and other climatic conditions regulate the rate of function of the entire pond.','The autotrophic components include the zooplankton, the free swimming and bottom dwelling forms ,while the consumers are represented by the phytoplankton, some algae and the floating, submerged and marginal plants found at the edges.','The decomposers are the fungi, bacteria and flagellates especially abundant in the bottom of the pond','A pond contains autotrophs for conversion of inorganic into organic material with the help of sun, heterotrophs to consume autotrophs,decomposers to perform decomposition and nutrient cycling , all in an unidirectional mode with dissipation of energy as heat loss to the environment','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(361006,'biology','Which of the following statement regarding Productivity is correct','GPP = NPP + R','A constant input of solar energy is the basic requirement for any ecosystem to function and sustain. Primary production is defined as the amount of biomass or organic matter produced per unit area over a time period by\nplants during photosynthesis. It is expressed in terms of weight (gm–2 ) or energy (kcal m–2). The rate of biomass production is called productivity.\nIt is expressed in terms of gm–2 yr –1 or (kcal m–2) yr–1 to compare the productivity of different ecosystems. It can be divided into gross primary productivity (gpp) and net primary productivity (npp). Gross primary productivity of an ecosystem is the rate of production of organic matter during photosynthesis. A considerable amount of gpp is utilised by plants in respiration. Gross primary productivity minus respiration losses (r), is the net primary productivity (npp). Gpp – r = npp, in other words gpp = npp + r;\nReference   NCERT class 12 biology chapter 14 page 243','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','A constant input of solar energy is not very necessary for any ecosystem to function and sustain.','Primary production is defined as the amount of biomass or organic matter produced per unit area over a time period by plants during photosynthesis and is expressed  in terms of gm–2 yr –1 or (kcal m–2) yr–1','The rate of biomass production is called productivity.\nIt is expressed in terms of  weight (gm–2 ) or energy (kcal m–2)to compare the productivity of different ecosystems.','GPP = NPP + R','NEW','12-242','12-206',NULL),
(361007,'biology','Which of the following statement regarding Productivity is correct','Primary productivity depends on the plant species inhabiting a particular area and on a variety of environmental factors, availability of nutrients and photosynthetic capacity of plants.','Net primary productivity is the available biomass for the consumption to heterotrophs (herbiviores and decomposers). Secondary productivity is defined as the rate of formation of new organic matter by consumers. Primary productivity depends on the plant species inhabiting a particular area. It also depends on a variety of environmental factors, availability of nutrients and photosynthetic capacity of plants. Therefore, it varies in different types of ecosystems. The annual net primary productivity of the whole biosphere is approximately 170 billion tons (dry weight) of organic matter. Of this, despite occupying about 70 percent of the surface, the productivity of the oceans are only 55 billion tons. Rest of course, is on land. \nReference   NCERT class 12 biology chapter 14 page 243','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Gross primary productivity is the available biomass for the consumption to heterotrophs (herbiviores and decomposers).','Secondary productivity is defined as the rate of formation of new organic matter by producers','Primary productivity depends on the plant species inhabiting a particular area and on a variety of environmental factors, availability of nutrients and photosynthetic capacity of plants.','The annual net primary productivity of the whole biosphere is approximately 155 billion tons (dry weight) of organic matter,despite occupying about 70 percent of the surface, the productivity of the oceans are only 70 billion tons.','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(361008,'biology','Assertion: Primary Productivity varies with different ecosystems\n\nReason: Primary productivity is independent of plant species inhabiting a particular area,environmental factors, availability of nutrients and photosynthetic capacity of plants.','Assertion is true, but reason is false','Primary productivity depends on the plant species inhabiting a particular area. It also depends on a variety of environmental factors, availability of nutrients and photosynthetic capacity of plants. Therefore, it varies in different types of ecosystems.Reference   NCERT class 12 biology chapter 14 page 243','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(311702,'biology','Hisardale is a new breed of sheep developed in Punjab by one of the breeding technique in which superior male of one breed is mated with superior females of another breed. Identify the breeding technique from the option given below. [2016]','Cross breeding','Hisardale is a new breed of sheep\ndeveloped in Punjab by crossing Bikaneri\newes and Marino rams. Cross breeding is a\nmethod in which superior male of one breed\nis mated with superior females of another\nbreed. It allows the desirable qualities of\ntwo different breeds to be combined.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Inbreeding','Out crossing','Out breeding','Cross breeding','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(361009,'biology','Assertion: Primary Productivity of oceans is less than half of total despite being occupying 70 percent of the surface\n\nReason : The photosynthetic capacity of higher green plant species inhabiting land is much higher than phytoplanktons.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The annual net primary productivity of the whole biosphere is approximately 170 billion tons (dry weight) of organic matter. Of this, despite occupying about 70 percent of the surface, the productivity of the oceans are only 55 billion tons. This is because the photosynthetic capacity of higher green plants are greater as compared to phytoplanktons, which are the primary producers in the ocean.,the rest of course, is on land. Reference   NCERT class 12 biology chapter 14 page 243','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(361010,'biology','Which of the following is correct regarding decomposition','The important steps in the process of decomposition are fragmentation, leaching, catabolism, humification and mineralisation.','You may have heard of the earthworm being referred to as the farmer’s ‘friend’. This is so because they help in the breakdown of complex organic matter as well as in loosening of the soil. Similarly, decomposers break down complex organic matter into inorganic substances like  CO₂ , water and nutrients and the process is called decomposition. Dead plant remains such as leaves, bark, flowers and dead remains of animals, including fecal matter, constitute detritus, which is the raw material for decomposition. The important steps in the process of decomposition are fragmentation, leaching, catabolism, humification and mineralisation.\nReference   NCERT class 12 biology chapter 14 page 243','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','The earthworm being referred to as the farmer’s ‘friend as they become manure when they die and do not cause any change in the texture of the soil','Decomposers break down complex inorganic matter into organic substances like  CO₂ , water and nutrients and the process is called decomposition','Dead plant remains such as leaves, bark, flowers and dead remains of animals, except fecal matter, constitute detritus, which is the raw material for decomposition.','The important steps in the process of decomposition are fragmentation, leaching, catabolism, humification and mineralisation.','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(361011,'biology','Assertion: The earthworm being referred to as the farmer’s ‘friend’\n\nReason: Earthworms help in the breakdown of complex organic matter as well as in loosening of the soil, the more nutrients are available to the plants','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The earthworm being referred to as the farmer’s ‘friend’. This is so because they help in the breakdown of complex organic matter as well as in loosening of the soil.\nReference   NCERT class 12 biology chapter 14 page 243','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(361012,'biology','Which of the following is incorrect regarding decomposition','By the process of leaching, water insoluble inorganic nutrients go down into the soil horizon and get precipitated as unavailable salts.','Detritivores (e.G., earthworm) break down detritus into smaller particles.This process is called fragmentation. By the process of leaching, water-soluble inorganic nutrients go down into the soil horizon and get precipitated as unavailable salts. Bacterial and fungal enzymes degrade detritus into simpler inorganic substances. This process is called as catabolism. It is important to note that all the above steps in decomposition operate simultaneously on the detritus. Humification and mineralisation occur during decomposition in the soil.Reference   NCERT class 12 biology chapter 14 page 243','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Detritivores (e.g., earthworm) break down detritus into smaller particles by fragmentation. By the process of leaching, water-soluble inorganic nutrients go down into the soil horizon and get precipitated as unavailable salts.','By the process of leaching, water insoluble inorganic nutrients go down into the soil horizon and get precipitated as unavailable salts.','Bacterial and fungal enzymes degrade detritus into simpler inorganic substances by catabolism.','Humification and mineralisation occur during decomposition in the soil.','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(361013,'biology','Assertion: Fragmentation,Leaching and Catabolism are steps in decomposition that operate simultaneously on the detritus.\n\nReason: Humification and mineralisation occur during decomposition in the soil.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Detritivores (e.G., earthworm) break down detritus into smaller particles.This process is called fragmentation. By the process of leaching, water-soluble inorganic nutrients go down into the soil horizon and get precipitated as unavailable salts. Bacterial and fungal enzymes degrade detritus into simpler inorganic substances. This process is called as catabolism. It is important to note that all the above steps in decomposition operate simultaneously on the detritus so, as to quicken the process and each step is interdependent in decomposition. Humification and mineralisation occur during decomposition in the soil.Reference   NCERT class 12 biology chapter 14 page 243','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-243','12-207',NULL),
(361014,'biology','Which of the following is correct regarding decomposition','Decomposition rate is slower if detritus is rich in lignin and chitin, and quicker, if detritus is rich in nitrogen and water-soluble substances like sugars, and climatic factors as well.','Humification leads to to accumulation of a dark coloured amorphous substance called humus that is highly resistant to microbial action and undergoes decomposition at an extremely slow rate. Being colloidal in nature it serves as a reservoir of nutrients. The humus is further degraded by some microbes and release of inorganic nutrients occur by the process known as mineralisation. Decomposition is largely an oxygen-requiring process. The rate of decomposition is controlled by chemical composition of detritus and climatic factors. In a particular climatic condition, decomposition rate is slower if detritus is rich in lignin and chitin, and quicker, if detritus is rich in nitrogen and water-soluble substances like sugars. Temperature and soil moisture are the most important climatic factors that regulate decomposition through their effects on the activities of soil microbes. Warm and moist environment favour decomposition whereas low temperature and anaerobiosis inhibit decomposition resulting in build up of organic materials.Reference   NCERT class 12 biology chapter 14 page 243 and 244','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Humification leads to to accumulation of a dark coloured amorphous substance called mucus that is highly resistant to microbial action and undergoes decomposition at an extremely slow rate.','Being colloidal in nature the humus serves as a reservoir of nutrients,degraded by some microbes and released by the process of fragmentation','Decomposition rate is slower if detritus is rich in lignin and chitin, and quicker, if detritus is rich in nitrogen and water-soluble substances like sugars, and climatic factors as well.','Temperature and soil moisture are the least important climatic factors that regulate decomposition through their effects on the activities of soil microbes. Warm and moist environment inhibit decomposition whereas low temperature and aerobiosis favour decomposition resulting in build up of organic materials','NEW','12-244','12-208',NULL),
(361015,'biology','Which of the following statements regarding Energy Flow is correct','Plants capture only 2-10 % of the PAR and this small amount of energy sustains the entire living world.','Except for the deep sea hydro-thermal ecosystem, sun is the only source of energy for all ecosystems on earth. Of the incident solar radiation less than 50 % of it is photosynthetically active radiation (par). We know that plants and photosynthetic bacteria (autotrophs), fix sun’s radiant energy to make food from simple inorganic materials. Plants capture only 2-10 % of the par and this small amount of energy sustains the entire living world. So, it is very important to know how the solar energy captured by plants flows through different organisms of an ecosystem. All organisms are dependent for their food on producers, either directly or indirectly. So you find unidirectional flow of energy from the sun to producers and then to consumers. Reference   NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 245','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','All ecosystems including deep sea hydro-thermal ecosystem, depend on sun as the only source of Energy','Of the incident solar radiation lmore than 50 % of it is photosynthetically active radiation (PAR), plants and photosynthetic bacteria (autotrophs), fix Sun’s radiant energy to make food from simple inorganic materials.','Plants capture only 2-10 % of the PAR and this small amount of energy sustains the entire living world.','All organisms are not dependent for their food on producers, either directly or indirectly','NEW','12-245','12-209',NULL),
(361016,'biology','Assertion: Ecosystems are exempt from the Second Law of thermodynamics.\n\nReason :  They need a constant supply of energy to synthesise molecules they require, to counteract the universal tendency toward increasing disorderliness.','Assertion is false, but Reason is true','Further, ecosystems are not exempt from the second law of thermodynamics. They need a constant supply of energy to synthesisethe molecules they require, to counteract the universal tendency toward increasing disorderliness. Reference   NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 245','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is false','Assertion is false, but Reason is true','NEW','12-245','12-209',NULL),
(361017,'biology','With regards to Energy flow, which of the following statements is correct','No energy that is trapped into an organism remains in it for ever.','The green plant in the ecosystem are called producers. In a terrestrial ecosystem, major producers are herbaceous and woody plants. Likewise, producers in an aquatic ecosystem are various species like phytoplankton, algae and higher plants.\nYou have read about the food chains and webs that exist in nature. Starting from the plants (or producers) food chains or rather webs are formed such that an animal feeds on a plant or on another animal and in turn is food for another. The chain or web is formed because of this interdependency. No energy that is trapped into an organism remains in it for ever. The energy trapped by the producer, hence, is either passed on to a consumer or the organism dies.Reference   NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 245','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','The green plant in the ecosystem are called consumers because they consume large quantities of water and CO₂  in photosynthesis process','A food web is formed because of indepedency','No energy that is trapped into an organism remains in it for ever.','The energy trapped by the consumer, is passed on to a producer or the organism dies','NEW','12-245','12-209',NULL),
(361018,'biology','With regards to Energy flow, which of the following statements is incorrect','Primary consumers are carnivores and secondary consumers are herbivores','Death of organism is the beginning of the detritus food chain/web.All animals depend on plants (directly or indirectly) for their food needs. They are hence called consumers and also heterotrophs. If they feed on the producers, the plants, they are called primary consumers, and if the animals eat other animals which in turn eat the plants (or their produce) they are called secondary consumers. Likewise, you could have tertiary consumers too. Obviously the primary consumers will be herbivores. Some common herbivores are insects, birds and mammals in terrestrial ecosystem and molluscs in aquatic ecosystem.Reference   NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 245','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Death of organism is the beginning of the detritus food chain/web.','All animals depend on plants for their food needs, and they become a part of heterotrophs or consumers','Primary consumers are carnivores and secondary consumers are herbivores','Examples of primary consumers are insects, birds and mammals in terrestrial ecosystem and molluscs in aquatic ecosystem','NEW','12-245','12-209',NULL),
(361019,'biology','With regards to Energy flow, which of the following statements is correct','Grass-->Goat--> Man is an example of a simple grazing Food chain','The consumers that feed on these herbivores are carnivores, or more correctly primary carnivores (though secondary consumers). Those animals that depend on the primary carnivores for food are labelled secondary carnivores. A simple grazing food chain (gfc) is depicted. The detritus food chain (dfc) begins with dead organic matter. Reference   NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 245 and 246','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','The consumers that feed on herbivores are secondary carnivores','Animals that depend on the primary carnivores for food are labelled as tertiary carnivores.','Grass-->Goat--> Man is an example of a simple grazing Food chain','The grazing food chain (GFC) begins with dead organic matter.','NEW','12-245','12-209',NULL),
(361020,'biology','With regard to Detritus Food chain which of the following statement is incorrect','In an aquatic ecosystem, DFC is the major conduit for energy flow.','The detritus food chain (dfc) begins with dead organic matter. It is made up of decomposers which are heterotrophic organisms, mainly fungi and bacteria. They meet their energy and nutrient requirements by degrading dead organic matter or detritus. These are also known as saprotrophs (sapro: to decompose). Decomposers secrete digestive enzymes that breakdown dead and waste materials into simple, inorganic materials, which are subsequently absorbed by them . In an aquatic ecosystem, gfc is the major conduit for energy flow.Reference   NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 246','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','The Detritus Food Chain is made up of decomposers which are heterotrophic organisms, mainly fungi and bacteria.','Saprotrophs meet their energy and nutrient requirements by degrading dead organic matter or detritus.','Decomposers secrete digestive enzymes that breakdown dead and waste materials into simple, inorganic materials, which are subsequently absorbed by them','In an aquatic ecosystem, DFC is the major conduit for energy flow.','NEW','12-246','12-210',NULL),
(361021,'biology','With regards to Energy Flow, select the correct option','In a terrestrial ecosystem, a much larger fraction of energy flows through the detritus food chain than through the GFC.','In an aquatic ecosystem, gfc is the major conduit for energy flow, as against this, in a terrestrial ecosystem, a much larger fraction of energy flows through the detritus food chain than through the gfc. Detritus food chain may be connected with the grazing food chain at some levels: some of the organisms of dfc are prey to the gfc animals, and in a natural ecosystem, some animals like cockroaches, crows, etc., are omnivores.These natural interconnection of food chains make it a food web.\nReference:   NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 246','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','In a terrestrial ecosystem, a much larger fraction of energy flows through the detritus food chain than through the GFC.','Detritus food chain is never connected with the grazing food chain at any levels','Most of the organisms of GFC are prey to the DFC animals,','Omnivores are the reason behind formation of food chains','NEW','12-246','12-210',NULL),
(361022,'biology','Assertion: Omnivores in an ecosystem lead to formation of Food webs\n\nReason: Natural interconnection of food chains, by an organism belonging to more than one level make it a food web.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Detritus food chain may be connected with the grazing food chain at some levels: some of the organisms of dfc are prey to the gfc animals, and in a natural ecosystem, some animals like cockroaches, crows, etc., are omnivores.These natural interconnection of food chains make it a food web. Reference   NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 246','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is false','Assertion is false, but Reason is true','NEW','12-246','12-210',NULL),
(361023,'biology','With regards to trophic level, choose the incorrect answer','The amount of energy increases at each successive trophic levels.','Organisms occupy a place in the natural surroundings or in a community according to their feeding relationship with other organisms. Based on the source of their nutrition or food, organisms occupy a specific place in the food chain that is known as their trophic level. Producers belong to the first trophic level, herbivores (primary consumer) to the second and carnivores (secondary consumer) to the third .The important point to note is that the amount of energy decreases at\nsuccessive trophic levels. Reference   NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 246 and 247','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Organisms occupy a place in the natural surroundings or in a community according to their feeding relationship with other organisms.','Organisms occupy a specific place in the food chain,known as their trophic level based on their source of food and nutrition','Producers belong to the first trophic level, herbivores (primary consumer) to the second and carnivores (secondary consumer) to the third','The amount of energy increases at each successive trophic levels.','NEW','12-246','12-210',NULL),
(361024,'biology','Assertion : Measurement of biomass in terms of dry weight is more accurate\n\nReason : Dry weight is less dependant on environmental and physiological conditions and provides better insight','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Measurement of biomass in terms of dry weight is more accurate. Why? \nBiomass in terms of dry weight is more accurate as wet weight may vary based on the physiological and environmental conditions of the species. Hence, dry weight is considered to be a better alternative to provide insights into ecosystem functioning and productivity\nReference:  NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 247','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is false','Assertion is false, but Reason is true','NEW','12-247','12-211',NULL),
(361025,'biology','With regards to Energy Flow, select the incorrect answer','Organisms at each trophic level depend on those at the higher trophic level for their energy demands.','The important point to note is that the amount of energy decreases at successive trophic levels. When any organism dies it is converted to detritus or dead biomass that serves as an energy source for decomposers.\nOrganisms at each trophic level depend on those at the lower trophic level for their energy demands. Each trophic level has a certain mass of living material at a particular time called as the standing crop. The standing crop is measured as the mass of living organisms (biomass) or the number in a unit area. The biomass of a species is expressed in terms of fresh or dry weight. Measurement of biomass in terms of dry weight is more accurate.\nReference: NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 247','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','When any organism dies it is converted to detritus or dead biomass that serves as an energy source for decomposers.','Organisms at each trophic level depend on those at the higher trophic level for their energy demands.','Each trophic level has a certain mass of living material at a particular time called as the standing crop, measured as biomass/unit area','The biomass of a species is expressed in terms of dry weight usually, which is more accurate.','NEW','12-247','12-211',NULL),
(361026,'biology','Assertion: The number of trophic levels in the grazing food chain is restricted.\n\nReason: Energy transfers follows 10% law, where only 10% energy is gained in a higher trophic level in addition to the original energy in the lower trophic level','Assertion is true, but reason is false','The number of trophic levels in the grazing food chain is restricted as the transfer of energy follows 10 % law – only 10 % of the energy is transferred to each trophic level from the lower trophic level. In nature, it is possible to have so many levels – producer, herbivore, primary carnivore, secondary carnivore in the grazing food chain \nReference: NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 247','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is false','Assertion is false, but Reason is true','NEW','12-247','12-211',NULL),
(51044,'chemistry','What is the shape of the graph that is drawn for the relationship between pressure and volume','Hyperbola','According to Boyle\'s law, Pressure (P) and Volume (V) are inversely proportional to one another. \nSo the graph between P and 1/V will be a straight line. But the graph between P and V will be a hyperbola.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 140; Paragraph  No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Circle','Hyperbola','Circular','Straight line','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(361027,'biology','Assertion : Only 10 % of the energy is transferred to each trophic level from the lower trophic level\n\nReason : Remaining energy is either used up by the organism, or dissipated in the form of heat','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The number of trophic levels in the grazing food chain is restricted as the transfer of energy follows 10 % law – only 10 % of the energy is transferred to each trophic level from the lower trophic level. In nature, it is possible to have so many levels – producer, herbivore, primary carnivore, secondary carnivore in the grazing food chain \nReference: NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 247, also refer to figure 14.3','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is false','Assertion is false, but Reason is true','NEW','12-247','12-211',NULL),
(361028,'biology','With regards to Ecological pyramids, which of the following statements is true','Only three top-carnivores are supported in an ecosystem based on production of nearly 6 millions plants in a grassland ecosystem','The base of a pyramid is broad and it narrows towards the apex. One gets a similar shape, whether you express the food or energy relationship between organisms at different trophic levels. This, relationship is expressed in terms of number, biomass or energy. The base of each pyramid represents the producers or the first trophic level while the apex represents tertiary or top level consumer.Pyramid of biomass shows a sharp decrease in biomass at higher trophic levels, only three top-carnivores are supported in an ecosystem based on production of nearly 6 millions plants in a grassland ecosystem.Inverted pyramid of biomass-small standing crop of phytoplankton supports large standing crop of zooplankton. Any calculations of energy content, biomass or numbers, has to include all organisms at that trophic level, \nReference:  NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 247 and 248 and refer to all images in page 248','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only three top-carnivores are supported in an ecosystem based on production of nearly 6 millions plants in a grassland ecosystem','Pyramid of biomass shows a sharp increase in biomass at higher trophic levels','Erect pyramid of biomass is obtained when small standing crop of phytoplankton supports large standing crop of zooplankton','Any calculations of energy content, biomass or numbers, has to include only the top predators at that trophic level.','NEW','12-247','12-211',NULL),
(361029,'biology','With regards to Ecological pyramids, which of the following statements is incorrect','A given species may occupy only one trophic level in the same ecosystem at the same time','The three types of ecological pyramids that are usually studied are (a) pyramid of number; (b) pyramid of biomass and (c) pyramid of energy.One must remember that the trophic level represents a functional level, not a species as such. A given species may occupy more than one trophic level in the same ecosystem at the same time; for example, a sparrow is a primary consumer when it eats seeds, fruits, peas, and a secondary consumer when it eats insects and worms.\nReference:  NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 248 and 249.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','The three types of ecological pyramids that are usually studied are pyramid of number, of biomass and of energy.','Each trophic level represents a functional level, not a species as such.','A given species may occupy only one trophic level in the same ecosystem at the same time','Human beings belong to many trophic levels as they are omnivores.','NEW','12-248','12-212',NULL),
(361030,'biology','With regards to Ecological pyramids, which of the following statements is true','In most ecosystems, all the pyramids, of number, of energy and biomass are upright','In most ecosystems, all the pyramids, of number, of energy and biomass are upright, i.E., producers are more in number and biomass than the herbivores, and herbivores are more in number and biomass than the carnivores. Also energy at a lower trophic level is always more than at a higher level.The pyramid of biomass in sea is generally inverted because the biomass of fishes far exceeds that of phytoplankton.Pyramid of energy is always upright, can never be inverted, because when energy flows from a particular trophic level to the next trophic level,some energy is always lost as heat at each step. Each bar in the energy pyramid indicates the amount of energy present at each trophic level in a given time or annually per unit area.\nReference:  NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 249','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','In most ecosystems, all the pyramids, of number, of energy and biomass are upright','The pyramid of biomass in sea is generally inverted because the biomass of phytoplanktons far exceeds that of fishes','Pyramid of energy is always inverted, can never be erect.','Each bar in the energy pyramid indicates the amount of energy present at each trophic level in a given time or annually per unit volume.','NEW','12-249','12-213',NULL),
(361031,'biology','Assertion: In most ecosystems, all the pyramids, of number, of energy and biomass are upright\n\nReason : The quantity of producers is often more than consumers, and energy is always higher at lower trophic levels.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','In most ecosystems, all the pyramids, of number, of energy and biomass are upright, i.E., producers are more in number and biomass than the herbivores, and herbivores are more in number and biomass than the carnivores. Also energy at a lower trophic level is always more than at a higher level.The pyramid of biomass in sea is generally inverted because the biomass of fishes far exceeds that of phytoplankton.\nReference:  NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 249','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is false','Assertion is false, but Reason is true','NEW','12-249','12-213',NULL),
(361032,'biology','Assertion: The pyramid of biomass in sea is generally inverted \n\nReason : The biomass of phytoplanktons far exceeds that of fishes','Assertion is true, but reason is false','In most ecosystems, all the pyramids, of number, of energy and biomass are upright, i.E., producers are more in number and biomass than the herbivores, and herbivores are more in number and biomass than the carnivores. Also energy at a lower trophic level is always more than at a higher level.The pyramid of biomass in sea is generally inverted because the biomass of fishes far exceeds that of phytoplankton.\nReference:  NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 249','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is false','Assertion is false, but Reason is true','NEW','12-249','12-213',NULL),
(361033,'biology','Assertion : Pyramid of energy is always upright, can never be inverted, \n\nReason :Energy flows from a particular trophic level to the next trophic level, with loss of energy in the form of heat at each level','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','In most ecosystems, all the pyramids, of number, of energy and biomass are upright, i.E., producers are more in number and biomass than the herbivores, and herbivores are more in number and biomass than the carnivores. Also energy at a lower trophic level is always more than at a higher level.The pyramid of biomass in sea is generally inverted because the biomass of fishes far exceeds that of phytoplankton.Pyramid of energy is always upright, can never be inverted, because when energy flows from a particular trophic level to the next trophic level,some energy is always lost as heat at each step. Each bar in the energy pyramid indicates the amount of energy present at each trophic level in a given time or annually per unit area.\nReference:  NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 249','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is false','Assertion is false, but Reason is true','NEW','12-249','12-213',NULL),
(361034,'biology','With regards to Ecological pyramids, Which of the following statements is incorrect ?','Ecological pyramids can accomodate a simple food web','However, there are certain limitations of ecological pyramids such as it does not take into account the same species belonging to two or more trophic levels. It assumes a simple food chain, something that almost never exists in nature; it does not accommodate a food web. Moreover, saprophytes are not given any place in ecological pyramids even though they play a vital role in the ecosystem.\nReference: NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 250','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Ecological pyramids such as it does not take into account the same species belonging to two or more trophic levels','Ecological pyramids assume a simple food chain, something that almost never exists in nature;','Ecological pyramids can accomodate a simple food web','Saprophytes are not given any place in ecological pyramids even though they play a vital role in the ecosystem.','NEW','12-250','12-214',NULL),
(361035,'biology','Which of the following represents an erect pyramid ?','Pyramid of energy in a savannah grassland','The pyramid of biomass in sea is generally inverted because the biomass of fishes far exceeds that of phytoplankton.And always remember that pyramid of energy is always an erect one.','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','The pyramid of biomass in sea','The pyramid of number in a tree with many dependant insects','Pyramid of energy in a savannah grassland','Pyramid of numbers in a savannah where the number of carnivores exceed herbivores','NEW','12-249','12-213',NULL),
(361036,'biology','With regards to Ecological succession, choose the incorrect answer','Changes in community composition and structure lead to a community that is in near equilibrium with the environment and that is called a flash community.','The characteristics of population and community and also their response to environment and how such responses vary from an individual response. Let us examine another aspect of community response to environment over time. An important characteristic of all communities is that their composition and structure constantly change in response to the changing environmental conditions. This change is orderly and sequential, parallel with the changes in the physical environment. These changes lead finally to a community that is in near equilibrium with the environment and that is called a climax community.\nReference   NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 250','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','The characteristics of population and community and also their response to environment and how such responses vary from an individual response','An important characteristic of all communities is that their composition and structure constantly change in response to the changing environmental conditions','Change in community composition and structure is orderly and sequential, parallel with the changes in the physical environment.','Changes in community composition and structure lead to a community that is in near equilibrium with the environment and that is called a flash community.','NEW','12-250',NULL,NULL),
(361037,'biology','With regards to Ecological succession, choose the correct answer','During succession some species colonise an area and their population become more numerous whereas populations of other species decline and even disappear.','The gradual and fairly predictable change in the species composition of a given area is called ecological succession. During succession some species colonise an area and their population become more numerous whereas populations of other species decline and even disappear. The entire sequence of communities that successively change in a given area are called sere(s). The individual transitional communities are termed seral stages or seral communities. In the successive seral stages there is a change in the diversity of species of organisms, increase in the number of species and organisms as well as an increase in the total biomass.\nReference   NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 250','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','The gradual and fairly predictable change in the species composition of a given area is called ecological precision.','During succession some species colonise an area and their population become more numerous whereas populations of other species decline and even disappear.','The entire sequence of communities that never change in a given area are called sere(s)','In successive seral stages there is no change in the diversity of species of organisms, number of species and organisms as well as an increase in the total biomass.','NEW','12-250',NULL,NULL),
(361038,'biology','With regards to Ecological succession, choose the incorrect answer','Succession and evolution would have been perpendicular processes a long time ago for the outcome of present day.','The present day communities in the world have come to be because of succession that has occurred over millions of years since life started on earth. Actually succession and evolution would have been parallel processes at that time. Succession is hence a process that starts in an area where no living organisms are there – these could be areas where no living organisms ever existed, say bare rock; or in areas that somehow, lost all the living organisms that existed there. The former is called primary succession, while the latter is termed secondary succession.\nReference   NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 250','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','The present day communities in the world have come to be because of succession that has occurred over millions of years since life started on earth','Succession and evolution would have been perpendicular processes a long time ago for the outcome of present day.','Succession is a process that begins in an area where no living organisms ever existed','As succession proceeds, the numbers and types of animals and decomposers also change','NEW','12-250',NULL,NULL),
(361039,'biology','With regards to Ecological succession, choose the incorrect answer','Soil is very insignificant in establishment of a biotic community in primary succession','Examples of areas where primary succession occurs are newly cooled lava, bare rock, newly created pond or reservoir. The establishment of a new biotic community is generally slow. Before a biotic community of diverse organisms can become established, there must be soil. \nReference   NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 250','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Primary succession is a process that begins in an area where no life ever existed','Secondary succession is a process that occurs in a place where once life existed','Primary succession can occur in newly cooled lava, bare rock, newly created pond or reservoir and initial establishment of life is generally slow.','Soil is very insignificant in establishment of a biotic community in primary succession','NEW','12-250',NULL,NULL),
(361040,'biology','With regards to Ecological succession, choose the incorrect answer','Primary succession is faster than secondary succession','Depending mostly on the climate, it takes natural processes several hundred to several thousand years to produce fertile soil on bare rock.Secondary succession begins in areas where natural biotic communities have been destroyed such as in abandoned farm lands, burned or cut forests, lands that have been flooded. Since some soil or sediment is present, succession is faster than primary succession.\nReference   NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 250 and 251','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Depending mostly on the climate, it takes natural processes several hundred to several thousand years to produce fertile soil on bare rock.','Secondary succession begins in areas where natural biotic communities have been destroyed','Secondary succession can happen in abandoned farm lands, burned or cut forests, lands that have been flooded.','Primary succession is faster than secondary succession','NEW','12-250',NULL,NULL),
(361041,'biology','With regards to Ecological Plant succession, choose the correct answer','As succession proceeds, the numbers and types of animals and decomposers also change','Description of ecological succession usually focuses on changes in vegetation. However, these vegetational changes in turn affect food and shelter for various types of animals. Thus, as succession proceeds, the numbers and types of animals and decomposers also change. At any time during primary or secondary succession, natural or human induced disturbances (fire, deforestation, etc.), can convert a particular seral stage of succession to an earlier stage. Also such\ndisturbances create new conditions that encourage some species and discourage or eliminate other species.\nReference   NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page  251','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Description of ecological succession usually focuses on changes in terrain of the area','Changes in vegetation never affects food and shelter of life forms in a given area','As succession proceeds, the numbers and types of animals and decomposers also change','The gradual and fairly predictable change in the species composition of a given area is called ecological precision.','NEW','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(361042,'biology','With regards to Ecological Plant succession, choose the correct answer','Both hydrarch and xerarch successions lead to medium water (mesic) conditions.','Based on the nature of the habitat – whether it is water (or very wet areas) or it is on very dry areas – succession of plants is called hydrarch or xerarch, respectively. Hydrarch succession takes place in wet areas and the successional series progress from hydric to the mesic conditions. As against this, xerarch succession takes place in dry areas and the series progress from xeric to mesic conditions. Hence, both hydrarch and xerarch successions lead to medium water conditions (mesic) – neither too dry (xeric) nor too wet (hydric).Reference   NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 251','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Based on the nature of the habitat -water or  dry areas – succession of plants is called xerarch or hydrarch, respectively','Xerarch succession takes place in wet areas and the successional series progress from mesic to the hydric conditions','Hydrarch succession takes place in dry areas and the series progress from xeric to mesic conditions','Both hydrarch and xerarch successions lead to medium water (mesic) conditions.','NEW','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(361043,'biology','With regards to Ecological Plant succession, choose the incorrect answer','In primary succession on rocks these are usually birds which break down rock, helping in weathering and soil formation.','The species that invade a bare area are called pioneer species. In primary succession on rocks these are usually lichens which are able to secrete acids to dissolve rock, helping in weathering and soil formation. These later pave way to some very small plants like bryophytes, which are able to take hold in the small amount of soil. They are, with time, succeeded by higher plants, and after several more stages, ultimately a stable climax forest community is formed.\nReference:  NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 251','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','The species that invade a bare area are called pioneer species','In primary succession on rocks these are usually birds which break down rock, helping in weathering and soil formation.','Usually after colonisation by lichens on rocks in primary succession, mosses take over holding a small amount of soil.','Higher plants are involved in the establishment of a climax community','NEW','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(361044,'biology','With regards to Ecological Plant succession, choose the incorrect answer','The climax community remains unstable as long as the environment remains unchanged.','The climax community remains stable as long as the environment remains unchanged. With time the xerophytic habitat gets converted into a mesophytic one. In primary succession in water, the pioneers are the small phytoplanktons,which are replaced with time by rooted-submerged plants, rooted-floating angiosperms followed by free-floating plants, then reed- swamp, marsh-meadow, scrub and finally the trees. The climax again would be a forest. With time the water body is converted into land .Reference   NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 251','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','The climax community remains unstable as long as the environment remains unchanged.','With time the water body is converted into land','With time the xerophytic habitat gets converted into a mesophytic one','In primary succession in water, the pioneers are the small zooplanktons','NEW','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(311703,'biology','Biodiversity loss occurs due to the reasons given below.\n\n(i) Habitat loss and fragmentation\n(ii) Co-extinction\n(iii) Over-exploitation\n(iv) Alien species invasion\nIdentify the correct reasons.\n[2017]','(1), (2),(3) and (4)','Biodiversity refers to the variety found in\nbiota due to the genetic make-up of plants\nand animals to cultural diversity. The main\ncause of the loss of biodiversity can be\nattributed to the influence of human beings\non the world\'s ecosystem. The important\nfactors causing loss of biodiversity are -\nhabitat loss, habitat fragmentation,\ndisturbances, over exploitation of\nresources, pollution, exotic species, co￾extinction, alien species invasion, intensive\nagriculture and forestry','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(1) and (2)','(1), (2) and (3)','(2), (3) and (4)','(1), (2),(3) and (4)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(361045,'biology','With regards to Ecological Plant succession, choose the correct answer','All succession whether taking place in water or on land, proceeds to a similar climax community','In secondary succession the species that invade depend on the condition of the soil, availability of water, the environment as also the seeds or other propagules present. Since soil is already there, the rate of succession is much faster and hence, climax is also reached more quickly. What is important to understand is that succession, particularly primary succession, is a very slow process, taking maybe thousands of years for the climax to be reached. Another important fact is to understand that all succession whether taking place in water or on land, proceeds to a similar climax community – the mesic.\nReference:  NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 251','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','In secondary succession the species that invade does not depend on the condition of the soil, availability of water, the environment and requires only seeds or other propagules to be  present.','Primary succcession is always faster than secondary succession','All succession whether taking place in water or on land, proceeds to a similar climax community','Mesic conditions may take upto 1000 years in secondary succcession with favourable conditions.','NEW','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(361046,'biology','With regards to primary plant succession which of the following option is a correct presentation ?','Phytoplanktons->Submerged plants->Submerged free floating plants->Reed Swamp->Marsh meadow->Scrub->Forest','In primary succession in water, the pioneers are the small phytoplanktons,which are replaced with time by rooted-submerged plants, rooted-floating angiosperms followed by free-floating plants, then reed- swamp, marsh-meadow, scrub and finally the trees.\nReference:  NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 251','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Phytoplanktons->Submerged plants->Submerged free floating plants->Reed Swamp->Marsh meadow->Scrub->Forest','Phytoplanktons->Submerged free floating plants->Submerged plants->Reed Swamp->Marsh meadow->Scrub->Forest','Phytoplanktons->Submerged free floating plants->Submerged plants->Marsh meadow->Reed Swamp->Scrub->Forest','Phytoplanktons->Submerged plants->Submerged free floating plants->Reed Swamp->Marsh meadow->Forest->Scrub','NEW','12-251',NULL,NULL),
(361101,'biology','In the context of Nutrient Cycling, Which of the following statement is true ?','Nutrients are never lost from the ecosystems, they are recycled time and again indefinitel, through the various components of an ecosystem','You have studied in Class XI that organisms need a constant supply of nutrients to grow, reproduce and regulate various body functions. The amount of nutrients, such as carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus, calcium, etc., present in the soil at any given time, is referred to as the standing state. It varies in different kinds of ecosystems and also on a seasonal basis. What is important is to appreciate that nutrients which are never lost from the ecosystems, rather they are recycled time and again indefinitely. The movement of nutrient elements through the various components of an ecosystem is called nutrient cycling. Another name of nutrient cycling is biogeochemical cycles (bio: living organism, geo: rocks, air, water).\nReference: NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 252','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','The amount of nutrients, present in the soil at any given time, is referred to as the dynamic state.','Standing state is constant for all ecosystems.on a seasonal basis.','Nutrients are never lost from the ecosystems, they are recycled time and again indefinitel, through the various components of an ecosystem','Nutrient cycles are also called biogeothermal cycles','NEW','12-253',NULL,NULL),
(361102,'biology','In the context of Nutrient Cycling, Which of the following statement is true ?','Environmental factors regulate the rate of release of nutrients into the atmosphere','Nutrient cycles are of two types: (a) gaseous and (b) sedimentary.\nThe reservoir for gaseous type of nutrient cycle (e.g., nitrogen, carbon cycle) exists in the atmosphere and for the sedimentary cycle (e.g., sulphur and phosphorus cycle), the reservoir is located in Earth’s crust. Environmental factors, e.g., soil, moisture, pH, temperature, etc., regulate the rate of release of nutrients into the atmosphere.The function of the reservoir is to meet with the deficit which occurs due to imbalance in the rate of influx\nand efflux.\nReference:  NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 253','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Nutrient cycles are of three types,solid,liquid and gaseous.','The reservoir for gaseous type of nutrient cycle exists in the Earth\'s crust and reservoir for sedimentary type of nutrient cycle exists in the Atmosphere','The function of the reservoir is not to meet with the deficit which occurs due to imbalance in the rate of influx\nand efflux.','Environmental factors regulate the rate of release of nutrients into the atmosphere','NEW','12-253',NULL,NULL),
(361103,'biology','Assertion: The reservoir for nitrogen, carbon cycle exists in the atmosphere \n\nReason :The reservoir for gaseous type of nutrient cycle exists in the atmosphere, nitrogen and carbon cycles are gaseous type','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Nutrient cycles are of two types: \n(a) gaseous \n(b) sedimentary.\nThe reservoir for gaseous type of nutrient cycle (e.g., nitrogen, carbon cycle) exists in the atmosphere and for the sedimentary cycle (e.g., sulphur and phosphorus cycle), the reservoir is located in Earth’s crust. Environmental factors, e.g., soil, moisture, pH, temperature, etc., regulate the rate of release of nutrients into the atmosphere.\nReference:  NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 253','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is false','Assertion is false, but Reason is true','NEW','12-253',NULL,NULL),
(361104,'biology','Assertion: The reservoir for sulphur and phosphorus cycle is located in Earth’s crust.\n\nReason: Sulphur and Phosphorus are elements belonging to sedimentary cycle','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Nutrient cycles are of two types: \n(a) gaseous \n(b) sedimentary.\nThe reservoir for gaseous type of nutrient cycle (e.g., nitrogen, carbon cycle) exists in the atmosphere and for the sedimentary cycle (e.g., sulphur and phosphorus cycle), the reservoir is located in Earth’s crust. Environmental factors, e.g., soil, moisture, pH, temperature, etc., regulate the rate of release of nutrients into the atmosphere.\nReference:  NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 253','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is false','Assertion is false, but Reason is true','NEW','12-253',NULL,NULL),
(51045,'chemistry','Carbon dioxide gas (1.00 mole) at 373 K occupies 536 mL at 50.0 atm pressure. What is the calculated value of the pressure using ideal gas equation?','57.1','V = 526/1000 = 0.536 L\nn = 1.00 mol\nT = 373 K\nPV = nRT\nP = nRT/V = 1.00 x 0.0821 x 373/0.536\n= 57.1 atm\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 158; Exercises)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','53.2','57.1','49.6','48','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(361105,'biology','With regards to the Carbon Cycle, Choose the correct statement','71 % of the total quantity of global carbon, is found dissolved in oceans and is responsible for regulation of the carbon cycle','When you study the composition of living organisms, carbon constitutes 49 % of dry weight of organisms and is next only to water. If we look at the total quantity of global carbon, we find that 71 % carbon is found dissolved in oceans. This oceanic reservoir regulates the amount of  CO₂  in the atmosphere (Figure 14.6). Do you know that the atmosphere only contains about 1% of total global carbon? Fossil fuel also represent a reservoir of carbon.\nReference: NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 254','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','When you study the composition of living organisms, water constitutes 49 % of dry weight of organisms and is next only to carbon','71 % of the total quantity of global carbon, is found dissolved in oceans and is responsible for regulation of the carbon cycle','The Atmosphere contains 29% of carbon reservoir','Fossil fuels are not a reservoir of carbon','NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL),
(361106,'biology','With regards to the Carbon Cycle,Choose the incorrect statement','40 kgs of carbon is fixed annually in the biosphere through photosynthesis.','Carbon cycling occurs through atmosphere, ocean and through living and dead organisms. According to one estimate 4 × (10^13) kg of carbon is fixed annually in the biosphere through photosynthesis. A considerable amount of carbon returns to the atmosphere as CO₂  through respiratory activities of the,producers and consumers. Decomposers also contribute substantially\nto CO₂  pool by their processing of waste materials and dead organic matter of land or oceans.\nReference: NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 254','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Carbon cycling occurs through atmosphere, ocean and through living and dead organisms.','40 kgs of carbon is fixed annually in the biosphere through photosynthesis.','A considerable amount of carbon returns to the atmosphere as CO₂  through respiratory activities of the,producers and consumers','Decomposers also contribute substantially to CO₂  pool by their processing of waste materials and dead organic matter of land or oceans','NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL),
(361107,'biology','With regards to the Carbon Cycle,Choose the incorrect statement','Deforestation causes depletion of CO₂  from the atmosphere and afforestation increases the CO₂  content in the atmosphere.','Some amount of the fixed carbon is lost to sediments and removed from circulation. Burning of wood, forest fire and combustion of organic matter, fossil fuel, volcanic activity are additional sources for releasing CO₂  in the atmosphere. Human activities have significantly influenced the carbon cycle. Rapid deforestation and massive burning of fossil fuel for energy and transport have significantly increased the rate of release of  CO₂  into the atmosphere (see greenhouse effect in Chapter 16).\nAfforestation is planting of trees, and they do not increase  CO₂  levels, they inturn cause fall in CO₂  levels as they get fixed by photosynthesis.\n\nReference: NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 254','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Some amount of the fixed carbon is lost to sediments and removed from circulation.','Burning of wood, forest fire and combustion of organic matter, fossil fuel, volcanic activity are additional sources for releasing CO₂  in the atmosphere','Rapid deforestation and massive burning of fossil fuel for energy and transport have significantly increased the rate of release of  CO₂  into the atmosphere.','Deforestation causes depletion of CO₂  from the atmosphere and afforestation increases the CO₂  content in the atmosphere.','NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL),
(361108,'biology','In context of the Phosphorus cycle, Choose the incorrect statement','Animals do not require need large quantities of Phosphorus to make shells, bones and teeth, they only require calcium.','Phosphorus is a major constituent of biological membranes, nucleic acids and cellular energy transfer systems. Many animals also need large quantities of this element to make shells, bones and teeth. The natural reservoir of phosphorus is rock, which contains phosphorus in the form of phosphates. When rocks are weathered, minute amounts of these phosphates dissolve in soil solution and are absorbed by the roots of the plants .\nReference:  NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 254','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Phosphorus is a major constituent of biological membranes, nucleic acids and cellular energy transfer systems','Animals do not require need large quantities of Phosphorus to make shells, bones and teeth, they only require calcium.','The natural reservoir of phosphorus is rock, which contains phosphorus in the form of phosphates','When rocks are weathered, minute amounts of these phosphates dissolve in soil solution and are absorbed by the roots of the plants','NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL),
(361109,'biology','Assertion: The natural reservoir of phosphorus is rock, which contains phosphorus in the form of phosphates\n\nReason: When rocks are weathered, minute amounts of these phosphates dissolve in soil solution and are absorbed by the roots of the plants','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The natural reservoir of phosphorus is rock, which contains phosphorus in the form of phosphates. When rocks are weathered, minute amounts of these phosphates dissolve in soil solution and are absorbed by the roots of the plants.\n Reference:  NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 254','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is false','Assertion is false, but Reason is true','NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL),
(361110,'biology','In context of the Phosphorus cycle, choose the incorrect statement','There is respiratory release of phosphorus into atmosphere.like carbon','Herbivores and other animals obtain this element from plants. The waste products and the dead organisms are decomposed by phosphate-solubilising bacteria releasing phosphorus. Unlike carbon cycle, there is no respiratory release of phosphorus into atmosphere. The other two major and important differences between carbon and phosphorus cycle are firstly, atmospheric inputs of phosphorus through rainfall are much smaller than carbon inputs, and, secondly, gaseous exchanges of phosphorus between organism and environment are negligible.\nReference: NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 254','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Herbivores and other animals obtain Phosphorus from plants.','The waste products and the dead organisms are decomposed by phosphate-solubilising bacteria releasing phosphorus','There is respiratory release of phosphorus into atmosphere.like carbon','Human beings contain stores of phosphorus in our bone and teeth in the form of hydroxy apatite with calcium','NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL),
(311704,'biology','Explant is required to be disinfected before placing in culture. This is done by [2017]','Clorax and hypochlorite','Before transferring on the culture\nmedium, the explant is first of all disinfected\nby surface sterilization using clorax water,\nsodium or calcium hypochlorite solution\nor methiolate. Too much care must be taken\nin this operation so that the cells do not\ndie.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Autoclaving','Ultra-violet rays','Clorax and hypochlorite','X-rays','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(361111,'biology','In context of the Phosphorus cycle, choose the incorrect statement','Phosphorus and Calcium also have a gaseous type of nutrient cycle','The other two major and important differences between carbon and phosphorus cycle are firstly, atmospheric inputs of phosphorus through rainfall are much smaller than carbon inputs, and, secondly, gaseous exchanges of phosphorus between organism and environment are negligible.\nReference:  NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 254','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Atmospheric inputs of phosphorus through rainfall are much smaller than carbon inputs','Gaseous exchanges of phosphorus between organism and environment are negligible.','Sulphur, nitrogen and carbon are involved with gaseous type of nutrient cycle','Phosphorus and Calcium also have a gaseous type of nutrient cycle','NEW','12-254',NULL,NULL),
(361112,'biology','In Reference   to Ecological services, choose the correct answer\n\ni) Healthy ecosystems are the base for a wide range of economic, environmental and aesthetic goods and services.\n\nii) Ecosystem services do not include mitigation of droughts and floods, provide storage site for carbon and also provide aesthetic, cultural and spiritual values. \n\niii)Ecosystem services incliude healthy forest ecosystems that purify air and water, cycle nutrients, generate fertile soils, provide wildlife habitat, maintain biodiversity and  pollinate crops,\n\niv) Soil formation accounts for 50% of ecosysytem services, 10% for nutrient cycling and recreational services,6% for wildlife habitat and climate regulation.','only ii) is incorrect','Healthy ecosystems are the base for a wide range of economic, environmental and aesthetic goods and services. The products of ecosystem processes are named as ecosystem services, for example, healthy forest ecosystems purify air and water, mitigate droughts and floods, cycle nutrients, generate fertile soils, provide wildlife habitat, maintain biodiversity, pollinate crops, provide storage site for carbon and also provide aesthetic, cultural and spiritual values. Though value of such services of biodiversity is difficult to determine, it seems reasonable to think that biodiversity should carry a hefty price tag.Out of the total cost of various ecosystem services, the soil\nformation accounts for about 50 %, and contributions of other services like recreation and nutrient cycling, are less than 10 % each.\nReference: NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 255','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','only i) is correct','only iv) is incorrect','only ii) is incorrect','All are correct','NEW','12-255',NULL,NULL),
(361113,'biology','Choose the incorrect statement with regards to Ecological Services','Researchers have put an average price tag of US $ 33 billion a year on these fundamental ecosystems services, which are largely taken for granted because they are free','Robert Constanza and his colleagues have very recently tried to put price tags on nature’s life-support services. Researchers have put an average price tag of US $ 33 trillion a year on these fundamental ecosystems services, which are largely taken for granted because they are free. This is nearly twice the value of the global gross national product GNP which is (US $ 18 trillion).Out of the total cost of various ecosystem services, the soil\nformation accounts for about 50 %, and contributions of other services like recreation and nutrient cycling, are less than 10 % each. The cost of climate regulation and habitat for wildlife are about 6 % each.\nReference:  NCERT class 12, chapter 14, page 255','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Robert Constanza and his colleagues have very recently tried to put price tags on nature’s life-support services.','Researchers have put an average price tag of US $ 33 billion a year on these fundamental ecosystems services, which are largely taken for granted because they are free','Out of the total cost of various ecosystem services, the soil formation accounts for about 50 %.','Out of the total cost of various ecosystem services, contributions of other services like recreation and nutrient cycling, are less than 10 % each, climate regulation and habitat for wildlife are about 6 % each.','NEW','12-255',NULL,NULL),
(361114,'biology','With Context to Ecosystem, Choose the incorrect statement','Species composition and decomposition are the two main structural features of an ecosystem','An ecosystem is a structural and functional unit of nature and it comprises abiotic and biotic components. Abiotic components are inorganic materials- air, water and soil, whereas biotic components are producers, consumers and decomposers. Each ecosystem has characteristic physical structure resulting from interaction amongst abiotic and biotic components. Species composition and stratification are the two main structural features of an ecosystem. Based on source of nutrition every organism occupies a place in an ecosystem.\nReference  : NCERT Class 12, chapter 14, page 256','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','An ecosystem is a structural and functional unit of nature and it comprises abiotic and biotic components.','Abiotic components are inorganic materials- air, water and soil, whereas biotic components are producers, consumers and decomposers','Each ecosystem has characteristic physical structure resulting from interaction amongst abiotic and biotic components.','Species composition and decomposition are the two main structural features of an ecosystem','NEW','12-256',NULL,NULL),
(361115,'biology','With Context to Ecosystem, Choose the incorrect statement','Primary productivity is the rate of capture of solar energy or biomass production of the decomposers of the DFC.','Productivity, decomposition, energy flow, and nutrient cycling are the four important components of an ecosystem. Primary productivity is the rate of capture of solar energy or biomass production of the producers. It is divided into two types: gross primary productivity (GPP) and net primary productivity (NPP). Rate of capture of solar energy or total production of organic matter is called as GPP.\nReference  : NCERT Class 12, chapter 14, page 256','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Productivity, decomposition, energy flow, and nutrient cycling are the four important components of an ecosystem.','Primary productivity is the rate of capture of solar energy or biomass production of the decomposers of the DFC.','Primary productivity is divided into two types: gross primary productivity (GPP) and net primary productivity (NPP).','Rate of capture of solar energy or total production of organic matter is called as GPP','NEW','12-256',NULL,NULL),
(361116,'biology','With Context to Ecosystem, Choose the incorrect statement','Deposition involves three processes, namely fragmentation of detritus, leaching and catabolism','GPP. NPP is the remaining biomass or the energy left after utilisation of producers. Secondary productivity is the rate of assimilation of food energy by the consumers. In decomposition, complex organic compounds of detritus are converted to  CO₂ , water and inorganic nutrients by the decomposers. Decomposition involves three processes, namely fragmentation of detritus, leaching and catabolism.\nReference  : NCERT Class 12, chapter 14, page 256','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','GPP. NPP is the remaining biomass or the energy left after utilisation of producers.','Secondary productivity is the rate of assimilation of food energy by the consumers','In decomposition, complex organic compounds of detritus are converted to  CO₂ , water and inorganic nutrients by the decomposers.','Deposition involves three processes, namely fragmentation of detritus, leaching and catabolism','NEW','12-256',NULL,NULL),
(361117,'biology','With Context to Ecosystem, Choose the correct statement','The storage and movement of nutrient elements through the various components of the ecosystem is called nutrient cycling','Energy flow is unidirectional. First, plants capture solar energy and then, food is transferred from the producers to decomposers. Organisms of different trophic levels in nature are connected to each other for food or energy relationship forming a food chain. The storage and movement of nutrient elements through the various components of the ecosystem is called nutrient cycling;nutrients are repeatedly used through this process.\nReference  : NCERT Class 12, chapter 14, page 256','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Energy flow is always bidirectional','Plants capture solar energy and then, food is transferred from the decomposers to producers','Organisms of different trophic levels in nature are connected to each other for food or energy relationship in a linear manner forming a food web','The storage and movement of nutrient elements through the various components of the ecosystem is called nutrient cycling','NEW','12-256',NULL,NULL),
(361118,'biology','With Context to Ecosystem, Choose the correct statement','Products of ecosystem processes are named as ecosystem services','Nutrient cycling is of two types—gaseous and sedimentary. Atmosphere or hydrosphere is the reservoir for the gaseous type of cycle (carbon), whereas Earth’s crust is the reservoir for sedimentary type (phosphorus). Products of ecosystem processes are named as ecosystem services, e.g., purification of air and water by forests.The biotic community is dynamic and undergoes changes with the passage of time. These changes are sequentially ordered and constitute ecological succession. Succession begins with invasion of a bare lifeless area by pioneers which later pave way for successors and ultimately a stable climax community is formed. The climax community remains stable as long as the environment remains unchanged.\nReference  : NCERT Class 12, chapter 14, page 256','Ecology >> Ecosystem >>  Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Atmosphere or hydrosphere is the reservoir for the gaseous type of cycle (phosphorus), whereas Earth’s crust is the reservoir for sedimentary type (carbon, sulphur)','The Abiotic community is dynamic and undergoes changes with the passage of time, which are sequentially ordered and constitute ecological succession','Products of ecosystem processes are named as ecosystem services','Succession begins with invasion of a bare lifeless area by pioneers which later pave way for successors and ultimately an unstable climax community, that remains unstable as long as the environment remains unchanged.','NEW','12-256',NULL,NULL),
(370101,'biology','___________refers to the sum total of diversity that exists at all levels of biological organisation.','Biodiversity','Biodiversity refers to the variety and variability of living organisms on the planet earth.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:259,paragraph no:5','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Biodiversity Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Biodiversity','Biosphere','Biophora','Biology','NEW','12-259','12-217',NULL),
(370102,'biology','Biodiversity can also be defined as','All of them.','Biodiversity can also be defined as the variety of life forms, gene pools and habitats found in an area.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:259,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Biodiversity Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','The variety of life forms in an area.','The organisms with different gene pools living in an area.','The variety of habitats found in an area.','All of them.','NEW','12-259','12-217',NULL),
(370103,'biology','Edward Wilson was a','Socio-biologist','Edward Wilson was a socio-biologist.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:258,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Biodiversity Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Botanist','Wildlife Biologist','Socio-biologist','German physicts','NEW','12-258','12-216',NULL),
(370104,'biology','The term ____________ was popularised by socio-biologist Edward Wilson to describe the combined diversity at all the levels of biological organization.','Biodiversity','The term "Biodiversity" was popularised by socio-biologist Edward Wilson to describe the combined diversity at all the levels of biological organization.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:258,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Biodiversity Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Biodiversity','Ecology','Ecosystem','Environment','NEW','12-258','12-216',NULL),
(370105,'biology','The genetic variation shown by the medicinal plant Rauwolfia vomitoria growing in different Himalayan ranges might be in terms of','Concentration and potency of active chemical','The genetic variation shown by the medicinal plant Rauwolfia vomitoria growing in different Himalayan ranges might be in terms of potency and concentration of active chemical.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:259,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Biodiversity Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Concentration and potency of active chemical','Potential and potency of active chemical','Concentration and temperature of active chemical','Temperature and potential of active chemical','NEW','12-259','12-217',NULL),
(370106,'biology','Which of the following is not the level of biodiversity?','Theta diversity','Given following are the levels of biodiversity\n(i) Genetic diversity, (ii) Species diversity, (iii) Ecosystem diversity.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:258,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Biodiversity Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Genetic diversity','Species diversity','Ecosystem diversity','Theta diversity','NEW','12-258','12-216',NULL),
(370107,'biology','Who described the term "Biodiversity" as combined diversity at all levels of biological organizations?','Edward Wilson','The term "Biodiversity" was popularised by socio-biologist Edward Wilson to describe the combined diversity at all the levels of biological organizations.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:258,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Biodiversity Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Edward Wilson','Norman Myers','UNESCO','World Wildlife Fund','NEW','12-258','12-216',NULL),
(370108,'biology','Which diversity is at species level?','Species diversity','Species diversity is the diversity at the species level\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:259,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Biodiversity Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Ecological diversity','Species diversity','Genetic diversity','All the above','NEW','12-259','12-217',NULL),
(370109,'biology','In Western ghats, which species having greater diversity than the Eastern ghats?','Amphibian species','The Western ghats have a greater amphibian species diversity than Eastern ghats\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:259,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Biodiversity Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Reptile species','Arachnids species','Amphibian species','All the above','NEW','12-259','12-217',NULL),
(370110,'biology','At the ecosystem level, which country has the greater ecosystem diversity than a Scandinavian country?','India','At the ecosystem level, India, for instance, with its deserts, rain forests, mangroves, coral leaves, wetlands, estuaries, and alpine meadows has greater ecosystem diversity than a Scandinavian country like Norway\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:259,paragraph no:3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Biodiversity Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Spain','Australia','India','Turkey','NEW','12-259','12-217',NULL),
(370111,'biology','It has taken ________ of years of evolution, to accumulate this rich diversity in nature.','Millions','It has taken millions of years of evolution, to accumulate this rich diversity in nature.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:258,paragraph no:3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Biodiversity Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Thousands','Lakhs','Millions','Billions','NEW','12-258','12-216',NULL),
(370112,'biology','What is Biodiversity referred to as?','Genetic, species  and  ecological diversity in a region','Biodiversity referred to as Genetic, species  and  ecological diversity in a region.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:259,paragraph no:1,2,3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Biodiversity Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Species in an ecosystem','Genes  and  species in a region','Genetic, species  and  ecological diversity in a region','Genes and species in an ecosystem','NEW','12-259','12-217',NULL),
(370201,'biology','According to IUCN(2004), how many plants and animals species are described so far?','More than 1.5 million','According to IUCN (2004) the total number of plants and animals species are described so far is slightly more than 1.5 million.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:259,paragraph no:5','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Species on Earth and in India',NULL,'2','mcq','Less than 1.5 million','More than 1.3 million','1.5 million','More than 1.5 million','NEW','12-259','12-217',NULL),
(370202,'biology','IUCN stands for','International Union for Conservation of Nature and Natural Resources','IUCN stands for International Union for Conservation of Nature and Natural Resources.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:259,paragraph no:5','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Species on Earth and in India',NULL,'2','mcq','International Union for Conservation of Nature and Natural Resources','Indian Union for Conservation of Nature and Natural Resources','International Union for Conservation of Nature','Indian Union for Conservation of Nature','NEW','12-259','12-217',NULL),
(370203,'biology','Whose estimate global species diversity at about 7 million?','Robert May','The sound estimate made by Robert May places the global species diversity at about 7 million.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:259,paragraph no:5','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Species on Earth and in India',NULL,'2','mcq','Robert Brown','Robert Hook','Robert Boyle','Robert May','NEW','12-259','12-217',NULL),
(370204,'biology','What is the % of species of animals and plants in earth\'s biodiversity(respectively)?','>70,22','More than 70 % of all the species recorded are animals, while plants (including algae, fungi, bryophytes, gymnosperms and angiosperms) comprise no more than 22 % of the total.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:260,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Species on Earth and in India',NULL,'2','mcq','<80,20','<70,30','>70,22','60,40','NEW','12-260','12-218',NULL),
(370205,'biology','Which group among the animals is the most species - rich taxonomic group?','Insects','Among animals, insects are the most species-rich taxonomic group making up more than 70 % of the total species\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:260,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Species on Earth and in India',NULL,'2','mcq','Insects','Molluscs','Amphibia','Mammalia','NEW','12-260','12-218',NULL),
(370206,'biology','Given below is the pie chart related to proportionate number of species of major taxa of invertebrates. Match the following to give right replacement for A, B, and C from the diagram (3701)','A-Fishes,B-Reptiles,C-Birds','Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:260,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Species on Earth and in India',NULL,'2','match','A,Fishes','B,Reptiles','C,Birds',NULL,'NEW','12-260','12-218','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/370206q.png'),
(370207,'biology','Given below is the pie chart related to proportionate number of species of major taxa of vertebrates. Match the following to give right replacement for A, B, and C from the diagram (3702)','A-Crustaceans,B-Molluscs,C-Insects','Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:260,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Species on Earth and in India',NULL,'2','match','A,Crustaceans','B,Molluscs','C,Insects',NULL,'NEW','12-260','12-218','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/370207q.png'),
