(370211,'biology','According to Robert May’s global estimates, how much % of the total species have been recorded so far?','22%','According to Robert May’s global estimates, only 22 % of the total species have been recorded so far.','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Species on Earth and in India',NULL,'2','mcq','70%','30%','22%','18%','NEW','12-261','12-219',NULL),
(370212,'biology','How many of the following statements are true regarding biodiversity ?\n1. More than 70 % of all the species recorded are animals.\n2. Out of every 10 animals on this planet, 7 are insects.\n3. The number of fungi species in the world is more than the combined total of the species of fishes, amphibians, reptiles and mammals.\n4. Number of fishes is very less than that of mammals.','3','The given statements 1, 2, and 3 are correct- 1 More than 70 % of all the species recorded are animals.; 2. Out of every 10 animals on this planet, 7 are insects.; 3. The number of fungi species in the world is more than the combined total of the species of fishes, amphibians, reptiles and mammals. Statement D. "Number of fishes is very less than that of mammals" is wrong\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:260,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Species on Earth and in India',NULL,'2','mcq','2','3','4','1','NEW','12-261','12-219',NULL),
(370301,'biology','For many group of animals or plants, there are interesting patterns in diversity, the most well- known being the _____ gradient in diversity.','Latitudinal','For many group of animals or plants, there are interesting patterns in diversity, the most well- known being the latitudinal gradient in diversity.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:261,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Patterns of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','Latitudinal','Longitudinal','Meridial','Zonal','NEW','12-261','12-219',NULL),
(370302,'biology','Which country located near the equator has nearly 1,400 species of birds?','Columbia','Colombia located near the equator has nearly 1,400 species of birds.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:261,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Patterns of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','Columbia','Colombo','Croatia','Columbus','NEW','12-261','12-219',NULL),
(370303,'biology','Which are the countries at 41° N having 105 species and at 71° N having only 56 species respectively?','New york,Greenland','New York at 41° N has 105 species and Greenland at 71° N only 56 species.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:26,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Patterns of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','Norway,Sweden','Spain,Italy','New york,Greenland','Spain,Greenland','NEW','12-261','12-219',NULL),
(370304,'biology','India, with much of its land area in the tropical latitudes, has how many species of birds?','More than 1200','India, with much of its land area in the tropical latitudes, has more than 1,200 species of birds.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:26,paragraph no:','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Patterns of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','More than 1300','More than 1800','More than 1200','More than 1400','NEW','12-261','12-219',NULL),
(370305,'biology','The largely tropical Amazonian rain forest is located in which continent having the greatest biodiversity on earth?','South America','The largely tropical Amazonian rain forest in South America has the greatest biodiversity on earth- it is home to more than 40,000 species of plants, 3,000 of fishes, 1,300 of birds, 427 of mammals, 427 of amphibians, 378 of reptiles and of more than 1,25,000 invertebrates.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:261,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Patterns of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','South Africa','South America','North America','Australia','NEW','12-261','12-219',NULL),
(370306,'biology','Which among the following hypothesis have been proposed by ecologists and evolutionary biologists?\n1) Tropical environments, unlike temperate ones, are less seasonal, relatively more constant and predictable.\n2) Speciation is generally a function of time\n3) There is more solar energy available in the tropics, which contributes to higher productivity','1,2, and 3','Ecologists and evolutionary biologists have proposed various hypotheses; some important ones are (a) Speciation is generally a function of time, unlike temperate regions subjected to frequent glaciations in the past, tropical latitudes have remained relatively undisturbed for millions of years and thus, had a long evolutionary time for species diversification, (b) Tropical environments, unlike temperate ones, are less seasonal, relatively more constant and predictable. Such constant environments promote niche specialisation and lead to a greater species diversity and (c) There is more solar energy available in the tropics, which contributes to higher productivity; this in turn might contribute indirectly to greater diversity.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:261,paragraph no:3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Patterns of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','only 1 and 2','only 1 and 3','1,2, and 3','only 2 and 3','NEW','12-261','12-219',NULL),
(370307,'biology','Species diversity as we move away from the equator towards the poles tends to show what effect?','Decreases','Species diversity Decreases as we move away from the equator towards the poles.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:261,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Patterns of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','Increases','Decreases','First increases then decreases','First decreases then increases','NEW','12-261','12-219',NULL),
(370308,'biology','___________is generally a function of time, unlike temperate regions subjected to frequent glaciations in the past','Speciation','Speciation is generally a function of time, unlike temperate regions subjected to frequent glaciations in the past\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:261,paragraph no:3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Patterns of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','fillup','Sp,eci,ati,on',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-261','12-219',NULL),
(370309,'biology','_______ lattitudes have remained relatively undisturbed for millions of years','Tropical','Speciation is generally a function of time, unlike temperate regions subjected to frequent glaciations in the past, tropical latitudes have remained relatively undisturbed for millions of years and thus, had a long evolutionary time for species diversification,\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:261,paragraph no:3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Patterns of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','Tropical','Temperate','Neither tropical nor temperate','Both a and b','NEW','12-261','12-219',NULL),
(370310,'biology','________ lattitudes had a long evolutionary time for species diversification','Tropical','Tropical latitudes have remained relatively undisturbed for millions of years and thus, had a long evolutionary time for species diversification\nevolutionary time for species diversification,\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:261,paragraph no:3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Patterns of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','Tropical','Temperate','Polar','Both a and b','NEW','12-261','12-219',NULL),
(370311,'biology','_____________ environment unlike temperate ones, are less seasonal, relatively more constant and predictable.','Tropical','Tropical environments, unlike temperate ones, are less seasonal, relatively more constant and predictable\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:262,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Patterns of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','Tropical','Temperate','Polar','Both a and b','NEW','12-262','12-220',NULL),
(370312,'biology','_______  are constant environments promote niche specialisation and lead to a greater species diversity','Tropical','Tropical environments, unlike temperate ones, are less seasonal, relatively more constant and predictable. Such constant environments promote niche specialisation and lead to a greater species diversity and\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:262,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Patterns of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','Tropical','Temperate','Polar','Both a and b','NEW','12-262','12-220',NULL),
(370313,'biology','Who was Alexander von Humboldt?','German Naturalist and Geographer','Alexander von Humboldt was the great German naturalist and geographer\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:262,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Patterns of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','German Naturalist and Geographer','American Naturalist and Geographer','English Naturalist and Geographer','Finnish Naturalist and Geographer','NEW','12-262','12-220',NULL),
(370314,'biology','The relation between species richness and area for a wide variety of taxa (angiosperm plants, birds, bats, freshwater fishes) turns out to be a','Rectangular hyperbola','The relation between species richness and area for a wide variety of taxa (angiosperm plants, birds, bats, freshwater fishes) turns out to be a rectangular hyperbola.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:262,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Patterns of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','Rectangular hyperbola','SIgmoid','Linear','Rectilinear','NEW','12-262','12-220',NULL),
(370315,'biology','In the equation related to species-area, log S = log C + Z log A, A represents ?','Area','In the equation log S = log C + Z log A where A= Area\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:262,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Patterns of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','Absorbance','Angle','Area','Altitude','NEW','12-262','12-220',NULL),
(311705,'biology','Which of the following is a viral disease of poultry birds? [2017]','Ranikhet','Coccidiosis is a protozoan disease.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Anthrax','Ranikhet','Coccidiosis','Red rot','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(370316,'biology','Species-area relationships among very large areas like the entire continents, the Z values will be in the range of ?','0.6 to 1.2','If you analyse the species-area relationships among very large areas like the entire continents, you will find that the slope of the line to be much steeper (Z values in the range of 0.6 to 1.2)..\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:262,paragraph no:3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Patterns of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','0.5 to 1.0','0.6 to 1.2','1.5 to 2.1','0.6 to 1.0','NEW','12-262','12-220',NULL),
(370317,'biology','The ecologists discovered that the value of Z lies in the range between','0.1 to 0.2','Ecologists have discovered that the value of Z lies in the range of 0.1 to 0.2, regardless of the taxonomic group or the region\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:262,paragraph no:3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Patterns of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','1.0 to 2.0','1.0 to 3.0','0.1 to 0.2','0.2 to 0.5','NEW','12-262','12-220',NULL),
(370318,'biology','According to the concept of species area relations','The number of species in an area increases with the size of the area.','The species-area relationship or species-area curve describes the relationship between the area of a habitat, or of part of a habitat, and the number of species found within that area. Ecologists have proposed a wide range of factors determining the slope and elevation of the species-area relationship.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:262,paragraph no:3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Patterns of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','The number of species in an area increases with the size of the area.','Larger species require larger habitat areas than do smaller snecies.','Most species within any given area are endemic','The larger the area, the greater the extinction rate.','NEW','12-262','12-220',NULL),
(370319,'biology','Who found that plots with more species showed less year-to-year variation in total biomass and increased diversity contributed to higher productivity?','David Tilman','David Tilman’s long-term ecosystem experiments using outdoor plots provide some tentative answers. Tilman found that plots with more species showed less year-to-year variation in total biomass and increased diversity contributed to higher productivity.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:263,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Patterns of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','Paul Ehrlich','Robert May','David Tilman','Robert Brown','NEW','12-263','12-221',NULL),
(370320,'biology','Who used the ‘rivet popper hypothesis\'','Paul Ehrlich','The ‘rivet popper hypothesis’ used by Stanford ecologist Paul Ehrlich.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:263,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Patterns of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','Paul Ehrlich','Robert May','David Tilman','Robert Brown','NEW','12-263','12-221',NULL),
(370321,'biology','Which of the following statement is true regarding the "Rivet Popper" hypothesis','Airplane corresponds to ecosystem and each rivet corresponds to a species that makes up the ecosystem.','In an airplane (ecosystem) all parts are joined together using thousands of rivets (species). If every passenger travelling in it starts popping a rivet to take home (causing a species to become extinct), it may not affect flight safety (proper functioning of the ecosystem) initially, but as more and more rivets are removed, the plane becomes dangerously weak over a period of time. Furthermore, which rivet is removed may also be critical. Loss of rivets on the wings (key species that drive major ecosystem functions) is obviously a more serious threat to flight safety than loss of a few rivets on the seats or windows inside the plane..\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:263,paragraph no:3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Patterns of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','In an airplane all parts are joined together using thousands of rivets, where the rivets are ecosystems and the planes are the biosphere','If every passenger pops a rivet to take home, it will never affect the flight, even though they are critical parts in a plane','With the removal of each critical rivet from the wings, the plane gets lighter and soars higher,','Airplane corresponds to ecosystem and each rivet corresponds to a species that makes up the ecosystem.','NEW','12-263','12-221',NULL),
(370501,'biology','The colonisation of Tropical Pacific islands has led to','Extinction of more than 2,000 native species of Birds','The colonisation of tropical Pacific Islands by humans is said to have led to the extinction of more than 2,000 species of native birds.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:263,paragraph no:3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Loss of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','Extinction of more than 2,000 native species of Birds','Endangerment of more than 2,000 species of frogs','Vulnerabilty of 1,500 species of insects','Extinction of more than 2,000 native species of reptiles','NEW','12-263','12-221',NULL),
(370502,'biology','Some examples of recent extinctions include?','Thylacine','Some examples of recent extinctions include the dodo (Mauritius), quagga (Africa), thylacine (Australia), Steller’s Sea Cow (Russia) and 3subspecies (Bali, Javan, Caspian) of tiger.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:263,paragraph no:3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Loss of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','Thylacine','Bumblebees','Cows','Sea Horse','NEW','12-263','12-221',NULL),
(370503,'biology','Grim scenario of extinctions is due to the fact that','More than 15500 species world-wide are facing the threat of extinction','Grim scenario of extinctions is the fact that more than 15,500 species world-wide are facing the threat of extinction.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:264,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Loss of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','More than 15500 species world-wide are facing the threat of extinction','More than 5,500 species world-wide are facing the threat of extinction','Less than 1500 species world-wide are facing the threat of extinction','Less than 15,000 species world-wide are facing the threat of extinction','NEW','12-264','12-222',NULL),
(370504,'biology','How much % of all bird species in the world face the threat of extinction?','12','12 % of all bird species, 23 % of all mammal species, 32 % of all amphibian species and 31% of all gymnosperm species in the world face the threat of extinction.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:264,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Loss of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','16','90','12','31','NEW','12-264','12-222','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/370504q.png'),
(370505,'biology','How much % of all mammal species in the world face the threat of extinction?','23','12 % of all bird species, 23 % of all mammal species, 32 % of all amphibian species and 31% of all gymnosperm species in the world face the threat of extinction.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:264,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Loss of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','20','23','15','22','NEW','12-264','12-222',NULL),
(370506,'biology','How much % of all amphibian species in the world face the threat of extinction?','32','12 % of all bird species, 23 % of all mammal species, 32 % of all amphibian species and 31% of all gymnosperm species in the world face the threat of extinction.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:264,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Loss of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','32','50','90','14','NEW','12-264','12-222',NULL),
(370507,'biology','How much % of all gymnosperm species in the world face the threat of extinction?','31','12 % of all bird species, 23 % of all mammal species, 32 % of all amphibian species and 31% of all gymnosperm species in the world face the threat of extinction.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:264,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Loss of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','40','20','60','31','NEW','12-264','12-222',NULL),
(370508,'biology','Since the origin and diversification of life on earth how many episodes of mass extinction of species have occured?','5','Since the origin and diversification of life on earth there were five episodes of mass extinction of species.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:264,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Loss of Biodiversity',NULL,'2','mcq','4','5','7','10','NEW','12-264','12-222',NULL),
(370601,'biology','The term "The Evil Quartet" is related to','Four major causes ef biodiversity losses.','The four major causes of biodiversity loss are also known as The Evil Quartet.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:264,paragraph no:4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Causes of biodiversity losses',NULL,'2','mcq','Four major causes of forest loss','Four major causes of population explosion','Four major causes of air pollution','Four major causes ef biodiversity losses.','NEW','12-264','12-222',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(370602,'biology','Which is the most important cause driving animals and plants to extinction?','Habitat loss and fragmentation','Habitat loss and fragmentation is the most important cause driving animals and plants to extinction.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:264,paragraph no:5','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Causes of biodiversity losses',NULL,'2','mcq','Habitat loss and fragmentation','Alien species invasions','Over-exploitation','Co-extinctions','NEW','12-264','12-222',NULL),
(370603,'biology','The ‘lungs of the planet’','Amazon Rain Forests','The Amazon rain forest it is so huge that it is called the ‘lungs of the planet’\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:264,paragraph no:5','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Causes of biodiversity losses',NULL,'2','mcq','Amazon Rain Forests','The Sunderbans','The Savannahs','The Alps','NEW','12-264','12-222',NULL),
(370604,'biology','The Amazon rain forests are being cut and cleared for cultivatiion of which crop','Soya beans','The Amazon rain forest (it is so huge that it is called the ‘lungs of the planet’) harbouring probably millions of species is being cut and cleared for cultivating soya beans\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:264,paragraph no:5','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Causes of biodiversity losses',NULL,'2','mcq','Cereals','Soya beans','Corn','Cotton','NEW','12-264','12-222',NULL),
(370605,'biology','Humans have always depended on nature for food and shelter, but when ‘need’ turns to ‘greed’, it leads to','Overexploitation of Natural resources','Humans have always depended on nature for food and shelter, but when ‘need’ turns to ‘greed’, it leads to over -exploitation of natural resources.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:264,paragraph no:6','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Causes of biodiversity losses',NULL,'2','mcq','Overexploitation of Natural resources','Underexploitation of technology','Underexploitation of Natural resources','Underexploitation of manpower','NEW','12-264','12-222',NULL),
(370606,'biology','Steller’s sea cow and passenger pigeon are extinct because of which activity','Overexploitation by humans','Many species extinctions in the last 500 years (Steller’s sea cow, passenger pigeon) were due to overexploitation by humans\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:265,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Causes of biodiversity losses',NULL,'2','mcq','Alien species invasions','Overexploitation by humans','Co-extinctions','Natural extinctions','NEW','12-265','12-223',NULL),
(370607,'biology','What happens when an alien species is introduced unintentionally or deliberately in the environment?','Turns invasive and causes decline in indigenous species','When alien species are introduced unintentionally or deliberately for whatever purpose, some of them turn invasive, and cause decline or extinction of indigenous species.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:265,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Causes of biodiversity losses',NULL,'2','mcq','Turns invasive and causes decline in indigenous species','Reverses extinction and causes rise in indigenous species','Causes and promotes growth of indigenous species','Turns invasive and causes flourishment of indigenous species','NEW','12-265','12-223',NULL),
(370608,'biology','The Nile perch introduced into which lake caused extinction of native species?','Victoria lake','The Nile perch introduced into Lake Victoria in east Africa led eventually to the extinction of an ecologically unique assemblage of more than 200 species of cichlid fish in the lake.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:265,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Causes of biodiversity losses',NULL,'2','mcq','Chilka lake','Victoria lake','Pink lake','Pulicat lake','NEW','12-265','12-223',NULL),
(370609,'biology','Introduction of Nile perch into Lake Victoria in East Africa led to the extinction of which species?','Cichlid fish','The Nile perch introduced into Lake Victoria in east Africa led eventually to the extinction of an ecologically unique assemblage of more than 200 species of cichlid fish in the lake.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:265,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Causes of biodiversity losses',NULL,'2','mcq','Cichlid fish','Goldfish','Piranha','Cutla fish','NEW','12-265','12-223',NULL),
(370610,'biology','Illegal introduction of which fish as a part of aquaculture threatens other indigeneous species?','Clarias gariepinus','The recent illegal introduction of the African catfish Clarias gariepinus for aquaculture purposes is posing a threat to the indigenous catfishes in our rivers.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:265,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Causes of biodiversity losses',NULL,'2','mcq','Clarias gariepinus','Clarias gariepricus','Sircaria gariepinus','Sircaria gariepicus','NEW','12-265','12-223',NULL),
(370611,'biology','When a species becomes extinct, the plant and animal species associated with it in an obligatory way also become extinct.\nWhat is this phenomenon called?','Co-extinctions','When a species becomes extinct, the plant and animal species associated with it in an obligatory way also become extinct. When a host fish species becomes extinct\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:265,paragraph no:3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Causes of biodiversity losses',NULL,'2','mcq','Alien species invasions','Habitat loss and fragmentation','Over-exploitation','Co-extinctions','NEW','12-265','12-223',NULL),
(370612,'biology','Which of the following caused damage and posed a threat to our native species?\n1) Carrot grass (Parthenium)\n2) Lantana\n3) Water hyacinth (Eicchornia)','1,2 and 3','The environmental damage caused and threat posed to our native species by invasive weed species like carrot grass (Parthenium), Lantana and water hyacinth (Eicchornia).\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:265,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Causes of biodiversity losses',NULL,'2','mcq','only 2 and 3','only 1 and 3','1,2 and 3','only 1 and 2','NEW','12-265','12-223',NULL),
(370613,'biology','Match the following','Nile Perch in Lake Victoria-invasive species,Narrowly utilitarian-Obvious reasons for biodiversity conservation,Main cause of biodiversity loss-Habitat destruction,Hot spots-High endemism','Nile Perch in Lake Victoria is an invasive species. Narrowly utilitarian of species is an obvious reason for biodiversity conservation. Main cause of biodiversity loss is Habitat destruction. Hot spots have high endemism.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:265 and 266','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Causes of biodiversity losses',NULL,'3','match','Nile Perch in Lake Victoria,invasive species','Narrowly utilitarian,Obvious reasons for biodiversity conservation','Main cause of biodiversity loss,Habitat destruction','Hot spots,High endemism','NEW','12-265','12-223',NULL),
(370614,'biology','When a host fish species becomes extinct, its unique assemblage of ____ also meets the same fate.','Parasite','When a species becomes extinct, the plant and animal species associated with it in an obligatory way also become extinct. When a host fish species becomes extinct, its unique assemblage of parasites also meets the same fate. Another example is the case of a coevolved plant-pollinator mutualism where extinction of one invariably leads to the extinction of the other.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:265,paragraph no:3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Causes of biodiversity losses',NULL,'2','mcq','Parasite','Ammensal','Prey','All of them','NEW','12-265','12-223',NULL),
(370615,'biology','Biodiversity loss occurs due to - \n1. Habitat loss and fragmentation; \n2. Co-extinction\n3. Over-exploitation\n4. Alien species invasion.','All of them','Biodiversity loss occurs due to given following - A. Habitat loss and fragmentation; B. Co-extinction; C. over-exploitation; D. Alien species invasion.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:264,paragraph no:4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Causes of biodiversity losses',NULL,'2','mcq','1,2 only','2,3 only','2,4 only','All of them','NEW','12-264','12-222',NULL),
(370616,'biology','An exotic species that is introduced to a new area, spreads rapidly and eliminates the native species is called','Invasive species','An exotic species that is introduced to a new area, spreads rapidly and eliminates native species is called an invasive species.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:265,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Causes of biodiversity losses',NULL,'3','mcq','Immigrant species','Invasive species','Destructive species','Native species','NEW','12-265','12-223',NULL),
(370701,'biology','Which of the following statement regarding Biodiversity Conservation is incorrect?','Less than 5 % of the drugs currently sold in the market worldwide are derived from plants','The narrowly utilitarian arguments for conserving biodiversity are obvious; humans derive countless direct economic benefits from nature- food (cereals, pulses, fruits), firewood, fibre, construction material, industrial products (tannins, lubricants, dyes, resins, perfumes ) and products of medicinal importance. More than 25 % of the drugs currently sold in the market worldwide are derived from plants and 25,000 species of plants contribute to the traditional medicines used by native peoples around the world.Nobody knows how many more medicinally\nuseful plants there are in tropical rain forests waiting to be explored. With increasing resources put into ‘bioprospecting’ (exploring molecular, genetic and species-level diversity for products of economic importance), nations endowed with rich biodiversity can expect to reap enormous benefits.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:265,paragraph no:5','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Biodiversity Conservation',NULL,'2','mcq','The narrowly utilitarian arguments for conserving biodiversity are obvious','Humans derive countless direct economic benefits from nature','Less than 5 % of the drugs currently sold in the market worldwide are derived from plants','By increasing resources put into ‘bioprospecting’ ,nations endowed with rich biodiversity can expect to reap enormous benefits.','NEW','12-265','12-223',NULL),
(370702,'biology','How much % of the drugs currently sold in the market worldwide are derived from plants?','More than 25 %','More than 25 % of the drugs currently sold in the market worldwide are derived from plants\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:265,paragraph no:5','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Biodiversity Conservation',NULL,'2','mcq','More than 35 %','More than 20 %','More than 25 %','More than 40 %','NEW','12-265','12-223',NULL),
(370703,'biology','How many species of plants contribute to the traditional medicines used by native peoples around the world?','25,000','25,000 species of plants contribute to the traditional medicines used by native peoples around the world.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:265,paragraph no:5','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Biodiversity Conservation',NULL,'2','mcq','30,000','25,000','2,500','20,000','NEW','12-265','12-223',NULL),
(370704,'biology','Exploring molecular, genetic and species-level diversity for products of economic importance like medicines is called','Bioprospecting','Exploring molecular, genetic and species-level diversity for products of economic importance like medicines is called Bioprospecting','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Biodiversity Conservation',NULL,'2','mcq','Bioremediation','Bioprospecting','Bioprocessing','Ecosystem services','NEW','12-265','12-223',NULL),
(370705,'biology','The narrowly utilitarian arguments for conserving include which of the following from the given list? \n(i) Industrial products like dyes, lubricants\n(ii) Ecosystem services like photosynthesis for air\n(iii) Pollinators layer of bees, birds and bats\n(iv) Firewood, fibre and construction material\n(v) The aesthetic pleasure of walking through thick forests\n(vi) Products of medicinal importance\n(vii) Watching spring flowers in full bloom\n(viii) Our moral duty to care for the well-being of each species.','(i), (iv) and (vi)','The narrowly utilitarian arguments for conserving include the following- (i) Industrial products like dyes, lubricants, (iv) Firewood, fibre and construction material, (vi) Products of medicinal importance.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:265,paragraph no:5','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Biodiversity Conservation',NULL,'2','mcq','(i), (ii), (v) and (vii)','(ii), (iii), (v) and (vii)','(i), (iv) and (vi)','(iii), (v), (vii) and (viii)','NEW','12-265','12-223',NULL),
(370706,'biology','Amazon forests are called the Lungs of The World because','Amazon forest is estimated to produce, through photosynthesis, 20 % of the total O₂ in the earth’s atmosphere','Amazon forest is estimated to produce, through photosynthesis, 20 % of the total O₂ in the earth’s atmosphere\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Biodiversity Conservation',NULL,'2','mcq','Amazon forest is estimated to produce, through photosynthesis, 20 % of the total O₂ in the earth’s atmosphere','Amazon looks like a lung shaped area on the map','Amazon is known to be a pioneer source of medicine for respiratory disease','Amazon forests aere richly diverse in Biodiversity','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(370707,'biology','What is the process without which plants cannot give us fruits or seeds and propagate?','Pollination','Pollination is the process without which plants cannot give us fruits or seeds.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Biodiversity Conservation',NULL,'2','mcq','Pollination','Invasion','Scalloping','Inflorescence','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(370708,'biology','The ethical argument for conserving biodiversity is related to conservation of which of the following ?\n1) Millions of plant species\n2) Millions of animal species\n3) Millions of microbe species','1,2 and 3','The ethical argument for conserving biodiversity relates to what we owe to millions of plant, animal and microbe species with whom we share this planet.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Biodiversity Conservation',NULL,'2','mcq','only 1 and 2','only 2 and 3','1,2 and 3','only 1 and 3','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(370709,'biology','Choose the incorrect statement regarding Biodiversity Conservation','When we conserve and protect the whole ecosystem, its biodiversity at all\nlevels is not protected','The ethical argument for conserving biodiversity relates to what we owe to millions of plant, animal and microbe species with whom we share this planet. Philosophically or spiritually, we need to realise that every species has an intrinsic value, even if it may not be of current or any economic value to us. We have a moral duty to care for their well-being and pass on our biological legacy in good order to future generations.When we conserve and protect the whole ecosystem, its biodiversity at all levels is protected\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Biodiversity Conservation',NULL,'2','mcq','We owe to millions of plant, animal and microbe species with whom we share this planet.','Philosophically or spiritually, every species has an intrinsic value,','When we conserve and protect the whole ecosystem, its biodiversity at all\nlevels is not protected','All of them','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(370710,'biology','The maximum number of species extinction due to habitat destruction is seen with regards to','Endemic species','The number of species that become extinct due to habitat destruction is greatest in tropical ecosystems with many endemic species.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Biodiversity Conservation',NULL,'3','mcq','Endemic species','Epidemic species','Pandemic species','Extinct species','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(370801,'biology','Conservation and protection of the whole ecosystem, its biodiversity at all levels and saving the entire forest to save the tiger. This approach is called ?','In situ','When we conserve and protect the whole ecosystem, its biodiversity at all levels is protected - we save the entire forest to save the tiger. This approach is called in situ (on site) conservation.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','Ex situ','In situ','Ex-vivo','In-vitro','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(370802,'biology','When an animal or plant is endangered or threatened and needs urgent measures to save it from extinction What approach is desirable approach?','Ex situ','When there are situations where an animal or plant is endangered or threatened (organisms facing a very high risk of extinction in the wild in the near future) and needs urgent measures to save it from extinction, ex situ (off site) conservation is the desirable approach.','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','Ex situ','In situ','Ex-vivo','In-vivo','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(370803,'biology','Which of the following are related to in situ conservation?','All the above','In situ conservation– Faced with the conflict between development and conservation, many nations find it unrealistic and economically not feasible to conserve all their biological wealth.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','Conflict between development and conservation','Unrealistic goals and plans','Economically not feasible','All the above','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(370804,'biology','Choose the incorrect statement regarding Biodiversity Conservation','Initially 34 biodiversity hotspots were identified but subsequently nine more have been added to the list, bringing the total number of biodiversity hotspots in the world to 42','Invariably, the number of species waiting to be saved from extinction far exceeds the conservation resources available. On a global basis, this problem has been addressed by eminent conservationists. They identified for maximum protection certain ‘biodiversity hotspots’ regions with very high levels of species richness and high degree of endemism (that is, species confined to that region and not found anywhere else). Initially 25 biodiversity hotspots were identified but subsequently nine more have been added to the list, bringing the total number of biodiversity hotspots in the world to 34. These hotspots are also regions of accelerated habitat loss.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','Invariably, the number of species waiting to be saved from extinction far exceeds the conservation resources available and this problem has been raised by eminent conservationalists globally','Conservationalists have identified for maximum protection, certain ‘biodiversity hotspots’ regions with very high levels of species richness and high degree of endemism','Initially 34 biodiversity hotspots were identified but subsequently nine more have been added to the list, bringing the total number of biodiversity hotspots in the world to 42','These hotspots are also regions of accelerated habitat loss.','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(370805,'biology','Initially how many biodiversity hotspots were identified?','25','Initially 25 biodiversity hotspots were identified but subsequently nine more have been added to the list, bringing the total number of biodiversity hotspots in the world to 34.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','25','34','9','21','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(370806,'biology','Total number of biodiversity hotspots in the world ?','34','Initially 25 biodiversity hotspots were identified but subsequently nine more have been added to the list, bringing the total number of biodiversity hotspots in the world to 34.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','34','40','49','35','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(370807,'biology','How many % of the earth\'s land area is covered by all the biodiversity hotspots?','Less than 2%','All the biodiversity hotspots put together cover less than 2 % of the earth’s land area\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','More than 3%','Less than 2%','More than 5 %','Less than 10 %','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(370808,'biology','Which of these areas cover our country’s exceptionally high biodiversity regions?','Western Ghats and Sri Lanka, Indo-Burma and Himalaya','3of these hotspots – Western Ghats and Sri Lanka, Indo-Burma and Himalaya – cover our country’s exceptionally high biodiversity regions.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','Western Ghats and Nagaland, Indo-Burma and Himalaya','Eastern Ghats and Sri Lanka, Indo-Burma and Himalaya','Western Ghats and Sri Lanka, Indo-Burma and Himalaya','Western Ghats and Sri Lanka, Meghalaya and Nepal','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(370809,'biology','In India, ecologically unique and biodiversity-rich regions are legally protected as  which of the following?','All of these','In India, ecologically unique and biodiversity-rich regions are legally protected as biosphere reserves, national parks and sanctuaries.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','Biosphere reserves','National parks','Sanctuaries','All of these','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370810,'biology','Number of Biosphere reserves in India','14','India now has ,14 biosphere reserves.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','15','18','20','14','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370811,'biology','Number of National Parks in India','90','India now has ,90 national parks.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','100','90','60','30','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
