(370813,'biology','Match the sacred groves with their location Western Ghat regions of Karnataka and Maharashtra and the Sarguja, Chanda and Bastar areas of Madhya Pradesh','Khasi and Jaintia-Meghalaya,Aravalli Hills-Rajasthan,Western Ghats-Karnataka and Maharashtra, Sarguja Chanda and Bastar,Madhya Pradesh','Sacred groves are found in Khasi and Jaintia Hills in Meghalaya, Aravalli Hills of Rajasthan, Western Ghat regions of Karnataka and Maharashtra and the Sarguja, Chanda and Bastar areas of Madhya Pradesh.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','match','Khasi and Jaintia,Meghalaya','Aravalli Hills,Rajasthan','Western Ghats,Karnataka and Maharashtra','Sarguja Chanda and Bastar,Madhya Pradesh','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370814,'biology','Sacred groves are the last refuges for a large number of rare and threatened plants in which state?','Meghalaya','In Meghalaya, the sacred groves are the last refuges for a large number of rare and threatened plants\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','Kerala','Meghalaya','Nagaland','Mizoram','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370815,'biology','In which approach threatened animals and plants are taken out from their natural habitat and placed in special settings where they can be protected and given special care','Ex situ conservation','In Ex situ conservation approach, threatened animals and plants are taken out from their natural habitat and placed in special setting where they can be protected and given special care.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','Ex situ conservation','In situ conservation','In-vivo Conseration','In-vitro Conservation','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370816,'biology','Ex situ conservation approach includes which of the following?','All the above','Ex situ Conservation serves Zoological parks, botanical gardens and wildlife safari parks.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','Botanical gardens','Zoological parks','Wildlife safari parks','All the above','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370817,'biology','Gametes of threatened species can be preserved in viable and fertile condition for long periods using which of the following techniques?','Cryopreservation','Gametes of threatened species can be preserved in viable and fertile condition for long periods using cryopreservation techniques\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','Cryopreservation','Ex-situ conservation','In-situ conservation','Tissue Culture','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370818,'biology','Plants can be propagated using which technique after preservation?','Tissue Culture','In cryopreservation technique eggs can be fertilised , in vitro and plants can be propagated using tissue culture methods.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','Tissue Culture','Grafting','Vegetative propagation','ZIFT','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370819,'biology','What is the difference between a threatened species and an endangered species ?','A threatened species means that the population is likely to become endangered, an endangered species has population numbers so low that it is likely to become extinct.','Threatened Species are any species which are vulnerable to endangerment in the near future. Species that are threatened are sometimes characterised by the population dynamics measure of critical depensation, a mathematical measure of biomass related to population growth rate.An endangered species is a species that is very likely to become extinct in the near future, either worldwide or in a particular political jurisdiction. Endangered species may be at risk due to factors such as habitat loss, poaching and invasive species.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','A threatened species is already extinct, an endangered species means that the population\'s numbers have increased greatly over the last 5 years.','A threatened species and an endangered species are the same thing.','A threatened species means that the population is likely to become endangered, an endangered species is already extinct','A threatened species means that the population is likely to become endangered, an endangered species has population numbers so low that it is likely to become extinct.','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370820,'biology','An endemic species is','One found naturally in just one geographic area','Endemic species are species confined to that region and not found anywhere else\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','One found in many different geographic areas.','One found naturally in just one geographic area','One found only on islands.','One that has been introduced to a new geographic area.','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(370821,'biology','Sacred groves are specially useful in','Conserving rare and threatened species','Sacred grooves are method for insitu conservation of biodiversity and conserving rare threatened species. Sacred groves of India are forest fragments of varying sizes, which are communally protected, and which usually have a significant religious connotation for the protecting community.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','Generating environmental awareness','Preventing soil erosion','Year-round flow of water in rivers','Conserving rare and threatened species','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370822,'biology','Seeds of different genetic strains of commercially important plants can be kept for long periods in','Seed banks','Now gametes of threatened species can be preserved in viable and fertile condition for long periods using cryopreservation techniques, eggs can be fertilised in vitro, and plants can be propagated using tissue culture methods. Seeds of different genetic strains of commercially important plants can be kept for long periods in seed banks.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','Seed banks','Sperm banks','Gene banks','Herbarium','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370823,'biology','In 1992, where did the UNO organise a summit to discuss concern about biodiversity?','Rio De Janeiro','The historic Convention on Biological Diversity (‘The Earth Summit’) held in Rio de Janeiro in 1992, called upon all nations to take appropriate measures for conservation of biodiversity and sustainable utilisation of its benefits\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','Rio De Janeiro','New York','Los Angeles','Tokyo','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370824,'biology','The World Summit,2002 on Sustainable Development was held at?','Johannesburg, South Africa','The World Summit on Sustainable Development held in 2002 in Johannesburg, South Africa, 190 countries pledged their commitment to achieve by 2010, a significantreduction in the current rate of biodiversity loss at global, regional and local levels\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','Washington,USA','Johannesburg, South Africa','London,England','New Delhi, India','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370825,'biology','How many countries pledged their commitment at the World Summit on Sustainable Development in 2002 [ RE NEET 2022]','190','The World Summit on Sustainable Development held in 2002 in Johannesburg, South Africa, 190 countries pledged their commitment to achieve by 2010, a significant reduction in the current rate of biodiversity loss at global, regional and local levels\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','190','109','119','199','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370826,'biology','Which of the following was discussed and pledged to, in the World Summit on Sustainable Development, in 2002?','Goal to significantly reduce the current rate of biodiversity loss at global, regional and local levels by 2010','The World Summit on Sustainable Development held in 2002 in Johannesburg, South Africa, 190 countries pledged their commitment to achieve by 2010, a significant reduction in the current rate of biodiversity loss at global, regional and local levels\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','Goal to significantly reduce the current rate of biodiversity loss at global, regional and local levels by 2010','Goal to significantly reduce the current rate of biodiversity loss at global, regional and local levels by 2020','Goal to significantly reduce the current rate of biodiversity loss at global level only by 2010','Goal to significantly reduce the current rate of biodiversity loss at global level only by 2020','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370827,'biology','What is the significance of sacred groves in the context of conservation in India?','They provide a sanctuary for endangered plant species.','India has also a history of religious and cultural traditions\nthat emphasised protection of nature. In many cultures, tracts of forest were set aside, and all the trees and wildlife within were venerated and given total protection.','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'1','mcq','They serve as tourist attractions.','They play a crucial role in religious ceremonies.','They provide a sanctuary for endangered plant species.','They are centers for commercial logging.','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370828,'biology','Which of the following regions in India is NOT known for its sacred groves?','Tamil Nadu','Such sacred groves are found in Khasi and Jaintia\nHills in Meghalaya, Aravalli Hills of Rajasthan, Western Ghat regions of Karnataka and Maharashtra and the Sarguja, Chanda and Bastar areas of Madhya Pradesh','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,NULL,'mcq','Madhya Pradesh','Maharashtra','Tamil Nadu','Karnatka','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370829,'biology','What practice is commonly associated with sacred groves in various Indian cultures?','Ritualistic ceremonies and prayers','India has also a history of religious and cultural traditions\nthat emphasised protection of nature. In many cultures, tracts of forest were set aside, and all the trees and wildlife within were venerated and given total protection.','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'1','mcq','Commercial exploitation of resources','Ritualistic ceremonies and prayers','Industrial development projects','Agricultural expansion','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370830,'biology','Which are the largest sacred groves in India ?','Deodar in Shimla, Himachal Pradesh','The largest sacred groves in India are the Hariyali in Chamoli district, Uttrakhand and the deodar in Shipin, Shimla, Himachal Pradesh.','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'1','mcq','Jaintia hills in Meghalaya','Deodar in Shimla, Himachal Pradesh','Aravalli hills of Rajasthan','Chanda and Bastar areas of Madhya Pradesh','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370831,'biology','What is Devarkadu?','Larger sacred groves in Karnatka','The sacred groves in Karnatka can be classified into two types: smaller sacred groves called Kans and the larger ones are called devarkadu or Devarkan. The smaller groves are entirely protected and the larger ones act as resources providing both ecological secuirity to the people ans sustainance.','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'1','mcq','Smaller groves in Karnatka','larger groves in Karnatka','Smaller groves in Rajasthan','largerv groves in Rajasthan','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
