(370846,'biology','Which of the following is not the features of sacred groves?','extinct in the wild animals are maintained','" In many cultures, tracts of forest\nwere set aside, and all the trees and wildlife within were venerated and\ngiven total protection. Such sacred groves are found in Khasi and Jaintia\nHills in Meghalaya, Aravalli Hills of Rajasthan,"','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'1','mcq','tracts of forest\nare set aside','trees and wildlife within are venerated','trees and wildlife within are\ngiven total protection','extinct in the wild animals are maintained','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370847,'biology','There are\nmany animals that have become extinct in the wild but continue to be\nmaintained in______','zoological parks','"There are\nmany animals that have become extinct in the wild but continue to be\nmaintained in Zoological parks','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'3','mcq','zoological parks','Wildlife sanctuary','Sacred groves','National parks','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370848,'biology','In recent years ______ conservation has\nadvanced beyond keeping threatened species in enclosures. Fill in the blanks','4. Both 1 and 3','In recent years ex situ conservation has\nadvanced beyond keeping threatened species in enclosures. Ex situ is also called as off site Conservation','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'1','mcq','1. Ex situ','2. In situ','3. Off site','4. Both 1 and 3','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370849,'biology','In Vitro technique can be used in','Fertilization of eggs outside the body','Now gametes\nof threatened species can be preserved in viable and fertile condition for\nlong periods using cryopreservation techniques, eggs can be fertilised in\nvitro, and plants can be propagated using tissue culture methods.','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','Cryopreservation','Propagation of plants using tissue culture method','Fertilization of eggs outside the body','None of these','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370850,'biology','How many nations had been invited in the Earth Summit?','All the nations','The historic Convention\non Biological Diversity (‘The Earth Summit’) held in Rio de Janeiro in\n1992, called upon all nations to take appropriate measures for\nconservation of biodiversity and sustainable utilisation of its benefits.','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'3','mcq','190','All the nations','192','202','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370851,'biology','How many nations attended the World Summit?','190','In\na follow-up, the World Summit on Sustainable Development held in 2002\nin Johannesburg, South Africa, 190 countries pledged their commitment\nto achieve by 2010, a significant reduction in the current rate of biodiversity\nloss at global, regional and local levels.','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'1','mcq','190','192','202','180','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370852,'biology','When was Earth Summit held?','1992','"The historic Convention\non Biological Diversity (‘The Earth Summit’) held in Rio de Janeiro in\n1992, called upon all nations to take appropriate measures for\nconservation of biodiversity and sustainable utilisation of its benefits."','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'2','mcq','2002','2010','1992','1990','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370853,'biology','When was World Summit held?','2002','"In\na follow-up, the World Summit on Sustainable Development held in 2002\nin Johannesburg, South Africa, 190 countries pledged their commitment\nto achieve by 2010, a significant reduction in the current rate of biodiversity\nloss at global, regional and local levels."','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> How do we conserve Biodiversity?',NULL,'1','mcq','2002','2010','1992','1990','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370901,'biology','Which of the following regions of the globe exhibits highest species diversity? [NEET 2020]','Amazon forests','Amazon forests are the region of the globe exhibiting highest species diversity. As an ecosystem, the Amazon is one of the most biodiverse places on earth. Over 3 million species live in the rainforest, and over 2,500 tree species (or one-third of all tropical trees that exist on earth) help to create and sustain this vibrant ecosystem.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Western Ghats of India','Madagascar','Himalayas','Amazon forests','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370902,'biology','According to Robert May, the global species diversity is about [NEET 2020]','7 million','Some extreme estimates range from 20 to 50 million, but a more conservative and scientifically sound estimate made by Robert May places the global species diversity at about 7 million. If we accept Robert May\'s global estimates, only 22 % of the total species have been recorded so far.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:259,paragraph no:5','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1.5 million','20 million','50 million','7 million','NEW','12-259','12-217',NULL),
(370903,'biology','Which of the following is the most important for animals and plants being driven to extinction? [NEET 2019]','Habitat loss and fragmentation','Habitat loss and fragmentation is the most important cause of driving the animals and plants to extinction. When large habitats are broken into small fragments due to various human activities, mammals and birds requiring large territories and certain animals with migratory habitats are badly affected, leading to population declines. The same can be applicable to the plant (forest) loss and degradation as millions of species are being cut and cleared for the expansion of agricultural land, harvesting timber, forest fire, as well as overgrazing.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:264,paragraph no:5','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Alien species invasion','Habitat loss and fragmentation','Drought and floods','Economic exploitation','NEW','12-264','12-222',NULL),
(370904,'biology','Decline in the population of indian native fishes due to introduction of Clarias gariepinus in river Yamuna can be categorised as \n[Odisha NEET 2019]','Alien species invasion','The recent illegal introduction of the African catfish Clarias gariepinus for aquaculture purposes is posing a threat to the indigenous catfishes in indian rivers. This is an example of alien species invasion \nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:265,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Co-extinction','Habitat fragmentation','Over-exploitation','Alien species invasion','NEW','12-265','12-223',NULL),
(370905,'biology','Alexander von Humboldt described for the first time [NEET 2017]','Species area relationships','Alexander von Humboldt described species area relationship for the first time. He observed that within a region, species richness increases with increasing explored area, but only upto a limit.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:262,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Laws of limiting factor','Species area relationships','Population growth equation','Ecological biodiversity','NEW','12-262','12-220',NULL),
(370906,'biology','Which of the following is correctly matched? [NEET 2016]','Parthenium hysterophorus - Threat to biodiversity','Parthenium hysterophorus is commonly known as congress grass or carrot weed. It is herbaceous annual plant of Family Asteraceae. It is a deadly invasive, noxious weed infesting cropped and non-cropped areas. It rapidly colonises area replacing the native vegetation and causes a number of human health related problems such as skin allergy, rhinitis and eye irritations. Also, being toxic and unpalatable it causes fodder scarcity. Hence, it is considered a threat to the biodiversity.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:265,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Aerenchyma - Opuntia','Age pyramid - Biome','Parthenium hysterophorus - Threat to biodiversity','Stratification - Population','NEW','12-265','12-223',NULL),
(370907,'biology','Red list contains data or information on [NEET 2016]','Threatened species','A red data book or red list is a catalogue of taxa facing risk of extinction. Red data book or red list was initiated in 1963.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:263,paragraph no:4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','All economically important plants','Plants whose products are in international trade','Threatened species','Marine vertebrates only.','NEW','12-263','12-221',NULL),
(370908,'biology','Which is the national aquatic animal of India? [NEET 2016]','River dolphin','River dolphin found in holy river Ganga, Brahmaputra, Indus and its tributaries is the National aquatic animal of India. Presence of river dolphin in Ganga indicates pure and freshwater.','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Blue whale','Sea-horse','Gangetic shark','River dolphin','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(370909,'biology','Which of the following is the most important cause of animals and plants being driven to extinction? [NEET 2016]','Habitat loss and fragmentation','Destruction of natural habitat causes the most serious threat to the biodiversity. Over-population, urbanisation and industrialisation lead to the destruction or fragmentation of natural habitats to fulfill the requirement of additional land. Loss of habitat results in annihilation of plants, microorganisms and forcing out of animals which in alien lands die out after some time. Fragmentation of habitats results in disruption of complex interactions amongst species, destruction of species in the cleared regions, annihilation of species restricted to deeper undisturbed parts of forests and decreased biodiversity in the habitat fragments\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:264,paragraph no:5','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Habitat loss and fragmentation','Co-extinctions','Over-exploitation','Alien species invasion','NEW','12-264','12-222',NULL),
(370910,'biology','A species facing extremely high risk of extinction in the immediate future is called [AIPMT 2014]','Critically endangered','The taxon under critically endangered category are facing very high risk of extinction in the wild and can become extinct at any moment in the immediate future.','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Vulnerable','Endemic','Critically endangered','Extinct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(370911,'biology','The organization which publishes the Red list of species is [AIPMT 2014]','IUCN','IUCN is International Union of Conservation of Nature and Natural Resources which is now called World Conservation Union (WCU). It has its headquarters at Morges, Switzerland. It maintains a red data book or red list which is a catalogue of taxa facing risk of extinction. Red data book or red list was initiated in 1963. The Red list of year 2000 has made assessment of 18,000 species.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:259,paragraph no:5','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','ICFRE','IUCN','UNEP','WWF','NEW','12-259','12-217',NULL),
(370912,'biology','Given below is a  pie chart diagram of the extent of global diversity of invertebrates. \nWhat groups the four portions (A-D) represent respectively? (3704)','A-Insects, B-Molluscs, C-Crustaceans, D-Other animal groups.','A-Insects,\nB-Molluscs,\nC-Crustaceans,\nD-Other animal groups.','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A-Insects, B-Crustaceans, C-Other animal groups, D-Molluscs','A-Crustaceans, B-Insects, C-Molluscs, D-Other animal groups','A-Molluscs, B-Other animal groups, C-Crustaceans, D-Insects','A-Insects, B-Molluscs, C-Crustaceans, D-Other animal groups.','NEW','12-260','12-218','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/370912q.png'),
(370913,'biology','Which of the following represent maximum number of species among global biodiversity? [NEET 2013;2012]','Fungi','Fungi is a large kingdom of over 72,000 species. They are achlorophyllous, heterotrophic, spore forming, non-vascular, eukaryotic organisms which contain chitin or fungal cellulose in their walls and possess glycogen as food reserve. They are major decomposers of many  ecosystems and are associate of many organisms.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:260,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Fungi','Mosses and Ferns','Algae','Lichens','NEW','12-260','12-218',NULL),
(370914,'biology','Which of the following has maximum genetic diversity in India? [Karnataka NEET 2013, 2011]','Rice','Genetic diversity is the diversity in the numbers and types of genes as well as chromosomes present in different species and the variations in the genes and their alleles in the same species. Oryza sativa (rice) has 32,000-50,000 genes.','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Mango','Wheat','Groundnut','Rice','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(370915,'biology','Which organization publishes the ‘Red Data Book’? [Karnataka NEET 2013]','IUCN','IUCN publishes the ‘Red Data Book’\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:259,paragraph no:5','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','IUCN','UNEP','WWF','GEF','NEW','12-259','12-217',NULL),
(370916,'biology','Which one of the following have the highest number of species in nature? [NEET 2019]','Insects','Insects have highest number of species found in nature. The insecta is the largest class of animals. It has over 7,00,000 species. The insects are the most successful land invertebrates and the only major competitors with humans for dominance in the world.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:260,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Fungi','Insects','Birds','Angiosperms','NEW','12-260','12-218',NULL),
(370936,'biology','The Earth Summit held in Rio de Janeiro in 1992 was called [NEET 2019]','For conservation of biodiversity and sustainable utilisation of its benefits','‘The Earth Summit’ held in Rio de Janeiro in 1992, called upon all nations to take appropriate measures for conservation of biodiversity and sustainable utilisation of its benefits.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','For immediate steps to discontinue use of CFCs that were damaging the ozone layer','To reduce CO₂ emissions and global warming','For conservation of biodiversity and sustainable utilisation of its benefits','To assess threat posed to native species by invasive weed species.','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370937,'biology','Which one of the following is not a method of in situ conservation of biodiversity ? [NEET 2019]','Botanical garden','Botanical garden comes under ex-situ method of conservation of biodiversity.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Sacred grove','Biosphere reverse','Wildlife sanctuary','Botanical garden','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370938,'biology','Western Ghats have a large number of plant and animal species that are not found anywhere else. Which of the following terms will you use to notify such species? [Odisha NEET 2019]','Endemic','Western Ghats have a large number of plant and animal species that are not found anywhere else. The species are called endemic species \nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Endemic','Vulnerable','Threatened','Keystone','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(370939,'biology','All of the following are included in ‘ex-situ conservation’ except [NEET 2018]','Sacred groves','Sacred groves come under in-situ conservation and represent the pristine forest patches around places of worship which are held in high esteem by tribal communities. Cutting of trees and branches is prohibited due to religious reasons. Wildlife safari parks, botanical gardens and seed banks come under ex-situ conservation.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Wildlife safari parks','Sacred groves','Botanical gardens','Seed banks.','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370940,'biology','Which one of the following is related to ex-situ conservation of threatened animals and plants? [NEET 2017]','Wildlife safari parks.','Ex-situ conservation is conservation of selected rare or threatened animals and plants in places outside their natural homes. It includes offsite collections like botanical gardens, zoological parks, wildlife safari parks, gene banks, etc.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Biodiversity hotspots','Amazon rainforest','Himalayan region','Wildlife safari parks.','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370941,'biology','The region of biosphere reserve which is legally protected and where no human activity is allowed is known as [NEET 2017]','Core zone','Core zone or Natural zone area of a biosphere reserve is undisturbed and legally protected ecosystem. No human activity is allowed in this zone. Little human activity is allowed in the buffer zone whereas in transition zone, an active cooperation is present between reserve management and local people for activities like settlements, cropping, etc. Restoration region is degraded area which is selected for restoration to near natural form.','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Buffer zone','Transition zone','Restoration zone','Core zone','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(370942,'biology','How many hotspots of biodiversity in the world have been identified till date by Norman Myers? [NEET2 2016]','34','Biodiversity hotspots are a method to identify those regions of the world where attention is needed to address biodiversity loss and to guide investments in conservation. The idea was first developed by Norman Myers in 1988 to identify tropical forests hotspots characterised both by exceptional levels of plant endemism and serious habitat loss which he then expanded to a more global scope. Currently 34 biodiversity hotspots have been identified most of which occur in tropical forests.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','17','25','34','43','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(370943,'biology','Which of the following national parks is home to the famous musk deer or hangul? [NEET2 2016]','Dachigam National Park, Jammu and Kashmir','Dachigam National Park situated at Jammu and Kashmir is famous for conservation of musk deer. At Keibul Lamjao National Park, Manipur, brown deer (Sangai) is protected.At Bandhavgarh Natronal Park, MP, the tiger is protected.Eaglenest Wildlife Sanctuary, Arunachal Pradesh, protects elephants and red panda.','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Keibul Lamjao National Park, Manipur','Bandhavgarh National Park, Madhya Pradesh','Eaglenest Wildlife Sanctuary, Arunachal Pradesh','Dachigam National Park, Jammu and Kashmir','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(370944,'biology','The species confined to a particular region and not found elsewhere is termed as [NEET 2015]','Endemic','The species confined to a particular region and not found elsewhere is termed as endemic species\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Endemic','Rare','Keystone','Alien','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(370945,'biology','In which of the following, both pairs have correct combination? [2015 cancelled]','In-situ conservation : National Park, Ex-situ conservation : Botanical Garden','In-situ (on site) conservation is conservation and protection of the whole ecosystem and its biodiversity at all levels, in order to protect the threatened species. 2in-situ methods are being used to save biodiversity viz., hotspots and protected areas. Protected areas include national parks, sanctuaries, biosphere reserves and sacred groves. Ex-situ (off site) conservation is conservation of selected rare plants/animals in places outside their natural homes. Ex-situ conservation includes offsite collections, seed banks, gene banks, in vitro fertilization, cryopreservation techniques and tissue culture.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','In-situ conservation : Seed Bank ,Ex-situ conservation : National Park','In-situ conservation : Tissue culture, Ex-situ conservation : Sacred groves','In-situ conservation : National Park, Ex-situ conservation : Botanical Garden','In-situ conservation : Cryopreservation, Ex-situ conservation : Wildlife Sanctuary','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(370946,'biology','Cryopreservation of gametes of threatened species in viable and fertile condition can be referred to as [2015 Cancelled]','Advanced ex situ conservation of biodiversity.','Cryopreservation is an advanced method of ex-situ conservation. It involves preservation at –196°C in liquid nitrogen. It can maintain tissue culture, embryos, animal cells/tissues, spermatozoa indefinitely. The cryopreserved material is revived through special technique, when required.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','In situ conservation by sacred groves','In situ cryo-conservation of biodiversity','In situ conservation of biodiversity','Advanced ex situ conservation of biodiversity.','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370947,'biology','An example of ex-situ conservation is [AIPMT 2014]','Seed bank','Ex-situ (off site) conservation is conservation of selected rare plants/animals in places outside their natural homes. Ex-situ conservation includes offsite collections, seed banks, gene banks, in vitro fertilization, cryopreservation techniques and tissue culture.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','National park','Seed bank','Wildlife Sanctuary','Sacred grove','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370948,'biology','Which one of the following is not used for ex-situ plant conservation? [NEET 2013]','Shifting cultivation','Ex-situ conservation is conservation of selected rare plants/animals in places outside their natural homes. It includes botanical gardens or zoological parks, seed banks, cryopreservation, field gene banks and sacred plants. Many wild and domesticated species are well managed and collected in botanical gardens, zoological parks, wildlife safari parks, arboreta, etc. Most of these have capture breeding programmes to restore the decreasing number of animals and helping the survival of existing individuals of the species. Gene banks are institutes that maintain stocks of viable seeds (seed banks), live plants (orchards), tissue culture and frozen germplasm with the whole range of genetic viability.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Shifting cultivation','Botanical gardens','Field gene banks','Seed banks','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370949,'biology','The largest tiger reserve in India is [Karnataka NEET 2013]','Nagarjunansagar- Srisailam','Nagarjunasagar - Srisailam Tiger Reserve is the largest tiger reserve in India. It is present in Andhra Pradesh with a total area of 3568 km2. The core area of this reserve is 1200 km2.','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Valmiki','Nagarjunansagar- Srisailam','Periyar','Nagarhole','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(370950,'biology','Give the correct descending order of organisms with reference to their estimated number found in \nAmazon forest. \n (a) Plants \n (b) Invertebrates \n (c) Fishes \n (d) Mammals \n (e) Birds \n Choose the correct answer from the options given below : [ RE NEET 2022]','(4) (b) > (a) > (c) > (e) > (d)','Biodiversity refers to the variety of organisms present on the planet Earth.\nThe largest rainforest, Amazon forest, located in Brazil, South America, has the greatest biodiversity on the Earth.\nIt is composed of nearly 40,000 species of plants, 3,000 of fishes, 1,300 of birds, 427 of mammals, 427 of amphibians, 378 of reptiles and more than 1,25,000 of invertebrates. More than two million insect species are yet to be discovered from here.\nDescending order of species diversity\nSo arranging these taxonomic groups in descending order of the number of different species that they exhibit, gives us the following order -(b) > (a) > (c) > (e) > (d)','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) (a) > (b) > (e) > (d) > (c)','(2) (a) > (c) > (d) > (b) > (e)','(3) (b) > (a) > (e) > (d) > (c)','(4) (b) > (a) > (c) > (e) > (d)','NEW','12-260','12-218',NULL),
(51046,'chemistry','Carbon dioxide gas (1.00 mole) at 373 K occupies 536 mL at 50.0 atm pressure. What is the calculated value of the pressure using real gas equation?','49.6','(P + an2/V2)(V - nb) = nRT.\n(P + 3.61 x (1.00/0.536)2)(0.536 - 1.00 x 0.0428) = 1.00 x 0.0821 x 373)\n(P + 12.57)(0.493) = 30.62\nP + 12.57 = 62.12\nP = 49.6 atm\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 158; Exercises)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','53.2','57.1','49.6','48','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(370951,'biology','The World Summit on sustainable development held in 2002 in Johannesburg, South Africa pledged for:[ RE NEET 2022]','(1) A significant reduction in the current rate of biodiversity loss.','At the World Summit on Sustainable Development, held in Johannesburg, South Africa in 2002, biological diversity was addressed as the primary topic. The Summit also endorsed the target to achieve by 2010 - a significant reduction in the rate of biodiversity loss at global, regional and national levels as a contribution to poverty alleviation and to the benefit of all life on earth.','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) A significant reduction in the current rate of biodiversity loss.','(2) Declaration of more biodiversity hotspots.','(3) Increase in agricultural production','(4) Collection and preservation of seeds of different genetic strains of commercially important plants.','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(370952,'biology','The species of plants that plays a vital role in controlling the relative abundance of other species in a \ncommunity is called _______. [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(2) Keystone species','A species of plant that play vital role in controlling relative abundance of other species in a community \nis called keystone species. They are important for overall structure and function of an ecosystem. Exotic \nspecies or Alien species are organisms that have been introduced into an area outside their normal distribution. Endemic species exist only in one particular geographical region or area.','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Exotic species','(2) Keystone species','(3) Alien species','(4) Endemic species','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(370953,'biology','In \'rivet popper hypothesis\', Paul Ehrlich compared the rivets in an airplane to','(3) Species within a genus','A proper perspective through an analogy, the \'rivet popper hypothesis\' is used by Paul Ehrlich \nconsidered Airplane as an ecosystem and rivets used to join all parts together is considered as species.\nTherefore, he compared rivets in an airplane to species within a genus.','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Ecosystem','(2) Genera within a family','(3) Species within a genus','(4) Genetic diversity','NEW','12-263','12-221',NULL),
(370954,'biology','Which of the following is/are cause(s) of biodiversity losses ?','(1) Over-exploitation, habitat loss and fragmentation.','Biodiversity loss is caused by five primary drivers: habitat loss, invasive species, overexploitation (extreme hunting and fishing pressure), pollution, climate change associated with global warming. In each case, human beings and their activities play direct roles.','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Over-exploitation, habitat loss and fragmentation.','(2) Climate change only','(3) Over-Exploitation only','(4) Habitat loss and fragmentation only','NEW','12-264','12-222',NULL),
(370955,'biology','Match List I with List II.\nList I (Interacting species)- List II\n(Name of interaction)\n \nA. A Leopard and a Lion in a forest/grassland I. Competition\nB. A Cuckoo laying egg in a Crow’s nest II. Brood parasitism\nC. Fungi and root of a higher plant in Mycorrhizae III. Mutualism\nD. A cattle egret and a Cattle in a field IV. Commensalism\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below. [ NEET 2023]','(4) A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV','A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III','(2) A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II','(3) A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV','(4) A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(370956,'biology','Match List I with List II :\nList I\n(Interaction)\nList II\n(Species A and B)\nA. Mutualism I. +(A), O(B)\nB. Commensalism II. –(A), O(B)\nC. Amensalism III. +(A), –(B)\nD. Parasitism IV. +(A), +(B)\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below: [ NEET 2023]','(1) A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III','A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III','(2) A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II','(3) A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II','(4) A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(371101,'biology','Which of the following is considered a hot-spot of biodiversity in India ? [AIIMS 2013]','Western Ghats','Hot spot are those areas which were rich in biodiversity but now under threat due to direct or indirect interference of human activites. These regions are on the edge to get some of their species extinct due to humans. Western Ghats in India are under threat due to continuous developmental activities and Doon valley is under threat due to continuous mining activities.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Indo-Gangetic Plain','Eastern Ghats','Aravalli Hills','Western Ghats','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(371102,'biology','The largest Tiger reserve in India is [AIIMS 2014]','Nagarjunsagar - Srisailam','Nagarjunsagar-Srisailam Tiger Reserve is the largest Tiger reserve in India and the only Tiger Reserve in Andhra Pradesh state.','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Nagarhole','Valmiki','Nagarjunsagar - Srisailam','Periyar','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(371103,'biology','While answering the question, you are required to choose any one of the following four responses based on Assertion and Reason.\n\nAssertion : Tropical rain forests are disappearing fast from developing countries such as India. \n\nReason : No value is attached to these forests because these are poor in biodiversity. \n[AIIMS 2012, 2013]','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Tropical rain forests are located in the equatorial regions where the annual rainfall exceeds 140 cm. They are also called jungles and cover one twelveth of earth\'s surface but contain more than half of the earth\'s flora and fauna (i.e., rich in biodiversity). Now-a-days these forests are disappearing due to excessive cutting of forests for domestic purposes like fuel, furnitures, accomodations, cloths, resin, gum, etc.\nThe diagram below shows the present and past distribution of these forests in India diagram (e3705)','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-259','12-217',NULL),
(371104,'biology','While answering the question, you are required to choose any one of the following four responses based on Assertion and Reason. \n\nAssertion : Diversity observed in the entire geographical area is called gamma diversity. \n\nReason : Biodiversity decreases from high altitude to low altitude. \n[AIIMS 2014]','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The diversity present in the ranges of communities in an entire geographical area is known as Gamma diversity. The term was introduced by R. H. Whittaker. Also, gamma diversity = alpha diversity x Beta diversity.Biodiversity increases from high altitudes to low altitudes. As the favorable conditions for the species richness decrease at high altitudes.\nSo, the correct answer is \'Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-261','12-219',NULL),
(371105,'biology','While answering the question, you are required to choose any one of the following four responses based on Assertion and Reason. \n\nAssertion : A sanctuary is formed for the conservation of animals only. \n\nReason : Restricted human activities are allowed in sanctuaries.\n[AIIMS 2014]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','A sanctuary is an area which is reserved for the protection of wild animals only. The activities like harvesting of timber, collection of minor forest products and private ownership rights are allowed, however, such activities should not have any adverse effect on animals.','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(371106,'biology','While answering the question, you are required to choose any one of the following four responses based on Assertion and Reason.[AIIMS 2017]\n\nAssertion: Communities that comprise of more species tend to be more stable. \n\nReason: A higher number of species results in less animal variation in total biomass.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Communities with higher number of species are more stable as it can resist occasional disturbances. A stable community should show less variation in productivity from year to year and resistance towards alien species.','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-262','12-220',NULL),
(371107,'biology','Using the diagram determine the percentage of bird species that will be lost if the island\'s inhabitable land area is reduced from 100,000 km² to 1 km². (3706)','93 percent of the bird species will be lost.','"log S=log C + Z log A,\nS =C A ^Z\nwhere S = Number of bird species\nA= Area\nZ = Slope of the line (reqression coefficient= 0.15)\nC = y intercept"','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','17 percent of the bird species will be lost.','20 percent of the bird species will be lost.','All of bird species will be lost.','93 percent of the bird species will be lost.','NEW','12-262','12-220','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/371107q.png'),
(371201,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding biodiversity and choose the correct option\n1. Biodiversity is the term popularised by the sociobiologist Ernest Heckel\n\n2. There are more than 20,000 species of ants, 3,00,000 species of beetles, 28,000 species of fishes and nearly 20,000 species of orchids\n\n3. A single species might show high diversity at the genetic level\n\n4. The Eastern Ghats have a greater amphibian species diversity than the Western Ghats','Only option 2 and 3 are correct','Biodiversity is the term popularised by the sociobiologist Edward Wilson. The common man would find it hard to believe that there are more than 20,000 species of ants, 3,00,000 species of beetles, 28,000 species of fishes and nearly 20,000 species of orchids. A single species might show high diversity at the genetic level over its distributional range.The diversity can occur at species level also, for example, the Western Ghats have a greater amphibian species diversity than the Eastern Ghats. \nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:258-259,section 15.1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only option 2 and 3 are incorrect','Only option 1 and 2 are correct','Only option 2 and 3 are correct','Only option 1,2 and 3 are correct','NEW','12-259','12-217',NULL),
(371202,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding biodiversity of the world and choose the correct options \n\n1. The total number of plant and animal species described by IUCN so far is slightly more than 1.5 million\n\n2. It has taken about 200 years of evolution, to accumulate the rich diversity in nature, but we could lose all that wealth in more than a  million years\n\n3. According to  Robert May, the global species diversity is about 20 millions\n\n4. More than 70 % of all the species recorded are animals, while plants comprise no more than 22 % of the total.','Only option 1 and 4 are correct','According to the International Union for Conservation of Nature and Natural Resources (IUCN) (2004), the total number of plant and animal species described so far is slightly more than 1.5 million. It has taken millions of years of evolution, to accumulate this rich diversity in nature, but we could lose all that wealth in less than 2centuries if the present rates of species losses continue.Some extreme estimates range from 20 to 50 million, but a more conservative and scientifically sound estimate made by Robert May places the global species diversity at about 7 million.More than 70 % of all the species recorded are animals, while plants (including algae, fungi, bryophytes, gymnosperms and angiosperms) comprise no more than 22 % of the total.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:259,paragraph no:4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only option 1 and 2 are correct','Only option 1,3 and 4  are correct','Only option 1 and 4 are correct','Only option 1,2 and 3 are correct','NEW','12-259','12-217',NULL),
(371203,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding biodiversity and choose the coorect options\n\n1. Among animals, insects are the most species-rich taxonomic group, making up more than 70 % of the total.\n\n2.The number of fungi species in the world is more than the combined total of the species of animals\n\n3. India has only 8.1 % of the world’s land area, its share of the global species diversity is an impressive 2.4 %.\n\n4. India is not one of the 5 mega diversity countries of the world.','Only option 1 is correct','Among animals, insects are the most species-rich taxonomic group, making up more than 70 % of the total. That means, out of every 10 animals on this planet, 7 are insects.The number of fungi species in the world is more than the combined total of the species of fishes, amphibians, reptiles and mammals and not that of all animals. India has only 2.4 % of the world’s land area, its share of the global species diversity is an impressive 8.1 %. India is one of the 12 mega diversity countries of the world.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:260-261 ,section 15.1.1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only option 1 and 2 are correct','Only option 1 is incorrect','Only option 1 is correct','Only option 1,2 and 3 are correct','NEW','12-261','12-219',NULL),
(51047,'chemistry','The pressure of a gas in a vessel is P. If the mass m of the molecules is halved and the speed v doubled, what will the new pressure be in terms of P','2P','P and vrms relation = P = 1/3 (mN/V)Vrms^2\nUsing this, P\' = (1/3)(m/2)(N/V)(2vrms)^1/2\nP\' = 2P\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 147; Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','P','P/2','4P','2P','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(371204,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding species biodiversity and choose the correct options\n\n1. Nearly 45,000 species of plants and animals have been recorded from India.\n\n2.The diversity of plants and animals is not uniform throughout the world but shows a rather uneven distribution.\n\n3. In general, species diversity decreases as we move away from the equator towards the poles.\n\n4. India, with much of its land area in the tropical latitudes, has more than 1,200 species of birds.','Only option 2,3 and 4 are correct','Nearly 45,000 species of plants and twice as many of animals have been recorded from India.The diversity of plants and animals is not uniform throughout the world but shows a rather uneven distribution. For many group of animals or plants, there are interesting patterns in diversity, the most well- known being the latitudinal gradient in diversity. In general, species diversity decreases as we move away from the equator towards the poles. With very few exceptions, tropics (latitudinal range of 23.5° N to 23.5° S) harbour more species than temperate or polar areas.India, with much of its land area in the tropical latitudes, has more than 1,200 species of birds.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:261,paragraph no:1 and 2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','only option 1 is correct','Only option 2,3 and 4 are correct','Only option 2,3 and 4 are incorrect','option1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-261','12-219',NULL),
(371205,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding patterns of biodiversity and select the correct options\n\n1. The largely tropical kerala rain forest has the greatest biodiversity on earth\n\n2. The rain forests in inida are home to more than 40,000 species of plants, 3,000 of fishes, 1,300 of birds, 427 of mammals, 427 of amphibians, 378 of reptiles and of more than 1,25,000 invertebrates.\n\n3. Tropical environments, unlike temperate ones, are less seasonal, relatively more constant and predictable.\n\n4. Within a region species richness increases with increasing explored area, but only up to a limit.','only option 3 and 4 are correct','The largely tropical Amazonian rain forest in South America has the greatest biodiversity on earth- it is home to more than 40,000 species of plants, 3,000 of fishes, 1,300 of birds, 427 of mammals, 427 of amphibians, 378 of reptiles and of more than 1,25,000 invertebrates.Tropical environments, unlike temperate ones, are less seasonal, relatively more constant and predictable. Such constant environments promote niche specialisation and lead to a greater species diversityDuring his pioneering and extensive explorations in the wilderness of South American jungles, the great German naturalist and geographer Alexander von Humboldt observed that within a region species richness increased with increasing explored area, but only up to a limit.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:261-262, section 15.1.2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','only option 1 is correct','only option 3 and 4 are correct','Only option 2,3 and 4 are correct','option1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-262','12-220',NULL),
(371206,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding species biodiversity and choose the correct options\n\n1. A stable community should  show too much variation in productivity from year to year\n\n2. The ‘rivet popper hypothesis was used by Stanford ecologist Paul Ehrlich.\n\n3. Loss of key species does more damage to ecosystem\n\n4. The IUCN Red List (2004) documents that 784 species have been conserved in-situ','only option 2 and 3 are correct','A stable community should not show too much variation in productivity from year to year; it must be either resistant or resilient to occasional disturbances (natural or man-made), and it must also be resistant to invasions by alien species. The ‘rivet popper hypothesis’ used by Stanford ecologist Paul Ehrlich. Key species that drive major ecosystem functions is obviously a more serious threat if it is lost. The IUCN Red List (2004) documents the extinction of 784 species (including 338 vertebrates, 359 invertebrates and 87 plants) in the last 500 years.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:263,paragraph no:1,2,3 and 4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','only option 2 and 3 are incorrect','option1,2,3 and 4 are correct','only option 2 and 3 are correct','only option 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-263','12-221',NULL),
(371207,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding loss of biodiversity and choose the correct options\n\n1. Some examples of recent extinctions include the dodo (Mauritius), quagga (Africa), thylacine (Australia), Steller’s Sea Cow (Russia) and 3subspecies (Bali, Javan, Caspian) of tiger.\n\n2. Presently, 12 % of all bird species, 23 % of all mammal species, 32 % of all amphibian species and 31% of all gymnosperm species in the world face the threat of extinction.\n\n3. The accelerated rates of species extinctions that the world is facing now are largely due to natural phenomenons\n\n4. The current species extinction rates are estimated to be 10 -15 times faster than in the pre-human times','Only option 1 and 2 are correct','The IUCN Red List (2004) documents the extinction of 784 species (including 338 vertebrates, 359 invertebrates and 87 plants) in the last 500 years. Some examples of recent extinctions include the dodo (Mauritius), quagga (Africa), thylacine (Australia), Steller’s Sea Cow (Russia) and 3subspecies (Bali, Javan, Caspian) of tiger. The last twenty years alone have witnessed the disappearance of 27 species. More than 15,500 species world-wide are facing the threat of extinction. Presently, 12 % of all bird species, 23 % of all mammal species, 32 % of all amphibian species and 31% of all gymnosperm species in the world face the threat of extinction.The accelerated rates of species extinctions that the world is facing now are largely due to human activities. The current species extinction rates are estimated to be 100 to 1,000 times faster than in the pre-human times\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:263-264, section 15.1.4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','only option 3 and 4 are correct','Only option 1 and 2 are correct','only option 1 is correct','option1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-263','12-221',NULL),
(311706,'biology','Assertion : Cattle breeds can be improved by superovulaton and embryo transplantaion. \n\nReason : Superovulation in high milk-yielding cows is induced by hormonal injection.[2013]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Cattle breeds can be improved by super\novulation and embryo transfer technique.\nIt is also known as Multiple Ovulation\nEmbryo Transfer Technology (MOET). In\nthis method, a cow is administered\nhormones with FSH like activity, to induce\nsuper ovulation instead of one egg, which\nthey normally yield per cycle they produce\n6-8 eggs. The animal is either mated with\nan elite bull or artificially inseminated. The\nfertilised eggs at 8-32 cells stages are\nremoved non surgically and transferred to\nsurrogate mothers. The genetic mother is\navailable for another round of super\novulation.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','If the Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','If both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(371208,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding loss of biodiversity and choose the correct options\n\n1. Habitat loss and fragmentation is the most important cause driving animals and plants to extinction.\n\n2. Once covering more than 14 % of the earth’s land surface, amazon rain forests now cover no more than 6 %.\n\n3. The Amazon rain forest harbouring probably millions of species is being cut and cleared for making solar power projects\n\n4. Steller’s sea cow, passenger pigeon were extinct due to overexploitation by humans.','Only option 3 is incorrect','Habitat loss is the most important cause driving animals and plants to extinction. The most dramatic examples of habitat loss come from tropical rain forests. Once covering more than 14 % of the earth’s land surface, these rain forests now cover no more than 6 %.The Amazon rain forest (it is so huge that it is called the ‘lungs of the planet’) harbouring probably millions of species is being cut and cleared for cultivating soya beans or for conversion to grasslands for raising beef cattle.Many species extinctions in the last 500 years (Steller’s sea cow, passenger pigeon) were due to overexploitation by humans.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:264,paragraph no:4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only option 3 is correct','option1,2,3 and 4 are correct','Only option 3 is incorrect','Only option 1 and 2 are correct','NEW','12-264','12-222',NULL),
(371209,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding loss of biodiversity and choose the correct option.\n\n1. When alien species are introduced unintentionally or deliberately for whatever purpose, they make the ecosystem suitable for indegenous species\n\n2. When a species becomes extinct, the plant and animal species associated with it in an obligatory way also become extinct.\n\n3. When a host fish species becomes extinct, its unique assemblage of parasites also meets the same fate.\n\n4. Parthenium, Lantana and water hyacinth (Eicchornia) are examples of alien species invasion','only option 1 is incorrect','When alien species are introduced unintentionally or deliberately for whatever purpose, some of them turn invasive, and cause decline or extinction of indigenous species.  Example of invasive weed species are  carrot grass (Parthenium), Lantana and water hyacinth (Eicchornia).When a species becomes extinct, the plant and animal species associated with it in an obligatory way also become extinct. When a host fish species becomes extinct, its unique assemblage of parasites also meets the same fate.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:265, paragraph no:2 and 3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','only option 1 is correct','Only option 1 and 2 are correct','only option 1 is incorrect','option1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-265','12-223',NULL),
(371210,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding conservation of biodiversity and choose the correct options\n\n1.Humans derive countless direct economic benefits from nature- food (cereals, pulses, fruits), firewood, fibre, construction material, industrial products (tannins, lubricants, dyes, resins, perfumes ) and products of medicinal importance.\n\n2. More than 25 % of the drugs currently sold in the market worldwide are derived from plants and 25,000 species of plants contribute to the traditional medicines used by native peoples around the world.\n\n3. Amazon forest is estimated to produce, through photosynthesis, 2 % of the total O₂ in the earth’s atmosphere.\n\n4. The broadly utilitarian argument says that biodiversity plays a little or no role in many ecosystem services that nature provides.','Only options 1 and 2 are correct','The narrowly utilitarian arguments for conserving biodiversity are obvious; humans derive countless direct economic benefits from nature- food (cereals, pulses, fruits), firewood, fibre, construction material, industrial products (tannins, lubricants, dyes, resins, perfumes ) and products of medicinal importance. More than 25 % of the drugs currently sold in the market worldwide are derived from plants and 25,000 species of plants contribute to the traditional medicines used by native peoples around the world.The broadly utilitarian argument says that biodiversity plays a major role in many ecosystem services that nature provides. Amazon forest is estimated to produce, through photosynthesis, 20 % of the  total O₂ in the earth’s atmosphere.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:265-266, section 15.2.1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only options 1 and 2  are incorrect','All options are correct','Only option 1 is incorrect','Only options 1 and 2 are correct','NEW','12-265','12-223',NULL),
(371211,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding conservation of biodiversity and choose the correct options\n\n1.The ethical argument for conserving biodiversity relates to what we owe to millions of plant, animal and microbe species with whom we share this planet.\n\n2. In ex-situ conservation, we conserve and protect the whole ecosystem and its biodiversity at all levels.\n\n3. ‘Biodiversity hotspots’ are regions with very high levels of species richness and high degree of endemism\n\n4. Ex situ conservation methods include protective maintenance of threatened species in zoological parks and botanical gardens, in vitro fertilisation, tissue culture propagation and cryopreservation of gametes.','Only 2 is incorrect','The ethical argument for conserving biodiversity relates to what we owe to millions of plant, animal and microbe species with whom we share this planet. Philosophically or spiritually, we need to realise that every species has an intrinsic value, even if it may not be of current or any economic value to us.When we conserve and protect the whole ecosystem, its biodiversity at all levels is protected - we save the entire forest to save the tiger. This approach is called in situ (on site) conservation. However, when there are situations where an animal or plant is endangered or threatened (organisms facinga very high risk of extinction in the wild in the near future) and needs urgent measures to save it from extinction, ex situ (off site) conservationis the desirable approach.On a global basis, the problem of species going to extinction has been addressed by eminent conservationists. They identified for maximum protection certain ‘biodiversity hotspots’ regions with very high levels of species richness and high degree of endemism (that is, species confined to that region and not found anywhere else).\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266-267, section 15.2.2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 2 is correct','All options are correct','Only 2 is incorrect','Only option 1 and 2 are incorrect','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(371212,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding conservation of biodiversity and choose the correct options \n\n1. 3 of these hotspots – Western Ghats and Sri Lanka, Indo-Burma and Himalaya cover India’s rich biodiversity regions.\n\n2. Although all the biodiversity hotspots put together cover less than 2 % of the earth’s land area, the number of species they collectively harbour is extremely low\n\n3. Strict protection of biodiversity hotspots could reduce the ongoing mass extinctions by almost 30 %.\n\n4. Recently, 9 ‘biodiversity hotspots’ in the world have been proposed for intensive conservation efforts.','Only 1 and 3 are correct','Initially 25 biodiversity hotspots were identified but subsequently nine more have been added to the list, bringing the total number of biodiversity hotspots in the world to 34. These hotspots are also regions of accelerated habitat loss. 3of these hotspots – Western Ghats and Sri Lanka, Indo-Burma and Himalaya – cover our country’s exceptionally high biodiversity regions. Although all the biodiversity hotspots put together cover less than 2 % of the earth’s land area, the number of species they collectivelyharbour is extremely high and strict protection of these hotspots could reduce the ongoing mass extinctions by almost 30 %.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 3 are incorrect','Only 1 and 3 are correct','option1,2,3 and 4 are correct','only option 1 and 2 are correct','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(51048,'chemistry','What is the ratio fo urms and ump for oxygen at 298K?','1.224','The ratio of root mean square speed, represented as urms to the most probable speed, represented as ump is always the same for identical conditions and same gas. It is √3RT/M / √8RT/πM = 1.224.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 147; Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1.124','1.224','1.228','1.128','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(371213,'biology','Consider the following statements reagding conservation of biodiversity  and choose the correct option\n\n1. Sacred groves are found in Khasi and Jaintia Hills in Meghalaya, Aravalli Hills of Rajasthan, Western Ghat regions of Karnataka and Maharashtra and the Sarguja, Chanda and Bastar areas of Madhya Pradesh and are example of in-situ conservation\n\n2. India now has 90 biosphere reserves, 90 national parks and  wildlife sanctuaries.\n\n3. The Earth Summit was held in Rio de Janeiro in 1992\n\n4. The World Summit on Sustainable Development was held in 2002 in Johannesburg, South Africa,','Only 1 ,3 and 4 are correct','Sacred groves are found in Khasi and Jaintia Hills in Meghalaya, Aravalli Hills of Rajasthan, Western Ghat regions of Karnataka and Maharashtra and the Sarguja, Chanda and Bastar areas of Madhya Pradesh are example of in-situ conservation. In Meghalaya, the sacred groves are the last refuges for a large number of rare and threatened plants.In India, ecologically unique and biodiversity-rich regions are legally protected as biosphere reserves, national parks and sanctuaries. India now has 14 biosphere reserves, 90 national parks and 448 wildlife sanctuaries.The historic Convention on Biological Diversity (‘The Earth Summit’) held in Rio de Janeiro in 1992, called upon all nations to take appropriate measures for conservation of biodiversity and sustainable utilisation of its benefits. In a follow-up, the World Summit on Sustainable Development held in 2002 in Johannesburg, South Africa, 190 countries pledged their commitment to achieve by 2010, a significant reduction in the current rate of biodiversity loss at global, regional and local levels. \nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:2 and 4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','only option 3 is correct','Only 1 ,3 and 4 are correct','only option 1 and 2 are correct','option1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(371214,'biology','Assertion : The genetic variation shown by the medicinal plant Rauwolfia vomitoria growing in different Himalayan ranges might be in terms of the potency and concentration of the active chemical (reserpine) that the plant produces\n\nReason : A single species might show high diversity at the genetic level over its distributional range.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','A single species might show high diversity at the genetic level over its distributional range. The genetic variation shown by the medicinal plant Rauwolfia vomitoria growing in different Himalayan ranges might be in terms of the potency and concentration of the active chemical (reserpine) that the plant produces. India has more than 50,000 genetically different strains of rice, and 1,000 varieties of mango.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:259,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-259','12-217',NULL),
(371215,'biology','Assertion : Diversity can also be found at species level \n\nReason : Western Ghats have a greater amphibian species diversity than the Eastern Ghats.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Biodiversity can occur at species level also. The diversity at the species level, for example, the Western Ghats have a greater amphibian species diversity than the Eastern Ghats.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:259,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-259','12-217',NULL),
(371216,'biology','Assertion : The total number of plant and animal species described so far is slightly more than 1.5 million\n\nReason : In India, it is estimated that there are probably more than 1,00,000 plant species and more than 3,00,000 animal species yet to be discovered and described.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','According to the International Union for Conservation of Nature and Natural Resources (IUCN) (2004), the total number of plant and animal species described so far is slightly more than 1.5 million. If we accept Robert May’s global estimates, only 22 % of the total species have been recorded so far.  Applying this proportion to India’s diversity figures, we estimate that there are probably more than 1,00,000 plant species and more than 3,00,000 animal species yet to be discovered and described.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:259 and 261, section 15.1.1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-259','12-217',NULL),
(371217,'biology','Assertion : Robert May places the global species diversity at about 15 million.\n\nReason : Among animals, mammals are the most species-rich taxonomic group, making up more than 70 % of the total.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Robert May places the global species diversity at about 7 million.Among animals, insects are the most species-rich taxonomic group, making up more than 70 % of the total. That means, out of every 10 animals on this planet, 7 are insects.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No: 259-260,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-259','12-217',NULL),
(371218,'biology','Assertion : More than 70 % of all the species recorded are animals, while plants comprise no more than 22 % of the total.\n\nReason :  The number of fungi species in the world is more than the combined total of the species of fishes, amphibians, reptiles and mammals.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','More than 70 % of all the species recorded are animals, while plants (including algae, fungi, bryophytes, gymnosperms and angiosperms) comprise no more than 22 % of the total.Among animals, insects are the most species-rich taxonomic group, making up more than 70 % of the total. That means, out of every 10 animals on this planet, 7 are insects. Again, how do we explain this enormous diversification of insects? The number of fungi species in the world is more than the combined total of the species of fishes, amphibians, reptiles and mammals.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:260,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-260','12-218',NULL),
(311707,'biology','Assertion : In case of vegetatively propagated crops, pure-line selection is not required. \n\nReason : Hybrid vigour is mostly used in vegetatively propagated plants. [2013]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','In case of vegetatively propagated crops,\npure line selection is not required. Pure-\nline selection is useful only for sexually\nreproducing plants. Hybrid vigour is most\nprofitably used in vegetatively propagated\ncrops because they do not involve sexual\nreproduction and hence no loss of hybrid\nsuperiority.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','If the Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','If both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(371219,'biology','Assertion : Although India has only 2.4 % of the world’s land area, its share of the global species diversity is an impressive 8.1 %.\n\nReason : The largely tropical Amazonian rain forest in South America has the greatest biodiversity on earth- it is home to more than 40,000 species of plants, 3,000 of fishes, 1,300 of birds, 427 of mammals, 427 of amphibians, 378 of reptiles and of more than 1,25,000 invertebrates.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Although India has only 2.4 % of the world’s land area, its share of the global species diversity is an impressive 8.1 %. That is what makes india one of the 12 mega diversity countries of the world.A forest in a tropical region like Equador has up to 10 times as many species of vascular plants as a forest of equal area in a temperate region like the Midwest of the USA. The largely tropical Amazonian rain forest in South America has the greatest biodiversity on earth- it is home to more than 40,000 species of plants, 3,000 of fishes, 1,300 of birds, 427 of mammals, 427 of amphibians, 378 of reptiles and of more than 1,25,000 invertebrates. Scientists estimate that in these rain forests there might be at least 2million insect species waiting to be discovered and named.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:261,paragraph no: 1 and 2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-261','12-219',NULL),
(371220,'biology','Assertion : A forest in a tropical region lclose to the Equator has up to 10 times as many species of vascular plants as a forest of equal area in a temperate region like the Midwest of the USA.\n\nReason :Tropical environments, unlike temperate ones, are less seasonal, relatively more constant and predictable. Such constant environments promote niche specialisation and lead to a greater species diversity','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','A forest in a tropical region like Equator has up to 10 times as many species of vascular plants as a forest of equal area in a temperate region like the Midwest of the USA because Tropical environments, unlike temperate ones, are less seasonal, relatively more constant and predictable. Such constant environments promote niche specialisation and lead to a greater species diversity and There is more solar energy available in the tropics, which contributes to higher productivity; this in turn might contribute indirectly to greater diversity.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:261,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-261','12-219',NULL),
(371301,'biology','Assertion : Species richness increased with increasing explored area, but only up to a limit.\n\nReason : Biodiversity is not only essential for ecosystem health but imperative for the very survival of the human race on this planet.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','German naturalist and geographer Alexander von Humboldt observed that within a region species richness increased with increasing explored area, but only up to a limit. In fact, the relation between species richness and area for a wide variety of taxa (angiosperm plants, birds, bats, freshwater fishes) turns out to be a rectangular hyperbola We know enough to realise that rich biodiversity is not only essential for ecosystem health but imperative for the very survival of the human race on this planet.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:262-263,section 15.1.2 and 15.1.3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-262','12-220',NULL),
(371302,'biology','Assertion : A stable community should not show too much variation in productivity from year to year\n\nReason : Key species are less in biomass but have a large role in ecosystem stability','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','A stable community should not show too much variation in productivity from year to year; it must be either resistant or resilient to occasional disturbances (natural or man-made), and it must also be resistant to invasions by alien species. (According to the ‘rivet popper hypothesis’ used by Stanford ecologist Paul Ehrlich., the key species are low in bio mass but have a large role to play in the stability of ecosystem\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:263,paragraph no:1 and 2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-263','12-221',NULL),
(371303,'biology','Assertion : The IUCN Red List (2004) documents the extinction of 784 species\n\nReason : The last twenty years alone have witnessed the disappearance of 7 species.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','The IUCN Red List (2004) documents the extinction of 784 species (including 338 vertebrates, 359 invertebrates and 87 plants) in the last 500 years. The last twenty years alone have witnessed the disappearance of 27 species.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:263, paragraph no:4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-263','12-221',NULL),
(371304,'biology','Assertion : Recent extinctions include the dodo (Mauritius), quagga (America), Thylacine (Australia), Steller’s Sea Cow (Russia) and 3 subspecies (Bali, Javan, Caspian) of tiger.\n\nReason : These extinctions were due to overexploitation by humans','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Some examples of recent extinctions include the dodo (Mauritius), quagga (Africa), thylacine (Australia), Steller’s Sea Cow (Russia) and 3subspecies (Bali, Javan, Caspian) of tiger. The last twenty years alone have witnessed the disappearance of 27 species.Many species extinctions in the last 500 years (Steller’s sea cow, passenger pigeon) were due to overexploitation by humans. Presently many marine fish populations around the world are over harvested, endangering the continued existence of some commercially important species.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:263,paragraph no:4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-263','12-221',NULL),
(371305,'biology','Assertion : Presently, 12 % of all bird species, 23 % of all mammal species, 32 % of all amphibian species and 31 % of all gymnosperm species in the world face the threat for extinction\n\nReason : Extinction is random but some plants and animals are more vulnerable to become extinct.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Adding to the grim scenario of extinctions is the fact that more than 15,500 species world-wide are facing the threat of extinction. Presently, 12 % of all bird species, 23 % of all mammal species, 32 % of all amphibian species and 31% of all gymnosperm species in the world face the threat of extinction.  Extinction is not random but some plants and animals are more vulnerable to become extinct example the gymnosperms have more chances of becoming extinct among the plants\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:264,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-264','12-222',NULL),
(371306,'biology','Assetion : The current species extinction rates are estimated to be 100 to 1,000 times faster than in the pre-human times\n \nReason : The accelerated rates of species extinctions that the world is facing now are largely due to human activities.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The difference is in the rates; the current species extinction rates are estimated to be 100 to 1,000 times faster than in the pre-human times and our activities are responsible for the faster rates. Ecologists warn that if the present trends continue, nearly half of all the species on earth might be wiped out within the next 100 years.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:264,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-264','12-222',NULL),
(371307,'biology','Assertion : Once covering more than 14 % of the earth’s land surface, Rain forests now cover no more than 6 %.\n\nReason : Habitat loss and fragmentation is the most important cause driving animals and plants to extinction.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Habitat loss and fragmentation is the most important cause driving animals and plants to extinction. The most dramatic examples of habitat loss come from tropical rain forests. Once covering more than 14 % of the earth’s land surface, these rain forests now cover no more than 6 %. They are being destroyed fast.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:264,paragraph no:4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-264','12-222',NULL),
(371308,'biology','Assertion : When an alien species are introduced unintentionally or deliberately for whatever purpose, some of them turn invasive, and cause decline or extinction of indigenous species.\n\nReason : Amazon rain forests were cut to cultivate soya bean','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','When alien species are introduced unintentionally or deliberately for whatever purpose, some of them turn invasive, and cause decline or extinction of indigenous species.\nThe Nile perch introduced into Lake Victoria in east Africa led eventually to the extinction of an ecologically unique assemblage of more than 200 species of cichlid fish in the lake. The Amazon rain forest harbouring probably millions of species is being cut and cleared for cultivating soya beans or for conversion to grasslands for raising beef cattle.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No: 264-265, section no 15.1.4','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-265','12-223',NULL),
(371309,'biology','Assertion : If a plant species becomes extinct, the pollinating insect feeding on it can also become extinct\n\nReason : When a species becomes extinct, the plant and animal species associated with it in an obligatory way also become extinct.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','When a species becomes extinct, the plant and animal species associated with it in an obligatory way also become extinct. When a host fish species becomes extinct, its unique assemblage of parasites also meets the same fate. Another example is the case of a coevolved plant-pollinator mutualism where extinction of one invariably leads to the extinction of the other.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:265,paragraph no:3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-265','12-223',NULL),
(371310,'biology','Assertion : Food (cereals, pulses, fruits), firewood, fibre, construction material, industrial products (tannins, lubricants, dyes, resins, perfumes ) and products of medicinal importance come under narrow utilitarian arguments\n\nReason : The narrowly utilitarian arguments for conserving biodiversity are where humans derive direct economic benefits from nature','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The narrowly utilitarian arguments for conserving biodiversity are  obvious; humans derive countless direct economic benefits from nature- food (cereals, pulses, fruits), firewood, fibre, construction material, industrial products (tannins, lubricants, dyes, resins, perfumes ) and products of medicinal importance.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:265,paragraph no:5','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-265','12-223',NULL),
(51049,'chemistry','The compressibility factor (Z) for a real gas at its critical state is:','0.375','Z = PV/RT\nAt critical point:\nZ = a/27b²\nV = 3b\nT = 8a/27Rb\nSubstitute in Z\nZ = 0.375\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 152; Paragraph 5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','3.375','0.375','3.735','3.75','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(371311,'biology','Assertion : More than 75 % of the drugs currently sold in the market worldwide are derived from plants\n\nReason : There might still be nearly 15 million species on earth waiting to be discovered or named','Both Assertion and Reason are false','More than 25 % of the drugs currently sold in the market worldwide are derived from plants and 25,000 species of plants contribute to the traditional medicines used by native peoples around the world.More than 1.5 million species have been recorded in the world, but there might still be nearly 6 million species on earth waiting to be discovered or named\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No: 259 and 265,Section 15.1.1 and 15.2.1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-265','12-223',NULL),
(371312,'biology','Assertion : Amazon rain forests are called the lungs of planet\n\nReason : Amazon forest is estimated to produce, through photosynthesis, 20 % of the total O₂ in the earth’s atmosphere.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The Amazon rain forest is so huge that it is called the ‘lungs of the planet. Amazon forest is estimated to produce, through photosynthesis, 20 % of the total O₂ in the earth’s atmosphere.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:1','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(371313,'biology','Assertion : The ethical argument for conserving biodiversity relates to what we owe to millions of plant, animal and microbe species with whom we share this planet.\n\nReason : Every species has an intrinsic value, even if it may not be of current or any economic value to us, we have a moral duty to care and pass on the legacy','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The ethical argument for conserving biodiversity relates to what we owe to millions of plant, animal and microbe species with whom we share this planet. Philosophically or spiritually, we need to realise that every species has an intrinsic value, even if it may not be of current or any economic value to us. We have a moral duty to care for their well-being and pass on our biological legacy in good order to future generations.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(371314,'biology','Assertion : Biodiversity Hotspots have a very low degree of endemism\n\nReason : Endemism means that a species is not confined to that region and found anywhere and everywhere','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Biodiversity hotspots are regions with very high levels of species richness and high degree of endemism (that is, species confined to that region  and not found anywhere else).\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:5','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(371315,'biology','Assertion : The total number of biodiversity hotspots in the world are 34.\n\nReason : Initially there were 25 biodiversity hotspots but then 9 more were added','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Initially 25 biodiversity hotspots were identified but subsequently nine more have been added to the list, bringing the total number of biodiversity hotspots in the world to 34. These hotspots are also regions of accelerated habitat loss.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:5','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(371316,'biology','Assertion  : India has 3 biodiversity hotspots  \n\nReason : Western Ghats and Sri Lanka, Indo-Burma and Himalaya – cover india’s exceptionally high biodiversity regions.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Initially 25 biodiversity hotspots were identified but subsequently nine more have been added to the list, bringing the total number of biodiversity hotspots in the world to 34. These hotspots are also regions of accelerated habitat loss. 3of these hotspots – Western Ghats and Sri Lanka, Indo-Burma and Himalaya – cover india’s exceptionally high biodiversity regions.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:5','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(371317,'biology','Assertion : Biodiveristy hotspots cover a large area of earth\'s  land \n\nReason : The number of species they collectively harbour is extremely low','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Although all the biodiversity hotspots put together cover less than 2 % of the earth’s land area, the number of species they collectively harbour is extremely high and strict protection of these hotspots could reduce the ongoing mass extinctions by almost 30 %.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:5','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(371318,'biology','Assertion : In India, ecologically unique and biodiversity-rich regions are legally protected\n \nReason : These areas have only animals species in them','Assertion is true but Reason is false','In India, ecologically unique and biodiversity-rich regions are legally protected as biosphere reserves, national parks and sanctuaries. These regions protect both animals and plant species\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(371319,'biology','Assertion : India now has 14 biosphere reserves, 90 national parks and 448 wildlife sanctuaries.\n\nReason : People aren\'t allowed to visit any of these areas in a bid to protect wildlife','Assertion is true but Reason is false','India now has 14 biosphere reserves, 90 national parks and 448 wildlife sanctuaries. People often visit national parks and wildlife sanctuaries\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(371320,'biology','Assertion : Sacred groves are found in Khasi and Jaintia Hills in Meghalaya, Aravalli Hills of Rajasthan, Western Ghat regions of Karnataka and Maharashtra and the Sarguja, Chanda and Bastar areas of Madhya Pradesh\n\nReason : Protection of sacred groves comes under in-situ conservation','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','In many cultures, tracts of forest were set aside, and all the trees and wildlife within were venerated and given total protection. Such sacred groves are found in Khasi and Jaintia Hills in Meghalaya, Aravalli Hills of Rajasthan, Western Ghat regions of Karnataka and Maharashtra and the Sarguja, Chanda and Bastar areas of Madhya Pradesh. In Meghalaya, the sacred groves are the last refuges for a large number of rare and threatened plants.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:2','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(371321,'biology','Assertion : Biosphere reserves are a part of in-situ conservation \n\nReason : In in-situ conservation,we conserve and protect the whole ecosystem, its biodiversity at all levels is protected','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','When we conserve and protect the whole ecosystem, its biodiversity at all levels is protected - we save the entire forest to save the tiger. This approach is called in situ (on site) conservation. Biosphere resrves, national parks and wildlife sanctuaries are a part of in-situ conservation\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(371322,'biology','Assertion :  A biosphere reserve has 3 zones\n\nReason : The core zone in biosphere reserve is the region where no human activity is allowed','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','When we conserve and protect the whole ecosystem, its biodiversity at all levels is protected - we save the entire forest to save the tiger. This approach is called in situ (on site) conservation.Biosphere reserve is an example of in-situ conservation\nBiosphere reserve is protected area with multipurpose activities. It has 3zones\n(a) Core zone - without any human interference\n(b) Buffer zone - with limited human activity\n(c) Transition zone - human settlement, grazing cultivation etc., are allowed\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:266,paragraph no:3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-266','12-224',NULL),
(371323,'biology','Assertion : Zoological parks and botanical gardens are examples of ex-situ conservation \n\nReason : In Ex situ Conservation threatened animals and plants are taken out from their natural habitat and placed in special setting where they can be protected and given special care.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Ex situ Conservation– In this approach, threatened animals and plants are taken out from their natural habitat and placed in special setting where they can be protected and given special care. Zoological parks, botanical gardens and wildlife safari parks serve this purpose.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(371324,'biology','Assertion : Wildife sanctuaries and wildlife parks both are a part of in-situ conservation\n \nReason : A botanical garden has dead plants and animals preserved for sightseeing','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Wildlife sanctuaries are a part of in-situ conservation while as wildlife parks are a part of ex situ conservation. Botanical gardens have live plants where they are protected and given special care \nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(371325,'biology','Assertion : Cryopreservation is an important part of ex-situ conservation\n\nReason : Gametes of threatened species can be preserved in viable and fertile condition for long periods using cryopreservation techniques,','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','In recent years ex situ conservation has advanced beyond keeping threatened species in enclosures. Now gametes of threatened species can be preserved in viable and fertile condition for long periods using cryopreservation techniques, eggs can be fertilised in vitro, and plants can be propagated using tissue culture methods. Seeds of different genetic strains of commercially important plants can be kept for long periods in seed banks.\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: Page No:267,paragraph no:3','Ecology >> Biodiversity and Conservation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-267','12-225',NULL),
(90101,'chemistry','The most abundant element in Universe is','Hydrogen','Hydrogen is the most abundant element in the universe.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 284; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Hydrogen','Oxygen','Silicon','Carbon','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90102,'chemistry','Hydrogen is ________ most abundant element in earth.','Third','Hydrogen is the third most abundant element on the Earth\'s surface after oxygen and silicon.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 284; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Third','Second','First','Fourth','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90103,'chemistry','In atomic form hydrogen consists of','1 proton,1 electron','A hydrogen atom is made of one proton and one electron. Because hydrogen has one proton, it has an atomic number of one.  It is the only element whose atoms don\'t have any neutrons.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 284; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','2 protons,2 electrons','1 proton,1 electron','1 proton,2 electron','1 proton,3 electron','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90104,'chemistry','In elemental form hydrogen exists as','Diatomic form','In elemental form hydrogen exists as a diatomic molecule and is called dihydrogen.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 284; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Diatomic form','Triatomic form','Monatomic form','Ionic form','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90105,'biology','How many of the following is/are  Incorrect?  \n\ni. Filtrate is more technically, the acid-soluble pool \n\nii. Thousands of organic compounds are found in the acid-soluble pool \n\niii. % Weight of Calcium (Ca) in human is more than in Earth’s crust \n\niv.Filtrate is more technically, the acid- insoluble pool','iii and iv only','One is called the filtrate or more technically, the acid-soluble\npool, and the second, the retentate or the acid-insoluble fraction. Scientists\nhave found thousands of organic compounds in the acid-soluble pool. Calcium (Ca) % Weight in human is 1.5 where as in Earth’s crust it is 3.6. Refer NCERT 1, TABLE 9.1 A Comparison of Elements Present in Non-living and Living Matter* .(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:142 (last paragraph) 143, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Chemical Composition',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii only','iii and iv only','i and iiii only','ii and iv only','NEW','11-143','11-105',NULL),
(90106,'biology','Which among the following is incorrect? \n\ni. All the carbon compounds derived from living tissues can be called ‘biomolecules\'\n\nii.Living organisms do not have inorganic elements and compounds in them        \n\niii. Wet weight of living tissue is its weight after drying it out.','ii and iii only','Wet weight of living tissue is its weight before drying out.\nInorganic elements are present in humans in the form of essential elements.\nAll the living tissue compounds containing carbon and hydrogen and substituted elements make up Organic biomolecules\n(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:143, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Chemical Composition',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii only','iii only','ii and iii only','i and iii only','NEW','11-143','11-105',NULL),
(90107,'biology','Organic compounds are carbon compounds when _____  all the carbon compounds oxidised to gaseous form (CO₂ , water vapour) and are removed.','Burnt','Organic compounds are carbon compounds when burnt  all the carbon compounds oxidised to gaseous form (CO₂ , water vapour) and are removed.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:143, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Chemical Composition',NULL,'2','mcq','Burnt','Distillation','Nitration','Cooling','NEW','11-143','11-105',NULL),
(90108,'biology','Ash contains which of the following:','Inorganic elements only','if the tissue is fully burnt, all the carbon compounds are oxidised to gaseous form (CO₂ , water vapour) and are removed. What is remaining is called ‘ash’. This ash contain inorganic elements (like calcium, magnesium etc).(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:143, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Chemical Composition',NULL,'2','mcq','Inorganic elements only','Organic compounds Only','Equal concentration of organic and  inorganic constituents','Mainly organic and little concentration of inorganic elements','NEW','11-143','11-105',NULL),
(90109,'biology','Elemental analysis gives','Elemental composition of living tissues in the form of C, H, O and Cl etc','elemental analysis gives elemental composition of living tissues in the form of hydrogen, oxygen, chlorine, carbon etc.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:143, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Chemical Composition',NULL,'2','mcq','Elemental composition of living tissues in the form of C, H, O and Cl etc','Analysis of different compounds','Analysis of all inorganic compounds','All of them','NEW','11-143','11-105',NULL),
(90110,'biology','Analysis of compounds gives:','The organic and inorganic constituent present in living tissues','analysis for compounds gives an idea of the kind of organic and inorganic constituents present in living tissues.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:143, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Chemical Composition',NULL,'3','mcq','Organic constituent present in living tissues','The organic and inorganic constituent present in living tissues','Inorganic constituent present in living tissues','Elemental and compound composition','NEW','11-143','11-105',NULL),
(90111,'biology','% Weight of Hydrogen(H),Carbon (C),O₂ (O),Nitrogen (N) and Sulphur (S) in Human beign is more compared to they % Weight in Earth’s crust.','TRUE','Reference   NCERT class 11, TABLE 9.1 A Comparison of Elements Present in Non-living and Living Matter* page no_143','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Chemical Composition',NULL,'3','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-143','11-105',NULL),
(90112,'biology','How many of the following statement(s) is/are correct? \n\ni. In our skeletal muscle, under anaerobic conditions, lactic acid is formed.\n\nii.Under normal aerobic conditions, pyruvic acid is formed. \n\niii.In human,during fermentation, the same pathway leads to the production of ethanol (alcohol). \n\niv. In yeast,during fermentation, the same pathway leads to the production of ethanol (alcohol).','i, ii and iv are correct','In our skeletal muscle, under anaerobic conditions, lactic acid is formed. Under normal aerobic conditions, pyruvic acid is formed. In yeast, during fermentation, the same pathway leads to the production of ethanol (alcohol).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Chemical Composition',NULL,'2','mcq','i and  ii are correct','ii is correct','i, ii and iv are correct','iv is correct','NEW','11-155','11-112',NULL),
(90113,'biology','The activity of an enzyme can be affected by a change in the conditions which can alter the ........... structure of the protein.','Tertiary Structure','The activity of an enzyme can be affected by a change in the conditions which can alter the tertiary structure of the protein. Reference  : NCERT Page 157','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Chemical Composition',NULL,'2','fillup','Te,rt,ia,ry, St,ru,ct,ure',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-157','11-114',NULL),
(90114,'biology','b\' represents which of the following in diagram (0906)','Glycerol','Reference  : Chapter 09 , pg no 145','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Chemical Composition',NULL,'1','mcq','Glycerol','Glycine','Alanine','Serine','NEW','11-145','11-107','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/90114q.png'),
(90201,'chemistry','Hydrogen present  in_______of periodic table.','Both a and c','So if you glance at the periodic table, you will see hydrogen is the first element in the table.  It is the smallest element on the table. It has atomic number one, which means it has only one electron orbiting it its shell.  In fact, Hydrogen has only one shell.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 284; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Position of hydrogen',NULL,'1','mcq','First group','Second group','First period','Both a and c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90202,'chemistry','Elements in the periodic table are arranged on the base of __________.','Electronic configuration','In the modern periodic table, the elements are arranged on the basis of their increasing atomic number/ based on electronic configuration.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 284; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Position of hydrogen',NULL,'1','fillup','Elec,tro,nic, con,fig,ur,at,ion, ma',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90203,'chemistry','Hydrogen has electronic configuration of','1s¹','So if you glance at the periodic table, you will see hydrogen is the first element in the table.  It is the smallest element on the table. It has atomic number one, which means it has only one electron orbiting it its shell. In fact, Hydrogen has only one shell.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 284; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Position of hydrogen',NULL,'1','mcq','1s²','1s¹','1s⁰','2s²','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90204,'chemistry','The electronic configuration is similar to outer electronic electronic configuration of hydrogen is','Alkali metals','Both hydrogen and alkali metals have one electron in their outermost shell .\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 284; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Position of hydrogen',NULL,'2','mcq','Alkali metals','Alkaline metals','Alkaline earth metals','Group 14','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90205,'chemistry','Hydrogen being short by one electron in outermost shell resembles','Halogens','ns²np⁵ is the electronic configuration of halogens,having vacancy of one electron  to gain the nearest noble gas configuration like hydrogen which also need one electron to gain most stable electronic configuration, similar to He(Noble gas)\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 284; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Position of hydrogen',NULL,'2','mcq','Halogens','Alkali metals','Group 14','Alkaline earth metals','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90206,'chemistry','Hydrogen like Alkali metals lose electron to form','Unipositive ion','Both Alkali metals and hydrogen have one electron in their outermost shell which the lose to form unipositive ion.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 284; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Position of hydrogen',NULL,'2','mcq','Unipositive ion','Dipositive ion','Uninegative ion','Dinegative ion','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90207,'chemistry','Hydrogen like halogens have tendency to form','Uninegative ion','Both hydrogen and halogens have shortage of one electron in their outermost shell to gain the nearest noble gas configuration,By gaining one electron they both form uninegative ion.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 284; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Position of hydrogen',NULL,'2','mcq','Unipositive ion','Dipositive ion','Dinegative ion','Uninegative ion','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90208,'chemistry','Unlike Alkali metals, hydrogen hardly remove electron due to very high ______________.','Ionization enthalpy','Hydrogen has 1 electron in the s orbital and the effect of nucleus on it will be very high and because of this, the ionization enthalpy is high unlike the other alkali metals.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 284; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Position of hydrogen',NULL,'1','fillup','Ion,iz,at,ion en,th,al,py',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90209,'chemistry','In terms of ionization potential,hydrogen resembles','Halogens','The points of similarities between Hydrogen and Halogens are:\n\n(i)Both Hydrogen and Halogens require one electron in order to attain a stable electronic configuration.\n\n(ii)They both are non-metallic in nature with respect to metals.\n\n(iii)Both have high electronegativity as compared to metals.\n\n(iv)In terms of ionization enthalpy, hydrogen resembles more with halogens,\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 285; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Position of hydrogen',NULL,'1','fillup','Ha,lo,gens,chal,co',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90210,'chemistry','Match the following. diagram(n0901)','(i) - (c) ; (ii) - (d) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (a)','∆i H of Li is 520 kJ mol⁻¹, F is 1680 kJ mol⁻¹ and that of H is 1312 kJ mol⁻¹\n.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 285; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Position of hydrogen',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) - (c) ; (ii) - (d) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (a)','(i) - (d) ; (ii) - (a) ; (iii) - (c) ; (iv) - (b)','(i) - (c) ; (ii) - (d) ; (iii) - (a) ; (iv) - (b)','(i) - (a) ; (ii) - (d) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (c)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/90210q.png'),
(90211,'chemistry','Like Halogens hydrogen forms ________.','Diatomic molecule','Hydrogen forms diatomic molecules like halogens.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 285; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Position of hydrogen',NULL,'1','fillup','Dia,to,m,ic m,ole,cu,le',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90212,'chemistry','In terms of reactivity,hydrogen is _______ reactive than halogens.','less','Is hydrogen less reactive than halogens? Yes!!\nHalogens are more reactive than hydrogen because, in case of halogens they have 7 valence electrons in their valence shell so they acquire 1 more electron to complete their octet attaining noble / inert gas configuration.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 285; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Position of hydrogen',NULL,'1','fillup','le,ss,mo,re',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/90212q.png'),
(90213,'chemistry','Loss of electron from Hydrogen results in','H⁺','Loss of the electron from hydrogen atom results in nucleus (H⁺) of ~1.5×10⁻³ pm size.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 285; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Position of hydrogen',NULL,'1','mcq','H⁺','H⁻','H²⁺','H','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(90214,'chemistry','The size of H⁺ is','1.5×10⁻³','Loss of the electron from hydrogen atom results in nucleus (H⁺) of ~1.5×10⁻³ pm size.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 285; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Position of hydrogen',NULL,'1','fillup','1.5,×10⁻³,1.59',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/90214q.png'),
(90215,'chemistry','The normal atomic size lies in range of','50-200pm','Normal atomic and ionic size is of the range of  50 to 200pm.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 285; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Position of hydrogen',NULL,'1','mcq','50-200pm','150-200pm','200-250pm','100-200pm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/90215q.png'),
(90216,'chemistry','H⁺ always exists in','Combined form','The size of H⁺ is extremely small,which renders it highly unstable,so it always exists in combined form.\nH⁺ ions are not free to exist.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 285; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Position of hydrogen',NULL,'2','mcq','Free form','Combined form','Atomic form','As a free radical','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/90216q.png'),
(90301,'chemistry','H₂ is almost _______ by mass in the universe.','70%','70% of the total mass of the universe\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 285; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Dihydrogen-Occurance',NULL,'1','fillup','70%,80%',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90302,'chemistry','The giant planets Jupiter and Saturn consists mostly of','H₂','The giant planets Jupiter and Saturn consist mostly of hydrogen. However, due to its light nature, it is much less abundant (0.15% by mass) in the earth’s atmosphere.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 285; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Dihydrogen-Occurance',NULL,'1','mcq','H₂','O₂','N₂','He','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90303,'chemistry','The % abundance of Hydrogen on earth','0.15%','The giant planets Jupiter and Saturn consist mostly of hydrogen. However, due to its light nature, it is much less abundant (0.15% by mass) in the earth’s atmosphere.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 285; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Dihydrogen-Occurance',NULL,'1','mcq','70%','0.15%','15.40%','80%','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/90303q.png'),
(90304,'chemistry','The combined form of hydrogen on earth constitutes','15.40%','In the combined form it constitutes 15.4% of the earth\'s crust and the oceans. In the combined form besides in water, it occurs in plant and animal tissues, carbohydrates, proteins, hydrides including\nhydrocarbons and many other compounds.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 285; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Dihydrogen-Occurance',NULL,'1','mcq','0.15%','70%','80%','15.40%','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90305,'chemistry','In plants and animals, Hydrogen exists in','Combined state','In the combined form besides in water, it occurs in plant and animal tissues, carbohydrates, proteins, hydrides including hydrocarbons and many other compounds.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 285; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Dihydrogen-Occurance',NULL,'1','mcq','Free state','Combined state','Ionic state','Diatomic state','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90306,'biology','What is phospholipids?','Lipid having phosphorous and a phosphorylated organic compound in them','These are phospholipids. They are found in cell membrane. Lecithin is one example. Some tissues especially the neural tissues have lipids with more complex structures.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:144, paragraph no:2) Refer to the structure of Phospholipids given in diagram (e0907)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Lipids and Nitrogen Bases',NULL,'2','mcq','Lipid having phosphorous and a phosphorylated organic compound in them','Inorganic compound with phosphorous Ion','Isolated phosphorous with fatty acids','Lipid having phosphorous and a non phosphorylated inorganic compound in them','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(90307,'biology','Which among the following is correct about phospholipids?  \n\ni. They are found in cell membrane.        \n\nii. Lecithin is a phospholipid         \n\niii.They are phosphorylated inorganic compound \n\niv. They are marcomolecules','i and ii only','These are phospholipids. They are found in cell membrane. Lecithin is\none example. Some tissues especially the neural tissues have lipids with\nmore complex structures.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:144, paragraph no:2)"','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Lipids and Nitrogen Bases',NULL,'1','mcq','i only','ii and iii only','i and ii only','i, ii and iii','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(90308,'biology','Some tissues especially the neural tissues have lipids with more complex structures.','TRUE','These are phospholipids. They are found in cell membrane. Lecithin is\none example. Some tissues especially the neural tissues have lipids with\nmore complex structures.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:144, paragraph no:2)".(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:144, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Lipids and Nitrogen Bases',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(90309,'biology','Identify the compund from the diagram (0905)','Palmitic acid','Reference  : NCERT class 11 Page 145 Figure 9.1','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Lipids and Nitrogen Bases',NULL,'3','mcq','Palmitic acid','Glycerol','Lecithin','Cholestrol','NEW','11-145','11-107','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/90309q.png'),
(90310,'biology','Identify the compund from the diagram (0906)','Glycerol','Reference  : NCERT class 11 Page 145 Figure 9.1','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Lipids and Nitrogen Bases',NULL,'3','mcq','Palmitic acid','Glycerol','Lecithin','Cholestrol','NEW','11-145','11-107','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/90310q.png'),
