(90410,'biology','One feature common to all those compounds found in the acid soluble pool.','They have molecular weights ranging from 18 to around 800 daltons (Da) approximately','There is one feature common to all those compounds found in the acid soluble pool. They have molecular weights ranging from 18 to around 800 daltons (Da) approximately.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:146, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Primary, Secondary Metabolites and  Biomolecules',NULL,'3','mcq','They have similar molecular formula','They have molecular weights ranging from 18 to around 800 daltons (Da) approximately','They have molecular weights ranging from 2 to around 10 daltons (Da) approximately','Same chemical and physical properties','NEW','11-146','11-108',NULL),
(90411,'biology','The acid insoluble fraction, has only four types of organic compounds which are _______','Proteins, nucleic acids, polysaccharides and lipids','The acid insoluble fraction, has only four types of organic compounds i.e., proteins, nucleic acids, polysaccharides and lipids.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:146, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Primary, Secondary Metabolites and  Biomolecules',NULL,'3','mcq','Polysaccharides, glycerine, inorganic compounds and fats','Proteins, ions, Polymeric substance and fats','Proteins, nucleic acids, polysaccharides and lipids','Vitamins, fats, lipids and organic compounds','NEW','11-146','11-108',NULL),
(51050,'chemistry','Which of the following do you think is a correct relationship between the molar mass of gas temperature and its pressure?','M = dRT/P','We know that the ideal gas equation is given by PV = nRT. Number of moles = n; can also be written as m/M, so PV = nRT becomes, PV = mRT/M. We also have density d = m/V, so molar mass M = dRT/P \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 145; Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','M = dRT/P','M = dRT/V','PV = nRT','M = VRT/P','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90412,'biology','The classes of acid insoluble fraction i.e., proteins, nucleic acids, polysaccharides and lipids. These compounds with the exception of lipids, have molecular weights in the range of','Ten thousand daltons and above','The acid insoluble fraction, has only four types of organic compounds i.e., proteins, nucleic acids, polysaccharides and lipids. These classes of compounds with the exception of lipids, have molecular weights in the range of ten thousand daltons and above.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:146, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Primary, Secondary Metabolites and  Biomolecules',NULL,'2','mcq','Ten thousand daltons and above','Thousand daltons','Below ten thousand daltons','Only ten thousand daltons','NEW','11-146','11-108',NULL),
(90413,'biology','Molecular weights less than one thousand dalton and are usually referred to as ________','Micromolecules','chemical compounds found in living organisms are of two types. One, those which have molecular weights less than one thousand dalton and are usually referred to as micromolecules or simply biomolecules.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:146, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Primary, Secondary Metabolites and  Biomolecules',NULL,'3','fillup','Mi,cro,mol,ec,ules',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-146','11-108',NULL),
(90414,'biology','Those which are found in the acid insoluble fraction are called macromolecules or biomacromolecules','TRUE','chemical compounds found in living organisms are of two types those which have molecular weights less than one thousand dalton and are usually referred to as micromolecules or simply biomolecules while those which are found in the acid insoluble fraction are called macromolecules or biomacromolecules.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:146, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Primary, Secondary Metabolites and  Biomolecules',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-146','11-108',NULL),
(90415,'biology','Molecular weights of lipids dosen\'t exceed______ and they come under _______','800 Da, Acid insoluble fraction','The molecules in the insoluble fraction with the exception of lipids are polymeric substances.lipids, whose molecular weights do not exceed 800 Da, come under acid insoluble fraction.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:146, paragraph no:4)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Primary, Secondary Metabolites and  Biomolecules',NULL,'2','mcq','700 Da, Acid soluble fraction','500 Da, Acid Insoluble fraction','800 Da, Acid insoluble fraction','400, Acid soluble fraction','NEW','11-146','11-108',NULL),
(90416,'biology','Why do lipids, whose molecular weights do not exceed 800 Da, come under acid insoluble fraction, i.e., macromolecular fraction?  \n\ni. Lipids are arranged into structures like cell membrane and other membranes .when membranes are broken into pieces form vesicles which are not water soluble  \n\nii. Membrane fragments in the form of vesicles get separated along with the acid insoluble pool and hence in the macromolecular fraction  \n\niii.  Lipids are strictly macromolecules','i and ii only','Lipids are indeed small molecular weight compounds and are present not only as such but also arranged into structures like cell membrane and other membranes. When we grind a tissue, we are disrupting the cell structure. Cell membrane and other membranes are broken into pieces, and form vesicles which are not water soluble. Therefore, these membrane fragments in the form of vesicles get separated along with the acid insoluble pool and hence in the macromolecular fraction. Lipids are not strictly macromolecules.  .(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:147, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Primary, Secondary Metabolites and  Biomolecules',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii only','i and iii only','i, ii and iii','iii only','NEW','11-147','11-109',NULL),
(90417,'biology','The acid soluble pool represents roughly ____________','Cytoplasmic composition','The acid soluble pool represents roughly the cytoplasmic composition.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:147, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Primary, Secondary Metabolites and  Biomolecules',NULL,'2','mcq','Cytoplasmic composition','Organelles composition','Both a and b','Entire chemical composition','NEW','11-147','11-109',NULL),
(90418,'biology','The macromolecules from cytoplasm and organelles become the________','Acid insoluble fraction','The macromolecules from cytoplasm and organelles become the acid insoluble fraction. Together they represent the entire chemical composition of living tissues or organisms.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:147, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Primary, Secondary Metabolites and  Biomolecules',NULL,'2','mcq','Acid insoluble fraction','Acid soluble fraction','Entire chemical fraction','Acid soluble as well as acid insoluble fractions','NEW','11-147','11-109',NULL),
(90419,'biology','Match the following Component with they % of the total cellular mass','Proteins-10-15,Nucleic acids-5 to 7,Lipids-2,Ions-1','Reference  :NCERT class 11 TABLE 9.4 pageno-147','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Primary, Secondary Metabolites and  Biomolecules',NULL,'3','match','Proteins,10-15','Nucleic acids,5 to 7','Lipids,2','Ions,1','NEW','11-147','11-109',NULL),
(90420,'biology','The correct order of chemical composition of living tissues / cells in term of % of the total cellular mass is -','Proteins > Nucleic acid > Carbohydrate > Lipid','Reference  :NCERT class 11  TABLE 9.4 pageno-147','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Primary, Secondary Metabolites and  Biomolecules',NULL,'2','order','1,Proteins','2,Nucleic acid','3,Carbohydrate','4,Lipid','NEW','11-147','11-109',NULL),
(90421,'biology','Which of the following is not a secondary metabolite?\n i. Cellulose \nii. Morphine \niii. Cholesterol \niv. Glucose','iii and iv only','Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Table 9.3','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Primary, Secondary Metabolites and  Biomolecules',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii only','ii and iii only','iii and iv only','iv only','NEW','11-146','11-108',NULL),
(90501,'chemistry','Dihydrogen can be prepared in laboratory by reacting granulated zinc with','Dilute HCl','It can also be prepared by the reaction of zinc with aqueous alkali.\n\nZn + 2NaOH → Na₂ZnO₂(Sodium zincate) + H₂\n\nIt is usually prepared by the reaction of granulated zinc with dilute hydrochloric acid.\nZn + 2H⁺ → Zn₂+ + H₂\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 286; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Preparation of  H₂',NULL,'1','mcq','Conc.HCl','Dilute HCl','Gaseous HCl','Dilute H₂SO₄','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90502,'chemistry','Match the following. diagram(n0903)','(i) - (c) ; (ii) - (d) ; (iii) - (a) ; (iv) - (b)','High purity (>99.95%) dihydrogen isobtained by electrolysing warm aqueous barium hydroxide solution between nickel electrodes.\n\nDihydrogen can also be prepared by the reaction of zinc with aqueous alkali.\nZn + 2NaOH → Na₂ZnO₂(Sodium zincate) + H₂\n\nDihydrogen is usually prepared by the reaction of granulated zinc with dilute hydrochloric acid.\nZn + 2H⁺ → Zn₂+ + H₂\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 286; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Preparation of  H₂',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) - (a) ; (ii) - (c) ; (iii) - (d) ; (iv) - (b)','(i) - (a) ; (ii) - (b) ; (iii) - (c) ; (iv) - (d)','(i) - (c) ; (ii) - (d) ; (iii) - (a) ; (iv) - (b)','(i) - (d) ; (ii) - (c) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (a)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/90502q.png'),
(90503,'chemistry','Electrolysis of acidic water by using Pt electrodes gives','H₂ Gas','Electrolysis of acidified water using platinum electrodes gives hydrogen.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 286; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Preparation of  H₂',NULL,'1','mcq','H⁺  ion','H⁻ Ion','H₂ Gas','Atomic H','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90504,'chemistry','Electrolysis of Barium hydroxide between nickel electrodes yield ______','Hydrogen','High purity (>99.95%) dihydrogen is obtained by electrolysing warm aqueous barium hydroxide solution between nickel electrodes.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 286; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Preparation of  H₂',NULL,'1','mcq','Ba','Oxygen','Hydrogen','Nickel','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90505,'chemistry','H₂ Gas is obtained as byproduct in the manufacture of','NaOH','It is obtained as a byproduct in the manufacture of sodium hydroxide and chlorine by the electrolysis of brine solution.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 286; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Preparation of  H₂',NULL,'1','mcq','KOH','LiOH','NaOH','NaCl','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90506,'chemistry','Which reaction not occurs during the electrolysis of brine solution.\n\n1) 2Cl(aq)  ->  Cl₂(g) + 2e⁻ at anode\n2) 2H₂O(l) + 2e⁻  ->  H₂(g) + 2OH⁻(aq) at cathode\n3) 2Na⁺(aq) + 2Cl⁻(aq) + 2H₂O(l)  ->  Cl₂(g) + H₂(g) + 2Na⁺(aq) + 2OH⁻(aq)\n4) H₂(g) + Cl₂(g)  ->  2HCl(g)','4','The reactions that occur during the electrolysis of brine are as follows:\n\nAt the anode: 2Cl⁻(aq) -> Cl₂(g) + 2e⁻\n\nAt the cathode: 2H₂O(l) + 2e⁻ -> H₂(g) + 2OH⁻(aq)\n\nThe overall reaction is:\n\n2NaCl(aq) + 2H₂O(l) -> Cl₂(g) + H₂(g) + 2Na⁺(aq) + 2OH⁻(aq)\n\nThe fourth reaction, H₂(g) + Cl₂(g) -> 2HCl(g), is not involved in the electrolysis of brine. This is the reaction between hydrogen and chlorine gases to form hydrogen chloride gas. Although hydrogen and chlorine gases are produced during the electrolysis of brine, they do not react with each other to form HCl gas under the conditions of the electrolysis. Therefore, option 4 is the reaction that does not occur during the electrolysis of brine solution.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 286; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Preparation of  H₂',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90507,'chemistry','Match the following. diagram(n0904)','(i) - (c) ; (ii) - (d) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (a)','The mixture of CO and H2 is called water gas. As this mixture of CO and H2 is used for the synthesis of methanol and a number of hydrocarbons, it is also called synthesis gas or \'syngas\'. Nowadays \'syngas\' is produced from sewage, saw-dust, scrap wood, newspapers etc. The process of producing \'syngas\' from coal is called \'coal gasification\'.\nC(s) + H₂O(g) → CO(g) + H₂(g)\n\nPresently ~77% of the industrial dihydrogen is produced from petro-chemicals 18% from coal, 4% from electrolysis of aqueous solutions and 1% from other sources.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 286; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Preparation of  H₂',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) - (d) ; (ii) - (a) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (c)','(i) - (a) ; (ii) - (d) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (c)','(i) - (a) ; (ii) - (b) ; (iii) - (c) ; (iv) - (d)','(i) - (c) ; (ii) - (d) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (a)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/90507q.png'),
(90508,'chemistry','Which one is water gas shift reaction ?','CO(g) + H₂O(g) → CO₂(g) + H₂(g)','CO(g) + H₂O(g) → CO₂(g) + H₂(g)\nThis is called water-gas shift reaction. Carbon dioxide is removed by scrubbing with sodium arsenite solution.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 286; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Preparation of  H₂',NULL,'1','mcq','H₂(g) + Cl₂(g) → 2HCl(g)','2H₂O(l) + 2e⁻ → H₂(g) + 2OH⁻(aq)','CO(g) + H₂O(g) → CO₂(g) + H₂(g)','C(s) + H₂O(g) → CO(g) + H₂(g)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90509,'chemistry','Carbon dioxide is removed by scrubbing with __________.','Sodium arsenite solution','CO(g) + H₂O(g) → CO₂(g) + H₂(g)\nThis is called water-gas shift reaction. Carbon dioxide is removed by scrubbing with sodium arsenite solution.Reference: \n(NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 286; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Preparation of  H₂',NULL,'1','fillup','So,diu,m ars,en,ite sol,uti,on',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
