(90601,'chemistry','Which statement is wrong about dihydrogen?\n\na.Lighter than air\n\nb.Soluble in water\n\nc.Incombustible gas','only b and c','Dihydrogen is a colourless, odourless, tasteless, combustible gas. It is lighter than air and insoluble in water.\nH₂ is lighter than the air and insoluble in water.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 286; paragraph no.5)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Properties of H₂-Physical properties and Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','only a','only b','only a and c','only b and c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311708,'biology','Assertion : Yeasts such as Saccharomyces cerevisiae are used in baking industry. \n\nReason :  CO₂  produced during fermentation causes bread dough to rise by thermal expansion. [2015]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Yeast is a fermentation agent. It is a known\nfact that yeast raises bread dough to rise\nand hence, yeast is also used to increase\nthe volume, making the dough porous and\nthe product soft. It is the  CO₂ \nthat is produced by the yeast that helps\nthe dough to rise.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','If the Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','If both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90602,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\n(A): H₂ is inert at room temperature.\n\n(R): The H-H bond dissociation energy is highest to break the H-H bond at room temperature.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','The chemical behaviour of dihydrogen (and for that matter any molecule) is determined, to a large extent, by bond dissociation enthalpy. The H–H bond dissociation enthalpy is the highest for a single bond between two atoms of any element. What inferences would you draw from this fact ? It is because of this factor that the dissociation of dihydrogen into its atoms is only ~0.081% around 2000K which increases to 95.5% at 5000K.Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 286; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Properties of H₂-Physical properties and Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90603,'chemistry','Which statement is wrong about dihydrogen ?\n\na.Dissociation of dihydrogen into its atoms is 0.081% at 2000K.\n\nb.Dissociation of dihydrogen into its atoms is 95.5% at 5000K.\n\nc. Atomic hydrogen is produced at a high temperature by using electric arc or UV-radiations.\n\nd. Dissociation of dihydrogen into its atoms is 0.081% at 1000K','only d','Atomic hydrogen is produced at high temperature in an electric are or under ultraviolet radiations The dissociation of dihydrogen at 2000 K is only 0.081%\n  \nH–H bond dissociation enthalpy is highest for a single bond for any diatomic molecule.  \n\nDihydrogen yields atomic hydrogen when irradiated with UV light.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 286; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Properties of H₂-Physical properties and Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','only a','only b','only a and c','only d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90604,'chemistry','Hydrogen  accomplishes reactions by','All of these','It accomplishes reactions by\n (i) loss of the only electron to give H⁺\n(ii) gain of an electron to form H⁻ , and\n(iii) sharing electrons to form a single covalent bond.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 287; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Properties of H₂-Physical properties and Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Loss of electron to form  H⁺','Gain of electron to form  H⁻','Sharing electrons to make Covalent bond','All of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90605,'chemistry','Match the following. diagram(n0906)','(i) - (c) ; (ii) - (d) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (a)','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 287; paragraph no.2) diagram(ne0901)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Properties of H₂-Physical properties and Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) - (d) ; (ii) - (a) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (c)','(i) - (c) ; (ii) - (d) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (a)','(i) - (a) ; (ii) - (b) ; (iii) - (c) ; (iv) - (d)','(i) - (a) ; (ii) - (d) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (c)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/90605q.png'),
(90606,'chemistry','H₂ reacts with metals and gives','Metal hydrides','Metals have tendency to lose electron this electron is gained by H to make negative ion so called hydride.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 287; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Properties of H₂-Physical properties and Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Metal hydrides','Metal hydrates','Metal hydroxide','Metal oxides','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90607,'chemistry','H₂ reacts metal ions and reduce them to','Neutral metal','Metals which are less active can easily reduce to corresponding Metal by gaining electron from hydrogen.\nNote : less active metals means less ability to remove electrons.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 287; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Properties of H₂-Physical properties and Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Neutral metal','Metal anion','More oxidation no.M','Free radical','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90608,'chemistry','Hydrogen converts metal oxide to','Corresponding metal','Metals which are less active can easily reduce to corresponding Metal by gaining electron from hydrogen.\nNote: less active metals means less ability to remove electrons.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 287; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Properties of H₂-Physical properties and Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Corresponding metal','Metal anion','More oxidation no.M','Less cationic M','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90609,'chemistry','Margarine is prepared by ________ of vegetable oil in the presence of Ni catalyst.','Hydrogenation','Hydrogenation is addition of hydrogen,hydrogen can only be added to unsaturated molecules.\nNote: unsaturated molecules are those,in which Valency of atom is not fully satisfied.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 287; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Properties of H₂-Physical properties and Chemical properties',NULL,'1','fillup','Hy,dro,ge,na,tion,dra',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90610,'chemistry','Hydroformylation of olefins yields','Aldehydes','Hydroformylation of olefins yields aldehydes which further undergo reduction to give alcohols.Reference: \n(NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 287; paragraph no.5)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Properties of H₂-Physical properties and Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Ketones','Aldehydes','Alcohols','Enols','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90611,'chemistry','Choose the wrong statement.\n\na. Dihydrogen oxidize chlorine into Cl⁻  ion\n\nb. An electron pair is shared between H and Cl to form covalent bond\n\nc. An electron is donated to chlorine by hydrogen\n\nd. Dihydrogen reduces Cu(2) oxide to Cu in zero oxidation state','only a and c','(i) Dihydrogen reduces chlorine into chloride (Cl⁻) ion and itself gets oxidised to H⁺ ion by chlorine to form hydrogen chloride. An electron pair is shared between H and Cl leading to the formation of a covalent molecule.\n\n(ii) Dihydrogen is reduced by sodium to form NaH. An electron is transferred from Na to H leading to the formation of an ionic compound, Na⁺ H⁻.\n\n(iii) Dihydrogen reduces copper(II) oxide to copper in zero oxidation state and itself gets oxidised to H2O, which is a covalent  molecule.Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 287; Problem 9.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Properties of H₂-Physical properties and Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','only a','only b','only c','only a and c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90612,'chemistry','Choose the wrong statement.\n\na. Dihydrogen reduces Sodium into Na⁺  ion.\n\nb. An electron pair is shared between H and Na to form covalent bond.\n\nc. An electron is donated to Na by hydrogen.','All of these','(i) Dihydrogen reduces chlorine into chloride (Cl⁻) ion and itself gets oxidised to H⁺ ion by chlorine to form hydrogen chloride. An electron pair is shared between H and Cl leading to the formation of a covalent molecule.\n\n(ii) Dihydrogen is reduced by sodium to form NaH. An electron is transferred from Na to H leading to the formation of an ionic compound, Na⁺ H⁻.\n\n(iii) Dihydrogen reduces copper(II) oxide to copper in zero oxidation state and itself gets oxidised to H2O, which is a covalent  molecule.Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 287; Problem 9.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Properties of H₂-Physical properties and Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','only a','only b','only c','All of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90613,'chemistry','Choose the wrong statement.\n\na. Hydrogen reduces copper(2) to Cu(0) .\n\nb. An electron is donated by hydrogen to copper.\n\nc. Hydrogen itself oxidize to water, during reduction of less active metals.\n\nd. an electron is transferred from Cu to H and Cu itself change to zero oxidation state.','only d','(i) Dihydrogen reduces chlorine into chloride (Cl⁻) ion and itself gets oxidised to H⁺ ion by chlorine to form hydrogen chloride. An electron pair is shared between H and Cl leading to the formation of a covalent molecule.\n\n(ii) Dihydrogen is reduced by sodium to form NaH. An electron is transferred from Na to H leading to the formation of an ionic compound, Na⁺ H⁻.\n\n(iii) Dihydrogen reduces copper(II) oxide to copper in zero oxidation state and itself gets oxidised to H2O, which is a covalent  molecule.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 287; Problem 9.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Properties of H₂-Physical properties and Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','only a','only b','only c','only d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90614,'biology','Which among the following is Incorrect regarding to the primary structure of protein? \ni.A protein is imagined as a helix  \nii.the left end represented by the first amino acid \niii. the right end is represented by the last amino acid','i only','A protein is imagined as a line, the left end represented by the first amino acid and the right end represented by the last aminoacid.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:149, paragraph no:4)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Proteins and  Structure of Proteins',NULL,'2','mcq','i only','i and iii only','ii and iii only','i,ii and iii','NEW','11-149','11-111',NULL),
(90615,'biology','Match the following','First amino acid-N terminal,Last amino acid-C terminal','The first amino acid is also called as N-terminal amino acid. The last amino acid is called the Cterminal amino acid.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:150, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Proteins and  Structure of Proteins',NULL,'2','match','First amino acid,N terminal','Last amino acid,C terminal',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-150','11-112',NULL),
(90616,'biology','Which among the following is false?','In proteins, only left handed helices are observed','A protein thread does not exist throughout as an extended rigid rod. The thread is folded in the form of a helix (similar to a revolving staircase). Of course, only some portions of the protein thread are arranged in the form of  helix. In proteins, only right handed helices are observed(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:150, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Proteins and  Structure of Proteins',NULL,'1','mcq','A protein thread does not exist throughout as an extended rigid rod.','The thread is folded in the form of a helix (similar to a revolving staircase)','Of course, only some portions of the protein thread are arranged in the form of a helix','In proteins, only left handed helices are observed','NEW','11-150','11-112',NULL),
(90617,'biology','In proteins, only right handed helices are observed. Other regions of the protein thread are folded into other forms in which is called as the','Secondary structure','In proteins, only right handed helices are observed. Other regions of the protein thread are folded into other forms in what is called the secondary structure(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:150, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Proteins and  Structure of Proteins',NULL,'1','mcq','Tertiary structure','Primary structure','Secondary structure','Quaternary structure','NEW','11-150','11-112',NULL),
(90618,'biology','The long protein chain is also folded upon itself like a hollow woolen ball, giving rise to the','Tertiary structure','In addition, the long protein chain is also folded upon itself like a hollow woolen ball, giving rise to the tertiary structure (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:150, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Proteins and  Structure of Proteins',NULL,'2','mcq','Secondary structure','Quaternary structure','Tertiary structure','Primary structure','NEW','11-150','11-112',NULL),
(90619,'biology','________structure of protein is absolutely necessary for the many biological activities of proteins.','Tertiary','Tertiary structure is absolutely necessary for the many biological activities of proteins(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:150, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Proteins and  Structure of Proteins',NULL,'3','mcq','Tertiary','Secondary','Primary','Quarternary','NEW','11-150','11-112',NULL),
(90620,'biology','Which among the following is Incorrect to the structure aspects of protein?','None of the Above','Some proteins are an assembly of more than one polypeptide or subunits. The manner in which these individual folded polypeptidesor subunits are arranged with respect to each other (e.g. linear string of spheres, spheres arranged one upon each other in the form of a cube or plate etc.) is the architecture of a protein otherwise called the quaternary structure of a protein(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:150, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Proteins and  Structure of Proteins',NULL,'3','mcq','Some proteins are an assembly of more than one polypeptide or subunits.','The manner in which individual folded polypeptides or subunits are arranged with respect to each other (e.g. linear string of spheres, spheres in the form of a cube or plate) is the architecture of a protein','The architecture of a protein otherwise called the quaternary structure of a protein','None of the Above','NEW','11-150','11-112',NULL),
(90621,'biology','Which among the following is false regrading haemoglobin and its subunits?','Adult human haemoglobin consists of 6 subunits.','Adult human haemoglobin consists of 4 subunits. Two of these are identical to each other. Hence, two subunits of α type and two subunits of β type together constitute the human haemoglobin (Hb)(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:150, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Proteins and  Structure of Proteins',NULL,'3','mcq','Adult human haemoglobin consists of 6 subunits.','Two of these ( subunits) are identical to each other','Haemoglobin thus has two subunits of α type and two subunits of β type','Hence, two subunits of α type and two subunits of β type together constitute the human haemoglobin (Hb)','NEW','11-150','11-112',NULL),
