(90704,'biology','The process of formation glycosidic bond between the individual monosaccharides of is called as','Dehydration','glycosidic bond is formed by dehydration(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:151, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Nature of Bond Linking Monomers in a Polymer',NULL,'2','mcq','Dehydration','Hydration','Carboxylation','Oxidation','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(90705,'biology','The glycosidic bond is formed between','Two carbon atoms of two adjacent monosaccharides','In a polysaccharide the individual monosaccharides are linked by a glycosidic bond. This bond is also formed by dehydration. This bond is formed between two carbon atoms of two adjacent monosaccharides(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:151, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Nature of Bond Linking Monomers in a Polymer',NULL,'2','mcq','Two carbon atoms of two adjacent monosaccharides','Two O₂ atoms of two adjacent monosaccharides','(-NH2) group of amino acids','Carboxyl (-COOH) groups of monosacchrides','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(90706,'biology','Which among the following is Incorrect?','None of the Above','In a nucleic acid a phosphate moiety links the 3’-carbon of one sugar of one nucleotide to the 5’-carbon of the sugar of the succeeding nucleotide. The bond between the phosphate and hydroxyl group of sugar is an ester bond. As there is one such ester bond on either side, it is called phosphodiester bond(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:151, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Nature of Bond Linking Monomers in a Polymer',NULL,'2','mcq','In a nucleic acid a phosphate moiety links the 3’-carbon of one sugar of one nucleotide to the 5’-carbon of the sugar of the succeeding nucleotide','The bond between the phosphate and hydroxyl group of sugar is an ester bond.','As there is one such ester bond on either side, it is called phosphodiester bond','None of the Above','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(90707,'biology','Nucleic acids exhibit a wide variety of secondary structures','TRUE','Nucleic acids exhibit a wide variety of secondary structures. For example, one of the secondary structures exhibited by DNA is the famous Watson-Crick model(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:151, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Nature of Bond Linking Monomers in a Polymer',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(90708,'biology','Secondary structures exhibited by DNA is the famous Watson-Crick model Which explains?  \n\ni. DNA exists as a double helix. \n\nii. The two strands of polynucleotides are antiparallel i.e., run in the opposite direction \n\niii. Each strand appears to be helical structure \n\niv. The nitrogen bases are projected more or less perpendicular to this backbone but face inside.','i,ii,iii & iv','This model says that DNA exists as a double helix. The two strands of polynucleotides are antiparallel i.e., run in the opposite direction.The nitrogen bases are projected more or less perpendicular to this backbone but face inside(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:151, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Nature of Bond Linking Monomers in a Polymer',NULL,'2','mcq','i only','i and iii only','i, ii and iii only','i,ii,iii & iv','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(90709,'biology','In DNA the backbone is formed by','Sugar-phosphate-sugar chain','The backbone is formed by the sugarphosphate-sugar chain(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:151, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Nature of Bond Linking Monomers in a Polymer',NULL,'2','mcq','Sugar-phosphate-sugar chain','Sugar-carbon-sugar chain','Peptide bonds','Glycosidic bond','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(90710,'biology','A and G of one strand compulsorily base pairs with T and C , respectively, on the other strand.','TRUE','The nitrogen bases are projected more or less perpendicular to this backbone but face inside. A and G of one strand compulsorily base pairs with T and C, respectively, on the other strand(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:151, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Nature of Bond Linking Monomers in a Polymer',NULL,'3','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(90711,'biology','There are_____hydrogen bonds between A and T and ______ hydrogen bonds between G and C.','Two, Three','There are two hydrogen bonds between A and T and three hydrogen bonds between G and C(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:152, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Nature of Bond Linking Monomers in a Polymer',NULL,'3','mcq','Two, Three','Three, Two','Two,One','Two,Four','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(90712,'biology','Match the following','Each strand appears like-a helical staircase,Each step of ascent is represented by-a pair of bases','There are two hydrogen bonds between A and T and three hydrogen bonds between G and C. Each strand appears like a helical staircase. Each step of ascent is represented by a pair of bases(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:152, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Nature of Bond Linking Monomers in a Polymer',NULL,'3','match','Each strand appears like,a helical staircase','Each step of ascent is represented by,a pair of bases',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-152',NULL,NULL),
(90713,'biology','At each step of ascent, the strand turns ____ and one full turn of the helical strand would involve _____ base pairs.','36°,Ten','At each step of ascent, the strand turns 36°. One full turn of the helical strand would involve ten steps or ten base pairs(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:152, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Nature of Bond Linking Monomers in a Polymer',NULL,'2','mcq','35°, Nine','34°,Eight','36°,Ten','37°,Ten','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(90714,'biology','Match the following measurements corresponding to a B-DNA feature','Pitch-34Å,The rise per base pair-3.4Å','The pitch would be 34Å. The rise per base pair would be 3.4Å. This form of DNA with the mentioned salient features is called BDNA(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:152, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Nature of Bond Linking Monomers in a Polymer',NULL,'2','match','pitch,34Å','The rise per base pair,3.4Å',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(90715,'biology','Identify the given the diagram (0926)','Secondary structure of DNA','NCERT class 11 Figure 9.5 page.no-151','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Nature of Bond Linking Monomers in a Polymer',NULL,'1','mcq','Primary structure of DNA','Secondary structure of DNA','Tertiary structure of DNA','Quaternary structure of DNA','NEW','11-151',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/90715q.png'),
(90801,'chemistry','Match the following. diagram(n0905)','(i) - (d) ; (ii) - (c) ; (iii) - (a) ; (iv) - (b)','• The largest single use of dihydrogen is in the synthesis of ammonia which is used in the manufacture of nitric acid and nitrogenous fertilizers.\n\n• Dihydrogen is used in the manufacture of vanaspati fat by the hydrogenation of polyunsaturated vegetable oils like soyabean, cotton seeds etc\n.\n• It is used in the manufacture of bulk organic chemicals, particularly methanol.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 287; Paragraph no.5)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Uses of H₂',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) - (d) ; (ii) - (a) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (c)','(i) - (d) ; (ii) - (c) ; (iii) - (a) ; (iv) - (b)','(i) - (a) ; (ii) - (b) ; (iii) - (c) ; (iv) - (d)','(i) - (d) ; (ii) - (c) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (a)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/90801q.png'),
(90802,'chemistry','Which one is used in cutting and welding ?','Oxy-Hydrogen','Atomic hydrogen and oxy-hydrogen torches find use for cutting and welding purposes. Atomic hydrogen atoms (produced by dissociation of dihydrogen with the help of an electric arc) are allowed to recombine on the surface to be welded to generate the temperature of 4000 K.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 288; Paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Uses of H₂',NULL,'1','mcq','Oxy-Hydrogen','Hydrogen gas','Syngas','Hydrogen','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90803,'chemistry','Which one is used on surface of substance to be welded to generate the T upto 4000K ?','Atomic hydrogen','Atomic hydrogen and oxy-hydrogen torches find use for cutting and welding purposes. Atomic hydrogen atoms (produced by dissociation of dihydrogen with the help of an electric arc) are allowed to recombine on the surface to be welded to generate the temperature of 4000 K.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 288; Paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Uses of H₂',NULL,'1','mcq','Atomic hydrogen','Molecular hydrogen','Cationic hydrogen','Anionic hydrogen','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90804,'chemistry','Dihydrogen is used in ________ to generate electrical energy.','Fuel cell','Dihydrogen is used in fuel cells for generating electrical energy.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 288; Paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Uses of H₂',NULL,'1','fillup','Fu,el, cell,re,mo,te',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90805,'biology','Which among the following is incorrect according to the concept of metabolism?','None of the Above','Metabolic pathways are similar to the automobile traffic in a city. These pathways are either linear or circular. These pathways crisscross each other, i.e., there are traffic junctions. Flow of metabolites through metabolic pathway has a definite rate and direction like automobile traffic. This metabolite flow is called the dynamic state of body constituents. What is most important is that this interlinked metabolic traffic is very smooth and without a single reported mishap for healthy conditions.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:152, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Concept of Metabolism',NULL,'1','mcq','Metabolic pathways are similar to the automobile traffic in a city. These pathways are either linear or circular','Metabolic pathways criss-cross each other, i.e., there are traffic junctions','Flow of metabolites through metabolic pathway has a definite rate and direction like automobile traffic.','None of the Above','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(90806,'biology','Metabolite flow is called the dynamic state of body constituents','TRUE','Flow of metabolites through metabolic pathway has a definite rate and direction like automobile traffic.This metabolite flow is called the dynamic state of body constituents. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:152, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Concept of Metabolism',NULL,'1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(90807,'biology','The Incorrect statement about the catalysed reaction?','These proteins with catalytic power are named co-factors.','Another feature of these metabolic reactions is that every chemical reaction is a catalysed reaction. There is no uncatalysed metabolic conversion in living systems. Even CO₂ dissolving in water, a physical process, is a catalysed reaction in living systems. The catalysts which hasten the rate of a given metabolic conversation are also proteins. These proteins with catalytic power are named enzyme(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:152, paragraph no:2).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Concept of Metabolism',NULL,'2','mcq','Another feature of these metabolic reactions is that every chemical reaction is a catalysed reaction.','There is no uncatalysed metabolic conversion in living systems.Even CO₂ dissolving in water, a physical process, is a catalysed reaction in living systems.','The catalysts which hasten the rate of a given metabolic conversation are also proteins.','These proteins with catalytic power are named co-factors.','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(90901,'chemistry','Dihydrogen Combines with almost all elements except','Noble gases','Dihydrogen, under certain reaction conditions, combines with almost all elements, except noble gases,Reference: \n(NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 288; Paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrides',NULL,'1','mcq','Noble gases','Chalcogens','Halogens','Transiotion metals','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90902,'chemistry','If E is the symbol of element then hydrides of hydrogen can be represented as','Both a and b','If ‘E’ is the symbol of an element then hydride can be expressed as EHx (e.g., MgH2) or EmHn (e.g., B₂H₆).\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 288; Paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrides',NULL,'1','mcq','EₘHₙ','EHₓ','Both a and b','None of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90903,'chemistry','Hydrides can be classified into','All of these','The hydrides are classified into three categories :\n\n(i) Ionic or saline or saltlike hydrides\n\n(ii) Covalent or molecular hydrides\n\n(iii) Metallic or non-stoichiometric hydrides\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 288; Paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrides',NULL,'1','mcq','Ionic Hydrides','Covalent Hydrides','Molecular Hydrides','All of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90904,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\n(A): Significant covalent character is found in the lighter metal hydrides such as LiH, BeH₂ and MgH₂.\n\n(R): Lighter metals have high electronegativity than heavier metals in the same group','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','However, significant covalent character is found in the lighter metal hydrides such as LiH, BeH₂ and MgH₂.\nGreater the electronegativity difference,greater will be the ionic character and smaller will be the Covalent Character and vice versa.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 288; Paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrides-Ionic Hydrides',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90905,'chemistry','Match the following. diagram(n0907)','(i) - (d) ; (ii) - (c) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (a)','BeH₂/ MgH₂,Covalent due to less electronegative difference between H and Be/Mg\nCrystalline form of ionic compounds have ions in bound form,unable to move and carry current.\nIn molten form ions are separated and can pass electricity.\n2H⁻ → H₂(g) + 2e⁻    At anode\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 288; Paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrides-Ionic Hydrides',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) - (d) ; (ii) - (a) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (c)','(i) - (a) ; (ii) - (b) ; (iii) - (c) ; (iv) - (d)','(i) - (d) ; (ii) - (c) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (a)','(i) - (b) ; (ii) - (c) ; (iii) - (a) ; (iv) - (d)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/90905q.png'),
(90906,'chemistry','Which reaction is not possible ?\n\na. NaH(s) + H₂O(aq) →  NaOH(aq) + H₂(g)\n\nb. 8LiH + A₂lCl₆ → 2LiAlH₄ + 6LiCl\n\nc. 2LiH + B₂H₆ → 2LiBH₄\n\nd. 2Li + O₂ → 2LiO','only d','NaH(s)+ H₂O(aq) → NaOH(aq) + H₂(g)\n\n8LiH + Al₂Cl₆ → 2LiAlH₄ + 6LiCl\n\n2LiH + B₂H₆ → 2LiBH₄\n\nLithium hydride is rather unreactive at moderate temperatures with O₂ or Cl₂. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 288; Paragraph no.4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrides-Ionic Hydrides',NULL,'2','mcq','only a','only b','only c','only d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90907,'chemistry','Dihydrogen forms molecular hydrides with most of','p-block','Dihydrogen forms molecular compounds with most of the p-block elements.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 288; Paragraph no.5)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrides-Covalent hydrides',NULL,'1','mcq','p-block','s-block','d-block','f-block','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90908,'chemistry','Electron-deficient Molecular hydrides act as','Lewis acids','Acid is that specie which accepts electrons(lewis concept) being e - deficient they love to accept electrons and act as lewis acid.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 288; Paragraph no.5)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrides-Covalent hydrides',NULL,'2','mcq','Lewis acids','Lewis base','Nucleophile','Electrophile','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90909,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds will be produced when lithium hydride reacts with boron trifluoride (BF3)?','LiBH₄','NaH(s)+ H₂O(aq) → NaOH(aq) + H₂(g)\n\n8LiH + Al₂Cl₆ → 2LiAlH₄ + 6LiCl\n\n2LiH + B₂H₆ → 2LiBH₄\n\nLithium hydride is rather unreactive at moderate temperatures with O₂ or Cl₂. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 288; Paragraph no.4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrides-Covalent hydrides',NULL,'2','mcq','LiBH₄','LiHBH₃','Li₂HBF₄','Li₂BF₅','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90910,'chemistry','Choose the wrong statement.\n\na. Electron rich hydrides have excess electrons(Lone pairs).\n\nb. Electron rich hydrides act as lewis base.\n\nc. Electron rich hydrides can make ionic bond.\n\nd. Electron rich hydrides have ability to make hydrogen bond','only c','Electron-rich hydrides have excess electrons which are present as lone pairs. Elements of group 15-17 form such compounds. (NH₃ has 1- lone pair, H₂O – 2 and HF –3 lone pairs). What do you expect from the behaviour of such compounds ? They will behave as Lewis bases i.e., electron donors. The presence of lone pairs on highly electronegative atoms like N, O and F in hydrides results in hydrogen bond formation between the molecules. This leads to the association of molecules.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 288; Paragraph no.6)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrides-Covalent hydrides',NULL,'2','mcq','only a','only b','only c','only d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90911,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\n(A): Hydrides of N,O,and F has low melting points than their corresponding group members.\n\n(R): They have high polarizability','Both assertion and reason are false','Hydrides of N,O,and F have high melting points and boiling points than their other group members,this is due to ability of making H-bond ,thisability decrease down the group.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 288; Problem no.9.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrides-Covalent hydrides',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90912,'chemistry','The metals of d or f block mostly form','Interstitial hydrides','Interstitial or non stoichiometric hydrides are formed by many d-block and f-block elements.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 289; Paragraph no.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrides-Metallic hydrides',NULL,'1','mcq','Interstitial hydrides','Saline hydrides','Covalent hydrides','Ionic hydrides','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91001,'chemistry','Match the following in the given diagram(n0908)','(i) - (d) ; (ii) - (c) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (a)','A major part of all living organisms is made up of water. Human body has about 65% and some plants have as much as 95% water. It is a crucial compound for the survival of all life forms. It is a solvent of great importance. The distribution of water over the earth’s surface is not uniform. The estimated world water supply is given in Table.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 289; Paragraph no.4) diagram(ne0903)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Water-Physical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) - (d) ; (ii) - (a) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (c)','(i) - (d) ; (ii) - (c) ; (iii) - (a) ; (iv) - (b)','(i) - (d) ; (ii) - (c) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (a)','(i) - (c) ; (ii) - (d) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (a)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/91001q.png'),
(91002,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement.\n\na. Water has colour and taste\n\nb. Water shows hydrogen bonding in liquid and solid state\n\nc. H₂O is liquid while  H₂S and H₂Se are gases','only b and c','It is a colourless and tasteless liquid.The unusual properties of water in the condensed phase (liquid and solid states) are due to the presence of extensive hydrogen bonding between water molecules. This leads to high freezing point, high boiling point, high heat of vaporisation and high heat of fusion in comparison to H2S and H2Se. \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 289; Paragraph no.4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Water-Physical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','only a','only b','only c','only b and c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91003,'chemistry','Match the following. diagram(n0909)','(i) - (b) ; (ii) - (c) ; (iii) - (d) ; (iv) - (a)','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 290; Table 9.3) diagram(ne0904)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Water-Physical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) - (d) ; (ii) - (a) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (c)','(i) - (a) ; (ii) - (b) ; (iii) - (d) ; (iv) - (c)','(i) - (b) ; (ii) - (c) ; (iii) - (d) ; (iv) - (a)','(i) - (a) ; (ii) - (b) ; (iii) - (c) ; (iv) - (d)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/91003q.png'),
(91004,'chemistry','The solvent for the transportation of ions and molecules in plants and animal metabolism','H₂O','It is an excellent solvent for transportation of ions and molecules required for plant and animal metabolism. Due to hydrogen bonding with polar molecules, even covalent compounds like alcohol and carbohydrates dissolve in water.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 290; Paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Water-Physical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','H₂O','H₂S','H₂Se','H₂Po','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91005,'chemistry','Covalent Compounds can also be solubilized in water due to','H-bonding','Oxygen in water have lone pair which can be donated to partial positive charge of hydrogen of molecule(dissolving)so called hydrogen bond.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 290; Paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Water-Physical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Dipole-dipole interaction','H-bonding','Ion-dipole interaction','London dispersion forces','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91006,'biology','Chemical compounds undergo two types of changes which are?  \ni.Chemical reactions  \nii.Physical reactions  \niii. Mechanical reactions  \niv.Mixed reactions','i and ii only','Chemical compounds undergo two types of changes. A physical process and when bonds are broken and new bonds are formed during transformation, this will be called a chemical reaction(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:154, paragraph no:2','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Chemical Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','i only','i and iii only','i and ii only','iii only','NEW','11-154','11-113',NULL),
(91007,'biology','The two types of physical changes are?  \ni. Change in shape without breaking of bonds  \nii.Change in state of matter   \niii. Beaking the bonds  \niv. Formation of new bonds','i and ii only','A physical change simply refers to a change in shape without breaking of bonds. This is a physical process. Another physical process is a change in state of matter: when ice melts into water, or when water becomes a vapour.These are also physical processes(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:154, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Chemical Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii only','i only','i and iv only','iii and iv only','NEW','11-154','11-113',NULL),
(91008,'biology','Hydrolysis of starch into glucose is an _______','Organic chemical reaction','hydrolysis of starch into glucose is an organic chemical reaction(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:154, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Chemical Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Organic chemical reaction','Inorganic chemical reaction','Organic physical reaction','Inorganic physical reaction','NEW','11-154','11-113',NULL),
(91009,'biology','Catalysed reactions proceed at rates vastly higher than that of uncatalysed ones because:  \ni. Enzymes work at slow rate \nii. When enzyme catalysed reactions are observed, the rate would be vastly higher than the same but uncatalysed reaction. \niii.Change in temperature','ii only','Catalysed reactions proceed at rates vastly higher than that of uncatalysed ones. When enzyme catalysed reactions are observed, the rate would be vastly higher than the same but uncatalysed reaction(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:155, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','i and ii only','ii only','iii only','i and iii only','NEW','11-155','11-113',NULL),
(91010,'biology','Identify the enzyme in the given diagram (0930)','Carbonic anhydrase','NCERT class 11 page no-155','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Carbonic anhydrase','Hydrolases','Lyases','Transferases','NEW','11-155','11-113','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/91010q.png'),
(91011,'biology','Pick the incorrect statement','None of the Above','by using the enzyme present within the cytoplasm called carbonic anhydrase, the reaction speeds dramatically with about 600,000 molecules being formed every second. The enzyme has accelerated the reaction rate by about 10 million times(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:155, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Carbonic anhydrase is present with the cytoplasm','With out carbonic anhydrase 200 molecules of H2CO3 being formed in an hour','With carbonic anhydrase, the reaction speeds dramatically with about 600,000 molecules being formed every second.','None of the Above','NEW','11-155','11-114',NULL),
(91012,'biology','Which among the following is true during the enzymes chemical reaction?  \ni. Proteins with three dimensional structures including an ‘active site’, convert a substrate (S) into a product (P)   \nii.The substrate has to diffusetowards the ‘active site’. There is thus, no absolute need of formation of an ‘ES’ complex  \niii.During the state where substrate is bound to the enzyme active site, a new structure of the substrate called transition state structure is formed','i and iii only','Proteins with three dimensional structures including an ‘active site’, convert a substrate (S) into a product (P)  The substrate has to diffuse towards the ‘active site’. There is obligatory formation of an ‘ES’ complex  During the state where substrate is bound to the enzyme active site, a new structure of the substrate called transition state structure is formed(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:154, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','i and ii only','i and iii only','iii only','ii only','NEW','11-154','11-115',NULL),
(91013,'biology','In enzymatic reaction after the expected bond breaking/making is completed, the product is released from the active site. The structure of substrate gets transformed into the structure of product(s). The pathway of this transformation must go through the  transition state structure.','TRUE','With the increase in substrate concentration, the velocity of the enzymatic\nreaction rises at first. The reaction ultimately reaches a maximum velocity\n(Vmax) which is not exceeded by any further rise in concentration of the\nsubstrate.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:155, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Chemical Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-155','11-115',NULL),
(91014,'biology','Enzyme-substrate complex is: \ni. Short-lived   \nii. Dissociates into its product(s) P and the unchanged enzyme with an intermediate formation of the enzyme-product complex (EP).  \niii. Exist for long time as ES complex','i and ii only','Each enzyme (E) has a substrate (S) binding site in its molecule so that a highly reactive enzyme-substrate complex (ES) is produced. This complex is short-lived and dissociates into its product(s) P and the unchanged enzyme with an intermediate formation of the enzyme-product complex (EP)(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:156, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Chemical Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','iii only','i and iii only','i and ii only','ii and iii only','NEW','11-156','11-115',NULL),
(91015,'biology','The activity of an enzyme can be affected by a change in the conditions which can alter','Tertiary structure of the protein','The activity of an enzyme can be affected by a change in the conditions which can alter the tertiary structure of the protein(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:157, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Chemical Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Primary structure of the protein','Secondary structure of the protein','Tertiary structure of the protein','All the Above','NEW','11-157','11-116',NULL),
(91016,'biology','The activity of an enzyme can be affected by','Temperature','The activity of an enzyme can be affected by a change in the conditions which can alter the tertiary structure of the protein. These include temperature, pH, change in substrate concentration or binding of specific chemicals that regulate its activity(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:157, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Chemical Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Temperature','Stability','Concentration of enzyme','All the Above','NEW','11-157','11-116',NULL),
(91017,'biology','The effect of low temperature on enzyme is','Preserves the enzyme in a temporarily inactive state','Enzymes generally function in a narrow range of temperature and pH. Each enzyme shows its highest activity at a particular temperature and pH called the optimum temperature and optimum pH.Activity declines both below and above the optimum value. Low temperature preserves the enzyme in a temporarily inactive state (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:157, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Chemical Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Destroy the enzyme','Preserves the enzyme in a temporarily inactive state','Increase the activity of enzyme','Slow down the reaction','NEW','11-157','11-116',NULL),
(91018,'biology','The effect of high temperature','Destroys enzymatic activity because proteins are denatured by heat.','high temperature destroys enzymatic activity because proteins are denatured by heat(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:157, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Chemical Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Increase the rate of reaction','Decrease the rate of reaction','Destroys enzymatic activity because proteins are denatured by heat.','All the Above','NEW','11-157','11-116',NULL),
(91019,'biology','When the binding of the chemical shuts off enzyme activity, the process is called _______','Inhibition','The activity of an enzyme is also sensitive to the presence of specific chemicals that bind to the enzyme. When the binding of the chemical shuts off enzyme activity, the process is called inhibition and the chemical is called an inhibitor(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:158, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Chemical Reactions',NULL,'2','fillup','In,hi,bi,ti,on',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-158','11-117',NULL),
(91020,'biology','When the inhibitor closely resembles the substrate in its molecular structure and inhibits the activity of the enzyme, it is known as: \ni. Non-competititve Inhibitor  \nii. Competitive inhibitor   \niii.Co-factors','ii only','When the inhibitor closely resembles the substrate in its molecular structure and inhibits the activity of the enzyme, it is known as competitive inhibitor(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:158, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Chemical Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii only','ii only','ii and iii only','i and iii only','NEW','11-158','11-117',NULL),
(91021,'biology','Inhibition of succinic dehydrogenase by _______ which closely resembles the substrate succinate in structure.','Malonate','inhibition of succinic dehydrogenase by malonate which closely resembles the substrate succinate in structure(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:158, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Chemical Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Lipase','Malonate','Oxidoreductases','Isomerases','NEW','11-158','11-117',NULL),
(91022,'biology','Competitive inhibitors are often used in the control of ______','Bacterial pathogens','competitive inhibitors are often used in the control of bacterial pathogens(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:158, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Chemical Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Yeast formation','Pest control','Bacterial pathogens','Tissue culture','NEW','11-158','11-117',NULL),
(91023,'biology','Identify the parts in the diagram (0927)','a-Activation energy without enzyme,b-Activation energy with enzyme','NCERT class 11 Page 157 Figure 9.6','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Chemical Reactions',NULL,'3','match','a,Activation energy without enzyme','b,Activation energy with enzyme',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-157','11-115','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/91023q.png'),
(91024,'biology','Mark the parts in the diagram Effect of change in pH diagram (0928)','a-Enzyme activity,b-pH','NCERT class 11 Page 157 Figure 9.7','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Chemical Reactions',NULL,'3','match','a,Enzyme activity','b,pH',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-157','11-116','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/91024q.png'),
(91025,'biology','Mark the parts in the diagram Effect of change in Temperature diagram (0928)','a-Enzyme activity,b-Temperature','NCERT class 11 Page 157 Figure 9.7','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Chemical Reactions',NULL,'3','match','a,Enzyme activity','b,Temperature',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-157','11-116','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/91025q.png'),
(91026,'biology','Mark the parts in the diagram (0929)','a-Vmax/2,b-Km','NCERT class 11 Page 156 Figure 9.6','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Chemical Reactions',NULL,'3','match','a,Vmax/2','b,Km',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-156','11-115','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/91026q.png'),
(91101,'chemistry','Choose the wrong statement.\n\na. Gaseous water molecule is bent molecule with a bond angle of 104.5 degree\nb. In water molecule,Oxygen is sp³ hybridized\nc. In gas phase water molecule are associated by hydrogen bonds\nd. The crystalline form of water is ice','only c','In gas phase molecules are far apart unable to develop h- bond, O in water making two sigma bonds and two lone pair present on O, so total four hybrid orbitals needed which is possible in sp³.\nStructure of water is bent instead of tetrahedral due to lone lone pair repulsion.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 290; Paragraph no.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Structure of water',NULL,'2','mcq','only a','only b','only c','only d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91102,'chemistry','Covalent Compounds can also be solubilized in water due to','Cubic form','The crystalline form of water is ice. At atmospheric pressure ice crystallises in the hexagonal form, but at very low temperatures it condenses to cubic form. Density of ice is less than that of water. Therefore, an ice cube floats on water. In winter season ice formed on the surface of a lake provides thermal insulation which ensures the survival of the aquatic life. This fact is of great ecological significance.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 290; Paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Structure of water',NULL,'1','mcq','Hexagonal form','Cubic form','Rhombic form','Tetrahedral form','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91103,'chemistry','The density of ice is ________ liquid water due to fixing of hydrogen bonds in ice.','Less than','In ice Hydrogen bond fixed in a specific direction leads to large no.of empty spaces ,volume increase then according to d=m/v ,density will decrease.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 290; Paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Structure of water',NULL,'2','fillup','Le,ss, th,an',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91104,'chemistry','Ice on the surface of lake provide ___________ which ensures the survival of aquatic life.','Thermal insulation','Density of ice is less than that of water.Therefore, an ice cube floats on water. In winter season ice formed on the surface of a lake provides thermal insulation which ensures the survival of the aquatic life. This fact is of great ecological significance.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 290; Paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Structure of water',NULL,'1','fillup','Th,erm,al ins,ulat,ion',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91105,'biology','Function of enzyme Hydrolases is: \ni. Catalysing hydrolysis of ester, ether, peptide,glycosidic  \nii. Catalysing a transfer of a group  \niii. Catalysing hydrolysis of C-C, C-halide or P-N bonds','i and iii only','Hydrolases Enzymes catalysing hydrolysis of ester, ether, peptide, glycosidic, C-C, C-halide or P-N bonds(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:158, paragraph no:4)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Classification of Enzymes and  Co-factors',NULL,'3','mcq','i only','ii and iii only','i and iii only','iii only','NEW','11-158','11-117',NULL),
(91106,'biology','Enzymes that catalyse removal of groups from substrates by mechanisms other than hydrolysis leaving double bonds are','Lyases','Lyases Enzymes that catalyse removal of groups from substrates by mechanisms other than hydrolysis leaving double bonds(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:158, paragraph no:4)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Classification of Enzymes and  Co-factors',NULL,'3','mcq','Dehydrogenases','Hydrolases','Transferases','Lyases','NEW','11-158','11-117',NULL),
(91107,'biology','Isomerases includes','All enzymes catalysing inter conversion of optical, geometric or positional isomers','Isomerases Includes all enzymes catalysing inter-conversion of optical, geometric or positional isomers(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:159, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Classification of Enzymes and  Co-factors',NULL,'1','mcq','Enzymes catalysing a transfer of a group','Catalysing hydrolysis of C-C, C-halide or P-N bonds','Enzyme catalyses the rate of reaction involving production of Hydroxylated products','All enzymes catalysing inter conversion of optical, geometric or positional isomers','NEW','11-159','11-117',NULL),
(91108,'biology','Enzymes catalysing the linking together of 2 compounds are','Ligases','Ligases Enzymes catalysing the linking together of 2 compounds(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:159, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Classification of Enzymes and  Co-factors',NULL,'1','mcq','Hydrolases','Transferases','Lyases','Ligases','NEW','11-159','11-117',NULL),
(91109,'biology','Function of enzyme Ligases is','Catalyses joining of C-O, C-S, C-N, P-O bonds','Enzymes catalysing the linking together of 2 compounds, e.g.,\nenzymes which catalyse joining of C-O, C-S, C-N, P-O etc. bonds. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:159, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Classification of Enzymes and  Co-factors',NULL,'2','mcq','Catalyses joining of C-O, C-S, C-N, P-O bonds','Catalyses a transfer of a group','Catalyses hydrolysis of C-C, C-halide or P-N bonds','All of them','NEW','11-159','11-117',NULL),
(91110,'biology','Enzymes are composed of one or several polypeptide chains','TRUE','Enzymes are composed of one or several polypeptide chains. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:159, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Classification of Enzymes and  Co-factors',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-159','11-117',NULL),
(91111,'biology','Co-factors are','Non-protein constituents','there are a number of cases in which non-protein constituents called cofactors are bound to the the enzyme to make the enzyme catalytically active(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:159, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Classification of Enzymes and  Co-factors',NULL,'2','mcq','Non-protein constituents','Polypeptide chains','Protein constituents','Made of lipids','NEW','11-159','11-117',NULL),
(91112,'biology','Function of co-factor is','Bound to the the enzyme to make the enzyme catalytically active','Enzymes are composed of one or several polypeptide chains. However, there are a number of cases in which non-protein constituents called cofactors are bound to the the enzyme to make the enzyme catalytically active(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:159, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Classification of Enzymes and  Co-factors',NULL,'2','mcq','Bound to the the enzyme to make the enzyme catalytically non-active','Increase concentration of enzymes','Bound to the the enzyme to make the enzyme catalytically active','Slow down reaction','NEW','11-159','11-117',NULL),
(91113,'biology','The protein portion of the enzymes is called','apoenzyme','the protein portion of the enzymes is called the apoenzyme(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:159, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Classification of Enzymes and  Co-factors',NULL,'1','fillup','ap,oe,nz,yme',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-159','11-118',NULL),
(91114,'biology','Kinds of cofactors are: i. Prosthetic groups  ii. Co-enzymes iii. Non-Metal ions','i and ii only','Enzyms are composed of one or several polypeptide chains  However, non protein constituents called cofactor may assist in catalyticactivityof Enzyme.  Three kinds of cofactor may be identified prosthetic groups, coenzyme and metal ions.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:159, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Classification of Enzymes and  Co-factors',NULL,'1','mcq','i, ii and iii','i and ii only','ii and iii only','i and iii only','NEW','11-159','11-118',NULL),
(91115,'biology','_________are organic compounds and are distinguished from other cofactors in that they are tightly bound to the apoenzyme','Prosthetic groups','(apoenzyme. Three kinds of cofactors may be identified: prosthetic groups,\nco-enzymes and metal ions. Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:159, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Classification of Enzymes and  Co-factors',NULL,'2','mcq','Prosthetic groups','Co-enzymes','Metal ions','Irreversible Inhibitor','NEW','11-159','11-118',NULL),
(91116,'biology','In peroxidase and catalase, which catalyze the breakdown of hydrogen peroxide to water and oxygen, haem is the prosthetic group and it is a part of the active site of the enzyme','TRUE','Prosthetic groups are organic compounds and are distinguished from other cofactors in that they are tightly bound to the apoenzyme. Forexample, in peroxidase and catalase, which catalyze the breakdown of hydrogen peroxide to water and oxygen, haem is the prosthetic group and it is a part of the active site of the enzyme','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Classification of Enzymes and  Co-factors',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-159','11-118',NULL),
(91117,'biology','_________are organic compounds but their association with the apoenzyme is only transient, usually occurring during the course of catalysis','Co-enzymes','Co-enzymes are also organic compounds but their association with the apoenzyme is only transient, usually occurring during the course of catalysis. Furthermore, co enzymes serve as co-factors in a number of different enzyme catalyzed reactions(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:159, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Classification of Enzymes and  Co-factors',NULL,'1','mcq','Prosthetic groups','Co-enzymes','Metal ions','Non metal ions','NEW','11-159','11-118',NULL),
(91118,'biology','The essential chemical components of many coenzymes are','Vitamins','The essential chemical components of many coenzymes are vitamins(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:159, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Classification of Enzymes and  Co-factors',NULL,'2','mcq','Proteins','Fats','Carbohydrates','Vitamins','NEW','11-159','11-118',NULL),
(91119,'biology','Coenzyme nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide (NAD) and NADP contain','Vitamin niacin','coenzyme nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide (NAD) and NADP contain the vitamin niacin(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:159, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Classification of Enzymes and  Co-factors',NULL,'1','mcq','Vitamin niacin','Niacin regulator','All vitamins','Proteins','NEW','11-159','11-118',NULL),
(91120,'biology','_______ is a cofactor for the proteolytic enzyme carboxypeptidase','Zinc','A number of enzymes require metal ions for their activity which form coordination bonds with side chains at the active site and at the same time form one or more cordination bonds with the substrate, e.g., zinc is a cofactor for the proteolytic enzyme carboxypeptidase(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:159, paragraph no:4)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Classification of Enzymes and  Co-factors',NULL,'2','mcq','Zinc','Carbon','Iron','Sulphur','NEW','11-159','11-118',NULL),
(91121,'biology','Pick up the correct statement. \ni. Catalytic activity is lost when the co-factor is removed from the enzyme  \nii. Co-factor play a crucial role in the catalytic activity of the enzyme   \niii. Catalytic activity is gained in the absence of co-factors','i and ii only','Catalytic activity is lost when the co-factor is removed from the enzyme which testifies that they play a crucial role in the catalytic activity of the enzyme(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:159, paragraph no:5)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Classification of Enzymes and  Co-factors',NULL,'2','mcq','i only','i and ii only','i and iii only','ii and iii only','NEW','11-159','11-118',NULL),
(91122,'biology','Match the following nature of co-factors accordingly','Prosthetic groups-organic compounds,Co-enzymes-organic compounds,metal ions-inorganic compounds','apoenzyme. Three kinds of cofactors may be identified: prosthetic groups,\nco-enzymes and metal ions. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:159, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Classification of Enzymes and  Co-factors',NULL,'2','match','Prosthetic groups,organic compounds','Co-enzymes,organic compounds','Metal ions,inorganic compounds',NULL,'NEW','11-159','11-118','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/91122q.png'),
(91201,'chemistry','Ice has highly ordered 3-dimensional ___________ structure.','Hydrogen bonded','Ice has a highly ordered three dimensional hydrogen bonded structure as shown in\nFig.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 290; Paragraph no.4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Structure of Ice',NULL,'1','fillup','Hyd,ro,ge,n bo,nd,ed',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91202,'chemistry','X-rays analysis of ice showed that each oxygen atom is surrounded tetrahedrally by four oxygen at a distance of','276pm','X-rays shows that each oxygen atom is surrounded tetrahedrally by four other oxygen atoms at a distance of 276 pm.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 291; Paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Structure of Ice',NULL,'1','mcq','276pm','275pm','275.2pm','270pm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91203,'chemistry','Hydrogen bonding give ice an open type structure with ______________.','wide holes','Hydrogen bonding gives ice a rather open type structure with wide holes. These holes can hold some other molecules of appropriate size interstitially.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 291; Paragraph no.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Structure of Ice',NULL,'1','fillup','wi,de ho,les',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(91204,'biology','Which of the following is the least likely to be involved in stabilising the threedimensional folding of most proteins? [NEET 2016]','Ester bonds','Tertiary structure or three dimensional structure of protein is stabilised by several types of bonds—hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, van der Waal’s interactions, covalent bonds and hydrophobic bonds.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:150, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Hydrogen bonds','Electrostatic interaction','Hydrophobic interaction','Ester bonds','NEW','11-150','11-112',NULL),
(91205,'biology','A typical fat molecule is made up of [NEET 2016]','One glycerol and three fatty acid molecules','Neutral or true fats are triglycerides which are formed by esterification of three molecules of fatty acids with one molecule of trihydric alcohol, glycerol (glycerine or trihydroxy propane). (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:144, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','One glycerol and one fatty acid molecule','Three glycerol and three fatty acid molecules','Three glycerol molecules and one fatty acid molecule','One glycerol and three fatty acid molecules','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91206,'biology','Which one of the following statements is wrong? [NEET 2016]','Glycine is a sulphur containing amino acid.','Glycine is a neutral amino acid. Cysteine and methionine are sulphur containing amino acid. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:144, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Uracil is a pyrimidine.','Glycine is a sulphur containing amino acid.','Sucrose is a disaccharide.','Cellulose is a polysaccharide','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91207,'biology','The chitinous exoskeleton of arthropods is formed by the polymerisation of [2015]','N acetyl glucosamine','Chitin is a structural polysaccharide that constitutes the exoskeleton of arthropods. It is a complex carbohydrate. They have as building blocks, amino-sugars and chemically\nmodified sugars (e.g., glucosamine, N-acetyl galactosamine(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:149, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','N acetyl glucosamine','Lipoglycans','Keratin sulphate and chondroitin sulphate','D glucosamine','NEW','11-149','11-111',NULL),
(91208,'biology','Which of the following biomolecules does have a phosphodiester bond? [2015]','Nucleic acids in a nucleotide','A nucleotide has three chemically distinct components. One is a heterocyclic compound\nthe second is a monosaccharide and the third a phosphoric acid or phosphate.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:149 paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Amino acids in a polypeptide','Nucleic acids in a nucleotide','Fatty acids in a diglyceride','Monosaccharides in a polysaccharide','NEW','11-149','11-111',NULL),
(91209,'biology','Which one of the following statements is incorrect? [2015 cancelled]','The presence of the competitive inhibitor decreases the k m of the enzyme for the substrate.','Competitive inhibition is a reversible inhibition where inhibitor competes with the normal substrate for the active site of enzyme. A competitive inhibitor is usually chemically similar to the normal substrate and therefore, fits into the active site of an enzyme and binds with it. The inhibition is thus due to substrate analogue. The enzyme, now cannot act upon the substrate and reaction products are not formed. E.g., the activity of succinate dehydrogenase is inhibited by malonate. K m value or Michaelis constant is defined as the substrate concentration at which half of the enzyme molecules are forming enzyme substrate (ES) complex, or concentration of the substrate when the velocity of the enzyme reaction is half the maximal possible. A smaller K m value indicates greater affinity of the enzyme for its substrate, hence, shows a quicker reaction. The competitive inhibitor decreases the affinity of enzyme for substrate, thus increases the K m value. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:158, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','The competitive inhibitor does not affect the rate of breakdown of the enzymesubstrate complex.','The presence of the competitive inhibitor decreases the k m of the enzyme for the substrate.','A competitive inhibitor reacts reversibly with the enzyme to form an enzymeinhibitor complex.','In competitive inhibition, the inhibitor molecule is not chemically changed by the enzyme','NEW','11-158','11-116',NULL),
(91210,'biology','Select the option which is not correct with respect to enzyme action. [2014]','Addition of lot of succinate does not reverse the inhibition of succinic dehydrogenase by malonate.','The reduction of activity of succinate dehydrogenase by malonate is an example of competitive inhibition. Competitive inhibition is a reversible inhibition where inhibitor competes with the normal substrate for the active site of enzyme. A competitive inhibitor is usually similar to the normal substrate and, therefore, fits into the active site of an enzyme and binds with it. The enzyme, now cannot act upon the substrate and reaction products are not formed. Hence, action of an enzyme may be reduced or inhibited. Since a competitive inhibitor occupies the site only temporarily, the enzyme action is not permanently affected. Thus, addition of a lot of succinate can reverse the inhibition of succinic dehydrogenase by malonate.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Substrate binds with enzyme at its active site.','Addition of lot of succinate does not reverse the inhibition of succinic dehydrogenase by malonate.','A noncompetitive inhibitor binds the enzyme at a site distinct from that which binds the substrate.','Malonate is a competitive inhibitor of succinic dehydrogenase','NEW','11-158','11-117',NULL),
(91211,'biology','Which one of the following is a non-reducing carbohydrate? [2014]','Sucrose','In nonreducing sugars, a free aldehyde or ketonic group is absent. Sucrose is a nonreducing sugar formed by condensation of one molecule each of glucose and fructose with release of a water molecule. A glycosidic bond is established between carbon atom (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:148, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Maltose','Sucrose','Lactose','Ribose 5phosphate','NEW','11-148','11-110',NULL),
(91212,'biology','A phosphoglyceride is always made up of [NEET 2013]','A saturated or unsaturated fatty acid esterified to a glycerol molecule to which a phosphate group is also attached.','Phosphoglycerides are the triesters of fatty acids (either saturated or unsaturated) and glycerol to which a phosphate group is also attached. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:144, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','A saturated or unsaturated fatty acid esterified to a glycerol molecule to which a phosphate group is also attached.','A saturated or unsaturated fatty acid esterified to a phosphate group which is also attached to a glycerol molecule.','Only a saturated fatty acid esterified to a glycerol molecule to which a phosphate group is also attached.','Only an unsaturated fatty acid esterified to a glycerol molecule to which a phosphate group is also attached','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91213,'biology','Macromolecule chitin is [NEET 2013]','Nitrogen containing polysaccharide','Exoskeletons of arthropods, for example, have a complex\npolysaccharide called chitin. These complex polysaccharides are mostly\nhomopolymers.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:149, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Sulphur containing polysaccharide','Simple polysaccharide','Nitrogen containing polysaccharide','Phosphorous containing polysaccharide','NEW','11-149','11-111',NULL),
(91214,'biology','Transition state structure of the substrate formed during an enzymatic reaction is [NEET 2013]','Transient and unstable','Transition state is formation of unstable intermediate structural state. During this, substrate bonds are broken and new bonds are established that transform the substrate molecules into products. This state is transient and highly unstable. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:156, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Transient and unstable','Permanent and stable','Transient but stable','Permanent but unstable','NEW','11-156','11-114',NULL),
(91215,'biology','The essential chemical components of many coenzymes are [NEET 2013]','Vitamins','Coenzyme is the non protein organic group which gets attached to the apoenzyme to form holoenzyme or conjugate enzyme. It helps in removing a product of chemical reaction besides bringing contact between the substrate and the enzyme. Most of the coenzymes are made of water soluble vitamins B and C, e.g., thiamine, riboflavin, nicotinamide, pyridoxine(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:159, paragraph no:3).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Carbohydrates','Vitamins','Proteins','Nucleic acids','NEW','11-159','11-118',NULL),
(91216,'biology','Which of the following statements about enzymes is wrong? [Karnataka NEET 2013]','Enzymes are mostly proteins but some are lipids also.','Enzymes are mostly proteins but some are RNA (ribozymes). No lipid working as enzymes are known.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:154, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Enzymes are denatured at high temperatures.','Enzymes are mostly proteins but some are lipids also.','Enzymes are highly specific.','Enzymes require optimum ph and temperature for maximum activity','NEW','11-154','11-112',NULL),
(91217,'biology','Uridine, present only in RNA is a [Karnataka NEET 2013]','Nucleoside','A nucleoside is pentose sugar and base together, without the phosphate group. Uracil is present as uridine in RNA only. Uracil + Ribose ® Uridine (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:144, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Nucleoside','Nucleotide','Purine','Pyrimidine','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91218,'biology','Which one is the most abundant protein in the animal world? [2012]','Collagen','Collagen is an insoluble fibrous protein found extensively in the connective tissue of skin, tendons and bone. Collagen accounts for over (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:148, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Trypsin','Haemoglobin','Collagen','Insulin','NEW','11-148','11-110',NULL),
(91219,'biology','Which one of the following is wrong statement? [2012]','Phosphorus is a constituent of cell membranes, certain nucleic acids and all proteins.','Phosphorus is present in plasma membrane in the form of phospholipid bilayer. It is an essential component of all nucleic acids not ‘certain’ nucleic acids. Moreover, phosphorus is never found in proteins. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:144, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Anabaena and nostoc are capable of fixing nitrogen in freeliving state also.','Root nodule forming nitrogen fixers live as aerobes under freeliving conditions.','Phosphorus is a constituent of cell membranes, certain nucleic acids and all proteins.','Nitrosomonas and nitrobacter are chemoautotrophs','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91220,'biology','Which one of the following biomolecules is correctly characterized? [Mains 2012]','Lecithin a phosphorylated glyceride found in cell membrane.','Lecithin is a triglyceride lipid where one fatty acid is replaced by phosphoric acid which is linked to additional nitrogenous group called choline. It is a common membrane lipid. It is an amphipathic phospholipid having both hydrophilic polar and hydrophobic nonpolar groups. The hydrocarbon chains of two fatty acids function as hydrophobic nonpolar tails whereas the phosphate and choline behave as hydrophilic polar head group of the molecule. Palmitic acid is a saturated fatty acid (as it does not possess double bonds in its carbon chain) and contains (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:145, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Lecithin a phosphorylated glyceride found in cell membrane.','Palmitic acid an unsaturated fatty acid with18 carbon atoms.','Adenylic acid adenosine with a glucose phosphate molecule.','Alanine amino acid contains an amino group and an acidic group anywhere in the molecule','NEW','11-145','11-107',NULL),
(91221,'biology','Three of the following statements about enzymes are correct and one is wrong. which one is wrong? [Mains 2010]','Most enzymes are proteins but some are lipids','Almost all enzymes are proteins. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:154, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Enzymes require optimum ph for maximal activity.','Enzymes are denatured at high temperature but in certain exceptional organisms they are effective even at temperatures 80° 90° c.','Enzymes are highly specific.','Most enzymes are proteins but some are lipids','NEW','11-154','11-112',NULL),
(311709,'biology','Assertion : Somatic embryos can be induced from any cell in plant tissue culture. \n\nReason : Any living plant cell is capable of differentiating into somatic embryos. [2017]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Somatic embryos are non- zygotic embryo\nlike structures that develop into from any\ntype of tissue in plant tissue culture.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','If the Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','If both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91222,'biology','Carbohydrates are commonly found as starch in plants storage organs. Which of the following five properties of starch make it useful as a storage material?\n(1) easily translocated\n(2) chemically nonreactive\n(3) easily digested by animals\n(4) osmotically inactive\n(5) synthesized during photosynthesis.','(2) and (4)','Starch is the major storage carbohydrate of plants. In most plant species it is accumulated in the chloroplast of leaves, whereas in storage organ it accumulates in amyloplast as reserve starch. It is the osmotically inactive form of photosynthetic product and is a hexosan polysaccharide made of large number of glucose unit so, chemically non reactive. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:148, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','(1), (3) and (5)','(1) and (5)','(2) and (3)','(2) and (4)','NEW','11-148','11-110',NULL),
(91223,'biology','Cellulose is the major component of cell walls of [2008]','Pythium','The cell wall of most fungi consist of chitin or cellulose. In Pythium, the hyphal wall contains cellulose whereas, in yeast the cell wall is thin and is composed of chitin in combination with other compounds (carbohydrates, glucan and mannan). The bacterial cell wall contains Nacetyl glucosamine and Nacetyl muramic acid.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Pseudomonas','Saccharomyces','Pythium','Xanthomonas','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91224,'biology','Modern detergents contain enzyme preparations of [2008]','Alkaliphiles','Modern detergents contain enzyme preparation of alkaline protease which are called alkaliphiles, for removing protein stain.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Thermoacidophiles','Thermophiles','Acidophiles','Alkaliphiles','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91225,'biology','About 98 percent of the mass of every living organism is composed of just six elements including carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen, O₂ and [2007]','Phosphorus and sulphur','Living organisms requires','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Sulphur and magnesium','Magnesium and sodium','Calcium and phosphorus','Phosphorus and sulphur','NEW','11-143','11-105',NULL),
(91226,'biology','An organic substance bound to an enzyme and essential for its activity is called [2006]','Coenzyme','Enzymes are simple if they are made of only proteins (e.g., pepsin, amylase etc.) while conjugate enzymes have an additional nonprotein cofactor which may be organic or inorganic. Loosely attached organic cofactor is coenzyme. It plays an accessory role in enzyme catalyzed processes often by acting as a donor or acceptor of a substance involved in the reaction. ATP and NAD are common coenzymes.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:159, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Isoenzyme','Coenzyme','Holoenzyme','Apoenzyme','NEW','11-159','11-118',NULL),
(91227,'biology','The catalytic efficiency of two different enzymes can be compared by the [2005]','Km value','m value or Michaelis constant is defined as the substrate concentration at which half of the enzyme molecules are forming (ES) complex or concentration of the substrate when the velocity of the enzyme reaction is half the maximal possible. The K m varies from enzyme to enzyme and is used in characterizing the different enzymes. A smaller K m value indicates greater affinity of the enzyme for its substrate, hence, shows a quicker reaction. K m value is a constant characteristic of an enzyme for its conversion of a substrate.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Formation of the product','Ph of optimum value','Km value','Molecular size of the enzyme','NEW','11-157','11-116',NULL),
(91228,'biology','Which one of the following statements regarding enzyme inhibition is correct? [2005]','Competitive inhibition is seen when the substrate and the inhibitor compete for the active site on the enzyme.','When the inhibitor closely resembles the substrate in its molecular structure and inhibits the activity of the enzyme, it is known as competitive inhibitor. Due to its close structural similarity with thesubstrate, the inhibitor competes with the substrate for the substratebinding site of the enzyme(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:158, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Competitive inhibition is seen when a substrate competes with an enzyme for binding to an inhibitor protein.','Competitive inhibition is seen when the substrate and the inhibitor compete for the active site on the enzyme.','Noncompetitive inhibition of an enzyme can be overcome by adding large amount of substrate.','Noncompetitive inhibitors often bind to the enzyme irreversibly','NEW','11-158','11-117',NULL),
(91229,'biology','Enzymes, vitamins and hormones can be classified into a single category of biological chemicals, because all of these [2005]','Help in regulating metabolism','Enzymes control all the life processes. They increase the rate of a biological reaction. The magnitude of increase may be greater than those affected by other catalysts. Vitamins are accessory indispensable food factor, organic in nature (organic acid, amino acid esters, alcohols, steroids etc.) required by an organism in small amounts to maintain normal growth and regulate the metabolic processes. Hormones are biologically active organic substance that are produced in minute quantities by some specialized organs and exert physiological effects at sites remote from their origin. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:154, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Help in regulating metabolism','Are exclusively synthesized in the body of a living organism as at present','Are conjugated proteins','Enhance oxidative metabolism','NEW','11-154','11-118',NULL),
(91230,'biology','Which of the following is the simplest amino acid? [2005]','Glycine','Glycine is considered as the simplest amino acid as it has one amino group, one carboxylic group and no substituent functional group. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:144, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Alanine','Asparagine','Glycine','Tyrosine','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91231,'biology','Match List-I with List-II. [NEET 2021]\n\n(a) Protein                           (i) C = C double bonds\n\n(b) Unsaturated fatty acid  (ii) Phosphodiester bonds  \n\n(c) Nucleic acid                 (iii) Glycosidic bonds\n\n(d) Polsyaccharide            (iv) Peptide bonds\n\n Choose the correct answer from the options given below \n    \n     (a) (b) (c) (d)\n(1) (iv) (iii) (i) (ii)\n(2) (iv) (i) (ii) (iii)\n(3) (i) (iv) (iii) (ii)\n(4) (ii) (i) (iv) (iii)','2',NULL,'Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(91232,'biology','Following are the statements with Reference   to \'lipids\'. \n\n(a) Lipids having only single bonds are called unsaturated fatty acids \n\n(b) Lecithin is a phospholipid. \n\n(c) Trihydroxy propane is glycerol. \n\n(d) Palmitic acid has 20 carbon atoms including carboxyl carbon. \n\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below[NEET 2021]','(b) and (c) only','Fatty acids could be saturated (without double bond) or unsaturated (with one or more C=C doublebonds). Reference  : NCERT 11th Class Page No144','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','(b) and (e) only','(a) and (b) only','(c) and (d) only','(b) and (c) only','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91301,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\n(A): water act as an amphoteric substance\n(R): Act as acid with base and with acid act as base','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','With ammonia it acts as acid and with HCl it acts as base.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 291; Paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91302,'chemistry','The autoprotolysis of water gives','H₃O⁺(aq) + HO⁻(aq)','The auto-protolysis (self-ionization) of water takes place as follows :\nH₂O(aq) + H₂O(aq) → H₃O⁺(aq) + HO⁻(aq)\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 291; Paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','H₃O⁺(aq) + HO⁻(aq)','H₂O⁺(aq) + HO⁻(aq)','H₂O⁺(aq) + H₂O⁻(aq)','none','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91303,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement.\n\na. Highly electropositive metals reduce water to hydrogen gas.\n\nb. Water is oxidized to O₂ during photosynthesis.\n\nc. F₂ oxidizes water to  O₂ .','All are correct','Redox Reactions Involving Water: \nWater can be easily reduced to dihydrogen by highly electropositive metals.\n2H₂O + (l) 2Na (s)  → 2NaOH (aq) + H₂ (g) \nThus, it is a great source of dihydrogen.\n\nWater is oxidised to O2 during photosynthesis.\n6CO₂(g) + 12H₂O(l) → C₆H₁₂O₆ (aq) + 6H₂O(l)+ 6O₂(g)\n\nWith fluorine also it is oxidised to O2\n.2F₂(g) + 2H₂O(l) → 4H⁺(aq) + 4F⁻(aq) + O₂ (g)\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 291; Paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','only a','only b','only c','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91304,'chemistry','Which reaction is not hydrolysis reaction ?\n\na. NaH(s) + H₂O(aq) →  NaOH(aq) + H₂(g)\n\nb.P₄O₁₀(s) + 6H₂O(l) → 4H₃PO₄(aq)\n\nc. N³⁻(s) + H₂O(l) → NH₃(g) +3OH⁻(aq)\n\nd. BaCl₂(s) + 2H₂O(l) → BaCl₂.2H₂O(aq)','only d','Hydrolysis Reaction: Due to high dielectric constant, it has a very strong hydrating tendency. It dissolves many ionic compounds. However, certain covalent and some ionic compounds are hydrolysed in water.\n\nP₄O₁₀(s) + 6H₂O(l) → 4H₃PO₄(aq)\n\nSiCl₄ (l) +2H₂O(l) → SiO₂ (s) + 4HCl (aq) \n\nN³⁻(s) + H₂O(l) → NH₃(g) +3OH⁻(aq)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','only a','only b','only c','only d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91305,'chemistry','Hydrate formation occurs in way namely','All of these','(4) Hydrates Formation: From aqueous solutions many salts can be crystallised as hydrated salts. Such an association of water is of different types viz.,\n\n(i) coordinated water e.g., [Cr(H₂O)₆]³⁺.3Cl⁻\n\n(ii) interstitial water e.g., BaCl .2H₂O₂\n\n(iii) hydrogen-bonded water e.g.[Cu(H₂O)₄]²⁺SO₄²⁻.H₂O in CuSO₄.5H₂O\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 291; Paragraph no.4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Co-ordinated water','Interstitial water','Hydrogen bonded water','All of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91306,'chemistry','Match the following. diagram(n0910)','(i) - (c) ; (ii) - (d) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (a)','Hydrates Formation: From aqueous solutions many salts can be crystallised as hydrated salts. Such an association of water is of different types viz.,\n(i) Coordinated water - [Cr(H₂O)₆]Cl₃\n(ii) Interstitial water e.g - BaCl₂.2H₂O\n(iii) Hydrogen-bonded water e.g - [Cu(H₂O)₄]SO₄.H₂O\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 291; Paragraph no.4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) - (d) ; (ii) - (a) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (c)','(i) - (a) ; (ii) - (b) ; (iii) - (c) ; (iv) - (d)','(i) - (d) ; (ii) - (c) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (a)','(i) - (c) ; (ii) - (d) ; (iii) - (b) ; (iv) - (a)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/91306q.png'),
(91307,'chemistry','The number of hydrogen bonded water molecules in   [Cu(H₂O)₄]SO₄.H₂O is','one','One water molecule which is outside the coordination sphere is H- bonded while other four are Coordinated.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 291; Problem no.9.4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','one','two','three','four','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91308,'biology','Role of enzyme in reactions is to/as [2000]','Decrease activation energy','All molecules require certain amount of energy for activation (to overcome energy barrier) before they can react. This energy is called activation energy. This energy is recovered when products are formed. The essence of an enzyme is its ability to speed up (catalyze) a reaction by making or breaking specific covalent bonds (bonds in which atoms are held together by sharing of electrons). Enzymes act by somehow lowering the temperature at which a given bond is unstable i.e., they speed up a reaction by lowering the activation energy. It is the magnitude of the activation energy which determines how fast the reaction will proceed. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:156, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Decrease activation energy','Increase activation energy','Inorganic catalyst','None of the above','NEW','11-156','11-115',NULL),
(91309,'biology','Which factor is responsible for inhibition of enzymatic process during feedback? [2000]','End product','Feedback inhibition or end product inhibition is the inhibition of the activity of an enzyme catalysing some early reactions of the series by the end product of the metabolic pathway. For example a substrate A is converted into a product F through B, C, D and E intermediate products.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Substrate','Enzymes','End product','Temperature','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91310,'biology','Enzymes are not found in [2000]','Virus','Viruses do not have enzymes so they cannot synthesize proteins. They multiply only inside the living host cell and for multiplication and metabolism they take over the machinery of the host cell. They lack their own cellular machinery and enzymes.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Fungi','Algae','Virus','Cyanobacteria','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91311,'biology','ATP is [2000]','Nucleotide','ATP is a nucleotide as it is composed of adenine, ribose sugar and phosphoric acid. There are two additional phosphate groups attached to the phosphate group of AMP. The last two phosphate molecules are connected by high energy bonds.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:149, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Nucleotide','Nucleoside','Nucleic acid','Vitamin','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91312,'biology','Which of the following have carbohydrate as prosthetic group? [2000]','Glycoprotein','Glycoproteins are proteins that contain sugars like carbohydrates as prosthetic group. In most glycoproteins, the linkage is between asparagine and NacetylDglucosamine. Some glycoproteins are immunoglobulins, membrane proteins and muscle proteins. Lipoproteins are protein complexed with lipids like triglycerides, phospholipids etc. Nucleoproteins are proteins associated with nucleic acids and chromoproteins are proteins associated with pigments e.g., cytochrome, phytochrome.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Glycoprotein','Chromoprotein','Lipoprotein','Nucleoprotein','NEW','11-159','11-119',NULL),
(91313,'biology','Cellulose, the most important constituent of plant cell wall is made up of [1998]','Unbranched chain of glucose molecules linked by b1, 4 glycosidic bond',NULL,'Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Branched chain of glucose molecules linked by b1, 4 glycosidic bond in straight chain and a1, 6 glycosidic bond at the site of branching','Unbranched chain of glucose molecules linked by b1, 4 glycosidic bond','Branched chain of glucose molecules linked by a1, 6 glycosidic bond at the site of branching','Unbranched chain of glucose molecules linked by a1, 4 glycosidic bond','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(91314,'biology','Lactose is composed of [1998]','Glucose + galactose','Lactose is popularly known as milk sugar. It is a disaccharide composed of one molecule of glucose and one molecule of galactose. The covalent bond that joins these two monosaccharide units is called glycosidic bond or glycosidic linkage. It is a reducing sugar.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Glucose + galactose','Fructose + galactose','Glucose + fructose','Glucose + glucose','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91315,'biology','Cofactor (prosthetic group) is a part of holoenzyme. it is [1997]','Accessory nonprotein substance attached firmly','Prosthetic groups are organic compounds and are distinguished from\nother cofactors in that they are tightly bound to the apoenzyme. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:159, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Loosely attached organic part','Loosely attached inorganic part','Accessory nonprotein substance attached firmly','None of these','NEW','11-159','11-119',NULL),
(91316,'biology','Which is a typical example of ‘feedback inhibition’? [1996]','Allosteric inhibition of hexokinase by glucose 6phosphate','Feedback inhibition or end product inhibition occurs when the end product of a metabolic pathway inhibits the activity of an enzyme catalysing some early reactions of the series. The end product is the inhibitor and the enzyme inactivated is called allosteric enzyme. The enzyme is regulated by modulators that bind noncovalently at site other than the active site. An example of feedback inhibition is the inhibition of the activity of the enzyme hexokinase by glucose','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Cyanide and cytochrome reaction','Sulpha drugs and folic acid synthesizer bacteria','Allosteric inhibition of hexokinase by glucose 6phosphate','Reaction between succinic dehydrogenase and succinic acid','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91317,'biology','In which of the following groups are all polysaccharides? [1996]','Glycogen, cellulose and starch','Cellulose is a\npolymeric polysaccharide consisting of only one type of monosaccharide\ni.e., glucose. Cellulose is a homopolymer. Starch is a variant of this but\npresent as a store house of energy in plant tissues. Animals have another\nvariant called glycogen. Inulin is a polymer of fructose. In a\npolysaccharide chain (say glycogen), the right end is called the reducing\nend and the left end is called the non-reducing end.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:148, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Sucrose, glucose and fructose','Maltose, lactose and fructose','Glycogen, sucrose and maltose','Glycogen, cellulose and starch','NEW','11-148','11-110',NULL),
(91318,'biology','What are the most diverse molecules in the cell? [1996]','Proteins','Proteins show enormous diversity because of different proportions and sequences of twenty amino acid within the protein molecule. A large number of permutations and combinations of these amino acids are responsible for the unlimited variety of proteins. Proteins are the most abundant and most varied of the macromolecules having one or more polypeptides (chains of amino acids). The proteins constitute almost (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:149, paragraph no:4)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Lipids','Mineral salts','Proteins','Carbohydrates','NEW','11-149','11-111',NULL),
(91319,'biology','Which purine base is found in RNA? [1996]','Guanine','The bases are of two typespurines and pyrimidines. The purine derivatives adenine (A) and guanine (G) are double ring structures whereas pyrimidine derivatives thymine, cytosine and uracil are single ring structures. Thymine (T) and cytosine (C) are found in DNA and cytosine (C) and uracil (U) is found in RNA. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:149, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Thymine','Uracil','Cytosine','Guanine','NEW','11-149','11-111',NULL),
(91320,'biology','Which of the following nucleotide sequences contains 4 pyrimidine bases? [1994]','GATCAATGC','In the given question there are','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','GATCAATGC','GCUAGACAA','UAGCGGUAA','Both b and c','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91321,'biology','The four elements that make up 99% of all elements found in a living system are [1994]','H, o, c and n','Carbon, hydrogen, O₂ and nitrogen are called four big elements of living body they make up about (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:143, tabel 9.1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','C, h, o and p','C, n, o and p','H, o, c and n','C, h, o and s','NEW','11-143','11-105',NULL),
(91322,'biology','Which is wrong about nucleic acids? [1993]','Length of one helix is 45 å in B-dna.','Together with polysaccharides and polypeptides these\ncomprise the true macromolecular fraction of any living tissue or cell.\nFor nucleic acids, the building block is a nucleotide. A nucleotide has\nthree chemically distinct components. One is a heterocyclic compound,\nthe second is a monosaccharide and the third a phosphoric acid or\nphosphate. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:149, paragraph no:2-3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','DNA is single stranded in some viruses.','RNA is double stranded occasionally.','Length of one helix is 45 å in B-dna.','One turn of zdna has 12 bases','NEW','11-149','11-111',NULL),
(91323,'biology','Glycogen is a polymer of [1993]','Glucose','Glycogen (animal starch) is a polysaccharide consisting of a highly branched polymer of glucose occurring in animal tissues, especially in liver and muscle cells. It is the major store of carbohydrate energy in animal cells. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:148, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Galactose','Glucose','Fructose','Sucrose','NEW','11-148','11-110',NULL),
(91324,'biology','In RNA, thymine is replaced by [1992]','Uracil','All nitrogenous basis are same in DNA and RNA except Uracil in place of Thymine in RNA','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Adenine','Guanine','Cytosine','Uracil','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91325,'biology','Amino acids are mostly synthesised from [1992]','Alpha ketoglutaric acid','Amino acids are mostly synthesized from aketoglutaric acid. These are the precursors of amino acids. A five carbon compound formed during Krebs’ cycle is a aketoglutaric acid which is the first dicarboxylic acid formed. Pyruvic acid converted into alanine, aketoglutaric acid into glutamic acid, OAA into aspartic acid, polymerization of such amino acids results into formation of proteins.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Mineral salts','Fatty acids','Volatile acids','Alpha ketoglutaric acid','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91326,'biology','Which is distributed more widely in a cell? [1992]','RNA','RNA occurs in the nucleus as well as in the cytoplasm of the eukaryotic cells and in prokaryotic cell, it is found in the cytoplasm. DNA is found in the nucleus, mitochondria and chloroplast. Chloroplast and sphaerosomes are found only in cytoplasm.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','DNA','RNA','Chloroplasts','Sphaerosomes','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91327,'biology','Living cell contains 60 – 95% water. Water present in human body is [1992]','65 – 70%','Water is the most abundant substance of living beings. The water content of actively living cells varies between (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:142, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','60 – 65%','50 – 55%','75 – 80%','65 – 70%','NEW','11-142','11-104',NULL),
(91328,'biology','Adenine is [1992]','Purine','The heterocyclic compounds in nucleic\nacids are the nitrogenous bases named adenine, guanine, uracil,\ncytosine, and thymine. Adenine and Guanine are substituted purines\nwhile the rest are substituted pyrimidines.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:145, paragraph no:1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Purine','Pyrimidine','Nucleoside','Nucleotide','NEW','11-145','11-107',NULL),
(91329,'biology','Enzymes having slightly different molecular structure but performing identical activity are [1991]','Isoenzymes','Enzymes having slightly different molecular structures but performing identical activities are called isoenzymes. Over','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Holoenzymes','Isoenzymes','Apoenzymes','Coenzymes','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91330,'biology','A nucleotide is formed of [1991]','Nitrogen base, sugar and phosphate','Nucleotide is an organic compound consisting of a nitrogencontaining purine or pyrimidine base linked to a sugar (ribose or deoxyribose) and a phosphate group. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:144, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Purine, pyrimidine and phosphate','Purine, sugar and phosphate','Nitrogen base, sugar and phosphate','Pyrimidine, sugar and phosphate','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91331,'biology','DNA is composed of repeating units of [1991]','Deoxyribonucleotides','DNA is the largest macromolecule in the organisms. It is a long, double chain of deoxyribonucleotide, or deoxyribotide units. The two deoxyribonucleotide chains are twisted around a common axis to form a righthanded double helix (spiral) that encloses a cylindrical space in it.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:151, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Ribonucleosides','Deoxyribonucleosides','Ribonucleotides','Deoxyribonucleotides','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(91332,'biology','The basic unit of nucleic acid is [1991]','Nucleotide','The nucleic acids (DNA and RNA) are the molecules having complex structure and very high molecular weights. The nucleic acid is composed of a large number of nucleotide molecules joined into a linear, unbranched chain. Nucleotide is an organic compound consisting of a nitrogencontaining purine or pyrimidine base linked to a sugar (ribose or deoxyribose) and a phosphate group. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:144, paragraph no:3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Pentose sugar','Nucleoid','Nucleoside','Nucleotide','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91333,'biology','Mineral associated with cytochrome is [1991]','Fe','Cytochromes are generally membranebound hemoproteins that contain heme groups and carry out electron transport. The heme group is a highly conjugated ring system (which means its electrons are very mobile) surrounding a metal ion, which readily interconverts between the oxidation states. For many cytochromes the metal ion present is that of iron, which interconverts between Fe','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Cu','Mg','Cu and mg','Fe','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91334,'biology','Which is not consistent with double helical structure of DNA? [1990]','A+T/C+G is not constant.','The density of DNA decreases on heating as hydrogen bonds breakdown. According to Chargaff’s rules, the amount of adenine is always equal to that of thymine, and the amount of guanine is always equal to that of cytosine i.eA = T and G = C. The base ratio A + T/ G + C may vary form species to species, but is constant for a given species.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','A =T  C = G','Density of DNA decreases on heating.','A+T/C+G is not constant.','Both a and b','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91335,'biology','RNA does not possess [1988]','Thymine','All nitrogenous basis are same in DNA and RNA except Uracil in place of Thymine in RNA','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Uracil','Thymine','Adenine','Cytosine','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91336,'biology','In double helix of DNA, the two DNA strands are [1988]','Coiled around a common axis','According to WatsonCrick model, the DNA molecule consists of two long, parallel chains which are joined together by short crossbars at regular intervals. The two chains are spirally coiled around a common axis in a regular manner to form a right handed double helix. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:151, paragraph no:2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Coiled around a common axis','Coiled around each other','Coiled differently','Coiled over protein sheath','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(91337,'biology','Which of the following are not secondary metabolites in plants? [NEET 2021]','Amino acids, glucose','(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:146, Table 9.3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Rubber, gums','Morphine, codeine','Amino acids, glucose','Vinblastin, curcumin','NEW','11-146','11-108',NULL),
(91338,'biology','Identify the incorrect pair [NEET 2021]','Drugs – Ricin','NCERT 11 Class Table 9.3 Page 146','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Drugs – Ricin','Alkaloids – Codeine','Toxin – Abrin','Lectins – Concanavalin A','NEW','11-146','11-108',NULL),
(91339,'biology','Read the following statements on lipids and find out correct set of statements:\n\n(a) Lecithin found in the plasma membrane is a glycolipid\n\n(b) Saturated fatty acids possess one or more c = c bonds\n\n(c) Gingely oil has lower melting point, hence remains as oil in winter\n\n(d) Lipids are generally insoluble in water but soluble in some organic solvents\n\n(e) When fatty acid is esterified with glycerol, monoglycerides are formed[NEET 2022]','(c), (d) and (e) only','NCERT Class 11 Page 144','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','(a), (b) and (c) only','(a), (d) and (e) only','(c), (d) and (e) only','(a), (b) and (d) only','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
