(91401,'chemistry','Hard water Contain Ca and Mg in form of','All of these','Presence of calcium and magnesium salts in the form of hydrogencarbonate, chloride and sulphate in water makes water ‘hard’. Hard water does not give lather with soap.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 291; Paragraph no.5)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hard and soft water',NULL,'1','mcq','Bicarbonates','Chloride','Sulphates','All of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91402,'chemistry','Soft water is free from soluble ________.','Ca/Mg','Water free from  soluble salts of calcium and magnesium is called Soft water.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 291; Paragraph no.5)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hard and soft water',NULL,'1','fillup','Ca/Mg,Ca/Ba',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91403,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\n(A): Hard water scum with soap\n\n(R): Soap containing sodium stearate reacts with Ca/Mg salts and precipitate in form of  (C₁₇H₃₅COO)₂M ,M=Ca,Mg','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Hard water forms scum/precipitate with soap. Soap containing sodium stearate (C₁₇H₃₅COONa) reacts with hard water to precipitate out Ca/Mg stearate.\n2C₁₇H₃₅COONa(aq) + M²⁺(aq) → (C₁₇H₃₅COO)₂M ↓ + 2Na⁺ (aq)M=Ca,Mg\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 291; Paragraph no.5)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hard and soft water',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91404,'chemistry','Choose the wrong statement.\n\na. Hardness of water is of two types i-e temporary hardness,permanent hardness\n\nb. Temporary hardness is due to presence of bicarbonates of Ca/Mg\n\nc. Temporary hardness can be removed by either boiling or adding lime','all are correct','(i)The hardness of water is of two types;temporary hardness, and permanent hardness.\n\n(ii)Temporary hardness is due to the presence of magnesium and calcium hydrogencarbonates.\n\n(iii)Temporary hardness can be removed by boiling or adding lime.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 292; Paragraph no.1 and 2 )','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hard and soft water',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','all are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91405,'chemistry','In hard water the soluble Mg(HCO₃)₂ converts to insoluble on boiling','Mg(OH)₂','Ksp =  [Mg²⁺]  [OH⁻]² more the concentration of ions ,ions will be precipitate out ksp value will be high as compared to MgCO₃     \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 292; Paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hard and soft water-Temporary hardness',NULL,'2','mcq','Mg(OH)₂','MgCO₃','MgO','MgH','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91406,'chemistry','Ca(HCO₃)₂ converts to insoluble on boiling','CaCO₃','Ksp =  [Ca²⁺]  [OH⁻]² The solubility product constant of Ca(OH)₂ is small as compared to CaCO₃\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 292; Paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hard and soft water-Temporary hardness',NULL,'2','mcq','Ca(OH)₂','CaCO₃','CaO','MgH','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91407,'chemistry','In clark\'s method calculated amount of __________ added to hard water.','Ca(OH)₂','Clark’s method: In this method calculated amount of lime is added to hard water. It precipitates out calcium carbonate and magnesium hydroxide which can be filtered off.\nThe reaction is :\nCa(OH)₂ +  Ca(HCO₃)₂ → 2CaCO₃ ↓ + 2H₂O\n\nCa(OH)₂ +  Mg(HCO₃)₂→ 2CaCO₃ ↓ + Mg(OH)₂ + 2H₂O\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 292; Paragraph no.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hard and soft water-Temporary hardness',NULL,'1','fillup','Ca,(OH)₂,CO₃',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91408,'chemistry','Permanent hardness is due to insoluble salts of Mg and Ca in form of','Both b and c','Permenant hardness is due to the  presence of soluble salts of magnesium and calcium in the form of chlorides and sulphates in water.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 292; Paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hard and soft water-Permanent hardness',NULL,'1','mcq','OH⁻(hydroxide)','Cl⁻(Chloride)','SO₄²⁻(Sulphates)','Both b and c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91409,'chemistry','Complete the following reaction.\n MCl₂  +  Na₂CO₃ →','MCO₃ + 2NaCl','Permenant hardness can be removed by treating with washing soda \nTreatment with washing soda (sodium carbonate): Washing soda reacts with soluble calcium and magnesium chlorides and sulphates in hard water to form insoluble\ncarbonates..The reaction in general is given as:\n\nMCl₂  +  Na₂CO₃ → MCO₃ ↓ + 2NaCl (M=Mg ,Ca)\n\nMSO₄  + Na₂CO₃ → MCO₃ ↓ + Na₂SO₄ (M=Mg,Ca)\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 292; Paragraph no.4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hard and soft water-Permanent hardness',NULL,'1','mcq','MHCO₃ + 2NaCl','MCO₃ + 2NaCl','M(OH)₂ + 2NaCl','MCO₃ + Cl₂  + 2Na','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91410,'chemistry','Sodium hexametaphosphate (Na₆P₆O₁₈) commercially called as','Calgon','Sodium hexameta- phosphate (Na₆P₆O₁₈), commercially called ‘calgon’.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 292; Paragraph no.5)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hard and soft water-Permanent hardness',NULL,'1','mcq','Calgon','Calcite','Clark','Adduct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91411,'chemistry','Calgon reacts with Ca and Mg and keep them in solution in form of _______.','[Na₂MP₆O₁₈]²⁻','Sodium hexameta- phosphate (Na₆P₆O₁₈), commercially called ‘calgon’,when added to hard water, the\nfollowing reactions take place.\nNa₆P₆O₁₈ →  [Na₂P₆O₁₈]⁻   \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 292; Paragraph no.5)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hard and soft water-Permanent hardness',NULL,'1','mcq','[Na₂MP₆O₁₈]²⁻','[Na₆M₂P₆O₁₈]²⁻','[Na₆MP₆O₁₈]⁻','[Na₆M₂P₆O₁₈]⁻','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91412,'chemistry','Zeolite process also called as','Ion-exchange method','Ion-exchange method: This method is also called zeolite/permutit process. Hydrated sodium aluminium silicate is zeolite/permutit. For the sake of simplicity, sodium aluminium silicate (NaAlSiO4) can be written as NaZ. When this is added in hard water, exchange reactions take place.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 292; Paragraph no.6)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hard and soft water-Permanent hardness',NULL,'1','mcq','Ion-exchange method','Metal exchange method','Resin exchange method','Metal-resin exchange method','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91413,'chemistry','Choose the wrong statement.\n\na. Hydrated NaAlSiO₄ is called zeolite.\n\nb. NaAlSiO₄ is written as NaZ for the sake of simplicity.\n\nc. NaZ can not be reused .\n\nd. Na from zeolite is exchanged by Ca and Mg .','only c','Ion-exchange method: This method is also called zeolite/permutit process. Hydrated sodium aluminium silicate is zeolite/permutit. For the sake of simplicity, sodium aluminium\nsilicate (NaAlSiO₄) can be written as NaZ. When this is added in hard water, exchange reactions take place.\n2NaZ (s) + M²⁺ (aq) → MZ₂(s) + 2Na⁺ (aq)  [M= Mg,Ca]\n\nPermutit/zeolite is said to be exhausted when all the sodium in it is used up. It is regenerated for further use by treating with an aqueous sodium chloride solution.\nMZ₂(s) + 2NaCl (aq) → 2NaZ(s)  + MCl₂(aq)\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 292; Paragraph no.6)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hard and soft water-Permanent hardness',NULL,'2','mcq','only a','only b','only c','only d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91414,'chemistry','Zeolite after using in removing of permanent hardness can be regain by reacting it with','NaCl','Permutit/zeolite is said to be exhausted when all the sodium in it is used up. It is regenerated for further use by treating with an aqueous sodium chloride solution.\nMZ₂(s) + 2NaCl (aq) → 2NaZ(s)  + MCl₂(aq)\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 292; Paragraph no.6)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hard and soft water-Permanent hardness',NULL,'1','mcq','NaCl','HCl','KCl','HBr','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91415,'chemistry','Modern method to remove permanent hardness of water is','Synthetic resin method','Synthetic resins method: Nowadays hard water is softened by using synthetic cation exchangers. This method is more efficient than zeolite process. Cation exchange resins contain large organic molecule with - SO₃H group and are water insoluble. Ion exchange resin (RSO₃H) is changed to RNa by treating it with NaCl. The resin exchanges Na+ ions with Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions present in hard water to make the water soft. Here R is resin anion.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 292; Paragraph no.7)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hard and soft water-Permanent hardness',NULL,'1','mcq','Calgon\'s method','Ion- exchange method','Synthetic resin method','Treatment with washing soda','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91416,'chemistry','Cation exchange resins contain large organic molecule with','-SO₃H','Synthetic resins method: Nowadays hard water is softened by using synthetic cation exchangers. This method is more efficient than zeolite process. Cation exchange resins contain large organic molecule with - SO₃H group and are water insoluble. Ion exchange resin (RSO₃H) is changed to RNa by treating it with NaCl. The resin exchanges Na+ ions with Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions present in hard water to make the water soft. Here R is resin anion.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 292; Paragraph no.7)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hard and soft water-Permanent hardness',NULL,'1','mcq','-CO₃H','-OH','-SO₃H','-Cl⁻','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51051,'chemistry','What are the units of “b” in van der Waals equation?','L/mol','The ideal gas equation is given as (P – an2/V2)(V – nb) = nRT. So by considering the equation, we can understand that the units of the volume are equal to the units of a number of moles X be so the units of b. So b’s units = volume / number of moles so it is L/mol.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 150; Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','L/mol','L mol','1/L mol','L','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91417,'chemistry','Ion exchange resins(RSO₃H) is converted to RNa by reacting it with','NaCl','Synthetic resins method: Nowadays hard water is softened by using synthetic cation exchangers. This method is more efficient than zeolite process. Cation exchange resins contain large organic molecule with - SO₃H group and are water insoluble. Ion exchange resin (RSO₃H) is changed to RNa by treating it with NaCl. The resin exchanges Na+ ions with Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions present in hard water to make the water soft. Here R is resin anion.\n\nThe resin can be regenerated by adding aqueous NaCl solution.The reaction is:\n2RNa (s) + M²⁺(aq) → 2Na⁺(aq)  + R₂M(s)\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 292; Paragraph no.7)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hard and soft water-Permanent hardness',NULL,'1','mcq','NaAlSiO₄','Na₂CO₃','NaOH','NaCl','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91418,'chemistry','Organic resins exchange Na⁺ with','Both a and b','Synthetic resins method: Nowadays hard water is softened by using synthetic cation exchangers. This method is more efficient than zeolite process. Cation exchange resins contain large organic molecule with - SO₃H group and are water insoluble. Ion exchange resin (RSO₃H) is changed to RNa by treating it with NaCl. The resin exchanges Na+ ions with Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions present in hard water to make the water soft. Here R is resin anion.\n\nThe resin can be regenerated by adding aqueous NaCl solution.The reaction is:\n2RNa (s) + M²⁺(aq) → 2Na⁺(aq)  + R₂M(s)\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 292; Paragraph no.7)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hard and soft water-Permanent hardness',NULL,'1','mcq','Ca²⁺','Mg²⁺','-SO₃H','Both a and b','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91419,'chemistry','The Resins used in synthetic resins method is regain by reacting with','NaCl','Synthetic resins method: Nowadays hard water is softened by using synthetic cation exchangers. This method is more efficient than zeolite process. Cation exchange resins contain large organic molecule with - SO₃H group and are water insoluble. Ion exchange resin (RSO₃H) is changed to RNa by treating it with NaCl. The resin exchanges Na+ ions with Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions present in hard water to make the water soft. Here R is resin anion.\n\nThe resin can be regenerated by adding aqueous NaCl solution.The reaction is:\n2RNa (s) + M²⁺(aq) → 2Na⁺(aq)  + R₂M(s)\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 292; Paragraph no.7)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hard and soft water-Permanent hardness',NULL,'1','mcq','HCl','NaCl','KCl','All of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/91419q.png'),
(91501,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is correct?\n\na. Hydrogen peroxide is used in pollution control treatment\n\nb. Hydrogen peroxide can be prepared from acidified Barium peroxide\n\nc. On hydrolysis peroxymonosulphate gives H2O2','a,b','Hydrogen peroxide is an important chemical used in pollution control treatment of domestic and industrial effluents\nHydrogen peroxide cann be prepared by the following methods.\n(i) Acidifying barium peroxide and removing excess water by evaporation under reduced pressure gives hydrogen peroxide.\nBaO₂.8H₂O (s) + H₂SO₄ (aq) →  BaSO₄ (s) +H₂O₂ aq + 8H₂O(l)\n(ii)Peroxodisulphate, obtained by electrolytic oxidation of acidified sulphate solutions at high current density, on hydrolysis yields hydrogen peroxide\nThis method is now used for the laboratory preparation of D₂O₂.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 293; Paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrogen peroxide',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,b,c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91502,'chemistry','Industrially  H₂O₂ is prepared by autoxidation of ______','2-alkylanthraquinols','Industrially Hydrogen Peroxide can be prepared by the auto- oxidation of 2-alklylanthraquinols. In this case 1%  H₂O₂ is formed. It is extracted with water and concentrated to ~30% (by mass) by distillation under reduced pressure. It can be further concentrated to ~85% by careful distillation under low pressure. The remaining water can be frozen out to obtain pure  H₂O₂.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 293; Paragraph no.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrogen peroxide',NULL,'1','fillup','2-,al,kyl,an,th,ra,qui,nols,3-',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91503,'chemistry','Auto-oxidation of 2-alkylanthraquinol yields','1% H₂O₂','Industrially Hydrogen Peroxide can be prepared by the auto- oxidation of 2-alklylanthraquinols. In this case 1%  H₂O₂ is formed. It is extracted with water and concentrated to ~30% (by mass) by distillation under reduced pressure. It can be further concentrated to ~85% by careful distillation under low pressure. The remaining water can be frozen out to obtain pure  H₂O₂\n.Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 293; Paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrogen peroxide',NULL,'1','mcq','30% H₂O₂','1% H₂O₂','85% H₂O₂','0.1% H₂O₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91504,'chemistry','H₂O₂ obtained by auto-oxidation of 2-alkylanthraquinol can be concentrated to 85% by distillation under ___________.','Low pressure','Industrially Hydrogen Peroxide can be prepared by the auto- oxidation of 2-alklylanthraquinols. In this case 1%  H₂O₂ is formed. It is extracted with water and concentrated to ~30% (by mass) by distillation under reduced pressure. It can be further concentrated to ~85% by careful distillation under low pressure. The remaining water can be frozen out to obtain pure  H₂O₂\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 293; Paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrogen peroxide',NULL,'1','fillup','Low, pre,ss,ure,hi,gh',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91505,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is true.\n\n(i)H₂O₂ is dark blue is colour.\n\n(ii)It is miscible in water in all proportions .\n\n(iii)The hydrate formed is H₂O₂.H₂O\n\n(iv)A 30% solution of H₂O₂  is marketed as ‘100 volume’ hydrogen peroxide means that one millilitre of 30% H₂O₂ solution will give 100 mL of oxygen at STP.\n\n(v)MP of hydrogen peroxide is 201K.','(ii) , (iii) ,(iv) are correct (i) and (v) are wrong','In the pure state H₂O₂ is an almost colourless (very pale blue) liquid.H₂O₂is miscible with water in all proportions and forms a hydrate H₂O₂ .H₂O (mp 221K). A 30% solution of H₂O₂ is marketed as ‘100 volume’ hydrogen peroxide. It means that one millilitre of 30% H₂O₂ solution will give 100 mL of oxygen at STP. Commercially marketed sample is 10 V, which means that the sample contains 3% H₂O₂\n.Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 293; Paragraph no.4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrogen peroxide-Physical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) , (ii) , (iiii) are correct (iv) and (v) are wrong.','(ii) , (iii) ,(iv) are correct (i) and (v) are wrong','(i) (iii) and (v) are correct (iv) and (ii) is wrong','(v) and (iv) are correct (i) ,(ii) and (iii) are wrong','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91506,'chemistry','The strength of 10 volume hydrogen peroxide is','3% H₂O₂','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 293; Problem no.9.5)\ndiagram(ne0905)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrogen peroxide-Physical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','30% H₂O₂','1% H₂O₂','3% H₂O₂','10% H₂O₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51052,'chemistry','What is the ratio of the crtitical temperature to the Boyle\'s temperature of a gas','8 / 27','The maximum temperature gas can remain liquid is known as critical temperature. The temperature till which a gas behaves like an ideal gas is Boyle’s temperature. Boyle’s temperature TB is given by a/Rb critical temperature Tc is given by 8a/27Rb. So the ratio is 8/27. \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','8','27','27/8','8 / 27','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91507,'biology','Which of the following regarding property of amino acids is/are correct?\n\ni)Amino acids have evaporating capability to form steam\n\nii)The ionizing nature of both amino group and carboxyl group makes them unique. \n\niii)The ionizing property of the amino acids makes it different from other organic compounds.   \n\niv)Valine is an example of acidic amino acid.','ii and iii both','The specific property of amino acids is the ionizing nature of the amino group and the carboxyl group. This property changes the structure of amino acids in water and helps in the formation of zwitter ion at isoelectric pH. Valine is a neutral amino acid.( Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 144; Paragraph no 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','ii only','iii only','ii and i both','ii and iii both','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91508,'biology','Select the correct option regarding structure of alanine. Select the correct option :\n\nStatement I)R group in amino acids is a carboxyl group.\n\nStatement II)The R group is methyl group incase of Alanine with a carboxyl group, an amino group and hydrogen atom, present in three other positions,','Only II is correct','The R group is variable group of the amino acids. The alpha carbon contains carboxyl, amino and a hydrogen atom. The R group varies. If it is a methyl group then amino acid is called alanine and if R is hydrogen the it is called as glycine.( Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 143,144; Paragraph 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both statements are true','Only II is correct','Only I is correct','Both Statements are false','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91509,'biology','Assertion :Lipids are soluble in water and examples are simple fatty acids. \n\nReason :Lipids are hydrophilic in nature and contain a Fatty acid side chain soluble in water.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','The lipids are the compounds those are insoluble in water or are called as hydrophobic in nature. The lipids could be simple fatty acids that mainly consist of a carboxyl group attached to R group. ( Reference  :  NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 144; Paragraph no 2 )','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91510,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding lipids. Select the correct :\n \ni)Trihydroxy propane is a simple lipid. It is also called as glycerol.\n\nii)Fatty acids are esterified to glycerol to produce monoglycerides, diglycerides and triglycerides.\n\niii)Lipids having a phosphorylated group esterified to glycerol skeleton are called as phospholipids.  \n\niv)Lecithin is an example of carbohydrate','i,ii and iii','The glycerol is the simplest lipid also called as Trihydroxy propane. The lipids esterified with glycerol are then converted to mono, di and tri glycerides. Moreover, some lipids also contain phosphorous and are called as phospholipids found in cell membranes.Lecithin is a phospholipid.( Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 144; Paragraph no 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii','ii and iii','iv and iii','i,ii and iii','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91511,'biology','Which of the following statement/s is/are correct about lipids?\n\ni)Oils have higher melting that s why they remain as waxes in winters.\n\nii)Palmitic acid is a lipid that has 18 carbon atoms including carboxyl carbon.\n\niii)Lecithin as an example of phospholipid, compounds that make up cell membrane. \n\niv)Archidonic acid has 12 carbons including carboxyl carbon.','iii only','The oils and fats are types of lipids. Oils have lower melting and remain as oils in winters. Palmitic acid is another lipid that consist of 16 carbon atoms while archidonic acid consist of 20 carbons including carboxyl carbon.Lecithin is a phospholipid.( Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 144; Paragraph no 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','i only','iv only','iii only','iv and iii','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91512,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the nucleoside and select the correct one: \n \ni)If a phosphate group is esterified to a sugar then it becomes a nucleoside.\n\nii)Adenylic acid is an example of nucleoside.\n\niii)Adenosine, cytidine and guanosine are examples of nucleosides. \n\niv)If a phosphate group is added to a nucleotide then it is called as nucleoside.','iii only','When the sugar are without a phosphate group then it is called as nucleoside. Some of the major examples are adenosine, guanosine, cytidine, thymidine etc. The absence of phosphate in a sugar molecule forms a nucleoside.( Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 144; Paragraph no 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','i  and iii','ii only','iii only','iv and iii','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91513,'biology','Assertion: The nucleosides consist of a pentose sugar molecule attached to nitrogen ring of nitrogenous base\n\nReason: The pentose sugar that is attached with a nitrogenous base is called a nucleotide, and consists of a N glycosidic bond','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','The nucleotide and nucleosides are two different terms. The nucleoside consist of a five carbon sugar along with a nitrogenous base while the nucleotide includes phosphate molecule as well. (Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 144; Paragraph no 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91514,'biology','Consider the following and select the correct option about representation of different compounds.\n \ni)Glycerol is a three carbon molecule attached with three hydroxyl groups.\n\nii)Ribose is a five carbon sugar while glucose is a hexose.\n\niii)Fatty acids are also called as diglycerides.  \n\niv) Glucose consist of seven(6 in ring+1 side chain) atoms.','i and ii','The structures varies depending on number of carbon atoms as well. Glucose is a six carbon sugar with both cyclical and linear representation. Ribose consist of five carbons. Moreover, 3 the fatty acids esterified to a glycerol molecule is called a triglyceride. (Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 145; Figure 9.1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii','ii only','ii and iii','iii and i','NEW','11-145','11-107',NULL),
(91515,'biology','Consider the following statement about secondary metabolites and select the correct option.','Alkaloids, flavonoids and rubber are the secondary metabolites.','The primary metabolites(carbohydrates, amino acids and fats) were observed in the animal tissues whereas, the while observing fungal and plants cells compounds other than primary metabolites called as secondary metabolites were discovered like alkaloids,flavanoids and rubber are secondary metabolites.Moreover,morphine,rubber and gums are also the secindart metabolites.Thus, primary and secondary metabolites are two different terms and they include different substances.( Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 146; Paragraph 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Alkaloids, flavonoids and rubber are the secondary metabolites.','Morphine, rubber, gums etc are examples of primary metabolites.','Primary metabolites obtained from plant sources are called secondary metabolites','Carbohydrates, fats and amino acids are included under Secondary metabolites','NEW','11-146','11-108',NULL),
(91516,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding primary and secondary metabolites. Choose the correct option?\n\n1)Primary metabolites includes rubber, gums etc.\n\n2)Secondary metabolites have ecological importance but their importance in host organisms is still unknown.\n\n3)Primary metabolites are useful for humans commercially.  \n\n4)Secondary metabolites are the same as the primary metabolites and they have similar functions.','2 only','The primary and secondary metabolites have their own importance but secondary metabolites uses in host organisms are still not known. Moreover, they have ecological importance and are also used for the human welfare.Rubber and gums are the secondary metabolites and primary and secondary metabolites are two different terms having different functions.( Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 146; Paragraph 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','1 only','2 only','3 and 2','2 and 4','NEW','11-146','11-108',NULL),
(91517,'biology','Assertion: Proteins, lipids, carbohydrates and nucleic acids are the four types of organic compounds that belong to the acid insoluble fraction group.\n\nReason: The molecular weights of the four different types of organic compounds, except lipids are similar','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The acid insoluble fraction has four types of organic compounds like proteins, nucleic acids, polysaccharides and lipids. All three types with the exception of lipids has a molecular weight  of about thousand Daltons and above.( Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 146; Paragraph 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW','11-146','11-108',NULL),
(91518,'biology','Select the right one in following statements regarding bio macromolecules.Select the correct one?\n\ni)Water is present in the least amount in human body.   \n\nii)The amount of water is between 30-20 percent in a cell\'s chemical composition.\n\niii)Water is the most abundant compound in cells.\n\niv)Carbohydrates are the most abundant compound in cells.','iii only','It is observed that water is the most abundant chemical present in the human body and its chemical composition in cells is 70-90%. Carbohydrates have a composition of 3% in the cells and ions are present in least amount. (Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 147; Paragraph 2; Figure 9.4)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','i  and iii','ii and iv','iii only','i only','NEW','11-147','11-109',NULL),
(91519,'biology','Assertion: Lipids are small molecular weight substances and are present in cell structures as well that are disrupted on grinding and are water insoluble , not included in macromolecules.\n\nReasoning: Lipids are called as the polymeric substances and are macromolecules because they have high molecular weights.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','The lipids have molecular weight less than 8 Daltons but still included in insoluble acid fraction because when a tissue is disrupted, vesicles that are insoluble in water are formed. Thus, lipids are not included in the macromolecules.( Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 146,147 ; Paragraph 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW','11-147','11-109',NULL),
(91520,'biology','Mark the correct statement/s regarding proteins?\n \ni)Proteins are the homopolymers.\n\nii)Proteins are polypeptides that consist of linear chains of amino acids.\n\niii)The basic unit of the proteins is DNA and RNA.\n\niv)There are 22 different types of amino acids in the human body.','ii only','Proteins are the polypeptides consisting of linear chains of amino acids. There are twenty different types of amino acids , and their combination results in heteropolymers. Proteins can never be the homopolymers.The basic unit of proteins is amino acids.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 147)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','i  and iii','ii only','ii and iii','iv only','NEW','11-147','11-109',NULL),
(91521,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding proteins?Select the correct option.\n \ni)Thymidine, guanine and uridine are examples of amino acids.\n\nii)The proteins are involved in several functions like transport of nutrients across cell membrane, some are enzymes etc.\n\niii)The amino acids are categorized into essential and non-essential amino acids on the basis of their need in the body. \n\niv)The amino acids synthesized by the body itself are called essential amino acids.','ii and iii','The essential and non-essential are two types of amino acids. The ones that body can make are non-essential while those taken through diet are essential amino acids. Proteins are also involved in transportation across cell membranes. Alanine, proline are the examples of amino acids. But here the three mentioned(Thymidine, Guanine and Uridine) are nucleosides.( Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 147)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii','ii and iii','iv and i','iii and iv','NEW','11-147','11-109',NULL),
(51053,'chemistry','A gas at a temperature of 27°C has a volume of 60.0 mL. What temperature change is needed to increase this gas to a volume of 90.0 mL?','An increase of 150°C','We will use Charles\'s law. First, we must convert the temperature to kelvins by adding 273 to get 300 K as the initial temperature. Think of this as a proportionality: If the volume is multiplied by 3/2 the temperature will also have to be multiplied by 3/2  Thus the final temperature is 450 K, which represents a 150 K increase.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 142; Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A reduction of 150°C','An increase of 150°C','A reduction of 40.5°C','An increase of 40.5°C','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91522,'biology','Assertion: Antibodies are the protein molecules that attach to an antigen and mount an immune response\n\nReasoning: Antibodies have a specific site for attachment to antigen and immune cells, and help in mounting of an immune response','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Antibodies and receptors are two different types of proteins those are involved in different functions in the body. Antibodies protects the body against infections  by binding to specific sites on antigen and immune cells and aid in phagocytosis,proteins also make up receptors that help in receiving sensations and perception of stimuli(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 147; Figure 9.2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW','11-147','11-109',NULL),
(91523,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the proteins. Choose the correct option? \n\ni)Collagen is most abundant protein in biosphere. \n\nii)Collagen and trypsin are two different hormones.\n\niii)Ribulose phosphate is most abundant in the whole biosphere.\n\niv)Collagen is the most abundant protein in the animal world.','iv only','Ribulose bisphosphate carboxylase oxygenase is the most abundant protein in the whole biosphere, while Collagen is the most abundant protein in the animal world.Collagen is a structural protein and Trypsin is the active form of enzyme Trypsinogen.( Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 148)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','i only','ii and iv','iii and iv','iv only','NEW','11-148','11-110',NULL),
(91524,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding polysaccharides. Select the correct option?\n \ni)Paper from plant pulp, cotton fibres and plant cell wall contain cellulose..\n\nii)Arthropods exoskeleton are made up of complex amino acids called cellulose.\n\niii)The complex polysaccharides are mostly heteropolymers.','i only','Paper from plant pulp, cotton fibres and plant cell wall contains cellulose whereas the arthropods exoskeleton contains chitin. All the complex polysaccharides are mostly homopolymers.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 148)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii','ii and iii','iii and iv','i only','NEW','11-148','11-110',NULL),
(91525,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding polysaccharides. Select the correct option?\n \ni)Polysaccharides are micro molecules in nature.\n\nii)Polysaccharides are the carbohydrates that are made up of threads of monosaccharide units as building blocks.\n\niii)Proteins are the heteropolymers that consist of amino acids as building blocks.\n\niv)Cellulose is made up of different types of monosaccharide units.','ii and iii','Polysaccharides are the long chains of sugars those are made up of monosaccharides as building blocks. Cellulose is a type of polysaccharide that is made up of only one type of monosaccharide unit called glucose.Moreover, polysaccharides are macromolecular in nature.Proteins are the polypeptides consisting of linear chains of amino acids. There are twenty different types of amino acids , and their combination results in heteropolymers. Proteins can never be the homopolymers.The basic unit of proteins is amino acids.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 147)(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 149)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','i and iii','ii and iii','iii only','iv and iii','NEW','11-149','11-111',NULL),
(91526,'biology','Assertion: Starch forms helical tertiary structures and can hold 12 molecules.\n\nReasoning: The starch-iodine complex is yellow in colour and starch can hold iodine molecules in the helical portion.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','The polysaccharides consist of reducing end and a non-reducing end. Starch forms helical secondary structure and can hold iodine molecules that gives blue colour. (Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 148)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW','11-148','11-110',NULL),
(91527,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding nucleic acids. Select the correct option?\n\ni)Nucleic acids are present in large amount in the living cell and is considered as the micro molecule.\n\nii)Nucleic acids ac belongs to the acid soluble fraction molecules.\n\niii)Nucleotides are the building blocks of nucleic acids and they occupies largest macromolecular fraction in a cell.\n\niv)Histones are the building blocks of nucleic acids.','iii only','Nucleic acids are a type of macromecules belonging to acid insoluble fraction group. It is made up of nucleotide units. It is considered as the most abundant macromolecule found in the living tissue.Histone molecules are proteins that help in packaging DNA (Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 149)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','i only','ii and iii','iii only','iv and iii','NEW','11-149','11-111',NULL),
(91528,'biology','Assertion: Adenine and guanine are the substituted pyrimidines while thymidine and uridine are substituted purines.\n\nReason: The nucleotides are made up of three different chemical compounds like hexose sugar, nitrogenous base, phosphate','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','The nucleic acids are made of three distinct chemical substances. One is heterocyclic ring that can be purine or pyrimidine, one is phosphoric acid and a pentose sugar unit. Adenine and guanine are purines while all others are substituted pyrimidines  ( Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 149)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW','11-149','11-111',NULL),
(91529,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding nucleic acids. Select the correct option?\n\ni)DNA and RNA are the same types of nucleic acids, in different organisms.\n\nii)Deoxyribose sugar is the one in which O₂ atom is removed from carbon number 2 of the sugar.\n\niii)Ribonucleic acid contains ribose sugar while deoxyribonucleic acid contains deoxyribose sugar.\n\niv)Ribulose bisphosphate contains deoxyribose sugar while DNA contains ribose sugar.','ii and iii','The ribose and deoxyribose are two different sugars. In deoxyribose, the O₂ atom is removed from Carbon 2 and is present in DNA whereas, ribose sugar is present in RNA.Ribulose bisphosphate dehydrgenase contains ribose sugar while DNA contains deoxyribose sugar.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 149)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','i and iv','iii and iv','i and ii','ii and iii','NEW','11-149','11-111',NULL),
(91530,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding protein structures. Select the correct option?\n \nStatement  I)The primary structure is the one in which the sequence of the amino acids is determined.\n\nStatement II)The secondary structure is usually either an alpha helix or beta pleated sheets','Both statements are true','The primary structure is the positional information of a protein molecule. In the primary structure, the sequence of the amino acids is determined.The secondary structure involves the folding of polypeptide chains to form beta pleated sheets or alpha helix.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 149)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Both statements are true','Only II is correct','Only I is correct','Both Statements are false','NEW','11-149','11-111',NULL),
(91531,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding protein structures. Select the correct option?\n\ni) The left end of a protein acts as the last amino acid.\n\nii)The amino acids always start with the C-terminal amino acid.\n\niii)The right end of a protein acts as the first amino acid.\n\niv)The last amino acid in a protein structure is called C-terminal amino acid while first one is called as N-terminal amino acid.','iv only','The protein acts as a line in which right end depicts the last amino acid while left end depicts the first amino acid. The first amino acid is referred to as N-terminal amino acid while last one is C-terminal amino acid.( Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 149,150)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','i only','ii only','iii only','iv only','NEW','11-150','11-112',NULL),
(91532,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding various levels of protein structures. Select the correct option/s?\n\ni)Beta-pleated sheets is a primary structure.\n\nii)Alpha helix and beta-pleated sheets are two different types of secondary structures.\n\niii)Alpha helical structure is primary in nature. \n\niv)Secondary structure is the final structure of proteins.','ii only','The primary structure is the first stage of proteins after which the proteins fold into a alpha helical structure or a beta-pleated sheets that are the types of secondary structures.The last stage of protein structure is quaternary structure.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 150; Figure 9.3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','i and ii','ii and iii','iii and iv','ii only','NEW','11-150','11-112',NULL),
(91533,'biology','Assertion:Some proteins fold upon themselves to give rise to woolen ball tertiary structure.\n\nReasoning: The quarternary structure determines the secondary and tertiary structures, based on folding and arrangement.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','After the secondary structures long polypeptide chains are folded back to form a tertiary structure like wollen ball. Some proteins contain more than one polypeptide those are folded in firm of spheres giving rise to quaternary structure.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 150)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW','11-150','11-112',NULL),
(91534,'biology','Assertion: The adult haemoglobin has a quarternary structure\n\nReasoning: The adult haemoglobin contains four subunits with amino acids and that indicates the primary structure of human haemoglobin.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The haemoglobin is a protein present in the human body. This adult haemoglobin consist of four subunits . The two subunits are identical to eachother. These are two alpha subunits and two beta subunits. Moreover, haemoglobin possess a quaternary structure( this refers to arrangement of these globin chains with disulphide bridges and Haem co enzyme in the core).(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 150)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW','11-150','11-112',NULL),
(91535,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding bond linking monomers in a polymer. Select the correct option/s? \n\nI)The peptide bond is formed by the linking of carboxyl group on one end and an amino group of the other amino acid. \n\nII)The formation of the peptide bond results in the elimination of a single water molecule.','Both statements are true','The amino acids are linked with each other through the peptide bonds. The peptide bond is formed between the carboxyl group of one amino acid and the amino group of next amino acid. This results in the elimination of a water molecule. ( Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 151)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Both statements are true','Only II is correct','Only I is correct','Both Statements are false','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(91536,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding bond linking monomers in a polymer. Select the correct option/s? \n\ni) In a polysaccharides, the individual saccharides are held together by glycosidic bonds.  \n\nii)The gylcosidic bond is formed by the process of hydration in which a molecule of water is added.  \n\niii)The bond between the phosphate group and the hydroxyl group in a sugar is peptide bond.  \n\niv)The three amino acids linked with each other form a tetrapeptide.','i only','In a polysaccharide, the induvidual monosaccharides are held by the glycodic bonds formed as a result of dehydration process that involves the elimination of a water molecule. Whereas, the bond formed between a phosphate group and hydroxyl group of a sugar is the ester bond.The three amino acids linked with each other forms a dipeptide.( Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 151)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','i only','ii only','iii only','iv only','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(91537,'biology','Consider the following statements. Select the correct option/s? \n\nStatement I)In a nucleic acid, the ester bonds are formed by the connection of 3\' C of one sugar of one nucleotide with the 5\' C of other nucleotide.\n\nStatement II)The ester bond links the molecules of a nucleic acid by the ester bonds with the help of phosphate groups.If one ester bond is present on either side ,it is called as phosphodiester.','Both statements are true','The glycosidic bond is formed by dehydration process. However, in a nucleic acid, the phosphate group links 3\' carbon of one nucleotide to the 5\' carbon of another nucleotide resulting in the formation of ester bond. If ester bond is present on either side then it is called phosphodiester bond. ( Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 151)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Both statements are true','Only II is correct','Only I is correct','Both Statements are false','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(91538,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding bond linking monomers in a polymer. Select the correct option/s? \n\ni)The DNA is present in the form of a single helix.  \n\nii)The two strands of a double helical structure of DNA are parallel to each other.  \n\niii)The model of DNA double helical structure was proposed by Watson and Crick.  \n\niv)DNA is only present in animals .','iii only','The secondary structures of DNA were proposed by Watson and Crick. They showed that DNA exist as a double helix consisting of two strands that are anti parallel to eachother. DNA is present in every living tissue. ( Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 151)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','i and iii','ii only','iii only','iii and iv','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(91539,'biology','Assertion:In a phosphodiester bond, the guanine and adenine are linked with each other forming three hydrogen bonds while thymine and cytosine with each other forming two hydrogen bonds.\n\nReason: The sugar-phosphate makes the backbone of a DNA  in which nitrogen bases are projected more or less parallel to the backbone.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','The sugar-phosphate long chains form backbone of DNA. In this, nitrogen bases are orojected more or less perpendicular to the backbone. Adenine and thymine forms two hydrogen bonds while guanine and cytosine forms three bonds. This pairing is specific owing to the number of H atoms present in the structure attracted by electronegative atoms present in the structure of the nitrogenous bases. ( Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 151)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(91540,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding bond linking monomers in a polymer. Select the correct option/s? \n\ni)Adenine and thymine forms three hydrogen bonds and guanine and cytosine form two hydroge bonds.   \n\nii)One full turn of a helical structure involves twelve base pairs.  \n\niii)The rise per base pair is 3.4 A° and the pitch would be 34 A°.   \n\niv)The rise per base pair is 34A° and pitch would be 3.4A°.','iii only','The adenine and thymine always forms two hydrogen bonds and guanine and cytosine forms three hydrogen bonds. The base pairs are present in the form of staircase. One full turn of helical strand contains ten base pairs. The pitch would be 34A° and the rise per base pair is 3.4A°.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 152)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','i only','iii only','i and ii','iii and iv','NEW','11-152',NULL,NULL),
(91541,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding bond linking monomers in a polymer. Select the correct option/s?\n \ni) The biomolecules continue to remain in the same state throughout the life of an organism.  \n\nii)The biomolecules are transformed into other types of substances by a process called esterification.\n\niii)Metabolism involves the chemical reactions that allows the biomolecules to remain in the same state.\n\niv)The biomolecules are transformed into different kinds of molecules by the constant chemical reactions, that is metabolism.','iv only','The researches have shown that biomolecules do not remain in the same state. Instead they have a turn over. The process of transformation of biomolecules is called its metabolism. Through this process, different molecules are formed.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 152)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','i only','ii only','iii only','iv only','NEW','11-152',NULL,NULL),
(91542,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding bond linking monomers in a polymer. Select the incorrect option/s? \n\ni) All the biomolecular transformation reactions are uncatalyzed. \n\nii)All the chemical reactions occuring in living systems are accelerated with the help of nucleic acids.\n\niii) All enzymes are proteins but all proteins are not enzymes. \n\niv)The reactions are catalyzed with the help of enzymes those are protein in nature.','i and ii','Each and every single chemical reaction, even a physical reaction, is catalyzed. The catalyzation means speeding up the reactions and enzymes are essential for this aspect. Thus, we can say that all enzymes are proteins but all proteins are not enzymes.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 152)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','i and ii','i and iii','ii and iv','iii and iv','NEW','11-152',NULL,NULL),
(91543,'biology','Assertion: The metabolic reactions possess a great series of uncatalyzed reactions occuring through different metabolic pathways.\n\nReason: The conversion of biomolecules involves either circular or linear metabolic pathways. Each and every metabolic reaction is a catalyzed reaction.','Assertion is false, but Reason is true.','The biomolecules always turn over. These are converted to other substances by the formation or breaking of compounds.The conversion occurs through metabolic reactions that possess metabolic pathways either linear or circular. Moreover, every metabolic reaction is a catalyzed reaction.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 152).','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is false, but Reason is true.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW','11-152',NULL,NULL),
(91544,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding metabolic basis for living. Choose the correct option? \n\ni)The conversion of lactic acid to glucose follows a process called glycolysis. \n\nii)The process of glycolysis occurs in 8 metabolic steps. \n\niii)Most amount of energy is stored in the form of creatine phosphate molecules. \n\niv)The chemical bonds store large amount of energy in the form of ATP.','iv only','The conversion of glucose to lactic acid is called glycolysis that occurs in 10 metabolic steps. Moreover, the most important energy currency in living cells is bond energy stored in a chemical called ATP.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 153)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','i only','ii and iv','iii and iv','iv only','NEW','11-153',NULL,NULL),
(91545,'biology','Assertion: The degradation of the substances from complex to simpler ones involves release of the energy. \n\nReason: Glucose is the universal energy currency','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','The synthesis of simple substances from complex substances is called degradation(catabolism).It follows a catabolic pathway and involves release of the energy and anabolic involves use of enegy for making complex substances. ATP is the universal energy currency.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 153)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW','11-153',NULL,NULL),
(91546,'biology','Consider the following statements regardind living state. Select the correct ones? \n\ni)The concentration of all the biomolecules are same in a human body\n.  \nii)The concentration of hormones in the human body is measured in nanograms/mL.\n \niii)The living states cannot exist without the metabolism. \n\niv) Without degradation, there would be no living state.','ii and iii','The biomolecules have different concentrations in the human body like hormones concentration is given in nanogram/mL etc. It is also stated that the living states cannot exixt without the metabolism reactions that includes both anabolic and catabolic reactions.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 153)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii','ii and iii','iii and iv','i and iv','NEW','11-153',NULL,NULL),
(91547,'biology','Assertion:The equilibrium state stops the reactions and is very important aspect of reactions but, the living systems are maintained at a non- equilibrium state in order to perform work. \n\nReasoning:  The chemical reactions must go on to keep body work normally.Thus, a steady state is acheived so that body can perform fuctions perfectly.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The living state is the non-equilibrium steady state to be able to perform work. In other words, living pricesss is a constant effortto prevent falling into equilibrium.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 153)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW','11-153',NULL,NULL),
(91548,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding enzymes. Select incorrect options? \n\ni)Enzymes are very sensitive to high temperatures.\n\nii) Enzymes isolated from the organism working at high temperatures are not observed till date.\n\niii)Thermal stability is possessed by the organisms whose enzymes work at very high temperature.\n\niv)The inorganic catalysts can bear high temperatures as compared to the enzyme catalysts.','ii only','The thermophillic organism possess the ability of withstanding high temperatures,Thermus Aquaticus is an example of an Archeae bacteria , whose DNA polymerase works at high temperatures and is being used in PCR for amplification process . In other words, they are thermally stable.Each enzyme shows its highest activity at a particular temperature and pH called the optimum temperature and optimum pH. Activity declines both below and above the optimum value. Low temperature preserves the enzyme in a temporarily inactive state whereas high temperature destroys enzymatic activity because proteins are denatured by heat.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 154)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','i only','ii only','iii and ii','i and iv','NEW','11-154','11-113',NULL),
(91549,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding enzymes. Select incorrect options? \n\ni)Some amino acids behave as enzymes called as ribozymes \n\nii)All the chemical reactions that are catalyzed by enzymes are retarding in nature\n\niii)The enzymes consist of an inactive site for attaching of substrate.\n\niv)Enzymes are same as that of inorganic catalysts.','All statements are false','The nucleic acids behaving as enzymes are called as ribozymes. The enzymes are mainly used for catalyzung reactions or in other words speeding the reactions. The enzymes consist of an active site for attachement of the substrate. Moreover, the enzyme catalysts are different from the inorganic catalysts.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 154)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','i and ii','ii and iii','iii and iv','All statements are false','NEW','11-154','11-112',NULL),
(91550,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding chemical reactions of enzymes. Select the correct ? \n\ni) Melting of ice into water and formation of vapours is a chemical process.\n\nii)Only formation of bonds is called as chemical reaction. \n\niii) The formation of bonds and breaking of the bonds are parts of a chemical reaction.\n\niv)The change in the shape and state both are called as physical processes.','iii and iv','The change on state and shape of a molecule is a physical process like melting of ice into water and water into vapours. Whereas, new bonds formation and breaking of bonds is termed as chemical reaction.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 154)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','iii only','i only','iii and iv','iv only','NEW','11-154','11-113',NULL),
(91551,'biology','Assertion: The reaction Ba(OH)2+H2SO4----------BaSO4+2H₂O is a chemical reaction that is inorganic in nature\n\nReason: The breakdown of the starch in presence of water is an organic reaction.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Ba(OH)2+H2SO4------BaSO4+2H₂O is a chemical reaction. It is an example of inorganic reaction. Whereas, the breakdown of starch in the presence of water is an organic reaction. The breakdown in the presence of water is called as hydrolysis.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 154)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW','11-154','11-113',NULL),
(91552,'biology','Assertion: The formation of carbonic acid is an irreversible reaction that involves the cytoplasmic enzyme catalysed by carbonic anhydrase.\n\nReason: The carbonic anhydrase is an enzyme that accelerated the chemical reaction upto 1 million times.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','The reaction of CO₂  and water gives carbonic acid. It occurs in the presence of carbonic anhydrase enzyme that speeds up the reaction upto 10 million times. It is a reversible reaction.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 155)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW','11-155','11-113',NULL),
(91553,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding chemical reactions of enzymes. Select the incorrect option?','Lactic acid and ethanol are formed under aerobic conditions.','The anaerobic conditions in skeleton mucles form lactic acid while two molecules of pyruvic acid is formed under aerobic conditions.The same pathway leads to the formation of ethanol during fermentation.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 155)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Lactic acid and ethanol are formed under aerobic conditions.','Glucose is converted to two molecules of pyruvic acid.','Lactic acid is formed under anaerobic conditions in the muscle.','In yeast, ethanol is formed during fermentation.','NEW','11-155','11-114',NULL),
(91554,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding the rates of chemical conversions,Select the correct option/s? \n\ni) The transient state structure is the necessary structure for transformation pathway.\n\nii)The activation energy is the difference in the average energy content of S from its transition state.\n\niii)Endothermic are energy gaining while exothermic are energy requiring reactions.\n\niv)The potential energy content is represented on x-axis of activation energy graph.','i and ii','the endothermic are energy requiring reactions while the exothermic are spontaneous reactions.In the activation energy graph, y-axis shows potential enegy content while x-axis shows progression of structural transformation.(Reference  :NCERT 11 Class Biology;Page 156)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii','ii and iii','i only','i and iv','NEW','11-156','11-115',NULL),
(91555,'biology','Assertion:For the transformation of substrate into product ,transient state structure must be acheived to form the final product.\n\nReason: Y-axis represents potential energy content that indicates if product is at lower level than S,the heat will be released.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The enzyme substrate complex is transformed into enzyme product complex. The structure of substrate in transformed into product.This whole pathway is called as transition state structure. In the graph,y-axis represents potentail energy while x-axis represents structural transformation progression. If P is at low level than S the reaction is exothermic.(Reference  :NCERT 11 Class Biology;Page 156; Figure 9.6)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW','11-156','11-115',NULL),
(91556,'biology','Assertion:E+S-->ES-->EP-->E+P this reaction is very important for catalysis showing steps in catalysis\n\nReason: The presence of the active site ,optimum pH and temperature are essential for catalysis to take place.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The formation of ES complex is essential for the catalysis. The reaction is E+S---ES----EP----E+P the last is fornation of enzyme product complex.(Reference  :NCERT 11 Class Biology;Page 156)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW','11-156','11-115',NULL),
(91557,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding the nature of enzyme action,Select the incorrect option/s  \n\ni)Every single enzyme consist of atleast an active site for the proper binding of the substrate. \n\nii)Enzyme substrate complex is formed by combining of an enzyme with substrate\n\niii)Enzyme substrate complex is highly reactive and the enzyme dissociates readily.\n\niv)The formation of enzyme product complex is the last step of catalysis.','iv only','Every enzyme has a substrate binding site that forms a highly reactive enzyme substrate complex.This enzyme is short-lived and dissociates into the products and unchanged enzyme. This leads to the internediate formation of enzyme product complex and the formation of product after disassociation of this complex is the final step in catalysis. (Reference  :NCERT 11 Class Biology;Page 156)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','i only','ii only','iii only','iv only','NEW','11-156','11-115',NULL),
(91558,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding the nature of enzymes action,Select the correct option/s?\n\ni)The chemical bonds of substrate are broken down to form E-P complex.\n\nii)After releasing the product the enzymes are destroyed. \n\niii)The E-P complex is formed at the temperature above the optimum value. \n\niv)The enzyme once used cannot be used again in the catalytic cycle.','i only','The active site of an enzyme is in close proximity to substrate after binding. It breaks the substrate chemical bonds and form E-P complex. the products are released and the enzymes is ready to pass through the catalytic cycle once again. (Reference  :NCERT 11 Class Biology;Page 157)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','i only','ii only','iii only','iv only','NEW','11-157','11-115',NULL),
(91559,'biology','Assertion: The first step of the catalytic cycle involves binding of a substrate to active site that induced changes in the structure of the enzyme and this binding is very important step in catalysis\n\nReason :Fitting of a substrate to the enzyme helps in the binding of substrate to the active site and this induces catalysis.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Firstly, the substrate binds to the active site of the enzyme. The binding induced changes in the changes in the enzyme shape to make it fit tightly. (Reference  :NCERT 11 Class Biology;Page 157)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW','11-157','11-115',NULL),
(91560,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding the temperature and pH of enzymes?Select the correct option/s?','Low temperature preserves enzymes in temporarily inactive state.','The temperature and pH at which enzymes fuction properly is called optimum value.At low temperatures, the enzymes are preserved in inactive state while at high temperature, enzyme activity is lost because the tertiary structure of enzymes is destroyed.(Reference  :NCERT 11 Class Biology;Page 157)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Low temperature preserves enzymes in temporarily inactive state.','At pH above the optimum value, the enzymes can function normally.','The secondary structure of enzymes is lost at low temperatures.','At optimum pH,enzymes fails to function properly.','NEW','11-157','11-116',NULL),
(91561,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding the temperature and pH of enzymes.Select the correct option/s? \n\ni)An enzyme functions in a narrow range of temperature and pH. \n\nii)The temperture at which enzyme shows minimum activity is called optimum temperature. \n\niii) The pH at which the enzyme shows maximum activity is called as optimum pH.\n\niv)The enzymes can work at the highest temperatures as well above optimum temperature without denaturing.','iii and i','The enzymes working depends on temperature and pH. the enzymes work best in a narrow range of temperature and pH.The temperature and pH at which enzymes show maximum activity is called optimum pH or temperature. They cannot work below and above optimum value. (Reference  :NCERT 11 Class Biology;Page 157)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii','ii and iii','iii and i','iii only','NEW','11-157','11-116',NULL),
(91562,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding the concentraion of enzymes.Select the incorrect option/s? \n\ni)The chemicals called inhibitors stops the chemical reactions by a process called inhibition. \n\nii)The inhibitors are very similar to those of substrate thus it can affect the enzyme acitivity.   \n\niii)Due to the close resemblance of the product and the inhibitors the enzyme activity is increased.  \n\niv)Succinate resembles malonate and causes the inhibition of succinate carboxylase.','iii and iv','The inhibitors are the chemicals those resembles the substrates. Sometimes,the inhibitors occupies the active site and halts the enzyme activity.For example, succinate dehydrogenase inhibition by malonate that resembles succinate in structure.(Reference  :NCERT 11 Class Biology;Page 158)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','i only','ii only','ii and iv','iii and iv','NEW','11-158','11-117',NULL),
(91563,'biology','Assertion: The enzymes working depends on the concentration of the products. If more products are available, then less substrates will be obtained.\n\n Reason : The enzyme activity is inhibited by the concentration of the active sites.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','The enzymes working depends on the concentration of substrates. If the amount of substrates starts decreasing then enzymes will stop working and shut off its activity.More the active sites, more is the binding to the substrate and more product is formed by enzyme catalysis(Reference  :NCERT 11 Class Biology;Page 158)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW','11-158','11-116',NULL),
(91564,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding nomenclature of enzymes. Select the right option?','The hydrolysis of glycosidic bond,ether,ester is catalyzed by hydrolases.','The enzymes are classified into six different categories.The hydrolases enzyme catalyzes hydrolysis of ester,ether,glycosidic bond,peptide etc.The oxidoreductase enzyme is also called dehydrogenases. The transferases tranfer the groups other  than hydrogen between substrates.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology;Page 158; Paragraph 1,2,3,4)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Dehydrogenases are also known as transferases.','The hydrolysis of glycosidic bond,ether,ester is catalyzed by hydrolases.','There are four different categories of enzymes.','Transferases involves transfer of hydrogen.','NEW','11-158','11-117',NULL),
(91565,'biology','Assertion:The breakdown of ether,ester and peptide bond in the presence of water is called as hydrolysis and enzymes used for catalyzation are called hydrolases.\n\nReason:The lyases form double bonds by the removal of groups through different processes excluding hydrolysis.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Hydrolases catalyzes the hydrolysis of the ether,ester and several other bonds while the lyases involves the removal of a group by mechanisms other than hydrolysis.This results in the formation of double bonds. (Reference  :NCERT 11 Class Biology;Page 158;Paragraph 4,5)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW','11-158','11-117',NULL),
(91566,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding nomenclature of enzymes. Select the right option?\nSelect the incorrect statements\n\ni)The enzyme leading to the formation of a double bond by hydrolysis is lyases\n\nii) The enzyme responsible for the attachment of two different atoms within a molecule is ligases. \n\niii)Hydrolases and transferases results in the formation of double bonds.\n\niv) The enzymes responsible for the formation of geometric isomers only are isomerases.','All of them','Lyases: Enzymes that catalyse removal of groups from substrates by mechanisms other than hydrolysis leaving double bonds.\nLigases: Enzymes catalysing the linking together of 2 compounds, e.g., enzymes which catalyse joining of C-O, C-S, C-N, P-O etc. bonds.\nHydrolases: Enzymes catalysing hydrolysis of ester, ether, peptide, glycosidic, C-C, C-halide or P-N bonds.\nTransferases: Enzymes catalysing a transfer of a group, G (other than hydrogen) between a pair of substrate S and S’.\nIsomerases: Includes all enzymes catalysing inter-conversion of optical, geometric or positional isomers.','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','i and ii','ii and iii','iii and iv','All of them','NEW','11-158','11-117',NULL),
(91567,'biology','Assertion:Cofactors are the non-protein constituents and these compounds helps in increasing the catalytic activity of enzyme\n\nReason:An enzyme without the presence of a cofactor, can function normally without losing its activity','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','The non-protein part of an enzyme is called cofactor that enhances activity of enzyme.It is also observed that without their presence, the enzyme cannot fuction properly.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 159;Paragraph 3,4)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW','11-159','11-118',NULL),
(91568,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding cofactors. Select the right option/s?\n\ni)Cofactors are the protein constituents that helps in increasing the activation energy.  \n\nii)To make ezymes catalytically active, cofactors are used. \n\niii)Coenzymes are organic in nature and are tightly bound to the apoenzyme\n\niv)Haem is an example of the metal ion.','ii only','Cofactors are the non-protein constituents those help in making enzyme catalytically active.  Prosthetic groups are the organic compounds that are tightly bound to the apoenzymes. Moreover,heam is an example of prosthetic group.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 154;Paragraph 1,2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii','ii only','iii and iv','All statements are true','NEW','11-159','11-118',NULL),
(51054,'chemistry','The compressibility factor (Z) for a real gas at a low pressure is:','Z=1-Pb/RT','(P+a/Vm2 )(Vm-b) = RT [Van der waals equation of state]\nVm-b ≈ Vm\nSo the equation becomes (P+a/Vm2 )Vm= RT\n⇒PVm+a/Vm = RT\nDivide all terms by RT\nPVm/RT+a/VmRT = RT/RT\nPVm/RT = 1- a/VmRT\nZ = 1- a/VmRT [Z = PVm/RT]\nThe answer is d\nReference: (JEE Main 2020 Exam)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Z = 1+RT/Pb','Z=1-Pb/RT','Z = 1-a/VmRT','Z=1+Pb/RT','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91569,'biology','Cosider the following statements about the biomolecules. Choose the correct option?','There are 20 different amino acids which include acidic , basic and neutral amino acids.','The amino acids are linked through peptide bonds in a protein or peptide molecule. The five types of nitrogen bases in a nucleotide are adenine,guanine,cytosine,thymidine and uridine. Moreover, oils and fats undergoes esterification to form glycerol.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 159,160)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','The amino acids are linked together by phosphodiester bond in a protein.','There are 20 different amino acids which include acidic , basic and neutral amino acids.','There are four types of nucleotides that are adenine,guanine,cytosine and uracil.','Oils undergoes esterification process leading to the formation of monosaccharide.','NEW','11-159','11-118',NULL),
(91570,'biology','Assertion:Polymers are different for different biomolecules, polymers of proteins are amino acids, polymers of nucleic acids are nucleotides\n\nReason : Amino acids are linked by peptide bonds in the formation of proteins and nucleic acids are linked in the formation of nucleotides','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','The building blocks makes the backbone of the compounds or macromecules.Similarly, amino acids are building blocks of proteins while nucleotides are the ones that make up nucleic acids.(Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 159; Paragraph 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW','11-159','11-118',NULL),
(91571,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding the biomolecules.Select the correct option/s?','Relative abundance of carbon and hydrogen is higher in living organisms','An examination has revealed that the relative abundance of carbon and hydrogen is higher in living organisms than earth\'s crust.The elements present living organisms are also present in earths crust. (Reference  : NCERT 11 Class Biology; Page 142)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Relative abundance of carbon and hydrogen is higher in living organisms','All elements those exist in humans are not present in earths crust.','The carbon is present in large amounts in the human body.','Carbon and hydrogen have higher relative abundance in earths crust','NEW','11-142','11-104',NULL),
(91572,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Biomolecules, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Different organisms are made up of different compounds\n\n2) The percentage of carbon found in a living organism is higher than the earth crust\'s.\n\n3) The abundance of elements found in an organism is defined in per unit mass\n\n4) Analysis has shown different element lists for both living organism and the earth crust','Only 2 and 3 are correct','All living organisms are made of the same chemicals\nThe relative abundance of carbon and hydrogen with respect to other elements is higher in any living organism than in earth’s crust\nWe obtain a list of elements like carbon, hydrogen, O₂ and several others and their respective content per unit mass of a living tissue\nIf we perform such an analysis on a plant tissue, animal tissue or a microbial paste, we obtain a list of elements. If the same analysis is performed on a piece of earth’s crust as an example of non-living matter, we obtain a similar list\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 142, para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-142','11-104',NULL),
(91573,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer \n\nAssertion: Elemental analysis of a sample from the earth\'s crust reveals similar composition when compared to any living organism \n\nReason: All organisms are made up of same chemicals','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If the same analysis is performed on a piece of earth’s crust as an example of non-living matter, we obtain a similar list. All the elements present in a sample of earth’s crust are also present in a sample of living tissue\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 142, para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-142','11-104',NULL),
(91574,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Analysing Chemical Composition, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Chemical analysis allows us to discover the type of organic compound in a sample \n\n2) Thousands of organic compounds are found in the acid-insoluble pool\n\n3) In chemical analysis, the sample tissue is ground in trichloroacetic acid\n\n4) The filtrate obtained after being strained is known as the acid-soluble pool technically.','Only 1, 3 and 4 are correct','To get an answer, one has to perform a chemical analysis.\nScientists have found thousands of organic compounds in the acid-soluble pool.\nWe can take any living tissue (a vegetable or a piece of liver, etc.) and grind it in trichloroacetic acid (Cl3CCOOH) using a mortar and a pestle\nIf we were to strain this through a cheesecloth or cotton we would obtain two fractions. One is called the filtrate or more technically, the acid-soluble pool, and the second, the retentate or the acid-insoluble fraction\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 142, para 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1, 3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-142','11-104',NULL),
(91575,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n \nAssertion: It is important to strain the thick slurry obtained after grinding the sample living tissue\n\nReason: We can obtain two fractions which are the filtrate and retentate','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','We obtain a thick slurry. If we were to strain this through a cheesecloth or cotton we would obtain two fractions. One is called the filtrate or more technically, the acid-soluble pool, and the second, the retentate or the acid-insoluble fraction\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 142, para 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-142','11-104',NULL),
(91576,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Analysing Chemical Composition, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Biomolecules are all the carbon compounds found in living tissues\n\n2) O₂ is the most abundant element in both the earth crust and living organisms\n\n3) Living organisms are only made up of organic compounds \n\n4) The percentage of silicone found in a living organism is higher than the earth crust','Only 1 and 2 are correct','All the carbon compounds that we get from living tissues can be called ‘biomolecules’\nRefer table 9.1 from NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 142, O₂ has the highest percentage weight in both the earth crust and living organism\nHowever, living organisms have also got inorganic elements and compounds in them\nRefer table 9.1 from NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 142, Silicone in the earth crust is around 27.7 percent while in human it is negligible\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 143, para 1 )','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-143','11-105',NULL),
(91577,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct statement\n\nAssertion: Earth\'s crust has biomolecules\n\nReason: The earth crust contains carbon compounds only','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','All the carbon compounds that we get from living tissues can be called ‘biomolecules’. The earth  crust is not a living thing therefore it is not suitable to apply the term \'biomolecules in its case\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 143, para 1 )','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-143','11-105',NULL),
(91578,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Analysing Chemical Composition, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Sodium Chloride is a type of compound\n\n2) Phosphate is acid-insoluble\n\n3) When a living tissue is completely combusted, the carbon compounds are reduced.\n\n4) Elemental analysis provides the elemental composition of living tissues in form of elements only','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Sodium Chloride (NaCl) is a type of compound\nInorganic compounds like sulphate,phosphate, etc., are also seen in the acid-soluble fraction.\nIf the tissue is fully burnt, all the carbon compounds are oxidised to gaseous form (CO₂ , water vapour) and are removed.\nElemental analysis gives elemental composition of living tissues in the form of hydrogen, oxygen, chlorine, carbon etc\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 143, para 1 )','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-143','11-105',NULL),
(91579,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Analysis for compounds allows us to classify amino acids, nucleotides etc\n\nReason: It gives elemental composition of living tissues','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Analysis for compounds gives an idea of organic and inorganic constituents in living tissues.From a biological point of view, we shall classify them into amino acids, nucleotide bases, fatty acids etc.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 143, para 1 )','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-143','11-105',NULL),
(91580,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Amino Acids, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Amino acids are inorganic compounds\n\n2) The type of amino acid depends on the nature of the R group present\n\n3) The acidic group is located on the same carbon as the amino group\n\n4) They are four occupied valency positions in amino acid','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','Amino acids are organic compounds \nBased on the nature of R group there are many amino acids.\nAmino acids are organic compounds containing an amino group and an acidic group as substituents on the same carbon\nThere are four substituent groups occupying the four valency positions\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 143, para 2 )','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-143','11-105',NULL),
(91581,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Substituted methane are also known as α-amino acids\n\nReason: It contains an amino group and an acidic group as substituents on the opposite carbon, α-carbon','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Amino acids are organic compounds containing an amino group and an acidic group as substituents on the same carbon i.e., the α-carbon. Hence, they are called α-amino acids. They are substituted methanes\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 143, para 2 )','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-143','11-105',NULL),
(91582,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Amino Acids, choose the correct statements\n\n1)There are only 20 types of amino acids that make up proteins\n\n2) Glycine is a type of amino acid \n\n3) Alanine contains hydroxy methyl as its R group\n\n4) Serine is not a proteinaceous amino acid','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Those amino acids which occur in proteins are only of twenty types\nThe R group in these proteinaceous amino acids could be a hydrogen (the amino acid is called glycine), a methyl group (alanine), hydroxy methyl (serine)\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 144, para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(311710,'biology','Assertion : A major advantage of tissue culture is protoplast fusion. \n\nReason : A hybrid is formed by the fusion of naked protoplasts of two plants. [2017]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','An important technique of tissue culture,\nsomatic hybridization results in the\nproduction of somatic hybrid plants. Two\ndifferent plant varieties each with a\ndesirable character can be made to undergo\nprotoplast fusion, which further can be\ngrown into a new plant.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','If the Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','If both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91583,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Amino Acids, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Structure of amino acid is unchangeable, based on fixed charges on the atoms in its structure\n\n2) Tyrosine is a type of aromatic amino acid\n\n3) One of the property of amino acids is the ionizable nature of –NH₂ and –COOH groups\n\n4) The chemical and physical properties of amino acids depends solely on the R group','Only 2 and 3 are correct','In solutions of different pH, the structure of amino acids changes.\nSimilarly, there are aromatic amino acids (tyrosine, phenylalanine, tryptophan)\nA particular property of amino acids is the ionizable nature of –NH₂ and –COOH groups\nThe chemical and physical properties of amino acids are essentially of the amino, carboxyl and the R functional groups\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 144, para 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91584,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: In solutions of different pH, the structure of amino acids changes.\n\nReason: The chemical and physical properties of amino acids are essentially of the amino, carboxyl and the R functional groups, and they form ions with respect to the pH.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','A particular property of amino acids is the ionizable nature of –NH₂ and –COOH groups. Hence in solutions of different pH, the structure of amino acids changes.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 144, para 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91585,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Lipids, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Lipids are categorized into saturated and unsaturated lipids\n\n2) Unsaturated means there is a presence of one or more double bond\n\n3) A carboxyl group attached to ethyl can form a fatty acid\n\n4) Palmitic acid is a type of fatty acid in lipids.','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','Fatty acids could be saturated (without double bond) or unsaturated (with one or more C=C double bonds).Unsaturated means with one or more C=C double bonds\nA fatty acid has a carboxyl group attached to an R group. The R group could be a methyl (–CH3), or ethyl (–C2H5 ) or higher number of –CH₂ groups (1 carbon to 19 carbons)\nPalmitic acid has a R group of 16 carbons including carboxyl carbon\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 144, para 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','!,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91586,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Arachidonic acid is soluble in water\n\nReason: Lipids are soluble in water','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Lipids are generally water insoluble therefore Arachidonic acid is insoluble too\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 144, para 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91587,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Lipids, choose the correct statements \n\n1) Oils have higher melting point than fats\n\n2) Lecithin is a type of glycerol\n\n3) Phospholipids can be found in cell membranes\n\n4) Glycerol is also known as trihydroxy propane','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Oils have lower melting point\nSome lipids have phosphorous and a phosphorylated organic compound in them. These are phospholipids. Lecithin is one example.\nThey are found in cell membrane\nAnother simple lipid is glycerol which is trihydroxy propane\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 144, para 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91588,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Oils remain in liquid form at low temperatures\n\nReason: It has a low melting point','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Oils have lower melting point  and hence remain as oil in winters\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 144, para 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91589,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Nitrogen Bases, choose the correct statements\n\n1) There are 5 types of nitrogen bases\n\n2) Uracil is a type of nucleoside\n\n3) DNA is a polymer of nucleosides primarily\n\n4) Phosphate group is found in nucleotides','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Some of these are nitrogen bases – adenine, guanine, cytosine, uracil, and thymine.\nUracil is a nitrogen base\nNucleic acids like DNA and RNA consist of nucleotides only\nA phosphate group is also found esterified to the sugar they are called nucleotides\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 144, para 4)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91590,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Adenylic acid can be found in DNA \n\nReason: DNA functions as a genetic material','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Adenylic acid, thymidylic acid, guanylic acid, uridylic acid and cytidylic acid are nucleotides. Nucleic acids like DNA and RNA consist of nucleotides only.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 144, para 4)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-144','11-106',NULL),
(91591,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Vinblastin is a secondary metabolite\n\nReason: It is categorised as drug and does not belong to any of the primary metabolites','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Vinblastin is a drug hence a secondary metabolite. It is not considered as primary metabolites because it is not sugar, amino acid, lipid or nucleic acid \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 146, para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-146','11-108',NULL),
(91592,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Biomacromolecules, choose the correct statements\n\n1)The compounds found in the acid-soluble pool weights between 18 to 800 daltons approximately\n\n2) Polysaccharides are insoluble in acid \n\n3) Proteins are biomacromolecules\n\n4) Micromolecules have weight of more than 1000 daltons','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Compounds found in the acid soluble pool have molecular weights ranging from 18 to around 800 daltons (Da) approximately\nThe acid insoluble fraction, has only four types of organic compounds i.e., proteins, nucleic acids, polysaccharides and lipids.\nThose which are found in the acid insoluble fraction are called macromolecules or biomacromolecules\nThose which have molecular weights less than one thousand dalton and are usually referred to as micromolecules\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 146, para 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-146','11-108',NULL),
(91593,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: All sugars are biomacromolecules\n\nReason: They are found in the acid-insoluble fraction','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','The acid insoluble fraction, has only four types of organic compounds i.e., proteins, nucleic acids, polysaccharides and lipids. Those which are found in the acid insoluble fraction are called macromolecules or biomacromolecules\nOnly polysaccharides are biomacromolecules\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 146, para 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-146','11-108',NULL),
(91594,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Biomacromolecules, choose the correct statements\n\n1) All biomacromolecules weight more than ten thousand daltons\n\n2) Lipid is not a polymeric substance\n\n3) There are only 4 types of organic substances that can be found in the acid-insoluble fraction\n\n4) Micromolecules are found in the acid-insoluble fraction','Only 2 and 3 are correct','These classes of compounds with the exception of lipids, have molecular weights in the range of ten thousand daltons and above\nThe molecules in the insoluble fraction with the exception of lipids are polymeric substances\nThe acid insoluble fraction, has only four types of organic compounds i.e., proteins, nucleic acids, polysaccharides and lipids\nThose which are found in the acid insoluble fraction are called macromolecules or biomacromolecules.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 146, para 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-146','11-108',NULL),
(91601,'chemistry','Hydrogen peroxide has_______ structure.','Nonplanar','Hydrogen peroxide has a non-planar structure\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 294; Paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrogen peroxide-Structure',NULL,'1','fillup','Non,pla,nar,pol',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91602,'chemistry','The dihedral angle in solid hydrogen peroxide is _______.','90.2degree','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 294; Paragraph no.1)\ndiagram(ne0906)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrogen peroxide-Structure',NULL,'1','fillup','90.2,deg,ree,89.2',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91603,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Lipids appear in the acid-insoluble fraction \n\nReason: Its molecular weight does not exceed 800 Da','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Lipid\'s molecular weight do not exceed 800 Da. When we grind a tissue, we are disrupting the cell structure. Cell membrane and other membranes are broken into pieces, and form vesicles which are not water soluble. Therefore, these membrane fragments in the form of vesicles get separated along with the acid insoluble pool and hence in the macromolecular fraction. Lipids are not strictly macromolecules.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 147, para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-147','11-109',NULL),
(91604,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Proteins, choose the correct statements\n\n1) GLUT-4 is a type of protein that allows transportation of glucose into cell\n\n2) The monomer of protein is amino acid\n\n3) Amino acids in proteins are linked by polypeptide bond\n\n4) Collagen is a type of protein','Only 1,2 and 4 are correct','Refer to Table 9.5 from NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 147, GLUT-4 enables glucose transport into cells and is a protein\nEach protein is a polymer of amino acids.\nProteins are polypeptides. They are linear chains of amino acids linked by peptide bonds not polypeptide bonds \nCollagen is a protein and acts as an intercellular ground substance \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 147, para 4 and 5)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 4 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-147','11-109',NULL),
(91605,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: The monomer of insulin is amino acid\n\nReason: Insulin is a protein','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Insulin is an enzyme where enzymes are proteins. Therefore, insulin is made up of amino acids linked by peptide bonds\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 147, para 5)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-147','11-109',NULL),
(91606,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Proteins, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Proteins can be heteropolymers or homopolymers\n\n2) Our body can produce both essential and non-essential amino acids\n\n3) Essential proteins can be obtained from dietary proteins\n\n4) Certain proteins function to fight infectious organisms','Only 3 and 4 are correct','A protein is a heteropolymer and not a homopolymer. A homopolymer has only one type of monomer repeating ‘n’ number of times.\nAmino acids can be essential or non-essential. The latter are those which our body can make, while we get essential amino acids through our diet/food.\nDietary proteins are the source of essential amino acids.\nProteins carry out many functions in living organisms, some transport nutrients across cell membrane, some fight infectious organisms, etc\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 147, para 5)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-147','11-109',NULL),
(91607,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: We need to consume a lot of foods containing non-essential amino acids.\n\nReason: Our bodies can naturally produce the essential amino acids while the non-essential can only be obtained via dietary proteins','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Dietary proteins are the source of essential amino acids. Therefore, amino acids can be essential or non-essential. The latter are those which our body can make, while we get essential amino acids through our diet/food.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 147, para 5)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-147','11-109',NULL),
(91608,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Polysaccharides, choose the correct answer\n\n1) Polysaccharides are carbohydrates\n\n2) Many monosaccharides are linked together to form a polysaccharide\n\n3) Cellulose is a heteropolymer\n\n4) Polysaccharides are found in the acid-soluble pool','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Polysaccharides are long chains of sugars, they are carbohydrates\nThey are threads (literally a cotton thread) containing different monosaccharides as building blocks.\nCellulose is a polymeric polysaccharide consisting of only one type of monosaccharide i.e., glucose. Cellulose is a homopolymer.\nThe acid insoluble pellet also has polysaccharides (carbohydrates) as another class of macromolecules\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 148, para 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-148','11-110',NULL),
(91609,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below. Choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Cellulose is a homopolymer\n\nReason: It is made up of glucose only','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Cellulose is a polymeric polysaccharide consisting of only one type of monosaccharide i.e., glucose. Cellulose is a homopolymer. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 148, para 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-148','11-110',NULL),
(91610,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Polysaccharides, choose the correct answer\n\n1) Starch presents in both animals and plants\n\n2) Glycogen is a store house of energy in animals\n\n3) Starch can form helical secondary structures\n\n4) Glycogen has a reducing end and a non-reducing end','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','Starch is a variant of this but present as a store house of energy in plant tissues. Animals have another variant called glycogen.\nStarch forms helical secondary structures. In fact, starch can hold I2 molecules in the helical portion.\nIn a polysaccharide chain (say glycogen), the right end is called the reducing end and the left end is called the non-reducing end.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 148, para 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-148','11-110',NULL),
(91611,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Cellulose cannot hold I2\n\nReason: Cellulose is a homopolymer','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Cellulose does not contain complex helices and hence cannot hold I2\n\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 148, para 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-148','11-110',NULL),
(91612,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Polysaccharides, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Chitin makes up the exoskeleton of arthropods\n\n2) Inulin is a polymer of sucrose\n\n3) The starch-I2 is yellow in colour.\n\n4) Plants\' cell walls are made up of cellulose','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Exoskeletons of arthropods, for example, have a complex polysaccharide called chitin\nInulin is a polymer of fructose. \nThe starch-I2 is blue in colour\nPlant cell walls are made of cellulose.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 148, para 2 and pg 149, para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-148','11-110',NULL),
(91613,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Chitin is a homopolymer\n\nReason: It is a complex polysaccharide','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Exoskeletons of arthropods, for example, have a complex polysaccharide called chitin. These complex polysaccharides are mostly homopolymers.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 149, para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-149','11-111',NULL),
(91614,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Nucleic Acids, choose the correct statements\n\n1) A nucleotide is made up of 2 chemically distinct compounds\n\n2) One of the compounds in nucleotide is a polysaccharide\n\n3) Nucleic acids are polynucleotides\n\n4) The true macromolecular fraction of any living tissue is made up of polynucleotides,polysaccharides and polypeptides','Only 3 and 4 are correct','A nucleotide has three chemically distinct components\nOne is a heterocyclic compound, the second is a monosaccharide and the third a phosphoric acid or phosphate. It is a monosaccharide not polysaccharide\nThe other type of macromolecule that one would find in the acid-insoluble fraction of any living tissue is the nucleic acid. These are polynucleotides\nTogether with polysaccharides and polypeptides these comprise the true macromolecular fraction of any living tissue or cell\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 149, para 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-149','11-111',NULL),
(91615,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Nucleic acid is a polynucleotide\n\nReason: It is found in the acid-insoluble fraction','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The other type of macromolecule that one would find in the acid insoluble fraction of any living tissue is the nucleic acid. These are polynucleotides. For nucleic acids, the building block is a nucleotide. \nNucleic acids are made up of more than two nucleotides\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 149, para 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-149','11-111',NULL),
(91616,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Nucleic Acids, choose the correct statements\n\n1) A phosphorus is needed to form a nucleotide\n\n2) There are 5 nitrogenous bases in total\n\n3) Uracil is a pyrimidine\n\n4) Ribose is found in both DNA and RNA','Only 2 and 3 are correct','A nucleotide has three chemically distinct components. One is a heterocyclic compound, the second is a monosaccharide and the third a phosphoric acid or phosphate\nThe heterocyclic compounds in nucleic acids are the nitrogenous bases named adenine, guanine, uracil, cytosine, and thymine.\nAdenine and Guanine are substituted purines while the rest are substituted pyrimidines\nA nucleic acid containing deoxyribose is called deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) while that which contains ribose is called ribonucleic acid (RNA)\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 149, para 2 and 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-149','11-111',NULL),
(91617,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: DNA differs from RNA by their skeletal heterocyclic rings\n\nReason: DNA has purines while RNA has pyrimidines','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','The skeletal heterocyclic ring is called as purine and pyrimidine respectively. However both of these are found in DNA and RNA. DNA and RNA differs by the type of sugar they have. \nThe sugar found in polynucleotides is either ribose (a monosaccharide pentose) or 2’ deoxyribose. A nucleic acid containing deoxyribose is called deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) while that which contains ribose is called\nribonucleic acid (RNA).\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 149, para 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-149','11-111',NULL),
(91618,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Structure of Proteins, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Protein structure can be described in 4 different levels \n\n2) Proteins contain only one type of amino acid\n\n3) Proteins are heteropolymers\n\n4) Primary structure of protein is described as a linear structure','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Biologists describe the protein structure at four levels.\nProteins are made up of more than 1 type of amino acids\nProteins are heteropolymers containing strings of amino acids\nThe primary structure is imagined as a linear structure\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 149, para 4)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-149','11-111',NULL),
(91619,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Primary structure is the simplest form of protein\n\nReason: It is imagined as a line without folding','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The sequence of amino acids i.e., the positional information in a protein – which is the first amino acid, which is second, and so on – is called the primary structure. It is imagined as a line\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 149, para 4)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-149','11-111',NULL),
(91620,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Structure of Proteins, choose the correct statements\n\n1)The first amino acid is known as the C-terminal amino acid\n\n2) In proteins, only left-handed helices are observed\n\n3)Tertiary structure of a protein gives a 3D view\n\n4) There are 2 types of secondary structure of a protein','Only 3 and 4 are correct','The first amino acid is also called as N-terminal amino acid. The last amino acid is called the C-terminal amino acid\nIn proteins, only right handed helices are observed\nThe tertiary structure of a protein  gives us a 3-dimensional view of a protein\nThe secondary structure of protein can be seen in alpha-helix structure or beta-pleated sheet\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 150, para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-150','11-112',NULL),
(91621,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Tertiary structure of protein gives us a three dimensional view of the protein\n\nReason: It contains disulphide bonds','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','In addition, the long protein chain is also folded upon itself like a hollow woolen ball, giving rise to the tertiary structure.This gives us a 3-dimensional view of a protein. And the bond which is present in tertiary structure is disulfide bonds, hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, and hydrophobic interactions. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 150, para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-150','11-112',NULL),
(91622,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Structure of Proteins, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Haemoglobin is an example of quaternary structure of protein\n\n2) All the subunits in haemoglobin are similar to each other\n\n3) All proteins are assembled by more than one polypeptide \n\n4) Adult human haemoglobin consists of 4 subunits','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Haemoglobin is a quaternary structure of protein\nTwo of these are identical to each other. Hence, two subunits of α type and two subunits of β type together constitute the human haemoglobin\nSome proteins are an assembly of more than one polypeptide or subunits. Proteins can also appear in less complex form.\nAdult human haemoglobin consists of 4 subunits. Two of these are identical to each other.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 150, para 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-150','11-112',NULL),
(91623,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: There are two subunits that make up the human haemoglobin\n\nReason: haemoglobin comprises one alpha subunit and one beta subunit','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Adult human haemoglobin consists of 4 subunits. Two of these are identical to each other. Hence, two subunits of α type and two subunits of β type together constitute the human haemoglobin\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 150, para 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-150','11-112',NULL),
(91624,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Nature of Bond Linking Monomers in a Polymer, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Monosaccharides in a polysaccharide are linked by hydrogen bonds\n\n2) Bonding between monosaccharides are formed by hydrolysis\n\n3) Bonding is formed between two carbon atoms of two adjacent monosaccharides\n\n4) Dehydration is involved in the formation of peptide bonds between amino acids in protein','Only 3 and 4 are correct','In a polysaccharide the individual monosaccharides are linked by a glycosidic bond\nThis bond is also formed by dehydration\nThis bond is formed between two carbon atoms of two adjacent monosaccharides.\nIn a polypeptide or a protein, amino acids are linked by a peptide bond which is formed when the carboxyl (-COOH) group of one amino acid reacts with the amino (-NH2) group of the next amino acid with the elimination of a water moiety (the process is called dehydration)\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 151, para 1 )','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(91625,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Peptide bond is formed between amino acids to form a polypeptide\n\nReason: Addition of water takes place between amino acids','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','In a polypeptide or a protein, amino acids are linked by a peptide bond which is formed when the carboxyl (-COOH) group of one amino acid reacts with the amino (-NH2) group of the next amino acid with the elimination of a water moiety (the process is called dehydration).\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 151, para 1 )','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(91626,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Nature of Bond Linking Monomers in a Polymer, choose the correct statements\n\n1) The bond between nucleic acids is known as a phosphodiester bond \n\n2) The bond between the phosphate and hydroxyl group of sugar is an ester bond\n\n3) Glycosidic bond is formed by hydrolysis\n\n4) In a nucleic acid a phosphate moiety links the phosphate of a nucleotide to the phosphate of next nucleotide','Only 1 and 2 are correct','The bond between the phosphate and hydroxyl group of sugar is an ester bond. As there is one such ester bond on either side, it is called phosphodiester bond\nThe bond between the phosphate and hydroxyl group of sugar is an ester bond.\nIn a polysaccharide the individual monosaccharides are linked by a glycosidic bond. This bond is also formed by dehydration.\nIn a nucleic acid a phosphate moiety links the 3’-carbon of one sugar of one nucleotide to the 5’-carbon of the sugar of the succeeding nucleotide\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 151, para 1 )','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(91627,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: The bond between nucleic acids is known as phosphodiester bond\n\nReason: In a nucleic acid a phosphate moiety links the 3’-carbon of one sugar of one nucleotide to the 5’-carbon of the sugar of the succeeding nucleotide','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','In a nucleic acid a phosphate moiety links the 3’-carbon of one sugar of one nucleotide to the 5’-carbon of the sugar of the succeeding nucleotide. The bond between the phosphate and hydroxyl group of sugar is an ester bond. As there is one such ester bond on either side, it is called phosphodiester bond\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 151, para 1 )','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(91628,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Nature of Bond Linking Monomers in a Polymer, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Nucleic acids exhibit a wide variety of tertiary structures\n\n2) DNA is a secondary structure\n\n3) The two strands of polynucleotides are antiparallel\n\n4) The nitrogenous bases are facing inside of the backbone','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','Nucleic acids exhibit a wide variety of secondary structures.\nFor example, one of the secondary structures exhibited by DNA is the famous Watson-Crick model\nThe two strands of polynucleotides are antiparallel i.e., run in the opposite direction\nThe nitrogen bases are projected more or less perpendicular to this backbone but face inside\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 151, para 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(91629,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: The DNA exists as a double helix structure\n\nReason: DNA has two strands that runs opposite to each other','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','DNA exists as a double helix. The two strands of polynucleotides are antiparallel i.e., run in the opposite direction.A and G of one strand compulsorily base pairs with T and C, respectively, on the other strand\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 151, para 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(91630,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Nature of Bond Linking Monomers in a Polymer, choose the correct statements\n\n1) The backbone of DNA is formed by sugar and nitrogenous base\n\n2) There are two hydrogen bonds between G and C \n\n3) One full turn of the helical strand would involve 10 base pairs\n\n4) Each strand appears like a helical staircase','Only 3 and 4 are correct','The backbone is formed by the sugar-phosphate-sugar chain.\nThere are two hydrogen bonds between A and T and three hydrogen bonds between G and C\nOne full turn of the helical strand would involve ten steps or ten base pairs\nEach strand appears like a helical staircase\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 151, para 2 and pg 152, para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-151',NULL,NULL),
(91631,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: One full turn of the helical strand would involve 5 base pairs \n\nReason: At each step of ascent, the strand turns 72 degree','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Each strand appears like a helical staircase. Each step of ascent is represented by a pair of bases. At each step of\nascent, the strand turns 36°. One full turn of the helical strand would involve ten steps or ten base pairs.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 152, para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-152',NULL,NULL),
(91632,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Concept of Metabolism, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Biomolecules can be changed into other biomolecules\n\n2) Metabolism is the chemical process of breaking and making of biomolecules  in living organisms\n\n3) Majority of metabolic processes are isolated on its own \n\n4) Hydrolysis of glycosidic bond in a disaccharide is not a metabolic reaction','Only 1 and 2 are correct','One of the greatest discoveries ever made was the observation that all these biomolecules have\na turn over. This means that they are constantly being changed into some other biomolecules and also made from some other biomolecules\nThis breaking and making is through chemical reactions constantly occuring in living organisms. Together all these chemical reactions are called metabolism\nMajority of these metabolic reactions do not occur in isolation but are always linked to some other reactions\nA few examples for such metabolic transformations are: removal of CO  from amino acids making an amino acid into an amine, removal of amino group in a nucleotide base; hydrolysis of a glycosidic bond in a disaccharide\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 152, para 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-152',NULL,NULL),
(91633,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Transformation of biomolecules can take place \n\nReason: Biomolecules are present in a certain concentration','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','They are constantly being changed into some other biomolecules and also made from some other biomolecules. This breaking and making is through chemical reactions constantly occuring in living organisms. Together all these chemical reactions are called metabolism. Each of the metabolic reactions results in the transformation of biomolecules\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 152, para 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-152',NULL,NULL),
(91634,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Concept of Metabolism, choose the correct statements\n\n1)All metabolic pathways are linear\n\n2) Metabolite flow is called the dynamic state of body constituents\n\n3) Metabolic reactions are all catalysed\n\n4) Flow of metabolites through metabolic pathway does not have a definite rate','Only 2 and 3 are correct','These pathways are either linear or circular. These pathways crisscross each other\nThis metabolite flow is called the dynamic state of body constituents.\nAnother feature of these metabolic reactions is that every chemical reaction is a catalysed reaction. There is no uncatalysed metabolic conversion in living systems.\nFlow of metabolites through metabolic pathway has a definite rate and direction like automobile traffic\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 152, para 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-152',NULL,NULL),
(91635,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Enzymes are involved in all metabolic processes \n\nReason: Enzymes has catalytic power and all metabolic reactions are catalysed','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','There is no uncatalysed metabolic conversion in living systems.The catalysts which hasten the rate of a given metabolic conversation are also proteins. These proteins with catalytic power are named enzymes.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 152, para 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-152',NULL,NULL),
(91636,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding  Metabolic Basis for Living, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Acetic acid to cholesterol is an anabolic reaction\n\n2) Catabolic pathway involves converting simple structure into a complex structure\n\n3) Assembly of a protein from amino acids consumes energy .\n\n4) Biosynthetic pathway is another name for anabolic pathway','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Metabolic pathways can lead to a more complex structure from a simpler structure (for example, acetic acid becomes cholesterol). It involves the anabolic pathway.\nThe latter constitute degradation and hence are called catabolic pathways\nAssembly of a protein from amino acids requires energy input.\nThe former cases are called biosynthetic pathways or anabolic pathways\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 153, para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-153',NULL,NULL),
(91637,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Glycolysis is a biosynthetic pathway\n\nReason: It requires energy input','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','On the other hand, catabolic pathways lead to the release of energy. For example, when glucose is degraded to lactic acid in our skeletal muscle, energy is liberated. This metabolic pathway from glucose to lactic acid which occurs in 10 metabolic steps is called glycolysis. Biosynthetic pathways are anabolic pathways\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 153, para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-153',NULL,NULL),
(91638,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Metabolic basis for living, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Glycolysis occurs in 12 steps\n\n2) Conversion of glucose to lactic acid is a catabolic reaction\n\n3) Energy liberated during degradation is stored in the form of chemical bonds \n\n4) ADP is used as energy currency in living systems','Only 2 and 3 are correct','This metabolic pathway from glucose to lactic acid which occurs in 10 metabolic steps is called glycolysis not 12 steps\n Metabolic pathways can lead to a simpler structure from a complex structure (for example, glucose becomes lactic acid in our skeletal muscle) known as catabolic pathway \nLiving organisms have learnt to trap this energy liberated during degradation and store it in the form of chemical bonds\nThe most important form of energy currency in living systems is the bond energy in a chemical called adenosine triphosphate (ATP).\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 153, para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-153',NULL,NULL),
(91639,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: The energy liberated during glycolysis will be released in the form of heat \n\nReason: It is not useful to the living organism\'s system','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','This metabolic pathway from glucose to lactic acid which occurs in 10 metabolic steps is called glycolysis. Living organisms have learnt to trap this energy liberated during degradation and store it in the form of chemical bonds. As and when needed, this bond energy is utilised for biosynthetic, osmotic and mechanical work that we perform\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 153, para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-153',NULL,NULL),
(91640,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Living State, choose the correct statements\n\n1) The steady state is a non-equilibrium state\n\n2) Living state and metabolism are not synonymous\n\n3) Blood concentration of glucose in a normal healthy individual is 4.2 nanograms/L– 6.1 nanograms/L\n\n4) Metabolism provides a mechanism for the production of energy.','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Any chemical or physical process moves spontaneously to equilibrium. The steady state is a non-equilibrium state.\nThe living state and metabolism are synonymous. Without metabolism there cannot be a living state\nThe blood concentration of glucose in a normal healthy individual is 4.2 mmol/L– 6.1 mmol/L, it is not calculated in the unit of nanogram\nMetabolism provides a mechanism for the production of energy. Hence the living state and metabolism are synonymous\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 153, para 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-153',NULL,NULL),
(91641,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Living state must be a non-equilibrium steady state\n\nReason: Living organisms work continuously','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','As living organisms work continuously, they cannot afford to reach equilibrium. Hence the living state is a non-equilibrium steadystate to be able to perform work; living process is a constant effort to prevent falling into equilibrium. Systems at equilibrium cannot perform work.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 153, para 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-153',NULL,NULL),
(91642,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Enzymes, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Ribozymes are nucleic acids that behave like enzymes\n\n2) Enzymes have primary, secondary and tertiary structures\n\n3) All enzymes are proteins\n\n4) Enzyme catalyst are very much similar to inorganic catalysts','Only 1 and 2 are correct','There are some nucleic acids that behave like enzymes. These are called ribozymes\nAn enzyme like any protein has a primary structure, i.e., amino acid sequence of the protein. An enzyme like any protein has the secondary and the tertiary structure\nAlmost all enzymes are proteins with some exception\nEnzyme catalysts differ from inorganic catalysts in many ways\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 154, para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-154','11-112',NULL),
(91701,'chemistry','This reaction is\n2Fe²⁺ +2H⁺ + H₂O₂ →  2Fe³⁺ + 2H₂O','Oxidizing action of H₂O₂ in acidic medium','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 294; Paragraph no.2)\ndiagram(ne0907)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrogen peroxide-Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Oxidizing action of H₂O₂ in acidic medium','Reducing action of H₂O₂ in acidic medium','Oxidizing action of H₂O₂ in Basic medium','Reducing action of H₂O₂ in basic medium','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91702,'chemistry','This reaction is\nHOCl + H₂O₂ → H₃O⁺ + Cl⁻ + O₂','Reducing action of H₂O₂ in acidic medium','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 294; Paragraph no.2)\ndiagram(ne0907)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrogen peroxide-Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Oxidizing action of H₂O₂ in acidic medium','Reducing action of H₂O₂ in acidic medium','Oxidizing action of H₂O₂ in Basic medium','Reducing action of H₂O₂ in basic medium','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91703,'chemistry','This reaction is\nMn²⁺ + H₂O₂ → Mn⁴⁺ + 2OH⁻','Oxidizing action of H₂O₂ in Basic medium','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 294; Paragraph no.2) diagram(ne0907)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrogen peroxide-Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Oxidizing action of H₂O₂ in acidic medium','Reducing action of H₂O₂ in acidic medium','Oxidizing action of H₂O₂ in Basic medium','Reducing action of H₂O₂ in basic medium','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91704,'chemistry','This reaction is\nI₂+ H₂O₂ + 2OH⁻ →2I⁻ + 2H₂O + O₂','Reducing action of H₂O₂ in basic medium','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 294; Paragraph no.2) diagram(ne0907)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrogen peroxide-Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Oxidizing action of H₂O₂ in acidic medium','Reducing action of H₂O₂ in acidic medium','Oxidizing action of H₂O₂ in Basic medium','Reducing action of H₂O₂ in basic medium','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91705,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Water turning into vapour is a chemical reaction \n\nReason: The bonds are not broken','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','A physical change simply refers to a change in shape without breaking of bonds. This is a physical process. Another physical process is a change in state of matter: when ice melts into water, or when water becomes a vapour.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 154, para 2)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-154','11-113',NULL),
(91706,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Chemical Reactions, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Catalysed reactions proceed at a higher rate compared to uncatalysed reactions\n\n2) Carbonic anhydrase is found within the cytoplasm \n\n3) A metabolic pathway is a single step chemical reaction\n\n4) A metabolic pathway involves only a single type of enzyme','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Catalysed reactions proceed at rates vastly higher than that of uncatalysed ones.\nEnzyme present within the cytoplasm called carbonic anhydrase\nA multistep chemical reaction, when each of the steps is catalysed by the same enzyme complex or different enzymes, is called a metabolic pathway\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 155, para 1 )','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-155','11-113',NULL),
(91707,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Conversion of pyruvic acid from glucose is a metabolic pathway \n\nReason: It involves different enzymes catalysed metabolic reactions','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','A multistep chemical reaction, when each of the steps is catalysed by the same enzyme complex or different enzymes, is called a metabolic pathway. Glucose becomes pyruvic acid through ten different enzyme catalysed metabolic reactions so it is a metabolic pathway.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 155, para 1 )','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-155','11-114',NULL),
(91708,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Enzymes, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Under aerobic conditions, lactic acid is produced in our skeletal muscle\n\n2) During fermentation of yeast, ethanol is produced\n\n3) Substrate is a chemical converted into product and that fits into active site of enzyme\n\n4) Metabolic pathway with one or two additional reactions gives rise to only certain metabolic end products','Only 2 and 3 are correct','In our skeletal muscle, under anaerobic conditions, lactic acid is formed. Under normal aerobic conditions, pyruvic acid is formed\nIn yeast, during fermentation, the same pathway leads to the production of ethanol (alcohol).\nThe chemical which is converted into a product is called a ‘substrate’.\nAt this stage you should know that this very metabolic pathway with one or two additional reactions gives rise to a variety of metabolic end products\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 155, para 1 and 2 )','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-155','11-114',NULL),
(51055,'chemistry','A molecule named o-nitrophenol consists of ______________hydrogen bond/s','intramolecular','The presence of hydrogen between molecules is intermolecular hydrogen bond and presence of hydrogen bond in the molecule itself is intramolecular hydrogen bond. In o-nitrophenol, there is a hydrogen bond between the hydrogen of the hydroxide group and oxygen. \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 138; Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','intramolecular','intermolecular','both intermolecular and intramolecular','neither intermolecular nor intramolecular','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91709,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: In the formation of pyruvic acid, glucose acts as the substrate\n\nReason: Glucose undergoes metabolic pathways in the process','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The chemical which is converted into a product is called a ‘substrate. Glucose is converted into pyruvic acid as an end product \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 155, para 2 )','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-155','11-114',NULL),
(91710,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Enzymes, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Binding of substrate to active site of enzyme will form the ES complex\n\n2) ES complex formation is a transient phenomenon\n\n3) Enzyme will reduce activation energy of a reaction\n\n4) During the state where substrate is bound to the enzyme active site, a new structure of the substrate called transition state structure is formed','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','It is now understood that the substrate ‘S’ has to bind the enzyme at its ‘active site’ within a given cleft or pocket. The substrate has to diffuse towards the ‘active site’. There is thus, an obligatory formation of an ‘ES’ complex. E stands for enzyme.\nThis complex formation is a transient phenomenon\nRefer Figure 9.6 on NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 156, activation energy with enzyme is much lower\nDuring the state where substrate is bound to the enzyme active site, a new structure of the substrate called transition state structure is formed.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 156, para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-156','11-114',NULL),
(91711,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: The substrate has to diffuse towards the ‘active site’\n\nReason: So that ES complex is formed','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Substrate ‘S’ has to bind the enzyme at its ‘active site’ within a given cleft or pocket. The substrate has to diffuse towards the ‘active site’. There is thus, an obligatory formation of an ‘ES’ complex. E stands for enzyme.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 156, para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-156','11-114',NULL),
(91712,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Enzymes, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Stability is related to energy status of the involved molecule or even structure\n\n2) If product has lower energy than substrate, it is an exothermic reaction\n\n3) Activation energy is the total energy required by ‘S’ from that of this transition state\n\n4) Heat is required to form products from substrates','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Stability is something related to energy status of the molecule or the structure\nIf ‘P’ is at a lower level than ‘S’, the reaction is an exothermic reaction.\nThe difference in average energy content of ‘S’ from that of this transition state is called ‘activation energy’.\nOne need not supply energy (by heating) in order to form the product. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 156, para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-156','11-115',NULL),
(91713,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Enzymes make transition of ‘S’ to ‘P’ easier\n\nReason: It provides all the energy required by substrate to overcome the transition state and become product','Assertion is true but Reason is false','The difference in average energy content of ‘S’ from that of this transition state is called ‘activation energy’. Enzymes eventually bring down this energy barrier making the transition of ‘S’ to ‘P’ more easy\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 156, para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-156','11-115',NULL),
(91714,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Enzymes, choose the correct statements\n\n1) ES complex is highly reactive\n\n2) Enzyme will be altered at the end of reaction\n\n3) Enzyme can only be used once \n\n4) ES complex is short-lived','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Each enzyme (E) has a substrate (S) binding site in its molecule so that a highly reactive enzyme-substrate complex (ES) is produced\nThis complex is short-lived and dissociates into its product(s) P and the unchanged enzyme with an intermediate formation of the enzyme-product complex (EP).\nThe enzyme releases the products of the reaction and the free enzyme is ready to bind to another molecule of the substrate and run through the catalytic cycle once again.\nThis complex is short-lived \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 156, para 2 and pg 157 para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-156','11-115',NULL),
(91715,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Enzyme is able to change its shape\n\nReason: This allows enzyme-product complex to form','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The binding of the substrate induces the enzyme to alter its shape,\nfitting more tightly around the substrate. The active site of the enzyme, now in close proximity of the substrate breaks the chemical bonds of the substrate and the new enzyme- product complex is formed.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 157 para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-157','11-115',NULL),
(51056,'chemistry','The vapour pressure of water at 300 K in a closed container is 0.4 atm. If the volume of container is doubled, its vapour pressure at 300 K will be','0.4 atm','Vapour pressure depends on T only and it does not depend on container volume.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.2 atm','0.4 atm','0.8 atm','0.6 atm','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91716,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Enzymes, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Enzymes generally function in a wide range of temperature \n\n2) Low temperature preserves the enzyme in a permanent inactive state\n\n3) Optimum pH is a particular pH where enzyme shows its highest activity\n\n4) At high temperature, enzymes denatures','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Enzymes generally function in a narrow range of temperature and pH\nLow temperature preserves the enzyme in a temporarily inactive state\nEach enzyme shows its highest activity at a particular temperature and pH called the optimum temperature and optimum pH.\nHigh temperature destroys enzymatic activity because proteins are denatured by heat.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 157 para 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-157','11-116',NULL),
(91717,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Enzymes are not pH sensitive but temperature sensitive \n\nReason: They function efficiently in all pH but only under its optimum temperature','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Enzymes generally function in a narrow range of temperature and pH. Activity declines both below and above the optimum value\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 157 para 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-157','11-116',NULL),
(91718,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Enzymes, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Inhibitors shuts off enzyme activity\n\n2) Competitive inhibitor has a similar structure as the target substrate\n\n3) Malonate is an example of competitive inhibitor \n\n4) As long as substrate concentration increases, the rate will keep on increasing','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','When the binding of the chemical shuts off enzyme activity, the process is called inhibition and the chemical is called an inhibitor.\nWhen the inhibitor closely resembles the substrate in its molecular structure and inhibits the activity of the enzyme, it is known as competitive inhibitor.\nInhibition of succinic dehydrogenase by malonate which closely resembles the substrate succinate in structure therefore it\'s a competitive inhibitor\nWith the increase in substrate concentration, the velocity of the enzymatic reaction rises at first. The reaction ultimately reaches a maximum velocity (Vmax) which is not exceeded by any further rise in concentration of the substrate\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 158, para 1,2 and 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-158','11-117',NULL),
(91719,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: When enzymatic reaction reaches a maximum velocity it will not exceeded by any further rise in concentration of the substrate.\n\nReason: Enzyme molecules are fewer than the substrate molecules','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','With the increase in substrate concentration, the velocity of the enzymatic reaction rises at first. The reaction ultimately reaches a maximum velocity (Vmax) which is not exceeded by any further rise in concentration of the substrate. This is because the enzyme molecules are fewer than the substrate molecules and after saturation of these molecules, there are no free enzyme molecules to bind with the additional substrate molecules\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 158, para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-158','11-117',NULL),
(91720,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Succinate is the competitive inhibitor of malonate\n\nReason: Succinate resembles structure of malonate and binds to active site of enzyme replacing the substrate','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Due to its close structural similarity with the substrate, the inhibitor competes with the substrate for the substrate binding site of the enzyme. Consequently, the substrate cannot bind and as a result, the enzyme action declines, e.g., inhibition of succinic dehydrogenase by malonate which closely resembles the substrate succinate in structure.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 158, para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-158','11-117',NULL),
(91721,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Enzymes, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Enzymes are divided into 4 main classes\n\n2) Hydrolases can catalyse hydrolysis of P-N bond\n\n3) Dehydrogenases catalyse oxidoreduction between two substrates\n\n4) Hydrolases catalyse the hydrolysis of P-O bond','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Enzymes are divided into 6 classes each with 4-13 subclasses\nHydrolases: Enzymes catalysing hydrolysis of ester, ether, peptide, glycosidic, C-C, C-halide or P-N bonds.\nOxidoreductases/dehydrogenases: Enzymes which catalyse oxidoreduction between two substrates S and S’\nLigases: Enzymes catalysing the linking together of 2 compounds, e.g., enzymes which catalyse joining of C-O, C-S, C-N, P-O etc. bonds.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 158, para 2 and para 1)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-158','11-117',NULL),
(91722,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Co-factors, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Enzymes are composed of more than one polypeptide chains\n\n2) There are two types of co-factors\n\n3) Apoenzyme is known as the protein portion of an enzyme\n\n4) Presence of co-factor is necessary for the functional activity of enzymes','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Enzymes are composed of one or several polypeptide chains\nThree kinds of cofactors may be identified: prosthetic groups, co-enzymes and metal ions\nThe protein portion of the enzymes is called the apoenzyme\nCatalytic activity is lost when the co-factor is removed from the enzyme which testifies that they play a crucial role in the catalytic activity of the enzyme.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 159, para 2 and para 6)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-159','11-118',NULL),
(91723,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: NAD is classified as a co-enzyme\n\nReason: It can tightly bind to apoenzyme','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Prosthetic groups are organic compounds and are distinguished from other cofactors in that they are tightly bound to the apoenzyme\nCo-enzymes are also organic compounds but their association with the apoenzyme is only transient. . The essential chemical components of many coenzymes are vitamins, e.g., coenzyme nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide (NAD) and NADP contain the vitamin niacin\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 159, para 4)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-159','11-118',NULL),
(91724,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Co-factors, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Haem is a prosthetic group\n\n2) Co-enzymes are inorganic compounds\n\n3) Zinc is a cofactor for the proteolytic enzyme carboxypeptidase\n\n4) Association of co-enzymes with the apoenzyme usually occurs during the course of catalysis','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','For example, in peroxidase and catalase, which catalyze the breakdown of hydrogen peroxide to water and oxygen, haem is the prosthetic group and it is a part of the active site of the enzyme.\nCo-enzymes are also organic compounds \nZinc is a cofactor for the proteolytic enzyme carboxypeptidase.\nCo-enzymes are also organic compounds but their association with the apoenzyme is only transient, usually occurring during the course of catalysis\n(Reference : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 159, para 3,4 and 5)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','!,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-159','11-118',NULL),
(91725,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason below, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Without haem, hydrogen peroxide cannot be broken down\n\nReason: Haem is the coenzyme for this activity and it is part of the enzyme involved','Assertion is true but Reason is false','In peroxidase and catalase, which catalyze the breakdown of hydrogen peroxide to water and oxygen, haem is the prosthetic group and it is a part of the active site of the enzyme.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11, Chapter 9, pg 159, para 3)','Cell Structure And Functions >> Biomolecules >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-159','11-118',NULL),
(310101,'chemistry','0.25 gm of organic compound on analysis by Dumas method produce 32 ml of nitrogen gas at STP . The percentage of nitrogen is?','16','Percentage (%) of N, 0.00125*(volume of N2 collected/weight of compound)*100 => 0.00125*(32/0.25)*100= 16','PRINCIPLES RELATED TO PRACTICAL CHEMISTRY>>QUANTITATIVE ANALYSIS>>DUMAS METHOD',NULL,'2','MCQ','10','16','20','15','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(310102,'chemistry','In Dumas\' method of estimation of nitrogen 0.35 g of an organic compound gave 55 mL of nitrogen collected at 300 K \n temperature and 715 mm pressure. The percentage composition\n of nitrogen in the compound of nitrogen in the compound would be (aqueous tension at 300 K = 15 mm).','16.45','Weight of the organic compound taken(W\') = 0.35g, volm = 55ml, T=300K, R=0.821, P=715-15= 700mm, M=28, W=?  we know PV= (WRT)/M, put all the values in this equation, find W and then find the percentage by using (W/W\')*100, which will be (0.058/0.35)*100=16.45.','PRINCIPLES RELATED TO PRACTICAL \n CHEMISTRY>>QUANTITATIVE ANALYSIS>>DUMAS METHOD',NULL,'2','MCQ','15.45','16.45','17.45','14.45','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(310103,'chemistry','Which of the following methods is used for the estimation of nitrogen in organic compounds?','Dumas method','Dumas method is used for finding the amount of nitrogen in an organic compound. The sample is weighed, mixed with copper(II) oxide, and heated in a tube.','PRINCIPLES RELATED TO PRACTICAL \n CHEMISTRY>>QUANTITATIVE ANALYSIS>>DUMAS METHOD',NULL,'1','MCQ','Hypobromite method.','Rast method','Dumas method','Carius method','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(310104,'chemistry','In Dumas method, the gas which is collected in Nitrometer is','Dinitrogen','while following the dumas method for the determination of nitrogen, any nitrogen present in the compound is converted into oxides of nitrogen, which are led over hot copper to reduce them to nitrogen gas.','PRINCIPLES RELATED TO PRACTICAL \n CHEMISTRY>>QUANTITATIVE ANALYSIS>>DUMAS METHOD',NULL,'1','MCQ','Dinitrogen','Nitric Oxide','Ammonia','Dihydrogen','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(310105,'chemistry','In Dumas method, 0.3 g of an organic compound gave 45 ml of nitrogen at STP. The percentage of nitrogen is.','18.7','Percentage (%) of N, 0.00125*(volume of N2 collected/weight of compound)*100 => 0.00125*(45/0.3)*100= 18.75','PRINCIPLES RELATED TO PRACTICAL \n CHEMISTRY>>QUANTITATIVE ANALYSIS>>DUMAS METHOD',NULL,'2','MCQ','16.9','18.7','23.2','29.6','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(310106,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is/are correct?','Nitrogen gas is quantitatively estimated \n in Dumas method','Dumas method is used for finding the amount of nitrogen in an organic compound.','PRINCIPLES RELATED TO PRACTICAL \n CHEMISTRY>>QUANTITATIVE ANALYSIS>>DUMAS METHOD',NULL,'1','MCQ','Aluminium wire is used in \n Beilstein test.','Nitrogen gas is quantitatively estimated \n in Dumas method','In Kjeldahl\'s method. organic \n compound is reacted with \n conc. H2SO4. K2SO4 and \n Na2SO4 are also added.','All organic compounds \n contain both C and H.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(310107,'chemistry','Amongst the following, which liquids are taken in nitrometer in Dumas method?','KOH(aqueous) and Hg','The nitrogen containing organic compounds, when heated with\n copper oxide in an atmosphere of carbon dioxide, yields free \n nitrogen in addition to carbon dioxide and water.','PRINCIPLES RELATED TO PRACTICAL \n CHEMISTRY>>QUANTITATIVE ANALYSIS>>DUMAS METHOD',NULL,'2','MCQ','Water and dilute sulphuric acid','Water and mercury','KOH(aqueous) and Hg','Hg and dilute sulphuric acid','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(310108,'biology','Which of the following would prove useful to improve quality and productivity of livestock industry?','Newer technologies','In addition to conventional practices of animal breeding and care, newer technologies also have to be applied to achieve improvement in quality and productivity.','Biology in Human Welfare >> Strategies for enhancement in food production >> Animal Husbandry',NULL,'2','mcq','Newer technologies','Coventional techniques','Insectiside sprays','Reducing prices','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(310201,'chemistry','0.5 g of an organic compound containing N on Kjeldahlising required 29 ml of N/5H₂SO₄ for complete neutralization of NH3. \n The percentage of N in the compound is','16.2','% of N2 = (28*0.129*100)\\(1000*0.5) = 16.2','PRINCIPLES RELATED TO PRACTICAL \n CHEMISTRY>>QUANTITATIVE ANALYSIS>>KJELDAHL\'S METHOD',NULL,'2','MCQ','34.3','16.2','21.6','14.8','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51057,'chemistry','Which gas has the highest value of the b - van der vaals constant, given their critical volumes?','0.432L','Vc = 3b = 3 × 4N × (4/3) πr³ Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.025L','0.312L','0.245L','0.432L','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(310202,'chemistry','In Kjeldahl\'s method, ammonia from 5 g of food neutralizes 30 ml of 0.1 N acid. The percentage of nitrogen in the food is:','0.84','According to Kjeldahl’s method, the % of nitrogen in food can be calculated using the following formula:\n\nPercentage of nitrogen in food = (Volume of acid x Normality of acid x 14 x 100) / Weight of sample, Here, the volume of acid used is 30 ml, the normality of acid is 0.1 N, and the weight of the sample is 5 g. Substituting these values in the formula, we get: Percentage of nitrogen in food = (30 x 0.1 x 14 x 100) / 5 = 84%\n\nTherefore, the percentage of nitrogen in the food = 84%.','PRINCIPLES RELATED TO PRACTICAL \n CHEMISTRY>>QUANTITATIVE ANALYSIS>>KJELDAHL\'S METHOD',NULL,'2','MCQ','0.84','8.4','16.8','1.68','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(310203,'chemistry','Quantitative measurement of nitrogen in an organic compound is done by the method','Kjeldahl\'s method','Quantitative measurement of nitrogen in an organic compound is\n done by the Kjeldahl\'s method.In Kjeldahl\'s method, nitrogen \n present in organic compound is converted into ammonia and \n its amount is estimated by titrating with base.','PRINCIPLES RELATED TO PRACTICAL \n CHEMISTRY>>QUANTITATIVE ANALYSIS>>KJELDAHL\'S METHOD',NULL,'2','MCQ','Berthelot method','Beilstein method','Lassaigne test','Kjeldahl\'s method','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(310204,'biology','For the______ to be realised in dairying, the cattle have to be well looked after.','High yielding potential','For the yield potential to be realised the cattle have to be well looked after – they have to be housed well, should have adequate water and be maintained disease free.','Biology in Human Welfare >> Strategies for enhancement in food production >> Dairy Farm Management',NULL,'2','mcq','High yielding potential','High breeding potential','Older','Young','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(310205,'biology','Stringent cleanliness and hygiene (both of the cattle and the handlers) are of paramount importance while __________ the dairy products','Milking','Stringent cleanliness and hygiene (both of the cattle and the handlers) are of paramount importance while milking, storage and transport of the milk and its products','Biology in Human Welfare >> Strategies for enhancement in food production >> Dairy Farm Management',NULL,'2','fillup','lk,Mi,ing',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(310206,'biology','The mechanization of milking, storing and transporting milk and its products reduces the chances of','Direct contact of handler with products','Nowadays, of course, much of these processes have become mechanised, which reduces chance of direct contact of the produce with the handler.','Biology in Human Welfare >> Strategies for enhancement in food production >> Dairy Farm Management',NULL,'2','mcq','Handler and machines','Animals and machines','Handler and customers','Direct contact of handler with products','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(310207,'biology','Regular visits by a ________ would be mandatory to improve dairying practices.','Vet doctor','Regular visits by a veterinary doctor would be mandatory.','Biology in Human Welfare >> Strategies for enhancement in food production >> Dairy Farm Management',NULL,'2','mcq','Doctor','Vet doctor','Farmer','Dietician','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
