(311515,'biology','Isolated protoplasts from two different varieties of plants can be fused to get hybrid protoplasts, which can be further grown to form a new plant. These hybrids are called=','Somatic hybrids','Isolated protoplasts from two different varieties of plants – each having a desirable character – can be fused to get hybrid protoplasts, which can be further grown to form a new plant. These hybrids are called somatic hybrids while the process is called somatic hybridisation.','Biology in Human Welfare >> Strategies for enhancement in food production >> Tissue Culture',NULL,'2','mcq','Sexual hybrids','Somatic hybrids','Genetic hybrids','Somatohybrids','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311516,'biology','Isolated protoplasts from two different varieties of plants can be fused to get hybrid protoplasts, which can be further grown to form a new plant. These hybrids are called Somatic hybrids\n while the process is called ___________','Somatic hybridisation','Isolated protoplasts from two\ndifferent varieties of plants – each having a desirable character – can be\nfused to get hybrid protoplasts, which can be further grown to form a\nnew plant. These hybrids are called somatic hybrids while the process\nis called somatic hybridisation.','Biology in Human Welfare >> Strategies for enhancement in food production >> Tissue Culture',NULL,'2','fillup','So,ma,t,ic h,ybr,idisa,tion',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311517,'biology','Formation of pomato was a result from','Somatic hybridisation','Imagine a situation when a protoplast of tomato is fused with that of potato, and then they are grown – to form new hybrid plants combining tomato and potato characteristics. Well, this has been achieved – resulting in formation of pomato; unfortunately this plant did not have all the desired combination of characteristics for its commercial utilisation.','Biology in Human Welfare >> Strategies for enhancement in food production >> Tissue Culture',NULL,'2','mcq','Totipotency','Somatic hybridisation','Tissue culture','Biofortification','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311601,'biology','By which method was a new breed ‘Hisardale’ of sheep formed by using Bikaneri ewes and Marino rams?  [NEET 2020]','Cross breeding','Hisardale is a new breed of sheep developed in Punjab by crossing Bikaneri ewes and Marino rams. In  cross-breeding, superior males of one breed are mated  with superior females of another breed.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Out crossing','Mutational breeding','Cross breeding','Inbreeding','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311602,'biology','Select the incorrect statement.[NEET 2019]','Inbreeding selects harmful recessive gene that reduce fertility and productivity.','Inbreeding strategies allow the desirable \nqualities of more closely related individuals to be \ncontinued within the same breed for 4-6 generations. \nIt increases homozygosity and thus, is necessary for evolving a pureline. Inbreeding exposes harmful\nrecessive genes that are eliminated by selection. It also\nhelps in accumulation of superior genes and elimination\nof less desirable genes. Therefore, this approach where\nthere is selection at each step, increases the productivity\nof inbred population.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Inbreeding helps in accumulation of superior genes and elimination of undesirable genes.','Inbreeding increases homozygosity.','Inbreeding is essential to evolve purelines, in any aniamal.','Inbreeding selects harmful recessive gene that reduce fertility and productivity.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311603,'biology','Homozygous purelines in cattle can be obtained by[NEET 2017]','Matting of related individuals of same breed','When breeding is between animals of the\nsame breed for 4-6 generations, it is called inbreeding.\nInbreeding, as a rule, increases homozygosity. Thus,\ninbreeding is necessary if we want to obtain a pureline in\nany animal.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Matting of unrelated individuals of same breed','Matting of individuals of different breed','Matting of individuals of diffrent species','Matting of related individuals of same breed','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311604,'biology','Interspecific hybridisation is the matting of [NEET 2016]','Two different related species','In interspecific hybridisation, a species is\nmated with a different related species of the same genus.\nInterspecific hybrids are generally difficult to produce,\nbut they are important in plant breeding, particularly\nin breeding for disease resistance. This is also called\nintrageneric hybridisation.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Animals within same breed without having common ancestors','Two different related species','Superior males and females of different breeds','More closely related individuals within same breed for 4-6 generations.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311605,'biology','Among the following edible fishes, which one is a marine fish having rich source of omega-3 fatty acids? [NEET 2016]','Mackerel','Mackerel is a marine fish,rich in omega-3-fatty\nacids. Mystus, Mangur and Mrigala are freshwater fishes','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Mystus','Mangur','Mrigala','Mackerel','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311606,'biology','Outbreeding is an important strategy of animal husbandry because it [NEET 2015]','Is useful in overcoming inbreeding depression','Outbreeding is the breeding of unrelated\nanimals, which may be between individuals of the\nsame breed (but having no common ancestors), or\nbetween different breeds (cross breeding) or different\nspecies (inter specific hybridisation). Outbreeding is an\nimportant strategy of animal husbandry as it helps to\novercome inbreeding depression.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Is useful in overcoming inbreeding depression','Exposes harmful recessive genes that are eliminated by selection','Helps in accumulation of superior genes','Is useful in producing purelines','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311607,'biology','A true breeding plant is [NEET- II 2016]','Near homozygous and produces offspring of its own kind','A plant whose genotype is in homozygous condition and can express the certain type of phenotype is called as true breeding','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','One that is able to breed on its own','Produced due to cross-pollination among unrelated plants','Near homozygous and produces offspring of its own kind','Always homozygous recessive in its genetic constitution.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311608,'biology','A system of rotating crops with legume or grass pasture to improve soil structure and fertility is called[NEET-II 2016]','Ley farming','Ley farming is an agricultural system where\nthe field is alternately seeded for grain and left fallow\nfor growing hay or used for pasture. During the fallow/\npasture period the soil is filled with roots of grasses and\nother plants. New ploughing mixes them in the soil\nand also increases the amount of nitrogen in the soil\nespecially when legume forage are used. It also protects\nsoil from erosion by maintaining constant soil coverage.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Strip farming','Shifting agriculture','Ley farming','Contour farming','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311609,'biology','A cell at telophase stage is observed by a student in a plant brought from the field. He tells his teacher that this cell is not like other cells at telophase stage. There is no formation of cell plate and thus the cell is containing more number of chromosomes as compared to other dividing cells. This would result in     [NEET-I 2016]','Polyploidy.','Polyploidy is the phenomenon of occurrence\nof more than two sets of chromosomes in the nucleus of\na cell. Polyploidy is more common in plants. Polyploidy\narises as a result of total non-disjunction of chromosomes\nduring mitosis or meiosis.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Somaclonal variation','Polyteny','Aneuploidy','Polyploidy.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311610,'biology','In plant breeding programmes, the entire collection ( of plants/ seeds) having all the diverse alleles for all genes in a given crop is called  [NEET 2013]','Germplasm collection','Germplasm of any crop species consists of (i)\ncultivated improved varieties, (ii) improved varieties\nthat are no more in cultivation, (iii) old local or ‘desi’\nvarieties, (iv) purelines and (v) wild species related to the\ncrop species.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Evaluation and selection of parents','Germplasm collection','Selection of superior recomb inants','Cross - hybridisation among the selected parents.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311611,'biology','An alga which can be employed as food for human being is  [NEET 2014]','Chlorella','Chlorella is a single celled chlorophycean alga\nwith rich protein content. It is considered as a source of\nSCP (single cell protein) and also as food source during\nlong space travels.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ulothrix','Chlorella','Spriogyra','Polysiphonia','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311612,'biology','A protoplast is a cell  [NEET 2015]','Without cell wall','A protoplast is a cell which has its cell wall\nremoved by mechanical or enzymatic means.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Undergoing devision','Without cell wall','Without plasma membrane','Without nucleus','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311613,'biology','A technique of micropropagation is [NEET 2015 Cancelled]','Somatic embryogenesis','Micropropagation is the latest method of\nobtaining a large number of plantlets from plant tissue\nculture. It is called micropropagation because of the minute\nsize of the propagules. It involves repeated subculture of\nthe explant by changing the medium so as to form a large\nnumber of plantlets from that single explant. Somatic\nembryogenesis i.e., developing embryos from somatic\ncells is one of the techniques of micropropagation.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Protoplast fusion','Embryo rescue','Somatic hybridisation','Somatic embryogenesis','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311614,'biology','Which of the following enhances or induces fusion of protoplasts? [NEET 2015 Cancelled]','Polyethylene glycol and sodium nitrate','When a hybrid is produced by fusion ofsomatic\ncells of two varieties or species, it is known as somatic\nhybrid. The process of producing somatic hybrids is\ncalled somatic hybridization. First, the cell wall of the\nplant cells are removed then the protoplasts of the two\ncells are brought in contact and made to fuse by means\nof electrofusion or chemicals like polyethylene glycol\n(PEG) and sodium nitrate. The fused protoplasts soon\ndevelop their own walls and are called somatic hybrids.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','IAA and kinetin','IAA and gibberellins','Sodium chloride and potassium chloride','Polyethylene glycol and sodium nitrate','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311615,'biology','To obtain virus - free healthy plants from a diseased\none by tissue culture technique, which part/parts\nof the diseased plant will be taken? [NEET 2014]','Both apical and axillary meristems','Meristem is a localized group of cells, which\nare actively dividing and undifferentiated but ultimately\ngiving rise to permanent tissue. Although the plant is\ninfected with a virus, yet the meristem is free of virus.\nTherefore, meristem can be removed and grown in vitro\nto obtain virusfree plants.Cultivation of axillary or apical\nshoot meristems is called meristem culture. The apical\nor axillary meristems are generally free from virus.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Apical meristem only.','Palisade parenchyma','Both apical and axillary meristems','Epidermis only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311616,'biology','Tissue culture technique can produce infinite number of new plants from a small parental tissue. The economic importance of the technique is in raising [Karnataka NEET 2013]','Genetically uniform population identical to the original parent','Plant tissue culture, also called micro￾propagation, is the growth of plant cells outside the\nplant body in a suitable culture medium which contains\nmixture of nutrients in solid or liquid form, under\ncontrolled environmental condition. Tissue culture\ntechnique is based on totipotent nature of plant cell or\nphenomenon of totipotency i.e., each and every plant cell\nhas inherent capacity to develop into complete plant. The\nentirely vegetatively produced descendents of somatic\ncells are collectively called clone. They are genetically\nidentical to parents.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Genetically uniform population identical to the original parent','Homozygous diploid plant','New species','Variants through picking up somaclonal variations.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311617,'biology','Which of the following statements is not true about somatic embryogenesis? [Karnataka NEET 2013]','Somatic embryos is induced usually by an auxin such as 2, 4-D.','Somatic embryos develop from somatic cells.\nTheir development is comparable to that of a zygotic\nembryo. They are just like a normal embryo except that\ntheir development is induced from a diploid somatic\ncell. Somatic embryo culture is induced by a high\nconcentration of an auxin. Microspores are haploid cells\nwhich do not give rise to somatic embryo.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','The pattern of development of a somatic embryo \nis comparable to that of a zygotic embryo.','Somatic embryos can develop from microspores','Somatic embryos is induced usually by an auxin such as 2, 4-D.','A somatic embryo develops from a somatic cell','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311618,'biology','By which method was a new breed ‘Hisardale’ of sheep formed by using Bikaneri ewes and Marino rams? [NEET 2020]','Cross breeding','Hisardale is a new breed of sheep developed in Punjab by crossing Bikaneri ewes and Marino rams. In cross-breeding, superior males of one breed are mated with superior females of another breed.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Out crossing','Mutational breeding','Cross breeding','Inbreeding','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311619,'biology','Select the incorrect statement [NEET 2019]','Inbreeding selects harmful recessive gene that reduce fertility and productivity.','Inbreeding strategies allow the desirable qualities of more closely related individuals to be continued within the same breed for 4-6 generations. It increases homozygosity and thus, is necessary for evolving a pureline. Inbreeding exposes harmful recessive genes that are eliminated by selection. It also helps in accumulation of superior genes and elimination of less desirable genes. Therefore, this approach where there is selection at each step, increases the productivity of inbred population','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Inbreeding helps in accumulation of superior genes and elimination of undesirable genes','Inbreeding increases homozygosity','Inbreeding is essential to evolve purelines, in any animal','Inbreeding selects harmful recessive gene that reduce fertility and productivity.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311620,'biology','Match List-I with List-Il  (NEET 2021)\nList-I                             List-Il  \n\n(a)Protoplast fusion     (i)Totipotency\n\n(b)Plant tissue culture (ii) Pomato\n\n(c)Meristem culture    (iii) Somaclones\n\n(d)Micropropagation   (iv) Virus-free plants\n\nChoose the correct answer from the options given\nbelow.\n      (a) (b) (c) (d)\n(1) (iv) (iii) (ii) (i)\n(2) (iii) (iv) (ii) (i)\n(3) (ii) (i) (iv) (iii)\n(4) (iii) (iv) (i) (ii)','3','Isolated protoplasts from two different varieties of plants each having a desirable character can be fused to get hybrid protoplasts, this has been achieved and resulted in formation of pomato.      The capacity to generate a whole plant from any cell/explant is called totipotency.         Even if the plant is infected with a virus, the meristem (apical and axillary) is free of virus.          The method of producing thousands of plants through tissue culture is called micro-\npropagation. Each of these plants will be genetically identical to the original plant from which they were grown, i.e., they are somaclones.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311621,'biology','Mutations in plant cells can be induced by(NEET 2021)','Gamma rays','Gamma rays are ionizing radiation and used in inducing mutations in seeds and other planting materials such as cuttings, pollen, or tissue-cultured calli','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Zeatin','Kinetin','Infrared rays','Gamma rays','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311801,'biology','Statement I: In addition to livestock, animal husbandry also includes the rearing and selling of fishes and fowl\n\nStatement II: The rearing, catching and selling of fish, molluscs and crustaceans is collectively included in fisheries','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Animal husbandry refers to the agricultural practice of breeding and raising of livestock that are useful to humans, such as cows, buffaloes, camels, etc. By extension, it also includes fisheries (fish, mollusks and crustaceans) and poultry farming (fowl). Fisheries refer to the practice of rearing, catching, selling, etc. of fish, molluscs and crustaceans.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311802,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) Animal husbandry includes care and breeding of all animals for human use and study including elephants and lions\n\n(ii) Both apiculture and fisheries are a part of animal husbandry\n\n(iii) Fisheries is a part of animal husbandry but apiculture is not\n\n(iv) Systematic rearing and raising of organisms that yield products of human interest such as bees, fish, silkworms, etc. is referred to as animal husbandry\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are INCORRECT?','(i) and (iii) only','Animal husbandry refers to the agricultural practice of breeding and raising of livestock that are useful to humans, such as cows, buffaloes, camels, etc. By extension, it also includes fisheries (fish, mollusks and crustaceans), poultry farming (fowl) and apiculture (beekeeping). Animal husbandry includes systematic techniques for raising livestock and other living organisms to yield better quality and quantity of products such as milk, silk, wool, meat, etc.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) and (ii) only','(i) and (iii) only','(ii) and (iv) only','(i) only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311803,'biology','Assertion: India\'s livestock productivity per unit is less than the average world productivity\n\nReason: More than 70% of the livestock population in the world is found in India and China','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','It is true that more than 70% of the world livestock population is found in India and China. However, the productivity per unit livestock in India is very low and less than the world average.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311804,'biology','Statement I: Embryo transfer techniques have been utilized to increase livestock productivity\n\nStatement II: Animal husbandry includes breeding of livestock only through natural means','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','To increase livestock productivity, newer technologies have been applied which include embryo transfer technologies. Animal husbandry includes breeding of livestock that are of human use. And hence, increase in livestock productivity and yield is a part of animal husbandry. This implies that in addition to the traditional natural breeding methods, the newer technologies such as embryo transfer are also included in the methods of animal husbandry.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311805,'biology','Assertion: Resistance of cattle to diseases is an important factor to increase milk yield\nReason: Quality of breed dictates the milk yielding potential','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','Milk yield is primarily dependent on the quality of breeds (in a given climate) in a farm. The better the quality, the higher is the yield. In addition to the breed quality, resistance to diseases and their prevention is also important in increasing the yield because a healthier cattle would be able to produce more milk for longer durations.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311806,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) Automation of the milking process\n\n(ii) The housing and quantity of fodder given to the cattle\n\n(iii) Amount of milk consumed by humans of different age groups\n\n(iv) Sterilization of the storage containers for milk transport\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are NOT considered a part of dairy farm management?','(iii) only','Dairy farm management does not include keeping records of the milk consumption by humans. It refers solely to the management of animals for milk and its products for human consumption. Automation of the milking process is a desirable feature in dairy farms as it reduces direct contact between the handlers and the produce. The quality of the housing and feed of the cattle are crucial towards maintaining disease free and healthy animals, which leads to increased milk yield. Cleanliness and hygiene are also important considerations to ensure the quality of milk for consumption. Thus, sterilization of storage containers is also an important consideration.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) only','(i) and (iii) only','(iii) only','(i) and (iv) only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311807,'biology','Assertion: Technologies that reduce direct contact between the handlers and dairy produce are adopted.\n\nReason: The feeding of cattle in farms is carried out in a scientific manner.','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','Technologies that automate the processes of milking and storage are adopted in dairy farms to reduce direct contact between handlers and produce. This is because maintenance of stringent hygiene and cleanliness measures is an important consideration at farms.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51059,'biology','Which of the following statement is not correct? [ RE NEET 2022]','(3) The rhizome is aerial with no distinct nodes and internodes','Rhizome is an underground (sub-aerial) stem. It bears distinct nodes and internodes.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(1) Rhizome is a condensed form of stem','(2) The apical bud in rhizome always remains above the ground','(3) The rhizome is aerial with no distinct nodes and internodes','(4) The rhizome is thick, prostrate and branched','NEW','11-069',NULL,NULL),
(311808,'biology','Statement I: Rearing of birds such as turkey and hen is considered as poultry farm management\n\nStatement II: Rearing of pigeons for meat can also be considered a part of poultry farm management','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Poultry farm management includes rearing of domesticated birds such as chicken, duck, turkey and geese. However, rearing of pigeons to yield meat can also be considered a part of poultry farm management.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311809,'biology','Statement I: Lions and tigers are two different breeds of cats\n\nStatement II: In animal husbandry, different breeds of animals are bred together to yield offsprings with more desirable qualities','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','Lions and tigers are two different species belonging to the same cat Family, Felidae, in the animal kingdom. A breed is a group of animals within the same species having similar characteristics such as appearance, features, size, etc. For example, Labrador and Golden Retriever are two different breeds of dogs. Different breeds of animals are often interbred for integration of the desired traits and their expression in the future generations.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311810,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) Purelines can be developed in cattle by inbreeding\n\n(ii) Inbreeding only refers to mating of related or unrelated animals of the same breed\n\n(iii) Inbreeding refers to mating of closely related animals of the same breed\n\n(iv) Harmless recessive alleles can be removed from the genepool using inbreeding\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(i), (iii) and (iv) only','Inbreeding refers to the mating of closely related animals of the same breed for 4-5 generations. When unrelated animals from the same breed are mated together, it is termed as out breeding. Inbreeding leads to homozygosity, the condition where both alleles of a specific gene are the same. Thus, purelines can be developed in cattle by inbreeding. Inbreeding also brings out harmful, recessive genes which are eliminated eventually by selection. Only the catlle with superior genes expressing the desirable traits are selected for further breeding.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii) and (iii) only','(i), (iii) and (iv) only','(ii) and (iii) only','(i) and (iv) only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311811,'biology','Assertion: Purelines in cattle are developed by inbreeding\n\nReason: Inbreeding increases homozygosity','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Inbreeding refers to the mating of closely related animals of the same breed for 4-5 generations. When unrelated animals from the same breed are mated together, it is termed as out breeding. Inbreeding leads to homozygosity, the condition where both alleles of a specific gene are the same. Thus, purelines can be developed in cattle by inbreeding.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311812,'biology','Statement I: Continuous inbreeding may lead to increase in infertility in breeding populations\n\nStatement II: Superior males and females are selected after every generation for inbreeding','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Continuous inbreeding, especially close inbreeding, often leads to reduction in fertility (or, increase in infertility) and productivity in the breeding populations. This is referred to as inbreeding depression. In order to improve the desired traits and development of purelines, superior males and females are selected after every generation of inbreeding is raised. In cattle, superior female is one that produces more milk per lactation and a superior male is one that produces stronger progeny as  opposed to other males.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311813,'biology','Statement I: Outbreeding can prevent inbreeding depression\n\nStatement II: Crossing unrelated animals from the same breed can increase productivity','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Inbreeding depression refers to the condition that is caused by a decrease in fertility and productivity after a series of inbreeding cycles. It can be prevented or reversed by mating selected animals from the population with unrelated superior animals from the same breed. This is referred to as outbreeding. This eventually leads to increase in productivity and fertility as well.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311814,'biology','Assertion: Inbreeding helps in development of new, superior breeds after cross-breeding\n\nReason: Crossbreeding refers to the mating of superior males of one breed with superior females of another breed','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','Crossbreeding refers to the mating of superior males and females from different breeds. This leads to combination of the desirable features of the two different breeds. The resulting breed can then be subjected to selection and inbreeding to develop a new, stable and superior breed. Inbreeding promotes homozygosity and exposes harmful recessive genes. That is why inbreeding is conducted to develop purelines and bring stability in the breed traits.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51060,'biology','In Calotropis, aestivation is [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(3) Valvet','When sepals or petals in a whorl just touch one another at margin, without overlapping, as in Calotropis,it is said to be valvate.\nImbricate aestivation is exhibited by Cassia,\nTwisted aestivation is exhibited by China rose,\nVexillary aestivation is exhibited by Pea.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','(1) Imbricate','(2) Twisted','(3) Valvet','(4) Vexillary','NEW','11-074',NULL,NULL),
(311815,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) Outcrossing and outbreeding are two types of crossbreeding\n\n(ii) Hisardale is an improved breed of sheep developed by crossbreeding\n\n(iii) Jersey is an improved cow breed developed by crossbreeding\n\n(iv) Outbreeding includes outcrossing and crossbreeding\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are INCORRECT?','(i) and (iii) only','Outbreeding is the breeding of unrelated animals, which may be between individuals of the same breed (but having no common ancestors), or between different breeds (cross breeding) or different species (inter specific hybridisation). Thus, it includes both outcrossing and crossbreeding. Hisardale is a superior sheep breed developed by crossbreeding. Jersey is an improved cow breed that was developed as a consequence of natural inbreeding. It was first registered as a separate breed in 1700s in the UK.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) and (iii) only','(ii) and (iii) only','(iii) and (iv) only','(i) and (ii) only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311816,'biology','Assertion: Mule is an example of outbreeding\n\nReason: Interspecific hybridization involves mating between males and females from different species','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Mule is a cross between a horse and a donkey, which are two different species. Hence it is an example of interspecific hybridization. Interspecific hybridization is a subclass of outbreeding.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311817,'biology','Consider the following statements: \n\n(i) In animal breeding, unrelated animals within a breed are those that have no common ancestor in the previous 4-5 generations.\n\n(ii) Interspecific hybridization can occur between tigers and lions\n\n(iii) Cross breeding involves inbreeding between superior males and females of different breeds\n\n(iv) Outcrossing for 4-5 generations is required for overcoming inbreeding depression.\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(i) and (ii) only','In animal breeding, unrelated animals within a breed are those that have no common ancestors in the previous 4-5 generations. Interspecific hybridization refers to mating between two different, related species. Since tigers and lions belong to the same Family in the animal kingdom. it is possible to conduct interspecific hybridization between them. A cross between a male lion and a female tiger is referred to as a liger. A cross between a male tiger and a female lion is referred to as a tigon. Cross breeding refers to a cross between two different breeds and inbreeding always occurs between related animals of the same breed. For overcoming inbreeding depression, outcrossing for only one generation is sufficient.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii) and (iii) only','(i), (iii) and (iv) only','(i) and (ii) only','(i) and (iii) only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311818,'biology','Statement I: Artificial insemination involves induction of super ovulation in cows\n\nStatement II: Multiple Ovulation Embryo Transfer Technology (MOET) often leads to the development of more than one embryo','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','Artificial insemination involves the injection of sperm from a male to the reproductive tract of the selected female cattle. MOET involves  administering hormones to cows such that they produce 6-8 eggs per ovulation cycle instead of one. The cow is then mated or artificially inseminated to yield multiple embryos that are recovered non surgically and implanted in surrogate mothers.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311819,'biology','Assertion: A female produces multiple eggs during ovulation cycle as part of MOET using hormones.\n\nReason: Superovulation is caused by hormones with FSH-like activity','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','During MOET, a female cattle member is administered with FSH-like hormones that cause follicular maturation and lead to superovulation. Superovulation refers to the condition of producing multiple eggs per ovulation cycle instead of one.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311820,'biology','Statement I: MOET is only applied to increase the number of milk yielding cows\n\nStatement II: MOET shortens the time required to increase herd size','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','MOET technology involves the production of multiple eggs by female cattle after hormone action. This eventually leads to the development of multiple embryos after fertilization. Thus, the herd size can be increased in a shorter time period. MOET is not just applied to cows. It has been successfully demonstrated for sheep, rabbits, mares, buffaloes, etc.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311821,'biology','Statement I: Apiculture refers to rearing of honeybees in artificial conditions for honey\n\nStatement II: Honey is the only commercial product obtained from apiculture','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Apiculture refers to the maintenance of honebee hives for the production of honey. The hives are made naturally by honeybees usually. Other than honey, beeswax is another apiculture product that finds applications in formulating cosmetics and polishes from beeswax.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51061,'biology','Radial symmetry is NOT found in adults of phylum ______. [NEET 2023]','(1) Hemichordata','Radial symmetry is NOT found in adults of the phylum Hemichordata. Hemichordata is a phylum made up of two classes, which include both the enteropneusts and the pterobranchs.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','(1) Hemichordata','(2) Coelenterata','(3) Echinodermata','(4) Ctenophora','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311822,'biology','Assertion: Apiculture should be practiced in closed spaces with no interference from the outer environment\n\nReason: Bees are pollinators of many crop plants such as lilies','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Apiculture should be practiced in an area where there are sufficient bees pastures of wild shrubs, flowering plants and cultivated crops. Bees are pollinators of many crop plants. They take the nectar from flower and convert it to honey in beehives. In turn, they pollinate the crop plants leading to high crop yields.Lilies are foul smelling and pollinated by beetles or through water pollination, not by bees.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311823,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) Keeping hives in crop fields during flowering increases honey production.\n\n(ii) Bees reared by apiculture are incapable of assisting plants in pollination.\n\n(iii) Apis indica is the only species of honeybee reared in apiculture.\n\n(iv) Bees can increase the pollination efficiency of certain crops such as apple.\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are INCORRECT?','(ii) and (iii) only','Keeping beehives in crop fields during flowering period increases pollination efficiency and improves both crop and honey yield. Bees reared by apiculture are capable of pollinating crop plants. There are many other species of honey bee other than Apis indica that are reared in apiculture like Apis mellifera(Italian Bee)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) and (ii) only','(ii) and (iii) only','(ii) only','(i) and (iii) only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311824,'biology','Statement I: Pisciculture is mainly dedicated to the conservation of endangered fish \n\nStatement II: Other than fish, fisheries also include other aquatic animals of human use such as oysters, crabs, etc.','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','Fishery industry deals with the catching, rearing, selling and processing of fish and fish products for human use. While certain fish farms are dedicated to conserving endgangered fish species, it is not the goal of the fishery industry in general. Fisheries include fishes and other aquatic animals of human use that include oysters and crabs.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311825,'biology','Statement I: Pisciculture refers to the production of fish for human consumption\n\nStatement II: Blue revolution refers to employment of practices for increasing fish populations in the oceans and rivers','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Pisciculture refers to the production of specifically fish in ponds and reservoirs for human consumption. Blue Revolution refers to the government initiative wherein aquaculture and pisciculture have been promoted to increase the production of fish and other aquatic animals for human use, leading to an increase in the livelihood of fishermen and other farmers.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311826,'biology','Assertion: Green Revolution led to the developments in plant breeding techniques\n\nReason: Plant breeding cannot improve the crop yield and pest resistance in plants','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Developments in plant breeding techniques led to Green Revolution and not vice versa. Plant breeding can improve the crop yields and resistance to pests.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311827,'biology','Statement I: Plant breeding is a relatively new practice for improving crop quality\n\nStatement II: The present day plant crops are derived after domestication of wild crops in the ancient times','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','Plant breeding is being practiced conventionally for thousands of years. Human ancestors domesticated different wild crops in the ancient times to give rise to the present day food crops.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311828,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding steps in plant breeding:\n\n(i) Selection of parents with desired traits and generation of purelines\n\n(ii) Collection of the germplasm of a given crop\n\n(iii) Selection of superior recombinants and their inbreeding for stability of traits\n\n(iv) Testing of the new cultivars and their release in the agricultural land\n\n(v) Crosshybridization among the selected parents\n\nWhich of the following options best represents the sequence of the above statements as conducted in plant breeding experiments?','(ii), (i), (v), (iii), (iv)','Plant breeding programs are carried out in a systematic manner. The steps involved in creating a new genetic variety of crop are germplasm collection, selection of parents, crosshybridization, selection of superior recombinants, and their testing and release in agricultural land. The first step in plant breeding is germplasm collection, which refers to the entire collection of all the diverse alleles for all the genes in a given crop. Collection of all possible genetic variations in a particular crop helps in selection of plants with desired traits, which is the second step in plant breeding. The selected plants may undergo inbreeding to develop homozygous purelines. Once purelines are established, these parent plants are cross hybridized to form recombinant plants. This is the third plant breeding step. Eventually, the recombinant plants are screened and evaluated extensively to select the recombinants with superior traits. These recombinants are then self pollinated for several generations (inbreeding) to develop purelines with stable traits. In the final step, the newly developed variant of crop (cultivar) is tested for yield and other traits such as disease resistance and then cultivated in agricultural fields for comparison against local crop cultivar.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','(ii), (v), (i), (iii), (iv)','(ii), (i), (v), (iii), (iv)','(i), (v), (iii), (iv), (ii)','(i), (ii), (v), (iv), (v)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51062,'biology','Match the following : \nType of flower Example \n(A) Zygomorphic (I) Mustard \n(B) Hypogynous (II) Plum \n(C) Perigynous (III) Cassia \n(D) Epigynous (IV) Cucumber \n Select the correct option : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(4) (A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)','(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(1) (A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(IV), (D)-(III)','(2) (A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)','(3) (A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(III), (D)-(II)','(4) (A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)','NEW','11-072',NULL,NULL),
(311829,'biology','Assertion: In plant breeding, all recombinants obtained after cross hybridization possess the combination of the desired traits from their parents\n\nReason: The germplasm collection is extensively screened for the selection of parent plants with desired traits','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Collection of the germplasm of a specific crop and its extensive screening for the purpose of selecting plants with desired traits is the first step in every plant breeding programme. However, the results of cross hybridization generally yields different recombinants with different combinations of traits from the parent plants. Usually only one in a few hundred to a few thousand recombinants show the desirable combination of traits.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311830,'biology','Consider the following statements in the context of classical plant breeding:\n\nStatement I: Different genetic variants of the same plant species can be cross hybridized to yield recombinants with superior traits\n\nStatement II: Genes from other living organisms are added to the plant germplasm to increase genetic variability','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','In conventional plant breeding programmes, the germplasm (entire collection of all genetic variations of a particular crop) is screened to find plants with desired traits. Thus, two different genetic variants of the same crop (species) are taken and cross hybridized to yield recombinant crops having superior traits. However, the deliberate manipulation of a plant genepool with the addition of foreign genes is not conducted under such programmes.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311831,'biology','Statement I: Conventional plant breeding can be utilized for developing variants having better tolerance to drought conditions\n\nStatement II: The recombinant plants undergo inbreeding after selection to attain homozygosity','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Conventional plant breeding techniques have been utilized to improve the tolerance of many crop plants to drought conditions and other abiotic stresses, e.g. millets. Once the recombinant plants with the desired traits have been selected, they are often self pollinated for several generations to attain a state of homozygosity (inbreeding). This ensures that the acquired traits are stable and inherited through generations.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311832,'biology','Consider the following statements: \n\n(i) Plant breeding involves the natural recombination of genetic traits of different crop variants\n\n(ii) Plant breeding involves the introduction of foreign genes before hybridization of different crop variants\n\n(iii) Plant breeding helps in recombining only those traits that lead to high crop yield\n\n(iv) Plant breeding integrates two (or more) different traits from different crop varieties into a single recombinant variant\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(i) and (iv) only','Plant breeding takes advantage of the genetic variability within a plant crop species. Plants having one or more desirable traits are selected from all possible genetic variants available in nature (germplasm) and are allowed to cross hybridize. The cross hybridization process may be natural or induced by human interference but the recombination of traits takes place without any external manipulation. Thus, the recombination of traits is a natural process and yields a number of different recombinants having different combinations of the parent traits. Thus, plant breeding can help in integrating two or more entirely different traits into a single recombinant variant. In addition to high crop yields, these techniques have also been applied to increase the nutritional value of food crops, increase their tolerance to abiotic stresses such as high salinity, drought conditions, etc., and increased resistance to infections and pests. Foreign genes of any kind are not introduced into the plant genome in conventional plant breeding programmes.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) and (iv) only','(ii) and (iii) only','(i) and (iii) only','(ii) and (iv) only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311833,'biology','Assertion: Homozygosity is an important factor in the commercial success of recombinant crops\n\nReason: The parent plants are often inbred to develop homozygous purelines','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','In order for a recombinant crop variant to be commercially viable, it is important that it expresses the desired, superior traits throughout generations of breeding in agricultural fields. This is only possible when the traits are expressed in a stable manner in all the progeny. The stable expression of the desired traits is enabled by self pollination of the recombinant plants for many generations until they reach homozygosity. Homozygosity refers to the state where both the alleles of a particular gene are identical, hence expressing the same trait. Thus, homozygosity in the recombinant variants is an important factor towards their commercial success. Inbreeding of parent plants is conducted before their cross hybridization to ensure the homozygosity of the genetic traits. This increases the probability of yielding recombinants with the desired combination of traits.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311834,'biology','Assertion: Green Revolution in India was a consequence of successful plant breeding experiments\n\nReason: High yieding wheat and rice crops led to a massive increase in food production during 1960\'s','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Plant breeding experiments led to the development of high yielding wheat and rice crops in the 1960\'s which led to a significant increase in the food production in India. This phase of increased food production because of high yielding crops is referred to as the Green Revolution.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311835,'biology','Statement I: Rigorous testing of recombinant cultivars is conducted in different agroclimatic conditions \n\nStatement II: Testing of recombinant cultvars in research fields precedes testing in farmers\' fields to determine ideal cultivation conditions','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','After the selection of recombinant plants (cultivars) with the desired tests, rigorous testing procedures are undertaken to determine their success in real life agricultural situations. First, these cultivars are grown in research fields and their performance (crop yields, disease resistance, etc.) is recored under various ideal conditions such as fertilizer application, irrigation methods and other crop management practices. This helps in optimizing the different cultivation factors (fertilizer, irrigation, etc.) and also ensures stable expression of the desired traits. Next, these cultivars are grown in farmers\' fields at least for 3 seasons at several locations representing the different agroclimatic zones where the crop is grown. Their performance is also compared to the best available local crop cultivar that is available in the region.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311836,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) Development of semi dwarf varieties of wheat and rice led to a dramatic increase in their production.\n\n(ii) Norman E. Borlaug first developed semi dwarf wheat varieties in India\n\n(iii) Jaya and Ratna are examples of semi-dwarf wheat varieties that were developed in India\n\n(iv) Sonalika is an example of a disease resistant wheat variety\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are INCORRECT?','(ii) and (iii) only','The wheat and rice production in India has increased significantly during 1960-2000 due to the development of semi-dwarf varieties of wheat and rice.  Nobel laureate Noman E. Borlaug first developed semi-dwarf variety of wheat  at the International Centre for Wheat and Maize in Mexico. He later came to India upon invitation and led to the inception of Green Revolution in India. Jaya and Ratna are examples of semi-dwarf rice with high yields that were developed in India in the 1960\'s. Sonalika is a disease resistant and high yielding wheat variety that was introduced in Indian agricultural fields in 1963.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) and (ii) only','(ii) and (iii) only','(i) and (iii) only','(ii) only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311837,'biology','Statement I: IR-8 is a semi dwarf rice variety developed by IARI in India in 1960\'s\n\nStatement II: Taichung Native I is a semi dwarf rice variety native to Taiwan','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','IR-8 is semi dwarf rice variety that was developed at the International Rice Research Institute (IRRI), Phillipines. It was introduced in India in the year 1966 along with Taichung Native I. Taichung Native I is a semi dwarf rice variety native to Taiwan.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311838,'biology','Assertion: Plant breeding led to the cultivation of Saccharum officinarum having high sugar content in north India\n\nReason: Saccharum barberi and Saccharum officinarum were crossed to yield sugarcane with low sugar content but high adaptability','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Saccharum barberi was grown in north India which had poor sugar content and yield. Saccharum officinarum was grown in south India that had higher sugar content, but it could not be cultivated in north India. Thus, these two species were crossed using plant breeding techniques that led to a variety of sugarcane having high yield and sugar cane and ability to be cultivated in north India. It was the recombinant variety that has the ability to grow in north India. The original Saccharum officinarum still cannot be cultivated in north India.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311839,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) Hybrid jowar is an example of a high yielding crop that can grow in soil with low water content\n\n(ii) Saccharum barberi is a superior high yielding sugarcane variety developed by plant breeding\n\n(iii) The resistance of a crop to disease causing pathogens is not dependent on its genetic constitution\n\n(iv) Plant breeding can lead to increase in food production by developing disease resistant varieties\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(i) and (iv) only','Hybrid jowar is an example of a high yielding crop that is adapted to grow under water stress, i.e. grow in low water conditions. Saccharum barberi is a wild type sugarcane variety that was originally grown in north India. It had poor sugar content and low yield. It was crossed with Saccharum officinarum from south India to yield a high yielding superior variety of sugarcane. The resistance of a plant crop to disease causing pathogens is determined by its genetic constitution. That is why, plant breeding techniques can be utilized to yield superior crops with higher resistance to diseases. Plant breeding has led to the development of disease resistant varieties of many food crops (e.g. Sonalika and Kalyan Sona in wheat). Due to their higher resistance to certain diseases, these crops don\'t die easilty and hence lead to high yield. This eventually leads to higher food production.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) and (iv) only','(ii) and (iii) only','(i), (ii) and (iv) only','(iv) only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311840,'biology','Assertion: Development of disease resistant cultivars led to reduction in fungicide use\n\nReason: Fungi cause red rot in sugarcane and brown rust in wheat','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','Plant breeding techniques led to the development of disease resistant cultivars. In other words, these cultivars were more resistant to diseases caused by bacteria, fungi or viruses. Thus, the use of fungicides (fungi-killing chemicals) and bacteriocides (bacteria killing chemicals) was reduced when these superior disease resistant cultivars were introduced in agricultural fields. Fungi infection causes red rot in sugarcane and brown rust in wheat.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51063,'biology','The root that originates from the base of the stem are','Fibrous root','Page 66, XI NCERT','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Primary root','Prop root','Lateral root','Fibrous root','NEW','11-066',NULL,NULL),
(311901,'biology','Statement I: Pusa Swarnim is a Brassica variety with resistance to disease caused by fungi\n\nStatement II: Resistance to viruses cannot be developed by conventional hybridization and selection techniques','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Pusa swarnim is a variety of Brassica that is resistant to white rust, a disease caused by fungi. Conventional hybridization and selection (plant breeding) method has been applied to develop Pusa sadabahar, a variety of Chilli resistant against chilly mosaid virus and tobacco mosaic virus.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311902,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) Himgiri is a wheat variety resistant to disease caused by fungi\n\n(ii) Conventional hybridization and selection method was used to introduce resistance to yellow mosaic virus in mung bean\n\n(iii) Black rot and stripe rust are examples of bacterial and fungal diseases, respectively, in \n\n(iv) Radiation induced mutations may lead to disease resistance in plants\nWhich of the above statements is/are INCORRECT?','(ii) only','Himgiri is a wheat variety resistant to lead and stripe rust, a disease caused by fungi. In mung bean, resistance to yellow mosaic virus was introduced by mutation breeding and not conventional hybridization and selection. In mutation breeding, mutations (rearrangements in base sequence of DNA) in the plant genome are introduced artificially by chemical or radiation exposure. Black rot and stripe rust are examples of bacterial and fungal diseases, respectively, in plants. Radiations such as gamma rays can induce mutations in the genome which may lead to disease resistance in plants. This technique is referred to as mutation breeding and has been used to induce resistance to viruses in mung bean. [Hint: All diseases with \'rot\' in their names are bacterial and those with \'rust\' in their names are fungal].','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii) and (iv) only','(ii) and (iv) only','(iii) and (iv) only','(ii) only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311903,'biology','Assertion: Mutation breeding involves introduction of mutations in plant genome that are specific to a physical trait\n\nReason: Irradiation by gamma rays can induce change in the base sequence of DNA','Both Assertion and Reason are false','In mutation breeding, plants are exposed to radiations or chemicals that can induce changes in the base sequence of DNA leading to mutations. However, these mutations are random and occur throughout the plant genome at random (non specific) locations that may or may not be a gene. Plants with the desirable traits are selected from a number of mutant plants having different mutation patterns. Irradiation by gamma rays is a common method to induce mutations (changes in base sequence of DNA/genome).','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311904,'biology','Assertion: Plant breeding cannot introduce resistance to every plant disease\n\nReason: Number of disease resistant genes in a crop is limited','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Conventional plant breeding involves the hybridization of two varieties of a specific crop having the desired traits and further selection of superior recombinants. The number of gene variants among the different varieties of a single crop is limited (i.e. genes conferring resistance to every disease are not there). It is possible that there are diseases to which no genes for resistance are present within the crop\'s germplasm. Hence, conventional plant breeding cannot introduce resistance to every disease.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311905,'biology','Statement I: Mutation breeding has led to development of okra (bhindi) varieties resistant to yellow mosaic virus\n\nStatement II:Traits from wild relatives of crops can be introduced in their cultivated varieties to yield superior recombinants','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','Mutation breeding has led to the development of mung bean varieties resistant to yellow mosaic virus. In okra (bhindi), wild varieties were hybridized with cultivated varieties. This led to the development of the superior recombinant variety, Parbhani Kranti, which had both disease resistance to yellow mosaic virus (wild variety trait) and high yield (cultivated variety trait).','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311906,'biology','Statement I: Conventional plant breeding does not involve transfer of resistance genes from one crop species to another\n\nStatement II:Hybridization and selection method involves sexual reproduction among the parent plants','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Conventional plant breeding is also referred to as hybridization and selection method. In conventional plant breeding, both the source and target plants are of the same crop species. The resistance genes from one variety are transferred to another variety of the same crop species. This transfer of genes is achieved by sexual reproduction between the source and target plants (parent plants).','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51064,'biology','Root hair that develops from a region of','Maturation','PAGE 67, XI NCERT','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Elongation','Root cap','Meristematic activity','Maturation','NEW','11-067',NULL,NULL),
(51065,'biology','Q: In a longitudinal section of a root, starting from the tip upward, the four zones occur in the following orders','Root cap, cell division, cell enlargement, cell maturation','Fig.5.3 Page 67, XI NCERT','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Root cap, cell division, cell maturation, cell enlargement','Cell division, cell enlargement, cell maturation, root cap','Cell division, cell maturation, cell enlargement, root cap','Root cap, cell division, cell enlargement, cell maturation','NEW','11-067',NULL,NULL),
(51066,'biology','Sweet potato is a modified','Adventitious root','PAGE 67, XI NCERT','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Fibrous root','Rhizome','Adventitious root','Taproot','NEW','11-067',NULL,NULL),
(311907,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) Hairy leaves is a natural adaptation for resistance against insect pests\n\n(ii) Solid stems and smooth leaves are some examples of natural plant adaptations against pests\n\n(iii) Aspartic acid content in maize may determine the degree of resistance of the crop to stem borers \n\n(iv) Parbhani Kranti and Pusa Sawani are two superior varieties of okra having increased pest resistance\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are INCORRECT?','(iv) only','Many plants have hairy leaves that are associated with resistance to insect pests such as jassids and leaf beetle. Solid stems in wheat and smooth leaves in cotton are other adaptations to resist stem sawfly and bollworms. Aspartic acid content has been linked to resistance to certain pests in maize plants. Varieties with higher aspartic acid have increased resistance to maize stem borers. Parbhani Kranti and Pusa Sawani are both examples of superior okra varieties. Pusa Sawani is resistant to pests (shoot and fruit borer) but Parbhani Kranti has increased resistance to the yellow mosaic virus.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(ii) only','(ii) and (iv) only','(iv) only','(iii) and (iv) only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311908,'biology','Statement I: Superior varieties of beans and mustard have been developed by conventional breeding for resistance against aphids\n\nStatement II: Bt cotton is an example of pest resistant cotton developed by plant breeding techniques','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Pusa Sem 2 and Pusa Gaurav are varieties of flat beans and mustard, respectively that have been developed by plant breeding for increased resistance against various pests including aphids. Bt cotton is an example of pest resistant cotton plant. However, it is not developed by using plant breeding techniques. It is an example of genetically engineered plant wherein a gene from a bacteria (foreign gene) is introduced in the genome of a plant (such as cotton).','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311909,'biology','Assertion: Hidden hunger leads to reduced lifespan and mental abilities\n\nReason: Lack of micronutrients in diet is linked to increased risk of disease','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Hidden hunger refers to the state of malnutrition caused by deficiencies of micronutrients and proteins in diet. Deficiencies of essential micronutrients and vitamins is related to high risk of diseases, and reduced mental abilties and lifespan.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311910,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) Atlas 66 is a wheat variety with a high protein content\n\n(ii) Golden rice is an example of a biofortified food crop\n\n(iii) Plant breeding can be utilized for improving vitamin content in food crops\n\n(iv) Biochemical composition of crops such as amino acid content cannot be changed by plant breeding\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(i), (ii) and (iii) only','Atlas 66 is a variety of wheat having a high protein content. It has been used as a donor plant in breeding programmes. Biofortification refers to the improvement in the nutritional quality of a food crop by plant breeding or other biotechnological means. Golden rice is enriched by vitamin A that is not present in naturally occuring rice varieties. Hence, it is an example of biofortified food crop. Plant breeding has been utilized to increase the content of vitamins in a variety of crops such as vitamin A enriched carrots, vitamin C enriched bitter gourds, etc. The biochemical composition of crops can be changed by plant breeding, including amino acid content, oil quality and quantity, vitamin production, etc. A maize variety with significant higher production of amino acids has been developed.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) and (iii) only','(i), (iii) and (iv) only','(i), (ii) and (iii) only','(ii), (iii) and (iv) only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311911,'biology','Assertion: Conventional plant breeding cannot be applied to develop golden rice\n\nReason: A rice variety enriched with high content of iron has been developed','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','Golden rice is a variety of rice that has been genetically engineered to produce beta-carotene, a precursor of Vitamin A and store it in the edible grain. Genes from other plants and bacteria were added to the rice genome to achieve this. On the other hand, conventional plant breeding techniques involve the cross hybridization of different varieties of the same crop species to yield a superior recombinant. Thus, conventional breeding cannot be utilized to develop golden rice. A rice variety containing as much as five times iron as in commonly cultivated rice has been developed by breeding techniques.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311912,'biology','Assertion: Plant breeding techniques can be applied to develop a crop that produces a micronutrient which is absent in its germplasm collection\n\nReason: Vitamin A enriched carrots have been developed and released by ICMR, New Delhi','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Plant breeding techniques involve the cross hybridization of different varieties of the same crop species to yield a superior recombinant.The source of biofortification are in the same crop species. The source can be either a different cultivated variety, a wild variety or the germplasm of the crop. Thus, if the gene to a particular micronutrient is not available within the crop species\' gene pool/germplasm, then it cannot be enriched in a crop using plant breeding. Vitamin A is one of the most abundant micronutrient produced by carrots. Carrots enriched with high Vitamin A has been released by IARI, New Delhi.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51067,'biology','Q: Choose the mismatched pair of leaf character with its example:','Palmately compound - Alstonia','PAGE 71, XI NCERT','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Palmately compound - Alstonia','Alternate Phyllotaxy - China- rose','Leaf tendril - Pea','Opposite phyllotaxy - Calotropis','NEW','11-071',NULL,NULL),
(311913,'biology','Statement I: Shift from a plant based diet to meat-based diet can decrease the demand of cereals\n\nStatement II: Bacterial cells can be consumed as a source of dietary protein','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','A shift from a plant based diet to a meat-based one would further increase the demand of cereals. This is because it requires around 3-10 kg of grains to yield 1 kg of meat. Microbial cells can be grown on industrial scale as source of dietary proteins. These are referred to as single cell proteins (SCP).','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311914,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) Edible mushrooms are a type of single cell protein (SCP)\n\n(ii) Methylophilus methylotrophus is a cyanobacteria that can produce high amount of proteins\n\n(iii) Microscopic fungi can be a potential SCP\n\n(iv) All kinds of algae can be consumed as SCP\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(iii) only','Single cell protein refers to microbial cells that can be consumed as food. Edible mushrooms belong to the class of multicellular fungi. Hence, they are not a type of SCP. Methylophilus methylotrophus is not a cyanobacteria; it is a bacterial species that can produce a high amount of proteins. Microscopic fungi are mostly unicellular in nature and many of them are utilized in fermentation processes. Hence, they can be considered a potential candidate as an SCP. All kinds of algae cannot be consumed as SCP. Only those algae that have high nutritional value and do not produce toxic elements can be consumed, e.g. Spirulina.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) and (ii) only','(ii) and (iii) only','(i) and (iii) only','(iii) only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311915,'biology','Assertion: SCP consumption can reduce environmental pollution\n\nReason: Spirulina can grow in waste water from processing plants and sewage','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Some SCP, such as Spirulina can grow in waste water from processing plants, manure and sewage containing organic compounds. They can serve as rich source of proteins, minerals and carbohydrates. Thus, SCP consumption may indirectly lead to reduction in environmental pollution.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311916,'biology','Statement I: Whole plants can be grown in test tubes using explants \n\nStatement II: The composition of nutrient medium is important for the growth of plants in tubes','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Explants are parts of plants that have the ability to regenerate and can give rise to a whole plant. Whole plants can be grown in test tubes by culturing explants in specific nutrient media. The composition of nutrient medium is essential for the growth of plant. It must contain a carbon source such as sucrose, inorganic salts, growth regulators, vitamins, etc.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311917,'biology','Assertion: Tissue culture can be used to create identical genetic copies of plants\n\nReason: The capacity to regenerate a whole plant from a single cell/explant is called totipotency','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','Tissue culture can be used to create identical genetic copies of plants. In general, when an explant is cultured in test tubes, it gives rise to a whole plant. Every plant generated in this manner is genetically identical to the explant from which it grows. Hence, tissue culture can give rise to plant clones. These plants are referred to as somaclones. Totipotency refers to the capability of a cell/explant to regenerate into a whole cell.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311918,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) Tissue culture enables the growth of healthy plant from diseased plant\n\n(ii) Protoplasts are single plant cells with all cell structures that can be grown to give rise to whole plant\n\n(iii) It is not possible to fuse the characteristics of two different species using plant tissue culture\n\n(iv) Somatic hybrids are plants obtained after fusion of two different protoplasts and subsequent growth in test tube\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(i) and (iv) only','Tissue culture can be used to recover healthy plants from diseased plants. This is achieved by culturing and growing the apical or axillary meristem of the plant that does not infected by a pathogen e.g. virus. Protoplasts are single plant cells whose cell wall has been digested, in other words, they are plant cells with only one layer of plasma membrane. It is possible to fuse the characteristics of two different plant species using tissue culture. This is achieved by a technique called as somatic hybridization, wherein protoplasts from two different varieties or species of crops can be fused and grown in lab conditions to form a hybrid plant. These hybrid plants are referred to as somatic hybrids. Pomato is one of the most prominent example of somatic hybridization wherein protoplasts from tomato and potato were fused to generate pomato.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) and (iv) only','(i), (ii) and (iv) only','(ii) and (iii) only','(ii), (iii) and (iv) only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(311919,'biology','Statement I: Micropropagation involves the growth of a large number of plants by culturing explants in test tubes\n\nStatement II: Somatic hybridization is a technique involving the hybridization of two different plants obtained in micropropagation','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Micropropagation is a tissue culture method of obtaining a large number of plantlets by culturing explants in lab conditions. Somatic hybridization involves the fusion of protoplasts, single plant cells without a cell wall, to yield a hybrid protoplast which is grown into a hybrid plant.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51068,'biology','Which of the following shows whorled phyllotaxy?','Alstonia','PAGE 71, XI NCERT','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Mustard','China rose','Alstonia','Calotropis','NEW','11-071',NULL,NULL),
(311920,'biology','Assertion: Healthy plants can be grown by culturing apical meristem from diseased plants in vitro\n\nReason: Sterile conditions are a prerequisite in maintaining plant tissue culture','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','Healthy plants can be grown by culturing meristem from diseased plants in vitro because the apical meristem does not contain the pathogenic agent such as virus. Sterile conditions are maintained in plant tissue culture systems to prevent the unneccessary growth of bacteria, fungi and other microbes in the culture medium, which may hamper the growth of the cultured plants.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >>Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290101,'chemistry','The natural process used in polypeptides is ?','Copolymerisation','Copolymerisation has been used by nature in polypeptides which may contain as many as 20 different amino acids. \n \n In each step of condensation polymerisation, a distinct functionalised species is produced which is independent of each other, so this process is called as step growth polymerisation. \n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 433 Paragraph-1, Page- 437 , Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Copolymerisation','Homopolymerisation','Step growth polymerisation','Monomerisation','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290102,'chemistry','Assertion (A): The use of polymers has completely revolutionised industrial scenario.\n \n Reason (R): The polymers are the backbone of four major industries : plastics, elastomers, fibres and paints and varnishes.\n \n In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given\n below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','The use of polymers in the manufacture of plastic buckets, cups and saucers, children’s toys, packaging bags, synthetic clothing materials, automobile tyres, gears and seals, electrical insulating materials and machine parts has completely revolutionised the daily life as well as the industrial scenario.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 433 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is incorrect (R) is correct.','(A) is correct but (R) is incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290103,'chemistry','Polymers are not the backbone of','Cement industry','The use of polymers in the manufacture of plastic buckets, cups and saucers, children’s toys, packaging bags, synthetic clothing materials, automobile tyres, gears and seals, electrical insulating materials and machine parts has completely revolutionised the daily life as well as the industrial scenario. \n \n Indeed, the polymers are the backbone of four major industries viz. plastics, elastomers, fibres and paints and varnishes.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 433 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Plastics and fibres industry','Elastomer industry','Cement industry','Paints and Varnishes industry','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290104,'chemistry','Which statement is not true?','Monomers are linked to each other by either ionic bond or hydrogen bonds','The word ‘polymer’ is coined from two Greek words: poly means many and mer \n eans unit or part.\n \n The term polymer is defined as very large molecules having high molecular mass (10³ -10⁷u). \n \n These are also referred to as macromolecules, which are formed by joining of repeating structural units on a large scale\n \n The repeating units are derived from simple and reactive molecules known as monomers and are linked to each other by covalent bonds.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 433 Paragraph-3)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','The word \'polymer\' means many unit or part','Polymer are macromolecules','Monomers are simple and reactive molecules','Monomers are linked to each other by either ionic bond or hydrogen bonds','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290105,'chemistry','Which among the following can not be the molecular mass of a polymer','10⁹','The term polymer is defined as very large molecules having high molecular mass (10³ -10⁷u). \n \n These are also referred to as macromolecules, which are formed by joining of repeating structural units on a large scale.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 433 Paragraph-3)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','10³','10⁵','10⁷','10⁹','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290106,'chemistry','Polymers are referred as','macromolecules','The term polymer is defined as very large molecules having high molecular mass (10³ -10⁷u). \n \n These are also referred to as macromolecules, which are formed by joining of repeating structural units on a large scale.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 433 Paragraph-3)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','micromolecules','macromolecules','biomolecules','supermolecules','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290107,'chemistry','Which is of them is not true for monomers?','Monomers are non reactive molecules','The repeating structural units are derived from some simple and reactive molecules known as monomers and are linked to each other by covalent bonds. \n \n The process of formation of polymers from respective monomers is called polymerisation.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 433 Paragraph-3)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Monomers are the repeating structural units','Monomers are linked to each other by covalent bonds.','Polymerisation is formation of polymers from monomers','Monomers are non reactive molecules','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290108,'chemistry','Monomers are linked to each other by','covalent bond','The repeating structural units are derived from some simple and reactive molecules known as monomers and are linked to each other by covalent bonds. \n \n The process of formation of polymers from respective monomers is called polymerisation.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 433 Paragraph-3)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','ionic bond','covalent bond','hydrogen bond','peptide bond','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290109,'chemistry','Formation of polymers from respective monomers is :','Polymerisation','The repeating structural units are derived from some simple and reactive molecules known as monomers and are linked to each other by covalent bonds. \n \n The process of formation of polymers from respective monomers is called polymerisation.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 433 Paragraph-3)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Copolymerisation','Homopolymerisation','Polymerisation','Monomerisation','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290110,'biology','The universe expanded and hence, the temperature___________ according to the big bang theory.','Decreased','The universe expanded and hence, the temperature came down, according to the big bang theory.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','3','mcq','Increased','Decreased','First increased, the decreased','First decreased, then increased','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290111,'biology','The gases_________condensed under gravitation to form galaxies of the present-day universe.','Hydrogen and Helium','Hydrogen and Helium formed sometime later. The gases condensed under gravitation and formed the galaxies of the present-day universe.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','3','mcq','Hydrogen and Helium','Carbon and Nitrogen','Hydrogen and Nitrogen','Carbon and Helium','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290112,'biology','In the solar system of the milky way galaxy, the earth was supposed to have been formed about_________years back.','4.5 billion','In the solar system of the milky way galaxy, the earth was supposed to have been formed about 4.5 billion years back.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','3','mcq','2.5 billion','3.5 billion','4.5 billion','5.5 billion','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290113,'biology','In the solar system of the___________, the earth was supposed to have been formed about 4.5 billion years back.','Milky way galaxy','In the solar system of the milky way galaxy, the earth was supposed to have been formed about 4.5 billion years back.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','3','mcq','Canis Major Galaxy','Canis Minor galaxy','Cartwheel galaxy','Milky way galaxy','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290114,'biology','Initially, there was no _____on early earth.','Atmosphere','There was no atmosphere on early earth.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','1','mcq','Gases','Minerals','Atmosphere','All of them','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290115,'biology','All of these covered the surface of early Earth except for?','Nitrogen','Water vapour , methane,  CO₂ , and ammonia released from molten mass covered the surface.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','3','mcq','Water vapours','Methane','Nitrogen','Ammonia','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290116,'biology','Water was broken up into Hydrogen and O₂ by_________.','UV Rays','The UV rays from the sun broke up water into Hydrogen and O₂ and the lighter H₂ escaped.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','3','mcq','Radio waves','UV Rays','X-Rays','All of above','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290117,'biology','________escaped after water was broken into its elements by UV Rays.','Hydrogen','The UV rays from the sun broke up water into Hydrogen and O₂ and the lighter H₂ escaped.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','3','mcq','Oxygen','Hydrogen','Chlorine','Sodium','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290118,'biology','Which of the following gases combined to form water,  CO₂ , and other gases?','O₂ with Ammonia and Methane','O₂ is combined with ammonia and methane to form water, CO₂, and others.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','3','mcq','Hydrogen with Ammonia and Methane','O₂ and Sulphur','Helium with Ammonia and Methane','O₂ with Ammonia and Methane','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290119,'biology','O₂ is combined with ammonia and methane to form water, CO₂, and others. This led to the formation of_____,','Ozone layer','O₂ is combined with ammonia and methane to form water, CO₂, and others. The ozone layer was formed','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','2','mcq','Ozone layer','Sun','Milky way galaxy','Aurora borealis','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290120,'biology','Life appeared 500 million years after the formation of the earth, i.e., almost__________back.','4 billion years','Life appeared 500 million years after the formation of the earth, i.e., almost four billion years back','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','3','mcq','3 billion years','4 billion years','5 billion years','6 billion years','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290121,'biology','Life appeared______after the formation of the earth.','500 million years','Life appeared 500 million years after the formation of the earth, i.e., almost four billion years back','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','3','mcq','500 million years','600 million years','700 million years','800 million years','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290122,'biology','Early______thinkers thought units of life called spores were transferred to different planets including earth.','Greek','Early Greek thinkers thought units of life called spores were transferred to different planets including earth.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','3','mcq','Greek','Roman','British','Northern','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290123,'biology','Early Greek thinkers thought units of life called ___________ were transferred to different planets including earth.','Spores','Early Greek thinkers thought units of life called spores were transferred to different planets including earth.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','3','mcq','Pores','Micropores','Cells','Spores','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290124,'biology','___________ is still a favorite idea for some astronomers.','Panspermia','Panspermia is still a favourite idea for some astronomers.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','1','mcq','Spontaneous origin','Panspermia','Abiogenesis','Spontaneous generation','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290125,'biology','For a long time, it was also believed that life came out of _________, called the theory of spontaneous generation.','Decaying matter','For a long time, it was also believed that life came out of decaying and rotting matter like straw, mud, etc. This was the theory of spontaneous generation.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','3','mcq','Vegetative matter','Decaying matter','Healthy matter','Living matter','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290126,'biology','Which scientist dismissed the theory of spontaneous generation through his careful experimentation on Yeast?','Louis Pasteur','Louis Pasteur by careful experimentation demonstrated that life comes only from pre-existing life. He showed that in pre-sterilized flasks, life did not come from killed yeast while in another flask open to the air, new living organisms arose from ‘killed yeast’.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','2','mcq','Marie Curie','Charles Darwin','Louis Pasteur','Albert Einstein','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290127,'biology','Louis Pasteur by careful experimentation demonstrated that life comes only from ___________.','Pre-existing life','Louis Pasteur by careful experimentation demonstrated that life comes only from pre-existing life. He showed that in pre-sterilized flasks, life did not come from killed yeast while in another flask open to the air, new living organisms arose from ‘killed yeast’.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','3','mcq','Decaying matter','Pre-existing life','Amoeba','Rotten trees','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290128,'biology','In flasks open to_________, during Louis Pasteur\'s experiment, new organisms arose from killed yeast.','Air','Louis Pasteur by careful experimentation demonstrated that life comes only from pre-existing life. He showed that in pre-sterilized flasks, life did not come from killed yeast while in another flask open to the air, new living organisms arose from ‘killed yeast’.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','3','mcq','Air','Water','Light','All of above','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(290129,'biology','The question remaining unanswered after Louis Pasteur\'s experiments on Yeast was ___________.','How the first life form came on earth?','Louis Pasteur by careful experimentation demonstrated that life comes only from pre-existing life. He showed that in pre-sterilized flasks, life did not come from killed yeast while in another flask open to the air, new living organisms arose from ‘killed yeast’. Spontaneous generation theory was dismissed once and for all. However, this did not answer how the first life form came on the earth.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','3','mcq','How the first life form came on earth?','How water came on earth?','How the Big Bang occurred?','How did Sun\'s radiation reach earth?','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290130,'biology','__________suggested that the formation of life could have come by chemical evolution.','Oparin and Haldane','Oparin of Russia and Haldane of England proposed that the first form of life could have come from pre-existing non-living organic molecules (e.g. RNA, protein, etc.) and that formation of life was preceded by chemical evolution, i.e., the formation of diverse Organic molecules from inorganic constituents.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','2','mcq','Curie and Haldane','Darwin and Einstein','Oparin and Haldane','Darwin and Oparin','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290131,'biology','The formation of diverse organic molecules from inorganic constituents is-','Chemical evolution','Oparin of Russia and Haldane of England proposed that the first form of life could have come from pre-existing non-living organic molecules (e.g. RNA, protein, etc.) and that formation of life was preceded by chemical evolution, i.e., the formation of diverse organic molecules from inorganic constituents.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','3','mcq','Physical evolution','Chemical evolution','Biological evolution','Both b and c','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290132,'biology','In 1953, _________ created similar conditions as those in early Earth on a laboratory scale.','S.L. Miller','In 1953, S.L. Miller, an American scientist created similar conditions on a laboratory scale','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','3','mcq','Marie Curie','Charles Darwin','Louis Pasteur','S.L. Miller','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290133,'biology','S.L. Miller created electric discharge in a closed flask containing_____________ at 800°C.','CH₄, H₂, NH₃, and water vapor','S.L. Miller created electric discharge in a closed flask containing CH₄, H₂, NH₃, and water vapor at 800°C. He observed the formation of amino acids.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','3','mcq','CH₄, H₂, NH₄OH, and water moisture','CH₄, H₂, NH₃, and water vapor','CH₄, H₂, N₂, and water vapor','CH₄, H₂O, NH₃, and water vapor','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290134,'biology','S.L. Miller created electric discharge in a closed flask containing CH₄, H₂, NH₃, and water vapor at________.','800°C','S.L. Miller created electric discharge in a closed flask containing CH₄, H₂, NH₃, and water vapor at 800°C. He observed the formation of amino acids.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','3','mcq','Evolution','12-127','12-111','1000°C','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290135,'biology','S.L. Miller created electric discharge in a closed flask containing CH₄, H₂, NH₃, and water vapor at 800°C and observed the formation of_________.','Amino acids','S.L. Miller created electric discharge in a closed flask containing CH₄, H₂, NH₃, and water vapor at 800°C. He observed the formation of amino acids.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','3','mcq','Amino acids','Lipids','Glucose','All of above','NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290136,'biology','We know about how the first self-replicating metabolic capsule of life arose through extensive experiments and research.','FALSE','We have no idea about how the first self-replicating metabolic capsule of life arose.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-127','12-111',NULL),
(290137,'biology','The first non-cellular forms of life could have originated __________ years back.','3 billion','The first non-cellular forms of life could have originated 3 billion years back.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','3','mcq','2 billion','3 billion','4 billion','5 billion','NEW','12-128','12-112',NULL),
(290138,'biology','The first non-cellular forms of life would have been giant molecules like  _________.','RNA, and proteins and polysaccharides','The first non cellular forms of life could have originated 3 billion years back. They would have been giant molecules (RNA, Protein, Polysaccharides, etc.).','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','2','mcq','RNA , Fatty acids and proteins','RNA, and proteins and polysaccharides','DNA, fatty acids and protein','DNA, RNA and protein','NEW','12-128','12-112',NULL),
(290139,'biology','The first cellular form of life did not possibly originate till about ____________ years ago.','2000 million','The first cellular form of life did not possibly originate till about 2000 million years ago.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','3','mcq','2000 million','2000 trillion','2000 billion','5000 million','NEW','12-128','12-112',NULL),
(290140,'biology','All early life forms were _________.','Aquatic','The first cellular form of life did not possibly originate till about 2000 million years ago. These were probably single-cells. All life forms were in water environment only. This version of a biogenesis, i.e., the first form of life arose slowly through evolutionary forces from non-living molecules is accepted by majority.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Origin of Life','Evolution','1','mcq','Aerial','Terrestrial','Aquatic','Amphibians','NEW','12-128','12-112',NULL),
