(290208,'chemistry','Polymers are not classified on the basis of their','molecular mass','Polymers can also be classified on the basis of their structure, molecular forces or modes of polymerisation.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 434 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','structure','molecular forces','modes of polymerisation','molecular mass','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290209,'chemistry','Which among the following belongs to man-made polymers?','Buna-S and Buna-N','A variety of synthetic polymers as plastic (polythene), synthetic fibres (nylon 6,6) and synthetic rubbers (Buna - S) are examples of man-made polymers extensively used in daily life as well as in industry.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 434 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Classification',NULL,'2','mcq','Nylon-6,6 and Rayon','Rayon and Buna-N','Buna-S and Buna-N','Buna-S and Rayon','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290210,'chemistry','Plastic, fibres and Buna-S are:','Synthetic polymers','A variety of synthetic polymers as plastic (polythene), synthetic fibres (nylon 6,6) and synthetic rubbers (Buna - S) are examples of man-made polymers extensively used in daily life as well as in industry.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 434 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Biodegradable polymers','Synthetic polymers','Natural polymer','Semi-synthetic polymers','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290301,'chemistry','Addition polymerisation is a type of','chain growth polymerisation','There are two broad types of polymerisation reactions, i.e., the addition or chain growth polymerisation and condensation or step growth polymerisation.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 434 Paragraph-3)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','chain growth polymerisation','condensation polymerisation','step growth polymerisation','all of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290302,'chemistry','Step growth polymerisation is','condensation polymerisation','There are two broad types of polymerisation reactions, i.e., the addition or chain growth polymerisation and condensation or step growth polymerisation.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 434 Paragraph-3)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','chain growth polymerisation','condensation polymerisation','addition polymerisation','all of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290303,'chemistry','On the basis of mode of polymerisation, polymers can be classified as','addition and condensation polymers','There are two broad types of polymerisation reactions, i.e., the addition or chain growth polymerisation and condensation or step growth polymerisation.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 434 Paragraph-3)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','addition and condensation polymers','addition and homopolymers','homopolymers and copolymers','None of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290304,'chemistry','The monomers used in addition polymerisation are','unsaturated compounds','In this type of polymerisation, the molecules of the same monomer or different monomers add together on a large scale to form a polymer. \n \n The monomers used are unsaturated compounds, e.g., alkenes, alkadienes and their derivatives.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 434 Paragraph-4)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','unsaturated compounds','saturated compounds','bifunctional groups','all of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290305,'chemistry','The monomers used in addition polymerisation are','both a and c','In this type of polymerisation, the molecules of the same monomer or diferent monomers add together on a large scale to form a polymer. \n \n The monomers used are unsaturated compounds, e.g., alkenes, alkadienes and their derivatives.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 434 Paragraph-4)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','alkenes','alkanes','alkadienes','both a and c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290306,'chemistry','The chain growth polymerisation can take place through the formation of\n \n a. covalent compounds\n \n b. ionic species\n \n c. free radicals','b,c','This mode of polymerisation leads to an increase in chain length and chain growth can take place through the formation of either free radicals or ionic species.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 434 Paragraph-4)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','c only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290307,'chemistry','The chain growth polymerisation takes place most commonly through the formation of\n \n a. covalent compounds\n \n b. ionic species\n \n c. free radicals','c only','However, the free radical governed addition or chain growth polymerisation is the most common mode.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 434 Paragraph-4)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','c only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290308,'chemistry','Arrange the following according to their occurrence,\n \n I. Chain initiation \n \n II.Chain propagation,\n \n III.Chain termination','I>II>III','The process starts with the addition of phenyl free radical formed by the peroxide to the ethene double bond thus generating a new and larger free radical. \n \n This step is called chain initiating step. \n \n As this radical reacts with another molecule of ethene, another bigger sized radical is formed. \n \n The repetition of this sequence with new and bigger radicals carries the reaction forward and the step is termed as chain propagating step. \n \n Ultimately, at some stage the product radical thus formed reacts with another radical to form the polymerised product. \n \n This step is called the chain terminating step.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 434 Paragraph-5)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','I>II>III','III>II>I','II>III>I','I>III>II','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290309,'chemistry','The reaction mechanism of formation of polythene is.','free radical formation','Polythene is formed by free radical mechanism.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 434 Paragraph-5)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','ionic species formation','free radical formation','bifunctional group formation','all of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290310,'chemistry','Which of the following does not act as a free-radical generating initiator?','Ethyl alcohol','A variety of alkenes or dienes and their derivatives are polymerised in the presence of a free radical generating initiator (catalyst) like benzoyl peroxide, acetyl peroxide, tert-butyl peroxide, etc.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 434 Paragraph-5)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','tert-butyl peroxide','Acetyl peroxide','Benzoyl peroxide','Ethyl alcohol','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290311,'chemistry','Which of the following does act as a free-radical generating initiator in polythene synthesis?','Benzoyl peroxide','the polymerisation of ethene to polythene consists of heating or exposing to light a mixture of ethene with a small amount of benzoyl peroxide initiator.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 434 Paragraph-5)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','tert-butyl peroxide','Acetyl peroxide','Benzoyl peroxide','Ethyl alcohol','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290312,'chemistry','Determine the step of free radical mechanism shown in diagram?(n2901)','Chain initiation','The given Diagram shows chain initiation step. \n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 434 Paragraph-6)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Chain initiation','Chain propagation','Chain termination','Auto-saturation','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/290312q.png'),
(290313,'chemistry','What is the reagent omitted in the given diagram(n2902)','Benzoyl peroxide','The polymerisation of ethene to polythene consists of heating or exposing to light a mixture of ethene with a small amount of benzoyl peroxide initiator.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 434 Paragraph-5)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Acetyl peroxide','Benzoyl peroxide','Benzoate','tert-butyl peroxide','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/290313q.png'),
(290314,'chemistry','Name the marked compound in the diagram(n2903)','Phenyl radical','The process starts with the addition of phenyl free radical formed by the peroxide to the ethene double bond thus generating a new and larger free radical. \n \n This step is called chain initiating step. \n \n As this radical reacts with another molecule of ethene, another bigger sized radical is formed. \n \n The repetition of this sequence with new and bigger radicals carries the reaction forward and the step is termed as chain propagating step. \n \n Ultimately, at some stage the product radical thus formed reacts with another radical to form the polymerised product. \n \n This step is called the chain terminating step.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 434 Paragraph-5)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Phenyl radical','Benzoyl peroxide','Benzoate','ethene radical','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/290314q.png'),
(290315,'chemistry','Determine the step of the free radical mechanism shown in diagram(n2904)','Chain propagation','The repetition of this sequence with new and bigger radicals carries the reaction forward and the step is termed as chain propagating step.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 434 Paragraph-5 & Diagram- page 435)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Chain initiation','Chain propagation','Chain termination','Auto-saturation','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/290315q.png'),
(290316,'chemistry','Name the product of the reaction shown in the below diagram(n2905)','polythene','For termination of the long chain, these free radicals can combine in different ways to form polythene. \n \n One mode of termination of chain.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 435 Paragraph-1)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Phenyl radical','ethene radical','polythene intermediate','polythene','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/290316q.png'),
(290317,'chemistry','Determine the step of free radical mechanism shown in diagram(n2906)','Chain termination','For termination of the long chain, these free radicals can combine in different ways to form polythene. \n \n One mode of termination of chain.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 435 Paragraph-1)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Chain initiation','Chain propagation','Chain termination','Auto-saturation','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/290317q.png'),
(290318,'chemistry','Homopolymers and copolymers are the types of','addition polymers','The addition polymers formed by the polymerisation of a single monomeric species are known as homopolymers, for example polythene discussed above is a homopolymer.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 435 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','condensation polymers','addition polymers','linear polymers','branched polymers','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290319,'chemistry','The repeated addition of same monomer molecules having double or triple bonds is known as','homopolymer','The addition polymers formed by the polymerisation of a single monomeric species are known as homopolymers, for example polythene discussed above is a homopolymer.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 435 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','fibre','copolymer','homopolymer','elastomer','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290320,'chemistry','The monomer unit of polythene is','ethene','The addition polymers formed by the polymerisation of a single monomeric species are known as homopolymers, for example polythene discussed above is a homopolymer.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 435 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','ethene','ethyne','ethane','ethanol','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290321,'chemistry','Copolymers are','polymers made by addition polymerisation from two different monomers','The polymers made by addition polymerisation from two different monomers are termed as copolymers. \n \n Buna-S, which is formed by polymerisation of buta–1, 3–diene and styrene is an example of copolymer formed by addition polymerisation.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 435 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','polymers made by addition polymerisation from two different monomers','polymers made by condensation polymerisation from two different monomers','polymers made by condensation polymerisation of a single monomer','polymers made by addition polymerisation of a single monomer','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290322,'chemistry','Buna-S is a copolymer of','1, 3-butadiene and styrene','The polymers made by addition polymerisation from two different monomers are termed as copolymers. \n \n Buna-S, which is formed by polymerisation of buta–1, 3–diene and styrene is an example of copolymer formed by addition polymerisation.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 435 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','ethene and styrene','1, 3-butadiene and ethene','1, 3-butadiene and styrene','ethene and propene','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290323,'chemistry','Identify the missing reagent in the diagram(n2907)','1,3-butadiene','The polymers made by addition polymerisation from two different monomers are termed as copolymers. \n \n Buna-S, which is formed by polymerisation of buta–1, 3–diene and styrene is an example of copolymer formed by addition polymerisation.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 435 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','ethene','styrene','1,3-butadiene','acrylonitrile','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/290323q.png'),
