(291009,'chemistry','Which polymer is used in the manufacture of paints and lacquers?','Glyptal','Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 443 Paragraph-Table) diagram(ne2901)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','Bakelite','Glyptal','Polypropene','Polyvinyl chloride','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291010,'chemistry','The formation of which of the following polymers involves hydrolysis reaction?','Nylon 6','Nylon 6: \n\nIt is obtained by heating caprolactum with water at a high temperature.\n\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 437 Paragraph-8) Refer diagram(ne2952)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','Nylon 6','Bakelite','Nylon 6,6','Terylene','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291011,'biology','The wings of a bird and the wings of an insect are [AIPMT 2015]','Analogous structures and represent convergent evolution','Analogous organs are the organs which have similar function but are different in their structural details and origin. The analogous structures are the result of convergent evolution. The wings of an insect are analogous to wings of a bird because the basic structure of the wings of the insects is different from the wings of bird. However, their function is similar.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Phylogenetic structures and represent divergent evolution','Homologous structures and represent convergent evolution','Homologous structures and represent divergent evolution','Analogous structures and represent convergent evolution','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(40503,'chemistry','Which statement is wrong regarding the conditions to write lewis structure:\n\na. Inner shell electrons are involved in cehmical bond\n\nb. Each combining atom contributes atleast one electron\n\nc. The combining atoms attain the  outer shell noble gas configuration as result of bonding\n\nd. We can\'t write lewis structure for molecules having dfferent atoms','Only a and d','Elements attain stability by attainiing noble gas configuration. \n\nThey reach the octet configuration by tranferring or sharing their electrons with other atoms. \n\nThe inner shell electrons are not free to involve in chemical reaction.\n\nAtoms contribute electrons to form bonds. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Covalent bond',NULL,'1','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c','Only a and d','NEW','11-102','11-102',NULL),
(291012,'biology','Industrial melanism is an example of [AIPMT 2015]','Natural selection','Natural selection is the most widely accepted theory concerning the principal causal mechanism of evolutionary change profounded by Charles Darwin and Alfred Russel Wallace. It results from the differential reproduction (some members of a population produce abundant offspring, some only a few and still others none), one phenotype as compared with other phenotypes in the same population. This determines the relative share of different genotypes which individuals possess and propagate in a population. Industrial melanism supports evolution by natural selection. It is an adaptation where the moths living in the industrial areas developed melanin pigments to match their bodies to the tree trunks.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Mutation','Neo-Lamarckism','Neo-Darwinism','Natural selection','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291013,'biology','The forelimbs of cats, and lizards used in walking; the forelimbs of whales used in swimming, and the forelimbs of bats used in flying are an example of [AIPMT 2014]','Homologous organs','The organs which have the same fundamental structure but are different in functions are called homologous organs. These organs follow the same basic plan of organisation during their development. But in the adult condition, these organs are modified to perform different functions as an adaptation to different environments. The forelimbs of cat, lizard, whale and bat have the same basic structural plan. In each case the forelimb consists of humerus, radio-ulna, carpals, metacarpals and digits. The skeletal parts of the forelimbs of all these vertebrates are similar in structure and arrangement. But the forelimbs of these animals have different shapes and functions. In lizard they are used for walking, in cat for running, in whale for swimming and in bat for flying.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Analogous organs','Adaptive radiation','Homologous organs','Convergent evolution','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291014,'biology','Which one of the following are analogous structures? [AIPMT 2014]','Both Wings of bat and wings of pigeon & Gills of prawn and lungs of man.','The organs which have similar functions but are different in their structural details and origin are called analogous organs. The wings of bat are analogous to wings of pigeon. It is due to the fact that the basic structure of the wings of the mammal is different from the wings of bird. However, their function is similar. Similarly, gills of prawn and lungs of man, both are respiratory organs yet they have very different basic structure.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Wings of bat and wings of pigeon','Gills of prawn and lungs of man','Thorns of Bougainvillea and tendrils of Cucurbita','Both Wings of bat and wings of pigeon & Gills of prawn and lungs of man.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291015,'biology','The process by which organisms with different evolutionary histories evolve similar phenotypic adaptations in response to a common environmental challenge is called [NEET 2013]','Convergent evolution','Convergent evolution is the development of superficially similar structures in unrelated organisms, usually because the organisms live in the same kind of environment. Examples are the wings of insects and birds and the streamlined bodies of whales and fish. One can say that it is the similar habitat that has resulted in selection of similar adaptive features in different groups of organisms but toward the same function. An example of convergent evolution is the similar nature of the flight/wings of insects, birds, pteridosaurs and bats. All four serve the same function and are similar in structure, but each evolved independently. Some species of the lens of eyes also evolved independently in various animals.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Non-random evolution','Adaptive radiation','Natural selection','Convergent evolution','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291016,'biology','The eye of an octopus and the eye of a cat show different patterns of structure, yet they perform similar functions. This is an example of [NEET 2013]','Analogous organs that have evolved due to convergent evolution','Eye of octopus, bat and man are examples of analogous organs showing convergent evolution which the development of superficially similar structures in unrelated organisms, usually because the organisms live in the same kind of environment.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Analogous organs that have evolved due to convergent evolution','Analogous organs that have evolved due to divergent evolution','Homologous organs that have evolved due to convergent evolution','Homologous organs that have evolved due to divergent evolution','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291101,'chemistry','Assertion: Polyesters are used as glass reinforcing materials in safety helmets.\n\nReason: Dacron fibre (terylene) is crease resistant and is used in blending with cotton and wool fibres.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','The assertion that polyesters are used as glass reinforcing materials in safety helmets is true. The reason that Dacron fibre (terylene) is crease resistant and is used in blending with cotton and wool fibres is also true and explains why polyesters are useful in safety helmets. The crease-resistant property of Dacron fibre makes it a good choice for blending with other fibres to create materials that are resistant to wrinkling and stretching, which is important in the production of safety helmets.','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291102,'chemistry','Assertion: Bakelite is a thermosetting polymer.\n\nReason: Bakelite can be reused or remoulded.','Assertion is true but reason is false','The assertion that Bakelite is a thermosetting polymer is true. This means that once it is formed, it cannot be melted and re-moulded like a thermoplastic polymer can. However, the reason that Bakelite can be reused or remoulded is false. Once Bakelite is formed, it cannot be reused or remoulded, which is a characteristic of thermosetting polymers','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291103,'chemistry','Assertion: Nylon 6 is used for the manufacture of tyre cords, fabrics, and ropes.\n\nReason: Nylon 6 is a homopolymer.','Assertion is false but reason is true','The assertion that Nylon 6 is used for the manufacture of tyre cords, fabrics, and ropes is true. However, the reason that Nylon 6 is a homopolymer is false. Nylon 6 is actually a copolymer of caprolactam and water.','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291104,'chemistry','Assertion: Bakelite is a thermosetting polymer that cannot be reused or remoulded.\n\nReason: Bakelite is formed by cross-linking of linear chains of the polymer cerelac.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Bakelite is a thermosetting polymer that undergoes cross-linking of linear chains of the polymer novolac, which forms an infusible solid mass. This means that once Bakelite has been formed, it cannot be melted or remoulded, making it a permanent material that cannot be reused.','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291105,'chemistry','Assertion: The vulcanization process improves the physical properties of natural rubber.\n\nReason: Natural rubber becomes soft at high temperature and brittle at low temperatures and shows low water absorption capacity.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Natural rubber shows high water absorption capacity.\n"Vulcanisation of rubber: \n\nNatural rubber becomes soft at high temperature (>335 K) and brittle at low temperatures (<283 K) and shows high water absorption capacity. \n\nIt is soluble in nonpolar solvents and is non-resistant to attack by oxidising agents. \n\nTo improve upon these physical properties, a process of vulcanisation is carried out.\n\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 440 Paragraph-6)"','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291106,'chemistry','Assertion: Butadiene-styrene copolymer is a good substitute for natural rubber.\n\nReason: Copolymers have properties quite different from homopolymers.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Copolymers have properties that are quite different from homopolymers. For example, the butadiene-styrene copolymer is quite tough and is a good substitute for natural rubber.','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291107,'chemistry','Assertion: The molecular mass of polymers is always expressed as a single value.\n\nReason: The growth of polymer chains during synthesis is independent of the availability of monomers in the reaction mixture.','Both Assertion and reason are false','The molecular mass of polymers is not expressed as a single value, but as an average due to the presence of chains of varying lengths in the polymer sample. The reason is incorrect because the growth of polymer chains during synthesis is dependent upon the availability of monomers in the reaction mixture.','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291108,'chemistry','Assertion: Aliphatic polyesters are biodegradable polymers.\n\nReason: They contain functional groups that are similar to those present in biopolymers','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Aliphatic polyesters are one of the important classes of biodegradable polymers.\n Aliphatic polyesters are biodegradable polymers that contain functional groups similar to those present in biopolymers, such as hydroxyl and carboxyl groups, which make them susceptible to microbial degradation\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 442 Paragraph-3)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291109,'chemistry','Assertion: Nylon 2-nylon 6 is an alternating copolymer of glycine and amino caproic acid.\n\nReason: Nylon 2-nylon 6 is a homopolymer of amino caproic acid.','Both Assertion and reason are false','Nylon 2-nylon 6 is an alternating copolymer of glycine and amino caproic acid, which makes the assertion false. \n\nThe reason is also false because Nylon 2-nylon 6 is not a homopolymer of amino caproic acid.','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291110,'chemistry','Assertion: Buna-N is obtained by the copolymerization of 1, 3 – buta–1, 3–diene and acrylonitrile in the presence of a peroxide catalyst.\n\nReason: Buna-N is used for making oil seals, tank lining, etc., because of its superior resistance to the action of petrol, lubricating oil, and organic solvents.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Buna-N is a synthetic rubber that is obtained by the copolymerization of 1, 3 – buta–1, 3–diene and acrylonitrile in the presence of a peroxide catalyst. It is used for making oil seals, tank lining, etc., because of its superior resistance to the action of petrol, lubricating oil, and organic solvents.','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291111,'chemistry','Assertion: Neoprene or polychloroprene is resistant to vegetable and mineral oils.\n\nReason: Neoprene is formed by the free radical polymerization of chloroprene','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Buna-N is a synthetic rubber that is obtained by the copolymerization of 1, 3 – buta–1, 3–diene and acrylonitrile in the presence of a peroxide catalyst. It is used for making oil seals, tank lining, etc., because of its superior resistance to the action of petrol, lubricating oil, and organic solvents.','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(40504,'chemistry','Which one is not following octet rule?','PCl₅','PCl₅ have 10 electrons in outer shell of P so not following octet rule. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Covalent bond',NULL,'2','mcq','H₂O','PCl₅','CO₂','HCl','NEW','11-102','11-102',NULL),
(291112,'chemistry','Assertion: Nylon is a synthetic condensation polymer.\n\nReason: Nylon is formed by the condensation reaction of a diamine and a diacid.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Nylon is a synthetic polymer that is formed by the condensation reaction of a diamine and a diacid. In this reaction, water is released as a byproduct. The reaction involves the formation of amide bonds between the monomers, resulting in the formation of a long-chain polymer. This process is called condensation polymerization.\n\nTherefore, both the Assertion and Reason are true, and the Reason correctly explains the Assertion.','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291113,'chemistry','Assertion: Teflon is a chemically inert and corrosion-resistant material.\n\nReason: Teflon is manufactured by heating tetrafluoroethene with a free radical or persulphate catalyst at high pressures.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Teflon is manufactured by heating tetrafluoroethene with a free radical or persulphate catalyst at high pressures. The resulting material, Teflon, is known for its chemical inertness and resistance to corrosive reagents.','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291114,'chemistry','Assertion: The addition polymerization of appropriate alkenes or their derivatives forms polymers such as Polythene, Teflon, and Orlon.\n\nReason: The condensation polymerization of bi- or polyfunctional monomers containing -NH2, -OH, and -COOH groups forms polymers such as Nylon, Bakelite, and Dacron.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion that the addition polymerization of appropriate alkenes or their derivatives forms polymers such as Polythene, Teflon, and Orlon is true. The reason given, that the condensation polymerization of bi- or polyfunctional monomers containing -NH2, -OH, and -COOH groups forms polymers such as Nylon, Bakelite, and Dacron, is also true. However, the reason is not the correct explanation for the assertion as the two processes, addition polymerization and condensation polymerization, are distinct and occur through different mechanisms. Therefore, both the assertion and the reason are true, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291201,'biology','Random unidirectional change in allele frequencies that occurs by chance in all populations and especially in small populations is known as [Karnataka NEET 2013]','Genetic drift','Genetic drift (Sewall Wright effect) is the random change in the frequency of alleles in a population over successive generations in the gametes. Each new generation differs from its parental generation with regard to allele frequencies simply because of random variation in the distribution of gametes. This process is more rapid in smaller populations, or when the alleles concerned confer no apparent benefit compared to their counterparts.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Migration','Natural selection','Genetic drift','Mutation','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(291202,'biology','Which of the following statements is correct about the origin and evolution of man? [Odisha NEET 2019]','Neanderthal men lived in Asia between 1,00,000 and 40,000 years back','Agriculture came around 10,000 years back and human settlements started. About 15 mya primates called Dryopithecus and Ramapithecus were existing. They were hairy and walked like gorillas and chimpanzees. Homo erectus probably ate meat.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Agriculture came around 50,000 years back','The Dryopithecus and Ramapithecus primates existed 15 million years ago and walked like men.','Homo habilis probably ate meat.','Neanderthal men lived in Asia between 1,00,000 and 40,000 years back','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291203,'biology','The chronological order of human evolution from early to the recent is [NEET-II 2016]','Ramapithecus→Australopithecus→Homo habilis →homo erectus','The fossils of Ramapithecus were discovered by Edward Levis from the Pliocene rocks of Shivalik hills in India. They were present in the Pliocene era nearly 14-15 million years ago (mya). Australopithecus was the first ape-man found in African Pliocene era rocks present 5 mya. Homo habilis was believed to be present in East Africa 2 mya in the Pliocene era. Homo erectus appeared nearly 1.5 mya with a cranial capacity of 800-1300 cc.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Australopithecus→Ramapithecus→Homo habilis→homo erectus','Ramapithecus→Australopithecus→Homo habilis →homo erectus','Ramapithecus→Homo habilis→australopithecus→homo erectus','Australopithecus→Homo habilis→ramapithecus →homo erectus','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291204,'biology','Which of the following had the smallest brain capacity? [2015 Cancelled]','Homo habilis','The brain capacity gradually increased from early human ancestors to modern man. Homo Habilis had 650 - 800 cc brain capacity which increased around 900 ccs in Homo erectus. The Neanderthal man (Homo neanderthalensis) had 1400 cc brain capacity which evolved to around 1450 cc in Homo sapiens.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Homo neanderthalensis','Homo habilis','Homo erectus','Homo sapiens','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291205,'biology','From his experiments, S.L. Miller produced amino acids by mixing the following in a closed flask [NEET 2020]','CH₄, H₂, NH₃, and water vapor at 800°C','In 1953, S.L. Miller, an American scientist created similar conditions in a laboratory scale. He created electric discharge in a closed flask containing CH₄, H₂, NH₃, and water vapor at 800°C and observed formation of amino acids','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','CH₄, H₂, NH₃, and water vapor at 800°C','CH₃, H₂, NH₄ and water vapor and water vapor at 800°C','CH₄, H₂, NH₃, and water vapor at 600°C','CH₃, H₂, NH₃ and water vapor at 600°C','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291206,'biology','Which of the following refer to the correct example(s) of organisms which have evolved due to changes in the environment brought about by anthropogenic action? [NEET 2020] \n(1) Darwin’s Finches of Galapagos islands. \n(2) Herbicide-resistant weeds. \n(3) Drug-resistant eukaryotes. \n(4) Man-created breeds of domesticated animals like dogs.','(2), (3) and (4)','Herbicide resistant weeds, drug resistant eukaryotes and man-created breeds of domesticated animals like dogs are examples of evolution by anthropogenic action. Darwin’s Finches of Galapagos islands are example of natural selection, adaptive radiation and Founder’s effect.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','only (1)','(1) and (3)','(2), (3) and (4)','only (4)','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291207,'biology','Embryological support for evolution was disapproved by\n [NEET 2020]','Karl Ernst von Baer','Embryological support for evolution was disapproved by Karl Ernst von Baer. He noted that embryos never pass through the adult stages of other animals','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Karl Ernst von Baer','Alfred Wallace','Charles Darwin','Oparin','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291208,'biology','Flippers of penguins and dolphins are examples [NEET 2020]','Convergent evolution','Analogous structures are result of convergent evolution, i.e., different structures evolving for the same function and hence having similarity. For example the eye of the octopus and of mammals, the flippers of penguins and dolphins.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','Adaptive radiation','Convergent evolution','Industrial melanism','Natural selection','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291209,'biology','The similarity of bone structure in the forelimbs of many vertebrates is an example [NEET 2018]','Homology','Homologous organs are those organs that have the same fundamental structure but are different in functions. The wings of birds and forelimbs of horses show homology because skeletal parts of their forelimbs are similar in structure and arrangement but have different functions.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','Homology','Analogy','Convergent evolution','Adaptive radiation','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291210,'biology','Among the following sets of examples for divergent evolution, select the incorrect option [NEET 2018]','Eye of octopus, bat, and man','Eye of octopus, bat, and man are examples of analogous organs as they all help in vision but are different in structure showing convergent evolution. The forelimbs, heart, and brain of the mentioned mammals perform different functions in each organism while similar in structure, hence they are homologous organs.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Forelimbs of man, bat and cheetah','Heart of bat, man, and cheetah','The brain of the bat, man, and cheetah','Eye of octopus, bat, and man','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291211,'biology','Variations caused by mutation, as proposed by Hugo de Vries, are [NEET 2019]','Random and directionless','Hugo de Vries (1901) put forward the theory of evolution called mutation theory. He believed that mutation causes evolution. Mutations are discontinuous variations that are random and directionless.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Small and directionless','Random and directional','Random and directionless','Small and directional','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291212,'biology','According to Hugo de Vries, the mechanism of evolution is [NEET 2018]','Saltation','Hugo de Vries (1901) proposed the mutation theory of evolution and stated that evolution is a jerky process in which new species are evolved due to discontinuous sudden variations or saltation. These are the single-step large mutations occurring in the population.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Multiple step mutations','Saltation','Phenotypic variations','Minor mutations','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291213,'biology','A gene locus has two alleles A, a. If the frequency of dominant allele A is 0.4, then what will be the frequency of homozygous dominant, heterozygous and homozygous recessive individuals in the population? [NEET 2018]','0.16 (AA); 0.48 (Aa); 0.36 (aa)','Frequency of dominant allele (A) = 0.4 Applying Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium; p + q = 1 q = 1 – 0.4 = 0.6; p2 + q2 + 2pq = 1 Frequency of homozygous dominant genotype (p2  AA) = (0.4)2 = 0.16 Frequency of heterozygous genotype (2pq  Aa) = 2 × 0.4 × 0.6 = 0.48Frequency of homozygous recessive genotype (q2  aa) = (0.6)2 = 0.36','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','0.16 (AA); 0.36 (Aa); 0.48 (aa)','0.36 (AA); 0.48 (Aa); 0.16 (aa)','0.16 (AA); 0.24 (Aa); 0.36 (aa)','0.16 (AA); 0.48 (Aa); 0.36 (aa)','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291214,'biology','The factor that leads to the Founder effect in a population Is [NEET 2021]','Genetic drift','A founder effect occurs when a new colony is started by a few members of the original population. This small population size means that the colony may have: reduced genetic variation from the original population. a non-random sample of the genes in the original population.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Genetic drift','Natural selection','Genetic recombination','Mutation','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291215,'biology','Match the following [NEET 2021]','Adaptive radiation-Darwin Finches, Convergent evolution-Wings of Butterfly and Bird, Divergent evolution-Bones forelimbs in Man and Whale, Evolution by anthropogenic action-Selection of resistant varieties due to excessive use of herbicides and pesticides.','Adaptive radiation is a change that occurs in the organism by adapting according to the environment, e.g. Darwin finches. Convergent evolution is a process where distant species develop similar structures, e.g. wings of butterflies and birds. Divergent evolution is a process by which an inbreeding species diverges into two descendant species, e.g. bones of forelimbs in men and whales. Evolution by anthropogenic action means evolution occurring due to human activities, e.g. selection of resistant varieties due to excessive use of herbicides and pesticides.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','match','Adaptive radiation,Darwin Finches','Convergent evolution,Wings of Butterfly and Bird','Divergent evolution,Bones forelimbs in Man and Whale','Evolution by anthropogenic action,Selection of resistant varieties due to excessive use of herbicides and pesticides','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291216,'biology','Natural selection where more individuals acquire specific character value other than the mean character value, leads to [NEET 2022]','Directional change','Natural selection can lead to stabilization (in which more individuals acquire mean character value), directional change (more individuals acquire value other than the mean character value), or disruption (more individuals acquire peripheral character value at both ends of the distribution curve).','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','Stabilising change','Directional change','Disruptive change','Random change','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291217,'biology','Which of the following statements is not true?[NEET 2022]','Flippers of penguins and dolphins are a pair of homologous organs','Homology indicates common ancestry as homologous organs are similar in structure but different in functions according to their needs while analogous organs are opposite to it. Hence flippers of penguins and dolphins are analogous organs as they help in swimming in spite of different structures and the same for the potato and sweet potato, they are analogous to each other.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Analogous structures are a result of convergent evolution','Sweet potato and potato is an example of analogy','Homology indicates common ancestry','Flippers of penguins and dolphins are a pair of homologous organs','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291218,'biology','“Homo sapiens” implies [NEET 2013]','Human beings','Homo sapiens are simply human beings. Humans belong to an order of mammals called primates and are placed in the family Hominidae. Hominidae includes modern and archaic human beings and also consists of neanderthal, a subspecies of Homo sapiens. Humans and their ancestors are more closely related to the apes.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Human race','Human beings','Modern man','None of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291219,'biology','Thorn of Bougainvillea and tendril of Cucurbita are examples of [AIPMT 2013]','Homologous organs','Thorn of Bougainvillea and tendril of Cucurbita are examples of homologous organs.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Analogous organs','Homologous organs','Vestigial organs','Retrogressive evolution','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291220,'biology','The forelimbs of cats and lizards are used in walking; the forelimbs of whales are used in swimming, and the forelimbs of bats used in flying are examples of [AIPMT 2014]','Homologous organs','Organs that have a common fundamental anatomical plan and similar embryonic origin whatever various functions they may perform are regarded as homologous organs. For example, the flippers of a whale, a bat\'s wing, the fore-limb of a horse, a bird\'s wing, and the forelimbs of a human are structurally as well as functionally different.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Analogous organs','Adaptive radiation','Homologous organs','Convergent evolution','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291221,'biology','The diagram represents Miller\'s experiment. Choose the correct combination of labeling. [ NEET 2015 ] Refer to the diagram (2901)','A–electrodes, B– NH3 + H₂ + H₂O + CH4, C–cold water, D–vacuum, E–U trap','Figure 7.1NCERT Class 12 Page no: 128 diagram matic representation of Miller’s experiment','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','A–electrodes, B– NH3 + H₂ + H₂O + CH4, C–cold water, D–vacuum, E–U trap','A–electrodes, B–NH4 + H₂ + CO₂ + CH3, C–hot water, D–vacuum, E–U trap','A–electrodes, B–NH3 + H₂O, C–hot water, D–tap, E–U trap','A–electrodes, B–NH3 + H₂ + H₂O + CH4, C–steam, D– vacuum, E–U trap','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/291221q.png'),
(291222,'biology','A population is in Hardy- Weinberg equilibrium for a gene with only two alleles. If the gene frequency of an allele A is 0.7, the genotype frequency of Aa is [AIPMT 2014, 2016]','0.42','It can be calculated by the formula given in NCERT, p²+2pq+q²=1>>Aa=2pq=2×0.7×0.3=0.42','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','0.21','0.42','0.36','0.7','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291223,'biology','According to the Hardy-Weinberg principle, allele and genotype frequencies in a population will remain constant from generation to generation in the absence of other evolutionary influences. It makes several assumptions which were given below. \ni.Random Mating\nii. Sexual Reproduction\niii.Non-overlapping Generations \niv.Occurrence of Natural Selection\nv.Small size of the population. \nIdentify two assumptions that do not meet for the population to reach Hardy-Weinberg Equilibrium.  NEET 2017]','iv and v','The occurrence of natural selection and the small size of the population does not meet the criteria for a population to reach Hardy-Weinberg Equilibrium. For Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium to be reached, natural selection should not be occurring. If populations are undergoing natural selection at the locus under consideration, allele frequencies will be continuously changing in a specific direction and Hardy-Weinberg Equilibrium predicts that allele frequencies will stay constant. It assumes that the population size is very large.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','iv and v','ii and iv','iii, iv and v','i, ii and iii','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291224,'biology','Which of the following was most similar to modern man? [2017]','Cro-Magnon man','The Cro-Magnon which is one of the fossils belonging to the Homo Sapiens species, it\'s skeleton specially the skull was almost identical to the modern man.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Java man','Neanderthal man','Homo habilis','Cro-Magnon man','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291225,'biology','Assertion: Organic compounds, that first evolved on earth, required for the origin of life were protein and nucleic acid. \n\nReason: All lives were formed in a water environment only. [NEET 2016]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','Organic compounds that first evolved on the earth where ich was required for the origin of life were protein and nucleic acid. All life forms were in an aquatic environment only.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are wrong.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291226,'biology','Identify the fossil of man who showed the following \ncharacteristics. \n (A) Brain capacity of 1400 cc \n (B) Used hides to protect their body \n (C) Buried their dead bodies \n In the light of above statements, choose the correct \nanswer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(2) Neanderthal man','Neanderthal with a brain size of 1400cc lived in near East and central Asia, Europe and North Africa between 100000 to 40000 year ago. They used hides to protect their body and buried their dead. It is mentioned that Neanderthal man exists later than Homo erectus and Ramapithecus.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','(2) Neanderthal man','(1) Homo erectus','(3) Homo habilits','(4) Australopithecus','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291227,'biology','Select the correct group/set of Australian Marsupials exhibiting adaptive radiation.','(1) Numbat, Spotted cuscus, Flying phalanger','Australian Marsupials - Numbat, Spotted cuscus, Flying phalanger.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Numbat, Spotted cuscus, Flying phalanger','(2) Mole, Flying squirrel, Tasmanian tiger cat','(3) Lemur, Anteater, Wolf','(4) Tasmanian wolf, Bobcat, Marsupial mole','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291228,'biology','Select the correct statement regarding mutation theory of evolution. [ RE NEET 2022]','(3) Single step large mutation is a cause of speciation','Hugo de Vries, based on his work on evening primrose brought forth the idea of mutations. He believed that mutation caused speciation and hence called it saltation (Single step large mutation).\n\nOption (a) is incorrect because mutations are sudden (random) and directionless.\n\nOption (b) is incorrect because mutation theory was proposed by Hugo de Vries.\n\nOption (c) is incorrect because Darwinian variations are small and directional changes, but they are not related to mutation theory of evolution.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) This theory was proposed by Alfred Wallace','(2) Variations are small directional changes','(3) Single step large mutation is a cause of speciation','(4) Large differences due to mutations arise gradually in a population','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
