(40505,'chemistry','Which one has single covalent bond?','PCl₅','When two atoms share one electron pair they form single covalent bond. \n\nPCl5 forms single covalent bond, while SO2, C2H4, CO2 form double bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Covalent bond',NULL,'1','mcq','SO₂','C₂H₄','CO₂','PCl₅','NEW','11-102','11-102',NULL),
(291301,'biology','Select the incorrect statement with respect to Evolution and Phylogeny','To understand the changes in flora and fauna that have occurred over millions of years on earth, it is not necessary to understand the context of origin of life,','Evolutionary Biology is the study of history of life forms on earth. What exactly is evolution? To understand the changes in flora and fauna that have occurred over millions of years on earth, we must have an understanding of the context of origin of life, i.e., evolution of earth, of stars and indeed of the universe itself. What follows is the longest of all the construed and conjectured stories. This is the story of origin of life and evolution of life forms or biodiversity on planet earth in the context of evolution of earth and against the background of evolution of universe itself.When we look at stars on a clear night sky we are, in a way, looking back in time. Stellar distances are measured in light years. What we see today is an object whose emitted light started its journey millions of year back and from trillions of kilometres away and reaching our eyes now\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 126','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Evolutionary Biology is the study of history of life forms on earth','To understand the changes in flora and fauna that have occurred over millions of years on earth, it is not necessary to understand the context of origin of life,','When we look at stars on a clear night sky we are, in a way, looking back in time. Stellar distances are measured in light years.','Stellar distances are measured in light years,an object is viewed using a telescope because emitted light started its journey millions of year back and from trillions of kilometres away and reaching our eyes now','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291302,'biology','Select the incorrect statement with respect to Evolution and Phylogeny','The universe is very old – almost 20 million years old, with huge clusters of galaxies','However, when we see objects in our immediate surroundings we see them instantly and hence in the present time. Therefore, when we see stars we apparently are peeping into the past.The origin of life is considered a unique event in the history of universe. The universe is vast. Relatively speaking the earth itself is almost only a speck. The universe is very old – almost 20 billion years old. Huge clusters of galaxies comprise the universe. Galaxies contain stars and clouds of gas and dust. Considering the size of universe, earth is indeed a speck. The Big Bang theory attempts to explain to us the origin of universe. It talks of a singular huge explosion unimaginable in physical terms.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 126','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','When we see objects in our immediate surroundings we see them instantly and hence in the present time','When we see stars we apparently are peeping into the past.','The universe is very old – almost 20 million years old, with huge clusters of galaxies','The Big Bang theory attempts to explain to us the origin of universe, talking of a singular huge explosion unimaginable in physical terms.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291303,'biology','Assertion: When we see stars we apparently are peeping into the past\n\nReason:What we see today is an object whose emitted light started its journey millions of year back and from trillions of kilometres away and reaching our eyes now','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','When we look at stars on a clear night sky we are, in a way, looking back in time. Stellar distances are measured in light years. What we see today is an object whose emitted light started its journey millions of year back and from trillions of kilometres away and reaching our eyes now. Reference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 126','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291304,'biology','With regards to Big Bang Theory, which of the following statements is false','The gases exanded under the influence of gravity and formed the galaxies of the present day universe','The Big Bang theory attempts to explain to us the origin of universe. It talks of a singular huge explosion unimaginable in physical terms.The universe expanded and hence, the temperature came down. Hydrogen and Helium formed sometime later.The gases condensed under gravitation and formed the galaxies of the present day universe. In the solar system of the milky way galaxy, earth was supposed to have been formed about 4.5 billion years back. There was no atmosphere on early earth. Water vapour, methane, CO₂  and ammonia released from molten mass covered the surface.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 127','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The Big Bang theory attempts to explain to us the origin of universe, it talks of a singular huge explosion unimaginable in physical terms','The universe expanded and, the temperature came down with hydrogen and helium forming sometime later','The gases exanded under the influence of gravity and formed the galaxies of the present day universe','There was no atmosphere on early earth. Water vapour, methane, CO₂  and ammonia released from molten mass covered the surface','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291305,'biology','With regards to events during the Big Bang Theory, which of the following statements is false','Life appeared 500 billion years after the formation of earth, almost four billion years back.','The UV rays from the sun brokeup water into Hydrogen and O₂ and the lighter Hydrogen escaped. O₂ combined with ammonia and methane to form water, CO₂  and others. The ozone layer was formed. As it cooled, the water vapor fell as rain, to fill all the depressions and form oceans. Life appeared 500 million years after the formation of earth, i.e., almost four billion years back.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 127','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The UV rays from the sun brokeup water into Hydrogen and O₂ and the lighter Hydrogen escaped','O₂ combined with ammonia and methane to form water, CO₂  and others and the ozone layer formed.','As water vapor cooled, the water vapor fell as rain, to fill all the depressions and form oceans.','Life appeared 500 billion years after the formation of earth, almost four billion years back.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51069,'biology','Q: Given below are two statements. Other is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R):\n\nAssertion (A):\n\nA flower is defined as modified shoot wherein the shoot apical meristem changes to floral meristem.\n\nReason (R):\n\nInternode of the shoot gets condensed to produce different floral appendages laterally at successive nodes instead of leaves.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:','Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.',NULL,'Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291306,'biology','With regards to theories of evolution, select the INCORRECT statement','Louis Pasteur proved that the theory of spontaneous generation was wrong ,and also found the  answer to how the first life form came on earth','Did life come from outerspace? Some scientists believe that it came from outside. Early Greek thinkers thought units of life called spores were transferred to different planets including earth. ‘Panspermia’ is still a favourite idea for some astronomers. For a long time it was also believed that life came out of decaying and rotting matter like straw, mud, etc. This was the theory of spontaneous generation.Louis Pasteur by careful experimentation demonstrated that life comes only from pre-existing life. He showed that in pre-sterilised flasks, life did not come from killed yeast while in another flask open to air, new living organisms arose from ‘killed\nyeast’. Spontaneous generation theory was dismissed once and for all. However, this did not answer how the first life form came on earth.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 127','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The theory of spontaneous generation, implied that living beings emerged from non living things','The theory of Panspermia implied that life originated iin space and was scattered by a meteor shower.','Louis Pasteur by careful experimentation demonstrated that life comes only from pre-existing life in his swan flask experiments.','Louis Pasteur proved that the theory of spontaneous generation was wrong ,and also found the  answer to how the first life form came on earth','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291307,'biology','With regards to theories of evolution, select the correct statement','Analysis of meteorite content revealed similar compounds indicating that similar processes are  occuring elsewhere in space','Oparin of Russia and Haldane of England proposed that the first form of life could have come from pre-existing non-living organic molecules (e.g. RNA, protein, etc.) and that formation of life was preceded by chemical evolution, i.e., formation of diverse organic molecules from inorganic constituents. The conditions on earth were – high temperature, volcanic storms, reducing atmosphere containing methane,ammonia,etc. In 1953, S.L. Miller, an American scientist created similar conditions in a laboratory scale .He created electric discharge in a closed flask containing methane, hydrogen, ammonia and water vapour at 800 °C. He observed formation of amino acids. In similar experiments others observed, formation of sugars, nitrogen bases, pigment and fats. Analysis of meteorite content also revealed similar compounds indicating that similar processes are occurring elsewhere in space. With this limited evidence, the first part of the conjectured story, i.e., chemical evolution was more or less accepted.We have no idea about how the first self replicating metabolic capsule\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 127','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Oparin of England and Haldane of Russia proposed that the first form of life could have come from pre-existing non-living organic molecules','In 1935, S.L. Miller, an English scientist created similar conditions based on Chemical Evolution in a laboratory scale','Miller created electric discharge in a closed flask containing methane, hydrogen, ammonia and water vapour at 8000 °C and observed formation of organic compounds','Analysis of meteorite content revealed similar compounds indicating that similar processes are  occuring elsewhere in space','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291308,'biology','With regards to the concept of Chemical Evolution, Select the correct statement','The first cellular form of life did not possibly originate till about 2000 million years ago.','The first non-cellular forms of life could have originated 3 billion years back.They would have been giant molecules (RNA, Protein,Polysaccharides, etc.). These capsules reproduced their molecules perhaps. The first cellular form of life did not possibly originate till about 2000 million years ago. These were probably single-cells. All life forms were in water environment only. This version of a biogenesis, i.e., the first form of life arose slowly through evolutionary forces from non-living molecules is accepted by majority.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 127 and 128','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The first non-cellular forms of life could have originated 3 million years back.','The first life forms were giant molecules, whose capsules reproduced molecules','The first cellular form of life did not possibly originate till about 2000 million years ago.','The version of a biogenesis, where all life forms were believed to originate in an aquatic environment, slowly through evolutionary forces from non-living molecules is not accepted by majority.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291309,'biology','With regards to Theories of Special Creation,Select the correct statement','All living organisms that we see today were created as such','Conventional religious literature tells us about the theory of special creation. This theory has three connotations. One, that all living organisms (species or types) that we see today were created as such. Two, that the diversity was always the same since creation and will be the same in future also. Three, that earth is about 4000 years old. All these ideas were strongly challenged during the nineteenth century.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 128','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','All living organisms that we see today were created as such','The diversity has changed since creation and will change in future also','Earth is about 400 Billion years old','Ideas regarding the theories of Special creation,were strongly challenged during the eighteenth century','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291310,'biology','Assertion: Many life forms that existed in the past do not exist any more\n\nReason: There had been extinctions of different life forms in the years gone and many new forms have risen.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Based on observations made during a sea voyage in a sail ship called H.M.S. Beagle round the world, Charles Darwin concluded that existing living forms share similarities to varying degrees not only among themselves but also with life forms that existed millions of years ago. Many such life forms do not exist any more. There had been extinctions of different life forms in the years gone by just as new forms of life arose at different periods of history of earth. There has been gradual evolution of life forms.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 128','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51070,'biology','Correct position of floral parts over thalamus in the mustard plant is:\n\n1.Gynoecium occupies the highest position, while the other parts are situated below it.\n\n2.Margin of the thalamus grows upward, enclosing the ovary completely, and other parts arise below the ovary.\n\n3.Gynoecium is present in 3. the center and other parts cover it partially.\n\n4.Gynoecium is situated in the center, and other parts of the flower are located at the rim of the thalamus at the same level','4','Page 73, XI NCERT','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-073',NULL,NULL),
(291311,'biology','Assertion: Those who are better fit in an environment, leave more progeny than others\n\nReason : Fitness, according to Darwin, refers to both physical and reproductive fitness','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','Any population has built in variation in characteristics. Those characteristics which enable some to survive better in natural conditions (climate, food, physical factors, etc.) would outbreed others that are less-endowed to survive under such natural conditions. Another word used is fitness of the individual or population. The fitness, according to Darwin, refers ultimately and only to reproductive fitness.Hence, those who are better fit in an environment, leave more progeny than others. These, therefore, will survive more and hence are selected by nature. He called it natural selection and implied it as a mechanism of evolution.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 129','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291312,'biology','Assertion: All the existing life forms share similarities and share common ancestors.\n\nReason : The geological history of earth does not correlate with the biological history of earth','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','Let us also remember that Alfred Wallace, a naturalist who worked in Malay Archipelago had also come to similar conclusions around the same time. In due course of time, apparently new types of organisms are recognisable. All the existing life forms share similarities and share common ancestors. However, these ancestors were present at different periods in the history of earth (epochs, periods and eras). The geological history of earth closely correlates with the biological history of earth. A common permissible conclusion is that earth is very old, not thousand of years as was thought earlier but billions of years old.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 129','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291313,'biology','Assertion: Some of the fossils appear similar to modern organisms\n\nReason : They represent extinct organisms','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Evidence that evolution of life forms has indeed taken place on earth has come from many quarters. Fossils are remains of hard parts of life-forms found in rocks. Rocks form sediments and a cross-section of earth\'s crust indicates the arrangement of sediments one over the other during the long history of earth. Different-aged rock sediments contain fossils of different life-forms who probably died during the formation of the particular sediment.Some of them appear similar to modern organisms. They represent extinct organisms (e.g., Dinosaurs). Some of the fossils that resemble today\'s organisms point out to the evidence where all of today\'s organisms are a result of evolution that has been happening for ages.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 129','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291314,'biology','With regards to Embryological support for evolution ,Select the correct statement','It is based on the observation of certain features during embryonic stage common to all vertebrates that are absent in adult.','Embryological support for evolution was also proposed by Ernst Heckel based upon the observation of certain features during embryonic stage common to all vertebrates that are absent in adult. For example, the embryos of all vertebrates including human develop a row of vestigial gill slit just behind the head but it is a functional organ only in fish and not found in any other adult vertebrates. However, this proposal was disapproved on careful study performed by Karl Ernst von Baer. He noted that embryos never pass through the adult stages of other animals.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 129','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','It was proposed by Karl Ernst von Baer','It is based on the observation of certain features during embryonic stage common to all vertebrates that are absent in adult.','The embryos of all invertebrates develop a row of vestigial gill slit in front of the head','This proposal was disapproved on careful study performed by Ernst Heckel','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291315,'biology','Assertion: Comparative anatomy and morphology shows similarities and differences among organisms of today and those that existed years ago.\n\nReason : Homology indicates common ancestry.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Comparative anatomy and morphology shows similarities and differences among organisms of today and those that existed years ago.Such similarities can be interpreted to understand whether common ancestors were shared or not. For example whales, bats, Cheetah and human (all mammals) share similarities in the pattern of bones of forelimbs Though these forelimbs perform different functions in these animals, they have similar anatomical structure – all of them have humerus, radius, ulna, carpals, metacarpals and phalanges in their forelimbs. Hence, in these animals, the same structure developed along different directions due to adaptations to different needs. This is divergent evolution and these structures are homologous. Homology indicates common ancestry. Other examples are vertebrate hearts or brains.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 129 and 130','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291316,'biology','Assertion: Homology is based on convergent evolution\n\nReason : Anology is a result of divergent evolution','Both Assertion and reason are false','Homology is based on divergent evolution whereas analogy refers to a situation exactly opposite. Wings of butterfly and of birds look alike. They are not anatomically similar structures though they perform similar functions. Hence, analogous structures are a result of convergent evolution - different structures evolving for the same function and hence having similarity. Other examples of analogy are the eye of the octopus and of mammals or the flippers of Penguins and Dolphins.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 131','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291317,'biology','Assertion: Eye of octopus and Eye of Man are analogous structures and result of convergent evolution.\n\nReason: Even though eyes of octopus and human serve as organs for vision, they are different in structure','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Homology is based on divergent evolution whereas analogy refers to a situation exactly opposite. Wings of butterfly and of birds look alike. They are not anatomically similar structures though they perform similar functions. Hence, analogous structures are a result of convergent evolution - different structures evolving for the same function and hence having similarity. Other examples of analogy are the eye of the octopus and of mammals or the flippers of Penguins and Dolphins.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 131','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291318,'biology','With regards to Evidences for evolution ,Select the correct statement','Thorn and tendrils of Bougainvillea and Cucurbita represent divergent evolution','Homology indicates common ancestry. Other examples are vertebrate hearts or brains. In plants also, the thorn and tendrils of Bougainvillea and Cucurbita represent homology.Homology is based on divergent evolution whereas analogy refers to a situation exactly opposite. Wings of butterfly and of birds look alike. They are not anatomically similar structures though they perform similar functions. Hence, analogous structures are a result of convergent evolution - different structures evolving for the same function and hence having similarity. Other examples of analogy are the eye of the octopus and of mammals or the flippers of Penguins and Dolphins.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 131','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Homology is based on convergent evolution','Thorn and tendrils of Bougainvillea and Cucurbita represent divergent evolution','Analogous structures are a result of divergent evolution','Examples of homologous structures are the eye of the octopus and of mammals or the flippers of Penguins and Dolphins.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291319,'biology','Assertion: Similarities in proteins and genes performing a given function among diverse organisms give clues to common ancestry.\n\nReason : Biochemical similarities point to the same shared ancestry as structural similarities among diverse organisms.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','In the same line of argument, similarities in proteins and genes performing a given function among diverse organisms give clues to common ancestry. These biochemical similarities point to the same shared ancestry as structural similarities among diverse organisms.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 131','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291320,'biology','With regards to evolution by natural selection,Select the correct statement','Before industrialisation,there were more white-winged moths on trees than dark-winged','Another interesting observation supporting evolution by natural selection comes from England. In a collection of moths made in 1850s, i.e., before industrialisation set in, it was observed that there were more white-winged moths on trees than dark-winged or melanised moths. However, in the collection carried out from the same area, but after industrialisation, i.e., in 1920, there were more dark-winged moths in the same area, i.e., the proportion was reversed.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 131,132','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','After industrialisation,there were equal dark-winged and white-winged moths in the same area','Before industrialisation,there were more white-winged moths on trees than dark-winged','Before industrialisation,there were equal white-winged and dark-winged moths.','After industrialisation, there were more white-winged moths than dark-winged moths.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291321,'biology','Assertion: After industrialisation ,there were more dark-winged moths than white winged moths\n\nReason : During post- industrialisation period, the tree trunks became dark due to industrial smoke and soots, and this helped the dark moths to camouflage and escape predators.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Another interesting observation supporting evolution by natural selection comes from England. In a collection of moths made in 1850s, i.e., before industrialisation set in, it was observed that there were more white-winged moths on trees than dark-winged or melanised moths. However, in the collection carried out from the same area, but after industrialisation, i.e., in 1920, there were more dark-winged moths in the same area, i.e., the proportion was reversed.The explanation put forth for this observation was that ‘predators will spot a moth against a contrasting background’. During post- industrialisation period, the tree trunks became dark due to industrial smoke and soots.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 131,132.','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291322,'biology','Assertion:Before industrialisation set in, it was observed that there were more white-winged moths on trees than dark-winged.\n\nReason :Before industrialisation set in, thick growth of almost white-coloured lichen covered the trees, helped white moths to camouflage and escape predators.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Another interesting observation supporting evolution by natural selection comes from England. In a collection of moths made in 1850s, i.e., before industrialisation set in, it was observed that there were more white-winged moths on trees than dark-winged or melanised moths. However, in the collection carried out from the same area, but after industrialisation, i.e., in 1920, there were more dark-winged moths in the same area, i.e., the proportion was reversed.The explanation put forth for this observation was that ‘predators will spot a moth against a contrasting background’. During post- industrialisation period, the tree trunks became dark due to industrial smoke and soots.Before industrialisation set in, thick growth of almost white-coloured lichen covered the trees - in that background the white winged moth survived but the dark-coloured moth were picked out by predators.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 131,132','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291323,'biology','With regards to Evidences for evolution ,Select the correct statement','Evolution is a stochastic process based on chance events in nature and chance mutation in the organisms.','Excess use of herbicides, pesticides, etc., has only resulted in selection of resistant varieties in a much lesser time scale. This is also true for microbes against which we employ antibiotics or drugs against eukaryotic organisms/cell. Hence, resistant organisms/cells are appearing in a time scale of months or years and not centuries. These are examples of evolution by anthropogenic action. This also tells us that evolution is not a directed process in the sense of determinism. It is a stochastic process based on chance events in nature and chance mutation in the organisms.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 131','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Excess use of herbicides and pesticides has only resulted in selection of resistant varieties in a much larger time scale','Resistant organisms/cells are appearing in a time scale of centuries','Evolution is a directed process in the sense of determinism','Evolution is a stochastic process based on chance events in nature and chance mutation in the organisms.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291324,'biology','With regards to Adaptive radiation ,Select the correct statement','All the varieties, he conjectured, evolved on the island itself','During his journey Darwin went to Galapagos Islands. There he observed an amazing diversity of creatures. Of particular interest, small black birds later called Darwin’s Finches amazed him. He realised that there were many varieties of finches in the same island. All the varieties, he conjectured, evolved on the island itself. From the original seed-eating features, many other forms with altered beaks arose, enabling them to become insectivorous and vegetarian finches\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 132,133','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','During his journey Darwin went to Madagascar Islands, he observed an amazing diversity of creatures.','Small blue birds later called Darwin’s Finches amazed him','All the varieties, he conjectured, evolved on the island itself','From the original fruit-eating features, many other forms with altered beaks arose, enabling them to become insectivorous.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291325,'biology','Assertion : Process of evolution of different species in a given geographical area starting from a point and literally radiating to other areas of geography (habitats) is called adaptive radiation.\n\nReason : Darwin’s falcons represent one of the best examples of this phenomenon.','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','This process of evolution of different species in a given geographical area starting from a point and literally radiating to other areas of geography (habitats) is called adaptive radiation. Darwin’s finches represent one of the best examples of this phenomenon.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 133','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291326,'biology','Assertion : Australian marsupials is an example of adaptive radiation\n\nReason : A number of marsupials, each different from the other evolved from a common ancestral stock, but all within the Australian island continent.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','This process of evolution of different species in a given geographical area starting from a point and literally radiating to other areas of geography (habitats) is called adaptive radiation. Darwin’s finches represent one of the best examples of this phenomenon. Another example is Australian marsupials. A number of marsupials, each different from the other evolved from an ancestral stock, but all within the Australian island continent.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 133','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291327,'biology','With regards to Biological Evolution and Darwin\'s theory ,Select the correct statement','Some organisms are better adapted to survive in an otherwise hostile environment','The essence of Darwinian theory about evolution is natural selection. The rate of appearance of new forms is linked to the life cycle or the life span. Microbes that divide fast have the ability to multiply and become millions of individuals within hours.Nature selects for fitness.One must remember that the so-called fitness is based on characteristics which are inherited. Hence, there must be a genetic basis for getting selected and to evolve.Another way of saying the same thing is that some organisms are better adapted to survive in an otherwise hostile environment. Adaptive ability is inherited. It has a genetic basis. Fitness is the end result of the ability to adapt and get selected by nature.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 134','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The essence of Darwinian theory about evolution is Artificial selection','Fitness is based on characteristics which are acquired','Some organisms are better adapted to survive in an otherwise hostile environment','Adaptive ability does not have a genetic basis','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291328,'biology','Assertion : In due course of time one variant population outgrows the others and appears as new species.\n\nReason : Some organisms are better adapted to survive in an otherwise hostile environment, and survive better, while the other species struggling may get eliminated from that population','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','A colony of bacteria(say A) growing on a given medium has built-in variation in terms of ability to utilise a feed component. A change in the medium composition would bring out only that part of the population (say B) that can survive under the new conditions. In due course of time this variant population outgrows the others and appears as new species.Here we say that fitness of B is better than that of A under the new conditions. Nature selects for fitness. One must remember that the so-called fitness is based on characteristics which are inherited. Hence, there must be a genetic basis for getting selected and to evolve. Another way of saying the same thing is that some organisms are better adapted to survive in an otherwise hostile environment.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 134','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51071,'biology','Ray florets have:','Inferior ovary','Page 73, XI NCERT','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Superior ovary','Hypogynous ovary','Half inferior ovary','Inferior ovary','NEW','11-073',NULL,NULL),
(291329,'biology','Assertion : Fitness is the end result of the ability to adapt and natural selection.\n\nReason : Branching descent and natural selection are the two key concepts of Darwinian Theory of Evolution','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Adaptive ability is inherited. It has a genetic basis. Fitness is the end result of the ability to adapt and get selected by nature. Branching descent and natural selection are the two key concepts of Darwinian Theory of Evolution\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 134','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291330,'biology','Assertion : Giraffes, slowly, over the years, have come to acquire long necks., according to Lamarck\'s theory.\n\nReason : According to Darwin\'s theory, Giraffes who in an attempt to forage leaves on tall trees had to adapt by elongation of their necks.','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','Even before Darwin, a French naturalist Lamarck had said that evolution of life forms had occurred but driven by use and disuse of organs. He gave the examples of Giraffes who in an attempt to forage leaves on tall trees had to adapt by elongation of their necks. As they passed on this acquired character of elongated neck to succeeding generations, Giraffes, slowly, over the years, came to acquire long necks.Nobody believes this conjecture any more. Darwin\'s theory stated that the giraffes that had long slender necks survived and the ones that were short, became extinct, where as Lamarck stated that in order to survive Giraffes grew long necks over generations.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 134,135','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291401,'biology','With regards to Biological Evolution ,Select the correct statement','The world we see, inanimate and animate, is only the success stories of evolution','The world we see, inanimate and animate, is only the success stories of evolution. When we describe the story of this world we describe evolution as a process. On the other hand when we describe the story of life on earth, we treat evolution as a consequence of a process called natural selection. We are still not very clear whether to regard evolution and natural selection as processes or end result of unknown processes.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 135','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','The world we see, inanimate and animate, is only the success stories of evolution','When we describe the story of this world we describe evolution as a consequence of artificial selection.','When we describe the story of life on earth, we don\'t treat evolution as a process','We are very clear as to regard evolution and natural selection as processes or end result of unknown processes.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291402,'biology','With regards to Biological Evolution ,Select the correct statement','Natural selection is based on certain observations which are factual','It is possible that the work of Thomas Malthus on populations influenced Darwin.Natural selection is based on certain observations which are factual.The novelty and brilliant insight of Darwin was this: he asserted that variations, which are heritable and which make resource utilisation better for few (adapted to habitat better) will enable only those to reproduce and leave more progeny. Hence for a period of time, over many generations, survivors will leave more progeny and there would be a change in population characteristic and hence new forms appear to arise.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 135','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','The work of Thomas Matthew on populations influenced Darwin','Natural selection is based on certain observations which are factual','Variations which are acquired and which make resource utilisation better for few will not enable those to reproduce and leave more progeny','Over many generations, survivors will leave less progeny and there would be a change in population characteristic and hence new forms do not appear.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291403,'biology','Assertion : Population size will grow exponentially if everybody reproduced maximally\n\nReason : Variations, which are heritable and which make resource utilisation better for few will enable only those to reproduce and leave more progeny.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Natural selection is based on certain observations which are factual. For example, natural resources are limited, populations are stable in size except for seasonal fluctuation, members of a population vary in characteristics (infact no two individuals are alike) even though they look superficially similar, most of variations are inherited etc. The fact that theoretically population size will grow exponentially if everybody reproduced maximally (this fact can be seen in a growing bacterial population) and the fact that population sizes in reality are limited, means that there had been competition for resources. Only some survived and grew at the cost of others that could not flourish. The novelty and brilliant insight of Darwin was this: he asserted that variations, which are heritable and which make resource utilisation better for few (adapted to habitat better) will enable only those to reproduce and leave more progeny.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 135','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291404,'biology','With regards to Mechanism of Evolution ,Select the correct statement','Mendel had talked of inheritable factors influencing phenotype','Mendel had talked of inheritable \'factors\' influencing phenotype, Darwin either ignored these observations or kept silence. In the first decade of twentieth century, Hugo deVries based on his work on evening primrose brought forth the idea of mutations – large difference arising suddenly in a population.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 135','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Mendel had talked of inheritable factors influencing phenotype','Darwin considered Mendel\'s observations','Hugo deVries based on his work on morning glory brought forth the idea of mutations','Mutations are large differences arising gradually in a population.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51072,'biology','What type of placentation is seen in sweet pea?','Marginal',NULL,'Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Basal','Axile','Free-central','Marginal','NEW','11-075',NULL,NULL),
(51073,'biology','Placentation, in which ovules develop on the inner wall of the ovary or in peripheral part, is:','Parietal',NULL,'Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Free central','basal','Axile','Parietal','NEW','11-075',NULL,NULL),
(291405,'biology','With regards to the concept of mutations ,Select the correct statement','Hugo deVries believed mutation caused speciation and hence called it saltation','Hugo deVries based on his work on evening primrose brought forth the idea of mutations – large difference arising suddenly in a population. He believed that it is mutation which causes evolution and not the minor variations (heritable) that Darwin talked about. Mutations are random and directionless while Darwinian variations are small and directional. Evolution for Darwin was gradual while deVries believed mutation caused speciation and hence called it saltation (single step large mutation). Studies in population genetics, later, brought out some clarity.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 135','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Mutations are random and directional','Darwinian variations are small and directionless','Evolution for Darwin was sudden','Hugo deVries believed mutation caused speciation and hence called it saltation','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291406,'biology','Keeping the Hardy Weinberg concept in mind, Choose the incorrect statement','Hardy Weinberg Principle stresses on the concept and proves Genetic Disequilibrium','In a given population one can find out the frequency of occurrence of alleles of a gene or a locus. This frequency is supposed to remain fixed and even remain the same through generations. Hardy-Weinberg principle\nstated it using algebraic equations. This principle says that allele frequencies in a population are stable and is constant from generation to generation. The gene pool (total genes and their alleles in a population) remains a constant. This is called\ngenetic equilibrium.Natural selection pattern can be stabilising, directional or disruptive based on the phenotype and stability in an environment.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 136','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','One can find out the frequency of occurrence of alleles of a gene or a locus in a given population,and this frequency is supposed to remain constant across generations','The Hardy-Weinberg principle says that allele frequencies in a population are stable and remain constant from generation to generation.','Hardy Weinberg Principle stresses on the concept and proves Genetic Disequilibrium','Natural selection pattern can be stabilising, directional or disruptive based on the phenotype and stability in an environment','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291407,'biology','Which of the following statements is incorrect with respect to Hardy-Weinberg Principle','When frequency measured, differs from expected values, the difference (direction) indicates the extent of stability and the absence of evolutionary change','Sum total of all the allelic frequencies is 1. Individual frequencies, for example, can be named p, q, etc. In a diploid, p and q represent the frequency of allele A and allele a. The frequency of AA individuals in a population is simply p². This is simply stated in another ways, i.e., the probability that an allele A with a frequency of p appear on both the chromosomes of a diploid individual is simply the product of the probabilities, i.e., p² Similarly of aa is q², of Aa 2pq. Hence,p²+2pq+q²=1. This is a binomial expansion of (p+q)² .When frequency measured, differs from expected values, the difference (direction) indicates the extent of evolutionary change. Disturbance in genetic equilibrium, or Hardy- Weinberg equilibrium, i.e., change of frequency of alleles in a population would then be interpreted as resulting in evolution.Five factors are known to affect Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium. These are gene migration or gene flow, genetic drift, mutation, genetic recombination and natural selection.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 136 and 137','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Sum total of all the allelic frequencies is 1, and the Algebraic expression of this concept is p²+2pq+q²=1.','In a diploid, when p and q represent the frequency of allele A and allele a, frequency of AA individuals in a population is simply p².','When frequency measured, differs from expected values, the difference (direction) indicates the extent of stability and the absence of evolutionary change','Five factors known to affect Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium are gene migration or gene flow, genetic drift, mutation, genetic recombination and natural selection.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291408,'biology','Assertion: When frequency measured, differs from expected values in accordance to Hardy Weinberg principle, the difference (direction) indicates the extent of evolutionary change\n\nReason: The changes within a stable population, cause the emergence of a specific variant and it attributes to its survival and emergence and indicates Evolution through genetic filtration','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Sum total of all the allelic frequencies is 1. Individual frequencies, for example, can be named p, q, etc. In a diploid, p and q represent the frequency of allele A and allele a. The frequency of AA individuals in a population is simply p². This is simply stated in another ways, i.e., the probability that an allele A with a frequency of p appear on both the chromosomes of a diploid individual is simply the product of the probabilities, i.e., p² Similarly of aa is q², of Aa 2pq. Hence,p²+2pq+q²=1. This is a binomial expansion of (p+q)² .When frequency measured, differs from expected values, the difference (direction) indicates the extent of evolutionary change. Disturbance in genetic equilibrium, or Hardy- Weinberg equilibrium, i.e., change of frequency of alleles in a population would then be interpreted as resulting in evolution.Five factors are known to affect Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium. These are gene migration or gene flow, genetic drift, mutation, genetic recombination and natural selection.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 136 and 137','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291409,'biology','Assertion : Gene migration affects the Hardy Weinberg principle\n\nReason: When migration of a section of population to another place and population occurs, gene frequencies change in the original as well as in the new population.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Five factors are known to affect Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium. These are gene migration or gene flow, genetic drift, mutation, genetic recombination and natural selection. When migration of a section of population to another place and population occurs, gene frequencies change in the original as well as in the new population. New genes/alleles are added to the new population and these are lost from the old population. There would be a gene flow if this gene migration, happens multiple times. If the same change occurs by chance, it is called genetic drift. Sometimes the change in allele frequency is so different in the new sample of population that they become a different species. The original drifted population becomes founders and the effect is called founder effect.Reference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 137','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291410,'biology','With regards to the factors affecting Hardy-Weinberg Principle, choose the incorrect option','Even though, the change in allele frequency is so different in the new sample of population, they remain as a stable species','Five factors are known to affect Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium. These are gene migration or gene flow, genetic drift, mutation, genetic recombination and natural selection. When migration of a section of population to another place and population occurs, gene frequencies change in the original as well as in the new population. New genes/alleles are added to the new population and these are lost from the old population. There would be a gene flow if this gene migration, happens multiple times. If the same change occurs by chance, it is called genetic drift. Sometimes the change in allele frequency is so different in the new sample of population that they become a different species. The original drifted population becomes founders and the effect is called founder effect.Reference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 137','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','There would be a gene flow if gene migration, happens multiple times','If the same change occurs by chance following a gene flow, it is called genetic drift.','Even though, the change in allele frequency is so different in the new sample of population, they remain as a stable species','Founder effect is when, the immigrant population has a greatly diverse allele frequency and alters the entire population, and in process results in the evolution of a new species.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291411,'biology','With regards to Hardy Weinberg principle and Speciation select the correct answer','A critical analysis makes us believe that variation due to mutation or variation due to recombination during gametogenesis, or due to gene flow or genetic drift results in changed frequency of genes and alleles in future generation.','Microbial experiments show that pre-existing advantageous mutations when selected will result in observation of new phenotypes. Over few generations, this would result in Speciation. Natural selection is a process in which heritable variations enabling better survival are enabled to reproduce and leave greater number of progeny. A critical analysis makes us believe that variation due to mutation or variation due to recombination during gametogenesis, or due to gene flow or genetic drift results in changed frequency of genes and alleles in future generation. Coupled to enhance reproductive success, natural selection makes it look like different population. Natural selection can lead to stabilisation (in which more individuals acquire mean character value), directional change (more individuals acquire value other than the mean character value) or disruption (more individuals acquire peripheral character value at both ends of the distribution curve Reference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 137','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Microbial experiments show that pre-existing advantageous mutations when selected will not result in observation of new phenotypes and speciation.','Natural selection is a process in which heritable variations enabling lesser survival are enabled to reproduce and leave lesser number of progeny.','A critical analysis makes us believe that variation due to mutation or variation due to recombination during gametogenesis, or due to gene flow or genetic drift results in changed frequency of genes and alleles in future generation.','Natural selection can lead to stabilisation in which more individuals acquire value other than the mean character value','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291412,'biology','With regards to Hardy Weinberg principle and Speciation select the correct answer','Natural selection can lead to stabilisation directional or disruptive changes in the population.','Natural selection can lead to stabilisation (in which more individuals acquire mean character value), directional change (more individuals acquire value other than the mean character value) or disruption (more individuals acquire peripheral character value at both ends of the distribution curve\n Reference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 137','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Natural selection can lead to stabilisation in which more individuals acquire value other than the mean character value','Natural selection can lead to directional change, in which more individuals acquire mean character value.','Natural Selection can lead to disruption in which more individuals acquire central value in the distribution curve','Natural selection can lead to stabilisation directional or disruptive changes in the population.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291413,'biology','With regards to Evolution, select the incorrect statement','500 billion years ago, the first invertebrates were formed and active','About 2000 million years ago (mya) the first cellular forms of life appeared on earth. The mechanism of how non-cellular aggregates of giant macromolecules could evolve into cells with membranous envelop is not known. Some of these cells had the ability to release Oxygen. The reaction could have been similar to the light reaction in photosynthesis where water is split with the help of solar energy captured and channelised by appropriate light harvesting pigments. Slowly single-celled organisms became multi-cellular life forms. By the time of 500 mya, invertebrates were formed and active. Jawless fish probably evolved around 350 mya. Sea weeds and few plants existed probably around 320 mya.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 137 and 138','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','About 2000 million years ago (mya) the first cellular forms of life appeared on earth','Some of the cells amongst the primitive cells, had the ability to release O₂ by a reaction,similar to the light reaction in photosynthesis where water is split with the help of solar energy.','500 billion years ago, the first invertebrates were formed and active','Jawless fish probably evolved around 350 mya, sea weeds and few plants existed probably around 320 mya.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291414,'biology','With regards to Evolution, select the correct statement','Coelacanth (lobefins) fishes are the ancestors of amphibians.','We are told that the first organisms that invaded land were plants. They were widespread on land when animals invaded land. Fish with stout and strong fins could move on land and go back to water. This was about 350 mya. In 1938, a fish caught in South Africa happened to be a Coelacanth which was thought to be extinct. These animals called lobefins evolved into the first amphibians that lived on both land and water. There are no specimens of these left with us. However, these were ancestors of modern day frogs and salamanders. The amphibians evolved into reptiles. They lay thick- shelled eggs which do not dry up in sun unlike those of amphibians. Again we only see their modern day descendents, the turtles, tortoises and crocodiles.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 138 and 139','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','The first organisms that invaded land were amphibians.and they appeared even before plants','Fish with stout and strong fins could move on land and go back to water, were found 50 billion years ago','In 1938, a fish caught in South America happened to be a Coelacanth which was thought to be extinct.','Coelacanth (lobefins) fishes are the ancestors of amphibians.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51074,'biology','Identify the correct features of Mango and Coconut fruits.\n\n(i)In both, fruit is a drupe\n\n(ii)Endocarp is edible in both\n\n(iii)Mesocarp in Coconut is fibrous, and in Mango, it is fleshy\n\n(iv)In both, the fruit develops from the monocarpellary ovary\n\nSelect the correct option from below:','1. (i), (iii) and (iv) only','Page 76, XI NCERT','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','1. (i), (iii) and (iv) only','2. (i), (ii) and (iii) only','3. (i) and (iv) only','4. (i) and (ii) only','NEW','11-076',NULL,NULL),
(40506,'chemistry','Which one have multiple double covalent bonds?','CO₂','When two atoms share 2 electron pair they form double covalent bonds. \n\nNaCl and CaCl₂ are ionic compounds so do not form covalent bonds. \n\nC₂H₄ forms one double bond. CO₂ has two double bonds. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Covalent bond',NULL,'1','mcq','CaCl₂','C₂H₄','CO₂','NaCl','NEW','11-102','11-102','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/40506q.png'),
(291415,'biology','With regards to Evolution, select the incorrect statement','The biggest of the land reptiles,  Tyrannosaurus rex was about 30 feet in height and had huge fearsome dagger like teeth.','The amphibians evolved into reptiles. They lay thick- shelled eggs which do not dry up in sun unlike those of amphibians. Again we only see their modern day descendents, the turtles, tortoises and crocodiles.In the next 200 millions years or so, reptiles of different shapes and sizes dominated on earth. Giant ferns (pteridophytes) were present but they all fell to form coal deposits slowly. Some of these land reptiles went back into water to evolve into fish like reptiles probably 200 mya (e.g. Ichthyosaurs). The land reptiles were, of course, the dinosaurs. The biggest of them, i.e., Tyrannosaurus rex was about 20 feet in height and had huge fearsome dagger like teeth. \nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 139','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','The amphibians evolved into reptiles and laid thick- shelled eggs which do not dry up in sun unlike those of amphibians.','Giant ferns (pteridophytes) were present 200 mya but they all fell to form coal deposits slowly.','Some of these land reptiles went back into water to evolve into fish like reptiles probably 200 mya like the Icthyosaurs.','The biggest of the land reptiles,  Tyrannosaurus rex was about 30 feet in height and had huge fearsome dagger like teeth.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291416,'biology','With regards to Evolution, select the correct statement','Mammals were viviparous and protected their unborn young inside the mother’s body','About 65 mya, the dinosaurs suddenly disappeared from the earth. We do not know the true reason. Some say climatic changes killed them. Some say most of them evolved into birds. The truth may live in between. Small sized reptiles of that era still exist today. The first mammals were like shrews. Their fossils are small sized. Mammals were viviparous and protected their unborn young inside the mother’s body. Mammals were more intelligent in sensing and avoiding danger at least.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 140','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','About 65 bya, the dinosaurs suddenly disappeared from the earth','The first mammals were the size of a blue whale','Mammals were viviparous and protected their unborn young inside the mother’s body','Birds were more intelligent in sensing and avoiding danger at least, in the evolution period','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291417,'biology','With regards to Evolution, select the correct statement','The most successful story is the evolution of man with language skills and self-consciousness.','When reptiles came down mammals took over this earth. There were in South America mammals resembling horse, hippopotamus, bear, rabbit, etc. Due to continental drift, when South America joined North America, these animals were overridden by North American fauna. Due to the same continental drift pouched mammals of Australia survived because of lack of competition from any other mammal. Lest we forget, some mammals live wholly in water. Whales, dolphins, seals and sea cows are some examples. Evolution of horse, elephant, dog, etc., are special stories of evolution. The most successful story is the evolution of man with language skills and self-consciousness.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 140','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','When mammals came down reptiles took over this earth.','Due to continental drift, when North America joined South America, animals in North America were overridden by South American fauna.','Due to the continental drift ,pouched mammals of Australia were dispersed across Africa and Madagascar.','The most successful story is the evolution of man with language skills and self-consciousness.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291418,'biology','Assertion: The Continental Drift ensured the survival of Marsupials in Australia\n\nReason: The Contential Drift resulted in the separation and isolation of Australia, and kept the Marsupials free from Competition.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Due to continental drift, when South America joined North America, these animals were overridden by North American fauna. Due to the same continental drift pouched mammals of Australia survived because of lack of competition from any other mammal. Lest we forget, some mammals live wholly in water. Whales, dolphins, seals and sea cows are some examples. Evolution of horse, elephant, dog, etc., are special stories of evolution. The most successful story is the evolution of man with language skills and self-consciousness. Reference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 140','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true, but reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291419,'biology','With regards to Human Evolution, Select the correct statement','About 15 mya, primates called Dryopithecus and Ramapithecus , walked like chimpanzees and gorillas on Earth','About 15 mya, primates called Dryopithecus and Ramapithecus were existing. They were hairy and walked like gorillas and chimpanzees. Ramapithecus was more man-like while Dryopithecus was more ape-like. Few fossils of man-like bones have been discovered in Ethiopia and Tanzania. These revealed hominid features leading to the belief that about 3-4 mya, man-like primates walked in eastern Africa. They were probably not taller than 4 feet but walked up right.Two mya, Australopithecines probably lived in East African grasslands. Reference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 140','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','About 15 mya, primates called Dryopithecus and Ramapithecus , walked like chimpanzees and gorillas on Earth','Dryopithecus was more man-like while Ramapithecus was more ape-like.','Man-like bones discovered in Madagascar and Galapagos. revealed hominid features leading to the belief that about 3-4 mya, man-like primates walked erect in eastern Africa.','4 mya, Australopithecines probably lived in East African grasslands.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291420,'biology','With regards to Human Evolution, Select the incorrect statement','The first human-like being the hominid and was called Homo erectus, with a brain capacity between 650- 800cc and they did not eat meat.','Two mya, Australopithecines probably lived in East African grasslands. Evidence shows they hunted with stone weapons but essentially ate fruit. Some of the bones among the bones discovered were different. This creature was called the first human-like being the hominid and was called Homo habilis. The brain capacities were between 650-800cc. They probably did not eat meat. Fossils discovered in Java in 1891 revealed the next stage, i.e., Homo erectus about 1.5 mya. Homo erectus had a large brain around 900cc.The skull of baby chimpanzee is more like adult human skull than adult chimpanzee skull.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 140','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Australopithecines hunted with stone weapons and fed on fruits','The first human-like being the hominid and was called Homo erectus, with a brain capacity between 650- 800cc and they did not eat meat.','Homo erectus fossils were discovered in Java and had a brain capacity more than 600 cc.','The skull of baby chimpanzee is more like adult human skull than adult chimpanzee skull.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291421,'biology','With regards to Human Evolution, Select the incorrect statement','Homo erectus was the first vegetarian.','Homo erectus probably ate meat. The Neanderthal man with a brain size of 1400cc lived in near east and central Asia between 1,00,000-40,000 years back. They used hides to protect their body and buried their dead. Homo sapiens arose in Africa and moved across continents and developed into distinct races. During ice age between 75,000-10,000 years ago modern Homo sapiens arose. \nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 141','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Homo erectus was the first vegetarian.','The Neanderthal man with a brain size of 1400cc lived in near east and central Asia between 1,00,000-40,000 years back.','Neanderthal used hides to protect their body and buried their dead.','Homo sapiens arose in Africa around the time of Ice Age, around 75,000 to 10,000 yeras ago and moved across continents and developed into distinct races','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291422,'biology','With regards to Human Evolution, Select the incorrect statement','Agriculture came around 1000 years back and human settlements began.','Pre-historic cave art developed about 18,000 years ago.One such cave paintings by Pre-historic humans can be seen at Bhimbetka rock shelter in Raisen district of Madhya Pradesh. Agriculture came around 10,000 years back and human settlements started. The rest of what happened is part of human history of growth and decline of civilisations.\nReference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 141','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Pre-historic cave art developed about 18,000 years ago.','Cave paintings by Pre-historic humans can be seen at Bhimbetka rock shelter in Raisen district of Madhya Pradesh','Agriculture came around 1000 years back and human settlements began.','Among the stories of evolution of individual species, the story of evolution of modern man is most interesting and appears to parallel evolution of human brain and language.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291423,'biology','In the context of Evolution, choose the incorrect statement','It is generally believed that variations in a population will not result in variable fitness.','The origin of life on earth can be understood only against the background of origin of universe especially earth. Most scientists believe chemical evolution, i.e., formation of biomolecules preceded the appearance of the first cellular forms of life. The subsequent events as to what happened to the first form of life is a conjectured story based on Darwinian ideas of organic evolution by natural selection. Diversity of life forms on earth has been changing over millions of years. It is generally believed that variations in a population result in variable fitness. Reference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 141','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Most scientists believe chemical evolution,(formation of biomolecules) preceded the appearance of the first cellular forms of life.','The subsequent events as to what happened to the first form of life is a conjectured story based on Darwinian ideas of organic evolution by natural selection.','Diversity of life forms on earth has been changing over millions of years','It is generally believed that variations in a population will not result in variable fitness.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291424,'biology','In the context of Evolution, choose the incorrect statement','Analogy is accounted for by the idea of branching descent.','Other phenomena like habitat fragmentation and genetic drift may accentuate these variations leading to appearance of new species and hence evolution. Homology is accounted for by the idea of branching descent. Study of comparative anatomy, fossils and comparative biochemistry provides evidence for evolution. Among the stories of evolution of individual species, the story of evolution of modern man is most interesting and appears to parallel evolution of human brain and language. Reference   NCERT Class 12, Chapter 7,Page 141','Genetics and Evolution >> Evolution >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Phenomena like habitat fragmentation and genetic drift may accentuate variations leading to appearance of new species and hence hasten evolution.','Analogy is accounted for by the idea of branching descent.','Study of comparative anatomy, fossils and comparative biochemistry provides evidence for evolution.','Among the stories of evolution of individual species, the story of evolution of modern man is most interesting and appears to parallel evolution of human brain and language.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50101,'chemistry','The properties related to single particle of matter is/are _______.','All of these','The properties\nrelated to single particle of matter are atomic size,\nionization enthalpy, electronic charge density, molecular\nshape and polarity.\nIn all the three states of  water chemical composition of water remains the same\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 136; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Introduction, intermolecular forces and thermal energy',NULL,'2','mcq','atomic size','ionization enthalpy','electronic charge density','All of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50102,'chemistry','Which of the following statement(s) is/are correct?\n\na. Chemical properties of a substance do not change with the change of its physical state; but rate of chemical reactions do depend upon the physical state of substances.\n\nb. London forces, dipole-dipole forces and dipole-induced dipole forces are examples of intermolecular forces.','both a and b','Chemical properties don\'t change with change in physical state but only rate of reaction does.\n\nVan der Waals forces (Intermolecular forces) vary considerably in magnitude and include dispersion forces or London forces, dipole-dipole forces, and dipole-induced dipole forces\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 136;Paragraph 1 and Page No. 137; Paragraph 1).','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Introduction, intermolecular forces and thermal energy',NULL,'2','mcq','only a','only b','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50103,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion : Three states of matter are the result of balance between intermolecular forces and thermal energy of the molecules\n\nReason : Intermolecular forces tend to keep the molecules together but thermal energy of molecules tends to keep them apart.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Intermolecular forces tend to keep the molecules together but thermal energy of the molecules tends to keep them apart. \n\nThree states of matter are the result of balance between intermolecular forces and the thermal energy of the molecules. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No.139; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Introduction, intermolecular forces and thermal energy',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50104,'chemistry','Who explained the deviation of real gases from the ideal behaviour through intermolecular forces?','Johannes van der Waals','Johannes van der Waals explained the deviation of real gases from the ideal behaviour through intermolecular forces. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 137; Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Introduction, intermolecular forces and thermal energy',NULL,'1','mcq','Johannes van der Waals','Gay Lussac','Charles','None of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50105,'chemistry','The type of interaction between the two molecules in the given diagram is?\n(n501)','Dipole - Dipole','Dipole-dipole forces act between the molecules possessing permanent dipole. \n\nEnds of the dipoles possess “partial charges”.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 138; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Introduction, intermolecular forces and thermal energy',NULL,'3','mcq','Dipole- Induced dipole','Dipole - Dipole','Dispersion forces','Ion-dipole interaction','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/50105q.png'),
(50201,'chemistry','The first reliable measurement on properties of gases was made by Anglo-Irish scientist called','Robert Boyle','The first reliable measurement on properties of gases was made by Anglo-Irish scientist Robert Boyle in 1662.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 140; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'1','mcq','Robert Boyle','Johannes van der Waals','Joseph Gay Lussac','Jacques Charles','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50202,'chemistry','At constant temperature, the pressure of a fixed amount (i.e., number of moles n) of gas varies inversely with ___','Volume','Robert Boyle concluded that at constant temperature, the pressure of a fixed amount (i.e., number of moles n) of gas varies inversely with its volume. \n\nThis is known as Boyle’s law.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 140; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'1','mcq','Concentration','Volume','Surface area of molecules','All of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/50202q.png'),
(50203,'chemistry','Which among the following graphs corresponds to boyle\'s law in the given diagram(n503)','Only b','According to boyle\'s law, at constant temperature, the pressure is inversely proportional to volume therefore with increasing volume pressure decreases.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 140; Paragraph No .4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'3','mcq','Only a','Only b','Both','None of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/50203q.png'),
(50204,'chemistry','A sample of gas occupies 10 L under a pressure of 1 atm. What will be its volume if the pressure is increased to 2 atm? Assuming that temperature of the gas sample does not change','5L','"Given: P₁ = 1 atm; V₁ = 10 L; P₂ = 2 atm \n\nThen, applying Boyle\'s Law to find V₂ put \n\nP₁xV₁ = P₂xV₂, \n\nwe get V₂ = 5L\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 140; Paragraph No.5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'1','mcq','6L','10L','5L','1L','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50205,'chemistry','A balloon is filled with hydrogen at room temperature. It will burst if pressure exceeds 0.2 bar. If at 1 bar pressure the gas occupies 2.27 L volume, upto what volume can the balloon be expanded?','11.35L','According to Boyle’s Law P₁ x V₁ = P₂ x V₂\n\nIf P1 is 1 bar, V₁ will be 2.27 L\n\nIf P₂ = 0.2  \n\nSince balloon bursts at 0.2 bar pressure, the volume of balloon should be less than 11.35 L. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 142; Problem No. 5.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'1','mcq','10.5L','11L','11.35L','12.35L','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50206,'chemistry','Which law states that at constant pressure, the volume of a fixed mass of a gas is directly proportional to its absolute temperature?','Charles\'s law','Charles’ law states that at constant pressure, the volume of a fixed mass of a gas is directly proportional to its absolute temperature.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 142; Paragraph No.6)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'3','mcq','Charles\'s law','Gay Lussac’s Law','Boyle\'s Law','None of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/50206q.png'),
(50207,'chemistry','On a ship sailing in Pacific Ocean where temperature is 23.4°C, a balloon is filled with 2 L air. What will be the volume of the balloon when the ship reaches Indian ocean, where temperature is 26.1°C?','2.018L','V₁ = 2 L , \n\nT₁ = (23.4 + 273)K = 299.1K, T₂ = (26.1 + 273)K \n\nNow from Charles\'s law \n\nV₁xT₂ = V₂xT₁ \n\nTherefore after putting values of the above, \n\nwe get V2 = 2.018L\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 143; Problem No. 5.2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'1','mcq','2.018L','3.09L','0.2018L','20.18L','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/50207q.png'),
(50208,'chemistry','When the temperature is raised through 1°C the volume is increased by 1/273 times of the original volume. This is a statement of','Charles’ law','Charles’ law states that volume of a given mass of a gas increases or decreases by 1/273 of the original volume at 0°C for each degree rise or fall in temperature respectively, provided pressure is kept constant \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 142; Paragraph No.2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'1','mcq','Avogadro Law','Charles’ law','Gay Lussac’s Law','Boyle\'s Law','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/50208q.png'),
(50209,'chemistry','Which curve shows Charles’ law in the given diagram(n504)','2','According to Charles’ law,\n\nV ∝ T ⇒ V = kT or V × 1÷T = k\n\n⇒ V versus 1/T gives a hyperbolic graph\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 142; Paragraph No.5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'3','mcq','2','4','3','1','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/50209q.png'),
(50210,'chemistry','Applying Gay Lussac\'s law, at constant volume, as pressure increases, temperature ________','increases','Applying Gay Lussac\'s law, at constant volume, as pressure increases, temperature should also increase.\n\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 143; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'2','mcq','increases','first increases then becomes constant','decreases','first increases then decreases','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50211,'chemistry','“One gram molecules of a gas at S.T.P. occupies 22.4 litres”. This fact was derived from','Avogadro’s hypothesis','According to Avogadro’s hypothesis, at STP molar volume of an ideal gas or a combination of ideal gases is 22.71098 L mol–¹\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 144; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'1','mcq','Charles\'s law','Avogadro’s hypothesis','Gay Lussac’s Law','Boyle\'s Law','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50212,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are wrong?\n\na. Equal volumes of all gases under the same conditions of temperature and pressure contain equal\nnumber of molecules\n\nb. At constant volume, pressure of a fixed amount of a gas varies directly with the temperature','None of these','These statements are correct according to Avogadro Law and Gay Lussac’s Law respectively. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 143 and 144)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Both a and b','None of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50213,'chemistry','What is the correct order of volumes in the given diagram(n506)','V₄ > V₃ > V₂ > V₁','According to Gay Lussac’s Law, at constant volume, pressure of a fixed amount of a gas varies directly with the temperature.\n\nTherefore at a given temperature for two values of pressure, the value of volume will be lower for higher pressure and vice versa.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 143; Paragraph No.3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'3','mcq','V₁ > V₂ > V₃ > V₄','V₃ > V₂ > V₁ > V₄','V₃  > V₄ > V₂ > V₁','V₄ > V₃ > V₂ > V₁','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/50213q.png'),
(50214,'chemistry','Match A,B and C in the given diagram(n507)','A-600K, B-400K, C-200K','At a given point of volume, if we compare pressure at different temperatures then higher the temperature, higher will be pressure.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 141; Figure 5.5(a))','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'3','mcq','A-400K, B-200K, C-600K','A-600K, B-200K, C-400K','A-600K, B-400K, C-200K','A-200K, B-400K, C-600K','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/50214q.png'),
(50215,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion : Gases become denser at high pressure\n\nReason : At high pressure, gases deviate from boyle\'s law','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','When a given mass of gas is compressed, the same number of molecules occupy a smaller space. Hence, gases become denser at high pressure.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 141; Paragraph No.3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50216,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion: The lowest hypothetical or imaginary temperature at which gases are supposed to occupy zero volume is called absolute zero\n\nReason: Volume of gas at -273.15 Celsius becomes zero i.e gas doesn\'t exist at this temperature','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The lowest hypothetical or imaginary temperature at which gases are supposed to occupy zero volume is called absolute zero. \n\nGas doesn\'t exist at this temperature.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 143; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50217,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion : Molar volume of an ideal gas at 273.15K and I bar is 22.4L\n\nReason : Volume of a gas is directly proportional to temperature','Assertion is false but Reason is true','Molar volume of an ideal gas at 273.15K and I atm is 22.7L; Volume of a gas is directly proportional to temperature at constant pressure.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 144; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50218,'chemistry','Which of the following is incorrect for pressure units?','1.01325 bar is equal to 0.875 atmosphere','We know, 1 bar = 10⁵ Pa ⇒ 1 Pa = 10⁻⁵ bar \n\nand \n\n1 atm = 101325 Pa\n\n1 atm = 101325 Pa = 101325 × 10⁻⁵bar\n\n⇒ 1 atm = 1.01325 bar','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'2','mcq','1 atmosphere is equal to 1.01325 bar','1.01325 bar is equal to 0.875 atmosphere','1.01325 × 10⁵ Pa is equal to 1.01325 bar','1 atmosphere is equal to 1.01325 × 10⁵ kg m⁻¹ s⁻²','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50219,'chemistry','The slope of the graph between log P and log V at constant temperature for a given mass of a gas is','-1','According to Boyle\'s law,\n\nP ∝ 1/V or P = k/V\n\nApplying log on both sides,\n\nlog P = log k – log V\n\nComparing with y = mx + c\n\nWe get slope, m = –1\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 140; Paragraph No.4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'3','mcq','-1/2','-1','1/T','1/n','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50220,'chemistry','The density of gas 1 is twice that of gas 2 at the same temperature. The molecular weight of gas 2 is thrice that of 1. The ratio of pressure acting on 1 and 2 will be','6 : 1','According to ideal gas equation\n\nP₁ = d₁RT/M₁\n\nP₂ = d₂RT/M₂\n\nP₁P₂ = d₁ x M₂/ d₂ x M₁ = 6 : 1\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 145; Paragraph No.5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'1','mcq','6 : 1','7 : 8','3 : 2','1 : 1','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50221,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion :  surface tension decreases as the temperature is raised\n\nReason: Increase in temperature increases the kinetic energy of the molecules and effectiveness of intermolecular attraction decreases','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Increase in temperature increases the kinetic energy of the molecules and effectiveness of intermolecular attraction decreases, so surface tension decreases as the temperature is raised.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 156; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50222,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true\n\na)  Theory is a model that enables us to better understand our observations. \n\nb)  The theory that attempts to elucidate the behaviour of gases is known as kinetic molecular theory.','Both a and b','A theory is a model that enables us to better understand our observations. \n\nThe theory that attempts to elucidate the behaviour of gases is known as kinetic molecular theory.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 149; Paragraph No.2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'3','mcq','Both a and b','A only','B only','None of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50223,'chemistry','The substances in the atmosphere include','dioxygen, dinitrogen, carbon dioxide, water vapour, etc.','The thin layer of atmosphere is vital to our life. \n\nIt shields us from harmful radiations and contains substances like dioxygen, dinitrogen, carbon dioxide, water vapour, etc.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 139; Paragraph No.8)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'1','mcq','only dioxygen','only carbon dioxide','only water vapours','dioxygen, dinitrogen, carbon dioxide, water vapour, etc.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50224,'chemistry','Which of the following are characteristics of gases?\n\na.Gases are highly compressible.\n\nb.Gases exert pressure equally in all directions.\n\nc. Gases have much lower density than solids and liquids.\n\nd. The volume and shape of gases are not fixed. They assume volume and shape\nof the container.','all of these','The gaseous state is characterized by the following physical properties.\n\n• Gases are highly compressible.\n\n• Gases exert pressure equally in all\ndirections.\n\n• Gases have much lower density than the\nsolids and liquids.\n\n• The volume and the shape of gases are\nnot fixed. These assume volume and shape\nof the container.\n\n• Gases mix evenly and completely in all\nproportions without any mechanical aid.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 140; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'2','mcq','only a and b','only a and c','only b and d','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50225,'chemistry','Kelvin scale of temperature is also called_____ scale','Thermodynamic','Degree sign is not used while writing the temperature in absolute temperature scale, i.e., Kelvin scale. \n\nKelvin scale of temperature is also called Thermodynamic scale of temperature and is used in all scientific works. \n\nThus we add 273 (more precisely 273.15) to the celsius temperature to obtain temperature at Kelvin scale.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 142; Paragraph No.4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'1','mcq','Thermodynamic','Celsius','Thermometer','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50226,'chemistry','"Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion : The graph between p and 1/V is a straight line passing through origin.\n\nReason : At  high pressures, gases deviate from Boyle’s law and under such conditions a straight line is not obtained in the graph.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','The graph between p and1/V is a straight line passing through origin. \n\nA high pressures, gases deviate from Boyle’s law and under such conditions a straight line is not obtained in the graph.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 141; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Gas laws',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50227,'biology','The description of life forms and their structure became meaningful and helpful in framing research questions in','Physiology or Evolutionary Biology.','After the detailed description was utilized in the later day reductionist biology where living processes drew more attention from scientists than the description of life formsand their structure, this description became meaningful and helpful in framing research questions in physiology or evolutionary biology.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 63; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Root',NULL,'2','mcq','Pharmacology','Physiology','Evolutionary Biology','Physiology or Evolutionary Biology.','NEW','11-063','11-055',NULL),
(50228,'biology','The description of the diverse forms of life on earth was made only by observation, that included a description mainly of','Gross structural features','The description of the diverse forms of life on earth was made only by observation – through naked eyes or later through magnifying lenses and microscopes. This description is mainly of gross structural features, both external and internal.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 64; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Root',NULL,'2','mcq','Gross structural features','Functional Features','Habitat and ecosystem','A  and  B both','NEW','11-064','11-056',NULL),
(50229,'biology','Katherine Esau received the _______________ from President George Bush in 1989','National Medal of Science','Katherine Esau received the National Medal of Science from President George\nBush in 1989.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 64; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Root',NULL,'1','mcq','President\'s Honor Award','Botanist of the Year Award','National Medal of Science','Nobel Prize in Botany','NEW','11-064','11-056',NULL),
(50230,'biology','Katherine Esau studied _______ in Russia and Germany','Agriculture','Katherine studied agriculture in Russia and Germany and received her doctorate\nin 1931 in United States.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 64; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Root',NULL,'1','mcq','Anatomy of Plants','Agriculture','Marine biology','Livestock','NEW','11-064','11-056',NULL),
(50231,'biology','The portion of a plant above the ground forms the','Shoot system','The part of the plant that is above ground is known as the shoot system\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 65; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Root',NULL,'1','mcq','Nutrient-collection vessel system','Flowering parts','Root System','Shoot system','NEW','11-065','11-057',NULL),
(50232,'biology','In majority of the dicotyledonous plants, the formation of primary root takes place by','Elongation of the radicle','In majority of the dicotyledonous plants, the direct elongation of the radicle\nleads to the formation of primary root which grows inside the soil.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 65; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Root',NULL,'2','mcq','expansion of the base of the stem','Branching of lateral roots','Elongation of the radicle','Fusion of Secondary Roots','NEW','11-065','11-057',NULL),
(50233,'biology','The primary roots and its branches constitute the','Tap Root System','The primary roots and its branches constitute the Tap Root System. The roots that originate from the base of the stem in monocotyledonous plants constitute the fibrous root system. Roots that arise from parts of the plant other than the radicle and are called adventitious roots\n(NCERT Class 11; Page No:65)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Root',NULL,'1','mcq','Fibrous Root System','Tap Root System','Adventitious root system','Both A  and  B','NEW','11-065','11-057',NULL),
(50234,'biology','Consider the following statements about the functions of a root system of a plant. Choose the incorrect option','Facilitate pollination','The main functions of the root system are absorption of water and minerals from the soil, providing a proper anchorage to the plant parts, storing reserve food material and synthesis of plant growth regulators\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 66; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Root',NULL,'1','mcq','Absorption of water and minerals from the soil','Providing anchorage to the plant','Storing reserve food material','Facilitate pollination','NEW','11-066','11-058',NULL),
(50235,'biology','Pneumatophores are roots that help to get oxygen for _____ in plants.','Respiration','In some plants such as Rhizophora growing in swampy areas, many roots come out of the ground and grow vertically upwards. Such roots, called pneumatophores, help to get oxygen for respiration.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 67; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Root',NULL,'1','mcq','Respiration','Evaporation','Reproduction','Digestion','NEW','11-067',NULL,NULL),
(50236,'biology','Rhizophora grows in what kind of area?','Swampy Areas','In some plants such as Rhizophora growing in swampy areas, many roots come out of the ground and grow vertically upwards. Such roots, called pneumatophores, help to get oxygen for respiration\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 67; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Root',NULL,'1','mcq','Forests','Swampy Areas','Deserts','Sea Water','NEW','11-067',NULL,NULL),
(50237,'biology','The hanging structures that support a banyan tree are called','Prop Roots','Prop roots are modified roots that help support a banyan tree. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 67; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Root',NULL,'1','mcq','Prop Roots','Fibrous Roots','Pneumatophores','Tendrils','NEW','11-067',NULL,NULL),
(50238,'biology','A few millimeters, right above the root cap is the','Region of meristematic activity','A few millimeters above the root cap is the region of meristematic\nactivity. The cells of this region are very small, thin-walled and with dense protoplasm. They\ndivide repeatedly\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 67; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Root',NULL,'1','mcq','Region of elongation','Region of meristematic activity','Region of maturation','Root Hair system','NEW','11-067','11-059',NULL),
(50239,'biology','Consider the following statements about the root cap and choose the correct option','Both It is a thimble-like structure that covers the root at the apex and It protects the tender apex of the root as it makes its way through the soil','Root cap is present at apex and protects the root. Root hairs form at the region of maturation instead of at the root cap.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 67; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Root',NULL,'2','mcq','It gives rise to delicate, thread-like structures called root hairs','It is a thimble-like structure that covers the root at the apex','It protects the tender apex of the root as it makes its way through the soil','Both It is a thimble-like structure that covers the root at the apex and It protects the tender apex of the root as it makes its way through the soil','NEW','11-067','11-059',NULL),
(50301,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is correct?\n\na. At constant T and n V ∝ 1/p = Boyle’s Law\n\nb. At constant p and n V ∝ T = Charles’ Law \n\nc. At constant p and T  V ∝ n-Avogadro Law = Ideal gas equation-PV=nRT','All of these','The three combined together in a single equation is known as ideal gas equation.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 145; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'3','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c','All of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50302,'chemistry','Ideal gas equation is a relation between four variables and it describes the state of any gas, therefore, it is also called equation of ____','States','Ideal gas equation is a relation between four variables and it describes the state of any gas, therefore, it is also called equation of state.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 145; Paragraph No.4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'1','mcq','States','Commons','Equality','Integration','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50303,'chemistry','At 25°C and 760 mm of Hg pressure a gas occupies 600 mL volume. What will be its pressure at a height where temperature is 10°C and volume of the gas is 640 mL.','676.6 mm Hg','It is based on ideal gas equation, here P₁V₁/T₁ = P₂V₂/T₂\n\nPutting values in this in their standard form, we get that P₂ = 67606 mm Hg\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 146; Problem No. 5.3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'1','mcq','6.766 mm Hg','67.66 mm Hg','676.6 mm Hg','0.676 mm Hg','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50304,'chemistry','The density of neon will be highest at \n\n(1) STP \n\n(2) 0°C and 2 atm \n\n(3) 273°C and 1 atm \n\n(4) 273°C and 2 atm','2','According to ideal gas equation,\n\nd = PM/RT\n\n∴ d ∝ P/T\n\nTherefore option 2 is correct\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 146; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50305,'chemistry','Which among the following is correct?\n\na. The total pressure exerted by the mixture of non-reactive gases is equal to the sum of the partial pressures of individual gases.\n\nb. Pressure exerted by saturated water vapour is called aqueous tension.','Both of these','According to Dalton’s Law\n\nThe total pressure exerted by the mixture of non-reactive gases is equal to the sum of the partial pressures of individual gases. \n\nPressure exerted by saturated water vapour is called aqueous tension.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 146; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'2','mcq','Only b','Only a','Both of these','None of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/50305q.png'),
(50306,'chemistry','For the general equation written as Pᵢ = XᵢPtotal, Xᵢ represents the _____ 0f the gas','Mole fraction','From Dalton’s law, partial pressure of gas = mole fraction x Total pressure of mixture\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 147; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'1','mcq','Density','Mole fraction','Partial pressure','Temperature','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/50306q.png'),
(50307,'chemistry','A neon-dioxygen mixture contains 70.6 g dioxygen and 167.5 g neon. If pressure of the mixture of gases in the cylinder is 25 bar. What is the partial pressure of neon in the mixture?','19.75 bar','Number of moles of O2 = 70.6 g / 32.00 g/mol = 2.20625 mol\nNumber of moles of Ne = 167.5 g / 20.18 g/mol = 8.30455 mol\n\nThe total number of moles of gases in the mixture is the sum of the moles of dioxygen and neon:\n\nTotal number of moles = 2.20625 mol + 8.30455 mol = 10.5108 mol\n\nNext, we can calculate the mole fraction of each gas:\n\nMole fraction of O2 = 2.20625 mol / 10.5108 mol = 0.2097\nMole fraction of Ne = 8.30455 mol / 10.5108 mol = 0.7903\n\nFinally, we can calculate the partial pressures of dioxygen and neon using the total pressure of the mixture (25 bar):\n\nPartial pressure of O2 = 0.2097 × 25 bar = 5.243 bar\nPartial pressure of Ne = 0.7903 × 25 bar = 19.757 bar\n\nTherefore, the partial pressure of dioxygen in the mixture is 5.243 bar and the partial pressure of neon is 19.757 bar.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 147; Problem No. 5.4 )','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'1','mcq','197.5 bar','19.75 bar','15.75 bar','0.1975 bar','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/50307q.png'),
(50308,'chemistry','Which of these mixture of gases at room temperature does not obey Dalton\'s law of partial pressure?','NH₃ and HCl','Dalton\'s law states that the pressure exerted by the mixture of non-reactive gases is\nequal to the sum of the partial pressures\nof individual gases. \n\nNH₃ and HCl mixture would not follow Dalton\'s law of partial pressure since they react with each other. Ammonia and HCl reacts to form ammonium chloride.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 146; Paragraph No. 2 )','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'3','mcq','NH₃ and HCl','NO₂ and O₂','CO and CO₂','SO₂ and SO₃','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50309,'chemistry','A vessel has 6 g of oxygen at a pressure P and temperature 400 K. A small hole is made in it so that O₂\nleaks out. How much O₂leaks out if the pressure is P/2 and temperature 300 K?','2 g','PV = nRT, P₁ = P and P₂ = P/2 \n\nusing P₁x W₂T₂=  P₂ x W₁ x t₁, we get W₂ = 4g of oxygen\n\nTherefore O₁ will be 2 g\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 145; Paragraph No.5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'1','mcq','3 g','5 g','4 g','2 g','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(40507,'chemistry','Match the following:','CO₂-double covalent bond,H₂O-single covalent bond,N₂-triple covalent bond,He-duplet of electrons','H-O-H single covalent bond, O=C=O double covalent bond, N⫢N triple covalent bond, He=1s² duplet of electrons. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 103; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Covalent bond',NULL,'3','match','CO₂,double covalent bond','H₂O,single covalent bond','N₂,triple covalent bond','He,duplet of electrons','NEW','11-102','11-102',NULL),
(50310,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion : Hot air balloon rises up by displacing the denser air of the atmosphere.\n\nReason : The given mass of a gas becomes less denser','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','In a hot air balloon, temperature is high\n\n∵ d ∝ 1/T\n\n∵ density is lower as a result of which it displaces denser air and rises up\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 146; Paragraph No.2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50311,'chemistry','R in PV = nRT (ideal gas equation) is called','Universal gas constant','R is called gas constant. It is same for all gases.\n\nTherefore it is also called Universal Gas Constant.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 145; Paragraph No.3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'1','mcq','Van der waal constant','Boyle constant','Universal gas constant','Charles constant','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50312,'chemistry','Pressure exerted by saturated water vapour is called ___ tension','aqueous','Gases are generally collected over water and therefore are moist. \n\nPressure of dry gas can be calculated by subtracting vapour pressure of water from the total pressure of the moist gas which contains water vapours also. \n\nPressure exerted by saturated water vapour is called aqueous tension.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 146; Paragraph No.3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'1','mcq','aqueous','surface','viscous','limited','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50313,'chemistry','What will be the pressure exerted by a mixture of 3.2 g of methane and 4.4 g of carbon dioxide contained in a 9 dm³ flask at 27 °C','8.31 x 10⁴ Pa','The number of moles of methane is 16/3.2 =0.2.\n\nThe pressure exerted by methane is P= nRT/V\n\n= 0.2×8.314×300/9 x 10⁻³ = 5.54 x 10⁴ Pa\n\nThe number of moles of carbon dioxide = 0.1\n\nThe pressure exerted by Carbon dioxide is P` = n`RT/V = 2.77 x 10⁴ Pa\n\nTotal pressure = P + P` = 8.31 x 10⁴ Pa\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 147; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'2','mcq','10³ Pa','1 Pa','8.31 x 10⁴ Pa','10⁴ Pa','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50314,'chemistry','What will be the pressure of the gaseous mixture when 0.5 L of H₂ at 0.8 bar and 2.0 L of dioxygen at 0.7 bar are introduced in a 1 L vessel at 27°C?','1.8 bar','The partial pressure of hydrogen gas\n\nP`V` = PV = 0.8 x0.5 / 1 = 0.4 bar\n\nThe partial pressure of oxygen gas  \n\nP`x V`` = Px V = 1.4 bar \n\nTotal pressure = P` + P`` = 1.8 bar\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 146; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'1','mcq','1.5 bar','1.8 bar','0.18 bar','18 bar','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50315,'chemistry','Which of the following  statements are true\n\na) All gases obey Charles’ law at very low pressures and high temperatures.\n\nb) Gay Lussac’s law states that at constant volume, pressure of a fixed amount of a gas varies directly with the\ntemperature','both a and b','All gases obey Charles’ law at very low pressures and high temperatures. \n\nGay Lussac’s law states that at constant volume, pressure of a fixed amount of a gas varies directly with the temperature.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 143; Paragraph No.3 and 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50316,'chemistry','A mixture of dihydrogen and dioxygen at one bar pressure contains 20% by weight of dihydrogen. Calculate the partial pressure of dihydrogen.','0.8 bar','In 100 g of mixture, the mass of hydrogen and oxygen will be 20 and 80 g respectively.\n\nThe number of moles of hydrogen are 10\n\nThe number of moles of oxygen are 2.5\n\nThe mole fraction of hydrogen is 0.8\n\nPartial pressure of hydrogen is 0.8× Total pressure = 0.8 bar\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 146; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'2','mcq','0.8 bar','0.2 bar','2 bar','8 bar','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50317,'chemistry','2.9 g of a gas at 95 °C occupied the same volume as 0.184 g of dihydrogen at 17 °C, at the same pressure. What is the molar mass of the gas','40 g','Let M be the molar mass of the gas.\n\nThe number of moles of the gas, n=  2.9/M\n\nThe volume of the gas is V= nRT/P\n\nV= 2.9R×368/MP\n\nThe number of moles of dihydrogen are n` = 0.092\n\nThe volume of dihydrogen gas is =  0.092×R×290/P\n\nAt same pressure P, the volume of gas is equal to volume of dihydrogen.\n\n0.092R×290= 2.9R×368/M\n\nM = 40\n\nThus, the molar mass of the gas is 40g/mol\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 146; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'2','mcq','40 g','20g','30 g','10 g','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/50317q.png'),
(50318,'chemistry','Calculate the volume occupied by 8.8 g of Carbon dioxide at 31.1°C and 1 bar pressure.R = 0.083 bar L K⁻¹ mol⁻¹','5.05 L','The number of moles is n = 0.2 \n\nThe volume occupied is  V = nRT/P\n\nV= 0.2×0.083×304.1 = 5.05 L\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 145; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'1','mcq','5.05 L','5.5 L','55.5 L','0.5 L','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/50318q.png'),
(50319,'chemistry','Calculate the temperature of 4.0 mol of a gas occupying 5 dm³ at 3.32 bar. (R = 0.083 bar dm³ K⁻¹ mol⁻¹).','50 K','no. of moles (n) = 4\n\nP = 3.32 bar\n\nR = 0.083 bar dm³ K⁻¹ mol⁻\n\nVolume = 5 dm³\n\nAccording to ideal gas equation, PV = nRT\n\nT = 50 K\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 145; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'1','mcq','0.5 K','50 K','500 K','5 K','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/50319q.png'),
(50320,'chemistry','Density of a gas is found to be 5.46 g/dm³  at 27⁰C and 2 bar pressure. What will be its density at STP?','3 g/dm³','P= dRt/M\n\nor d∝ P/T\n\nd₁/d₂ = P₁T₂/P₂T₁\n\nTherefore d₂ =  3 g/dm³  \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 146; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'1','mcq','5.46 g/dm³','3 g/dm³','4.6 g/dm³','3.5 g/dm³','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50321,'chemistry','A vessel of 120 mL capacity contains a certain amount of gas at 35 °C and 1.2 bar pressure. The gas is transferred to another vessel of volume 180 mL at 35 °C. What would be its pressure?','0.8 bar','P` x V` = P x V\n\nP` = 0.8 bar\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 140; Paragraph No.4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'1','mcq','8 bar','0.2 bar','2 bar','0.8 bar','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50322,'chemistry','At 0°C, the density of a certain oxide of a gas at 2 bar is same as that of dinitrogen at 5 bar. What is the molecular mass of the oxide?','70 g / mol','d= MP/RT\n\nM x P = M` x P` \n\nP = 2 bar , M` = 28, P` = 5 bar(Dinitrogen)\n\nM x 2 = 28 x 5\n\nM = 70 g/ mol\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 146; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'1','mcq','70 g / mol','700 g / mol','7 g / mol','0.70 g / mol','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50323,'chemistry','34.05 mL of phosphorus vapour weighs 0.0625 g at 546 °C and 0.1 bar pressure. What is the molar mass of phosphorus?','124.98 g','The molar mass of phosphorus,\n\n M= wRT/PV = 124.98 g\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 146; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'1','mcq','124.98 g','12 g','1.24g','0.124 g','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50324,'chemistry','Calculate the total pressure in a mixture of 8 g of dioxygen and 4 g of dihydrogen confined in a vessel of 1  dm³  at 27°C. R = 0.083 bar  L K⁻¹ mol⁻¹','56.025 bar','The partial pressure of oxygen is P = nRT/V = 0.225 bar\n\nThe partial pressure of hydrogen gas is P` = n`RT/V` = 49.8 bar\n\nTotal pressure P + P` = 56.025 bar.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 146; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Ideal gas equation and Dalton’s Law',NULL,'2','mcq','56.025 bar','5.6025 bar','0.5 bar','5625 bar','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50325,'biology','Stem develops from the _________ of the embryo of the germinating seed.','Plumule','Stem develops from the plumule of the embryo of a germinating seed.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Stem',NULL,'1','mcq','Epicotyl','Hypocotyl','Radicle','Plumule','NEW','11-068',NULL,NULL),
(50326,'biology','The function of the stem tendrils is to','Help plants climb','Stem tendrils which develop from axillary buds, are slender and spirally coiled and help plants to climb such as in gourds and grapevines.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 68; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Stem',NULL,'1','mcq','Protect plants from animals','Store food in them','Help plants climb','Act as organs of perennation','NEW','11-068',NULL,NULL),
(50401,'chemistry','The speed and energy of all the molecules of a gas at any instant are','Different','The speed and energy of all the molecules of the gas at any instant are not the same.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 147; Paragraph No.3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Kinetic energy and molecular speed',NULL,'1','mcq','Same','Different','Zero','Maximum','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50402,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is wrong?\n\n1. Maxwell and Boltzmann have shown that actual distribution of molecular speeds depends on temperature and molecular mass of a gas.\n\n2. The speed possessed by maximum number of molecules in a gas is called the most probable speed.','None of these','Maxwell and Boltzmann have shown that actual distribution of molecular speeds depends on temperature and molecular mass of a gas. \n\nThe speed possessed by maximum number of molecules in a gas is called the most probable speed.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 148; Paragraph No.1 and 2 )','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Kinetic energy and molecular speed',NULL,'1','mcq','Only 1','Only 2','1 and 2','None of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50403,'chemistry','Root mean square speed, average speed and the most probable speed are in the order :','Uᵣₘₛ > Uₐᵥ> Uₘₚ','Root mean square speed, average speed and the most probable speed have following\nrelationship:\n\nurms > uav > ump\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 148; Paragraph No.4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Kinetic energy and molecular speed',NULL,'3','mcq','Uᵣₘₛ > Uₐᵥ> Uₘₚ','Uᵣₘₛ > Uₘₚ > Uₐᵥ','Uₐᵥ> Uₘₚ > Uᵣₘₛ','None of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(50404,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true\n\na)  The mean square speed is not the  direct measure of the average kinetic energy of gas molecules\n\nb)  The ratio between the root mean square speed, average speed and the most probable speed is given as \nump: uav : urms: : 1 : 1.128 : 1.224','b only','The mean square speed is the direct measure of the average kinetic energy of gas molecules.\n\nThe ratio between the root mean square speed, average speed and the most probable speed is given as \n\nump: uav : urms: : 1 : 1.128 : 1.224.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 148; Paragraph No.1 and 5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Kinetic energy and molecular speed',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/50404q.png'),
(50405,'chemistry','If pressure of a fixed quantity of a gas is increased 4 times keeping the temperature constant, the r.m.s. velocity will be','same','Root mean square speed does not depend on pressure.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 148; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Kinetic energy and molecular speed',NULL,'1','mcq','4 times','3 times','half','same','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/50405q.png'),
(50406,'chemistry','At what temperature would the RMS velocity of oxygen be the same as that of methane at 27°C?','600K','We know root mean square velocity = square root of (3RT/M)\n\nTherefore after putting values of T, M for dioxygen and methane respectively in S.I units, we get temperature the RMS velocity of oxygen will be same as that of methane at 27°C is 600K\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 148; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Kinetic energy and molecular speed',NULL,'1','mcq','200K','500K','600K','400K','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50407,'chemistry','Match A and B correctly in the given diagram(n508)','A-chlorine, B-nitrogen','Most probable speed is inversely proportional to molecular mass.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 148; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Kinetic energy and molecular speed',NULL,'3','mcq','A-chlorine, B-chlorine','A-nitrogen, B-nitrogen','A-nitrogen, B-chlorine','A-chlorine, B-nitrogen','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/50407q.png'),
(50408,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion : In Maxwell - Boltzmann distribution of speeds, the curve broadens at higher temperature\n\nReason : At a particular temperature, the individual speed of molecules as well as the distribution of speeds remains the same','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Speed distribution curve broadens at higher temperature because number of molecules moving at higher speed increases. \n\nAt a particular temperature, the individual speed of molecules keeps changing but the distribution of speeds remains same\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 148; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Kinetic energy and molecular speed',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50409,'chemistry','What is the velocity corresponding to point X in the given diagram(n509)','√2RT/M','The maximum in the curve represents speed possessed by maximum number of\nmolecules. \n\nThis speed is called most probable speed, ump and it\'s value is √2RT/M\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 148; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Kinetic energy and molecular speed',NULL,'3','mcq','√2RT/M','√3RT/M','√8RT/πM','√2RT/πM','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/50409q.png'),
(50410,'biology','Match the following','parallel venation-monocotyledons,reticulate venation-dicotyledonous plants','Leaves of dicotyledonous plants generally possess reticulate venation, while parallel venation is the characteristic of most monocotyledons(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 70; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Leaf',NULL,'2','match','parallel venation,monocotyledons','reticulate venation,dicotyledonous plants',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-070','11-060',NULL),
(50411,'biology','Multifoliate compound leaf (palmate compound leaf) is found in -','Silkcotton (bombax)','In palmately compund the leaflets are attached to a common point at the tip of petiole ,as in cotton silk(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 71; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Leaf',NULL,'2','mcq','Tamarind','Silkcotton (bombax)','Moringa','Rose','NEW','11-071','11-060',NULL),
(50412,'biology','Arrangement of leaves on stem or its branches is called -','Phyllotaxy','Phyllotaxy is the pattern of arrangement of leaves on the stem or branch(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 71; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Leaf',NULL,'2','mcq','Phyllotaxy','Venation','Vernation','Heterophylly','NEW','11-071','11-061',NULL),
(50413,'biology','Match the following (Types of Phyllotaxy with examples)','alternate type-mustard and sun flower plants,opposite type-Calotropis and guava plants,whorled-Alstonia','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 71; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Leaf',NULL,'3','match','alternate type,mustard and sun flower plants','opposite type,Calotropis and guava plants','whorled,Alstonia',NULL,'NEW','11-071','11-061',NULL),
