(50423,'biology','Leaves of dicotyledonous plants generally possess','Reticulate Venation','Leaves of dicotyledonous plants generally possess reticulate venation, while parallel venation is the characteristic of most monocotyledons\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 70; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Leaf',NULL,'2','mcq','No Venation','Reticulate Venation','Parallel Venation','Radial Venation','NEW','11-070','11-060',NULL),
(50501,'chemistry','Which among the following are among the assumptions of the  ideal gas theory?\n\na. The actual volume of the molecules is negligible in comparison to the empty space between them.\n\nb. There is no force of attraction between the particles of a gas at ordinary temperature and\npressure.','Both a and b','Both of the statements are postulates of kinetic theory of gases\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 149; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'1','mcq','Only a','Only b','Both a and b','None of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50502,'chemistry','The total energy before and after collision of gas molecules:','Remains same','Collisions of gas molecules are perfectly elastic. \n\nThis means that total energy of molecules before and after the collision remains same.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 149; Paragraph No.7)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'1','mcq','Remains same','Gets halved','Doubles','Always increases','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50503,'chemistry','Which statement is correct?\n\na. At any particular time, different particles in the gas have different speeds and hence different kinetic energies.\n\nb. There may be exchange of energy between colliding molecules, their individual energies may change, but the\nsum of their energies remains constant.','Both of these','At any particular time, different particles in the gas have different speeds and hence different kinetic energies. \n\nThere may be exchange of energy between colliding molecules, their individual energies may change, but the sum of their energies remains constant.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 149; Paragraph No.7 and 8)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Both of these','None of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50504,'chemistry','According to the  kinetic theory of gases, the collisions between molecules of a gas','occur in straight line','Particles of a gas move in all possible directions in straight lines\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 149; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'1','mcq','occur in straight line','occur in zig zag path','result in setting down of molecules','result in change of velocity and total energy','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/50504q.png'),
(50505,'chemistry','Identify A and B in the given diagram(n510)','A-CO,B-CH₄','A is CO and B is CH₄\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 150; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'3','mcq','A-CO,B-CH₄','A-CO₂, B-CH₄','A-CH₄,B-CO','A-CO₂, B-NH₄','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/50505q.png'),
(50506,'chemistry','Identity A and B in the given diagram(n511)','A-Real gas, B-Ideal gas','A is a Real gas and B is an Ideal gas\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 150; Paragraph No.3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'3','mcq','A-Ideal gas, B- Real gas','A-Real gas, B- Real gas','A-Ideal gas, B- Ideal gas','A-Real gas, B-Ideal gas','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/50506q.png'),
(50507,'chemistry','Van der Waal found that two assumptions made by kinetic theory of gases were wrong, one of them is that gas molecules are','Point particles without significant volume','According to van der Waal\'s, two postulates of kinetic theory of gases were wrong. These are:\n\n(1) Forces of attraction between gas molecules are zero (Pressure correction)\n\n(2) Volume occupied by the molecules themselves is negligibly small as compared to the total volume of the gas (volume correction)\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 151; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'1','mcq','Compressible','Point particles without significant volume','Spherical','Very large','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/50507q.png'),
(50508,'chemistry','Correct statement among the following is\n\n1. Repulsive interactions are short-range interactions and are significant when molecules are almost in contact.\n\n2. The real gases show ideal behaviour when pressure approaches zero.','Both of these','Repulsive interactions are short-range interactions and are significant when molecules are almost in contact. \n\nReal gases show ideal behaviour when pressure approaches zero.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 151; Paragraph No.3 and 5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'1','mcq','Only 1','Only 2','Both of these','None of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50509,'chemistry','In van der Waal’s equation of state for a non-ideal gas, the term that accounts for intermolecular forces is','[P + a/V²]','A real gas experiences attractive intermolecular forces from other molecules, and as an effect, the force with which a molecule hits the wall of container decreases. \n\nThus pressure of real gas is lesser than that of ideal gas\n\nPideal = Preal + an²/V²\n\nThis above expression takes intermolecular forces into account.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 151; Paragraph No. 3 )','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'1','mcq','V – 1','[P + a/V²]','RT - n','a/b','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50510,'chemistry','In the equation [P + a/V²] [V- nb], a and b are','Van\nder Waals constants','Constants a and b are called van der Waals constants and their value depends on the characteristic of a gas\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 151; Paragraph No. 5 )','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'2','mcq','Boltzmann constant','Van\nder Waals constants','Gas constant','Avogadro\'s constant','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50511,'chemistry','The deviation from ideal behaviour can be measured in terms of ____ factor','compressibility','The deviation from ideal behaviour can be measured in terms of compressibility factor Z, which is the ratio of product pV and nRT\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 151; Paragraph No. 7 )','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'1','mcq','compressibility','deviation','independent','constant','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50512,'chemistry','For an ideal gas, Z = 1 at','All temperatures','For ideal gas Z = 1 at all temperatures and pressures because pV = nRT\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 151; Paragraph No. 8)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'1','mcq','300 K','273K','0 K','All temperatures','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50513,'chemistry','At high pressure all  gases have Z','Z > 1','At high pressure all the gases have Z > 1. They are more difficult to compress.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 151; Paragraph No. 8)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'1','mcq','Z < 1','Z > 1','0','Negative','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/50513q.png'),
(50514,'chemistry','At intermediate pressures, most gases have Z','Z < 1','At intermediate pressures, most gases have Z\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 151; Paragraph No. 8)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'1','mcq','Z > 1','Z < 1','0','Negative','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/50514q.png'),
(50515,'chemistry','The temperature at which a real gas obeys ideal gas law over an appreciable range of pressure is called','Boyle point','The temperature at which a real gas obeys ideal gas law over an appreciable range of pressure is called Boyle temperature or Boyle point. \n\nBoyle point of a gas depends upon its nature\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 152; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'1','mcq','Freezing point','Boyle point','Melting point','Boiling point','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/50515q.png'),
(50516,'chemistry','Above their Boyle point, real gases show ____ deviations from ideality','Positive','Above their Boyle point, real gases show positive deviations from ideality and Z values are greater than one.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 152; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'1','mcq','Positive','Negative','Neutral','Relative','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50517,'chemistry','At A pressure and B temperature gases show ideal behaviour','A-low, B-High','At low pressure and high temperature gases show ideal behaviour. These conditions are different for different gases.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 152; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'3','mcq','A-low, B-low','A-high, B-high','A-low, B-High','A-high, B-low','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/50517q.png'),
(50518,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true','A-actual molar volume, B-molar volume','Compressibility factor is the ratio of actual molar volume of a gas to the molar volume of the gas. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 152; Paragraph No.2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'3','match','A,actual molar volume','B,molar volume',NULL,NULL,'NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/50518q.png'),
(50519,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true\n\na) Deviation from ideal behaviour becomes apparent when pressure vs volume plot is drawn\n\nb} Theoretical model of gases do not correspond with the experimental observations','a only','Our theoretical model of gases corresponds very well with the experimental observations.  \n\nDeviation from ideal behaviour also becomes apparent when pressure vs volume plot is drawn. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 152; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','Both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/50519q.png'),
(50520,'chemistry','Which of the following gas always shows positive deviation from ideal gas behaviour?','H₂','Z versus pressure graph for H₂ shows positive deviation always i.e. at all pressures.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 152; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'1','mcq','H₂','CO₂','NH₃','CH₄','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/50520q.png'),
(50521,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion: At constant temperature, pV vs V plot for real gases is not a straight line.\n\nReason: At high pressure all gases have Z > 1 but at intermediate pressure most gases have Z < 1.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','At high pressure all the gases have Z > 1. \n\nThey are more difficult to compress.\n\nAt constant temperature, pV vs V plot for real gases is not a straight line.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 151; Paragraph No. 8)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50522,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion: At constant temperature PV vs P plot for real gases is not a straight line\n\nReason: In the curves of dihydrogen and helium, as the pressure increases the value of PV also increases','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','PV vs p plot for real gases is not a straight line as real gases don\'t follow ideal gas equation\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 150; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50523,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion: The gases show ideal behavior when the volume occupied is large so that the volume of the molecules can be neglected in comparison to it\n\nReason: The behavior of gas becomes more ideal when pressure is low','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Gases show ideal behaviour when the volume occupied is large so that the volume of the molecules can be neglected in comparison to it. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 152; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50524,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion: Compressibility factor Z is the ratio of actual molar volume of a gas to the calculated molar volume of it, if it were an ideal gas at that temperature and pressure\n\nReason: At high pressure all the gases have Z<1 and can be easily compressed','Assertion is true but Reason is false','At high pressure the gases have Z >1 and are difficult to compress\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 151; Paragraph No. 8)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50525,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion: The value of van der Waal\'s constant \'a\' of Cl₂ is larger than that of F₂.\n\nReason: The larger the surface area, the higher will be intermolecular forces of attraction.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','van der Waal\'s constant \'a\' of Cl₂ is larger since \'a\' depends on intermolecular interactions and intermolecular interaction increases with an increase in surface area\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 158; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50526,'chemistry','Which of these statements are correct?\n\na) At very low temperature, intermolecular forces become significant.\n\nb) The real gases show ideal behaviour when pressure approaches zero.','both a and b','At very low temperature, intermolecular forces become significant \n\nThe real gases show ideal behaviour when pressure approaches zero\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 151; Paragraph No. 5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'2','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50527,'chemistry','Which of this statements are true\n\na) A gas that follows Boyle’s law, Charles’ law and Avogadro law strictly is hypothetical.\n\nb) It is assumed that intermolecular forces are not present between the molecules of an ideal gas.\n\nc) Real gases follow gas laws only under certain specific conditions when forces of interaction are practically negligible.','a, b and c','A gas that follows Boyle’s law, Charles’ law and Avogadro law strictly is called an ideal gas. Such a gas is hypothetical. \n\nIt is assumed that intermolecular forces are not present between the molecules of an ideal gas. \n\nReal gases follow gas laws only under certain specific conditions when forces of interaction are practically negligible. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 145; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Real gas behavior and deviation from ideal gas behavior',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','a and b only','a, b and c','b and c only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50528,'biology','In marginal placentation, the ovules are arranged -','ovules are borne on the ridge forming two rows','In marginal placentation the placenta forms a ridge along the ventral suture of the ovary and the ovules are borne on this ridge forming two rows, as in pea(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 75; Paragraph No. 4)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Inflorescence And Flower',NULL,'2','mcq','Along the inner wall of the carpel in a syncarpous ovary','ovules are borne on the ridge forming two rows','In the middle of the ovary','To the base of the ovary','NEW','11-075',NULL,NULL),
(50529,'biology','When the ovules are borne on central axis and septa are absent, as in Dianthus and Primrose the placentation is called','Free central placentation','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 75-76; Paragraph No. 4-1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Inflorescence And Flower',NULL,'2','mcq','Marginal placentation','Parietal placentation','Axile placentation','Free central placentation','NEW','11-075',NULL,NULL),
