(50535,'biology','Basal placentaticin develops when the ovary has -','Single ovule','Basal placentation is developed at the base of ovary and a single ovule is attached to it and it is seen in Sunflower(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 76; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Inflorescence And Flower',NULL,'2','mcq','Single ovule','Many ovules','Many locules','Single ovule in each locule','NEW','11-076',NULL,NULL),
(50536,'biology','Ovary is one chambered but it may be two or more chambered due to formation of the false septa and ovules develop on the inner wall of the ovary or on peripheral part. in this case placentation is -','Parietal','Ovary is one chambered but it may be two or more chambered due to formation of the false septa and ovules develop on the inner wall of the ovary or on peripheral part. This is parietal placentation.\nParietal placentation is seen in mustard(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 75; Paragraph No. 4)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Inflorescence And Flower',NULL,'2','mcq','Parietal','Marginal','Axile','Basal','NEW','11-075',NULL,NULL),
(50537,'biology','In ______ type of inflorescence the main axis continuous to grow , the flowers are borne laterally in acropetal succession.','Racemose','(NCERT Class 11 Page No:72 Figure 5.12)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Inflorescence And Flower',NULL,'2','mcq','Cymose','Racemose','Capitulum','None of the above','NEW','11-072',NULL,NULL),
(50538,'biology','When a ____________ transforms into a flower, it is always solitary','Shoot Tip','When a shoot tip transforms into a flower, it is always solitary','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Inflorescence And Flower',NULL,'1','mcq','Shoot Tip','Root Tip','Axillary Bud','Thalamus','NEW','11-072',NULL,NULL),
(50539,'biology','A flower is a modified shoot wherein the shoot apical meristem changes to','Floral Meristem','A flower is a modified shoot wherein the shoot apical meristem changes to floral meristem\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 70; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Inflorescence And Flower',NULL,'2','mcq','Basal Meristem','Successive shoot tips','Lateral Meristem','Floral Meristem','NEW','11-070',NULL,NULL),
(50540,'biology','A flower may have a part termed as perianth, such as in a lily, where','the calyx and corolla are not distinct','In some flowers like lily, the calyx and corolla are not distinct and are termed as perianth.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 72; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Inflorescence And Flower',NULL,'3','mcq','the calyx and corolla are not distinct','the androecium and calyx are not distinct','the androecium and corolla are not distinct.','the androecium and gynoecium are not distinct.','NEW','11-072',NULL,NULL),
(50541,'biology','The accessory parts of a flower are','Calyx and corolla','Calyx and corolla are accessory organs, while androecium and gynoecium are reproductive organs of a flower.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 72; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Inflorescence And Flower',NULL,'2','mcq','Androecium, Corolla and calyx','Gynoecium, corolla and calyx','Corolla, gynoecium and androecium','Calyx and corolla','NEW','11-072',NULL,NULL),
(50542,'biology','The calyx may be having its sepals united or free. A sepal with united sepals is known as','Gamosepalous','Sepals that are united form a gamosepalous calyx.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 72; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Inflorescence And Flower',NULL,'2','mcq','Unisepalous','Monosepalous','Polysepalous','Gamosepalous','NEW','11-072',NULL,NULL),
(50543,'biology','Another name for vexillary type Aestivation is ______.','papilionaceous','In pea and bean flowers, there are five petals, the largest (standard) overlaps the two lateral petals (wings) which in turn overlap the two smallest anterior petals (keel); this type of aestivation is known as vexillary or papilionaceous\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 74; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Inflorescence And Flower',NULL,'3','mcq','twisted','papilionaceous','imbricate','valvate','NEW','11-074',NULL,NULL),
(50544,'biology','The style connects the ovary to the _____.','stigma','The style connects the ovary to the stigma\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 72; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Inflorescence And Flower',NULL,'1','mcq','petals','anthers','stigma','ovule','NEW','11-072',NULL,NULL),
(50601,'chemistry','The critical temperature of CO₂ gas is 304K. The gas cannot be liquefied at','608K','Gases can only be liquefied below their critical temperature\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','200K','273K','145K','608K','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50602,'chemistry','Volume of one mole of gas at critical temperature is called A and pressure at this temperature is called B.Match A and B','A-Critical volume, B-Critical pressure','Volume of one mole of the gas at critical temperature is called critical volume (VC) and pressure at this temperature is called critical pressure (pC)\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 152; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'3','mcq','A-Critical volume, B-Critical concentration','A-Critical volume, B-Critical pressure','A-Critical pressure, B-Critical volume','A-Critical pressure,  B-Critical concentration','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50603,'chemistry','Gases possess characteristic critical temperature which depends upon the magnitude of intermolecular forces between the gas particles. \n\nCritical temperatures of ammonia and carbon dioxide are 405.5 K and 304.10 K respectively. Which of these gases will liquify first when you start cooling from 500 K to their critical temperature ?','Ammonia','Ammonia will liquify first because its critical temperature will be reached first. \n\nLiquefaction of CO2 will require more cooling.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'2','mcq','Ammonia','Carbon dioxide','Depends on number of moles taken','Both at the same time','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50604,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion:  Gases do not liquefy above their critical temperature, even\non applying high pressure.\n\nReason: Above critical temperature, the molecular speed is high and\nintermolecular attractions cannot hold the molecules together\nbecause they escape because of high speed.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Gases do not liquefy above their critical temperature, even on applying high pressure because above critical temperature, the molecular speed is high and intermolecular attractions cannot hold the molecules together because they escape because of high speed\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 152; Paragraph No.4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50605,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion:  At critical temperature liquid passes into gaseous state imperceptibly and continuously.\n\nReason: The density of liquid and gaseous phase is equal to critical temperature.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','At critical temperature, liquid passes into gaseous state imperceptibly and continuously; the surface separating two phases disappears\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/50605q.png'),
(50606,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion:  Liquids and solids are hard to compress\n\nReason: Magnitude of the repulsive forces between molecules rises very rapidly as the distance between them decreases','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Intermolecular forces discussed so far are all attractive. Molecules also exert repulsive forces on one another. \n\nWhen two molecules are brought into close contact with each other, the repulsion between the electron clouds and that between the nuclei of two molecules comes into play. \n\nMagnitude of the repulsion rises very rapidly as the distance separating the molecules decreases. This is the reason that liquids and solids are hard to compress\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 139; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/50606q.png'),
(50607,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion: On cooling, ammonia liquefies whereas carbon dioxide requires more cooling\n\nReason: Critical temperature of ammonia and carbon dioxide are 405K and 304K respectively','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Ammonia reaches it critical temperature first and therefore below that it can liquefy while as carbon dioxide is yet to reach that\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/50607q.png'),
(50608,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion : All the gases should be cooled below their critical temperature for liquefaction\n\nReason : Cooling slows down the movement of molecules therefore intermolecular forces may hold the slowly moving molecules together and the gas liquefies','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Gases can be liquefied below their critical temperature and not at a temperature higher than it\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Paragraph No.2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/50608q.png'),
(50609,'chemistry','Upto what temperature and pressure does carbon dioxide remains as a gas','30.98°C and 70 atmospheric pressure','At 30.98°C carbon dioxide remains gas upto 73 atmospheric pressure.  \n\nAt 73 atmospheric pressure, liquid carbon dioxide appears for the first time. The temperature 30.98°C is called critical temperature of carbon dioxide\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 152; Paragraph No.3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'2','mcq','37.98°C and 730 atmospheric pressure','37.98°C and 73 atmospheric pressure','30.98°C and 70 atmospheric pressure','25.98°C and 1 atmospheric pressure','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50610,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\n\na.It is possible to change a gas into liquid or a liquid into gas by a process in which always a single phase is present.\n\nb.The term fluid is used for either a liquid or a gas to recognise this continuity. Thus a liquid can be viewed as a very dense gas.','Both a and b','It is possible to change a gas into liquid or a liquid into gas by a process in which always a single phase is present.\n\nThe term fluid is used for either a liquid or a gas to recognise this continuity. Thus a liquid can be viewed as a very dense gas\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Paragraph No.3 and 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Both a and b','None of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50611,'chemistry','Critical temperature for H₂ is','33.2K','Critical temperature for H₂ is 33.2K\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Table No. 5.4 )','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','25.2K','33.2K','273K','300K','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50612,'chemistry','Critical pressure for H₂ is','12.97 bar','Critical pressure for H₂ is 12.97 bar\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Table No. 5.4 )','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','12.97 bar','100 bar','1 bar','5 bar','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50613,'chemistry','Critical volume for H₂ is','0.065 dm³ mol⁻¹','Critical volume for H₂ is 0.065 dm³ mol⁻¹\n\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Table No. 5.4 )','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','0.650 dm³ mol⁻¹','6.50dm³ mol⁻¹','650 dm³ mol⁻¹','0.065 dm³ mol⁻¹','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/50613q.png'),
(50614,'chemistry','Critical temperature for He is','5.3K','Critical temperature for He is 5.3K\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Table No. 5.4 )','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','4K','25K','5.3K','274K','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50615,'chemistry','Critical pressure for He is','2.29 bar','Critical pressure for He is 2.29 bar\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Table No. 5.4 )','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquefaction of gases and Critical parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','4 bar','2.29 bar','3 bar','1 bar','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50701,'chemistry','Which of the following is correct regarding viscosity?','It is the internal resistance of a liquid to flow','Viscosity is the internal resistance offered by an adjacent layer when a layer starts to flow.\nIt decreases with increase in temperature. \n\nCoefficient of viscosity is denoted by η.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 156; Paragraph No. 5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquid state',NULL,'1','mcq','It increases with increase in temperature of the liquid','It is the internal resistance of a liquid to flow','Coefficient of viscosity is not represented by ‘η’','All of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50702,'chemistry','S.I unit of surface tension is','Nm⁻¹','Surface tension is defined as the force acting per unit length perpendicular to the line drawn on the surface of liquid. \n\nIt is denoted by Greek letter γ (Gamma). \n\nIt has dimensions of kg s–2 and in SI unit it is expressed as N m–1\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 156; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquid state',NULL,'1','mcq','Nm⁻¹','dynecm²','Nm','Nm²','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50703,'chemistry','At 1 atm pressure boiling temperature is called ____ boiling point','normal','At 1 atm pressure boiling temperature is called normal boiling point.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Paragraph No. 5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquid state',NULL,'1','mcq','normal','standard','constant','None of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50704,'chemistry','If pressure is 1 bar then the boiling point is called ___ boiling point','standard','If pressure is 1 bar then the boiling point is called standard boiling point.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Paragraph No. 5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquid state',NULL,'1','mcq','normal','constant','standard','All of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/50704q.png'),
(50705,'chemistry','At high altitudes atmospheric pressure is A. Therefore liquids at high altitudes boil at B temperatures. Match A and B','A-low, B-lower','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 155; Paragraph No.2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquid state',NULL,'3','mcq','A-low, B-lower','A-low, B-higher','A-high, B-lower','A-high, B-higher','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50706,'chemistry','The point at which density of a substance in gaseous and  liquid state are same is  called','Critical point','The point at which density of a substance in gaseous and liquid state are same is called Critical point.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 155; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquid state',NULL,'1','mcq','Critical point','Isotonic point','Isoelectric point','Ideal point','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50707,'chemistry','A liquid rise (or fall) in a thin capillary as soon as the capillary touches the surface of the liquid because of _________','Surface tension','Liquid tends to rise (or fall) in the capillary because of surface tension. \n\nLiquids wet the things because they spread across their surfaces as thin film. Moist soil grains are pulled together because surface area of thin film of water is reduced. It is surface tension which gives stretching property to the surface of a liquid.\n\nOn flat surface, droplets are slightly flattened by the effect of gravity; but in the gravity free environments drops are perfectly spherical.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 156; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquid state',NULL,'1','mcq','Capillarity','Tensile strength','Surface tension','All of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50708,'chemistry','The type of flow in which there is a regular gradation of velocity in passing from one layer to the next is called ___ flow','laminar','The type of flow in which there is a regular gradation of velocity in passing from one layer to the next is called laminar flow.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 156; Paragraph No. 5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquid state',NULL,'1','mcq','regular','normal','turbulent','laminar','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50709,'chemistry','Which of the following is correct?\n\na. Greater the viscosity, the more slowly the liquid flows.\n\nb.1 poise = 1 g cm⁻¹s⁻¹','Both a and b','The greater the viscosity, the more slowly a liquid flows.\n\n1 poise = 1 g cm⁻¹s⁻¹\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 157; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquid state',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Both a and b','None of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50710,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion : The temperature at which vapour pressure of a liquid is equal to the external pressure is called boiling temperature\n\nReason : At high altitude atmospheric pressure is high','Assertion is true but Reason is false','The temperature at which vapour pressure of a liquid is equal to the external pressure is called boiling temperature.\n\nAt high altitudes atmospheric pressure is low.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Paragraph No.5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquid state',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/50710q.png'),
(50711,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion: Liquids tend to have maximum number of molecules at their surface.\n\nReason :  Small liquid drops have spherical shape.','Assertion is false but Reason is true','Liquids tend to have minimum number of molecules at their surface. Small liquid drops have spherical shape.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 155; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquid state',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50712,'chemistry','Which of the following doesn\'t decrease with rise in temperature?','Vapour pressure','Vapour pressure increases with rise in temperature.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 155; Paragraph No.3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquid state',NULL,'1','mcq','Density','Surface tension','Vapour pressure','Viscosity','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50713,'chemistry','Which of these statements are/are true.\n\na) Pressure is exerted by the gas as a result of collision of the particles with the walls of the container.\n\nb) Collisions of gas molecules are perfectly inelastic.','a only','Pressure is exerted by the gas as a result of collision of the particles with the walls of the container.\n\nCollisions of gas molecules are perfectly inelastic.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 149; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquid state',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','Both a and b','All of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50714,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion: At high altitudes, liquid boils at lower temperature in comparison to that at sea level\n\nReason: At higher altitudes, atmospheric pressure is low','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','At high altitudes atmospheric pressure is low. \n\nTherefore liquids at high altitudes boil at lower temperatures in comparison to that at sea level.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 155; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquid state',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50715,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion : The normal boiling point of water is 100 degree celsius and standard boiling point is 99.6 degree celsius\n\nReason : The temperature at which vapour pressure of liquid is equal to atmospheric pressure is calling boiling point at that pressure','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','1 bar pressure is slightly less than 1 atm pressure. \n\nThe normal boiling point of water is 100 °C (373 K), its standard boiling point is 99.6 °C (372.6 K)\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 155; Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquid state',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50716,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion : Viscosity of liquid decreases as the temperature rises\n\nReason : At high temperature, molecules have higher kinetic energy and can overcome the intermolecular forces to flow faster','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Viscosity of liquids decreases as the temperature rises because at high temperature molecules have high kinetic energy and can overcome the intermolecular forces to slip past one another between the layers.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 157; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquid state',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50717,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion: Windowpanes of old buildings become thicker at the bottom\n\nReason: Glass is an extremely viscous liquid','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Glass being viscous flows down at the bottom.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 157; Paragraph No.2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Liquid state',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50718,'biology','The function of the outer covering of endospermis is to','separate the embryo','The outer covering of endosperm separates the embryo by a proteinous layer called aleurone layer\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 77; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> The Seed',NULL,'1','mcq','provide nutrition','store protein','stimulate growth factors','separate the embryo','NEW','11-077',NULL,NULL),
(50801,'chemistry','Dipole-induced dipole interactions are present in which of the following pairs?','HCl and He atoms','HCl is polar (m ≠ 0) and He is non-polar (m = 0) gives dipole-induced dipole interactions\n(NEET 2013)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Cl₂ and CCl₄','HCl and He atoms','Cl₂ and CCl4','H₂O and alcohol','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50802,'chemistry','Which one of the following is the correct order of interactions?','covalent > dipole-dipole > hydrogen bonding > van der Waal','The strength of interaction follows the order: van der Waals’ < hydrogen-bonding < dipole-dipole < covalent. \n\nIt is so because bond length of H-bond is larger than that of a covalent bond. And also covalent bond is strongest because, the greater the extent of overlapping, the stronger is the bond formed.\n\n(NEET 1993)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','order','1,covalent','2,dipole-dipole','3,hydrogen bonding','4,van der Waal','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50803,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are wrong for gases?','Mass of a gas cannot be determined by weighing a container in which it is enclosed','Mass of the gas = Mass of the cylinder including gas – Mass of empty cylinder. \n\nSo, mass of a gas can be determined by weighing the container in which it is enclosed.\n\n(NEET 1999)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Confined gas exerts uniform pressure on the walls of its container in all directions','Volume of the gas is equal to volume of container confining the gas','Gases do not have a definite shape and volume','Mass of a gas cannot be determined by weighing a container in which it is enclosed','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50804,'chemistry','At 25°C and 730 mm pressure, 380 mL of dry oxygen was collected. If the temperature is constant, what volume will the oxygen occupy at 760 mm pressure?','365 mL','V₁ = 380 mL, P₁ = 730 mm, V₂ = ?, P₂ = 760 mm. \n\nFrom Boyle’s law, P₁V₁ = P₂V₂ \n\nTherefore V2 = 365 ml\n(NEET 1999)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','365 mL','569 mL','265 mL','621 mL','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50805,'chemistry','Pressure remaining the same, the volume of a given mass of an ideal gas increases for every degree centigrade rise in temperature by definite fraction of its volume at','0 °C','According to Charles’ law which states that “The volume of the given mass of a gas increases or decreases by 1÷273 of its volume at 0°C for each degree rise or fall of temperature at constant pressure.”\n(NEET 1989)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','0 °C','its critical temperature','absolute zero','its Boyle temperature','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50806,'chemistry','A mixture of N₂ and Ar gases in a cylinder contains 7 g of N₂ and 8 g of Ar. If the total pressure of the mixture of the gases in the cylinder is 27 bar, the partial pressure of N₂ is [Use atomic masses (in g mol–1): N = 14, Ar = 40','15 bar','Number of moles of N₂ = 0.2.5 moles\n\nNumber of moles of Ar = 0 2. mol \n\nMole fraction of N₂ = 0.55 \n\nPartial pressure of N₂ gas = mole fraction × total pressure\n(NEET 2020)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','12 bar','15 bar','150 bar','11 bar','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50807,'chemistry','The volume occupied by 1.8 g of water vapour at 374°C and 1 bar pressure will be [Use R = 0.083 bar L K–1mol–1]','5.37 L','Using PV = nRT, we get that V= 5.37 L\n\n(Odisha NEET 2019)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','96.66 L','55.87 L','3.10 L','5.37 L','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/50807q.png'),
(50808,'chemistry','Equal moles of hydrogen and oxygen gases are placed in a container with a pin-hole through which both can escape. What fraction of the oxygen escapes in the time required for one-half of the hydrogen to escape?','1/8','1/8 is the correct answer\n\n(NEET-1 2016)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','3/8','1/8','1/2','1/4','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/50808q.png'),
(50809,'chemistry','What is the density of N₂ gas at 227°C and 5.00 atm pressure? (R = 0.082 L atm K⁻¹mol⁻¹)','3.41 g/mL','Using P = dRT/M (M=Molar mass), we get d = 3.41 g/mL\n\n(Karnataka NEET 2013)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1.40 g/mL','3.41 g/mL','2.81 g/mL','0.29 g/mL','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/50809q.png'),
(50810,'chemistry','A certain gas takes three times as long to effuse out as helium. Its molecular mass will be','36 u','Using graham\'s law of effusion, rate of effusion = square root of 1÷Molecular mass\n\nTherefore taking ratio of two rates, we get molecular mass should be 36 u\n(NEET 2012)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','9 u','36 u','27 u','64 u','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/50810q.png'),
(50811,'chemistry','Two gases A and B having the same volume diffuse through a porous partition in 20 and 10 seconds respectively. The molecular mass of A is 49 u. Molecular mass of B will be','12.25 u','Using graham\'s law of effusion, rate of effusion = square root of 1÷Molecular mass\n\nTherefore taking ratio of two rates, we get molecular mass should be 12.25 u\n\n(NEET 2011)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','12.25 u','25.00 u','50.00 u','6.50 u','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50812,'chemistry','A gaseous mixture was prepared by taking equal moles of CO and N₂. If the total pressure of the mixture was found 1 atmosphere, the partial pressure of the nitrogen (N₂) in the mixture is','0.5 atm','pCO + pN₂ = 1 atm  [Moles of CO = moles of N₂ )atm\n\n2pN₂ = 1 atm\n\nSo pN₂ = 0.5 atm\n\n(NEET 2011)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','0.5 atm','0.8 atm','0.9 atm','1 atm','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50813,'chemistry','The diagram above (n515) represents the molecular speed distribution curve of chlorine and nitrogen at','Volume will become greater by a factor of 1.6',': From ideal gas equation, V ∝ T/P\n\nGiven T₁ = 15 + 273 = 288 K, P₁ = 1.5 bar\n\nT₂ = 25 + 273 = 298 K, P₂ = 1 bar\n\nTherefore after taking ratio of the two, we get that volume will become greater by a factor of 1.6\n\n(NEET 2011)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Volume will become greater by a factor of 1.6','Volume will become greater by a factor of 1.1','Volume will become smaller by a factor of 0.70','Volume will become greater by a factor of 2.5','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/50813q.png'),
(50814,'chemistry','The pressure exerted by 6.0 g of methane gas in a 0.03 m3 vessel at 129°C is (Atomic masses: C = 12.01, H = 1.01 and R = 8.314 J K–1 mol–1)','41648 Pa','Using PV = nRT and n = given mass/ molecular mass, we get that P = 41648 Pa\n\n(NEET 2010)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','215216 Pa','13409 Pa','41648 Pa','31684 Pa','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50815,'chemistry','Which of the following mixtures of gases does not obey Dalton’s law of partial pressure?','Cl₂ and SO₂','Cl₂ + SO₂ (sunlight )→  SO₂Cl₂ (Sulphuryl chloride)\n\nDalton’s law of partial pressure is applicable only in those cases where gases are non-reacting.  \n\nAs Cl₂ and SO₂ reacts  to form SO₂Cl₂ so this law is not obeyed in given case.\n\n(NEET 1996)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Cl₂ and SO₂','CO₂ and He','O₂ and CO₂','N₂ and O₂','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50816,'chemistry','At what temperature, the rate of effusion of N₂ would be 1.625 times than the rate of SO₂ at 50°C?','100°C','Rate of effusion ∝ √T/M\n\nTherefore taking ratios of two rates, we get that the answer is 100°C\n\n(NEET 1996)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','373°C','620°C','100°C','173°C','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50817,'chemistry','50 mL of hydrogen diffuses out through a small hole of a vessel, in 20 minutes. The time taken by 40 mL of oxygen to diffuse out is','64 minutes','At STP conditions used earlier (0 °C and 1 atm pressure), value of R is 8.20578 × 10⁻² L atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ \n\n(NEET 1992)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','32 minutes','64 minutes','8 minutes','12 minutes','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50818,'chemistry','Under what conditions will a pure sample of an ideal gas not only exhibit a pressure of 1 atm but also a concentration of 1 mole litre–1? (R = 0.082 litre atm mol–1 deg–1)','When T = 12 K','Using PV = nRT, we get that 12K  an ideal gas not only exhibit a pressure of 1 atm but also a concentration of 1 mole litre–1\n\n(NEET 1993)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','At STP','When V = 22.4 litres','When T = 12 K','Impossible under any conditions','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50819,'chemistry','The correct value of the gas constant ‘R’ is close to','0.082 litre-atmosphere K⁻¹ mol⁻¹','The gas constant is denoted by the symbol R. \n\nIt is equivalent to the Boltzmann constant, but expressed in units of energy per temperature increment per mole.\n\nThe value of gas constant R is 8.314 J / mol·K or 0.08205 Litre - atmosphere K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ \n\n(NEET 1992)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','0.082 litre-atmosphere K','0.082 litre-atmosphere K⁻¹ mol⁻¹','0.082 litre-atmosphere m⁻¹ mol⁻¹','0.082 litre⁻¹ atmosphere⁻¹ K mol','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50820,'chemistry','Select one correct statement. In the gas equation, PV = nRT','n moles of the gas have a volume V','In ideal gas equation, PV = nRT\n\nn moles of the gas have volume V\n\n(NEET 1992)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','n is the number of molecules of a gas','V denotes volume of one mole of the gas','n moles of the gas have a volume V','P is the pressure of the gas when only one mole of gas is present','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50821,'chemistry','At constant temperature, in a given mass of an ideal gas','the product of pressure and volume always remains constant','According to Boyle’s law at constant  temperature, P ∝  1÷V or PV = constant\n\n(NEET 1991)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','the ratio of pressure and volume always remains constant','volume always remains constant','pressure always remains constant','the product of pressure and volume always remains constant','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50822,'chemistry','If P, V, M, T and R are pressure, volume, molar mass, temperature and gas constant respectively, then for an ideal gas, the density is given by','PM/RT','Using PV=nRT, we can get density as well\n(NEET 1989)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','RT/PM','P/RT','M/V','PM/RT','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50823,'chemistry','Correct gas equation is','P₁V₁/P₂V₂ = T₁/T₂','P₁V₁/P₂V₂ = T₁/T₂ is the correct expression for combined gas law\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 145; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','V₁P₂=TV₂','P₁V₁/P₂V₂ = T₁/T₂','P₁T₁/V₁ = P₂T₂/V₂','P1T1/P₂T₂ = V1V₂','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50824,'chemistry','By what factor does the average velocity of a gaseous molecule increase when the temperature (in Kelvin) is doubled?','1.4','Average velocity is directly proportional to square root of temperature in kelvin, therefore velocity increases by 1.4 times when temperature is doubled\n\n(NEET 2011)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','2','2.8','4','1.4','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50825,'chemistry','The temperature of a gas is raised from 27°C to 927°C. The root mean square speed of the gas','gets doubled','Root mean square speed is directly proportional to √T, therefore after taking ratio of the two temperatures (in kelvin), we get that velocity gets doubled\n(NEET 1994)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','remains same','gets halved','gets doubled','√927/27','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50826,'chemistry','The ratio among most probable velocity, mean velocity and root mean square velocity is given by','√2 : √8 / π : √3','The ratio among most probable velocity, mean velocity and root mean square velocity is given by √2 : √8 / π : √3\n\n(NEET 1994)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1 : 2 : 3','3 : 2 : 1','√2 : √8 / π : √3','V2 : √3 : √8 / π','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50827,'chemistry','The root mean square velocity at STP for the gases H₂, N₂, O₂ and HBr are in the order','H₂ > N₂ > O₂ > HBr','Root mean square velocity is inversely proportional to the square root of molecular mass\n\n(NEET 1991)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','order','1,H₂','2,N₂','3,O₂','4,HBr','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50828,'chemistry','Root mean square velocity of a gas molecule is proportional to','m⁻¹/²','Root mean square velocity of a gas molecule is proportional to m⁻¹/²\n\n(NEET 1990)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','m⁻¹/²','m⁰','m¹/²','m','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50829,'chemistry','The energy absorbed by each molecule (A2) of a substance is 4.4 × 10⁻¹⁹ J and bond energy per molecule is 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. The kinetic energy of the molecule per atom will be','2 × 10⁻²⁰ J',': Energy absorbed by each molecule \n= 4.4 × 10⁻¹⁹J\n\nEnergy required to break the bond = 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J\n\nRemaining energy to get converted to kinetic energy\n= (4.4 × 10⁻¹⁹ – 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁹) J = 0.4 × 10⁻¹⁹ J per molecule\n\n\\ Kinetic energy per atom = 0.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ J or 2 × 10⁻²⁰  J\n(NEET 2009)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ J','2× 10⁻¹⁹J','2 × 10⁻²⁰ J','4 × 10⁻²⁰ J','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50830,'chemistry','If a gas expands at constant temperature, it indicates that','kinetic energy of molecules remains the same','At constant temperature, K.E. of molecules remains the same.\n\n(NEET (2008)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','kinetic energy of molecules remains the same','number of the molecules of gas increases','kinetic energy of molecules decreases','pressure of the gas increases.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50831,'chemistry','Average molar kinetic energy of CO and N₂ at same temperature is','KE₁ = KE₂','As temperatures are same and KE is independent of molecular mass, so KE₁ = KEv.\n\n(NEET 2000)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','KE₁ = KE₂','KEv > KEv','KE₁ < KE₂','Can\'t say anything. Both volumes are not given','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50832,'chemistry','The average kinetic energy of an ideal gas, per molecule in S.I. units, at 25°C will be','6.17 × 10⁻²¹ J',': Temperature (T) = 25°C = 298 K.\n\nTherefore, K.E. per molecule 3RT/Na = 3 x 8.314 x 298/2 x (6x10²³) = 6.17 × 10⁻²¹J\n\n(NEET 1996)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','6.17 × 10⁻²⁰ J','7.16 × 10⁻²⁰ J','61.7 × 10⁻²⁰ J','6.17 × 10⁻²¹ J','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50833,'chemistry','At STP, 0.50 mol H₂ gas and 1.0 mol He gas','have equal average kinetic energies','This is because average kinetic energy depends only on temperature\n\n(NEET 1993)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','have equal average kinetic energies','have equal molecular speeds','occupy equal volumes','have equal effusion rates','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50834,'chemistry','Internal energy and pressure of a gas per unit volume are related as','P = 2÷3 E','P×1=RT\n\nAlso, internal energy E= 2÷3 RT\n\nThus; E= 2÷3P or P= 3÷2 E\n\n(NEET 1993)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','P = 2÷3 E','P = 3÷2 E','P = 1÷2 E','P = 2E','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50835,'chemistry','A closed flask contains water in all its three states solid, liquid and vapour at 0°C. In this situation, the average kinetic energy of water molecules will be','the greatest in vapour state','Velocity and hence average K.E. of water molecules is maximum in the gaseous state.\n\n(NEET 1992)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','the greatest in all the three states','the greatest in vapour state','the greatest in the liquid state','the greatest in the solid state','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50836,'chemistry','Which is not true in case of an ideal gas?','All molecules of the gas move with same speed','Molecules in an ideal gas move with different speeds. Due to collision between the particles their speed changes\n\n(NEET 1992)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','It cannot be converted into a liquid','There is no interaction between the molecules','All molecules of the gas move with same speed','At a given temperature, PV is proportional to the amount of the gas','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50837,'chemistry','A gas at 350 K and 15 bar has molar volume 20 percent smaller than that for an ideal gas under the same conditions. The correct option about the gas and its compressibility factor (Z) is','Z < 1 and attractive forces are dominant','VᵢDₑₐₗ = V, Vᵣₑₐₗ = V – 0.2 V = 0.8 V\n\nZ = Vᵣₑₐₗ/VᵢDₑₐₗ = 0.8\n\nif value of Z < 1 then attractive forces are dominant.\n\n(NEET 2019)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Z < 1 and repulsive forces are dominant','Z > 1 and attractive forces are dominant','Z > 1 and repulsive forces are dominant','Z < 1 and attractive forces are dominant','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50838,'chemistry','A gas such as carbon monoxide would be most likely to obey the ideal gas law at','high temperatures and low pressures','Real gases show ideal gas behaviour at high temperatures and low pressures\n\n(NEET 2015)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','low temperatures and high pressures','high temperatures and high pressures','low temperatures and low pressures','high temperatures and low pressures','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50839,'chemistry','Maximum deviation from ideal gas is expected from','NH₃(g)','NH₃ is a polar molecule, thus more attractive forces between NH₃ molecules\n\n(NEET 2013)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CH₄(g)','NH₃(g)','H₂(g)','N₂(g)','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50840,'chemistry','van der Waals’ real gas, acts as an ideal gas, at which conditions?','High temperature, low pressure','At low pressure and high temperature van der Waals real gas acts as ideal gas and observed to obey PV = nRT relation\n\n(NEET 2002)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','High temperature, low pressure','Low temperature, high pressure','High temperature, high pressure','Low temperature, low pressure','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50841,'chemistry','When is deviation more in the behaviour of a gas from the ideal gas equation PV = nRT?','Low temperature, high pressure','At low temperature and high pressure, there is a deviation from the ideal behaviour in gases\n\n(NEET 1993)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','High temperature, low pressure','Low temperature, high pressure','High temperature, high pressure','Low temperature, low pressure','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50842,'chemistry','A gas is said to behave like an ideal gas when the relation PV/T = constant. When do you expect a real gas to behave like an ideal gas?','When the temperature is high and pressure is low','At high temperature and low pressure the effect of a/V2 and b is negligible. \n\nTherefore Z = 1\n\n(NEET 1991)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','When the temperature is low','When both the temperature and pressure are low','When both the temperature and pressure are high','When the temperature is high and pressure is low','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50843,'chemistry','In van der Waals’ equation of state for a non-ideal gas, the term that accounts for intermolecular forces is','(P + a/V²)','van der Waals’ equation for 1 mole is (P + a/V²) x (V – b) = RT\n\nHere (P + a/V²) represents the intermolecular forces and (V – b) is the correct volume\n\n(NEET 1990)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(V – b)','(RT)²','RT','(P + a/V²)','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50844,'chemistry','Given van der Waals’ constant for NH₃, H₂, O₂ and CO₂ are respectively 4.17, 0.244, 1.36 and 3.59, which one of the following gases is most easily liquefied?','NH₃','van der Waals’ constant ‘a’ signifies the intermolecular forces of attraction between the particle  of gas. \n\nSo, higher the value of ‘a’, easier will be the  liquefaction of gas.\n\n(NEET 2018)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','NH₃','H₂','O₂','CO₂','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50845,'chemistry','An ideal gas, obeying kinetic theory of gases cannot be liquefied, because','forces acting between its molecules are negligible','A gas can only be liquefied, if some forces  of attraction are acting in its molecules. \n\nAccording to kinetic theory, an ideal gas is devoid of force of attraction in its molecules, therefore it cannot be liquefied.\n\n(NEET 1995)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','it solidifies before becoming a liquid','forces acting between its molecules are negligible','its critical temperature is above 0°C','its molecules are relatively small in size','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50846,'chemistry','The beans are cooked earlier in pressure cooker because','boiling point increases with increasing pressure','More is the pressure, greater will be the boiling point.\n\n(NEET 2011)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','boiling point increases with increasing pressure','boiling point decreases with increasing pressure','extra pressure of pressure cooker softens the beans','internal energy is not lost while cooking in pressure cooker','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50847,'chemistry','Which one among the following is the correct option for right relationship between CP and CV for one mole of ideal gas ?','CP–CV=R','PV=nRT. \n\nFrom this equation we can see that at constant temperature and pressure n-moles of any gas will have the same volume because n,R,T and p are constant.\n\n(NEET 2021)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CP+CV=R','CP–CV=R','CP = RCV','CV=RCP','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50848,'chemistry','Choose the correct option for the total pressure (in atm.) in a mixture of 4g O₂ and 2g H₂ confined in a total volume of one litre at 0°C is: [Given R = 0.082 L atm mol–¹ᴷ–¹, T=273 K]','25.18','From the ideal gas equation,  PV = nRT \n\nPₜₒₜₐₗ × 1 = 9 8 × 0.082 × 273 \n\nPₜₒₜₐₗ  = 25.18 atm\n\n(NEET 2021)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2.518','2.602','25.18','26.02','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50849,'chemistry','Choose the correct option for graphical representation of Boyle\'s law, which shows a graph of pressure vs volume of a gas at different temperatures: \nRefer diagram(n512)','4','According to Boyle\'s law, pressure of a gas is inversely proportional to volume.\n\nAt a given pressure, volume is directly proportional to temperature.\n\n(NEET 2021)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50850,'chemistry','Which one is not correct mathematical equation for Dalton’s Law of partial pressure? Here p = total pressure\n\nwhere pi = partial pressure of iᵗʰ gas\n\n𝞆ᵢ = mole fraction of iᵗʰ gas in gaseous mixture \n\nwhere 𝞆ᵢ = mole fraction of iᵗʰ gas in gaseous mixture\n\np i ⁰= pressure of iᵗʰ gas in pure state','𝛒ᵢ = 𝞆ᵢ𝛒ᵢᴼ','Dalton’s law of partial pressure states that the total pressure by the mixture of non-reactive gases is equal to the sum of the partial pressures of individual gases.\n\n• pTotal = p1 + p2 + p3\n\n• Also, pi = 𝞆ᵢ p; where pi and 𝞆ᵢ i are the partial pressure and mole fraction of the gas respectively and p is the total pressure.\n\n• pTotal = p1 + p2 + p3\n\n(NEET 2022)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','𝛒ᵢ = p1 + p2 + p3','𝛒ᵢ=n1 RT/V+n2 RT/V+n3 RT/V','𝛒ᵢ = According to Boyle\'s law','𝛒ᵢ = 𝞆ᵢ𝛒ᵢᴼ','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50851,'chemistry','A 10.0 L flask contains 64 g of oxygen at 27ºC. (Assume O₂ gas is behaving ideally). \nThe pressure inside the flask in bar is (Given R = 0.0831 L bar K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)','4.9','1 mole of oxygen = 32g\n\ntherefore, 2mols = 64g\n\nWhen we input the given parameters into the ideal gas equation expression PV= nRT, \n\nThe correct value of pressure is 4.9\n\n(NEET 2022)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2.5','498.6','49.8','4.9','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50852,'chemistry','Which one of the following is the correct order of interactions?','van der Waals’ < dipole-dipole < hydrogen bonding < covalent','van der Waals’ < dipole-dipole < hydrogen bonding < covalent\n\n(NEET 1993)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','covalent < hydrogen bonding < van der Waals’ < dipole-dipole','van der Waals’ < hydrogen bonding < dipoledipole < covalent','van der Waals’ < dipole-dipole < hydrogen bonding < covalent','dipole-dipole < van der Waals’ < hydrogen bonding < covalent','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50853,'chemistry','An ideal gas, obeying kinetic theory of gases cannot be liquefied, because','forces acting between its molecules are negligible','forces acting between molecules of the ideal gas are negligible\n\n(NEET 1995)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','it solidifies before becoming a liquid','forces acting between its molecules are negligible','its critical temperature is above 0°C','its molecules are relatively small in size','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50854,'chemistry','In van der Waals’ equation of state for a non-ideal gas, the term that accounts for intermolecular forces is','❴p+a/V²❵','The term that account for molecular forces in the equation is ❴p+a/V²❵\n\n(NEET 1990)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(V – b)','(RT)–1','❴p+a/V²❵','RT','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50901,'chemistry','Which of this statements are correct.\n\na) Individual molecules of a liquid substance do not boil but the bulk of it boils. \n\nb) Water can exist as ice, which is a solid; it can exist as liquid; or it can exist in the gaseous state as water vapour or steam.\n\nc) Characteristics of the three states of water depends on the energy of the molecules and on the manner in which the molecules aggregates.','all of these','Individual molecules of a liquid substance  does not boil but the bulk of it boils. \n\nWater can exist as ice, which is a solid; it can exist as liquid; or it can exist in the gaseous state as water vapour or steam. \n\nCharacteristics of the three states of water depends on the energy of the molecules and on the manner in which the molecules aggregates.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 136; Paragraph No. 1 and 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a and b only','a and c only','b and c only','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50902,'chemistry','Which of this statement is true.\n\na) Ion-dipole forces are van der Waals forces. \n\nb) Atoms and nonpolar molecules are electrically symmetrical and have no dipole moments.','b only','Ion-dipole forces are not van der Waals forces. \n\nAtoms and nonpolar molecules are electrically symmetrical and have no dipole moments.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 137; Paragraph No. 3 and 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50903,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason.\n\nAssertion: In molecules that possess dipole-induced dipole forces, an induced dipole is developed in one of the molecules.\n\nReason: Permanent dipole of the polar molecule induces dipole on the electrically neutral molecule by deforming its electronic cloud.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Permanent dipole of the polar molecule induces dipole on the electrically neutral molecule by deforming its electronic cloud.\n\nThus an induced dipole is developed in the other molecule.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 138; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50904,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason.\n\nAssertion: The interaction between polar molecules with its neighbouring molecules is stronger than the london forces.\n\nReason: This is because only partial charges are involved.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The polar molecules interact with neighbouring molecules. \n\nThis interaction is stronger than the London forces but is weaker than ion-ion interaction because only partial charges are involved.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 138; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50905,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason.\n\nAssertion: The attractive forces between polar molecules decreases with the increase of distance between the dipoles.\n\nReason: The interaction energy is inversely proportional to distance between polar molecules.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The polar molecules interact with neighbouring molecules. \n\nThis interaction is stronger than the London forces but is weaker than ion-ion interaction because only partial charges are involved.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 138; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50906,'chemistry','A particularly particularly strong type of dipole-dipole interaction is;','hydrogen bonding','A particularly strong type of dipole-dipole interaction is hydrogen bonding.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 137; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','intermolecular bonding','hydrogen bonding','polar bonding','london force','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50907,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason.\n\nAssertion: Temporary dipoles are induced in molecules.\n\nReason: Force of attraction between two temporary dipoles is known as London force.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The London dispersion force is a temporary attractive force that results when the electrons in two adjacent atoms occupy positions that make the atoms form temporary dipoles.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 137; Paragraph No. 5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50908,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true.\n\na) When molecular interactions are very weak, molecules do not cling together to make liquid or solid.\n\nb) Strength of the hydrogen bond is determined by the coulombic interaction between the lone-pair electrons of the electronegative atom of one molecule and the hydrogen atom of other molecule.','both a and b','When molecular interactions are very weak, molecules do not cling together to make liquid or solid.\n\nStrength of the hydrogen bond is determined by the coulombic interaction between the lone-pair electrons of the electronegative atom of one molecule and the hydrogen atom of other molecule.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 137; Paragraph No. 1 and 5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50909,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true.\n\na) When molecules are already in close contact; they resist further compression\n\nb) When  molecules resist further compression, their repulsive interactions decreases','a only','When molecules are already in close contact; they resist further compression; as that would result in the increase of repulsive interactions.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 139; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50910,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason.\n\nAssertion: Thermal energy is responsible for movement of particles.\n\nReason: It is the measure of average kinetic energy of the particles of the matter.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Thermal energy is the measure of average kinetic energy of the particles of the matter and is thus responsible for movement of particles.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 139; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50911,'biology','Tricarpellary, syncarpous gynoecium is found in flowers of [NEET2016]','Liliaceae','Members of Liliaceae possess tricarpellary, syncarpous gynoecium with superior ovary. The ovary is trilocular with two to many ovules in each loculus.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 81; Paragraph No. 1-2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Fabaceae','Poaceae','Liliaceae','Solanaceae','NEW','11-081',NULL,NULL),
(50912,'chemistry','Which of these statements is/are true.\n\na) When molecular interactions are very weak, molecules do not cling together to make liquid or solid.\n\nb) Strength of the hydrogen bond is determined by the coulombic interaction between the lone-pair electrons of the electronegative atom of one molecule and the hydrogen atom of other molecule.','both a and b','At constant temperature, product of pressure and volume of a fixed amount of gas is constant.\n\nThe value of constant k for Boyle\'s law depends upon the amount of the gas, temperature of the gas and the units in which p and V are expressed.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 140; Paragraph No. 4 and 5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50913,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true.\n\na) The value of constant k for Charles law is determined by the pressure of the gas, its amount and the units in which volume V is expressed.\n\nb) Charles found that for all gases, at any given pressure, graph of volume vs temperature (in celsius) is a straight line and on extending to zero volume, each line intercepts the temperature axis at – 273.15 °C.','both a and b','The value of constant k for Charles law is determined by the pressure of the gas, its amount and the units in which volume V is expressed. \n\nCharles found that for all gases, at any given pressure, graph of volume vs temperature (in celsius) is a straight line and on extending to zero volume, each line intercepts the temperature axis at – 273.15 °C.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 143; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50914,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true.\n\na) The mathematical relationship between pressure and temperature was given by Robert Boyle\n\nb) All gases obey Charles’ law at very low pressures and high temperatures','b only','The mathematical relationship between pressure and temperature was given by Joseph Gay Lussac.\n\nAll gases obey Charles’ law at very low pressures and high temperatures.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 143; Paragraph No. 2 and 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50915,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true.\n\na) Since volume of a gas is directly proportional to the number of moles; one mole of each gas at standard temperature and pressure (STP) will have same volume.\n\nb) As the temperature and pressure remain constant, the volume of a gas depends upon number of molecules of the gas.','both a and b','Since volume of a gas is directly proportional to the number of moles; one mole of each gas at standard temperature and pressure (STP) will have same volume. b) As the temperature and pressure remain constant, the volume of a gas depends upon number of molecules of the gas.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 144; Paragraph No. 1 and 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50916,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true.\n\na) The density of a gas is directly proportional to its molar mass.\n\nb) A gas that follows Boyle’s law, Charles’ law and Avogadro law strictly is called an ideal gas.\n\nc) It is assume that intermolecular forces are not present between the molecules of an ideal gas.','all of these','A gas that follows Boyle’s law, Charles’ law and Avogadro law strictly is called an ideal gas. Such a gas is hypothetical. \n\nIt is assumed that intermolecular forces are not present between the molecules of an ideal gas.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 145; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','a and b only','a and c only','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50917,'chemistry','In a mixture of gases, the pressure exerted by the individual gas is called;','partial pressure','In a mixture of gases, the pressure exerted by the individual gas is called partial pressure.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 145; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','partial pressure','total pressure','atmospheric pressure','absolute pressure','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50918,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason.\n\nAssertion: We can obtain only average value of speed of gas molecules\n\nReason: The speed and energy of all gas molecules at any instance are not the same','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','When gas molecules are moving, they collide with each other and with the walls of the container. This results in change of their speed and redistribution of energy. So the speed and energy of all the molecules of the gas at any instant are not the same. Thus, we can obtain\nonly average value of speed of molecules.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 147; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50919,'chemistry','Which of this statements are true with respect to Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution of speeds curve.\n\na) The maximum in the curve represents speed possessed by maximum number of molecules.\n\nb) This maximum speed is called most probable speed, uₘₚ\n\nc) On increasing temperature, most probable speed decreases.','a and b only','In the Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution of speeds curve, the maximum in the curve represents speed possessed by maximum number of molecules. \n\nThis maximum speed is called most probable speed, uₘₚ . On increasing temperature, most probable speed increases.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 148; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a and b only','a and c only','b and c only','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50920,'chemistry','Which of this statements are true.\n\na) The mean square speed is the direct measure of the average kinetic energy of gas molecules.\n\nb) If we take the square root of the mean of the square of speeds then we get the  root mean square speed.','both a and b','The mean square speed is the direct measure of the average kinetic energy of gas molecules. \n\nIf we take the square root of the mean of the square of speeds then we get a value of speed which is different from most probable speed and average speed. This speed is called root mean square speed.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 148; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50921,'chemistry','Which of this statements are true.\n\na) If a molecule has variable speed, then it must have a constant kinetic energy.\n\nb) Kinetic theory of gases allows us to derive all the gas laws theoretically','b only','At constant temperature, product of pressure and volume of a fixed amount of gas is constant.\n\nThe value of constant k for Boyle\'s law depends upon the amount of the gas, temperature of the gas and the units in which p and V are expressed.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 140; Paragraph No. 4 and 5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50922,'chemistry','Which of this statements are true.\n\na) Real gases do not follow ideal gas equation perfectly under all conditions.\n\nb) At very high pressures, the measured volume is more than calculated volume.\n\nc) At constant temperature pV vs p plot for real gases is not a straight line.','a and c only','Real gases do not follow ideal gas equation perfectly under all conditions.\n\nAt very high pressures, the measured volume is less than calculated volume.\n\nAt constant temperature pV vs p plot for real gases is not a straight line.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 150; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','a and b only','a and c only','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50923,'chemistry','Real gases show ideal behaviour when conditions of temperature and pressure are such that the intermolecular forces are practically','negligible','Real gases show ideal behaviour when conditions of temperature and pressure are such that the intermolecular forces are practically negligible.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 151; Paragraph No. 6)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','high','low','adjusted','negligible','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50924,'chemistry','Which of this statements are true.\n\na) The behaviour of the gas becomes more ideal when pressure is very low.\n\nb) Upto what pressure a gas will follow the ideal gas law, depends upon nature of the gas and its temperature.','both a and b','The behaviour of the gas becomes more ideal when pressure is very low Upto what pressure a gas will follow the ideal gas law, depends upon nature of the gas and its temperature.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 152; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50925,'chemistry','First complete data on pressure-volume-temperature relations of a substance in both gaseous and liquid state was obtained by Thomas Andrews on _________.','Carbon dioxide','First complete data on pressure-volume-temperature relations of a substance in both gaseous and liquid state was obtained by Thomas Andrews on Carbon dioxide.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 152; Paragraph No. 5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Ammonia','Carbon dioxide','Water','Chlorine','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50926,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following.\n\nAssertion: Liquids have definite volume.\n\nReason: The molecules of a liquid do not separate from each other.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Liquids have definite volumes because molecules of a liquid do not separate from each other"\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50927,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason.\n\nAssertion: Liquids can flow.\n\nReason: The molecules of liquids can move past one another freely.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Liquids can flow. Reason is because the molecules of liquids can move past one another freely.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50928,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true.\n\na) When a liquid is heated in an open vessel, the liquid vapourises from the surface.\n\nb) The condition of free vapourisation throughout the liquid is called boiling.\n\nc) The temperature at which vapour pressure of liquid is equal to the external pressure is called boiling temperature at that pressure.','all of these','When a liquid is heated in an open vessel, the liquid vapourises from the surface.\n\nThe condition of free vapourisation throughout the liquid is called boiling.\n\nThe temperature at which vapour pressure of liquid is equal to the external pressure is called boiling temperature at that pressure.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','b only','b and c only','a and b only','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50929,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true.\n\na) A gas below the critical temperature can be liquified by applying pressure, and is called vapour of the substance.\n\nb) Under normal conditions liquids are denser than gases.','both a and b','A gas below the critical temperature can be liquified by applying pressure, and is called vapour of the substance.\n\nUnder normal conditions liquids are denser than gases.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Paragraph No. 1 and 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50930,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason.\n\nAssertion: Standard boiling point of the liquid is slightly lower than the normal boiling point.\n\nReason: 1 bar pressure is slightly less than 1 atm pressure.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Standard boiling point of the liquid is slightly lower than the normal boiling point because 1 bar pressure is slightly less than 1 atm pressure. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Paragraph No. 4 and Page No. 155; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50931,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason.\n\nAssertion: A molecule in the bulk of liquid does not experience any net force\n\nReason: The molecule experiences equal intermolecular forces from all sides','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','A molecule in the bulk of liquid experiences equal intermolecular forces from all sides. The molecule, therefore does not experience any net force.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 155; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50932,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason. \n\nAssertion: Liquids tend to have minimum number of molecules at their surface.\n\nReason: The molecules on the surface experience a net downward force and have more energy than the molecules in the bulk, which do not experience any net force.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The molecules on the surface experience a net downward force and have more energy than the molecules in the bulk, which do not\nexperience any net force. Therefore, liquids tend to have minimum number of molecules at their surface.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 156; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50933,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true.\n\na) The energy required to increase the surface area of the liquid by one unit is defined as surface energy.\n\nb) Liquid tends to rise (or fall) in the capillary because of surface tension.','both a and b','The energy required to increase the surface area of the liquid by one unit is defined as surface energy.\n\nLiquid tends to rise (or fall) in the capillary because of surface tension.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 156; Paragraph No. 1 and 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50934,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true?\n\na) The magnitude of surface tension of a liquid depends on the attractive forces between the molecules.\n\nb) Surface tension increases as the temperature is raised.','a only','The magnitude of surface tension of a liquid depends on the attractive forces between the molecules.\n\nSurface tension decreases as the temperature is raised.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 156; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50935,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true?\n\na) Greater the viscosity, the more slowly the liquid flows.\n\nb) Hydrogen bonding and van der Waals forces are strong enough to cause high viscosity.','both a and b','Greater the viscosity, the more slowly the liquid flows. \n\nHydrogen bonding and van der Waals forces are strong enough to cause high viscosity. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 157; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50936,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true?\n\na) Physical properties of ice, water and steam are very similar\n\nb) Intermolecular forces are the forces of attraction and repulsion between interacting particles\n\nc) Attractive intermolecular forces are known as van der Waals forces','b and c only','Physical properties of ice, water and steam are very different.  \n\nIntermolecular forces are the forces of attraction and repulsion between interacting particles.\n\nAttractive intermolecular forces are known as van der Waals forces\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 136; Paragraph No. 2 and Page No. 137; Paragraph No. 2 and 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a only','b only','a and b only','b and c only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50937,'biology','Which one of the following organisms is correctly matched with its three characteristics? [Mains 2012]','Onion: bulb, imbricate aestivation, axile placentation','Onion: bulb, imbricate aestivation, axile placentation\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 80; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Pea: C3 pathway, endospermic seed, vexillary aestivation','Tomato: twisted aestivation, axile placentation, berry','Onion: bulb, imbricate aestivation, axile placentation','Maize: C3 pathway, closed vascular bundles, scutellum','NEW','11-080',NULL,NULL),
(50938,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason. \n\nAssertion: Nature of intermolecular forces, molecular interactions and effect of thermal energy on the motion of particles determines the state of a substance.\n\nReason: This is because a balance between these three determines the state of a substance.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','It is necessary to understand the nature of intermolecular forces, molecular interactions and effect of thermal energy on the motion of particles because a balance between these determines the state of a substance.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 137; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(40508,'chemistry','Which scientist refined the Lewis postulations by abandoning the idea of the stationary cubical arrangement of the octet, and by introducing the term covalent bond.','Langmuir','Langmuir (1919) refined the Lewis postulations by abandoning the idea of the stationary cubical arrangement of the octet, and by introducing the term covalent bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Covalent bond',NULL,'1','mcq','Langmuir','Paul','Nyholm','Gillespie','NEW','11-102','11-102',NULL),
(50939,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true?\n\na) The attractive force decreases with the increase of distance between the dipoles.\n\nb) Interaction energy is inversely proportional to distance between polar molecules.\n\nc) Besides dipole- dipole interaction, polar molecules can interact by London forces also.','all of these','The attractive force decreases with the increase of distance between the dipoles.\n\nInteraction energy is inversely proportional to distance between polar molecules.\n\nBesides dipole- dipole interaction, polar molecules can interact by London forces also.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 138; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','a and b only','a and c only','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50940,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true?\n\na) Permanent dipole of the polar molecule induces dipole on the electrically neutral molecule by deforming its electronic cloud.\n\nb) Interaction energy is proportional to 1/r 6 where r is the distance between two molecules.','both a and b','Permanent dipole of the polar molecule induces dipole on the electrically neutral molecule by deforming its electronic cloud. Thus an induced dipole is developed in the other molecule.  \n\nInteraction energy is proportional to 1/r 6 where r is the distance between two molecules.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 138; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50941,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true?\n\na) Strength of the hydrogen bond is determined by the coulombic interaction between the lone-pair electrons of the electronegative atom of one molecule and the hydrogen atom of other molecule.\n\nb) When thermal energy of molecules is reduced by lowering the temperature; the gases can be very easily liquified.','both a and b','Strength of the hydrogen bond is determined by the coulombic interaction between the lone-pair electrons of the electronegative atom of one molecule and the hydrogen atom of other molecule.\n\nWhen thermal energy of molecules is reduced by lowering the temperature; the gases can be very easily liquified.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 139; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50942,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true?\n\na) Simplicity of gases is due to the fact that the forces of interaction between their molecules are negligible.\n\nb) Density of a gas is directly proportional to its molar mass.','both a and b','Simplicity of gases is due to the fact that the forces of interaction between their molecules are negligible.\n\nDensity of a gas is directly proportional to its molar mass.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 140; Paragraph No. 2 and Page No. 144; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50943,'chemistry','Each line of the volume vs temperature graph is called','isobar','Each line of the volume vs temperature graph is called isobar.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 143; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','isobar','isochore','isochrome','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50944,'chemistry','Each line of the Pressure vs temperature (Kelvin) graph at constant molar volume  is called','isochore','Each line of the Pressure vs temperature (Kelvin) graph at constant molar volume is called is called isochore.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 143; Paragraph No. 5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','isobar','isochore','isochrome','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50945,'chemistry','The critical temperature, pressure and volume are called','critical constant','The critical temperature, pressure and volume are called critical constants.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 143; Paragraph No. 5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','gas constant','rate constant','critical constant','flow constant','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50946,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true.\n\na) It is found that real gases follow, Boyle’s law, Charles law and Avogadro law perfectly under all conditions.\n\nb) At high pressure, molecules do not strike the walls of the container with full impact because these are dragged back by other molecules due to molecular attractive forces.','b only','It is found that real gases do not follow, Boyle’s law, Charles law and Avogadro law perfectly under all conditions.\n\nAt high pressure, molecules do not strike the walls of the container with full impact because these are dragged back by other molecules due to molecular attractive forces.\n\n"Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 150; Paragraph No. 4 and Page No. 151; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50947,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true.\n\na) Viscosity coefficient is the force when velocity gradient is unity.\n\nb) SI unit of viscosity coefficient is 1 Newton second per square metre (N s m⁻²) = pascal second (Pa s = 1 kg m⁻¹s⁻¹).','both a and b','Viscosity coefficient is the force when velocity gradient is unity.\n\nSI unit of viscosity coefficient is 1 newton second per square metre (N s m⁻²) = pascal second (Pa s = 1 kg m⁻¹s⁻¹).\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 150; Paragraph No. 4 and Page No. 157; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50948,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true.\n\na) The magnitude of surface tension of a liquid does not  depend on the attractive forces between the molecules.\n\nb) Increase in temperature increases the kinetic energy of the molecules and effectiveness of intermolecular attraction decreases.','b only','The magnitude of surface tension of a liquid depends on the attractive forces between the molecules. \n\nWhen the attractive forces are large, the surface tension is large. \n\nIncrease in temperature increases the kinetic energy of the molecules and effectiveness of intermolecular attraction decreases.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 150; Paragraph No. 4 and Page No. 157; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(50949,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true.\n\na) When a liquid flows over a fixed surface, the layer of molecules in the immediate contact of surface is stationary.\n\nb) The energy required to increase the surface area of the liquid by one unit is defined as surface energy.','both a and b','When a liquid flows over a fixed surface, the layer of molecules in the immediate contact of surface is stationary.\n\nThe energy required to increase the surface area of the liquid by one unit is defined as surface energy.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 150; Paragraph No. 4 and Page No. 156; Paragraph No. 1 and 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50950,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true?\n\na) Since water boils at low temperature on hills, the pressure cooker is used for cooking food. \n\nb) In hospitals surgical instruments are sterilized in autoclaves in which boiling point of water is increased by increasing the pressure above the atmospheric pressure by using a weight covering the vent.','both a and b','Since water boils at low temperature on hills, the pressure cooker is used for cooking food. \n\nIn hospitals surgical instruments are sterilized in autoclaves in which boiling point of water is increased by increasing the pressure above the atmospheric pressure by using a weight covering the vent.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 155; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(50951,'biology','Which one of the following is a xerophytic plant in which the stem is modified into the flat green and succulent structure? [Mains 2010]','Opuntia','Opuntia is a xerophytic plant which lives in dry habitat. The plant has fleshy organs where water and mucilage are stored. The stem is modified into flat green structure, therefore, Opuntia is also called as phylloclades. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 67; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Opuntia','Casuarina','Hydrilla','Acacia','NEW','11-067',NULL,NULL),
(50952,'biology','Diadelphous stamens are found in[NEET 2021]','Pea','Pea has a diadelphous androecium i.e. all the stamens unite to form two bundles(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 75; Paragraph No. 2) .','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','China rose and citrus','China rose','Citrus','Pea','NEW','11-075',NULL,NULL),
(51001,'biology','A fruit developed from hypanthodium inflorescence is called [NEET 2009]','Syconus','Syconous fruit develops from a hypanthodium inflorescence, e.g., Ficus. Hypanthodium is a box like inflorescence where the box is formed by the flesly receptacle. It opens to exterior by a single pore called ostiole. The hollow pear shaped fleshy receptacle encloses a number of minute male and female flowers, it becomes fleshy and forms the fruit\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; )','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Sorosis','Syconus','Caryopsis','Hesperidium','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51002,'biology','An example of axile placentation is [NEET 2009]','Lemon','Axile placentation occur in china rose,lemon and tomato(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 75; Paragraph No. 3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Dianthus','Lemon','Marigold','Argemone','NEW','11-075',NULL,NULL),
(51003,'biology','Cotyledons and testa respectively are edible parts in [NEET 2009]','Groundnut and pomegranate','Groundnut is dry, one chambered, one seeded fruit developing from a superior bi or poly carpellary ovary. The edible part is cotyledons and embryo lobe. Pomegranate is balausta type of fruit. The fruits develop from multilocular syncarpous inferior ovary. Testa is fleshy  and  edible.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Walnut and tamarind','French bean and coconut','Cashew nut and litchi','Groundnut and pomegranate','NEW','11-077',NULL,NULL),
(51004,'biology','An example of a seed with endosperm, perisperm, and caruncle is [NEET 2009]','Castor','In castor seed testa and tegmen are united together. Seed coat is tough and bright due to scleroprotein. Over narrower end a brownish pad is found which is called caruncle. Caruncle is carbohydrate in nature. This protects micropyle and develops as an integumental outgrowth after fertilization.Below seed coat a very thin membrane is found over kernel and called perisperm (the persistant nucellus).Below perisperm there is a large, white, swollen and oily mass called endsoperm.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Coffee','Lily','Castor','Cotton','NEW','11-077',NULL,NULL),
(51005,'biology','The fleshy receptacle of syconus of fig encloses a number of [NEET 2008]','Achenes','Syconus develop from hypanthodium type of inflorescence. The flask shaped fleshy receptacle encloses female flower which produces small achene like fruitlets and has a small pore protected by scale leaves. Example : syconus of fig (Ficus carica).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Berries','Mericarps','Achenes','Samaras','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51006,'biology','Dry indehiscent single seeded fruit formed from bicarpellary syncarpous inferior ovary is [NEET 2008]','Cypsella','Cypsella is a dry, one chambered, one seeded fruit developing from an inferior, bicarpellary ovary, e.g., sunflower, marigold, cosmos, etc. Caryopsis or grain is a small, dry one seeded fruit developing from superior monocarpellary ovary. Pericarp fused with the seed coat, e.g., rice, wheat, maize, etc. Cremocarp is a bilocular, two seeded fruit developing from an inferior bicarpellary ovary. It is characteristic fruit of umbelliferae, e.g., Coriander, Cuminum, etc. Berry or bacca develops from mono or multicarpellary superior or inferior syncarpous ovary with axile or parietal placentation, e.g., tomato, banana, brinjal, guava, grapes etc.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Berry','Cremocarp','Caryopsis','Cypsella','NEW','11-073',NULL,NULL),
(51007,'biology','Replum is present in the ovary of flower of [NEET 2008]','Mustard','Replum is a false septum formed due to the ingrowth of parietal placenta. This makes the ovary bilocular. It is mainly seen in the ovary of flowers of Brassicaceae (Cruciferae) Family e.g., mustard, candytuft etc.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Sun flower','Pea','Lemon','Mustard','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51008,'biology','The fruit is chambered, developed from inferior ovary and has seeds with succulent testa in [NEET 2008]','Pomegranate','In pomegranate, the whole fruit is covered by a hard rind made up of exocarp and a part of mesocarp. It develops from multilocular syncarpous inferior ovary. Mesocarp forms plate like infolding (i.e. chambered) and the seeds are covered by endocarp and contain bright red succulent testa.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Guava','Cucumber','Pomegranate','Orange','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51009,'biology','Endosperm is consumed by developing embryo in the seed of [NEET 2008]','Pea','During the process of the development of the embryo, the food stored up in the endosperm is continuously drawn up by the developing embryo and thus completely exhausted. Such seeds are known as exalbuminous or nonendospermic. The common examples are : exalbuminous – gram, pea, bean, tamarind, orchid, etc.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 77; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2','11-077','2','mcq','Pea','Maize','Coconut','Castor','NEW','11-077',NULL,NULL),
(51010,'chemistry','Gases possess characteristic critical temperature which depends upon the magnitude of intermolecular forces between the gas particles. \nCritical temperatures of ammonia and carbon dioxide are 405.5 K and 304.10 K respectively. \nWhich of these gases will liquify first when you start cooling from 500 K to their critical temperature ?','ammonia','Ammonia will liquify first because its critical temperature will be reached first.\n\nLiquefaction of Carbon dioxide will require more cooling.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 154; Problem 5.5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','ammonia','carbon dioxide','all of these','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51011,'chemistry','_____________  plotted isotherms of carbon dioxide at various temperatures noticed that at high temperatures isotherms look like that of an ideal gas and the gas cannot be liquified even at very high pressure.','Thomas Andrews','Thomas Andrews  plotted isotherms of carbon dioxide at various temperatures noticed that at high temperatures isotherms look like that of an ideal gas and the gas cannot be liquified even at very high pressure.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Robert Boyle','Thomas Andrews','Amedeo Avogadro','Gay Lussac','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51012,'chemistry','The diagram shown below represents the variation of _____________ for some gases. (n510)','compressibility','The graph represents the variation in compressibility for some gases.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 152; Figure  No. 5.12)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','partial pressures','isotherms','temperature','compressibility','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/51012q.png'),
(51013,'chemistry','The diagram shown below represents the _________ of carbon dioxide at various temperatures (n510)','isotherms','The graph (510) shown above represents the isotherms of carbon dioxide at various temperatures.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Figure  No. 5.13)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','partial pressures','isotherms','temperature','compressibility','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/51013q.png'),
(51014,'chemistry','In diagram given below, point A represents (n513)','gaseous state','Point A in the represents gaseous state.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Figure  No. 5.13)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','solid state','liquid state','gaseous state','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/51014q.png'),
(51015,'chemistry','In diagram given below, point D represents (n513)','liquid state','Point A in the represents liquid state.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Figure  No. 5.13)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','solid state','liquid state','gaseous state','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/51015q.png'),
(51016,'chemistry','In diagram below, point under the dome shaped area represents the existence of A and B carbon dioxide in equilibrium. (n513)','A-liquid B-gaseous','Point under the dome shaped area represents existence of liquid and gaseous carbon dioxide in equilibrium.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Figure  No. 5.13)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A-liquid B-solid','A-gaseous B-solid','A-liquid B-gaseous','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/51016q.png'),
(51017,'chemistry','Which of these statements are incorrect? \n\na) All the gases upon compression at constant temperature (isothermal compression) show the same behaviour.\n\nb) Gases should be cooled above their critical temperature for liquefaction.','a only','All the gases upon compression at constant temperature (isothermal compression) show the same behaviour.\n\nGases should be cooled above their critical temperature for liquefaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Paragraph No.2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51018,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason.\n\nAssertion: Intermolecular interactions may hold the closely and slowly moving molecules together and the gas liquifies.\n\nReason: Compression brings the molecules in close vicinity and cooling slows down the movement of molecules and slowly moving molecules together and the gas liquifies.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Compression brings the molecules in close vicinity and cooling slow down the movement of molecules therefore, intermolecular interactions may hold the closely and slowly moving molecules together and the gas liquifies.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Paragraph No.2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51019,'chemistry','On diagram below, we can move from point A to F vertically by __________ the temperature, then we can reach the point G by B the gas at the constant temperature along this isotherm. (n513)','increasing','We can move from point A to F vertically by increasing the temperature, then we can reach the point G by B the gas at the constant temperature along this isotherm"\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Figure  No. 5.13)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','increasing','decreasing','compressing','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/51019q.png'),
(51020,'chemistry','In diagram below, we can reach the point G by _________  the gas at the constant temperature along this isotherm. (n513)','compressing','We can move from point A to F vertically by increasing the temperature, then we can reach the point G by B the gas at the constant temperature along this isotherm"\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Figure  No. 5.13)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','increasing','decreasing','compressing','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/51020q.png'),
(51021,'chemistry','Which of these statements are correct?\n\na) It is possible to change a gas into liquid or a liquid into gas by a process in which always single phase is present.\n\nb) We can use isotherms of gases for predicting the conditions for liquefaction of gases.','both a and b','It is possible to change a gas into liquid or a liquid into gas by a process in which always single phase is present.\n\nWe can use isotherms of gases for predicting the conditions for liquefaction of gases.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 156; Paragraph No.  2 and Page 152 Paragraph No. 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51022,'chemistry','In diagram below, we can move vertically down towards D by ___________ the temperature. (n513)','decreasing','We can move vertically down towards D by lowering the temperature.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Figure  No. 5.13)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','decreasing','removing','increasing','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/51022q.png'),
(51023,'chemistry','As soon as we cross the point H on the critical isotherm on diagram below, we get (n513)','liquid','As soon as we cross the point H on the critical isotherm on diagram(513) below, we get liquid.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Figure  No. 5.13)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','liquid','gas','solid','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/51023q.png'),
(51024,'chemistry','In diagram below, the steep line represents the _________ of liquid. (n513)','isotherm','The steep line represents the isotherm of liquid.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Figure  No. 5.13)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','temperature','pressure','viscosity','isotherm','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/51024q.png'),
(51025,'chemistry','In diagram below, Even a slight compression results in steep rise in pressure indicating very _______ compressibility of the liquid. (n513)','low','Even a slight compression results in steep rise in pressure indicating very low compressibility of the liquid.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Figure  No. 5.13)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','high','low','decreasing','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/51025q.png'),
(51026,'chemistry','In diagram below,  At 21.5 °C, carbon dioxide remains as a gas only upto point __________. (n513)','B','At 21.5 °C, carbon dioxide remains as a gas only upto point B.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Figure  No. 5.13)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/51026q.png'),
(51027,'chemistry','In diagram below, at point 1, liquid and gaseous carbon dioxide coexist and further application of pressure results in the condensation of more gas until the point 2 is reached. (n513)','1-A, 2-B','At point B. liquid and gaseous carbon dioxide coexist and further application of pressure results in the condensation of more gas until the point C is reached.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Figure  No. 5.13)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1-A, 2-B','1-C, 2-D','1-B, 2-A','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/51027q.png'),
(51028,'chemistry','Which of these statements are true with respect to diagram below,\n\na) At point C, all the gas has been condensed and further application of pressure merely compresses the liquid as shown by steep line.\n\nb) A slight compression from volume V₂ to V₃ results in steep rise in pressure from p₂ to p₃.(n513)','both a and b','At point C, all the gas has been condensed and further application of pressure merely compresses the liquid as shown by steep line. \n\nA slight compression from volume V₂ to V₃ results in steep rise in pressure from p₂ to p₃.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Figure  No. 5.13)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/51028q.png'),
(51029,'chemistry','Which of these statement is true with respect to diagram below,\n\na) Below 30.98°C, only length of the horizontal line increases at lower temperatures.\n\nb) At critical point horizontal portion of the isotherm merges into one point. (n513)','both a and b','Below 30.98 °C (critical temperature) each curve shows the similar trend. Only length of the horizontal line increases at lower temperatures. \n\nAt critical point horizontal portion of the isotherm merges into one point.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Figure  No. 5.13)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/51029q.png'),
(51030,'chemistry','Which of these statement is true with respect to diagram below,\n\na) As the temperature is lowered, shape of the curve changes and data show considerable deviation from ideal behaviour.\n\nb) At 30.98°C carbon dioxide remains gas upto 73 atmospheric pressure. (n513)','both a and b','As the temperature is lowered, shape of the curve changes and data show considerable deviation from ideal behaviour.\n\nAt 30.98°C carbon dioxide remains gas upto 73 atmospheric pressure.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Figure  No. 5.13)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/51030q.png'),
(51031,'chemistry','"Which of these statement is true with respect to diagram below,\n\na) The temperature 30.98°C is called critical temperature (T𝒸) of carbon dioxide. \n\nb) It is the highest temperature at which liquid carbon dioxide is observed.  (n513)','both a and b','The temperature 30.98°C is called critical temperature (TC) of carbon dioxide. \n\nThis is the highest temperature at which liquid carbon dioxide is observed.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Figure  No. 5.13)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/51031q.png'),
(51032,'chemistry','In diagram below, carbon dioxide is a _______ above critical temperature. (n513)','gas','Carbon dioxide is a gas above critical temperature.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 153; Figure  No. 5.13)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','solid','liquid','gas','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/51032q.png'),
(51033,'chemistry','For the expression. \n\nZ= pV/nRT,  \n\nZ stands for;','compressibility factor','For the expression. \n\nZ= pV/nRT,  Z stands for compressibility factor\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 151; Figure  No. 7)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','viscosity','compressibility factor','Gas laws','all of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51034,'chemistry','In diagram given below, for gases which deviate from ideality, value of Z deviates from ___________ (n514)','unity','For gases which deviate from ideality, value of Z deviates from unity.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 152; Figure  No. 5.12)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','unity','infinity','zero','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/51034q.png'),
(51035,'chemistry','In diagram below, the graph of Z vs p will be a ______  line parallel to pressure axis. (n514)','straight','The graph of Z vs p will be a straight line parallel to pressure axis.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 152; Figure  No. 5.12)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','straight','curve','zig-zag','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/51035q.png'),
(51036,'chemistry','In diagram given below, at very low pressures all gases shown have Z ________ 1 and behave as ideal gas. (n514)','equal to','At very low pressures all gases shown have Z greater than 1 and behave as ideal gas.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 152; Figure  No. 5.12)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','equal to','less than','greater than','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/51036q.png'),
(51103,'chemistry','The interaction energy of temporary attraction forces between two dipoles is inversely proportional to sixth\n \n power of the distance between two interacting particles but their\n \n magnitude depends upon\n \n a) charge of interacting particles\n \n b) mass of interacting particles\n \n c) polarisability of interacting particles\n \n d) strength of permanent dipoles in the particles','only c','London dispersion forces are temporary force between two dipoles and it operates only over very short distance . Large or more complex are the molecules, greater is the magnitude of London forces. This is obviously due to the fact that the large electron clouds are easily distorted or polarised. Hence, greater the polarisability of the interacting particles, greater is the magnitude of the interaction energy.','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','a,c','b,c','only c','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51103q.png'),
(51104,'chemistry','Dipole-dipole forces act between the molecules possessing permanent\n \n dipole. Ends of dipoles possess ‘partial charges’. The partial charge is\n \n (a) more than unit electronic charge\n \n (b) equal to unit electronic charge\n \n (c) less than unit electronic charge \n \n (d) 1.6 x 10⁻¹⁰ C','c,d','Dipole-dipole forces act between the molecules possessing permanent dipole and \n \n ends of dipoles possess ‘partial charges’. Partial charges present on ends of a dipole \n \n are always less than the unit electronic charge.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 138 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','c,d','a,d','b,d','only d','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51104q.png'),
(51105,'chemistry','The pressure of a 1 : 4 mixture of dihydrogen and dioxygen enclosed in a vessel is one atmosphere. What would be the partial pressure of dioxygen?\n \n (a) 8x10⁵ atm\n \n (b) 0.008 Nm⁻² \n \n (c) 8 x10⁴ Nm⁻² \n \n (d) 0.789 atm','c,d','Given that, pressure of a 1 : 4 mixture of H ₂ and O ₂ enclosed in a vessel is one atmosphere. This suggests that the moles ratio of H ₂ and O ₂ is 1:4. Thus, partial pressure of dioxygen (O ₂ ) is given as\n \n Partial pressure of O ₂= Mole fraction of O ₂ x total pressure of mixture = 4/(1+4)*1=0.789 atm = 8*10⁴ Nm⁻²\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 147 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','c,d','a,d','b,d','only d','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51105q.png'),
(51106,'chemistry','As the temperature increases, average kinetic energy of molecules increases. What would be the effect of increase of temperature on pressure provided the volume is constant ?\n \n (a) Increases\n \n (b) Decreases\n \n (c) Becomes double \n \n (d) Becomes half','only a','As the temperature increases, average kinetic energy of molecules increases. From Gay-Lussac’s law, at constant volume, as the temperature is increased, pressure increases.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 143 paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','only a','only b','a,c','b,d','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51106q.png'),
(51107,'chemistry','Gases possess characteristic critical temperature which depends upon the magnitude of intermolecular forces between the particles. Following are the critical temperatures of some gases\n \n (H2) = 33.2 K\n \n (He)=5.3 K\n \n (O2)=154.3 K\n \n (N2)=126 K\n \n From the above data what would be the order of liquefaction of these gases? Start writing the order from the gas liquefying first\n \n (a) He, O2, N2\n \n (b)He, O2, H2,\n \n (c) N2, H2, He\n \n (d) O2, N2, H2,','c,d','Higher the critical temperature, more easily is the gas liquified. Hence, order of liquefaction starting with the gas liquefying first will be O ₂ , N ₂ , H ₂ , He.','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','c,d','a,b.','only c','only d','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51107q.png'),
(51108,'chemistry','What is unit of viscosity coefficient ?\n \n a) (Nm)/s²\n \n b)N/m²\n \n c)Pascal\n \n d)Nm','a,b','F=ηA(du/dz)\n \n F=force=[N]\n \n η=viscosity coefficient\n \n (du/dz)=velocity gradient=[m/s]\n \n A=[m²]\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 156 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','c,d','a,b','only c','only d','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51108q.png'),
(51109,'chemistry','Atmospheric pressures recorded in different cities are as follows:\n \n Shimla(1.01 x 10⁵),Bangalore(1.2 x 10⁵),Delhi (1.02 x 10⁵),Mumbai (1.21 x 10⁵)\n \n Consider the above data and mark the place at which liquid will boil first and which last.\n \n (a) Shimla \n \n (b) Bangalore \n \n (c) Delhi \n \n (d) Mumbai','a,d','Shimla has the lowest atmospheric pressure among all the four cities. Thus, at Shimla liquid will boil first because lower the atmospheric pressure, lower is the boiling point.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 154 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','c,d','a,d','a,b','a,c','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51109q.png'),
(51110,'chemistry','How will the viscosity of liquid and kinetic energy of molecules be affected by the increase \n \n in temperature?\n \n a)viscosity increases\n \n b)viscosity decreases\n \n c)kinetic energy increases\n \n d)kinetic energy decreases','a,c','With increase of temperature, the kinetic energy of the molecules of liquid increases\n \n which can overcome the intermolecular forces. Hence, the liquid starts flowing.\n \n In other words the viscosity of a liquid decreases with increase in temperature. It has\n \n been found that the decrease is about 2% per degree rise in temperature.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 157 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','a,d','b,c','b,d','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51110q.png'),
(51111,'chemistry','How does the surface tension of a liquid vary with increase in  temperature?\n \n (a) Remains same \n \n (b) Decreases\n \n (c) Increases \n \n (d) Becomes zero at the critical temperature','b,d','The surface tension of liquids generally decreases with increase of temperature and becomes zero at the critical temperature. The decrease in surface tension with increase of temperature is obviously due to the fact that with increase of temperature, the kinetic energy of the molecules increases and therefore, the intermolecular attraction decreases.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 155 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','a,d','b,c','b,d','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51111q.png'),
(51112,'chemistry','With regard to the gaseous state of matter which of the following statements are correct ?\n \n (a) Gases have much higher density than the solids and liquids. \n \n (b) Complete disorder of molecules\n \n (c) Random motion of molecules \n \n (d) Fixed position of molecules','b,c','In gaseous state, molecules are in a state of random motion i.e., it is the state in which molecules are disorderly arranged. Gaseous state have higher entropy than the liquid as well as solid.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 139 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','a,d','b,c','b,d','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51113,'chemistry','Which of the following figures does not represent 1 mole of oxygen  gas at STP?\n \n (a) 16 g of gas \n \n (b) 22.4 L of gas\n \n (c) 6,022 x 10²³ dioxygen molecules \n \n (d) 10.2 L of gas','a,b','It is known that, 1 mole of a gas = gram molecular mass = Avogadro\'s number of molecules = 22.4 L at STR\n \n Thus, 1 mole of O₂ gas = 32 g of O₂ = 6. 02 x10²³ molecules = 22.4L of O at STP\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 144 paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','a,b','b,c','b,d','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51113q.png'),
(51114,'chemistry','Under which of the following conditions applied together, a gas   deviates most from the ideal behaviour?\n \n (a) Low pressure \n \n (b) High pressure \n \n (c) Low temperature \n \n (d) there are no conditions for the gas to be ideal.','b,c','A gas which obeys the ideal gas equation, pV nRT = under all conditions of temperature and pressure is called an ‘ideal gas’. However, there is no gas which obeys the ideal gas equation under all conditions of temperature and pressure. Hence, the concept of ideal gas is only theoretical or hypothetical. The gases are found to obey the gas laws fairly well when the pressure is low or the temperature is high. Such gases are, therefore, known as ‘real gases’. All gases are real gases. Hence, at high pressure and low temperature, a real gas deviates most from ideal behaviour.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 145 paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','a,b.','b,c','b,d','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51114q.png'),
(51115,'chemistry','Which of the following changes decrease the vapour pressure of water kept in a sealed vessel ?\n \n (a) Increasing the quantity of water\n \n (b) Adding salt to water\n \n (c) Decreasing the volume of the vessel to one-half\n \n (d) Decreasing the temperature of water','b,d','When salt is added, less surface area is available for the water molecules to vaporise because salt molecules occupy the position of water molecules on the surface. Hence, vapour pressure of the water decreases. Moreover, vapour pressure also decreases with decrease in temperature because kinetic energy of the molecules decrease.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 154 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','a,b.','b,c','b,d','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51115q.png'),
(51116,'chemistry','If 1 g of each of the following gases are taken at STP, which of the gases will occupy greatest volume and smallest volume?\n \n a) CO\n \n b) H₂O\n \n c) CH₄ \n \n d) NO','c,d','Volume occupied by 1 g of any substance is : 22.4 l / Molar mass of the substance.\n \n Thus, 1 g CH₄ will occupy maximum volume while 1 g of NO will occupy minimum volume at STP.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 144 paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','a,b.','b,c','c,d','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51116q.png'),
(51117,'chemistry','The behaviour of matter in different states is governed by various physical laws.What are the factors that determine\n \n the state of matter ?\n \n a)Temperature \n \n b)Pressure\n \n c)mass\n \n d)density','a,b,c','Temperature, pressure, mass and volume are the factors which determine the different states of matter. i.e., solid, liquid and gas.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 140 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b,c','b,d,a','a,c,d','b,c,d','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51117q.png'),
(51118,'chemistry','Which type of intermolecular forces are present in the molecules HF, \n \n HCl, HBr and HI? \n \n a)dipole dipole\n \n b)London forces\n \n c)hydrogen bonding\n \n d)covalent bonding','a,b,c','In HCl, HBr and HI, dipole-dipole and London forces are present because molecules possess permanent dipole. In HF dipole-dipole, London forces and hydrogen bonding are present\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 137 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b,c','b,d,a','a,c,d','b,c,d','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51118q.png'),
(51119,'chemistry','Looking at the trend of boiling points of HCl, HBr and HI, explain : For dipole-dipole interaction and London interaction, which statement is true?\n \n a) Electronegativity of chlorine, bromine and iodine decreases in the order are present\n \n b)London forces are predominant. \n \n c)boiling point increases on moving from HCl to HI\n \n d)Electronegativity of chlorine, bromine and iodine increases in the order are present','a,b,c','Electronegativity of chlorine, bromine and iodine decreases in the order are present Cl >Br >I Therefore, dipole moment should decrease from HCl to HI. Thus, dipole-dipole interaction should decrease from HCI to HI. But boiling point increases on moving from HCl to HI. This means that London forces are predominant. This is so because London forces increases as the number of electrons in a molecule increases and in this case number of electrons is increasing from HCl towards HI.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 137 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b,c','b,d,a','a,c,d','b,c,d','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51119q.png'),
(51120,'chemistry','Why is boiling point of hydrogen fluoride highest while that of  hydrogen chloride lowest?\n \n a)Hydrogen fluoride has highest dipole moment attribute\n \n b) Hydrogen fluoride has lowest dipole moment attribute\n \n c)Fluorine has highest electronegativity .\n \n d)Fluorine has lowest electronegativity.','a,c','Hydrogen fluoride has highest dipole moment attributes due to highest electronegativity of fluorine as well as presence of hydrogen bonding in HF. Therefore, HF has highest boiling point\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 139 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','a,b.','b,c','none of them are true','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51120q.png'),
(51121,'chemistry','Choose the true statement about standard temperature and pressure conditions\n \n a) T= 273.15 K\n \n b) P= 1 atm\n \n c)molar volume of any gas is 22.4 L','all of them are true','When temperature and pressure of a gas is 273.15 K (or 0 oC) and 1 atm (or 1 bar or 105 \n \n pascal), such conditions are called standard temperature and pressure conditions (STP). \n \n Under these conditions, the volume occupied by 1 mole of each and every gas is 22.4L. \n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 144 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','a,b.','b,c','all of them are true','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51121q.png'),
(51122,'chemistry','Chose true statement about ideal gases:\n \n a)A gas that follows Boyle’s law, Charle\'s law and Avogadro’s law is called an ideal gas\n \n b)At low pressure and high temperature, a real gas behaves as an ideal gas\n \n c)Almost all gases are ideal gas.','a,b','A gas that follows Boyle’s law, Charle\'s law and Avogadro’s law is called an ideal gas.At low pressure and high temperature, a real gas behaves as an ideal gas. Almost all gases are real gas.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 145 paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','a,b','b,c','all of them are true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(40313,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the organisms shown in the image below: \n\ni. Have organ level of body organisation.\n\nii. Alimentary canal is incomplete with a well-developed muscular pharynx.\n\niii. Sexes are separate (dioecious): (a) is male and (b) is female.\n\niv. Often males are longer than females.\n\nWhich of the above statements are true for diagram ?(0455)','only iii','Roundworms have organ-system level of body organisation. Alimentary canal is complete with a well- developed muscular pharynx. Sexes are separate (dioecious), i.e., males and females are distinct. Often females are longer than males. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 52; paragraph 1) Fig 4.10','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Platyhelminthes,Aschelminthes,Annelida','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','i and ii','iii and iv','only iii','None of the above','NEW','11-052','11-043','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40313q.png'),
(51123,'chemistry','Two different gases ‘A’ and ‘ ’ B are filled in separate containers of  equal capacity under the same conditions of temperature and   pressure. On increasing the pressure slightly the gas ‘A’ liquefies but   gas B does not liquify even on applying high pressure until it is  cooled. Choose statements that explain this phenomenon:\n \n a)The temperature above which a gas cannot be liquefied howsoever high pressure may be  applied on the gas is called critical temperature\n \n b) Since, gas ‘A’ liquifies easily, this suggests gas ‘A’ is below its critical temperature\n \n c)gas ‘B’ does not liquefy easily even on applying high pressure. This suggests that gas ‘B’ is above its critical temperature.','all of them are true','The temperature above which a gas cannot be liquefied howsoever high pressure may be applied on the gas is called critical temperature. Since, gas ‘A’ liquifies easily, this suggests gas ‘A’ is below its critical temperature. On the other hand, gas ‘B’ does not liquefy easily even on applying high pressure. This suggests that gas ‘B’ is above its critical temperature.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 152 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','a,b.','b,c','all of them are true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51124,'chemistry','Choose correct statement for universal gas constant (R):\n \n a) ‘R´ is work done by the gas per mole per kelvin.\n \n b)\'R\' is the same for all gases.\n \n c)\'R\' is not the same for all gases.','a,b','Value of universal gas constant ( R) is same for all gases.\n \n Unit of R depends upon those units in which p, V and T are measured as, R=pV/nT. If \n \n pressure is measured in Pascal, per mole volume is measured in m³ and temperature is \n \n measured in Kelvin then units of ‘R ’ are Pam³ K⁻¹ mol ⁻¹ or J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. Since, joule is the unit \n \n of work done, so ‘ R’ is work done by the gas per mole per kelvin.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 145 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','a,b','b,c','all of them are true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51125,'chemistry','Choose correct statements about Kinetic theory:\n \n a) One of the assumptions of kinetic theory of gases states that “there is no force of attraction between the molecules of a gas.”\n \n b)It is not possible to liquefy an ideal gas because there is no intermolecular forces of attractions between the molecules of an ideal gas.\n \n c)The statement a is true for all gases.','a,b','One of the assumptions of kinetic theory of gases states that “there is no force of attraction between the molecules of a gas.”\n \n This statement is correct only for ideal gases. It is not possible to liquefy an ideal gas because there is no intermolecular forces of attractions between the molecules of an ideal gas.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 149 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','a,b','b,c','all of them are true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51126,'chemistry','The magnitude of surface tension of liquid depends on the attractive forces between the molecules. Which of them is correct?\n \n a) hexane < alcohol\n \n b) alcohol < water\n \n c)alcohol < hexane\n \n d) water<alcohol','a,b','Electronegativity of chlorine, bromine and iodine decreases in the order are present Cl >Br >I Therefore, dipole moment should decrease from HCl to HI. Thus, dipole-dipole interaction should decrease from HCI to HI. But boiling point increases on moving from HCl to HI. This means that London forces are predominant. This is so because London forces increases as the number of electrons in a molecule increases and in this case number of electrons is increasing from HCl towards HI.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 137 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','a,b','b,c','all of them are true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51127,'chemistry','Which statement is true\n \n a)Pressure exerted by saturated water vapour is called aqueous tension.\n \n b)P(moist gas) = P(dry gas) + aqueous tension\n \n c)P(moist gas) = P(dry gas) - aqueous tension','a,b','Whenever a gas is collected over water, it is moist and saturated with water vapours which exert their own pressure. The pressure due to water vapours is called aqueous tension thus, the total pressure of the gas (p moist gas) is P(moist gas) = P(dry gas) + aqueous tension,Thus P(dry gas)=P(moist gas)- aqueous tension.Hence, the correction term applied to the total pressure of the gas in order to obtain pressure of dry gas is P moist gas - aqueous tension.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 146 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','a,b','b,c','all of them are true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51128,'chemistry','Which statement is true \n \n a)The energy which arises due to motion of atoms or molecules in a body is known as thermal energy.\n \n b) It is a measure of average kinetic energy of the particles\n \n c) It decreases with increase in temperature.','a,b','The energy which arises due to motion of atoms or molecules in a body is known as thermal energy. It is a measure of average kinetic energy of the particles. It increases with increase in temperature.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 139 paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','a,b','b,c','all of them are true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51129,'chemistry','Which intermolecular forces exist between HF molecules in liquid state?\n \n a)dipole dipole\n \n b)H-bond\n \n c)london forces','a,b','H-F is a polar covalent molecule in which dipole-dipole interactions exists. Since, it contains an electronegative atom, F bonded to H atom, therefore, H-bond exists between its molecules. Due to these H-bonds, -HF molecules exists in liquid state. Hence, in liquid state, H-bonds as well as dipole dipole interactions exists within HF molecules.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 137 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','a,b','b,c','all of them','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51130,'chemistry','Which statement is true?\n \n a) there is no force of attraction between the molecules of a gas.\n \n b)Real gases can be liquefied by cooling and compressing the gas\n \n c)Assumption a) is not applicable for real gases.','b,c','Real gases can be liquefied by cooling and compressing the gas. This proves that force of \n \n attraction exist among the molecules\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 151 paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','a,b','b,c','all of them','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51131,'chemistry','Compressibility factor, Z of a gas is given as Z=pV/nRT\n \n Which statement is true?\n \n a)For ideal gas, compressibility factor, Z = 1.\n \n b)Above Boyle’s temperature,Z>1\n \n c)At intermediate pressures ,most gases have Z>1','a,b','Following important points come out from the compressibility factor, Z=pV/nRT\n \n (i) For ideal gas Z =1at all temperatures and pressures because pV nRT = .\n \n (ii) At very low pressure all gases shown have Z~1and behave as ideal gas.\n \n (iii) At high pressure, all the gases have Z >1. These are more difficult to compress.\n \n (iv) At intermediate pressures most gases have Z <1\n \n \n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 151 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','a,b','b,c','all of them','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51132,'chemistry','The critical temperature ( 30.98°C) Tc and critical pressure ( 73 atm) pc of CO2 .So..\n \n a) CO2 gas cannot be liquified above 30.98°C and 73 atm\n \n b)high temperature and pressure may be applied on CO2 gas\n \n c)CO2 gas cannot be liquified at 32.°C and 80 atm.','b,c','The given critical temperature and pressure of CO2 gas are 30.98°C and 73 atm respectively. This suggests that CO2 gas cannot be liquified above 30.98°C and 73 atm howsoever high temperature and pressure may be applied on CO2 gas. Hence, CO2 gas cannot be liquified at 32.°C and 80 atm.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 152 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','a,b.','b,c','all of them','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51133,'chemistry','For real gases the relation between p V, and T is given by van der Waals’ equation\n \n (p+an²/V²)(V-nb)=nRT . Which of them is correct for the value  of ‘ b ’ \n \n a)H₂<He\n \n b)He>O₂\n \n c)O₂<CO₂','a,c','Molar volume occupied by the gas molecules µ size of the molecules and van der Waals’ constant ‘b ’ represents molar volume of the gas molecules. Hence, value of ‘ b ’ increases in the following order H₂ < He < O₂ < CO₂ \n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 151 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','a,b','b,c','all of them','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51201,'biology','Assertion: In china rose, a single leaf arises at each node in an alternate manner.\n \nReason: China rose has ‘whorled’ type phyllotaxy.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','In alternate type of phyllotaxy, a single leaf arises at each node in alternate manner, as in china rose. If more than two leaves arise at a node and form a whorl, it is called Whorled. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 71; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Assertion and Reasoning',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-071',NULL,NULL),
(51202,'biology','Assertion: A flower may be bisexual. \n\nReason: A flower may have either only stamens or only carpels.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','When a flower has both androecium and gynoecium, it is bisexual. A flower having either only stamens or only carpels is unisexual.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 72; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Assertion and Reasoning',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-072',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51202q.png'),
(51203,'biology','Assertion: In mango and coconut, the fruit is known as a drupe and are one seeded. \n\nReason: Mango and coconuts are fruits that develop from monocarpellary superior ovaries.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','They develop from monocarpellary superior ovaries and are one seeded.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 76; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Assertion and Reasoning',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-076',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51203q.png'),
(51204,'biology','Assertion: The description of the diverse forms of life on earth was made by observation through naked eyes or  through magnifying lenses and microscopes. \n\nReason: Biology remained as a natural history for a long time','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','The description of the diverse forms of life on earth was made only by\nobservation – through naked eyes or later through magnifying lenses\nand microscopes. This description is mainly of gross structural features,\nboth external and internal. In addition, observable and perceivable\nliving phenomena were also recorded as part of this description. Before\nexperimental biology or more specifically, physiology, was established\nas a part of biology, naturalists described only biology. Hence, biology\nremained as a natural history for a long time. ( Reference  : NCERT, page 63, paragraph 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Assertion and Reasoning',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-063',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51204q.png'),
(51205,'biology','Assertion: Biology remained as a natural history for a long time. \n\nReason: Naturalists included physiology as a part of biology before experimental biology was discovered.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Before experimental biology or more specifically, physiology, was established as a part of biology, naturalists described only biology. This is why biology remained as a natural history for a long time.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 64; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Assertion and Reasoning',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-064',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51205q.png'),
(51206,'biology','Assertion: Katherine Esau reported in her early publications that the curly top virus spreads through a plant. \n\nReason: The curly top virus supposedly spread through the xylem tissue, according to Katherine.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Katherine Esau reported in her early publications that the curly top virus spreads through a plant via the food-conducting or phloem tissue.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 64; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Assertion and Reasoning',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-064',NULL,NULL),
(51207,'biology','Assertion: The Diversity of Flowering Plants by Katherine Esau which was published in 1965, was referred to as the Webster’s of plant biology. \n\nReason: It was a revised book.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','The Anatomy of Seed Plants by Katherine Esau was published in 1960. It was referred to as Webster’s of plant biology – it is encyclopediac.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 64; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Assertion and Reasoning',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-064',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51207q.png'),
(51208,'biology','Assertion: The underground part of the flowering plant is the root system. \n\nReason: It is underground so that absorption of water and minerals from the soil can be done successfully.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The root system of the plant not only helps to anchor it into the ground, but also effectively absorbs all the important nutrients.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 66; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Assertion and Reasoning',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-066',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51208q.png'),
(51209,'biology','Assertion: Angiosperms are all characterized by presence of roots, stems, leaves, flowers and fruits. \n\nReason: The angiosperms show a large diversity in external structure or morphology.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','In some plants, like grass, Monstera and the banyan tree, roots arise from parts of the plant other than the radicle and are called adventitious roots\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 66; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Assertion and Reasoning',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-066',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51209q.png'),
(51210,'biology','Assertion: Adventitious roots do not develop from elongation of the radicle. \n\nReason: The direct elongation of the radicle leads to the formation of primary root.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','In some plants, like grass, Monstera and the banyan tree, roots arise from parts of the plant other than the radicle and are called adventitious roots. The main functions of the root system are absorption of water and minerals from the soil, providing a proper anchorage to the plant parts, storing reserve food material and synthesis of plant growth regulators.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 66; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Assertion and Reasoning',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-066',NULL,NULL),
(51211,'biology','Assertion: In monocotyledonous plants, the primary root is replaced by a large number of roots that originate from the base of the stem. \n\nReason: In monocotyledonous plants, the primary root is short lived.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','In monocotyledonous plants, the primary root is short lived and is replaced by a large number of roots. These roots originate from the base of the stem and constitute the fibrous root system.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 66; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Assertion and Reasoning',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-066',NULL,NULL),
(51212,'biology','Assertion: The root cap is present at the base of the root. \n\nReason: The root cap allows root hairs to grow as they absorb water and minerals from the soil.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','The root is covered at the apex by a thimble-like structure called the root cap, and the root hairs are formed a few millimeters above the root cap. \n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 67; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Assertion and Reasoning',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-067',NULL,NULL),
(51213,'biology','Assertion: The cells proximal to the region of meristematic activity (in region of elongation) undergo rapid elongation. \n\nReason: They give rise to root hairs.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The cells proximal to region of meristematic activity, i.e.: the cells of region of elongation undergo rapid elongation and enlargement and are responsible for the growth of the root in length\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 67; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Assertion and Reasoning',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-067',NULL,NULL),
(51214,'biology','Assertion: In some plants, such as tap roots of carrot, turnip and adventitious roots of sweet potato, get swollen. \n\nReason: Their roots have modified to store food','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Roots of many plants modify to assist plants, such as in case of the above mentioned plants, their roots swell up to store food\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 67; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Assertion and Reasoning',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-067',NULL,NULL),
(51215,'biology','Assertion: In some plants such as Rhizophora, growing in swampy areas, many roots grow deeper into the soil of the ground and grow vertically downwards. \n\nReason: They do this to get water','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Plants that grow in swampy areas, such as Rhizophora, many roots come outof the ground and grow vertically upwards to get oxygen for respiration\nof the ground and grow vertically upwards to get oxygen for respiration.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 67; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Assertion and Reasoning',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-067',NULL,NULL),
(51216,'biology','Assertion: The stems of plants can also help to tide over conditions that are unfavourable for growth. \n\nReason: Stems have tendrils that develop from axillary buds.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Stems do have tendrils, but they are not the reason why plants are able to survive unfavourable conditions. Stems are modified to act as organs of perennation to help them overcome these conditions.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 68; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Assertion and Reasoning',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-068',NULL,NULL),
(51217,'biology','Assertion: Many plants have modifications that help protect plants from browsing animals. \n\nReason: Thorns are found in many plants.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Thorns are found in many plants such as Citrus, Bougainvillea. They protect plants from browsing animals\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 68; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Assertion and Reasoning',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-068',NULL,NULL),
(51218,'biology','Assertion: In plants like mint and jasmine a thick medial branch arises from the apex of the main axis and arch upwards. \n\nReason: A thick medial branch arises from the apex of the main axis and after growing horizontally below ground for some time arch upwards','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','In plants like mint and jasmine a slender lateral branch arises from the base of the main axis and after growing aerially for some time arch downwards to touch the ground\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 69; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Assertion and Reasoning',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-069',NULL,NULL),
(51301,'chemistry','For ideal gas,number of moles per litre in terms of its pressure P,gas constant R,and temperature T is:\n \n a)PT/R\n \n b)PRT\n \n c)P/RT\n \n d)RT/p','only c','PV=nRT n/V=P/RT\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 145 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only c','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51302,'chemistry','Value of gas constant is\n \n a)0.082 litre atm\n \n b)0.987 calmol⁻¹K⁻¹\n \n c)8.3mol⁻¹K⁻¹\n \n d)83mol⁻¹K⁻¹','only c','R = 8.3144598(48) J⋅mol⁻¹⋅K⁻¹=0.082 LatmK⁻¹mol⁻¹\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 145 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only c','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51303,'chemistry','Kinetic theory of gases proves\n \n a)only Boyle\'s law\n \n b)only Charle\'s law\n \n c)only Avogadro\'s law\n \n d)all of these','only d','Kinetic theory of gases proves all the given gas laws.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 145 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only d','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51304,'chemistry','According to kinetic theory of gases,in an ideal gas between two successive collisions a gas molecule travels\n \n a)in wavy path\n \n b)in a straight way\n \n c)with an accelerated path\n \n d)in circular path','only b','According to kinetic theory the gas molecules are in state of constant rapid motion in all possible directions colloiding in a random manner with another and with the walls of the container and between two successive collisions molecules travel in straight line path but show haphazard motion due to collisions.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 145 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only b','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51305,'chemistry','As the temperature is raised from 20°C to 40°C the average kinetic energy of neon atoms changes by a factor of which of the following?\n \n a)313/293\n \n b)(313/293)¹/²\n \n c)1/2\n \n d)2','only a','(K.E of neon at 40°C)/(K.E of neon at 20°C)=(3/2Kx313)/(3/2Kx293)=313/293\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 145 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only a','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51306,'chemistry','In the van der Waals equation of state of the gas law,the constant \'b\' is a measure of\n \n a)volume occupied by the molecules\n \n b)intermolecular attraction\n \n c)intermolecular repulsions\n \n d)intermolecular collisions per unit volume','only a','In der van der Waals equation b is for volume correction\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 151 paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only a','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51307,'chemistry','Which one of the following statements us NOT true about the effect of an increase in temperature on the distribution of molecular speeds in gas?\n \n a)The area under the distribution curve remains the same under the lower temperature\n \n b)The distribution becomes broader\n \n c)The fraction of the molecules with the most probable speed increases\n \n d)the most probable speed increases','only c','At higher temperature more molecules have higher velocities and less molecules have low velocities. With increase in temperature the most probable velocity increase but the fraction of such molecules decreases.\n \n Reference NCERT class 11 page 147 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only c','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51308,'chemistry','The most probable speed increases if 10⁻⁴dmof water is introduced into a 1.0dm³ flask at 300K , how many moles of water are in vapour phase when equilibrium is established?\n \n (Given: Vapour pressure of H2O at 300 K is 3170 Pa,R=8.314 JK-⁻¹mol⁻¹)\n \n a)5.56x10⁻³ mol\n \n b)1.53x10⁻²mol\n \n c)4.46*10-⁻²mol\n \n d)1.27x10⁻³mol','only d','PV=nRT\n \n n=PV/RT=1.27x10⁻³\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 145 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only d','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51309,'chemistry','The molecular velocity of any gas is:\n \n a)inversely proportional to absolute temperature\n \n b)directly proportional to square of temperature\n \n c)directly proportional to square root of temperature\n \n d)inversely proportional of the square root','only c','The different type of velocity\n \n Most probable:a=√(2RT/M),\n \n average:v\':√(8RT/3.14M),\n \n root mean square:v=√(3RT/M)\n \n All the above cases velocity x square root of T\n \n (Reference NCERT 11 page 156 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only c','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51310,'chemistry','The compressibility factor for a real gas at high pressure is \n \n a) 1+(RT/pb)\n \n b)1\n \n c)1+(pb/RT)\n \n d)1-(pb/RT)','only c','(P+a/V²)(V-b)=RT at high pressure a/V² can be neglected \n \n PV-Pb=RT and PV=RT+Pb\n \n PV/RT=1+Pb/RT\n \n Z=1+Pb/RT: Z>1 at high pressure \n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 151 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only c','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51311,'chemistry','For gaseous state , if most probable speed is denoted by C*,average speed by C\' and mean square speed by C,then for a large number of molecules the ratios of these speeds are:\n \n a)C*:C\':C=1.225:1.128:1\n \n b)C*:C\':C=1.128:1.225:1\n \n c)C*:C\':C=1:1.128:1.225\n \n d)C*:C\':C=1:1.225:1.128','only c','C*=√(2RT/M),C\':√(8RT/3.14M),C=√(3RT/M)\n \n so C*:C\'C=1:√(4/3.14):√(3/2)=1:1.128:1.225\n \n (Reference NCERT 11 page 156 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only c','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51312,'chemistry','If Z is compressibility factor van der Waals equation at low pressure can be written as \n \n a)Z=1+(RT/Pb)\n \n b)Z=1=1-(a/VRT)\n \n c)Z=1-(Pb/RT)\n \n d)Z=1+(Pb/RT)','only b','Compressibility factor Z=PV/RT\n \n van der Waals eq for one mole real gas\n \n (P+a/V²)(V-b)=RT\n \n At low pressure V-b~V\n \n (P+a/V²)=RT\n \n PV=RT-a/V\n \n PV/RT=1-a/VRT\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 151 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only b','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51313,'chemistry','The ratio of masses of oxygen and nitrogen in a particular gaseous mixture is 1:4 the ratio of number of their molecules is:\n \n a)1:4\n \n b)7:32\n \n c)1:8\n \n d)3:16','only b','Number of moles of O₂= w/32 N₂=(4w)/28\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 144 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only b','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51314,'chemistry','The intermolecular interaction that is dependant on the inverse cube of distance between the molecules is \n \n a)London forces\n \n b)hydrogen forces\n \n c)ion-ion interaction\n \n d)ion-dipole interaction','only b','Hydrogen bond is a type of strong electrostatic dipole dipole interaction and dependent on the inverse cube of distance between the molecular ion-dipole interaction\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 139 paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only b','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51315,'chemistry','Two closed bulbs of equal volume (V)containing an ideal gas initially at pressure p and temperature T1 are connected through a narrow tube of negligible volume as shown in figure below.The temperature of one of the bulbs is then raised to T2.The final pressure P2 is:\n \n a)2p1(T2/(T1+T2)\n \n b)2P1((T1T2)/(T1+T2)\n \n c)P1((T1T2)/(T1+T2)\n \n d)2p1(T1/(T1+T2)','only a','For a given mass of an ideal gas , the volume and amount of the gas are directly proportional if the temperature and pressure are constant. Hence in the given case : (nt)=(nt)t\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 145 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> JEE Mains',NULL,'3','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only a','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51316,'biology','Assertion: The pollen grains are produced in pollen-sacs. \n\nReason: Anther is usually bilobed.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.',': The pollen grains are produced in pollen-sacs of androecium because it is the male reproductive part of the flower(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 75; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-075',NULL,NULL),
(51317,'biology','Assertion: Gram and pea seeds are dicotyledonous. \n\nReason: Gram and pea seeds have one cotyledons.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Gram and pea seeds have two cotyledons, so they are dicotyledonous(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 76; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-076',NULL,NULL),
(51318,'biology','Assertion: Wheat and maize seeds are monocotyledonous. \n\nReason: Wheat and maize seeds have only one cotyledon','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Wheat and maize seeds have one cotyledons, so they are monotyledonous(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 77; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-077',NULL,NULL),
(51319,'biology','Assertion: The cotyledons in a dicotyledonous seed are often fleshy. \n\nReason: They provide shape to the seed','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The cotyledons in a dicotyledonous seed are often fleshy and full of reserve food materials(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 76; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-076',NULL,NULL),
(51320,'biology','Assertion: In plants such as bean, gram and pea, the mature seeds are called non-endospermous. \n\nReason: The endosperm formed in bean, gram and pea is a result of triple fertilization','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','In plants such as bean, gram and pea, the endosperm is not present in mature seeds\nand such seeds are called non-endospermous\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 77; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-077',NULL,NULL),
(51321,'biology','Assertion: Various morphological features are used to describe a flowering plant.\n\nReason: The plant is described beginning with its habit, vegetative characters – roots, stem and leaves','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Various morphological features are used to describe a flowering plant. If you read a description, you will notice that the plant is described beginning with roots, stem and leaves. \nThe description has to be brief, in a simple and scientific language and presented in a proper sequence. The plant is described beginning with its habit, vegetative characters – roots, stem and leaves and then floral characters’ inflorescence and flower parts.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 64; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-064',NULL,NULL),
(51322,'biology','Assertion: In a Floral Diagram of a flower, Calyx, corolla, androecium and gynoecium are drawn in successive whorls, calyx being the outermost and the gynoecium being in the centre. \n\nReason: It is easier to draw a larger whorl for calyx to give an idea about the size of the flower','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','As these whorls are arranged in the same fashion in the actual flower, with calyx being the outermost whorl and gynoecium being the most hidden whorls, their sequence in the floral diagram is also similar.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 78; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is the incorrect explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-078',NULL,NULL),
(51323,'biology','Consider the following statements about the stem of a plant and choose the correct options :\n\na. In pineapple the lateral branches originate from the basal and underground portion of the main stem.\n\nb. In mint a slender lateral branch arises from base of the main axis and after growing aerially for some time arch downwards .\n\nc. In strawberry , a lateral branch ultimately comes out obliquely upwards giving rise to leafy shoots.','a,b','In banana, pineapple\nand Chrysanthemum, the lateral branches originate from the basal and\nunderground portion of the main stem, grow horizontally beneath the\nsoil and then come out obliquely upward giving rise to leafy shoots.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Class 11; Page no. 69)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','None of these','NEW','11-069',NULL,NULL),
(51324,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding the root of a plant.\n\ni. Roots that arise from parts of the plant other than the radicle are called adventitious roots.\n\nii. The direct elongation of the radicle leads to the formation of the primary root.\n\niii. In the majority of the dicotyledonous plants, the primary root is short-lived and is replaced by a large number of roots.\n\nSelect the correct answer using the code below','i and ii only','(iii) is not correct as the replacement of primary root by a large number of roots (formation of fibrous root system) occurs in monocotyledonous plants(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 66; Paragraph No. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','i only','i and ii only','ii only','iii only','NEW','11-066',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51324q.png'),
(51325,'biology','Consider the following statements about the Placentation and Choose the correct option','When the ovules are borne on central axis and septa are absent, the placentation is called free central.','Placentation is the arrangement of ovules within the ovary and it is of 5 kinds. Parietal placentation is characterized with ovule development on the inner wall. Placenta develops at the base of ovary with a single ovule attachment in case of basal placentation. ‘D’ is the correct statement because in case of free central placentation, the ovules are born on central axis and septa are not present.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 75; Paragraph No. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','The arrangement of ovaries within the ovules is known as placentation','The ovules develop on the outer wall in parietal placentation.','In basal placentation, the ovary develops at the base of placenta and four ovules are attached to it.','When the ovules are borne on central axis and septa are absent, the placentation is called free central.','NEW','11-075',NULL,NULL),
(20101,'chemistry','_______ are the fundamental building blocks of matter.','Atoms','"Atoms are the fundamental building blocks of matter."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.29; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Atoms','Molecules','Ions','Both atoms and molecules','NEW','11-029','11-029',NULL),
(20102,'chemistry','The continued subdivisions of matter would ultimately yield _______ .','Atoms','"The continued subdivisions of matter would ultimately yield atoms which would not be further divisible."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.29; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Atoms','Molecules','Particles','Ions','NEW','11-029','11-029',NULL),
(20103,'chemistry','The word ‘atom’ has been derived from the Greek word _______.','A-tomio','"The word ‘atom’ has been derived from the Greek word ‘a-tomio."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.29; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','A-tomio','A-tomino','A-tonio','A-tomi','NEW','11-029','11-029',NULL),
(20104,'chemistry','The Greek word A-tomio means _______ and _______.','Uncut-able ,non divisible','"The Greek word A-tomio means ‘uncut-able’ or ‘non-divisible’."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.29; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Uncut-able ,non divisible','Smallest and granular','Smallest and building block','Easily divisible and small','NEW','11-029','11-029',NULL),
(20105,'chemistry','The atomic theory of matter was first proposed on a firm scientific basis by _______.','John Dalton','"The atomic theory of matter was first proposed on a firm scientific basis by John Dalton, a British school teacher in 1808."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.29; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Rutherford','John Dalton','Heisenberg','J. J. Thomson','NEW','11-029','11-029',NULL),
(20106,'chemistry','John Dalton\'s proposed theory is known as _______.','Dalton’s atomic theory','"The theory proposed by John Dalton is known as Dalton’s atomic theory."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.29; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Dalton’s atomic theory','Dalton\'s particle theory','Gold particle theory','Dalton\'s theory of matter','NEW','11-029','11-029',NULL),
(20107,'chemistry','Dalton\'s atomic theory was able to successfully explain which of the following laws?\n \n (i) Law of conservation of mass\n \n (ii) Law of constant composition\n \n (iii)Law of multiple proportions.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','All (i), (ii), and (iii)','"Dalton’s atomic theory was able to explain the law of conservation of mass, law of constant composition and law of multiple proportion very successfully."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.29; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Only (i) and (ii)','Only (i) and (iii)','Only (ii) and (iii)','All (i), (ii), and (iii)','NEW','11-029','11-029',NULL),
(20108,'chemistry','Which of the following substances get electrically charged when rubbed with silk or fur?\n \n (i) Glass and ebonite\n \n (ii) Ebonite and silk protein\n \n (iii) Silk and glass\n \n (iv) Fur and glass','Glass and ebonite','"Substances like glass or ebonite when rubbed with silk or fur get electrically charged."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.29; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Glass and ebonite','Ebonite and silk protein','Silk and glass','Fur and glass','NEW','11-029','11-029',NULL),
(20109,'chemistry','The atoms are made of sub-atomic particles i.e.,\n \n (i) Neutrons \n \n (ii)Protons \n \n (iii)Electrons \n \n (iv)Molecules','Only (i), (ii), and (iii)','"Atoms are made of sub-atomic particles, i.e., electrons, protons and neutrons."\n\nMolecules are made up of one or more atoms. It is a group of two or more atoms held together by chemical bonds.\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.29; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Only (iv)','Only (i), (iii) and (iv)','Only (ii), (iii) and (iv)','Only (i), (ii), and (iii)','NEW','11-029','11-029',NULL),
(20110,'biology','Which of the following is not a Kingdom defined by R.H. Whittaker in his Five kingdom classification:','Kingdom Algae','R.H. Whittaker (1969) proposed a Five Kingdom Classification. The kingdoms defined by him were named Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae, and Animalia. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 17; Paragraph No.1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Introduction And Classifications',NULL,'2','mcq','Kingdom Algae','Kingdom Fungi','Kingdom Protista','Kingdom Monera','NEW','11-017','11-011',NULL),
(20111,'biology','Earlier classification systems included bacteria, blue green algae, fungi, mosses, ferns, gymnosperms, and the angiosperms under ‘Plants’. The character that unified this whole kingdom was _______ in their cell ?','Presence of cell wall','Earlier classification systems included bacteria, blue green algae, fungi, mosses, ferns.The character that unified this whole kingdom was that all the organisms included had a cell wall in their cells. This placed together groups which widely differed in other characteristics. It brought together the prokaryotic bacteria and the blue green algae ( cyanobacteria) with other groups which were eukaryotic. It also grouped together the unicellular organisms and the multicellular ones, say, for example, Chlamydomonas and Spirogyra were placed together under algae. The classification did not differentiate between the heterotrophic group – fungi, and the autotrophic green plants, though they also showed a characteristic difference in their walls composition – the fungi had chitin in their walls while the green plants had a cellulosic cell wall. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 17; Paragraph No.3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Introduction And Classifications',NULL,'2','mcq','Mode of nutrition','Presence of cell wall','Phylogenetic relationship','Mode of reproduction','NEW','11-017','11-011',NULL),
(20112,'biology','Earlier classification systems included bacteria, blue green algae, fungi, mosses, ferns, gymnosperms and the angiosperm under ‘Plants’. How many of the following widely differed in other characteristics?','All of them','Earlier classification systems included bacteria, blue green algae, fungi, mosses, ferns,\n gymnosperms and the angiosperms under ‘Plants’. The character that unified this whole\n kingdom was that all the organisms included had a cell wall in their cells. This placed\n together groups which widely differed in other characteristics. It brought together the\n prokaryotic bacteria and the blue green algae (cyanobacteria) with other groups which were\n eukaryotic. It also grouped together the unicellular organisms and the multicellular ones, say,\n for example, Chlamydomonas and Spirogyra were placed together under algae. The\n classification did not differentiate between the heterotrophic group – fungi, and the\n autotrophic green plants. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 17; Paragraph No.3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Introduction And Classifications',NULL,'2','mcq','It brought together the prokaryotic and eukaryotic organisms','It grouped together the unicellular and the multicellular organisms','It did not differentiate between the heterotrophic and autotrophic organisms','All of them','NEW','11-017','11-011',NULL),
(20113,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Aristotle\'s classification system. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. Aristotle used simple morphological characters to classify plants into trees, shrubs and herbs.\n\n2. Aristotle also divided animals into two groups, those which had red blood and those that did not.\n\n3. In Aristotles\' time a Two Kingdom system of classification with Plantae and Animalia kingdoms was developed that included all plants and animals respectively\n\n4. Aristotle was the earliest to attempt a more scientific basis for classification.','1,2,and 4 are correct','Aristotle was the earliest to attempt a more scientific basis for classification. He used simple morphological characters to classify plants into trees, shrubs and herbs. He also divided animals into two groups, those which had red blood and those that did not. In Linnaeus\' time a Two Kingdom system of classification with Plantae and Animalia kingdoms was developed that included all plants and animals respectively. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 16; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Introduction And Classifications',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 2 and 3 are correct','1,2,and 4 are correct','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Both 2 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-016','11-010',NULL),
(40314,'biology','Match the following with the correct aspect in osmoregulation and excretion.','Annelida-Nephridia,Platyhelminthes-flame cells,Humans-Kidneys,Malphigian tubules-Cockroach','Annelida has excretory organ Nephridia,Platyhelminthes has excretory organ flame cells both of which also play role in osmoregulation.\nAll vertebrates have kidneys – Excretory product is urea\nFlame cells in planaria\nEarthworms have Nephridia\nCockroaches have malpighian tubules\nPrawns have antennal glands or green glands\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 51 and 52)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Platyhelminthes,Aschelminthes,Annelida','Animal Kingdom','2','match','Annelida,Nephridia','Platyhelminthes,flame cells','Humans,Kidneys','Malphigian tubules,Cockroach','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(40315,'biology','True coelom appeared first in the course of evolution in?','Annelida','Annelids are the first eucoelomates. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 49; Figure 4.4)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Platyhelminthes,Aschelminthes,Annelida','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Aschelminthes','Chordata','Echinodermata','Annelida','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(20114,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Linnaeus\'s classification system. Choose the correct option.\n\n1. In Linnaeus\' time a Two Kingdom system of classification with Plantae and Animalia kingdoms was developed that included all plants and animals respectively. \n\n2. The 2 Kingdom classification system did not distinguish between the eukaryotes and prokaryotes, unicellular and multicellular organisms and photosynthetic (green algae) and non-photosynthetic (fungi) organisms. \n\n3. Classification of organisms into plants and animals was easily done and was difficult to understand\n\n4. A large number of organisms did not fall into either category.','only 3 is incorrect','In Linnaeus\' time a Two Kingdom system of classification with Plantae and Animalia kingdoms was developed that included all plants and animals respectively. This system did not distinguish between the eukaryotes and prokaryotes, unicellular and multicellular organisms and photosynthetic (green algae) and non-photosynthetic (fungi) organisms. Classification of organisms into plants and animals was easily done and was easy to understand, but, a large number of organisms did not fall into either category. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 16; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Introduction And Classifications',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Only 1 is incorrect.','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect.','only 3 is incorrect','NEW','11-016','11-010',NULL),
(20115,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Whittaker classification system. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. R.H. Whittaker proposed a Five Kingdom Classification.\n\n2. The kingdoms defined by him were named Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae and Animalia.\n\n3. The main criteria for classification used by him include cell structure, body organisation, mode of nutrition, reproduction and phylogenetic relationships.\n\n4. The five kingdom classification was proposed in the year 1969','All 1,2,3 and 4 are correct','R.H. Whittaker (1969) proposed a Five Kingdom Classification. The kingdoms defined by him were named Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae and Animalia. The main criteria for classification used by him include cell structure, body organisation, mode of nutrition, reproduction and phylogenetic relationships. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 17; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Introduction And Classifications',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are correct.','All 1,2,3 and 4 are correct','2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-017','11-011',NULL),
(20116,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Early Classification systems. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. The character that unified the whole kingdom containing Plants,Cyanobacteria and fungi was that all the organisms included had a cell wall in their cells.\n\n2. Earlier classification systems included bacteria, blue-green algae, fungi, mosses, ferns, gymnosperms and the angiosperms under ‘Plants’.\n\n3. It brought together the prokaryotic bacteria and the blue green algae (Cyanobacteria) together.\n\n4. It also grouped the unicellular organisms and the multicellular ones, Chlamydomonas and Spirogyra  under algae.\n\n5. The classification did not differentiate between the heterotrophic group – fungi, and the autotrophic green plants, though they also showed a characteristic difference in their wall composition.','1,2 3, 4 and 5 are correct.','Earlier classification systems included bacteria, blue green algae, fungi, mosses, ferns, gymnosperms and the angiosperms under ‘Plants’. The character that unified this whole kingdom was that all the organisms included had a cell wall in their cells. This placed together groups which widely differed in other characteristics. It brought together the prokaryotic bacteria and the blue green algae (cyanobacteria) with other groups which were eukaryotic. It also grouped together the unicellular organisms and the multicellular ones, say, for example, Chlamydomonas and Spirogyra were placed together under algae. The classification did not differentiate between the heterotrophic group – fungi, and the autotrophic green plants, though they also showed a characteristic difference in their walls composition. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 17; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Introduction And Classifications',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1,2 and 5 are correct.','1,2 3, 4 and 5 are correct.','only 3 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect.','NEW','11-017','11-011',NULL),
(20117,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the five kingdom classifications. Choose the correct option. \n1. All prokaryotic organisms are grouped under Kingdom Monera\n\n2. The unicellular eukaryotic organisms are placed in Kingdom Protista.\n\n3. Kingdom Protista now includes Chlamydomonas, and Chlorella (earlier placed in Algae within Plants and both having cell walls).  \n\n4. Kingdom Protista also includes Paramoecium, mycoplasma and Amoeba (which were earlier placed in the animal kingdom and lack cell walls).','1,2 and 3 are correct','All prokaryotic organisms were grouped together under Kingdom Monera and the unicellular eukaryotic organisms were placed in Kingdom Protista. Kingdom Protista has brought together Chlamydomonas, Chlorella (earlier placed in Algae within Plants and both having cell walls) with Paramoecium and Amoeba (which were earlier placed in the animal kingdom which lack cell wall). (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 18; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Introduction And Classifications',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are correct.','Both 1 and 4 are correct.','1,2 and 3 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-018','11-012',NULL),
(20201,'chemistry','From the following, which one option is correct?\n \n (i) Like charges attract and unlike charges repel.\n \n (ii) Like charges repel and unlike charges attract.\n \n (ii) Like charges always repel while unlike charges may attract or repel\n \n (iv) All are irrelevant\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer(s) from the given below:','Only (ii)','"Like charges repel each other and unlike charges attract each other."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.30; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Electron',NULL,'1','mcq','Only (ii)','Both (i) and (ii)','Only (iii)','Only (iv)','NEW','11-030','11-030',NULL),
(20202,'chemistry','_______ showed that if electricity is passed through a solution of an electrolyte, chemical reactions occurred at the electrodes, which resulted in the liberation and deposition of matter at the electrodes.','Michael Faraday','"In 1830, Michael Faraday showed that if electricity is passed through a solution of an electrolyte, chemical reactions occurred at the electrodes, which resulted in the liberation and deposition of matter at the electrodes."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.30; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Electron',NULL,'1','mcq','Rutherford','John Dalton','Michael Faraday','J. J. Thomson','NEW','11-030','11-030',NULL),
(20203,'chemistry','The results of Faraday’s experiment suggested the _______ nature of electricity.','Particulate','"In 1830, Michael Faraday showed that if electricity is passed through a solution of an electrolyte, chemical reactions occurred at the electrodes, which resulted in the liberation and deposition of matter at the electrodes. \n\nThe results of his experiments suggested the particulate nature of electricity."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.30; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Electron',NULL,'2','mcq','Wave','Particulate','Electromagnetic','Dual','NEW','11-030','11-030',NULL),
(20204,'chemistry','In mid 1850s, Faraday began to study electrical discharge in partially evacuated tubes. These tubes are known as _______.','Cathode ray discharge tube','"In mid 1850s many scientists mainly Faraday began to study electrical discharge in partially evacuated tubes, known as cathode ray discharge tubes."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.30; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Electron',NULL,'1','mcq','Anode ray discharge tube','Electricity discharge tube','Cathode ray discharge tube','Current discharge tube','NEW','11-030','11-030',NULL),
(20205,'chemistry','The electrical discharge through the gases in the cathode ray tube could be observed only at _______ and _______.','Very low pressures, very high voltages','"The electrical discharge through the gases in the cathode ray tube could be observed only at very low pressures and at very high voltages."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.30; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Electron',NULL,'3','mcq','Very high pressures, very high voltages','Very low pressures, very low voltages','Very low pressures, very high voltages','Very high pressures, very low voltages','NEW','11-030','11-030',NULL),
(20206,'chemistry','_______ travel in the cathode ray tube from the negative electrode (cathode) to the positive electrode (anode).','Cathode rays','"Cathode rays travel in the cathode ray tube from the negative electrode (cathode) to the positive electrode (anode)."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.30; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Electron',NULL,'1','mcq','Cathode rays','Anode rays','X-rays','γ-rays','NEW','11-030','11-030',NULL),
(20207,'chemistry','The flow of current from cathode to anode in the cathode ray tube was further checked by making a hole in the anode and coating the tube behind the anode with phosphorescent material _______','Zinc sulphide','"The flow of current from cathode to anode was further checked by making a hole in the anode and coating the tube behind anode with phosphorescent material zinc sulphide."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.30; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Electron',NULL,'2','mcq','Zinc sulphide','Zinc sulphate','Zinc nitrate','Magnesium sulphate','NEW','11-030','11-030',NULL),
(20208,'chemistry','The flow of current through a stream of particles moving in the tube from cathode to anode in a discharge tube is known as _______.','Cathode rays or cathode ray particles','"The flow of current from cathode to anode are known as cathode rays or cathode ray particles."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.30; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Electron',NULL,'1','mcq','Cathode rays or cathode ray particles','Anode rays or anode ray particles','Alpha particles','Helium ions','NEW','11-030','11-030',NULL),
(20209,'chemistry','During the cathode ray discharge tube experiments, it was observed that the cathode rays _______.','Start from cathode and move towards the anode','"The results of discharge tube experiments were:\n\nThe cathode rays start from cathode and move towards the anode."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.30; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Electron',NULL,'1','mcq','Start from anode and move towards the cathode','Start from cathode and move towards the anode','Start from cathode and get dispersed in the tube','Start from anode and get dispersed in the tube','NEW','11-030','11-030',NULL),
(20301,'chemistry','What were the major problems the scientists were facing after the discovery of sub-atomic particles?\n \n (i) To account for the stability of atom.\n \n (ii)To compare the behaviour of elements in terms of both physical and chemical properties.\n \n (iii)To explain the formation of different kinds of molecules by the combination of different atoms.\n \n (iv)To understand the origin and nature of the characteristics of electromagnetic radiations absorbed or emitted by atoms.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','All (i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','"The major problems before the scientists after the discovery of sub-atomic particles were:\n\n(i) To account for the stability of atom.\n\n(ii) To compare the behaviour of elements in terms of both physical and chemical properties.\n\n(iii) To explain the formation of different kinds of molecules by the combination of different atoms.\n\n(iv) To understand the origin and nature of the characteristics of electromagnetic radiation absorbed or emitted by atoms."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.32; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Atomic models',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (iii)','(iii), (iv)','(i), (ii), (iv)','All (i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','NEW','11-032','11-032',NULL),
(20302,'chemistry','Match the subatomic particle to its absolute (per Coulumb) and relative charge.\n \n 1) Electron a) +1.602176 × 10⁻¹⁹ i) 0\n \n 2) Proton b) 0 ii)+1\n \n 3) Neutron c) -1.602176 × 10⁻¹⁹ iii) -1','1-c-iii 2-a-ii 3-b-i','"The tabulated values of absolute charge/coulumb for electron, proton and neutron are –1.602176×10⁻¹⁹, +1.602176×10⁻¹⁹, 0 and of realtive charge are 0,1,1 respectively."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.33; Table 2.1 Properties of Fundamental Particles','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Atomic models',NULL,'2','mcq','1-a-iii 2-b-i 3-c-ii','1-b-i 2-a-ii 3-c-iii','1-c-iii 2-a-ii 3-b-i','1-c-ii 2-b-iii 3-a-i','NEW','11-033','11-033','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/20302q.png'),
(20303,'chemistry','Match the subatomic particle to its mass in kg and mass in amu. \n \n 1) Electron a) 1.6726216 × 10⁻²⁷ i) 1.00727\n \n 2) Proton b) 1.674927 × 10⁻²⁷ ii) 1.00867 \n \n 3) Neutron c) 9.109382 × 10⁻³¹ iii) 0.00054','1-c-iii 2-a-i 3-b-ii','"The tabulated values of massess for elctron, proton, and neutron are 9.\n\n109382×10⁻³¹, 1.6726216×10⁻²⁷, 1.674927×10⁻²⁷, and of mass/amu are 0.00054, 1.00727, 1.00867 respectively."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.33; Table 2.1 Properties of Fundamental Particles','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Atomic models',NULL,'3','mcq','1-a-i 2-b-ii 3-c-iii','1-c-iii 2-a-i 3-b-ii','1-b-iii 2-a-i 3-c-ii','1-c-i 2-b-ii 3-a-iii','NEW','11-033','11-033',NULL),
(20304,'chemistry','After the discovery of subatomic particles, different atomic models were proposed to explain _______.','The distributions of these charged particles in an atom','"Different atomic models were proposed to explain the distributions of these charged particles in an atom."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.33; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Atomic models',NULL,'1','mcq','The distributions of these charged particles in an atom','The mass of an atom','The charge on an atom','The stability of an atom','NEW','11-033','11-033',NULL),
(20305,'chemistry','In the atomic model proposed by J. J. Thomson, in 1898, the atom was assumed to possess a _______ shape.','Spherical','"J. J. Thomson, in 1898, proposed that an atom possesses a spherical shape (radius approximately 10⁻¹⁰m) in which the positive charge is uniformly distributed. \n\nThe electrons are embedded into it in such a manner as to give the most stable electrostatic arrangement."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.33; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Thomson Model of Atom',NULL,'2','mcq','Elliptically oval','Elliptical','Circular','Spherical','NEW','11-033','11-033',NULL),
(40316,'biology','_________ phylum have dorso-ventrally flattened body','Platyhelminthes','Platyhelminthes have dorso-ventrally flattened body, hence are called flatworms. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 51; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Platyhelminthes,Aschelminthes,Annelida','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Ctenophora','Aschelminthes','Platyhelminthes','Annelida','NEW','11-051','11-042',NULL),
(20306,'chemistry','The radius of the atom, as proposed by Thomson in 1898, was assumed to be approximately equal to _______.','10⁻¹⁰ m','"J. J. Thomson, in 1898, proposed that an atom possesses a spherical shape (radius approximately 10⁻¹⁰ m) in which the positive charge is uniformly distributed. \n\nThe electrons are embedded into it in such a manner as to give the most stable electrostatic arrangement."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.33; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Thomson Model of Atom',NULL,'2','mcq','10⁻¹⁰ m','10⁻¹¹ m','10⁻¹⁰ nm','10⁻¹¹ nm','NEW','11-033','11-033',NULL),
(20307,'chemistry','In the atomic model proposed by J. J. Thomson, in 1898, the distribution of charges were proposed in a way that the positive charge was assumed to be _______ , whereas, the negative charge was assumed to be _______.','Uniformly distributed, embedded into it in such a manner as to give the most stable electrostatic arrangement','"J. J. Thomson, in 1898, proposed that an atom possesses a spherical shape (radius approximately 10⁻¹⁰m) in which the positive charge is uniformly distributed. \n\nThe electrons are embedded into it in such a manner as to give the most stable electrostatic arrangement."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.33; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Thomson Model of Atom',NULL,'3','mcq','Uniformly distributed, embedded into it in such a manner as to give the most stable electrostatic arrangement','Uniformly distributed, also uniformly distributed outside the positive charge','Randomly scattered, uniformly distributed around the atom','Randomly scattered, embeded outside the positivecharge','NEW','11-033','11-033',NULL),
(20308,'chemistry','What did J. J. Thomson propose in his hypothesis about the structure of an atom?\n \n (i) Possesses a spherical shape.\n \n (ii)Possesses oval shape.\n \n (iii)Has a radius approximately equal to 10‐¹⁰ nm.\n \n (iv)The positive charge is uniformly distributed.\n \n (v) The positive charge is not uniformly distributed.\n \n (vi)The electrons are embedded into it in such a manner as to give the most stable electrostatic arrangement.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(i), (iv), (vi) only','"J. J. Thomson, in 1898, proposed that an atom possesses a spherical shape (radius approximately 10⁻¹⁰m) in which the positive charge is uniformly distributed. \n\nThe electrons are embedded into it in such a manner as to give the most stable electrostatic arrangement."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.33; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Thomson Model of Atom',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii) only','(i), (iv), (vi) only','(iii), (iii), (v) only','(iv), (vi) only','NEW','11-033','11-033',NULL),
(20309,'chemistry','The atomic model proposed by J. J. Thomson, in 1898, had given many names as:\n \n (i) Watermelon model\n \n (ii) Plum pudding model\n \n (iii) Raisin pudding model\n \n (iv) Gold foil model\n \n (v) Thomson\'s atomic model\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(i), (ii), (iii) only','"Many different names are given to this model, for example, plum pudding, raisin pudding or watermelon."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.33; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Thomson Model of Atom',NULL,'2','mcq','All (i), (ii), (iii), (iv), (v)','(i), (ii), (iii) only','(i), (ii), (iv) only','(iii), (iv), (v) only','NEW','11-033','11-033',NULL),
(20310,'chemistry','The atomic model proposed by J. J. Thomson, in 1898, can be visualised as a pudding or watermelon of _______ with plums or seeds of _______ embedded into it.','Positive charge, electrons','"This model can be visualised as a pudding or watermelon of positive charge with plums or seeds (electrons) embedded into it."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.33; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Thomson Model of Atom',NULL,'3','mcq','Positive charge, α-particles','Electrons, positive charge','Positive charge, electrons','Electrons, α-particles','NEW','11-033','11-033',NULL),
(20311,'chemistry','An important feature of the atomic model proposed by J. J. Thomson is that the mass of the atom is assumed to be _______ over the atom.','Uniformly distributed','"An important feature of this (Thomson\'s) model is that the mass of the atom is assumed to be uniformly distributed over the atom."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.33; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Thomson Model of Atom',NULL,'1','mcq','Uniformly distributed','Uniformly scattered','On the periphery','Randomly scattered','NEW','11-033','11-033','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/20311q.png'),
(20312,'chemistry','Although the Thomson\'s atomic model was able to explain the _______ , but was not consistent with the _______.','Overall neutrality of the atom, results of later experiments.','"Although this model was able to explain the overall neutrality of the atom, but was not consistent with the results of later experiments."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.33; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Thomson Model of Atom',NULL,'2','mcq','Overall neutrality of the atom, mass obtained of an atom','Overall stability of the atom, results of later experiments','Overall mass of the atom, charge distribution within the atom','Overall neutrality of the atom, results of later experiments.','NEW','11-033','11-033',NULL),
(20313,'chemistry','Thomson was awarded Nobel Prize for physics in _______.','1906','"Thomson was awarded Nobel Prize for physics in 1906."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.33; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Thomson Model of Atom',NULL,'2','mcq','1905','1906','1907','1908','NEW','11-033','11-033',NULL),
(20314,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct regarding Millikan’s Oil Drop Method?\n \n (i) The electrical charge on the oil droplets was acquired by exposing them to cathode rays.\n \n (ii) The fall of these charged oil droplets can be retarded, accelerated or made stationary depending upon the charge on the droplets and the polarity and strength of the voltage applied to the plate.\n \n (iii) Millikan concluded that the magnitude of electrical charge, q, on the droplets is always an integral multiple of the electrical charge, e, that is, q = ne, where n = 1, 2, 3... .\n \n (iv) In chamber, the forces acting on oil drops are: \n \n (a) gravitational (b) magnetic due to magnetic field, and (c) the force of attraction between oil molecules\n \n (v) By measuring the rate of fall of these droplets, Millikan was able to measure the charge of oil droplets.\n \n (vi) The air inside the chamber was ionized by passing a beam of X-rays through it.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(ii), (iii), (vi) only','"Millikan’s Oil Drop Method:\n In this method, oil droplets in the form of mist, produced by the atomiser, were allowed to enter through a tiny hole in the upper plate of electrical condenser. The downward motion of these droplets was viewed through the telescope, equipped with a micrometer eye piece. By measuring the rate of fall of these droplets, Millikan was able to measure the mass of oil droplets. The air inside the chamber was ionized by passing a beam of X-rays through it. The electrical charge on these oil droplets was acquired by collisions with gaseous ions. The fall of these charged oil droplets can be retarded, accelerated or made stationary depending upon the charge on the droplets and the polarity and strength of the voltage applied to the plate. By carefully measuring the effects of electrical field strength on the motion of oil droplets, Millikan concluded that the magnitude of electrical charge, q, on the droplets is always an integral multiple of the electrical charge, e, that is, q = n e, where n = 1, 2, 3... . In chamber, the forces acting on oil drop are: gravitational, electrostatic due to electrical field and a viscous drag force when the oil drop is moving."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.32; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Atomic models',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (iv), (v), only','(i), (ii), (iii), (v) only','(ii), (iii), (vi) only','(ii), (iv), (v), (vi) only','NEW','11-032','11-032',NULL),
(40317,'biology','Which one is a blood sucker ?','Hirudinaria (leech)','Hirudinaria (Blood sucking leech)..(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 52; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Platyhelminthes,Aschelminthes,Annelida','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Nereis','Pheretima (earthworm)','Hirudinaria (leech)','All of them','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(20315,'chemistry','_______ observed that there are certain elements which emit radiation on their own and named this phenomenon as radioactivity.','Henri Becqueral','"Henri Becqueral (1852-1908) observed that there are certain elements which emit radiation on their own and named this phenomenon as radioactivity and the elements known as radioactive elements."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.33; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Atomic models',NULL,'1','mcq','Henri Becqueral','Röentgen','J.J. Thomson','Ernest Rutherford','NEW','11-033','11-033',NULL),
(20316,'chemistry','Which of the following is correct?\n \n (i) From radioactive elements three kinds of rays i.e., α, β and γ-rays are emitted.\n \n (ii) From radioactive elements four kinds of rays i.e., α, β, γ and X-rays are emitted.\n \n (iii) α-rays consists of high energy particles carrying two units of positive charge and four unit of atomic mass.\n \n (iv) β-rays are negatively charged particles similar to electrons.\n \n (v) β-rays are positively charged particles similar to protons.\n \n (vi) The γ-rays are high energy radiations like X-rays, are neutral in nature and do not consist of particles.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(i), (iii), (iv), (vi) only','"It was observed that three kinds of rays i.e., α, β and γ-rays are emitted. Rutherford found that α-rays consists of high energy particles carrying two units of positive charge and four unit of atomic mass. He concluded that α- particles are helium nuclei as when α- particles combined with two electrons yielded helium gas. β-rays are negatively charged particles similar to electrons. The γ-rays are high energy radiations like X-rays, are neutral in nature and do not consist of particles."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.33, 34; Paragraph 4, 1)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Atomic models',NULL,'2','mcq','(i), (ii), (iv), (vi) only','(i), (iii), (iv), (vi) only','(i), (iii), (v), (vi) only','(ii), (iv), (v), (vi) only','NEW','11-033','11-033','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/20316q.png'),
(20317,'chemistry','Order of penetration power of the radioactive particles is _______.','α-particle < β-particle < γ-particle','"As regards penetrating power, α-particles are the least, followed by β-rays (100 times that of α–particles) and γ-rays (1000 times of that α-particles)."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 34; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Atomic models',NULL,'3','mcq','α-particle > β-particle > γ-particle','α-particle < β-particle > γ-particle','α-particle > β-particle < γ-particle','α-particle < β-particle < γ-particle','NEW','11-033','11-033',NULL),
(20318,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): Röentgen (1845-1923) in 1895 showed that when electrons strike a material in the cathode ray tubes, produce rays which can cause fluorescence in the fluorescent materials placed outside the cathode ray tubes. Röentgen named them X-rays. These rays are used to study the interior of the objects.\n \n Reason(R): It was noticed that X-rays are produced effectively when electrons strike the dense metal anode, called targets.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','"Wilhalm Röentgen (1845-1923) in 1895 showed that when electrons strike a material in the cathode ray tubes, produce rays which can cause fluorescence in the fluorescent materials placed outside the cathode ray tubes. Since Röentgen did not know the nature of the radiation, he named them X-rays and the name is still carried on. It was noticed that X-rays are produced effectively when electrons strike the dense metal anode, called targets. These are not deflected by the electric and magnetic fields and have a very high penetrating power through the matter and that is the reason that these rays are used to study the interior of the objects. These rays are of very short wavelengths (∼0.1 nm) and possess electro-magnetic character."\n"The γ-rays are high energy radiations like X-rays, are neutral in nature and do not consist of particles."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.33, 34; Paragraph 3, 1)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Atomic models',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-033','11-033',NULL),
(20319,'chemistry','In Rutherford\'s experimemt, which led to the discovery of Rutherford\'s nuclear atomic model, Rutherford bombarded on very thin gold foil, which had a circular fluorescent zinc sulphide screen around it, with α-particles. Whenever α-particles struck the screen, _______ was produced at that point.','A tiny flash of light','"In α-particle scattering experiment, a stream of high energy α-particles from a radioactive source was directed at a thin foil (thickness ∼ 100 nm) of gold metal. \n\nThe thin gold foil had a circular fluorescent zinc sulphide screen around it. \n\nWhenever α–particles struck the screen, a tiny flash of light was produced at that point."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.34; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Rutherford’s Nuclear Model of Atom',NULL,'2','mcq','A burn spot','A minute electric current','A tiny hole in the sheet','A tiny flash of light','NEW','11-034','11-034',NULL),
(20320,'chemistry','The results of scattering experiment were quite unexpected from what was assumed of _______ model of atom.','Thomson\'s','"The results of scattering experiment were quite unexpected. \n\nAccording to Thomson model of atom, the mass of each gold atom in the foil should have been spread evenly over the entire atom, and α-particles had enough energy to pass directly through such a uniform distribution of mass. \n\nIt was expected that the particles would slow down and change directions only by a small angles as they passed through the foil."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.34; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Rutherford’s Nuclear Model of Atom',NULL,'1','mcq','Bohr\'s','Rutherford’s','Thomson\'s','Milikan\'s','NEW','11-034','11-034',NULL),
(20321,'chemistry','From what was expected of Thomson\'s model of atom, the mass of each gold atom in the foil should have been _______ , however, Rutherford\'s experimental observation suggested the mass of atom to be _______.','Spread evenly over the entire atom, unevenly distributed within the atom','"The results of scattering experiment were quite unexpected. \n\nAccording to Thomson model of atom, the mass of each gold atom in the foil should have been spread evenly over the entire atom, and α-particles had enough energy to pass directly through such a uniform distribution of mass. \n\nIt was expected that the particles would slow down and change directions only by a small angles as they passed through the foil."\n\n"A few positively charged α–particles were deflected. \n\nThe deflection must be due to enormous repulsive force showing that the positive charge of the atom is not spread throughout the atom as Thomson had presumed. \n\nThe positive charge has to be concentrated in a very small volume that repelled and deflected the positively."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.34; Paragraph 3,4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Rutherford’s Nuclear Model of Atom',NULL,'2','mcq','Unevenly distributed within the atom, spread evenly over the entire atom','Spread evenly over the entire atom, unevenly distributed within the atom','Unevenly distributed within the atom, randomly scattered throughout the atom','Concentrated to one side of an atom, spread evenly over the entire atom','NEW','11-034','11-034',NULL),
(20322,'chemistry','In the Rutherford\'s experiment, since α-particles had enough energy to pass directly through a uniform distribution of mass within the atom as assumed by Thomson, it was expected that the particles _______ as they passed through the foil. However what actually observed was _______.','Would slow down and change directions only by a small angles, the deflection occurred unevenly throughout the gold foil','"The results of scattering experiment were quite unexpected. \n\nAccording to Thomson model of atom, the mass of each gold atom in the foil should have been spread evenly over the entire atom, and α-particles had enough energy to pass directly through such a uniform distribution of mass. It was expected that the particles would slow down and change directions only by a small angles as they passed through the foil."\n\n"A few positively charged α–particles were deflected(unevenly). The deflection(unevenly) must be due to enormous repulsive force showing that the positive charge of the atom is not spread throughout the atom as Thomson had presumed. The positive charge has to be concentrated in a very small volume that repelled and deflected the positively."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.34; Paragraph 3,4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Rutherford’s Nuclear Model of Atom',NULL,'3','mcq','Would slow down and change directions only by a small angles, the deflection occurred unevenly throughout the gold foil','Would move unaffectedly and do not deflect at all, the deflection occurred unevenly throughout the gold foil','Would speed up and change directions only by a small angles, the deflection occurred evenly throughout the gold foil','Would hit hard by the sheet and get completely deflected, the deflection did not occur at all and they pass through the sheet unaffected','NEW','11-034','11-034',NULL),
(20323,'chemistry','The observations of Rutherford\'s α-particle scattering experiment were:\n \n (i) Most of the α-particles passed through the gold foil deflected.\n \n (ii) Most of the α-particles passed through the gold foil undeflected.\n \n (iii) A small fraction of the α-particles was deflected by small angles.\n \n (iv) A large fraction of the α-particles was deflected by large angles.\n \n (v) A large number of α-particles(~20,000) bounced back, that is, were deflected by nearly 180°.\n \n (vi) A very few α-particles bounced back, that is, were deflected by nearly 180°.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(ii), (iii), (vi) only','"In Rutherford\'s experiment, it was observed that:\n\n  (i) Most of the α-particles passed through the gold foil undeflected.\n\n  (ii) A small fraction of the α-particles was deflected by small angles.\n\n  (iii) A very few α-particles (∼1 in 20,000) bounced back, that is, were deflected by nearly 180°."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.34; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Rutherford’s Nuclear Model of Atom',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i)','(i), (ii), (v) only','(ii), (iii), (vi) only','(i), (ii), (iii) only','NEW','11-034','11-034',NULL),
(20324,'chemistry','In the Rutherford\'s experiment, it was observed that, a very few α-particles, that is, _______ were deflected by the angle of nearly _______.','∼1 in 20,000, 180°','"In Rutherford\'s experiment, it was observed that:\n\n→ A very few α-particles (∼1 in 20,000) bounced back, that is, were deflected by nearly 180°."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.34; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Rutherford’s Nuclear Model of Atom',NULL,'3','mcq','∼10 in 20,000, 120°','∼100 in 20,000, 90°','∼1 in 20,000, 180°','∼1 in 2,000, 180°','NEW','11-034','11-034',NULL),
(20325,'chemistry','In the Rutherford\'s scattering experiment, it was observed that most of the α-particles passed through the sheet of gold foil, on which they were bombarded, undeflected. This led to the conclusion that _______.','Most of the space in the atom is empty','"On the basis of the observations of scattering experiment, Rutherford drew the following conclusions regarding the structure of atom:\n\n→ Most of the space in the atom is empty as most of the α–particles passed through the foil undeflected."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.34; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Rutherford’s Nuclear Model of Atom',NULL,'3','mcq','Most of the space in the atom is occuied by particles','Most of the space in the atom is empty','Most of the space in the atom is not empty','Most of the space in the atom is occuied by nucleus','NEW','11-034','11-034',NULL),
(20326,'chemistry','In the Rutherford\'s scattering experiment, it was observed that a few positively charged α-particles were deflected. This deflection by a few particles must be due to _______ showing that _______ and deflected the positively charged α-particles.','Enormous repulsive force, the positive charge of the atom is not spread throughout the atom but must be concentrated in a very small volume that repelled','"On the basis of the observations of scattering experiment, Rutherford drew the following conclusions regarding the structure of atom:\n\n→ A few positively charged α-particles were deflected. The deflection must be due to enormous repulsive force showing that the positive charge of the atom is not spread throughout the atom as Thomson had presumed. The positive charge has to be concentrated in a very small volume that repelled and deflected the positively charged α-particles."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.34; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Rutherford’s Nuclear Model of Atom',NULL,'2','mcq','Attractive force, the negative charge of the atom is not spread throughout the atom but must be concentrated in a very small volume that repelled','Attractive forrce, the positive charge of the atom is not spread throughout the atom but must be concentrated in a very small volume that repelled','Enormous repulsive force, the positive charge of the atom is not spread throughout the atom but must be concentrated in a very small volume that repelled','Enormous repulsive force, the negative charge of the atom is not spread throughout the atom but must be concentrated in a very small volume that repelled','NEW','11-034','11-034','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/20326q.png'),
(20327,'chemistry','In the Rutherford\'s scattering experiment, it was calculated that the volume occupied by the nucleus is _______ as compared to the total volume of the atom.','Negligibly small','"On the basis of the observations of scattering experiment, Rutherford drew the following conclusions regarding the structure of atom:\n\n→ Calculations by Rutherford showed that the volume occupied by the nucleus is negligibly small as compared to the total volume of the atom. The radius of the atom is about 10⁻¹⁰ m, while that of nucleus is 10⁻¹⁵ m. One can appreciate this difference in size by realising that if a cricket ball represents a nucleus, then the radius of atom would be about 5 km."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.34; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Rutherford’s Nuclear Model of Atom',NULL,'1','mcq','Slightly large','Slightly small','Very large','Negligibly small','NEW','11-034','11-034','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/20327q.png'),
(40318,'biology','Choose the correct statement among the following \n\ni)A closed circular system – annelida. \n\nii)Nereis -aquatic, dioecious. \n\niii)Pheretima and leech- monoecious.','i, ii and iii','In annelida A closed circulatory system is present and earthworms and leeches are monoecious.Nereis, an aquatic form, is dioecious. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 52; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Platyhelminthes,Aschelminthes,Annelida','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Only i and ii','Only ii and iii','Only i and iii','i, ii and iii','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(20328,'chemistry','The radius of the atom is about _______ , while that of nucleus is _______.','10⁻¹⁰ m, 10⁻¹⁵ m','"On the basis of the observations, Rutherford drew the following conclusions regarding the structure of atom:\n\n→ Calculations by Rutherford showed that the volume occupied by the nucleus is negligibly small as compared to the total volume of the atom. The radius of the atom is about 10⁻¹⁰ m, while that of nucleus is 10⁻¹⁵ m. One can appreciate this difference in size by realising that if a cricket ball represents a nucleus, then the radius of atom would be about 5 km."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.34; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Rutherford’s Nuclear Model of Atom',NULL,'2','mcq','10⁻¹⁰ nm, 10⁻¹⁵ nm','10⁻¹⁵ m, 10⁻¹⁰ m','10⁻¹⁰ m, 10⁻¹⁵ m','10⁻¹⁵ m, 10⁻¹⁵ m','NEW','11-034','11-034',NULL),
(20329,'chemistry','Keeping in mind the difference in size between a nucleus and an atom, one can realise that if a cricket ball represents a nucleus, then the radius of atom would be about _______.','5 km','"On the basis of the observations, Rutherford drew the following conclusions regarding the structure of atom:\n\n → Calculations by Rutherford showed that the volume occupied by the nucleus is negligibly small as compared to the total volume of the atom. The radius of the atom is about 10⁻¹⁰ m, while that of nucleus is 10⁻¹⁵ m. One can appreciate this difference in size by realising that if a cricket ball represents a nucleus, then the radius of atom would be about 5 km."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.34; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Rutherford’s Nuclear Model of Atom',NULL,'2','mcq','5 mm','5 km','5 nm','5 Å','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(20330,'chemistry','The conclusions drawn from the Rutherford experiment were:\n \n (i) Most of the space in the atom is empty.\n \n (ii) Most of the space in the atom is filled with subatomic particles.\n \n (iii)The positive charge is dispersed in a large volume.\n \n (iv) The positive charge is concentrated in a very small volume.\n \n (v) The volume occupied by the nucleus is negligibly small as compared to the total volume of the atom.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(i), (iv), (v) only','"On the basis of the observations of scattering experiment, Rutherford drew the following conclusions regarding the structure of atom:\n\n  (i) Most of the space in the atom is empty as most of the α–particles passed through the foil undeflected.\n\n  (ii) A few positively charged α-particles were deflected. The deflection must be due to enormous repulsive force showing that the positive charge of the atom is not spread throughout the atom as Thomson had presumed. The positive charge has to be concentrated in a very small volume that repelled and deflected the positively charged α-particles.\n\n  (iii) Calculations by Rutherford showed that the volume occupied by the nucleus is negligibly small as compared to the total volume of the atom. The radius of the atom is about 10⁻¹⁰ m, while that of nucleus is 10⁻¹⁵ m."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.34; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Rutherford’s Nuclear Model of Atom',NULL,'2','mcq','(ii), (iv) only','(i), (iv), (v) only','(iii), (iv), (v) only','(i), (ii), (iii) only','NEW','11-034','11-034',NULL),
(20331,'chemistry','Rutherford\'s proposed atomic model is known as _______.','Rutherford’s nuclear model of atom','"On the basis of above observations and conclusions, Rutherford proposed the nuclear model of atom."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.35; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Rutherford’s Nuclear Model of Atom',NULL,'3','mcq','Rutherford’s atomic model','Rutherford’s nuclear model of atom','Rutherford’s α-particle scattering model of atom','Rutherford’s gold foil model of atom','NEW','11-034','11-034',NULL),
(20332,'chemistry','According to the Rutherford\'s nuclear model of atom, the positive charge of the atom is _______.','Densely concentrated in an extremely small region','"On the basis of above observations and conclusions, Rutherford proposed the nuclear model of atom. According to this model:\n\n→ The positive charge and most of the mass of the atom was densely concentrated in extremely small region. This very small portion of the atom was called nucleus by Rutherford."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.35; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Rutherford’s Nuclear Model of Atom',NULL,'2','mcq','Evenly distributed throughout the volume of atom','Densely concentrated in an extremely large region of atom','Densely concentrated in an extremely small region','Randomly scattered throughout the atomic volume','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(20333,'chemistry','According to the Rutherford\'s nuclear model of atom, most of the mass of the atom is _______.','Densely concentrated in an extremely small region','"On the basis of above observations and conclusions, Rutherford proposed the nuclear model of atom. According to this model:\n\nThe positive charge and most of the mass of the atom was densely concentrated in extremely small region. This very small portion of the atom was called nucleus by Rutherford."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.35; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Rutherford’s Nuclear Model of Atom',NULL,'2','mcq','Densely concentrated in an extremely large region of atom','Evenly distributed throughout the volume of atom','Randomly scattered throughout the atomic volume','Densely concentrated in an extremely small region','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(20334,'chemistry','According to the Rutherford\'s nuclear model of atom, the small region within an atom where the positive charge and most of the mass of the atom is densely concentrated is called _______.','Nucleus','"On the basis of above observations and conclusions, Rutherford proposed the nuclear model of atom. According to this model:\n\nThe positive charge and most of the mass of the atom was densely concentrated in extremely small region. This very small portion of the atom was called nucleus by Rutherford."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.35; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Rutherford’s Nuclear Model of Atom',NULL,'2','mcq','Nucleus','Nucleon','Nucleolus','Nucleous','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(20335,'chemistry','According to the Rutherford\'s nuclear model of atom, the nucleus is surrounded by _______ that move around the nucleus in circular paths called _______.','High speed electrons, orbits','"On the basis of above observations and conclusions, Rutherford proposed the nuclear model of atom. According to this model:\n\nThe nucleus is surrounded by electrons that move around the nucleus with a very high speed in circular paths called orbits. Thus, Rutherford’s model of atom resembles the solar system in which the nucleus plays the role of sun and the electrons that of revolving planets."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.35; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Rutherford’s Nuclear Model of Atom',NULL,'3','mcq','Slow speed electrons, orbitals','High speed protons, orbitals','High speed electrons, orbits','High speed electrons, shell','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(20401,'biology','The kingdom not having the well defined boundaries is ______','Protista','All single-celled eukaryotes are placed under Protista, but the boundaries of this kingdom are not well defined. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 20; Paragraph No. 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'3','mcq','Protista','Animalia','Fungi','Plantae','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(20402,'biology','Asexual reproduction in Diatoms is by','Binary fission','Diatoms,Dinoflagellates and Euglenoids reproduce asexually by binary fission','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'2','mcq','Binary fission','Spore formation','Conjugation','None of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20403,'biology','Red tide is caused by','Gonyaulax','Gonyaulax spreads on the water surface and the sea appears red which is very harmful to the fishes ,As this Red Gonyaulax releases toxic substances(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 21; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'2','mcq','Gonyaulax','Euglena','Chlorella','Bacteria','NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(20404,'biology','Golden alage is the other name of','Desmids','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 20; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'1','fillup','D,es,m,i,ds',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(20405,'biology','Soap box shaped cell wall is seen in organisms belonging to which Phylum','Chrysophytes','In diatoms cell wall forms two thin overlapping shells which fit together as a soap box','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'2','mcq','Chrysophytes','Euglenoids','Slime moulds','Prtozoans','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(20406,'biology','Bioluminescence is shown by','Dinoflagellates','They show bioluminecence due to the presence of photogenic granules in their cytoplasm','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'2','fillup','D,in,of,la,gel,l,at,es',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(20407,'biology','In euglenoids the cell membrane is surrounded by','Pellicle','Cell wall is absent in euglenoids . Instead a protein rich layer pellicle is present which helps in flexibility.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 21; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'2','mcq','Cellwall','Plasma','Glycocalyx','Pellicle','NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(20408,'biology','Consider the following statements about Protista:\n i. All single-celled eukaryotes are placed under Protista.\n\n ii. What may be ‘a photosynthetic protistan’ to one biologist may be ‘a plant’ to another.\n\n iii. Members of Protista are primarily aquatic.\n\n iv. Protists reproduce by sexual as well as asexual reproduction.\n\n Which of the above statements are false?','None of the above','All single-celled eukaryotes are placed under Protista, but the boundaries of this kingdom are not well defined. What may be ‘a photosynthetic protistan’ to one biologist may be ‘a plant’ to another. Members of Protista are primarily aquatic. Protists reproduce asexually and sexually by a process involving cell fusion and zygote formation.\n (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 20; Paragraph No. 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'2','mcq','Only ii','Only iii','Only iv','None of the above','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(20409,'biology','Match List 1 with List 2:using below diagram (0210)','i-q ii-r iii-s iv-p','Chrysophytes- This group includes diatoms and golden algae (desmids). They are found in fresh water as well as in marine environments. They are microscopic and float passively in water currents (plankton). Dinoflagellates-These organisms are mostly marine and photosynthetic Euglenoids-Majority of them are freshwater organisms found in stagnant water.\n Slime moulds are saprophytic protists.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'2','mcq','i-q ii-r iii-s iv-p','i-r ii-q iii-s iv-p','i-r ii-q iii-p iv-s','i-q ii-r iii-p iv-s','NEW','11-020','11-014','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/20409q.png'),
(20410,'biology','Match List 1 with List 2 using below diagram (0211)','a-iv b-iii c-ii d-i','Dinoflagellates: They appear yellow, green, brown, blue or red depending on the main\n pigments present in their cells.\n Euglenoids: Instead of a cell wall, they have a protein rich layer called pellicle which makes\n their body flexible.\n Slime Moulds: Under suitable conditions, they form an aggregation called plasmodium which\n may grow and spread over several feet.\n Paramoecium is a protozoan. All protozoans are heterotrophs and live as predators or\n parasites. They are believed to be primitive relatives of animals.\n diagram (e0211)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'2','mcq','a-iv b-iii c-ii d-i','a-iv b-iii c-i d-ii','a-iii b-iv c-i d-ii','a-iv b-ii c-iii d-i','NEW','11-021','11-015','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/20410q.png'),
(20411,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the organism shown in the image\ni. These organisms are mostly marine and photosynthetic.\n\nii. They appear yellow, green, brown, blue or red depending on the main pigments present in their cells.\n\niii. The cell wall has stiff cellulose plates on the inner surface.\n\niv. Most of them have a single flagella.\n\n Which of the above statements are true? diagram (0212)','i,ii','Dinoflagellates- These organisms are mostly marine and photosynthetic. They appear\n yellow, green, brown, blue or red depending on the main pigments present in their cells.\n The cell wall has stiff cellulose plates on the outer surface. Most of them have two flagella;\n one lies longitudinally and the other transversely in a furrow between the wall plates.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'2','mcq','i,ii,iii','i,ii','iii,iv','ii,iii,iv','NEW','11-021','11-015','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/20411q.png'),
(20412,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the organism shown in the diagram below:  \n\ni. Majority of them are freshwater organisms found in stagnant water. \n\nii. Instead of a cell wall, they have a protein rich layer called pellicle which makes their body flexible. \n\niii. They are photosynthetic. \n\niv. They behave like heterotrophs by predating on other smaller organisms. \n\nv. They have pigments which are identical to those present in higher plants. \n\nWhich of the above statements are false? (0213)','None of the above','Majority of them are fresh water organisms found in stagnant water. Instead of a cell wall, they have a protein rich layer called pellicle which makes their body flexible. They have two flagella, a short and a long one. Though they are photosynthetic in the presence of sunlight, when deprived of sunlight they behave like heterotrophs by predating on other smaller organisms. Interestingly, the pigments of euglenoids are identical to those present in higher plants.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'2','mcq','Only iv','Only iii','i and v','None of the above','NEW','11-021','11-015','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/20412q.png'),
(20413,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the organism shown in the image below:\n\n i. These are aquatic.\n\n ii. Actively moving organisms.\n\n iii. They have a cavity (gullet) that opens to the inside of the cell surface.\n\n iv. The coordinated movement of rows of cilia causes the water laden with food to be steered into the gullet.\n\n Choose the correct option diagram (0214)','Only iii is false.','Ciliated protozoans: These are aquatic, actively moving organisms because of the\n presence of thousands of cilia. They have a cavity (gullet) that opens to the outside of the\n cell surface. The coordinated movement of rows of cilia causes the water laden with food to\n be steered into the gullet. Example: Paramoecium','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'1','mcq','All the statements are true.','Only iii is false.','Only iv is true.','All the statements are false','NEW','11-021','11-015','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/20413q.png'),
(20414,'biology','Match List 1 with List 2: using below diagram (0215)','i-r ii-q iii-p iv-s','Amoeboid protozoans: They move and capture their prey by putting out pseudopodia\n (false feet) as in Amoeba.\n Flagellated protozoans: The parasitic forms cause diseases such as sleeping sickness.\n Example: Trypanosoma.\n Ciliated protozoans: they have a cavity (gullet) that opens to the outside of the cell surface.\n The coordinated movement of rows of cilia causes the water laden with food to be steered\n into the gullet.\n Sporozoans: This includes diverse organisms that have an infectious spore-like stage in\n their life cycle. The most notorious is Plasmodium (malarial parasite) which causes malaria','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'2','mcq','i-r ii-q iii-p iv-s','i-s ii-q iii-p iv-r','i-q ii-r iii-p iv-s','i-s ii-p iii-r iv-q','NEW','11-021','11-015','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/20414q.png'),
(20415,'biology','Which of the following is a saprophytic prostistan','Slime moulds','Slime moulds are saprophytic feeding on dead and decaying leaves. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 21; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'2','mcq','Chrysophytes','Euglenoids','Slime moulds','Dinoflagellates','NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(20416,'biology','Which are the chief producers of oceans','Diatoms','Diatoms are planktons which produces abundant of food material for the heterotrophs to feed in the oceans (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 20; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'2','mcq','Diatoms','Euglena','Slime moulds','Bacteria','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(20417,'biology','During favourable conditions slime moulds grow and spread over several feet called as','Plasmodium','Plasmodium is aggregation of several slime moulds (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 21; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'2','mcq','Group','Drone','Plasmodium','Slime','NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(20418,'biology','Which group of organisms forms a connecting link between plants and animals?','Euglenoids','Euglenoids are generally photosynthetic but when deprived of sunlight they become heterotrophic.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'3','fillup','Eu,gl,en,oi,ds',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(20419,'biology','Which of the following are considered as ancestors of kingdom fungi','Slime moulds','Slime moulds are considered as the ancestors of kingdom fungi as the are unicellular saprophytes','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'2','mcq','Euglenoids','Dinoflagellates','Slime moulds','Protozoans','NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL);
