(20424,'biology','Which among the following Protista behave as photosynthetic in presence of sunlight and heterotrophs in absence of sunlight','Euglena','Euglena Though they are photosynthetic in the presence of sunlight, when deprived of sunlight they behave like heterotrophs by predating on other smaller organisms(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 21; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'3','mcq','Gonyaulax','Euglena','Desmids','Amoeba','NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(20425,'biology','The following which don\'t hold true for slime moulds','During favourable conditions, the plasmodium differentiates and forms fruiting bodies','During unfavourable conditions, the plasmodium differentiates and forms fruiting bodies bearing spores at their tips. The spores possess true walls. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 21; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'2','mcq','The spores are dispersed by air currents.','Spores on fruiting bodies possess true walls. They are extremely resistant and survive for many years','body moves along decaying twigs and leaves engulfing organic material','During favourable conditions, the plasmodium differentiates and forms fruiting bodies','NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(20426,'biology','Being gritty ______ containing soil is used in polishing, filtration of oils and syrups.','Diatoms','Diatoms have left behind large amount of cell wall deposits in their habitat; this accumulation over billions of years is referred to as ‘diatomaceous earth’. Being gritty this soil is used in polishing, filtration of oils and syrups. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 20; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'2','fillup','Di,at,o,ms',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(20427,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n i. Chrysophytes are found in fresh water as well as in marine environments.\n\n ii. Most of the Dinoflagellates have two flagella; one lies longitudinally and the other transversely in a furrow between the wall plates.\n\n iii. Euglenoids have two flagella, a short and a long one.\n\n iv. All protozoans are heterotrophs and live as predators or parasites.\n How many of the above statements are false?','None of the above','All the statements are true.\nThey are found in fresh water as well as in marine environments.\nMost of them have two flagella; one lies longitudinally and the other transversely in a furrow between the wall plates\nAll protozoans are heterotrophs and live as predators or parasites. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 20 and 21)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','None of the above','NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(40319,'biology','Identify the organism shown in diagram (0420)','Nereis','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 52; Figure 4.11(a) )','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Platyhelminthes,Aschelminthes,Annelida','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Nereis','Leech','Earthworm','Ascaris','NEW','11-052','11-043','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40319q.png'),
(40320,'biology','Identify the organism shown in diagram (0421)','Leech','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 52; Figure 4.11(b) )','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Platyhelminthes,Aschelminthes,Annelida','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Nereis','Leech','Earthworm','Ascaris','NEW','11-052','11-043','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40320q.png'),
(20428,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding slime moulds:\n i. The body moves along fresh twigs and leaves engulfing organic material.\n\n ii. Under suitable conditions, they form an aggregation called plasmodium which may grow and spread over several feet.\n\n iii. During unfavourable conditions, the plasmodium differentiates and forms fruiting bodies bearing spores at their sides laterally branching.\n\n iv. The spores possess true walls.\n\n v. The spores are dispersed by air currents.\n\n How many of the above statements are false?','2','Only i and iii are false.\nThe body moves along decaying twigs and leaves engulfing organic material. Under suitable conditions, they form an aggregation called plasmodium which may grow and spread over several feet. During unfavourable conditions, the plasmodium differentiates and forms fruiting bodies bearing spores at their tips. The spores possess true walls. They are extremely resistant and survive for many years, even under adverse conditions. The spores are dispersed by air currents. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 21; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','None of the above','NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(20429,'biology','__________ includes diverse organisms that have an infectious spore-like stage\n in their life cycle.','Sporozoans','Sporozoans include diverse organisms that have an infectious spore-like stage in their life cycle. The most notorious is Plasmodium (malarial parasite) which causes malaria, a disease which has a staggering effect on human population.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 22; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'2','fillup','S,po,roz,oa,ns',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(20430,'biology','________ causes a disease which has a staggering effect on human population.','Plasmodium','Plasmodium (malarial parasite) causes malaria, a disease which has a staggering effect on the human population. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 22; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'2','mcq','Entamoeba','Trypanosoma','Paramoecium','Plasmodium','NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(20431,'biology','Flagellated protozoans are:','Both Free-living and Parasitic','Flagellated protozoans: The members of this group are either free-living or parasitic. They have flagella. The parasitic forms cause diaseases such\n as sleeping sickness. Example: Trypanosoma. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 22; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Protista',NULL,'2','mcq','Free-living','Parasitic','Both Free-living and Parasitic','Sessile','NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(20501,'chemistry','Select the correct statements:\n \n (i) Difference between the isobars is due to the presence of different number of neucleons present in the nucleus.\n \n (ii) Isobars are the atoms with same mass number but different atomic number.\n \n (iii) Atoms with identical atomic number but different atomic mass number are known as Isotones.\n \n (iv) Isobars are the atoms with same atomic number but different mass number.\n \n (v) Difference between the isotopes is due to the presence of different number of neutrons present in the nucleus.\n \n (vi) Atoms with identical atomic number but different atomic mass number are known as Isotopes.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(ii), (v), (vi) only','"Isobars are the atoms with same mass number but different atomic number. For example, ¹⁴C (Z = 6) and ¹⁴N (Z = 7)."\n"Atoms with identical atomic number but different atomic mass number are known as Isotopes. It is evident that difference between the isotopes is due to the presence of different number of neutrons present in the nucleus. For example, hydrogen (H, Z = 1) has three isotopes: the one containing only one proton is called protium (¹H), the one containing 1 proton and 1 neutron is called deuterium (²D) and the other one possessing 1 proton and 2 neutrons is called tritium (³T)."\n"Other examples of commonly occuring isotopes are: carbon atoms (C, Z = 6) containing 6, 7 and 8 neutrons besides 6 protons (¹²C, ¹³C, ¹⁴C); chlorine atoms (Cl, Z = 17) containing 18 and 20 neutrons besides 17 protons (³⁵Cl, ³⁷Cl)."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.35; Paragraph 5','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Isobars and Isotopes',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv), (v), (vi)','(ii), (iii), (v), (vi) only','(v), (vi) only','(ii), (v), (vi) only','NEW','11-035','11-035','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/20501q.png'),
(20502,'chemistry','Select the correct statement(s):\n \n (a) 99.985% of hydrogen atoms contain only one proton called protium (¹H).\n \n (b) Isotopes containing 1 proton and 1 neutron is called deuterium (²D, 0.015%).\n \n (c) Isotopes containing 1 proton and 2 neutrons is called tritium (³T).\n \n (d) Tritium is found in trace amounts on the earth.','All of the above','"For example, considering of hydrogen element (Z = 1), 99.985% of hydrogen atoms contain only one proton. This isotope is called protium (¹H). Rest of the percentage of hydrogen atom contains two other isotopes, the one containing 1 proton and 1 neutron is called deuterium (²D, 0.015%) and the other one possessing 1 proton and 2 neutrons is called tritium (³T). The latter isotope is found in trace amounts on the earth."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.35; Paragraph 5','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Isobars and Isotopes',NULL,'3','mcq','(a), (b), (c), only','(b) only','(c), (d) only','All of the above','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(20503,'chemistry','Select the correct statement(s):\n \n (a) Carbon atoms contain 6, 7 and 8 neutrons besides 6 protons (¹²C, ¹³C, ¹⁴C).\n \n (b) Chlorine atoms containing 18 and 20 neutrons besides 18 protons (³⁵Cl, ³⁷Cl).\n \n (c) The composition of any atom can be represented by using the normal element symbol (X) with super-script on the left hand side as the atomic mass number (A) and subscript (Z) on the left hand side as the atomic number.\n \n (d) Number of neutrons present in the nucleus have very high effect on the chemical properties of an element.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (a), (c) are correct','"The composition of any atom can be represented by using the normal element symbol (X) with super-script on the left hand side as the atomic mass number (A) and subscript (Z) on the left hand side as the atomic number."\n"Other examples of commonly occuring isotopes are: carbon atoms (C, Z = 6) containing 6, 7 and 8 neutrons besides 6 protons (¹²C, ¹³C, ¹⁴C); chlorine atoms (Cl, Z = 17) containing 18 and 20 neutrons besides 17 protons (³⁵Cl, ³⁷Cl). An important point to mention regarding isotopes is that chemical properties of atoms are controlled by the number of electrons, which are determined by the number of protons in the nucleus. Number of neutrons present in the nucleus have very little effect on the chemical properties of an element"\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.35; Paragraph 4, 5 ,6','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Isobars and Isotopes',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (a), (b) are correct','Only (b) is correct','Only (a), (c) are correct','All (a), (b), (c), (d) are correct','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(20504,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion (A): All the isotopes of a given element show same chemical behaviour.\n \n Reason (R):Chemical properties of atoms are controlled by the number of electrons.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"An important point to mention regarding isotopes is that chemical properties of atoms are controlled by the number of electrons, which are determined by the number of protons in the nucleus. \n\nNumber of neutrons present in the nucleus have very little effect on the chemical properties of an element. \n\nTherefore, all the isotopes of a given element show same chemical behaviour."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.35; Paragraph 6','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Isobars and Isotopes',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-035','11-035','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/20504q.png'),
(20505,'chemistry','Select the correct statement(s) from the following options:\n \n (a) Chemical properties of atoms are controlled by the number of electrons.\n \n (b) Electrons are determined by the number of protons in the nucleus.\n \n (c) Number of neutrons present in the nucleus have very little effect on the chemical properties of an element.\n \n (d) All the isotopes of a given element show different chemical behaviour.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the given below:','Only (a), (b), (c) are correct','"An important point to mention regarding isotopes is that chemical properties of atoms are controlled by the number of electrons, which are determined by the number of protons in the nucleus. \n\nNumber of neutrons present in the nucleus have very little effect on the chemical properties of an element. \n\nTherefore, all the isotopes of a given element show same chemical behaviour."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.35; Paragraph 6','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Isobars and Isotopes',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (a), (b), (c) are correct','Only (b), (c), (d) are correct','Only (d) is correct','Only (c), (d) are correct','NEW','11-035','11-035','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/20505q.png'),
(20506,'chemistry','Isobars are atoms with same _______.','Mass number','"Isobars are the atoms with same mass number but different atomic number. \n\nFor example, ¹⁴C (Z = 6) and ¹⁴N (Z = 7)."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.35; Paragraph 5','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Isobars and Isotopes',NULL,'1','mcq','Atomic number','Mass number','Number of protons','Number of neutrons','NEW','11-035','11-035','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/20506q.png'),
(20507,'chemistry','Calculate the number of protons, neutrons and electrons in ⁸⁰₃₅Br.','35, 45, 35','"In this case, ⁸⁰₃₅Br, Z = 35, A = 80, species is neutral.\n\nNumber of protons = number of electrons = Z = 35\n\nNumber of neutrons = 80 – 35 = 45."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.36; Problem 2.1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Isobars and Isotopes',NULL,'2','mcq','35, 35, 45','35, 35, 35','35, 45, 35','45, 35, 35','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(20508,'chemistry','The number of electrons, protons and neutrons in a species are equal to 18, 16 and 16 respectively. \n \n Assign the proper symbol to the species.','³²₁₆S²⁻','"The atomic number is equal to number of protons = 16. \n\nThe element is sulphur (S). \n\nAtomic mass number = number of protons + number of neutrons = 16 + 16 = 32. \n\nSpecies is not neutral as the number of protons is not equal to electrons. \n \nIt is anion (negatively charged) with charge equal to excess electrons = 18 – 16 = 2. \n \nSymbol is ³²₁₆S²⁻.\n\nNote: Before using the notation for chemical symbol, find out whether the species is a neutral atom, a cation or an anion. If it is a neutral atom, the equation is valid, i.e., number of protons = number of electrons = atomic number. If the species is an ion, determine whether the number of protons are larger (cation, positive ion) or smaller (anion, negative ion) than the number of electrons. Number of neutrons is always given by A - Z, whether the species is neutral or ion."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.36; Problem 2.2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Isobars and Isotopes',NULL,'3','mcq','³²₁₆S⁰','³³₁₆S²⁻','³²₁₆S²⁻','³¹₁₆S¹⁻','NEW','11-036','11-036',NULL),
(20509,'biology','Sleeping sickness is caused by______','Trypanosoma','Flaggelated Protozoan Causes sleeping sickness Ex-Trypanosoma (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page 22)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Protozoans',NULL,'1','fillup','Tr,yp,a,no,so,ma',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(20510,'biology','Match The Following','Amoebic protozoan-Amoeba, Flagellated protozoan-Trypanosoma, Ciliated protozoan-Paramoecium, Sporozoan-Plasmodium','Amoebic protozoan-Amoeba, \nFlagellated protozoan-Trypanosoma, \nCiliated protozoan-Paramoecium, \nSporozoan-Plasmodium\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 22)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Protozoans',NULL,'1','match','Amoebic protozoan,Amoeba','Flagellated protozoan,Trypanosoma','Ciliated protozoan,Paramoecium','Sporozoan,Plasmodium','NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
