(20512,'biology','Marine forms having silica shells on their surface is characteristic of following prostita','Amoeboid protozoans','Marine forms of amoeboid protozoans have silica shells on their surface. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 22; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Protozoans',NULL,'2','mcq','Ciliated protozoans','Dinoflagellates','Amoeboid protozoans','Halophiles','NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(20513,'biology','Which among the following is false about ciliated protozoans','Cavity (gullet) opens to the inside of the cell surface.','Ciliated protozoans have a cavity (gullet) that opens to the outside of the cell surface. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 22; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Protozoans',NULL,'3','mcq','Coordinated movement of rows of cilia causes the water laden with food to be steered into the gullet','Cavity (gullet) opens to the inside of the cell surface.','Actively moving organisms because of the presence of thousands of cilia.','These are Aquatic Protistans','NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(40321,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the organisms shown in the image below: \n\ni. (a) is Nereis and (b) is Hirudinaria.\n\nii. Neural system consists of paired ganglia.\n\niii. Double ventral nerve cord is present.\n\niv. They possess longitudinal and circular muscles which help in locomotion.\n\nWhich of the above statements are true for diagram ?(0456)','All the statements are true.','They possess longitudinal and circular muscles which help in locomotion. Neural system consists of paired ganglia (sing. ganglion) connected by lateral nerves to a double ventral nerve cord. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 52; paragraph 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Platyhelminthes,Aschelminthes,Annelida','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','i,ii,iv','ii,iii,iv','i,ii,iii','All the statements are true.','NEW','11-052','11-043','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40321q.png'),
(20601,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion (A): Rutherford\'s nuclear model of an atom is like a small scale solar system with the nucleus playing the role of the sun and the electrons being similar to the lighter planets.\n \n Reason (R): Classical mechanics, when applied to the solar system, shows that the planets describe well-defined orbits around the sun.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"Rutherford\'s nuclear model of an atom is like a small scale solar system with the nucleus playing the role of the massive sun and the electrons being similar to the lighter planets. \n\nWhen classical mechanics* is applied to the solar system, it shows that the planets describe well-defined orbits around the sun."\n\n"* Classical mechanics is a theoretical science based on Newton’s laws of motion. It specifies the laws of motion of macroscopic objects."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.36; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Drawbacks of Rutherford Model',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-036','11-036',NULL),
(20602,'chemistry','Select the correct statement(s): \n \n (a) The gravitational force between the planets is given by the expression (Gm₁m₂/r²).\n \n (b) The coulomb force between electron and the nucleus is given by the expression (Gm₁m₂/r²).\n \n (c) The similarity between the solar system and nuclear model suggests that electrons should move around the nucleus in well defined orbits.\n \n (d) The coulomb force between electron and the nucleus is mathematically dissimilar to the gravitational force.\n \n (e) An electron in the nuclear model describing planet like orbits is under acceleration.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (a), (c), (e) are correct','"The gravitational force between the planets is given by the expression (Gm₁m₂/r²), where m₁ and m₂ are the masses, r is the distance of separation of the masses and G is the gravitational constant. \n\nThe theory can also calculate precisely the planetary orbits and these are in agreement with the experimental measurements.\n\nThe similarity between the solar system and nuclear model suggests that electrons should move around the nucleus in well defined orbits. \n\nFurther, the coulomb force (kq₁q₂/r²), where q₁ and q₂ are the charges, r is the distance of separation of the charges and k is the proportionality constant) between electron and the nucleus is mathematically similar to the gravitational force. \n\nHowever, when a body is moving in an orbit, it undergoes acceleration even if it is moving with a constant speed in an orbit because of changing direction. \n\nSo an electron in the nuclear model describing planet like orbits is under acceleration."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.36; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Drawbacks of Rutherford Model',NULL,'3','mcq','All (a), (b), (c), (d), (e) are correct','Only (a), (b), (d) are correct','Only (b), (c) are correct','Only (a), (c), (e) are correct','NEW','11-036','11-036','/ncert/pyq/biology/fillup/20602q.png'),
(20603,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion (A): An electron in an orbit emits electromagnetic radiation.\n \n Reason (R): According to the electromagnetic theory of Maxwell, charged particles when accelerated should emit electromagnetic radiation.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"According to the electromagnetic theory of Maxwell, charged particles when accelerated should emit electromagnetic radiation. \n\nTherefore, an electron in an orbit will emit radiation, the energy carried by radiation comes from electronic motion."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.36; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Drawbacks of Rutherford Model',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-036','11-036',NULL),
(20604,'chemistry','Select the incorrect statements:\n \n (a) Based on Rutherford\'s theory, since an electron in an orbit emits radiation, the energy carried by electromagnetic radiation comes from electronic motion. The orbit will thus continue to shrink.\n \n (b) Rutherford\'s nuclear atomic model explains the stability of an atom.\n \n (c) If the electrons were stationary, electrostatic attraction between the dense nucleus and the electrons would pull the electrons toward the nucleus to form a miniature version of Thomson’s model of atom.\n \n (d) Rutherford\'s nuclear atomic model explains the distribution of the electrons around the nucleus and their energies very well.','Only (b) and (d)','"According to the electromagnetic theory of Maxwell, charged particles when accelerated should emit electromagnetic radiation. \n\nTherefore, an electron in an orbit will emit radiation, the energy carried by radiation comes from electronic motion. The orbit will thus continue to shrink. \n\nCalculations show that it should take an electron only 10⁻⁸ s to spiral into the nucleus. But this does not happen. Thus, the Rutherford\'s model cannot explain the stability of an atom. \n\nIf the motion of an electron is described on the basis of the classical mechanics and electromagnetic theory, the question arises that since the motion of electrons in orbits is leading to the instability of the atom, then why not consider electrons as stationary around the nucleus. \n\nIf the electrons were stationary, electrostatic attraction between the dense nucleus and the electrons would pull the electrons toward the nucleus to form a miniature version of Thomson’s model of atom. \n\nAnother serious drawback of the Rutherford model is that it says nothing about distribution of the electrons around the nucleus and the energies of these electrons."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.36; Paragraph 2; Page no. 37; Paragraph 1, 2)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Drawbacks of Rutherford Model',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (a) and (c)','Only (b) and (c)','Only (b) and (d)','All are incorrect','NEW','11-037','11-037',NULL),
(20605,'biology','Network of hyphae is known as','Mycelium','fungi are filamentous. Their bodies consist of long, slender thread-like structures called hyphae. The network of hyphae is known as mycelium. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 22; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'1','fillup','My,ce,li,um',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-022','11-016','/ncert/pyq/biology/fillup/20605q.png'),
(20606,'biology','Dikaryophase is shown by','Both ascomycetes and basidiomycetes','In higher fungi, meiosis is delayed and occurs just before meiosis. Cells often have two nuclei or dikaryon (n+n) in the phase between plasmogamy and karyogamy.Such multinucleated cells are called dikaryotic cells.\nThe stage is known as dikaryophase which occurs in both ascomycetes and basidiomycetes.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 23; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'1','mcq','Ascomycetes','Basidiomycetes','Deuteromycetes','Both ascomycetes and basidiomycetes','NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(20607,'biology','The correct order in the sexual stages of fungi is','Plasmogamy > Karyogamy > Meiosis','The fusion of two protoplasm between two gametes is called plasmogamy. Fusion of two nuclei is called karyogamy. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 23; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'2','order','1,Plasmogamy','2,Karyogamy','3,Meiosis',NULL,'NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(20608,'biology','Sexual spores are','All of these','Sexual reproduction takes place through oospores,ascospores and basidiospores. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 23; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'2','mcq','Oospores','Ascospores','Basidiospores','All of these','NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(20609,'biology','Some hyphae are continuous tube with multinucleated cytoplasm called as_____ hyphae','Coenocytic','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 22; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'1','fillup','C,oe,no,cy,tic',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(20610,'biology','Yeast reproduces vegetatively by','Budding','Budding is the process of vegetative reproduction in which a small outgrowth from the body develops into a new individual. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 22; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'2','mcq','Fragmentation','Budding','Fission','Conjugation','NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
