(20626,'biology','Match List 1 with List 2:using below diagram (0228)','i-b ii-c iii-d iv-a','Members of phycomycetes are found in aquatic habitats and on decaying wood in moist and\n damp places or as obligate parasites on plants.\n Ascomycetes- They are saprophytic, decomposers, parasitic or coprophilous (growing on\n dung).\n Basidiomycetes- They grow in soil, on logs and tree stumps and in living plant bodies as\n parasites, e.g., rusts and smuts.\n Deuteromycetes- Some members are saprophytes or parasites while a large number of\n them are decomposers of litter and help in mineral cycling.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'2','mcq','i-d ii-c iii-b iv-a','i-b ii-c iii-d iv-a','-a ii-b iii-c iv-d','i-c ii-a iii-d iv-b','NEW','11-023','11-017','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/20626q.png'),
(40322,'biology','A feature that doesn\'t exist in annelids is?','Pseudocoelom','Annelids are the first eucoelomates which possess true coelom. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 49; Figure 4.4)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Platyhelminthes,Aschelminthes,Annelida','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Nephridia (osmoregulatory +excretory organ).','Lateral swimming appendages in aquatic forms like nereis','Longitudinal and circular muscles helping in locomotion','Pseudocoelom','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(20627,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Phycomycetes:\n i. The mycelium is aseptate and coenocytic.\n\n ii. Asexual reproduction takes place by spores which are endogenously produced in sporangium.\n\n iii. Sexual reproduction takes place by zoospores or by aplanospores.\n\n iv. A zygospore is formed by fusion of two gametes.\n Which of the above statements are false?','Only iii','Phycomycetes- The mycelium is aseptate and coenocytic. Asexual reproduction takes place by zoospores (motile) or by aplanospores (non-motile). These spores are endogenously produced in sporangium. A zygospore is formed by fusion of two gametes. \nThe asexual spores are generally not found, but vegetative reproduction by fragmentation is common. The sex organs are absent.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 23; Paragraph No. 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii','iii and iv','Only iii','None of the above.','NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(20628,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Ascomycetes:\n\n i. Commonly known as sac-fungi, the ascomycetes are mostly unicellular and rarely multicellular.\n\n ii. Mycelium is branched and aseptate.\n\n iii. Mycelium on germination produce conidia.\n\n iv. Many members like morels and truffles are edible.\n Which of the above statements are true?','Only iv','Ascomycetes- Commonly known as sac-fungi, the ascomycetes are mostly multicellular,\n e.g., Penicillium, or rarely unicellular, e.g., yeast (Saccharomyces). Mycelium is branched\n and septate. The asexual spores are conidia produced exogenously on the special mycelium\n called conidiophores. Conidia on germination produce mycelium. Many members like\n morels and truffles are edible and are considered delicacies.\n (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 23; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'2','mcq','ii,iii,iv','i,ii,iii','Only iv','All the statements are true.','NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(20629,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Basidiomycetes:\n i. The mycelium is branched and septate.\n\n ii. The asexual spores are generally not found.\n\n iii. The sex organs are absent.\n\n iv. Vegetative reproduction by fragmentation is common. \n\nWhich of the above statements are false?','None of the above','Basidiomycetes- The mycelium is branched and septate. The asexual spores are generally\n not found, but vegetative reproduction by fragmentation is common. The sex organs are\n absent, but plasmogamy is brought about by fusion of two vegetative or somatic cells of\n different strains or genotypes.\n (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 24; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'2','mcq','Only ii and iv','Only iii and iv','i,ii and iii','None of the above','NEW','11-024','11-018',NULL),
(20630,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Deuteromycetes:\n i. Only the asexual or vegetative phases of these fungi are known.\n\n ii. Once sexual stages of members of dueteromycetes were discovered they were often moved to ascomycetes and basidiomycetes.\n\n iii. The deuteromycetes reproduce only by asexual spores known as deuterospores.\n\n iv. The mycelium is aseptate and coenocytic.\n\n v. Most of the members are saprophytes or parasites while few of them are decomposers of litter and help in mineral cycling.\n\n Which of the above statements are true?','Only i and ii','Deuteromycetes- Commonly known as imperfect fungi because only the asexual or\n vegetative phases of these fungi are known. Once perfect (sexual) stages of members of\n deuteromycetes were discovered they were often moved to ascomycetes and\n basidiomycetes. The deuteromycetes reproduce only by asexual spores known as conidia.\n The mycelium is septate and branched. Some members are saprophytes or parasites\n while a large number of them are decomposers of litter and help in mineral cycling. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 24; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'2','mcq','Only i and ii','i,ii,iii,v','i,ii,iii','All the statements are true.','NEW','11-024','11-018',NULL),
(20631,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n A: Deuteromycetes are commonly known as imperfect fungi.\n\n\n R: Only the asexual or vegetative phases of deuteromycetes are known.','Both A and R are correct and R is a correct explanation of A','Deuteromycetes- Commonly known as imperfect fungi because only the asexual or vegetative phases of these fungi are known. When the sexual forms of these fungi were discovered they were moved into classes they rightly belong to. \n (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 24; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is a correct explanation of A','Both A and R are correct but R is not a correct explanation of A.','A is correct but R is incorrect.','Both A and R are incorrect.','NEW','11-024','11-018',NULL),
(20632,'biology','Match the following according to the asexual reproduction of phycomycetes','Zoospores-motile, Aplanospores-nonmotile, zygospores-formed by fusion of two gametes','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 23; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'2','match','Zoospores,motile','Aplanospores,nonmotile','zygospores, formed by fusion of two gametes',NULL,'NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(20633,'biology','Pick the correct statement of Ascomycetes','Saprophytic,decomposers,parasitic,coprophilous','Ascomycetes are saprophytic, decomposers, parasitic or coprophilous (growing on dung) (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 23 and 24)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'3','mcq','Saprophytic, decomposer, autotrophs','Heterotrophic,aquatic, obligate parasites','Chemosynthetic, decomposer,parasites','Saprophytic,decomposers,parasitic,coprophilous','NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(20634,'biology','White spots seen on mustard leaves are due to?','Fungus','White spots seen on mustard leaves are due to a parasitic fungus.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 22; Paragraph No. 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'2','mcq','Bacteria','Virus','Fungus','Protozoa','NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(20635,'biology','Match the following:','Yeast-used to make bread and beer,Puccinia-Wheat rust,Penicillium,source of antibiotics','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 22; Paragraph No. )4','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'2','match','Yeast,used to make bread and beer','Puccinia,Wheat rust','Penicillium,source of antibiotics',NULL,'NEW','11-022','11-016','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/20635q.png'),
(20636,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n i. Fungi are cosmopolitan and occur in air, water, soil and on animals and plants.\n\n ii. Fungi prefer to grow in warm and humid places.\n\n iii. When a fungus reproduces sexually, two haploid hyphae of compatible mating types come together and fuse.\n\n iv. In all fungi the fusion of two haploid cells immediately results in diploid cells (2n).\n How many of the above statements are false?','1','All the statements are true except iv.\n When a fungus reproduces sexually, two haploid hyphae of compatible mating types come\n together and fuse. In some fungi the fusion of two haploid cells immediately results in diploid\n cells (2n). However, in other fungi (ascomycetes and basidiomycetes), an intervening\n dikaryotic stage (n + n, i.e., two nuclei per cell) occurs; such a condition is called a dikaryon\n and the phase is called dikaryophase of fungus.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 23; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(20637,'biology','Which of the following forms the basis of division of the kingdom fungii into various classes?','All of the them','The morphology of the mycelium, mode of spore formation and fruiting bodies form the basis for the division of the kingdom into various classes.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 23; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'2','mcq','morphology of the mycelium','mode of spore formation','fruiting bodies','All of the them','NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(20638,'biology','Match the following:','Saprophytes-absorb soluble organic matter from dead substrates,Parasites-depend on living plants and animals,Lichens-live in association with algae,Mycorrhiza-live in association with roots of higher plants','Most fungi are heterotrophic and absorb soluble organic matter from\n dead substrates and hence are called saprophytes. Those that depend\n on living plants and animals are called parasites. They can also live as\n symbionts – in association with algae as lichens and with roots of higher\n plants as mycorrhiza.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 22; Paragraph No. 6)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'2','match','Saprophytes,absorb soluble organic matter from dead substrates','Parasites,depend on living plants and animals','Lichens,live in association with algae','Mycorrhiza,live in association with roots of higher plants','NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(20639,'biology','Penicillium and yeast belong to class ___________?','Ascomycetes','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 23; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'2','fillup','A,sc,om,yce,tes',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(20640,'biology','Mushrooms, bracket fungi or puffballs belong to class _________?','Basidiomycetes','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 24; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'2','fillup','Ba,si,dio,myc,et,es',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-024','11-018',NULL),
(20641,'biology','Arrange the following in the sequence in which they are formed in basidiomycetes:','Plasmogamy>Basidium formation >Karyogamy and meiosis>basidiospores production','Plasmogamy is brought about by fusion of two vegetative or somatic cells of different strains or genotypes. The resultant structure is dikaryotic which ultimately gives rise to basidium. Karyogamy and meiosis take place in the basidium producing four basidiospores. The basidiospores are exogenously produced on the basidium (pl.: basidia). The basidia are arranged in fruiting bodies called basidiocarps. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 24; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'3','mcq','Plasmogamy>Basidium formation >Karyogamy and meiosis>basidiospores production','Basidium>Karyogamy and meiosis>basidiospores>Plasmogamy','Karyogamy and meiosis>basidiospores>Plasmogamy>Basidium','Karyogamy and meiosis>Plasmogamy>Basidium>basidiospores','NEW','11-024','11-018',NULL),
(20642,'biology','The basidiospores are _________ produced on the basidium.','only exogenously','Plasmogamy is brought about by fusion of two vegetative or somatic cells of different strains or genotypes. The resultant structure is dikaryotic which ultimately gives rise to basidium. Karyogamy and meiosis take place in the basidium producing four basidiospores. The basidiospores are exogenously produced on the basidium (pl.: basidia). The basidia are arranged in fruiting bodies called basidiocarps. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 24; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'2','mcq','only exogenously','only endogenously','mostly exogenously but sometimes endogenously','mostly endogenously but sometimes exogenously','NEW','11-024','11-018',NULL),
(20643,'biology','The basidia are arranged in fruiting bodies called __________.','Basidiocarps','Plasmogamy is brought about by fusion of two vegetative or somatic cells of different strains or genotypes. The resultant structure is dikaryotic which ultimately gives rise to basidium. Karyogamy and meiosis take place in the basidium producing four basidiospores. The basidiospores are exogenously produced on the basidium (pl.: basidia). The basidia are arranged in fruiting bodies called basidiocarps. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 24; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'2','fillup','Ba,sid,ioc,arps',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-024','11-018',NULL),
(20644,'biology','When the sexual forms of _______ fungi were discovered they were moved into respective classes.','Deuteromycetes','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 24; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Kingdom Fungi',NULL,'2','mcq','Basidiomycetes','Deuteromycetes','Ascomycetes','Phycomycetes','NEW','11-024','11-018',NULL),
(20701,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion (A): Electromagnetic radiation possess dual nature. \n Reason (R): Electromagnetic radiations possess only wave like properties.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(A) is true but (R) is false.','"Further studies proved the dual character of the electromagnetic radiations which means that radiations possess both wave like and particle like properties."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.37; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Wave nature of electromagnetic radiations',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-037','11-037',NULL),
(20702,'chemistry','Select the correct statements: \n \n (a) Heated objects absorb and emit radiations known as thermal radiations. \n \n (b) Charged objects absorb and emit radiations known as thermal radiations. \n \n (c) Thermal radiations consist of electromagnetic waves of various frequencies or wavelengths. \n \n (d) Thermal radiations consist of electromagnetic waves of various frequencies but same wavelengths. \n \n (e) First active study of thermal radiation laws occured in the 1850’s. \n \n (f) The theory of electromagnetic waves and the emission of such waves by accelerating charged particles was developed in the early 1870’s by James Clerk Maxwell.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (a), (c), (e) and (f) are correct','"In the mid-nineteenth century, physicists actively studied absorption and emission of radiation by heated objects. These are called thermal radiations. \n\nIt is now a well-known fact that thermal radiations consist of electromagnetic waves of various frequencies or wavelengths. \n\nIt is based on a number of modern concepts, which were unknown in the mid-nineteenth century. \n\nFirst active study of thermal radiation laws occured in the 1850’s and the theory of electromagnetic waves and the emission of such waves by accelerating charged particles was developed in the early 1870’s by James Clerk Maxwell, which was experimentally confirmed later by Heinrich Hertz. "\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.37; Paragraph 5','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Wave nature of electromagnetic radiations',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (a), (c), (e) and (f) are correct','Only (a), (c), (d) and (f) are correct','Only (a), (b), (e) and (f) are correct','Only (b), (d), (e) and (f) are correct','NEW','11-037','11-037',NULL),
(20703,'chemistry','_______ in _______ gave a comprehensive explanation about the interaction between the charged bodies and the behaviour of electrical and magnetic fields on macroscopic level?','James Maxwell, 1870','"James Maxwell (1870) was the first to give a comprehensive explanation about the interaction between the charged bodies and the behaviour of electrical and magnetic fields on macroscopic level."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.37; Paragraph 6','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Wave nature of electromagnetic radiations',NULL,'2','mcq','James Maxwell, 1870','Neil Bohr, 1850','Rutherford, 1870','Heinrich Hertz,1850','NEW','11-037','11-037','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/20703q.png'),
(20704,'chemistry','When an electrically charged particle moves under accelaration, it produces or transmits waves/radiations called _______.','Electromagnetic radiations','"He (James Maxwell) suggested that when electrically charged particle moves under accelaration, alternating electrical and magnetic fields are produced and transmitted. \n\nThese fields are transmitted in the forms of waves called electromagnetic waves or electromagnetic radiation."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.37; Paragraph 6','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Wave nature of electromagnetic radiations',NULL,'2','mcq','Electrostatic radiations','Nucleoomagnetic radiations','Electromagnetic radiations','Radioactive radiations','NEW','11-037','11-037','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/20704q.png'),
(20705,'chemistry','Select the correct statements:\n \n (a) Electrical and magnetic fields are transmitted by charged particles, moving under accelration, in the forms of waves called electromagnetic waves or electromagnetic radiation. \n \n (b) The electric and magnetic field components of an electromagnetic wave have the same wavelength, frequency, speed and amplitude.\n \n (c) The electric and magnetic field components of an electromagnetic wave vibrate in two parallel planes.\n \n (d) In earlier days (Newton) light was supposed to be made of particles (corpuscules).\n \n (e) It was only in the 19th century when particle nature of light was established.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (a), (b) and (d) are correct','"He (James Maxwell) suggested that when electrically charged particle moves under accelaration, alternating electrical and magnetic fields are produced and transmitted. These fields are transmitted in the forms of waves called electromagnetic waves or electromagnetic radiation."\n\n"In earlier days (Newton) light was supposed to be made of particles (corpuscules). It was only in the 19th century when wave nature of light was established."\n\n"Maxwell was again the first to reveal that light waves are associated with oscillating electric and magnetic character. These components have the same wavelength, frequency, speed and amplitude, but they vibrate in two mutually perpendicular planes."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.37; Paragraph 6, 7, 8; Fig 2.6)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Wave nature of electromagnetic radiations',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (a) and (b) are correct','Only (b) and (c) are correct','Only (a), (b) and (d) are correct','All (a), (b), (c), (d) and (e) are correct','NEW','11-037','11-037',NULL),
(20706,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion (A): Electromagnetic wave motion is complex in nature.\n \n Reason (R): The oscillating electric and magnetic fields produced by oscillating charged particles are perpendicular to each other and to the direction of propagation of the wave.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"Although electromagnetic wave motion is complex in nature, we will consider here only a few simple properties:\n\n→ The oscillating electric and magnetic fields produced by oscillating charged particles are perpendicular to each other and both are perpendicular to the direction of propagation of the wave."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.38; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Wave nature of electromagnetic radiations',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-038','11-038',NULL),
(20707,'chemistry','Select the correct statements regarding the properties of electromagnetic radiation:\n \n (a) These do not require medium to travel.\n \n (b) They can travel in vacuum.\n \n (c) Their wave motion is complex in nature.\n \n (d) The wave has two components; electric field and magnetic field, which are perpendicular to each other and to the direction of propagation of wave.\n \n (e) There are many types of electromagnetic radiations, which differ from one another in wavelength (or frequency), which constitute what is called electromagnetic spectrum.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','All (a), (b), (c), (d), (e)','"Although electromagnetic wave motion is complex in nature, we will consider here only a few simple properties:\n\n(i) The oscillating electric and magnetic fields produced by oscillating charged particles are perpendicular to each other and both are perpendicular to the direction of propagation of the wave.\n\n(ii) Unlike sound waves or waves produced in water, electromagnetic waves do not require medium and can move in vacuum.\n\n(iii) There are many types of electromagnetic radiations, which differ from one another in wavelength (or frequency). These constitute what is called electromagnetic spectrum."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.38; Paragraph 1, 2, 3)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Wave nature of electromagnetic radiations',NULL,'2','mcq','(a), (d), (e)','(a), (b), (d), (e)','(a), (b), (e)','All (a), (b), (c), (d), (e)','NEW','11-038','11-038',NULL),
(20708,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion (A): Electromagnetic spectrum constitute many types of electromagnetic radiations, which differ from one another in wavelength (or frequency). \n \n Reason (R): Different regions of the spectrum are identified by different names.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"There are many types of electromagnetic radiations, which differ from one another in wavelength (or frequency). These constitute what is called electromagnetic spectrum. Different regions of the spectrum are identified by different names."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.38; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Wave nature of electromagnetic radiations',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-038','11-038','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/20708q.png'),
(10802,'chemistry','X is the amount of a substance that contains as many particles as there are atoms in exactly 12g of the Y isotope. What are X and Y respectively?','A → One mole, \n B →¹²C','One mole is the amount of a substance that contains as many particles or entities as there are atoms in exactly 12 g (0.012 kg) of the ¹²C isotope.\n \n Thus, X and Y respectively are one mole and ¹²C.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Mole Concept And Molar Masses',NULL,'2','mcq','A → One mole, \n B → ¹⁴C','A → Percentage composition, \n B → ¹²C','A → One mole, \n B →¹²C','A → Percentage composition, \n B → ¹⁴C','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(20709,'chemistry','Select the correct statements:\n \n (a) Radio frequency region around 10⁶ Hz is used for radar.\n \n (b) Microwave region around 10¹⁰ Hz is used for broadcasting.\n \n (c) Infrared region around 10¹³ Hz is used for heating.\n \n (d) The small portion around 10¹⁵ Hz is called visible light.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (c) and (d) are correct','"Different regions of the spectrum are identified by different names. Some examples are: \n\n(a) radio frequency region around 10⁶ Hz, used for broadcasting; \n\n(b) microwave region around 10¹⁰ Hz, used for radar; \n\n(c) infrared region around 10¹³ Hz, used for heating; \n\n(d) ultraviolet region around 10¹⁶ Hz, a component of sun’s radiation;\n\n(e) the small portion around 10¹⁵ Hz, what is ordinarily called visible light."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.38; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Wave nature of electromagnetic radiations',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (a) and (b) are correct','Only (b) and (c) are correct','Only (c) and (d) are correct','Only (a), (c) and (d) are correct','NEW','11-038','11-038','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/20709q.png'),
(20710,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): Visible light is the only part which can be seen by our eyes.\n \n Reason(R): Different kinds of instruments are used to detect electromagnetic radiation\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','"The small portion around 10¹⁵ Hz, is what is ordinarily called visible light. It is only this part which our eyes can see (or detect). Special instruments are required to detect non-visible radiation."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.38; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Wave nature of electromagnetic radiations',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-038','11-038','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/20710q.png'),
(20711,'chemistry','The SI unit for frequency is _______.','Hertz','"The SI unit for frequency is Hertz."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.38; Paragraph 5','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Wave nature of electromagnetic radiations',NULL,'1','mcq','Hertz','Coulomb','Second','Meter','NEW','11-038','11-038',NULL),
(20712,'chemistry','The number of waves that pass a given point in one second is known as _______.','Frequency','"The number of waves that pass a given point in one second is known as frequency."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.38; Paragraph 5','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Wave nature of electromagnetic radiations',NULL,'1','mcq','Wave number','Time period','Frequency','Wavelength','NEW','11-038','11-038','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/20712q.png'),
(20713,'chemistry','The SI unit for wavelength is _______.','Meter','"Wavelength should have the units of length and as you know that the SI units of length is meter (m)."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.38; Paragraph 6','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Wave nature of electromagnetic radiations',NULL,'1','mcq','Meter','Hertz','Wavenumber','Angstrom','NEW','11-038','11-038',NULL),
(20714,'chemistry','In vaccum all types of electromagnetic radiations, regardless of wavelength, travel:\n \n (i) At the same speed\n \n (ii) With the velocity of 3.0 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹\n \n (iii) With the speed of light\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','All (i), (ii), (iii)','"In vaccum all types of electromagnetic radiations, regardless of wavelength, travel at the same speed, i.e., 3.0 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹. This is called speed of light and is given the symbol ‘c‘."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.38; Paragraph 7','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Wave nature of electromagnetic radiations',NULL,'2','mcq','(i)','(iii)','(i), (iii)','All (i), (ii), (iii)','NEW','11-038','11-038',NULL),
(20715,'chemistry','The number of wavelengths per unit length is known as _______.','Wave number','"The number of wavelengths per unit length is known as wave number."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.39; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Wave nature of electromagnetic radiations',NULL,'1','mcq','Wave number','Time period','Frequency','Wavelength','NEW','11-039','11-039',NULL),
(20716,'chemistry','The Vividh Bharati station of All India Radio, Delhi, broadcasts on a frequency of 1,368 kHz (kilo hertz). \n \n Calculate the wavelength of the electromagnetic radiation emitted by transmitter. Which part of the electromagnetic spectrum does it belong to?','219.3 m, Radiowave','"The wavelength, λ, is equal to c/ν, where c is the speed of electromagnetic radiation in vacuum and ν is the frequency. Substituting the given values, we have:\n\n  λ = c/v = 3.0 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹/1368 kHz = 219.3 m. \n\nThis is a characteristic radiowave wavelength."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.39; Problem 2.3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Wave nature of electromagnetic radiations',NULL,'3','mcq','219.3 m, Radiowave','280.9 m, Radiowave','298.0 m , Visible light','240.0 m , Infrared','NEW','11-039','11-039',NULL),
(20717,'chemistry','The wavelength range of the visible spectrum extends from violet (400 nm) to red (750 nm). \n \n Express these wavelengths in frequencies (Hz). (1nm = 10⁻⁹ m).','4.0 × 10¹⁴ to 7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz','"Frequency of violet light:\n  v = c/λ = 3.0 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹/ 400 x 10⁻⁹ m = 7.50 × 10¹⁴ Hz. \n\nFrequency of red light:\n  v = c/λ = 3.0 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹/ 750 x 10⁻⁹ m = 4.00 × 10¹⁴ Hz.\n\nThe range of visible spectrum is from 4.0 × 10¹⁴ to 7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz in terms of frequency units."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.39; Problem 2.4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Wave nature of electromagnetic radiations',NULL,'3','mcq','4.0 × 10¹² to 7.5 × 10¹² Hz','4.0 × 10¹⁴ to 7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz','4.0 × 10¹⁵ to 7.5 × 10¹⁵ Hz','4.0 × 10¹³ to 7.5 × 10¹³ Hz','NEW','11-039','11-039',NULL),
(20718,'chemistry','Calculate (a) wavenumber and (b) frequency of yellow radiation having wavelength 5800 Å.','1.724 x 10⁶ m⁻¹ and 5.172 x 10¹⁴ s⁻¹','"(a) Calculation of wavenumber:\n\nλ = 5800 Å = 5800 × 10⁻⁸ cm = 5800 × 10⁻¹⁰ m.\n\nWave number = 1/λ = 1/5800 x 10⁻¹⁰ = 1.724 x 10⁶ m⁻¹ = 1.724 x 10⁴ cm⁻¹.\n \n(b) Calculation of the frequency (ν):\n \nv = c/λ = 3.0 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹/ 5800 × 10⁻¹⁰ m = 5.172 x 10¹⁴ s⁻¹."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.39; Problem 2.5','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Wave nature of electromagnetic radiations',NULL,'3','mcq','1.724 x 10⁷ m⁻¹ and 5.172 x 10¹⁵ s⁻¹','1.724 x 10⁶ m⁻¹ and 5.172 x 10¹⁵ s⁻¹','1.724 x 10⁶ m⁻¹ and 5.172 x 10¹⁴ s⁻¹','1.724 x 10⁷ m⁻¹ and 5.172 x 10¹⁴ s⁻¹','NEW','11-039','11-039',NULL),
(20719,'biology','Viroids are','All the Above','Viroids are free RNA and lack the protein that is present in virus hence called as viroids. RNA of viroids have low molecular weight. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 27; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Acellular Organisms And Lichens',NULL,'3','mcq','Free RNA','Lacks protein coat','RNA with Low molecular weight','All the Above','NEW','11-027','11-021',NULL),
(20720,'biology','______are the agents which are similar in size to viruses and consists abnormal folded protein','Prions','Prions are the agents that has size similar to viruses and consisting of abnormally folded protein. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 27; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Acellular Organisms And Lichens',NULL,'1','mcq','Lichens','Prions','Viroids','Fungi','NEW','11-027','11-021',NULL),
(20721,'biology','In the five kingdom classification of Whittaker there is no mention of lichens and some acellular organisms like _______.','All of the them','In the five kingdom classification of Whittaker there is no mention of lichens and some acellular organisms like viruses, viroids and prions. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 25; Paragraph No. 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Acellular Organisms And Lichens',NULL,'2','mcq','Prions.','Viruses','Viroids','All of the them','NEW','11-025','11-019',NULL),
(20722,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason:\n Assertion: Viruses were found to be smaller than bacteria.\n\n\n Reason: Viruses passed through bacteria-proof filters.\n Choose the correct statement:','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the\n assertion.','Virus means venom or poisonous fluid. Dmitri Ivanowsky (1892) recognised certain microbes\n as causal organism of the mosaic disease of tobacco. These were found to be smaller than\n bacteria because they passed through bacteria-proof filters. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 26; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Acellular Organisms And Lichens',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the\n assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of\n the assertion.','Reason is true but assertion is false.','Assertion is true but reason is false.','NEW','11-026','11-020',NULL),
(20723,'biology','A virus is a _______?','Nucleoprotein','A virus is a nucleoprotein and the genetic material is infectious.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 26; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Acellular Organisms And Lichens',NULL,'2','mcq','DNA','RNA','Both 1 and 2','Nucleoprotein','NEW','11-026','11-020',NULL),
(20724,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n i. Viruses that infect animals have either single or double stranded RNA or double stranded DNA.\n\n ii. Bacterial viruses or bacteriophages (viruses that infect the bacteria) are usually double stranded DNA viruses.\n\n iii. Viruses that infect plants have single stranded RNA.\n How many of the above statements are false?','None of the above','All the statements are true.\n In general, viruses that infect plants have single stranded RNA and viruses that infect\n animals have either single or double stranded RNA or double stranded DNA. Bacterial\n viruses or bacteriophages (viruses that infect the bacteria) are usually double stranded DNA\n viruses.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 26; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Acellular Organisms And Lichens',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','None of the above','NEW','11-026','11-020',NULL),
(20725,'biology','Diseases like mumps, smallpox, herpes, AIDS and influenza are caused by?','Viruses','Viruses cause diseases like mumps, smallpox, herpes and influenza. AIDS in humans is\n also caused by a virus.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 26; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Acellular Organisms And Lichens',NULL,'2','mcq','Bacteria','Fungii','Viruses','Protozoans','NEW','11-026','11-020',NULL),
(20726,'biology','In plants, symptoms like mosaic formation, leaf rolling and curling, yellowing\n and vein clearing, dwarfing and stunted growth are indicative of:','Viral disease','In plants, the symptoms of viral infection can be mosaic formation, leaf rolling and curling,\n yellowing and vein clearing, dwarfing and stunted growth.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 26; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Acellular Organisms And Lichens',NULL,'2','mcq','Bacterial disease','Viral disease','Fungal disease','Deficiency of minerals in the soil','NEW','11-026','11-020',NULL),
(20727,'biology','Capsomeres of viruses are arranged in _________ geometric forms?','helical or polyhedral','The protein coat called capsid made of small subunits called capsomeres, protects the\n nucleic acid. These capsomeres are arranged in helical or polyhedral geometric forms.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 26; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Acellular Organisms And Lichens',NULL,'2','mcq','only helical','only polyhedral','helical or polyhedral','double helix','NEW','11-026','11-020',NULL),
(20728,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n i. The RNA of the viroid is of high molecular weight.\n\n ii. Cr–Jacob disease (CJD) in humans is caused by prions.\n\n iii. Lichens are symbiotic associations between algae and fungi.\n How many of the above statements are false?','1','Only statement i is false. The RNA of the viroid was of low molecular weight. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 27)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Acellular Organisms And Lichens',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','None of the above','NEW','11-027','11-021',NULL),
(20801,'chemistry','Which of the following experimental phenomenon can be explained by the wave nature of the electromagnetic radiation?\n \n (i) diffraction\n \n (ii) interference\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (i) and (ii)','"Some of the experimental phenomenon such as diffraction and interference can be explained by the wave nature of the electromagnetic radiation."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.39; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Particle Nature of Electromagnetic Radiation: Planck’s Quantum Theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Only (i)','Only (ii)','Both (i) and (ii)','Both of these are related to particle, not wave nature of electromagnetic radiation.','NEW','11-039','11-039',NULL),
(20802,'chemistry','Black body radiations and photoelectric effect _______ of electromagnetic radiations.','Support particle nature','"Following are some of the observations which could not be explained with the help of even the electromagentic theory of 19th century physics (known as classical physics):\n  \n(i) the nature of emission of radiation from hot bodies (black -body radiation)\n \n(ii) ejection of electrons from metal surface when radiation strikes it (photoelectric effect)"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.39; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Particle Nature of Electromagnetic Radiation: Planck’s Quantum Theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Support wave nature','Support particle nature','Support both particle and wave nature','Are irrelevent to the nature','NEW','11-039','11-039',NULL),
(20803,'chemistry','The explaination for phenomenon of the black body radiation was given by _______.','Max Planck','"The first concrete explanation for the phenomenon of the black body radiation was given by Max Planck in 1900."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.40; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Particle Nature of Electromagnetic Radiation: Planck’s Quantum Theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Max Planck','James Maxwell','Albert Einstein','Newton','NEW','11-039','11-039',NULL),
(20804,'chemistry','Select the correct statements:\n \n (a) Hot objects emit electromagnetic radiations over a wide range of wavelengths.\n \n (b) As the temperature is raised, a higher proportion of long wavelength (red light) is generated.\n \n (c) As the temperature is raised, a higher proportion of short wavelength (blue light) is generated.\n \n (d) Intensities of radiations of different wavelengths emitted by hot body depend upon its temperature.\n \n (e) Intensities of radiations of different wavelengths emitted by hot body depend upon the mass of hot body.\n \n (f) Objects made of different material and kept at different temperatures emit different amount of radiation.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (a), (c), (d), (f) are correct','"Black Body Phenomenon:\n\nHot objects emit electromagnetic radiations over a wide range of wavelengths. At high temperatures, an appreciable proportion of radiation is in the visible region of the spectrum. As the temperature is raised, a higher proportion of short wavelength (blue light) is generated. \n\nFor example, when an iron rod is heated in a furnace, it first turns to dull red and then progressively becomes more and more red as the temperature increases. As this is heated further, the radiation emitted becomes white and then becomes blue as the temperature becomes very high. This means that red radiation is most intense at a particular temperature and the blue radiation is more intense at another temperature. This means intensities of radiations of different wavelengths emitted by hot body depend upon its temperature. \n\nBy late 1850’s it was known that objects made of different material and kept at different temperatures emit different amount of radiation."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.40; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Particle Nature of Electromagnetic Radiation: Planck’s Quantum Theory',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (a), (c), (d), (f) are correct','Only (a) is correct','Only (b), (c), (f) are correct','Only (a), (e) are correct','NEW','11-040','11-040',NULL),
(20805,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): When an iron rod is heated in a furnace, it first turns to dull red and then progressively becomes more and more red as the temperature increases. As this is heated further, the radiation emitted becomes white and then becomes blue as the temperature becomes very high.\n \n Reason(R): Intensities of radiations of different wavelengths emitted by hot body depend upon its temperature. As the temperature increases, more intense redaiations begin to emit.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"Black Body Phenomenon:\n\nAt high temperatures, an appreciable proportion of radiation is in the visible region of the spectrum. As the temperature is raised, a higher proportion of short wavelength (blue light) is generated. \n\nFor example, when an iron rod is heated in a furnace, it first turns to dull red and then progressively becomes more and more red as the temperature increases. As this is heated further, the radiation emitted becomes white and then becomes blue as the temperature becomes very high. This means that red radiation is most intense at a particular temperature and the blue radiation is more intense at another temperature. This means intensities of radiations of different wavelengths emitted by hot body depend upon its temperature."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.40; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Particle Nature of Electromagnetic Radiation: Planck’s Quantum Theory',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-040','11-040',NULL),
(20806,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): When the surface of an object is irradiated with light, a part of radiant energy is generally reflected as such, a part is absorbed and a part of it is transmitted. \n \n Reason(R): Ordinary objects are as a rule imperfect absorbers of radiation.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"When the surface of an object is irradiated with light (electromagnetic radiation), a part of radiant energy is generally reflected as such, a part is absorbed and a part of it is transmitted. \n\nThe reason for incomplete absorption is that ordinary objects are as a rule imperfect absorbers of radiation."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.40; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Particle Nature of Electromagnetic Radiation: Planck’s Quantum Theory',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-040','11-040',NULL),
(20807,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): A black body emits and absorbs radiations of all frequencies uniformly. \n \n Reason(R): Black body by definition is an ideal body that emits and absorbs radiations of all frequencies uniformly. If it does not do this, it is not a black body.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"An ideal body, which emits and absorbs radiations of all frequencies uniformly, is called a black body and the radiation emitted by such a body is called black body radiation."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.40; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Particle Nature of Electromagnetic Radiation: Planck’s Quantum Theory',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-040','11-040',NULL),
(40323,'biology','True regarding Annelids \n\ni)Are aquatic (marine, freshwater), terrestrial, freeliving and sometimes parasite. \n\nii)Have neural system which consists of paired ganglia connected by lateral nerves to double ventral nerve cord. \n\niii)Non~segmented.','Only i and ii','Annelids may be aquatic (marine and fresh water) or terrestrial; free-living, and sometimes parasiticNeural system consists of paired ganglia (sing. ganglion) connected by lateral nerves to a double ventral nerve cord. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 52; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Platyhelminthes,Aschelminthes,Annelida','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Only i and ii','Only ii and iii','Only i and iii','i, ii and iii','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(20808,'chemistry','Select the correct statement:\n \n (a) In practice, black body exists.\n \n (b) In practice, no such body exists like a black body.\n \n (c) Carbon black approximates fairly closely to black body.\n \n (d) A good physical approximation to a black body is a cavity with a tiny hole, which has no other opening.\n \n (e) Any ray entering the hole will be reflected by the cavity walls and will be eventually emmitted out of the walls.\n \n (f) A black body is also a perfect radiator of radiant energy.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (b), (c), (d) and (f) are correct','"In practice, no such body (as black body) exists. Carbon black approximates fairly closely to black body. A good physical approximation to a black body is a cavity with a tiny hole, which has no other opening. Any ray entering the hole will be reflected by the cavity walls and will be eventually absorbed by the walls. A black body is also a perfect radiator of radiant energy."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.40; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Particle Nature of Electromagnetic Radiation: Planck’s Quantum Theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Only (a), (c), (d) and (e) are correct','Only (b), (d) and (f) are correct','Only (b), (c), (d) and (f) are correct','Only (b) and (c) are correct','NEW','11-040','11-040',NULL),
(20809,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): A black body is in thermal equilibrium with its surroundings.\n \n Reason(R): It radiates same amount of energy per unit area as it absorbs from its surrounding in any given time.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"A black body is in thermal equilibrium with its surroundings. It radiates same amount of energy per unit area as it absorbs from its surrounding in any given time."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.40; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Particle Nature of Electromagnetic Radiation: Planck’s Quantum Theory',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-040','11-040',NULL),
(20810,'chemistry','Intensity of radiation emitted from a black body and its spectral distribution depends only on its _______.','Temperature','"The amount of light emitted (intensity of radiation) from a black body and its spectral distribution depends only on its temperature."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.40; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Particle Nature of Electromagnetic Radiation: Planck’s Quantum Theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Temperature','Wavelength','Thickness','Wave number','NEW','11-040','11-040',NULL),
(20811,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct regarding Plank theory?\n \n a) Absorption and emmission of radiation arises from oscillators i.e., atoms in the wall of black body. \n \n b) The oscillation frequency of these oscillators is changed by interaction with oscillators of electromagnetic radiation.\n \n c) Planck assumed that radiation could be sub-divided into discrete chunks of energy.\n \n d) Atoms and molecules could emit or absorb energy only in discrete quantities and not in a continuous manner.\n \n e) The name quantum was given to the smallest quantity of energy that can be emitted or absorbed in the form of electromagnetic radiation.\n \n f) The energy (E) of a quantum of radiation is proportional to its frequency (ν).\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','All (a), (b), (c), (d), (e) and (f) are correct','"Max Planck arrived at a satisfactory relationship by making an assumption that absorption and emmission of radiation arises from oscillator i.e., atoms in the wall of black body. Their frequency of oscillation is changed by interaction with oscilators of electromagnetic radiation. Planck assumed that radiation could be sub-divided into discrete chunks of energy. He suggested that atoms and molecules could emit or absorb energy only in discrete quantities and not in a continuous manner. He gave the name quantum to the smallest quantity of energy that can be emitted or absorbed in the form of electromagnetic radiation. \n\nThe energy (E) of a quantum of radiation is proportional to its frequency (ν) and is expressed by equation: \n\nE = hν."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.41; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Particle Nature of Electromagnetic Radiation: Planck’s Quantum Theory',NULL,'3','mcq','All (a), (b), (c), (d), (e) and (f) are correct','(a), (c), (d), and (f) are correct','(b), (c), and (e) are correct','(a), (b), (c), and (f) are correct','NEW','11-041','11-041',NULL),
(20812,'biology','One of the major components of cell wall of most fungi is [NEET 2016]','Chitin','Fungal cell wall contains chitin or fungal cellulose along with other polysaccharides, proteins, lipids and a number of other substances.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 22; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Cellulose','Hemicellulose','Chitin','Peptidoglycan','NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(20813,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): The distribution of intensity in the radiation from black body is a function of frequency or wavelength at different temperatures.\n \n Reason(R): Quantum is smallest quantity of energy that can be emitted or absorbed in the form of electromagnetic radiation.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','"He gave the name quantum to the smallest quantity of energy that can be emitted or absorbed in the form of electromagnetic radiation."\n\n"With the help of Planck\'s theory, Planck was able to explain the distribution of intensity in the radiation from black body as a function of frequency or wavelength at different temperatures."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.41; Paragraph 1, 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Particle Nature of Electromagnetic Radiation: Planck’s Quantum Theory',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-041','11-041',NULL),
(40401,'chemistry','Kossel and lewis theory explains:','Chemical bonding','All elements want to reach stability by attaining octet configuration. \n\nAccording to Kossel and Lewis rule, elements reach octet configuration by either by transfer of valence electrons from one atom to another by forming ionic bond or by sharing of valence electrons by formation of covalent bonds. \n\nThis is known as octet rule. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Octet rule',NULL,'1','mcq','Chemical bonding','Metallic bonding','Hydrogen bonding','Vander Waal forces','NEW','11-102','11-102',NULL),
(20814,'chemistry','Which of the following observations which could not be explained with the help of classical physics?\n \n (i) The two waves of the same or different frequencies combine to give a wave whose distribution at each point in space is the algebraic or vector sum of disturbances at that point resulting from each interfering wave (interference).\n \n (ii) Ejection of electrons from metal surface when radiation strikes it (photoelectric effect).\n \n (iii) Variation of heat capacity of solids as a function of temperature.\n \n (iv) The electromagnetic wave, when strikes an obstacle, bends around its corners (diffraction).\n \n (v) Line spectra of atoms with special reference to hydrogen.\n \n (vi) The nature of emission of radiation from hot bodies (black-body radiation).\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(ii), (iii), (v), (vi) only','"Some of the experimental phenomenon such as diffraction (the bending of wave around an obstacle) and interference (the combination of two waves of the same or different frequencies to give a wave whose distribution at each point in space is the algebraic or vector sum of disturbances at that point resulting from each interfering wave) can be explained by the wave nature of the electromagnetic radiation. \n\nHowever, following are some of the observations which could not be explained with the help of even the electromagentic theory of 19th century physics (known as classical physics): \n\n(i) The nature of emission of radiation from hot bodies (black -body radiation).\n\n(ii) Ejection of electrons from metal surface when radiation strikes it (photoelectric effect). \n\n(iii) Variation of heat capacity of solids as a function of temperature. \n\n(iv) Line spectra of atoms with special Reference to hydrogen. These phenomena indicate that the system can take energy only in discrete amounts (a concept of quantum physics)."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.39, 40 ; Paragraph 2, 1)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Particle Nature of Electromagnetic Radiation: Planck’s Quantum Theory',NULL,'2','mcq','(iii), (v) only','(i), (iii), (vi) only','(i), (v), (vi) only','(ii), (iii), (v), (vi) only','NEW','11-041','11-041',NULL),
(20815,'biology','The imperfect fungi which are decomposers of litter and help in mineral cycling belong to [NEET 2015]','Deuteromycetes','Deuteromycetes are the imperfect fungi which include all those fungi in which sexual stage is either absent or not known. Some members are saprophytes or parasites while a large number of them are decomposers of litter and help in mineral cycling. E.g., Colletotrichum, Helminthosporium etc.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 24; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Phycomycetes','Ascomycetes','Deuteromycetes','Basidiomycetes','NEW','11-024','11-018',NULL),
(20816,'biology','The structures that help bacteria to attach to rocks and/or host tissues are [NEET 2015]','Fimbriae','The fimbriae are small bristle like fibres sprouting out of the cell. In some bacteria, they are known to help attach the bacteria to rocks in streams and also to the host tissues.\nReference   Class 11 NCERT, Chapter Cell The Unit of Life, Page 129.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Mesosomes','Holdfast','Rhizoids','Fimbriae','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20817,'biology','Select the wrong statement. [NEET 2015]','The viroids were discovered by D.J.Ivanowsky.','In 1971, T.O. Diener discovered a new infectious agent that was smaller than viruses and caused potato spindle tuber disease. It was found to be a free RNA; it lacked the protein coat that is found in viruses, hence the name viroid. The RNA of the viroid was of low molecular weight.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','The term ‘contagium vivum fluidum’ was coined by M. W. Beijerinck.','Mosaic disease in tobacco and aids in human being are caused by viruses.','The viroids were discovered by D.J.Ivanowsky.','M.W Stanley showed that viruses could be crystallised','NEW','11-027','11-021',NULL),
(20818,'biology','In which group of organisms the cell walls form two thin overlapping shells which fit together? [NEET 2015]','Chrysophytes','Chrysophytes include diatoms and desmids. The body of diatoms is covered by a transparent siliceous shell (silica deposited in cell wall) known as frustule. The frustule is made of two valves, epitheca and hypotheca, which fit together like a soap box.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 20; Paragraph No. 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Dinoflagellates','Slime moulds','Chrysophytes','Euglenoids','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(20819,'biology','Pick up the wrong statement. [NEET 2015]','Nuclear membrane is present in monera.','Kingdom Monera consists of prokaryotic organisms, characterised by absence of nuclear envelope around nucleus and absence of membranebound cell organelles.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 17; Table 2.1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Some fungi are edible.','Nuclear membrane is present in monera.','Cell wall is absent in animalia.','Protists have photosynthetic and heterotrophic modes of nutrition','NEW','11-017','11-011',NULL),
(20820,'biology','Choose the wrong statement. [NEET 2015]','Morels and truffles are poisonous mushrooms.','Morels are Ascomycetes with edible ascocarps that have fleshy spongelike conical cap or pileus and a stalk like stipe, e.g., Morchella esculenta. Truffles are also edible members of Ascomycetes with tuberlike subterranean ascocarps that are often dug out with the help of trained dogs and pigs, e.g., Tuber aestivum.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 24; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Morels and truffles are poisonous mushrooms.','Yeast is unicellular and useful in fermentation.','Penicillium is multicellular and produces antibiotics.','Neurospora is used in the study of biochemical genetics','NEW','11-024','11-018',NULL),
(20821,'biology','Cell wall is absent in [NEET 2015]','Mycoplasma','Mycoplasma (KingdomMonera) are the simplest and smallest free living prokaryotes which are devoid of a cell wall. Plasma membrane forms the outer boundary of the cell of mycoplasma. Nostoc is a cyanobacterium (KingdomMonera), in which cell wall comprises of peptidoglycans. Aspergillus is a fungus (KingdomFungi) in which cell wall is mainly made of chitin. Funaria is a bryophyte (KingdomPlantae) in which cell wall is cellulosic in nature.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 20; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Mycoplasma','Nostoc','Aspergillus','Funaria','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(20822,'biology','True nucleus is absent in [NEET 2015 cancelled]','Anabaena','Anabaena is a prokaryotic organism. It is a cyanobacteria (blue green algae) which belongs to Kingdom Monera. Like all other prokaryotes, it lacks a true nucleus and other cell organelles.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 19; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Vaucheria','Volvox','Anabaena','Mucor','NEW','11-019','11-013',NULL),
(20823,'biology','Five kingdom system of classification suggested by R.H. Whittaker is not based on\n[NEET 2014]','Presence or absence of well defined nucleus','R.H. Whittaker considered complexity of cell structure and structural (body) organisation, mode of nutrition, ecological life style and phylogenetic relationships for the five kingdom system of classification.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 17; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Presence or absence of well defined nucleus','Reproduction','Mode of nutrition','Cell structure and body organisation','NEW','11-017','11-011',NULL),
(20824,'biology','Which of the following shows coiled RNA strand and capsomeres? [NEET 2014]','Tobacco mosaic virus','Tobacco mosaic virus is a RNA virus that causes tobacco mosaic disease. It has single stranded coiled RNA molecule as its genetic material a part of which hangs outside the protein coat. Protein coat consists of approximately (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 26; Figure 2.6)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Polio virus','Tobacco mosaic virus','Measles virus','Retrovirus','NEW','11-026','11-020',NULL),
(20825,'biology','Viruses have [NEET 2014]','DNA enclosed in a protein coat','Viruses are nucleoprotein entities which are able to utilize synthetic machinery of a living cell of the host organism for its multiplication which does not involve growth and division. They have either RNA or DNA as genetic material and a protein coat.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 25; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','DNA enclosed in a protein coat','Prokaryotic nucleus','Single chromosome','Both DNA and rna','NEW','11-025','11-019',NULL),
(20826,'biology','Archaebacteria differ from eubacteria in [NEET 2014]','Cell membrane structure','The archaebacteria are the ‘ancient’ bacteria that include extremophiles like methanogens, halophiles and thermophiles. They represent some of the most ancient of life forms that persist today.They have both eubacterial and eukaryotic characters besides the features unique to them. Their mode of reproduction, nutrition and cell shape and size resembles a typical eubacteria. Their cell walls are made of a variety of polymers, but do not contain peptidoglycan unlike eubacteria. Lipids of their cytoplasmic membranes are ether linked unlike eubacteria which contain glycerol ester lipids in their cell membrane.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Cell membrane structure','Mode of nutrition','Cell shape','Mode of reproduction','NEW','11-019','11-013',NULL),
(20827,'biology','Which structures perform the function of mitochondria in bacteria? [NEET 2014]','Mesosomes','Mesosome is a characteristic circular to villiform specialisation of bacterial cell membrane that develops as an ingrowth. It consists of vesicles, tubules and lamellae. Mesosomes may be septal or lateral. Septal mesosome connects nucleoid with plasma membrane and assists in replication and septum formation during cells division. Lateral mesosome is not connected with nucleoid and contains respiratory enzymes and performs functions similar to eukarytoic mitochondria and hence is also called chondrioid. They also increase the surface area of plasma membrane and enzymatic contact.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Nucleoid','Ribosomes','Cell wall','Mesosomes','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20828,'biology','The motile bacteria are able to move by [NEET 2014]','Flagella','Flagellum is the organ of motility in bacteria. Bacterial flagella are unistranded, equivalent to a single microtubular fibre and formed of protein called flagellin. They perform rotatory movements.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Fimbriae','Flagella','Cilia','Pili','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20829,'biology','Anoxygenic photosynthesis is characteristic of [NEET 2014]','Rhodospirillum','In Rhodospirillum, electron donor is organic compound instead of water hence no oxygen is released, i.e., anoxygenic photosynthesis occurs. In other plants water is used as electron donor and H + and O','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Rhodospirillum','Spirogyra','Chlamydomonas','Ulva','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20830,'biology','Which of the following are likely to be present in deep sea water? [NEET 2013]','Archaebacteria','Archaebacteria belong to a group of prokaryotic organisms called Monera. These include the methanogens, which produce methane; the thermoacidophilic bacteria, which live in extremely hot and acidic environments (such as hot springs); and the halophilic bacteria, which can only function at high salt concentrations and are abundant in the world’s oceans.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 19; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Blue-green algae','Saprophytic fungi','Archaebacteria','Eubacteria','NEW','11-019','11-013',NULL),
(20831,'biology','Pigment containing membranous extensions in some cyanobacteria are [NEET 2013]','Chromatophores','Chromatophore is a pigmented lamellar or vesicular structure that can be isolated from disrupted photosynthetic bacteria or cyanobacteria. Their plasma membrane may be projected in olds into the cytoplasm forming lamellae that have, therefore, double unit membrane structure. The pigments and most of the enzymes required for the light induced electron transport and phosphorylation processes of photosynthesis, are located in the plasma membrane and lamellae.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Pneumatophores','Chromatophores','Heterocysts','Basal bodies','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20832,'biology','Why is a capsule advantageous to a bacterium? [Karnataka NEET 2013]','It allows bacterium to “hide” from host’s immune system.','Stype bacteria or virulent bacteria are capsulated. The capsule is made up of polysaccharides and amino acids. It is a tough and thick mucilage covering. It gives protection to bacteria against host’s immune system.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','It protects the bacterium from desiccation.','It provides means of locomotion.','It allows bacterium to “hide” from host’s immune system.','It allows the bacterium to attach to the surface','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20833,'biology','Which one of the following is true for fungi? [Karnataka NEET 2013]','They are heterotrophs.','Fungi are achlorophyllous, heterotrophic, spore forming, nonvascular, eukaryotic organismswhich often contain chitin or fungal cellulose in their walls. Hence, their cell wall is rigid. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 22; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','They lack a rigid cell wall.','They are heterotrophs.','They lack nuclear membrane.','They are phagotrophs','NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(20834,'biology','Which statement is wrong for viruses? [NEET 2012]','All of them have helical symmetry.','Viruses are the microorganisms which injects a living host and remain as intercellular parasite. They inject their genetic material which replicates using host cell machinery and reform their structure and attain viable structure capable enough to affect other cells. They have ability to synthesize nucleic acids by replication. Antibiotics do not acts against virus as they are made up of polymorphic protein coat. Viruses have different type of symmetry like icosahedral and helical','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','All are parasites.','All of them have helical symmetry.','They have ability to synthesize nucleic acids and proteins.','Antibiotics have no effect on them','NEW','11-026','11-020',NULL),
(20835,'biology','Maximum nutritional diversity is found in the group [NEET 2012]','Monera','Though the bacterial structure is very simple, they are very complex in behaviour. Compared to many other organisms, bacteria as a group show the most extensive metabolic diversity. Some of the bacteria are autotrophic, i.e., they synthesize their own food from inorganic substrates. They may be photosynthetic autotrophic or chemosynthetic autotrophic. The vast majority of bacteria are heterotrophs, i.e., they do not synthesize their own food but depend on other organisms or on dead organic matter for food.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 19; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Fungi','Animalia','Monera','Plantae','NEW','11-019','11-013',NULL),
(20836,'biology','Nuclear membrane is absent in [NEET 2012]','Nostoc','Penicillium and Agaricus are fungi while Volvox is an alga. All three are eukaryotes thus have a membrane bound nucleus. Nostoc is a cyanobacterium, i.e., prokaryote, so it lacks true nucleus, thus nuclear membrane is absent.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 19; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Penicillium','Agaricus','Volvox','Nostoc','NEW','11-019','11-013',NULL),
(20837,'biology','The cyanobacteria are also referred to as [NEET 2012]','Blue green algae','Cyanobacteria is a phylum consisting of two groups of photosynthetic eubacteria: the bluegreen bacteria (formerly known as bluegreen algae, or cyanophyta), which comprise the vast majority of members, and the grassgreen bacteria, or chloroxybacteria.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 19; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Protists','Golden algae','Slime moulds','Blue green algae','NEW','11-019','11-013',NULL),
(20838,'biology','The most abundant prokaryotes helpful to humans in making curd from milk and in production of antibiotics are the ones categorised as [NEET 2012]','Heterotrophic bacteria','Maximum number of antibiotics are produced by mycelial bacteria known as actinomycetes and most of the actinomycetes are saprotrophic (heterotrophic). Lactic acid bacteria that are used in preparation of curd are also heterotrophic ones.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Cyanobacteria','Archaebacteria','Chemosynthetic autotrophs','Heterotrophic bacteria','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20839,'biology','Which one single organism or the pair of organisms is correctly assigned to its or their named taxonomic group? [NEET 2012]','Yeast used in making bread and beer is a fungus.','Yeast is a group of unicellular fungi of the class ascomycetes. They occur as single cell or as a group or chain of cells. Yeast of the genus Saccharomyces ferments sugar and are used to make bread and beer.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 23; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Paramecium and plasmodium belong to the same kingdom as that of penicillium.','Lichen is a composite organism formed from the symbiotic association of an algae and a protozoan.','Yeast used in making bread and beer is a fungus.','Nostoc and anabaena are examples ofprotista','NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(20840,'biology','Which one of the following microbes forms symbiotic association with plants and helps them in their nutrition? [NEET 2012]','Glomus','Azotobacter, Aspergillus and Trichoderma all are free living microbes that help plants in their nutrition. Glomus is a fungus that symbiotically forms endomycorrhiza that helps in absorption of nutrition specially phosphorus from soil.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Azotobacter','Aspergillus','Glomus','Trichoderma','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20841,'biology','In the five kingdom classification,Chlamydomonas and Chlorella have been included in \n[MAINS 2012]','Protista','In order to develop phylogenetic classification, R.H. Whittaker (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 18; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Protista','Algae','Plantae','Monera','NEW','11-018','11-012',NULL),
(20842,'biology','Which one of the following also acts as a catalyst in a bacterial cell? [NEET 2011]','23s rRNA','23s rRNA  acts as a catalyst in a bacterial cell.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','5s rRNA','snRNA','hnRNA','23s rRNA','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20843,'biology','In eubacteria, a cellular component that resembles eukaryotic cell is [NEET 2011]','Plasma membrane','Plasma membrane of eubacteria resembles plasma membrane of eukaryotic cell. But nucleus, ribosomes and cell wall are little different in eukaryotic cell in their structure and organization from eubacterial cell.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Plasma membrane','Nucleus','Ribosomes','Cell wall','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20844,'biology','Which one of the following organisms is not an eukaryote? [NEET 2011]','Escherichia coli','Escherichia coli (bacterium) is not an example of eukaryotic cell. It is a typical example of prokaryotic cell.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Paramecium caudatum','Escherichia coli','Euglena viridis','Amoeba proteus','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20845,'biology','Which one of the following is incorrectly matched? [NEET 2011]','Puccinia - smut','Rust is a group of parasitic fungi of the phylum Basidiomycota. Many of these species attack the leaves and stems of cereal crops. Pathogens of rust are Puccinia, Uromyces, Melampsora, Hemileia.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 24; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Root pressure - guttation','Puccinia - smut','Root - exarch protoxylem','Cassia - imbricate aestivation','NEW','11-024','11-018',NULL),
(20846,'biology','The pathogen microsporum responsible for ringworm disease in humans belongs to the same kingdom of organisms as that of [Mains 2011]','Rhizopus, a mould','The pathogen Microsporum is genus of Kingdom Fungi that causes diseases of skin andhair in humans and animals like dog, cat, monkey. Ringworm is caused by the dermatophyte fungispecies of Microsporum, Trichophyton and Epidermophyton. Rhizopus, a black bread mould belongs to group zygomycetes of Kingdom Fungi.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 23; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Taenia, a tapeworm','Wuchereria, a filarial worm','Rhizopus, a mould','Ascaris, a round worm','NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(20847,'biology','Virus envelope is known as [NEET 2010]','Capsid','The nucleic acid of a virus is surrounded by a protein coat called the capsid. The capsid is composed of protein subunits called capsomeres. In some viruses, the capsid is covered by an envelope, which usually consists of some combination of lipids, proteins and carbohydrates.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 26; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Capsid','Virion','Nucleoprotein','Core','NEW','11-026','11-020',NULL),
(20848,'biology','Single celled eukaryotes are included in [NEET 2010]','Protista','Protista include all unicellular and colonial eukaryotes except those of green and red algae. The protistan cells are typically eukaryotic having membrane bound organelles like mitochondria, chloroplasts, Golgi bodies, endoplasmic reticulum, nucleus etc. Protista is commonly known as kingdom of unicellular eukaryotes. Kingdom fungi contains achlorophyllous, spore producing, heterotrophic, multicellular or multinucleate eukaryotic organisms (unicellular yeasts are also included amongst fungi because their sexual reproduction is similar to that of some fungi). Monerans are basically unicellular prokaryotes. Archaea (ancient bacteria) are also a type of monerans which live in primitive environment like high temperature, high salt content, acidic pH, etc.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 20; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Protista','Fungi','Archaea','Monera','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(20849,'biology','Some hyper thermophilic organisms that grow in highly acidic (ph 2) habitats belong to the two groups [NEET 2010]','Eubacteria and archaea','There are two major groups of monerans archaebacteria (ancient bacteria) and eubacteria (true bacteria). Eubacteria is of further two types – bacteria and cyanobacteria. Thermoacidophiles are a type of archaebacteria which live in extremely acidic environment (pH(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 19; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Eubacteria and archaea','Cyanobacteria and diatoms','Protists and mosses','Liverworts and yeasts','NEW','11-019','11-013',NULL),
(20850,'biology','One of the free-living, anaerobic nitrogen-fixer is [NEET 2010]','Rhodospirillum','Many free living bacteria and blue green algae are capable to fix atmospheric nitrogen Rhodospirillum is a free living photosynthetic anaerobic nitrogen fixing nonsulphur bacteria. It is capable of synthesizing its organic food in presence of light and in absence of O','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Beijernickia','Rhodospirillum','Rhizobium','Azotobacter','NEW','11-019','11-013',NULL),
(20901,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement regarding photoelectric effect:\n \n (i) There is no time lag between the striking of light beam and the ejection of electrons from the metal surface.\n \n (ii) The number of electrons ejected is inversely proportional to the intensity or brightness of light.\n \n (iii) The electrons are ejected from the metal surface after sometime as the beam of light strikes the surface.\n \n (iv) The kinetic energies of electrons increase with the increase of frequency of the light used.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(ii), (iii) only','"The results observed in this (photoelectric effect) experiment were:\n\n(i) The electrons are ejected from the metal surface as soon as the beam of light strikes the surface, i.e., there is no time lag between the striking of light beam and the ejection of electrons from the metal surface.\n\n(ii) The number of electrons ejected is proportional to the intensity or brightness of light.\n\n(iii) For each metal, there is a characteristic minimum frequency, ν₀ (also known as threshold frequency), below which photoelectric effect is not observed. \n\nAt a frequency ν > ν₀, the ejected electrons come out with certain kinetic energy. The kinetic energies of these electrons increase with the increase of frequency of the light used."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.41; Paragraph 5','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Photoelectric effect',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii) only','(ii), (iii) only','(iii), (iv) only','(i), (ii), (iii) only','NEW','11-041','11-041',NULL),
(20902,'chemistry','In the photoelectric effect experiment, which of the following characteristics depend upon the brightness of light?\n \n (i) Number of electrons ejected.\n \n (ii) Kinetic energy associated with electrons.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(i) only','"All the above results (of photoelectric effect experiment) could not be explained on the basis of laws of classical physics. According to latter, the energy content of the beam of light depends upon the brightness of the light. In other words, number of electrons ejected and kinetic energy associated with them should depend on the brightness of light. It has been observed that though the number of electrons ejected does depend upon the brightness of light, the kinetic energy of the ejected electrons does not."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.41; Paragraph 6','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Photoelectric effect',NULL,'2','mcq','(i) only','(ii) only','Both (i) and (ii)','The options are irrelevant to the statement.','NEW','11-041','11-041',NULL),
(20903,'chemistry','Select the correct statements regarding photoelectric effect:\n \n (i) Einstein (1905) was able to explain the photoelectric effect using Planck’s quantum theory of electromagnetic radiation as a starting point.\n \n (ii) When a photon of sufficient energy strikes an electron, it transfers its energy instantaneously to the electron during the collision and the electron is ejected without any time lag or delay.\n \n (iii) Kinetic energy of the ejected electron is proportional to the frequency of the electromagnetic radiation.\n \n (iv) Kinetic energy of the ejected electron is inversely proportional to the frequency of the electromagnetic radiation.\n \n (v) If the striking photon has energy equal to hν and the minimum energy required to eject the electron is hν₀, then the difference in energy (hν – hν₀) is transferred as the kinetic energy of the photoelectron.\n \n (vi) If the striking photon has energy equal to hν and the minimum energy required to eject the electron is hν₀, then the sum of energies (hν + hν₀) is transferred as the kinetic energy of the photoelectron.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(i), (ii), (iii), (v) only','"Einstein (1905) was able to explain the photoelectric effect using Planck’s quantum theory of electromagnetic radiation as a starting point. Shining a beam of light on to a metal surface can, therefore, be viewed as shooting a beam of particles, the photons. When a photon of sufficient energy strikes an electron in the atom of the metal, it transfers its energy instantaneously to the electron during the collision and the electron is ejected without any time lag or delay. Greater the energy possessed by the photon, greater will be transfer of energy to the electron and greater the kinetic energy of the ejected electron. In other words, kinetic energy of the ejected electron is proportional to the frequency of the electromagnetic radiation. Since the striking photon has energy equal to hν and the minimum energy required to eject the electron is hν₀, then the difference in energy (hν – hν₀) is transferred as the kinetic energy of the photoelectron."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.42; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Photoelectric effect',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (iii), (iv), (vi) only','(i), (ii), (iii) only','(i), (ii), (iii), (v) only','(ii), (iii), (v) only','NEW','11-042','11-042',NULL),
