(21101,'chemistry','Calculate energy of one mole of photons of radiation whose frequency is 5 ×10¹⁴ Hz.','199.51 kJmol⁻¹','"Energy (E) of one photon is given by the expression:\n\nE = hν \n\nh = 6.626 ×10⁻³⁴ Js \n\nν = 5×10¹⁴ s⁻¹ (given) \n\nE = (6.626 ×10⁻³⁴ Js) × (5 ×10¹⁴ s⁻¹) = 3.313 ×10⁻¹⁹ J \n\nEnergy of one mole of photons = (3.313 ×10⁻¹⁹ J) × (6.022 × 10²³ mol⁻¹) = 199.51 kJmol⁻¹"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.43; Problem 2.6)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Problem',NULL,'2','mcq','109.51 kJmol⁻¹','199.51 kJmol⁻¹','190.51 kJmol⁻¹','299.51 kJmol⁻¹','NEW','11-043','11-043',NULL),
(21102,'chemistry','A 100 watt bulb emits monochromatic light of wavelength 400 nm.Calculate the number of photons emitted per second by the bulb.','2.012 x 10²⁰ s⁻¹','"Power of the bulb = 100 watt = 100 Js⁻¹\n\nEnergy of one photon, E = hν = hc/λ \n\nh = 6.626 ×10⁻³⁴ Js\n\nc = 3 x 10⁸ ms⁻¹\n\nE = 6.626 x 10⁻³⁴ Js x 3 x 10⁸ ms⁻¹/400 x 10⁻⁹ m = 4.969 × 10⁻¹⁹ J\n\nNumber of photons emitted = 100 Js⁻¹/4.969x10⁻¹⁹ J = 2.011 x 10²⁰ s⁻¹"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.43; Problem 2.7','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Problem',NULL,'2','mcq','2.012 x 10¹⁸ s⁻¹','2.012 x 10²¹ s⁻¹','2.012 x 10¹⁹ s⁻¹','2.012 x 10²⁰ s⁻¹','NEW','11-043','11-043',NULL),
(21103,'chemistry','When electromagnetic radiation of wavelength 300 nm falls on the surface of sodium, electrons are emitted with a kinetic energy of 1.68 ×10⁵ Jmol⁻¹.\n \n What is the minimum energy needed to remove an electron from sodium? What is the maximum wavelength that will cause a photoelectron to be emitted?','3.84 x 10⁻¹⁹ J and 517 nm','"The energy (E) of a 300 nm photon is given by,\n\n E = hν = hc/λ \n\n h = 6.626 ×10⁻³⁴ Js\n\n c = 3 x 10⁸ ms⁻¹\n\n E = 6.626 x 10⁻³⁴ Js x 3 x 10⁸ ms⁻¹/300 x 10⁻⁹ m = 6.626 × 10⁻¹⁹ J\n\n Energy of one mole of photons = (6.626 ×10⁻¹⁹ J) × (6.022 × 10²³ mol⁻¹) = 3.99 × 10⁵ Jmol⁻¹\n\n The minimum energy needed to remove one mole of electrons from sodium \n\n = (3.99 – 1.68) 10⁵ Jmol⁻¹ = 2.31 × 10⁵ Jmol⁻¹ \n\n The minimum energy for one electron \n\n = 2.31 × 10⁵ Jmol⁻¹/6.022 × 10²³ mol⁻¹ = 3.84 × 10⁻¹⁹ J\n\n This corresponds to the wavelength \n\n ∴ λ = hc/E = 6.626 x 10⁻³⁴ Js x 3 x 10⁸ ms⁻¹/3.84 × 10⁻¹⁹ J = 517 nm \n\n (This corresponds to green light)"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.43; Problem 2.8)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Problem',NULL,'3','mcq','3.84 x 10⁻¹⁹ J and 517 nm','3.84 x 10⁻¹⁸ J and 550 nm','3.84 x 10⁻¹⁹ J and 550 nm','3.84 x 10⁻¹⁸ J and 517 nm','NEW','11-043','11-043',NULL),
(21104,'chemistry','The threshold frequency ν₀ for a metal is 7.0 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹. Calculate the kinetic energy of an electron emitted when radiation of frequency ν = 1.0 × 10¹⁵ s⁻¹ hits the metal.','1.988 ×10⁻¹⁹ J','"According to Einstein’s equation\n\n Kinetic energy = 1/2 mₑv² = h(ν – ν₀) \n\n= (6.626 x 10⁻³⁴ Js) (1.0 × 10¹⁵ s⁻¹ – 7.0 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹)\n\n= (6.626 x 10⁻³⁴ Js) (10.0 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹ – 7.0 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹)\n \n= (6.626 x 10⁻³⁴ Js) × (3.0 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹) = 1.988 ×10⁻¹⁹ J"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.43; Problem 2.9','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Problem',NULL,'2','mcq','1.988 ×10⁻¹⁷ J','1.988 ×10⁻¹⁸ J','1.988 ×10⁻²⁰ J','1.988 ×10⁻¹⁹ J','NEW','11-043','11-043',NULL),
(21105,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Protista. Choose the correct option. \n1. We include Chrysophytes, Dinoflagellates, Euglenoids, Slime moulds and Protozoans under Protista.\n\n2. Members of Protista are primarily aquatic.\n\n3. All single-celled eukaryotes are placed under Protista, but the boundaries of this kingdom are well-defined.\n\n4. This kingdom forms a link with the others dealing with plants, animals and fungi.','1,2 and 4 are correct','All single-celled eukaryotes are placed under Protista, but the boundaries of this kingdom are not well defined. What may be ‘a photosynthetic protistan’ to one biologist may be ‘a plant’ to another. In this book we include Chrysophytes, Dinoflagellates, Euglenoids, Slime moulds and Protozoans under Protista. Members of Protista are primarily aquatic. This kingdom forms a link with the others dealing with plants, animals and fungi. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 20; Paragraph No. 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 4 are correct.','Only 1 is incorrect.','1,2 and 4 are correct','Both 1 and 2 are correct.','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(21106,'biology','Consider the following statements. Choose the correct option. \n1. Dinoflagellates include diatoms and golden algae (desmids).\n\n2. Chrysophytes appear yellow, green, brown, blue or red depending on the main pigments present in their cells.\n\n3. Euglenoids have a protein-rich layer called pellicle which makes their body flexible.\n\n4. Slime moulds move along growing twigs and leaves engulfing organic material.','Only 3 is correct','Chrysophytes includes diatoms and golden algae (desmids). Dinoflagellates appear yellow, green, brown, blue or red depending on the main pigments present in their cells. Euglenoids they have a protein rich layer called pellicle which makes their body flexible. Slime moulds moves along decaying twigs and leaves engulfing organic material. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 20-21; Section 2.2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are correct','Only 3 is correct','Both 1 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(21107,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Chrysophytes. Choose the correct option. \n1. Euglenoids are mostly marine but can also be freshwater organisms\n\n2. Chrysophytes are found in freshwater as well as in marine environments.\n\n3. Slime moulds move along growing twigs and leaves engulfing organic material.\n\n4. Dinoflagellate organisms are mostly marine.','Both 2 and 4 are correct','Chrysophytes are found in fresh water as well as in marine environments. Euglenoids are fresh water organisms found in stagnant water. Slime moulds moves along decaying twigs and leaves engulfing organic material. Dinoflagellates organisms are mostly marine. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 20-21; Section 2.2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Both 2 and 4 are correct','Both 1 and 4 are correct','2,3 and 4 are correct.','NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(21108,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Chrysophytes. Choose the correct option. \n1. In diatoms the cell walls form two thin overlapping shells, which fit together as in a soap box.\n\n2. The ‘diatomaceous earth’ soil is used in polishing, and filtration of oils, petrol, and syrups.\n\n3. The walls are embedded with silica and thus the walls are indestructible.\n\n4. Diatoms are the chief ‘decomposers’ in the oceans.','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','In diatoms the cell walls form two thin overlapping shells, which fit together as in a soap box. The walls are embedded with silica and thus the walls are indestructible. Thus, diatoms have left behind large amount of cell wall deposits in their habitat; this accumulation over billions of years is referred to as ‘diatomaceous earth’. Being gritty this soil is used in polishing, filtration of oils and syrups. Diatoms are the chief ‘producers’ in the oceans. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 20; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Both 2 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(21109,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Dinoflagellates. Choose the correct option. \n1. These organisms are mostly heterotrophic. \n\n2. The cell wall has stiff cellulose plates on the outer surface.\n\n3. Most of them have two flagella; one lies longitudinally and the other transversely in a furrow between the wall plates.\n\n4. Very often, red dinoflagellates (Example: Gonyaulax) undergo such rapid multiplication that they make the sea appear red (red tides).','2,3 and 4 are correct.','These organisms are mostly marine and photosynthetic. The cell wall has stiff cellulose plates on the outer surface. Most of them have two flagella; one lies longitudinally and the other transversely in a furrow between the wall plates. Red dinoflagellates (Example: Gonyaulax) undergo such rapid multiplication that they make the sea appear red (red tides).(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 21; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','2,3 and 4 are correct.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are correct.','NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(21110,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Euglenoids. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. Instead of a cell wall, they have a protein-rich layer called a pellicle which makes their body flexible.\n\n2. They have two flagella, and both are long. \n\n3. The pigments of euglenoids are identical to those present in primitive animals.\n\n4. Though they are photosynthetic in the presence of sunlight, when deprived of sunlight they behave like heterotrophs by predating on other smaller organisms.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Majority of them are fresh water organisms found in stagnant water. Instead of a cell wall, they have a protein rich layer called pellicle which makes their body flexible. They have two flagella, a short and a long one. Though they are photosynthetic in the presence of sunlight, when deprived of sunlight they behave like heterotrophs by predating on other smaller organisms. Interestingly, the pigments of euglenoids are identical to those present in higher plants. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 21; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are correct','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(21111,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Slime moulds. Choose the correct option. \n1. The body moves along decaying twigs and leaves engulfing organic material.\n\n2. Under unsuitable conditions, they form an aggregation called plasmodium which may grow and spread over several feet.\n\n3. Slime moulds are saprophytic protists.\n\n4. During favourable conditions, the plasmodium differentiates and forms fruiting bodies bearing spores at their tips.','Both 1 and 3 are correct.','Slime moulds are saprophytic protists. The body moves along decaying twigs and leaves engulfing organic material. Under suitable conditions, they form an aggregation called plasmodium which may grow and spread over several feet. During unfavourable conditions, the plasmodium differentiates and forms fruiting bodies bearing spores at their tips. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 21; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1,2 and 3 are correct','Both 1 and 3 are correct.','Both 1 and 2 are correct','1,3 and 4 are correct.','NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(21112,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding protozoans. Choose the correct option. \n1. Flagellated protozoans live in freshwater, seawater or moist soil.\n\n2. Sporozoans includes diverse organisms that have an infectious spore-like stage in their life cycle.\n\n3.  Ciliated protozoans are free-living or parasitic. \n\n4. Amoeboid protozoans are aquatic, actively moving organisms.','1,3 and 4 are incorrect.','Amoeboid protozoans live in fresh water, sea water or moist soil. Flagellated protozoans are either free-living or parasitic. Ciliated protozoans are aquatic, actively moving organisms because of the presence of thousands of cilia. Sporozoans includes diverse organisms that have an infectious spore-like stage in their life cycle.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 21-22; Paragraph No. 1 to 4 )','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1,3 and 4 are incorrect.','2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(21113,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding protozoans Choose the correct option. \n1. Entamoeba moves and captures their prey by putting out pseudopodia (true feet). \n\n2. The parasitic forms of flagellated protozoans cause diseases such as night sickness.\n\n3. The most notorious sporozoan is Plasmodium (malarial parasite) which causes malaria, a disease which has a staggering effect on the human population.\n\n4. Paramoecium has a cavity (gullet) that opens to the outside of the cell surface.','Both 3 and 4 are correct','Amoeba moves and captures their prey by putting out pseudopodia (false feet). The parasitic forms of flagellated protozoans cause diaseases such as sleeping sickness.The most notorious is sporozoan is Plasmodium (malarial parasite) which causes malaria, a disease which has a staggering effect on human population. Paramoecium have a cavity (gullet) that opens to the outside of the cell surface.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 21-22; section 2.2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 3 and 4 are correct','Both 1 and 2 are correct.','1,2 and 3 are correct','1,3 and 4 are correct.','NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(21114,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding fungi. Choose the correct option. \n1. The common mushroom we eat and toadstools are also fungi.\n\n2. White spots seen on mustard leaves are due to a parasitic fungus.\n\n3. Fungi cause diseases in plants eg; wheat rust-causing Puccinia\n\n4. Except for unicellular yeasts, fungi are filamentous.','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','The common mushroom you eat and toadstools are also fungi. White spots seen on mustard leaves are due to a parasitic fungus. Some unicellular fungi, e.g., yeast are used to make bread and beer. Other fungi cause diseases in plants and animals; wheat rust-causing Puccinia is an important example. Some are the source of antibiotics, e.g., Penicillium. With the exception of yeasts which are unicellular, fungi are filamentous. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 22; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1,2 and 3 are correct','1,3 and 4 are incorrect','Both 3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(21115,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding fungi. Choose the correct option. \n1. Their bodies consist of long, slender thread-like structures called hyphae. The network of hyphae is known as mycelium.\n\n2. Some hyphae are continuous tubes filled with multinucleated cytoplasm – these are called coenocytic hyphae.\n\n3. They can also live as symbionts – in association with algae as mycorrhiza and with roots of higher plants as lichens.\n\n4. Reproduction in fungi can take place by vegetative means – fragmentation, fission and budding.','Only 3 is incorrect','Their bodies consist of long, slender thread-like structures called hyphae. The network of hyphae is known as mycelium. Some hyphae are continuous tubes filled with multinucleated cytoplasm – these are called coenocytic hyphae. Others have septae or cross walls in their hyphae. They can also live as symbionts – in association with algae as lichens and with roots of higher plants as mycorrhiza. Reproduction in fungi can take place by vegetative means – fragmentation, fission and budding. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 22; Paragraph No. 6)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Only 3 is incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are correct','1, 3 and 4 are incorrect.','NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(21116,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding reproduction in fungi. Choose the correct option. \n1. Asexual reproduction is by spores called conidia or sporangiospores or zoospores. \n\n2. Sexual reproduction is by oospores, ascospores and basidiospores.\n\n3. Fusion of protoplasms between only two motile gametes called plasmogamy.\n\n4. Meiosis in zygotes results in diploid spores.','Both 1 and 2 are correct.','Asexual reproduction is by spores called conidia or sporangiospores or zoospores. Sexual reproduction is by oospores, ascospores and basidiospores. Fusion of protoplasms between two motile or non-motile gametes called plasmogamy. Meiosis in zygote results in haploid spores. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 23; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Both 1 and 2 are correct.','Both 3 and 4 are correct','1,2 and 3 are correct.','NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(40402,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nA: Beryllium do not follow octet rule.\n\nR: because of orbitals it occupy.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Every atom tends to gain 8 electrons in their valence shell. \n\nThis is called octet rule. \n\nThey achieve this by either sharing or transfering of electrons between atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Octet rule',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-102','11-102',NULL),
(21117,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding reproduction in fungi. Choose the correct option. \n1. When a fungus reproduces sexually, two haploid hyphae of compatible mating types come together and fuse.\n\n2. In some fungi the fusion of two haploid cells immediately results in diploid cells (2n).\n\n3. The fungi form fruiting bodies in which equational division occurs, leading to formation of diploid spores.\n\n4. However, in ascomycetes and basidiomycetes, an intervening dikaryotic stage (n + n, i.e., two nuclei per cell) occurs, such a condition is called a dikaryon.','1, 2 and 4 are correct.','When a fungus reproduces sexually, two haploid hyphae of compatible mating types come together and fuse. In some fungi the fusion of two haploid cells immediately results in diploid cells (2n). However, in other fungi (ascomycetes and basidiomycetes), an intervening dikaryotic stage (n + n, i.e., two nuclei per cell) occurs; such a condition is called a dikaryon and the phase is called dikaryophase of fungus. Later, the parental nuclei fuse and the cells become diploid. The fungi form fruiting bodies in which reduction division occurs, leading to formation of haploid spores. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 23; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 2 and 3 are correct','1, 2 and 4 are correct.','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Both 2 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(21118,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the mode of nutrition of fungi. Choose the correct option. \n1. Members of Phycomycetes are found in aquatic habitats and on decaying wood in moist and damp places or as obligate parasites on plants.\n\n2. Members of Ascomycetes are saprophytic, decomposers, parasitic or coprophilous (growing on dung).\n\n3. Members of Dueteromycetes grow in soil, on logs and tree stumps and in living plant bodies as parasites\n\n4. Some members of Basidiomycetes are saprophytes or parasites while a large number of them are decomposers of litter','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect.','Members of phycomycetes are found in aquatic habitats and on decaying wood in moist and damp places or as obligate parasites on plants. Members of Ascomycetes are saprophytic, decomposers, parasitic or coprophilous (growing on dung). Members of Basidiomycetes grow in soil, on logs and tree stumps and in living plant bodies as parasites. Some members of Dueteromycetes are saprophytes or parasites while a large number of them are decomposers of litter (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 23-24; Section: 2.3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','1 and 3 is incorrect.','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect.','only 2 is incorrect','NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(21119,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the structure of fungi. Choose the correct option. \n1. Mycelium of Mucor is aseptate and coenocytic.\n\n2. Mycelium of Alternaria is septate and branched.\n\n3. Mycelium of Aspergillus is branched and septate.\n\n4. Mycelium of Agaricus is branched and septate.','All 1,2,3 and 4 are correct','Mycelium of Mucor is aseptate and coenocytic. Mycelium of Alternaria is septate and branched. Mycelium of Aspergillus is is branched and septate. Mycelium of Agaricus is branched and septate.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 23-24; Section: 2.3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are correct.','All 1,2,3 and 4 are correct','2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(21120,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding asexual reproduction in fungi. Choose the correct option. \n1. In dueteromycetes, asexual spores are generally not found. \n\n2. In Claviceps, the asexual spores are conidia produced exogenously on the special mycelium called conidiophores. \n\n3. In rhizopus, asexual reproduction takes place by zoospores (motile) or by aplanospores (non-motile). \n\n4. In Colletotrichum, asexual reproduction takes place only by asexual spores known as conidia.','Only 1 is incorrect','In Basidiomycetes, the asexual spores are generally not found. In claviceps, the asexual spores are conidia produced exogenously on the special mycelium called conidiophores. In rhizopus, asexual reproduction takes place by zoospores (motile) or by aplanospores (non-motile). In Colletotrichum, asexual reproduction takes place only by asexual spores known as conidia. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 23-24; Section: 2.3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are correct.','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect.','only 2 is incorrect','NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(21121,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding sexual reproduction in fungi. Choose the correct option. \n1. In ustilago, karyogamy and meiosis take place in the basidium producing four basidiospores.\n\n2. In Albugo, a zygospore is formed by the fusion of two gametes.\n\n3. In Puccinia, Sexual spores are called ascospores which are produced endogenously in sac-like asci (singular ascus).\n\n4. When the sexual forms dueteromycetes, were discovered they were moved into classes they rightly belong to.','only 3 is incorrect','In ustilago, karyogamy and meiosis take place in the basidium producing four basidiospores. In Albugo, a zygospore is formed by fusion of two gametes. In Ascomycetes, Sexual spores are called ascospores which are produced endogenously in sac like asci (singular ascus). When the sexual forms dueteromycetes, were discovered they were moved into classes they rightly belong to.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 23-24; Section: 2.3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect.','1,2 and 4 are incorrect.','only 3 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect.','NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(21122,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding kingdom Plantae. Choose the correct option. \n1. Life cycle of plants has two distinct phases – the diploid sporophytic and the haploid gametophytic – that alternate with each other.\n\n2. A few members are partially heterotrophic such as the insectivorous plants or parasites. Bladderwort and Venus fly trap are examples of insectivorous plants and Cuscuta is a parasite.\n\n3. Kingdom Plantae includes all eukaryotic chlorophyll-containing organisms commonly called plants.\n\n4. The repetition of the two phases of the life cycle is called the repeatation of generation.','1,2 and 3 are correct','Kingdom Plantae includes all eukaryotic chlorophyll-containing organisms commonly called plants. A few members are partially heterotrophic such as the insectivorous plants or parasites. Bladderwort and Venus fly trap are examples of insectivorous plants and Cuscuta is a parasite.  Life cycle of plants has two distinct phases – the diploid sporophytic and the haploid gametophytic – that alternate with each other. The lengths of the haploid and diploid phases, and whether these phases are free– living or dependent on others, vary among different groups in plants. This phenomenon is called alternation of generation.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 25; paragraph no: 1 and 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are correct.','Both 1 and 4 are correct.','1,2 and 3 are correct','1,2 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-025','11-019',NULL),
(21123,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding kingdom Animalia. Choose the correct option. \n1. They follow a definite growth pattern and grow into adults that have a definite shape and size.\n\n2. They digest their food in an internal cavity and store food reserves as glycogen or fat.\n\n3. Their mode of nutrition is sporozoic – by ingestion of food.\n\n4. This kingdom is characterised by heterotrophic eukaryotic organisms that are multicellular and their cells lack cell walls.','1,2 and 4 are correct','This kingdom is characterised by heterotrophic eukaryotic organisms that are multicellular and their cells lack cell walls. They directly or indirectly depend on plants for food. They digest their food in an internal cavity and store food reserves as glycogen or fat. Their mode of nutrition is holozoic – by ingestion of food. They follow a definite growth pattern and grow into adults that have a definite shape and size. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 25; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 4 are correct.','Only 1 is incorrect.','1,2 and 4 are correct','Both 1 and 2 are correct.','NEW','11-025','11-019',NULL),
(21124,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding viruses. Choose the correct option. \n1. The name virus which means venom or poisonous fluid was given by Dmitri Ivanowsky (1892) recognised certain microbes as causal organisms of the mosaic disease of tomato.\n\n2. viruses are non-cellular organisms that are characterised by having an inert crystalline structure outside the living cell.\n\n3. M.W. Beijerinek (1935) showed that viruses could be crystallised and crystals consist largely of proteins.\n\n4. Once they infect a cell they take over the machinery of the host cell to replicate themselves, killing the host.','Both 2 and 4 are correct','The viruses are non-cellular organisms that are characterised by having an inert crystalline structure outside the living cell. Once they infect a cell they take over the machinery of the host cell to replicate themselves, killing the host. The name virus that means venom or poisonous fluid was given by Dmitri Ivanowsky (1892) recognised certain microbes as causal organism of the mosaic disease of tobacco. These were found to be smaller than bacteria because they passed through bacteria-proof filters. M.W. Beijerinek (1898) demonstrated that the extract of the infected plants of tobacco could cause infection in healthy plants and called the fluid as Contagium vivum fluidum (infectious living fluid). W.M. Stanley (1935) showed that viruses could be crystallised and crystals consist largely of proteins. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 25-26; Section: 2.6)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Both 2 and 4 are correct','Both 1 and 4 are correct','2,3 and 4 are correct.','NEW','11-025','11-019',NULL),
(21125,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the structure of the virus. Choose the correct option. \n1. A virus is a nucleoprotein and the genetic material is infectious.\n\n2. Some virus contains both RNA and DNA.\n\n3. The protein coat called capsid made of small subunits called capsomeres, protects the nucleic acid.\n\n4. In general, viruses that infect animals have single-stranded RNA and viruses that infect plants have either single or double-stranded RNA or double-stranded DNA.','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','In addition to proteins, viruses also contain genetic material, that could be either RNA or DNA. No virus contains both RNA and DNA. A virus is a nucleoprotein and the genetic material is infectious. In general, viruses that infect plants have single stranded RNA and viruses that infect animals have either single or double stranded RNA or double stranded DNA. Bacterial viruses or bacteriophages (viruses that infect the bacteria) are usually double stranded DNA viruses (Figure 2.6b). The protein coat called capsid made of small subunits called capsomeres, protects the nucleic acid. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 26; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Both 2 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-026','11-020',NULL),
(21126,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding viroids, prions and lichens. Choose the correct option. \n1. Viroid was found to be a free RNA; it lacked the protein coat that is found in viruses\n\n2. Prions are agents consisting of the abnormally folded protein.\n\n3. Lichens are symbiotic associations i.e. mutually useful associations, between algae and fungi. The algal component is known as phycobiont and the fungal component is mycobiont. \n\n4. Algae prepare food for fungi and fungi provide shelter and absorb mineral nutrients and water for their partner.','All 1,2,3 and 4 are correct','Viroid was found to be a free RNA; it lacked the protein coat that is found in viruses. Prions are agent consisting of abnormally folded protein. Lichens are symbiotic associations i.e. mutually useful associations, between algae and fungi. The algal component is known as phycobiont and fungal component as mycobiont. Algae prepare food for fungi and fungi provide shelter and absorb mineral nutrients and water for its partner. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 27; Paragraph No. 1-3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are correct.','All 1,2,3 and 4 are correct','2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-027','11-021',NULL),
(21201,'chemistry','Select the correct statements regarding white light: \n \n (a) The speed of light is independent of the nature of the medium through which it passes.\n \n (b) When a ray of white light is passed through a prism, the wave with shorter wavelength bends more than the one with a longer wavelength.\n \n (c) A ray of white light, when passes through a prism, spreads out into a series of coloured bands called spectrum.\n \n (d) The spectrum of white light, ranges from red at 7.50 × 10¹⁴ Hz to violet at 4 × 10¹⁴ Hz.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (b) and (c)','"The speed of light depends upon the nature of the medium through which it passes. As a result, the beam of light is deviated or refracted from its original path as it passes from one medium to another. It is observed that when a ray of white light is passed through a prism, the wave with shorter wavelength bends more than the one with a longer wavelength. Since ordinary white light consists of waves with all the wavelengths in the visible range, a ray of white light is spread out into a series of coloured bands called spectrum. The light of red colour which has longest wavelength is deviated the least while the violet light, which has shortest wavelength is deviated the most.The spectrum of white light, that we can see, ranges from violet at 7.50 × 10¹⁴ Hz to red at 4 × 10¹⁴ Hz. Such a spectrum is called continuous spectrum."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.44; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Evidence for the quantized Electronic Energy Levels: Atomic spectra',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (a) and (b)','Only (b) and (c)','Only (a) and (d)','Only (c) and (d)','NEW','11-044','11-044',NULL),
(21202,'chemistry','The spectrum of white light ranging from violet to red is called a continuous spectrum because _______.','The violet colour merges into blue, blue into green, green into yellow and so on.','"Ordinary white light consists of waves with all the wavelengths in the visible range, a ray of white light is spread out into a series of coloured bands called spectrum. Such a spectrum is called continuous spectrum because violet merges into blue, blue into green and so on. A similar spectrum is produced when a rainbow forms in the sky."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.44; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Evidence for the quantized Electronic Energy Levels: Atomic spectra',NULL,'3','mcq','Different colours are seen as different bands in the spectrum.','The colours continuously absorb energy to form a spectrum.','The violet colour merges into blue, blue into green, green into yellow and so on.','It is a continuous band of coloured lines with white light separating them.','NEW','11-044','11-044',NULL),
(21203,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): The spectrum of radiation emitted by a substance that has absorbed energy is called an emission spectrum.\n \n Reason(R): The spectrum of white light, ranges from red at 7.50 × 10¹⁴ Hz to violet at 4×10¹⁴ Hz.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(A) is true but (R) is false.','"The spectrum of radiation emitted by a substance that has absorbed energy is called an emission spectrum. Atoms, molecules or ions that have absorbed radiation are said to be “excited”. To produce an emission spectrum, energy is supplied to a sample by heating it or irradiating it and the wavelength (or frequency) of the radiation emitted, as the sample gives up the absorbed energy, is recorded."\n\n"The spectrum of white light, that we can see, ranges from violet at 7.50 × 10¹⁴ Hz to red at 4 × 10¹⁴ Hz. Such a spectrum is called continuous spectrum."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.44; Paragraph 1, 2)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Evidence for the quantized Electronic Energy Levels: Atomic spectra',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-044','11-044',NULL),
(21204,'chemistry','The study of emission or absorption spectra is referred to as _______.','Spectroscopy','"The study of emission or absorption spectra is referred to as spectroscopy."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.44; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Evidence for the quantized Electronic Energy Levels: Atomic spectra',NULL,'1','mcq','Spectroscopy','Microscopy','Kinetics','Thermodynamics','NEW','11-044','11-044',NULL),
(21205,'chemistry','Select the incorrect statements regarding emission spectra: \n \n (a) The spectrum of the visible light is discrete as all wavelengths (red to violet) of the visible light are represented in the spectra.\n \n (b) The emission spectra of atoms in the gas phase do not show a continuous spread of wavelength from red to violet, rather they emit light only at specific wavelengths with dark spaces between them. \n \n (c) The spectra with visible lines separated by dark spaces between them are called line spectra or atomic spectra because the emitted radiation is identified by the appearance of bright lines in the spectra.\n (d) Different elements may have asimilar line emission spectrum.\n (e) The characteristic lines in atomic spectra can be used in chemical analysis to identify unknown atoms.\n (f) The element helium (He) was discovered in the sun by spectroscopic method.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(a) and (d) only','"The spectrum of the visible light is continuous as all wavelengths (red to violet) of the visible light are represented in the spectra. The emission spectra of atoms in the gas phase, on the other hand, do not show a continuous spread of wavelength from red to violet, rather they emit light only at specific wavelengths with dark spaces between them. Such spectra are called line spectra or atomic spectra because the emitted radiation is identified by the appearance of bright lines in the spectra. Line emission spectra are of great interest in the study of electronic structure. Each element has a unique line emission spectrum. The characteristic lines in atomic spectra can be used in chemical analysis to identify unknown atoms in the same way as fingerprints are used to identify people. The exact matching of lines of the emission spectrum of the atoms of a known element with the lines from an unknown sample quickly establishes the identity of the latter."\n"The element helium (He) was discovered in the sun by spectroscopic method."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.44; Paragraph 4, 5, 6)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Evidence for the quantized Electronic Energy Levels: Atomic spectra',NULL,'3','mcq','(a), (b) and (f) only','(b) and (e) only','(a) and (d) only','(b), (c), (d) and (f) only','NEW','11-044','11-044',NULL),
(21206,'chemistry','Select the correct statements regarding Hydrogen spectrum:\n \n (i) The Balmer series of lines are the only lines in the hydrogen spectrum which appear in the infrared region of the electromagnetic spectrum.\n \n (ii) The Balmer series of lines are the only lines in the hydrogen spectrum which appear in the visible region of the electromagnetic spectrum.\n \n (iii) The Balmer series of lines are the only lines in the hydrogen spectrum which appear in the UV region of the electromagnetic spectrum.\n \n (iv) If spectral lines are expressed in terms of wavenumber (ṽ) , then the visible lines of the hydrogen spectrum obey the following formula: \n \n wavenumber, ṽ = 109,677[1/n₁²-1/n₂²] cm⁻¹\n \n (v) The first five series of lines that correspond to n₁ = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 are known as Lyman, Balmer, Paschen, Bracket and Pfund series, respectively.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(ii), (iv), (v) only','"The hydrogen spectrum consists of several series of lines named after their discoverers. Balmer showed in 1885 on the basis of experimental observations that if spectral lines are expressed in terms of wavenumber (ṽ), then the visible lines of the hydrogen spectrum obey the following formula:\n\n  ṽ = 109,677[1/2²-1/n²] cm⁻¹\n\nwhere n is an integer equal to or greater than 3 (i.e., n = 3,4,5,....)\n\nThe series of lines described by this formula are called the Balmer series. The Balmer series of lines are the only lines in the hydrogen spectrum which appear in the visible region of the electromagnetic spectrum. The Swedish spectroscopist, Johannes Rydberg, noted that all series of lines in the hydrogen spectrum could be described by the following expression :\n\n  ṽ = 109,677[1/n₁²-1/n₂²] cm⁻¹\n\nwhere,\n\nn₁ = 1, 2,........ \n\nn₂ = n₁ + 1, n₁ + 2,......\n\nThe first five series of lines that correspond to n₁ = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 are known as Lyman, Balmer, Paschen, Bracket and Pfund series, respectively."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.44; Paragraph 7\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.45; Paragraph 1, 2, 3)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Evidence for the quantized Electronic Energy Levels: Atomic spectra',NULL,'3','mcq','(ii), (iv), (v) only','(i), (iv), (v) only','(ii), (iii) only','All (i), (ii), (iii), (iv), (v)','NEW','11-045','11-045',NULL),
(21207,'chemistry','Select the correct statements regarding line spectrum:\n \n (a) Line spectrum for any element is same for all elements and is not a characteristic feature.\n \n (b) Of all the elements, hydrogen atom has the simplest line spectrum.\n \n (c) Of all the elements, helium atom has the simplest line spectrum.\n \n (d) Line spectrum becomes more and more complex for heavier atom with increase in mass number.\n \n (e) There is irregularity in the line spectrum of each element.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(b), (d) only','"Of all the elements, hydrogen atom has the simplest line spectrum. Line spectrum becomes more and more complex for heavier atom. There are, however, certain features which are common to all line spectra, i.e., \n\n(i) line spectrum of element is unique and \n\n(ii) there is regularity in the line spectrum of each element."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.45, 46; Paragraph 4, 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Developments leading to the Bohr\'s model of atom - Evidence for the quantized Electronic Energy Levels: Atomic spectra',NULL,'3','mcq','(a), (c) only','(b), (d), (e) only','(b), (d) only','(c) only','NEW','11-045','11-045',NULL),
(21208,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): Robert Bunsen (1811-1899) was one of the first investigators to use line spectra to identify elements. \n \n Reason(R): Elements like rubidium (Rb), caesium (Cs) thallium (Tl), indium (In), gallium (Ga) and scandium (Sc) were discovered when their minerals were analysed by spectroscopic methods.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','German chemist, Robert Bunsen (1811-1899) was one of the first investigators to use line spectra to identify elements. Elements like rubidium (Rb), caesium (Cs) thallium (Tl), indium (In), gallium (Ga) and scandium (Sc) were discovered when their minerals were analysed by spectroscopic methods."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.44; Paragraph 5, 6','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Evidence for the quantized Electronic Energy Levels: Atomic spectra',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-044','11-044',NULL),
(21209,'biology','Assertion: Being eukaryotes, the protistan cell body contains a well-defined nucleus and other membrane-less organelles.\n\n\nReason: Protists reproduce asexually by a process involving cell fusion and zygote formation.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Being eukaryotes, the protistan cell body contains a well defined nucleus and other membrane-bound organelles. Some have flagella or cilia. Protists reproduce asexually and sexually by a process involving cell fusion and zygote formation. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 20; Paragraph No. 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(21210,'biology','Assertion: The spores of slime moulds possess true walls.\n\nReason: During unfavourable conditions, the plasmodium differentiates and forms fruiting bodies bearing spores at their tips.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','During unfavourable conditions, the plasmodium differentiates and forms fruiting bodies bearing spores at their tips. The spores possess true walls. They are extremely resistant and survive for many years, even under adverse conditions. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 21; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(21211,'biology','Assertion: Fungi are cosmopolitan and occur in air, water, soil and on animals and plants.\n\n\nReason: They prefer to grow in warm and humid places.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Fungi are cosmopolitan and occur in air, water, soil and on animals and plants. They prefer to grow in warm and humid places. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 22; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(21212,'biology','Assertion: Kingdom Fungi is divided into 4 classes\n\n\nReason: The morphology of the mycelium, mode of spore formation and fruiting bodies form the basis for the division of the kingdom into various classes.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Kingdom Fungi is divided into 4 classes Phycomycetes, Ascomycetes, Basidiomycetes and Deuteromycetes. The morphology of the mycelium, mode of spore formation and fruiting bodies form the basis for the division of the kingdom into various classes. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 23; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(21213,'biology','Assertion: Neurospora is used extensively in biochemical and genetic work.\n\n\nReason: Members of Basidiomycetes like morels and truffles are edible and are considered delicacies.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Neurospora is used extensively in biochemical and genetic work. Members of ascomycetes  like morels and truffles are edible and are considered delicacies. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 24; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-024','11-018',NULL),
(21214,'biology','Assertion: In basidiomycetes, plasmogamy is brought about by the fusion of two vegetative or somatic cells of different strains or genotypes\n\n.\nReason: The basidiospores are exogenoussly produced on the basidium','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','In basidiomycetes the sex organs are absent, but plasmogamy is brought about by fusion of two vegetative or somatic cells of different strains or genotypes. The resultant structure is dikaryotic which ultimately gives rise to basidium. Karyogamy and meiosis take place in the basidium producing four basidiospores. The basidiospores are exogenously produced on the basidium(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 24; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-024','11-018',NULL),
(40403,'chemistry','Kössel and Lewis developed theory of chemical combination between atoms known as ________________.','Electronic theory of chemical bonding','Kössel and Lewis in 1916 developed an important theory of chemical combination between atoms known as electronic theory of chemical bonding. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Octet rule',NULL,'1','mcq','Electronic theory of chemical bonding','Electronic theory of molecular bonding','Electronic theory of nuclear bonding','Electronic theory of atomic bonding','NEW','11-102','11-102',NULL),
(21215,'biology','Assertion: Lichens are very good pollution indicators.\n\n\nReason: They do not grow in polluted areas.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Lichens are very good pollution indicators – they do not grow in polluted areas.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 27; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-027','11-021',NULL),
(21216,'biology','Assertion: Viruses are obligate parasites.\n\n\nReason: They are inert outside their specific host cell.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Viruses are obligate parasites. They are inert outside their specific host cell. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 26; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-026','11-020',NULL),
(21217,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Enaima did not have blood cells.\n\n\nReason- Anaima were the animals having red blood cells in them.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Enaima had red blood and Anaima did not according to Aristotle classification.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21218,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Classification is necessary to study all living organisms.\n\nReason- In classification, the individuals of different categories are grouped into categories.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','To study all the existing and even extinct living organisms is nearly impossible for us. So it has become very essential to group the individuals into categories on the basis of various characteristics. This is referred to as classification which makes it possible to study all the existing and extinct living organisms by studying the category-wise characteristics.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-016','11-010',NULL),
(21219,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Aristotle divided plants into two group.\n\n\nReason- Aristotle was not the earliest to attempt a more scientific basis for classification.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Aristotle was the earliest to attempt a more scientific basis for classification. He used simple morphological characters to classify plants into trees, shrubs and herbs. He also divided animals into two groups, those which had red blood and those that did not.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21220,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Aristotle used simple anatomical characters to classify plants into trees, shrubs and herbs. \n\n\nReason- Aristotle gave two kingdom classification.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','He used simple morphological characters\nto classify plants into trees, shrubs and herbs. He also divided animals\ninto two groups, those which had red blood and those that did not.\nIn Linnaeus\' time a Two Kingdom system of classification with\nPlantae and Animalia kingdoms was developed','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-016','11-010',NULL),
(21221,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Two kingdom classification system did not distinguish between the eukaryotes and prokaryotes.\n\n\nReason- Classification of organisms into plants and animals was easily done and was easy to understand.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','In Linnaeus\' time a Two Kingdom system of classification with Plantae and Animalia kingdoms was developed that included all\nplants and animals respectively. This system did not distinguish between the eukaryotes and prokaryotes, unicellular and multicellular organisms\nand photosynthetic (green algae) and non-photosynthetic (fungi) organisms. Classification of organisms into plants and animals was easily done and was easy to understand, but, a large number of organisms did not fall into either category. Hence the two kingdom classification used for a long time was found inadequate.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-016','11-010',NULL),
(40404,'chemistry','Which of the following molecule do not follow octet rule:','All of these','There are three general exceptions to the octet rule: Molecules, such as NO, with an odd number of electrons; Molecules in which one or more atoms possess more than eight electrons, such as SF6; and. \n\nMolecules such as BCl3, in which one or more atoms possess less than eight electrons. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Octet rule',NULL,'3','mcq','NO','SF₆','BCl₃','All of these','NEW','11-102','11-102',NULL),
(40405,'chemistry','Which of the following molecule do not follow octet rule:','PCl₅','An atom having electrons less than 8 or more than 8 will not obey the octet rule. \n\nPCl5 has more than 8 electrons i.e. 10 electrons violating the octet rule. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Octet rule',NULL,'2','mcq','PCl₅','NaCl','NaF','CO₂','NEW','11-102','11-102',NULL),
(21222,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- The two kingdom classification used for a long time was found inadequate. \n\n\nReason- A large number of organisms did not fall into either category in two kingdom classification.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','In Linnaeus\' time a Two Kingdom system of classification with Plantae and Animalia kingdoms was developed that included all\nplants and animals respectively. This system did not distinguish between the eukaryotes and prokaryotes, unicellular and multicellular organisms\nand photosynthetic (green algae) and non-photosynthetic (fungi) organisms. Classification of organisms into plants and animals was easily done and was easy to understand, but, a large number of organisms did not fall into either category. Hence the two kingdom classification used for a long time was found inadequate.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-017','11-011',NULL),
(21223,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Two Kingdom classification and choose the correct option.\n1. It was given by Linnaeus. \n\n2. This system distinguished between photosynthetic (green algae) and non-photosynthetic (fungi) organisms. \n\n3. This system did not distinguish between the eukaryotes and prokaryotes\n\n4. This system did not distinguish between unicellular and multicellular organisms.','Only 2 is incorrect','In Linnaeus\' time a Two Kingdom system of classification with Plantae and Animalia kingdoms was developed that included all\nplants and animals respectively. This system did not distinguish between the eukaryotes and prokaryotes, unicellular and multicellular organisms\nand photosynthetic (green algae) and non-photosynthetic (fungi) organisms. Classification of organisms into plants and animals was easily done and was easy to understand, but, a large number of organisms did not fall into either category. Hence the two kingdom classification used for a long time was found inadequate.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 3 is incorrect','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','All the statements are correct','NEW','11-017','11-011',NULL),
(21224,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Besides, gross morphology a need was also felt for including other characteristics like cell structure, nature of wall, mode of nutrition etc\n\n\nReason- Classification systems for the living organisms have not undergone several changes over the time.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Besides, gross morphology a need was also felt for including other characteristics like cell structure, nature of wall, mode of nutrition, habitat, methods of reproduction, evolutionary relationships, etc. Classification systems for the living organisms have hence, undergone several changes over the time.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-017','11-011',NULL),
(21225,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Plant and animal kingdoms have been a constant under all different systems.\n\n\nReason- The number and nature of other kingdoms have also been understood differently by different scientists over the time.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Though plant and animal kingdoms have been a constant under all different systems, the understanding of what groups/organisms be included under these kingdoms have been changing; the number and nature of other kingdoms have also been understood differently by different scientists over the time.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW',NULL,'11-011',NULL),
(21226,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- R.H. Whittaker proposed a Five Kingdom Classification.\n\n\nReason- It was proposed in 1979.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','R.H. Whittaker (1969) proposed a Five Kingdom Classification.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-017','11-011',NULL),
(21227,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- The three-domain system has also been proposed that divides the Kingdom Monera into two domains.\n\n\nReason- Carl Woese proposed six-kingdom classification.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Carl Woese proposed six-kingdom classification. The three-domain system has also been proposed that divides the Kingdom Monera into two domains, leaving the remaining eukaryotic kingdoms in the third domain and thereby a six kingdom classification.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-017','11-011',NULL),
(21228,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Five Kingdom Classification and choose the correct option.\n1. The main criteria for classification include cell structure, body organisation, mode of nutrition, reproduction and phylogenetic relationships. \n\n2. R.H. Whittaker in 1969 proposed the classification.\n\n3. R.H. Whittaker proposed a Five Kingdom Classification.\n\n4. The kingdoms defined by him were named Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae and Animalia.','All the statements are correct','R.H. Whittaker (1969) proposed a Five Kingdom Classification. The kingdoms defined by him were named Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae\nand Animalia. The main criteria for classification used by him include cell structure, body organisation, mode of nutrition, reproduction and phylogenetic relationships.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 3 is incorrect','Only 4 is correct','Both 3 and 4 are correct','All the statements are correct','NEW','11-017','11-011',NULL),
(21229,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- The third Kingdom proposed was Kingdom Monera.\n\n\nReason- Kingdom Monera was given by Haeckel.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Third kingdom proposed was Protista, given by Haeckel.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-017','11-011',NULL),
(21230,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- The fourth kingdom was given by Copeland.\n\n\nReason- The fourth kingdom proposed was Fungi.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Monera was 4th Kingdom, given by Copeland.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-017','11-011',NULL),
(21231,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- The three domains were Bacteria, Archaea and Eukarya.\n\n\nReason- These domains were proposed by R.H. Whittaker.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','The three domains were Bacteria, Archaea and Eukarya. These domains were proposed by Carl Woose.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-017','11-011',NULL),
(21232,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Earlier, Chlamydomonas and Spirogyra were placed together under algae. \n\n\nReason- Earlier classification systems included bacteria, blue green algae, fungi, mosses, ferns, gymnosperms and the angiosperms under ‘Plants’.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Earlier classification systems included bacteria, blue green algae, fungi, mosses,\nferns, gymnosperms and the angiosperms under ‘Plants’. This placed together groups which widely differed in other characteristics. It brought together the prokaryotic bacteria and the blue green algae (cyanobacteria) with other groups which were eukaryotic. It also grouped together the unicellular organisms and the multicellular ones, say, for example, Chlamydomonas and Spirogyra were placed together under algae.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-017','11-011',NULL),
(21233,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Earlier classification did not differentiate between the heterotrophic group – fungi, and the autotrophic green plants.\n\n\nReason- Fungi had glycogen in their walls while the green plants had a cellulosic cell wall.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','The classification did not differentiate between the heterotrophic group – fungi, and the autotrophic green plants, though they also showed a characteristic difference in their walls composition – the fungi had chitin in their walls while the green plants had a cellulosic cell wall','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-017','11-011',NULL),
(21234,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- All prokaryotic organisms were grouped together under Kingdom Monera and the unicellular eukaryotic organisms were placed in Kingdom Protista. \n\n\nReason- Chlamydomonas and Chlorella are placed in Protista.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','All prokaryotic organisms were grouped together under Kingdom Monera and the unicellular eukaryotic organisms were placed in Kingdom Protista. Kingdom Protista has brought together Chlamydomonas, Chlorella.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-017','11-011',NULL),
(21235,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Chlamydomonas was earlier placed in Algae but shifted into Protista\n\n\nReason- The criteria for classification changed.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Kingdom Protista has brought together Chlamydomonas, Chlorella (earlier placed in Algae within Plants and both having cell walls) with Paramoecium and Amoeba (which were earlier placed in the animal kingdom which lack cell wall). It has put together organisms which, in earlier classifications, were placed in different kingdoms. This happened because the criteria for classification changed.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-017','11-011',NULL),
(21236,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Over time, an attempt has been made to evolve a classification system which reflects only the morphological, physiological and reproductive similarities.\n\n\nReason- Classifications are also phylogenetic, i.e., is based on anatomical relationships.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Over time, an attempt has been made to evolve a classification system which reflects not only the morphological, physiological and reproductive similarities, but is also phylogenetic, i.e., is based on evolutionary relationships.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-017','11-011',NULL),
(21237,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Monera and choose the correct option.\n1. They are the most abundant micro-organisms. \n\n2. They also live in extreme habitats such as hot springs, deserts, snow and deep oceans where very few other life forms can survive.\n\n3. Billions of bacteria are present in a handful of soil. \n\n4. Bacteria are the sole members of the Kingdom Monera.','Only 3 is incorrect','Bacteria are the sole members of the Kingdom Monera. They are the most abundant micro-organisms. Bacteria occur almost everywhere. Hundreds\nof bacteria are present in a handful of soil. They also live in extreme habitats such as hot springs, deserts, snow and deep oceans where very few other\nlife forms can survive.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 3 is incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 3 and 4 are correct','All the statements are correct','NEW','11-018','11-012',NULL),
(21238,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Monera and choose the correct option.\n1. Bacteria as a group show the most extensive metabolic diversity. \n\n2. Though the bacterial structure is very simple, they are very complex in behaviour. \n\n3. Bacteria are grouped under four categories based on their shape.\n\n4. No bacteria is a parasite.','Only 4 is incorrect','Though the bacterial structure is very simple, they are very complex in behaviour. Compared to many other organisms, bacteria as a group show the most extensive metabolic diversity. Bacteria are grouped under four categories based on their shape. Many of them live in or on other organisms as parasites.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 3 is incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 3 and 4 are correct','All the statements are correct','NEW','11-018','11-012',NULL),
(21239,'biology','Consider these statements regarding shapes of bacteria and choose the correct option.\n1. Baccilus is rod shaped.\n\n2. Spirallium is Spiral in shape.\n\n3. Vibrio is colon shaped.\n\n4. Coccus is circular in shape.','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','Bacteria are grouped under four categories based on their shape: the spherical Coccus (pl.: cocci), the rod-shaped Bacillus (pl.: bacilli), the comma-shaped Vibrium (pl.: vibrio) and the spiral Spirillum (pl.: spirilla)','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 3 is incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','All the statements are correct','NEW','11-018','11-012',NULL),
(21240,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- In Bacteria, respiratory enzymes are associated with plasma membrane.\n\n\nReason- Cell wall is made of Peptidoglycan in Bacteria.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','In Bacteria, respiratory enzymes are associated with plasma membrane. Cell wall is made of Peptidoglycan in Bacteria.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-018','11-012',NULL),
(21301,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): Neils Bohr (1913) was the first to explain quantitatively the general features of the structure of hydrogen atom and its spectrum. \n \n Reason(R): He used Planck’s concept of quantisation of energy to explain structure of atom.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"Neils Bohr (1913) was the first to explain quantitatively the general features of the structure of hydrogen atom and its spectrum. He used Planck’s concept of quantisation of energy."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.46; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Bohr\'s model for hydrogen atom',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-047','11-047',NULL),
(21302,'chemistry','The postulates which constitutes the basis of Bohr’s model for hydrogen atom are:\n \n (i) The electron in the hydrogen atom can move around the nucleus in a circular path of fixed radius and energy. These paths are called orbits.\n \n (ii) Orbits are arranged concentrically around the nucleus.\n \n (iii) Orbits are arranged linearly around the nucleus.\n \n (iv) The energy of an electron in the orbit changes with time.\n \n (v) The electron will move from a lower stationary state to a higher stationary state when required amount of energy is absorbed by the electron or energy is emitted when electron moves from higher stationary state to lower stationary state.\n \n (vi) The energy change take place in a continuous manner between orbits.\n \n (vii) The frequency of radiation is absorbed or emitted according to Bohr’s frequency rule i.e., when transition occurs between two stationary states that differ in energy by ∆E, is given by:\n \n v = ∆E/h = (E₂ - E₁)/h\n \n where E₁ and E₂ are the energies of the lower and higher allowed energy states respectively.\n \n (viii) The angular momentum of an electron is quantised. In a given stationary state it can be expressed as in equation \n \n mₑvr = nh/2π, n = 1,2,3..... \n \n where mₑ is the mass of electron, v is the velocity and r is the radius of the orbit in which electron is moving.\n \n (ix) An electron can move only in those orbits for which its angular momentum is the fraction of h/2π.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(i), (ii), (v), (vii), and (viii)','"Bohr’s model for hydrogen atom is based on the following postulates:\n\n(i) The electron in the hydrogen atom can move around the nucleus in a circular path of fixed radius and energy. These paths are called orbits, stationary states or allowed energy states. These orbits are arranged concentrically around the nucleus.\n\n(ii) The energy of an electron in the orbit does not change with time. However, the electron will move from a lower stationary state to a higher stationary state when required amount of energy is absorbed by the electron or energy is emitted when electron moves from higher stationary state to lower stationary state. The energy change does not take place in a continuous manner.\n\n(iii) The frequency of radiation absorbed or emitted when transition occurs between two stationary states that differ in energy by ∆E, is given by:\n\nv = ∆E/h = (E₂ - E₁)/h\n\nwhere E₁ and E₂ are the energies of the lower and higher allowed energy states respectively. This expression is commonly known as Bohr’s frequency rule.\n\n(iv) The angular momentum of an electron is quantised. In a given stationary state it can be expressed as in equation \n\nmₑvr = nh/2π, n = 1,2,3..... \n\nwhere mₑ is the mass of electron, v is the velocity and r is the radius of the orbit in which electron is moving. Thus an electron can move only in those orbits for which its angular momentum is integral multiple of h/2π. That means angular momentum is quantised."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.46; Paragraph 3, 4, 5, 6)\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.47; Paragraph 1, 2)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Bohr\'s model for hydrogen atom',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii), (vi), (vii), and (viii)','(i), (iii), (v), and (viii)','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv), (v), (vi), (vii), and (viii)','(i), (ii), (v), (vii), and (viii)','NEW','11-047','11-047',NULL),
(21303,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): The frequency of radiation is absorbed or emitted, when transition occurs between two stationary states that differ in energy by ∆E, according to Bohr’s frequency rule.\n \n Reason(R): The frequency of radiation absorbed or emitted when transition occurs between two stationary states that differ in energy by ∆E, is given by:\n v = ∆E/h = (E₂ - E₁)/h\n \n where E₁ and E₂ are the energies of the lower and higher allowed energy states respectively. This expression is commonly known as Bohr’s frequency rule.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"Bohr’s model for hydrogen atom is based on the following postulates:\n\n(iii) The frequency of radiation absorbed or emitted when transition occurs between two stationary states that differ in energy by ∆E, is given by:\n\nv = ∆E/h = (E₂ - E₁)/h\n\nwhere E₁ and E₂ are the energies of the lower and higher allowed energy states respectively. This expression is commonly known as Bohr’s frequency rule."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.46; Paragraph 5','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Bohr\'s model for hydrogen atom',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-046','11-046',NULL),
(21304,'chemistry','The angular momentum of an electron in a given stationary state can be expressed as mₑvr = h/2π. Based on this expression, an electron can move only in those orbits for which its angular momentum is _______.','Integral multiple of h/2π','"Bohr’s model for hydrogen atom is based on the following postulates:\n\n→ The angular momentum of an electron is quantised. In a given stationary state it can be expressed as in equation \n\nmₑvr = nh/2π, n = 1,2,3..... \n\nwhere mₑ is the mass of electron, v is the velocity and r is the radius of the orbit in which electron is moving. Thus an electron can move only in those orbits for which its angular momentum is integral multiple of h/2π. That means angular momentum is quantised."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.47; Paragraph 1, 2)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Bohr\'s model for hydrogen atom',NULL,'2','mcq','Equal to n','Integral multiple of h/2π','Multiple of n','Fraction of h/2π','NEW','11-047','11-047',NULL),
(21305,'chemistry','Select the correct statements from Bohr\'s theory for hydrogen atom:\n \n (a) The stationary states for electron are numbered n = 1,2,3.......... These integral numbers are known as Principal quantum numbers.\n \n (b) The radii of the stationary states are expressed as: \n rₙ = n²aₒ\n where, a₀ = 52.9 pm. \n \n (c) The radius of the first stationary state, i.e., 52.9 pm, is called the Bohr orbit.\n \n (d) As n increases, the value of r will decrease. In other words the electron will be present close to the nucleus.\n \n (e) The most important property associated with the electron is the radius of its orbit in its stationary state. \n \n (f) The most important property associated with the electron is the energy of its stationary state. \n \n (g) The energy of the stationary state of an electron is given by the expression:\n \n Eₙ = -Rₕ [1/n²] n = 1, 2 ,3......\n where Rₕ is called Rydberg constant and its value is 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J.\n \n (h) The energy of the lowest state, also called as the ground state, is \n E₁ = –0.545 × 10⁻¹⁸ J.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (a),(b), (c), (f), and (g)','"According to Bohr’s theory for hydrogen atom: \n\n(a) The stationary states for electron are numbered n = 1,2,3.......... These integral numbers are known as Principal quantum numbers. \n\n(b) The radii of the stationary states are expressed as: \n\nrₙ = n²a₀ \n  \nwhere, a₀ = 52.9 pm. \n\nThus the radius of the first stationary state, called the Bohr orbit, is 52.9 pm. Normally the electron in the hydrogen atom is found in this orbit (i.e., n = 1). As n increases, the value of r will increase. In other words the electron will be present away from the nucleus.\n\n(c) The most important property associated with the electron, is the energy of its stationary state. It is given by the expression:\n\nEₙ = -Rₕ [1/n²] n = 1, 2 ,3......\n\nwhere Rₕ is called Rydberg constant and its value is 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J.\n\nThe energy of the lowest state, also called as the ground state, is \n            E₁ = –2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J (1/1²) = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J.\n\nThe energy of the stationary state for n = 2, will be \n            E₂ = –2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J (1/2²) = –0.545 × 10⁻¹⁸ J."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.47; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Bohr\'s model for hydrogen atom',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (a), (b), (d), and (e)','Only (a),(b), (c), (f), and (g)','Only (b), (c), and (g)','Only (b) and (e)','NEW','11-047','11-047',NULL),
(21306,'chemistry','What does the negative energy (energy with negative sign for all possible orbits) of the electron in a hydrogen atom means?\n \n (i) The energy of an electron in the atom is lower than the energy of a free electron at rest which is taken as zero.\n \n (ii) When the electron is free from the influence of nucleus it has a negative value of energy which becomes more negative.\n \n (iii) When the electron is attracted by the nucleus the energy is absorbed which means a negative value.\n \n (iv) Energy is released by hydrogen atom in its ground state.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (i)','"When the electron is attracted by the nucleus and is present in orbit n, the energy is emitted and its energy is lowered. The energy of the electron in a hydrogen atom has a negative sign for all possible orbits. This negative sign means that the energy of the electron in the atom is lower than the energy of a free electron at rest. A free electron at rest is an electron that is infinitely far away from the nucleus and is assigned the energy value of zero. \n\nMathematically, this corresponds to setting n = infinity in the equation Eₙ = -Rₕ [1/n²], so that E∞ = 0. When the electron is attracted by the nucleus and is present in orbit n, the energy is emitted and its energy is lowered. This corresponds to the situation in which as the electron gets closer to the nucleus (i.e., as n decreases), Eₙ becomes larger in absolute value and more and more negative. The most negative energy value is given by n = 1 which corresponds to the most stable orbit. We call this the ground state."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.47; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Bohr\'s model for hydrogen atom',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i)','Only (ii) and (iv)','Only (iii) and (iv)','Only (iv)','NEW','11-047','11-047',NULL),
(21307,'chemistry','Which of the following option(s) is not correctly matched?\n \n (i) Energy associated with Bohr\'s orbit, Eₙ = -2.18 x 10⁻¹⁸ x Z²/ n².\n \n (ii) Energy gap between two orbits, ∆E = Rₕ[1/n₁² - 1/n₂²].\n \n (iii) Kinetic energy of the ejected electron, hv = hv₀ +1/2 mₑv².\n \n (iv)Energy of one mole of photons, E = n₀hc/λ.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (iii)  and (iv)','(a) "The energies of the stationary states associated with these kinds of ions (also known as hydrogen like species) are given by the expression:\nEₙ = -2.18 x 10⁻¹⁸ x Z²/ n² (J)"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.48; Paragraph 1\n\n(b) "The energy gap between the two orbits is given by the expression:\nEnergy gap between two orbits, ∆E = Rₕ[1/n₁² - 1/n₂²]"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.48; Paragraph 5\n\n(c) "Since the striking photon has energy equal to hν and the minimum energy required to eject the electron is hν₀, then the difference in energy (hν – hν₀) is transferred as the kinetic energy of the photoelectron. Following the conservation of energy principle, the kinetic energy of the ejected electron is given by the expression:\nhν = hν₀ + 1/2 mₑv²"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.42; Paragraph 3\n\n(d) "The energy (E) of a photon is given by,\n               E = nhν = nhc/λ\n  Therefore, energy of 1 mole of photons (n = 1) is:\n               E = hν = hc/λ"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.41; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Bohr\'s model for hydrogen atom',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (ii)','Only (iii)  and (iv)','Only (i)','Only (iv)','NEW','11-048','11-048',NULL),
(21308,'chemistry','What are the frequency and wavelength of a photon emitted during a transition from n = 5 state to the n = 2 state in the hydrogen atom?','6.91 x 10¹⁴ Hz and 434 nm','"Since n₁ = 5 and n₂ = 2, this transition gives rise to a spectral line in the visible region of the Balmer series. \n\n∆E = Rₕ [1/n₁² - 1/n₂²] \n\n∆E = 2.18 x 10⁻¹⁸ J [1/5² - 1/2²] = -4.58 x 10⁻¹⁹ J.\n\nIt is an emission energy. \n\nThe frequency of the photon (taking energy in terms of magnitude) is given by, \n\nv = ∆E/h = -4.58 x 10⁻¹⁹ J/6.62 x 10⁻³⁴ Js = 6.91 x 10¹⁴ Hz \n\nλ = c/v = 3 x 10⁸ ms⁻¹/6.91 x 10¹⁴ Hz = 434 nm"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.49; Problem 2.10)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Bohr\'s model for hydrogen atom',NULL,'3','mcq','6.91 x 10¹⁴ Hz and 434 nm','6.91 x 10¹⁵ Hz and 434 nm','6.91 x 10¹⁵ Hz and 444 nm','6.91 x 10¹⁴ Hz and 443 nm','NEW','11-048','11-048',NULL),
(40406,'chemistry','Which of the following molecule followed octet rule:','NaCl','The octet rule is only applicable to the main group elements. \n\nThe molecules of the halogens, oxygen, nitrogen, and carbon are known to obey the octet rule. \n\nIn general, the elements that obey this rule include the s-block elements and the p-block elements (except hydrogen, helium, and lithium).','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Octet rule',NULL,'2','mcq','AlCl₃','NaCl','BeCl₂','PCl₅','NEW','11-102','11-102',NULL),
(21309,'chemistry','Calculate the energy associated with the first orbit of He⁺. What is the radius of this orbit?','-8.72 x 10⁻¹⁸ J and 0.02645 nm','Energy associated with Bohr\'s orbit, \n\n"Eₙ = -2.18 x 10⁻¹⁸ J x Z²/ n²\n\nFor He⁺, n = 1, Z = 2,\n\nE = -2.18 x 10⁻¹⁸ J x 2²/ 1² = -8.72 x 10⁻¹⁸ J\n\nThe radius of the orbit is given by equation,\n\nrₙ = 0.0529 nm x n²/Z \n\nSince, n = 1 and Z = 2,\n\nrₙ = 0.0529 nm x 1²/2 = 0.02645 nm"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.49; Problem 2.11','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Bohr\'s model for hydrogen atom',NULL,'3','mcq','-8.72 x 10⁻¹⁸ J and 0.02645 nm','-8.72 x 10⁻¹⁸ J and 0.02645 mm','-8.72 x 10⁻¹⁹ J and 0.02645 nm','-8.72 x 10⁻¹⁹ J and 0.20645 nm','NEW','11-048','11-048',NULL),
(21310,'chemistry','Which of the following are true limitations of Bohr\'s Model?\n \n (i) It fails to account for the doublet, (i.e., two closely spaced lines) of the hydrogen atom spectrum observed by using sophisticated spectroscopic techniques.\n \n (ii) This model successfully explains the spectrum of smaller atoms other than hydrogen.\n \n (iii) This model successfully account for the stability and line spectra of hydrogen like ions (for example, He⁺, Li²⁺, Be³⁺, and so on).\n \n (iv) This model also explains the spectrum of atoms other than hydrogen, for example, helium atom which possesses only two electrons, lithium atom, etc.\n \n (v) Bohr’s theory is unable to explain the splitting of spectral lines in the presence of magnetic field (Zeeman effect) or an electric field (Stark effect).\n \n (vi) It could not explain the ability of atoms to form molecules by chemical bonds.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(i), (iii), (v), (vi) only','"Bohr’s model of the hydrogen atom was no doubt an improvement over Rutherford’s nuclear model, as it could account for the stability and line spectra of hydrogen atom and hydrogen like ions (for example, He⁺, Li²⁺, Be³⁺, and so on). However, Bohr’s model was too simple to account for the following points:\n\n(i) It fails to account for the finer details (doublet, that is two closely spaced lines) of the hydrogen atom spectrum observed by using sophisticated spectroscopic techniques. This model is also unable to explain the spectrum of atoms other than hydrogen, for example, helium atom which possesses only two electrons. Further, Bohr’s theory was also unable to explain the splitting of spectral lines in the presence of magnetic field (Zeeman effect) or an electric field (Stark effect).\n\n(ii) It could not explain the ability of atoms to form molecules by chemical bonds."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.49; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Bohr\'s model for hydrogen atom',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii), (vi) only','(i), (iii), (v), (vi) only','(i), (ii), (iv), (vi) only','(ii), (v), (vi) only','NEW','11-049','11-049',NULL),
(21311,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Mycoplasma are resistant to penicillin. \n\n\nReason- Mycoplasma are called Jokers of Plant Kingdom.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Mycoplasma are called Jokers of Plant Kingdom and are resistant to penicillin.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(21312,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Cell wall in mycoplasma is not made of peptidoglycan. \n\n\nReason- Mycoplasma are PPLO.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Mycoplasma are pleuro pneumonic like organisms, PPLO. Cell wall is absent in them.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(21313,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- All single-celled eukaryotes are placed under Protista.\n\n\nReason- The boundaries of this kingdom are very well defined.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','All single-celled eukaryotes are placed under Protista, but the boundaries of this kingdom are not well defined.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(21314,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Chrysophytes, Dinoflagellates, Euglenoids, Slime moulds and Protozoans come under Protista. \n\n\nReason- Members of Protista can be terrestrial as well as aquatic.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Chrysophytes, Dinoflagellates, Euglenoids, Slime moulds and Protozoans come under Protista. Members of Protista are primarily(mainly) aquatic(so they can even be terrestrial protista).','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(21315,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- All Protists have flagella or cilia. \n\n\nReason- Protista forms a link with the others dealing with bacteria, animals and fungi.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','This kingdom forms a link with the others dealing with plants, animals and fungi. Being eukaryotes, the protistan cell body contains a well defined nucleus and other membrane-bound organelles. Some have flagella or cilia.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(21316,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Flagella in Protists have tubulin protein. \n\n\nReason- Protists reproduce asexually and sexually by a process involving cell fusion and zygote formation.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Flagella in Protists have tubulin protein. Protists reproduce asexually and sexually by a process involving cell fusion and zygote formation.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(21317,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Chrysophytes includes diatoms and dinoflagellates. \n\n\nReason- They are found exclusively in fresh water.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','This group includes diatoms and golden algae (desmids). They are found in fresh water as well as in marine environments.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(21318,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Most of Chrysophytes are photosynthetic. \n\n\nReason- Chrysophytes are microscopic and float passively in water currents (plankton).','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','They are microscopic and float passively in water currents (plankton). Most of them are photosynthetic.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(21319,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- In diatoms the cell walls form two thin overlapping shells, which fit together as in a soap box. \n\n\nReason- This arrangement is epitheca over hypotheca.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','In diatoms the cell walls form two thin overlapping shells, which fit together as in a soap box, epitheca over hypotheca.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(21320,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- The walls are indestructible in diatoms.\n\n\nReason- The walls are embedded with silica.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The walls are embedded with silica and thus the walls are indestructible.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(21321,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Diatoms have left behind large amount of cell wall deposits in their habitat.\n\n\nReason- The walls are embedded with silica and are indestructible.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The walls are embedded with silica and thus the walls are indestructible. Thus, diatoms have left behind large amount of cell wall deposits in their habitat.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(21322,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion-  Being gritty the soil made of cell wall is used in polishing, filtration of oils and syrups. \n\n\nReason- Accumulation of silica over billions of years is referred to as ‘diatomaceous earth’.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Diatoms have left behind large amount of cell wall deposits in their habitat; this accumulation over billions of years is referred to as ‘diatomaceous earth’. Being gritty this soil is used in polishing, filtration of oils and syrups.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(21323,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Diatoms are the chief ‘producers’ in the oceans.\n\n\nReason- They are responsible for 50% of total organic matter synthesised.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','They are responsible for 50% of total organic matter synthesised. Diatoms are the chief ‘producers’ in the oceans.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(21324,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Dinoflagellates and choose the correct option.\n1. They may kill marine animals such as fishes.\n\n2. They undergo rapid multiplication that they make the sea appear red.\n\n3. Example: Gonyaulax\n\n4. Toxins are released by them.','All the statements are correct','Very often, red dinoflagellates (Example: Gonyaulax) undergo such rapid multiplication that they make the sea appear red (red tides). Toxins released by such large\nnumbers may even kill other marine animals such as fishes.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are correct','All the statements are correct','NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(21325,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Dinoflagellates and choose the correct option.\n1. The cell wall has stiff cellulose plates on the outer surface. \n\n2. They are golden brown in colour. \n\n3. They show bioluminescence.\n\n4. These organisms are mostly marine and photosynthetic.','All the statements are correct','The cell wall has stiff cellulose plates on the outer surface. These organisms are mostly marine and photosynthetic. They appear yellow, green, brown, blue or red depending on the main pigments present in their cells and emit light.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are correct','All the statements are correct','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(21326,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Most of dinoflagellates  have two flagella; one lies longitudinally and the other transversely in a furrow between the wall plates.\n\n\nReason- These flagella are called whirling whips.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','These flagella are called whirling whips. Most of them have two flagella; one lies longitudinally and the other transversely in a furrow between the wall plates.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(21327,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Euglenoids are found only in marine water. \n\n\nReason- They have equal two flagella.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Majority of them are fresh water organisms. They have two flagella, a short and a long one.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(21328,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Euglenoids show mixotrophic nutrition. \n\n\nReason- Though they are photosynthetic in the presence of sunlight, when deprived of sunlight they behave like heterotrophs by predating on other smaller organisms.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Though they are photosynthetic in the presence of sunlight, when deprived of sunlight they behave like heterotrophs by predating on other smaller organisms, hence show mixotrophic nutrition.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-020','11-014',NULL),
(21329,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Euglena is a Euglenoid. \n\n\nReason- The pigments of euglenoids are identical to those present in higher plants.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The pigments of euglenoids are identical to those present in higher plants. Example: Euglena','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(21330,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Euglenoids have a protein rich layer called pellicle which makes their body flexible.\n\n\nReason- Cell wall is present in them.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Instead of a cell wall, they have a protein rich layer called pellicle which makes their body flexible.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(21331,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- The body in slime moves along decaying twigs and leaves engulfing organic material. \n\n\nReason- Slime moulds are saprophytic protists.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Slime moulds are saprophytic protists. The body moves\nalong decaying twigs and leaves engulfing organic\nmaterial.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(21332,'biology','Consider these statements regarding reproduction of slime moulds and choose the correct option.\n1. They are extremely resistant and spores are dispersed by air currents. \n\n2. The spores do not possess true walls.\n\n3. During unfavourable conditions,the plasmodium differentiates and forms fruiting bodies bearing spores at middle.\n\n4. Under suitable conditions, they form an aggregation called plasmodium which may grow and spread over small area.','Only 1 is correct','Under suitable conditions, they form an aggregation called plasmodium which may grow and spread over several feet. During unfavourable conditions, the plasmodium differentiates and forms fruiting bodies bearing spores at their tips. The spores possess true walls. They are extremely resistant and survive for many years, even under adverse conditions. The spores are dispersed by air currents.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Only 2 is correct','All the statements are correct','All the statements are incorrect','NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(21333,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- All protozoans are heterotrophs and live as predators or parasites. \n\n\nReason- They are believed to be primitive relatives of fungi.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','All protozoans are heterotrophs and live as predators or parasites.They are believed to be primitive relatives of animals.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(40407,'chemistry','Which of the following molecule followed octet rule:','All of these','The octet rule is only applicable to the main group elements. \n\nThe molecules of the halogens, oxygen, nitrogen, and carbon are known to obey the octet rule. \n\nIn general, the elements that obey this rule include the s-block elements and the p-block elements (except hydrogen, helium, and lithium).','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Octet rule',NULL,'2','mcq','NaCl','NaF','CO₂','All of these','NEW','11-102','11-102',NULL),
(21334,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Ameboid protozoans and choose the correct option.\n1. Some of them such as Entamoeba and Amoeba are parasites.\n\n2. Marine forms have carbonate shells on their surface.\n\n3. They move and capture their prey by putting out pseudopodia.\n\n4. These organisms live in fresh water, sea water or moist soil.','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','These organisms live in fresh water, sea water or moist soil. They move and capture their prey by putting out pseudopodia (false feet) as in Amoeba. Marine forms have silica shells on their surface. Some of them such as Entamoeba are parasites.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Only 1 is correct','All the statements are incorrect','NEW','11-021','11-015',NULL),
(21335,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Flagellated protozoans and choose the correct option.\n1. Example: Trypanosoma.\n\n2. They cause diaseases such as dysentary. \n\n3. They have flagella. \n\n4.  The members of this group are only free-living.','Both 1 and 3 are correct','The members of this group are either free-living or parasitic. They have flagella. The parasitic forms cause diaseases such as sleeping sickness. Example: Trypanosoma.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Only 2 is correct','All the statements are correct','NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(21336,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Ciliated protozoans are aquatic and are actively moving.\n\n\nReason- They have presence of thousands of cilia.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','These are aquatic, actively moving organisms because of the presence of thousands of cilia.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(21337,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- In ciliated protozoans, the coordinated movement of rows of cilia causes the water laden with food to be steered into the gullet. \n\n\nReason- Paramoecium has a cavity (gullet) that opens to the outside of the cell surface.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','They have a cavity (gullet) that opens to the outside of the cell surface. The coordinated movement of rows of cilia causes the water laden with food to be steered into the gullet. Example: Paramoecium.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(21338,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Sporozoans have an infectious spore-like stage in their life cycle. \n\n\nReason- The most notorious is Plasmodium (malarial parasite) which causes malaria.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','This includes diverse organisms that have an infectious spore-like stage in their life cycle. The most notorious is Plasmodium (malarial parasite) which causes malaria, a disease which has a staggering effect on human population.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(21339,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- The fungi constitute a unique kingdom of saprophytic organisms. \n\n\nReason- They show a great diversity in anatomy and habitat.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','The fungi constitute a unique kingdom of heterotrophic organisms. They show a great diversity in morphology and habitat.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(21340,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Fungi are cosmopolitan and occur in air, water, soil and on animals and plants. \n\n\nReason- The common mushroom we eat and toadstools are fungi.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Fungi are cosmopolitan and occur in air, water, soil and on animals and plants. The common mushroom you eat and toadstools are also fungi.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(21341,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Fungi can be source of antibiotics i.e. Penicillium. \n\n\nReason- Fungi prefer to grow in cold and humid places.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Fungi are cosmopolitan and occur in air, water, soil and on animals and plants. They prefer to grow in warm and humid places.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(21342,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Some Fungi have septae or cross walls in their hyphae. \n\n\nReason- Some hyphae are continuous tubes filled with multinucleated cytoplasm called coenocytic hyphae.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Some hyphae are continuous tubes filled with multinucleated cytoplasm – these are called coenocytic hyphae. Others have septae or cross walls in their hyphae.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21401,'chemistry','The French physicist, _______ , in _______ proposed that matter, like radiation, should also exhibit dual behaviour i.e., both particle and wavelike properties.','Louis de Broglie, 1924','"The French physicist, de Broglie, in 1924 proposed that matter, like radiation, should also exhibit dual behaviour i.e., both particle and wavelike properties."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.49; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Towards quantum mechanical model of the atom',NULL,'2','mcq','Louis de Broglie, 1924','Werner Heisenberg, 1926','Bohr, 1906','Heinrich Hertz,1918','NEW','11-049','11-049',NULL),
(21402,'chemistry','What will be the wavelength of a ball of mass 0.1 kg moving with a velocity of 10 ms⁻¹ ?','6.626× 10⁻³⁴m ( J=kgm² s⁻²)','"According to de Brogile equation, \n\nλ = h/mv \n\n= 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js/(0.1 kg)(10 ms⁻¹) = 6.626× 10⁻³⁴m ( J=kgm² s⁻²)"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.50; Problem 2.12','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Towards quantum mechanical model of the atom',NULL,'2','mcq','6.626 × 10⁻³⁰m ( J=kgm² s⁻²)','6.626× 10⁻³⁴m ( J=kgm² s⁻²)','6.626 × 10⁻³²m ( J=kgm² s⁻²)','6.626 × 10⁻³¹m ( J=kgm² s⁻²)','NEW','11-050','11-050',NULL),
(21403,'chemistry','The mass of an electron is 9.1 ×10⁻³¹ kg. If its K.E. is 3.0×10⁻²⁵ J, calculate its wavelength.','896.7 nm','"Since, K.E. = ½ mv² \n\nv = [2K.E./m]¹/² = [2 x 3 x 10⁻²⁵ kgm²s⁻²/9.1 x 10⁻³¹ kg]¹/² = 812 ms⁻¹ \n\nλ = h/mv = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js/(9.1 x 10⁻³¹ kg x 812 ms⁻¹) \n\n= 8967 × 10⁻¹⁰ m = 896.7 nm"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.50; Problem 2.13','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Towards quantum mechanical model of the atom',NULL,'3','mcq','890.7 nm','89.67 nm','876.7 nm','896.7 nm','NEW','11-050','11-050',NULL),
(21404,'chemistry','Calculate the mass of a photon with wavelength 3.6 Å.','6.135 × 10⁻²⁹ kg','"λ = 3.6 Å = 3.6 × 10⁻¹⁰ m \n\nVelocity of photon = velocity of light = 3 x 10⁸ ms⁻¹\n\nm = h/λv = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js/(3.6 x 10⁻¹⁰ m x 3 x 10⁸ ms⁻¹)\n \n= 6.135 × 10⁻²⁹ kg"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.50; Problem 2.14','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Towards quantum mechanical model of the atom',NULL,'3','mcq','6.135 × 10⁻³⁰ kg','6.135 × 10⁻³¹ kg','6.135 × 10⁻²⁹ kg','6.135 × 10⁻²⁸ kg','NEW','11-050','11-050',NULL),
(21405,'chemistry','Select the correct statements regarding Heisenberg’s Uncertainty Principle:\n \n (a) It states that it is impossible to determine simultaneously, the exact position and exact momentum (or velocity) of an electron. \n \n (b) ∆x . ∆pₓ < h/4π, where ∆x is the uncertainty in position and ∆pₓ (or ∆vₓ) is the uncertainty in momentum (or velocity) of the particle.\n \n (c) If the position of the electron is known with high degree of accuracy (∆x is small), then the velocity of the electron will be uncertain [∆(vₓ) is large].\n \n (d) If the velocity of the electron is known precisely (∆(vₓ) is small), then the position of the electron will be uncertain (∆x will be large).\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(a), (c) and (d)','"Werner Heisenberg a German physicist in 1927, stated uncertainty principle which is the consequence of dual behaviour of matter and radiation. It states that it is impossible to determine simultaneously, the exact position and exact momentum (or velocity) of an electron. Mathematically, it can be given as in equation \n\n∆x . ∆pₓ ≥ h/4π\nor  ∆x . ∆(mvₓ) ≥ h/4π\nor  ∆x . ∆vₓ ≥ h/4πm\n\nwhere ∆x is the uncertainty in position and ∆pₓ (or ∆vₓ) is the uncertainty in momentum (or velocity) of the particle. If the position of the electron is known with high degree of accuracy (∆x is small), then the velocity of the electron will be uncertain [∆(vₓ) is large]. On the other hand, if the velocity of the electron is known precisely (∆(vₓ) is small), then the position of the electron will be uncertain (∆x will be large). Thus, if we carry out some physical measurements on the electron’s position or velocity, the outcome will always depict a fuzzy or blur picture."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.51; Paragraph 1,2)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Towards quantum mechanical model of the atom',NULL,'3','mcq','(b), (c) and (d)','(a), (c) and (d)','(a), (b) and (c)','All (a), (b), (c) and (d)','NEW','11-051','11-051',NULL),
(21406,'chemistry','Select the incorrect statements regarding significance of Heisenberg’s Uncertainty Principle:\n \n (a) Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle doesn\'t rule out the existence of definite paths or trajectories of electrons and other similar particles.\n \n (b) The trajectory of an object is determined by its location and velocity at various moments.\n \n (c) The position of an object and its velocity fix its trajectory.\n \n (d) For a sub-atomic object such as an electron, it is possible simultaneously to determine the position and velocity at any given instant to an arbitrary degree of precision.\n \n (e) The effect of Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle is significant only for motion of microscopic objects and is negligible for that of macroscopic objects.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(a) and (d)','"One of the important implications of the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle is that it rules out existence of definite paths or trajectories of electrons and other similar particles. The trajectory of an object is determined by its location and velocity at various moments. If we know where a body is at a particular instant and if we also know its velocity and the forces acting on it at that instant, we can tell where the body would be sometime later. We, therefore, conclude that the position of an object and its velocity fix its trajectory. Since for a sub-atomic object such as an electron, it is not possible simultaneously to determine the position and velocity at any given instant to an arbitrary degree of precision, it is not possible to talk of the trajectory of an electron. The effect of Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle is significant only for motion of microscopic objects and is negligible for that of macroscopic objects."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.51; Paragraph 5,6)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Towards quantum mechanical model of the atom',NULL,'3','mcq','(a) and (d)','(b) and (c)','(c) and (d)','(b) and (d)','NEW','11-051','11-051',NULL),
(21407,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): If uncertainty principle is applied to an object of mass about a milligram (10⁻⁶ kg), the associated uncertainties are hardly of any real consequence. \n \n Reason(R): If uncertainty principle is applied to an object of mass 10⁻⁶ kg, then\n ∆v.∆x = h/4πm = 10⁻²⁸ m⁻²s⁻¹\n \n The value of ∆v∆x obtained is extremely small and is insignificant. Therefore, one may say that in dealing with milligram-sized or heavier objects, the associated uncertainties are hardly of any real consequence.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"If uncertainty principle is applied to an object of mass about a milligram (10⁻⁶ kg), then\n\n∆v.∆x = h/4πm = 10⁻²⁸ m⁻²s⁻¹\n\nThe value of ∆v∆x obtained is extremely small and is insignificant. Therefore, one may say that in dealing with milligram-sized or heavier objects, the associated uncertainties are hardly of any real consequence."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.51, 52; Paragraph 7, 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Towards quantum mechanical model of the atom',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-051','11-051',NULL),
(40408,'biology','Identify the organism shown in diagram (0422)','Locust','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 53; Figure 4.12(a) )','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Arthropoda,Mollusca,Echinodermata','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Locust','Butterfly','Scorpion','Prawn','NEW','11-053','11-044','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40408q.png'),
(21408,'chemistry','A microscope using suitable photons is employed to locate an electron in an atom within a distance of 0.1 Å. What is the uncertainty involved in the measurement of its velocity?','5.79 x 10⁶ m/s','"∆x.∆p = h/4π or ∆x.m∆v = h/4π \n\n∆v = h/4π∆xm \n\n∆v = 6.626 x 10⁻³⁴ Js/(4 x 3.14)(0.1 x 10⁻¹⁰ m)(9.11 x 10⁻³¹ kg) \n\n= 5.79 x 10⁶ m/s"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.52; Problem 2.15','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Towards quantum mechanical model of the atom',NULL,'2','mcq','5.79 x 10⁷ m/s','5.79 x 10⁸ m/s','5.79 x 10⁵ m/s','5.79 x 10⁶ m/s','NEW','11-052','11-052',NULL),
(21409,'chemistry','The effect of Heisenberg uncertainty principle is significant only for motion of _______ objects.','Microscopic','"The effect of Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle is significant only for motion of microscopic objects and is negligible for that of macroscopic objects."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.51; Paragraph 6','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Towards quantum mechanical model of the atom',NULL,'1','mcq','Microscopic','Macroscopic','Microscalic','Both (a) and (b)','NEW','11-051','11-051',NULL),
(21410,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Many members like morels and truffles are edible and are considered delicacies.\n\n\nReason- Fruiting bodies of Sac-fungi are called ascocarps.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Many members like morels and truffles are edible and are considered delicacies. These asci are arranged in different types of fruiting bodies called ascocarps.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-024','11-018',NULL),
(21411,'chemistry','A golf ball has a mass of 40 g, and a speed of 45 m/s. If the speed can be measured within accuracy of 2%, calculate the uncertainty in the position.','1.46 × 10⁻³³ m','"The uncertainty in the speed is 2%, i.e.,\n\n45 x (2/100) = 0.9 m/s\n\nUsing the equation,\n\n∆x = h/4πm∆v\n\n= 6.626 x 10⁻³⁴ Js/(4 x 3.14)(40 g x 10⁻³ kgg⁻¹)(0.9 m/s) \n\n= 1.46 × 10⁻³³ m\n\nThis is nearly ~ 10⁻¹⁸ times smaller than the diameter of a typical atomic nucleus."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.52; Problem 2.16','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Towards quantum mechanical model of the atom',NULL,'3','mcq','1.46 × 10⁻⁶⁸ m','1.46 × 10⁻³³ m','1.46 × 10⁻³⁵ m','1.46 × 10⁻³⁴ m','NEW','11-052','11-052',NULL),
(21412,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): An electron beam undergoes diffraction, a phenomenon characteristic of waves. This fact has been put to use in making an electron microscope. \n \n Reason(R): An electron microscope is a powerful tool in modern scientific research because it achieves a magnification of about 15 billion times.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(A) is true but (R) is false.','"De Broglie’s prediction was confirmed experimentally when it was found that an electron beam undergoes diffraction, a phenomenon characteristic of waves. This fact has been put to use in making an electron microscope, which is based on the wavelike behaviour of electrons just as an ordinary microscope utilises the wave nature of light. An electron microscope is a powerful tool in modern scientific research because it achieves a magnification of about 15 million times."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.50; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Towards quantum mechanical model of the atom',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-050','11-050',NULL),
(21413,'chemistry','Select the correct statements regarding failure of Bohr\'s model:\n \n (a) In Bohr model, an electron is regarded as a charged particle moving in well defined circular orbits about the nucleus. \n \n (b) The wave character of the electron is considered in Bohr model. \n \n (c) An orbit is a clearly defined path and this path can completely be defined only if both the position and the velocity of the electron are known exactly at the same time. \n \n (d) Bohr model of the hydrogen atom considers both dual behaviour of matter and Heisenberg uncertainty principle.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(a) and (c)','"In Bohr model, an electron is regarded as a charged particle moving in well defined circular orbits about the nucleus. The wave character of the electron is not considered in Bohr model. Further, an orbit is a clearly defined path and this path can completely be defined only if both the position and the velocity of the electron are known exactly at the same time. This is not possible according to the Heisenberg uncertainty principle. Bohr model of the hydrogen atom, therefore, not only ignores dual behaviour of matter but also contradicts Heisenberg uncertainty principle."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.52; Paragraph 5','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Towards quantum mechanical model of the atom',NULL,'3','mcq','(a) and (c)','(b) and (c)','(a) and (d)','(a), (c) and (d)','NEW','11-052','11-052',NULL),
(21414,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- In Basidiomycetes, asexual spores are generally not found.\n\n\nReason- The mycelium is branched and septate in them.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The mycelium is branched and septate. The asexual spores are generally not found.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-024','11-018',NULL),
(21415,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion-The sex organs are absent in bracket fungi.\n\n\nReason- In them, plasmogamy is brought about by fusion of two vegetative or somatic cells of different strains or genotypes.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The sex organs are absent, but plasmogamy is brought about by fusion of two vegetative or somatic cells of different strains or genotypes.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(40409,'biology','Which mosquito isn\'t a vector?','None of the above','Vectors – Anopheles for Plasmodium(malaria), Culex for Wuchereria(Filariasis) and Aedes for Dengue (Mosquitoes).\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 53; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Arthropoda,Mollusca,Echinodermata','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Anopheles','Culex','Aedes','None of the above','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(21416,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- The basidiospores are endogenously produced on the basidium.\n\n\nReason- Karyogamy and meiosis take place in the basidium producing two basidiospores.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Karyogamy and meiosis take place in the basidium producing four basidiospores. The basidiospores are exogenously produced on the basidium.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(21417,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Rusts and smuts are included in Basidiomycetes. \n\n\nReason- Ustilago is a rust, Puccinia is a smut and Agaricus is mushroom.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','The basidia are arranged in fruiting bodies called basidiocarps. Some common members are Agaricus (mushroom) ,Ustilago (smut) and Puccinia (rust fungus).','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-024','11-018',NULL),
(21418,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Deuteromycetes is known as imperfect fungi.\n\n \nReason- Only the asexual or vegetative phases of these fungi are known.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Commonly known as imperfect fungi because only the asexual or vegetative phases of these fungi are known.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-024','11-018',NULL),
(21419,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- The mycelium is septate and branched in Deutromycetes. \n\n\nReason- The deuteromycetes reproduce only by asexual spores known as conidia.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The deuteromycetes reproduce only by asexual spores\nknown as conidia. The mycelium is septate and branched.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-024','11-018',NULL),
(21420,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- All members of Deutromycetes are decomposers of litter and help in mineral cycling. \n\n\nReason- Alternaria, Colletotrichum and Trichoderma are unbranched.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','The mycelium is septate and branched. Some members are saprophytes or parasites while a large number of them are decomposers of litter and help in mineral cycling. Some examples are Alternaria, Colletotrichum and Trichoderma.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(21421,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Kingdom Plantae includes all prokaryotic chlorophyll-containing organisms commonly called plants. \n\n\nReason- Life cycle of plants has two distinct phases – the diploid gametophytic and the haploid sporophytic – that alternate with each other.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Kingdom Plantae includes all eukaryotic chlorophyll-containing organisms commonly called plants. Life cycle of plants has two distinct phases – the diploid sporophytic and the haploid gametophytic – that alternate with each other.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-025','11-019',NULL),
(21422,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Cuscuta is insectivorous. \n\n\nReason- Bladderwort and Venus fly trap are examples of parasitic plants.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Bladderwort and Venus fly trap are examples of insectivorous plants. Cuscuta is a parasite.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-025','11-019',NULL),
(21423,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Animal Kingdom and choose the correct option.\n1. They digest their food in an internal cavity and store food reserves as glucose or fat. \n\n2. They directly or indirectly depend on plants for food. \n\n3. Their cells lack cell walls. \n\n4. This kingdom is characterised by heterotrophic eukaryotic organisms that are multicellular or unicellular.','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','This kingdom is characterised by heterotrophic eukaryotic organisms that are multicellular and their cells lack cell walls. They directly or indirectly depend on plants for food. They digest their food in an internal cavity and store food reserves as glycogen or fat.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 3 and 4 are correct','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','Only 2 is correct','All the statements are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21424,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Animal Kingdom and choose the correct option.\n1. All forms show elaborate sensory and neuromotor mechanism.\n\n2. Some of them are capable of locomotion.\n\n3.  They follow an indefinite growth pattern and grow into adults that have a definite shape and size\n\n4.  Their mode of nutrition is heterozoic – by ingestion of food.','All the statements are incorrect','Their mode of nutrition is holozoic – by ingestion of food. They follow a definite growth pattern and grow into adults that have a definite shape and size. Higher forms show elaborate sensory and neuromotor mechanism. Most of them are\ncapable of locomotion.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 3 and 4 are correct','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','Only 2 is correct','All the statements are incorrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21425,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- In the five kingdom classification of Whittaker there is no mention of lichens and some acellular organisms.\n\nReason- Viruses, viroids and prions are cellular.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','In the five kingdom classification of Whittaker there is no mention of lichens and some acellular organisms like viruses, viroids and prions.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-017','11-011',NULL),
(21426,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Viruses did not find a place in classification.\n\nReason- The viruses are non-cellular organisms that are characterised by having an inert crystalline structure outside the living cell.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Viruses did not find a place in classification since they are not considered truly ‘living’, if we understand living as those organisms that have a cell structure. The viruses are non-cellular organisms that are characterised by having an inert crystalline structure outside the living cell.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-025','11-019',NULL),
(21427,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Virus means venom or poisonous fluid. \n\n\nReason- Virus once they infect a cell they take over the machinery of the host cell to replicate themselves, killing the host.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Virus means venom or poisonous fluid. \nOnce they infect a cell they take over the machinery of the host cell to replicate\nthemselves, killing the host.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-025','11-019',NULL),
(21428,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Pasteur (1892) recognised certain microbes as causal organism of the mosaic disease of tobacco.\n\n\nReason- Ivanowsky (1898) demonstrated that the extract of the infected plants of tobacco could cause infection in healthy plants.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Dmitri Ivanowsky (1892) recognised certain microbes as causal organism of the mosaic disease of tobacco. These were found to be smaller than bacteria because they passed through bacteria-proof filters.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-026','11-020',NULL),
(21429,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Virus were found to be smaller than bacteria.\n\n\nReason- They passed through bacteria-proof filters.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Dmitri Ivanowsky (1892) recognised certain microbes as causal organism of the mosaic disease of tobacco. These were found to be smaller than bacteria because they passed through bacteria-proof filters. M.W. Beijerinek (1898) demonstrated that the extract of the infected plants of tobacco could cause infection in healthy plants and named the new pathogen “virus” and called the fluid as Contagium vivum fluidum (infectious living fluid).','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-026','11-020',NULL),
(21430,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Viruses are obligate parasites.\n\n\nReason- They are inert outside their specific host cell.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','They are inert outside their specific host cell. Viruses are obligate parasites.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-026','11-020',NULL),
(21431,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- M.W. Beijerinek named the new pathogen “virus” and called the fluid as Contagium vivum fluidum.\n\n\nReason- W.M. Stanley (1935) showed that viruses could be crystallised and crystals consist largely of proteins.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','M.W. Beijerinek (1898) demonstrated that the extract of the infected plants of tobacco could cause infection in healthy plants and named the new pathogen “virus” and called the fluid as Contagium vivum fluidum (infectious living fluid). W.M. Stanley (1935) showed that viruses could be crystallised and crystals consist largely of proteins.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-026','11-020',NULL),
(21432,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Virus can have both DNA and RNA.\n\n\nReason- A virus is a nucleoprotein and the protein is infectious.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','No virus contains both RNA and DNA. A virus is a nucleoprotein and the genetic material is infectious.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-026','11-020',NULL),
(21433,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- In general, viruses that infect plants have single stranded RNA.\n\n\nReason- Viruses that infect animals have either single or double stranded DNA or double stranded RNA.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','In general, viruses that infect plants have single stranded RNA and viruses that infect animals have either single or double stranded RNA or double stranded DNA.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-026','11-020',NULL),
(21434,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Bacteriophages are viruses that infect the bacteria.\n\n\nReason- They are  usually double stranded DNA viruses or ss RNA viruses.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Bacterial viruses or bacteriophages (viruses that infect the bacteria) are usually double stranded DNA viruses.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-026','11-020',NULL),
(21435,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- The protein coat of virus is called capsid that is made of small subunits called capsomeres.\n\n\nReason- It protects the genetic material.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The protein coat called capsid made of small subunits called capsomeres, protects the nucleic acid.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-026','11-020',NULL),
(21436,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Viruses cause diseases like mumps, small pox, herpes, pneumonia and influenza. \n\n\nReason- Capsomeres are arranged in circular or polyhedral geometric forms.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Capsomeres are arranged in helical or polyhedral geometric forms. Pneumonia is caused by bacteria.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-026','11-020',NULL),
(21437,'biology','Consider these statements regarding viral symptoms in plants and choose the correct option.\n1. Yellowing of leaves occur and veins start to clear.\n\n2. Dwarfing and stunted growth is seen among plants.\n\n3. The leaves roll and curl.\n\n4. Mosaic formation occurs.','All the statements are correct','In plants, the symptoms can be mosaic formation, leaf rolling and curling, yellowing and vein clearing, dwarfing and stunted growth.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Assertion and Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 3 and 4 are correct','Only 2 is correct','Both 3 and 4 are correct','All the statements are correct','NEW','11-026','11-020',NULL),
(21501,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): Classical mechanics fails when applied to microscopic objects like electrons, atoms, molecules etc.\n \n Reason(R): Classical mechanics ignores the concept of dual behaviour of matter especially for sub-atomic particles and the uncertainty principle.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"Classical mechanics, based on Newton’s laws of motion, successfully describes the motion of all macroscopic objects such as a falling stone, orbiting planets etc., which have essentially a particle-like behaviour. However it fails when applied to microscopic objects like electrons, atoms, molecules etc. This is mainly because of the fact that classical mechanics ignores the concept of dual behaviour of matter especially for sub-atomic particles and the uncertainty principle."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.53; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-053','11-053',NULL),
(21502,'chemistry','The branch of science that takes into account the dual (wave and particle) behaviour of matter is called _______.','Quantum mechanics','"The branch of science that takes into account this dual (wave and particle) behaviour of matter is called quantum mechanics."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.53; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom',NULL,'1','mcq','Quantum mechanics','Spectroscopy','Classical mechanics','Kinetics','NEW','11-053','11-053',NULL),
(21503,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \nAssertion(A): Quantum mechanics is a theoretical science that deals with the study of the motions of the microscopic objects that have both observable wave like and particle like properties. \n \nReason(R): When quantum mechanics is applied to macroscopic objects (for which wave like properties are insignificant) the results are the same as those from the classical mechanics.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','"Quantum mechanics is a theoretical science that deals with the study of the motions of the microscopic objects that have both observable wave like and particle like properties. It specifies the laws of motion that these objects obey. When quantum mechanics is applied to macroscopic objects (for which wave like properties are insignificant) the results are the same as those from the classical mechanics."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.53; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-053','11-053',NULL),
(21504,'chemistry','Quantum mechanics was developed independently in 1926 by:\n \n (a) Werner Heisenberg\n (b) Erwin Schrödinger\n (c) Louis de Broglie.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (a) and (b)','"Quantum mechanics was developed independently in 1926 by Werner Heisenberg and Erwin Schrödinger."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.53; Paragraph 5','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (a) and (b)','Both (a) and (c)','Both (b) and (c)','All (a), (b) and (c)','NEW','11-053','11-053',NULL),
(21505,'chemistry','The fundamental equation of quantum mechanics was developed by _______.','Erwin Schrödinger','"The fundamental equation of quantum mechanics was developed by Schrödinger and it won him the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1933."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.53; Paragraph 5','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom',NULL,'2','mcq','Werner Heisenberg','Erwin Schrödinger','Louis de Broglie','Both a and b','NEW','11-053','11-053',NULL),
(21506,'chemistry','The total energy of the system (an atom or particle whose energy does not change with time) takes into account:\n \n (i) The kinetic energies of electrons.\n \n (ii) The kinetic energies of nuclei.\n \n (iii) Attractive potential between the electrons and nuclei.\n \n (iv) Repulsive potential among the electrons.\n \n (v) Repulsive potential among the nuclei.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv), (v)','"The total energy of the system (an atom or particle whose energy does not change with time) takes into account the kinetic energies of all the subatomic particles (electrons, nuclei), attractive potential between the electrons and nuclei and repulsive potential among the electrons and nuclei individually."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.53; Paragraph 6','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom',NULL,'2','mcq','(i), (ii), (iii)','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv), (v)','(iii), (iv), (v)','(i), (ii), (iii), (v)','NEW','11-053','11-053',NULL),
(21507,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Lichens are mutually useful associations, between algae and fungi. \n\n\nReason- The algal component is known as mycobiont and fungal component as phycobiont','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Lichens are symbiotic associations i.e. mutually useful associations, between algae and fungi. The algal component is known as phycobiont and fungal component as mycobiont, which are autotrophic and heterotrophic, respectively.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-027','11-021',NULL),
(21508,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Lichens are very good pollution indicators.\n\n\nReason- Lichens do not grow in polluted areas.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Lichens are very good pollution indicators – they do not grow in polluted areas.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-027','11-021',NULL),
(21509,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Term Lichen was coined by Theophrastus.\n\n\nReason- Lichens are indicators of sulphur dioxide pollution.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Term Lichen was coined by Theophrastus. Lichens are indicators of sulphur dioxide pollution.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-027','11-021',NULL),
(21510,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Fungi and choose the correct option.\n1. Yeast are used to make bread and beer. \n\n2. Black spots seen on mustard leaves are due to a parasitic fungus. \n\n3. Puccinia causes wheat rust.\n\n4. Yeast is unicellular.','Only 2 is incorrect','White spots seen on mustard leaves are due to a parasitic fungus. Some unicellular fungi, e.g., yeast are used to make bread and beer. Other fungi cause diseases in plants and animals; wheat rust-causing Puccinia is an important example.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 2 is incorrect','Only 4 is correct','Only 1 is correct','Both 1 and 3 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21511,'biology','Consider these statements regarding structure of Fungus and choose the correct option.\n1. The cell walls of fungi are composed of chitin and peptidoglycan. \n\n2. The network of mycelium is called hyphae. \n\n3. Their bodies consist of long, slender thread-like structures called mycelium. \n\n4. Fungi are filamentous.','Only 4 is correct','The cell walls of fungi are composed of chitin and polysaccharides. With the exception of yeasts which are unicellular, fungi are filamentous. Their bodies consist of long, slender thread-like structures called hyphae. The network of hyphae is known as mycelium.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 4 is correct','Only 2 is incorrect','Only 1 is correct','Both 1 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(21512,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Most fungi are heterotrophic. \n\n\nReason- Most fungi are heterotrophic and absorb soluble organic matter from dead substrates.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Most fungi are heterotrophic and absorb soluble organic matter from dead substrates and hence are called saprophytes.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(21513,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Fungi that depend on living plants and animals are called parasites. \n\n\nReason- They can also live as symbionts – in association with algae as mycorrhiza and with roots of higher plants as lichens.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Those that depend on living plants and animals are called parasites. They can also live as symbionts – in association with algae as lichens and with roots of higher\nplants as mycorrhiza.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-027','11-021',NULL),
(21514,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Reproduction in fungi can take place by asexual means – fragmentation, fission and budding. \n\n\nReason- vegetative Reproduction is by conidia or sporangiospores or zoospores.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Reproduction in fungi can take place by vegetative means –fragmentation, fission and budding. Asexual reproduction is by spores called conidia or sporangiospores or zoospores.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-022','11-016',NULL),
(21515,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- In Fungi, sexual reproduction is by oospores, ascospores and basidiospores. \n\n\nReason- The various spores are produced in distinct structures called fruiting bodies.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Sexual reproduction is by oospores, ascospores and basidiospores. The various spores are produced in distinct structures called fruiting bodies.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-024','11-018',NULL),
(21516,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Fusion of protoplasms between two motile or non-motile gametes is called karyogamy. \n\n\nReason- Fusion of two nuclei called plasmogamy.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Fusion of protoplasms between two motile or non-motile gametes called plasmogamy. Fusion of two nuclei called karyogamy.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(21517,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- When a fungus reproduces sexually, two haploid hyphae of compatible mating types come together and fuse. \n\n\nReason- Meiosis in zygote resulting in haploid spores.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Meiosis in zygote resulting in haploid spores. When a fungus reproduces sexually, two haploid hyphae of compatible mating types come together and fuse.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-023','11-017',NULL),
(21518,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Bacteria depend on other organisms or on dead organic matter for food.\n\n\nReason- Some of the bacteria are autotrophic.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Some of the bacteria are autotrophic, i.e., they synthesise their own food from inorganic substrates. They may be photosynthetic autotrophic or chemosynthetic autotrophic. The vast majority of bacteria are heterotrophs, i.e., they depend on other organisms or on dead organic matter for food.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21519,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- The vast majority of bacteria are heterotrophs.\n\n\nReason- They may be photosynthetic autotrophic or chemosynthetic heterotrophs.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Some of the bacteria are autotrophic, i.e., they synthesise their own food from inorganic substrates. They may be photosynthetic autotrophic or chemosynthetic autotrophic. The vast majority of bacteria are heterotrophs, i.e., they depend on other organisms or on dead organic matter for food.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21520,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Archaebacteria are special since they live in some of the most harsh habitats.\n\n\nReason- They live in extreme salty areas, hot springs and marshy areas.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','These bacteria are special since they live in some of the most harsh habitats such as extreme salty areas (halophiles), hot springs (thermoacidophiles) and marshy areas (methanogens).','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-019','11-013',NULL),
(21521,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Archaebacteria differ from other bacteria in having a different cell wall structure.\n\n\nReason- In them, peptidoglycan is absent and branch chain lipids are present.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Archaebacteria differ from other bacteria in having a different cell wall structure and this feature is responsible for their survival in extreme conditions.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-019','11-013',NULL),
(40410,'biology','Find the feature that is not found in arthropoda.','They are radially symmetrical','Arthropods are bilaterally symmetrical, triploblastic, segmented and coelomate animals They are mostly dioecious. Fertilisation is usually internal. They are mostly oviparous. Development may be direct or indirect.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 53; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Arthropoda,Mollusca,Echinodermata','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','They are mostly dioecious','Fertilisation is usually internal','They are mostly oviparous','They are radially symmetrical','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(21522,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Methanogens are present in the gut of several ruminant animals such as cows and buffaloes.\n\n\nReason- Methanogens are responsible for the production of carbon dioxide (biogas) from the dung of these.\nanimals.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Methanogens are present in the gut of several ruminant animals such as cows and buffaloes and they are responsible for the production of methane (biogas) from the dung of these animals.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-019','11-013',NULL),
(21523,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- Azotobacter and Biejerinca are free living bacteria. \n\n\nReason- They fix nitrogen as well as sulphur.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Azotobacter and Biejerinca are free living nitrogen fixing bacteria.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-019','11-013',NULL),
(21524,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Archaebacteria and choose the correct option.\n1. Methanogens live in marshy areas.\n\n2. Bacteria living in hotsprings have high pH for surviving high temperatures. \n\n3. Thermoacidophiles are found in hotsprings.\n\n4. They are found in salty areas and are called halophiles.','Only 2 is incorrect','These bacteria are special since they live in some of the most harsh habitats such as extreme salty areas (halophiles), hot springs (thermoacidophiles) having low pH and high temperature tolerance, and marshy areas (methanogens).','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are correct','All the statements are correct','NEW','11-019','11-013',NULL),
(21525,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\nAssertion- There are a very few different eubacteria or ‘true bacteria’. \n\n\nReason- They are characterised by the presence of a flexible cell wall, and if motile, a flagellum.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','There are thousands of different eubacteria or ‘true bacteria’. They are characterised by the presence of a rigid cell wall, and if motile, a flagellum.','Diversity In Living World >> Biological Classification >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-019','11-013',NULL),
(40101,'chemistry','No elements can exist independently except:','Noble gases','Noble gases have completely filled valance shell.\n\nAn atom bonds with other atoms to completely fill its valance shell. \n\nThe valence shell of Noble gases are already filled, so they exist independently. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 100; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Introduction',NULL,'3','mcq','Alkali metals','Halogens','Noble gases','Chalcogens','NEW','11-100','11-100',NULL),
(40102,'chemistry','Atoms combine to form:','Molecules','A group of atoms bond together to exist as one species. It is the smallest unit  of matter. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 100; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Molecules','Ions','Compounds','Structures','NEW','11-100','11-100','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40102q.png'),
(40103,'chemistry','A chemical bond is a force that holds ______ together in different chemical species.','Atoms and Ions','The attractive force that holds various constituents (atoms, ions, etc.) together in different chemical species is called a chemical bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 100; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Electrons and Protons','Atoms and Ions','Ions and Compounds','Atoms and Molecules','NEW','11-100','11-100',NULL),
(40104,'chemistry','Atoms make chemical bonds to ______ their energy.','Lower','Atoms bond  with each other to form compounds to attain lower energies than they possess as individual atoms. \n\nReference : (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 100; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Introduction',NULL,'1','fillup','Lo,w,er,hi,gh',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-100','11-100',NULL),
(40105,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Matter is made up of one or different type of elements.\n\nR: Under normal conditions no  element exists as an independent atom in nature.','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Matter is made up of one or different type of elements. \n\nUnder normal conditions no other element exists as an independent atom in nature, except noble gases. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 100; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-100','11-100',NULL),
(40106,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: A group of atoms is found to exist together as one species having characteristic properties. Such a group of atoms is called a molecule.\n\nR: There must be some force which holds these constituent atoms together in the molecules.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','However, a group of atoms is found to exist together as one species having characteristic properties. \n\nSuch a group of atoms is called a molecule. \n\nObviously there must be some force which holds these constituent atoms together in the molecules. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 100; paragraph','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-100','11-100',NULL),
(40107,'chemistry','The evolution of various theories of valence and the interpretation of the nature of chemical bonds have closely been related to:','All of these','The evolution of various theories of valence and the interpretation of the nature of chemical bonds have closely been related to the developments in the understanding of the structure of atom, the electronic configuration of elements and the periodic table. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 100; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','The structure of atom','The electronic configuration of elements','The periodic table','All of these','NEW','11-100','11-100','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40107q.png'),
(40108,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Since the formation of chemical compounds takes place as a result of combination of atoms of various elements in different ways, it raises many questions.\n\nR: To answer such questions different theories and concepts have been put forward from time to time.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Since the formation of chemical compounds takes place as a result of combination of atoms of various elements in different ways, it raises many questions. \n\nWhy do atoms combine? Why are only certain combinations possible? Why do some atoms combine while certain others do not? Why do molecules possess definite shapes? \n\nTo answer such questions different theories and concepts have been put forward from time to time. Scientists have developed various theories and models to explain the formation of chemical compounds, such as the Lewis theory, VSEPR theory, and molecular orbital theory, among others.\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 100; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-100','11-100','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40108q.png'),
(40109,'biology','Identify the symmetry in the diagram (0402)','Bilateral Symmetry','Animals like annelids, arthropods, etc., where the body can be divided into identical left and right halves in only one plane, exhibit bilateral symmetry (Figure 4.1b).. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 47; Figure 4.1(b)) diagram (e0402)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Basis of Classification','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Radial Symmetry','Bilateral Symmetry','Assymetry','None of the above','NEW','11-047','11-038','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40109q.png'),
(40110,'biology','Diploblastic and triploblastic are terms that describe -','The number of tissue layers during embryonic developments','Animals in which the cells are arranged in two embryonic layers, an external ectoderm and an internal endoderm, are called diploblastic\nanimals, e.g., coelenterates. An undifferentiated layer, mesoglea, is present in between the ectoderm and the endoderm.Those animals in which the developing embryo has a third germinal layer, mesoderm, in between the ectoderm and endoderm, are called triploblastic animals (platyhelminthes to chordates).(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 47; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Basis of Classification','Animal Kingdom','1','mcq','The number of invaginations during development','The number of heads during development','The number of tissue layers during embryonic developments','The number of cell types during development','NEW','11-047','11-038',NULL),
(40111,'biology','Match the parts in the following diagram (0403)','a-Mesoglea,b-Ectoderm,c-Endoderm,d-Mesoderm','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 47; Figure 4.2 ) diagram (e0404)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Basis of Classification','Animal Kingdom','3','match','a,Mesoglea','b,Ectoderm','c,Endoderm','d,Mesoderm','NEW','11-047','11-038','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/40111q.png');
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(40112,'biology','An undifferentiated layer present between ectoderm and endoderm is called -','Mesoglea','In diploblastic animals Mesoderm is absent instead an undifferentiated layer called mesoglea is present. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 47; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Basis of Classification','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Mesoglea','Mesoderm','Mesentery','Parenchyma','NEW','11-047','11-038',NULL),
(40113,'biology','Metamerism refers to','Body is externally and internally divided into segments with a serial repetition of at least some organs','In some animals, the body is externally and internally divided into segments with a serial repetition of at least some organs. For example, in earthworm, the body shows this pattern called metameric segmentation and the phenomenon is known as metamerism. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 48; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Basis of Classification','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Mesoblastic development','Occurrence of mesoderm','Body is externally and internally divided into segments with a serial repetition of at least some organs','Metastasis','NEW','11-048','11-039',NULL),
(40114,'biology','Metamerism is the characteristic of','Annelida','Metamerism is shown by organisms that have segmented body.The property of having repeated segments, as in annelids. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 48; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Basis of Classification','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Platyhelminthes','Mollusca','Porifera','Annelida','NEW','11-048','11-039',NULL),
(40115,'biology','The body cavity shown in diagram is of animals called as :(0404)','Coelomate','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 48; Figure 4.3(a) ) diagram (e0403)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Basis of Classification','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Coelomate','Pseudocoelomate','Acoelomate','None of the above','NEW','11-048','11-039','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40115q.png'),
(40116,'biology','The body cavity shown in diagram is of animals called as :(0405)','Pseudocoelomate','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 48; Figure 4.3(b) ) diagram (e0403)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Basis of Classification','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Coelomate','Pseudocoelomate','Acoelomate','None of the above','NEW','11-048','11-039','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40116q.png'),
(40117,'biology','The body cavity shown in diagram is of animals called as :(0406)','Acoelomate','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 48; Figure 4.3(c) ) diagram (e0403)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Basis of Classification','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Coelomate','Pseudocoelomate','Acoelomate','None of the above','NEW','11-048','11-039','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40117q.png'),
(40200,'biology','Spongocoel and canal are lined by','Choanocytes','Collar cells are flagellated cells that lines the spongocoel. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 49; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Porifera,Coelenterata,Ctenophora','Animal Kingdom','1','mcq','Porocytes','Choanocytes','Spicules','Amoebocytes','NEW','11-049','11-040',NULL),
(40201,'chemistry','Kossel and lewis independently succeeded in explaining chemical bonding in the year ____.','1916','In 1916, Kossel put forth his theory of the ionic chemical bond (octet rule), also independently advanced in the same year by Gilbert N. Lewis. \n\nReference : (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'1','mcq','1919','1916','1917','19109','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40202,'chemistry','Lewis proposed atoms having positvely charged nucleus and inner electrons collectively called as____.','Kernel','According to Lewis, the nucleus and inner sheel form kernel. \n\nThe outer shell can accomodate a maximum of eight electrons. \n\nThese eight outershell electrons occupy the corners of a cube which surround the kernel. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'1','mcq','Kernel','Charges','Orbitals','Octet','NEW','11-101','11-101','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40202q.png'),
(40203,'chemistry','According to lewis, atoms can have maximum 8 electrons in its  ______ shell.','Valence','The outer shell  or the valence shell of an atom can accommodate a maximum of eight electrons. \n\nOnly the outer or valence  shell electrons take part in chemical combination and they are known as valence electrons. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'1','mcq','Valence','Inactive','Inner','Active','NEW','11-101','11-101','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40203q.png'),
(40204,'chemistry','Lewis proposed that the valence electrons occupy the ________ of cube surrounding the kernel.','Corners','According to Lewis, the nucleus and inner sheel form kernel. The outer shell can accomodate a maximum of eight electrons. These eight  outershell electrons occupy the corners of a cube which surround the kernel. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'1','mcq','Corners','Angle','Junction','Deviation','NEW','11-101','11-101','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40204q.png'),
(40205,'chemistry','Sodium will occupy single croner of cube because it has ______ in its valance shell.','1 electron','According to Lewis, the outershell electrons occupy the corners of a cube which surround the kernel.\n\nSince Sodium has one electron in its valence or outershell, it occupies one corner of the cube. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'1','mcq','2 electrons','1 electron','3 electrons','Zero electron','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40206,'chemistry','The elements of group 18 will occupy _____.','8 corners of cube','According to Lewis, the outer shell electrons occupy the corners of a cube which surround the kernel. \n\nSince elements of groups 18 have 8 electrons in its outer or valence shell, it will occupy all the eight corners of the cube. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'3','mcq','8 corners of cube','1 corner of cube','6 corners of cube','no corner of cube','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40207,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement about achievement of octet by atoms:\n\na. Atoms gain octet by complete transfer of electron from one atom to other\n\nb. Atoms complete their octet by sharing of electrons \n\nc. Atoms gain octet by electrostatic force','Only a and b','Atoms achieve the stable octet when they are linked by chemical bonds.They can form covalent bonds by sharing of electrons and ionic bonds when electrons are transferred from one atom to another. \n\nReference : (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c','Only a and b','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40208,'chemistry','Ionic compounds attain octet by:','Transfer of electron from one atom to other','Sodium has an electronic configuration 2,8,1 and needs to loose one electron from its valance shell to attain octet configuration. \n\nChlorine has an electronic configuration 2,8,7 and needs to gain one electron to attain octet. \n\nSo Sodium transfers one electron to Chlorine atom to form an ionic bond to reach octet configuration and stability. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'1','mcq','Sharing of electrons between two atoms','Transfer of electron from one atom to other','Electrostatic forces','Loss of electrons','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40209,'chemistry','Covalent compounds gain octet by:','Sharing of electrons between two atoms','Two atoms can share electrons between them by formation of covalent bonds to reach octet configuration. \n\nChlorine atom has electronic configuration, [Ne]3s2 3p5 and needs one electron to reach argon configuration. \n\nEach chlorine atom contributes one electron to the shared pair. In the process both chlorine atoms attain the outer shell octet of the nearest noble gas (i.e., argon). The shared electrons form a covalent bond.\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'1','mcq','Sharing of electrons between two atoms','Transfer of electron from one atom to other','By electrostatic forces','Loss of electrons','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40210,'chemistry','Which electrons can take part in chemical bonding?','Outer electrons','In the formation of a chemical bond, only the outer shell electrons take part in chemical combination and they are known as valence electrons. \n\nThe inner shell electrons are well protected and are generally not involved in chemical combinations.\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'1','mcq','Outer electrons','Inner electrons','Excited electrons','Protons','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40211,'chemistry','In lewis structure, valance electrons are represented by:','Both a and b','In Lewis structure, dots and crossed are used to represent the valence electrons of two different atoms involved in the bond formation. \n\nReference : (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'1','mcq','Dot','Cross','Plus','Both a and b','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40212,'chemistry','The number of dots in lewis structure represent the number of ?','Valence electrons','Since only the valence electrons take part in a chemical reaction, only those electrons are represented in the Lewis dot structure. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'3','mcq','Valence electrons','Inner electrons','Core electrons','All electrons','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40213,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statement about valency:\n\na. Valency of atoms is equal to number of dots in lewis structure\n\nb. Valency of atoms is equal to group number\n\nc. Valency of atoms is also equal to 8 minus the group number\n\nd. Valency of atoms is equal to number of non bonding electrons','Only d','The group valence of the elements is generally either equal to the number of dots in Lewis symbols or 8 minus the number of dots or valence electrons.\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c','Only d','NEW','11-101','11-101','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40213q.png'),
(40411,'biology','______ is known as living fossil.','Limulus (king crab)','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 53; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Arthropoda,Mollusca,Echinodermata','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Limulus (king crab)','Locusta (locust)','Lacifer','Loligo','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(40412,'biology','Which phylum is the second largest animal phylum?','Mollusca','Mollusca is the 2nd largest phylum next to arthropoda. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 53; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Arthropoda,Mollusca,Echinodermata','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Mollusca','Arthopoda','Chordata','Cnidaria','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(40214,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statement:\n\na. Highly electronegative halogens and highly electropositive alkali metals are separated by the noble gases in periodic table\n\nb. Halogens make positive ions by gain of electron and alkali metals forms negative ions by loss of electron\n\nc. Elements lose or gain electrons in order to gain noble gas configuration\n\nd. The negative and positive ions are stabilized by Covalent bonding','Only b and d','Highly electronegative halogens and highly electropositive alkali metals are separated by the noble gases in periodic table. To form ionic compounds metals readily lose electrons and nonmetals (such as halogens) gain electrons.The negative and positive ions are stabilized by ionic or electrovalent bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c','Only b and d','NEW','11-101','11-101','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40214q.png'),
(40215,'chemistry','Sodium looses an electron and attains the electronic configuration of:','Ne','Ne =1s²2s²2p⁶\n\nNa⁺ = 1s²2s²2p⁶     \n                                                                                                  \nThe electronic configurationof Na = 1s²2s²2p⁶ 3s1  \n\nIt looses one one electron from 3s to reach the octet configuration of Ne.\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'3','mcq','Ne','Xe','Kr','He','NEW','11-101','11-101','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40215q.png'),
(40216,'chemistry','[Ne]3s²3p⁶ is similar to:','Ar','The electronic configuration of the given element is [Ne]3s²3p⁶. In this Neon has 10 electrons and 8 electrons from 3s²3p⁶ subshell  gives total of 18 electrons. \n\nThe atomic no. of Ar is 18. So the element is Argon. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'3','mcq','Ar','Ne','Xe','He','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40217,'chemistry','The electronic configuration of O²⁻ , F⁻ , N³⁻ is similar to:','Ne','Oxygen has 8 electrons, O²⁻ has 10 electrons. Fluorine has 9 electrons l, F⁻ has 8 electrons. \n\nNitrogen has 7 electrons and N³⁻ has 8 electrons. \n\nO²⁻, F⁻, N³⁻ have total 10 electrons similar to Neon so have same electronic configuration similar to Ne. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'3','mcq','Ar','Ne','He','Ra','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40218,'chemistry','The ions of same period of periodic table are:','Isoelectronic','Li⁺, Be²⁺, B³⁺, C⁴⁺ all having 2 electrons, so isoelectronic, and present in same period O²⁻,  F⁻,  N³⁻, and Ne all have 10 electrons so isoelectronic and present in same period. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'3','mcq','Isoelectronic','Isoprotonic','Isotonic','None of these','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40219,'chemistry','The bonds formed by the electrostatic interaction of ions is:','Ionic bond','The highly electronegative halogens can gain electrons easily to form anions and the highly electropositive alkali metals can loose electrons easily to forma cations. \n\nSuch positively and negatively charged ions have an electrostatic attraction resulting in the formation of an ionic or electrovalent bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'1','mcq','Ionic bond','Covalent bond','Metallic bond','Chemical bond','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40220,'chemistry','Electrovalency is equal to:','Number of unit charges on ion','The bond formed, as a result of the electrostatic attraction between the positive and negative ions was termed as the electrovalent bond. \n\nThe electrovalence is thus equal to the number of unit charge(s) on the ion. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'1','mcq','Number of unit charges on ion','Number of bonds','Number of neutrons','Number of electronegative atoms attached','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40221,'chemistry','The electrovalence of halogen family is:','Negative electrovalence of one','Halogens have one electrons less in their valence shell to attain octet configuration, so they readily accept an electron and carry a charge of one minus. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'1','mcq','Negative electrovalence of one','Negative electrovalence of two','Positive electrovalence of one','Positive electrovalence of two','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40222,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statement:\n\na. In order to explain the formation of chemical bond in terms of electrons, a number of attempts were made.\n\nb. Kössel and Lewis succeeded independently in giving a satisfactory explanation.\n\nc. They were the first to provide some logical explanation of valence which was based on the metals and ligands relationship.\n\nd. They also provide some logical explanation of valence which was based on the inertness of noble gases.','Only c','In order to explain the formation of chemical bond in terms of electrons, a number of attempts were made, but it was only in 1916 when Kössel and Lewis succeeded independently in giving a satisfactory explanation. \n\nThey were the first to provide some logical explanation of valence which was based on the inertness of noble gases. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c','Only d','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40223,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Lewis pictured the atom in terms of a neutral charged ‘Kernel\'.\n\nR: The outer shell that could accommodate a maximum of eight electrons.','Assertion is false but the reason is true','Lewis pictured the atom in terms of a positively charged ‘Kernel’ (the nucleus plus the inner electrons) and the outer shell that could accommodate a maximum of eight electrons.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40413,'biology','In molluscs mouth contains a file like rasping organ for feeding called _______.','Radula','The mouth contains a file-like rasping organ for feeding, called radula.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 53; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Arthropoda,Mollusca,Echinodermata','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Operculum','Mantle','Sting','Radula','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(40224,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: According to Lewis the single outer shell electron of sodium would occupy one corner of the cube.\n\nR: According to Lewis in the case of a noble gas all the eight corners of cube would be occupied.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Lewis, further assumed that these eight electrons occupy the corners of a cube which surround the ‘Kernel’. Thus the single outer shell electron of sodium would occupy one corner of the cube, while in the case of a noble gas all the eight corners would be occupied. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40225,'chemistry','The ________ of electrons, represents a particularly stable electronic arrangement.','Octet','The octet of electrons, represents a particularly stable electronic arrangement. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'1','mcq','Octet','Duplet','Gain','Loss','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40226,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statement according to Lewis contributions:\n\na. Lewis postulated that atoms achieve the stable octet when they are linked by covalent bond only.\n\nb. Lewis proposed that the valence atoms occupy the corners of cube surrounding the kernel.\n\nc. Lewis introduced simple notations to represent valence electrons in an atom.These notations are called Lewis symbols.','Only a and b','Lewis postulated that atoms achieve the stable octet when they are linked by chemical bonds. \n\nLewis proposed that the valence electrons occupy the corners of cube surrounding the kernel. \n\nLewis introduced simple notations to represent valence electrons in an atom. These notations are called Lewis symbols. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'1','mcq','Only a','Only a and b','Only c','Only b and c','NEW','11-101','11-101','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40226q.png'),
(40227,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: In the case of sodium and chlorine, the bond is formed by the sharing of a pair of electrons between sodium and chlorine.\n\nR: In the case of other molecules like Cl2, H2, F2, etc., the bond is formed by the transfer of an electron from one aton to another atoms.','Both assertion and reason are false','In the case of sodium and chlorine, this can happen by the transfer of an electron from sodium to chlorine thereby giving the Na+ and Cl– ions. In the case of other molecules like Cl2, H2, F2, etc., the bond is formed by the sharing of a pair of electrons between the atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-101','11-101','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40227q.png'),
(40301,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: The group valence of the elements is generally either equal to the number of dots in Lewis symbols or 8 minus the number of dots or valence electrons.\n\nR: The number of valence electrons helps to calculate the common or group valence of the element.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explaination of assertion','The number of valence electrons helps to calculate the common or group valence of the element.\n\nThe group valence of the elements is generally either equal to the number of dots in Lewis symbols or 8 minus the number of dots or valence electrons.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis Symbols',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explaination of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explaination of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40302,'chemistry','Choose an appropriate option for the given statement: "In the periodic table, the highly electronegative halogens and the highly electropositive alkali metals are separated by"','Noble gases','In the periodic table, the highly electronegative halogens and the highly electropositive alkali metals are separated by the noble gases. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis Symbols',NULL,'3','mcq','Noble gases','Group trends','Unknown properties','Post transition metals','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40303,'chemistry','Choose an appropriate option for the given statement: "The formation of a negative ion from a halogen atom and a positive ion from an alkali metal atom is associated with"','Gain and loss of electron','The formation of a negative ion from a halogen atom and a positive ion from an alkali metal atom is associated with the gain and loss of an electron by the respective atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis Symbols',NULL,'2','mcq','Gain and loss of electron','Generation of free radical','Sharing of alternate proton','Sharing of alternate bond','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40304,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: The negative and positive ions thus formed attain unstable noble gas electronic configurations.\n\nR: The noble gases have a particularly stable outer shell configuration of eight (octet) electrons, ns²np⁶.','Assertion is false but the reason is true','The negative and positive ions thus formed attain stable noble gas electronic configurations. \n\nThe noble gases (with the exception of helium which has a duplet of electrons) have a particularly stable outer shell configuration of eight (octet) electrons, ns²np⁶. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis Symbols',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40414,'biology','The space between the hump and the mantle is called the ________ in which feather like _______ are present','mantle cavity,gills','The space between the hump and the mantle is called the mantle cavity in which feather like gills are present. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 53; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Arthropoda,Mollusca,Echinodermata','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','mantle cavity,book gills','hump cavity,lungs','gills,mantle cavity','mantle cavity,gills','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(40305,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: The negative and positive ions are stabilized by electrostatic attraction.\n\nR: The formation of a negative ion from a halogen atom and a positive ion from an alkali metal atom is associated.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','The negative and positive ions are stabilized by electrostatic attraction. \n\nThe formation of a negative ion from a halogen atom and a positive ion from an alkali metal atom is associated with the gain and loss of an electron by the respective atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis Symbols',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-101','11-101','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40305q.png'),
(40306,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Atoms achieve the stable octet when they are linked by chemical bonds.\n\nR: The formation of NaCl from sodium and chlorine.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Lewis postulated that atoms achieve the stable octet when they are linked by chemical bonds. \n\nThe negative and positive ions are stabilized by electrostatic attraction. \n\nFor example, the formation of NaCl from sodium and chlorine. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.2-5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis Symbols',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-101','11-101','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40306q.png'),
(40307,'chemistry','"Choose the correct statement regarding Kössel drew, in relation to chemical bonding: a. The noble gases have a particularly stable outer shell configuration of eight (octet) electrons, ns²np⁶. b. Atoms achieve the stable octet when they are linked by chemical bonds c. In the periodic table, the highly electronegative halogens and the highly electropositive alkali metals are separated by the noble gases. "','Only a and c','Kössel, in relation to chemical bonding, drew attention to the following facts: In the periodic table, the highly electronegative halogens and the highly electropositive alkali metals are separated by the noble gases.\n\nThe negative and positive ions thus formed attain stable noble gas electronic configurations. \n\nThe noble gases (with the exception of helium which has a duplet of electrons) have a particularly stable outer shell configuration of eight (octet) electrons, ns²np⁶. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis Symbols',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and c','Only b and c','Only a and b','All statements are correct','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40308,'chemistry','A chemical bond formed between ions of opposite charge:','Electrovalent bond','The bond formed, as a result of the electrostatic attraction between the positive and negative ions was termed as the electrovalent bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis Symbols',NULL,'1','mcq','Electrovalent bond','Covalent bond','Metallic bond','Chemical bond','NEW','11-101','11-101','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40308q.png'),
(40309,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: The bond formed, as a result of the electrostatic attraction between the positive and negative ions was termed as the electrovalent bond.\n\nR: The electrovalence is thus equal to the number of unit charge(s) on the ion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','The bond formed, as a result of the electrostatic attraction between the positive and negative ions was termed as the electrovalent bond. \n\nThe electrovalence is thus equal to the number of unit charge(s) on the ion. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis Symbols',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40310,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: The electrovalence is equal to the number of unit charge(s) on the ion.\n\nR: Calcium is assigned a positive electrovalence of two, while chlorine a negative electrovalence of one.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','The electrovalence is thus equal to the number of unit charge(s) on the ion. Thus, calcium is assigned a positive electrovalence of two, while chlorine a negative electrovalence of one. \n\nReference: ( NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis Symbols',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40311,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect option:\n\na. Lewis postulations provide the basis for the modern concepts regarding  the formation of ionic crystalline compounds.\n\nb. Kössel’s postulations provide the basis for the modern concepts regarding ion-formation by electron transfer.\n\nc. At the same time Kössel’s did recognise the fact that a large number of compounds did not fit into these concepts.\n\nd. Lewis postulated that atoms achieve the stable octet when they are linked by chemical bonds.','Only a','Kössel’s postulations provide the basis for the modern concepts regarding ion-formation by electron transfer and the formation of ionic crystalline compounds. \n\nHis views have proved to be of great value in the understanding and systematisation of the ionic compounds. \n\nAt the same time he did recognise the fact that a large number of compounds did not fit into these concepts. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis Symbols',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only c','Only a and c','Only b and c','NEW','11-101','11-101','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40311q.png'),
(40312,'chemistry','His views have proved to be of great value in the understanding and systematisation of the ionic compounds:','Kössel','Kössel’s postulations provide the basis for the modern concepts regarding ion-formation by electron transfer and the formation of ionic crystalline compounds. \n\nHis views have proved to be of great value in the understanding and systematisation of the ionic compounds. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis Symbols',NULL,'1','mcq','Lewis','Kössel','Nyholm and Ronald Gillespie','Friedrich Hund','NEW','11-101','11-101','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40312q.png'),
(40509,'chemistry','Which statement is wrong regarding the conditions to write Lewis-Langmuir theory\n\na. The Lewis-Langmuir theory can be understood by considering the formation of the chlorine molecule, Cl2. \n\nb. The Cl atom with electronic configuration, [Ne]3s2 3p5, is one electron short of the Neon configuration.\n\nc. The formation of the Cl2 molecule can be understood in terms of the transfering of a pair of electrons from the one chlorine to another atom, each chlorine atom contributing 8 electrons to the shared pair.\n\nd.  In the process both chlorine atoms attain the outer shell octet of the nearest noble gas (i.e., argon).','Only b and c','The Lewis-Langmuir theory can be understood by considering the formation of the chlorine molecule, Cl2. \n\nThe Cl atom with electronic configuration, [Ne]3s2 3p5, is one electron short of the argon configuration. \n\nThe formation of the Cl2 molecule can be understood in terms of the sharing of a pair of electrons between the two chlorine atoms, each chlorine atom contributing one electron to the shared pair. \n\nIn the process both chlorine atoms attain the outer shell octet of the nearest noble gas (i.e., argon). \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Covalent bond',NULL,'3','mcq','Only a and c','Only b','Only b and c','Only a and d','NEW','11-102','11-102',NULL),
(40510,'chemistry','Which of the following has single covalent bond?','NH₃','When two atoms share one electron pair they form single covalent bond. \n\nNH₃ forms single covalent bond, while SO2, C2H4, CO2 form double bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Covalent bond',NULL,'2','mcq','SO₂','NH₃','CO₂','C₂H₄','NEW','11-102','11-102',NULL),
(40511,'chemistry','Identify the type of bonding in the given molecule: diagram(n0402)','Covalent bond','In the carbon dioxide molecule, we have two double bonds between the carbon and oxygen atoms. \n\nSimilarly in ethene molecule the two carbon atoms are joined by a double bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Covalent bond',NULL,'2','mcq','Covalent bond','Metallic bond','Ionic bond','None of these','NEW','11-102','11-102','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/40511q.png'),
(40512,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: The three carbon atoms in the ethyne molecule, a triple bond is formed.\n\nR: When combining atoms share three electron pairs as in the case of two nitrogen atoms in the N₂ molecule.','Assertion is false but the reason is true','When combining atoms share three electron pairs as in the case of two nitrogen atoms in the N2 molecule and the two carbon atoms in the ethyne molecule, a triple bond is formed. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Covalent bond',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-102','11-102',NULL),
(40513,'chemistry','Which statement is wrong regarding the covalent bonding:\n\na. When two atoms share two electron pair they are said to be joined by a single covalent bond.\n\nb. If two atoms share two pairs of electrons, the covalent bond between them is called a double bond.\n\nc. In the carbon dioxide molecule, we have two double bonds between the carbon and oxygen atoms. Similarly in ethyne molecule the two carbon atoms are joined by a double bond.','Only a and c','When two atoms share one electron pair they are said to be joined by a single covalent bond. \n\nIn many compounds we have multiple bonds between atoms. The formation of multiple bonds envisages sharing of more than one electron pair between two atoms. \n\nIf two atoms share two pairs of electrons, the covalent bond between them is called a double bond. \n\nFor example, in the carbon dioxide molecule, we have two double bonds between the carbon and oxygen atoms. \n\nSimilarly in ethene molecule the two carbon atoms are joined by a double bond.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Covalent bond',NULL,'3','mcq','Only a and c','Only b and c','Only c','Only a and b','NEW','11-102','11-102',NULL),
(40514,'chemistry','In the water molecule, H atom attain a ____ electrons diagram(n0403)','2e-','In the water molecule, H atom attain a duplet of electrons and O, the octet.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Covalent bond',NULL,'2','mcq','2e-','3e-','8e-','6e-','NEW','11-102','11-102','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/40514q.png'),
(40515,'biology','Match the following options correctly.','Urochordata-notochord is present only in larval tail,Cephalochordata-Notochord extends from head to tail region and persistent throughout their life.Vertebrata-possess notochord during the embryonic period later replaced by vertebral column','In Urochordata, notochord is present only in larval tail, while in Cephalochordata, it extends from head to tail region and is persistent throughout their life. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 55; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Hemichordata,Protochordates','Animal Kingdom','2','match','Urochordata,notochord is present only in larval tail','Cephalochordata,Notochord extends from head to tail region and persistent throughout their life.','Vertebrata,possess notochord during the embryonic period later replaced by vertebral column',NULL,'NEW','11-055','11-046',NULL),
(40601,'chemistry','The lewis dot structures provide an electronic picture of bonding in molecules and ions on the base of?','Both a and b','The Lewis dot structures provide a picture of bonding in molecules and ions in terms of the shared pairs of electrons and the octet rule. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 103; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis structure',NULL,'1','mcq','Octet rule','Shared pair of electrons','Both a and b','Electronic configuration','NEW','11-103','11-103',NULL),
(40602,'chemistry','Which step is not involved in writing the lewis structure:\n\na. Counting the total number of valence electrons of combining atoms\n\nb. Adding electrons to total valance electrons if it is anion ,or subtracting electrons from total valence electrons if it is cation\n\nc. Distribution of electrons on the base of knowledge of skeletal structre of compound\n\nd. Add two electrons for uninegative ion or subtract two electrons for unipositive ion','Only d','CO₃²⁻ (Lewis Structure) First, count total valence electrons which is 4 for C and 6 for O so total valence electrons are10 two negative charges mean addition of two electrons to total valence electrons so now total electrons are 12 Least electronegative atom occupies central position so C will be central atom now just distribute electrons according to valency of atoms C can make 4 bonds so first place single bonds between C and O. \n\nOne bond left this will be double bond with one oxygen so  8 electrons are utilized still having 4 electrons now justify the valency of O (can make two bonds) if we make two bonds for all O the valency  of C will disturb so these 4 electrons remain in form of negative charge on Oxygen. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 103; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis structure',NULL,'3','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c','Only d','NEW','11-103','11-103',NULL),
(40603,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: In the CH₄ molecule there are five valence electrons available for bonding (1 from carbon and 4 from the four hydrogen atoms).\n\nR: The total number of electrons required for writing the structures are obtained by adding the valence electrons of the combining atoms.','Assertion is false but the reason is true','The total number of electrons required for writing the structures are obtained by adding the valence electrons of the combining atoms. \n\nFor example, in the CH₄ molecule there are eight valence electrons available for bonding (4 from carbon and 4 from the four hydrogen atoms). \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 103; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis structure',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-103','11-103','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40603q.png'),
(40604,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: For anions, each negative charge would mean addition of one electron.\n\nR: The CO₃²– ion, the two negative charges indicate that there are two additional electrons than those provided by the neutral atoms.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','For anions, each negative charge would mean addition of one electron. \n\nFor cations, each positive charge would result in subtraction of one electron from the total number of valence electrons. \n\nFor example, for the CO₃²– ion, the two negative charges indicate that there are two additional electrons than those provided by the neutral atoms. \n\nFor NH₄+ ion, one positive charge indicates the loss of one electron from the group of neutral atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 103; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis structure',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-103','11-103',NULL),
(40605,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: For cations, each positive charge would result in subtraction of two electron from the total number of valence electrons.\n\nR:  NH₄+ ion, one positive charge indicates the loss of one electron from the group of neutral atoms.','Assertion is false but the reason is true','For cations, each positive charge would result in subtraction of one electron from the total number of valence electrons. \n\nFor example, for NH₄+ ion, one positive charge indicates the loss of one electron from the group of neutral atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 103; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis structure',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-103','11-103',NULL),
(40606,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: In the NF₃ and CO₃²–, nitrogen and carbon are the central atoms whereas fluorine and oxygen occupy the terminal positions.\n\nR: In general, the highest electronegative atom occupies the central position in the molecule/ion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false','In general the least electronegative atom occupies the central position in the molecule/ion. For example in the NF₃ and CO₃²–, nitrogen and carbon are the central atoms whereas fluorine and oxygen occupy the terminal positions. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 103; Paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis structure',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-103','11-103','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40606q.png'),
(40607,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: After accounting for the shared pairs of electrons for single bonds, the remaining electron pairs are either utilized for multiple bonding or remain as the lone pairs. \n\nR: In general, the highest electronegative atom occupies the central position in the molecule/ion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false','After accounting for the shared pairs of electrons for single bonds, the remaining electron pairs are either utilized for multiple bonding or remain as the lone pairs. \n\nThe basic requirement being that each bonded atom gets an octet of electrons. \n\nIn general the least electronegative atom occupies the central position in the molecule/ion. For example in the NF₃ and CO₃²–, nitrogen and carbon are the central atoms whereas fluorine and oxygen occupy the terminal positions. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 103; Paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis structure',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-103','11-103','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40607q.png'),
(40608,'chemistry','Choose the total number of valence electrons of Carbon and Oxygen atoms:','10e–','The total number of valence electrons of carbon and oxygen atoms. \n\nThe outer (valence) shell configurations of carbon and oxygen atoms are: 2s² 2p² and 2² 2p⁴, respectively. \n\nThe valence electrons available are 4 + 6 =10. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 104; Problem no. 4.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis structure',NULL,'3','mcq','6e–','8e–','10e–','12e–','NEW','11-103','11-103',NULL),
(40609,'chemistry','Choose the total number of valence electrons of Nitrite ion NO₂–:','18e–','The total number of valence electrons of the nitrogen atom, the oxygen atoms and the additional one negative charge (equal to one electron). \n\nN(2s2 2p3), O(2s2 2p4), 5 + (2 × 6) +1 = 18 electrons. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 104; Problem no. 4.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis structure',NULL,'3','mcq','12e–','14e–','16e–','18e–','NEW','11-103','11-103',NULL),
(40610,'chemistry','Choose the total number of valence electrons of Carbon dioxide CO₂ :','16e–','1 Oxygen has 6 valence electrons , so 2 Oxygen = 12 electrons\n1 carbon has 4 valence electrons.\n\nTotal valence electrons = 12+4 = 16 electrons\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 104; Problem no. 4.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis structure',NULL,'3','mcq','12e–','18e–','16e–','8e–','NEW','11-103','11-103',NULL),
(40611,'chemistry','Choose the total number of valence electrons of Nitric Acid HNO₃ :','24e–','The HNO₃ Lewis structure has 24 valence electrons. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 104; Problem no. 4.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis structure',NULL,'3','mcq','12e–','24e–','16e–','18e–','NEW','11-103','11-103',NULL),
(40612,'chemistry','Choose the total number of valence electrons of Carbonate ion CO₃²– :','24e–','The carbonate ion CO₃²–, Carbon has 4 valence electrons; Oxygen has six, we have 3 Oxygens, and this negative 2 means we have an extra two valence electrons. \n\nAdd that all up: 4 plus 18 plus 2: 24 valence electrons. \n\nReference : ( NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 104; Problem no. 4.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis structure',NULL,'3','mcq','12e–','24e–','16e–','18e–','NEW','11-103','11-103',NULL),
(40613,'chemistry','Choose the total number of valence electrons of Nitrogen trifluoride NF₃ :','26e–','Nitrogen has 5 and fluorine has seven valence electrons.\n\nThe total number of valence electrons in a NF₃ molecule = 5 + 7*3 = 26. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 104; Problem no. 4.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis structure',NULL,'3','mcq','22e–','24e–','26e–','18e–','NEW','11-103','11-103',NULL),
(40614,'chemistry','Choose the total number of valence electrons of Ozone molecule O₃ :','18e–','Oxygen atom has six electron in its valence shell. \n\nIn O₃ molecule, there are three oxygen atoms. \n\nTherefore, 6*3=18 electrons. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 104; Problem no. 4.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis structure',NULL,'3','mcq','22e–','24e–','26e–','18e–','NEW','11-103','11-103',NULL),
(40615,'chemistry','Choose the total number of valence electrons of Oxygen molecule O₂ :','12e–','Oxygen atom has six electron in its valence shell. \n\nIn O₂ molecule, there are two oxygen atoms. \n\nTherefore, 6*2=12 electrons. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 104; Problem no. 4.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis structure',NULL,'3','mcq','12e–','14e–','6e–','8e–','NEW','11-103','11-103','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40615q.png'),
(40616,'chemistry','Choose the total number of valence electrons of Hydrogen molecule H₂ :','2e–','Hydrogen is in group 1, that means it has 1 valence electron. \n\nBut we have 2 Hydrogen atoms. \n\nTherefore, 1*2=2 electrons. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 104; Problem no. 4.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lewis structure',NULL,'3','mcq','2e–','1e–','Zero electron','None of these','NEW','11-103','11-103','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40616q.png'),
(40617,'biology','Which among the following is incorrect regarding characteristic of Osteichthyes?','Air bladder is absent','Osteichthyes includes both marine and fresh water fishes with bony endoskeleton. Their body is streamlined. Mouth is mostly terminal .They have four pairs of gills which are covered by an operculum on each side. Skin is covered with cycloid/ctenoid scales. Air bladder is present which regulates buoyancy. Heart is two chambered (one auricle and one ventricle).(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 57; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Cyclostomata and Pisces','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','They have four pairs of gills and Heart is two chambered','Skin is covered with cycloid/ctenoid scales and terminal mouth','Air bladder is absent','They are cold-blooded animals and body is streamlined with bony endoskeleton','NEW','11-057','11-048',NULL),
(40618,'biology','Consider the following statements about Chondrichthyes and Osteichthyes: \n\ni. In chondrichthyes placoid scales are present while in osteichthyes cycloid/ctenoid scales are present. \n\nii. Mouth is located ventrally in chondrichthyes while in osteichthyes mouth is mostly terminal. \n\niii. In chondrichthyes fertilisation is internal while in osteichthyes fertilisation is usually external. \n\niv. Chondrichthyes- mostly viviparous, Osteichthyes- mostly oviparous. \n\nWhich of the above statements are false?','None of the above.','All the statements are true. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 56,57)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Cyclostomata and Pisces','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','i and ii','iii and iv','Only i','None of the above.','NEW','11-056','11-047',NULL),
(40619,'biology','Match List 1 with List 2 as shown in the diagram ?(0457)','i-c ii-a iii-b iv-d','(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 56,57)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Cyclostomata and Pisces','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','i-a ii-b iii-c iv-d','i-c ii-b iii-a iv-d','i-c ii-a iii-b iv-d','i-b ii-a iii-d iv-c','NEW','11-056','11-047','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40619q.png'),
(40701,'chemistry','The formal charge on central O in O₃ is:','1+','Formal charge on an atom in a lewis structure = [Total number of valence electrons in the free atom] - [Total number of non-bonding electrons(lone pairs] - 1/2[total number of bonding electrons (shared] \n\nfirst of all write the lewis structure of O₃ and mark the central atom for convenience as 1 Oxygen have 6 electrons in its valence shell in free state, In ozone central O is making 3 bonds so 6 bonding electrons. \n\nOxygen have two lone pairs in free state but in ozone one lone pair is used to make third bond so now have only one lone pair so according to formula F. \n\nC(O₃) = 6-2-1/2(6) = 1 so central oxygen has 1+ charge. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 105; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Formal charge',NULL,'3','mcq','1+','Zero','1-','2+','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(40702,'chemistry','Lowest energy structure is the one with the ________ formal charge.','Smallest','Generally the lowest energy structure is the one with the smallest formal charges on the atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 105; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Formal charge',NULL,'1','mcq','Smallest','Negative','Highest','Positive','NEW','11-105','11-105','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40702q.png'),
(40703,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Lewis dot structures, in general, do not represent the actual shapes of the molecules.\n\nR: The Lewis dot structures provide a picture of bonding in molecules and ions in terms of the shared pairs of electrons and the octet rule.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Lewis dot structures, in general, do not represent the actual shapes of the molecules. \n\nThe Lewis dot structures provide a picture of bonding in molecules and ions in terms of the shared pairs of electrons and the octet rule. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 104; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Formal charge',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-105','11-105','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40703q.png'),
(40704,'chemistry','Identify the statement: "In case of polyatomic ions, the net charge is possessed by the ion as a whole and not by a particular atom."','Formal charge','In case of polyatomic ions, the net charge is possessed by the ion as a whole and not by a particular atom. \n\nIt is, however, feasible to assign a formal charge on each atom. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 104; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Formal charge',NULL,'1','mcq','Formal charge','Negative charge','Positive charge','Neutral charge','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(40705,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement regarding formal charge:\n\na. In case of polyatomic ions, the net charge is possessed by the ion as a whole and not by a particular atom.\n\nb. The formal charge of an atom may be defined as the difference between the number of valence electrons of that atom in an isolated or free state and the number of electrons assigned to that atom in the Lewis structure.\n\nc. It is, however, unattainable to assign a formal charge on each atom.','Only a and b','It is, however, feasible to assign a formal charge on each atom. \n\nThe formal charge of an atom in a polyatomic molecule or ion may be defined as the difference between the number of valence electrons of that atom in an isolated or free state and the number of electrons assigned to that atom in the Lewis structure. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 104; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Formal charge',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and b','Only a and c','Only b and c','All of these','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(40706,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: The formal charge counting is based on the assumption that the atom in the molecule owns one electron of each shared pair and both the electrons of a lone pair.\n\nR: Generally the lowest energy structure is the one with the largest formal charges on the atoms.','Assertion is true but the reason is false','The counting is based on the assumption that the atom in the molecule owns one electron of each shared pair and both the electrons of a lone pair. \n\nGenerally the lowest energy structure is the one with the smallest formal charges on the atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 105; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Formal charge',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanationof assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(40707,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement:\n\na. Formal charges do not indicate real charge separation within the molecule.\n\nb. Indicating the charges on the atoms in the Lewis structure only helps in keeping track of the valence electrons in the molecule.\n\nc. Lewis dot structures, in general, do not represent the actual shapes of the molecules.','All of these','We must understand that formal charges do not indicate real charge separation within the molecule.  \n\nIndicating the charges on the atoms in the Lewis structure only helps in keeping track of the valence electrons in the molecule. \n\nLewis dot structures, in general, do not represent the actual shapes of the molecules. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 105; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Formal charge',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and b','Only a and c','Only b and c','All of these','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(40708,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Formal charges help in the selection of the lowest energy structure from a number of possible Lewis structures for a given species.\n\nR: The formal charge is a factor based on a pure covalent view of bonding in which electron pairs are shared equally by neighbouring atoms.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Formal charges help in the selection of the lowest energy structure from a number of possible Lewis structures for a given species. \n\nGenerally the lowest energy structure is the one with the smallest formal charges on the atoms. \n\nThe formal charge is a factor based on a pure covalent view of bonding in which electron pairs are shared equally by neighbouring atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 105; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Formal charge',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(40709,'chemistry','The formal charge on single bonded terminal oxygen atom of ozone is:','-1','Formal charge on double bonded O atom =6–4–0.5(4)=0\n\nFormal charge on single bonded O atom=6–6–0.5(2)=−1 .  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 105; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Formal charge',NULL,'2','mcq','-1','-2','1','Zero','NEW','11-105','11-105','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40709q.png'),
(40710,'chemistry','The overall formal charge on Carbon dioxide CO₂ is:','Zero','Carbon dioxide has zero formal charge because of neutral molecule.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 105; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Formal charge',NULL,'2','mcq','-1','-2','1','Zero','NEW','11-105','11-105','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40710q.png'),
(40711,'chemistry','The formal charge on Oxygen in Nitric Acid HNO₃ is:','-1','The nitric acid molecule is neutral. \n\nHowever, its structure contains an oxygen with a formal charge of -1 and a nitrogen with a formal charge of +1. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 105; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Formal charge',NULL,'2','mcq','-1','-2','1','Zero','NEW','11-105','11-105','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40711q.png'),
(40712,'chemistry','The formal charge on Oxygen atom in Carbonate ion CO₃²– is:','-2','The formal charge on Oxygen atom in Carbonate ion CO₃²– is calculated as: \n\n[O − O||C − ∗O]2−  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 105; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Formal charge',NULL,'2','mcq','-1','-2','1','Zero','NEW','11-105','11-105','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40712q.png'),
(40713,'chemistry','The formal charge on Nitrogen trifluoride NF₃ is:','Zero','Formal charge for each Fluorine atom is = 7 – 0.5*2 – 6 = 0. \n\nAll the atoms inside the NF₃ molecule have the least possible formal charge values.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 105; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Formal charge',NULL,'2','mcq','-1','-2','1','Zero','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(40714,'biology','Select the odd number from the list -','Bufo','Bufo is an amphibian while others belongs to aves. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 57; Paragraph No. 6)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Class Tetrapoda','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Corvus (crow)','Columba (pigeon)','Bufo','Neophron (vulture)','NEW','11-058','11-049',NULL),
(40715,'biology','Select the odd number from the list -','Calotes','Calotes is a reptile whiles others belongs to aves. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 58; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Class Tetrapoda','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Aptenodytes (penguin)','Pavo (peacock)','Calotes','Psittacula (parrot)','NEW','11-058','11-049',NULL),
(40716,'biology','Which among the following is incorrect characteristic of Reptiles?','Fertilisation is external','Fertilisation is internal.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 58; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Class Tetrapoda','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Reptilia refers to their creeping or crawling mode of locomotion','body is covered by dry and cornified skin, epidermal scales or scutes','Reptiles are poikilotherms. Snakes and lizards shed their scales as skin cast','Fertilisation is external','NEW','11-058','11-049',NULL),
(40717,'biology','Which of the following is/are true regarding amphibia. \n\ni)Respiration is by gills, lungs and through skin. \n\nii)Sexes may or mayn\'t be separate,Fertilisation is external.\n\niii)The heart is three chambered.','Only i and iii','Respiration is by gills, lungs and through skin. The heart is three chambered (two auricles and one ventricle). These are cold-blooded animals. Sexes are separate. Fertilisation is external.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 57; Paragraph No. 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Class Tetrapoda','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Only i','Only i and ii','Only i and iii','All of them','NEW','11-057','11-048',NULL),
(40718,'biology','Which of the following is/are true regarding reptiles? \n\ni) Crocodilus (Crocodile), Alligator (Alligator).Hemidactylus (Wall lizard), are all reptiles. \n\nii)They are mostly terrestrial animals.\n\niii)Chelone (Turtle), Testudo (Tortoise), Chameleon (Tree lizard) are not reptiles.','Only i and ii','Chelone (Turtle), Testudo (Tortoise), Chameleon (Tree lizard), Calotes (Garden lizard), Crocodilus (Crocodile), Alligator (Alligator). Hemidactylus (Wall lizard), Poisonous snakes – Naja (Cobra), Bangarus (Krait), Vipera (Viper),are all reptiles.Reptiles are mostly terrestrial animals.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 58; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Class Tetrapoda','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Only i','Only i and ii','Only i and iii','All of them','NEW','11-058','11-049',NULL),
(40719,'biology','Vertebrates having air sacs connected to lungs for supplement respiration are called','Birds','Aves have air sacs to supplement respiration. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 59; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Class Tetrapoda','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Birds','Reptiles','Mammals','Amphibia','NEW','11-058','11-049',NULL),
