(40734,'biology','The most unique mammalian characteristic is the presence of _______.','Mammary glands','The most unique mammalian characteristic is the presence of milk producing glands (mammary glands). (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 59; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Class Tetrapoda','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Mammary glands','Possessing skin hair','Presence of ear pinna','Four chambered heart','NEW','11-059','11-050',NULL),
(40735,'biology','Which among the following is/are characteristic/s of mammals\n\ni)The skin of mammals is unique in possessing hair and homeothermous. \n\nii) Different types of teeth are present in the jaw.\n\niii)Possession of pinna, external ear.','i, ii and iii','The skin of mammals is unique in possessing hair and They are homoiothermous. Different types of teeth are present in the jaw. Presence of external ear or pinna is observed in all.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 60; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Class Tetrapoda','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Only i and ii','Only ii and iii','Only i and iii','i, ii and iii','NEW','11-059','11-050',NULL),
(40801,'chemistry','Octet rule is mainly applied to?','Second period','The octet rule, though useful, is not universal. \n\nIt is quite useful for understanding the structures of most of the organic compounds and it applies mainly to the second period elements of the periodic table. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 105; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Limitations of octet rule',NULL,'1','mcq','Second period','First period','Third period','Noble gases','NEW','11-105','11-105','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40801q.png'),
(40802,'chemistry','The elements having less than four valence electrons unable to attain _______.','Octet','LiCl,BeH₂,BCl₃ ,BF₃ ,AlCl₃ in all these central atom (Li,B,Be,Al) have less than 4 electrons octet rule is not satisfied. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 105; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Limitations of octet rule',NULL,'2','mcq','Octet','Triad','Duplet','Sextet','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(40803,'chemistry','Which one have odd electron system?','Both a and b','An odd electron bond means there is an odd number of the electron in the overall molecule. The molecule has unpaired electrons. \n\nIn NO, the valence electrons in nitrogen are 5, and valence electrons in oxygen are 6. \n\nOn addition, we get, 6+5=11. \n\nTherefore, the NO molecule has an odd number of electrons. \nThere are 2 bonds between nitrogen and oxygen. The nitrogen has one unpaired electron. So, it has an odd electron bond. In NO2 the valence electrons in nitrogen are 5 and the valence electrons in oxygen are 6. \n\nSo, by adding we get, 6+6+5=17. \n\nTherefore, NO2 molecules have an odd number of electrons. There are 2 bonds between nitrogen and oxygen. The nitrogen has one unpaired electron. So, it has an odd electron bond.\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 105; paragraph no.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Limitations of octet rule',NULL,'2','mcq','NO₂','NO','Both a and b','N₂O','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(40804,'chemistry','Odd electron molecules unable to attain______','Octet','There are a no. of molecules whose total no. of valence electrons is an odd number.  \n\nIt is not possible for all of the atoms in such a molecule to satisfy the octet rule. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 105; paragraph no.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Limitations of octet rule',NULL,'2','mcq','Octet','Triad','Duplet','Sextet','NEW','11-105','11-105','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40804q.png'),
(40805,'chemistry','Which of the following can expand its octet?','S','Elements of the third period and onwards have d subshell along with s and p these atoms can expand their octet by using vacant orbitals. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 105; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Limitations of octet rule',NULL,'2','mcq','S','O','N','C','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(40806,'chemistry','Which one have expanded octet:','H₂SO₄','The central atom Sulphur has more than eight valence electrons. \n\nHence, they exhibit an expanded octet. \n\nThis is possible due to the availability of 3d orbitals.NO₂, LiCl, CO₂ follow tha octet rule. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 105; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Limitations of octet rule',NULL,'3','mcq','H₂SO₄','NO₂','LiCl','CO₂','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(40807,'chemistry','Total number of electrons around the central atom of PF₅ :','10 electrons','Phosphorus has five valenece shell electrons.\n\nIf we look at structure of PF₅, the phosphorous atom is attached to five single bonds that means it have 10 electrons. \n\nPhosphorus has more than 8 electrons in their valence shell and so called expanded octet.\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 105; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Limitations of octet rule',NULL,'3','mcq','12 electrons','8 electrons','10 electrons','18 electrons','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(40808,'chemistry','Which of the following compound of S obeyed octet rule?','SCl₂','Sulphur in H₂SO₄ and SF₆ have 10 electrons in valence shell except SCl₂(have 8 e). So it obeys octet rule. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Limitations of octet rule',NULL,'3','mcq','H₂SO₄','SCl₂','SO₄²⁻','SF₆','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(40809,'chemistry','Which of the following is not the drawback of Octet theory:\n\na. Do not explain the bonding of noble gases (Xe,Kr)\n\nb. Do not explain shape of the molecules\n\nc. Do not explain the energy of molecules\n\nd. Do not explain the nature of bonds','Only d','It is clear that octet rule is based upon the chemical inertness of noble gases. \n\nHowever, some noble gases (for example xenon and krypton) also combine with oxygen and fluorine to form a number of compounds like XeF₂, KrF₂, XeOF₂ etc.\n\nThis theory does not account for the shape of molecules. It does not explain the relative stability of the molecules being totally silent about the energy of a molecule. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Limitations of octet rule',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c','Only d','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(40810,'chemistry','Which of the following is incorrect statement regarding octet rule:\n\na. The octet rule refers to the tendency of atoms to prefer to have eight electrons in the valence shell.\n\nb. There are three types of exceptions to the octet rule.\n\nc. It applies mainly to the fifth period elements of the periodic table.','Only c','The octet rule, though useful, is not universal. \n\nIt is quite useful for understanding the structures of most of the organic compounds and it applies mainly to the second period elements of the periodic table. \n\nThere are three types of exceptions to the octet rule. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Limitations of octet rule',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b and c','Only c','None of these','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(40811,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: In some compounds, the number of electrons surrounding the central atom is less than eight.\n\nR: This is especially the case with elements having more than four valence electrons. For example; non-metals.','Assertion is true but the reason is false','In some compounds, the number of electrons surrounding the central atom is less than eight. \n\nThis is especially the case with elements having less than four valence electrons. \n\nExamples are LiCl, BeH₂ and BCl₃. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Limitations of octet rule',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(40812,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Molecules with an odd number of electrons are nitric oxide, NO and nitrogen dioxide, NO₂.\n\nR: The octet rule is not satisfied for the atoms with an odd number of electrons.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','In molecules with an odd number of electrons like nitric oxide, NO and nitrogen dioxide, NO₂, the octet rule is not satisfied for all the atoms.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Limitations of octet rule',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(40813,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: The octet rule is satisfied for the Hydrogen molecule.\n\nR: Two hydrogen atoms can form a covalent bond to make a hydrogen molecule.','Both assertion and reason are false','Hydrogen, the only element in the first row besides Helium, fulfills the octet rule, by sharing two electrons only. \n\nTwo hydrogen atoms form a covalent bond to make a hydrogen molecule. Each contributes one electron and forms a system that is much more stable than the isolated atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Limitations of octet rule',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(40901,'chemistry','Electrovalent bond is formed between_______','Ions','Ionic bonds are formed by electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions. \n\nElectronpositive elements loose electrons to form cations and electronegative elements accept electrons to form anions. \n\nThe electrostatic attraction between carions and anions result in ionic bonds. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'1','mcq','Molecules','Ions','Atoms','None of these','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40902,'chemistry','Greater the Ionization energy lesser will be the chances of formation of?','Ionic bond','Greater ionization energy meand atoms cannot loose electrons readily to form positive ions (cations). \n\nIf ions are not formed readily, ionic bonds are not formed readily. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'2','mcq','Ionic bond','Covalent bond','Metallic bond','Hydrogen bond','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40903,'chemistry','Greater electron affinity increase the chances of formation of ?','Ionic bond','Greater electron affinity greater will be anion formation ability so like to make ionic bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'2','mcq','Ionic bond','Covalent bond','Metallic bond','Hydrogen bond','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40904,'chemistry','Higher electronegative atoms will exhibit:','Ionic character','Electronegativity is ability to attract the shared pair electrons towards itself and getting more negative charge this leads to more ionic character. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'2','mcq','Ionic character','Covalent character','Paramagnetic character','Diamagnetic character','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40905,'chemistry','The arrangement of cations and anions in solids is called __________.','Crystal lattice','The crystal lattice is the symmetrical three-dimensional structural arrangements of atoms, ions or molecules inside a crystalline solid. \n\nUsually ionic salts i.e.compounds consisting of cations and anions form crystal lattice. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'1','mcq','Crystallography','Metallic lattice','Crystal lattice','Amorphous solid','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40906,'chemistry','Positive ion formation requires _______of electron from neutral atom.','Removal','Positive ion is formed when an electron is removed from a neutral atom. Na forms Na+ by removal of an electron.\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'1','mcq','Removal','Substitution','Addition','Spinning','NEW','11-106','11-106','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/40906q.png'),
(40907,'chemistry','Anion formation requires ______ of electron in neutral atom.','Addition','Anion or negatively charged ion is formed when an electron is added to a neutral atom. \n\nCl forms Cl- by addition of one elecxtron to its valence shell.\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'1','mcq','Removal','Substitution','Addition','Spinning','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40908,'chemistry','Ionization Enthalpy is always:','Endothermic','Ionization energy is energy required to remove an electron from an isolated neutral atom, Ionization energies are always positive numbers, because energy must be supplied (an endothermic energy change) to separate electrons from atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'2','mcq','Exothermic','Endothermic','Neutral','May be endothermic or exothermic','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40909,'chemistry','Electron gain enthalpy is always:','May be endothermic or exothermic','Electron gain enthalpy is the enthalpy change when an isolated gaseous atom (X) gains an electron to form its corresponding anion. \n\nIt can be exothermic or endothermic. \n\nWhen an electron is added to an atom, the electron gain enthalpy for such elements is negative. The electron gain enthalpy of halogens is highly negative. \n\nSometimes it is endothermic because after adding one electron the incoming electron feels repulsion. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'2','mcq','Endothermic','Exothermic','Neutral','May be endothermic or exothermic','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40910,'chemistry','Ionic bond will be formed between atoms having:','Both a and b','Ionic bonds are formed by electrostattic atttraction between cations and anions.  \n\nFree radicals are unstable and cannot form such bonds. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'1','mcq','High electron affinity','Low ionization Energy','Both a and b','None of these','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40911,'chemistry','Metals mostly forms:','Cations','Metals are electropositive and has low ionization energy and can readily loose electrons to form cations. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'2','mcq','Cations','Anions','Free radicals','Radicals','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40912,'chemistry','Non-metals like to form:','Anions','Non-metals are electronegative and has high electron affinity enthalpy and can readily ccept electrons to for to form anions.\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'2','mcq','Radicals','Cations','Anions','Free radicals','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40913,'chemistry','Which one is complex cations having two non-metals:','NH₄⁺','The ammonium ion, NH₄⁺ (made up of two non-metallic elements) is an exception. \n\nIt forms the cation of a number of ionic compounds. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'1','mcq','NH₄⁺','CO₃²-⁺','PH₄⁺','SH₄⁺','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40914,'chemistry','Ionic compounds mostly exist in:','Crystalline form','Ionic compounds in the crystalline state consist of orderly three-dimensional arrangements of cations and anions held together by coulombic interaction energies.\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'1','mcq','Amorphous form','Crystalline form','Giant solids','None of these','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40915,'chemistry','In a crystal lattice the cations and anions are held together by:','Coulombic force','Ionic compounds in the crystalline state consist of orderly three-dimensional arrangements of cations and anions held together by coulombic interaction energies.\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'1','mcq','Coulombic force','Electrostatic force','Nucleus force','None of these','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40916,'chemistry','The lattice formation energy of NaCl (rock salt) is:','-788 kj/mol','The ionization enthalpy for Na+(g) formation from Na(g) is 495.8 kJ mol–1; \n\nwhile the electron gain enthalpy for the change Cl(g) + e– → Cl– (g) is, – 348.7 kJ mol–1 only. \n\nThe sum of the two, 147.1 kJ mol-1 is more than compensated for by the enthalpy of lattice formation of NaCl(s) (–788 kJ mol –1). \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'1','mcq','788 kj/mol','-788 kj/mol','-787 kj/mol','-780 kj/mol','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40917,'chemistry','The ionization enthalpy of Na⁺ formation from Na is:','495.8 kj/mol','The ionization enthalpy for Na+(g) formation from Na(g) is 495.8 kJ mol–1; \n\nwhile the electron gain enthalpy for the change Cl(g) + e– → Cl– (g) is, – 348.7 kJ mol–1 only. \n\nThe sum of the two, 147.1 kJ mol-1 is more than compensated for by the enthalpy of lattice formation of NaCl(s) (–788 kJ mol –1).\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'1','mcq','495.8 kj/mol','490kj/mol','480kj/mol','-495.8kj/mol','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40918,'chemistry','The electron gain enthalpy of Cl⁻ from Cl is:','-348.7kj/mol','The ionization enthalpy for Na+(g) formation from Na(g) is 495.8 kJ mol–1; \n\nwhile the electron gain enthalpy for the change Cl(g) + e– → Cl– (g) is, – 348.7 kJ mol–1 only. \n\nThe sum of the two, 147.1 kJ mol-1 is more than compensated for by the enthalpy of lattice formation of NaCl(s) (–788 kJ mol –1).\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'1','mcq','348.7kj/mol','-348.7kj/mol','147.1kj/mol','384.7kj/mol','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40919,'chemistry','The enthalpy of formation of NaCl from its ions is:','147.1kj/mol','The ionization enthalpy for Na+(g) formation from Na(g) is 495.8 kJ mol–1; \n\nwhile the electron gain enthalpy for the change Cl(g) + e– → Cl– (g) is, – 348.7 kJ mol–1 only. \n\nThe sum of the two, 147.1 kJ mol-1 is more than compensated for by the enthalpy of lattice formation of NaCl(s) (–788 kJ mol –1).\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'1','mcq','147.1kj/mol','-147.1kj/mol','174.1kj/mol','-174.1kj/mol','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40920,'chemistry','The lattice formation enthalpy is always _____.','Exothermic','In ionic solids, the sum of the electron gain enthalpy and the ionization enthalpy may be positive but still the crystal structure gets stabilized due to the energy released in the formation of the crystal lattice. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'1','fillup','Exo,ther,mic,en,do,ther,mic',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40921,'chemistry','The energy absorbed during the formation of NaCl is compensated by Energy ______ by lattice formation','Released','In ionic solids, the sum of the electron gain enthalpy and the ionization enthalpy may be positive but still the crystal structure gets stabilized due to the energy released in the formation of the crystal lattice. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'1','fillup','Re,leas,ed,ab,sorb,ed',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40922,'chemistry','The stability of ions depends on :','Lattice energy','A qualitative measure of the stability of an ionic compound is provided by its enthalpy of lattice formation and not simply by achieving octet of electrons around the ionic species in gaseous state. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 107; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'1','mcq','Lattice energy','Formation energy','Electronegativity','None of these','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40923,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement:\n\na. The electron gain enthalpy, Δₑ₉H, is the enthalpy change, when a gas phase atom in its ground state gains an electron.\n\nb. The electron gain process may be exothermic or endothermic.\n\nc. The electron gain process is always endothermic.','Only a and b','The electron gain enthalpy, Δₑ₉H, is the enthalpy change, when a gas phase atom in its ground state gains an electron. \n\nThe electron gain process may be exothermic or endothermic. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and c','Only a and b','Only b','Only c','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40924,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement:\n\na. Electron affinity, is the positive of the energy change accompanying electron gain.\n\nb. The electron gain process may be exothermic or endothermic.\n\nc. The ionization energy is always endothermic.','Only b and c','The electron gain process may be exothermic or endothermic. \n\nThe ionization, on the other hand, is always endothermic. \n\nElectron affinity, is the negative of the energy change accompanying electron gain.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and c','Only b and c','Only b','Only c','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40925,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Ionic compounds crystallise in different crystal structures determined by the size of the ions, their packing arrangements and other factors. \n\nR: Ionic compounds in the crystalline state consist of orderly three-dimensional arrangements of cations and anions held together by coulombic interaction energies.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Ionic compounds in the crystalline state consist of orderly three-dimensional arrangements of cations and anions held together by coulombic interaction energies. \n\nThese compounds crystallise in different crystal structures determined by the size of the ions, their packing arrangements and other factors. \n\nThe crystal structure of sodium chloride, NaCl (rock salt), for example is shown in diagram.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.5) diagram(ne0415)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40926,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Most ionic compounds have cations derived from metallic elements and anions from non-metallic elements.\n\nR: The ammonium ion, NH₄+ is an exception for the aforementioned assertion. It forms the cation of a number of ionic compounds.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Most ionic compounds have cations derived from metallic elements and anions from non-metallic elements. \n\nThe ammonium ion, NH₄+(made up of two nonmetallic elements) is an exception. It forms the cation of a number of ionic compounds. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40927,'chemistry','What is the relation of ionic bond with high negative value of electron gain enthalpy?','Favours','Ionic bonds will be formed more easily between elements with comparatively low ionization enthalpies and elements with comparatively high negative value of electron gain enthalpy. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'2','mcq','Hindrance','Favours','Inverse','None of these','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40928,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statement:\n\na. The crystal structure gets stabilized due to the energy released in the formation of the crystal lattice.\n\nb. In ionic solids, the sum of the electron gain enthalpy and the ionization enthalpy may be negative.\n\nc. Therefore, the energy released in the processes is more than the energy absorbed.','Only b','In ionic solids, the sum of the electron gain enthalpy and the ionization enthalpy may be positive but still the crystal structure gets stabilized due to the energy released in the formation of the crystal lattice. \n\nTherefore, the energy released in the processes is more than the energy absorbed. \n\nThus a qualitative measure of the stability of an ionic compound is provided by its enthalpy of lattice formation and not simply by achieving octet of electrons around the ionic species in gaseous state. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only b and c','Only a and c','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40929,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: A qualitative measure of the stability of an ionic compound is provided by its enthalpy of lattice formation and not simply by achieving octet of electrons around the ionic species in gaseous state.\nR: The energy released in the formation of the crystal lattice is more than the energy absorbed','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','In ionic solids, the sum of the electron gain enthalpy and the ionization enthalpy may be positive but still the crystal structure gets stabilized due to the energy released in the formation of the crystal lattice. \n\nTherefore, the energy released in the processes is more than the energy absorbed. \n\nThus a qualitative measure of the stability of an ionic compound is provided by its enthalpy of lattice formation and not simply by achieving octet of electrons around the ionic species in gaseous state. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40930,'chemistry','The decreasing order of electron gain enthalpy of F Cl S andO is:','O<S<F<Cl','The amount of energy released when an electron is added to an isolated gaseous atom is electron gain enthalpy. \n\nDue to electron-electron repulsion in a small-sized F atom, the electron gain enthalpy of F is less negative than that of CI.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'3','order','1,O','2,S','3,F','4,Cl','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40931,'chemistry','Which of the following element has the highest electron gain enthalpy?','Chlorine','Chlorine has the highest electron gain enthalpy. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'2','mcq','Oxygen','Chlorine','Sulfur','Neon','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40932,'chemistry','Which of the following element has the least negative electron gain enthalpy?','Oxygen','Oxygen has the least negative electron gain enthalpy. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Ionic or electrovalent bond',NULL,'2','mcq','Oxygen','Chlorine','Sulfur','Neon','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(40933,'biology','One of the representatives of phylum arthropoda is [NEET 2013]','Silver fish','Phylum Arthropoda is the largest phylum of Animalia which includes insects. Examples include Apis, silkworm, Laccifer, silver fish (Lepisma), locust, etc. Puffer fish and flying fish (Exocoetus) are examples of superclass Pisces, while cuttle fish (Sepia) belongs to Mollusca.','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Puffer fish','Flying fish','Cuttle fish','Silver fish','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(40934,'biology','The characteristics of class reptilia are [Karnataka NEET 2013]','Body covered with dry and cornified skin, scales over the body are epidermal, they do not have external ears','Reptiles represent the first class of vertebrates fully adapted for life in dry places on land. The characters of reptiles are in fact a combination of characters that are found in fish and amphibians on one hand and birds and mammals on the other. Their exoskeleton is of horny epidermal scales, shields, plates and scutes. The skin is dry, cornified and devoid of glands. Reptiles lack external ears and have immovable eyelids.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 58; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Body covered with moist skin which is devoid of scales, the ear is represented by a tympanum, alimentary canal, urinary and reproductive tracts open into a common cloaca','Fresh water animals with bony endoskeleton, airbladder to regulate buoyancy','Marine animals with cartilaginous endoskeleton, body covered with placoid scales','Body covered with dry and cornified skin, scales over the body are epidermal, they do not have external ears','NEW','11-058','11-049',NULL),
(40935,'biology','Which one of the following groups of animals reproduces only by sexual means? [Karnataka NEET 2013]','Ctenophora','In ctenophores, asexual reproduction is absent. They are monoecious and fertilization is generally external. In cnidaria, asexual reproduction (budding) is found in the polyps and sexual reproduction is found in the medusa form. Both asexual and sexual reproduction occur in porifera (sponges). Asexual reproduction occurs by budding and gemmules. In protozoa, asexual reproduction takes place by binary fission, budding etc. and sexual reproduction takes place by syngamy and conjugation.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 51; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Cnidaria','Porifera','Protozoa','Ctenophora','NEW','11-051','11-042',NULL),
(40936,'biology','Sharks and dog fishes differ from skates and rays because [Karnataka NEET 2013]','Their pectoral fins are distinctly marked off from cylindrical bodies','Sharks and dogfishes have cylindrical body while skates and rays have both of their pectoral fins fused. It gives a winglike appearance and are not distinct from body.','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Gill slits are ventrally placed','Head and trunk are widened considerably','Distinct demarcation between body and tail','Their pectoral fins are distinctly marked off from cylindrical bodies','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(40937,'biology','Which one of the following is one of the paths followed by air or oxygen during respiration in the adult male periplaneta americana as it enters the animal body? [Karnataka NEET 2013]','Spiracle in metathorax, trachea, tracheloes, oxygen diffuses into cells','The respiratory system is well developed in a cockroach in order to compensate the poorly developed circulatory system. It consists of tracheae, tracheoles and spiracles. The main tracheal trunks open to the exterior on body surface through','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Spiracle in metathorax, trachea, tracheloes, oxygen diffuses into cells','Mouth, bronchial tube, trachea, oxygen enters cells','Spiracles in prothorax, tracheoles, trachea, oxygen diffuses into cells.','Hypopharynx, mouth, pharynx, trachea, tissues','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(40938,'biology','Pheretima and its close relatives derive nourishment from [2012]','Decaying fallen leaves and soil organic matter','Pheretima (earthworm) and related organisms feed upon the decaying organic matter found in the soil. They also feed on the bits of plants and animal matter. Thus, they are saprophytes.','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Sugarcane roots','Decaying fallen leaves and soil organic matter','Soil insects','Small pieces of fresh fallen leaves of maize','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(40939,'biology','Which one of the following categories of animals, is correctly described with no single exception in it? [Mains 2012]','All bony fishes have four pairs of gills and an operculum on each side','All bony fishes have four pairs of gills and an operculum on each side, it has no exception rest all options has one or more exception.Crocodile in reptiles has 4 chambered heart.Some sponges may live in fresh water.Ornithorhynchus is an oviparous mammal.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 57; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','All reptiles possess scales, have a three chambered heart and are cold blooded(poikilothermal).','All bony fishes have four pairs of gills and an operculum on each side','All sponges are marine and have collared cells.','All mammals are viviparous and possess diaphragm for breathing','NEW','11-057','11-048',NULL),
(41403,'chemistry','Which is not the postulate of VSEPR theory:\n\na. Electron pairs like to occupy that place in space that minimize repulsion between them\n\nb. The valance shell is taken as sphere with the electron pairs localizing on the spherical surface at maximum distance from one another\n\nc. A multiple bond is treated as if it is a single electron pair \n\nd. Valence shell electrons of bonding atoms overlap at maximum distance','Only d','Valence bond theory describes a covalent bond as the overlap of half-filled atomic orbitals (each containing a single electron) that yield a pair of electrons shared between the two bonded atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 113; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c','Only d','NEW','11-112','11-112',NULL),
(40940,'biology','What will you look for to identify the sex of the following? [2011]','Male shark - claspers borne on pelvic fins','Tail end is straight in female Ascaris, while tail end is curved ventral words in male Ascaris. Anal cerci is present in both male and female cockroach, while anal style is present only in the male cockroach. The forelimbs in both frogs (male and female) bear small articular pads dorsally at the joints of digit, but the males possess a special nuptial, copulatory pad on ventral side of the first finger of each forelimb. Copulatory pad appears merely as rough patches, but during breeding season, these become thick and sticky. In copulation, the male strongly grips a female under her armpits by means of these pads. Claspers are modified inner edges of pelvic fins in male sharks. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 57; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Female ascari - sharply curved posterior end','Male frog - copulatory pad on the first digit of the hind limb','Female cockroach - anal cerci','Male shark - claspers borne on pelvic fins','NEW','11-057','11-048',NULL),
(40941,'biology','Which one of the following groups of animals is correctly matched with its characteristic feature without any exception? [2011]','Chondrichthyes : possess cartilaginous endoskeleton','Crocodile in reptiles has 4 chambered heart.Some chordates maybe jawless like agnatha.Ornithorhynchus is oviparous mammal.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 56; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Reptilia : possess 3chambered heart with an incompletely divided ventricle','Chordata : possess a mouth with an upper and a lower jaw','Chondrichthyes : possess cartilaginous endoskeleton','Mammalia : give birth to young ones','NEW','11-056','11-047',NULL),
(40942,'biology','Which one of the following statements is totally wrong about the occurrence of notochord, while the other three are correct? [Mains 2011]','It is absent throughout life in humans from the very beginning.','Humans are mammals which are chordates. Phylum chordata includes animals which possess a notochord either throughout or during early embryonic life. In Ascidia (urochordata), notochord is present only in larval tail while in Amphioxus (cephalochordata), it extends from head to tail region and is persistent throughout their life. It is replaced by a vertebral column in adult frog.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 55; Paragraph No. 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','It is present only in larval tail in ascidian.','It is replaced by a vertebral column in adult frog.','It is absent throughout life in humans from the very beginning.','It is present throughout life in amphioxus','NEW','11-055','11-046',NULL),
(40943,'biology','Ureters act as urinogenital ducts in [Mains 2011]','Male frogs','In male frogs, two ureters act as urinogenital duct which open into the cloaca. They run backwards from the kidneys and open into the cloaca. In female ureters carry urine alone, while in male both sperms and urine are carried. Hence, are called urinogenital ducts.','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Human males','Human females','Both male and female frogs','Male frogs','NEW','11-057','11-048',NULL),
(40944,'biology','One example of animals having a single opening to the outside that serves both as mouth as well as anus is [2010]','Fasciola','In Fasciola (flatworms) the body has a single cavity with one opening to the outside. The single opening functions as both mouth for ingestion (intake of food) and anus for egestion (undigested food is passed out). It is called blind sac plan. Other examples are coelenterates.','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Octopus','Asterias','Ascidia','Fasciola','NEW','11-051','11-042',NULL),
(40945,'biology','Which one of the following statements about all the four of spongilla, leech, dolphin and penguin is correct? [2010]','Spongilla has special collared cells called choanocytes, not found in the remaining three.','Spongilla is a common, widely distributed fresh water sponge belonging to phylum porifera. Canal system in Spongilla is essentially of rhagon type with choanocytes restricted to small rounded chambers. It is not found in leech, dolphin and penguin. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 49; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Penguin is homoiothermic while the remaining three are poikilothermic.','Leech is a fresh water form while all others are marine.','Spongilla has special collared cells called choanocytes, not found in the remaining three.','All are bilaterally symmetrical','NEW','11-049','11-040',NULL),
(40946,'biology','Which one of the following kinds of animals are triploblastic? [2010]','Flatworms','Triploblastic is a condition which describes an animal having a body composed of three embryonic germ layers : the ectoderm, mesoderm and endoderm. Most multicellular animals belonging to phylum platyhelminthes to phylum chordata are triploblastic. Ctenophores, sponges and corals are diploblastic.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 51; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Flatworms','Sponges','Ctenophores','Corals','NEW','11-051','11-042',NULL),
(40947,'biology','Which one of the following statements about certain given animals is correct? [Mains 2010]','Roundworms (aschelminthes) are pseudocoelomates','Acoelomates are animals having no body cavity or coelom. Examples are poriferans coelenterates, ctenophora, platyhelminthes and nemertinea. In pseudocoelomates, body space is pseudocoelom or false coelom. Examples are ectoprocta, aschelminthes. In coelomates, body space is a true coelom enclosed by mesoderm on both sides. Remaining phyla of bilateria, from annelida to arthropoda are coelomates. Molluses and insects are coelomates while flatworms are acoelomates.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 52; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Roundworms (aschelminthes) are pseudocoelomates','Molluses are acoelomates','Insects are pseudocoelomates','Flatworms(Platyhelminthes) are pseudocoelomates.','NEW','11-057','11-048',NULL),
(40948,'biology','In which one of the following organisms its excretory organs are correctly stated? [Mains 2010]','Earthworm – pharyngeal, integumentary and septal nephridia','Nephridia is the excretory organ of the earthworm. Earthworms have three types of nephridial structures called as septal, integumentary and pharyngeal nephridia. These three nephridial structures are present on different positions in the body and also vary in structures. Septal and pharyngeal nephridia are both enteronephric i.e., nitrogen products are expelled in gut. Integumentary nephridia is exonephric i.e., nitrogen waste products are directly discharged outside. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 52; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Humans – kidneys, sebaceous glands and tear glands','Earthworm – pharyngeal, integumentary and septal nephridia','Cockroach – malpighian tubules and enteric caeca','Frog – kidneys, skin and buccal epithelium','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(40949,'biology','Crocodile and penguin are similar to whale and dogfish in which one of the following features? [Mains 2010]','Have gill slits at some stage','Animals belonging to Phylum Chordata are fundamentally characterised by the presence of a notochord, a dorsal hollow nerve cord and paired pharyngeal gill slits. Crocodile, penguin, whale and dogfish are all chordates. All of them have gill slits or have had it during embryonic development. Thus, paired gill slits are present in these animal at some stage of life. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 55; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Possess a solid single stranded central nervous system','Lay eggs and guard them till they hatch','Possess bony skeleton','Have gill slits at some stage','NEW','11-055','11-046',NULL),
(40950,'biology','Which one of the following groups of animals is bilaterally symmetrical and triploblastic? [2009]','Aschelminthes (round worms)','Aschelminthes is a superphylum consisting of pseudocoelomates. These are mostly aquatic, free living or parasitic. Their body is slender, bilaterally symmetrical and triploblastic. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 52; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Aschelminthes (round worms)','Ctenophores','Sponges','Coelenterates','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(40951,'biology','If a live earthworm is pricked with a needle on its outer surface without damaging its gut, the fluid that comes out is [2009]','Coelomic fluid','Coelom or body cavity of earthworm is filled with coelomic fluid. It lies between body wall and alimentary canal. So if a live earthworm is pricked with a needle on its outer surface without damaging the gut then only coelomic fluid will come out.','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Coelomic fluid','Haemolymph','Slimy mucus','Excretory fluid','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(40952,'biology','Which one of the following pairs of animals comprises ‘jawless fishes’? [2009]','Lampreys and hag fishes','Agnatha is subphylum or superclass of marine and fresh water vertebrates that lack jaws. They are fishlike animals with cartilaginous skeletons and welldeveloped sucking mouthparts with horny teeth. The only living agnathans are lampreys and hagfishes (Class Cyclostomata), which are parasites or scavengers.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 56; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Mackerals and rohu','Lampreys and hag fishes','Guppies and hag fishes','Lampreys and eels','NEW','11-056','11-047',NULL),
(40953,'biology','Which one of the following in birds, indicates their reptilian ancestry? [2008]','Scales on their hind limbs','Birds have originated from some ancestral reptilian stalk. These two classes have so many features in common that link the two groups. The evidence of reptilian ancestry of birds is furnished by their comparative anatomy, embryology and palaeontology. One of the features is that all birds have horny epidermal scales confined to the lower parts of their legs and feet, which are exactly like the epidermal scales of the reptiles. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 58; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Two special chambers crop and gizzard in their digestive tract','Eggs with a calcareous shell','Scales on their hind limbs','Fourchambered heart','NEW','11-058','11-049',NULL),
(40954,'biology','Ascaris is characterized by [2008]','Presence of neither true coelom nor metamerism','Ascaris belong to the Phylum Nematoda of Superphylum Aschelminthes. They have a cylindrical body without showing any metamerism, a pseudocoel (false coelom) and a complete digestive tract lined by endodermal epithelium. The cuticle covering the body surface bears minute transverse striations giving a pseudosegmented appearance to the worm.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 52; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Presence of true coelom but absence of metamerism','Presence of true coelom and metamerism (metamerisation)','Absence of true coelom but presence of metamerism','Presence of neither true coelom nor metamerism','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(40955,'biology','Which one of the following pairs of items correctly belongs to the category of organs mentioned against it? [2008]','Nephridia of earthworm and malpighian tubules of cockroach excretory organs','Nephridia are excretory organs of earthworm which consist of a simple or branched tube formed by the ingrowth of ectoderm with cilia at the inner end. Excretory products diffuse into the nephridium and are wafted to the exterior by ciliary action. Malpighian tubules are the organs that are involved in the excretion of nitrogenous wastes in cockroach. It open into the intestine; selectively extract from the blood uric acid, whichtogether with water and salts – is deposited into the hindgut and excreted in the faeces.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 52; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Nephridia of earthworm and malpighian tubules of cockroach excretory organs','Wings of honey bee and wings of crow homologous organs','Thorn of bougainvillea and tendrils of cucurbita analogous organs','Nictitating membrane and blind spot in human eye vestigial organs','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(40956,'biology','Which one of the following phyla is correctly matched with its two general characteristics? [2008]','Arthropoda body divided into head, thorax and abdomen and respiration by tracheae','Arthropods are the largest phylum of Kingdom Animalia that characteristically possesses an outer body layer – the cuticle. The body is composed of segments usually forming distinct specialized body regions, i.e., head, thorax and abdomen. In them the trachea or windpipe or book lungs are the respiratory organs found in terrestrial forms, which help in respiration. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 53; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Echinodermata pentamerous radial symmetry and mostly internal fertilization','Mollusca normally oviparous and development through a trochophore or veliger larva','Arthropoda body divided into head, thorax and abdomen and respiration by tracheae','Chordata notochord at some stage and separate anal and urinary openings to the outside','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(40957,'biology','Which one of the following is not a characteristic of phylum annelida? [2008]','Pseudocoelom','Phylum Annelida comprises invertebrates, which are segmented worms having cylindrical soft bodies showing metameric segmentation. These are triploblastic animals showing bilateral symmetry. A true coelom is present which is filled with coelomic fluid containing cells. Annelids are perhaps the first animals to have a true schizocoelic coelom.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 53; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Pseudocoelom','Ventral nerve cord','Closed circulatory system','Segmentation','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(40958,'biology','What is common between parrot, platypus and kangaroo? [2007]','Homeothermy','Homoiothermy is the maintenance by an animal of its internal body temperature at a relatively constant value by using metabolic processes to counteract fluctuations in the temperature of the environment. Homoiothermy occurs in birds and mammals, which are described as endotherms. The heat produced by their tissue metabolism and the heat lost to the environment are balanced by various means to keep body temperature constant: (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 59 and 60)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Toothless jaws','Functional postanal tail','Ovoparity','Homeothermy','NEW','11-059','11-050',NULL),
(40959,'biology','What is true about nereis, scorpion, cockroach and silver fish? [2007]','They all possess dorsal heart.','Nereis, scorpion, cockroach and silver fish are all invertebrates and thus possess dorsal heart. Nereis is a marine animal while other animals mentioned in the question are terrestrial. Nereis belongs to Phylum Annelida while rest of the animals belong to Phylum Arthropoda. Jointed appendages are present in scorpion, cockroach and silver fish. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 55; Table 4.1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','They all possess dorsal heart.','None of them is aquatic.','They all belong to the same phylum.','They all have jointed paired appendages','NEW','11-055','11-046',NULL),
(40960,'biology','Biradial symmetry and lack of cnidoblasts are the characteristics of [2006]','Ctenoplana and beroe','Ctenophora is a small phylum of exclusively marine, invertebrate animals. Ctenoplana and Beroe are examples of ctenophora. They have biradial symmetry (a combination of radial and bilateral symmetries). They lack the specialized stinging cells (nematocysts) found in coelenterates, but one species (Haeckelia rubra) incorporates those of its jellyfish prey for its own defense.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 51; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Hydra and starfish','Starfish and sea anemone','Ctenoplana and beroe','Aurelia and paramecium','NEW','11-051','11-042',NULL),
(40961,'biology','Two common characters found in centipede, cockroach, and crab are [2006]','Jointed legs and chitinous exoskeleton','Centipede, cockroach, and crab all belong to phylum arthropoda which are characterized by jointed legs and chitinous exoskeleton. Arthropods have bilaterally symmetrical and metamerically segmented body with haemocoel and open blood vascular system.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 53; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Book lungs and antennae','Compound eyes and anal cerci','Jointed legs and chitinous exoskeleton','Green gland and tracheae','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(40962,'biology','In which one of the following sets of animals do all the four give birth to young ones? [2006]','Kangaroo, hedgehog, dolphin, loris','Kangaroo, hedgehog, dolphin and Loris are mammals and thus give brith to young ones. Ostrich and kiwi are brids that lay eggs. Platypus is a most primitive living mammal that lays eggs. Other animals in the options are mammals and give birth to young ones. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 60; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Kangaroo, hedgehog, dolphin, loris','Lion, bat, whale, ostrich','Platypus, penguin, bat, hippopotamus','Shrew, bat, cat, kiwi','NEW','11-060','11-051',NULL),
(40963,'biology','Which one of the following is not a living fossil? [2006]','Archaeopteryx','Living fossil is a term for any living species (or clade) of organism which closely resembles species otherwise only known from fossils and has no close living relatives. These species have all survived major extinction events, and generally retain low taxonomic diversities. Some examples of living fossils in animals are – coelocanth, coral (polyp), crocodylia (crocodiles, gavials and alligators), horseshoe crab (Limulus polyphemus), monotremes (Platypus and Echidna), snoutnosed frog (Nasikabatrachus sahyadrensis). Archaeopteryx is the earliest and most primitive known bird to date. It lived in the Jurassic Period around.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12; Page 130; Paragraph No. 1 and fig. 7.2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Peripatus','King crab','Sphenodon','Archaeopteryx','NEW',NULL,'11-050',NULL),
(40964,'biology','Metameric segmentation is the characteristic of [2006]','Annelida and arthropoda','The term metamerism refers to a linear repetition of parts in an animal body. It occurs in three highly organized phyla : Annelida, Arthropoda and Chordata. Each segment is called a metamere, or somite. Segmentation often affects both external and internal structures. Such a condition is called metameric segmentation. In chordates, the segmentation is apparent only in the embryonic stage. In the adult chordates, segmentation is visible in the internal structures, such as vertebrae, ribs, nerves and blood vessels. Other animals have unsegmented bodies.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 52 and 53)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Mollusca and chordata','Platyhelminthes and arthropoda','Echinodermata and annelida','Annelida and arthropoda','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(40965,'biology','Which one of the following is a matching set of a phylum and its three examples? [2006]','Mollusca loligo, teredo, octopus','Mollusca includes those animals which have soft bodies, usually furnished with a shell. The body is often divided into a head, with eyes or tentacles, a muscular foot and a visceral mass housing the organs. Loligo (squid or sea arrow), Teredo (shipworm), Octopus are some of their examples. In option (a) Spongilla and Euplectella belong to porifera but Pennatula (the sea pen or sea feather) belongs to coelenterata. In option (b) Physalia and Aurelia belong to cnidaria but Bonellia belongs to Phylum Annelida. In option (c) Planaria and Schistosoma belong to platyhelminthes but Enterobius (Pinworm) belongs to aschelminthes.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 54; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Porifera spongilla, euplectella, pennatula','Cnidaria bonellia, physalia, aurelia','Platyhelminthes planaria, schistosoma, enterobius','Mollusca loligo, teredo, octopus','NEW','11-054','11-050',NULL),
(40966,'biology','In contrast to annelids the platyhelminthes show [2005]','Absence of body cavity','Platyhelminthes do not have body cavity so they are acoelomates. In annelids, the body cavity is true and schizocoelous. Both annelids and platyhelminthes have bilateral symmertry. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 51; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Absence of body cavity','Bilateral symmetry','Radial symmetry','Presence of pseudocoel','NEW','11-051','11-042',NULL),
(40967,'biology','In arthropoda, head and thorax are often used to form cephalothorax, but in which one of the following classes, is the body divided into head thorax and abdomen? [2004]','Insecta','Body in arthropoda is segmented.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 53; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Insecta','Myriapoda','Crustacea','Arachnida and curstacea','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(40968,'biology','The animals with bilateral symmetry in young stage, and radial pentamerous symmetry in the adult stage, belong to the phylum [2004]','Echinodermata','Echinoderms are triploblastic animals with organ system level of organisation. Larval forms possess bilateral symmetry while adults have radial symmetry. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 54; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Annelida','Mollusca','Cnidaria','Echinodermata','NEW','11-054','11-045',NULL),
(40969,'biology','Similarity in ascaris lumbricoides and anopheles stephensi is [2000]','Sexual dimorphism','Sexual dimorphism is the difference in the form of individuals of different sexes but of same species. Sexes in Ascaris are separate and sexual dimorphism is well defined. Males are smaller than females. They possess a recurved tail with pre and post anal papillae, a cloaca, and a pair of spicules or penial setae. In Anopheles, the ends of maxillary palps in males are clubshaped while in females they are not.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 52 and 53)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Sexual dimorphism','Metamerism','Anaerobic respiration','Endoparasitism','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(40970,'biology','What is true for mammalia? [2000]','Platypus is oviparous.','Mammals are viviparous i.e., they give birth to young ones. Protherians (e.g., Platypus) are primitive mammals and lay eggs, so they are oviparous. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 60; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Platypus is oviparous.','Bats have feather.','Elephant is ovoviviparous.','Diaphragm is absent in them','NEW','11-059','11-050',NULL),
(40971,'biology','The canal system is a characteristic feature of [1999]','Sponges','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 49; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Echinoderms','Sponges','Helminthes','Coelenterates','NEW','11-049','11-040',NULL),
(40972,'biology','Which of the following is not found in birds? [1999]','Forelimb','Forelimbs are absent in birds as they are modified into wings for flight. They are attached high on the back, to the anterior or thoracic region of the trunk, and are very powerful when compared with the size and strength of the bird. Each wing is elongated, flattened and distally pointed with its longitudinal axis at right angles to that of the trunk. Hindlimb is made of three parts thigh, shank and foot. Pectoral girdle on each side consists of three bones a large coracoid, scapula and clavicle. Pelvic girdle consists of ilium, ischium and pubis.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 58; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Pelvic girdle','Pectoral girdle','Hindlimb','Forelimb','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(40973,'biology','The long bones are hollow and connected by air passages. They are the characteristics of [1998]','Aves','Aves are the animals that fly so their body weight should be less and for this their bones are hollow and connected by air passages. Reptilia, land vertebrates and mammals do not have hollow bones.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 58; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Reptilia','Land vertebrates','Aves','Mammals','NEW','11-058','11-049',NULL),
(40974,'biology','Solenocytes are the main excretory structures in [1998]','Platyhelminthes','Solenocytes are also called flame cells. It is a cup shaped cell, which contains group of cilia and this is the main excretory organ of platyhelminthes. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 51; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Echinodermates','Platyhelminthes','Annelids','Molluscs','NEW','11-051','11-042',NULL),
(40975,'biology','Most appropriate term to describe the life cycle of obelia is [1998]','Metagenesis','Obelia belongs to the Phylum Coelenterata. In Obelia, lifecycle includes two clearly defined phases : a fixed polypoid phase (hydroid colony) and a pelagic medusoid phase. Hydroid colony has no gonads and reproduces by asexual budding to give rise to medusae. On the other hand, medusae reproduce exclusively by sexual method (ova and sperms) to give rise to new hydroid colonies. This fact apparently seems to have given rise to the idea of alternation of generations, also called metagenesis. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 50; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Metamorphosis','Neoteny','Metagenesis','All of these','NEW','11-050','11-041',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(40976,'biology','Coelom is found between [1996]','Mesoderm and body wall (endoderm)','Coelom is a fluidfilled cavity that forms the main body cavity of vertebrate and most invertebrate animals. It is found between mesoderm and body wall (endoderm). (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 48; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Body wall and ectoderm','Ectoderm and endoderm','Mesoderm and body wall (endoderm)','Mesoderm and ectoderm','NEW','11-048','11-039',NULL),
(40977,'biology','Pneumatic bone is found in [1996]','Pigeon','Pneumatic bone is present in pigeon to keep the bones light weight because the pigeon has to fly. Pneumatic bone has a hollow cavity, which makes it light.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 58; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Shark','Rana','Pigeon','Whale','NEW','11-058','11-049',NULL),
(40978,'biology','The nephridia in earthworm are analogous to [1996]','Flame cells of planaria','Analogous organs are organs of very disparate organisms and are superficially similar but have evolved from vastly different origins. They have same function but different structure. The nephridia in earthworm are analogous to flame cells of Planaria since both of them have excretory functions. Nematoblasts of Hydra are organs of locomotion, food capture and anchorage. Gills and trachea are organs of respiration in prawn and insects respectively.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 51; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Nematoblasts of hydra','Flame cells of planaria','Gills of prawn','Trachea of insects','NEW','11-051','11-042',NULL),
(40979,'biology','One of the special characters of coelenterata only is the occurrence of [1994]','Nematocysts','The cells characteristic of the coelenterates include stinging cells (cnidocytes or cnidoblasts or nematoblasts) for offence and defence. The stinging cells, when discharged, give out from a sac, the cnide or cnidocyst or nematocyst, a long threadtube that may coil around the prey, or attach to it, or inject a toxin, called hypnotoxin, into it to paralyse it.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 50; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Polymorphism','Flame cells','Hermaphroditism','Nematocysts','NEW','11-050','11-041',NULL),
(40980,'biology','Which one occurs in echinodermata? [1991]','Radial symmetry','Radial symmetry is the arrangement of parts in an organ or organism such that cutting through the centre of the structure in any direction produces two halves that are mirror images of each other. All animals belonging to the cnidaria (e.g., jellyfish) and echinodermata (e.g., starfish) are radially symmetrical. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 54; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Bilateral symmetry','Radial symmetry','Porous body','Soft skin','NEW','11-054','11-045',NULL),
(40981,'biology','Exoskeleton of arthropods is composed of[NEET 2022]','Chitin','Exoskeleton of arthropods is composed of Chitin','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Cutin','Cellulose','Chitin','Glucosamine','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(40982,'biology','In which of the following animals, digestive tract has additional chambers like crop and gizzard?[NEET 2022]','Pavo, Psittacula, Corvus','Pavo, Psittacula, Corvus are birds, they contain a digestive system compriising of additional chambers like crop and gizzard.','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Corvus, Columba, Chameleon','Bufo, Balaenoptera, Bangarus','Catla, Columba, Crocodilus','Pavo, Psittacula, Corvus','NEW','11-058','11-049',NULL),
(40983,'biology','Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\n\nAssertion (A) : All vertebrates are chordates but all chordates are not vertebrates.\n\nReason (R) : Notochord is replaced by vertebral column in the adult vertebrates.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the option given below :\n[NEET 2022]','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','All vertebrates are chordates but all chordates are not vertebrates, Chordates are characterized by the presence of notochord during any stage in their lifetime or lifecycle, but vertebrates are those animals who posses a vertebral column, by transformation of notochord. Notochord is replaced by vertebral column in the adult vertebrates.\n:','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','11-055','11-046',NULL),
(40984,'biology','Select the correct statements : \n (A) Platyhelminthes are triploblastic \npseudocoelomate and bilaterally symmetrical \norganisms. \n (B) Ctenophores reproduce only sexually and \nfertilization is external. \n (C) In tapeworm, fertilization is internal but sexes \nare not separate. \n (D) Ctenophores are exclusively marine, diploblastic and bioluminescent organisms. \n(E) In sponges, fertilization is external and \ndevelopment is direct. \n Choose the correct answer from the options given \nbelow :[ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(2) (B), (C) and (D) only','Explanation: (A) Platyhelminthes are triploblastic pseudocoelomate and bilaterally symmetrical organisms. This statement is incorrect. Platyhelminthes are triploblastic, but they are acoelomate (lacking a body cavity) and exhibit bilateral symmetry.\n\n(B) Ctenophores reproduce only sexually, and fertilization is external. This statement is correct. Ctenophores reproduce through external fertilization, where the male and female gametes are released into the water and fertilization occurs outside the body.\n(C) In tapeworm, fertilization is internal, but sexes are not separate. This statement is correct. Tapeworms are hermaphroditic, meaning they have both male and female reproductive organs within the same individual. Fertilization occurs internally.\n\n(D) Ctenophores are exclusively marine, diploblastic, and bioluminescent organisms. This statement is correct. Ctenophores are exclusively marine organisms, and they are diploblastic, having two germ layers (ectoderm and endoderm). Many species of ctenophores are bioluminescent\n(E) In sponges, fertilization is external, and development is direct. This statement is not included in the given options.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is 2. (B), (C), and (D) only.','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','(1) (A), (C) and (D) only','(2) (B), (C) and (D) only','(3) (A) and (E) only','(4) (B) and (D) only','NEW','11-060','11-051',NULL),
(40985,'biology','Select the correct statements with reference to chordates.\nA. Presence of a mid-dorsal, solid and double nerve cord.\nB. Presence of closed circulatory system.\nC. Presence of paired pharyngeal gill slits.\nD. Presence of dorsal heart\nE. Triploblastic pseudocoelomate animals.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below: [ NEET 2023]','(1) B and C only','B and C only','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','1','mcq','(1) B and C only','(2) B, D and E only','(3) C, D and E only','(4) A, C and D only','NEW','11-055','11-046',NULL),
(40986,'biology','The unique mammalian characteristics are: [ NEET 2023 ]','(1) hairs, pinna and mammary glands','Characteristics features of mammals:\n(1) Body is covered with hair.\n(2) Skin is provided with sweat and sebaceous glands.\n(3) Heart is four chambered.\n(4) Fertilization is internal.\n(5) Females have mammary glands to produce milk to nourish their young ones.\n(6) External ear-pinna present.\n(7) Eyes have eye lids.\n(8) Warm-blooded.\n(9) Respiration through lungs.\n(10) Body cavity dividedinto thorax and abdomen by muscular diaphragm.','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','1','mcq','(1) hairs, pinna and mammary glands','(2) hairs, pinna and indirect development','(3) pinna, monocondylic skull and mammary glands','(4) hairs, tympanic membrane and mammary glands','NEW','11-055','11-046',NULL),
(40987,'biology','Select the Incorrect statements with respect to Cyclostomes : \n (a) They lack scales and paired fins. \n (b) They have circular mouth with Jaws. \n (c) They bear 6-15 pairs of gills. \n (d) They migrate to deep sea for spawning. \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below : [ RE NEET 2022]','(3) (b) and (d) only','Option (d) is the correct answer because statement (b) is false as cyclostomes have a circular mouth without jaws and statement (d) is false as they migrate from marine water to fresh water for spawning. Statements (a) and (c) are correct for cyclostomes as they have 6-15 pairs of gills and body is divoid of scales and paired fins.','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','1','mcq','(1) (a) and (b) only','(2) (b) and (c) only','(3) (b) and (d) only','(4) (a) and (d) only','NEW','11-056','11-047',NULL),
(40988,'biology','Which of the following animals has three chambered heart ? [ RE NEET 2022]','(3) Chelone','Chelone','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','1','mcq','(1) Scoliodon','(2) Hippocampus','(3) Chelone','(4) Pteropus','NEW','11-056','11-047',NULL),
(40989,'biology','Match List I with List II.\nList I - List II\nA. Taenia - I. Nephridia\nB. Paramoecium - II. Contractile vacuole\nC. Periplaneta - III. Flame cells\nD. Pheretima - IV. Urecose gland\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below: [ NEET 2023]','(2) A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I','A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','1','mcq','(1) A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III','(2) A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I','(3) A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III','(4) A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(40990,'biology','Match List-I with List-II. \nList-I List-II\n(A) Contractile vacuole (I) Asterias\n(B) Water vascular system (II) Amoeba\n(C) Canal system (III) Spongilla\n(D) Flame cells (IV) Taenia\n Choose the correct answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(4) (A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)','(A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1','Animal Kingdom','1','mcq','(1) (A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)','(2) (A)-(I), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)','(3) (A)-(III), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)','(4) (A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(40991,'biology','Which one of the following pairs is mismatched?','Pila globosa-pearl oyster','Pila globosa is a species of freshwater snail, and is not related to oysters or pearl formation. Therefore, the pair is mismatched. The other pairs are correctly matched as follows: Apis indica - honey bee - Apis indica is one of the species of honey bees that are commonly found in India. Laccifer lacca - lac insect - Laccifer lacca is a species of scale insect that is commonly used for producing lacquer. Bombyx mori - silk worm - Bombyx mori is a species of moth that is widely cultivated for the silk it produces.','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Pila globosa-pearl oyster','Apis indica - honey bee','Laccifer lacca - lac insect','Bombyx mori- silkworm','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(40992,'biology','Match the following genera with their respective phylum:\n\nColumn I - Column II\n\n(a) Ophiura - (i) Mollusca\n\n(b) Physalia - (ii) Platyhelminthes\n\n(c) Pinctada - (iii)Echinodermata\n\n(d) Planaria - (iv)Coelenterata\n\nSelect the correct option:','iii,iv, i, ii',NULL,'Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','iv,i, iii, ii','iii,iv, i, ii','i,iii,iv,ii','iii,iv, ii, i','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(40993,'biology','Which one of the following features are not true for chordates?\n\n(a) Heart is dorsal.\n\n(b) Pharynx is perforated by gill slits.\n\n(c) Central nervous system is ventral, solid and single.\n\n(d) Post-anal tail is present.\n\n(e) Notochord is present.\n\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the option give below:','(a) and (c) only',NULL,'Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','(e) only','(a), (d) and (c) only','(b) and (c) only','(a) and (c) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(40994,'biology','From the following statements select the wrong one:','Animals belonging to the phylum Porifera are exclusively marine','Page 49,50 XI NCERT','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Prawn has two pairs of antennae','Millipedes have two pairs of appendages in each segment of the body','Animals belonging to the phylum Porifera are exclusively marine','Nematocysts are characteristics of the phylum Cnidaria','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(40995,'biology','Which one of the following groups of animals is correctly matched with its one characteristic feature without even a single exception?','Chondrichthyes - possess cartilaginous endoskeleton',NULL,'Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Chordata possess a mouth provided with an upper and a lower jaw','Chondrichthyes - possess cartilaginous endoskeleton','Mammalia - give birth to young ones','Reptilia possesses 3-incomplete heart with one incompletely divided ventricle','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(40996,'biology','Given below are two statements:\n\nI : Amphibians and reptiles have a 3-chambered heart with two atria and a single ventricle, and are oviparous in nature\n\nII : Crocodiles possess a 4 chambered heart with two ventricles and two atria and are viviparous in nature\n\nSelect the most appropriate option:','I is correct but II is incorrect',NULL,'Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','I is correct but II is incorrect','I is incorrect but II is correct','Both I and II are correct','Both I and II are incorrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(41001,'chemistry','The energy required to break one mole of an ionic solid into its constituent ions is:','Lattice energy','The Lattice Enthalpy of an ionic solid is defined as the energy required to completely separate one mole of a solid ionic compound into gaseous constituent ions. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 107; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lattice enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','Ionization enthalpy','Lattice energy','Electron gain enthalpy','Dissolution Enthalpy','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(41002,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement about lattice enthalpy:\n\na. 788kj energy required to separate one mole of ionic NaCl into its ions.\n\nb. Lattice enthalpy calculation involves both attractive forces between opposite charges and repulsive forces between like charges.\n\nc. Lattice enthalpy can be calculated by interactions between like and opposite charges only.\n\nd. Crystal geometry is not much important in lattice energy calculations.','Only a and b','The ionization enthalpy for Na+(g) formation from Na(g) is 495.8 kJ mol–1 ; \n\nwhile the electron gain enthalpy for the change Cl(g) + e–→ Cl– (g) is, – 348.7 kJ mol–1 only. \n\nThe sum of the two, 147.1 kJ mol-1 is more than compensated for by the enthalpy of lattice formation of NaCl(s) (–788 kJ mol –1). \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 107; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lattice enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b and d','Only a and c','Only a and b','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(41003,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Lattice enthalpy involves both the attractive forces between ions of opposite charges and the repulsive forces between ions of like charge.\n\nR: The solid crystal being three dimensional; it calculate lattice enthalpy directly from the interaction of forces of attraction and repulsion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false','This process involves both the attractive forces between ions of opposite charges and the repulsive forces between ions of like charge. \n\nThe solid crystal being three dimensional; it is not possible to calculate lattice enthalpy directly from the interaction of forces of attraction and repulsion only. Factors associated with the crystal geometry have to be included. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 107; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lattice enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(41004,'chemistry','Consider the lattice enthalpy mathematically represent as given the diagram.\n(n0405)','ΔU-molar lattice energy, ΔH-molar lattice enthalpy, ΔVₘ-change of the volume per mole, p-outer pressure','The Lattice Enthalpy of an ionic solid is defined as the energy required to completely separate one mole of a solid ionic compound into gaseous constituent ions. \n\nΔU = molar lattice energy, ΔH = molar lattice enthalpy, ΔVₘ = change of the volume per mole, p = outer pressure. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 107; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lattice enthalpy',NULL,'2','match','ΔU,molar lattice energy','ΔH,molar lattice enthalpy','ΔVₘ,change of the volume per mole','p,outer pressure','NEW','11-106','11-106','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/match/41004q.png'),
(41005,'chemistry','Which of the following has highest lattice energy?','NaF','NaF shows highest lattice energy among these compounds since Na+ features ions of the same charge, the lattice energy increases as the size of the ions increases. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 107; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Lattice enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','NaF','KF','RbF','LiF','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(41006,'biology','Given are the four matches of phyla with their characteristic cells [2013]\n\na. coelenterata - nematocytes\n\nb. porifera - choanocytes\n\nc. ctenophora - solenocytes\n\nd. platyhelminthes - nephrocytes\n\nMark the option that has both correct matches','A and B','Coelenterates possess cnidoblasts or cnidocytes (which contain the stinging capsules or nematocysts) present on the tentacles and the body. Cnidoblasts are used for anchorage, defense and for the capture of prey. \nPoriferans possess Choanocytes or collar cells line the spongocoel and the canals.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 49 and 50)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Aiims Questions','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','A and B','B and C','C and D','B and D','NEW','11-049','11-049',NULL),
(41007,'biology','Cockroaches are brown or black bodied animals that are included in class _______ of phylum_______. [2014]','Insecta; arthropoda','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 53; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Aiims Questions','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Reptilia; annelida','Insecta; arthropoda','Insecta; annelida','Reptilia; arthropoda','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(41008,'biology','Which are exclusively viviparous? [2012]','Whales','Whales are exclusively viviparous. In viviparous animals (majority of mammals including human beings), the zygote develops into a young one inside the body of the female organism. After attaining a certain stage of growth, the young ones are delivered out of the body of the female organism.','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Previous Year Aiims Questions','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Bony fishes','Cartilagenous fishes','Sharks','Whales','NEW','11-059','11-050',NULL),
(41101,'chemistry','Equilibrium distance between the nuclei of two bonded atoms in a molecule is:','Bond length','Bond length is defined as the equilibrium distance between the nuclei of two bonded atoms in a molecule. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 107; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','Bond order','Bond length','Bond strength','Bond enthalpy','NEW','11-107','11-107',NULL),
(41102,'chemistry','Which radius of an atom\'s core which is in contact with the core of an adjacent atom in a bonded situation:','Covalent radius','The covalent radius is measured approximately as the radius of an atom’s core which is in contact with the core of an adjacent atom in a bonded situation.\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 107; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','Covalent radius','Van der waals radius','Ionic radius','None of these','NEW','11-107','11-107',NULL),
(41103,'chemistry','Match the following:','Half of the distance between two similar atoms in a bonded situation-Covalent radius,Half of the distance between two similar atoms in a non-bonded situation-Van der waals radius,van der waals radius of Cl-180pm,Covalent radius of Cl-99pm','Covalent and van der Waals radii in a chlorine molecule. The inner circles correspond to the size of the chlorine atom (r vdw and r c are van der Waals and covalent radii respectively). \n\nAs shown in diagram \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 107; paragraph no.3) diagram(ne0401)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'3','match','Half of the distance between two similar atoms in a bonded situation,Covalent radius','Half of the distance between two similar atoms in a non-bonded situation,Van der waals radius','van der waals radius of Cl,180pm','Covalent radius of Cl,99pm','NEW','11-107','11-107',NULL),
(41104,'chemistry','The bond length of Cl₂ molecule:','198pm','Covalent and van der Waals radii of chlorine are depicted in diagram \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 107; paragraph no.4) diagram(ne0401)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','198pm','189pm','180pm','108pm','NEW','11-107','11-107',NULL),
(41105,'chemistry','The Van der waals length of Cl₂ molecule in non bonded situation:','360pm','Covalent and van der Waals radii of chlorine are depicted in diagram \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 107; paragraph no.4) diagram(ne0401)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','306pm','360pm','350pm','305pm','NEW','11-107','11-107',NULL),
(41106,'chemistry','Bond length is ______ of Covalent radius.','Double','The covalent radius is half of the distance between two similar atoms joined by a covalent bond in the same molecule. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 107; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'1','fillup','Dou,ble,tri,ple',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-107','11-107',NULL),
(41107,'chemistry','Van der waals radius is always _______ than Covalent radius.','Greater','The van der Waals radius represents the overall size of the atom which includes its valence shell in a nonbonded situation. \n\nFurther, the van der Waals radius is half of the distance between two similar atoms in separate molecules in a solid. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 107; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'3','fillup','Grea,ter,sma,llar',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-107','11-107',NULL),
(41108,'chemistry','Bond length is the sum of:','Covalent radius','Bond length is the sum of covalent radius. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 107; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','Covalent radius','Van der waals radius','Both a and b','None of these','NEW','11-107','11-107',NULL),
(41109,'chemistry','Bond length of triple bond is always _______.','Smallest','More electron density between two nuclei more closer will be two bonded nuclei smaller will be the bond length.\n\nAs shown in diagram \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Table 4.2) diagram(ne0402)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'1','fillup','Small,est,lar,gest',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-107','11-107',NULL),
(41110,'chemistry','Identify the correct order of bond length:','Alkanes>Alkenes>Alkynes>C-H','Bond length of alkanes (C-C) 154pm\n\nbond length of alkenes(C=C) 133pm\n\nbond length of alkynes 120pm\n\nbond length of (C-H) 107pm\n\nAs shown in diagram \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Table 4.2) diagram(ne0402)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'2','order','1,Alkanes','2,Alkenes','3,Alkynes','4,C-H','NEW','11-107','11-107',NULL),
(41111,'chemistry','Bond length ______ as we move along group in a periodic table.','Increase','Halogens down group size increase so bond length increase F-F =144pm, Cl-Cl = 199pm, Br-Br = 228pm, I-I = 267pm. \n\nAs shown in diagram \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Table 4.3) diagram(ne0403)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'2','fillup','In,crea,se,dec,rea,se',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-107','11-107',NULL),
(41112,'chemistry','Match the following:','HF-92pm,HCl-127pm,HBr-141pm,HI-160','HF-92pm, HCl-127pm, HBr-141pm, HI-160. \n\nAs shown in diagram \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Table 4.3) diagram(ne0403)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'3','match','HF,92pm','HCl,127pm','HBr,141pm','HI,160','NEW','11-107','11-107',NULL),
(41113,'chemistry','The bond length of O=O is','121pm','Nitrogen make triple bond 109pm O=O make double bond 121pm F-F make single bond 144pm more electron density between two bonded nuclei, more closer they will and have smaller bond length. \n\nAs shown in diagram \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Table 4.3) diagram(ne0403)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'2','mcq','109pm','121pm','144pm','None of these','NEW','11-107','11-107',NULL),
(41114,'chemistry','Match the following:','Covalent radius of P-110pm,Covalent radius of S-104pm,Covalent radius of As-121pm,Covalent radius of Se-104pm','As shown in diagram \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Table 4.4) diagram(ne0404)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'3','match','Covalent radius of P,110pm','Covalent radius of S,104pm','Covalent radius of As,121pm','Covalent radius of Se,104pm','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41115,'chemistry','Bond length can be measured by:','All of these','Bond lengths are measured by spectroscopic, X-ray diffraction and electron-diffraction techniques. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 107; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'2','mcq','Spectroscopy','X-ray diffraction','Electron diffraction','All of these','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41116,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Each atom of the bonded pair contributes to the bond length. \n\nR:  In the case of a covalent bond, the contribution from each atom is called the covalent radius of that atom.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Each atom of the bonded pair contributes to the bond length. \n\nIn the case of a covalent bond, the contribution from each atom is called the covalent radius of that atom. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 107; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41117,'chemistry','Bond length of alkane is:','154pm','Bond length of alkanes (C-C) 154pm, bond length of alkenes(C=C) 133pm, bond length of alkynes 120pm and bond length of (C-H) 107pm.\n\nAs shown in diagram \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Table 4.2) diagram(ne0402)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','154pm','133pm','120pm','107pm','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41118,'chemistry','Bond length of alkene is:','133pm','Bond length of alkanes (C-C) 154pm, bond length of alkenes(C=C) 133pm, bond length of alkynes 120pm and bond length of (C-H) 107pm. \n\nAs shown in diagram \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Table 4.2) diagram(ne0402)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','154pm','133pm','120pm','107pm','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41119,'chemistry','Bond length of alkyne is:','120pm','Bond length of alkanes (C-C) 154pm, bond length of alkenes(C=C) 133pm, bond length of alkynes 120pm and bond length of (C-H) 107pm.\n\nAs shown in diagram \n\nReference : (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Table 4.2) diagram(ne0402)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','154pm','133pm','120pm','107pm','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41120,'chemistry','Bond length of C-H is:','107pm','Bond length of alkanes (C-C) 154pm, bond length of alkenes(C=C) 133pm, bond length of alkynes 120pm and bond length of (C-H) 107pm. \n\nAs shown in diagram \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Table 4.2) diagram(ne0402)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','154pm','133pm','120pm','107pm','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41121,'chemistry','Angle between the orbitals containing bonding electron pair around the central atom in a molecule:','Bond angle','Bond angle is defined as the angle between the orbitals containing bonding electron pairs around the central atom in a molecule/complex ion. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','Bond order','Bond angle','Bond length','Bond strength','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41122,'biology','Consider following statement regarding Phylum Cnidaria and choose the correct option\n\n1. All Cnidarians are free living such as aurelia\n\n2. Cnidocytes are present on the tentacles and body of cnidarians\n\n3. Cnidarians are asymmetrical organisms\n\n4. Tissue level organization is found in cnidarians','2 and 4 both are correct','All cnidarians are not free-swimming, some of them are sessile and exhibits radial symmetry in which any plane passing from the center of body divides the animal into equal halves. Cnidocytes (which contain the stinging capsules or nematocysts) present on the tentacles and the body. Cnidarians exhibit tissue level of organisation and are diploblastic. (Reference  : NCERT Biology class 11; Page 50; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','only 1 is correct','only 2 is correct','1 and 3 both are correct','2 and 4 both are correct','NEW','11-050','11-041',NULL),
(41123,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement about bond angle:\n\na. Bond angle is expressed in degrees\n\nb. Bond angle gives idea about distribution of orbitals around the central atom in a molecule\n\nc. It helps in determining the shape of molecule\n\nd. Bond angle of H-O-H is 104.5 degree','All statements are correct','Bond angle is expressed in degree which can be experimentally determined by spectroscopic methods. \n\nIt gives some idea regarding the distribution of orbitals around the central atom in a molecule/complex ion and hence it helps us in determining its shape. \n\nFor example H–O–H bond angle in water can be represented as diagram \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Paragraph 2) diagram(ne0405)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c and d','All statements are correct','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41124,'chemistry','Amount of energy required to break one mole of bonds of a particular type between two atoms in a gaseous state is:','Bond Enthalpy','Bond enthalpy is defined as the amount of energy required to break one mole of bonds of a particular type between two atoms in a gaseous state.\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','Bond Enthalpy','Bond angle','Bond length','Bond strength','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41125,'chemistry','The unit of bond Enthalpy is:','kj/mol-1','The unit of bond enthalpy is kJ mol–1. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','kjmol','kj/mol-1','j/mol','Kgj/mol','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41126,'chemistry','The bond enthalpy of H₂ is:\n\n H₂(g) → H(g) + H(g)','435.8kj/mol-1','The H – H bond enthalpy in hydrogen molecule is 435.8 kJ mol–1. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','-435.8kj/mol','435.8kj/mol-1','453,8kj/mol-1','-453.8kj/mol','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41127,'chemistry','Match the following:','O₂(g) → O(g) + O(g)-ΔₐH⁰=498kj/mol,N₂(g) → N(g) + N(g)-ΔₐH⁰=946.0kj/mol,HCl(g) → H(g) + Cl(g)-ΔₐH⁰=431.0kj/mol,H₂O(g) → H(g) + OH(g)-ΔₐH⁰=502kj/mol','The H – H bond enthalpy in hydrogen molecule is 435.8 kJ mol–1. \n\nH2(g) →  H(g) + H(g); ΔₐH⁰ = 435.8 kJ mol–1 \n\nSimilarly the bond enthalpy for molecules containing multiple bonds, for example O₂ and N2 will be as under : \n\nO₂ (O = O) (g) →  O(g) + O(g); ΔₐH⁰ = 498 kJ mol–1 \n\nN₂ (N º N) (g) →  N(g) + N(g); ΔₐH⁰ = 946.0 kJ mol–1. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'3','match','O₂(g) → O(g) + O(g),ΔₐH⁰=498kj/mol','N₂(g) → N(g) + N(g),ΔₐH⁰=946.0kj/mol','HCl(g) → H(g) + Cl(g),ΔₐH⁰=431.0kj/mol','H₂O(g) → H(g) + OH(g),ΔₐH⁰=502kj/mol','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41128,'chemistry','Bond strength is directly linked to?','Bond Enthalpy','It is important that larger the bond dissociation enthalpy, stronger will be the bond in the molecule. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'2','mcq','Bond length','Bond Enthalpy','Bond order','None of these','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41129,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: In case of polyatomic molecules,the measurement of bond strength is more complicated.\n\nR: Different bonds break with different energy demand as they lie in different chemical environment.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','OH of water and OH of ethanol both have different chemical environment so break with different energy demand. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 109; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41130,'chemistry','Mean or average bond energy is obtained by:','By dividing total bond dissociation enthalpy by the number of bonds broken','Polyatomic molecules the term mean or average bond enthalpy is used. \n\nIt is obtained by dividing total bond dissociation enthalpy by the number of bonds broken.\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 109; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','By dividing total bond dissociation enthalpy by the number of bonds broken','By adding total bond dissociation enthalpy with the number of bonds broken','By subtracting total bond dissociation enthalpy from the number of bonds broken','None of these','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41131,'chemistry','Average bond enthalpy of Water is:','464.5kj/mol-1','Average bond enthalpy of Water is 464.5kj/mol-1.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 109; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','464.5kj/mol-1','-464.5kj/mol','454.5kj/mol-1','434.5kj/mol','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41132,'chemistry','Number of bonds between two bonded atoms:','Bond order','In the Lewis description of covalent bond, the Bond Order is given by the number of bonds between the two atoms in a molecule. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 109; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'1','mcq','Bond length','Bond strength','Bond Energy','Bond order','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41133,'chemistry','Match the following:','Bond order of  H₂-One,Bond order of  O₂-Two,Bond order of  N₂-Three,Bond Energy of  N₂-946kj/mol','In the Lewis description of covalent bond, the Bond Order is given by the number of bonds between the two atoms in a molecule. \n\nThe bond order, for example in H2 (with a single shared electron pair), in O2 (with two shared electron pairs) and in N2 (with three shared electron pairs) is 1,2,3 respectively. \n\nSimilarly in CO (three shared electron pairs between C and O) the bond order is 3. For N2, bond order is 3 and it is 946 kJ mol–1; being one of the highest for a diatomic molecule.\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 109; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'3','match','Bond order of  H₂,One','Bond order of  O₂,Two','Bond order of  N₂,Three','Bond Energy of  N₂,946kj/mol-1','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41134,'chemistry','Which one having same bond order:\n\na. NO⁺\nb. CO\nc. N₂\nd. Acetylene','All have same bond order','N₂, NO⁺, CO and acetylene all have 28 electrons so isoelectronic, will have same bond order. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 109; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'3','mcq','Only a','Only b and c','Only c and d','All have same bond order','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41135,'chemistry','Stability of molecules is directly linked to:','Bond order','Stability of molecules is directly linked to bond order. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 109; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'3','mcq','Bond order','Bond length','Both a and b','None of these','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41136,'chemistry','Bond length is inversely linked to:','Bond order','Bond length is inversely linked to bond order. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 109; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'3','mcq','Bond strength','Bond order','Both a and b','None of these','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41137,'chemistry','Greater the bond strength smaller will be the bond length and greater will be:','Bond order','Greater the bond strength smaller will be the bond length and greater will be the bond order. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 109; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'3','mcq','Bond order','Bond energy','Both a and b','None of these','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41138,'chemistry','The bond enthalpy of O₂ is:\n\nO₂ (g) →  O(g) + O(g)','498 kJ mol–1','The bond enthalpy for molecules containing multiple bonds, for example O₂ and N₂ will be as under: \n\nO₂ (O = O) (g) →  O(g) + O(g); ΔₐH⁰ = 498 kJ mol–1 \n\nN₂ (N º N) (g) →  N(g) + N(g); ΔₐH⁰ = 946.0 kJ mol–1. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'3','mcq','4.98 kJ mol–1','498 kJ mol–1','49.8 kJ mol–1','4.98 kJ mol','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41139,'chemistry','The bond enthalpy of N₂ is:\n\nN₂ (g) →  N(g) + N(g)','946.0 kJ mol–1','The bond enthalpy for molecules containing multiple bonds, for example O₂ and N₂ will be as under : \n\nO₂ (O = O) (g) →  O(g) + O(g); ΔₐH⁰ = 498 kJ mol–1 \n\nN₂ (N º N) (g) →  N(g) + N(g); ΔₐH⁰ = 946.0 kJ mol–1. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'3','mcq','9.46 kJ mol–1','904 kJ mol–1','946.0 kJ mol–1','906 kJ mol–1','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41140,'chemistry','The decreasing order of bond dissociation enthalpy of halogens is:','Cl₂>Br₂>F₂>I₂','As bond length increases, bond dissociation enthalpy decreases. Thus, bond dissociation enthalpy decreases from F₂ to I₂. \n\nBut the bond dissociation enthalpy of F₂ is lower than Cl₂ and Br₂. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 109; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'3','order','1,Cl₂','2,Br₂','3,F₂','4,I₂','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41141,'chemistry','The bond enthalpy of HCl is:\n\nHCl (g) → H(g) + Cl (g)','431 kJ mol–1','Larger the bond dissociation enthalpy, stronger will be the bond in the molecule. \n\nFor a heteronuclear diatomic molecules like HCl, we have; \n\nHCl (g) → H(g) + Cl (g) ΔₐH⁰ = 431.0 kJ mol–1. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'3','mcq','4.31 kJ mol–1','431 kJ mol–1','43.1 kJ mol–1','4.31 kJ mol','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41142,'chemistry','The bond enthalpy of OH is:\n\nOH(g) → H(g) + O(g)','427 kJ mol–1','In case of polyatomic molecules, the measurement of bond strength is more complicated. \n\nWe have; OH(g) → H(g) + O(g) ΔₐH⁰ =427 kJ mol–1. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'3','mcq','427 kJ mol–1','431 kJ mol–1','479 kJ mol–1','436 kJ mol','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41143,'chemistry','The bond enthalpy of H₂O is:\n\nH₂O(g) → H(g) + OH(g)','502 kJ mol–1','In case of polyatomic molecules, the measurement of bond strength is more complicated. \n\nWe have; H₂O(g) → H(g) + OH(g) ΔₐH⁰ =502 kJ mol–1. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'3','mcq','493 kJ mol–1','431 kJ mol–1','559 kJ mol–1','502 kJ mol–1','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41144,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: It is important that larger the bond dissociation enthalpy, stronger will be the bond in the molecule.\n\nR: The aforementioned assertion is followed by a heteronuclear diatomic molecules like HCl.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','It is important that larger the bond dissociation enthalpy, stronger will be the bond in the molecule. \n\nFor a heteronuclear diatomic molecules like HCl. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41145,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: In case of polyatomic molecules, the measurement of bond strength is even less complicated.\n\nR: In case of H₂O molecule, the enthalpy needed to break the two O – H bonds is not the same.','Assertion is false but the reason is true','In case of polyatomic molecules, the measurement of bond strength is more complicated. \n\nFor example in case of H₂O molecule, the enthalpy needed to break the two O – H bonds is not the same. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 108; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41146,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: The difference in the ΔₐH⁰ value shows that the reactant (O – H bond) undergoes some change because of changed chemical environment.\n\nR: This is for some difference in energy of the same O – H bond in different molecules like C₂H₅OH (ethanol) and water.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','The difference in the ΔₐH⁰ value shows that the reactant undergoes some change because of changed chemical environment. \n\nThis is the reason for some difference in energy of the same O – H bond in different molecules like C₂H₅OH (ethanol) and water. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 109; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41147,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: F2 and O2 ²–  both have bond order 1.\n\nR: Atoms, ions, or molecules which have an equal number of valence electrons and the same atom connectivity are known as isoelectronic.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Isoelectronic molecules and ions have identical bond orders; for example, F₂ and O₂ ²⁻ have bond order 1. \n\nN₂, CO and NO+ have bond order 3. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 109; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41148,'chemistry','Match the following:','CO-N₂, NH₃-PH₃, Na-Si4+, Be²+,Li+','Isoelectronic molecules and ions have identical bond orders; for example, F₂ and O₂ ²⁻ have bond order 1. \n\nN₂, CO and NO+ have bond order 3. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 109; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'2','Match','CO,N₂','NH₃,PH₃','Na,Si4+','Be²+,Li+','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41149,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statement regarding isoelectronic species/molecules:\n\na. Cl and mg²⁺  ions are isoelectronic.\n\nb. In order to be isoelectronic, Be²⁺, Li⁺, and Ne would have to have the same number of electrons.\n\nc. Na⁺ is isoelectronic with Li⁺. Both have 10 number of electrons.','Only a and c','Na⁺ is isoelectronic with Mg²⁺. Both have 10 number of electrons. \n\nAtoms and ions that have the same electron configuration are said to be isoelectronic.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 109; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Bond parameters',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and b','Only a and c','Only b and c','All of these','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41150,'biology','Consider following statement regarding Echinoderms and choose the correct option\n\n1. Exoskeleton is made up of CaCo3 which provides protection against dessication \n\n2. They have distinct larva and adult which are radially and bilaterally symmetrical respectively\n\n3. Well developed canal system, lines the body and uses same opening for water inlet and outlet. \n\n4. All echinoderms have mouth which is present ventrally but anus is located dorsally','only 4 is correct','Echinoderms have well developed endoskeleton. Exoskeleton is present outside of the body while endoskeleton present inside the body such as in human. Their endoskeleton is made up of calcium carbonate. Adults of echinoderms have radial symmetry while their larval forms exhibits bilateral form. Canal system only exists in sponges while echinoderms have water vascular system that has an opening called medroporite for water inlet and outlet. They have mouth present ventrally and anus is located on dorsal side(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 11; Page 54; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','only 1 is correct','1 and 2 both are correct','2 and 3 both are correct','only 4 is correct','NEW','11-054','11-045',NULL),
(41151,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason.\n\nAssertion: In hemichordates, excretion is done by trunk region \n\nReason: Protonephridia are the major excretory organ having an opening called nephrostome for removal of wastes','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','Hemichordates have three body regions i.e. proboscis region, neck and trunk and excretion is done by proboscis glands that are present in probosics region While metanephridia are excretory organs which have a ciliated funnel nephrostome that collects wastes from cells and pulls into metanephridia (Reference  : NCERT Biology class 11; Page 54; Paragraph No. 4)\nA survey of animal kingdom presents a variety of excretory structures. In most of the invertebrates, these structures are simple tubular forms whereas vertebrates have complex tubular organs called kidneys. Some of these structures are mentioned here. Protonephridia or flame cells are the excretory structures in Platyhelminthes (Flatworms, e.g., Planaria), rotifers, some annelids and the cephalochordate – Amphioxus. Protonephridia are primarily concerned with ionic and fluid volume regulation, i.e., osmoregulation.','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-054','11-045',NULL),
(41152,'biology','Consider following statement regarding chordates and choose the correct option\n\n1. Lungs of all chordates produce hormones helpful in respiration.\n\n2. Ventral tubular nerve cord develops after birth\n\n3. They have pharyngeal slits, notochord and ventral heart\n\n4. Heart in four chambered with six types of vessels','only 3 is correct','Animals belonging to the phylum Chordata have lungs but lungs do not produce any kind of hormones that helps in respiration. Chordates are fundamentally characterised by the presence of a notochord, a dorsal hollow nerve cord and paired pharyngeal gill slits. They have a heart located on the ventral side of the body which is four-chambered only in birds and mammals while all other chordates have variations in heart structure having two and three chambers in amphibians and reptiles respectively. (Reference  : NCERT Biology class 11; Page 55; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','only 1 is correct','1 and 2 both are correct','only 3 is correct','3 and 4 both are correct','NEW','11-055','11-046',NULL),
(41153,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason.\n\nAssertion: Poison stings are located dorsally on mouth in bony fishes such as Trygon \n\nReason: Clasper is a poison sting present on pelvic fins','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','Only the members of class Chondrichthyes have poison stings that are present in the tail. Trygon is a cartilaginous fish with poison stings. Clasper is a male copulatory organ that is present within pelvic fins(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 11; Page 57; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-057','11-048',NULL),
(41154,'biology','Consider following statement regarding cyclostomata and choose the correct option\n\n1. Electrical organ is present in them which can detect or transmit electric current\n\n2. Respiration is performed by 6-8 gill slits which are present within mouth\n\n3. They are devoid of bony vertebra and mouth without jaws\n\n4. Their development is indirect and larva after metamorphosis moves to river','only 3 is correct','Members of class Cyclostomata do not have an electric organ. It is usually present in cartilaginous fishes. Respiration is formed by gill slits which are in the form of 6-15 pairs and present all over the body. Their vertebral column is made up of cartilage instead of bones and circular mouth without jaws. Their larvae, after metamorphosis, return to the ocean instead of river where it survives as adult. (Reference  : NCERT Biology class 11; Page 56; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','only 1 is correct','1 and 3 both are correct','only 3 is correct','2 and 4 both are correct','NEW','11-057','11-048',NULL),
(41155,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason.\n\nAssertion: All sharks have an operculum which is the structural modification of their limbs\n\nReason: Common opening of digestive system and reproductive system in fishes is termed as operculum','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','Sharks are the members of class Chondricthyes which are cartilaginous fishes and do not have an operculum which is gill covering. Operculum opens and allows the water to move over the gills. Fishes do not have a common opening for digestive and reproductive system. it is present in amphibians that is termed as cloaca. (Reference  : NCERT Biology class 11; Page 56; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-056','11-047',NULL),
(41404,'chemistry','Identify the correct order of Lone pair(lp) and bond pair(bp) repulsion:','lp-lp>lp-bp>bp-bp','lp-lp repulsion are maximum as lone pairs occupy large space as compared bond pairs because lone pairs are under the hold of only one nucleus while bond pairs lie between two nuclei, more space more will be repulsion. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 113; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'2','mcq','lp-lp>lp-bp>bp-bp','lp-lp<lp-bp<bp-bp','lp-lp>lp-bp=bp-bp','lp-lp<lp-bp>bp-bp','NEW','11-112','11-112',NULL),
(41156,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason.\n\nAssertion: All fishes are cold-blooded (poikilothermous) animals such as shark \n\nReason: Animals such as poikilotherms need to feed continously.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','All fishes are cold-blooded animals so they are termed as poikilothermous. They do not have the ability to regulate their body temperature and they do not need to feed continously. However, they need to maintain their body temperature (Reference  : NCERT Biology class 11; Page 57; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-057','11-048',NULL),
(41157,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding class amphibia and choose the correct option.\n \n1. They have lidless eyes which have rectal glands to secrete salt\n\n2. Skeleton has long bones which are hollow and have air cavities \n\n3. Placoid scales are present on the skin of amphibians\n\n4. They are oviparous and have indirect development','only 4 is correct','The eyes of the amphibians have eyelids that cover the eye and provide production but did not secrete salts because rectal glands are present in cartilaginous fishes. Moreover, the eyes of fishes are lidless. Long hollow bones with air cavities are present only in birds because they have to fly so they reduce their body weight. No scales are present on the skin of amphibians. They have moist skin due to the presence of glandular secretions. They lay eggs development includes larval stages (Reference  : NCERT Biology class 11; Page 57; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','only 1 is correct','1 and 2 both are correct','2 and 3 both are correct','only 4 is correct','NEW','11-057','11-048',NULL),
(41158,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding class reptilia and choose the incorrect statements.\n\n1. Heart is four chambered in all crocodiles\n\n2. External ear is present in the form of tympanum\n\n3. Fertilization takes place within body of reptiles\n\n4. Viviparity is found only in lizards','2 and 4 both are incorrect','Heart is usually three chambered in all reptiles except crocodiles in which ventricle is completely separated into right and left halves. There is no external ear in reptiles. Tympanic membrane acts as ear and fertlization is internal which is inside the body of female. Viviparous are those animals which give birth to their offsprings and this quality is found mostly in mammals while the reptiles are egg laying animals  (Reference  : NCERT Biology class 11; Page 58; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','only 1 is incorrect','1 and 2 both are incorrect','only 3 is incorrect','2 and 4 both are incorrect','NEW','11-058','11-049',NULL),
(41159,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason.\n\nAssertion: The body of aquatic reptiles is covered with Placoid scales and mucoid secretions\n\nReason: Reptiles such as tuatara use blubber to respire in water','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','Reptiles are mostly terrestrial in nature. Their skin is dry without any glands so they do not have mucoid secretions on their body as well as placoid scales are present in fishes. Reptiles have keeled and granular types of scales. Blubber is a type of fat present in marine mammals and helps in thermoregulation (Reference  : NCERT Biology class 11; Page 58; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-058','11-049',NULL),
(41160,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason.\n\nAssertion: Forelimbs of birds are modified into wings.\n\nReason: Birds are the only one for being able to fly in animal kingdom','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','In birds, forelimbs are modified into wings while the feathers are made up a protein named as keratin and helps in flight as well as thermoregulation. Birds are not the only one to fly in animal kingdom because insects have excellent flying ability which belongs to class hexapoda and phylum Arthropoda. (Reference  : NCERT Biology class 11; Page 58; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-058','11-049',NULL),
(41161,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- There is a need for classification of animals.\n\nReason- Over a billion species of animals have been described till now.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','As over a million species of animals have been described till now, the need for classification becomes all the more important. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 46; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-046','11-037',NULL),
(41162,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Multicellular eukaryotic holozoic organisms are called Metazoans. \n\nReason- Metazoans belong to Animal kingdom.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Multicellular eukaryotic holozoic organisms are called Metazoans. Metazoans belong to Animal kingdom.','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-046','11-037',NULL),
(41163,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Metazoans can be divided into parazoa and eumatazoa. \n\nReason- Parazoa has true tissues and developed organs.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Metazoans can be divided into parazoa and eumatazoa. Parazoa has no tissues or developed organs, it is cellular.','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-046','11-037',NULL),
(41164,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Parazoa includes animals higher than poriferans. \n\nReason- Eumetazoa includes sponges.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Parazoa includes Sponges. Eumetazoa includes animals above Sponges.','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-046','11-037',NULL),
(41165,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- All members of Animalia are multicellular.\n\nReason- All animals exhibit the same pattern of organisation of cells.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Though all members of Animalia are multicellular, all of them do not exhibit the same pattern of organisation of cells. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 46; Para 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-046','11-037',NULL),
(41166,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Sponges exhibit cellular level of organisation.\n\nReason- In sponges, the cells are arranged as loose cell aggregates.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','In sponges, the cells are arranged as loose cell aggregates, i.e., they exhibit cellular level of organisation. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 46; Para 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-046','11-037',NULL),
(41167,'biology','Consider these statements and choose the correct option.\n\n1. In Coelenterates, the cells performing the same function are arranged into tissues.\n\n2. In animals like Annelids, Arthropods, Molluscs, cells are arranged as loose cell aggregates.\n\n3. In Platyhelminthes and other higher phyla, tissues are grouped together to form organs.\n\n4. In Echinoderms and Chordates, organs have associated to form functional systems.','Only 2 is incorrect','In coelenterates, the arrangement of cells is more complex. Here the cells performing the same function are arranged into tissues, hence is called tissue level of organisation. A still higher level of organisation, i.e., organ level is exhibited by members of Platyhelminthes and other higher phyla where tissues are grouped together to form organs, each specialised for a particular function. In animals like Annelids, Arthropods, Molluscs, Echinoderms and Chordates, organs have\nassociated to form functional systems, each system concerned with a specific physiological function. This pattern is called organ system level of organisation. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 46; Para 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Only 3 is incorrect','Only 1 is correct','Only 2 is incorrect','NEW','11-046','11-037',NULL),
(41168,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Digestive system of Platyhelminthes is called incomplete.\n\nReason- Digestive system in Platyhelminthes has only a single opening to the outside of the body that serves as both mouth and anus.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The digestive system in Platyhelminthes has only a single opening to the outside of the body that serves as both mouth and anus, and is hence called incomplete. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 47; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-047','11-038',NULL),
(41169,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- A complete digestive system has two openings, mouth and anus. \n\nReason- Organ systems in different groups of animals exhibit same patterns of complexities.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Organ systems in different groups of animals exhibit various patterns of complexities. A complete digestive system has two openings, mouth and anus. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 47; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-047','11-038',NULL),
(41170,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- In open types of circulation, the blood is pumped out of the heart and the cells and tissues are directly bathed in it.\n\nReason- In closed type of circulation, the blood is circulated through a series of vessels of same diameters.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','The circulatory system may be of two types: (i) open type in which the blood is pumped out of the heart and the cells and tissues are directly bathed in it and\n(ii) closed type in which the blood is circulated through a series of vessels of varying diameters (arteries, veins and capillaries). (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 47; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-047','11-038',NULL),
(31213,'chemistry','Assertion: In OF₂ molecule oxygen exhibits -2 oxidation state.\n\nReason: Fluorine being the electropositive element is assigend with +1 oxidation state.','Both assertion and reason are false.','Each of the atoms of fluorine, with outer electronic configuration 2s²2p⁵, shares one electron with oxygen in the OF molecule. \n\nBeing highest electronegative element, fluorine is given oxidation state –1. So the oxidation state of O will be +1.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 92; Paragraph no. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Oxidation states',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are false.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','NEW','11-092','11-092',NULL),
(41171,'biology','Consider these statements and choose the correct option.\n\n1. Sponges are mostly symmetrical.\n\n2. When any plane passing through the central axis of the body divides the organism into two identical halves, it is called bilateral symmetry.\n\n3. Symmetry in which the body can be divided into identical left and right halves in only one plane, exhibit radial symmetry.\n\n4. Annelids possess radial symmetry.','All the statements are incorrect.','Animals can be categorised on the basis of their symmetry. Sponges are mostly asymmetrical, i.e., any plane that passes through the centre does not divide them into equal halves. When any plane passing through the central axis of the body divides the organism into two identical halves, it is called radial symmetry.\nCoelenterates, ctenophores and echinoderms have this kind of body plan. Animals like annelids, arthropods, etc., where the body can be divided into identical left and right halves in only one plane, exhibit bilateral symmetry.','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','Only 3 is correct','All the statements are incorrect.','NEW','11-047','11-038',NULL),
(41172,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- In asymmetrical symmetry, any plane that passes through the centre does not divide the body into equal halves.\n\nReason- Coelenterates, ctenophores and echinoderms have this kind of body plan.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Sponges are mostly asymmetrical, i.e., any plane that passes through the centre does not divide them into equal halves. When any plane passing through the central axis of the body divides the organism into two identical halves, it is called radial symmetry. Coelenterates, ctenophores and echinoderms have this kind of body plan. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 47; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-047','11-038',NULL),
(41173,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Animals in which the cells are arranged in two embryonic layers, are called diploblastic.\n\nReason- In triploblastic animals, mesoglea is present in between the ectoderm and the endoderm.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Animals in which the cells are arranged in two embryonic layers, an external ectoderm and an internal endoderm, are called diploblastic animals, e.g., coelenterates. An undifferentiated layer, mesoglea, is present in between the ectoderm and the endoderm. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 47; Para 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-047','11-038',NULL),
(41174,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Mesoglea is a differentiated layer present in diploblastic animals. \n\nReason- Ctenophora is a triploblastic animal.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Triploblastic animals are Platyhelminthes to Chordates. An undifferentiated layer, mesoglea, is present in between the ectoderm and the endoderm. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 47; Para 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-047','11-038',NULL),
(41175,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- The body cavity, which is lined by mesoderm is called coelom. \n\nReason- Aschelminthes, annelids, molluscs, arthropods, echinoderms, hemichordates and chordates are coelomates.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','The body cavity, which is lined by mesoderm is called coelom. Animals possessing coelom are called coelomates, e.g.,annelids, molluscs, arthropods, echinoderms, hemichordates and chordates. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 48; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-048','11-039',NULL),
(41176,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- When the body cavity is not lined by mesoderm, instead, the mesoderm is present as scattered pouches in between the ectoderm and endoderm is called pseudocoelom. \n\nReason- The the body cavity is absent in aschelminthes.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','The animals in which the body cavity is absent are called acoelomates, e.g., platyhelminthes. In some animals, the body cavity is not lined by mesoderm, instead, the mesoderm is present as scattered pouches in between the ectoderm and endoderm. Such a body cavity is called pseudocoelom and the animals possessing them are called pseudocoelomates, e.g., aschelminthes. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 48; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-048','11-039',NULL),
(41177,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- In metamerism, the body is only externally divided into segments with a serial repetition of at least some organs. \n\nReason- Roundworms show metameric segmentation.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','In some animals, the body is externally and internally divided into segments with a serial repetition of at least some organs. For example, in earthworm, the body shows this pattern called metameric segmentation and the phenomenon is known as metamerism. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 48; Para 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-048','11-039',NULL),
(41178,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Sponges have blind-sac body plan.\n\nReason- The blind sac body plan is the body plan where the there are two openings of the digestive system.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Sponges have cell-aggregrate body plan. The blind sac body plan is the body plan where the there is a single opening of the digestive system.','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-049','11-040',NULL),
(41179,'biology','Consider these statements and choose the correct option.\n\n1. Notochord is a endodermally derived rod-like structure.\n\n2. Notochord is present in chordates.\n\n3. Animals with notochord are called chordates.\n\n4. Echinoderms are non-chordates.','Only 1 is incorrect','Notochord is a mesodermally derived rod-like structure formed on the dorsal side during embryonic development in some animals. Animals with notochord are called chordates and those animals which do not form this structure are called non-chordates, e.g., porifera to echinoderms. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 48; Para 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','Only 3 is correct','All the statements are correct.','NEW','11-055','11-046',NULL),
(41180,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Deuterostomia are animals characterized by their anus forming before their mouth during embryonic development.\n\nReason- Chordates are deutrostomes.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Deuterostomia are animals characterized by their anus forming before their mouth during embryonic development. Chordates are deutrostomes.','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-055','11-046',NULL),
(41181,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- In protostomes, the first opening in the embryo becomes the mouth.\n\nReason- Tube-within-tube body plan is generally found in higher animal groups from nematodes to chordates.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','In protostomes, the first opening in the embryo becomes the mouth. A Tube-within-tube body plan is generally found in higher animal groups from nematodes to chordates.','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-049','11-040',NULL),
(41182,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- In echinoderms, larval forms possess bilateral symmetry while adults have radial symmetry.\n\nReason- Echinoderms show retrogressive metamorphosis.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Echinodermata exhibits radial or bilateral symmetry depending on the stage. In echinoderms, larval forms possess bilateral symmetry while adults have radial symmetry and show retrogressive metamorphosis.','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-054','11-047',NULL),
(41183,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Members of Porifera phylum are commonly known as sponges. \n\nReason- Members of Porifera are filter- feeders.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Members of Porifera are filter- feeders. Members of this phylum are commonly known as sponges. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 49; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-049','11-040',NULL),
(41184,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Sponges are primitive multicellular animals and have cellular level of organisation. \n\nReason- Sponges have a water vascular or canal system.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Sponges have a water transport or canal system. These are primitive multicellular animals and have cellular level of organisation. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 49; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-049','11-040',NULL),
(41185,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Choanocytes or collar cells line the spongocoel and the canals.\n\nReason- Digestion is extracellular.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Digestion is intracellular in sponges. Choanocytes or collar cells line the spongocoel and the canals. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 49; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-049','11-040',NULL),
(41186,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Choanocytes differentiate sex-cells in sponges.\n\nReason- Sponges reproduce asexually by fragmentation.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Choanocytes differentiate sex-cells in sponges. Sponges reproduce asexually by fragmentation. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 49; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-049','11-040',NULL),
(41187,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- The body in sponges is supported by a skeleton made up of spicules or spongin fibres. \n\nReason- Spicules can be calcareous or siliceous.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The body in sponges is supported by a skeleton made up of spicules or spongin fibres. They are made of calcium and silica. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 49; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-049','11-040',NULL),
(41188,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Sponges and choose the correct option.\n\n1. Water enters through minute pores (ostia) in the body.\n\n2. Water enters in the sponges into a central cavity, spongocoel.\n\n3. Water goes out through the ostia. \n\n4. Pathway of water transport is helpful in food gathering, respiratory exchange and removal of waste.','Only 3 is incorrect','Water enters through minute pores (ostia) in the body wall into a central cavity, spongocoel, from where it goes out through the osculum. This pathway of water transport is helpful in food gathering, respiratory exchange and removal of waste. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 49; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Only 3 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','Only 1 is incorrect','All the statements are correct.','NEW','11-049','11-040',NULL),
(41189,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Spongilla is a freshwater sponge.\n\nReason- Sponges are hermaphrodite.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Spongilla (Fresh water sponge). Sexes are not separate (hermaphrodite), i.e., eggs and sperms are produced by the same individual in sponges. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 49; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-049','11-040',NULL),
(41190,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Sponges have a larval stage which is morphologically same to the adult.\n\nReason- Fertilisation is external in sponges.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Fertilisation is internal and development is indirect having a larval stage which is morphologically distinct from the adult. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 49; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-050','11-041',NULL),
(41191,'biology','Consider these statements and choose the correct option.\n\n1. Sycon is a bath sponge.\n\n2. Poriferans are mostly freshwater.\n\n3. Sponges reproduces asexually by forming gemmules.\n\n4. Collar cells help in the ingestion of food in Sponges.','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Euspongia is called as bath sponge. Sponges are generally marine. Gemmules are internal buds in sponges which help them in asexual reproduction. Collar cells help in ingestion of food in sponges. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 50; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Only 4 is correct','Only 2 is correct','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Only 3 is correct','NEW','11-050','11-041',NULL),
(41192,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Cnidarians are aquatic, mostly freshwater, sessile or free-swimming, radially symmetrical animals.\n\nReason- Physalia is also known as Sea Pen.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Physalia is also called as portuguese man-of-war. They are aquatic, mostly marine, sessile or free-swimming, radially symmetrical animals. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 50; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-050','11-041',NULL),
(41193,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- The name cnidaria is derived from the cnidoblasts or cnidocytes.\n\nReason- Cnidoblasts contain the stinging capsules or nematocysts.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The name cnidaria is derived from the cnidoblasts or cnidocytes (which contain the stinging capsules or nematocysts) present on the tentacles and the body. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 50; Para 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-050','11-041',NULL),
(41194,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Cnidarians exhibit tissue level of organisation and are triploblastic. \n\nReason- Cnidoblasts are used for anchorage, defense and for the capture of food.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Cnidoblasts are used for anchorage, defense and for the capture of prey. Cnidarians exhibit tissue level of organisation and are diploblastic. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 50; Para 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-050','11-041',NULL),
(41195,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Cnidarians have a central gastro-vascular cavity with a single opening.\n\nReason- Cnidarians have anus on hypostome.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','They have a central gastro-vascular cavity with a single opening, mouth on hypostome. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 50; Para 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-050','11-041',NULL),
(41196,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Digestion in Cnidarians is first intracellular and then extracellular.\n\nReason- Corals have a skeleton composed of calcium sulphate.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Digestion is first extracellular and intracellular then. Corals have a skeleton composed of calcium carbonate. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 50; Para 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-050','11-041',NULL),
(41197,'biology','Consider these statements and choose the correct option.\n\n1. Adamsia is also known as Sea anemone.\n\n2. Gorgonia can also be called as Sea-fan.\n\n3. Meandrina is the Brain coral.\n\n4. Pennatula can also be called as Sea-pen.','All the statements are correct.','Physalia -Portuguese man-of-war\nAdamsia -Sea anemone \nPennatula -Sea-pen \nGorgonia -Sea-fan \nMeandrina -Brain coral (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 50; Para 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Only 4 is correct','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','All the statements are correct.','NEW','11-050','11-041',NULL),
(41198,'biology','Consider these statements and choose the correct option.\n\n1. Cnidarians exhibit two basic body forms called polyp and medusa.\n\n2. Medusa is a sessile and cylindrical form like Hydra.\n\n3. Polyp is umbrella-shaped and free-swimming like Aurelia\n\n4. Aurelia is also known as the jelly fish.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Cnidarians exhibit two basic body forms called polyp and medusa. The former is a sessile and cylindrical form like Hydra, Adamsia, etc. whereas, the latter is umbrella-shaped and free-swimming like Aurelia or jelly fish. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 50; Para 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','NEW','11-050','11-041',NULL),
(41199,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Cnidarians which exist in both forms exhibit alternation of generation Metagenesis.\n\nReason- Polyps produce medusae sexually and medusae form the polyps asexually.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Those cnidarians which exist in both forms exhibit alternation of generation (Metagenesis), i.e., polyps produce medusae asexually and medusae form the polyps sexually (e.g., Obelia).','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-050','11-041',NULL),
(41201,'chemistry','The canonical forms of Ozone are:','Two','The ozone molecule can be equally represented by the structures I and II. \n\nAs shown in diagram  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 109; Paragraph 5) diagram(ne0406)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Resonance structures',NULL,'1','mcq','Three','Two','One','Four','NEW','11-109','11-109',NULL),
(41202,'chemistry','The O-O bond lengths in ozone is:','128pm','The normal O-O and O=O  bond lengths are 148pm and 121pm respectively but in ozone it is experimentally calculated that O-O bond length in ozone is 128pm which is intermediate value between O-O and O=O bond lengths showing that ozone have both single and double bond character. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 109; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Resonance structures',NULL,'1','mcq','128pm','148pm','121pm','120pm','NEW','11-109','11-109',NULL),
(41203,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nA: A single lewis structure cannot describe a molecule accurately.\n\nR: A number of structures with similar energy, positions of nuclei, bonding and non bonding pairs of electrons are necassary to describe a molecule accurately.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','According to the concept of resonance, whenever a single Lewis structure cannot describe a molecule accurately, a number of structures with similar energy, positions of nuclei, bonding and non-bonding pairs of electrons are taken as the canonical structures of the hybrid which describes the molecule accurately. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 109; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Resonance structures',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-109','11-109',NULL),
(41204,'chemistry','The lowest energy canonical form of a molecule is called as?','Resonance hybrid','The III structure represents the structure of O₃ more accurately. This is also called resonance hybrid. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 109; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Resonance structures',NULL,'1','mcq','Resonance hybrid','Resonance form','Resonance energy','None of these','NEW','11-109','11-109',NULL),
(41205,'chemistry','How many Resonance forms CO₃²⁻ have?','Three','The single lewis structure based on the presence of two single bonds and one double bond between C and O atoms is inadequate to re- presents equal bonds.\n\nAccording to the experimental findings all C-O bonds are equivalent this indicate that CO₃²⁻ have negative charge delocalize over three oxygen exibiting resonance. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 110; Problem no.4.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Resonance structures',NULL,'2','mcq','Three','Two','One','Four','NEW','11-110','11-110',NULL),
(41206,'chemistry','The structure of CO₂ have:','Both a and b','The experimentally determined C-O bond length in CO₂ is 115 pm. \n\nThe lengths of normal C=O double bond is 121pm and carbon to Oxygen triple bond is 110pm so the C=O in CO₂ lies between the values for normal C=O and C to O triple bond showing that CO₂ is actualy resonance hybrid between double and triple bond that\'s why showing both character. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 110; Problem no.4.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Resonance structures',NULL,'2','mcq','Double bond character','Triple bond Character','Both a and b','Single bond Character','NEW','11-110','11-110',NULL),
(41207,'chemistry','How many Resonance forms CO₂ have?','Three','Lewis structures and to consider that the structure of CO₂ is best described as a hybrid of the canonical or resonance forms I, II and III. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 110; Problem no.4.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Resonance structures',NULL,'2','mcq','Three','Two','One','Four','NEW','11-110','11-110',NULL),
(41208,'chemistry','Which statement is correct about resonance:\n\na. Resonance stabilizes the molecule \n\nb. The energy of resonance hybrid is less than the any single canonical forms\n\nc. Resonace averages the bond chracteristics as whole','All statements are correct','Resonance stabilizes the molecule as the energy of the resonance hybrid is less than the energy of any single cannonical structure; and resonance averages the bond characteristics as a whole.\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 110; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Resonance structures',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c','All statements are correct','NEW','11-110','11-110',NULL),
(41209,'chemistry','Which one is not true about resonance:\n\na. The canonical forms have no real existance\n\nb. The molecule does not exist for a certain fraction of time in one cannonical form and for other fractions of time in other cannonical forms\n\nc. There is equilibrium between the cannonical forms\n\nd. The molecule have multiple structure which is resonance hybrid of all canonical forms','Only c and d','There is no such equilibrium between the cannonical forms. \n\nThe molecule have a single structure which is resonance hybrid of all canonical forms\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 110; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Resonance structures',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c and d','All statements are not true','NEW','11-110','11-110',NULL),
(41210,'chemistry','How many Resonance forms CO have?','Two','The carbon monoxide has two resonance structures limit. \n\nIn other words, the bond in this molecule can be described from two different configurations: In the first configuration, between C and O exists a double bond, and the two atoms have zero formal charge on it. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 110; Problem no.4.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Resonance structures',NULL,'2','mcq','Three','Two','One','Four','NEW','11-110','11-110',NULL),
(41211,'chemistry','How many chemical bond CCl₄ have?','Four','CCl₄, there are fewer carbon atoms than chlorine atoms, so the carbon atom goes into the middle of the molecule, with four chlorine atoms arranged around it. Among the carbon and each chlorine atom is a single chemical bond, resulted four.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 110; Problem no.4.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Resonance structures',NULL,'2','mcq','Three','Two','One','Four','NEW','11-110','11-110',NULL),
(41212,'chemistry','How many Resonance forms NO₃⁻ have?','Three','In the nitrate ion, the central nitrogen atom is sp² hybridized, and the three oxygen atoms are singly bonded to it with one of the oxygen atoms also having a double bond with nitrogen. The position of the double bond can be shifted to any of the three oxygen atoms.\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 110; Problem no.4.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Resonance structures',NULL,'2','mcq','Three','Two','One','Four','NEW','11-110','11-110',NULL),
(41213,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Aschelminthes are also known as roundworms.\n\nReason- The body of the aschelminthes is circular in longitudinal-section.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','The body of the aschelminthes is circular in cross-section, hence, the name roundworms. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 52; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(41214,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Roundworms are exclusively terrestrial and parasitic. \n\nReason- Roundworms have organ level of body organisation.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Roundworms have organ-system level of body organisation. They may be freeliving, aquatic and terrestrial or parasitic in plants and animals. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 52; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(41215,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Alimentary canal is complete with under-developed muscular pharynx in Aschelminthes.\n\nReason- An excretory tubule removes body wastes in roundworms.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Alimentary canal is complete with a well-developed muscular pharynx. An excretory tube removes body wastes from the body cavity through the excretory pore. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 52; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(41216,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Roundworms show sexual dimorphism.\n\nReason- Males and females are distinct and often females are longer than males.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Sexes are separate (dioecious), i.e., males and females are distinct. Often females are longer than males. Therefore they show sexual dimorphism. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 52; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(41217,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- In indirect development in roundworms, the young ones resemble the adult.\n\nReason- Elephantiasis is caused by Aedes mosquito.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Elephantiasis is transmitted by female Culex mosquito.  Fertilisation is internal and development may be direct (the young ones resemble the adult) or indirect. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 52; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(41218,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- In roundworms, development may be direct or indirect.\n\nReason- Ancylostoma is the Hookworm.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Ancylostoma (Hookworm). \nFertilisation is internal and development may be direct (the young ones resemble the adult) or indirect. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 52; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(41219,'biology','Consider these statements regarding roundworms and choose the correct option.\n\n1. They are pseudocoelomates.\n\n2. They are triploblastic.\n\n3. They are bilaterally symmetrical.\n\n4. Alimentary canal is incomplete.','Only 4 is incorrect.','They are bilaterally symmetrical, triploblastic and pseudocoelomate animals. Alimentary canal is complete. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 52; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are incorrect','Only 3 is correct','Only 4 is incorrect.','All the statements are correct.','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(41220,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Annelida is named so, as their body surface is distinctly marked out into segments or metameres.\n\nReason- In Greek, annulus means little ring.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Their body surface is distinctly marked out into segments or metameres and, hence, the phylum name Annelida (Latin, annulus : little ring). (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 52; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(41221,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Annelida possess transverse and circular muscles which help in locomotion. \n\nReason- Aquatic annelids like Nereis possess longitudinal appendages.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Annelids possess longitudinal and circular muscles which help in locomotion. Aquatic annelids like Nereis possess lateral appendages. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 52; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(41222,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Parapodia help in swimming in Nereis.\n\nReason- A closed circulatory system is present in Annelids.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Aquatic annelids like Nereis possess lateral appendages, parapodia, which help in swimming. A closed circulatory system is present. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 52; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(41223,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Nereis, an aquatic form, is monoecious.\n\nReason- Earthworms and leeches are dioecious.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Nereis, an aquatic form, is dioecious, but earthworms and leeches are monoecious. Reproduction is sexual. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 52; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(41224,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Annelida and choose the correct option.\n\n1. Nephridia help in osmoregulation and excretion. \n\n2. Neural system consists of single ganglia.\n\n3. Ganglia are connected by lateral nerves to a single ventral nerve cord. \n\n4. Hirudinaria is a blood sucking leech.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Nephridia (sing. nephridium) help in osmoregulation and excretion. Neural system consists of paired ganglia (sing. ganglion) connected by lateral nerves to a double ventral nerve cord. Hirudinaria (Blood sucking leech). (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 52; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Only 2 is incorrect','Only 3 is incorrect','Only 4 is correct','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(41225,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Annelida and choose the correct option.\n\n1. Pheretima is a earthworm.\n\n2. They are exclusively free-living.\n\n3. They are exclusively marine.\n\n4. They are triploblastic and metamerically segmented.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','They may be aquatic (marine and fresh water) or terrestrial; free-living, and sometimes parasitic. They exhibit organ-system level of body organisation and bilateral symmetry. Pheretima (Earthworm). (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 52; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Only 2 is incorrect','Only 3 is incorrect','Only 4 is correct','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','NEW','11-052','11-043',NULL),
(41226,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Arthropoda is the largest phylum of Animalia which includes insects. \n\nReason- Over three-fourths of all named species on earth are arthropods.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Arthropoda is the largest phylum of Animalia which includes insects. Over two-thirds of all named species on earth are arthropods. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 53; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(41227,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- The body of arthropods is covered by chitinous endoskeleton. \n\nReason- The body consists of head, neck and abdomen.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','The body of arthropods is covered by chitinous exoskeleton. The body consists of head, thorax and abdomen. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 53; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(41228,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Arthropods have jointed appendages.\n\nReason- Arthropods are mostly monoecious.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','They have jointed appendages (arthros-joint, poda-appendages). They are mostly dioecious. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 53; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(41229,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Statocysts are balancing organs that are present in Arthropods.\n\nReason- Respiratory organs are gills, book gills, book lungs or moist skin.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Respiratory organs are gills, book gills, book lungs or tracheal system. Statocysts are balancing organs. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 53; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(41230,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Excretion in Arthropods takes place through malpighian tubules. \n\nReason- Arthropods are mostly viviparous.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Excretion takes place through malpighian tubules. They are mostly oviparous. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 53; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(41231,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Development in Arthropods is always indirect.\n\nReason- Locusta is a living fossil.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Living fossil – Limulus (King crab).Development may be direct or indirect. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 53; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(41232,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Arthropda and choose the correct option.\n\n1. Laccifer is a regarious pest. \n\n2. Bombyx is the lac insect.\n\n3. Anopheles, Culex and Aedes are mosquitoes which act like parasites.\n\n4. Apis is a silkworm.','All the statements are incorrect.','Apis (Honey bee), Bombyx (Silkworm), Laccifer (Lac insect).Vectors – Anopheles, Culex and Aedes (Mosquitoes). Gregarious pest – Locusta (Locust). Living fossil – Limulus (King crab). (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 53; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Only 2 is incorrect','Statements 1, 3 and 4 are incorrect.','both 1 and 2 are incorrect','All the statements are incorrect.','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(41233,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Mollusca is the second largest animal phylum.\n\nReason- Molluscs are terrestrial or marine.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Mollusca is the second largest animal phylum. Molluscs are terrestrial or aquatic (marine or fresh water). (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 53; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(41234,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Molluscs are bilaterally symmetrical.\n\nReason- Torsion can cause some mollusc to develop asymmetry.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Due to torsion or twisting, bilateral symmetry of molluscs is lost. They are generally bilaterally symmetrical. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 53; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(41235,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Body in Molluscs is covered by a calcareous shell.\n\nReason- Molluscs are segmented with a distinct head, muscular foot and visceral hump.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Body is covered by a calcareous shell and is unsegmented with a distinct head, muscular foot and visceral hump. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 53; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(41236,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- The chest of molluscs contains a file-like rasping organ for feeding, called radula.\n\nReason-  The space between the mouth and the mantle is called the mantle cavity.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','The mouth contains a file-like rasping organ for feeding, called radula. The space between the hump and the mantle is called the mantle cavity. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 53; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(41237,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion-  A soft and spongy layer of skin forms a mantle over the visceral hump in Molluscs.\n\nReason- Feather like gills are present in the visceral hump.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','The space between the hump and the mantle is called the mantle cavity in which feather like gills are present. A soft and spongy layer of skin forms a mantle over the visceral hump. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 53; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(41238,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Feather like gills have only respiratory functions.\n\nReason- Molluscs are usually monoecious and oviparous with indirect development.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','They are usually dioecious and oviparous with indirect development. The space between the hump and the mantle is called the mantle cavity in which feather like gills are present. They have respiratory and excretory functions. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 53; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(41239,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Mollusca are spiny bodied.\n\nReason- Echinoderms have exoskeleton made of calcareous ossicles.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','These animals have an endoskeleton of calcareous ossicles and, hence, the name Echinodermata (Spiny bodied). (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 54; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-054','11-045',NULL),
(41240,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Echinodermata are both marine and fresh-water.\n\nReason- Digestive system is incomplete in starfish.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','All are marine. Digestive system is complete in Starfish. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 54; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-054','11-045',NULL),
(41241,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Mouth is present on the lower (ventral) side and anus on the upper (dorsal) side in starfishes.\n\nReason- Fertilisation is usually internal in Echinoderms.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Fertilisation is usually external in Echinoderms. Digestive system is complete with mouth on the lower (ventral) side and anus on the upper (dorsal) side. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 54; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-054','11-045',NULL),
(41242,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- An excretory system is absent in echinoderms.\n\nReason- Water vascular system of echinoderms helps in locomotion, capture and transport of food and respiration.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The most distinctive feature of echinoderms is the presence of water vascular system which helps in locomotion, capture and transport of food and respiration.\nAn excretory system is absent. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 54; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-054','11-045',NULL),
(41243,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Echinodermata or starfishes are hemaphrodites. \n\nReason- The larvae echinoderms are radially symmetrical but adults are bilaterally symmetrical.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','The adult echinoderms are radially symmetrical but larvae are bilaterally symmetrical. Sexes are separate. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 54; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-054','11-045',NULL),
(41244,'biology','Consider these statements reagarding Echinoderms and choose the correct option.\n\n1. Antedon is known as the brittle star.\n\n2. Cucumaria is the Sea cucumber.\n\n3. Common name of Ophiura is Sea Lily.\n\n4. Asterias is the Star fish and Echinus is Sea urchin.','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Examples: Asterias (Star fish), Echinus (Sea urchin), Antedon (Sea lily), Cucumaria (Sea cucumber) and Ophiura (Brittle star). (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 54; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Only 1 is correct','Only 2 is incorrect','NEW','11-054','11-045',NULL),
(41245,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion-  Hemichordata was earlier considered as a sub-phylum under phylum Chordata. \n\nReason- Hemichordates have a rudimentary structure in the collar region called stomochord.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Hemichordata was earlier considered as a sub-phylum under phylum Chordata. But now it is placed as a separate phylum under non-chordata. Hemichordates have a rudimentary structure in the collar region called stomochord. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 54; Para 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-054','11-045',NULL),
(41246,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- The body of hemichordates is conical and is composed of an anterior proboscis, a collar and a long trunk.\n\nReason- Circulatory system is of closed type in them.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','The body is cylindrical and is composed of an anterior proboscis, a collar and a long trunk. Circulatory system is of open type. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 54; Para 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-054','11-045',NULL),
(41247,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Excretory organ is proboscis gland in hemichordates.\n\nReason- Respiration takes place through collar in Balanoglossus','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Excretory organ is proboscis gland. Respiration takes place through gills. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 54; Para 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-054','11-045',NULL),
(41248,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Development is indirect in Saccoglossus. \n\nReason- Hemichordates are unisexual and fertilisation is external in them.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Development is indirect. Sexes are separate. Fertilisation is external. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 54; Para 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-054','11-045',NULL),
(41249,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Chordates and choose the correct option.\n\n1. They possess a post anal tail and a closed circulatory system.\n\n2. They have notochord and are bilaterally symmetrical, triploblastic, coelomate with organ-system level of organisation. \n\n3. Paired pharyngeal gill slits are absent in them.\n\n4. They have single hollow ventral nerve cord.','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Animals belonging to phylum Chordata are fundamentally characterised by the presence of a notochord, a dorsal hollow nerve cord and paired pharyngeal gill slits. These are bilaterally symmetrical, triploblastic, coelomate with organ-system level of organisation. They possess a post anal tail and a closed circulatory system. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 55; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Only 3 is correct','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Statements 1, 2 and 3 are correct.','NEW','11-055','11-046',NULL),
(41250,'biology','Consider these statements regarding non-chordates and choose the correct option.\n\n1. Central nervous system is ventral, solid and double.\n\n2. Post-anal tail is absent.\n\n3. Heart is dorsal (if present).\n\n4. Gill slits are present.','Only 4 is incorrect','Central nervous system is ventral, solid hollow and double. Post-anal tail is absent. Gill slits are absent. Heart is dorsal (if present). (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 55; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are correct','NEW','11-055','11-046',NULL),
(41251,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Phylum Chordata is divided into two subphyla: Urochordata and Cephalochordata.\n\nReason- Subphyla Urochordata and Cephalochordata are often referred to as Tunicates.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Phylum Chordata is divided into three subphyla: Urochordata or Tunicata, Cephalochordata and Vertebrata. Subphyla Urochordata and Cephalochordata are often referred to as protochordates. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 55; Para 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-055','11-046',NULL),
(41252,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Protochordates are exclusively marine.\n\nReason- The members of subphylum Vertebrata do not possess notochord during the embryonic period.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','The members of subphylum Vertebrata possess notochord during the embryonic period. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 55; Para 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-055','11-046',NULL),
(30434,'chemistry','What is the general outer electronic configuration of d-Block elements?','(n−1)d^(1−10)ns^(0−2)','The elements of Group 3 to 12 in the centre of the Periodic Table. \n\nThese are characterised by the filling of inner d orbitals by electrons and are therefore referred to as d-Block Elements. \n\nThese elements have the general outer electronic configuration (n-1)d1-10ns0-2. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 84; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'2','mcq','(n−1)d^(1−10)ns^(0−2)','(n−1)d^(1−8)ns^(0−2)','(n−1)d^(1−10)ns','(n+1)d^(1−10)ns^(0−2)','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(41253,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- In Cephalochordates, notochord is present only in larval tail.\n\nReason- In Urochordata, notochord extends from head to tail region and is persistent throughout their life.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','In Urochordata, notochord is present only in larval tail, while in Cephalochordata, it extends from head to tail region and is persistent throughout their life. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 55; Para 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-055','11-046',NULL),
(41254,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- All vertebrates are chordates but all chordates are not vertebrates.\n\nReason- The notochord is replaced by a cartilaginous or bony vertebral column in the adult.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The notochord is replaced by a cartilaginous or bony vertebral column in the adult. Thus all vertebrates are chordates but all chordates are not vertebrates. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 55; Para 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-055','11-046',NULL),
(41255,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Protochordates and choose the correct option.\n\n1. Branchiostoma can be both Amphioxus or Lancelet.\n\n2. Branchiostoma is a Tunicate.\n\n3. Doliolum is a vertebrate. \n\n4. Ascidia and Salpa are Urochordates.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect.','Examples: Urochordata – Ascidia, Salpa, Doliolum; Cephalochordata – Branchiostoma(Amphioxus or Lancelet). (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 55; Para 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect.','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','NEW','11-055','11-046',NULL),
(41256,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Vertebrates and choose the correct option.\n\n1. Kidneys are present for excretion and osmoregulation.\n\n2. Paired appendages are present which may be fins or limbs.\n\n3. Vertebrates have a ventral muscular heart with two, three or four chambers.\n\n4. They are divided into super class Agnatha and Gnathostomata.','Only 4 is incorrect','The subphylum Vertebrata is further divided into divisions Agnatha (lacks jaw) and Gnathostomata (bears jaw). Besides the basic chordate characters, vertebrates have a ventral muscular heart with two, three or four chambers, kidneys for excretion and osmoregulation and paired appendages which may be fins or limbs. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 56; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','All the statements are correct','NEW','11-056','11-047',NULL),
(41257,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Tetrapods bear limbs.\n\nReason- Tetrapoda is divided into classes Amphibia, Reptilia, Aves, Mammals.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Tetrapoda is divided into classes Amphibia, Reptilia, Aves, Mammals. Tetrapods bear limbs. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 56; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-056','11-047',NULL),
(41258,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Pisces is divided into Chondrichthyes, Osteichthyes and Cyclostomes.\n\nReason- Pisces can bear either limbs or fins.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Pisces is divided into Chondrichthyes and Osteichthyes and have only fins. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 56; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-056','11-047',NULL),
(41259,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- All living members of the class Cyclostomata are endoparasites on some fishes. \n\nReason- Cyclostomes live in fresh water but migrate for spawning to marine water.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','All living members of the class Cyclostomata are ectoparasites on some fishes. Cyclostomes are marine but migrate for spawning to fresh water.','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-056','11-047',NULL),
(41260,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Cyclostomes have an elongated body bearing 4-15 pairs of gill slits for respiration.\n\nReason- Cyclostomes have a sucking and circular mouth with jaws.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','They have an elongated body bearing 6-15 pairs of gill slits for respiration. Cyclostomes have a sucking and circular mouth without jaws. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 56; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-056','11-047',NULL),
(41261,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Cranium and vertebral column in Cyclostomes are bony.\n\nReason- Body of cyclostomes is covered with scales and paired fins.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Body of cyclostomes is devoid of scales and paired fins. Cranium and vertebral column are cartilaginous. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 56; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-056','11-047',NULL),
(41262,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Cyclostomes after spawning, within a few days, they die.\n\nReason- Larvae of Cyclostomes after metamorphosis, return to the ocean.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','After spawning, within a few days, they die. Their larvae, after metamorphosis, return to the ocean. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 56; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-056','11-047',NULL),
(41263,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Circulation is of closed type.\n\nReason- Petromyzon is hagfish and Myxine is lamprey.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Circulation is of closed type. Examples: Petromyzon (Lamprey) and Myxine (Hagfish). (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 56; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-056','11-047',NULL),
(41264,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Chondrichthyes have to swim constantly to avoid sinking.\n\nReason- Chondrichthyes do not have air bladder.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Due to the absence of air bladder, they have to swim constantly to avoid sinking. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 56; Para 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-056','11-047',NULL),
(41265,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Chondrichthyes are fresh water animals with streamlined body.\n\nReason- Mouth is located terminally in them.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','They are marine animals with streamlined body. Mouth is located ventrally. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 56; Para 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-056','11-047',NULL),
(41266,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Chondrichthyes have cartilaginous endoskeleton.\n\nReason- In Chondrichthyes, gill slits are separate and covered with operculum.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','They are marine animals with streamlined body and have cartilaginous endoskeleton. Gill slits are separate and without operculum (gill cover). (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 56; Para 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-056','11-047',NULL),
(41267,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion-  In Chondrichthyes, skin is tough, containing minute placoid scales. \n\nReason- Teeth are modified placoid scales which are laterally directed in Chondrichthyes.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','The skin is tough, containing minute placoid scales. Teeth are modified placoid scales which are backwardly directed. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 56; Para 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-056','11-047',NULL),
(41268,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Torpedo possess poison sting.\n\nReason- Trygon have electric organs.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','Some of them have electric organs (e.g., Torpedo) and some possess poison sting (e.g., Trygon). (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 57; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-057','11-048',NULL),
(41269,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Chondrichthyes are cold-blooded or poikilothermous animals.\n\nReason- Chondrichthyes lack the capacity to regulate their body temperature.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','They are cold-blooded (poikilothermous) animals, i.e., they lack the capacity to regulate their body temperature. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 57; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-057','11-048',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(41270,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Chondrichthyes males having pelvic fins bear claspers.\n\nReason- Chondrichthyes show internal fertilisation and many of them are Oviparous.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','In males pelvic fins bear claspers. They have internal fertilisation and many of them are viviparous. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 57; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-057','11-048',NULL),
(41271,'biology','Consider these statements and choose the correct option.\n\n1. Carcharodon is known as the Great white shark.\n\n2. Pristis is also known as the Poison Sawfish.\n\n3. Scoliodon is the Cat fish.\n\n4. Trygon is called the Sting ray.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect.','Examples: Scoliodon (Dog fish), Pristis (Saw fish), Carcharodon (Great white shark), Trygon (Sting ray). (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 57; Para 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect.','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','NEW','11-057','11-048',NULL),
(41272,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Osteichthyes include only marine fishes.\n\nReason- Mouth in Osteichthyes is mostly ventral.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','It includes both marine and fresh water fishes with bony endoskeleton. Their body is streamlined. Mouth is mostly terminal. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 57; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-057','11-048',NULL),
(41273,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Osteichthyes is covered with cycloid/ctenoid scales. \n\nReason- Bony fishes have two pairs of gills which are covered by an operculum.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','They have four pairs of gills which are covered by an operculum on each side. Skin is covered with cycloid/ctenoid scales. (Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 11; Page 57; Para 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','11-057','11-048',NULL),
(41301,'chemistry','Which of the following is not non-polar covalent bond?','HF','The Covalent bond between two similar atoms is non-polar as both combining atoms being same have same electronegativity so the electronegativity difference is zero and electron density is equally shared between atoms bond is non-polar. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 110; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'2','mcq','O₂','H₂','N₂','HF','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41302,'chemistry','Which one is most polar?','HF','The Covalent bond between two similar atoms is non-polar as both combining atoms being same have same electronegativity so the electronegativity difference is zero and electron density is equally shared between atoms bond is non-polar. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 110; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'2','mcq','HF','HCl','HBr','HI','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41303,'chemistry','The type of chemical bond Hydrofluoric acid has:','Polar Covalent bond','Hydrogen fluoride is a molecule of one hydrogen atom and one fluorine atom bonded by a polar covalent bond.\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 111; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'3','mcq','Non-Polar Covalent bond','Polar Covalent bond','Ionic bond','Hydrogen bond','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41304,'chemistry','Dipole moment is due to ________ difference','Electronegativity','Dipole moments occur due to the difference in electronegativity between two chemically bonded atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 111; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'3','fillup','Elec,tro,ne,po,si,ga,ti,vi,ty',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41305,'chemistry','The product of magnitude of charge and distance between the positive and negative charge is known as?','Dipole moment','Dipole moment(𝜇) = Chrage (Q)  × distance of separation(r). \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 111; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'1','mcq','Dipole moment','Magnetic moment','Arm moment','None of these','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41306,'chemistry','Which is SI unit of Dipole moment?','Cm','The SI units for electric dipole moment are coulomb-meter; however, a commonly used unit in atomic physics and chemistry is the debye. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 111; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'3','mcq','Cm','Debye(D)','Both a and b','M','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41307,'chemistry','1D=','3.33564  ×  10⁻³⁰Cm','Net dipole moment (1D = 3.33564 × 10–30 C m) is the resultant of the dipole moments. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 111; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'1','mcq','333564  ×  10⁻³⁰Cm','33.3564  ×  10⁻³⁰Cm','3.33564  ×  10⁻³⁰Cm','3335.64  ×  10⁻³⁰Cm','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41308,'chemistry','The direction of dipole moment is from:','Positive center to negative center','Dipole moment is a vector quantity and by convention it is depicted by a small arrow with tail on the negative centre and head pointing towards the positive centre. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 111; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'1','mcq','Negative center to positive center','Positive center to negative center','No direction need it is a scalar quantity','Any direction','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41309,'chemistry','Dipole moment depends on:','All of these','The dipole moment not only depend upon the individual dipole moments of bonds known as bond dipoles but also on the spatial arrangement of various bonds in the molecule. \n\nIn such case, the dipole moment of a molecule is the vector sum of the dipole moments of various bonds. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 111; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'1','mcq','Dipole moment of individual bonds','Geometry of molecules','Symmetry of molecules','All of these','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41310,'chemistry','Match the following:','Dipole moment of H₂O-1.85D,Dipole moment of BeF₂-Zero,Dipole moment of  NF₃-0.23D,Dipole moment of  NH₃-1.47D','The product of the magnitude of the charge and the distance between the centers of the positive and negative charges. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 111; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'3','match','Dipole moment of H₂O, 1.85D','Dipole moment of BeF₂,Zero','Dipole moment of  NF₃,0.23D','Dipole moment of  NH₃,1.47D','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41311,'chemistry','The molecules having linear geometry always have:','Zero dipole moment','Linear molecules like O=C=O and F-Be-F have zero dipole moment, this is because in linear geometry, dipole moments pointed in opposite direction with resultant dipole moment equal to zero. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 111; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'1','mcq','Mximum dipole moment','Zero dipole moment','Minimum dipole moment','None of these','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41312,'chemistry','Which one of these have zero dipole moment?','Both a and b','CH₄ ,CCl₄ both have tetrahedral geometry, having individual bond dipole moments in opposite direction, which give zero resultant, moreover tetrahedral molecules having all four atoms same exibit symmetry, Symmetry if present cause dipole moemnt zero. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 111; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'1','mcq','CH₄','CCl₄','Both a and b','H₂O','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41313,'chemistry','The dipole moment of BF₃ is _______.','Zero','BF₃ the dipole moment is zero although the B-F bonds are oriented at 120 degree to one another, the three bond moments give a net sum of zero as the resultant of any two is equal and opposite to the third.  \n\nReference : (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 111; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'1','mcq','Zero','1','3','4','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41314,'chemistry','The dipole moment of NF₃ is _______ than the dipole moment of NH₃','Less','The dipole moment of NF₃ is less than the dipole moment of NH₃ ,both milecules have same shape (pyramidal) with lone pair of electrons on N Although F is more electronegative than Nitrogen.still have less dipole NH₃ this is due to direction of orbital dipole and bond dipole ,In case of NH₃ bond dipole and orbital dipole due to lone pair is in same direction while in case of NF₃ they are in opposite direction cancels the dipole of each other. \n\nReference : (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 112; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'2','mcq','Less','Greater','Stronger','Weaker','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41315,'chemistry','Which one have greater Covalent character?','SiCl₄','As we move along period size of cation decrease  size order will be Si⁴⁺<Al³⁺<Mg²⁺. \n\nAccording to Fajans smaller the size of cation greater will be its polarization power and greater will be the Covalent character. \n\nPolarization power  𝛼  Charge on cation  𝛼  Covalent character  Polarization power  𝛼  1/ size of cation. \n\nReference: ( NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 112; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'3','mcq','MgCl₂','AlCl₃','SiCl₄','All have same Covalent character','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41316,'chemistry','Which one have smallest Covalent character?','NaCl','Cu²⁺ =3d¹⁰4s⁰\nCo²⁺ =  3d⁷4s⁰\nMg²⁺ = 2s²2p⁶\nNa⁺ =   2s²2p⁶\n\nCu²⁺ < Co²⁺<Mg²⁺ < Na⁺  \n\nTransition metals with  (n-1)dⁿns⁰ are more polarizing than alkali and alkaline earth metals having noble gas configuration  ns²np⁶ according to  size Na⁺ is largest among them so will have smaller covalent character. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 112; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'3','mcq','CuCl₂','CoCl₂','MgCl₂','NaCl','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41317,'chemistry','The property of polarization is related to:','Cation','The cation polarises the anion,pulling the electronic charge toward itself and thereby increasing the electronic charge between the two. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 112; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'2','mcq','Cation','Anion','Radical','Free radical','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41318,'chemistry','Covalent character is directly linked to :','All of these','The cation polarises the anion, pulling the electronic charge toward itself and thereby increasing the electronic charge between the two. \n\nThis is precisely what happens in a covalent bond, i.e., buildup of electron charge density between the nuclei. \n\nThe polarising power of the cation, the polarisability of the anion and the extent of distortion (polarisation) of anion are the factors, which determine the percent covalent character of the ionic bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 112; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'1','mcq','Polarisation power of cations','Polarizibility of anions','Distortion of electron cloud of anion','All of these','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41319,'chemistry','The term polarizibilty is related to:','Electric Dipole','Polarizability is a measure of how easily an electron cloud is distorted by an electric field. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 112; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'2','mcq','Cations','Anions','Free radical','Electric Dipole','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41320,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: When covalent bond is formed between two similar atoms, the shared pair of electrons is equally repelled by the two atoms.\n \nR: As a result electron pair is situated exactly between the two identical nuclei.','Assertion is false but the reason is true','When covalent bond is formed between two similar atoms, for example in H₂, O₂, Cl₂, N₂ or F₂, the shared pair of electrons is equally attracted by the two atoms. \n\nAs a result electron pair is situated exactly between the two identical nuclei. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 110; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41321,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statement:\n\na. The existence of a hundred percent ionic or covalent bond represents an ideal situation. \n\nb.  In reality no bond or a compound is either completely covalent or ionic.\n\nc. Even in case of covalent bond between two hydrogen atoms, there is some ionic character.','None of these','The existence of a hundred percent ionic or covalent bond represents an ideal situation. \n\nIn reality no bond or a compound is either completely covalent or ionic. Even in case of covalent bond between two hydrogen atoms, there is some ionic character.\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 110; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only c','Only b and c','None of these','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(30711,'chemistry','Which term can be used for both covalent or metallic radius?','Atomic radius','We use the term Atomic Radius to refer to both covalent or metallic radius depending on whether the element is a non-metal or a metal. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 86; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Atomic radius',NULL,'1','mcq','Ionic radius','Atomic radius','Electron affinity','Electronegativity','NEW','11-086','11-086',NULL),
(41322,'chemistry','The bond form as a result of electron pair is situated exactly between the two identical nuclei is called __________.','Non-Polar Covalent bond','When covalent bond is formed between two similar atoms, for example in H₂, O₂, Cl₂, N₂ or F₂, the shared pair of electrons is equally attracted by the two atoms. \n\nAs a result electron pair is situated exactly between the two identical nuclei.\n\nThe bond so formed is called nonpolar covalent bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 110; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'1','mcq','Non-Polar Covalent bond','Polar Covalent bond','Ionic bond','Coordinate Covalent bond','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41323,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA:  In case of a heteronuclear molecule like HF, the shared electron pair between the two atoms gets displaced more towards fluorine.\n\nR:  Since the electronegativity of fluorine is far greater than that of hydrogen. The resultant covalent bond is a polar covalent bond.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Contrary to this in case of a heteronuclear molecule like HF, the shared electron pair between the two atoms gets displaced more towards fluorine since the electronegativity of fluorine is far greater than that of hydrogen. \n\nThe resultant covalent bond is a polar covalent bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 111; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41324,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement:\n\na. Dipole moment is the product of the magnitude of the charge and the distance between the centres of positive and negative charge.\n\nb. As a result of polarisation, the molecule possesses the dipole moment.\n\nc. Dipole moment is usually expressed in Debye units (D). 1 D = 3.141599 Cm','Only a and b','As a result of polarisation, the molecule possesses the dipole moment which can be defined as the product of the magnitude of the charge and the distance between the centres of positive and negative charge. \n\nDipole moment is usually expressed in Debye units (D). \n\n1 D = 3.33564 × 10–³⁰ C m.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 111; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and b','Only a and c','Only b and c','All of these','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41325,'chemistry','Identify the representation of given molecule: diagram(n0406)','Dipole moment','Dipole moment is represented by the crossed arrow put on Lewis structure of the molecule. \n\nThe cross is on positive end and arrow head is on negative end. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 111; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'2','mcq','Dipole moment','Lewis structure','Resonance form','Molecular structure','NEW','11-111','11-111','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/41325q.png'),
(30101,'chemistry','Who gave the Law of Triads','Johann Dobereiner','Johann Dobereiner in early 1800’s was the first to consider the idea of trends among properties of elements.Since Dobereiner’s relationship, referred to as the Law of Triads, seemed to work only for a few elements, it was dismissed as coincidence.\nReference; NCERT, class 11, Page 75','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Genesis of Periodic Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','A.E.B Chancourtois','Johann Dobereiner','Mendeleev','Moseley','NEW','11-075','11-075',NULL),
(30102,'chemistry','Why was Dobereiner’s Law of Triads dismissed?','It was applicable for only certain elements.','Since Dobereiner’s relationship, referred to as the Law of Triads, seemed to work only for a few elements, it was dismissed as coincidence.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 75; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Genesis of Periodic Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Triads were result of wrong mass measurement.','It was applicable for only certain elements.','It was applicable only for first 3 elements.','Triads of elemens have drastic difference in their properties.','NEW','11-075','11-075',NULL),
(30103,'chemistry','Cylindrical table of elements was given by','A.E.B Chancourtois','A.E.B. de Chancourtois in 1862, arranged the known elements in order of increasing atomic weights and made a cylindrical table of elements to display the periodic recurrence of properties.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 75; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Genesis of Periodic Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','A.E.B Chancourtois','Mendeleev','Johann Dobereiner','Moseley','NEW','11-075','11-075',NULL),
(30104,'chemistry','Which scientist plotted the physical properties against atomic weight?','Lothar Meyer','Lothar Meyer plotted the physical properties such as atomic volume, melting point and boiling point against atomic weight and obtained a periodically repeated pattern.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 75; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Genesis of Periodic Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Lothar Meyer','Newland','Dobereiner','Moseley','NEW','11-075','11-075',NULL),
(30105,'chemistry','Which scientist published the periodic table for the first time?','Mendeleev','The Russian chemist Dmitri Mendeleev was the first scientist to make a periodic table similar to the one used today.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 76; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Genesis of Periodic Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Mendeleev','Newland','Dobereiner','Moseley','NEW','11-075','11-075',NULL),
(30106,'chemistry','Which Scientist arrange the elements into a periodic table with increasing order of atomic weights?','Newlands','John Newlands arranged the elements in increasing order of their atomic weights. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 75; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Genesis of Periodic Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Dobereiner','Newlands','Meyer','Moseley','NEW','11-075','11-075',NULL),
(30107,'chemistry','Law of octaves is true only for elements upto:','Calcium','John Alexander Newlands in 1865 profounded the Law of Octaves. \n\nHe arranged the elements in increasing order of their atomic weights and noted that every eighth element had properties similar to the first element, as shown in the diagram \n\nThe relationship was just like every eighth note that resembles the first in octaves of music. Newland’s Law of Octaves seemed to be true only for elements up to calcium \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 75; Paragraph No. 3) (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 75); diagram(ne0302)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Genesis of Periodic Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Magnesium','Calcium','Boron','Zinc','NEW','11-075','11-075',NULL),
(30712,'chemistry','Match the the following elements to their atomic radius:','C-77 pm,B-88 pm,Be-111 pm,O66 pm','Atomic radii of the givaen elements are shown in the diagram \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 86; diagram(ne0309)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Atomic radius',NULL,'3','match','C,77 pm','B,88 pm','Be,111 pm','O,66 pm','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(30108,'chemistry','Mendeleev’s system of classifying elements was more elaborate than that of __________','Meyer','Mendeleev\'s system of classifying elements was more elaborate than that of Lothar Meyer\'s. He fully recognized the significance of periodicity and used broader range of physical and chemical properties to classify the elements.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Genesis of Periodic Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Meyer','Dobereiner','Moseley','Newland','NEW','11-076','11-076',NULL),
(30109,'chemistry','____  was the scientist, who is generally credited with the development of the Modern Periodic Table.','Mendeleev','Mendeleev the scientist who is generally credited with the development of the Modern Periodic Table.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 76; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Genesis of Periodic Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Mendeleev','Meyer','Moseley','Newland','NEW','11-076','11-076',NULL),
(30110,'chemistry','Which of the following are defects to Mendeleev\'s periodic table?\n\na. He used atomic mass as fundamental property.\n\nb. He relied on the similarities in the empirical formulas and properties of the compounds formed by the elements.\n\nc. Mendeleev arranged the elements according to similarities in the properties and not in increasing order of their atomic masses.\n\nd. Position of isotope is uncertain in the table.','c,d','These are the defects of Mendeleev periodic table a.Mendeleev arranged elements in horizontal rows and vertical columns of a table in order of their increasing atomic weights in such a way that the elements with similar properties.\n\nb.Mendeleev relied on the similarities in the empirical formulas and properties of the compounds formed by the elements. \n\nc.He realized that some of the elements did not fit in with his scheme of classification if the order of atomic weight was strictly followed. He ignored the order of atomic mass.\n\nd.According to increasing order of atomic weight rule different isotopes of same element must be given different places in the periodic table because they have different atomic weights.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 76; Paragraph No. 3,4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Genesis of Periodic Classification',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,d','c,d','b,c,d','NEW','11-076','11-076',NULL),
(30111,'chemistry','Which element was called as Eka-Aluminium by Mendeleev?','Gallium','Mendeleev published his Periodic Table. \n\nHe left the gap under aluminium and a gap under silicon, and called these elements Eka- Aluminium and Eka-Silicon. \n\nMendeleev predicted not only the existence of gallium and germanium, but also described some of their general physical properties. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 76; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Genesis of Periodic Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Indium','Germanium','Gallium','Arsenic','NEW','11-076','11-076',NULL),
(30112,'chemistry','Which element was called as Eka-Silicon by Mendeleev?','Germanium','Mendeleev published his Periodic Table. He left the gap under aluminium and a gap under silicon, and called these elements Eka- Aluminium and Eka-Silicon. \n\nMendeleev predicted not only the existence of gallium and germanium, but also described some of their general physical properties. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 76; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Genesis of Periodic Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Tin','Arsenic','Germanium','Gallium','NEW','11-076','11-076',NULL),
(30201,'chemistry','In which type spectra of elements did Moseley observed the regularities?','Characteristic X-ray spectra','Henry Moseley observed regularities in the characteristic X-ray spectra of the elements. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 78; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Modern periodic law',NULL,'1','mcq','Infrared spectra','Continuos X-ray spectra','Characteristic X-ray spectra','All of these','NEW','11-078','11-078',NULL),
(30202,'chemistry','Which among the following plots would give straight line?\n\na. √v against atomic mass\n\nb. √v against atomic number\n\nc. v against atomic mass\n\nd. v against atomic number','Only b','A plot of √v (where v is frequency of X-rays emitted) against atomic number (Z) gave a straight line and not the plot of √v vs atomic mass.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 78; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Modern periodic law',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','a and c only','b and d only','NEW','11-078','11-078',NULL),
(30203,'chemistry','The physical and chemical properties of the elements are periodic functions of their ___','Atomic numbers','The physical and chemical properties of the elements are periodic functions of their atomic numbers.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 78; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Modern periodic law',NULL,'1','mcq','Atomic numbers','Formula masses','Molecular masses','No. of Protons','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(30204,'chemistry','Which  group consists of most electronegative element and is numbered correctly according to IUPAC?','17','According to the recommendation of International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC), the groups are numbered from 1 to 18 replacing the older notation of groups IA … VIIA, VIII, IB… VIIB and 0. and electronegativity increases as we go across the group.\nSo, Group 17 has the highest electronegative elements.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 78; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Modern periodic law',NULL,'1','mcq','14','18','17','13','NEW','11-078','11-078',NULL),
(30205,'chemistry','Which scientist is credited with the discovery of transuranium elements?','Seaborg','Glenn T. Seaborg’s work in the middle of the 20th century starting with the discovery of plutonium in 1940, followed by those of all the transuranium elements from 94 to 102 led to reconfiguration of the periodic table placing the actinoids below the lanthanoids. \n\nIn 1951, Seaborg was awarded the Nobel Prize in chemistry for his work. Element 106 has been named Seaborgium (Sg) in his honour.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; End line)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Modern periodic law',NULL,'1','mcq','Moseley','Seaborg','Newlands','Proust','NEW','11-078','11-078',NULL),
(30206,'chemistry','Sixth period of periodic table has _____ elements.','32','2 S block elements + 6 P block elements + 10 D block elements + 14 f block elements= 32.\n\nThe subsequent periods consists of 8, 8, 18, 18 and 32 elements, respectively. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 78; paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Modern periodic law',NULL,'1','mcq','8','18','32','58','NEW','11-078','11-078',NULL),
(30207,'chemistry','In Modern periodic table series are called as ______','Periods','A modern version,the so-called “long form” of the Periodic Table of the elements, is the most convenient and widely used. \n\nThe horizontal rows (which Mendeleev called series) are called periods and the vertical columns, groups.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 78; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Modern periodic law',NULL,'1','fillup','Pe,ri,ods,Fa,mi,li,es',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-078','11-078',NULL),
(30208,'chemistry','Pitchblende is an ore of','Uranium','Neptunium and plutonium like actinium and protoactinium are also found in pitchblende; an ore of uranium. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 78; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Modern periodic law',NULL,'1','mcq','Neptunium','Plutonium','Uranium','Thorium','NEW','11-078','11-078',NULL),
(30209,'chemistry','How many of following statements is/are the correct from the given options?\n\nA.Out of the known elements, recently discovered are man-made.\n\nB.Elements having similar outer electronic configuration are referred to as families.\n\nC.There are altogether 7 periods and first period consist of 2 elements.\n\nD.New elements with very high atomic number are stable considerably.','3','A.Out of the known elements, recently discovered are man-made.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 75; Paragraph No. 2)\n\nB.Elements having similar outer electronic configurations in their atoms are arranged in vertical columns, referred to as groups or families.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 78; Paragraph No. 3)\n\nC.There are altogether seven periods. The period number corresponds to the highest principal quantum number (n) of the elements in the period. The first period contains 2 elements.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 78; Paragraph No. 4)\n\nD.The new elements with  very high atomic numbers are so unstable that only minute quantities, sometimes only a few atoms of them are obtained.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 78; Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Modern periodic law',NULL,'3','mcq','2','4','3','1','NEW','11-078','11-078',NULL),
(30210,'chemistry','Assertion: Periodic law is consequence of periodic variation in electronic configuration.\n\nReason: Electronic configuration determine the chemcial properties of elements and their compounds.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','It is now recognized that the Periodic Law is essentially the consequence of the periodic variation in electronic configurations, which indeed determine the physical and chemical properties of elements and their compounds.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 78; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Modern periodic law',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are false.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','NEW','11-078','11-078',NULL),
(30211,'chemistry','The properties of the elements are a ________ function of their atomic weights.','Periodic','The properties of the elements are a periodic function of their atomic weights. \n\nMendeleev\'s periodic law states that the properties of elements are the periodic function of their atomic mass.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 76; Mendeleeve statement)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Modern periodic law',NULL,'1','mcq','Periodic','Repeated','Ordinary','Estimated','NEW','11-076','11-076',NULL),
(30212,'chemistry','Identify the correct statement(s): \n\na. When Mendeleev developed his Periodic Table, chemists knew nothing about the internal structure of atom.\n\nb. Elements having similar outer electronic configurations in their atoms are arranged in horizontal rows.\n\nc. The atomic number is a more fundamental property of an element than its atomic mass.','Both a and c','a.We must bear in mind that when Mendeleev developed his Periodic Table, chemists knew nothing about the internal structure of atom. However, the beginning of the 20th century witnessed profound developments in theories about sub-atomic particles. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 78; Paragraph No. 1)\n\nb.The horizontal rows (which Mendeleev called series) are called periods and the vertical columns, groups. Elements having similar outer electronic configurations in their atoms are arranged in vertical columns, referred to as groups or families. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 78; Paragraph No. 4)\n\nc.Henry Moseley observed regularities in the characteristic X-ray spectra of the elements. A plot of ν (whereν is frequency of X-rays emitted) against atomic number (Z ) gave a straight line and not the plot of ν vs atomic mass. He thereby showed that the atomic number is a more fundamental property of an element than its atomic mass.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 78; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Modern periodic law',NULL,'1','mcq','Only b','Both a and b','Both a and c','All of these','NEW','11-078','11-078',NULL),
(30213,'chemistry','How many naturally occurring elements found in earth\'s crust and ocean?','94','The Periodic Law revealed important analogies among the 94 naturally occurring elements (neptunium and plutonium like actinium and protoactinium are also found in pitch blende – an ore of uranium). \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 78; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Modern periodic law',NULL,'1','fillup','3,4,5,7,8,9,',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-078','11-078',NULL),
(30214,'chemistry','The atomic number is equal to the __________.','Nuclear charge','The atomic number is equal to the nuclear charge (i.e., number of protons) or the number of electrons in a neutral atom.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 78; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Modern periodic law',NULL,'1','mcq','Nuclear charge','Atomic charge','Atomic radii','Half life of an atom','NEW','11-078','11-078',NULL),
(30215,'biology','The plant body of green algae may be -\n \n(i) Unicellular \n\n(ii) Colonial \n\n(iii) Filamentous \n\n(iv) Mat like','(i), (ii), (iii)','Green algae show wide variation in body forms. The\nplant body may be unicellular, colonial, or filamentous. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 32; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','(i), (ii)','(i), (ii), (iv)','(i), (ii), (iii)','NEW','11-032','11-026',NULL),
(30216,'biology','The major pigments in green algae are _____ and_____; and stored food is ____','Chlorophyll a and b , starch','Green algae have chlorophylls a and b and store starch as a food reserve inside their storage bodies. Being photosynthetic green algae have major starch as a stored food. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 34; Table 3.1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','3','mcq','Chlorophyll a and d, Starch','Chlorophyll a and c, floridean starch','Chlorophyll a and b , starch','Chlorophyll a and c, mannitol','NEW','11-034','11-028',NULL),
(30217,'biology','Types of chloroplasts found in different species of green Algae are','All the above','Different shapes of chloroplasts are seen in different species of green algae\nChloroplasts come in different shapes- discoid, cup-shaped, girdle shaped, reticulate, plate-like, ribbon-shaped, or even spiral. Cup-shaped chloroplasts are found in organisms such as Chlamydomonas, a green alga. Girdle shaped chloroplasts are found in organisms such as the filamentous green algae Ulothrix. Reticulate chloroplasts are found in the free-living, filamentous green algae Oedogonium. Higher plants have oval, discoid shaped chloroplasts.','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','1','mcq','Spiral and reticulate','Plate like and cup shaped','Discoid','All the above','NEW','11-032','11-026',NULL),
(30218,'biology','Most of the members of chlorophyceae have one or more storage bodies called pyrenoids located in the _______.','Chloroplast','The members of green algae have one or more storage bodies called pyrenoids located in the chloroplasts. Pyrenoids contain protein besides starch.\n (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 32; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Mitochondria','Chloroplast','In cytosol','In nucleus','NEW','11-032','11-026',NULL),
(30219,'biology','Pyrenoid contains -','Protein besides starch','They are storage bodies surrounded by starch and made up of protein structures. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 32; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Polysaccharide besides lipid','Starch besides lipid','Protein besides starch','Starch besides glycogen','NEW','11-032','11-026',NULL),
(30220,'biology','Most of the members have ______ storage bodies called pyrenoid','One or more','Most of the members have one or more storage bodies called pyrenoids located in the chloroplasts. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 32; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','1','mcq','Only one','Atleast ten','One or more','Nil','NEW','11-032','11-026',NULL),
(30221,'biology','Green algae have a cell wall made up of-','Outer layer of pectose and inner layer of cellulose','Green algae usually have a rigid cell wall made of an inner layer of cellulose and an outer layer of pectose. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 32; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Outer layer of pectose and inner layer of cellulose','Inner layer of pectose and outer layer of cell wall','Outer layer of Cellulose +inner layer of algin','Inner layer of Cellulose +outer layer of  peptidoglycan','NEW','11-032','11-026',NULL),
(30222,'biology','The major pigments in green algae are _____ and_______','Chl a, chl b','Green algae have chlorophylls a and b and store starch as a food reserve inside their storage bodies. Being photosynthetic green algae have major starch as a stored food. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 34; Table 3.1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','3','mcq','Chl a, chl d','Chl a, chl c','Chl a, chl b','Chl a, chl c','NEW','11-034','11-028',NULL),
(30223,'biology','In green algae \n(i) Some members show vegetative reproduction by fragmentation \n\n(ii)Asexual reproduction by production of various spores \n\n(iii)Sexual reproduction may be isogamous, anisogamous or oogamous \n\n(iv) vegetative reproduction by different types of spores','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','Vegetative reproduction usually takes place by fragmentation or by the formation of different types of spores. Asexual reproduction is by flagellated zoospores produced in zoosporangia. Sexual reproduction shows considerable variation in the type and formation of sex cells and it may be isogamous, anisogamous, or oogamous. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 32; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','1','mcq','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','(i), (ii)','(i), (ii), (iv)','(i), (ii), (iii)','NEW','11-032','11-026',NULL),
(30224,'biology','Identify the diagram (0302)','Volvox','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 31; Figure 3.1) diagram (e0325)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Volvox','Ulothrix','Fucus','Dictyota','NEW','11-031','11-025','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/30224q.png'),
(30225,'biology','Identify the diagram (0303)','Ulothrix','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 31; Figure 3.1) diagram (e0326)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Volvox','Ulothrix','Fucus','Dictyota','NEW','11-031','11-025','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/30225q.png'),
(30226,'biology','Chalmydomonas, volvox, ulothrix, spirogyra and chara belong to','Chlorophyceae','Some commonly found\ngreen algae are: Chlamydomonas, Volvox, Ulothrix, Spirogyra, and Chara. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 31; Figure 3.1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','1','mcq','Phaeophyceae','Rhodophyceae','Chlorophyceae','Cyanophyceae','NEW','11-031','11-025',NULL),
(30227,'biology','Which of the following pigments are found in brown algae?\n \n(i)Chlorophyll a and b \n\n(ii)Xanthophylls and carotenoids \n\n(iii)Chlorophyll a and c, \n\n(iv)Fucoxanthin','(ii), (iii), (iv)','Brown Algae possess chlorophyll a, c, carotenoids, and xanthophylls. They vary in color from olive green to various shades of brown depending upon the amount of the xanthophyll pigment, and fucoxanthin present in them. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 32; Paragraph No. 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','(ii), (iii), (iv)','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','(i), (ii)','(i), (ii), (iv)','NEW','11-032','11-026',NULL),
(30228,'biology','The members of Phaeophyceae or brown algae are found primarily in -','Marine habitat.','They mostly thrive in salt water. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 32; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','1','mcq','Fresh water','Marine habitat.','Terrestrial habitat','On rock','NEW','11-032','11-026',NULL),
(30229,'biology','Laminarin and mannitol, reserved food in brown algae are -','Complex carbohydrate','Food is stored as complex carbohydrates, which may be in the form of laminarin or mannitol. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 33; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Lipids','Complex carbohydrate','Proteins','Lipoprotein','NEW','11-033','11-027',NULL),
(30230,'biology','Laminaria, ectocarpus, dictyota, sargassum, and fucus are the examples -','Brown algae','Examples of Phaeophyceae. The common forms are Ectocarpus, Dictyota,\nLaminaria, Sargassum and Fucus. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 33; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Red algae','blue green algae','Brown algae','Green algae','NEW','11-033','11-027',NULL),
(30231,'biology','Which of the following is not a feature of the brown algae?','Most common pigment is chlorophyll b','The most common pigment in brown algae is chlorophyll a and c. They possess chlorophyll a, c, carotenoids, and xanthophylls. They vary in color from olive green to shades of brown depending on the amount of the xanthophyll pigment, and fucoxanthin present in them. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 32; Paragraph No. 4)\nThe members of Phaeophyceae or brown algae are found primarily in marine habitats. They show great variation in size and form. They range from simple branched, filamentous forms (Ectocarpus) to profusely branched forms as represented by kelps, which may reach a height of 100 meters. The plant body is usually attached to the substratum by a holdfast, and has a stalk, the stipe and leaf like photosynthetic organ – the frond.','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Multicellularity and large size','Almost exclusively marine','Attached forms have hold fast','Most common pigment is chlorophyll b','NEW','11-032','11-026',NULL),
(30713,'chemistry','Arrange the following elements in the increasing order of atomic radii:','F < Li< Mg < Na','Atomic radii of the givaen elements are shown in the diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 86; diagram(ne0309)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Atomic radius',NULL,'3','order','1,F','2,Li','3,Mg','4,Na','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(30232,'biology','Identify the Class of Algae with the following descriptions\n\na.Multicellular algae make up this group. \n\nb.Are composed of either branched filaments or leaf-like outgrowth giant kelps may reach up to 100m long.','Phaeophyceae','The members of Phaeophyceae or brown algae are found primarily in marine habitats. They show great variation in size and form. They range from simple branched, filamentous forms (Ectocarpus) to profusely branched forms as represented by kelps, which may reach a height of 100 meters. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 32; Paragraph No. 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Rhodophyceae','Phaeophyceae','Chlorophyceae','Chrysophyceae','NEW','11-032','11-026',NULL),
(30233,'biology','The major pigments of brown algae are','Chlorophyll a and c, fucoxanthin','Brown algae possess chlorophyll a, c, carotenoids, and fucoxanthin as the important pigment in them. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 32; Paragraph No. 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Chlorophyll a and c, fucoxanthin','Chlorophyll a and b, fucoxanthin','Chlorophyll a and d, fucoxanthin','Chlorophyll a  and d, phycoerythrin','NEW','11-032','11-026',NULL),
(30234,'biology','Usually, plant body of brown algae consists of- \n(i)Hold fast \n\n(ii)Stipe \n\n(iii)Frond \n\n(iv)Rhizoids','(i), (ii), (iii)','The plant body is usually attached to the substratum by a holdfast and has a stalk, the stipe, and leaf like a photosynthetic organ –the frond. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 33; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','1','mcq','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','(i), (ii), (iii)','(i), (ii)','(i), (ii), (iv)','NEW','11-033','11-027',NULL),
(30235,'biology','In most of the brown algae, asexual reproduction takes place by-','Pear shaped / pyriform biflagellate zoospores (have lateral, unequal flagella)','Asexual reproduction in most brown algae is by biflagellate zoospores that are pear-shaped and have two unequal laterally attached flagella. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 33; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','1','mcq','Auxospores','Aplanospores','Pear shaped / pyriform biflagellate zoospores (have lateral, unequal flagella)','Non Motile spore','NEW','11-033','11-027',NULL),
(30236,'biology','In brown algae the gametes are -','Pyriform / pear shaped gametes having unequal 2 laterally attached flagella','Sexual reproduction may be isogamous, anisogamous, or oogamous. Union of gametes may take place in water or within the oogonium (oogamous species). The gametes are pyriform (pear-shaped) and bear two laterally attached flagella. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 33; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','3','mcq','Only isogametes','Only coenaplanogamete','Only multiflagellate gamete','Pyriform / pear shaped gametes having unequal 2 laterally attached flagella','NEW','11-033','11-027',NULL),
(30237,'biology','Which one is correct about sexual reproduction in brown algae? \n(i)Sexual reproduction may be isogamous, anisogamous or oogamous \n\n(ii)Union of gametes may take place in water or within the oogonium (oogamous species).\n\n(iii)Union of gametes may take place in air or outside the oogonium (oogamous species).','(i), (ii)','Sexual reproduction may be isogamous, anisogamous, or oogamous. Union of gametes may take place in water or within the oogonium (oogamous species). The gametes are pyriform (pear-shaped) and bear two laterally attached flagella. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 33; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','1','mcq','(i), (ii), (iii)','(i), (ii)','(i) only','(i) and (iii)','NEW','11-033','11-027',NULL),
(30238,'biology','Identify the diagram (0304)','Laminaria','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 31; Figure 3.1) diagram (e0327)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Volvox','Ulothrix','Fucus','Laminaria','NEW','11-031','11-025','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/30238q.png'),
(30239,'biology','Identify the diagram (0305)','Dictyota','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 31; Figure 3.1) diagram (e0328)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Volvox','Ulothrix','Fucus','Dictyota','NEW','11-031','11-025','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/30239q.png'),
(30240,'biology','Mark the parts in the Fucus diagram (0306)','a-Air bladder,b-Frond,c-Midrib,d-Holdfast','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 31; Figure 3.1) diagram (e0329)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','3','match','a,Air bladder','b,Frond','c,Midrib','d,Holdfast','NEW','11-031','11-025','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/30240q.png'),
(30241,'biology','Which of the following is correct?\n(i) In brown algae as well as red algae vegetative reproduction takes place by fragmentation \n\n(ii)In red algae algal predominant pigment is r-phycoerythrin · \n\n(iii) Reserved food, floridean starch in red algae is very similar to amylopectin and glycogen in structure \n\n(iv) Examples of red algae are adiantum, porphyra, polysiphonia','(i), (ii), (iii)','The members of Rhodophyceae are commonly called red algae because of the predominance of the red pigment, r phycoerythrin in their body. The food is stored as floridean starch which is very similar to amylopectin and glycogen in structure.red algae and brown algae usually reproduce vegetatively by fragmentation. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 33; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','1','mcq','(i), (iii)','(i), (ii), (iii)','(i), (ii)','only iv','NEW','11-033','11-027',NULL),
(30242,'biology','Chlorophyll a, d and r-phycoerythrin are the major pigments in the members of','Rhodophyceae','The common name of Rhodophyceae is red algae which have major pigments chlorophyll a and d, phycoerythrin. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 33; Table 3.1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Rhodophyceae','Phaeophyceae','Chlorophyceae','Cyanophyceae','NEW','11-033','11-027',NULL),
(30243,'biology','Which of the following statements is not a characteristic of the red algae-','Post fertilization development is like any other algae','They occur in both well-lighted regions close to the surface of water and also at great depths in oceans where relatively little light penetrates.\nThey reproduce asexually by non-motile spores and sexually by non-motile gametes. Sexual reproduction is oogamous and accompanied by complex post fertilisation developments. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 34; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','3','mcq','Floridean starch (reserve food)','They reproduce asexually by non-motile spores and sexually by non-motile gametes.','Post fertilization development is like any other algae','They occur in both well-lighted regions close to the surface of water and also at great depths in oceans where relatively little light penetrates.','NEW','11-032','11-026',NULL),
(30714,'chemistry','Identify the variation of atomic radius with atomic number across the second period?','Atomic radius decreases when atomic no. increases','When an Atomic radius of an element decreases, the atomic no. of an element increases; this is applicable only across the period. \n\nIt is shown in diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 87; diagram(ne0320)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Atomic radius',NULL,'2','mcq','Atomic radius decreases when atomic no. increases','Atomic radius remains equal with atomic no.','Atomic radius increases when atomic no. decreases','Atomic radius increases when atomic no. increases','NEW','11-093','11-093',NULL),
(30244,'biology','Match the Algae with its commercial product','Algin-brown algae,Carrageen-red algae,Agar-Gelidium and Gracilaria','Certain marine brown and red algae produce large amounts of hydrocolloids (water holding substances), e.g., algin (brown algae) and carrageen (red algae) which are used commercially. Agar, one of the commercial products obtained from Gelidium and Gracilaria are used to grow microbes and in preparations of ice-creams and jellies. Chlorella a unicellular alga rich in proteins is used as food supplement even by space travellers. The algae are divided into three main classes: Chlorophyceae, Phaeophyceae and Rhodophyceae.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 32; Paragraph No.1 )','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','2','match','Algin,Brown algae','Carrageen,Red algae','Agar,Gelidium and Gracilaria',NULL,'NEW','11-032','11-026','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/30244q.png'),
(30245,'biology','Porphyra and polysiphonia belong to','Rhodophyceae','Examples of red algae. The common members are Polysiphonia,\nPorphyra, Gracilaria, and Gelidium.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 34; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Chlorophyceae','Rhodophyceae','Xanthophyceae','Phaeophyceae','NEW','11-034','11-028',NULL),
(30246,'biology','Identify the diagram (0310)','Porphyra','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 31; Figure 3.1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Laminaria','Fucus','Porphyra','Polysiphonia','NEW','11-031','11-025','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/30246q.png'),
(30247,'biology','Identify the diagram (0311)','Polysiphonia','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 31; Figure 3.1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Laminaria','Fucus','Porphyra','Polysiphonia','NEW','11-031','11-025','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/30247q.png'),
(30248,'biology','Many species of_______  are among the 70 species of marine algae used as a food \n\n(i)Porphyra \n\n(ii)Laminaria  \n\n(iii)Sargassum \n\n(iv)Chlorella','(i), (ii), (iii)','Many species of Porphyra,Laminaria and Sargassum are among the 70 species of marine algae used as food. Chlorella is used as a space food by space travellers (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 32; Paragraph No.1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','(i), (ii), (iii)','(i), (ii)','(i), (ii), (iv)','NEW','11-032','11-025',NULL),
(30249,'biology','Match the following','Volvox-colonial forms,Ulothrix and Spirogyra-filamentous forms,kelps-massive plant bodies','The form and size of algae are highly variable, ranging from colonial\nforms like Volvox and the filamentous forms like Ulothrix and Spirogyra\n. A few of the marine forms such as kelps, form massive plant\nbodies. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 30; Paragraph No. 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','2','match','Volvox,colonial forms','Ulothrix and Spirogyra,filamentous forms','kelps,massive plant bodies',NULL,'NEW','11-030','11-024',NULL),
(30250,'biology','Match the following','Asexual reproduction-zoospores,anisogamous-Eudorina,oogamous-Volvox and Fucus,isogamous-Ulothrix and Spirogyra','Asexual reproduction is by the production of different types of\nspores, the most common being the zoospores. Sexual reproduction\ntakes place through fusion of two gametes. These gametes can be\nflagellated and similar in size (as in Ulothrix) or non-flagellated (non-motile)\nbut similar in size (as in Spirogyra). Such reproduction is called\nisogamous. Fusion of two gametes dissimilar in size, as in species of\nEudorina is termed as anisogamous. Fusion between one large, nonmotile\n(static) female gamete and a smaller, motile male gamete is termed\noogamous, e.g., Volvox, Fucus. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 30; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','2','match','Asexual reproduction,zoospores','anisogamous,Eudorina','oogamous,Volvox and Fucus','isogamous,Ulothrix and Spirogyra','NEW','11-030','11-024',NULL),
(30251,'biology','Vegetative reproduction in phaeophyceae is by','Fragmentation','Vegetative reproduction takes place by fragmentation (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 33; paragraph no. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','1','mcq','Budding','Fragmentation','Fission','Soap box formation','NEW','11-033','11-027',NULL),
(30252,'biology','The protoplast contains, in addition to plastids, a centrally located vacuole and nucleus. this statement is true for','Phaeophyceae','The vegetative cells have a cellulosic wall usually covered on the outside by a gelatinous coating of algin. The protoplast contains, in addition to plastids, a centrally located vacuole and nucleus. The plant body is usually attached to the substratum by a holdfast, and has a stalk, the stipe and leaf like photosynthetic organ – the frond.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 33; paragraph no. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Algae','Plant kingdom','1','mcq','Rhodophyceae','Phaeophyceae','Chlorophyceae','Algae','NEW','11-033','11-027',NULL),
(30302,'chemistry','The naming of the new elements had been traditionally the privilege of the ____','discoverer','The naming of the new elements had been traditionally the privilege of the discoverer (or discoverers) and the suggested name was ratified by the IUPAC.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 78; Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Nomenclature of elements',NULL,'1','fillup','dis,co,imp,ver,er,ro',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-078','11-078',NULL),
(30303,'chemistry','Americans named element 104 as ___','Rutherfordium','Both American and Soviet scientists claimed credit for discovering element 104. \n\nThe Americans named it Rutherfordium whereas Soviets named it Kurchatovium.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 80; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Nomenclature of elements',NULL,'1','fillup','Rut,her,for,di,um,Kuc',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-080','11-080',NULL),
(30304,'chemistry','Match the following:','Unt-Lr,Unp-Db,Uuh-Lv,Uus-Ts','As shown in diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 80; (ne0308)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Nomenclature of elements',NULL,'3','match','Unt,Lr','Unp,Db','Uuh,Lv','Uus,Ts','NEW','11-080','11-080',NULL),
