(30307,'chemistry','Noble gas with highest atomic number known is','Oganesson','As shown in diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 80; diagram(ne0308)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Nomenclature of elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Oganesson','Radon','Xenon','Neon','NEW','11-080','11-080',NULL),
(30308,'chemistry','Atomic number 113 has IUPAC name __','Nihonium','As shown in diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 80; diagram(ne0308)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Nomenclature of elements',NULL,'1','fillup','Ni,ho,ni,um',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-080','11-080',NULL),
(30309,'chemistry','Atomic number 114 has IUPAC name ____','Flerovium','Atomic number 114 has IUPAC name Flerovium. As shown in diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 80; diagram(ne0308)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Nomenclature of elements',NULL,'1','fillup','Fl,er,ov,ium',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-080','11-080',NULL),
(30310,'chemistry','Scientists, before collecting the reliable data on the new element, at times get tempted to claim for its discovery. Pickout:','Statement is correct.','Scientists, before collecting the reliable data on the new element, at times get tempted to claim for its discovery. \n\nFor example, both American and Soviet scientists claimed credit for discovering element 104. The Americans named it Rutherfordium whereas Soviets named it Kurchatovium. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 80; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Nomenclature of elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Not applicable for all elements.','Statement is correct.','Statement is incrorrect.','Only applicable for some elements.','NEW','11-080','11-080',NULL),
(30311,'chemistry','In terms of IUPAC nomenclature, symbol for Ununquadium is ______.','Uuq','In terms of IUPAC nomenclature, symbol for Ununquadium is Uuq. \n\nAs shown in diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 80; diagram(ne0308)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Nomenclature of elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Unq','Uuq','Unu','Uno','NEW','11-080','11-080',NULL),
(30312,'chemistry','A systematic nomenclature be derived directly from the atomic number of the element using the numerical roots: are:','For 0 and numbers 1-9','A systematic nomenclature be derived directly from the atomic number of the element using the numerical roots for 0 and numbers 1-9.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 80; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Nomenclature of elements',NULL,'1','mcq','For 0 and numbers 1','For 0 and numbers 1-9','For 0 and even numbers','For 0 and odd numbers','NEW','11-080','11-080',NULL),
(30313,'chemistry','Match the following:','4-quad, 0-nil, 1-un, 9-een','Notation for the IUPAC nomenclature of the elements are as follows as shown in diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 80; diagram(ne0317)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Nomenclature of elements',NULL,'1','match','4,quad','1,un','0,nil','9,een','NEW','11-082','11-082',NULL),
(30314,'biology','Sphagnum is also called -','peat moss','Species of Sphagnum, a moss, provide peat that has long been used as fuel. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','peat moss','Club moss','Spike moss','Reindeer moss','NEW','11-035','11-029',NULL),
(30315,'biology','Sphagnum (a moss) provides-','Peat(fuel)','Peat is used as an alternative to coal. Species of Sphagnum, a moss, provide peat that has long been used as fuel. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','1','mcq','Oil','Peat(fuel)','Agar','Antibiotics','NEW','11-035','11-029',NULL),
(30316,'biology','Mosses are of great ecological importance because of- \n\n(i)Its contribution to prevent soil erosion \n\n(ii)Its contribution in ecological succession \n\n(iii)Its capability to remove CO from the atmosphere ·','(i), (ii)','Mosses along with lichens are the first organisms to colonise rocks and hence, are of great ecological importance. They decompose rocks making the substrate suitable for the growth of higher plants. Since mosses form dense mats on the soil, they reduce the impact of falling rain and prevent soil erosion. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','(i), (ii)','(i), (ii), (iii)','(i), (iii)','(ii), (iii)','NEW','11-035','11-029',NULL),
(30317,'biology','The mosses which form dense extensive mats on the soil prevents -','Reduce the impact of falling rain and prevent soil erosion.','Mosses form dense mats on the soil, they reduce the impact of falling rain and prevent soil erosion. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','1','mcq','Uprooting of trees','Reduce the impact of falling rain and prevent soil erosion.','Falling of leaves','Evaporation of water from the soil','NEW','11-035','11-029',NULL),
(30318,'biology','Bryophytes are not characterized by -','The well developed root system and vascular tissue','Bryophytes lack well-developed root systems and vascular tissues. They lack true roots, stems, or leaves. They may possess root-like, leaf-like, or stem-like structures. Rhizoids are unicellular or multicellular filamentous outgrowths that extend from the thallus of bryophytes like liverworts and mosses(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','The well developed root system and vascular tissue','Rhizoids','Alternation of generation','Presence of chlorophyll','NEW','11-035','11-029',NULL),
(30319,'biology','Asexual reproduction in liverworts is by fragmentation of _____ or by the formation of _____','Thalli, gemma','Asexual reproduction in liverworts takes place by fragmentation of thalli, or by the formation of specialized structures called gemmae (sing. gemma). (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; Paragraph No. 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','1','mcq','Thalli, gemma','Gemma, thalli','Funaria,protonema','prothallus, buds','NEW','11-035','11-029',NULL),
(30320,'biology','Which of the following statements is correct about Bryophytes?\n\n(i)The first stage is the protonema stage, which develops directly from a spore. It is a creeping, green, branched and frequently filamentous stage. \n\n(ii)The second stage is the leafy stage, which develops from the secondary protonema as a lateral bud. They consist of upright, slender axes bearing spirally arranged leaves.\n\n(iii) The third stage is development of vascular tissues','(i), (ii)','The predominant stage of the life cycle of a moss is the gametophyte which consists of two stages. The first stage is the protonema stage, which develops directly from a spore. It is a creeping, green, branched, and frequently filamentous stage. The second stage is the leafy stage, which develops from the secondary protonema as a lateral bud. They consist of upright, slender axes bearing spirally arranged leaves. They are attached to the soil through multicellular and branched rhizoids. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 36; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','(i), (ii)','(i), (iii)','(i), (ii), (iii)','(ii), (iii)','NEW','11-036','11-030',NULL),
(30321,'biology','Funaria requires water for completion of life cycle because','Fertilization occurs in water only','Gametes require water for their transportation. mosses being a bryophyte requires water for fertilization. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 36; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','1','mcq','Fertilization occurs in water only','Funaria is a hydrophyte','Plants dry up and die without water','No branching and sex organs cannot develop without water','NEW','11-036','11-030',NULL),
(30322,'biology','Sphagnum is also called peat moss because','It grows in acidic marshes (bog) and helps in peat formation','Peat is used as an alternative to coal. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; Paragraph No. 3) \nSphagnum grows on the top of peat bog and when it dies, much of its dead material is found in the peat bog. Thus, it does not decay to form peat','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','It grows in acidic marshes (bog) and helps in peat formation','It is found in Bituminous type of coal','It decays to form the best quality of coal','It fossilizes quickly and is a substitute for fossil fuels','NEW','11-035','11-029',NULL),
(30323,'biology','Which one is correct about Marchantia (liverwort)?\n\n(i)Plant body is monoecious or dioecious \n\n(ii) Sporophyte is differentiated into foot, seta and capsule \n\n(iii) Spore germinates to form a free living gametophytes \n\n(iv) After meiosis, spores are produced within the capsule.','(ii), (iii), (iv)','During sexual reproduction, male and female sex organs are produced either on the same or on different thalli. The sporophyte is differentiated into a foot, seta, and capsule. After meiosis, spores are produced within the capsule. These spores germinate to form free-living gametophytes. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; Paragraph No. 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','1','mcq','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','(i), (ii), (iv)','(ii), (iii), (iv)','(ii), (iii)','NEW','11-035','11-029',NULL),
(30324,'biology','Which among the following isn\'t a moss?','Nest moss','Nest moss belongs to Pteridophyta. Common examples of mosses are Funaria, Polytrichum, and Sphagnum. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 36; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Nest moss','Funaria','Sphagnum','Polytrichum','NEW','11-036','11-030',NULL),
(30325,'biology','Asexual reproduction in liverworts takes place by-','Fragmentation of thalli or by gemmae formation','Asexual reproduction in liverworts takes place by fragmentation of thalli, or by the formation of specialized structures called gemmae (sing. gemma)(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; Paragraph No. 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Fragmentation of thalli or by gemmae formation','Gemmae formation + diploid spores formation','Spores formation + isogamy','Fragmentation + zoospores formation','NEW','11-035','11-029',NULL),
(30326,'biology','In mosses, gametophyte has 2 stages. what are these stages?','1st stage is protonema and the second stage is leafy stage','The predominant stage of the life cycle of a moss is the gametophyte which\nconsists of two stages. The first stage is the protonema stage, which\ndevelops directly from a spore. It is a creeping, green, branched and\nfrequently filamentous stage. The second stage is the leafy stage, which\ndevelops from the secondary protonema as a lateral bud. They consist of\nupright, slender axes bearing spirally arranged leaves. They are attached\nto the soil through multicellular and branched rhizoids. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 36; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','1st stage is sporogonium phase and 2nd protonema stage','1st stage is protonema and the second stage is leafy stage','1st stage is gemmae formation and 2nd one is meiosis','1st stage is zygote and 2nd one is spore mother cell','NEW','11-036','11-030',NULL),
(30327,'biology','Protonema-\n (i)Is a stage of gametophytic generation \n\n(ii)Is a creeping, green, branched stages and developes directly from a spore \n\n(iii)Produces lateral bud which forms leafy plant body \n\n(iv)develops indirectly from the spores.','(i), (ii), (iii)','The predominant stage of the life cycle of a moss is the gametophyte which\nconsists of two stages. The first stage is the protonema stage, which\ndevelops directly from a spore. It is a creeping, green, branched and\nfrequently filamentous stage. The second stage is the leafy stage, which\ndevelops from the secondary protonema as a lateral bud. They consist of\nupright, slender axes bearing spirally arranged leaves. They are attached\nto the soil through multicellular and branched rhizoids. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 36; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','1','mcq','(i), (ii), (iii)','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','(i), (ii), (iv)','(ii), (iii), (iv)','NEW','11-036','11-030',NULL),
(30328,'biology','In mosses, Vegetative reproduction occurs by-','Fragmentation and budding in the secondary protonema','Bud from secondary protonema develops into the leafy stage. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 36; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Fragmentation and budding in the secondary protonema','Gemmae and adventious bud formation','Gemmae and tubers formation','By multiflagellate spores formation','NEW','11-036','11-030',NULL),
(30329,'biology','The sporophyte in mosses is -','More elaborated than that in liverworts','After fertilization, the zygote develops into a sporophyte, consisting of a foot, seta and capsule. The sporophyte in mosses is more elaborate than that in liverworts. The capsule contains spores. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Less elaborated than that in liverworts','More elaborated than that in liverworts','Equally elaborated than that in liverworts','Independent of gametophyte','NEW','11-035','11-029',NULL),
(30330,'biology','Spores dispersal in mosses occurs by -','Elaborate mechanism','The mosses have an elaborate mechanism of spore dispersal. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 36; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Simple mechanism','Elaters','Elaterophores','Elaborate mechanism','NEW','11-036','11-030',NULL),
(30331,'biology','In bryophytes meiosis occurs in -','Spore mother cells','Meiosis occurs in the spore mother cell and produces spores. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','3','mcq','Spores','Gametes mother cells','Gametes','Spore mother cells','NEW','11-036','11-030',NULL),
(30332,'biology','The multicellular sporophytic phase is expected in the life cycle of -','Bryophytes','In bryophytes, An antherozoid fuses with the egg to produce the zygote. Zygotes do not undergo reduction division immediately. They produce a multicellular body called a sporophyte. The sporophyte is not free-living but attached to the photosynthetic gametophyte and derives nourishment from it.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Euglenoids','Green algae','Diatoms','Bryophytes','NEW','11-036','11-030',NULL),
(30333,'biology','You are given an unknown plant to study in the laboratory. you find that it has chlorophyll, no xylem. its multicellular sex organs are enclosed in a layer of jacket cells. its gametophyte stage is free living. the plant probably belongs to','Bryophyte','Bryophytes lack true roots, stems, or leaves. They may possess root-like, leaf-like, or stem-like structures. The sex organs in bryophytes are multicellular.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Chlorophyceae','Bryophyte','Pteridophyte','Gymnosperm','NEW','11-036','11-030',NULL),
(30334,'biology','Place the following groups of plants in order, beginning with those that first appeared on the earth and progressing ·award those that appeared most recently in time -','Algae > Moss > Ferns >','As per the phylogentic classification, under plant kingdom the first one to come is algae then bryophytes which include mosses and liverworts then comes the pteridophytes including ferns. After pteridophytes, comes gymnosperm and at last the angiosperm. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 29)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','2','order','1,Algae','2,Moss','3,Ferns',NULL,'NEW','11-029','11-023',NULL),
(30335,'biology','A moss sperm(antherozoid) moves by means of','2 flagella','Being a bryophyte, the male sex organ is an antherozoid that is biflagellated. Antherozoids have 2 flagella(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','2','mcq','Cilia','3 flagella','2 flagella','Many flagella','NEW','11-035','11-029',NULL),
(30336,'biology','The embryo formed by fusion of male and female gametes in bryophyte develops in the –','Archiegonium','The female sex organ called archegonium is flask-shaped and produces a single egg. The antherozoids are released into the water where they come in contact with archegonium. In archegonium further development takes place. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','1','mcq','Protonema','Sporangium','Antheridium','Archiegonium','NEW','11-035','11-029',NULL),
(30337,'biology','In the alternation of generations, the sporophyte generation is ____ and the gametophyte generation is _','2n, n','The sporophyte is diploid and the gametophyte is haploid. the sporophyte is the plant body formed on the fusion of gametes. therefore, it is diploid. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes','Plant kingdom','3','mcq','n, 2n','2n, 2n','2n, n','n, n','NEW','11-035','11-029',NULL),
