(30344,'biology','In Bryophytes, antheridium produce biflagellate _____.','Antherozoids','The sex organs in bryophytes are multicellular. The male sex organ is called antheridium. They produce biflagellate antherozoids.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Bryophytes',NULL,'1','mcq','Antherozoids','Archegonoid','Both Antherozoids & Archegonoid','None of them','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30401,'chemistry','Consider the following assertion and reason:\n\nA: Hydrogen can be placed in s-block.\n\nR: Hydrogen has only one electron.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Hydrogen has one electron and hence can be placed in s block. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 82; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are false.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion','NEW','11-079','11-079',NULL),
(30402,'chemistry','What can be outer electronic configuration of Platinum?','5d⁹ 6s¹','As shown in the referred diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No.79; diagram(ne0306)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'2','mcq','5d⁹ 6s¹','5d¹⁰ 6s⁰','5d⁸ 6s²','5d⁹ 6s⁰','NEW','11-081','11-081',NULL),
(30403,'chemistry','The first period (n = 1) starts with the filling of the lowest level (1s) and therefore has __ elements.','Two','The first period (n = 1) starts with the filling of the lowest level (1s) and therefore has two elements. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 81; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','One','Two','Three','Four','NEW','11-081','11-081',NULL),
(30404,'chemistry','An element’s location in the Periodic Table reflects the quantum numbers of the ___ orbital filled.','Last','An element’s location in the Periodic Table reflects the quantum numbers of the last orbital filled.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 81; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Last','Fourth','First','Middle','NEW','11-081','11-081',NULL),
(30405,'chemistry','The 4f transition series ends at which element?','Lu','The sixth period (n = 6) contains 32 elements and successiven electrons enter 6s, 4f, 5d and 6p orbitals, in the order - filling up of the 4f orbitals begins with cerium (Z = 58) and ends at lutetium (Z = 71) to give the 4f-inner transition series. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 81; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Ce','Lu','Cm','Sm','NEW','11-081','11-081',NULL),
(30406,'chemistry','Which period includes most of the man-made elements?','Seventh','The seventh period (n = 7) is similar to the sixth period with the successive filling up of the 7s, 5f, 6d and 7p orbitals and includes most of the man-made radioactive elements. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 81; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Sixth','Fifth','Seventh','First','NEW','11-082','11-082',NULL),
(30407,'chemistry','Why the 4f and 5f-inner transition series of elements are placed separately in the Periodic Table?','both a and b','The 4fand 5f-inner transition series of elements are placed separately in the Periodic Table to maintain its structure and to preserve the principle of classification by keeping elements with similar properties in a single column.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 82; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'2','mcq','To maintain its structure.','To preserve the principle of classification by keeping elements with similar properties in a single column.','To highlight the f block elements as exception periodic rule.','both a and b','NEW','11-079','11-079',NULL),
(30408,'chemistry','Lanthanum belongs which block of the periodic table?','f-block','As shown in the referred diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No.79; diagram(ne0306)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','s-block','f-block','p-block','d-block','NEW','11-079','11-079',NULL),
(30409,'chemistry','Match the following:','Rb-s-block,Hf-d-block,Bk-f-block,Bi-p-block','As shown in the referred diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No.79; diagram(ne0306)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'3','match','Rb,s-block','Bi,p-block','Hf,d-block','Bk,f-block','NEW','11-082','11-082',NULL),
(30410,'chemistry','Which element is special case in periodic table and placed at top the periodic table?','Hydrogen','The exception is hydrogen. \n\nIt has only one s-electron and hence can be placed in group 1 (alkali metals). It can also gain an electron to achieve a noble gas arrangement and hence it can behave similar to a group 17 (halogen family) elements.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 82; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'2','mcq','Helium','Carbon','Oxygen','Hydrogen','NEW','11-079','11-079',NULL),
(30411,'chemistry','The electronic configuration of the element which is just above the element with atomic number 42 in the same group is:','1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁵ 4s¹','As shown in the referred diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No.79; diagram(ne0306)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'3','mcq','1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁵ 4s²','1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁶ 4s⁰','1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶','1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁵ 4s¹','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(30412,'chemistry','The elements with atomic numbers 34, 52 and 84 are all ________.','chalcogens','They are the halogens (Group 17) and the chalcogens (Group 16). \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 84; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'2','mcq','noble gases','halogens','alkali metals','chalcogens','NEW','11-079','11-079',NULL),
(30413,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC official name of element with atomic number 114?','Flerovium','As shown in the referred diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No.79; diagram(ne0306)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'2','mcq','Ununtetranium','Copernicium','Rontgenium','Flerovium','NEW','11-081','11-081',NULL),
(30414,'chemistry','What would be the IUPAC name for the element with atomic number 119?','Ununennium','The roots for 1 and 9 are un and en respectively. Hence, the symbol and the name respectively are Uue and Ununennium. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 81; Table 3.4 and Problem 3.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Unennillium','Ununennium','Unnilennium','Unununnium','NEW','11-081','11-081',NULL),
(30415,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC official name of element with atomic number 112?','Copernicium','IUPAC official name of element with atomic number 112 is Copernicium. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 81; Table 3.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Ununbium','Copernicium','Rontgenium','Meitnerium','NEW','11-080','11-080',NULL),
(30416,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC symbol of element with atomic number 109?','Mt','As shown in diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 80; diagram(ne0308)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Une','Db','Mt','Lv','NEW','11-080','11-080',NULL),
(30417,'chemistry','Match the following:','Ts-Uus,Fl-Uuq,Nh-113,No-Unb','As shown in diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 80; diagram(ne0308)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'3','match','Ts,Uus','Fl,Uuq','Nh,113','No,Unb','NEW','11-080','11-080',NULL),
(30418,'chemistry','Arrange the following elements in the increasing order of atomic number:','Lr < Db < Mt < Ds','As shown in diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 80; diagram(ne0308)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'3','order','1,Lr','2,Db','3,Mt','4,Ds','NEW','11-080','11-080',NULL),
(30419,'chemistry','What is the symbol of element having atomic number 107?','Uns','As shown in diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 80; diagram(ne0308)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Uns','Uus','Uuh','Unh','NEW','11-083','11-083',NULL),
(30420,'chemistry','Which of the following elements belong to group to which Cu belongs?','Rg','The group to which Copper (Cu) belongs is group 11, also known as the coinage metals group. The other elements in this group are Gold (Au) , Silver (Ag) and Roentegenium(Rg). \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 83;\\','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Ds','Rg','Cn','Sg','NEW','11-080','11-080',NULL),
(30421,'chemistry','Which of the following statement(s) is/are correct about an element whose Z = 108?\n\nA:Systematic name is Unniloctium.\n\nB:Official name is Hassium.\n\nC:Symbol is Unh.','A and B','As shown in diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 80; diagram(ne0308)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'2','mcq','A and B','A only','B and C','C only','NEW','11-081','11-081',NULL),
(30422,'chemistry','3-d transition series start with ___','Sc','3d transition series of elements. \n\nThis starts from scandium (Z = 21) which has the electronic configuration 3d14s2. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 81; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Ti','Cr','Sc','Zn','NEW','11-082','11-082',NULL),
(30423,'chemistry','Assertion: 5th period of the periodic table consists of 18 elements.\n\nReason: Total number of orbital available here are 9 and maximum electrons that can be accomodated are 18.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','When n = 5, l = 0, 1, 2, 3. The order in which the energy of the available orbitals 4d, 5s and 5p increases is 5s < 4d < 5p. \n\nThe total number of orbitals available are 9. \n\nThe maximum number of electrons that can be accommodated is 18; and therefore 18 elements are there in the 5th period.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 82; Problem 3.2 )','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are false.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','NEW','11-081','11-081',NULL),
(30424,'chemistry','Subshells also referred to as:','Orbitals','The filling of electrons into different subshells, also referred to as orbitals (s, p, d, f ) in an atom. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 81; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Groups','Orbitals','Atom','Energy','NEW','11-081','11-081',NULL),
(30425,'chemistry','What indicates the value of n for the outermost or valence shell?','Period','The period indicates the value of n for the outermost or valence shell.\n\nIn other words, successive period in the Periodic Table is associated with the filling of the next higher principal energy level (n = 1, n = 2, etc.). \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 81; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Period','Energy','Groups','Orbitals','NEW','11-081','11-081','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/30425q.png'),
(30426,'chemistry','The third period (n = 3) begins at sodium, and the added electron enters a __________ orbital.','3s','The third period (n = 3) begins at sodium, and the added electron enters a 3s orbital. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 81; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','4s','3s','2s','1s','NEW','11-082','11-082','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/30426q.png'),
(30427,'chemistry','_________ elements are included in inner transition series','actinoid','Filling up of the 5f orbitals after actinium (Z = 89) gives the 5f-inner Inner transition series known as the actinoid series.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 82; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'1','fillup','p,block,acti,noid,s,block',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-082','11-082','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/30427q.png'),
(30501,'chemistry','Elements of group 16 are also knowns as','Chalcogens','Preceding the noble gas family are two chemically important groups of non-metals. \n\nThey are the halogens (Group 17) and the chalcogens (Group 16).\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 84; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration Groupwise',NULL,'1','mcq','Halogens','Alkali metals','Heavy metals','Chalcogens','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(30502,'chemistry','Consider the following assertion and reason:\n\nA: Non-metallic character increases as we go from left to right across a period.\n\nR: Metallic character increases as we go down the group.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','The non-metallic character of an atom is its tendency to gain electron and this increases as as we move from left to right across a period because as we move left to right in a peroid, electrons are added to the same outermost shell and at the same time protons are are also added in the nucleaus which results into increased nuclear charge, hence the ability of an atom to become anion by accepting outside electron increases. The metallic character of an atom is its tendency to lose electron which increases as we go down the group.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 84; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration Groupwise',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are false.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(30503,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true with respect to d-block elements?\n\na. All are metals.\n\nb. They mostly form colored ions.\n\nc. They are oftenly used as catalysts.','All a, b, c','They are all metals.\n\nThey mostly form coloured ions, exhibit variable valence (oxidation states), paramagnetism and oftenly used as catalysts. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 84; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration Groupwise',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','All except a','Only a and c','All a, b, c','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(30504,'chemistry','Which among the following elements do not show the most of the properties of other transition elements?','Cd','Zn, Cd and Hg which have the electronic configuration, (n-1) d10ns2 do not show most of the properties of transition elements. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 84; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration Groupwise',NULL,'2','mcq','Cs','Ca','Cd','Ce','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(30505,'chemistry','The chemistry of the early actinoids is more complicated than the corresponding ___________.','Lanthanoids','The chemistry of the early actinoids is more complicated than the corresponding lanthanoids. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 84; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration Groupwise',NULL,'1','mcq','Lanthanoids','Transition metals','Noble gases','Halogen Family','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(30506,'chemistry','Consider the following assertion and reason:\n\nA: The chemistry of the early actinoids is more complicated than the corresponding lanthanoids.\n\nR: Actinoids have large number of oxidation states.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','The chemistry of the early actinoids is more complicated than the corresponding lanthanoids, due to the large number of oxidation states possible for these actinoid elements. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 84; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration Groupwise',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(30507,'chemistry','Which of the following is correct about an element with Electronic configuration [Uuo] 8s²?','It is an alkaline earth metal.','[Uuo] 8s² will have atomic number 120 and will be in group 2 and thus will be an alkaline earth metal. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 82; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration Groupwise',NULL,'3','mcq','It is an alkaline earth metal.','Its atomic number is 118.','It will be placed in group 12.','It is an alkali metal.','NEW','11-082','11-082',NULL),
(30508,'chemistry','The principal quantum number (n ) defines the main energy level known as ___.','shell','The principal quantum number (n ) defines the main energy level known as shell.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 81; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration Groupwise',NULL,'1','fillup','she,ll,sch,ed',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-081','11-081',NULL),
(30509,'chemistry','How many elements are there in second period?','8','The second period (n = 2) starts with lithium and the third electron enters the 2s orbital. \n\nThe next element, beryllium has four electrons and has the electronic configuration 1s22s2. \n\nStarting from the next element boron, the 2p orbitals are filled with electrons when the L shell is completed at neon (2s22p6). Thus there are 8 elements in the second period. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 81; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration Groupwise',NULL,'1','mcq','8','6','10','12','NEW','11-081','11-081',NULL),
(30510,'chemistry','Elements in the same group have _______ valence shell electronic configurations.','Similar','Elements in the same vertical column or group have similar valence shell electronic configurations. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 82; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration Groupwise',NULL,'1','mcq','Similar','Highest','Lowest','Dissimilar','NEW','11-082','11-082',NULL),
(30511,'chemistry','The p-Block Elements comprise those belonging to Group 13 to 18 and these together with the s-Block Elements are called as ____','Representative Elements or Main Group Elements','The p-Block Elements comprise those belonging to Group 13 to 18 and these together with the s-Block \n\nElements are called the Representative Elements or Main Group Elements. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 84; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration Groupwise',NULL,'1','mcq','Main group Elements.','Representative elements.','Transition elements.','Representative Elements or Main Group Elements','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(30512,'chemistry','The elements after Uranium are called ___ elements.','Transuranium','The elements after uranium are called Transuranium Elements.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 84; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration Groupwise',NULL,'1','mcq','Transuranium','Ununnilium','Ununbium','Meitnerium','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(30513,'chemistry','The element Z=117 will be placed in which group number in future?','17','Element with Z = 117, would belong to the halogen family (Group 17) and the electronic configuration would be [Rn] 5f14 6d10 7s2 7p5. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 84; Problem . 3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration Groupwise',NULL,'2','mcq','8','7','17','18','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(30514,'chemistry','How many of the following statements are correct?\n\nA. Noble gases exhibit very high chemical reactivity.\n\nB. Chalcogens and halogens have low electronegativity\n\nC. Lanthanoids are included in  atomic numbers from 58 to 61, while actinoids include atomic numbers from 71 to 103.\n\nD. Actinoids are radioactive elements.','1','Only option D is correct. Actinoid elements are radioactive. \n\nMany of the actinoid elements have been made only in nanogram quantities or even less by nuclear reactions and their chemistry is not fully studied. \n\nLanthanides include atomic numbers 57–71, from lanthanum through lutetium.\n\nThe actinide series starts from atomic number 90 to 103.Noble gases have very low chemical reactivity. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 84; Paragraph 2,3 and 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration Groupwise',NULL,'3','mcq','2','4','1','3','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(30515,'biology','Which is known as the maiden hair tree','Gingko biloba','It is a living fossil.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 39; Figure 3.4 (c))','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Gymnosperms',NULL,'1','fillup','Gi,ng,ko, bi,lo,ba,as,mb',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30516,'biology','Which one is a conifer?','Pinus','All living conifers are woody plants, and most are trees. Living conifers are all in the order Pinales. Typical examples include cedars, cypresses, firs, junipers, kauris, larches, pines. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 39; Figure 3.4 (b))','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Gymnosperms',NULL,'1','mcq','Gnetum','Cycas','Pinus','All of them','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30517,'biology','Which one clearly describes pinus?','Monoecious- male (microsporangiate) and female (megasporangiate) cones are produced on same plant','Pinus is homothallic monoecious.The male or female cones or strobili may be\nborne on the same tree (Pinus). However, in cycas male\ncones and megasporophylls are borne on different trees.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 38 and 39)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Gymnosperms',NULL,'2','mcq','Monoecious- male (microsporangiate) and female (megasporangiate) cones are produced on same plant','Monoecious- male and female sporophylls born on same strobilus','Dioecious - male and female cones are produced on different plants','Monoecious - micro and megasporocarp develop on same plant','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30518,'biology','Megasporangium is equivalent to','Ovule','Megasporangium is equivalent to an ovule. An ovule generally has a single embryo sac formed from a megaspore through reduction division. The megaspore mother cell is differentiated from one of\nthe cells of the nucellus. The nucellus is protected by\nenvelopes and the composite structure is called an\novule. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 39; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Gymnosperms',NULL,'2','mcq','Anther','Ovary','Ovule','Megaspore mother cell','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30519,'biology','The nucellus is protected by envelopes and the composite structure is called ____.','Ovule','The megaspore mother cell is differentiated from one of the cells of the nucellus. The nucellus is protected by envelopes and the composite structure is called an ovule. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 39; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Gymnosperms',NULL,'2','mcq','Antheridium','Ovule','Ovary','Megaspore mother cell','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30520,'biology','Megaspore mother cell is differentiated from one of the cells of the -','Nucellus','The nucellus is rich in nutrients. The megaspore mother cell is differentiated from one of the cells of the nucellus. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 39; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Gymnosperms',NULL,'2','mcq','Nucellus','Ovary','Megaspore','Microspore','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30521,'biology','Megaspore mother cell undergoes ____ division to form megaspore-','Meiotic','Meiosis is a reduction division that occurs in reproducing cells to form gametes/spores. The\nmegaspore mother cell divides meiotically to form four\nmegaspores. One of the megaspores enclosed within the\nmegasporangium develops into a multicellular female\ngametophyte that bears two or more archegonia or\nfemale sex organs. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 39; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Gymnosperms',NULL,'2','mcq','Mitotic','Meiotic','Reductional mitosis','Equivocal meiosis','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30522,'biology','Megaspore develops into a multicellular structure called -','Female gametophyte','The megaspore mother cell divides meiotically to form four megaspores. One of the megaspores enclosed within the megasporangium develops into a multicellular female gametophyte that bears two or more archegonia or female sex organs. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 39; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Gymnosperms',NULL,'2','mcq','Male gametophyte','Female gamete','Female gametophyte','Megaspore mother cell','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30523,'biology','In gymnosperms, the multicellular female gametophyte is retained within -','Megasporangium or ovule','In gymnosperms, The\nmegaspore mother cell divides meiotically to form four\nmegaspores. One of the megaspores enclosed within the\nmegasporangium develops into a multicellular female\ngametophyte that bears two or more archegonia or\nfemale sex organs. The multicellular female gametophyte\nis also retained within megasporangium. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 39; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Gymnosperms',NULL,'1','mcq','Ovary','Microsporangiate cone','Archegonia','Megasporangium or ovule','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30524,'biology','Which among the following is the tallest tree species?','Sequoia','It is the largest or the tallest tree. So it is called the father of the forest.One of the gymnosperms, the giant redwood tree Sequoia is one of the tallest tree species. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 38; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Gymnosperms',NULL,'1','mcq','Sequoia','Mahogany','Banyan','Eucalyptus','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30525,'biology','In Gymnosperms,the pollen grain is -','Reduced male gametophyte','The strobili bearing microsporophylls and microsporangia are called microsporangiate or male strobili. The microspores develop into a male gametophytic generation which is highly reduced and is confined to only a limited number of cells. This reduced gametophyte is called a pollen grain. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 39; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Gymnosperms',NULL,'2','mcq','Female gamete','Reduced male gametophyte','Megaspore','Male cone','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30526,'biology','In Gymnosperms,the development of pollen grains occurs within -','Microsporangium','The development of pollen grain takes place in microsporangium. Where reduction division takes place. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 39; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Gymnosperms',NULL,'2','mcq','Ovary','Ovule','Megasporangium','Microsporangium','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30527,'biology','Which one is CORRECT about male and female gametophytes? \n\n(i)In bryophytes and pteridophytes they have independent free-living existence \n\n(ii)In gymnosperms and angiosperms they have no independent free-living existence \n\n(iii)In bryophytes, pteridophytes and angiosperms they have free-living life','(i), (ii)','Different plant groups exhibit different life cycle. There are two alternating phases in life cycle of every plant. The gametophyte (haploid) and the sporophyte (diploid). When the haploid gametophyte is dominant, photosynthetic and free living/independant  the  it is the haplontic life cycle which is found in algae Volvox, Spirogyra  and some species of Chlaydomonas exhibit this pattern. When the diploid sporophyte is dominant, photosynthetic and independant  it is called diplontic life cycle which is exhibited an alga, Fucus sp., and all seed bearing plants i.e., gymnosperms and angiosperms. Bryophytes and pteridophytes, interestingly, exhibit an intermediate condition (Haplo-diplontic) where both phases are multicellular but they differ in dominant phases. In Bryophytes the haploid gametophyte is dominant, photosynthetic and free living/independant  and the diploid sporophyte is short lived and multicellular. In pteridophytes, the diploid sporophyte dominant, photosynthetic and independant  which alternates with photosynthetic and independant but short lived gametophyte. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 42)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Gymnosperms',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii)','(i), (ii), (iii)','(i), (iii)','(ii), (iii)','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30528,'biology','In Gymnosperms, which of the following terms describe a female sex organ-','Archegonia','One of the megaspores enclosed within the megasporangium develops into a multicellular female gametophyte that bears two or more archegonia or female sex organs.\n (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 39; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Gymnosperms',NULL,'3','mcq','Microgametophyte','Archegonia','Microsporangium','Antheridia','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30529,'biology','In gymnosperms male gametophyte -','Is highly reduced and confined to only a limited number of cells','The microspores develop into a male gametophytic generation which is highly reduced and is confined to only a limited number of cells. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 39; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Gymnosperms',NULL,'1','mcq','Is highly developed','Has an independent life','Is highly reduced and confined to only a limited number of cells','Is produced in macrosporangiate cone','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30530,'biology','In gymnosperms, pollination takes place by-','Wind','The pollen grain is released from the microsporangium. They are carried in air currents and come in contact with the opening of the ovules borne on megasporophylls. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 39; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Gymnosperms',NULL,'2','mcq','Wind','Water','Insects','Animals','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30531,'biology','In gymnosperms, during pollination,pollen grains are transferred to-','Micropyle end of ovule','Ovules are naked in gymnosperms. The gymnosperms (gymnos : naked, sperma : seeds) are plants in which\nthe ovules are not enclosed by any ovary wall and remain exposed, both\nbefore and after fertilisation.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 38; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Gymnosperms',NULL,'3','mcq','Stigma','On archegonia','On ovary','Micropyle end of ovule','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30532,'biology','Identify the diagram (0322)','Gingko','(Reference  : NCERT class 11; Page No 37; Figure 3.4)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Gymnosperms',NULL,'2','mcq','Cycas','Pinus','Gingko','Savia','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/30532q.png'),
(30601,'chemistry','Most of elements in periodic table are:','Metals','Metals comprise more than 78% of all known elements and appear on the left side of the Periodic Table.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 85; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Metals, Non-Metals and Metalloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Non-Metals','Metals','Semi-Metals','Metalloids','NEW','11-085','11-085',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(30602,'chemistry','Which of the following metals is not solid at room temperature?','Mercury','Metals are usually solids at room temperature [mercury is an exception; gallium and caesium also have very low melting points. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 85; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Metals, Non-Metals and Metalloids',NULL,'2','mcq','Mercury','Gallium','Caesium','Germanium','NEW','11-085','11-085',NULL),
(30603,'chemistry','Which of the following property describe metals but not non-metals?','Generally have higher boiling point.','Metals usually have high melting and boiling points. They are good conductors of heat and electricity. They are malleable and ductile. \n\nIn contrast, non-metals are located at the top right hand side of the Periodic Table. In fact, in a horizontal row, the property of elements change from metallic on the left to non-metallic on the right. Non-metals are usually solids or gases at room temperature with low melting and boiling points (boron and carbon are exceptions). \n\nThey are poor conductors of heat and electricity. Most nonmetallic solids are brittle and are neither malleable nor ductile. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 85; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Metals, Non-Metals and Metalloids',NULL,'2','mcq','They are brittle in nature.','Generally have higher boiling point.','They are usually gases at room temperature.','They are poor conductors of electricity.','NEW','11-085','11-085',NULL),
(30604,'chemistry','Which of the following non-metal have high boiling point?','Boron','Non-metals are usually solids or gases at room temperature with low melting and boiling points (boron and carbon are exceptions). \n\nSo it is confirmed that Boron and Carbon have high melting and boiling points. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 85; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Metals, Non-Metals and Metalloids',NULL,'2','mcq','Oxygen','Boron','Sulphur','Caesium','NEW','11-085','11-085',NULL),
(30605,'chemistry','Choose the odd one with respect to semi-metals.','Boron','The elements (ne.g., silicon, germanium, arsenic, antimony and tellurium) bordering this line and running diagonally across the Periodic Table show properties that are characteristic of both metals and non- metals. \n\nThese elements are called Semi-metals or Metalloids.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 85; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Metals, Non-Metals and Metalloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Silicon','Boron','Germanium','Arsenic','NEW','11-085','11-085',NULL),
(30606,'chemistry','Arrange the following elements in the increasing order of metallic character : Ge, Ca, K, Br.','Br < Ge < Ca < K','Metallic character increases down a group and decreases along a period as we move from left to right.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 85; Problem 3.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Metals, Non-Metals and Metalloids',NULL,'3','order','1,Br','2,Ge','3,Ca','4,K','NEW','11-085','11-085',NULL),
(30607,'chemistry','Arrange the following elements in the decreasing order of metallic character:','Na > Mg > Be > Si','Metallic character increases down a group and decreases along a period as we move from left to right. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 85; Problem 3.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Metals, Non-Metals and Metalloids',NULL,'3','order','1,Na','2,Mg','3,Be','4,Si','NEW','11-085','11-085',NULL),
(30608,'chemistry','Match the following:','Representative elements-Be and Fr, Metalloids-Po and As, Metals-Hg and Zr, Non Metals-Kr and Se','The p-Block Elements comprise those belonging to Group 13 to 18 and these together with the s-Block Elements are called the Representative Elements or Main Group Elements. \n\nAs shown in the diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 83; diagram(ne0306)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Metals, Non-Metals and Metalloids',NULL,'3','match','Representative elements,Be and Fr','Metalloids,Po and As','Metals,Hg and Zr','Non Metals,Kr and Se','NEW','11-083','11-083',NULL),
(30609,'chemistry','Elements which show properties of both metals and non-metals are called ____ .','metalloids','Elements which show properties of both metals and non metals are called metalloids. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 85; Paragraph no. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Metals, Non-Metals and Metalloids',NULL,'1','fillup','met,all,oi,ds,non',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-085','11-085',NULL),
(30610,'chemistry','How many of the following statements are correct?\n\nA.Change from metallic to non metallic character is abrupt, if one goes from left to right the period table.\n\nB. Chemical reactivity tends to be high in group 1 and group 17 elements.\n\nC. Reactivity decreases down the group in the group 17.\n\nD. Reactivity increases down the group in group 1.','3','There are many observable patterns in the physical and chemical properties of elements as we descend in a group or move across a period in the Periodic Table. \n\nFor example, within a period, chemical reactivity tends to be high in Group 1 metals, lower in elements towards the middle of the table, and increases to a maximum in the Group 17 non-metals. \n\nLikewise, within a group of representative metals (say alkali metals) reactivity increases on moving down the group, whereas within a group of non-metals (say halogens), reactivity decreases down the group. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 85; Paragraph no. 1,2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Metals, Non-Metals and Metalloids',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-085','11-085',NULL),
(30611,'biology','The male sex organ in a flower is _____','Stamen','In angiosperms, The male sex organ in a flower is the stamen. Each stamen consists of a slender filament with an anther at the tip. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 40; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Angiosperms',NULL,'2','mcq','Stamen','Ovules','Pollen grain','Stigma','OLD',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30612,'biology','Stamen consists of \n(i)Slender filaments  \n\n(ii)Anther at tip \n\n(iii) Anther, filaments and style','(i), (ii)','The male sex organ in a flower is the stamen. Each stamen consists of a slender filament with an anther at the tip. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 40; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Angiosperms',NULL,'2','mcq','(i), (ii), (iii)','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','(i), (ii)','(ii), (iii), (iv)','OLD',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30613,'biology','Pollen mother cells are present in','In anthers','Pollen mother cells are present within the anthers. Within the anthers, the pollen\nmother cell divide by meiosis to produce microspores which matures\ninto pollen grains. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 40; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Angiosperms',NULL,'1','mcq','In anthers','In pollen tube','At the middle of stamen','In ovary','OLD',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30614,'biology','Microspores mature into','Pollen grains','Within the anthers, the pollen mother cell divide to produce microspores which mature into pollen grains(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 40; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Angiosperms',NULL,'1','mcq','Pollen grains','Flowers','Seeds','Egg cell','OLD',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30615,'biology','The type of division taking place in pollen mother cell to produce pollen is','Meiosis','Meiosis is a reduction division that takes place in the pollen mother cell to produce pollen grains. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 40; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Angiosperms',NULL,'1','mcq','Meiosis','Mitosis','Replicative mitosis','Equivocal meiosis','OLD',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30616,'biology','Pistil consists of','A swollen ovary at its base, a long slender style and stigma','The female sex organ in a flower is the pistil. Pistil\nconsists of a swollen ovary at its base, a long slender style and stigma.\nInside the ovary, ovules are present. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 40; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Angiosperms',NULL,'1','mcq','A swollen ovary at its base, a long slender style and stigma','A swollen ovary at its base, a long slender stamen and stigma','A swollen style at its base, a long slender style and stigma','A swollen ovary at its base, a long slender style and anthers','OLD',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30617,'biology','The female sex organ in a flower is','Pistil','The female sex organ in a flower is the pistil. Pistil\nconsists of a swollen ovary at its base, a long slender style and stigma.\nInside the ovary, ovules are present. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 40; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Angiosperms',NULL,'2','mcq','Pistil','Stamen','Floral whorls','Pedicle','OLD',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30618,'biology','Ovules are present in ______and within the ovules _______is present','Ovary, megaspore mother cell','Inside the ovary, ovules are present. Generally each ovule has a\nmegaspore mother cell that undergoes meiosis to form four haploid\nmegaspores. Three of them degenerate and one divide to form the embryo\nsac. Each embryo-sac has a three-celled egg apparatus – one egg cell\nand two synergids, three antipodal cells and two polar nuclei. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 40; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Angiosperms',NULL,'2','mcq','Pistle,microspore mother cell','Stamen, megaspore mother cell','Ovary, megaspore mother cell','Ovary, ovary','OLD',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30619,'biology','Megaspore mother cell by meiosis produces ______','4 haploid megaspores','Inside the ovary, ovules are present. Generally each ovule has a\nmegaspore mother cell that undergoes meiosis to form four haploid\nmegaspores. Three of them degenerate and one divide to form the embryo\nsac. Each embryo-sac has a three-celled egg apparatus – one egg cell\nand two synergids, three antipodal cells and two polar nuclei. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 40; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Angiosperms',NULL,'2','mcq','4 diploid megaspores','4 diploid microspores','4 haploid microspores','4 haploid megaspores','OLD',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30620,'biology','Match the following (according to the fate of four haploid cells produced by megaspore mother cell)','3-degenerate, 1-divides to form embryo sac','Inside the ovary, ovules are present. Generally each ovule has a\nmegaspore mother cell that undergoes meiosis to form four haploid\nmegaspores. Three of them degenerate and one divide to form the embryo\nsac. Each embryo-sac has a three-celled egg apparatus – one egg cell\nand two synergids, three antipodal cells and two polar nuclei. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 40; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Angiosperms',NULL,'3','match','3,degenerate','1,divides to form embryo sac',NULL,NULL,'OLD',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30621,'biology','The embryo sac consists of \n(i)3 celled Egg apparatus  \n\n(ii)Three Antipodal cells \n\n(iii)Two polar nuclei \n\n(iv)Two Synergids','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','Inside the ovary, ovules are present. Generally each ovule has a\nmegaspore mother cell that undergoes meiosis to form four haploid\nmegaspores. Three of them degenerate and one divide to form the embryo\nsac. Each embryo-sac has a three-celled egg apparatus – one egg cell\nand two synergids, three antipodal cells and two polar nuclei. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 40; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Angiosperms',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii), (iii)','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','(i), (ii), (iv)','(i), (ii)','OLD',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30622,'biology','The Egg apparatus consists of','1 egg cell and 2 synergids','Inside the ovary, ovules are present. Generally each ovule has a\nmegaspore mother cell that undergoes meiosis to form four haploid\nmegaspores. Three of them degenerate and one divide to form the embryo\nsac. Each embryo-sac has a three-celled egg apparatus – one egg cell\nand two synergids, three antipodal cells and two polar nuclei. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 40; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Angiosperms',NULL,'3','mcq','1 egg cell and 2 synergids','1 nuclei and 2 synergids','3 antipodals','Two polar nuclei and 1 egg cell','OLD',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30623,'biology','Polar nuclei fuse to form','Diploid secondary nucleus','The polar nuclei eventually fuse to produce a diploid secondary nucleus (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 41; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Angiosperms',NULL,'3','mcq','Diploid secondary nucleus','Haplodiploid secondary nucleus','diploid primary nucleus','Haploid secondary nucleus','OLD',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30624,'biology','Choose the incorrect statement','Synergids and antipodals become seeds after fertilisation','Pollen tubes enter the embryo sac where two male gametes are discharged. One of the male gametes fuses with the egg cell (syngamy) to form a zygote. The other male gamete fuses with the diploid secondary nucleus to produce the triploid primary endosperm nucleus (PEN). Because of the occurrence of two fusions i.e., syngamy and triple fusion, this event is termed double fertilization, an event unique to angiosperms. After fertilization synergids and antipodals cells degenerate. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 41; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Angiosperms',NULL,'3','mcq','One of the male gamete fuses with diploid secondary nucleus to form primary endosperm nucleus','One of the male gametes fuses with egg cell called as syngamy','Double fertilisation consists formation of syngamy and triple fusion','Synergids and antipodals become seeds after fertilisation','OLD',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30625,'biology','Unique event in angiosperm is','Double fertilisation','Because of the occurrence of two fusions i.e., syngamy and triple fusion, this event is termed double fertilization, an event unique to angiosperms. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 41; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Angiosperms',NULL,'2','mcq','Double fertilisation','Flowering','Syngamy','Meiosis','OLD',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30626,'biology','Match the following based on their fate','Ovary-fruits, Ovules-seeds','In angiosperms after fertilization ovules develop into seeds and ovary into fruit(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 41; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Angiosperms',NULL,'2','match','Ovary,fruits','Ovules,seeds',NULL,NULL,'OLD',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30627,'biology','A natural classification system for flowering plants was given by?','George Bentham and Joseph Dalton Hooker','Natural classification systems developed were based on natural affinities among the organisms and considered, not only the external features, but also internal features, like ultra-structure, anatomy, embryology and phytochemistry. Such a classification for flowering plants was given by George Bentham and Joseph Dalton Hooker.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 29,30; paragraph No.1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Angiosperms',NULL,'2','mcq','George Bentham and Joseph Dalton Hooker','R.H. Whittaker','Carolus Linnaeus','Aristotle','OLD',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30628,'biology','Which of the following is termed as pollination?','Pollen grain carried by wind to the stigma of a pistil.','Pollen grain, after dispersal from the anthers, are carried by wind or various other agencies\nto the stigma of a pistil. This is termed as pollination.\n(ref. NCERT biology class 11; page 41; Paragraph 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Angiosperms',NULL,'1','mcq','Pollen mother cell dividing by meiosis to produce microspores.','Microspore maturing into pollen grain.','Pollen grain carried by wind to the stigma of a pistil.','Pollen grains germinating on the stigma.','OLD',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30629,'biology','In the given diagram,  a and b refers to the chromosomal makeup in the lifecycle of Angiosperms. What is ploidy in a and b\n(0323)','2n generation and n generation','Sporophytic(2n)  and gametophytic (n )generation (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 41; Figure 3.6)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Angiosperms',NULL,'2','mcq','2n generation and n generation','n generation and 2n generation','2n generation and 3n generation','None of the Above','OLD',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/30629q.png'),
(30701,'chemistry','Arrange the following elements in the increasing order of atomic radii:','S < P < Si < Al','As shown in the diagram \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 86; (ne0309)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Atomic radius',NULL,'3','order','1,S','2,P','3,Si','4,Al','NEW','11-086','11-086',NULL),
(30702,'chemistry','Match the the following elements to their atomic radius:','F-64 pm,Cl-99 pm,At-140 pm,I-133 pm','As shown in the diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 86; (ne0309)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Atomic radius',NULL,'3','match','F,64 pm','Cl,99 pm','At,140 pm','I,133 pm','NEW','11-086','11-086',NULL),
(30703,'chemistry','Which of the following parameters should be used when comparing size of a noble gas with some other element?','Van Der Waals radius','Note that the atomic radii of noble gases are not considered here. \n\nBeing monoatomic, their (non-bonded radii) values are very large. \n\nIn fact radii of noble gases should be compared not with the covalent radii but with the van der Waals radii of other elements. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 86; Paragraph no. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Atomic radius',NULL,'1','mcq','Covalent radius','Ionic radius','Van Der Waals radius','Co-ordinate radius','NEW','11-086','11-086',NULL),
(30704,'chemistry','The distance between two adjacent copper atoms in solid copper is 256 pm. It’s metallic radius is __ pm .','128','For metals, we define the term “Metallic Radius” which is taken as half the internuclear distance separating the metal cores in the metallic crystal. \n\nFor example, the distance between two adjacent copper atoms in solid copper is 256 pm; hence the metallic radius of copper is assigned a value of 128 pm. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 86; Paragraph no. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Atomic radius',NULL,'2','fillup','2,4,8,1,3,7',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-086','11-086',NULL),
(30705,'chemistry','Atomic radius can be measured by:','X-Ray diffraction technique','Atomic radii can be measured by X-ray or other spectroscopic methods. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 86; Paragraph no. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Atomic radius',NULL,'1','mcq','Millikan’s oil drop method','X-Ray diffraction technique','Vernier Calliper','Gamma Ray Scattering Technique','NEW','11-086','11-086',NULL),
(30706,'chemistry','Assertion: Atomic radius increases regularly with atomic number within a vertical column.\n\nReason:  Inner energy levels are filled with electrons, which serve to shield the outer electrons from the pull of the nucleus.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion.','Within a family or vertical column of the periodic table, the atomic radius increases regularly with atomic number as illustrated in Fig. 3.4(b). \n\nFor alkali metals and halogens, as we descend the groups, the principal quantum number (n) increases and the valence electrons are farther from the nucleus. This happens because the inner energy levels are filled with electrons, which serve to shield the outer electrons from the pull of the nucleus. Consequently the size of the atom increases as reflected in the atomic radii.\n\n(Reference; NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 86; Paragraph no. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Atomic radius',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion and reason both are wrong statements.','NEW','11-086','11-086',NULL),
(30707,'chemistry','Size of an atom is  about _____ in radius.','1.2 A','The size of an atom (~ 1.2 Å i.e., 1.2 × 10⁻¹⁰ m in radius) is very small. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 85; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Atomic radius',NULL,'1','mcq','1.2 A','2.1 A','10.2 A','12.1 A','NEW','11-085','11-085',NULL),
(30708,'chemistry','___ radius is the distance between the atoms bound by covalent bond in a molecule.','Covalent','One practical approach to estimate the size of an atom of a non-metallic element is to measure the distance between two atoms when they are bound together by a single bond in a covalent molecule and from this value, the “Covalent Radius” of the element can be calculated. \n\nFor example, the bond distance in the chlorine molecule (Cl2) is 198 pm and half this distance (99 pm), is taken as the atomic radius of chlorine.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 86; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Atomic radius',NULL,'1','mcq','Covalent','Coordinate Covalent','Metallic','Ionic','NEW','11-086','11-086',NULL),
(30709,'chemistry','Find out the incorrect statement regarding atom:','There are practical ways by which size of individual atom can be measured.','The electron cloud surrounding the atom does not have a sharp boundary, the determination of the atomic size cannot be precise. \n\nIn other words, there is no practical way by which the size of an individual atom can be measured. \nThe size of an atom (~ 1.2 Å i.e., 1.2 × 10⁻¹⁰ m in radius) is very small. \n\nHowever, an estimate of the atomic size can be made by knowing the distance between the atoms in the combined state. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 86; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Atomic radius',NULL,'2','mcq','Determination of atomic size cannot be precise.','Size of atom is about 1.2 A in radius.','An estimate of the atomic size can be made by knowing the distance between the atoms in the combined state.','There are practical ways by which size of individual atom can be measured.','NEW','11-086','11-086','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/30709q.png'),
(2000044578,'physics','Light of wavelength $$\\lambda$$ which is less than threshold wavelength is incident on a photosensitive material. If incident wavelength is decreased, then stopping potential will','A','According to photoelectric equation<br>$\\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } - \\phi  = \\frac{1}{2}m{v^2} = e{V_S}$<br>Symbols have their usual meaning<br>If we decrease the wavelength $$\\lambda$$, then stopping potential ${V_S}$ will increase.','Photoelectric Effect','Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter','Easy',NULL,'Increase','Decrease','Be zero','Become exactly half',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30710,'chemistry','Mark the correct size of an atom:','~ 1.2 Å','You can very well imagine that finding the size of an atom is a lot more complicated than measuring the radius of a ball. \n\nDo you know why? Firstly, because the size of an atom (~ 1.2 Å i.e., 1.2 × 10⁻¹⁰ m in radius) is very small. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 85; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Atomic radius',NULL,'1','mcq','~ 1.2 Å','~ 1.12 Å','~ 12.2 Å','~ 2.2 Å','NEW','11-085','11-085','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/30710q.png'),
(30801,'chemistry','Arrange the elements and ions in decreasing order of their radius:','Mg > Li > Li⁺ > Mg²⁺','It is shown in the diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 93; diagram(ne0307)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionic Radius',NULL,'3','order','1,Mg','2,Li','3,Li⁺','4,Mg²⁺','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(30802,'chemistry','What are isoelectronic species?','Two or more species with same number of atoms, same number of valence electrons and same structure regardless of the nature of elements involved.','Two or more species with same number of atoms, same number of valence electrons and same structure regardless of the nature of elements involved are isoelectronic. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 87; Footnote)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionic Radius',NULL,'2','mcq','Two or more species with same number of valence electrons.','Two or more species with same structure and same number of atoms.','Two or more neutral atoms with same number of atoms, same number of valence electrons with different structure.','Two or more species with same number of atoms, same number of valence electrons and same structure regardless of the nature of elements involved.','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(30803,'chemistry','N⁻³ and Na⁺ have same ____________.','No. of electrons','Elements having same number of valence electrons are isoelectronic. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 87; Footnote)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionic Radius',NULL,'1','mcq','Atomic no.','Mass No.','No. of electrons','No. of neutrons','NEW','11-080','11-080',NULL),
(30804,'chemistry','For any general element A, the decreasing order of its ionic radius in its different forms will be?','A⁻ > A > A⁺ > A⁺²','In general, the ionic radii of elements exhibit the same trend as the atomic radii. \n\nA cation is smaller than its parent atom because it has fewer electrons while its nuclear charge remains the same. \n\nThe size of an anion will be larger than that of the parent atom because the addition of one or more electrons would result in increased repulsion among the electrons and a decrease in effective nuclear charge.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 87; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionic Radius',NULL,'3','order','1,A⁻','2,A','3,A⁺','4,A⁺²','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(30805,'chemistry','Arrange the following species in their increasing order of their size?','Al³⁺ < Mg²⁺ < Al < Mg','As shown in diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 80; diagram(ne0308)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionic Radius',NULL,'3','order','1,Al³⁺','2,Mg²⁺','3,Al','4,Mg','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(30806,'chemistry','The removal of an electron from an atom results in the formation of ____.','Cation','The removal of an electron from an atom results in the formation of a cation, whereas gain of an electron leads to an anion.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 87; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionic Radius',NULL,'1','mcq','Cation','Anion','Cathode','Anode','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(30807,'chemistry','The gain of an electron leads to _____.','Anion','The removal of an electron from an atom results in the formation of a cation, whereas gain of an electron leads to an anion. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 87; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionic Radius',NULL,'1','mcq','Cation','Anion','Cathode','Anode','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(30808,'chemistry','In isoelectric species, Atomic radii would be different because of their different ___.','Nuclear charges','When we find some atoms and ions which contain the same number of electrons, we call them isoelectronic species*. \n\nFor example,N 3-, O 2–, F– , Na⁺ and Mg²⁺ have the same number of electrons (10). \n\nTheir radii would be different because of their different nuclear charges. More is the nuclear charge smaller is the size\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 87; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionic Radius',NULL,'1','mcq','Nuclear charges','Energy','State','Electron','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(30809,'biology','Which one of the following statements is wrong? \n[NEET 2015 cancelled]','Mannitol is stored food in rhodophyceae.','Food is stored as complex carbohydrates, which may be in the\nform of laminarin or mannitol in brown algae or phaeophyceae. Rhodophyceae algae store food in the form of floridean starch. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 33; Table 3.1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Chlorella and spirulina are used as space food.','Mannitol is stored food in rhodophyceae.','Algin and carrageenan are products of algae.','Agaragar is obtained from gelidium andgracilaria','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30810,'biology','In which of the following, the gametophyte is not independent free living? \n[NEET 2015 cancelled]','Pinus','In gymnosperms (like Pinus), the male and female gametophytes do not have an independent free-living existence. They remain within the sporangia retained on the sporophytes i.e., the female gametophyte (within megasporangium) and male gametophyte (within microsporangium). (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 39; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Pteris','Pinus','Funaria','Marchantia','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30811,'biology','Read the following five statements (a to e) and select the option with all correct statements.\n a. mosses and lichens are the first organisms to colonize bare rock. \n\nb. Selaginella is a homosporous pteridophyte. \n\nc. Coralloid roots in cycas have vam.\n\nd. Main plant body in bryophytes is gametophytic, whereas in pteridophytes it is sporophytic. \n\ne. In gymnosperms, male and female gametophytes are present within sporangia located on the sporophyte. \n\n[NEET 2015 cancelled]','a, d and e','Selaginella is a heterosporous pteridophyte.Genera like Selaginella and Salvinia which produce two kinds of spores, macro (large) and micro (small) spores, are known as heterosporous. Coralloid roots of Cycas harbor blue-green algae like Nostoc, and Anabaena and are not a type of VAM (vesicular-arbuscular mycorrhiza) which is a symbiotic association with fungus. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 38)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','a, d and e','b, c and e','a, c and d','b, c and d','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30812,'biology','Male gametes are flagellated in [NEET 2015 cancelled]','Ectocarpus','Ectocarpus produces biflagellate gametes. The gametes are pyriform (pear-shaped) and bear two laterally attached flagella. The common forms are Ectocarpus, Dictyota, Laminaria, Sargassum and Fucus. Anabaena is a cyanobacterium and does not reproduce sexually. Spirogyra produces a non flagellated male gamete during conjugation, where entire cell content functions as a gamete. Polysiphonia also produces non-flagellated spermatia. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 33; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Ectocarpus','Spirogyra','Polysiphonia','Anabaena','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30813,'biology','Which one of the following is wrong about chara? [AIPMT 2014]','Upper antheridium and lower oogonium','All species of Chara reproduce sexually and show highly advanced oogamy. The sex organs are the most distinctive features of the Order Charales and are the most complicated among the thallophytes. Male and female gametangia are called antheridia and oogonia respectively. Male fructification (cluster of antheridia) is called globule and the female is nucule. They are borne at the nodes of short branches, globule towards the lower side, and nucule (female structure) towards the upper side.','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Upper oogonium and lower round antheridium','Globule and nucule present on the same plant','Upper antheridium and lower oogonium','Globule is male reproductive structure','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30814,'biology','Which of the following is responsible for peat formation? [AIPMT 2014]','Sphagnum','Among the bryophytes, Sphagnum accounts by far the most important place economically. It is popularly called bog moss or peat moss. It is perennial and its growth continues year after year. Older portions undergo death but do not decompose due to the secretion of acid that accounts for the antibacterial and antifungal actions. The increasing mass of dead remains accumulates year after year and forms a compact dark-colored mass rich in carbon which is called peat. Peat is used as fuel. Paraffin, acetic acid, peat tar, and ammonia are formed as byproducts of peat obtained for industrial uses. Species of Sphagnum, a moss, provide peat that have long been used as\nfuel, and as packing material for trans-shipment of living material because\nof their capacity to hold water.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Marchantia','Riccia','Funaria','Sphagnum','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30815,'biology','Male gametophyte with the least number of cells is present in [AIPMT 2014]','Lilium','Pteris has a multicellular gametophytic prothallus which has both antheridia and archegonia. Funaria has a bisexual leafy gametophyte which is the dominant phase of life. In both Lilium (an angiosperm) and Pinus (a gymnosperm), the male gametophyte is highly reduced and is dependent on the sporophyte.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 40; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Pteris','Funaria','Lilium','Pinus','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30816,'biology','Select the wrong statement. [NEET 2013]','In oomycetes, female gamete is smaller and motile, while male gamete is larger and nonmotile.','In oomycetes, like other oogamous organisms female gamete is large and nonmotile, while the male gamete is small and motile.Fusion between one large, nonmotile\n(static) female gamete and a smaller, motile male gamete is termed\noogamous, e.g., Volvox, Fucus.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 30; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','In oomycetes, female gamete is smaller and motile, while male gamete is larger and nonmotile.','Chlamydomonas exhibits both isogamy and anisogamy and fucus shows oogamy.','Isogametes are similar in structure, function and behaviour.','Anisogametes differ either in structure, function or behaviour','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30817,'biology','Isogamous condition with non flagellated gametes is found in [NEET 2013]','Spirogyra','Chlamydomonas have flagellated gametes that are similar or dissimilar in size. In Volvox and Fucus, nonmotile female gametes and motile male gametes are produced (oogamy). Spirogyra has gametes that are similar in size (isogamous) and are non flagellated.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 30; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Volvox','Fucus','Chlamydomonas','Spirogyra','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30818,'biology','Monoecious plant of chara shows the occurrence of [NEET 2013]','Upper oogonium and lower antheridium on the same plant','All species of Chara reproduce sexually and show highly advanced oogamy. The sex organs are the most distinctive features of the Order Charales and are the most complicated among the thallophytes. Male and female gametangia are called antheridia and oogonia respectively. Male fructification (cluster of antheridia) is called globule and the female is nucule. They are borne at the nodes of short branches, globule towards the lower side, and nucule (female structure) towards the upper side.','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Upper antheridium and lower oogonium on the same plant','Upper oogonium and lower antheridium on the same plant','Antheridiophore and archegoniophore on the same plant','Stamen and carpel on the same plant','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30819,'biology','Read the following statements (a e)and answer the question which follows them.\n \n(a) in liverworts, mosses and ferns gametophytes are free-living. \n\n(b) Gymnosperms and some ferns are heterosporous. \n\n(c) Sexual reproduction in fucus, volvox, and Albugo is oogamous. \n\n(d) The sporophyte in liverworts is more elaborate than that in mosses. \n\n(e) Both pinus and Marchantia are dioecious. \n\nHow many of the above statements are correct?\n [NEET 2013]','Three','a) A dominant, independent, photosynthetic, thalloid, or erect phase is represented by a haploid gametophyte and it alternates with the short-lived multicellular sporophyte totally or partially dependent on the gametophyte for its anchorage and nutrition. All bryophytes represent\nthis pattern.\nb) Genera like Selaginella and Salvinia which produce two kinds of spores, macro (large) and micro (small) spores, are known as heterosporous. The gymnosperms are heterosporous.\nc) Fusion between one large, nonmotile (static) female gamete and a smaller, motile male gamete is termed oogamous, e.g., Volvox, Fucus.\nd) The sporophyte in mosses is more elaborate than that in liverworts. \ne) The male or female cones or strobili may be borne on the same plant in Pinus, however Marchantia is dioecious.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page No. 30, 32, 35, 36, 38 and 39)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Three','Four','One','Two','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30820,'biology','What is common in all the three, funaria, Dryopteris, and ginkgo? [Karnataka NEET 2013]','Presence of archegonia','In Funaria (Bryophyta), Dryopteris (Pteridophyta), and Ginkgo (Gymnosperm) female sex organ archegonium are formed. Funaria lacks independent sporophyte and vascular tissues while independent gametophyte is absent in Ginkgo. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 36, 38, and 39)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Presence of archegonia','Well developed vascular tissues','Independent gametophyte','Independent sporophyte','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30821,'biology','Which one of the following is wrongly matched? [Karnataka NEET 2013]','Spirogyra motile gametes','In Spirogyra, gametes are nonmotile and sexual reproduction takes place by conjugation. Sargassum belongs to the Phaeophyceae group of algae. They are commonly called as ‘brown algae’ and contain photosynthetic pigments chlorophyll a and c. Puffballs are Basidiomycetes with a stalked rounded structure that sends out puffs of spores, e.g., Lycoperdon oblongisporum. Nostoc is a colonial cyanobacterium. It enriches its habitat with nitrogen by fixing atmospheric nitrogen and also causes water bloom. Sexual reproduction\ntakes place through fusion of two gametes. These gametes can be\nflagellated and similar in size (as in Ulothrix) or non-flagellated (non-motile)\nbut similar in size (as in Spirogyra).(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 30; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Spirogyra motile gametes','Sargassum chlorophyll','Basidiomycetes puffballs','Nostoc water blooms','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30822,'biology','The plant body is thalloid in \n[Karnataka NEET 2013]','Marchantia','Sphagnum and Funaria belong to Class Bryopsida of Division Bryophyta. They are typically mosses. The plant body has radial symmetry and is essentially leafy. Salvinia belongs to the division Pteridophyta. It has a sporophyte plant body with true leaves, stems, and roots. Marchantia belongs to Class Hepaticopsida of Division Bryophyta. They are also called liverworts. The plant body is a dorsoventrally flattened thallus. The plant body of a liverwort is thalloid, e.g., Marchantia. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Sphagnum','Salvinia','Marchantia','Funaria','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30823,'biology','Which one of the following is common to multicellular fungi, filamentous algae, and protonema of mosses? [AIPMT 2012]','Multiplication by fragmentation','Algae and moss are included in plant kingdom while fungi constitute a separate kingdom. Among them, mosses invariably show diplontic life cycle while others may or may not. Algae and moss are autotrophic while fungi are heterotrophs. But they all show multiplication by fragmentation.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 30; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Diplontic life cycle','Members of kingdom plantae','Mode of nutrition','Multiplication by fragmentation','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30824,'biology','Which one of the following is a correct statement? [AIPMT 2012]','Origin of seed habit can be traced in pteridophytes','In the majority of the pteridophytes, all the spores are of similar kinds; such plants are called homosporous. Genera like Selaginella and Salvinia which produce two kinds of spores, macro (large) and micro (small) spores, are known as heterosporous. The megaspores and microspores germinate and give rise to female and male gametophytes, respectively. The female gametophytes in these plants are retained on the parent sporophytes for variable periods. The development of the zygotes into young embryos takes place within the female gametophytes. This event is a precursor to the seed habit considered an important step in evolution. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 38; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Pteridophyte gametophyte has a protonemal and leafy stage.','In gymnosperms, female gametophyte isfreeliving.','Antheridiophores and archegoniophores are present in pteridophytes.','Origin of seed habit can be traced in pteridophytes','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30825,'biology','Cycas and adiantum resemble each other in having [AIPMT 2012]','Motile sperms','Cycas is a gymnosperm and Adiantum is a pteridophyte. Cambium and seeds are absent in pteridophytes, while vessels are absent in both of these two groups. Both Cycas and Adiantum resemble each other in having multiciliated sperms. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 38)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Seeds','Motile sperms','Cambium','Vessels','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30826,'biology','Which one of the following pairs is wrongly matched? [AIPMT Mains 2012]','Salvinia – prothallus','A prothallus is used to describe the gametophyte stage in the \nlifecycle of a Fern or any Pteridophyte. Prothallus is developed from a \ngerminating spore. It is homosporous in nature and contains both male \nand female sex organs known as antheridia and archegonia respectively.','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Ginkgo – archegonia','Salvinia – prothallus','Viroids – RNA','Mustard – synergids','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30827,'biology','Read the following five statements (a e)and answer as asked next to them.\n(a) in equisetum, the female gametophyte is retained on the parent sporophyte.\n\n(b) in ginkgo, male gametophyte is not independent.\n\n(c) the sporophyte in riccia is more developed than that in polytrichum.\n\n(d) sexual reproduction in volvox is isogamous.\n\n(e) the spores of slime moulds lack cell walls.\n\nhow many of the above statements are correct? [AIPMT Mains 2012]','One','Equisetum is a pteridophyte and in pteridophytes, the main plant body is a sporophyte which is differentiated into true root, stem, and leaves. Gametophytes are small or inconspicuous and free-living, mostly photosynthetic thalloid called prothallus. Riccia is a liverwort and Polytrichum is a moss. The sporophyte in mosses is more elaborate than that in liverworts. Volvox shows an oogamous type of sexual reproduction, i.e., the fusion between one large, non-motile (static) female gamete and a smaller, motile male gamete. During unfavorable conditions, the slime mould differentiates and forms fruiting bodies bearing spores at their tips. The spores possess true walls. They are extremely resistant and survive for many years, even under adverse conditions.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 36; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Two','Three','Four','One','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30828,'biology','How many organisms in the list given below are autotrophs?\n\nLactobacillus, nostoc, chara, Nitrosomonas,Nitrobacter, streptomyces, saccharomyces,Trypanosoma, Porphyra, Wolffia \n\n[AIPMT Mains 2012]','Six','Autotrophic nutrition involves the manufacture of organic materials from inorganic raw materials with the help of energy obtained from outside sources. It is of two types – chemosynthesis and photosynthesis. The organisms which can manufacture their organic food from inorganic raw materials with the help of energy derived from exergonic chemical reactions are called chemoautotrophs. Nitrosomonas and Nitrobacter are chemoautotrophic nitrifying bacteria. Those organisms that can manufacture organic compounds from inorganic raw materials with the help of solar energy in the presence of photosynthetic pigments are called photoautotrophs. E.g., Nostoc, Chara, Porphyra and Wolffia.','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Four','Five','Six','Three','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30829,'biology','The gametophyte is not an independent, free-living generation in \n[AIPMT 2011]','Pinus','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 39; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Polytrichum','Adiantum','Marchantia','Pinus','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30830,'biology','Compared with the gametophytes of the bryophytes, the gametophytes of vascular plants tend to be [AIPMT 2011]','Smaller and to have smaller sex organs','In bryophytes, the dominant phase of the life cycle is the gametophytic plant body. In contrast, vascular plants have sporophytic plant bodies in most of their life cycle and reduced, smaller gametophytes which have smaller sex organs. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 39; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Smaller but to have larger sex organs','Larger but to have smaller sex organs','Larger and to have larger sex organs','Smaller and to have smaller sex organs','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30831,'biology','Archegoniophore is present in[AIPMT 2011]','Marchantia','Marchantia is a dioecious plant. Male plants bear antheridiophores and female plants bear archegoniophores. Antheridiophores consist of a stalk and a disc-like portion called a receptacle. Archegoniophore is composed of a stalk and disc-like receptacle at its distal end. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 36; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Marchantia','Chara','Adiantum','Funaria','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30832,'biology','Prokaryotic autotrophic nitrogen-fixing symbiont is found in [AIPMT 2011]','Cycas','Cycas form a facultative symbiotic association with autotrophic nitrogen-fixing cyanobacteria. Cycas provide fixed carbon and a stable environment to the cyanobacteria in exchange for fixed nitrogen. These cyanobacteria are endosymbionts and live within the roots of Cycas. In addition to normal roots, Cycas develops specialized symbiotic organs at a young age called precoralloid roots which transform into coralloid roots upon successful colonization by cyanobacteria. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 38; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Alnus','Cycas','Cicer','Pisum','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30833,'biology','Selaginella and Salvinia are considered to represent a significant step toward the evolution of seed habit because [AIPMT mains 2011]','Embryo develops in female gametophyte which is retained on parent sporophyte','They are heterosporous. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 38; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Female gametophyte is free and gets dispersed like seeds.','Female gametophyte lacks archegonia.','Megaspores possess endosperm and embryo surrounded by seed coat.','Embryo develops in female gametophyte which is retained on parent sporophyte','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30834,'biology','Consider the following four statements whether they are correct or wrong. \na. the sporophyte in liverworts is more elaborate than that in mosses. \n\nb. Salvinia is heterosporous. \n\nc. the lifecycle in all seed-bearing plants are diplontic.\n\nd. in pinus male and female cones are borne on different trees.\n\n the two wrong statements together are [AIPMT mains 2011]','A and D','The sporophyte in mosses is more elaborate than that in liverworts. The male and female cones or strobili are borne on the same tree in Pinus. In Cycas male cones and megasporophylls are borne on different trees. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 36; Paragraph No. 3).','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','A and C','A and D','B and C','A and B','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30835,'biology','Algae have cells made up of \n[AIPMT 2010]','Cellulose, galactans and mannans','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 33; Table 3.1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Cellulose, galactans and mannans','Hemicellulose, pectins and proteins','Pectins, cellulose and proteins','Cellulose, hemicellulose and pectins','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30836,'biology','Which one of the following is monoecious?[AIPMT 2010]','Pinus','Monoecious plants have separate male and female flowers on the same plant. Pinus have both the male and female cones or strobili on the same tree. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 39; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Marchantia','Cycas','Pinus','Date palm','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30837,'biology','Which one of the following is a vascular cryptogam? [AIPMT Mains 2010]','Equisetum','Pteridophytes are known as vascular cryptogams (Gk Kryptos = hidden + gamos= wedded). They reproduce by spores rather than seeds. They are the first vascular land plant. The pteridophyte Equisetum belongs to the Class Sphenophtya. All vegetative parts of it possess vascular tissues (i.e. hadrome equivalent to xylem and leptome equivalent to phloem) organized in definite groups of steles. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 38; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Ginkgo','Marchantia','Cedrus','Equisetum','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30838,'biology','Mannitol is the stored food in [AIPMT 2009]','Fucus','Fucus is a brown alga i.e. belongs to Class Phaeophyta. In this alga, the accumulation product of photosynthesis is Dmannitol (sugar alcohol) and the reserve food material is laminarin.','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Porphyra','Fucus','Gracillaria','Chara','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30839,'biology','Which one of the following is considered important in the development of seed habit? [AIPMT 2009]','Heterospory','The differentiation of spores into microspores and megaspores, and their dependence on the parent sporophyte for nutrition, are certain features in the life cycle of Selaginella, which have been considered as the essential prerequisites for the formation of seeds, characteristic of spermatophytes. It is generally agreed, that the seed plants arose from the heterosporous vascular plants that instead of discharging the megaspore acquired the habit of retaining it within the megasporangium.','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Heterospory','Haplontic life cycle','Freeliving gametophyte','Dependent sporophyte','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/30839q.png'),
(30840,'biology','Which one of the following has a haplontic life cycle?  [AIPMT 2009]','Funaria','Funaria exhibits gametophytic (n) as well as sporophytic (2n). (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 43; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Polytrichum','Ustilago','Wheat','Funaria','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30841,'biology','Which one of the following plants is monoecious?  [AIPMT 2009]','Pinus','The male or female cones or strobili may be borne on the same tree (Pinus).(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 39; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Pinus','Cycas','Papaya','Marchantia','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30842,'biology','Select one of the following pairs of important features distinguishing gnetum from cycas and pinus and showing affinities with angiosperms. [AIPMT 2009]','Presence of vessel elements and absence of archegonia','In gymnosperm except for Order Gnetales (Gnetum) xylem consists of xylem parenchyma and tracheids with bordered pits but lacks vessels. So, Gnetales are the most advanced among gymnosperms. They lack archegonia in female gametophytes thus showing similarity with angiosperm and act as a connecting link between the two.','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Perianth and two integuments','Embryo development and apical meristem','Absence of resin duct and leaf venation','Presence of vessel elements and absence of archegonia','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30843,'biology','In which one of the following male and female gametophytes do not have a free-living independent existence? [AIPMT 2008]','Cedrus','In gymnosperm (like Cedrus) the male and female gametophytes do not have an independent free-living existence. They remain within the sporangia retained on the sporophytes i.e., female gametophyte with megasporangium and male gametophyte within microsporangium. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 39; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Polytrichum','Cedrus','Pteris','Funaria','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30844,'biology','Which one of the following is heterosporous? [AIPMT 2008]','Salvinia','The sporophyte of pteridophyte produces meiospores inside sporangia, which may be homosporous (e.g., Equisetum, Adiantum, Dryopteris, etc.) or heterosporous (e.g., Salvinia, Selaginella, etc.).(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 38; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Adiantum','Equisetum','Dryopteris','Salvinia','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30845,'biology','In gymnosperms, the pollen chamber represents [AIPMT 2008]','A cavity in the ovule in which pollen grains are stored after pollination','In gymnosperms, the pollen chamber represents the microsporangium in which pollen grains develop. The microspore is generally a globular sac-like structure having a large number of microspores. The microspores are also termed pollen grains. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 39; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','A cavity in the ovule in which pollen grains are stored after pollination','An opening in the megagametophyte through which the pollen tube approaches the egg','The microsporangium in which pollen grains develop','A cell in the pollen grain in which the sperms are formed','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30846,'biology','Spore dissemination in some liverworts is aided by [AIPMT 2007]','Elaters','An elater is a cell (or structure attached to a cell) that is hygroscopic and therefore will change shape in response to changes in moisture in the environment. Elaters come in a variety of forms but are always associated with plant spores. In plants that do not have seeds, they function in dispersing the spores to a new location. In the liverworts, elaters are cells that develop in the sporophyte alongside the spores. They are complete cells, usually with helcial thickenings at maturity that respond to moisture content. In most liverworts, the elaters are unattached, but in some leafy species (such as Frullania) a few elaters will remain attached to the inside of the sporangium (spore capsule). The elaters by hygroscopic movement help in spore dispersal.','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Indusium','Calyptra','Peristome teeth','Elaters','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30847,'biology','Flagellated male gametes are present in all three of which one of the following sets? [AIPMT 2007]','Riccia, dryopteris and cycas','Flagellated male gametes are mostly seen in lower groups of plants like algae, bryophytes, and pteridophytes. It is also seen in certain gymnosperms like Cycas. The bryophytes like Riccia have the male gametes which are biflagellate. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35, 38, and 39)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Zygnema, saprolegnia and hydrilla','Fucus, marsilea and calotropis','Riccia, dryopteris and cycas','Anthoceros, funaria and spirogyra','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30848,'biology','If you are asked to classify the various algae into distinct groups, which of the following characters you should choose? [AIPMT 2007]','Types of pigments present in the cell','Algae are a group of chlorophyllous, nonvascular plants with those plant bodies. Different algae show different pigments present in the cell-like chlorophyll a, b, xanthophylls, carotenes, etc. These pigments provide the base for the classification of various groups of algae into different classes. Members of Chlorophyceae possess chlorophyll a, b pigments, Bacillariophyceae contains diatomin pigment whereas that of Phaeophyceae has fucoxanthin, Rhodophyceae has r-phycocyanin and r-phycoerythrin and Cyanophyceae has phycobilin pigment.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 33; Table 3.1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Nature of stored food materials in the cell','Structural organization of thallus','Chemical composition of the cell wall','Types of pigments present in the cell','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30849,'biology','In the prothallus of a vascular cryptogam, the antherozoids and eggs mature at different times. as a result [AIPMT 2007]','Self fertilization is prevented','In the prothallus of vascular cryptogams, the antherozoids and eggs mature at different times. The spores on germination give rise to the prothallus. The antherozoids are biflagellated or multiflagellated. The egg is produced inside the venter, water is essential for fertilization and it is always cross-fertilization. Self-fertilization is prevented.','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','There is high degree of sterility','One can conclude that the plant is apomictic','Self fertilization is prevented','There is no change in success rate of fertilization','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30901,'chemistry','Increasing order of 1st Ionisation energy of second period is:','Li < B < Be < C','As shown in the diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 86; diagram(ne0309)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'3','order','1,Li','2,B','3,Be','4,C','NEW','11-086','11-086',NULL),
(30902,'chemistry','Which of the following elements has the highest second ionization energy?','Na','Second Ionization energy is the energy required to remove second electron from an atom or ion in the gaseous state. \n\nAmong the given elements, Sodium has the highest second ionization energy because when it loses one electron from the outermost shell, it forms Na+ ion which has a stable configuration of the noble gas Neon that is 1s2 2s2 2p6','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','Mg','O2','Ne','Na','NEW','11-088','11-088',NULL),
(30903,'chemistry','Identify the correct statement:','All ionisation enthalpy are positive.','A quantitative measure of the tendency of an element to lose electron is given by its Ionization Enthalpy. \n\nIt represents the energy required to remove an electron from an isolated gaseous atom (X) in its ground state.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 87; Paragraph no. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','All ionisation enthalpy are positive.','First electron gain enthalpy of N is negative.','First electron gain enthalpy of noble gases are highly negative.','All of these','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(260121,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a role of carbonyl compounds?','They are not synthesized in human body.','Carbonyl compounds are used in many food products and pharmaceuticals to add flavours. \n\nSome of these families are manufactured for use as solvents (i.e., acetone) and for preparing materials like adhesives, paints, resins, perfumes, plastics, fabrics, etc.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-358 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','They are used in many food products and pharmaceutical to add flavours.','Some of carbonyl compounds are manufactured for use as solvents and for preparing materials like adhesives, paints, resins, perfumes, plastics, fabrics etc.','They are not synthesized in human body.','Both (a) and (b)','NEW','12-358','12-228',NULL),
(30904,'chemistry','Assertion (A) : Generally, ionisation enthalpy decreases from left to right in a period.\n\nReason (R) : When successive electrons are added to the orbitals in the same principal quantum level, the shielding effect of inner core of electrons does not increase very much to compensate for the increased attraction of the electron to the nucleus.','Assertion is wrong statement and reason is correct statement.','When we move from lithium to fluorine across the second period, successive electrons are added to orbitals in the same principal quantum level and the shielding of the nuclear charge by the inner core of electrons does not increase very much to compensate for the increased attraction of the electron to the nucleus. \n\nThus, across a period, increasing nuclear charge outweighs the shielding. Consequently, the outermost electrons are held more and more tightly and the ionization enthalpy increases across a period. \n\nAs we go down a group, the outermost electron being increasingly farther from the nucleus, there is an increased shielding of the nuclear charge by the electrons in the inner levels. \n\nIn this case, increase in shielding outweighs the increasing nuclear charge and the removal of the outermost electron requires less energy down a group. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 89; Paragraph no. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion is correct statement and reason is wrong statement.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion and reason both are wrong statements.','Assertion is wrong statement and reason is correct statement.','NEW','11-089','11-089',NULL),
(30905,'chemistry','Assertion (A) : Boron has a smaller first ionisation enthalpy than beryllium.\n\nReason (R) : The penetration of a 2s electron to the nucleus is more than the 2p electron hence 2p electron is more shielded by the inner core of electrons than the 2s electrons.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion.','In beryllium, the electron removed during the ionization is an s-electron whereas the electron removed during ionization of boron is a p-electron. \n\nThe penetration of a 2s-electron to the nucleus is more than that of a 2p-electron; hence the 2p electron of boron is more shielded from the nucleus by the inner core of electrons than the 2s electrons of beryllium.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 89; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion and reason both are wrong statements.','NEW','11-089','11-089',NULL),
(30906,'chemistry','The order of screening effect of electrons of s, p, d and f orbitals of a given shell of an atom on its outer shell electrons is:','s > p > d > f','The effective nuclear charge experienced by a valence electron in an atom will be less than the actual charge on the nucleus because of “shielding” or “screening” of the valence electron from the nucleus by the intervening core electrons. \n\nWhen we consider the same principal quantum level, an s-electron is attracted to the nucleus more than a p-electron.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 89; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','f > d > p > s','s > p > d > f','p < d < s > f','f < p > s > d','NEW','11-089','11-089',NULL),
(30907,'chemistry','Those elements impart colour to the flame on heating in it, the atoms of which require low energy for the ionisation. The elements of which of the following groups will impart colour to the flame?','1 & 2','As alkali metals and alkaline earth metals have low ionisation energy there electron can excite easily to impart color to flame. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 89; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','6 & 7','1 & 2','17','15','NEW','11-089','11-089',NULL),
(30908,'chemistry','Which elements has highest first ionisation enthalpy in periodic table?','He','As shown in diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 80; diagram(ne0326)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','N','He','H','F','NEW','11-080','11-080',NULL),
(30909,'chemistry','Arrange the following elements in the increasing order of their ionisation energy:','Caesium < Rubidium < Sodium < Lithium','The following elements in the increasing order of their ionisation energy are as shown in the diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 86; diagram(ne0310)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'3','order','1,Caesium','2,Rubidium','3,Sodium','4,Lithium','NEW','11-086','11-086',NULL),
(30910,'chemistry','Match the following elements with their respective ionisation energies(kJ/mole):','Be-899,B-801,N-1402,O-1314','As shown in the diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 86; diagram(ne0309)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'3','match','Be,899','B,801','N,1402','O,1314','NEW','11-086','11-086',NULL),
(30911,'chemistry','The Ip1, Ip2, Ip3, Ip4 and Ip5 pf an element are 7.1, 14.3, 34.5, 46.8, 162.2 eV respectively. The elements is likely to be (Ip represents Ionisation potential).','Silicon','Drastic rise in Ip corresponds to valency of the element. So by here valency will be 4 and so the element is Silicon. When we move from lithium to fluorine across the second period, successive electrons are added to orbitals in the same principal quantum level and the shielding of the nuclear charge by the inner core of electrons does not increase very much to compensate for the increased attraction of the electron to the nucleus. \n\nThus, across a period, increasing nuclear charge outweighs the shielding. Consequently, the outermost electrons are held more and more tightly and the ionization enthalpy increases across a period. \n\nAs we go down a group, the outermost electron being increasingly farther from the nucleus, there is an increased shielding of the nuclear charge by the electrons in the inner levels. \n\nIn this case, increase in shielding outweighs the increasing nuclear charge and the removal of the outermost electron requires less energy down a group. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 89; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','Calcium','Fluorine','Silicon','Sodium','NEW','11-089','11-089',NULL),
(30912,'chemistry','The first and second ionisation enthalpy of an element are 419 and 3051 kJ/mole respectively. The element belongs to group:','1','As the second ionisation enthalpy is far greater than the first ionisation enthalpy, the second electron was taken out of an extremely stable configuration that is the noble gas configuration. \n\nAs the element attains noble gas configuration after removal of first electron, the element should belong to group 1.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 88; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','17','15','NEW','11-088','11-088',NULL),
(30913,'chemistry','An element E has successive ionization enthalpies as x, 2x, 5x, 100x kJ/mol respectively. Formula of its halide is:','EX₃','As the fourth ionisation enthalpy is far greater than the third ionisation enthalpy, the fourth electron was taken out of an extremely stable configuration, that is, the noble gas configuration. \n\nAs the element attains noble gas configuration after removal of third electron, the element should belong to group 3 or it can be aluminium.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 89; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','EX','EX₂','EX₃','EX₄','NEW','11-089','11-089',NULL),
(30914,'chemistry','In plot of first ionisation enthalpy against atomic number, the minimas occur at:','Alkali metals','In plot of first ionisation enthalpy and atomic number, the minimas occur at alkali metals. \n\nAs shown in the diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 88; diagram(ne0310)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','Alkali metals','Halogens','Group 18 elements','Alkaline earth metals','NEW','11-088','11-088',NULL),
(30915,'chemistry','Find out the incorrect order of Ionisation energy:','Ne > Ne⁺','Ne⁺ should have higher ionisation energy than Ne.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 89; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','Be > B','N> O','Li > Na','Ne > Ne⁺','NEW','11-089','11-089',NULL),
(30916,'chemistry','A quantitative measure of the tendency of an element to lose electron is given by its ____ Enthalpy.','Ionisation enthalpy','A quantitative measure of the tendency of an element to lose electron is given by its Ionization Enthalpy. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 88; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'1','fillup','Io,ni,sa,ti,on, en,th,al,py',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-088','11-088',NULL),
(30917,'chemistry','For carbon atom, which ionisation energy will be highest?','4ᵗʰ','The second ionization enthalpy will be higher than the first ionization enthalpy because it is more difficult to remove an electron from a positively charged ion than from a neutral atom. \n\nIn the same way the third ionization enthalpy will be higher than the second and so on. The term “ionization enthalpy”, if not qualified, is taken as the first ionization enthalpy. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 88; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','1ˢᵗ','2ⁿᵈ','3ʳᵈ','4ᵗʰ','NEW','11-088','11-088',NULL),
(30918,'chemistry','When is the shielding effect more effective, in general?','When inner shells are completely filled.','In general, shielding is effective when the orbitals in the inner shells are completely filled. \n\nThis situation occurs in the case of alkali metals which have single outermost ns-electron preceded by a noble gas electronic configuration.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 89; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','When outer shell is partially filled.','When inner shells are completely filled.','When inner shells are partially filled.','When inner shells are half- filled and outer shells are empty.','NEW','11-089','11-089',NULL),
(30919,'chemistry','Assertion: Oxygen has lesser ionisation enthaply as compared to Nitrogen.\n\nReason: There is electron-electron repulsion involved in electrons occupying same p-orbital in Oxygen.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion.','Another “anomaly” is the smaller first ionization enthalpy of oxygen compared to nitrogen. \n\nThis arises because in the nitrogen atom, three 2p-electrons reside in different atomic orbitals (Hund’s rule) whereas in the oxygen atom, two of the four 2p-electrons must occupy the same 2p-orbital resulting in an increased electron-electron repulsion. \n\nConsequently, it is easier to remove the fourth 2p-electron from oxygen than it is, to remove one of the three 2p-electrons from nitrogen.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 89; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion and reason both are wrong statements.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement.','NEW','11-089','11-089','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/30919q.png'),
(31001,'chemistry','Match the following elements with their respective Electron Gain Enthalpies(kJ/mole):','F--328,At--270,O--141,Ne-+116','Electron gain enthalpies of related elements are as follows in the given diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 90; diagram(ne0311)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electron gain enthalpy',NULL,'3','match','F,-328','At,-270','O,-141','Ne,+116','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31002,'chemistry','Arrange the following elements in the increasing order of their negative electron gain enthalpy: O, Po, F, At.','O < Po < At <F','Electron gain enthalpies of related elements are as follows in the given diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 90; diagram(ne0311)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electron gain enthalpy',NULL,'3','order','1,O','2,Po','3,At','4,F','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31003,'chemistry','Which of the following elements has lowest negative electron gain enthalpy in Group 1?','Cs','Electron gain enthalpies of related elements are as follows in the given diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 90; diagram(ne0311)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electron gain enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','Li','H','Rb','Cs','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31004,'chemistry','Arrange the elements of group 16 in order of increasing negative electron gain enthalpy:','O < Te < Se < S','Electron gain enthalpies of related elements are as follows in the given diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 90; diagram(ne0311)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electron gain enthalpy',NULL,'3','order','1,O','2,Te','3,Se','4,S','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31005,'chemistry','Arrange the following elements in increasing order of their negative electron gain enthalpy: O, F, S, Cl.','O < S < F <Cl','Electron gain enthalpy of O or F is less negative than that of the succeeding element. \n\nThis is because when an electron is added to O or F, the added electron goes to the smaller n = 2 quantum level and suffers significant repulsion from the other electrons present in this level. \n\nFor the n = 3 quantum level (S or Cl), the added electron occupies a larger region of space and the electron-electron repulsion is much less. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 90; Paragraph no. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electron gain enthalpy',NULL,'3','order','1,O','2,S','3,F','4,Cl','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31006,'chemistry','Arrange the elements of group 17 in order of increasing negative electron gain enthalpy:','I < Br < F < Cl','Electron gain enthalpies of related elements are as follows in the given diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 90; diagram(ne0311)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electron gain enthalpy',NULL,'3','order','1,I','2,Br','3,F','4,Cl','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31007,'chemistry','Mark the statement that is not correct:','Oxygen has the highest magnitude of negative electron gain enthalpy in group 16.','Electron gain enthalpies of related elements are as follows in the given diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 90; diagram(ne0311)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electron gain enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','Magnitude of negative electron gain enthalpy decreases down the group in group 1.','All the elements of group 17 have higher magnitude of negative electron gain enthalpy than that of sulphur.','Oxygen has the highest magnitude of negative electron gain enthalpy in group 16.','Group 0 elements have positive electron gain enthalpy.','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31008,'chemistry','Which of the following elements have highest magnitude of positive electron gain enthalpy?','Ne','Electron gain enthalpies of related elements are as follows in the given diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 90; diagram(ne0311)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electron gain enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','Ar','Cs','Ne','He','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31009,'chemistry','Electron gain enthalpy is:','When an electron is added to a neutral gaseous atom to convert it into a negative ion, the enthalpy change accompanying the process is defined as the Electron Gain Enthalpy.','When an electron is added to a neutral gaseous atom (X) to convert it into a negative ion, the enthalpy change accompanying the process is defined as the Electron Gain Enthalpy (∆egH). \n\nElectron gain enthalpy provides a measure of the ease with which an atom adds an electron to form anion. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 89; Paragraph no. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electron gain enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','When an electron is added to a positively charged gaseous atom to convert it into a neutral atom, the enthalpy change accompanying the process is defined as the Electron Gain Enthalpy.','Always exothermic.','Always positive.','When an electron is added to a neutral gaseous atom to convert it into a negative ion, the enthalpy change accompanying the process is defined as the Electron Gain Enthalpy.','NEW','11-089','11-089',NULL),
(31010,'chemistry','An electron is added to a neutral gaseous atom and energy is released. Here:','Electron affinity is positive.','If energy is released when an electron is added to an atom, the electron affinity is taken as positive, contrary to thermodynamic convention. \n\nIf energy has to be supplied to add an electron to an atom, then the electron affinity of the atom is assigned a negative sign.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 90; footnote)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electron gain enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','Electron gain enthalpy is positive.','Electron affinity is negative.','The atom must be highly electronegative.','Electron affinity is positive.','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31011,'chemistry','Mark the correct relation between electron gain enthalpy and electron affinity:','∆egH = –Ae – 5/2 RT','Electron affinity is defined as absolute zero and, therefore at any other temperature (T) heat capacities of the reactants and the products have to be taken into account in ∆egH = –Ae – 5/2 RT. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 90; Footnote)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electron gain enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','∆egH = –Ae – 5/2 RT','∆egH = Ae – 5/2 RT','∆egH = –Ae','∆egH = –Ae + 5/2 RT.','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31012,'chemistry','Assertion (A) : Electron gain enthalpy becomes less negative as we go down a group.\n\nReason (R) : Size of the atom increases on going down the group and the added electron would be farther from the nucleus.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion.','As a general rule, electron gain enthalpy becomes more negative with increase in the atomic number across a period. \n\nThe effective nuclear charge increases from left to right across a period and consequently it will be easier to add an electron to a smaller atom since the added electron on an average would be closer to the positively charged nucleus. \n\nWe should also expect electron gain enthalpy to become less negative as we go down a group because the size of the atom increases and the added electron would be farther from the nucleus.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 90; Paragraph no. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electron gain enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement.','Assertion and reason both are wrong statements.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31013,'chemistry','Process of adding electron to an element can be:','Either endothermic or exothermic','Depending on the element, the process of adding an electron to the atom can be either endothermic or exothermic. \n\nFor many elements energy is released when an electron is added to the atom and the electron gain enthalpy is negative.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 89; Paragraph no. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electron gain enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','Either endothermic or exothermic','Exothermic only','Endothermic only','No energy involved','NEW','11-089','11-089',NULL),
(31014,'chemistry','Which one of the following will have least negative electron gain enthalpy?','P','Electron gain enthalpy generally becomes more negative across a period as we move from left to right. \n\nWithin a group, electron gain enthalpy becomes less negative down a group. However, adding an electron to the 2p-orbital leads to greater repulsion than adding an electron to the larger 3p-orbital. \n\nHence the element with most negative electron gain enthalpy is chlorine; the one with the least negative electron gain enthalpy is phosphorus.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 90; Problem 3.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electron gain enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','P','F','S','Cl','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(41405,'chemistry','Match the following:','Two bond pairs-Linear geometry,Three bond pairs-Trigonal planar geometry,Four bond pairs-Tetrahedral,Five bond pairs-Trigonal bipyramidal','In the compounds of AB2, AB3, AB4, AB5 and AB6, the arrangement of electron pairs and the B atoms around the central atom A are : linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral, trigonalbipyramidal and octahedral, respectively. \n\nSuch arrangement can be seen in the molecules like BF3 (AB3), CH4 (AB4) and PCl5 (AB5) as depicted diagram by their ball and stick models. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 113; Paragraph 4) diagram(ne0407)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'3','match','Two bond pairs,Linear geometry','Three bond pairs,Trigonal planar geometry','Four bond pairs,Tetrahedral','Five bond pairs,Trigonal bipyramidal','NEW','11-113','11-113',NULL),
(31015,'chemistry','Assertion: Group 17 elements (the halogens) have very high negative electron gain enthalpies.\n\nReason: They can attain stable noble gas electronic configurations by picking up an electron.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Group 17 elements (the halogens) have very high negative electron gain enthalpies because they can attain stable noble gas electronic configurations by picking up an electron.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 89; Paragraph No. 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electron gain enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','11-089','11-089',NULL),
(31016,'chemistry','Which element considered to have the greatest ability to attract electrons?','Fluorine','Pauling, an American scientist, in 1922 assigned arbitrarily a value of 4.0 to fluorine, the element considered to have the greatest ability to attract electrons. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 91; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electron gain enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','Fluorine','Nitrogen','Sodium','Oxygen','NEW','11-091','11-091',NULL),
(31017,'chemistry','Electron gain enthalpies have large negative values toward the _______.','Non-Metals','Electron gain enthalpies have large negative values toward the upper right of the periodic table preceding the noble gases, ie. non metals.. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 90; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electron gain enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','Noble gases','Transition metals','Non-Metals','Alkali metals','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31018,'biology','Bryophytes can be separated from algae because they \n[AIPMT 1997]','Possess archegonia','The plant body of bryophytes is more differentiated than that of algae. It is thallus-like and prostrate or erect, and attached to the substratum\nby unicellular or multicellular rhizoids. They lack true roots, stem or\nleaves. They may possess root-like, leaf-like or stem-like structures. The\nmain plant body of the bryophyte is haploid. It produces gametes, hence\nis called a gametophyte. The sex organs in bryophytes are multicellular.\nThe male sex organ is called antheridium. They produce biflagellate\nantherozoids. The female sex organ called archegonium is flask-shaped\nand produces a single egg. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Possess archegonia','Contain chloroplast','Are thalloid forms','Have no conducting tissue','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31019,'biology','Multicellular branched rhizoids and leafy gametophytes are the characteristics of \n[AIPMT 1997]','Some bryophytes','The plant body of bryophytes is more differentiated than that of algae. It is thallus-like and prostrate or erect, and attached to the substratum by unicellular or multicellular rhizoids. They lack true roots, stem or leaves. They may possess root-like, leaf-like or stem-like structures. The main plant body of the bryophyte is haploid. It produces gametes, hence is called a gametophyte. The sex organs in bryophytes are multicellular. The male sex organ is called antheridium. They produce biflagellate antherozoids. The female sex organ called archegonium is flask-shaped and produces a single egg. (Reference : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Some bryophytes','Pteridophytes','All bryophytes','Gymnosperms','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31020,'biology','Brown algae are characterized by the presence of [AIPMT 1997]','Fucoxanthin','Brown algae are members of the Class Phaeophyceae. Their chromatophores are ellipsoidal or discoid. They contain chlorophyll a, chl c, band carotenes, and xanthophyll pigments (e.g., lutein, flavoxanthin, violaxanthin). They also contain a large amount of a brown pigment – fucoxanthin which masks the green color of chlorophyll pigment. This gives characteristic brown color to these plants, hence the name brown algae.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 33; Table 3.1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Fucoxanthin','Haematochrome','Phycocyanin','Phycoerythrin','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31021,'biology','Which of the following plant kingdom is called ‘amphibians’? [AIPMT 1996]','Bryophyta','Bryophytes are also called amphibians of the plant kingdom because\nthese plants can live in soil but are dependent on water for sexual\nreproduction. They usually occur in damp, humid and shaded localities. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Pteridophyta','Thallophyta','Tracheophyta','Bryophyta','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31022,'biology','A gymnosperm leaf carries 16 chromosomes,the number of chromosomes in its endosperm will be [AIPMT 1996]','8','Gymnosperms show distinct alternation of generations. The sporophytic phase is dominant. The sporophyte is differentiated into root, stem, and leaves. So the number of chromosomes in a leaf cell is diploid. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 42; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','12','8','16','24','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31023,'biology','The pyrenoids are made up of \n[AIPMT 1996]','Proteinaceous centre and starchy sheath','Pyrenoids are found in many bryophytes and algae. They are small, spherical bodies found in the cytoplasm of a plant cell. They are rich in proteins and are surrounded by a starch sheath. (Reference : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 32; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Proteinaceous centre and starchy sheath','Core of nucleic acid surrounded by protein sheath','Core of protein surrounded by fatty sheath','Core of starch surrounded by sheath of protein','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31024,'biology','The plant body of moss (funaria) is [AIPMT 1995]','Predominantly gametophyte with sporophyte','A dominant, independent, photosynthetic, thalloid or erect phase is\nrepresented by a haploid gametophyte and it alternates with the short\nlived multicelluler sporophyte totally or partially dependent on the\ngametophyte for its anchorage and nutrition. All bryophytes represent\nthis pattern. and liverworts and mosses belongs to division bryophyta.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 43; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Completely sporophyte','Predominantly gametophyte with sporophyte','Completely gametophyte','Predominantly sporophyte with gametophyte','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31025,'biology','Sexual reproduction is absent in [AIPMT 1995]','Nostoc','Sexual reproduction takes place through fusion of two gametes. These gametes can be flagellated and similar in size (as in Ulothrix) or non-flagellated (non-motile) but similar in size (as in Spirogyra). Such reproduction is called isogamous. Fusion of two gametes dissimilar in size, as in species of Eudorina is termed as anisogamous. Fusion between one large, non motile (static) female gamete and a smaller, motile male gamete is termed oogamous, e.g., Volvox, Fucus. whereas nostoc belongs to monera which do not show sexual reproduction.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 30; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Spirogyra','Nostoc','Ulothrix','Volvox','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31026,'biology','In Chlorophyceae, the mode of sexual reproduction is \n[AIPMT 1994]','All of these','"Sexual reproduction takes place through fusion of two gametes. These gametes can be flagellated and similar in size (as in Ulothrix) or non-flagellated (non-motile) but similar in size (as in Spirogyra). Such reproduction is called isogamous. Fusion of two gametes dissimilar in size, as in species of Eudorina is termed as anisogamous. Fusion between one large, non motile (static) female gamete and a smaller, motile male gamete is termed oogamous, e.g., Volvox, Fucus. whereas nostoc belongs to monera which do not show sexual reproduction.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 30; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Isogamy','Anisogamy','Oogamy','All of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31027,'biology','In bryophytes \n[AIPMT 1994]','Sporophytes are dependent upon gametophytes','"A dominant, independent, photosynthetic, thalloid or erect phase is represented by a haploid gametophyte and it alternates with the short lived multicelluler sporophyte totally or partially dependent on the gametophyte for its anchorage and nutrition. All bryophytes represent\nthis pattern. and liverworts and mosses belongs to division bryophyta. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 43; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Both generations are independent','Gametophytes are dependent upon sporophytes','Sporophytes complete their life cycle','Sporophytes are dependent upon gametophytes','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31028,'biology','Which one is the most advanced from an evolutionary viewpoint? [AIPMT 1994]','Pinus','Pinus is more advanced from the evolutionary point of view. It is a gymnosperm (of phanerogams) having a well-developed vascular conducting system and bears seeds. While others Selaginella, Funaria, and Chlamydomonas do not bear seeds. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 38; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Selaginella','Funaria','Chlamydomonas','Pinus','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31029,'biology','Pinus differs from mango in having [AIPMT 1993]','Ovules not enclosed in ovary','Pinus is a gymnosperm plant that has a well-developed conducting tissue system but its seeds are naked. Whereas mango is an angiosperm plant in which seeds are enclosed in the ovary and fruit is present. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 38; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Tree habit','Green leaves','Ovules not enclosed in ovary','Wood','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31030,'biology','Pteridophytes differ from bryophytes and thallophytes in having [AIPMT 1993]','Vascular tissues','Pteridophytes differ from bryophytes and thallophytes in having well developed vascular tissue system. Vascular tissues play an important role in conducting water and food materials to the plants. Whereas these are absent in bryophytes and thallophytes. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 36; Paragraph No. 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Vascular tissues','Motile antherozoids','Archegonia','Alternation of generations','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31031,'biology','Chloroplast of chlamydomonas is [AIPMT 1993]','Cupshaped','Chloroplast in Chlamydomonas is cup-shaped. It is a one-celled structure whereas stellate, spiral, and collar-shaped chloroplasts are present in Zygnema, Spirogyra, and Ulothrix respectively. The chloroplasts may be\ndiscoid, plate-like, reticulate, cup-shaped, spiral or ribbon-shaped in\ndifferent species. in chlorophyacea. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 32; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Stellate','Cupshaped','Collarshaped','Spiral','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31032,'biology','In ulothrix/spirogyra, reduction division (meiosis) occurs at the time of [AIPMT 1993]','Zygospore germination','In Ulothrix/Spirogyra reduction division (meiosis) occurs at the time of zygospore formation. The plant body of Ulothrix and Spirogyra is gametophytic (haploid), they produce zoogametes (n) which fuses to form zygospores. Sporophytic generation is represented only by the one-celled zygote. There are no free-living sporophytes. Meiosis in the zygote results in the formation of haploid spores. The haploid spores divide mitotically and form the gametophyte. The dominant, photosynthetic phase in such plants is the free-living gametophyte. This kind of life cycle is termed as haplontic. Many algae such as Volvox, Spirogyra and some species of Chlamydomonas represent this pattern. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 42; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Gamete formation','Zoospore formation','Zygospore germination','Vegetative reproduction','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31033,'biology','Protonema occurs in the life cycle of [AIPMT 1993]','Funaria','Protonema occurs in the life cycle of Funaria. The spore is the first cell of gametophytic generation and it germinates to form a filamentous branched alga-like structure called protonema. If gives rise to the new plant. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 36; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Riccia','Funaria','Anthoceros','Spirogya','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31034,'biology','A plant having seeds but lacking flowers and fruits belongs to [AIPMT 1992]','Gymnosperms','A plant having seeds but lacking flowers and fruit belong to gymnosperms. Gymnosperms are vascular land plants and bear seeds that are naked i.e., ovules not enclosed in the ovary. Hence, flowers are absent. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 38; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Pteridophytes','Mosses','Ferns','Gymnosperms','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31035,'biology','Which one of the following is not common between funaria and selaginella? [AIPMT 1992]','Roots','The root is not common between Funaria and Selaginella. Funaria is a bryophyte and has archegonium, embryo, and flagellated sperms which are also present in Selaginella. Selaginella is a pteridophyte and it has a root that is absent in Funaria. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 36; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Archegonium','Embryo','Flagellate sperms','Roots','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31036,'biology','The plant group that produces spores and embryo but lacks vascular tissues and seeds is [AIPMT 1992]','Bryophyta','Bryophytes are the plants that produce spores and embryos but they do not have a vascular tissue system. While rhodophytes and phaeophytes are algae and produce spores (no embryos) only and pteridophytes produce spores, embryos, and a well-developed vascular tissue system. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Pteridophyta','Rhodophyta','Bryophyta','Phaeophyta','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31037,'biology','A plant in which sporophytic generation is represented by zygote is [AIPMT 1992]','Chlamydomonas','A plant in which sporophytic generation is represented by zygote is Chlamydomonas. It is a type of algae that has a gametophytic plant body (haploid). It reproduces sexually by gametes which are isogametes that fuse to produce diploid zygote which is the only sporophytic generation. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 42; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Pinus','Selaginella','Chlamydomonas','Dryopteris','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31038,'biology','Bryophytes are amphibians because [AIPMT 1992]','They require a layer of water for carrying out sexual reproduction','Bryophytes are also called amphibians of the plant kingdom because\nthese plants can live in soil but are dependent on water for sexual\nreproduction. They usually occur in damp, humid and shaded localities. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','They require a layer of water for carrying out sexual reproduction','They occur in damp places','They are mostly aquatic','All the above','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31039,'biology','In pinus/gymnosperms, the haploid structure is [AIPMT 1990]','Megaspore, pollen grain and endosperm','In Pinus/gymnosperms, the endosperm is produced before fertilization and hence it is haploid. Megaspore and pollen grains are structures of male gametophytes and it is also haploid. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 42; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Megaspore, endosperm and embryo','Megaspore, pollen grain and endosperm','Megaspore, integument and root','Pollen grain, leaf and root','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31040,'biology','An evolutionary important character of selaginella is [AIPMT 1989]','Heterosporous nature','The evolutionary important character of Selaginella is its heterosporous nature. Selaginella produces two types of spores microspores and megaspores. Heterospory in the life cycle of Selaginella leads to the formation of seed habit. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 38; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Heterosporous nature','Rhizophore','Strobili','Ligule','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31041,'biology','In pinus/cycas/gymnosperms, the endosperm is [AIPMT 1989]','Haploid','In Pinus/Cycas/gymnosperms the endosperm is haploid because it is produced before fertilization. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 42; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Triploid','Haploid','Diploid','Tetraploid','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31042,'biology','Which of the following plants is monoecious? [NEET 2021]','Chara','Monoecious or homothallic condition in Chara (green algae) is used to denote upper oogonium and lower antheridium on the same plant. The organisms, which possess both the reproductive organs are called bisexual.','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Cycas circinalis','Carica papaya','Chara','Marchantia polymorpha','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31043,'biology','Floridean starch has structure similar to [NEET 2020]','amylopectin and glycogen','In Rhodophyceae, The food is stored as floridean starch\nwhich is very similar to amylopectin and glycogen in structure. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 33; Paragraph No. 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','starch and cellulose','amylopectin and glycogen','mannitol and algin','laminarin and cellulose.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31044,'biology','Which of the following pairs is of unicellular algae?[NEET 2020]','Chlorella and Spirulina','Gelidium, Gracilaria, Laminaria and Sargassum\nare multicellular. Anabaena is filamentous blue green\nalgae. Volvox is colonial. The form and size of algae is highly variable, ranging from colonial\nforms like Volvox and the filamentous forms like Ulothrix and Spirogyra\n. A few of the marine forms such as kelps, form massive plant\nbodies.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 30; Paragraph No. 4)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Laminaria and Sargassum','Gelidium and Gracilaria','Anabaena and Volvox','Chlorella and Spirulina','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31045,'biology','Which one is wrongly matched?[NEET 2018]','Uniflagellate gametes – Polysiphonia','A non-motile male gamete is the marking feature of Polysiphonia. polysiphonia belongs to red algae and red algae contains non motile gametes. red algae never have a motile gamete. They reproduce asexually by non-motile spores and sexually by non-motile gametes. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 33; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','Uniflagellate gametes – Polysiphonia','Biflagellate zoospores – Brown algae','Gemma cups – Marchantia','Unicellular organism – Chlorella','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31046,'biology','Strobili cones are found in [NEET 2020]','Equisetum.','The sporophytes of pteridophytes bear sporangia that are subtended by leaf-like appendages called sporophylls. In some cases sporophylls may form distinct compact structures called strobili or cones, e.g., Selaginella, Equisetum.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 36; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','Salvinia','Pteris','Marchantia','Equisetum.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31047,'biology','Which of the following statements is correct? [NEET 2018]','Ovules are not enclosed by ovary wall in\ngymnosperms.','Gymnosperms have naked ovule that means naked seeds. Selaginella and Salvinia produce two kinds of spores, macro (large) and micro (small) therefore called as heterosporous. Equisetum (horse tail) is a pteridophyte belonging to sphenopsida. Stems are branched in Cedrus and unbranched in Cycas. The gymnosperms (gymnos : naked, sperma : seeds) are plants in which the ovules are not enclosed by any ovary wall and remain exposed, both before and after fertilisation. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 38; paragraph no. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','Ovules are not enclosed by ovary wall in\ngymnosperms.','Selaginella is heterosporous, while Salvinia is\nhomosporous.','Horsetails are gymnosperms.','Stems are usually unbranched in both Cycas and\nCedrus.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31048,'biology','Choose the correct answer from the options given below : [ RE NEET 2022]  diagram (0370)','(2) (a) - (iii), (b) - (iv), (c) - (ii), (d) - (i)','(2) (a) - (iii), (b) - (iv), (c) - (ii), (d) - (i)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) (a) - (iii), (b) - (i), (c) - (ii), (d) - (iv)','(2) (a) - (iii), (b) - (iv), (c) - (ii), (d) - (i)','(3) (a) - (iii), (b) - (ii), (c) - (i), (d) - (iv)','(4) (a) - (ii), (b) - (iii), (c) - (i), (d) - (iv)','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/31048q.png'),
(31049,'biology','Which classes of algae possess pigment fucoxanthin and pigment phycoerythrin, respectively? [ NEET 2023(Phase 2) ]','(4) Phaeophyceae and Rhodophyceae','The prime pigment found in phaeophyceae that gives its members their characteristic colour is \nxanthophyll pigment, fucoxanthin.\nThe members of rhodophyceae are commonly called red algae because of the predominance of the \nred pigment, r-phycoerythrin in their body.','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Chlorophyceae and Rhodophyceae','(2) Rhodophyceae and Phaeophyceae','(3) Phaeophyceae and Chlorophyceae','(4) Phaeophyceae and Rhodophyceae','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31050,'biology','Which of the following statements is true ? [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(1) All pteridophytes exhibit haplo-diplontic pattern.','All pteridophytes exhibit haplo-diplontic pattern.','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) All pteridophytes exhibit haplo-diplontic pattern.','(2) Seed bearing plants follow haplontic pattern','(3) Most algal genera are diplontic','(4) Most bryophytes do not have haplo-diplontic life \ncycle.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31051,'biology','In angiosperms the correct sequence of events in formation of female gametophyte in the ovule is : \n (A) 3 successive free nuclear divisions functional megaspore. \n (B) Degeneration of 3 megaspores. \n (C) Meiotic division in megaspore mother cell. \n (D) Migration of 3 nuclei towards each pole. \n (E) Formation of wall resulting in seven celled embryosac. \n Choose the correct answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023]','(4) (C), (B), (A), (D), (E)','The correct sequence of events for the formation of the female gametophyte in the ovule in angiosperms is:\n\nMeiotic division in the megaspore mother cell (C)\nDegeneration of 3 megaspores (B)\n3 successive free nuclear divisions in the functional megaspore (A)\nMigration of 3 nuclei towards each pole (D)\nFormation of the wall resulting in a seven-celled embryo sac (E)\nTherefore, the correct answer is option 4: (C), (B), (A), (D), (E).','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) (A), (B), (C), (D), (E)','(2) (C), (E), (A), (D), (B)','(3) (B), (C), (A), (D), (E)','(4) (C), (B), (A), (D), (E)','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31052,'biology','Match List-I List-II. \nList-I List-II\n(A) Pteropsida (I) Psilotum\n(B) Lycopsida (II) Equisetum\n(C) Psilopsida (III) Adiantum\n(D) Sphenopsida (IV) Selaginella\n Choose the correct answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(4) (A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)','(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) (A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)','(2) (A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)','(3) (A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)','(4) (A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31101,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a numerical scale of electronegativity of elements?','Kelvin-Planck scale','A number of numerical scales of electronegativity of elements viz., Pauling scale, Mulliken-Jaffe scale, Allred-Rochow scale have been developed.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 90; Paragraph no. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronegativity',NULL,'1','mcq','Pauling scale','Mulliken-Jaffe scale','Allred-Rochow scale','Kelvin-Planck scale','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31102,'chemistry','Which is the most widely used electronegativity scale?','Pauling scale','The one which is the most widely used is the Pauling scale.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 90-91; Last line of page 90)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronegativity',NULL,'1','mcq','Pauling scale','Mulliken-Jaffe scale','Allred-Rochow scale','Pearson- Allen Scale','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31103,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement:','Ionisation enthalpy is inversely proportional to electron gain enthalpy.','Ionisation enthalpy is inversely proportional to electron gain enthalpy.\n\nAs shown in diagram. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 91; diagram(ne0312)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronegativity',NULL,'3','mcq','Electronegativity is inversely proportional to atomic radius.','Non metallic character is proportional to electronegativity.','Atomic radius is inversely proportional to ionisation enthalpy.','Ionisation enthalpy is inversely proportional to electron gain enthalpy.','NEW','11-091','11-091',NULL),
(31104,'chemistry','Mark the correct statement with respect to electronegativity:','Electronegativity provides a means of predicting the nature of force that holds a pair of atoms together.','Linus Pauling, an American scientist, in 1922 assigned arbitrarily a value of 4.0 to fluorine. \n\nThough electronegativity is not a measurable quantity, it does provide a means of predicting the nature of force that holds a pair of atoms together.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 90; Paragraph no. 1,2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronegativity',NULL,'2','mcq','Linus Pauling, an American scientist, in 1922 by careful experimentation measured the electronegativity of fluorine as 4 units.','Electronegativity is a quantitative property and is measurable.','Electronegativity provides a means of predicting the nature of force that holds a pair of atoms together.','Along a period, as effective nuclear charge increases electronegativity decreases.','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31105,'chemistry','Arrange the elements in decreasing order of their electronegativity:','Si > Al > Li > Na','Electronegativity generally increases across a period from left to right (say from lithium to fluorine) and decrease down a group (say from fluorine to astatine) in the periodic table. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 91; Paragraph no. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronegativity',NULL,'3','order','1,Si','2,Al','3,Li','4,Na','NEW','11-091','11-091',NULL),
(31106,'chemistry','Arrange the elements in decreasing order of their electronegativity:','Be > Mg > Li > Na','Electronegativity generally increases across a period from left to right (say from lithium to fluorine) and decrease down a group (say from fluorine to astatine) in the periodic table. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 91; Paragraph no. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronegativity',NULL,'3','order','1,Be','2,Mg','3,Li','4,Na','NEW','11-091','11-091',NULL),
(31107,'chemistry','Match the elements with their respective value of electronegativity on Pauling scale:','F-4,Na-0.9,I-2.5,Rb-0.8','Electronegativity Values (on Pauling scale) across the periods, as shown in the diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 91; diagram(ne0313)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronegativity',NULL,'3','match','F,4','Na,0.9','I,2.5','Rb,0.8','NEW','11-091','11-091',NULL),
(31108,'chemistry','Match the elements with their respective value of electronegativity on Pauling scale:','Cl-3,O-3.5,S-2.5,B-2','Electronegativity Values (on Pauling scale) down a family, as shown in the diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 91; diagram(ne0314)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronegativity',NULL,'3','match','Cl,3','O,3.5','S,2.5','B,2','NEW','11-091','11-091',NULL),
(31109,'chemistry','Match the elements with their respective value of electronegativity on Pauling scale:','Be-1.5,Mg-1.2,Si-1.8,Li-1','Electronegativity Values (on Pauling scale) down a family, as shown in the diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 91; diagram(ne0314)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronegativity',NULL,'3','match','Be,1.5','Mg,1.2','Si,1.8','Li,1','NEW','11-091','11-091',NULL),
(31110,'chemistry','Arrange the elements in decreasing order of their electronegativity:','N > S > P > B','Electronegativity Values (on Pauling scale) down a family, as shown in the diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 91; diagram(ne0314)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronegativity',NULL,'3','order','1,N','2,S','3,P','4,B','NEW','11-091','11-091',NULL),
(31111,'chemistry','All of the following element pairs have approximately same electronegativity on Pauling scale except:','I,P','Electronegativity Values (on Pauling scale) down a family, as shown in the diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 91; diagram(ne0314)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronegativity',NULL,'2','mcq','Cl,N','C,S','I,P','Be,Al','NEW','11-091','11-091',NULL),
(31214,'chemistry','Assertion: In Na₂O molecule oxygen exhibits -2 oxidation state. \n\nReason: Oxygen being more electronegative accepts two electrons one from each of two sodium atoms.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','In Na₂O, oxygen being more electronegative accepts two electrons, one from each of the two sodium atoms and, thus, shows oxidation state –2. \n\nOn the other hand sodium with electronic configuration 3s1 loses one electron to oxygen and is given oxidation state +1. \n\nThus, the oxidation state of an element in a particular compound can be defined as the charge acquired by its atom on the basis of electronegative consideration from other atoms in the molecule.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 92; Paragraph no. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Oxidation states',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are false.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','NEW','11-092','11-092',NULL),
(31216,'chemistry','Identify the formula of oxide with group 17:','Cl₂O₇','The formula of oxide with group 17 is Cl₂O₇, as it is shown in diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 93; diagram(ne0323)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Oxidation states',NULL,'2','mcq','Cl₂O₂','Cl₂O₇','K₂O₂','K₂O₇','NEW','11-093','11-093',NULL),
(31218,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Chlorophyceae:\n\ni. They are usually grass green due to the dominance of pigments chlorophyll a and b.\n\nii. Most of the members have one or more storage bodies called pyrenoids located in the golgi body.\n\niii. Have a rigid cell wall made of an outer layer of cellulose and an inner layer of pectose.\n\niv. Asexual reproduction is by non-flagellated zoospores produced in zoosporangia.\n\nAll of them statements are false except?','Only i','The members of chlorophyceae are commonly called green algae. They are usually\ngrass green due to the dominance of pigments chlorophyll a and b.\nMost of the members have one or more storage bodies called pyrenoids located in the\nchloroplasts.\nGreen algae usually have a rigid cell wall made of an inner layer of cellulose and an outer\nlayer of pectose.\nAsexual reproduction is by flagellated zoospores produced in zoosporangia.\n(ref. NCERT biology class 11, page 32)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','i and ii','Only i','iii and iv','None of the above','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31219,'biology','Consider the following statements about Phaeophyceae:\ni. Primarily in marine habitats.\n\nii. Branched forms as represented by kelps may reach a height of 100 metres.\n\niii. They possess chlorophyll a and c.\n\niv. They vary in colour from olive green to various shades of brown depending upon the amount of chlorophyll a and c.\n\nv. Asexual reproduction in most brown algae is by biflagellate zoospores.\n\nAll of them statements are true except?','Only iv','The members of phaeophyceae or brown algae are found primarily in marine habitats. They\nshow great variation in size and form. They range from simple branched, filamentous forms\n(Ectocarpus) to profusely branched forms as represented by kelps, which may reach a\nheight of 100 metres. They possess chlorophyll a, c, carotenoids and xanthophylls.\nThey vary in colour from olive green to various shades of brown depending upon the amount\nof the xanthophyll pigment, fucoxanthin present in them.\nAsexual reproduction in most brown algae is by biflagellate zoospores that are pear-shaped\nand have two unequal laterally attached flagella.\n(ref. NCERT biology class 11; page 32,33)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','i,ii,iii','Only iv','Only i','None of the above','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31220,'biology','Consider the following statements about Rhodophyceae:\ni. Majority of the red algae are marine with greater concentrations found in the colder areas.\n\nii. They occur in well-lighted regions close to the surface of water.\n\niii. Do not occur at great depths in oceans because relatively little light penetrates.\n\niv. The red thalli of most of the red algae are unicellular.\n\nv. The food is stored as floridean starch which is very similar to amylopectin and glycogen in structure.\n\nWhich of the above statements are true?','Only ii and v','Majority of the red algae are marine with greater concentrations found in the warmer areas.\nThey occur in both well-lighted regions close to the surface of water and also at great\ndepths in oceans where relatively little light penetrates. The red thalli of most of the red\nalgae are multicellular.\nThe food is stored as floridean starch which is very similar to amylopectin and glycogen in\nstructure.\n(ref. NCERT biology class 11; page 33)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','ii,iii and v','Only ii and v','i,ii and v','All the statements are true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31222,'biology','Label the following diagram given below:\n\ni. Air bladder\n\nii. Frond\n\niii. Midrib\n\niv. Holdfast\n(0344)','a-i b-ii c-iii d-iv','The given diagram is of Fucus which is a brown algae. (Ref. NCERT biology class 11; Figure 3.1) diagram (e0329)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a-i b-ii c-iii d-iv','a-iii b-i c-ii d-iv','a-i b-iv c-iii d-ii','a-i b-iii c-iv d-ii','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/31222q.png'),
(31223,'biology','The following group of organisms shown in the diagram help in fixing carbon dioxide on earth. The fraction of total carbon dioxide on earth fixed by them is:  (0346)','At least 50%','At least a half of the total carbon dioxide fixation on earth is carried out by algae through\nphotosynthesis. Being photosynthetic they increase the level of dissolved oxygen in their\nimmediate environment. diagram (e0347)\n(ref. NCERT biology class 11; page 32; paragraph 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','At least 20%','At least 50%','At least 30%','At least 40%','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/31223q.png'),
(31301,'chemistry','The ____ element of each of the groups 1 and 2 and groups 13-17 differs in many respects from the other members of their respective group.','First','The first element of each of the groups 1 (lithium) and 2 (beryllium) and groups 13-17 (boron to fluorine) differs in many respects from the other members of their respective group. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 93; Paragraph no. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Anomalous properties of second period',NULL,'1','mcq','First','Last','Third','Middle','NEW','11-093','11-093',NULL),
(31302,'chemistry','Bonds formed by which of the following elements has the least ionic character than the others.','Be','The first element of each of the groups 1 (lithium) and 2 (beryllium) and groups 13-17 (boron to fluorine) differs in many respects from the other members of their respective group. \n\nFor example, lithium unlike other alkali metals, and beryllium unlike other alkaline earth metals, form compounds with pronounced covalent character; the other members of these groups predominantly form ionic compounds. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 93; Paragraph no. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Anomalous properties of second period',NULL,'1','mcq','Mg','Be','Cs','Ba','NEW','11-093','11-093','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/31302q.png'),
(31303,'chemistry','Behaviour of Li and Be is more similar with ____ and ____ respectively.','Mg and Al','In fact the behaviour of lithium and beryllium is more similar with the second element of the following group i.e., magnesium and aluminium, respectively. \n\nThis sort of similarity is commonly referred to as diagonal relationship in the periodic properties. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 93; Paragraph no. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Anomalous properties of second period',NULL,'1','mcq','Na and Mg','Mg and Al','Al and Mg','Mg and Na','NEW','11-093','11-093','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/31303q.png'),
(31304,'chemistry','The anomalous behaviour of lithium in it’s group cannot be attributed to it\'s:','High charge','The anomalous behaviour is attributed to their small size, large charge/ radius ratio and high electronegativity of the elements.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 93; Paragraph no. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Anomalous properties of second period',NULL,'1','mcq','Small size','High electronegativity','Large charge to radius ration','High charge','NEW','11-093','11-093',NULL),
(31305,'chemistry','First member of each group has only ___ orbitals available for bonding.','4','In addition, the first member of group has only four valence orbitals (2s and 2p) available for bonding, whereas the second member of the groups have nine valence orbitals (3s, 3p, 3d). \n\nAs a consequence of this, the maximum covalency of the first member of each group is 4. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 93; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Anomalous properties of second period',NULL,'1','mcq','2','4','6','1','NEW','11-093','11-093','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/31305q.png'),
(31306,'chemistry','First member of p-block elements displays ____ ability to form p – p π multiple bonds to itself and to other second period elements compared to subsequent members of the same group.','Greater','First member of p-block elements displays greater ability to form p – p multiple bonds π π to itself (ne.g., C = C, C ≡ C, N = N, N ≡ Ν) and to other second period elements (ne.g., C = O, C = N, C ≡ N, N = O) compared to subsequent members of the same group. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 93; last line)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Anomalous properties of second period',NULL,'1','mcq','Greater','Equal','Shorter','Negligible','NEW','11-093','11-093',NULL),
(31307,'chemistry','Maximum covalency of Al is ___ .','6','The first member of group has only four valence orbitals (2s and 2p) available for bonding, whereas the second member of the groups have nine valence orbitals (3s, 3p, 3d). \n\nAs a consequence of this, the maximum covalency of the first member of each group is 4, whereas the other members of the groups can expand their valence shell to accommodate more than four pairs of electrons e.g., aluminium. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 93; Paragraph no. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Anomalous properties of second period',NULL,'1','fillup','4,6,2,3',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-093','11-093',NULL),
(31308,'chemistry','Match the metallic radius of the following element:','Be-111, Na-186, Li-152, Mg-160','The metallic radius of the following element are as follows in the diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 93; diagram(ne0324)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Anomalous properties of second period',NULL,'3','match','Be,111','Na,186','Li,152','Mg,160','NEW','11-093','11-093','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/31308q.png'),
(31309,'chemistry','Match the ionic radius of the following element:','Be-31, Na-102, Li-76, Mg-72','The ionic radius of the following element are as follows in the diagram  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 93; diagram(ne0324)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Anomalous properties of second period',NULL,'3','match','Be,31','Na,102','Li,76','Mg,72','NEW','11-093','11-093','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/31309q.png'),
(31310,'biology','Consider the following statements about the plant shown in the diagram below, Which of the above statements are true?\n\ni. Roots have fungal association in the form of mycorrhiza.\n\nii. The stems are branched.\n\niii. The male or female cones or strobili are borne on the same tree. (0363)','All the statements are true.','The given image is of pinus. All of the given statements are true regarding pinus.\n(ref. NCERT biology class 11; page 38,39)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','i and iii','ii and iii','i and ii','All the statements are true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/31310q.png'),
(31311,'biology','Consider the following statements about Angiosperms:\ni. The pollen grains and ovules are developed in specialised structures called flowers.\n\nii. The seeds are enclosed in fruits.\n\niii. The giant redwood tree Sequoia is one of the tallest trees in Angiosperm.\n\niv. Wolffia is one of the smallest trees in Angiosperm.\n\nWhich of the above statements are true?','i,ii,iv','The giant redwood tree Sequoia is one of the tallest trees belonging to Gymnosperm.\nUnlike the gymnosperms where the ovules are naked, in the angiosperms or flowering\nplants, the pollen grains and ovules are developed in specialised structures called flowers. In\nangiosperms, the seeds are enclosed in fruits. The angiosperms are an exceptionally large\ngroup of plants occurring in a wide range of habitats. They range in size from the smallest\nWolffia to tall trees of Eucalyptus (over 100 metres).\n(ref. NCERT biology class 11; page 40; Paragraph 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'1','mcq','i,ii,iii','i,ii,iv','Only i and ii','All the statements are true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31312,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the plants shown in the diagram \nbelow:  \n i. Leaves in (a) have reticulate venation.\n\nii. Leaves in (b) have parallel venation.\n\niii. (a) has trimerous flowers.\n\niv. (b) has tetramerous or pentamerous flowers.\n\nWhich of the above statements are true?\n(0364)','i and ii','The dicotyledons are characterised by seeds having two cotyledons, reticulate venations\nin leaves, and tetramerous or pentamerous flowers, i.e., having four or five members in\neach floral whorls. The monocotyledons on the other hand are characterised by single\ncotyledonous seeds, parallel venation in leaves, and trimerous flowers having three\nmembers in each floral whorls. \n(ref. NCERT biology class 11; page 40; Paragraph 1)\ndiagram (e0365)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','i and iii','ii and iv','i and ii','iii and iv','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/31312q.png'),
(41406,'chemistry','ABₙ type of molecules represent  n=1,2,3,4,5,6,7','Molecules without lone pair on central atom','In the compounds of AB2, AB3, AB4, AB5 and AB6, the arrangement of electron pairs and the B atoms around the central atom A are : linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral, trigonalbipyramidal and octahedral, respectively. \n\nSuch arrangement can be seen in the molecules like BF3 (AB3), CH4 (AB4) and PCl5 (AB5) as depicted diagram by their ball and stick models. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 113; Paragraph 4) diagram(ne0407)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Molecules without lone pair on central atom','Molecules with lone pair on central atom','Molecule having only lone pairs','None of these','NEW','11-113','11-113',NULL),
(31313,'biology','Consider the following statements about reproduction in Angiosperms:\ni. Each ovule has a megaspore mother cell that undergoes meiosis to form four haploid megaspores. One of them degenerates and three fuse to produce the triploid primary endosperm nucleus (PEN).\n\nii. Each embryo-sac has a three-celled egg apparatus consisting of one egg cell and two polar nuclei.\n\niii. The pollen tubes enter the embryo-sac where one male gamete is discharged.\n\niv. The synergids and antipodals provide nourishment to the developing embryo.\n\nWhich of the above statements are true?','None of the above','Each ovule has a megaspore mother cell that undergoes meiosis to form four haploid\nmegaspores. Three of them degenerate and one divide to form the embryo sac. Each\nembryo-sac has a three-celled egg apparatus – one egg cell and two synergids, three\nantipodal cells and two polar nuclei. The polar nuclei eventually fuse to produce a diploid\nsecondary nucleus.\nThe pollen grains germinate on the stigma and the resulting pollen tubes grow through the\ntissues of stigma and style and reach the ovule. The pollen tubes enter the embryo-sac\nwhere two male gametes are discharged. One of the male gametes fuses with the egg cell\n(syngamy) to form a zygote. The other male gamete fuses with the diploid secondary\nnucleus to produce the triploid primary endosperm nucleus (PEN). Because of the\noccurrence of two fusions i.e., syngamy and triple fusion, this event is termed as double\nfertilisation, an event unique to angiosperms. The zygote develops into an embryo (with one\nor two cotyledons) and the PEN develops into endosperm which provides nourishment\nto the developing embryo. The synergids and antipodals degenerate after fertilisation.\n(ref. NCERT biology class 11; page 40,41)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','i,ii,iii','ii,iii,iv','Only i and ii','None of the above','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31314,'biology','Life cycle of 3 plants- X,Y and Z are described below:\n\nX: A dominant, independent, photosynthetic, thalloid or erect phase is represented by a haploid gametophyte and it alternates with the short- lived multicellular sporophyte totally or partially dependent on the gametophyte for its anchorage and nutrition.\n\nY: The diploid sporophyte is represented by a dominant, independent, photosynthetic, vascular plant body. It alternates with multicellular, saprophytic/autotrophic,independent but short-lived haploid gametophyte.\n\nZ: The diploid sporophyte is the dominant, photosynthetic, independent phase of the plant. The gametophytic phase is represented by the single to few-celled haploid gametophyte.\n\nChoose the correct statement:','Plant Z maybe a gymnosperm or angiosperm.','A dominant, independent, photosynthetic, thalloid or erect phase is represented by a haploid gametophyte and it alternates with the short- lived multicellular sporophyte totally or partially dependent on the gametophyte for its anchorage and nutrition. All bryophytes represent this pattern. The diploid sporophyte is represented by a dominant, independent, photosynthetic, vascular plant body. It alternates with multicellular, saprophytic/autotrophic, independent but short-lived haploid gametophyte. All pteridophytes exhibit this pattern. The diploid sporophyte is the dominant, photosynthetic, independent phase of the plant. The gametophytic phase is represented by the single to few-celled haploid gametophyte. All seed bearing plants i.eGymnosperms and angiosperms, follow this pattern.\n(ref. NCERT biology class 11; page 42,43)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Plant X is most probably a pteridophyte.','Plant Y is most probably a bryophyte.','Plant Z maybe a gymnosperm or angiosperm.','All of them.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31315,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n1. The main plant body of the bryophyte is diploid.\n\n2. The sex organs in bryophytes are unicellular.\n\n3. Bryophytes are used for medicinal purposes and as soil-binders.\n\n4. In pteridophytes, the main plant body is a sporophyte.\n\nAll of them statements are false except:','Only 4','The main plant body of the bryophyte is haploid. The sex organs in bryophytes are multicellular. In pteridophytes, the main plant body is a sporophyte. Pteridophytes are used for medicinal purposes and as soil-binders.\n(ref. NCERT biology class 11; page 35,36)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','1,2,4','1,3,4','Only 3','Only 4','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31316,'biology','Consider the following life cycle of a plant X:\n\nThe sporangia produce spores by meiosis in spore mother cells. The spores germinate to give rise to inconspicuous photosynthetic thalloid gametophytes called prothallus. The gametophytes bear male and female sex organs called antheridia and\narchegonia, respectively. Fusion of male gamete with the egg present in the archegonium results in the formation of zygote. The female gametophytes in these plants are retained on the parent sporophytes for variable periods. The development\nof the zygotes into young embryos takes place within the female gametophytes.\n\nPlant X most probably belongs to:','Pteridophyta','The sporophytes bear sporangia that are subtended by leaf-like appendages called sporophylls. In some cases sporophylls may form distinct compact structures called strobili or cones (Selaginella, Equisetum). The sporangia produce spores by meiosis in spore mother cells. The spores germinate to give rise to inconspicuous, small but multicellular,free-living, mostly photosynthetic thalloid gametophytes called prothallus. The gametophytes bear male and female sex organs called antheridia and archegonia, respectively. Water is required for transfer of antherozoids – the male gametes released from the antheridia, to the mouth of archegonium. Fusion of male gamete with the egg present in the archegonium results in the formation of zygote. The development of the zygotes into young embryos takes place within the female gametophytes. This event is a precursor to the seed habit considered an important step in evolution.\n(ref. NCERT biology class 11; page 37,38)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Bryophyta','Pteridophyta','Angiospermae','Gymnospermae','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31317,'biology','Consider the following life cycle of a plant X:\n\nIt produces haploid microspores and megaspores. The megaspore mother cell is differentiated from one of the cells of the nucellus. The nucellus is protected by envelopes and the composite structure is called an ovule. The pollen grain is released\nfrom the microsporangium. They come in contact with the opening of the ovules borne on megasporophylls. Following fertilisation, zygote develops into an embryo and the ovules into seeds.\n\nPlant X most probably belongs to:','Gymnospermae','The gymnosperms are heterosporous; they produce haploid microspores and megaspores.The two kinds of spores are produced within sporangia that are borne on sporophylls which are arranged spirally along an axis to form lax or compact strobili or cones. The strobili bearing microsporophylls and microsporangia are called microsporangiate or male strobili. The development of pollen grains take place within the microsporangia. The cones bearing megasporophylls with ovules or megasporangia are called macrosporangiate or female strobili.The megaspore mother cell is differentiated from one of the cells of the nucellus. The nucellus is protected by envelopes and the composite structure is called an ovule. The ovules are borne on megasporophylls which may be clustered to form the female cones. The megaspore mother cell divides meiotically to form four megaspores. One of the megaspores enclosed within the megasporangium develops into a multicellular female gametophyte that bears two or more archegonia or female sex organs. The pollen grain is released from the microsporangium. They are carried in air currents and come in contact with the opening of the ovules borne on megasporophylls. The pollen tube carrying the male gametes grows towards archegonia in the ovules and discharge their contents near the mouth of the archegonia. Following fertilisation, zygote develops into an embryo and the ovules into seeds. These seeds are not covered.\n(ref. NCERT biology class 11; page 38,39)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Bryophyta','Pteridophyta','Angiospermae','Gymnospermae','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31318,'biology','Consider the following statements:\ni. Bryophytes can live in soil but are dependent on water for sexual reproduction.\n\nii. In pteridophytes the main plant is a sporophyte which is differentiated into true root, stem and leaves.\n\niii. In gymnosperms ovules are not enclosed by any ovary wall.\n\niv. Double fertilisation is unique to angiosperms.\n\nWhich of the above statements are true?','All the statements are true','Bryophytes are plants which can live in soil but are dependent on water for sexual reproduction. In pteridophytes the main plant is a sporophyte which is differentiated into true root, stem and leaves. The gymnosperms are the plants in which ovules are not enclosed by any ovary wall. In angiosperms, One male gamete fuses with egg cell(syngamy) and other fuses with diploid secondary nucleus (triple fusion). This phenomenon of two fusions is called double fertilisation and is unique to angiosperms.\n(ref. NCERT biology class 11; page 43,44)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii','iii and iv','Only iv','All the statements are true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31319,'biology','Which of the following group of plants bear archegonia?\ni. Mosses \n\nii. Liverworts \n\niii. Horsetails \n\niv. Ferns \n\nv. Gymnosperms','All of them','Archegonia is the female reproductive organ in ferns and mosses and in some cycads and gymnosperms too.(ref. NCERT biology class 11; page 43,44)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','i,ii,iii,iv','ii,iii,iv','Only iv and v','All of them','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31320,'biology','Mention the ploidy of the following:\ni. Protonemal cell of a moss\n\nii. Primary endosperm nucleus in dicot\n\niii. Leaf cell of a moss\n\niv. Prothallus cell of a ferm\n\nv. Gemma cell in Marchantia','n,3n,n,n,n respectively','Protonema occurs in the life cycle of Funaria. The spore is the first cell of gametophytic generation and it germinates to form a filamentous branched alga-like structure called protonema. If gives rise to the new plant. protonema is haploid.  primary endosperm nucleus is formed as a result of double fertilisation. it is formed by fusion of male nuclei and central nuclei making it a triploid. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 36; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','n,2n,2n,n,n respectively','2n,3n,n,n,n respectively','n,3n,n,n,n respectively','n,3n,2n,n,n respectively','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(31321,'biology','Match List 1 with List 2:','Chlamydomonas-Algae, Cycas-Gymnosperm, Selaginella-Pteridophyte, Sphagnum-Moss','Chlamydomonas is an example of green algae, cycas belongs to cycadae, gymnosperms. selaginella is a pteridophyte and sphagnum is a moss, a bryophyte. (ref. NCERT biology class 11; page 43,44)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','match','Chlamydomonas, Algae','Cycas, Gymnosperm','Selaginella, Pteridophyte','Sphagnum, Moss','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31322,'biology','Choose the correct statement for bryophytes \n\n(i) They play an important role in plant succession on bare rocks/soil. \n\n(ii)The main plant body of the bryophyte is diploid. It produces gametes, hence is called a gametophyte. \n\n(iii)They decompose rocks making the substrate suitable for the growth of higher plants. \n\n(iv)The bryophytes are divided into liverworts and mosses.','(i), (iii), (iv)','They play an important role in plant succession on bare rocks/soil. The main plant body of the bryophyte is haploid. It produces gametes, hence is called a gametophyte.They decompose rocks making the substrate suitable for the growth of higher plants. The bryophytes are divided into liverworts and mosses.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 35; paragraph no. 1,2,3)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'1','mcq','(i), (iii), (iv)','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','(i), (iii)','(i), (ii), (iv)','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31323,'biology','Choose the correct statements for gymnosperms \n(i)The leaves may be simple or compound.\n\n(ii)In Cycas the palmate leaves persist for a few years \n\n(iii)The cones bearing megasporophylls with ovules or megasporangia are called microsporangiate or female strobili \n\n(iv)The ovules are borne on megasporophylls which may be free to form the female cones','only (i)','The leaves may be simple or compound.In Cycas the pinnate leaves persist for a few years. The cones bearing megasporophylls with ovules or megasporangia are called macrosporangiate or female strobili. The ovules are borne on megasporophylls which may be clustered to form the female cones. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 38,39)','Diversity In Living World >> Plant Kingdom >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','only (i)','(i), (ii)','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','only (iv)','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340101,'biology','Biotechnology deals with industrial scale production of biopharmaceuticals and biologicals using genetically modified','All of them','Biotechnology deals with industrial scale production of biopharmaceuticals and biologicals using genetically modified microbes, fungi, plants and animals.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Microbes only','Fungi Only','Plants and animals only','All of them','NEW','12-207','12-177',NULL),
(340102,'biology','The applications of biotechnology includes all, except','Conventional hybridization','The applications of biotechnology include therapeutics, diagnostics, genetically modified crops for agriculture, processed food, bioremediation, waste treatment, and\nenergy production.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Therapeutics And Diagnostics','Food processing','Waste Treatment, And Energy Production.','Conventional hybridization','NEW','12-207','12-177',NULL),
(340103,'biology','Which of the following does not come under applications of biotechnology?','Culturing disease causing organisms','Culturing disease causing organisms doesnt come under applications of biotechnology.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Bioremediation','Culturing disease causing organisms','waste treatment','Energy production','NEW','12-207','12-177',NULL),
(340104,'biology','Three critical research areas of biotechnology are. \nA. Providing the best catalyst in the form of the improved organisms usually a microbe or pure enzyme.\n\nB. Multiple Ovulation Transfer Technology (MOET). \n\nC. Create optimal conditions through engineering for a catalyst to act. \n\nD. Downstream processing technologies.','A ,C and D','Three of the most critical research areas of biotechnology are providing the best catalyst in the form of an improved organism, usually in the form of a microbe or pure enzyme, creating optimal conditions through engineering for a catalyst to function, and downstream processing technologies to purify the protein/organic compound. Multiple Ovulation transfer technology (MOET) is a technique to increase the reproductive rate of organisms and is not a critical area of biotechnology.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','A and B','B and C','A, B and C','A ,C and D','NEW','12-207','12-177',NULL),
(340105,'biology','Biotechnology, essentially deals with Industrial and Domestic scale production of biopharmaceuticals and biologicals using genetically modified microbes, fungi, plants and animals','FALSE','Biotechnology deals with industrial scale production of biopharmaceuticals and biologicals using genetically modified microbes, fungi, plants and animals','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-207','12-177',NULL),
(340106,'biology','Which of the following is  for increasing food production?\ni) Agro-chemical based agriculture.\nii) Organic agriculture\niii)Genetically Improved crop varieties','i, ii and iii','Agro-chemical based agriculture, Organic agriculture and Genetically engineered crop-based agriculture are for increasing food production.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','ii and iii','i and ii','iii','i, ii and iii','NEW','12-207','12-177',NULL),
(340107,'biology','Number of times food supply increased because of green revolution?','3 times','The increase in the yeild during green revolution was mainly due to improved crop yeild, it succeeded in tripling the food supply.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','3 times','2 times','4 times','None of the above','NEW','12-208','12-179',NULL),
(340108,'biology','The increase in yield by green revolution was  due to','All of them','Increased yields have partly been due to the use of improved crop varieties, but mainly due to the use of better management practices and use of agrochemicals (fertilisers and pesticides).','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Improved crop varieties','Better management practices','Use of fertilizers and pesticides','All of them','NEW','12-208','12-179',NULL),
(340109,'biology','The increase in yield by green revolution was mainly due to\n \nA.Improved crop varieties \n\nB.better management practices \n\nC.agrochemicals','B and C','Increased yields have partly been due to the use of improved crop varieties, but mainly due to the use of better management practices and use of agrochemicals (fertilisers and pesticides)','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','A and B','B and C','A and C','A ,B and C','NEW','12-208','12-179',NULL),
(340110,'biology','GM plants have been useful to','Reduce post harvest loss','Some benefits of genetic engineering in agriculture are increased crop yields, reduced costs for food or drug production, reduced need for pesticides, enhanced nutrient composition and food quality, resistance to pests and disease, greater food security, and medical benefits to the world\'s growing population.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Increase the water holding capacity of aquatic plants','Reduce post harvest loss','Increase need of pesticides','Developing wild crop varieties','NEW','12-208','12-179',NULL),
(340111,'biology','Plants, bacteria, fungi and animals whose genes have been altered by manipulation are called','Genetically modified organisms','Plants, bacteria, fungi and animals whose genes have been altered by manipulation are called Genetically Modified Organisms (GMO).','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Genetically modified organisms','Hybrid organisms','Pest resistant organisms','None of the above','NEW','12-208','12-179',NULL),
(340112,'biology','Genetic Modification has not been used to','Transfer the gene from parent to offspring','Genetic modification by the introduction of the recombinant DNA has resulted in the creation of tailor-made plants that can be pest resistant, drought resistant, herbicide resistant, etc. It has supplied alternative to dairy farm industries by modifying plants and animals. It has also resulted in the creation of biofortified crops that have enhanced nutritional values of food.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Create Tailor Made Plants','Supply Alternative Resources To Industries','Enhanced Nutritional Value Of Food','Transfer the gene from parent to offspring','NEW','12-208','12-179',NULL),
(340113,'biology','GM plant  golden rice is rich in','Vitamin A','Golden rice is a genetically modified, biofortified crop. Biofortification increases the nutritional value of crops. Golden rice is genetically modified to produce beta-carotene, which is not normally present in rice. Beta-carotene is converted into vitamin A when metabolized by the human body.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Vitamin A','Iron','Vitamin b','Vitamin K','NEW','12-208','12-179',NULL),
(340114,'biology','Strains of Bacillus thuringiensis are used in producing','Bioinsecticidal plants','Bacillus thuringiensis strains have been used for designing bio insecticidal plants. A gene from this bacteria have insecticidal property which is transferred to cotton plants to produce Bt cotton which is resistant I to bollworm insect which is a major pest of cotton.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Bioinsecticidal plants','Biominer zation','Biometullurgical techniques','Biofertilizers','NEW','12-208','12-179',NULL),
(340115,'biology','a.The applications of biotechnology is the \na) production of pest resistant crops. \n\nb) sustaining wild variety of crops \n\nc) reducing post harvest loss \n\nd) preventing early exhaustion of soil fertility. \n\nWhich of the following statement is true for biotechnology applications','a, c and d','Some of the applications of biotechnology in agriculture are the production of pest resistant plants, which could decrease the amount of pesticide used.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','a and d only','a, b and c','a only','a, c and d','NEW','12-208','12-179',NULL),
(340116,'biology','Bt toxin is produced by a bacterium called ___________','Bacillus thuringiensis','Bt toxin is produced by a bacterium called Bacillus thuringiensis','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Bacillus vulgaris','Bacillus thuringiensis','Bacillus ramosus','all the above','NEW','12-208','12-179',NULL),
(340117,'biology','Bt toxin gene has been cloned from the bacteria and been expressed in plants to provide resistance to insects without the need for insecticides. Select these resistant plants from the given list \n1. Cotton \n\n2. Corn \n\n3. Rice \n\n4. Tomato \n\n5. Potato \n\n6. Soyabean.','all of these','Bt toxin gene has been cloned from the bacteria and been expressed in plants to provide resistance to insects without the need for insecticides; in effect created a bio-pesticide. Examples are Bt cotton, Bt corn, rice, tomato, potato and soyabean etc','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','1,2,3,4','2,3,4,5 only','1,2,5,6','all of these','NEW','12-208','12-179',NULL),
(340118,'biology','Which of the statement is not true for the applications of biotechnology','Helps to maintain the ecological balance','The applications of Biotechnology includes, made crops more tolerant to abiotic stresses (cold, drought, salt, heat).\nReduced reliance on chemical pesticides (pest-resistant crops).\nHelped to reduce post harvest losses.\nIncreased efficiency of mineral usage by plants (this prevents early\nexhaustion of fertility of soil).\nEnhanced nutritional value of food, e.g., golden rice, i.e., Vitamin ‘A’\nenriched rice. Maintaing ecological balance is drawback of using GMO or crops.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','3','mcq','Production of pest resistance plants to decrease the use of pesticides','Enhanced nutritional value of the food crops','Helps to maintain the ecological balance','Reduces the post harvest loss','NEW','12-208','12-179',NULL),
(340119,'biology','What are the ways in agriculture to increase the yield from fields and reduce the harmful effects on environment','Use of Genetically Modified Crops','For farmers in the developing world, agrochemicals are often too expensive, and further increases in yield with existing varieties are not possible using conventional breeding. The alternative path that our understanding of genetics can show so that farmers may obtain maximum yield from their fields. The way to minimise the use of fertilisers and chemicals so that their harmful effects on the environment are reduced by using genetically modified crops. This is a possible solution.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Conventional breeding','Use of Genetically Modified Crops','Use of agrochemicals','All of them','NEW','12-208','12-179',NULL),
(340120,'biology','Green revolution is related to _______________','Agriculture','Green revolution occured between 1950-1960. It is related to agriculture. It mainly focused on increasing agricultural production. It started in Mexico.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Milk','Water','Animals','Agriculture','NEW','12-208','12-179',NULL),
(340121,'biology','Who is the Father of Green Revolution in India','M.S Swaminathan','The green revolution is led by Norman Borlaug. In India it started by M S Swaminathan. Especially in states of Haryana, Punjab and Uttar Pradesh','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','3','mcq','M. K Gandhi','M.S Swaminathan','Neel Kranthi','Norman Borlaug','NEW','12-208','12-179',NULL),
(340122,'biology','GMO stands for','Genitically Modified Organisms','GMO Stands for Genitically Modified Organisms.The genes of the organisms are modified to get the desired product are called GMO, these can be bacteria, plants, fungi or animal','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Genetically Mutant Organisms','Genitically Modified Organisms','Genitically Matured Organisms','Genetically Modified Organs','NEW','12-208','12-178',NULL),
(340123,'biology','Was green revolution enough to feed the growing human population?','No','The Green Revolution succeeded in tripling the food supply but yet\nit was not enough to feed the growing human population','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Yes','No','more than enough food was produced, that helped India to even export','none of the above','NEW','12-208','12-178',NULL),
(340124,'biology','WHICH OF THE FOLLOWING IS CORRECT?','for farmers in the developing world,\nagrochemicals are often too expensive','However, for farmers in the developing world,\nagrochemicals are often too expensive, and further increases in yield with\nexisting varieties are not possible using conventional breeding.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','1','mcq','for farmers in the developing world,\nagrochemicals are often not expensive','for farmers in the developing world,\nagrochemicals are often too expensive','further increases in yield with\nexisting varieties are possible using conventional breeding.','further increases in yield with\nexisting varieties are not possible using hybridization.','NEW','12-208','12-178',NULL),
(340125,'biology','GMO has made crops more tolerant to','all of these','GM\nplants have been useful in many ways. Genetic modification has:\n(i) made crops more tolerant to abiotic stresses (cold, drought, salt,\nheat).','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','1','mcq','Cold and Heat','Drought','salt','all of these','NEW','12-208','12-179',NULL),
(340126,'biology','Tissue culture was learnt by scientists in ______.','1950s','It was learnt by scientists, during 1950s, that whole plants could be regenerated from explants.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','1940s','1950s','1960s','1970s','NEW',NULL,'12-178',NULL),
(340127,'biology','_______ is the technique whereby whole plants could be regenerated from explants.','Tissue culture','It was learnt by scientists, during 1950s, that whole plants could be regenerated from explants.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','1','mcq','Biosynthesis','Biofortification','Fortification','Tissue culture','NEW',NULL,'12-178',NULL),
(340128,'biology','______ is the part of a plant taken out and grown in a test tube.','Explants','Explants, i.e., any part of a plant taken out and grown in a test tube','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Explants','Implants','Stem','Pollen','NEW',NULL,'12-178','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/340128q.png'),
(340129,'biology','The capacity to generate a whole plant from any cell/explant is called _______.','Totipotency','This capacity to generate a whole plant from any cell/explant is called totipotency.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Pluripotency','Totipotency','Nuclear reprogramming','Regeneration','NEW',NULL,'12-178','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/340129q.png'),
(340130,'biology','Totipotency is the basic principle of','Tissue culture','Totipotency is the basic principle of tissue culture.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Tissue culture','Sericulture','Pisciculture','Silviculture','NEW',NULL,'12-178','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/340130q.png'),
(340131,'biology','The nutrient medium for totipotency must provide a _______ source..','Carbon','The nutrient medium must provide a carbon source such as sucrose and also inorganic salts, vitamins, amino acids and growth regulators like auxins, cytokinins etc.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','1','mcq','Oxygen','Nitrogen','Carbon','Iron','NEW',NULL,'12-178',NULL),
(340132,'biology','Growth regulators for totipotency include\n i) Auxin   \nii) Myosin   \niii)Cytokininis    \niv)ADP','Both i and iii','The nutrient medium must provide a carbon source such as sucrose and also inorganic salts, vitamins, amino acids and growth regulators like auxins, cytokinins etc.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','1','mcq','Both i and ii','Both i and iii','Both i and iv','Both ii and iii','NEW',NULL,'12-178',NULL),
(340133,'biology','The method of producing thousands of plants through tissue culture is called __________.','Micro-propagation','The method of producing thousands of plants through tissue culture is called micro- propagation.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Propagation','Micro-propagation','Radial propagation','Macro-propagation','NEW',NULL,'12-178',NULL),
(340134,'biology','Plants produced by means of micro-propagating are _____________.','Genetically identitical','Each of these plants will be genetically identical to the original plant from which they were grown, i.e., they are somaclones.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','1','mcq','Genetically identitical','Genetically non identical','Genetic hybrids','Genetically recombinant','NEW',NULL,'12-178',NULL),
(340135,'biology','Genetically identitical plants are called ________.','Somaclones','Each of these plants will be genetically identical to the original plant from which they were grown, i.e., they are somaclones.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','1','mcq','Gametoclone','Somaclones','Gymnosperms','Angiosperms','NEW',NULL,'12-178',NULL),
(340136,'biology','Food plants like ______ and _______ have been produced on commercial scale using totipotency.','Tomato, Banana','Many important food plants like tomato, banana, apple, etc., have been produced on commercial scale using this method.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Tomato, Avacado','Tomato, Banana','Orange, Banana','Tomato, Orange','NEW',NULL,'12-178',NULL),
(340137,'biology','Even if the plant is infected with a virus, which part of the plant is free of virus?','Meristem','Even if the plant is infected with a virus, the meristem (apical and axillary) is free of virus.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Flowers','Petals','Roots','Meristem','NEW',NULL,'12-178',NULL),
(340138,'biology','Scientists have succeeded in culturing meristems of ___________','Banana','Scientists have succeeded in culturing meristems of banana, sugarcane, potato, etc.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Banana','Tomato','Beans','Peppers','NEW',NULL,'12-178',NULL),
(340139,'biology','Scientists have even isolated single cells from plants and after digesting their cell walls have been able to isolate naked___________.','Protoplasts','Scientists have even isolated single cells from plants and after digesting their cell walls have been able to isolate naked protoplasts (surrounded by plasma membranes).','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Protoplasts','Chloroplasts','Chlorophyll','Stomata','NEW',NULL,'12-178',NULL),
(340140,'biology','Isolated protoplasts from two different varieties of plants can be fused to get hybrid protoplasts, which can be further grown to form a new plant. These hybrids are called=','Somatic hybrids','Isolated protoplasts from two different varieties of plants – each having a desirable character – can be fused to get hybrid protoplasts, which can be further grown to form a new plant. These hybrids are called somatic hybrids while the process is called somatic hybridisation.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Sexual hybrids','Somatic hybrids','Genetic hybrids','Somatohybrids','NEW',NULL,'12-178','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/340140q.png'),
(340141,'biology','Isolated protoplasts from two different varieties of plants can be fused to get hybrid protoplasts, which can be further grown to form a new plant. These hybrids are called Somatic hybrids\n while the process is called ___________','Somatic hybridisation','Isolated protoplasts from two\ndifferent varieties of plants – each having a desirable character – can be\nfused to get hybrid protoplasts, which can be further grown to form a\nnew plant. These hybrids are called somatic hybrids while the process\nis called somatic hybridisation.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','fillup','So,ma,t,ic h,ybr,idisa,tion',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW',NULL,'12-178','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/340141q.png'),
(41407,'chemistry','ABₙEₙ type of molecules represent:','Molecules with lone pair on central atom','As shown in diagram \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 115; Table 4.7) diagram(ne0409)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Molecules without lone pair on central atom','Molecules with lone pair on central atom','Molecule having only lone pairs','None of these','NEW','11-113','11-113',NULL),
(340142,'biology','Formation of pomato was a result from','Somatic hybridisation','Imagine a situation when a protoplast of tomato is fused with that of potato, and then they are grown – to form new hybrid plants combining tomato and potato characteristics. Well, this has been achieved – resulting in formation of pomato; unfortunately this plant did not have all the desired combination of characteristics for its commercial utilisation.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Introduction and  Biotechnological Applications In Agriculture','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Totipotency','Somatic hybridisation','Tissue culture','Biofortification','NEW','12-208','12-179','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/340142q.png'),
(340201,'biology','Bt toxin is harmful to insects like','All of them','Bt is a bacterium that is not toxic to humans or other mammals but is toxic to certain insects when ingested. Bt works as an insecticide by producing a crystal-shaped protein (Cry toxin) that specifically kills certain insects. Bt toxin is harmful to insects like Lepidoterans (tobacco budworm, armyworms)Coleopterans (bettles)Dipterans (flies and mosquito).','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Lepidoterans (tobacco budworm, armyworms)','Coleopterans (bettles)','Dipterans (flies and mosquito)','All of them','NEW','12-208','12-179',NULL),
(340202,'biology','Bacillus thuringiensis forms protein crystals during a particular phase of their growth. These crystals contain a toxic protein ,what is it called?','Bt Toxin','B. thuringiensis forms protein crystals during a particular phase of their growth. These crystals contain an insecticidal toxin which is known as Bt toxin.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Bt Toxin','Cry','pesticide','Sry','NEW','12-208','12-179',NULL),
(340203,'biology','Bt toxin genes are isolated from Bacillus thuringiensis and incorporated into crop plants making them insecticidal. The choice of genes depend upon','Both a and b','Bt toxin genes are isolated from Bacillus thuringiensis and incorporated into crop plants making them insecticidal. The choice of genes depend upon Crop plant and targeted pests.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Crop plant only','Targeted pest only','Both a and b','Crop and Fertility of Soil','NEW','12-209','12-180',NULL),
(340204,'biology','Find unrelated to Bt toxin gene','cryIIAbc','the proteins encoded by the genes cryIAc and cryIIAb control the cotton bollworms, that of cryIAb controls corn borer.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','cryIAc','cryIIAbc','cryIAb','none','NEW','12-209','12-180',NULL),
(340205,'biology','Which of the following is correct?','Both a and b','Given following are correct statements- a. The proteins encoded by the genes crylAc and cryllAb control cotton bollworms; b. Protein encoded by crylAb controls corn borer .','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','The proteins encoded by the genes crylAc and cryllAb control cotton bollworms','Protein encoded by crylAb controls corn borer','Both a and b','Proteins encoded by crylAc and crylAb control flies','NEW','12-209','12-180',NULL),
(340206,'biology','Which Gene of Bacillus thuringiensis that codes for a protein that controls corn borer','cryIAb','There are a number of them, for example, the proteins encoded\nby the genes cryIAc and cryIIAb control the cotton bollworms, that of\ncryIAb controls corn borer.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','cryIIAb','cryIAc','cryIAb','cry gene','NEW','12-209','12-180',NULL),
(340207,'biology','cryIAc and cryIIAb endotoxins obtained from Bacillus thuringiensis are effective against?','Boll worms','The proteins encoded by the genes cryIAc and cryIIAb control the cotton bollworms effectively.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Flies','Nematodes','Boll worms','Corn borer','NEW','12-209','12-180',NULL),
(340208,'biology','Bt cotton is resistant to','Bollworm','Bacillus thuringiensis (Bt) crops are plants genetically engineered (modified) to contain the endospore (or crystal) toxins of the bacterium, Bt to be resistant to certain insect pests like bollworms ( from the given), .','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Viruses','Abiotic stress','Bollworm','Herbicide','NEW','12-209','12-180',NULL),
(340209,'biology','Which of the following nematode infects the roots of tobacco plants and causes a great reduction in yield?','Meloidegyne incognitia','Meloidogyne incognita infect the roots of tobacco plants and causes great reduction in yield.Meloidogyne arenaria (peanut root knot nematode), and the more recently introduced Meloidogyne enterolobii (guava root knot nematode, synonym Meloidogyne mayaguensis)','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Meloidegyne arenaria','Meloidegyne enterolobii','Meloidegyne incognitia','Enterobius','NEW','12-209','12-180',NULL),
(340210,'biology','The RNAi stands for','RNA interference','RNAi stands for \'RNA interference\', which is the silencing of gene expression by the administration of double-stranded RNA (dsRNA).','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','RNA inactivation','RNA initiation','RNA interference','RNA interferon','NEW','12-209','12-180',NULL),
(340211,'biology','RNA interference is a method of','cell defence','RNAi is a regulatory mechanism for an estimated 30% of all protein-coding genes(in mammals). It helps to protect cells against certain viruses by targeting viral RNA for destruction. It helps to silence potentially disruptive transposons in the genome by destroying RNA copies arising from transposon replication. RNAi is a precise and efficient tool for knockout of specific genes when studying gene function in experimental organisms. It also has potential for new forms of targeted gene therapy.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','cell proliferation','cell defence','cell differentiation','micropropagation','NEW','12-209','12-180',NULL),
(41408,'chemistry','Which one these have tetrahedral arrangement of electron pairs around central atom','Both a and b','As shown in diagram \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 114; Table 4.6) diagram(ne0408)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'1','mcq','CH₄','NH₄⁺','Both a and b','CO₃²⁻','NEW','11-113','11-113',NULL),
(340212,'biology','Which of the following is/are correct about the process of RNA interference? \n1.This is used to prevent the infestation of protozoans. \n\n2. It takes place in some eukaryotic and all prokaryotic organisms as a method of cellular defense. \n\n3. The method involves silencing of a specific mRNA due to a complementary dsRNA molecule. \n\n4. .A novel strategy was adopted to prevent infestation','3 and 4','A novel strategy was adopted to prevent this infestation which was based on the process of RNA interference (RNAi). RNAi takes place in all eukaryotic organisms as a method of cellular defense. This method involves silencing of a specific mRNA due to a complementary dsRNA molecule that binds to and prevents translation of the mRNA (silencing).','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','3 and 4','2,3 and 4','1 and 3','3 only','NEW','12-209','12-180',NULL),
(340213,'biology','mRNA silencing is called','RNAi','RNA interference (RNAi) is a biological process in which RNA molecules inhibit gene expression or translation, by neutralizing targeted mRNA molecules. Historically, RNAi was known by other names, including co-suppression, post-transcriptional gene silencing (PTGS), and quelling.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','RNAi','RNA activation','RNA is not producing interferon','RNA without initiation codon','NEW','12-209','12-180',NULL),
(340214,'biology','In RNAi, silencing of a specific mRNA due to a','complementary dsRNA','RNAi takes place in all eukaryotic organisms as a method of cellular defense. This method involves silencing of a specific mRNA due to a complementary dsRNA molecule that binds to and prevents translation of the mRNA (silencing). The source of this complementary RNA could be from an infection by viruses having RNA genomes or mobile genetic elements (transposons) that replicate via an RNA intermediate','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','complementary ssDNA','complementary dsRNA','complementary dsDNA','complementary ssRNA','NEW','12-209','12-180',NULL),
(340215,'biology','The source of this complementary dsRNA could be from','both a and b','The source of this complementary RNA could be from an infection by viruses having RNA genomes or mobile genetic elements (transposons) that replicate via an RNA intermediate','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','mobile genetic elements (transposons)','an infection by viruses having RNA genomes','both a and b','none of the above','NEW','12-209','12-180',NULL),
(340216,'biology','Which vectors were used for introduction of nematode-specific genes into the host plant','Agrobacterium','Using Agrobacterium vectors, nematode-specific genes were introduced into the host plant','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Agrobacterium','E. coli','Retroviral vector','Lentiviral vector','NEW','12-209','12-180',NULL),
(340217,'biology','Nematode-specific genes were introduced into the host plant. The introduction of DNA was such that it produced','both sense and anti-sense RNA','A nematode Meloidegyne incognitia infects the roots of tobacco plants and causes a great reduction in yield.The introduction of DNA was such that it produced both sense and anti-sense RNA in the host cells.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','both sense and anti-sense RNA','a particular hormone','an antifeedant','a toxic protein','NEW','12-209','12-180',NULL),
(340218,'biology','Which of the statemnt is correct:\n (a)The introduction of DNA into the host produce both sense and anti-sense RNA in the host cells.\n\n (b) The introduction of DNA into the host plant produce only antisense RNA strand in the host cells.','a only','The introduction of DNA was such that it produced both sense and anti-sense RNA in the host cells.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of the above','NEW','12-209','12-180',NULL),
(340219,'biology','what happened when nematode-specific genes were introduced into the host plant','All the above','Nematode-specific genes were introduced into the host plant. The introduction of DNA\nwas such that it produced both sense and anti-sense RNA in the host cells. These two RNA’s being complementary to each other formed a double stranded (dsRNA) that initiated RNAi and thus, silenced the specific mRNA of the nematode. The consequence was that the parasite could not survive in a transgenic host expressing specific interfering RNA. The transgenic\nplant therefore got itself protected from the parasite.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','The introduction of DNA was such that it produced both sense and anti-sense RNA in the host cells','two RNA’s being complementary to each other formed a double stranded (dsRNA) that initiated RNAi and thus, silenced the specific mRNA of the nematode','The transgenic plant therefore got itself protected from the parasite','All the above','NEW','12-209','12-180',NULL),
(340220,'biology','What was the consequence when parasite was introduced in a transgenic host expressing specific interfering RNA?','the parasite could not survive','The introduction of DNA was such that it produced both sense and anti-sense RNA in the host cells. These two RNA’s being complementary to each other formed a double stranded (dsRNA) that initiated RNAi and thus, silenced the specific mRNA of the nematode.The consequence was that the parasite could not survive in a transgenic host expressing specific interfering RNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','the parasite growth increased','the parasite could not survive','the parasite growth declined','none of the above','NEW','12-209','12-180',NULL),
(340221,'biology','A plant expressing a gene from another organisms is','Transgenic','A plant expressing a gene from another organisms is known as transgenic.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Transgenic','Clone','Transformed','Somoclonal variant','NEW','12-209','12-180',NULL),
(340222,'biology','Why does Bt toxin not kill\nthe Bacillus?','the Bt toxin protein exist as inactive protoxin','Why does this toxin not kill\nthe Bacillus? Actually, the Bt toxin protein exist as inactive protoxins but\nonce an insect ingest the inactive toxin, it is converted into an active form\nof toxin due to the alkaline pH of the gut','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','1','mcq','It is not self reactive','It recognises self antigens, thus doesn\'t react','The bacteria has special Anti- toxin for the Bt toxin','the Bt toxin protein exist as inactive protoxin','NEW','12-208','12-178',NULL),
(340223,'biology','How many of the following is correct?\nA. Bt toxin protein exist as inactive protoxins \nB. once an insect ingest the inactive toxin, it is converted into an active form of toxin \nC.it is converted into an active form\nof toxin due to the alkaline pH of the gut \nD. Alkaline pH crystallizes the  protoxin. \nE. The activated toxin binds to the surface of foregut epithelial cells\nF. Bt toxin create pores that cause cell swelling and lysis and eventually cause death\nof the insect.','4','Actually, the Bt toxin protein exist as inactive protoxins but\nonce an insect ingest the inactive toxin, it is converted into an active form\nof toxin due to the alkaline pH of the gut which solubilise the crystals.\nThe activated toxin binds to the surface of midgut epithelial cells and create pores that cause cell swelling and lysis and eventually cause death\nof the insect.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','1','3','4','6','NEW','12-208','12-178',NULL),
(340224,'biology','Assertion: Most Bt toxins are insect group specific. \nReason: The toxin is coded by a gene named cry.','Both A and R are correct','most Bt toxins are insect-group specific. The toxin is coded by a gene named cry. There are a number of them, for example, the proteins encoded by the genes cryIAc and cryIIAb control the cotton bollworms, that of cryIAb controls corn borer.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','1','mcq','Both A and R are correct','A is false and R is true','A and R, both are false','A is true and R is false','NEW','12-209','12-180',NULL),
(340225,'biology','Nematodes can parasitize in','All of the above','Several nematodes parasitise a wide variety of\nplants and animals including human beings.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Bt Cotton and Pest Resistant Plants','Biotechnology and its applications','1','mcq','Human','Plants','Animals','All of the above','NEW','12-209','12-180',NULL),
(340301,'biology','Assertion: The recombinant DNA technological processes have made immense impact in the area of healthcare by enabling mass production of safe and more effective therapeutic drugs. \n\nReason: the recombinant therapeutics do not induce unwanted immunological responses as is common in case of similar products isolated from non-human sources.','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','The recombinant DNA technological processes have made immense impact in the area of healthcare by enabling mass production of safe and more effective therapeutic drugs.The recombinant therapeutics do not induce unwanted immunological responses as is common in case of similar products isolated from non-human sources.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Genetically Engineered Insulin','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','A and R both are correct but R is not the correct reason for the Assertion','A and R, both are false','A is true and R is false','NEW','12-210','12-181',NULL),
(340302,'biology','At present, how many recombinant therapeutics have been approved for human-use the world  and In India respectively, Which are presently being marketed?','30,12','At present, about 30 recombinant therapeutics have been approved for human-use the world over. In India, 12 of these are presently being marketed.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Genetically Engineered Insulin','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','30,14','30,12','12,40','12,30','NEW','12-210','12-181',NULL),
(340303,'biology','Assertion:Management of adult-onset diabetes is possible by taking insulin at regular time intervals.\n\nReason:Insulin can be orally administered to diabetic people.','A is true and R is false','Management of adult-onset diabetes is possible by taking insulin at regular time intervals.Insulin cannot be orally administered to diabetic people.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Genetically Engineered Insulin','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','A and R both are correct but R is not the correct reason for the Assertion','A and R, both are false','A is true and R is false','NEW','12-210','12-181',NULL),
(340304,'biology','Why insulin not administered orally to diabetic patient?','because it is digestible','Insulin cannot be taken by mouth because it is digestible. Oral insulin would be obliterated in the stomach, long before it reached the bloodstream where it is needed. Once injected, it starts to work and is used up in a matter of hours.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Genetically Engineered Insulin','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Insulin is bitter in taste','because it is digestible','Insulin will lead to sudden decrease in blood sugar if given orally','Insulin leads to peptic ulcer if given orally','NEW','12-210','12-181',NULL),
(340305,'biology','Insulin used for diabetes was earlier extracted from pancreas of __________','Both Slaughtered cattle and Pigs','Insulin used for diabetes was earlier extracted from pancreas of slaughtered cattle and pigs','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Genetically Engineered Insulin','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Humans','Slaughtered cattle','Pigs','Both Slaughtered cattle and Pigs','NEW','12-211','12-182',NULL),
(340306,'biology','Insulin consists of how many polypeptide chains?','2','Insulin consists of two short polypeptide chains : chain A and chain B','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Genetically Engineered Insulin','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','1','3','2','4','NEW','12-211','12-182',NULL),
(340307,'biology','Insulin consists of two short polypeptide chains: chain A and chain B, that are linked together by ____________','disulphide bridges','Insulin consists of two short polypeptide chains: chain A and chain B, that are linked together by disulphide bridges','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Genetically Engineered Insulin','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','hydrogen bonds','disulphide bridges','DNA ligase','Covalent bonds','NEW','12-211','12-182',NULL),
(340308,'biology','Assertion: In mammals, including humans, insulin is synthesised as a pro-hormone which contains an extra peptide called the C peptide.\n\nReason: Like a pro-enzyme protein, the pro-hormone peptide(protein) also needs to be processed before it becomes a fully mature and functional hormone.','A and R both are correct but R is not the correct reason for the Assertion','In mammals, including humans, insulin is synthesised as a pro-hormone (like a pro-enzyme, the pro-hormone also needs to be processed before it becomes a fully mature and functional hormone) Which contains an extra strech called C peptide,this is to make sure the enzyme or hormone acts on its target site and does not act prematurely or earlier or at any other site than required.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Genetically Engineered Insulin','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','A and R both are correct but R is not the correct reason for the Assertion','A and R, both are false','A is true and R is false','NEW','12-211','12-182',NULL),
(340309,'biology','Which of the following peptide chain is removed during maturation of pro-insulin into insulin?','C-peptide','C peptide is not present in the mature insulin and is removed during maturation proinsulin into insulin.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Genetically Engineered Insulin','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','A peptide','B-peptide','C-peptide','A and C peptide','NEW','12-211','12-182',NULL),
(340310,'biology','The bacterium used in the systhesis of human insulin artificially is ?','Escherichia coli','The bacterium used in the systhesis of human insulin artificially is Escherichia coli,By culturing large quantities of the modified bacteria and then killing and opening them,the insulin could be extracted,purified and used very inexpensively.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Genetically Engineered Insulin','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Clostridium botulinum','Escherichia coli','Bacilus vulgaris','Acetobacter aceti','NEW','12-211','12-182',NULL),
(340311,'biology','Eli Lilly company produced DNA sequences corresponding to following types of chains of Insulin gene.\n 1.A chain \n\n2. B chain \n\n3. C chain\n \n4. D chain.','1 and 2','In 1983, Eli Lilly an American company prepared two DNA sequences corresponding to A and B, chains of human insulin and introduced them in plasmids of E. coli to produce insulin chains. Chains A and B were produced separately, extracted and combined by creating disulfide bonds to form human insulin.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Genetically Engineered Insulin','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','1','2','1 and 2','1,2,3 and 4','NEW','12-211','12-182',NULL),
(340312,'biology','The main challenge for production of insulin using ____________ was getting insulin assembled into a mature form.','rDNA techniques','The main\nchallenge for production of insulin using rDNA techniques\nwas getting insulin assembled into a mature form.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Genetically Engineered Insulin','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Genetic modification','rDNA techniques','Genetic correction','Gene Therapy','NEW','12-211','12-182',NULL),
