(340707,'biology','Choose the incorrect  :\n1) There are an estimated 200,000 varieties of rice in India alone.\n\n 2)The diversity of rice in India is second richest in the world. \n\n3)  Indian Basmati was crossed with semi-dwarf varieties and claimed as an invention or a novelty. \n\n4)Most of the industrialised nations are poor financially but rich in biodiversity and traditional knowledge.','2 and 4','1) There are an estimated 200,000 varieties of rice in India alone. The diversity of rice in India is one of the richest in the world.2)  Indian Basmati was crossed with semi-dwarf varieties and claimed as an invention or a novelty. 3)Most of the industrialised nations are rich financially but poor in biodiversity and traditional knowledge.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Ethical Issues','Biotechnology and its applications','3','mcq','1 and 2','2 and 4','2,3 and 4','1,2,3 and 4','NEW','12-214','12-185',NULL),
(340708,'biology','Basmati rice is distinct for its unique aroma and flavour,there is Reference   to Basmati in ___________','all the above','There is Reference   to Basmati in ancient texts, folklore and poetry, as it has been grown for centuries.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Ethical Issues','Biotechnology and its applications','3','mcq','poetry','folklore','ancient texts','all the above','NEW','12-214','12-185',NULL),
(340709,'biology','In 1997,  Company got patent rights on Basmati rice through the US Patent and Trademark Office Was from?','America','In 1997, an American company got patent rights on Basmati rice through the US Patent and Trademark Office.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Ethical Issues','Biotechnology and its applications','3','mcq','India','America','Russia','Europe','NEW','12-214','12-185',NULL),
(340710,'biology','Traditional knowledge related to bio-resources can be exploited to develop modern applications and can also be used in commercialisation to save ?','all the above','Traditional knowledge related to bio-resources can be exploited to develop modern applications and can also be used to save time, effort and expenditure during their commercialisation.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Ethical Issues','Biotechnology and its applications','3','mcq','time','effort','expenditure','all the above','NEW','12-214','12-185',NULL),
(41412,'chemistry','The possible angles in Trigonal bipyramidal geometry','90°, 120°','The possible angles in Trigonal bipyramidal geometry is 90°, 120°.\n\nAs shown in diagram \n\nReference : ( NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 114; Table 4.6) diagram(ne0410)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'3','mcq','90°, 180°','180°, 120°','90°, 120°','90°, 100°','NEW','11-113','11-113',NULL),
(340711,'biology','The Indian Parliament has recently cleared which amendment of the Indian Patents Bill?','second','The Indian Parliament has recently cleared the second amendment of the Indian Patents Bill, that takes such issues into consideration, including patent terms emergency provisions and research and development initiative.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Ethical Issues','Biotechnology and its applications','3','mcq','third','first','second','fourth','NEW','12-214','12-185',NULL),
(340712,'biology','Use of bio-resources by multinational companies and other organisations without proper authorisation from the countries and people concerned without compensatory payment is called?','Biopiracy','Use of bio-resources by multinational companies and other organisations without proper authorisation from the countries and people concerned without compensatory payment is called Biopiracy.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Ethical Issues','Biotechnology and its applications','3','mcq','Biodiversity','Biopiracy','Biopatent','Bio resources','NEW','12-214','12-185',NULL),
(340713,'biology','Indian Patents bill takes consideration into issues like','All of these','The Indian Parliament has recently cleared the second amendment\nof the Indian Patents Bill, that takes such issues into consideration,\nincluding patent terms emergency provisions and research and\ndevelopment initiative.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Ethical Issues','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','patent terms emergency provisions','Research','Development initiatives','All of these','NEW','12-214','12-185',NULL),
(340714,'biology','How many of the following are correct about ethical issues regarding biotechnology? \nA. There has been growing realisation of the injustice, inadequate\ncompensation and benefit sharing between undeveloped and developing\ncountries.\nB.  some nations are developing laws to prevent such\nunauthorised exploitation of their bio-resources and traditional\nknowledge.\nC. the developing and\nthe underdeveloped world is rich in biodiversity and traditional\nknowledge related to bio-resources.  D. Several attempts\nhave also been made to patent uses, products and processes based on\nIndian traditional herbal medicines, e.g., turmeric neem.','3','There has been growing realisation of the injustice, inadequate\ncompensation and benefit sharing between developed and developing\ncountries. Therefore, some nations are developing laws to prevent such\nunauthorised exploitation of their bio-resources and traditional\nknowledge.  Several attempts\nhave also been made to patent uses, products and processes based on\nIndian traditional herbal medicines, e.g., turmeric neem. In contrast the developing and\nthe underdeveloped world is rich in biodiversity and traditional\nknowledge related to bio-resources.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Ethical Issues','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-214','12-185',NULL),
(340715,'biology','What is true about the patent granted on Basmati to the American company?  \nA. This allowed the company to sell a ‘new’ variety of\nBasmati, in the US and abroad.\nB. This ‘new’ variety of Basmati had actually\nbeen derived from Indian farmer’s varieties.\nC. The\npatent extends to functional equivalents, implying that other people\nselling Basmati rice could not be restricted by the patent. \nD. If we are\nnot vigilant and immediately counter these patent applications,\nother countries/individuals may encash on our rich legacy and we may be able to do anything about it.','A and B','This allowed the company to sell a ‘new’ variety of\nBasmati, in the US and abroad. This ‘new’ variety of Basmati had actually\nbeen derived from Indian farmer’s varieties. The\npatent extends to functional equivalents, implying that other people\nselling Basmati rice could be restricted by the patent. If we are not\nvigilant and we do not immediately counter these patent applications,\nother countries/individuals may encash on our rich legacy and we may\nnot be able to do anything about it.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Ethical Issues','Biotechnology and its applications','1','mcq','A and B','A and C','B and C','C and D','NEW','12-214','12-185',NULL),
(340716,'biology','There is growing public anger that certain companies are being\ngranted patents for products and technologies that make use of the\n_______, plants and other ________ resources that have long\nbeen identified, developed and used by farmers and ________ people\nof a specific region/country. Fill in the blanks.','genetic materials, biological, indigenous','There is growing public anger that certain companies are being\ngranted patents for products and technologies that make use of the\ngenetic materials, plants and other biological resources that have long\nbeen identified, developed and used by farmers and indigenous people\nof a specific region/country','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Ethical Issues','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','genetic materials, biological, indigenous','indigenous, genetic materials, biological','biological, indigenous, genetic material','none of the above','NEW','12-214','12-185',NULL),
(340717,'biology','Find out the INCORRECT statements. \nA. The manipulation of living organisms by the human race cannot go on any further, without regulation.\nB. Some ethical standards are required to evaluate the morality of all human activities that might help or harm living organisms\nC. The modification/usage of living organisms for public services has also not created problems with patents granted for the same.','Only C','The manipulation of living organisms by the human race cannot go on any further, without regulation. Some ethical standards are required to evaluate the morality of all human activities that might help or harm living organisms. The modification/usage of living organisms for public services (as food and medicine sources, for example) has also created problems with patents granted for the same.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Ethical Issues','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Only C','only A','only B','both A and B','NEW','12-214','12-185',NULL),
(340801,'biology','Bt cotton variety that was developed by the introduction of toxin gene of Bacillus thuringiensis (Bt) is resistant to [NEET 2020]','Insect pests','Bt cotton is resistance to cotton bollworm infestation. The genes cry I Ac and cry IIAb control cotton bollworms, thus acts as bio-pesticide.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Insect pests','Fungal diseases','Plant nematodes','Insect predators.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340802,'biology','What triggers activation of protoxin to active toxin of Bacillus thuringiensis in bollworm? [NEET 2019]','Alkaline pH of gut','Some strains of Bacillus thuringiensis produce proteins that kill certain insects such as lepidopterans and dipterans. B. thuringiensis forms protein crystals during a particular phase of their growth. These crystals contain a toxic insecticidal protein which exists as inactive protoxins but once an insect ingest the inactive toxin, it is converted into an active form of toxin due to the alkaline pH of the gut which solubilise the crystals.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Acidic pH of stomach','Body temperature','Moist surface of midgut','Alkaline pH of gut','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340803,'biology','Which of the following is true for Golden rice? [NEET 2019]','It is vitamin A enriched, with a gene from daffodil.','Rice is a staple food in many countries, particularly in Asia, but does not contain vitamin A or its immediate precursors. By inserting two genes from daffodil and one gene from a bacterial species into rice plants, Swiss researchers have produced rice capable of synthesising b-carotene, the precursor of vitamin A. Vitamin A is required by all individuals as it is present in retina of eyes. Deficiency of vitamin A causes night blindness and skin disorders. This rice is called ‘Golden rice’ because of yellow colour of rice grains due to the presence of b-carotene.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','It has yellow grains, because of a gene introduced from a primitive variety of rice.','It is vitamin A enriched, with a gene from daffodil.','It is pest resistant, with a gene from Bacillus thuringiensis.','It is drought tolerant, developed using Agrobacterium vector.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340804,'biology','In RNAi, the genes are silenced using [Odisha NEET 2019]','ds-RNA','This method\ninvolves silencing of a specific mRNA due to a complementary dsRNA\nmolecule that binds to and prevents translation of the mRNA (silencing).','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','ds-RNA','ss-DNA','ss-RNA','ds-DNA.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340805,'biology','Which part of the tobacco plant is infected by Meloidogyne incognita? [NEET1 2016]','Root','Meloidogyne incognita is a nematode which infects the roots of the tobacco plants and causes a great reduction in the yield.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Stem','Root','Flower','Leaf','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340806,'biology','Golden rice is a genetically modified crop plant where the incorporated gene is meant for biosynthesis of [AIPMT 2015]','Vitamin A','Golden rice is a transgenic variety of rice (Oryzasativa) which contains good quantities of b-carotene (provitamin A - inactive state of vitamin A). b-carotene is a principal source of vitamin A. Since the grains of this rice is yellow in colour due to b-carotene, it is commonly called golden rice.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Omega 3','Vitamin A','Vitamin B','Vitamin C.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340807,'biology','In Bt cotton, the Bt toxin present in plant tissue as protoxin is converted into active toxin due to [AIPMT 2015 Cancelled]','Alkaline pH of the insect gut.','Soil bacterium Bacillus thuringiensis produces proteins that kill certain insects like lepidopterans (tobacco budworm, armyworm), coleopterans (beetles) and dipterans (flies, mosquitoes). B. thuringiensis forms some protein crystals. These crystals contain a toxic insecticidal protein. This toxin does not kill the Bacillus(bacterium) because it exists as inactive protoxins in them. But, once an insect ingests it, it is converted into an active form of toxin due to the alkaline pH of the alimentary canal. The activated toxin binds to the surface of midgut epithelial cells and create pores that cause swelling and lysis and finally cause death of the insect.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Action of gut microorganisms.','Presence of conversion factors in insect gut.','Alkaline pH of the insect gut.','Acidic pH of the insect gut.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340808,'biology','The crops engineered for glyphosate are resistant/tolerant to [AIPMT 2015 Cancelled]','Herbicides','Glyphosate is a broad spectrum herbicide which especially kills broad leaved herbs. Crop plants may also get affected by the herbicide, thus now crop plants are genetically engineered for glyphosate resistance. So, when glyphosate herbicide is applied, only weeds and no crop plants get harmed.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Insects','Herbicides','Fungi','Bacteria.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340809,'biology','Which of the following Bt crops is being grown in India by the farmers? [NEET 2013]','Cotton','Bt toxin genes were isolated from Bacillus thuringiensis and incorporated into the several crop plants such as cotton. The choice of genes depends upon the crop and targeted pest, as most Bt toxins are insect group specific. The toxin is coded by a gene named cry. There are numerous genes. Two cry genes cry I Ac and cryII Ab have been incorporated in cotton. The genetically modified crop is called Bt cotton as it contains Bt toxin genes against cotton bollworms.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Brinjal','Soybean','Maize','Cotton','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340810,'biology','RNA interference involves [Karnataka NEET 2013]','Silencing of specific mRNA due to complementary RNA.','RNA interference (RNAi) is the phenomenon of inhibiting activity of a gene through production of both sense and antisense RNA. RNAi takes place in all eukaryotic organisms as a method of cellular defense. This method involves a specific mRNA silencing. It is due to a complementary RNA molecule which binds to and prevents translation of the mRNA causing its silencing.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Synthesis of cDNA and RNA using reverse transcriptase.','Silencing of specific mRNA due to complementary RNA.','Interference of RNA in synthesis of DNA.','Synthesis of mRNA from DNA.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340811,'biology','A ‘new’ variety of rice was patented by a foreign company, though such varieties have been present in India for a long time. This is related to [NEET 2018]','Basmati.','In 1997, a Texas company got patent rights on Basmati rice through the US Patent and Trademark Office. This allowed the company to sell a ‘new’ variety of Basmati, in the US and abroad. This new variety of Basmati had actually been derived from Indian farmers’ varieties. Indian Basmati was crossed with semi-dwarf varieties and claimed as an invention or a novelty. It caused a brief diplomatic crisis between India and United States with India threatening to take the matter to WTO (World Trade Organisation) as a violation of TRIPS (Trade Related Aspects of Intellectual Property Rights). Both voluntarily and due to review decisions by United States patent office, Rice Tec lost most of the claims of the patent.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Co-667','Sharbati Sonora','Lerma Rojo','Basmati.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340812,'biology','In India, the organisation responsible for assessing the safety of introducing genetically modified organisms for public use is [NEET 2018]','Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee (GEAC).','Indian government has set up organisation such as GEAC (Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee) which makes decisions regarding the validity of GM research and safety of introducing GM organisms for public services.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Indian Council of Medical Research (ICMR).','Council for Scientific and Industrial Research (CSIR)','Research Committee on Genetic Manipulation (RCGM)','Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee (GEAC).','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340813,'biology','Use of bioresources by multinational companies and organisations without authorisation from the concerned country and its people is called [NEET 2018]','Biopiracy','Some organisations and multinational companies exploit or patent biological resources or bioresources of other nations without proper authorisation from the countries concerned. This is called biopiracy.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Bio-infringement','Biopiracy','Biodegradation','Bioexploitation.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340814,'biology','Bt cotton variety that was developed by the introduction of toxin gene of Bacillus thuringiensis (Bt) is resistant to [NEET 2020]','insect pests','Bt cotton is resistance to cotton bollworm infestation. The genes cry I Ac and cry IIAb control cotton bollworms, thus acts as bio-pesticide','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','insect pests','fungal diseases','plant nematodes','insect predators.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340815,'biology','What triggers activation of protoxin to active toxin of Bacillus thuringiensis in bollworm? [NEET 2019]','Alkaline pH of gut','Some strains of Bacillus thuringiensis produce proteins that kill certain insects such as lepidopterans and dipterans. B. thuringiensis forms protein crystals during a particular phase of their growth. These crystals contain a toxic insecticidal protein which exists as inactive protoxins but once an insect ingest the inactive toxin, it is converted into an active form of toxin due to the alkaline pH of the gut which solubilise the crystals','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Acidic pH of stomach','Body temperature','Moist surface of midgut','Alkaline pH of gut','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340816,'biology','Which of the following is true for Golden rice? [NEET 2019]','It is vitamin A enriched, with a gene from daffodil.','Rice is a staple food in many countries, particularly in Asia, but does not contain vitamin A or its immediate precursors. By inserting two genes from daffodil and one gene from a bacterial species into rice plants, Swiss researchers have produced rice capable of synthesising carotene , the precursor of vitamin A. Vitamin A is required by all individuals as it is present in retina of eyes. Deficiency of vitamin A causes night blindness and skin disorders. This rice is called ‘Golden rice’ because of yellow colour of rice grains due to the presence of carotene.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','It has yellow grains, because of a gene introduced from a primitive variety of rice','It is vitamin A enriched, with a gene from daffodil.','It is pest resistant, with a gene from Bacillus thuringiensis','It  is  drought  tolerant,  developed using Agrobacterium vector','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340817,'biology','Match the following columns and select the correct option. [NEET 2020]\nColumn-I                                                  Column-II\n(A)  Bt cotton                                      (i) Gene therapy\n(B)  Adenosine deaminase\ndeficiency                                           (ii) Cellular defence \n(C)RNAi                                              (iii) Detection of HIV infection\n(D)PCR                                               (iv) Bacillus thuringiensis','A(iv) B(i) C(ii) D(iii)','Bt toxin is produced by a bacterium called Bacillus thuringiensis (Bt for short). The process of RNA interference (RNAi). RNAi takes place in all eukaryotic organisms as a method of cellular defense.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','A(iv) B(i) C(ii) D(iii)','A(iii)	B(ii)	C(i)	D(iv)','A(ii)	B(iii)	C(iv)  D(i)','A(i) B(ii) C(iii)   D(iv)','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/340817q.png'),
(340818,'biology','Which of the following statements is not correct? [NEET 2020]','The functional insulin has A and B chains linked together by hydrogen bonds','Insulin consists of two short polypeptide chains : Chain A and chain B, that are linked together by disulphide bridges','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','In man insulin is synthesised as a proinsulin','The proinsulin has an extra peptide called C-peptide','The functional insulin has A and B chains linked together by hydrogen bonds','Genetically engineered insulin is produced in\nE.Coli','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340819,'biology','A ‘new’ variety of rice was patented by a foreign company, though such varieties have been present in India for a long time. This is related to','Basmati.','In 1997, a Texas company got patent rights on Basmati rice through the US Patent and Trademark Office. This allowed the company to sell a ‘new’ variety of Basmati, in the US and abroad. This new variety of Basmati had actually been derived from Indian farmers’ varieties. Indian Basmati was crossed with semi-dwarf varieties and claimed as an invention or a novelty. It caused a brief diplomatic crisis between India and United States with India threatening to take the matter to WTO (World Trade Organisation) as a violation of TRIPS (Trade Related Aspects of Intellectual Property Rights). Both voluntarily and due to review decisions by United States patent office, Rice Tec lost most of the claims of the patent.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Co-667','Sharbati Sonora','Lerma Rojo','Basmati.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340820,'biology','In India, the organisation responsible for assessing the safety of introducing genetically modified organisms for public use is','Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee (GEAC).',': Indian government has set up organisation such as GEAC (Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee) which makes decisions regarding the validity of GM research and safety of introducing GM organisms for public services.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Indian Council of Medical Research (ICMR)','Council for Scientific and Industrial Research (CSIR)','Research Committee on Genetic Manipulation (RCGM)','Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee (GEAC).','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340821,'biology','When gene targetting involving gene amplification is attempted in an individual\'s tissue to treat disease, it is known as : [NEET 2021]','Gene therapy','Gene therapy is a collection of methods that allows correction of a gene defect that has been diagnosed in a child/embryo. Here genes are inserted into a person’s cells and tissues to treat a disease. Amplification is sed to increase the expression of desired gene.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Safety testing','Biopiracy','Gene therapy','Molecular diagnosis','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(41413,'chemistry','The molecular geometry of  SF₆ :','Octahedral','The molecular geometry of SF₆ is Octahedral. \n\nAs shown in diagram\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 114; Table 4.6) diagram(ne0410)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'3','mcq','Tetrahedral','Octahedral','Trigonal planar','Sea-saw','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(340822,'biology','Now a days it is possible to detect the mutated gene causing cancer by allowing radioactive probe to hybridise its complimentary DNA in a clone of cells, followed by its detection using autoradiography because [NEET 2021]','mutated gene does not appear on a photographic film as the probe has no complementarity with it','A single stranded DNA or RNA, tagged with a radioactive molecule\n(probe) is allowed to hybridise to its complementary DNA in a clone of\ncells followed by detection using autoradiography. The clone having the\nmutated gene will hence not appear on the photographic film, because\nthe probe will not have complementarity with the mutated gene.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','mutated gene does not appear on photographic film as the probe has complementarity with it','mutated gene partially appears on a photographic film','mutated gene completely and clearly appears on a photographic film','mutated gene does not appear on a photographic film as the probe has no complementarity with it','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340823,'biology','With regard to insulin choose correct options. \n(a) C-peptide is not present in mature insulin. \n\n(b) The insulin produced by rDNA technology has Cpeptide. \n\n(c) The pro-insulin has C-peptide \n\nd) A-peptide and B-peptide of insulin are interconnected by disulphide bridges. \n\nChoose the correct answer from the option given below [NEET 2021]','(a), (c) and (d) only','Insulin consists of two short polypeptide\nchains: chain A and chain B, that are linked together by\ndisulphide bridges  In mammals, including\nhumans, insulin is synthesised as a pro-hormone (like a\npro-enzyme, the pro-hormone also needs to be processed\nbefore it becomes a fully mature and functional hormone)\nwhich contains an extra stretch called the C peptide.\nThis C peptide is not present in the mature insulin and is\nremoved during maturation into insulin.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','(a) and (d) only','(b) and (d) only','(b) and (c) only','(a), (c) and (d) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340824,'biology','The Adenosine deaminase deficiency results into [NEET 2021]','Dysfunction of Immune system','Adenosine deaminase (ADA) deficiency is an inherited disorder that damages the immune system and causes severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID). People with SCID lack virtually all immune protection from bacteria, viruses, and fungi.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Addison\'s disease','Dysfunction of Immune system','Parkinson\'s disease','Digestive disorder','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340825,'biology','For effective treatment of the disease, early diagnosis and understanding its pathophysiology is very important. Which of the following molecular diagnostic techniques is very useful for early detection? [NEET 2021]','ELISA Technique','Effective treatment of a disease, early diagnosis and\nunderstanding its pathophysiology is very important. Using conventional\nmethods of diagnosis (serum and urine analysis, etc.) early detection is\nnot possible. Recombinant DNA technology, Polymerase Chain Reaction\n(PCR) and Enzyme Linked Immuno-sorbent Assay (ELISA) are some of\nthe techniques that serve the purpose of early diagnosis.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Hybridization Technique','Western Blotting Technique','Southern Blotting Technique','ELISA Technique','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340826,'biology','In gene therapy of Adenosine Deaminase (ADA) deficiency, the patient requires periodic infusion of genetically engineered lymphocytes because : [NEET 2022]','Genetically engineered lymphocytes are not immortal cells','Option (4) is the correct answer as genetically engineered lymphocyctes are not immortal cells and die after some time.\nOption (3) is not the correct answer as the lymphocytes from patient’s blood are grown in culture, outside the body but it is not the correct reason.\nIn option (2), if the gene isolated from bone marrow cells producing ADA is introduced into cells at early embryonic stages, it could be a permanent cure.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Retroviral vector is introduced into these lymphocytes.','Gene isolated from marrow cells producing ADA is introduced into cells at embryonic stages','Lymphocytes from patient\'s blood are grown in culture, outside the body.','Genetically engineered lymphocytes are not immortal cells','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340827,'biology','Statements related to human Insulin are given below.\nWhich statement(s) is/are correct about genetically engineered Insulin? \n(a) Pro-hormone insulin contain extra stretch of C-peptide\n\n(b) A-peptide and B-peptide chains of insulin were produced separately in E.coli, extracted and combined by creating disulphide bond between them.\n\n(c) Insulin used for treating Diabetes was extracted from Cattles and Pigs.\n\n(d) Pro-hormone Insulin needs to be processed for converting into a mature and functional hormone.\n\n(e) Some patients develop allergic reactions to the foreign insulin.\n\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:  [NEET 2022]','(b) only','Option (2) is the correct answer as genetically engineered insulin has A-peptide and B-peptide chains of insulin which are produced separately in E.coli, then they are extracted and combined by creating disulphide bond between them.\nStatement (a) is incorrect as genetically engineered insulin does not have an extra stretch of C-peptide.\n\nStatement (c) is incorrect as insulin obtained from cattles and pigs is not genetically engineered insulin.\n\nStatement (d) is incorrect because conversion of pro-insulin to insulin is not required during production of insulin by genetic engineering as A-peptide and B-peptide chains are produced separately.\n\nStatement (e) is incorrect as allergic reactions to insulin are mostly seen when the insulin is obtained from animals.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','(a), (b) and (d) only','(b) only','(c) and (d) only','(c), (d) and (e) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(340828,'biology','Transposons can be used during which one of the following ? [NEET 2022]','Gene Silencing','Option (2) is the correct answer as the source of the complementary RNA for RNAi could be mobile\ngenetic elements (transposons) that replicate via an RNA intermediate.\nOption (3) is incorrect as autoradiography usally follows hybridisation.\nOption (1) is incorrect because polymerase chain reaction is used to make copies of the DNA sample\nand does not need transposons.\nOption (4) is incorrect because transposons are not required during gene sequencing.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Polymerase Chain Reaction','Gene Silencing','Autoradiography','Gene sequencing','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341001,'biology','The scientific process by which crop plants are enriched with certain desirable nutrients is called [2013]','Bio-fortification','Biofortification is the process by which the nutritional quality of food crops is improved through agronomic practices, conventional plant breeding, or modern biotechnology.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Crop protection','Breeding','Bio-fortification','Bioremediation','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341002,'biology','Which of the following is a variety of Brassica resistance to white rust disease? [2014]','Pusa Swarnim (Karan rai)','Pusa Swarnim is a variety of Brassica. It is resistant to white rust. White rust is caused by the oomycete Albugo candida or its close relatives. The symptoms of white rust are chlorosis on leaf surfaces, swelling of roots and white blister-like growths in stems. These symptoms can also deform the shapes of flowers.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Himgiri','Pusa Kamal','Pusa Swarnim (Karan rai)','Pusa Sadabahar','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341003,'biology','The first clinical gene therapy was given in 1990 to a 4 years old girl with enzyme deficiency of [2014]','Adenosine deaminase (ADA)','Gene therapy is an experimental technique that uses genes to treat or prevent disease. The first clinical gene therapy was given for treating adenosine deaminase deficiency. A four-year old girl became the first gene therapy patient on September 14, 1990 at the NIH Clinical Center. Adenosine deaminase deficiency, also called ADA deficiency or ADA-SCID is an autosomal recessive metabolic disorder that causes immunodeficiency. ADA deficiency is due to a lack of the enzyme adenosine deaminase.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Adenosine deaminase (ADA)','Tyrosine oxidase','Monamine oxidase','Glutamate dehydrogenase','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341004,'biology','Select the correct statement(s)-\n (1) IARI has released a mustard variety rich in vitamin C.\n\n (2) Pusa Sawani variety of Okra is resistant to aphids. \n\n(3) Hairiness of leaves provides resistance to insect pests. \n\n(4) Agriculture accounts for approximately 33% of India\'s GDP and employes nearly 62% of the population. [AIIMS 2015]','(1), (3) and (4)','Pusa Sawani variety of Okra is resistant to shoot and fruit borer.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(1) and (2)','(2) and (3)','(1), (3) and (4)','None of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341005,'biology','Read the following four statements (1-4) about certain mistakes in two of them \n1. The first transgenic buffalo, Rosie produced milk which was human alpha- lactal albumin enriched. \n\n2. Restriction enzymes are used in isolation of DNA from other macro-molecules. \n\n3. Downstream processing is one of the steps of R-DNA technology. \n\n4. Disarmed pathogen vectors are also used in transfer of R-DNA into the host. Which are the two statements having mistakes? [AIIMS 2015]','Statement 1 and 2','(A) Rosie was the first transgenic cow and its milk was rich in human alpha-lactalbumin protein.\n\n(B) Restriction enzymes are used in the isolation of the specific segment of DNA from the DNA of the organism because these enzymes are capable of cutting the DNA at the specific location.\n\n(C) Downstream processing is one of the steps in the r-DNA technology. It involves the extraction and purification of the desired product.\n\n(D) Disarmed pathogens like Agrobacterium tumifaciens, phage viruses are also used in the transfer of r-DNA into the host.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Statement 2 and 3','Statement 3 and 4','Statement 1 and 3','Statement 1 and 2','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341006,'biology','A transgenic food crop which may help in solving the problem of night blindness in developing countries is [AIIMS 2016]','Golden rice','A transgenic food crop which may help in solving the problem of night blindness in developing countries is golden rice. Golden rice is genetically modified rice that has been engineered to have elevated levels of beta carotene in it. It is a pre cursor of vitamin A, which gives it a characteristic golden colour.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Golden rice','Bt soyabean','Flavr - savr tomato','Starlink maize','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341007,'biology','Which variety of rice was patented by a U.S. company even though the highest number of varieties of this rice is found in India ? [AIIMS 2017]','Basmati','Basmati rice was patented by a US company even though the highest number of varieties of this rice is found in India.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Sharbati Sonara','Co-667','Basmati','Lerma Roja','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341008,'biology','Name of the Norman Borlaug is associated with[AIIMS 2015]','Green revolution','Norman Borlaug was an Amerian Agromist. He is known as "Father of Green Revolution". He contributed to the extensive increase in agricultural production termed the Green Revolution.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Green revolution','Yellow revolution','White revolution','Blue revolution','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341009,'biology','Golden rice is a transgenic crop of the future with the following trait:[AIIMS 2005]','High vitamin A content','Golden rice is a transgenic variety of rice (Oryzasativa) which contains good quantities of b-carotene (provitamin A - inactive state of vitamin A). b-carotene is a principal source of vitamin A. Since the grains of this rice is yellow in colour due to b-carotene, it is commonly called golden rice.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','high lysine (essential amino acid) content','insect resistance','high protein content','High vitamin A content','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341010,'biology','Golden rice is a promising transgenic crop. When released for cultivation, it will help in :[AIPMT 2006]','alleviation of vitamin-A deficiency','Golden rice is a transgenic variety of rice (Oryzasativa) which contains good quantities of b-carotene (provitamin A - inactive state of vitamin A). b-carotene is a principal source of vitamin A. Since the grains of this rice is yellow in colour due to b-carotene, it is commonly called golden rice.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','alleviation of vitamin-A deficiency','pest resistance','herbicide tolerance','producing a petrol-like fuel from rice','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341011,'biology','Microbes found to be very useful in genetic engineering are :[AIPMT 2006]','Escherichia coli and Agrobacterium tumefaciens','E. coli is a perfect model for several reasons: E. coli is a single-celled organism that can be manipulated and killed with no ethical concerns. It has a rapid growth rate and is very easy to culture and grow. Finally, and most importantly, E. coli genetics are well-studied and can be manipulated easily.\nThe basis of Agrobacterium-mediated genetic engineering is that the T-DNA of A. tumefaciens is excised and integrates into the plant genome as part of the natural infection process by this bacterium. An Agrobacterium cell act as the vehicle for a transforming plasmid. a Ti plasmid with functional Vir genes.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Escherichia coli and Agrobacterium tumefaciens','Vibrio cholerae and a tailed bacteriophage','Diplococcus sp. and Pseudomonas sp.','Crown gall bacterium and Caenorhabditis elegans','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341012,'biology','Cry 1 endotoxins obtained from Bacillus Thuringiensis are effective against[AIPMT 2008]','Boll worms','The toxin is coded by a gene cryIAc named cry. There are a number of them, for example, the proteins encoded by the genes cryIAc and cryIIAb control the cotton bollworms, that of cryIAb controls corn borer.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Boll worms','Mosquitoes','Flies','Nematodes','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341013,'biology','Human insulin is being commercially produced from a transgenic species of[AIPMT 2008]','Escherichia','In 1983, Eli Lilly an American company prepared two DNA sequences corresponding to A and B, chains of human insulin and introduced them in plasmids of E. coli to produce insulin chains. Chains A and B were produced separately, extracted and combined by creating disulfide bonds to form human insulin.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Saccharomyces','Escherichia','Mycobacterium','Rhizobium','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341014,'biology','Which one of the following is commonly used in transfer of foreign DNA into crop plants ?[AIPMT 2009]','Agrobacterium tumefaciens','Using Agrobacterium vectors, nematode-specific genes were\nintroduced into the host plant.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Agrobacterium tumefaciens','Meloidogyne incognita','Penicillium expansum','Trichoderma harzianum','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341015,'biology','What is true about Bt toxin ? [AIPMT 2009]','The inactive protoxin gets converted into active form in the insect gut.','The Bt toxin protein exist as inactive protoxins but\nonce an insect ingest the inactive toxin, it is converted into an active form of toxin due to the alkaline pH of the gut which solubilise the crystals. The activated toxin binds to the surface of midgut epithelial cells and create pores that cause cell swelling and lysis and eventually cause death of the insect.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Bt protein exists as active toxin in the Bacillus.','The activated toxin enters the ovaries of the pest to sterilise it and thus prevent its multiplication.','The concerned Bacillus has antitoxins.','The inactive protoxin gets converted into active form in the insect gut.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341016,'biology','The genetic defect - adenosine deaminase (ADA) deficiency may be cured permanently by:[AIPMT 2009]','introducing bone marrow cells producing ADA into cells at early embryonic stages.','In some children ADA deficiency can be cured by bone marrow transplantation; in others it can be treated by enzyme replacement therapy, in which functional ADA is given to the\npatient by injection.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','administering adenosine deaminase activators.','introducing bone marrow cells producing ADA into cells at early embryonic stages.','enzyme replacement therapy.','periodic infusion of genetically engineered lymphocytes having functional ADA cDNA.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341017,'biology','Transgenic plants are the ones:[AIPMT 2009]','generated by introducing foreign DNA into a cell and regenerating a plant from that cell.','Genes of one species can be modified or genes can be transplanted from one species to another. The transgenic plants, usually normal in appearance and character, differ from the parent only with respect to the function and influence of the inserted gene. This directed genetic engineering of plants requires that the gene be introduced into plant cells capable of regenerating into intact plants. The transformed plant cell must retain its capacity to regenerate.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','generated by introducing foreign DNA into a cell and regenerating a plant from that cell.','produced after protoplast fusion in artificial medium.','grown in artificial medium after hybridization in the field.','produced by a somatic embryo in artificial medium.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341018,'biology','The bacterium Bacillus thuringiensis is widely used in contemporary biology as:[AIPMT 2009]','Insecticide','Bt toxin gene has been cloned from the bacteria and been expressed in plants to provide\nresistance to insects without the need for insecticides;','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Insecticide','Agent for production of dairy products','Source of industrial enzyme','Indicator of water pollution','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341019,'biology','A genetically engineered micro-organism use successfully in bioremediation of oil spills is a\nspecies of [AIPMT 2007 prelims]','Pseudomonas','Xanthomonas is used for bioproduction of xanthan gum which is a polysaccharide and is used as a food additive. Bacillus thuringiensis (Bt) produces Bt toxin during sporulation. The toxin bind to receptors on midgut epithelial cells of gallworm, become inserted into the plasma membrane where they form pores leading to cell death through osmotic lysis; thus serving as biopesticide. Trichoderma serve as very effective biocontrolling agent for soil borne plant disease management. It is a free-living fungus, and reduces pathogenic infections by different mechanisms like competition, antibiosis, hyphal interactions, mycoparasitism, , and enzyme secretion. Pseudomonas putida is used in bioremediation owing to its aerobic metabolism with ability to degrade organic pollutants such as toluene and to convert styrene oil to biodegradable plastic Polyhydroxyalkanoates (PHA).','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Trichoderma','Xanthomonas','Bacillus','Pseudomonas','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341020,'biology','Which one of the following is not a biofertilizer? [AIPMT 2011 prelims]','Agrobacterium','Agrobacterium is a gene transfer agent.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Mycorrhiza','Agrobacterium','Rhizobium','Nostoc','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341021,'biology','The process of RNA interference has been used in the development of plants resistant to [AIPMT 2011 prelims]','Nematodes','RNAi i.e., RNA interference is used in the development of plants resistant to nematode like Meloidegyne\nincognita.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Insects','Nematodes','Fungi','Viruses','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(341022,'biology','Tobbacco plants resistant to a nematode have been developed by the introduction of DNA that produced (in the host cell) [AIPMT 2012]','both sense and anti-sense RNA','Using Agrobacterium vectors, nematode-specific genes were introduced into the host plant. The introduction of DNA was such that it produced both sense and anti-sense RNA in the host cells. These two RNA’s being complementary to each other formed a double stranded (dsRNA) that initiated RNAi and thus, silenced the specific mRNA of the nematode. The consequence was that the parasite could not survive in a transgenic host expressing specific interfering RNA','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','a toxic protein','both sense and anti-sense RNA','a particular harmone','an antifeedant','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341023,'biology','The first clinical gene therapy was given for treating: [AIPMT 2012]','Adenosine deaminase deficiency','The first clinical gene therapy was given in 1990 to a 4-year old girl\nwith adenosine deaminase (ADA) deficiency.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Adenosine deaminase deficiency','Diabetes mellitus','Chicken pox','Rheumatoid arthritis','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341024,'biology','Green revolution occured during [AIPMT 2012]','1960\'s','Green revolution occured between 1950-1960. It is related to agriculture. It mainly focused on increasing agricultural production. It started in Mexico.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','1950\'s','1960\'s','1970\'s','1980\'s','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341025,'biology','Which of the following Bt crop is being grown in India by the farmers [AIPMT 2013]','Cotton','Bt cotton is being grown in India by the farmers','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Maize','Cotton','Brinjal','Soybeen','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341026,'biology','The two polypeptides of human insulin are linked together by [AIPMT 2016]','Disulphide bridge','Insulin consists of two short polypeptide chains: chain A and chain B, that are linked together by\ndisulphide bridges','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Hydrogen bonds','Phosphodiester bond','Covalent bond','Disulphide bridge','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341027,'biology','Which part of the tobacco plant is infected by Meloidogyne incognita? [AIPMT 2016]','Root','A nematode Meloidegyne incognitia infects the roots of tobacco plants and causes a great reduction\nin yield.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Flower','Leaf','Stem','Root','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341028,'biology','During the processing of proinsulin into the mature insulin','C-peptide is removed from proinsulin','This C peptide is not present in the mature insulin and is\nremoved during maturation into insulin.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','C-peptide is added to proinsulin','C-peptide is removed from proinsulin','B-peptide is added to proinsulin','B-peptide is removed from proinsulin','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341029,'biology','Match the following columns and select the correct option. \n     Column-I                                        Column-II\n(A) Biopiracy                                (i) Gene therapy\n(B) Adenosine deaminase\ndeficiency                                     (ii) Right granted for biological entities\n(C) Biopatent                               (iii) Detection of HIV infection\n(D) PCR                                        (iv) Illegal removal of biological entities','A-(iv), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iii)','Biopiracy-laws to prevent such unauthorised exploitation of their bio-resources and traditional\nknowledge. Biopatent- A biopatent is a patent granted by the government to the inventor for biological entities and for products obtained from them. The first clinical gene therapy was given in 1990 to a 4-year old girl with adenosine deaminase (ADA) deficiency. PCR is now routinely used to detect HIV in suspected AIDS patients. It is being used to detect mutations in genes in suspected cancer patients too. It is a powerful techqnique to identify many other genetic disorders.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)','A-(iv), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iii)','A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)','A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341030,'biology','Select incorrect statement, regarding chemical structure of insulin. [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(1) Mature insulin molecule consists of three \npolypeptide chains-A, B and C.','The chemical structure of insulin consists of two polypeptide chains, not three. The two chains are referred to as the A chain and the B chain. The A chain contains 21 amino acids, while the B chain contains 30 amino acids. These two chains are held together by disulfide bridges.\n\nThe correct structure of mature insulin is a dimer, composed of an A chain and a B chain connected by disulfide bridges. The C-peptide, which is derived from the precursor molecule proinsulin, is removed during the processing of proinsulin to form mature insulin. Therefore, statement 3 is also correct.\n\nSo, the incorrect statement is 1: "Mature insulin molecule consists of three polypeptide chains - A, B and C."','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Mature insulin molecule consists of three \npolypeptide chains-A, B and C.','(2) Insulin is synthesized as prohormone which \ncontains extra stretch of C-peptide.','(3) C-peptide is not present in mature insulin \nmolecule.','(4) Polypeptide chains A and B are linked by \ndisulphide bridges','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341031,'biology','Match List-I with List-II. \n List-I List-II \n (A) Gene therapy (I) Separation of DNA \nfragments \n (B) RNA interference (II) Diagnostic test for \nAIDS \n (C) ELISA (III) Cellular defence \n (D) Gel Electrophoresis (IV) Allows correction of a \ngene defect. \n Choose the correct answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(3) (A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)','(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','(1) (A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)','(2) (A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(I)','(3) (A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)','(4) (A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341032,'biology','An improved variety of transgenic basmati rice\n(2010)','(b) gives high yield and is rich in vitamin A','NCERT XII: 208','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','(a) does not require chemical fertilizers and growth\nhormones','(b) gives high yield and is rich in vitamin A','(c) is completely resistant to all insect pests and\ndiseases of paddy','(d) gives high yield but has no characteristic aroma.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341033,'biology','Bacillus thuringiensis forms protein crystals which\ncontain insecticidal protein. This protein\n(Mains 2011)','(a) binds with epithelial cells of midgut of the insect\npest ultimately killing it','NCERT XII: 208-209','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','(a) binds with epithelial cells of midgut of the insect\npest ultimately killing it','(b) is coded by several genes including the gene cry','(c) is activated by acid pH of the foregut of the\ninsect pest','(d) does not kill the carrier bacterium which is itself\nresistant to this toxin','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341034,'biology','Match the organisms with its use in biotechnology.\n(a) Bacillus thuringiensis - (I) Cloning vector\n(b) Thermus aquaticus - (ii) Construction of first rDNA molecule\n(c) Agrobacterium tumefaciens - (iii) DNA polymerase \n(d) Salmonella typhimurium - (iv) Cry protein','(a) - (iv), (b) - (iii), (c) - (i), (d) - (ii)','NCERT XII: 209','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(a) - (iv), (b) - (iii), (c) - (i), (d) - (ii)','(a) - (i), (b) - (iii), (c) - (iv), (d) - (ii)','(a) - (iv), (b) - (ii), (c) - (i), (d) - (iii)','(a) - (iv), (b) - (i), (c) - (ii), (d) - (ii)','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341035,'biology','Some of the characteristics of Bt cotton are\n(2010)','(d) high yield and resistance to bollworms.','NCERT XII: 209','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(a) long fibre and resistance to aphids','(b) medium yield, long fibre and resistance to beetle\n pests','(c) high yield and production of toxic protein\ncrystals which kill dipteran pests','(d) high yield and resistance to bollworms.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341036,'biology','The first human hormone produced by recombinant\nDNA technology is\n(2014)','(a) insulin','NCERT XII: 210','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(a) insulin','(b) estrogen','(c) thyroxin','(d) progesterone','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341037,'biology','A single strand of nucleic acid tagged with a radioactive molecule is called','Probe','NCERT XII: 210','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Vector','Selectable marker','Plasmid','Probe','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341038,'biology','Maximum number of existing transgenic animals\nis of','(b) mice','NCERT XII: 212','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','(a) fish','(b) mice','(c) cow','(d) pig','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341039,'biology','Milk of transgenic \'Cow Rosie\' was nutritionally more balanced product for human babies than natural cow milk because it contained:','Human alpha - lactalbumin','NCERT XII: 213','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Human protein alpha 1 antitrypsin','Human alpha - lactalbumin','Human insulin like growth factor','Human enzyme adenosine deaminase(ADA)','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341040,'biology','Genetic engineering has been successfully used for\nproducing\n\n(b) transgenic models for studying new treatments\nfor certain cardiac diseases\n(c) transgenic cow-Rosie which produces high fat\nmilk for making ghee\n(d) animals like bulls for farm work as they have\nsuper power.','(a) transgenic mice for testing safety of polio\nvaccine before use in humans','NCERT XII: 213','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','(a) transgenic mice for testing safety of polio\nvaccine before use in humans',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341041,'biology','The laws and rules to prevent unauthorised exploitation of bio-resources are termed as','Biopatenting','NCERT XII: 214','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Biopatenting','Bioethics','Bioengineering','Biopiracy','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341042,'biology','Main objective of production/use of herbicide resistant GM crop is to','Eliminate weeds from the field without the use of manual layout','NCERT XII: 214','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Eliminate weeds from the field without the use of manual layout','Eliminate weeds from the field without the use of herbicide','Encourage eco-friendly herbicide','Reduce herbicide accumulation in food particles for health safety','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341101,'biology','Statement I: Diagnosis of COVID-19 using polymerase chain reaction can be considered an application of biotechnology\n\nStatement II: Growth of insulin producing organisms in controlled conditions is an application of biotechnology','Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','Polymerase chain reaction is a molecular technique to replicate a gene of interest into millions of identical copies. It is used to diagnosis various diseases including COVID-19. E. coli transformed to produce insulin are cultured at a large scale and this is one of the breakthroughs in the field of medicine brought about by recombinant DNA technology or modern biotechnology.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','Both Statement I and Statement II are false.','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false.','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341102,'biology','Assertion: Purification of biopharmaceuticals is an active part of biotechnology research\n\nReason: Downstream processing is a significant part of biotechnology','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Downstream processing refers to the procedures for separation and purification of the desired compound from the cultured biomass. It is one of the most important part of a biotechnological process and is an active research area. Purification of biopharmaceuticals produced by living organisms is a part of biotechnological process and research.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341103,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding applications of biotechnology:\n\n(i) Biotechnology can be applied to increase the crop yied and reduce the use of pesticides\n\n(ii) Biotechnology can be applied to increase the crop yield but not to reduce the use of pesticides\n\n(iii) Biotechnology can be applied to improve the tolerance of crops to water shortage but not change the nutritional value\n\n(iv) Biotechnology can be applied to grow crops with different nutritional values and to improve their tolerance to water shortage\n\nWhich of the above statements are CORRECT?','(i) and (iv) only','The techniques of recombinant technology have been applied to manipulate the genes of crop plants such that they have improved yield, can be resistant to pests and be tolerant to different abiotic stresses, such as low water (drought conditions), high salinity, etc. Such crop plants fall under the category of genetically modified organisms.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) and (iv) only','(ii) and (iii) only','(i) only','(ii) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341104,'biology','Statement I: Downstream processing refers to the replication of a foreign gene when it is placed in a host genome downstream to the origin of replication\n\nStatement II: Downstream processing is an important part of biotechnology procedures utilized for industrial production of pharmaceuticals.','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true.','Downstream processing refers to the procedures for separation and purification of the desired compound from the cultured biomass. It is one of the most important part of a biotechnological process and is an active research area. Many pharmaceuticals are produced by genetically manipulated microbial cells. For clinical use, it is important to separate those pharmaceuticals from the rest of the microbial biomass and other culture media constituents, and purify it to exact measure. Thus, downstream processing is one of the most important parts of the biotechnological processes in the industrial and commercial aspect.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','Both Statement I and Statement II are false.','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false.','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(41414,'chemistry','The molecular geometry of  SO₂ :','Bent','It is AB₂E type molecule having one lone pair and two bond pairs,the  arrangement of electron pairs is trigonal but due to presence of lone pair bonds try to occupy that place which minimize repulsion, note: geometry is determined by bond pairs only. \n\nAs shown in diagram\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 115; Table 4.7) diagram(ne0411)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'3','mcq','Bent','Octahedral','Trigonal planar','See-saw','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(41415,'chemistry','The Molecular geometry of  NH₃ :','Trigonal pyramidal','The electronic arrangement around the phosphorus is tetrahedral due to presence of lone pair it adopt trigonal pyramidal shape so as to minimize the repulsion between bond pairs and lone pair. \n\nAs shown in diagram \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 115; Table 4.7) diagram(ne0411)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'3','mcq','Bent','Octahedral','Trigonal planar','Trigonal pyramidal','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(341105,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding genetically modified organisms (GMOs): \n\n(i) E. coli expressing human insulin is an example of a GMO\n\n(ii) Wheat varieties developed by interbreeding to have high crop yield can be considered a GMO\n\n(iii) Bacteria with manipulated genes for overexpression of a bacterial metabolite\n\n(iv) The variety of rice enriched with Vitamin A is an example of a GMO\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are INCORRECT?','(ii) and (iii) only','Genetically modified organisms (GMOs) are those organisms that have their genes manipulated by recombinant DNA technology to be used for human use. E. coli has been used as a microbial factory for the production of insulin, a human hormone, using the techniques of RDT. Hence, it is classified as a GMO. Similarly, RDT techniques have been applied to genetically modify bacterial and other microbial species such that they produce a specific metabolite at a significantly higher level than in wild type bacteria. Such bacteria (and other such organisms), hence, are also referred to as GMOs. Crop varieties obtained by interbreeding are not termed as GMOs because the genetic changes involved are natural that can also introduce undesired features to the resultant crop variety. Only those crop varieties are called GMOs that have been genetically manipulated/modified by human interference using the principles of RDT for the manifestation of a specific set of phenotypes or features, e.g. increased nutritional value, higher yields, etc. All wild type rice varieties do not contain Vitamin A. Some varieties have been genetically modified to produce Vitamin A. These rice varities are characterized by their golden color and hence are called golden rice.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(ii), (iii) and (iv) only','(iii) only','(ii) and (iii) only','(i) and (iv) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341106,'biology','Assertion: Plants have been genetically modified to be resistant to pests and insects\n\nReason: Bacterial proteins that are toxic to pests can be expressed in plants using recombinant DNA technology to make them pest resistant','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Bacterial proteins that are toxic to some insects have been successfully expressed in plants such as cotton to make them pest resistant. Example is Bt cotton, wherein Bt protein from the bacteria Bacillus thuriengensis has been expressed in cotton to prevent bollworm infestation.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341107,'biology','Consider the following statements\n\n(i) Plants can be genetically modified to express genes from other plants but not bacteria\n\n(ii) Prokaryotic genes can be expressed in eukaryotic cells given the proper cloning vector\n\n(iii) Bacterial plasmids cannot be used as cloning vectors in plants\n\n(iv) Human enzymes can be expressed in plants using Ti plasmid\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are INCORRECT?','(i) only','Plants have been genetically modified to express genes from other plants, humans as well as bacteria. Bt cotton is a genetically modified crop that expresses the Bt protein from a bacteria. This protein is toxic to certain pests and imparts pest resistance to cotton crop. This is also an example of how prokaryotic genes can be expressed in eukaryotic cells.  Since plants are eukaryotes, their cellular machinery is more complex than that of bacteria. Hence, simple bacterial plasmids cannot be used as cloning vectors in plants. Ti plasmid from Agrobacterium tumefaciens is the preferred cloning vector for plants and it can be used to express human genes in plants too.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(ii) and (iii) only','(i) and (iii) only','(i) only','(i) and (iv) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341108,'biology','Assertion: The Bt toxin is a biopesticide\n\nReason: Bacillus thuringiensis produces protein crystals that kill insects','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','B. thuringiensis produces a protein known as Bt which is toxic to several insects and crop pests. This has been expressed in plant cells to prevent pest infestation. Since this toxin has natural origins and helps in killing crop pests, it is known as a biopesticide.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341109,'biology','Statement I: The Bt protein is toxic to insects because of the alkaline pH of their gut\n\nStatement II: Bt toxin is present as insoluble crystals in bacteria','Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','The Bt toxin protein exist as inactive and insoluble protoxins in bacteria but\nonce an insect ingest the inactive toxin, it is converted into an active form of toxin due to the alkaline pH of the gut which solubilise the crystals. The activated toxin binds to the surface of midgut epithelial cells and create pores that cause cell swelling and lysis and eventually cause death of the insect.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','Both Statement I and Statement II are false.','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false.','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341110,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) The Bt toxin is toxic to both the bacteria that produces it and to insects\n\n(ii) The Bt toxin has been exclusively introduced in cotton crop \n\n(iii) Introduction of the Bt toxin gene in crops led to decrease in crop damage by pests\n\n(iv) Production of Bt cotton involves genetic modification of bacteria\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are INCORRECT?','(i), (ii) and (iv) only','The Bt toxin is not toxic to the bacteria that produces it. It is present as an inactive protoxin in Bacillus thuringiensis. However, it converts to its active toxin form in insect gut and leads to its death. In addition to cotton, the Bt gene has been introduced in potato, tomato, brinjal, etc. crops. Production of Bt cotton does not involve genetic modification in bacteria as the Bt toxin is naturally produced by the bacteria Bacillus thuringiensis. Instead, the plant that expresses the Bt gene is a genetically modified organism.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) only','(ii) and (iii) only','(i) and (iii) only','(i), (ii) and (iv) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(41416,'chemistry','The molecular geometry of H₂O:','Bent','The electronic arrangement around the O of water is tetrahedral due to presence of lone pairs it adopt bent or angular shape so as to minimize the repulsion between bond pairs and lone pairs. \n\nAs shown in diagram \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 115; Table 4.7) diagram(ne0411)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'3','mcq','Octahedral','Bent','Trigonal planar','See-saw','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(341111,'biology','Assertion: Ti plasmid from Agrobacterium tumefaciens can be used as a cloning vector for Bt toxin gene expression in plant cells\n\nReason: The Bt toxin gene is naturally expressed by plasmid of Bacillus thuringiensis','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','The Bt toxin gene, also referred to as the cry gene, is expressed naturally by a plasmid present in the bacteria B. thuringiensis. The gene is taken from this plasmid via restriction digestion and ligated to Ti plasmid (from Agrobacterium tumefaciens) for cloning in plant cells.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341112,'biology','Bt crops are one of the most successful examples of genetically modified crops. Consider the following statements in respect to the development of Bt crops:\n\n(i) The cry gene is ligated to the Ti plasmid of Agrobacterium tumefaciens for cloning in plant cells\n\n(ii) The plant cells are infected with Bacillus thuringiensis to allow cry gene to integrate to plant cell genome\n\n(iii) The plant cells are infected with Agrobacterium tumefaciens containing recombinant Ti plasmid \n\n(iv) The plant cells are infected with Bacillus thuringiensis spores containing Cry proteins\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(i) and (iii) only','The cry genes code for different types of Bt toxin proteins, or Cry proteins that are toxic to different types of insects. This gene has been introduced in plant cells via recombinant DNA technology. The cry gene from B. thuringiensis is cut via restriction digestion and inserted into Ti plasmid of Agrobacterium tumefaciens. This recombinant Ti plasmid acts as a cloning vector in plant cells. B. thuringiensis is not a plant pathogen, hence it does not infect plant cells.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) only','(i) and (iii) only','(ii) and (iv) only','(ii) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341113,'biology','Statement I: There is only one cry gene that codes for the toxin that kills all types of insects\n\nStatement II: The Cry proteins, encoded by the cry gene(s), are responsible for higher crop yields of some plants','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true.','The cry gene family consists of a number of genes that code for proteins specifically toxic to certain types of insects and pests. For example, the cryIAc gene is responsible for toxins against bollworms and cryIAb is responsible for toxins against corn borer. Thy Cry proteins or the Bt toxin proteins have been expressed in some crops such as cotton, brinjal, soyabean, etc. leading to an increase in pest resistance of these crops. The decrease in pest-related damages led to an increase in overall yields of these crops.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','Both Statement I and Statement II are false.','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false.','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341114,'biology','Assertion: RNA interference (RNAi) is an important technique of cellular defense in eukaryotic organisms\n\nReason: Genes can be silenced at the post-transcriptional level by double stranded RNA (dsRNA)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','RNA interference (RNAi) is an important cellular defense mechanism used by eukaryotic cells to prevent viral infections. In addition, it can also be used in regulation of gene expression at the mRNA level (post-transcription level). The mRNA can be silenced by the presence of dsRNA that is complementary to the mRNA. Thus, the amount of protein getting translated is reduced. [*Both A and R are two different applications of RNAi, they are correlated. But R is not the correct reason for A]','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341115,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding RNAi in plants: \n\n(i) RNAi is a natural phenomenon that occurs in all plant cells.\n\n(ii) RNAi occurs only in specific plants that have been genetically modified to perform RNAi.\n\n(iii) Tobacco plants were genetically modified by dsRNA to work against nematode infections.\n\n(iv) Tobacco plants were genetically modified by nematode genes to induce RNAi against them.\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(i) and (iv) only','RNA interference (RNAi) is used by eukaryotic cells as a technique for genetic regulation and cellular defense. SInce plants are eukaryotic, they also naturally use RNAi for gene expression regulation. In order to increase resistance of tobacco plants against a specific nematode, Meloidegyne incognitia, nematode-specific genes were introduced in the plant genome using Ti plasmid as cloning vector. The introduction of DNA was such that it produced both sense and anti-sense RNA in the host cells. These two RNAs being complementary to each other formed a double stranded (dsRNA) that initiated RNAi and thus, silenced the specific mRNA.of the nematode. This eventually enabled the plant to stave off nematode infection.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(ii) and (iv) only','(i) and (iv) only','(ii), (iii) and (iv) only','(ii) and (iii) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341116,'biology','Statement I: RNA interference is a technique developed by plant cells after genetic modification\n\nStatement II: Genetically modified tobacco plants can utilize RNA interference against certain pests','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true.','RNA interference is a natural cellular mechanism utilized by all eukaryotic cells including plant cells for cell defense and regulation of gene expression at mRNA level. Genetic modification of tobacco plants such that it expresses both sense and antisense pest-specific RNA in the plant cells allows RNAi to be initiated. This eventually leads to blocking of expression of pest RNA leading to its death.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','Both Statement I and Statement II are false.','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false.','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(41417,'chemistry','The molecular geometry of SF₄','See-saw','The electronic arrangement around the S of  SF₄ is trigonal bipyramidal presence of lone pairs force it adopt see-saw shape so as to minimize the repulsion between bond pairs and lone pairs see-saw is actually a distorted tetrahedron. \n\nAs shown in diagram\n\nReference : (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 115; Table 4.7) diagram(ne0411)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'3','mcq','Trigonal planar','Octahedral','See-saw','Bent','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(341117,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding: \n\n(i) Meloidegyne incognitia is a nematode that infects the roots and leaves of tobacco plants\n\n(ii) Meloidegyne incognitia is a nematode that infects only the roots of tobacco plants\n\n(iii) Plants have been genetically modified by Bt toxin gene to develop resistance to Meloidegyne incognitia\n\n(iv) Plants can be genetically modified to synthesize interfering RNA that silences mRNA of Meloidegyne incognitia\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are INCORRECT?','(i) and (iii) only','Meloidegyne incognitia is a nematode parasite that infects the roots of tobacco plants. Plants have been made resistant to this parasite by the expression of interfering RNA which form dsRNA with the nematode mRNA, which initiates the process of RNA interference. This leads to silencing of nematode mRNA leading to death of the parasite.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(ii) and (iii) only','(i) and (iii) only','(i) and (iv) only','(iii) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341118,'biology','Assertion: RNAi is a mechanism to \'silence\' genes by preventing the translation of mRNA that has a complementary \'antisense\' RNA\n\nReason: RNAi is induced after the formation of dsRNA.','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','RNA interference or RNAi occurs when a double stranded RNA (dsRNA) complimentary to an mRNA binds to it and prevents its translation to proteins. Thus, the gene from which the mRNA arose is not expressed, hence \'silenced.\' The formation of a double stranded RNA (dsRNA) initiates the process of RNAi.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341119,'biology','Statement I: Transposons are genetic elements that show mobility in a chromosomal genome\n\nStatement II: Transposons are a new kind of cloning vector used for inducing RNA interference','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false.','Transposons are genetic elements that show mobility within a chromosome. Transposons that replicate via RNA intermediate can induce RNAi in cells leading to gene silencing. However, they have not been utilized as cloning vectors.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','Both Statement I and Statement II are false.','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false.','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341120,'biology','Statement I: Recombinant therapeutics are chemicals and drug molecules that have been obtained by recombining two different types of drugs in a living organism\n\nStatement II: Recombinant therapeutics prevent unwanted immunological reactions in humans','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true.','Recombinant therapeutics are those molecules that have been expressed in living organisms by recombinant DNA technology, e.g. human insulin in E. coli.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','Both Statement I and Statement II are false.','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false.','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341121,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) Insulin is a synthetic drug molecule that is used to regulate glucose levels exclusively in diabetic patients\n\n(ii) Insulin is a hormone that regulates glucose levels in all healthy human individuals\n\n(iii) Insulin from cattle and pigs can be utilized to manage diabetes in human individuals\n\n(iv) Some strains of E. coli naturally synthesize insulin, hence they are used as primary source of insulin for therapeutics\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(ii) and (iii) only','Insulin is an endocrine hormone produced in the pancreas to regulate the levels of blood sugar in the body. Diabetic patients do not have enough insulin to regulate their blood sugar levels, hence they often require insulin from external sources. Earlier, insulin extracted from slaughtered pigs and cattle was utilized to manage diabetes. However, after the inception of rDNA technology, E. coli was genetically modified to produce the different peptide chains of human insulin, which was then used for diabetic patients. E. coli is a prokaryote and lacks the ability to naturally synthesize human proteins and peptides such as insulin.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) only','(i) and (iv) only','(iii) only','(ii) and (iii) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341122,'biology','Assertion: Recombinant E. coli is capable of synthesizing insulin in its mature form\n\nReason: Genes coding for the insulin peptide chains were inserted in E. coli plasmids','(A) is false but (R) is true','E. coli is not capable of producing insulin in its mature form. Insulin is synthesized as prohormone consisting of an extra peptide chain known as the C peptide. To form mature, functional insulin, C peptide is cleaved from the prohormone and the A and B peptide chains are linked via disulphide linkages. In order to synthesize insulin in E. coli, the genes coding for the A and B peptide are separately inserted into the E. coli plasmid vectors. The A and B peptide chains are extracted after expression and linked via sulphide bonds to form human insulin.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341123,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) Functional insulin in mammals consists of two polypeptide chains linked by disulphide bonds\n\n(ii) Functional insulin in mammals consists of two polypeptide chains linked by a C peptide\n\n(iii) Insulin is synthesized as an inactive prohormone in mammals\n\n(iv) Eli Lilly was the first company to synthesize human insulin using recombinant E. coli\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are INCORRECT?','(ii) only','Insulin is synthesized as an inactive prohormone in all mammals consisting of an extra \'C peptide\' in between the A and B polypeptide chains. In order to produce mature and functional insulin, the C peptide is cleaved and the A and B chains are linked via disulphide linkages. Eli Lilly was the first company to obtain human recombinant insulin. It was achieved by expressing the DNA sequences of A and B chains separately in E. coli plasmids and extracting them. After extraction, the poypeptide chains were linked via sulphide bonds to form human recombinant insulin.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(ii) only','(ii) and (iv) only','(i), (iii) and (iv) only','(iv) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341124,'biology','Statement I: Mature insulin consists of two polypeptide chains linked together via sulphide bridges\n\nStatement II: Eli Lilly was the first company to extract mature insulin from recombinant E. coli','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false.','Mature insulin is produced by cleaving an inactive prohormone into two polypeptide chains, A and B, and then combining them by disulphide bonds. Eli Lilly was the first company to produce and commercialize recombinant human insulin. However, it is impossible to obtain mature insulin from E. coli since the bacteria does not have the cellular machinery to cleave and then link two polypeptide chains together. Instead, Eli Lilly were able to obtain recombinant A and B polypeptide chains of insulin expressed separately in E. coli and then combined them via chemical means by sulphide bonds.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','Both Statement I and Statement II are false.','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false.','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341125,'biology','Statement I: Insulin consists of two different polypeptide chains, hence it is a protein\n\nStatement II: Insulin is a hormone that is used in diabetes management','Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','Insulin consists of two short polypeptide chains: chain A and chain B, that are linked together by\ndisulphide bridges. Hence, it is chemically a protein. Functionally, it acts as a hormone which regulates blood sugar levels. Diabetics are not able to produce insulin properly leading to dysregulated blood sugar levels. Hence, insulin is supplied to diabetics through external means.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','Both Statement I and Statement II are false.','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false.','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341126,'biology','Statement I: Adenosine deaminase (ADA) is required for proper functioning of the immune system\n\nStatement II: ADA deficiency is an inherited genetic disorder','Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','ADA is an enzyme that is required for the proper functioning of immune system in humans. Its deficiency is caused by the deletion of gene coding for ADA. This deficiency can be passed onto offsprings by parents. Hence, ADA deficiency disorder is an inherited genetic disorder.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','Both Statement I and Statement II are false.','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false.','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341127,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) Adenosine deaminase (ADA) deficiency cannot be treated by bone marrow transplantation\n\n(ii) Retroviruses have been used as vectors in gene therapy\n\n(iii) Lymphocytes are genetically engineered to treat ADA deficiency disorder\n\n(iv) ADA deficiency disorder can be caused by deletion of the gene encoding for ADA\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(ii), (iii) and (iv) only','Before the advent of rDNA technology, ADA deficiency was partly treated by bone marrow transplantation. ADA deficiency disorder severely compromises the immune system of the affected individuals. It is caused by the deletion of or mutations in the gene coding for ADA. Gene therapy for ADA deficiency involves the introduction of functional ADA complementary DNA (cDNA) into the lymphocytes of the affected individual through a retrovirus vector. These genetically engineered lymphocytes are then reinserted into the patient for treatment.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii) and (iii) only','(i), (ii) and (iv) only','(ii), (iii) and (iv) only','(iii) and (iv) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341128,'biology','Assertion: ADA deficiency can be permanently cured if functional gene is introduced in early embryonic stages\n\nReason: Functional ADA gene is inserted in mature lymphocytes in the bone marrow','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','If the functional ADA gene is introduced during the early embryonic stages, then all cells formed therafter by embryonic division would have the functional ADA gene. Thus, ADA deficiency would be cured permanently. In the current gene therapy regime, the functional gene is inserted into the mature lymphocytes taken from the affected individual.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341129,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) PCR and ELISA are both examples of molecular diagnostics\n\n(ii) ELISA is a molecular diagnostic technique but PCR is not.\n\n(iii) PCR can be used to detect infections but ELISA cannot be used to detect infections.\n\n(iv) PCR utilizes DNA complementary base pairing and ELISA uses antigen-antibody interactions for diagnosis\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(i) and (iv) only','Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) and enzyme linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) both are examples of molecular diagnostic techniques since they detect diseases using molecular interaction. PCR and ELISA both can be used for detection of infections. PCR enables the replication of a gene of interest into a million copies in in vitro conditions. Whereas ELISA is based on antibody-antigen interactions and detects pathogenic antigens (proteins, etc.) or other antibodies produced in response to the pathogen.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) and (iv) only','(ii) and (iii) only','(i), (iii) and (iv) only','(ii), (iii) and (iv) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341130,'biology','Assertion: PCR can be utilized to detect genetic disorders\n\nReason: PCR utilizes DNA polymerase and a pair of specific primers to create copies of a gene of interest','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','PCR is a molecular technique to create multiple copies of a gene/DNA of interest. It achieves it by using a set of specific primers that bind to the region of interest via complementary base pairing. The primer is then extended along the length of the region of interest by DNA polymerase and dNTPs. Thus, one molecule is copied to two molecules at the end of one PCR cycle. In this way, millions of copies of the gene of interest can be created over a few cycles. PCR is used to detect the presence of pathogenic DNA in samples.Genetic disorders are those wherein genes are mutated/deleted leading to a loss/reduction of function. Such genes can be detected by PCR because of its amplification potential.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341131,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) Autoradiography and PCR both can be utilized to detect gene mutations\n\n(ii) ELISA can detect the presence of pathogenic proteins but not pathogenic DNA\n\n(iii) PCR can be used to detect genetic mutations but not autoradiography\n\n(iv) PCR cannot detect the presence of pathogenic antigens \n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(i), (ii) and (iv) only','In autoradiography, a single stranded DNA or RNA is tagged with a radioactive molecule (probe) and allowed to hybridise to its complementary DNA in a clone of\ncells. Radioactivity appears on a photographic film if the complementary DNA is present. PCR amplifies a particular gene of interest into a million copies. Both autoradiograhy and PCR can be used to detect mutations in genes. PCR is a DNA-based technique and hence cannot be used to detect pathogenic proteins. ELISA can only detect pathogenic proteins since it is based on the interaction of antibodies with antigens.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) and (ii) only','(i), (ii) and (iv) only','(ii) and (iii) only','(ii) and (iv) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341132,'biology','Statement I: Transgenic animals are similar to recombinant bacteria in regard to the presence of a foreign gene\n\nStatement II: Transgenic animals are defined as animals capable of transferring genes from one species to another','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false.','Transgenic animals are those that possess and express a foreign gene. They are similar to recombinant bacteria in the context that they contain a foreign gene. While interbreeding is a natural phenomenon in some animals species and leads to formation of cross breeds and it is not an example of transgenic animals','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','Both Statement I and Statement II are false.','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false.','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341133,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) Transgenic animals cannot be designed to study the effects of genetic changes on physiology\n\n(ii) Transgenic mice have been developed to study the role of genes in Alzheimer\'s disease\n\n(iii) Animals can be genetically modified to be susceptible to certain chemicals\n\n(iv) Genetic modification in animals cannot allow the study of human diseases\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(ii) and (iii) only','Transgenic animals can be designed to study the effects of genetic changes on physiological changes such as growth. The biological role of many biomolecular factors can be deduced using transgenic animals. Transgenic mice are being used as disease models to study genetic role in diseases such as Alzheimer\'s. Transgenic animals that are susceptible to certain chemicals are used in chemical safety testing. Development of transgenic animals specifically for the study of diseases of human interest, such as Alzheimer\'s, cystic fibrosis, etc. is one of the most important part in genetic engineering. Many disease models are available for the study of diseases and their effects on the body, however most of them are transgenic mice.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) and (iv) only','(ii) and (iii) only','(iii) and (iv) only','(i) and (iii) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341134,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) Disease models are transgenic animals that help in understanding the relationship between genes and human disease\n\n(ii) Disease models are transgenic animals that are represent for all the disease in Humans.\n\n(iii) Like recombinant bacteria, transgenic animals can also synthesize products of human interest\n\n(iv) Transgenic animals can only be used to study genetic effects but not for synthesis of products of human interest\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are INCORRECT?','(ii) and (iv) only','Transgenic models that help in understanding the development of a human disease, its genetic roots and its treatment with drugs, are referred to as disease models. Transgenic mice have been developed as models for a number of human diseases. Transgenic animals can be used to study the effects of genes and also for production of things of human interest, such as biological products of medicinal importance.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(ii) and (iv) only','(i) and (iii) only','(ii) and (iii) only','(ii) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341135,'biology','Assertion: Transgenic animals can be used to study genetic regulation\n\nReason: Transgenic animals possess foreign gene(s)','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Transgenic animals are those that possess and express a foreign gene. They are genetically modified animals developed to emulate modified physiology, diseases, etc. Hence, the effects produced by the addition or deletion of a specific set of gene(s) can be easily studied in transgenic animals.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341136,'biology','Statement I: The first transgenic cow secreted human lactalbumin in its milk\n\nStatement II: Transgenic mice have been developed to replace monkeys for vaccine safety testing','Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','The first transgenic cow was genetically modified to express and secrete human lactalbumin in its milk. Transgenic mice have been developed to test the safety of polio vaccine. If successful, these mice would replace monkeys for vaccine testing.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','Both Statement I and Statement II are false.','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false.','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341137,'biology','Statement I: The genetic engineering approval committee (GEAC) is an Indian Govt body for evaluating the safety of releasing GM organisms for public service\n\nStatement II: The release of GM organisms in the ecosystem would lead to improvement in the ecological balance','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false.','The GEAC was set up by the Govt. of India to oversee the validity of GM research and the safety of introducing GM-organisms for public services. The release of GM organisms in the ecosystem may have unpredictable results including negative ones.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','Both Statement I and Statement II are false.','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false.','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341138,'biology','Assertion: Regulation of genetic manipulation of living organisms is required\n\nReason: Companies exploit biological resources and traditional knowledge without proper consent','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','There are several moral and biological factors that necessitate the existence of regulations for GMO use and release for public use. Unchecked manipulation of living organisms and release of GMOs could have many unpredictable and negative results. One of the other reasons for GMO regulations is that companies use existing traditional knowledge, biological resources and genetic materials without proper consent or approval from the concerned community or country.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341139,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n(i) Use of transgenic mice to study the physiological effects of vaccines\n\n(ii) Genetic manipulation of organisms to increase their susceptibility to drugs\n\n(iii) Multinational companies use and patent traditional knowledge\n\n(iv) Release of transgenic mice in the wild\n\nWhich of the above statement(s) can be considered as unethical?','(iii) and (iv) only','Transgenic mice have been developed to study the safety of vaccines. Genetic manipulation of organisms to increase their susceptibility to drugs is a common practice in chemical safety testing. However, it is heavily regulated by govt. authorities. The patenting of traditional knowledge by MNCs is unethical as traditional knowledge refers to the knowledge that has been passed down through generations. Additionally, release of transgenic mice in the wild is also unethical as it may be harmful to both the transgenic mice and the wild organisms in general.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii) and (iii) only','(ii) and (iii) only','(ii) and (iv) only','(iii) and (iv) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341140,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n(i) Biopiracy refers to the stealing of bioresources by anti social groups and selling them for monetary profit\n\n(ii) Biopiracy refers to the exploitation of biological resources by companies without getting proper approval from the country/community involved\n\n(iii) Patenting of a traditional herb without compensation or authorization from the concerned country can be termed as biopiracy\n\n(iv) Biopiracy cannot be regulated through laws as multiple countries are involved in such cases\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(ii) and (iii) only','Biopiracy is the term used to refer to the use of bio-resources by multinational companies and other organisations without proper authorisation from the countries and people concerned without compensatory payment. Exploitation of indigenous knowledge without information to the local communities is now considered unethical and many countries including India have framed laws against biopiracy.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(ii) and (iii) only','(i) and (iii) only','(ii), (iii) and (iv) only','(ii) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341141,'biology','Assertion: The development and patenting of a new Basmati rice variety by an American company is an example of biopiracy\n\nReason: Exploitation of bioresources and indigenous knowledge without proper approval or compensation to the concerned community is unethical','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','The new Basmati variety that was patented was in fact developed from the Indian farmers\'s varieties. Hence, traditional knowledge of Indian farmers was used here to develop something that was termed \'novel.\' Hence, this is an example of biopiracy.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341142,'biology','Statement I: Whole plants can be grown in test tubes using explants \n\nStatement II: The composition of nutrient medium is important for the growth of plants in tubes','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Explants are parts of plants that have the ability to regenerate and can give rise to a whole plant. Whole plants can be grown in test tubes by culturing explants in specific nutrient media. The composition of nutrient medium is essential for the growth of plant. It must contain a carbon source such as sucrose, inorganic salts, growth regulators, vitamins, etc.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341143,'biology','Assertion: Tissue culture can be used to create identical genetic copies of plants\n\nReason: The capacity to regenerate a whole plant from a single cell/explant is called totipotency','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','Tissue culture can be used to create identical genetic copies of plants. In general, when an explant is cultured in test tubes, it gives rise to a whole plant. Every plant generated in this manner is genetically identical to the explant from which it grows. Hence, tissue culture can give rise to plant clones. These plants are referred to as somaclones. Totipotency refers to the capability of a cell/explant to regenerate into a whole cell.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(41418,'chemistry','AB₃E₂ type of molecules have :','T- shaped molecular geometry','The electronic arrangement in AB₃E₂ is trigonal bipyramidal the presence of 2 lone pairs force it adopt T-shaped so as to minimize the repulsion between bond pairs and lone pairs.\n\nAs shown in diagram \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 115; Table 4.7) diagram(ne0411)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'3','mcq','L- shaped molecular geometry','T- shaped molecular geometry','V- shaped molecular geometry','None of these','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(41419,'chemistry','The molecular geometry of IF₅:','Square pyramid','The electronic arrangement in this type of molecules(5 bp,1 lp) is octahedral but lp exerts repulsion as a result bond piars adjust them as far apart as possible so as to minimize the repulsive forces between them so this type of molecules adopt square pyramid shape.\n\nAs shown in diagram\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 115; Table 4.7) diagram(ne0411)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'3','mcq','Octahedral','Square pyramid','See-saw','T- shaped molecular geometry','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(341144,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) Tissue culture enables the growth of healthy plant from diseased plant\n\n(ii) Protoplasts are single plant cells with all cell structures that can be grown to give rise to whole plant\n\n(iii) It is not possible to fuse the characteristics of two different species using plant tissue culture\n\n(iv) Somatic hybrids are plants obtained after fusion of two different protoplasts and subsequent growth in test tube\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(i) and (iv) only','Tissue culture can be used to recover healthy plants from diseased plants. This is achieved by culturing and growing the apical or axillary meristem of the plant that does not infected by a pathogen e.g. virus. Protoplasts are single plant cells whose cell wall has been digested, in other words, they are plant cells with only one layer of plasma membrane. It is possible to fuse the characteristics of two different plant species using tissue culture. This is achieved by a technique called as somatic hybridization, wherein protoplasts from two different varieties or species of crops can be fused and grown in lab conditions to form a hybrid plant. These hybrid plants are referred to as somatic hybrids. Pomato is one of the most prominent example of somatic hybridization wherein protoplasts from tomato and potato were fused to generate pomato.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) and (iv) only','(i), (ii) and (iv) only','(ii) and (iii) only','(ii), (iii) and (iv) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341145,'biology','Statement I: Micropropagation involves the growth of a large number of plants by culturing explants in test tubes\n\nStatement II: Somatic hybridization is a technique involving the hybridization of two different plants obtained in micropropagation','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Micropropagation is a tissue culture method of obtaining a large number of plantlets by culturing explants in lab conditions. Somatic hybridization involves the fusion of protoplasts, single plant cells without a cell wall, to yield a hybrid protoplast which is grown into a hybrid plant.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341146,'biology','Assertion: Healthy plants can be grown by culturing apical meristem from diseased plants in vitro\n\nReason: Sterile conditions are a prerequisite in maintaining plant tissue culture','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','Healthy plants can be grown by culturing meristem from diseased plants in vitro because the apical meristem does not contain the pathogenic agent such as virus. Sterile conditions are maintained in plant tissue culture systems to prevent the unneccessary growth of bacteria, fungi and other microbes in the culture medium, which may hamper the growth of the cultured plants.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341201,'biology','Hepatitis-B vaccine has been produced using','Yeast cells','The hepatitis B vaccine is made by taking the part of the virus that makes surface protein ("surface protein gene") and putting it into yeast cells. The yeast cells then produce many copies of the protein that are subsequently used to make the vaccine.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Yeast cells','E.Coli','Bacillus Thuringenesis','Retrovirus','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341202,'biology','Which of the following things happened in the year 1997?','both B and C','The first clinical gene therapy was given in 1990 to a 4-year old girl\nwith adenosine deaminase (ADA) deficiency.  In 1997, the first transgenic cow, Rosie, produced human protein-enriched milk In\n1983, Eli Lilly an American company prepared two  DNA sequences\ncorresponding to A and B, chains of human insulin. In 1997, an American\ncompany got patent rights on Basmati rice through the US Patent and\nTrademark Office.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','A. 4 year old girl was given ADA gene therapy','B. Rosie produced human alpha lactalbumin','C. American company got patent on Basmati Rice','both B and C','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341203,'biology','Give the order of these events according to their chronology. \nA. 4 year old girl was given ADA gene therapy \nB. Rosie produced human alpha lactalbumin \nC. American company got patent on Basmati Rice\nD. Eli Lilly, an American company, produced human Insulin.','D> A> B=C','The first clinical gene therapy was given in 1990 to a 4-year old girl\nwith adenosine deaminase (ADA) deficiency.  In 1997, the first transgenic cow, Rosie, produced human protein-enriched milk In\n1983, Eli Lilly an American company prepared two  DNA sequences\ncorresponding to A and B, chains of human insulin. In 1997, an American\ncompany got patent rights on Basmati rice through the US Patent and\nTrademark Office.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','D> A> B=C','C> D> B=A','B> C> D=A','B=C>D>A','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341204,'biology','Which is India\'s first transgenic crop?','Bt Brinjal','Bt brinjal was the first transgenic crop developed in India, with a desired gene isolated from the Bacillus thuringiensis bacteria.\nThis is an insect-resistance gene that protects the crop from insect attack. This gene was also inserted into the Cotton crop, resulting in Bt cotton.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Bt Brinjal','Bt Cotton','Bt Corn','Bt Tomato','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341205,'biology','The Bt toxin produced by the cry gene is','Crystalline','B. thuringiensis\nforms protein crystals during a particular phase of their growth.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Crystalline','Amorphous','Glycoprotein','Lipopolysaccharide','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341206,'biology','Do all the strains of Bacillus thuringenesis produce Bt toxin that kills certain insects?','No','Some strains of Bacillus thuringiensis produce proteins that\nkill certain insects such as lepidopterans (tobacco budworm, armyworm),\ncoleopterans (beetles) and dipterans (flies, mosquitoes). B. thuringiensis\nforms protein crystals during a particular phase of their growth.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','No','Yes','All of them produce Bt toxin but some are not toxic for insect and do not kill insects','Only some produce Bt toxin, others produce other toxins to kill insects','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341207,'biology','Bt toxin gene has been cloned from','Bacteria','Bt toxin gene has\nbeen cloned from the bacteria and been expressed in plants to provide\nresistance to insects without the need for insecticides;','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'1','mcq','Bacteria','Protozoa','Unicellular fungi','Unicellular Algae','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341208,'biology','How many polypeptides are present in human insulin?','51','Insulin is made up of two short polypeptide chains, A and B, that are joined together by disulfide bridges.\nHuman insulin is made up of 51 amino acids that are organized into two polypeptide chains: A (21 amino acids) and B (30 amino acids).','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'1','mcq','31','51','52','33','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341209,'biology','Which is the longest chain of human insulin?','Chain B','Insulin is made up of two short polypeptide chains, A and B, that are joined together by disulfide bridges. Human insulin is made up of 51 amino acids that are organized into two polypeptide chains: A (21 amino acids) and B (30 amino acids).','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Chain C','Chain A','Chain B','all are equal length','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341210,'biology','Which is the shortest chain of human insulin?','Chain A','Insulin is made up of two short polypeptide chains, A and B, that are joined together by disulfide bridges. Human insulin is made up of 51 amino acids that are organized into two polypeptide chains: A (21 amino acids) and B (30 amino acids).','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Chain C','Chain A','Chain B','all are equal length','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341211,'biology','Severe Combined Imuno-deficiency occurs due to','ADA deficiency','Adenosine deaminase deficiency (ADA deficiency) is a metabolic disorder that causes immunodeficiency. It is caused by mutations in the ADA gene. It accounts for about 10–15% of all cases of autosomal recessive forms of severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID) among non-inbred populations.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Insulin deficiency','ADA deficiency','HIV infection','Cystic fibrosis','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341212,'biology','Transgenetic models exists for which of the following autosomal dominant disease?','Alzhimer\'s disease','Today transgenic models exist for many human diseases\nsuch as cancer, cystic fibrosis, rheumatoid arthritis and Alzheimer’s.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Alzhimer\'s disease','Cystic fibrosis','Phenylketonuria','All of the above','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341213,'biology','For every genetically different strain of rice how many varieties are present?','4','There are an estimated 200,000 varieties of rice in India alone. India has more than 50,000 genetically different strains\nof rice. Therefore, there are 200000/50000= 4 varieties for every genetically different strain of rice.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','4','27','50000','200000','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341214,'biology','The first ever human hormone produced by recombinant DNA technology is','Insulin','The first human hormone produced by recombinant DNA technology is insulin.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Progesterone','Insulin','Estrogen','Thyroid hormone','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341215,'biology','Which of the following is not used as a biopesticide?','Xanthomonas campestris','Xanthomonas campestris is a gram-negative bacteria.\nIt causes black rot in plants.\nIt is not used as a biofertilizer.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Bacillus thuringiensis','Xanthomonas campestris','Trichoderma harzianum','Nuclear Polyhedrosis Virus','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341216,'biology','A transgenic food crop which may help in solving the problem of night blindness in developing countries is _______','Golden Rice','Golden rice has been engineered to produce β-carotene, a precursor of vitamin A. Hence, it may help in solving the problem of night blindness in developing countries.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'1','mcq','Golden Rice','Flavr Savr tomatoes','BT soybean','Starlink maize','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341217,'biology','Plants are more easily manipulated by genetic engineering than are animals because','More vectors are available to transfer rDNA into plant cells','Plants have the ability to grow into a complete plant from a single cell. While animals are only produced by their species and reproduction. Hence, they are more readily manipulated by genetic engineering than an animal.\nSo, the correct option is \'a somatic plant cell can grow into a complete plant\'.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Plant genes do not contain introns','More vectors are available to transfer rDNA into plant cells','Totipotency is a feature generally associated with plant cells and not with animal cells','Genes can be inserted into animal cells by microinjection','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341218,'biology','The GM plant called Golden rice produces','β-carotene','Golden rice has been engineered to produce β-carotene, a precursor of vitamin A. Hence, it may help in solving the problem of night blindness in developing countries.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'1','mcq','β-carotene','θ-carotene','δ-carotene','α-carotene','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341219,'biology','Polio vaccine is tested on transgenic ............. for safety precautions.','mice','Safety of polio vaccine is tested on transgenic mice, before using this vaccine on humans. It is because mice is supposed to be very much similar to the human beings in their genomic arrangement. This is the reason, mice are used in most of the medical researches.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'1','mcq','mice','monkey','pig','cow','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(341220,'biology','Which of the following are not used as molecular diagnostic method?','MICROSCOPY','PCR, ELISA, ELFA are molecular diagnostic method based on DNA and antigen- Antibody reaction. MICROSCOPY is viewing the tissues under microscope and seeing the cells not molecules.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology And Its Applications >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'1','mcq','PCR','ELISA','ELFA','MICROSCOPY','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
