(40228,'chemistry','In which of the following process each atom attains a stable outer octet of electrons:\n\na. Bond formation \n\nb. Sharing of electrons from one atom to another\n\nc. Transfer of electrons between atoms','All of these','The bond is formed by the sharing of a pair of electrons between the atoms. \n\nIn the process each atom attains a stable outer octet of electrons.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b and c','Only c','All of these','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40229,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statement:\n\na. Lewis postulated that atoms achieve the stable octet when they are linked by chemical bonds.\n\nb. In the formation of a molecule, only the outer shell electrons take part in chemical combination and they are known as chemical bonds.\n\nc. The inner shell electrons are well protected and are generally involved in the combination process.','Only b and c','In the formation of a molecule, only the outer shell electrons take part in chemical combination and they are known as valence electrons. The inner shell electrons are well protected and are generally not involved in the combination process. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only a and b','Only c','Only b and c','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(41420,'chemistry','The molecular geometry of which of the follwing is same:\n\na. XeF₄ \nb. PtCl₄²⁻\nc. IF₅ \nd. PH₃','Only a and b','XeF₄  ,PtCl₄²⁻ both have square planar geometry. \n\nAs shown in diagram\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 115; Table 4.7) diagram(ne0411)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'3','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only a and b','Only c and d','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(40230,'chemistry','The number of dots around the symbol represents the number of valence electrons diagram This number of valence electrons helps to calculate: diagram(n0401)','Group valence of the element','The number of dots around the symbol represents the number of valence electrons. \n\nThis number of valence electrons helps to calculate the common or group valence of the element.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'1','mcq','Group valence of the element','Valence electron of the element','Octet of electrons','Outer shell of electron','NEW','11-101','11-101','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/40230q.png'),
(40231,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: The group valence of the elements is generally either equal to the number of dots in Lewis symbols or 8 minus the number of dots or valence electrons.\n\nR: The number of valence electrons helps to calculate the common or group valence of the element.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explaination of assertion','The number of valence electrons helps to calculate the common or group valence of the element.\n\nThe group valence of the elements is generally either equal to the number of dots in Lewis symbols or 8 minus the number of dots or valence electrons.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explaination of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explaination of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40232,'chemistry','Choose an appropriate option for the given statement: "In the periodic table, the highly electronegative halogens and the highly electropositive alkali metals are separated by"','Noble gases','In the periodic table, the highly electronegative halogens and the highly electropositive alkali metals are separated by the noble gases. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'3','mcq','Noble gases','Group trends','Unknown properties','Post transition metals','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40233,'chemistry','Choose an appropriate option for the given statement: "The formation of a negative ion from a halogen atom and a positive ion from an alkali metal atom is associated with"','Gain and loss of electron','The formation of a negative ion from a halogen atom and a positive ion from an alkali metal atom is associated with the gain and loss of an electron by the respective atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'2','mcq','Gain and loss of electron','Generation of free radical','Sharing of alternate proton','Sharing of alternate bond','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40234,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: The negative and positive ions thus formed attain unstable noble gas electronic configurations.\n\nR: The noble gases have a particularly stable outer shell configuration of eight (octet) electrons, ns²np⁶.','Assertion is false but the reason is true','The negative and positive ions thus formed attain stable noble gas electronic configurations. \n\nThe noble gases (with the exception of helium which has a duplet of electrons) have a particularly stable outer shell configuration of eight (octet) electrons, ns²np⁶. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40235,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: The negative and positive ions are stabilized by electrostatic attraction.\n\nR: The electrostatic force is an attractive and repulsive force between particles is caused due to their electric charges so negative and positive ions are held by the electrostatic force of attraction','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','The negative and positive ions are stabilized by electrostatic attraction. \n\nThe formation of a negative ion from a halogen atom and a positive ion from an alkali metal atom is associated with the gain and loss of an electron by the respective atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40236,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Atoms achieve the stable octet when they are linked by chemical bonds.\n\nR: The formation of NaCl from sodium and chlorine.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Lewis postulated that atoms achieve the stable octet when they are linked by chemical bonds. \n\nThe negative and positive ions are stabilized by electrostatic attraction. \n\nFor example, the formation of NaCl from sodium and chlorine. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.2-5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40237,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement regarding Kössel drew, in relation to chemical bonding:\n\na. The noble gases have a particularly stable outer shell configuration of eight (octet) electrons, ns²np⁶.\n\nb. The negative and neutral ions thus formed attain stable noble gas electronic configurations. \n\nc. In the periodic table, the highly electronegative halogens and the highly electropositive alkali metals are separated by the noble gases.','Only a and c','Kössel, in relation to chemical bonding, drew attention to the following facts: In the periodic table, the highly electronegative halogens and the highly electropositive alkali metals are separated by the noble gases.\n\nThe negative and positive ions thus formed attain stable noble gas electronic configurations. \n\nThe noble gases (with the exception of helium which has a duplet of electrons) have a particularly stable outer shell configuration of eight (octet) electrons, ns²np⁶. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and c','Only b and c','Only a and b','All statements are correct','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40238,'chemistry','A chemical bond formed between ions of opposite charge:','Electrovalent bond','The bond formed, as a result of the electrostatic attraction between the positive and negative ions was termed as the electrovalent bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'1','mcq','Electrovalent bond','Covalent bond','Metallic bond','Chemical bond','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40239,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: The bond formed, as a result of the electrostatic attraction between the positive and negative ions was termed as the electrovalent bond.\n\nR: The electrovalence is thus equal to the number of unit charge(s) on the ion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','The bond formed, as a result of the electrostatic attraction between the positive and negative ions was termed as the electrovalent bond. \n\nThe electrovalence is thus equal to the number of unit charge(s) on the ion. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 101; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40240,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: The electrovalence is equal to the number of unit charge(s) on the ion.\n\nR: Calcium is assigned a positive electrovalence of two, while chlorine a negative electrovalence of one.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','The electrovalence is thus equal to the number of unit charge(s) on the ion. Thus, calcium is assigned a positive electrovalence of two, while chlorine a negative electrovalence of one. \n\nReference: ( NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40241,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect option:\n\na. Lewis postulations provide the basis for the modern concepts regarding  the formation of ionic crystalline compounds.\n\nb. Kössel’s postulations provide the basis for the modern concepts regarding ion-formation by electron transfer.\n\nc. At the same time Kössel’s did recognise the fact that a large number of compounds did not fit into these concepts.\n\nd. Lewis postulated that atoms achieve the stable octet when they are linked by chemical bonds.','Only a','Kössel’s postulations provide the basis for the modern concepts regarding ion-formation by electron transfer and the formation of ionic crystalline compounds. \n\nHis views have proved to be of great value in the understanding and systematisation of the ionic compounds. \n\nAt the same time he did recognise the fact that a large number of compounds did not fit into these concepts. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only c','Only a and c','Only b and c','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40242,'chemistry','His views have proved to be of great value in the understanding and systematisation of the ionic compounds:','Kössel','Kössel’s postulations provide the basis for the modern concepts regarding ion-formation by electron transfer and the formation of ionic crystalline compounds. \n\nHis views have proved to be of great value in the understanding and systematisation of the ionic compounds. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Kossel-lewis approach',NULL,'1','mcq','Lewis','Kössel','Nyholm and Ronald Gillespie','Friedrich Hund','NEW','11-101','11-101',NULL),
(40814,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement regarding octet rule: \n\na. The given molecules does\'nt satisfy octet rule due to incomplete valence electrons.\n\nb. The given molecule satisfy octet rule due to the charge present on it.\n\nc. A molecule without a charge can only satisfy an octet rule.\n\ndiagram(n0404)','Only a','In molecules with an odd number of electrons like nitric oxide, NO and nitrogen dioxide, NO₂, the octet rule is not satisfied for all the atoms.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Limitations of octet rule',NULL,'3','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c','Only b and c','NEW','11-105','11-105','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/40814q.png'),
(40815,'chemistry','Justify the statement: " In a number of compounds of third period elements there are more than eight valence electrons around the central atom."','Expanded octet','In a number of compounds of these elements there are more than eight valence electrons around the central atom.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Limitations of octet rule',NULL,'2','mcq','Expanded octet','Odd-electron molecules','Incomplete octet','None of these','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(40816,'chemistry','Justify the statement: "The case with elements having less than four valence electrons."','Incomplete octet','The number of electrons surrounding the central atom is less than eight. \n\nThis is especially the case with elements having less than four valence electrons.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Limitations of octet rule',NULL,'2','mcq','Expanded octet','Odd-electron molecules','Incomplete octet','None of these','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(40817,'chemistry','Justify the statement: "The molecules like nitric oxide, NO and nitrogen dioxide, NO₂, the octet rule is not satisfied for all the atoms."','Odd-electron molecules','In molecules with an odd number of electrons like nitric oxide, NO and nitrogen dioxide, NO₂, the octet rule is not satisfied for all the atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Limitations of octet rule',NULL,'2','mcq','Expanded octet','Odd-electron molecules','Incomplete octet','None of these','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(40818,'chemistry','Choose the appropriate statement regarding octet rule:\n\na. Elements in and beyond the third period of the periodic table have also available for bonding.\n\nb. Elements in and beyond the third period of the periodic table have, apart from 3s and 3p orbitals, 3d orbitals also available for bonding.\n\nc. In a number of compounds of third period elements there are more than eight valence electrons around the central atom.','Only a and c','Elements in and beyond the third period of the periodic table have, apart from 3s and 3p orbitals, 3d orbitals also available for bonding. \n\nIn a number of compounds of these elements there are more than eight valence electrons around the central atom. \n\nThis is termed as the expanded octet. Obviously the octet rule does not apply in such cases.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 106; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Limitations of octet rule',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only a and b','Only a and c','Only c','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(41326,'chemistry','Dipole moment arrow symbolises the direction of the shift of ____________ in the molecule.','Electron density','Dipole moment arrow symbolises the direction of the shift of electron density in the molecule. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 111; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'1','mcq','Pair of electrons','Electron density','Nuclear Charge','Mass density','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41327,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: The direction of crossed arrow is opposite to the conventional direction of dipole moment vector. \n\nR: Arrow symbolises the direction of the shift of hydride in the molecule.','Assertion is true but the reason is false','This arrow symbolises the direction of the shift of electron density in the molecule. \n\nNote that the direction of crossed arrow is opposite to the conventional direction of dipole moment vector. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 111; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41328,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statement regardind dipole moment:\n\na. Dipole moment is a Scalar quantity.\n\nb. In dipole moment arrow, head is on positive end and arrow cross is on negative end.\n\nc. This arrow symbolises the direction of the shift of electron density in the molecule.\n\nd. Dipole moment also on the spatial arrangement of various bonds in the molecule.','Only a and b','Dipole moment is a vector quantity. \n\nIn dipole moment arrow, cross is on positive end and arrow head is on negative end. This arrow symbolises the direction of the shift of electron density in the molecule. \n\nDipole moment also on the spatial arrangement of various bonds in the molecule. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 111; Paragraph 3-5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'1','mcq','Only a and b','Only a and c','Only b and d','Only c and d','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41329,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: In case of polyatomic molecules the dipole moment of a molecule is the vector sum of the dipole moments of various bonds.\n\nR: In such case, the dipole moment not only depend upon the individual dipole moments of bonds known as bond dipoles but also on the spatial arrangement of various bonds in the molecule.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','In case of polyatomic molecules the dipole moment not only depend upon the individual dipole moments of bonds known as bond dipoles but also on the spatial arrangement of various bonds in the molecule. \n\nIn such case, the dipole moment of a molecule is the vector sum of the dipole moments of various bonds. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 111; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41330,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statement:\n\na. The dipole moment in case of BeF₂ is zero.\n\nb. In tetra-atomic molecule, for example in BF₃, the dipole moment is also zero.\n\nc.In case of NH₃ and NF₃ molecule, the resultant dipole moment of NH₃ is greater than that of NF₃.','None of these','The dipole moment in case of BeF₂ is zero. \n\nThis is because the two equal bond dipoles point in opposite directions and cancel the effect of each other. \n\nIn tetra-atomic molecule, for example in BF₃, the dipole moment is zero although the B – F bonds are oriented at an angle of 120 to one another, the three bond moments give a net sum of zero as the resultant of any two is equal and opposite to the third. \n\nIn case of NH₃ and NF₃ molecule. Both the molecules have pyramidal shape with a lone pair of electrons on nitrogen atom. Although fluorine is more electronegative than nitrogen, the resultant dipole moment of NH₃ (4.90 × 10–30 C m) is greater than that of NF₃ (0.8 × 10–30 C m). \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 111; Paragraph 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and c','Only b','All of these','None of these','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41331,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: In case of NH₃ and NF₃ molecule, the resultant dipole moment of NH₃ is greater than that of NF₃.\n\nR: This is because, in case of NH₃ the orbital dipole due to lone pair is in the opposite direction as the resultant dipole moment of the N – H bonds, whereas in NF₃ the orbital dipole is in the direction same to the resultant dipole moment of the three N–F bonds.','Assertion is true but the reason is false','In case of NH₃ and NF₃ molecule. Both the molecules have pyramidal shape with a lone pair of electrons on nitrogen atom. Although fluorine is more electronegative than nitrogen, the resultant dipole moment of NH₃ ( 4.90 × 10–30 C m) is greater than that of NF₃ (0.8 × 10–30 C m). \n\nThis is because, in case of NH₃ the orbital dipole due to lone pair is in the same direction as the resultant dipole moment of the N – H bonds, whereas in NF₃ the orbital dipole is in the direction opposite to the resultant dipole moment of the three N–F bonds. \n\nThe orbital dipole because of lone pair decreases the effect of the resultant N – F bond moments. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 111; Paragraph 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Polarity of bonds',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41401,'chemistry','Who proposed VSEPR theory in 1940','Sidwick and Powell','Sidwick and Powell in 1940, proposed a simple theory based on the repulsive interactions of the electron pairs in the valence shell of the atoms. \n\nIt was further developed and redefined by Nyholm and Gillespie (1957). \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 113; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Sidwick and Powell','Nylholm and Gillespie','Goldstein','Guldberg and waage','NEW','11-112','11-112',NULL),
(41402,'chemistry','The shape of the molecule depends on?','Both a and b','The shape of a molecule depends upon the number of valence shell electron pairs (bonded or nonbonded) around the central atom. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 113; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Bonding electrons around central atom','Non-bonding electrons around central atom','Inner shell electrons of central atom','Both a and b','NEW','11-112','11-112',NULL),
(41427,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement regarding VSEPR theory:\n\na. This theory provides a simple procedure to predict the shapes of covalent molecules.\n \nb. Nyholm and Gillespie in 1940, proposed a simple theory based on the repulsive interactions of the electron pairs in the valence shell of the atoms.\n\nc. It was further developed and redefined by Sidgwick and Powell (1957).','Only a','Lewis concept is unable to explain the shapes of molecules. This theory provides a simple procedure to predict the shapes of covalent molecules. \n\nSidgwick and Powell in 1940, proposed a simple theory based on the repulsive interactions of the electron pairs in the valence shell of the atoms. \n\nIt was further developed and redefined by Nyholm and Gillespie (1957). \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 112; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only a and c','Only a and b','All of these','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(41428,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: While the lone pairs are localised on the central atom, each bonded pair is shared between two atoms. \n\nR: As a result, the lone pair electrons in a molecule occupy even less space as compared to the bonding pairs of electrons.','Assertion is true but the reason is false','While the lone pairs are localised on the central atom, each bonded pair is shared between two atoms. \n\nAs a result, the lone pair electrons in a molecule occupy more space as compared to the bonding pairs of electrons. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 113; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(41429,'chemistry','The molecular geometry of Ammonia is:','Trigonal pyramidal','Theoretically the shape should have been tetrahedal but actually it is found to be trigonal pyramidal, this is due to lp-bp repulsion which is more than bp-bp repulsion so the angle is reduced from 107 from 109.5. \n\nAs shown in diagram  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 116; Table 4.8) diagram(ne0412)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Bent','Trigonal pyramidal','See-saw','T-shaped','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(41430,'chemistry','Choose the correct molecule by identifying their shape; Trigonal planar, and molecule type AX₃E₀:','BF₃','It is AX₃E₀ type molecule, three bond pairs remain as far apart as possible to minimize the repulsion between bond pairs and adopt the trigonal planar geometry.\n\nAs shown in diagram \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 114; Table 4.6) diagram(ne0410)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'2','mcq','BF₃','NH₃','ClF₃','BrF₃','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(41431,'chemistry','Choose the correct molecule by identifying their shape; Trigonal pyramidal, and molecule type AX₃E₁:','NH₃','It is AX₃E₁ type molecule, it is found to be trigonal pyramidal, this is due to lp-bp repulsion which is more than bp-bp repulsion so the angle is reduced from 107 from 109.5. \n\nAs shown in diagram  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 116; Table 4.8) diagram(ne0412)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'2','mcq','BF₃','NH₃','ClF₃','BrF₃','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(41432,'chemistry','Choose the correct molecule by identifying their shape; T-shaped, and molecule type AX₃E₂:','ClF₃','It is AX₃E₂ type molecule, In (a) the lp are at equatorial position so there are less lp-bp repulsions as compared to others in which the lp are at axial positions. \n\nSo structure (a) is most stable. (T-shaped). \n\nAs shown in diagram\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 117; Table 4.8) diagram(ne0413)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'2','mcq','BF₃','NH₃','ClF₃','CH₄','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(41433,'chemistry','Choose the correct molecule by identifying their shape; Tetrahedral and molecule type AX₄E₀:','CH₄','It is AX₄E₀ type molecule, has tetrahedral molecular geometry.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 117; Table 4.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'2','mcq','BF₃','NH₃','ClF₃','CH₄','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(41434,'chemistry','The VSEPR Theory is able to predict geometry of a large number of molecules, especially the compounds of ____________ accurately.','p-block elements','The VSEPR Theory is able to predict geometry of a large number of molecules, especially the compounds of p-block elements accurately. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 113; Paragraph 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'1','mcq','s-block elements','p-block elements','d-block elements','f-block elements','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(41435,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement regarding VSEPR theory:\n\na. This theory is able to predict geometry of a large number of molecules, especially the compounds of d-block elements accurately.\n\nb. It is also quite successful in determining the geometry quite-accurately even when the energy difference between possible structures is very small. \n\nc. The theoretical basis of this theory is not clear and continues to be a subject of doubt and discussion.','Only b and c','The VSEPR Theory is able to predict geometry of a large number of molecules, especially the compounds of p-block elements accurately. \n\nIt is also quite successful in determining the geometry quite-accurately even when the energy difference between possible structures is very small. \n\nThe theoretical basis of the VSEPR theory regarding the effects of electron pair repulsions on molecular shapes is not clear and continues to be a subject of doubt and discussion.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 113; Paragraph 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and b','Only a and c','Only b and c','All of these','NEW','11-116','11-116',NULL),
(41436,'chemistry','Identify the molecule having no lone pair on their central atom:','SF₆','SF₆ molecule contains one sulfur atom and six fluorine atoms. \n\nIn SF₆ lewis structure, each fluorine atom has made single bonds with center sulfur atom.  \n\nAs shown in diagram  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 116; Table 4.8) diagram(ne0412)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'2','mcq','SF₆','XeF₄','ClF₃','BrF₃','NEW','11-116','11-116',NULL),
(41437,'chemistry','Identify the molecule having lone pair on their central atom:','XeF₄','XeF₄ molecule is hypervalent with six electron pairs around the central xenon atom. \n\nThese pairs adopt an octahedral arrangement. Four of the pairs are bonding pairs, and two are lone pairs.\n\nAs shown in diagram\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 115; Table 4.7) diagram(ne0411)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'2','mcq','CH₄','XeF₄','NH₄+','HgCl₂','NEW','11-116','11-116',NULL),
(41501,'chemistry','Which theory introduced quantum mechanical principles?','Valence bond theory(VBT)','Similarly the VSEPR theory gives the geometry of simple molecules but theoretically, it does not explain them and also it has limited applications. \n\nTo overcome these limitations the two important theories based on quantum mechanical principles are introduced. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 117; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Valence bond theory(VBT)','Lewis Theory','VSEPR','None of these','NEW','11-116','11-116',NULL),
(41502,'chemistry','Valence bond theory was first introduced by:','Heitler and London','Valence bond theory was introduced by Heitler and London (1927) and developed further by Pauling and others. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 117; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Heitler and London','Nylholm and Gillespie','Pauling','Lewis and Waage','NEW','11-117','11-117',NULL),
(41503,'chemistry','VBT is based on the knowledge of:','All of these','The valence bond theory is based on the knowledge of atomic orbitals, electronic configurations of elements, the overlap criteria of atomic orbitals, the hybridization of atomic orbitals and the principles of variation and superposition. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 117; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Atomic orbitals','Electronic Configurations of elements','Overlapping of Atomic orbitals','All of these','NEW','11-117','11-117',NULL),
(41504,'chemistry','How many types of repulsive and attractive forces operate during formation of H₂ molecule?','Both a and b','Nucleus of one hydrogen atom attracts its own electrons and electrons of second H so two attractive forces per H similarly for second hydrogen so total four attractive forces operate on closing of two hydrogen atoms. \n\nThe electrons of one H repels the electrons of second H so one repulsive force b/w hydrogen atom same force between nuclei of two H atoms so there are two repulsive forces as a whole 4 attractive forces 2 repulsive forces attractive forces are more than repulsive forces that\'s why bond is formed. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 117; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'3','mcq','Four attractive forces','Two repulsive forces','Both a and b','Four repulsive forces','NEW','11-117','11-117',NULL),
(41505,'chemistry','When net force of attraction balances the force of repulsion with minimum P.E a bond is _______.','Formed','In general, greater the overlap the stronger is the bond formed between two atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 118; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','fillup','For,med,bro,ken',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-118','11-118',NULL),
(41506,'chemistry','Bond Formation always _______ energy.','Release','When two atoms come close they minimize energy by releasing it the E released is called Bond enthalpy. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 118; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Release','Absorb','Utilize','Suppress','NEW','11-118','11-118',NULL),
(41507,'chemistry','The merging of atomic orbitals is called overlapping, which statement is not related to overlapping concept:\n\na. Overlapping results in pairing of electrons\n\nb. More stronger overlapping more stronger will be bond\n\nc. Covalent bond is formed as a result of overlapping of atomic orbitals\n\nd. Ionic bond is formed as a result of overlapping','Only d','In the formation of hydrogen molecule, there is a minimum energy state when two hydrogen atoms are so near that their atomic orbitals undergo partial interpenetration. \n\nThis partial merging of atomic orbitals is called overlapping of atomic orbitals which results in the pairing of electrons. The extent of overlap decides the strength of a covalent bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 118; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c','Only d','NEW','11-118','11-118',NULL),
(41508,'chemistry','Covalent bond is a ________bond.','Directional','Covaleny bond is formed by the overlapping of atomic orbitals, atomic orbitals have specific orientations, so bond formed by them will also a directional bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 119; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','fillup','Di,rec,tion,al,non',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-119','11-119',NULL),
(41509,'chemistry','H-H bond is formed by:','1s-1s overlap','The valance shell of H has one subshell that is 1s, s-subshell have one electron overlap with 1s of second hydrogen to make H-H bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 119; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','1s-1s overlap','2s-2s overlap','1s-2s overlap','1s-1s⁰ overlap','NEW','11-119','11-119',NULL),
(41510,'chemistry','The success of VBT results in explaination of except:\n\na. Shape of molecules \n\nb. Formation and directional properties of bonds\n\nc. Bond strength of polyatomic molecules\n\nd. Hybridizations of atomic orbitals','Only c','The valence bond theory explains the shape, the formation and directional properties of bonds in polyatomic molecules like CH4, NH3 and H2O, etc. in terms of overlap and hybridisation of atomic orbitals. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 119; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c','Only d','NEW','11-119','11-119',NULL),
(41511,'chemistry','Positive overlap occurs by the overlap of atomic orbitals having:','Same phase signs and orientation','When orbitals of two atoms come close to form bond their overlap may be positive, negative or zero overlap depending upon the phase sign and direction of orientation of amplitude of orbital wave function in space. \n\nNote; Phase signs do not related to charge. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 119; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Opposite phase signs and orientation','Same phase signs and orientation','Same phase signs and opposite orientation','Opposite phase signs and same orientation','NEW','11-119','11-119',NULL),
(41512,'chemistry','Negative overlap occurs by the overlap of atomic orbitals having:','Opposite phase signs and same orientation','When orbitals of two atoms come close to form bond their overlap may be positive, negative or zero overlap depending upon the phase sign and direction of orientation of amplitude of orbital wave function in space. \n\nNote; Phase signs do not related to charge. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 119; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Opposite phase signs and orientation','Same phase signs and orientation','Same phase signs and opposite orientation','Opposite phase signs and same orientation','NEW','11-119','11-119',NULL),
(260122,'chemistry','Which one of the following carbonyl compound is obtained from the meadow sweet ?\nRefer diagram(n26001)','B','Carbonyl compounds add fragrance and flavour to nature, for example, vanillin (from vanilla beans), salicylaldehyde (from meadow sweet) and cinnamaldehyde (from cinnamon) have very pleasant fragrances. \n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-358 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Introduction',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','12-358','12-228','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260122q.png'),
(41513,'chemistry','Zero overlap occurs by the overlap of atomic orbitals having:','Same phase signs and opposite orientation','When orbitals of two atoms come close to form bond their overlap may be positive, negative or zero overlap depending upon the phase sign and direction of orientation of amplitude of orbital wave function in space. \n\nNote; Phase signs do not related to charge. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 119; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Opposite phase signs and orientation','Same phase signs and orientation','Same phase signs and opposite orientation','Opposite phase signs and same orientation','NEW','11-119','11-119',NULL),
(41514,'chemistry','The uniform 4 C-H bonds and 109,5 degree angle between HCH can\'t be explained by only:','Overlapping concept','VBT fails to explain the uniformity of 4 C-H bonds and 109.5 degree angle in methane by only overlapping concept according to VBT in CH₄ 3 C-H bonds are formed by the overlap of three 2p orbitals from C with 1s orbitals of 3 hydrogens and 1 C-H bond by 2s from C and 1s from H s-subshell have no direction this bond may be in any direction since p subshell have three p orbitals at 90⁰ so the resultant three bonds must be at  90⁰ but HCH bond is  109.5⁰. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 119; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Overlapping concept','Hybridization','Hyperconjugation concept','Electron repulsion concept','NEW','11-119','11-119',NULL),
(41515,'chemistry','The Covalent bond may be calssified into:','Both 𝛔-bond and 𝝅-bond','The covalent bond may be classified into two types depending upon the types of overlapping: (i) Sigma(s) bond, and (ii) pi(p) bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 120; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','𝛔-bond','𝝅-bond','Both 𝛔-bond and 𝝅-bond','None of these','NEW','11-120','11-120',NULL),
(41516,'chemistry','𝛔-bond is formed by:','Head on overlap','Sigma type of covalent bond is formed by the end to end (head-on) overlap of bonding orbitals along the internuclear axis. \n\nThis is called as head on overlap or axial overlap\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 120; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Head on overlap','Paralell overlap','Side ways overlap','Antiparallel overlap','NEW','11-120','11-120',NULL),
(41517,'chemistry','F₂ is formed by the:','p-p overlap','p–p overlapping takes place between half filled p-orbitals of the two approaching atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 120; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','p-p overlap','s-p overlap','s-s overlap','s-d overlap','NEW','11-120','11-120',NULL),
(41518,'chemistry','C-H bond is formed by the:','s-p overlap','s-p overlapping occurs between half filled s-orbitals of one atom and half filled p-orbitals of another atom. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 120; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','p-p overlap','s-p overlap','s-s overlap','s-d overlap','NEW','11-120','11-120',NULL),
(41519,'chemistry','𝝅-bond is formed by:','Both b and c','In the formation of p bond the atomic orbitals overlap in such a way that their axes remain parallel to each other and perpendicular to the internuclear axis. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 120; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Head on overlap','Paralell overlap','Side ways overlap','Both b and c','NEW','11-120','11-120',NULL),
(41520,'chemistry','Before the formation of 𝝅-bond  between two atoms they must be bonded by:','𝛔-bond','It is important to note that in the formation of multiple bonds between two atoms of a molecule, pi bond(s) is formed in addition to a sigma bond.\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 120; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'3','mcq','𝝅-bond','𝛔-bond','Ionic bond','none','NEW','11-120','11-120',NULL),
(41521,'chemistry','𝝅-electron density lies above and below the plane of ______ axis.','Internuclear','The orbitals formed due to sidewise overlapping consists of two saucer type charged clouds above and below the plane of the participating atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 120; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Internuclear','Intranuclear','Sagittal','Transverse','NEW','11-120','11-120',NULL),
(41522,'chemistry','𝛔-bond is ________ bond than 𝝅-bond .','Stronger','𝝅-bond is formed by the sideway overlap and 𝝅-electron density lies away from the nuclei while 𝛔-bond is formed by the head on overlap 𝛔-electron density lies between two nuclei, 𝛔-electrons are tightly held as compared to 𝝅-electrons so 𝛔-bond is stronger bond than 𝝅-bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 120; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Stronger','Weaker','Same strength','In some molecules it is stronger','NEW','11-120','11-120',NULL),
(41523,'chemistry','Who introduced the concept of hybridization?','Pauling','Pauling introduced the concept of hybridisation. \n\nAccording to him the atomic orbitals combine to form new set of equivalent orbitals known as hybrid orbitals. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 120; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Lewis','Pauling','Nyholm and Gillespie','Friedrich Hund','NEW','11-120','11-120',NULL),
(41524,'chemistry','The process of mixing of atomic orbitals of different shapes and energy to give hybrid orbitals of same shape and energy is called __________.','Hybridization','The phenomenon is known as hybridisation which can be defined as the process of intermixing of the orbitals of slightly different energies so as to redistribute their energies, resulting in the formation of new set of orbitals of equivalent energies and shape. \n\nFor example when one 2s and three 2p-orbitals of carbon hybridise, there is the formation of four new sp3 hybrid orbitals. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 120; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Hybridization','Active electrons','Excitation of electron','Covalent bonding','NEW','11-120','11-120',NULL),
(41525,'chemistry','Same number of atomic orbitals mixed to form same number of :','Hybrid orbitals','When one 2s and three 2p-orbitals of carbon hybridise, there is the formation of four new sp3 hybrid orbitals. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 120; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Hybrid orbitals','Atomic orbitals','Molecular orbitals','None of these','NEW','11-120','11-120',NULL),
(260224,'chemistry','The resonance in the carbonyl compounds is between','a dipole structure and a neutral structure','The high polarity of the carbonyl group is explained on the basis of resonance involving a neutral and a dipolar structures.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph-2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'2','mcq','dipole structures','a dipole structure and a neutral structure','neutral structure','any of the above','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(41526,'chemistry','Which one is not the feature of Hybridization:\n\na. The number of hybrid orbitals is not equal to the number of atomic orbitals that get mixed\n\nb. The hybridised orbitals arealways equivalent in energy and shape\n\nc. The hybrid orbitals are more effective in forming stable bonds than pure atomic orbitals \n\nd. The hybrid orbitals are directed in space in some preffered directions to have minimum repulsion between electron pairs','Only a','The hybridised orbitals arealways equivalent in energy and shape.The hybrid orbitals are more effective in forming stable bonds than pure atomic orbitals. The hybrid orbitals are directed in space in some preffered directions to have minimum repulsion between electron pairsReference : ( NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 121; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c','Only d','NEW','11-121','11-121',NULL),
(41527,'chemistry','Which of the following are conditions for hybridization:\n\na. Only valence shell orbitals are hybridized \n\nb. Almost same energy orbitals undergo hybridization\n\nc. Excitation of electron is not always necessary prior to hybridization\n\nd. Completely filled may also participate in hybridization','All statements are correct','The orbitals present in the valence shell of the atom are hybridised. The orbitals undergoing hybridisation should have almost equal energy. Promotion of electron is not essential condition prior to hybridisation. It is not necessary that only half filled orbitals participate in hybridisation. In some cases, even filled orbitals of valence shell take part in hybridisation. Reference : ( NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 121; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c and d','All statements are correct','NEW','11-121','11-121',NULL),
(41528,'chemistry','Sp hybridization invovles mixing of:','One s and one p orbital','One s and one p orbital mixed to form two equivalent sp hybrid orbitals. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 121; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','One s and one p orbital','One s and two p orbital','One s and three p orbital','One s and one d orbital','NEW','11-121','11-121',NULL),
(41529,'chemistry','The suitable p-orbital for sp hybridization is:','p(along z- axis)','The suitable orbitals for sp hybridisation are s and pz, if the hybrid orbitals are to lie along the z-axis. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 121; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','p(along z- axis)','p(along x- axis)','p(along y- axis)','p(along zx- axis)','NEW','11-121','11-121',NULL),
(41530,'chemistry','Sp hybrid orbital has:','50% s character','Each sp hybrid orbitals has 50% s-character and 50% p-character. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 121; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','50% s character','25% s character','33.3% s character','All of these','NEW','11-121','11-121',NULL),
(41531,'chemistry','The molecule in which central atom is making two sigma bonds undergo:','sp-hybridization','Two hybrid orbitals are required for two sigma bonds which is only possible in sp hybridization. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 121; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','sp³ -hybridization','sp² -hybridization','sp-hybridization','dsp² -hybridization','NEW','11-121','11-121',NULL),
(41532,'chemistry','The central C in CO₂ involved:','sp-hybridization','Two hybrid orbitals are required for two sigma bonds which is only possible in sp hybridization in carbon dioxide c making two sigma bonds, one with each oxygen. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 121; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','sp³ -hybridization','sp² -hybridization','sp-hybridization','dsp² -hybridization','NEW','11-121','11-121',NULL),
(41533,'chemistry','Match the following:','BeCl₂-Linear geometry,sp hybrid orbitals-180°,Number of 𝛔-bonds equal to-Number of hybrid orbitals,Lone pairs undergo-Hybridization','The ground state electronic configuration of Be is 1s²2s². \n\nIn the exited state one of the 2s-electrons is promoted to vacant 2p orbital to account for its bivalency. \n\nOne 2s and one 2p-orbital gets hybridised to form two sp hybridised orbitals. \n\nThese two sp hybrid orbitals are oriented in opposite direction forming an angle of 180°. \n\nEach of the sp hybridised orbital overlaps with the 2p-orbital of chlorine axially and form two Be-Cl sigma bonds. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 121; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'3','match','BeCl₂,Linear geometry','sp hybrid orbitals,180°','Number of 𝛔-bonds equal to,Number of hybrid orbitals','Lone pairs undergo,Hybridization','NEW','11-121','11-121',NULL),
(41534,'chemistry','sp² hybridization involves mixing of:','One s and  two p','sp² hybridisation is involvement of one s and two p-orbitals in order to form three equivalent sp2 hybridised orbitals. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 121; Paragraph 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','One s and one p orbital','One s and  two p','One s and three p','One s and one d','NEW','11-121','11-121',NULL),
(41535,'chemistry','The hybridization of B in BCl₃:','sp² -hybridization','The ground state electronic configuration of B is ₅B = 1s²2s²2p¹ \n\nAccording to this B can only form one bond having one unpaired electron in p but in BCl₃ it is making 3 bonds this can be explained by excitation of electron from 2s to 2py. \n\nThe excited state configuration of B is ₅B = 1s²2s¹2p². \n\nThe uniform nature of three B-Cl bond can be explained on the base of hybridization which give equivalent orbitals so results in equal bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 122; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','sp³ -hybridization','sp² -hybridization','sp-hybridization','dsp² -hybridization','NEW','11-122','11-122',NULL),
(41536,'chemistry','sp² -hybridization has:','Trigonal planar','sp² -hybridization have trigonal planar geometry with angle of 120° between hybrid orbitals. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 122; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Linear','Trigonal planar','Tetrahedral','Square planar','NEW','11-122','11-122',NULL),
(41537,'chemistry','sp³ -hybridization involves mixing of :','One s and three p orbital','sp³ hybridisation can be explained by taking the example of CH4 molecule in which there is mixing of one s-orbital and three p-orbitals of the valence shell to form four sp3 hybrid orbital of equivalent energies and shape. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 122; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','One s and one p orbital','One s and  two p orbital','One s and three p orbital','One s and one d orbital','NEW','11-122','11-122',NULL),
(41538,'chemistry','The %age s -character in sp³  hybrid orbital is','25%','There is 25% s-character and 75% p-character in each sp3 hybrid orbital. \n\nThe four sp3 hybrid orbitals so formed are directed towards the four corners of the tetrahedron. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 122; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','50%','25%','33.30%','100%','NEW','11-122','11-122',NULL),
(41539,'chemistry','The four sp³ hybrid orbitals are directed towards the corners of:','Tetrahedron','The four sp3 hybrid orbitals so formed are directed towards the four corners of the tetrahedron. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 122; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Octahedron','Pentagon','Tetrahedron','Trigonal','NEW','11-122','11-122',NULL),
(41540,'chemistry','sp³ hybrid orbitals have:','75% p-Character','There is 25% s-character and 75% p-character in each sp3 hybrid orbital. \n\nThe four sp3 hybrid orbitals so formed are directed towards the four corners of the tetrahedron. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 122; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','75% p-Character','25% p-Character','33.3% p-Character','66.6% p-Character','NEW','11-122','11-122',NULL),
(41541,'chemistry','The structure of  CH₄ ,H₂O ,NF₃  can be explained on the base of :','sp³ -hybridization','The ground state electronic configuration of C,N, and O is ₇N = 1s²2s²2p³  ₈O = 1s²2s²2p⁴   ₆C = 1s²2s²2p². \n\nIn case of N, it has three unpaired electrons in p subshell (1e in each orbital of p subshell) and a pair of electrons in 2s, so 2p (2px,2py,2pz) and 2s mixed to give 4 sp³ hybrid orbitals one sp³ hybrid orbital have paired electrons so will not take parts in bonding, \n\nwhile other 3 overlap with 1s of H same O of water undergo sp³ hybridization with two sp³ hybrid orbital completely filled and two half filled which overlap with 1s of H. \n\nIn case of C it first undergo shifting of one electron from 2s to 2p and then mixed to give sp³ hybrid orbitals which overlap with 1s of H. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 122; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'3','mcq','sp³ -hybridization','sp² -hybridization','sp-hybridization','Square planar','NEW','11-122','11-122',NULL),
(41542,'chemistry','The hybridization state of C in C₂H₆ (ethane):','sp³ -hybridization','One of the sp³ hybrid orbital overlap with the sp³ hybrid orbital of second C and remaining 3 sp³ hybrid orbitals overlap with 1s of 3 H, in tetrahedral form. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 123; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','sp³ -hybridization','sp² -hybridization','sp-hybridization','Square planar','NEW','11-123','11-123',NULL),
(41543,'chemistry','The hybridization state of C in C₂H₄ (ethene):','sp² -hybridized','One of the sp²  hybrid orbital overlap with the sp² hybrid orbital of second C and remaining two sp² hybrid orbitals overlap with 1s of two H, the unhybridized orbital on both C which is at 90 degree to hybrid orbital undergo side way overlap and make pi-bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 123; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','sp² -hybridized','sp³ -hybridized','sp-hybridized','sp³d²','NEW','11-123','11-123',NULL),
(41544,'chemistry','The hybridization state of C in C₂H₂ (ethyne):','sp-hybridized','One of the sp hybrid orbital overlap with the sp hybrid orbital of second C and remaining 1 sp hybrid orbitals overlap with 1s of H, the 2 unhybridized orbital on both C which is at 90 degree to hybrid orbital undergo side way overlap and make 2 pi-bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 124; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','sp³ -hybridized','sp² -hybridized','sp-hybridized','sp³d²','NEW','11-123','11-123',NULL),
(41545,'chemistry','The elements of third period empty d subshell along with:','s and p','The elements present in the third period contain d orbitals in addition to s and p orbitals. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 124; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','s and p','s and d','Both of these','None of these','NEW','11-124','11-124',NULL),
(41546,'chemistry','The State of hybridization of Pt in  [Pt(Cl)₄]²⁻ ion is:','dsp²','Pt is making 4 bonds which can be possible in dsp² hybridization. \n\nPt is transition metal having d subshell so it will invole its d -subshell in bonding 4d 5s and 5p have comparable energy so get mixed and show dsp² hybridization. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 124; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'3','mcq','sp³d','dsp²','sp³d²','d²sp³','NEW','11-124','11-124',NULL),
(41547,'chemistry','The state of hybridization of P in PF₅ is:','sp³d','The ground state electronic configuration of P is ₁₅P = [Ne]3s²3p³3d⁰ (can make 3 bonds only). \n\nThe excited state electronic configuration of P ₁₅P = [Ne]3s¹3p³3d¹ ( can make 5 bonds ). \n\nIn excited state one electron from 3s transfer to empty 3d since 3s,3p,3d have almost same energy so can mixed and show sp³d hybridization, each sp³d hybrid orbital overlap with 3p orbital of Cl and form 5 bonds each sp³d hybrid orbital is directed towards the corner of trigonal  bipyramidal. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 124; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'3','mcq','sp³d','dsp²','sp³d²','d²sp³','NEW','11-125','11-125',NULL),
(41548,'chemistry','All bond angles in trigonal bipyramidal geometry are _______.','Unequal','3 bonds lie in same plane having angle 120 degree with each other. \n\nThese bonds are termed as equatorial bonds. \n\nRemaining two bonds lie above and below the equatorial bonds making an angle of 90 degree with equatorial plane, these bonds are called axial bonds. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 125; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Unequal','Equal','One forth','Half','NEW','11-125','11-125',NULL),
(41549,'chemistry','Axial bonds in trigonal bipyramidal geometry are slightly _______ than equatorial bonds.','Longer','Axial bonds feel more repulsion than equatorial bonds that\'s why they are slightly longer. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 125; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Longer','Shorter','Two half','Three quarter','NEW','11-125','11-125',NULL),
(41550,'chemistry','The State of hybridization of S in SF₆ is:','sp³d²','The ground state electronic configuration of S is ₁₆S = [Ne]3s²3p⁴3d⁰ (can make only 2 bonds). \n\nThe excited state electronic configuration of S when one electron from 3s and and from 3o transfer to empty 3d results in 6 unoaired electron ₁₆S = [Ne]3s¹3p³3d² (can make 6 bonds) 3s,3p,3d have comparable energies so get mixed and undergo sp³d² hybridization having octahedral geometry each sp³d² hybrid orbital overlap with singly occupied 3p orbital of F. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 125; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','sp³d','dsp²','sp³d²','d²sp³','NEW','11-125','11-125',NULL),
(41551,'chemistry','Match the following:','[CrF₆]³⁻-sp³d²,sp³d² - BrF₅, [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ -d²sp³ , PCl₅ -sp³d','The electronic configuration for [CrF₆]³⁻ is sp³d², sp³d² is BrF₅, [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ is d²sp³ and PCl₅ is sp³d. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 125; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'3','match','[CrF₆]³⁻ , sp³d²','sp³d² , BrF₅','[Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ ,d²sp³','PCl₅ ,sp³d','NEW','11-125','11-125',NULL),
(41552,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement regarding VBT theory:\n\na. It also does not give any reason for the difference in bond dissociation enthalpies and bond lengths in molecules.\n\nb. It also gives no idea about the shapes of polyatomic molecules.\n\nc. It is based on the knowledge of atomic orbitals, electronic configurations of elements (Units 2), the overlap criteria of atomic orbitals, the hybridization of atomic orbitals and the principles of variation and superposition.','Only c','The valence bond theory is based on the knowledge of atomic orbitals, electronic configurations of elements (Units 2), the overlap criteria of atomic orbitals, the hybridization of atomic orbitals and the principles of variation and superposition. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 117; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c','All of these','NEW','11-117','11-117',NULL),
(41553,'chemistry','Why is the shape of NH₃ molecule pyramidal?','Lone pair on N atom','The presence of a lone pair of electrons on N increases the repulsions thats why the shape of NH₃ molecule is pyramidal. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 119; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Lone pair on N atom','3 Hydrogen atoms','Neutral molecule','Electrostatic force','NEW','11-119','11-119',NULL),
(41554,'chemistry','Why is it so that CH₄ molecule has tetrahedral shape and HCH bond angles are 109.5°?','Hydrogen atoms repel equally','CH₄ molecule has tetrahedral shape and HCH bond angles are 109.5° because the hydrogen atoms repel equally, and because this geometry puts the greatest distance between all four bonded electrons pairs. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 119; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Hydrogen atoms repel equally','Carbon atom has no charge','Density of a molecule','None of these','NEW','11-119','11-119',NULL),
(41555,'chemistry','Formation of NO₂ involves:','sp² -hybridized','NO₂ involves an sp2 hybridization. \n\nThe most simple way to determine the hybridization of NO₂ is the Lewis structure and calculating the number of bonds and lone electron pairs around the nitrogen atom. \n\nThere are 2 sigma bonds and 1 lone electron pair. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 121; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','sp² -hybridized','sp³ -hybridized','sp-hybridized','sp³d²','NEW','11-121','11-121',NULL),
(41556,'chemistry','Formation of CCl₄ involves:','sp³ -hybridized','CCl₄ has a tetrahedral arrangement of the electron pairs about the carbon atom that requires sp³ hybridization. \n\nThe four sp³ hybrid orbitals from carbon are used to form the four bonds to chlorine. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 121; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','sp² -hybridized','sp³ -hybridized','sp-hybridized','sp³d²','NEW','11-121','11-121',NULL),
(41557,'chemistry','Formation of XeF₄ involves:','sp³d²','In XeF₄, Xe is the central atom and it is sp³d² hybridized having 2 lone pair on it, and have square planar geometry. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 121; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','sp² -hybridized','sp³ -hybridized','sp-hybridized','sp³d²','NEW','11-121','11-121',NULL),
(41558,'chemistry','Match the following:','sp³d-PCl₅, sp²-BF₃, sp³d²-SF₆, sp³-CH₄','The process of Hybridization in which 1s and 3p orbitals of same element involves in mixing and recasting and forms new hybrid orbital of same energy, symmetry, and definite orientation in space is called as sp3 hybridization. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 121; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'3','match','sp³d,PCl₅','sp²,BF₃','sp³d²,SF₆','sp³,CH₄','NEW','11-121','11-121',NULL),
(41559,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: In ethane molecule both the carbon atoms assume sp hybrid state.\n\nR: One of the four sp3 hybrid orbitals of carbon atom overlaps axially with similar orbitals of other atom to form sp3-sp3 sigma bond.','Assertion is false but the reason is true','In ethane molecule both the carbon atoms assume sp3 hybrid state. \n\nOne of the four sp3 hybrid orbitals of carbon atom overlaps axially with similar orbitals of other atom to form sp3-sp3 sigma bond while the other three hybrid orbitals of each carbon atom are used in forming sp3–s sigma bonds with hydrogen atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 123; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-123','11-123',NULL),
(41560,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: In the formation of ethene molecule, one of the sp2 hybrid orbitals of carbon atom overlaps axially with sp2 hybridised orbital of another carbon atom to form C–C sigma bond.\n\nR: While the other two sp2 hybrid orbitals of each carbon atom are used for making sp2–s sigma bond with two hydrogen atoms.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','In the formation of ethene molecule, one of the sp2 hybrid orbitals of carbon atom overlaps axially with sp2 hybridised orbital of another carbon atom to form C–C sigma bond. \n\nWhile the other two sp2 hybrid orbitals of each carbon atom are used for making sp2–s sigma bond with two hydrogen atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 123; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-123','11-123',NULL),
(41561,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: In the formation of ethyne molecule, both the carbon atoms undergo sp-hybridisation having two unhybridised orbital.\n\nR: Each of the two unhybridised p orbitals of both the carbon atoms overlaps sidewise to form two s bonds between the carbon atoms.','Assertion is true but the reason is false','In the formation of ethyne molecule, both the carbon atoms undergo sp-hybridisation having two unhybridised orbital i.e., 2py and 2px. \n\nEach of the two unhybridised p orbitals of both the carbon atoms overlaps sidewise to form two p bonds between the carbon atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 124; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-124','11-124',NULL),
(41562,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statement:\n\na. The elements present in the third period contain d orbitals in addition to s and p orbitals. \n\nb. The energy of the 3d orbitals are comparable to the energy of the 3s and 3p orbitals.\n\nc. The energy of 3d orbitals are also comparable to those of 4s and 4p orbitals. \n\nd. As a consequence the hybridisation involving either 3s, 3p and 3d or 3d, 4s and 4p is possible.\n\ne. The difference in energies of 3p and 4s orbitals is significant, no hybridisation involving 3p, 3d and 4s orbitals is possible.','None of these','The elements present in the third period contain d orbitals in addition to s and p orbitals. \n\nThe energy of the 3d orbitals are comparable to the energy of the 3s and 3p orbitals. \n\nThe energy of 3d orbitals are also comparable to those of 4s and 4p orbitals. \n\nAs a consequence the hybridisation involving either 3s, 3p and 3d or 3d, 4s and 4p is possible. \n\nHowever, since the difference in energies of 3p and 4s orbitals is significant, no hybridisation involving 3p, 3d and 4s orbitals is possible.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 124; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'3','mcq','Only b and c','Only b and d','Only c and e','None of these','NEW','11-124','11-124',NULL),
(41563,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: In SF₆ the central sulphur atom has the ground state outer electronic configuration 3s23p4.\n \nR: In the exited state the available six orbitals i.e., one s, three p and two d are singly occupied by electrons.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','In SF₆ the central sulphur atom has the ground state outer electronic configuration 3s23p4. \n\nIn the exited state the available six orbitals i.e., one s, three p and two d are singly occupied by electrons.   \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 124; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-124','11-124',NULL),
(41564,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statement regarding Formation of SF₆:\n\na. In SF₆ the central sulphur atom has the ground state outer electronic configuration 3s2 3p4.\n\nb. These six sp3d2 hybrid orbitals overlap with singly occupied orbitals of fluorine atoms to form six S–F sigma bonds.\n\nc. SF₆ molecule has a regular tetraahedral geometry.\n\nd.  These orbitals hybridise to form six new sp3d2 hybrid orbitals, which are projected towards the six corners of a regular octahedron in SF6.','Only c','In SF₆ the central sulphur atom has the ground state outer electronic configuration 3s23p4. \n\nIn the exited state the available six orbitals i.e., one s, three p and two d are singly occupied by electrons. \n\nThese orbitals hybridise to form six new sp3d2 hybrid orbitals, which are projected towards the six corners of a regular octahedron in SF6. \n\nThese six sp3d2 hybrid orbitals overlap with singly occupied orbitals of fluorine atoms to form six S–F sigma bonds. \n\nThus SF6 molecule has a regular octahedral geometry.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 124; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VB theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only  b and c','Only c','Only c and d','NEW','11-124','11-124',NULL),
(41601,'chemistry','Molecular orbital theory was developed by:','F.Hund and R.S Mulliken','Molecular orbital (MO) theory was developed by F. Hund and R.S. Mulliken in 1932. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 125; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Nylholm and goldsteine','Guldberg and p-Waage','Bronsted and lowry','F.Hund and R.S Mulliken','NEW','11-125','11-125',NULL),
(41602,'chemistry','Which one is feature of MO theory:\n\na. The electrons in a molecule are present in the various MO as the electrons of atoms are present in AO\n\nb. The atomic orbitals (AO) of compareable energies and proper symmetry combine to form molecular orbitals (MO)\n\nc. The number of Molecular orbitals formed are equal to number of atomic orbitals combine \n\nd. The molecular orbitals like atomic orbitals are filled according to aufbau, hund and pauli exclusion principle','All statements are true','The electrons in a molecule are present in the various MO as the electrons of atoms are present in AO. \n\nThe atomic orbitals (AO) of compareable energies and proper symmetry combine to form molecular orbitals (MO). \n\nThe number of Molecular orbitals formed are equal to number of atomic orbitals combine. \n\nThe molecular orbitals like atomic orbitals are filled according to aufbau, hund and pauli exclusion principle.\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 126; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c and d','All statements are true','NEW','11-126','11-126',NULL),
(41603,'chemistry','Match the following:','Molecular orbital-Polycentric,Lower energy MO-Bonding molecular orbitals,Higher energy MO-Antibonding MO,The place of finiding an electron in a molecule-Molecular orbital','As shown in diagram\n\n Reference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 126; paragraph no.1) diagram(ne0414)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'3','match','Molecular orbital,Polycentric','Lower energy MO,Bonding molecular  orbitals','Higher energy MO,Antibonding MO','The place of finiding an electron in a molecule,Molecular orbital','NEW','11-126','11-126',NULL),
(41604,'chemistry','The amplitude of electron wave in an atomic orbital is represented by:','Ѱ','According to wave mechanics, the atomic orbitals can be expressed by wave functions (ψ ’s) which represent the amplitude of the electron waves. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 126; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Ѱ','φ','ἦ','None of these','NEW','11-126','11-126',NULL),
(41605,'chemistry','Molecular orbitals are formed by:','Linear combinations of atomic orbitals','Atomic orbitals linearly combine to form MO  \n\nѰ (MO)  =  Ѱ (A) +  Ѱ (B) (bonding molecular orbitals)\n\nѰ (MO)  =  Ѱ (A) -  Ѱ (B)  (Anti- bonding molecular orbitals)\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 126; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Parallel combinations of atomic orbitals','Linear combinations of atomic orbitals','Linear combinations of  molecular orbitals','Parallel combinations of molecular orbitals','NEW','11-126','11-126',NULL),
(41606,'chemistry','Ѱ (A) + Ѱ(B) of two bonded atoms A-B leads to:','Bonding MO (𝞂)','Atomic orbitals linearly combine to form MO  Ѱ (𝞂)  =  Ѱ (A) +  Ѱ (B) (bonding molecular orbitals). \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 126; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Bonding MO (𝞂)','Anti Bonding MO (𝞂)','Bonding MO (𝞂*)','Anti-Bonding MO (𝞂*)','NEW','11-126','11-126',NULL),
(41607,'chemistry','Ѱ (A) - Ѱ(B) of two bonded atoms A-B leads to:','Anti-Bonding MO (𝞂*)','Atomic orbitals linearly combine to form MO   Ѱ (𝞂*)  =  Ѱ (A) -  Ѱ (B) (Anti-bonding molecular orbitals). \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 126; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Bonding MO (𝞂)','Anti Bonding MO (𝞂)','Bonding MO (𝞂*)','Anti-Bonding MO (𝞂*)','NEW','11-126','11-126',NULL),
(41608,'chemistry','Which statement is true about Bonding molecular orbitals:\n\na. They are formed by the Constructive interference of Atomic orbitals\n\nb. Electron density in bonding molecular orbitals lie between two bonded nuclei\n\nc. Electron in Bonding molecular orbital tends to hold the two nuclei together and stabilize the molecule\n \nd. Bonding molecular orbitals always possess lower energy','All statements are true','Bonding molecular orbitals are formed by the Constructive interference of Atomic orbitals. \n\nElectron density in bonding molecular orbitals lie between two bonded nuclei. \n\nElectron in Bonding molecular orbital tends to hold the two nuclei together and stabilize the molecule. \n\nBonding molecular orbitals always possess lower energy. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 127; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c and d','All statements are true','NEW','11-127','11-127',NULL),
(41609,'chemistry','Which statement is true about Anti-Bonding molecular orbitals:\n\na. They are formed by the destructive interference of Atomic orbitals\n\nb. Electron density in anti-bonding molecular orbitals lie away from nuclei\n\nc. Electron in anti-bonding molecular orbital tends to hold the two nuclei together\n\nd. Anti-bonding molecular orbitals always possess higher energy than parent Atomic orbitals','a,b,d only','Bonding molecular orbitals are formed by the Constructive interference of Atomic orbitals while Antibinding Molecular Orbitals are formed by the destructive interference of atomic orbitals. They have higher energy and the electron density is low around the nuclei. Electrons placed in anti- bonding molecular orbital tend to separate the two nuclei and destabilise a molecule. \nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 127; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b,c only','a,b,d only','b,c,d only','All statements are true','NEW','11-127','11-127',NULL),
(41610,'chemistry','1s orbital can only combine with:','1s','The combining atomic orbitals must have the same or nearly the same E. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 127; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'1','mcq','1s','2s','1p','2p','NEW','11-127','11-127',NULL),
(41611,'chemistry','2pₓ can only combine with_______ of other atom.','2px','The combining atomic orbitals must have the same symmetry: 2px -2px, 2py-2py, 2pz-2pz. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 127; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'1','mcq','2px','2py','2pz','2s','NEW','11-127','11-127',NULL),
(41612,'chemistry','The combining atomic orbitals must overlap to the ______ extent.','Maximum','The combining atomic orbitals must have the same symmetry and same size to do maximum overlap thus make stronger bond as maximum e density lie between nuclei 2px -2p, 2py-2p, 2pz-2pz. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 127; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Maximum','Minimum','Parallel','Slightest','NEW','11-127','11-127',NULL),
(41613,'chemistry','𝞂  molecular orbitals are _______ around the bond axis.','Symmetrical','The linear combination of orbitals centered on 2 nuclei produces two molecular orbitals which are symmetrical around the bond axis designated as σ and σ*. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 127; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Symmetrical','Asymmetrical','Disproportion','Altered','NEW','11-127','11-127',NULL),
(41614,'chemistry','𝛑 molecular orbitals are ______ around the bond axis.','Asymmetrical','Molecular orbitals obtained by paralell overlap are designated as 𝛑  and 𝛑* orbitals as they are not symmetrical due to having lobe above or below the plane(bond axis). \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 127; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Symmetrical','Asymmetrical','Consonant','Proportion','NEW','11-127','11-127',NULL),
(41615,'chemistry','The Antibonding molecular orbitals of second period on the base of energy:','σ*2s<σ*2p𝓏>𝛑* 2pᵧ<𝛑* 2pₓ','In the same manner, the 2s and 2p atomic orbitals (eight atomic orbitals on two atoms) give rise to the following eight molecular orbitals: \n\n\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 129; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'3','mcq','σ*2s<σ*2p𝓏<𝛑* 2pᵧ<𝛑* 2pₓ','σ*2s>σ*2p𝓏>𝛑* 2pᵧ>𝛑* 2pₓ','σ*2s<σ*2p𝓏>𝛑* 2pᵧ<𝛑* 2pₓ','σ*2s>σ*2p𝓏<𝛑* 2pᵧ<𝛑* 2pₓ','NEW','11-129','11-129',NULL),
(41616,'chemistry','The bonding molecular orbitals of second period on the base of energy:','σ2s<σ2p𝓏<𝛑2pᵧ<𝛑2pₓ','In the same manner, the 2s and 2p atomic orbitals (eight atomic orbitals on two atoms) give rise to the following eight molecular orbitals: \n\nBonding molecular orbitals σ2s<σ2p𝓏<𝛑2pᵧ<𝛑2pₓ \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 128; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'3','mcq','σ2s<σ2p𝓏<𝛑2pᵧ<𝛑2pₓ','σ2s>σ2p𝓏>𝛑2pᵧ>𝛑2pₓ','σ2s<σ2p𝓏>𝛑2pᵧ<𝛑2pₓ','σ2s<σ2p𝓏>𝛑2pᵧ>𝛑2pₓ','NEW','11-128','11-128',NULL),
(41617,'chemistry','The energy sequence of MO given is for\n\nσ1s<σ*1s<σ2s<σ*2s<σ2p𝓏<𝛑2pₓ=𝛑2pᵧ<𝛑*2pₓ=𝛑*2pᵧ<σ*2p𝓏','O₂ and F₂','The increasing order of energies of various molecular orbitals for O2 and F2 is given below: \n\nσ1s<σ*1s<σ2s<σ*2s<σ2p𝓏<𝛑2pₓ=𝛑2pᵧ<𝛑*2pₓ=𝛑*2pᵧ<σ*2p𝓏  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 129; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'3','mcq','O₂ and F₂','N₂ and F₂','C₂ and B₂','C₂ and Be₂','NEW','11-129','11-129',NULL),
(41618,'chemistry','The energy sequence of MO given is for second period elements except\n\nσ1s<σ*1s<σ2s<σ*2s<𝛑2pₓ=𝛑2pᵧ<σ2p𝓏<𝛑*2pₓ=𝛑*2pᵧ<σ*2p𝓏','O₂, F₂ and Ne','The energy sequence of MO given is for second period elements except O₂, F₂ and Ne: \n\nσ1s<σ*1s<σ2s<σ*2s<𝛑2pₓ=𝛑2pᵧ<σ2p𝓏<𝛑*2pₓ=𝛑*2pᵧ<σ*2p𝓏 \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 129; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'3','mcq','N₂ and F₂','O₂, F₂ and Ne','C₂ and B₂','C₂ and Be₂','NEW','11-129','11-129',NULL),
(41619,'chemistry','Choose the correct answer regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nA: The molecule is stable if Nᵦ is greater than Nₐ  Nᵦ = bonding electron  Nₐ = antibonding electrons\n\nB: More bonding orbitals are occupied so the bonding influence is stronger and molecule is stable','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','The stability of the molecule can be restated in terms of bond order as follows: \n\nA positive bond order (i.e., Nb > Na) means a stable molecule while a negative (i.e., Nb <Na) or zero (i.e., Nb = Na) bond order means an unstable molecule. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 129; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-129','11-129',NULL),
(41620,'chemistry','Choose the correct answer regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nA: The molecule is unstable if Nᵦ is lesser than Nₐ     Nᵦ = bonding electrons   Nₐ = antibonding electrons\n\nB: Antibonding influence is stronger and stable molecule is unstable','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','The stability of the molecule can be restated in terms of bond order as follows: \n\nA positive bond order (i.e., Nb > Na) means a stable molecule while a negative (i.e., Nb <Na) or zero (i.e., Nb = Na) bond order means an unstable molecule. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 129; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-129','11-129',NULL),
(41621,'chemistry','One half the difference between the number of electrons in bonding and the antibonding orbitals is _____:','Bond order','Bond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ ) \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 129; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Bond order','Bond length','Bond energy','None of these','NEW','11-129','11-129',NULL),
(41622,'chemistry','When Nᵦ> Nₐ the bond order will be:','Positive','Bond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ ) \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 129; paragraph no.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Negative','Positive','Zero','None of these','NEW','11-129','11-129',NULL),
(41623,'chemistry','When Nᵦ< Nₐ the bond order will be:','Negative','Bond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ ) \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 129; paragraph no.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Negative','Positive','Zero','None of these','NEW','11-129','11-129',NULL),
(41624,'chemistry','When Nᵦ= Nₐ the bond order will be:','Zero','Bond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ ) \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 129; paragraph no.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Negative','Positive','Zero','None of these','NEW','11-129','11-129',NULL),
(41625,'chemistry','Match the following:','Bond order 1-Single bond,Bond order 2-Double bond,Bond order 3-Triple bond,Bond order 0-No bond','Integral bond order values of 1, 2 or 3 correspond to single, double or triple bonds respectively as studied in the classical concept. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 129; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'3','match','Bond order 1,Single bond','Bond order 2,Double bond','Bond order 3,Triple bond','Bond order 0,No bond','NEW','11-129','11-129',NULL),
(41626,'chemistry','Bond order is directly linked to:','All of these','Bond order (b.o.) is  one half the difference between the number of electrons present in the bonding and the antibonding orbitals i.e.\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 129; paragraph no.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Bond length','Bond enthalpy','Bond strength','All of these','NEW','11-129','11-129',NULL),
(41627,'chemistry','If all the molecular orbitals are doubly occupied then they are:','Diamagnetic','If all the molecular orbitals in a molecule are doubly occupied, the substance is diamagnetic (repelled by magnetic field). \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 129; paragraph no.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Diamagnetic','Paramagnetic','Ferromagnetic','All of these','NEW','11-129','11-129',NULL),
(41628,'chemistry','If any of the molecular orbitals is singly occupied then they are','Paramagnetic','If one or more molecular orbitals are singly occupied it is paramagnetic (attracted by magnetic field), e.g., O₂ molecule\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 129; paragraph no.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Diamagnetic','Paramagnetic','Ferromagnetic','All of these','NEW','11-129','11-129',NULL),
(41629,'chemistry','The electronic Configuration of H₂ molecule:','(σ1s²)','It is formed by the combination of two H atoms since each H has one e in 1s orbital so on combining it will have 2 electrons in (σ1s) molecular  orbital. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 129; paragraph no.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','(σ2s²)','(σ1s²)','(σ*1s²)','(σ1s¹)','NEW','11-129','11-129',NULL),
(41630,'chemistry','The bond order of H₂ molecule:','One','Bond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ ) there are 2 electrons in bonding molecular orbital and zero electron in antibonding molecular orbital so 2-0 =2/2 = 1. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 129; paragraph no.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','One','Two','Three','Four','NEW','11-129','11-129',NULL),
(41631,'chemistry','H₂ molecule is:','Diamagnetic','H₂ = (σ1s²),(σ*1s) since no unpaired electron present so it is diamagnetic. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Paramagnetic','Diamagnetic','Ferromagnetic','None of these','NEW','11-130','11-130',NULL),
(41632,'chemistry','The electronic Configuration of He₂:','(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²','The electronic configuration of helium atom is 1s². Each helium atom contains 2 electrons, therefore, in He₂ molecule there would be 4 electrons.\n\nThese electrons will be accommodated in σ1sand σ*1s molecular orbitals leading to electronic configuration: (σ1s)²(σ*1s)²  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²','(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²','(σ1s)²(σ*2s)²','(σ1s)²(σ1s)²','NEW','11-130','11-130',NULL),
(41633,'chemistry','The bond order of He₂:','Zero','Bond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )= 1/2(2-2) = zero. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','One','Two','Zero','Three','NEW','11-130','11-130',NULL),
(41634,'chemistry','He₂ molecule is:','Diamagnetic','He₂ = (σ1s)²(σ*1s) since no unpaired electron present so it is diamagnetic. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Metamagnetic','Paramagnetic','Ferromagnetic','Diamagnetic','NEW','11-130','11-130',NULL),
(41635,'chemistry','The bond order of Be₂ molecule is:','Zero','Be₂ = (σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)² \n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2(Nᵦ-Nₐ) = 1/2(4-4) = zero bond order showing that this molecule doesn\'t exist. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','One','Two','Zero','Three','NEW','11-130','11-130',NULL),
(41636,'chemistry','The electronic configuration of Li₂ molecule:','(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²','The electronic configuration of Li= 1s²2s¹  \n\nso 2 Li atoms on combining will have 6 electrons which are filled in MO (σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)². \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²','(σ1s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2s)²','(σ2s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²','(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ*2s)','NEW','11-130','11-130',NULL),
(41637,'chemistry','The electronic Configuration of C₂:','(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pᵧ²=𝛑2pₓ²)','The  electronic Configuration of C is 1s²2s²2p² 6 electrons so two carbon atoms on combining will have 12 electrons which are filled (σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pᵧ²=𝛑2pₓ²). \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)(𝛑2pᵧ²=𝛑2pₓ²)','(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pᵧ²=𝛑2pₓ²)','(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ1s)(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pᵧ²=𝛑2pₓ²)','(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pᵧ¹=𝛑2pₓ¹)','NEW','11-130','11-130',NULL),
(41638,'chemistry','The bond order of C₂  molecule is:','Two','C₂=  (σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pᵧ²=𝛑2pₓ²) \n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2(Nᵦ-Nₐ) = 1/2(8-4) = 2 bond order showing that this molecule have double bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130; paragraph no.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','One','Two','Zero','Three','NEW','11-130','11-130',NULL),
(41639,'chemistry','The molecule of C₂ is:','Diamagnetic','The electronic configuration of C₂ = (σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pᵧ²=𝛑2pₓ²) since no unpaired electrons present so it is diamagnetic. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130; paragraph no.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Diamagnetic','Paramagnetic','Ferromagnetic','None of these','NEW','11-130','11-130',NULL),
(41640,'chemistry','The electronic configuration of O₂ is:','(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pᵧ²=𝛑2pₓ²)(𝛑*2pᵧ¹=𝛑*2pₓ¹)','The electronic configuration of O = 1s²2s²2p⁴ total 8 electrons so two O atoms on combining will have 16 electrons which are fille as \n\nO₂ = (σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pᵧ²=𝛑2pₓ² (𝛑*2pᵧ¹=𝛑*2pₓ¹). \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pᵧ²=𝛑2pₓ²)','(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pᵧ²=𝛑2pₓ²)(𝛑*2pᵧ¹=𝛑*2pₓ¹)','(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)(𝛑2pᵧ²=𝛑2pₓ²)','(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)(𝛑2pᵧ²=𝛑2pₓ²)','NEW','11-130','11-130',NULL),
(41641,'chemistry','The bond order of O₂ is:','Two','O₂ = (σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pᵧ²=𝛑2pₓ²)(𝛑*2pᵧ¹=𝛑*2pₓ¹) Bond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ ) = 1/2(10-6) = 2 bond order showing that this molecule have double bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','One','Two','Zero','Three','NEW','11-130','11-130',NULL),
(41642,'chemistry','The molecule of O₂ is:','Paramagnetic','O₂ = (σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pᵧ²=𝛑2pₓ²)(𝛑*2pᵧ¹=𝛑*2pₓ¹) Since it has unpaired electrons in antiboding MO so it is paramagnetic. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Paramagnetic','Diamagnetic','Ferromagnetic','None of these','NEW','11-130','11-130',NULL),
(41643,'chemistry','The molecule of B₂ is:','Paramagnetic','B₂ = KK(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²((𝛑2pᵧ¹=𝛑2pₓ¹) since it is having unpaired electron so it is paramagnetic. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 131; fig.4.21)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Paramagnetic','Diamagnetic','Ferromagnetic','None of these','NEW','11-131','11-131',NULL),
(41644,'chemistry','The molecule of F₂ is:','Diamagnetic','F₂ = (σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pᵧ²=𝛑2pₓ²)(𝛑*2pᵧ²=𝛑*2pₓ²) since it doesn\'t have unpaired electron so it is diamagnetic. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 131; fig.4.21)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Paramagnetic','Diamagnetic','Ferromagnetic','None of these','NEW','11-131','11-131',NULL),
(41645,'chemistry','The molecule of Ne₂ is:','Diamagnetic','Ne₂ = (σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pᵧ²=𝛑2pₓ²)(𝛑*2pᵧ²=𝛑*2pₓ²)(σ2p𝓏)² since it doesn\'t have unpaired electron so it is diamagnetic. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 131; fig.4.21)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Paramagnetic','Diamagnetic','Ferromagnetic','None of these','NEW','11-131','11-131',NULL),
(41646,'chemistry','Molecular Orbital Theory is another theory to explain ___________ using orbitals','Chemical bonding','Molecular Orbital Theory is another theory to explain chemical bonding using orbitals. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 128; paragraph no.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Molecular bonding','Chemical bonding','Electronic configuration','Geometry','NEW','11-128','11-128',NULL),
(41647,'chemistry','The bond order of which of the following is always zero:','He','The bond order of Helium is always zero. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','O₂','F₂','C₂','He','NEW','11-128','11-128',NULL),
(41648,'chemistry','The bond order of F₂ is:','One','F₂ molecule is σ(2s)2, σ*(2s)2, σ(2pz)2, π(2px)2= π(2py)2, π*(2px)2= π*(2py)2 Bond Order of F2 molecule is 1. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','One','Two','Zero','Three','NEW','11-128','11-128',NULL),
(260225,'chemistry','Write the correct IUPAC name of the compound CH₃CH(OCH₃)CHO?','2-methoxypropanal','The IUPAC names of open chain aliphatic aldehydes and ketones are derived from the names of the corresponding alkanes by replacing the ending –e with –al and –one respectively. \n\nIn case of aldehydes the longest carbon chain is numbered starting from the carbon of the aldehyde group while in case of ketones the numbering begins from the end nearer to the carbonyl group.\n\n Reference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-359 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'2','mcq','2-methoxypropanal','2-methylpropanal','2-methoxypropanol','1-methoxypropanal','NEW','12-359','12-229',NULL),
(260226,'chemistry','(CH₃)₂C=CHCOCH₃ Write its common name?','Mesityl oxide','Some ketones have historical common names, the simplest dimethyl ketone is called acetone. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-359 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'2','mcq','Pthaldehyde','Acrolein','Mesityl oxide','Isomesityl oxide','NEW','12-359','12-229',NULL),
(41649,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: From the electronic configuration of Li₂ molecule it is clear that there are four electrons present in bonding molecular orbitals and two electrons present in antibonding molecular orbitals.\n \nR: Its bond order, therefore, is ½ (4 – 2) = 1. It means that Li₂ molecule is stable and since it has no unpaired electrons it should be diamagnetic.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','From the electronic configuration of Li₂ molecule it is clear that there are four electrons present in bonding molecular orbitals and two electrons present in antibonding molecular orbitals. Its bond order, therefore, is ½ (4 – 2) = 1. It means that Li₂ molecule is stable and since it has no unpaired electrons it should be diamagnetic. Indeed diamagnetic Li₂ molecules are known to exist in the vapour phase. \nReference : ( NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-128','11-128',NULL),
(41650,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: From the electronic configuration of O₂ molecule it is clear that ten electrons are present in bonding molecular orbitals and six electrons are present in antibonding molecular orbitals.\n\nR: Its bond order, therefore, is ½ (4 – 2) = 1. It means that O₂ molecule is stable and since it has no unpaired electrons it should be diamagnetic.','Assertion is true but the reason is false','From the electronic configuration of O₂ molecule it is clear that ten electrons are present in bonding molecular orbitals and six electrons are present in antibonding molecular orbitals. Bond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ ) = 1/2(10-6) = 2 bond order showing that this molecule have double bond. Since it has unpaired electrons in antiboding MO so it is paramagnetic. Reference : ( NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130; paragraph no.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> MO theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-128','11-128',NULL),
(41701,'chemistry','The physical bond formed between the highly electronegative atom and Hydrogen is called as:','H-Bond','When two bonded atoms bind through through a covalent bond and one of them has high electronegative value and other is Hydrogen then the bond electron density is not distributed equally it lies more towards highly electronegative atom in this poles generated in the molecule and Hydrogen pole of molecules get attached to negative pole of other molecule via H-bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 131; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> H-bond',NULL,'1','mcq','H-Bond','X-bond','Covalent bond','Ionic bond','NEW','11-131','11-131',NULL),
(41702,'chemistry','Which of the following can\'t make a H-bond:','C','Thus, hydrogen bond can be defined as the attractive force which binds hydrogen atom of one molecule with the electronegative atom (F, O or N) of another molecule. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 131; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> H-bond',NULL,'1','mcq','C','O','F','N','NEW','11-131','11-131',NULL),
(41703,'chemistry','The molecule of HF has:','Intermolecular H-bond','In HF molecule, the hydrogen bond exists between hydrogen atom of one molecule and fluorine atom of another molecule\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 132; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> H-bond',NULL,'1','mcq','Intermolecular H-bond','Intra-molecular H-bond','Both a and b','None of these','NEW','11-132','11-132',NULL),
(41704,'chemistry','o-nitrophenol has:','Intra-molecular H-bond','Intramolecular hydrogen bond is formed when hydrogen atom is in between the two highly electronegative (F, O, N) atoms present within the same molecule. \n\nFor example, in o-nitrophenol the hydrogen is in between the two oxygen atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 132; paragraph no.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> H-bond',NULL,'1','mcq','Intra-molecular H-bond','Intermolecular H-bond','Both a and b','None of these','NEW','11-132','11-132',NULL),
(41705,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: This partially positively charged hydrogen atom forms a bond with the other more electronegative atom.\n\nR: When Nitrogen, oxygen and fluorine are attached to a hydrogen atom to form covalent bond, the electrons of the covalent bond are shifted towards the more electronegative atom.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Nitrogen, oxygen and fluorine are the higly electronegative elements. \n\nWhen they are attached to a hydrogen atom to form covalent bond, the electrons of the covalent bond are shifted towards the more electronegative atom. \n\nThis partially positively charged hydrogen atom forms a bond with the other more electronegative atom. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 131; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> H-bond',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-131','11-131',NULL),
(41706,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Hydrogen bond is stronger than the covalent bond.\n\nR: In HF molecule, the hydrogen bond exists between hydrogen atom of one molecule and fluorine atom of another molecule.','Assertion is false but the reason is true','The partially positively charged hydrogen atom forms a bond with the other more electronegative atom, this bond is known as hydrogen bond and is weaker than the covalent bond. \n\nFor example, in HF molecule, the hydrogen bond exists between hydrogen atom of one molecule and fluorine atom of another molecule. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 131; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> H-bond',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Assertion is false but the reason is true','NEW','11-131','11-131',NULL),
(260227,'chemistry','What is the common name of dimethyl ketone?','Acetone','Some ketones have historical common names, the simplest dimethyl ketone is called acetone. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-359 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'1','mcq','Ether','Acetone','Acetophenone','Benzophenone','NEW','12-359','12-229',NULL),
(41707,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement:\n\na. Hydrogen bonding is the attractive force which binds hydrogen atom of one molecule with the electronegative atom (F, O or N) of another molecule.\n\nb. Intramolecular hydrogen bond is formed between two different molecules of the same or different compounds.\n\nc. Intermolecular hydrogen bond is formed when hydrogen atom is in between the two highly electronegative (F, O, N) atoms present within the same molecule.','Only a','Hydrogen bonding is the attractive force which binds hydrogen atom of one molecule with the electronegative atom (F, O or N) of another molecule. \n\nIntermolecular hydrogen bond is formed between two different molecules of the same or different compounds. \n\nIntramolecular hydrogen bond is formed when hydrogen atom is in between the two highly electronegative (F, O, N) atoms present within the same molecule. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 132; paragraph no.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> H-bond',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only a and b','Only a and c','all of these','NEW','11-132','11-132',NULL),
(41708,'chemistry','o-nitrophenol\'s hydrogen structured as:','O-H-O','In o-nitrophenol the hydrogen is in between the two oxygen atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 132; paragraph no.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> H-bond',NULL,'1','mcq','O-H-C','O-H-O','O=H+','C=H','NEW','11-132','11-132',NULL),
(41709,'chemistry','Arrange the bonds in order of increasing ionic character in the molecules:','N₂ < SO₂ < ClF₃ < K₂O','On this basis the order of increasing ionic character in the given molecules is N₂ < SO₂ < ClF₃ < K₂O < LiF. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 134; Exercise no.4.19)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exercise',NULL,'3','order','1,N₂','2,SO₂','3,ClF₃','4,K₂O','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(41710,'chemistry','Is there any change in the hybridisation of B atoms as a result of the following reaction?  BF3 + NH3 → F3B.NH','sp3 Hybridization','After the reaction, hybridization of B changes to sp3 but that of N remains unchanged. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 134; Exercise no.4.26)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','Remains unchanged','sp3 Hybridization','sp2 Hybridization','sp Hybridization','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(41711,'chemistry','Is there any change in the hybridisation of N atoms as a result of the following reaction?  BF3 + NH3 → F3B.NH','Remains unchanged','After the reaction, hybridization of B changes to sp3 but that of N remains unchanged. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 134; Exercise no.4.26)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','Remains unchanged','sp3 Hybridization','sp2 Hybridization','sp Hybridization','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(41712,'chemistry','Describe the change in hybridisation (if any) of the Al atom in the following reaction: AlCl3 + Cl- →  AlCl4-','sp3 Hybridization','Here in the given equation AlCl3, Al is shifting its hybridization state from sp2 to sp3. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 134; Exercise no.4.25)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','Remains unchanged','sp3 Hybridization','sp2 Hybridization','sp Hybridization','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(41713,'chemistry','What is the total number of sigma and pi bonds in the C₂H₂ molecules?','Three sigma and two pi-bonds','There are three sigma and two pi-bonds in C₂H₂. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 135; Exercise no.4.28)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','One sigma and two pi-bonds','Three sigma and two pi-bonds','5 sigma and one pi bond','Two sigma and two pi-bonds','NEW','11-103','11-103',NULL),
(41714,'chemistry','What is the total number of sigma and pi bonds in the C₂H₄ molecules?','5 sigma and one pi bond','There are 5 sigma and one pi bond in C₂H₄ molecule.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 135; Exercise no.4.28)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','One sigma and two pi-bonds','Three sigma and two pi-bonds','5 sigma and one pi bond','Two sigma and two pi-bonds','NEW','11-103','11-103',NULL),
(41715,'chemistry','Which out of NH₃ and NF₃ has higher dipole moment?','NH₃','The dipole moment of NH₃ is higher in comparison to NF₃. The fluorine is more electronegative than N and pulls the electrons. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 134; Exercise no.4.23)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','NH₃','NF₃','Both are equal','Dipole moment is not applicable','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41801,'chemistry','Arrange the bonds in order of increasing ionic character in the molecules:','N₂ < SO₂ < ClF₃ < K₂O','On this basis the order of increasing ionic character in the given molecules is N₂ < SO₂ < ClF₃ < K₂O < LiF. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 134; Exercise no.4.19)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exercise',NULL,'3','order','1,N₂','2,SO₂','3,ClF₃','4,K₂O','NEW','11-134','11-134',NULL),
(41802,'chemistry','Is there any change in the hybridisation of B atoms as a result of the following reaction?  BF3 + NH3 → F3B.NH','sp3 Hybridization','After the reaction, hybridization of B changes to sp3 but that of N remains unchanged. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 134; Exercise no.4.26)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','Remains unchanged','sp3 Hybridization','sp2 Hybridization','sp Hybridization','NEW','11-134','11-134',NULL),
(41803,'chemistry','Is there any change in the hybridisation of N atoms as a result of the following reaction?  BF3 + NH3 → F3B.NH','Remains unchanged','After the reaction, hybridization of B changes to sp3 but that of N remains unchanged. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 134; Exercise no.4.26)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','Remains unchanged','sp3 Hybridization','sp2 Hybridization','sp Hybridization','NEW','11-134','11-134',NULL),
(41804,'chemistry','Describe the change in hybridisation (if any) of the Al atom in the following reaction: AlCl3 + Cl- →  AlCl4-','sp3 Hybridization','Here in the given equation AlCl3, Al is shifting its hybridization state from sp2 to sp3. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 134; Exercise no.4.25)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','Remains unchanged','sp3 Hybridization','sp2 Hybridization','sp Hybridization','NEW','11-134','11-134',NULL),
(41805,'chemistry','What is the total number of sigma and pi bonds in the C₂H₂ molecules?','Three sigma and two pi-bonds','There are three sigma and two pi-bonds in C₂H₂. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 135; Exercise no.4.28)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','One sigma and two pi-bonds','Three sigma and two pi-bonds','5 sigma and one pi bond','Two sigma and two pi-bonds','NEW','11-135','11-135',NULL),
(41809,'chemistry','What is the total number of sigma and pi bonds in the C₂H₄ molecules?','5 sigma and one pi bond','There are 5 sigma and one pi bond in C₂H₄ molecule.  \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 135; Exercise no.4.28)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','One sigma and two pi-bonds','Three sigma and two pi-bonds','5 sigma and one pi bond','Two sigma and two pi-bonds','NEW','11-135','11-135',NULL),
(41810,'chemistry','Which out of NH₃ and NF₃ has higher dipole moment?','NH₃','The dipole moment of NH₃ is higher in comparison to NF₃. The fluorine is more electronegative than N and pulls the electrons. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 134; Exercise no.4.23)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','NH₃','NF₃','Both are equal','Dipole moment is not applicable','NEW','11-134','11-134',NULL),
(41811,'chemistry','Which of the following species contains equal number of σ and  𝞹-bonds?','XeO₄','(CN)₂ 3σ 4𝞹, CH₂(CN)₂ 6σ 4𝞹, HCO₃⁻ 4σ 1𝞹, XeO₄ 4σ 4𝞹. (NEET 2015)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','(CN)₂','(CH)₂(CN)₂','HCO₃ ⁻','XeO₄','NEW','11-115','11-115',NULL),
(41812,'chemistry','Which one of the following molecules contains no 𝞹- bond?','H₂O','O=S=O  2𝞹 bond\n\nO=C=O  2𝞹 bond\n\nO=N-O⁻ 1𝞹 bond\n\nH-O-H no 𝞹 bond (NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','SO₂','NO₂','CO₂','H₂O','NEW','11-102','11-102',NULL),
(41813,'chemistry','Which of the following pairs of compounds is isoelectronic and isostructural?','None of these','TeI₂ and XeF₂ have 158 and 72 electrons respectively so not isoelectronic and have bent and linear geometry so also not isostructural IBr₂⁻and XeF₂ have 124 and 72 electrons respectively so not isoelectronic and have linear  geometry so also isostructural IF₃ and XeF₂ have 80 and 72 electrons respectively so not isoelectronic and have T-shaped and linear geometry so also not isostructural. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 116) (NEET 2017)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','TeI₂, XeF₂','IBr₂ ⁻ , XeF₂','IF₃, XeF₂','None of these','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(41814,'chemistry','The hybridizations of atomic orbitals of nitrogen in NO₂⁺ , NO₃⁻ and NH₄⁺ respectively are:','sp, sp² and sp³','NO₂⁺, O=N⁺=O having two sigma bonds which is only possible in sp, NO₃⁻ Three sigma bonds possible in sp² hybridization, NH₄⁺ Four sigma bonds possible in sp³ hybridization. \n\nNote: Number of sigma bonds is equal to number of hybrid orbitals. \n\nIf lone pair present this also involve in hybridization so number of sigma bonds + number of lone pairs = Hybrid orbitals. (NEET-II 2016)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','sp, sp³ and sp²','sp², sp³ and sp','sp, sp² and sp³','sp² , sp and sp³','NEW','11-125','11-125',NULL),
(41815,'chemistry','Which of the following pairs of ions is isoelectronic and isostructural?','CO₃²⁻, NO₃⁻','CO₃²⁻ \nC =  6 electrons\nO = 8×3 = 24 electrons\nnegative charges = 2\n6 + 24 + 2 = 32 electrons\n\nNO₃⁻\nN = 7 electrons\nO = 8×3 = 24 electrons\nnegative charge = 1\n7 + 24 + 1 = 32 electrons\n\nso CO₃²⁻, NO₃⁻ isoelectronic\n(NEET-II 2016)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','CO₃²⁻, NO₃⁻','ClO₃⁻ , CO₃²⁻','SO₃²⁻, NO₃⁻','ClO₃⁻ , SO₃²⁻','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(41816,'chemistry','The correct geometry and hybridization for XeF₄ are:','Octahedral, sp³d²','Number of sigma bonds + number of lone pairs = Hybrid orbitals, XeF₄ making four sigma bonds and have two lone pairs , 4 + 2 = 6 hybrid orbitals, this is possible in sp³d² with octahedral geometry \nIt is octahedral in geometry while its shape will be square planar.\n\n(NEET-II 2016)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Octahedral, sp³d²','Trigonal bipyramidal, sp³ d','Planar triangle, sp³ d³','Square planar, sp³d² .','NEW','11-115','11-115',NULL),
(41817,'chemistry','Among the following, which one is a wrong statement?','SeF₄ and CH₄ have same shape.','SeF₄ and CH₄ do not have same shape\n\nSeF₄  4 sigma and one lone pair so 5 hybrid orbitals (sp³d)\n\nCH₄ only four sigma bonds present so 4 hybrid orbitals(sp³)\n\nboth undergoing different hybridization so will not have same shape\n(NEET-II 2016)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','PH₅ and BiCl₅ do not exist.','p𝛑-d𝛑 bonds are present in SO₂.','SeF₄ and CH₄ have same shape.','I₃⁺ has bent geometry','NEW','11-115','11-115',NULL),
(41818,'chemistry','In which of the following pairs, both the species are not isostructural?','XeF₄, XeO₄','XeF₄, 4 sigma bonds, two lone pairs so will have 6 hybrid orbitals and undergo sp³d² hybridization due to lone pair it will have square planar structure instead of ocxtahedral XeO₄ four sigma bond but n lone pair so 4 hybrid orbitals it needs which is possible in sp³ hybridization will have tetrahedral structure (NEET 2015)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Diamond, Silicon carbide','NH₃, PH₃','XeF₄, XeO₄','SiCl₄, PCl₄⁺','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41819,'chemistry','Maximum bond angle at nitrogen is present in which of the following?','NO₂⁺','NO₂⁺ sp hybridized 180°  bond angle\n\nNO₃⁻ sp² hybridized 120° bond angle\n\nNO₂  sp² hybridized 134° bond angle \n\nNO₂⁻ sp² hybridized  115°  bond angle\n(NEET 2015)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','NO₂⁺','NO₃⁻','NO₂','NO₂⁻','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41820,'chemistry','Which one of the following species has planar triangular shape?','NO₃⁻','NO₃⁻ sp² hybridized 120° bond angle trigonal planar shape. (NEET 2014)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','N₃','NO₃⁻','NO₂⁻','CO₂','NEW','11-115','11-115',NULL),
(41821,'chemistry','XeF₂ is isostructural with:','ICl₂⁻','ICl₂⁻ two sigma bonds and 3 lone pairs present so 5 hybrid orbitals required which is possible in sp³d hybridization XeF₂ two sigma bonds and 3 lone pairs present so 5 hybrid orbitals required which is possible in sp³d hybridization both have same hybridization so isostructural. (NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','SbCl₃','BaCl₂','TeF₂','ICl₂⁻','NEW','11-115','11-115',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(41822,'chemistry','Which of the following is a polar molecule?','SF₄','Polarity depends on electronegativity difference and dipole moment all have zero dipole moment except SF₄ so it is polar. (NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','SiF₄','XeF₄','BF₃','SF₄','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41823,'chemistry','The outer orbitals of C in ethene molecule can be considered to be hybridized to give three equivalent sp² orbitals. The total number of sigma (σ) and pi (𝛑) bonds in ethene molecule is:','5 sigma (σ) and 1 pi (𝛑) bonds','H₂C= CH₂ each C making three sigma bonds two with H and one common sigma bond with other carbon so total 5 sigma bonds present both carbon making one pi bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 123; Paragraph 1)  (Karnataka NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','3 sigma (σ) and 2 pi (𝛑) bonds','4 sigma (σ) and 1 pi (𝛑) bonds','5 sigma (σ) and 1 pi (𝛑) bonds','1 sigma (σ) and 2 pi (𝛑) bonds.','NEW','11-103','11-103',NULL),
(41824,'chemistry','In which of the following pairs both the species have sp³ hybridization?','NF₃, H₂O','NF₃, three sigma bonds with F and has one lone pair so total 4 hybrid orbitals it needs which is possible in sp³ H₂O 2 sigma bonds with H and has 2 lone pair so total 4 hybrid orbitals it needs which is possible in sp³ both has sp³ hybridization. \n\nReference : ( NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 122; Paragraph 1)  (Karnataka NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','SiF4, BeH₂','NF₃, H₂O','NF₃, BF₃','H₂S, BF₃','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41825,'chemistry','Consider the following species : CN⁺, CN⁻ , NO and CN. Which one of these will have the highest bond order?','CN⁻','NO = 7 + 8 elctrons = 15 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(𝛑*2pₓ)¹=(𝛑*2pᵧ)⁰\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2(Nᵦ-Nₐ)\n                           1/2(10-5)   = 2.5\n\nCN⁻  =  6  + 8  = 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2(Nᵦ-Nₐ)\n                           1/2(10-4)   = 3\n\nCN⁺  =   6 +  6  = 12 electrons\n\nσ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2(Nᵦ-Nₐ)\n\n                           1/2(8-4)   =   2\n\nCN  = 6 + 7 = 13 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)¹\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ)\n                           1/2(9-4)   =   2.5\n\nCN⁻   has highest bond order\n\n(NEET 2018)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','NO','CN⁻','CN⁺','CN','NEW','11-109','11-109',NULL),
(41826,'chemistry','Which one of the following pairs of species have the same bond order?','CN⁻, CO','CN⁻  =  6  + 8  = 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                           1/2(10-4)   =   3\n\nCO = 6 + 8 = 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                           1/2(10-4)   =   3\n(NEET 2017)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','O₂, NO⁺','CN⁻, CO','N₂, O₂⁻','CO, NO','NEW','11-109','11-109',NULL),
(41827,'chemistry','Which of the following is paramagnetic?','O₂⁻','O + O⁻  = 8 + 9 = 17 electrons\n\nσ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2p)²(𝛑*2pₓ)²=(𝛑*2pᵧ)¹\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (10-7) = 1.5\n\nCO = 6 + 8 = 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                           1/2(10-4)   =   3\n\nCN⁻  =  6  + 8  = 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                           1/2(10-4)   =   3\n\nNO = 7 + 7 elctrons = 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2p)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                           1/2(10-4)   =   3\n\nO₂⁻ have unpaired electron while all other has paired electron\nparamagnetic behaviour is related to unpiared electron\n(NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','CN⁻','NO⁺','CO','O₂⁻','NEW','11-128','11-128',NULL),
(41828,'chemistry','The pair of species that has the same bond order in the following is:','CO, NO⁺','"O + O  = 8 + 8 = 16 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2p)²(𝛑*2pₓ)¹=(𝛑*2pᵧ)¹\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (10-6) = 2\n\nN + N  = 7+ 7= 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2p)²(σ2p𝓏)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (10-4) = 3\n\nCO = 6 + 8 = 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                           1/2(10-4)   =   3\n\nCN⁻  =  6  + 8  = 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                           1/2(10-4)   =   3\n\nNO⁺  = 7 + 7 elctrons = 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2p)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                           1/2(10-4)   =   3\n\n(Karnataka NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','CO, NO⁺','NO⁻ , CN⁻','O₂, N₂','O₂, B₂','NEW','11-109','11-109',NULL),
(41829,'chemistry','In which of the following ionization processes the bond energy increases and the magnetic behaviour changes from paramagnetic to diamagnetic?','NO → NO⁺','O + O  = 8 + 8 = 16 electrons\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(𝛑*2pₓ)¹=(𝛑*2pᵧ)¹\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (10-6) = 2\nParamagnetic\nO + O⁺  = 8 + 7 = 15 electrons\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(𝛑*2pₓ)¹\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (10-5) = 2.5\nParamagnetic\nN + N  = 7+ 7= 14 electrons\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)²\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (10-4) = 3\nDiamagnetic\nN + N⁺  = 7+ 6= 13 electrons\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)¹\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (9-4) = 2.5\nParamagnetic\nC + C = 6+ 6= 12 electrons\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (8-4) = 2\nDiamagnetic\nC + C⁺ = 6+ 5= 11 electrons\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)¹\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (7-4) = 1.5\nParamagnetic\nNO = 7 + 8 elctrons = 15 electrons\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(𝛑*2pₓ)¹=(𝛑*2pᵧ)⁰\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                           1/2(10-5)   =   2.5\nParamagnetic\nNO⁺ = 7 + 7 elctrons = 14 electrons\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                           1/2(10-4)   =   3\nDiamagnetic\nNO → NO⁺ bond order increase,bond energy increase magnetic behav-\nour changes\n\n(Karnataka NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','O₂ → O₂⁺','C₂ → C₂⁺','NO → NO⁺','N₂ → N₂⁺','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41830,'chemistry','Identify a molecule which does not exist:','He₂','He-He = (σ1s)²(σ*1s)²\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                           1/2(2-2)   =   0  no bond will be formed\n(NEET 2020)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','He₂','Li₂','C₂','O₂','NEW','11-130','11-130',NULL),
(41831,'chemistry','Which of the following diatomic molecular species has only pi bonds according to Molecular Orbital Theory?','C₂','"O + O  = 8 + 8 = 16 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(𝛑*2pₓ)¹=(𝛑*2pᵧ)¹\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (10-6) = 2\nparamagnetic\n\nN + N  = 7+ 7= 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ)\n                             1/2 (10-4) = 3\nDiamagnetic\n\nC + C = 6+ 6= 12 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ)\n                             1/2 (8-4) = 2\nDiamagnetic\n\nBe + Be = 4+ 4= 8 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)¹=(𝛑2pᵧ)¹\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (8-4) = 2\nParamagnetic   \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130) (NEET 2019)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Be₂','O₂','N₂','C₂','NEW','11-128','11-128',NULL),
(260228,'chemistry','When the aldehyde group is attached to a ring, which of the following suffix is added after the full name of the cycloalkane?','Carbaldehyde','The IUPAC names of open chain aliphatic aldehydes and ketones are derived from the names of the corresponding alkanes by replacing the ending –e with –al and –one respectively. \n\nIn case of aldehydes the longest carbon chain is numbered starting from the carbon of the aldehyde group while in case of ketones the numbering begins from the end nearer to the carbonyl group. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-359 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'2','mcq','Aldehyde','Carboxaldehyde','Carbaldehyde','Cycloaldehyde','NEW','12-359','12-229',NULL),
(41832,'chemistry','Which of the following is paramagnetic?','O₂','"O + O  = 8 + 8 = 16 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(𝛑*2pₓ)¹=(𝛑*2pᵧ)¹\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (10-6) = 2\nparamagnetic\n\nN + N  = 7+ 7= 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (10-4) = 3\nDiamagnetic\n\nC + C = 6+ 6= 12 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (8-4) = 2\nDiamagnetic\n\nBe + Be = 4+ 4= 8 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)¹=(𝛑2pᵧ)¹\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (8-4) = 2\n\nParamagnetic\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130) \n\n(NEET 2019)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','N₂','H₂','Li₂','O₂','NEW','11-128','11-128',NULL),
(41833,'chemistry','Decreasing order of stability of O₂, O₂ ⁻ , O₂ ⁺ and O₂ ²⁻ is:','O₂⁺ > O₂ > O₂ ⁻ > O₂ ²⁻','Stability is directly proportional to bond order\n\nO₂⁺  bond order = 2.5\nO₂  bond order = 2\nO₂ ⁻ bond order 1.5\nO₂ ²⁻ bond order 1 \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130) \n(NEET 2015)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','O₂²⁻ > O₂ ⁻ > O₂ > O₂⁺','O₂ > O₂⁺ > O₂ ²⁻ > O₂ ⁻','O₂ – > O₂ ²– > O₂ ⁺> O₂','O₂⁺ > O₂ > O₂ ⁻ > O₂ ²⁻','NEW','11-109','11-109',NULL),
(41834,'chemistry','The correct bond order in the following species is:','O₂ ⁻ < O₂ ⁺ < O₂ ²⁺','Stability is directly proportional to bond order\n\nO₂⁺  bond order = 2.5\nO₂  bond order = 2\nO₂ ⁻ bond order 1.5\nO₂ ²⁻ bond order 1 \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130)  (NEET 2015)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','O₂ ⁺ < O₂ ⁻ < O₂ ²⁺','O₂ ⁻ < O₂ ⁺ < O₂ ²⁺','O₂ ²⁺ < O₂ ⁺ < O₂ ⁻','O₂ ²⁺ < O₂ ⁻ < O₂ ⁺','NEW','11-109','11-109',NULL),
(41835,'chemistry','Which one of the following compounds shows the presence of intramolecular hydrogen bond?','Cellulose','H₂O₂, HCN and conc. CH₃COOH form intermolecular hydrogen bonding while cellulose has intramolecular hydrogen bonding. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 132) (NEET-II 2016)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','H₂O₂','HCN','Cellulose','Concentrated acetic acid','NEW','11-109','11-109',NULL),
(41836,'chemistry','C-O bond length is minimum in:','CO','C-O bond length is minimum in CO because isoelectronic has the same bond order according to molecular orbital theory. (NEET2020)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','CO','CO2','CO₃²⁻','HCOO–','NEW','11-125','11-125',NULL),
(41837,'chemistry','Molecules are held together in a crystal by:','Van der Waal’s attraction','Molecules in a crystalline structure are held together by the weak Van der Waal\'s interaction. (NEET 2020)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Van der Waal’s attraction','hydrogen bond','electrostatic attraction','dipole-dipole attraction','NEW','11-125','11-125',NULL),
(41838,'chemistry','Which of the molecules does not have a permanent dipole moment?','CS₂','The structure of CS₂ is linear and therefore it does not have permanent dipole moment. \n\nIt is represented as S = C = S. (NEET 2016)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','CS₂','H₂S','SO₂','SO₃','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(90913,'chemistry','The metals of which group can\'t make hydrides','All of these','However, the metals of group 7, 8 and 9 do not form any hydride. Even from group 6, only chromium forms CrH.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 289; Paragraph no.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrides-Metallic hydrides',NULL,'1','mcq','7th-group','8th-group','9th-group','All of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90914,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\n(A): Interstitial hydrides are non-stoichiometric.\n\n(R): Metallic hydrides are deficient in hydrogen,law of constant composition not applicable','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Unlike saline hydrides, interstitial hydrides are almost always non- stoichiometric, being deficient in hydrogen.\nIn such hydrides, the law of constant composition does not hold good.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 289; Paragraph no.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrides-Metallic hydrides',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90915,'chemistry','The hydrogen deficiency in ZrH is_________','1.3-1.75','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 289; Paragraph no.2) diagram(ne0902)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrides-Metallic hydrides',NULL,'1','fillup','1.3-1.75,1.5-1.8',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90916,'chemistry','The hydrogen deficiency in PdH is _________','0.6-0.8','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 289; Paragraph no.2) diagram(ne0902)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrides-Metallic hydrides',NULL,'1','fillup','0.6-0.8,0.6-0.7',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90917,'chemistry','Law of constant composition does not hold good in _______','LaH','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 289; Paragraph no.2) diagram(ne0902)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrides-Metallic hydrides',NULL,'1','fillup','La,H,Li',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90918,'chemistry','Choose the wrong statement.\n\na. In metallic hydrides,Hydrogen occupies interstices in metal lattice\n\nb. The hydrides of Ni,Pd,Ce,Ac don\'t have hydrogen in interstices of metal lattice while all other metals have.\n\nc.The property of absorption of hydrogen by transition metals is used in catalytic reduction.\n\nd. Pd and Pt act as storage media of hydrogen','only b','hydrogen occupies interstices in the metal lattice producing distortion without any change in its type. Consequently, they were termed as interstitial hydrides. However, recent studies have shown that except for hydrides of Ni, Pd, Ce and Ac, other hydrides of this class have lattice different from that of the parent metal. The property of absorption of hydrogen on transition metals is widely used in catalytic reduction / hydrogenation reactions for the preparation of large number of compounds. Some of the metals (e.g., Pd, Pt) can accommodate a very large volume of hydrogen and, therefore, can be used as its storage media. This property has high potential for hydrogen storage and as a source of energy.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 289; Paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrides-Metallic hydrides',NULL,'2','mcq','only a','only b','only c','only d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(90919,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\n(A): Phosphorus with outer electronic configuration 3s²3p³ can not make PH₅\n\n(R): High 𝚫ₐH Value of hydrogen and 𝝙ₑ₉H Value of hydrogen do not favour to exhibit the highest oxidation state of P','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Although phosphorus exhibits +3 and +5 oxidation states, it cannot form PH₅. Besides some other considerations, high ∆aH value of dihydrogen and ∆egH value of hydrogen do not favour to exhibit the highest oxidation state of P, and consequently the formation of PH₅.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 289; Problem 9.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrides-Metallic hydrides',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91420,'chemistry','Which statement is correct about the following reaction ?\n\n2RH(s) + M²⁺(aq) → MR₂(s) +2H⁺(aq)    M=Na,Ca,Mg   \n\na. H⁺ exchanges for  Na⁺ ,Ca²⁺ ,Mg²⁺ all other cation remain in solution.\n\nb. This process leads to decrease the ph of water.\n\nc. It is cation exchange process\n\nd. All statements are right','only d','Pure de-mineralised (de-ionized) water free from all soluble mineral salts is obtained by passing water successively through a cation exchange (in the H⁺ form) and an anion-\nexchange (in the OH⁺ form) resins:\n2RH(s) + M²⁺ (aq) → MR₂(s) +2H⁺(aq)    M=Na,Ca,Mg   \nIn this cation exchange process, H⁺ exchange for Na⁺ , Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺ and other cations present in water. This process results in proton release and thus makes the water acidic.\n\n:Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 292; Paragraph no.8)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hard and soft water-Permanent hardness',NULL,'2','mcq','only a','only b','only c','only d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91421,'chemistry','Which statement is correct statement about the following reactions ?\n\nRNH₂(s) + H₂O(l) → RNH₃⁺.OH⁻(s)     RNH₃⁺.OH⁻(s) + X⁻(aq) →  RNH₃⁺.X⁻(s) + OH⁻(aq)   X=Cl,bicarbonates,sulphates\n\na. OH⁻ exchanges anions present in hard water.\n\nb. This process produce no change to the ph of water.\n\nc. It is an anion exchange reaction.\n\nd. All statements are right','only d','Pure de-mineralised (de-ionized) water free from all soluble mineral salts is obtained by passing water successively through a cation exchange (in the H⁺ form) and an anion-\nexchange (in the OH⁺ form) resins:\n\nIn the cation exchange process:2RH(s) + M²⁺ (aq) → MR₂(s) +2H⁺(aq)    M=Na,Ca,Mg   ;here H⁺ exchange for Na⁺ , Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺ and other cations present in water. This process results in proton release and thus makes the water acidic.\n\nIn the anion exchange process:RNH₂(s) + H₂O(l) ⇌ RNH₃⁺.OH⁻(s)  ;   RNH₃⁺.OH⁻(s) + X⁻(aq) ⇌  RNH₃⁺.X⁻(s) + OH⁻(aq)   X=Cl,bicarbonates,sulphates; OH⁻ exchanges for anions like Cl⁻ , HCO³⁻, SO₄²⁻ etc. present in water. OH⁻ ions, thus, liberated neutralise the H⁺ ions set free in the cation exchange.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 293; Paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hard and soft water-Permanent hardness',NULL,'2','mcq','only a','only b','only c','only d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91422,'chemistry','The exhausted anion and cation resin beads can be regenerated by treatment with','First dilute acid then alkali solution','In the synthetic resin method for demineralising water, exhausted cation and anion exchange resin beds are regenerated by treatment with dilute acid and alkali solutions respectively.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 293; Paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hard and soft water-Permanent hardness',NULL,'1','mcq','First dilute Base then acid solution','First dilute acid then alkali solution','Only alkali solution','With water','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91801,'chemistry','H₂O₂ is stored in _______.','Wax lined glass','Hydrogen Peroxide is stored in wax-lined glass or plastic vessels in dark. Urea can be added as a stabiliser.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 294; Paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrogen peroxide-Storage',NULL,'1','fillup','Wa,x li,ned gl,ass,en,amal',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91802,'chemistry','______ is added to hydrogen peroxide as stabilizer.','Urea','Hydrogen Peroxide is stored in wax-lined glass or plastic vessels in dark. Urea can be added as a stabiliser.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 294; Paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrogen peroxide-Storage',NULL,'1','fillup','Ur,ea,amo,nia',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91901,'chemistry','Choose the wrong statement about Hydrogen peroxide.\n\na. Used as hair bleach and as a strong disinfectant.\n\nb. Serve as starting material for manufacturing of sodium perborate and per- carbonate.\n\nc. Used in synthesis of hydroquinone,Tartaric acid and cephalosporins antibiotics.\n\nd. All statements are true','only a','(i)In daily life it is used as a hair bleach and as a mild disinfectant. As an antiseptic it is sold in the market as perhydrol.\n\n(ii) It is used to manufacture chemicals like sodium perborate and per -carbonate, which are used in high quality detergents.\n\n(iii) It is used in the synthesis of hydroquinone, tartaric acid and certain food products and pharmaceuticals (cephalosporin) etc.\n\n(iv) It is employed in the industries as a leather, oils, fats, etc.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 294; Paragraph no.4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrogen peroxide-Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','only a','only b','only c','only d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91902,'chemistry','Hydrogen peroxide serve as pollution control agent in ______.','Green chemistry','Nowadays it is also used in Environmental (Green) Chemistry. For example, in pollution control treatment of domestic and industrial effluents, oxidation of cyanides, restoration of aerobic conditions to sewage\nwastes, etc.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 294; Paragraph no.4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrogen peroxide-Uses',NULL,'1','fillup','Gr,een, che,mis,try,sa,fe',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(91903,'chemistry','Heavy water can be prepared by exhaustive electrolysis of ______.','Water','Moderator in nuclear reactors and in exchange reactions for the study of reaction mechanisms. It can be prepared by exhaustive electrolysis of water oras a by-product in some fertilizer industries.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 294; Paragraph no.5)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Hydrogen peroxide-Uses',NULL,'1','fillup','Wa,ter,dis,till,ed',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92101,'chemistry','Dihydrogen can release ______ more energy than petrol.','Three times','Dihydrogen can release more energy than petrol (about three times). Moreover, pollutants in combustion of dihydrogen will be less than petrol. The only pollutants will be the oxides of dinitrogen (due to the presence of dinitrogen as impurity with dihydrogen).\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 295; Paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Dihydrogen as fuel',NULL,'1','mcq','Three times','Four times','Two times','10 times','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2000044727,'physics','Light of certain frequency and intensity incident on a photosensitive material causes photoelectric effect. If both the frequency and intensity are doubled, the photoelectric saturation current becomes','D','Photoelectric current is proportional to intensity of incident radiation and hence it gets doubled.<br>The photoelectric saturation current does not depend on frequency of the incident light','Photoelectric Effect','Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter','Easy',NULL,'Halved','Quadrupled','Unchanged','Doubled',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92102,'chemistry','The pollutants in combustion of dihydrogen are ____.','Oxides of dinitrogen','Dihydrogen can release more energy than petrol (about three times). Moreover, pollutants in combustion of dihydrogen will be less than petrol. The only pollutants will be the oxides of dinitrogen (due to the presence of dinitrogen as impurity with dihydrogen).\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 295; Paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Dihydrogen as fuel',NULL,'1','mcq','Oxides of dinitrogen','Hydrides of dinitogen','Hydroxide of dinitrogen','Deutrides of dinitrogen','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92103,'chemistry','A cylinder of compressed dihydrogen weighs _______ more than petrol tank of same amount.','30 times','A cylinder of compressed dihydrogen weighs about 30 times as much as a tank of petrol containing the same amount of energy.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 295; Paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Dihydrogen as fuel',NULL,'1','mcq','30 times','20 times','10 times','5 times','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92104,'chemistry','The tanks for the storage of dihydrogen are','All of these','The storage would require expensive insulated tanks. Tanks of metal alloy like NaNi₅, Ti-TiH₂,Mg–MgH₂ etc. are in use for storage of dihydrogen in small quantities.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 295; Paragraph no.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Dihydrogen as fuel',NULL,'1','mcq','NaNi₅','Ti-TiH₂','Mg-MgH₂','All of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92105,'chemistry','The principle of hydrogen economy is transportation and storage of energy in form of _____.','Both a and b','The basic principle of hydrogen economy is the transportation and storage of energy in the form of liquid or gaseous dihydrogen. Advantage of hydrogen economy is that energy is transmitted in the form of dihydrogen and not as electric power.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 295; Paragraph no.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Dihydrogen as fuel',NULL,'1','mcq','Liquid dihydrogen','Gaseous dihydrogen','Both a and b','None of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92106,'chemistry','In india a pilot project using dihydrogen fuel was launched in ______.','October 2005','It is for the first time in the history of India that a pilot project using dihydrogen as fuel was launched in October 2005 for running automobiles.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 295; Paragraph no.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Dihydrogen as fuel',NULL,'1','fillup','Oc,to,ber 20,05,09,Se,pt,em',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92201,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about hydrogen is incorrect ?','Dihydrogen does not act as a reducing agent.','H⁺ ions gets associated with H₂O to form hydronium ions H₃O⁺,which exists freely in solution.\n\nHydrogen has three isotopes namely protium , deuterium , tritium (radioactive) . Out of the three the most abundant is protium and the least abundant is tritium .Triitium is radioactive emitting low energy β radiations.\n\nHydrogen never acts as cation in ionic salts\n\nDihydrogen is a strong reducing agent . It can reduce metal ions in aqueous solutions and oxides of metals (less active than iron into corresponding metals)\nIn general yH₂ + MₓO → xM + yH₂O.\n\n(NEET-I 2016)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Hydronium ion, H₃O⁺ exists freely in solution.','Dihydrogen does not act as a reducing agent.','Hydrogen has three isotopes of which tritium is the least common','Hydrogen never acts as cation in ionic salts.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92202,'chemistry','Which of the following is electron-deficient ?','(BH₃)₂','Usually hydrides of Boron are electron deficient.\n(NEET 2013)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(BH₃)₂','PH₃','(CH₃)₂','(SiH₃)₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92203,'chemistry','The method used to remove temporary hardness of water is','Clark’s method','Temporary hardness of water can be removed  by clarks method and boiling  \n(NEET 2019)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Synthetic resins method','Calgon’s method','Clark’s method','Ion-exchange method','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92204,'chemistry','The number of hydrogen bonded water molecule(s) associated with CuSO₄.5H₂O is','1','Only one water molecule, which is outside the brackets (coordination sphere), is hydrogen-bonded. The other four molecules of water are coordinated.\n(Odisha NEET 2019)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','3','1','2','5','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92205,'chemistry','Match the following and identify the correct option. diagram(n0912)','A(iii), B(i),C(ii) ,D(iv)','As this mixture of CO and H₂ is used for the synthesis of methanol and a number of hydrocarbons, it is also called synthesis gas or \'syngas\'.\n\nTemporary hardness of water can be removed by boiling and Clark\'s method . In temporary hardness Ca(HCO₃)₂ and Mg(HCO₃)₂ are removed.\n\nUsually hydrides of Boron are electron deficient.\n\nH₂O₂ has non planar structure. \n(NEET 2020)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A(iii), B(i),C(ii) ,D(iv)','A(iii),B(ii)C(i),D(iv)','A(iii),B(iv),C(ii),D(i)','A(i), B(iii),C(ii) ,D(iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/92205q.png'),
(92206,'chemistry','One would expect proton to have very large','hydration energy','Because of its size we can expect proton to have a very large hydration enthalpy.\nH⁺ being very small will have very large hydration enthalpy .(Size is inversly proportional to hydration enthalpy)\n(NEET 1993)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','charge','ionization potential','hydration energy','radius','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92207,'chemistry','The ionization of hydrogen atom would give rise to','proton','Ionisation of hydrogen would lead one electron to get removed from hydrogen making it a unipositive hydrogen ion or equivalently called as a proton.\n\n(NEET 1990)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','hydride ion','hydronium ion','proton','hydroxyl ion','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92208,'chemistry','Which one of the following pairs of substances on reaction will not evolve H₂ gas?','Copper and HCl (aqueous)','In the electrochemical series  Copper lies below hydrogen and hence Copper cannot replace Hydroge  from HCl solution .\nBut Iron and Sodium lies above Hydrogen in electrochemical series and hence it can replace hydrogen from its solution liberating H₂ \n(NEET 1998)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Copper and HCl (aqueous)','Iron and steam','Iron and H₂SO₄ (aqueous)','Sodium and ethyl alcohol','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92209,'chemistry','Water gas is produced by','passing steam through a red hot coke','Reaction of steam on hydrocarbons or coke at high temperatures in the presence of catalyst yields hydrogen\nCH₄ + H₂O(g) →CO(g) + 3H₂(g)\n\nThe mixture of CO and H₂ is called water gas. As this mixture of CO and H₂ is used for the synthesis of methanol and a number of hydrocarbons, it is also called synthesis gas\nor \'syngas\'. Nowadays \'syngas\' is produced from sewage, saw-dust, scrap wood, newspapers etc. The process of producing \'syngas\' from coal is called \'coal gasification\'.\nC(s) + H₂O(g)→CO(g) + H₂(g) \n(NEET 1992)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','passing steam through a red hot coke','saturating hydrogen with moisture','mixing oxygen and hydrogen in the ratio of 1 : 2','heating a mixture of CO2 and CH4 in petroleum refineries','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92210,'chemistry','Which of the following metal evolves hydrogen on reacting with cold dilute HNO₃?','Mg','Mg reacts with nitric acid to give Mg(NO₃)₂ and evolves H₂ gas.\nMg + 2HNO₃ → Mg(NO₃)₂ + H₂\n(NEET 1998)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Mg','Al','Fe','Cu','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92211,'chemistry','Which of the following groups of ions makes the water hard?','Magnesium and chloride','Presence of calcium and magnesium salts in the form of hydrogencarbonate, chloride and sulphate in water makes water ‘hard’.\n(NEET 1994)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Sodium and bicarbonate','Magnesium and chloride','Potassium and sulphate','Ammonium and chloride','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92212,'chemistry','At its melting point, ice is lighter than water because','ice crystals have hollow hexagonal arrangement of H₂O molecules','The crystalline form of water is ice. At atmospheric pressure ice crystallises in the hexagonal form, but at very low temperatures it condenses to cubic form. Density of ice is less than that of water. Therefore, an ice cube floats on water. In winter season ice formed on the surface of a lake provides thermal insulation which ensures the survival of the aquatic life. This fact is of great ecological significance.\n(NEET 1992)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','H₂O molecules are more closely packed in solid state','ice crystals have hollow hexagonal arrangement of H₂O molecules','on melting of ice the H2O molecules shrinks in\nsize','ice forms mostly heavy water on first melting','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92213,'chemistry','Some statements about heavy water are given below.\n\n(i) Heavy water is used as a moderator in nuclear reactors.\n\n(ii) Heavy water is more associated than ordinary water.\n\n(iii)Heavy water is more effective solvent than ordinary water.\n\nWhich of the above statements are correct?','(i) and (ii)','Heavy water is extensively used as a moderator in nuclear reactors and in exchange reactions for the study of reaction mechanisms. It can be prepared by exhaustive electrolysis of water or as a by-product in some fertilizer industries. It is used for the preparation of other deuterium compounds.\n(NEET 1992)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(i) and (ii)','(i), (ii) and (iii)','(ii) and (iii)','(i) and (iii)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92214,'chemistry','The reaction of H₂O₂ with H₂S is an example of ......... reaction.','oxidation','H₂O₂ + H₂S → 2H₂O + S\nSince here sulphur is getting oxidised and H₂O₂ is getting reduced  to H₂O .Here H₂O₂ helps in oxidising H₂S to S. Hence it is an oxidation reaction.\n(NEET 1988)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','addition','oxidation','reduction','acidic','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92215,'chemistry','Hydrogen peroxide molecules are','diatomic and form X⁻ ions','The H₂O₂ is diatomic and forms H⁺ + HO₂⁻ (X⁻) (hydroperoxide ion).\n(NEET 1991)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','monoatomic and form X₂²⁻ ions','diatomic and form X⁻ ions','diatomic and form X₂⁻ ions','monoatomic and form X⁻ ions.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92216,'chemistry','The O – O – H bond angle in H₂O₂ is','97°','Bond angle of O – O – H in H₂O₂ is 97°.\n(NEET 1994)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','106°','109°28′','120°','97°','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92217,'chemistry','The volume strength of 1.5 N H₂O₂ solution is','8.4','Given strength of H₂O₂ is 1.5  .\nWe also know that the equivalent weight of H₂O₂ is 17 g\nHence volume strength x Equivalent weight = 1.5 x 17 = 25.5g\nFrom the equation we have , 2H₂O₂ →  2H₂O + O₂  .At STP , 68 g of  H₂O₂ gives 22.4 L of oxygen from stoichiometry.\nBy cross multiplying \n68g of H₂O₂ → 22.4 L of oxygen\n25.5g of H₂O₂ → y L of oxygen\ny = (25.5 x 22.4)/68 = 8.4 litre of oxygen.\nHence volume strenghth is 8.4 \n \n(NEET - 1997 , 1996)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','8.8','8.4','4.8','5.2','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92218,'chemistry','The structure of H₂O₂  is','non-planar','Hydrogen peroxide has a non-planar structure.\n(NEET - 2003)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','spherical','non-planar','planar','linear','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92219,'chemistry','Tritium , a radioactive isotope of Hydrogen emits which of the following particles?','Beta','Tritium is radioactive and emits low energy β- particles (t1⁄2, 12.33 years).\n(NEET - 2021)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Alpha','Beta','Gamma','Neutrons','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92220,'chemistry','Which of the following is the true structure of hydrogen peroxide?\ndiagram(n0913)','(b)','(NEET - 1989)\ndiagram(ne0906)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(a)','(b)','(c)','(d)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/92220q.png'),
(92301,'chemistry','Which of the following is true ?','Alkali metals have low ionisation ethalpy while hydrogen has high ionistaion enthalpy','Hydrogen, therefore, has resemblance to alkali metals, which lose one electron to form unipositive ions, as well as with halogens, which gain one electron to form uninegative ion. Like alkali metals, hydrogen forms oxides, halides and sulphides. However, unlike alkali metals, it has a very high ionization enthalpy and does not possess metallic characteristics under normal conditions.\n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 284 , paragraph :1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Hydrogen is short of one electron to gain noble gas configuration unlike alkali metals','Alkali metals do react with oxygen , Sulphur and halogen while hydrogen does not form sulphides , halides annd oxides','Alkali metals have low ionisation ethalpy while hydrogen has high ionistaion enthalpy','Alkali metals can lose one electron and form a uninegative ion while Hydrogen does not form uninegative ions','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92302,'chemistry','Regarding isotpoes of hydrogen which can be true among the following?','³₁H has 1 electron 1 proton and 2 neutrons','These isotopes differ from one another in respect of the presence of neutrons. Ordinary hydrogen, protium, has no neutrons, deuterium (also known as heavy hydrogen) has one and tritium has two neutrons in the\nnucleus.\n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 285 , paragraph :4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','³₁H has 1 electron 1 proton and 2 neutrons','¹₁H has 1 electron ,1 proton and 1 neutron','²₁H has 1 electron , 2 proton and  1 neutrons','³₁H has 1 electron 2 proton and 2 neutrons','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92303,'chemistry','Harold C Urey isolated an isotope of Hydrogen by some method . What is the mass number of the isotope he isolated and by which method?','2 , Physical methods','In the year 1934, an American scientist, Harold C. Urey, got Nobel Prize for separating hydrogen isotope of mass number\n2 by physical methods.\n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 285 , paragraph :4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1 , Centrifugation','2 , Physical methods','1 , Chemical methods','2 , Chromatography','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92304,'chemistry','The different  bond dissociation enthalpy affects one common property of different isotopes of hydrogen  .Which is that property that gets affected?','Rate of reactions','Since the isotopes have the same electronic configuration, they have almost the same chemical properties. The only difference is in their rates of reactions, mainly due to their different enthalpy of bond dissociation\n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 286 , paragraph :1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Reaction with halogens','Acidity','Heat of combustion','Rate of reactions','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92305,'chemistry','The isotopes of hydrogen have different values of melting and boiling point due to','Difference in mass','physical properties these isotopes differ considerably due to their large mass differences.\n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 286 , paragraph :1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Difference in number of electrons','Difference in mass','Difference in size','Difference in radioactivity','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92306,'chemistry','During the electrolysis of Brine the followig takes place?\n\n(i)Chlorine is deposited at anode\n\n(ii)Hydrogen is liberated at anode.\n\n(iii)The pH of the reaction keeps on decreasing .\n\n(iv)Hydrogen is the main product obtained during the elctrolysis of brine solution .','(i) is true (ii) , (iii) , (iv) are false','It is obtained as a byproduct in the manufacture of sodium hydroxide and chlorine by the electrolysis of brine solution. During the reaction chlorine is liberated at anode and hydrogen is liberated at cathode. Since the electrolysis leads to the formation of NaOH the pH keeps on increasing . \n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 286 , paragraph :3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) ,(ii) and (iii) are true , (iv) is false','(i) is true (ii) , (iii) , (iv) are false','(i) , (iv) are true but (ii) and (iiii) are false','(ii) is true , (iii), (iv) and (i) are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92307,'chemistry','Assertion : A mixture of CO and Hydrogen is used in the preperation of methanol.\n\nReason: This mixture can be prepared from sewage , saw dust and scrap wood.','Both asssertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion','The mixture of CO and H2 is called water gas. As this mixture of CO and H2 is used for the synthesis of methanol and a number of hydrocarbons, it is also called synthesis gas or \'syngas\'. Nowadays \'syngas\' is produced from sewage, saw-dust, scrap wood, newspapers etc. The process of producing \'syngas\' from coal is called \'coal gasification\'.\n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 286 , paragraph :5)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only assertion is true , reason is false','Both asssertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Both asssertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion','Reason is true but assertion  is false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92308,'chemistry','Regarding the proportion of Dihydrogen  coming from different sources , which is true?','18 % from coal and 4 % from electrolysis','Presently ~77% of the industrial dihydrogen is produced from petro-chemicals, 18% from coal, 4% from electrolysis of aqueous solutions and 1% from other sources.\n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 286 , paragraph :7)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','77 % from petrochemicals and 4 % from electrolysis of aqueous solutions','77 % from coal and 2 % from other sources','18 % from coal and 4 % from electrolysis','77 % from electrolysis and 18% from coals','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92309,'chemistry','Assertion :It is because of this factor that the dissociation of dihydrogen into its atoms is only ~0.081% around 200K which increases to 95.5% at 5000K.\n\nReason: The H–H bond dissociation enthalpy is the lowest for a single bond between two atoms of any element..','Both assertion and  reason are false','The H–H bond dissociation enthalpy is the highest for a single bond between two atoms of any element. What inferences would you draw from this fact ? It is because of this factor that the dissociation of dihydrogen into its atoms is only ~0.081% around 2000K which increases to 95.5% at 5000K.\n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 286 , paragraph :9)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both asssertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Both asssertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both assertion and  reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92310,'chemistry','Regarding to varius reaction of hydrogen which is true among the following?\n\n(i)The reaction of hydrogen with oxygen to form water is highly endothermic.\n\n(ii)Habers process, in which hydrogen reacts with dinitrogen takes place at 673K.\n\n(iii)During the formation of hydrogen halides , hydrogen can react with iodine even in dark.','(i) , (iii) are false , (ii) is true','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 287; paragraph no.2)\ndiagram(ne0901)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(i) is true , (ii) and (iii) are false','(i) and (ii) are true , (iii) is false','(ii) and (iii) are false , (i) is true','(i) , (iii) are false , (ii) is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92311,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement','Hydrogen reduces oxides of metals  (which is active less than hydrogen) into corresponding metals.','Reactions with metal ions and metal oxides: It reduces some metal ions in aqueous solution and oxides of metals (less active than iron) into corresponding metals.\n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 287 , paragraph :2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','When hydrogen reacts with alkali metals to form hydrides hydrogen get oxiidised and alkali metals get reduced.','Hydrogen reduces oxides of metals  (which is more active than hydrogen) into corresponding metals.','When hydrogen reduces oxides of metals into corresponding metals , hydrogen itself acts as an oxididing agent','Hydrogen reduces oxides of metals  (which is active less than hydrogen) into corresponding metals.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92312,'chemistry','In welding purpose , high temperature is obtained due to which property of hydrogen?','High bond enthalpy','Atomic hydrogen and oxy-hydrogen torches find use for cutting and weldingpurposes. Atomic hydrogen atoms (produced by dissociation of dihydrogen with the help of an electric arc) are allowed to recombine on the surface to be welded to generate the temperature of 4000 K.\n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 288 , paragraph :1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','High electronegativity','High ionisation enthalpy','High bond enthalpy','high electron gain enthalpy','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92313,'chemistry','The following statements are regarding saline hydrides. Which is the false statement','BeH₂ is non polymeric but MgH₂ is polymeric','These are stoichiometric compounds of dihydrogen formed with most of the s-block elements which are highly electropositive in character. However, significant covalent character is found in the lighter metal hydrides such as LiH, BeH₂ and MgH₂. In fact BeH₂ and MgH₂ are polymeric in structure. The ionic hydrides are crystalline, non-volatile and non- conducting in solid state. However, their melts conduct electricity and on electrolysis liberate dihydrogen gas at anode, which confirms the existence of H– ion.\n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 288 , paragraph :4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Mostly s block elemets form these type of hydrides','BeH₂ is non polymeric but MgH₂ is polymeric','Significant covalent character is found in LiH','They are non volatile in solid state','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92314,'chemistry','Electrolysis of which of the following gives pure dihydrogen','warm aqueous barium hydroxide','High purity (>99.95%) dihydrogen is obtained by electrolysing warm aqueous barium hydroxide solution between nickel electrodes.\n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 286 , paragraph :4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Acidified water','warm aqueous barium hydroxide','KOH solution','cold dilute barium hydroxide','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92315,'chemistry','Which of the following is false','Water cannot act as a oxidising agent','Hydrogen peroxide can act as both oxidising agent and reducing agent in both acid and alkali media\nHydrogen act as oxidising agent on reaction with alkali metals to mmetal hydrides . It also reduces metal oxides to metals .\nWater can act as both oxidising and reducing agent.','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Hydrogen peroxide can act as oxidising agent','Water cannot act as a oxidising agent','Hydrogen peroxide can act as reducing agent','Hydrogen can act as both oxidising agent and reducing agent','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92316,'chemistry','Among metallic hydrides which of the following has lattice similar to parent lattice?','Ac , Ce , Pd and Ni','Studies have shown that except for hydrides of Ni, Pd, Ce and Ac, other hydrides of this class have lattice different from that of the parent metal. \n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 289 , paragraph :2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ni , Pd , La and Ac','Ac , Ce , Pd and Ni','Pd , Pt , Ac and Ti','Cr , Ac , Ce and Zr','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92317,'chemistry','Which among the following can remove temporary hardness of water?','slaked lime','In Clarks method calculated amount of lime [Ca(OH)₂ - slaked lime] is added','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Hydrolith','slaked lime','quick lime','Milk of lime','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92318,'chemistry','Equivalent weight of hydrogen peroxide is','17','Equivalent weight of hydrogen peroxide is 17\n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen )','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','17','34','68','8','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92319,'chemistry','Saline hydrides react with water to form  what kind of solution','Basic','Saline hydrides react violently with water producing dihydrogen gas.\nNaH (s)  + H₂O (aq)  → NaOH (aq) +  H₂(g) \n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 288 , paragraph :5)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Acidic','Basic','Neutral','Amphoteric','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92320,'chemistry','Choose the correct series of reactions leading alcohols from alkenes','Alkenes(hydroformylation)→Aldehydes (Reduction)→Alcohols','Hydroformylation of olefins yields aldehydes which further undergo reduction to give alcohols.\n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 287 , Above problem 9 .1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Olefins(hydrogeation )→Aldehydes (Reduction)→Alcohols','Alkenes(hydroformylation)→Ketones(Reduction)→Alcohols','Alkenes(hydroformylation)→Aldehydes (Reduction)→Alcohols','Alkenes(hydroformylation)→Aldehydes (oxidation)→Alcohols','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92321,'chemistry','Select the correct statements about water\n\n(i)At atmospheric pressure ice crystallises in the hexagonal form, but at very low temperatures it condenses to cubic form.\n\n(ii)In the gas phase water is a bent molecule with a bond angle of 105.4°, and O–H bond length of 97.5 pm as.\n\n(iii)At atmospheric pressure ice crystallises in the cubic form, but at very low temperatures it condenses to hexagonal form.\n\n(iv)In the gas phase water is a bent molecule with a bond angle of 104.5°, and O–H bond length of 95.7 pm as.','(i) and (iv) are correct , (ii) and (iii) are wrong','In the gas phase water is a bent molecule with a bond angle of 104.5°, and O–H bond length of 95.7 pm.\nThe crystalline form of water is ice. At atmospheric pressure ice crystallises in the hexagonal form, but at very low temperatures it condenses to cubic form.\n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 290 , paragraph :2 and 3 )','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(i) and (ii) are correct , (iii) and (iv) are wrong','(i) and (iv) are correct , (ii) and (iii) are wrong','(ii) and (iv) are correct , (i) and (iii) are wrong','(i) and (iii) are correct , (ii) and (iv) are wrong','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92322,'chemistry','This substane used in the manufacture of chemicals like sodium perborate and sodium percarbonate is\na. Hydrogen peroxide\n\nb. Ethylanthraquinol\n\nc. Barium sulphate\n\nd. Deuterium oxide','a only','Hydrogen Peroxide is used to manufacture chemicals like sodium perborate and per -carbonate, which are used in high quality detergents.','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a only','d only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92323,'chemistry','Dihydrogen on an industrial scale is prepared by one among the following reaction','Water gas shift reaction','Dihydrogen on the industrial scale is prepared by the water-gas shift reaction from petrochemicals.\n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 295 , Summary , paragraph :3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Auto oxidation reaction','Water gas shift reaction','Boschs reaction','Coal gasification','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92324,'chemistry','Dihydrogen is used in fuel cells for generating electrical energy.Which properties of dihydrogen make it useful for generating electrical energy?\n\na. It does not impart any pollution .\n\nb. It has a very low molecular mass.\n\nc. More energy per unit mass\n\nd. Pollution can be minimised by adding water.','c,d','For mass basis dihydrogen can release more energy than petrol (about three times). Moreover, pollutants in combustion of dihydrogen will be less than petrol. The only pollutants will be the oxides of dinitrogen (due to the presence of dinitrogen as impurity with dihydrogen). This, of course, can be minimised by injecting a small amount of water into the cylinder to lower the temperature so that the reaction between dinitrogen and dioxygen may not take place.(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 289 , paragraph :2)\nWe cant tell that it does not release any pollutants . It do releases some like mentioned above','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','all of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92325,'chemistry','The H-O-H bond angle in water and O-O-H bond angle in Hydrogen peeroxide in gas phase are respectievely.','104.5° and 94.8°','In the gas phase water is a bent molecule with a bond angle of 104.5°, and O–H bond length of 95.7 pm.\n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 290 , paragraph :2  ) diagram(ne0906)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','104.5° and 90.2°','105.4° and 90.2°','104.5° and 94.8°','104.5° and 101.9°','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92326,'chemistry','Hydrogen bonding is least and maximum in following respectievely','Water in vapour and water in ice state','The water molecule is highly polar in nature due to its bent structure.This property leads to hydrogen bonding which is the maximum in ice and least in water vapour.\n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 296 ,Summary , paragraph :7)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Water in vapour and liquid water','Water in vapour and water in ice state','Water in  liquid and water in solid','Water in vapour and water in liquid.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92327,'chemistry','Which property of water leads to its capability to form hydrogen bond','High polar nature of water','The water molecule is highly polar in nature due to its bent structure.This property leads to hydrogen bonding which is the maximum in ice and least in water vapour.\n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 296 ,Summary , paragraph :7)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','High thermal capacity of water','High heat capacity of water.','High polar nature of water','High heat of vapourisation of water.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92328,'chemistry','A chemical substance used in moderator is made by eletrical enrichment of normal water . Which is the chemical ?','Heavy water','Heavy water, D₂O is another important compound which is manufactured by the electrolytic enrichment of normal water. It is essentially used as a moderator in nuclear\nreactors.\n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 296 ,Summary , paragraph :8)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Hard water','Heavy water','Dueterium monoxide','Hydrogen peroxide','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92329,'chemistry','To convert hydrogen gas to liquid hydrogen , we need to cool it to what temperature','20K','Also, dihydrogen gas is converted into liquid state by cooling to 20K.\n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 295 Paragraph :2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','30K','20K','150K','2.5K','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92330,'chemistry','Assertion : Ammonia is added as a stabiliser in hydrogen peroxide\n\nReason : Hydrogen peroxide get decomposed easily','Assertion is false reason is true','Hydrogen Peroxide is stored in wax-lined glass or plastic vessels in dark. Urea can be added as a stabiliser.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 294; Paragraph no.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion is true reason is false','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is false reason is true','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(92331,'chemistry','Both temporary and permanent hardness can be removed by the use of zeolites, and synthetic ion-exchangers .','TRUE','Both temporary and permanent hardness can be removed by the use of zeolites, and synthetic ion-exchangers.\n(Reference : NCERT Class 11 Chapter 9 :Hydrogen pg no 296 ,Summary , paragraph :4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> Hydrogen >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100222,'chemistry','Which techniques can be used to determine the concentration of alkali metals in a sample?','Both a and b','Alkali metals can be detected by the respective flame tests and can be determined by flame photometry or atomic absorption spectroscopy. These elements when irradiated with light, the light energy absorbed may be sufficient to make an atom lose electron. Only option D is incorrect. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 10.1.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'2','mcq','Flame photometry','Atomic absorption spectroscopy','Both a and b','None of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
