(100239,'chemistry','What makes up the Lithosphere?\n\nA. Earth\'s upper layer\n\nB. Earth\'s lower layer\n\nC. Crust\n\nD. Upper mantle\n\nE. Lower mantle','A+C+D','Lithosphere comprises of the Earth’s outer layer: its crust and part of the upper mantle\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 301; Table 10.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','mcq','A+B+C+D','B+C+D+E','B+C+D+E','A+C+D','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100240,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement.','The superoxide ion is stable only in the presence of small cations','The correct statement is "The superoxide ion is stable only in the presence of larger cations.." \n\nThis is because of lattice energy effects. All the other statements are true.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 301; Paragraph 10.1.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'2','mcq','The alkali metals are highly reactive due to their large size and low ionization enthalpy.','Alkali metal oxides react with moisture to form hydroxides.','Alkali metals burn vigorously in oxygen forming oxides.','The superoxide ion is stable only in the presence of small cations','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100241,'chemistry','Arrange the group 1 elements in increasing order of their reactivity.','Li < K < Rb < Cs','The reactivity of these metals increases down the group. \n\nAs the size increases, it becomes easier to remove outermost electron because of poor screening effect.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 301; Paragraph 10.1.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','mcq','Cs < Rb < K < Li','Li < K < Cs < Rb','Rb < K < Li < Cs','Li < K < Rb < Cs','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100242,'chemistry','What do the alkali metals, tarnish in dry air, to form?','Oxide','The alkali metals tarnish in dry air due to the formation of their oxides which in turn react with moisture to form hydroxides. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 301; Paragraph 10.1.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Oxide','Hydroxide','Carbonate','Sulphide','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100243,'chemistry','What is formed when Lithium is burnt with oxygen?','Monoxide','Lithium forms monoxide.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 301; Paragraph 10.1.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'2','mcq','Dioxide','Monoxide','Peroxide','Superoxide','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100244,'chemistry','What is formed when Sodium is burnt with oxygen?','Peroxide','Sodium forms peroxide.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 301; Paragraph 10.1.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Peroxide','Monoxide','Dioxide','Superoxide','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100245,'chemistry','M + O₂ → MO₂ (superoxide) (Major)\n\nWhich of the following element M cannot be?','Na','Though, Na forms superoxides, in the question superoxides as major product is asked. \n\nNa forms superoxide in smaller amount.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 301; Paragraph 10.1.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','mcq','Na','K','Rb','Cs','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100246,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of K in KO₂?','1','In all the oxides the oxidation state of the alkali metal is +1. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 301; Paragraph 10.1.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','1/2','3','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100247,'chemistry','Which among the following reacts exceptionally with Nitrogen to form nitride?','Lithium','Lithium shows exceptional behaviour in reacting directly with Nitrogen of air to form the nitride\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 301; Paragraph 10.1.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'2','mcq','Caesium','Lithium','Sodium','Potassium','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100248,'chemistry','What is used to store alkali metals?','Kerosene oil','Because of their high reactivity towards air and water, alkali metals are normally kept in kerosene oil. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 301; Paragraph 10.1.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'2','mcq','Petrol','Diesel','Kerosene oil','Non reactive gases','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(260229,'chemistry','The name of simplest aromatic aldehyde carrying the aldehyde group on a benzene ring is/are?','Benzaldehyde','The IUPAC names of open chain aliphatic aldehydes and ketones are derived from the names of the corresponding alkanes by replacing the ending –e with –al and –one respectively. \n\nIn case of aldehydes the longest carbon chain is numbered starting from the carbon of the aldehyde group while in case of ketones the numbering begins from the end nearer to the carbonyl group. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-359 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'1','mcq','Carbaldehyde','Formaldehyde','Benzaldehyde','Acetaldehyde','NEW','12-359','12-229',NULL),
(100249,'chemistry','Mark the statement that is correct.','Reaction of lithium with water is less vigorous than that of sodium with water.','It may be noted that although Lithium has most negative E value, its reaction with water is less vigorous than that of Sodium which has the least negative E value among the alkali metals.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(ii))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','mcq','Sodium has the most negative standard reductiion potential value among the alkali metals','Reaction of lithium with water is more vigorous than that of sodium with water.','Lithium has least negative standard reduction potential value.','Reaction of lithium with water is less vigorous than that of sodium with water.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100250,'chemistry','Mark the statement which is not incorrect.','All the alkali metal hydrides are ionic solids with high melting points.','All the alkali metal hydrides are ionic solids with high melting points. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(iii))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'2','mcq','All the alkali metal hydrides are covalent solids with low melting points.','All the alkali metal hydrides are ionic solids with low melting points.','All the alkali metal hydrides are covalent solids with high melting points.','All the alkali metal hydrides are ionic solids with high melting points.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100251,'chemistry','Mark the false statement.','All the alkali metal react with dihydrogen at about 673K.','The alkali metals react with dihydrogen at about 673K (lithium at 1073K) to form hydrides. \n\nAll the alkali metal hydrides are ionic solids with high melting points.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(iii))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'2','mcq','All the alkali metal hydrides are ionic solids.','All the alkali metal react with dihydrogen at about 673K.','The alkali metals are highly reactive due to their large size and low ionization enthalpy.','All the alkali metal hydrides have high melting point.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100252,'chemistry','At about what temperature Lithium combines with Dihydrogen?','1073 K','The alkali metals react with dihydrogen at about 673K (lithium at 1073K) to form hydrides. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(iii))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'2','mcq','1073 K','673 K','400 ℃','Both b and c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100253,'chemistry','At about what temperature alkali metal combine with Dihydrogen?','673 K','The alkali metals react with dihydrogen at about 673K (lithium at 1073K) to form hydrides. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(iii))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','1073 K','673 K','1073℃','673 ℃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100254,'chemistry','Which of the following metals forms halides with higher covalent character?','Li','The alkali metals readily react vigorously with halogens to form ionic halides, MX . \n\nHowever, Lithium halides are somewhat covalent. \n\nIt is because of the high polarisation capability of Lithium ion.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(iv))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Li','Na','Cs','K','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100255,'chemistry','Which property of Liithium ion makes it\'s halides covalent?','High polarisation capability','Lithium halides are somewhat covalent. \n\nIt is because of the high polarisation capability of lithium ion Polarizability is the property of anions not cations.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(iv))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','mcq','Low polarisation capability','Low polarizability','High polarisation capability','High polarizability','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100256,'chemistry','What is the distortion of electron cloud of the anion by the cation is called ?','Polarisation','The distortion of electron cloud of the anion by the cation is called polarisation. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(iv))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Polarisation','Polarisability','Contortion','Ionisation','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100257,'chemistry','Mark the correct statement.','The standard electrode potential measures the reducing power.','The distortion of electron cloud of the anion by + the cation is called polarisation\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(iv))\n\nThe alkali metals are strong reducing agents. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(v))\n\nWith the small size of its ion, lithium has the highest hydration enthalpy which accounts for its high negative standard reduction potential value and its high reducing power. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(v))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'2','mcq','The lithium ion is very small in size and has high tendency to distort electron cloud around the positive halide ion.','The alkali metals are weak reducing agents','With the small size of its ion, lithium has the highest hydration enthalpy which accounts for its high negative standard oxidation potential value and its high reducing power.','The standard electrode potential measures the reducing power.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100258,'chemistry','Anions of how much size can be easily distorted?','Large','Anions with large size can be easily distorted,\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(iv))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Large','Small','Intermediate','Medium','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100259,'chemistry','Which among the following is most covalent in nature?','LiI','Since anion with large size can be easily distorted, among halides, lithium iodide is the most covalent in nature. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(iv))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'2','mcq','LiI','LiF','CsF','CsI','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100260,'chemistry','Which among the following statement is correct?','Na is the least powerful reducing agent.','The alkali metals are strong reducing agents, lithium being the most and sodium the least powerful. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(v))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','mcq','Li is the most powerful oxidising agent.','Na is the least powerful reducing agent.','Cs is the most powerful reducing agent.','Cs is the least powerful reducing agent.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100261,'chemistry','Which among the following is most powerful reduciing agent?','Li','The alkali metals are strong reducing agents, lithium being the most and sodium the least powerful. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(v))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Li','Na','K','Cs','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100262,'chemistry','Which among the following is not represented in standard electrode potential?','Density','Sublimation enthalpy, Hydration enthalpy, and Ionisation enthalpy are expressed in standard electrode potential. \n\nHowever, density is expressed in g/cc or mass/volume.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(v))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Sublimation enthalpy','Ionisation enthalpy','Hydration enthalpy','Density','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100263,'chemistry','Match the reaction with appropriate situation.','Sublimation-M(s) → M(g),Ionisation-M(g) → M⁺(g) + e-,Hydration-M⁺(g) + H₂O → M⁺ (aq),Oxidation-M + O₂ → MO₂','The correct match is -\n\nSublimation - M(s) → M(g)\n\nIonisation - M(g) → M⁺(g) + e-\n\nHydration - M⁺(g) + H₂O → M⁺ (aq),\n\nOxidation - M + O₂ → MO₂\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(v))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','match','Sublimation,M(s) → M(g)','Ionisation,M(g) → M⁺(g) + e-','Hydration,M⁺(g) + H₂O → M⁺ (aq)','Oxidation,M + O₂ → MO₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100264,'chemistry','The standard reduction potential for Cl₂/Cl⁻ is +1.36, for I₂/I⁻ is +0.53, for Ag⁺/Ag is +0.79, and for Li⁺/Li is –3.04. Arrange the following ionic species in decreasing order of reducing strength:','Li > I⁻ > Ag > Cl⁻','The given data in the question is of standard reduction potentials. \n\nThe standard electrode potential gives us idea about the tendency of particular element to reduce itself. \n\nThe greater the tendency to reduce itself, the lesser the reducing strength. \n\nThus, standard reduction potential is inversely proportional to reducing strength. \n\nTo get better idea about reducing strengths, we convert all standard reduction potentials to standard oxidation potentials since standard oxidation potential is directly proportional to reducing strength.\n\nThe standard oxidation potentials are - Cl⁻/Cl₂ is -1.36, I⁻/I₂  is -0.53  Ag/Ag⁺ is -0.79  Li/Li⁺ is +3.04.\n\nThus the correct order is Li > I⁻ > Ag > Cl⁻\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Problem 10.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','mcq','I₂ > Li⁺ > Cl₂ > Ag⁺','Cl₂ > Ag⁺ > I₂ > Li⁺','Ag > CI⁻ > Li > l⁻','Li > I⁻ > Ag > Cl⁻','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100265,'chemistry','What is the colour of solution when alkali metals are dissolved in Ammonia?','Deep blue','The alkali metals dissolve in liquid ammonia giving deep blue solutions which are conducting in nature. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(vi))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'2','mcq','Deep blue','Bronze','Light blue','Navy blue','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100266,'chemistry','What is the nature of soluution of alkali metals dissolved in liquid ammonia?.','Conducting','The alkali metals dissolve in liquid ammonia giving deep blue solutions which are conducting in nature. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(vi))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'2','mcq','Conducting','Semi-conducting','Super-conducting','Nonconducting','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100267,'chemistry','The alkali metals dissolve in liquid ammonia giving deep blue solutions. Which among the following is reason for blue colour?','Ammoniated electron','The blue colour of the solution is due to the ammoniated electron which absorbs energy in the visible region of light and thus imparts blue colour to the solution. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(vi))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Alkali metal','Ammonia','Electron','Ammoniated electron','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100268,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement.','In concentrated solution, the blue colour changes to bronze colour and becomes paramagnetic.','The blue colour of the solution is due to the ammoniated electron which absorbs energy in the visible region of light and thus imparts blue colour to the solution. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(vi)) \n\nIn concentrated solution, the blue colour changes to bronze colour and becomes diamagnetic. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(vi)) \n\nLithium metal is used to make useful alloys, for example with lead to make ‘white metal’ bearings for motor engines.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.7) \n\nNowadays vehicles use lead-free petrol. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','mcq','The blue colour of the solution of alkali metal in Ammonia is due to the Ammoniated electron.','In concentrated solution, the blue colour changes to bronze colour and becomes paramagnetic.','Lithium metal is used to make useful alloys, for example with lead to make ‘white metal’ bearings for motor engines.','Now-a-days, vehicles use lead-free petrol.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100269,'chemistry','Which of the following is the correct about magnetic property of solution of alkali metals in Ammonia?:','Paramagnetic','The solutions are paramagnetic and on standing slowly liberate hydrogen resulting in the formation of amide. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(vi))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Paramagnetic','Diamagnetic','Ferromagnetic','Antiferromagnetic','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100270,'chemistry','On standing slowly, which gas does the solution of alkali metal in Ammonia liberate?','Hydrogen','The solutions are paramagnetic and on standing slowly liberate Hydrogen.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(vi))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Hydrogen','Nitrogen','Ammonia','Oxygen','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100271,'chemistry','On standing slowly, what compound is formed in the solution of alkali metal in ammonia?','Amide','The solutions are paramagnetic and on standing slowly liberate Hydrogen resulting in the formation of amide. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(vi))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Metal hydroxide','Co-ordination compound of metal with ammonia','Amide','Ammonia','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100272,'chemistry','What is the colour of concentrated solution of alkali metal in Ammonia?','Bronze','In concentrated solution, the blue colour changes to bronze colour and becomes diamagnetic. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(vi))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'2','mcq','Bronze','Light blue','Brown','Deep blue','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100273,'chemistry','Which of the following suiggests correctly about magnetic property of concentrated solution of alkali metal in Ammonia?','Diamagnetic','In concentrated solution, the blue colour changes to bronze colour and becomes diamagnetic. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(vi))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Diamagnetic','Paramagnetic','Ferromagnetic','Antiferromagnetic','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100274,'chemistry','In concentrated solution of alkali metal in ammonia, how many moles of amide is formed for each mole of Hydrogen gas released?','2','M⁺₍ₐₘ₎ + e⁻ + NH₃ (l)  → MNH₂₍ₐₘ₎ + 1⁄2H₂(g) \n\n(where ‘am’ denotes solution in ammonia.). \n\n1 mole of amide is formed when half mole hydrogen is liberated. \n\nThus, when 1 mole hydrogen is liberated, 2 moles of amide is formed.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.6(vi)Reaction)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','1.5','3','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100275,'chemistry','Which alkali metal is used in thermonuclear reactions?','Li','Lithium is used in thermonuclear reactions.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Li','Na','K','Fr','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100276,'chemistry','Which alkali metal is used in white metal with lead?','Li','Lithium s used in white metal with lead.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Li','Na','K','Fr','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100277,'chemistry','What is the alloy of Li and Al used for?','To make aircraft parts.','The alloy of Li and Al used to make aircraft parts.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','To make aircraft parts.','To make armour plates.','To make electrochemical cells','To make white metal.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
