(100549,'chemistry','Which of the following is used as laboratory agent?\n\nA) Na₂CO₃  B) NaOH  C) NaHCO₃  D) NaCl','Both A) and B)','Both Na₂CO₃ and NaOH are used as lab reagent with former being used for both qualitative and quantitative analysis.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305;306 Paragraph 10.4(USES))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A) and B)','Both A) and D)','Both C) and D)','All except C)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100714,'chemistry','Match the hydration energy released with group two elements. (in kJ/mol)','Mg-1921 kJ/mol,Ca-1577 kJ/mol,Sr-1443 kJ/mol,Ba-1305 kJ/mol','The correct match is Mg - 1921 kJ/mol ,Ca - 1577 kJ/mol, Sr - 1443 kJ/mol, Ba - 1305 kJ/mol\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; TABLE 10.2 )','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','match','Mg,1921 kJ/mol','Ca,1577 kJ/mol','Sr,1443 kJ/mol','Ba,1305 kJ/mol','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100718,'chemistry','Match the boiling point with group two elements. (in K)','Ca-1767 K,Sr-1655 K, Ba-2078 K, Ra-1973 K','The following is the correct match of boiling points\n\nCa - 1767 K, Sr - 1655 K, Ba - 2078 K, Ra - 1973 K\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; TABLE 10.2 )','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','match','Ca,1767 K','Sr,1655 K','Ba,2078 K','Ra,1973 K','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100719,'chemistry','Arrange the following elements in decreasing order of density','Sr > Be > Mg > Ca','Ca < Mg < Be < Sr < Ba < Ra is the trend of density of group 2 elements. \n\nThis is because volume of atoms increases from Be to Ca and then it decreases from Sr to Ra. \n\nDensity is mass/volume.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; TABLE 10.2 )','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','mcq','Be > Mg > Ca > Sr','Sr > Be > Mg > Ca','Ca > Be > Mg > Sr','Sr > Ca > Mg > Be','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100720,'chemistry','Arrange the following group 2 elements in decreasing order of density','Ra > Ba > Sr > Ca','Ca < Mg < Be < Sr < Ba < Ra is the trend of density of group 2 elements. \n\nThis is because volume of atoms increases from Be to Ca and then it decreases from Sr to Ra. \n\nDensity is mass/volume.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; TABLE 10.2 )','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','mcq','Ra > Ba > Sr > Ca','Ca > Sr > Ba > Ra','Ra > Ca > Sr > Ba','Ba > Ra > Sr > Ca','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100721,'chemistry','Which group 2 element has least density?','Ca','Ca < Mg < Be < Sr < Ba < Ra is the trend of density of group 2 elements. \n\nThis is because volume of atoms increases from Be to Ca and then it decreases from Sr to Ra. \n\nDensity is mass/volume.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; TABLE 10.2 )','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Be','Mg','Ca','Sr','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100722,'chemistry','Arrange the following elements in decreasing order of abundance in the crust','Ba > Sr > Be > Ra','The relative abundance of elements in lithosphere is Be - 2 ppm Mg - 2.76 % by weight Ca - 4.6 % by weight Sr - 384 ppm Ba - 390 ppm   Ra - 10⁻⁶ ppm\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; TABLE 10.2 )','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','mcq','Ra > Sr > Be > Ba','Ba > Sr > Be > Ra','Ra > Be > Sr > Ba','Sr > Ba > Be > Ra','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100723,'chemistry','Match the elements with their standard reduction potential.','Be-(-1.97),Mg-(-2.36),Ca-(-2.84),Sr-(-2.89)','The correct match is Be : -1.97, Mg : -2.36, Ca : -2.84, Sr : -2.89\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; TABLE 10.2 )','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','match','Be,(-1.97)','Mg,(-2.36)','Ca,(-2.84)','Sr,(-2.89)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100724,'chemistry','The atomic and ionic radii of the alkaline earth metals are ______ than/to those of the corresponding alkali metals in the same periods.','smaller','The atomic and ionic radii of the alkaline earth metals are smaller than those of the corresponding alkali metals in the same periods. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; Paragraph 10.6.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','smaller','larger','equal','slightly larger','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100725,'chemistry','Within the group, the atomic and ionic radii _______ with ________ in atomic number.','increases increase','Within the group, the atomic and ionic radii increase with increase in atomic number. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; Paragraph 10.6.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','decreases increase','decreases decrease','increases increase','increases decrease','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100726,'chemistry','How does ionization enthalpy varies down the group?','It decreases','The alkaline earth metals have low ionization enthalpies due to fairly large size of the atoms. \n\nSince the atomic size increases down the group, their ionization enthalpy decreases. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; Paragraph 10.6.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','It increases','It decreases','It remains unchanged','It varies irregularly','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100727,'chemistry','What is the comparison between the first ionisation enthalpies of the alkaline earth metals to those of the corresponding Group 1 metals?','Ionization enthalphy of alkaline earth metals is higher than those of corresponding group 1 element','The first ionisation enthalpies of the alkaline earth metals are higher than those of the corresponding Group 1 metals. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; Paragraph 10.6.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Ionization enthalphy of alkaline earth metals is lower than those of corresponding group 1 element','Ionization enthalphy of alkaline earth metals is equal to those of corresponding group 1 element','Ionization enthalphy of alkaline earth metals is higher than those of corresponding group 1 element','Ionisation enthalphies cannot be compared between groups.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100728,'chemistry','What is the comparison between the second ionisation enthalpies of the alkaline earth metals to those of the corresponding Group 1 metals?','Ionization enthalphy of alkaline earth metals is lower than those of corresponding group 1 element','It is interesting to note that the second ionisation enthalpies of the alkaline earth metals are smaller than those of the corresponding alkali metals. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; Paragraph 10.6.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Ionization enthalphy of alkaline earth metals is equal to those of corresponding group 1 element','Ionization enthalphy of alkaline earth metals is higher than those of corresponding group 1 element','Ionization enthalphy of alkaline earth metals is lower than those of corresponding group 1 element','Ionisation enthalphies cannot be compared between groups.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100729,'chemistry','Arrange the following ions in decreasing order of their hydration enthalpies.','Be²⁺ > Mg² ⁺> Ca²⁺ > Sr²⁺','The hydration enthalpies of alkaline earth metal ions decrease with increase in ionic size down the group.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; Paragraph 10.6.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','mcq','Sr²⁺ > Ca²⁺  > Mg²⁺ > Be²⁺','Sr²⁺ > Mg²⁺ > Ca²⁺ > Be²⁺','Sr²⁺ > Be²⁺ > Mg² ⁺> Ca²⁺','Be²⁺ > Mg² ⁺> Ca²⁺ > Sr²⁺','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100730,'chemistry','Arrange the following ions in decreasing order of their ionic sizes','Sr²⁺ > Ca²⁺  > Mg²⁺ > Be²⁺','Ionic size increases down the group.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; Paragraph 10.6.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','mcq','Sr²⁺ > Ca²⁺  > Mg²⁺ > Be²⁺','Be²⁺ > Mg² ⁺> Ca²⁺ > Sr²⁺','Sr²⁺ > Be²⁺ > Mg² ⁺> Ca²⁺','Sr²⁺ > Mg²⁺ > Ca²⁺ > Be²⁺','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100731,'chemistry','The hydration enthalpies of alkaline earth metal ions are _______ to those of alkali metal ions.','larger','The hydration enthalpies of alkaline earth metal ions are larger than those of alkali metal ions.\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; Paragraph 10.6.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','smaller','larger','equal','incomparable','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100732,'chemistry','Compounds of alkaline earth metals are ______ hydrated than those of alkali metals.','more','Compounds of alkaline earth metals are more extensively hydrated than those of alkali metals\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; Paragraph 10.6.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','lesser','more','equal','incomparable','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
