(100777,'chemistry','Metallic ______ is used for making windows of X-ray tubes.','Be','Metallic Beryllium is used for making windows of X-ray tubes.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 308; Paragraph 10.6.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Be','Mg','Ca','Sr','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100778,'chemistry','What is the use of Mg-Al alloys?','In the construction of aircraft.','Magnesium-aluminium alloys being light in mass are used in air-craft construction.\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 308; Paragraph 10.6.7','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','In the construction of X-ray window tubes.','In the construction of aircraft.','In removing air from vaccum tubes.','In making flash powders','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100779,'chemistry','Match the elements (or compounds) with their uses','Ca-Remove air from tubes, Mg-Flash powder, MgOH-Antacid, MgCO₃- Toothpaste','Magnesium (powder and ribbon) is used in flash powders and bulbs. \n\nA suspension of magnesium hydroxide in water (called milk of magnesia) is used as antacid in medicine. \n\nMagnesium carbonate is an ingredient of toothpaste. \n\nCalcium and barium metals, owing to their reactivity with oxygen and nitrogen at elevated temperatures, have often been used to remove air from vacuum tubes.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 308; Paragraph 10.6.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','match','Ca,Remove air from tubes','Mg,Flash powder','MgOH,Antacid','MgCO₃,Toothpaste','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100780,'chemistry','Which two metals have often been used to remove air from vacuum tubes?','Ca and Ba','Calcium and Barium metals have often been used to remove air from vacuum tubes.\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 308; Paragraph 10.6.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'2','mcq','Ba and Sr','Ca and Ba','Ca and Sr','Sr and Mg','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100781,'chemistry','Reactivity with which two gases make Ca and Ba suitable for making vacuum tubes?','Oxygen and Nitrogen','Calcium and Barium metals, owing to their reactivity with Oxygen and Nitrogen, have often been used to remove air from vacuum tubes. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 308; Paragraph 10.6.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','mcq','Oxygen and Nitrogen','Chlorine and Nitrogen','Oxygen and Chlorine','Oxygen and Neon','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100782,'chemistry','Why Calcium and Barium metals have often been used to remove air from vacuum tubes?','Because of their high reactivity with Oxygen and Nitrogen at elevated temperature.','Calcium and Barium metals, owing to their reactivity with Oxygen and Nitrogen at elevated temperatures, have often been used to remove air from vacuum tubes. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 308; Paragraph 10.6.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','mcq','Because of their high reactivity with Oxygen and Nitrogen at lower temperature.','Because of their low reactivity with Oxygen and Nitrogen at elevated temperature.','Because of their low reactivity with Oxygen and Nitrogen at low temperature.','Because of their high reactivity with Oxygen and Nitrogen at elevated temperature.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100783,'chemistry','Which compounds of Radium are used in Radiotherapy?','Radium salts','Radium salts are used in radiotherapy.\n\nReference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 308; Paragraph 10.6.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','mcq','Radium carbonates','Radium salts','Radium bicarbonates','Radium sulphates','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100784,'chemistry','Radium salts are used for the treatments of which of the following disease?','Cancer','Radium salts are used in radiotherapy, for example, in the treatment of cancer. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 308; Paragraph 10.6.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Brain tumours','Cancer','Hypothyroidism','Hyperthyroididsm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100785,'chemistry','Match the elements with their electronic configuration.','Ca-[Ar] 4s², Ra-[Rn] 7s², Sr-[Kr] 5s², Ba-[Xe] 6s²','The correct match is - Ca - [Ar] 4s²,  Ra - [Rn] 7s²,  Sr - [Kr] 5s²,  Ba - [Xe] 6s²\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Electronic Configuration Table)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','match','Ca,[Ar] 4s²','Ra,[Rn] 7s²','Sr,[Kr] 5s²','Ba,[Xe] 6s²','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100814,'chemistry','The alkaline earth metal hydroxides are  ____ stable than alkali metal hydroxides.','less','The alkaline earth metal hydroxides are, less basic and less stable than alkali metal hydroxides \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','more','equally','less','cannot be compared','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100815,'chemistry','Complete the following reaction: Be(OH)₂ + 2HCl + 2H₂O →','[Be(OH)₄]Cl₂','Be(OH)₂ + 2HCl + 2H₂O → [Be(OH)₄]Cl₂ \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'2','mcq','[Be(OH)₄]Cl₂','[Be(OH)₄]Cl','[Be(OH)₄]Cl₃','[Be(OH)₄]Cl₄','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100816,'chemistry','Be(OH)₂ + 2OH⁻ → [Be(H₂O)₄]Cl₂ This ion formed is called _______.','beryllate ion','Be(OH)₂ + 2OH⁻ → [Be(H₂O)₄]Cl₂ (Beryllate ion) \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','beryllate ion','beryllate hydroxide ion','berylium ion','berylium hydroxide ion','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100817,'chemistry','Assertion : Except for Beryllium halides, all other halides of alkaline earth metals are ionic in nature.\n\nReason : Beryllium halides are essentially covalent and soluble in organic solvents.\n\nChoose the correct option.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','Except for Beryllium halides, all other halides of alkaline earth metals are ionic in nature. \n\nBeryllium halides are essentially covalent and soluble in organic solvents. \n\nThe correct explanation to the assertion is - These have low ionization energy values and can create ionic bonds.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The statement of the Assertion is false but the Reason is true','Both the statements are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100818,'chemistry','What is the structure of Beryllium chloride in the solid state?','Chain','Beryllium chloride has a chain structure in the solid state. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','Polymeric','Linear','Chain','Rock-salt','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100819,'chemistry','In the vapour phase BeCl₂ tends to form a chloro-bridged ________ .','dimer','In the vapour phase BeCl₂ tends to form a chloro-bridged dimer which dissociates into the linear monomer at high temperatures of the order of 1200 K.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','dimer','trimer','tetramer','pentamer','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100820,'chemistry','SrCl₂ exists as a-','hexahydrate','The tendency to form halide hydrates gradually decreases (for example, MgCl₂·8H₂O, CaCl₂·6H₂O, SrCl₂·6H₂O and BaCl₂·2H₂O) down the group. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','dihydrate','trihydrate','pentahydrate','hexahydrate','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100821,'chemistry','The tendency to form halide hydrates gradually ________ down the group','decreases','The tendency to form halide hydrates gradually decreases (for example, MgCl₂·8H₂O, CaCl₂·6H₂O, SrCl₂·6H₂O and BaCl₂·2H₂O) down the group. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','increases','decreases','remains same','is irregular','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100822,'chemistry','Assertion :The dehydration of hydrated chlorides, bromides and iodides of Ca, Sr and Ba can be achieved on heating.\n\nReason : The corresponding hydrated halides of Be and Mg on heating suffer hydrolysis.\n\nChoose the correct option.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The dehydration of hydrated chlorides, bromides and iodides of Ca, Sr and Ba can be achieved on heating; however, the corresponding hydrated halides of Be and Mg on heating suffer hydrolysis. \n\nThe correct explanation is "Tendency to form alkaline earth halide hydrates gradually decreases down the group. \n\nThis favours the dehydration of hydrates of larger cations on heating"\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The statement of the Assertion is false but the Reason is true','Both the statements are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100823,'chemistry','The fluorides of group 2 elements are relatively ____ soluble than/to the chlorides.','less','The fluorides of group 2 elements are relatively less soluble than the chlorides owing to their high lattice energies. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','more','less','equally','not comparable','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100824,'chemistry','Why are the fluorides of group 2 elements are relatively less soluble than the chlorides?','Because of high lattice energies','The fluorides of group 2 elements are relatively less soluble than the chlorides owing to their high lattice energies. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','Because of low lattice energies','Because of high lattice energies','Because of low hydration energies','Because of low solvation energies','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100825,'chemistry','How many of the following statements are correct? \n\n1) Carbonates of alkaline earth metals are soluble in water.\n\n2) Carbonates of alkaline earth metals can be precipitated by addition of a sodium or ammonium carbonate solution to a solution of a soluble salt of these metals. \n\n3) The solubility of carbonates in water increases as the atomic number of the metal ion increases.','1','Carbonates of alkaline earth metals are insoluble in water and can be precipitated by addition of a sodium or ammonium carbonate solution to a solution of a soluble salt of these metals. \n\nThe solubility of carbonates in water decreases as the atomic number of the metal ion increases. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'3','mcq','3','2','1','0','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100826,'chemistry','What do carbonates decompose to on heating?','Carbon dioxide and oxide','All the carbonates decompose on heating to give carbon dioxide and the oxide.\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','Carbon monoxide and oxide','Carbon dioxide and oxide','Carbon trioxide and oxide','Carbon and oxide','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100827,'chemistry','Beryllium carbonate is unstable. In atmosphere of which compound is it kept?','Carbon dioxide','Beryllium carbonate is unstable and can be kept only in the atmosphere of CO₂.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','Carbon monoxide','Carbon dioxide','Carbon trioxide','Carbon sesquioxide','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100828,'chemistry','The thermal stability of group 2 carbonates increases with -','increasing cationic size','The thermal stability of group 2 carbonates increases with increasing cationic size.\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','increasing cationic size','decreasing cationic size','decreasing electropositivity.','increasing ionisation enthalpy','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100829,'chemistry','The thermal stability of group 2 carbonates ________ with increasing cationic size.','increases','The thermal stability of group 2 carbonates increases with increasing cationic size.\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','increases','decreases','remains same','none of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100830,'chemistry','The thermal stability of group 2 carbonates increases with ________ cationic size.','increasing','The thermal stability of group 2 carbonates increases with increasing cationic size.\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','decreasing','increasing','diminishing','reducing','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100831,'chemistry','The sulphates of the alkaline earth metals are described as -','white solids','The sulphates of the alkaline earth metals are all white solids and stable to heat.\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','white solids','black solids','black gases','white gases','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100832,'chemistry','The sulphates of the alkaline earth metals are  ______ when heated.','stable','The sulphates of the alkaline earth metals are all white solids and stable to heat. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','stable','decompose','unstable','burn','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100833,'chemistry','Assertion : BeSO₄ and MgSO₄ are readily soluble in water \n\nReason :The greater hydration enthalpies of Be²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions overcome the lattice enthalpy factor.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','BeSO₄, and MgSO₄ are readily soluble in water; the solubility decreases from CaSO₄ to BaSO₄. \n\nThe greater hydration enthalpies of Be²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions overcome the lattice enthalpy factor and therefore their sulphates are soluble in water. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7 and Page 310, Problem 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is false but the Reason is true.','Both the statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100834,'chemistry','What is the trend of solubility from CaSO₄ to BaSO₄?','It decreases','The solubility decreases from CaSO₄ to BaSO₄.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','It decreases','It increases','It remains same','It is irregular','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
