(101318,'chemistry','How many of these statements are true?\n \n1) The mobilities of the alkali metal ions in aqueous solution are Li⁺ < Na⁺ < K⁺ < Rb⁺ < Cs⁺.\n\n2) Lithium is the only alkali metal to form a nitride directly. \n\n3) E° for M²⁺ (aq) + 2e⁻ → M(s)  (where M = Ca, Sr or Ba) is nearly constant.','3','1) The mobilities of the alkali metal ions in aqueous solution are Li⁺ < Na⁺ < K⁺ < Rb⁺ < Cs⁺.\n\n2) Lithium is the only alkali metal to form a nitride directly. \n\n3) E° for M²⁺ (aq) + 2e⁻ → M(s) (where M = Ca, Sr or Ba) is nearly constant.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 313-314; Back Exercise)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Back Exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101319,'chemistry','How many of these statements are not true? \n\n1) A solution of Na₂CO₃ is alkaline. \n\n2) Alkali metals are prepared by electrolysis of their fused chlorides.\n\n3) K is found to be more useful than Na.\n\n4) LiI is more soluble than KI in ethanol.','1','1) A solution of Na₂CO₃ is alkaline. \n\n2) Alkali metals are prepared by electrolysis of their fused chlorides.\n\n3) Na is found to be more useful than K.\n\n4) LiI is more soluble than KI in ethanol \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 313-314; Back Exercise )','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Back Exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101320,'chemistry','How many of these statements are true? \n\n1) A solution of Na₂CO₃ is alkaline. \n\n2) Alkali metals are prepared by electrolysis of their fused chlorides.\n\n3) K is found to be more useful than Na.\n\n4) LiI is more soluble than KI in ethanol.','3','1) A solution of Na₂CO₃ is alkaline. \n\n2) Alkali metals are prepared by electrolysis of their fused chlorides.\n\n3) Na is found to be more useful than K.\n\n4) LiI is more soluble than KI in ethanol \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 313-314; Back Exercise )','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Back Exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101321,'chemistry','How many of these statements are not true? \n\n1) A solution of Na₂CO₃ is alkaline. \n\n2) BaO is soluble but BaSO₄ is insoluble in water.\n\n3) BeO is almost insoluble but BeSO₄ is soluble in water.\n\n4) LiI is more soluble than KI in ethanol.','0','1) A solution of Na₂CO₃ is alkaline.\n\n2) ) BaO is soluble but BaSO₄ is insoluble in water.\n\n3) BeO is almost insoluble but BeSO₄ is soluble in water.\n\n4) LiI is more soluble than KI in ethanol.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 313-314; Back Exercise )','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Back Exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','0','1','2','3','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101322,'chemistry','How many of these statements are true? \n\n1) A solution of Na₂CO₃ is alkaline. \n\n2) BaO is soluble but BaSO₄ is insoluble in water.\n\n3) BeO is almost insoluble but BeSO₄ is soluble in water.\n\n4) LiI is more soluble than KI in ethanol.','4','1) A solution of Na₂CO₃ is alkaline.\n\n2) BaO is soluble but BaSO₄ is insoluble in water.\n\n3) BeO is almost insoluble but BeSO₄ is soluble in water.\n\n4) LiI is more soluble than KI in ethanol.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 313-314; Back Exercise )','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Back Exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','0','1','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101401,'chemistry','Crude sodium chloride obtained by crystallisation of brine solution does not contain.','MgSO₄','Brine solution, contains sodium sulphate, calcium sulphate, calcium chloride and magnesium chloride as impurities.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4(USES))\n[NEET-2019 (Odisha)]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','CaSO₄','MgSO₄','Na₂SO₄','MgCl₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101402,'chemistry','Which of the alkali metal chloride (MCl) form its dihydrate salt (MCl.2H₂O) easily?','LiCl','LiCl is deliquescent and crystallises as a hydrate, LiCl.2H2O whereas other alkali metal chlorides do not form hydrates.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.3.1(USES))\n[NEET-2019 (Odisha)]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','KCl','LiCl','CsCl','RbCl','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101403,'chemistry','Enzyme that utilise ATP in phosphate transfer require an alkaline earth metal (M) as the cofactor. M is :','Mg','All enzymes that utilise ATP in phosphate transfer require magnesium as the cofactor.\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 312; Paragraph 10.10)\n[NEET-2019]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Be','Mg','Ca','Sr','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101404,'chemistry','Ionic mobility of which of the following alkali metal ions is lowest when aqueous solution of their salts are put under an electric field?','Li','As lithium ion has smallest size, it\'s charge density is maximum and it forms a huge hydration shell which reduces it\'s mobility.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 10.1.2)\n[NEET-2019]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Na','K','Rb','Li','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101405,'chemistry','Which of the following oxide is most acidic in nature?','BeO','Down the group, basic strength increases and acidic strength decreases.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)\n[NEET-2018]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','MgO','BeO','CaO','BaO','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101406,'chemistry','Among CaH₂, BeH₂, BaH₂, the order of ionic character is.','BeH₂ < CaH₂ < BaH₂','Because of high ionisation enthalpy and small size it forms compounds which are largely covalent and get easily hydrolysed. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph 10.8)\n[NEET-2018]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','BeH₂ < CaH₂ < BaH₂','BeH₂ < BaH₂ < CaH₂','BaH₂ < BeH₂ < CaH₂','BeH₂ < BaH₂ < CaH','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101407,'chemistry','The suspension of slaked lime in water is known as.','Milk of lime','The aqueous solution is known as lime water and a suspension of slaked lime in water is known as milk of lime. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 311; Paragraph 10.9) \n[NEET-(Phase-2)-2016]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Lime water','Quick Lime','Milk of lime','Aqueous solution of slaked lime','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101408,'chemistry','In context with Beryllium which one of the following statements is incorrect?','It’s salts rarely hydrolyse.','Beryllium has exceptionally small atomic and ionic sizes and thus does not compare well with other members of the group. \n\nBecause of high ionisation enthalpy and small size it forms compounds which are largely covalent and get easily hydrolysed.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph 10.8)\n[NEET-(Phase-2)-2016)]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','It is rendered passive by nitric acid.','It forms Be₂C','It’s salts rarely hydrolyse.','Its hydride is electron deficit and polymeric','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101409,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is false?','Ca²⁺ ions are not important in maintaining regular beating of heart.','About 99 % of body calcium is present in bones and teeth. \n\nIt also plays important roles in neuromuscular function, interneuronal transmission, cell membrane integrity and blood coagulation. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 312; Paragraph 10.10) \n[NEET-2016]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Mg²⁺ ions are important for green parts of plants.','Mg²⁺ ions form complex with ATP.','Ca²⁺ are important for blood clotting.','Ca²⁺ ions are not important in maintaining regular beating of heart.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101410,'chemistry','On heating which of the following releases CO₂ most easily?','MgCO₃','The thermal stability increases with increasing cationic size. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7) \n(NEET-2015)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','MgCO₃','CaCO₃','K₂CO₃','Na₂CO₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101411,'chemistry','Solubility of alkaline earth metal sulphates in water decrease in sequence','Mg > Ca > Sr > Ba','The size of anions being much larger compared to cations, the lattice enthalpy will remain almost constant within a particular group. \n\nSince the hydration enthalpies decrease down the group, solubility will decrease as found for alkaline earth metal carbonates and sulphates.\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7) \n[AIPMT-2015]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Mg > Ba > Sr > Ca','Ba > Sr > Ca > Mg','Mg > Ba > Ca > Sr','Mg > Ca > Sr > Ba','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101412,'chemistry','The function of “sodium pump” is a biological process operating in each and every cell of all animals. Which of the following biologically important ions is also constituent of the pump?','K⁺','These ionic gradients demonstrate that a discriminatory mechanism, called the sodium-potassium pump, operates across the cell membranes which consumes more than one-third of the ATP used by a resting animal and about 15 kg per 24 h in a resting human.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Paragraph 10.5) \n[AIPMT-2015]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Mg²⁺','Fe²⁺','Ca²⁺','K⁺','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101413,'chemistry','Which one of the alkali metals, forms only, the normal oxide, M₂O on heating in air?','Li','On combustion Li forms Li₂O; sodium gives the peroxide Na₂O₂ and potassium and rubidium give superoxide (MO₂)\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 301; Paragraph 10.1.6) \n[NEET 2012]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Rb','K','Li','Na','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101414,'chemistry','The ease of adsorption of the hydrated alkali metal ions on an ion-exchange resins follows the order.','Rb⁺ < K⁺ < Na⁺ < Li⁺','The order of decreasing hydration enthalpy of alkali metal ions is  \n\nLi⁺ > Na⁺ > K⁺ > Rb⁺ \n\nThus, ease of adsorption of hydrated ions is in the order : \n\nRb⁺ < K⁺ < Na⁺ < Li⁺\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 301;Table 10.1) \n[AIPMT 2012}','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Li⁺ < K⁺ < Na⁺ < Rb⁺','Rb⁺ < K⁺ < Na⁺ < Li⁺','K⁺ < Na⁺ < Rb⁺ < Li⁺','Na⁺ < Li⁺ < K⁺ < Rb⁺','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101415,'chemistry','The sequence of ionic mobility in aqueous solution is','Cs⁺ > Rb⁺ > K⁺ > Na⁺','Smaller the size of cation, higher will be the hydration and its effective size will increase and hence mobility in aqueous solution will decrease. \n\nHence, the correct sequence of ionic mobility in aqueous solution of the given cations is  \n\nCs+ > Rb+ > K+ > Na+.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 10.1.2) \n[AIPMT 2008]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Rb⁺ > K⁺ > Cs⁺ > Na⁺','Na⁺ > K⁺ > Rb⁺ > Cs⁺','K⁺ > Na⁺ > Rb⁺ > Cs⁺','Cs⁺ > Rb⁺ > K⁺ > Na⁺','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101416,'chemistry','When a substance (A) reacts with water it produces a combustible gas (B) and a solution of substance (C) in water. When another substance (D) reacts with this solution of (C), it also produces the same gas (B) on warming but (D) can produce gas (B) on reaction with dilute sulphuric acid at room temperature. Substance (A) imparts a deep golden yellow colour to a smokeless flame of Bunsen burner. Then (A), (B), (C) and (D) respectively are -','Na, H₂, NaOH, Zn','Thus, the correct answer is Na, H₂, NaOH, Zn.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Paragraph 10.5) \n[AIPMT 1998]\n\nRefer diagram(ne1001)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Ca, H₂, Ca(OH)₂, Sn','K, H₂, KOH, Al','Na, H₂, NaOH, Zn','CaC₂, C₂H₂, Ca(OH)₂, Fe','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101417,'chemistry','Which one of the following properties of alkali metals increases in magnitude as the atomic number rises?','Ionic radius','In a group, ionic radius increases with increase in atomic number whereas the m.pt. decreases down in a group due to weakening of metallic bonds. \n\nSimilarly, electronegativity and ionization energy also decrease down the group.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 10.1) \n[AIPMT 1989]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ionic radius','Melting point','Electronegativity','First ionization energy','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101418,'chemistry','In the case of alkali metals, the covalent character decreases in the order.','MI > MBr > MCl > MF','Alkali metals are highly electropositive and halogens are electronegative. \n\nThus, for the halides of a given alkali metal, the covalent character decreases with increase in electronegativity of halogens. \n\nOrder of covalent character of halides is MI > MBr > MCl > MF\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 3) \n[AIPMT 2009]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','MF > MCl > MBr > MI','MF > MCl > MI > MBr','MI > MBr > MCl > MF','MCl > MI > MBr > MF','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101419,'chemistry','The alkali metals form salt-like hydrides by the direct synthesis at elevated temperature. The thermal stability of these hydrides decreases in which of the following orders.','LiH > NaH > KH > RbH > CsH','The ionic character of the bonds in hydrides increases from LiH to CsH due to weakening of M—H bond so, thermal stability of these hydrides decreases in the order of \n\nLiH > NaH > KH > RbH > CsH.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 2) \n[AIPMT 2008]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','NaH > LiH > KH > RbH > CsH','LiH > NaH > KH > RbH > CsH','CsH > RbH > KH > NaH > LiH','KH > NaH > LiH > CsH > RbH','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101420,'chemistry','Which compound will show the highest lattice energy?','NaF','With the same anion, smaller the size of the cation, higher is the lattice energy. \n\nTherefore, NaF will show the highest lattice energy among the given compounds.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 3) \n[AIPMT 1993]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','RbF','CsF','NaF','KF','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101421,'chemistry','In Castner-Kellner cell for production of sodium hydroxide-','sodium amalgam is formed at mercury cathode.','In Castner-Kellner cell, sodium amalgam is formed at mercury cathode. \n\nA brine solution is electrolysed using a mercury cathode and a carbon anode\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph last) \n[Karnataka NEET 2013]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','brine is electrolyzed using graphite electrodes.','molten sodium chloride is electrolysed.','sodium amalgam is formed at mercury cathode.','brine is electrolyzed with Pt electrodes.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101422,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is incorrect?','Aluminium reacts with excess NaOH to give Al(OH)₃.','Al reacts with NaOH to give sodium aluminate\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)\n[Mains 2011]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Pure sodium metal dissolves in liquid ammonia to give blue solution.','Aluminium reacts with excess NaOH to give Al(OH)₃.','NaOH reacts with glass to give sodium silicate.','NaHCO₃ on heating gives Na₂CO₃.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101423,'chemistry','In which of the following processes, fused sodium hydroxide is electrolysed at a 330 °C temperature for extraction of sodium?','Castner’s process','In Castner’s process, for production of sodium metal, sodium hydroxide (NaOH) is electrolysed at temperature 330 °C.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph last) \n[AIPMT 2000]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Castner’s process','Down’s process','Cyanide process','Both (b) and (c).','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101424,'chemistry','Which of the following is known as fusion mixture?','Mixture of K₂CO₃ + Na₂CO₃','K₂CO₃ and Na₂CO₃ mixture is called as fusion mixture.\n\n[AIPMT 1994]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Mixture of Na₂CO₃ + NaHCO₃','Na2CO3.10H2O','Mixture of K₂CO₃ + Na₂CO₃','NaHCO₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101425,'chemistry','Washing soda has formula-','Na₂CO₃.10H₂O','Na₂CO₃.10H₂O is washing soda.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.4) \n[AIPMT 1990]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Na₂CO₃.7H₂O','Na₂CO₃.10H₂O','Na₂CO₃.3H₂O','Na₂CO₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101426,'chemistry','The following metal ion activates many enzymes, participates in the oxidation of glucose to produce ATP and with Na, is responsible for the transmission of nerve signals','K','Potassium ions are the most abundant cations within cell fluids, where they activate many enzymes, participate in the oxidation of glucose to produce ATP and, with sodium, are responsible for the transmission of nerve signals.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Paragraph 10.5) \n[NEET 2020]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Fe','Cu','Ca','K','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101427,'chemistry','Magnesium reacts with an element (X) to form an ionic compound. If the ground state electronic configuration of (X) is 1s² 2s² 2p³,the simplest formula for this compound is.','Mg₃X₂','Thus, the correct answer is Mg₃X₂. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Paragraph 10.5) Refer diagram(ne1002)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Mg₂X₃','MgX₂','Mg₂X','Mg₃X₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101428,'chemistry','Electronic configuration of calcium atom may be written as','[Ar]4s²','₂₀Ca →1s², 2s², 2p⁶, 3s², 3p⁶, 4s²\n\n₁₈Ar → 1s², 2s²,  2p⁶, 3s², 3p⁶\n\n₂₀Ca → [Ar]4s²\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307;Table 10.2) \n[AIPMT 1992]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','[Ne]4p²','[Ne]4s²','[Ar]4s²','[Ar]4p²','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101429,'chemistry','Compared with the alkaline earth metals, the alkali metals exhibit','lower ionization energies','The alkali metals are larger in size and have smaller nuclear charge thus they have lower ionization energy in comparison to alkaline earth metals.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; Paragraph 10.6.5) \n[AIPMT 1990]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','highest boiling points.','greater hardness','lower ionization energies','smaller ionic radii','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101430,'chemistry','Which of the following atoms will have the smallest size?','Be','Overall order Na > Mg > Li > Be. \n\nThus, Be has smallest size.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; Paragraph 10.2) \n[NEET 2020]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Mg','Na','Li','Be','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101431,'chemistry','HCl was passed through a solution of CaCl₂, MgCl₂ and NaCl. Which of the following compound(s) crystallise(s)?','Only MgCl₂','CaCl₂ and MgCl₂ are more soluble than NaCl. \n\nThus, when HCl was passed through a solution containing CaCl₂, MgCl₂ and NaCl, only NaCl got crystallised.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7 [ii]) \n[NEET 2019]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only NaCl','Only MgCl₂','NaCl, MgCl₂ and CaCl₂','Both MgClv and CaCl₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101432,'chemistry','Which of the following is an amphoteric hydroxide?','Be(OH)₂','Be(OH)₂ is amphoteric in nature as it reacts with acid and alkali both.\n\nBe(OH)₂ + 2OH– → [Be(OH)₄]²⁻\n\nBe(OH)₂ + 2HCl + 2H₂O → [Be(OH)₄]Cl₂\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7[i]) \n[AIPMT 2011]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ca(OH)₂','Be(OH)₂','Sr(OH)₂','Mg(OH)₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101433,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds has the lowest melting point?','CaI₂','As the covalent character in compound increases and ionic character decreases, melting point of the compound decreases. \n\nSo, CaI₂ has the highest  covalent character and lowest melting point.\n\n[AIPMT 2010]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CaCl₂','CaBr₂','CaF₂','CaI₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101434,'chemistry','Which of the following alkaline earth metal sulphates has hydration enthalpy higher than the lattice enthalpy?','BeSO₄','The hydration enthalpy of BeSO₄ is higher than  its lattice energy. \n\nWithin group 2, the hydration energy decreases down the group while lattice energy is almost the same.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Paragraph 10.5)\n[AIPMT 2010]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','BeSO₄','BaSO₄','SrSO₄','CaSO₄','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101435,'chemistry','Which one of the following compounds is a peroxide?','BaO₂','BaO₂ has a peroxide linkage.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)\n[AIPMT 2010]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','KO₂','BaO₂','NO₂','MnO₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101436,'chemistry','Property of the alkaline earth metals that increases with their atomic number','solubility of their hydroxides in water','The solubility of an ionic compound depends on two factors :\n\n(a) lattice energy, and (b) hydration energy In case of alkaline earth metal hydroxides, the lattice energy decreases as we move down the group. \n\nThis decrease is more than the decrease in the hydration energy down the group.\n\n[AIPMT 2010]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','solubility of their sulphates in water','electronegativity','ionization energy','solubility of their hydroxides in water','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101437,'chemistry','Which of the following oxides is not expected to react with sodium hydroxide?','CaO','CaO being a basic oxide does not react with NaOH, however SiO₂ (acidic oxide), BeO (amphoteric oxide) and B₂O₃ (acidic oxide) react with NaOH\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7) \n[AIPMT 2009]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CaO','SiO₂','BeO','B₂O₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101438,'chemistry','The correct order of increasing thermal stability of K₂CO₃, MgCO₃, CaCO₃ and BeCO₃ is','BeCO₃ < MgCO₃ < CaCO₃ < K₂CO₃','In all cases, for a particular set of group 1 or group 2 compounds, the thermal stability increases down the group as the ionic radius of the cation increases, and its polarising power decreases. \n\nGroup 1 compounds tend to be more thermally stable than group 2 compounds because group 1 cation has a smaller charge and a larger ionic radius, and so, a lower polarising power, particularly when adjacent metals on the same period are compared.\n\nHence, the order of increasing thermal stability of K₂CO₃, MgCO₃, CaCO₃ and BeCO₃ is BeCO₃ < MgCO₃ < CaCO₃ < K₂CO₃.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7 [iii]) \n[AIPMT 2007]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','BeCO₃ < MgCO₃ < CaCO₃ < K₂CO₃','MgCO₃ < BeCO₃ < CaCO₃ < K₂CO₃','K₂CO₃ < MgCO₃ < CaCO₃ < BeCO₃','BeCO₃ < MgCO₃ < K₂CO₃ < CaCO₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101439,'chemistry','In which of the following the hydration energy is higher than the lattice energy?','MgSO₄','When hydration energy exceeds lattice energy, the compound becomes soluble in water. \n\nThe solubility of alkaline earth metal sulphates decreases in the order : \n\nBeSO₄ > MgSO₄ > CaSO₄ > SrSO₄ > BaSO₄ > RaSO₄ \n\nvery high high sparingly soluble almost insoluble. \n\nThe solubilities of BeSO₄ and MgSO₄ are due to high energy of solvation of smaller Be⁺² and Mg⁺² ions.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7 [iii]) \n[AIPMT 2007]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','MgSO₄','RaSO₄','SrSO₄','BaSO₄','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101440,'chemistry','The solubility in water of sulphate down the Be group is Be > Mg > Ca > Sr > Ba. This is due to','high heat of solvation for smaller ions like Be⁺²','As we move down the group from BeSO₄ to BaSO₄, the enthalpy of hydration of the positive ion becomes smaller due to increase in ionic size. \n\nSalts of heavier metal ions are less soluble than those of lighter ions.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7 [iii]) \n[AIPMT 1995]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','increasing molecular weight.','decreasing lattice energy','increase in melting points','high heat of solvation for smaller ions like Be⁺²','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101441,'chemistry','All the following substances react with water. The pair that gives the same gaseous product is','Ca and CaH₂','The pair which gives the same gaseous product is Ca and CaH₂.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 308; Paragraph 10.6) Refer diagram(ne1003)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Ca and CaH₂','Na and Na₂O₂','K and KO₂','Ba and BaO₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101442,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is false?','Beryllium hydroxide is more basic than barium hydroxide.','Beryllium hydroxide although amphoteric, is however less basic than barium hydroxide\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7[i]) \n\n[AIPMT 1994]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Barium hydroxide is more soluble in water than magnesium hydroxide','Beryllium hydroxide is more basic than barium hydroxide.','Barium carbonate melts at a higher temperature than calcium carbonate.','Strontium decomposes water readily than beryllium.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101443,'chemistry','The product obtained as a result of a reaction of  nitrogen with CaC₂ is','Ca(CN)₂','CaC₂ + N₂ → CaCN₂ + C. CaCN₂ is nitrolim\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 308; Paragraph 10.6.6) \n\n[NEET-I 2016]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ca₂CN','CaCN','CaCN₃','Ca(CN)₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101444,'chemistry','Which one of the following is present as an active ingredient in bleaching powder for bleaching action','Ca(OCl)₂','Active ingredient in bleaching powder for bleaching action is Ca(OCl)₂.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 311; Paragraph slaked lime) \n\n[AIPMT 2011]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CaOCl₂','Ca(OCl)₂','CaO₂Cl','CaCl₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101445,'chemistry','Match List-I with List-II for the compositions of substances and select the correct answer using the code given.','Plaster of Paris-CaSO₄·1/2 H₂O, Epsomite-MgSO₄·7H₂O, Kieserite-MgSO₄·H₂O, Gypsum-CaSO₄·2H₂O','The correct match is \n\nPlaster of Paris - CaSO₄·1/2 H₂O\n\nEpsomite - MgSO₄·7H₂O\n\nKieserite - MgSO₄·H₂O \n\nGypsum - CaSO₄·2H₂O\n\n[Mains 2011]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','match','Plaster of Paris,CaSO₄·1/2 H₂O','Epsomite,MgSO₄·7H₂O','Kieserite,MgSO₄·H₂O','Gypsum,CaSO₄·2H₂O','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101446,'chemistry','The compound A on heating gives a colourless gas and a residue that is dissolved in water to obtain B. Excess of CO₂ is bubbled through aqueous solution of B, C is formed which is recovered in the solid form. Solid C on gentle heating gives back A. The compound is -','CaCO₃','Thus, the correct answer is CaCO₃.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph 10.9) \n\n[Mains 2010]\n\ndiagram(ne1004)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CaCO₃','Na₂CO₃.10H₂O','K₂CO₃','CaSO4.2H₂O','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101447,'chemistry','Which of the following represents Calcium chlorite?','Ca(ClO₂)₂','Since the valency of calcium is 2 and a chlorite ion is ClO²⁻, therefore calcium chlorite is Ca(ClO₂)₂\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 311; Paragraph slaked lime) \n\n[AIPMT 1996]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Ca(ClO₃)₂','CaClO₂','Ca(ClO4)₂','Ca(ClO₂)₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101448,'chemistry','Identify the correct statement','Gypsum contains a lower percentage of calcium than Plaster of Paris','Gypsum is CaSO₄·2H₂O and Plaster of Paris is (CaSO₄)₂·H₂O. \n\nTherefore, gypsum contains a lower percentage of calcium than Plaster of Paris\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 311; Paragraph Last) \n\n[AIPMT 1995]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Gypsum is obtained by heating Plaster of Paris','Plaster of Paris is obtained by partial oxidation of gypsum','Plaster of Paris can be obtained by hydration of gypsum.','Gypsum contains a lower percentage of calcium than Plaster of Paris','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101449,'chemistry','Bleaching powder is obtained by the action of chlorine gas and','dry slaked lime.','Cl₂ gas reacts with dry slaked lime, Ca(OH)₂ to give bleaching powder.\n\nCa(OH)₂ + Cl₂ →. CaOCl₂ + H₂O\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 311; Paragraph slaked lime) \n\n[AIPMT 1988]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','dry slaked lime.','dry CaO','concentrated solution of Ca(OH)₂','dilute solution of Ca(OH)₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101450,'chemistry','Which of the following metal ions play an important role in muscle contraction?','Ca⁺²','Calcium is an essential element for the contraction of muscles.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 312; Paragraph 10.10) \n\n[AIPMT 1994]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Ca⁺²','K⁺','Na⁺','Mg⁺²','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101451,'chemistry','Among the following alkaline earth metal halides, one which is covalent and soluble in organic solvents is','Beryllium chloride','Beryllium chloride is covalent and soluble in a organic solvent.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7 (ii)) \n\n[NEET 2021]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Calcium chloride','Strontium chloride','Magnesium chloride','Beryllium chloride','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101452,'chemistry','Match the following-','Li-electrochemical cells,Na-coolant in fast breeder reactors,KOH-absorbent for carbon dioxide,Cs-photochemical cell','• Cs is used in photoelectric cell due to its low ionisation enthalpy \n\n• KOH used to adsorb CO₂ and changes into K₂CO₃\n\n• Liquid sodium metal is used as a coolant in fast breeder nuclear reactor\n\n• Lithium is used in electrochemical cells\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 302; Paragraph 10.1.7) \n[NEET 2022]','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','match','Li,electrochemical cells','Na,coolant in fast breeder reactors','KOH,absorbent for carbon dioxide','Cs,photochemical cell','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101501,'chemistry','The alkaline earth metals form compounds which are predominantly ionic but less ionic than the corresponding compounds of alkali metals due to -\n\nA. Increased nuclear charge\n\nB. Smaller size\n\nC. 2 electrons in s orbital\n\nD. Higher ionisation enthalpy','A and B','The alkaline earth metals form compounds which are predominantly ionic but less ionic than the corresponding compounds of alkali metals. \n\nThis is due to increased nuclear charge. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A and B','B and C','C and D','A and D','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/101501q.png'),
(101502,'chemistry','What is the trend of variation of atomic size among following elements?\n\nMg, Sr, Ba, K, Na, Cs.','Cs > K > Ba > Sr > Na > Mg','The following is the trend of variation of atomic size among elements of s-block. \n\nIt is important to learn this trend as questions can be asked because s-block being smaller block \n\nCs > Rb > K > Ba > Sr > Ca > Na > Mg > Li > Be\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 301; Paragraph 10.1)\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; Paragraph 10.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Ba > Sr > Cs > K > Na > Mg','Ba > Cs > Sr > K > Mg > Na','Ba > Sr > Cs > Na > K > Mg','Cs > K > Ba > Sr > Na > Mg','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/101502q.png'),
(101503,'chemistry','Arrange the following elements in the increasing order of Metallic bond strength.\n\nMg, Na, Ba, Cs, Rb.','Cs < Rb < Ba < Na < Mg','Metallic bond strength varies inversely with Atomic radius/ Atomic size.\n\nThe following is the order  Cs < Rb < K < Ba < Sr < Ca < Na < Mg < Li < Be\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 301; Table 10.1)\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; Table 10.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Ba < Rb < Cs < Mg < Na','Cs < Ba < Rb < Mg < Na','Cs < Rb < Ba < Na < Mg','Na < Ba < Rb < Na < Cs','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/101503q.png'),
(101504,'chemistry','An element (A) was first melted and then kept in Oxygen-rich environment. After some time, yellow coloured solid (B) was formed.This solid was brought in strong magnetic field. It was observed that the solid moved from weak magnetic field to strong magnetic field. Because of the moisture in air, yellow compound turned to white crystalline solid (C). Solid (C) is also used in the manufacture of soaps. What is (B)?','Potassium superoxide','Element (A) is Potassium. Potassium forms superoxide when reacted with Oxygen. \n\nK (A)  +  O₂  →  KO₂ (B)          KO₂  +  H₂O  →  KOH (C)  +  H₂O₂  +  O₂\n\nColour of superoxides is yellow and they are paramagnetic in nature. \n\nParamagnetic substances move from weaker magnetic filed to stronger magnetic field. \n\nPotassium superoxide when reacts with moisture it forms Potassium hydroxide, Hydrogemn peroxide and Oxygen gas is released. \n\nThus, (A), (B), (C) are Potassium, Potassium superoxide and Potassium hydroxide respectively. \n\nKOH is used in the manufacture of soft soaps.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.1.7)\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Potassium peroxide','Potassium superoxide','Potassium hydroxide','Sodium peroxide','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101505,'chemistry','In which of the following photoelectric effect is found maximum?','Cs','Photoelectric effect will be found maximum in Caesium. \n\nThis is because it is easier to remove electrons from the outermost shell of Cs because of poor shielding of inner orbitals.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 10.1.1)\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.1.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Li','Na','K','Cs','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101506,'chemistry','Potassium carbonate react with HCl to form compund (A), gas (B) and water. Compound (A) and gas (B) are -','Potassium chloride and Carbon dioxide','Metal halides can be prepared by the reaction of the appropriate oxide, hydroxide or carbonate with aqueous hydrohalic acid (HX).\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Potassium chloride and Hydrogen','Potassium bicarbonate and Carbon dioxide','Potassium chloride and Carbon dioxide','Potassium superoxide and Carbon dioxide','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101507,'chemistry','A plant sample was put in an air-tight jar with compound A. It was observed that after some time the plant wilted and was dead ultimately. What do you think compound A would be?','Potassium hydroxide','KOH is used as an excellent absorbent of Carbon dioxide. \n\nIt absorbs all the Carbon dioxide present making jar\'s environment Carbon dioxide-deficient thus hindering photosynthesis.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.1.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Caustic soda','Carbon dioxide','Potassium chloride','Potassium hydroxide','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101508,'chemistry','To remove excess water from Sulphuric acid which of the following cannot be used?','NaCl','Here we check for the compound having deliquescence. Deliquescent compounds absorb moisture.\n\nLiCl is deliquescent.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.3.1)\n\nCrystals of Sodium hydroxide are deliquescent.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph last)\n\nMgCl₂ is deliquescent\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.3.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','LiCl','NaOH','MgCl₂','NaCl','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101509,'chemistry','Common products of hydrolysis of MO₂ and M₂O₂ types of oxides are -','H₂O₂ and OH⁻','MO₂  +  H₂O  →  2M⁺  +  H₂O₂  +  OH⁻  +  O₂\n\nM₂O₂  +  H₂O  →  2M⁺  +  H₂O₂  +  OH⁻\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','H₂O₂ and O₂','H₂ and O₂','H₂O₂ and OH⁻','H₂ and OH⁻⁻','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101510,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is correct about salts of oxo-acids?','They are soluble in water and are thermally stable.','The salts of oxo-acids are soluble in water and are thermally stable.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','They are soluble in water but are thermally unstable.','They are thermally stable but are insoluble in water','They are soluble in water and are thermally stable.','They are neither thermally stable nor soluble in water.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101511,'chemistry','Which of the following is not the end product of preparation of NaOH in Castner-Kellner cell?','O₂','Reactions of Castner-Kellner cells are:\n\nCathode: Na⁺  +  e⁻ +  Hg →  Na amalgam\n\nAnode:  Cl⁻  →  1/2 Cl₂  +  e⁻\n\nThe amalgam is treated with water to give Sodium hydroxide and Hydrogen gas.\n\n2Na-amalgam  +   2H₂O  →  2NaOH  +  2Hg  +  H₂ \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph Caustic soda)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','H₂','O₂','Cl₂','Hg','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101512,'chemistry','Which among the following is the exceptional behaviour of Lithium?','Lithium nitrate on decomposition give lithium oxide.','The correct statements are -\n\nLiF and Li₂O are comparatively much less soluble in water unlike other alkali metals.\n\nLi unlike other alkali metals forms no ethynide on reaction with ethyne.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph - 10.3.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','LiF is more soluble in water while other alkali metals are less soluble','Li₂O is more soluble in water while other alkali metals are less soluble','Li unlike other alkali metals forms ethynide on reaction with ethyne','Lithium nitrate on decomposition give lithium oxide.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101513,'chemistry','The metal that gives Crimson red colour during flame test and with Mg is used to make armour plates is -','Li','The metal that gives Crimson red colour during flame test and with Mg is used to make armour plates is Li.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph - 10.1.5)\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 301; Paragraph - 10.1.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Li','Ca','Sr','Rb','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101514,'chemistry','Which among the following has negative ionisation enthalpy?','Fr','Fr has the negative ionisation enthalpy of -375 kJ/mol.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Table - 10.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Li','Na','Cs','Fr','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101515,'chemistry','572 g of washing soda was taken and was heated till the temperature of 102℃. After some time, the mass was measured. It was found tht mass of washing soda reduced. What is the mass of the compound left?','248 g','Washing soda, that is, Sodium carbonate loses water of crystallisation to form monohydrate at 375 K i.e 102℃.\n\nMolar weight of Na₂CO₃.10 H₂O = 286 g \n\n       Na₂CO₃.10 H₂O + heat →  Na₂CO₃.H₂O  +  9H₂O\n\n572 g means 2 moles of Sodium carbonate is taken, therefore balanced reaction is -\n\n      2Na₂CO₃.10 H₂O + heat →  2Na₂CO₃.H₂O  +  18H₂O\n\nMass of 2Na₂CO₃.10 H₂O = 572 g\n\nMass of 2Na₂CO₃.H₂O = 572 - mass of 18H₂O = 572 - 324 = 248 g\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Properties of sodium carbonate)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','206 g','124 g','224 g','248 g','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101516,'chemistry','4LiNO₃  + △  →\n\n2NaNO₃  + △  →\n\nWhich is the common gas that is released in both the reactions?','O₂','4LiNO₃  + △  →  2Li₂O  +  4NO₂  +  O₂\n\n2NaNO₃  + △  →  2NaNO₂  + O₂\n\nThus, the common gas is O₂\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph - 10.3.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','NO₂','NO','O₂','N₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101517,'chemistry','Calculate the percentage of water of crystallisation in Washing soda?','62.90%','Molar weight of Na₂CO₃.10 H₂O = 286 g \n\nMolar weight of H₂O = 18 g. \n\nSince, there are 10 moles of H₂O in washing soda therefore the weight of water of crystallisation is 180 g.\n\nPercentage of water of crystallisation = 180/286 = 62.90 %\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Properties of sodium carbonate)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','6.29 %','169 %','62.90%','16.9 %','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101518,'chemistry','Due to which of the following, solution of sodium carbonate is alkaline in nature?','Anionic hydrolysis','Na₃CO₃  →  Na⁺  +  CO₃²⁻ \n\nCO₃²⁻  +  H₃O  →  HCO₃⁻  +  OH⁻ \n\nSince the anionic part of compound undergoes hydrolysis it is called Anionic hydrolysis.\n\nCarbonate part of sodium carbonate undergoes hydrolysis due to which the solution becomes alkaline.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Properties of sodium carbonate)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Cationic hydrolysis','Anionic hydrolysis','Cationic-anionic hydrolysis','Anionic-cationic hydrolysis','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101519,'chemistry','Which of the following is coloured?','RbO₂','Oxides, peroxides and halides of alkali metals are colourless. \n\nSuperoxides may be yellow or orange.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.1 and 10.2.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','KCl','Na₂O₂','Li₂O','RbO₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101520,'chemistry','In which of the following energy required is maximum?\n\nBa  →  Ba⁺   +  e⁻ \n \nLi  →  Li⁺   +  e⁻     \n\nRa  →  Ra⁺   +  e⁻  \n\nCa  →  Ca⁺   +  e⁻','D','The given reacctions denote ionisation process\n\nBa  →  Ba⁺   +  e⁻  IE = 503 kJ/mol\n\nLi  →  Li⁺   +  e⁻     IE =  520 kJ/mol\n\nRa  →  Ra⁺   +  e⁻  IE = 509 kJ/mol\n\nCa  →  Ca⁺   +  e⁻  IE = 590 kJ/mol\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Table - 10.1)\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Table - 10.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101521,'chemistry','Which among the following is the route to prepare BeH₂?','BeCl₂ + LiAlH₄','BeCl₂  +  LiAlH₄  →  BeH₂  +  LiCl  +  AlCl₃\n\nBe(OH)₂  +  2HCl  +  2H₂O  →  [Be(OH)₄Cl₂\n\nBe(OH)₂  +  NaOH  →  Na[Be(OH)₄]\n\nBe  +  H₂  →  No reaction\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; Paragraph 10.6.6(iii))\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 308; Paragraph 10.7(i))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Be(OH)₂ + 2HCl + 2H₂O','Be(OH)₂ + NaOH','Be + H₂','BeCl₂ + LiAlH₄','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101522,'chemistry','Which among the following is not used in aircraft?','Ba','Li, Mg and Al are used in aircraft.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 301; Paragraph 10.1.7)\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; Paragraph 10.6.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ba','Mg','Al','Li','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101523,'chemistry','Which among the following is not crystalline?','Calcium oxide','Calcium oxide is a white amorphous solid. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Sodium carbonate','Calcium oxide','Sodium chloride','Lithium chloride','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101524,'chemistry','Which among the following forms the product that has rock-salt structure?','CaCO₃  →','CaCO₃  → CaO  +  CO₂. the product CaO has rock-salt structure.\n\nCa(OH)₂  +  CO₂  →  CaCO₃  +  H₂O\n\nCaCO₃  +  CO₂  +  H₂O →  Ca(HCO₃)₂\n\n2Ca(OH)₂  +  Cl₂  →  CaCl₂  +  Ca(OCl)₂  +  2H₂O\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 308; Paragraph 10.7(i))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Ca(OH)₂  +  CO₂  →','CaCO₃  +  CO₂  +  H₂O →','2Ca(OH)₂  +  Cl₂  →','CaCO₃  →','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101525,'chemistry','How many moles of Ammonia will be required to obtain the following products in the given balanced reaction:\n\nM  + ___   →  [M(NH₃)₂]²⁺  +  2[e(NH₃)]₃','5NH₃','M  + (x + y)NH₃   →  [M(NH₃)ₓ]²⁺  +  2[e(NH₃)ᵧ]\n\nHere, x and y are 2 and 3 respectively.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; Paragraph 10.6.6(vi))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','6NH₃','4NH₃','5NH₃','3NH₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101526,'chemistry','How much Gypsum is required to prepare 1 kg Plaster of paris?','1186.2 g','2(CaSO₄.2H₂O)  →  2(CaSO₄).H₂O  +  3H₂O\n\nMolecular weight of Gypsum, 2(CaSO₄.2H₂O) = 344 g [here we are taking stoichiometric co-efficients also into calculattions for easy understanding]\n\nMolecular weight of Plaster of Paris, 2(CaSO₄).H₂O =  290 g\n\n290 g of Plaster of Paris is obtained from 344 g Gypsum. \n\nTherefore to obtain 1 kg i.e 1000 g Plaster of Paris  we will require y g Gypsum.\n\nTherefore y = [344 x 1000]/ 290 = 344000/290 = 1186.2 g\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph 10.9 Calcium sulphate topic)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1432.6 g','1168.2 g','1268.2 g','1186.2 g','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101527,'chemistry','Which of the following is correct about the structure of BeCl₂?','It has 3-centered 4-electron bonds','Thus,has 3-centered 4-electron bonds\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 308; Paragraph 10.7(ii))\ndiagram(ne1005)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','It has 3-centered 4-electron bonds','It has 4-centered 3-electron bonds','It has 3-centered 2-electron bonds','It has 2-centered 3-electron bonds','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101528,'chemistry','How many of the following statements are correct?\n\n1. Since the enthalpies of decomposition are very high, alkaline earth metal oxides are thermally stable.\n\n2. Carbonates of alkaline earth metals can be precipitated by addition of sodium or ammonium bicarbonate to the the solution of soluble salts of these metals.\n\n3. The nitrates of alkaline earth metals can be prepared by dissolution of carbonates in concentrated nitric acid.','0','The correct statements are-\n\n1. Since the enthalpies of formation are very high, alkaline earth metal oxides are thermally stable.\n\n2. Carbonates of alkaline earth metals can be precipitated by addition of sodium or ammonium carbonate to the the solution of soluble salts of these metals.\n\n3. The nitrates of alkaline earth metals can be prepared by dissolution of carbonates in dilute nitric acid.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 308; Paragraph 10.7 (i)(iii))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','0','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101529,'chemistry','Which of the reaction is written correctly?','BeO  +  C  +  Cl₂  →  BeCl₂  +  CO    [At 600 K - 800 K]','BeO  +  C  +  Cl₂  ⇋  BeCl₂  +  CO    [At 600 K - 800 K] \n\nThis reaction is a reversible reaction. However, Forward reaction takes place at 600 K - 800 K.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; Paragraph 10.6.6(ii))\n\nAlthough the reaction of Al(OH)₃ in excess NaOH  is not mentioned in NCERT but it is important to remember the product formed as it is mentioned in the book.\n\nAl(OH)₃  +  excess NaOH  →  [Al(OH)₄]⁻  (Aluminate ion)\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.8.1(iii))\n\nMgCl₂  +  8H₂O  →  MgCl₂.8H₂O       \n\nMgCl₂ is  deliquescent and crystallises as MgCl₂.8H₂O \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.3.2(vi))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','BeCl₂  +  CO  →  BeO  +  C  +  Cl₂    [At 600 K - 800 K]','Al(OH)₃  +  excess NaOH  →  [Al(OH)₆]²⁻','MgCl₂  +  6H₂O  →  MgCl₂.6H₂O','BeO  +  C  +  Cl₂  →  BeCl₂  +  CO    [At 600 K - 800 K]','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101530,'chemistry','Which among the following is not used in paper industry?','CaO','All the following options are used in the manufacture of paper or in paper industry except CaO.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Uses of Quick lime)\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303;Paragraph 10.4 )','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Na₂CO₃.10 H₂O','CaCO₃','NaOH','CaO','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101531,'chemistry','Why are alkaline earth metals considered strongly electropositive?','Because of low ionisation enthalpy.','Alkaline earth metals are strongly electropositive because of low ionisation potential.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Paragraph 10.6.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Because of small cationic size.','Beacause of high hydration energy.','Because of presence of last electron in s-shell','Because of low ionisation enthalpy.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101532,'chemistry','A compound X when heated 797℃- 997℃ forms compound Y and CO₂  is released. Later, Y is treated with H₂O and a white amorphous powder is formed. This powder is treated with Cl₂ gas to form Z. The compound Z is the same compound which is obtained if compound X is treated with Hydrochloric acid. What is Z?','CaCl₂','The appearance of Ca(OH)₂ is white, solid and it is amorphous. \n\nThus, compound X, Yand Z are CaCO₃, CaO and CaCl₂ respectively.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.9) diagram(ne1006)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Ca(OH)₂','CaCl₂','CaCO₃','CaO','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101533,'chemistry','Why is cement also called \'Portland cement\'?','Because it resembles natural limestone in the isle of Portland.','Cement also called \'Portland cement\' because it resembles natural limestone in the isle of Portland.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 311; Paragraph: Uses of cement)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Because it was first introduced by scientist Portland','Because it was first found in Portland, England.','Because Portland is one of the ingredients used to make the cement.','Because it resembles natural limestone in the isle of Portland.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101534,'chemistry','Metal nitrate + heat  →  Metal monoxide + NO₂ + O₂\n\nHere metal cannot be -','Ra','4LiNO₃+ heat  →  2Li₂O  +  4NO₂ + O₂\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.3.1(vi))\n\n2M(NO₃)₂  +  heat  → 2MO + 4NO₂ + O₂  [Where M = Be, Mg, Ca, Sr, Ba]\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 308; Paragraph 10.7(iii))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Li','Be','Ca','Ra','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101535,'chemistry','Which among the following fluids serves as medium for transport of Ca ion?','Plasma','All the Calcium passes through plasma.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 308; Paragraph 10.10)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Plasma','CSF','Lymph','All of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101536,'chemistry','A compound X was kept in CO₂ atmosphere. After a while the amorphous solid changed to white fluffy powder  Y. The powder Y was then heated to 1200 K. The solid product formed was tested with litmus paper. What would be the results?','Red litmus turned blue.','CaO is white amorphous solid and  compound X. CaCO₃ is white fluffy powder and also compound Y.\n\n  CaO  +  CO₂   →  CaCO₃                                            CaCO₃  → CaO  +  CO₂   [1200 K]\n\nWhen powder Y i.e  CaCO₃ was heater at 1200 K, CaO and CO₂ are formed. Here solid product is CaO and it is basic oxide thus turns red litmus blue.\n\nPurple litmus changes color from violet to red when exposed to acidic substance and violet to blue if exposed to basic substance.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 308; Paragraph 10.7(i))\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Red litmus turned blue.','Blue litmus turned red.','Both blue and red litmus showed no colour change.','Purple litmus turned red','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101537,'chemistry','Which of the following combination is used as flux in the extraction of iron ?','CaCO₃ + MgCO₃','Calcium carbonate along with Magnesium carbonate is used as a flux in the extraction of metals such as iron.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph-Uses of CaCO₃)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CaCl₂ + MgCl₂','CaCl₂ + MgCO₃','CaSO₄ + MgCO₃','CaCO₃ + MgCO₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101538,'chemistry','How many among the following are the reasons for alkaline earth metals being different than alkali metals?\n\n1. Reduced ionic size.\n\n2. Extra electron in s-orbital.\n\n3. Increased charge.\n\n4. Lower first ionisation enthalpy.','2','Reduced atomic annd cationic sizes and increased cationic charge are the reasons for alkaline earth metals being different than alkali metals.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 312; Paragraph-second last)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101539,'chemistry','Which among the following is a constructional material?','Cement','Portland cement is an important constructional material.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 312; Paragraph-second last)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Cement','Plaster of Paris','Calcium carbonate','Gypsum','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101540,'chemistry','Which among the following is ionic in nature?','MgCl₂','LiI is partially ionic but also has higher covalent character while MgCl₂ is typical ionic compound.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 308; Paragraph 10.7(i)(ii))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','MgCl₂','BeO','BeCl₂','LiI','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101541,'chemistry','During an experiment, two metal salts were tested by flame test. Salt A and Salt B showed apple green and red violet colour respectively. What is the conclusion?','Metal in Salt A is harder in nature','Apple green colour is shown by Ba which is group-2 element and red violet is shown by Rb which is group 1 element.\n\nSalt A and Salt B are salts of group-2 and group-1 elements respectively.\n\nThe alkaline earth metals, in general, are silvery white, lustrous and relatively soft but harder than the alkali metals.\n\nAlkaline earth metals have higher cationic charge and second IE of these metals is higher than Group-1 elements.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 307; Paragraph 10.6.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Metal in Salt A is harder in nature','Metal in Salt B has greater second IE.','Metal in Salt A belongs to group - 1','Metal in Salt B has higher cationic charge.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101601,'chemistry','The alkali metals are low melting. Which of the following alkali metal is expected to melt if the room temperature rises to 30°C?','Cs','Among alkali metals, melting point decreases as the strength of metallic bonding decreases with increasing size of the atom. Thus, Cs has the  lowest melting point (28.5°C) and will melt at 30°C.\n  Melting      Li           Na          K          Rb         Cs\n   point        454K      371K     336K    312K      302K  .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.301 Table 10.1','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Na','K','Li','Cs','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101602,'chemistry','Alkali metals react with water vigorously to form hydroxides anddihydrogen. Which of the following alkali metals reacts with water least vigorously?','Li','Although lithium has most Electronegative alue (Table 10.1), its reaction with water is less vigorous than that of sodium which has the least Electronegative\nvalue among the alkali metals.\n Both melting point and heat of reaction of alkali metals with water  decrease down the group from Li to Cs. Although the heat of reaction of  Li is the highest, but due to its high melting point, even this heat is \nnot sufficient to melt the metal, which exposes greater surface to water for reaction. As a result, Li has the least reactivity but the reactivity increases as the melting point of alkali metals decreases \ndown the group from Li to Cs.  .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.302','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Li','Na','K','Cs','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101603,'chemistry','The reducing power of a metal depends oh various factors. Suggest the factor which makes Li, the strongest reducing agent in aqueous solution','Hydration enthalpy','Lithium has highest hydration enthalpy which accounts for its high negative E° value and its high reducing power. .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.301 Table 10.1','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Sublimation enthalpy','Ionisation enthalpy','Hydration enthalpy','Electron-gain enthalpy','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101604,'chemistry','Metal carbonates decompose on heating to give metal oxide and  carbon dioxide. Which of the metal carbonates is most stable thermally?','BaCO₃','Thermal stability of metal carbonates increases as the electropositive  character of the metal or the basicity of the metal hydroxide increases  from Be(OH)2 to Ba(OH)2. Thus, BaC03 is the most stable.\n The thermal stability increases with increasing cationic size. .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.309','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','MgCO₃','CaCO₃','SrCO₃','BaCO₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101605,'chemistry','Which of the carbonates given below is unstable in air and is kept in CO₂ atmosphere to avoid decomposition.','BeCO₃','Beryllium carbonate is unstable and can be kept only in the atmosphere of CO2.\nDue to least electropositive character or least basicity of Be, BeC03 is less stable and hence decomposes to give BeO and C02. .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.309','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','BeCO₃','MgCO₃','CaCO₃','BaCO₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101606,'chemistry','Metals form basic hydroxides. Which of the following metal hydroxides is the least basic?','Mg(OH)₂','As the ionization enthalpy increases from Mg →Ba, the M – O bond becomes weaker and weaker down the group and hence basicity increases down the  group. Thus, Mg(OH)2 is least basic.\nThe solubility, thermal stability and the basic character of these hydroxides increase with increasing atomic number from Mg(OH)2 to Ba(OH)2 .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.309','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Mg(OH)₂','Ca(OH)₂','Sr(OH)₂','Ba(OH)₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101607,'chemistry','Some of the Group 2 metal halides are covalent and soluble in  organic solvents. Among the following metal halides, the one which is soluble in ethanol is','BeCl2','Due to small size, high electronegativity and high ionization enthalpy of Be, BeCl2 is covalent and hence most soluble in organic solvents such as ethanol.','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','BeCl2','MgCl2','CaCl2','SrCl2','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101608,'chemistry','The order of decreasing ionization enthalpy in alkali metals is','Li > Na > K > Rb','Ionization enthalpy decreases with increase in Size of the atom in a group This is because the effect of increasing size outweighs the increasing nuclear charge, and the outermost electron is very well screened from the nuclear charge.. Hence, the order is:\nLi > Na > K > Rb.  .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.300','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Na > Li > K > Rb','Rb < Na < K < Li','Li > Na > K > Rb','K < Li < Na < Rb','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101609,'chemistry','The solubility of metal halides depends on their nature, lattice enthalpy and hydration enthalpy of the individual ions. Amongst  fluorides of alkali metals, the lowest solubility of LiF in water is due  to','low ionization enthalpy of lithium atom.','The low solubility of LiF in water is due to its high lattice enthalpy .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.303','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','ionic nature of lithium fluoride','high lattice enthalpy.','high hydration enthalpy for lithium ion.','low ionization enthalpy of lithium atom.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101610,'chemistry','Amphoteric hydroxides react with both alkalies and acids. Which of the following group 2 metal hydroxides is soluble in sodium \nhydroxide?','Be(OH)₂','Beryllium hydroxide is amphoteric in nature as it reacts with acid and alkali both.\n2HCl + Be(OH)₂ →       BeCl₂ + H₂O.\nBe(OH)₂ + 2OH – →      [Be(OH)₄ ]²⁻\n                                         Beryllate ion            .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.309','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Be(OH)₂','Mg(OH)₂','Ca(OH)₂','Ba(OH)₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101611,'chemistry','In the synthesis of sodium carbonate, the recovery of ammonia is done by treating NH₄Cl with Ca(OH)₂. The by-product obtained in this process is','CaCl₂','NH3 is recovered when the solution containing NH 4 Cl is treated with Ca(OH)2 . Calcium chloride is obtained as a by-product .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.305','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CaCl₂','NaCl','NaOH','NaHCO3','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(101612,'chemistry','When sodium is dissolved in liquid ammonia, a solution of deep blue colour is obtained. The colour of the solution is due to','ammoniated electron','The blue colour of the solution is due to the ammoniated electron which absorbs energy in the visible region of light and thus imparts blue colour to the solution.\nM+(x+y)NH3→ M+(NH3)x + e–(NH3)y .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.302','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','ammoniated electron','sodium ion','sodium amide','ammoniated sodium ion','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101613,'chemistry','By adding gypsum to cement','setting time of cement increases.','The raw materials for the manufacture of cement are limestone and clay. When clay and lime are strongly heated together they fuse and react to form ‘cement clinker’. This clinker is mixed with 2-3% by weight of gypsum\n(CaSO₄·2H₂O) to form cement.\nGypsum (CaS04 -2H20) is added to the components to increase the setting time of cement so that it gets sufficiently  hardened. Setting of cement is an exothermic process and involves  hydration of calcium aluminates and , silicates.\n .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.312','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','setting time of cement becomes less.','setting time of cement increases.','colour of cement becomes light.','shining surface is obtained.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101614,'chemistry','Dead burnt plaster is','CaSO₄','Above 393 K, no water of crystallisation is left and anhydrous calcium sulphate, CaSO₄ is formed. This is known as ‘dead burnt plaster’. .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.311','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','CaSO₄','CaSO₄.1/2H₂O','CaSO₄.H₂O','CaSO₄.2H₂O','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101615,'chemistry','Suspension of slaked lime in water is known as','milk of lime','Suspension of slaked lime in water is known as milk of lime.\nThe aqueous solution of Calcium Hydroxide known as lime water and a suspension of slaked lime in water is known as milk of lime .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.311','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','lime water','quick lime','milk of lime','aqueous solution of slaked lime','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101616,'chemistry','Which of the following elements does not form hydride by direct heating with dihydrogen?','Be','Due to high ionization enthalpy and small size, Be does not react with hydrogen by direct heating.','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Be','Mg','Sr','Ba','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101617,'chemistry','The formula of soda ash is','Na₂CO₃','On heating washing soda, it loses its water of crystallization. Above 373 K, it becomes completely anhydrous white powder called soda ash. .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.305','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','NaHCO₃.10H₂O','Na₂CO₃.2H₂O','Na₂CO₃.H₂O','Na₂CO₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101618,'chemistry','A substance which gives brick red flame and breaks down on heating to give oxygen and brown gas is','Calcium nitrate','2Ca(N0₃)₂→2CaO + 4NO₂ + O₂\n\nNO2 is a brown gas. Ca2+ imparts brick red colour to the flame .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.309','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Magnesium nitrate','Calcium nitrate','Barium nitrate','Strontium nitrate','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101619,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is true about Ca(OH)₂?','It is used in the preparation of bleaching powder.','2Ca(OH)₂+ 2Cl₂ ------>CaCl₂ +Ca(OCl)₂+ 2H₂O .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.311','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','It is used in the preparation of bleaching powder.','It is a light blue solid.','It does not possess disinfectant property.','It is used in the manufacture of cement.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101620,'chemistry','A chemical A is used for the preparation of washing soda to recover ammonia. When C02 is bubbled through an aqueous solution of A, the solution tons milky.  It is used in white washing due to disinfectant nature. What is the  chemical formula of A?','Ca(OH)₂','The chemical A used for the preparation of washing soda and which reacts with CO2 to form a milky solution is likely to be calcium hydroxide, Ca(OH)2.\n\nWhen CO2 is bubbled through a solution of calcium hydroxide, it reacts to form calcium carbonate, CaCO3, which is insoluble in water and forms a milky white suspension. This reaction is the basis for the formation of precipitated calcium carbonate (PCC), which is an important industrial chemical.','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ca(CO₃)₂','CaO','Ca(OH)₂','CaC0₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101621,'chemistry','Dehydration of hydrates of halides of.calcium, barium and strontium, i.e., CaCl2.6H20, BaCl2.2H20, SrCl2.2H20, can be achieved by heating. These become wet oh keeping in air. Which \nof the following statements is correct about these halides?','All of the above.','Chlorides of alkaline earth metals are hydrated salts. Due to their  hygroscopic nature, they can be used as dehydrating agents to absorb moisture from air.The tendency to form halide\nhydrates gradually decreases down the group .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.309','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Act as dehydrating agents.','Can absorb moisture from air','Tendency to form hydrate decreases from calcium to barium.','All of the above.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101622,'chemistry','Metallic elements are described by their standard electrode  potential, fusion enthalpy, atomic size, etc. The alkali metals are characterized by which of the following properties\n(a) High boiling point. \n\n(b) High negative standard electrode potential.\n\n(c) High density.\n\n(d) Large atomic size.','b,d','The melting and boiling points of the alkali metals are low indicating weak metallic bonding due to the presence of only a single valence electron in them.\nBecause of the large size, these elements have low density which increases down the group from Li to Cs. However, potassium is lighter than sodium. .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.300','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101623,'chemistry','Several sodium compounds find use in industries. Which of the following compounds are used for textile industry?\n\n(i) Na₂CO₃            \n\n(ii) NaHCO₃             \n\n(iii) NaOH                \n\n(iv) NaCl','i,iii','Na₂CO₃   and NaOH are used in textile industry. .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.305,306','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i,ii','i,ii,iii','i,iii','ii,iv','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101624,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds are readily soluble in water?\n\n(i) BeSO4               \n\n(ii) MgSO4               \n\n(iii) BaSO4                 \n\n(iv) SrSO4','i,ii','the solubility decreases from CaSO4 to BaSO4.The greater hydration enthalpies of Be 2+ and\nMg2+ ions overcome the lattice enthalpy factor and therefore their sulphates are soluble in water .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.309','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','i,ii','ii,iii','iii,iv','i,iii','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101625,'chemistry','When zeolite, which is hydrated sodium aluminium silicate, is \ntreated with hard water, the sodium ions are exchanged with which of the\n following ion(s)?\n\n(i) H+ions                                                \n\n(ii) Mg2+ions’\n\n(iii) Ca2+ ions                                            \n\n(iv) SO₄²⁻  ions','ii,iii','Sodium zeolite removes Ca2+ and Mg2+ ions from hard water. Na₂Z + M2+ → MZ + 2Na+ (M = Ca, Mg) where, Z = Al₂Si₂O8H₂0','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i,ii','ii,iii','iii,iv','i,iii','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101626,'chemistry','Identify the correct formula of halides of alkaline earth metals from the following.\n\n(a) BaCl₂.2H₂O\n\n(b) BaCl₂ .4H₂O\n\n(c) CaCl₂ . 6H₂O\n\n(d) SrCl₂.4H₂O','i,iii','The tendency to form halide hydrates gradually decreases (for example,MgCl 2 ·8H 2 O, CaCl 2 ·6H 2 O, SrCl 2 ·6H 2 O and BaCl2 ·2H2 O) down the group. .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.309','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i,ii','ii,iii','iii,iv','i,iii','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101627,'chemistry','Choose the correct statements from the following.\n(i) Beryllium is not readily attacked by acids because of the presence of an oxide film on the surface of the metal.\n\n(ii) Beryllium sulphate is readily soluble in water as the greater hydration enthalpy of Be2+ overcomes the lattice enthalpy factor.\n\n(iii) Beryllium exhibits coordination number more than four.\n\n(iv) Beryllium oxide is purely acidic in nature.','i,ii','(i) Beryllium is not readily attacked by acids because of the presence of an oxide film on the surface of the metal.\n\n(ii) Beryllium sulphate is readily soluble in water as the greater hydration enthalpy of Be2+ overcomes the lattice enthalpy factor.\n\niii.Beryllium does not exhibit coordination number more than four as in its valence shell there are only four orbitals.\n \niv.) The oxide and hydroxide of beryllium, unlike the hydroxides of other elements in the group, are amphoteric in nature. .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.310','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','i,ii','ii,iii','iii,iv','i,iii','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101628,'chemistry','Which of the following are the correct reasons for anomalous behaviour of lithium?\n(i) Exceptionally small size of its atom\n\n(ii) Its high polarising power\n\n(iii) It has high degree of hydration\n\n(iv) Exceptionally low ionisation enthalpy','i,ii','The anomalous behaviour of lithium is due to the : (i) exceptionally small size of its atom and ion, and (ii) high polarising power (i.e., charge/ radius ratio). .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.304','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i,ii','ii,iii','iii,iv','i,iii','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101629,'chemistry','Assertion (A): The carbonate of lithium decomposes easily on heating to form lithium oxide and CO₂ .\n\nReason (R) : Lithium being very small in size polarises large carbonate ion leading to the formation of more stable Li₂O and CO₂.','Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A.','Lithium is least reactive but the strongest reducing agent among all the alkali metals.\nOn combustion in air Lithium forms mainly monoxide, Li2O and the nitride, Li3N unlike other alkali metals. .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.304','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct but R is not the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are not correct','A is not correct but R is correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101630,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Beryllium carbonate is kept in the atmosphere of carbon dioxide.\n\nReason (R) : Beryllium carbonate is unstable and decomposes to give beryllium oxide and carbon dioxide.','Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A.','Beryllium carbonate is unstable and can be kept only in the atmosphere of CO 2. The thermal stability increases with increasing cationic size. .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.309','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct but R is not the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are not correct','A is not correct but R is correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101631,'chemistry','Match the elements given in Column Iwith the properties mentioned in Column II. Refer diagram(n1004)','(i)→ (c), (ii)→ (b), (iii)→(d), (iv)→(a), (e)','Li – Most negative E° among alkali metals Due to very high hydration energy the resulting E° is most negative\n\n(ii) Na – Strongest monoacidic base  .Alkalies are more acidic than alkaline earth metals. LiOH has covalent character\n\n(iii) Ca – insoluble oxalate .Calcium oxalate is insoluble in water\n\n(iv) Ba – Insoluble sulphate .Hydration energy decreases as size of cation increases .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.309','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i)→ (c), (ii)→ (b), (iii)→(d), (iv)→(a), (e)','(i)→ (b), (ii)→ (c), (iii)→(d), (iv)→(a), (e)','(i)→ (c), (ii)→ (d), (iii)→(b), (iv)→(a), (e)','(i)→ (e), (ii)→ (b), (iii)→(d), (iv)→(a), (c)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/101631q.png'),
(101632,'chemistry','Match the compounds given in Column I with their uses mentioned in Column II.\nColumn I                                                                                                      Column II\n(i) CaCO3                                                                                           (a) Dentistry, ornamental work\n(ii) Ca(OH)2                                                                                       (b) Manufacture of sodium carbonate from caustic soda\n(iii) CaO                                                                                              (c) Manufacture of high quality paper\n(iv) CaSO4                                                                                         (d) Used in white washing','(i)→ (c), (ii) → (d), (iii) → (b), (iv) → (a)','CaCO3 – Manufacture of high quality paper\n\nCa(OH)2 – Used in white washing\n\n CaO – Manufacture of sodium carbonate from caustic soda\n\n CaS04 – Dentistry, ornamental work .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.311','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(i)→ (b), (ii) → (d), (iii) → (d), (iv) → (a)','(i)→ (c), (ii) → (d), (iii) → (b), (iv) → (a)','(i)→ (d), (ii) → (c), (iii) → (b), (iv) → (a)','(i)→ (c), (ii) → (d), (iii) → (a), (iv) → (b)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101633,'chemistry','Match the elements given in Column I with the colour they impart to the\nflame given in Column II. Refer diagram(n1003)','(i)→ (f), (ii) → (d), (iii) → (b), (iv) → (c) ,(v)→ (e), (vi) → (a)','Cs – Blue\n\n Na-Yellow\n\nK- Violet\n\n Ca-Brick red\n\n Sr – Crimson red  \n\n Ba – Apple green  .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.300 and 308','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(i)→ (f), (ii) → (d), (iii) → (b), (iv) → (c) ,(v)→ (e), (vi) → (a)','(i)→ (f), (ii) → (d), (iii) → (b), (iv) → (c) ,(v)→ (a), (vi) → (e)','(i)→ (f), (ii) → (d), (iii) → (a), (iv) → (c), (v)→ (e), (vi) → (b)','(i)→ (f), (ii) → (d), (iii) → (b), (iv) → (e), (v)→ (c), (vi) → (a)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/101633q.png'),
(101634,'chemistry','When heated in air, the alkali metals form various oxides. Which is the oxides formed by Li','Li₂O','On combustion in air Li forms mainly monoxide, Li2O and the nitride, Li3N unlike other alkali metals .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.304','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Li₂O₂','LiO₂','Li₂O','LiO','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101635,'chemistry','When heated in air, the alkali metals form various oxides. Which is the oxides formed by Na','Na₂O₂+NaO₂','On combustion in excess of air sodium forms the peroxide, Na₂O₂ (and some superoxide NaO₂ ) .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.303','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Na₂O₂+Na₂O','Na₂O','NaO₂','Na₂O₂+NaO₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101636,'chemistry','When heated in air, the alkali metals form various oxides. Which is the oxides formed by K','K₂O₂+K₂O+KO₂','On combustion potassium  form the superoxides KO₂, oxide(K₂O), peroxide (K₂O₂)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','K₂O₂+K₂O','K₂O₂+K₂O+KO₂','K₂O','K₂O₂+KO₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101637,'chemistry','Why do beryllium and magnesium not impart colour to the flame in the flame test?','very high ionization energy in comparison to other alkaline earth metals','The electrons in beryllium and magnesium are too strongly bound to get excited by flame. Hence, these elements do not impart any colour to the flame. .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.308','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Large atomic size','very high ionization energy in comparison to other alkaline earth metals','Low melting point','High Electropositive nature','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101638,'chemistry','An element from Group 2 which forms an amphoteric oxide and a water soluble sulphate.','Be','Beryllium hydroxide is amphoteric in nature as it reacts with acid and alkali both. .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.309','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Mg','Be','Sr','Ca','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101639,'chemistry','Why are BeSO4 and MgSO4 readily soluble in water while CaSO4, SrSO4 and BaSO4 are insoluble.','Very high hydration enthalpy','BeSO4 , and MgSO4 are readily soluble in water; the solubility decreases from CaSO4 to BaSO4.The greater hydration enthalpies of Be 2+ and\nMg2+ ions overcome the lattice enthalpy factor and therefore their sulphates are soluble in water. .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.309','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','High lattice enthalpy','Very high hydration enthalpy','High Ionisation enthalpy','High Electropositive nature','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101640,'chemistry','All compounds of alkali metals are easily soluble in water but lithium compounds are more soluble in organic solvents.Why?','Both A and B','(i) exceptionally small size of its atom and ion, and (ii) high polarising power (i.e., charge/ radius ratio). As a result, there is increased\ncovalent character of lithium compounds which is responsible for their solubility in organic solvents .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.304','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','exceptionally small size of its atom','high polarising power','Both A and B','least reactivity','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
