(101706,'chemistry','Statement I : None of the alkaline earth metal hydroxides dissolve in alkali.\n\nStatement II : Solubility of alkaline earth metal hydroxides in water increases down the group','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is\ncorrect.','The solubility, thermal stability and the\nbasic character of these hydroxides increase\nwith increasing atomic number from Mg(OH) 2\nto Ba(OH) 2 .  .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.309','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year JEE Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is\ncorrect.','Statement I and Statement II both are\nincorrect.','Statement I and Statement II both are correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101707,'chemistry','Match List I with List II :Refer diagram(n1001)','(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)','Cs           Poor water solubility of I– salt\n K            Most abundant element in cell fluid\n Na          Bicarbonate salt used in fire extinguisher\n Li            Carbonate salt decomposes easily on heating','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year JEE Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)','(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)','(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)','(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/101707q.png'),
(101708,'chemistry','Assertion A : Lithium halides are some what\ncovalent in nature.\n\nReason R : Lithium possess high polarisation\ncapability.','Both A  and R are correct R is correct  explanation of A','lithium halides are somewhat covalent. It is because of the high polarisation capability of lithium ion (The distortion of electron cloud of the anion by the cation is called polarisation)..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.302','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year JEE Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','Both A  and R are correct R is NOT correct explanation of A','Both A  and R are correct R is correct  explanation of A','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101709,'chemistry','Match the column. Refer diagram(n1005)','A- P , B- S , C- Q , D- R','Ca(OCl)₂   -Bleach ;Milk of lime reacts with chlorine to formhypochlorite, a constituent of bleaching powder\n(B) CaO  - Cement ;It is an important primary material for manufacturing cement\n(C) CaCO3 - Antacid ; It is also used as an antacid                                                                            \n(D) CaSO4.1/2 H2O  -  Plaster of paris                                                .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.311','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year JEE Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','A- P , B- S , C- Q , D- R','A- P , B- Q , C- S , D- R','A- P , B- S , C- R , D- Q','A- S , B- Q, C- R , D- P','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/101709q.png'),
(101710,'chemistry','Match the column. Refer diagram(n1006)','(A) -(R) ; (B) -(S) ; (C) -(Q) ; (D) -(P)','Radium salts are used in radiotherapy, for example, in the treatment of cancer \nMetallic beryllium is used for making windows of X-ray tubes\nMagnesium (powder and ribbon) is used in flash powders and bulbs, incendiary bombs and signals\nCalcium is used in the extraction of metals from oxides which are difficult to reduce with carbon.\n.Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.308','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year JEE Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(A) -(R) ; (B) -(S) ; (C) -(Q) ; (D) -(P)','(A) -(P) ; (Q) -(S) ; (C) -(Q) ; (D) -(R)','(A) -(P) ; (B) -(Q) ; (C) -(R) ; (D) -(S)','(A) -(R) ; (B) -(Q) ; (C) -(S) ; (D) -(P)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/101710q.png'),
(101711,'chemistry','Which of the following order of melting point is correct','LiF > LiCl , NaCl < MgO','Melting point is directly proportional to lattice energy \nm.p directly proportional to Lattice energy directly proportional to Charge/size\nSo, as both LiCl and LiF are having the same charge so the melting point would depend upon the size.\n MgO is having +2  and  NaCl is having +1  charge, so the melting point  is  MgO greater than that of NaCl','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year JEE Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','LiF > LiCl , NaCl > MgO','LiF < LiCl , NaCl > MgO','LiF > LiCl , NaCl < MgO','LiF < LiCl , NaCl > MgO','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101712,'chemistry','How many of the following metals are used in photoelectric cell?\nLi, Na, Rb, Cs','1','Caesium is used in devising photoelectric cells..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.303','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year JEE Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101713,'chemistry','Among the following compounds how many are amphoteric in nature\nBe(OH)₂   , BeO,  Ba(OH)₂   , Sr(OH)₂','2','Be(OH)2, BeO\nBeryllium hydroxide is amphoteric in nature as it reacts with acid and alkali both.\nBeO is amphoteric while oxides of other elements are ionic in nature. \n.Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.309','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year JEE Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101714,'chemistry','In the following reactions, product (A) and (B), respectively, are:\nNaOH + Cl2 -> (A) + side products (hot & conc.)\n\nCa(OH)2+ Cl2 -> (B) + side products (dry)','NaClO3 and Ca(OCl)2','6NaOH + 3Cl2 -> 5NaCl + NaClO3 + 3H2O\n\n2Ca(OH)2 + Cl2 -> Ca(OCl)2 + CaCl2 + H2O','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year JEE Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','NaClO3 and Ca(ClO3)2','NaOCl and Ca(ClO3)2','NaOCl and Ca(OCl)2','NaClO3 and Ca(OCl)2','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101715,'chemistry','When gypsum is heated to 393K, it forms:','CaSO4. 1/2 H2O','CaSO4. 1/2 H2O hemihydrate of calcium sulphate  is obtained when gypsum, CaSO4·2H 2 O, is heated to 393 K.\n.Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.311','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Previous Year JEE Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CaSO4. 1/2 H2O','Dead burnt plaster','CaSO4 5H2O','Anhydrous CaSO4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101901,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of K in KO2','1','The superoxide species is represented as\nO2–; since the compound is neutral,therefore, the oxidation state of potassium is +1..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.301','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Intext and Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1','4','-1','-2','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101902,'chemistry','The E° for Cl2/Cl– is +1.36, for I2/I– is + 0.53, for Ag+ /Ag is +0.79, Na+ /Na is –2.71 and for Li+ /Li is – 3.04.\n Arrange the following ionic species in decreasing order of reducing strength:\nI–, Ag, Cl–, Li, Na','Li > Na > I– > Ag > Cl–','high negative E° value indicate  high reducing power..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.302','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Intext and Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Li > I– >  Na > Ag > Cl–','Li > Na > I– > Ag > Cl–','Li > Na > I– > Cl– > Ag','Li > Cl– >  Ag > I– > Na','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101903,'chemistry','Why is KO2 paramagnetic','one unpaired electron in π*2p molecular orbital of O₂²⁻','The superoxide O2– is paramagnetic because of one unpaired electron in π*2p molecular orbital.Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.303','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Intext and Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','one unpaired electron in π2p molecular orbital of O₂²⁻','one unpaired electron in π*2p molecular orbital of O₂²⁻','K+ contains paired electrons','KO₂ is a peroxide','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101904,'chemistry','Why does the solubility of alkaline earth\nmetal hydroxides in water increase down\nthe group?','Because the lattice enthalpy decreases much more than the hydration enthalpy with increasing ionic size.','Among alkaline earth metal hydroxides, the anion being common the cationic radius will influence the lattice enthalpy. Since lattice enthalpy decreases much\nmore than the hydration enthalpy with increasing ionic size, the solubility increases as we go down the group.Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.310','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Intext and Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Because solubility increases with an increase in temperature','Because the anion is common in alkaline earth metal hydroxides','Because the lattice enthalpy decreases much more than the hydration enthalpy with increasing ionic size.','The hydration enthalpy is increasing','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101905,'chemistry','Why does the solubility of alkaline earth metal carbonates and sulphates in water decrease down the group?','The size of anions being much larger compared to cations, the lattice  enthalpy will remain almost constant within a particular group so hydrationenthalpies decrease down the group.','The size of anions being much larger compared to cations, the lattice enthalpy will remain almost constant within a particular group. Since the hydration enthalpies decrease down the group,solubility will decrease as found for alkaline earth metal carbonates and sulphates.Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.310','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Intext and Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Solubility will increase as found for alkaline earth metal carbonates and sulphates','Boiling points increasing down the group','The size of cations being much smaller compared to anions, the lattice enthalpy will remain almost constant within a particular group so hydration enthalpies increases down the group.','The size of anions being much larger compared to cations, the lattice  enthalpy will remain almost constant within a particular group so hydrationenthalpies decrease down the group.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101906,'chemistry','Which of the alkali metal is having least melting point ?','Cs','As the size of metal increases, the metallic bonding decreases and melting point decreases.','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Intext and Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Na','K','Rb','Cs','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101907,'chemistry','Which one of the following alkali metals gives hydrated salts ?','Li','Lithium has highest number of hydrated salts Due to small size of Li, it has high charge density that attracts water molecules strongly.','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Intext and Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Li','Na','K','Cs','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101908,'chemistry','Which one of the alkaline earth metal carbonates is thermally the most stable ?','BaCO3','CO₃²⁻ (Carbonate) being a bigger anion stabilises a bigger cation more efficiently. Thus, Ba2+ being bigger in size is the most stable.','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Intext and Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','MgCO3','CaCO3','SrCO3','BaCO3','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101909,'chemistry','The number molecules of water crystallised in Nitrate salts of Mg and Ba are respectively','6,Anhydrous Salt','Magnesium nitrate crystallises with six molecules of water, whereas barium nitrate crystallises as the anhydrous salt. This again shows a decreasing tendency to form hydrates\nwith increasing size and decreasing hydration enthalpy. .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.309','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Intext and Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','4,6','6,2','5,5','6,Anhydrous Salt','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101910,'chemistry','Match the Following-','does not exhibit coordination number more than four-Be,absorbs moisture and carbon dioxide-CaO,salts are used in the treatment of cancer-Ra,Found in high concentration inside RBC-K','Beryllium does not exhibit coordination number more than four as in its valence shell there are only four orbitals..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.310\nCalcium oxide On exposure to atmosphere, it absorbs moisture and carbon dioxide.Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.310\nRadium salts are used in radiotherapy, for example, in the treatment of cancer.Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.309\n10 mmolL–1 (Na+) and 105 mmolL–1 (K+). These ionic gradients Is due to the sodium-potassium pump,that operates across the cell membranes.Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.306','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> NCERT Intext and Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','match','does not exhibit coordination number more than four,CaO','absorbs moisture and carbon dioxide,Be','salts are used in the treatment of cancer,K','Found in high concentration inside RBC,Rb','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102001,'chemistry','Assertion: Potassium and caesium are used in photoelectric cells. \n\nReason: Potassium and caesium emit electrons on exposure to light.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Due to very low ionisation potential so they emit electrons on exposure to light this property makes caesium and potassium\nuseful as electrodes in photoelectric cells..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.301','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102002,'chemistry','Assertion : BeSO4 is soluble in water while BaSO4 is not.\n\nReason : Hydration energy decreases down the group from Be to Ba and lattice energy remains almost constant.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The solubility decreases from CaSO4 to BaSO4  .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.309','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102003,'chemistry','Assertion : The ionization enthalpies of alkali metals increase progressively as we move down the group form Li to Cs.\n\n\n\nReason : The ionic radii increase as we move down the group from Li to Cs.','Assertion is False and Reason is true','The ionization enthalpies of alkali metals decrease\nprogressively as we move down the group from Li to Cs.','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102004,'chemistry','Assertion : Lithium salts are hydrated.\nReason : Lithium has higher polarising power than other alkali metal group members.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Li+ has maximum degree of hydration due to\nits small size and for this reason lithium salts are mostly\nhydrated.','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102005,'chemistry','Assertion : s-block elements are highly electropositive.\nReason : The valence electrons present in s-orbital are loosely held.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The loosely held s-electron in the outermost valence\nshell of these elements makes them the most electropositive\nmetals which readily give ions, M+ or M2+.','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102006,'chemistry','Assertion : The carbonate of lithium decomposes easily on heating to form lithium oxide and CO2.\n\nReason : Lithium being very small in size polarises large carbonate ion leading to the formation of more stable Li2O and CO2.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Lithium carbonate is not so stable to heat; lithium being very small in size polarises a\nlarge CO32– ion leading to the formation of more stable Li2O and CO2..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.303','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102007,'chemistry','Assertion : Generally alkali and alkaline earth metals form superoxides.\n\nReason : There is a single bond between O and O in superoxides.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Generally alkali and alkaline earth metals form superoxides.In superoxides, there is a three-electron bond between two O atoms. \nsingle bond between O and O is usually seen in Peroxides','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102008,'chemistry','Assertion:The solubility of the alkaline earth metal hydroxides in water increases with increase in atomic number down the group\n\nReason : The alkaline earth metal hydroxides are basic in character. The only exception is Be(OH)2 which is amphoteric.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','The solubility of the alkaline earth metal hydroxides\nin H2O increases with increase in atomic number down the\ngroup. This is due to the reason that both lattice energy and\nhydration energy decrease down the group as the size of the\ncation increases but lattice energy decreases more rapidly\nthan the hydration energy and hence, their solubility increases\ndown the group.','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102009,'chemistry','Assertion : Electrode potential is a measure of the tendency of an  element to lose electrons in the aqueous solution. So, the reducing property can be correlated in terms of electrode potentials (E°) of alkali metals.\n\nReason : More negative is the electrode potential, higher is the tendency of the element to loose electrons and hence, stronger is the reducing agent.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The standard electrode potential (E0) which measures the reducing power..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.302','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102010,'chemistry','Assertion : Beryllium carbonate is kept in the atmosphere of carbon dioxide.\n\nReason : Beryllium carbonate is unstable and decomposes to give beryllium oxide and carbon dioxide.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Beryllium carbonate is unstable and can be kept only in the atmosphere of CO2..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.309','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102011,'chemistry','Assertion : The first element (Li) of group I differs considerably from the rest of the elements of the same group.\n\nReason : Lithium has small ionic and atomic radii, high  electronegativity and ionization enthalpy, high polarising power of its  cation and absence of d-electrons in its valence shell compared to rest of the elements of the same group.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The anomalous behaviour of lithium is due to the : (i) exceptionally small size of its atom and ion, and (ii) high polarising power.Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.304','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102012,'chemistry','Assertion (A): The\ncarbonate of lithium decomposes easily on heating to form lithium oxide andCO2.\n\nReason (R): Lithium being\nvery small in size polarises large carbonate ion leading to the formation of\nmore stableLi2Oand CO2','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Unlike other alkali metal carbonates; the carbonate of lithium decomposes on heating to form its oxide. Its oxide is stablised by polarisation.','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102013,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Lithium and beryllium exhibit some properties that are different from other elements in their respective groups.\n\nReason (R): Lithium and beryllium resemble second elements from the following  group in their properties, resulting in a diagonal relationship in the  periodic table.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','The diagonal relationship is due to the similarity in ionic sizes and /or charge/radius ratio of the elements..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.300','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102014,'chemistry','A: Sodium and potassium are abundant among the alkali metals.\nR: Lithium, rubidium and caesium have much lower abundances','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Among the alkali metals sodium and potassium are abundant and lithium, rubidium and caesium have much lower abundances.Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.209','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102015,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Francium is highly abundant and is not radioactive. \nReason (R): Francium is highly radioactive; its longest-lived isotope ²²³Fr has a half-life of only 21 minutes.','Assertion is False and Reason is true','Francium is highly radioactive; its longest-lived isotope 223Fr has a half-life of only 21 minutes. .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.209','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102016,'chemistry','Assertion(A): The ionization enthalpies of the alkali metals are considerably low.\n\nReason(R): They decrease down the group from Li to Cs.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','The ionization enthalpies of the alkali metals are considerably low and decrease down the group from Li to Cs. This is because the effect of increasing size outweighs the increasing nuclear charge, and the outermost electron is\nvery well screened from the nuclear charge.Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.300','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102017,'chemistry','Assertion(A): The alkali metals are the most electropositive metals.\n\nReason(R): They have a loosely held s-electron in the outermost valence shell.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The loosely held s-electron in the outermost valence shell of these elements makes them the most electropositive metals. .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.300','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102018,'chemistry','Assertion(A): Alkali metals are never found in free state in nature.\n\nReason(R): They are the most electropositive metals and readily lose electrons.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Alkali metals readily lose electron to give monovalent M+ ions. Hence they are never found in free state in nature..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.300','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102019,'chemistry','Assertion(A): The monovalent ions of alkali metals are smaller than the parent atoms.\n\nReason(R): The atomic and ionic radii of alkali metals increase on moving down the group.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','The monovalent ions (M+) are smaller than the parent atom. A cation has a fewer number of electrons than its parent atom, while its nuclear charge remains the same. So due to stronger force of nucleus The cation size decreases\nThe atomic and ionic radii of alkali metals increase on moving down the group .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.300','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102020,'chemistry','Assertion(A): The ionization enthalpies of the alkali metals decrease  down the group from Li to Cs.\n\nReason(R): The effect of increasing size outweighs the increasing  nuclear charge, and the outermost electron is well screened from the  nuclear charge.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The ionization enthalpies of the alkali metals are considerably low and decrease down the group from Li to Cs. This is because the effect of increasing size outweighs the increasing nuclear charge, and the outermost electron is\nvery well screened from the nuclear charge.Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.300','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102021,'chemistry','Assertion(A): The outermost electron of the alkali metals is very well screened from the nuclear charge.\n\nReason(R): The effect of increasing size outweighs the increasing nuclear charge.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','the effect of increasing size outweighs the increasing nuclear charge, and the outermost electron is very well screened from the nuclear charge..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.300','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102022,'chemistry','Assertion (A): All the alkali metals are soft and light metals with low density.\n\nReason (R): Due to the large size of alkali metals, they have low density which increases down the group from Li to Cs.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','All the alkali metals are silvery white, soft and light metals. Because of the large size, these elements have low density which increases down the group from Li to Cs..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.300','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102023,'chemistry','Assertion (A): The melting and boiling points of the alkali metals are  low.\nReason (R): weak metallic bonding exsist in alkali metals resulting from the presence of only a  single valence electron','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The melting and boiling points of the alkali metals are low indicating weak metallic bonding due to the presence of only a single valence electron in them..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.300','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102024,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Alkali metals impart characteristic colour to an  oxidizing flame.\n\nReason (R): The heat from the flame excites the outermost orbital  electron to a higher energy level, causing emission of radiation in the  visible region of the spectrum.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The alkali metals and their salts impart characteristic colour to an oxidizing flame. This is because the heat from the flame excites the outermost orbital electron to a higher energy level. When the excited electron comes back to the ground state, there is emission of radiation in the visible region of the spectrum ..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.300','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102025,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Alkali metals are highly reactive due to their large size and low ionization enthalpy.\n\nReason (R): The reactivity of alkali metals increases down the group','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','The alkali metals are highly reactive due to their large size and low ionization enthalpy. The reactivity of these metals increases down the group.Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.301','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102026,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Alkali metals tarnish in moist air.\n\nReason (R): The alkali metals tarnish in moist air due to the formation of their oxides which in turn react with moisture to form hydroxides.','Assertion is False and Reason is False','The alkali metals tarnish in dry air due to the formation of their oxides which in turn react with moisture to form hydroxides..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.301','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is False','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102027,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Alkali metals burn vigorously in oxygen.\n\nReason (R): Alkali metals burn in oxygen, forming oxides. Lithium forms  monoxide, sodium forms peroxide, and the other metals form superoxides.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','They burn vigorously in oxygen forming oxides.The high reactive due to their large size and low ionization enthalpy. \nLithium forms monoxide, sodium forms peroxide, the other metals form superoxides..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.301','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102028,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Kerosene oil is used to store alkali metals.\n\nReason (R): Because of their high reactivity towards air and water, they are normally kept in kerosene oil.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Because of their high reactivity towards air and water, alkali metals are normally kept in kerosene oil..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.301','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102029,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Lithium reacts less vigorously with water than sodium.\n\nReason (R): Lithium has a smaller size and higher hydration energy compared to sodium.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Li reaction with water is less vigorous than that of sodium which has the least negative E° value among the alkali metals. This behaviour of lithium is attributed to its small size and very high hydration energy..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.302','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102030,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Potassium has a vital role in biological systems.\n\nReason (R): Potassium  chloride and potassium hydroxide are used in various  applications.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Potassium has a vital role in biological systems. Potassium\nchloride is used as a fertilizer. Potassium hydroxide is used in the manufacture of soft soap..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.303','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102031,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Lithium halides are more covalent than other halides.\n\nReason (R): Lithium has a small ion size and Low polarisation capability, making it less  distortion of the electron cloud of the  halide ion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Lithium halides are somewhat covalent.\nThe Li+ ion is very small in size and has high tendency to distort electron cloud around the negative halide ion. Since anion with large size can be easily distorted, among halides,lithium iodide is the most covalent in\nnature.Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.302','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102032,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Alkali metals dissolve in liquid ammonia to form blue  solutions.\n\nReason (R): The blue colour of the solution is due to the ammoniated  electron which absorbs energy in the visible region of light.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The alkali metals dissolve in liquid ammonia giving deep blue solutions which are conducting in nature.The blue colour of the solution is due to the ammoniated electron which absorbs energy in the visible region of light and thus imparts blue colour to the solution..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.302','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102033,'chemistry','Assertion (A): The alkali metals dissolved in liquid ammonia are conducting in nature.\n\nReason (R): The electrical conductivity of the solution is due ammoniated electron only.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','The alkali metals dissolve in liquid ammonia giving deep blue solutions which are conducting in nature.The electrical conductivity of the solution is due to both ammoniated cations and ammoniated electrons.They conduct electricity as they are charged particles and can freely move in the solution.Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.302','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102034,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Caesium is used in devising photoelectric cells.\n\nReason (R):The ionization enthalpies of the Cs are considerably low','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Alkali metals elements when irradiated with light, the light energy absorbed may be sufficient to make an atom lose electronThis property makes caesium and potassium useful as electrodes in photoelectric cells..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.302','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102035,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Cement When mixed with water,sets fast to  give a hard mass\n\nReason (R):The hydration of the molecules of the constituents and their rearrangementon addition of water','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Cement When mixed with water, the setting of cement takes place to give a hard mass. This is due to the hydration of the molecules of the constituents and their\nrearrangement..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.312','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102036,'chemistry','Assertion (A): The alkaline earth metals form compounds which are predominantly ionic\n\nReason (R):Increased nuclear charge and smaller size.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The alkaline earth metals form compounds whichare predominantly ionic but less ionic than the corresponding compounds of alkali metals. This is due to increased nuclear charge and smaller size..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.309','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102037,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Beryllium combine with hydrogen upon heating to form BeH2\n\nReason (R):BeH2 can be prepared by the reaction of BeCl2 with LiAlH4','Assertion is False and Reason is true','All the elements except beryllium combine with hydrogen upon heating to form their hydrides,\nMH2.\nBeH2, however, can be prepared by the reaction of BeCl2 with LiAlH4..Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.308','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(102038,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Beryllium and magnesium are kinetically inert to oxygen and water\n\nReason (R): There is a formation of an oxide film over Beryllium and magnesiumon  surface.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Beryllium and magnesium are kinetically inert to oxygen and water because of the formation of an oxide film on their surface. .Reference Class 11 Chapter 10 The s-block elements Page No.308','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Advanced Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true and Reason is false','Assertion is False and Reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
