(250650,'chemistry','Oxidation of phenol with chromic acid produces a conjugated diketone known as?','Benzoquinone','Oxidation of phenol with chromic acid produces a conjugated diketone known as benzoquinone.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 343; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Alcohols and Phenols 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Benzene','Benzoquinone','Hydroxybenzoic Acid','2,4,6-trinitrophenol','NEW','12-343','12-213',NULL),
(250651,'chemistry','In the presence of air, phenols are slowly oxidised to dark coloured mixtures containing ____________.','Quinones','In the presence of air, phenols are slowly oxidised to dark coloured mixtures containing quinones.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 343; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Alcohols and Phenols 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Quinones','Cyclic Alcohol','Cyclic Alkanes','Cyclic Alkanes','NEW','12-343','12-213',NULL),
(250652,'chemistry','Identify the final product in the following reaction: in the given \ndiagram(n2510)','Addition Reaction','Solution: The following reaction is the Kolbe’s Reaction – Treating phenol with sodium hydroxide generates phenoxide ion. The mechanism of Kolbe reaction proceeds through the nucleophilic addition of phenoxide to carbon dioxide, yielding the salicylate.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 342; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Alcohols and Phenols 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Elimination Reaction','Substitution Reaction','Addition Reaction','Rearrangement Reaction','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250652q.png'),
(250891,'chemistry','The most reactive compound among the following towards electrophilic aromatic substitution reaction is: - diagram(250891Q)','1','Hint: In toluene, if the electron-donating group is attached at ortho or para position then the reactivity of toluene increases.\n\nAs the electron-donating groups are attached at the ortho and para position of toluene, the electron density of toluene increases. If an electron-withdrawing group is present then the electron density of toluene decreases and the reactivity of toluene decreases.\n\nIn o-cresol, the OH group is attached which increases the electron density of the benzene ring and the reactivity of o-cresol\n\nincreases toward electrophilic aromatic substitution reaction.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-341','12-211','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250891q.png'),
(250892,'chemistry','which of the following is incorrect reaction sequence to prepare phenol? - diagram(250892Q)','3','Chlorobenzene does not react with NaOH at STP condition. It reacts with NaOH at high pressure and temperature. Thus, in the third reaction phenol is not obtained as a product.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-332','12-202','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250892q.png'),
(250893,'chemistry','consider the following reaction and identify the product (P) : - diagram(250893Q)','2','Hint: Rearrangement of carbocation\n\nWhen alcohol reacts with HBr then substitution reaction takes place and the OH group is substituted by the Br atom. In the first step, the OH group is converted into an H2O group, and a secondary carbocation is formed as a product. Then rearrangement of carbocation takes place and more stable tertiary carbocation is obtained. \nIn the first step, tertiary carbocation reacts with bromide ion, and an alkyl halide is obtained as a product.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-330','12-200','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250893q.png'),
(260234,'chemistry','Write the correct IUPAC NAME of the given compound in diagram?(n26005)','4-oxopentanal','The IUPAC names of open chain aliphatic aldehydes and ketones are derived from the names of the corresponding alkanes by replacing the ending –e with –al and –one respectively. \n\nIn case of aldehydes the longest carbon chain is numbered starting from the carbon of the aldehyde group while in case of ketones the numbering begins from the end nearer to the carbonyl group.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-359 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'3','mcq','4-pentanone','4-oxopentanal','4-aldopentanone','2-oxopentanal','NEW','12-359','12-229','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260234q.png'),
(250895,'chemistry','match the list 1 with list 2: - Refer diagram (250895Q) and choose the correct answer from the options :','3','Hint: Phenol undergoes oxidation and form benzoquinone\n\n1. When phenol reacts with CO2 in the presence of a base then an electrophilic substitution reaction takes place. In this reaction, CO2 acts as an electrophile. Phenol with CO2 in the presence of base forms an intermediate, in the next step intermediate is acidified with acid and a salicylic acid or a Ortho hydroxybenzoic acid is obtained as a product. \n\nThe reaction is known as Kolbe’s reaction.     2. When phenol is treated with chloroform in the presence of sodium hydroxide, a –CHO group is introduced at ortho position of a benzene ring. This reaction is known as Reimer - Tiemann reaction. The intermediate substituted benzal chloride is hydrolyzed in the presence of alkali to produce salicylaldehyde.   3. When phenol is heated with zinc dust then benzene is obtained as a product. 4. Oxidation of phenol with chromic acid produces a conjugated diketone known as benzoquinone.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-343','12-213','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250895q.png'),
(250896,'chemistry','which of the following is correct ? Refer diagram (250896Q)','both 1 and 2 are correct','Hint: In polar solvent, tribromophenol is obtained on bromination\n\nThe reaction of phenol with Br2 depends on the solvent system. If the polar solvent is used then bromination occurs at all the ortho and para positions. \n\nIf a solvent is non-polar like CS2 or CHCl3 (CHCl3 has a dipole moment of 1.08 D)then bromination at only at the ortho or para position.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1 is correct but 2 is incorrect','1 is incorrect but 2 is correct','both 1 and 2 are correct','both 1 and 2 are incorrect','NEW','12-342','12-212','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250896q.png'),
(250897,'chemistry','which of the following is not a product of dehydration of - diagram (250897Q) diagram(250897Q-2) ?','2','Hint: Carbocation based E1 elimination\nExplanation:\nE1 = 2 step reaction\nsatzeff elimination  - diagram(250897E)\nProduct b cannot be obtained by the dehydration of the given alcohol as it will involve one tert carbocation into second tert carbocation. There is no significant difference in the stability of these carbocations. These two carbocations are shown below.   - diagram(250897E-2)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250897q.png'),
(250898,'chemistry','given below are two statements - diagram(250898Q) chhose the correect option :','1 is correct but 2 is incorrect','Hint: Reaction follows SN1 mechanism\n\nIn the Lucas test, primary, secondary, and tertiary alcohols are distinguished on the basis of their reactivity with conc. \nH\nC\nl\n+\nZ\nn\nC\nl\n2\n , known as Lucas Reagent. \n\nTertiary alcohol gives immediate white turbidity with Lucas reagent, secondary alcohol gives white turbidity in 5 minutes, and primary alcohol does not react with Lucas reagent at room temperature.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1 is incorrect but 2 is correct','both 1 and 2 are correct','both 1 and 2 are incorrect','1 is correct but 2 is incorrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250898q.png'),
(250899,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is correct about stability of ions of ethanol and phenol:','Delocalization of pi electrons in phenoxide ion stabilize the ion','The ion of phenol which is known as phenoxide ion is resonance stabilized. In phenoxide ion, delocalisation of π-electrons takes place.\nBut in ethoxide ion it does not happen.\n\nHence, option one is the correct answer.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','Delocalization of pi electrons in phenoxide ion stabilize the ion','Delocalization of pi electrons in ethoxide ion stabilize the ion','Inductive effect of phenyl and ethyl group stabilize the ions','Localization of sigma electrons in phenoxide ion stabilizes the ion','NEW','12-342','12-212',NULL),
(250900,'chemistry','compound A on reaction with Na gives compund B and with PCl5 gives C. B and C react together to give diethyl ether . A,Band C are respectively :','C2H5OH,C2H5ONa, C2H5Cl','Hint: The third step is an example of the Williamson synthesis reaction.\n\nCompound A is alcohol. When alcohol reacts with Na then sodium alkoxide is formed as a product (B). When alcohol A\n\nreacts with PCl5 then alkyl chloride is formed as a product (C).\n\nWhen B reacts with C then nucleophilic substitution reaction occurs and ether is obtained as a product.\n\nThe reactions are as follows: - diagram(250900E)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','C2H5OH, C2H6 , C2H5Cl','C2H5OH, C2H5Cl, C2H5ONa','C2H5Cl, C2H6 ,C2H5OH','C2H5OH,C2H5ONa, C2H5Cl','NEW','12-345','12-315',NULL),
(250933,'chemistry','The correct acidic order of the following is\ndiagram(n2539)','III > I > II','p-nitrophenol has the highest acidity due to its electron withdrawing group (-NO₂). p-cresol is least acidic due to its electron donating group (-CH₃).\nNEET 2001','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','I > II > III','III > I > II','II > III > IV > I','I > III > II','NEW','12-341','12-211','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250933q.png'),
(250934,'chemistry','The alkene R – CH = CH₂ reacts readily with B₂H₆ and the product on oxidation with alkaline hydrogen peroxides produces.\ndiagram(n2540)','d','The reaction will takes place through following mechanism.\nNEET 1995\ndiagram(ne2541)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-330','12-200','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250934q.png'),
(250935,'chemistry','. On heating glycerol with conc. H2SO4, a compound is obtained which has bad odour. The compound is.','acrolein','Alcohols undergo dehydration when react with conc. H2SO4 to form alkene. the reaction involves formation of intermediate which upon immediate break down yield final product. \nNEET 1994','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','acrolein','formic acid','allyl alcohol','glycerol sulphate','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250935q.png'),
(250936,'chemistry','Ethanol and dimethyl ether form a pair of functional isomers. The boiling point of ethanol is higher than that of dimethyl ether, due to the presence of:','H-bonding in ethanol','Presence of -OH group in ethanol is responsible for hydrogen bonding. while ether do not have -OH group.\nNEET 1993','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','H-bonding in ethanol','H-bonding in dimethyl ether','CH3 group in ethanol','CH3 group in dimethyl ether','NEW','12-347','12-217',NULL),
(250937,'chemistry','Increasing order of acid strength among p-methoxyphenol, p-methylphenol and p-nitrophenol is','p-methoxyphenol, p-methylphenol, p-nitrophenol','NO₂ has electron withdrawing effect so it increase the acidity of phenol while -CH₃ and -OCH₃ are electron donating group so they decrease the acidity of phenol. -OCH₃ has more electron donating effect than -CH₃ so decrease in acidity is also greater.\nNEET 1993','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','p-nitrophenol, p-methoxyphenol, p-methylphenol','p-methylphenol, p-methoxyphenol, p-nitrophenol','p-nitrophenol, p-methylphenol, p-methoxyphenol','p-methoxyphenol, p-methylphenol, p-nitrophenol','NEW','12-337','12-207',NULL),
(250938,'chemistry','Which one of the following on oxidation gives a ketone?','Secondary alcohol','On Oxidation Primary alcohol make aldehyde while secondary alcohol make ketone. \nNEET 1993','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','Primary alcohol','Secondary alcohol','Tertiary alcohol','All of these','NEW','12-339','12-209',NULL),
