(250964,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name of the organic compound formed in the following chemical:\ndiagram(n2556)','2-methyl butan-2-ol','Ketone give  tertiary alcohol when react with Grignard reagents by following mechanism.\nNEET 2021\ndiagram(ne2557)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','2-methyl propan-2-ol','pentan-2-ol','pentan-3-ol','2-methyl butan-2-ol','NEW','12-331','12-201','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250964q.png'),
(250965,'chemistry','Given below are two statements\n\nStatement I: The acidic strength of monosubstituted nitrophenol is higher than phenol because of electron withdrawing nitro group.\nStatement II: o-nitrophenol, m-nitrophenol and p-nitrophenol will have same acidic strength as they have one nitro group attached to the phenolic ring.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.','Nitro group is electron withdrawing group so it can increase the acidic strength of phenol. if nitro group is ortho and para directing than it will have more acidic character than meta directing nitro group.\nNEET 2022','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.','Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.','NEW','12-337','12-207',NULL),
(250966,'chemistry','The correct IUPAC name of the following compound is\ndiagram(n2558)','1-bromo-5-chloro-4-methylhexan-3-ol','Parent chain contain 6 carbon so it is hexanol. Numering starts near to the OH group and all the substitute numbered accordingly.\nNEET 2022\ndiagram(ne2559)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','1-bromo-5-chloro-4-methylhexan-3-ol','6-bromo-2-chloro-4-methythexan-4-ol','1-bromo-4-methyl-5-chlorohexan-3-ol','6-bromo-4-methyl-2-chlorohexan-4-ol','NEW','12-326','12-196','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250966q.png'),
(250967,'chemistry','The product formed from the following reaction sequence is\ndiagram(n2560)','d','Following reaction will take place.\nNEET 2022\ndiagram(ne2561)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250967q.png'),
(250968,'chemistry','Given below are two statements:\n\nStatement I: In Lucas test, primary, secondary and tertiary alcohols are distinguished on the basis of their reactivity with conc. HCl + ZnCl₂, known as Lucas Reagent.\nStatement II: Primary alcohols are most reactive and immediately produce turbidity at room temperature on reaction with Lucas Reagent.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect','Alcohols can be differentiated using Lucas test. Tertiary alcohols form turbidity immediatly at room temperature secondary alcohols take 5 minutes to form turbidity while primary alcohol do not give turbidity at room temperature. \nNEET 2022','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect','Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct','NEW','12-338','12-208',NULL),
(250969,'chemistry','CH3CH2CH=CH2 -------------> Z in the presence ofB2H6 , H2O , H2O2 , OH-  (NEET 2020 )','CH3CH2CH2OH','The reaction is Hydroboration-oxidation\'( NEET 2020 )','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','CH3CH2CH2OH','CH3CH2OHCH3','CH3CH2CH2CHO','CH3CH2CH2CH3','NEW','12-330','12-200',NULL),
(250970,'chemistry','"The compound A on treatment with Na gives B, and  with PCl₅ gives C. B and C react together to give diethyl ether. A, B and C are in the order "','C₂H₅OH, C₂H₅Cl, C₂H₅ONa','"The compound A is ethanol which on treatment with sodium gives sodiumethoxide (compound B). This compound on reacting with PCl₅ gives ethyl chloride (compound C). (NEET 2018)"','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','C₂H₅OH, C₂H₅ONa, C₂H₅Cl','C₂H₅OH, C₂H₅ONa, C₂H₅Cl','C₂H₅Cl, C₂H₆, C₂H₅OH','C₂H₅OH, C₂H₅Cl, C₂H₅ONa','NEW','12-345','12-215',NULL),
(250971,'chemistry','The most acidic among the following is refer to diagram(250971Q):','2','Hint: Acidity of a compound depends on the stability of conjugate ion The acidity of a compound is increased by the presence of an electron withdrawing substituent whereas the acidity decreases when the electron releasing substituent is present. Methyl (–CH3) is an electron releasing substituent whereas phenyl is an electron withdrawing substituent. Furthermore, the stability of a phenoxide ion is more due to the present of resonance effect which is greater than the stabilization in the phenoxide ion. That is why it is a strong acid among all the compounds.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-345','12-215','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250971q.png'),
(250972,'chemistry','The product \'D\' in the above mentioned reaction is refer to diagram(250972Q):','4','Hint: The first reaction is an oxidation reaction and the second reaction is an electrophilic aromatic substitution reaction. Alkaline KMnO4is a strong oxidizing agent and therefore it oxidizes the whole carbon chain directly attached to the benzene nucleus into the –COOH group. Furthermore, the Br2 group in the presence of a halogen carrier causes bromination whereas ethyl alcohol in an acidic medium results in esterification.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-339','12-209','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250972q.png'),
(250973,'chemistry','Alkyl halides prepared by among the gollowing reactions is/are Refer to diagram(250973Q):','1','Reactions (I) and (IV) can yield alkyl halide due to the presence of anhydrous ZnCl2 as it acts a catalyst. Reaction (III) is also possible because halogenation will take place by SN1 mechanism and alcohol is tertiary resulting in stable carbocation','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-338','12-208','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250973q.png'),
(250974,'chemistry','Which one of the following compounds will be most readily dehydrated refer to diagram(250974Q)?','2','HINT: Here stability of carbocation is checked. The compound gives more stable carbocation is more reactive. Dehydration of alcohols involves the formation of a carbocation intermediate. The reaction follows E1 mechanism. The more the stability of carbocation, the higher is the ease of dehydration. The order of stability of carbocation, given by the given compounds, is. Thus, the compound given in the option readily undergoes dehydration.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-340','12-210','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250974q.png'),
(250975,'chemistry','The reaction that does not give benzoic acid as the major product is refer to diagram(250975Q):','3','Hint:- Oxidation of alcohols PCC is a soft oxidizing agent so it can oxidize alcohol to aldehyde but not aldehyde to acid.diagram(250975E)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-339','12-209','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250975q.png'),
(250976,'chemistry','When the vapours of a secondary alcohol is passed over heated copper at 573 K, the product formed is','A Ketone','"When the vapours of a secondary alcohol are passed over heated copper at 573 K, dehydrogenation takes place and a ketone is formed. (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 340; Paragraph No. 5)"','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','A Carboxylic Acid','An Aldehyde','A Ketone','An Alkene','NEW','12-340','12-210',NULL),
(250977,'chemistry','The increasing order of reactivity of the following compounds toward acid-catalyzed dehydration is refer to diagram(250977Q):','2','Hint: The order of alcohol is 3o > 2o > 1o The reactivity of acid catalysed dehydration of alcohol depends on the stability of carbocation. The stability order of carbocation is as follows: , , , As the alkyl group increases the stability of carbocation also increases because of +I effect of alkyl group.  In vinyl carbocation positive charge formed in sp2 carbon hence it is least stable. The order of stability of carbocation is as follows: 3o > 2o > 1o > vinyl . Thus, the reactivity order is  (b) < (a) < (c) < (d)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-344','12-214','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250977q.png'),
(250978,'chemistry','Which of the following is the strongest acid refer to diagram(250978Q):','1','Hint- Higher the electron-withdrawing effect, the higher will be the acidic character The p- nitrophenol is more acidic than phenol because of the deactivating nature of the nitro group and after losing the protons it makes more resonance stabilization as a nitro group is having strong deactivating nature so it attracts electrons towards it.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-337','12-207','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250978q.png'),
(250979,'chemistry','Refer to diagram(250979Q):','3','Hint: In polar solvent, tribromophenol is obtained on bromination The reaction of phenol with Br2 depends on the solvent system. If the polar solvent is used then bromination occurs at all the ortho and para positions. If a solvent is non-polar like CS2 or CHCl3 (CHCl3 has a dipole moment of 1.08 D)then bromination at only at the ortho or para position.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-341','12-211','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250979q.png'),
(250980,'chemistry','The reaction of phenol with chloroform in the presence of dilute NaOH finally introduces a functional group X.The X is:','-CHO','Hint: Follow Reimer-Tiemann reaction. Explanation: It is a Reimer-Tiemann reaction where an activated benzene ring i.e. phenol undergoes formylation in the presence of a base. In this reaction, phenol reacts with chloroform in the presence of dilute sodium hydroxide and salicylaldehyde is formed as a product.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','-CH2Cl','-COOH','-CHCl2','-CHO','NEW','12-343','12-213',NULL),
(250981,'chemistry','When phenol is treated with NaOH and CHCl3 , the product formed is:','Salicylaldehyde','HINT: Reimer-Tiemann reaction. Explanation: When phenol treated with chloroform in presence of aqueous sodium hydroxide at 340 K to form 2− hydroxybenzaldehyde or salicylaldehyde.  This reaction is called Reimer-Tiemann reaction.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','Benzaldehyde','Salicylaldehyde','Salicylic acid','Benzoic acid','NEW','12-343','12-213',NULL),
(250982,'chemistry','The product \'Z\' in the mentioned reaction in diagram(250982Q) is:','Benzoic acid','Zn dust acts as a reducing agent and converts the –OH group into – H due to which phenol is converted to benzene. Benzene reacts with CH3Cl in presence of anhy. AlCl3 (Friedel –Craft’s alkylation) form toluene which subsequently gets converted into benzoic acid by treatment with alkaline KMnO4.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','Toluene','Benzaldehyde','Benzoic acid','Benzene','NEW','12-339','12-209','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250982q.png'),
(250983,'chemistry','The phenol distilled with Zn dust followed by fredel-crafts alkylation with propyl chloride in the presence of AlCl3 to give a compound\'B\'.\'B\' is oxidised in the presence of air to form compound \'C\' .The structural formula of \'C\' is refer to diagram (250983Q):','2','Hint: B is cumene In the first reaction, phenol reacts with Zn dust and benzene is obtained as a product. In the next step, benzene reacts with propyl chloride in the presence of AlCl3 to give a compound B. The compound B is isopropylbenzene or cumene. Then cumene undergoes oxidation reaction with O2 and form cumene hydroperoxide, that is C. When C further reacts with an dilute acid then phenol is obtained as a product.diagram(250983E)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-332','12-202','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250983q.png'),
(250984,'chemistry','In preparation of alkene from alcohol using Al2O3 which is an effective factor:','Temprature','HINT: Temperature is very effective factor in dehydration of alcohols. Explanation: Temperature is the effective factor for dehydration of alcohol by Al2O3. At higher temperautre (350∘C−380∘C) 1∘ alcohol undergoes intramolecular dehydration to yield an alkene: R C H 2 C H 2 O H 350 − 380 0 C −−−−−−→ A l 2 O 3 R C H = C H 2 + H 2 O ( I n t r a m o l e c u l a r d e h y d r a t i o n ) while at lower temperature (220∘C − 250∘C) it undergoes intermolecular dehydration to yield to ether. RCH2CH2OH + OHCH2CH2R → RCH2CH2OCH2CH2R + H2O (Intermolecular dehydration)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','Porosity of Al2O3','Temprature','Concerntration','Surface area of Al2O3','NEW','12-338','12-208',NULL),
(250985,'chemistry','The reaction can be classified as refer to diagram(250985Q):','4','HINT: Williamson ether synthesis reaction The formation of ether by reaction of an alcohol in the presence of base followed by alkylation is known as Williamson ether synthesis.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-345','12-215','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250985q.png'),
(250986,'chemistry','Refer to diagram(250986Q):','1','Williamson synthesis is the reaction of alkyl halides with sodium alkoxide or sodium phenoxide to form ethers .In this reaction , alkyl halide should be primary and alkoxide, should be bulkier as it follows SN2 mechanism. CH 3 − CH 3 | C | CH 3 − ONa + CH 3 CH 2 Cl −−−→ − NaCl CH 3 − CH 3 | C | CH 3 − O − CH 2 − CH 3 The Étard reaction is a chemical reaction that involves the direct oxidation of an aromatic or heterocyclic bound methyl group to an aldehyde using chromyl chloride. For example, toluene can be oxidized to benzaldehyde. In Gattermann – Koch reaction, benzene is treated with carbon monoxide in the acidic medium in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride to give benzaldehyde.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-345','12-215','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250986q.png'),
(250987,'chemistry','Which one of the following does not give nucleophilic substituion with alcohol?','Ether','Hint: Ether mixed with alcohol then no reaction occurs Acetyl chloride and acetic anhydride react with alcohol and form an ester as a product. The reaction is an example of a nucleophilic acyl substitution reaction. In a nucleophilic substitution reaction, strong nucleophiles substitute weak nucleophiles. The reaction is as follows: But when Alcohol reacts with ether then no reaction occurs because ether and alcohol both act as a nucleophile and two nucleophiles do not react with each other.  Hence, ether is the correct answer.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','CH3COCl','Acetic Anhydride','Ether','None of these','NEW','12-347','12-217',NULL),
(250988,'chemistry','The product B in the given reaction is: Refer diagram (250988Q)','4','Reaction follows SN1 mechanism.\n\nBecause of the large size of the iodide, it approaches the smaller alkyl group to avoid steric hindrance in case of\n\nunsymmetrical ether. Hence,\n\nalkyl halide is formed from a smaller alkyl group in unsymmetrical ether. Hence,\n\noption 4th is the correct answer.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-347','12-217','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250988q.png'),
(250989,'chemistry','Consider the following reaction: Refer diagram (250989Q)','4','Refer diagram (250989E)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-347','12-217','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250989q.png'),
(250990,'chemistry','The product formed in the below mentioned reaction is (250990Q)','4','In this reaction, I- will attack the less substituted carbon.\n\nSince mixed ether is given in this reaction, note that iodide will bond with the smaller alkyl group to minimise the steric repulsion as iodide ion is bulky itself.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-347','12-217',NULL),
(250991,'chemistry','The product Z in the below mentioned reaction is (250991Q)','4','PBr3 causes substitution of the alcoholic group by –Br . Bromoethane then undergoes dehydrohalogenation by alc. KOH to form\n\nethene, an olefin that is converted back to ethanol by H2SO4 and H2O as shown in the flowchart. Refer diagram (250991E)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-342','12-214',NULL),
(251015,'chemistry','Match List-I with List - II.\ndiagram(n2566)','(a) - (ii), (b) – (iv), (c) – (i), (d) – (iii)','These are common names of benzene-1,3-diol, 2-methylphenol, benzene-1,2-diol and Benzene-1,4-diol respectively. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 327; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(a) - (iii), (b) – (iv), (c) – (ii), (d) – (i)','(a) - (ii), (b) – (iv), (c) – (i), (d) – (iii)','(a) - (i), (b) – (iv), (c) – (ii), (d) – (iii)','(a) - (iv), (b) – (iii), (c) – (ii), (d) – (i)','NEW','12-327','12-197','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251015q.png'),
(251016,'chemistry','Dihydroxy derivatives of benzene are known as_____','all of above','Dihydroxy derivatives of benzene are known as 1, 2-, 1, 3- and 1, 4-benzenediol. e.g. benzene- diol.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 327; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1,2- benzenediol','1,3-benzenediol','1,4-benzenediol','all of above','NEW','12-327','12-197',NULL),
(251017,'chemistry','The structure of intermediate A in the following reaction is \ndiagram(n2567)','d','It is an example of Reimer-Tiemann reaction. In this reaction The intermediate substituted benzal chloride is hydrolysed in the presence of alkali to produce salicylaldehyde.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 343; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-343','12-213','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251017q.png'),
(251018,'chemistry','Which of the following reaction(s) can be used for the preparation of alcohol?\ndiagram(n2568)','(I) and (II) only','Secondary alcohols prepared by treating ketones with sodium borohydride (NaBH4) or lithium aluminium hydride (LiAlH4). Carboxylic acids are reduced to primary alcohols in excellent yields by lithium aluminium hydride, a strong reducing agent\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 330; Paragraph No. 3 and 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(I) and (II) only','(IV) only','(III) and (IV) only','(I), (III) and (IV) only','NEW','12-330','12-200','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251018q.png'),
(251019,'chemistry','In the reaction in the given diagram, the product Z is \ndiagram(n2569)','CH₃OH','Commercially, acids are reduced to alcohols by converting them to the esters, followed by their reduction using hydrogen in the presence of catalyst (catalytic hydrogenation).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 330; Paragraph No.5)\ndiagram(ne2502)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CH₃OCH₃','C₂H₅OH','CH₃OH','C₂H₅OCH₃','NEW','12-330','12-200','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251019q.png'),
(251020,'chemistry','Which one of the following organic compound has highest boiling point?\ndiagram(n2570)','c','Boiling points of alcohols are higher in comparison to other classes of compounds, namely hydrocarbons, ethers, haloalkanes and haloarenes of comparable molecular masses. In alcohols, the boiling points decrease with increase of branching in carbon chain.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 333; Paragraph No. 2,4)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-333','12-213','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251020q.png'),
(251021,'chemistry','Which set of compounds is arranged according to correct order of increasing boiling point:','pentan-1-ol > pentanal > ethoxyethane > n-butane','Boiling points of alcohols are higher in comparison to other classes of compounds, namely hydrocarbons, ethers, haloalkanes and haloarenes of comparable molecular masses. In alcohols, the boiling points decrease with increase of branching in carbon chain.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 333; Paragraph No. 2,4, Page 334; Paragraph No.2)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','n-Butane > ethoxyethane > pentanal > pentan-1-ol','pentan-1-ol > pentanal > ethoxyethane > n-butane','ethoxyethane > n-butane >pentan-1-ol > pentanal','pentanal  >ethoxyethane > n-butane >pentan-1-ol','NEW','12-333','12-213',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(251022,'chemistry','Which of the following reaction(s) show that phenols are acidic in nature?\ndiagram(n2571)','(II) and (III) only','phenols react with active metals such as sodium, potassium and aluminium to yield corresponding alkoxides/phenoxides and hydrogen. In addition to this, phenols react with aqueous sodium hydroxide to form sodium phenoxides. The above reactions show that alcohols and phenols are acidic in nature.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 335; Paragraph No.1 and 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(I) and (II) only','(II) and (III) only','(III) and (IV) only','(I), (III) and (IV) only','NEW','12-335','12-215','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251022q.png'),
(251023,'chemistry','Which statement(s) can be deduce from the given reaction?\n\ni Water is stronger acid than alcohols.\nii Alcohols are better proton donor than water.\niii Alkoxides are stronger bases.\niv  alkoxide ion is a better proton acceptor than hydroxide ion.\ndiagram(n2572)','(I), (III) and (IV) only','Alcohols are, however, weaker acids than water.water is a better proton donor (i.e.,stronger acid) than alcohol. that an alkoxide ion is a better proton acceptor than hydroxide ion, which suggests that alkoxides are stronger bases (sodium ethoxide is a stronger base than sodium hydroxide).\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 336; Paragraph No.1,2)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(I) and (II) only','(II) and (III) only','(III) and (IV) only','(I), (III) and (IV) only','NEW','12-336','12-216','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251023q.png'),
(251024,'chemistry','Assertion (A):  Addition reaction of water to but-1-ene in acidic or basic medium yields butan-1-ol\n\nReason (R): Addition of water in acidic medium proceeds through the formation of secondary carbocation.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given\nbelow:','(A) is wrong but (R) is  correct.','Addition reaction of water to butene gives butan-2-ol and not butan-1-ol. Secondary carbocation is formed during this process.\nAlkenes react with water in the presence of acid as catalyst to form alcohols.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 329; Paragraph No. 4)\ndiagram(ne2506)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is incorrect explanation of (A).','(A) is correct  (R) is wrong','(A) is wrong but (R) is  correct.','NEW','12-329','12-199',NULL),
(251025,'chemistry','Assertion (A):  IUPAC name of the  compound given in the diagram is 2-ethoxy-2-methylethane. \n\nReason (R): In IUPAC nomenclature, ether is regarded as hydrocarbon derivative in which a hydrogen atom is replaced by ---OR or ---OAr group.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given\nbelow:\ndiagram(n2574)','(A) is wrong but (R) is  correct.','The correct IUPAC name of the given compound is 1-(2-propoxy) propane.\neg.CH₃– O – CH₂ – CH₂ – OCH₃, 1,2-dimethoxyethane.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 327; Table No. 11.2)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is incorrect explanation of (A).','(A) is correct  (R) is wrong','(A) is wrong but (R) is  correct.','NEW','12-327','12-197','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251025q.png'),
(251026,'chemistry','Assertion (A):  Bond angle in ethers is slightly less than the tetrahedral angle.\n\nReason (R): There is repulsion between the two bulky(---R) groups.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given\nbelow:','(A) is wrong but (R) is  correct.','The bond angle is slightly greater than the tetrahedral angle due to the repulsive interaction between the two bulky (–R) groups.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 329; Paragraph No. 2)\nThe bond angle in alcohols is slightly less than the tetrahedral angle (109°-28′).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 329; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is incorrect explanation of (A).','(A) is correct  (R) is wrong','(A) is wrong but (R) is  correct.','NEW','12-329','12-199',NULL),
(251027,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Like bromination of benzene, bromination of phenol is also carried out in the presence of Lewis acid.\n\nReason (R): Lewis acid polarises the bromine molecule.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given\nbelow:','(A) is wrong but (R) is  correct.','The usual halogenation of benzene takes place in the presence of a Lewis acid.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 342; Paragraph No. 2)\nIn case of phenol, the polarisation of bromine molecule takes place even in the absence of Lewis acid. It is due to the highly activating effect of –OH group attached to the benzene ring.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 342; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is incorrect explanation of (A).','(A) is correct  (R) is wrong','(A) is wrong but (R) is  correct.','NEW','12-342','12-212',NULL),
(251028,'chemistry','Assertion (A): o-nitrophenol is less soluble in water than the m and p- isomers\n\nReason (R): m and p-nitrophenols exist as associated molecules\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given\nbelow:','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not correct explanation of (A).','O-nitrophenol is steam volatile due to intramolecular hydrogen bonding.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 341; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct  (R) is wrong','(A) is wrong but (R) is  correct.','NEW','12-341','12-211',NULL),
(251029,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Ethanol is a weaker acid than phenol\n\nReason (R): Sodium ethoxide may be prepared by the reaction of ethanol with aqueous NaOH.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(A) is correct  (R) is wrong','Sodium ethoxide is prepared by the reaction of Ethanol with Sodium metal and not by NaOH solution.\nPhenoxide ion can be stabilised by resonance , so it reacts with NaOH.\ndiagram(ne2562)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct  (R) is wrong','(A) is wrong but (R) is  correct.','NEW','12-342','12-212',NULL),
(251030,'chemistry','State True or False?\na. Phenol forms 2,4,6 tribromophenol on treatment with bromine in CS₂ at 273K\nb. In the above reaction,bromine polarises in carbon disulphide and activates the ring towards electrophillic substitution reaction.','F,F','Phenol forms 2,4,6-tribromophenol on treatment with bromine in water and not in  non -polar solvents like carbon disulphide as given in diagram\nPhenol forms phenoxide ion in present of polar solvents like water. This phenoxide ion activates the ring towards electrophillic substitution reaction.\ndiagram(ne2563)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','T,T','T,F','F,T','F,F','NEW','12-342','12-212',NULL),
(251031,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Phenol give o- and p-nitrophenol on nitration with conc nitric acid and sulphuric acid\n\nReason (R): ---OH group in phenol is o-,p-directing.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given\nbelow:','(A) is wrong but (R) is  correct.','With dilute nitric acid at low temperature (298 K), phenol yields a mixture of ortho and para nitrophenols.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 341; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct  (R) is wrong','(A) is wrong but (R) is  correct.','NEW','12-341','12-211',NULL),
(251032,'chemistry','The intermediate formed during the industrial preparation of phenol is','Iso-propylbenzene hydroperooxide','Phenol is manufactured from the hydrocarbon, cumene. Cumene (isopropylbenzene) is oxidised in the presence of air to cumene hydroperoxide. It is converted to phenol and acetone by treating it with dilute acid.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 332; Paragraph No. 4) \ndiagram(ne2564)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Cumene','Iso-propylbenzene hydroperooxide','Propanone','Benzene diazonium chloride','NEW','12-332','12-202',NULL),
(251033,'chemistry','Which of the following is a biocatalyst and used in industries','Zymase','Zymase is used in the industrial preparation of wines.\nGlucose and fructose undergo fermentation in the presence of another enzyme, zymase, which is found in yeast.In wine making, grapes are the source of sugars and yeast.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 344; Paragraph No. 5)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Cumene','Urease','Zymase','Sucralose','NEW','12-344','12-214',NULL),
(251034,'chemistry','The compounds used as inhalational anaesthetic is','Diethyl ether','Diethyl ether has been used widely as an inhalation anaesthetic. But due to its slow effect and an unpleasant recovery period, it has been replaced, as an anaesthetic, by other compounds.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 345; Left side paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Methanol','Aspirin','Diethyl ether','Methylethyl ether','NEW','12-345','12-215',NULL),
(251035,'chemistry','State True or False?\na. In Kolbe reaction, phenoxide ion is treated with carbon dioxide because the reactivity of phenol towards electrophillic substitution reaction is more in comparison to phenoxide ion.\nb. Dipole moment of phenol is smaller than that of methanol','F,T','The ability to give lone pair of electrons to the benzene ring is more in phenoxide ion compared to phenol. Therefore the reactivity of phenoxide ion towards electrophillic substiution reaction is more than phenols so it reacts easily with weak electrophile like carbon dioxide.\nKolbe’s Reaction – Treating phenol with sodium hydroxide generates phenoxide ion. The mechanism of Kolbe reaction proceeds through the nucleophilic addition of phenoxide to carbon dioxide, yielding the salicylate.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 342; Paragraph No. 3)\nIn phenol carbon is sp² hybridised while carbon of methanol is sp³ hybridised. Thus, C----O bond in phenol is less polar than C---O bond in methanol and therefore, the dipole moment of phenol is smaller than that of methanol.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','T,T','T,F','F,T','F,F','NEW','12-342','12-212',NULL),
(251036,'chemistry','When tert-butyl bromide  reacts with Sodium tert butoxide, the product not formed during the reaction is:\na. 1-methylpropano-2-ol\nb. iso-butylene\nc. 2-methylpropan-2-ol\nd. Ether','a,d','When tert-butyl bromide reacts with sodium tert-butoxide instead of substitution','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','NEW','12-345','12-215',NULL),
(251037,'chemistry','Monochlorination of toluene in sunlight followed by hydrolysis with aq. NaOH yields\na. o-cresol\nb. m-cresol\nc. 2,4-dihydroxytoluence\nd. benzyl alchohol','only d','In presence of sunlight, benzyl chloride is formed which undergoes hydrolysis with NaOH to form benzyl alchohol. \ndiagram(n2575)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,d','only d','NEW','12-338','12-208',NULL),
(251038,'chemistry','How many alcohols with molecular formula C₄H₁₀O are chiral in nature?','1','Total no. of isomers possible for butanol are three namely:\n1. Butan-1-ol\n2. Butan-1-ol\n3. 2-methylpropan-2-ol\nChiral carbon is that carbon which is bonded to four different substituents.\nHere, only Butan-2-ol has a chiral carbon.\ndiagram(ne2565)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-338','12-208',NULL),
(251039,'chemistry','What is the  correct order of reactivity of alcohols in the  reaction given in \ndiagram(n2576)?','c','The reaction proceeds through nucleophillic substitution , so it depends on the stability of carbocation.\nThe tertiary carbocation is more stable than secondary or primary . Higher the stability of intermediate, higher is the reactivity of the reactant molecule. So the correct answer is option c.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 338; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-338','12-208','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251039q.png'),
(251040,'chemistry','Which of the following will convert methanol to methanal only.\na. Catalytic hydrogenation\nb. treatment with LiAlH₄\nc. treatment with Correy\'reagent\nd. treatment with KMnO₄','c only','Pyridinium chlorochromate is also know as Correy\'s reagent. It oxidises primary alcohol to aldehydes and not carboxylic acids\nA better reagent for oxidation of primary alcohols to aldehydes in good yield is pyridinium chlorochromate (PCC).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 340; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','c only','all of the above','NEW','12-340','12-210',NULL),
(251041,'chemistry','Which of the following compound is aromatic alcohol?\ndiagram(n2577)','b,c','Phenol is an aromatic compound but not an aromatic alcohol.\nAromatic alcohols are compounds in which -OH group is not directly attached with benzene ring directly.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b,c,d','a,d','b,c','a only','NEW','12-336','12-206','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251041q.png'),
(251042,'chemistry','Which of the following compound in the given diagram can act as strongest base?\ndiagram(n2578)','b','Weakest acid has the strongest conjugate base. Among all these acid, ROH is the weakest acid, therefore it has strongest conjugate base.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-345','12-215','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251042q.png'),
(251043,'chemistry','Arrange the following compounds in increasing order of their boiling point:\nPropan-1-ol, butan-1-ol, butan-2-ol, pentan-1-ol','Propan-1-ol, butan-2-ol, butan-2-ol, pentan-1-ol','Boiling point increases with increase in number of carbon atoms because of increase in molecular mass. Further, primary alcohol has higher boiling points than secondary alcohols due to increase in surface area.\n\nIn alcohols, the boiling points decrease with increase of branching in carbon chain. (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 333; Paragraph No. 2,4)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Propan-1-ol, butan-2-ol, butan-2-ol, pentan-1-ol','Propan-1-ol, butan-1-ol, butan-2-ol, pentan-1-ol','Propan-1-ol, butan-2-ol, butan-1-ol, pentan-1-ol','Propan-1-ol, butan-1-ol, butan-2-ol, pentan-1-ol','NEW','12-333','12-203',NULL),
(251044,'chemistry','Which of the following is used to convert RCHO into RCH₂OH?\na. H₂/Pd\nb. LiAlH₄\nc. NaBH₄\nd. Reaction with RMgX followed by hydrolysis.','a,b,c','Alcohol can be produced from aldehyde by reducing agents such as H₂/Pd, LiAlH₄ ,NaBH₄ .\nHowever RMgX gives secondary alcohols with any aldehyde other than methanal.\ndiagram(ne2566)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,b,c','all of the above','NEW','12-330','12-200',NULL),
(251045,'chemistry','Which of the following reactions will yield phenol? \ndiagram(n2579)','a,b,c','Haloarenes such as Chlorobenzene are less reactive towards nucleophilic substitution reactions, so it requires very high temperature and pressure. \ndiagram(ne2567)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,b,c','all of the above','NEW','12-332','12-202','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251045q.png'),
(251046,'chemistry','Which of the following reagents can be used to oxidise primary alcohol to aldehydes?\na. CrO₃ in anhydrous medium\nb. KMnO₄\nc. Pyridinium chlorochromate\nd. Heat in the presence of Cu at 573 K','a,c,d','Acidic KMnO₄ oxidises primary alcohol into carboxylic acids. Other reagent oxidises to aldehydes\nPyridinium chlorochromate is also known as Correy\'s reagent. It oxidises primary alcohol to aldehydes and not carboxylic acids\nA better reagent for oxidation of primary alcohols to aldehydes in good yield is pyridinium chlorochromate (PCC).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 340; Paragraph No. 2)\nCrO₃ in anhydrous medium is used as the oxidising agent for the isolation of aldehydes.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 340; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,b,c','a,c,d','NEW','12-339','12-209',NULL),
(251047,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds are benzylic alcohols? \ndiagram(n2580)','b,c','Benzylic alcohols: In these alcohols, the —OH group is attached to a sp³ hybridised carbon atom next to an aromatic ring. Allylic and benzylic alcohols may be primary, secondary or tertiary. (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 324; Paragraph No. 4,7; Page 325; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','all of the above','NEW','12-324','12-194','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251047q.png'),
(260230,'chemistry','Select the correct dissimilarities between C^O and C=Cbond?','Carbon-oxygen double bond is polar  and carbon-carbon double bond is polar due to ion ion interaction.','The carbon-oxygen double bond is polarised due to higher electronegativity of oxygen relative to carbon. \n\nHence, the carbonyl carbon is an electrophilic (Lewis acid), and carbonyl oxygen, a nucleophilic (Lewis base) centre. \n\nCarbonyl compounds have substantial dipole moments and are polar than ethers. \nThe high polarity of the carbonyl group is explained on the basis of resonance involving a neutral and a dipolar structures.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph-2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'3','mcq','Carbon-oxygen double bond is polar  and carbon-carbon double bond is polar due to ion ion interaction.','Carbon oxygen bond length is 123 pm than that of carbon-carbon bond length is 134 pm.','Carbonyl compounds undergo nucleophilic addition reactions but compounds containing ethylenic double bonds undergo electrophilic addition reactions.','All of the above.','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(251048,'chemistry','Select the correct option(s) from the following statements which explain about "Denaturation of Alcohol",Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:\na. The commercial alcohol is made unfit for drinking by mixing in it some copper sulphate (to give it a colour) and pyrimidine (a foul smelling liquid). It is known as denaturation of alcohol.\nb. In the body methanal may cause blindness and death.\nc. A methanol poisoned patient is treated by giving diluted ethanol orally.\nd. On giving ethanol, Kidneys get time to excrete methanol.','only d','The commercial alcohol is made unfit for drinking by mixing in it some copper sulphate (to give it a colour) and pyridine (a foul smelling liquid). It is known as denaturation of alcohol. (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 344; Paragraph No. 7)\nEthanol mixed with methanal is called denatured alcohol. In the body, methanol is oxidised first to ethanal and then to methanoic acid, which cause blindness and death.\n\nA methanol poisoned patient is treated by giving Intravenous infusions of diluted ethanol. The enzyme resposible for the oxidation of aldehyde to acid is swamped allowing time for kidneys to excrete methanol.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 340; Paragraph No. 7)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only b and c','Only a and d','Only a,b and d','only d','NEW','12-340','12-210',NULL),
(251049,'chemistry','Which of the following statement(s) is/are false?\n\n a. p-nitrophenol is more volatile than p-nitrophenol due to presence of intermolecular hydrogen bonding\n b. o-cresol is much less acidic than o-nitrophenol\n c. p-nitrophenol is more volatile than p-nitrophenol due to presence of intramolecular hydrogen bonding','a,c','O-nitrophenol is steam volatile due to intramolecular hydrogen bonding.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 341; Paragraph No. 4)\nDue to -I and --R effect of nitro group, O----H bond is easily dissociated in o-nitrophenol.However methyl group in o-cresol has +I effect . So o-nitrophenol is more acidic than o-cresol.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','only b','NEW','12-341','12-211',NULL),
(251050,'chemistry','Match the structures of the compounds given in the diagram(n2581) with the name of the compounds.','A⟶(4), B⟶(3), C⟶(1) ,D⟶(2)','IUPAC name of Catechol is 1,2-dihydrobenzene\nCresols are methyl phenols.\nIUPAC name of Resorcinol is 1,3-dihydrobenzene\nIUPAC name of Hydroquinone is 1,4-dihydrobenzene\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 327; Paragraph No. 1,2)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A⟶(4), B⟶(3), C⟶(2) ,D⟶(1)','A⟶(4), B⟶(3), C⟶(1) ,D⟶(2)','A⟶(3), B⟶(4), C⟶(2) ,D⟶(1)','A⟶(3), B⟶(4), C⟶(1) ,D⟶(2)','NEW','12-327','12-197','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251050q.png'),
(251051,'chemistry','Select the correct option(s) from the following statements:\na. Anisole is methoxy benzene\nb. Phenetole is ethylphenyl ether\nc. Hydroquinol is 1,4 hydrodroxybenzene','a,b','Phenetole are also known as ethylphenyl ehter or ethoxybenzene.\nIUPAC name of Catechol is 1,2-dihydrobenzene\nCresols are methyl phenols.\nIUPAC name of Resorcinol is 1,3-dihydrobenzene\nIUPAC name of Hydroquinone is 1,4-dihydrobenzene\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 327; Paragraph No. 1,2)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','all of the above','NEW','12-327','12-197',NULL),
(251052,'chemistry','Match the starting materials with the products formed on reaction with HI in the given diagram(n2582).','A⟶(4), B⟶(5), C⟶(2) ,D⟶(1)','A is symmetrical so products are CH₃I and CH₃OH\nB is unsymmetrical ether, so it follows SN₂ mechanism and the Iodide ion attacks primary alkyl group.\nC contains tertiary alkyl group, so it follows SN₁ mechanism and products are (CH₃)₃C---I and CH₃OH.\nD contain strong O---Ph bond due to partial double bond character from resonance. So the iodide ion attacks on alkyl group','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A⟶(4), B⟶(3), C⟶(2) ,D⟶(1)','A⟶(4), B⟶(3), C⟶(1) ,D⟶(5)','A⟶(4), B⟶(5), C⟶(2) ,D⟶(1)','A⟶(4), B⟶(3), C⟶(1) ,D⟶(2)','NEW','12-347','12-217','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251052q.png'),
(251053,'chemistry','Match the items of Column I with items of Column II given in diagram(n2583)','A⟶(5), B⟶(6), C⟶(4) ,D⟶(3) ,E⟶(1) ,F⟶(2)','A. Ethylene glycol is used as an antifreeze agent.\nB. Ethanol is used in perfumes\nC. Phenol+conc. nitric acid gives picric acid\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 341; Paragraph No. 6)\nD. Methanol, CH3OH, also known as ‘wood spirit’, was produced by\ndestructive distillation of wood.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 344; Paragraph No. 1,2)\nE.Ferric chloride used for phenolic group detection.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A⟶(4), B⟶(3), C⟶(2) ,D⟶(1) ,E⟶(5) ,F⟶(6)','A⟶(4), B⟶(1), C⟶(6) ,D⟶(2) ,E⟶(3) ,F⟶(5)','A⟶(4), B⟶(1), C⟶(2) ,D⟶(6) ,E⟶(3) ,F⟶(2)','A⟶(5), B⟶(6), C⟶(4) ,D⟶(3) ,E⟶(1) ,F⟶(2)','NEW','12-344','12-214','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251053q.png'),
(251054,'chemistry','Match the items of Column I with items of Column II as per the given diagram(n2584)','A⟶(4), B⟶(1), C⟶(6) ,D⟶(5) ,E⟶(3) ,F⟶(2)','Methanol, CH3OH, also known as ‘wood spirit’, was produced by the destructive distillation of wood. (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 344; Paragraph No. 1,2)\nB. Kolbe’s Reaction – Treating phenol with sodium hydroxide generates phenoxide ion. The mechanism of Kolbe reaction proceeds through the nucleophilic addition of phenoxide to carbon dioxide, yielding the salicylate.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 342; Paragraph No. 3)\nC. Williamson synthesis, It is an important laboratory method for the preparation of symmetrical and unsymmetrical ethers.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 345; Paragraph No. 6)\nE. On treating phenol with chloroform in the presence of sodium hydroxide, a –CHO group is introduced at ortho position of the benzene ring. This reaction is known as Reimer - Tiemann reaction.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 343; Paragraph No. 1)\nF. Ethanol, C₂H₅OH, is obtained commercially by fermentation.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 344; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A⟶(4), B⟶(3), C⟶(2) ,D⟶(1) ,E⟶(5) ,F⟶(6)','A⟶(4), B⟶(1), C⟶(6) ,D⟶(2) ,E⟶(3) ,F⟶(5)','A⟶(4), B⟶(1), C⟶(6) ,D⟶(5) ,E⟶(3) ,F⟶(2)','A⟶(5), B⟶(6), C⟶(4) ,D⟶(3) ,E⟶(1) ,F⟶(2)','NEW','12-344','12-214','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251054q.png'),
(251055,'chemistry','The process of converting alkyl halides into alcohols involves:','substitution reaction','The process of converting alkyl halides itno alcohols involves substitution reaction.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','addition reaction','substitution reaction','dehydrohalogenation reaction','rearrangement reaction','NEW','12-341','12-211',NULL),
(251056,'chemistry','IUPAC name of m-cresol is','3-methylphenol','IUPAC name of m-cresol is 3-methylphenol.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 327; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','3-methylphenol','3-chlorophenol','3-methoxyphenol','Benzene 1,3-diol','NEW','12-327','12-207',NULL),
(251057,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds will react with sodium hydroxide solution in water','C₆H₅OH','Phenoxide ion generated by treating phenol with sodium hydroxide.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 342; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','C₆H₅OH','C₆H₅CH₂OH','(CH₃)₃COH','C₂H₅OH','NEW','12-342','12-212',NULL),
(251058,'chemistry','Phenol is less acidic than','o-nitrophenol','In substituted phenols, the presence of electron withdrawing groups such as nitro group, enhances the acidic strength of phenol.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 337; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','ethanol','o-nitrophenol','o-methylphenol','o-methoxyphenol','NEW','12-337','12-207',NULL),
(251059,'chemistry','Which of the following is most acidic?','m-chlorophenol','m-chlorophenol contains electron withdrawing group (-Cl) at meta position. Presence of electron withdrawing group increases the acidic strength.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Benzyl alcohol','Cyclohexanol','Phenol','m-chlorophenol','NEW','12-337','12-207',NULL),
(251060,'chemistry','Mark the correct order of decreasing acid strength of the following compound in the diagram(n2585)','II > IV > I > III> V','Presence of electron withdrawing group on phenol increases the acidic strength, while electron donating group decreasing the acidic strength\n\n-OCH₃ at meta position exerts -I effect but not +R effect. \n\n-OCH₃ at para position exerts  +R effect. So m-methoxyphenol is more acidic than p-methoxy phenol.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','V > IV > II > I > III','II > IV > I > III> V','IV > V > III > II > I','V > IV > III > II > I','NEW','12-350','12-220','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251060q.png'),
(251061,'chemistry','Mark the correct increasing order of reactivity of the following compound in the given diagram(n2586) with HBr/ HCl?','II <  III < I','The reactions follows Sn1 mechanism and depends on the stability of carbocation.\n\nPresence of electron withdrawing group decreases its stabiltiy while electron donating group increases the stability of carbocation.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','I < II < III','II< I < III','II <  III < I','III < II < I','NEW','12-337','12-207','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251061q.png'),
(251062,'chemistry','Arrange the following compounds in increasing order of boiling point.\nPropan-1-ol, butano-1-ol, butan-2-ol, pentan-1-ol','Propanol, butan-2-ol, butanol, pentanol','The boiling points of alcohols and phenols increase with increase in the number of carbon atoms.In alcohols, the boiling points decrease with increase of branching in carbon chain.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 333; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Propanol, butan-3-ol, butanol, pentanol','Propanol, butanol, butan-2-ol, pentanol','Propanol, butan-2-ol, butanol, pentanol','Propanol, butanol, butan-2-ol, pentanol','NEW','12-333','12-203',NULL),
(251063,'chemistry','Phenol can be distinguished from ethanol by the reactions with\n\na. Br₂/water\nb. Na\nc. neutral FeCl₃\nd. All of these','a,c','When phenol is treated with bromine water, 2,4,6-tribromophenol is formed. Ethanol does not react with bromine water.\n\nPhenol gives purple color on treated with neutral FeCl₃ while ethanol does not give any reaction.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 342; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,b,c','NEW','12-342','12-212',NULL),
(251064,'chemistry','Assertion (A):  Boiling points of alcohols and ethers are high\n\nReason (R): They can form intermolecular hydrogen bonding.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are incorrect.','The boiling point of alcohols is much greater than ethers due to the presence of hydrogen bonding in alcohols.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 347; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is incorrect explanation of (A).','(A) is correct  (R) is wrong','Both (A) and (R) are incorrect.','NEW','12-347','12-217',NULL),
(251065,'chemistry','Match the starting material given in Column I with the products formed by Column II in the reaction with HI as given in diagram(n2587)','A⟶(4), B⟶(5), C⟶(2) ,D⟶(1)','(A) forms CH₃I and CH₃OH \n\n(B) being unsymmetrical ether follows Sn2 mechanism. So the halide attacks the smaller alkyl group forming CH₃I\n\n(C) follows Sn1 mechanism and halide ion attacks the tertiary alkyl group forming (CH₃)₃C-I and CH₃OH\n\n(D)  O-C₆H₆ has partial double bond character so halide ion attacks the alkyl group.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Examplar Based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A⟶(4), B⟶(3), C⟶(2) ,D⟶(1)','A⟶(4), B⟶(1), C⟶(6) ,D⟶(2)','A⟶(4), B⟶(1), C⟶(6) ,D⟶(5)','A⟶(4), B⟶(5), C⟶(2) ,D⟶(1)','NEW','12-345','12-215','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251065q.png'),
(251305,'chemistry','Propan-2-ol → Propene\n\nThe reaction can be carried out by ?','Sulphuric acid','Alcohols undergo dehydration (removal of a molecule of water) to form alkenes on treating with a protic acid such as H2SO4.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 338; Paragraph No. 6)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Conversion Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Sulphuric acid','Acetic anhydride','Acidic KMnO4','Phosphorous pentoxide','NEW','12-338','12-208',NULL),
(251306,'chemistry','Butan-2-one → Butan-2-ol\n\nThe reaction can be carried out by ?','LiAlH4','Secondary alcohols prepared by treating ketones with sodium borohydride (NaBH4) or lithium aluminium hydride (LiAlH4). Carboxylic acids are reduced to primary alcohols in excellent yields by lithium aluminium hydride, a strong reducing agent\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 330; Paragraph No. 3 and 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Conversion Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','LiOH','LiAlH4','NaOH','Ca(OH)₂','NEW','12-330','12-200',NULL),
(251307,'chemistry','Propene → Propan-2-ol\n\nThe reaction can be carried out by ?','Water in presence of acid','Alkenes react with water in the presence of acid as catalyst to form alcohols.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 329; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Conversion Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Acidic KMnO4','Phosphoric acid','Acetic acid','Water in presence of acid','NEW','12-329','12-199',NULL),
(251308,'chemistry','Benzyl chloride → Benzyl alcohol\n\nThe reaction can be carried out by ?','Treatment with NaOH followed by Acid','Reaction will proceed as given in the diagram(ne2592)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Conversion Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Treatment with NaOH followed by Acid','Treatment with KMnO4','Water in presence of acid','Treatment with PCC followed by hydrolysis','NEW','12-331','12-201',NULL),
(260336,'chemistry','Which of the following reactions is/are incorrect regarding the preparation of ketone?','Treating a nitrile with Grignard reagent.','Treatment of acyl chlorides with dialkyl cadmium, prepared by the reaction of cadmium chloride with Grignard reagent, gives ketones\n\nTreating a nitrile with Grignard reagent followed by hydrolysis yields a ketone\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-363 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'3','mcq','Treatment of acyl chlorides with dialkyl cadmium, prepared by the reaction of cadmium chloride with Grignard reagent, gives ketones','When benzene or substituted benzene is treated with acid chloride in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride, it gives the corresponding ketone. This reaction is known as Friedel-Craft’s acylation reaction','Treating a nitrile with Grignard reagent.','All of the above','NEW','12-363','12-233',NULL),
(260337,'chemistry','Give names of the reagents to bring about the following transformations: Hexan-1-ol to hexanal?','PCC','Aldehydes and ketones are generally prepared by oxidation of primary and secondary alcohols. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'2','mcq','O₃/ Zn dust','PCC','CrO₃','DIBAL-H','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(260338,'chemistry','Give names of the reagents to bring about the following transformations: Cyclohexanol to cyclohexanone?','CrO₃','Use of chromic oxide (CrO₃ ):\n\nToluene or substituted toluene is converted to benzylidene diacetate on treating with chromic oxide in acetic anhydride. \n\nThe benzylidene diacetate can be hydrolysed to corresponding benzaldehyde with aqueous acid\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-363 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'2','mcq','O₃/ Zn dust','conc. H2SO4','CrO₃','DIBAL-H','NEW','12-363','12-233',NULL),
(260339,'chemistry','Give names of the reagents to bring about the following transformations: p-Fluorotoluene to p-fluorobenzaldehyde?','CrO₃','Use of chromic oxide (CrO₃ ):\n\nToluene or substituted toluene is converted to benzylidene diacetate on treating with chromic oxide in acetic anhydride. \n\nThe benzylidene diacetate can be hydrolysed to corresponding benzaldehyde with aqueous acid\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-363 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'2','mcq','O₃/ Zn dust','PCC','CrO₃','DIBAL-H','NEW','12-363','12-233',NULL),
(260340,'chemistry','Give names of the reagents to bring about the following transformations: Ethanenitrile to ethanal?','DIBAL-H','Alternatively, nitriles are selectively reduced by diisobutylaluminium hydride, (DIBAL-H) to imines followed by hydrolysis to aldehydes.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'2','mcq','O₃/ Zn dust','PCC','CrO₃','DIBAL-H','NEW','12-362','12-232',NULL),
(260341,'chemistry','Give names of the reagents to bring about the following transformations: Allyl alcohol to propenal?','PCC','Aldehydes and ketones are generally prepared by oxidation of primary and secondary alcohols. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'2','mcq','O₃/ Zn dust','PCC','CrO₃','DIBAL-H','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(260342,'chemistry','Give names of the reagents to bring about the following transformations: But-2-ene to ethanal?','O₃/ Zn dust','Ozonolysis of alkenes:\n\nOzonolysis of alkenes followed by reaction with zinc dust and water gives aldehydes,ketones or a mixture of both depending on the substitution pattern of the alkene.\n\nBy hydration of alkynes: \n\nAddition of water to ethyne in the presence of H₂SO₄ and HgSO₄ gives acetaldehyde. All other alkynes give ketones in this reaction. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'2','mcq','O₃/ Zn dust','PCC','CrO₃','DIBAL-H','NEW','12-362','12-232',NULL),
(260343,'chemistry','Write the name of products of the following reaction diagram:\n(n26020)','propiophenone','When benzene or substituted benzene is treated with acid chloride in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride, it affords the corresponding ketone. \n\nThis reaction is known as Friedel-Crafts acylation reaction.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-364 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'3','mcq','propiophenone','1-phenylpropanone','propanone','p-nitrobenzaldehyde','NEW','12-364','12-234','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260343q.png'),
(260344,'chemistry','Write the name of products of the following reaction diagram:\n(n26021)','1-phenylpropanone','Treatment of acyl chlorides with dialkyl cadmium, prepared by the reaction of cadmium chloride with Grignard reagent, gives ketones.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-363 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'3','mcq','propiophenone','1-phenylpropanone','propanone','p-nitrobenzaldehyde','NEW','12-363','12-233','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260344q.png'),
(260345,'chemistry','Write the name of products of the following reaction diagram:\n(n26022)','Acetone','Hydration of propyne in the presence of  H₂SO₄ and HgSO₄ gives propanone.\n\n"By hydration of alkynes: \n\nAddition of water to ethyne in the presence of H₂SO₄ and HgSO₄ gives acetaldehyde. All other alkynes give ketones in this reaction. "\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'3','mcq','Acetone','Acetaldehyde','Formaldehyde','Ethanol','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260345q.png'),
(260346,'chemistry','Write the name of products of the following reaction diagram:\n(n26023)','p-nitrobenzaldehyde','Use of chromyl chloride : \n\nChromyl chloride oxidises methyl group to a chromium complex, which on hydrolysis gives corresponding benzaldehyde.\n\nThis reaction is called Etard reaction. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -6)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'3','mcq','propiophenone','1-phenylpropanone','propanone','p-nitrobenzaldehyde','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260346q.png'),
(260347,'chemistry','What is the product of the reaction shown in the diagram?\n(n26128)','C','Acyl chloride (acid chloride) is hydrogenated over catalyst, palladium on barium sulphate. \n\nThis reaction is called Rosenmund reduction.\n\nAldehydes can be prepared by hydrogenation of acid chlorides.\n\nReference:(NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260347q.png'),
(260516,'chemistry','Name the intermediate product in the following diagram(n26027)','hemiacetal','Aldehydes react with one equivalent of monohydric alcohol in the presence of dry hydrogen chloride to yield alkoxy alcohol intermediate, known as hemiacetals, which further react with one more molecule of alcohol to give a gem-dialkoxy compound known as acetal.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-367 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','hemiacetal','acetal','cyanide ion','carboxylate ion','NEW','12-367','12-237','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260516q.png'),
(260517,'chemistry','Aldehydes react with one equivalent of  ________ in the presence of dry hydrogen chloride to yield hemi acetals.','monohydric alcohol','Aldehydes react with one equivalent of monohydric alcohol in the presence of dry hydrogen chloride to yield alkoxy alcohol intermediate, known as hemiacetals, which further react with one more molecule of alcohol to give a gem-dialkoxy compound known as acetal.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-367 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','dihydric alcohol','monohydric alcohol','cyanohydrins','acetyl alcohol','NEW','12-367','12-237',NULL),
(260518,'chemistry','Aldehydes react with one equivalent of monohydric alcohol in the presence of dry hydrogen chloride to yield ______ intermediate.','alkoxyalcohol','Aldehydes react with one equivalent of monohydric alcohol in the presence of dry hydrogen chloride to yield alkoxy alcohol intermediate, known as hemiacetals, which further react with one more molecule of alcohol to give a gem-dialkoxy compound known as acetal.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-367 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','alcohol','alkyl','alkoxyalcohol','alkoxy','NEW','12-367','12-237',NULL),
(260519,'chemistry','Alkoxy Alcohol intermediate, known as ______.','hemiacetals','Aldehydes react with one equivalent of monohydric alcohol in the presence of dry hydrogen chloride to yield alkoxy alcohol intermediate, known as hemiacetals, which further react with one more molecule of alcohol to give a gem-dialkoxy compound known as acetal.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-367 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','acetals','ketals','hemiacetals','stable alkoxy intermediate','NEW','12-367','12-237',NULL),
(260520,'chemistry','________further react with one more molecule of alcohol to give a gem-dialkoxy.','hemiacetals','Aldehydes react with one equivalent of monohydric alcohol in the presence of dry hydrogen chloride to yield alkoxy alcohol intermediate, known as hemiacetals, which further react with one more molecule of alcohol to give a gem-dialkoxy compound known as acetal.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-367 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','acetals','ketals','hemiacetals','stable alkoxy intermediate','NEW','12-367','12-237',NULL),
(260521,'chemistry','Gem-dialkoxy compound known as ____.','acetals','Aldehydes react with one equivalent of monohydric alcohol in the presence of dry hydrogen chloride to yield alkoxy alcohol intermediate, known as hemiacetals, which further react with one more molecule of alcohol to give a gem-dialkoxy compound known as acetal.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-367 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','acetals','ketals','hemiacetals','stable alkoxy intermediate','NEW','12-367','12-237',NULL),
(260522,'chemistry','What is the other reagent except ketone in the given diagram(n26028)','ethylene glycol','Ketones react with ethylene glycol under similar conditions to form cyclic products known as ethylene glycol ketals.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-367 Paragraph -5)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','glycine','ethylene glycol','glycolic acid','ethanol','NEW','12-367','12-237','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260522q.png'),
(260523,'chemistry','Which of the following alternative is correct for the treatment of ketones with ethylene glycol in the presence of dry HCl?','HCl protonates the oxygen of the carbonyl group','Ketones react with ethylene glycol under similar conditions to form cyclic products known as ethylene glycol ketals.\n\nDry hydrogen chloride protonates the oxygen of the carbonyl compounds and therefore, increases the electrophilicity of the carbonyl carbon facilitating the nucleophilic attack of ethylene glycol.\n\nAcetals and ketals are hydrolysed with aqueous mineral acids to yield corresponding aldehydes and ketones respectively. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-367 Paragraph -5)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','The product obtained is ethylene glycol hemi-ketal','In this reaction, electrophilicity of carbonyl carbon decreases','HCl protonates the oxygen of the carbonyl group','The product thus obtained is cyanohydrin','NEW','12-367','12-237',NULL),
(260524,'chemistry','Dry hydrogen chloride protonates the oxygen of the carbonyl compounds and therefore ________  the electrophilicity.','increases','Dry hydrogen chloride protonates the oxygen of the carbonyl compounds and therefore, increases the electrophilicity of the carbonyl carbon facilitating the nucleophilic attack of ethylene glycol. \n\nAcetals and ketals are hydrolysed with aqueous mineral acids to yield corresponding aldehydes and ketones respectively.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','increases','decreases','has no effect','None of the above','NEW','12-368','12-238',NULL),
(260525,'chemistry','Acetals and ketals are hydrolysed with _____ to yield corresponding aldehydes and ketones.','aqueous mineral acid','Dry hydrogen chloride protonates the oxygen of the carbonyl compounds and therefore, increases the electrophilicity of the carbonyl carbon facilitating the nucleophilic attack of ethylene glycol. \n\nAcetals and ketals are hydrolysed with aqueous mineral acids to yield corresponding aldehydes and ketones respectively.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','strong alkali','mild alcoholic solution','mild acid','aqueous mineral acid','NEW','12-368','12-238',NULL),
(260526,'chemistry','Addition of ammonia and its derivative is ________.','reversible','Addition of ammonia and its derivatives: \n\nNucleophiles, such as ammonia and its derivatives H₂N-Z add to the carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones. \n\nThe reaction is reversible and catalysed by acid. \n\nThe equilibrium favours the product formation due to rapid dehydration of the intermediate to form >C=N-Z.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','irreversible','electrophillic','reversible','None of the above','NEW','12-368','12-238',NULL),
(260527,'chemistry','In addition of ammonia and its derivative,the equilibrium favours the ______ formation.','product','Addition of ammonia and its derivatives: \n\nNucleophiles, such as ammonia and its derivatives H₂N-Z add to the carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones. \n\nThe reaction is reversible and catalysed by acid. \n\nThe equilibrium favours the product formation due to rapid dehydration of the intermediate to form >C=N-Z.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','reactant','product','carbon dioxide','ammonia','NEW','12-368','12-238',NULL),
(260528,'chemistry','The equilibrium favours the product formation due to rapid ________ of the intermediate.','dehydration','Addition of ammonia and its derivatives: \n\nNucleophiles, such as ammonia and its derivatives H₂N-Z add to the carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones. \n\nThe reaction is reversible and catalysed by acid. \n\nThe equilibrium favours the product formation due to rapid dehydration of the intermediate to form >C=N-Z.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','hydration','dehydration','hydrohalogenation','hydrogenation','NEW','12-368','12-238',NULL),
(260529,'chemistry','Aldehydes and ketones are reduced to primary and secondary alcohols respectively by _____','catalytic reduction','Aldehydes and ketones are reduced to primary and secondary alcohols respectively by sodium borohydride or lithium aluminium hydride as well as by catalytic hydrogenation.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','catalytic oxidation','catalytic reduction','alkaline solution','all of the above','NEW','12-368','12-238',NULL),
(260530,'chemistry','Reagent(s) used for the reduction of aldehydes and ketones are all ,except ?','Grignard reagent','Aldehydes and ketones are reduced to primary and secondary alcohols respectively by sodium borohydride or lithium aluminium hydride as well as by catalytic hydrogenation.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','LiAlH₄','NaBH₄','Catalytic hydrogenation','Grignard reagent','NEW','12-368','12-238',NULL),
(260531,'chemistry','Name the reaction shown in the diagram(n26029)','Clemmensen','Reduction to hydrocarbons: \n\nThe carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones is reduced to CH₂ group on treatment with zinc amalgam and concentrated hydrochloric acid [Clemmensen reduction] or with hydrazine followed by heating with sodium or potassium hydroxide in high boiling solvent such as ethylene glycol (Wolff-Kishner reduction).\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Rosenmund','Clemmensen','Stephen','Wolff-Kishner','NEW','12-368','12-238','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260531q.png'),
(260532,'chemistry','Name the reaction shown in the diagram(n26030)','Wolff-Kishner','Reduction to hydrocarbons: \n\nThe carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones is reduced to CH₂ group on treatment with zincamalgam and concentrated hydrochloric acid [Clemmensenreduction] or with hydrazine followed by heating with sodium or potassium hydroxide in high boiling solvent such as ethylene glycol (Wolff-Kishner reduction).\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Rosenmund','Clemmensen','Stephen','Wolff-Kishner','NEW','12-368','12-238','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260532q.png'),
(260533,'chemistry','All of the following can be easily oxidised to carboxylic acids, except','Ketones','Aldehydes differ from ketones in their oxidation reactions. \n\nAldehydes are easily oxidised to carboxylic acids on treatment with common oxidising agents like nitric acid, potassium permanganate, potassium dichromate, etc. \n\nEven mild oxidising agents, mainly Tollens’ reagent and Fehlings’ reagent also oxidise aldehydes.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-369 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Alcohol','Aldehydes','Ketones','All of these','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(260534,'chemistry','Aldehydes are easily converted to carboxylic acids on treatment with','Oxidising agent','Aldehydes differ from ketones in their oxidation reactions. \n\nAldehydes are easily oxidised to carboxylic acids on treatment with common oxidising agents like nitric acid, potassium permanganate, potassium dichromate, etc. \n\nEven mild oxidising agents, mainly Tollens’ reagent and Fehlings’ reagent also oxidise aldehydes.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-369 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','HCl','Oxidising agent','Reducing agent','Acetone','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(260535,'chemistry','Which of the following reagents is not used in the given reaction?"RCHO--------->RCOOH"','Potassium permanganate','Aldehydes differ from ketones in their oxidation reactions. \n\nAldehydes are easily oxidised to carboxylic acids on treatment with common oxidising agents like nitric acid, potassium permanganate, potassium dichromate, etc. \n\nEven mild oxidising agents, mainly Tollens’ reagent and Fehling\'s’ reagent also oxidise aldehydes.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-369 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Nitric acid','Potassium dichromate','Tollen’s reagent','Potassium permanganate','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(260536,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is/are correct?','Oxidation of ketone involves carbon-carbon bond cleavage to give a mixture of carboxylic acids having lesser number of C-atoms than the parent ketones','Ketones are generally oxidised under vigorous conditions, i.e.,strong oxidising agents and at elevated temperatures. \n\nTheir oxidation involves carbon-carbon bond cleavage to afford a mixture of carboxylic acids having lesser number of carbon atoms than the parent ketone.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-369 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Aldehydes are generally oxidised under vigorous conditions','Ketones are easily oxidised to carboxylic acids even under mild oxidising agents','Oxidation of ketone involves carbon-carbon bond cleavage to give a mixture of carboxylic acids having lesser number of C-atoms than the parent ketones','All of the above','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(260537,'chemistry','Agents which are  used to distinguish aldehydes from ketones.','Mild oxidising agent','The mild oxidising agents given below are used to distinguish aldehydes from ketones.\n \nTollens’ test: \n\nOn warming an aldehyde with freshly prepared ammoniacal silver nitrate solution (Tollens’ reagent), a bright silver mirror is produced due to the formation of silver metal. \n\nThe aldehydes are oxidised to corresponding carboxylate anion.\n \nThe reaction occurs in alkaline medium.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-369 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Mild reducing agent','Strong reducing agent','Mild oxidising agent','Strong oxidising agent','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(260538,'chemistry','The Tollen\'s reaction occurs in which medium?','alkaline','The mild oxidising agents given below are used to distinguish aldehydes from ketones. \n\nTollens’ test: On warming an aldehyde with freshly prepared ammoniacal silver nitrate solution (Tollens’ reagent), a bright silver mirror is produced due to the formation of silver metal. \n\nThe aldehydes are oxidised to corresponding carboxylate anion. \n\nThe reaction occurs in alkaline medium.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-369 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','acidic','alkaline','neutral','O containing medium','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(260539,'chemistry','Name the reaction given in the diagram(n26031)','Tollens','The mild oxidising agents given below are used to distinguish aldehydes from ketones. \n\nTollens’ test: On warming an aldehyde with freshly prepared ammoniacal silver nitrate solution (Tollens’ reagent), a bright silver mirror is produced due to the formation of silver metal. \n\nThe aldehydes are oxidised to corresponding carboxylate anion. \n\nThe reaction occurs in alkaline medium.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-369 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Fehling\'s','Tollens','Clemmensen','Haloform','NEW','12-369','12-239','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260539q.png'),
(260540,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following:\n\nAssertion (A): A bright silver mirror is produced during the warming of an aldehyde with freshly prepared  ammonical silver nitrate solution.\n\nReason (R): A bright silver mirror is produced due to the formation of silver metal.','Both A and R are correct; and R is the correct explanation of A','The mild oxidising agents given below are used to distinguish aldehydes from ketones.\n\nTollens’ test: On warming an aldehyde with freshly prepared ammoniacal silver nitrate solution (Tollens’ reagent), a bright silver mirror is produced due to the formation of silver metal. \n\nThe aldehydes are oxidised to corresponding carboxylate anion. \n\nThe reaction occurs in alkaline medium.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-369 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are correct; and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are correct; but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is correct; but R is incorrect','R is correct; but A is incorrect','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(260541,'chemistry','Name the reaction given in the diagram(n26032)','Fehling\'s','Fehling reagent comprises of two solutions, Fehling solution A and Fehling solution B. \n\nFehling solution A is aqueous copper sulphate and Fehling solution B is alkaline sodium potassium tartrate (Rochelle salt). \n\nThese two solutions are mixed in equal amounts before test. \n\nOn heating an aldehyde with Fehling’s reagent, a reddish brown precipitate is obtained. \nAldehydes are oxidised to corresponding carboxylate anion. \n\nAromatic aldehydes do not respond to this test.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-369 Paragraph -5)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Fehling\'s','Tollens','Clemmensen','Haloform','NEW','12-369','12-239','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260541q.png'),
(260542,'chemistry','Composition of Fehling solution A and Fehling solution B is?','an aqueous copper sulphate and alkaline sodium potassium tartrate (Rochelle salt) respectively','Fehling reagent comprises of two solutions, Fehling solution A and Fehling solution B. \n\nFehling solution A is aqueous copper sulphate and Fehling solution B is alkaline sodium potassium tartrate (Rochelle salt). \n\nThese two solutions are mixed in equal amounts before test. \n\nOn heating an aldehyde with Fehling’s reagent, a reddish brown precipitate is obtained.\n \nAldehydes are oxidised to corresponding carboxylate anion. \n\nAromatic aldehydes do not respond to this test.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-369 Paragraph -5)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','an aqueous copper sulphate (Rochelle salt) and acidic sodium potassium tartrate respectively','an acidic sodium potassium tartrate (Rochelle salt) and aqueous copper sulphate respectively','an aqueous copper sulphate and alkaline sodium potassium tartrate (Rochelle salt) respectively','an alkaline sodium potassium tartrate (Rochelle salt) and aqueous copper sulphate respectively','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(260543,'chemistry','On heating an aldehyde with Fehling’s reagent, a reddish brown precipitate is obtained due to?','Cu₂O','Fehling reagent comprises of two solutions, Fehling solution A and Fehling solution B. \n\nFehling solution A is aqueous copper sulphate and Fehling solution B is alkaline sodium potassium tartrate (Rochelle salt). \n\nThese two solutions are mixed in equal amounts before test. \n\nOn heating an aldehyde with Fehling’s reagent, a reddish brown precipitate is obtained.\n \nAldehydes are oxidised to corresponding carboxylate anion. \n\nAromatic aldehydes do not respond to this test.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-369 Paragraph -5)\ndiagram(ne26099)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','CuO','Cu₂O','CuSO₄','Cu(OH)₂','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(260544,'chemistry','Which of the following carbonyl group give the positive Fehling test?','Aliphatic aldehydes','Fehling reagent comprises of two solutions, Fehling solution A and Fehling solution B.\n \nFehling solution A is aqueous copper sulphate and Fehling solution B is alkaline sodium potassium tartrate (Rochelle salt). \n\nThese two solutions are mixed in equal amounts before test.\n\nOn heating an aldehyde with Fehling’s reagent, a reddish brown precipitate is obtained.\n \nAldehydes are oxidised to corresponding carboxylate anion. \n\nAromatic aldehydes do not respond to this test.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-369 Paragraph -5)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Aliphatic aldehydes','Aromatic aldehydes','Ketones','Both a and b','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(260545,'chemistry','Iodoform reaction with sodium hypoiodite is also used for detection of ______ group.','acetyl','Aldehydes and ketones having at least one methyl group linked to the carbonyl carbon atom (methyl ketones) are oxidised by sodium hypohalite to sodium salts of corresponding carboxylic acids having one carbon atom less than that of carbonyl compound. \n\nThe methyl group is converted to haloform. \n\nThis oxidation does not affect a carbon-carbon double bond, if present in the molecule.\n\nIodoform reaction with sodium hypoiodite is also used for detection of CH₃CO group or CH₃CH(OH) group which produces CH₃CO group on oxidation.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-370 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','alcohol','aldehyde','ketone','acetyl','NEW','12-370','12-240',NULL),
(260546,'chemistry','Name the reaction given in the diagram(n26033)','Haloform','Aldehydes and ketones having at least one methyl group linked to the carbonyl carbon atom (methyl ketones) are oxidised by sodium hypohalite to sodium salts of corresponding carboxylic acids having one carbon atom less than that of carbonyl compound. \n\nThe methyl group is converted to haloform. \n\nThis oxidation does not affect a carbon-carbon double bond, if present in the molecule.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-370 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Fehling\'s','Tollens','Clemmensen','Haloform','NEW','12-370','12-240','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260546q.png'),
(260547,'chemistry','Name the reaction given in the diagram(n26034)','Haloform','Aldehydes and ketones having at least one methyl group linked to the carbonyl carbon atom (methyl ketones) are oxidised by sodium hypohalite to sodium salts of corresponding carboxylic acids having one carbon atom less than that of carbonyl compound. \n\nThe methyl group is converted to haloform. \n\nThis oxidation does not affect a carbon-carbon double bond, if present in the molecule.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-370 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Fehling\'s','Tollens','Clemmensen','Haloform','NEW','12-370','12-240','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260547q.png'),
(30715,'chemistry','Identify the variation of atomic radius with atomic number for alkali metals and halogens?','Atomic radius increases when atomic no. increases','When an Atomic radius of an element increases, the atomic no. of an element increases; this is applicable for alkali metals and halogens. \n\nIt is shown in diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 87; diagram(ne0321)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Atomic radius',NULL,'2','mcq','Atomic radius decreases when atomic no. increases','Atomic radius remains equal with atomic no.','Atomic radius increases when atomic no. decreases','Atomic radius increases when atomic no. increases','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(260548,'chemistry','An organic compound contains 69.77% carbon, 11.63% hydrogen and rest oxygen.\n\nThe molecular mass of the compound is 86. \n\nIt does not reduce Tollen’s reagent but forms an addition compound with sodium hydrogen sulphite and give positive iodoform test on vigorous oxidation it gives ethanoic and propanoic acid. \n\nWhat is the possible structure of the compound? diagram(n26072)','a','Carbonyl compounds give positive iodoform test. Hence, it may be an aldehyde or ketone. \n\nBut it doesn\'t reduce Tollen\'s reagent. Hence, it is not an aldehyde as aldehydes give positive Tollens test.\n\nThus option (c) and (d) both are carboxylic acid are ruled out.  \n\nAs only methyl ketones give positive iodoform test. \n\nHence, In option (d) cyclohexanone, there is no methyl group is present and it is also ruled out. \n\nSo option (a) pentan-2-one, is the correct answer because it is a methyl ketone and give positive iodoform test.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 page-387 question-12.19 )','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-387','12-257','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260548q.png'),
(260549,'chemistry','Select the incorrect alternatives for haloform reaction.','CH₃—O group undergoes this reaction','Aldehydes and ketones having at least one methyl group linked to the carbonyl carbon atom (methyl ketones) are oxidised by sodium hypohalite to sodium salts of corresponding carboxylic acids having one carbon atom less than that of carbonyl compound. \n\nThe methyl group is converted to haloform. \n\nThis oxidation does not affect a carbon-carbon double bond, if present in the molecule.\n\nIodoform reaction with sodium hypoiodite is also used for detection of CH₃CO group or CH₃CH(OH) group which produces CH₃CO group on oxidation.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-370 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','CH₃—O group undergoes this reaction','CH₃CO and CH₃CH(OH) groups take part in this reaction','Sodium hypohalite is used as a reagent in this reaction','Both (b) and (c)','NEW','12-370','12-240',NULL),
(260550,'chemistry','The aldehydes and ketones undergo a number of reactions due to the _____ nature of α-hydrogen.','acidic','Acidity of α-hydrogens of aldehydes and ketones: \n\nThe aldehydes and ketones undergo a number of reactions due to the acidic nature of α-hydrogen. \n\nThe acidity of α-hydrogen atoms of carbonyl compounds is due to the strong electron withdrawing effect of the carbonyl group and resonance stabilisation of the conjugate base.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-371 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','alcoholic','acidic','basic','neutral','NEW','12-371','12-241',NULL),
(260551,'chemistry','The acidity of α-hydrogen atoms of carbonyl compounds is due to the strong  ______ effect of carbonyl group.','electron withdrawing','Acidity of α-hydrogens of aldehydes and ketones: \n\nThe aldehydes and ketones undergo a number of reactions due to the acidic nature of α-hydrogen. \n\nThe acidity of α-hydrogen atoms of carbonyl compounds is due to the strong electron withdrawing effect of the carbonyl group and resonance stabilisation of the conjugate base.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-371 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','electron donating','electron withdrawing','oxidising','all of the above','NEW','12-371','12-241',NULL),
(260552,'chemistry','The acidity of α-hydrogen atoms of carbonyl compounds is due resonance stabilisation of the conjugate _____.','base','Acidity of α-hydrogens of aldehydes and ketones: \n\nThe aldehydes and ketones undergo a number of reactions due to the acidic nature of α-hydrogen. \n\nThe acidity of α-hydrogen atoms of carbonyl compounds is due to the strong electron withdrawing effect of the carbonyl group and resonance stabilisation of the conjugate base.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-371 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','acid','base','both a and b','none of the above','NEW','12-371','12-241',NULL),
(260553,'chemistry','Name the reaction given in the diagram(n26035)','Aldol condensation','Aldol condensation: \n\nAldehydes and ketones having at least one α-hydrogen undergo a reaction in the presence of dilute alkali as catalyst to form β-hydroxy aldehydes (aldol) or β-hydroxy ketones (ketol), respectively. \n\nThis is known as Aldol reaction.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-371 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Aldol condensation','Cross aldol','Cannizaros reaction','Clemmensen reduction','NEW','12-371','12-241','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260553q.png'),
(260554,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is/are correct about the aldol reaction?','The product formed is β-hydroxy aldehydes (aldol) or β-hydroxy ketone (ketol)','Aldol condensation: \n\nAldehydes and ketones having at least one α-hydrogen undergo a reaction in the presence of dilute alkali as catalyst to form β-hydroxy aldehydes (aldol) or β-hydroxy ketones (ketol), respectively. \n\nThis is known as Aldol reaction.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-371 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Aldehyde and ketones having at least one β-hydrogen','The reaction is carried out in the presence of concentrated alkali','The product formed is β-hydroxy aldehydes (aldol) or β-hydroxy ketone (ketol)','All of the above','NEW','12-371','12-241',NULL),
(260555,'chemistry','Name the reaction given in the diagram(n26036)','Cross aldol','Cross aldol condensation: \n\nWhen aldol condensation is carried out between two different aldehydes and / or ketones, it is called cross aldol condensation. \n\nIf both of them contain α-hydrogen atoms, it gives a mixture of four products. \n\nThis is illustrated below by aldol reaction of a mixture of ethanal and propanal.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-372 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Aldol condensation','Cross aldol','Haloform','Cannizzaro','NEW','12-372','12-242','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260555q.png'),
(260556,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following:\n\nAssertion (A): Benzaldehyde on heating with concentrated alkali give a, [3-unsaturated carbonyl compound.\n\nReason (R): Benzaldehyde do not have an  α  H-atom.','R is correct; but A is incorrect','Aldol condensation: \n\nAldehydes and ketones having at least one α-hydrogen undergo a reaction in the presence of dilute alkali as catalyst to form β-hydroxy aldehydes (aldol) or β-hydroxy ketones (ketol), respectively. \n\nThis is known as Aldol reaction. \n\nFormaldehyde and benzaldehyde do not undergo aldol condensation because they both do not have an α-Hydrogen atom. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-371 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are correct; and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are correct; but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is correct; but R is incorrect','R is correct; but A is incorrect','NEW','12-371','12-241',NULL),
(260557,'chemistry','Name the reaction given in the diagram(n26037)','Cannizzaro','Cannizzaro reaction: \n\nAldehydes which do not have an α-hydrogen atom, undergo self oxidation and reduction (disproportionation) reaction on heating with concentrated alkali. \n\nIn this reaction, one molecule of the aldehyde is reduced to alcohol while another is oxidised to carboxylic acid salt.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-372 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Aldol condensation','Cross aldol','ESR','Cannizzaro','NEW','12-372','12-242','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260557q.png'),
(260558,'chemistry','Name the reaction given in the diagram(n26038)','Cannizzaro','Cannizzaro reaction: \n\nAldehydes which do not have an α-hydrogen atom, undergo self oxidation and reduction (disproportionation) reaction on heating with concentrated alkali. \n\nIn this reaction, one molecule of the aldehyde is reduced to alcohol while another is oxidised to carboxylic acid salt.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-372 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Aldol condensation','Cross aldol','ESR','Cannizzaro','NEW','12-372','12-242','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260558q.png'),
(260559,'chemistry','An organic compound with the molecular formula, C₉H₁₀O forms 2,4-DNP derivative, reduces Tollen’s reagent and undergoes Cannizzaro reaction. \nOn vigorous oxidation, it gives 1,2-benzenedicarboxylic acid. Identify the compound?','2- ethylbenzaldehyde','Cannizzaro reaction: \n\nAldehydes which do not have an α-hydrogen atom, undergo self oxidation and reduction (disproportionation) reaction on heating with concentrated alkali. \n\nIn this reaction, one molecule of the aldehyde is reduced to alcohol while another is oxidised to carboxylic acid salt.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-372 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','2- methylbenzaldehyde','2- ethylbenzaldehyde','2- ethylbenzoate','2- ethylbenzoic acid','NEW','12-372','12-242',NULL),
(260560,'chemistry','Name the reaction given in the diagram(n26039)','ESR','Electrophilic substitution reaction: \n\nAromatic aldehydes and ketones undergo electrophilic substitution at the ring in which the carbonyl group acts as a deactivating and meta-directing group.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-373 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Aldol condensation','Cross aldol','ESR','Cannizzaro','NEW','12-373','12-243','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260560q.png'),
(260561,'chemistry','Arrange the following compounds in increasing order of their reactivity in nucleophilic addition reactions.','Ethanal>Propanal>Propanone>Butanone','Aldehydes are generally more reactive than ketones in nucleophilic addition reactions due to steric and electronic reasons. \n\nSterically, the presence of two relatively large substituents in ketones hinders the approach of nucleophile to carbonyl carbon than in aldehydes having only one such substituent.\n\nElectronically, aldehydes are more reactive than ketones because two alkyl groups reduce the electrophilicity of the carbonyl carbon more effectively than in former.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-366 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','order','1,Ethanal','2,Propanal','3,Propanone','4,Butanone','NEW','12-363','12-233',NULL),
(260562,'chemistry','Arrange the following compounds in increasing order of their reactivity in nucleophilic addition reactions.','p-Nitrobenzaldehyde>Benzaldehyde>p-Tolualdehyde>Acetophenone','Aldehydes are generally more reactive than ketones in nucleophilic addition reactions due to steric and electronic reasons. \n\nSterically, the presence of two relatively large substituents in ketones hinders the approach of nucleophile to carbonyl carbon than in aldehydes having only one such substituent.\n\nElectronically, aldehydes are more reactive than ketones because two alkyl groups reduce the electrophilicity of the carbonyl carbon more effectively than in former.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-366 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','order','1,Benzaldehyde','2,p-Tolualdehyde','3,p-Nitrobenzaldehyde','4,Acetophenone','NEW','12-366','12-236',NULL),
(260563,'chemistry','Write the name of products of the following reaction diagram(n26040)','hydrazone','Addition of ammonia and its derivatives: \n\nNucleophiles, such as ammonia and its derivatives H₂N-Z add to the carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones. \n\nThe reaction is reversible and catalysed by acid. \n\nThe equilibrium favours the product formation due to rapid dehydration of the intermediate to form >C=N-Z.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -2) diagram(ne26100)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','oxime','hydrazone','semicarbazone','Schiff\'s base','NEW','12-368','12-238','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260563q.png'),
(260564,'chemistry','Write the name of products of the following reaction diagram(n26041)','oxime','Addition of ammonia and its derivatives: \n\nNucleophiles, such as ammonia and its derivatives H₂N-Z add to the carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones. \n\nThe reaction is reversible and catalysed by acid. \n\nThe equilibrium favours the product formation due to rapid dehydration of the intermediate to form >C=N-Z.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -2) diagram(ne26100)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','oxime','hydrazone','semicarbazone','Schiff\'s base','NEW','12-368','12-238','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260564q.png'),
(260565,'chemistry','Write the name of products of the following reaction diagram(n26042)','semicarbazone','Addition of ammonia and its derivatives: \n\nNucleophiles, such as ammonia and its derivatives H₂N-Z add to the carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones. \n\nThe reaction is reversible and catalysed by acid. \n\nThe equilibrium favours the product formation due to rapid dehydration of the intermediate to form >C=N-Z.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph 2) diagram(ne26100)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','oxime','hydrazone','semicarbazone','Schiff\'s base','NEW','12-368','12-238','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260565q.png'),
(260566,'chemistry','Write the name of products of the following reaction diagram(n26043)','Schiff\'s base','Addition of ammonia and its derivatives: \n\nNucleophiles, such as ammonia and its derivatives H₂N-Z add to the carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones. \n\nThe reaction is reversible and catalysed by acid. \n\nThe equilibrium favours the product formation due to rapid dehydration of the intermediate to form >C=N-Z.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -2) diagram(ne26100)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','oxime','hydrazone','semicarbazone','Schiff\'s base','NEW','12-368','12-238','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260566q.png'),
(260567,'chemistry','Predict the products formed when cyclohexane carbaldehyde reacts with following reagents. \nSemicarbazide and weak acid refer diagram(n26071)','A','Addition of ammonia and its derivatives: \n\nNucleophiles, such as ammonia and its derivatives H₂N-Z add to the carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones. \n\nThe reaction is reversible and catalysed by acid. \n\nThe equilibrium favours the product formation due to rapid dehydration of the intermediate to form >C=N-Z.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','12-368','12-238','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260567q.png'),
(260568,'chemistry','Which of the statements are true about the addition of nucleophile to carbonyl compounds:\n\nI.  Addition of HCN to the carbonyl compounds is base catalysed.\n\nII. Addition of alcohols to the carbonyl compounds is acid catalysed.\n\nIII.Addition of ammonia can be done through base catalysed mechanism.','Both I and II are true','Addition of hydrogen cyanide (HCN) to aldehydes and ketones:\n\nAldehydes and ketones react with hydrogen cyanide (HCN) to yield cyanohydrins. This reaction occurs very slowly with pure HCN. \n\nTherefore, it is catalysed by a base and the generated cyanide ion (CN-) being a stronger nucleophile readily adds to carbonyl compounds to yield corresponding cyanohydrin.\n\nAddition of alcohols: \n\nAldehydes react with one equivalent of monohydric alcohol in the presence of dry hydrogen chloride to yield alkoxyalcohol intermediate, known as hemiacetals.\n\nDry hydrogen chloride protonates the oxygen of the carbonyl compounds and therefore, increases the electrophilicity of the carbonyl carbon facilitating the nucleophilic attack.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-367 Paragraph -last)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Only I is true','Both II and III are true','Both I and III are true','Both I and II are true','NEW','12-367','12-237',NULL),
(260818,'chemistry','Guess the reagent in the given diagram(n26055)','NaOH','Acidic hydrolysis of esters gives directly carboxylic acids while basic hydrolysis gives carboxylates, which on acidification give corresponding carboxylic acids.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-377 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Methods of Preparation of Carboxylic Acids',NULL,'3','mcq','H₂O','H₃O⁺','H₃O⁻','NaOH','NEW','12-377','12-247','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260818q.png'),
(260819,'chemistry','Name the reagent used in the given diagram(n26056)','Jones reagent','Primary alcohols are readily oxidised to carboxylic acids with common oxidising agents such as potassium permanganate (KMnO₄ ) in neutral, acidic or alkaline media or by potassium dichromate (K₂Cr₂O₇ ) and chromium trioxide (CrO₃ ) in acidic media (Jones reagent).\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-375 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Methods of Preparation of Carboxylic Acids',NULL,'3','mcq','Dry ether','Jones reagent','Grignard reagent','Potassium permanganate + alkali','NEW','12-375','12-245','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260819q.png'),
(260820,'chemistry','Name the reagent used in the given diagram(n26057)','Potassium permanganate + alkali','Aromatic carboxylic acids can be prepared by vigorous oxidation of alkyl benzenes with chromic acid or acidic or alkaline potassium permanganate.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-376 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Methods of Preparation of Carboxylic Acids',NULL,'3','mcq','Dry ether','Jones reagent','Grignard reagent','Potassium permanganate + alkali','NEW','12-376','12-246','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260820q.png'),
(260821,'chemistry','Name the reagent used in the given diagram(n26058)','Tollens Reagent','The mild oxidising agents given below are used to distinguish aldehydes from ketones.\n \nTollens’ test: On warming an aldehyde with freshly prepared ammoniacal silver nitrate solution (Tollens’ reagent), a bright silver mirror is produced due to the formation of silver metal. \n\nThe aldehydes are oxidised to corresponding carboxylate anion. The reaction occurs in alkaline medium.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-369 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Methods of Preparation of Carboxylic Acids',NULL,'3','mcq','Dry ether','Jones reagent','Tollens Reagent','Potassium permanganate + alkali','NEW','12-369','12-239','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260821q.png'),
(260822,'chemistry','Which catalyst is used to convert acetophenone to benzoic acid refer diagram?(n26059)','Potassium permanganate + alkali','Aromatic carboxylic acids can be prepared by vigorous oxidation of alkyl benzenes with chromic acid or acidic or alkaline potassium permanganate.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-376 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Methods of Preparation of Carboxylic Acids',NULL,'3','mcq','Fehling\'s','Jonas reagent','Tollens Reagent','Potassium permanganate + alkali','NEW','12-377','12-247','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260822q.png'),
(260823,'chemistry','Which of the following types of hydrolysis of esters give directly carboxylic acids and carboxylates respectively statement?','Acidic hydrolysis and basic hydrolysis','Acid chlorides when hydrolysed with water give carboxylic acids or more readily hydrolysed with aqueous base to give carboxylate ions which on acidification provide corresponding carboxylic acids.\n\nAnhydrides on the other hand are hydrolysed to corresponding acid(s) with water.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-377 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Methods of Preparation of Carboxylic Acids',NULL,'2','mcq','Acidic hydrolysis and basic hydrolysis','Basic hydrolysis and acidic hydrolysis','Acidic hydrolysis and acidic hydrolysis','Basic hydrolysis and basic hydrolysis','NEW','12-378','12-248',NULL),
(261003,'chemistry','The carboxylic acids unlike _____ they react with weaker bases.','phenol','The carboxylic acids like alcohols evolve hydrogen with electropositive metals and form salts with alkalies similar to phenols. \n\nHowever, unlike phenols they react with weaker bases such as carbonates and hydrogen carbonates to evolve carbon dioxide. \n\nThis reaction is used to detect the presence of carboxyl group in an organic compound.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-379 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','methanol','ethanol','aldehydes','phenol','NEW','12-378','12-248',NULL),
(261004,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following: \n\nAssertion (A): Carboxylic acids are more acidic than phenols. \n\nReason (R): The carboxylate ion is less stabilised than phenoxide ion.','A is correct; but R is incorrect','Carboxylic acids are weaker than mineral acids, but they are stronger acids than alcohols and many simple phenols.\n\nThe higher acidity of carboxylic acids as compared to phenols can be understood by this way.\n\nThe conjugate base of carboxylic acid, a carboxylate ion, is stabilised by two equivalent resonance structures in which the negative charge is at the more electronegative oxygen atom. \n\nThe conjugate base of phenol, a phenoxide ion, has non-equivalent resonance structures in which the negative charge is at the less electronegative carbon atom. \n\nTherefore, resonance in phenoxide ion is not as important as it is in carboxylate ion. \n\nFurther, the negative charge is delocalised over two electronegative oxygen atoms in carboxylate ion whereas it is less effectively delocalised over one oxygen atom and less electronegative carbon atoms in phenoxide ion.\n\nThus, the carboxylate ion is more stabilised than phenoxide ion, so carboxylic acids are more acidic than phenols.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-379 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are correct; and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are correct; but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is correct; but R is incorrect','R is correct; but A is incorrect','NEW','12-378','12-248',NULL),
(261016,'chemistry','Name the catalyst omitted in the given diagram(n26061)','P₂O₅','Carboxylic acids on heating with mineral acids such as H2SO4 or with P2O5 give corresponding anhydride.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-381 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','P₂O₅','acetic acid','HCl','KOH','NEW','12-381','12-251','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261016q.png'),
(261017,'chemistry','Choose the possible catalyst in the diagram:\n\na. H₂SO₄\n\nb. HCl\n\nc. KMnO₄\ndiagram(n26062)','a,b','Carboxylic acids are esterified with alcohols or phenols in the presence of a mineral acid such as concentrated H2SO4 or HCl gas as a catalyst.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-381 Paragraph -5)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,b,c','a,c','NEW','12-381','12-251','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261017q.png'),
(261018,'chemistry','The following steps are given for the mechanism of esterification of carboxylic acids. \n\nI. The protonated esters loses a proton to give the ester. \n\nII. Protonation of the carbonyl oxygen activates the carbonyl group towards nucleophilic addition of the alcohol. \n\nIII. Proton transfer in the tetrahedral intermediate occurs.\n\nWrite the correct order of the steps.','II, III and I','Carboxylic acids are esterified with alcohols or phenols in the presence of a mineral acid such as concentrated H₂SO₄ or HCl gas as a catalyst.\n\nThe esterification of carboxylic acids with alcohols is a kind of nucleophilic acyl substitution. \n\nProtonation of the carbonyl oxygen activates the carbonyl group towards nucleophilic addition of the alcohol. \n\nProton transfer in the tetrahedral intermediate converts the hydroxyl group into -OH₂ group, which, being a better leaving group, is eliminated as neutral water molecule. \n\nThe protonated ester so formed finally loses a proton to give the ester.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-381 Paragraph -5)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','I, II and III','I, III and II','II, III and I','III, II and I','NEW','12-381','12-251',NULL),
(261019,'chemistry','Guess the omitted by-product in diagram(n26063)','POCl₃','The hydroxyl group of carboxylic acids, behaves like that of alcohols and is easily replaced by chlorine atom on treating with PCl₅ , PCl₃ or SOCl₂. \n\nThionyl chloride (SOCl₂ ) is preferred because the other two products are gaseous and escape the reaction mixture making the purification of the products easier.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-382 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','PCl₃','POCl₃','H₃PO₃','SO₂','NEW','12-382','12-252','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261019q.png'),
(261020,'chemistry','Guess the omitted by-product in diagram(n26064)','H₃PO₃','The hydroxyl group of carboxylic acids, behaves like that of alcohols and is easily replaced by chlorine atom on treating with PCl₅ , PCl₃ or SOCl₂.\n\nThionyl chloride (SOCl₂ ) is preferred because the other two products are gaseous and escape the reaction mixture making the purification of the products easier.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-382 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','PCl₃','POCl₃','H₃PO₃','SO₂','NEW','12-382','12-252','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261020q.png'),
(261021,'chemistry','Guess the omitted by-product in diagram(n26065)','SO₂','The hydroxyl group of carboxylic acids, behaves like that of alcohols and is easily replaced by chlorine atom on treating with PCl₅ , PCl₃ or SOCl₂. \n\nThionyl chloride (SOCl₂ ) is preferred because the other two products are gaseous and escape the reaction mixture making the purification of the products easier.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-382 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','PCl₃','POCl₃','H₃PO₃','SO₂','NEW','12-382','12-252','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261021q.png'),
(261022,'chemistry','What is the intermediate in diagram(n26066)','ammonium salt','Carboxylic acids react with ammonia to give ammonium salt which on further heating at high temperature give amides.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-382 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','ammonium salt','amide','acetamide','acetate','NEW','12-382','12-252','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261022q.png'),
(261023,'chemistry','Sodium borohydride does not reduce the ______ group.','carboxyl','Carboxylic acids are reduced to primary alcohols by lithium aluminium hydride or better with diborane. \n\nDiborane does not easily reduce functional groups such as ester, nitro, halo, etc. \nSodium borohydride does not reduce the carboxyl group.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-383 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','carboxyl','aldehydic','alcoholic','none of the above','NEW','12-383','12-253',NULL),
(261024,'chemistry','Name the catalyst omitted in the given diagram(n26067)','Diborane','Carboxylic acids are reduced to primary alcohols by lithium aluminium hydride or better with diborane. \n\nDiborane does not easily reduce functional groups such as ester, nitro, halo, etc. \n\nSodium borohydride does not reduce the carboxyl group.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-383 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','LiAlH₄','Diborane','borohydride','Hydrazine','NEW','12-383','12-253','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261024q.png'),
(261025,'chemistry','Diborane easily reduce the functional group such as?','carboxylic acid','Carboxylic acids are reduced to primary alcohols by lithium aluminium hydride or better with diborane. \n\nDiborane does not easily reduce functional groups such as ester, nitro, halo, etc. \n\nSodium borohydride does not reduce the carboxyl group.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-383 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','carboxylic acid','ester','both b and d','nitro','NEW','12-383','12-253',NULL),
(261026,'chemistry','Name the reaction shown in diagram(n26068)','Kolbe electrolysis','Alkali metal salts of carboxylic acids also undergo decarboxylation on electrolysis of their aqueous solutions and form hydrocarbons having twice the number of carbon atoms present in the alkyl group of the acid. \n\nThe reaction is known as Kolbe electrolysis.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-383 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','catalytic hydrogenation','Kolbe electrolysis','HVZ','Friedel-crafts','NEW','12-383','12-253','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261026q.png'),
(261027,'chemistry','Soda Lime is the mixture of?','NaOH and CaO in the ratio of 3 :1','Carboxylic acids lose carbon dioxide to form hydrocarbons when their sodium salts are heated with soda lime (NaOH and CaO in the ratio of 3 : 1). \n\nThe reaction is known as decarboxylation.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-383 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','NaOH and CaO in the ratio of 3 : 2','NaOH and CaO in the ratio of 1: 3','NaOH and CaO in the ratio of 3 :1','NaOH and CaO in the ratio of 2: 3','NEW','12-383','12-253',NULL),
(261028,'chemistry','Alkali metal salts of carboxylic acids also undergo decarboxylation on electrolysis of their aqueous solutions and form hydrocarbons having twice the number of C-atoms present in the alkyl group of the acid. The reaction is known as?','Kolbe electrolysis','Alkali metal salts of carboxylic acids also undergo decarboxylation on electrolysis of their aqueous solutions and form hydrocarbons having twice the number of carbon atoms present in the alkyl group of the acid. \n\nThe reaction is known as Kolbe electrolysis.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-383 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Reduction','Kolbe electrolysis','HVZ','Friedel-crafts','NEW','12-383','12-253',NULL),
(261029,'chemistry','Name the reaction shown in diagram(n26069)','HVZ','Carboxylic acids having an α-hydrogen are halogenated at the α-position on treatment with chlorine or bromine in the presence of small amount of red phosphorus to give α-halo carboxylic acids. \n\nThe reaction is known as Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-383 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Decarboxylation','Kolbe','HVZ','Friedel-crafts','NEW','12-383','12-253','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261029q.png'),
(261030,'chemistry','Carboxylic acids having an α-hydrogen are halogenated  in the presence of small amount of _____ phosphorus.','red','Carboxylic acids having an α-hydrogen are halogenated at the α-position on treatment with chlorine or bromine in the presence of small amount of red phosphorus to give α-halo carboxylic acids. \n\nThe reaction is known as Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-383 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','red','white','black','yellow','NEW','12-383','12-253',NULL),
(261031,'chemistry','Name the reaction shown in diagram(n26070)','ESR','Aromatic carboxylic acids undergo electrophilic substitution reactions in which the carboxyl group acts as a deactivating and meta-directing group. \n\nThey however, do not undergo Friedel-Crafts reaction (because the carboxyl group is deactivating and the catalyst aluminium chloride (Lewis acid) gets bonded to the carboxyl group).\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-384 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','ESR','decarboxylation','halogenation','NAR','NEW','12-384','12-254','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261031q.png'),
(261032,'chemistry','Aromatic carboxylic acids do not undergo _______ reaction.','Friedel-Crafts reaction','Aromatic carboxylic acids undergo electrophilic substitution reactions in which the carboxyl group acts as a deactivating and meta-directing group. \n\nThey however, do not undergo Friedel-Crafts reaction (because the carboxyl group is deactivating and the catalyst aluminium chloride (Lewis acid) gets bonded to the carboxyl group).\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-384 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Friedel-Crafts reaction','reduction','halogenation','EAR','NEW','12-384','12-254',NULL),
(261033,'chemistry','The catalyst aluminium chloride act as _____ in ring substitution.','Lewis acid','Aromatic carboxylic acids undergo electrophilic substitution reactions in which the carboxyl group acts as a deactivating and meta-directing group. \n\nThey however, do not undergo Friedel-Crafts reaction (because the carboxyl group is deactivating and the catalyst aluminium chloride (Lewis acid) gets bonded to the carboxyl group).\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-384 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Lewis acid','Lewis base','hydrogen acceptor','none of the above','NEW','12-384','12-254',NULL),
(261034,'chemistry','Aromatic carboxylic acids do not undergo Friedel-Crafts reaction because the _______ group is deactivating','carboxyl','Aromatic carboxylic acids undergo electrophilic substitution reactions in which the carboxyl group acts as a deactivating and meta-directing group. \n\nThey however, do not undergo Friedel-Crafts reaction (because the carboxyl group is deactivating and the catalyst aluminium chloride (Lewis acid) gets bonded to the carboxyl group).\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-384 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','carbon','oxygen','carboxyl','ring','NEW','12-384','12-254',NULL),
(261035,'chemistry','Why the aromatic carboxylic acids do not undergo Friedel-Crafts reaction?','Carboxyl group act as a deactivating and meta-directing group','Aromatic carboxylic acids undergo electrophilic substitution reactions in which the carboxyl group acts as a deactivating and meta-directing group. \n\nThey however, do not undergo Friedel-Crafts reaction (because the carboxyl group is deactivating and the catalyst aluminium chloride (Lewis acid) gets bonded to the carboxyl group).\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-384 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Carboxyl group act as a deactivating and meta-directing group','Carboxyl group act as a deactivating and ortho-directing group','Carboxyl group act as a activating and or para-directing group','Carboxyl group acts as deactivating and the catalyst','NEW','12-384','12-254',NULL),
(261036,'chemistry','Name the product form during the decarboxylation of malonic acid?','Acetic acid','Alkali metal salts of carboxylic acids also undergo decarboxylation on electrolysis of their aqueous solutions and form hydrocarbons having twice the number of carbon atoms present in the alkyl group of the acid. \n\nThe reaction is known as Kolbe electrolysis.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-383 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Acetic acid','Ethanone','Propanone','Formic acid','NEW','12-383','12-253',NULL),
(261037,'chemistry','The reactivity of acid derivatives towards nucleophilic acyl substitution reactions decrease in the order of?','Acid chloride > anhydride > ester > amide','Electron withdrawing group (EWG) stabilises the carboxylate anion and strengthens the acid.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-380 Paragraph -Last)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Acid chloride > anhydride > ester > amide','Acid chloride > ester > anhydride > amide','Amide > anhydride > ester > acid chloride','Amide > ester > acid chloride > anhydride','NEW','12-380','12-250',NULL),
(261038,'chemistry','3-Hydroxyhexanal on heating yields?','Hexen-2-enal','The name aldol is derived from the names of the two functional groups, aldehyde and alcohol, present in the products.\n\nThe aldol and ketol readily lose water to give α,β-unsaturated carbonyl compounds which are aldol condensation products and the reaction is called Aldol condensation.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-371 Paragraph -last)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Hexen-2-enal','Hexan-2-one','Hexanal','Pentan-3-one','NEW','12-371','12-241',NULL),
(261429,'chemistry','Give the IUPAC name of the compound given in the below diagram(n26112)','3-phenylprop-2-en-1-al','The IUPAC names of open chain aliphatic aldehydes and ketones are derived from the names of the corresponding alkanes by replacing the ending –e with –al and –one respectively. \n\nIn case of aldehydes the longest carbon chain is numbered starting from the carbon of the aldehyde group while in case of ketones the numbering begins from the end nearer to the carbonyl group.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-359 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1-phenylprop-1-en-2-al','1-phenylprop-2-en-1-al','3-phenylprop-2-en-1-al','2-phenylprop-1-en-1-al','NEW','12-359','12-229','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261429q.png'),
(261430,'chemistry','Benzophenone can be obtained by:\n\na. benzoyl chloride+benzene+AlCl₃\n\nb. benzoyl chloride+ diphenyl cadmium\n\nc. benzal chloride+ phenyl magnesium chloride','a,b','Benzophenone can be produced by Friedal Craft acylation reaction or the reaction between benzoyl chloride and diphenyl Cd\n\ndiagram(ne26101)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,c','a,b','b,c','All of the above','NEW','12-364','12-234',NULL),
(261431,'chemistry','Through which of the following reactions number of Carbon atoms can be increased in the chain?\n\na. Grignard reagent\n\nb. Cannizaro\'s reaction\n\nc. Aldol condensation\n\nd. HVZ reaction','a,c','Aldol condensation: \n\nAldehydes and ketones having at least one α-hydrogen undergo a reaction in the presence of dilute alkali as catalyst to form β-hydroxy aldehydes (aldol) or β-hydroxy ketones (ketol), respectively. \n\nThis is known as Aldol reaction. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-371 Paragraph -2)\n\na) Cannizaro reaction: \n\nAldehydes which do not have an α-hydrogen atom, undergo self oxidation and reduction (disproportionation) reaction on heating with concentrated alkali. \n\nIn this reaction, one molecule of the aldehyde is reduced to alcohol while another is oxidised to carboxylic acid salt.\n\nb) Hell volhard zelinsky reaction: \n\nCarboxylic acids having an α-hydrogen are halogenated at the α-position on treatment with chlorine or bromine in the presence of small amount of red phosphorus to give α-halocarboxylic acids. \n\nThe reaction is known as Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky reaction.\n\nReference:(NCERT - Class 12 )','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,b,c','NEW','12-371','12-241','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261431q.png'),
(261039,'chemistry','Which of the following pair of compounds can be distinguished by means of Tollens reagent test?','CH₃CHO and CH₃COCH₃','Tollens’ test: \n\nOn warming an aldehyde with freshly prepared ammoniacal silver nitrate solution (Tollens’ reagent), a bright silver mirror is produced due to the formation of silver metal.\n\nThe aldehydes are oxidised to corresponding carboxylate anion.\n\nThe reaction occurs in alkaline medium.\n\nThis test is used to distinguish between an aldehyde and ketone.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-369 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','CH₃CHO and CH₃COCH₃','CH₃COCH₃ and CH₃CH₂COCH₃','CH₃COCH₃ and CH₃COOCH₂CH₃','HCHO and CH₃CHO','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(261101,'chemistry','Which of the following acid is used in rubber, textile, dyeing, leather and electroplating industries?','Methanoic acid','Methanoic acid is used in rubber, textile, dyeing, leather and electroplating industries. \n\nEthanoic acid is used as solvent and as vinegar in food industry. \n\nHexanedioic acid is used in the manufacture of nylon-6, 6. \n\nEsters of benzoic acid are used in perfumery. \n\nSodium benzoate is used as a food preservative. \n\nHigher fatty acids are used for the manufacture of soaps and detergents.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-384 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Hexanedioic acid','Ethanoic acid','Methanoic acid','Sodium benzoate','NEW','12-384','12-254',NULL),
(261102,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement, ketones and aldehydes are used as?','All are correct','In chemical industry aldehydes and ketones are used as solvents, starting materials and reagents for the synthesis of other products.\n \nFormaldehyde is well known as formalin (40%) solution used to preserve biological specimens and to prepare bakelite (a phenol-formaldehyde resin), urea-formaldehyde glues and other polymeric products.\n\nAcetaldehyde is used primarily as a starting material in the manufacture of acetic acid, ethyl acetate, vinyl acetate, polymers and drugs. \n\nBenzaldehyde is used in perfumery and in dye industries. \n\nAcetone and ethyl methyl ketone are common industrial solvents.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-373 Paragraph -last)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Starting material for manufacturing of acetic acid','Benzaldehydes is used in perfumery industry','Acetone is used as common solvent','All are correct','NEW','12-373','12-243',NULL),
(2661218,'chemistry','Which one is most reactive towards nucleophilic addition reaction refer diagram(n26083)','d','Aldehydes are generally more reactive than ketones in nucleophilic addition reactions due to steric and electronic reasons. \n\nSterically, the presence of two relatively large substituents in ketones hinders the approach of nucleophile to carbonyl carbon than in aldehydes having only one such substituent. \n\nElectronically, aldehydes are more reactive than ketones because two alkyl groups reduce the electrophilicity of the carbonyl carbon more effectively than in former.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-366 Paragraph -3)\n(NEET:2014)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-366','12-236','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/2661218q.png'),
(261223,'chemistry','Consider the reaction : RCHO + NH₂NH₂ → RCH=N — NH₂ What sort of reaction is it?','Nucleophilic addition-elimination reaction','Addition of ammonia and its derivatives: \n\nNucleophiles, such as ammonia and its derivatives H₂N-Z add to the carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones. \n\nThe reaction is reversible and catalysed by acid. \n\nThe equilibrium favours the product formation due to rapid dehydration of the intermediate to form >C=N-Z.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -2)\n(MAINS:2012)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Electrophilic addition-elimination reaction','Free radical addition-elimination reaction','Electrophilic substitution-elimination reaction','Nucleophilic addition-elimination reaction','NEW','12-368','12-238',NULL),
(261224,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds will give a yellow precipitate with iodine and alkali?','Acetophenone','Aldehydes and ketones having at least one methyl group linked to the carbonyl carbon atom (methyl ketones) are oxidised by sodium hypohalite to sodium salts of corresponding carboxylic acids having one carbon atom less than that of carbonyl compound. \n\nThe methyl group is converted to haloform. \n\nThis oxidation does not affect a carbon-carbon double bond, if present in the molecule.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-370 Paragraph -1)\n(MAINS:2012)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Acetophenone','Methyl acetate','Acetamide','2-Hydroxypropane','NEW','12-370','12-240',NULL),
(261225,'chemistry','Clemmensen reduction of a ketone is carried out in the presence of which of the following?','Zn-Hg with HCl','Reduction to hydrocarbons: \n\nThe carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones is reduced to CH₂ group on treatment with zincamalgam and concentrated hydrochloric acid [Clemmensenreduction] or with hydrazine followed by heating with sodium or potassium hydroxide in high boiling solvent such as ethylene glycol (Wolff-Kishner reduction).\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -4)\n(NEET:2011)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','LiAlH₄','Zn-Hg with HCl','Glycol with KOH','H₂ and Pt as catalyst','NEW','12-368','12-238',NULL),
(261226,'chemistry','The order of reactivity of phenyl magnesium bromide (PhMgBr) with the following compounds given in diagram(n26087)','I > II > III','Greater the number of alkyl / phenyl groups attached to the carbonyl groups lower will be its reactivity.\n\n+R-effect is stronger than +I-effect.\n\n(MAINS:2011)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','III > II > I','II > I > III','I > III > II','I > II > III','NEW','12-380','12-250','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261226q.png'),
(261227,'chemistry','Which of the following reactions will not result in the formation of carbon-carbon bonds?','Cannizzaro reaction','Cannizzaro reaction: \n\nAldehydes which do not have an α-hydrogen atom, undergo self oxidation and reduction (disproportionation) reaction on heating with concentrated alkali.\n\nIn this reaction, one molecule of the aldehyde is reduced to alcohol while another is oxidised to carboxylic acid salt.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-372 Paragraph -3)\n(NEET:2010)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Reimer–Tiemann reaction','Cannizzaro reaction','Wurtz reaction','Friedel–Crafts acylation','NEW','12-372','12-242',NULL),
(261228,'chemistry','Which one of the following compounds will be most readily dehydrated given in diagram(n26088)','c','The ease of dehydration of the given compounds can be explained on the basis of the stability of the carbocation formed.\n \nIn case of options (a), (b) and (d), a secondary carbocation is formed but the presence of an electron withdrawing C=O group adjacent to the positively charged carbon, intensifies the charge and hence, destabilises the species.\n\nHowever, in case of option (c), a secondary carbocation is formed, but the electron withdrawing C=O group is present farther away, as a result, the effect of this group is diminished and hence, the carbocation is relatively more stable.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -4)\n(MAINS;2012)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-368','12-238','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261228q.png'),
(261229,'chemistry','Which of the following, on being warmed with iodine solution and NaOH, will give iodoform compound(s) given in diagram(n26089)','(i), (ii) and (iii)','Aldehydes and ketones having at least one methyl group linked to the carbonyl carbon atom (methyl ketones) are oxidised by sodium hypohalite to sodium salts of corresponding carboxylic acids having one carbon atom less than that of carbonyl compound. \n\nThe methyl group is converted to haloform. \n\nThis oxidation does not affect a carbon-carbon double bond, if present in the molecule.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-370 Paragraph -1)\n(MAINS:2012)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (iii) and (iv)','Only (ii)','(i), (ii) and (iii)','(i) and (ii)','NEW','12-370','12-240','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261229q.png'),
(261230,'chemistry','Acetophenone when reacted with a base, C₂H₅ONa, yields a stable compound which has the structure given in diagram(n26090)','c','The first step is a simple condensation reaction. \n\nThe last step is an example of E1 mechanism and the leaving group is hydroxide, which is unusual. \n\nStill this step manages to take place owing to the stability incorporated therein the product, which is a conjugated carbonyl compound.\n\n(NEET:2008)\ndiagram(ne26090)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261230q.png'),
(261231,'chemistry','A strong base can abstract an a-hydrogen from?','ketone','The aldehydes and ketones undergo a number of reactions due to the acidic nature of α-hydrogen.\n\nThe base (OH–) ion removes one of the a-hydrogen atom (which is somewhat acidic) from aldehydes and ketones to form a carbanion or the enolate ion. \n\nThe acidity of a-hydrogen is due to resonance stabilization of enolate anion.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-371 Paragraph -1)\n(NEET:2008)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','ketone','amine','alkene','alkyne','NEW','12-371','12-241',NULL),
(261232,'chemistry','Reduction of aldehydes and ketones into hydrocarbons using zinc amalgam and conc. HCl is called?','Clemmensen reduction','Reduction to hydrocarbons: \n\nThe carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones is reduced to CH₂ group on treatment with zincamalgam and concentrated hydrochloric acid [Clemmensenreduction] or with hydrazine followed by heating with sodium or potassium hydroxide in high boiling solvent such as ethylene glycol (Wolff-Kishner reduction).\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -4)\n(NEET:2007)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Cope reduction','Dow reduction','Wolff–Kishner reduction','Clemmensen reduction','NEW','12-368','12-238',NULL),
(261233,'chemistry','Which one of the following on treatment with 50% aqueous sodium hydroxide yields the corresponding alcohol and acid?','benzaldehyde','Cannizzaro reaction: \n\nAldehydes which do not have an α-hydrogen atom, undergo self oxidation and reduction (disproportionation) reaction on heating with concentrated alkali. \n\nIn this reaction, one molecule of the aldehyde is reduced to alcohol while another is oxidised to carboxylic acid salt.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-372 Paragraph -3)\n(NEET:2007)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','benzaldehyde','butanal','Propanone','Tolualdehyde','NEW','12-372','12-242',NULL),
(261234,'chemistry','The product formed in aldol condensation is?','a beta-hydroxy aldehyde or a beta-hydroxy ketone','Aldol condensation: \n\nAldehydes and ketones having at least one α-hydrogen undergo a reaction in the presence of dilute alkali as catalyst to form β-hydroxy aldehydes (aldol) or β-hydroxy ketones (ketol), respectively. \n\nThis is known as Aldol reaction.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-371 Paragraph -2)\n(NEET:2007)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a beta-hydroxy aldehyde or a beta-hydroxy ketone','an alpha-hydroxy aldehyde or ketone','an alpha, beta unsaturated ester','a beta-hydroxy acid.','NEW','12-371','12-241',NULL),
(261235,'chemistry','Nucleophilic addition reaction will be most favoured in?','ethanal','The reactivity of the carbonyl group towards the addition reactions depends upon the magnitude of the positive charge on the carbonyl carbon atom.\n\nThe introduction of group with –I effect increases the reactivity while introduction of alkyl group (+I effect) decreases the reactivity. +I-effect increases, reactivity decreases.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-366 )\n(NEET:2006)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','ethanal','propanal','pent-2-one','propanone','NEW','12-366','12-236',NULL),
(261236,'chemistry','A carbonyl compound reacts with hydrogen cyanide to form cyanohydrin which on hydrolysis forms a racemic mixture of a-hydroxy acid. The carbonyl compound is?','acetaldehyde','Aldehydes and ketones react with hydrogen cyanide (HCN) to yield cyanohydrins. \nThis reaction occurs very slowly with pure HCN. \n\nTherefore, it is catalysed by a base and the generated cyanide ion (CN-) being a stronger nucleophile readily adds to carbonyl compounds to yield corresponding cyanohydrin. \n\nCyanohydrins are useful synthetic intermediates.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-367 Paragraph -2)\n(NEET:2006)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','formaldehyde','acetaldehyde','acetone','diethyl ketone','NEW','12-367','12-237',NULL),
(261237,'chemistry','The major organic product formed from the following reaction given in diagram(n26091)','b','Aldehydes and ketones are reduced to primary and secondary alcohols respectively by sodium borohydride (NaBH₄) or lithium aluminium hydride (LiAlH₄) as well as by catalytic hydrogenation.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -2) (NEET:2005)\ndiagram(ne26091)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-368','12-238','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261237q.png'),
(261238,'chemistry','In this reaction : CH₃CHO + HCN → CH₃CH(OH)CN --ᴴᴼᴴ-----> CH₃CH(OH)COOH an asymmetric centre is generated. The acid obtained would be?','50% D + 50% L-isomer','Lactic acid (CH₃CH(OH)COOH) is an optically active compound due to the presence of asymmetric carbon atom. \n\nIt exists in D- and L-form, the ratio of which is found to be  (1 : 1), because when an asymmetric compound is prepared from a symmetric compound the product is always a racemic mixture(1:1).\n\n(NEET:2003)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','D-isomer','L-isomer','50% D + 50% L-isomer','20% D + 80% L-isomer','NEW','12-367','12-237',NULL),
(261239,'chemistry','When m-chlorobenzaldehyde is treated with 50% KOH solution, the product(s) obtained is (are)refer diagram(n26092)','b','Cannizzaro reaction: \n\nAldehydes which do not have an α-hydrogen atom, undergo self oxidation and reduction (disproportionation) reaction on heating with concentrated alkali. \n\nIn this reaction, one molecule of the aldehyde is reduced to alcohol while another is oxidised to carboxylic acid salt.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-372 Paragraph -3)\n(NEET:2003)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-372','12-242','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261239q.png'),
(261240,'chemistry','A and B in the following reactions are, \ndiagram(n26093)','d','Addition of ammonia and its derivatives: \n\nNucleophiles, such as ammonia and its derivatives H₂N-Z add to the carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones. \n\nThe reaction is reversible and catalysed by acid. \n\nThe equilibrium favours the product formation due to rapid dehydration of the intermediate to form >C=N-Z.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -2)\n(NEET:2003)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-368','12-238','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261240q.png'),
(261241,'chemistry','Relate the structures given in the diagram(n26094)','resonating structures','They are resonating forms because the position of the atomic nuclei remains the same and only electron redistribution has occurred.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-366 Paragraph -2)\n(NEET:2002)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','resonating structures','tautomers','geometrical isomers','optical isomers','NEW','12-366','12-236','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261241q.png'),
(261242,'chemistry','Which of the following is incorrect?','Tollens’ reagent is used in detection of unsaturation.','Tollens’ reagent is a solution of ammoniacal silver nitrate and used for the detection of —CHO group. \n\nAldehydes reduce Tollens’ reagent and itself gets oxidised to convert Ag+ ions to Ag powder which forms the silver coloured mirror in the test tube. \n\nSo, this test is also known as silver mirror test.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-369 Paragraph -4)\n(NEET:2001)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','FeCl₃ is used in detection of phenol.','Fehling solution is used in detection of glucose','Tollens’ reagent is used in detection of unsaturation.','NaHSO₃ is used in detection of carbonyl compound.','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(261243,'chemistry','Polarisation in acrolein can be described using given diagram(n26095)','d','O-atom is more electronegative than C-atom, therefore O-atom bears partial –ve charge and C-atom to which it is attached bear partial +ve charge.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-366 Paragraph -2)\n(NEET:2000)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-368','12-238','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261243q.png'),
(261244,'chemistry','First product of the reaction between RCHO and NH₂NH₂ is?','RCHNNH₂','Addition of ammonia and its derivatives: \n\nNucleophiles, such as ammonia and its derivatives H₂N-Z add to the carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones. \n\nThe reaction is reversible and catalysed by acid.\n\nThe equilibrium favours the product formation due to rapid dehydration of the intermediate to form >C=N-Z.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -2)\n(NEET:2000)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','RCHNNH₂','RCHNH','RCH₂NH₂','RCON₃','NEW','12-367','12-237',NULL),
(261245,'chemistry','An ester (A) with molecular formula, C₉H₁₀O₂ was treated with excess of CH₃MgBr and the complex so formed, was treated with H₂SO₄ to give an olefin (B). \nOzonolysis of (B) gave a ketone with molecular formula C₈H₈O which shows +ve iodoform test. The structure of (A) is?','C₆H₅COOC₂H₅','Reference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-370 Paragraph -1) (NEET:1998)\ndiagram(ne26096)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','H₃CCH₂COC₆H₅','C₂H₅COOC₆H₅','C₆H₅COOC₂H₅','p-H₃CO – C₆H₄ – COCH₃','NEW','12-370','12-240',NULL),
(261246,'chemistry','Iodoform test is not given by?','3-pentanone','Aldehydes and ketones having at least one methyl group linked to the carbonyl carbon atom (methyl ketones) are oxidised by sodium hypohalite to sodium salts of corresponding carboxylic acids having one carbon atom less than that of carbonyl compound. \n\nThe methyl group is converted to haloform. \n\nThis oxidation does not affect a carbon-carbon double bond, if present in the molecule.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-370 Paragraph -1)\n(NEET:1998)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','ethanal','ethanol','2-pentanone','3-pentanone','NEW','12-370','12-240',NULL),
(261247,'chemistry','Phenylmethanol can be prepared by reducing the benzaldehyde with?','Zn and HCl','Reduction to hydrocarbons:\n \nThe carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones is reduced to CH₂ group on treatment with zincamalgam and concentrated hydrochloric acid [Clemmensenreduction] or with hydrazine followed by heating with sodium or potassium hydroxide in high boiling solvent such as ethylene glycol (Wolff-Kishner reduction).\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -4)\n(NEET:1997)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','CH₃I and Mg','CH₃Br and Na','CH₃Br','Zn and HCl','NEW','12-368','12-238',NULL),
(261248,'chemistry','The oxidation of toluene with CrO₃ in the presence of (CH₃CO)₂O gives a product A, which on treatment with aqueous NaOH produces?','C₆H₅COONa','Use of chromic oxide (CrO₃): \n\nToluene or substituted toluene is converted to benzylidene diacetate on treating with chromic oxide in acetic anhydride.\n \nThe benzylidene diacetate can be hydrolysed to corresponding benzaldehyde with aqueous acid. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-363 Paragraph -1)\n(NEET:1995)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','C₆H₅COONa','2, 4-diacetyl toluene','C₆H₅CHO','(C₆H₅CO)₂O','NEW','12-363','12-233',NULL),
(30920,'chemistry','The energy required to remove the second most loosely bound electron is called','Second ionisation enthalpy','We can define the second ionization enthalpy as the energy required to remove the second most loosely bound electron. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 88; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','Second ionisation enthalpy','Third ionisation enthalpy','Fourth ionisation enthalpy','First ionisation enthalpy','NEW','11-088','11-088',NULL),
(261249,'chemistry','When aniline reacts with oil of bitter almonds condensation takes place and benzal derivative is formed. This is known as?','Schiff ’s base','Addition of ammonia and its derivatives: \n\nNucleophiles, such as ammonia and its derivatives H₂N-Z add to the carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones. The reaction is reversible and catalysed by acid. \n\nThe equilibrium favours the product formation due to rapid dehydration of the intermediate to form >C=N-Z.\n\nBenzaldehyde reacts with primary aromatic amines to form Schiff ’s base (Benzylidene aniline).\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -2)\n(NEET:1995)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Schiff ’s base','Benedict’s reagent','Millon’s base','Schiff\'s reagent','NEW','12-368','12-238',NULL),
(261250,'chemistry','Compound A has a molecular formula C₂Cl₃OH. It reduces Fehling’s solution and on oxidation, it gives a monocarboxylic acid B. If A is obtained by the action of chlorine on ethyl alcohol, then compound A is?','chloral','Fehling reagent comprises of two solutions, Fehling solution A and Fehling solution B. \n\nFehling solution A is aqueous copper sulphate and Fehling solution B is alkaline sodium potassium tartrate (Rochelle salt). \n\nThese two solutions are mixed in equal amounts before test. \n\nOn heating an aldehyde with Fehling’s reagent, a reddish brown precipitate is obtained. \n\nAldehydes are oxidised to corresponding carboxylate anion. \n\nAromatic aldehydes do not respond to this test.\n\nThe compound ‘A’ reduces Fehling’s solution thus, it must have free —CHO group. \n\nThus, the compound A is chloral.  \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-369 Paragraph -5) (NEET:1994)\ndiagram(ne26102)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','methyl chloride','monochloroacetic acid','chloral','chloroform.','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(261251,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds will undergo self aldol condensation in the presence of cold dilute alkali?','CH₃CH₂CHO','Aldol condensation: \n\nAldehydes and ketones having at least one α-hydrogen undergo a reaction in the presence of dilute alkali as catalyst to form β-hydroxy aldehydes (aldol) or β-hydroxy ketones (ketol), respectively. \n\nThis is known as Aldol reaction.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-371 Paragraph -2)\n(NEET:1994)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CHC — CHO','CH₂CHCHO','benzaldehyde','CH₃CH₂CHO','NEW','12-371','12-241',NULL),
(261252,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds will give positive test with Tollens’ reagent?','Acetaldehyde','The mild oxidising agents given below are used to distinguish aldehydes from ketones.\n\nTollens’ test: \n\nOn warming an aldehyde with freshly prepared ammoniacal silver nitrate solution (Tollens’ reagent), a bright silver mirror is produced due to the formation of silver metal. \n\nThe aldehydes are oxidised to corresponding carboxylate anion. \n\nThe reaction occurs in alkaline medium.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-369 Paragraph -4)\n(NEET:1994)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Acetic acid','acetamide','acetone','Acetaldehyde','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(261253,'chemistry','(CH₃)₂C=CHCOCH₃ can be oxidised to (CH₃)₂C=CHCOOH by?','NaOI','Aldehydes and ketones having at least one methyl group linked to the carbonyl carbon atom (methyl ketones) are oxidised by sodium hypohalite to sodium salts of corresponding carboxylic acids having one carbon atom less than that of carbonyl compound.\n \nThe methyl group is converted to haloform. \n\nThis oxidation does not affect a carbon-carbon double bond, if present in the molecule.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-370 Paragraph -1)\n(NEET:1993)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','chromic acid','NaOI','potassium permanganate','Cu at 300°C','NEW','12-370','12-240',NULL),
(261254,'chemistry','In which of the following, the number of carbon atoms does not remain same when carboxylic acid is obtained by oxidation?','CH₃COCH₃','Ketones on oxidation give carboxylic acids with lesser number of carbon atoms.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-369 Paragraph -3)\n(NEET:1992)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','CH₃COCH₃','CCl₃CH₂CHO','CH₃CH₂CH₂OH','CH₃CH₂CHO','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(261255,'chemistry','Acetaldehyde reacts with?','nucleophiles only','Acetaldehyde reacts only with nucleophiles.\n\nSince the mobile p-electrons of carbon oxygen double bond are strongly pulled towards oxygen, carbonyl carbon is electron deficient and carbonyl oxygen is electron rich. \n\nThus, the electron deficient carbonyl carbon is most susceptible to attack by electron rich nucleophilic reagent, i.e., by base.\n\nThe nucleophile, then attacks the protonated carbonyl group to form addition product.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-369 Paragraph -4) (NEET:1991)\ndiagram(ne26103)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','electrophiles only','nucleophiles only','free radicals only','both electrophiles and nucleophiles','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(261256,'chemistry','The reagent which can be used to distinguish acetophenone from benzophenone is?','I₂ and NaOH.','Acetophenone reacts with NaOH and I₂ to give yellow ppt. of CHI₃ but benzophenone (C₆H₅COC₆H₅) does not. \n\nHence, it can be used to distinguish between them.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-370 Paragraph -1)\n(NEET:1990)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine','sodium sulphite','Benedict reagent','I₂ and NaOH.','NEW','12-370','12-240',NULL),
(261257,'chemistry','A polymer is obtained when a carbonyl compound is allowed to stand. It is a white solid. The polymer is shown in diagram?(n26097)','trioxane','(NEET:1989)\ndiagram(ne26097)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','trioxane','paraformaldehyde','formose','metaldehyde','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261257q.png'),
(261258,'chemistry','The given compound describes a condensation polymer which can be obtained in two ways : \n\neither treating 3 molecules of acetone (CH₃COCH₃) with conc. sulphuric acid or passing propyne (CH₃CCH) through a red hot tube.\n \nThe polymer is?','mesitylene','Acetone forms mesitylene (1, 3, 5- trimethylbenzene) on distillation with conc.sulphuric acid H₂SO₄.\n(NEET:1989)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','mesityl oxide','phorone','deacetonyl alcohol','mesitylene','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(261259,'chemistry','This polymer (B) is obtained when acetone is saturated with hydrogen chloride gas, B can be,  \ndiagram(n26098)','phorone','(NEET:1989)\ndiagram(ne26101)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','phorone','formose','mesityl oxide','deacetonyl alcohol','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261259q.png'),
(261260,'chemistry','If formaldehyde and KOH are heated, then we get?','methyl alcohol','Cannizzaro reaction:\n\nAldehydes which do not have an α-hydrogen atom, undergo self oxidation and reduction (disproportionation) reaction on heating with concentrated alkali.\n\nIn this reaction, one molecule of the aldehyde is reduced to alcohol while another is oxidised to carboxylic acid salt.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-372 Paragraph -3)\n(NEET:1988)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','acetylene','methyl alcohol','ethyl formate','methane','NEW','12-372','12-242',NULL),
(261261,'chemistry','Formalin is an aqueous solution of?','formaldehyde','In chemical industry aldehydes and ketones are used as solvents, starting materials and reagents for the synthesis of other products. \n\nFormaldehyde is well known as formalin (40%) solution used to preserve biological specimens and to prepare bakelite (a phenol-formaldehyde resin), urea-formaldehyde glues and other polymeric products. \n\nAcetaldehyde is used primarily as a starting material in the manufacture of acetic acid, ethyl acetate, vinyl acetate, polymers and drugs. \n\nBenzaldehyde is used in perfumery and in dye industries. \n\nAcetone and ethyl methyl ketone are common industrial solvents. \n\nMany aldehydes and ketones, e.g., butyraldehyde, vanillin, acetophenone, camphor, etc. are well known for their odours and flavours.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-373 Paragraph -2)\n(NEET:1988)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','formic acid','fluorescein','formaldehyde','furfuraldehyde','NEW','12-373','12-243',NULL),
(261262,'chemistry','The reaction that does not give benzoic acid as the major product is, refer diagram(n26099)','c','PCC (Pyridinium chlorochromate) stops oxidation at the aldehyde stage, thereby preventing the further oxidation of aldehydes to carboxylic acids.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -3)\n(NEET:2019)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-366','12-236','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261262q.png'),
(261263,'chemistry','Which one of the following esters gets hydrolysed most easily under alkaline conditions refer diagram(n26100)','b','Electron withdrawing groups increase the reactivity towards nucleophilic substitution reaction and–NO2 is a strong electron withdrawing group.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-377 Paragraph -3)\n(NEET:2015)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-377','12-247','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261263q.png'),
(261264,'chemistry','The correct decreasing order of their reactivity towards hydrolysis of compounds given in diagram(n26101)','(ii) > (iv) > (i) > (iii)','The ease of hydrolysis depends upon the magnitude of the +ve charge on the carbonyl group.\n\nElectron withdrawing groups increase the magnitude of positive charge and electron donating groups decrease the magnitude of positive charge. \n\nHence, the decreasing order of reactivity towards hydrolysis is:\n\n(ii) > (iv) > (i) > (iii)\n(NEET:2007)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) > (ii) > (iii) > (iv)','(iv) > (ii) > (i) > (iii)','(ii) > (iv) > (i) > (iii)','(ii) > (iv) > (iii) > (i)','NEW','12-366','12-236','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261264q.png'),
(261265,'chemistry','In the reaction what is product P shown in diagram(n26102)','b','Grignard reagents react with carbon dioxide (dry ice) to form salts of carboxylic acids which in turn give corresponding carboxylic acids after acidification with mineral acid. \n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-376 Paragraph -4)\n(NEET:2002)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-376','12-246','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261265q.png'),
(261266,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds gives benzoic acid on hydrolysis?','Benzoyl chloride','Acidic hydrolysis of esters gives directly carboxylic acids while basic hydrolysis gives carboxylates, which on acidification give corresponding carboxylic acids.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-377 Paragraph -3)\n(NEET:1996)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Chlorobenzene','Benzoyl chloride','Chlorophenol','Chlorotoluene','NEW','12-377','12-247',NULL),
(261267,'chemistry','Carboxylic acids have higher boiling points than aldehydes, ketones and even alcohols of comparable molecular mass. It is due to their?','formation of intermolecular H-bonding','Simple aliphatic carboxylic acids having up to four carbon atoms are miscible in water due to the formation of hydrogen bonds with water. \n\nThe solubility decreases with increasing number of carbon atoms. \n\nHigher carboxylic acids are practically insoluble in water due to the increased hydrophobic interaction of hydrocarbon part. \n\nBenzoic acid, the simplest aromatic carboxylic acid is nearly insoluble in cold water. \n\nCarboxylic acids are also soluble in less polar organic solvents like benzene, ether, alcohol, chloroform.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-379 Paragraph -2)\n(NEET:2018)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','formation of intramolecular H-bonding','formation of carboxylate ion','more extensive association of carboxylic acid via van der Waals’ forces of attraction','formation of intermolecular H-bonding','NEW','12-379','12-249',NULL),
(261268,'chemistry','The major product of the following reaction in given diagram(n26103)','c','Carboxylic acids react with ammonia to give ammonium salt which on further heating at high temperature give amides.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-382 Paragraph -3)\n(NEET:2019)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-382','12-252','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261268q.png');
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(261269,'chemistry','The correct order of strengths of the carboxylic acids shown in diagram(n26104)','II > III > I','Electron withdrawing group (EWG) stabilises the carboxylate anion and strengthens the acid. \n\nAcidic strength ∝ – I effect \n\nAs oxygen is more electron withdrawing (II) and (III) show greater – I effect than (I). \n\nThus, (I) is least acidic. \n\nOut of (II) and (III), (II) is more acidic than (III) as distance of O increases from —COOH group and acidic strength decreases.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-380 Paragraph -Last)\n(NEET-II 2016)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','I > II > III','II > III > I','III > II > I','II > I > III','NEW','12-380','12-250','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261269q.png'),
(261270,'chemistry','The correct order of decreasing acid strength of \n\nTrichloroacetic acid (A), \n\nTrifluoroacetic acid (B), \n\nAcetic acid (C) \n\nFormic acid (D)','B > A > D > C','Electron withdrawing group (EWG) stabilises the carboxylate anion and strengthens the acid. \n\nAs –I effect increases, —COOH group becomes more electron deficient and tendency to lose H+ ions increases i.e., acid strength increases. \n\nAs +I effect increases, acid strength decreases.\n\nThus, correct order of acid strength is\n\nCF₃COOH > CCl₃COOH > HCOOH > CH₃COOH\n\n(B) > (A) > (D) > (C)\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-380 Paragraph -Last)\n(NEET:2012)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','B > A > D > C','B > D > C > A','A > B > C > D','A > C > B > D','NEW','12-380','12-250',NULL),
(261271,'chemistry','Which one of the following is most reactive towards electrophilic reagent shown in diagram\n(n26105)','d','Electron withdrawing group (EWG) stabilises the carboxylate anion and strengthens the acid & vice versa.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-380 Paragraph -Last)\n(NEET:2011 & 2012)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-380','12-250','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261271q.png'),
(261272,'chemistry','An organic compound A on treatment with NH₃ gives B, which on heating gives C. C when treated with Br₂ in the presence of KOH produces ethyl amine. Compound A is?','propanoic acid','Carboxylic acids react with ammonia to give ammonium salt which on further heating at high temperature give amides.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-382 Paragraph -3)\n(MAINS:2011)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','ethanoic acid','propanoic acid','butanoic acid','2-methyl,propanoic acid','NEW','12-382','12-252',NULL),
(261273,'chemistry','Propionic acid with Br₂/P yields a dibromo product. Its structure would be, \ndiagram(n26106)','c','Carboxylic acids having an α-hydrogen are halogenated at the α-position on treatment with chlorine or bromine in the presence of small amount of red phosphorus to give α-halo carboxylic acids. \n\nThe reaction is known as Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-383 Paragraph -4)\n(NEET:2009)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-383','12-253','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261273q.png'),
(261274,'chemistry','Which of the following represents the correct order of the acidity in the given compounds?','FCH₂COOH > ClCH₂COOH > BrCH₂COOH > CH₃COOH','Electron withdrawing group (EWG) stabilises the carboxylate anion and strengthens the acid & vice versa. \n\nAcidity decreases as the –I effect of the group decreases, F is the most electronegative atom and hence it has highest –I effect among the halogens.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-380 Paragraph -Last)\n(NEET:2007)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','FCH₂COOH > CH₃COOH > BrCH₂COOH > ClCH₂COOH','BrCH₂COOH > ClCH₂COOH > FCH₂COOH > CH₃COOH','FCH₂COOH > ClCH₂COOH > BrCH₂COOH > CH₃COOH','CH₃COOH > BrCH₂COOH > ClCH₂COOH > FCH₂COOH','NEW','12-380','12-250',NULL),
(261275,'chemistry','In a set of reactions acetic acid yielded a product D will be, \ndiagram(n26107)','d','The hydroxyl group of carboxylic acids, behaves like that of alcohols and is easily replaced by chlorine atom on treating with PCl₅ , PCl₃ or SOCl₂. \n\nThionyl chloride (SOCl₂ ) is preferred because the other two products are gaseous and escape the reaction mixture making the purification of the products easier.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-382 Paragraph -2)\n(NEET:2005)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-382','12-252','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261275q.png'),
(261276,'chemistry','The –OH group of an alcohol or the – COOH group of a carboxylic acid can be replaced by –Cl using?','phosphorus pentachloride','The hydroxyl group of carboxylic acids, behaves like that of alcohols and is easily replaced by chlorine atom on treating with PCl5, PCl3 or SOCl2 . \n\nThionyl chloride (SOCl2 ) is preferred because the other two products are gaseous and escape the reaction mixture making the purification of the products easier.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-382 Paragraph -2)\n(NEET:2004)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','phosphorus pentachloride','hypochlorous acid','chlorine','HCl','NEW','12-382','12-252',NULL),
(261277,'chemistry','Which one of the following orders of acid strength is correct?','RCOOH > HOH > ROH > C₂H₂','Carboxylic acids are weaker than mineral acids, but they are stronger acids than alcohols and many simple phenols (pKa is ~16 for ethanol and 10 for phenol).\n \nIn fact, carboxylic acids are amongst the most acidic organic compounds.\n\nCarboxylic acid is much stronger than water and alcohol. \n\nSince the carboxylate ion after the removal of proton is stabilised by resonating structures. \n\nThe—OH in alcohols is almost neutral. \n\nAcetylene is also weakest acid among the given examples.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-380 Paragraph -4)\n(NEET:2003)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','RCOOH > ROH > HOH >C₂H₂','RCOOH > HOH > ROH > C₂H₂','RCOOH > HOH > HC=CH > ROH','RCOOH > HC=CH > HOH > ROH','NEW','12-380','12-250',NULL),
(261278,'chemistry','In a set of the given reactions, acetic acid yielded a product C, \ndiagram(n26108)','d','The hydroxyl group of carboxylic acids, behaves like that of alcohols and is easily replaced by chlorine atom on treating with PCl₅ , PCl₃ or SOCl₂. \n\nThionyl chloride  is preferred because the other two products are gaseous and escape the reaction mixture making the purification of the products easier.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-382 Paragraph -2)\n\n(NEET:2003)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-382','12-252','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261278q.png'),
(261279,'chemistry','Ethyl benzoate can be prepared from benzoic acid by using?','ethyl alcohol and dry HCl','Carboxylic acids are esterified with alcohols or phenols in the presence of a mineral acid such as concentrated H₂SO₄ or HCl gas as a catalyst.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-381 Paragraph -5)\n\n(NEET:2000)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','ethyl alcohol','ethyl alcohol and dry HCl','ethyl chloride','sodium ethoxide.','NEW','12-381','12-251',NULL),
(261280,'chemistry','Reduction by LiAlH₄ of hydrolysed product of an ester gives?','two alcohols','Reduction of hydrolysed product of ester by LiAlH₄ produces two alcohols.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-377 Paragraph -3)\n\n(NEET:2000)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','two alcohols','two aldehydes','one acid and one alcohol','two acids.','NEW','12-377','12-247',NULL),
(261281,'chemistry','Which one of the following compounds will react with NaHCO₃ solution to give sodium salt and carbon dioxide?','Acetic acid','Carboxylic acids are weaker than mineral acids, but they are stronger acids than alcohols and many simple phenols (pKa is ~16 for ethanol and 10 for phenol).\n \nIn fact, carboxylic acids are amongst the most acidic organic compounds.\n\nNaHCO₃ is weakly basic, so it can only react with the acid CH₃COOH. \n\nWhile phenol is weakly acidic and n-hexanol is neutral, they do not react with NaHCO₃.\n\nCH₃COOH + NaHCO₃ → CH₃COONa + CO₂ + H₂O\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-380 Paragraph -4)\n\n(NEET:1999)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Acetic acid','n-Hexanol','Phenol','Both (b) and (c)','NEW','12-380','12-250',NULL),
(261282,'chemistry','Which one of the following product is formed when adipic acid is heated,\ndiagram(n26109)','a','Carboxylic acids on heating with mineral acids such as H₂SO₄ or with P₂O₅ give corresponding anhydride.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-381 Paragraph -4)\n\n(NEET:1995)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-381','12-251','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261282q.png'),
(261283,'chemistry','An acyl halide is formed when PCl₅ reacts with an?','acid','The hydroxyl group of carboxylic acids, behaves like that of alcohols and is easily replaced by chlorine atom on treating with PCl₅, PCl₃ or SOCl₂. \n\nThionyl chloride (SOCl₂ ) is preferred because the other two products are gaseous and escape the reaction mixture making the purification of the products easier.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-382 Paragraph -2)\n\n(NEET;1994)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','alcohol','amide','acid','ester','NEW','12-382','12-252',NULL),
(261284,'chemistry','Benzoic acid gives benzene on being heated with X and phenol gives benzene on being heated with Y. Therefore, X and Y are respectively?','soda-lime and zinc dust.','Carboxylic acids lose carbon dioxide to form hydrocarbons when their sodium salts are heated with soda lime (NaOH and CaO in the ratio of 3 : 1). \n\nThe reaction is known as decarboxylation.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-383 Paragraph -2)\n\n(NEET:1992)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','soda-lime and copper','Zn dust and NaOH','Zn dust and soda-lime','soda-lime and zinc dust.','NEW','12-383','12-253',NULL),
(261285,'chemistry','A is a lighter phenol and B is an aromatic carboxylic acid. \n\nSeparation of a mixture of A and B can be carried out easily by using a solution of?','sodium bicarbonate','Carboxylic acids are weaker than mineral acids, but they are stronger acids than alcohols and many simple phenols (pKa is ~16 for ethanol and 10 for phenol). \n\nIn fact, carboxylic acids are amongst the most acidic organic compounds.\n\nCarboxylic acids dissolve in NaHCO3 but phenols do not.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-380 Paragraph -4)\n\n(NEET:1992)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','sodium hydroxide','sodium sulphate','calcium chloride','sodium bicarbonate','NEW','12-380','12-250',NULL),
(261286,'chemistry','The compound formed when malonic acid is heated with urea is?','barbituric acid','Carboxylic acids react with ammonia to give ammonium salt which on further heating at high temperature give amides.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-382 Paragraph -3)\n\n(NEET:1989)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','cinnamic acid','butyric acid','barbituric acid','crotonic acid','NEW','12-382','12-252',NULL),
(261287,'chemistry','Among the following the strongest acid is?','CH₂ClCOOH','Electron withdrawing group (EWG) stabilises the carboxylate anion and strengthens the acid & vice versa.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-380 Paragraph -Last)\n\n(NEET:1988)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','CH₃COOH','CH₂ClCOOH','CH₂ClCH₂COOH','CH₃CH₂COOH','NEW','12-380','12-250',NULL),
(261288,'chemistry','Which of the following is the correct decreasing order of acidic strength of (i) methanoic acid (ii) ethanoic acid (iii)propanoic acid (iv) butanoic acid','(i) > (ii) > (iii) > (iv)','Electron withdrawing group (EWG) stabilises the carboxylate anion and strengthens the acid & vice versa. \n\n+I effect of the alkyl group increases from CH₃ to CH₃CH₂ to CH₃CH₂CH₂ to CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂, resulting in decrease of acid character. \n\nTherefore, the order is (i) > (ii) > (iii) > (iv).\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-380 Paragraph -Last)\n\n(NEET:1988)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) > (ii) > (iii) > (iv)','(ii) > (iii) > (iv) > (i)','(i) > (iv) > (iii) > (ii)','(iv) > (i) > (iii) > (ii)','NEW','12-380','12-250',NULL),
(261289,'chemistry','Among the given compounds, the most susceptible to nucleophilic attack at the carbonyl group is?','CH₃COCl','CH₃COCl is most susceptible to nucleophilic attack. \n\nThe susceptibility of a substrate towards nucleophilic attack depends on how good a leaving group is attached to it. \n\nCl– is a weak base and therefore, a good leaving group.\n\n(NEET:2010)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','CH₃COOCH₃','CH₃CONH₂','CH₃COOCOCH₃','CH₃COCl','NEW','12-382','12-252',NULL),
(261290,'chemistry','The relative reactivities of acyl compounds towards nucleophilic substitution are in the order of?','acyl chloride > acid anhydride > ester > amide','The susceptibility of a substrate towards nucleophilic attack depends on how good a leaving group is attached to it.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-380 Paragraph -2)\n\n(NEET:2008)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','acyl chloride > ester > acid anhydride > amide','acid anhydride > amide > ester > acyl chloride','acyl chloride > acid anhydride > ester > amide','ester > acyl chloride > amide > acid anhydride.','NEW','12-380','12-250',NULL),
(261291,'chemistry','Self condensation of two moles of ethyl acetate in presence of sodium ethoxide yields?','acetoacetic ester','Ethyl acetate undergoes Claisen condensation in presence of sodium ethoxide involving a-hydrogen atom in which two molecules of ethyl acetate combine together to form acetoacetic ester.\n\n(NEET:2006)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','ethyl propionate','ethyl butyrate','acetoacetic ester','methyl acetoacetate.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(261292,'chemistry','Which one of the following esters cannot undergo Claisen self-condensation?','C₆H₅COOC₂H₅','The esters having active methylene group (– CH₂ –), show Claisen condensation reaction.\n\nAs C₆H₅ – COOC₂H₅ has no a-hydrogen atom or active methylene group, so it cannot undergo Claisen condensation reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-381 Paragraph -5)\n\n(NEET:1998)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','C₆H₅CH₂COOC₂H₅','C₆H₅COOC₂H₅','C₆H₁₁CH₂COOC₂H₅','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂COOC₂H₅','NEW','12-381','12-251',NULL),
(261293,'chemistry','Sodium formate on heating yields?','sodium oxalate and H₂','Heating carboxylate ions leads to dehydrogenation leads to ionic salt formation.\n\n(NEET:1993)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','oxalic acid and H₂','sodium oxalate and H₂','CO₂ and NaOH','sodium oxalate.','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(261294,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name of the organic compound formed in the following chemical reaction?\ndiagram(n26110)','2-methyl butan-2-ol','(NEET:2021)\nDiagram(ne26104)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2-methylpropan-2-ol','pentan-2-ol','pentan-3-ol','2-methyl butan-2-ol','NEW','12-359','12-229','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261294q.png'),
(261295,'chemistry','Consider the above reaction and identify the missing reagent/chemical. diagram(n26111)','(3) CaO','Carboxylic acids lose carbon dioxide to form hydrocarbons when their sodium salts are heated with soda lime (NaOH and CaO in the ratio of 3 : 1). \n\nThe reaction is known as decarboxylation.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class:12 page:383 paragraph-2)\n(NEET:2021)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(1) B2H6','(2) Red Phosphorus','(3) CaO','(4) DIBAL-H','NEW','12-383','12-253','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261295q.png'),
(10803,'chemistry','In order to determine the mole precisely, the mass of a carbon A was determined by a B and found to be equal to C g. What are A, B and C respectively?','A → 12 atom, \n B → Mass spectrometer, \n C → 1.992648x I0⁻²³','In order to determine the mole precisely, the mass of a carbon–12 atom was determined by a mass spectrometer and found to be equal to 1.992648 ×10⁻²³ g.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Mole Concept And Molar Masses',NULL,'2','mcq','A → 12 atom, \n B → Mass spectrometer, \n C → 1.992648x I0⁻²³','A → 12 atom, \n B → Atomic spectrometer, \n C → 1.992648x I0⁻²³','A → 14 atom, \n B → Mass spectrometer, \n C → 1.992648 x I0⁻²³','A → 12 atom, \n B → Mass spectrometer, \n C → 1.992648 x I0⁻²¹','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(261296,'chemistry','Given below are two statements :\n\nStatement I: The boiling points of aldehydes and ketones are higher than hydrocarbons of comparable\nmolecular masses because of weak molecular association in aldehydes and ketones due to dipole - dipole\ninteractions.\n\nStatement II: The boiling points of aldehydes and ketones are lower than the alcohols of similar molecular\nmasses due to the absence of H-bonding.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the given below:','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct','• The boiling points of aldehydes and ketones are higher than hydrocarbons of comparable molecular masses due to weak molecular association in aldehydes and ketones arising out of the dipole - dipole interaction.\n\n• Alcohols involved intermolecular hydrogen bonding, because of which the boiling point of aldehydes and ketones are lower than the alcohols of similar molecular masses.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-365 Paragraph -1)\n(NEET:2022)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect','Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct','NEW','12-365','12-235',NULL),
(261297,'chemistry','Choose the correct answer from the options given below.\ndiagram(n26112)','(4) (a) – (iv), (b) – (iii), (c) – (ii), (d) – (i)','Nucleophiles, such as ammonia and its derivatives H₂N-Z add to the carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones.\n \nThe reaction is reversible and catalysed by acid.The equilibrium favours the product formation due to rapid dehydration of the intermediate to form>C=N-Z.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-365 Paragraph -1)\n\n(NEET:2022)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(1) (a) – (iii), (b) – (iv), (c) – (ii), (d) – (i)','(2) (a) – (ii), (b) – (iii), (c) – (iv), (d) – (i)','(3) (a) – (i), (b) – (iii), (c) – (ii), (d) – (iv)','(4) (a) – (iv), (b) – (iii), (c) – (ii), (d) – (i)','NEW','12-365','12-235','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261297q.png'),
(261298,'chemistry','Compound X on reaction with O₃ followed by Zn/H₂O gives formaldehyde and 2-methylpropanal as products.\nThe compound X is?','3-Methylbut-1-ene','By ozonolysis of alkenes:\n\nOzonolysis of alkenes followed by reaction with zinc dust and water gives aldehydes,ketones or a mixture of both depending on the substitution pattern of the alkene.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -last)\n(NEET:2022)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','3-Methylbut-1-ene','2-Methylbut-1-ene','2-Methylbut-2-ene','Pent-2-ene','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(261299,'chemistry','Which one of the following is not formed when acetone reacts with 2-pentanone in the presence of dilute NaOH followed by heating?\ndiagram(n26113)','B','Cross Aldol condensation reaction:\n\nBoth reactants contain a-Hydrogens, so multiple products are possible which are as follows:\n\n(NEET:2022)\ndiagram(ne26105)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','12-372','12-242','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261299q.png'),
(261300,'chemistry','The product formed in the following chemical reaction is\ndiagram(n26122)','D','Aldehydes and ketones are reduced to primary and secondary alcohols respectively by sodium borohydride or lithium aluminium hydride  as well as by catalytic hydrogenation.\nNaBH₄ does not reduce esters.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -last)\n(NEET:2021)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','12-368','12-238','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261300q.png'),
(261401,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following:\n\nAssertion (A): The π-bond in carbonyl group C=O is formed by the overlap of p-orbital of C with the p-orbital of O.\n\nReason (R): In carbonyl group C=O the C and O π-bond is formed by the sharing of non-bonding electrons of oxygen to the empty p -orbital of carbon.','A is correct but R is not  correct','The carbonyl carbon atom is sp₂-hybridised and forms three sigma (s) bonds. \n\nThe fourth valence electron of carbon remains in its p-orbital and forms a p-bond with oxygen by overlap with p-orbital of an oxygen.\n\nThe bond is formed by the sharing of one electron from unhybridised p- orbital of carbon with one electron  from oxygen p-orbital giving rise a π-bond between them.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are correct; and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are correct; but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is correct but R is not  correct','A is not correct but R is correct','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(261402,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following:\n\nAssertion (A): Aldehydes can not be prepared from alkynes except acetaldehyde.\n\nReason (R): In alkynes, electron in triple bond are less exposed and thus less reactive towards electrophilic reagents.','Both A and R are correct; but R is not the correct explanation of A','Addition of water to ethyne in the presence of H₂SO₄ and HgSO₄ gives acetaldehyde. \n\nAll other alkynes give ketones in this reaction.\n\nWater is added to the alkynes according to Markovnikov\'s rule:\n\nThe negative part goes to that carbon atom of triple bond which has lesser number of hydrogen atom.\n\nHence OH- group goes to that carbon atom which is always present between the alkyne chain rather then going to the terminal carbon atom of alkyne.\n\nSo it is not possible to prepare aldehydes from alkynes except acetaldehyde.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are correct; and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are correct; but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is correct but R is not  correct','A is not correct but R is correct','NEW','12-362','12-232',NULL),
(261403,'chemistry','Which carbonyl compound is formed from 1,1,2-trimethyl propanol on oxidation?','No product','Aldehydes and ketones are generally prepared by oxidation of primary and secondary alcohols, respectively.\n\nAs tertiary alcohols are resistant to oxidation therefore there will be no product formed from the 1,1,2-trimethyl propanol.\n\nReference:(NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(CH₃)₂C=O','CH₃_C(CH₃)₂_COOH','H₃C_C(CH₃)₂_(CH₃)C=O','No product','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(261404,'chemistry','What is the product of the reaction?\ndiagram(n26127)','B','Dehydrogenation  is suitable method  for volatile alcohols and is of industrial application. \n\nIn this method alcohol vapours are passed over heavy metal catalysts (Ag or Cu). \nPrimary and secondary alcohols give aldehydes and ketones, respectively.\n\nTertiary alcohols are not dehydrogenated but undergo dehydration forming alkenes.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','12-361','12-231','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261404q.png'),
(261405,'chemistry','When a benzene derivative is treated with  carbon monoxide and hydrogen chloride in presence of cuprous chloride , benzaldehyde or substituted benzaldehyde is formed. This reaction is','Gatterman koch reaction','Gatterman – Koch reaction:\n\nWhen benzene or its derivative is treated with carbon monoxide and hydrogen chloride in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride or cuprous chloride, it gives benzaldehyde or substituted benzaldehyde.\n\nThis reaction is known as Gatterman-Koch reaction.\n\nReference:(NCERT - Class 12 Page-363 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Stephen reaction','Etard reaction','Rosenmund reaction','Gatterman koch reaction','NEW','12-363','12-233',NULL),
(261406,'chemistry','Which one is the preparative method for ketones from Grignards reagent as a starting material?','Reaction of Grignards reagent with Cd','Treatment of acyl chlorides with dialkylcadmium, prepared by the reaction of cadmium chloride with Grignard reagent, gives ketones.\n\nReference:(NCERT - Class 12 Page-363 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Reaction of Grignards reagent with NH₃','Reaction of Grignards reagent with Cd','Reaction of Grignards reagent with CO₂','Reaction of Grignards reagent with alcohols','NEW','12-363','12-233',NULL),
(261407,'chemistry','Which set is the reduced products of aldehydes and ketones?','Primary alcohols, secondary alcohols and alkanes','Reduction of Aldehydes and Ketones:\n\nAldehydes and ketones are reduced to primary and secondary alcohols respectively by sodium borohydride or lithium aluminium hydride as well as by catalytic hydrogenation.\nReduction to hydrocarbons: \n\nThe carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones is reduced to CH₂ group on treatment with zinc- amalgam and concentrated hydrochloric acid.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -last)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Primary alcohols, secondary alcohols and alkanes','Secondary alcohols, tertiary alcohols and alkanes','Primary, secondary and tertiary alcohols','Primary alcohols, secondary alcohols and ethers','NEW','12-368','12-238',NULL),
(261408,'chemistry','Product formed when benzaldehyde undergoes aldol condensation with Acetophenone?','1,3-Diphenylprop-2-en-1-one','Aldehydes and ketones having at least one α-hydrogen undergo a reaction in the presence of dilute alkali as catalyst to form β-hydroxy aldehydes (aldol) or β-hydroxy ketones (ketol), respectively. \n\nThis is known as Aldol reaction.\n\nThe aldol and ketol readily lose water to give α,β-unsaturated carbonyl compounds which are aldol condensation products and the reaction is called Aldol condensation.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-371 Paragraph -last)diagram(ne26113)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1,3-Diphenylprop-2-en-1-one','1,2-Diphenylprop-2-en-1-one','2-methyl pentanoic acid','none of the above','NEW','12-371','12-41',NULL),
(261409,'chemistry','Which of them will not undergo aldol condensation?\n\na. Propionaldehyde\n\nb. Formaldehyde\n\nc. Acetone\n\nd. Acetaldehyde','b only','Aldehydes and ketones having at least one α-hydrogen undergo a reaction in the presence of dilute alkali as catalyst to form β-hydroxy aldehydes (aldol) or β-hydroxy ketones (ketol), respectively. \n\nThis is known as Aldol reaction.\n\nThe aldol and ketol readily lose water to give α,β-unsaturated carbonyl compounds which are aldol condensation products and the reaction is called Aldol condensation.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-371 Paragraph -last) diagram(ne26113)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','b only','a,d','NEW','12-371','12-241',NULL),
(261410,'chemistry','In the following reaction reactant A is?\ndiagram(n26135)','A','Anhydrides on the other hand are hydrolysed to corresponding acid(s) with water.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-377 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','12-377','12-247','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261410q.png'),
(261411,'chemistry','Choose the right statement for the following reaction:\ndiagram(n26138)','The oxidation is taking place at anode','Decarboxylation:\n\nWhen an aqueous solution of sodium or potassium salt of carboxylic acid is electrolysed an alkane is formed.\n\nThe reaction involves oxidation of carboxylate ion at anode which decarboxylates to alkyl free radical which dimerize to give alkane.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-383 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The reduction is taking place at cathode','The oxidation is taking place at anode','Ionization of carboxylate ion is taking place','Free radicals dimerize to form alkane','NEW','12-383','12-253','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261411q.png'),
(261412,'chemistry','What would be the final product of the reaction?\ndiagram(n26139)','CH₃CH₂CH₂COOH','Nitriles are hydrolysed to amides and then to acids in the presence of H+ or OH− as catalyst. \n\nMild reaction conditions are used to stop the reaction at the amide stage.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-376 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CH₃CH₂CH₂CN','CH₃CH₂CH₂COOH','CH₃CH₂CH₂OH','CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO','NEW','12-376','12-246','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261412q.png'),
(261413,'chemistry','What is the reagent for the reaction?\ndiagram(n26140)','Anhydrous AlCl₃','From benzene or substituted benzenes:\n\nWhen benzene or substituted benzene is treated with acid chloride in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride, it affords the corresponding ketone. \n\nThis reaction is known as Friedel-Crafts acylation reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-364 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CrO₃','Anhydrous AlCl₃','Ether','CrO₂Cl₂','NEW','12-364','12-234','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261413q.png'),
(261414,'chemistry','What is the product of the reaction?\ndiagram(n26141)','D','This reaction can not occur because there are substituents are present on both sides of the keto group.\n\nDue to steric hindrance it is not possible to carry out this reaction and also there is no alpha hydrogen is present.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261414q.png'),
(10804,'chemistry','In three moles of Ethane (C₂H₆), calculate the number of moles of Hydrogen atoms.','18','Each mole of Ethane has 6 moles of Hydrogen atom, so 3 moles of Ethane has 18 moles of Hydrogen atom.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 26 Problem-1.10)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Mole Concept And Molar Masses',NULL,'2','mcq','6','12','18','9','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(10805,'chemistry','What will be the weight of CO having the same number of O-atoms as present in 22 g of CO₂ ?','28 g','Molar Mass of CO₂ = 44 which has two moles of oxygen atom. 22 g of CO₂ will have one mole of oxygen atoms. \n \n One mole of CO has one mole of oxygen atoms which weighs 12 + 16 = 28 g. \n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Paragraph-9)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Mole Concept And Molar Masses',NULL,'2','mcq','28 g','22 g','44 g','72 g','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(261415,'chemistry','Which of the following option is correct:\n\nAssertion A: In nucleophilic addition reaction, the oxygen of carbonyl group attacks the nucleophile as shown in the diagram.\n\nReason R: The oxygen attacks nucleophile because oxygen being more electronegative than the carbon of carbonyl group, it attack the nucleophile and nucleophile become more electron rich species.\ndiagram(n26142)','Both A and R are not correct','Mechanism of nucleophilic addition reactions\n\nA nucleophile attacks the electrophilic carbon atom of the polar carbonyl group from a direction approximately perpendicular to the plane of sp₂ hybridised orbitals of carbonyl carbon.\n\nThe hybridisation of carbon changes from sp₂ to sp₃ in this process, and a tetrahedral alkoxide intermediate is produced.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-366 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are correct; and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are correct; but R is not the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are not correct','A is correct  but R is not correct','NEW','12-366','12-236',NULL),
(261416,'chemistry','Given below are two statements:\n\nASSERTION (A): The Electron withdrawing groups increase the acidity of carboxylic acid.\n\nREASON (R): The electron withdrawing group has the ability to increase electron density in a molecule through the carbon atom it is bonded to. By increasing electron density, it increases the acidity of carboxylic acids.\n\nIn the light of above statements choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','A is correct  but R is not correct','Effect of substituents on the acidity of carboxylic acids:\n\nSubstituents may affect the stability of the conjugate base and thus, also affect the acidity of the carboxylic acids. \n\nElectron withdrawing groups increase the acidity of carboxylic acids by stabilising the conjugate base through delocalisation of the negative charge by inductive and/or resonance effects. \n\nConversely, electron donating groups decrease the acidity by destabilising the conjugate base.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-380 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are correct; and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are correct; but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is correct  but R is not correct','A is not correct but R is correct','NEW','12-380','12-250',NULL),
(261417,'chemistry','What is the final product Y in the given reaction?\ndiagram(n26143)','acetaldehyde','In first step an alcohol is formed.\n\nIn second step oxidation of alcohol is taking place.\n\nAldehydes and ketones are generally prepared by oxidation of primary\nand secondary alcohols, respectively.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','acetic acid','propyl aldehyde','acetaldehyde','acetone','NEW','12-361','12-231','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261417q.png'),
(261418,'chemistry','A compound (C₅H₁₀O) forms a phenylhydrazone give negative Tollen\'s and iodoform tests and is reduced to pentane. what is the compound?','CH₃CH₂COCH₂CH₃','Phenylhydrazone formation indicates a carbonyl compound.\n\nSince the negative Tollens test rules out the CH₃C=O group and the reduction product pentane establishes the C\'s to be in a continuous chain.\n\nThe compound is CH₃CH₂COCH₂CH₃.\n\nReference:(NCERT - Class-12)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CH₃COCH₂CH₂CH₃','CH₃CH₂COCH₂CH₃','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CHO','none of these','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(261419,'chemistry','A compound (C₅H₈O₂) is reduced to pentane with H₂NOH it forms a dioxime and also give positive iodoform and Tollens tests. \nDeduce its structure?','CH₃COCH₂CH₂CHO','Reduction to pentane indicates 5 C\'s in a continuous chain.\n\nThe dioxime show two carbonyl groups.\n\nThe positive iodoform test points out to CH₃C=O while the positive Tollens test establish a HC=O.\n\nThe compound is CH₃COCH₂CH₂CHO.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class-12)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CHO','CH₃CH₂COCH₂CHO','CH₃COCH₂CH₂CH₃','CH₃COCH₂CH₂CHO','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(261420,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is incorrect?','--NO₂ group increases the acid strength due to resonance effect when at m-position','The presence of electron withdrawing group on the phenyl of aromatic carboxylic acid increases their acidity while electron donating groups decrease their acidity.\n\nAs nitro is an electron withdrawing group so it enhances its acidic strength.\n\nThe nitro group on benzene ring exerts an electron attracting inductive effect as well as electron attracting resonance effect.\n\nSince resonance effect operates at o- and p-positions only.\n\nReference:(NCERT - Class 12 Page-381 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','--NO₂ on benzene ring increases the acidity of benzoic acid','m-nitrobenzoic acid is stronger acid than benzoic acid','m-nitrobenzoic acid is weaker acid than o- and p-nitrobenzoic acid','--NO₂ group increases the acid strength due to resonance effect when at m-position','NEW','12-381','12-251',NULL),
(261421,'chemistry','An organic compound A with molecular formula C₆H₆ on treating with CH₃CH₂CH₂Cl in the presence of anhydrous AlCl₃ gives B.\n\nB on treatment with nitration mixture gives C.\n\nCompound C on reacting with K₂Cr₂O₇/H₂SO₄ gives D.\n\nThen the compound D is?\ndiagram(n26144)','B','1.n-propyl benzene\n\n2.para nitro propyl benzene\n\n3.para nitro 3-phenyl propanoic acid\n\nFrom alkylbenzenes\n\nAromatic carboxylic acids can be prepared by vigorous oxidation of alkyl benzenes with chromic acid or acidic or alkaline potassium permanganate. \n\nThe entire side chain is oxidised to the carboxyl group irrespective of length of the side chain. \n\nPrimary and secondary alkyl groups are oxidised in this manner while tertiary group is not affected.\n\nSuitably substituted alkenes are also oxidised to carboxylic acids with these oxidising reagents.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-381 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','12-381','12-251','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261421q.png'),
(261422,'chemistry','Assertion (A):  Compounds containing ---CHO group are easily oxidised to corresponding carboxylic acids.\n\nReason (R): Carboxylic acids can be reduced to alcohols by the treatment of NaBH₄\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given\nbelow:','(A) is correct  (R) is wrong','Compounds with ----CHO can be easily oxidised to ---COOH due to electron withdrawing nature of C==O group.\n\nThis makes the C---H bond weak which gets oxidised to carboxylic acids.\n\nAldehydes and ketones are reduced to primary and secondary alcohols respectively by sodium borohydride or lithium aluminium hydride  as well as by catalytic hydrogenation.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is incorrect explanation of (A).','(A) is correct  (R) is wrong','(A) is wrong but (R) is  correct.','NEW','12-368','12-238',NULL),
(261423,'chemistry','Assertion (A):  The α-hydrogen atom in carbonyl compounds is less acidic.\n\nReason (R): The anion formed after the loss of α hydrogen atom is resonance stabilised\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given\nbelow:','(A) is wrong but (R) is  correct.','Acidity of α-hydrogens of aldehydes and ketones: \n\nThe aldehydes and ketones undergo a number of reactions due to the acidic nature of α-hydrogen. \n\nThe acidity of α-hydrogen atoms of carbonyl compounds is due to the strong electron withdrawing effect of the carbonyl group and resonance stabilisation of the conjugate base.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-371 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is incorrect explanation of (A).','(A) is correct  (R) is wrong','(A) is wrong but (R) is  correct.','NEW','12-371','12-241',NULL),
(261424,'chemistry','Assertion (A):  Aromatic aldehydes can undergo Cannizaro reaction\n\nReason (R): Aromatic aldehydes are almost as reactive as formaldehyde\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given\nbelow:','(A) is correct  (R) is wrong','Cannizzaro reaction: \n\nAldehydes which do not have an α-hydrogen atom, undergo self oxidation and reduction (disproportionation) reaction on heating with concentrated alkali. \n\nIn this reaction, one molecule of the aldehyde is reduced to alcohol while another is oxidised to carboxylic acid salt.\n\nFormaldehyde is more reactive than aromatic aldehydes because of the electron donating effect of the ring, which makes the carbonyl group less electrophillic\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-372 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is incorrect explanation of (A).','(A) is correct  (R) is wrong','(A) is wrong but (R) is  correct.','NEW','12-372','12-342',NULL),
(261425,'chemistry','Assertion (A): All ketones  react with Tollen\'s reagent to form silver mirror\n\nReason (R): Both aldehyde and ketones contain a carbonyl group\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given\nbelow:','(A) is wrong but (R) is  correct.','The mild oxidising agents given below are used to distinguish aldehydes from ketones. \n\nTollens’ test: \n\nOn warming an aldehyde with freshly prepared ammoniacal silver nitrate solution (Tollens’ reagent), a bright silver mirror is produced due to the formation of silver metal. \n\nThe aldehydes are oxidised to corresponding carboxylate anion. The reaction occurs in alkaline medium.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-369 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is incorrect explanation of (A).','(A) is correct  (R) is wrong','(A) is wrong but (R) is  correct.','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(261426,'chemistry','The product C formed in given reaction is diagram(n26110)','methyl ethanoate','Reference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page 365-370) diagram(ne26098)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','ethanoic acid','methyl methanoate','methyl ethanoate','methanoic acid','NEW','12-365','12-235','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261426q.png'),
(261427,'chemistry','The product formed in the following chemical reaction is\nIn relation with the given diagram(n26111)','2-methyl-2-methoxypropane','diagram(ne26099)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','sodium tert-butoxide','2-methyl-2-methoxypropane','2,2-dimethy propane','2 methyl propan2ol','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261427q.png'),
(261428,'chemistry','Compound \'A\' was prepared by oxidation of compound \'B\' with alkaline KMnO₄. Compound \'A\' on reduction with lithium aluminum hydride gets converted back to compound \'B\'. When compound \'A\' is heated with compound B in the presence of H₂SO₄. produces the fruity smell of compound C to which family the compounds \'B\' belong to?','alcohol','"Carboxylic acids are esterified with alcohols or phenols in the presence of a mineral acid such as concentrated H₂SO₄ or HCl gas as a catalyst.\n\nThe esterification of carboxylic acids with alcohols is a kind of nucleophilic acyl substitution. \n\nProtonation of the carbonyl oxygen activates the carbonyl group towards nucleophilic addition of the alcohol. \n\nProton transfer in the tetrahedral intermediate converts the hydroxyl group into -OH₂ group, which, being a better leaving group, is eliminated as neutral water molecule. \nThe protonated ester so formed finally loses a proton to give the ester.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-381 Paragraph -5)"\ndiagram(ne26100)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','alcohol','carboxylic acid','ester','aldehyde','NEW','12-381','12-251',NULL),
(261432,'chemistry','In Clemensen reduction, carbonyl compounds are treated with','zinc amalgam+HCl','Reduction to hydrocarbons: \n\nThe carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones is reduced to CH₂ group on treatment with zincamalgam and concentrated hydrochloric acid [Clemmensenreduction] or with hydrazine followed by heating with sodium or potassium hydroxide in high boiling solvent such as ethylene glycol (Wolff-Kishner reduction).\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -4)\n\n(NEET:2011)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','zinc amalgam+HCl','sodium amalgam+HCl','zinc amalgam+nitric acid','All of the above','NEW','12-368','12-238',NULL),
(261433,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds will give butonone oxidation with alkaline KMnO₄','Butan-2-ol','diagram(ne26102)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Butan-1-ol','Butan-2-ol','Butanal','none of the above','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(261434,'chemistry','The product formed when benzaldehyde is treated with concentrated aq. KOH solution is,\n\nRefer the structure given in diagram(n26113)','b','Benzaldehyde has no α hydrogen, so it will undergo Cannizaro reaction to produce benzyl alcohol and potassium benzoate.\n\nCannizzaro reaction: \n\nAldehydes which do not have an α-hydrogen atom, undergo self oxidation and reduction (disproportionation) reaction on heating with concentrated alkali. \n\nIn this reaction, one molecule of the aldehyde is reduced to alcohol while another is oxidised to carboxylic acid salt.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-372 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','All of the above','NEW','12-372','12-242','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261434q.png'),
(261435,'chemistry','Match the common names with the IUPAC names  according to the given structure diagram(n26114)','A⟶(4), B⟶(5), C⟶(1) ,D⟶(2), E⟶(3)','Carbonyl compounds add fragrance and flavour to nature, for example, vanillin (from vanilla beans), salicylaldehyde (from meadow sweet) and cinnamaldehyde (from cinnamon) have very pleasant fragrances.\n \nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-358 Paragraph -1) diagram(ne26103)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A⟶(2), B⟶(1), C⟶(4) ,D⟶(4), E⟶(3)','A⟶(4), B⟶(5), C⟶(1) ,D⟶(2), E⟶(3)','A⟶(4), B⟶(3), C⟶(2) ,D⟶(4), E⟶(1)','A⟶(4), B⟶(1), C⟶(2) ,D⟶(3), E⟶(4)','NEW','12-358','12-228','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261435q.png'),
(261436,'chemistry','Match the acids given with their correct IUPAC names according to structure diagrams given below: diagram(n26115)','A⟶(2), B⟶(5), C⟶(4) ,D⟶(1), E⟶(3)','diagram(ne26104)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A⟶(2), B⟶(1), C⟶(4) ,D⟶(4), E⟶(3)','A⟶(4), B⟶(5), C⟶(1) ,D⟶(2), E⟶(3)','A⟶(2), B⟶(5), C⟶(4) ,D⟶(1), E⟶(3)','A⟶(4), B⟶(1), C⟶(2) ,D⟶(3), E⟶(4)','NEW','12-360','12-230','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261436q.png'),
(261437,'chemistry','Arrange the following in decreasing order of their acidic strength in relation to given diagram(n26116)','FCH₂COOH >ClCH₂COOH>PhCH₂COOH > CH₃COOH> CH₃CH₂OH','More the electron withdrawing group, more is the acidic strength.\n\nAlcohols are weaker acids than carboxylic acid.\n\nPhenyl group increases acidity due to resonance.','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','FCH₂COOH >PhCH₂COOH > CH₃CH₂OH> CH₃COOH > ClCH₂COOH','CH₃CH₂OH >PhCH₂COOH >FCH₂COOH > CH₃COOH > ClCH₂COOH','FCH₂COOH >ClCH₂COOH>PhCH₂COOH > CH₃COOH> CH₃CH₂OH','none of the above','NEW','12-371','12-241','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261437q.png'),
(261438,'chemistry','Treatment of Ph---O---CO---Ph with NaOH solution yields:\n\na. phenol\n\nb. sodium phenoxide\n\nc. sodium benzoate\n\nd. benzophenone','b,c','It is a nucleophillic substitution reaction\n\n"A nucleophile attacks the electrophilic carbon atom of the polar carbonyl group from a direction approximately perpendicular to the plane of sp2 hybridised orbitals of carbonyl carbon. \n\nThe hybridisation of carbon changes from sp2 to sp3 in this process, and a tetrahedral alkoxide intermediate is produced.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-366 Paragraph -2)" diagram(ne26105)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,b,c','c only','NEW','12-366','12-236',NULL),
(261439,'chemistry','Which of the following conversions can be carried out by Clemmensen reduction?\n\na. Benzaldehyde into benzyl alcohol\n\nb. Cyclohexanone into cyclohexane\n\nc. Benzoyl chloride into benzaldehyde\n\nd. Benzophenone into diphenyl methan','b,d','Reduction to hydrocarbons: \n\nThe carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones is reduced to CH₂ group on treatment with zincamalgam and concentrated hydrochloric acid [Clemmensenreduction] or with hydrazine followed by heating with sodium or potassium hydroxide in high boiling solvent such as ethylene glycol (Wolff-Kishner reduction).\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -4) diagram(ne26106)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','b,d','NEW','12-368','12-238',NULL),
(261440,'chemistry','Which of the following is the correct representation for the intermediate of nucleophilic addition reaction to the given compound ,Choose the correct answer with respect to the below diagram(n26118)','a,b','The carbonyl carbon atom is sp2 -hybridised. Being planar two orientation of molecule regarding attack of nucleophile is possible.\n\nThe bond angles are approximately 120° as expected of a trigonal coplanar structure.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -1) diagram(ne26107)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','b,d','NEW','12-361','12-231','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261440q.png'),
(261441,'chemistry','The products formed on reaction of propanal with 2-methylpropanal in the presence of NaOH?\n\na. 3-hydroxy-2,2 dimethylpentanal\n\nb. 3-hydroxy-2,4 dimethylpentanal\n\nc. 3-hydroxy-2-dimethylpentanal\n\nd. 3-hydroxy-2,2,4-trimethylpentanal\n\ne. 3-hydroxy-1-dimethylpentanal','a,b,c,d','Cross aldol condensation: \n\nWhen aldol condensation is carried out between two different aldehydes and / or ketones, it is called cross aldol condensation. \n\nIf both of them contain α-hydrogen atoms, it gives a mixture of four products. \n\nReferenc: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-372 Paragraph -1) diagram(ne26108)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','a,b,c','a,b,c,d','All of the above','NEW','12-372','12-242',NULL),
(261442,'chemistry','Match the reactions with suitable reagents according to the given diagrams(n26118)','A⟶(3), B⟶(4), C⟶(1) ,D⟶(2)','The carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones is reduced to CH₂ group on treatment with zinc amalgam and concentrated hydrochloric acid [Clemmensen reduction] or with hydrazine followed by heating with sodium or potassium hydroxide in high boiling solvent such as ethylene glycol (Wolff-Kishner reduction).\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -4) diagram(ne26109)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A⟶(2), B⟶(1), C⟶(4) ,D⟶(3)','A⟶(3), B⟶(1), C⟶(2) ,D⟶(4)','A⟶(3), B⟶(4), C⟶(1) ,D⟶(2)','A⟶(3), B⟶(2), C⟶(1) ,D⟶(4)','NEW','12-368','12-238','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261442q.png'),
(261443,'chemistry','Which of the following products will be formed on addition to but-1-yne under these conditions? Choose the correct answer in relation to the given diagram(n26119)','b','By hydration of alkynes: \n\nAddition of water to ethyne in the presence of  H₂SO₄ and HgSO₄ gives acetaldehyde. \nAll other alkynes give ketones in this reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -2) diagram(ne26110)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','a,b,c','All of the above','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261443q.png'),
(261444,'chemistry','The reagent which does not react with both, acetone and benzaldehyde?','Fehling\'s solution','Fehling reagent comprises of two solutions, Fehling solution A and Fehling solution B. \n\nFehling solution A is aqueous copper sulphate and Fehling solution B is alkaline sodium potassium tartrate (Rochelle salt). \n\nThese two solutions are mixed in equal amounts before test. \n\nOn heating an aldehyde with Fehling’s reagent, a reddish brown precipitate is obtained. \nAldehydes are oxidised to corresponding carboxylate anion. \n\nKetones are not oxidised.\n\nAromatic aldehydes do not respond to this test.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-369 Paragraph -5)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Sodium hydrogen sulphite','Phenolic acid','Fehling\'s solution','Grignard reagent','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(261445,'chemistry','Structure of \'A\' and type of isomerism in the reaction is:\ndiagram(n26120)','Prop-1-en-2-ol, tautomerism','By hydration of alkynes: \n\nAddition of water to ethyne in the presence of  H₂SO₄ and HgSO₄ gives acetaldehyde. \nAll other alkynes give ketones in this reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -2) diagram(ne26111)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Prop-1-en-2-ol, metamerism','Prop-2-en-1-ol, tautomerism','Prop-2-en-2-ol, geometrical isomerism','Prop-1-en-2-ol, tautomerism','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261445q.png'),
(10806,'chemistry','Arrange the following in the order of increasing mass (Atomic mass of O = 16, Cu = 63 and N = 14)\n \n I. One atom of Oxygen \n \n II. One atom of Nitrogen \n \n III. 1 x I0⁻¹⁰ mole of Oxygen \n \n IV. 1 x I0⁻¹⁰ mole of Copper','II < I < III < IV','The correct sequence is II < I < III < IV\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Paragraph-9) diagram(ne0106)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Mole Concept And Molar Masses',NULL,'3','mcq','II < I < III < IV','I < II < III < IV','III < II < IV < I','IV < II < III < I','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(261446,'chemistry','Which is the most suitable reagent for the following conversion in the diagram given below diagram(n26121)','Iodine and NaOH solution','Aldehydes and ketones having at least one methyl group linked to the carbonyl carbon atom (methyl ketones) are oxidised by sodium hypohalite to sodium salts of corresponding carboxylic acids having one carbon atom less than that of carbonyl compound. \n\nThe methyl group is converted to haloform. \n\nThis oxidation does not affect a carbon-carbon double bond, if present in the molecule.\nIodoform reaction with sodium hypoiodite is also used for detection of CH₃CO group or CH₃CH(OH) group which produces CH₃CO group on oxidation.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-370 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Tollen\'s reagent','Benzoyl peroxide','Iodine and NaOH solution','Sn and NaOH solution','NEW','12-370','12-240','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261446q.png'),
(261447,'chemistry','Benzal chloride is produced by','Side chain chlorination of toluene','By side chain chlorination followed by hydrolysis Side chain chlorination of toluene gives benzal chloride, which on hydrolysis gives benzaldehyde. \n\nThis is a commercial method of manufacture of benzaldehyde. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-363 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Side chain chlorination of benzene','Etard reaction','Gatterman Koch reaction','Side chain chlorination of toluene','NEW','12-363','12-233',NULL),
(261448,'chemistry','Electrophile produced in Friedal craft acylation of benzene with benzoyl chloride is','Benzoylinium cation','From benzene or substituted benzenes:\n\nWhen benzene or substituted benzene is treated with acid chloride in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride, it affords the corresponding ketone. \n\nThis reaction is known as Friedel-Crafts acylation reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-364 Paragraph -2) diagram(ne26112)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Al cation','Phenyl cation','Benzoylinium cation','Phenyl anion','NEW','12-364','12-234',NULL),
(261449,'chemistry','The correct order of increasing acidic strength is :','ethanol < phenol < acetic acid < chloroacetic acid','Carboxylic acids are weaker than mineral acids, but they are stronger acids than alcohols and many simple phenols.\n\nThe higher acidity of carboxylic acids as compared to phenols can be understood by this way.\n\nThe conjugate base of carboxylic acid, a carboxylate ion, is stabilised by two equivalent resonance structures in which the negative charge is at the more electronegative oxygen atom. \n\nThe conjugate base of phenol, a phenoxide ion, has non-equivalent resonance structures in which the negative charge is at the less electronegative carbon atom. \n\nTherefore, resonance in phenoxide ion is not as important as it is in carboxylate ion. \n\nFurther, the negative charge is delocalised over two electronegative oxygen atoms in carboxylate ion whereas it is less effectively delocalised over one oxygen atom and less electronegative carbon atoms in phenoxide ion.\n\nThus, the carboxylate ion is more stabilised than phenoxide ion, so carboxylic acids are more acidic than phenols.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-379 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','phenol < ethanol < chloroacetic acid < acetic acid','ethanol < phenol < chloroacetic acid < acetic acid','ethanol < phenol < acetic acid < chloroacetic acid','chloroacetic acid < acetic acid < phenol < ethanol','NEW','12-379','12-249',NULL),
(261450,'chemistry','Compound Ph--COO---Ph can be prepared by the reaction of:','phenol and benzoyl chloride in the presence of pyridine','The given reaction is Schotten Baumann reaction and given in the diagram(ne26114)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','phenol and benzoic acid in the presence of NaOH','phenol and benzoyl chloride in the presence of pyridine','phenol and benzoyl chloride in the presence of zinc chloride','phenol and benzaldehyde in the presence of palladium','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(261451,'chemistry','Which of them in the given diagram(n26144) does not gives Cannizaro\'s reaction:','d only','Since ethanal contains α-hydrogen atom so it will not ungergo Cannizzaro reaction.\n\nCannizzaro reaction: \n\nAldehydes which do not have an α-hydrogen atom, undergo self oxidation and reduction (disproportionation) reaction on heating with concentrated alkali. \n\nIn this reaction, one molecule of the aldehyde is reduced to alcohol while another is oxidised to carboxylic acid salt.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-372 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a only','a,b','b,c','d only','NEW','12-372','12-242','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261451q.png'),
(261452,'chemistry','Compounds A and C in the following reaction in the given diagram(n26145) is :','positional isomers','The given reaction will proceed as per given in the diagram(ne26115). Thus compound A and C are postional isomers','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','identical','positional isomers','functional isomers','optical isomers','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261452q.png'),
(261453,'chemistry','In Clemmensen reduction, carbonyl compound is treated with:','zinc amalgam +HCl','Reduction to hydrocarbons: \n\nThe carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones is reduced to CH₂ group on treatment with zincamalgam and concentrated hydrochloric acid [Clemmensenreduction] or with hydrazine followed by heating with sodium or potassium hydroxide in high boiling solvent such as ethylene glycol (Wolff-Kishner reduction).\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','zinc amalgam +HCl','sodium amalgam+ HCl','zinc amalgam + nitric acid','sodium amalgam+ nitric acid','NEW','12-368','12-238',NULL),
(261454,'chemistry','Which of them in the given diagram(n26146) does not undergo aldol condensation?','b,d','Since option (b) and (d) do not contain α-hydrogen atom, so they do not undergoes aldol condensation.\n\n"Aldol condensation:\n\nAldehydes and ketones having at least one α-hydrogen undergo a reaction in the presence of dilute alkali as catalyst to form β-hydroxy aldehydes (aldol) or β-hydroxy ketones (ketol), respectively. \n\nThis is known as Aldol reaction.\n\nThe aldol and ketol readily lose water to give α,β-unsaturated carbonyl compounds which are aldol condensation products.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-371 Paragraph -2)\n(NEET:2017)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,d','b,d','NEW','12-371','12-241','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261454q.png'),
(261455,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is not true?','Butanal has weak ionic dipole interactions','The boiling points of aldehydes and ketones are higher than hydrocarbons and ethers of comparable molecular masses. It is due to weak molecular association in aldehydes and ketones arising out of the dipole-dipole interactions. Also, their boiling points are lower than those of alcohols of similar molecular masses due to absence of intermolecular hydrogen bonding. Reference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-365 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','A large difference in the boiling points is present between butanal and butan-1-ol','Butanal has weak ionic dipole interactions','Butanol shows intermolecular H bonding','All are correct','NEW','12-365','12-235',NULL),
(261456,'chemistry','Select the correct IUPAC for the compounds in the given diagram(n26147)','(c) is 3-oxo-pentan-1-al','The correct IUPAC name of the compound is:\n\na. 3-phenylprop-2-en-1-al\n\nb. Cyclohexanecarbaldehyde\n\nc. 3-oxo-pentan-1-al\n\nd. But-2-en-1-al','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(a) is  3-phenylprop-1-en-2-al','(b) is cyclohexanal','(c) is 3-oxo-pentan-1-al','(d) is But-2-en-1-aldehyde','NEW','12-360','12-330','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261456q.png'),
(261457,'chemistry','Select the correct IUPAC for the compounds in the given diagram(n26148)','(a) is  4-nitropropiophenone','The correct IUPAC name of the compound is:\n\na. 4-nitropropiophenone\n\nb. 2-hydroxycyclopentanecarbaldehyde\n\nc. Phenyl acetaldehyde','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(a) is  4-nitropropiophenone','(b) is Hydroxycyclopentanecarbaldehyde','(c) is Phenylaldehyde','None of them are correct','NEW','12-360','12-230','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261457q.png'),
(261458,'chemistry','Name the products formed on oxidation of 2, 5-dimethylhexan-3-one:\n\na. 3-methylpropanoic acid\n\nb. 2-methylpropanoic acid\n\nc. Methylpropa-1-noic acid','a,b','The reaction will proceed as given in the diagram(ne26116)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,b,c','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(261459,'chemistry','Which of them is the correct order of acidic strength:','FCH₂COOH > ClCH₂COOH > C₆H₅CH₂COOH> CH₃COOH > CH₃CH₂OH','Carboxylic acids are weaker than mineral acids, but they are stronger acids than alcohols and many simple phenols.\n\nThe acidic strength increases with the addition of electron withdrawing group like flourine.','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','FCH₂COOH > ClCH₂COOH > C₆H₅CH₂COOH> CH₃COOH > CH₃CH₂OH','FCH₂COOH > ClCH₂COOH > CH₃COOH >C₆H₅CH₂COOH > CH₃CH₂OH','FCH₂COOH < ClCH₂COOH <C₆H₅CH₂COOH<CH₃COOH < CH₃CH₂OH','None of the above','NEW','12-371','12-241',NULL),
(261501,'chemistry','Which one of the following in the given diagram (261501Q) will be formed on vigorous oxidation by permangate solution (CH₃)₂C=CHCH₂CHO','d','C=C will be oxidised to C=O and CHO will also be oxidised to COOH','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261501q.png'),
(261502,'chemistry','End product of the following reaction diagram(n26150) is','CH₂=CHCOOH','The reaction will proceed as given in the diagram(ne26117)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CH₃CH(OH)COOH','CH₂(OH)CH₂COOH','CH₂=CHCOOH','ClCH₂CH(OH)COOH','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261502q.png'),
