(261504,'chemistry','In the given diagram(n26152) Maximum dehydration takes place in that of:','b','In option b , conjugation of carbocation is formed, so it becomes maximum stable\n\nMaximum dehydration takes place during the formation of more stable carbocation.\n\nThe reaction is in the given diagram(ne26118)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261504q.png'),
(261505,'chemistry','When CH₂=CHCOOH is reduced with LiAlH₄, the compound obtained will be','CH₂=CH₂COOH','LiAlH₄ can reduced COOH bond to CH₂OH but does not reduce C= Cbond, so CH₂=CH₂COOH will be formed','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CH₃CH₂COOH','CH₂=CH₂COOH','CH₃CH₂CH₂OH','CH₃CH₂CHO','NEW','12-368','12-238',NULL),
(261506,'chemistry','The general formula CₙH₂ₙO₂ could be for open chain','carboxylic acids','CₙH₂ₙO₂ is the general formula for carboxylic acids and esters.','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','diketones','carboxylic acids','diols','dialdehydes','NEW','12-358','12-228',NULL),
(261507,'chemistry','Which of the following order in the diagram(n26153)is correct','iii>ii>iv>i','Electron withdrawing group (EWG) stabilises the carboxylate anion and strengthens the acid.\n\nHowever o-nitrobenzoate is stabilised by intramolecule H bonding , so its acidic streght is more \n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-380 Paragraph -Last)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i>ii>iii>iv','ii>iv>iii>i','ii>iv>i>iii','iii>ii>iv>i','NEW','12-380','12-250','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261507q.png'),
(261508,'chemistry','Which one of them undergoes reaction with 50% sodium hydroxide solution to give the corresponding alcohol and acid?','Benzaldehyde','It will follow Cannizaro reaction.\n\n"Cannizzaro reaction: \n\nAldehydes which do not have an α-hydrogen atom, undergo self oxidation and reduction (disproportionation) reaction on heating with concentrated alkali. \n\nIn this reaction, one molecule of the aldehyde is reduced to alcohol while another is oxidised to carboxylic acid salt.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-372 Paragraph -3)"','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Phenol','Benzaldehyde','Butanal','Benzoic acid','NEW','12-372','12-242',NULL),
(261509,'chemistry','Among the following acids which has the lowest pKa value','HCOOH','HCOOH is the strongest acid','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(CH₃)₂CHCOOH','HCOOH','CH₃COOH','CH₃CH₂CHO','NEW','12-380','12-05',NULL),
(261510,'chemistry','The increasing order of rate of HCN addition to compound A to D is: \n\nA. HCHO\n\nB. CH₃COCH₃\n\nC. PhCOCH₃\n\nD. PhCOPh','D<C<B<A','The reactivity towards nucleophillic addition reactions is given by: \n\nSmaller Aldehydes>Larger Aldehydes> Ketones\n\nSo, Formaldehyde has greater reactivity than acetone.\n\nPhCOCH₃ >PhCOPh because PhCOPh has greater steric hindrance.','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','D<C<B<A','C<D<B<A','A<B<C<D','D<B<C<A','NEW','12-366','12-236',NULL),
(261511,'chemistry','The correcct order of increasing acid strength of the compounds :\n\nA. CH₃COOH\nB. CH₃OCHCOOH\nC. CF₃COOH\nD. (CH₃)₂CHCOOH','D<A<B<C','Electron withdrawing group (EWG) stabilises the carboxylate anion and strengthens the acid.\n\nElectron donating group decreases the acidic strength.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-380 Paragraph -Last)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','D<A<B<C','C<D<B<A','A<B<C<D','D<B<C<A','NEW','12-380','12-250',NULL),
(261512,'chemistry','A liquid was mixed with ethanol and a drop of conc. sulphuric acid was added. A compound with a fruity smell was formed. The liquid was:','CH₃COOH','Carboxylic acids are esterified with alcohols or phenols in the presence of a mineral acid such as concentrated H₂SO₄ or HCl gas as a catalyst.\n\nThe esters so formed have a fruity smell.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-381 Paragraph -5)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','HCHO','CH₃COCH₃','CH₃COOH','CH₃OH','NEW','12-381','12-251',NULL),
(261513,'chemistry','Which of the following on heating with aqueous KOH, produces acetaldehyde?','CH₃CHCl₂','Acetaldehyde will be formed as product as given in the diagram(ne26119)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CH₃CH₂Cl','CH₂ClCH₂Cl','CH₃CHCl₂','CH₃COCl','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(261514,'chemistry','Iodoform can be prepared from all except:','Isobutyl alcohol','Iodoform test is positive for ketones, acetaldehydes and methyl secondary  alcohols\n\nAldehydes and ketones having at least one methyl group linked to the carbonyl carbon atom (methyl ketones) are oxidised by sodium hypohalite to sodium salts of corresponding carboxylic acids having one carbon atom less than that of carbonyl compound. \n\nThe methyl group is converted to haloform. \n\nThis oxidation does not affect a carbon-carbon double bond, if present in the molecule.\n\nIodoform reaction with sodium hypoiodite is also used for detection of CH₃CO group or CH₃CH(OH) group which produces CH₃CO group on oxidation.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-370 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Ethyl methyl ketone','Isopropyl alcohol','3-Methyl 2-butanone','Isobutyl alcohol','NEW','12-370','12-240',NULL),
(261515,'chemistry','An organic compound A upon reacting with NH₃ gives B. On heating B gives C. C in the presence of KOH reacts with Br₂ to give CH₃CH₂NH₂. A is:','CH₃CH₂COOH','The reaction will proceed as given in the diagram(ne26120)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CH₃COOH','CH₃CH₂CH₂COOH','CH₃CH(CH₃)COOH','CH₃CH₂COOH','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(261516,'chemistry','In the reaction diagram, the product C is: (n26154)','Ethylene','The reaction will proceed as given in the diagram(ne26121)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Acetaldehyde','Acetylene','Ethylene','Acetylchloride','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261516q.png'),
(261517,'chemistry','The correct decreasing order for acidic strength is:','NO₂CH₂COOH>CNCH₂COOH>FCH₂COOH>ClCH₂COOH','Electron withdrawing group (EWG) stabilises the carboxylate anion and strengthens the acid.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-380 Paragraph -Last)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','NO₂CH₂COOH>FCH₂COOH>CNCH₂COOH>ClCH₂COOH','FCH₂COOH>CNCH₂COOH>NO₂CH₂COOH>ClCH₂COOH','NO₂CH₂COOH>FCH₂COOH>CNCH₂COOH>ClCH₂COOH','NO₂CH₂COOH>CNCH₂COOH>FCH₂COOH>ClCH₂COOH','NEW','12-380','12-250',NULL),
(261518,'chemistry','The major product of the following reaction diagram(n26155) is:','RCHO','Nitriles are selectively reduced by diisobutylaluminium hydride, (DIBAL-H) to imines followed by hydrolysis to aldehydes.\n \nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','RCOOH','RCONH₂','RCHO','RCH₂NH₂','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261518q.png'),
(261601,'chemistry','The given reaction diagram will undergo: (n26156)','Rosenmund reaction','Acyl chloride (acid chloride) is hydrogenated over catalyst, palladium on barium sulphate. \n\nThis reaction is called Rosenmund reduction.\n\nAldehydes can be prepared by hydrogenation of acid chlorides.\n\nReference:(NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Named Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Stephen reaction','Etard reaction','Rosenmund reaction','Gatterman-Koch reaction','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261601q.png'),
(261602,'chemistry','The given reaction diagram  will undergo: (n26157)','Stephen reaction','Nitriles are reduced to corresponding imine with stannous chloride in the presence of hydrochloric acid, which on hydrolysis give corresponding aldehyde.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Named Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Stephen reaction','Etard reaction','Rosenmund reaction','Gatterman-Koch reaction','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261602q.png'),
(261603,'chemistry','The product formed in the reaction diagram will be: (n26158)','Benzaldehyde','Use of chromyl chloride : \n\nChromyl chloride oxidises methyl group to a chromium complex, which on hydrolysis gives corresponding benzaldehyde.\n\nThis reaction is called Etard reaction. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -6)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Named Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Benzene','Benzaldehyde','Benzoic acid','Chromium complex','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261603q.png'),
(261604,'chemistry','The given reaction diagram will undergo: (n26159)','Etard reaction','Use of chromyl chloride : \n\nChromyl chloride oxidises methyl group to a chromium complex, which on hydrolysis gives corresponding benzaldehyde.\n\nThis reaction is called Etard reaction. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -6)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Named Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Stephen reaction','Etard reaction','Rosenmund reaction','Gatterman-Koch reaction','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261604q.png'),
(261605,'chemistry','The given reaction diagram will undergo: (n26160)','Gatterman-Koch reaction','Gatterman – Koch reaction: \n\nWhen benzene or its derivative is treated with carbon monoxide and hydrogen chloride in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride or cuprous chloride, it gives benzaldehyde or substituted benzaldehyde.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-363 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Named Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Stephen reaction','Etard reaction','Rosenmund reaction','Gatterman-Koch reaction','NEW','12-363','12-233','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261605q.png'),
(261606,'chemistry','The reactant used in Gatterman Koch reaction is','Benzene','Gatterman – Koch reaction: \n\nWhen benzene or its derivative is treated with carbon monoxide and hydrogen chloride in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride or cuprous chloride, it gives benzaldehyde or substituted benzaldehyde.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-363 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Named Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Toluene','Benzene','RCN','Bezoyl chloride','NEW','12-363','12-233',NULL),
(261607,'chemistry','The given reaction diagram will undergo: (n26161)','Friedel craft acylation','When benzene or substituted benzene is treated with acid chloride in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride, it affords the corresponding ketone. \n\nThis reaction is known as Friedel-Crafts acylation reaction.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-364 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Named Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Stephen reaction','Etard reaction','Friedel craft acylation','Tollens reaction','NEW','12-364','12-234','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261607q.png'),
(261608,'chemistry','The given reaction diagram will undergo: (n26162)','Wolff Kishner reduction','Reduction to hydrocarbons: \n\nThe carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones is reduced to CH₂ group on treatment with zincamalgam and concentrated hydrochloric acid [Clemmensenreduction] or with hydrazine followed by heating with sodium or potassium hydroxide in high boiling solvent such as ethylene glycol (Wolff-Kishner reduction).\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Named Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Clemmensen reduction','Wolff Kishner reduction','Clemmensen oxidation','Wolff Kishner oxidation','NEW','12-368','12-238','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261608q.png'),
(261609,'chemistry','The given reaction diagram is: (n26163)','Fehling\'s reaction','Fehling reagent comprises of two solutions, Fehling solution A and Fehling solution B. \n\nFehling solution A is aqueous copper sulphate and Fehling solution B is alkaline sodium potassium tartrate (Rochelle salt). \n\nThese two solutions are mixed in equal amounts before test. \n\nOn heating an aldehyde with Fehling’s reagent, a reddish brown precipitate is obtained.\n \nAldehydes are oxidised to corresponding carboxylate anion. \n\nAromatic aldehydes do not respond to this test.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-369 Paragraph -5)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Named Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Tollens reaction','Haloform reaction','Wolff-Kishner reduction','Fehling\'s reaction','NEW','12-369','12-239','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261609q.png'),
(261610,'chemistry','The given reaction diagram is: (n26164)','Haloform reaction','Aldehydes and ketones having at least one methyl group linked to the carbonyl carbon atom (methyl ketones) are oxidised by sodium hypohalite to sodium salts of corresponding carboxylic acids having one carbon atom less than that of carbonyl compound. \n\nThe methyl group is converted to haloform. \n\nThis oxidation does not affect a carbon-carbon double bond, if present in the molecule.\n\nIodoform reaction with sodium hypoiodite is also used for detection of CH₃CO group or CH₃CH(OH) group which produces CH₃CO group on oxidation.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-370 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Named Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Tollens reaction','Haloform reaction','Aldol reaction','Fehling\'s reaction','NEW','12-370','12-240','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261610q.png'),
(261611,'chemistry','The given reaction diagram is:(n26165)','Aldol reaction','Aldol condensation: \n\nAldehydes and ketones having at least one α-hydrogen undergo a reaction in the presence of dilute alkali as catalyst to form β-hydroxy aldehydes (aldol) or β-hydroxy ketones (ketol), respectively. \n\nThis is known as Aldol reaction.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-371 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Named Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Tollens reaction','Haloform reaction','Aldol reaction','Fehling\'s reaction','NEW','12-371','12-241','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261611q.png'),
(261612,'chemistry','The given reaction diagram  is: (n26166)','Cross Aldol reaction','Cross aldol condensation: \n\nWhen aldol condensation is carried out between two different aldehydes and / or ketones, it is called cross aldol condensation. \n\nIf both of them contain α-hydrogen atoms, it gives a mixture of four products. \n\nThis is illustrated below by aldol reaction of a mixture of ethanal and propanal.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-372 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Named Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Tollens reaction','Cross Aldol reaction','Aldol reaction','Cannizaro reaction','NEW','12-372','12-242','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261612q.png'),
(261613,'chemistry','The given reaction diagram undergoes: (n26167)','Cannizaro reaction','Cannizzaro reaction:\n\nAldehydes which do not have an α-hydrogen atom, undergo self oxidation and reduction (disproportionation) reaction on heating with concentrated alkali. \n\nIn this reaction, one molecule of the aldehyde is reduced to alcohol while another is oxidised to carboxylic acid salt.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-372 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Named Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Tollens reaction','Cross Aldol reaction','Aldol reaction','Cannizaro reaction','NEW','12-372','12-242','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261613q.png'),
(261614,'chemistry','The given reaction diagram forms: (n26168)','R-H','Alkali metal salts of carboxylic acids also undergo decarboxylation on electrolysis of their aqueous solutions and form hydrocarbons having twice the number of carbon atoms present in the alkyl group of the acid. \n\nThe reaction is known as Kolbe electrolysis.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-383 Paragraph -3). Refer diagram(ne26122)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Named Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','R-OH','R-COOH','R-H','R-CHO','NEW','12-383','12-253','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261614q.png'),
(261615,'chemistry','The given reaction diagram undergoes: (n26169)','Decarboxylation','Alkali metal salts of carboxylic acids also undergo decarboxylation on electrolysis of their aqueous solutions and form hydrocarbons having twice the number of carbon atoms present in the alkyl group of the acid. \n\nThe reaction is known as Kolbe electrolysis.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-383 Paragraph -3). Refer diagram(ne26122)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Named Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Reduction','Decarboxylation','Halogenation','Ring substitution','NEW','12-383','12-253','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261615q.png'),
(261616,'chemistry','The given reaction diagram undergoes: (n26170)','HVZ reaction','Hell volhard zelinsky reaction: Carboxylic acids having an α-hydrogen are halogenated at the α-position on treatment with chlorine or bromine in the presence of small amount of red phosphorus to give α-halocarboxylic acids. The reaction is known as Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky reaction. Reference:(NCERT - Class 12 ,Page no. 383)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Named Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Kolbe reaction','Cross Aldol reaction','HVZ reaction','Cannizaro reaction','NEW','12-383','12-253','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261616q.png'),
(261701,'chemistry','The product formed in the reaction diagram will be: (n26171)','CH₃CH=CH₂CH₂CHO','Alternatively, nitriles are selectively reduced by diisobutylaluminium hydride, (DIBAL-H) to imines followed by hydrolysis to aldehydes. \n\nRefer diagram(ne26123)\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','CH₃CH=CH₂CHO','CH₃CH=CH₂CH₂CHO','CH₃CH₂CH=CHCHO','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CHO','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261701q.png'),
(261702,'chemistry','The 2nd reagent in the given reaction diagram is: (n26172)','H₂O','Esters are selectively reduced by diisobutylaluminium hydride, (DIBAL-H), followed by hydrolysis to aldehydes. \n\nRefer diagram(ne26125)\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','H₂O','H₂','H₂SO4','DBS','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261702q.png'),
(261703,'chemistry','The product formed in the reaction diagram will be: (n26173)','B','Use of chromic oxide (CrO₃ ): \n\nToluene or substituted toluene is converted to benzylidene diacetate on treating with chromic oxide in acetic anhydride. \n\nThe benzylidene diacetate can be hydrolysed to corresponding benzaldehyde with aqueous acid.\n\nRefer diagram(ne26126)\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-363 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','12-363','12-233','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261703q.png'),
(261704,'chemistry','The product formed in the reaction diagram will be: (n26174)','C','By side chain chlorination followed by hydrolysis Side chain chlorination of toluene gives benzal chloride, which on hydrolysis gives benzaldehyde. \n\nThis is a commercial method of manufacture of benzaldehyde. \n\nRefer diagram(ne26127)\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-363 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','12-363','12-233','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261704q.png'),
(261705,'chemistry','The product formed in the reaction diagram will be: (n26175)','Both B and C','Treatment of acyl chlorides with dialkyl cadmium, prepared by the reaction of cadmium chloride with Grignard reagent, gives ketones\n\nTreating a nitrile with Grignard reagent followed by hydrolysis yields a ketone\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-363 Paragraph -4)\n\nRefer diagram(ne26128)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Propiophenone','Propanone','1-Phenylpropanone','Both B and C','NEW','12-363','12-233','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261705q.png'),
(261706,'chemistry','The product formed in the reaction diagram will be:(n26176)','B','Ketones react with ethylene glycol under similar conditions to form cyclic products known as ethylene glycol ketals.\n\nDry hydrogen chloride protonates the oxygen of the carbonyl compounds and therefore, increases the electrophilicity of the carbonyl carbon facilitating the nucleophilic attack of ethylene glycol.\n\nAcetals and ketals are hydrolysed with aqueous mineral acids to yield corresponding aldehydes and ketones respectively. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-367 Paragraph -5)\n\nRefer diagram(ne26129)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','12-367','12-237','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261706q.png'),
(261707,'chemistry','The product formed in the reaction diagram(n26177) will be:\n\na. 3- Hydroxybutanal\n\nb. But-2-enal\n\nc. 4-Hydroxy-4-methylpentan-2-one\n\nd. 4-Methylpent-3-en-2-one','c,d','Aldol condensation: \n\nAldehydes and ketones having at least one α-hydrogen undergo a reaction in the presence of dilute alkali as catalyst to form β-hydroxy aldehydes (aldol) or β-hydroxy ketones (ketol), respectively. \n\nThis is known as Aldol reaction.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-371 Paragraph -2)\n\nRefer diagram(ne26130)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','NEW','12-371','12-231','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261707q.png'),
(261708,'chemistry','The product formed in the reaction diagram will be: (n26178)','m-Nitrobenzaldehyde','Electrophilic substitution reaction: \n\nAromatic aldehydes and ketones undergo electrophilic substitution at the ring in which the carbonyl group acts as a deactivating and meta-directing group.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-373 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Benzoic acid','Benzyl alcohol','m-Nitrobenzaldehyde','p-Nitrobenzaldehyde','NEW','12-373','12-243','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261708q.png'),
(261709,'chemistry','The product formed in the reaction diagram will be: (n26179)','Decanoic acid','Primary alcohols are readily oxidised to carboxylic acids with common oxidising agents such as potassium permanganate (KMnO₄ ) in neutral, acidic or alkaline media or by potassium dichromate (K₂Cr₂O₇ ) and chromium trioxide (CrO₃ ) in acidic media (Jones reagent).\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-375 Paragraph -2)\n\nRefer diagram(ne26131)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Pentanal','Decanal','Decanoic acid','Dec-1-ene','NEW','12-375','12-245','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261709q.png'),
(261710,'chemistry','The product formed in the reaction diagram will be: (n26180)','Benzoic acid','Nitriles are hydrolysed to amides and then to acids in the presence of H + or OH − as catalyst. \n\nMild reaction conditions are used to stop the reaction at the amide stage.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-376 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Benzoic acid','Benzyl alcohol','Benaldehyde','Benzene','NEW','12-376','12-246','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261710q.png'),
(261711,'chemistry','The product formed in the reaction diagram will be: (n26181)','Ethanoic anhydride','Carboxylic acids on heating with mineral acids such as H2SO4 or with P2O5 give corresponding anhydride.\n\nRefer diagram(ne26132)\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-381 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Methanoic anhydride','Dicarboxylic acid','Ethanoic anhydride','Butanoic acid','NEW','12-381','12-251','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261711q.png'),
(261712,'chemistry','Which of them can\'t be formed during the reaction mentioned in the diagram (n26182) on heating?','Benzamide','Carboxylic acids react with ammonia to give ammonium salt which on further heating at high temperature give amides.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-252-253 Paragraph -last)\n\nRefer diagram(ne26133)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ammonium phthalate','Phthalamide','Phthalimide','Benzamide','NEW','12-352','12-222','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261712q.png'),
