(261806,'chemistry','Benzaldehyde → 2-phenylpropan-1-ol\n\nThe reaction can be carried out by ?','Treatment with Dil NaOH,H⁺ followed by H₂/Ni','Reaction will proceed as given in the diagram(ne26139)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Conversion Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Treatment with NaBH4 followed by hydrolysis','Treatment with Cu followed by Dil. NaOH','Treatment with Dil NaOH,H⁺ followed by H₂/Ni','Treatment with CH3CH2CHO followed by CH3MgBr','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(261807,'chemistry','Benzaldehyde → 2-hydroxyphenylacetic acid\n\nThe reaction can be carried out by ?','Treatment with HCN followed by H⁺','Reaction will proceed as given in the diagram(ne26140)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Conversion Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Treatment with NaBH4 followed by hydrolysis','Treatment with Cu followed by Dil. NaOH','Treatment with HCN followed by H⁺','Treatment with H2/Pd followed by  conc. HNO3+H2SO4','NEW','12-367','12-237',NULL),
(261808,'chemistry','Benzoic acid → m-nitrobenzyl alcohol\n\nThe reaction can be carried out by ?','Treatment with conc. HNO3+H2SO4 followed by SOCl2 followed by NaBH4/H⁺','Reaction will proceed as given in the diagram(ne26140)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Conversion Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Treatment with conc. HNO3+H2SO4 followed by SOCl2 followed by NaBH4/H⁺','Treatment with Cu followed by Dil. NaOH','Treatment by NaBH4/H⁺ followed by SOCl2','Treatment with H2/Pd followed by  conc. HNO3+H2SO4','NEW','12-384','12-254',NULL),
(261901,'chemistry','Assertion: Boiling points of carboxylic acids are higher than hydrocarbons and weakly polar compounds of comparable molecular masses.\n\nReason: Carboxylic acids have a polar carbonyl group (C=O) and a hydroxyl group (-OH) which allows them to form intermolecular hydrogen bonds.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Carboxylic acids have a polar carbonyl group (C=O) and a hydroxyl group (-OH) which allows them to form intermolecular hydrogen bonds. These hydrogen bonds increase the boiling points of carboxylic acids compared to hydrocarbons and weakly polar compounds of comparable molecular masses.','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','12-365','12-235',NULL),
(261902,'chemistry','Assertion: Oxidation reactions are used to distinguish aldehydes from ketones.\n\nReason: Aldehydes are easily oxidised to carboxylic acids by mild oxidising reagents such as Tollens’ reagent and Fehling’s reagent, whereas ketones are not oxidized by these reagents.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','The assertion that oxidation reactions are used to distinguish aldehydes from ketones is true because aldehydes and ketones have different oxidation reactions. Aldehydes can be oxidized to carboxylic acids by mild oxidizing agents such as Tollens’ reagent and Fehling’s reagent, while ketones cannot be oxidized by these reagents. The reason that aldehydes are easily oxidized while ketones are not is also true, as aldehydes have a hydrogen atom attached to the carbonyl group that can be easily oxidized to a carboxylic acid, while ketones lack this hydrogen atom and cannot undergo this oxidation reaction.','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(261903,'chemistry','Assertion: The α-hydrogens in aldehydes and ketones are acidic.\n\nReason: Aldehydes having at least one α-hydrogen, undergo Aldol condensation in the presence of a base to give α-hydroxyaldehydes','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true that the α-hydrogens in aldehydes and ketones are acidic, which is because the α-carbon adjacent to the carbonyl carbon is electron-deficient and hence can be deprotonated by a base. \n\nAldehydes having at least one α-hydrogen, undergo Aldol condensation in the presence of a base to give α-hydroxyaldehydes (aldols)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','12-371','12-241',NULL),
(261904,'chemistry','Assertion: Carboxylic acids are more acidic than alcohols and most simple phenols.\n\nReason: Carboxylic acids have a carboxyl group (-COOH), which is more acidic due to resonance stabilization of the conjugate base.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','The assertion that carboxylic acids are more acidic than alcohols and most simple phenols is true because the carboxyl group (-COOH) is an electron-withdrawing group that stabilizes the conjugate base, making it more acidic. The reason given, which is that carboxylic acids have a carboxyl group that is more acidic due to resonance stabilization of the conjugate base, is the correct explanation for the assertion.','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','12-380','12-250',NULL),
(261905,'chemistry','Assertion: The carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones can be reduced to a methylene group by Clemmensen reduction or Wolff-Kishner reduction.\n\nReason: Aldehydes are easily oxidized to carboxylic acids by mild oxidizing reagents such as Tollens’ reagent and Fehling’s reagent','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','he given assertion that the carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones can be reduced to a methylene group by Clemmensen reduction or Wolff-Kishner reduction is true \n\nThe given reason that aldehydes are easily oxidized to carboxylic acids by mild oxidizing reagents such as Tollens’ reagent and Fehling’s reagent, which can be used to distinguish aldehydes from ketones is also true, but does not explain the assertion','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','12-368','12-238',NULL),
(261906,'chemistry','Assertion: Aldehydes, ketones, and carboxylic acids  boil at higher temperatures than hydrocarbons \n\nReason: Aldehydes, ketones, and carboxylic acids contain a carbonyl group which makes them non-polar molecules.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Aldehydes, ketones, and carboxylic acids are highly polar molecules that boil at higher temperatures than hydrocarbons and weakly polar compounds of comparable molecular masses. This is because the carbonyl group in these molecules makes them highly polar.','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','12-365','12-235',NULL),
(261907,'chemistry','Assertion: Carboxylic acids can form esters through a nucleophilic substitution reaction with alcohols in the presence of an base catalyst.\n\nReason: In this reaction, the carboxylic acid reacts with an alcohol to form an ether and a molecule of water is eliminated.','Both Assertion and reason is false','The reaction between carboxylic acids and alcohols to form esters is known as esterification. Esterification is a nucleophilic substitution reaction that takes place in the presence of an acid catalyst, typically sulfuric acid or hydrochloric acid. During the reaction, the carboxylic acid reacts with the alcohol to form an ester and a molecule of water is eliminated. The mechanism involves the protonation of the carboxylic acid, followed by the nucleophilic attack of the alcohol on the carbonyl carbon of the carboxylic acid. The product formed is an ester, which is an important class of compounds used in various applications such as flavoring agents, fragrances, and solvents.','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason is false','NEW','12-381','12-251',NULL),
(261908,'chemistry','Assertion: On heating an aldehyde with Fehling’s reagent, a reddish brown precipitate is obtained.\n\nReason: Aldehydes are oxidised to corresponding carboxylate anion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Fehling\'s test is used to distinguish aldehydes from ketones. Aldehydes react with Fehling\'s reagent to form carboxylate anions, which reduce the copper ions in the Fehling\'s reagent to form a reddish brown precipitate of copper(I) oxide. The reason for this is that aldehydes are easily oxidized to carboxylic acids, and in the presence of Fehling\'s reagent, they are oxidized to the corresponding carboxylate anions. Therefore, the assertion that a reddish-brown precipitate is obtained on heating an aldehyde with Fehling\'s reagent is true, and the reason that aldehydes are oxidized to corresponding carboxylate anion is the correct explanation for this observation.','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason is false','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(261909,'chemistry','Assertion: Tollens\' test is used to distinguish between aldehydes and ketones.\n\nReason: The aldehydes are oxidised to corresponding carboxylate anion in alkaline medium.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Tollens\' reagent contains ammoniacal silver nitrate solution, which is used to oxidize aldehydes to carboxylate anions. In the presence of aldehydes, silver ions are reduced to metallic silver, producing a silver mirror. This reaction occurs in an alkaline medium.','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason is false','NEW','12-369','12-239',NULL),
(261910,'chemistry','Assertion: Dry hydrogen chloride increases the electrophilicity of the carbonyl carbon during the reaction of aldehydes or ketones with alcohols.\n\nReason: Dry hydrogen chloride protonates the oxygen atom of the carbonyl compound.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Dry hydrogen chloride protonates the oxygen of the carbonyl compounds and therefore, increases the electrophilicity of the carbonyl carbon facilitating the reaction with alcohols.','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason is false','NEW','12-367','12-237',NULL),
(261911,'chemistry','Assertion: Sodium hydrogensulphite adds to aldehydes and ketones to form subsititution products.\n\nReason: The position of the equilibrium lies largely to the left hand side for most of the aldehydes.','Both Assertion and reason is false','Sodium hydrogensulphite adds to aldehydes and ketones to form addition products, and the position of the equilibrium lies largely to the right-hand side for most aldehydes and to the left for most ketones due to steric reasons. This is because steric hindrance in ketones prevents the nucleophilic addition of sodium hydrogensulphite to the carbonyl group, leading to an equilibrium that favors the starting ketone over the addition product. On the other hand, aldehydes have only one substituent, which results in less steric hindrance and a higher yield of the addition product.','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason is false','NEW','12-367','12-237',NULL),
(261912,'chemistry','Assertion: Aldehydes are generally more reactive than ketones in nucleophilic addition reactions.\n\nReason: Sterically, the presence of two relatively large substituents in ketones hinders the approach of nucleophile to carbonyl carbon','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Sterically, the presence of two relatively large substituents in ketones hinders the approach of nucleophile to carbonyl carbon than in aldehydes having only one such substituent. Electronically, two alkyl groups reduce the electrophilicity of the carbonyl carbon more effectively in ketones than in aldehydes, making aldehydes more electrophilic and thus more reactive in nucleophilic addition reactions.','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason is false','NEW','12-366','12-236',NULL),
(261913,'chemistry','Assertion: The solubility of aldehydes and ketones decreases with increasing alkyl chain length.\n\nReason: The hydrophillic character of the alkyl group dominates over the hydrophobic character of the carbonyl group','Assertion is true but reason is false','The solubility of aldehydes and ketones decreases with increasing alkyl chain length because the hydrophobic character of the alkyl group dominates over the hydrophilic character of the carbonyl group, making the molecule less soluble in polar solvents like water.','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason is false','NEW','12-365','12-235',NULL),
(261914,'chemistry','Assertion: Aldehydes and ketones show a characteristic chemical reaction with 2,4-DNP.\n\nReason: Aldehydes and ketones react with 2,4-DNP to form a brightly coloured crystalline compound, 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazone','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','The assertion is true, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion. Aldehydes and ketones react with 2,4-DNP to form a brightly coloured crystalline compound, 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazone, which has a characteristic melting point. This reaction is used as a test for the presence of aldehydes and ketones in a sample.','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason is false','NEW','12-368','12-238',NULL),
(261915,'chemistry','Assertion: Benzaldehyde can be prepared by treating toluene or substituted toluene with chromic oxide in acetic anhydride, followed by hydrolysis.\n\nReason: Chromic oxide in acetic anhydride converts toluene or substituted toluene to benzylidene diacetate, which can be hydrolyzed to benzaldehyde with aqueous acid.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','The assertion is true that benzaldehyde can be prepared by treating toluene or substituted toluene with chromic oxide in acetic anhydride, followed by hydrolysis. The reason is also correct, that chromic oxide in acetic anhydride converts toluene or substituted toluene to benzylidene diacetate, which can be hydrolyzed to benzaldehyde with aqueous acid. In this reaction, chromic oxide in acetic anhydride converts the methyl group in toluene to a chromate ester, which then reacts with acetic anhydride to form benzylidene diacetate. The benzylidene diacetate can be hydrolyzed to benzaldehyde with aqueous acid. Therefore, the reason provides a correct and logical explanation for the assertion.','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason is false','NEW','12-363','12-233',NULL),
(261916,'chemistry','Assertion: Alkynes can be used to prepare ketones by hydration in the presence of H2SO4 and HgSO4.\n\nReason: The hydration of alkynes with H2SO4 and HgSO4 converts the triple bond of alkynes into a carbonyl group, producing ketones as the products.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','The assertion is true that alkynes can be used to prepare ketones by hydration in the presence of H2SO4 and HgSO4, except for ethyne which gives acetaldehyde. The reason is also correct, that the hydration of alkynes with H2SO4 and HgSO4 converts the triple bond of alkynes into a carbonyl group, producing ketones as the products. Hence, the reason provides a correct and logical explanation for the assertion.','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason is false','NEW','12-362','12-232',NULL),
(270101,'chemistry','__________constitute an important class of organic compounds derived by replacing one or more hydrogen atoms of ammonia molecule by alkyl/aryl group(s).','Amines','Amines constitute an important class of organic compounds derived by replacing one or more hydrogen atoms of ammonia molecule by alkyl/aryl group(s). \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 389; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Amines',NULL,'1','mcq','Amines','Amides','Anhydrides','Acetamides','NEW','12-389','12-259',NULL),
(270113,'chemistry','All of them are synthetic amines, except\na. polymers\nb. dye stuffs\nc. ephedrine\nd. drugs','c only','Synthetic examples of amines include polymers, dye stuffs and drugs. Two biologically active compounds namely adrenaline and ephedrine containing secondary amino group. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 389; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Amines',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','c only','d only','NEW','12-389','12-259',NULL),
(270114,'chemistry','Which of the following contains a tertiary amino group?','Benadryl','Adrenaline and ephedrine contains secondary amino group are used to increase bp.  \nNovocain, is used in anesthetic industry. \nBenadryl, a well known antihistaminic drug also contains tertialry amino group.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 389; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Amines',NULL,'1','mcq','Adrenaline','Ephedrine','Proline','Benadryl','NEW','12-389','12-259',NULL),
(270436,'chemistry','The solution used in Hoffman bromamide degradation reaction can\'t be\na. polar aprotic solution\nb. aqueous solution of base\nc. ethanolic solution of base','a','Hoffmann synthesis of primary amine by the reaction of amide and bromine in the presence of NaOH. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 394; Paragraph No. 2)\ndiagram(ne2760)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Preparation of Amines',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c only','all of the above','NEW','12-394','12-264',NULL),
(270437,'chemistry','Complete the given reaction diagram(n2772).','a = Ar-NH2 and b = Ar-N-(CH₃)₂','Nitration and then subsequent reduction of benzene gives aniline which further reacts with alkyl halides and shows ammonolysis.    \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 395; intext question 13.3)\ndiagram(ne2771)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Preparation of Amines',NULL,'3','mcq','a = Ar-NO₂ and b = Ar-N-(CH₃)₂','a = Ar-NH2 and b = Ar-N-(CH₃)₂','a = Ar-NO₂ and b = Ar-NH₃','a = Ar-NH₃ and b = Ar-NH₃','NEW','12-395','12-265','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270437q.png'),
(270603,'chemistry','This reaction is the basis for the separation of amines from the non basic organic compounds insoluble in water.','RN⁺H₃X⁻ + OH⁻ → RNH₂ +H₂0 +X⁻','Amine salts are soluble in water but insoluble in organic solvents like ether. This reaction is the basis for the separation of amines from the non basic organic compounds insoluble in water.   Reference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 397; Paragraph No.2)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Chemical reactions of Amines',NULL,'3','mcq','RN⁺H₃X⁻ + OH⁻ → RNH₂ +H₂0 +X⁻','RN⁺H4X⁻ + OH⁻ → RNH₂ +H₂0 +X⁻','RN⁺H₃X⁻ + HCl → RNH₂ +H₂0 +X⁻','RCN → RCH₂NH₂','NEW','12-397','12-267',NULL),
(270640,'chemistry','Complete the given reaction diagram(n2797).','N-Ethylbenzenesulphonyl amide','The reaction of benzenesulphonyl chloride with primary amine yields N-ethylbenzenesulphonyl amide.  \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 401; Paragraph No. 6). diagram(ne2799)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Chemical reactions of Amines',NULL,'3','mcq','N-Ethylbenzenesulphonyl amide','N,N-Diethylbenzenesulphonamide','N-alkylbenzenesulphonyl amide','Benzenediazonium chloride','NEW','12-401','12-271','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270640q.png'),
(270641,'chemistry','Complete the given reaction diagram(n2798).','N,N-Diethylbenzenesulphonamide','In the reaction with secondary amine, N,N-diethylbenzenesulphonamide is formed. It does not contain any hydrogen atom attached to nitrogen atom, it is not acidic and hence insoluble in alkali. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 402; Paragraph No. 8). diagram(ne27100)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Chemical reactions of Amines',NULL,'3','mcq','N,N-Diethylbenzenesulphonamide','N-Ethylbenzenesulphonyl amide.','N-Alkylbenzenesulphonyl amide','Benzenediazonium chloride','NEW','12-402','12-272','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270641q.png'),
(270642,'chemistry','Complete the given reaction diagram(n27101)','2,4,6-Tribromoaniline','Aniline reacts with bromine water at room temperature to give a white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromoaniline. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 402; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Chemical reactions of Amines',NULL,'3','mcq','2,4,6-Tribromoaniline','2,4-Dibromoaniline','4,6-Dibromoaniline','2,4-Dibromoaniline','NEW','12-402','12-272','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270642q.png'),
(270643,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R):  \n\nAssertion (A): Activating effect of –NHCOCH₃ group is less than that of amino group.\n\nReason (R): The lone pair of electrons on nitrogen is less available for donation to benzene ring by resonance.','Both assertion and reason are correct','The lone pair of electrons on nitrogen of acetanilide interacts with oxygen atom due to resonance. Hence, the lone pair of electrons on nitrogen is less available for donation to benzene ring by resonance. Therefore, activating effect of –NHCOCH₃ group is less than that of amino group. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 402; Paragraph No. 6)\ndiagram(ne27103)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Chemical reactions of Amines',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct','Both assertion and reason are wrong','Assertion is wrong and reason is correct','Assertion is correct and reason is wrong','NEW','12-402','12-272',NULL),
(270644,'chemistry','Choose the correct option for given statement:                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     ESR of aniline is controlled by _______.','acetylation','The activating effect of –NH₂ group be controlled by protecting the -NH₂ group by acetylation with acetic anhydride, then carrying out the desired substitution followed by hydrolysis of the substituted amide to the substituted amine. Reference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 402; Paragraph No. 5)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Chemical reactions of Amines',NULL,'1','mcq','acetylation','oxidation','reduction','nitration','NEW','12-402','12-272',NULL),
(270645,'chemistry','Choose the correct option for given statement:                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        In the strongly acidic medium, aniline is protonated to form the anilinium ion which is __________ directing.','meta','In the strongly acidic medium, aniline is protonated to form the anilinium ion which is meta directing.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 403; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Chemical reactions of Amines',NULL,'1','mcq','ortho','para','meta','ortho, para, meta','NEW','12-403','12-273',NULL),
(270646,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R):                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 Assertion (A): Besides the ortho and para derivatives, significant amount of meta derivative is also formed, during nitration of aniline.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Reason (R): In strongly acidic medium, aniline is protonated to form the anilinium ion which is meta directing.','Both assertion and reason are correct','Direct nitration of aniline yields tarry oxidation products in addition to the nitro derivatives. Moreover, in the strongly acidic medium, aniline is protonated to form the anilinium ion which is meta directing. That is why besides the ortho and para derivatives, significant amount of meta derivative is also formed. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 403; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Chemical reactions of Amines',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct','Both assertion and reason are wrong','Assertion is wrong and reason is correct','Assertion is correct and reason is wrong','NEW','12-403','12-273',NULL),
(270647,'chemistry','Major product formed on nitration of aniline is?','p-Nitroaniline','Since, nitration of aniline occurs in strong acidic conditions, aniline is protonated to form the anilinium ion which is meta directing and exerts electron withdrawing inductive effect (-I). As the distance increases the -I effect decreases, therefore we get para product (50%) as a major one, meta product (47%) and ortho only 2%. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 403). diagram(ne27104)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Chemical reactions of Amines',NULL,'1','mcq','o-Nitroaniline','m-Ntroaniline','p-Nitroaniline','None','NEW','12-403','12-273',NULL),
(270648,'chemistry','Choose the correct option for given statement:                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      Aniline reacts with concentrated sulphuric acid to form __________ which on heating with sulphuric acid at 453-473 K produces ___________, commonly known as __________, as the major product.','anilinium hydrogensulphate, p-aminobenzene sulphonic acid, sulphanilic acid','Aniline reacts with concentrated sulphuric acid to form anilinium hydrogensulphate which on heating with sulphuric acid at 453-473 K produces p-aminobenzene sulphonic acid, commonly known as sulphanilic acid, as the major product. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 403; Paragraph No. 4). diagram(ne27105)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Chemical reactions of Amines',NULL,'2','mcq','anilinium hydrogensulphate, o-aminobenzene sulphonic acid, sulphanilic acid','anilinium hydrogensulphate, m-aminobenzene sulphonic acid, sulphanilic acid','anilinium hydrogensulphate, p-aminobenzene sulphonic acid, sulphanolic acid','anilinium hydrogensulphate, p-aminobenzene sulphonic acid, sulphanilic acid','NEW','12-403','12-273',NULL),
(270649,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R):                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Assertion (A): Aniline does not undergo Friedel-Crafts reaction.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Reason (R): Aniline undergoes salt formation with aluminium chloride. Due to this, nitrogen of aniline acquires positive charge and hence acts as a strong deactivating group for further reaction.','Both assertion and reason are correct','Aniline does not undergo Friedel-Crafts reaction (alkylation and acetylation) due to salt formation with aluminium chloride, the Lewis acid, which is used as a catalyst. Due to this, nitrogen of\naniline acquires positive charge and hence acts as a strong deactivating group for further reaction. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 403; Paragraph No. 5)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Chemical reactions of Amines',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct','Both assertion and reason are wrong','Assertion is wrong and reason is correct','Assertion is correct and reason is wrong','NEW','12-403','12-273',NULL),
(270650,'chemistry','Arrange the following in increasing order of basic strength:                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    C₂H₅NH₂, C₆H₅NH₂, NH₃, C₆H₅CH₂NH₂ and (C₂H₅)₂NH','C₆H₅NH₂ < NH₃ < C₆H₅CH₂NH₂ <  C₂H₅NH₂ <  (C₂H₅)₂NH','Basic character of amines is basically linked with the presence of lone pair on the nitrogen atom. more the tendency of the nitrogen atom to donate that electron pair more will be its basic strength. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 404; intext question 13.4)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Chemical reactions of Amines',NULL,'3','mcq','C₆H₅NH₂ < NH₃ < C₆H₅CH₂NH₂ <  C₂H₅NH₂ <  (C₂H₅)₂NH','C₆H₅NH₂ < NH₃ < C₆H₅CH₂NH₂ > C₂H₅NH₂ <  (C₂H₅)₂NH','C₆H₅NH₂ < NH₃ < C₆H₅CH₂NH₂ <  C₂H₅NH₂ >  (C₂H₅)₂NH','C₆H₅NH₂ > NH₃ > C₆H₅CH₂NH₂ > C₂H₅NH₂ >  (C₂H₅)₂NH','NEW','12-404','12-274',NULL),
(270720,'chemistry','Choose the correct option for given statement:                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Coupling reaction is an example of ______.','ESR','Benzene diazonium chloride reacts with phenol in which the phenol molecule at its para position is coupled with the diazonium salt to form p-hydroxyazobenzene. This type of reaction is known as coupling reaction. Similarly the reaction of diazonium salt with aniline yields p-aminoazobenzene. This is an example of electrophilic substitution reaction. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 406; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Diazonium salts and their Chemical reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','ESR','NSR','EAR','NAR','NEW','12-406','12-276',NULL),
(270721,'chemistry','Choose the correct option for given statement:                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          The cyano group __________ be introduced by nucleophilic substitution of chlorine in chlorobenzene but cyanobenzene __________ be easily obtained from diazonium salt.','cannot, can','Aryl fluorides and iodides cannot be prepared by direct halogenation. The cyano group cannot be introduced by nucleophilic substitution of chlorine in chlorobenzene but cyanobenzene can be easily obtained from diazonium salt. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 406; Paragraph No. 5)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Diazonium salts and their Chemical reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','cannot, can','cannot, cannot','can, can','can, cannot','NEW','12-406','12-276',NULL),
(270819,'chemistry','Which one of the following nitro-compounds does not react with nitrous acid?','(CH₃)₃CNO₂','Tertiary nitroalkanes do not show reaction with nitrous acid beacuse of the unavailability of alpha hydrogen atom.\nReference: (NCERT class-12) (NEET-II 2016)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CH₃CH₂CH₂NO₂','(CH₃)₂CHCH₂NO₂','(CH₃)₃CNO₂','CH₃COCH(CH₃)NO₂','NEW','12-403','12-273',NULL),
(270820,'chemistry','Nitrobenzene on reaction with conc. HNO₃/H₂SO₄ at 80–100°C forms which one of the following products?','1, 3-Dinitrobenzene','NO₂ is a meta directing group, it directs the upcoming electrophile on meta position.\nReference: (NCERT class-12) (NEET 2013). \ndiagram(ne27111)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1, 4-Dinitrobenzene','1, 2, 4-Trinitrobenzene','1, 2-Dinitrobenzene','1, 3-Dinitrobenzene','NEW','12-403','12-273',NULL),
(270821,'chemistry','Identify the compound that will react with Hinsberg\'s reagent to give a solid which dissolves in alkali. diagram(n27109)','3','When Hinsberg reagent reacts with primary amine, it forms a white solid that solublises in alkaine solution.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 401; Paragraph No. 5) (NEET 2021)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-401','12-271','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270821q.png'),
(270822,'chemistry','The reagent ‘R’ in the given sequence of chemical reaction is _________. diagram(n27110)','CH₃CH₂OH','Ethanol acts as a mild reducing agent. It converts the diazonium salts into arenes, and itself get oxidised to ethanal.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 405; Paragraph No. 8) (NEET 2021).\ndiagram(ne27112)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','CH₃CH₂OH','CH₃CH₂NH₂','CH₃CH₂COOH','CH₃CH₂COH','NEW','12-405','12-275','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270822q.png'),
(270823,'chemistry','Which of the following reactions is suitable to synthesize chlorobenzene? diagram(n27111)','1','Benzene in the presence of anhydrous FeCl₃ reacts with Cl₂ to form chlorobenzene.\nReference: (NCERT class-12) (NEET 2022)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-404','12-274','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270823q.png'),
(10807,'chemistry','Which one of the following will have the largest number of atoms?','52 g of He','52 gm of Ne has 2.6 moles of Ne.\n \n 52 gm of Ar has 2.8 moles of Ar.\n \n 52 u of He has 13 x 1.66 x 10⁻²⁴ moles of He.\n \n 52 gm of He has 13 moles of He which is highest among the options.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Paragraph-9)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Mole Concept And Molar Masses',NULL,'2','mcq','52 g of He','52 g of Ne','52 g of Ar','52 u of He','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(270824,'chemistry','Given below are two statements; \n\nStatement I: Primary aliphatic amines react with HNO₂ to give unstable diazonium salts. \n\nStatement II: Primary aromatic amines react with HNO₂ to form diazonium salts which are stable even above 300 K. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect.','When a primary aliphatic amine reacts with HNO₂, it forms highly unstable diazonium salts that turns into N₂ and alcohol. While, primary aromatic amines give stable diazonium salts at 273-278 K.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 404; Paragraph No. 2) (NEET 2022)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both statement I and statement II are correct.','Both statement I and statement II are incorrect.','Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect.','Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct.','NEW','12-404','12-274',NULL),
(270825,'chemistry','The product formed from the following reaction sequence is _________. diagram(n27112)','4','Nitrile group is first reduced with (i) LiAlH₄, H₂O forming primary aromatic amine. \nThis amine undergoes diazotization reaction in the presence of (ii) NaNO₂, HCl and then subsequent hydrolysis to form benzylealcohol. \nReference:  (NCERT: Class 12; Page No. 406; Paragraph No. 1)(NEET 2022) \ndiagram(ne27113)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-406','12-276','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270825q.png'),
(270934,'chemistry','Which product is formed, when acetonitrile is hydrolysed partially with cold concentrated with cold concentrated HCl?','Acetamide','Partial hydrolysis of cyanides lead to formation of amides. These amides on reduction with lithium aluminum hydride yield amines\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 393; Paragraph No. 5). \ndiagram(ne2749)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Methyl cyanide','Acetic anhydride','Acetic acid','Acetamide','NEW','12-393','12-263',NULL),
(270935,'chemistry','Product P in the given reaction is diagram(n27125)','b','Reaction proceeds as \ndiagram(ne27120)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-393','12-263','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270935q.png'),
(270936,'chemistry','What is the product obtained in the following reaction as per the given diagram(n27126)','a','In alkaline solution, the reduction is terminated at the formation of  hydroxylamine as given in the diagram(ne27121).  When under acidic conditions, the reaction proceeds further to form aniline.\n"Nitration of benzene gives nitrobenzene by electrophilic substitution reaction. Which on further reduction shows nucleophilic substitution reaction and gives aniline.   \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 395; intext question 13.3)\ndiagram(ne2766)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-395','12-265','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270936q.png'),
(270937,'chemistry','Aniline is reacted with bromine water and the resulting product is treated with an aqueous solution of sodium nitrite in presence of dilute hydrochloric acid. The compound so formed is converted into tetraflouroborate which is subsequently heated to dry. The final product is','2,4,6-tribromobenzene','The reaction proceeds as per the diagram\n2,4,6-tribromobenzene"When arenediazonium chloride is treated with fluoroboric acid, arene diazonium fluoroborate is precipitated which on heating decomposes to yield aryl fluoride. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 405; Paragraph No. 8)"\n"When arenediazonium chloride is treated with fluoroboric acid, arene diazonium fluoroborate is precipitated which on heating decomposes to yield aryl fluoride. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 405; Paragraph No. 8)"\ndiagram(ne27122)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','p-bromoaniline','p-bromoflourobenzene','1,3,5-tribromobenzene','2,4,6-tribromobenzene','NEW','12-405','12-275',NULL),
(270938,'chemistry','Aniline in a set of reactions yielded a product D. The structure of product D in the given reaction is: refer diagram(n27127)','Ph-CH₂OH','The reaction proceeds as per the given diagram\n"The Cl⁻, Br⁻ and CN⁻ nucleophiles can easily be introduced in the benzene ring in the presence of Cu(I) ion. This reaction is called Sandmeyer reaction. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 405; Paragraph No. 4)"\ndiagram(ne27123)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ph-NHOH','Ph-NHCH₂CH₃','Ph-CH₂NH₂','Ph-CH₂OH','NEW','12-405','12-275','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270938q.png'),
(270939,'chemistry','Aniline in a set of following reactions yielded a colored product Y as given diagram(n27128)','a','The reaction proceeds as per the given diagram\nReference: (NCERT class-12) (NEET-II 2016)\nReference: (NCERT class-12) (NEET 2014).  diagram(ne27124)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-405','12-275','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270939q.png'),
(270940,'chemistry','For carbylamine reaction, we need hot alcoholic KOH and','any primary amine and chloroform','Aliphatic and aromatic primary amines on heating with chloroform and ethanolic potassium hydroxide form isocyanides or carbylamines which are foul smelling substances. Secondary and tertiary amines do not show this reaction. This reaction is known as carbylamine reaction or isocyanide test and is used as a test for primary amines. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 401; Paragraph No.1). diagram(ne2790)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','any primary amine and chloroform','chloroform and silver powder','a primary amine and alkyl halide','a monoalkylamine and trichloromethane','NEW','12-401','12-271',NULL),
(270942,'chemistry','The action of nitrous acid on an aliphatic primary amine gives','alcohol','Primary aliphatic amines react with nitrous acid to form aliphatic diazonium salts which being unstable, liberate nitrogen gas and alcohols. Quantitative evolution of nitrogen is used in estimation of amino acids and proteins. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 401; Paragraph No. 3)\ndiagram(ne27125)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','secondary amine','nitro alkane','alcohol','alkyl nitrite','NEW','12-401','12-271',NULL),
(270943,'chemistry','An aniline on nitration gives which one of them diagram(n27129)','d','NO₂⁺  acts as electrophile and attacks both ortho and para positions, so both (a) and (b) are possible.\n"As in aniline ortho and para positions to the –NH₂ group become centres of high electron density. Thus –NH₂ group is ortho and para directing and a powerful activating group. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 402; Paragraph No. 2)"','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-402','12-272','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270943q.png'),
(10808,'chemistry','Which has highest molar mass?','CO₂','Molar mass of CO₂ = 44 u, H₂O = 18 u, Methane = 16 u, NaOH = 40 u.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Mole Concept And Molar Masses',NULL,'1','mcq','H₂O','CH₄','CO₂','NaOH','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(270944,'chemistry','The compound obtained by heating a mixture of ethylamine and chloroform with ethanolic potassium hydroxide is','an ethyl isocyanide','"Aliphatic and aromatic primary amines on heating with chloroform and ethanolic potassium hydroxide form isocyanides or carbylamines which are foul smelling substances. Secondary and tertiary amines do not show this reaction. This reaction is known as carbylamine reaction or isocyanide test and is used as a test for primary amines. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 401; Paragraph No.1). diagram(ne27126)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','an amide','an amide and nitro compound','an ethyl isocyanide','an alkyl halide','NEW','12-401','12-271',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(270945,'chemistry','Phenyl isocyanide are prepared by which of the following reaction?','carbylamine reaction','"Aliphatic and aromatic primary amines on heating with chloroform and ethanolic potassium hydroxide form isocyanides or carbylamines which are foul smelling substances. Secondary and tertiary amines do not show this reaction. This reaction is known as carbylamine reaction or isocyanide test and is used as a test for primary amines. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 401; Paragraph No.1). diagram(ne27126)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Reimer-Teimann reaction','carbylamine reaction','Rosenmund reaction','Wurtz reaction','NEW','12-401','12-271',NULL),
(270946,'chemistry','What would be the final product C obtained in this reaction: diagram(n27130)','c','Reaction proceeds as given diagram(ne27127)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-401','12-271','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270946q.png'),
(270947,'chemistry','Which of the following are more basic than aniline?','Benzylamine','In benzylamine, the lone pair of electron is not involved in resonance, thus it is more basic than aniline.\n"In case of substituted aniline, it is observed that electron releasing groups like –OCH₃, –CH₃ increase basic strength whereas electron withdrawing groups like –NO₂, –SO₃H, –COOH, –X decrease it. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 399; Paragraph No.3)"','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Benzylamine','Diphenylamine','Triphenylamine','p-Nitroaniline','NEW','12-399','12-269',NULL),
(270948,'chemistry','Predict the product in the given reaction: diagram(n27131)','d','Reaction proceeds as given diagram(ne27128)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-399','12-269','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270948q.png'),
(270949,'chemistry','Match the compounds given in List I with their characteristics reactions given in List II. Select the correct option in the given diagram(n27132)','A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)','Aliphatic and aromatic primary amines on heating with chloroform and ethanolic potassium hydroxide form isocyanides or carbylamines which are foul smelling substances. Secondary and tertiary amines do not show this reaction. This reaction is known as carbylamine reaction or isocyanide test and is used as a test for primary amines. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 401; Paragraph No.1)\ndiagram(ne27129)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)','A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)','A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)','A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)','NEW','12-401','12-271','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270949q.png'),
(270950,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about primary amines is false?','Aryl amines react with nitrous acid to produce phenols.','Whne aryl amines react with nitrous acid , diazonium salts are produced.\n"Primary aliphatic amines react with nitrous acid to form aliphatic diazonium salts which being unstable, liberate nitrogen gas and alcohols. Quantitative evolution of nitrogen is used in estimation of amino acids and proteins. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 401; Paragraph No. 3)"','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Alkyl amines are stronger bases than aryl amines.','Alkyl amines react with nitrous acid to produce alcohols.','Aryl amines react with nitrous acid to produce phenols.','Alkyl amines are stronger bases than ammonia.','NEW','12-401','12-271',NULL),
(270951,'chemistry','Indicate which nitrogen compound amongst the following would undergo Hoffman reaction to furnish the primary amine?','RCONH₂','CH₃CONH₂ + Br₂ + 4KOH → CH₃NH₂ + 2KBr + K₂CO₃ + 2H₂O (Hoffmann bromamide reaction). \n"Hoffmann synthesis of primary amine by the reaction of amide and bromine in the presence of NaOH. \n\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 394; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','RCONHCH₃','RCOONH₄','RCONH₂','RCONHOH','NEW','12-394','12-264',NULL),
(270952,'chemistry','Amides may be converted into amines by a reaction named after','Hoffmann','CH₃CONH₂ + Br₂ + 4KOH → CH₃NH₂ + 2KBr + K₂CO₃ + 2H₂O (Hoffmann bromamide reaction). \n"Hoffmann synthesis of primary amine by the reaction of amide and bromine in the presence of NaOH. \n\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 394; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Hoffmann','Claisen','Perkin','Kekule','NEW','12-394','12-264',NULL),
(270953,'chemistry','Intermediates formed during reaction of RCONH₂ with Br₂ and KOH are','RCONHBr and RNCO','CH₃CONH₂ + Br₂ + 4KOH → CH₃NH₂ + 2KBr + K₂CO₃ + 2H₂O (Hoffmann bromamide reaction). \n"Hoffmann synthesis of primary amine by the reaction of amide and bromine in the presence of NaOH. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 394; Paragraph No. 2)\nMechanism of reaction is given in diagram(ne27130)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','RCONHBr and RNCO','RNHCOBr and RNCO','RNH-Br and RCONHBr','RCONBr₂','NEW','12-394','12-264',NULL),
(270954,'chemistry','Electrolytic reduction of nitrobenzene in weakly acidic medium gives','aniline','The electrolytic reduction of nitrobenzene in strongly acidic medium gives phenylhydroxyl amine which on rearrangement produces  p-aminophenol. \nIn weakly acidic medium, aniline is formed\nReference: (NCERT class-12) (NEET2015)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','N-phenylhydroxylamine','nitrosobenzene','aniline','p-hydroxyaniline','NEW','12-394','12-264',NULL),
(270955,'chemistry','In a set of reactions propionic acid yielded a compound D. The structure of compound D is as per the given reaction diagram(n27133)','CH₃CH₂NH₂','Reference: (NCERT class-12) (NEET2006)\nThe reaction proceeds as diagram(ne27131)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CH₃CH₂NH₂','CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂','CH₃CH₂CONH₂','CH₃CH₂NHCH₃','NEW','12-394','12-264','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270955q.png'),
(270956,'chemistry','In a set of reactions m-bromobenzoic acid yielded a compound D. The structure of compound D is as per the given reaction diagram(n27134)','c','Reference: (NCERT class-12) (NEET2011)\nThe reaction proceeds as diagram(ne27132)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-394','12-264','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270956q.png'),
(270957,'chemistry','The angle C-N-C is _____ in trimethylamine','108°','Due to the presence of unshared pair of electrons, the angle C–N–E (where E is C or H) is less than 109.5°, it is 108° in case of trimethylamine.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 389; Paragraph No. 3) \ndiagram(ne27115)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','109.5°','107.5°','108°','120°','NEW','12-389','12-259',NULL),
(270958,'chemistry','Select the correct option(s) from the following statements which explain about "Uses of amines",Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:\na. Adrenaline,ephedrine and Novocain are biologically active compounds, containing secondary amino group, are used to used to increase the blood pressure.\nb. Benadryl is an antihistaminic drug containing a tertiary amino group.\nc. Surfactants contain secondary amino group.\nd. In the preparation of dyes, quaternary ammonium salts are intermediates','b,c,d','Adrenaline and ephedrine contains secondary amino group are used to increase bp.  \nNovocain, is used in anesthetic industry. \nBenadryl, a well known antihistaminic drug also contains tertiary amino group.\nQuaternary ammonium salts are used as surfactants. Diazonium slats are intermediates in the preparation of dyes.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 389; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','a,b,c','b,c,d','all of the above','NEW','12-389','12-259',NULL),
(270959,'chemistry','Select the correct option(s) from the following statements :\na. Lower aliphatic amines are gases, while butanamine is liquid\nb. Ethanamine has fishy odor\nc. Aniline when left for days becomes colored\nd. Methy amine is water insoluble','a,b,c','The lower aliphatic amines are gases with fishy odour.  \nPrimary amines with three or more carbon atoms are liquid.Aniline are usually colorless but get colored on storage due to atmospheric oxidation.\nLower aliphatic amines are soluble in water because they can form hydrogen bonds with water molecules.\n\n\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 395; Paragraph No.1)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,b,c','all of the above','NEW','12-395','12-265',NULL),
(270960,'chemistry','Select the correct option(s) from the following statements which explain about "Benzenediazonium chloride",Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:\na. It is colorless amorphous solid.\nb. It is stable in cold water, but reacts with water when warmed.\nc. It decomposes easily in moist state.\nd. Benzenediazonium flouroborate and chloroborate is water insoluble and stable and room temperature.','b only','Benzenediazonium chloride is a colourless crystalline solid. It is readily soluble in water and is stable in cold water but reacts with water when warmed.\nIt decomposes easily in the dry state. Benzenediazonium floroborate is water insoluble and stable at room temperature.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 405; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','a,b,c','all of the above','NEW','12-405','12-275',NULL),
(270961,'chemistry','Reaction of diazonium salt without involving the displacement of nitrogen is\na. Gatterman reaction\nb. Treatment of reducing agent with mild reducing agents\nc. Coupling reaction\nd. Increasing the temperature of diazonium salt solution to rise upto 283 K','c only','Chlorine or bromine can be introduced in the benzene ring by treating the diazonium salt solution with corresponding halogen acid in the presence of copper powder. This is referred as Gatterman reaction. \nCertain mild reducing agents like hypophosphorus acid or ethanol reduce diazonium salts to arenes and themselves gets oxidised to phosphorus and ethanal.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 405; Paragraph No. 5,8)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','c only','all of the above','NEW','12-405','12-275',NULL),
(270962,'chemistry','Select the correct option(s) from the following statements which explain about "Coupling reaction",Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:\na. Involves retention of diazo group.\nb. Benzene diazonium chloride reacts with phenol in which the phenol molecule is at its para position is coupled with the diazonium salt to form p-hydroxybenzene.\nc. It is an example of electrophillic addtion reaction\nd. p-Aminobenzene is a yellow dye.','a,d','Benzene diazonium chloride reacts with phenol in which the phenol molecule at its para position is coupled with the diazonium salt to form p-hydroxyazobenzene. \nThis type of reaction is known as coupling reaction. \nSimilarly the reaction of diazonium salt with aniline yields p-aminoazobenzene. This is an example of electrophilic substitution reaction. \np-aminoazobenzen is a yellow dye while p-hydroxyazobenzene is orange dye.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 406; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','NEW','12-406','12-276',NULL),
(270963,'chemistry','For the preparation of primary amine from alkyl halide with simulataneous addition of one CH₂ group in the carbon chain by , the reagent used as source of nitrogen is','potassium cyanide','The reagent used is KCN. Reaction mechanism is given in the following diagram(ne27133).','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','potassium cyanide','sodium amide','sodium azide','potassium phthalimide','NEW','12-406','12-276','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270963q.png'),
(270964,'chemistry','Select the incorrect option(s) from the following statements:\na. Source of nitrogen in Gabriel synthesis is NaOH and sodium nitrite.\nb. Hoffman bromamide degradation is shown by ArCH₂NH₂\nc. p-aminoazobenzene is a yellow dye.','a,b','Gabriel synthesis:\n"Phthalimide on treatment with ethanolic potassium hydroxide forms potassium salt of phthalimide which on heating with alkyl halide followed by alkaline hydrolysis produces the corresponding primary amine. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 394; Paragraph No. 1)"\nBenzene diazonium chloride reacts with phenol in which the phenol molecule at its para position is coupled with the diazonium salt to form p-hydroxyazobenzene. \nThis type of reaction is known as coupling reaction. \nSimilarly the reaction of diazonium salt with aniline yields p-aminoazobenzene. This is an example of electrophilic substitution reaction. \np-aminoazobenzen is a yellow dye while p-hydroxyazobenzene is orange dye.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 406; Paragraph No. 3)\n"Hoffmann synthesis of primary amine by the reaction of amide and bromine in the presence of NaOH. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 394; Paragraph No. 2)\ndiagram(ne2760)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','a,b','all of the above','NEW','12-394','12-264','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270964q.png'),
(270965,'chemistry','The product obtained by the treatment of 2-phenylpropanamide with NaOBr is','1-phenylethanamine','"Hoffmann synthesis of primary amine by the reaction of amide and bromine in the presence of NaOH. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 394; Paragraph No. 2)\ndiagram(ne2760)"','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','2-phenylpropanamide','2-phenylpropanamine','1-phenylethanamine','none of the above','NEW','12-394','12-264',NULL),
(270966,'chemistry','State True or False?\na. The gas evolved when methylamine reacts with nitrous acid is hydrogen\nb. Reduction of aromatic nitro compound using Fe and HCl gives an aromatic oxime.','F,F','Primary aliphatic amines react with nitrous acid to form aliphatic diazonium salts which being unstable, liberate nitrogen gas and alcohols. Quantitative evolution of nitrogen is used in estimation of amino acids and proteins. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 401; Paragraph No. 3)\n"In the presence of Sn/HCl or Fe/HCl, Nitrogen containing aromatic compound can be reduced to primary aromatic amine that can be diazotised easily.\nReference: (NCERT class-12) (NEET-II 2016)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','T,T','T,F','F,T','F,F','NEW','12-401','12-271',NULL),
(270967,'chemistry','Select the correct option(s) from the following statements :\na. pKb of aniline is more than that of methylamine\nb. Aniline does not undergo Friedal Crafts reaction\nc. Diazonium salts of aromatic amines are less stable than those of aliphatic amines.','a,b','Lone pair of electrons in aniline is delocalised due to resonance , making the electron density on nitrogen decrease.\n+I effect due to methy group in methylamine makes it more basic compared to aniline.\n\nAniline being a base forms salt with Lewis acid. As a result it does not undergo Friedal Craft reaction.Reference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 403; Paragraph No. 5)\n\nThe diazonium salts of aromatic amines are more stable due to resonance.Primary aliphatic amines react with nitrous acid to form aliphatic diazonium salts which being unstable, liberate nitrogen gas and alcohols. Quantitative evolution of nitrogen is used in estimation of amino acids and proteins. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 401; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,b,c','NEW','12-401','12-271','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270967q.png'),
(270968,'chemistry','An aromatic compound \'A\' on treatment with aqueous ammonia and heating forms compound B which on heating with Br₂ and KOH forms a compound C of molecular formula C₆H₇N. . Compound C is','Aniline','The reaction proceeds as:\n"CH₃CONH₂ + Br₂ + 4NaOH → CH₃NH₂ + 2NaBr + Na₂CO₃ + 2H₂O (Hoffmann bromamide reaction). \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 394; Paragraph No. 2) \ndiagram(ne27135)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Benzamide','Benzoic acid','Aniline','Benzenamine','NEW','12-394','12-264','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270968q.png'),
(270969,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R):                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  \n\nAssertion (A): Only a small amount of HCl is required in reduction of nitro compounds with iron scrap and HCl in the presence of steam                                                                                                                                                                                            \n\nReason(R): Ferric chloride gets hydrolysed to release HCl during the reaction.','(A) is correct  (R) is wrong','Ferrous chloride is formed during the reaction instead of ferric chloride.\n\nReduction with iron scrap and hydrochloric acid is preferred because FeCl₂ formed gets hydrolysed to release hydrochloric acid during the reaction. Thus, only a small amount of hydrochloric acid is required to initiate the reaction. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 392; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is incorrect explanation of (A).','(A) is correct  (R) is wrong','(A) is wrong but (R) is  correct.','NEW','12-392','12-262',NULL),
(270905,'chemistry','___________ are the most usefull intermediates in the synthesis of dyestuff.','Diazonium salts','Diazonium salts of the primary aromatic amines undergoe coupling reaction to give dye molecules. Reference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 389; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'0','mcq','Isocyanates','Quaternary ammonium salts','Diazonium salts','Tertiary amines','NEW','12-389','12-259',NULL),
(270906,'chemistry','Complete the following reaction:\ndiagram(n27115)','Anilinium hydrogensulphate','Sulfonation of aniline with concentrated sulfuric acid gives the intermediate anilinium hydrogensulphate that produces sulfanilic acid on heating with sulfuric acid.  \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 403; Paragraph No. 3). \ndiagram(ne27114)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Anilinium hydrogensulphite','Anilinium hydrogensulphate','Anilinium hydrogensulphide','Anilinium hydrogensulphonate','NEW','12-403','12-273','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270906q.png'),
(270907,'chemistry','Bromination of aniline can be controlled by _________ to get monosubstituted product.','protecting the amino group','Aniline is a highly reactive compound due to the presence of NH₂ group that activates it towards aromatic electrophilic substitution reaction. \nIts bromination give trisubstituted product. Hence, the activating effect of NH₂ can be controlled by protecting it by acetylation. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 402; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','protecting the amino group','reducing the concentration of aniline','reducing the concentration of bromine','using FeCl₃','NEW','12-402','12-272',NULL),
(270908,'chemistry','Nitrous acid used for the synthesis of aryldiazonium salts, is prepared in-situ by the reaction of _________ with HCl.','sodium nitrite','Nitrous acid is produced by the reaction of NaNO₂ with HCl. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 404; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','sodium nitrate','sodium nitrite','sodiumbicarbonate','sodiumsulphite','NEW','12-404','12-274',NULL),
(270909,'chemistry','Amines serve as ___________ due to the presence of ________ in their structure.','Lewis bases, lone pair of electron','Compounds capable of donating lone pair of electrons are called Lewis bases. \nAmines (primary, secondary and tertiary) having lone pair of electron on nitrogen atom are good examples of Lewis bases. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 397; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Lewis acids, H atom','Lewis bases, H atom','Lewis acids, lone pair of electron','Lewis bases, lone pair of electron','NEW','12-397','12-267',NULL),
(10809,'chemistry','How much Copper can be obtained from 100 g of Copper sulphate?','39.81 g','1 mole of Copper sulphate contains one mole of Copper. \n \n Molar mass of Copper sulphate = 159.5 g,\n \n Thus the Cu that can be obtained from 159.5 g of Copper sulphate is 63.5 g. \n \n Cu obtained from 100 g of CuSO₄ = 63.5/159.5 x 100 = 39.81 g.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 26 Problem-1.7)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Mole Concept And Molar Masses',NULL,'2','mcq','63.5 g','159.5 g','39.81 g','127 g','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(270910,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R): \n Assertion (A): Basicity of amines depend upon the formation of cations in acidic medium.\n Reason (R): The more stable the cation is relative to the amine, more basic is the amine.','Both the assertion (A) and reason (R) are correct','Basicity of amines increases when the cation formed is stabilized by disperprsal of positive charge i.e through conjugation or +I effect. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 398; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the assertion (A) and reason (R) are correct','Both the assertion (A) and reason (R) are wrong','Assertion (A) is correct and reason (R) is wrong','Assertion (A) is wrong and reason (R) is correct','NEW','12-398','12-268',NULL),
(270911,'chemistry','Write True or False?\n\na. Coupling reactions involve removal of diazo group\n\nb. Coupling reaction is Nucleophillic substituition type of reaction.','F,F','Benzene diazonium chloride reacts with phenol in which the phenol molecule at its para position is coupled with the diazonium salt to form p-hydroxyazobenzene. \nThis type of reaction is known as coupling reaction. \nSimilarly the reaction of diazonium salt with aniline yields p-aminoazobenzene. This is an example of electrophilic substitution reaction. \np-aminoazobenzen is a yellow dye while p-hydroxyazobenzene is orange dye.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 406; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'0','mcq','T,T','T,F','F,T','F,F','NEW','12-406','12-276',NULL),
(270912,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement of the following:','both b and c','Arylamines are less basic than ammonia because the lone pair of nitrogen is involved in resonance. Hence, it is not freely available for protonation. \nOn the other hand, aliphatic amines are more basic than ammonia due to higher +I effect caused by the alkyl groups, creating high electron density on nitrogen atom.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 397; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Arylamines are more basic than ammonia','Arylamines are less basic than ammonia','Alphatic amines are more basic than ammonia','both b and c','NEW','12-397','12-267',NULL),
(270913,'chemistry','If pKb value of an amine increases, it will become _______.','less basic','Lesser the Kb value of amines or greater the pKb values, less basic is the amine. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 397; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','more basic','less basic','less acidic','neutral','NEW','12-397','12-267',NULL),
(270914,'chemistry','What will be the product when an aryldiazonium salt is heated with water?','Phenol','Diazonium salts get hydrolysed on heating resulting in the formation of an alcohol and N₂ gas.\n Reference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 405; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Phenol','Amine','Amide','Ester','NEW','12-405','12-275',NULL),
(270915,'chemistry','The reaction in which an aryl diazonium compound is converted to an azo compound is called ________.','azo-coupling','The electrophilic aromatic substitution reaction of an aryl diazonium compound with an other aromatic compound is called azo-coupling reaction.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 406; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','diazotisation','carbylamine','azo-coupling','deamination','NEW','12-406','12-276',NULL),
(270916,'chemistry','Which of the following reaction can not be used for the formation of primary aromatic amines?','Gabriel phthalimide synthesis','Primary aromatic amines can not be formed by gabriel synthesis method because aryl halides do not give nucleophilic substitution reaction with phthalimide salt produced during the reaction. Reference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 394; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Reduction of nitriles','Reduction of nitro compounds','Decarboxylation of amino acids','Gabriel phthalimide synthesis','NEW','12-394','12-264',NULL),
(270917,'chemistry','The product of the reaction of propanamide with Br₂ in the presence of NaOH is _______.','ethylamine','Ethylamine can be synthesised by reaction of propanamide with Br₂ in the presence of NaOH (Hoffman bromamide reaction). \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 394; Paragraph No. 2)\ndiagram(ne27116)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','ethylamine','methylamine','propylamine','butylamine','NEW','12-394','12-264',NULL),
(270918,'chemistry','Which of the following organic compound gives secondary amine on reduction?','Methyl isocyanide','Methyl isocyanide gives secondary amine on reduction.\nReference: (NCERT class-12)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Methyl isocyanide','Nitromethane','Nitrobenzene','Methyl cyanide','NEW','12-394','12-264',NULL),
(270919,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R):\nAssertion (A): N-methyl acetamide does not give Hoffmann bromamide degradation reaction. \nReason (R): It can not form the required intermediate, sodium salt of N-bromamide during the reaction.','Both the assertion (A) and reason (R) are correct.','N-methyl acetamide does not give Hoffmann bromamide degradation reaction because it can not form the required intermediate, sodium salt of N-bromamide.\nReference: (NCERT class-12)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion (A) is correct and reason (R) is wrong','Assertion (A) and reason (R) both are wrong','Both the assertion (A) and reason (R) are correct.','Assertion (A) is wrong and reason (R) is correct','NEW','12-394','12-264',NULL),
(270920,'chemistry','Identify the cannonical structures of aniline. \ndiagram(n27117)','B','Resonance stabilised structures of aniline are also known as cannonical structures. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 399; Paragraph No. 2)\ndiagram(ne27117)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','12-399','12-269','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270920q.png'),
(270921,'chemistry','The following diagram represents the resonance structures of _______. \ndiagram(n27118)','anilinium ion','These are the resonance stabilised forms of anilinium ion.\nAnilinium hydrogen ion and anilinium anions are the wrong terms.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 399; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','anilinium ion','aniline','anilinium hydrogen ion','anilinium anion','NEW','12-399','12-269','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270921q.png'),
(270922,'chemistry','The compound, ephidrine contains ________ type of amine. \ndiagram(n27119)','secondary','Amines in which nitrogen atom is attached with two alkyl moeties are classified as secondary amines.\nReference: (NCERT class-12)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','primary','secondary','tertiary','quaternary','NEW','12-390','12-260','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270922q.png'),
(270923,'chemistry','Match the following columns:\ndiagram(n27120)','(A)-(i), (B)-(iv), (C)-(iii), (D)-(ii)','Primary amine is the one in which one hydrogen in ammonia is replaced by alkyl or aryl group. \nIn secondary amines two hydrogens in ammonia get replaced by alkyl or aryl groups. \nIn tertiary amines three hydrogens in ammonia get replaced by alkyl or aryl groups. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No.390; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(A)-(iv), (B)-(iii), (C)-(ii), (D)-(i)','(A)-(ii), (B)-(i), (C)-(iii), (D)-(iv)','(A)-(i), (B)-(iv), (C)-(iii), (D)-(ii)','(A)-(iii), (B)-(iv), (C)-(i), (D)-(ii)','NEW','12-390','12-260','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270923q.png'),
(270924,'chemistry','Identify wrong IUPAC name of the following:','N-Butylaminoethane','According to IUPAC nomenclature, the largest alkyl group is considered as the parent chain and rest as substituents.\nTherefore, N-Butylaminoethane is the wrong name in which the ethyl group is considered as the parent chain and butyl group as substituent. \nAll other options are correct according to IUPAC nomenclature rules.\nReference: (NCERT class-12)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','N-Methylaniline','1-Phenylethanamine','Propane-1,2-diamine','N-Butylaminoethane','NEW','12-390','12-260',NULL),
(270925,'chemistry','Which of the following is not an IUPAC name of amine?\ni) Ethylmethylamine\nii) Propan-1-amine\niii) Aniline\niv) Benzenamine','Ethylmethylamine','According to IUPAC nomenclature of amines containing two or more alkyl groups, first the largest alkyl group is considered as the parent chain and rest as substituents.\nThen locant nitrogen is used to designate the subsitituent attached to it.\nFor example, CH₃NHCH₂CH₃ is named as N-methylethanamine. It is commonly named as \'Ethylmethylamine\'.\nAll other options are correct IUPAC names of the amines.\nAniline is a common name but it is also acceptable as IUPAC name.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No.390; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Ethylmethylamine','Propan-1-amine','Aniline','Benzenamine','NEW','12-390','12-260',NULL),
(270926,'chemistry','Amine salt on treatment with base like NaOH  ________.','regenerates to the parent amine','Usually salts of amines regenerate the parent amines when treated with strong bases like NaOH.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No.396; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','decomposes','gives rearrangement reaction','polymerises','regenerates to the parent amine','NEW','12-396','12-266',NULL),
(270927,'chemistry','Each of three sp³ hybridised N atom in amines overlap with carbon or hydrogen orbitals. While the fourth orbital of nitrogen in _______ amines contains an unshared pair of electron.','all of these','The fourth orbital of nitrogen in all amines contains an unshared pair of electrons. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No.389; Paragraph No. 3)\ndiagram(ne27119)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','primary','secondary','tertiary','all of these','NEW','12-389','12-259',NULL),
(270928,'chemistry','Complete the following reaction. diagram(n27121)','4-Methoxyaniline','Reduction of nitro compounds with palladium or platinum metal over H₂ in the presence of ethanol gives the corresponding amines.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No.392; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Anisole','Aniline','4-Methylaniline','4-Methoxyaniline','NEW','12-392','12-262','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270928q.png'),
(270929,'chemistry','Complete the following reaction. diagram(n27122)','H₂/Pd, ethanol','Reduction of nitro compounds with palladium or platinum metal over H₂ in the presence of ethanol gives the corresponding amines.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No.392; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','H₂/NH₃, ethanol','H₂/Pd, ethanol','H₂O/Pd','H₂/AlH₃, H₂O','NEW','12-392','12-262','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270929q.png'),
(270930,'chemistry','Complete the following reaction in the given diagram and choose the appropriate answer. diagram(n27123)','A','Diazonium group can be replaced by nitro group when heated with aqueous solution of NaNO₂ in the presence of Cu.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 406; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','12-406','12-276','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/270930q.png'),
(270931,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding following two statements:\n\nStatement I: Ethylamine gives highly unstable diazonium salt when reacts with nitrous acid.\nStatement II: When 2-amono-6-methoxybenzothiazole (primary aromatic amine) reacts with nitrous acid, it forms the diazonium salt that is stable even after 300 K.','Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect','Diazonium salts of primary aliphatic amines are highly unstable. They cannot be isolated even at room temperature.\nWhile the diazonium salts of aromatic amines are relatively more stable. \nThey can be stored in a solution kept at 273-278 K for a short period of time. But at higher temperature these salts decompose rapidly. \nTherefore, Statement I is correct but stayement II is wrong.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 404; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect','Statement II is correct but statement I is incorrect','Both statements I and II are correct','Both statements I and II are incorrect','NEW','12-404','12-274',NULL),
(270932,'chemistry','Reaction of primary amine with chlorofom in the presence of alcohlic KOH results in the formation of a/an _________.','isocyanide','When a primary amine reacts with chlorofom in the presence of alcohlic KOH, isocynide or carbylamine is formed. \nIt is a specific test reaction to identify primary amines.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 401; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','isocyanide','cyanide','alcohol','aldehyde','NEW','12-401','12-271',NULL),
(270933,'chemistry','When benzene diazonium chloride reacts with phenol, it forms p-hydroxyazobenzene that is a ________ compound due to __________.','coloured, extended conjugation system','The product of a diazo-coupling reaction is a coloured substance having extended conjugation system, joining both the azo group and the aromatic rings.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 406; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','coloured, extended conjugation system','colourless compound, unconjugated system','water insoluble, non-polar system','both b and c','NEW','12-406','12-276',NULL),
(270970,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R):                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  \n\nAssertion (A): Aromatic primary amines can\'t be prepared by Gabriel phthalimide reactions                                                                                                                                                                                           \n\nReason(R): Aryl halides do not undergo nucleophillic substitution reactions easily','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Gabriel phthalimide reaction depends on nucleophillic attack by phthalimide , since aryl halides do not undergo nucleophillic substitution reacions, therefore primary aromatic amines can\'t be prepared.\n\n"Gabriel phthalimide reaction are used for the synthesis of primary amine from alkylhalide and phthalimide in the presence of alkali. "\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 394; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is incorrect explanation of (A).','(A) is correct  (R) is wrong','(A) is wrong but (R) is  correct.','NEW','12-394','12-264',NULL),
(271014,'chemistry','Best method for preparing primary amines from alkyl halides without changing the number of  carbon atoms in the chain is','Gabriel phthalimide synthesis','"Gabriel synthesis is used for the preparation of primary amines. \n\n"Phthalimide on treatment with ethanolic potassium hydroxide forms potassium salt of phthalimide which on heating with alkyl halide followed by alkaline hydrolysis produces the corresponding primary amine. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 394; Paragraph No. 1)"','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Hoffman bromamide reaction','Gabriel phthalimide synthesis','Sandmeyer reaction','Reaction with Ammonia','NEW','12-394','12-264',NULL),
(271015,'chemistry','Which of the following compound will not undergo azo coupling reaction with benzene diazonium chloride?','Nitrobenzene','In nitrobenzene, the nitro group is an electron withdrawing group. It will not favour the azo coupling reaction where Diazonium cation acts as weak electrophile. The other compound will proceed as given in the diagram(ne27144)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Aniline','Phenol','Anisole','Nitrobenzene','NEW','12-406','12-276',NULL),
(271016,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds is the weakest Bronsted base? Refer diagram(n27143)','c','Phenol is the weakest Bronsted base as phenol is more acidic and thus has the least tendency to accept a proton. Thus it is the weakest Bronsted base among the options.','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-397','12-267','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/271016q.png'),
(271017,'chemistry','Which of the following should be most volatile?','CH₃CH₂CH₃','Amines has higher boiling point due to the presence of Hydrogen bonding which is absent in Hydrocarbons. \nSo , hydrocarbons are more volatile than amines','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂','(CH₃)₃N','CH₃CH₂NHCH₃','CH₃CH₂CH₃','NEW','12-395','12-265',NULL),
(271018,'chemistry','Which of the following cannot be prepared by Sandmeyer\'s reaction?\n\na. Chlorobenzene\n\nb. Bromobenzene\n\nc. Iodobenzene\n\nd. Flourobenzene','c,d','Iodobenzene and flourobenzene can be prepared by direct reaction of diazonium salts with KI and HBF4.\n\nSandmeyer Reaction:\n"The Cl⁻, Br⁻ and CN⁻ nucleophiles can easily be introduced in the benzene ring in the presence of Cu(I) ion. This reaction is called Sandmeyer reaction. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 405; Paragraph No. 4)". Refer diagram(ne27145)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,b,c','NEW','12-405','12-275',NULL),
(271019,'chemistry','Reduction of nitrobenzene by which of the following reagent gives aniline?\n\na. Sn/HCl\nb. Fe/HCl\nc. H2/Pd\nd. Sn/NH4OH','a,b,c','"In the presence of Sn/HCl or Fe/HCl, Nitrogen containing aromatic compound can be reduced to primary aromatic amine . H2/Pd can also reduce nitro group to amino group.\nReference: (NCERT class-12)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,b,c','NEW','12-405','12-275',NULL),
(271020,'chemistry','Which of the following species are involved in the carbylamine test?\n\na. R-NC\nb. CHCl₃\nc. COCl₂\nd. NaNO₂+ HCl','a,b','"Aliphatic and aromatic primary amines on heating with chloroform and ethanolic potassium hydroxide form isocyanides or carbylamines which are foul smelling substances. Secondary and tertiary amines do not show this reaction. This reaction is known as carbylamine reaction or isocyanide test and is used as a test for primary amines. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 401; Paragraph No.1).','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,b,c','NEW','12-401','12-271',NULL),
(271021,'chemistry','The reagents that can be used to convert benzenediazonium chloride to benzene are:\n\na. SnCl₂/HCl\nb. CH₃CH₂OH\nc. H₃PO₂\nd. LIAlH₄','b,c','Certain mild reducing agents like hypophosphorus acid or ethanol reduce diazonium salts to arenes and themselves gets oxidised to phosphorus and ethanal. Reference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 405; Paragraph No. 5,8). Refer diagram(ne27146)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,b,c','NEW','12-401','12-271',NULL),
(271022,'chemistry','The product of the following reaction is: Refer diagram(n27144)','a,b','The reaction will proceed as given in the diagram(ne27147)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,b,c','NEW','12-402','12-272','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/271022q.png'),
(271023,'chemistry','Arenium ion involved in the bromination of aniline is: Refer diagram(n27145)','a,b,c','Mechanism of the reaction is given in the diagram(ne27148)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,b,c','NEW','12-402','12-272','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/271023q.png'),
(271024,'chemistry','Which of the following amines can be prepared by Gabriel synthesis?\n\na. Isobutyl amine\nb. 2-phenylethylamine\nc. N-methylbenzylamine\nd. Aniline','a,b','"Gabriel synthesis is used for the preparation of primary amines. Here isobutylamine and 2-phenylethylamine are primary amines.\n\n"Phthalimide on treatment with ethanolic potassium hydroxide forms potassium salt of phthalimide which on heating with alkyl halide followed by alkaline hydrolysis produces the corresponding primary amine. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 394; Paragraph No. 1)"','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,b,c','NEW','12-394','12-264',NULL),
(271025,'chemistry','Under which of the following reaction conditions, aniline given p-nitro derivative as the major product?\n\na. Acetyl chlrodie/pyridine followed by reaction with conc. H2SO4 + conc. HNO3 \n\nb. Acetic anhydride/ pyridine followed by conc. H2SO4 + conc. HNO3\n\nc. Dil. HCl followed by reaction with conc.  H2SO4 + conc. HNO3\n\nd. Reaction with conc.  H2SO4 + conc. HNO3','a,b','The reaction will proceed as given in the diagram(ne27149)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,b,c','NEW','12-402','12-272',NULL),
(271026,'chemistry','Which of the following reactions belong to electrophillic aromatic substitution?\n\na. Bromination of acetanilide\n\nb. Coupling reaction of aryldiazonium salts \n\nc. Diazotisation of aniline\n\nd. Acylation of aniline','a,b','Bromination of acetanilide and coupling reaction of aryldiazonium salts is an example of electrophillic aromatic substitution reactions.\n\nThe electrophilic aromatic substitution reaction of an aryl diazonium compound with an other aromatic compound is called azo-coupling reaction.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 406; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Exemplar based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,b,c','NEW','12-402','12-272',NULL),
(240338,'chemistry','IUPAC name of this compound is_________. diagram(n2456)','tetrachloromethane','There are several sets of rules for IUPAC nomenclature. \n\n1. The longest continuous carbon chain should be found and named.\n\n2. List the names of the organisations that make up this chain.\n\n3. Starting at the end of the chain closest to a substituent group, sequentially number the chain.\n\n4. Indicate each substituent group\'s location with the relevant number and name.\n\n5. Put the name together by listing the groups alphabetically using the whole name. (e.g., cyclopropyl before isobutyl)\n\n(Reference: NCERT Table 10.1  Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 292 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Nomenclature',NULL,'3','mcq','carbon tetrachloride','tetrachloromethane','n-propyl fluoride','1-fluoropropane','NEW','12-293','12-163','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240338q.png'),
(240339,'chemistry','IUPAC name of this compound is__________. diagram(n2457)','1-fluoropropane','There are several sets of rules for IUPAC nomenclature. \n\n1. The longest continuous carbon chain should be found and named.\n\n2. List the names of the organisations that make up this chain.\n\n3. Starting at the end of the chain closest to a substituent group, sequentially number the chain.\n\n4. Indicate each substituent group\'s location with the relevant number and name.\n\n5. Put the name together by listing the groups alphabetically using the whole name. (e.g., cyclopropyl before isobutyl)\n\n(Reference: NCERT Table 10.1  Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 292 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Nomenclature',NULL,'3','mcq','carbon tetrachloride','tetrachloromethane','n-propyl fluoride','1-fluoropropane','NEW','12-293','12-163','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240339q.png'),
(240340,'chemistry','IUPAC name of this compound is_______. diagram(n2459)','3-bromo-2-methylbut-1-ene','There are several sets of rules for IUPAC nomenclature. \n\n1. The longest continuous carbon chain should be found and named.\n\n2. List the names of the organisations that make up this chain.\n\n3. Starting at the end of the chain closest to a substituent group, sequentially number the chain.\n\n4. Indicate each substituent group\'s location with the relevant number and name.\n\n5. Put the name together by listing the groups alphabetically using the whole name. (e.g., cyclopropyl before isobutyl)\n\n(Reference: NCERT Table 10.1  Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 292 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Nomenclature',NULL,'3','mcq','4-bromopent-2-ene','3-bromo-2-methylbut-1-ene','4-bromo-3-methylpent-2-ene','1-bromo-2-methylbut-2-ene','NEW','12-293','12-163','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240340q.png'),
(240341,'chemistry','Common name of this compound is_____. diagram(n2448)','tert-butyl bromide','The common names of alkyl halides are derived by naming the alkyl group followed by the name of halide.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Table 10.1  Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 292 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Nomenclature',NULL,'3','mcq','2-bromo-2-methylpropane','tert-butyl bromide','vinyl chloride','chloroethene','NEW','12-293','12-163','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240341q.png'),
(240342,'chemistry','IUPAC name of this compound is______. diagram(n2451)','1-chloro-2-methylbenzene','There are several sets of rules for IUPAC nomenclature. \n\n1. The longest continuous carbon chain should be found and named.\n\n2. List the names of the organisations that make up this chain.\n\n3. Starting at the end of the chain closest to a substituent group, sequentially number the chain.\n\n4. Indicate each substituent group\'s location with the relevant number and name.\n\n5. Put the name together by listing the groups alphabetically using the whole name. (e.g., cyclopropyl before isobutyl)\n\n(Reference: NCERT Table 10.1 Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 292)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Nomenclature',NULL,'3','mcq','allyl bromide','3-bromopropene','o-chlorotoluene','1-chloro-2-methylbenzene','NEW','12-293','12-163','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240342q.png'),
(240343,'chemistry','Common name of this compound in the given diagram is_______. (n2449)','vinyl chloride','The common names of alkyl halides are derived by naming the alkyl group followed by the name of halide.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Table 10.1  Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 292 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Nomenclature',NULL,'3','mcq','2-bromo-2-methylpropane','tert-butyl bromide','vinyl chloride','chloroethene','NEW','12-293','12-163','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240343q.png'),
(240529,'chemistry','Which of the following named reaction is used to synthesise haloarenes from primary aromatic amines?','Sandmeyer reaction','When a primary aromatic amine, dissolved or suspended in cold aqueous mineral acid, is treated with sodium nitrite, a diazonium salt is formed. \n\nThis is called Sandmeyer Reaction.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 296; Paragraph 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Sandmeyer reaction','Swarts reaction','Finkelstein\nreaction','Stroke reaction','NEW','12-295','12-165',NULL),
(240530,'chemistry','Which among the following is/are dihalogen derivatives of propane:','All of the above','Dihalogen derivatives of propane are:\n\n1. ClCH₂CH₂CH₂Cl\n\n2. ClCH₂CHClCH₃\n\n3. Cl₂CHCH₂CH₃\n\n(Reference: Intext Question 10.3 Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 297)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','ClCH₂CH₂CH₂Cl','ClCH₂CHClCH₃','Cl₂CHCH₂CH₃','All of the above','NEW','12-295','12-165',NULL),
(240531,'chemistry','Which is the intermediate species formed in the Sandmeyer Reaction?','Diazonium salt','When a primary aromatic amine, dissolved or suspended in cold aqueous mineral acid, is treated with sodium nitrite, a diazonium salt is formed.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 296; Paragraph 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Diazonium salt','Cation','Anion','Free Radical','NEW','12-295','12-165',NULL),
(240532,'chemistry','The diazonium salt formed in Sandmeyer reaction is reacted with which reagent ?','Cu₂X₂','The diazonium salt formed in Sandmeyer reaction is reacted with Cu₂X₂. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 296; Paragraph 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Cu₂X₂','CuX','Cu₂X','Cu₂X₃','NEW','12-295','12-165',NULL),
(240533,'chemistry','What is the product formed in the given reaction. diagram(n2402)','Aryl halide','When a primary aromatic amine, dissolved or suspended in cold aqueous mineral acid, is treated with sodium nitrite, a diazonium salt is formed. \n\nMixing the solution of freshly prepared diazonium salt with cuprous chloride or cuprous bromide results in the replacement of the diazonium group by –Cl or –Br.\n\n(Reference :NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 296; Paragraph 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','Aryl halide','Alkyl halide','Diazonium salt','Alkyl dihalide','NEW','12-295','12-165','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240533q.png'),
(240534,'chemistry','With which of the following reagent ,there is no need of Cu₂X₂ ?','KI','In this reaction, catalysis by copper(I) is not mandatory, as iodide itself is easily and reversibly oxidizable and, thus, performs the function which copper(I) ions do in the Sandmeyer reaction.\n  \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 296; Paragraph 5)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','KI','KCl','NaI','NaCl','NEW','12-295','12-165',NULL),
(240535,'chemistry','What is the product formed in this reaction diagram?(n2403)','Both A & C','The reactant follow Markovnikov\'s rule during the reaction. \n\nThe common name of 2-chlorobutane is sec-butyl chloride. so the answer is both.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Example: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 297)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','2-chlorobutane','chlorobutane','sec-butylchloride','Both A & C','NEW','12-295','12-165','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240535q.png'),
(240536,'chemistry','Why is sulphuric acid not used during the reaction of alcohols with KI?','As H₂SO₄  is oxidising agent','Since H₂SO₄  is an oxidizing agent, it oxidizes HI (produced in the reaction to I₂). \n\nHence, the reaction between alcohol and HI to produce alkyl iodide cannot occur. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 297;intext Question)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','As H₂SO₄  is oxidising agent','H₂SO₄  is strong acid','H₂SO₄  is reducing agent','It can only be used as a solvent','NEW','12-295','12-165',NULL),
(240537,'chemistry','How many different dihalogen derivatives of propane are possible?','4','Propane has 3 carbon atoms. \n\nConsidering that the two terminal carbons are equivalent. it can give only 4 different dihalogens.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 297;intext Question)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','4','5','2','1','NEW','12-295','12-165',NULL),
(240717,'chemistry','Assertion : in the transition state, carbon is simultaneously bonded to five atoms in Sɴ2 reactions. \n\nReason: In the transition state, the carbon atom is simultaneously bonded to incoming nucleophile and the outgoing leaving group.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','In the transition state, the carbon atom is simultaneously bonded to incoming nucleophile and the outgoing leaving group. \n\nSuch structures are unstable and cannot be isolated. \n\nThus, in the transition state, carbon is simultaneously bonded to five atoms. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 302 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-300','12-171',NULL),
(240723,'chemistry','The relative rate of Sɴ2 reaction is:','methyl > ethyl > isopropyl > tert-butyl','Thus the order of reactivity followed is:\n\nMethyl > Ethyl > isopropyl> Tert-butyl\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 303)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','methyl > ethyl > isopropyl > tert-butyl','Ethyl > methyl > isopropyl > tert-butyl','methyl > isopropyl > ethy l> tert-butyl','methyl > tert-butyl > ethyl > isopropyl','NEW','12-300','12-171',NULL),
(240724,'chemistry','Polar protic solvents are:','All of the above','Sɴ1 reactions are generally carried out in polar protic solvents (like water, alcohol, acetic acid, etc.)  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 303 Paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','water','alcohol','acetic acid','All of the above','NEW','12-301','12-173',NULL),
(240725,'chemistry','The reaction between tert-butyl bromide and hydroxide ion yields tert-butyl alcohol and follows','Sɴ1','The reaction between tert- butyl bromide and hydroxide ion yields tert-butyl alcohol and follows the first order kinetics, i.e., the rate of reaction depends upon the concentration of only one reactant, which is tert- butyl bromide. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 303 second Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Sɴ1','Sɴ2','E1','E2','NEW','12-301','12-173',NULL),
(240726,'chemistry','Statements true regarding Sɴ1 substitution reaction are:  \n\na) It occurs in two steps. \n\nb) The carbocation  formed is then attacked by nucleophile in step II to complete the substitution reaction.','Both a and b','It occurs in two steps. \n\nIn step I, the polarised C—Br bond undergoes slow cleavage to produce a carbocation and a bromide ion. \n\nThe carbocation thus formed is then attacked by nucleophile in step II to complete the substitution reaction. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 303 third Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','Both a and b','None of the above','NEW','12-301','12-173',NULL),
(240727,'chemistry','What can be depicted from given reaction: diagram(n2411)','Sɴ1','In step I, the polarised C—Br bond undergoes slow cleavage to produce a carbocation and a bromide ion. \n\nThe carbocation thus formed is then attacked by nucleophile in step II to complete the substitution reaction. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 303 third Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Sɴ1','Sɴ2','E1','E2','NEW','12-301','12-173','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240727q.png'),
(240728,'chemistry','Statements true regarding Sɴ1 substitution reaction are: \n \na) It occurs in two steps. \n\nb) The 1st step is slowest. \n\nc) 1st step is irreversible.','a and b','Step I is the slowest and reversible in Sɴ1. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 304 first Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','a and b','only c','a and c','only b','NEW','12-301','12-173',NULL),
(240729,'chemistry','Assertion: The rate of reaction depends only on the concentration of alkyl halide and not on the concentration of hydroxide ion in Sɴ1. \n\nReason: The rate of reaction does not depends upon the slowest step.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Since the rate of reaction depends upon the slowest step, the rate of reaction depends only on the concentration of alkyl halide and not on the concentration of hydroxide ion. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 304 first Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-301','12-173',NULL),
(240730,'chemistry','Assertion: In case of alkyl halides, tertiary alkyl halides undergo Sɴ1 reaction very fast. \n\nReason:  Greater the stability of carbocation, greater will be its ease of formation from alkyl halide and faster will be the rate of reaction.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Tertiary alkyl halides undergo Sɴ1 reaction very fast because of the high stability of tert -carbocations. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 304 first Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-301','12-173',NULL),
(240731,'chemistry','The order of reactivity followed in Sɴ1 is:','Tertiary halide > Secondary halide > Primary halide','In case of alkyl halides, 30 alkyl halides undergo Sɴ1 reaction very fast because of the high stability of 3° carbocations. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 304 first Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Primary halide > Tertiary halide > Secondary halide','Tertiary halide > Secondary halide > Primary halide','Primary halide > Secondary halide > Tertiary halide','All have same','NEW','12-301','12-173',NULL),
(240732,'chemistry','If the reagent is KCN, and nucleophile is CN then substitution product is','RCN','If the reagent is KCN, and nucleophile is CN then substitution product is RCN\n\n(Reference: NCERT Table 10.4 Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 300)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','ROH','RNC','RCN','R-I','NEW','12-302','12-174',NULL),
(240733,'chemistry','For a given alkyl group, the reactivity of the halide, R-X, follows the same order in both the mechanisms that is:','R–I> R–Br>R–Cl>>R–F','For a given alkyl group, the reactivity of the halide, R-X, follows the same order in both the mechanisms R–I> R–Br>R–Cl>>R–F. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 304 third Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','R–I> R–Br>R–Cl>>R–F','R–F> R–Br>R–Cl>>R–I','R–F> R–Cl>R–Br>>R–I','R–Cl> R–F>R–Br>>R–I','NEW','12-302','12-174',NULL),
(240734,'chemistry','In the following pairs of halogen compounds, which would undergo Sɴ2 reaction faster? diagram(n2412)','A','Benzyl chloride is primary halide and therefore undergoes Sɴ2\nreaction faster. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Eg 10.6 NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 304)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','Both A and B','Can\'t Undergo Sɴ2','NEW','12-302','12-174','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240734q.png'),
(240735,'chemistry','In the following pairs of halogen compounds, which would undergo Sɴ2 reaction faster? diagram(n2413)','A','As iodine is a better leaving group because of its large size, it will be released at a faster rate in the presence of incoming nucleophile.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Eg 10.6 NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 304)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','Both A and B','Can\'t Undergo Sɴ2','NEW','12-302','12-174','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240735q.png'),
(240736,'chemistry','Plane of plane polarised light produced by passing ordinary light through Nicol prism is rotated when it is passed through the solutions of certain compounds. Such compounds are called','Optically active','Plane of plane polarised light produced by passing ordinary light through Nicol prism is rotated when it is passed through the solutions of certain compounds. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 305 first Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Optically active','Optically inactive','Chiral','Radio active','NEW','12-303','12-175',NULL),
(240737,'chemistry','The angle by which the plane polarised light is rotated is measured by :','Polarimeter','The angle by which the plane polarised light is rotated is measured by an instrument called polarimeter.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 305 first Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Opticalmeter','Polarimeter','Lightmeter','Rotator','NEW','12-303','12-175',NULL),
(240738,'chemistry','If the compound rotates the plane of plane polarised light to the right, i.e., clockwise direction, it is called :','Dextrorotatory','If the compound rotates the plane of plane polarised light to the right, i.e., clockwise direction, it is called dextrorotatory (Greek for right rotating) or the d-form and is indicated by placing a positive (+) sign before the degree of rotation. \n\nIf the light is rotated towards left (anticlockwise direction), the compound is said to be laevo-rotatory or the l-form and a negative (–) sign is placed before the degree of rotation.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 305 second Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Dextrorotatory','Levorotatory','(-)-D-form','(+)-L-form','NEW','12-303','12-175',NULL),
(240739,'chemistry','If the compound rotates the plane of plane polarised light to the left, i.e., anti clockwise direction, it is called','Levorotatory','If the compound rotates the plane of plane polarised light to the right, i.e., clockwise direction, it is called dextrorotatory (Greek for right rotating) or the d-form and is indicated by placing a positive (+) sign before the degree of rotation. \n\nIf the light is rotated towards left (anticlockwise direction), the compound is said to be laevo-rotatory or the l-form and a negative (–) sign is placed before the degree of rotation. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 305 second Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Dextrorotatory','Levorotatory','(-)-D-form','(+)-L-form','NEW','12-303','12-175',NULL),
(240740,'chemistry','Assertion: Negative (–) sign is placed before the degree of rotation for dextrorotatory. \n\nReason: l-form and is indicated by placing a positive (+) sign before the degree of rotation.','Both assertion and reason are false.','If the compound rotates the plane of plane polarised light to the right, i.e., clockwise direction, it is called dextrorotatory (Greek for right rotating) or the d-form and is indicated by placing a positive (+) sign before the degree of rotation. \n\nIf the light is rotated towards left (anticlockwise direction), the compound is said to be laevo-rotatory or the l-form and a negative (–) sign is placed before the degree of rotation.\n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 305 second Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-303','12-175',NULL),
(240741,'chemistry','(+) and (–) isomers of a compound are called','Optical Isomer','(+) and (–) isomers of a compound are called optical isomers and the phenomenon is termed as optical isomerism. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 305 second Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Stereoisomer','Enantiomers','Metamers','Optical Isomer','NEW','12-303','12-175',NULL),
(240742,'chemistry','The observation of Louis Pasteur (1848) that crystals of certain compounds exist in the form of mirror images laid the foundation of modern','Stereochemistry','The observation of Louis Pasteur (1848) that crystals of certain compounds exist in the form of mirror images laid the foundation of modern stereochemistry. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 305 last Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Stereochemistry','Stoichiometry','Optical Isomerism','Metamerism','NEW','12-303','12-175',NULL),
(240743,'chemistry','Observations of Louis Pasteur laid the foundation of modern stereochemistry. Correct Statements are:\n \na) He demonstrated that aqueous solutions of both types of crystals showed optical rotation, equal in magnitude (for solution of equal concentration) but opposite in direction. \n\nb) He believed that this difference in optical activity was associated with the three dimensional arrangements of atoms in the molecules (configurations) of two types of crustal.','both a and b','The observation of Louis Pasteur (1848) that crystals of certain compounds exist in the form of mirror images laid the foundation of modern stereochemistry.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 305 last Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','both a and b','both observations were wrong','NEW','12-303','12-175',NULL),
(240744,'chemistry','Who developed the first prism that produced plane polarised light?','William Nicol','William Nicol (1768- 1851) developed the first prism that produced plane polarised light. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 305 second Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','C. Le Bel','Jacobus Hendricus Van’t Hoff','William Nicol','Marie Curie','NEW','12-303','12-175',NULL),
(240745,'chemistry','Which scientist received the first Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1901 for his work on solutions?','Jacobus Hendricus Van’t Hoff','Jacobus Hendricus Van’t Hoff (1852-1911) received the first Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1901 for his work on solutions.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 306 first Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','C. Le Bel','Jacobus Hendricus Van’t Hoff','William Nicol','Marie Curie','NEW','12-304','12-176',NULL),
(240746,'chemistry','Who described asymmetric carbon?','Both A and B','Dutch scientist, J. Van’t Hoff and French scientist, C. Le Bel in the same year (1874), independently argued that the spatial arrangement of four groups (valencies) around a central carbon is tetrahedral and if all the substituents attached to that carbon are different, the mirror image of the molecule is not superimposed (overlapped) on the molecule; such a carbon is called asymmetric carbon or stereocenter. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 306 first Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','C. Le Bel','Jacobus Hendricus Van’t Hoff','William Nicol','Both A and B','NEW','12-304','12-176',NULL),
(240825,'chemistry','Configuration of B in the reaction is: diagram(n2421)','Inverted','If (A) is the only compound obtained, the process is called retention of configuration. Note that configuration has been rotated in A. \n\nIf (B) is the only compound obtained, the process is called inversion of configuration. Configuration has been inverted in B. \n\nIf a 50:50 mixture of A and B is obtained then the process is called racemisation and the product is optically inactive, as one isomer will rotate the plane polarised light in the direction opposite to another.\n \nNCERT Figure (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 308.)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Rotated','Inverted','Racemised','Both A and B','NEW','12-306','12-178','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240825q.png'),
(240826,'chemistry','Assertion: In racemisation product obtained is optically active. \n\nReason: one isomer will rotate the plane polarised light in same direction to another.','Both assertion and reason are false.','If a 50:50 mixture of A and B is obtained then the process is called racemisation and the product is optically inactive, as one isomer will rotate the plane polarised light in the direction opposite to another. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 309 first Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-306','12-178',NULL),
(240827,'chemistry','In case of optically active alkyl halides, the product formed as a result of Sɴ2 mechanism has which type of configuration?','Inverted','In case of optically active alkyl halides, the product formed as a result of Sɴ2 mechanism has the inverted configuration as compared to the reactant. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 309 second Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Rotated','Inverted','Racemised','Both A and B','NEW','12-306','12-178',NULL),
(240828,'chemistry','Assertion: In case of optically active alkyl halides, the product formed as a result of Sɴ2 mechanism has the inverted configuration as compared to the reactant. \n\nReason: The nucleophile attaches itself on the side opposite to the one where the halogen atom is present.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','In case of optically active alkyl halides, the product formed as a result of Sɴ2 mechanism has the inverted configuration as compared to the reactant. This is because the nucleophile attaches itself on the side opposite to the one where the halogen atom is present. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 309 second Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-306','12-178',NULL),
(240829,'chemistry','What is the configuration of the product in the reaction is: diagram(n2422)','Inversion','When (–)-2-bromooctane is allowed to react with sodium hydroxide, (+)-octan-2-ol is formed with the –OH group occupying the position opposite to what bromide had occupied. \n\n(Reference:NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 309 Second Paragraph.)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Racemisation','Inversion','Retention','R configuration','NEW','12-306','12-178','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240829q.png'),
(240830,'chemistry','In case of optically active alkyl halides, Sɴ1 reactions are accompanied by which configuration?','Racemisation','In case of optically active alkyl halides, Sɴ1 reactions are accompanied by racemisation.  \n\n(Reference: Class 12 Chemistry NCERT, pg 309 third Paragraph. )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Racemisation','Inversion','Retention','S configuration','NEW','12-306','12-178',NULL),
(240831,'chemistry','What kind of mixture does the product of this reaction forms? diagram(n2423)','Racemisation','In case of optically active alkyl halides, Sɴ1 reactions are accompanied by racemisation. \n\n(Reference: Class 12 Chemistry NCERT, pg 309 third Paragraph. )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Racemisation','Inversion','Retention','E configuration','NEW','12-306','12-178','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240831q.png'),
(240832,'chemistry','Carbon on which halogen atom is directly attached is called','α-carbon','Carbon on which halogen atom is directly attached is called α-carbon.\n \nNCERT BOX ( Class 12 Chemistry NCERT, pg 309. )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','α-carbon','β-carbon','δ carbon','r carbon','NEW','12-307','12-179',NULL),
(240833,'chemistry','The carbon atom adjacent to α-carbon is called','β-carbon','The carbon atom adjacent to α-carbon is called β-carbon.\n \nNCERT Box( Class 12 Chemistry NCERT, pg 309. )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','α-carbon','β-carbon','δ carbon','r carbon','NEW','12-307','12-179',NULL),
(240834,'chemistry','There is elimination of hydrogen atom from β-carbon and a halogen atom from the α-carbon atom when halogen is heated with which reagent?','Alcoholic KOH','When a haloalkane with β-hydrogen atom is heated with alcoholic solution of potassium hydroxide, there is elimination of hydrogen atom from β-carbon and a halogen atom from the α-carbon atom.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 309 last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Aqueous KOH','Alcoholic KOH','Basic KOH','Acidic KOH','NEW','12-307','12-179',NULL),
(240835,'chemistry','When a haloalkane with β-hydrogen atom is heated with alcoholic solution of potassium hydroxide, there is elimination of hydrogen atom from β-carbon and a halogen atom from the α-carbon atom. This reaction is called','Elimination','When a haloalkane with β-hydrogen atom is heated with alcoholic solution of potassium hydroxide, there is elimination of hydrogen atom from β-carbon and a halogen atom from the α-carbon atom. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 309 last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Substitution','Elimination','Isomerisation','Inversion','NEW','12-307','12-179',NULL),
(240836,'chemistry','What kind of process does the given reaction represents: diagram(n2424)','Elimination','When a haloalkane with β-hydrogen atom is heated with alcoholic solution of potassium hydroxide, there is elimination of hydrogen atom from β-carbon and a halogen atom from the α-carbon atom.\n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 309 last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Substitution','Elimination','Isomerisation','Inversion','NEW','12-307','12-179','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240836q.png'),
(240837,'chemistry','Assertion: It is less likely for the electron rich nucleophile to approach electron rich arenes.\n \nReason: Possible repulsion happens.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Because of the possible repulsion, it is less likely for the electron rich nucleophile to approach electron rich arenes. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Line  Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 312 Paragraph 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-308','12-180',NULL),
(240838,'chemistry','Product formed in elimination reaction is','Alkene','When a haloalkane with β-hydrogen atom is heated with alcoholic solution of potassium hydroxide, there is elimination of hydrogen atom from β-carbon and a halogen atom from the α-carbon atom. \n\nAs a result, an alkene is formed as a product. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 309 last Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Alkene','Alcohol','Alkane','Alkyne','NEW','12-308','12-180',NULL),
(240839,'chemistry','If the reagent is NaI, and nucleophile is I⁻ then substitution product is','R-I','Nucleophile I⁻ will replace the leaving group in the reactant and give R-I\n\n(Reference: NCERT Table 10.4 Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 300 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','ROH','RNC','RCN','R-I','NEW','12-308','12-180',NULL),
(240840,'chemistry','Assertion: Elimination reactions are also called  β-elimination.\n \nReason: β-hydrogen atom is involved in elimination','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Since β-hydrogen atom is involved in elimination, it is often called β-elimination.\n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 310 first Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-308','12-180',NULL),
(240841,'chemistry','“In dehydrohalogenation reactions, the preferred product is that alkene which has the greater number of alkyl groups attached to the doubly bonded carbon atoms.” Which rule is this?','Saytzeff Rule','If there is possibility of formation of more than one alkene due to the availability of more than one β-hydrogen atoms, usually one alkene is formed as the major product. \n\nThese form part of a pattern first observed by Russian chemist, Alexander Zaitsev (also pronounced as Saytzeff) who in 1875 formulated a rule which can be summarised as “in dehydrohalogenation reactions, the preferred product is that alkene which has the greater number of alkyl groups attached to the doubly bonded carbon atoms.” \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 310 second Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Saytzeff Rule','N+1 Rule','Baldwin Rules for Ring Closure','Bredt\'s Rule','NEW','12-308','12-180',NULL),
(240842,'chemistry','If the reagent is AgCN, and Nucleophile is AgCN then substitution product is','RNC','An ambident nucleophile is one that can attack an electrophile made up of two or more atoms. \n\nThe cyanide anion is an example of one of these nucleophiles. \n\nWhen a cyanide anion is present, reactivity from carbon results in nitriles, whereas reaction from nitrogen results in isocyanides.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Table 10.4 Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 300 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','ROH','RNC','RCN','R-I','NEW','12-308','12-180',NULL),
(240843,'chemistry','“In dehydrohalogenation reactions, the preferred product is that alkene which has the greater number of alkyl groups attached to the doubly bonded carbon atoms.” Who stated this rule?','Alexander Zaitsev','If there is possibility of formation of more than one alkene due to the availability of more than one β-hydrogen atoms, usually one alkene is formed as the major product. \n\nThese form part of a pattern first observed by Russian chemist, Alexander Zaitsev (also pronounced as Saytzeff) who in 1875 formulated a rule which can be summarised as “in dehydrohalogenation reactions, the preferred product is that alkene which has the greater number of alkyl groups attached to the doubly bonded carbon atoms.”\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 310 second Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Jacobus Hendricus','Alexander Zaitsev','William Nicol','C. Le Bel','NEW','12-308','12-180',NULL),
(240844,'chemistry','What is the major product in the given? diagram(n2425)','Pent-2-ene','In dehydrohalogenation reactions, the preferred product is that alkene which has the greater number of alkyl groups attached to the doubly bonded carbon atoms.” \n\nThus, 2-bromopentane gives pent-2-ene as the major product. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 310 second Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Pent-1-ene','Pent-2-ene','Pentane','Pent-3-ene','NEW','12-308','12-180','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240844q.png'),
(240845,'chemistry','An alkyl halide with β-hydrogen atoms has competing routes which depends on\n \nA) nature of alkyl halide \n\nB) strength and size of base/nucleophile \n\nC) reaction conditions. \n\nSelect the correct statements.','All A,B,C','An alkyl halide with β-hydrogen atoms when reacted with a base or a nucleophile has two competing routes: substitution (Sɴ1 and Sɴ2) and elimination. \n\nWhich route will be taken up depends upon the nature of alkyl halide, strength and size of base/nucleophile and reaction conditions. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 310)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','A and B','A and C','Only B','All A,B,C','NEW','12-308','12-180',NULL),
(240846,'chemistry','A primary alkyl halide will prefer:','Sɴ2','A primary alkyl halide will prefer a Sɴ2 reaction, a secondary halide- Sɴ2 or elimination depending upon the strength of base/nucleophile and a tertiary halide- Sɴ1 or elimination depending upon the stability of carbocation or the more substituted alkene. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Box  NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 310 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Sɴ1','Sɴ2','Either Sɴ1 or Sɴ2 or elimination depending upon the strength of base/nucleophile.','E1','NEW','12-308','12-180',NULL),
(240938,'chemistry','A mixture of an alkyl halide and aryl halide gives an alkyl arene when treated with sodium in dry ether and is called','Wurtz-Fittig','A mixture of an alkyl halide and aryl halide gives an alkyl arene when treated with sodium in dry ether and is called Wurtz-Fittig reaction. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 316 first Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','Swartz Reaction','Wurtz reaction','Fitting reaction','Wurtz-Fittig','NEW','12-314','12-186',NULL),
(240939,'chemistry','Aryl halides give analogous compounds when treated with sodium in dry ether, in which two aryl groups are joined together. It is called','Fitting reaction','Aryl halides also give analogous compounds when treated with sodium in dry ether, in which two aryl groups are joined together. \n\nIt is called Fittig reaction. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 316 second Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','Swartz Reaction','Wurtz reaction','Fitting reaction','Wurtz-Fittig','NEW','12-314','12-186',NULL),
(240940,'chemistry','What kind of reaction does the following reaction depicts? diagram(n2440)','Wurtz-Fittig','A mixture of an alkyl halide and aryl halide gives an alkyl arene when treated with sodium in dry ether and is called Wurtz-Fittig reaction. \n\nNCERT Reaction(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 316 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Swartz Reaction','Wurtz reaction','Fitting reaction','Wurtz-Fittig','NEW','12-314','12-186','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240940q.png'),
(240941,'chemistry','Product in the given reaction will be: diagram(n2441)','Alkylarene','A mixture of an alkyl halide and aryl halide gives an alkyl arene when treated with sodium in dry ether and is called Wurtz-Fittig reaction. \n\nNCERT Reaction(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 316 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Alkylarene','Benzene','Diphenyl','Anthracene','NEW','12-314','12-186','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240941q.png'),
(240942,'chemistry','What kind of reaction does the given reaction depicts? diagram(n2442)','Fitting reaction','Aryl halides also give analogous compounds when treated with sodium in dry ether, in which two aryl groups are joined together. \n\nIt is called Fittig reaction.\n \nNCERT Reaction(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 316 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Swartz Reaction','Wurtz reaction','Fitting reaction','Wurtz-Fittig','NEW','12-314','12-186','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240942q.png'),
(240943,'chemistry','Product in the given reaction is diagram(n2443)','Diphenyl','Aryl halides also give analogous compounds when treated with sodium in dry ether, in which two aryl groups are joined together. \n\nIt is called Fittig reaction. \n\nNCERT Reaction(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 316 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Alkylarene','Benzene','Diphenyl','Naphthalene','NEW','12-314','12-186','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240943q.png'),
(240944,'chemistry','Identify A and B from the reaction. diagram(n2472)','Cyclohexylmagnesium bromide and cyclohexane','A mixture of an alkyl halide and aryl halide gives an alkyl arene when treated with sodium in dry ether and is called Wurtz-Fittig reaction. \n\n(Reference: Intext Question 10.9 Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 316)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Cyclohexylmagnesium bromide and cyclohexane','Magnesium bromide and benzene','Alkyl magnesium bromide and cyclohexane','Alkyl magnesium bromide and hexane','NEW','12-314','12-186','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240944q.png'),
(240945,'chemistry','Identify C and D from the reaction. diagram(n2473)','RMgBr and R','A mixture of an alkyl halide and aryl halide gives an alkyl arene when treated with sodium in dry ether and is called Wurtz-Fittig reaction. \n\nThe product C is a grignard reagent and the final product is propane. \n\n(Reference: Intext Question 10.9 Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 316)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','MgBr and benzene','RMgBr and R','R and RMgBr','KBr and R','NEW','12-314','12-186','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240945q.png'),
(240946,'chemistry','Assertion: Sɴ1 mechanism is ruled out inn phenyl cation. \n\nReason: In case of haloarenes, the phenyl cation formed as a result of self-ionisation will not be stabilised by resonance.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','In case of haloarenes, the phenyl cation formed as a result of self-ionisation will not be stabilised by resonance and therefore, Sɴ1 mechanism is ruled out. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Line Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 312 Paragraph 3 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-314','12-186',NULL),
(241021,'chemistry','Which compounds are usually produced for aerosol propellants, refrigeration and air conditioning purposes?','Freons','These are usually produced for aerosol propellants, refrigeration and air conditioning purposes. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 318 second Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Freons','Dichloromethane','Chloroform','Carbon tetrachloride','NEW','12-316','12-188',NULL),
(241022,'chemistry','In stratosphere, which compound is able to initiate radical chain reactions that can upset the natural ozone balance?','Freons','In stratosphere, freon is able to initiate radical chain reactions that can upset the natural ozone balance.\n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 318 second Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Freons','Dichloromethane','Chloroform','Carbon tetrachloride','NEW','12-316','12-188',NULL),
(241023,'chemistry','Which compound were the first chlorinated organic insecticides?','DDT','DDT, the first chlorinated organic insecticides, was originally prepared in 1873, but it was not until 1939 that Paul Muller of Geigy Pharmaceuticals in Switzerland discovered the effectiveness of DDT as an insecticide. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 318 third Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','DDT','Dichloromethane','Chloroform','Carbon tetrachloride','NEW','12-316','12-188',NULL),
(241024,'chemistry','The use of which compound increased enormously on a worldwide basis after World War II?','DDT','The use of DDT increased enormously on a worldwide basis after World War II, primarily because of its effectiveness against the mosquito that spreads malaria and lice that carry typhus. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 318 third Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','DDT','Dichloromethane','Chloroform','Carbon tetrachloride','NEW','12-316','12-188',NULL),
(241025,'chemistry','The use of which compound was banned in the United States in 1973?','DDT','The use of DDT was banned in the United States in 1973, although it is still in use in some other parts of the world. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 318 third Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','DDT','Dichloromethane','Chloroform','Carbon tetrachloride','NEW','12-316','12-188',NULL),
(241026,'chemistry','Which compound is not metabolised very rapidly by animals; instead, it is deposited and stored in the fatty tissues?','DDT','The chemical stability of DDT and its fat solubility compounded the problem. \n\nDDT is not metabolised very rapidly by animals; instead, it is deposited and stored in the fatty tissues. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 318 third Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','DDT','Dichloromethane','Chloroform','Carbon tetrachloride','NEW','12-316','12-188',NULL),
(241027,'chemistry','Name of this compound is: \nRefer diagram(n2445)','DDT','Name of this compound is DDT.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Diagram NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 318)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','DDT','Dichloromethane','Chloroform','Carbon tetrachloride','NEW','12-316','12-188','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241027q.png'),
(241028,'chemistry','Assertion: The use of DDT increased enormously on a worldwide basis after World War II. \n\nReason: Its effectiveness against the mosquito that spreads malaria and lice that carry typhus.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','The use of DDT increased enormously on a worldwide basis after World War II, primarily because of its effectiveness against the mosquito that spreads malaria and lice that carry typhus. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 318 third Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-316','12-188',NULL),
(241106,'chemistry','Chlorobenzene reacts with Mg in dry ether to give a  compound (A) which further reacts with ethanol to yield','Benzene','The first product is grignard reagent which then reacts with ethanol to give benzene.\n\n(NEET 1993)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Phenol','Benzene','Ethyl benzene','Phenyl ether','NEW','12-308','12-180',NULL),
(241107,'chemistry','Chirality of compound is  \nRefer diagram(n2471)','R','A carbon having 4 different subtitutent arranged such that lowest priority group is in the back and the rest are arranged such that they are in clockwise direct, the compound has R configuration.\n\n(NEET 2005)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','R','S','E','Z','NEW','12-304','12-176','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241107q.png'),
(241108,'chemistry','Which of the following acids does not exhibit optical isomerism?','Maelic Acid','In order for a compound to exhibit optical isomerism, all the 4 valencies should be filled. \n\nMaleic acid does not, therefore, show optical isomerism. \n\nOnly those compounds with chiral centres and/or a lack of symmetry components display optical isomerism. (Chiral carbon is a compound whose four valencies are met by four different groups.) \n\n(NEET 2012)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Maelic Acid','Alpha amino acid','Lactic acid','Tartaric acid','NEW','12-303','12-175',NULL),
(241109,'chemistry','Elimination reaction of 2-bromopentane to form  pent-2-ene is \n\n(A) Beta elimination reaction \n\n(B) Follows Zaitsev rule \n\n(C) Dehydrohalogenation reaction \n\n(D) Dehydration reaction','(A), (B), (C)','Elimination reaction of 2-bromopentane to form  pent-2-ene is beta elimination reaction. \n\nIt follows Zaitsev rule and the reaction is a dehydrohalogenation reaction.\n\n(NEET 2020)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(A), (B), (C)','(A), (B), (D)','(D), (B), (C)','(A), (D)','NEW','12-307','12-179',NULL),
(241110,'chemistry','The compound A on treatment with Na gives B, and  with PCl₅ gives C. B and C react together to give diethyl ether. \nA, B and C are in the order','C₂H₅OH, C₂H₅ONa, C₂H₅Cl','The compound A is ethanol which on treatment with sodium gives sodiumethoxide (compound B). \n\nThis compound on reacting with PCl₅ gives ethyl chloride (compound C).\n\n(NEET 2018)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','C₂H₅OH, C₂H₅ONa, C₂H₅Cl','C₂H₅OH, C₂H₆, C₂H₅Cl','C₂H₅Cl, C₂H₆, C₂H₅OH','C₂H₅OH, C₂H₅Cl, C₂H₅ONa','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241111,'chemistry','In an Sɴ1 reaction on chiral centres, there is','inversion more than retention leading to partial  racemisation','In Sɴ1 reaction, the reactant ionizes to give a carbocation. \n\nThis carbocation can be attacked from any side, however, the leaving group has not gone too far yet due to which it causes some hindrance and there is more inversion then racemic mixture. \n\n(NEET 2015)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','inversion more than retention leading to partial  racemisation','100% retention','100% racemisation','100% inversion','NEW','12-301','12-173',NULL),
(10901,'chemistry','What is the percentage of Carbon, Hydrogen and Oxygen in Ethanol?','52.14%, 13.13%, 34.73%','Molecular formula of ethanol is: C₂H₅OH \n \n Molar mass of ethanol is: (2×12.01 + 6×1.008 + 16.00) g = 46.068 g.\n \n Mass percent of carbon= 24.02g/46.068g×100 = 52.14%.\n \n Mass percent of hydrogen= 6.048g/46.068g×100 = 13.13%.\n \n Mass percent of oxygen= 16.00 g/46.068g×100 = 34.73%.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 19 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Percentage Composition',NULL,'2','mcq','52.14%, 13.13%, 34.73%','13.13%, 34.73%, 52.14%','13.13%, 52.14%, 34.73%','55.14%, 13.13%, 34.73%','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(241112,'chemistry','Consider the reaction,  CH₃CH₂CH₂Br + NaCN  —>CH₃CH₂CH₂CN + NaBr \nThis reaction will be the fastest in :','N , N\'-dimethylformamide (DMF)','The presence or absence of a solvent can either hinder or not hinder a nucleophile\'s approach to the carbon atom, affecting the pace of reaction. \n\nTetrahydrofuran, a polar aprotic solvent, is preferable to polar protic solvents for this reaction because the latter will create a hydrogen bond with the nucleophile, preventing it from attacking the carbon with the leaving group.\n\n(NEET 2016)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','ethanol','methanol','water','N , N\'-dimethylformamide (DMF)','NEW','12-303','12-175',NULL),
(241113,'chemistry','Two possible stereo-structures of CH₃CHOHCOOH, which are optically active, are called','enantiomers','Enantiomers are a pair of molecules that can only be found in their mirror-image forms and cannot be superimposed with one another. \n\nEnantiomers are chemically similar to one another in every other way.\n\n(NEET 2015)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','atropisomers','enantiomers','mesomers','Diastereomers','NEW','12-303','12-175',NULL),
(241114,'chemistry','Which of the biphenyl is optically active?\nRefer diagram(n2474)','D','The ortho-position of the benzene rings in biphenyl compounds that have a suitable substitution causing steric hindrance. \n\nThe biphenyl system becomes non-planar and consequently optically active molecules due to this steric hindrance.\n\n(NEET 2016)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','12-303','12-175','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241114q.png'),
(241115,'chemistry','The reaction of C₆H₅CH=CHCH₃ with HBr produces.','C','The reaction proceeds with the formation of a carbocation as shown: \n\n(NEET 2015 cancelled)\n\nRefer diagram(ne2406)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','12-300','12-172','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241115q.png'),
(241116,'chemistry','In the replacement reaction;\nThe reaction will be most favourable if M happens to be?\nRefer diagram(241116Q)','Rb','Tertiary halide shows Sɴ1 mechanism i.e., ionic mechanism. \n\nIn the given reaction negative ion will attack on carbocation.\n \nThus greater the tendency of ionisation (greater ionic character in M – F bond) more favourable will be reaction. \n\nThe most ionic bond is Rb – F in the given examples thus most favourable reaction will be with Rb–F.\n\n(Mains 2012)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Na','K','Rb','Li','NEW','12-301','12-173','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241116q.png'),
(241117,'chemistry','When chlorine is passed through propene at 400°C, which of the following is formed?','Allyl chloride','At 400°C temperature, substitution occurs instead of addition.\n \n(NEET 1993)\n\nRefer diagram(ne2407)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','PVC','Allyl chloride','Propyl chloride','1,2-dichloroethane','NEW','12-290','12-160',NULL),
(241118,'chemistry','The hydrolysis reaction that takes place at the slowest rate, among the following is: \nRefer diagram(241118Q)','a','Aryl halides are less reactive as compared to alkyl halides as the halogen atom in these compounds is firmly attached and cannot be replaced nucleophiles such as OH–, NH2 –, etc. \n\nIn chlorobenzene, the electron pair of chlorine atom is in conjugation with p-electrons of benzene ring. \n\nThus C Cl bond acquires double bond character and is difficult to break.\n(Odisha NEET 2019)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-309','12-181','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241118q.png'),
(241119,'chemistry','The compound C₇H₈ undergoes the following reactions;\nThe product C is?\nRefer diagram(n2480)','m-bromotoluene','The reaction takes place as follows. \n\n(NEET 2018)\n\nRefer diagram(ne2408)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','m-bromotoluene','o-bromotoluene','3-bromo-2,4,6-trichlorotoluene','p-bromotoluene','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241119q.png'),
(241120,'chemistry','Identify A and predict the type of reaction. \nRefer diagram(241120Q)','1','Refer to diagram(241120E)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW',NULL,'incomplete q','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241120q.png'),
(241121,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct? \n\nFor the following reactions: \n\nRefer diagram(n2482)','(A) is elimination, (B) is substitution and (C) is addition reaction','Saturated compound is converted into unsaturated compound by removal of group of atoms hence, it is an elimination reaction. \n\n—Br group is replaced by — OH group hence, it is a substitution reaction. \n\nAddition of bromine converts an unsaturated compound into a saturated compound hence, it is an addition reaction.\n \n(NEET-I 2016)\n\nRefer diagram(ne2410)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(A) is elimination, (B) and (C) are substitution reactions.','(A) is substitution, (B) and (C) are addition reactions.','(A) and (B) are elimination reactions and (C) is addition reaction.','(A) is elimination, (B) is substitution and (C) is addition reaction','NEW','12-298','12-170','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241121q.png'),
(241122,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds will undergo racemisation when solution of KOH hydrolyses? \nRefer diagram(n2483)','(iv) only','Due to chirality  in 4th compound, only compound (iv) will undergo racemisation. \n\n(NEET 2014)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) and (ii)','(ii) and (iv)','(iii) and (iv)','(iv) only','NEW','12-305','12-177','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241122q.png'),
(241123,'chemistry','Given I and II are: \nRefer diagram(n2484)','a pair of conformers','I and II are staggered and eclipsed conformers.\n\n(Karnataka NEET 2013)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','identical','a pair of conformers','a pair of geometrical isomers','a pair of optical isomers','NEW',NULL,'-','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241123q.png'),
(241124,'chemistry','Consider the reactions, the mechanisms of reactions (i) and (ii) are respectively: \nRefer diagram(n2485)','Sɴ2 and Sɴ2','If reaction is Sɴ1, there will be the formation of carbocation and the rearrangement takes place. \n\nIn these reactions there is no rearrangement hence both are Sɴ2 mechanism.\n\n(Mains 2011)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Sɴ1 and Sɴ2','Sɴ1 and Sɴ1','Sɴ2 and Sɴ2','Sɴ2 and Sɴ1','NEW','12-300','12-172','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241124q.png'),
(241125,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds undergoes nucleophilic substitution reaction most easily? \nRefer diagram(n2486)','a','Electron withdrawing groups like — NO₂ facilitates nucleophilic substitution reaction in chlorobenzene.\n\n(Mains 2011)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-301','12-173','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241125q.png'),
(10902,'chemistry','Which formula represents the simplest whole number ratio of various atoms present in a compound?','Empirical','An empirical formula represents the simplest whole number ratio of various atoms present in a compound.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 19 Paragraph-6)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Percentage Composition',NULL,'1','mcq','Molecular','Atomic','Simple','Empirical','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(241126,'chemistry','Which one is most reactive towards Sɴ1 reaction?','C₆H₅C(CH₃)(C₆H₅)Br','Sɴ1 reactions proceed via the formation of a carbocation intermediate. \n\nMore stable is the carbocation more reactive is the alkyl / aryl halide towards Sɴ1. \n\nIn C₆H₅C⁺(CH₃)(C₆H₅)Br carbocation, the two phenyl rings by their +R effect and —CH₃ by its +I effect diminish the positive charge and make it stable.\n\n(NEET 2010)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','C₆H₅CH(C₆H₅)Br','C₆H₅CH(CH₃)Br','C₆H₅C(CH₃)(C₆H₅)Br','C₆H₅CH₂Br','NEW','12-301','12-173',NULL),
(241127,'chemistry','The correct order of increasing reactivity of C—X bond towards nucleophile in the following compounds is: \nRefer diagram(n2487)','I < II < IV < III','The order of reactivity is dependent on the stability of the intermediate carbocation formed by cleavage of C—X bond. \n\nThe 3° carbocation (formed from III) will be more stable than its 2° counterpart (formed from IV) which in turn will be more stable than the arenium ion (formed from I). \n\nAlso, the aryl halide has a double bond character in the C—X bond which makes the cleavage more difficult. \n\nHowever, in spite of all the stated factors, II will be more reactive than I due to the presence of the electron withdrawing –NO2 group. C—X bond becomes weak and undergoes nucleophilic substitution reaction.\n\n(NEET 2010)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','I < II < IV < III','II < III < I < IV','IV < III < I < II','III < II < I < IV','NEW','13-302','12-164','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241127q.png'),
(241128,'chemistry','In the following reaction, the product ‘X’ is: \nRefer diagram(n2488)','C₆H₅CH₃','The reaction can take place as follows: \n\n(AIPMT 2010)\n\nRefer diagram(ne2411)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','C₆H₅CH₂OCH₂C₆H₅','C₆H₅CH₂OH','C₆H₅CH₃','C₆H₅CH₂CH₂C₆H₅','NEW','12-313','12-185','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241128q.png'),
(241129,'chemistry','Which of the following reactions is an example of nucleophilic substitution reaction? \nRefer diagram(n2489)','b','OH is a strong nucleophile and can easily replace the halide from alkyl halide. \n\nIt is the only substitution reaction.\n\n(NEET 2009)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','13-298','12-170','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241129q.png'),
(241130,'chemistry','How many stereoisomers does this molecule have? \n\nCH₃CH=CHCH₂CHBrCH₃','4','The given compound may be written as Both geometrical isomerism (cis-trans form) and optical isomerism is possible in the given compound.\n \nNo. of optical isomer = 2ⁿ = 2¹ = 2 (where n = no. of asymmetric carbon) Hence total\n\nno. of stereoisomers = 2 + 2 = 4\n\n(NEET 2008)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','8','2','4','6','NEW','12-305','12-177',NULL),
(241131,'chemistry','In a Sɴ2 substitution reaction of the type which one of the following has the highest relative rate? \nRefer diagram(n2490)','c','Sɴ2 mechanism is followed in case of primary and secondary alkyl halides i.e. \nSɴ2 reaction is favoured by small groups on the carbon atoms attached to halogen so, CH₃ – X > R – CH₂ – X > R₂CH – X > R₃C – X. \n\nPrimary is more reactive than secondary and tertiary alkyl halides.\n\n(NEET 2008)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-300','12-172','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241131q.png'),
(241132,'chemistry','If there is no rotation of plane polarised light by a compound in a specific solvent, though to be chiral, it may mean that:','the compound is certainly meso','Meso compound does not rotate plane polarised light. \n\nCompound which contains tetrahedral atoms with four different groups but the whole molecule is achiral, is known as meso compound. \n\nIt possesses a plane of symmetry and is optically inactive. \n\nOne of the asymmetric carbon atoms turns the plane of polarised light to the right and other to the left and to the same extent so that the rotation due to upper half is compensated by the lower half, i.e., internally compensated, and finally there is no rotation of plane polarised light.\n\n(NEET 2007)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','the compound is certainly meso','there is no compound in the solvent','the compound may be a racemic mixture','the compound is certainly a chiral','NEW','12-307','12-179',NULL),
(241133,'chemistry','CH₃–CHCl–CH₂–CH₃ has a chiral centre. Which one of the following represents its R-configuration? \nRefer diagram(n2491)','b','In the b option the compound has R configuration, which can be represented as:\n \n(NEET 2007)\n\nRefer diagram(ne2412)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-302','12-174','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241133q.png'),
(241134,'chemistry','Which of the following is not chiral?','3-bromopentane','Due to absence of asymmetric carbon atom there is no chirality in the structure.\n\n(NEET 2006)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','2-hydroxypropanoic acid','2-butanol','2,3-dibromopentane','3-bromopentane','NEW','12-306','12-178',NULL),
(241135,'chemistry','Which of the following undergoes nucleophilic substitution exclusively by Sɴ1 mechanism?','Benzyl chloride','Sɴ1 reaction is favoured by heavy (bulky) groups on the carbon atom attached to halogens and nature of carbonium ion in substrate is Benzyl > Allyl > Tertiary > Secondary > Primary > Methyl halides.\n\n(NEET 2005)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Ethyl chloride','Isopropyl chloride','Chlorobenzene','Benzyl chloride','NEW','12-303','12-175',NULL),
(241136,'chemistry','Which of the following is least reactive in a nucleophilic substitution reaction?','CH₂=CHCl','The non-reactivity of the chlorine atom in vinyl chloride can be explained from the molecular orbital point of view as follows. \n\nIf the chlorine atom has sp² hybridisation, the C – Cl bond will be a s-bond and the two lone pairs of electrons would occupy the other two sp² orbitals. \n\nThis would leave a p orbital containing a lone pair, and this orbital could now conjugate with the p-bond of the ethylenic link. \n\nThus two M.O’s will be required to accommodate these four p-electrons. \n\nFurthermore, since chlorine is more electronegative than carbon, the electrons will tend to be found in the vicinity of the chlorine atom. \n\nNevertheless, the chlorine atom has now lost full control of the lone pair and so, is less negative than it would have been had there been no conjugation. \n\nSince two carbon atoms have acquired a share in the lone pair, each carbon atom acquires a small negative charge. \n\nHence, owing to delocalisation of bonds (through conjugation), the vinyl chloride molecule has an increased stability. \n\nBefore the chlorine atom can be displaced by some other group, the lone pair must be localised again on the chlorine atom. \n\nThis requires energy, and so the chlorine is more firmly bound.\n\n(NEET 2004)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(CH₃)₃C–Cl','CH₂=CHCl','CH₃CH₂Cl','CH₂=CHCH₂Cl','NEW','12-304','12-176',NULL),
(241137,'chemistry','Which of the following pairs of compounds are enantiomers? \nRefer diagram(n2492)','a','These two are non-superimposable mirror images of each other, so they are enantiomers.\n(NEET 2003)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-307','12-177','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241137q.png'),
(241138,'chemistry','Reactivity order of halides for dehydrohalogenation is','R – I > R – Br > R – Cl > R – F','F, Cl, Br, I belong to the same group orderly. \n\nAtomic radii go on increasing as the nuclear charge increases in preceding downwards in a group. \n\nThe decreasing order of bond length C – I > C – Br > C – Cl > C – F. \n\nThe order of bond dissociation energy R – F > R – Cl > R – Br > R – I. \n\nDuring dehydrohalogenation C – I bond breaks more easily than C – F bond. \n\nSo reactivity order of halides is, R – I > R – Br > R – Cl > R – F.\n\n(NEET 2002)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','R – F > R – Cl > R – Br > R – I','R – I > R – Br > R – Cl > R – F','R – I > R – Cl > R – Br > R – F','R – F > R – I > R – Br > R – Cl','NEW','12-304','12-176',NULL),
(241139,'chemistry','Z in the above reaction sequence is: \nRefer diagram(n2493)','CH₃CH₂CH₂NHCOCH₃','The reaction takes place as follows: \n\n(NEET 2002)\n\nRefer diagram(241139E)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CH₃CH₂CH₂NHCOCH₃','CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂','CH₃CH₂CH₂CONHCH₃','CH₃CH₂CH₂CONHCOCH₃','NEW','-',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241139q.png'),
(241140,'chemistry','CH₃CH₂CH(Cl)CH₃ obtained by chlorination of n-butane will be:','racemic mixture','Chlorination of n-butane takes place via free radical formation. i.e\n \n(NEET 2001)\n\nRefer diagram(ne2413)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','meso form','racemic mixture','d-form','l-form','NEW','12-294','12-166',NULL),
(241141,'chemistry','An organic compound A(C₄H₉Cl) on reaction with Na/diethyl ether gives a hydrocarbon which on monochlorination gives only one chloro derivative then, A is','t-butyl chloride','Wurtz reaction: It involves the reaction of alkyl halides with Na in ether to form higher alkanes. \n\nIn the given problem, compound A is t-butyl chloride, in this compound all — CH groups have primary hydrogen only and able to give only, one chloro derivative.\n\n(NEET 2001)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','t-butyl chloride','s-butyl chloride','isobutyl chloride','n-butyl chloride','NEW','12-311','12-183',NULL),
(241142,'chemistry','A compound of molecular formula C₇H₁₆ shows optical isomerism, compound will be:','2,3-dimethylpentane','Organic compounds exhibit the property of enantiomerism (optical isomerism) only when their molecules are chiral. \n\nMost chiral compounds have a chiral centre, which is an atom bonded to four different atoms or groups. \n\n2,3-dimethylpentane has one chiral C-atom and does not have any symmetric element.\n\n(NEET 2001)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','2,3-dimethylpentane','2,2-dimethylbutane','2-methylhexane','none of these','NEW','12-307','12-179',NULL),
(241143,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds is not chiral?','DCH₂CH₂CH₂Cl','The compound c has no chiral ‘C’-atom. \n\nAll the ‘C’ atoms are attached to two identical ‘H’ atoms, so they are not asymmetrical.\n\n(NEET 1998)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CH₃CHDCH₂Cl','CH₃CH₂CHDCl','DCH₂CH₂CH₂Cl','CH₃CHClCH₂D','NEW','12-306','12-180',NULL),
(241144,'chemistry','Replacement of Cl of chlorobenzene to give phenol requires drastic conditions. \nBut chlorine of 2,4-dinitrochlorobenzene is readily replaced because:','NO₂ withdraws e– from ortho/para positions','Withdrawal of electrons by — NO₂ groups from ortho/ para positions cause easier removal of — Cl atom due to the development of positive charge on o- and p- positions.\n\n(NEET 1997)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','NO₂ donates e– at meta position','NO₂ withdraws e– from ortho/para positions','NO₂ makes ring electron rich at ortho and para','NO₂ withdraws e– from meta position','NEW','12-313','12-185',NULL),
(241145,'chemistry','The alkyl halide is converted into an alcohol by:','substitution','The alkyl halide is converted into an alcohol by substitution as there is only a replace of group.\n\n(NEET 1997)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','elimination','dehydrohalogenation','addition','substitution','NEW','12-300','12-172',NULL),
(241146,'chemistry','The following reaction is described as: \nRefer diagram(n2494)','Sɴ2','Sɴ2 reaction are bimolecular reactions where rate of reaction depends on the concentration of both substrate and nucleophile. \n\nWhen OH⁻ attacks the substrate from the opposite side of the leaving group i.e., \nBr⁻ a transition state results, to which both OH and Br are partially bonded to carbon atom.\n\n(NEET 1997)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Sɴ2','Sₙ0','E2','Sɴ1','NEW','12-301','12-173','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241146q.png'),
(241147,'chemistry','Reaction of t-butyl bromide with sodium methoxide produces:','isobutylene','Isobutylene is obtained. \n\nThus, the reaction produces isobutylene.\n \n(NEET 1994)\n\nRefer diagram(ne2414)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','sodium t-butoxide','t-butyl methyl ether','isobutane','isobutylene','NEW','-',NULL,NULL),
(241148,'chemistry','Which chloro derivative of benzene among the following would undergo hydrolysis most readily with aqueous sodium hydroxide to furnish the corresponding hydroxy derivative? \n\nRefer diagram(n2495)','a','Cl in 2, 4, 6-trinitrochlorobenzene is activated by three –NO₂ groups at o-and p-positions and hence undergoes hydrolysis most readily.\n\n(NEET 1989)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-312','12-184','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241148q.png'),
(241149,'chemistry','Trichloroacetaldehyde, CCl₃CHO reacts with chlorobenzene in presence of sulphuric acid and produces: \n\nRefer diagram(n2496)','c','It gives D.D.T. \n\n(NEET 2009)\n\nRefer diagram(ne2415)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-318','12-190','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241149q.png'),
(241150,'chemistry','The major product formed in dehydrohalogenation reaction of 2-bromopentane is pent-2-ene\nThis product formation is based on?','Saytzeff\'s Rule','The regioselectivity of the olefin (alkene), produced by the elimination process of 2 or 3 alkyl halides, is predicted by Saytzeff\'s rule. \n\nThe carbon atom with the fewest substituents loses a proton during the elimination reaction. \n\nThe Saytzeff\'s product is the name given to the matching olefin. \n\n(NEET 2021)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Saytzeff\'s Rule','Hund\'s Rule','Hoffmann Rule','Huckel\'s Rule','NEW','12-310','12-182',NULL),
(241151,'chemistry','The major product of the following chemical reaction is: \nRefer diagram(241151Q)','1','In presence of peroxide the addition of HBr to unsymmetrical alkene takes place by anti-Markovnikov\'s rule. \n\nThe hydrogen will go to less hydrogenated carbon atom and the bromine will add at the terminal carbon atom.\n\n(NEET 2021)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-295','12-167','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241151q.png'),
(241152,'chemistry','The correct sequence of bond enthalpy of \'C-X\' bond is','CH₃–F > CH₃–Cl > CH₃–Br > CH₃–I','Correct sequence of bond enthalpy of C–X bond is\n\nCH₃–F > CH₃–Cl > CH₃–Br > CH₃–I\n\n(NEET 2021)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CH₃–F < CH₃–Cl < CH₃–Br < CH₃–I','CH₃–F > CH₃–Cl > CH₃–Br > CH₃–I','CH₃–F < CH₃–Cl > CH₃–Br > CH₃–I','CH₃–Cl > CH₃–F > CH₃–Br > CH₃–I','NEW','12-293','12-164',NULL),
(241153,'chemistry','The reagent ‘R’ in the given sequence of chemical reaction is: refer diagram(n24104)','CH₃CH₂OH','Certain mild reducing agents like hypophosphorus acid or ethanol reduce diazonium salts to arene and themselves get oxidised to phosphorous acid and ethanal respectively. \n\n(NEET 2022)\n\nRefer diagram(ne2404)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','H₂O','CH₃CH₂OH','HI','CuCN/KCN','NEW','12-296','12-168','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241153q.png'),
(241154,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\n\nAssertion (A): ICI is more reactive than I₂\nReason (R): I-CI bond is weaker than I-I bond\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','In general, interhalogen compounds are more reactive than halogens (except fluorine). \n\nThis is because X – X\' bond in interhalogens is weaker than X – X bond in halogens excepts F – F bond. \n\nTherefore I –Cl is more reactive than I₂ because of weaker I – Cl bond then I – I bond.\n\n(NEET 2022)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct.','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241155,'chemistry','Which of the following sequence of reactions is suitable to synthesize chlorobenzene?','Benzene, Cl₂, anhydrous FeCl₃','Benzene reacts with chlorine in presence of anhydrous FeCl₃ to give chlorobenzene.\n \n(NEET 2022)\n\nRefer diagram(ne2405)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Benzene, Cl₂, anhydrous FeCl₃','Phenol, NaNO₂, HCl, CuCl','Benzene, HCl','Aniline, HCl, heating','NEW','12-314','12-186',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(241156,'chemistry','given compound is an example of : Refer diagram(241156Q)','allylic halide','Hint: Carbon adjacent to double-bond carbon is known as allylic carbon\n\nThe structure of the given compound is as follows:\n\n\n\nThe carbon adjacent to double-bond carbon is known as allylic carbon. X is attached to an allylic carbon. Thus, it is known as allylic halide. \n\nThus, option 4th is correct.','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','vicinal halides','benzylic halide','aryl halide','allylic halide','NEW','12-290','12-160','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241156q.png'),
(241157,'chemistry','for the following compounds what is the correct sequence of bond enthalpy of C-X bond:','CH3-F >   CH3-Cl >  CH3-Br >   CH3-I','Hint: As the size of halogen increases, C-X (X=halogen) bond energy decreases.\n\nThe value of bond enthalpy is as follows:\n\nC - F is 485 kJ/mol, C - Cl is 327 kJ/mol, C - Br is 185 kJ/mol, and C - I is 213 kJ/mol.\n\nThe increasing order of the size of halogen is F< Cl< Br<I\n\nAs the size of the halogen increases, the strength of the C-X bond decreases. \n\nHence, the correct answer is option 4th.','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','CH3-F<   CH3-Cl   >CH3-Br >  CH3-I','CH3-Cl > CH3-F > CH3-Br  >  CH3-I','CH3-F <  CH3-Cl <  CH3-Br <    CH3-I','CH3-F >   CH3-Cl >  CH3-Br >   CH3-I','NEW','12-293','12-164',NULL),
(241158,'chemistry','The product formed in the given reaction diagram(241158Q) is:','3','Hint: Alkyl bromide gives an elimination reaction in the presence of an ethanolic KOH.\n\nExplanation:  \n\nIn the first step, the free radical addition reaction takes place. The reaction follows the anti-Markovnikov rule. \nIn the second step, an elimination reaction occurs and an alkene is obtained as a product.','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-310','12-182','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241158q.png'),
(241159,'chemistry','incorrect statement regarding chirality is :','enantomers are superimposable mirror images on each other','Hint: Enantiomers are non-superimposable mirror images of each other\n\n1.  A mixture containing two enantiomers in equal proportions will have zero optical rotation, as the rotation due to one isomer will be canceled by the rotation due to the other isomer. Such a mixture is known as a racemic mixture or racemic modification.\n\nHence, A racemic mixture shows zero optical rotation it is a correct statement.\n\n2. SN1 reaction yields a 1:1 mixture of both enantiomers it is a correct statement. In SN1, sp2 hybridized carbocation is formed which is planar in nature hence, 1:1 mixture of both enantiomers is obtained as a product. \n\n\n\n3. The product obtained by SN2 reaction of haloalkane having chirality at the reactive site shows the inversion of configuration. It is a correct statement. In SN2, nucleophile attacks from the backside hence, inversion of the configuration takes place.\n\n4. Enantiomers are superimposable mirror images of each other is a wrong statement because enantiomers are non-superimposable mirror images of each other.\n\nHence, option 4th is the correct answer.','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a racemic mixture shows zero optical rotation','SN1 mixture yields 1:1 mixture of both enantomers','the product obtained by SN1 reaction of halokane having chirality at the reaction site shows the inversion of configuration','enantomers are superimposable mirror images on each other','NEW','12-306','12-178',NULL),
(241160,'chemistry','which of the following reacton cannot form new carbon-carbon bonds?','Cannizaro reaction','Hint: In one of the reaction both alcohol and carboxylate ion are obtained as product.\n\nCannizzaro reaction is a disproportionation reaction, so there is no new bond formation between C-C .\n\n1. Reimer-Tiemann reaction,\n\n\n\n(Here, a new C-C bond is formed.)\n\n2. Cannizaro reaction, \n\n2\nHCHO\n \nConc\n.\n−−→\n \nNaOH\n \nCH\n3\nOH\n \n+\n \nHCOONa\n(No new C-C bond is formed in this reaction.)\n\n3. Wurtz reaction,\n\n2\nRX\n \n+\n \nDry\n \nNa\n \nEther\n−−→\n \nR\n−\nR\n(One new C-C bond is formed.)\n\n4. Friedel-Crafts acylation,\n\n\n\n(New C-C bond is formed.)\n\nThus, among the given reactions, only the Cannizaro reaction does not involve the formation of a new C-C bond.','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Reimer-Tiemann reaction','Cannizaro reaction','Wurtz Reaction','Friedel-Craft Acylation','NEW','-',NULL,NULL),
(10903,'chemistry','Which formula shows the exact number of different types of atoms present in a molecule of a compound?','Molecular','The molecular formula shows the exact number of different types of atoms present in a molecule of a compound.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 19 Paragraph-6)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Percentage Composition',NULL,'1','mcq','Empirical','Molecular','Atomic','Compound','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(10904,'chemistry','Find empirical formula of the compound if M =68% (atomic mass = 34) and remaining 32 % oxygen.','MO','Thus, option (a) is correct.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 19 Paragraph-7) diagram(ne0119)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Percentage Composition',NULL,'2','mcq','MO','MO₂','M₂O','M₂O₃','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(241161,'chemistry','select the correct option : ASSERTION (A) chlorine is an electron withdrawing group but it is orhto para directing in electrophiliic aromatic substitution.  REASON (R) : inductive effect of chlorine destabiizes the intermediate carbocation formed during electrophillic substitution ,however due to more pronunced resonance effect , the halogen stabilizes the carbocation at ortho and para position.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Hint: Cl shows both inductive as well +R effect\n\nHalogen attached with benzene ring show both -I and +M effect. Due to -I effect halogen atom slightly deactivates but halogen is o/p directing because of +M effect.  \n\nInductive effect of chlorine destabilises the intermediate carbocation formed during the electrophilic substitution, however, due to the more pronounced resonance effect, thus, halogen is ortho-para directing. \n\nHence, option one is the correct answer.','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a incorrect explanation of the  assertion.','A is true but R is false','A is fallse but R is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241162,'chemistry','the incorrect method for synthesis of alkenes is :','treatin alkyl halides with aquesous KOH solution','Hint: Alkyl halide gives a substitution reaction with aqu. KOH\n\nWhen alkyne reacts with Na in liquid NH3 then reduction takes place and trans alkene is obtained as a product. The reaction is as follows:\n\n\n\nWhen alkyl halide reacts with alcoholic KOH then an elimination reaction occurs and an alkene is obtained as a product. The reactions are as follows:\n\n\n\nDihalides in which two halogen atoms are attached to two adjacent carbon atoms are known as vicinal dihalides. Vicinal dihalides on treatment with zinc metal lose a molecule of ZnX2 to form an alkene. This reaction is known as dehalogenation. The reaction is as follows:\n\nCH2BrCH2Br  + Zn  →  CH2=CH2 + ZnBr2 \n\nWhen alkyl halides react with an aqueous KOH solution then a substitution reaction takes place and alcohol is obtained as a product. \n\nCH3CH2Br  + aqu. KOH  →  CH3CH2OH + KBr','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','treatment of alkynes with Na in liquid NH3','heating alkyl halides with alcoholic KOH solution','treatin alkyl halides with aquesous KOH solution','treating vincinal dihalides with zn metal','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241163,'chemistry','In the following sequence of reactions, the end product (C) is \ndiagram(241163Q)','Ethyl Alcohol','Hint: Product B is an acid. In the first step, a methyl bromide reacts with KCN and a nucleophilic substitution reaction takes place. In this reaction, Br is substituted by the cyanide group. In the second step, ethane nitrile undergoes hydrolysis reaction and CN group is converted into COOH group. In the final step, a reduction reaction takes place. Ethanoic acid is reduced to ethanol','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Acetone','Methane','Acetaldehyde','Ethyl Alcohol','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241163q.png'),
(241164,'chemistry','The correct reaction among the following in the diagram(241164Q):','2','Hint: Follow Wurtz-Fittig reaction in Haloalkane And Haloarene in 12th NCERT','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-316','12-188','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241164q.png'),
(241224,'chemistry','What is order of reactivity of four isomeric bromobutanes in Sɴ1 reaction?','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br < (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br < CH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃ < (CH₃)₃CBr','Of the two primary bromides, the carbocation intermediate derived from (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br is more stable than derived from CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br because of greater electron donating inductive effect of (CH₃)₂CH- group.\n \nTherefore, (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br is more reactive than CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br in Sɴ1 reactions. \n\nCH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃ is a secondary bromide and (CH₃)₃CBr is a tertiary bromide. Hence the above order is followed in Sɴ1. \n\nThe reactivity in Sɴ2 reactions follows the reverse order as the steric hindrance around the electrophilic carbon increases in that order.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 305 Example 10.7 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br > CH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃ > (CH₃)₃CBr > CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br < (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br < CH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃ < (CH₃)₃CBr','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br > (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br > CH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃ > (CH₃)₃CBr','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br > (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br < CH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃ > (CH₃)₃CBr','NEW','12-305','12-175',NULL),
(241225,'chemistry','What is order of reactivity of four given isomeric compounds in Sɴ1 reaction?','C₆H₅C(CH₃)(C₆H₅)Br > C₆H₅CH(C₆H₅)Br > C₆H₅CH(CH₃)Br > C₆H₅CH₂Br','Of the two secondary bromides, the carbocation intermediate obtained from C₆H₅CH(C₆H₅)Br is more stable than obtained from C₆H₅CH(CH₃)Br because it is stabilised by two phenyl groups due to resonance. \n\nTherefore, the former bromide is more reactive than the latter in Sɴ1 reactions. \nA phenyl group is bulkier than a methyl group. \n\nTherefore, C₆H₅CH(C₆H₅)Br is less reactive than C₆H₅CH(CH₃)Br in Sɴ2 reactions.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 305 Example 10.7)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','C₆H₅C(CH₃)(C₆H₅)Br > C₆H₅CH(C₆H₅)Br > C₆H₅CH(CH₃)Br > C₆H₅CH₂Br','C₆H₅C(CH₃)(C₆H₅)Br < C₆H₅CH(C₆H₅)Br > C₆H₅CH(CH₃)Br > C₆H₅CH₂Br','C₆H₅C(CH₃)(C₆H₅)Br > C₆H₅CH(C₆H₅)Br < C₆H₅CH(CH₃)Br > C₆H₅CH₂Br','C₆H₅C(CH₃)(C₆H₅)Br < C₆H₅CH(C₆H₅)Br < C₆H₅CH(CH₃)Br < C₆H₅CH₂Br','NEW','12-305','12-175',NULL),
(241226,'chemistry','What is order of reactivity of four given isomeric compounds in Sɴ2 reaction?','C₆H₅C(CH₃)(C₆H₅)Br < C₆H₅CH(C₆H₅)Br < C₆H₅CH(CH₃)Br < C₆H₅CH₂Br','Of the two secondary bromides, the carbocation intermediate obtained from C₆H₅CH(C₆H₅)Br is more stable than obtained from C₆H₅CH(CH₃)Br because it is stabilised by two phenyl groups due to resonance. \n\nTherefore, the former bromide is more reactive than the latter in Sɴ1 reactions. \n\nA phenyl group is bulkier than a methyl group. \n\nTherefore, C₆H₅CH(C₆H₅)Br is less reactive than C₆H₅CH(CH₃)Br in Sɴ2 reactions.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 305 Example 10.7)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','C₆H₅C(CH₃)(C₆H₅)Br > C₆H₅CH(C₆H₅)Br > C₆H₅CH(CH₃)Br > C₆H₅CH₂Br','C₆H₅C(CH₃)(C₆H₅)Br < C₆H₅CH(C₆H₅)Br > C₆H₅CH(CH₃)Br > C₆H₅CH₂Br','C₆H₅C(CH₃)(C₆H₅)Br > C₆H₅CH(C₆H₅)Br < C₆H₅CH(CH₃)Br > C₆H₅CH₂Br','C₆H₅C(CH₃)(C₆H₅)Br < C₆H₅CH(C₆H₅)Br < C₆H₅CH(CH₃)Br < C₆H₅CH₂Br','NEW','12-305','12-175',NULL),
(241227,'chemistry','Which of the following is the observation of Louis Pasteur laid the foundation of modern stereochemistry?','Crystals of certain compounds exist in the form of mirror images.','The observation of Louis Pasteur (1848) that crystals of certain compounds exist in the form of mirror images laid the foundation of modern stereochemistry.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 305 Last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Chiral carbons have different groups attached to it.','Crystals of certain compounds exist in the form of mirror images.','Chiral carbons have symmetry.','Enantiomers are imposable over other.','NEW','12-305','12-175',NULL),
(241228,'chemistry','Which of the following statement/s are/is correct?\n\nI) The sign of optical rotation is not necessarily related to the absolute (actual) configuration of the molecule.\n\nII) Chiral carbons have different groups attached to it.\n\nIII) Crystals of certain compounds exist in the form of mirror images.\n\nIV) Enantiomers are imposable over other.','I), II), III)','Crystals of certain compounds exist in the form of mirror images. \n\nChiral carbons have different groups attached to it. \n\nThe sign of optical rotation is not necessarily related to the absolute (actual) configuration of the molecule. \n\nThe observation of Louis Pasteur (1848) that crystals of certain compounds exist in the form of mirror images laid the foundation of modern stereochemistry.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 305 Last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','I), II),','I), II), III)','II), III)','Only III)','NEW','12-305','12-175',NULL),
(241229,'chemistry','How many routes can a b-hydrogen atom follow when reacted with a base?','2','A chemical reaction is the result of competition; it is a race that is won by the fastest runner. \n\nA collection of molecules tend to do, by and large, what is easiest for them. \n\nAn alkyl halide with b-hydrogen atoms when reacted with a base or a nucleophile has two competing routes: substitution and elimination.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 310 Paragraph 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2','4','3','5','NEW','12-310','12-180',NULL),
(241230,'chemistry','How many resonance structure are of haloarene?','4','In haloarenes, the electron pairs on halogen atom are in conjugation with p-electrons of the ring and the following resonating structures are possible. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 311 Paragraph 5)\n\nRefer diagram(ne2416)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2','4','3','5','NEW','12-311','12-181',NULL),
(241231,'chemistry','What increases the reactivity of haloarenes?','An electron withdrawing group at ortho- and para-positions.','The presence of an electron withdrawing group (-NO₂) at ortho- and para-positions increases the reactivity of haloarenes.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 312 Paragraph 6)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','An electron withdrawing group at ortho- and para-positions.','An electron donating group at ortho- and para-positions.','An electron withdrawing group at ortho- position.','An electron withdrawing group at meta position.','NEW','12-312','12-182',NULL),
(241232,'chemistry','Which of the following is example of electrophilic reaction?','all of above','Haloarenes undergo the usual electrophilic reactions of the benzene ring such as halogenation, nitration, sulphonation and Friedel-Crafts reactions.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 314 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','halogenation','nitration','sulphonation','all of above','NEW','12-314','12-184',NULL),
(241233,'chemistry','The o,p- directing halogens gives rise to _________resonating structures.','4','The o, p-directing influence of halogen atom can be easily understood if we consider the resonating structures of halobenzene as shown: \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 300 Paragraph 2)\n\nRefer diagram(ne2417)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2','4','3','5','NEW','12-300','12-170',NULL),
(241234,'chemistry','What kind of reaction condition is required for electrophilic substitution reaction?','drastic','The electrophilic substitution reactions in haloarenes occur slowly and require more drastic conditions as compared to those in benzene.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 300 Paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','mild','high temperature','low temperature','drastic','NEW','12-300','12-170',NULL),
(241235,'chemistry','Select the statement/s that are/is correct?\n\nI) The halogen are o,p-directors.\n\nII) Halogens have -I effect on the benzene ring.\n\nIII) Halogens deactivate the ring by withdrawing the electrons from the it.\n\nIV) Electrophilic reactions in haloarenes are slow and require drastic conditions..','I), II), III), IV)','Due to resonance, the electron density increases more at ortho- and para-positions than at meta-positions. \n\nFurther, the halogen atom because of its –I effect has some tendency to withdraw electrons from the benzene ring. \n\nAs a result, the ring gets somewhat deactivated as compared to benzene and hence the electrophilic substitution reactions in haloarenes occur slowly and require more drastic conditions as compared to those in benzene.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 314 Paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','I), II),','I), II), III), IV)','II), III)','Only III)','NEW','12-314','12-184',NULL),
(241236,'chemistry','Given below are two statements.\n\nReason: The electrophilic substitution reactions in haloarenes occur slowly and require more drastic conditions.\n\nAssertion: The halogen atom because of its –I effect has some tendency to withdraw electrons from the benzene ring. \n\nAs a result, the ring gets somewhat deactivated as compared to benzene and hence the electrophilic substitution reactions in haloarenes occur slowly and require more drastic conditions.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Due to resonance, the electron density increases more at ortho- and para-positions than at meta-positions. \n\nFurther, the halogen atom because of its –I effect has some tendency to withdraw electrons from the benzene ring. \n\nAs a result, the ring gets somewhat deactivated as compared to benzene and hence the electrophilic substitution reactions in haloarenes occur slowly and require more drastic conditions as compared to those in benzene.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 314 Paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-314','12-184',NULL),
(241237,'chemistry','Which of the following is the major product of the given reaction? \n\nRefer diagram(n2498)','c','The reaction produces two products, i.e, 1,4-dichlorobenzene and 1,2-dichlorobenzene. \n\nThe former is the major product.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 314 Paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-314','12-184','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241237q.png'),
(241238,'chemistry','Which of the following is the minor product of the given reaction? \n\nRefer diagram(n2499)','b','The reaction produces two products, i.e, 1-chloro-2-nitrobenzene and 1-chloro-4-nitro-benzene. The former is the minor product.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 314 Paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','DELETED','12-314','12-184','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241238q.png'),
(10905,'chemistry','Determine the empirical formula of an oxide of iron, which has 69.9% iron and 30.1% dioxygen by mass.','Fe₂O₃','Thus, option (d) is correct.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page-25 Problem-1.3) diagram(ne0107)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Percentage Composition',NULL,'2','mcq','FeO','FeO₂','Fe₂O','Fe₂O₃','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(241239,'chemistry','Which of the following is the major product of the given reaction? \n\nRefer diagram(n24100)','c','The reaction produces two products, i.e, 4-chlorobenzenesulfonic acid and 2-chlorobenzenesulfonic acid. \n\nThe former is the major product.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 314 Paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-314','12-184','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241239q.png'),
(241240,'chemistry','Which of the following is the minor product of the given reaction? \n\nRefer diagram(n24101)','b','Due to resonance, the electron density increases more at ortho- and para-positions than at meta-positions. Further, the halogen atom because of its –I effect has some tendency to withdraw electrons from the benzene ring. As a result, the ring gets somewhat deactivated as compared to benzene and hence the electrophilic substitution reactions in haloarenes occur slowly and require more drastic conditions as compared to those in benzene.','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','DELETED','12-314','12-184','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241240q.png'),
(241241,'chemistry','Which of the following is the major product of the given reaction? \n\nRefer diagram(n2498)','c','The reaction produces two products, i.e, 4-chloroacetophenone and 2-chloroacetophenone. \n\nThe former is the major product.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 314 Paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-314','12-184','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241241q.png'),
(241242,'chemistry','Which of the following are the uses of chloroform?','All of above','Chemically, chloroform is employed as a solvent for fats, alkaloids, iodine and other substances. \n\nThe major use of chloroform today is in the production of the freon refrigerant R-22. \n\nIt was once used as a general anaesthetic in surgery but has been replaced by less toxic, safer anaesthetics, such as ether.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317 Paragraph 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Acts as solvent for fats, alkaloids and iodine','In the production of the freon refrigerant','Anaesthetic in surgery','All of above','NEW','12-317','12-187',NULL),
(241243,'chemistry','How much inhaled amount of chloroform can cause dizziness?','900 parts per million','Breathing about 900 parts of chloroform per million parts of air (900 parts per million) for a short time can cause dizziness, fatigue, and headache.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317 Paragraph 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','700 parts per million','800 parts per million','900 parts per million','600 parts per million','NEW','12-317','12-187',NULL),
(241244,'chemistry','Which of the following are the uses of carbon tetrachloride?','All of above','Until the mid 1960s, it was also widely used as a cleaning fluid, both in industry, as a degreasing agent, and in the home, as a spot remover and as fire extinguisher.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317 Paragraph 5)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cleaning fluid','Degreasing agent','Spot remover','All of above','NEW','12-317','12-187',NULL),
(241245,'chemistry','Select the correct statements.\n\nI) Human exposure to ultraviolet rays leading to increased skin cancer.\n\nII) Exposure to CCl₄ can make the heart beat irregularly or stop.\n\nIII) Carbon tetrachloride under extreme conditions can lead to death.\n\nIII) Carbon tetrachloride under mild conditions can lead to coma.','I), II), III)','In severe cases, these effects can lead rapidly to stupor, coma, unconsciousness or death. Exposure to CCl₄ can make the heart beat irregularly or stop. \n\nDepletion of the ozone layer is believed to increase human exposure to ultraviolet rays, leading to increased skin cancer, eye diseases and disorders, and possible disruption of the immune system.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317 Paragraph 5)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','I), II),','I), II), III)','II), III)','III), IV)','NEW','12-317','12-187',NULL),
(241246,'chemistry','Who discovered the effectiveness of DDT as an insecticide?','Paul Muller','Paul Muller of Geigy Pharmaceuticals in Switzerland discovered the effectiveness of DDT as an insecticide. \n\nPaul Muller was awarded the Nobel Prize in Medicine and Physiology in 1948 for this discovery.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 318 Paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Victor Grignard','Jacobus Hendricus','William Nicole','Paul Muller','NEW','12-318','12-188',NULL),
(241247,'chemistry','Which of the following is the structure of DDT? Refer diagram(n24103)','c','DDT is a chlorophenylethane that has additional chloro substituents at positions 4 of the phenyl substituents, making it 1,1,1-trichloro-2,2-diphenylethane. \n\nThis organochlorine pesticide is frequently used.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 318 Paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-318','12-188','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241247q.png'),
(241248,'chemistry','Which alcohol shows the highest reactivity with halogen acids:','tertiary alcohols','This reaction follows Sₙ1 mechanisms, so carbocations are formed as intermediate product.\nSo tertiary carbocation is more stable than secondary and primary carbocation.\n\n" Tertiary alkyl halides undergo Sɴ1 reaction very fast because of the high stability of tert -carbocations. \nFurther, greater the stability of carbocation, greater will be its ease of formation from alkyl halide and faster will be the rate of reaction. In case of alkyl halides,  alkyl halides undergo SN1 reaction very fast because of the high stability of carbocations.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 304 Paragraph 1 )"\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Exemplar Class 12 )"','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','primary alcohols','secondary alcohols','tertiary alcohols','alcohols do not react with halogen acids','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241249,'chemistry','Which of the following alcohols will yield the corresponding alkyl chloride on reaction with concentrated HCl at room temperature? Refer diagram (241249Q)','d only','This reaction follows Sₙ1 mechanisms, so carbocations are formed as intermediate product.\nSo tertiary carbocation is more stable than secondary and primary carbocation.\n\n"The reactions of primary and secondary alcohols with HCl require the presence of a catalyst, ZnCl₂.With tertiary alcohols, the reaction is conducted by simply shaking the alcohol with concentrated HCl at room temperature.  \n(Reference  : NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 294; Paragraph No.1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','d only','NEW','12-294','12-164','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241249q.png'),
(241250,'chemistry','Identify the compound Y in the following reaction.','chlorobenzene','When a primary aromatic amine, dissolved or suspended in cold aqueous mineral acid, is treated with sodium nitrite, a diazonium salt is formed. Mixing the solution of freshly prepared diazonium salt with cuprous chloride or cuprous bromide results in the replacement of the diazonium group by –Cl or –Br.\n(Reference :NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 296; Paragraph 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','chlorobenzene','1,4 dichlorobenzene','benzene','1,3 dichlorobenzene','NEW','12-296','12-164',NULL),
(241251,'chemistry','Toluene reacts with halogen in the presence of iron chloride giving ortho and para halo compounds. The reaction is','electrophillic substitution reaction','In this reaction, Cl is generated as electrophile which attacks benzene ring. So it is electrophillic substitution reaction.\nThe mechanism is given in the diagram(ne2418)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','nucleophillic substitution reaction','nucleophillic addition reaction','electrophillic substitution reaction','free radical mechanism','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241252,'chemistry','Which of the following reaction in given diagram(241252Q) is a halogen exchange reaction?','a only','When one halogen is replaced by other halogen, the reaction is termed as halogen exchange reaction. An example of such reaction is Finkelstein reaction.\n\n"Alkyl iodides are often prepared by the reaction of alkyl chlorides/ bromides with NaI in dry acetone. This reaction is known as Finkelstein reaction.  \n(Reference  : NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 295; Paragraph No.5)"\n\nIn option d. halogen replaces the hydrogen of benzene ring , so it is not a halogen exchange reaction.','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','a,d','c only','a only','NEW','12-295','12-163','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241252q.png'),
(241253,'chemistry','Which reagent is used for the following reaction ?\nCH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃⟶CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Cl + CH₃CH₂CH₂CHClCH₃','Chlorine gas in UV light','In this reaction, free chlorine radical is produced in the presence of UV light, which initiates the furthur reaction.\n\n"Free radical chlorination or bromination of alkanes gives a complex mixture of isomeric mono- and poly haloalkanes  \n(Reference  : NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 294; Paragraph No.4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Chlorine gas in dark','Chlorine gas in UV light','NaCl in presence of suphuric acid','All of the above','NEW','12-294','12-162',NULL),
(241254,'chemistry','Which of the following structres is enantiomeric with the molecule (A) given in the diagram(n24105)','i only','The stereoisomers related to each other as non- superimposable mirror images are called enantiomers.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 307 second Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','I,ii','i only','ii,iii only','all of the above','NEW','12-307','12-177','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241254q.png'),
(241255,'chemistry','Which of the following is an example of vic dihalide:\na. 3,4-dichlorobutane\nb. Ethylidene chloride\nc. Allyl chloride\nd. 1,2- dichloroethane','a,d','vic-dihalides contain two halogen atoms present on the two adjacent carbon atoms.','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','a only','a,b,c','a,d','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241256,'chemistry','Assertion:Chlorine containing antibiotic, chloramphenicol, is very effective for the treatment of typhoid fever.\n\nReason: Our body produces iodine containing hormone, thyroxine, the deficiency of which causes a disease called goiter.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Chlorine containing antibiotic, chloramphenicol, produced by microorganisms is very effective for the treatment of typhoid fever. \n\nChlorine containing antibiotic, chloramphenicol, produced by microorganisms is very effective for the treatment of typhoid fever.  \n"Our body produces iodine containing hormone, thyroxine, the deficiency of which causes a disease called goiter. \n\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 289 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-289','12-159',NULL),
(241257,'chemistry','Assertion:The reaction of alcohols with sodium or potassium iodide in 95% orthophosphoric acid gives good yields of R-I.\n\nReason:The carbon-oxygen bond in phenols has a partial double bond character and is difficult to break being stronger than a single bond.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Good yields of R—I may be obtained by heating alcohols with sodium or potassium iodide in 95% orthophosphoric acid.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 294; Paragraph No.1)"\n"It is due to the presence of the nature of carbocation present in its structure. \n\nGreater the stability of carbocation bonded with -OH, greater will be its ease of formation from alkyl halide and faster will be the rate of reaction. \n\nThe order of reactivity of alcohols with a given haloacid is acid is 3 >2>1   \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 294; Paragraph No.1)"','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-294','12-164',NULL),
(241258,'chemistry','The position of Br in the compound in CH₃==CHC(Br)(CH₃)₂','allyl','Allyl halides contain halogen bonded to sp³ Carbon next to double bond while aryl halides have halogen bonded to aromatic ring.\n\n"These are the compounds in which the halogen atom is bonded to an sp³ -hybridised carbon atom adjacent to carbon-carbon double bond (C=C) i.e. to an allylic carbon.  \n(Reference  :NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 290; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','allyl','aryl','tertiary','secondary','NEW','12-290','12-160',NULL),
(241259,'chemistry','Chlorobenzene is formed by reaction of chlorine with benzene in the presenc of AlCl₃. Which of the following species attacks the benzene ring in this reaction?','Cl⁺','AlCl₃ being electron deficient forms [AlCl₄]⁻ during the cleavage of chlorine molecule. Thus only Cl⁺ attacks the benzene ring in the reaction.\nHaloarenes undergo the usual electrophilic reactions of the benzene ring such as halogenation, nitration, sulphonation and Friedel-Crafts reactions.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 314 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Cl⁻','Cl⁺','AlCl₃','[AlCl₄]⁻','NEW','12-314','12-184',NULL),
(241336,'chemistry','What will be the major products when compound ‘A’ with molecular formula C₇H₈ is treated with Cl₂ in the presence of FeCl₃.','p-chlorotoluene','Reaction proceeds as given in the diagram(ne2420)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','o-chlorotoluene','2-bromo-2-methylheptane','Chlorooctane','p-chlorotoluene','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241337,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds will have the highest mielting point in the given diagram(n24121)','II','Since compound II has most symmetry , so it will have the maximum boiling point.','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','I','II','III','both I and II have the same melting point','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241337q.png'),
(241338,'chemistry','Give the IUPAC name of neo-pentylbromide','I-bromo -2,2-dimethylpropane','The structure of the compound is given in the diagram(ne2430)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','2-bromo -2,2-dimethylpropane','I-bromo -2,2-dimethylpropane','I-bromo -2,2-dimethylbutane','I-bromo-dimethylpropane','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241339,'chemistry','Which of them will yield 1-methylcyclohexane by its reaction with HCl?\n\na. Methylene cyclohexane\n\nb. 1-Methyl cyclohexene\n\nc. Methyl cyclohexane','a,b','Both   Methylene cyclohexane and 1-Methyl cyclohexene will yield  1-methylcyclohexane by its reaction with HCl as per the given diagram(ne2431)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','a only','b,c','a,c','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241340,'chemistry','Which of them is a test to detect the presence of double bond in a molecule:\n\na. Bayer\'s test\n\nb. Br2 in CCl4 test\n\nc. Fehling reaction','a,b','Bayer\'s test:\nOn adding alk. KMnO4 to unsaturated solution, the pink color disappears.\n\nBr2 in CCl4 test:\n"In the laboratory, addition of bromine in CCl₄ to an alkene resulting in discharge of reddish brown colour of bromine constitutes an important method for the detection of double bond in a molecule. \n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 295; Paragraph No.4)"','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','b only','NEW','12-295','12-165',NULL),
(241341,'chemistry','Diphenyls are potential threat to the environment. How are these produced from aryl halides?\n\na. Incomplete combustion of mineral oil\n\nb. Exhaust air from vehicles\n\nc. Fittig Reaction','a,b,c','Aryl halides also give analogous compounds when treated with sodium in dry ether, in which two aryl groups are joined together. \n\nIt is called Fittig reaction. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 316 second Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','c only','b,c','a,b,c','NEW','12-316','12-186',NULL),
(241342,'chemistry','IUPAC name of the DDT is','2,2-bis (4-chlorophenyl)-1,1,1-trichloroethane','DDT, the first chlorinated organic insecticides, was originally prepared in 1873, but it was not until 1939 that Paul Muller of Geigy Pharmaceuticals in Switzerland discovered the effectiveness of DDT as an insecticide. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 318 third Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1,2,3,4,5,6-hexachlorocyclohexane','2,2- (4-chlorophenyl)-1,1,1-trichloroethane','2,2-bis (4-chlorophenyl)-1,1,1-trichloroethane','2,2-bis (4-chlorophenyl)-1,1,1-trichloromethane','NEW','12-318','12-188',NULL),
(241343,'chemistry','Major product formed when HCl is added to isobutylene is','2-chloro-2-methylpropane','The reaction follows Markinikov rule as given the diagram(n24122)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','2-chloro-1-methylpropane','propane','2-chloro-2-methylpropane','both b and c','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241344,'chemistry','From which end Cyanide ion acts as a stronger nucleophile in aqueous medium?','C end','Groups like cyanides and nitrites possess two nucleophilic centres and are called ambident nucleophiles. \n\nSince, C---C is stronger than C---N bond, so cyanide ion attacks mostly through Carbon to form R-CN.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 300 last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','C end','N end','NH₂ end','Cyanides possesses only N as nucleophilic centre','NEW','12-300','12-170',NULL),
(241345,'chemistry','Match the compounds given in List-I with List-II and select the suitable option using the codes given below.\ndiagram(n2491)','(A)-(3), (B)-(4), (C)-(1), (D)-(2)','Chlorine containing antibiotic, chloramphenicol, produced by microorganisms is very effective for the treatment of typhoid fever. \n\n"Our body produces iodine containing hormone, thyroxine, the deficiency of which causes a disease called goiter. \n\n"Synthetic halogen compounds, viz. chloroquine is used for the treatment of malaria.\n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 289 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(A)-(3), (B)-(4), (C)-(1), (D)-(2)','(A)-(3), (B)-(4), (C)-(2), (D)-(1)','(A)-(4), (B)-(3), (C)-(2), (D)-(1)','(A)-(4), (B)-(3), (C)-(1), (D)-(2)','NEW','12-289','12-159','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241345q.png'),
(241346,'chemistry','Assertion (A):  Phosphorus chlorides are preferred over thionyl chloride for the preparation of alkyl chlorides from alcohols\n\nReason (R): Phosphorus chlorids give pure alkyl halides\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are incorrect.','The hydroxyl group of an alcohol is replaced by halogen on reaction with concentrated halogen acids, phosphorus halides or thionyl chloride  \n\n"Thionyl chloride is best reagent as the byproduct formed ie. SO₂ and HCl in this reaction are escapable gases\n  \n\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 294; Paragraph No.1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is incorrect explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is incorrect','Both (A) and (R) are incorrect.','NEW','12-294','12-164',NULL),
(241347,'chemistry','Assertion (A):  The boiling points of alkyl halides decrease in the order\nRI > RBr > RCl > RF\n\nReason (R): The boiling points of alkyl chlorides, bromides and iodides are considerably higher than that of the hydrocarbon of comparable molecular mass.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','The presence of an electron withdrawing group (-NO₂) at ortho- and para-positions increases the reactivity of haloarenes towards nucleophillic substitution reaction\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 312 Paragraph 6)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is incorrect','Both (A) and (R) are incorrect.','NEW','12-312','12-182',NULL),
(241348,'chemistry','Assertion (A):  Presence of a nitro group at ortho or para position increases the reactivity of haloarenes towards nucleophilic substitution.\n\nReason (R): Nitro group, being an electron withdrawing group decreases the electron density over the benzene ring\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Branched compound have low boiling point.\n\n"As the number of carbons in an alkane increases, so do its boiling points. This is due to the fact that the intermolecular attractive forces, while individually small, become cumulatively stronger as the molecule\'s atom and electron counts rise. Branched alkanes often have lower boiling points than unbranched alkanes with the same carbon concentration. The stronger van der Waals interactions between the molecules of the unbranched alkanes lead this to happen."\n(Reference:NCERT Figure 10.1 NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 298 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is incorrect','Both (A) and (R) are incorrect.','NEW','12-298','12-168',NULL),
(241349,'chemistry','Assertion (A):  In monohaloarenes, further electrophilic substitution occurs at ortho and para positions\n\nReason (R): Halogen atom is a ring deactivator.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','In this reaction, Cl is generated as electrophile which attacks benzene ring. So it is electrophillic substitution reaction(ESR).\n\nHalogen in ESR increases electron density at o and p position and increases the reactivity.','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is incorrect','Both (A) and (R) are incorrect.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241350,'chemistry','Assertion (A):  Aryl iodides can be prepared by reaction of arenes with\niodine in the presence of an oxidising agent.\n\nReason (R): Oxidising agent oxidises Iodine into HI.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(A) is correct but (R) is incorrect','Since H₂SO₄  is an oxidizing agent, it oxidizes HI (produced in the reaction to I₂). \n\nHence, the reaction between alcohol and HI to produce alkyl iodide cannot occur. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 297;intext Question)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is incorrect','Both (A) and (R) are incorrect.','NEW','12-297','12-167',NULL),
(241351,'chemistry','Assertion (A):  It is difficult to replace chlorine by -OH in chlorobenzene in comparsion to that in chloroethane\n\nReason (R): Chlorine carbon (C--Cl) bond in chlorobenzene has a partial double bond character due to resonance\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','C—Cl bond acquires a partial double bond character due to resonance. \n\nAs a result, the bond cleavage in haloarene is difficult than haloalkane and therefore, they are less reactive towards nucleophilic substitution reaction. \n\n( Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 311 last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is incorrect','Both (A) and (R) are incorrect.','NEW','12-311','12-181',NULL),
(241352,'chemistry','Assertion (A):   Nitro group is meta- directing group.\n\nReason (R):Nitration of chlorobenzene leads to the formation of m-nitrochlorobenzene.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(A) is correct but (R) is incorrect','The nitro because of its –I effect has some tendency to withdraw electrons from the benzene ring and leads to formation of m-nitrochlorobenzene on chlorination.\n\nAs a result, the ring gets somewhat deactivated as compared to benzene and hence the electrophilic substitution reactions in haloarenes occur slowly and require more drastic conditions as compared to those in benzene.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 314 Paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is incorrect','Both (A) and (R) are incorrect.','NEW','12-314','12-184',NULL),
(241353,'chemistry','Match the compounds given in List-I with List-II and select the suitable option using the codes given below.\ndiagram(n24124)','(A)-(2), (B)-(1), (C)-(4), (D)-(3)','A mixture of an alkyl halide and aryl halide gives an alkyl arene when treated with sodium in dry ether and is called Wurtz-Fittig reaction. \n\nAryl halides also give analogous compounds when treated with sodium in dry ether, in which two aryl groups are joined together. \nIt is called Fittig reaction. \n\n\n"Alkyl iodides are often prepared by the reaction of alkyl chlorides/ bromides with NaI in dry acetone. \n\nThis reaction is known as Finkelstein reaction.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 316 first Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(A)-(3), (B)-(4), (C)-(1), (D)-(2)','(A)-(3), (B)-(4), (C)-(2), (D)-(1)','(A)-(4), (B)-(3), (C)-(2), (D)-(1)','(A)-(2), (B)-(1), (C)-(4), (D)-(3)','NEW','12-316','12-186','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241353q.png'),
(241505,'chemistry','Identify the compound A and B from the given reaction :\n\nROH+ SOCl₂----> R-Cl + (A) + (B)','SO₂+HCl','The hydroxyl group of an alcohol is replaced by halogen on reaction with concentrated halogen acids, phosphorus halides or thionyl chloride  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 294; Paragraph No.1)\ndiagram(ne2443)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','POCl₃+HCl','PCl₃+H₂0','SO₂+HCl','HCl only','NEW','12-294','12-164',NULL),
(241707,'chemistry','The structure of the major product formed in the following reaction diagram(n24123) is','b','Since I is attached to benzene ring directly, so it attains partial double bond character.\n\nHowever, Cl can be be substituted easily with CN ion.','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241707q.png'),
(241708,'chemistry','Which of the following is the correct order of decreasing Sn2 reactivity?','RCH₂X>RCHX>R₃CX','For Sn2 mechanism: Primary halides have the highest reactivity followed by secondary halides and tertiary halides.\n(Reference: NCERT Class 12; Page no. 304)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','R₂CHX>R₃CX>RCH₂X','RCH₂X>R₃CX>RCHX','RCH₂X>RCHX>R₃CX','R₃CX>R₃CX>RCH₂X','NEW','12-304','12-171',NULL),
(241709,'chemistry','The correct order of Sₙ1 reactivity in the given diagram(n24124) is','B>C>A','"This reaction follows Sₙ1 mechanisms, so carbocations are formed as intermediate product.\nSince tertiary carbocation is more stable than secondary and primary carbocation , therefore tertiary alcohol will have the highest reactivity to halogen acids.\n\nCompound B has highest stability as its double bond is in resonance with the the carbocation formed.\n\n"" Tertiary alkyl halides undergo Sɴ1 reaction very fast because of the high stability of tert -carbocations. \nFurther, greater the stability of carbocation, greater will be its ease of formation from alkyl halide and faster will be the rate of reaction. In case of alkyl halides,  alkyl halides undergo SN1 reaction very fast because of the high stability of carbocations.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 304 Paragraph 1 )""\n\n"','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','B>C>A','B>A>C','C>B>A','A>B>C','NEW','12-304','12-173','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241709q.png'),
(241710,'chemistry','How many chiral compounds are possible on monochlorination of 2-methyl butane?','2','2 chiral compounds can be formed as given in the diagram(ne2433)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','8','2','4','6','NEW','12-308','12-177',NULL),
(241711,'chemistry','Compound A, C₈H₉Br, gives a yellow precipitate when warned with alcoholic AgNO₃. Oxidation of (A) gives an acid (B), C₈H₆O₄. (B) easily forms anhydride on heating. Identify the compound (A) from the given diagram(n24125)','d','The reaction will proceed as given in the diagram(ne2434)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241711q.png'),
(241712,'chemistry','The major organic compound formed by the reaction of 1,1,1 trichloroethane with silver powder is:','2-Butyne','The reaction will proceed as given in the diagram(ne2435)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Acetylene','Ethene','2-Butyne','2-Butene','NEW','12-316','12-188',NULL),
(241713,'chemistry','The synthesis of alkyl flourides is best accomplished by:','Swarts reaction','Swarts reaction:\nThe synthesis of alkyl fluorides is best accomplished by heating an alkyl chloride/bromide in the presence of a metallic fluoride such as AgF, Hg₂F₂ , CoF₂  or SbF₃\n   \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 296; Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Finkelstein reaction','Swarts reaction','Free radical flourination','Sandmeyer\'s reaction','NEW','12-296','12-166',NULL),
(241714,'chemistry','2-Chloro-2-methypentane on reaction with sodium methoxide in methanol yields majorly : diagram(n24126)','iii only','The reaction will proceed as given in the diagram(ne2436)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','iii only','i and ii','All of the above','i and iii','NEW',NULL,'-','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241714q.png'),
(241715,'chemistry','The increasing order of the reactivity of the follwoing halides for the SN1 reaction is:\n\nI. CH₃CH(Cl)CH₂CH₃\n\nII. CH₃CH₂CH₂Cl\n\nIII. p-H₃CO-C₆H₄-CH₂Cl','II<I<III','"Reaction proceeds through SN1 mechanism"" and a carbocation will be formed as an intermediate in the reaction.\n\nTertiary alkyl halides undergo Sɴ1 reaction very fast because of the high stability of tert -carbocations. Further, greater the stability of carbocation, greater will be its ease of formation from alkyl halide and faster will be the rate of reaction. \n\nOption III has highest stability due to +M effect of -OCH₃ group.\n\nIn case of alkyl halides, alkyl halides undergo SN1 reaction very fast because of the high stability of carbocations. (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 304 Paragraph 1 )"','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','III<II<I','II<I<III','I<III<II','II<III<I','NEW','12-304','12-173',NULL),
(241716,'chemistry','Which of the following in the given diagram(n24127), upon treatment with tert-BuONa followed by addition of bromine water, fails to decolourize the color of bromine?','a','Only unsaturated compound show decolourisation as given in the diagram(ne2437)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW',NULL,'-','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241716q.png'),
(241717,'chemistry','The major product of the following reaction diagram(n24150) is','b','The reaction proceeds via E2 mechanism and dehydrohalogenation occurs and given in diagram(ne2438).','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-311','12-180','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241717q.png'),
(290211,'chemistry','Which of the following form the basis for the classification of polymers?\n \n a. source from which polymer is derived \n \n b. structure of polymer\n \n c. molecular forces \n \n d. mode of polymersiation\n \n e. Number of molecules in its structure\n \n f. Number of bonds in its structure','All except e and f','Polymers can also be classified on the basis of their structure, molecular forces or modes of polymerisation.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 433 Paragraph-4)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Classification',NULL,'2','mcq','All except e and f','All except c and d','All except a and b','All of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290332,'chemistry','Which of them is false for LDP ?','hard and rigid','Low density polythene is chemically inert and tough but flexible and a poor conductor of electricity.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 435 Paragraph-5)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','chemically inert','tough','hard and rigid','poor conductor of electricity','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290333,'chemistry','Choose the correct for the following \n \n Assertion (A) Low density polythene is used in the insulation of electricity carrying wires.\n \n Reason (R) LDP is chemically inert.','Both A and R are correct; R is not the correct explanation of A','Low density polythene is chemically inert and tough but flexible and a poor conductor of electricity.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 435 Paragraph-5)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are correct; R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are correct; R is not the correct explanation of A','A is correct; R is incorrect.','A is incorrect; R is correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290334,'chemistry','Which among the following is not a property of Low density polythene?','Rigidity','Low density polythene is chemically inert and tough but flexible and a poor conductor of electricity.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 435 Paragraph-5)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Elasticity','Tensile Strength','Inert','Rigidity','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290335,'chemistry','Polymer used in the insulation of electricity carrying wires and manufacture of squeeze bottles, toys and flexible pipes','Low density polythene','LDP is used in the insulation of electricity carrying wires and manufacture of squeeze bottles, toys and flexible pipes.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 436 Paragraph-1)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Low density polythene','High density polythene','teflon','melamine','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290336,'chemistry','High Density Polythene is formed in the presence of catalyst','Ziegler-Natta','It is formed when addition polymerisation of ethene takes place in a hydrocarbon solvent in the presence of a catalyst such as triethylaluminium and titanium tetrachloride (Ziegler-Natta catalyst) at a temperature of 333 K to 343 K and under a pressure of 6-7 atmospheres.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 436 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','peroxide','Ziegler-Natta','H₂/Ni','Br₂/Ni','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290337,'chemistry','Which of the following is used as the catalyst in the manufacture of polythene by Ziegler-Natta method?','Titanium tetrachloride and triethylaluminium','It is formed when addition polymerisation of ethene takes place in a hydrocarbon solvent in the presence of a catalyst such as triethylaluminium and titanium tetrachloride (Ziegler-Natta catalyst) at a temperature of 333 K to 343 K and under a pressure of 6-7 atmospheres.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 436 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Titanium dioxide','Titanium tetrachloride and triphenylaluminium','Titanium isoperoxide','Titanium tetrachloride and triethylaluminium','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290338,'chemistry','Which type of polymer High Density Polythene is?','linear polymers','High density polythene (HDP) thus produced, consists of linear molecules and has a high density due to close packing.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 436 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','network polymers','branched polymers','linear polymers','cross-linked polymers','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290339,'chemistry','Identify the polymer whose general structure is given in diagram(n2909)','High density polythene','High density polythene (HDP) thus produced, consists of linear molecules and has a high density due to close packing.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 436 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','rubber','Low density polythene','High density polythene','melamine','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/290339q.png'),
