(290382,'chemistry','Which one of the following polymers is prepared by condensation polymerisation?','Nylon-6,6','Nylon 6,6: \n \n It is prepared by the condensation polymerisation of hexamethylenediamine with adipic acid under high pressure and at high temperature.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 437 Paragraph-5)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','teflon','styrene','Natural rubber','Nylon-6,6','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290383,'chemistry','Which one of the following polymers is prepared by addition polymerisation?','teflon','Except teflon all are condensation polymer.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 434 Paragraph-4)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','teflon','styrene','bakelite','Nylon-6,6','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290384,'chemistry','Name the polymer formed in the given diagram(n2920)','Buna-S','a mixture of buta–1, 3–diene and styrene can form a copolymer.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 440 Paragraph-1)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','nylon-6','Buna-S','Buna-N','Nylon-6,6','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/290384q.png'),
(290385,'chemistry','Which polymer is used as substitute for natural rubber?','Buna-S','Butadiene - styrene copolymer is quite tough and is a good substitute for natural rubber. \n \n It is used for the manufacture of autotyres, floortiles, footwear components, cable insulation, etc.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 440 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','isoprene','Buna-S','Buna-N','neoprene','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290386,'chemistry','Buna-S is not used for making of ?','glass reinforcing material','butadiene - styrene copolymer is quite tough and is a good substitute for natural rubber. \n \n It is used for the manufacture of autotyres, floortiles, footwear components, cable insulation, etc.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 440 Paragraph-2)\n \n Dacron fibre (terylene) is crease resistant and is used in blending with cotton and wool fibres and also as glass reinforcing materials in safety helmets, etc.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 438 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','autotyres','floortiles','cable insulation','glass reinforcing material','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290387,'chemistry','Stretching in polymer is attributed to','intermolecular forces','In elastomeric polymers, the polymer chains are held together by the weak intermolecular forces. \n \n These weak binding forces permit the polymer to be stretched.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 440 Paragraph-3)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','crosslinks','intermolecular forces','both','none of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290388,'chemistry','Elastomers retract to its original position due to','crosslinks','A few ‘crosslinks’ are introduced in between the chains, which help the polymer to retract to its original position after the force is released.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 440 Paragraph-3)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','H bonding','intermolecular forces','crosslinks','All of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290389,'chemistry','Monomer of natural rubber is','isoprene','Natural rubber may be considered as a linear polymer of isoprene (2-methyl-1, 3-butadiene) and is also called as cis - 1, 4 - polyisoprene.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 440 Paragraph-4)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','isoprene','1,3-butadiene','styrene','neoprene','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290390,'chemistry','Identify the polymer shown in the diagram(n2921)','natural rubber','Natural rubber may be considered as a linear polymer of isoprene (2-methyl-1, 3-butadiene) and is also called as cis - 1, 4 - polyisoprene.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 440 Paragraph-4)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','neoprene','Gutta- Percha','polypropylene','natural rubber','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/290390q.png'),
(290391,'chemistry','How does the presence of double bonds in rubber molecules influence their structure and reactivity?','The double bonds do not allow the polymer chain to come closer','Natural rubber is cis-poly isoprene. It is obtained by polymerisation of isoprene units at 1,4 positions. \n \n In rubber molecule, double bonds are located between C2 and C3 of each isoprene unit. \n \n These cis-double bonds do not allow the polymer chain to come closer.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 440 Paragraph-3)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','The double bonds do not allow the polymer chain to come closer','It is responsible for the strong force present within the polymer chain','Double bond decreases the elasticity of polymer','It is responsible for the strong force present within the polymer chain','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290392,'chemistry','Natural rubber becomes soft and brittle respectively at which temperature?','>335K, <283K','Vulcanisation of rubber: \n \n Natural rubber becomes soft at high temperature (>335 K) and brittle at low temperatures (<283 K) and shows high water absorption capacity. \n \n It is soluble in nonpolar solvents and is non-resistant to attack by oxidising agents. \n \n To improve upon these physical properties, a process of vulcanisation is carried out.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 440 Paragraph-6)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','>253K, <273K','<253K, >273K','>335K, <283K','>300K, <283K','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290393,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is not incorrect?','Natural rubber has high water absorption capacity','Vulcanisation of rubber: \n \n Natural rubber becomes soft at high temperature (>335 K) and brittle at low temperatures (<283 K) and shows high water absorption capacity. \n \n It is soluble in non polar solvents and is non-resistant to attack by oxidising agents. \n \n To improve upon these physical properties, a process of vulcanisation is carried out.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 440 Paragraph-6)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Natural rubber becomes hard at low temperature of <283 K','Natural rubber is resistant to attack by oxidising agents','Natural rubber has high water absorption capacity','Vulcanisation is done to improve the reactivity of natural rubber','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290394,'chemistry','The cis-polyisoprene molecular chains are held together by?','Weak van der walls forces','The cis-polyisoprene molecule consists of various chains held together by weak van der Waals interactions and has a coiled structure. \n \n Thus it can be stretched like a spring and exhibits elastic properties.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 440 Paragraph-5)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Hydrogen bonding','Covalent bonds','Ionic bonds','Weak van der walls forces','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290395,'chemistry','During the process of vulcanisation, raw rubber is heated with','sulphur','Vulcanisation consists of heating a mixture of raw rubber with sulphur and an appropriate additive.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 440 Paragraph-6)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','oxygen','sulphur','phosphorus','hydrogen','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290396,'chemistry','The purpose of vulcanisation is to make natural rubber','Stiff','On vulcanisation, sulphur forms cross links at the reactive sites of double bonds and thus the rubber gets stiffened.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 441 Paragraph-1)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Stiff','Loose','Reactive','All of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290397,'chemistry','In the manufacture of tyre rubber, what % of sulphur is used as a crosslinking agent','5','In the manufacture of tyre rubber, 5% of sulphur is used as a crosslinking agent.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 441 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','2','5','7','1','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290398,'chemistry','Guess the structure shown in diagram(n2922)','vulcanised rubber','After vulcanisation sulphur forms cross links in natural rubber.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 441 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Buna-N','neoprene','natural rubber','vulcanised rubber','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/290398q.png'),
(290399,'chemistry','Synthetic rubber is capable of getting stretched to','Two times its length','Synthetic rubber is any vulcanisable rubber like polymer, which is capable of getting stretched to twice its length. \n \n However, it returns to its original shape and size as soon as the external stretching force is released.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 441 Paragraph-3)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Four times its length','Two times its length','Eight times its length','Originial length','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290417,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement for HDP ?','Temperature used is 333K to 343K','High density polythene: \n \n It is formed when addition polymerisation of ethene takes place in a hydrocarbon solvent in the presence of a catalyst such as triethylaluminium and titanium tetrachloride (Ziegler-Natta catalyst) at a temperature of 333 K to 343 K and under a pressure of 6-7 atmospheres.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 436 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions-2',NULL,'2','mcq','Catalyst used is peroxide or traces of oxygen','Polymerisation of ethene occurs in hydrocarbon solvent only','Temperature used is 333K to 343K','Pressure used is 7-9 atm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290418,'chemistry','The solvent used during HDP polymerisation is?','Hydrocarbon solvent','High density polythene: \n \n It is formed when addition polymerisation of ethene takes place in a hydrocarbon solvent in the presence of a catalyst such as triethylaluminium and titanium tetrachloride (Ziegler-Natta catalyst) at a temperature of 333 K to 343 K and under a pressure of 6-7 atmospheres.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 436 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions-2',NULL,'1','mcq','Aqueous solvent','Hydrocarbon solvent','Ionic solvent','Fused solid','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290419,'chemistry','State True/False\n \n a)HDP is chemically inert\n \n b)HDP is also called linear polymer.','T,T','High density polythene (HDP) are chemically inert and consists of linear molecules and has a high density due to close packing.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 436 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions-2',NULL,'2','mcq','T,T','T,F','F,T','F,F','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290420,'chemistry','State True/False\n \n a)HDP is not tough and hard.\n \n b)HDP is for manufacturing buckets, dustbins, bottles, pipes, etc.','F,T','High density polymers are also chemically inert and more tough and hard. \n \n It is used for manufacturing buckets, dustbins, bottles, pipes, etc.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 436 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions-2',NULL,'2','mcq','T,T','T,F','F,T','F,F','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290421,'chemistry','Teflon is','Polytetrafluoroethene','Polytetrafluoroethene (Teflon) \n \n Teflon is manufactured by heating tetrafluoroethene with a free radical or persulphate catalyst at high pressures.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 436 Paragraph-3)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions-2',NULL,'1','mcq','Polytrifluoroethene','Polytetrafluoroethene','Polyacrylonitrile','Polyamides','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290422,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is not true for Teflon?','Manufactured by heating tetraflouroethane at high pressure','Teflon is manufactured by heating tetrafluoroethene with a free radical or persulphate catalyst at high pressures.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 436 Paragraph-3)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions-2',NULL,'2','mcq','Persulphate catalyst is used during polymerisation','Used in making gaskets','Manufactured by heating tetraflouroethane at high pressure','None of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290423,'chemistry','State True/False\n \n a)Teflon is chemically inert.\n \n b)Teflon is not resistant to attack by corrosive reagents.','T,F','It is chemically inert and resistant to attack by corrosive reagents. \n \n It is used in making oil seals and gaskets and also used for non – stick surface coated utensils.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 436 Paragraph-3)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions-2',NULL,'2','mcq','T,T','T,F','F,T','F,F','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290424,'chemistry','State True/False\n \n a) Teflon is used in making oil seals and gaskets.\n \n b)Teflon is used for non – stick surface coated utensils.','T,T','It is chemically inert and resistant to attack by corrosive reagents. \n \n It is used in making oil seals and gaskets and also used for non – stick surface coated utensils.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 436 Paragraph-3)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions-2',NULL,'2','mcq','T,T','T,F','F,T','F,F','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290425,'chemistry','State True/False\n \n a)Polyacrylonitrile is used as a substitute for wool\n \n b)PAN is also called as dacron.','T,F','Polyacrylonitrile is used as a substitute for wool in making commercial fibres as orlon or acrilan.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 436 Paragraph-5)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions-2',NULL,'2','mcq','T,T','T,F','F,T','F,F','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290426,'chemistry','Which of them is not true for Polyacrylonitile?','condensation polymer','The addition polymerisation of acrylonitrile(homopolymer) in presence of a peroxide catalyst leads to the formation of polyacrylonitrile.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 436 Paragraph-4)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions-2',NULL,'1','mcq','homopolymer','addition polymer','condensation polymer','chain growth polymer','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290427,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is incorrect for Condensation Polymerisation?','Every step is dependent on the previous step','In condensation polymerisation, the product of each step is again a bi-functional species and the sequence of condensation goes on. \n \n Since, each step produces a distinct functionalised species and is independent of each other, this process is also called as step growth polymerisation.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 437 Paragraph-2)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions-2',NULL,'2','mcq','Also called Step growth polymerisation','Each step produces distinct functionalised species','Every step is dependent on the previous step','Their is loss of simple molecules such as water, alcohol, hydrogen chloride, etc.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290428,'chemistry','Which is not true for polymers possessing amide linkages ?','polyesters','These polymers possessing amide linkages(polyamides) are important examples of synthetic fibres and are termed as nylons.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 437 Paragraph-4)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions-2',NULL,'2','mcq','Nylon','Polyamide','polyesters','Are synthetic fibres','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290429,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a method of producing polyamides?','polymerisation of amino acids with their lactams','The general method of preparation consists of the condensation polymerisation of diamines with dicarboxylic acids or condensation of amino acids or their lactams.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 437 Paragraph-4)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions-2',NULL,'2','mcq','polymerisation of diamines with dicarboxylic acids','condensation of amino acids','condensation of their lactams','polymerisation of amino acids with their lactams','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290430,'chemistry','Nylon 6,6 is produced at','high pressure and at high temperature','Nylon 6,6: \n \n It is prepared by the condensation polymerisation of hexamethylenediamine with adipic acid under high pressure and at high temperature.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 437 Paragraph-5)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions-2',NULL,'2','mcq','high pressure and at low temperature','low pressure and at high temperature','high pressure and at high temperature','low pressure and at low temperature','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290431,'chemistry','State true/false\n \n a)Nylon 6, 6 is fibre forming solid.\n \n b)It possess high tensile strength','T,T','Nylon 6, 6 is fibre forming solid.\n \n It possess high tensile strength. \n \n This characteristic can be attributed to the strong intermolecular forces like hydrogen bonding.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 437 Paragraph-6)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions-2',NULL,'2','mcq','T,T','T,F','F,T','F,F','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290432,'chemistry','Nylon 6, 6 contain strong intermolecular forces like','hydrogen bonding.','Nylon 6, 6 is fibre forming solid.It possess high tensile strength. \n \n This characteristic can be attributed to the strong intermolecular forces like hydrogen bonding.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 437 Paragraph-6)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions-2',NULL,'1','mcq','ionic force','van de wall force','hydrogen bonding.','none of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290433,'chemistry','Select the correct statement for synthetic rubbers?','They are copolymers of 1,4 butadiene derivatives with unsaturated monomers','Thus, synthetic rubbers are either homopolymers of 1, 3 - butadiene derivatives or copolymers of 1, 3 - butadiene or its derivatives with another unsaturated monomer.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 441 Paragraph-3)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Types of polymerisation reactions-2',NULL,'1','mcq','They are homopolymers of 1,4 butadiene derivatives','They are copolymers of 1,4 butadiene derivatives','They are copolymers of 1,4 butadiene derivatives with unsaturated monomers','They are copolymers of 1,4 butadiene derivatives with saturated monomers','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
