(290829,'chemistry','Which one of the following statements is not true?','Natural rubber has the trans-configuration at every double bond.','cis-1, 4-polyisoprene is a natural rubber.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 440 Paragraph-4)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Buna-S is a copolymer of butadiene and styrene','Natural rubber is a 1,4-polymer of isoprene','In vulcanization, the formation of sulphur bridges between different chains make rubber harder and strongerf','Natural rubber has the trans-configuration at every double bond.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290830,'chemistry','Which one of the following polymers is prepared by condensation polymerisation?','Nylon-6,7','Nylon 6,6: \n \n It is prepared by the condensation polymerisation of hexamethylenediamine with adipic acid under high pressure and at high temperature.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 437 Paragraph-5)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','teflon','Nylon-6,7','styrene','Neoprene','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290831,'chemistry','The monomer of the polymer given in diagram(n2939)','a','Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 434 Paragraph-4)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a','b','c','d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/290831q.png'),
(290832,'chemistry','Which one of the following is a chain growth polymer?','Polystyrene','Chain-growth polymers involve a series of reactions each of which consume a reactive particle and produces another similar one. \n \n The reactive particles may be free radicals or ions (cation or anion) to which monomers get added by a chain reaction. \n \n It is an important reaction of alkenes and conjugated dienes or indeed of all kinds of compounds that contains C – C double bonds.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 434 Paragraph-4)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Protein','Polystyrene','Nucleic acid','Starch','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290916,'chemistry','Which of them is considered as step growth polymers?\n\na. Nucleic acid\n\nb. Proteins\n\nc. Starch\n\nd. LDP','a,b,c','During the polymerisation of nucleic acid, protein and starch, water molecule or any neurtral molecule is removed. \n\nThus, these are examples of condensation polymers or step growth polymerisation.\n\nThese polycondensation reactions may result in the loss of some simple molecules as water, alcohol, hydrogen chloride, etc., and lead to the formation of high molecular mass condensation polymers.\n\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 437 Paragraph-1)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Exampler based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,c','a,d','a,b,c','b,c,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290917,'chemistry','Match the materials given in Column I with their polymers in Column II as per the given diagram(n2953)','A⟶(6), B⟶(5), C⟶(1) ,D⟶(3), E⟶(2), F⟶(4)','Nylon 6 is used for the manufacture of tyre cords, fabrics and ropes. Nylon 6,6 is used in making sheets, bristles for brushes and in textile industry\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 438 Paragraph-1, Page-437 Paragraph 7)\nMelamine is used in the manufacture of unbreakable crockery.Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 439 Paragraph-2)\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 443 Paragraph-Table) diagram(e2901)\ndiagram(ne2909)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Exampler based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A⟶(2), B⟶(1), C⟶(4) ,D⟶(6), E⟶(3), F⟶(5)','A⟶(6), B⟶(5), C⟶(1) ,D⟶(3), E⟶(2), F⟶(4)','A⟶(2), B⟶(3), C⟶(6) ,D⟶(4), E⟶(1), F⟶(5)','A⟶(5), B⟶(1), C⟶(2) ,D⟶(3), E⟶(4), F⟶(6)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/290917q.png'),
(290918,'chemistry','Match the polymers given in Column I with the type of linkages present in them in Column II as per the given diagram(n2954)','A⟶(2), B⟶(4), C⟶(1) ,D⟶(4), E⟶(3)','These polymers possessing amide linkages are important examples of synthetic fibres and are termed as nylons.\n\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 437 Paragraph-4) diagram(ne2910)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Exampler based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A⟶(4), B⟶(2), C⟶(1) ,D⟶(4), E⟶(3)','A⟶(2), B⟶(4), C⟶(1) ,D⟶(4), E⟶(3)','A⟶(2), B⟶(4), C⟶(3) ,D⟶(4), E⟶(1)','A⟶(2), B⟶(4), C⟶(1) ,D⟶(2), E⟶(3)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/290918q.png'),
(290919,'chemistry','Match the polymers given in Column I with the mode of polymerisation in Column II as per the given diagram(n2955)','A⟶(4), B⟶(1), C⟶(2)','HDP is formed when addition polymerisation of ethene takes place in a hydrocarbon solvent in the presence of a catalyst such as triethylaluminium and titanium tetrachloride (Ziegler-Natta catalyst) at a temperature of 333 K to 343 K and under a pressure of 6-7 atmospheres.\n\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 436 Paragraph-2)\n\nNylon 6,6: \n\nIt is prepared by the condensation polymerisation of hexamethylenediamine with adipic acid under high pressure and at high temperature.\n\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 437 Paragraph-5)\ndiagram(ne2911)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Exampler based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A⟶(4), B⟶(3), C⟶(2)','A⟶(3), B⟶(1), C⟶(2)','A⟶(3), B⟶(1), C⟶(4)','A⟶(4), B⟶(1), C⟶(2)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/290919q.png'),
(290920,'chemistry','Match the materials given in Column I with their main applications in Column II as per the given diagram(n2956)','A⟶(4), B⟶(5), C⟶(1) ,D⟶(6), E⟶(4), F⟶(3)','Bakelite is used for making combs, phonograph records, electrical switches and handles of various utensils. \n\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 438 Paragraph-3)\n\nLDP is used in the insulation of electricity carrying wires and manufacture of squeeze bottles, toys and flexible pipes. \n\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 436 Paragraph-1)\n\nNylon 6 is used for the manufacture of tyre cords, fabrics and ropes. Nylon 6,6 is used in making sheets, bristles for brushes and in textile industry.\n\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 438 Paragraph-1, Page-437 Paragraph 7)\ndiagram(ne2912)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Exampler based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A⟶(2), B⟶(1), C⟶(4) ,D⟶(6), E⟶(3), F⟶(5)','A⟶(6), B⟶(5), C⟶(1) ,D⟶(3), E⟶(2), F⟶(4)','A⟶(4), B⟶(5), C⟶(1) ,D⟶(6), E⟶(4), F⟶(3)','A⟶(5), B⟶(1), C⟶(2) ,D⟶(3), E⟶(4), F⟶(6)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/290920q.png'),
(290921,'chemistry','Vulcanization improves the quality of rubber by making it\n\na. more elastic\n\nb. soluble in inorganic solvent\n\nc. crystalline\n\nd. more stiff','a,d','Vulcanisation of rubber: \n\nNatural rubber becomes soft at high temperature (>335 K) and brittle at low temperatures (<283 K) and shows high water absorption capacity. \n\nIt is soluble in non polar solvents and is non-resistant to attack by oxidising agents. \n\nTo improve upon these physical properties, a process of vulcanisation is carried out.\n\nOn vulcanisation, sulphur forms cross links at the reactive sites of double bonds and thus the rubber gets stiffened. \n\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 440 Paragraph-6)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Exampler based Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,c','a,d','a,b,c','All of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290922,'chemistry','Which of the following polymers of glucose is stored by animals?','Glycogen','The carbohydrates are stored in animal body as glycogen. It is also known as animal starch because its structure is similar to amylopectin and is rather more highly branched. It is present in liver, muscles and brain.Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 419 Paragraph-3)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Exampler based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cellulose','Amylose','Amylopectin','Glycogen','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290923,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a semisynthetic polymer?','cis-polyisoprene','Cellulose derivatives as cellulose acetate (rayon) and cellulose nitrate, etc. are the usual examples of semisynthetic polymer.\n\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 434 Paragraph-1)\n\n"cis-1, 4-polyisoprene is a natural rubber. It is not a semisynthetic polymer\n\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 440 Paragraph-4)"','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Exampler based Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','cis-polyisoprene','Cellulose nitrate','Cellulose acetate','Vulcanised rubber','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290924,'chemistry','The commercial name of polyacrylonitrile is','Orlon','Polyacrylonitrile is used as a substitute for wool in making commercial fibres as orlon or acrilan.\n\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 436 Paragraph-5)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Exampler based Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Dacron','Orlon','PVC','Bakelite','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290925,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is not true about low density polyethene?','Highly branched structure','Low density polythene: It is obtained by the polymerisation of ethene under high pressure of 1000 to 2000 atmospheres at a temperature of 350 K to 570 K in the presence of traces of dioxygen or a peroxide initiator (catalyst). \n\nThe low density polythene (LDP) is obtained through the free radical addition and H-atom abstraction. \n\nThese polymers have straight chain structure with some branches. Low density polythene is chemically inert and tough but flexible and a poor conductor of electricity\n\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 435 Paragraph-4)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Exampler based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Tough','Hard','Poor conductor of electricity','Highly branched structure','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290926,'chemistry','The monomer of the given polymer will be: Refer diagram(n2957)','a','a\' will polymerise as given in the diagram(ne2950)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Exampler based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/290926q.png'),
(290927,'chemistry','Which of the following polymer can be formed by using the following monomer units? Refer diagram(n2958)','Nylon-6','Nylon 6: It is obtained by heating caprolactum with water at a high temperature.\n\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 437 Paragraph-8)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Exampler based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Nylon 6,6','Nylon 2-nylon-6','Melamine polymer','Nylon-6','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/290927q.png'),
(290928,'chemistry','Which of the following are characteristics of thermosetting polymers?\n\na. Heavily branched cross linked polymers\n\nb. Linear slightly branched long  chain molecules\n\nc. Becomes infusible on moulding so cannot be reused\n\nd. Soften on heating and harden on cooling can be reused.','a,c','Bakelite is thermosetting polymer because it cannot be reused or remoulded.\n\nIt undergoes extensive cross linking in moulds and again become infusible thats why it becomes irreversible to mould and is thermosetting type\n\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 438 Paragraph-3)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Exampler based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290929,'chemistry','Which of the following polymers are thermoplastic?\n\na. Teflon\n\nb. Natural rubber\n\nc. Neoprene\n\nd. Polystyrene','a,d','Polythenes are linear or slightly branched long chain molecules. \n\nThese are capable of repeatedly softening on heating and hardening on cooling and are thus thermoplastic polymers.\n\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 435 Paragraph-3)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Exampler based Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,b,c','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(290930,'chemistry','Which of the following polymers are used as fibre?\n\na. Polytetrafluoroethane\n\nb. Polychloroprene\n\nc. Nylon\n\nd. Terylene','c,d','Nylon and terylene are used as fibre.\n\n"These polymers possessing amide linkages are important examples of synthetic fibres and are termed as nylons.\n\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 437 Paragraph-4)"','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Exampler based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290931,'chemistry','Which of the following are addition polymers?\n\na. Nylon\nb. Melamine formaldehyde resins\nc. Orlon\nd. Polystyrene','c,d','"The addition polymerisation of acrylonitrile(homopolymer) in presence of a peroxide catalyst leads to the formation of polyacrylonitrile.\nPolyacrylonitrile is used as a substitute for wool in making commercial fibres as orlon or acrilan.\n\nPolystyrene is formed by the addition polymerisation of styrene\n\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 436 Paragraph-4)"','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Exampler based Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290932,'chemistry','Which of the following polymers are condensation polymers?\n\na. Bakelite\nb. Teflon\nc. Butyl rubber\nd. Melamine formaldehyde resin','a,d','These polycondensation reactions may result in the loss of some simple molecules as water, alcohol, hydrogen chloride, etc., and lead to the formation of high molecular mass condensation polymers. \n\nBakelite is fomed by condesation polymerisation of phenol and formaldehyde.\n\nMelanine formaldehyde is formed by polymerisation of melamine and formaldehyde.\n\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 437 Paragraph-1) . Refer diagram(ne2951)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Exampler based Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290933,'chemistry','Which of the following monomers form biodegradable polymers?\n\na. 3-hydroxybutanoic acid + 3-hydroxypentanoic acid\n\nb. Glycine + amino caproic acid\n\nc. Ethylene glycol + phthalic acid\n\nd. Caprolactum','a,b','Poly β-hydroxybutyrate – co-β-hydroxy valerate (PHBV) It is obtained by the copolymerisation of 3-hydroxybutanoic acid and 3 - hydroxypentanoic acid. PHBV is used in speciality packaging, orthopaedic devices and in controlled release of drugs. PHBV undergoes bacterial degradation in the environment.Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 442 Paragraph-4)\n\nNylon-2-nylon 6 is an alternating polyamide copolymer of glycine (H₂N–CH₂ – COOH) and amino caproic acid [H₂N (CH₂ )₅COOH] and is biodegradable.Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 442 Paragraph-5)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Exampler based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(290934,'chemistry','Which of the following polymers can have strong intermolecular forces?\n\na. Nylon\n\nb. Polystyrene\n\nc. Rubber\n\nd. Polyesters','a,d','Both nylon and polyesters have strong intermolecular forces of attractions.\nNylon 6, 6 is fibre forming solid It possess high tensile strength.  \nThis characteristic can be attributed to the strong intermolecular forces like hydrogen bonding. \n\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page- 437 Paragraph-6)','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Exampler based Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291115,'chemistry','Assertion: Formaldehyde reacts with phenol and melamine to form the corresponding condensation polymer products.\n\nReason: Condensation polymerization reactions occur through the interaction of bi- or polyfunctional monomers containing -NH2, -OH, and -COOH groups.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Formaldehyde reacts with phenol and melamine to form the corresponding condensation polymer products. The reason for this is the interaction of bi- or polyfunctional monomers containing -NH2, -OH, and -COOH groups, which is characteristic of condensation polymerization reactions. Therefore, both the assertion and the reason are true, and the reason provides the correct explanation for the assertion.','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(291116,'chemistry','Assertion: Biodegradable polymers such as PHBV and Nylon-2-Nylon-6 have been developed as alternatives to synthetic polymeric wastes.\n\nReason: The potential environmental hazards of synthetic polymeric wastes have led to the development of biodegradable polymers.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Biodegradable polymers such as PHBV and Nylon-2-Nylon-6 have been developed as alternatives to synthetic polymeric wastes. The reason for this development is also mentioned in the text, which is the potential environmental hazards of synthetic polymeric wastes. Therefore, both the assertion and the reason are true, and the reason provides the correct explanation for the assertion','Organic chemistry >> Polymers >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(280110,'chemistry','Which among the following is the most common oligosaccharides?','Disaccharides','Amongst these oligosaccharides the most common are disaccharides.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry class 12; Page 412; Paragraph no. 14.1.1(ii))','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Carbohydrates',NULL,'1','mcq','Monosaccharides','Disaccharides','Trisaccharides','Tetrasaccharides','NEW','12-412','12-282',NULL),
(280111,'chemistry','Which of the following is a common molecule obtained in hydrolysis of sucrose and maltose?','Glucose','One molecule of sucrose on hydrolysis gives one molecule of glucose and one molecule of fructose whereas maltose gives two molecules of only glucose.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry class 12; Page 412; Paragraph no. 14.1.1(ii))','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Carbohydrates',NULL,'2','mcq','Fructose','Glucose','Galactose','Mannose','NEW','12-412','12-282',NULL),
(280112,'chemistry','Carbohydrates which yield a large number of monosaccharide units on hydrolysis are called ______.','polysaccharides','Carbohydrates which yield a large number of monosaccharide units on hydrolysis are called polysaccharides.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry class 12; Page 412; Paragraph no. 14.1.1(iii))','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Carbohydrates',NULL,'1','mcq','tetrasaccharides','disaccharides','oligosaccharides','polysaccharides','NEW','12-412','12-282',NULL),
(280113,'chemistry','Which of the following is true about polysaccharides?','Polysaccharides are also called non-sugars.','Polysaccharides yield a large number of monosaccharide units on hydrolysis are called polysaccharides. Some common examples are starch, cellulose, glycogen, gums, etc. Polysaccharides are not sweet in taste, hence they are also called non-sugars.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry class 12; Page 412; Paragraph no. 14.1.1(iii))','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Carbohydrates',NULL,'2','mcq','Polysaccharides are sweet in taste.','Polysaccharides are also called non-sugars.','Some common examples are glucose and galactose.','Polysaccharides yield two to ten monosaccharide units on hydrolysis.','NEW','12-412','12-282',NULL),
(280114,'chemistry','Select the incorrect statement.','Glucose does not reduce the Tollens\' reagent.','a. All monosaccharides whether aldose or ketose are reducing sugars.\n b. The two monosaccharide units obtained on hydrolysis of a disaccharide may be same or different. For example, one molecule of sucrose on hydrolysis gives one molecule of glucose and one molecule of fructose whereas maltose gives two molecules of only glucose.\n c. Fructose reduces Fehling solution.\n d. All monosaccharides, whether aldehydes and ketones, are reducing agents. Hence, glucose can reduce Tollen\'s reagent.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry class 12; Page 412; Paragraph no.14.1.1(iii))','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Carbohydrates',NULL,'2','mcq','All monosaccharides are reducing sugars.','The monosaccharide units formed by hydrolysis of a disaccharide may be same or different.','Fructose reduces Fehling solution.','Glucose does not reduce the Tollens\' reagent.','NEW','12-412','12-282',NULL),
(280115,'chemistry','What would be the name of carbohydrate containing ketone group and 5 carbons?','Ketopentose','The name of carbohydrate containing ketone group and 5 carbons is the Ketopentose.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry class 12; Page 412; Table 14.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Carbohydrates',NULL,'2','mcq','Aldopentose','Aldohexose','Ketopentose','Ketotetrose','NEW','12-412','12-282',NULL),
(280116,'chemistry','What is the name of a carbohydrate with an aldehyde group and 7 carbons?','Aldoheptose','The name of carbohydrate containing aldehyde group and 7 carbons is the Aldoheptose.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry class 12; Page 412; Table 14.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Carbohydrates',NULL,'2','mcq','Aldopentose','Aldoheptose','Aldohexose','Ketoheptose','NEW','12-412','12-282',NULL),
(280117,'chemistry','Which one of the following is the general formula for carbohydrates?','Cₓ(H₂O)ᵧ','Most carbohydrates have the formula Cₓ(H₂O)ᵧ, and were considered as hydrates of carbon from where the name carbohydrate was derived.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 411; Paragraph 14.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Carbohydrates',NULL,'1','mcq','Cₓ(HO)ᵧ','Cₓ(H₂O)ᵧ','Cₓ(H₂O₂)ᵧ','Cₓ(HO₂)ᵧ','NEW','12-411','12-281',NULL),
(280118,'chemistry','A carbohydrate that cannot be hydrolyzed further to give simpler unit of polyhydroxy aldehyde or ketone is called?','Monosaccharide','A carbohydrate that cannot be hydrolyzed further to give simpler unit of polyhydroxy aldehyde or ketone is called a monosaccharide. \n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 432; Exercise 14.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Carbohydrates',NULL,'2','mcq','Oligosaccharide','Monosaccharide','Polysaccharide','Disaccharide','NEW','12-412','12-282',NULL),
(280119,'chemistry','Depending upon the number of monosaccharides they provide on hydrolysis, Oligosaccharides are classified as?','Disaccharides, trisaccharides and tetrasaccharides.','Oligosaccharides are classified as disaccharides, trisaccharides, tetrasaccharides, etc., depending upon the number of monosaccharides they provide on hydrolysis. \n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 412; Paragraph 14.1.1(ii))','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Carbohydrates',NULL,'2','mcq','Disaccharides, polysaccharide and tetrasaccharides.','Disaccharides, trisaccharides and polysaccharide.','Disaccharides, trisaccharides and tetrasaccharides.','Disaccharides, trisaccharides and monosaccharide.','NEW','12-412','12-282',NULL),
(280120,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is true?','Aldose are monosaccharides that contain aldehyde group.','Monosaccharides are further classified on the basis of number of carbon atoms and the functional group present in them. \n If a monosaccharide contains an aldehyde group, it is known as an aldose and if it contains a keto group it is known as a ketose. \n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 412; Paragraph 14.1.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Carbohydrates',NULL,'3','mcq','Aldose are monosaccharides that contain aldehyde group.','Monosaccharides are classified depending on the units obtained on hydrolysis.','If a monosaccharide contains an aldehyde group it is known as a ketose.','Monosaccharides cannot be classified depending on number of carbon.','NEW','12-412','12-282',NULL),
(280412,'chemistry','On hydrolysis, sucrose gives equimolar mixture of D-(+)-glucose and D-(-) fructose. Complete the equation below:\n C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁ + H₂O →','C₆H₁₂O₆ + C₆H₁₂O₆','One of the common disaccharides is sucrose which on hydrolysis gives equimolar mixture of D-(+)-glucose and D-(-) fructose.\n The equation is: C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁ + H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + C₆H₁₂O₆\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 417; Paragraph 14.1.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Disaccharides',NULL,'2','mcq','C₆H₁₂O₆ + C₆H₁₂O₆ + H₂O','2C₆H₁₂O₆','2C₆H₁₂O₆ + H₂O','C₆H₁₂O₆ + C₆H₁₂O₆','NEW','12-417','12-287',NULL),
(280508,'chemistry','Which of the following is predominant constituent of plant cell wall?','Cellulose','Cellulose occurs exclusively in plants and it is the most abundant organic substance in plant kingdom. It is a predominant constituent of cell wall of plant cells.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 419; Paragraph no. 14.1.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Polysaccharides',NULL,'1','mcq','Cellulose','Starch','Glycogen','Mannose','NEW','12-419','12-289',NULL),
(280509,'chemistry','Which of the following is incorrect about cellulose?','Cellulose is composed of β-D Glucose and β-L glucose.','Cellulose is a straight chain polysaccharide composed only of β-D-glucose units which are joined by glycosidic linkage between C1 of one glucose unit and C4 of the next glucose unit.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 419; Paragraph no. 14.1.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Polysaccharides',NULL,'2','mcq','Cellulose is straight chain polysaccharide.','Cellulose is composed of β-D Glucose and β-L glucose.','It is major component of cell wall of plants.','Glucose units are joined by glycosidic linkage between C1 of one glucose unit and C4 of the next glucose unit.','NEW','12-419','12-289',NULL),
(280510,'chemistry','Which of the following is known as animal starch?','Glycogen','Glycogen is also known as animal starch because its structure is similar to amylopectin and is rather more highly branched.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 419; Paragraph no. 14.1.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Polysaccharides',NULL,'1','mcq','Glycogen','Cellulose','Insulin','Galactose','NEW','12-419','12-289',NULL),
(280511,'chemistry','Which polysaccharide is similar to amylopectin in structure?','Glycogen','Glycogen is also known as animal starch because its structure is similar to amylopectin and is rather more highly branched.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 419; Paragraph no. 14.1.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Polysaccharides',NULL,'1','mcq','Glycogen','Cellulose','Insulin','Lactose','NEW','12-419','12-289',NULL),
(280512,'chemistry','Glycogen is not present in :','Plant tissues','Glycogen is present in liver, muscles and brain. When the body needs glucose, enzymes break the glycogen down to glucose. It is also found in yeast and fungi.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 419; Paragraph no. 14.1.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Polysaccharides',NULL,'1','mcq','Muscles of human','Yeast','Liver','Plant tissues','NEW','12-419','12-289',NULL),
(280718,'chemistry','Choose the odd one with respect to essential amino acids.','Serine','Serine is a non-essential amino acid while Phenylalanine, Methionine and Threonine are essential amino acids.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Table 14.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Proteins',NULL,'2','mcq','Serine','Phenylalanine','Methionine','Threonine','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280719,'chemistry','Which amino acid has the side chain shown in the figure?\n diagram(n2805)','Tryptophan','The amino acid with the side chain shown is Tryptophan.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Table 14.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Proteins',NULL,'3','mcq','Serine','Tyrosine','Histidine','Tryptophan','NEW','12-420','12-290','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/280719q.png'),
(280720,'chemistry','Match the following amino acids with their single letter code.','A-Alanine, F-Phenylalanine, Y-Tyrosine, V-Valine','A-Alanine, F-Phenylalanine, Y-Tyrosine, V-Valine\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Table 14.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Proteins',NULL,'3','match','A,Alanine','F,Phenylalanine','Y,Tyrosine','V,Valine','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280721,'chemistry','What is the basis for amino acid classification as neutral, acidic and basic?','Relative number of amino and carboxyl groups.','Amino acids are classified as acidic, basic or neutral depending upon the relative number of amino and carboxyl groups in their molecule.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Paragraph 14.2.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Classification of amino acids',NULL,'1','mcq','Relative number of amino and carboxyl groups.','Relative number of amino and hydroxyl groups.','Relative number of hydroxyl and carboxyl groups.','Relative number of hydroxyl and amide groups.','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280722,'chemistry','What will be nature of amino acid if it has equal number amino and carboxyl groups?','Neutral','Equal number of amino and carboxyl groups makes it neutral; more number of amino than carboxyl groups makes it basic and more carboxyl groups as compared to amino groups makes it acidic.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 421; Paragraph 14.2.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Classification of amino acids',NULL,'2','mcq','Neutral','Acidic','Basic','Polar','NEW','12-421','12-291',NULL),
(280723,'chemistry','Amino acids which can be synthesised in body are called _____ amino acids.','non-essential','The amino acids, which can be synthesised in the body, are known as nonessential amino acids.\n On the other hand, those which cannot be synthesised in the body and must be obtained through diet, are known as essential amino acids.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 421; Paragraph 14.2.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Classification of amino acids',NULL,'1','mcq','essential','non-essential','non-synthetic','synthetic','NEW','12-421','12-291',NULL),
(280724,'chemistry','Which among the following are not the properties of amino acids?','Low melting points','Amino acids are usually colourless, crystalline solids.\n These are water-soluble, high melting solids and behave like salts rather than simple amines or carboxylic acids.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 422; Paragraph 14.2.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Classification of amino acids',NULL,'1','mcq','Colorless','Crystalline solids','Low melting points','Water soluble','NEW','12-422','12-292',NULL),
(280725,'chemistry','Which among the following options is not true about \' Zwitter ion\' ?','The zwitter ion easily reacts with bases but not with acids.','In aqueous solution, the carboxyl group can lose a proton and amino group can accept a proton, giving rise to a dipolar ion known as zwitter ion. This is neutral but contains both positive and negative charges. In zwitter ionic form, amino acids show amphoteric behaviour as they react both with acids and bases.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 422; Paragraph 14.2.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Classification of amino acids',NULL,'1','mcq','Zwitter ion shows amphoteric behaviour.','Zwitter ion has a dipolar nature.','The zwitter ion easily reacts with bases but not with acids.','Zwitter ion is neutral but contains both positive and negative charges.','NEW','12-422','12-292',NULL),
(280726,'chemistry','What is true about zwitter ion?','It is neutral but contains both positive and negative charges.','The Zwitter ion is neutral but contains both positive and negative charges.\n In zwitter ionic form, amino acids show amphoteric behaviour as they react both with acids and bases.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 422; Paragraph 14.2.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Classification of amino acids',NULL,'2','mcq','It is neutral but contains no charge.','It is neutral but contains only positive charges.','It is neutral but contains both positive and negative charges.','It is negatively charged and contains R side chains only.','NEW','12-422','12-292',NULL),
(280727,'chemistry','Which of the following amino acids is not optically active?','Glycine','Except glycine, all other naturally occurring α-amino acids are optically active, since the α-carbon atom is asymmetric.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 422; Paragraph 14.2.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Classification of amino acids',NULL,'2','mcq','Glycine','Glutamic acid','Alanine','Serine','NEW','12-422','12-292',NULL),
(280728,'chemistry','Choose the correct statements.\n A. Most of the naturally occuring amino acids have L-configuration.\n B. In zwitter ionic form amino acids show amphoteric nature.\n C. All amino acids are optically active.','Both A and B','A. Most naturally occurring amino acids have L-configuration. L-Amino Acids are represented by writing the –NH2 group on left hand side.\n B. In zwitter ionic form, amino acids show amphoteric behaviour as they react both with acids and bases.\n C. Except glycine, all other naturally occurring α-amino acids are optically active.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 422; Paragraph 14.2.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Classification of amino acids',NULL,'2','mcq','Only A','Both A and B','Both B and C','A, B and C','NEW','12-422','12-292',NULL),
(280806,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n Assertion: Insulin containing 51 amino acids is considered as protein.\n Reason: Insulin ordinarily has a well defined conformation of a protein.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The distinction between a polypeptide and a protein is not very sharp. Polypeptides with fewer amino acids are likely to be called proteins if they ordinarily have a well defined conformation of a protein such as insulin which contains 51 amino acids.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 422; Paragraph 14.2.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Structure of proteins',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','12-422','12-292',NULL),
(280807,'chemistry','Which of the following is false for fibrous proteins?','They are soluble in water.','When the polypeptide chains run parallel and are held together by hydrogen and disulphide bonds, then fibre– like structure is formed.\n Such proteins are generally insoluble in water. Some common examples are keratin (present in hair, wool, silk) and myosin (present in muscles), etc.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 422; Paragraph no. 14.2.3 (a))','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Structure of proteins',NULL,'2','mcq','Polypeptide chains are held together by hydrogen and disulphide bonds.','They are soluble in water.','Keratin and myosin are the examples of it.','Parallel chains of polypeptides exist.','NEW','12-422','12-292',NULL),
(280808,'chemistry','Which of the following is incorrect statement?','Insulin is a fibrous protein.','Globular proteins are usually soluble in water. Insulin and albumins are the common examples of globular proteins. Keratin is a fibrous protein, that are generally insoluble in water.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 423; Paragraph no. 14.2.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Structure of proteins',NULL,'2','mcq','Globular proteins are generally soluble in water.','Insulin is a fibrous protein.','Albumin is a globular protein.','Keratin is water insoluble protein.','NEW','12-423','12-293',NULL),
(280809,'chemistry','Each polypeptide in a protein has amino acids linked with each other in a specific sequence.The sequence of amino acids is said to be the______ structure of that protein.','Primary','Each polypeptide in a protein has amino acids linked with each other in a specific sequence and it is this sequence of amino acids that is said to be the primary structure of that protein.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 423; Paragraph 14.2.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Structure of proteins',NULL,'1','mcq','Primary','Secondary','Tertiary','Quaternary','NEW','12-423','12-293',NULL),
(280810,'chemistry','Given below are two statements\n Statement I : Each polypeptide in a protein has amino acids linked together in a specific sequence, and this sequence of amino acids is referred to as the protein\'s primary structure.\n Statement II : Any change in the primary structure, i.e., the amino acid sequence, results in a different protein.\n In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Each polypeptide in a protein has amino acids linked with each other in a specific sequence and it is this sequence of amino acids that is said to be the primary structure of that protein. Any change in this primary structure i.e., the sequence of amino acids creates a different protein.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 423; Paragraph 14.2.3 (i))','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Structure of proteins',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.','Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect.','Statement I is incorrect, and Statement II is correct.','NEW','12-423','12-293',NULL),
(280811,'chemistry','Which of the following structures refers to shape in which a long polypeptide chain can exist?','Secondary','The secondary structure of protein refers to the shape in which a long polypeptide chain can exist.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 423; Paragraph 14.2.3 (ii))','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Structure of proteins',NULL,'1','mcq','Primary','Secondary','Tertiary','Quaternary','NEW','12-423','12-293',NULL),
(280812,'chemistry','In which of the following polypeptide chain forms all possible hydrogen bonds?','α-Helix','α-Helix is one of the most common ways in which a polypeptide chain forms all possible hydrogen bonds by twisting into a right handed screw.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 423; Paragraph 14.2.3 (ii))','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Structure of proteins',NULL,'2','mcq','α-Plated sheet','α-Helix','β-Helix','β-plated sheet','NEW','12-423','12-293',NULL),
(280813,'chemistry','In which structure are all peptide chains stretched to nearly their maximum length and then arranged side by side, bound together by intermolecular hydrogen bonds?','β-plated sheet','In β-pleated sheet structure all peptide chains are stretched out to nearly maximum extension and then laid side by side which are held together by intermolecular hydrogen bonds.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 423; Paragraph 14.2.3 (ii))','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Structure of proteins',NULL,'2','mcq','α-Plated sheet','α-Helix','β-Helix','β-plated sheet','NEW','12-423','12-293',NULL),
(280814,'chemistry','Which structure of proteins give rise to molecular shapes like fibrous and globular?','Tertiary','The tertiary structure of proteins represents overall folding of the polypeptide chains i.e., further folding of the secondary structure.\n It gives rise to two major molecular shapes that is fibrous and globular.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 423; Paragraph 14.2.3 (iii))','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Structure of proteins',NULL,'1','mcq','Primary','Secondary','Tertiary','Quaternary','NEW','12-423','12-293',NULL),
(280815,'chemistry','The spatial arrangement of a protein\'s multiple polypeptide chains with respect to each other is known as the:','Quaternary structure','Some of the proteins are composed of two or more polypeptide chains referred to as sub-units. The spatial arrangement of these subunits with respect to each other is known as quaternary structure.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 423; Paragraph 14.2.3 (iv))','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Structure of proteins',NULL,'1','mcq','Primary structure','Secondary structure','Tertiary structure','Quaternary structure','NEW','12-423','12-293',NULL),
(280816,'chemistry','A native protein refers to:','a biological protein with an unique three-dimensional structure and function.','Protein found in a biological system with a unique three-dimensional structure and biological activity is called a native protein.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 424; Paragraph 14.2.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Structure of proteins',NULL,'1','mcq','an artificial protein exhibiting the same function as the natural one.','a biological protein with an unique three-dimensional structure and function.','a biological protein existing in its secondary structure.','the unique amino acid sequence of a biological protein.','NEW','12-424','12-294',NULL),
(280817,'chemistry','Which of the following does not cause denaturation of proteins?','exposure to distilled water','When a protein in its native form, is subjected to physical change like change in temperature or chemical change like change in pH, the hydrogen bonds are disturbed. Due to this, globules unfold and helix get uncoiled and protein loses its biological activity. This is called denaturation of protein.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 424; Paragraph 14.2.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Structure of proteins',NULL,'2','mcq','exposure to temperature','changes in pH','disturbance of hydrogen bonds','exposure to distilled water','NEW','12-424','12-294',NULL),
(280818,'chemistry','Which of the following structures remain intact during denaturation?','Primary','During denaturation secondary and tertiary structures are destroyed but primary structure remains intact.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 424; Paragraph 14.2.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Structure of proteins',NULL,'1','mcq','Primary','Secondary','Tertiary','Quaternary','NEW','12-424','12-294',NULL),
(280819,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n Assertion: The melting points and solubility in water of amino acids are generally higher than that of the corresponding halo acids.\n Reason: Amino acids exist as dipolar ions in the form of zwitter ion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The melting points and solubility in water of amino acids are generally higher than that of the corresponding halo acids as amino acids exist as dipolar ions in the form of zwitter ion. On the other hand, halo-acids do not exhibit such dipolar behaviour.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 425; Intext Questions 14.5)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Structure of proteins',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','12-425','12-295',NULL),
(280911,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\n Assertion: Vitamin C must be supplied regularly in our diet.\n\n Reason: Vitamin C is readily excreted in urine and not stored in our body,','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','B group vitamins and vitamin C are soluble in water so they are grouped together. Water soluble vitamins must be supplied regularly in diet because they are readily excreted in urine and cannot be stored (except vitamin B12) in our body.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 426; Paragraph 14.4.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Vitamins',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(280912,'chemistry','Which of the following B group vitamin is stored in our body?','B₁₂','Water soluble vitamins must be supplied regularly in diet because they are readily excreted in urine and cannot be stored (except vitamin B12) in our body.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 426; Paragraph 14.4.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Vitamins',NULL,'2','mcq','B₁₂','B₃','B₂','B₉','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(280913,'chemistry','Match the following Vitamins with their deficiency diseases.','Vitamin A-Night blindness, Vitamin C-Scurvy, Vitamin D-Rickets, Vitamin B₁-Beri-beri','Lack of Vitamin A causes Xerophthalmia (hardening of cornea of eye) and night blindness.\n Lack of Vitamin C causes Scurvy (bleeding gums).\n Lack of Vitamin D causes Rickets (bone deformities in children) and osteomalacia (soft bones and joint pain in adults).\n Lack of Vitamin B₁ causes Beri beri (loss of appetite, retarded growth).\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 426; Table 14.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Vitamins',NULL,'3','match','Vitamin A, Night blindness','Vitamin C, Scurvy','Vitamin D, Rickets','Vitamin B₁, Beri-beri','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(280914,'chemistry','Which of the following vitamins deficiency leads to Xerophthalmia?','A','Lack of Vitamin A causes Xerophthalmia (hardening of cornea of eye) and night blindness.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 426; Table 14.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Vitamins',NULL,'2','mcq','A','C','D','K','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(280915,'chemistry','Riboflavin is the another name for _____.','Vitamin B₂','Riboflavin is the another name for Vitamin B₂.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 426; Table 14.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Vitamins',NULL,'1','mcq','Vitamin B₁₂','Vitamin B₂','Vitamin B₁','Vitamin A','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(280916,'chemistry','What is the another name for Vitamin B₆?','Pyridoxine','Vitamin B₆ is also called Pyridoxine.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 426; Table 14.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Vitamins',NULL,'1','mcq','Pyridoxine','Riboflavin','Ascorbic acid','Thiamine','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(280917,'chemistry','What is the another name for Vitamin B₁?','Thiamine','Vitamin B₁ is also called Thiamine.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 426; Table 14.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Vitamins',NULL,'1','mcq','Pyridoxine','Riboflavin','Thymine','Thiamine','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(280918,'chemistry','Which of the following vitamin deficiency is responsible for increase blood clotting time?','Vitamin K','Deficiency in Vitamin K causes increased blood clotting time.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 426; Table 14.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Vitamins',NULL,'2','mcq','Vitamin C','Vitamin B₂','Vitamin E','Vitamin K','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(280919,'chemistry','Which of the following is related to Scurvy?','Ascorbic acid','Deficiency in Vitamin K causes Scurvy (bleeding gums).\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 426; Table 14.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Vitamins',NULL,'2','mcq','Riboflavin','Ascorbic acid','Tocopherol','Thiamine','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(280920,'chemistry','Which vitamin deficiency causes increased fragility of RBCs?','Vitamin E','Deficiency in Vitamin E causes increased fragility of RBCs.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 426; Table 14.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Vitamins',NULL,'2','mcq','Vitamin E','Vitamin A','Vitamin D','Vitamin B₆','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(280921,'chemistry','For which vitamin, meat, fish, egg and curd are rich source and its deficiency causes RBC deficient in haemoglobin?','Vitamin B₁₂','Meat, fish, egg and curd are rich source of Vitamin B₁₂. Deficiency in Vitamin B₁₂ causes RBC deficiency in haemoglobin.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 426; Table 14.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Vitamins',NULL,'2','mcq','Vitamin A','Vitamin B₂','Vitamin B₁₂','Vitamin B₁','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(280922,'chemistry','Which disease results in soft bones and joint pain in adults?','Osteomalacia','Osteomalacia results in soft bones and joint pain in adults.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 426; Table 14.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Vitamins',NULL,'2','mcq','Rickets','Osteomalacia','Xerophthalmia','Beri beri','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(280923,'chemistry','Fissuring at the corners of mouth is caused due to deficiency of Vitamin \'X\'. Which of the following is true for Vitamin \'X\'?','It is also called as Riboflavin.','Vitamin \'X\' is identified to be Vitamin B₂. Deficiency of Vitamin B₂ causes fissuring at the corners of mouth. Vitamin B₂ is also called Riboflavin.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 426; Table 14.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Vitamins',NULL,'3','mcq','It is also called as Riboflavin.','It is fat soluble vitamin.','It is stored in liver.','It can be obtained from sunlight.','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(281110,'chemistry','Which of the following is major sex hormone in males?','Testosterone','Testosterone is the major sex hormone produced in males. It is responsible for development of secondary male characteristics (deep voice, facial hair, general physical\n constitution).\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 431; Paragraph 14.6)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'1','mcq','Estrogen','Progesterone','Testosterone','Cortisol','NEW','12-431','12-301',NULL),
(281111,'chemistry','Which of the following is major sex hormone in females?','Estradiol','Estradiol is the main female sex hormone. It is responsible for development of secondary female characteristics and participates in the control of menstrual cycle.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 431; Paragraph 14.6)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'1','mcq','Estradiol','progesterone','Prolactin','Cortisol','NEW','12-431','12-301',NULL),
(281112,'chemistry','What products would not be formed when a nucleotide from DNA containing thymine is hydrolysed?','Guanine','When a nucleotide from the DNA containing thymine is hydrolyzed, thymine β-D-2- deoxyribose and phosphoric acid are obtained as products.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 431; Intext Question 14.7)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'2','mcq','Thymine','Phosphoric acid','Deoxyribose','Guanine','NEW','12-431','12-301',NULL),
(281113,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n Assertion: When RNA is hydrolysed, there is no relationship among the quantities of different bases obtained.\n Reason: RNA has double stranded structure with complementary chains.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','When RNA is hydrolysed, there is no relationship among the quantities of different bases obtained. The structure of DNA is a double strand whereas RNA is a single strand molecule.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 431; Intext Question 14.8)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','12-431','12-301',NULL),
(281212,'chemistry','The RBC deficiency is deficiency disease of?','Vitamin B₁₂','Deficiency in Vitamin B₁₂ causes RBC deficiency in haemoglobin.\n (NEET 2021)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Vitamin B₁','Vitamin B','Vitamin B₁₂','Vitamin B₆','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(281213,'chemistry','Which one of the following sets of monosaccharides forms sucrose?','α-D-glucopyranose and β-D-fructofuranose.','Sucrose is formed by the condensation of α-D-glucopyranose and β-D-fructofuranose.\n (2012)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','α-D-galactopyranose and α-D-glucopyranose.','α-D-glucopyranose and β-D-fructofuranose.','β-D-glucopyranose and α-D-fructofuranose.','α-D-glucopyranose and β-D-fructopyranose.','NEW','12-417','12-287',NULL),
(281214,'chemistry','Which one of the following statements is not true regarding (+)–lactose?','(+)–Lactose is a reducing sugar and does not exhibit mutarotation.','(+)-Lactose is a reducing sugar and all reducing sugars show mutarotation.\n (2011)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','On hydrolysis (+)–lactose gives equal amount of D(+)–glucose and D(+)–galactose.','(+)–Lactose is a b-glucoside formed by the union of a molecule of D(+)–glucose and a molecule of D(+)–galactose.','(+)–Lactose is a reducing sugar and does not exhibit mutarotation.','(+)–Lactose, C12H22O11 contains 8 –OH groups.','NEW','12-418','12-288',NULL),
(281215,'chemistry','Which of the statements about “Denaturation” given below are correct?\n (1) Denaturation of proteins causes loss of secondary and tertiary structures of the protein.\n (2) Denaturation leads to the conversion of double strand of DNA into single strand.\n (3) Denaturation affects primary structure which gets distorted.','(1) and (2)','Denaturation does not change the primary structure of protein.\n (Mains 2011)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','(2) and (3)','(1) and (3)','(1) and (2)','(1), (2) and (3)','NEW','12-425','12-295',NULL),
(281216,'chemistry','Which one of the following does not exhibit the phenomenon of mutarotation?','(+)–Sucrose','Sucrose does not show mutarotation.\n Mutarotation is the phenomenon of change in optical rotation shown by freshly prepared solutions of sugars. \n However, this property is not exhibited by all sugars.\n Only those sugars which have a free aldehyde (—CHO) or ketone (>C O) group are capable of showing mutarotation. \n Sucrose lacks free aldehyde or ketone group and is therefore, incapable of showing mutarotation.\n (2010)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','(+)–Sucrose','(+)–Lactose','(+)–Maltose','(–)–Fructose','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(281217,'chemistry','On hydrolysis of starch, we finally get ______.','glucose','Glucose is produced commercially by the hydrolysis of starch by boiling it with dil. H₂SO₄ at 393 K under pressure of 2-3 bar.\n (1991)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','glucose','fructose','both (a) and (b)','sucrose','NEW','12-413','12-283',NULL),
(281218,'chemistry','Fructose reduces Tollens’ reagent due to?','Enolisation of fructose followed by conversion to aldehyde by base.','Under alkaline conditions of the reagent, fructose gets converted into a mixture of glucose and mannose (Lobry de Bruyn van Ekenstein rearrangement) both of which contain the –CHO group and hence, reduce Tollens’ reagent to give silver mirror test.\n (2010)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','Asymmetric carbons.','Primary alcoholic group.','Secondary alcoholic group.','Enolisation of fructose followed by conversion to aldehyde by base.','NEW','12-416','12-286',NULL),
(281219,'chemistry','Number of chiral carbons in β-D-(+) glucose is?','Four','The structure of β-D-glucose has four asymmetric carbon atoms.\n (2004)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Five','Six','Three','Four','NEW','12-415','12-285',NULL),
(281220,'chemistry','Glycolysis is?','Oxidation of glucose to pyruvate.','Glycolysis is the first stage in the oxidation of glucose. \n It is an anaerobic process and involves the degradation of glucose into two molecules of pyruvate with the generation of two molecules of ATP.\n (2003)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Oxidation of glucose to glutamate.','Conversion of pyruvate to citrate.','Oxidation of glucose to pyruvate.','Conversion of glucose to haem.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(281221,'chemistry','Cellulose is polymer of?','Glucose','Cellulose is a straight chain polysaccharide composed of β-D-glucose units joined by β-glycosidic. \n These isomers differ only in the orientation (or configuration) at C1 atom\n (2002)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Glucose','Fructose','Ribose','Sucrose','NEW','12-419','12-289',NULL),
(281222,'chemistry','Which of the following gives positive Fehling solution test?','Glucose','Fehling’s solution is used in addition with Tollen’s reagent to differentiate between reducing and non-reducing sugars.\n Glucose is a reducing sugar and it gives a positive result for Fehling’s test.\n (2001)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Sucrose','Glucose','Fats','Protein','NEW','12-412','12-282',NULL),
(281223,'chemistry','α-D-glucose and β-D-glucose are?','Anomers','Glucose forms a stable hemiacetal between the –CHO group and the –OH group on the 5th carbon. \n In this process, the 1st ‘C’ atom becomes asymmetric giving two isomers which differ in the configuration of the asymmetric carbon. \n These two isomers are called as anomers.\n (2000)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Epimers','Anomers','Enantiomers','Diastereomers','NEW','12-416','12-286',NULL),
(281224,'chemistry','Which of the following is the sweetest sugar?','Fructose','Fructose is the sweetest among all the sugars and is highly soluble in water. \n (1999)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Fructose','Glucose','Sucrose','Maltose','NEW','12-416','12-286',NULL),
(281225,'chemistry','Glucose molecule reacts with X number of molecules of phenyl hydrazine to yield osazone. \n The value of X is ?','Three','Glucose first reacts with phenylhydrazine giving phenylhydrazone. \n Then the adjacent —CHOH group is oxidized by a 2nd phenyl hydrazine molecule and itself is reduced to aniline. \n The resulting carbonyl group reacts with 3rd phenyl hydrazine molecule giving osazone.\n (1998)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Two','One','Four','Three','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(281226,'chemistry','The oxidation of glucose is one of the most important reactions in a living cell. \n What is the number of ATP molecules generated in cells from one molecule of glucose?','38','C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + 38 ATP\n Oxidation of glucose generates 38 ATP molecules.\n (1995)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','28','38','12','18','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(281227,'chemistry','The α-D-glucose and β-D-glucose differ from each other due to difference in carbon atom with respect to its ______.','configuration','These isomers differ only in the orientation (or configuration) at C1 atom.\n (1995)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','number of OH groups','size of hemiacetal ring','conformation','configuration','NEW','12-416','12-286',NULL),
(281228,'chemistry','Chemically considering digestion is basically?','Hydrolysis','Digestion is basically hydrolysis reaction in which large molecules are hydrolysed to give smaller molecules.\n For example when we eat proteins, it will hydrolyse and form amino acids.\n (1994)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Anabolism','Hydrogenation','Hydrolysis','Dehydrogenation.','NEW','12-425','12-295',NULL),
