(281262,'chemistry','Phospholipids are esters of glycerol with?','Two carboxylic acid residues and one phosphate group.','Phospholipids may be regarded as derivatives of glycerol in which two of the hydroxyl groups are esterified with fatty acids while the third is esterified with some derivatives of phosphoric acid.\n (2003)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Three carboxylic acid residues.','Two carboxylic acid residues and one phosphate group.','One carboxylic acid residue and two phosphate groups.','Three phosphate groups.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(281263,'chemistry','The number of molecules of ATP produced in the lipid metabolism of a molecule of palmitic acid is?','130','In the lipid metabolism, a molecule of palmitic acid (C₁₅H₃₁ – COOH) produces 130 adenosine triphosphate molecules (ATP).\n (1998)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','56','36','130','86','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(281264,'chemistry','During the process of digestion, the proteins present in food materials are hydrolysed to amino acids. \n The two enzymes involved in the process are respectively?\n Proteins →Enzyme (A) Polypeptides → Enzyme (B) Amino acids.','Pepsin and trypsin.','When pepsin acts on the protein molecule, it breaks the bonds that hold the protein molecule together, called peptide bonds. \n When these bonds are broken, you get chains of amino acids linked together called polypeptides. \n Trypsin breaks the polypeptides and peptides into amino acids.\n (2006)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Invertase and zymase','Amylase and maltase','Diastase and lipase','Pepsin and trypsin.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(281265,'chemistry','Which of the following structures represents the peptide chain? \n diagram(n2809)','3','In peptide linkage i.e., – CONH – group, the carboxyl group of one amino acid molecule forms an amide by combination with the amino group of the next amino acid molecule with the liberation of water molecule.\n (2004)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-423','12-293','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/281265q.png'),
(281266,'chemistry','The correct corresponding order of names of four aldoses with configuration given below respectively is? \n diagram(n2810)','D-erythrose, D-threose, L-erythrose, L-threose.','The order of the aldoses is D-erythrose, D-threose, L-erythrose, L-threose. \n (NEET-II 2016) \n diagram(ne2812)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','L-erythrose, L-threose, L-erythrose, D-threose','D-threose, D-erythrose, L-threose, L-erythrose','L-erythrose, L-threose, D-erythrose, D-threose','D-erythrose, D-threose, L-erythrose, L-threose.','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/281266q.png'),
(281267,'chemistry','D(+)-glucose reacts with hydroxyl amine and yields an oxime. \n The structure of the oxime would be? \n diagram(n2811)','D','D(+) glucose reacts with hydroxyl amine and yield an oxime.\n In this reaction, the aldehyde group reacts with hydroxylamine to form CH=NOH group known as oxime group.\n (2014)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','12-413','12-283','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/281267q.png'),
(281268,'chemistry','Which statement is incorrect about a peptide bond?','C – N bond length in proteins is longer than usual bond length of N – C bond.','Peptide bond is formed by the reaction of —COOH group of one amino acid with the —NH2 group of another amino acid.\n As some double bond character is found between C – N bond, the bond length of C–N in protein should be smaller than the usual C–N bond.\n (2001)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','C – N bond length in proteins is longer than usual bond length of N – C bond.','Spectroscopic analysis shows planar structure of O–C– NH – group.','C – N bond length in proteins is smaller than usual bond length of C – N bond.','None of the above.','NEW','12-422','12-292',NULL),
(281269,'chemistry','The RBC deficiency is deficiency disease of:','Vitamin B₁₂','Vitamin B₁₂ deficiency causes Pernicious anaemia (RBC deficient in hemoglobin).\n (NEET 2021)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','Vitamin B₁₂','Vitamin B₆','Vitamin B₁','Vitamin B₂','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(281270,'chemistry','The incorrect statement regarding enzymes is','Enzymes are polysaccharides.','Enzymes are complex nitrogenous organic compounds which are produced by living plants and animals. They are protein molecules of high molecular mass. They are not polysaccharides.\n (NEET 2022)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','Enzymes are biocatalysts.','Like chemical catalysts enzymes reduce the activation energy of bio processes.','Enzymes are polysaccharides.','Enzymes are very specific for a particular reaction and substrate.','NEW','12-425','12-295',NULL),
(281271,'chemistry','The reaction of concentrated sulphuric acid with carbohydrates (C12H22O11) is an example of','Dehydration','Dehydration removes water molecule. The sulfuric acid dehydrates the carbohydrate removing water and leaving behind carbon. The hot steam create bubbles in the carbon making a carbon foam.','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Dehydration','Oxidation','Reduction','Sulphonation','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(281272,'chemistry','Which one is not a D-sugar?','B','In D-sugar, the OH group attached to the second last carbon must be on the right hand side. If in the second last carbon the OH group is present on the right side then the compund is D.','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','12-414','12-284',NULL),
(281273,'chemistry','Sucrose can be formed by','α-D-glucopyranose and β-D-fructofuranose','Sucrose = α-D-glucopyranose + β-D-fructofuranose. The Carbon atom next to Oxygen atom will be numbered ar 1.','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','α-D-galactopyranose and β-D-glucopyranose','α-D-glucopyranose and β-D-fructofuranose','β-D-galactopyranose and α-D-fructofuranose','α-D-galactopyranose and β-D-fructopyranose','NEW','12-417','12-287',NULL),
(281274,'chemistry','Sucrose on hydrolysis gives:','D(+) Glucose + D(-) Fructose','Sucrose is composed of α-D- glucopyranose unit and a β-D-fructofuranose unit. These units are joined by α-β-glycosidic linkage between C-1 of the glucose unit and C-2 of the fructose unit.','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','L(+) Glucose + D(+) Fructose','L(-) Glucose + L(-) Fructose','D(+) Glucose + D(-) Fructose','D(+) Glucose + L(-) Fructose','NEW','12-417','12-287',NULL),
(281275,'chemistry','Which is the correct statement among the following?','Starch is a polymer of α-glucose.','Starch is a polymer of α-glucose and consists of two components—Amylose and Amylopectin. Protein is the polymers of α-amino acids. It is made up of different amino acids.','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Starch is a polymer of α-glucose','Amylose is a component of cellulose','Proteins are composed of only one type of amino acids','In the cyclic structure of fructose, these are four carbons and one oxygen atom','NEW','12-418','12-288',NULL),
(281276,'chemistry','In Humans, the main oxygen carrier in the blood is','Haemoglobin','Hemoglobin or haemoglobin, abbreviated Hb or Hgb, is the iron-containing oxygen-transport metalloprotein in the red blood cells of almost all vertebrates as well as the tissues of some invertebrates. Hemoglobin in blood carries oxygen from the lungs or gills to the rest of the body.The normal adult hemoglobin molecule contains two alpha-globulin chains and two beta-globulin chains. Each globulin chain contains an important iron-containing porphyrin compound termed heme. Embedded within the heme compound is an iron atom that is vital in transporting oxygen and carbon dioxide in our blood.The iron contained in hemoglobin is also responsible for the red color of blood. (2017)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Hemocyanin','Proteins','Haemoglobin','Both Microorganisms and Haemoglobin','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(281277,'chemistry','Carbohydrates which can reduce Fehling\'s solution and Tollen\'s reagent are referred as:','Reducing sugar','All those carbohydrates which reduce Fehling’s solution and Tollens’ reagent are referred to as reducing sugars. Examples of reducing sugar is galactose, glucose and xylose. (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 432; Exercise 14.2)"','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Reducing agent','Oxidizing agent','Reducing sugar','Oxidizing sugar','NEW','12-412','12-282',NULL),
(281278,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n \n Assertion : Carbohydrates support the biochemical processes inside the plant cells.\n \n Reason : Glycogen acts as the stored nutrient in plants that are used by the plant when it is unable to perform photosynthesis.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Hydrolysis breaks the glycosidic bond converting sucrose into D(+) Glucose + D(–) Fructose.','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','12-411','12-281',NULL),
(281279,'chemistry','Sort the carbohydrates according to their class.','Ribose-Monosaccharide, Lactose-Disaccharide, Glycogen-Polysaccharides, Galactose-Disaccharide','i. A carbohydrate that cannot be hydrolysed further to give simpler unit of polyhydroxy aldehyde or ketone is called a monosaccharide. For example glucose, ribose and fructose.\n ii. Carbohydrates that yield two monosaccharide units, on hydrolysis, are called disaccharides. i.e, Lactose and Maltose.\n iii. Carbohydrates which yield a large number of monosaccharide units on hydrolysis are called polysaccharides. i.e., Cellulose, Starch and Glycogen.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 432; Exercise 14.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','match','Ribose,Monosaccharide','Lactose,Disaccharide','Glycogen,Polysaccharides','Maltose,Disaccharide','NEW','12-412','12-282',NULL),
(281280,'chemistry','Given below are two statements\n Statement I : Glycogen is identical to starch, with the exception that it is an primary energy source while starch is a storage unit.\n Statement II : Glycogen is a polysaccharide carbohydrate found in animals and humans, whereas starch is a polysaccharide carbohydrate found in plants.\n In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options below.','Statement I is incorrect, and Statement II is correct.','Glycogen is a polysaccharide carbohydrate found in animals and humans, whereas starch is a polysaccharide carbohydrate found in plants. Both glycogen and starch are energy storage units. Starch is a carbohydrate made up of two components: amylose (15%) and amylopectin (85%). However, glycogen is made up of only one component, which has a structure identical to amylopectin. Glycogen is also more branched than amylopectin. Glycogen is sometimes known as animal starch since it is analogous to starch. Hence, statement I is incorrect, and statement II is correct.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 432; Exercise 14.6)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.','Statement I is correct, and Statement II is incorrect.','Statement I is incorrect, and Statement II is correct.','NEW','12-419','12-289',NULL),
(281281,'chemistry','Although starch and cellulose are both glucose polymers, they are not the same. Choose the best explanation.','Starch is a 1-4 linkage of α glucose monomers and Cellulose is formed by 1-4 linkage of β glucose monomers.','Starch consists of two ingredients; amylose and amylopectin. Amylose is a long linear chain of ­α-D-(+)-glucose units joined by glycosidic association C1-C4. Amylopectin is a branched-chain polymer consisting of α‐D-glucose units in which the chain is formed by glycosidic connection C1-C4 and glycosidic connection C1-C6 branches.\n Cellulose, on the other hand, is a straight-chain polysaccharide with β - D-glucose units joined by glycosidic linkage C1-C4. \n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 432; Exercise 14.8)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Starch is a straight chain polymer of glucose and Cellulose is a branched chain polymer of glucose.','Starch is a 1-4 linkage of α glucose monomers and Cellulose is formed by 1-4 linkage of β glucose monomers.','Starch is a 1-4 glycosidic linkage of α glucose monomers and Cellulose is formed by 1-4 peptide linkage of β glucose monomers.','Starch is a 1-4 linkage of α glucose monomers and Cellulose is formed by 1-6 linkage of α glucose monomers.','NEW','12-418','12-288',NULL),
(281282,'chemistry','When glucose is oxidised with bromine water, it produces gluconic acid. This reaction indicates:','An aldehyde group is present.','Bromine water is a mild oxidising agent that selectively oxidises aldehyde to carboxylic acid. It does not oxidise alcohol or ketone. Hence, This reaction indicates the presence of an aldehyde group.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 432; Exercise 14.9) \n diagram(ne2819)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A primary alcoholic group is present.','All the carbon atoms are linked with a single bond.','A ketone group is present.','An aldehyde group is present.','NEW','12-413','12-283',NULL),
(281283,'chemistry','When D-glucose is treated with a strong oxidising agent such as HNO₃, it forms:','Saccharic acid','When D-glucose is exposed to Nitric acid, aldehydic group and primary alcoholic group of glucose are oxidized to carboxylic group, and saccharic acid is produced.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 432; Exercise 14.9) \n diagram(ne2820)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Saccharic acid','Gluconic acid','Benzoic acid','Butanoic acid','NEW','12-414','12-284',NULL),
(281284,'chemistry','∝-helix and β-pleated sheet are present in the:','Secondary structure of proteins','The secondary structure of protein refers to the shape in which a long polypeptide chain can exist. They are found to exist in two different types of structures viz. α-helix and β-pleated sheet structure. \n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 432; Exercise 14.13)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Primary structure of proteins','Secondary structure of proteins','Tertiary structure of proteins','Quaternary structure of proteins','NEW','12-423','12-293',NULL),
(281285,'chemistry','α-helix structure of proteins is stabilised by:','Hydrogen bonding between C=O and –NH– groups of the peptide bond.','α-helix and β-pleated sheet structure arise due to the regular folding of the backbone of the polypeptide chain due to hydrogen bonding between C=O and –NH– groups of the peptide bond.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 432; Exercise 14.14)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Hydrogen bonding between R side chain and carboxyl groups of the amino acids.','Peptide linkage between two amino acid residues.','Dipole-dipole force of the amino acid residues develop attraction between polypeptide chains.','Hydrogen bonding between C=O and –NH– groups of the peptide bond.','NEW','12-423','12-293',NULL),
(281286,'chemistry','Choose the correct option(s) from the following statements that explain "Globular and Fibrous proteins":\n a. Fibrous proteins are spherical in shape, whereas globular proteins are long and narrow.\n b. Fibrous proteins are not soluble in water, but globular proteins are.\n c. Fibrous proteins include keratin, collagen, and elastin, while globular proteins include haemoglobin, myoglobin, and insulin.\n d. Fibrous proteins perform specific biological function in the body, whereas globular proteins serve as a framework to maintain cells in place.','Both b and c','a. Statement (a) is incorrect as fibrous proteins are long and narrow, whereas globular proteins are spherical in shape.\n b. Statement (b) is correct as fibrous proteins are not soluble in water, but globular proteins are.\n c. Statement (c) is incorrect too because fibrous proteins include keratin, collagen, and elastin, while globular proteins include haemoglobin, myoglobin, and insulin.\n d. Statement (d) is incorrect as fibrous proteins are structural in nature, which means they help keep cells in form by acting as scaffolding or a framework. Globular proteins, on the other hand, are functional, which implies they perform a specific biological role in the body.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 432; Exercise 14.15)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both c and d','Both a and d','NEW','12-423','12-293',NULL),
(281287,'chemistry','In ionic form, the biomolecule that exhibits amphoteric behaviour is:','Amino acids','Amino acids comprise a basic amino group that accepts a proton and an acidic COOH group that loses a proton, resulting in the formation of a dipolar ion known as the Zwitter ion. Amino acids exhibit amphoteric behaviour in zwitter ionic state, reacting with both acids and bases.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 432; Exercise 14.16)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Vitamins','Amino acids','Nucleic acids','Oligosaccharides','NEW','12-422','12-292',NULL),
(281288,'chemistry','Mark the false statement about protein denaturation.','The protein\'s primary structure is disrupted.','Protein denaturation causes the following changes: \n a. The hydrogen bonds are broken.\n b. The globules unfold, helix uncoils, and protein loses biological activity.\n c. The secondary and tertiary structures are destroyed.\n d. The primary structure remains intact.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 432; Exercise 14.18)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The protein\'s hydrogen bonds are disrupted.','The globules unfold and helix get uncoiled','Secondary and tertiary structures of protein are destroyed.','The protein\'s primary structure is disrupted.','NEW','12-424','12-294',NULL),
(281289,'chemistry','Vitamin responsible for the coagulation of blood is:','Vitamin K','Vitamin K is responsible for the coagulation of blood.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 432; Exercise 14.19)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Vitamin C','Vitamin D','Vitamin E','Vitamin K','NEW','12-427','12-297',NULL),
(281290,'chemistry','Arrange the vitamins by their source.','Vitamin A-Fish liver oil, Vitamin B₁₂-Curd, Vitamin E-Sunflower oil, Vitamin B₂-Kidney','Sources of vitamin A are fish liver oil, carrots, butter and milk.\n Sources of vitamin B₁₂ are meat, fish, egg and curd.\n Sources of vitamin E are vegetable oils like wheat germ oil, sunflower oil, etc.\n Sources of vitamin B₂ are milk, egg white, liver, kidney.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 432; Exercise 14.20)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','match','Vitamin A,Fish liver oil','Vitamin B₁₂,Curd','Vitamin E,Sunflower oil','Vitamin B₂,Kidney','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(281291,'chemistry','Choose the correct option from the following statements that explain "Nucleic Acids":\n a. There are mainly two types of nucleic acids deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) and ribonucleic acid (RNA).\n b. Nucleic acids are always genetic material for an organism.\n c. Nucleic acids, also known as polynucleotides, are nucleotide long chain polymers.\n d. The sequence of nucleotides in the chain of a nucleic acid is called its secondary structure.','Both a and c','a. Statement (a) is correct as there are mainly two types of nucleic acids deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) and ribonucleic acid (RNA).\n b. Statement (b) is incorrect because nucleic acids are not always genetic material for an organism, they are present as both RNA and DNA.\n c. Statement (c) is correct since nucleic acids are long chain polymers of nucleotides, so they are also called polynucleotides.\n d. Statement (b) is incorrect the sequence of nucleotides in the chain of a nucleic acid is called its primary structure. \n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 432; Exercise 14.21)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both c and d','Both a and c','NEW','12-428','12-298',NULL),
(281292,'chemistry','The main difference between nucleotides and nucleosides is that:','A nucleotide has a phosphoric acid group at the 5′ position, whereas a nucleoside has an OH group.','The main difference lies in their molecular composition as Nucleosides contain only sugar and a base whereas Nucleotides contain sugar, base and a phosphate group as well.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 432; Exercise 14.22) \n diagram(ne2821)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A nucleotide has a aldehyde group 5′ position, whereas a nucleoside has an OH group.','A nucleoside has a ribose sugar and a base, whereas a nucleotide has a glucose and a base.','A nucleotide has a phosphoric acid group at the 5′ position, whereas a nucleoside has an OH group.','A nucleotide has a aldehyde group at 5′ position, whereas a nucleoside has a keto group at 5′ position.','NEW','12-428','12-298',NULL),
(281293,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n \n Assertion : The two strands of DNA are not identical, but rather complementary.\n \n Reason : In the helical structure of DNA, Cytosine forms hydrogen bond with guanine, while adenine forms hydrogen bond with thymine.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The two strands of DNA are held together in the helical structure by hydrogen bonds formed between specific pairings of nucleotides. Cytosine makes a hydrogen bond with guanine, whereas adenine forms a hydrogen bond with thymine. As a result, the two strands are complementary to one another.\n Hence, Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion. \n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 432; Exercise 14.23)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','12-429','12-299',NULL),
(281294,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement.','DNA can only replicate during cell division, whereas RNA can replicate at any time.','a. DNA has the unique property of replication, whereas RNA does not.\n b. DNA is in charge of hereditary material transmission, whereas RNA is in charge of protein synthesis.\n c. DNA has a double-stranded α-helix structure, whereas RNA has a single-stranded α-helix structure.\n d. As pyrimidine bases, DNA contains cytosine and thymine, whereas RNA has cytosine and uracil.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 429; Exercise 14.2.5)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','DNA can only replicate during cell division, whereas RNA can replicate at any time.','DNA is in charge of hereditary material transmission, whereas RNA is in charge of protein synthesis.','DNA has a double-stranded α-helix structure, whereas RNA has a single-stranded α-helix structure.','As pyrimidine bases, DNA contains cytosine and thymine, whereas RNA has cytosine and uracil.','NEW','12-429','12-299',NULL),
(10906,'chemistry','A compound contains 4.07% Hydrogen, 24.27% Carbon and 71.65% Chlorine. Its molar mass is 98.96 g. What is its molecular formula?','C₂H₄Cl₂ .','Moles of Hydrogen = 4.07 g/1.008g = 4.04. \n \n Moles of Carbon = 24.27 g/12.01g= 2.021. \n \n Moles of Chlorine = 71.65g/35.453g= 2.02. \n \n Since 2.021 is smallest value, division by it gives a ratio of 2:1:1 for H:C:Cl. CH₂Cl is, thus, the empirical formula of the above compound. \n \n Molar mass by empirical formula mass = 98.96/49.48g = 2 coefficient.Empirical formula = CH₂Cl, n = 2. \n \n Hence molecular formula is C₂H₄Cl₂.\n \n Both a and b cannot be the answer because option a show empirica; formula while in question molecular formula is asked.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 19 Problem-1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Percentage Composition',NULL,'2','mcq','CH₂Cl','C₂H₄Cl₂ .','CHCl','CH₂Cl₂','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(281295,'chemistry','Choose the correct option(s) from the following statements that explain "Ribonucleic acids":\n a. RNA molecules start protein synthesis on their own.\n b. RNA molecules are divided into three types: messenger RNA (m-RNA), ribosomal RNA (r-RNA), and transfer RNA (t-RNA).\n c. The secondary structure of RNA is permanently folded back on itself.\n d. The sugar moiety of an RNA molecule is β-D-ribose.','Both b and d','a. Proteins are synthesised by various RNA molecules in the cell, but the message for a particular protein\'s synthesis is present in DNA.\n b. RNA molecules are divided into three types: messenger RNA (m-RNA), ribosomal RNA (r-RNA), and transfer RNA (t-RNA).\n c. The secondary structure of RNA is a single-stranded helix that sometimes folds back on itself.\n d. The sugar moiety of an RNA molecule is β-D-ribose.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 429; Exercise 14.5.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both a and b','Both c and d','Both a and c','Both b and d','NEW','12-429','12-299',NULL),
(281296,'chemistry','If a carbohydrate has the L-configuration, it means that:','the -OH group of the chiral carbon is on the left hand side.','According to the diagram, (+) Glyceraldehyde isomer has \'D\' configuration. It means that when its structural formula is written on paper according to particular conventions, the -OH group is on the right hand side. All compounds that can be chemically associated to the D (+) isomer of glyceraldehyde have D-configuration, whereas those that can be correlated to the \'L\' (-) isomer of glyceraldehyde have L-configuration. The -OH group is on the left side of the L (-) isomer.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 417; Paragraph 14.1.2.1 ) \n diagram(ne2818)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','the -OH group of the chiral carbon is on the right hand side.','the -OH group of the chiral carbon is on the left hand side.','the -OH group of the chiral carbon is pointing downwards.','the -OH group of the chiral carbon is pointing upwards.','NEW','12-417','12-287',NULL),
(281297,'chemistry','Rather than acting as simple amines or carboxylic acids, amino acids behave like salts. This behaviour is caused by:','The presence of both acidic (carboxyl) and basic (amino) groups in the same molecule.','Instead of being simple amines or carboxylic acids, amino acids behave like salts. The presence of both acidic (carboxyl) and basic (amino) groups in the same molecule causes this behaviour. In aqueous solution, the carboxyl group can lose a proton and the amino group can accept a proton, resulting in zwitter ion, a dipolar ion. This is neutral, yet it contains both positive and negative charges. Amino acids exhibit amphoteric behaviour in zwitter ionic state, reacting with both acids and bases.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 422; Paragraph 14.2.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The R side chain of amino acids contribute to the nature of amino acids.','The presence of both acidic (carboxyl) and basic (amino) groups in the same molecule.','The carboxyl group loses its -OH resulting in the amphoteric behaviour of amino acids.','The negative amino group causes the central carbon to lose hydrogen.','NEW','12-422','12-292',NULL),
(281298,'chemistry','Choose the correct option(s) from the following statements that explain "Peptide Linkage":\n a. Peptide linkage is an amide formed between –COOH group and –NH₂ group.\n b. A tripeptide contains four amino acids linked by three peptide linkages.\n c. A polypeptide with more than ten amino acid residues, having molecular mass higher than 100u is called a protein.\n d. Insulin, a polypeptide of 51 amino acids, is an example of a protein.','Both a and d','a. Statement (a) is correct as the peptide linkage is an amide formed between –COOH group and –NH₂ group.\n b. Statement (b) is incorrect because a tripeptide contains three amino acids linked by two peptide linkages. \n c. Statement (c) is incorrect too as a polypeptide with more than hundred amino acid residues, having molecular mass higher than 10,000u is called a protein.\n d. Statement (d) is correct because if polypeptides with fewer amino acids will be called proteins if they ordinarily have a well defined conformation of a protein such as insulin which contains 51 amino acids.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 422; Paragraph 14.2.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both c and d','Both a and d','NEW','12-422','12-292',NULL),
(281299,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n \n Assertion: Glucose does not produce bisulphite addition products when reacting with sodium hydrogen sulphite, whereas all other aldehydes do.\n \n Reason: The glucose -CHO group reacts with the -OH group at C-5 to form a cyclic hemiacetal.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Despite having the aldehyde group, glucose does not give Schiff’s test and it does not form the hydrogensulphite addition product with NaHSO₃. This is due to the fact that this reaction occurs in the presence of a free aldehyde group, but there is no free -CHO group in the structure of glucose. The glucose -CHO group reacts with the -OH group at C-5 to form a cyclic hemiacetal.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 415; Paragraph 14.1.2.1 )','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','12-415','12-285',NULL),
(281300,'chemistry','Hydrolysis of sucrose is an inversion reaction because:','when sucrose is hydrolyzed, the rotational sign shifts from dextro (+) to laevo (-).','Sucrose is dextrorotatory but after hydrolysis gives dextrorotatory glucose and laevorotatory fructose. Since the laevorotation of fructose (–92.4°) is more than dextrorotation of glucose (+ 52.5°), the mixture is laevorotatory. Thus, hydrolysis of sucrose brings about a change in the sign of rotation, from dextro (+) to laevo (–) and the reaction is inversion.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 417; Paragraph 14.1.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','when sucrose is hydrolyzed, the rotational sign shifts from laevo (-) to dextro (+).','when sucrose is hydrolyzed, the rotational sign shifts from dextro (+) to laevo (-).','when sucrose is hydrolyzed, the glycosidic bond is inverted.','when sucrose is hydrolyzed, the rotational sign remains unchanged.','NEW','12-417','12-287',NULL),
(281310,'chemistry','Which of the following is an example of protein denaturation?','Curdling of milk','Curdling of milk is an example of protein denaturation caused by the production of lactic acid by bacterial activity.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 424; Paragraph 14.2.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Rancidification of butter','Curdling of milk','Fruit decay','Production of bread','NEW','12-424','12-294',NULL),
(10907,'chemistry','A welding fuel gas contains carbon and hydrogen only. Burning a small sample of it in oxygen gives 3.38 g carbon dioxide, 0.690 g of water and no other products. A volume of 10.0 L (measured at STP) of this welding gas is found to weigh 11.6 g. Calculate the empirical formula.','CH','Thus, option (a) is correct.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 19 Paragraph-7) diagram(ne0118)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Percentage Composition',NULL,'2','mcq','CH','CH₂','C₂H','C₃H₄','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(281311,'chemistry','Select the correct option(s) from the following statements about " Biomolecules":\n a. Plants are the primary producers of carbohydrates.\n b. Proteins occur in every part of the body.\n c. Hormones are necessary for the human body and cannot be obtained artificially.\n d. Fat solutble vitamins cannot be synthesized by human body.','Both a and b','a. Statement (a) is correct as plants are the primary producers of carbohydrates.\n b. Statement (b) is also correct as proteins occur in every part of the body.\n c. Statement (c) is incorrect because hormones are necessary for the human body but they can be produced and obtained artificially.\n d. Statement (d) is also incorrect because most of the vitamins cannot be synthesized by human body.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Unit 14)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both c and d','Both a and d','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(281312,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n \n Assertion: The tertiary structure of proteins represents the overall folding of the polypeptide chains, which is additional folding of the secondary structure.\n \n Reason: The tertiary structure resembles the pleated folds of drapery.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','The tertiary structure of proteins represents the overall folding of the polypeptide chains, which is additional folding of the secondary structure. \n All peptide chains in a β-pleated sheet structure are stretched to nearly maximum extension and then arranged side by side, bound together by intermolecular hydrogen interactions. The structure resembles the pleated folds of drapery and therefore is known as β-pleated sheet.\n Hence, The assertion is true, but the reason is false.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 423; Paragraph 14.2.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','12-423','12-293',NULL),
(281421,'chemistry','Amino acids are classified as acidic, basic or neutral depending upon the relative number of amino and carboxyl groups in their molecule. Which of the following are acidic? Refer diagram(n2817)','b,d','The amino acids that are classified as acidic are those that have an additional carboxyl group in their structure, besides the one present in the amino acid structure. This additional carboxyl group gives the amino acid a net negative charge at physiological pH.\n\nThe acidic amino acids are aspartic acid (Asp, D) and glutamic acid (Glu, E). Both of these amino acids have an additional carboxyl group in their side chain.','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','b,d','NEW','12-421','12-291','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/281421q.png'),
(281422,'chemistry','Lysine is : \n\na. alpha amino acids\nb. basic amino acid\nc. amino acid synthesised in body\nd. beta-amino acids','a,b','Lysine is a basic amino acid. This means it has a positively charged amino group in its structure that can accept a proton, giving it an overall positive charge at physiological pH.\n\nLysine is also an alpha-amino acid, meaning its amino group is attached to the alpha carbon atom in its backbone structure.\n\nLysine is an essential amino acid, which means it cannot be synthesized by the body and must be obtained through the diet.','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','b,d','NEW','12-421','12-291',NULL),
(281423,'chemistry','Which of the following monosaccharides are present as five membered cyclic structure (furanose structure)?\n\na. Ribose\nb. Glucose \nc. Fructose\nd. Galactose','a,c','Ribose and fructose has five membered cyclic furanose structure because it include 5 carbon atom','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','b,d','NEW','12-416','12-286',NULL),
(281424,'chemistry','In fibrous proteins, polypeptide chains are held together by ......... .\n\na. van der Waals forces\nb. disulphide linkage \nc. electrostatic forces of attraction\nd. hydrogen bonds','b,d','"When the polypeptide chains run parallel and are held together by hydrogen and disulphide bonds, then fibre– like structure is formed.\nSuch proteins are generally insoluble in water. Some common examples are keratin (present in hair, wool, silk) and myosin (present in muscles), etc.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 422; Paragraph no. 14.2.3 (a))"','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','b,d','NEW','12-422','12-292',NULL),
(281425,'chemistry','Which of the following are purine bases?\n\na. Guanine\nb. Adenine\nc. Thymine\nd. Uracil','a,b','Adenine and guanine are purines with two connected ring structures. Uracil has 4 nitrogen atoms in its ring while 3 Cytosine has nitrogen atom in its ring.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 428; Paragraph 14.5.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','b,d','NEW','12-428','12-298',NULL),
(281426,'chemistry','Which of the following terms are correct about enzyme?\n\na. Proteins\nb. Dinucleotides\nc. Nucleic acids\nd. Biocatalysts','a,d','Enzymes are proteins that act as biological catalysts by accelerating chemical reactions.','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','a,d','a,c','b,d','NEW','12-425','12-295',NULL),
(281427,'chemistry','Assertion (A): D (+) - Glucose is dextrorotatory in nature.\n\nReason (R) :‘D’ represents its dextrorotatory nature.','Assertion is true but reason is false','The letter \'D\' actually represents the stereochemistry or configuration of the molecule at the asymmetric carbon farthest from the carbonyl group. In the case of D (+) - Glucose, this carbon is the fifth carbon (C-5) atom, which has the -OH group pointing to the right in its Fischer projection.','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','12-414','12-284',NULL),
(281428,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Vitamin D can be stored in our body.\n\nReason (R): Vitamin D is fat soluble vitamin.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','The reason "Vitamin D is a fat-soluble vitamin" is also true. Because vitamin D is a fat-soluble vitamin, it can be stored in fatty tissues in the body, as well as in the liver, where it is converted to its active form.','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(11001,'chemistry','Stoichiometry, deals with the calculation of _________ of the reactants and products in a chemical reaction.','Masses and Volumes','Stoichiometry, thus, deals with the calculation of masses (sometimes volumes also) of the reactants and the products involved in a chemical reaction.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 20 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Stoichiometry And Stoichiometric Calculations',NULL,'2','mcq','Masses and Volumes','Volumes and Moles','Moles and Masses','Moles','NEW','11-020','11-020',NULL),
(281429,'chemistry','Assertion (A) β-glycosidic linkage is present in maltose.\n\nReason (R) Maltose is composed of two glucose units in which C - 1 of one glucose unit is linked to C - 4 of another glucose unit','Assertion is false but reason is true','Maltose, another disaccharide, is composed of two a-D-glucose units in which C1 of one glucose is linked to C4 of another glucose unit. The free aldehyde group can be produced at C1 of second glucose in solution and it shows reducing properties so it is a reducing sugar. Hydrolysis of maltose gives two molecules of d-glucose.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 417; Paragraph no. 14.1.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','12-417','12-287',NULL),
(281430,'chemistry','Assertion (A) All naturally occurring a-aminoacids except glycine are optically active.\n\nReason (R) Most naturally occurring amino acids have L-configuration','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','The assertion states that all naturally occurring α-amino acids, except glycine, are optically active. This means that they have a chiral center and can rotate the plane of polarized light passing through them.\n\nMost naturally occurring amino acids have L-configuration. This means that their chiral carbon atoms are arranged in a left-handed orientation. Amino acids with L-configuration are commonly found in proteins, while those with D-configuration are less commonly found in proteins.','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','12-422','12-292',NULL),
(281431,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Glycine must be taken through diet.\n\nReason (R) It is an essential amino acid.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Glycine is a non-essential amino acid, which means that it can be synthesized by the human body and does not need to be obtained through the diet.','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(281432,'chemistry','Assertion (A) In presence of enzyme, substrate molecule can be attacked by the reagent effectively.\n\nReason (R) Active sites of enzymes hold the substrate molecule in a suitable position.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Enzymes are biological catalysts that increase the rate of chemical reactions by lowering the activation energy required for the reaction to occur. Enzymes achieve this by binding to substrate molecules at specific sites on their surface called active sites. The active site provides a specific environment that is optimal for the reaction to occur. This can involve stabilizing the transition state of the reaction, providing a suitable orientation for the substrate molecules to react, and even participating in the chemical reaction itself.\n\nThe active sites of enzymes hold the substrate molecule in a suitable position to facilitate the reaction. This ensures that the reaction occurs with high specificity and efficiency. Once the reaction is complete, the products are released from the active site, and the enzyme is ready to catalyze another reaction.\n\nTherefore, both the assertion and the reason are true, and they are related to each other.','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-425','12-295',NULL),
(281514,'chemistry','The pyrimidine bases present in DNA are','cytosine and thymine','DNA contains four bases viz. adenine (A), guanine (G), cytosine (C) and thymine (T). RNA also contains four bases, the first three bases are same as in DNA but the fourth one is uracil (U).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 428; Paragraph 14.5.1) JEE Main 2014','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','cytosine and adenine','cytosine and guanine','cytosine and thymine','cytosine and uracil','NEW','12-428','12-298',NULL),
(281515,'chemistry','In both DNA and RNA, heterocyclic base and phosphate ester linkage are at','C₅\' and C₁\' respectively of the sugar molecule','In both DNA and RNA, the heterocyclic base and phosphate ester linkage are attached to different carbon atoms of the sugar molecule. In RNA, the base is attached to the 1\' carbon, while the phosphate is attached to the 5\' carbon. In DNA, the base is attached to the 1\' carbon, while the phosphate is attached to the 5\' carbon.','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','C₁\' and C₅\' respectively of the sugar molecule','C₂\' and C₅\' respectively of the sugar molecule','C₅\' and C₂\' respectively of the sugar molecule','C₅\' and C₁\' respectively of the sugar molecule','NEW','12-428','12-298',NULL),
(281516,'chemistry','Insulin production and its action in human body are responsible for the level of diabetes. This compound belongs to which of the following categories?','A hormone','Insulin is a hormone that is responsible for regulating blood sugar levels in the human body. It is produced by beta cells in the pancreas and released into the bloodstream in response to rising blood glucose levels. Insulin helps the body\'s cells to take up glucose from the bloodstream and use it for energy or store it for later use.','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','A coenzyme','A hormone','An enzyme','An antibiotic','NEW','12-430','12-300',NULL),
(281517,'chemistry','Which base is present in RNA but not in DNA?','Uracil','DNA contains four bases viz. adenine (A), guanine (G), cytosine (C) and thymine (T). RNA also contains four bases, the first three bases are same as in DNA but the fourth one is uracil (U).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 428; Paragraph 14.5.1) [AIEEE 2004]','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','Uracil','Cytosine','Guanine','Thymine','NEW','12-428','12-298',NULL),
(281518,'chemistry','The reason for double helical structure of DNA is operation of','hydrogen bonding','The double helical structure of DNA is due to hydrogen bonds between complementary nitrogenous bases on the two strands of DNA. The hydrogen bonds hold the two strands together, forming a stable structure that allows for accurate replication and transmission of genetic information. The double helix also helps to protect the genetic information from damage. [AIEEE 2003]','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','van der Waals\' forces','dipole-dipole interaction','hydrogen bonding','electrostatic attractions','NEW','12-423','12-293',NULL),
(281519,'chemistry','Complete hydrolysis of cellulose gives','D-glucose','Complete hydrolysis of cellulose results in the release of many glucose molecules, which can then be used as an energy source by the organism. Therefore, the end product of complete hydrolysis of cellulose is D-glucose.','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','D-fructose','D-ribose','D-glucose','L-glucose','NEW','12-419','12-289',NULL),
(281520,'chemistry','RNA contains','ribose sugar and uracil','RNA contains ribose sugar and uracil. It consists of cytosine and uracil as pyrimidine and guanine and adenine as purine bases.','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','ribose sugar and thymine','ribose sugar and uracil','deoxyribose sugar and uracil','deoxyribose sugar and thymine','NEW','12-428','12-298',NULL),
(110108,'chemistry','Non-metals readily form','Anions','As we move along period atomic size decrease due to increase of\n atomic No.Shell No.also not change along period,so the hold of \n nucleus on outermost electrons increase, so any incoming \n electron will feel attraction and readily added to form anion.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 316; Paragraph No.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Cations','Anions','Radical','ions','NEW','11-316',NULL,NULL),
(110114,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R)\n (A)-In NO₃⁻, PO₄³⁻, N and P both carry 5+ oxidation state.\n (R)-Three-coordination with pi-bond involving one nitrogen p-orbital, in PO₄³⁻ four cooordination involving s,p,d orbitals contributing to the pi bond','1-Both assertion and reason are true ,and reason is correct explanation of assertion','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 317; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Introduction',NULL,'3','mcq','1-Both assertion and reason are true ,and reason is correct explanation of assertion','2-Both assertion and reason are true ,and reason is not correct explanation of assertion','3-Assertion is true but reason is false','4-Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-317',NULL,NULL),
(110115,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R)\n (A)-Polarization of cations decrease along group.\n (R)-d or f-subshell involve ,inert pair effect prevents ns electrons to be removed','2-Both assertion and reason are true ,but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 317; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Introduction',NULL,'3','mcq','1-Both assertion and reason are true ,and reason is correct explanation of assertion','2-Both assertion and reason are true ,but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','3-Assertion is true but reason is false','4-Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-317',NULL,NULL),
(110809,'chemistry','The tendency to behave as lewis acid decrease as we move along group of B-family due to','increase in size','down the group size increase as well as screening effect prevents the M\n to behave as lewis acid ,more easy a metal can accept electron more \n the strength of acid. \n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 319; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','increase in size','increase in electronegativity','incraese in atomic mass','increase in electron affinity','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
(110810,'chemistry','AlCl₃ form dimer to complete its','both a and b','Boron being electrin deficient tends to complete its octet by making bridge\n or acting as lewis acid.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 319; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Octet','Valence shell','both a and b','none','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
(110811,'chemistry','The geometry of BCl₃.NH₃ is','Tetrahedral','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 319; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Pyramidal','trigonal bipyramidal','Tetrahedral','Trigonal planar','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
(110812,'chemistry','which one is more covalent','AlCl₃','More the polarization power of a cation,more will be the covalent character, polarization power of cation is directly linked with oxidation state and inversly linked with size of cation, as we move down size increase so polarization decrease and covalent character is also decrease, more-\n over heavier members like to exist in 1+ oxidation state \n \n Al is in 3+ oxidation state, more oxidation state of a cation more will be the abilty to distort the electron cloud\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 319; Paragraph No.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','TlCl₃','AlCl₃','InCl₃','GaCl₃','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
(110813,'chemistry','Trichlorides of B-Family on hydrolysis form','[M(OH)₄]⁻','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 319; Paragraph No.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','[M(OH)₄]⁻','[M(OH)₄]','[M(OH)₄]⁺','[M₂(OH)₈]²⁻','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
