(111305,'chemistry','The aqueous solution of boraqx is basic due to formation of','NaOH','when borax is disso;lved in water ,two species form ,one is orthoboric\n acid and second is NaOH ,orthoboric acid is highly weak acid ,while \n NaOH is strong base ,that\'s why the solution is basic.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 320; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13- elements-Borax',NULL,'2','mcq','NaOH','HCl','H₂O','H₃BO₃','NEW','11-320',NULL,NULL),
(111308,'chemistry','CoO on heating with borax on a loop of platimum wire shows','Blue bead','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 320; Paragraph 7)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13- elements-Borax',NULL,'1','mcq','Green bead','Yellow bead','Red bead','Blue bead','NEW','11-320',NULL,NULL),
(111405,'chemistry','Boric acid is weak due to','it hardly release H⁺','weak acid is unable to donate protons redadily, strength of acid depends\n on ease to donation of proton and stabilty of anion(form when proton \n leaves) more stable that anion more will be the strength of acid,in case of\n boric acid after removal of proton negative charged is localized on O\n so available for reaction means unstable,so its acid is weak, stroger the \n acid weal will be its conjugate base and vice versa.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 321; Problem No.11.4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13- elements-Boric acid',NULL,'2','mcq','it hardly release H⁺','it readily release H⁺','its conjugate base is stabilized','it give OH⁺','NEW','11-321',NULL,NULL),
