(112302,'chemistry','The common oxidation states exibited by C-family are','4+,2+','4+ when all electrons are involved in bonding,2+ when only p electrons\n are participating,2+ is more common in heavier mebers ,due to inert\n pair effect.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 324; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 14-elements-Oxidation state and trends in chemical reactivity',NULL,'2','mcq','4+,2+','4+,2+,1+','2+,1+','1+,3+,4+','NEW','11-324',NULL,NULL),
(112303,'chemistry','Compounds in 4+ oxidation state are generally','Covalent','Ionization enthalpy for 4+ oxidation state is very high, that\'s why difficult\n to remove 4th electron, so elements like to share this electron and make\n covalent bond.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 324; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 14-elements-Oxidation state and trends in chemical reactivity',NULL,'2','mcq','Ionic','Covalent','Neutral','amphoteric','NEW','11-324',NULL,NULL),
(112304,'chemistry','In heavier members ,the tendency to show 2+ oxidation state increase in the sequence','Ge<Sn<Pb','Inert pair effect icrease from Ge to Pb, which makes ns electrons to be\n less exposed to involve in bonding.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 324; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 14-elements-Oxidation state and trends in chemical reactivity',NULL,'2','mcq','Sn>Ge<Pb','Pb<Sn<Ge','Pb>Ge>Sn','Ge<Sn<Pb','NEW','11-324',NULL,NULL),
(112305,'chemistry','Carbon and Silicon mostly show','4+','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 324; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 14-elements-Oxidation state and trends in chemical reactivity',NULL,'1','mcq','4+','2+','3+','4+,2+','NEW','11-324',NULL,NULL),
