(113915,'chemistry','match the list 1 and 2 : list 1(compunds) a) borox b) kernite c) orthoboric acid d) borax bead  LIST 2 ( MOLECULAR FORMULA) : 1) NaBO2 + B2O3 2) Na2B4O7.4H2O 3) H3BO3 4) Na2B4O7.10H2O  Chose the correct answer from the given below :','a-4, b-2, c-3,d-1','Hint: Kernite is Na2B4O7·4H2O.\n\n1. Borax is Na2B4O7⋅10H2O. It is a white crystalline solid.  Borax dissolves in water to give an alkaline solution. The reaction is as follows:\n\nN\na\n2\n \nB\n4\nO\n7\n+\n7\nH\n2\nO\n→\n2\nN\na\nO\nH\n+\n4\nH\n3\nB\nO\n3\n(\nO\nr\nt\nh\no\nb\no\nr\ni\nc\n \na\nc\ni\nd\n)\n\n\n2. Kernite is Na2B4O7·4H2O.\n\n3. Orthoboric acid is H3BO3. It is a white crystalline solid, with soapy touch. It is sparingly soluble in water but highly soluble in hot water. \n\n4. Borax bead is a mixture of Sodium Meta Borate (NaBO2​) and Boric Oxide (B2​O3​). \n\nHence, first option is the correct answer.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a-4, b-2, c-3,d-1','a-2,b-4,c-1,d-3','a-3, b-3,c-4,d-4','a-1,b-1,c-2,d-2','NEW','11-320',NULL,NULL),
(113916,'chemistry','Which of the following is correct statement?','Ga+ is more stable than Al+','Hint: Gallium shows an inert pair effect but aluminum does not\n\nThe group 13th element shows a +1 and +3 oxidation state. Down the group, due to the poor shielding effect of intervening d and f orbitals, the increased effective nuclear charge holds ns electrons tightly (responsible for inert pair effect) and thereby, restricting their participation in bonding.\n\nAs a result of this, the only p-orbital electron may be involved in bonding. The relative stability of the +1 oxidation state progressively increases for heavier elements. The stability order of the +1 oxidation state is as follows:\n\nAl < Ga < In <Tl\n\nHence, option third is the correct answer.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Ga+ is more stable than In+','Al+ is more stable than In+','Ga+ is more stable than Al+','Al+ is more stable than Ga+','NEW','11-315',NULL,NULL),
(113917,'chemistry','identify correct relationship of stability from the options provided:','TlI>TlI3','Thermodynamic stability occurs when a system is in its lowest energy state, or in chemical equilibrium with its environment.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','TlI>TlI3','TlCl > TlCl3','InI3>InI','AlCl> AlCl3','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
(113918,'chemistry','Aluminium Chloride in acidfied aqueous solution forms complex A. the formula of A and hybridization state of Al in A is:','[ Al(H2O)6+3] ,sp3 d2','Hint: Hexaaquaaluminium(III) is the product (A)\n\nAluminium chloride in acidified aqueous solution forms a complex \'A. Complex A is Hexaaquaaluminium(III). The\n\ncoordination number of Al is 6 hence, its hybridization is sp3d2. The formula of Hexaaquaaluminium(III) is [Al(H2O)6]+3.\n\nHence, option one is the correct answer.\n\n[Al(H2O)6]+3=sp3d2','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','[ Al(H2O)6+3] ,sp3 d2','[ Al( H2O)4 +3] ,sp3','[ Al( H2O)4 +3] ,dsp2','[ Al( H2O) +3] ,d2 sp3','NEW','11-316',NULL,NULL),
(113919,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is incorrect?','Aluminium reacts with excess NaOH to give Al(OH)3','Hint: Aluminium form complex with excess of NaOH.\n\nAluminium dissolves in excess NaOH to liberate hydrogen and form meta aluminate\n\n2Al + 2NaOH + 6H2O → 2 Na[Al(OH)]4  + 3H2\n\nSo, Aluminium reacts with excess NaOH not to give Al(OH)3 but [Al(OH)4]- \n\nThe other statements are correct statements. \n\na. NaHCO3 on heating gives Na2CO3. The reaction is as follows: \n\nNaHCO3 → NaCO3 + CO2 + H2O\n\nb. Pure sodium metal dissolves in liquid ammonia to give a blue solution that is conducting in nature. \nThe blue colour of the solution is due to the ammoniated electron which absorbs energy in the visible region of light and thus imparts a blue colour to the solution. \n\nc. NaOH reacts with glass to give sodium silicate. The reaction is as follows:\n\nSiO2(s) + 2NaOH(aq) → Na2SiO3(aq) + H2O(l)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','NaHCO3 on heating gives Na2CO3','Pure sodium metal dissolves in  liquid ammonia to giveblue solution.','NaOH reacts with glass to give sodium silicate','Aluminium reacts with excess NaOH to give Al(OH)3','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
(113920,'chemistry','a compound that is least likely to behave as a lewis base:','BF3','Hint: Electron deficient compound cannot behave as lewis base.\n\nIn BF3, there is a p\nπ\n- p\nπ\n back bonding present between the fully-filled unutilized 2p orbitals of F\n\nand vacant 2p orbitals of boron. This renders the molecule less electron deficient. Therefore, it\n\nbehaves as a weak Lewis acid. Hence, the correct option is the second option.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','NH3','BF3','OH-','H2O','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(113921,'chemistry','Na2B4O7 into X + NaBO2 ,in this reaction the product X is :','B2O3','Hint: Borax bead is formed as a product.\n\nExplanation: \n\nBorax on heating loses water of crystallisation and swells to form a puffy mass which further heating melts to give a clear liquid which solidifies to a transparent glass like bead consist of sodium metaborate NaBO2 and boric anhydride B2O3.\n\nBorax bead test is used to identify the nature of cation present in solution by observing the colour of the bead. The color and bead of copper formed during its borax bead test in a hot oxidizing flame is Green when hot and blue when cold.\n\nThe reaction is as follows: \n\nN\na\n2\nB\n4\nO\n7\n heat \n \n \n−−−→\nB\n2\nO\n3\n+\nN\na\nB\nO\n2\n\n\nSo, option 2 is the correct answer.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','H3BO3','B2O3','Na2B2O5','NaB3O5','NEW','11-320',NULL,NULL),
(113922,'chemistry','in borax bead test, which compound is formed :','metaborate','HINT: Metaborate form in Borax bead test.\n\nExplanation: \nThe borax bead test is a type of test which is carried out in order to determine the presence of specific metals particularly in salts or minerals.\n\nIn this test, the oxides of the metal which are present in the minerals react with the borax bead in order to form metaborates.\n\nThe reaction involved is : \nN\na\n2\nB\n4\nO\n7\n.\n10\nH\n2\nO\nΔ\n→\nN\na\n2\nB\n4\nO\n7\nΔ\n→\nN\na\nB\nO\n2\n+\nB\n2\nO\n3','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','orthoborate','metaborate','double oxide','tetraborate','NEW','11-320',NULL,NULL),
(113923,'chemistry','which of the following is not correct about diborane:','both boron atoms are sp2 hybridized','Hint: The hybridization of B in B2H6 is sp3.\n\nThe structure of B2H6 is as follows:\n\n\nThe boron forms four sigma bonds hence hybridization is sp3 , not sp2. \n\nIt has two banana bonds and the banana bond is 3-center-2-electrons bond.\nThe four terminal hydrogen atoms and the two boron atoms lie in one plane. Above and below this plane, there are two bridging hydrogen atoms.\n\nHence, option one is the answer.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','both boron atoms are sp2 hybridized','there are two 3-centered two electron bonds','four terminal B-H bond are two centered two electron bonds','four terminal hydrogen atoms and two boron atoms lie in one plane','NEW','11-321',NULL,NULL),
(113924,'chemistry','PbO2→ PbO   ,  ΔG208 < 0    SnO2 →   SnO  ΔG298 > 0  the most probable oxidation state of Pb  and Sn will be :','Pb+4, Sn+2','Hint: If  \nΔ\nG\n value is less than zero then the reaction is spontaneous\n\nThe reaction is as follows:\n\nPbO\n2\n \n→\n \nPbO\n                     \nΔ\nG\n298\n \n<\n0\n\nSnO\n2\n→\nSnO\n                       \nΔ\nG\n298\n \n>\n \n0\nThe \nΔ\nG\n value for \nPbO\n2\n \n→\n \nPbO\n is negative hence, the reaction is spontaneous. It indicates that Pb4+ is less stable than Pb2+.\n\nThe \nΔ\nG\n value for \nSnO\n2\n→\nSnO\n is negative hence, the reaction is non-spontaneous. It indicates that Sn4+ is more stable than Sn2+.\n\nHence, option 4th is the correct answer.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Pb +4, Sn+4','Pb +4 , Sn+2','Pb+2, Sn+2','Pb+4, Sn+2','NEW','11-324',NULL,NULL),
(113925,'chemistry','The chain Length of silicon can be controlled by adding _______','(CH3)3SiCl','Hint: Chlorotrimethylsilane is used to control chain length\n\nThe silicones are a group of organosilicon polymers. They have (-R2SiO-) as a repeating unit. The starting materials for the manufacture of silicones are alkyl or aryl substituted silicon chlorides, RnSiCl(4–n), where R is the alkyl or aryl group. \n\nThe chain length of the polymer can be controlled by adding (CH3)3SiCl which blocks the ends as shown below :','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CH3SiCl3','SiCl4','(CH3)2 SiCl2','(CH3)3SiCl','NEW','11-329',NULL,NULL),
(113926,'chemistry','choose the correct statement :','diamond is sp3 hybridized and graphite is sp2 hybridized','Hint: In diamonds, carbon atoms are arranged in a tetrahedral manner. \n\n1. Graphite is soft and slippery. For this reason, graphite is used as a dry lubricant in machines running at high temperatures. \nThus, statement one is incorrect.\n\n2. Diamond is an example of covalent or network Solids. In diamond, each carbon atom undergoes sp3 hybridization and is linked to four other carbon atoms by using hybridized orbitals in a tetrahedral fashion. \nThe structure extends in space and produces a rigid three-dimensional network of carbon atoms.\nHence, the second statement is wrong. \n\n3. Diamond and graphite both are allotropes of carbon hence covalent compounds not ionic. Hence, statement third is incorrect.\n\n4. In diamond each carbon atom undergoes sp3 hybridization and is linked to four other carbon atoms by using hybridized orbitals in a tetrahedral fashion.  \nGraphite has a layered structure. Layers are held by van der Waals forces. Each layer is composed of planar hexagonal rings of carbon atoms. \nEach carbon atom in a hexagonal ring undergoes sp2 hybridization and makes three sigma bonds with three neighboring carbon atoms.\n\nHence statement fourth is correct.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','both diamond and graphite are used as dry lubricants .','diamond and graphite have a two dimentional network','diamond is covalent and graphite is ionic','diamond is sp3 hybridized and graphite is sp2 hybridized','NEW','11-325',NULL,NULL),
(113927,'chemistry','match the allotropes of carbon given in list 1 and their characteristic given in list 2. LIST 1 : a)coke b) diamond c) fullerene d)graphite LIST 2: 1) carbon atoms are sp3 hybridized 2)used as a dry lubricant 3)used as areducing agent 4)cage like molecules .  choose the correct option from below :','a)2 b)3 c)3 d) 2c','Hint: Fullerenes are cage-like molecules.\n\n1. Coke is used as a fuel and largely as a reducing agent in metallurgy. \n2. Diamond has a crystalline lattice. In diamond, each carbon atom undergoes sp3 hybridization and is linked to four other carbon atoms by using hybridized orbitals in a tetrahedral fashion. \n3. Fullerenes are the only pure form of carbon because they have smooth structures without having ‘dangling’ bonds. Fullerenes are cage-like molecules.\n4. Graphite cleaves easily between the layers and, therefore, is very soft and slippery. For this reason, graphite is used as a dry lubricant in machines running at high temperatures, where oil cannot be used as a lubricant. \n\nThus, option 4th is the correct answer.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a)3 b)2 c)4 d)3','a)4 b)4 c)1 d)1','a)1 b)2 c)2 d)4','a)2 b)3 c)3 d) 2c','NEW','11-325',NULL,NULL),
(113928,'chemistry','incorrect statement among the following is :','feldsspars are not alluminosilicates','Feldspars are classified into two main groups based on their chemical composition: potassium feldspars and plagioclase feldspars.\n\nPotassium Feldspars (K-feldspars): These include minerals like orthoclase and microcline. They are primarily composed of potassium (K), aluminum (Al), and silicon (Si), along with oxygen.\n\nPlagioclase Feldspars: These feldspars include minerals such as albite, oligoclase, and labradorite. Unlike the potassium feldspars, plagioclase feldspars contain a significant amount of sodium (Na) and calcium (Ca) in addition to aluminum and silicon.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','quartz is extensively used as piezoelectric material','silicones are a group of organosillicon polymers,which have (-R2SiO- ) as a repeating unit','Basic structural unit in silicates is the SiO₄ tetrahedron.','feldsspars are not alluminosilicates','NEW','11-330',NULL,NULL),
(113929,'chemistry','silicate ,among the following, that has only one oxygen atom of (SiO4)- shared with the next unit is :','pyrosilicate','Hint: The formula of pyrosilicate is Si2O76- \n\nPyro silicate (or Soro silicate or disilicate) contain Si2O76- ions which are formed by joining two tetrahedral SiO44- which share one oxygen atom at one corner (one oxygen is removed while joining). \n\nOnly oxygen atom is shared in pyrosilicate. The structure is as follows:','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','sheetsilicate','pyrosilicate','three dimensional silicate','linear chain silicate','NEW','11-330',NULL,NULL),
(113930,'chemistry','incorrect statement among the following about zeolites is :','some of the SiO4 ions are replaced by (AlO4)-5 and (AlO6)-9 ions in zeolites','HINT: Zeolites have 3-D open structure.\n\nExplanation: \n\nZeolites are crystalline solids structures made of silicon, aluminum and oxygen that form a framework with cavities and channels inside where cations, water and/or small molecules may reside.\n\nZeolites are aluminosilicates having three dimensional open structure in which four or six membered rings predominates. Thus, due to open chain structure, they have cavities and can take up water and other small molecules.\n\nThey are often also referred to as molecular sieves. Zeolites are the most important inorganic cation exchangers and adsorptive materials.\n\nIt shows higher cation exchange selectivity, good resistance to temperature and ionizing radiations, and excellent compatibility with the environment.\nIn zeolites, some of the the Si4+ ions may be replaced by Al3+. This results in unbalanced anionic charge. To maintain electrical neutrality, positive ions must be introduced.\n\n\nS\ni\nO\n4\n−\n4\n units in zeolites can\'t be replaced by \nA\nl\nO\n5\n−\n4\n and \nA\nl\nO\n9\n−\n6','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','they have an open structure which enables them to take up small molecules','zeolites are alluminosilicates having 3D network','some of the SiO4 ions are replaced by (AlO4)-5 and (AlO6)-9 ions in zeolites','they are used as cation exhanger','NEW','11-330',NULL,NULL),
(113931,'chemistry','A compound among the following having a similar structure to graphite is:','BN','Carbon atoms form a layer like structure with a hexagonal arrangement of carbon atoms. Graphite has a layered structure that consists of rings of six carbon atoms arranged in widely spaced horizontal sheets. Boron nitride (BN) structure is similar to graphite ie sheets of hexagonal rings with alternate B and N atoms.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','B','B4C','B2H6','BN','NEW','11-326',NULL,NULL),
