(114007,'chemistry','Catenation, i.e., linking of similar atoms depends on size and \nelectronic  configuration of atoms. The tendency of catenation in Group \n14 elements follows the order','C » Si > Ge = Sn',NULL,'Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','C > Si > Ge > Sn','C » Si > Ge = Sn','Si > C > Sn > Ge','Ge > Sn > Si > C','NEW','11-318',NULL,NULL),
(114008,'chemistry','Silicon has a strong tendency to form polymers like silicones. The chain length of silicone polymer can be controlled by adding','Me3SiCl','The chain length of the polymer can be controlled by adding (CH3)3SiCl  which blocks the ends','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','MeSiCl3','Me2SiCl2','Me3SiCl','Me4Si','NEW','11-328',NULL,NULL),
(114009,'chemistry','Ionisation enthalpy (∆ tH1 kJ mol-1) for the elements of Group 13 follows the order.','B > Al < Ga > In < Tl','ionisation enthalpy values as expected\nfrom the general trends do not decrease\nsmoothly down the group. The decrease from\nB to Al is associated with increase in size. The\nobserved discontinuity in the ionisation\nenthalpy values between Al and Ga, and\nbetween In and Tl are due to inability of d- and\nf-electrons ,which have low screening effect, to\ncompensate the increase in nuclear charge','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','B > Al > Ga > In > Tl','B < Al < Ga< In <Tl','B < Al > Ga < In < Tl','B > Al < Ga > In < Tl','NEW','11-317',NULL,NULL),
(114010,'chemistry','In the structure of diborane','2 boron atoms and 4 terminal hydrogen atoms lie in the same plane and 2 bridging hydrogen atoms lie in the perpendicular plane.','Four terminal hydrogen atoms and two boron atoms lie in the same plane \nand two hydrogen atoms forming bridges lie in a plane perpendicular to \nthe rest of the molecule.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','all hydrogen atoms lie in one plane and boron atoms lie in a plane perpendicular to this plane','2 boron atoms and 4 terminal hydrogen atoms lie in the same plane and 2 bridging hydrogen atoms lie in the perpendicular plane.','4 bridging hydrogen atoms and boron atoms lie in one plane and \ntwo terminal hydrogen atoms lie in a plane perpendicular to this plane.','all the atoms are in the same plane.','NEW','11-321',NULL,NULL),
(114011,'chemistry','A compound X, of boron reacts with NH3 on heating to give another compound Y which is called inorganic benzene. The compound X can be prepared by treating BF3 with lithium aluminium hydride. The compounds X and Y are represented by the formulas.','B₂H₆,B₃N₃H₆','The compound X is boron trihydride or diborane (B2H6) and the compound Y is borazine or inorganic benzene (B3N3H6). The balanced equation for the reaction between X and NH3 to form Y is:\n\n2B₂H₆ + 6NH₃ → 2B₃N₃H₆ + 12H₂\n\nAnd the preparation of X from BF3 and LiAlH4 can be represented by the following equation:\n\nBF3 + LiAlH4 → B2H6 + LiF + AlH3','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','B₂H₆,B₃N₃H₆','B₂O₃, B₃N₃H₆','BF₃, B₃N₃H₆','B₃N₃H₆ , B₂H₆','NEW','11-321',NULL,NULL),
(114012,'chemistry','Quartz is extensively used as a piezoelectric material, it contains','Si','Quartz is a crystalline form of silica.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Pb','Si','Ti','Sn','NEW','11-322',NULL,NULL),
(114013,'chemistry','The most commonly used reducing agent is','SnCl2','4 oxidation state of Sn is more stable than +2 oxidation state. Therefore, Sn2+ can be easily oxidised to Sn4+ and hence SnCl2 acts a reducing agent.\n\nSnCl2 + 2Cl → SnCl4 + 2e','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','AlCl3','PbCl2','SnCl4','SnCl2','NEW','11-324',NULL,NULL),
(114014,'chemistry','Dry ice is','solid CO2','Solid CO2 is known as dry ice','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Solid NH3','Solid SO2','solid CO2','solid N2','NEW','11-328',NULL,NULL),
(114015,'chemistry','Cement, the important building material is a mixture of oxides of several elements. Besides calcium, iron and sulphur, oxides of elements of which of the group(s) are present in the mixture?','groups 2,13 and 14','Cement contains elements of group 2 (Ca), group 13 (Al) and group 14 (Si).','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','group 2','groups 2,13 and 14','groups 2 and 13','groups 2 and 14','NEW','11-322',NULL,NULL),
(114016,'chemistry','Assertion (A): If aluminium atoms replace a few silicon atoms in three dimensional network of silicon dioxide, the overall structure acquires a negative charge.\n\nReason (R): Aluminium is trivalent while silicon is tetravalent.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','In aluminosilicates (anion), some of the silicon atoms are replaced by \naluminium. Since, aluminium is trivalent while silicon is tetravalent, \nhence we get negatively charged ion','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are not correct.','A is not correct but R is correct.','NEW','11-329',NULL,NULL),
(114017,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Silicones are water repelling in nature.\n\nReason (R): Silicones are organosilicon polymers, which have (-R2SiO-) as repeating unit.','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.','Silicones are organosilicon polymers and they are hydrophobic in nature. Silicones neither react nor absorb water molecules.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are not correct.','A is not correct but R is correct.','NEW','11-329',NULL,NULL),
(114018,'chemistry','The reason for small radius of Ga compared to Al is_________ .\n\n(a) poor screening effect of d and f orbitals\n\n(b) increase in nuclear charge\n\n(c) presence of higher orbitals\n\n(d) higher atomic number','a,b','The additional 10 d-electrons offer poor screening effect for the outer electrons from the increased nuclear charge in Gallium. Hence, atomic radius of Gallium is less than that of aluminium.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','c,d','b,c','a,d','NEW','11-318',NULL,NULL),
(114019,'chemistry','The linear shape of C02 is due to  ______ .\n\n(a) sp3 hybridisation of carbon\n\n(b) sp hybridisation of carbon\n\n(c) pπ-pπ bonding between carbon and oxygen\n\n(d) sp2 hybridisation of carbon','b,c','The linear shape of C02 is due to pπ-pπ bonding between carbon and oxygen and sp hybridisation of carbon.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','c,d','b,c','a,d','NEW','11-328',NULL,NULL),
(114020,'chemistry','Me3SiCl is used during polymerisation of organo silicones because\n\n(a) the chain length of organo silicone polymers can be controlled by adding Me3\n\n(b) Me3SiCl blocks the end terminal of silicone polymer.\n\n(c) Me3SiCl improves the quality and yield of the polymer. –\n\n(d) Me3SiCl acts as a catalyst during polymerization.','a,b','The chain length of the polymer can be controlled by adding Me3SiCl which blocks the ends of the silicon polymer.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','c,d','b,c','a,d','NEW','11-329',NULL,NULL),
(114021,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\n\n(a) Fullerenes have dangling bonds.\n\n(b) Fullerenes are cage-like molecules.\n\n(c) Graphite is thermodynamically most stable allotrope of carbon.\n\n(d) Graphite is slippery and hard and therefore used as a dry lubricant','b,c','Fullerenes are cage-like (soccer or rugby ball) molecules and graphite is thermodynamically most stable allotrope of carbon. Thus, options (b) and (c) are correct','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','c,d','b,c','a,d','NEW','11-326',NULL,NULL),
(114101,'chemistry','CCl4 is immiscible in water, whereas SiCl4 is easily hydrolysed.Why?','Si has d-orbitals to accommodate the lone pair of electrons donated by oxygen atom of water molecules \nwhereas carbon has no d orbital for bonding.','SiCl4. undergoes hydrolysis by initially accepting lone pair of electrons from water molecule in d orbitals of Si, finally leading to the formation of Si(OH)4','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  JEE Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CCl4 doesn\'t get ionized but SiCl4 does','The Si-Cl bond is weaker than C-Cl bond','Silicon is more electropositive than carbon','Si has d-orbitals to accommodate the lone pair of electrons donated by oxygen atom of water molecules \nwhereas carbon has no d orbital for bonding.','NEW','11-329',NULL,NULL),
(114102,'chemistry','A : Carbon form two oxides CO and CO2, where CO is neutral while CO2 is acidic.\nR : CO2 will combine with water to give carbonic acid and CO is soluble in water','[A] and [R] both are correct and [R] is not\ncorrect explanation of [A]','CO2 will form carbonic acid with water and it is\nacidic in nature, while CO is neutral but there is no\nrelation of neutrality with solubility.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  JEE Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','[A] and [R] both are correct and [R] is correct\nexplanation of [A]','[A] and [R] both are correct and [R] is not\ncorrect explanation of [A]','[A] is correct while [R] is false','[A] is false while [R] is correct','NEW','11-328',NULL,NULL),
(114103,'chemistry','Arrange the following elements in increasing order\nof metallic character\nSi, K, Mg and Be','Si < Be < Mg < K','Based on the electronegativity of the given\nelements, the correct increasing order of metallic\ncharacter is\nSi < Be < Mg < K','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  JEE Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Si < Mg < Be < K','Be < Mg < Si < K','Si < Be < Mg < K','Si < Be < Mg < K','NEW','11-318',NULL,NULL),
(114104,'chemistry','The role of SiO2 in Cu extraction is','Converts FeO to FeSiO3','It converts FeO to FeSiO3','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  JEE Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Converts FeO to FeSiO3','Converts CaO to CaSiO3','Reduces Cu2S to Cu','None of these','NEW','11-328',NULL,NULL),
(114105,'chemistry','The correct order of bond strength of C–C, Si–Si,\nGe–Ge, Sn–Sn is____?','C–C > Si–Si > Ge–Ge > Sn–Sn','Bond strength decreases on moving down. For\ncarbon family','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  JEE Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','C–C > Si–Si > Ge–Ge > Sn–Sn','C–C > Si–Si > Ge–Ge = Sn–Sn','C–C < Si–Si < Ge–Ge < Sn–Sn','C–C > Si–Si > Sn–Sn > Ge–Ge','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
(114106,'chemistry','The Geometery around the borane product\nBF3+ NaH ----> Product + NaF','Tetrahedral','BF3+ NaH ----> B2H6 + NaF;B2H6 has Tetrahedral geometery','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  JEE Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Trigonal Planar','Tetrahedral','Pyramidal','Planar','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
(114107,'chemistry','Which of the following is correct statement?','B2H6  is Lewis acid','B2H6 is deficient in electrons by \' 2. Hence, it can act as Lewis acid. \nAll the B-H bonds are not equal. Bridge B-H bond length is greater than terminal B-H bonds.B2H6 is a non-planar\nmolecule. Maximum number of hydrogen in one plane is 4','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  JEE Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','B2H6  is Lewis acid','All theB-H  bonds in B2H6 are equal','B2H6 has planar structure','Maximum number of hydrogen in the plane is six.','NEW','11-320',NULL,NULL),
(114108,'chemistry','The stability of oxidation state +1 among  Al, Ga, In and Tl increases in the sequence','Al < Ga < In < Tl','On moving down the group, the stability of +3 oxidation state decreases while +1 oxidation state increases due\nto inert pair effect','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  JEE Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Al < Ga < In < Tl','Tl < In < Ga < Al','In < Tl < Ga < Al','Ga < In < Al < Tl','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
(114109,'chemistry','Which group 13 element has lowest melting point and is close to a metalloid?','Ga','The expected order of decrease in melting points is B > Al > Ga > In > Tl . But this order is incorrect. Due to structure\nchanges, melting point instead of decreasing, increases from Ga to Tl  and as such Ga has  the lowest melting point. Hence\nthe actual order is B > Al > Tl > In > Ga','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  JEE Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Al','Ga','In','Tl','NEW','11-318',NULL,NULL),
(114110,'chemistry','Consider the following statements:\nStatement-1: Boric acid is a weak acid in aqueous solution.\nStatement-2 : It acts as a Lewis acid due to the presence of incomplete octet of boron','Both statements are correct','Boric acid is a weak acid because it does not completely ionize in water or other aqueous solution. It is not able to release\nH+ ions on its own because firstly it receives hydroxide ions OH−from water molecule in order to complete its octet and\nthen it releases H+ ions.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  JEE Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both statements are correct','Statement-1 is correct and statement-2 is incorrect','Statement-1 is incorrect and statement-2 is correct','Both the statements are incorrect','NEW','11-320',NULL,NULL),
(114111,'chemistry','Assertion: Boron is unable to form BF₆³⁻ .\n\nReason: Boron is very small in size.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','ue to non-availability of d orbitals, boron is unable to expand its octet. Therefore, the maximum covalence of boron cannot exceed 4.\nBoron is the first element from top in group 13 and the atomic size increases down the group. Therefore, boron is smallest in size','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  JEE Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct','NEW','11-322',NULL,NULL),
(114112,'chemistry','FeO + SiO2 → FeSiO3\nConsidering the extraction of copper, SiO2 and FeSiO3 are respectively:','Flux and slag','During extraction of copper, iron oxide present as impurity is removed by adding SiO2 and converting it into FeSiO3\nFeO + SiO2 → FeSiO3\nFlux                       Slag','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  JEE Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Flux and slag','Slag and flux','Gauge and flux','Gauge and slag','NEW','11-322',NULL,NULL),
(114113,'chemistry','Glass is a','super-cooled liquid','Glass is a transparent or translucent amorphous supercooled solid solution (supercooled liquid) of silicates and borates.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  JEE Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','micro-crystalline solid','super-cooled liquid','gel','polymeric mixture.','NEW','11-322',NULL,NULL),
(114114,'chemistry','Which of the following are Lewis acids?','BCl3 and AlCl3','Lewis acid is an electron pair acceptor.Both BCl3 and AlCl3 have vacant p- orbital and thus incomplete octet.Hence they will act as Lewis acid.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  JEE Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','AlCl3 and SiCl4','PH3 and SiCl4','BCl3 and AlCl3','PH3 and BCl3','NEW','11-320',NULL,NULL),
(114115,'chemistry','Assertion: Among the carbon allotropes, diamond is an insulator, whereas, graphite is a good conductor of electricity. \nReason: Hybridization of carbon in diamond and graphite are sp3 and sp2, respectively.','[A] and [R] both are correct and [R] is correct\nexplanation of [A]','Diamond is a bad conductor of electricity due to the non-availability of\n free electrons. Graphite is a good conductor of electricity due to the \nfourth valence electron of each carbon which is free to move.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  JEE Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','[A] and [R] both are correct and [R] is correct\nexplanation of [A]','[A] and [R] both are correct and [R] is not\ncorrect explanation of [A]','[A] is correct while [R] is false','[A] is false while [R] is correct','NEW','11-325',NULL,NULL),
(114116,'chemistry','Boron cannot form which one of the following anions?','BF63-','Because of the non-availability of d-orbitals, boron is unable to expand its octet. Hence the maximum covalence of boron cannot exceed 4','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  JEE Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','B(OH)4–','BO2–','BF63-','BH4–','NEW','11-316',NULL,NULL),
(114117,'chemistry','The amorphous form of silica is','kieselguhr','Kieselguhr is a type of amorphous silica, also known as diatomaceous earth, which is composed of the microscopic remains of diatoms, a type of algae. It is often used in filtration processes and as a natural insecticide.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  JEE Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','cristobalite','kieselguhr','tridymite','quartz.','NEW','11-322',NULL,NULL),
(114118,'chemistry','Example of a three-dimensional silicate is','Feldspars','Feldspars are an example of three-dimensional silicate','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  JEE Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Beryls','Zeolites','Feldspars','Ultramarines','NEW','11-330',NULL,NULL),
(114301,'chemistry','Standard electrode potential values, EV for Al 3+ /Al is –1.66 V and that of Tl3+/Tl is +1.26 V. Based on the data which statement is true?','Aluminium easily form +3 ions as it is more electropositive than thallium','Aluminium easily form +3 ions as it is more electropositive than thallium\nStandard electrode potential values for two half cell reactions suggest that aluminium has high tendency to make Al 3+ (aq) ions, whereas Tl 3+ is not only unstable in solution but is a powerful oxidising agent also. Thus Tl + is more stable in solution\nthan Tl 3+ .Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 311','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  NCERT Box Questions and Intext Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Electrode potential values for two half cell reactions suggest that aluminium has low tendency to make Al 3+ (aq) ions,','Tl 3+ is  stable in solution','Tl 3+ is a powerful reducing agent','Aluminium easily form +3 ions as it is more electropositive than thallium','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
(114302,'chemistry','White fumes appear around the bottle of anhydrous aluminium chloride.Why?','Anhydrous AlCl3 partially hydrolysed with atmospheric moisture to liberate HCl gas','Anhydrous aluminium chloride is partially hydrolysed with atmospheric moisture to liberate HCl gas. Moist HCl appears white in colour..Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 312','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  NCERT Box Questions and Intext Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','AlCl3 is volatile.','Anhydrous AlCl3 partially hydrolysed with atmospheric moisture to liberate HCl gas','Accumlation of moisture around the bottle of AlCl3','Formation of Al(OH)3','NEW','11-320',NULL,NULL),
(114303,'chemistry','Why is boric acid considered as a weak acid?','Because it ionizes partially in water','Because it is not able to release H+ ions on its own. It receives OH – ions from water molecule to complete its octet and in turn releases H + ions.Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 313','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  NCERT Box Questions and Intext Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Because it does not dissolve in water','Because it ionizes partially in water','Because it has a high concentration of hydrogen ions','Because it reacts strongly with bases','NEW','11-320',NULL,NULL),
