(114305,'chemistry','Which Member of Group 14 is commonly found in +2 oxidation state,','Pb','lead is commonly found in +2 oxidation state,.Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 316','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  NCERT Box Questions and Intext Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Ge','C','Pb','Sn','NEW','11-322',NULL,NULL),
(114306,'chemistry','Which Member of Group 14 used as semiconductor','Ge ,Si','silicon and germanium are used as semiconductor.Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 316','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  NCERT Box Questions and Intext Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ge ,Si','C','Pb','Sn','NEW','11-322',NULL,NULL),
(114307,'chemistry','[SiF₆]²⁻ is known whereas [SiCl₆]²⁻ not. Give  reasons.','Both a and c','The main reasons are :\n(i) six large chloride ions cannot be accommodated around Si 4+ due to limitation of its size.\n(ii) interaction between lone pair of chloride ion and Si 4+ is not very strong.\n.Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 317','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  NCERT Box Questions and Intext Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','large chloride ions cannot be accommodated around Si 4+','Fluorine is a stronger oxidizing agent than chlorine','interaction between lone pair of chloride ion and Si4+ is not very strong.','Both a and c','NEW','11-329',NULL,NULL),
(114401,'chemistry','Assertion: Boron is a metal.\n\nReason: Boron has a metallic luster and is a good conductor of electricity.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','The assertion that boron is a metal is incorrect because boron is actually a metalloid, which means it has some properties of metals and some of nonmetals. However, the reason that boron has a metallic luster and is a good conductor of electricity is correct, as these are properties that some metalloids share with metals.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-318',NULL,NULL),
(114402,'chemistry','Assertion: The melting points of the Group 14 elements increase down the group.\n\nReason: The metallic character of the elements increases down the group.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The melting points of the Group 14 elements (Carbon, Silicon, Germanium, Tin, Lead) increase down the group with the exception of Pb whose melting point is higher than Sn. This is due to the increase in atomic size and the number of electrons involved in metallic bonding.\n\nThe metallic character of the elements increases down the group. This is because, as the atomic size increases down the group, the outermost electrons become farther from the nucleus, resulting in easier loss of electron and an increase in metallic character.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-318',NULL,NULL),
(114403,'chemistry','Assertion: Carbon dioxide is a covalent compound. \n\nReason: Carbon and oxygen are both non-metals.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Carbon dioxide is a covalent compound, meaning it is formed by sharing of electrons between carbon and oxygen atoms. Both carbon and oxygen are non-metals, and as such they tend to form covalent compounds rather than ionic compounds that are formed between metals and non-metals.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-328',NULL,NULL),
(114404,'chemistry','Assertion: Silicon is a metalloid.\n\nReason: Silicon has properties of both metals and non-metals','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Silicon is a metalloid element that has properties of both metals and non-metals. It has an intermediate electrical conductivity that is between that of a metal and a non-metal, which means it can conduct electricity to some extent but not as well as a metal. Silicon also has a shiny, metallic appearance, but it is brittle and lacks the malleability and ductility of a true metal. Additionally, silicon is an important semiconductor material used in electronic devices.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-328',NULL,NULL),
(114405,'chemistry','Assertion: Carbon is widely distributed in nature in the elemental state.\n\nReason: Carbon exists as metal carbonates, hydrocarbons, and carbon dioxide gas in air.','The assertion and reason are both incorrect.','Both statements are not correct because carbon is not found in nature in its elemental state in significant quantities. Instead, it exists in various forms such as diamond, graphite, and coal. These forms of carbon are the result of the combination of carbon with other elements such as hydrogen, oxygen, and nitrogen.\n\nSimilarly, the second statement is not entirely correct because while carbon exists as carbon dioxide gas in the air, it is not the only form in which it is found in nature. Carbon is also present in various minerals and rocks in the form of metal carbonates, such as calcium carbonate (limestone) and magnesium carbonate (dolomite)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','The assertion and reason are both incorrect.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-325',NULL,NULL),
(114406,'chemistry','Assertion: The first ionization enthalpy of group 14 members decreases down the group.\nReason: The influence of inner core electrons is visible here.','The assertion is correct, and the reason is true.','The first ionization enthalpy of group 14 members decreases down the group due to the influence of inner core electrons.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','The assertion is correct, and the reason is true.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-317',NULL,NULL),
(114407,'chemistry','Assertion: The electronegativity values for group 14 elements decrease down the group.\nReason: The small size of the elements in this group makes them slightly more electronegative than group 13 elements.','The assertion is incorrect, but the reason is true','The electronegativity values for elements from Si to Pb are almost the \nsame, but the small size of the elements in this group makes them \nslightly more electronegative than group 13 elements.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','The assertion is incorrect, but the reason is true','NEW','11-317',NULL,NULL),
(114408,'chemistry','Assertion: All group 14 members are metals.\nReason: Tin and lead are soft metals with low melting points.','The assertion is incorrect, but the reason is true','Carbon and silicon are non-metals, germanium is a metalloid, whereas tin and lead are soft metals with low melting points.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','The assertion is incorrect, but the reason is true','NEW','11-317',NULL,NULL),
(114409,'chemistry','Assertion: Carbon and silicon mostly exhibit the +2 oxidation state.\nReason: The inability of ns2 electrons of the valence shell to participate in bonding results in this trend.','The assertion is incorrect, and the reason is false','Carbon and silicon mostly show +4 oxidation state, whereas in heavier \nmembers, the tendency to show +2 oxidation state increases in the \nsequence Ge<Sn<Pb due to the inability of ns2 electrons of valence\n shell to participate in bonding.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','The assertion is incorrect, and the reason is false','NEW','11-328',NULL,NULL),
(114410,'chemistry','Carbon can exceed its covalence more than four.\nReason: Other group 14 elements can exceed their covalence more than four due to the presence of d orbitals in them','The assertion is false, but the reason is true.','Carbon cannot exceed its covalence more than four, whereas other group \n14 elements can do so due to the presence of d orbitals in them.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','The assertion is false, but the reason is true.','NEW','11-327',NULL,NULL),
(114411,'chemistry','Assertion: Silicon is the most abundant element by mass in the earth’s crust.\nReason: Silicon is present in nature in the form of silica and silicates','The assertion is correct, and the reason is true.','Silicon is the second (27.7% by mass) most abundant element on the \nearth’s crust and is present in nature in the form of silica and \nsilicates.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','The assertion is correct, and the reason is true.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-329',NULL,NULL),
(114412,'chemistry','Assertion:Carbon is unique in its ability to form pπ–pπ multiple bonds. \n\nReason: This ability is due to carbon\'s small size and high electronegativity','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','The assertion "Carbon is unique in its ability to form pπ–pπ multiple bonds" is true.However, the reason "This ability is due to carbon\'s small size and high electronegativity" is not entirely accurate. Carbon\'s ability to form pπ–pπ multiple bonds is actually due to its unique electron configuration, which allows it to form four covalent bonds with other atoms. This is because carbon has four valence electrons in its outer shell, which can be shared with other atoms to form these strong covalent bonds.While carbon\'s small size and high electronegativity do contribute to its ability to form multiple bonds, it is not the sole reason. Other factors, such as its ability to hybridize its orbitals, also play a role.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-316',NULL,NULL),
(114413,'chemistry','Assertion: Heavier elements do not form pπ–pπ bonds. \n\nReason: Their atomic orbitals are too large and diffuse to have effective overlapping','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','The assertion "Heavier elements do not form pπ–pπ bonds" is generally true.The reason provided, "Their atomic orbitals are too large and diffuse to have effective overlapping" is also generally true. Heavier elements have larger atomic orbitals, which can be less effective at overlapping with other atoms, leading to weaker bonds.This concept is discussed in NCERT Class 11 Chemistry Chapter 11 "The p-Block Elements", on page number 290.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-316',NULL,NULL),
(114414,'chemistry','Assertion: Carbon atoms tend to link with one another through covalent bonds to form chains and rings. \n\nReason: The C-C bonds are very weak','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','The assertion "Carbon atoms tend to link with one another through covalent bonds to form chains and rings" is generally true.However, the reason provided, "The C-C bonds are very weak" is not true. In fact, carbon-carbon (C-C) bonds are quite strong, and their strength is one of the reasons why carbon is able to form long chains and complex structures.The actual reason why carbon atoms tend to form chains and rings is due to their unique electron configuration, which allows them to form multiple covalent bonds with other atoms, including other carbon atoms. This ability to form multiple bonds allows carbon to form long chains and complex structures, such as rings and branched chains.This concept is discussed in NCERT Class 11 Chemistry Chapter 11 "The p-Block Elements", on page number 291','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-327',NULL,NULL),
(114415,'chemistry','Assertion: The tendency to show catenation decreases down the group. \n\nReason: This is because the size increases and electronegativity decreases down the group.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','The assertion "The tendency to show catenation decreases down the group" is generally true.The reason provided, "This is because the size increases and electronegativity decreases down the group" is also generally true. As we move down the group, the atomic size of the elements increases due to the addition of new shells. This leads to weaker bonding between the atoms, making it difficult for the heavier elements to form strong covalent bonds and chains. Additionally, the electronegativity of the elements decreases down the group, which also contributes to weaker bonding.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-317',NULL,NULL),
(114416,'chemistry','Assertion: Lead shows catenation. \n\nReason: Its bond enthalpy is greater than that of carbon.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','The assertion "Lead shows catenation" is not generally true. Lead does not form long chains or complex structures like carbon, due to the larger size and weaker bonding of its atoms.The reason provided, "Its bond enthalpy is greater than that of carbon" is also not accurate. Bond enthalpy is the energy required to break a particular bond, and it is not directly related to an element\'s ability to form chains or catenation.This concept is discussed in NCERT Class 11 Chemistry Chapter 11 "The p-Block Elements", on page number 291.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-317',NULL,NULL),
(114417,'chemistry','Assertion: Carbon is able to show allotropic forms due to its property of catenation and pπ–pπ bond formation. \n\nReason: Due to its property of catenation and pπ–pπ bond formation.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Due property of catenation and pπ– pπ bond formation, carbon is able to show allotropic forms.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-325',NULL,NULL),
(114418,'chemistry','Assertion: Diamond is a soft substance. \n\nReason: The C-C bonds in diamond are weak.','Assertion is wrong statement and reason is wrong statement','It is very difficult to break extended covalent bonding and, therefore, diamond is a hardest substance on the earth.Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 318','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement and reason is wrong statement','NEW','11-326',NULL,NULL),
(114419,'chemistry','Assertion: Graphite conducts electricity along the sheet.\n\n Reason: The electrons in graphite are localised within the layers.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','The electrons are delocalised over the whole sheet. Electrons are mobile and,therefore, graphite conducts electricity along the sheet. .Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 318','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-326',NULL,NULL),
(114420,'chemistry','Assertion: Fullerenes are the only pure form of carbon. \n\nReason: Fullerenes have a smooth structure without having \'dangling\' bonds.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Fullerenes are the only pure form of carbon because they have smooth structure without having ‘dangling’ bonds..Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 318','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-326',NULL,NULL),
(114421,'chemistry','Assertion: Fullerenes are made by heating graphite in the presence of helium or argon.\n\n\nReason: Fullerenes consist of mainly C60 and traces of fullerenes consisting of even number of carbon atoms up to 350 or above','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','are made by the heating of graphite in an electric arc in the presence of inert gases such as helium or argon. The sooty material formed by condensation of vapourised C n small molecules consists of mainly C60 with smaller quantity of C 70 and traces of fullerenes\nconsisting of even number of carbon atoms up to 350 or above.Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 318','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-326',NULL,NULL),
(114422,'chemistry','Assertion:  The C-C distances in buckminsterfullerene are 143.5 pm and 138.3 pm, respectively\n\nReason: Buckminsterfullerene contains both single and double bonds. .','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','This ball shaped molecule has 60 vertices and each one is occupied by one carbon atom and it also contains both single and double bonds with\nC–C distances of 143.5 pm and 138.3 pm respectively. . Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 319','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-317',NULL,NULL),
(114423,'chemistry','Assertion: Carbon black is used as a filler in automobile tyres.\n\nReason: Carbon black is used as a white pigment in paints.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Carbon black is used as black pigment in black ink and as filler in automobile tyres. Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 319','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-327',NULL,NULL),
(114424,'chemistry','Assertion: Boron, aluminium, gallium, indium and thallium all have the same electronic configuration.\n\nReason: The outer electronic configuration of these elements is ns2np1.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','Boron, aluminium, gallium, indium and thallium have the same outer  electronic configuration of ns2np1, but they differ in their inner  electronic configuration. Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 309','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-315',NULL,NULL),
(114425,'chemistry','Assertion: Boron has a very high melting point.\n\nReason: Boron has a very strong crystalline lattice.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Due to very strong crystalline lattice, boron has unusually high melting point. Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 310','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-318',NULL,NULL),
(114426,'chemistry','Assertion: The abundance of boron in the earth\'s crust is greater than 0.001% by mass.\n\nReason: Boron mainly occurs as orthoboric acid, Na2B4O7.10H2O and kernite.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','The abundance of boron in the earth\'s crust is less than 0.0001% by  mass, and it is true that boron mainly occurs as orthoboric acid,  Na2B4O7.10H2O and kernite... Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 309','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-315',NULL,NULL),
(114427,'chemistry','Assertion: Gallium is a non-metal.\n\nReason: Gallium is almost exclusively metallic in character.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','Gallium is a metal, and it is true that gallium is almost exclusively metallic in character.. Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 309','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-315',NULL,NULL),
(114428,'chemistry','Assertion: Atomic radius increases on moving down the boron family.\n\nReason: The addition of one extra shell of electrons for each successive member leads to an increase in atomic radius.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Atomic radius is expected to increase on moving down the boron family as  one extra shell of electrons is added for each successive member.However, a deviation can be seen. Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 309','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-317',NULL,NULL),
(114429,'chemistry','Assertion: Gallium has a larger atomic radius than aluminium.\n\nReason: The presence of additional 10 d-electrons in gallium offers  better screening effect for the outer electrons from the increased  nuclear charge than in aluminium.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','Gallium has a smaller atomic radius than aluminium due to the presence  of additional 10 d-electrons that offer only a poor screening effect for  the outer electrons from the increased nuclear charge.. Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 309','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-317',NULL,NULL),
(114430,'chemistry','Assertion: The ionization enthalpy values for the boron family decrease smoothly down the group.\n\nReason: The decrease in ionization enthalpy values from boron to aluminium is associated with an increase in size.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','The ionization enthalpy values for the boron family do not decrease  smoothly down the group, and the decrease from boron to aluminium is  associated with an increase in size. Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 309','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-317',NULL,NULL),
(11002,'chemistry','State True(T) or False(F). \n \n A) Methane and Dioxygen are called reactants and carbon dioxide and water are called products. \n \n B)The coefficients 2 for O₂ and H₂O are called stoichiometric coefficients. \n \n For reaction refer diagram(n0109)','T,T','Here, Methane and Dioxygen are called reactants and Carbon dioxide and Water are called products.\n \n The coefficients 2 for O₂ and H₂O are called stoichiometric coefficients.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 20 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Stoichiometry And Stoichiometric Calculations',NULL,'3','mcq','T,T','T,F','F,T','F,F','NEW','11-020','11-020','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/11002q.png'),
(114431,'chemistry','Assertion: Gallium has a higher ionization enthalpy value than  aluminium.\n\nReason: The inability of d-electrons to compensate for the increase in  nuclear charge in gallium results in a discontinuity in ionization  enthalpy values between aluminium and gallium.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Gallium has a higher ionization enthalpy value than aluminium due to the  inability of d-electrons to compensate for the increase in nuclear  charge in gallium, resulting in a discontinuity in ionization enthalpy  values between aluminium and gallium.. Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 309','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-317',NULL,NULL),
(114432,'chemistry','Assertion:  The atomic radius of gallium is more than that of aluminium .\n\nReason: Thallium has a noble gas plus 14 f-electrons plus 10 d-electron core','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','The presence of additional 10 d-electrons offer only poor screening effect (Unit 2) for the outer electrons from the increased nuclear charge in gallium. Consequently, the atomic radius of gallium (135 pm) is less than that of aluminium (143 pm). Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 309','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-317',NULL,NULL),
(114433,'chemistry','Assertion: The sum of the first three ionisation enthalpies for each of the elements of Group 13 is very high.\n\nReason: The ionisation enthalpy values for these elements do not decrease smoothly down the group.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','ionisation enthalpy values as expected from the general trends do not decrease smoothly down the group. The decrease from B to Al is associated with increase in size. The observed discontinuity in the ionisation\nenthalpy values between Al and Ga, and between In and Tl are due to inability of d- and f-electrons ,which have low screening effect, to compensate the increase in nuclear charge.. Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 309','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-317',NULL,NULL),
(114434,'chemistry','Assertion: The electronic structure of Group 13 elements is more complex than that of the first two groups.\n\nReason: Gallium, indium and thallium have noble gas plus 10 d-electrons, and thallium has noble gas plus 14 f-electrons plus 10 d-electron  cores.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','boron and aluminium have noble gas core, gallium and indium have noble gas plus 10 d-electrons, and thallium has noble gas plus 14 f- electrons plus 10 d-electron cores.\nThus, the electronic structures of these elements are more complex than for the first two groups of elements. Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 309','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-315',NULL,NULL),
(114435,'chemistry','Assertion : The tendency for catenation decreases in the order C > Si > Ge > Sn.\nReason : The catenation depends on the strength of the element-element bond.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','As we move down the group 14, the elementelement bond energies decrease rapidly, viz. C–C (355 kJ mol–1), Si–Si (222 kJ mol–1), Ge–Ge (167 kJ mol–1) and Sn–Sn (155 kJ mol–1), so the tendency for catenation\ndecreases in the order C > Si > Ge > Sn.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-318',NULL,NULL),
(114436,'chemistry','Assertion : Boron differs from aluminium and other members of group 13 in a number of properties.\n\nReason : Boron shows anomalous behaviour.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','This is due to small atomic size, high electronegativity, high ionization energy and absence of d-orbital of B.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-317',NULL,NULL),
(114437,'chemistry','Assertion : Al forms [AlF6]3– but B does not form [BF6]3–.\nReason : B does not react with F2.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Due to the absence of d orbitals that the maximum covalence of B is 4. Since the d orbitals are available with Al and other elements, the maximum covalence can be expected beyond 4. Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 312','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-316',NULL,NULL),
(213105,'chemistry','Which is the correct order of electron gain enthalpy of group 17 elements ?','Cl > F > Br > I','Electron gain enthalpy of the elements of the group becomes less negative down the group. However, the negative electron gain enthalpy of fluorine is less than that of chlorine. It is due to small size of fluorine atom. As a result, there are strong interelectronic repulsions in the relatively small 2p orbitals of fluorine and thus, the incoming electron does not experience much attraction.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 198 Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Physical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Cl > F = Br > I','Cl > F > Br > I','Cl < F < Br < I','F > Cl > Br > I','NEW','12-198',NULL,NULL),
(114438,'chemistry','Assertion : Anhydrous AlCl3 is covalent but hydrated AlCl3 is ionic.\n\nReason : In water, Al2Cl6 dissociates into hydrated Al3+ and Cl– ions due to high heat of hydration of these ions.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Anhydrous AlCl3 is covalent but hydrated AlCl3 is ionic because In water, Al2Cl6 dissociates into hydrated Al3+ and Cl– ions due to high heat of hydration of these ions.','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >>  Advanced Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement','NEW','11-320',NULL,NULL),
(120206,'chemistry','How many sigma and pi bonds are present in CH₂=C=CHCH₃ respectively?','9 and 2','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 335; Problem No. 12.1) diagram(e1205)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Tetravalence of carbon and shapes of compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','2 and 9','9 and 2','10 and 1','1 and 10','NEW','11-335','11-257',NULL),
(120207,'chemistry','How many sigma and pi bonds are present in HC☰CCH=CHCH₃ respectively?','10 and 3','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 335; Problem No. 12.1) diagram(e1205)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Tetravalence of carbon and shapes of compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','10 and 3','3 and 10','12 and 1','1 and 12','NEW','11-335','11-257',NULL),
