(120664,'chemistry','Carbon atom of –CHO group is _______ while numbering the carbon chain.','Include','Carbon atom of –CHO group is include while numbering the carbon chain.Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 346; Paragraph No. 8)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of organic compounds with functional group',NULL,'1','fillup','Ex,In,clu,de',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-346','11-268',NULL),
(120665,'chemistry','Which is suffixed functional group and gets preference over C=C bond?','OH','OH is suffixed functional group and gets preference over C=C bond.the functional group is -COOH. Carboxylic acids have a higher priority than carbon-carbon double bonds (C=C). This means that the -COOH group will get preference over the C=C double bond when naming a compound. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 346; Paragraph No. 7)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of organic compounds with functional group',NULL,'2','mcq','COOH','OH','Cl, F','NO2','NEW','11-346','11-268',NULL),
(120666,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion: Double bond is suffixed functional group whereas NO2 is prefixed functional group.\n Reason: However, double bond gets preference over –NO2 group.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Double bond is suffixed functional group whereas NO2 is prefixed functional group therefore double bond gets preference over –NO2 group. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 346; Paragraph No. 6)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of organic compounds with functional group',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-346','11-268',NULL),
(120713,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name for the compound in the given diagram(1284)','Benzonitrile','Nitrile is used as suffix for cyanide group. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 345; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','Benzonitrile','Phenylnitrile','Benzylnitrile','None of these','NEW','11-345','11-267','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120713q.png'),
(120714,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name for the compound in the given diagram(1285)','1-Bromo-4-chlorobenzene','Alphabetical order is used while writing names. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 347; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','4-Bromo-1-chlorobenzene','1-Bromo-4-chlorobenzene','4-Chloro-1-Bromobenzene','None of these','NEW','11-347','11-269','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120714q.png'),
(120715,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name for the compound in the given diagram(1286)','2-Bromophenol','Alphabetical order is used while writing names and base compound is given number 1 and the rest according to lowest locant rule. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 347; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','1-Bromophenol','2-Bromophenol','1-Bromohydroxybenzene','None of these','NEW','11-347','11-269','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120715q.png'),
(120716,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name for the compound in the given diagram(1287)','3-Chloroaniline','Alphabetical order is used while writing names and base compound is given number 1 and the rest according to lowest locant rule. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 347; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','1-Chloroaniline','3-Chloroaniline','1-Chloro-3-aminebenzene','None of these','NEW','11-347','11-269','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120716q.png'),
(120718,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name for the compound in the given diagram(1288)','2-Methoxybenzoic acid','Substituent of the base compound is assigned number1 and then the direction of numbering is chosen such that the next substituent gets the lowest number. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 347; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','1-Methoxybenzoic acid','2-Methoxybenzoic acid','2-Carboxymethoxide','None of these','NEW','11-347','11-269','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120718q.png'),
(120719,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name for the compound in the given diagram(1289)','Benzaldehyde','Benzene carbaldehyde is also called as benzaldehyde. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 346; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','Benzoic acid','Benzaldehyde','Benzenol','None of these','NEW','11-346','11-268','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120719q.png'),
(120720,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name for the compound in the given diagram(1290)','Benzoic acid','Benzene carboxylic acid is also called as benzoic acid. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 346; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','Benzoic acid','Benzaldehyde','Benzenol','None of these','NEW','11-346','11-268','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120720q.png'),
(120721,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name for the compound in the given diagram(1291)','Acetophenone','Methylphenyl ketone is also called acetophenone. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 346; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','Benzoic acid','Benzaldehyde','Acetophenone','Benzenol','NEW','11-346','11-268','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120721q.png'),
(120722,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name for the compound in the given diagram(1292)','4-Phenylpentan-2-ol','When benzene is attached to an alkane with functional group then benzene is considered as side group. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 347; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','4-Phenylpentan-2-ol','1-Phenylpentan-4-ol','1-(1-methylbutan-1-ol)-benzene','None of these','NEW','11-347','11-269','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120722q.png'),
(120723,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name for the compound in the given diagram(1293)','(1,1-dimethylethyl)benzene','For a substituted benzene ring, numbering is done from the carbon attached to benzene. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 347; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','1-Methyl-1phenylpropane','(1,1-dimethylethyl)benzene','1,1-Diethyl-1phenylethane','None of these','NEW','11-347','11-269','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120723q.png'),
(120724,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name for the compound in the given diagram(1294)','(But-2-enyl)benzene','For a substituted benzene ring, numbering is done from the carbon attached to benzene. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 347; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','(But-2-enyl)benzene','(But-3-enyl)benzene','1-Phenylbut-2-ene','None of these','NEW','11-347','11-269','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120724q.png'),
(120725,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name for the compound in the given diagram(1295)','3,5-Dinitrobenzoic acid','For a substituted benzene ring, numbering is done from the carbon attached to benzene. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 347; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','3,5-Dinitrobenzoic acid','1,5-Dinitrobenzoic acid','3-Carboxylicdinitric acid','None of these','NEW','11-347','11-269','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120725q.png'),
(213108,'chemistry','Match the following','F₂-Yellow, Cl₂-Greenish yellow, Br₂-Red, I₂-Violet','The correct match is F₂-Yellow, Cl₂-Greenish yellow, Br₂-Red, I₂-Violet\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 199 Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Physical Properties',NULL,'3','match','F₂,Yellow','Cl₂,Greenish yellow','Br₂,Red','I₂,Violet','NEW','12-199',NULL,NULL),
(120726,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name for the compound in the given diagram(1296)','2,3-Difluoro-5-methylbenzoic acid','For a substituted benzene ring, numbering is done from the carbon attached to benzene. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 347; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','1,2-Difluoro-5-methylbenz-3oic acid','2,3-Difluoro-5-methylbenzoic acid','4,5-Difluoro-1-methylbenz-3-oic acid','None of these','NEW','11-347','11-269','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120726q.png'),
(120727,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name for the compound in the given diagram(1297)','2-Amino-5-nitrophenol','For a substituted benzene ring, numbering is done from the carbon attached to benzene,here ol is used as suffix therefore its carbon gets number 1 position for counting. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 347; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','6-Amino-3-nitrophenol','2-Amino-5-nitrophenol','4-Amino-1-nitrophen-3-ol','None of these','NEW','11-347','11-269','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120727q.png'),
(120728,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name for the compound in the given diagram(1298)','4-Amino-2-ethylphenol','For a substituted benzene ring, numbering is done from the carbon attached to benzene,here ol is used as suffix therefore it\'s carbon gets number 1 position for counting. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 347; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','1-Amino-3-ethylphen-4-ol','4-Amino-2-ethylphenol','5-Amino-1-ethylphen-2-ol','None of these','NEW','11-347','11-269','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120728q.png');
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(120729,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name for the compound in the given diagram(1299)','3-Iodo-5-nitrophenol','For a substituted benzene ring, numbering is done from the carbon attached to benzene,here ol is used as suffix therefore it\'s carbon gets number 1 position for counting, here iodo gets number 3 as it is alphabetically higher than nitro group. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 347; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','1-Iodo-3-nitrophen-5-ol','3-Iodo-5-nitrophenol','3-Iodo-1-nitrophen-5-ol','None of these','NEW','11-347','11-269','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120729q.png'),
(120730,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name for the compound in the given diagram(12100)','2,4,6-Tribromophenol','For a substituted benzene ring, numbering is done from the carbon attached to benzene,here ol is used as suffix therefore it\'s carbon gets number 1 position for counting. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 347; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','1,3,5-Tribromophen-2-ol','2,4,6-Tribromophenol','1-Hydroxy-2,4,5-Bromobenzene','None of these','NEW','11-347','11-269','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120730q.png'),
(120731,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name for the compound in the given diagram(12107)','Propylbenzene','For a substituted benzene ring, numbering is done from the carbon attached to benzene, here ring is the principal chain. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 347; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','1-phenylpropane','Propylbenzene','3-phenylpropane','None of these','NEW','11-347','11-269','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/120731q.png'),
(120732,'chemistry','When a benzene ring is attached to an alkane with a functional group, it is considered as ___________.','Substituent','When a benzene ring is attached to an alkane with a functional group, it is considered as substituent, instead of a parent. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 347; Paragraph No. 2 and 5)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','Substituent','Parent','Functional group','Disubstituent','NEW','11-347','11-269',NULL),
(120733,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion:Substituent of the base compound is assigned number1 and then the direction of numbering is chosen such that the next substituent gets the lowest number. \n Reason: The substituents appear in the name in alphabetical order.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','If benzene ring is disubstituted, the position of substituents is defined by numbering the carbon atoms of the ring such that the substituents are located at the lowest numbers possible. Substituent of the base compound is assigned number1 and then the direction of numbering is chosen such that the next substituent gets the lowest number. The substituents appear in the name in alphabetical order.Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 347; Paragraph No. 1 and 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-347','11-269',NULL),
(120734,'chemistry','The name for benzene as substituent is phenyl C6H5-, also abbreviated as _____.','Ph','The name for benzene as substituent is phenyl C6H5-, also abbreviated as Ph. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 347; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','yl','nyl','Ph','Phen','NEW','11-347','11-269',NULL),
(120735,'chemistry','Identify the correct structural formula for p-Nitroaniline : diagram(12141)','B','The structural formula of p-Nitroaniline is given in problem 12.10. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 348; Problem No. 12.10)\n \n diagram(e1212)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','11-348','11-270','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120735q.png'),
(120736,'chemistry','Identify the correct structural formula for o-Ethylanisole : diagram(12141)','A','The structural formula of o-Ethylanisole is given in problem 12.10. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 348; Problem No. 12.10)\n \n diagram(e1212)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','11-348','11-270','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120736q.png'),
(120737,'chemistry','Identify the correct structural formula for 4-Ethyl-1-fluoro-2-nitrobenzene : diagram(12141)','D','The structural formula of 4-Ethyl-1-fluoro-2-nitrobenzene is given in problem 12.10. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 348; Problem No. 12.10)\n \n diagram(e1212)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','11-348','11-270','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120737q.png'),
(120738,'chemistry','Identify the correct structural formula for 2,3 - Dibromo -1 - phenylpentane : diagram(12141)','C','The structural formula of 2,3 - Dibromo -1 - phenylpentane is given in problem 12.10. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 348; Problem No. 12.10)\n \n diagram(e1212)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','11-348','11-270','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120738q.png'),
(120739,'chemistry','In some cases, common name of benzene derivatives is taken as the ______ compound.','Base','In some cases, common name of benzene derivatives is taken as the base compound. \n \n For example, benzene derivatives such as toluene, phenol, and aniline are often referred to by their common names instead of their IUPAC names. Toluene is the common name for methylbenzene, phenol is the common name for hydroxybenzene, and aniline is the common name for aminobenzene.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 347; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Switch','Substituent','Formal','Base','NEW','11-347','11-269',NULL),
(120740,'chemistry','Choose the correct IUPAC name for the given compound: diagram(12138)','Propyl benzene','For a substituted benzene ring, numbering is done from the carbon attached to benzene. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 347; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','3-phenylpropane','2-benzylethane','Phenyl-1-butane','Propyl benzene','NEW','11-347','11-269','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/120740q.png'),
(120815,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion: Nitroalkanes and alkyl nitriles exhibit functional isomerism.\n Reason: Compounds having the same molecular formula but different functional groups are called functional isomers.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Nitroalkanes and alkyl nitriles have same formula but different functional groups. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 348; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Isomerism',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(120816,'chemistry','What arises due to different alkyl chains on either side of the functional group in the molecule?','Metamerism','Metamerism arises due to different alkyl chains on either side of the functional group in the molecule. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Isomerism',NULL,'1','mcq','Optical Isomerism','Metamerism','Positional Isomerism','Chain Isomerism','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(120817,'chemistry','Chain isomerism, position isomerism, functional group isomerism and metamerism are type of ________ isomerism.','Structural Isomerism','Chain isomerism, position isomerism, functional group isomerism and metamerism are type of structural isomerism. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 348; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Isomerism',NULL,'1','mcq','Stereo Isomerism','Structural Isomerism','Optical Isomerism','Geometrical Isomerism','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(120818,'chemistry','The phenomenon of existence of two or more compounds possessing the same molecular formula but different properties is known as ________.','Isomerism','The phenomenon of existence of two or more compounds possessing the same molecular formula but different properties is known as isomerism. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 348; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Isomerism',NULL,'1','mcq','Conformer','Isomerism','Isomorphism','Allotrope','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(120819,'chemistry','Which compounds have the same constitution and sequence of covalent bonds but differ in relative positions of their atoms or groups in space?','Stereoisomers','The compounds that have the same constitution and sequence of covalent bonds but differ in relative positions of their atoms or groups in space are called stereoisomers. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Isomerism',NULL,'1','mcq','Chain Isomers','Stereoisomers','Metamerism','Position Isomers','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(120820,'chemistry','Identify the molecular formula of aldehyde and ketone with respect to fuctional group isomerism:','C3H6O','The molecular formula C3H6O represents an aldehyde and a ketone. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 348; Paragraph No. 5)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Isomerism',NULL,'1','mcq','C2H5O','C3H6O','C4H7O2','C5H9O2','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(120821,'chemistry','Identify the given molecular formula with respect to metamerism: C4 H10 O','Methoxypropane and Ethoxyethane','Metamerism arises due to different alkyl chains on either side of the functional group in the molecule. For example, C4H10O represents methoxypropane (CH3OC3H7) and ethoxyethane (C2H5OC2H5). Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Isomerism',NULL,'1','mcq','Methoxypropane and Ethoxyethane','Methoxyethane and Ethoxypropane','Methoxypropane and Ethoxyepropane','Methoxyethane and Ethoxyethane','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(120822,'chemistry','Which of them is not a stereoisomer?','Functional isomer','Stereoisomerism can be classified as geometrical and optical isomerism. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Isomerism',NULL,'2','mcq','Geometrical isomer','Functional isomer','Optical Isomer','None of the above','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(120823,'chemistry','Which of them is shows structural isomerism?\n \n a. Chain isomers\n \n b. Metamerism\n \n c. Geometrical isomer','a,b','Structural isomerism can be classified as metamerism, chain isomerism, position isomerism and fuctional group isomerism. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 348; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Isomerism',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','all of the above','NEW','11-348','11-270','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/120823q.png'),
(120909,'chemistry','Identify the stbility of alkyl radicals:','Primary < Secondary < Tertiary','3 degree free radicals are more stable than 2 degree and 1 degree. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 350; Paragraph No. 5)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','Primary > Secondary > Tertiary','Primary < Secondary < Tertiary','Primary < Secondary > Tertiary','Primary > Secondary < Tertiary','NEW','11-350','11-272',NULL),
(120910,'chemistry','A reagent that brings an electron pair to the reactive site is called ____________.','Nucleophile','A reagent that brings an electron pair to the reactive site is called a nucleophile (Nu:) i.e., nucleus seeking and the reaction is then called nucleophilic. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','Electrophile','Hydrophile','Nucleophile','Hydrophobic','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(120911,'chemistry','During a polar organic reaction, a/an ___________ attacks an electrophilic centre of the substrate.','Nucleophile','During a polar organic reaction, a nucleophile attacks an electrophilic centre of the substrate which is that specific atom or part of the substrate which is electron deficient. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','Elecrtophile','Hydrophile','Nucleophile','Reagent','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(120912,'chemistry','Which among the following is a nucleophile ?','NC⁻','The nucleophile (NC−) approaches the alkyl halide carbon at an angle of 180° . A reagent that brings an electron pair to the reactive site is called a nucleophile (Nu:) i.e., nucleus seeking and the reaction is then called nucleophilic. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','BF₃','NC⁻','CH₃','None of these','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(120913,'chemistry','Which among the following is a electrophile ?','BF₃','BF₃ is electron deficient so it acts as electrophile.Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','BF₃','NC⁻','CH₃','NH₃','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(120914,'chemistry','R₃N: and H₂O are neutral molecules which can act as ____________.','Nucleophile','R₃N: and H₂O are neutral molecules which can act as nucleophile. The carbon atom in carbocations has sextet configuration; hence, it is electron deficient and can receive a pair of electrons from the nucleophiles. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','Electrophile','Hydrophile','Nucleophile','Reagent','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(120915,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct ?\n a. Carbon atom in alkyl halides becomes an electrophilic centre.\n b. HS⁻, N: are electrophiles while as Cl⁺ acts as nucleophile.','Only a','In neutral molecules such as alkyl halides, due to the polarity of the C-X bond a partial positive charge is generated on the carbon atom and hence the carbon atom becomes an electrophilic centre at which a nucleophile can attack.HS⁻, N: are nucleophiles while as Cl⁺ acts as electrophile. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Both','None of these','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(120916,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion: Heterolytic fission occurs readily in polar covalent bonds.\n Reason: Heterolytic fission involves breaking of bond in such a way that the shared pair of electrons go with one atom.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','In heterolytic cleavage, the bond breaks in such a fashion that the shared pair of electrons remains with one of the fragments(more electronegative). Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 6)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(120917,'chemistry','What do you called the the molecule on which attention is focused when both the reactants supply carbon to the new bond?','Substrate','If both the reactants supply carbon to the new bond then choice is arbitrary and in that case the molecule on which attention is focused is called substrate. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Ligand','Substrate','Reagent','Reactant','NEW','11-350','11-272',NULL),
(120918,'chemistry','When carbon-carbon bond is formed, the choice of naming the reactants as substrate and reagent is _________.','Arbitrary','When carbon-carbon bond is formed, the choice of naming the reactants as substrate and reagent is arbitrary. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 350; Paragraph No. 7)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Reasoned','Absolute','Arbitrary','Doubtful','NEW','11-350','11-272',NULL),
(120919,'chemistry','Identify the stbility of carbocations:','Tertiary > Secondary > Primary > Methyl','The observed order of carbocation stability is: C+H3 < CH3C+H2 < (CH3)2C+H < (CH3)3C+. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 10)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','Tertiary < Secondary < Primary < Methyl','Tertiary < Secondary < Primary > Methyl','Tertiary > Secondary > Primary << Methyl','Tertiary > Secondary > Primary > Methyl','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(120920,'chemistry','The organic reactions which proceed through heterolytic bond cleavage are called______.','Ionic Reaction','The organic reactions which proceed through heterolytic bond cleavage are called ionic or heteropolar or just polar reactions. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 10)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Ionic Reaction','Metallic Reaction','Coordinate Covalent Reaction','Covalent Reaction','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(23001,'chemistry','Assertion : Orbital angular momentum of (1s, 2s, 3s etc.) all s electrons is same. \n Reason : Orbital angular momentum depends on orientation of orbitals.','A is true but R is false','The Assertion is true, while the Reason is false.\n \n The orbital angular momentum of an electron in an s orbital is given by the equation L = √(l(l+1)ħ), where l is the azimuthal quantum number and ħ is the reduced Planck constant. For an s orbital, l = 0, which means that the orbital angular momentum is also zero. Therefore, the orbital angular momentum of all s electrons is the same and is equal to zero.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-056','11-056',NULL),
(120921,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion: A sequential account of each step, describing details of electron movement, energetics during bond cleavage and bond formation, and the rates of transformation of reactants into products (kinetics) is referred to as reaction mechanism.\n Reason: The knowledge of reaction mechanism helps in understanding the reactivity of organic compounds and in planning strategy for their synthesis.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','A sequential account of each step, describing details of electron movement, energetics during bond cleavage and bond formation, and the rates of transformation of reactants into products (kinetics) is referred to as reaction mechanism. The knowledge of reaction mechanism helps in understanding the reactivity of organic compounds and in planning strategy for their synthesis. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 5)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(120922,'chemistry','Heterolytic cleavage and homolytic cleavage occur by:','Covalent Bond','A covalent bond can get cleaved either by : (i) heterolytic cleavage, or by (ii) homolytic cleavage. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 6)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Ionic Bond','Metallic Bond','Coordinate Covalent Bond','Covalent Bond','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(120923,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion: Heterolytic cleavage of bromomethane will give (CH3)+and Br– .\n Reason: After heterolysis, one atom has a sextet electronic structure and a positive charge and the other, a valence octet with at least one lone pair and a negative charge.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','After heterolysis, one atom has a sextet electronic structure and a positive charge and the other, a valence octet with at least one lone pair and a negative charge. Thus, heterolytic cleavage of bromomethane will give (CH3)+and Br–. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 7)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(120924,'chemistry','Choose the correct form of methyl cation or methyl carbonium ion.','C+H3','The C+H3 ion is known as a methyl cation or methyl carbonium ion. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 8)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','C+H3','0','0','C+H4','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(120925,'chemistry','Match th following cations:','Ethyl cation-Primary carbocation, Isopropyl cation-Secondary carbocation, Tert butyl cation-Tertiary carbocation','Carbocations are classified as primary, secondary or tertiary depending on whether one, two or three carbons are directly attached to the positively charged carbon. Some other examples of carbocations are: CH3C+H2 (ethyl cation, a primary carbocation), (CH3)2C+H (isopropyl cation, a secondary carbocation), and (CH3)3C+ (tert-butyl cation, a tertiary carbocation). Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 8)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'3','match','Ethyl cation,Primary carbocation','Isopropyl cation,Secondary carbocation','Tert butyl cation,Tertiary carbocation',NULL,'NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(120926,'chemistry','___________ are highly unstable and reactive species.','Carbocations','Carbocations are highly unstable and reactive species. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 8)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Radicals','Carbocations','Carbanions','Free Radicals','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(120927,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion: Carbocations are highly stable and unreactive species.\n Reason: Alkyl groups directly attached to the positively charged carbon stabilise the carbocations due to inductive and hyperconjugation effects.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','Carbocations are highly unstable and reactive species. Alkyl groups directly attached to the positively charged carbon stabilise the carbocations due to inductive and hyperconjugation effects. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 8)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(120928,'chemistry','What is the shape of carbocations with sp2 hybridised positively charged carbon?','Trigonal planar','Carbocations have trigonal planar shape with positively charged carbon being sp2 hybridised. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349; Paragraph No. 8)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Trigonal planar','Pyramidal','Planar','Distorted Tetrahedron','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(120929,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion: Carbocations have trigonal planar shape with positively charged carbon being sp2 hybridised.\n Reason: The shape of C+H3 may be considered as being derived from the overlap of three equivalent C(sp2) hybridised orbitals with 1s orbital of each of the three hydrogen atoms.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Carbocations have trigonal planar shape with positively charged carbon being sp2 hybridised. Thus, the shape of C+H3 may be considered as being derived from the overlap of three equivalent C(sp2) hybridised orbitals with 1s orbital of each of the three hydrogen atoms. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349-50; Paragraph No. 8)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-350','11-272',NULL),
(120930,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement for a molecule of methyl carbocation:','The remaining carbon orbital is perpendicular to the molecular plane and contains no electrons.','Each bond may be represented as C(sp2)–H(1s) sigma bond. The remaining carbon orbital is perpendicular to the molecular plane and contains no electrons. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 350; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'3','mcq','The remaining carbon orbital is perpendicular to the molecular plane and contains 1 electrons.','The remaining carbon orbital is perpendicular to the molecular plane and contains 2 electrons.','The remaining carbon orbital is base of the molecular plane and contains no electrons.','The remaining carbon orbital is perpendicular to the molecular plane and contains no electrons.','NEW','11-350','11-272',NULL),
(120931,'chemistry','The ___________ can also give a species in which carbon gets the shared pair of electrons.','Heterolytic cleavage','The heterolytic cleavage can also give a species in which carbon gets the shared pair of electrons. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 350; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Heterolytic cleavage','Covalend bond formation','Homolytic cleavage','Ionic bond formation','NEW','11-350','11-272',NULL),
(120932,'chemistry','In ____________, the movement of a single electron takes place instead of an electron pair.','Homolytic cleavage','In homolytic cleavage, the movement of a single electron takes place instead of an electron pair. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 350; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Heterolytic cleavage','Covalend bond formation','Homolytic cleavage','Ionic bond formation','NEW','11-350','11-272',NULL),
(120933,'chemistry','When group Z attached to the carbon leaves without electron pair, which specie will be formed?','Methyl Anion','When group Z attached to the carbon leaves without electron pair, the methyl anion is formed. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 350; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Hydride Ion','Methyl Anion','Methyl Cation','Hydrogen Cation','NEW','11-350','11-272',NULL),
(120934,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement for carbanion:','A carbon species carrying a negative charge on carbon atom.','A carbon species carrying a negative charge on carbon atom is called carbanion. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 350; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','A carbon species carrying a negative charge on carbon atom.','A carbon species carrying a positive charge on carbon atom.','A carbon species carrying a lone pair of electrons on carbon atom.','A carbon species carrying no charge on carbon atom.','NEW','11-350','11-272',NULL),
(120935,'chemistry','What is the shape of carbon in methyl carbanion?','Distorted Tetrahedron','Carbon in carbanion is generally sp3 hybridised and its structure is distorted tetrahedron. \n \n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 350; Paragraph No. 2) diagram(e1213)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Trigonal planar','Pyramidal','Planar','Distorted Tetrahedron','NEW','11-350','11-272',NULL),
(120936,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct ?\n A. Carbon atom in alkyl halides becomes a nucleophilic centre.\n B. Carbocations are unstable and highly reactive species.\n C. Carbanions are also unstable and reactive species.','B and C Only','In neutral molecules such as alkyl halides, due to the polarity of the C-X bond a partial positive charge is generated on the carbon atom and hence the carbon atom becomes an electrophilic centre at which a nucleophile can attack.HS⁻, N: are nucleophiles while as Cl⁺ acts as electrophile.Carbocations are highly unstable and reactive species. Carbanions are also unstable and reactive species. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 349-350; Paragraph No. 8-2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','A and B Only','B and C Only','A and C Only','All of these','NEW','11-350','11-272',NULL),
(120937,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n \n Assertion: In homolytic cleavage, the movement of a single electron takes place instead of an electron pair.\n \n Reason: The movement of a single electron takes place instead of an electron pair','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','In homolytic cleavage, the movement of a single electron takes place instead of an electron pair. Thus, in homolytic cleavage, the movement of a single electron takes place instead of an electron pair. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 350; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-350','11-272',NULL),
(120938,'chemistry','Half-Headed Curved arrown is used to represent:','Single Electron Movement','The single electron movement is shown by ‘halfheaded\' (fish hook) curved arrow. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 350; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Single Electron Movement','Intermediate Formation','Half Reaction Completion','Free Radicals Movement','NEW','11-350','11-272',NULL),
(120939,'chemistry','Free radicals are also very reactive, likewise:','Carbocations and carbanions','Like carbocations and carbanions, free radicals are also very reactive. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 350; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','Sigma and Pi bonds','Carbocations and carbanions','Covalent and Ionic Bond','None of these','NEW','11-350','11-272',NULL),
(120940,'chemistry','Alkyl radical stability __________ as we proceed from primary to tertiary.','Increases','Alkyl radical stability increases as we proceed from primary to tertiary. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 350; Paragraph No. 5)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','fillup','In,De,cr,eas,es',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-350','11-272',NULL),
(213109,'chemistry','What is the correct order of X-X bond dissociation energy?','Cl-Cl > Br-Br > F--F > I-I','The correct order of X-X bond dissociation energy is  Cl-Cl > Br-Br > F--F > I-I\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 199 Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Physical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Cl-Cl > Br-Br > F--F > I-I','F-F > Cl-Cl > Br-Br > I-I','F-F < Cl-Cl < Br-Br < I-I','Cl-Cl < Br-Br < F--F < I-I','NEW','12-199',NULL,NULL),
(120941,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion: Organic reactions, which proceed by homolytic fission are called free radical or homopolar or nonpolar reactions.\n Reason: 3 degree free radicles are more stable.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Organic reactions, which proceed by homolytic fission are called free radical or homopolar or nonpolar reactions and 3 degree free radicles are more stable. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 350; Paragraph No. 6)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-350','11-272',NULL),
(120942,'chemistry','Choose the appropriate option: Ions are generally not formed in the reactions of organic compounds.','Correct','Ions are generally not formed in the reactions of organic compounds. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 350; Paragraph No. 5)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Correct','Incorrect','Incorrect for some exceptional cases','Correct, only when pi bonds are cleaved','NEW','11-350','11-272',NULL),
(120943,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion: Ions are generally not formed in the reactions of organic compounds. Molecules as such participate in the reaction.\n Reason: It is convenient to name one reagent as substrate and other as reagent.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Ions are generally not formed in the reactions of organic compounds. Molecules as such participate in the reaction. It is convenient to name one reagent as substrate and other as reagent. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 350; Paragraph No. 5)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-350','11-272',NULL),
(120944,'chemistry','Choose the appropriate option for the reagents:','Reagents attack the reactive site of the substrate.','Reagents attack the reactive site of the substrate. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 350; Paragraph No. 5)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Reagents attack the reactive site of the substrate.','Reagents attack by the substrate.','Substrate ectivate the reagents.','Reagents activate the substrate.','NEW','11-350','11-272',NULL),
(120945,'chemistry','The reactive site is _________ portion of the molecule.','Electron deficient','The reactive site may be electron deficient portion of the molecule (a positive reactive site) e.g., an atom with incomplete electron shell or the positive end of the dipole in the molecule. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Electron efficient','Electron deficient','Neutral','All of these','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(120946,'chemistry','Choose the appropriate option for the given statement: "An atom with incomplete electron shell or the positive end of the dipole in the molecule."','Electron deficient','The reactive site may be electron deficient portion of the molecule (a positive reactive site) e.g., an atom with incomplete electron shell or the positive end of the dipole in the molecule.Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Electron efficient','Electron deficient','Neutral','All of these','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(120947,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion: The reactive site may be electron deficient portion of the molecule (a positive reactive site) e.g., an atom with incomplete electron shell or the positive end of the dipole in the molecule. \n Reason: If the attacking species is electron rich, it attacks these sites.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The reactive site may be electron deficient portion of the molecule (a positive reactive site) e.g., an atom with incomplete electron shell or the positive end of the dipole in the molecule.If the attacking species is electron rich, it attacks these sites. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(120948,'chemistry','What will be the reactive site, if the attacking species is electron deficient?','Substrate','If attacking species is electron deficient, the reactive site for it is that part of the substrate molecule which can supply electrons, e.g., π electrons in a double bond. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Substrate','Reagent','Intermediate Specie','Both of the reactant','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(120949,'chemistry','A reagent that takes away an electron pair from reactive site is called ____________.','Electrophile','A reagent that takes away an electron pair from reactive site is called electrophile (E+) i.e., electron seeking and the reaction is called electrophilic. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Nucleophile','Electrophile','Hydrophile','Substituent','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(120950,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding electrophile: \n A) The electrophiles attack at nucleophilic centre.\n B) They donate electrons.\n C) They can be either neutral or positively charged species.','A and C Only','The electrophiles attack at nucleophilic centre, which is the electron rich centre of the substrate and the electrophiles receive electron pair from the substrate when the two undergo bonding interaction. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','A and B Only','B and C Only','A and C Only','All of these','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(213110,'chemistry','Which of the following has the highest hydration energy?','F⁻','F⁻ has the highest hydration energy\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 199 Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Physical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','F⁻','Cl⁻','Br⁻','I⁻','NEW','12-199',NULL,NULL),
(120951,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion: The electrophiles attack at nucleophilic centre, which is the electron deficient centre of the substrate.\n Reason: The electrophiles receive electron pair from the substrate when the two undergo bonding interaction.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','The electrophiles attack at nucleophilic centre, which is the electron rich centre of the substrate. Thus, the electrophiles receive electron pair from the substrate when the two undergo bonding interaction. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(120952,'chemistry','Curved arrown is used to represent:','Movement of an electron pair from the nucleophile to the electrophile','A curved-arrow notation is used to show the movement of an electron pair from the nucleophile to the electrophile.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Movement of an electron pair from the nucleophile to the electrophile','Movement of an electron pair from the electrophile to the nucleophile','Movement of an electron pair from the reactant to the product','Movement of an electron pair from the product to the reactant','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(120953,'chemistry','Match the following:','Nucleophile-NH3, Electrophile-BF3, Reagent-R-Mg-X, Substrate, NaCl','A reagent that brings an electron pair to the reactive site is called a nucleophile (Nu:) i.e., nucleus seeking and the reaction is then called nucleophilic.The electrophiles attack at nucleophilic centre, which is the electron rich centre of the substrate. Thus, the electrophiles receive electron pair from the substrate when the two undergo bonding interaction.If attacking species is electron deficient, the reactive site for it is that part of the substrate molecule which can supply electrons, e.g., π electrons in a double bond.If both the reactants supply carbon to the new bond then choice is arbitrary and in that case the molecule on which attention is focused is called substrate. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'3','match','Nucleophile,NH3','Electrophile,BF3','Reagent,R-Mg-X','Substrate,NaCl','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(120954,'chemistry','Justify the following statement: "Neutral molecules can also act as nucleophiles".','Due to the presence of lone pair of electrons','Neutral molecules can also act as nucleophiles due to the presence of lone pair of electrons. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','Due to the neutral molecule','Due to the presence of lone pair of electrons','Due to the abscence of any charge','Due to the polarity of the C-X bond','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(120955,'chemistry','What are the examples of neutral molecules?','Functional groups','Examples of electrophiles include carbocations (C+H3) and neutral molecules having functional groups like carbonyl group (>C=O) or alkyl halides (R3C-X, where X is a halogen atom). Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Functional groups','Nucleophiles','Electrophiles','Free radicals','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(120956,'chemistry','Justify the following statement: "Neutral molecules can also act as electrophiles".','Due to the polarity of the C-X bond','In neutral molecules such as alkyl halides, due to the polarity of the C-X bond a partial positive charge is generated on the carbon atom and hence the carbon atom becomes an electrophilic centre at which a nucleophile can attack. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'1','mcq','Due to the polarity of the C-X bond','Due to the presence of lone pair of electrons','Due to the abscence of any charge','Due to the neutral molecule','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(120957,'chemistry','Identify electrophilic centre in the following: CH3CH=O','C=O','The following carbon atom is electrophilic centers as they will have partial positive charge due to polarity of the bond. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Problem No. 12.13)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','C=O','CH3','-CHO','O-CH3','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(120958,'chemistry','Identify electrophilic centre in the following: CH3CN','C≡N','The following carbon atom is electrophilic centers as they will have partial positive charge due to polarity of the bond. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Problem No. 12.13)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','C≡N','CH3','-CHN','N-CH3','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(120959,'chemistry','Choose the formation of reactive intermediates when the following covalent bonds undergo heterolytic cleavage. CH3–SCH','0','Heterolytic cleavage results in making of charged species. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Problem No. 12.11)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'3','mcq','C2H5S-','0','CH4 + SCH2','S- + C2H6','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(120960,'chemistry','Nucleophiles are the halogen anions (I-, Cl-, Br-), the hydroxide ion (OH-), the cyanide ion (CN-), ammonia (NH3), and water. Justify:','These species have unshared pair of electrons','These species have unshared pair of electrons, which can be donated and shared with an electrophile. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Problem No. 12.12)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','These species have shared pair of electrons','These species have unshared pair of electrons','These species have lone pairs','Reactive sites have only six valence electrons','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(120961,'chemistry','Electrophiles are hydronium ion (H3O+, from Brønsted acids), boron trifluoride (BF3), aluminum chloride (AlCl3), and the halogen molecules fluorine (F2), chlorine (Cl2), bromine (Br2), and iodine (I2). Justify:','Reactive sites have only six valence electrons','Reactive sites have only six valence electrons; can accept electron pair from a nucleophile. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Problem No. 12.12)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Concepts in organic reaction mechanism',NULL,'2','mcq','These species have shared pair of electrons','These species have unshared pair of electrons','These species have lone pairs','Reactive sites have only six valence electrons','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(121026,'chemistry','The organic compounds having a ____ bond show electromeric effect in the presence of an attacking reagent only.','Multiple','It is a temporary effect. The organic compounds having a multiple bond (a double or triple bond) show this effect in the presence of an attacking reagent only.Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 355; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','Multiple','Only Double','Single','Only Tripple','NEW','11-355','11-277',NULL),
(121027,'chemistry','Electromeric effect is defined as the ____ transfer of a shared pair of pie-electrons to one of the atoms joined by a multiple bond on the demand of an attacking reagent.','Complete','Electromeric effect is complete transfer of pie electrons. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 355; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','Complete','Cleaved','Partial','Beyond','NEW','11-355','11-277',NULL),
(121028,'chemistry','In +E effect the ______ electrons of the multiple bond are transferred to that atom to which the reagent gets attached.','Pi (π)','In +E effect the pi−electrons of the multiple bond are transferred to that atom to which the reagent gets attached.Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 355; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'2','mcq','Pi (π)','Sigma (σ)','Ground state','Excited','NEW','11-355','11-277',NULL),
(121029,'chemistry','When inductive and electromeric effects operate in opposite directions, the ____ effect predominates.','Electromeric','Electromeric effect predominates inductive effect. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 355; Paragraph No. 4 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','Electromeric','Resonance','Mesomeric','Inductive','NEW','11-355','11-277',NULL),
(121030,'chemistry','Hyperconjugation is a general ____ interaction.','Stabilising','Hyperconjugation is a general stabilising interaction. It involves delocalisation of sigma electrons of C—H bond of an alkyl group directly attached to an atom of unsaturated system or to an atom with an unshared p orbital. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 355; Paragraph No. 5 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','Stabilising','Destabilising','Resonating','H Shifting','NEW','11-355','11-277',NULL),
(121031,'chemistry','In hyperconjugation, the sigma electrons of C—H bond of the alkyl group enter into ___ conjugation with the attached unsaturated system or with the unshared p orbital','Partial','In hyperconjugation, partial conjugation of sigma electrons happen. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 355; Paragraph No. 5 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','Partial','Complete','Beyond','Cleaved','NEW','11-355','11-277',NULL),
(121032,'chemistry','Hyperconjugation is a ____ effect.','Permanent','It is a permanent effect. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 355; Paragraph No. 5 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','fillup','per,man,ent,temp,ora,ry',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-355','11-277',NULL),
(121033,'chemistry','3 degree alkane having more methyl groups attached to it is more stable due to ___________.','Hyperconjugation','3 degree alkane having more methyl groups attached to it is more stable due to hyperconjugation. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 356; Paragraph No. 1 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'2','mcq','Resonance','Hyperconjugation','Induction','Hybridsation','NEW','11-356','11-278',NULL),
(121034,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct ?\n a. Hyperconjugation is also possible in alkenes, not alkylarenes.\n b. The hyperconjugation may also be regarded as no bond resonance.','Only b','Hyperconjugation in alekenes and alkylarenes is possible if are α hydrogens present. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 356; Paragraph No. 1 and 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Both','None of these','NEW','11-356','11-278',NULL),
(121035,'chemistry','Identify the given reaction:\n CH₃CH₂I + KOH(aq) → CH₃CH₂OH + KI','Nucleophilic substitution','Here iodide ion is substituted by hydroxide ion (Both are nucleophiles). Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 356; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','Nucleophilic substitution','Electrophilic substitution','Elimination','Addition','NEW','11-356','11-278',NULL),
(121036,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion: When inductive effect and electromeric effects operate in opposite directions,the electromeric effect predominates.\n Reason: Inductive effect is the complete transfer of shared pair of pi electrons to one of the atoms.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','When inductive and electromeric effects operate in opposite directions, the electomeric effect predominates. Electromeric effect is the complete transfer of shared pair of pi electrons to one of the atoms. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 355; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-355','11-277',NULL),
(121037,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion: The order of stability of carbo cation is 3ᵒ > 2ᵒ > 1ᵒ\n Reason: Carbon in carbo cation is in sp³ state of hybridization.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','The order of stability of carbo cation is 3ᵒ > 2ᵒ > 1ᵒ due to hyperconjugation and +I effect. Carbo cation is sp² hybrid. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 356; Paragraph No. 1 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-356','11-278',NULL),
(213111,'chemistry','With the increase in atomic number, the boiling and melting points of Halogens;','Increase steadily','With the increase in atomic number, the boiling and melting points of Halogens Increase steadily\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 199 Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Physical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Increase steadily','Decreases steadily','First increase then decrease','First decrease then increase','NEW','12-199',NULL,NULL),
(121038,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion: The movement of electrons in organic reactions can be shown by curved-arrow notation. It shows how changes in bonding occur due to electronic redistribution during the reaction.\n Reason: To show the change in position of a pair of electrons, curved arrow starts from the point from where an electron pair is shifted and it ends at a location to which the pair of electron may move.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The movement of electrons in organic reactions can be shown by curved-arrow notation. It shows how changes in bonding occur due to electronic redistribution during the reaction.To show the change in position of a pair of electrons, curved arrow starts from the point from where an electron pair is shifted and it ends at a location to which the pair of electron may move. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 352; Paragraph No. 1 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(121039,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion: The electron displacement in an organic molecule may take place in the ground state under the influence of an atom or a substituent group.\n Reason:The electron displacement in an organic molecule can also be in the presence of an appropriate attacking reagent.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','The electron displacement in an organic molecule may take place either in the ground state under the influence of an atom or a substituent group or in the presence of an appropriate attacking reagent. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 352; Paragraph No. 3 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(121040,'chemistry','The electron displacements due to the influence of an atom or a substituent group present in the molecule cause permanent __________ of the bond.','Polarlisation','The electron displacements due to the influence of an atom or a substituent group present in the molecule cause permanent polarlisation of the bond. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 352; Paragraph No. 3 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','Depolarlisation','Polarlisation','Induction','Electromeric effect','NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(121041,'chemistry','Inductive effect and resonance effects are examples of which type of electron displacements?','Permanent electron displacements','The electron displacements due to the influence of an atom or a substituent group present in the molecule cause permanent polarlisation of the bond. Inductive effect and resonance effects are examples of this type of electron displacements. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 352; Paragraph No. 3 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','Permanent electron displacements','Temporary electron displacements','Constant electron displacements','None of these','NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(121042,'chemistry','Which type of electron displacement effects are seen in a molecule when a reagent approaches to attack it?','Temporary electron displacement','Temporary electron displacement effects are seen in a molecule when a reagent approaches to attack it. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 352; Paragraph No. 3 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','Permanent electron displacement','Temporary electron displacement','Constant electron displacement','None of these','NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(121043,'chemistry','Which effect is related to the ability of substituent(s) to either withdraw or donate electron density to the attached carbon atom?','Inductive effect','The inductive effect is related to the ability of substituent(s) to either withdraw or donate electron density to the attached carbon atom. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 352; Paragraph No. 3 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','Electromeric effect','Inductive effect','Resonance effect','Mesomeric effect','NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(121044,'chemistry','Temporary electron displacement referred to as:','Polisability effect','Temporary electron displacement effects are seen in a molecule when a reagent approaches to attack it. This type of electron displacement is called electromeric effect or polarisability effect. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 352; Paragraph No. 3 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','Depolarisation','Resonance effect','Mesomeric effect','Polisability effect','NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(121045,'chemistry','In cholorethane (CH3CH2Cl) which type of bond is between C–Cl bond.','Polar covalent bond','Cholorethane (CH3CH2Cl) in which the C–Cl bond is a polar covalent bond. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 352; Paragraph No. 3 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'2','mcq','Non-polar covalent bond','Polar covalent bond','Coordinate covalent bond','Metallic bond','NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(121046,'chemistry','Justify the following statement: "Cholorethane (CH3CH2Cl) in which the C–Cl bond is a polar covalent bond."','Carbon-1 gains some positive charge and the chlorine some negative charge','Cholorethane (CH3CH2Cl) in which the C–Cl bond is a polar covalent bond.It is polarised in such a way that the carbon-1 gains some positive charge and the chlorine some negative charge. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 352; Paragraph No. 5 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','Carbon-1 possess high electronegativity and the chlorine possess low electronegativity','Carbon-1 gains some positive charge and the chlorine some negative charge','Carbon-1 possess low electronegativity and the chlorine possess high electronegativity','Carbon-1 gains some negative charge and the chlorine some positive charge','NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(213201,'chemistry','All the halogens exhibit _______ oxidation state.','1-','All the halogens exhibit –1 oxidation state. However, chlorine, bromine and iodine exhibit + 1, + 3, + 5 and + 7 oxidation states\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 199 Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','1-','1+','0','2+','NEW','12-199',NULL,NULL),
(121047,'chemistry','Justify the following statement: "The polar C–Cl bond induces polarity in the adjacent bonds."','Carbon-2 relatively carried smaller positive charge as compared to that on carbon – 1','In turn carbon-1, which has developed partial positive charge(δ+) draws some electron density towards it from the adjacent C-C bond. Consequently, some positive charge (δδ+) develops on carbon-2 also, where δδ+ symbolises relatively smaller positive charge as compared to that on carbon – 1. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 352; Paragraph No. 5 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'2','mcq','Carbon-2 relatively carried smaller positive charge as compared to that on carbon – 1','Carbon - 1 relatively carried smaller positive charge as compared to that on carbon – 2','Carbon-2 relatively carried larger positive charge as compared to that on carbon – 1','None of these','NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(121048,'chemistry','Justify the following statement: "The substitutents can be classified as electron-withdrawing or electron donating groups relative to hydrogen."','Inductive effect','Based on this ability, the substitutents can be classified as electron-withdrawing or electron donating groups relative to hydrogen. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 352; Paragraph No. 5 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','Mesomeric effect','Inductive effect','Electromeric effect','Resonance effect','NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(121049,'chemistry','How many bond length(s) Benzene should exhibit, due to C–C single and C=C double bonds?','Two different bond lengths','Benzene should exhibit two different bond lengths, due to C–C single and C=C double bonds. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 353; Paragraph No. 2 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','Two different bond lengths','Three different bond lengths','Six different bond lengths','Only one bond length','NEW','11-353','11-275',NULL),
(121050,'chemistry','The _________ structures are hypothetical and individually do not represent any real molecule.','Resonance','The resonance structures (canonical structures or contributing structures) are hypothetical and individually do not represent any real molecule. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 353; Paragraph No. 3 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','Resonance','Molecular','Atomic','Ionic','NEW','11-353','11-275',NULL),
(121051,'chemistry','What referred to difference in energy between the actual structure and the lowest energy resonance structure.','Resonance stabilisation energy','The difference in energy between the actual structure and the lowest energy resonance structure is called the resonance stabilisation energy or simply the resonance energy. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 353; Paragraph No. 3 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','Resonance stabilisation energy','Resonance excited energy','Resonance destabilisation energy','Resonance polarisation energy','NEW','11-353','11-275',NULL),
(121052,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n \n Assertion: The difference in energy between the actual structure and the lowest energy resonance structure is called the resonance stabilisation energy or simply the resonance energy.\n \n Reason: Resonating structures have the same positions of nuclei.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','The positions of all the atomic nuclei in the resonating structures are not the same.The difference in energy between the actual structure and the lowest energy resonance structure is called the resonance stabilisation energy or simply the resonance energy. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 353; Paragraph No. 3 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-353','11-275',NULL),
(121053,'chemistry','Justify the statement: "Among the resonance structures, the one which has all the atoms with octet of electrons".','Less separation of opposite charges and more dispersal of charge are more stable','Among the resonance structures, the one which has all the atoms with octet of electrons (except hydrogen which has a duplet), less separation of opposite charges and more dispersal of charge are more stable. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 354; Paragraph No. 3 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'3','mcq','More separation of same charges and less dispersal of charge are less stable','More separation of opposite charges and more dispersal of charge are less stable','Less separation of same charges and less dispersal of charge are more stable','Less separation of opposite charges and more dispersal of charge are more stable','NEW','11-354','11-276',NULL),
(121054,'chemistry','In -E effect the __________ of the multiple bond are transferred to that atom to which the attacking reagent does not get attached.','Pie - electrons','In -E effect the pie - electrons of the multiple bond are transferred to that atom to which the attacking reagent does not get attached. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 355; Paragraph No. 3 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','Pie - electrons','Sigma - electrons','Charged electrons','Lone Pairs','NEW','11-355','11-277',NULL),
(121055,'chemistry','Justify the statement: "In general, greater the number of alkyl groups attached to a positively charged carbon atom"','The greater is the hyperconjugation interaction and stabilisation of the cation','In general, greater the number of alkyl groups attached to a positively charged carbon atom, the greater is the hyperconjugation interaction and stabilisation of the cation. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 355; Paragraph No. 3 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'1','mcq','The greater is the hyperconjugation interaction and stabilisation of the cation','The greater is the hyperconjugation interaction and destabilisation of the cation','The greater is the hyperconjugation interaction and stabilisation of the anion','The greater is the hyperconjugation interaction and destabilisation of the anion','NEW','11-355','11-277',NULL),
(30921,'chemistry','Assertion: Aluminium will have first ionisation enthalpy value close to 760 kJ. \n\nReason: The first ionization enthalpy values of the third period elements, Na, Mg and Si are respectively 496, 737 and 786 kJ mol⁻¹.','Assertion is false. Reason is true.','The value for Al should be lower than that of Mg because of effective shielding of 3p electrons from the nucleus by 3s-electrons.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 89; Problem 3.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is false. Reason is true.','Both assertion and reason are false.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','NEW','11-089','11-089',NULL),
(121056,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n ASssertion: Hyperconjugation type of overlap stabilises the carbocation.\n Reason: Because electron density from the adjacent sigma bond helps in dispersing the positive charge.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','This type of overlap stabilises the carbocation because electron density from the adjacent sigma bond helps in dispersing the positive charge. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 355; Paragraph No. 6)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-355','11-277',NULL),
(121057,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n ASssertion: Hyperconjugation is also possible in alkenes and alkylarenes.\n Reason: Hyperconjugation is a general stabilising interaction.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Hyperconjugation is a general stabilising interaction. Hyperconjugation is also possible in alkenes and alkylarenes. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 355-6; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-355','11-277',NULL),
(121058,'chemistry','Justify the following statement: "There are various ways of looking at the hyperconjugative effect. One of the way is to regard C—H bond"','As possessing partial ionic character due to resonance.','There are various ways of looking at the hyperconjugative effect. One of the way is to regard C—H bond as possessing partial ionic character due to resonance.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Electron movement and effects',NULL,'2','mcq','As possessing partial ionic character due to resonance.','As possessing covalent character due to resonance.','As possessing metallic character due to resonance.','As possessing partial covalent character due to resonance.','NEW','11-356','11-278',NULL),
(121109,'chemistry','Which of the following is a method to separate glycerol from spent-lye in soap industry ?','Distillation under reduced pressure','Glycerol can be separated from spent-lye in soap industry by using this technique. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 358; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Chromatography','Distillation under reduced pressure','Fractional distillation','Crystallisation','NEW','11-358','11-280',NULL),
(121110,'chemistry','Which technique is applied to separate substances which are steam volatile and are immiscible with water ?','Steam distillation','Steam distillation technique is applied to separate substances which are steam volatile and are immiscible with water. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 359; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Steam distillation','Distillation under reduced pressure','Fractional distillation','Sublimation','NEW','11-359','11-281',NULL),
(121111,'chemistry','Which technique is employed in differential extraction?','Continuous extraction','In differential extraction, technique of continuous extraction is employed. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 360; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','Fractional distillation','Distillation under reduced pressure','Continuous extraction','Steam distillation','NEW','11-360','11-282',NULL),
(121112,'chemistry','____________ involves separation of a mixture over a column of adsorbent packed in a glass tube.','Column chromatography','Column chromatography involves separation of a mixture over a column of adsorbent (stationary phase) packed in a glass tube. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 361; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','Paper chromatography','Column chromatography','Gas chromatography','Thin layer chromatography','NEW','11-361','11-283',NULL),
(121113,'chemistry','What are the commonly used adsorbents in adsorption chromatography ?','Silica gel and alumina','Adsorption chromatography is based on the fact that different compounds are adsorbed on an adsorbent to different degrees. Commonly used adsorbents are silica gel and alumina. When a mobile phase is allowed to move. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 360; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Silica gel and copper chloride','Silica gel and alumina','Alumina and acetone','Silica gel and acetone','NEW','11-361','11-283',NULL),
(121114,'chemistry','In column chromatography, depending upon the degree to which the compounds are adsorbed, complete _________ takes place.','Separation','In column chromatography, depending upon the degree to which the compounds are adsorbed, complete separation takes place. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 361; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','Distillation','Separation','Extraction','Evaporation','NEW','11-361','11-283',NULL),
(121115,'chemistry','Thin layer chromatography involves separation of substances of a mixture over a thin layer of __________ coated on glass plate.','Adsorbent','Thin layer chromatography (TLC) is another type of adsorption chromatography, which involves separation of substances of a mixture over a thin layer of an adsorbent coated on glass plate. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 361; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Adsorbate','Solution','Compound','Adsorbent','NEW','11-361','11-283',NULL),
(121116,'chemistry','_____ chromatography is based on continuous differential partitioning of components of a mixture between stationary and mobile phases.','Partition','Partition chromatography is based on continuous differential partitioning of components of a mixture between stationary and mobile phases. Paper chromatography is a type of partition chromatography. In paper chromatography, a special quality paper known as chromatography paper is used. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 362; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Thin layer','Partition','Column','Gas','NEW','11-362','11-284',NULL),
(121117,'chemistry','In partition chromatography, the spots of the separated colourless compounds may be observed under ___ light.','Ultraviolet','The spots of the separated colourless compounds may be observed either under ultraviolet light or by the use of an appropriate spray reagent as discussed under thin layer chromatography. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 362; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Ultraviolet','Infrared','Visible','Radioactive','NEW','11-362','11-284',NULL),
(121118,'chemistry','What is the best and latest technique for isolation, purification and separation of organic compounds ?','Chromatography','Chromatography is the latest technique of separation and purification of organic compounds. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 359; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Crystallisation','Chromatography','Sublimation','Distillation','NEW','11-359','11-281',NULL),
(121119,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion: Glycerol is purified by distillation under reduced pressure.\n Reason: Method of distillation under reduced pressure is used to purify liquids having very high boiling points and those which decompose at or below their boiling points.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Glycerol can be separated from spent-lye in soap industry by using this technique. Method of distillation under reduced pressure is used to purify liquids having very high boiling points and those which decompose at or below their boiling points. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 358; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-358','11-280',NULL),
(121120,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion: Paper chromatography is a type of partition chromatography.\n Reason: Moving phase is liquid and stationary phase is solid.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Paper chromatography is a type of partition chromatography. Moving phase and stationary phase both are liquids. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 359; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-359','11-281',NULL),
(121121,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion: Various methods used for the purification of organic compounds are based on the nature of the compound and the impurity present in it.\n Reason: Once an organic compound is extracted from a natural source or synthesised in the laboratory, it is essential to purify it.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Once an organic compound is extracted from a natural source or synthesised in the laboratory, it is essential to purify it. Various methods used for the purification of organic compounds are based on the nature of the compound and the impurity present in it. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 356; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-356','11-278',NULL),
(121122,'chemistry','Justify the statement: "Finally, the purity of a compound is ascertained by determining"','Melting or boiling point','Finally, the purity of a compound is ascertained by determining its melting or boiling point. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 356; Paragraph No. 4 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Melting or boiling point','Hardness','Electrical conductivity','Density','NEW','11-356','11-278',NULL),
(121123,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion: On heating, some solid substances change from solid to vapour state without passing through liquid state.\n Reason: Sublimation is used to separate sublimable compounds from nonsublimable impurities.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','On heating, some solid substances change from solid to vapour state without passing through liquid state.The purification technique based on the above principle is known as sublimation and is used to separate sublimable compounds from nonsublimable impurities. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 357; Paragraph No. 1 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-357','11-279',NULL),
(121124,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion: If the difference in boiling points of two liquids is not much, simple distillation cannot be used to separate them.\n Reason: If simple distillation cannot be used to separate them and therefore fractional distillation is used.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','If the difference in boiling points of two liquids is not much, simple distillation cannot be used to separate them and therefore fractional distillation is used. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 357; Paragraph No. 3 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-357','11-279',NULL),
(121125,'chemistry','Which technique will be used when the vapours of liquids are formed within the same temperature range and are condensed simultaneously?','Fractional distillation','If the difference in boiling points of two liquids is not much, simple distillation cannot be used to separate them. The vapours of such liquids are formed within the same temperature range and are condensed simultaneously. The technique of fractional distillation is used in such cases. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 357; Paragraph No. 3 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Steam distillation','Fractional distillation','Both of these','None of these','NEW','11-357','11-279',NULL),
(121126,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion: In fractional distillation, vapours of a liquid mixture are passed through a fractionating column before condensation.\n Reason: The fractionating column is fitted over the mouth of the round bottom flask.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','In this technique, vapours of a liquid mixture are passed through a fractionating column before condensation. The fractionating column is fitted over the mouth of the round bottom flask. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 357; Paragraph No. 3 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-357','11-279',NULL),
(121127,'chemistry','A fractionating column provides many surfaces for ____________ between the ascending vapours and the descending condensed liquid.','Heat exchange','A fractionating column provides many surfaces for heat exchange between the ascending vapours and the descending condensed liquid. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 358; Paragraph No. 2 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Vapour exchange','Heat exchange','Vapour Pressure','Heat supplier','NEW','11-358','11-280',NULL),
(121128,'chemistry','After a series of successive distillations, the remaining liquid in the distillation flask gets enriched in __________ component.','High boiling','After a series of successive distillations, the remaining liquid in the distillation flask gets enriched in high boiling component. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 358; Paragraph No. 2 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','High boiling','Low boiling','High melting','Low melting','NEW','11-358','11-280',NULL),
(121129,'chemistry','Justify the following statement: "Adsorption chromatography is based on the fact that different compounds are adsorbed on an adsorbent to different degrees"','Thin layer chromatography','Adsorption chromatography is based on the fact that different compounds are adsorbed on an adsorbent to different degrees.Thin layer chromatography is based on the fact that different compounds are adsorbed on an adsorbent to different degrees. It is a chromatography technique used to separate non-volatile mixtures. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 357; Paragraph No. 2 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Methods of purification of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Thin layer chromatography','Partition chromatography','Paper chromatography','Column chromatography','NEW','11-357','11-279',NULL),
(121211,'chemistry','Which of the following methods is used for the estimation of nitrogen ?','Both','There are two methods for estimation of nitrogen: (i) Dumas method and (ii) Kjeldahl’s method. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 364; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Dumas method','Kjeldahl’s method','Both','None of these','NEW','11-364','11-286',NULL),
(121212,'chemistry','In Dumas method, the nitrogen containing organic compound, is heated with ____ oxide.','Copper','The nitrogen containing organic compound, when heated with copper oxide in an atmosphere of carbon dioxide. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 364; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Copper','Potassium','Nitrogen','Chlorine','NEW','11-364','11-286',NULL),
(121213,'chemistry','In Dumas’ method for estimation of nitrogen, 0.3g of an organic compound gave 50mL of nitrogen collected at 300K temperature and 715mm pressure. What is the percentage composition of nitrogen in the compound? (Aqueous tension at 300K=15 mm)','17.46%','Volume of nitrogen collected at 300K and 715mm pressure is 50 mL, *Actual pressure = 715-15 =700 mm, *Volume of nitrogen at STP = 273 x 700 x 50/300 x 760 = 41.9 ml, *22,400 mL of dinitrogen at STP weighs = 28g, *41.9 mL of nitrogen weighs= 28 x 41.9/22400 grams, *Percentage of nitrogen = 17.46% . Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 365; Problem No. 12.21)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','25.46%','17.46%','37.46%','47.46%','NEW','11-365','11-287',NULL),
(121214,'chemistry','In Kjeldahl’s method, the compound containing nitrogen is heated with concentrated ___ acid.','Sulphuric','The compound containing nitrogen is heated with concentrated sulphuric acid. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 365; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Sulphuric','Formic','Nitric','Acetic','NEW','11-365','11-287',NULL),
(121215,'chemistry','During estimation of nitrogen present in an organic compound by Kjeldahl’s method, the ammonia evolved from 0.5 g of the compound in Kjeldahl’s estimation of nitrogen, neutralized 10 mL of 1 M sulphuric acid. What is the percentage of nitrogen in the compound ?','56%','1 M of 10 mL H₂SO₄=1M \n 20 mL NH₃\n 1000 mL of 1M ammonia contains 14 g nitrogen\n 20 mL of 1M ammonia contains 14 x 20/1000 grams nitrogen\n Percentage of nitrogen = 14 x 20 x100/100 x 0.5 = 56%\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 366; Problem No. 12.22)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','5.60%','46%','56%','36%','NEW','11-366','11-288',NULL),
(121216,'chemistry','In carius method, a known mass of an organic compound is heated with fuming __ acid.','Nitric','In this method,a known mass of an organic compound is heated with fuming nitric acid in the presence of silver nitrate contained in a hard glass tube known as Carius tube\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 367; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Sulphuric','Formic','Nitric','Acetic','NEW','11-367','11-289',NULL),
(121217,'chemistry','In Carius method of estimation of halogen, 0.15 g of an organic compound gave 0.12 g of AgBr. What is the percentage of bromine in the compound ?','34.04%','Molar mass of AgBr = 108 + 80 = 188 g/mol\n 188 g AgBr contains 80 g bromine\n 0.12 g AgBr contains 80 x 0.12/188 grams bromine\n Percentage of bromine= 80 x 0.12 x 100/188 x 0.15 = 34.04%\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 367; Problem No. 12.23)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','34.04%','3.40%','0.30%','44.04%','NEW','11-367','11-289',NULL),
(213202,'chemistry','Which among the folowing cannot exhibit +1, + 3, + 5 and +7 oxidation states?','Flourine','All the halogens exhibit –1 oxidation state. However, chlorine, bromine and iodine exhibit + 1, + 3, + 5 and + 7 oxidation states\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 199 Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Iodine','Bromine','Chlorine','Flourine','NEW','12-199',NULL,NULL),
(121218,'chemistry','In estimation of sulphur, a known mass of an organic compound is heated in a Carius tube with ____ peroxide.','Sodium','A known mass of an organic compound is heated in a Carius tube with sodium peroxide or fuming nitric acid. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 367; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Sodium','Urea','Hydrogen','Carbamide','NEW','11-367','11-289',NULL),
(121219,'chemistry','In sulphur estimation, 0.157 g of an organic compound gave 0.4813 g of barium sulphate. What is the percentage of sulphur in the compound?','42.10%','Molecular mass of BaSO₄ = 137+32+64 = 233 g\n 233 g BaSO₄ contains 32 g sulphur\n 0.4813 g BaSO₄ contains 32 x 0.4813/233 grams sulphur\n Percentage of sulphur= 32 x 0.4813 x 100/ 233 x 0.5157 = 42.10%\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 367; Problem No. 12.24)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','42.10%','4.21%','0.42%','52.10%','NEW','11-367','11-289',NULL),
(121220,'chemistry','The percentage of _________ in an organic compound is usually found by difference between the total percentage composition and the sum of the percentages of all other elements.','Oxygen','The percentage of oxygen in an organic compound is usually found by difference between the total percentage composition and the sum of the percentages of all other elements. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 368; Paragraph No. 3 and 4 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','Oxygen','Carbon','Nitrogen','Hydrogen','NEW','11-368','11-290',NULL),
(121221,'chemistry','In estimatic of phosphorous,a known mass of an organic compound is heated with fuming nitric acid whereupon phosphorus present in the compound is oxidised to ____ acid.','Phosphoric','A known mass of an organic compound is heated with fuming nitric acid whereupon phosphorus present in the compound is oxidised to phosphoric acid. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 368; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Phosphoric','Ferric','Ferrous','Phosphonic','NEW','11-368','11-290',NULL),
(121222,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\nAssertion: If nitrogen or sulphur is also present in the compound, the sodium fusion extract is first boiled with concentrated nitric acid to decompose cyanide or sulphide of sodium formed during Lassaigne’s test.\nReason: These ions would otherwise interfere with sodium nitrate test for halogens.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If nitrogen or sulphur is also present in the compound, the sodium fusion extract is first boiled with concentrated nitric acid to decompose cyanide or sulphide of sodium formed during Lassaigne’s test. These ions would otherwise interfere with silver nitrate test for halogens. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 363; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-363','11-285',NULL),
(121223,'chemistry','The percentage composition of elements present in an organic compound is determined by the following methods:\n A) Carbon and Hydrogen\n B) Nitrogen and Sulphur\n C) Halogens and Oxygen\n D) Sodium and Phosphorus','A B and C Only','The percentage composition of elements present in an organic compound is determined by the following methods: Carbon,Hydrogen, Nitrogen, Sulphur, Halogens, Phosphorus and Oxygen. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 363; Paragraph No. 4 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','A B and C Only','A C and D Only','A B and D Only','All of these','NEW','11-363','11-285',NULL),
(121224,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion: The amount of ammonia produced is determined by estimating the amount of Nitric acid consumed in the reaction. \n Reason: The difference between the initial amount of acid taken and that left after the reaction gives the amount of acid reactedwith ammonia.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','The amount of ammonia produced is determined by estimating the amount of sulphuric acid consumed in the reaction. It is done by estimating unreacted sulphuric acid left after the absorption of ammonia by titrating it with standard alkali solution. The difference between the initial amount of acid taken and that left after the reaction gives the amount of acid reactedwith ammonia. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 365-6; Paragraph No. 4 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-365','11-287',NULL),
(121225,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n Assertion: Kjeldahl method is not applicable to compounds containing nitrogen in nitro and azo groups.\n Reason: Be cause nitrogen of these compounds does not change to ammonium sulphate under these conditions.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Kjeldahl method is not applicable to compounds containing nitrogen in nitro and azo groups and nitrogen present in the ring (e.g. pyridine) as nitrogen of these compounds does not change to ammonium sulphate under these conditions. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 366; Paragraph No. 2 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Assertion is false but Reason is true.','NEW','11-366','11-289',NULL),
(121226,'chemistry','Justify the following statement: "A known mass of an organic compound is heated with fuming nitric acid in the presence of silver nitrate contained in a hard glass tube"','Carius Method','A known mass of an organic compound is heated with fuming nitric acid in the presence of silver nitrate contained in a hard glass tube known as Carius tube. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 367; Paragraph No. 1 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Carius Method','Kjeldahl Method','Dumas Method','Lassaigne’s test','NEW','11-367','11-290',NULL),
(121227,'chemistry','The elements, carbon, hydrogen and nitrogen present in a compound are determined by an apparatus known as _________.','CHN elemental analyser','The elements, carbon, hydrogen and nitrogen present in a compound are determined by an apparatus known as CHN elemental analyser. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 369; Paragraph No. 3 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','CHN elemental analyser','CHN elemental calculator','CHN spirit analyser','CHN spirit calculator','NEW','11-369','11-292',NULL),
(121228,'chemistry','Justify the statement: "A definite mass of an organic compound is decomposed by heating in a stream of nitrogen gas"','For the estimation of Oxygen','However, oxygen can also be estimated directly as follows: A definite mass of an organic compound is decomposed by heating in a stream of nitrogen gas.The mixture of gaseous products containing oxygen is passed over red-hot coke when all the oxygen is converted to carbon monoxide. This mixture is passed through warm iodine pentoxide (I2O5) when carbon monoxide is oxidised to carbon dioxide producing iodine.The percentage of oxygen can be derived from the amount of iodine produced also. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 368; Paragraph No. 4 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','For the estimation of Oxygen','For the estimation of Nitrogen','For the estimation of Hydrogen','For the estimation of Sulphur','NEW','11-368','11-291',NULL),
(121229,'chemistry','The CHN elemental analyser requires only a very small amount of the substance ____________ and displays the values on a screen within a short time.','1-3 mg','The CHN elemental analyser requires only a very small amount of the substance 1-3mg and displays the values on a screen within a short time.Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 368; Paragraph No. 4 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','1-3 mg','0.1-0.3 mg','3-6 mg','0.3-0.6 mg','NEW','11-368','11-291',NULL),
(121230,'chemistry','0.3780 g of an organic chloro compound gave 0.5740 g of silver chloride in Carius estimation. Calculate the percentage of chlorine present in the compound?','37.60%','0.3780 g of organic compound gives 0.5740 g of AgCl.\n 143.32g of AgCl contains 35.5 g of Cl.\n 0.5740 g of AgCl contains 35.5 × 0.5740 / 143.32​ = 0.1421g of chlorine.\n The percentage of chlorine in the organic chloro compound is 0.1421×100 / 0.3780 = 37.60%\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 374; Exercise No. 12.34 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','37.60%','73.60%','67.30%','0.38%','NEW','11-374','11-297',NULL),
(121231,'chemistry','An organic compound contains 69% carbon and 4.8% hydrogen, the remainder being oxygen. Calculate the masses of carbon dioxide and water produced when 0.20 g of this substance is subjected to complete combustion?','0.50g','Percentage of carbon in organic compound =69% \n So, 100 g of organic compound contains 69 g of carbon. \n ∴ 0.2 g of organic compound will contain = 69×0.2 / 100 =0.138 g of carbon\n he molecular mass of carbon dioxide, CO2 =44 g \n So, 12 g of carbon is contained in 44 g of CO2\n 0.138 g of carbon will be contained =44×0.138 / 12 =0.506 g of carbon.\n Thus, 0.506 g of CO2 will be produced on complete combustion of 0.2 g of an organic compound.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 374; Exercise No. 12.32 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','0.50g','5.0g','0.20g','2.0g','NEW','11-374','11-297',NULL),
(121232,'chemistry','In the estimation of sulphur by Carius method, 0.468 g of an organic sulphur compound afforded 0.668 g of barium sulphate. Find out the percentage of sulphur in the given compound?','19.60%','The molar mass of BaSO4 is 233 g/mol. Thus, 233 g of BaSO4 corresponds to 32 g of sulphur.\n 0.668 g of BaSO4 will contain 32×0.668 / 233 =0.0917g of sulphur.\n The percentage of sulphur in the given compound is 0.0917×100 / 0.468 =19.6 %.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 374; Exercise No. 12.35 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Qualitative and quantitative of organic compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','19.60%','16.90%','12.67%','20.315','NEW','11-374','11-297',NULL),
(121326,'chemistry','Isomers of a substance must have the same:','Molecular formula','Isomers must have same molecular formula but different structural formula. (NEET 1991)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Structural formula','Physical properties','Chemical properties','Molecular formula','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(121327,'chemistry','How many chain isomers could be obtained from the alkane C₆H₁₄?','5','5-chain isomers are obtained from alkane C₆H₁₄ (NEET 1998)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','4','5','6','7','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(121328,'chemistry','A tertiary butyl carbocation is more stable than a secondary butyl carbocation because of which of the following?','Hyperconjugation','It is stable due to Hyperconjugation (NEET 2020)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','–I effect of – CH₃ groups','+R effect of – CH₃ groups','–R effect of – CH₃ groups','Hyperconjugation','NEW','11-355','11-277',NULL),
(121329,'chemistry','The most stable carbocation, among the following is:','CH₃—C⁺H—CH₂—CH₂—CH₃','Among the given carbocations, CH₃—C⁺H—CH₂—CH₂—CH₃ is most stable carbocation. As is consists of maximum number of a-hydrogens and stablised by hyperconjugation. (Odisha NEET 2019)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(CH₃)₃C—C⁺H—CH₃','CH₃—CH₂—C⁺H—CH₂—CH₃','CH₃—C⁺H—CH₂—CH₂—CH₃','CH₃—CH₂—C⁺H₂','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(121330,'chemistry','Which of the following carbocations is expected to be most stable in the given diagram(12128)','C','-I effect of nitro group will be lowest para position and therefore carbo cation will be most stable at that position. (NEET 2018)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','11-352','11-274','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121330q.png'),
(121331,'chemistry','The correct statement regarding electrophile is:','Electrophile can be either neutral or positively charged species and can form a bond by accepting a pair of electrons from a nucleophile','Electrophile can be either neutral or positively charged species and can form a bond by accepting a pair of electrons from a nucleophile. (NEET 2017)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Electrophile is a negatively charged species and can form a bond by accepting a pair of electrons from another electrophile','Electrophiles are generally neutral species and can form a bond by accepting a pair of electrons from a nucleophile','Electrophile can be either neutral or positively charged species and can form a bond by accepting a pair of electrons from a nucleophile','Electrophile is a negatively charged species and can form a bond by accepting a pair of electrons from a nucleophile','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(121332,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is not correct for a nucleophile?','Nucleophile is a Lewis acid','Nucleophiles are electron rich species hence, they are Lewis bases. (NEET 2015)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ammonia is a nucleophile','Nucleophiles attack low electron density sites','Nucleophiles are not electron seeking','Nucleophile is a Lewis acid','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(121333,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds can have hyperconjugation in the given diagram(12129)','III only','Hyperconjugation can occur only in compound III as it has α-hydrogen atom. (NEET 2015)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','I only','II only','III only','I and III','NEW','11-355','11-277','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121333q.png'),
(121334,'chemistry','In which of the following compounds, the C—Cl bond ionisation shall give most stable carbonium ion in the given diagram(12130)','D','D will be most stable due to hyperconjugation. (NEET 2015)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','11-355','11-277','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121334q.png'),
(121335,'chemistry','What is the hybridization of benzyl carbonium ion?','sp²','Carbo cations are sp² hybrid. (Karnataka NEET 2013)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','sp','sp²','sp³','sp²d','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(121336,'chemistry','Which of the following species is not electrophilic in nature?','H₃O⁺','H₃O⁺ has no vacant orbital. (NEET 2010)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Cl⁺','BH₃','H₃O⁺','NO₂⁺','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(121337,'chemistry','For (i) I⁻, (ii) Cl⁻, (iii) Br⁻ the increasing order of nucleophilicity would be:','iii > ii > i','In case of different nucleophiles, but present in the same group in the periodic table, then larger is the atomic mass, higher is the nucleophilicity. Hence, the increasing order of nucleophilicity of the halide ions here is iii > ii > i (NEET 2007)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','order','1,iii','2,ii','3,i',NULL,'NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(121338,'chemistry','Which amongst the following is the most stable carbocation in the given diagram(12131)','D','D is most stable as it has 9 alpha hydrogen for hyperconjugation. (NEET 2005)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','11-355','11-277','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121338q.png'),
(121339,'chemistry','Paper chromatography is an example of:','Partition chromatography','Paper chromatography is a type of partition chromatography. (NEET 2020)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Adsorption chromatography','Partition chromatography','Thin layer chromatography','Column chromatography','NEW','11-362','11-284',NULL),
(121340,'chemistry','The most suitable method of separation of 1 : 1 mixture of ortho and para-nitrophenols is:','Steam distillation','The o- and p-nitrophenols are separated by steam distillation since o-isomer is steam volatile due to intramolecular H-bonding while p-isomer is not steam volatile due to association of molecules by intermolecular H-bonding. (NEET 2017, 1999, 1993)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Chromatography','Crystallisation','Steam distillation','Sublimation','NEW','11-359','11-281',NULL),
(121341,'chemistry','The best method for the separation of naphthalene and benzoic acid from their mixture is:','Sublimation','Sublimation method is used for those organic substances which pass directly from solid to vapour state on heating and vice-versa on cooling e.g. benzoic acid, naphthalene, camphor, anthracene, etc. Naphthalene is volatile and benzoic acid is non-volatile due to the formation of dimer. (NEET 2005)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Chromatography','Crystallisation','Steam distillation','Sublimation','NEW','11-359','11-281',NULL),
(121342,'chemistry','In steam distillation of toluene, the pressure of toluene in vapour is:','Less than pressure of barometer','In steam distillation of toluene, the pressure of toluene in vapour is less than pressure of barometer, because it is carried out when a solid or liquid is insoluble in water and is volatile with steam but the impurities are non-volatile. (NEET 2001)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Equal to pressure of barometer','Less than pressure of barometer','Equal to vapour pressure of toluene in simple distillation','More than vapour pressure of toluene in simple distillation','NEW','11-359','11-281',NULL),
(121343,'chemistry','Which of the following technique is most suitable for purification of cyclohexanone from a mixture containing benzoic acid, isoamyl alcohol, cyclohexane and cyclohexanone?','IR spectroscopy','In the IR spectroscopy, each functional group appears at a certain peak . So, cyclohexanone can be identified by carbonyl peak. (NEET 1997)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Sublimation','Crystallisation','Evaporation','IR spectroscopy','NEW','11-356','11-278',NULL),
(121344,'chemistry','Nitrogen detection in an organic compound is carried out by Lassaigne’s test. The blue colour formed corresponds to which of the following formula?','Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃','Ferric ferrocyanide gives prussian blue color. (NEET Karnataka 2013)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Fe₃[Fe(CN)₆]₂','Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃','Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₂','Fe₃[Fe(CN)₆]₃','NEW','11-362','11-284',NULL),
(121345,'chemistry','The Lassaigne’s extract is boiled with conc. HNO₃ while testing for halogens. By doing so it:','Decomposes Na₂S and NaCN, formed','In case of Lassaigne’s test of halogens, it is necessary to remove sodium cyanide and sodium sulphide from the sodium extract if nitrogen and sulphur are present. This is done by boiling the sodium extract with conc. HNO₃ . (NEET 2011)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Decomposes Na₂S and NaCN, formed','Helps in the precipitation of AgCl','Increases the solubility product of AgCl','Increases the concentration of NO₃⁻ ions','NEW','11-362','11-284',NULL),
(121346,'chemistry','In sodium fusion test of organic compounds, the nitrogen of the organic compound is converted into:','Sodium cyanide','Sodium cyanide (Na + C + N → NaCN) (NEET 1991)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Sodamide','Sodium cyanide','Sodium nitrite','Sodium nitrate','NEW','11-362','11-284',NULL),
(121347,'chemistry','Lassaigne’s test is used in qualitative analysis to detect:','All of these','Lassaigne’s test is used in qualitative analysis to detect: S, Cl, N. (NEET 1989)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Nitrogen','Sulphur','Chlorine','All of these','NEW','11-362','11-284',NULL),
(121348,'chemistry','A blue colouration is not obtained when:','Copper sulphate solution reacts with K₄[Fe(CN)₆]','Copper sulphate solution reacts with K₄[Fe(CN)₆] to form a chocolate ppt. (NEET 1989)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ammonium hydroxide dissolves in copper sulphate','Copper sulphate solution reacts with K₄[Fe(CN)₆]','Ferric chloride reacts with sod. ferrocyanide','Anhydrous CuSO₄ is dissolved in water','NEW','11-363','11-285',NULL),
(121349,'chemistry','In Duma’s method for estimation of nitrogen, 0.25 g of an organic compound gave 40 mL of nitrogen collected at 300 K temperature and 725 mm pressure. If the aqueous tension at 300 K is 25 mm, the percentage of nitrogen in the compound is:','16.76','Mass of organic compound = 0.25 g, Experimental values, At STP\n V₁ = 40 mL, V₂ = ?\n P₁ = 725 – 25 = 700 mm, P₂ = 760 mm *P₁V₁/T₁ = P₂V₂/T₂\n V₂ = 33.52ml *22400 mL of N₂ at STP weighs = 28 g\n ∴ 33.52 mL of N₂ at STP weighs = 0.0419 g\n Percentage of nitrogen = 16.76\n (NEET 2015)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','16.76','15.76','17.76','18.2','NEW','11-364','11-286',NULL),
(121350,'chemistry','In the Kjeldahl’s method for estimation of nitrogen present in a soil sample, ammonia evolved from 0.75 g of sample neutralized 10 mL of 1 M H₂SO₄. The percentage of nitrogen in the soil is:','37.33','H₂SO4 + 2NH₃ → (NH₄)₂SO₄\n 10 mL of 1 M H₂SO₄ = 10 mmol\n Acid used for the absorption of ammonia = 10 mL of 2 N (or 1 M) H₂SO₄\n Percentage of nitrogen = 1.4 x N x V/W = 1.4 x 2 x 10/0.75 = 37.33\n (NEET 2014)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','37.33','45.33','35.33','43.33','NEW','11-366','11-288',NULL),
(121351,'chemistry','In Dumas’ method of estimation of nitrogen 0.35 g of an organic compound gave 55 mL of nitrogen collected at 300 K temperature and 715 mm pressure. The percentage composition of nitrogen in the compound would be (aqueous tension at 300 K = 15 mm)','16.45','Given : V₁ = 55 mL, V₂ = ?\n P₁ = 715 – 15 = 700 mm, P₂ = 760 mm\n T₁ = 300 K, T₂ = 273 K\n P₁V₁/T₁ = P₂V₂/T₂\n Volume of nitrogen at STP\n V₂ = 46.09 mL\n % of nitrogen = V2/W where W = the mass of organic compound\n % of nitrogen = 46.09/8 x 0.35 = 16.46\n (NEET 2011)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','17.45','16.45','15.45','14.45','NEW','11-364','11-286',NULL),
(121352,'chemistry','Which is the best-suited method for the separation of para and ortho-nitrophenols from 1:1 mixture?','Steam distillation','Steam distillation is the most suitable method of separation of 1:1 mixture of ortho and para nitrophenols as there is intramolecular H-bonds in ortho nitrophenol. (NEET 2017)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Crystallisation','Chromatography','Sublimation','Steam distillation','NEW','11-359','11-281',NULL),
(121353,'chemistry','Find the incorrect statement for a nucleophile:','A nucleophile is a Lewis acid','Lewis bases are sometimes called nucleophiles, water acts as a nucleophile (donates electrons), and the carbocation acts as an electrophile (receives electrons). (NEET 2015)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','A nucleophile is a Lewis acid','Ammonia is a nucleophile','Nucleophiles do not seek electron','Nucleophiles attack low electron density sites','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(121354,'chemistry','Which among the following is the most deactivating meta-directing group in aromatic substitution reaction?','0','-NO2 is most deactivating due to -1 and -M effect. (NEET 2013)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','-COOH','-SO3H','0','-CN','NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(121355,'chemistry','Find the compound which undergoes nucleophilic substitution reaction exclusively by an SN1 mechanism.','Benzyl chloride','Benzyl chloride undergoes exclusively SN1 mechanism because it forms resonance stabilized benzyl carbocation. (NEET 2016-19)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Benzyl chloride','Ethyl chloride','Chlorobenzene','Isopropyl chloride','NEW','11-356','11-278',NULL),
(121356,'chemistry','How many structural isomers are possible if one hydrogen in diphenylmethane is replaced by chlorine?','4','4 structural isomers are possible, when one of the hydrogen is replaced by a chlorine atom. ( NEET )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','8','4','6','7','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(121357,'chemistry','The correct order of increasing bond length of C-H,C-O,C-C and C=C is :','C-H<C=C<C-O<C-C','Hint: As the size of the atom decreases, the bond length of the bond also decreases. The C-H bond length is the shortest due to the small sizes of hydrogen and carbon atom.\nThe C-C bond length is the longest. The size of the atom increases as C>O>H.  As the size of an atom increases, the bond length also increases. The single bond is longer than the double bond. \nThe correct order of increasing bond length of  C-H, C-O, C-C and C=C is \nC-H<C=C<C-O<C-C\n.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','C-C<C=C<C-H<C-O','C-O<C-H<C-C<C=C','C-H<C-O<C-C<C=C','C-H<C=C<C-O<C-C','NEW','11-335','11-257',NULL),
(121358,'chemistry','the correct order of increasing electronegativity of hybrid orbitals of carbon is :','sp>sp2>sp3','Hint: As s character percentage increases, the electronegativity of carbon also increases.\n\nAs electronegativity of carbon increases as the s-character of the hybrid orbital increases. The percentage character in sp, sp2, and sp3 orbitals are as follows:\n\nsp =50% s character \nsp2=33.3% s character \nsp3 = 25% s character\n\nHence, the correct order regarding the electronegativity of hybrid orbitals of carbon is \nsp>sp2>sp3','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','sp>sp2<sp3','sp>sp2>sp3','sp<sp2>sp3','sp<sp2<sp3','NEW','11-335','11-257',NULL),
(121359,'chemistry','the general molecular formula, which represents the homologus series of alkanols is:','CnH2n+2O','Those derivatives of alkanes which are obtained by the replacement of —H of alkanes with —OH (hydroxyl groups) are called as alkanols i.e. alkane + ol. In other words, alkanols are nothing but alcohols. \nC\nn\nH\n2\nn\n+\n2\nAlkanes\n  \n−\nH\n−−→\n+\nO\nH\n \nC\nn\nH\n2\nn\n+\n1\nO\nH\n or CnH2n+2O','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','CnH2nOn','CnH2nO',NULL,'CnH2n+2O','NEW','11-340','11-262',NULL),
(30922,'chemistry','The following elements in the decreasing order of their ionisation energy:','Helium > Neon > Krypton > Xenon','The following elements in the decreasing order of their ionisation energy are as shown in the diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 86; diagram(ne0310)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'3','order','1,Helium','2,Neon','3,Krypton','4,Xenon','NEW','11-086','11-086',NULL),
(121360,'chemistry','the structure of trans 2-hexanal is : Refer diagram (121360Q)','3','HINT: For trans- conformation, the groups lie on the opposite sides of double bond.\n\nExplanation:\n\nSTEP 1: Consider the longest chain containing the double bond: If two groups (attached to the carbons of the double bond) are on the same side of the double bond, the isomer is a cis alkene.\n\nIf the two groups lie on opposite sides of the double bond, the isomer is a trans alkene.\n\nSTEP 2: The principal chain is having 6 C atoms .Here , aldehyde is the main functional group and the double bond is at 2nd position .So, 3rd option can\'t be correct .\n\nOnly the conformation is different in other two. But the trans- conformation is in 2nd option. \n\nThus, option 2 is correct.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-341','11-263','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121360q.png'),
(121365,'chemistry','The isomerism exhibited by n- propyl alcohol and isopropyl alcohol is','Position isomerism','Isomers:These are the compounds that have the same molecular formula but different structures or stereochemistries.\nThere is a wide range of classification of organic molecules based on their structures. Positional isomers: Isomers having different positions of their functional groups . The molecular formula of propyl alcohol is C3H8O.\nIn the positional isomers, these things might differ:\nThe position of the substituent.\nThe position of the functional group.\nArrangement of carbon atoms in the chain.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Metamerism','Position isomerism','Functional isomerism','More than one of the above','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(121366,'chemistry','CH3-CH3-CH3-CH2-CH3, (CH3)2-CH-CH3, and CH3-CH2--CH(CH3)2 increasing order of stability of free radicals formed from the homolytic fission of above mentioned three alkanes :','CH3-CH2 <CH3-CH-CH3< (CH3)2C-CH2CH3 <(CH3)3C','The stability of free radicals depends on hyperconjugation effect','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(CH3)2C-CH2-CH3 < CH3-CH-CH3< CH3-CH2< (CH3)3C','CH3-CH2 <CH3-CH-CH3< (CH3)2C-CH2CH3 <(CH3)3C','CH3-CH2 <CH3-CH-CH3<(CH3)3C< (CH3)2C-CH2CH3','(CH3)3C<(CH3)2C-CH2CH3<CH3-CH-CH3< CH3-CH2','NEW','11-355','11-277',NULL),
(121367,'chemistry','the species among the following that is not an electrophile is:','H3O+','Electron deficient species are known as electrophiles. H3O+ has a lone pair of electrons over oxygen for donation, so it is not an electron-deficient species, hence not an electrophile','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cl+','BF3','H3O+','NO2+','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(121368,'chemistry','the order of I-effect is','NR3 > -F > -OR','Hint: Electronegative element shows -I effect.\n\nAs the electronegativity of atom or group increases -I effect is also increases. Here, the order of atom according to their electronegativity is as follows:\n\nF> O> N\n\nBut nitrogen containing positive charge is more electronegative than neutral nitrogen, Thus the correct order of electronegativity is as follows:\n\n−\nN\nR\n+\n3\n \n>\n \nF\n \n>\n \nO\nR\n \nThus, correct order of –I effect is \n−\nN\nR\n+\n3\n \n>\n \nF\n \n>\n \nO\nR','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','NR3+ > -oR >- -F','-F> NR3 > -OR','NR3 > -F > -OR','-OR > NR3 > -F','NEW','11-352','11-274',NULL),
(121369,'chemistry','(-):CH2-C=O-CH3 and CH2=C-O-CH3 are:','resonating structures','Hint: Delocalization of lone pair with π bond\n\nResonance structures are sets of Lewis structures that describe the delocalization of electrons in a polyatomic ion or a molecule.\n\nIn resonance, the position of the π bond and the position of the lone pair is changed but the position of the atom remain the same.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','resonating structures','tautomers','geometrical isomers','optical isomers','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(121370,'chemistry','polarization of electrons in acrolein may be written as :','+CH2=CH_CH-O','In acrolein resonance takes place. The resonating structure of acrolein is as follows:\n\n\n\nFrom the resonance structure it is clear that, CHO group carbon gets positive charge, oxyygen gets negative charge and beta carbon gets positive charge.\n\nHence, the correct option is option fourth.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','+CH2=CH_-CHO','-CH2=CH_+CHO','-CH2=CH-C+HO','+CH2=CH_CH-O','NEW','11-353','11-275',NULL),
(121371,'chemistry','arrange the following in increasing order of stability a) (CH3)2 C-CH2-CH3  b) (CH3)3C  c) (CH3)2 CH d)CH3 CH2  e)CH3','e<d<c<a<b','Hint: Follow hyperconjugation','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','e<d<c<a<b','d<e<c<a<b','a<e<d<c,b','e<d<c<b<a','NEW','11-355','11-277',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(121372,'chemistry','the liquid compound x can be separated by steam distillation only if it is :','steam volatile ,immiscible with water','Hint - Compounds purified by steam distillation which are immiscible in water but steam volatile.\n\nIn steam distillation, hot steam is forced through the matrix of raw material, opening the cavities in which the oil is held, and volatilizing the oils.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','steam volatile ,immiscible with water','not steam volatile ,miscible with water','steam volatile ,miscible with water','not steam volatile ,immiscible with water','NEW','11-359','11-281',NULL),
(121373,'chemistry','In Lassaigne extract of an organic compound both nitrogen and sulphur are present ,which gives blood red colour due to Fe+3 due to formation of :','[Fe (SCN)]+2','Hint: Thiocyanate forms a complex with Fe3+ ion\n\nIn case, nitrogen and sulfur both are present in an organic compound, sodium thiocyanate is formed. It gives blood red color and no Prussian blue since there are no free cyanide ions. The reaction is as follows:\n\nN\na\n+\nC\n+\nN\n+\nS\n⟶\nN\na\nS\nC\nN\nF\ne\n3\n+\n+\nS\nC\nN\n−\n⟶\n[\nF\ne\n(\nS\nC\nN\n)\n]\n2\n+\n\n\nThus, the blood red color is due to the formation of \n[\nF\ne\n(\nS\nC\nN\n)\n]\n2\n+\n .Thus, option 1 is the correct answer.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','[Fe (SCN)]+2','Fe4 [Fe(CN)6]3 . x H2O','NaSCN','[Fe (CN)5NOS]-','NEW','11-362','11-284',NULL),
(121374,'chemistry','the Kjeldahl method for the estimation of nitrogen can be used to estimate the amount of nitrogen in which of the following compounds :','Aniline','Kjeldahl\'s method is not applicable to the compounds containing nitrogen having nitro and azo group and nitrogen present in the ring (pyridine), as the nitrogen of these compounds does not change to ammonium sulphate under these conditions.\n\nThus, only aniline can be used to estimate the amount of nitrogen in Kjeldahl\'s method. The structure is as follows:\n\n\n because the nitrogen in these compounds cannot be converted to ammonium sulphate under the conditions of this method.\nKjeldahl method does not directly detect N-N and N-O linkages','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Azobenzene','Noitrobenzene','Pyridine','Aniline','NEW','11-366','11-288',NULL),
(121375,'chemistry','the outer orbital of C in ethene molecule can be considered to be hybridized to be given three sp2 equivalent orbitals .the total no, of sigma and pie bonds in ethene molecule will be ;','5 and 1respectively','HINT: CH2=CH2 is ethene.\n\nExplanation: \n\nAll single bonds are sigma bonds and double bond contains one sigma and one pi bond.\n\nIn ethene, CH2=CH2 there are 5 sigma bonds and one pi bond. \n\nSigma and Pi Bonds | CK-12 Foundation\n\nThus, option 3 is the correct answer.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','3 and 1 respectively','4 and 1 respectively','5 and 1respectively','1 and 2 respectively','NEW','11-335','11-257',NULL),
(121376,'chemistry','the total no. of pi bond electrons in the following structure are :','8','Hint: Double bond contains one sigma bond and one pie bond.\n\nElectrons involved in pi bonds are pi electrons. Since, there are 4 \nπ\n-bonds in a given structure. Hence, the number of \nπ\n-electrons is 8 in the given compound. The structure is as follows:','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','4','8','12','16','NEW','11-335','11-257','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121376q.png'),
(121377,'chemistry','the correct order of acidity among following:','FCH2COOH> CICH2COOH> BrCH2COOH > CH3COOH','Hint: Acidity directly proportional to the stability of conjugate base\n\nThe conjugate base of given acid are as follows:\n\nFCH\n2\nCOO\n−\n,\n \nClCH\n2\nCOO\n−\n,\n \nBrCH\n2\nCOO\n−\n,\n \nand\n \nCH\n3\nCOO\n−\nThe stability of conjugate base depends on the -I effect of halogen, as the electronegativity of halogen increases the stability of conjugate base increases. The stability order of conjugate base is as follows:\n\nFCH\n2\nCOO\n−\n>\nClCH\n2\nCOO\n−\n>\n \nBrCH\n2\nCOO\n−\n>\nCH\n3\nCOO\n−\nHence, the correct order of the acidity is as follows:\n\n\nFCH2COOH >ClCH2COOH >BrCH2COOH >CH3COOH','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CH3COOH>BrCH2COOH> CICH2COOH >FCH2COOH','FCH2COOH> CH3COOH> BrCH2COOH> CICH2COOH','BrCH2COOH > CICH2COOH> FCH2COOH > CH3COOH','FCH2COOH> CICH2COOH> BrCH2COOH > CH3COOH','NEW','11-350','11-272',NULL),
(121378,'chemistry','match the list 1 and 2 : LIST 1 : a) separation of aniline water mixture  b)separation of aniline chloroform mixture c) separation of glycerol from spent-lye d) separation of different fraction of crude oil  LIST 2 : 1) fractional distillation 2) distillation under reduced pressure 3) distillation 4) steam distillation  choose the correct options from below :','a) 4   b)  3   c)  2   d)1','Hint: The steam distillation technique is applied to separate substances that are steam volatile and immiscible with water.\n\n1. A significant difference in the B.P. of chloroform = 334 K B.P. and aniline = 457 K is present thus it can be separated by  simple distillation.\n\n2. Steam distillation method is used when the substances involved are steam volatile and immiscible in water.\nAniline satisfies both these conditions and, hence, this method is used to separate aniline from aniline-water mixture.\n\n3. This method is used to purify liquids having very high boiling points and those, which decompose at or below their boiling points.. Spent lye is basically Glycerol containing liquor that is formed on saponification of fats with boiling lye.\n\n4. Fractional distillation is the use of distillation to separate a liquid mixture into different parts (fractions) that differ in boiling point. This method is used to purify liquids having very high boiling points and those, which decompose at or below their boiling points.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a)  1  b) 3    c) 2    d)4','a) 4   b) 1    c) 3    d) 2','a)   4 b) 2    c) 3  d) 1','a) 4   b)  3   c)  2   d)1','NEW','11-356','11-278',NULL),
(121379,'chemistry','the correct structure of 2,6 dimethyl-dec-4 ene is : Refer diagram (121379Q)','B','Hint: Numbering should be down in anticlockwise direction.\n\n2,6-Dimethyl-dec-4-ene indicates that the compound contains 10 carbon atoms because the parent chain name is \'dec\'. At the 2nd and 6th positions two methyl groups are present and at the 4th position double bond is present. Hence, the correct structure is','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','11-341','11-263','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121379q.png'),
(121380,'chemistry','which of the following chemical structure is non aromatic:','d','Every atom of a ring must be sp2 hybridized','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-339','11-261','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121380q.png'),
(121381,'chemistry','the no of sigma,pi and lone pair of electron in pyridine is respectively :','11,3,1','Hint: Pyridine has one lone pair. \n\nThe structure of pyridine is as follows:\n\n\n\nFrom the structure, it is clear that pyridine has 11 σ bonds, three π bonds, and one lone pair. \n\nThus, option 4th is the correct answer.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','12,2,1','11,2,0','12,3,0','11,3,1','NEW','11-335','11-257',NULL),
(121417,'chemistry','Nucleophile is a species that should have:\n\na. a pair of electrons to donate\n\nb. positive charge\n\nc. negative charge\n\nd. electron deficient species','a,c','A reagent that brings an electron pair to the reactive site is called a nucleophile (Nu:) i.e., nucleus seeking and the reaction is then called nucleophilic.The electrophiles attack at nucleophilic centre, which is the electron rich centre of the substrate. Thus, the electrophiles receive electron pair from the substrate when the two undergo bonding interaction.If attacking species is electron deficient, the reactive site for it is that part of the substrate molecule which can supply electrons, e.g., π electrons in a double bond.If both the reactants supply carbon to the new bond then choice is arbitrary and in that case the molecule on which attention is focused is called substrate.  Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 351; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','b,d','NEW','11-351','11-273',NULL),
(121418,'chemistry','Hyperconjugation involves delocalisation of :\n\na. electron of carbon hydrogen σ bond of an alkyl group directly attacked to an atom of unsaturated system.\n\nb. electron of carbon hydrogen σ bond of an alkyl group directly attacked to the positively charged carbon atom.\n\nc. π-electrons of carbon- carbon bond.\n\nd. lone pair of electrons','a,b','Hyperconjugation is a general stabilising interaction. It involves delocalisation of sigma electrons of C—H bond of an alkyl group directly attached to an atom of unsaturated system or to an atom with an unshared p orbital. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 355; Paragraph No. 5 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','b,d','NEW','11-355','11-277',NULL),
(121419,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Simple distillation can help in separating a mixture of propan-1–ol (boiling point 97°C) and propanone (boiling point 56°C).\n\nReason (R) Liquids with a difference of more than 20°C in their boiling points can be separated by simple distillation.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Simple distillation can separate a mixture of propan-1-ol and propanone because they have significantly different boiling points. Liquids with a difference in their boiling points of more than 20°C can be separated by simple distillation. During simple distillation, the component with the lower boiling point evaporates first, and its vapor is condensed and collected separately from the component with the higher boiling point.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-358','11-280',NULL),
(121420,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Energy of resonance hybrid is equal to the average of energies of all canonical forms.\n\nReason (R) Resonance hybrid cannot be presented by a single structure.','Assertion is false but reason is true','Resonance hybrid is a combination of all possible canonical forms, and its energy is a weighted average of the energies of all the contributing structures.\n\nResonance hybrid cannot be represented by a single structure, as it is a combination of two or more canonical forms. The actual electronic structure of a molecule is a hybrid of all the contributing structures, and this is referred to as a resonance hybrid.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','11-353','11-275',NULL),
(121421,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Pent-1-ene and pent-2-ene are position isomers.\n\nReason (R) Position isomers differ in the position of functional group or a substituent.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','The assertion (A) is correct: pent-1-ene and pent-2-ene are position isomers, meaning they have the same molecular formula (C5H10) but differ in the position of their double bond.\n\nThe reason (R) is also correct: position isomers differ in the position of a functional group or a substituent. In this case, the functional group is the double bond, and it is located in a different position in each isomer. In pent-1-ene, the double bond is located at the first carbon atom, whereas in pent-2-ene, the double bond is located at the second carbon atom.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(121422,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Sulphur present in an organic compound can be estimated quantitatively by Carius method.\n\nReason (R) Sulphur is separated easily from other atoms in the molecule and gets precipitated as light yellow solid.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion (A) is correct: sulfur present in an organic compound can be estimated quantitatively by the Carius method. The Carius method involves the oxidation of the organic compound with fuming nitric acid in a sealed tube, which converts the sulfur to sulfuric acid. The sulfuric acid is then precipitated and weighed to determine the amount of sulfur in the original sample.\n\nThe reason (R), however, is not correct explanation. While sulfur can be separated from other atoms in the molecule by the Carius method, it does not necessarily get precipitated as a light yellow solid. Instead, the sulfur is converted to sulfuric acid, which remains in solution after the oxidation step.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','11-367','11-289',NULL),
(121423,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Components of a mixture of red and blue inks can be separated by distributing the components between stationary and mobile phases in paper chromatography.\n\nReason (R) The coloured components of inks migrate at different rates because paper selectively retains different components according to the difference in their partition between the two phases','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','The assertion (A) is correct: Paper chromatography can separate the components of a mixture of red and blue inks based on their partitioning between a stationary phase (the paper) and a mobile phase (a solvent). The reason (R) is also correct: Coloured components of inks migrate at different rates because paper selectively retains different components according to the difference in their partition between the two phases.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','11-362','11-284',NULL),
(121508,'chemistry','The IUPAC name of CH3COCH(CH₃)₂. is','3-methyl-2-butanone','The IUPAC name for the compound CH3COCH(CH₃)₂ is 3-methyl-2-butanone because it has a butane chain with a ketone functional group on the second carbon, and substituents of ethyl and two methyl groups on the first and third carbons, respectively.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','Isopropylmethyl ketone','2-methyl-3-butanone','4-methylisopropyl ketone','3-methyl-2-butanone','NEW','11-341','11-263',NULL),
(121520,'chemistry','The IUPAC name of the compound shown below is: Refer diagram(12156)','3-ethyl4,4-dimethylheptane','The ethyl group is located at position 3 and the two methyl groups are at positions 4 and 4\'. The numbering starts from the end closest to the substituent. The prefix "4,4-dimethyl-" is used to denote the two methyl groups. The prefix "2," is used to indicate the position of the two methyl groups relative to each other. Refer diagram [AIEEE 2007](e1218)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','1,1-diethyl-2,2-dimethylpentane','4,4-dimethyl-5,5-diethylpentane','5,5-diethyl-4,4-dimethylpentane','3-ethyl4,4-dimethylheptane','NEW','11-341','11-263','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121520q.png'),
(121523,'chemistry','The number of stereoisomers possible for a compound of the molecular formula CH3-CH=CH(OH)-Me is: [AIEEE 2009]','4','The molecule CH3-CH=CH(OH)-Me has two stereocenters, the carbon atom adjacent to the double bond and the carbon atom adjacent to the hydroxyl group. \nThe four possible stereoisomers are:\n\n(R,R)-2-methyl-3-hydroxybutane\n\n(S,S)-2-methyl-3-hydroxybutane\n\n(R,S)-2-methyl-3-hydroxybutane\n\n(S,R)-2-methyl-3-hydroxybutane','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','3','2','4','6','NEW','11-349','11-271',NULL),
(121524,'chemistry','The alkene that exhibits geometrical isomerism is: [AIEEE 2009]','2-butene','The alkene that exhibits geometrical isomerism is 2-butene.\n\nGeometrical isomerism occurs in alkenes that have two different groups attached to each of the carbon atoms of the double bond. In 2-butene, there are two different groups (methyl and ethyl) attached to each of the carbon atoms of the double bond. The two possible isomers are cis-2-butene and trans-2-butene. Refer diagram(e1220)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','propene','2-methyl propene','2-butene','2-methyl-2-butene','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(121525,'chemistry','Out of the following, the alkene that exhibits optical isomerism is: [AIEEE 2010]','3-methyl-1-pentene','Optical isomerism or enantiomerism occurs in molecules that have a chiral center, which is a carbon atom bonded to four different groups. In 3-methyl-2-pentene, the second carbon atom of the double bond is bonded to three different groups (H, CH3, and a CH2CH2CH3 group), making it a chiral center. Refer diagram(e1221)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','3-methyl-2-pentene','4-methyl-1-pentene','3-methyl-1-pentene','2-methyl-2-pentene','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(121526,'chemistry','Identify the compound that exhibits tautomerism.[AIEEE 2011]','2-pentanone','2-pentanone exhibits tautomerism, which is a type of isomerism where a molecule can exist in two different forms that interconvert by a proton transfer. The two tautomers of 2-pentanone are the keto form and the enol form, which interconvert through a catalyzed reaction.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','2-butene','lactic acid','2-pentanone','Benzene','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(121527,'chemistry','In Carius method of estimation of halogens, 250 mg of an organic compound gave 141mg of AgBr. The percentage of bromine in the compound is (Atomic mass Ag=108, Br = 80) [JEE Main 2015]','24','Percentage of bromine = (mass of AgBr obtained / molecular weight of AgBr) x (molecular weight of Br / mass of the organic compound taken) x 100%\n\nFirst, let\'s calculate the molecular weight of AgBr:\n\nMolecular weight of AgBr = atomic weight of Ag + atomic weight of Br\n= 108 + 80\n= 188 g/mol\n\nNow, let\'s substitute the given values in the formula:\n\nPercentage of bromine = (141 mg / 188 g/mol) x (80 g/mol / 250 mg) x 100% \n= 24 %','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'3','mcq','24','36','48','60','NEW','11-367','11-289',NULL),
(121528,'chemistry','The distillation technique most suited for seperating glycerol from spent lye in the soap industry is : [JEE Main 2016]','distillation under reduced pressure','Glycerol can be separated from spent-lye in soap industry by using this technique. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 358; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','fractional distillation','steam distillation','distillation under reduced pressure','simple distillation','NEW','11-358','11-280',NULL),
(121529,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds will be suitable for Kjeldahl\'s method for nitrogen estimation? Refer diagram(12157)','b','Kjeldahl method is not applicable to compounds containing nitrogen in nitro and azo groups and nitrogen present in the ring (e.g. pyridine) as nitrogen of these compounds does not change to ammonium sulphate under these conditions. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 366; Paragraph No. 2 ) [JEE Main 2018]','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-365','11-287','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121529q.png'),
(121608,'chemistry','Assertion: Bonds lying in the plane of the paper in a molecule\'s 3-D representation are depicted using a normal line (—).\n\nReason:  In a 3-D representation of a molecule, wedges are shown in such a way that the broad end is towards the observer.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The three-dimensional (3-D) structure of organic molecules can be represented on paper by using certain conventions.For example, by using solid and dashed wedge formula, the 3-D image of a molecule from a two-dimensional picture can be perceived. In these formulas the solid-wedge is used to indicate a bond projecting out of the plane of paper, towards the observer. The dashed-wedge is used to depict the bond projecting out of the plane of the paper and away from the observer. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 338; Paragraph No.1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Assertion- Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-338','11-260',NULL),
(121609,'chemistry','Assertion: The framework model emphasizes the pattern of bonds of a molecule while ignoring the size of atoms.\n\nReason: Framework model shows  the bonds connecting the atoms of a molecule and not the atoms themselves.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','In the framework model only the bonds connecting the atoms of a molecule and not the atoms themselves are shown. This model emphasizes the pattern of bonds of a molecule while ignoring the size of atoms. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 338; Paragraph No.1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Assertion- Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-338','11-260',NULL),
(10120,'chemistry','Copper metallurgy in India dates back to the beginning of which cultures in the subcontinent?','Chalcolithic','Copper metallurgy in India dates back to the beginning of chalcolithic cultures in the subcontinent.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 2 Paragraph-3).','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Paleolithic','Chalcolithic','Neolithic','Mesolithic','NEW','11-002','11-002',NULL),
(10121,'chemistry','According to _______, tanning of leather and dying of cotton were practised during 1000–400 BCE.','Rigveda','According to Rigveda, tanning of leather and dying of cotton were practiced during 1000–400 BCE.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 2 Paragraph-4).','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Rigveda','Arthashastra','Sushruta Samhita','Rasopanishada','NEW','11-002','11-002',NULL),
(10122,'chemistry','The golden gloss of the black polished ware of ________ India could not be replicated and is still a chemical mystery.','northern','The golden gloss of the black polished ware of northern India could not be replicated and is still a chemical mystery.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 2 Paragraph-4).','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','northern','southern','eastern','western','NEW','11-002','11-002',NULL),
(10123,'chemistry','Which literature describes the production of salt from sea?','Arthashastra','Arthashastra describes the production of salt from the sea.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 2 Paragraph-4).','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Rigveda','Arthashastra','Sushruta Samhita','Rasopanishada','NEW','11-002','11-002',NULL),
(10124,'chemistry','Which literature explains the importance of Alkalies?','Sushruta Samhita','Sushruta Samhita explains the importance of Alkalies.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 2 Paragraph-5).','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Rigveda','Arthashastra','Sushruta Samhita','Rasopanishada','NEW','11-002','11-002',NULL),
(10125,'chemistry','What did ancient Indians know to prepare, according to \'The Charaka Samhita\'?','Oxides of tin','The Charaka Samhita mentions ancient Indians who knew how to prepare sulphuric acid, nitric acid, and oxides of copper, tin, and zinc; the sulphates of copper, zinc, and iron, and the carbonates of lead and iron.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 2 Paragraph-5).','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'3','mcq','Sulphates of lead','Carbonates of zinc','Nitrates of copper, tin, and zinc;','Oxides of tin','NEW','11-002','11-002',NULL),
(10126,'chemistry','What describes the preparation of gunpowder mixture?','Rasopanishada','Rasopanishada describes the preparation of a gunpowder mixture.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 2 Paragraph-6).','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Rigveda','Arthashastra','Sushruta Samhita','Rasopanishada','NEW','11-002','11-002',NULL),
(10127,'chemistry','Nagarjuna\'s work Ras Ratnakar does not deal with_________?','Methods of purification of liquids','Nagarjuna\'s work \'Ras Ratnakar\' deals with the formulation of mercury compounds. \n \n He has also discussed methods for the extraction of metals, like gold, silver, tin, and copper.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 2 Paragraph-7),','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Formulation of mercury compounds','Methods for the extraction of metals like gold and silver','Methods for the extraction of metals like tin and copper','Methods of purification of liquids','NEW','11-002','11-002',NULL),
(10128,'chemistry','Which literature describes methods by which metals could be identified by flame color?','Rasarnava','A book, Rasarnava, appeared around 800 CE. It discusses the uses of various furnaces, ovens, and crucibles for different purposes.\n \n It describes methods by which metals could be identified by flame colour.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 2 Paragraph-7).','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Rasopanishada','Ras Ratnakar','Rasarnava','Brihat Samhita','NEW','11-002','11-002',NULL),
(10129,'chemistry','What did Indians use to prepare soaps?','All of these','Indians began making soaps in the 18th century CE. \n \n Oil of Eranda and seeds of Mahua plant and calcium carbonate which were used for making soap.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 2 Paragraph-8).','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Oil of Eranda','Seeds of Mahua plant','Calcium carbonate','All of these','NEW','11-002','11-002',NULL),
(10130,'chemistry','Which classical text mentions about dye stuff?','Atharvaveda','A number of classical texts, like Atharvaveda (1000 BCE) mention some dye stuff, the material used were turmeric, madder, sunflower, orpiment, cochineal and lac. \n \n Some other substances having tinting property were kamplcica, pattanga and jatuka.\n \n Reference : (NCERT Class-11 Page- 3 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Rasarnava','Atharvaveda','Gandhayukli','Brihat Samhita','NEW','11-003','11-003',NULL),
(10131,'chemistry','Which among the following is/are substance/s with tinting property?','All of these','A number of classical texts, like Atharvaveda (1000 BCE) mention some dye stuff, the material used were turmeric, madder, sunflower, orpiment, cochineal and lac. \n \n Some other substances having tinting property were kamplcica, pattanga and jatuka.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 3 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Kamplcica','Pattanga','Jatuka','All of these','NEW','11-003','11-003',NULL),
(10132,'chemistry','Which classical text/s mention/s about fermentation?','All of these','Vedas and Kautilya’s Arthashastra mention about many types of liquors. \n \n Charaka Samhita also mentions ingredients, such as barks of plants, stem, flowers, leaves, woods, cereals, fruits and sugarcane for making Asavas.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 3 Paragraph-4).','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Vedas','Arthashastra','Charaka Samhita','All of these','NEW','11-003','11-003',NULL),
(10133,'chemistry','Who among the following was the first proponent of atomic theory?','Acharya Kanda','Acharya Kanda,born in 600 BCE, originally known by the name Kashyap, was the first proponent of the ‘atomic theory’.\n \n He formulated the theory of very small indivisible particles, which named ‘Paramãnu’ (comparable to atoms). \n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 3 Paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Acharya Kanda','Chakrapani','Nagarjuna','John Dalton','NEW','11-003','11-003',NULL),
(10134,'chemistry','Which term is used for the branch of technology that deals with extreme reduction of particle size?','Nanotechnology','Extreme reduction of particle size is termed as nanotechnology.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 3 Paragraph-6)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Microscopic studies','Nanotechnology','Microtechnology','Microscience','NEW','11-003','11-003',NULL),
(10135,'chemistry','What component of metal is used in the treatment of ailments, as per the description in Charaka Samhita?','Bhasma','Charaka Samhita describes the use of bhasma of metals in the treatment of ailments.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 3 Paragraph-6).','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Oxides','Carbonates','Minerals','Bhasma','NEW','11-003','11-003',NULL),
(10136,'chemistry','Which of the following is not described in Charaka Samhita?','Preparation of perfumes and cosmetics.','Charaka Samhita describes the use of bhasma of metals in the treatment of ailments.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 3 Paragraph-6).','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'2','mcq','Preparation of nitric acid and oxides of Cu, Sn and Zn.','Use of various parts of plant for the preparation of Asavas.','Preparation of perfumes and cosmetics.','Use of Bhasma of metals for treatment of ailments.','NEW','11-003','11-003',NULL),
(11003,'chemistry','CH₄ (g)+2O₂ (g) → CO₂ (g)+2H₂O(g)\n \n According to the above chemical reaction, following information have been given. \n \n (i) One mole of CH₄ (g) reacts with two moles of O₂ (g) to give one mole of CO₂ (g) and two moles of H₂O(g) \n \n (ii) One molecule of CH₄ (g) reacts with 2 molecules of O₂ (g) to give one molecule of CO₂ (g) and 2 molecules of H₂O(g) \n \n (iii) 22.7 L of CH₄ (g)reacts with 45.4 L of O₂ to give 22.7 L of CO₂ (g) and 45.4 L of H₂O (g) \n \n (iv) 16 g of CH₄ (g) reacts with 2 x 32 g of O₂ (g) to give 44 g of CO₂ (g) and 2 x 18 g of H₂O(g) \n \n From these information the correct interconversion should be ?','Mass ⇄ Moles ⇄ Number of molecules','From the given relationships, the data can be interconverted.\n \n mass ⇄ moles ⇄ number of molecules\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 20 Paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Stoichiometry And Stoichiometric Calculations',NULL,'3','mcq','Mass → Moles → Number of molecules','Mass ⇄ Moles ⇄ Number of molecules','Mass → Moles ⇄ Number of molecules','Mass = Moles → Number of molecules','NEW','11-020','11-020',NULL),
(11004,'chemistry','Which of the following equations is unbalanced?','P₄ (s) + O₂ (g) → P₄O₁₀ (s)','It can be balanced as follows:\n \n P₄ (s) + 5O₂ (g)-------- >P₄O₁₀ (s) \n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 20 Paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Stoichiometry And Stoichiometric Calculations',NULL,'2','mcq','4Fe(s)+ 3O₂ (g) → 2Fe₂O₃ (s)','2Mg(s) + O₂ (g) → 2MgO(s)','P₄ (s) + O₂ (g) → P₄O₁₀ (s)','CH₄ (g) + 2O₂ (g) → CO₂ (g) + 2H₂O(g)','NEW','11-020','11-020',NULL),
(11005,'chemistry','The reactant, which gets consumed first, limits the amount of product formed and is called the ______ reagent.','Limiting','The reactant, which gets consumed first, limits the amount of product formed and is, therefore, called the limiting reagent.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 21 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Stoichiometry And Stoichiometric Calculations',NULL,'1','mcq','Excess','Limiting','Chemical','Gaseous','NEW','11-021','11-021',NULL),
(11006,'chemistry','Calculate the amount of water (g) produced by the combustion of 16 g of Methane.','36 g','16 g of CH₄ corresponds to one mole Methane\n \n CH₄ (g) + 2O₂ (g) → CO₂ (g) + 2H₂O (g). \n \n Combustion of one mole of Methane produces two moles water thus it weighs 36 g.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 22 Problem-1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Stoichiometry And Stoichiometric Calculations',NULL,'2','mcq','16 g','18 g','36 g','32 g','NEW','11-022','11-022',NULL),
(11007,'chemistry','How many moles of Methane are required to produce 22g CO₂ (g) after combustion?','8 g','16 g of CH₄ corresponds to one mole.\n \n CH₄ (g) + 2O₂ (g) → CO₂ (g) + 2H₂O (g). \n \n 22g CO₂ means 1/2 mol which will be produced by 1/2 mole of Methane.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 22 Problem 1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Stoichiometry And Stoichiometric Calculations',NULL,'2','mcq','8 g','16 g','24 g','32 g','NEW','11-022','11-022',NULL),
(11008,'chemistry','Calculate the amount of Carbon dioxide that could be produced when 1 mole of Carbon is burnt in 16 g of Dioxygen.','0.5 mol','As only 16 g of Dioxygen is available, it can combine only with 0.5 mole of Carbon, i.e., Dioxygen is the limiting reactant. \n \n Hence,CO₂ produced = 22 g.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 22 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Stoichiometry And Stoichiometric Calculations',NULL,'2','mcq','0.5 mol','1 mol','2 mol','Insufficient oxygen to form carbon dioxide','NEW','11-022','11-022',NULL),
(11009,'chemistry','50.0 kg of N₂ (g) and 10.0 kg of H₂ (g) are mixed to produce NH₃ (g).Calculate the amount of NH₃ (g) formed.','56.1 kg','Number of moles of N₂ = 17.86×10² mol, Number of moles of H₂= 4.96×10³ mol. \n \n Balanced Reaction N₂ + 3H₂ = 2NH₃\n \n Since 3 mol H₂ (g) gives 2 mol NH₃ (g) therefore, 4.96×10³ mol H₂ (g) x 2/3 = 3.30×10³ mol NH₃ (g). \n \n 1 mol Ammonia weighs 17 g then total ammonia formed =3.30×10³ mol x 17 = 56.1 kg.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 22 Problem 1.5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Stoichiometry And Stoichiometric Calculations',NULL,'2','mcq','17.86 kg','49.68 kg','56.1 kg','59.2 kg','NEW','11-022','11-022',NULL),
(11010,'chemistry','If 2.00 × 10³ g Dinitrogen reacts with 1.00 ×10³ g of Dihydrogen to produce Ammonia.\n \n Will any of the two reactants remain unreacted? If yes, which one and what would be its mass?','H₂, 571.6 gm','Balanced Reaction N₂ + 3H₂ = 2NH₃, \n \n Since 3 mol H₂ (g) gives 2 mol NH₃(g). \n \n Moles of N₂ = 2000/28 =71.4, Moles of H₂ = 1000/2 = 500 mol. \n \n N₂ requires 214.2 moles of H₂.\n \n Thus N₂ is limiting. \n \n Total ammonia formed = 2 x 71.4 = 142.8 mol. \n \n Remaining H₂ = 500-214.2 = 285.8 mol= 285.8 x 2 = 571.6gm.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Stoichiometry And Stoichiometric Calculations',NULL,'2','mcq','N₂, 428.4 gm','H₂, 428.4 gm','N₂, 571.6 gm','H₂, 571.6 gm','NEW','11-022','11-022',NULL),
(11011,'chemistry','A solution is prepared by adding 2 g of a substance A to 18 g of water. Calculate the mass percent of the solute.','10%','Mass % of A = Mass of A/Mass of compound x 100 => 2/2 + 20 x 100 = 10%.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 23 Problem 1.6)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Stoichiometry And Stoichiometric Calculations',NULL,'1','mcq','5%','10%','15%','20%','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11012,'chemistry','A sample of drinking water was found to be severely contaminated with Chloroform, CHCl₃, supposed to be carcinogenic in nature. The level of contamination was 15 ppm (by mass). Express this in percent by mass.','1.5 x 10⁻³ %','PPM means parts per million. \n \n Mass % = 15/10⁶ x 100 = 1.5 x 10⁻³ %.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 23 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Stoichiometry And Stoichiometric Calculations',NULL,'2','mcq','1.266 x 10⁻⁴ %','1.09 x 10⁻³ %','1.5 x 10⁻⁴ %','1.5 x 10⁻³ %','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11013,'chemistry','Calculate the mass percent of Sulphur element present in Sodium sulphate.','22.54%','Formula of Sodium sulphate = Na₂SO₄. \n \n Mass of sodium sulphate = 142 g/mol. \n \n Mass % of sulphur in formula = 32/142 x 100= 22.54%.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 25 Problem-1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Stoichiometry And Stoichiometric Calculations',NULL,'2','mcq','22.54%','32.39%','45.07%','17.10%','NEW','11-025','11-025',NULL),
(11014,'chemistry','8 g of NaOH is dissolved in 18 g of H₂O.What is the mole fraction of NaOH in solution and molality (in mol/kg ) of the solution respectively?','0.167, 11.11 m','Moles of NaOH = 8/40 = 0.2. \n \n Mole fraction = 0.2/0.2 + 1 = 0.167.\n \n Molality = 0.2x 1000 /18 = 11.11. \n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 23 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Stoichiometry And Stoichiometric Calculations',NULL,'2','mcq','0.2, 11.11 m','0.2, 22.20 m','0.167, 22.20 m','0.167, 11.11 m','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11015,'chemistry','Calculate the molarity of NaOH in the solution prepared by dissolving its 4 g in enough water to form 250 mL of the solution.','0.4 M','Moles of NaOH = 4/40 = 0.1 mol. \n \n Molarity = no. of moles of solute/ Vol. of solution in litres = 0.1 x 1000/250 = 0.4M. \n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 24 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Stoichiometry And Stoichiometric Calculations',NULL,'2','mcq','0.1 M','0.4 M','0.8 M','None of these','NEW','11-024','11-024',NULL),
(11016,'chemistry','What is the concentration of sugar (C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁) in mol/L if its 20 g are dissolved in enough water to make a final volume up to 2 L?','0.0293 mol/L','Mass of Sucrose = 342g/mol. Molarity = 20 x 1000/342 x 2000 = 0.0293 M.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 24 Paragraph-1','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Stoichiometry And Stoichiometric Calculations',NULL,'2','mcq','0.0293 mol/L','0.0585 mol/L','0.0314 mol/L','0.0117 mol/L','NEW','11-024','11-024',NULL),
(11017,'chemistry','Calculate the molarity of a solution of Ethanol in water, in which the mole fraction of Ethanol is 0.040 (assume the density of water to be one).','2.31','No. of moles of water in 1L water = 1000g/18g = 55.55 mol. \n \n Acc. to question \n \n (nₑₜₕₐₙₒₗ) / (nₑₜₕₐₙₒₗ+n𝓌ₐₜₑᵣ) = 0.040 \n \n (n𝓌ₐₜₑᵣ) / (nₑₜₕₐₙₒₗ+n𝓌ₐₜₑᵣ) = 0.96 \n \n => 55.55 / (nₑₜₕₐₙₒₗ+ 55.55) = 0.96 \n \n => 55.55/ 0.96 = (nₑₜₕₐₙₒₗ+ 55.55)\n \n => 57.86 = (nₑₜₕₐₙₒₗ+ 55.55)\n \n =>nₑₜₕₐₙₒₗ = 57.86 - 55.55\n \n => nₑₜₕₐₙₒₗ = 2.31. \n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 24 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Stoichiometry And Stoichiometric Calculations',NULL,'2','mcq','1.96','2.22','2.31','2.47','NEW','11-024','11-024',NULL),
(11018,'chemistry','Calculate the mass of Sodium acetate (CH₃COONa) required to make 500 mL of 0.375 molar aqueous solution. Molar mass of sodium acetate is 82.0245 g/mol .','15.380 g','Mass of sodium acetate (CH3COONa) required to make 500 mL of 0.375 molar aqueous solution\n \n => 0.375 = M x 1000/500 x 82.0245 \n \n => M =15.380g. \n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 25 Problem-1.5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Stoichiometry And Stoichiometric Calculations',NULL,'2','mcq','13.35 g','15.380 g','26.541 g','41 g','NEW','11-025','11-025',NULL),
(11019,'chemistry','Calculate the concentration of Nitric acid in moles per litre in a sample which has a density, 1.41 g/mL and the mass percent of Nitric acid in it being 69%.','15.44 mol/L','Moles in 69 g of HNO₃= 69/63 (Molar mass of HNO₃) = 1.095 mol. \n Volume of 100g Nitric acid solution = 100/1.41 = 70.92 mL, \n Molarity of HNO₃= 1.095 x 1000/70.92 = 15.44 M.\n Reference : (NCERT Class-11 Page- 26 Problem-1.6)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Stoichiometry And Stoichiometric Calculations',NULL,'2','mcq','15.38 mol/L','15.44 mol/L','15.75 mol/L','16.1 mol/L','NEW','11-026','11-026',NULL),
(11020,'chemistry','The density of 3 M solution of NaCl is 1.25 g/mL. What is the molality of the solution?','2.79','Mass of NaCl in 1 L solution = 3 × 58.5 = 175.5 g.\n \n Mass of 1L solution = 1000 × 1.25 = 1250 g (since density = 1.25 g mL⁻¹) \n \n Mass of water in solution = 1250 –75.5 =1074.5 g. \n \n Molality = No. of moles of solute/Mass of solvent in kg = 3 x 1000/1074.5 = 2.79 m.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 24 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Stoichiometry And Stoichiometric Calculations',NULL,'2','mcq','1.3','2.21','2.79','3.107','NEW','11-024','11-024',NULL),
(11021,'chemistry','If the density of Methanol is 0.793 kg/L, what is its volume needed for making 2.5 L of its 0.25 M solution?','25.22 mL','Molar mass of Methanol = 32 g/mol. \n \n Molarity of given solution = 0.793 Kg/L /0.032 Kg/mol = 24.78 mol/L.\n \n Applying MₐVₐ = MₓVₓ \n \n => 0.25 x 2.5 = 24.78 x Vₓ\n \n => Vₓ = 25.22 mL.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 24 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Stoichiometry And Stoichiometric Calculations',NULL,'2','mcq','25 mL','25.22 mL','25.83 mL','25.97 mL','NEW','11-024','11-024',NULL),
(11101,'chemistry','The dimensions of pressure are the same as that of?','Energy per unit volume.','Pressure = Force/ Area, Therefore, dimensions of pressure = MLT⁻²/L² = ML⁻¹T⁻²\n \n Dimensions of energy per unit volume = Energy/ Volume = ML²T⁻²/ L³ = ML⁻¹T⁻²\n \n For Energy per unit volume, work out the dimensions of work done using Force and displacement.\n \n NEET 1995\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page-13 Last topic)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Force per unit volume.','Energy per unit volume.','Force','Energy','NEW','11-013','11-013',NULL),
(11102,'chemistry','Given the numbers : 161 cm, 0.161 cm, 0.0161 cm. The number of significant figures for the three numbers are?','3, 3 and 3 respectively','Zeros placed left to the number are never significant, therefore the no. of significant figures for the numbers 161 cm, 0.161 cm and 0.0161 cm are same, i.e., 3.\n \n NEET 1998 \n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 12 Paragraph-7,8)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','3, 3 and 4 respectively','3, 4 and 4 respectively','3, 4 and 5 respectively','3, 3 and 3 respectively','NEW','11-012','11-012',NULL),
(11103,'chemistry','Equal masses of H₂, O₂ and Methane have been taken in a container of volume V at temperature 27 °C in identical conditions. \n \n The ratio of the volumes of gases H₂ : O₂ : Methane would be?','16:01:02','According to Avogadro’s hypothesis, ratio of the volumes of gases will be equal to the ratio of their no. of moles.\n \n So no. of moles => nH₂ = w/2, nO₂=w/32, n(methane)=w/16, so ratio is 16:1:2.\n \n NEET 2014\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 15 Paragraph-8)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','8:16:01','16:08:01','16:01:02','8:01:02','NEW','11-015','11-015',NULL),
(11104,'chemistry','What volume of Oxygen gas (O₂) measured at 0°C and 1 atm, is needed to burn completely 1 L of Propane gas (C₃H₈) measured under the same conditions?','5 L','C₃H₈ + 5O₂ ---> 3CO₂ + 4H₂O.\n \n According to the above equation, 1 vol. or 1 litre of Propane requires 5 vol. or 5 litres of O₂ to burn completely.\n At STP, 1 mole of a gas occupies 22.4 L\n22.4 L of propane reacts with \n5\n×\n22.4\n L of \nO\n2\n\nSo, 1 L mole of propane gas will require 5 L of \nO\n2\n for complete combustion.\n NEET 2008\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 20 Paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','5 L','10 L','7 L','6 L','NEW','11-020','11-020',NULL),
(11105,'chemistry','0.24 g of a volatile gas, upon vaporisation, gives 45 mL vapour at NTP. What will be the vapour density of the substance?\n \n (Density of H₂ = 0.089 g/L)','59.93','Weight of gas = 0.24 g, \n \n Volume of gas = 45 mL = 0.045 litre and density of H₂ = 0.089 g/L \n \n Weight of 45 mL of H₂ = density × volume = 0.089 × 0.045 = 4.005 × 10⁻³ g.\n \n Therefore, vapour density = 0.24/4.005 × 10⁻³ g = 59.93.\n \n NEET 1996\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 15 Paragraph-6)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','95.93','59.93','95.39','5.993','NEW','11-015','11-015',NULL),
(11106,'chemistry','The molecular weight of O₂ and SO₂ are 32 and 64 respectively. At 15°C and 150 mm Hg pressure, one litre of O₂ contains ‘N’ molecules. The number of molecules in two litres of SO₂ under the same conditions of temperature and pressure will be ?','2 N','If 1 L of one gas contains N molecules, 2 L of any gas under the same conditions will contain 2 N molecules.\n \n NEET 1990\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 15 Paragraph-6)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','N/2','N','2 N','4 N','NEW','11-015','11-015',NULL),
(11107,'chemistry','What is the weight of Oxygen required for the complete combustion of 2.8 kg of Ethylene?','9.6 kg','For complete combustion, \n \n 2.8 kg of C₂H₄ requires \n \n = 96/28 x 2.8 x 10³ g \n \n = 9.6 x 10³ g \n \n = 9.6 Kg of O₂.\n \n NEET 1989\n \n Reference- Page- 20 Paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2.8 kg','6.4 kg','9.6 kg','96 kg','NEW','11-020','11-020',NULL),
(11108,'chemistry','An element, X has the following isotopic composition : ²⁰⁰X : 90% ¹⁹⁹X : 8.0% ²⁰²X : 2.0% The weighted average atomic mass of the naturally occurring element X is closest to?','200 amu','Average isotopic mass of X = 200 x 90 + 199 x 8+ 202 x 2/90 + 8 + 2 \n \n =18000 + 1592 + 404/100 \n \n = 199.96 amu \n \n = 200 amu.\n \n NEET 2007\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 17 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','201 amu','199 amu','202 amu','200 amu','NEW','11-017','11-017',NULL),
(11109,'chemistry','Boron has two stable isotopes, ¹⁰B(19%) and ¹¹B(81%). Calculate average at. wt. of boron in the periodic table.','10.8 u','Average atomic mass = 19 x 10 + 81 x 11/100 \n \n = 10.81 u.\n \n NEET 1990\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 17 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','10.8 u','11.2 u','10.2 u','10 u','NEW','11-017','11-017',NULL),
(11110,'chemistry','Which one of the followings has maximum number of atoms?','1 g of Li(s) [Atomic mass of Li = 7]','Li has the highest no. of moles on calculation.\n  \n1\ng Li\n=\n1\n7\n \nmol atom of Li\n\n\n=\n1\n7\n×\n6.022\n×\n10\n23\n \natoms of Li\n\n NEET 2020\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1 g of Ag(s) [Atomic mass of Ag = 108]','1 g of Mg(s) [Atomic mass of Mg = 24]','1 g of O₂(g) [Atomic mass of O = 16]','1 g of Li(s) [Atomic mass of Li = 7]','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11111,'chemistry','In which case is number of molecules of water maximum?','18 mL of water','0.18 g of water has 0.18/18 moles i.e 0.01 moles i.e 0.01 x Nₐ molecules.\n \n 0.00224 L of water vapours have 0.00224/22.4 moles i.e 0.00001 moles i.e 0.00001 x Nₐ molecules\n \n 10⁻³ mol of water has 0.001 moles i.e 0.001 x Nₐ molecules\n \n 18 mL of water- mass = vol x density = 18 mL x 1 g/mL = 18 g and 18 g of water has 18/18 moles i.e 1 mole i.e Nₐ molecules which is highest among the options.\n \n NEET 2018\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','18 mL of water','0.18 g of water','0.00224 L of water vapours at 1 atm and 273 K','10⁻³ mol of water','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11112,'chemistry','Suppose the elements X and Y combine to form two compounds XY₂ and X₃Y₂. When 0.1 mole of XY₂ weighs 10 g and 0.05 mol of X₃Y₂ weighs 9 g, the atomic weights of X and Y are-','40, 30','Let atomic weight of element X is x and that of element Y is y.\n \n For XY₂, 0.1=10/(x + 2y)\n \n =>x + 2y = 10/0.1 = 100.\n \n For X₃Y₂, 0.05 = 9/(3x + 2y)\n \n =>3x+2y = 180.\n \n On solving we get x = 40\n \n =>40 + 2y = 100 \n \n ⇒ 2y = 60 \n \n ⇒ y = 30.\n \n NEET- II 2016\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 17 Paragraph-8)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','40, 30','60, 40','20, 30','30, 20','NEW','11-017','11-017',NULL),
(11113,'chemistry','If Avogadro number Nₐ, is changed from 6.022 × 10²³/mol to 6.022 × 10²⁰/mol, this would change-','The mass of one mole of Carbon','If Avogadro number is changed to 6.022 × 10²⁰ atoms then mass of 1 mol of carbon = 12 x 6.022 x10²⁰/6.022 x 10²³ = 12 x 10⁻³g.\n \n NEET 2015\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The mass of one mole of Carbon','The ratio of chemical species to each other in a balanced equation','The ratio of elements to each other in a compound','The definition of mass in units of grams.','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11114,'chemistry','The number of water molecules is maximum in-','18 moles of water','1 mol water \n=\n6.02\n×\n10\n23\n molecules\n18 mol water \n=\n18\n×\n6.02\n×\n10\n23\n molecules\n1.8 gram water = 0.1 mol\n18 mol water has the maximum number of molecules\n \n NEET 2015','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1.8 gram of water','18 gram of water','18 moles of water','18 molecules of water','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11115,'chemistry','A mixture of gases contains H₂ and O₂ gases in the ratio of 1 : 4 (w/w). What is the molar ratio of the two gases in the mixture?','4:1','Let us take weight of H₂ = 2g so weight of O₂ will be = 2 x 4 = 8.\n \n So moles of H₂ = 1 and moles of O₂ = 8/32 = 1/4.\n \n Molar Ratio = 4:1.\n \n NEET 2015\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','16:1','2:1','1:4','4:1','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11116,'chemistry','Which has the maximum number of molecules among the following?','8 g H₂','As we know that the number of moles is the ratio of the given mass to the molecular mass of the substance which in turn is equivalent to the ratio of number of molecules to the Avogadro’s number. Therefore, we can write it as:\nmoles=givenmassmolecularmass=no.ofmoleculesNA\nSo, using the above formula we can easily calculate the number of molecules of every given substance.\nThe first one is 44gCO2\n, so we are given a mass of carbon dioxide as 44g\n and we know that the molecular mass of carbon dioxide is also 44g\n. Therefore the number of molecules will be calculated as:\n⇒4444=no.ofmoleculesNA⇒1×NA=no.ofmolecules\nSimilarly, we can calculate the number of molecules in 48gO3\n and we know that molecular mass of three atoms of oxygen is also 48g\n, so:\n⇒4848=no.ofmoleculesNA⇒1×NA=no.ofmolecules\nThen we have 8gH2\n, we know that the molecular mass of two hydrogen atoms is 2g\n and the given mass is 8g\n, hence the number of molecules will be equivalent to:\n⇒82=no.ofmoleculesNA⇒4×NA=no.ofmolecules\nLastly we have 64gSO2\n and the molecular mass of sulphur dioxide is again 64g\n, so the number of molecules will be:\n⇒6464=no.ofmoleculesNA⇒1×NA=no.ofmolecules\nTherefore, hydrogen contains the maximum number of molecules.\n \n Mains 2011','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','48 g O₂','44 g CO₂','8 g H₂','64 g SO₂','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11117,'chemistry','The number of atoms in 0.1 mol of a triatomic gas is-','1.806 × 10²³','No. of atoms = Nₐ × No. of moles × 3 = 6.023 × 10²³ × 0.1 × 3 = 1.806 × 10²³.\n \n NEET 2010\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','6.026 × 10²²','1.806 × 10²³','1.800 × 10²²','3.600 × 10²³','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11118,'chemistry','The maximum number of molecules is present in-','15 L of H₂ gas at STP','15 L H₂ = 6.023 × 10²³ x 15/22.4 = 4.033 x 10²³ molecules.\n \n 5 L N₂ = 6.023 × 10²³ x 5/22.4 = 1.344 x 10²³ molecules.\n \n 0.5 L H₂ = 6.023 × 10²³ x 0.5/22.4 = 0.1325 x 10²³ molecules.\n \n 10 g O₂ = 6.023 × 10²³ x 10/32 = 1.8821 x 10²³ molecules.\n \n NEET 2004\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','15 L of H₂ gas at STP','5 L of N₂ gas at STP','0.5 L of H₂ gas at STP','10 g of O₂ gas at STP','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11119,'chemistry','Which has maximum molecules?','2 g H₂','7 g N₂ has 0.25 moles i.e 0.25 x Nₐ molecules.\n \n 2 g H₂ has 1 mole i.e Nₐ molecules.\n \n 16 g NO₂ has 0.34 moles i.e 0.34 x Nₐ molecules.\n \n 16 g O₂ has 0.5 moles i.e 0.5 x Nₐ molecules.\n \n NEET 2002\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','7 g N₂','2 g H₂','16 g NO₂','16 g O₂','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11120,'chemistry','Specific volume of cylindrical virus particle is 6.02 × 10⁻² cc/g whose radius and length are 7 Å and 10 Å respectively. If Na = 6.023 × 10²³, find molecular weight of virus.','15.4 kg/mol','Volume of virus = πr² = 22/7 x (7 x 10⁻⁸)² x 10 x10⁻⁸ = 154 x 10⁻²³ cc. \n \n Wt. of one virus particle = volume/specific volume= 154 x 10⁻²³/6.02 × 10⁻², \n \n Molecular wt. of virus = Avogadro no. x wt of one virus = 154 x 10⁻²³/6.02 × 10⁻² x 6.023 × 10²³ = 15400g/mol = 15.4 kg/mol.\n \n NEET 2001\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','15.4 kg/mol','1.54 × 10⁴ kg/mo','3.08 × 10⁴ kg/mol','0.308 × 10⁴ kg/mol','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11121,'chemistry','The number of atoms in 4.25 g of NH₃ is approximately-','6 × 10²³','Molar mass of ammonia = 17, \n \n No. of atoms = 4.25/ 17 = 1/4 x 6.023 × 10²³ x 4 (1N + 3H) = 6.023 × 10²³.\n \n NEET 1999\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','4 × 10²³','1 × 10²³','2 × 10²³','6 × 10²³','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11122,'chemistry','Haemoglobin contains 0.334% of iron by weight. The molecular weight of haemoglobin is approximately 67200. The number of Iron atoms (Atomic weight of Fe is 56) present in one molecule of haemoglobin is-','4','Quantity of iron in one molecule = 67200/100 x 0.334 = 224.45 amu, \n \n No. of iron atoms in one molecule of haemoglobin = 224.45/56 = 4.\n \n NEET 1998\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','4','6','3','2','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11123,'chemistry','The number of moles of Oxygen in one litre of air containing 21% Oxygen by volume, under standard conditions, is-','0.0093 mol','Volume of oxygen in one litre of air = 21/100 x1000 = 210 mL,Therefore, no. of moles = 210/22400 = 0.0093 mol.\n \n NEET 1995\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.0093 mol','2.10 mol','0.186 mol','0.21 mol','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11124,'chemistry','The total number of valence electrons in 4.2 g of N₃⁻ ion is-\n \n (Nₐ is the Avogadro’s number)','1.6 N','Each Nitrogen atom has 5 valence electrons, therefore total number of valence electrons in N₃⁻ ion is 16.\n \n Since the molecular mass of N₃⁻ is 42, therefore total number of valence electrons in 4.2 g of N₃⁻ ion \n \n => 4.2/42 x 16 x Nₐ.\n \n NEET 1994\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2.1 Nₐ','4.2 Nₐ','1.6 Nₐ','3.2 Nₐ','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11125,'chemistry','The number of gram molecules of oxygen in 6.02 × 10²⁴ CO molecules is -','5 g molecules','Avogadro’s no., N = 6.02 × 10²³ molecules = 1 mole \n \n => 6.02 × 10²⁴ CO molecules = 10 moles CO = 10 g atoms of O = 5 g molecules of O₂.\n \n NEET 1990\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','10 g molecules','5 g molecules','1 g molecule','0.5 g molecules.','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11126,'chemistry','Ratio of Cₚ and Cᵥ of a gas ‘X’ is 1.4. The number of atoms of the gas ‘X’ present in 11.2 litres of it at NTP will be','6.02 × 10²³','Here, Cₚ/Cᵥ = 1.4, which shows that the gas is diatomic.\n \n 22.4 L at NTP = 6.02 × 10²³ molecules, 11.2 L at NTP = 3.01 × 10²³ molecules,\n \n Since gas is diatomic,11.2 L at NTP = 2 × 3.01 × 10²³ atoms = 6.02 × 10²³ atoms.\n \n NEET 1989\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','6.02 × 10²³','1.2 × 10²³','3.01 × 10²³','2.01 × 10²³','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11127,'chemistry','The number of Oxygen atoms in 4.4 g of CO₂ is-','1.2 × 10²³','1 mol of CO₂ = 44 g of CO₂,\n \n 4.4 g CO₂ = 0.1 mol CO₂= 6 × 10²² molecules = 2 × 6 × 10²² atoms of O = 1.2 × 10²³ atoms of O.\n \n NEET 1989\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1.2 × 10²³','6 × 10²²','6 × 10²³','12 × 10²³','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11128,'chemistry','1 cc N₂O at NTP contains-','All of the above.','As we know, 22400 cc of N₂O contain 6.02 × 10²³ molecules, \n \n 1 cc of N₂O contain 6.02x10²³/22400 molecules. \n \n Since in N₂O molecule there are 3 atoms = 3 x 6.02x10²³/22400 = 1.8 x 10²²/224 atoms.\n \n Since in N₂O molecule there are 22 electrons = 6.022 x 10²³/22400 x 22 = 1.32/ 224 x 10²³ electrons.\n \n NEET 1988\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','18/224 x 10²² atoms','6.02x10²³/22400 molecules','1.32/22.4 x10²³ electrons','All of the above.','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11129,'chemistry','An organic compound contains Carbon, Hydrogen and Oxygen. Its elemental analysis gave C, 38.71% and H, 9.67%. The empirical formula of the compound would be-','CH₃O','NEET 2008\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 19 Paragraph-6) diagram(ne0109)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CHO','CH₄O','CH₃O','CH₂O','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(11130,'chemistry','Percentage of Se in Peroxidase anhydrous enzyme is 0.5% by weight (at. wt. = 78.4) then minimum molecular weight of Peroxidase anhydrous enzyme is-','1.568 × 10⁴','In peroxidase anhydrous enzyme, 0.5% Se is present means, 0.5 g Se is present in 100 g of enzyme. \n \n In a molecule of enzyme one Se atom must be present. Hence,78.4 g Se will be present in 100/0.5 x 78.4 = 1.568 × 10⁴.\n \n NEET 2001\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 20 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1.568 × 10⁴','1.568 × 10³','15.68','2.136 × 10⁴','NEW','11-020','11-020',NULL),
(11131,'chemistry','Which of the following fertilizers has the highest Nitrogen percentage?','Urea','Urea (NH₂CONH₂), \n \n % of N = 28/60 x 100 = 46.66%\n \n Similarly, % of N in other compounds are : (NH₄)₂SO₄ = 21.2%; CaCN₂ = 35.0% and NH₄NO₃ = 35.0%.\n \n NEET 1993\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 20 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ammonium sulphate','Calcium cyanamide','Urea','Ammonium nitrate','NEW','11-020','11-020',NULL),
(11132,'chemistry','The number of moles of Hydrogen molecules required to produce 20 moles of Ammonia through Haber’s process is-','30','Balanced Reaction \n \n N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃, \n \n Since 20 moles of Ammonia is to be formed we need 30 moles of Hydrogen.\n \n NEET 2019\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 22 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','40','10','20','30','NEW','11-022','11-022',NULL),
(11133,'chemistry','The density of 2 M aqueous solution of NaOH is 1.28 g/cm³ . The molality of the solution is-\n \n [Given that molecular mass of NaOH = 40 g/mol]','1.67 m','Density = 1.28 g/cc, Conc. of solution = 2 M, Molar mass of NaOH = 40 g/mol,\n \n Volume of solution = 1 L = 1000 mL, \n \n Mass of solution = d × V = 1.28 × 1000 = 1280 g, \n \n Mass of solute = n × Molar mass = 2 × 40 = 80 g, \n \n Mass of solvent = (1280 – 80) g = 1200 g,\n \n Number of moles of solute = 80/40 = 2, \n \n Molality = 2 x 1000/1200 =1.67m.\n \n NEET-Odisha 2019\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 23 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1.20 m','1.56 m','1.67 m','1.32 m','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11134,'chemistry','A mixture of 2.3 g Formic acid and 4.5 g Oxalic acid is treated with conc. H₂SO₄. The evolved gaseous mixture is passed through KOH pellets. Weight (in g) of the remaining product at STP will be-','2.8','NEET 2018\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 21 Paragraph-1) diagram(ne0110)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1.4','3','2.8','0','NEW','11-021','11-021',NULL),
(11135,'chemistry','What is the mass of the precipitate formed when 50 mL of 16.9% solution of AgNO₃ is mixed with 50 mL of 5.8% NaCl solution?','7 g','NEET 2015\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 23 Paragraph-2) diagram(ne0111)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','3.5 g','7 g','14 g','28 g','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11136,'chemistry','20.0 g of a Magnesium carbonate sample decomposes on heating to give Carbon dioxide and 8.0 g Magnesium oxide. What will be the percentage purity of Magnesium carbonate in the sample?','84%','NEET 2015\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 23 Paragraph-2) diagram(ne0112)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','96%','60%','84%','75%','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11137,'chemistry','When 22.4 litres of H₂(g) is mixed with 11.2 litres of Cl₂(g), each at STP, the moles of HCl(g) formed is equal to-','1 mol of HCl(g)','NEET 2014\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 22 Paragraph-1) diagram(ne0113)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1 mol of HCl(g)','2 mol of HCl(g)','0.5 mol of HCl(g)','1.5 mol of HCl(g)','NEW','11-022','11-022',NULL),
(11138,'chemistry','1.0 g of Magnesium is burnt with 0.56 g O₂ in a closed vessel. Which reactant is left in excess and how much? \n \n (At. wt. Mg = 24, O = 16)','Mg, 0.16 g','NEET 2014\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 21 Paragraph-1) diagram(ne0114)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Mg, 0.16 g','O₂, 0.16 g','Mg, 0.44 g','O₂, 0.28 g','NEW','11-021','11-021',NULL),
(11139,'chemistry','6.02 × 10²⁰ molecules of Urea are present in 100 mL of its solution. The concentration of solution is-','0.01 M','Moles of urea = 6.02 x 10²⁰/6.02 x 10²³ = 0.001. \n \n Concentration of solution = 0.001 x 1000/100 = 0.01M.\n \n NEET 2013\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 24 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','0.001 M','0.1 M','0.02 M','0.01 M','NEW','11-024','11-024',NULL),
(11140,'chemistry','In an experiment it showed that 10 mL of 0.05 M solution of Chloride required 10 mL of 0.1 M solution of AgNO₃, which of the following will be the formula of the Chloride?\n \n (X stands for the symbol of the element other than Chlorine)','XCl₂','Millimoles of solution of chloride = 0.05 × 10 = 0.5.\n \n Millimoles of AgNO₃ solution = 10 × 0.1 = 1.\n \n So, the millimoles of AgNO₃ are double than the Chloride solution.XCl₂ +2AgNO₃ ---> 2AgCl + X(NO₃)₂.\n \n NEET 2013\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 24 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','X₂Cl₂','XCl₂','XCl₄','X₂Cl','NEW','11-024','11-024',NULL),
(11141,'chemistry','25.3 g of Sodium carbonate, Na₂CO₃ is dissolved in enough water to make 250 mL of solution. If Sodium carbonate dissociates completely, molar concentration of sodium ion, Na⁺ and carbonate ions, CO₃²⁻ are respectively (Molar mass of Na₂CO₃ = 106 g /mol) -','1.910 M and 0.955 M','NEET 2010\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 24 Paragraph-1) diagram(11141E)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.955 M and 1.910 M','1.910 M and 0.955 M','1.90 M and 1.910 M','0.477 M and 0.477 M','NEW','11-024','11-024',NULL),
(11142,'chemistry','10 g of Hydrogen and 64g of Oxygen were filled in a steel vessel and exploded. Amount of water produced in this reaction will be -','4 mol','10 g of H₂ = 5 mol and 64 g of O₂ = 2 mol. \n \n In this reaction, Oxygen is the limiting reagent so amount of H₂O produced depends on the amount of O₂.\n \n Since 0.5 mol of O₂ gives 1 mol of H₂O => 2 mol of O₂ will give 4 mol of H₂O.\n \n NEET 2009\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 21 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','3 mol','4 mol','1 mol','2 mol','NEW','11-021','11-021',NULL),
(11143,'chemistry','How many moles of Lead(II) chloride will be formed from a reaction between 6.5 g of PbO and 3.2 g HCl?','0.029','PbO + 2HCl → PbCl₂ + H₂O.\n \n Moles of PbO = 6.5/224 = 0.029mol, \n \n Moles of HCl = 3.2/36.5 = 0.087 mol.\n \n Formation of moles of Lead(II) chloride depends upon the no. of moles of PbO which acts as a limiting reagent here. \n \n So, no. of moles of PbCl₂ formed will be equal to the no. of moles of PbO i.e. 0.029.\n \n NEET 2008\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 21 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.011','0.029','0.044','0.333','NEW','11-021','11-021',NULL),
(11144,'chemistry','The mass of Carbon anode consumed (giving only Carbon dioxide) in the production of 270 kg of Aluminium metal from Bauxite by the Hall\'s process is-','90 kg','If 270 kg of aluminum is produced, first find the moles of aluminum produced. Then, use the ratio from the reaction to find the moles of carbon consumed. Finally, calculate the mass of carbon and convert it to kilograms.\n\nThe simplified explanation focuses on the key steps without going into detailed calculations. Follow the steps for final result NEET 2005\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 22 Paragraph-2) diagram(ne0116)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','270 kg','540 kg','90 kg','180 kg','NEW','11-022','11-022',NULL),
(11145,'chemistry','Molarity of liquid HCl, if density of solution is 1.17 g/cc is-','32.05 M','Density = 1.17 g/cc,1 cc. solution contains 1.17 g of HCl. \n \n Molarity = 1.17 x 1000/36.5 x 1 = 32.05.\n \n NEET 2001\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 24 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','36.5 M','18.25 M','32.05 M','42.1 M','NEW','11-024','11-024',NULL),
(11146,'chemistry','Volume of CO₂ obtained by the complete decomposition of 9.85 g of BaCO₃ is-','1.12 L','First you need to calculate moles of carbon dioxide, and barium carbonate, you will also need to use the stochiometric coefficients from balanced reaction and use the ideal gas equation to find the volumeNEET 2000 \n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 22 Paragraph-1) diagram(ne0117)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','2.24 L','1.12 L','0.84 L','0.56 L','NEW','11-022','11-022',NULL),
(11147,'chemistry','In the reaction, \n \n 4NH₃ + 5O₂ → 4NO + 6H₂O\n \nWhat happens when 1 mole of ammonia and 1 mole of O₂ are made to react to completion?','All the oxygen will be consumed','Here Oxygen is the limiting reagent according to the balanced equation given, so all the oxygen would be consumed.\n \n NEET 1998\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 21 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','All the oxygen will be consumed','1.0 mole of NO will be produced','1.0 mole of water is produced','All the Ammonia will be consumed.','NEW','11-021','11-021',NULL),
(11148,'chemistry','The amount of Zinc required to produce 224 mL of H₂ at STP on treatment with dilute H₂SO₄ will be-','0.65 g','Zn + H₂SO₄ → ZnSO₄ + H₂.\n \n Since 65 g of zinc reacts to liberate 22400 mL of H₂ at STP, \n therefore amount of Zinc needed to produce 224 mL of H₂ at STP \n = 65/22400 x 224 \n = 0.65 g.\n \n NEET 1996\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 22 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','65 g','0.065 g','0.65 g','6.5 g','NEW','11-022','11-022',NULL),
(11149,'chemistry','At STP the density of CCl₄ vapour in g/L will be nearest to-','6.87','Weight of 1 mol of CCl₄ vapour= 12 + 4 × 35.5 = 154 g, \n \n Density of CCl₄ vapour = 154/22.4 g/L = 6.875 g/L.\n \n NEET 1998\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 24 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','6.87','3.42','10.26','4.57','NEW','11-024','11-024',NULL),
(11150,'chemistry','An organic compound contains 78% (by wt.) Carbon and remaining percentage of Hydrogen.What is the right option for the empirical formula of this compound?\n \n [Atomic wt. of C is 12, H is 1]','CH₃','NEET 2021\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 19 Paragraph-7) diagram(11150E)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CH₂','CH₃','CH','CH₄','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(11151,'chemistry','In one molal solution that contains 0.5 mole of a solute, there is-','500 g of solvent','Molality is the moles of solute dissolved per kg of solvent therefore 500 g, 1 molal solution contains 0.5 of solute, as\n Molality = Moles of solute/Mass of solvent (in kg)\n \n 1 = 0.5/ Mass of solvent (in kg)\n \n Mass of solvent (in kg) = 0.5\n = 500 g\n \n NEET 2022\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 24 topic- molality)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1000 g of solvent','100 mL of solvent','500 mL of solvent','500 g of solvent','NEW','11-024','11-024',NULL),
(11152,'chemistry','The mass of a 2.5 mL solution (the density of the solution is 2.15 g/mL) in the correct significant figures is:\n(NEET - 2022)','5.4 g','Step 1:\nCalculate the mass of the solution as follows: mass = density × volume\nWhen we multiply the two numbers, the final answer should have the same number of significant figures as the number with the fewest significant figures.\nStep 2: Mass = 2.15 g mL⁻¹x 2.5 mL = 5.375 g\nThe answer must contain two significant figures, because 2.5 contains only two significant figures.\nThus, the mass of the substance is 5.4 g','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','5375 × 10⁻³ g','5.4 g','5.38','53.75g','NEW','11-012','11-012',NULL),
(11153,'chemistry','The number of significant figures in the following numbers is:\n(i) 161 cm  (ii) 0.0161  (iii) 1.61\n(AIPMT - 1998)','3,3,3','1. All non- zeroes are significant. So, 161 cm and 1.61 has 3 significant figures each.\n2. If any zero precedes the non-zero digit then it is not significant. The preceding zero indicates the location of the decimal point, in 0.0161 there are 3 significant figures.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','3,3,3','3,4,3','3,2,3','3,4,4','NEW','11-012','11-012',NULL),
(11154,'chemistry','Select the correct statements from the following:\n\nA: Atoms of all elements are composed of two fundamental particles.\n\nВ:The mass of the electron is 9.10939 × 10⁻³¹ kg\n\nC: All the isotopes of a given element show same chemical properties.\n\nD: Protons and electrons are collectively known as nucleons.\n\nE: Dalton\'s atomic theory regarded the atom as an ultimate particle of matter.\n\nChoose the correct answer from the option given below:\n\n(NEET - 2023)','B, C and E only','1. Atoms of all elements are composed of two fundamental particles. It is a wrong statement. Atoms consist of electrons, protons, and neutrons as the fundamental particles.\nIt has three fundamental particles.\n\n\n2. The mass  of the electron is 9.10939 x 10⁻³¹ kg. It is a correct statement.\n\n3. All the isotopes of a given element show the same chemical properties. It is a correct statement. The chemical properties of atoms are controlled by the number of electrons, which are determined by the number of protons in the nucleus. In isotopes the number of electrons are same. Hence, isotopes show the same chemical properties.\n\n4. Protons and electrons are collectively known as nucleons. It is a wrong statement. Protons and neutrons are collectively known as nucleons.\n\n5. Dalton\'s atomic theory regarded the atom as an utimate particle of matter. t is a correct\nstatement.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','B, C and E only','A, B and C only','C, D and E only','A and E only','NEW','11-016','11-016',NULL),
(11155,'chemistry','One mole of carbon atom weighs 12 g, the number of atoms in it is equal to,\n(Mass of carbon - 12 is 1. 9926 × ⁻²³ g)\n(NEET- 2020)','6. 022 x 10²³','one mole carbon = 12 gram\n\n1 mole is having = 6. 023 x 10²³ atoms\n\nso 12 gram carbon will have 6. 023 x 10²³ atom','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1.2 × 10²³','6.022 × 10²²','12 × 10²²','6. 022 x 10²³','NEW','11-017','11-017',NULL),
(11156,'chemistry','The percentage of Se in peroxidase anhydrous enzyme is 0.5% by weight\n(at. wt = 78.4) then the minimum molecular weight of peroxidase anhydrous\nenzymes Is:\n(AIPMT - 2001)','1.568 x 10⁴','In peroxidase anlydrous enzyme 0.55 Se is present means, 0.5gm. Se is present in 100gm of enzyme In a molecule of enzyme one Se atom must be present hence 78.4 gm Se will be present in\n100/0.5 x 78.4 = 1.568 x10⁴','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1.568 x 10⁴','1.568 x 10³','15.68','2. 136 × 10⁴','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(11157,'chemistry','In haemoglobin (Molecular wt = 67200) iron found is 0.33% (by weight). The number of iron atoms) in one molecule will be:\n(AIPMT - 1998)','4','The molecular weight of haemoglobin=67200\nIt contains 0.334% of iron by weight.\nWeight of iron = 0.334/100 x 67200 = 224.448\nNo. of atoms = Weight of iron in haemoglobin/Atomic weight \n= 224.448/56\n= 4.008\nNo. of iron atoms present in one molecule of haemoglobin is 4.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(11158,'chemistry','An organic compound contains 80% (by wt.) carbon and the remaining percentage of hydrogen. The empirical formula of this compound is:\n[Atomic wt. of C is 12, H is 1]\n(NEET - 2021)','CH₃','The amount of C and H in the organic compound is 80 g and 20 g. Calculate the ratio of C and H is as follows in the diagram:\nThe ratio of C and H in the empirical formula is 1:3.03 ~1:3.\nThe empirical formula is CH₃. (11158E)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CH₃','CH₄','CH','CH₂','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(11159,'chemistry','Percentage of C, H & N are given as follows:\nC=40%, H=13.33%, N=46.67%\nThe empirical formula will be:\n(AIPMT - 1998)','CH₄N','Consider the compound to be made of 100g. Convert percentile to weight in grams. then, convert wt. in grams to moles.\nMoles of C = 40/12 = 3.33\nMoles of H = 13.33/1 = 13.33\nMoles of N = 46.6/14 = 3.33\nNow divide each mole value by lowest value of n as:\nC = 3.33/3.33 = 1\nН = 13.33/3.33 = 4.00\nN = 3.33/3.33 = 1\nThe emperical formula is CH₄N.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CH₂N','C₂H₄N','CH₄N','CH₃N','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(11160,'chemistry','The percentages of C, H, and N in an organic compound are 40%, 13.3%, and 46.7% respectively.\nThe empirical formula of the compound is:\n(AIPMT - 2002)','CH₄N','Consider the compound to be made of 100g. Convert percentile to weight in grams. then, convert wt. in grams to moles.\nMoles of C = 40/12 = 3.33\nMoles of H = 13.33/1 = 13.33\nMoles of N = 46.6/14 = 3.33\nNow divide each mole value by lowest value of n as:\nC = 3.33/3.33 = 1\nН = 13.33/3.33 = 4.00\nN = 3.33/3.33 = 1\nThe emperical formula is CH₄N.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','C₃H₁₃N₃','CH₂N','CH₄N','CH₆N','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(11161,'chemistry','The mole ratio of H₂ and O₂ gas is 8:1. The ratio of their weight will be:\n(AIPMT - 1999)','1:2','Step 1: The formula of the number of moles is equal to\namount of substance/molar mass of substance \nThe mole ratio of H₂ and O₂ gas is 8.\nnH₂/nO₂= 8\n\nStep 2: Calculate the ratio of weight is as follows amount of H₂/molar mass of H₂ /weight of O₂/molar mass of O₂ = 8\n\nMolar mass of H₂ is 2 g mol⁻¹ and molar mass of O₂ is 32 g mol⁻¹\n\namount of H₂/2/weight of O₂/32 = 8\n\n16 x weight of H₂/weight of O₂ = 8\n\nweight of H₂/weight of O₂ = 8/16 = 1/2','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1:1','2:1','4: 1','1:2','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(11162,'chemistry','4NH₃ + 5O₂ → 6H₂O + 4NO\nWhen one mole ammonia and one mole oxygen are taken:\n(AIPMT - 1998)','Oxygen is completely consumed','4NH₃ + 5O₂ → 4NO + 6H₂O\n1NH₃ + 1.25O₂ → 1NO + 1.5H₂O\nHence 1 mole of NH; reacts with .125 moles of 02 to produce 1 mole of NO and 1.5 moles of H₂O. Hence when one mole of ammonia and one mole of oxygen are made to react to completion, then all the oxygen are made to react to completion, then all the oxygen is consumed.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Oxygen is completely consumed','Ammonia is completely consumed','Both (1) and (2) are correct','None of the above options is correct','NEW','11-021','11-021',NULL),
(11163,'chemistry','The weight of 70% HNO₃ concentrated nitric acid solution that should be used to prepare 250 mL of 2.0 M HNO₃ is:\n(AIPMT - 2013)','45.0 g conc. HNO₃','(Always remember: 1000 ml 1M sol of any substance is formed by its molecular mass)\n1000 ml of 1M sol =63 g HNO₃\n1000 ml of 2M sol=126 g HNO₃\n250 ml of 2M urea=126 x 250/1000 = 31.5g HNO₃\nGiven that 70 g HNO₃ is present in 100 g sol\n31.5 g HNO₃ will be present in 100 x 3.15/70 = 45 g','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','90.0 g conc. HNO₃','70.0 g conc. HNO₃','54.0 g conc. HNO₃','45.0 g conc. HNO₃','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11164,'chemistry','The density of a 2 M aqueous solution of NaOH is 1.28 g/ cm³. The molality of the solution is:\nmolecular mass of NaOH = 40 gmo⁻¹]\n(NEET - 2019)','1.67 m','Step 1: Moles of NaOH = 2 mol\nMass of NaOH = Vxd =1000 x1.28\nMass of NaOH =1280\nAlso, weight(H20)=1280 - 80 =1200g = 1.2 kg\nStep 2: Molality = number\nof mole of solute amount of solvent\nMolality = 2/1.2kg = 1.67 m\nThus, option 3 is the correct answer.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1.20 m','1.56 m','1.67 m','1.32 m','NEW','11-024','11-024',NULL),
(11165,'chemistry','The volume occupied by one water molecule (density = 1g cm³) is:\n(AIPMT - 2008)','3. 3. 0 x 10⁻²³ ans\ncm³','STEP 1:\nAccording to the mole concept,\n1 mol of water =6.02 x 10²³ molecules of water =18g\n\nTherefore, Mass of one molecule of water(by unitary method), m = 18/6.023 x 10²³ g\n\nSTEP 2:\nNow as, Density (d) = m/V\nBy rearranging the formula, V = m/d\n(Given, density = 1 g cm⁻³)\nHence, V = m/d = 18/6.023 x 10²³ x 1 = 3 x 10²³ cm³','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','9.0 x 10⁻²³ cm³','6. 023 x 10⁻²³ cm³','3. 3. 0 x 10⁻²³ ans\ncm³','5.5 × 10⁻²³ cm³','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11166,'chemistry','Which among the following has the maximum number of molecules?\n(AIPMT - 2002)','2 gm H₂','1 mole of any element contain 6.023 x 10²³ number of molecules.1 g mole of O₂ = 32 g O₂ => 16 g of O₂ = 0.5 g mole O₂1 g mole of N₂ = 28 g N₂=> 7 g N₂ = 0.25 g mole N₂1 g mole of H₂ = 2 g H₂=> 2 g H₂ = 1.0 g mole H₂1 g mole NO₂ = 14 + 16 x 2 = 46=> 16 g of NO₂ = 0.35 mole NO₂ 2 g H2 1 g mole H₂ contain maximum molecules.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','7 gm N₂','2 gm H₂','16 gm NO₂','16 gm O₂','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11167,'chemistry','The number of water molecules is maximum in?\n(NEET - 2015)','18 moles of water','Since mole is the largest unit, therefore it will have the highest number of molecules.\nWe know that, 1 mole of water contains = 6.02 x 10²³  molecules\n:18 moles of water contain = 18 × 6.02 x 10²³ molecules\nAs 1 mole of water= 18 g of water\n18 g of water contains = 6.02 × 10²³ molecules\nAlso, 1.8 g of water contains = 6.02 x 10²² molecules','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','18 molecules of water','1.8 g of water','18 g of water','18 moles of water','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11168,'chemistry','Calculate the mole fraction of the solute in a 1.00 m aqueous solution.\n(NEET - 2015)','0.0177','Given, m=1 i.e. 1 mole of solute is present in 1 kg or 1000 g of water.\nNumber of moles of water in 1000 g = 100/18 = 55.5\nMole fraction of solute = moles/ \nof solutetotal moles\n1/1+55.5 = 1/56.5 =0.0176','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.177','0.771','0.0534','0.0177','NEW','11-024','11-024',NULL),
(11169,'chemistry','The mass of CO₂ produced by heating 20 g of 20% pure limestone as per the given below equation is\n[CaCO₃ 1200 K CaO + CO₂ ]\n(NEET - 2023)','1.76 g','Step 1:\nIn the reaction, 20% pure limestone is used. First, find 20% of 20 g of limestone.\n100% = 20 g\n1% =20/100 g\n20 % =20/100 × 20 g \n= 4 g\n\nThe molar mass of CaCO₃ is 100 g/mol and CO₂ is 44 g.\nStep 2:\n100 g of CaCO₃ = 44 g of CO₂.\n1 g of CaCO₃ = 44/100 g of CO₂.\n4 g CaCO₃ = 44/100× 4 g of CO₂.\n= 1.76 g of CO₂','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1.32 g','1.12 g','1.76 g','2.64 g','NEW','11-021','11-021',NULL),
(11170,'chemistry','Mole fraction of solute in a 1.00 molal aqueous solution is:\n(AIPMT - 2011)','0.0177','1 molal aqueous solution = 1.0 mole in 1000 g water\nnsolvent = 1000/18 = 55.56\nnsolute = 1/1+ 55.56 = 0.0177','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.0177','0.0344','1.77','0.177','NEW','11-024','11-024',NULL),
(11171,'chemistry','A compound contains C, H, and O. If C = 40% and H = 6.67% and rest is\noxygen, then the empirical formula of the compound will be: \n(AIPMT - 1999)','CH₂O','Elements % No.of moles Simple Ratio\nC = 40% 40/12 = 3.33 3.33/3.33 = 1\nH = 6.7% 6.7/1 = 6.76.7/3.33 = 2\n0 = 53.3% 53.3/16 = 3.33 3.33/3.33 = 1\nThus, emipirical formula = CH₂O','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CH₂O','CH₄O','CH₄O₂','CHO','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(11172,'chemistry','An organic compound contains carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen. Its elemental analysis gave C, 38.71%, and H, 9.67%. The empirical formula of the compound would be:\n(AIPMT - 2008)','CH₃O','The empirical formula of the given compound can be calculated in the following diagram:(11172E)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CH₃O','CH₂O','CHO','CH₄O','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(11173,'chemistry','Match list-I with list-ll: Refer diagram (11173Q)\n(NEET - 2022)','(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)','1.At STP, the volume of 1 mole of O₂ gas is 22.4 L\n22.4L O₂ = 1 mol\n1 L O₂ = 1/22.4 mol\n4.48 L O₂ = 1/22.4 mol × 4.48\n= 0.2mol\nIn gram = 0.2 × 32\n= 6.4 g\n\n 2. 12.022 × 10²² molecules of H₂O\n1 mole = 6.022 x 10²³ H₂O molecules\n1 H₂O molecule = 1/6.02 x 10²³ mol\n12.022 x 1022 molecules of H₂O = 1/6.02 x 10²³ x 12.022 x 10²²\n0.2 mol\n\n3. 96 g of O₂\nNumber mole of O₂ = 96/32; = 3 mol\n\nAt STP, 1 mole is equal to 22.4 L\n3 mol of O₂ = 22.4 x 3\n= 67.2 L\n4. 88 g of  CO₂\nCalculate the number of mole of CO₂ as follows: \n= 88/44\n= 2 mol\n\n1 mole = 6.02 x 10²³ CO₂ molecules\n2 mol = 6.02 x 10²³ x 2 CO₂ molecules = 12.044 x 10²³ molecules','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)','(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)','(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)','(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11174,'chemistry','What mass of 95% pure CaCO will be required to neutralize 50 mL of 0.5 M\nHCl solution according to the following reaction?\nCaCO3(s) + 2HCl(aq) → CaCI2(aq) + CO2(g) + 2H2O(l)\n[Calculate upto the second place of decimal point]\n(NEET - 2022)','1.32 g','Step 1:\nThe given reaction is as follows:\n\nCaCO3(s) + 2HCl(aq) → CaCI2(aq) + CO2(g) + 2H2O(l)\nCalculate the mole of HCI is neutralized as follows:\nNumber of a mole of HCI= Molarity x volume of solution (L)\n\n= 0.5 × 50 mL x 1 L/1000 mL\n= 0.025 mol\n\nStep 2:\nFrom the reaction, it is clear that 1 mole CaCO3 reacts with 2 moles of HCI. Hence, 0.025 mol HCl is required for 0.0125 mol of CaCO3.\n\nCalculate the amount of CaCO3 as follows:\n\namount of CaCO3 = molar mass of CacO3 × number of moles \n= 100 × 0.0125\n= 1.25 g\n\nCaCO3 is 95% pure means 95 g CaCO3 in a 100 g sample.\n\n95 g CaCO3 is obtained from 100 g sample\n1g CaCO3 is obtained from = 100/95 g sample\n1.25 g CaCO3 is obtained from = 100/95× 1.25\n= 1.32 g','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','9.50 g','1.25 g','1.32 g','3.65 g','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11175,'chemistry','What is the mass of precipitate formed when 50 mL of 16.9% solution of AgNO₃ is mixed with 50 mL of 5.8% NaCI solution? (Ag = 107.8, N = 14, 0 = 16, Na= 23, CI = 35.5)\n(NEET - 2015)','7 g','To calculate the mass of AgCI precipitated, find mass of AgNO₃ and Nacl in 50 ml (given) with the help of mole concept.\n100 mL solution of AgNO₃ contains= 16.9g AgNO₃\n50 mL solution of AgNO₃ contains = 8.45g AgNO₃\nAlso, given,\n100 mL solution of NaCI contains= 5.8 g Nacl\n50 mL solution of NaCI contains= 2.9g NaCI\nNumber of moles of AgNO₃ = 8.45/169.8 = 0.049\nNumber of moles of NaCI = 2.9/58.5 = 0.049\nAgNO₃ + NaCI → AgCl - NaNO₃, Initial moles 0.049 0.049 0 0\nAfter reaction 0 0 0.049 0.049\nMass of AgCI precipitated = Number of moles of AgCl ×Molecular mass = 0.049 x 143.5\n= 7 g','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','28 g','3.5 g','7 g','14 g','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11176,'chemistry','The moles of lead (Il) chloride that will be formed from a reaction between 6.5 g of PbO and 3.2 g of HCl are reacted:\n(AIPMT - 2008)','0.029','Step 1:\nThe number of moles of product formed depends on the limiting reagent which can be defined as the reagent which is present in lesser quantity.\nSince the desired reaction is:\nPbO      +    2HCL     >  PbCl₂ + H₂O\n207.2 +16  2(35.5 + 1)  207.2 +71\n= 223.2      = 73            278.2\n\nFrom the stoichiometry of this well-balanced reaction, we come to know that 1 mole of PbO reacts with 2 moles of HCI, therefore PbO is the limiting reagent as it is lesser than HCI.\n\nHence, now we will calculate the amount of PbCl formed in terms of PbO.\n\nStep 2:\nIn terms of weight, we can say that\n223.2 g PbO gives PbCI₂ = 278.2g\n:.6.5 g PbO will give PbCI₂\n= 278.2/223.2 × 65 g\n= 278.2 x6.5/ 223.2x278.2 mol\n= 0.029 mol','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.044','0.333','0.011','0.029','NEW','11-021','11-021',NULL),
(11201,'chemistry','Who mentioned that atoms could form pairs or triplets, among other combinations and unseen forces cause interaction between them?','Acharya Kanda','Acharya Kanda formulated the theory of very small indivisible particles, which he named ‘Paramãnu’ (comparable to atoms).\n\nHe said these (Paramãnu) could form pairs or triplets, among other combinations and unseen forces cause interactions between them.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -3 Paragraph - 5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','John Dalton','Acharya Kanda','Dobereiner','Mendeleev','NEW','11-003','11-003',NULL),
(11202,'chemistry','Which of the following doesn\'t define the atoms according to Kanda?','An atom can be sensed by human organ','According to Kanda, all substances are aggregated form of smaller units called atoms (Paramãnu), which are eternal, indestructible, spherical, suprasensible and in motion in the original state. \n\nHe explained that this individual entity cannot be sensed through any human organ.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -3 Paragraph - 5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','An atom is spherical in shape','An atom can be sensed by human organ','An atom is eternal','An atom is in motion','NEW','11-003','11-003',NULL),
(11203,'chemistry','Arrange the following events in correct chronological order from the first event to the latest event -\n\ni) Pharmaceutical industry based on Ayurveda declined.\n\nii) Indigenous traditional techniques gradually declined.\n\niii) Iatrochemistry declined due to the introduction and practise of western medicinal system.\n\niv) European scientists started coming to India and modern chemistry started growing.','iii) → i) → ii) → iv)','Correct chronological order from first event to latest event is\n\niii) Iatrochemistry declined due to the introduction and practise of western medicinal system.\n\ni) Pharmaceutical industry based on Ayurveda declined.\n\nii) Indigenous traditional techniques gradually declined.\n\niv) European scientists started coming to India and modern chemistry started growing.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -3 Paragraph - 7)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','ii) → i) → iii) → iv)','iii) → iv) → ii) → i)','i) → ii) → iii) → iv)','iii) → i) → ii) → iv)','NEW','11-004','11-004',NULL),
(11204,'chemistry','Where are principles of Chemistry not applicable?','Managing computer data','Principles of chemistry are applicable in diverse areas, such as weather patterns, functioning of brain and operation of a computer, production in chemical industries, manufacturing fertilisers, alkalis, acids, salts, dyes, polymers, drugs, soaps, detergents, metals, alloys, etc., including new material.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -4 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Weather patterns','Operation of a computer','Functioning of brain','Managing computer data','NEW','11-004','11-004',NULL),
(11205,'chemistry','With a better understanding of chemical principles it has now become possible to design and synthesise new material having specific-\n\ni) Magnetic properties\n\nii) Electric properties\n\niii) Optical properties\n\niv) Biological properties\n\nWhich of the following are correct?','i) ii) iii)','With a better understanding of chemical principles it has now become possible to design and synthesise new material having specific magnetic, electric and optical properties.\n\nChemistry has helped in establishing industries which manufacture utility goods, like acids, alkalies, dyes, polymers metals, etc. \n\nThese industries contribute in a big way to the economy of a nation and generate employment.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -4 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','i) ii) iii)','i) ii) iv)','i) iii) iv)','ii) iii) iv)','NEW','11-004','11-004',NULL),
(11206,'chemistry','What is it that has mass and occupies space?','Matter','Anything which has mass and occupies space is called matter.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -4 Paragraph - 7)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Atom','Compound','Element','Matter','NEW','11-004','11-004',NULL),
(11207,'chemistry','Why was the metric system, which originated in France in late eighteenth century, more convenient?','Because it was based on decimal system','The metric system, which originated in France in late eighteenth century, was more convenient as it was based on the decimal system.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -7 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Because it was based on MKS system','Because it was based on decimal system','Because it was based on FPS system','Because it was based on SI system','NEW','11-007','11-007',NULL),
(11208,'chemistry','Who established the International System of Units (in French Le Systeme International d’Unités abbreviated as SI)?','11th General Conference on Weights and Measures','The International System of Units (in French Le Systeme International d’Unités - abbreviated as SI) was established by the11th General Conference on Weights and Measures (CGPM from Conference Generale des Poids et Measures). \n\nThe CGPM is an intergovernmental treaty organisation created by a diplomatic treaty known as Metre Convention, which was signed in Paris in 1875.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -7 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','12th General Conference on Weights and Measures','11th General Conference on Weights and Measures','14th General Conference on Weights and Measures','13th General Conference on Weights and Measures','NEW','11-007','11-007',NULL),
(11209,'chemistry','What do prefixes indicate?\n\ni) Multiples of a unit.\n\nii) Submultiples of a unit.\n\niii) Dimensions of the unit.','Both i) and ii)','The SI system allows the use of prefixes to indicate the multiples or submultiples of a unit.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -8 Above table 1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both ii) and iii)','Both i) and ii)','Only i)','Only ii)','NEW','11-008','11-008',NULL),
(11210,'chemistry','Numerical value of which entity/unit is used to define \'Ampere\'?','Elementary charge','The ampere, symbol A, is the SI unit of electric current. \n\nIt is defined by taking the fixed numerical value of the elementary charge e to be 1.602176634 × 10⁻¹⁹ when expressed in the unit C, which is equal to As, where the second is defined in terms of ∆VCs.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -8 table 1.2) diagram(ne0102)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Coulomb','Volt','Current','Elementary charge','NEW','11-008','11-008',NULL),
(11211,'chemistry','The Kelvin, symbol K, is the SI unit of thermodynamic temperature. Numerical value of which entity/unit is used to define \'Kelvin\'?','Boltzmann constant','The Kelvin, symbol k, is the SI unit of thermodynamic temperature. \n\nIt is defined by taking the fixed numerical value of the Boltzmann constant k to be 1.380649 × 10⁻²³ when expressed in the unit JK⁻¹, which is equal to kgm²s⁻²k⁻¹ where the kilogram, metre and second are defined in terms of h, c and ∆VCₛ.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -8 table 1.2) diagram (ne0102)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Fahrenheit','Celsius','Kilogram','Boltzmann constant','NEW','11-008','11-008',NULL),
(11212,'chemistry','Match the prefixes with the correct multiples given below. diagram(n0112)','i) - b)   ii) - d)   iii) - a)  iv) - c)','i)    Yocto - 10⁻²⁴               \nii)    Yotta - 10²⁴           \niii)   Zepto - 10⁻²¹            \niv)   Zetta - 10²¹ \n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -9 table 1.3) diagram(ne0103)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i) - a)   ii) - d)   iii) - b)  iv) - c)','i) - b)   ii) - c)   iii) - a)  iv) - d)','i) - b)   ii) - d)   iii) - a)  iv) - c)','i) - b)   ii) - d)   iii) - c)  iv) - d)','NEW','11-009','11-009',NULL),
(11213,'chemistry','What is used to determine the mass of a substance accurately in the laboratory?','Analytical balance','The mass of a substance can be determined accurately in the laboratory by using analytical balance.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -8 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Beam balance','Analytical balance','Triple beam balance','All of these','NEW','11-008','11-008',NULL),
(11214,'chemistry','In chemistry laboratories, smaller volumes are used. They are A or B units. What do A and B stand for?','A - cm³    B - dm³','In SI system, volume has units of m³. \n\nBut again, in chemistry laboratories, smaller volumes are used.\n \nHence, volume is often denoted in cm³ or dm³ units.\n\nTherefore A stands for cm³ and B stands for dm³.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -9 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','A - dm³    B - mm³','A - cm³    B - dm³','A - cm³     B - mm³','A - cm³    B - m³','NEW','11-009','11-009',NULL),
(11215,'chemistry','Instrument A is used to measure volume of liquids or solutions while instrument B is used to prepare a known volume of solution. What are A and B respectively?','All of these','In the laboratory, the volume of liquids or solutions can be measured by graduated cylinder, burette, pipette, etc. \n\nA volumetric flask is used to prepare a known volume of a solution.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -10 Paragraph - 1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Graduated cylinder, Volumetric flask','Burette, Volumetric flask','Pipette, Volumetric flask','All of these','NEW','11-010','11-010',NULL),
(11216,'chemistry','For getting reliable measurements, which devices have been calibrated by their manufacturers to give correct readings?','Both a and b','For getting reliable measurements, all devices such as metre sticks and analytical balances have been calibrated by their manufacturers to give correct readings.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -11 Paragraph - 1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Analytical balances','Metre sticks','Metre tapes','Both a and b','NEW','11-011','11-011',NULL),
(11217,'chemistry','Pt-Ir chosen was chosen for mass standard because -\n\ni) it is highly resistant to chemical attack.\n\nii) it can measure mass with highest accuracy.\n\niii) its mass will not change for an extremely long.','i) and iii)','The mass standard is the kilogram since 1889. \n\nIt has been defined as the mass of platinum-iridium (Pt-Ir) cylinder that is stored in an airtight jar at International Bureau of Weights and Measures in Sevres, France. \n\nPt-Ir was chosen for this standard because it is highly resistant to chemical attack and its mass will not change for an extremely long.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -11 Paragraph - 1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i) and iii)','i) and ii)','ii) and iii)','Only ii)','NEW','11-011','11-011',NULL),
(11218,'chemistry','State whether the following are true or false.\n\n1) Scientists are in search of a new standard for length.\n\n2) Work on this new standard focuses on ways to measure accurately the number of molecules in a well-defined mass of sample.\n\n3) The search for new standard of mass is being attempted through accurate determination of Avogadro constant.\n\n4) Density of a crystal of ultrapure silicon, has an accuracy of about 1 part in 10⁶.','1) F   2) F   3) T    4) T','Scientists are in search of a new standard for mass. This is being attempted through accurate determination of Avogadro constant. \n\nWork on this new standard focuses on ways to measure accurately the number of atoms in a well-defined mass of sample.\n \nOne such method, which uses X-rays to determine the atomic density of a crystal of ultrapure silicon, has an accuracy of about 1 part in 10⁶ but has not yet been adopted to serve as a standard. \n\nThere are other methods but none of them are presently adequate to replace the Pt-Ir cylinder.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -11 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1) T   2) F   3) T    4) T','1) F   2) F   3) T    4) T','1) F   2) F   3) T    4) F','1) F   2) F   3) F    4) T','NEW','11-011','11-011',NULL),
(11219,'chemistry','The metre was originally defined as A. What is A?','the length between two marks on a Pt-Ir bar kept at a temperature of 0°C (273.15 K).','The metre was originally defined as the length between two marks on a Pt-Ir bar kept at a temperature of 0°C (273.15 K). \n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -11 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','the length between two marks on a Pt-Ir bar kept at a temperature of 0°C (273.15 K).','1.65076373 × 10⁶ times the wavelength of light emitted by a krypton laser.','the length of path travelled by light in vacuum during a time interval of 1/299 792 458 of a second.','None of these','NEW','11-011','11-011',NULL),
(11220,'chemistry','In 1960 the length of the metre was defined as B. What is B?','1.65076373 × 10⁶ times the wavelength of light emitted by a krypton laser.','In 1960 the length of the metre was defined as 1.65076373 × 10⁶ times the wavelength of light emitted by a krypton laser.\n \nAlthough this was a cumbersome number, it preserved the length of the metre at its agreed value. \n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -11 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','The length of path travelled by light in vacuum during a time interval of 1/299 792 458 of a second.','The length between two marks on a Pt-Ir bar kept at a temperature of 0°C (273.15 K).','1.65076373 × 10⁶ times the wavelength of light emitted by a krypton laser.','None of these','NEW','11-011','11-011',NULL),
(11221,'chemistry','The metre was redefined in 1983 by CGPM as C. C is -','The length of path travelled by light in vacuum during a time interval of 1/299 792 458 of a second.','The metre was redefined in 1983 by CGPM as the length of path travelled by light in vacuum during a time interval of 1/299 792 458 of a second. \n\nSimilar to the length and the mass, there are reference standards for other physical quantities.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -11 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','The length of path travelled by light in vacuum during a time interval of 1/299 792 458 of a second.','1.65076373 × 10⁶ times the wavelength of light emitted by a krypton laser.','The length between two marks on a Pt-Ir bar kept at a temperature of 0°C (273.15 K).','None of these','NEW','11-011','11-011',NULL),
(11222,'chemistry','What is the reason behind every experimental measurement having some amount of uncertainty associated with it?','All of these','Every experimental measurement has some amount of uncertainty associated with it because of limitation of measuring instrument and the skill of the person making the measurement.\n\nRoom temperature also plays role in meausrement by altering the temperature readings. \n\nHence, all the experiments should be carried out at NTP to avoid any discrepancies.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -12 Paragraph - 5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Limitation of measuring instrument.','The skill of the person making the measurement.','Room temperature.','All of these','NEW','11-012','11-012',NULL),
(11223,'chemistry','The uncertainty is indicated by writing the certain digits and the Y uncertain digit/s. What does Y stand for?','Last','The uncertainty is indicated by writing the certain digits and the last uncertain digit.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -12 Paragraph - 6)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','First','Second','Last','Last two','NEW','11-012','11-012',NULL),
(11224,'chemistry','In a result, 11.2 mL, if we say that the 11 is certain and 2 is uncertain then the uncertainty would be (unless otherwise stated) ?','∓1','The uncertainty is indicated by writing the certain digits and the last uncertain digit. \n\nThus, if we write a result as 11.2 mL, we say the 11 is certain and 2 is uncertain and the uncertainty would be ∓1 in the last digit.\n\nUnless otherwise stated,an uncertainty of plus or minus 1 in the last digit is always understood.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -12 Paragraph - 6)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','∓1','∓2','-1','-2','NEW','11-012','11-012',NULL),
(11225,'chemistry','How many laws govern the combination of elements to form compounds?','Five','The combination of elements to form compounds is governed by the following five basic laws. Law of Conservation of Mass, Definite, Multiple Proportions, Gay Lussac\'s Law of Gaseous Volumes and Avagadro\'s Law.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -14 Paragraph - last)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Five','Four','Three','Seven','NEW','11-014','11-014',NULL),
(11226,'chemistry','The law of conservation of mass was the result of exact measurement of masses of reactants and products. Who performed the experiments carefully for this?','Lavoisier','The law of conservation of mass was the result of exact measurement of masses of reactants and products, and carefully planned experiments performed by Lavoisier.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -14 Paragraph - last)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Lavoisier','Gay Lussac','Joseph Proust','Joseph Louis','NEW','11-014','11-014',NULL),
(11227,'chemistry','"Irrespective of the source, a given compound always contains same elements combined together in the same proportion by mass".\nWhich law is demonstrated by the above statement?','Law of definite composition','"Irrespective of the source, a given compound always contains same elements combined together in the same proportion by mass."\n\nThe above statement demonstrates the law of Definite composition.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -15 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Law of constant composition','Law of multiple proportions','Law of definite composition','Law of conservation of mass','NEW','11-015','11-015',NULL),
(11228,'chemistry','Who mentioned that atoms of the same kind cannot combine and molecules of oxygen or hydrogen containing two atoms did not exist?','Dalton','Dalton and others at that time mentioned that atoms of the same kind cannot combine and molecules of oxygen or hydrogen containing two atoms did not exist\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -15 Last line)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Lavoisier','Joseph Proust','Avogadro','Dalton','NEW','11-015','11-015',NULL),
(11229,'chemistry','What is similar in each box? Refer diagram(n0110)','Number of molecules','Each box contains equal number of molecules.\n\nVolumes can\'t be the answer because the water\'s box contains 2 volumes of water vapours.\n\nMasses and electrons can\'t be the answer because the Hydrogen/, Oxygen and water all three have different masses and different no. of electrons\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -15 Last paragraph)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Volumes','Number of molecules','Masses','Number of electrons','NEW','11-015','11-015','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/11229q.png'),
(11230,'chemistry','What did Stanislao Cannizzaro\'s sketch of a course of chemical philosophy, emphasised on?','Importance of Avogadro\'s work','In 1860, the first international conference on chemistry was held in Karlsruhe, Germany, to resolve various ideas. \n\nAt the meeting, Stanislao Cannizzaro presented a sketch of a course of chemical philosophy, which emphasised on the importance of Avogadro’s work.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -16 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Importance of Louis\'s work','Importance of Avogadro\'s work','Importance of Dalton\'s work','Importance of Proust\'s work','NEW','11-016','11-016',NULL),
(11231,'chemistry','What is used for determining the atomic masses fairly accurately?','Mass spectroscopy','Today, we have sophisticated techniques e.g., mass spectrometry for determining the atomic masses fairly accurate.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -16 Paragraph-7)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Mass spectroscopy','Argon atom','Analytical balances','Cesium-133 atom','NEW','11-016','11-016',NULL),
(11232,'chemistry','Which among the following doesn\'t define the packing of constituent units in Sodium chloride?','Discrete','Some substances, such as sodium chloride, do not contain discrete molecules as their constituent units. \n\nIn such compounds, positive (sodium ion) and negative (chloride ion) entities are arranged in a three-dimensional structure,\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -17 Paragraph-8)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Systematic','Discrete','Aggregate','Three-dimensional structure','NEW','11-017','11-017',NULL),
(11233,'chemistry','What number of entities is present in the mole of a substance?','Same','An elementary entity may be an atom, a molecule, an ion, an electron, any other particle or specified group of particles. \n\nIt may be emphasised that the mole of a substance always contains the same number of entities, no matter what the substance may be.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -18 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Small','Large','Same','Different','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11234,'chemistry','Calculate the mass percent of Hydrogen in Water.','11.18 %','Thus, the mass percent of Hydrogen in water is 11.18 %.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -19 Paragraph-2) diagram(ne0121)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','11.18 %','88.79 %','10.08 %','12.06 %','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(11235,'chemistry','What do the Greek words \'stoicheion\' and \'metron\' mean?','Element and measure respectively','The word ‘stoichiometry’ is derived from two Greek words — stoicheion (meaning, element) and metron (meaning, measure).\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -20 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Element and measure respectively','Atom and measure respectively','Molecule and measure respectively','Compound and measure respectively','NEW','11-020','11-020',NULL),
(11236,'chemistry','A majority of reactions in the laboratories are carried out in X. What is X here?','Solutions','A majority of reactions in the laboratories are carried out in solutions.\n\nBurette is a laboratory apparatus commonly used to dispense and measure variable amounts of fluid.\n\nPipettes provide a clean and hygienic environment to aspirate, transport and dispense liquid samples.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -21 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Burettes','Pipettes','Solutions','Both a and b','NEW','11-021','11-021',NULL),
(11237,'chemistry','Under which category do many of the substances present around us fall into?','Mixtures','Many of the substances present around us are mixtures.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -24 Summary paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Elements','Compounds','Mixtures','Matter','NEW','11-024','11-024',NULL),
(11238,'chemistry','Which one is the inherent property of a substance?','Measurements','When the properties of a substance are studied, measurement is inherent. \n\nThe quantification of properties requires a system of measurement and units in which the quantities are to be expressed.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -24 Summary paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Particles','Units','Dimensions','Measurements','NEW','11-024','11-024',NULL),
(11239,'chemistry','What is the range of the measurements of quantities in chemistry?','10⁻³¹ to 10²³','The measurements of quantities in chemistry are spread over a wide range of 10⁻³¹ to 10²³.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -25 Summary paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','10⁻³¹ to 10²³','10⁻²³ to 10³¹','10⁻²⁰ to 10³⁰','10⁻¹⁰ to 10²⁰','NEW','11-025','11-025',NULL),
(11240,'chemistry','What is taken care of by specifying the number of significant figures, in which the observations are reported?','Uncertainty','The uncertainty is taken care of by specifying the number of significant figures, in which the observations are reported.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -25 Summary paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Uncertainty','Order of magnitude','Units','Dimensions','NEW','11-025','11-025',NULL),
(11241,'chemistry','What helps to express the measured quantities in different systems of units?','Dimensional analysis','The dimensional analysis helps to express the measured quantities in different systems of units.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -25 Summary paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Units','Dimensional analysis','Significant nos.','Measuring instruments','NEW','11-025','11-025',NULL),
(11242,'chemistry','Which among the following laws led to the Dalton’s atomic theory?\n\n1) The Law of Conservation of Mass, \n\n2) Law of Definite Proportions, \n\n3) Law of Multiple Proportions, \n\n4) Law of conservation of energy. \n\n5) Avogadro Law.','All except 4)','The combination of different atoms is governed by basic laws of chemical combination these being\n\n1) The Law of Conservation of Mass, \n\n2) Law of Definite Proportions, \n\n3) Law of Multiple Proportions, \n\n4) Gay Lussac’s Law of Gaseous Volumes  \n\n5) Avogadro Law. \n\nAll these laws led to the Dalton’s atomic theory, which states that atoms are building blocks of matter.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -25 Summary paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1)','Both 1) and 2)','All except 4)','All of these','NEW','11-025','11-025',NULL),
(11243,'chemistry','What can be calculated by determining the mass percent of different elements present in a compound and its molecular mass?','Molecular formula','The molecular formula can be calculated by determining the mass percent of different elements present in a compound and its molecular mass.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -25 Summary paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Molecular formula','Formula mass','Percentage by mass','Molality','NEW','11-025','11-025',NULL),
(11244,'chemistry','Which ratio/s is/are indicated by the coefficients of a balanced chemical reaction?','Molar','The coefficients of a balanced chemical reaction indicate the molar ratios and the respective number of particles taking part in a particular reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -25 Summary paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Atomic','Volume','Molar','All of these','NEW','11-025','11-025',NULL),
(11245,'chemistry','The ________ study of the reactants required or the products formed is called stoichiometry.','Quantitative','The quantitative study of the reactants required or the products formed is called stoichiometry.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -25 Summary paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Qualitative','Quantitative','Scientific','Physical','NEW','11-025','11-025',NULL),
(11246,'chemistry','Which among the following expresses the amount of substance present in a given volume of a solution?\n\n1)  Mass percent\n \n2)  Mole fraction \n\n3)  Molarity \n\n4)  Molality','All of these','The amount of substance present in a given volume of a solution is expressed in number of ways, e.g., mass percent, mole fraction, molarity and molality.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -25 Summary paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 2','Both 1 and 3','Both 2 and 3','All of these','NEW','11-025','11-025',NULL),
(11247,'chemistry','Determine the molecular formula of an oxide of iron, in which the mass percent of iron and oxygen are 69.9 and 30.1, respectively.','Fe₂O₃','Thus, the molecular formula is Fe₂O₃.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -26 Problem-1.8) diagram(ne0122)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Fe₃O₄','Fe₂O₃','FeO','None of these','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(11248,'chemistry','In three moles of Ethane (C₂H₆), calculate number of molecules of Ethane.','18.066 x 10²³','1 mole Ethane contains 6.022 x 10²³ molecules \n\nTherefore, 3 moles of Ethane has 3 x 6.022 x 10²³ molecules i. e 18.066 x 10²³ molecules.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -26 Problem-1.10)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','18.066 x 10²³','3','6.022 x 10²³','18.066 x 10²⁴','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11249,'chemistry','Pressure is determined as force per unit area of the surface. The SI unit of pressure, pascal is 1Pa = 1N m⁻². If mass of air at sea level is 1034 g cm⁻², calculate the pressure in pascal.','1.0 x 10⁵ Pa.','Mass of air column is given as 1034 grams per sq. cm, or 10340 kg per sq.m. \n\nThe gravitational force on this air column is (10340 x 9.8) N per sq.m = 101332 N per sq. m = 1.0 x 10⁵ Pa.\n \nThe result is rounded off to 2 decimal places because 9.8 is least precise with 2 SF.\n\nAnswer is 101332 Pa, or in 2 SF: 1.0 x 10⁵ Pa.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -26 Problem-1.13)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1.0 x 10⁵ Pa.','1.0 x 10⁶ Pa.','1 Pa','10 Pa','NEW','11-007','11-007',NULL),
(11250,'chemistry','A sample of drinking water was found to be severely contaminated with chloroform, CHCl₃ ,supposed to be carcinogenic in nature. The level of contamination was 15 ppm (by mass).Determine the molality of chloroform in the water sample.','1.266 x 10⁻⁴ m','Molar mass of chloroform =119.5  g/mol\n\nMass of solvent  = 1000000−15 = 999985 g\n\nMolality = (moles of solute/mass of solvent in g) x 1000 \n\n             = (15/119.5 × 999985) x 1000 = 1.266 x 10⁻⁴ m\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -26 Problem-1.17)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1.266 x 10⁻³ m','1.266 x 10⁻⁴ m','1.266 x 10⁻⁶ m','1.266 x 10⁻⁵ m','NEW','11-024','11-024',NULL),
(11251,'chemistry','What is chemistry also called the science of?','Atoms and molecules','Chemistry deals with the composition, structure, properties and interaction of matter and is of much use to human beings in daily life. \n\nThese aspects can be best described and understood in terms of basic constituents of matter that are atoms and molecules. \n\nThat is why, chemistry is also called the science of atoms and molecules.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -3 Paragraph-last)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Atoms and molecules','Elements and compounds','Chemicals','All of these','NEW','11-001','11-001',NULL),
(11252,'chemistry','If the speed of light is 3.0 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹, calculate the distance covered by light in 2.00 ns.','0.600 m','Speed of light = 3×10⁸ ms⁻¹\n\nTime = 2.00 nsec = 2×10⁻⁹s\n\nDistance covered = Speed x time\n\nThe distance travelled is the product of speed and time.\n\nThus, in 2.00 nsec, the light will travel a distance of 3.00×10⁸ ms⁻¹ × 2.00×10⁻⁹ s = 0.600 m\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -27 Problem-1.22)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','06.00 m','0.600 m','600.0 m','60.00 m','NEW','11-008','11-008',NULL),
(11253,'chemistry','Calcium carbonate reacts with aqueous HCl to give CaCl₂ and CO₂ according to the reaction,\n \nCaCO₃(s) + 2HCl (aq) → CaCl₂(aq) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(l)\n\nWhat mass of CaCO₃ is required to react completely with 25 mL of 0.75 M HCl?','0.9375 g','No. of moles of HCl present in 25 ml of 0.75 M of HCl solution =  (25/1000) × 0.75 = 0.01875 moles.\n\nAccording to the given reaction:\n\n2 moles of HCl react completely with 1 mole of Calcium Carbonate.\n\n⟹ 1 moles of HCl completely reacts with 0.5 mole of Calcium Carbonate.\n\nSo, 0.01875 moles of HCl will completely react with (0.5 × 0.01875 = 0.009375) moles of Calcium Carbonate.\n\nWeight of 0.009375 moles of Calcium carbonate = 100 × 0.009375 g = 0.9375 g\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -28 Problem-1.35)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','9.375 g','0.9375 g','93.75 g','0.09375 g','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11254,'chemistry','Chlorine is prepared in the laboratory by treating Manganese dioxide (MnO₂) with aqueous hydrochloric acid according to the reaction\n\n4HCl (aq) + MnO₂(s) → 2H₂O (l) + MnCl₂ (aq) + Cl₂ (g)\n\nHow many grams of HCl react with 5.0 g of Manganese dioxide?','8.4 g HCl','According to the given equation:\n\n4HCl (aq) + MnO₂(s) → 2H₂O (l) + MnCl₂ (aq) + Cl₂ (g)\n\n1 mole of MnO₂ reacts with 4 mole of HCl or 87 g MnO₂ reacts with 146 g HCl\n\n5 mg MnO₂ will react with (146/87) x 5 g HCl = 8.39 g HCl = 8.40 g HCl\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -28 Problem-1.36)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','8.4 g HCl','0.84 g HCl','0.084 g HCl','840 g HCl','NEW','11-021','11-021',NULL),
(11255,'chemistry','How many significant figures should be present in the answer of the following calculation - 5 × 5.364','26.8','5 × 5.364 = 26.82\nSince 5 has only one significant figure and 5.364 has four significant figures, the answer should have the same number of significant figures as the number with the least number of significant figures, which is 1. Therefore, the answer should be rounded to 26.8.\n\nTherefore, there are 4 Significant figures.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -28 Problem-1.31)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','20','26','26.8','Data insufficient','NEW','11-012','11-012',NULL),
(11256,'chemistry','How many significant figures are present in the following - 126,000','3','Zeros to the right are not significant if decimal is not present. Hence, the answer is 3.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -26 Problem-1.19)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','3','4','6','5','NEW','11-012','11-012',NULL),
(11257,'chemistry','If 10 volumes of Dihydrogen gas reacts with five volumes of Dioxygen gas, how many volumes of water vapour would be produced?','10','2H₂ + O₂ -----> 2H₂O\n\n2 volumes of Dihydrogen reacts with 1 volume of Dioxygen to give 2 volumes of water vapour.\n\nTherefore, 10 volumes of Dihydrogen reacts with 5 volume of Dioxyen to give 10 volumes of water vapour.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -27 Problem-1.26)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','5','10','5/2','3/2','NEW','11-021','11-021',NULL),
(11258,'chemistry','Convert the following into basic units: 28.7 pm','28.7 x 10⁻¹² m','The basic unit of length is metre. \n\n1 pm = 10⁻¹² m\n\nTherefore, 28.7 x 10⁻¹² m\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -27 Problem-1.27)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','28.7 x 10⁻¹² m','28.7 x 10⁻¹⁸m','28.7 x 10⁻¹⁰m','28.7 x 10⁻¹⁶m','NEW','11-009','11-009',NULL),
(11259,'chemistry','The following data are obtained when Dinitrogen and Dioxygen react together to form different combination according to the given table below. Which law of chemical combination is obeyed by the above experimental data? Refer diagram(n0111)','Law of multiple proportions.','If we double the mass of Dinitrogen in (i) and (ii), we have to double the mass of Dioxygen too, otherwise the question would change.\n\nWe double the masses of Dinitrogen to get the whole no. ratio of Dioxygen by fixing the mass of Dinitrogen.\n\nWhen the mass of Dinitrogen is 28g , mass of Dioxygen combined is 32,64,32, and 80 g.\n\nThe corresponding ratio is 1:2:1:5. \n\nIt is a simple whole number ratio.\n\nThis illustrates the law of multiple proportions.\n\nIt states that, "if two elements can combine to form more than one compound, the masses of one element that combine with a fixed mass of the other element, are in the ratio of small whole numbers.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -27 Problem-1.21)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Law of definite composition.','Law of conservation of mass','Law of multiple proportions.','Avogadro\'s law','NEW','11-015','11-015','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/11259q.png'),
(11260,'chemistry','In a reaction\n\nA + B₂  -------->  AB₂\n\nIdentify the limiting reagent, if any, in the following reaction mixtures-\n100 atoms of A + 100 molecules of B','None is the limiting reagent.','In the balanced chemical reaction,\n\nA + B₂  -------->  AB₂\n\n1 mole of A reacts with 1 mole of B to give 1 mole of AB₂.\n\n1 mole of A contains 6.022 x 10²³ atoms, however 1 mole of B contains 6.022 x 10²³ molecules of B (molecules because B is diatomic)\n\nThus, none of them is limiting.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -27 Problem-1.2)3','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','A is the limiting reagent.','B is the limiting reagent.','None is the limiting reagent.','Reaction cannot take place at all.','NEW','11-021','11-021',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(11261,'chemistry','In a reaction\n\nX + Y₂ -----> XY₂\n\nIdentify the limiting reagent, if any, in the following reaction mixtures- \n2.5 mol X + 5 mol Y₂','X is the limiting reagent.','In the balanced chemical reaction,\n\nX + Y₂ -----> XY₂\n\n1 mole of X reacts with 1 mole of Y to give 1 mole of XY₂.\n\nTherefore, 2.5 moles of X reacts with 2.5 moles of Y to give 2.5 moles of XY₂\n\nSince Y₂ is in excess by 2.5 moles, X is the limiting reagent.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -27 Problem-1.23)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','X is the limiting reagent.','Y is the limiting reagent.','None is the limiting reagent.','Reaction cannot take place at all.','NEW','11-021','11-021',NULL),
(11262,'chemistry','What is the number of HCl molecules present in 10 mL OF 0.1 M solution?','6.022 x 10²⁰','No. of moles in 10 mL of HCl = 10 x 10⁻³ x 0.1 = 10⁻³ moles\n\nTherefore, no. of molecules in 10⁻³ moles = 10⁻³ x 6.022 x 10²³ = 6.022 x 10²⁰\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -18 topic- mole concept)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','6.022 x 10²⁰','6.022 x 10²³','6.022 x 10²¹','6.022 x x10²⁴','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11263,'chemistry','The volume of a gas at 0℃ and 700 mm pressure is 760 cc. What is the no. of molecules present in the volume?','1.88 x x10²²','P = 700mm = 0.921 atm\n\nV = 760cc = 0.76 L\n\nR = 0.0821 Latm⁻¹ molK⁻¹\n\nT = 0 ∘C = 273 K\n\nn = PV/RT\n\n=0.921 × 0.76/0.0821 x 273\n\n​= 0.0321 moles \n\nTherefore no. of molecules = 0.0321 x 6.022 x 10²³ = 1.88 x x10²²\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -18 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','6.022 x 10²³','1.88 x 10²¹','1.88 x x10²²','18.8 x 10²⁰','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11264,'chemistry','How many moles of Magnesium phosphate, Mg₃(PO₄)₂ will contain 0.25 mol of Oxygen atoms?','3.125 x 10⁻²','1 mole of Magnesium phosphate Mg₃(PO₄)₂ contains 8 moles of oxygen atoms \ntherefore 0.25 moles of oxygen atoms will be present in 0.25/8 moles of Magnesium phosphate which is equal to 3.125 x 10⁻² moles\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -18 topic- mole concept)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2.5 x 10⁻²','0.02','3.125 x 10⁻²','1.25 x 10⁻²','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11265,'chemistry','Two oxides of a metal contains 50% and 40% of metal respectively. The formula of first oxide is MO. Then the formula of second oxide will be-','M₂O₃','we can use the concept of equivalent weights. Since the first oxide, MO2, contains 50% metal M, this means that the equivalent weight of M in this oxide is half the molecular weight of the oxide. Now, for the second oxide, since it contains 40% metal M, the equivalent weight of M in this oxide is 40% of the molecular weight of the oxide. By comparing the equivalent weights of M in both oxides, we can determine the formula of the second oxide. Let\'s calculate it:\nEquivalent weight of M in MO2 = (1/2) * molecular weight of MO2\nEquivalent weight of M in the second oxide = (40/100) * molecular weight of the second oxide Now, we can set up a simple proportion to find the molecular formula of the second oxide. (1/2) * molecular weight of MO2 = (40/100) * molecular weight of the second oxide Solving for the molecular weight of the second oxide will give us its formula.\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -19 Paragraph - last) diagram(ne0123)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','M₂O','MO','M₂O₅','M₂O₃','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(11266,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement.\n\na. Principles of chemistry are applicable in weather patterns.\n\nb. Chemistry contributes in a big way to the national economy.\n\nc. Cisplatin and taxol are superconducting ceramics.','a,b','Principles of chemistry are applicable in diverse areas, such as weather patterns, functioning of brain and operation of a computer, production in chemical industries, manufacturing fertilisers, alkalis, acids, salts, dyes, polymers, drugs, soaps, detergents, metals, alloys, etc., including new material.\n\nChemistry contributes in a big way to the national economy. It also plays an important role in meeting human needs for food, healthcare products and other material aimed at improving the quality of life.\n\n"Some of these drugs are Cisplatin and Taxol, which are effective in cancer therapy. \n\nThe drug AZT (Azidothymidine) is used for helping AIDS patients.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 4 Paragraph-3)"','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','none of them','NEW','11-004','11-004',NULL),
(11267,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement.\n\na. Liquids have definite volume and definite shape\n\nb. Solids take the shape of the container in which they are placed.\n\nc. Gases completely occupy the space in the container in which they are placed.','c only','(i) Solids have definite volume and definite shape.\n\n(ii) Liquids have definite volume but do not have definite shape. They take the shape of the container in which they are placed.\n\n(iii) Gases have neither definite volume no definite shape. They completely occupy the space in the container in which they are placed.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 4 Paragraph-last)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','c only','NEW','11-004','11-004',NULL),
(11268,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statement.\n\na. When all the constituent particles of a substance are same in chemical nature, it is said to be a homogenous mixture. \n\nb. Sugar solution and air are homogenous mixtures.\n\nc. Constituents particles of pure substances can be seperated using filtration, crystallisation.','a,c','When all the constituent particles of a substance are same in chemical nature, it is said to be a pure substance. \n\n"The components of a mixture can be separated by using physical methods, such as simple hand-picking, filtration, crystallisation, distillation, etc.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 5 Paragraph-6)"','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','c only','NEW','11-005','11-005',NULL),
(11269,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement:\n\na. The constituents of a compound can\'t be separated by physical method.\n\nb. Properties of a compound are different from those of its constituent elements.\n\nc. Oxygen burns in air with a pop sound.','a,b','The constituents of a compound can\'t be separated by physical method. They can be separated by chemical methods.\n\nAlso, the properties of a compound are different from those of its constituent elements.\n\n"It is interesting to note that hydrogen burns with a pop sound and oxygen is a supporter of combustion, but water is used as a fire extinguisher.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 6 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','c only','NEW','11-006','11-006',NULL),
(11270,'chemistry','Which of them is not a physical property of a substance?','Combustibility','Properties, like composition, combustibility, reactivity with acids and bases, etc. are chemical properties.\n"Physical properties, such as colour, odour, melting point, boiling point, density are used to describe matter.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 6 Paragraph-5)"','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Boiling point','Color','Combustibility','Melting point','NEW','11-006','11-006',NULL),
(11271,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement:\n\na.  The mass of a substance can be determined by using an analytical balance..\n\nb. A volumetric flask is used to prepare a known volume of a solution.\n\nc. In Kelvin scale, negaative temperature is not possible','a,b,c','The mass of a substance can be determined accurately in the laboratory by using analytical balance.\n\nA volumetric flask is used to prepare a known volume of a solution.In the laboratory, the volume of liquids or solutions can be measured by graduated cylinder, burette, pipette, etc.\n\nc. In Kelvin scale, negaative temperature is not possible\n\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page -8 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,b,c','NEW','11-008','11-008',NULL),
(11272,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement:\n\na. Law of Conservation of Mass was given by Lavoisier\n\nb. Law of Definite Proportions states a given compound always contains exactly the same proportion of elements by their atomic number\n\nc. Law of Definite Composition was given by J. Proust','a,c','Law of Conservation of Mass was given by Lavoisier. The conclusion is that matter can neither be created nor destroyed. \n\nThis is called ‘Law of Conservation of Mass’.\n\n"Proust concluded that irrespective of the source, a given compound always contains same elements combined together in the same proportion by mass.\nIt is sometimes referred as Law of Definite Composition\n\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 14 Paragraph-8)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,b,c','NEW','11-014','11-014',NULL),
(11273,'chemistry','Choose the wrong statement:\n\na. If two elements can combine to form more than one compound, the masses of one element that combine with a fixed mass of the other element, are in the ratio of small whole numbers.\n\nb. When gases combine they do so in simple ratio by mass, provided all gases are at same temperature and pressure.\n\nc. Equal volumes of all gases at the same temperature and pressure should contain the same no. of molecules.','b only','Law of  Multiple Proportions:\na. If two elements can combine to form more than one compound, the masses of one element that combine with a fixed mass of the other element, are in the ratio of small whole numbers.\n\nGay Lussac\'s Law\nb. When gases combine they do so in simple ratio by volume, provided all gases are at same temperature and pressure.\n\nAvogadro\'s Law\nc. Equal volumes of all gases at the same temperature and pressure should contain the same no. of molecules.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','a,c','b,c','NEW','11-015','11-015',NULL),
(11274,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement :-                                        \n\na)Solid have definite shape and volume.                                         \n\nb) Liquid have definite volume but not defined shape.                         \n\nc) Gas has both definite shape and volume','a only','(i) Solids have definite volume and definite shape.\n\n(ii) Liquids have definite volume but do not have definite shape. They take the shape of the container in which they are placed.\n\n(iii) Gases have neither definite volume nor definite shape. They completely occupy the space in the container in which they are placed.                                             REFERENCE: NCERT class 11-Page no 4- 8th paragraph','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','only b','b and c','a only','a,b and c','NEW','11-004','11-004',NULL),
(11275,'chemistry','Choose the wrong statements:                                               \n\na)Mass of a substance is the amount of matter present in it                        \n\nb)weight is the force exerted by gravity on an object.                   \n\nc)The weight of a substance is constant, whereas, its mass may vary from one place to another','only c','Mass of a substance is the amount of matter present in it, while weight is the force exerted by gravity on an object. The mass of a substance is constant, whereas, its weight may vary from one place to another due to change in gravity.                                   REFERENCE: NCERT class 11 - Page no 9- 1st paragraph','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a and b','only c','b and c','a,b and c','NEW','11-009','11-009',NULL),
(11276,'chemistry','Triple point of water is','0.01 °C or 279.16K or 32.01 ° F','0.01 °C or 279.16K (32.01 ° F).                \nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11 - Page no 8 - below the tabular column','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','0.01 °C','279.16K','0.01 °C or 279.16K','0.01 °C or 279.16K or 32.01 ° F','NEW','11-008','11-008',NULL),
(11277,'chemistry','Which of the following drugs is effective in cancer therapy ?  \n\na) cisplatin \n\nb) taxol \n\nc) Azidothymidine \n\nd) Cocaine','Both a & b','Some of these drugs are cisplatin and taxol, which are effective in cancer therapy. The drug AZT (Azidothymidine) is used for helping AIDS patients.           \nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11- Page no 4 - 3rd paragraph','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both a & b','Only d','Only c','a, b and c','NEW','11-004','11-004',NULL),
(11301,'chemistry','Two students performed the same experiment separately and each one of them recorded two readings of mass which are given below. Correct reading of mass is 3.0 g. On the basis of given data, mark the correct option out of the following statements.  Student A got readings of 2.01 & 1.99 . Student B got readings of 2.05 & 1.95.','(b) Results of student A are both precise and accurate.','For both the students, average value is close to the correct value. Hence, readings of both are accurate. But readings of student A are also close to each other (differ only by 0.02) and also close to the average value, hence readings of A are also precise. But readings of B are not close to each other (differ by 0.1) and hence are not precise. Thus, results of student A are both precise and accurate.       \nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11 exemplar - page no 1','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','(1)Results of both the students are neither accurate nor precise.','(b) Results of student A are both precise and accurate.','(c) Results of student B are neither precise nor accurate.','(d) Results of student B are both precise and accurate.','NEW','11-013','11-013',NULL),
(11302,'chemistry','A measured temperature on Fahrenheit scale is 100°F. What will this reading be on Celsius scale?','37.7°C','F = (9/5)°C + 32 = 100.  C = (100-32)5/9 = 37.7°C.             \nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11 exemplar - page no 1','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','37.7°C','33.4°C','30°C','40°C','NEW','11-010','11-010',NULL),
(11303,'chemistry','What will be the molarity of a solution, which contains 5.85 g of NaCl(s) per 1000 mL?','0.1 mol/L','N = ( No of moles of solute / Volume of solution in L) = (5.85/58.5) / 1 = 0.1 mol/L.     \nREFERANCE: NCERT class 11 Exemplar - page no 1','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','0.2 mol/L','0.01 mol/L','0.1 mol/L','0.02 mol/L','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11304,'chemistry','What is the mass per cent of carbon in carbon dioxide?','27.27%','Molecular mass of CO₂ =1×12 + 2×16 = 44 g\n1 g molecule of CO₂ contains 1 g atom of carbon\n44 g of CO₂ contains C = 12 g of carbon\n% of C in CO₂ = 12/44 x 100 =27.27%\nHence, the mass per cent of carbon in CO₂ is 27.27%.               \nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11 exemplar - page no 2','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','0.03%','27.27%','3.40%','28.70%','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(11305,'chemistry','If the density of a solution is 3.11 g m/L, the mass of 1.5 mL solution in significant figures is________','4.6 g','Density of solution = 3.11 g m/L\nVolume of solution =1.5 mL\nMass of solution = Volume x Density\n= 1.5 mL x 3.11 g m/L\n= 4.6 g (up to 2 significant figures)      \nREFERENCE: NCERT Examplar - Page no 3','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','4.662 g','4.66 g','4.6 g','0.46','NEW','11-013','11-013',NULL),
(11306,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about a compound is correct?\n\n(a) A molecule of a compound has atoms of different elements.\n\n(b) A compound cannot be separated into its constituent elements by physical methods of separation.\n\n(c) A compound retains the physical properties of its constituent elements.\n\n(d) The ratio of atoms of different elements in a compound is fixed.','a , b and d','A molecule of a compound has atoms of different elements.    \nA compound cannot be separated into its constituent elements by physical methods of separation.\nA compound does not retain the physical or chemical properties of its constituent elements.\nThe ratio of atoms of different elements in a compound is fixed.                 \nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11 exemplar - page no 3','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a , b and d','a and b','a,b,c and d','only c','NEW','11-005','11-005',NULL),
(11307,'chemistry','Which of the following statements indicates that law of multiple proportions are not being followed?\n\n(a) Sample of carbon dioxide taken from any source will always have carbon and oxygen in the ratio 1:2.\n\n(b) Carbon forms two oxides namely CO₂ and CO, where masses of oxygen which combine with fixed mass of carbon are in the simple ratio 2:1.\n\n(c) When magnesium bums in oxygen, the amount of magnesium taken for the reaction is equal to the amount of magnesium in magnesium oxide formed.\n\n(d) At constant temperature and pressure 200 mL of hydrogen will combine with 100 mL oxygen to produce 200 mL of water vapour.','a , c and d','The element, carbon, combines with oxygen to form two compounds, namely, carbon dioxide and carbon monoxide. In CO₂, 12 parts by mass of carbon combine with 32 parts by mass of oxygen while in CO, 12 parts by mass of carbon combine with 16 parts by mass of oxygen.\nTherefore, the masses of oxygen combine with a fixed mass of carbon (12 parts) in CO₂ and CO are 32 and 16 respectively. These masses of oxygen bear a simple ratio of 32 : 16 or 2 : 1 to each other.\nThis is an example of law of multiple proportion.    \nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11 exemplar - page no 4','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a , c and d','a , b and d','a , b and c','only c','NEW','11-015','11-015',NULL),
(11308,'chemistry','One of the statements of Dalton’s atomic theory is given below:\n“Compounds are formed when atoms of different elements combine in a fixed ratio”\nWhich of the following laws is related to this statement?\n\n(a) Law of conservation of mass\n\n(b) Law of definite proportions\n\n(c) Law of multiple proportions\n\n(d) Avogadro law.','b and c','(a, d) The statement is related to law of definite proportions and law of multiple proportions.                     \nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11 exemplar - page no 5','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a and d','a and b','b and c','c and d','NEW','11-016','11-016',NULL),
(11309,'chemistry','Which of the following statements indicates that law of multiple proportions is being followed?','Carbon forms two oxides namely CO₂ and CO, where masses of oxygen which combine with fixed mass of carbon are in the simple ratio 2:1.','The element, carbon, combines with oxygen to form two compounds, namely, carbon dioxide and carbon monoxide. In CO₂, 12 parts by mass of carbon combine with 32 parts by mass of oxygen while in CO, 12 parts by mass of carbon combine with 16 parts by mass of oxygen.\nTherefore, the masses of oxygen combine with a fixed mass of carbon (12 parts) in CO₂ and CO are 32 and 16 respectively. These masses of oxygen bear a simple ratio of 32 : 16 or 2 : 1 to each other.This is an example of law of multiple proportion.                           \nREFERENCE: NCERT exemplar 15Q','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Sample of carbon dioxide taken from any source will always have carbon and oxygen in the ratio 1:2.','Carbon forms two oxides namely CO₂ and CO, where masses of oxygen which combine with fixed mass of carbon are in the simple ratio 2:1.','When magnesium bums in oxygen, the amount of magnesium taken for the reaction is equal to the amount of magnesium in magnesium oxide formed.','At constant temperature and pressure 200 mL of hydrogen will combine with 100 mL oxygen to produce 200 mL of water vapour.','NEW','11-015','11-015',NULL),
(11310,'chemistry','One mole of oxygen gas at STP is equal to____ .\n\n(a) 6. 022 x 10²³ molecules of oxygen\n\n(b) 6.022 x 10²³ atoms of oxygen\n\n(c) 16 g of oxygen .\n\n(d) 32 g of oxygen','a,d','(a,d) 1 mole of Oxygen gas at STP = 6.022 x 10²³ molecules of Oxygen (Avogadro number) = 32 g of 0xygen. Hence, 1 mole of oxygen gas is equal to molecular weight of oxygen as well as Avogadro number.          \nREFERENCE: NCERT exemplar 16Q','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11311,'chemistry','One of the statements of Dalton’s atomic theory is given below:\n“Compounds are formed when atoms of different elements combine in a fixed ratio”\nWhich of the following laws in not related to this statement?\n\n(a) Law of conservation of mass\n\n(b) Law of definite proportions\n\n(c) Law of multiple proportions\n\n(d) Avogadro law','a,d','(a, d) The statement is related to law of definite proportions and law of multiple proportions.                   REFERENCE: NCERT exemplar 22Q','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','b,c','NEW','11-015','11-015',NULL),
(11312,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about a compound is incorrect?\n\n(a) A molecule of a compound has atoms of different elements.\n\n(b) A compound cannot be separated into its constituent elements by physical methods of separation.\n\n(c) A compound retains the physical properties of its constituent elements.\n\n(d) The ratio of atoms of different elements in a compound is fixed.','only c','A compound does not retain the physical or chemical properties of its constituent elements.                \n\n REFERENCE: NCERT examplar 12Q','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','only c','a,b & d','c & d','only a','NEW','11-005','11-005',NULL),
(11313,'chemistry','If the density of a solution is 3.12 g m/L, the mass of 1.5 mL solution in significant figures is________','4.7 g','density of solution = 3.12 g m/l\nvolume of solution =1.5 ml\nmass of solution = volume x density\n= 1.5 ml x 3.12 g m/l\n= 4.68 g = 4.7 g (up to 2 significant figures)                \n\nREFERENCE: NCERT examplar 11Q','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','4.7 g','4680 x 10³ g','4.680 g','46.80 g','NEW','11-013','11-013',NULL),
(11314,'chemistry','What will be the molarity of a solution, which contains 58.5 g of NaCl(s) per 500 mL?','2 mol /L','N = No of moles of solute/ Volume of solution in L = (58.5/58.5)/0.5 = 2 mol/L.                  REFERANCE: NCERT examplar 3Q','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','4 mol /L','20 mol / L','0.2 mol /L','2 mol /L','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11315,'chemistry','If 1000 mL of a 5 M solution is diluted to 1500 mL, what will be the molarity of the solution obtained?','3.33 M','M₁V₁ = M₂V₂ , M₂ = 3.33M            REFERANCE: NCERT examplar 4Q','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','1.5 M','1.32 M','0.017 M','3.33 M','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11316,'chemistry','What will be the molality of the solution containing 18.25 g of HCl gas in 1000 g of water?','0.5 m','Molality = No of moles of solute / Mass of solvent in Kg = 0.5 m.                REFERENCE examplar 7Q','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','0.5 m','0.1 m','1 M','0.1 M','NEW','11-024','11-024',NULL),
(11317,'chemistry','Which of the following terms are unitless?\n\n(a) Molality\n\n(b) Molarity\n\n(c) Mole fraction\n\n(d) Mass per cent','c,d','Mole fraction & Mass per cent are unitless.                                   \nREFERENCE: NCERT examplar 21Q','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11318,'chemistry','If the concentration of glucose (C₆H₁₂0₆) in blood is 0.8 g/L, what will be the molarity of glucose in blood?','0.0044 M','Molarity= Conc. in g/L / Molar mass = 0.80/180 = 0.0044M.              REFERENCE: NCERT examplar 6Q','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','0.0044 M','0.5 M','5M','50M','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11319,'chemistry','Assertion : One atomic mass unit is defined as one twelfth of the mass of one carbon – 12 atom.\n\nReason : Carbon-12 isotope is the most abundant isotope of carbon and has been chosen as standard.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','One atomic mass unit is defined as one-twelfth of the mass of one carbon - 12 atom because C-12 is chosen as the standard atom. This is because it has the equal number of protons and neutrons (6) and constitutes most of the matter.The most abundant isotope of carbon is Carbon - 12.\n\nThus both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Assertion is incorrect, reason is correct.','NEW','11-016','11-016',NULL),
(11320,'chemistry','Assertion : Significant figures for 0.200 is 3 whereas for 200 it is 1.\n\nReason : Zero at the end or right of a number are significant provided they are not on the right side of the decimal point.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Zero at the end or right of number are significant provided they are on the right side of the decimal point, For example 0.200 g has three significant figures.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Assertion is incorrect, reason is correct.','NEW','11-012','11-012',NULL),
(11321,'chemistry','Assertion : The empirical mass of ethene is half of its molecular mass.\n\nReason : The empirical formula represents the simplest whole number ratio of various atoms present in a compound.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.\n\nBoth Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion\n\nThe molecular formula of ethene is C₂H₄ and its empirical formula CH₂\n\nThus, Molecular formula = Empirical formula ×2','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Assertion is incorrect, reason is correct.','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(11322,'chemistry','Assertion: The number of O atoms in 16 g of oxygen and 16 g of ozone is same. \n\nReason: Each of the species represent 1 g-atom of oxygen.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Assertion is incorrect, reason is correct.','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11323,'chemistry','Assertion (A): The empirical mass of ethene is half of its molecular mass.                                                                                  \n\nReason (R): The empirical formula represents the simplest whole number ratio of various atoms present in a compound.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Empirical formula of ethene = CH₂\nEmpirical formula mass of ethene =14 amu\n\n= 1/2 — x molecular mass of ethene .\n\nEmpirical formula shows that ethene has (C : H)=1 : 2','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(11324,'chemistry','The number of atoms present in one mole of an element is equal to Avogadro number. Which of the following element contains the greatest number of atoms?','12 g He','For comparing number of atoms, first we calculate the moles as all are monoatomic\nand hence, moles  = NA number of atoms.\n\n Mole of 4g He = 4/4 = 1 mol\n\n46g Na = 46/23 = 2 mol\n\n0.40 g Ca = 0.40/40 = 0.1 mol\n\n12 g He = 12/4 = 3 mol\n\nREFERENCE: NCERT Examplar XI - 5th question','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','12 g He','4 g He','46 g Na','0.40 g Ca','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11325,'chemistry','If the concentration of glucose (C₆ H₁₂O₆ )  in blood is 0.9 g/L , what will be the molarity of glucose in blood?','0.005 M','In the given question, 0.9 g/L means that 1000 mL (or 1L) solution contains 0.9 g of\n glucose\n\nNumber of moles = 0 9. g glucose =  0.9/180 mol glucose\n= 5 × 10⁻³ mol  glucose\n(where, molecular mass of glucose C₆ H₁₂ O₆ = 12×6 + 12×1 + 6 × 16 = 180 u)\ni.e., 1L solution contains 0.05 mole glucose or the molarity of glucose is 0.005 M.\n\nREFERENCE: NCERT Examplar XI - 6th question','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','5 M','50 M','0.5 M','0.005 M','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11326,'chemistry','What will be the molality of the solution containing 18.25 g of HCl gas in 500 g of water?','1 m','Molality is defined as the number of moles of solute present in 1 kg of solvent. It is denoted by m.\n\nMolality (m) = Moles of solute/Mass of solvent (in kg)\nGiven that, Mass of solvent (H₂O) = 500 g = 0 5. kg\nWeight of HCl =18.25 g\nMolecular weight of HCl = 36.5\n\nMoles of HCL = 18.25/ 36.5 = 0.5\nm = 0.5/0.5 = 1m','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','1 m','0.1 M','0.5 M','0.5 m','NEW','11-024','11-024',NULL),
(11327,'chemistry','One mole of any substance contains 6.022 × 10²³ atoms/molecules.Number of molecules of H₂SO₄ present in 100 mL of 0.02M H₂SO₄ solution is ......... .','12.044 × 10²⁰molecules','One mole of any substance contains 6.022 × 10²³  atoms/molecules.\nHence, Number of millimoles of H₂SO₄ =  molarity volume in mL\n=  0.02 × 100 =  2  millimoles\n= 2×10⁻³ mol\nNumber of molecules = number of moles × NA\n       =2×10⁻³ mol × 6.022 × 10²³  =  12.044 × 10²⁰molecules\n\nREFERENCE: NCERT EXAMPLAR XI- 8th question','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','6.022 × 10²³ molecules','12.044 × 10²⁰molecules','12.044 × 10²molecules','6.022 × 10²⁰molecules','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11328,'chemistry','The empirical formula and molecular mass of a compound are CH₂O and 180 g respectively. What will be the molecular formula of the compound?','C₆ H₁₂O₆','Empirical formula mass = CH₂O = 12×1 + 2×1 + 16× 1 = 30\n\nMolecular mass = 180\n\nn\n =(Molecular mass\n/Empirical formula mass) = 180/3 = 6\n\nMolecular formula = n× empirical formula = 6 ×CH₂O =C₆ H₁₂O₆\n\nREFERENCE: NCERT Examplar XI - 9th question','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','C₉H₁₂O₉','CH₂O','C₆ H₁₂O₆','C₂H₄O₂','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(11329,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct about the reaction given below?\n\n4Fe(s) + 3O₂(g) → 2Fe₂O₃','Total mass of iron and oxygen in reactants = total mass of iron and oxygen in\n product therefore it follows law of conservation of mass','According to the law of conservation of mass,\nTotal mass of reactants = Total mass of products\nAmount of Fe₂O₃ is decided by limiting reagent.\n\nREFERENCE: NCERT Examplar XI - 13th questions','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Total mass of iron and oxygen in reactants = total mass of iron and oxygen in\n product therefore it follows law of conservation of mass','Total mass of reactants = total mass of product, therefore, law of multiple\n proportions is followed','Amount of Fe₂O₃ can be increased by taking any one of the reactants (iron or\n oxygen) in excess','Amount of Fe₂O₃ produced will decrease if the amount of any one of the\n reactants (iron or oxygen) is taken in excess','NEW','11-014','11-014',NULL),
(11330,'chemistry','Which of the following reactions is not correct according to the law of  conservation of mass?','C₃H₈ (s) + O₂(g) → CO₂ (g) +H₂O(g)','The law of conservation of mass states that in a chemical reaction, the total mass of the reactants must be equal to the total mass of the products. Therefore, a chemical reaction that violates this law would be incorrect.\n\nAmong the given reactions, the reaction that is not correct according to the law of conservation of mass is:\n\nC₃H₈ (s) + O₂(g) → CO₂ (g) + H₂O(g)\n\nTo balance this equation, we need 5 molecules of O₂ on the reactant side, but only 4 molecules of O₂ are present on the product side. Therefore, the total mass of the reactants is not equal to the total mass of the products, and this reaction violates the law of conservation of mass.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','2Mg(s) + O₂(g) → 2MgO(s)','C₃H₈ (s) + O₂(g) → CO₂ (g) +H₂O(g)','P₄ (s) +  5O₂(g) → P₄O₁₀(s)','CH₄(g) +  2O₂(g) → CO₂ (g) + H₂O(g)','NEW','11-014','11-014',NULL),
(11331,'chemistry','One mole of oxygen gas at STP is equal to………\n\n(a) 6.022 × 10²³\nmolecules of oxygen\n\n(b) 6.022 × 10²³\natoms of oxygen\n\n(c) 16 g of oxygen\n\n(d) 32 g of oxygen','a,d','1 mole of O₂\n gas at STP =  6.022 × 10²³\nmolecules of O₂ (Avogadro number) = 32 g of O₂\n .Hence, 1 mole of oxygen gas is equal to molecular weight of oxygen as well as Avogadro\n number.\n\nREFERENCE: NCERT Examplar XI - 16th questions','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','b,d','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11332,'chemistry','Sulphuric acid reacts with sodium hydroxide as follows:\n\nH₂SO₄ + 2NaOH →Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O\nWhen 1 L of 0.1 M sulphuric acid solution is allowed to react with 1 L of 0.1 M sodium hydroxide solution, the amount of sodium sulphate formed and its molarity in the solution obtained is','7.1g, 0.025 mol/L','Moles of H₂SO₄ taken = 0.1 moles\nMoles of NaOH taken = 0.1 moles\n\nAs H₂SO₄ and NaOH react in ratio 1:2, so 0.1 moles of H₂SO₄ reacts with  0.2 mole of NaOH which we don’t have.\n\n0.1 mole of NaOH reacts with 0.05 mole of H₂SO₄, so NaOH is Limiting reactant. Product is calculated w.r.t limiting reactant so Number of moles of Na₂SO₄ formed will also be equal to 0.05.\nMass of Na₂SO₄ = 0.05 x 142 = 7.1 g\n Molarity of Na₂SO₄ = No of moles / Volume in litre = 0.05 / 2 = 0.025 mol/L\n\nREFERENCE: NCERT Examplar XI - 17th questions','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','7.1g, 0.25mol/L','7.1g, 0.025 mol/L','8g, 0.0025mol/L','7.8g, 1.25mol/L','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11333,'chemistry','Which of the following pairs have the same number of atoms?\n\n(a) 16g of O₂(g) and 4g of H₂(g) \n\n(b) 16g of O₂(g) and 44g of CO₂\n\n(c) 28 g of N₂ and 32 g of O₂ \n\n(d) 12g of C(s) and 23g of Na(s)','c,d','(c) No of atom of Nitrogen= (Mass / Atomic weight) × 6.023 × 10²³ = (28/14)×6.023 × 10²³ = 2 × 6.023 × 10²³\n\nNo of atom of Oxygen= (Mass / Atomic weight) × 6.023 × 10²³ = (32/16) × 6.023 × 10²³  = 2 ×6.023 × 10²³ \n\n(d) No of atom of Carbon= (Mass / Atomic weight) × 6.023 × 10²³ = (12/12) × 6.023 × 10²³ = 6.023 × 10²³\n\nNo of atom of Sodium = (Mass / Atomic weight) × 6.023 × 10²³  = (23/23)×6.023 × 10²³ = 6.023 × 10²³\n\nREFERENCE: NCERT Examplar XI - 18th questions','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11334,'chemistry','Which of the following solutions have the same concentration?\n\n(a) 20 g of NaOH in 200 mL of solution\n\n(b) 0.5 mol of KC1 in 200 mL of solution\n\n(c) 40 g of NaOH in 100 mL of solution\n\n(d) 20 g of KOH in 200 mL of solution','a,b','Molarity = w × 1000 / M × V(mL) \n\n(a) Molarity = 20 × 1000 / 40 × 200 = 2.5 M\n(b) Molarity = 0.5 × 1000 / 200 =  2.5 M\n(c) Molarity = 40 × 1000 / 40 × 100 = 10 M \n(d) Molarity = 20 × 100 / 56 × 200 = 1.785 M\n\nREFERENCE: NCERT Examplar XI - 19th questions','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','b,c','a,d','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11335,'chemistry','16 g of oxygen has the same number of molecules as in \n\n(a) 16 g of CO\n\n(b) 28 g of N₂  \n      \n(c) 14g of N₂\n\n(d) 1.0g of H₂','c,d','No of molecules of O₂ in 16g of O₂ = (16/32)×6.023 × 10²³ = 0.5 ×  6.023 × 10²³\n\n0.5 moles is present in 14g of N₂ and 1g of H₂ , hence this sample will also have (0.5 ×  6.023 × 10²³) molecules.\n\n\nNo of molecules of N₂ = ( w/ mol wt) × 6.023 × 10²³ = (14/28) × 6.023 × 10²³ = 0.5 × 6.023 × 10²³\n\nNo of molecules of H₂ = (1/2) 6.023 × 10²³ = 0.5 ×6.023 × 10²³. \n\nREFERENCE: NCERT Examplar XI - 20th questions','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11336,'chemistry','Which of the following terms are unitless?\n\n(a) Molality\n\n(b) Molarity\n\n(c) Mole fraction\n\n(d) Mass per cent','c,d','Both mole fraction and mass per cent are unitless as both are ratios of moles and mass respectively.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11501,'chemistry','Calculate the mass of sodium acetate (CH₃COONa) required to make 1000 mL of 0.375 molar aqueous solution. Molar mass of sodium acetate is 82.0245 g /mol.','30.75g','Molar mass of sodium acetate is 82.0245 g/mol.  Mass of sodium acetate (CH₃COONa) required to make 1000 mL of 0.375 molar aqueous solution= 30.75g.                \nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11 - page no 25 - 5th question','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Numerical Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','30.75g','15.38g','1.53 g','3.07 g','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11502,'chemistry','The number of molecules in 8.5 g of NH₃ is approximately','3 × 10²³','Moles of NH₃ = 8.5/17 = 0.5mole ,   Number of molecule = 0.5 × 6.022 × 10²³ molecule = 3 × 10²³ molecule.                 REFERENCE:NCERT class 11 - page no 18','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Numerical Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','3 × 10²³','1.5 × 10²²','1.5 × 10²³','3× 10²²','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11503,'chemistry','6.025 × 10²⁰ molecules of acetic acid are present in 1000ml of its solution. The concentration of solution is','0.001 M','Moles of oxalic acid = 6.025 × 10²⁰/6.022 × 10²³ = 10⁻³. Molarity= (10⁻³ × 1000)/1000 = 0.001M.              REFERANCE: NCERT class 11- page no 23','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Numerical Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','0.002','20.4','22.4','0.001 M','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11504,'chemistry','Ammonia gas is passed into water, yielding a solution of density 0.95 g/cm³ and containing 18.6% NH₃ by\n weight. The mass of NH₃ per cc of the solution is','0.176 g','Molarity of NH₃ solution = (18.6×0.95×10)/17 = 10.4.        \nStrength (g/L) = molarity × mol. mass\n= 10.4 × 17 = 176.7 g/L.\n\n1 mL or 1 cm³ of solution contain NH₃ = 176.6/1000 = 0.176g     \nREFERANCE: NCERT class 11- page no 23','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Numerical Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','0.176 g','0.017','1.76','17.6','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11505,'chemistry','Mole fraction of solute in aqueous solution of 30% NaOH.','0.16','30% NaOH means 30% by mass i.e.,(30 g of NaOH /100 g of solution)\nWeight of NaOH = 30 g\nMol. mass NaOH = 40\nWeight of H₂O = 100 – 30 = 70\nMol. mass = 18.                     \nMol. fraction of NaOH =( Moles of NaOH /Moles of H₂O+Moles of NaOH) = (30/40)/ (70/18+30/40) = 0.16.                                                 REFERENCE: NCERT class 11 - page no 23','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Numerical Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.16','0.25','0.95','0.32','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11506,'chemistry','Concentrated aqueous sulphuric acid is 49% H₂SO₄ (w/v) and has a density of 1.80 gm/L. Molarity of solution','5M','M = (w/v% × 10)/Mol mass = 49×10/98 = 5M.             \nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11 - Page no : 23','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Numerical Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','5M','10M','1.8M','1.0M','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11507,'chemistry','An element, X has the following isotopic composition ⁵⁶X : 90%, ⁵⁷X : 2% ,⁵⁹X : 8%. The weighted average\natomic mass of the naturally occurring element X is closest to','52.7amu','∑ % × Atomic mass / 100 = (56×90)+(57×2)+(59×2) / 100 = 52.7 amu                   REFERENCE: NCERT class 11 - NCERT pg no - 19','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Numerical Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','52.7amu','56.14 amu','60 amu','55 amu','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(11508,'chemistry','The number of atoms in 0.1 mol of a diatomic gas is','0.2Nₐ','As diatomic gas means 3 atoms are present in a molecule\nNumber of atoms = 0.1 × 2 × Nₐ = 0.2Nₐ.                        REFERENCE : NCERT class 11 - page no - 15','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Numerical Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','0Nₐ','0.1Nₐ','Nₐ','0.2Nₐ','NEW','11-015','11-015',NULL),
(11509,'chemistry','How many g of dibasic acid (mol. weight 200) should be present in 100 ml. of the aqueous solution to give\nstrength of 0.1 N?','1g','Amount of acid (in gram) = N×E×Vₘₗ / 1000 , \n\nHere E = 200/2=100.\n\nAmount of acid =(0.1×100×100) / 1000 = 1g. \n\nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11 - page no - 23','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Numerical Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1g','10g','2g','20g','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11510,'chemistry','The nature of the solution obtained on mixing\n0.2 M, 100 mL HNO₃ with 0.1 M, 100 mL KOH is :','Acidic','M₁V₁ - M₂V₂ = M₃V₃.                    \n\n=(0.2×100) – (0.1×100) = M3×(200).\n=M₃ = 0.05 ( Acidic in nature).\nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11 - page no-23','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Numerical Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Basic','Can\'t be predicted.','Neutral','Acidic','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11511,'chemistry','10 g of A is mixed with 15 g of B. Find the mass %  of A in the binary solution :','40%','Mass % of A = (Mass of A / Total mass ) × 100 = (10/25)×100 = 40%.                         REFERENCE: NCERT class 11 - page no- 23','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Numerical Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','40%','20%','10%','67%','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11512,'chemistry','What amount of CO₂ will be produced by thermal decomposition of 300 g CaCO₃ :','132g','CaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g)\n\nFrom this equation, we can see that one mole of CaCO₃ produces one mole of CO₂. The molar mass of CaCO₃ is 100.1 g/mol, which means that 300 g of CaCO₃ contains:\n\nn(CaCO₃) = m/M = 300 g / 100.1 g/mol = 2.998 mol\n\nTherefore, the amount of CO₂ produced by the thermal decomposition of 300 g of CaCO₃ is:\n\nn(CO₂) = n(CaCO₃) = 2.998 mol\n\nThe molar mass of CO₂ is 44.01 g/mol, so the mass of CO₂ produced is:\n\nm(CO₂) = n(CO₂) * M(CO₂) = 2.998 mol * 44.01 g/mol = 132.0 g','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Numerical Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','132g','44g','150g','3g','NEW','11-022','11-022',NULL),
(11513,'chemistry','What will be the minimum molecular mass of an enzyme having 5.6 % of nitrogen in it :','250','N% = Mass of N × 100/ Total mass = 1×14×100/x                   5.6= 1×14×100/x.                        x= 250.                        REFERENCE:  NCERT class 11 - page no 18','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Numerical Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','25','250','2500','2.5','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11514,'chemistry','How much amount of HCl should be added in 500 mL of water in order to make 0.5 M solution :','9.125 g','M =n(b) × 1000/Vₛₒₗ(ml) = W(b) × 1000/(M(b) ×V )       \n\n0.5 = W(b) ×1000/ 36.5×500              \n\nW(b) = 9.125g.              \nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11 - page no 23','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Numerical Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','9.125 g','36.5 g','18.25 g','46.75 g','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11515,'chemistry','If AlCl₃  solution is 0.5 M then what will be the concentration of Cl⁻ ions in the solution :','1.5M','AlCl₃(s) → Al³⁺(aq) + 3 Cl⁻(aq)\n\n[Cl⁻] = 3 × [AlCl₃] = 3 × 0.5 M = 1.5 M\n\nSo the concentration of Cl⁻ ions in the solution is 1.5 M.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Numerical Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','0.5M','1.5M','3M','1M','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11516,'chemistry','What amount of carbon will be required to form 8.8 g of CO₂ after complete combustion :','2.4 g','The number of moles of CO₂ formed by the combustion of 8.8 g of CO₂ is:\n\nn(CO₂) = m/M = 8.8 g / 44 g/mol = 0.2 mol\n\nSince one mole of carbon reacts with one mole of oxygen to form one mole of carbon dioxide, the number of moles of carbon required to produce 0.2 mol of CO₂ is also 0.2 mol.\n\nThe molar mass of carbon is 12 g/mol, so the mass of carbon required is:\n\nm(C) = n(C)×M(C) = 0.2 mol × 12 g/mol = 2.4 g\n\nTherefore, the amount of carbon required to form 8.8 g of CO₂ after complete combustion is 2.4 g.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Numerical Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2.4 g','24 g','1.2 g','12 g','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11517,'chemistry','The number of molecules in 28 amu of N₂ :','1','28 amu means 2 N atoms\ni.e., 1 molecule of N₂.                REFERENCE: NCERT class 11 - Page no 17','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Numerical Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Nₐ','1','2','2Nₐ','NEW','11-017','11-017',NULL),
(11518,'chemistry','Find the molality of the solution if 4 g of NaOH is present in 44 g of the solution :','2.5 m','m = 0.1×1000/4 = 2.5m.          REFERENCE: NCERT class 11 - page no 24','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Numerical Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','90.9 m','25 m','0.25 m','2.5 m','NEW','11-024','11-024',NULL),
(11601,'chemistry','In a compound C, H and N atoms are present in  9 : 1 : 3.5 by weight. Molecular weight of compound is 108. Molecular formula of compound is [ JEE MAIN 2002 ]','C₆H₈N₂','For C , Percentage simple ratio is 3\nFor H , percentage simple ratio is 4\nFor N , percentage simple ratio is 1 \nEmpirical formula is C₃H₄N\n(C₃H₄N)ₙ = 108 \n(12×3 + 4×1 + 14)ₙ = 108 \nn = 108/54 = 2 \nMolecular formula = C₆H₈N₂','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','C₆H₈N₂','C₃H₄N','C₉H₁₂N₃','C₂H₆N₂','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(11602,'chemistry','Number of atoms in 558.5 gram Fe (at. wt. of Fe = 55.85 g/mol) is [JEE MAIN 2002]','Twice that in 60 g carbon','Fe (no. of moles) = \n558.5/55.85\n= 10 moles\nC (no. of moles) in 60 g of C = 60/12 = 5 moles.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Twice that in 60 g carbon','6.023 × 10²²','Half that in 8 g He','558.5 × 6.023 × 10²³','NEW','11-020','11-020',NULL),
(11603,'chemistry','What volume of hydrogen gas, at 273 K and 1 atm. pressure will be consumed in obtaining 21.6g of elemental boron (atomic mass = 10.8) from the reduction of boron trichloride by hydrogen?','67.2 L','2BCl + 3H → 2B + 6HCl\nNow, since 10.8 gm boron requires hydrogen  = (3/2) 22.4L at NTP \nNow, since 10.8 gm boron requires hydrogen = (3/2) × (22.4 × 21.6/10.8) = 67.2 L at NTP','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','67.2 L','44.8 L','22.4 L','89.6 L','NEW','11-021','11-021',NULL),
(11604,'chemistry','The ammonia evolved from the treatment of 0.30 g of an organic compound for the estimation of  nitrogen was passed in 100 mL of 0.1 M  sulphuric acid. The excess of acid required 20  mL of 0.5 M sodium hydroxide solution for  complete neutralization. The organic compound  is [ JEE MAIN 2004 ]','Urea','H₂SO₄ is dibasic.\n\n0.1 MH₂SO₄= 0.2 [M =2N]\nMeq of H₂SO₄ taken =100×0.2 = 20\n\nMeq of H₂SO₄ neutralised by\n NaOH = 20× 0.5 =10\n\nMeq of H₂SO₄ neutralised by\n NH₃ = 20 – 10 = 10 % of \n\nN₂ = ( 1.4 × Meq of acid neutrialised by NH₃ )/wt. of organic compound = (1.4×10)/0.3 = 46.6 % of nitrogen in urea = (14×2 × 100)/60 = 46.6\n\nSimilarly % of Nitrogen in Benzamide = 11.5% [C₆H₅CONH₂ = 12]\nAcctamide = 23.4% [CH₃CONH₂=59]Thiourea = 36.8% [NH₂CSNH₂ = 76]\n\nHence the compound must be urea.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'3','mcq','Urea','Benzamide','Acetamide','Thiourea','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11605,'chemistry','How many moles of magnesium phosphate, Mg₃(PO₄)₂ will contain 0.25 mole of oxygen atoms? [ JEE MAINS 2006 ]','3.125 × 10⁻² moles','1 Mole of Mg₃(PO₄)₂ contains 8 mole of oxygen atoms = 8 mole of oxygen atoms = 1 mole of Mg₃(PO₄)₂ mole of Mg₃(PO₄)₂\n\n0.25 mole of oxygen atom = (1/8) × 0.25 of Mg₃(PO₄)₂ = 3.125 × 10⁻² moles of  Mg₃(PO₄)₂','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','3.125 × 10⁻² moles','2.5 × 10⁻² moles','0.025 mole','1.25 × 10⁻² moles','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11606,'chemistry','The density (in g m/L) of a 3.60 M sulphuric  acid solution that is 29% H₂SO₄ (molar mass = 98 g /mol) by mass will be [ JEE MAINS 2007 ]','1.22','Since molarity of solution is 3.60 M. It means 3.6 moles of H₂SO₄ is present in its 1 litre solution.\n Mass of 3.6 moles of H₂SO₄ = Moles × Molecular mass = 3.6 × 98 g = 352.8 g\n\n= 1000 ml solution has 352.8 g of H₂SO₄\n\nGiven that 29 g of H₂SO₄ is present in 100 g of solution\n= 352.8 g of H₂SO₄ is present in = (100/29) 352.8 = 1216 g of solution\n \nDensity= Mass / volume = 1216/1000 = 1.216 g/ml = 1.22 ml','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','1.45','1.64','1.88','1.22','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11607,'chemistry','In the reaction, \n2Al(s) + 6HCl(aq) → 2Al³⁺(aq)+6Cl⁻(aq) + 3H₂(g) [ JEE MAINS 2007 ]','11.2 L H₂\n(g) at STP is produced for every\n mole HCl(aq) consumed','2Al(s) + 6HCl(aq) → 2Al³⁺(aq)+6Cl⁻(aq) + 3H₂(g)\n\n6 moles of HCl produces = 3 moles of H₂ = 3 × 22.4 L of H₂ at S.T.P\n1 mole of HCl produces = (3 × 22.4/ 6)L of H₂ at STP  = 11.2 L of H₂ at STP','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','11.2 L H₂\n(g) at STP is produced for every\n mole HCl(aq) consumed','6 L HCl(aq) is consumed for every 3 L H₂\n (g)\nproduced','33.6 L H₂\n (g) is produced regardless of\n temperature and pressure for every mole\n Al that reacts','67.2 H₂\n(g) at STP is produced for every mole Al that reacts.','NEW','11-021','11-021',NULL),
(11608,'chemistry','The molality of a urea solution in which 0.0100 g of urea, [(NH₂)₂CO] is added to 0.3000 dm³ of  water at STP is : [ JEE MAINS 2011]','5.55 × 10⁻⁴ m','Molality = Moles of solute / Mass of\n solvent in kg = (0.01/60)/0.3 =  5.55 × 10⁻⁴ m','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','5.55 × 10⁻⁴ m','33.3 m','3.33 × 10⁻² m','0.555 m','NEW','11-024','11-024',NULL),
(11609,'chemistry','A gaseous hydrocarbon gives upon combustion  0.72 g of water and 3.08 g. of CO₂. The empirical formula of the hydrocarbon is : [ JEE MAINS 2013 ]','C₇H₈','moles of water = 0.72 g / 18.015 g/mol\nmoles of water = 0.04 mol\n\nmoles of carbon dioxide = 3.08 g / 44.01 g/mol\nmoles of carbon dioxide = 0.07 mol\n\nSince the empirical formula of the hydrocarbon should have the smallest possible whole number ratio of atoms, we can divide the number of moles of carbon dioxide by 0.07 mol to get the mole ratio of carbon to hydrogen:\n\n0.07 mol CO₂ / 0.07 mol = 1 mol carbon\n0.04 mol H₂O / 0.07 mol = 0.57 mol hydrogen\n\n1 mol carbon / 1 mol hydrogen = 1.75 mol carbon / 1 mol hydrogen\n\nSince the ratio of carbon to hydrogen must be a whole number, we can multiply both sides by 4 to get:\n\n7 mol carbon / 4 mol hydrogen = 1.75 mol carbon / 1 mol hydrogen\n\nTherefore, the empirical formula of the hydrocarbon is C7H8.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','C₂H₄','C₃H₄','C₆H₅','C₇H₈','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(11610,'chemistry','The most abundant elements by mass in the body of a healthy human adult are :\n\nOxygen (61.4%) ; Carbon (22.9%), Hydrogen (10.0%) ; and Nitrogen (2.6%). The weight which a 75 kg person would gain if all 1H atoms are replaced by 2H atoms is [JEE M 2017]','7.5 kg','Percentage (by mass) of elements given in the body of a healthy human adult is :-\nOxygen = 61.4%, Carbon = 22.9%,\nHydrogen = 10.0% and Nitrogen = 2.6%\n\nTotal weight of person = 75 kg\n→Mass due to 1H is =  75 ×( 10/100) = 7.5 kg\n\nIf ¹H atoms are replaced by ²H atoms.\n\nMass gain by person would be = 7.5 kg','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','15 kg','37.5 kg','7.5 kg','10 kg','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11611,'chemistry','1 gram of a carbonate (M₂CO₃) on treatment with excess HCl produces 0.01186 mole of CO₂. The molar mass of M₂CO₃ in g/mol is :','84.3','M₂CO₃ + 2HCl → 2MCl + H₂O + CO₂\n\nFrom the equation, we can see that 1 mole of M₂CO₃ produces 1 mole of CO₂. Therefore, 0.01186 moles of CO₂ produced by the reaction corresponds to 0.01186 moles of M₂CO₃ consumed.\n\nTo find the molar mass of M₂CO₃, we need to know its mass. We know that 1 gram of M₂CO₃ contains 0.01186/2 = 0.00593 moles of M₂CO₃.\n\nThe molar mass (M) of M₂CO₃ can be calculated using the formula:\n\nM = m/n\n\nwhere m is the mass of M₂CO₃ in grams, and n is the number of moles of M₂CO₃.\n\nM = 1 g / 0.00593 mol = 168.4 g/mol\n\nHowever, M₂CO₃ has two M atoms in its formula, so we need to divide this result by 2 to get the molar mass of one M atom.\n\nMolar mass of one M atom = 168.4 g/mol / 2 = 84.2 g/mol','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','1186','84.3','118.6','11.86','NEW','11-021','11-021',NULL),
(11612,'chemistry','To neutralise completely 20 mL of 0.1 M aqueous solution of phosphorous acid (H₃PO₃), the  value of 0.1 M aqueous KOH solution required  is [JEE 2004]','40mL','N₁V₁ = N₂V₂ \n(Note : H₃PO₃ is dibasic, M = 2N)\n20×0.2 = 0.1×V (Thus. 0.1 M = 0.2 N)\n  Then,  V = 40 ml','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','10mL','20mL','40mL','60mL','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11613,'chemistry','With increase of temperature, which of these changes? [JEE 2002]','Molarity','Among all the given options molarity is correct because the term molarity involve\nvolume which increases on increasing\ntemperature.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Molality','Weight fraction of solute','Molarity','Mole fraction','NEW','11-024','11-024',NULL),
(11614,'chemistry','25ml of a solution of barium hydroxide on titration with a 0.1 molar solution of hydrochloric acid gave a litre value of 35ml. The molarity of barium hydroxide solution was [JEE 2003]','0.07M','25 × N = 0.1 × 35 ; N = 0.14\nBa(OH)₂ is diacid base\nhence N = M × 2 or M = N/2.   Then, M = 0.07 M','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','0.07M','0.14M','0.28M','0.7M','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11615,'chemistry','Density of a 2.05M solution of acetic acid in water is 1.02 g/mL. The molality of the solution is [JEE 2006]','2.28 mol /kg','Apply the formula d = M ( 1/m + M₂/1000)                               \n1.02= 2.05(1/m+60/1000).                            \nOn solving we get, m = 2.288 mol/kg','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','2.28 mol /kg','1.14 mol /kg','0.44 mol /kg','3.28 mol /kg','NEW','11-024','11-024',NULL),
(11616,'chemistry','6.02 × 10²⁰ molecules of urea are present in 100 ml of its solution. The concentration of urea solution is [JEE 2004] (Avogadro constant, Nₐ = 6.02 × 10²³/ mol )','0.01 M','Moles of urea present in 100 ml of sol.= 6.02 × 10²⁰/6.02× 10²³                              \nM=(6.02×10²⁰ × 1000)/(6.02 × 10²³×100) = 0.01M [M = Moles of solute present in 1L of\nsolution]]','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','0.02 M','0.01 M','0.001 M','0.1 M','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11617,'chemistry','Two solutions of a substance (non electrolyte) are mixed in the following manner. 480 ml of 1.5 M first solution + 520 ml of 1.2 M second solution. What is the molarity of the final mixture ? [ JEE 2005 ]','1.344 M','From the molarity equation. M₁V₁ + M₂V₂ = MV                              \nLet M be the molarity of final mixture,.                                         \nM = (M₁V₁ + M₂V₂)/V.                         \nM = 480×1.5 + 520×1.2 / 480+520 = 1.344M','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','1.344 M','1.20 M','2.70 M','1.50 M','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11618,'chemistry','At 300 K and 1 atm, 15 mL of a gaseous hydrocarbon requires 375 mL air containing 20% O₂ by volume for complete combustion. After combustion the gases occupy 330 mL. Assuming that the water formed is in liquid form and the volumes were measured at the same temperature and pressure, the formula of the hydrocarbon is: [JEE M 2016]','C₃H₈','CₓHₓ + (x + (3.76x/4.76)) O₂ → x CO₂ + (x/2) H₂O + (3.76x/4.76) N₂\n\nwhere (x + (3.76x/4.76)) is the amount of oxygen required for complete combustion, assuming air contains 20% oxygen by volume. \nTo solve this problem, we need to use the balanced chemical equation for the combustion of a hydrocarbon:\n\nCₓHₓ + (x + (3.76x/4.76)) O₂ → x CO₂ + (x/2) H₂O + (3.76x/4.76) N₂\n\nwhere (x + (3.76x/4.76)) is the amount of oxygen required for complete combustion, assuming air contains 20% oxygen by volume.\n\nLet\'s calculate the number of moles of air and hydrocarbon used in the combustion reaction:\n\nNumber of moles of air = 375 mL × 20/100 × 1/22.4 L/mol × 1 atm/1 L × 300 K/273 K = 0.6 mol\nNumber of moles of hydrocarbon = 15 mL × 1/1000 L/mL × 1/x L/mol = 0.015/x mol\n\nTotal number of moles of gases = 330 mL × 1/1000 L/mL × 1 atm/1 L × 300 K/273 K = 0.362 mol\n0.362 = x CO₂ + (x/2) H₂O + (3.76x/4.76) N₂\n\nSubstituting the values for the number of moles of air and hydrocarbon, we get:\n\n0.362 = 15/x CO₂ + (15/2x) H₂O + (3.76×15/4.76x) N₂ + 0.6 N₂\n\nSimplifying and solving for x, we get x = 3, which corresponds to the hydrocarbon C₃H₈.\n\nTherefore, the formula of the hydrocarbon is C₃H₈.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','C₃H₈','C₃H₃','CH₈','C₈H₈','NEW','11-021','11-021',NULL),
(11619,'chemistry','If we consider that 1/6, in place of 1/12, mass of carbon atom is taken to be the relative atomic mass unit, the mass of one mole of the substance  will [JEE 2005]','Decrease twice','Relative atomic mass = (Mass of one atom of the element/mass of one atom of the C-12) × 12.            \nNow if we use 1/6\nin place of 1/12 the\nformula becomes                  \nRelative atomic mass = (Mass of one atom of the element/mass of one atom of the C-12) × 6.                     \nRelative atomic mass decrease twice','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Be a function of the molecular mass of the\nsubstance','Remain unchanged','Increase two fold','Decrease twice','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11620,'chemistry','Experimentally it was found that a metal oxide has formula M₀.₉₈O. Metal M, present as M²+ and M³+ in its oxide. Fraction of the metal which exists as M³+ would be :','4.08%','For one mole of the oxide\nMoles of M = 0.98\nMoles of O²⁻= 1\nLet moles of M³+ = x\nMoles of M²⁺= 0.98– x\nOn balancing charge                 \n=(0.98 – x) × 2 + 3x – 2 = 0\nx = 0.04\n% of M³⁺ = (0.04/0.98)100 = 4.08%','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','7.01%','4.08%','6.05%','5.08%','NEW','11-019','11-019',NULL),
(11621,'chemistry','3 g of activated charcoal was added to 50 mL of acetic acid solution (0.06N) in a flask. After an hour it was filtered and the strength of the filtrate was found to be 0.042 N. The amount of acetic acid adsorbed (per gram of charcoal) is : [JEE M 2015]','18mg','Let the weight of acetic acid initially be w₁in 50 ml of 0.060 N solution.\nLet the N = (Normality = 0.06 N)\n 0.06 \n= 0.18 g = 180 mg.\nAfter an hour, the strength of acetic acid = 0.042 N\nso, let the weight of acetic acid be w₂\nN = 0.042 \nw₂ = 0.126 g = 126 mg\nSo amount of acetic acid adsorbed per 3g\n = 180 – 126 mg = 54 mg\nAmount of acetic acid adsorbed per g = 18','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','42mg','54mg','18mg','36mg','NEW','11-023','11-023',NULL),
(11701,'chemistry','Assertion : Equal moles of different substances contain same number of constituent particles.\n\nReason : Equal weights of different substances contain the same number of constituent particles.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Equal moles of different substances contain same number of constituent particles but equal weights of different substances do not contain the same number of constituent particles.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Assertion is incorrect, reason is correct.','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(11702,'chemistry','Assertion : 1.231 has three significant figures.\n\nReason : All numbers right to the decimal point are significant.','Assertion is incorrect, reason is correct.','1.231 has four significant figures all no. from left to right are counted, starting with the first digit that is not zero for calculating the no. of significant figure.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Assertion is incorrect, reason is correct.','NEW','11-012','11-012',NULL),
(11703,'chemistry','Assertion : Volume of a gas is inversely proportional to the number of moles of gas.\n\nReason : The ratio by volume of gaseous reactants and products is in agreement with their mole ratio.','Assertion is incorrect, reason is correct.','Volume of a gas is inversely proportional to  the number of moles of gas. This is a false statement because the volume of a gas is always directly proportional to its number of moles. This is explained by Avogadro\'s law.\n\nAnd the statement-2 is The ratio by volume of gaseous reactants andproducts is in agreement with their mole ratio. This is true.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Assertion is incorrect, reason is correct.','NEW','11-015','11-015',NULL),
(11704,'chemistry','Assertion: 1 Avogram is equal to 1 amu.\n\nReason: Avogram is reciprocal of Avogadro number.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Avogram is a unit of mass and weight equal to one gram divided by the Avogadro\'s number.\nThus, 1 Avogram is equal to 1 amu.\n\nBut Avogram is not reciprocal of Avogadro\'s number as Avogram is a unit of mass (gram) whereas Avogadro\'s number has units of molecules/mole.\n\nSince the two quantities have different units, they cannot be reciprocal of each other','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Assertion is incorrect, reason is correct.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11705,'chemistry','Assertion: When 4 moles of H₂ reacts with 2\nmoles of O₂, then 4 moles of water are formed.\n\nReason: O₂ will act as a limiting reagent.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.\n 2H₂ + O₂ →2H₂O\nIn this reaction, 2 moles of hydrogen react with 1 mole of oxygen to form 2 moles of water.\nSimilarly, 4 moles of hydrogen react with 2 moles of oxygen to form 4 moles of water.\nSince the reactants are present in stoichiometric ratios, there are no limiting reagents.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Assertion is incorrect, reason is correct.','NEW','11-021','11-021',NULL),
(11706,'chemistry','Assertion:  Vapour density of sulphur relative to oxygen is 2 because sulphur atom is twice as heavy as that of oxygen atom.\n\nReason: Vapour density depends upon the molecular state of the substance in vapour state.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Molecular weight of oxygen =32 \nMolecular weight of Sulphur =64\n Vapour density of Sulphur =2×( V.D of oxygen )\n  Vapour density depends on Molar mass but not on molecular state \n Hence Assertion is true but reason is wrong.','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Assertion is incorrect, reason is correct.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20210,'chemistry','Although the cathode rays themselves are not visible, but their behaviour can be observed with the help of certain kind of materials. These materials _______ and are called _______','Glow when hit by them, fluorescent or phosphorescent','"The results of discharge tube experiments were:\n\nThese rays themselves are not visible, but their behaviour can be observed with the help of certain kind of materials (fluorescent or phosphorescent) which glow when hit by them."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.30; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Electron',NULL,'3','mcq','Stay unaffected when hit by them, transluscent','Emit heat when hit by them, thermal conductors','Glow when hit by them, fluorescence or phosphorescence','Glow when hit by them, fluorescent or phosphorescent','NEW','11-030','11-030',NULL),
(20211,'chemistry','Television picture tubes are _______.','Cathode ray tubes','"Television picture tubes are cathode ray tubes."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.30; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Electron',NULL,'1','mcq','Cathode ray tubes','Anode ray tubes','Diode tubes','Electrical tubes','NEW','11-030','11-030',NULL),
(20212,'chemistry','Television pictures result due to _______ on the television screen coated with certain _______materials.','Fluorescence, fluorescent or phosphorescent','"Television pictures result due to fluorescence on the television screen coated with certain fluorescent or phosphorescent materials."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.30; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Electron',NULL,'1','mcq','Electrical conductivity, conductors','Diode tubes, semiconductors','Fluorescence, fluorescent or phosphorescent','Conductors, fluorescent or phosphorescent','NEW','11-030','11-030',NULL),
(20213,'chemistry','It was observed in the cathode ray tube experiments that in the absence of electrical or magnetic field, the cathode rays travel _______.','In straight line','"The results of discharge tube experiments were:\n\nIn the absence of electrical or magnetic field, these rays travel in straight lines."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.30; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Electron',NULL,'1','mcq','Towards electrical field','In straight line','Towards magnetic field','Towards cathode','NEW','11-030','11-030',NULL),
(20214,'chemistry','In the presence of electrical or magnetic field, the behaviour of cathode rays is similar to that expected from _______ particles, suggesting that the cathode rays consist of _______.','Negatively charged, electrons','"The results of discharge tube experiments were:\n\nIn the presence of electrical or magnetic field, the behaviour of cathode rays is similar to that expected from negatively charged particles, suggesting that the cathode rays consist of negatively charged particles, called electrons."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.30; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Electron',NULL,'1','mcq','Positively charged, protons','Negatively charged, electrons','Neutral, neutrons','Charged particles, ions','NEW','11-030','11-030',NULL),
(20215,'chemistry','Which of the following statements describes best the characteristics dependence of the cathode rays (electrons) upon the material of electrodes or the nature of gas used in the cathode ray tube?\n \n i. The characteristics of cathode rays (electrons) do not depend upon the material of electrodes and the nature of the gas present in the cathode ray tube.\n \n ii. The characteristics of cathode rays (electrons) depend upon both the material of electrodes and the nature of the gas present in the cathode ray tube.\n \n iii. The characteristics of cathode rays (electrons) do not depend upon the material of electrodes but depend upon the nature of the gas present in the cathode ray tube.\n \n iv. The characteristics of cathode rays (electrons) depend upon the material of electrodes but do not depend upon the nature of the gas present in the cathode ray tube.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (i)','"The results of discharge tube experiments were:\nThe characteristics of cathode rays (electrons) do not depend upon the material of electrodes and the nature of the gas present in the cathode ray tube. Thus, we can conclude that electrons are basic constituent of all the atoms."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.30; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Electron',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (i) and (ii)','Only (ii)','Only (i)','Only (iv)','NEW','11-030','11-030',NULL),
(20216,'chemistry','The results of discharge tube experiments were:\n \n i. The cathode rays start from cathode and move towards the anode.\n \n ii. These rays themselves are not visible but their behaviour can be observed with the help of certain kind of materials (fluorescent or phosphorescent) which glow when hit by them.\n \n iii. In the absence of electrical or magnetic field, these rays travel in straight lines.\n \n iv. In the presence of electrical or magnetic field, the behaviour of cathode rays are similar to that expected from negatively charged particles, suggesting that the cathode rays consist of negatively charged particles, called electrons.\n \n v. The characteristics of cathode rays (electrons) do not depend upon the material of electrodes and the nature of the gas present in the cathode ray tube. Thus, we can conclude that electrons are basic constituent of all the atoms.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','All (i), (ii), (iii), (iv), (v)','"The results of discharge tube experiments were:\n\n(i) The cathode rays start from cathode and move towards the anode.\n\n(ii) These rays themselves are not visible but their behaviour can be observed with the help of certain kind of materials (fluorescent or phosphorescent) which glow when hit by them.\n\n(iii) In the absence of electrical or magnetic field, these rays travel in straight lines.\n\n(iv) In the presence of electrical or magnetic field, the behaviour of cathode rays are similar to that expected from negatively charged particles, suggesting that the cathode rays consist of negatively charged particles, called electrons.\n\n(v) The characteristics of cathode rays (electrons) do not depend upon the material of electrodes and the nature of the gas present in the cathode ray tube. Thus, we can conclude that electrons are basic constituent of all the atoms."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.30; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Electron',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (iii)','All (i), (ii), (iii), (iv), (v)','Only (i) and (iv)','Only (ii), (iii), (iv)','NEW','11-030','11-030',NULL),
(20217,'chemistry','British physicist _______ measured the ratio of electrical charge (e) to the mass of electron (mₑ) by using cathode ray tube.','J. J. Thomson','"British physicist J.J. Thomson measured the ratio of electrical charge (e) to the mass of electron (mₑ) by using cathode ray tube and applying electrical and magnetic field perpendicular to each other as well as to the path of electrons."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.31; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Charge to Mass Ratio of Electron',NULL,'1','mcq','Rutherford','John Dalton','Michael Faraday','J. J. Thomson','NEW','11-031','11-031',NULL),
(20218,'chemistry','In Thomson experiment the amount of deviation of the particles from their path in the presence of electrical or magnetic field depends upon:\n \n (i) The magnitude of the negative charge on the particle.\n \n (ii)The mass of the particle. \n \n (iii)The strength of the electrical or magnetic field.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(i), (ii), (iii)','"In Thomson experiment the amount of deviation of the particles from their path in the presence of electrical or magnetic field depends upon:\n\n(i) The magnitude of the negative charge on the particle, greater the magnitude of the charge on the particle, greater is the interaction with the electric or magnetic field and thus greater is the deflection.\n\n(ii) The mass of the particle — lighter the particle, greater the deflection.\n\n(iii) The strength of the electrical or magnetic field — the deflection of electrons from its original path increases with the increase in the voltage across the electrodes, or the strength of the magnetic field."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.31; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Charge to Mass Ratio of Electron',NULL,'1','mcq','(i) and (ii)','(ii) and (iii)','(i) and (iii)','(i), (ii), (iii)','NEW','11-031','11-031',NULL),
(20219,'chemistry','In a cathode ray tube, if the charge on the electron is increased, the amount of deviation of the particle from its path _______ when electric and magnetic fields are applied.','Increases','"In Thomson experiment the amount of deviation of the particles from their path in the presence of electrical or magnetic field depends upon:\n\nThe magnitude of the negative charge on the particle, greater the magnitude of the charge on the particle, greater is the interaction with the electric or magnetic field and thus greater is the deflection."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.31; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Charge to Mass Ratio of Electron',NULL,'1','mcq','Decreases','Increases','Decreases initially and then increases','Increases initially and then decreases','NEW','11-031','11-031',NULL),
(20220,'chemistry','In a cathode ray tube, heavier particles tend to deflect _______ and lighter particles tend to deflect _______ under the influence of electric and magnetic field.','Less, more','"In Thomson experiment the amount of deviation of the particles from their path in the presence of electrical or magnetic field depends upon:\n\nThe mass of the particle — lighter the particle, greater the deflection."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.31; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Charge to Mass Ratio of Electron',NULL,'3','mcq','More, less','Less, more','Horizontally, Vertically','Vertically, Horizontally','NEW','11-031','11-031',NULL),
(20221,'chemistry','The deflection of electrons from its original path, under the influence of electric and magnetic fields, _______ with the increase in the voltage across the electrodes.','Increases','"In Thomson experiment the amount of deviation of the particles from their path in the presence of electrical or magnetic field depends upon:\n\nThe strength of the electrical or magnetic field — the deflection of electrons from its original path increases with the increase in the voltage across the electrodes, or the strength of the magnetic field."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.31; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Charge to Mass Ratio of Electron',NULL,'3','mcq','Increases','Decreases','Stays unaffected','Increases initially and then decreases','NEW','11-031','11-031',NULL),
(20222,'chemistry','The deflection of electrons from its original path, under the influence of electric and magnetic fields, _______ with the increase in the strength of the magnetic field.','Increases','"In Thomson experiment the amount of deviation of the particles from their path in the presence of electrical or magnetic field depends upon:\n/\nThe strength of the electrical or magnetic field - the deflection of electrons from its original path increases with the increase in the voltage across the electrodes, or the \nstrength of the magnetic field."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.31; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Charge to Mass Ratio of Electron',NULL,'2','mcq','Increases','Decreases','Stays unaffected','Increases initially and then decreases','NEW','11-031','11-031',NULL),
(20223,'chemistry','The value of e/mₑ is _______.','1.758820 × 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹','"The value of e/mₑ is = 1.758820 × 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.31; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Charge to Mass Ratio of Electron',NULL,'2','mcq','1.578820 × 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹','1.0758820 x 10⁻¹¹ C kg⁻¹','1.758820 × 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹','1.758820 x 10⁻¹¹ C g⁻¹','NEW','11-031','11-031',NULL),
(20224,'chemistry','The amount of charge on one electron was determined with the help of an experiment known as _______.','Oil drop experiment','"R.A. Millikan (1868-1953) devised a method known as oil drop experiment (1906-14), to determine the charge on the electrons."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.31; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Charge on the Electron',NULL,'3','mcq','Cathode ray tube experiment','Gold Foil experiment','Oil drop experiment','Aluminium foil experiment','NEW','11-031','11-031',NULL),
(20225,'chemistry','R.A. Millikan (1868-1953) devised a method known as oil drop experiment (1906-14), to determine _______.','The charge on the electrons','"R.A. Millikan (1868-1953) devised a method known as oil drop experiment (1906-14), to determine the charge on the electrons."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.31; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Charge on the Electron',NULL,'2','mcq','The charge on the electrons','The Charge on the protons','The mass of an electron','The mass of a Proton','NEW','11-031','11-031',NULL),
(20226,'chemistry','The amount of electrical charge on one electron is determined to be _______.','-1.602176 × 10⁻¹⁹ C','"The present accepted value of electrical charge is -1.602176 × 10⁻¹⁹ C."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.31; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Charge on the Electron',NULL,'2','mcq','-1.602176 × 10⁻¹⁹ C','-1.062176×10⁻¹⁹C','1.602176×10⁻¹⁹C','Both (a) and (c)','NEW','11-031','11-031',NULL),
(20227,'chemistry','The mass of an electron is equal to _______.','9.1094 x 10⁻³¹ kg','"The mass of the electron (mₑ) was determined by combining the results of oil drop experiment with Thomson’s value of e/mₑ ratio. \n\nIt was found to be equal to 9.1094 x 10⁻³¹ kg."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.31; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Charge on the Electron',NULL,'3','mcq','9.1094 x 10⁻³¹ g','-9.1094 x 10³¹ g','-9.0194 x 10⁻³¹ kg','9.1094 x 10⁻³¹ kg','NEW','11-031','11-031',NULL),
(20228,'chemistry','Electrical discharge carried out in the modified cathode ray tube led to the discovery of _______ carrying _______ particles.','Canal rays, positively charged','"Electrical discharge carried out in the modified cathode ray tube led to the discovery of canal rays carrying positively charged particles."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.32; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Protons and Neutrons',NULL,'2','mcq','Cathode rays, negatively charged','Canal rays, positively charged','Anode rays, neutral','Alpha rays, helium ions','NEW','11-032','11-032',NULL),
(20229,'chemistry','In the cathode rays, mass of negatively charged particles _______ the nature of gas present in the cathode ray tube.','Does not depend upon','"The characteristics of cathode rays(electrons) do not depend upon the material of electrodes and the nature ofthe gas present in the cathode ray tube."\n\nReference; NCERT Class 11; Pages 30-31; Paragraph 4,1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Protons and Neutrons',NULL,'2','mcq','Depends upon','Does not depend upon','Is propotional to','Is exponentially related to','NEW','11-032','11-032',NULL),
(20230,'chemistry','In the canal rays, mass of positively charged particles _______ the nature of gas present in the cathode ray tube.','Depends upon','"The characteristics of these positively charged particles are listed below:\n\nUnlike cathode rays, mass of positively charged particles depends upon the nature of gas present in the cathode ray tube. These are simply the positively charged gaseous ions."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.32; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Protons and Neutrons',NULL,'2','mcq','Depends upon','Does not depend uopn','Is propotional to','Is exponentially related to','NEW','11-032','11-032',NULL),
(20231,'chemistry','_______ are simply the positively charged gaseous ions.','Canal rays','"The characteristics of these positively charged particles are listed below:\n\nUnlike cathode rays, mass of positively charged particles depends upon the nature of gas present in the cathode ray tube. These are simply the positively charged gaseous ions."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.32; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Protons and Neutrons',NULL,'1','mcq','Cathode rays','Canal rays','Gamma rays','Beta rays','NEW','11-032','11-032',NULL),
(20232,'chemistry','The _______ of the canal rays particles depends on the gas from which these originate.','Charge to mass ratio','"The characteristics of these positively charged particles are listed below:\n\nThe charge to mass ratio of the particles depends on the gas from which these originate."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.32; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Protons and Neutrons',NULL,'3','mcq','Mass','Charge','Charge to mass ratio','Intensity','NEW','11-032','11-032',NULL),
(20233,'chemistry','Some of the positively charged particles carry _______ fundamental unit of electrical charge.','A multiple of the','"The characteristics of these positively charged particles are listed below:\n\nSome of the positively charged particles carry a multiple of the fundamental unit of electrical charge."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.32; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Protons and Neutrons',NULL,'2','mcq','A unit of the','A multiple of the','A factor of the','An average of the','NEW','11-032','11-032',NULL),
(20234,'chemistry','The behaviour of the canal rays particles in the magnetic or electrical field _______ that observed for electron or cathode rays.','Is opposite to','"The characteristics of these positively charged particles are listed below:\n\nThe behaviour of these particles in the magnetic or electrical field is opposite to that observed for electron or cathode rays."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.32; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Protons and Neutrons',NULL,'2','mcq','Is similar to','Is related to','Is opposite to','Is exponentially related to','NEW','11-032','11-032',NULL),
(20235,'chemistry','Select the correct options regarding the characteristics of positively charged particles (protons):\n \n (a) Unlike cathode rays, mass of positively charged particles doesn\'t depend upon the nature of gas present in the cathode ray tube.\n \n (b) The charge to mass ratio of the particles depends on the gas from which these originate.\n \n (c) Some of the positively charged particles carry a multiple of the fundamental unit of electrical charge.\n \n (d) The behaviour of these particles in the magnetic or electrical field is opposite to that observed for electron or cathode rays.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only b, c, d','"Electrical discharge carried out in the modified cathode ray tube led to the discovery of canal rays carrying positively charged particles. The characteristics of these positively charged particles are listed below:\n\n(i) Unlike cathode rays, mass of positively charged particles depends upon the nature of gas present in the cathode ray tube. These are simply the positively charged gaseous ions.\n\n(ii) The charge to mass ratio of the particles depends on the gas from which these originate.\n\n(iii) Some of the positively charged particles carry a multiple of the fundamental unit of electrical charge.\n\n(iv) The behaviour of these particles in the magnetic or electrical field is opposite to that observed for electron or cathode rays."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.32; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Protons and Neutrons',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only c, d','Only a, b, c','Only b, c, d','NEW','11-032','11-032',NULL),
(20236,'chemistry','The smallest and lightest positive ion was obtained from ______.','Hydrogen','"The smallest and lightest positive ion was obtained from hydrogen and was called proton."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.32; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Protons and Neutrons',NULL,'2','mcq','Oxygen','Hydrogen','Helium','Neon','NEW','11-032','11-032',NULL),
(20237,'chemistry','The smallest positive ion is called ______.','Proton','"The smallest and lightest positive ion was obtained from hydrogen and was called proton."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.32; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Protons and Neutrons',NULL,'2','mcq','Proton','Neutron','Electron','α-particle','NEW','11-032','11-032',NULL),
(20238,'chemistry','Electrically neutral subatomic particles are known as _______.','Neutron','"Electrically neutral subatomic particles are known as neutrons."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.32; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Protons and Neutrons',NULL,'1','mcq','Proton','Neutron','Electron','Both neutron and proton','NEW','11-032','11-032',NULL),
(20239,'chemistry','The electrically neutral subatomic particles were discovered by _______ in 1932 by bombarding a thin sheet of beryllium with _______.','Chadwick, α-particles','"Later, a need was felt for the presence of electrically neutral particle as one of the constituents of atom. \n\nThese particles were discovered by Chadwick (1932) by bombarding a thin sheet of beryllium with α-particles."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.32; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Protons and Neutrons',NULL,'2','mcq','Bohr, electron beam','John Dalton, neutrons','Chadwick, α-particles','J. J. Thomson, Hydrogen ions/protons','NEW','11-032','11-032',NULL),
(20240,'chemistry','The subatomic particles having a mass slightly greater than that of protons are known as _______.','Neutron','"When electrically neutral particles having a mass slightly greater than that of protons were emitted, Chadwick named these particles as neutrons."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.32; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Discovery of Protons and Neutrons',NULL,'3','mcq','Proton','Neutron','Electron','α-particle','NEW','11-032','11-032',NULL),
(20336,'chemistry','Rutherford’s nuclear model of atom resembles the _______ in which the nucleus plays the role of _______ and the electrons that of _______.','Solar system, sun, revolving planets','"On the basis of above observations and conclusions, Rutherford proposed the nuclear model of atom. According to this model:\n\nThe nucleus is surrounded by electrons that move around the nucleus with a very high speed in circular paths called orbits. Thus, Rutherford’s model of atom resembles the solar system in which the nucleus plays the role of sun and the electrons that of revolving planets."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.35; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Rutherford’s Nuclear Model of Atom',NULL,'2','mcq','Cosmic system, galaxies, stars','Solar system, sun, revolving planets','Solar system, galaxy, revolving planets','Centrifugal force system, uranium, enriched uranium','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(20337,'chemistry','According to Rutherford\'s nuclear atomic model, the electrons and the nucleus within an atom are held together by _______.','Electrostatic forces of attraction','"On the basis of above observations and conclusions, Rutherford proposed the nuclear model of atom. According to this model:\n\nElectrons and the nucleus are held together by electrostatic forces of attraction."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.35; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Rutherford’s Nuclear Model of Atom',NULL,'1','mcq','Electromagnetic forces of attraction','Gravitational forces of attraction','Magnetic forces of attraction','Electrostatic forces of attraction','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(20338,'chemistry','Select the correct statements regarding Rutherford\'s proposed nuclear model of atom:\n \n (i) The positive charge and most of the mass of the atom was densely concentrated in extremely small region. This very small portion of the atom was called nucleus by Rutherford.\n \n (ii) The nucleus is surrounded by electrons that move around the nucleus with a very high speed in circular paths called orbits.\n \n (iii) The nucleus is surrounded by a dense mass of positive charges that move around the atom with a very high speed.\n \n (iv) The positive charge and most of the mass of the atom was evenly distributed throughout the atom.\n \n (v) Rutherford’s model of atom resembles the solar system in which the nucleus plays the role of sun and the electrons that of revolving planets.\n \n (vi) Electrons and the nucleus are held together by electrostatic forces of attraction.\n \n (vii) Electrons and the nucleus are held together by electromagnetic forces of attraction.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(i), (ii), (v), (vi) only','"On the basis of above observations and conclusions, Rutherford proposed the nuclear model of atom. According to this model:\n\n(i) The positive charge and most of the mass of the atom was densely concentrated in extremely small region. This very small portion of the atom was called nucleus by Rutherford.\n\n(ii) The nucleus is surrounded by electrons that move around the nucleus with a very high speed in circular paths called orbits. Thus, Rutherford’s model of atom resembles the solar system in which the nucleus plays the role of sun and the electrons that of revolving planets.\n\n(iii) Electrons and the nucleus are held together by electrostatic forces of attraction."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.35; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Rutherford’s Nuclear Model of Atom',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii), (v), (vi) only','(i), (iii), (vi) only','(ii), (iv), (vi) only','(iii) ,(iv), (v) only','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(20339,'chemistry','The presence of positive charge on the nucleus is due to the _______ in the nucleus.','Protons','"The presence of positive charge on the nucleus is due to the protons in the nucleus."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.35; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Atomic Number and Mass Number',NULL,'1','mcq','Nutrinos','Photons','Neutrons','Protons','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(20340,'chemistry','The charge on the proton is _______ to that of electron.','Equal but opposite','"As established earlier, the charge on the proton is equal but opposite to that of electron."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.35; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Atomic Number and Mass Number',NULL,'1','mcq','Greater but opposite','Equal but opposite','Unequal but opposite','Smaller but opposite','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(20341,'chemistry','The number of protons present in the nucleus is equal to _______ , represented by symbol _______.','Atomic number, Z','"The number of protons present in the nucleus is equal to atomic number (Z)."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.35; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Atomic Number and Mass Number',NULL,'3','mcq','Atomic mass, Z','Atomic number, A','Atomic number, Z','Mass number, Z','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(20342,'chemistry','Since the atom is electrically neutral, the number of electrons in an atom _______ the number of protons i.e., atomic number, Z.','Is equal to','"In order to keep the electrical neutrality, the number of electrons in an atom is equal to the number of protons (atomic number, Z )."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.35; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Atomic Number and Mass Number',NULL,'1','mcq','Is equal to','Is greater than','Is smaller than','Is negligible compared to','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(20343,'chemistry','Atomic number is equal to _______.','Number of protons or number of electrons','"Atomic number (Z) = number of protons in the nucleus of an atom = number of electrons in a nuetral atom."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.35; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Atomic Number and Mass Number',NULL,'2','mcq','Number of nucleons','Number of neutrons','Sum of number of protons and electrons','Number of protons or number of electrons','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(20344,'chemistry','The protons and neutrons present in the nucleus are collectively known as _______.','Nucleons','"The protons and neutrons present in the nucleus are collectively known as nucleons."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.35; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Atomic number and mass number',NULL,'1','mcq','Nucleons','Atomic number','Isotopes','Isobars','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(20345,'chemistry','Number of protons + Number of neutrons = _______.','Mass number','"Number of protons + Number of neutrons = Mass number"\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.35; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Atomic number and mass number',NULL,'1','mcq','Mass number','Atomic number','Isotopes','Isotones','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(21601,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): When Schrödinger equation is solved for hydrogen atom, the solution gives the possible energy levels the electron can occupy and the corresponding wave function(s) (ψ) of the electron associated with each energy level.\n \n Reason(R): These quantized energy states and corresponding wave functions which are characterized by a set of three quantum numbers arise as a natural consequence in the solution of the Schrödinger equation.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"When Schrödinger equation is solved for hydrogen atom, the solution gives the possible energy levels the electron can occupy and the corresponding wave function(s) (ψ) of the electron associated with each energy level. These quantized energy states and corresponding wave functions which are characterized by a set of three quantum numbers (principal quantum number n, azimuthal quantum number l and magnetic quantum number mₗ) arise as a natural consequence in the solution of the Schrödinger equation."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.53; Paragraph 7','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Hydrogen Atom and the Schrödinger Equation',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-053','11-053',NULL),
(21602,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): When an electron is in any energy state, the wave function corresponding to that energy state contains all information about the electron.\n \n Reason(R): The wave function is a mathematical function whose value depends upon the coordinates of the electron in the atom and does not carry any physical meaning.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','"When an electron is in any energy state, the wave function corresponding to that energy state contains all information about the electron. The wave function is a mathematical function whose value depends upon the coordinates of the electron in the atom and does not carry any physical meaning."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.54; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Hydrogen Atom and the Schrödinger Equation',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-054','11-054',NULL),
(21603,'chemistry','Wave functions of hydrogen or hydrogen like species with one electron are called _______.','Atomic orbitals','"Wave functions of hydrogen or hydrogen like species with one electron are called atomic orbitals."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.54; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Hydrogen Atom and the Schrödinger Equation',NULL,'1','mcq','Atomic orbitals','Molecular orbitals','Degenerate orbitals','Subshells','NEW','11-054','11-054',NULL),
(23002,'chemistry','Assertion : Energy of electron is taken negative.\n Reason : Energy of electron at infinity is zero.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','In atomic physics, the energy of an electron in an atom is taken to be negative. This convention is based on the fact that the energy of an electron in a hydrogen atom is lower than its energy when it is free, and this difference in energy is taken to be negative. Therefore, in general, the energy of an electron in an atom is negative.\n \n Regarding Reason, it is also true that the energy of an electron at infinity is zero. When an electron is far away from any atom or molecule, it is considered to be at an infinite distance, and its energy is taken to be zero. This is because the energy of an electron is determined by its distance from the atomic nucleus or the positively charged ion in the case of a molecule, and as the distance approaches infinity, the energy of the electron approaches zero.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-048','11-048',NULL),
(21604,'chemistry','Select the correct statements regarding quantum mechanical wave functions: \n \n (a) Wave functions pertaining to one-electron species are called one-electron systems. \n \n (b) The probability of finding an electron at a point within an atom is proportional to the |Ψ| at that point.\n \n (c) The quantum mechanical results of the hydrogen atom successfully predict all aspects of the hydrogen atom spectrum including some phenomena that could not be explained by the Bohr model. \n \n (d) Schrödinger equation can be solved exactly for a multi-electron atom.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (a) and (c)','"The wave function is a mathematical function whose value depends upon the coordinates of the electron in the atom and does not carry any physical meaning. Such wave functions pertaining to one-electron species are called one-electron systems. The probability of finding an electron at a point within an atom is proportional to the |ψ|² at that point. The quantum mechanical results of the hydrogen atom successfully predict all aspects of the hydrogen atom spectrum including some phenomena that could not be explained by the Bohr model. Application of Schrödinger equation to multi-electron atoms presents a difficulty: the Schrödinger equation cannot be solved exactly for a multi-electron atom. This difficulty can be overcome by using approximate methods. Such calculations with the aid of modern computers show that orbitals in atoms other than hydrogen do not differ in any radical way from the hydrogen orbitals discussed above."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.54; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Hydrogen Atom and the Schrödinger Equation',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (a) and (c)','Both (b) and (c)','Both (c) and (d)','(a), (b) and (c)','NEW','11-054','11-054',NULL),
(21605,'chemistry','Select the correct statements regarding quantum mechanical model of atom: \n \n (a) The energy of electrons in atoms is not quantized. \n \n (b) The existence of quantized electronic energy levels is a direct result of the wave like properties of electrons and are allowed solutions of Schrödinger wave equation. \n \n (c) Both the exact position and exact velocity of an electron in an atom can be determined simultaneously. \n \n (d) An atomic orbital is the wave function Ψ for an electron in an atom.\n \n (e) |ψ|² is known as probability density and is always negative.\n \n (f) It is impossible to predict the region around the nucleus where electron will most probably be found from the value of |ψ|² at different points within an atom.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (b) and (d)','"Important Features of the Quantum Mechanical Model of Atom: \n\n Quantum mechanical model of atom is the picture of the structure of the atom, which emerges from the application of the Schrödinger equation to atoms. The important features are:\n\n1. The energy of electrons in atoms is quantized (i.e., can only have certain specific values). \n\n2. The existence of quantized electronic energy levels is a direct result of the wave like properties of electrons and are allowed solutions of Schrödinger wave equation.\n\n3. Both the exact position and exact velocity of an electron in an atom cannot be determined simultaneously (Heisenberg uncertainty principle). The path of an electron in an atom therefore, can never be determined or known accurately. \n\n4. An atomic orbital is the wave function ψ for an electron in an atom. Whenever an electron is described by a wave function, we say that the electron occupies that orbital. Since many suchwave functions are possible for an electron, there are many atomic orbitals in an atom. These “one electron orbital wave functions” or orbitals form the basis of the electronic structure of atoms. \n\n5. The probability of finding an electron at a point within an atom is proportional to the square of the orbital wave function i.e., |ψ|² at that point. |ψ|² is known as probability density and is always positive. From the value of |ψ|² at different points within an atom, it is possible to predict the region around the nucleus where electron will most probably be found."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.54; Paragraph 3-8)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Hydrogen Atom and the Schrödinger Equation',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (a) and (c)','Both (b) and (c)','Both (c) and (d)','Both (b) and (d)','NEW','11-053','11-053',NULL),
(21606,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): One electron orbital wave functions” or orbitals form the basis of the electronic structure of atoms.\n \n Reason(R): An orbital cannot contain more than two electrons.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','"An atomic orbital is the wave function ψ for an electron in an atom. Whenever an electron is described by a wave function, we say that the electron occupies that orbital. Since many suchwave functions are possible for an electron, there are many atomic orbitals in an atom. These “one electron orbital wave functions” or orbitals form the basis of the electronic structure of atoms. In each orbital, the electron has a definite energy. An orbital cannot contain more than two electrons."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.54; Paragraph 7','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Hydrogen Atom and the Schrödinger Equation',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-054','11-054',NULL),
(21607,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is incorrect:\n \n (i) In a multi-electron atom, the electrons are filled in various orbitals in the order of decreasing energy.\n \n (ii) All the information about the electron in an atom is stored in its orbital wave function Ψ.\n \n (iii) For each electron of a multi-electron atom, there shall, therefore, be an orbital wave function characteristic of the orbital it occupies.\n \n (iv) The probability of finding an electron at a point within an atom is proportional to the square of the orbital wave function i.e., |Ψ|² at that point.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(i)','"In a multi-electron atom, the electrons are filled in various orbitals in the order of increasing energy. For each electron of a multi-electron atom, there shall, therefore, be an orbital wave function characteristic of the orbital it occupies. All the information about the electron in an atom is stored in its orbital wave function ψ and quantum mechanics makes it possible to extract this information out of ψ. In a multi-electron atom, the electrons are filled in various orbitals in the order of increasing energy. The probability of finding an electron at a point within an atom is proportional to the square of the orbital wave function i.e., |Ψ|² at that point." \n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.54; Paragraph 7,8)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Hydrogen Atom and the Schrödinger Equation',NULL,'3','mcq','(i)','(i), (ii)','(iii), (iv)','(iv)','NEW','11-054','11-054',NULL),
(21608,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): The probability of finding an electron at a point within an atom is proportional to the square of the orbital wave function i.e., |Ψ|² at that point.\n \n Reason(R): |Ψ|² is known as probability density and is always positive. From the value of |Ψ|² at different points within an atom, it is possible to predict the region around the nucleus where electron will most probably be found.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"The probability of finding an electron at a point within an atom is proportional to the square of the orbital wave function i.e., |Ψ|² at that point. |Ψ|² is known as probability density and is always positive. From the value of |Ψ|² at different points within an atom, it is possible to predict the region around the nucleus where electron will most probably be found."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.54; Paragraph 8','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Hydrogen Atom and the Schrödinger Equation',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-054','11-054',NULL),
(21701,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): Qualitatively the orbitals can be distinguished by their size, shape and orientation. \n \n Reason(R): Shape and orientation mean that there is more probability of finding the electron along certain directions than along others.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"A large number of orbitals are possible in an atom. Qualitatively these orbitals can be distinguished by their size, shape and orientation. An orbital of smaller size means there is more chance of finding the electron near the nucleus. Similarly shape and orientation mean that there is more probability of finding the electron along certain directions than along others." \n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.55; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Orbitals and Quantum Numbers',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-054','11-054',NULL),
(21702,'chemistry','Each orbital is designated by three quantum numbers labelled as:\n \nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(i) Principal quantum number ‘n’ Azimuthal quantum number ‘l’ Magnetic orbital quantum number ‘mₗ’','"Atomic orbitals are precisely distinguished by what are known as quantum numbers. Each orbital is designated by three quantum numbers labelled as n, l and mₗ."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.55; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Orbitals and Quantum Numbers',NULL,'1','mcq','(i) Principal quantum number ‘n’ Azimuthal quantum number ‘l’ Magnetic orbital quantum number ‘mₗ’','(ii) Principal quantum number ‘l’ Azimuthal quantum number ‘n’ Magnetic orbital quantum number ‘mₗ','(iii) Principal quantum number ‘n’ Azimuthal quantum number ‘mₗ’ Magnetic orbital quantum number ‘l’','(iv) Principal quantum number ‘mₗ’ Azimuthal quantum number ‘l’ Magnetic orbital quantum number ‘n’','NEW','11-055','11-055',NULL),
(21703,'chemistry','Select the incorrect statements regarding principle quantum number:\n \n (a) The principal quantum number ‘n’ is a negative integer with value of n = -1, -2, -3.......\n \n (b) The principal quantum number determines the size and to large extent the energy of the orbital. \n \n (c) For hydrogen atom and hydrogen like species (He⁺, Li²⁺, .... etc.) energy and size of the orbital depends only on ‘n’.\n \n (d) The principal quantum number also identifies the subshell.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (a) and (d)','"The principal quantum number ‘n’ is a positive integer with value of n = 1,2,3....... The principal quantum number determines the size and to large extent the energy of the orbital. For hydrogen atom and hydrogen like species (He⁺, Li²⁺, .... etc.) energy and size of the orbital depends only on ‘n’. The principal quantum number also identifies the shell."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.55; Paragraph 2, 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Orbitals and Quantum Numbers',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (a) and (c)','Both (b) and (c)','Both (c) and (d)','Both (a) and (d)','NEW','11-055','11-055',NULL),
(21704,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): Size of a shell increases with increase of principal quantum number ‘n’. \n \n Reason(R): With the increase in the value of ‘n’, the number of allowed orbital increases and are given by ‘n²’. All the orbitals of a given value of ‘n’ constitute a single shell of atom and are represented by the following letters\n \n n = 1 2 3 4 ............\n \n Shell = K L M N ............\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"The principal quantum number determines the size and to large extent the energy of the orbital. With the increase in the value of ‘n’, the number of allowed orbital increases and are given by ‘n²’. All the orbitals of a given value of ‘n’ constitute a single shell of atom and are represented by the following letters:\n\nn = 1 2 3 4 ............\n\nShell = K L M N ..........\n\nThus, size of a shell increases with increase of principal quantum number ‘n’."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.55; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Orbitals and Quantum Numbers',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','11-055','11-055',NULL),
(21705,'chemistry','Size of an orbital increases with increase of _______.','Principal quantum number','"Size of an orbital increases with increase of principal quantum number ‘n’."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.55; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Orbitals and Quantum Numbers',NULL,'1','mcq','Azimuthal quantum number','Principal quantum number','Magnetic orbital quantum number','Spin quantum number','NEW','11-055','11-055',NULL),
(21706,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): The energy of the orbital increases with increase of n.\n \n Reason(R): As n increases, the electron will be located away from the nucleus. Since energy is required in shifting away the negatively charged electron from the positively charged nucleus, the energy of the orbital will increase with increase of n.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"Size of an orbital increases with increase of principal quantum number ‘n’. In other words the electron will be located away from the nucleus. Since energy is required in shifting away the negatively charged electron from the positively charged nucleus, the energy of the orbital will increase with increase of n."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.55; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Orbitals and Quantum Numbers',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','11-055','11-055',NULL),
(21707,'chemistry','Azimuthal quantum number ‘l’ is also known as:\n \n (a) Orbital angular momentum\n \n (b) Subsidiary quantum number\n \n (c) Magnetic orbital quantum number\n \n (d) Principal quantum number.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (a) and (b)','"Azimuthal quantum number ‘l’ is also known as orbital angular momentum or subsidiary quantum number."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.55; Paragraph 5','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Orbitals and Quantum Numbers',NULL,'1','mcq','(a) only','Only (b) and (c)','Only (a) and (b)','(a), (c) and (d)','NEW','11-055','11-055',NULL),
(21708,'chemistry','Select the correct statements regarding azimuthal quantum number:\n \n (a) It defines the two-dimensional shape of the orbital. \n \n (b) For a given value of n, l can have n values ranging from 0 to n – 1, that is, for a given value of n, the possible value of l are : l = 0, 1, 2, .......... (n–1). \n \n (c) Each shell consists of one or more shells. \n \n (d) The number of sub-shells in a principal shell is equal to the value of n.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (b) and (d)','"Azimuthal quantum number ‘l’:\n\n(i) It defines the three-dimensional shape of the orbital. \n\n(ii) For a given value of n, l can have n values ranging from 0 to n – 1, that is, for a given value of n, the possible value of l are : l = 0, 1, 2, ..........(n–1). For example, when n = 1, value of l is only 0. For n = 2, the possible value of l can be 0 and 1. For n = 3, the possible l values are 0, 1 and 2.\n\n(iii) Each shell consists of one or more sub-shells or sub-levels. \n\n(iv) The number of sub-shells in a principal shell is equal to the value of n. For example in the first shell (n = 1), there is only one sub-shell which corresponds to l = 0. There are two sub-shells (l = 0, 1) in the second shell (n = 2), three (l = 0, 1, 2) in third shell (n = 3) and so on."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.55; Paragraph 5-7)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Orbitals and Quantum Numbers',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (b) and (c)','Both (b) and (d)','Both (a) and (c)','Both (d) and (c)','NEW','11-055','11-055',NULL),
(21709,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): Each sub-shell is assigned an azimuthal quantum number (l). \n \n Reason(R): For a given value of n, l can have n values ranging from 0 to n – 1, that is, for a given value of n, the possible value of l are : l = 0, 1, 2, ..........(n–1). Sub-shells corresponding to different values of l are represented by the following symbols:\n \n Value for l: 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, ............\n \n Notation for sub-shell: s, p, d, f, g, h, ..........','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"For a given value of n, l can have n values ranging from 0 to n – 1, that is, for a given value of n, the possible value of l are : l = 0, 1, 2, ..........(n–1). \n\nFor example, when n = 1, value of l is only 0. For n = 2, the possible value of l can be 0 and 1. For n = 3, the possible l values are 0, 1 and 2. Each sub-shell is assigned an azimuthal quantum number (l). \n\nSub-shells corresponding to different values of l are represented by the following symbols:\n\nValue for l : 0 1 2 3 4 5 ............\n\nnotation for sub-shell : s p d f g h ............"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.55; Paragraph 7','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Orbitals and Quantum Numbers',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','11-055','11-055',NULL),
(21710,'chemistry','Magnetic orbital quantum number ‘mₗ’ gives information about _______.','The spatial orientation of the orbital with respect to standard set of co-ordinate axis.','"Magnetic orbital quantum number. ‘mₗ’ gives information about the spatial orientation of the orbital with respect to standard set of co-ordinate axis."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.55; Paragraph 8','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Orbitals and Quantum Numbers',NULL,'2','mcq','The spatial orientation of the orbital with respect to standard set of co-ordinate axis.','The three-dimensional shape of the orbital.','The size and to large extent the energy of the orbital.','Two spin states of the electron.','NEW','11-055','11-055',NULL),
(21711,'chemistry','George Uhlenbeck and Samuel Goudsmit proposed the quantum number known as the _______.','Spin quantum number','"In 1925, George Uhlenbeck and Samuel Goudsmit proposed the presence of the fourth quantum number known as the electron spin quantum number (mₛ)."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.56; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Orbitals and Quantum Numbers',NULL,'1','mcq','Azimuthal quantum number','Principal quantum number','Magnetic orbital quantum number','Spin quantum number','NEW','11-056','11-056',NULL),
(21712,'chemistry','Select the correct statements regarding spin quantum number:\n \n (a) An electron spins around its own axis, much in a similar way as earth spins around its own axis while revolving around the sun. \n \n (b) An electron has, besides charge and mass, intrinsic spin angular quantum number. \n \n (c) Spin angular momentum of the electron is a scalar quantity. \n \n (d) Spin angular momentum of the electron can have more than 2 orientations relative to the chosen axis. \n \n (e) The two orientations of spin angular momentum are distinguished by the spin quantum numbers mₛ which can take the values of +½ or –½. These are called the two spin states of the electron.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(a), (b) and (e)','"An electron spins around its own axis, much in a similar way as earth spins around its own axis while revolving around the sun. In other words, an electron has, besides charge and mass, intrinsic spin angular quantum number. Spin angular momentum of the electron — a vector quantity, can have two orientations relative to the chosen axis. \n\nThese two orientations are distinguished by the spin quantum numbers mₛ which can take the values of +½ or –½. \n\nThese are called the two spin states of the electron and are normally represented by two arrows, ↑ (spin up) and ↓ (spin down)." \n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.56; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Orbitals and Quantum Numbers',NULL,'3','mcq','(a), (b) and (c)','(c), (d) and (e)','Both (a) and (d)','(a), (b) and (e)','NEW','11-056','11-056',NULL),
(21713,'chemistry','Select the correct statements regarding spin quantum number:\n \n (a) The two spin states of the electron are normally represented by two arrows, ↑ (spin up) and ↓ (spin down).\n \n (b) These two orientations are distinguished by the spin quantum numbers mₛ which can take the values of +1 or –1.\n \n (c) Two electrons that have different mₛ values are said to have opposite spins.\n \n (d) An orbital cannot hold more than two electron.\n \n (e) The two electrons present in an orbital must have similar spins.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(a), (c) and (d)','"Spin angular momentum of the electron — a vector quantity, can have two orientations relative to the chosen axis. \n\nThese two orientations are distinguished by the spin quantum numbers mₛ which can take the values of +½ or –½. \n\nThese are called the two spin states of the electron and are normally represented by two arrows, ↑ (spin up) and ↓ (spin down). \n\nTwo electrons that have different ms values (one +½ and the other –½) are said to have opposite spins. \n\nAn orbital cannot hold more than two electrons and these two electrons should have opposite spins."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.56; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Orbitals and Quantum Numbers',NULL,'2','mcq','(a), (c) and (d)','(b), (c) and (e)','(b), (d) and (e)','(a), (b) and (d)','NEW','11-056','11-056',NULL),
(21714,'chemistry','Select the incorrect statements regarding informations provided by four quantum numbers:\n \n (a) n defines the subshell, determines the size of the orbital and also to some extent the energy of the orbital. \n \n (b) There are n subshells in the nth shell. l identifies the subshell and determines the shape of the orbital. \n \n (c) To some extent l also determines the energy of the orbital in a multi-electron atom. \n \n (d) mₗ designates the orientation of the orbital. \n \n (e) For a given value of l, mₗ has (2l+1) values, the same as the number of orbitals per subshell. \n \n (f) mₛ refers to orientation of the spin of the electron.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (a)','"The four quantum numbers provide the following information: \n\n(i) n defines the shell, determines the size of the orbital and also to a large extent the energy of the orbital. \n\n(ii) There are n subshells in the nth shell. l identifies the subshell and determines the shape of the orbital. There are (2l+1) orbitals of each type in a subshell, that is, one s orbital (l = 0), three p orbitals (l = 1) and five d orbitals (l = 2) per subshell. To some extent l also determines the energy of the orbital in a multi-electron atom.\n\n(iii) mₗ designates the orientation of the orbital. For a given value of l, mₗ has (2l+1) values, the same as the number of orbitals per subshell. It means that the number of orbitals is equal to the number of ways in which they are oriented. \n\n(iv) mₛ refers to orientation of the spin of the electron."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.56, 57; Paragraph 4, 1)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Orbitals and Quantum Numbers',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (a)','Only (d)','Both (a) and (d)','All are incorrect','NEW','11-056','11-056',NULL),
(21715,'chemistry','What is the total number of orbitals associated with the principal quantum number n = 3?','9','"For n = 3, the possible values of l are 0, 1 and 2. \n\nThus there is one 3s orbital (n = 3, l = 0 and mₗ = 0); there are three 3p orbitals (n = 3, l = 1 and mₗ = –1, 0, +1); there are five 3d orbitals (n = 3, l = 2 and mₗ = –2, –1, 0, +1, +2). \n\nTherefore, the total number of orbitals is 1 + 3 + 5 = 9. \n\nThe same value can also be obtained by using the relation; \nnumber of orbitals = n², i.e., 3² = 9."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.57; Problem 2.17','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Orbitals and Quantum Numbers',NULL,'3','mcq','9','4','25','16','NEW','11-057','11-057',NULL),
(21716,'chemistry','Using s, p, d, f notations, describe the orbital with the following quantum numbers:\n \n (a) n = 2, l = 1\n \n (b) n = 4, l = 0\n \n (c) n = 5, l = 3\n \n (d) n = 3, l = 2','n      l     orbital\na)2     1      2p\nb)4     0      4s\nc)5     3      5f\nd)3     2      3d','n     l     orbital\na)     2     1     2p\nb)     4     0     4s\nc)     5     3     5f\nd)     3     2     3d\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.57; Problem 2.18','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Orbitals and Quantum Numbers',NULL,'3','mcq','n     l     orbital\na)2     1      2p\nb)4     0      4s\nc)5     3      5f\nd)3     2      3d','n     l     orbital\na)2     1      2p\nb)4     0      4s\nc)5     3      4f\nd)3     2      3d','n     l     orbital\na)2     1      2p\nb)4     0      4s\nc)5     3      5d\nd)3     2      3d','n      l     orbital\na)2     1      2p\nb)4     0      4s\nc)5     3      5f\nd)3     2      3d','NEW','11-057','11-057',NULL),
(21717,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): The three quantum numbers labelling an atomic orbital can be used equally well to define its energy, shape and orientation.\n \n Reason(R): All three quantum numbers are not enough to explain the line spectra observed in the case of multi-electron atoms.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','"The three quantum numbers labelling an atomic orbital can be used equally well to define its energy, shape and orientation. But all these quantum numbers are not enough to explain the line spectra observed in the case of multi-electron atoms, that is, some of the lines actually occur in doublets (two lines closely spaced), triplets (three lines, closely spaced) etc. This suggests the presence of a few more energy levels than predicted by the three quantum numbers."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.56; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Orbitals and Quantum Numbers',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-056','11-056',NULL),
(21718,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): An orbit, as proposed by Bohr, is a circular path around the nucleus in which an electron moves. \n\n Reason(R): Bohr orbits have no real meaning and their existence can never be demonstrated experimentally.\n \n In the light of the below statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','"An orbit, as proposed by Bohr, is a circular path around the nucleus in which an electron moves. A precise description of this path of the electron is impossible according to Heisenberg uncertainty principle. Bohr orbits, therefore, have no real meaning and their existence can never be demonstrated experimentally."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.56; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Orbitals and Quantum Numbers',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-056','11-056',NULL),
(23003,'chemistry','Assertion : Bohr’s orbits are also called stationary states.\n Reason : Electrons are stationary in an orbit.','A is true but R is false','The Assertion is true, while the Reason is false.\n \n Bohr\'s model of the atom proposed that electrons orbit the nucleus in circular orbits at specific distances, and that these orbits correspond to specific energy levels. These energy levels are also known as stationary states, because in the absence of external forces, the electrons remain in their respective energy levels and do not emit or absorb radiation.However, the Reason is false. According to the principles of quantum mechanics, electrons do not have a definite position or velocity in an atom. Instead, they are described by wave functions, which give the probability of finding the electron at a particular location around the nucleus. The wave function for an electron in an atom is described as a standing wave, which has nodes and antinodes, and the probability of finding the electron at a node is zero. Therefore, electrons are not stationary in an orbit, but rather they have a probability distribution of being located in certain regions around the nucleus.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-046','11-046',NULL),
(21719,'chemistry','Select the correct statements regarding wave functions and atomic orbitals:\n \n (a) An atomic orbital is a quantum mechanical concept and refers to the one electron wave function Ψ in an atom. \n \n (b) The orbital wave function is characterized by three quantum numbers (n, l and mₗ) and its value depends upon the coordinates of the electron. \n \n (c) |Ψ| at any point in an atom gives the value of probability density at that point.\n \n (d) Probability density is the probability per unit volume.\n \n (e) The product of |Ψ|² and a small volume (called a volume element) yields the probability of finding the electron in that volume.\n \n (f) The total probability of finding the electron in a given volume can then be calculated by the sum of all the products of |Ψ| and the corresponding volume elements.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(a), (b), (d), (e)','"An atomic orbital is a quantum mechanical concept and refers to the one electron wave function ψ in an atom. It is characterized by three quantum numbers (n, l and mₗ) and its value depends upon the coordinates of the electron. ψ has, by itself, no physical meaning. It is the square of the wave function i.e., |Ψ|² which has a physical meaning. |Ψ|² at any point in an atom gives the value of probability density at that point. Probability density (|Ψ|²) is the probability per unit volume and the product of |Ψ|² and a small volume (called a volume element) yields the probability of finding the electron in that volume (the reason for specifying a small volume element is that |Ψ|² varies from one region to another in space but its value can be assumed to be constant within a small volume element). The total probability of finding the electron in a given volume can then be calculated by the sum of all the products of |Ψ|² and the corresponding volume elements. It is thus possible to get the probable distribution of an electron in an orbital."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.56; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Orbitals and Quantum Numbers',NULL,'3','mcq','(c), (d), (f)','(a), (d), (e), (f)','(a), (b), (d), (e)','(a), (c), (e)','NEW','11-056','11-056',NULL),
(21801,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): The orbital wave function or Ψ for an electron in an atom has no physical meaning.\n \n Reason(R): It is simply a mathematical function of the coordinates of the electron.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"The orbital wave function or Ψ for an electron in an atom has no physical meaning. It is simply a mathematical function of the coordinates of the electron."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.57; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Shapes of Atomic Orbitals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-056','11-056',NULL),
(21802,'chemistry','Select the correct statements regarding shapes of the atomic orbitals:\n \n (a) According to the German physicist, Max Born, the wave function (i.e.Ψ) at a point gives the probability density of the electron at that point.\n \n (b) For 1s orbital the probability density is maximum at the nucleus and it decreases sharply as we move away from it. \n \n (c) For 2s orbital the probability density first decreases sharply to zero and again starts increasing. After reaching a small maxima it decreases again and approaches zero as the value of r increases further.\n \n (d) The region where this probability density function reduces to zero is called nodal surfaces or simply nodes.\n \n (e) The number of nodes decreases with increase of principal quantum number n.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(b), (c), (d)','"According to the German physicist, Max Born, the square of the wave function (i.e.Ψ²) at a point gives the probability density of the electron at that point. Graphical representation of the obtained data shows that, for 1s orbital the probability density is maximum at the nucleus and it decreases sharply as we move away from it. On the other hand, for 2s orbital the probability density first decreases sharply to zero and again starts increasing. After reaching a small maxima it decreases again and approaches zero as the value of r increases further. The region where this probability density function reduces to zero is called nodal surfaces or simply nodes. In general, it has been found that ns-orbital has (n – 1) nodes, that is, number of nodes increases with increase of principal quantum number n. In other words, number of nodes for 2s orbital is one, two for 3s and so on."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.57; Paragraph 3, 4)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Shapes of Atomic Orbitals',NULL,'3','mcq','(a), (b), (c)','(b), (c), (d)','(c), (d), (e)','(a), (b), (c), (d)','NEW','11-057','11-057',NULL),
(21803,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): Number of nodes for 2s orbital is one, two for 3s, three for 4s and so on.\n Reason(R): Number of nodes increases with increase of principal quantum number n.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"In general, it has been found that ns-orbital has (n – 1) nodes, that is, number of nodes increases with increase of principal quantum number n. In other words, number of nodes for 2s orbital is one, two for 3s and so on."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.57; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Shapes of Atomic Orbitals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-057','11-057',NULL),
(21804,'chemistry','Boundary surface Diagrams of constant probability density for different orbitals give a fairly good representation of the _______.','Shapes of orbitasl','"The probability density variations can be visualised in terms of charge cloud Diagrams. In these Diagrams, the density of the dots in a region represents electron probability density in that region. Boundary surface Diagrams of constant probability density for different orbitals give a fairly good representation of the shapes of the orbitals."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.58; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Shapes of Atomic Orbitals',NULL,'1','mcq','Size of orbitals','Shapes of orbitasl','Orientation of orbitals','Spin of electrons','NEW','11-058','11-058',NULL),
(30923,'chemistry','Which of the following has closely related properties to the ionization enthalpy?','Atomic radius','There are two trends the first ionization enthalpy generally increases as we go across a period and decreases as we descend in a group. \n\nThese trends are illustrated in diagram respectively for the elements of the second period and the first group of the periodic table. \n\nYou will appreciate that the ionization enthalpy and atomic radius are closely related properties.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 88; Paragraph No. 4; diagram(ne0322)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','Electronegativity','Atomic radius','Electron affinity','Electropositivity','NEW','11-088','11-088',NULL),
(21805,'chemistry','Select the correct statements regarding Boundary surface Diagrams: \n \n (a) In Boundary surface Diagrams, a boundary surface or contour surface is drawn in space for an orbital on which the value of probability density |Ψ|² is constant. \n \n (b) For a given orbital, only that boundary surface Diagram of constant probability density is taken to be good representation of the shape of the orbital which encloses a region or volume in which the probability of finding the electron is average, say, 60% - 80%. \n \n (c) The boundary surface for |Ψ|² and |ψ| are non-identical.\n \n (d) It is impossible to draw a boundary surface Diagram of a rigid size in which the probability of finding the electron is 100%.\n \n (e) Boundary surface Diagram for an s orbital is actually a sphere centred on the nucleus.\n \n (f) All the s-orbitals are spherically symmetric, that is, the probability of finding the electron at a given distance is equal in all the directions.\n \n (g) If probability density |Ψ|² is constant on a given surface, |ψ| is variable over the surface.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (a), (d), (e), (f)','"Boundary surface Diagrams of constant probability density for different orbitals give a fairly good representation of the shapes of the orbitals. In this representation, a boundary surface or contour surface is drawn in space for an orbital on which the value of probability density |Ψ|² is constant. In principle many such boundary surfaces may be possible. However, for a given orbital, only that boundary surface Diagram of constant probability density* is taken to be good representation of the shape of the orbital which encloses a region or volume in which the probability of finding the electron is very high, say, 90%. *(If probability density |Ψ|² is constant on a given surface, |ψ| is also constant over the surface. The boundary surface for |Ψ|² and |ψ| are identical.) The question arises is: Why do we not draw a boundary surface Diagram, which bounds a region in which the probability of finding the electron is, 100 %? The answer to this question is that the probability density |Ψ|² has always some value, howsoever small it may be, at any finite distance from the nucleus. It is therefore, not possible to draw a boundary surface Diagram of a rigid size in which the probability of finding the electron is 100%. Boundary surface Diagram for an s orbital is actually a sphere centred on the nucleus. In two dimensions, this sphere looks like a circle. It encloses a region in which probability of finding the electron is about 90%. Thus, we see that 1s and 2s orbitals aremspherical in shape. In reality all the s-orbitals are spherically symmetric, that is, the probability of finding the electron at a given distance is equal in all the directions."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.58; Paragraph 1, 2)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Shapes of Atomic Orbitals',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (a), (d), (e), (f)','Only (c), (d), (f), (g)','Only (b), (c), (d), (f)','Only (a), (b), (e), (g)','NEW','11-058','11-058',NULL),
(21806,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): The size of the s orbital increases with increase in n, that is, 4s > 3s > 2s > 1s. \n \n Reason(R): The electron is located further away from the nucleus as the principal quantum number increases, thus proving that the size of the s orbital increases with increase in n.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"The size of the s orbital increases with increase in n, that is, 4s > 3s > 2s > 1s and the electron is located further away from the nucleus as the principal quantum number increases."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.58; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Shapes of Atomic Orbitals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-058','11-058',NULL),
(21807,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): Each p orbital consists of two sections called lobes that are on either side of the plane that passes through the nucleus.\n \n Reason(R): The probability density function is non-zero on the plane where the two lobes touch each other.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(A) is true but (R) is false.','"Boundary surface Diagrams for three 2p orbitals (l = 1) show that each p orbital consists of two sections called lobes that are on either side of the plane that passes through the nucleus. The probability density function is zero on the plane where the two lobes touch each other."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.58; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Shapes of Atomic Orbitals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-058','11-058',NULL),
(21808,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n AssertionA): The size, shape and energy of the three p orbitals are not identical.\n \n Reason(R): The lobes of the p-orbitals do not lie along the x, y or z axis.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are false.','"The size, shape and energy of the three p-orbitals are identical. They differ however, in the way the lobes are oriented. Since the lobes may be considered to lie along the x, y or z axis, they are given the designations 2pₓ, 2pᵧ, and 2p_z."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.59; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Shapes of Atomic Orbitals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','Both (A) and (R) are false.','NEW','11-059','11-059',NULL),
(21809,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): The order of the energy and size of various p orbitals is 4p > 3p > 2p. \n \n Reason(R): p orbitals increase in size and energy with increase in the principal quantum number.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"Like s orbitals, p orbitals increase in size and energy with increase in the principal quantum number and hence the order of the energy and size of various p orbitals is 4p > 3p > 2p."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.59; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Shapes of Atomic Orbitals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-059','11-059',NULL),
(21810,'chemistry','Select the correct statements regarding |Ψ|² for p-orbitals:\n \n (a) The probability density functions for p-orbital also pass through value zero, besides at zero and infinite distance, as the distance from the nucleus increases.\n \n (b) Number of radial node is 1 for 3p orbital, two for 4p orbital and so on.\n \n (c) The number of nodes are given by the (n –1) for p orbital.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (a) and (b)','"Like s orbitals, the probability density functions for p-orbital also pass through value zero, besides at zero and infinite distance, as the distance from the nucleus increases. The number of nodes are given by the n–2, that is number of radial node is 1 for 3p orbital, two for 4p orbital and so on."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.59; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Shapes of Atomic Orbitals',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (a) and (b)','Only (b) and (c)','Only (a)','Only (a) and (c)','NEW','11-059','11-059',NULL),
(21811,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): For l = 2, the minimum value of principal quantum number (n) has to be 3. \n \n Reason(R): For a given value of n, l can have n values ranging from 0 to n – 1. The value of l cannot be greater than (n–1).\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"For a given value of n, l can have n values ranging from 0 to n – 1, that is, for a given value of n, the possible value of l are : l = 0, 1, 2, ..........(n–1). \n\nFor example, when n = 1, value of l is only 0. For n = 2, the possible value of l can be 0 and 1. For n = 3, the possible l values are 0, 1 and 2."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.55; Paragraph 7\n\n"For l = 2, the orbital is known as d-orbital and the minimum value of principal quantum number (n) has to be 3 as the value of l cannot be greater than n–1. \n\nThere are five mₗ values (–2, –1, 0, +1 and +2) for l = 2 and thus there are five d orbitals."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.59; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Shapes of Atomic Orbitals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-059','11-059',NULL),
(21812,'chemistry','For l = 2, the orbital is known as _______.','d orbital','"For l = 2, the orbital is known as d-orbital."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.59; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Shapes of Atomic Orbitals',NULL,'1','mcq','d orbital','p orbital','s orbital','f orbital','NEW','11-059','11-059',NULL),
(21813,'chemistry','Select the incorrect statements regarding d orbital:\n \n (a) For l = 2, the orbital is known as d orbital. \n \n (b) There are five mₗ values (–2, –1, 0, +1 and +2) for l = 2 and thus there are five d orbitals.\n \n (c) The five d-orbitals are designated as dₓᵧ, dᵧ₂, dₓ₂, dₓ²-ᵧ² and d₂².\n \n (d) The shapes of the first four d-orbitals are dissimilar to each other.\n \n (e) All five 3d orbitals are non-equivalent in energy.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (d) and (e)','"For l = 2, the orbital is known as d-orbital and the minimum value of principal quantum number (n) has to be 3 as the value of l cannot be greater than n–1. There are five mₗ values (–2, –1, 0, +1 and +2) for l = 2 and thus there are five d orbitals. The five d-orbitals are designated as dₓᵧ, dᵧ², dₓ², dₓ²–ᵧ² and dz². The shapes of the first four d orbitals are similar to each other, where as that of the fifth one, dz², is different from others, but all five 3d orbitals are equivalent in energy."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.59; Paragraph 2, 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Shapes of Atomic Orbitals',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (d) and (e)','Only (a), (b) and (c)','Only (d)','All of the above','NEW','11-059','11-059',NULL),
(21814,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is correct regarding d-orbital?\n \n (a) The d orbitals for which n is greater than 3 (4d, 5d...) have shapes, energy and size different from 3d orbital.\n \n (b) The probability density functions for the np and nd orbitals are zero at the plane (s), passing through the nucleus (origin).\n \n (c) In case of dₓᵧ orbital, there are two nodal planes passing through the origin and bisecting the xy plane containing z-axis. These are called angular nodes.\n \n (d) The number of angular nodes are given by ‘l- n - 1’.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (b) and (c)','"The d orbitals for which n is greater than 3 (4d, 5d...) also have shapes similar to 3d orbital, but differ in energy and size. Besides the radial nodes (i.e., probability density function is zero), the probability density functions for the np and nd orbitals are zero at the plane (s), passing through the nucleus (origin). \n\nFor example, in case of Pz orbital, xy-plane is a nodal plane, in case of dₓᵧ orbital, there are two nodal planes passing through the origin and bisecting the xy plane containing z-axis. These are called angular nodes and number of angular nodes are given by ‘l’, i.e., one angular node for p orbitals, two angular nodes for ‘d’ orbitals and so on. The total number of nodes are given by (n–1), i.e., sum of l angular nodes and (n – l – 1) radial nodes."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.59; Paragraph 3, 4)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Shapes of Atomic Orbitals',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (a) and (b)','Only (b) and (c)','Only (c) and (d)','Only (b) and (d)','NEW','11-059','11-059',NULL),
(21815,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): The total number of nodes are given by (n–1). \n \n Reason(R): Total nodes = Angular nodes + Radial nodes \n \n Angular nodes = l, Radial nodes = (n – l – 1).\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"The total number of nodes are given by (n–1), i.e., sum of l angular nodes and (n – l – 1) radial nodes."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.59; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Shapes of Atomic Orbitals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-059','11-059',NULL),
(21901,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): The energy of an electron in a hydrogen atom is determined solely by the principal quantum number.\n \n Reason(R): The energy of the orbitals in hydrogen atom increases as follows :\n 1s < 2s = 2p < 3s = 3p = 3d <4s = 4p = 4d = 4f\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"The energy of an electron in a hydrogen atom is determined solely by the principal quantum number. The energy of the orbitals in hydrogen atom increases as follows:\n\n1s < 2s = 2p < 3s = 3p = 3d < 4s = 4p = 4d = 4f"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.60; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Energies of Orbitals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-060','11-060',NULL),
(71516,'chemistry','On increasing the pressure, in which direction will the gas phase reaction proceed to re-establish equilibrium, is predicted by applying the Le Chatelier’s principle. Consider the reaction,\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌2NH₃(g)\nWhich of the following is correct, if the total pressure at which the equilibrium is established, is increased without changing the temperature?','K will remain same','N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌2NH₃(g)\nAccording to Le Chatelier’s principle, at constant temperature, the equilibrium composition will change but K will remain same.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','K will remain same','K will decrease','K will increase','K will increase initially and decrease when pressure is very high','NEW','11-210','11-186',NULL),
(21902,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): An electron in a hydrogen atom has the same energy when it is in the 2s orbital as when it is present in 2p orbital.\n \n Reason(R): 2s and 2p are degenerate orbitals in hydrogen atom.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"The energy of the orbitals in hydrogen atom increases as follows:\n\n1s < 2s = 2p < 3s = 3p = 3d < 4s = 4p = 4d = 4f\n\nAlthough the shapes of 2s and 2p orbitals are different, an electron has the same energy when it is in the 2s orbital as when it is present in 2p orbital. The orbitals having the same energy are called degenerate." \n\n"Hence, according to above order, 2s and 2p are degenerate orbitals in hydrogen atom."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.60; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Energies of Orbitals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-059','11-059',NULL),
(21903,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct regarding hydrogen atom?\n \n (a) The 1s orbital in a hydrogen atom corresponds to the most stable condition and is called the ground state.\n \n (b) An electron residing in the 1s orbital is most strongly held by the nucleus. \n \n (c) An electron in the 2s, 2p or higher orbitals in a hydrogen atom is also in the most stable state state.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (a) and (b)','"The 1s orbital in a hydrogen atom corresponds to the most stable condition and is called the ground state and an electron residing in this orbital is most strongly held by the nucleus. An electron in the 2s, 2p or higher orbitals in a hydrogen atom is in excited state."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.60; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Energies of Orbitals',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (a) and (b)','Only (b) and (c)','Only(a) and (c)','Only (a), (b) and (c)','NEW','11-060','11-060',NULL),
(21904,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): The energy of an electron in a multielectron atom, depends not only on its principal quantum number (shell), but also on its azimuthal quantum number (subshell). \n \n Reason(R): Within a given principal quantum number, the energy of orbitals increases in the order: s < p < d < f\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','"The energy of an electron in a multielectron atom, unlike that of the hydrogen atom, depends not only on its principal quantum number (shell), but also on its azimuthal quantum number (subshell). That is, for a given principal quantum number, s, p, d, f ... all have different energies. Within a given principal quantum number, the energy of orbitals increases in the order:\n\ns < p < d < f"\n\n"Above statements are independent statements based on experimental observations, and one is not the reason of other."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.60; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Energies of Orbitals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-060','11-060',NULL),
(21905,'chemistry','The main reason for having different energies of the subshells is _______.','The mutual repulsion among the electrons in multielectron atoms.','"Within a given principal quantum number, the energy of orbitals increases in the order:\n\ns < p < d < f\n\n For higher energy levels, these differences are sufficiently pronounced and straggering of orbital energy may result, e.g., 4s < 3d and 6s < 5d ; 4f < 6p. The main reason for having different energies of the subshells is the mutual repulsion among the electrons in multielectron atoms."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.60; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Energies of Orbitals',NULL,'2','mcq','The mutual repulsion among the electrons in multielectron atoms.','The presence of attraction between the electron and nucleus.','The total attractive interactions are more than the repulsive interactions.','The total repulsive interactions are more than the attractive interactions.','NEW','11-060','11-060',NULL),
(21906,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct ragarding interactions present within an atom?\n \n (a) The only electrical interaction present in hydrogen atom is the attraction between the positively charged electron and the negatively charged nucleus. \n \n (b) In multielectron atoms, besides the presence of attraction between the electron and nucleus, there are repulsion terms between every electron and other electrons present in the atom. \n \n (c) The stability of an electron in a multi-electron atom is because total repulsive interactions are more than the attractive interactions. \n \n (d) The attractive interactions of an electron decreases with increase of positive charge (Ze) on the nucleus.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(b) only','"The only electrical interaction present in hydrogen atom is the attraction between the negatively charged electron and the positively charged nucleus. \n\nIn multi-electron atoms, besides the presence of attraction between the electron and nucleus, there are repulsion terms between every electron and other electrons present in the atom. \n\nThus the stability of an electron in a multi-electron atom is because total attractive interactions are more than the repulsive interactions. In general, the repulsive interaction of the electrons in the outer shell with the electrons in the inner shell are more important. On the other hand, the attractive interactions of an electron increases with increase of positive charge (Ze) on the nucleus."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.60; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Energies of Orbitals',NULL,'3','mcq','(a) only','(b) only','(a) and (c)','(b) and (d)','NEW','11-060','11-060',NULL),
(30924,'chemistry','Which element has a smaller first ionization enthalpy than beryllium in contrast?','Boron','The first ionization enthalpy of boron (Z = 5) is slightly less than that of beryllium (Z = 4) even though the former has a greater nuclear charge. \n\nWhen we consider the same principal quantum level, an s-electron is attracted to the nucleus more than a p-electron. \n\nIn beryllium, the electron removed during the ionization is an s-electron whereas the electron removed during ionization of boron is a p-electron. \n\nThe penetration of a 2s-electron to the nucleus is more than that of a 2p-electron; hence the 2p electron of boron is more shielded from the nucleus by the inner core of electrons than the 2s electrons of beryllium. \n\nTherefore, it is easier to remove the 2p-electron from boron compared to the removal of a 2s- electron from beryllium. \n\nThus, boron has a smaller first ionization enthalpy than beryllium. It is shown in diagram\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 89; Paragraph No. 4) diagram(ne0322)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Ionisation enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','Boron','Carbon','Oxygen','Nitrogen','NEW','11-089','11-089',NULL),
(21907,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): Due to the presence of electrons in the inner shells, the electron in the outer shell will not experience the full positive charge of the nucleus (Ze), but will only experience the effective nuclear charge. \n \n Reason(R): The effect of nucleus on the outer shell electrons is lowered due to the partial screening of positive charge on the nucleus by the inner shell electrons. This is known as the shielding of the outer shell electrons from the nucleus by the inner shell electrons, the net positive charge experienced by the outer electrons is known as effective nuclear charge.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"In general, the repulsive interaction of the electrons in the outer shell with the electrons in the inner shell are more important. On the other hand, the attractive interactions of an electron increases with increase of positive charge (Ze) on the nucleus. Due to the presence of electrons in the inner shells, the electron in the outer shell will not experience the full positive charge of the nucleus (Ze). The effect will be lowered due to the partial screening of positive charge on the nucleus by the inner shell electrons. This is known as the shielding of the outer shell electrons from the nucleus by the inner shell electrons, and the net positive charge experienced by the outer electrons is known as effective nuclear charge (Zₑբբ e)."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.60; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Energies of Orbitals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-060','11-060',NULL),
(21908,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct regarding energies of atomic orbitals?\n \n (a) The attractive force experienced by the outer shell electrons increases with increase of nuclear charge.\n \n (b) The energy of interaction between the nucleus and electron is called orbital energy.\n \n (c) The orbital energy decreases with the increase of atomic number (Z).\n \n (d) When the orbital energy decreases, it gets more positive.\n \n (e) Both the attractive and repulsive interactions doesn\'t depend upon the shell and shape of the orbital in which the electron is present.\n \n (f) Electrons present in spherical shaped s orbital shields the outer electrons from the nucleus more effectively as compared to electrons present in p and d orbitals.\n \n (g) Within a shell, due to spherical shape of s orbital, the s orbital electron spends less time close to the nucleus in comparison to p orbital electron which, in turn, spends lesser time in the vicinity of nucleus in comparison to d orbital electron.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (a), (b), (c) and (f)','"Despite the shielding of the outer electrons from the nucleus by the inner shell electrons, the attractive force experienced by the outer shell electrons increases with increase of nuclear charge. In other words, the energy of interaction between the nucleus and electron (that is orbital energy) decreases (that is more negative) with the increase of atomic number (Z). Both the attractive and repulsive interactions depend upon the shell and shape of the orbital in which the electron is present. For example electrons present in spherical shaped s orbital shields the outer electrons from the nucleus more effectively as compared to electrons present in p orbital. Similarly electrons present in p orbitals shield the outer electrons from the nucleus more than the electrons present in d orbitals, even though all these orbitals are present in the same shell. Further within a shell, due to spherical shape of s orbital, the s orbital electron spends more time close to the nucleus in comparison to p orbital electron which spends more time in the vicinity of nucleus in comparison to d orbital electron."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.61; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Energies of Orbitals',NULL,'2','mcq','All but not (d)','Only (b), (c), (e) and (g)','Only (b), (c), (f) and (g)','Only (a), (b), (c) and (f)','NEW','11-060','11-060',NULL),
(21909,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): The s orbital electron will be more tightly bound to the nucleus than p orbital electron which in turn will be better tightly bound than the d orbital electron.\n Reason(R): For a given shell (principal quantum number), the Zₑբբ experienced by the electron decreases with increase of azimuthal quantum number (l).\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"Electrons present in spherical shaped s orbital shields the outer electrons from the nucleus more effectively as compared to electrons present in p orbital. Similarly electrons present in p orbitals shield the outer electrons from the nucleus more than the electrons present in d orbitals, even though all these orbitals are present in the same shell. Further within a shell, due to spherical shape of s orbital, the s orbital electron spends more time close to the nucleus in comparison to p orbital electron which spends more time in the vicinity of nucleus in comparison to d orbital electron. In other words, for a given shell (principal quantum number), the Zₑբբ experienced by the electron decreases with increase of azimuthal quantum number (l), that is, the s orbital electron will be more tightly bound to the nucleus than p orbital electron which in turn will be better tightly bound than the d orbital electron."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.61; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Energies of Orbitals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-061','11-061',NULL),
(21910,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): The energy of electrons in s orbital will be lower (more negative) than that of p orbital electron which will have less energy than that of d orbital electron and so on. \n Reason(R): The lower the value of (n + l) for an orbital, the lower is its energy. If two orbitals have the same value of (n + l), the orbital with lower value of n will have the lower energy.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"The energy of electrons in s orbital will be lower (more negative) than that of p orbital electron which will have less energy than that of d orbital electron and so on. Since the extent of shielding from the nucleus is different for electrons in different orbitals, it leads to the splitting of energy levels within the same shell (or same principal quantum number), that is, energy of electron in an orbital depends upon the values of n and l. Mathematically, the dependence of energies of the orbitals on n and l are quite complicated but one simple rule is that, the lower the value of (n + l) for an orbital, the lower is its energy. If two orbitals have the same value of (n + l), the orbital with lower value of n will have the lower energy."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.61; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Energies of Orbitals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-061','11-061',NULL),
(21911,'chemistry','Energies of the orbitals in the same subshell decrease with increase in _______.','Atomic number','"Energies of the orbitals in the same subshell decrease with increase in the atomic number."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.61; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Energies of Orbitals',NULL,'1','mcq','Atomic number','Atomic mass','Charge on atom','Size of orbital','NEW','11-061','11-061',NULL),
(21912,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): Energy of 2s orbital of hydrogen atom is greater than that of 2s orbital of lithium and that of lithium is greater than that of sodium and so on, i.e., \n E₂ₛ(H) > E₂ₛ(Li) > E₂ₛ(Na) > E₂ₛ(K)\n Reason(R): Energies of the orbitals in the same subshell decrease with increase in the atomic number (Zeff).\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"Energies of the orbitals in the same subshell decrease with increase in the atomic number. For example, energy of 2s orbital of hydrogen atom is greater than that of 2s orbital of lithium and that of lithium is greater than that of sodium and so on, that is, \nE₂ₛ(H) > E₂ₛ(Li) > E₂ₛ(Na) > E₂ₛ(K)."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.61; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Energies of Orbitals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-061','11-061',NULL),
(22001,'chemistry','The filling of electrons into the orbitals of different atoms takes place according to the aufbau principle which is based on:\n \n (i) The Pauli’s exclusion principle \n (ii The Hund’s rule of maximum multiplicity .\n (iii)The relative energies of the orbitals.\n (iv) The relative sizes of sub-shells.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(i), (ii), (iii) only','"The filling of electrons into the orbitals of different atoms takes place according to the aufbau principle which is based on the Pauli’s exclusion principle, the Hund’s rule of maximum multiplicity and the relative energies of the orbitals."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.61; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Filling of Orbitals in Atom',NULL,'2','mcq','All but not (iv)','(ii), (iii) only','(i), (ii), (iii) only','Option (a) and (c) both','NEW','11-061','11-061',NULL),
(22002,'chemistry','The word ‘aufbau’ in German means _______.','Building up','"The word ‘aufbau’ in German means ‘building up’. The building up of orbitals means the filling up of orbitals with electrons."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.61; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Filling of Orbitals in Atom',NULL,'1','mcq','Building up','Movement','Feasibility','Control','NEW','11-061','11-061',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(22003,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): Inside an atom, electrons first occupy the lowest energy orbital available to them and enter into higher energy orbitals only after the lower energy orbitals are filled.\n\n Reason(R): In the ground state of the atoms, the orbitals are filled in order of their increasing energies according to aufbau principle.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"The aufbau principle states: In the ground state of the atoms, the orbitals are filled in order of their increasing energies. In other words, electrons first occupy the lowest energy orbital available to them and enter into higher energy orbitals only after the lower energy orbitals are filled."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.62; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Filling of Orbitals in Atom',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-062','11-062',NULL),
(22004,'chemistry','The correct order of increasing energy of atomic orbital is _______.','3s, 3p, 4s, 3d, 4p','"According to Aufbau Principle, following order of energies of the orbitals is extremely useful:\n  1s, 2s, 2p, 3s, 3p, 4s, 3d, 4p, 5s, 4d, 5p, 4f, 5d, 6p, 7s…"\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.62; Paragraph 2, 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Filling of Orbitals in Atom',NULL,'2','mcq','3s, 3p, 4s, 3d, 4p','3s, 3p, 3d, 4s 4p','3s, 3p, 3d, 4p, 4s','3s, 4s, 3d, 4p, 3p','NEW','11-062','11-062',NULL),
(22005,'chemistry','The number of electrons to be filled in various orbitals is restricted by the exclusion principle, given by _______.','Wolfgang Pauli','"The number of electrons to be filled in various orbitals is restricted by the exclusion principle, given by the Austrian scientist Wolfgang Pauli (1926)."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.62; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Filling of Orbitals in Atom',NULL,'1','mcq','Wolfgang Pauli','Bohr','Rutherford','Hund','NEW','11-062','11-062',NULL),
(22006,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): Only two electrons may exist in the same orbital and these electrons must have opposite spin.\n\n Reason(R): According to Pauli Exclusion principle, no two electrons in an atom can have the same set of four quantum numbers.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"According to Pauli Exclusion principle, no two electrons in an atom can have the same set of four quantum numbers. Pauli exclusion principle can also be stated as: Only two electrons may exist in the same orbital and these electrons must have opposite spin."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.62; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Filling of Orbitals in Atom',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-062','11-062',NULL),
(22007,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): Only two electrons may exist in the same orbital and these electrons must have opposite spin. \n\n Reason(R): The two electrons can have the same value of three quantum numbers n, l and mₗ, but must have the opposite spin quantum number.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"Pauli exclusion principle can also be stated as: Only two electrons may exist in the same orbital and these electrons must have opposite spin. This means that the two electrons can have the same value of three quantum numbersn, l and mₗ, but must have the opposite spin quantum number."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.62; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Filling of Orbitals in Atom',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-062','11-062',NULL),
(22008,'chemistry','The maximum number of electrons in the shell with principal quantum number n is equal to _______.','2n²','"The maximum number of electrons in the shell with principal quantum number n is equal to 2n²."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.62; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Filling of Orbitals in Atom',NULL,'1','mcq','2n','n²','2n²','n-1','NEW','11-062','11-062',NULL),
(22009,'chemistry','Select the correct statements regarding Hund\'s principle:\n \n (i) This rule deals with the filling of electrons into the orbitals belonging to the same subshell (that is, orbitals of equal energy, called degenerate orbitals).\n (ii) Pairing of electrons in the orbitals belonging to the same subshell (p, d or f) does not take place until each orbital belonging to that subshell has got two electron each.\n (iii)Half filled and fully filled degenerate set of orbitals acquire extra stability due to their symmetry.\n (iv)Pairing of electrons in the orbitals belonging to the same subshell (p, d or f) does not take place until each orbital belonging to that subshell is singly occupied.\n (v)The pairing of electrons will start in the p orbital with the entry of 5th electron.\n (vi)The pairing of electrons will start in the p orbital with the entry of 4th electron.\n (vii)The pairing of electrons will start in the p orbital with the entry of 7th electron.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(i), (iii), (iv), (vi) only','"Hund’s Rule of Maximum Multiplicity:\n This rule deals with the filling of electrons into the orbitals belonging to the same subshell (that is, orbitals of equal energy, called degenerate orbitals). It states: pairing of electrons in the orbitals belonging to the same subshell (p, d or f) does not take place until each orbital belonging to that subshell has got one electron each i.e., it is singly occupied. Since there are three p, five d and seven f orbitals, therefore, the pairing of electrons will start in the p, d and f orbitals with the entry of 4th, 6th and 8th electron, respectively. It has been observed that half filled and fully filled degenerate set of orbitals acquire extra stability due to their symmetry."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.63; Paragraph 1, 2)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Filling of Orbitals in Atom',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (iv), (vi) only','(i), (iii), (iv), (vi) only','(ii), (iii), (v) only','(ii), (iv), (v) only','NEW','11-062','11-062',NULL),
(22101,'chemistry','Select the incorrect statements regarding electronic configuration of atoms:\n \n (a) The distribution of electrons into orbitals of an atom is called its electronic configuration. \n (b) The electronic configuration of different atoms can be represented in two ways. For example :\n (i) sᵃ pᵇ dᶜ ...... notation\n (ii) Orbital diagram \n (c) In the orbital Diagram, each orbital of the subshell is represented by a box and the electron is represented by an arrow (↑) showing a positive spin or an arrow (↓) showing a negative spin. \n (d) The advantage first notation over the second is that it represents all the four quantum numbers. \n (e) The hydrogen atom has only one electron which goes in the orbital with the lowest energy, namely 2s. \n (f) The electrons in the completely filled shells are known as core electrons and the electrons that are added to the electronic shell with the highest principal quantum number are called valence electrons.\n (g) In potassium (K) and calcium (Ca), the 4s orbital, being higher in energy than the 3d orbitals, is occupied by one and two electrons respectively.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:\n Refer the below diagram for (b) ii, Orbital diagram(ne201)','(d), (e) and (g)','Electronic Configuration of Atoms: \nThe distribution of electrons into orbitals of an atom is called its electronic configuration. The electronic configuration of different atoms can be represented in two ways. For example :\n(i) sᵃ pᵇ dᶜ ...... notation\n(ii) Orbital diagram\nIn the first notation, the subshell is represented by the respective letter symbol and the number of electrons present in the subshell is depicted, as the super script, like a, b, c, ... etc. The similar subshell represented for different shells is differentiated by writing the principal quantum number before the respective subshell. In the second notation each orbital of the subshell is represented by a box and the electron is represented by an arrow (↑) showing a positive spin or an arrow (↓) showing a negative spin. The advantage of second notation over the first is that it represents all the four quantum numbers. \n\nThe hydrogen atom has only one electron which goes in the orbital with the lowest energy, namely 1s. The electronic configuration of the hydrogen atom is 1s¹ meaning that it has one electron in the 1s orbital. The second electron in helium (He) can also occupy the 1s orbital. Its configuration is, therefore, 1s². As mentioned above, the two electrons differ from each other with opposite spin, as can be seen from the below orbital diagram.\n\nThe third electron of lithium (Li) is not allowed in the 1s orbital because of Pauli exclusion principle. It, therefore, takes the next available choice, namely the 2s orbital. The electronic configuration of Li is 1s² 2s¹. The 2s orbital can accommodate one more electron. The configuration of beryllium (Be) atom is, therefore, 1s² 2s². \nThe electrons in the completely filled shells are known as core electrons and the electrons that are added to the electronic shell with the highest principal quantum number are called valence electrons. In potassium (K) and calcium (Ca), the 4s orbital, being lower in energy than the 3d orbitals, is occupied by one and two electrons respectively.\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.63; Paragraph 3-7, Page no.64; Paragraph 1\nHydrogen and Helium orbital diagram(ne201)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Electronic Configuration of Atoms',NULL,'3','mcq','(c), (d), (e), and (f)','(a), (b) and (d)','(d), (e) and (g)','(b), (c), (f) and (g)','NEW','11-063','11-063','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/22101q.png'),
(22102,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): The electronic configuration of the elements from sodium to argon can be written as (Na, [Ne] 3s¹) to (Ar, [Ne] 3s² 3p⁶).\n\n Reason(R): The electronic configuration of an element in a certain period can be written as the continual of the electronic configuration of the Nobel gas of the previous period.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"The electronic configuration of the elements sodium (Na, 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹) to argon (Ar,1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶), follow exactly the same pattern as the elements from lithium to neon with the difference that the 3s and 3p orbitals are getting filled now. This process can be simplified if we represent the total number of electrons in the first two shells by the name of element neon (Ne). The electronic configuration of the elements from sodium to argon can be written as (Na, [Ne] 3s¹) to (Ar, [Ne] 3s² 3p⁶)."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.63; Paragraph 9','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Electronic Configuration of Atoms',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both assertion and the statement of reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-063','11-063',NULL),
(22103,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): In the electronic configuration of the elements from sodium (Na, [Ne] 3s¹) to argon (Ar, [Ne] 3s² 3p⁶), the electrons in Ne are the core electrons and the electrons from Na to Ar are the valence electrons.\n \nReason(R): The electrons in the completely filled shells are known as core electrons and the electrons in the valence shell are called valence electrons. Since Ne represents the electronic configuration of inner filled shells of 3rd period elements, the above assertion is justified.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"The electrons in the completely filled shells are known as core electrons and the electrons that are added to the electronic shell with the highest principal quantum number are called valence electrons. For example, the electronic configuration of the elements from sodium to argon can be written as (Na, [Ne] 3s¹) to (Ar, [Ne] 3s² 3p⁶). Thus, the electrons in Ne represents core electrons and the electrons from Na to Ar are the valence electrons."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.64; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Electronic Configuration of Atoms',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both assertion and the statement of reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-064','11-064',NULL),
(22104,'chemistry','Match the electronic configuration with respective elements: \n (1) Boron (a) 1s² 2s² 2p⁴ \n (2) Nitrogen (b) 1s² 2s² 2p¹ \n (3) Oxygen (c) 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ \n (4) Carbon (d) 1s² 2s² 2p³ \n (5) Neon (e) 1s² 2s² 2p² \n (6) Fluorine (f) 1s² 2s² 2p⁵\n \n Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','1-b, 2-d, 3-a, 4-e, 5-c, 6-f','"In the next six elements—boron (B, 1s² 2s² 2p¹), carbon (C, 1s² 2s² 2p²), nitrogen (N, 1s² 2s² 2p³), oxygen (O, 1s² 2s² 2p⁴), fluorine (F, 1s² 2s² 2p⁵) and neon (Ne, 1s² 2s² 2p⁶), the 2p orbitals get progressively filled. This process is completed with the neon atom."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.63; Paragraph 8','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Electronic Configuration of Atoms',NULL,'3','mcq','1-b, 2-d, 3-a, 4-e, 5-c, 6-f','1-f, 2-d, 3-a, 4-e, 5-c, 6-b','1-b, 2-e, 3-d, 4-a, 5-c, 6-f','1-c, 2-d, 3-e, 4-a, 5-b, 6-f','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22105,'chemistry','The electronic configuration of the element sodium is _______.','1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹','"The electronic configuration of the element sodium is 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.63; Paragraph 9','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Electronic Configuration of Atoms',NULL,'1','mcq','1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p²','1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p¹','1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s²','1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22106,'chemistry','The electronic configuration of the element argon is _______.','1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶','"The electronic configuration of the element argon is 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.63; Paragraph 9','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Electronic Configuration of Atoms',NULL,'1','mcq','1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶','1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s³ 3p⁵','1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹ 3p⁷','1s² 2s² 2p⁷ 3s¹ 3p⁶','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22107,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): Chromium and copper have five and ten electrons in 3d orbitals rather than four and nine as their position would have indicated with two-electrons in the 4s orbital. \n\n Reason(R): Fully filled orbitals and half-filled orbitals have extra stability (that is, lower energy). Thus p³, p⁶, d⁵, d¹⁰,f⁷, f¹⁴ etc. configurations, which are either half-filled or fully filled, are more stable.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"Chromium and copper have five and ten electrons in 3d orbitals rather than four and nine as their position would have indicated with two-electrons in the 4s orbital. The reason is that fully filled orbitals and half-filled orbitals have extra stability (that is, lower energy). Thus p³, p⁶, d⁵, d¹⁰,f⁷, f¹⁴ etc. configurations, which are either half-filled or fully filled, are more stable. Chromium and copper therefore adopt the d⁵ and d¹⁰ configuration."\n"It has been found that there is extra stability associated with these electronic configurations."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.64; Paragraph 2, 5)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Electronic Configuration of Atoms',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-064','11-064',NULL),
(22108,'chemistry','Which of the following statements in relation to the energies of hydrogen atom orbitals is correct?\n \n (i) 3s orbital is lower in energy than 3p orbital.\n (ii)3p orbital is lower in energy than 3d orbital.\n (iii)3s and 3p orbitals are of lower energy than 3d orbital.\n (iv)3s, 3p and 3d orbitals all have the same energy.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (iv)','"The energy of an electron in a hydrogen atom is determined solely by the principal quantum number. Thus the energy of the orbitals in hydrogen atom increases as follows:\n  1s < 2s = 2p < 3s = 3p = 3d <4s = 4p = 4d = 4f"\n"The energy of an electron in a multi-electron atom, unlike that of the hydrogen atom, depends not only on its principal quantum number (shell), but also on its azimuthal quantum number (subshell)."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.60; Paragraph 1, 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Electronic Configuration of Atoms',NULL,'2','mcq','Only (i)','Only (ii)','Only (iii)','Only (iv)','NEW','11-060','11-060',NULL),
(22109,'chemistry','The elements in the periodic table after uranium (U) are:\n \n (i) Radioactive\n (ii) Short-lived \n (iii) Abundant in nature\n (iv) Produced artificially\n (v) Highly stable\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(i), (ii), and (iv)','"The elements after uranium (U) are all short-lived and all of them are produced artificially."\nThese elements are short lived because of the phenomenon of radioactivity. These elements, being radioactive, emit radiations; the emission of α-rays and β-rays causes the change in the proton number of the element. The change in proton number causes the change of element itself. Thus, new element is born after α-rays and β-rays emission.\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.64; Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Electronic Configuration of Atoms',NULL,'1','mcq','(i), (ii), and (iv)','(ii), (iii), and (v)','(i), and (v)','(i), (iii), and (iv)','NEW','11-064','11-064',NULL),
(22110,'chemistry','Select the correct statements regarding electronic configurations of elements:\n \n (i) The ground state electronic configuration of the atom of an element always corresponds to the state of the lowest total electronic energy.\n (ii)The valence electronic configurations of Cr and Cu are 3d⁵ 4s¹ and 3d¹⁰ 4s¹ respectively and not 3d⁴ 4s² and 3d⁹ 4s².\n (iii)The 4p orbital, being lower in energy than the 3d orbital, is filled first.\n (iv)The filling of the 4p orbital starts at gallium (Ga) and is completed at krypton (Kr).\n (v)From rubidium (Rb) to xenon (Xe), the pattern of filling the 5s, 4d and 5p orbitals is quite different from that of 4s, 3d and 4p orbitals as discussed above.\n (vi)In certain elements, where the two subshells (4s and 3d) differ slightly in their energies, an electron shifts from a subshell of lower energy (4s) to a subshell of higher energy (3d), provided such a shift results in all orbitals of the subshell of higher energy getting either completely filled or half filled.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(i), (ii), (iv), and (vi)','"A new pattern is followed beginning with scandium (Sc). The 3d orbital, being lower in energy than the 4p orbital, is filled first. Consequently, in the next ten elements, scandium (Sc), titanium (Ti), vanadium (V), chromium (Cr), manganese (Mn), iron (Fe), cobalt (Co), nickel (Ni), copper (Cu) and zinc (Zn), the five 3d orbitals are progressively occupied."\n"With the saturation of the 3d orbitals, the filling of the 4p orbital starts at gallium (Ga) and is completed at krypton (Kr). In the next eighteen elements from rubidium (Rb) to xenon (Xe), the pattern of filling the 5s, 4d and 5p orbitals are similar to that of 4s, 3d and 4p orbitals as discussed above."\n"The ground state electronic configuration of the atom of an element always corresponds to the state of the lowest total electronic energy. The electronic configurations of most of the atoms follow the basic rules of electron distribution within an atom. However, in certain elements such as Cu, or Cr, where the two subshells (4s and 3d) differ slightly in their energies, an electron shifts from a subshell of lower energy (4s) to a subshell of higher energy (3d), provided such a shift results in all orbitals of the subshell of higher energy getting either completely filled or half filled. The valence electronic configurations of Cr and Cu, therefore, are 3d⁵ 4s¹ and 3d¹⁰ 4s¹ respectively and not 3d⁴ 4s² and 3d⁹ 4s². It has been found that there is extra stability associated with these electronic configurations."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.64; Paragraph 2, 3, 5)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Electronic Configuration of Atoms',NULL,'2','mcq','(ii), (iv),and (v)','(i), (ii),and (iii)','(i), (ii), (iv), and (vi)','(iii), (v), and (vi)','NEW','11-064','11-064',NULL),
(22111,'chemistry','Match the electronic configuration with respective elements: \n \n (1) Cr (a) [Xe] 4f⁷ 5d¹ 6s² \n (2) Cu (b) [Xe] 4f¹⁴ 5d⁹ 6s¹ \n (3) Ag (c) [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹ \n (4) Gd (d) [Kr] 4d¹⁰ 5s¹ \n (5) Pt (e) [Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹\n \n Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','1-c, 2-e, 3-d, 4-a, 5-b','"The tabulated electronic configurations for crhromium(Cr), copper(Cu), silver(Ag), gadolinium(Gd), and platinium(Pt) are  [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹, [Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹, [Kr] 4d¹⁰ 5s¹, 4f⁷ 5d¹ 6s², [Xe] 4f¹⁴ 5d⁹ 6s¹ respectively."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Pages no.66,67; Table 2.6','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Quantum mechanical model of atom - Electronic Configuration of Atoms',NULL,'3','mcq','1-c, 2-e, 3-b, 4-a, 5-d','1-e, 2-a, 3-d, 4-c, 5-b','1-c, 2-e, 3-d, 4-a, 5-b','1-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-e, 5-b','NEW','11-064','11-064',NULL),
(22112,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): The completely filled or half filled subshells have symmetrical distribution of electrons in them and are therefore more stable. \n \n Reason(R): Electrons in the same subshell have equal energy but different spatial distribution. Consequently, their shielding of one another is relatively small and the electrons are more strongly attracted by the nucleus.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','"Causes of Stability of Completely Filled and Half-filled Subshells:\n → Symmetrical distribution of electrons:\n It is well known that symmetry leads to stability. The completely filled or half filled subshells have symmetrical distribution of electrons in them and are therefore more stable. Electrons in the same subshell have equal energy but different spatial distribution. Consequently, their shielding of one another is relatively small and the electrons are more strongly attracted by the nucleus."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.65; Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Causes of Stability of Completely Filled and Half-filled Subshells',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-064','11-064',NULL),
(22113,'chemistry','Select the correct statements regarding stability of completely filled and half-filled orbitals:\n \n (a) The stabilizing effect arises whenever two or more electrons with the same spin are present in the degenerate orbitals of a subshell.\n (b) The stabilizing effect arises whenever two or more electrons with the opposite spin are present in the degenerate orbitals of a subshell.\n (c) These electrons tend to exchange their positions and the energy released due to this exchange is called exchange energy.\n (d) The exchange energy is at the basis of Hund\'s rule that electrons which enter orbitals of equal energy have opposite spins as far as possible.\n (e) The exchange energy is at the basis of Hund’s rule that electrons which enter orbitals of equal energy have parallel spins as far as possible.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(a), (c) and (e)','"Causes of Stability of Completely Filled and Half-filled Subshells:\n → Exchange Energy: \n The stabilizing effect arises whenever two or more electrons with the same spin are present in the degenerate orbitals of a subshell. These electrons tend to exchange their positions and the energy released due to this exchange is called exchange energy. The number of exchanges that can take place is maximum when the subshell is either half filled or completely filled. As a result the exchange energy is maximum and so is the stability. The exchange energy is at the basis of Hund’s rule that electrons which enter orbitals of equal energy have parallel spins as far as possible."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.65; Paragraph 2, 3)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Causes of Stability of Completely Filled and Half-filled Subshells',NULL,'2','mcq','(a), (b) and (e)','(a), (c) and (e)','(b), (c) and (d)','(b) and (d)','NEW','11-065','11-065',NULL),
(22114,'chemistry','The extra stability of half-filled and completely filled subshell is due to:\n \n (i) Relatively small shielding.\n (ii) Relatively large shielding.\n (iii)Smaller coulombic repulsion energy.\n (iv)Larger coulombic repulsion energy.\n (v)Smaller exchange energy.\n (vi)Larger exchange energy.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(i), (iii), and (vi)','"Causes of Stability of Completely Filled and Half-filled Subshells:\n The extra stability of half-filled and completely filled subshell is due to: \n  (i) relatively small shielding, \n  (ii) smaller coulombic repulsion energy, and \n  (iii) larger exchange energy."\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.65; Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Causes of Stability of Completely Filled and Half-filled Subshells',NULL,'2','mcq','(i), (iv), and (v)','(ii), (iv), and (v)','(ii) and (iii)','(i), (iii), and (vi)','NEW','11-065','11-065',NULL),
(22301,'chemistry','Calculate the number of electrons which will together weigh one gram.','1.097 x 10²⁷ electrons per gram','"9.108 × 10⁻²⁸ g = Mass of 1 electron \n 1g = 1/ 9.108 x 10⁻²⁸ \n      = 1.097 x 10²⁷ electrons per gram"\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 69; Exercise question 2.1(i)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','1.097 x 10²⁸ electrons per gram','1.097 x 10²⁹ electrons per gram','1.097 x 10³⁰ electrons per gram','1.097 x 10²⁷ electrons per gram','NEW','11-033','11-033',NULL),
(22302,'chemistry','Calculate the mass and charge of one mole of electrons.','5.486 × 10⁻⁷ kg and 9.647 × 10⁴ C','"Mass of 1 mole of electrons:\n Mass of 1 electron = 9.108 × 10⁻³¹ kg\n Mass of I mole of electrons = 9.108 × 10⁻³¹ kg × 6.023 × 10²³ = 5.486 × 10⁻⁷ kg \n Charge of 1 mole of electrons:\n Charge of one electron = 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ C (C = coulomb)\n Charge of 1 mole of electrons = 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ C × 6.023 × 10²³ = 9.647 × 10⁴ C"\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 69; Exercise question 2.1(ii)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','5.486 × 10⁻⁸ kg and 9.647 × 10⁴ C','5.486 × 10⁻⁷ kg and 9.647 × 10⁵ C','5.486 × 10⁻⁸ kg and 9.647 × 10⁵ C','5.486 × 10⁻⁷ kg and 9.647 × 10⁴ C','NEW','11-033','11-033',NULL),
(22303,'chemistry','Calculate the total number of electrons present in one mole of methane.','6.023 × 10²⁴ electrons','"No. of electrons in a neutral atom = No. of protons of that atom = atomic no. of an atom\n Total number of electrons present in 1 methane (CH₄) molecule = atomic no. of C + 4(atomic no. of H) = 6 + 4 = 10\n Total number of electrons resent in 1 mole of methane =10 × 6.023 × 10²³ = 6.023 × 10²⁴ electrons"\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 69; Exercise question 2.2(i)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','6.023 × 10²³ electrons','6.023 × 10²² electrons','6.023 × 10²⁴ electrons','6.023 × 10²⁵ electrons','NEW','11-033','11-033',NULL),
(22304,'chemistry','Find (a) the total number and (b) the total mass of neutrons in 7 mg of ¹⁴C. \n (Assume that mass of a neutron = 1.675 × 10⁻²⁷ kg)','(a) 2.408 × 10²¹ neutrons \n(b) 4.035 × 10⁻⁶ kg','"The number of neutrons in an atom = mass number - atomic number.\n In case of ¹⁴C, no. of neutrons in one atom = 14 − 6 = 8\n\n (a) The number of neutrons present in 7 mg of ¹⁴C:\n No. of neutrons in 1 atom of ¹⁴C = 8 neutrons\n No. of neutrons in 1 mole of ¹⁴C = No.of neutons in 14 g of ¹⁴C = 8 x 6.023 x 10²³ neutrons\n No. of neutrons in 1 gram of ¹⁴C = (8 x 6.023 x 10²³) / 14 \n No. of neutrons in 1 mg of ¹⁴C = (8 x 6.023 x 10²³ x 10⁻³) / 14 \n No. of neutrons in 7 mg of ¹⁴C = (7 x 8 x 6.023 x 10²³ x 10⁻³) / 14 = 2.408 × 10²¹ neutrons \n\n (b) Total mass of neutrons in 7 mg of C¹⁴: \n Mass of 1 neutron = 1.675 × 10⁻²⁷ kg\n Mass of neutrons in 7 mg of C¹⁴ = Mass of 1 neutron x Total number of neutrons present in 7 mg of C¹⁴\n                                                    = 2.408 × 10²¹ × 1.675 × 10⁻²⁷ kg = 4.035 × 10⁻⁶ kg"\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 69; Exercise question 2.2(ii)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','(a) 2.408 × 10²² neutrons\n(b) 4.035 × 10⁻⁶ kg','(a) 2.408 × 10²² neutrons\n(b) 4.035 × 10⁻⁷ kg','(a) 2.408 × 10²¹ neutrons\n(b) 4.035 × 10⁻⁷ kg','(a) 2.408 × 10²¹ neutrons \n(b) 4.035 × 10⁻⁶ kg','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(22305,'chemistry','Find (a) the total number and (b) the total mass of protons in 34 mg of NH₃ at STP.','(a) 1.2044 × 10²² protons\n(b) 2.0138 × 10⁻⁵ kg','"The number of protons in an atom = atomic number.\n In case of NH₃, no. of protons in one molecule = atomic no. of N + 3(atomic no. of H) = 7 + 3 = 10\n\n (a) The number of protons present in 34 mg of NH₃: \n No. of protons in 1 molecule of NH₃ = 10 protons\n No. of protons in 1 mole of NH₃ = No.of protons in 17 g of NH₃ = 10 x 6.023 x 10²³ protons\n No. of protons in 1 gram of NH₃ = (10 x 6.023 x 10²³) / 17 \n No. of protons in 1 mg of NH₃ = (10 x 6.023 x 10²³ x 10⁻³) / 17 \n No. of protons in 34 mg of NH₃ = (34 x 10 x 6.023 x 10²³ x 10⁻³) / 17 = 1.2044 × 10²² protons\n\n (b) Total mass of protons in 34 mg of NH₃ at STP: \n Mass of 1 proton = 1.6726 × 10⁻²⁷ kg\n Mass of protons in 34 mg of NH₃ = Mass of 1 proton x Total number of protons present in 34 mg of NH₃\n                                                      = 1.204 × 10²² × 1.6726 × 10⁻²⁷ kg = 2.0138 × 10⁻⁵ kg"\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 69; Exercise question 2.2(iii)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','(a) 1.2044 × 10²¹ protons\n(b) 2.0138 × 10⁻⁶ kg','(a) 1.2044 × 10²² protons\n(b) 2.0138 × 10⁻⁶ kg','(a) 1.2044 × 10²¹ protons\n(b) 2.0138 × 10⁻⁵ kg','(a) 1.2044 × 10²² protons\n(b) 2.0138 × 10⁻⁵ kg','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(22306,'chemistry','Yellow light emitted from a sodium lamp has a wavelength (λ) of 580 nm. Calculate the frequency (ν) and wavenumber (ṽ) of the yellow light.','5.17 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹ and 1.72 × 10⁶ m⁻¹','"The wavelength of yellow light, λ = 580 nm = 580 × 10⁻⁹ m \n The frequency of yellow light, v = c / λ = 3.0 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹ / 580 × 10⁻⁹ m = 5.17 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹ \n The wave number of yellow light, ṽ = 1 / λ = 1 / 580 × 10⁻⁹ m = 1.72 × 10⁶ m⁻¹ " \nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 69; Exercise question 2.5','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','5.17 × 10¹⁵ s⁻¹ and 1.72 × 10⁷ m⁻¹','5.17 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹ and 1.72 × 10⁷ m⁻¹','5.17 × 10¹⁵ s⁻¹ and 1.72 × 10⁶ m⁻¹','5.17 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹ and 1.72 × 10⁶ m⁻¹','NEW','11-038,39','11-038,39',NULL),
(22307,'chemistry','Find energy of each of the photons which correspond to light of frequency 3 × 10¹⁵ Hz.','1.988 × 10⁻¹⁸ J','"The energy of photon is:\n E = hν = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js × 3 × 10¹⁵ ms⁻¹ = 1.988 × 10⁻¹⁸ J"   \nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 69; Exercise question 2.6(i)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','1.988 × 10⁻¹⁷ J','1.988 × 10⁻¹⁹ J','1.988 × 10⁻¹⁸ J','1.988 × 10⁻²⁰ J','NEW','11-041','11-041',NULL),
(22308,'chemistry','Find energy of each of the photons which have wavelength of 0.50 Å.','3.98 × 10⁻¹⁵ J','"The energy of photon is: \n E = hc / λ = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js x 3.0 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹) / 0.50 x 10⁻¹⁰ m = 3.98 × 10⁻¹⁵ J"\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 69; Exercise question 2.6(ii)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','3.98 × 10⁻¹⁶ J','3.98 × 10⁻¹⁷ J','3.98 × 10⁻¹⁸ J','3.98 × 10⁻¹⁵ J','NEW','11-041','11-041',NULL),
(22309,'chemistry','Calculate the frequency and wavelength of a light wave whose period is 2.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ s.','5 x 10⁹ s⁻¹ and 6 x 10⁻² m','"The frequency of the light wave with period, T is:\n ν = 1 / T = 1 / 2 x 10⁻¹⁰ = 5 x 10⁹ s⁻¹\nThe wavelength of light wave is:\n λ = c / v = 3.0 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹ / 5 x 10⁹ s⁻¹ = 6 x 10⁻² m"   \nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 69; Exercise question 2.7','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','5 x 10⁹ s⁻¹ and 6 x 10⁻³ m','5 x 10¹⁰ s⁻¹ and 6 x 10⁻² m','5 x 10¹⁰ s⁻¹ and 6 x 10⁻³ m','5 x 10⁹ s⁻¹ and 6 x 10⁻² m','NEW','11-038','11-038',NULL),
(22310,'chemistry','What is the number of photons of light with a wavelength of 4000 pm that provide 1J of energy?','2.01 × 10¹⁶ photons','"The wavelength of light is, λ = 4000 pm or 4000 × 10⁻¹² m = 4 × 10⁻⁹ m\n The energy one photon is, E = hc / λ = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js)(3.0 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹) / 4 × 10⁻⁹ m = 4.97 × 10⁻¹⁷ J\n 4.97 × 10⁻¹⁷ J of E is provided by = 1 photon\n 1 J of energy is provided by = 1 / 4.97 × 10⁻¹⁷ = 2.01 × 10¹⁶ photons"   \nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 69; Exercise question 2.8','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','2.01 × 10¹⁶ photons','4.01 × 10¹⁶ photons','0.01 × 10¹⁶ photons','1.01 × 10¹⁶ photons','NEW','11-041','11-041',NULL),
(22311,'chemistry','Electromagnetic radiation of wavelength 242 nm is just sufficient to ionise the sodium atom. Calculate the ionisation energy of sodium in kJ mol⁻¹.','494 kJ','"The ionization energy of sodium will be equal to the energy possessed by the radiation of wavelength 242 nm. The ionization energy of 1 Na atom is,\n Eᵢₒₙ = hc / λ = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js)(3.0 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹) / 242 x 10⁻⁹ m = 8.21 × 10⁻¹⁹ J \n The ionization energy per mole of Na is = 8.21 × 10⁻¹⁹ J x 6.023 × 10²³ = 494000 J = 494 kJ"   \nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 69; Exercise question 2.10','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','4940 kJ','49.4 kJ','49400 kJ','494 kJ','NEW','11-041','11-041',NULL),
(22312,'chemistry','A 25 watt bulb emits monochromatic yellow light of wavelength of 0.57 μm. Calculate the rate of emission of quanta per second.','7.18 × 10¹⁹ photons /s','"The energy of one photon of light of wavelength of 0.57 μm is, \n  E = hc / λ = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js)(3.0 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹) / 0.57 x 10⁻⁶ m = 3.487 × 10⁻¹⁹ J \n 25 watt power corresponds to 25 J per second\n 3.487 × 10⁻¹⁹ J energy is emitted by = 1 photon per second\n 1 J energy is emitted by = (1 / 3.487 × 10⁻¹⁹) photons per second\n 25 J energy is emitted by = (25 x 1) / 3.487 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 7.24 × 10¹⁹ photons per second\n Thus, the rate of emission of quanta per second = 7.24 × 10¹⁹ photons /s"  \nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 69; Exercise question 2.11','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','7.17 × 10²⁰ photons /s','6.17 × 10¹⁹ photons /s','7.18 × 10¹⁹ photons /s','7.24 × 10¹⁹ photons /s','NEW','11-041','11-041',NULL),
(22313,'chemistry','Electrons are emitted with zero velocity from a metal surface when it is exposed to radiation of wavelength 6800 Å. Calculate threshold frequency (v₀) and work function (W₀) of the metal.','4.41 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹ and 2.92 × 10⁻¹⁹ J','"Since, K.E of emitted electrons is zero, the work function of the metal will be = the energy of the striking radiation. \n Also, the threshold frequency of the metal will also be = the frequency of striking radiation.\n The radiation frequency, v = c / λ = 3.0 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹ / 6800 x 10⁻¹⁰ m = 4.41 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹\n Since, v = v₀ \n Therefore, threshold frequency, v₀ = 4.41 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹\n The radiation energy, E = hν₀ = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js × 4.41 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹ = 2.92 × 10⁻¹⁹ J\n Therefore, the work function, W₀ = 2.92 × 10⁻¹⁹ J"  \nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 69; Exercise question 2.12','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','4.41 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹ and 2.92 × 10⁻¹⁹ J','4.41 × 10¹⁵ s⁻¹ and 2.92 × 10⁻¹⁹ J','4.41 × 10¹⁵ s⁻¹ and 2.92 × 10⁻¹⁸ J','4.41 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹ and 2.92 × 10⁻¹⁸ J','NEW','11-042,43','11-042,43',NULL),
(22314,'chemistry','What is the wavelength of light emitted when the electron in a hydrogen atom undergoes transition from an energy level with n = 4 to an energy level with n = 2?','486 nm','"The expression for the wavelength of emitted or absorbed radiation as a result of transition of electrons between different energy levels is,\n 1 / λ = R [(1 / n₁²) - (1 / n₂²)] \n Substitute the relevant values in the above expression, \n 1 / λ = 109677 cm⁻¹ × [(1 / 2²) - (1 / 4²)] = 109677 cm⁻¹ × [(1 / 4) - (1 / 16)] = 109677 cm⁻¹ × [3 / 16] = 20,564.44 cm⁻¹\n λ = 1 / 20,564.44 cm⁻¹ = 4.86 × 10⁻⁵ cm = 4.86 × 10⁻⁷ m = 486 × 10⁻⁹ m = 486 nm \n The line belongs to bluish green colour."    \nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 69; Exercise question 2.13','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','406 nm','586 nm','386 nm','486 nm','NEW','11-045','11-045',NULL),
(22315,'chemistry','What is the maximum number of emission lines when the excited electron of a H atom in n = 6 drops to the ground state?','15','"The maximum number of emission lines when the excited electron of a H atom in n = 6 drops to the ground state (n = 1) is calculated from the expression:\n\n(n₂ - n₁)(n₂ - n₁ + 1) / 2 = (6 - 1)(6 - 1 + 1) / 2 = (5 x 6) / 2 = 15 lines"  \n  \nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 70; Exercise question 2.15','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','14','11','30','15','NEW','11-045','11-045',NULL),
(22316,'chemistry','The energy associated with the first orbit in the hydrogen atom is –2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J atom⁻¹. What is the energy associated with the fifth orbit?','−8.68 × 10⁻²⁰ J','"The expression for the energy associated with nth orbit in hydrogen atom is,\n \nEₙ = –2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J atom⁻¹ / n² \n \nTherefore, energy associated with fifth orbit is, \n \nE₅ = –2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J atom⁻¹ / 5² = −8.68 × 10⁻²⁰ J"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 70; Exercise question 2.16(i)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','−8.68 × 10⁻²¹ J','−8.68 × 10²⁰ J','8.68 × 10⁻²⁰ J','−8.68 × 10⁻²⁰ J','NEW','11-047','11-047',NULL),
(22317,'chemistry','Calculate the radius of Bohr’s fifth orbit for hydrogen atom.','1.3225 nm','"The radius of Bohr\'s nth orbit is given by the expression, \n\nrₙ = 0.0529 n² nm \n\nFor fifth orbit, the radius is,\n\nr₅ = 0.0529 × 5² nm = 1.3225 nm"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 70; Exercise question 2.16(ii)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','0.13225 nm','1.3225 nm','1.3225 mm','13.225 nm','NEW','11-047','11-047',NULL),
(22318,'chemistry','What is the energy in joules, required to shift the electron of the hydrogen atom from the first Bohr orbit to the fifth Bohr orbit and what is the wavelength of the light emitted when the electron returns to the ground state? The ground state electron energy is -2.18 × 10⁻¹¹ ergs.','2.093 × 10⁻¹⁸ J and 9.49 × 10⁻⁶ cm','"Ground state (n = 1) energy is given by the expression,\n\nE₁ = −2.18 × 10⁻¹¹ ergs\n\nNow, energy of the 5ᵗʰ orbit (n = 5) is given by the expression,\n\nEₛ = E₁ / (5)² = −2.18 × 10⁻¹¹ ergs / 25 = −0.0872 × 10⁻¹¹ ergs \n\nEnergy required to shift the electron from the 1st to the 5ᵗʰ orbit is,\n\nE₁₋₅ = E₅ - E₁ = [−0.0872 - (-2.18)] × 10⁻¹¹ ergs = 2.0928 × 10⁻¹¹ ergs\n\nNow, 1 erg = 10⁻⁷ J ⇒ 2.0928 × 10⁻¹¹ ergs = 2.0928 × 10⁻¹⁸ J \n\nNow, the wavelength of the light emitted when the electron returns to the ground state is, \n\n1 / λ = R [(1 / 1²) - (1 / 5²)] \n\nwhere, R = 1.0973 × 10⁵ cm⁻¹\n\n1 / λ = 1.0973 × 10⁵ cm⁻¹ [(1/1) - (1/25)] = 1.0973 × 10⁵ cm⁻¹ [0.96] = 1.0534 × 10⁵ cm⁻¹\n \n⇒ λ = 9.49 × 10⁻⁶ cm"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 70; Exercise question 2.18','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','2.093 × 10⁻¹⁸ J and 9.49 × 10⁻⁶ cm','2.093 × 10⁻²⁰ J and 9.49 × 10⁻⁷ cm','2.093 × 10⁻¹⁵ J and 9.49 × 10⁻⁸ cm','2.093 × 10⁻¹⁷ J and 9.49 × 10⁻⁵ cm','NEW','11-047','11-047',NULL),
(22319,'chemistry','The electron energy in hydrogen atom is given by Eₙ = (-2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ ) / n² J. Calculate the energy required to remove an electron completely from the n = 2 orbit. What is the longest wavelength of light in cm that can be used to cause this transition?','5.45 × 10⁻¹⁹ J and 3.647 × 10⁻⁵ cm','"Energy of an electron in nth shell is given by exression,\n \nEₙ = (-2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸) / n² J \n\nEnergy of electron in 2nd orbit (n = 2) is,\n\nE₂ = (-2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸) / 2² J = −5.45 × 10⁻¹⁹ J\n\nWhen electron is ejected from the atom, it gets outside the influence of nucleus. Thus, energy of electron at ∞ becomes,\n\nEᵢₙբ = 0\n\nEnergy required to remove an electron completely from the 2nd orbit to ∞ is,\n\nE₂₋ᵢₙբ = Eᵢₙբ - E₂ = 0 − (−5.45 × 10⁻¹⁹ J) = 5.45 × 10⁻¹⁹ J\n\nNow, the longest wavelength of the light that can be used to cause this transition is calculated from the expression, \n\nΔE = hc / λ\n\n5.45 × 10⁻¹⁹ J = hc / λ \n\nor, λ = hc / ΔE\n\nλ = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js)(3.0×10⁸ ms⁻¹) / 5.45 × 10⁻¹⁹ J = 3.647 × 10⁻⁷ m = 3.647 × 10⁻⁵ cm"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 70; Exercise question 2.19','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','5.45 × 10⁻¹² J and 3.647 × 10⁻⁹ cm','5.45 × 10⁻¹⁷ J and 3.647 × 10⁻⁸ cm','5.45 × 10⁻¹⁸ J and 3.647 × 10⁻⁷ cm','5.45 × 10⁻¹⁹ J and 3.647 × 10⁻⁵ cm','NEW','11-047','11-047',NULL),
(22320,'chemistry','Calculate the wavelength of an electron moving with a velocity of 2.05 × 10⁷ ms⁻¹.','3.55 × 10⁻¹¹ m','"The wavelength of an electron is calculated from de-Broglie equation, \n\nλ = h / mv = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js) / (9.11 x 10⁻³¹ kg x 2.05 x 10⁷ ms⁻¹) = 3.55×10⁻¹¹ m"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 70; Exercise question 2.20','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','3.55 × 10⁻¹¹ m','3.55 × 10⁻⁹ m','3.55 × 10⁻¹² m','3.55 × 10⁻¹⁰ m','NEW','11-050','11-050',NULL),
(22321,'chemistry','The mass of an electron is 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg. If its K.E. is 3.0 × 10⁻²⁵ J, calculate its wavelength.','8.97 × 10⁻⁷ m','"Wavelenght and K.E. relation can be obtained using following two equations:\n \nλ = h / mv\n \nK.E. = 1/2 mv² \n \nRelating these two equations, we get the expression,\n \nλ = h / (2 m K.E.)¹/² \n \nλ = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js) / (2 x 9.11 x 10⁻³¹ kg x 3 x 10⁻²⁵ J) ¹/² = 8.97 × 10⁻⁷ m"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 70; Exercise question 2.21','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','8.97 × 10⁻⁷ m','8.97 × 10⁻⁸ cm','8.97 × 10⁻⁷ cm','8.97 × 10⁻⁸ m','NEW','11-042','11-042',NULL),
(22322,'chemistry','An electron is in one of the 3d orbitals.Give the possible values of n, l and mₗ for this electron.','n = 3\n      l = 2\n mₗ = -2, -1, 0, 1, 2','"For 3d orbital, values of n, l and mₗ are:\n\n n = 3, l = 2 and mₗ = -2, -1, 0, 1, 2"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 70; Exercise question 2.25','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','n = 3\n      l = 2\n mₗ = -2, -1, 0, 1, 2','n = 3, 2 \n      l = 2 \n mₗ = -2, -1, 0, 1, 2','n = 3 \n      l = 3\n mₗ = -3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3','n = 3\n      l = 3\n mₗ = -2, -1, 0, 1, 2','NEW','11-055','11-055',NULL),
(22323,'chemistry','An atom of an element contains 29 electrons and 35 neutrons. \n \n Deduce (i) the number of protons and (ii) the electronic configuration of the element.','(i) 29\n(ii) [Ar]¹⁸ 3d¹⁰ 4s¹','"(i) The number of protons is equal to the number of electrons which also corresponds to the atomic number. Therefore number of protons in the given atom is 29.\n\n (ii) The electronic configuration of the element will be [Ar]¹⁸ 3d¹⁰ 4s¹."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 70; Exercise question 2.26','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','(i) 30\n(ii) [Ar]¹⁸ 3d¹⁰ 4s¹','(i) 29 \n(ii) [Ar]¹⁸ 3d⁹ 4s²','(i) 29\n(ii) [Ar]¹⁸ 3d⁸ 4s³','(i) 29\n(ii) [Ar]¹⁸ 3d¹⁰ 4s¹','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(213203,'chemistry','Match the A, B, C, D shown in diagram(n2107)','A-1 unpaired electron accounts for -1 or +1 oxidation state, B-3 unpaired electrons account for +3 oxidation state, C-5 unpaired electrons accoubtfor +3 oxidation states, D-7 unpaired electrons account for +7 oxidation state','The correct match is A-1 unpaired electron accounts for -1 or +1 oxidation state, B-3 unpaired electrons account for +3 oxidation state, C-5 unpaired electrons accoubtfor +3 oxidation states, D-7 unpaired electrons account for +7 oxidation state\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 199 Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements Properties',NULL,'3','match','A,1 unpaired electron accounts for -1 or +1 oxidation state','B,3 unpaired electrons account for +3 oxidation state','C,5 unpaired electrons accoubtfor +3 oxidation states','D,7 unpaired electrons account for +7 oxidation state','NEW','12-199',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/match/213203q.png'),
(22324,'chemistry','What transition in the hydrogen spectrum would have the same wavelength as the Balmer transition n = 4 to n = 2 of He⁺ spectrum?','n₂ = 2 to n₁ = 1','"The expression for the wavelength of emitted or absorbed radiation as a result of transition of electrons between different energy levels of atoms of atomic no. = Z is,\n\n 1 / λ = R Z² [(1 / n₁²) - (1 / n₂²)] \n\nAccording to the question,\nλH = λHe⁺\n\nTherefore,\n1 / λ = Rₕ ZH² [(1 / n₁²) - (1 / n₂²)] = Rₕ ZHe⁺² [(1 / 2²) - (1 / 4²)] \n\nwhere, Rₕ = Rydberg constant\nn₁, n₂ = Principle quantum numbers \nZ = Atomic number ( 1 for H, 2 for He) \n\n1 x [(1 / n₁²) - (1 / n₂²)] = 4 x [(1 / 4) - (1 / 16)] \n[(1 / n₁²) - (1 / n₂²)] = 3 / 4 \n\nThe equality is achieved with n₁ = 1, and n₂ = 2. ​\n\nSince both sides of the equation are equal, the transition is indeed the first line 2→1 in the Lyman series of the hydrogen spectrum.\n\nHence, the transition of Lyman series in hydrogen spectrum have the same wavelength as Balmer transition n₂ = 4 to n₁ = 2 in He⁺ spectrum."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 71; Exercise question 2.33','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','n₂ = 4 to n₁ = 2','n₂ = 3 to n₁ = 2','n₂ = 2 to n₁ = 1','n₂ = 4 to n₁ = 1','NEW','11-045','11-045',NULL),
(22325,'chemistry','Calculate the energy required for the process:\n \n He⁺ (g) → He²⁺ (g) + e⁻\n \nThe ionization energy for the H atom in the ground state is 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J atom⁻¹.','8.72 × 10⁻¹⁸ J ion⁻¹','"Ionization in Hydrogen atom occurs when electron makes a transition from n = 1 to n = ∞. \n\nThe energy required for this proces is called the ionisation energy. Thus,\n\nI.E. = Eᵢₙբ - E₁\n\nNow, the energy associated with Bohr\'s orbit is, \nEₙ = -2.18 x 10⁻¹⁸ J x Z² / n²\n\nEnergy associated with 1st orbit will be,\nEₙ = -2.18 x 10⁻¹⁸ J x 1² / 1² = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J\n \nSince, Eᵢₙբ = 0,\n\nThe I.E. for H atom will be calculated to be,\n\nI.E. = Eᵢₙբ - E₁\n= 0 - (-2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J) = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J atom⁻¹\n\nFor He⁺, n = 1, Z = 2,\n\nTherefore, the energy required for the given process, \n\nHe⁺ (g) → He²⁺ (g) + e⁻, is,\nI.E. = 2.18 x 10⁻¹⁸ J x 2² / 1² = 8.72 x 10⁻¹⁸ J ion⁻¹\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 71; Exercise question 2.34','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','9.72 × 10⁻¹⁸ J ion⁻¹','7.72 × 10⁻¹⁸ J ion⁻¹','8.72 × 10⁻¹⁹ J ion⁻¹','8.72 × 10⁻¹⁸ J ion⁻¹','NEW','11-048','11-048',NULL),
(22326,'chemistry','If the diameter of a carbon atom is 0.15 nm. Calculate the number of carbon atoms which can be placed side by side in a straight line across length of scale of length 20 cm long.','1.33 x 10⁹','"The diameter of 1 C atom = 0.15 nm = 0.15 × 10⁻⁷ cm.\n\nThe number of Carbon atoms that can be placed in 1 cm length = 1 / (0.15 × 10⁻⁷).\n\nTherefore, In 20 cm length, 20 / (0.15 x 10⁻⁷) = 1.33 x 10⁹ carbon atoms can be placed."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 71; Exercise question 2.35','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','2.33 x 10¹⁰','1.33 x 10¹⁰','2.33 x 10⁹','1.33 x 10⁹','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22327,'chemistry','2 ×10⁸ atoms of carbon are arranged side by side. Calculate the radius of carbon atom if the length of this arrangement is 2.4 cm.','0.06 nm','"The length covered by 2 x 10⁸ carbon atoms = 2.4 cm\n\nThe length covered by 1 carbon atom = 2.4 / (2 x 10⁸) = 1.2 x 10⁻⁸ cm\n\nThe length covered by 1 carbon atom corresponds to its diameter. Therefore,\n\nThe diameter of carbon atom = 1.2 x 10⁻⁸ cm\n\nThe radius of carbon atom will be = (1.2 x 10⁻⁸ cm) / 2 = 0.6 x 10⁻⁸ cm = 0.06 nm"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 71; Exercise question 2.36','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','0.006 nm','0.6 nm','0.08 nm','0.06 nm','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22328,'chemistry','The diameter of zinc atom is 2.6 Å. Calculate (a) radius of zinc atom in pm and (b) number of atoms present in a length of 1.6 cm if the zinc atoms are arranged side by side lengthwise.','(a) 130 pm\n(b) 6.154 × 10⁷ atoms','"(a) The diameter of zinc atom is = 2.6 Å = 2.6 × 10⁻¹⁰ m\n\nThe radius of Zn atom is = 2.6 × 10⁻¹⁰ m / 2 = 1.3 x 10⁻¹⁰ m = 130 x 10⁻¹² m = 130 pm \n\n(b) The diameter of 1 Zn atom = 2.6 × 10⁻⁸ cm\n\nThe number of Zn atoms that can be placed in 1 cm length = 1 / (2.6 × 10⁻⁸)\n\nThe number of Zn atoms present in 1.6 cm of length are = 1.6 / (2.6 × 10⁻⁸) = 6.154 × 10⁷ atoms."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 71; Exercise question 2.37','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','(a) 140 pm\n(b) 6.154 × 10⁷ atoms','(a) 130 pm\n(b) 6.154 × 10⁸ atoms','(a) 140 pm\n(b) 6.154 × 10⁸ atoms','(a) 130 pm\n(b) 6.154 × 10⁷ atoms','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22329,'chemistry','A certain particle carries 2.5 × 10⁻¹⁶ C of static electric charge. Calculate the number of electrons present in it.','1560','"1.6022 × 10⁻¹⁹ C charge is carried by = 1 electron\n\n1 C charge is carried by = 1 / (1.6022 × 10⁻¹⁹) electrons \n\n∴ 2.5 × 10⁻¹⁶ C charge is carried By= (2.5 × 10⁻¹⁶) / (1.6022 × 10⁻¹⁹) = 1560 electrons."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 71; Exercise question 2.38','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','1660','15600','156','1560','NEW','11-033','11-033',NULL),
(22330,'chemistry','An element with mass number 81 contains 31.7% more neutrons as compared to protons. Assign the atomic symbol.','⁸¹₃₅Br','"Let the number of protons be = x\n\nSince, there are 31.7% more neutrons as compared to protons, therefore,\n\nThe number of neutrons will be = x + 31.7% x = x + 0.317 x = 1.317 x \n\nThe mass number = number of protons + number of neutrons = 81 \n i.e.,\n\n x + 1.317 x = 81\n2.317 x = 81\nx = 81 / 2.317 = 35\n\nTherefore,\n\nno. of protons = 35\nno. of neutrons = 46\nHence, the symbol is ⁸¹₃₅Br"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 71; Exercise question 2.42','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','⁸¹₃₅Br','⁸¹₃₆Br','⁸¹₃₇Br','⁸³₃₅Br','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(22331,'chemistry','An ion with mass number 56 contains 3 units of positive charge and 30.4% more neutrons than electrons. Assign the symbol to this ion.','⁵⁶₂₆Fe³⁺','"Suppose, the number of electrons in the ion, M³⁺ = x \n\n∴ Number of neutrons = x + 30.4% x = x + 0.304 x = 1.304 x \n\nNumber of electrons in the neutral atom will be = x + 3 \n\n∴ Number of protons = x + 3\n\nMass number = Number of protons + Number of neutrons = 56\n i.e.,\n\n x + 3 + 1.304 x = 56\n or 2.304 x = 53 \n or x = 53 / 2.304 = 23\n\n∴ Number of protons = Atomic no. = x + 3 = 23 + 3 = 26\n\nHence, the symbol of the ion will be ⁵⁶₂₆Fe³⁺ "\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 71; Exercise question 2.44','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','⁵⁶₂₆Co³⁺','⁵⁶₂₆Fe³⁺','⁵⁶₂₆Fe²⁺','⁵⁶₂₇Co³⁺','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(213301,'chemistry','Being the most ______, F exhibits only –1 oxidation state.','electronegative','Being the most electronegative F exhibits only –1 oxidation state. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 200 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','electropositive','electronegative','stable','reactive','NEW','12-200',NULL,NULL),
(213302,'chemistry','All the halogens are highly-','Reactive','All the halogens are highly Reactive\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 200 Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Stable','Reactive','Electropositive','Gaseous','NEW','12-200',NULL,NULL),
(22332,'chemistry','Arrange the following type of radiations in increasing order of frequency: \n \n (a) radiation from microwave oven \n \n (b) amber light from traffic signal \n \n (c) radiation from FM radio \n \n (d) cosmic rays from outer space and \n \n (e) X-rays.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','FM < microwave < amber colour < X-rays < Cosmic rays','"The increasing order of frequency of given electromagnetic radiations is:\n \nFM < microwave < amber colour < X-rays < Cosmic rays."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 71; Exercise question 2.45','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','FM < microwave < amber colour < X-rays < Cosmic rays','Xrays > amber colour > microwave > FM > Cosmic rays','Cosmic rays < Xrays < amber colour < microwave < FM','Cosmic rays < Xrays > amber colour > microwave > FM','NEW','11-038','11-038',NULL),
(22333,'chemistry','In astronomical observations, signals observed from the distant stars are generally weak. If the photon detector receives a total of 3.15 × 10⁻¹⁸ J from the radiations of 600 nm, calculate the number of photons received by the detector.','10 photons','"The energy of 1 photon is,\n\nE = hν = hc / λ = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js)(3.0×10⁸ ms⁻¹) / (600 x 10⁻⁹ m) = 3.313 × 10⁻¹⁹ J\n\n3.31×10⁻¹⁹ J energy is produced by = 1 photon\n\n1 J energy is produced by = 1 / (3.31 × 10⁻¹⁹) photons\n\n3.15 × 10⁻¹⁸ J energy is produced by = 3.15 × 10⁻¹⁸ / 3.31 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 9.52 = 10 photons."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 72; Exercise question 2.48','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','11 photons','10 photons','100 photons','9 photons','NEW','11-039','11-039',NULL),
(22334,'chemistry','Lifetimes of the molecules in the excited states are often measured by using pulsed radiation source of duration nearly in the nano second range. If the radiation source has the duration of 2 ns and the number of photons emitted during the pulse source is 2.5 × 10¹⁵, calculate the energy of the source.','8.28 × 10⁻¹⁰ J','"Time duration, T = 2 ns = 2 × 10⁻⁹ s\n \nFrequency, v = 1 / T = (1 / 2 × 10⁻⁹ s) = 10⁹ / 2 s⁻¹ \n\nEnergy of one photon, E = hv = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js x 10⁹ / 2 s⁻¹ = 3.313 × 10⁻²⁵ J\n \nNo. of photons = 2.5 × 10¹⁵ \n\n∴ Energy of source = 3.313 × 10⁻²⁵ J × 2.5 × 10¹⁵ = 8.28 × 10⁻¹⁰ J."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 72; Exercise question 2.49','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','8.28 × 10⁻¹⁰ J','9.28 × 10⁻¹¹ J','9.28 × 10⁻¹⁰ J','8.28 × 10⁻¹¹ J','NEW','11-041','11-041',NULL),
(22335,'chemistry','The longest wavelength doublet absorption transition is observed at 589 nm and 589.6 nm. Calcualte energy difference between two excited states.','3.31 × 10⁻²² J','"λ₁ = 589 nm = 589 × 10⁻⁹ m \n\n ∴ v₁ = c / λ₁ = (3 x 10⁸ ms⁻¹) / (589 × 10⁻⁹ m) = 5.093 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹ \n\n λ₂ = 589.6 nm = 589.6 × 10⁻⁹ m \n\n ∴ v₂ = c / λ₂ = (3 x 10⁸ m/s) / (589.6 × 10⁻⁹ m) = 5.088 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹\n \n ΔE = E₂ − E₁ = h (v₂−v₁) \n\n  = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js)[(5.093 − 5.088) × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹] = 3.31 × 10⁻²² J"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 72; Exercise question 2.49','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','3.31 × 10⁻²² J','3.31 × 10⁻²⁴ J','33.1 × 10⁻²² J','6.31 × 10⁻²² J','NEW','11-048','11-048',NULL),
(22336,'chemistry','The ejection of the photoelectron from the silver metal in the photoelectric effect experiment can be stopped by applying the voltage of 0.35 V when the radiation 256.7 nm is used. Calculate the work function for silver metal.','4.48 eV','"The energy of the incident radiation is calculated as, \n\nE = hν = hc / λ = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js)(3.0 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹) / (256.7 x 10⁻⁹ m) = 7.74 × 10⁻¹⁹ J\n\nConverting the unit J in eV, \n\n1 eV = 1.602 x 10⁻¹⁹ J\n\nor, 1 J = 6.242 x 10¹⁸ eV\n\n∴ E = 7.74 × 10⁻¹⁹ J x 6.242 x 10¹⁸ eV J⁻¹ = 4.83 eV \n\nThe potential or voltage applied to stop the ejection of photoelectron is = 0.35 V\n\nThe potential applied gives the kinetic energy (K.E.) to the ejected electron. \n\nThe potential of 1 V gives energy = 1 eV to 1 electron\n\n ∴ Kinetic energy, K.E. of the ejeceted photoelectron will be = 0.35 eV\n\nAnd exactly equal amount of energy i.e. 0.35 eV will be required to make the K.E. = 0, i.e., to stop the ejection. \n\nNow, the work function, W₀ is the minimum energy required to eject the electron, and is given by the relation,\n\nWork function, W₀ = Energy of the incident radiation – K.E. of the photoelectron\n\n ∴ Work function = 4.83 eV − 0.35 eV = 4.48 eV\n \nADD. EXP. :\n\nThe voltage V in volts (V) is equal to the energy E in electron-volts (eV), divided by the electric charge Q in elementary charge or proton/electron charge (e), i.e.,\n  \nV (V) = E (eV) / Q (e)\n\nThe elementary charge is the electric charge of 1 electron with the e symbol.\n \nThe potential of 1 V gives energy = 1 eV to 1 electron."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 72; Exercise question 2.53','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','4.83 eV','5.48 eV','4.08 eV','4.48 eV','NEW','11-041','11-041',NULL),
(22337,'chemistry','Calculate the wavelength for the emission transition if it starts from the orbit having radius 1.3225 nm and ends at 211.6 pm. Name the series to which this transition belongs.','434 nm and Balmer series','"The radius of nth orbit of H-like species is given by the expression,\n\n rₙ = (0.0529 nm x n²) / Z = (0.529 Å x n²) / Z = (52.9 pm x n²) / Z\n r₁ = 1.3225 nm = 1322.5 pm = (52.9 pm x n₁²) / Z .......(1) \n r₂ = 211.6 pm = (52.9 pm x n₂²) / Z .......(2) \n\nDividing eq. (1) by eq. (2), we get \n\n r₁ / r₂ = 1322.5 pm / 211.6 pm = n₁² / n₂² \n r₁ / r₂ = n₁² / n₂² = 6.25 ⟹ n₁ / n₂ = 2.5\n ⟹ n₁ = 5 and n₂ = 2 \n\n​Thus, the transition is from the 5th orbit to the 2nd orbit. It belongs to the Balmer series. \n\nNow, the wavelength for the emission transition is calculated as,\n\nWave number, ṽ = 1 / λ =  Rₕ [(1 / n₁²) - (1 / n₂²)]\n                                       = 1.097 × 10⁷ m⁻¹ [(1 / 4) - (1 / 25)] = 1.097 × 10⁷ m⁻¹ x 0.21\n                                       = 2.3037 × 10⁶ m⁻¹\n\nλ = 1 / ṽ = 1 / 2.3037 × 10⁶ m⁻¹ = 0.43408 × 10⁻⁶ m = 434 × 10⁻⁹ m or 434 nm\n\nThe transition lies in the visible region."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 72; Exercise question 2.56','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','454 nm and Lyman series','454 nm and Balmer series','434 nm and Lyman series','434 nm and Balmer series','NEW','11-048','11-048',NULL),
(22338,'chemistry','If the velocity of the electron in Bohr’s first orbit is 2.19 × 10⁶ ms⁻¹, calculate the de Broglie wavelength associated with it.','3.32 × 10⁻¹⁰ m','"According to de Broglie\'s equation,\n\n  λ = h / mv \n\nwhere, λ = de-Broglie wavelength \nh = Planck\'s constant = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js \nm = mass of electron = 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg \nv = velocity of electron = 2.19 × 10⁶ ms⁻¹\n \nSubstituting the values, we get,\n\nλ = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js) / (9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg)(2.19 × 10⁶ ms⁻¹) = 3.32 × 10⁻¹⁰ m = 332 pm"\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 72; Exercise question 2.59','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','4.32 × 10⁻¹⁰ m','3.32 × 10⁻¹¹ m','5.32 × 10⁻¹¹ m','3.32 × 10⁻¹⁰ m','NEW','11-050','11-050',NULL),
(22339,'chemistry','The velocity associated with a proton moving in a potential difference of 1000 V is 4.37 × 10⁵ ms⁻¹. If the hockey ball of mass 0.1 kg is moving with this velocity, calcualte the wavelength associated with this velocity.','1.516 × 10⁻²⁸ m','"The associated wavelength is obtained from de-Broglie equation,\n \n  λ = h / mv\n\nwhere, λ = de-Broglie wavelength \nh = Planck\'s constant = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js \nm = mass of ball = 0.1 kg \nv = velocity of ball = 4.37 × 10⁵ ms⁻¹\n\nSubstituting the values, we get,\n\nλ = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js) / (0.1 kg)(4.37 × 10⁵ ms⁻¹) = 1.516 × 10⁻²⁸ m."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 72; Exercise question 2.60','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','1.516 × 10⁻²⁸ m','1.516 × 10⁻³⁰ m','1.516 × 10⁻²⁹ m','2.516 × 10⁻²⁸ m','NEW','11-050','11-050',NULL),
(22340,'chemistry','If the photon of the wavelength 150 pm strikes an atom and one of its inner bound electrons is ejected out with a velocity of 1.5 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹, calculate the energy with which it is bound to the nucleus.','7.63 × 10³ eV','"λ of incident photon = 150 pm\n\nEnergy of incident photon = hc / λ = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js)(3.0 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹) / 150 x 10 ⁻¹² m \n= 13.252 × 10⁻¹⁶ J\n\nK.E. of the electron ejected = 1/2 mv² = 1/2 (9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg)(1.5 × 10⁷ ms⁻¹)² \n= 1.025 × 10⁻¹⁶ J\n\nEnergy with which the electron was bound to the nucleus will be synonymous to the work function, W₀ i.e., the minimum energy required to eject the electron out of the influence of nucleus. Now, \n\nWork function, W₀ = Energy of the incident radiation – K.E. of the photoelectron\n\nSince, Work function, W₀ = Energy with which the electron was bound to the nucleus,\n\n∴ E = Energy of the incident radiation – K.E. of the photoelectron\n= 13.252 × 10⁻¹⁶ J − 1.025 × 10⁻¹⁶ J\n= 12.227 × 10⁻¹⁶ J\n= 12.227 × 10⁻¹⁶ J / 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ J eV⁻¹ = 7.63 × 10³ eV."\n\n"Energy in eV can also be calculated as,\n E = 12.227 × 10⁻¹⁶ J x 6.242 x 10¹⁸ eV J⁻¹ = 7.63 × 10³ eV."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 72; Exercise question 2.54','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','76.3 × 10³ eV','7.63 × 10³ eV','7.63 × 10⁴ eV','8.63 × 10³ eV','NEW','11-043','11-043',NULL),
(22341,'chemistry','The bromine atom possesses 35 electrons. It contains 6 electrons in 2p orbital, 6 electrons in 3p orbital and 5 electron in 4p orbital. Which of these electron experiences the lowest effective nuclear charge ?','4p','"In general, the repulsive interactions of the electrons in the outer shell with the electrons in the inner shell are of more importance. The attractive interactions of an electron increases with increase of positive charge (Ze) on the nucleus. Due to the presence of electrons in the inner shells, the electron in the outer shell will not experience the full positive charge of the nucleus (Ze). The effect will be lowered due to the partial screening of positive charge on the nucleus by the inner shell electrons. This is known as the shielding of the outer shell electrons from the nucleus by the inner shell electrons, and the net positive charge experienced by the outer electrons is known as effective nuclear charge (Zₑբբ e)."\n \nSince, the distance between the nucleus and 4p electrons is maximum as compared to 2p and 3p, 4p electrons will experience the lowest effective nuclear charge."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no.60; Paragraph 2 and NCERT class 11; Page no. 73; Exercise question 2.63','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','2p','3p','4p','All experience same effective nuclear charge','NEW','11-060','11-060',NULL),
(22342,'chemistry','How many subshells are associated with n = 4 ?','4','"Azimuthal quantum number, ‘l’, defines the three-dimensional shape of the orbital. For a given value of n, l can have n values ranging from 0 to n – 1, that is, for a given value of n, the possible value of l are : l = 0, 1, 2, .......... (n–1)"\n\nAccording to above exlpanation, for n = 4, the possible l values are 0, 1, 2 and 3. Thus, four sub-shells are associated with n = 4. These include 4s, 4p, 4d and 4f."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 73; Exercise question 2.67(a) and NCERT class 11; Page no.55; Paragraph 5','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','2','3','4','5','NEW','11-055','11-055',NULL),
(22343,'chemistry','How many electrons will be present in the subshells having mₛ value of –1/2 for n = 4 ?','16','"The number of electrons that can be occupied by a shell is given by the formula 2n². Thus, for n = 4, 2n² = 2(4)² = 32.\n\nAccording to Pauli Exclusion principle, maximum of two electrons occuy each given orbital in such a way that one electron has mₛ value of +1/2 and other -1/2.\n\nSo, for n = 4, there are total 16 orbitals available, and thus, 16 electrons can occupy these which have mₛ value = -1/2."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 73; Exercise question 2.67(b)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','10','12','14','16','NEW','11-056','11-056',NULL),
(22344,'chemistry','The unpaired electrons in Al and Si are present in 3p orbital. Which electrons will experience more effective nuclear charge from the nucleus ?','Si','"Both Al and Si have 3rd shell as their valence shell, and the atomic no. of Si is greater than that of Al. As a result, the attractive force of nucleus on valence electrons increases in Si, causing the reduction in the atomic radius of Si. Hence, the electrons are closer to the nucleus in Si than in Al. Therefore, the unpaired electrons in Si will experience more effective nuclear charge due to the smaller atomic radius of Si than that of Al."\n\nReference; NCERT class 11; Page no. 73; Exercise question 2.65','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','Si','Al','Both experience same effective nuclear charge from the nucleus','Doesn\'t affect by effective nuclear charge from the nucleus','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22401,'chemistry','The number of protons, neutrons and electrons in ¹⁷⁵₇₁Lu, respectively, are _______ , _______ and _______.','71, 104 and 71','Atomic no. = no. of protons, nₚ = no. of electrons, nₑ = 71\n \nMass no. = no. of protons, nₚ + no. of neutrons, nₙ\n\n nₚ = nₑ = 71\n nₚ + nₙ = 175\n nₙ = 175 − 71 = 104\n\nReference; NEET;2020','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','71, 104 and 71','104, 71 and 71','71, 71 and 104','175, 104 and 71','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(22402,'chemistry','Be²⁺ is isoelectronic with which of the following ions?','Li⁺','Isoelectronic species means species having same number of electrons. \n\nAmong the given options, Be⁺² is isoelectronic with Li⁺. Both contain 2 electrons. \n\nThey have electronic configuration of, Be⁺² = 1s² = Li⁺\n\nWhereas, no. of electrons in other given species are:\n\nH⁺ = 0, Na⁺ = 10, Mg²⁺ = 10\n\nReference; NEET; 2014','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','H⁺','Li⁺','Na⁺','Mg²⁺','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(213303,'chemistry','The reactivity of the halogens ____________ .','decreases down the group','The reactivity of the halogens  decreases down the group\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 200 Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','increases down the group','decreases down the group','remains constant','follows irregular pattern','NEW','12-200',NULL,NULL),
(22403,'chemistry','Which of the following options consists of isoelectronic species?','CO, CN⁻, NO⁺, C₂²⁻','CO → 6 + 8 = 14 electrons\nCO⁻ → 6 + 8 + 1 = 15 electrons\nCO⁺ → 6 + 8 - 1 = 13 electrons\nCN → 6 + 7 = 13 electrons\nCN⁻ → 6 + 7 + 1 = 14 electrons\nCN⁺ → 6 + 7 - 1 = 12 electrons\nNO → 7 + 8 = 15 electrons\nNO⁻ → 7 + 8 + 1 = 16 electrons\nNO⁺ → 7 + 8 − 1 = 14 electrons\nC₂ → 2 × 6 = 12 electrons\nC₂⁻ → 2 × 6 + 1 = 13 electrons\nC₂²⁻ → 2 × 6 + 2 = 14 electrons\n\nAmong all the given species, only CO, CN⁻, NO⁺, C₂²⁻ have same number of electrons. \n\nThus, these are isoelectronic.\n\nReference; NEET; 2000','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','CO, CN⁻, NO⁺, C₂²⁻','CO⁻, CN, NO, C₂⁻','CO⁺, CN⁺, NO⁻, C₂','CO, CN, NO, C₂','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(22404,'chemistry','The ion that is isoelectronic with CO is _______.','CN⁻','"CO → 6 + 8 = 14 electrons\n\n(a) N₂⁺ → 2 × 7 − 1 = 13 electrons\n\n(b) CN⁻ → 6 + 7 + 1 = 14 electrons \n\n(c) O²⁻ → 8 + 2 = 10 electrons\n\n(d) N₂⁻ → 2 × 7 + 1 = 15 electrons\n\nSince only CN⁻ has 14 electrons, like CO, it is isoelectronic with CO."\n\nReference; NEET; 1997','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','CN⁻','N₂⁺','O²⁻','N₂⁻','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(22405,'chemistry','Which one of the following is not isoelectronic with O²⁻?','Tl⁺','O²⁻ → 8 + 2 = 10 electrons\n\n(a) Tl⁺ → 81 - 1 = 80 electrons\n\n(b) Na⁺ → 11 - 1 = 10 electrons \n\n(c) N³⁻ → 7 + 3 = 10 electrons \n\n(d) F⁻ → 9 + 1 = 10 electrons\n\nOnly Tl⁺, among all the options, dosen\'t have 10 electrons like O²⁻\n\nSo, it is not isoelectronic with O²⁻\n\nReference; NEET; 1994','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Tl⁺','Na⁺','N³⁻','F⁻','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(22406,'chemistry','Which of the following series of transitions in the spectrum of hydrogen atom falls in visible region?','Balmer series','The hydrogen spectrum consists of several series of lines (of electromagnetic radiations of discrete frequencies), named after their discoverers. \n\nThese are called, Lyman series, Balmer series, Paschen series, Brackett series and Pfund series. Among these, Balmer series transitions fall in the visible region.\n\nReference; NEET; 2019','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Brackett series','Lyman series','Balmer series','Paschen series','NEW','11-045','11-045',NULL),
(22407,'chemistry','Calculate the energy in joule corresponding to light of wavelength 45 nm.\n (Planck’s constant, h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ Js, speed of light, c = 3 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹)','4.42 × 10⁻¹⁸ J','The relationship between the energy of light and its wavelength is given as,\n\nE = hc / λ \n\n= (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js)(3.0 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹) / (45 x 10⁻⁹ m) = 4.42 x 10⁻¹⁸ J \n\nHence, the energy of light corresponding to wavelength 45 nm is 4.42 x 10⁻¹⁸ J\n\nReference; NEET; 2014','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','6.67 × 10¹⁵ J','6.67 × 10¹¹ J','4.42 × 10⁻¹⁵ J','4.42 × 10⁻¹⁸ J','NEW','11-042','11-042',NULL),
(22408,'chemistry','The value of Planck’s constant is 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ Js. The speed of light is 3 × 10¹⁷ nm s⁻¹\n Which value is closest to the wavelength in nanometer of a quantum of light with frequency of 6 × 10¹⁵ s⁻¹?','50','Wavelength, λ, and frequency, v, of a radiation are related as,\n\nλ = c / v\n\nNow, speed of light, c = 3 × 10¹⁷ nm s⁻¹ \n\nfrequency, v = 6 × 10¹⁵ s⁻¹ \n\n∴ λ = c / v = (3 × 10¹⁷ nm s⁻¹) / (6 × 10¹⁵ s⁻¹) = 50 nm\n\nReference; NEET; 2013','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','50','10','75','25','NEW','11-038','11-038',NULL),
(22409,'chemistry','According to law of photochemical equivalence, the energy absorbed (in ergs/mole) is given as _______.\n (h = 6.62 × 10⁻²⁷ erg s, c = 3 × 10¹⁰ cm s⁻¹, NA = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)','1.196 × 10⁸ / λ','"The relationship between the enrgy of light and its wavelength is given as,\n\nE = hc x NA / λ\n\n= (6.62 × 10⁻²⁷ erg s)(3 × 10¹⁰ cm s⁻¹) x 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹ / λ = 1.196 × 10⁸ / λ\n \nReference; Karnataka NEET; 2013','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','1.196 × 10⁸ / λ','2.859 x 10⁵ / λ','2.859 × 10¹⁶ / λ','1.196 × 10¹⁶ / λ','NEW','11-043','11-043',NULL),
(22410,'chemistry','The energies E₁ and E₂ of two radiations are 25 eV and 50 eV respectively. The relation between their wavelengths i.e., λ₁ and λ₂ will be _______.','λ₁ = 2λ₂','"Given the values of E₁ = 25 eV and E₂ = 50 eV,\n\nE₁ = hc / λ₁ = 25 eV \n\nE₂ = hc / λ₂ = 50 eV\n\nDividing E₁ by E₂, \n\nE₁ / E₂ = λ₂ / λ₁ = 25 / 50\n\nλ₂ / λ₁ = 1/2 \n\nλ₁ = 2λ₂ "\n\nReference; Karnataka NEET; 2011','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','λ₁ = λ₂','λ₁ = 2λ₂','λ₁ = 4λ₂','λ₁ = 1/2 λ₂','NEW','11-048','11-048',NULL),
(22411,'chemistry','The value of Planck’s constant is 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ Js. The velocity of light is 3 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹. \n Which value is closest to the wavelength in nanometers of a quantum of light with frequency of 8 × 10¹⁵ s⁻¹ ?','4 × 10¹','"Wavelength, λ, and frequency, v, of a radiation are related as,\n\nλ = c / v\n\nwhere, c = speed of light = 3 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹\n\nν = frequency of a quantum of light = 8 × 10¹⁵ s⁻¹ \n\n∴ λ = c / v = (3 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹) / (8 × 10¹⁵ s⁻¹) = 0.375 × 10⁻⁷ m = 3.75 × 10¹ nm\n\nHence, 4 × 10¹ nm will be the closest value to the wavelength (in nm) of a quantum of light with frequency 8 × 10¹⁵ s⁻¹."\n\nReference; Karnataka NEET; 2003','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','2 × 10⁻²⁵','5 × 10⁻¹⁸','4 × 10¹','3 × 10⁷','NEW','11-042','11-042',NULL),
(22412,'chemistry','For given energy, E = 3.03 × 10⁻¹⁹ joules corresponding wavelength is _______.\n (h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ Js, speed of light, c = 3 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹)','656 nm','"The relationship between the energy of light and its wavelength is given as,\n\nE = hc / λ\n \nλ = hc / E \n\n= (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js)(3.0 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹) / (3.03 × 10⁻¹⁹ J) = 656 nm \n\nHence, the wavelength corresponding to light of energy 3.03 × 10⁻¹⁹ J is 656 nm."\n\nReference; Karnataka NEET; 2000','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','65.6 nm','6.56 nm','3.4 nm','656 nm','NEW','11-043','11-043',NULL),
(22413,'chemistry','What will be the longest wavelength line in Balmer series of spectrum?','656 nm','For single electron species, \n\n1 / λ = R Z² [(1 / n₁²) - (1 / n₂²)] \n\nwhere, R = Rydberg constant = 1.097 × 10⁷ x 10⁻⁹ nm⁻¹ = 1.097 × 10⁻² nm⁻¹ \n\nFor Hydrogen atom, Z = Atomic number = 1\nFor Balmer series, n₁ = 2 \n\nFor longest wavelength in Balmer series, minimum energy transition is to be considered because wavelength is inversely proportional to energy. \nSo, n₂ = 3 \n\n∴ 1 / λ = 1.097 × 10⁻² nm⁻¹ x 1² [(1 / 2²) - (1 / 3²)] \n\nλ = 656 nm\n\nReference; Karnataka NEET; 1996','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','546 nm','656 nm','566 nm','556 nm','NEW','11-045','11-045',NULL),
(22414,'chemistry','Based on equation E = – 2.178 × 10⁻¹⁸ J [Z² / n²] certain conclusions are written. \n Which of them is not correct?','For n = 1, the electron has a more negative energy than it does for n = 6 which means that the electron is more loosely bound in the smallest allowed orbit.','Using the equation, \n\nE = – 2.178 × 10⁻¹⁸ J [Z² / n²], \n\n(a) We can calculate the energy of an electron in different orbits using relevant values of n. Hence, this equation can be used to calculate the change in energy when the electron changes orbit by calculating difference in energy of different orbits.\n\n(b) When the electron is free from the influence of nucleus, the energy is taken as zero. The electron in this situation is associated with the stationary state of Principal Quantum number = n = ∞ and is called as ionized hydrogen atom. When the electron is attracted by the nucleus and is present in orbit n, the energy is emitted and its energy is lowered. And the energy is emitted most when n = 1. That is the reason for the presence of negative sign in the above equation and depicts its stability relative to theReference state of zero energy and n = ∞. \n\nTherefore, an electron has the most negative energy for n = 1. The lowest possible energy state is the most stable state of the system, which would be the one in which the electrons are most tightly bound, and is called the ground state. In the ground state, there is most force of attraction which means electrons in the ground state are most tightly bound to the nucleus. This state corresponds to the smallest allowed orbit (the orbit closest to the nucleus i.e., n = 1).\n\n(c) This also implies that the negative sign in equation simply means that the energy of electron bound to the nucleus is lower than it would be if the electrons were at the infinite distance from the nucleus.\n\n(d) As the electron comes closer to the nucleus, more energy is emitted, the value of n decreases and the radius of the orbit also decreases. Therefore, we can easily conclude that larger the value of n, the larger is the orbit radius.\n\nReference; NEET; 2013','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Equation can be used to calculate the change in energy when the electron changes orbit.','For n = 1, the electron has a more negative energy than it does for n = 6 which means that the electron is more loosely bound in the smallest allowed orbit.','The negative sign in equation simply means that the energy of electron bound to the nucleus is lower than it would be if the electrons were at the infinite distance from the nucleus.','Larger the value of n, the larger is the orbit radius.','NEW','11-047','11-047',NULL),
(22415,'chemistry','According to the Bohr theory, which of the following transitions in the hydrogen atom will give rise to the least energetic photon?','n = 6 to n = 5','"Energy of an electron in the nth orbit is given by the relation,\n\nE = -13.6 eV [Z² / n²]\n\nThus, E ∝ 1/n² \n\n⇒ Higher the value of n, lesser will be the energy.\n\nFor the transition n₂ → n₁, \n\nwe have,   ΔE ∝ (1/n₁² - 1/n₂²) \n\nIf we substitute the different given values of n₁ and n₂, we find that, of the given options, the term (1/n₁² - 1/n₂²) gives the least value with n₁ = 5 and n₂ = 6, which, in turn, will correspond to the least energy value. Thus, the least energetic photon results from the transition n = 6 to n = 5."\n\nReference; Mains; 2011','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','n = 6 to n = 1','n = 5 to n = 4','n = 6 to n = 5','n = 5 to n = 3','NEW','11-046','11-046',NULL),
(22416,'chemistry','The energy of second Bohr orbit of the hydrogen atom is –328 kJ mol⁻¹; hence the energy of fourth Bohr orbit would be _______.','–82 kJ mol⁻¹','Energy of nth Bohr orbit is given by,\n\nEₙ = -R (1 / n²) \n\nThus,\n\nE₂ = -R (1 / 2²) \nE₄ = -R (1 / 4²) = -R (1 / 2²)(1 / 2²) \n= E₂ (1 / 2²)\n\nGiven, E₂ = -328 kJ mol⁻¹\n\n∴ E₄ = -328 kJ mol⁻¹ × (1 / 4) = -82 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nReference; Mains; 2005','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','– 41 kJ mol⁻¹','–82 kJ mol⁻¹','–164 kJ mol⁻¹','–1311 kJ mol⁻¹','NEW','11-047','11-047',NULL),
(22417,'chemistry','The frequency of radiation emitted when the electron falls from n = 4 to n = 1 in a hydrogen atom will be _______.\n (Given ionization energy of H = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸J atom⁻¹ and h = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js)','3.08 × 10¹⁵ s⁻¹','Energy of an electron in the nth orbit is given by the relation,\n\nE = – 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J [Z² / n²]\n\nSince, I.E.₁ (H) = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J \n\n∴ E₁ = −2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J \n\n∴ E₄ = −2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J / 4² \n\nΔE = E₁₋₄ = E₄ - E₁ = [(−2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J / 4²) - (−2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J)] \n\n= [(−2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J / 4²) + (2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J)] \n= 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J (- (1 / 16) + 1) \n= 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J (1 - (1 / 16)) \n= 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J × 15 / 16 \n\nFrequency, v = ΔE / h = ((2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J) × 15) / (16 × (6.25 × 10⁻³⁴ Js)) \n  = 3.08 × 10¹⁵ s⁻¹\n\nReference; Mains; 2004','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','1.54 × 10¹⁵ s⁻¹','1.03 × 10¹⁵ s⁻¹','3.08 × 10¹⁵ s⁻¹','2.00 × 10¹⁵ s⁻¹','NEW','11-041','11-041',NULL),
(22418,'chemistry','In hydrogen atom, energy of first excited state is –3.4 eV. Then find out K.E. of same orbit of hydrogen atom.','3.4 eV','"KE is the energy with which the electron moves in the orbit. Hence the energy in an excited state is nothing but kinetic energy.\n\nThus, K.E. of H−atom = −T.E. = +3.4 eV\n\nKinetic energy = 1/2 mv² \n\nSince, v = 2πe² / nh \n\n∴ Kinetic energy, K.E. = 1/2 m(2πe² / nh)² = (πe² / nh)² × 2m\n\nTotal energy, Eₙ = −2π²me⁴ / n²h² = −(πe² / nh)² × 2m = −K.E.\n \n∴ K.E. = −Eₙ."\n\nReference; Mains; 2002','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','3.4 eV','6.8 eV','-13.6 eV','13.6 eV','NEW','11-042','11-042',NULL),
(22419,'chemistry','Who modified Bohr’s theory by introducing elliptical orbits for electron path?','Sommerfeld','"Bohr’s theory was given by Neil Bohr in 1915 after which the Bohr\'s model of atom was given. The Bohr atomic model explains the movement of electrons in the fixed orbit and not anywhere in between. Neil Bohr also explained that each orbit has its fixed energy level.\n\n -Bohr model failed to explain the effect of magnetic field on the spectra of atoms. It also violated the Heisenberg Uncertainty principle and it was not able to explain the spectra obtained from larger molecules.\n\n -Bohr\'s theory was further modified by Sommerfeld by introducing elliptical orbits for electron paths. His model explains the fine spectrum of hydrogen atoms.\n\n -The postulates of the Sommerfeld model are:\n\n(i) The orbits can both be circular or elliptical.\n\n(ii) When the path of electrons is elliptical, then there are two axes – major axis and minor axis. When the length of major and minor axis becomes equal then the orbit is circular.\n\n(iii) The angular momentum of electrons moving in the elliptical orbit is,\n  \nmvₖrₖ = kh/2π \n\nwhere mvₖ is the momentum of an electron revolving in the kᵗʰ orbit of radius rₖ.\n\n(iv) Sommerfeld suggested the sub energy levels s, p, d, f. He introduced the Azimuthal quantum number (l) which helps to determine the orbital angular quantum number.\n\n(v) He also explains that when the electrons get excited and jump from one orbital to another, the difference in energy depends on the sub energy level.\n\n(vi) He explains the splitting of individual spectral lines of hydrogen. However, the Sommerfeld model does not explain the Zeeman and Stark effect."\n\nReference; Mains; 1999','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Rutherford','Thomson','Hund','Sommerfeld','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22420,'chemistry','The Bohr orbit radius for the hydrogen atom (n = 1) is approximately 0.530 Å. The radius for the first excited state (n = 2) orbit (in Å) is _______.','2.11 Å','"The radius of Bohr orbit is given by,\n\nr = 0.53 n² Å / Z \n\nwhere, n = orbit number\n\nSince, Z = 1, for H-atom,\n\n∴ for the first excited state, i.e., n = 2,\n\nr₂ = 0.53 × 4 = 2.11 Å."\n\nReference; Mains; 1998','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','4.77 Å','1.06 Å','0.13 Å','2.11 Å','NEW','11-047','11-047',NULL),
(22421,'chemistry','In a Bohr’s model of an atom, when an electron jumps from n = 1 to n = 3, how much energy will be emitted or absorbed?','0.1936 × 10⁻¹⁰ ergs','"Energy of an electron in the nth orbit is given by the relation,\n\n E = – 2.18 × 10⁻¹¹ erg [Z² / n²]\n ∴ E₁ = −2.18 × 10⁻¹¹ erg\n ∴ E₃ = −2.18 × 10⁻¹¹ erg / 3² \n \nΔE = E₁₋₃ = E₃ - E₁ = [(−2.18 × 10⁻¹¹ erg / 3²) - (−2.18 × 10⁻¹¹ erg)] \n  = [(−2.18 × 10⁻¹¹ erg / 3²) + (2.18 × 10⁻¹¹ erg)] \n  = 2.18 × 10⁻¹¹ erg (- (1 / 9) + 1) \n  = 2.18 × 10⁻¹¹ erg (1 - (1 / 9)) \n  = 2.18 × 10⁻¹¹ erg × 8 / 9 \n  = 1.93 × 10¹¹ erg = 0.193 × 10⁻¹⁰ erg\n \nSince electron is going from n=1 to n=3, hence energy is absorbed."\nReference; Mains; 1996','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','2.389 × 10⁻¹² ergs','0.239 × 10⁻¹⁰ ergs','2.15 × 10⁻¹¹ ergs','0.1936 × 10⁻¹⁰ ergs','NEW','11-047','11-047',NULL),
(22422,'chemistry','The radius of hydrogen atom in the ground state is 0.53 Å. \n The radius of Li²⁺ ion (atomic number = 3) in a similar state is _______.','0.17 Å','"The radius of Bohr orbit is given by,\n\n  r = 0.53 n² Å / Z \n\n For H-atom in ground state, Z = 1 and n = 1, \n \n∴ r(H) = 0.53 \n\n For Li²⁺-ion in a similar state, Z = 3 and n = 1,\n\n ∴ r(Li²⁺) = (rH) / 3 = 0.17 A⁰."\n\nReference; Mains; 1995','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','0.53 Å','1.06 Å','0.17 Å','0.265 Å','NEW','11-047','11-047',NULL),
(22423,'chemistry','The energy of an electron in the nᵗʰ Bohr orbit of hydrogen atom is _______.','13.6/n² eV','"The energy of an electron in the nᵗʰ Bohr orbit of hydrogen atom is,\n \nE = -13.6 eV Z² / n² \n\nFor H-atom, Z = 1,\n\n∴ E = -13.6 eV / n²\n \nReference; Mains; 1992','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','13.6/n⁴ eV','13.6/n³ eV','13.6/n² eV','13.6/n eV','NEW','11-048','11-048',NULL),
(22424,'chemistry','The spectrum of He is expected to be similar to that of _______.','Li⁺','"The isoelectronic species are expected to show similar spectrum. \n\nFrom the given options, Li⁺ & He are isoelectronic species having equal no. of electrons, i.e., \n\nNo. of electrons in H = No. of electrons in Li⁺ = 2. \n\nTherefore the spectrum is expected to be same for both of them with same no. of electrons."\n\nReference; Mains; 1988','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','H','Li⁺','Na','He⁺','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22425,'chemistry','If r is the radius of the first orbit, the radius of nᵗʰ orbit of H⁻ atom is given by _______.','rn²','"The radius of Bohr orbit is given by,\n\n  r = 0.53 n² Å / Z \n\nFor H-atom in ground state, Z = 1 and n = 1, \n\n ∴ r(H), or r = radius of first orbit = 0.53 Å\n\nSo, the radius of nth orbit is, \n\n  rₙ ​= rn²."\n\nReference; Mains; 1988','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','rn²','rn','r/n','r²n²','NEW','11-047','11-047',NULL),
(22426,'chemistry','In hydrogen atom, the de Broglie wavelength of an electron in the second Bohr orbit is _______.\n [Given that Bohr radius, a₀ = 52.9 pm]','211.6π pm','"The angular momentum of an electron is given by the relation,\n\n  mvrₙ = nh / 2π \n\nwhere, rₙ = radius of nth orbit\n ∴ rₙ = nh / 2πmv \n  rₙ = (n / 2π) x (h / mv) \n\n The de Broglie wavelength, λ, is given by the relation,\n  λ = h / mv\n ∴ rₙ = (n / 2π) λ \n  rₙ = nλ / 2π ....... (i)\n\n Bohr\'s radius is also given by the relation,\n  rₙ = n²a₀ ....... (ii)\n\n where, a₀ = Bohr\'s radius of 1st orbit = 52.9 pm\n\n Comparing eq. (i) and (ii) for rₙ,\n  n²a₀ = nλ / 2π \n or, na₀ = λ / 2π\n  \nλ = 2πna₀\n\nFor 2nd Bohr orbit, n = 2, and, a₀ = 52.9 pm,\n ∴ λ = 2π(2)a₀ = 4πa₀ \n  λ = 4π x 52.9 pm\n or, λ = 211.6π pm."\n\nReference; Odisha NEET; 2019','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','211.6 pm','211.6π pm','52.9π pm','105.8 pm','NEW','11-050','11-050',NULL),
(22427,'chemistry','A 0.66 kg ball is moving with a speed of 100 m/s. \n The associated wavelength will be _______.\n (h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ Js)','1.0 × 10⁻³⁵ m','"According to de-Broglie equation, \n\n  λ = h / mv \n\nwhere, h = Planck’s constant = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ Js \n  m = 0.66 kg \n  v = 100 ms⁻¹ \n\nSubstituting given values in the above expression,\nλ = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ Js) / (0.66 kg)(100 ms⁻¹) = 0.1 x 10⁻³⁴ m\n\nThus, the associated wavelength is 1.0 × 10⁻³⁵ m."\n\nReference; Mains; 2010','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','6.6 × 10⁻³² m','6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ m','1.0 × 10⁻³⁵ m','1.0 × 10⁻³² m','NEW','11-050','11-050',NULL),
(22428,'chemistry','If uncertainty in position and momentum are equal, then uncertainty in velocity is _______.','1/2m √h/π','"According to Heisenberg\'s uncertainity principle,\n\n  Δx . Δpₓ = h / 4π \n\n or, Δx . mΔvₓ = h / 4π \n\n Given that, \n\n  Δx = mΔvₓ\n\n ⇒ mΔvₓ . \n\n  mΔvₓ = h / 4π \n\n  m² Δvₓ² = h / 4π \n\n  Δvₓ² = h / 4πm² \n  Δvₓ = √h/4πm² \n  Δvₓ = 1/2m √h/π."\n\nReference; Mains; 2008','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','1/m √h/π','√h/2π','√h/π','1/2m √h/π','NEW','11-051','11-051',NULL),
(22429,'chemistry','The measurement of the electron position is associated with an uncertainty in momentum, which is equal to 1 × 10⁻¹⁸ g cm s⁻¹. \n \n The uncertainty in electron velocity is _______ (mass of an electron is 9 × 10⁻²⁸ g).','1 × 10⁹ cms⁻¹','"The uncertainity in the momentum, \n\nΔpₓ = 1 × 10⁻¹⁸ gcms⁻¹\n \nMass of electron, m = 9 × 10⁻²⁸ g \n\nThe uncertainity in the velocity, Δvₓ = Δpₓ / m\n \n∴ Δvₓ = (1 × 10⁻¹⁸ gcms⁻¹) / (9 × 10⁻²⁸ g) \n  \nΔvₓ = 1 × 10⁹ cms⁻¹."\n\nReference; Mains; 2008','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','1 × 10⁵ cms⁻¹','1 × 10¹¹ cms⁻¹','1 × 10⁹ cms⁻¹','1 × 10⁶ cms⁻¹','NEW','11-051','11-051',NULL),
(22430,'chemistry','Given: The mass of electron is 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg, Planck constant is 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js, the uncertainty involved in the measurement of velocity within a distance of 0.1 Å is _______.','5.79 × 10⁵ ms⁻¹','"According to Heisenberg\'s uncertainity principle,\n\n  Δx . Δp ≥ h / 4π\n\n ​where,\n  h = Planck\'s constant = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js\n  Δx = Uncertainity in position\n  Δp = Uncertainity in momentum\n\n As we know that,\n  Δp = m.Δv\n ∴ (m.Δv) . Δx ≥ h / 4π \n\n ⇒ Δv . Δx ≥ h / 4πm \n  m = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg\n  Δx = 10⁻¹⁰ m\n∴ 10⁻¹⁰ m . \n\nΔv ≥ (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js) / (4 x 3.14 x 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg) \nΔv ≥ 5.79 × 10⁵ ms⁻¹."\n\nReference; Mains; 2006','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','5.79 × 10⁵ ms⁻¹','5.79 × 10⁶ ms⁻¹','5.79 × 10⁷ ms⁻¹','5.79 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹','NEW','11-051','11-051',NULL),
(22431,'chemistry','The uncertainty in momentum of an electron is 1 × 10⁻⁵ kg m/s. \n The uncertainty in its position will be _______.\n (h = 6.62 × 10⁻³⁴ kg m²/s)','5.27 × 10⁻³⁰ m','"According to Heisenberg\'s uncertainity principle,\n\n  Δx . Δpₓ ≥ h / 4π\n\n The uncertainty in the position of electron, Δx, is,\n\n  Δx = h / 4πΔp \n\n  = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js) / (4 x 3.14 x 1 × 10⁻⁵ kg ms⁻¹)\n \n  = 5.27 × 10⁻³⁰ m."\n\nReference; Mains; 1999','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','5.27 × 10⁻³⁰ m','1.05 × 10⁻²⁶ m','1.05 × 10⁻²⁸ m','5.25 × 10⁻²⁸ m','NEW','11-051','11-051',NULL),
(22432,'chemistry','The de Broglie wavelength of a particle with mass 1 g and velocity 100 m/s is _______.','6.63 × 10⁻³³ m','"The de Broglie wavelength, λ, is given by the relation,\n\nλ = h / mv\n\nNow,\nMass of particle, m = 1 gram = 10⁻³ kg\n\nVelocity, v = 100 ms⁻¹ \n\nDe broglie wavelength, λ = h / mv = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js) / (10⁻³ kg x 100 ms⁻¹)\n  \n= 6.626 × 10⁻³³ m."\n\nReference; Mains; 1999','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','6.63 × 10⁻³⁵ m','6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ m','6.63 × 10⁻³³ m','6.65 × 10⁻³⁵ m','NEW','11-050','11-050',NULL),
(22433,'chemistry','The position of both, an electron and a helium atom is known within 1.0 nm. \n Further the momentum of the electron is known within 5.0 × 10⁻²⁶ kg m s⁻¹. \n The minimum uncertainty in the measurement of the momentum of the helium atom is _______.','5.0 × 10⁻²⁶ kgms⁻¹','"According to Heisenberg\'s Uncertainity principle, the product of uncertainty in position and uncertainty in momentum is constant for a particle. i.e.,\n\n  Δx . Δp ≥ h / 4π\n\nSo,for a given uncertainty in position, the minimum uncertainty in momentum is equal to h/4πΔx, which is a constant.\n\n Given that,\n\n Position of both an electron & a helium atom is = 1 nm.\n\n Momentum of an electron is = 5 × 10⁻²⁶ kg ms⁻¹.\n\nWhen the postion of both the electron and helium atom is same, and the momentum of the electron is known, then the momentum of the helium atom will be same as the momentum of the electron. Or, we can say that the minimum uncetainty in momentum for helium is same as that for an electron for same uncertainty in their positions.\n \n∴ Momentum of Helium atom = 5 × 10⁻²⁶ kg ms⁻¹."\n\nReference; Mains; 1998','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','8.0 × 10⁻²⁶ kgms⁻¹','80 kgms⁻¹','50 kgms⁻¹','5.0 × 10⁻²⁶ kgms⁻¹','NEW','11-051','11-051',NULL),
(22434,'chemistry','Which of the following statements do not form a part of Bohr’s model of hydrogen atom?\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','The position and velocity of the electrons in the orbit cannot be determined simultaneously.','"Bohr’s model for hydrogen atom is based on the postulates few of which are discussed as follows:\n\n(a) The energy of an electron in the orbit does not change with time. However, the electron will move from a lower stationary state to a higher stationary state when required amount of energy is absorbed by the electron or energy is emitted when electron moves from higher stationary state to lower stationary state i.e.,\n\n  ∆E = Eբ – Eᵢ\n\nThe energy change does not take place in a continuous manner. Thus, energy of the electrons in the orbits is quantized.\n\n(b) The most important property associated with the electron is the energy of its stationary state. It is given by the expression:\n\n  Eₙ = −Rₕ (1 / n²)\n\n where, n = 1, 2, 3.... \n\nSince the energy has the negative sign in the equation, it means energy will increase with the increase in value of n. Therefore, the electron in the orbit nearest the nucleus has the lowest energy.\n\n(c) The electron in the hydrogen atom can move around the nucleus in a circular path of fixed radius and energy. These paths are called orbits, stationary states or allowed energy states. These orbits are arranged concentrically around the nucleus.\n\n(d) Bohr assumed that an electron in an atom is located at a definite distance from the nucleus and is revolving with a definite velocity around it. This is against Heisenberg\'s uncertainty principle, which states that the position and velocity of the electrons in the orbit cannot be determined simultaneously."\n\nReference; Mains; 1989','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','Energy of the electrons in the orbits is quantized.','The electron in the orbit nearest the nucleus has the lowest energy.','Electrons revolve in different orbits around the nucleus.','The position and velocity of the electrons in the orbit cannot be determined simultaneously.','NEW','11-037','11-037',NULL),
(22435,'chemistry','4d, 5p, 5f and 6p orbitals are arranged in the order of decreasing energy. The correct option is _______.','5f > 6p > 5p > 4d','"In the ground state of the atoms, the orbitals are filled in order of their increasing energies. Mathematically, the dependence of energies of the orbitals on their n and l values is quite complicated but one simple rule is that, the lower the value of (n + l) for an orbital, the lower is its energy. If two orbitals have the same value of (n + l), the orbital with lower value of n will have the lower energy.\n\nNow (n + l) values for 4d, 5p, 5f and 6p orbitals are,\n\n  4d = 4 + 2 = 6\n  5p = 5 + 1 = 6\n  5f = 5 + 3 = 8\n  6p = 6 + 1 = 7\n\n∴ Correct order of obital energy would be, \n\n5f > 6p > 5p > 4d."\n\nReference; NEET; 2019','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','5f > 6p > 4d > 5p','5f > 6p > 5p > 4d','6p > 5f > 5p > 4d','6p > 5f > 4d > 5p','NEW','11-060','11-060',NULL),
(22436,'chemistry','Orbital having 3 angular nodes and 3 total nodes is _______.','4f','Number of spherical / radial nodes in any orbital = n – l – 1\n Number of planar / angular nodes in orbital = l = 3\n The total number of nodes are given by (n – 1), i.e., sum of l angular nodes and (n – l – 1) radial nodes.\n Since, total number of nodes in given orbital = n –1 = 3\n ∴ n = 4\n Since, l = 3 \n ∴ the orbital is 4f.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','5p','3d','4f','6d','NEW','11-059','11-059',NULL),
(22437,'chemistry','Which one of the following is the wrong statement?\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','The energy of 2s-orbital is less than the energy of 2p-orbital in case of hydrogen like atoms.','"(a) Heisenberg’s uncertainty principle can be considered as a very precise mathematical statement that describes the nature of quantum systems. As such, we often consider two common equations related to the uncertainty principle. They are;\n\n  ∆x ⋅ ∆p ≥ h/4π\n\n  ∆E ⋅ ∆t ≥ h/4π\n\n where, h = Planck’s constant\n\n  ∆x = uncertainty in the position\n  ∆p = uncertainty in momentum\n  ∆E = uncertainty in the energy\n  ∆t = uncertainty in time measurement\n\n(b) The ground state electronic configuration of the atom of an element always corresponds to the state of the lowest total electronic energy. The electronic configurations of most of the atoms follow the basic rules of filling of orbitals. However, in certain elements such as Cu, or Cr, where the two subshells (4s and 3d) differ slightly in their energies, an electron shifts from a subshell of lower energy (4s) to a subshell of higher energy (3d), provided such a shift results in all orbitals of the subshell of higher energy getting either completely filled or half filled. The valence electronic configurations of Cr and Cu, therefore, are 3d⁵, 4s¹ and 3d¹⁰, 4s¹ respectively and not 3d⁴, 4s² and 3d⁹, 4s². It has been found that there is extra stability associated with these electronic configurations. Half filled and fully filled orbitals have greater stability due to greater exchange energy, greater symmetry and more balanced arrangement.\n\n(c) In case of hydrogen like atoms, orbital energy depends on the principal quantum number only. Hence, 2s-orbital will have energy equal to 2p-orbital.\n\n(d) The French physicist, de Broglie, in 1924 proposed that matter, like radiation, should also exhibit dual behaviour i.e., both particle and wavelike properties. This means that just as the photon has momentum as well as wavelength, electrons should also have momentum as well as wavelength. de Broglie, from this analogy, gave the following relation between wavelength (λ) and momentum (p) of a material particle:\n\n  λ = h / mv = h / p\n\n where, \n m = mass of the particle \n v = velocity of the particle\n p = momentum."\n\nReference; NEET; 2017','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','The uncertainty principle is ΔE × Δt ≥ h/4π','Half filled and fully filled orbitals have greater stability due to greater exchange energy, greater symmetry and more balanced arrangement.','The energy of 2s-orbital is less than the energy of 2p-orbital in case of hydrogen like atoms.','De-Broglie’s wavelength is given by,\n  λ = h / mv, \n where, \n m = mass of the particle, \n v = velocity of the particle.','NEW','11-051','11-051',NULL),
(22438,'chemistry','How many electrons can fit in the orbital for which n = 3 and l = 1?','2','"For n = 3 and l = 1, the subshell is 3p.\n\nThe p subshell has a maximum of 3 orbitals and each orbital contains a maximum of 2 electrons. \n\nSo, a maximum of 6 electrons fit in the 3 separate orbitals for which n=3 and l = 1, and a particular 3p orbital can accommodate only 2 electrons."\n\nReference; NEET-II; 2016','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','2','6','10','4','NEW','11-056','11-056',NULL),
(22439,'chemistry','Two electrons occupying the same orbital are distinguished by _______.','Spin quantum number','"For the two electrons occupying the same orbital, values of n, l and mₗ are same but mₛ is different,\n\ni.e., + 1/2 and - 1/2\n\nwhere, mₛ = spin quantum number."\n\nReference; NEET-I; 2016','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Azimuthal quantum number','Spin quantum number','Principal quantum number','Magnetic quantum number.','NEW','11-056','11-056',NULL),
(22440,'chemistry','Which is the correct order of increasing energy of the listed orbitals in the atom of titanium?\n (At. no. Z = 22)','3s < 3p < 4s < 3d','"Electronic configuration of titanium is,\n\nTi(22) : 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s² 3d²\n\n∴ Order of increasing energy is 3s < 3p < 4s < 3d."\n\nReference; NEET-I; 2015','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','4s < 3s < 3p < 3d','3s < 3p < 3d < 4s','3s < 3p < 4s < 3d','3s < 4s < 3p < 3d','NEW','11-060','11-060',NULL),
(22441,'chemistry','The number of d-electrons in Fe (Z = 26) is not equal to the number of electrons in which one of the following?','p-electrons in Cl (Z = 17)','"Let us consider the electronic configuration of each given option:\n\nElectronic configuration of Fe (Z = 26) is,\n\nFe (Z = 26) : 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s² 3d⁶\n\nNumber of d-electrons in Fe = 6\n\n(a) Electronic configuration of Fe⁺² (Z = 26) is,\n\nFe⁺² (Z = 26) : 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s² 3d⁴\n\nNumber of 3p-electrons in Fe⁺² = 6\n\n(b) Electronic configuration of Ne (Z = 10) is,\n\nNe (Z = 10) : 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ \n\nNumber of p-electrons in Ne = 6\n\n(c) Electronic configuration of Mg (Z = 12) is,\n\nMg (Z = 12) : 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² \n\nNumber of s-electrons in Mg = 6\n\n(d) Electronic configuration of Cl (Z = 17) is,\n\nCl (Z = 17) : 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁵\n\nNumber of p-electrons in Cl = 11\n\nIt is clear that, only the number of p-electrons in Cl differs from the number of d-electrons in Fe of all the given options."\n\nReference; NEET-I; 2015(Cancelled)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','3p-electrons in Fe⁺² (Z = 26)','p-electrons in Ne (Z = 10)','s-electrons in Mg (Z = 12)','p-electrons in Cl (Z = 17)','NEW','11-060','11-060',NULL),
(22442,'chemistry','What is the maximum number of orbitals that can be identified with the following quantum numbers?\n n = 3, l = 1, mₗ = 0','1','"Only one orbital, 3p₂ has following set of quantum numbers, n = 3, l = 1 and mₗ = 0\n \nMagnetic quantum numbers are assigned to p-orbitals as follows:\n\n p₂ : mₗ = 0 ;\n\n pₓ : 50% mₗ = +1 and 50% mₗ = −1 ;\n\n pᵧ : 50% mₗ = +1 and 50% mₗ = −1 ;\n\nThis happens because pₓ and pᵧ orbitals are not eigenstates of Ĺ₂ operator, but a linear combination of p₊₁ and p₋₁ wave functions."\n\nReference; NEET-I; 2014','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-055','11-055',NULL),
(22443,'chemistry','What is the maximum numbers of electrons that can be associated with the following set of quantum numbers?\n n = 3, l = 1 and m = –1','2','"The orbital associated with n = 3, l = 1 is 3p. \n\nThere are total of 3 3p-orbitals with 3 different mₗ values of -1, 0, +1. \n\nOne orbital (with mₗ = –1, either pₓ or pᵧ) of 3p-subshell can accommodate maximum of 2 electrons."\n\nReference; NEET; 2013','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','4','2','10','6','NEW','11-062','11-062',NULL),
(22444,'chemistry','The outer electronic configuration of Gd (At. No. 64) is _______.','[Xe] 4f⁷ 5d¹ 6s²','"The electronic configuration of ₆₄Gd is:\n\n  ₆₄Gd: [Xe] 4f⁷ 5d¹ 6s² ."\n\nReference; Karnataka NEET; 2013','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','[Xe] 4f⁵ 5d⁴ 6s¹','[Xe] 4f⁷ 5d¹ 6s²','[Xe] 4f³ 5d⁵ 6s²','[Xe] 4f⁴ 5d⁵ 6s¹','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22445,'chemistry','Maximum number of electrons in a subshell with l = 3 and n = 4 is _______.','14','"l = 3 and n = 4 represents 4f subshell. So, total number of electrons in a subshell = 2(2l + 1) = 2 (2 × 3 + 1) = 14 electrons.\n\nFor any sub-shell (defined by ‘l’ value) 2l + 1 values of mₗ are possible and these values are given by:\n\n mₗ = – l, – (l – 1), – (l – 2) ... 0, 1... (l – 2), (l – 1), l\n\nThus for l = 3, mₗ = –3, –2, –1, 0, +1, +2, +3 [2(3) + 1 = 7, seven f orbitals]. \n\nTherefore, f subshell consists of total of 7 orbitals. Each orbital can accomodate maximum of 2 electrons. Hence, f-subshell can contain maximum of 14 electrons."\n\nReference; Karnataka NEET; 2012','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','14','16','10','11','NEW','11-062','11-062',NULL),
(22446,'chemistry','The correct set of four quantum numbers for the valence electron of rubidium atom (Z = 37) is _______.','5, 0, 0, +1/2','"Electronic configuration of Rb (37) is:\n\nRb (37) : 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s² 3d¹⁰ 4p⁶ 5s¹\n\nThus, its valence electron lies in the 5s orbital\n \nFor 5s, n = 5, l = 0, m = 0, s = +1/2 or –1/2."\n\nReference; Karnataka NEET; 2012','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','5, 1, 1, +1/2','6, 0, 0, +1/2','5, 0, 0, +1/2','5, 1, 0, +1/2','NEW','11-056','11-056',NULL),
(22447,'chemistry','The total number of atomic orbitals in fourth energy level of an atom is _______.','16','"Total number of atomic orbitals in any energy level is given by n².\n\n For n = 4, n² = 16\n\nThus, 4th shell (or energy level) of an atom consists of 1 4s-orbital, 3 4p-orbitals, 5 4d-orbitals, 7 4f-orbitals."\n\nReference; Mains ; 2011','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','8','16','32','4','NEW','11-056','11-056',NULL),
(22448,'chemistry','If n = 6, the correct sequence for filling of electrons will be _______.','ns → (n – 2)f → (n – 1)d → np','"If the principle quantum number n = 6, the correct sequence of filling of electron will be:\n\n ns → (n − 2)f → (n − 1)d → np\n\nHere, n=6. Filling of 6th shell starts after Xe. \n\nElectronic configuration of elements after Xe indicates the similar pattern (except for few elements which show deviation). \n\nHence, the correct sequence of filling of electron will be 6s → 4f → 5d → 6p."\n\nReference; Mains ; 2011','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','ns → (n – 2)f → (n – 1)d → np','ns → (n – 1)d → (n – 2)f → np','ns → (n – 2)f → np → (n – 1)d','ns → np → (n – 1)d → (n – 2)f','NEW','11-060','11-060',NULL),
(22449,'chemistry','Maximum number of electrons in a subshell of an atom is determined by _______.','4l + 2','"For a given subshell, l, the number of orbitals are given by magnetic quantum number, mₗ = (2l + 1). \n\nSince each orbital can accommodate maximum of 2 electrons of opposite spin, so maximum number of electrons in a subshell = 2(2l + 1) = 4l + 2."\n\nReference; Mains ; 2009','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','2l + 1','4l – 2','2n²','4l + 2','NEW','11-062','11-062',NULL),
(22450,'chemistry','Which of the following is not permissible arrangement of electrons in an atom?','n = 3, l = 2, m = –3, s = –1/2','"In an atom, for any value of n, the values of l ranges from:\nl = 0,....., (n – 1)\n\nFor a given value of l, the values of mₗ are:\nmₗ = – l,..., 0,..., + l\n\nAnd the possible values of s are\ns = +1/2 or –1/2.\n\nIn option (a), we have n = 5, l = 3, m = 0, s = +1/2\n\nThis indicates an electron in one of the 4f orbitals with + spin. \n \nIn option (b), we have n = 3, l = 2, m = –3, s = –1/2\n\nNow for l = 2, mₗ = –3 is not possible, as possible values of mₗ for l = 2 are:\n\nmₗ = –2, –1, 0, +1 and +2 only\n\nIn option (c), we have n = 3, l = 2, m = –2, s = –1/2\n\nThis indicates an electron in one of the 3d orbitals with - spin. \n\nIn option (d), we have n = 4, l = 0, m = 0, s = –1/2\n\nThis indicates an electron in the 4s orbital with - spin."\n\nReference; Mains ; 2009','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','n = 5, l = 3, m = 0, s = +1/2','n = 3, l = 2, m = –3, s = –1/2','n = 3, l = 2, m = –2, s = –1/2','n = 4, l = 0, m = 0, s = –1/2','NEW','11-056','11-056',NULL),
(213304,'chemistry','The ready acceptance of an/a  _______ is the reason for the strong _______ nature of halogens.','electron, oxidising','The ready acceptance of an electron is the reason for the strong oxidising nature of halogens. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 200 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','electron, oxidising','electron, reducing','proton, reducing','proton, oxidising','NEW','12-200',NULL,NULL),
(22451,'chemistry','Consider the following sets of quantum numbers:\n \n n l m s\n \n (i) 3 0 0 +1/2\n (ii) 2 2 1 +1/2\n (iii) 4 3 –2 –1/2\n (iv) 1 0 –1 –1/2\n (v) 3 2 3 +1/2\n \n Which of these sets of quantum number is not possible?','(ii), (iv) and (v)','"(i) represents an electron in 3s orbital.\n\n(ii) is not possible as value of l varies from 0, 1, ... (n – 1).\n\n(iii) represents an electron in 4f orbital.\n\n(iv) is not possible as value of mₗ varies from – l ... + l.\n\n(v) is not possible as value of mₗ varies from – l, ... + l, it can never be greater than l."\n\nReference; Mains ; 2007','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii), (iii) and (iv)','(ii), (iv) and (v)','(i) and (iii)','(ii), (iii) and (iv)','NEW','11-056','11-056',NULL),
(22452,'chemistry','The orientation of an atomic orbital is governed by _______.','Magnetic quantum number','"Principal quantum number represents the name, size and energy of the shell to which the electron belongs. \n\nAzimuthal quantum number describes the spatial distribution of electron cloud and angular momentum. \n\nMagnetic quantum number describes the orientation or distribution of electron cloud. \n\nSpin quantum number represents the direction of electron spin around its own axis."\n\nReference; Mains ; 2006','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Principal quantum number','Azimuthal quantum number','Spin quantum number','Magnetic quantum number','NEW','11-055','11-055',NULL),
(22453,'chemistry','The following quantum numbers are possible for how many orbitals?\n n = 3, l = 2, m = +2','1','" n = 3, l = 2, mₗ = +2\n\nIt symbolises one of the five 3d-orbitals.\n\nl = 2 is the d subshell and it has five orbitals. The magnetic quantum numbers for d subshell are −2, −1, 0, 1, and 2. \n\nmₗ = 0 is the d₂² orbital, which is oriented along the z-axis. \n\nThe orbitals dₓ₂ and dᵧ₂ have mₗ = ±1 and lie in the xz and yz planes. \n\nmₗ = ±2 corresponds to dₓᵧ and dₓ² − ᵧ²; both lie in the xy plane."\n\nReference; Mains ; 2001','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-056','11-056',NULL),
(22454,'chemistry','Electronic configuration of calcium atom can be written as _______.','[Ar] 4s²','"Atomic no. of Ca = 20. It comes after the nobel gas Argon (Z = 18) in the periodic table.\n\n∴ Electronic configuration of Ca = [Ar] 4s²."\n\nReference; Mains ; 1992','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','[Ne] 4p²','[Ar] 4s²','[Ne] 4s²','[Kr] 4p²','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22455,'chemistry','In a given atom no two electrons can have the same values for all the four quantum numbers. This is called _______.','Pauli’s Exclusion principle','" (a) Hund\'s rule of maximum multiplicity states that for a given electron configuration, the lowest energy term is the one with the greatest value of spin multiplicity. This implies that if two or more orbitals of equal energy are available, electrons will occupy them singly before filling them in pairs.\n\n(b) The Aufbau principle states that in the ground state of the atoms, the orbitals are filled in order of their increasing energies. In other words, electrons first occupy the lowest energy orbital available to them and enter into higher energy orbitals only after the lower energy orbitals are filled.\n\n(c) Heisenberg’s Uncertainty Principle states that it is impossible to determine simultaneously, the exact position and exact momentum (or velocity) of an electron.\n\n(d) The Pauli Exclusion Principle states that, in an atom or molecule, no two electrons can have the same four quantum numbers. Only two electrons may exist in the same orbital and these electrons must have opposite spin."\n\nReference; Mains ; 1991','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Hund’s Rule','Aufbau principle','Uncertainty principle','Pauli’s Exclusion principle','NEW','11-062','11-062',NULL),
(22456,'chemistry','For azimuthal quantum number l = 3, the maximum number of electrons will be _______.','14','" l = 3 means f-subshell. \n\nThere are total of 7 f-orbitals, each can have maximum of 2 electrons. \n\nThus, maximum no. of electrons in f-subshell = 14."\n\nReference; Mains ; 1991','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','2','6','0','14','NEW','11-055','11-055',NULL),
(22457,'chemistry','The order of filling of electrons in the orbitals of an atom will be _______.','4s, 3d, 4p, 5s, 4d','"In the ground state of the atoms, the orbitals are filled in order of their increasing energies. Mathematically, the dependence of energies of the orbitals on their n and l values is quite complicated but one simple rule is that, the lower the value of (n + l) for an orbital, the lower is its energy. If two orbitals have the same value of (n + l), the orbital with lower value of n will have the lower energy.\n\nNow (n + l) values for 4d, 5p, 5f and 6p orbitals are,\n\n  3d = 3 + 2 = 5\n  4s = 4 + 0 = 4\n  4p = 4 + 1 = 5\n  4d = 4 + 2 = 6\n  5s = 5 + 0 = 5\n\n∴ Increasing order of obital energy would be, \n  4s < 3d < 4p < 5s < 4d."\n\nReference; Mains ; 1991','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','3d, 4s, 4p, 4d, 5s','4s, 3d, 4p, 5s, 4d','4s, 4p, 3d, 4d, 5s','3d, 4p, 4s, 4d, 5s','NEW','11-060','11-060',NULL),
(22458,'chemistry','The total number of electrons that can be accommodated in all the orbitals having principal quantum number 2 and azimuthal quantum number 1 are _______.','6','" n = 2, l = 1 indicates 2p-orbitals.\n\nSo, total number of electrons in a p-subshell = 2(2l + 1) = 2 (2 × 1 + 1) = 6 electrons."\n\nReference; Mains ; 1990','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','2','4','6','8','NEW','11-062','11-062',NULL),
(22459,'chemistry','The maximum number of electrons in a subshell is given by the expression _______.','4l + 2','"For a given subshell, l, the number of orbitals are given by magnetic quantum number, mₗ = (2l + 1). \n\nSince each orbital can accommodate maximum of 2 electrons of opposite spin, so maximum number of electrons in a subshell = 2(2l + 1) = 4l + 2."\n\nReference; Mains ; 1989','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','4l – 2','4l + 2','2l + 2','2n²','NEW','11-062','11-062',NULL),
(22460,'chemistry','The number of spherical nodes in 3p orbitals are/is _______.','1','"No. of radial nodes = n – l – 1\n\nFor 3p-orbitals, n = 3, l = 1\n\n∴ No. of radial / spherical nodes in 3p-orbitals = 3 – 1 – 1 = 1."\n\nReference; Mains ; 1988','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-059','11-059',NULL),
(22461,'chemistry','Uncertainty in position of an electron\n \n (Mass = 9.1 × 10⁻²⁸ g) moving with a velocity of 3 × 104cm/s accurate upto 0.001% will be\n \n (Use h/(4) in uncertainty expression where h = 6.626 × 10⁻²⁷erg second).\n \n Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','1.93 cm','Refer diagram(ne203)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','5.76 cm','7.68 cm','1.93 cm','3.84 cm','NEW','11-051','11-051',NULL),
(22462,'chemistry','Which one is a wrong statement?\n \n (a) Total orbital angular momentum of electron in s-orbital is equal to zero.\n \n (b) An orbital is designated by three quantum numbers while an electron in an atom is\n designated by four quantum numbers.\n \n (c) The electronic configuration of N atom is\n \n 1s² 2s² 2px¹ 2py¹ 2pz¹.\n \n (d) The value of m for dz₂ is zero. \n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(c)','"According to Hund’s rule of maximum\nmultiplicity, the correct configuration of \'N\' is,\n\n1s² 2s² 2px¹ 2py¹ 2pz¹ "\n\nRefrence: NEET 2018','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','(a)','(b)','(c)','(d)','NEW','11-055','11-055',NULL),
(22463,'chemistry','Which of the following pairs of d-orbitals will have\n electron density along the axes?\n \n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','dz², dx²-y²','“ dx² – y² and dz² orbitals have electron density along the axes while dxy, dyz and dxz orbitals have electron density in between the axes.”\n\nReference: NEET-II 2016','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','dz², dxz','dxz, dyz','dz², dx²-y²','dxy, dx²-y²','NEW','11-059','11-059',NULL),
(22464,'chemistry','The angular momentum of electron in ‘d’ orbital is equal to,\n \n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','√6ℏ','Refer diagram(ne204)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','2√3ℏ','ℏ','√6ℏ','√2ℏ','NEW','11-055','11-055',NULL),
(22465,'chemistry','The orbital angular momentum of a p-electron is given as,\n \n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','h/√2π','Refer diagram(ne205)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','h/√2π','√3×ℎ/2π','√3/2×ℎ/π','√6×ℎ/2π','NEW','11-055','11-055',NULL),
(22466,'chemistry','For which of the following sets of four quantum numbers, an electron will have the highest energy?\n \n a) n l m s\n \n b) 3 2 1 +1/2\n \n c) 4 2 -1 +1/2\n \n d) 4 1 0 -1/2\n \n e) 5 0 0 -1/2','(b)','"Energy of electron depends on the value of (n + l). The subshell are 3d, 4d, 4p and 5s, out of which 4d has the highest energy."\n\nReference: Mains 1994','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','(a)','(b)','(c)','(d)','NEW','11-060','11-060',NULL),
(22467,'chemistry','The electronic configuration of Cu (atomic number 29) is,\n \n i. 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s² 3d⁹\n \n ii. 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d¹⁰ 4s¹\n \n iii. 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s² 4p⁶ 5s² 5p¹\n \n iv. 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s² 4p⁶ 3d³','(ii)','"Electronic configuration of Cu is,\n 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d¹⁰ 4s¹ "\n\nReference: Mains 1991','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','(i)','(ii)','(iii)','(iv)','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22468,'chemistry','An ion has 18 electrons in the outermost shell, it is,','Cu⁺','" Cu+ ion has 18 electrons in its outermost shell.\n\nElectronic configuration of Cu+ is,\n\n1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d¹⁰ "\n\nReference: Mains 1990.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Cu⁺','Th⁺','Cs⁺','K⁺','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22469,'chemistry','Number of unpaired electrons in N²⁺ is/are,','1','“N²⁺ = 1s²2s²2p¹\n\nHence, No. of unpaired electrons = 1”\n\nReference: Mains 1989.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','2','0','1','3','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22470,'chemistry','Tritium, a radioactive isotope of hydrogen, emits which of the following particles?','Beta(β⁻)','"Tritium is radioactive and emits low energy E– particles ( –1 e°)."\n\nReference; NEET; 2021','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Alpha (α)','Beta(β⁻)','Gamma (γ)','Neutron (n)','NEW','11-033','11-033',NULL),
(22471,'chemistry','A particular station of All India Radio, New Delhi, broadcasts on a frequen cy o f 1, 368 kHz (kilohertz). \n \n The wavelength of the electromagnetic radiation emitted by the transmitter is : [speed of light c = 3.0 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹ ].','219.3 m','" λ=c/v\n\nλ= 3.0 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹/1368 × 10³ = 219.298m 219.3 m."\n\nReference; NEET; 2021','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','219.2 m','209.2 m','21.92cm','219.3 m','NEW','11-039','11-039',NULL),
(22472,'chemistry','From the following pairs of ions which one is not an iso-electronic pair?','Fe⁺²  , Mn⁺²','" ₂₆Fe → 3d⁶ 4s² , Fe⁺² → 3d⁶ (Total no. of electrons= 24)\n\n ₂₅Mn → 3d⁵ 4s², Mn⁺²→ 3d⁵ (Total no. of electrons=23)."\n\nReference; NEET; 2021','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','O⁻², F⁻','Na⁺, Mg⁺²','Fe⁺²  , Mn⁺³','Fe⁺²  , Mn⁺²','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22473,'chemistry','Identify the incorrect statement from the following.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','The shapes of dxy, dyz and dzx orbitals are similar to each other; and dx²-y² and dz²  are similar to each other','"In an atom, all the five 3d orbitals are equal in energy in free state i.e., degenerate \nThe shape of  dx²-y² is different then shape of dz².\n\nThe size of orbital depends on principal quantum number ‘n’ therefore all the five 3d orbitals are different in size when compared to the respective 4d orbitals.\n\nShape of orbitals depends on azimuthal quantum number ‘l’ therefore shapes of 4d orbitals are similar to the respective 3d orbitals."\n\nReference; NEET; 2022','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','All the five 5d orbitals are different in size when compared to the respective 4d orbitals.','All the five 4d orbitals have shapes similar to the respective 3d orbitals','In an atom, all the five 3d orbitals are equal in energy in free state','The shapes of dxy, dyz and dzx orbitals are similar to each other; and dx²-y² and dz²  are similar to each other','NEW','11-059','11-059',NULL),
(22474,'chemistry','If radius of second Bohr orbit of the He⁺ ion is 105.8 pm, what is the radius of third Bohr orbit of Li⁺² ion?','158.7 pm','Refer diagram(ne202)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','158.7 pm','15.87 pm','1.587 pm','158.7 Å','NEW','11-047','11-047',NULL),
(22475,'chemistry','When electromagnetic radiation of wavelength 300nm falls on the surface of a metal, electrons are emitted with the kinetic energy of 1.68 × 10⁵ J mol⁻¹ . The minimum energy needed to remove one mole of electron from the metal is: (h = 6.62 ×10⁻³⁴ Js, c = 3 × 10¹⁰ cm s⁻¹, NA = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)','2.31 × 10⁵ J mol⁻¹','Refer diagram (22475E) NEET 2022','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','2.31 × 10⁶ J mol⁻¹','3.84 × 10⁴ J mol⁻¹','3.84× 10⁻¹⁰ J mol⁻¹','2.31 × 10⁵ J mol⁻¹','NEW','11-042','11-042',NULL),
(22476,'chemistry','A monochromatic infrared range finder of power 1mW emits photons with wavelength 1000nm in 0.1 second. The number of photons emitted in 0.1 second is- Refer diagram (22476Q)','2','Refer diagram (22476E) NEET 2022','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-041','12-041','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/22476q.png'),
(22477,'chemistry','Incorrect statements among the following is: (Refer diagram 22477Q)','3','Refer diagram (22477E) NEET 2017','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22478,'chemistry','Ionization energy of second orbit of Li²⁺ will be','30.6 eV','Li²⁺ is a one electron system. The formula of energy of orbit is, E = -13.6 eV Z² / n² . For Li, Z=3 and we have n=2. (AIPMT-1999)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','122.4eV','40.8eV','30.6 eV','13.6 eV','NEW','11-041','11-041',NULL),
(213305,'chemistry','Which is the strong oxidising halogen?','F₂','F₂ is the strong oxidising halogen. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 200 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','F₂','Br₂','Ts₂','Cl₂','NEW','12-200',NULL,NULL),
(22479,'chemistry','Match the following. Refer diagram (22479Q)','A-(III), B-(IV), C-(II), D-(I)','(i) The Principal Quantum Number represents the principal energy level or shell in which an electron revolves around the nucleus. It is denoted by the letter n and can have any integral value except 0 i.e. n = 1, 2, 3, 4, etc.\n(ii) The azimuthal quantum number is a quantum number for an atomic orbital that determines its orbital angular momentum and describes the shape of the orbital. It takes up values from 0 to n-1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','A-(III), B-(IV), C-(I), D-(II)','A-(IV), B-(III), C-(I), D-(II)','A-(IV), B-(III), C-(II), D-(I)','A-(III), B-(IV), C-(II), D-(I)','NEW','11-056','11-056','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/22479q.png'),
(22480,'chemistry','The relation between n (number of permissible values of magnetic quantum number) for a given value of azimuthal quantum number(l) is: Refer diagram (22480Q)','2','The relation between n and l is as follows: n = 2l+1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-055','11-055','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/22480q.png'),
(22481,'chemistry','The number of angular nodes and radial nodes in 3s orbital are:','0 and 2 respectively','No. of angular nodes = l\nNo. of Radial nodes = n-l-1\nFor 3s; n=3 and l=0\n∴∴No. of angular nodes = 0\n∴∴No. of radial nodes = 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','0 and 2 respectively','1 and 0 respectively','3 and 0 respectively','0 and 1 respectively','NEW','11-059','11-059',NULL),
(22482,'chemistry','Which one of the following electrons in ground state will have least amount of energy?','An electron in hydrogen atom','Energy of orbital is given by n+l\nAn electron in a hydrogen atom is present in the 1s orbital. The value of n+l for 1s is 1.\nAn electron in the 2p orbital of a carbon atom. The value of n+l for 2p is 2+1 = 3.\nThe electron of copper atom present in 4s orbital. The value of n+l for 4s is 4+0 = 4.\nThe outermost electron in the sodium atom is present in the 3s orbital. The value of n+l for 3s is 3+0 = 3.\nHence, 1s has the least value of n+l. Thus, 1s is the answer.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','An electron in hydrogen atom','An electron in 2p orbital of carbon atom','The electron of copper atom present in 4s orbital','The outermost electron in sodium atom','NEW','11-060','11-060',NULL),
(22501,'chemistry','Indicate the wrong statements from the following.\n \n (i) The existence of atoms has been proposed since the time of early Indian and Greek philosophers (1400 B.C.).\n \n (ii) The ideas about the atom were just mere speculations up to the 19th century.\n \n (iii) Dalton\'s theory successfully explained the results of many experiments like substances like glass or ebonite when rubbed with silk or fur get electrically charged.\n \n (iv) John Dalton stated about the atom that “atom is the complex particle of matter” which was denied by scientists at the beginning of the 20th century.\n \n (v) John Dalton stated about the atom that “atom is the ultimate particle of matter” which was denied by scientists at the beginning of the 20th century.\n \n (vi) John Dalton stated about the atom that “atom is the complex particle of matter” which was accepted by scientists at the beginning of the 20th century.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(i), (iii), (iv) and (vi)','"The existence of atoms has been proposed since the time of early Indian and Greek philosophers (400 B.C.) who were of the view that atoms are the fundamental building blocks of matter." \n\n“These earlier ideas were mere speculations and there was no way to test them experimentally. These ideas remained dormant  for a very long time and were revived again by scientists in the nineteenth century.”\n\n“Dalton’s atomic theory regarded the atom as the ultimate particle of matter. Dalton’s atomic theory was able to explain the law of conservation of mass, law of constant composition, and law of multiple proportion very successfully. \n\nHowever, it failed to explain the results of many experiments, for example, it was known that substances like glass or ebonite when rubbed with silk or fur get electrically charged. \n\n“Experimental observations made by scientists towards the end of nineteenth and beginning of the twentieth century. These established that atoms are made of sub-atomic particles, i.e., electrons,  protons, and neutrons — a concept very different from that of Dalton.”\n\nReference; NCERT, class 11, Page 29, Paragraphs 1,2,3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(i), (iii), (iv) and (vi)','(i), (ii), (iv) and (vi)','(i), (iii), (iv) and (v)','All are incorrect','NEW','11-029','11-029',NULL),
(22502,'chemistry','Given below are four statements.\n \n (i) An insight into the structure of atom was obtained from the experiments on electrical discharge through gases. \n \n (ii) A cathode ray tube is made of glass containing two thin pieces of non-metal, called electrodes.\n \n (iii) The pressure of different gases could be adjusted by evacuation of the glass tubes.\n \n (iv) The present accepted value of electrical charge is 1.602176 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','All are correct but except (ii) and (iv)','“An insight into the structure of atom was obtained from the experiments on electrical discharge through gases.”\n\n“A cathode ray tube is made of glass containing two thin pieces of metal,\ncalled electrodes, sealed in it.”\n\n“The pressure of different ases could be adjusted by evacuation of the\nglass tubes.”\n\n“The present accepted value of electrical charge is  -1.602176 × 10⁻¹⁹C.”\n\nReference; NCERT, class 11, Page 30, Paragraphs 1,3 and Page 31, Paragraph 5','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','All are correct but except (ii) and (iv)','All are correct but except (i)','All are correct but except (iv)','All are correct','NEW','11-030','11-030',NULL),
(22503,'chemistry','Given below are five statements.\n \n (i) Experiments suggested that Dalton’s divisible atom is composed of sub-atomic particles carrying positive and negative charges. \n \n (ii) Experiments suggested that Dalton’s indivisible atom is composed of sub-atomic particles carrying positive and negative charges. \n \n (iii)The positively charged particle was characterised in 1919.\n \n (iv) Thomson model was proposed to explain the size of the nucleus.\n \n (v)Since Röentgen did not know the nature of the radiation, he named them β-rays and the name is still carried on. \n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','All are correct but not (ii) and (iii)','"The smallest and lightest positive ion was obtained from hydrogen and was called proton. This positively charged particle was characterised in 1919."\n\n"Observations obtained from the experiments mentioned in the previous sections have suggested that Dalton’s indivisible atom is composed of sub-atomic particles carrying positive and negative charges."\n\n"Different atomic models were proposed to explain the distributions of these charged particles in an atom. Although some of these models were not able to explain the stability of atoms, two of these models, one proposed by J.J. Thomson and the other proposed by Ernest Rutherford were widely accepted."\n\n"Since Röentgen did not know the nature of the radiation, he named them X-rays and the name is still carried on."\n\nReference; NCERT, class 11, Page 32, Paragraphs 2,3 and Page 33, Paragraphs 1,3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','All are correct but not (ii) and (v)','All are correct but not (ii) and (iii)','All are correct but not (ii) and (iv)','All are incorrect','NEW','11-032','11-032',NULL),
(22504,'chemistry','Given below are six statements.\n \n (i) X-rays having very short wavelength of ~0.01 nm.\n \n (ii) X-rays having very short wavelength of ~0.1 nm.\n \n (iii) The field Radioactivity was only developed by Marie Curie and Piere Curie.\n \n (iv) Rutherford and his students (Niels Bohr and Ernest Marsden) bombarded very thin gold foil with α–particles.\n \n (v) Rutherford and his students (Hans Geiger and Ernest Marsden) bombarded very thin gold foil with α–particles.\n \n (vi) Maxwell’s theory of electromagnetic waves and the emission of such waves by accelerating charged particles was confirmed later by Heinrich Hertz.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (i), (iii) and (iv)','"These rays are of very short wavelengths (~0.1 nm) and possess electro-magnetic character."\n\n"Henri Becqueral (1852-1908) observed that there are certain elements which emit radiation on their own and named this phenomenon as radioactivity and the elements known as radioactive elements. This field was developed by Marie Curie, Piere Curie, Rutherford and Fredrick Soddy."\n\n"Rutherford and his students (Hans Geiger and Ernest Marsden) bombarded very thin gold foil with a–particles."\n\nReference; NCERT, class 11, Page 33, Paragraphs 3,4 and Page 37, Paragraph 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Only (i), (iii) and (v)','Only (i), (iii) and (iv)','Only (i)and (iv)','Only (iii) and (vi)','NEW','11-033','11-033',NULL),
(22505,'chemistry','Which one is among the two developments that played a major role in the formulations of Bohr’s Atomic model.\n \n (i) The positive charge and most of the mass of the atom was densely concentrated in extremely small region. This very small portion of the atom was called nucleus by Rutherford.\n \n (ii) The nucleus is surrounded by electrons that move around the nucleus with a very high speed in circular paths called orbits. Thus, Rutherford’s model of atom resembles the solar system in which the nucleus plays the role of sun and the electrons that of revolving planets.\n \n (iii) Electrons and the nucleus are held together by electrostatic forces of attraction.\n \n (iv) Experimental results regarding atomic spectra.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (iv)','“In the conclusion, Rutherford proposed the nuclear model of atom. According to this model:\n\n(i) The positive charge and most of the mass of the atom was densely concentrated in extremely small region. This very small portion of the atom was called nucleus by Rutherford.\n\n(ii) The nucleus is surrounded by electrons that move around the nucleus with a very high speed in circular paths called orbits. Thus, Rutherford’s model of atom resembles the solar system in which the nucleus plays the role of sun and the electrons that of revolving planets.\n\n(iii) Electrons and the nucleus are held together by electrostatic forces of attraction.”\n\n“Twodevelopments played a major role in the formulation of Bohr’s model of atom. These were: \n\n(i) Dual character of the electromagnetic radiation which means that radiations possess both wave like and particle like properties, and \n\n(ii) Experimental results regarding atomic spectra.”\n\nReference; NCERT, class 11, Page 35, Paragraph 1 and Page 37, Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only (iii)','Only (iv)','Only (i)','Only (ii)','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(22506,'chemistry','Indicate the incorrect statements. \n \n (i)It is shown by the calculations that an electron should take only 10⁻⁸s to spiral into the nucleus.\n \n (ii)Interactions of radiations with matter have provided immense information regarding the structure of atoms and molecules.\n \n (iii)Light is the form of radiation known from early days and speculation about its nature dates back to remote ancient times.\n \n (iv)Maxwell was the first to reveal that light waves are associated with oscillating electric and magnetic character.\n \n (v)Classical mechanics is a theoretical science based on Newton’s laws of motion. It specifies the laws of motion of macroscopic objects.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','None is incorrect','"Calculations show that it should take an electron only 10⁻⁸ s to spiral into the nucleus. But this does not happen. Thus, the Rutherford model cannot explain the stability of an atom."\n\n"Classical mechanics is a theoretical science based on Newton’s laws of motion. It specifies the laws of motion ofmacroscopic objects."\n\n"interactions of radiations with matter haveprovided immense information regarding thestructure of atoms and molecules."\n\n"Light is the form of radiation known fromearly days and speculation about its naturedates back to remote ancient times."\n\n"Maxwell was again the first to reveal thatlight waves are associated with oscillatingelectric and magnetic character."\n\nReference; NCERT; class 11; Page 36, Paragraph 2 and Page 37, Paragraph 2,5,6','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','All are incorrect','None is incorrect','Only (i) and (ii) are incorrect','Only (v) is incorrect','NEW','11-036','11-036',NULL),
(22507,'chemistry','Indicate the incorect staements.\n \n (i) Different kinds of units are used to represent electromagnetic radiation.\n \n (ii) These radiations are characterised by the properties, namely, frequency(ν), wavelength (λ) and their velocity(v).\n \n (iii) The SI unit for frequency (ν) is hertz (Hz, s⁻¹), after Henry Hertz.\n \n (iv) Since electromagnetic radiation consists of different kinds of waves of much larger wavelengths, so meter as unit is used.\n \n (v) It is been observed that various types of electromagnetic radiations which differ from one another in wavelengths and frequencies.\n \n (vi)The other commonly used quantity specially in spectroscopy, is the wavelength (λ).\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (ii), (iii), (iv) and (vi)','“Different kinds of units are used to represent electromagnetic radiation.\n\nThese radiations are characterised by the properties, namely, frequency (n ) and wavelength (l).\n\nThe SI unit for frequency (n ) is hertz (Hz, s–1), after Heinrich Hertz. It is defined as the number of waves that pass a given point in one second.\n\nWavelength should have the units of length and as you know that the SI units of length is meter (m). \n\nSince electromagnetic radiation consists of different kinds of waves of much smaller wavelengths, smaller units are used.Fig.2.7 shows various types of electromagnetic radiations which differ from one another in wavelengths and frequencies.”\n\n“Other commonly used quantity specially in spectroscopy, is the wavenumber(ṽ). It is defined as the number of wavelengths per unit length.”\n\nReference; NCERT; class 11; Page 38, Paragraph 1,2,3 and Page 39, Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only (iii), (iv) and (vi)','Only (ii), (iii) and (iv)','Only (ii), (iii), (iv) and (vi)','Only (ii), (iii) and (vi)','NEW','11-038','11-038',NULL),
(22508,'chemistry','Indicate the correct statements.\n \n (i) The units of wavenumber are reciprocal of wavelength unit, i.e., m⁻¹. However commonly used unit is cm⁻¹.\n \n (ii) Wavenumber has no SI unit.\n \n (iii) The phenomenon, black -body radiation and photoelectric effect could not be explained by the electromagnetic theory.\n \n (iv) The phenomenon, black -body radiation and photoelectric effect could be explained by the electromagnetic theory.\n \n (v) The system can take energy only in discrete amounts. All possible energies cannot be taken up or radiated.\n \n (vi) Interference is the combination of two waves of the same or different wavelengths.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (I) and (v) are correct','"The other commonly used quantity specially in spectroscopy, is the wavenumber (ṽ). It is defined as the number of wavelengths per unit length. Its units are reciprocal of wavelength unit, i.e., m⁻¹. However commonly used unit is cm⁻¹ (not SI unit)."\n\n"Some of the experimental phenomenon such as diffraction* and interference** can be explained by the wave nature of the electromagnetic radiation. However, following are some of the observations which could not be explained with the help of even the electromagentic theory of 19th centuryphysics (known as classical physics):\n\n(i) the nature of emission of radiation from hot bodies (black -body radiation) \n\n(ii) ejection of electrons from metal surface when radiation strikes it (photoelectric effect)\n\n"These phenomena indicate that the systemcan take energy only in discrete amounts. All possible energies cannot be taken up or radiated."\n\n"Interference is the combination of two waves of the same or different frequencies to give a wave whose distribution at each point in space is the algebraic or vector sum of disturbances at that point resulting from each interfering wave."\n\nReference; NCERT; class 11; Page 39, Paragraph 1,2 and Page 40, Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','All are correct but not (iv) and (vi)','All are correct but not (ii),(iv) and (vi)','Only (I) and (V) are correct','All are correct but not (i),(iv) and (v)','NEW','11-039','11-039',NULL),
(31119,'chemistry','Assertion: The decrease in electronegativity down a group is accompanied by a decrease in non-metallic properties.\n\nReason: Non-metallic elements have strong tendency to gain electrons.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Non-metallic elements have strong tendency to gain electrons. \n\nTherefore, electronegativity is directly related to that non-metallic properties of elements. It can be further extended to say that the electronegativity is inversely related to the metallic properties of elements. \n\nThus, the increase in electronegativities across a period is accompanied by an increase in non-metallic properties (or decrease in metallic properties) of elements. \n\nSimilarly, the decrease in electronegativity down a group is accompanied by a decrease in non-metallic properties (or increase in metallic properties) of elements.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 92; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronegativity',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are false.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','NEW','11-092','11-092',NULL),
(22509,'chemistry','Indicate the incorrect statements.\n \n (i) For each metal, there is a characteristic minimum frequency,also known as threshold frequency below which photoelectric effect is not observed. \n \n Threshold frequency is denoted by vₑ.\n \n (ii) Quantisation has been compared to standing on a staircase. \n \n (iii) If the frequency of radiation > threshold frequency of metal, the ejected electrons come out with certain kinetic energy. \n \n (iv) Shining a beam of light on to a metal surface can, therefore, be viewed as shooting a beam of particles, the photons.\n \n (v) Greater the energy possessed by the photon, greater will be transfer of energy to the electron and greater the potential energy of the ejected electron.\n \n (vi) The no. photons do not depend upon the brightness of the beam.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (i), (v) and (vi)','“Quantisation has been compared to standing on a staircase. A person can stand on any step of a staircase, but it is not possible for him/her to stand in between the two steps.”\n\n“For each metal, there is a characteristic minimum frequency,vₒ (also known as threshold frequency) below which photoelectric effect is not observed. At a frequency v >vₒ, the ejected electrons come out with certain kinetic energy. The kinetic energies of these electrons increase with the increase of frequency of the light used.”\n\n“Shining a beam of light on to a metal surface can, therefore, be viewed as shooting a beam of particles, the photons.”\n\n“Greater the energy possessed by the photon, greater will be transfer of energy to the electron and greater the kinetic energy of the ejected electron. In other words, kinetic energy of the ejected electron is proportional to the frequency of the electromagnetic radiation.”\n\n“Lastly, a more intense beam of light consists of larger number of photons, consequently the number of electrons ejected is also larger as compared to that in an experiment in which a beam of weaker intensity of light is employed.”\n\nReference; NCERT; class 11; page 41; paragraphs 3,4 and page 42; paragraphs 2,3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only (i), (v) and (vi)','Only (i) and (v)','Only (vi)','Only (i) and (vi)','NEW','11-041','11-041',NULL),
(22510,'chemistry','Indicate the incorrect statement(s).\n \n (i) The wave nature of light posed a dilemma for scientists. It could explain the black body radiation and photoelectric effect.\n \n (ii) The particle nature of light was consistent with the known wave behaviour of light which could account for the phenomena of interference and diffraction.\n \n (iii) Accepting dual nature of light was the only solution to resolve the dilemma.\n \n (iv) The concept(dual nature of light) was totally alien to the way the scientists thought about matter and radiation but it took them some time to become convinced of its validity.\n \n (v) Microscopic particles like electrons also do not exhibit this wave-particle duality.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (i), (ii), (iv)  and (v)','"Dual Behaviour of Electromagnetic Radiation\n\nThe particle nature of light posed a dilemma for scientists. On the one hand, it could explain the black body radiation and photoelectric effect satisfactorily but on the other hand, it was not consistent with the known wave behaviour of light which could account for the phenomena of interference and diffraction. The only way to resolve the dilemma was to accept the idea that light possesses both particle and wave-like properties, i.e., light has dual behaviour. Depending on the experiment, we find that light behaves either as a wave or as a stream of particles. Whenever radiation interacts with matter, it displays particle like properties in contrast to the wavelike properties (interference and diffraction), which it exhibits when it propagates. This concept was totally alien to the way the scientists thought about matter and radiation and it took them a long time to become convinced of itsvalidity. It turns out, as you shall see later,that some microscopic particles like electronsalso exhibit this wave-particle duality."\n\nReference; NCERT; class 11; page 42; paragraphs 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only (iii)','Only (i), (ii), (iv)  and (v)','Only (iv) and (v)','Only (i) and (v)','NEW','11-042','11-042',NULL),
(22511,'chemistry','Indicate the incorrect statements.\n \n (i)As we know speed of light depends upon the nature of the medium through which it passes. But the beam of light is not deviated or refracted from its original path as it passes from one medium to another.\n \n (ii)The ordinary white light consists of waves with all the wavelengths in the UV-range.\n \n (iii)When the ordinary light passes through the prism, the light of red colour which has longest wavelength is deviated the most while the violet light, which has shortest wavelength is deviated the least.\n \n (iv)A similar spectrum(as prism) is produced when a rainbow forms in the sky. Remember that visible light is a large portion of the electromagnetic radiation.\n \n (v)When electromagnetic radiation interacts with matter, atoms and molecules may absorb energy and reach to a higher energy state. With higher energy, these are in an stable state.\n \n (vi)For returning to their normal energy state, the atoms and molecules gain more radiations in various regions of the electromagnetic spectrum.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','All are incorrect','"The speed of light depends upon the nature of the medium through which it passes. As a result, the beam of light is deviated or refracted from its original path as it passes from one medium to another."\n\n"Since ordinary white light consists of waves with all the wavelengths in the visible range, a ray of white light is spread out into a series of coloured bands called spectrum."\n\n"The light of red colour which has longest wavelength is deviated the least while the violet light, which has shortest wavelength is deviated the most."\n\n"A similar spectrum is produced when a rainbow forms in the sky. Remember that visible light is just a small portion of the electromagnetic radiation (Fig.2.7). When electromagnetic radiation interacts with matter, atoms and molecules may absorb energy and reach to a higher energy state. With higher energy, these are in an unstable state. For returning to their normal (more stable, lower energy states) energy state, the atoms and molecules emit radiations in various regions of the electromagnetic spectrum."\n\nReference; NCERT; class 11; page 44; paragraphs 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','All are incorrect but except (ii) and (iv)','All are incorrect but except (ii)','All are incorrect','All are incorrect but except (iii),(v) and (vi)','NEW','11-044','11-044',NULL),
(22512,'chemistry','Indicate the correct statements.\n \n (i) When a specie has absorbed radiation it is said to be “excited”.\n \n (ii) To produce an emission spectrum, energy is supplied to a sample by heating it or irradiating it and the wavelength (or frequency) of the radiation emitted, as the sample gives up the absorbed energy, is Recorded.\n \n (iii) An absorption spectrum is like the colourful photographic image of an emission spectrum.\n \n (iv) A continuum of radiation is passed through a sample which absorbs radiation of certain wavelengths. The certain wavelength which corresponds to the radiation absorbed by the matter, leave dark spaces in the bright continuous spectrum.\n \n (v) The characteristic lines in atomic spectra can be used to identify unknown atoms in the same way as fingerprints are used to identify people.\n \n (vi)The exact matching of lines of the emission spectrum of the atoms of an unknown element with the lines from an known sample quickly establishes the identity.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (i), (ii), (iv) and (v)','"Emission and Absorption Spectra \n\nThe spectrum of radiation emitted by a substance that has absorbed energy is called an emission spectrum. Atoms, molecules or ions that have absorbed radiation are said to be “excited”. To produce an emission spectrum, energy is supplied to a sample by heating it or irradiating it and the wavelength (or frequency) of the radiation emitted, as the sample gives up the absorbed energy, is recorded.\n\nAn absorption spectrum is like the photographic negative of an emission spectrum. A continuum of radiation is passed through a sample which absorbs radiation of certain wavelengths. The missing wavelength which corresponds to the radiation absorbed by the matter, leave dark spaces in the bright continuous spectrum. The study of emission or absorption spectra is referred to as spectroscopy. The spectrum of the visible light, as discussed above, was continuous as all wavelengths (red to violet) of the visible light are represented in the spectra. The emission spectra of atoms in the gas phase, on the other hand, do not show a continuous spread of wavelength from red to violet, rather they emit light only at specific wavelengths with dark spaces between them. Such spectra are called line spectra or atomic spectra because the emitted radiation is identified by the appearance of bright lines in the spectra (Fig. 2.10 page 45). Line emission spectra are of great interest in the study of electronic structure. Each element has a unique line emission spectrum. The characteristic lines in atomic spectra can be used in chemical analysis to identify unknown atoms in the same way as fingerprints are used to identify people. The exact matching of lines of the emission spectrum of the atoms of a known element with the lines from an unknown sample quickly establishes the identity of the latter, German chemist, Robert Bunsen (1811-1899) was one of the first investigators to use line spectra to identify elements. Elements like rubidium."\n\nReference; NCERT; class 11; page 44; paragraphs 2,3,4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only (ii), (iv) and (v)','Only (i) and  (ii)','Only (i), (ii), (iv) and (v)','Only (i), (ii) and (iv)','NEW','11-044','11-044',NULL),
(31120,'chemistry','Do the electronegativity of any element varies?','Yes, it is not constant','The electronegativity of any given element is not constant; it varies depending on the element to which it is bound. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 91; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronegativity',NULL,'1','mcq','Yes, it is not constant','It can be change for some elements','No, it is constant','It is not applicable for any element','NEW','11-091','11-091',NULL),
(31121,'chemistry','Which of them will have the highest value of electronegativity?','C','According to Pauling scale the electronegativity values are:\nB- 2.0, C-2.5, Na-0.9 , Mg- 1.2  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 90;','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronegativity',NULL,'2','mcq','Mg','Na','B','C','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(22513,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion: When an electric discharge is passed through gaseous hydrogen, the H₂ molecules dissociate.\n \n Reason: The H₂ molecules that dissociate, the energetically excited hydrogen atoms produced emit electromagnetic radiation of discrete frequencies. \n \n The hydrogen spectrum consists of several series of lines named after their discoverers.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','“Line Spectrum of Hydrogen When an electric discharge is passed through gaseous hydrogen, the H₂ molecules dissociate and the energetically excited hydrogen atoms produced emit electromagnetic radiation of discrete frequencies. The hydrogen spectrum consists of several series of lines named after their discoverers(Balmer, Lyman and more).”\n\nReference; NCERT; class 11; page 44; paragraph 6','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-044','11-044',NULL),
(22514,'chemistry','Indicate the incorrect statements.\n \n (i)Any sample of small size contains a small number of atoms.\n \n (ii)A single and the collection of atoms can contain all possible excited states at the time. \n \n (iii)The light emitted as these atoms fall to lower energy states is responsible for the spectrum.\n \n (iv) In atomic absorption, when white light is passed through excited atomic hydrogen and then through a slit and prism, the transmitted light is lacking in intensity at the same wavelengths as are emitted in\n \n (v) In atomic absorption, the recorded absorption spectrum is also a line spectrum and the photographic negative of the emission spectrum.\n \n Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','Only (i), (ii), and (iv) are incorect','Atomic emission-The light emitted by a sample of excited hydrogen atoms (or any other element) can be passed through a prism and separated into certain discrete wavelengths. Thus an emission spectrum, which is a photographic recording of the separated wavelengths is called as line spectrum. Any sample of reasonable size contains an enormous number of atoms. Although a single atom can be in only one excited state at a time, the collection of atoms contains all possible excited states. The light emitted as these atoms fall to lower energy states is responsible for the spectrum.\n\nAtomic absorption-When white light is passed through unexcited atomic hydrogen and then through a slit and prism, the transmitted light is lacking in intensity at the same wavelengths as are emitted in.\n\nThe recorded absorption spectrum is also a line spectrum and the photographic negative of the emission spectrum.\n\nReference; NCERT; class 11; page 45; figure 2.10 description','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only (i) and (iv) are incorect','Only (i) and (ii) are incorect','Only (i), (ii), and (iv) are incorect','All are incorrect','NEW','11-045','11-045',NULL),
(22515,'chemistry','From the following, how many statements are related to Atomic emission.\n \n (i) The light that passes through the prism is emitted by the excited sample. \n \n (ii) The light that passes through the prism is emitted by a white source of light. \n \n (iii) There is an absorbing sample that is placed between the source of light and the prism.\n \n (iv) There is a line spectrum and the photographic negative of the spectrum.\n \n Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','Only 1','Atomic emission-The light emitted by a sample of excited hydrogen atoms (or any other element) can be passed through a prism and separated into certain discrete wavelengths. Thus an emission spectrum, which is a photographic recording of the separated wavelengths is called as line spectrum. Any sample of reasonable size contains an enormous number of atoms. Although a single atom can be in only one excited state at a time, the collection of atoms contains all possible excited states. The light emitted as these atoms fall to lower energy states is responsible for the spectrum.\n\nAtomic absorption-When white light is passed through unexcited atomic hydrogen and then through a slit and prism, the transmitted light is lacking in intensity at the same wavelengths as are emitted in.\n\nThe recorded absorption spectrum is also a line spectrum and the photographic negative of the emission spectrum.\n\nReference; NCERT; class 11; page 45; figure 2.10 and its description','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Only 2','Only 1','Only 3','All 4','NEW','11-045','11-045',NULL),
(22516,'chemistry','Predict that the below statement is true or not and also match the series and their spectral region according to the given diagram:\n \n The Swedish spectroscopist, Johannes Rydberg, noted that all series of lines in the hydrogen spectrum could be described by the following expression:\n \n Ṽ = 109,677(1/n₁²-1/n₂²) cm⁻¹\n \n diagram(n2001)','True, (i) –(b), (ii) – (c ), (iii) –(d), (iv) –(a)','"The series of lines described by this formula are called the Balmer series. The Balmer series of lines are the only lines in the hydrogen spectrum which appear in the visible region of the electromagnetic spectrum. The Swedish spectroscopist, Johannes Rydberg, noted that all series of lines in the hydrogen spectrum could be described by the following expression:\n\nṼ = 109,677(1/n₁²-1/n₂²) cm⁻¹."\n\n"According to table 2.3 The Spectral Lines for Atomic Hydrogen, the spectral regions for Lyman, Balmer, Paschen, Bracket, Pfund series are UV, Visble, Infrared, Infrared, Infrared respectively."\n\nReference; NCERT; class 11; page 45; paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','True, (i) –(d), (ii) – (a ), (iii) –(d), (iv) –(c)','True, (i) –(b), (ii) – (c ), (iii) –(d), (iv) –(a)','False, (i) –(b), (ii) – (c ), (iii) –(d), (iv) –(a)','False, (i) –(a), (ii) – (c ), (iii) –(b), (iv) –(d)','NEW','11-045','11-045',NULL),
(31215,'chemistry','The valence of elements is usually ________ to the number of electrons in the outermost orbitals.','Equal','The valence of representative elements is usually (though not necessarily) equal to the number of electrons in the outermost orbitals and / or equal to eight minus the number of outermost electrons. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 92; Paragraph no. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Oxidation states',NULL,'1','mcq','Equal','Shorter','Greater','Against','NEW','11-092','11-092',NULL),
(31401,'chemistry','All chemical and physical properties are a manifestation of the __ of elements.','Electronic configuration','We know by now that the periodicity is related to electronic configuration. \n\nThat is, all chemical and physical properties are a manifestation of the electronic configuration of elements. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 94; Paragraph no. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Periodic Trends and Chemical reactivity',NULL,'1','mcq','Electronic configuration','Shape','Electronegativity','Electron affinity','NEW','11-094','11-094',NULL),
(213306,'chemistry','Which halogen oxidises other halide ions in solution or even in the solid phase?','F₂','F₂ oxidises other halide ions in solution or even in the solid phase. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 200 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','F₂','Br₂','Cl₂','l₂','NEW','12-200',NULL,NULL),
(22517,'chemistry','Indicate the correct statements,\n \n (i) Bohr theory for hydrogen can still be used to rationalize many points in the atomic structureand spectra\n \n (ii) The energy of an electron in the orbit does not change with time. \n \n (iii) angular momentum is the product of moment of inertia (I) and angular velocity (ω). \n \n (iv) According to Bohr\'s atomic model for hydrogen, radiation is emitted or absorbed only when transition of electron takes place from one quantised value of angular momentum to another.\n \n (v) The energy associated with the ground state is " –2.18×10⁻¹⁸ J"\n \n Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','All are correct','"Neils Bohr (1913) was the first to explain quantitatively the general features of the structure of hydrogen atom and its spectrum.He used Planck’s concept of quantisation of energy. Though the theory is not the modern quantum mechanics, it can still be used to rationalize many points in the atomic structure and spectra."\n\n" The energy of an electron in the orbit does not change with time. However, the electron will move from a lower stationary state to a higher stationary state when required amount of energy is absorbed by the electron or energy is emitted when electron moves from higher stationary state to lower stationary state."\n\n"Just as linear momentum is the product of mass (m) and linear velocity (v), angular momentum is the product of moment of inertia (I) and angular velocity (ω)."\n\n"Thus an electron can move only in those orbits for which its angular momentum is integral multiple of h/2π.  That means angular momentum is quantised. Radiation is emitted or absorbed only when transition of electron takes place from one quantised value of angular momentum to another. Therefore, Maxwell\'s Electromagnetic theory does not apply here, that is why only certain fixed orbits are allowed."\n\n"The energy of the lowest state, also called as the ground state, isE1 = –2.18×10⁻¹⁸ (112) = –2.18×10⁻¹⁸ J."\n\nReference; NCERT; class 11;  Pages 46, Paragraph 2,3 and Page 47, Paragraph 1,2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','All are correct','All are correct but not (i) and (ii)','None is correct','All are correct but except (ii)','NEW','11-046','11-046',NULL),
(22518,'chemistry','Indicate the correct statements.\n \n (i) Bohr’s theory can not be applied to the ions containing only one electron.\n \n (ii) The radii of Hydrogen like species can be expressed by, rₙ =52.9n²/Zpm\n \n (iii) From the equation(rₙ =52.9n²/Zpm), it is evident that the value of energy becomes more negative and that of radius becomes smaller with increase of Z. This means that electrons will be tightly bound to the nucleus.\n \n (iv) In case of absorption spectrum, nf > nᵢ and the term in the parenthesis is positive and energy is absorbed.\n \n (v) On the other hand in case of emission spectrum nᵢ > nf , ∆ E is negative and energy is released.\n \n Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','All are correct except (i)','"Bohr’s theory can also be applied to the ions containing only one electron, similar to that present in hydrogen atom."\n\n" Radii of species like hydrogen can be the expressed by,  rₙ =52.9n²/Zpm.\n\n"From the above equation(rₙ =52.9n²/Zpm), it is evident that the value of energy becomes more negative and that of radius becomes smaller with increase of Z . This means that electron will be tightly bound to the nucleus"\n\n"In case of absorption spectrum, nf > nᵢ and the term in the parenthesis is positive and energy is absorbed. On the other hand in case of emission spectrum nf > nᵢ , ∆E is negative and energy is released."\n\nReference; NCERT; class 11; page 48; paragraphs 1,2,3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','All are correct except (iv)','All are correct except (v)','All are correct except (i)','All are correct except (ii)','NEW','11-048','11-048',NULL),
(22519,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): By using Bohr\'s model, It is also possible to calculate the velocities of electrons moving in these orbits.\n \n Reason(R): Although the precise equation is not given here, qualitatively the magnitude of velocity of electron increases with increase of positive charge on the nucleus and decreases with increase of principal quantum number.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','It is also possible to calculate the velocities of electrons moving in these orbits. Although the precise equation is not given here, qualitatively the magnitude of velocity of electron increases with increase of positive charge on the nucleus and decreases with increase of principal quantum number."\n\nReference; NCERT; class 11; page 48; paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-048','11-048',NULL),
(22520,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): Line spectrum observed in case of hydrogen atom, can be explained quantitatively using Bohr’s mode.\n \n Reason(R):According to one of Bohr\'s assumption, radiation (energy) is absorbed if the electron moves from the orbit of higher Principal quantum number to the orbit of smaller Principal quantum number, whereas the radiation (energy) is emitted if the electron moves from higher orbit to lower orbit.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(A) is true but (R) is false.','Explanation of Line Spectrum of Hydrogen\n\nLine spectrum observed in case of hydrogen atom, and can be explained quantitatively using Bohr’s model. According to his assumptions, radiation (energy) is absorbed if the electron moves from the orbit of smaller Principal quantum number to the orbit of higher Principal quantum number, whereas the radiation (energy) is emitted if the electron moves from higher orbit to lower orbit."\n\nReference: NCERT; Class 11; Page 48; Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-048','11-048',NULL),
(22521,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): In case of large number of hydrogen atoms, different possible transitions can be observed and thus leading to large number of spectral lines.\n \n Reason(R): The brightness or intensity of spectral lines depends upon the number of photons of different wavelength or frequency absorbed or emitted.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','“The expression is similar to that used by Rydberg derived empirically using the experimental data available at that time. \n\nFurther, each spectral line, whether in absorption or emission spectrum, can be associated to the particular transition in hydrogen atom. \n\nIn case of large number of hydrogen atoms, different possible transitions can be observed and thus leading to large number of spectral lines. \n\nThe brightness or intensity of spectral lines depends upon the number of photons of same wavelength or frequency absorbed or emitted.”\n\nReference; NCERT, class 11, Page 48, Paragraphs 3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-048','11-048',NULL),
(22522,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): About the dual nature of matter, de Broglie said that the photon has momentum as well as wavelength, electrons should also have momentum as well as wavelength.\n \n Reason(R): de Broglie gave the relation between wavelength (λ) and momentum (p) of a material particle.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Dual Behaviour of Matter-\n\nThe French physicist, de Broglie, in 1924 proposed that matter, like radiation, should also exhibit dual behavior i.e., both particle and wavelike properties. This means that just as the photon has momentum as well as wavelength, electrons should also have momentum as well as wavelength, de Broglie, from this analogy, gave the following relation between wavelength (λ) and momentum (p) of a material particle.”\n\nReference; NCERT, class 11, Page 49, Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-049','11-049',NULL),
(22523,'chemistry','Indicate the incorrect statements. \n \n (i) According to de Broglie, every object in motion has a dual character.\n \n (ii) According to de Broglie, every object in motion has a wave character.\n \n (iii) The wavelengths associated with ordinary objects are so short (because of their large masses) that their wave properties cannot be detected. \n \n (iv) The wavelengths associated with electrons and other subatomic particles (with very small mass) can be detected experimentally.\n \n (v) Werner Heisenberg a French physicist in1927, stated uncertainty principle which is the consequence of dual behavior of matter and radiation.\n \n (vi) Werner Heisenberg a German physicist in1927, stated uncertainty principle which is the consequence of dual behavior of matter and radiation.\n \n Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','Only (i) and (v)','“It needs to be noted that according to deBroglie, every object in motion has a wave character. The wavelengths associated with ordinary objects are so short (because of their large masses) that their wave properties cannot be detected. The wavelengths associated with electrons and other subatomic particles (with very small mass) can however be detected experimentally. Results obtained from the following problems prove these points qualitatively.”\n\nHeisenberg’s Uncertainty Principle -\nWerner Heisenberg a German physicist in1927, stated uncertainty principle which is the consequence of dual behavior of matter and radiation. It states that it is impossible to determine simultaneously, the exact position and exact momentum (or velocity)of an electron.”\n\nReference; NCERT, class 11, Page 50, Paragraph 1,2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only (i) and (ii)','Only (i) and (v)','Only (iii) and (vi)','Only (iv)','NEW','11-050','11-050',NULL),
(22524,'chemistry','Indicate the incorrect statements,\n \n (i) As we need a metre stick to determine the thickness of a sheet, same as we must use a meter stick calibrated in units of smaller than the dimensions of an electron.\n \n (ii) An electron is considered as a point charge and is therefore, dimensionless.\n \n (iii) An electron is considered as a point charge and is therefore having dimensions.\n \n (iv) To observe an electron, we can illuminate it with “light” or electromagnetic radiation.\n \n (v) The “light” used to observe an electron, must have a wavelength smaller than the dimensions of an electron.\n \n (vi) The “light” used to observe an electron, must have a wavelength greater than the dimensions of an electron.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (iii) and (vi)','"The uncertainty principle can be best understood with the help of an example. \nSuppose you are asked to measure the thickness of a sheet of paper with an unmarked metre stick. \n\nObviously, the results obtained would be extremely inaccurate and meaningless, In order to obtain any accuracy, you should use an instrument graduated in units smaller than the thickness of a sheet of the paper. \n\nAnalogously, in order to determine the position of an electron, we must use a meter stick calibrated in units of smaller than the dimensions of electron (keep in mind that an electron is considered as a point charge and is therefore, dimensionless). \n\nTo observe an electron, we can illuminate it with “light” or electromagnetic radiation. The “light” used must have a wavelength smaller than the dimensions of an electron."\n\nReference; NCERT, class 11, Page 51, Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only (iii) and (vi)','Only (ii) and (vi)','Only (i) and (iv)','All are incorrect','NEW','11-051','11-051',NULL),
(22525,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): the case of a microscopic object like an electron, ∆v.∆x obtained is much larger and such uncertainties are of real consequence.\n \n Reason(R): an electron whose mass is 9.11×10⁻³¹ kg. \n \n According to Heisenberg uncertainty principle if one tries to find the exact location of the electron, say to an uncertainty of only 10⁻⁸ m, and the uncertainty ∆v in velocity would be 10m⁴s‐¹.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','“In the case of a microscopic object like an electron, ∆v.∆x obtained is much larger and such uncertainties are of real consequence. \n\nFor example, for an electron whose mass is 9.11×10⁻³¹ kg., \n\naccording to Heisenberg uncertainty principle, \n\nΔv. Δx =h/4πm = 10⁻⁴m⁻² s-1\n\nIt, therefore, means that if one tries to find the exact location of the electron, say to an uncertainty of only 10–⁸ m, then the uncertainty ∆v in velocity would be,\n\n10⁻⁴m⁻² s-1/10⁻⁸m  ≈10m⁴s‐¹\n\nReference; NCERT, class 11, Page 52, Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-051','11-051',NULL),
(31402,'chemistry','The chemical reactivity of an element can be best shown by its reactions with ____ and ____ .','Oxygen and Halogens','The chemical reactivity of an element can be best shown by its reactions with oxygen and halogens. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 94; Paragraph no. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Periodic Trends and Chemical reactivity',NULL,'1','mcq','Fluorine and Chlorine','Oxygen and Nitrogen','Nitrogen and Fluorine','Oxygen and Halogens','NEW','11-094','11-094',NULL),
(213307,'chemistry','______ oxidises water to oxygen whereas chlorine and bromine react with water to form corresponding hydrohalic and ________ acids.','Fluorine, hypohalous','Fluorine oxidises water to oxygen whereas chlorine and bromine react with water to form corresponding hydrohalic and hypohalous acids. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 200 Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Fluorine, halous','Fluorine, hypohalous','Iodine, hypohalous','Iodine, halous','NEW','12-200',NULL,NULL),
(22526,'chemistry','Indicate the correct statements.\n \n (i) The values of position and velocity calculated by Heisenberg uncertainty principle is so small that the classical picture of electrons moving in Bohr’s orbits cannot hold good.\n \n (ii) The values of position and velocity calculated by Heisenberg uncertainty principle is so large that the classical picture of electrons moving in Bohr’s orbits cannot hold good.\n \n (iii) The precise statements of the position and momentum of electrons have to be replaced by the statements of probability.\n \n (iv) An insight into the structure of the atom that was needed, which could account for wave-particle duality of matter and be consistent with Heisenberg uncertainty principle. This came with the advent of quantum mechanics.\n \n (v) Falling stones and orbiting planets are the examples of macroscopic objects.\n \n (vi) Classical mechanics, based on Newton’s laws of motion, successfully describes the motion of all microscopic objects.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (ii), (iii), (iv) and (v)','“Which is so large that the classical picture of electrons moving in Bohr’s orbits (fixed) cannot hold good. It, therefore, means that the precise statements of the position and momentum of electrons have to be replaced by the statements of probability, that the electron has at a given position and momentum. \n\nThis is what happens in the quantum mechanical model of atom.”\n\n“In fact an insight into the structure of the atom was needed which could account for wave-particle duality of matter and be consistent with Heisenberg uncertainty principle. This came with the advent of quantum mechanics.”\n\nQUANTUM MECHANICAL MODEL OF ATOM-\n\nClassical mechanics, based on Newton’s laws of motion, successfully describes the motion of all macroscopic objects such as a falling stone, orbiting planets etc., which have essentially a particle-like behaviour as shown in the previous section.”\n\nReference; NCERT, class 11, Page 52, Paragraph 3 and Page 53, Paragraph 1,2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only (ii), (iii), (iv) and (vi)','Only (ii), (iii), (iv) and (v)','Only (i), (iii), (iv) and (v)','Only (ii), (iii), (v) and (v)','NEW','11-052','11-052',NULL),
(22527,'chemistry','From the following, how many statements are incorrect.\n \n (i) In the Schrödinger equation is written as Ĥφ = EĤ where Ĥ is a mathematical operator called Hamiltonian.\n \n (ii) Schrödinger gave a recipe of constructing this operator from the expression for the total energy of the system.\n \n (iii) Schrödinger gave a recipe of constructing this operator from the expression for the negative energy of the system.\n \n (iv) The principal difference between the Hydrogen orbitals and orbitals of other than Hydrogen lies in the consequence of increased nuclear charge.\n \n (v) Because of this(nuclear charge) all the orbitals are somewhat contracted.\n \n (vi) Unlike orbitals of hydrogen or hydrogen like species, whose energies depend only on the quantum number ℓ, the energies of the orbitals in multi-electron atoms depend on quantum numbers n and ℓ.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only 2','“For a system (such as an atom or a molecule whose energy does not change with time) the Schrödinger equation is written as Ĥφ = EĤ where Ĥ is a mathematical operator called Hamiltonian. Schrödinger gave a recipe of constructing this operator from the expression for the total energy of the system.”\n\n“Application of  Schrödinger equation to multi-electron atoms presents a difficulty: the Schrödinger equation cannot be solved exactly for a multi-electron atom. \n\nThis difficulty can be overcome by using approximate methods. Such calculations with the aid of modern computers show that orbitals in atoms other than hydrogen do not differ in any radical way from the hydrogen orbitals discussed above. The principal difference lies in the consequence of increased nuclear charge. \n\nBecause of this all the orbitals are somewhat contracted. Further, as you shall see later (in subsections2.6.3 and 2.6.4), unlike orbitals of hydrogen or hydrogen like species, whose energies depend only on the quantum number n, the energies of the orbitals in multi-electron atoms depend on quantum numbers n and l.”\n\nReference; NCERT, class 11, Page 53, Paragraph 4 and Page 54, Paragraph 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 4','Only 1','Only 2','All are incorrect','NEW','11-053','11-053',NULL),
(22528,'chemistry','Indicate the incorrect statements. \n \n (i) Quantum mechanical model of atom is the picture of the structure of the atom, which emerges from the application of the Bohr’s Atomic model to atoms. \n \n (ii) Quantum mechanical model of atom is the picture of the structure of the atom, which emerges from the application of the Schrödinger equation to atoms.\n \n (iii) Whenever an electron is described by a wave function, we say that the electron occupies that orbital.\n \n (iv) It is only became possible by quantum mechanics that we can extract the information out of φ.\n \n (v) It is only became possible by quantum numbers that we can extract the information out of φ.\n \n (vi) There is only a small number of orbitals are possible in an atom.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (i), (v) and (vi)','Important Features of the Quantum Mechanical Model of Atom-\n\n“Quantum mechanical model of atom is the picture of the structure of the atom, which emerges from the application of the Schrödinger equation to atoms.”\n\nAn atomic orbital is the wave function φ for an electron in an atom. \n\nWhenever an electron is described by a wave function, we say that the electron occupies that orbital. Since many such wave functions are possible for an electron, there are many atomic orbitals in an atom. \n\nThese “one electron orbital wave functions” or orbitals form the basis of the electronic structure of atoms. In each orbital, the electron has a definite energy. \n\nAn orbital cannot contain more than two electrons. In a multi-electron atom, the electrons are filled in various orbitals in the order of increasing energy. \n\nFor each electron of a multi-electron atom, there shall, therefore, be an orbital wave function characteristic of the orbital it occupies. All the information about the electron in an atom is stored in its orbital wave function φ and quantum mechanics makes it possible to extract this information out of φ.”\n\nOrbitals and Quantum Numbers-\nA large number of orbitals are possible in an atom”\n\nReference; NCERT, class 11, Page 54, Paragraph 3,6,8','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only (i) and (vi)','Only (i) and (v)','Only (i), (iv) and (v)','Only (i), (v) and (vi)','NEW','11-054','11-054',NULL),
(22529,'chemistry','Indicate the correct statements. \n \n (i) Each orbital in an atom, therefore, is defined by a set of values for n and ℓ.\n \n (ii) Each orbital in an atom, therefore, is defined by a set of values for n, ℓ and mℓ\n \n (iii) We need spin quantum number because the other three quantum numbers are not sufficient to predict the all energy levels present in an atom. \n \n (iv) |φ|² varies from one region to another in space but its value can be assumed to be constant within a small volume element. \n \n (v) |φ|² varies from one region to another in space and also its value can not be assumed to be constant within a small volume element.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (ii), (iii) and (iv)','“Each orbital in an atom, therefore, is defined by a set of values for n, ℓ, and mℓ.”\n\n“Electron spin ‘s’ : The three quantum numbers labelling an atomic orbital can be used equally well to define its energy, shape and orientation. \n\nBut all these quantum numbers are not enough to explain the line spectra observed in the case of multi-electron atoms, that is, some of the lines actually occur in doublets (two lines closely spaced), triplets(three lines, closely spaced) etc. \n\nThis suggests the presence of a few more energy levels than predicted by the three quantum numbers.”\n\n“Orbit, orbital and its importance-\n\nProbability density|φ|2  is the probability per unit volume and the product of |φ|2  and a small volume (called a volume element) yields the probability of finding the electron in that volume (the reason for specifying a small volume element is that |φ|2  varies from one region to another in space but its value can be assumed to be constant within a small volume element).”\n\nReference; NCERT, class 11, Page 56, Paragraph1,2 + description Orbit, orbital and its importance','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only (ii), (iii) and (iv)','Only (i) and (iii)','Only (ii) and (iv)','Only (ii), (iii) and (v)','NEW','11-056','11-056',NULL),
(31403,'chemistry','Match the following:','Na₂O-basic, As₂O₃-amphoteric, Cl₂O₇-acidic, NO-Neutral','The normal oxide formed by the element on extreme left is the most basic (ne.g., Na₂O), whereas that formed by the element on extreme right is the most acidic (ne.g., Cl₂O₇). \n\nOxides of elements in the centre are amphoteric (ne.g., Al₂O₃, As₂O₃) or neutral (ne.g., CO, NO, N₂O). \n\nAmphoteric oxides behave as acidic with bases and as basic with acids, whereas neutral oxides have no acidic or basic properties.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 94; Paragraph no. 2 )','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Periodic Trends and Chemical reactivity',NULL,'3','match','Na₂O,basic','As₂O₃,amphoteric','Cl₂O₇,acidic','NO,Neutral','NEW','11-094','11-094','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/31403q.png'),
(213308,'chemistry','Ionisation enthalpy, electronegativity, and electrode potentials are all _______ for ________  than expected from the trends set by other halogens.','higher, fluorine','Ionisation enthalpy, electronegativity, and electrode potentials are all higher for fluorine  than expected from the trends set by other halogens. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 200 Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','lower, fluorine','higher, fluorine','lower, iodine','higher, iodine','NEW','12-200',NULL,NULL),
(22530,'chemistry','Indicate the correct statements. \n \n (i) Each orbital in an atom, therefore, is defined by a set of values for n and ℓ.\n \n (ii) Each orbital in an atom, therefore, is defined by a set of values for n, ℓ and mℓ\n \n (iii) We need spin quantum number because the other three quantum numbers are not sufficient to predict the all energy levels present in an atom. \n \n (iv) |φ|² varies from one region to another in space but its value can be assumed to be constant within a small volume element. \n \n (v) |φ|² varies from one region to another in space and also its value can not be assumed to be constant within a small volume element.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only (ii), (iii) and (iv)','“These probability density variation can be visualized in terms of charge cloud diagrams [Check Fig. 2.13(a)- on your text book]\n\nIn these Diagrams, the density of the dots in a region represents electron probability density in that region.”\n\n“Boundary surface Diagram for a s orbital is actually a sphere centered on the nucleus. \nIn two dimensions, this sphere looks like a circle. It encloses a region in which probability of finding the electron is about 90%.”\n\n“One angular node for p orbitals, two angular nodes for ‘d’ orbitals and so on.”\n\nReference; NCERT, class 11, Page 57, Paragraph 5, Page 58, Paragraph 2 and Page 59, Paragraph 4','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only (i), (iv) and (v)','Only (iv) and (v)','Only (i) and (v)','Only (ii), (iii) and (iv)','NEW','11-057','11-057',NULL),
(22531,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is labelled as Reason(R).\n \n Assertion(A): The extent of shielding from the nucleus is different for electrons in different orbitals.\n \n Reason(R): Shielding leads to the splitting of energy levels within the same shell (or same principal quantum number), that is, energy of electron in an orbital, that depends upon the values of n and ℓ.\n \n In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','“Since the extent of shielding from the nucleus is different for electrons in different orbitals, it leads to the splitting of energy levels within the same shell (or same principal quantum number), that is, energy of electron in an orbital, as mentioned earlier, depends upon the values of n and l. \n\nMathematically, the dependence of energies of the orbitals on n and l are quite complicated but one simple rule is that, the lower the value of (n + l) for an orbital, the lower is its energy. If two orbitals have the same value of (n + l), the orbital with lower value of n will have the lower energy.”\n\nReference; NCERT, class 11, Page 61, Paragraph 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is true but (R) is false.','(A) is false but (R) is true.','NEW','11-061','11-061',NULL),
(22532,'chemistry','Indicate the incorrect statements.\n \n (i) There is no single ordering of energies of orbitals which will be universally correct for all atoms.\n \n (ii) The energy of a given orbital depends upon effective nuclear charge and different type of orbitals are affected to different extent.\n \n (iii) In the filling of electrons, the orbitals are similar in energy and small changes in atomic structure may bring about a change in the order of filling.\n \n (iv) The electronic series is a useful guide to the building of the electronic structure of an atom.\n \n (v) The restriction imposed by Pauli’s exclusion principle on the number of electrons in an orbital helps in calculating the capacity of electrons to be present in any subshell.\n \n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','None is incorrect','“As you have learnt above, energy of a given orbital depends upon effective nuclear charge and different type of orbitals are affected to different extent. Thus, there is no single ordering of energies of orbitals which will be universally correct for all atoms.”\n\n“With respect to placement of outermost valence electrons, it is remarkably accurate for all atoms. \n\nFor example, valence electron in potassium must choose between 3d and 4s orbitals and as predicted by this sequence, it is found in 4sorbital. The above order should be assumed to be a rough guide to the filling of energy levels. \n\nIn many cases, the orbitals are similar in energy and small changes in atomic structure may bring about a change in the order of filling. \n\nEven then, the above series is a useful guide to the building of the electronic structure of an atom provided that it is remembered that exceptions may occur.”\n\n“The restriction imposed by Pauli’s exclusion principle on the number of electrons in an orbital helps in calculating the capacity of electrons to be present in any subshell.”\n\nReference; NCERT, class 11, Page 62, Paragraph 1,2,3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only (i)','Only (ii) and (v)','Only (i), (iv) and (v)','None is incorrect','NEW','11-062','11-062',NULL),
(22533,'chemistry','Indicate the correct statements.\n \n (i) In the elements scandium (Sc), titanium (Ti), vanadium (V) the five 3d orbitals are progressively occupied.\n \n (ii) The maximum number of electrons in the shell with principal quantum number n is equal to 2n².\n \n (iii) The 6s orbital opens in turn of Rubidium(Rb) and Cesium(Cs).\n \n (iv) From lanthanum(La) to mercury(Hg), the filling up of electrons takes place in 5f and 6d orbitals.\n \n (v) The electronic configurations of the known elements tabled in textbook is determined by spectroscopic methods.\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','All are correct except (iii) and (iv)','“A new pattern is followed beginning with scandium (Sc). The 3d orbital, being lower in energy than the 4p orbital, is filled first. \n\nConsequently, in the next ten elements, scandium (Sc), titanium (Ti), vanadium (V),chromium (Cr), manganese (Mn), iron (Fe),cobalt (Co), nickel (Ni), copper (Cu) and zinc(Zn), the five 3d orbitals are progressively occupied.”\n\n“Then comes the turn of the 6s orbital. \n\nIn cesium (Cs) and the barium (Ba), this orbital contains one and two electrons, respectively. \n\nThen from lanthanum(La) to mercury (Hg), the filling up of electrons takes place in 4f and 5d orbitals.”\n\n“The electronic configurations of the known elements (as determined by spectroscopic methods) are tabulated in Table 2.6 (page 66).”\n\nReference; NCERT, class 11, Page 64, Paragraph 2,3','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','All are correct except (iii)','All are correct except (v)','All are correct except (iii) and (iv)','All are correct','NEW','11-064','11-064',NULL),
(22534,'chemistry','From the following statements. The number of correct statement(s) is/are,\n \n (i) The Daltonian model of atom gave a detailed explanation of why the halogens are reactive. \n \n (ii) The number of exchanges that can take place is minimum when the subshell is either half filled or completely filled.\n \n (iii) When the exchange energy is minimum then it is reliable to the stability.\n \n (iv) For the following elements, the correct order of decreasing atomic number is, Nb<Rh< Mo\n \n (v) For the following elements, the correct order of decreasing atomic number is, Rh> Mo> Nb\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','1','For example, questions like why two or more atoms combine to form molecules, why some elements are metals while others are non-metals, why elements like helium and argon are not reactive but elements like the halogens are reactive, find simple explanation from the electronic configuration. \n\nThese questions have no answer in the Daltonian model of atom.”\n\n“The number of exchanges that can take place is maximum when the subshell is either half filled or completely filled (Fig.2.18). As a result the exchange energy is maximum and so is the stability.”\n\n“According to table 2.6, the correct order of decreasing atomic number is Rh(45)>Mo(42)>(Nb)”\n\nReference; NCERT, class 11, Page 64, Paragraph 4, Page 65, Paragraph 2 and class 11, Page 66','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-064','11-064',NULL),
(213309,'chemistry','_____ of the reactions of fluorine are ________.','Most, exothermic','Most of the reactions of fluorine are exothermic. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 200 Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Most, endothermic','Most, exothermic','Some, endothermic','Some, endothermic','NEW','12-200',NULL,NULL),
(22535,'chemistry','From the following, how many of the elements having exceptions to electronic configurations.\n \n (i) Gallium\n (ii) Molybdenum \n (iii) Rhodium\n (iv) Palladium\n (v) Silver\n \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','3','“According to the table 2.6, \n\nMolybdenum, Palladium, and Silver having exceptions to electronic configurations.\n \n[Ar]3d¹⁰4s²4p¹(Ga) , [Kr]4d⁵5s² (Mo), [Kr]4d⁸5s¹(Rh), [Kr]4d¹⁰(Pd), and [Kr]4d¹⁰5s¹ (Ag) “\n\nReference; NCERT, class 11, Page 66, Table 2.6','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','2','3','4','5','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22601,'chemistry','Which of the following conclusions could not be derived from Rutherford’s α-particle scattering experiment?\n \n (a) Most of the space in the atom is filled.\n \n (b) The radius of the atom is about 10 ⁻¹⁰ while that of nucleus is 10 ⁻¹⁵\n \n (c) Electrons move in a circular path of fixed energy called orbits.\n \n (d) Electrons and the nucleus are held together by electrostatic forces of attraction','a,c','Most of the space in the atom is empty.\n The concept of circular paths of fixed energy was proposed by Bohr and not derived from Rutherford’s scattering experiment.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','b,d','a,d','c,d','a,c','NEW','11-034','11-034',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(22602,'chemistry','Total number of orbitals associated with third shell will be _______.','9','No of orbitals in 3rd shell (n = 3) = n² = 3² = 9.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','3','6','9','12','NEW','11-055','11-055',NULL),
(22603,'chemistry','Which of the following statements concerning the quantum numbers are correct?\n \n (a) Angular quantum number determines the three dimensional shape of the orbital.\n \n (b) The principal quantum number determines the orientation and energy of the orbital.\n \n (c) Magnetic quantum number determines the size of the orbital.\n \n (d) Spin quantum number of an electron determines the orientation of the spin of electron relative to the chosen axis.','a,d','(a) Azimuthal quantum number l is also known as orbital angular momentum or subsidiary quantum number. It determines three-dimensional shape of the orbital.\n \n (b) The principal quantum number determines the size of the orbit.\n \n (c) Magnetic quantum number determines the orientation of the electron cloud in a subshell.\n \n (d) An electron spins around its own’ axis, much in a similar way as earth spins around its own axis while revolving around the sun. In other words, an electron has, besides charge and mass, intrinsic spin angular quantum number.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','a,d','c,d','b,d','NEW','11-055','11-055',NULL),
(22604,'chemistry','Out of the following pairs of electrons, identify the pairs of electrons present in degenerate orbitals:','(i) n = 3, l = 2, ml = -2, mₛ = -1/2\n(ii) n = 3, l= 2, ml = -1, mₛ = -1/2','Degenerate orbitals mean the orbitals of the same sub-shell of the same main shell, i.e., their n and l values are the same.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','(i) n = 3, l = 2, ml = -2, mₛ = -1/2\n(ii) n = 3, l= 2, ml = -1, mₛ = -1/2','(i) n = 3, l = 1,  ml = 1,    mₛ = +1/2\n(ii)     n =  3, l = 2,  ml = 1,   mₛ = +1/2','(i) n = 4, l = 1,  ml = 1,  mₛ = +1/2\n(ii) n =  3, l = 2,  ml = 1,   mₛ = +1/2','(i) n = 3, l = 3, ml = +2, mₛ = -1/2\n(ii)n = 3, l = 1, ml = +2, mₛ = +1/2','NEW','11-063','11-063',NULL),
(22605,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is not correct about the characteristics of cathode rays?','Characteristics of cathode rays depend upon the nature of gas present in the cathode ray tube.','Cathode rays consist of negatively charged material particles called electron. They were discovered by William Crookes. The characteristics of cathode rays do not depend upon the material of electrodes and the nature of the gas present in the cathode ray tube.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','They start from the cathode and move towards the anode.','They travel in straight line in the absence of an external electrical or magnetic field.','Characteristics of cathode rays do not depend upon the material of electrodes in cathode ray tube.','Characteristics of cathode rays depend upon the nature of gas present in the cathode ray tube.','NEW','11-030','11-030',NULL),
(22606,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about the electron is incorrect!','The mass of electron is greater than the mass of neutron.','The mass of electron is very small as compared to the mass of the neutron.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','It is a negatively charged particle.','The mass of electron is greater than the mass of neutron.','It is a basic constituent of all atoms.','It is a constituent of cathode rays.','NEW','11-033','11-033',NULL),
(22607,'chemistry','Which of the following properties of atom could be explained correctly by Thomson Model of atom?','Overall neutrality of atom.','According to Thomson model of atom, the mass of the atom is assumed to be uniformly distributed over the atom. This model was able to explain the overall neutrality of the atom.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Overall neutrality of atom.','Spectra of hydrogen atom.','Position of electrons, protons and neutrons in atom.','Stability of atom.','NEW','11-033','11-033',NULL),
(22608,'chemistry','The number of radial nodes for 3p orbital is .','1','Number of radial nodes = n-1 – 1\n For 3p orbital, n = 3 – l – 1 = 1\n Number of radial nodes = 3 – 1 – 1 = 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-059','11-059',NULL),
(22609,'chemistry','Number of angular nodes for 5d orbital is __________ .','2','Number of angular nodes in 5d orbital = l = 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-059','11-059',NULL),
(22610,'chemistry','Orbital angular momentum depends on','l','Orbital Angular momentum=√l(l + 1 ) h/4π , Hence it depends only on l','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','l','n and l','n and m','n and s','NEW','11-055','11-055',NULL),
(22611,'chemistry','Two atoms are said to be isobars if','Sum of the number of protons and neutrons is same but the number of protons is\ndifferent','Isobars have the same mass number (i.e., sum of protons and neutrons) but different atomic number (i.e., number of protons). e.g., ₁₈ Ar⁴⁰ and ₁₉ K⁴⁰ are isobars.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','They have same atomic number but different mass number','They have same number of electrons but different number of neutrons','They have same number of neutrons but different number of electrons','Sum of the number of protons and neutrons is same but the number of protons is\ndifferent','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(22612,'chemistry','Which of the following is responsible to rule out the existence of\n definite paths or trajectories of electrons?','Heisenberg’s uncertainty principle','Werner Heisenberg, a German physicist in 1927, stated uncertainty principle which states that it is impossible to determine simultaneously, the exact position and exact momentum of an electron. \n \n Mathematically, △X × △p ≥ h/4π\n \n The important implications of the Heisenberg uncertainty principle is that it rules out existence of definite paths or trajectories of electrons and other similar particles.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Pauli’s exclusion principle','Heisenberg’s uncertainty principle','Hund’s rule of maximum multiplicity','Aufbau principle','NEW','11-051','11-051',NULL),
(22613,'chemistry','Chlorine exists in two isotopic forms. Cl-37 and Cl-35 but its atomic\n mass is 35.5. This indicates the ratio of Cl-37 and Cl-35 is approximately','1:3','The fractional atomic mass ( 35.5) of chlorine is due to the fact that in ordinary chlorine atom, Cl-37 and Cl-35 are present in the ratio of 1 : 3. \n →Average atomic mass of Cl =(3×35) + (1×37)/ 4 = 35.5 amu','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','1:2','2:1','1:3','3:1','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(22614,'chemistry','If travelling at same speeds, which of the following matter waves have the shortest wavelength?','Alpha particle','From de-Broglie equation, \n wavelength, λ = h /mv \n For same speed of different particles, i.e., electron, proton, neutron and alpha-particle, As h is constant. Greater the mass of matter waves, lesser is wavelength and vice-versa. In these matter waves, alpha particle has higher mass, therefore, shortest wavelength.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Electron','Alpha particle','Neutron','Proton','NEW','11-034','11-034',NULL),
(22615,'chemistry','Which of the following sets of quantum numbers are correct?\n a) n = 1 , l = 1, m = 2\n b) n = 2 , l = 1, m = 1\n c) n = 3 , l = 2, m = -2\n d) n = 3 , l = 4 , m = -2','b,c','If n = 1, l ≠ 1 , (l < n). \n Hence, (a) is incorrect. \n \n If n = 2 , l = 0, 1, For l = 1, m = - 1, 0, + 1. \n Hence, (b) is correct.\n \n If n = 3, l = 0, 1, 2 \n For l = 2 , m = - 2 , - 1, 0, + 1, + 2. \n Hence, (c) is correct.\n \n If n = 3, l≠ 4 ( l < n). \n Hence, (d) is incorrect.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','NEW','11-055','11-055',NULL),
(22616,'chemistry','Which of the following options does not represent ground state electronic configuration of an atom?','1s²2s²2p⁶3s²3p⁶3d⁹4s¹','Correct configuration should be 1s²2s²2p⁶3s²3p⁶3d⁹4s¹ for the copper which has atomic number 29 ( ₂₉Cu). Due to extra stability of full filled orbital of d-subshell, the last electron enter into d-orbital insted of s-orbital.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','1s²2s²2p⁶3s²3p⁶3d⁸4s²','1s²2s²2p⁶3s²3p⁶3d⁹4s¹','1s²2s²2p⁶3s²3p⁶3d¹⁰4s¹','1s²2s²2p⁶3s²3p⁶3d⁵4s¹','NEW','11-063','11-063',NULL),
(22617,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about the electron is incorrect?','The mass of electron is equal to the mass of neutron','The mass of electron is very small as compared to the mass of the neutron.\n Mass of electron = 9.1 × 10⁻³¹kg \n Mass of neutron = 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷kg','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','It is a negatively charged particle','The mass of electron is equal to the mass of neutron','It is a basic constituent of all atoms','It is a constituent of cathode rays','NEW','11-033','11-033',NULL),
(22618,'chemistry','Which of the following properties of atom could be explained correctly by Thomson model of atom?','Overall neutrality of atom','J J Thomson, in 1898, proposed plum pudding, (raisin pudding or watermelon) model of atom. An important feature of this model is that the mass of the atom is assumed to be uniformly distributed over the atom. This model was able to explain the overall neutrality of the atom.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Overall neutrality of atom','Spectra of hydrogen atom','Position of electrons, protons and neutrons in atom','Stability of atom','NEW','11-033','11-033',NULL),
(22619,'chemistry','In which of the following pairs, the ions are isoelectronic?\n (a) Na⁺ , Mg²⁺ \n (b) Al³ ⁺, O ⁻ \n (c) Na⁺, O ²⁻ \n (d) N ³⁻, Cl','a,c','Na⁺ = 11-1 = 10e⁻\n Mg²⁺ = 12-2 = 10e⁻ \n Thus, they have same number of electrons. \n For, Al³⁺ = 13-3 = 10e⁻ , O⁻ = 8+1 = 9e ⁻\n \n They do not have same number of electrons.\n For , Na = 10e⁻ , O²⁻ = 8+2 = 10e⁻\n \n They have same number of electrons. \n For, N³ = 7+3 = 10e⁻ ,Cl⁻ = 17+1 = 18e⁻\n They do not have same number of electrons. \n \n Thus, Na⁺ is isoelectronic with Mg²⁺ \n and O²⁻.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','a,c','b,d','c,d','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22620,'chemistry','For the electrons of oxygen atom, which of the following statements is \n correct?\n \n (a) Zeff for an electron in a 2s orbital is the same as Zeff for an electron in a 2p orbital \n \n (b) An electron in the 2s orbital has the same energy as an electron in the 2p orbital \n \n (c) Zeff for an electron in1s orbital is the same as Zeff for an electron in a 2s orbital \n \n (d) The two electrons present in the 2s orbital have spin quantum numbers ms but of opposite sign','only d','(a) Electrons in 2s and 2 porbitals have different screen effect. Hence, their Zeff is different. \n Zeff of 2 s Z orbital eff > of 2 porbital \n Therefore, it is not correct. \n \n (b) Energy of 2s orbital < energy of 2 porbital. \n Hence, it is not correct. \n \n (c) Zeff of 1sorbital ≠ Z eff of 2s orbital \n Hence, it is incorrect.\n \n (d) For the two electrons of 2s orbital, the value of ms \n is + 1/2 and - 1 /2 . \n Hence, it is correct.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a & b','only d','a,b& c','only d','NEW','11-060','11-060',NULL),
(22621,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is incorrect for the probability density plots of 1s and 2s orbitals :','The probability density of electrons for 2s orbital decreases uniformly as\ndistance from the nucleus increases','The probability density of electrons in 2s orbital first increases then decreases and after that it begins to increases again as distance increases from nucleus.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','1s and 2s orbitals are spherical in shape','The probability of finding the electron is maximum near the nucleus','The probability of finding the electron at a given distance is equal in all\n directions','The probability density of electrons for 2s orbital decreases uniformly as\ndistance from the nucleus increases','NEW','11-054','11-054',NULL),
(22622,'chemistry','The pair of ions having same electronic configuration is ............ .','Fe³⁺,Mn²⁺','₂₄Cr= [Ar]3d⁵ , 4s¹ ₂₄Cr³⁺ = [Ar]3d³\n ₂₆Fe = [Ar]3d⁶, 4s² ₂₆Fe³⁺ = [Ar]3d⁵ \n ₂₅Mn = [Ar] 3d⁵, 4s² ₂₅Mn²⁺= [Ar] 3d⁵\n ₂₇Co = [Ar] 3d⁷ , 4s² ₂₇Co³⁺ = [Ar] 3d⁶\n ₂₁Sc = [Ar] 3d¹ , 4s² ₂₁Sc³⁺= [Ar]\n \n Thus, Fe³⁺ and Mn²⁺ have the same electronic configuration.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Cr³⁺,Fe³⁺','Fe³⁺,Mn²⁺','Fe³⁺,Co³⁺','Sc³⁺,Cr³⁺','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22623,'chemistry','Identify the pairs which are not of isotopes?\n (a) ¹²₆X , ¹³₆Y\n (b) ³⁵₁₇X , ³⁷₁₇Y\n (c) ¹⁴₆X , ¹⁴₇Y\n (d) ⁸₄X , ⁸₅Y','c,d','¹²₆X , ¹³₆Y have same atomic number but different mass number.\n ³⁵₁₇X , ³⁷₁₇Y have same atomic number but different mass number. Both these pairs are \n isotopes to each other.\n ¹⁴₆X , ¹⁴₇Y have different atomic number but same mass number.\n ⁸₄X , ⁸₅Y have different atomic number but same mass number. \n \n Both these pairs are isobars to each other.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(22624,'chemistry','Out of the following pairs of electrons, identify the pairs of electrons present in degenerate orbitals.\n \n (a) (i) n = 3, l = 2 , mₗ= - 2 , mₛ = - 1 /2 (ii) n = 3, l = 2 , mₗ= -1, mₛ = - 1 /2 \n \n (b) (i) n = 3,l =1, mₗ=1, mₛ = + 1 /2 (ii) n = 3, l = 2 , mₗ=1, mₛ = + 1 /2 \n \n (c) (i) n = 4, l =1, mₗ=1, mₛ = + 1 /2 (ii) n = 3, l = 2 , mₗ=1, mₛ= + 1 /2 \n \n (d) (i) n = 3, l = 2 , mₗ= + 2 , mₛ = -1 /2 (ii) n = 3, l = 2 , mₗ= + 2 , mₛ = + 1/2','a,d','Degenerate orbitals means the orbitals of the same subshell of the same main shell, i.e., their n and l value.\n \n (a) (i) 3dₓᵧ (ii) 3dyz \n (b) (i) 3pₓ (ii) 3dₓᵧ \n (c) (i) 4s (ii) 3dₓᵧ \n (d) (i) 3dₓ²₋ᵧ² (ii) 3d ₓ²₋ᵧ²\n Thus, 3dₓᵧ and 3dyz ; 3d ₓ²₋ᵧ² and 3d ₓ²₋ᵧ² ,represent pair of degenerate orbitals','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22625,'chemistry','In which of the following pairs, the ions are isoelectronic? \n \n (a) Na ⁺ , Mg²⁺\n \n (b) Al³⁺, O ⁻\n \n (c) Na⁺ , O ²⁻\n \n (d) N ³⁻, Cl','a,c','Na⁺ = 11-1 = 10e⁻\n Mg²⁺ = 12-2 = 10e⁻\n Thus, they have same number of electrons.\n \n Al³⁺ = 10e⁻ , O⁻ = 9e⁻\n They do not have same number of electrons.\n \n Na⁺ = 10e ,O²⁻ = 10e⁻ \n They have same number of electrons.\n \n N³⁻ = 10e⁻ , Cl⁻ = 18e⁻\n They do not have same number of electrons.\n \n Thus , Na⁺ is isoelectronic with Mg²⁺ and O²⁻.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','b,d','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22626,'chemistry','Assertion (A) All isotopes of a given element show the same type of chemical behaviour. \n \n Reason (R) The chemical properties of an atom are controlled by the number of electrons in the atom.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion. Isotopes have the same atomic number i.e., same number of electrons which are responsible for their chemical behaviour. Hence, these exhibit similar chemical properties.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-036','11-036',NULL),
(22627,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Black body is an ideal body that emits and absorbs radiations of all frequencies. \n \n Reason (R) The frequency of radiation emitted by a body goes from a lower frequency to higher frequency with an increase in temperature.','A is true but R is false','Assertion is true and reason is false. A body which absorbs and emits all radiations falling on it is called perfect black body. With rise in temperature, frequency increases.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-040','11-040',NULL),
(22628,'chemistry','Assertion (A) It is impossible to determine the exact position and exact momentum of an electron simultaneously.\n \n Reason (R) The path of an electron in an atom is clearly defined.','A is true but R is false','Assertion is true and reason is false. According to Heisenberg’s uncertainty principle, the exact position and exact momentum of an electron cannot be determined simultaneously. Thus, the path of electron in an atom is not clearly defined.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-051','11-051',NULL),
(22701,'chemistry','In a hydrogen atom, if energy of an electron in ground state is 13.6. ev, then that in the 2nd excited state is [ JEE 2002 ]','1.51 eV','2 nd excited state will be the 3rd energy level.\n E = 13.6eV/ n² = 13.6/9 = 1.51 eV','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','1.51 eV','3.4 eV','6.04 eV','13.6 eV','NEW','11-048','11-048',NULL),
(22702,'chemistry','Uncertainty in position of a minute particle of mass 25 g in space is 10⁻⁵m. What is the uncertainty in its velocity (in m/s)? (h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ Js) [JEE 2002]','2.1 × 10⁻²⁸ m/s','Δp.Δx = h / 4π \n Δx . m ΔV = h / 4π \n ΔV = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ / 4 × 3.14 × 0.025 × 10⁻⁵ = 2.1 × 10⁻²⁸ m/s','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'3','mcq','2.1 × 10²⁸ m/s','2.1 × 10⁻²⁸ m/s','2.1 m/s','2.1 × 10⁻¹⁸ m/s','NEW','11-052','11-052',NULL),
(22703,'chemistry','In a multi-electron atom, which of the following\n orbitals described by the three quantum\n members will have the same energy in the\n absence of magnetic and electric fields?[2005]\n (A) n = 1, l = 0, m = 0 \n \n (B) n = 2, l = 0, m = 0\n \n (C) n = 2, l = 1, m = 1 \n \n (D) n = 3, l = 2, m = 1\n \n (E) n = 3, l = 2, m = 0','(D) and (E)','The energy of an orbital is given by (n + l) in (d) and (c). (n + l) value is (3 + 2) = 5 hence they will have same energy, since there n values are also same.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','(D) and (E)','(C) and (D)','(B) and (C)','(A) and (B)','NEW','11-053','11-053',NULL),
(22704,'chemistry','According to Bohr\'s theory, the angular momentum of an electron in 5th orbit is [JEE 2006]','2.5h/π','Angular momentum of an electron in nth orbital is given by ,\n mvr = nh/2π \n For n = 5, we have \n Angular momentum of electron = 5h/2π = 2.5h/π','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','2.5h/π','5h/π','h/π','10h/π','NEW','11-047','11-047',NULL),
(22705,'chemistry','The increasing order of the ionic radii of the given isoelectronic species is : [JEE 2012]','Ca²⁺ , K⁺\n, Cl⁻\n, S²⁻','Among isoelectronic species ionic radii increases as the charge increases. \n Order of ionic radii Ca²⁺ < K⁺ < Cl⁻ < S²⁻ .\n The number of electrons remains the same but nuclear charge increases with increase in the atomic number causing decrease in size.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','Cl⁻, Ca²⁺ , K⁺, S²⁻','S²⁻, Cl⁻\n, Ca²⁺, K⁺','Ca²⁺ , K⁺\n, Cl⁻\n, S²⁻','K⁺, S²⁻, Ca²⁺, Cl⁻','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22706,'chemistry','The radius of the second Bohr orbit for hydrogen atom is :','2.12Å','Radius of nth Bohr orbit in H-atom \n = 0.53 n²Å \n Radius of II Bohr orbit = 0.53 × (2)²\n = 2.12 Å','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','2.12Å','1.65Å','0.529Å','2.12Å','NEW','11-047','11-047',NULL),
(22707,'chemistry','The number of d-electrons retained in Fe²⁺ (At. no. of Fe = 26) ion is','6','Fe++ (26 – 2 = 24) = 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s⁰\n 3d⁶ hence no. of d electrons retained is 6. \n [Two 4s electron are removed]','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','3','4','5','6','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22708,'chemistry','Calculate the wavelength (in nanometer) associated with a proton moving at 1.0 × 10³ m/s (Mass of proton = 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ kg and h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ Js) [JEE 2009]','0.40 nm','λ = h/mv = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ /1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ × 1.0 × 10³ = 0.40 nm','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','0.40 nm','2.5 nm','14.0 nm','0.32 nm','NEW','11-046','11-046',NULL),
(22709,'chemistry','The orbital angular momentum for an electron revolving in an orbit is given by √l(l+1).h/2π.This momentum for an s-electron will be given by [ JEE 2003]','0','For s-electron, l = 0 \n → Orbital angular momentum = √0(0+l)h/2π = 0','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','0','h/2π','√2 h/2π','1/2.h/2π','NEW','11-055','11-055',NULL),
(22710,'chemistry','Which one of the following groupings represents a collection of isoelectronic species ?(At. nos : Cs : 55, BReason : 35) [JEE 2003]','N³⁻, F⁻ and Na⁺','N³⁻, F⁻ and Na⁺ contain 10 electrons each.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','N³⁻, F⁻ and Na⁺','Be, Al³⁺, Cl⁻','Ca²⁺, Cs⁺, Br','Na⁺, Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22711,'chemistry','In Bohr series of lines of hydrogen spectrum, the third line from the red end corresponds to which one of the following inter-orbit jumps of the electron for Bohr orbits in an atom of hydrogen [JEE 2003]','5→2','The lines falling in the visible region comprise Balmer series. Hence the third line from red would be \n n₁ =2, n₂ = 5 i.e. 5 : 2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','5→2','4→1','2→5','3→2','NEW','11-046','11-046',NULL),
(22712,'chemistry','The de Broglie wavelength of a tennis ball of mass 60 g moving with a velocity of 10 metres per second is approximately [JEE 2003]','10⁻³³ metres','λ = h/mv = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴/60 × 10⁻³ × 10 = 10⁻³³','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','10⁻³¹ metres','10⁻¹⁶ metres','10⁻²⁵ metres','10⁻³³ metres','NEW','11-050','11-050',NULL),
(22713,'chemistry','Which of the following sets of quantum numbers is correct for an electron in 4f orbital ? [JEE 2004]','n = 4, l = 3, m = + 1, s = + ½','The possible quantum numbers for 4f electron are \n n = 4, l = 3, m = – 3, –2 –1, 0, 1 , 2 , 3 and s = ±1/2 \n \n Of various possiblities only possible is n = 4, l = 3, m = + 1, s = + ½','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','n = 4, l = 3, m = + 1, s = + ½','n = 4, l = 4, m = – 4, s = – ½','n = 4, l = 3, m = + 4, s = + ½','n = 3, l = 2, m = – 2, s = + ½','NEW','11-064','11-064',NULL),
(22714,'chemistry','Consider the ground state of Cr atom (X = 24). The number of electrons with the azimuthal quantum numbers, l = 1 and 2 are, respectively [JEE 2004]','12 and 5','Electronic configuration of Cr atom \n (z = 24) =1s², 2s² 2p⁶ ,3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁵ , 4s ¹\n when l = 1, p - subshell, \n Numbers of electrons = 12 \n when l = 2, d - subshell, \n Numbers of electrons = 5','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','16 and 4','12 and 5','12 and 4','16 and 5','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22715,'chemistry','The wavelength of the radiation emitted, when in a hydrogen atom electron falls from infinity to stationary state 1, would be (Rydberg constant = 1.097×10⁷ / m)','91 nm','1/λ = R (1/n₁² - 1/n₂²)\n 1/λ = 1.097 × 10⁷ ( 1/1 - 1/∞) \n λ = 91nm','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','406 nm','192 nm','91 nm','9.1×10⁻⁸ nm','NEW','11-045','11-045',NULL),
(22716,'chemistry','Which one of the following sets of ions represents the collection of isoelectronic species?','₁₉K⁺ , ₂₀Ca⁺ , ₂₁Sc⁺ , ₁₇Cl⁻','₁₉K⁺ , ₂₀Ca⁺ , ₂₁Sc⁺ , ₁₇Cl⁻ each contains 18 electrons.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','K⁺, Cl⁻, Mg²⁺, Sc³⁺','Na⁺\n, Ca²⁺, Sc³⁺, F⁻','₁₉K⁺ , ₂₀Ca⁺ , ₂₁Sc⁺ , ₁₇Cl⁻','Na⁺\n, Mg²⁺, Al³⁺, Cl⁺','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22717,'chemistry','Uncertainty in the position of an electron (mass = 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg) moving with a velocity 300 m/s , accurate upto 0.001% will be [JEE 2006] [h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴Js ]','1.92 × 10⁻² m','m = 9.1 × 10⁻³¹kg , h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ Js\n \n ΔV = (300 × 0.001)/100 = 0.003 m/s\n \n From Heisenberg\'s uncertainity principle\n Δx = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ / 4 × 3.14 × 0.03 × 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ = 1.92 × 10⁻² m','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'3','mcq','1.92 × 10⁻² m','19.2 × 10⁻² m','3.84 × 10⁻² m','5.76× 10⁻² m','NEW','11-052','11-052',NULL),
(22718,'chemistry','Which one of the following sets of ions represents a collection of isoelectronic species? [ JEE 2006 ]','K⁺, Cl⁻, Ca²⁺, Sc³⁺','Isoelectronic - Same no of electrons \n K⁺, Cl⁻, Ca²⁺, Sc³⁺ has same no electrons (18e⁻)','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','N³⁻, O²⁻, F⁻, S⁻','Li⁺, Na⁺, Mg²⁺, Ca²⁺','K⁺, Cl⁻, Ca²⁺, Sc³⁺','Ba²⁺, Sr²⁺, K⁺, Ca²⁺','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22719,'chemistry','Which of the following sets of quantum numbers represents the highest energy of an atom? [JEE 2007]','n = 3, l = 2, m = 1, s = +1/2','→ n = 3, l = 0 means 3s-orbital and n + l = 3 \n → n = 3, l =1 means 3p-orbital n + l = 4 \n → n = 3, l = 2 means 3d-orbital n + l = 5 \n → n = 4, l = 0 means 4s-orbital n + l = 4 \n Increasing order of energy among these orbitals is \n 3s < 3p < 4s < 3d \n → 3d has highest energy.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','n = 3, l = 0, m = 0, s = +1/2','n = 3, l = 1, m = 1, s = +1/2','n = 3, l = 2, m = 1, s = +1/2','n = 4, l = 0, m = 0, s = +1/2','NEW','11-061','11-061',NULL),
(22720,'chemistry','Which one of the following constitutes a group of the isoelectronic species? [JEE 2008]','NO⁺ ,C²⁻₂ ,CN⁻ , N₂','Species having same number of electrons are isoelectronic calculating the number of electrons in each species given here, we \n get. \n CN⁻ (6 + 7 + 1 = 14); \n N₂ (7 + 7 = 14); \n O₂²⁻(8 + 8 +2 = 18) ; \n C²⁻₂ (6 + 6 + 2 = 14); \n O2 (8 + 8 + 1 = 17) ; \n NO⁺ (7 + 8 – 1 = 14) \n CO (6 + 8 = 14) ; \n NO (7 + 8 = 15) \n From the above calculation we find that all \n the species listed in choice have 14 \n electrons each so it is the correct answer','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','C²₂ ,O⁻₂ ,CO, NO','NO⁺ ,C²⁻₂ ,CN⁻ , N₂','CN⁻ , N₂ ,O₂²⁻ ,C₂²⁻','N₂ ,O⁻₂ , NO⁺ ,CO','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22721,'chemistry','The ionization enthalpy of hydrogen atom is 1.312 × 10⁶ J/mol . The energy required to excite the electron in the atom from n = 1 to n = 2 is [JEE 2008]','9.84 × 10⁵ J/mol','(ΔE), The energy required to excite an electron in an atom of hydrogen from n = 1 to n = 2 is ΔE (difference in energy E2 and E1 ) \n Values of E₂ and E₁ are , E₂= -1.312 × 10⁶ × 1²/ 2² = -3.28 × 10⁵ J/mol\n \n ΔE is given by the relation = -1.312 × 10⁶ J/mol \n →ΔE = E₂ - E₁ = (–3.28 × 10⁵ + 1.312 × 10⁶ ) J/mol = 9.84 × 10⁵ J/mol','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','9.84  J/mol','9.84 × 10³ J/mol','9.84 × 10⁸ J/mol','9.84 × 10⁵ J/mol','NEW','11-048','11-048',NULL),
(22722,'chemistry','In an atom, an electron is moving with a speed of 600 m/s with an accuracy of 0.005%. Certainity with which the position of the electron can be located is ( h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ kg m²/s , mass of electron, em = 9.1 × 10⁻³¹kg) ( JEE 2009)','3.84 × 10⁻³ m','According to Heisenberg uncertainty principle.\n Δx . m ΔV = h / 4π \n Δx = h/4πm ΔV = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ / 4 × 3.14 × 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ × 0.03 = 1.92 × 10⁻³ meter','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','5.10 × 10⁻³ m','3.84 × 10⁻³ m','1.92 × 10⁻³ m','1.52 × 10⁻⁴ m','NEW','11-051','11-051',NULL),
(22723,'chemistry','The energy required to break one mole of Cl – Cl bonds in Cl₂ is 242 kJ /mol. The longest wavelength of light capable of breaking a single Cl – Cl bond is [ JEE 2010 ]','494 nm','Energy required to break one mole of Cl – Cl bonds in Cl₂\n →242 × 10³/ 6.023 × 10²³ = hc/λ =6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3 × 10⁸/ λ','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','594 nm','640 nm','700 nm','494 nm','NEW','11-043','11-043',NULL),
(22724,'chemistry','Ionisation energy of He⁺ is 19.6 × 10⁻¹⁸ J/atom. The energy of the first stationary state (n = 1) of Li²⁺ is [JEE 2010]','– 4.41 × 10⁻¹⁷ J/atom','I. E = (Z²/n²) × 13.6eV-----(i)\n \n I₁/I₂ = Z₁²×n₂² / Z₂²× n₁² ----(ii)\n \n Given \n \n I₁ = – 19.6 × 10⁻¹⁸ \n Z₁ = 2, \n n₁ = 1 , \n Z₂ = 3 and \n n₂ = 1 \n Substituting these values in equation (ii).\n \n - 19.6 × 10⁻¹⁸ / I₂ = 4 × 1 / 1 × 9\n I₂ = – 4.41 × 10⁻¹⁷ J/atom','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'3','mcq','4.41 × 10⁻¹⁶ J/atom','– 4.41 × 10⁻¹⁷ J/atom','–2.2 × 10⁻¹⁵ J/atom','8.82 × 10⁻¹⁷ J/atom','NEW','11-047','11-047',NULL),
(22725,'chemistry','The frequency of light emitted for the transition n = 4 to n = 2 of the He⁺ is equal to the transition in H atom corresponding to which of the following? ( JEE 2011)','n = 2 to n = 1','For He⁺ , \n \n γ = 1/λ = RZ²( 1/2² - 1/4²) \n \n For H \n γ = 1/λ = RZ²( 1/n₂² - 1/n₁²) \n \n For same frequency,\n \n z² = ( 1/2²- 1/4²) = ( 1/n₂² - 1/n₁²) \n Since, z = 2 \n n₁ = 1 , n₂ = 2.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','n = 2 to n = 1','n = 3 to n = 2','n = 4 to n = 3','n = 3 to n = 1','NEW','11-045','11-045',NULL),
(22726,'chemistry','The electrons identified by quantum numbers n and l : can be placed in order of increasing energy as : [JEE 2012]','n = 4, l = 0','Accroding to Bohr Bury\'s (n + l) rule, increasing order of energy (D) < (B) < (C) < (A). \n Note : If the two orbitals have same value of (n + l) then the orbital with lower value of n will be filled first.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','n = 4, l = 1','n = 4, l = 0','n = 3, l = 2','n = 3, l = 1','NEW','11-063','11-063',NULL),
(22727,'chemistry','The increasing order of the ionic radii of the \n given isoelectronic species is : [2012]','Ca²⁺ , K⁺, Cl⁻, S²⁻','Among isoelectronic species ionic radii increases as the charge increases. \n \n Order of ionic radii Ca²⁺ , K⁺, Cl⁻, S²⁻ \n \n The number of electrons remains the same but nuclear charge increases with increase in the atomic number causing decrease in size.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Cl⁻\n, Ca²⁺ , K⁺, S²⁻','S²⁻, Cl⁻, Ca²⁺ , K⁺','Ca²⁺ , K⁺, Cl⁻, S²⁻','K⁺, S²⁻, Ca²⁺, Cl⁻','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(22728,'chemistry','Energy of an electron is given by E = – 2.178 × 10⁻¹⁸ J ( Z²/n²). Wavelength of light required to no excite an electron in an hydrogen atom from level n = 1 to n = 2 will be : [ JEE 2013 ]','1.214 × 10⁻⁷ m','ΔE = – 2.178 × 10⁻¹⁸ ( 1/1² - 1/2²)= hc/λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3 × 10⁸/ λ\n \n λ = 1.214 × 10⁻⁷ m','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','1.214 × 10⁻⁷ m','2.816 × 10⁻⁷ m','6.500 × 10⁻⁷ m','8.5 × 10⁻⁷ m','NEW','11-047','11-047',NULL),
(22729,'chemistry','The correct set of four quantum numbers for the valence electrons of rubidium atom (Z = 37) is: [JEE 2014 ]','5,0,0,+1/2','The electronic configuration of Rubidium (Rb = 37) is 1s²2s²2p⁶3s²3p⁶3d¹⁰4s²4p⁶5s¹ \n Since last electron enters in 5s orbital \n Hence n = 5, l = 0, m=0 , s =+1/2','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','5,0,0,+1/2','5,1,0,+1/2','5,1,1,+1/2','5,0,1,+1/2','NEW','11-053','11-053',NULL),
(22730,'chemistry','Which of the following is the energy of a possible excited state of hydrogen? [JEE M 2015]','–3.4 eV','The energies (in eV/atom) of electron in a hydrogen atom in first, second, third, fourth and fifth energy levels are -13.6, -3.4, -1.51, -0.85 and -0.544 respectively. Hence, energy of a possible excited state of hydrogen is -3.4 eV.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','–3.4 eV','6.8eV','13.6 eV','–6.8 eV','NEW','11-044','11-044',NULL),
(22731,'chemistry','A stream of electrons from a heated filaments was passed two charged plates kept at a potential difference V esu. If e and m are charge and mass of an electron, respectively, then the value of h/l (where l is wavelength associated with electron wave) is given by: [JEE M 2016]','√2meV','Kinetic energy,K = 1/2mv² = eV\n \n v = √ 2eV/m → (I)\n Here, v is the velocity of the electron.\n \n The de broglie wavelength is λ = h/mv\n h/λ = mv →(II)\n \n Substitute equation (I) in equation (II)\n \n h/λ = √2meV','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','√2meV','√meV','meV','2meV','NEW','11-042','11-042',NULL),
(22732,'chemistry','The radius of the second Bohr orbit for hydrogen atom is : [JEE M 2017]','2.12Å','Radius of nth Bohr orbit in H-atom \n = 0.53 n²Å \n Radius of II Bohr orbit = 0.53 × (2)²\n = 2.12 Å','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','1.65Å','4.76Å','0.529Å','2.12Å','NEW','11-047','11-047',NULL),
(22733,'chemistry','The group having isoelectronic species is : [JEE M 2017]','O²⁻\n , F⁻\n , Na⁺, Mg²⁺','Isoelectronic species have same no. of electrons.\n O²⁻ , F⁻ , Na⁺, Mg²⁺ has an 10e⁻','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','O²⁻\n , F⁻\n , Na⁺, Mg²⁺','O⁻\n, F⁻\n, Na, Mg⁺','O²⁻\n, F⁻\n, Na , Mg²⁺','O⁻\n , F⁻\n, Na⁺ , Mg²⁺','NEW','11-067','11-067',NULL),
(22901,'chemistry','Maximum number of electrons in a subshell with l = 2 and n = 3 is','10','Total no of electrons= 4l + 2 = 4(2) + 2 = 10 electrons','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Numerical questions',NULL,'1','mcq','14','6','10','18','NEW','11-062','11-062',NULL),
(22902,'chemistry','The energy absorbed by each molecule (A₂) of a substance is 4.4 × 10⁻¹⁷J and bond energy per molecule is 4.0 ×\n 10⁻¹⁷ J. The kinetic energy of the molecule per atom will be','2 × 10⁻¹⁸ J','KE = Energy observed by molecule – Energy required to break one bond\n \n KE = 4.4 × 10⁻¹⁷J - 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁷ J /2 = 0.4 × 10⁻¹⁷J /2 = 2 × 10⁻¹⁸ J','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Numerical questions',NULL,'1','mcq','2 × 10⁻¹⁸ J','2 × 10⁻⁸ J','2 × 10⁻²⁰ J','2 J','NEW','11-041','11-041',NULL),
(22903,'chemistry','In H-atom, if \'x\' is the radius of first Bohr orbit, de-Broglie wavelength of an electron in 3 orbit is','6πx','r1 = x \n \n r3 = r1 × 3² = 9x\n \n λ = h/mv →mvr = nh/2π\n \n mv = nh /2πr\n \n λ = h /( nh/2πr) = 2π(9x)/3 = 6πx','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Numerical questions',NULL,'2','mcq','3πx','6πx','9x/2','x/2','NEW','11-047','11-047',NULL),
(22904,'chemistry','Wavelength of photon which have energy equal to average of energy of photons with λ1 = 4000Å and λ2 = 6000Å will be','4800 Å','E = (E1 + E2) / 2\n \n 1/λ = 1/2 ( 1/λ₁ + 1/λ₂) \n \n 1/λ = ( λ₁ + λ₂) /( 2λ₁ + λ₂)\n \n λ = 2λ₁λ₂/λ₁ + λ₂ = 2 x 4000 x 6000/10000 \n \n λ = 4800 Å','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Numerical questions',NULL,'2','mcq','5000 Å','4800 Å','9600 Å','2400 Å','NEW','11-048','11-048',NULL),
(22905,'chemistry','The photoelectric threshold wavelength for a metal surface is 6600Å. The work function for the metal is','1.87 eV','φ = hc/λ = 12400eV.Å /λ = 12400ÅeV/6600Å = 1.87 eV','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Numerical questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2.87 eV','1.87 eV','3.75 eV','0.94 eV','NEW','11-042','11-042',NULL),
(23004,'chemistry','Assertion : K.E. of two subatomic particles, having same de-Broglie’s wavelength is same. \n Reason : de-Broglie’s wavelength is directly related to mass of subatomic particles.','A is false but R is true','The Assertion is false, while the Reason is true.\n \n According to the de Broglie hypothesis, every particle has a wavelength associated with it, given by the equation λ = h/p, where λ is the de Broglie wavelength, h is Planck\'s constant, and p is the momentum of the particle. The de Broglie wavelength , h is Planck\'s constant, and p is the momentum of the particle. The de Broglie wavelength is directly proportional to the momentum of the particle and inversely proportional to its mass.\n \n Therefore, Reason is true, as the de Broglie wavelength is related to the mass of subatomic particles. For example, heavier particles like protons and neutrons have a shorter de Broglie wavelength than lighter particles like electrons.\n \n However, Assertion is false, as the kinetic energy of subatomic particles with the same de Broglie wavelength can be different depending on their mass and momentum. Kinetic energy is given by the equation KE = p^2/2m, where m is the mass of the particle. Two particles with the same de Broglie wavelength can have different momenta and therefore different kinetic energies if they have different masses.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-050','11-050',NULL),
(23005,'chemistry','Assertion : Electronic energy for hydrogen atom of different orbitals follow the sequence : \n 1s < 2s = 2p < 3s = 3p = 3d. \n Reason : Electronic energy for hydrogen atom depends only on n and is independent of ‘l’ & ‘m’ values.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','The Assertion is true, while the Reason is also true.\n \n The electronic energy levels of a hydrogen atom are given by the equation E = (-13.6 eV) × (Z²/n²), where Z is the atomic number (which is 1 for hydrogen), n is the principal quantum number (1, 2, 3, etc.), and eV is the electron volt, a unit of energy. The energy levels of the hydrogen atom correspond to different orbitals, where each orbital is defined by a set of quantum numbers (n, l, m).\n \n In the case of hydrogen, the energy of the electron depends only on the principal quantum number (n) and is independent of the values of the angular momentum quantum number (l) and the magnetic quantum number (m). Therefore, Reason is true.\n \n Regarding Assertion, it is also true that the electronic energy for hydrogen atoms of different orbitals follows the sequence 1s < 2s = 2p < 3s = 3p = 3d. This sequence is based on the values of n and l for each orbital. The 1s orbital has the lowest energy, followed by the 2s and 2p orbitals, which have the same energy. The 3s, 3p, and 3d orbitals all have the same energy, which is higher than the energy of the 2s and 2p orbitals.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-061','11-061',NULL),
(23006,'chemistry','Assertion : Wavelength for first line of any series in hydrogen spectrum is biggest among all other lines of the same series. \n Reason : Wavelength of spectral line for an electronic transition is inversely related to difference in the energy levels involved in the transition.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','The Assertion is true, while the Reason is also true.\n \n The hydrogen spectrum consists of several series of spectral lines that are emitted when electrons in the hydrogen atom transition from higher energy levels to lower energy levels. Each series is named after the scientist who first described it, such as the Lyman series, Balmer series, Paschen series, etc.\n \n For each series, the first line has the longest wavelength and the lines become closer together as the energy levels approach each other. Therefore, Assertion is true.\n \n Regarding Reason, it is also true that the wavelength of a spectral line for an electronic transition is inversely related to the difference in energy levels involved in the transition. This relationship is given by the Rydberg formula, which relates the wavelength of a spectral line to the difference in energy levels between the initial and final states of the electronic transition. The formula is:\n \n 1/λ = R [ (1/n²(1)) - (1/n²(2)) ] where λ is the wavelength of the spectral line, R is the Rydberg constant, and n1 and n2 are the initial and final quantum numbers of the energy levels involved in the transition. As the energy difference between the energy levels increases, the wavelength of the spectral line decreases. Therefore, Reason is also true.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-044','11-044',NULL),
(23007,'chemistry','Assertion : Zn(II) salts are diamagnetic. \n Reason : Zn²+ ion has one unpaired electron.','A is true but R is false','The Assertion is true, while the Reason is false.\n \n Zinc (Zn) has an electronic configuration of [Ar]3d¹⁰4s², and when it loses two electrons to form Zn²⁺, it has a completely filled 3d¹⁰ electronic shell, with no unpaired electrons. Therefore, the Zn²⁺ ion is diamagnetic and does not exhibit any magnetic moment in the presence of an external magnetic field.The diamagnetic character of Zn²⁺ is due to the fact that all of its electrons are paired up in the lowest energy orbitals available, leaving no unpaired electrons to generate a magnetic field.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-066','11-066',NULL),
(23008,'chemistry','Assertion : In third energy level there is no f-subshell. \n Reason : For n = 3, the possible values of l are 0, 1, 2 and for f-subshell l = 3.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','In third energy level there is no f-subshell. For n = 3, the possible values of l are 0, 1, 2 and for f-subshell l = 3.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-061','11-061',NULL),
(31404,'chemistry','Which of the following is the most basic?','Na₂O','The normal oxide formed by the element on extreme left is the most basic (ne.g., Na₂O), whereas that formed by the element on extreme right is the most acidic (ne.g., Cl₂O₇). \n\nOxides of elements in the centre are amphoteric (ne.g., Al₂O₃, As₂O₃) or neutral (ne.g., CO, NO, N₂O).\n\nAmphoteric oxides behave as acidic with bases and as basic with acids, whereas neutral oxides have no acidic or basic properties \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 94; Paragraph no. 2 )','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Periodic Trends and Chemical reactivity',NULL,'1','mcq','Na₂O','Al₂O₃','MgO','SiO₂','NEW','11-094','11-094','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/31404q.png'),
(23009,'chemistry','Assertion : The charge to mass ratio of the particles in anode rays depends on nature of gas taken in the discharge tube. \n Reason : The particles of anode rays carry positive charge.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','While both statements are correct, they are not an accurate explanation of the phenomena of anode rays.\n \n Anode rays are streams of charged particles, specifically positively charged ions or negatively charged electrons, that are produced when a high voltage is applied between two electrodes in a gas-filled tube. The nature of the gas in the tube affects the charge-to-mass ratio of the particles, but it does not determine whether the particles carry a positive or negative charge. The charge of the particles depends on the electrode from which they are emitted: positively charged ions are produced by the anode, while negatively charged electrons are produced by the cathode.\n \n Therefore, while both statements are correct, they do not provide a complete or accurate explanation of the phenomena of anode rays.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-030','11-030',NULL),
(23010,'chemistry','Assertion : Angular momentum of an electron in an atom is quantized. \n Reason : In an atom only those orbits are permitted in which angular momentum of the electron is a natural number multiple of h /2π','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both statements A and R are correct.\n \n The angular momentum of an electron in an atom is quantized, meaning it can only take on certain discrete values. This was first proposed by Niels Bohr in his model of the hydrogen atom. Bohr\'s model stated that electrons in atoms move in circular orbits around the nucleus, and that the angular momentum of the electron in these orbits is quantized, or restricted to certain discrete values.\n \n The statement in R refers to Bohr\'s model of the hydrogen atom, where he proposed that only certain orbits, called stationary states, are permitted for electrons in an atom. These stationary states are characterized by their principal quantum number (n), which corresponds to the size and energy of the orbit. According to Bohr\'s model, the angular momentum (L) of an electron in a stationary state is given by L = n(h/2π), where h is Planck\'s constant.\n \n Thus, the statement in R is correct: only those orbits are permitted in which the angular momentum of the electron is a natural number multiple of h/2π. This is because, in Bohr\'s model, the angular momentum of the electron must be quantized and restricted to certain discrete values.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-046','11-046',NULL),
(23011,'chemistry','Assertion : The radius of second orbit of He+ is equal to that of first orbit of hydrogen. \n Reason : The radius of an orbit in hydrogen like species is directly proportional to n and inversely proportional to Z.','A is false but R is true','The Assertion is false. According to Bohr\'s model of the atom, the radius of an electron orbit in a hydrogen-like species is proportional to n²/Z, where n is the principal quantum number and Z is the atomic number. Therefore, for He+ (Z=2), the radius of the second orbit would be (2²/2) times that of the first orbit of hydrogen (Z=1). This means that the radius of the second orbit of He+ would be four times larger than the radius of the first orbit of hydrogen, and not equal to it.\n \n The Reason is true. According to Bohr\'s model, the radius of an electron orbit in a hydrogen-like species is directly proportional to n and inversely proportional to Z. This means that for two hydrogen-like species with the same value of n, the orbit with the smaller value of Z will have a larger radius. Similarly, for two hydrogen-like species with the same value of Z, the orbit with the larger value of n will have a larger radius. Therefore, Reason is true, but Assertion is false.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-048','11-048',NULL),
(23012,'chemistry','Assertion : The orbitals having equal energy are known as degenerate orbitals. \n Reason : The three 2p orbitals are degenerate in the presence of external magnetic field.','A is true but R is false','A is true because orbitals that have the same energy level are called degenerate orbitals. This is because they have the same energy but different spatial orientations.\n \n However, R is false because the three 2p orbitals are not degenerate in the presence of an external magnetic field. In fact, the presence of an external magnetic field will cause the three 2p orbitals to split into three different energy levels, with the m=0 orbital having the lowest energy and the m=±1 orbitals having higher energy. This is known as the Zeeman effect and it is caused by the interaction between the magnetic field and the magnetic moment of the electron. The splitting of the 2p orbitals in a magnetic field is used in many spectroscopic techniques to study the electronic structure of atoms and molecules.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-060','11-060',NULL),
(23013,'chemistry','Assertion : In a multielectron atom, the electrons in different sub-shells have different energies.\n Reason : Energy of an orbital depends upon n + l value.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true statements.\n \n A is true because in a multielectron atom, the electrons in different subshells experience different effective nuclear charges due to shielding by other electrons. As a result, they have different energies.\n \n R is also true because the energy of an atomic orbital in a hydrogen-like atom (an atom with one electron) is determined by the principal quantum number (n) and the azimuthal quantum number (l) according to the expression E = -13.6 eV/n^2 * Z^2, where Z is the atomic number. However, in a multielectron atom, the energy of an orbital also depends on the screening or shielding effects of the other electrons, which increases with increasing value of l. Therefore, the energy of an orbital in a multielectron atom is determined by the values of both n and l, and is generally different from the energy of an orbital with the same n and l value in a hydrogen-like atom.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-061','11-061',NULL),
(213310,'chemistry','All halogens react with hydrogen to give _________  but affinity for hydrogen _______ from fluorine to iodine.','hydrogen halide, decreases','All halogens react with hydrogen to give hydrogen halide but affinity for hydrogen decreases from fluorine to iodine.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 201 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','hydrogen halide, increases','hypohalous acids, increases','hydrogen halide, decreases','hypohalous acids, decreases','NEW','12-201',NULL,NULL),
(23014,'chemistry','Assertion : Isotopes of an element have almost similar chemical properties.\n Reason : Isotopes have same electronic configuration.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Isotopes of an element have almost similar chemical properties because they have the same number of protons, which determines the element\'s chemical properties. However, isotopes can have different numbers of neutrons, which can affect their physical properties, such as their atomic mass and radioactivity.Isotopes have same electronic configuration.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-035','11-035',NULL),
(23015,'chemistry','Assertion : The number of angular nodes in 3dz² is zero. \n Reason : Number of angular nodes of atomic orbitals is equal to value of l.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','The Assertion is true because the 3dz² orbital has a l value of 2 and a m value of 0, which gives it a nodal plane along the z-axis. However, there are no nodal planes that pass through the nucleus. Therefore, the number of angular nodes in the 3dz² orbital is zero.\n \n The Reason is also true because the number of angular nodes in an atomic orbital is equal to its l value. For example, an s orbital (l=0) has no angular nodes, a p orbital (l=1) has one angular node, a d orbital (l=2) has two angular nodes, and so on.\n \n However, the Reason does not correctly explain Assertion. The fact that the number of angular nodes in the 3dz² orbital is zero is due to the specific geometry of the orbital, which has a nodal plane along the z-axis, rather than simply being a consequence of its l value. Therefore, Assertion and Reason are both true, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-059','11-059',NULL),
(130324,'chemistry','Carbon atom attached to one other carbon atom is called?','primary','Based upon the number of carbon atoms attached to a carbon atom, the carbon atom is termed as primary (1°), secondary (2°), tertiary (3°) or quaternary (4°).  \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-375, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','primary','secondary','tertiary','quarternary','NEW','11-375','11-297',NULL),
(130325,'chemistry','Terminal carbon atoms are?','primary','Terminal carbon atoms are always primary.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-375, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','fillup','pr,im,ary,sec,on,da',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-375','11-297',NULL),
(130326,'chemistry','Quaternary carbon is also known as_____','neo','neo or quaternary carbon is attached to four carbon atoms.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-375, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','fillup','neo,te,rt,ia,ry',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-375','11-297',NULL),
(130327,'chemistry','Number of isomers for C₆H₁₄, C₇H₁₆ and C₁₀H₂₂ respectively are?','Five, nine and seventy-five respectively','C₆H₁₄ has got five isomers and C₇H₁₆ has nine. \n\nAs many as 75 isomers are possible for C₁₀H₂₂.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-376, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'2','mcq','Five, twelve and eighty respectively','Five, nine and seventy-five respectively','Five, nine and sixty-five respectively','Five, ten and seventy-five respectively','NEW','11-376','11-298',NULL),
(130328,'chemistry','General formula for alkyl groups is?','CₙH₂ₙ₊₁','Alkyl are the group that are obtained by removing one hydrogen atom from the alkane. \n\nThus, the general formula for alkyl groups is CₙH₂ₙ₊₁ .\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-376, Paragraph - 5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','CₙH₂ₙ-₂','CₙH₂ₙ₊₂','CₙH₂ₙ','CₙH₂ₙ₊₁','NEW','11-376','11-298',NULL),
(130329,'chemistry','Which of the following is not an isomer of pentanol?','3,3-dimethylpentanol','All the isomers of pentanol must contain a total of 5 carbons. \n\nIn the given options, all the compounds except the 3,3-dimethylpentol have 5 carbons atoms. \n\n3,3-Dimethylpentol has total 7 carbon atoms. Hence not an isomer of pentanol.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-376, Paragraph - example) diagram(ne1302)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'2','mcq','3-methylbutan-1-ol','2,2-dimethylpropan-1-ol','3,3-dimethylpentanol','3-methylbutan-2-ol','NEW','11-376','11-298',NULL),
(130331,'chemistry','Match the compounds with their IUPAC names given in the diagram?(n13129)','1-(pentan-3-ol),2-(3-methyl-butan-1-ol),3-(2-methyl-butan-1-ol),4-(2-methyl-butan-2-ol)','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-376, Paragraph - example) diagram(ne1302)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','match','1,(pentan-3-ol)','2,(3-methyl-butan-1-ol)','3,(2-methyl-butan-1-ol)','4,(2-methyl-butan-2-ol)','NEW','11-376','11-298','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/match/130331q.png'),
(130332,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name of the following compound? diagram(n1308)','4–ethyl–2–methylhexane','There are several sets of rules for IUPAC nomenclature. \n\n1 The longest continuous carbon chain should be found and named.\n\n2. List the names of the organisations that make up this chain.\n\n3. Starting at the end of the chain closest to a substituent group, sequentially number the chain.\n\n4. Indicate each substituent group\'s location with the relevant number and name.\n\n5. Put the name together by listing the groups alphabetically using the whole name. (e.g., cyclopropyl before isobutyl). \n\nThus the name of the given compound is 4–Ethyl–2–methylhexane.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-377, Paragraph - Table) diagram(ne1303)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','4–ethyl–2–methylhexane','3,3-diethyl-4-methyl-5-isopropyl-octane','2,2,4,4-tetramethyloctane','3,3-diethyl-5-isopropyl-4-methyloctane','NEW','11-377','11-299','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130332q.png'),
(130333,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name of the following compound given in the diagram?(n1309)','3,3-diethyl-5-isopropyl-4-methyloctane','There are several sets of rules for IUPAC nomenclature. \n\n1 The longest continuous carbon chain should be found and named.\n\n2. List the names of the organisations that make up this chain.\n\n3. Starting at the end of the chain closest to a substituent group, sequentially number the chain.\n\n4. Indicate each substituent group\'s location with the relevant number and name.\n\n5. Put the name together by listing the groups alphabetically using the whole name. (e.g., cyclopropyl before isobutyl). \n\nThus the name of the given compound is 3,3-Diethyl-5-isopropyl-4-methyloctane.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-377, Paragraph - Table) diagram(ne1303)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','2, 2, 4, 4-tetramethyloctane','3,3-diethyl-4-methyl-5-isopropyl-octane','3,3-diethyl-5-isopropyl-4-methyloctane','3,4,4,5–tetramethylheptane','NEW','11-377','11-299','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130333q.png'),
(213311,'chemistry','The acidic strength of thes acids varies in the order:','HF < HCl < HBr < HI','The acidic strength of the acids varies in the order: HF < HCl < HBr < HI\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 201 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','HF > HCl > HBr > HI','HF < HCl < HBr < HI','HF> HBr > HI > HCl','HF< HBr > HI > HCl','NEW','12-201',NULL,NULL),
(130334,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name of the following compound given in the diagram?(n1310)','5-sec–butyl-decane','There are several sets of rules for IUPAC nomenclature. \n\n1 The longest continuous carbon chain should be found and named.\n\n2. List the names of the organisations that make up this chain.\n\n3. Starting at the end of the chain closest to a substituent group, sequentially number the chain.\n\n4. Indicate each substituent group\'s location with the relevant number and name.\n\n5. Put the name together by listing the groups alphabetically using the whole name. (e.g., cyclopropyl before isobutyl). \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-377, Paragraph - Table) diagram(ne1303)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','3,3-diethyl-4-methyl-5-isopropyl-octane','5-sec–butyl-decane','2-ethyl-4-butyloctane','3–ethyl–5–methylheptane','NEW','11-377','11-299','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130334q.png'),
(130335,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name of the following compound given in the diagram?(n1311)','5-neopentyl nonane','There are several sets of rules for IUPAC nomenclature. \n\n1 The longest continuous carbon chain should be found and named.\n\n2. List the names of the organisations that make up this chain.\n\n3. Starting at the end of the chain closest to a substituent group, sequentially number the chain.\n\n4. Indicate each substituent group\'s location with the relevant number and name.\n\n5. Put the name together by listing the groups alphabetically using the whole name. (e.g., cyclopropyl before isobutyl). \n\nThus the name of the given compound is 5-(2,2– Dimethylpropyl)nonane.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-377, Paragraph - Table) diagram(ne1303)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','3,3-diethyl-4-methyl-5-isopropyl-octane','5-sec–butyl-4-isopropyldecane','5-neopentyl nonane','3–ethyl–5–methylheptane','NEW','11-377','11-299','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130335q.png'),
(130336,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name of the following compound given in the diagram?(n1312)','3–ethyl–5–methylheptane','There are several sets of rules for IUPAC nomenclature. \n\n1 The longest continuous carbon chain should be found and named.\n\n2. List the names of the organisations that make up this chain.\n\n3. Starting at the end of the chain closest to a substituent group, sequentially number the chain.\n\n4. Indicate each substituent group\'s location with the relevant number and name.\n\n5. Put the name together by listing the groups alphabetically using the whole name. (e.g., cyclopropyl before isobutyl). \n\nThus the name of the given compound is 3–Ethyl–5–methylheptane.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-377, Paragraph - Table) diagram(ne1303)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','2,2,4,4-tetramethylpentane','5-sec–butyl-4-isopropyldecane','5-(2,2–dimethylpropyl)nonane','3–ethyl–5–methylheptane','NEW','11-377','11-299','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130336q.png'),
(130337,'chemistry','IUPAC name of tetra – tert-butylmethane is?','3,3-di-tert-butyl-2,2,4,4-tetramethylpentane','There are several sets of rules for IUPAC nomenclature. \n\n1 The longest continuous carbon chain should be found and named.\n\n2. List the names of the organisations that make up this chain.\n\n3. Starting at the end of the chain closest to a substituent group, sequentially number the chain.\n\n4. Indicate each substituent group\'s location with the relevant number and name.\n\n5. Put the name together by listing the groups alphabetically using the whole name. (e.g., cyclopropyl before isobutyl)\n\nThus the compound will be named as 3,3-Di-tert-butyl -2, 2, 4, 4 -tetramethylpentane (The naming is based on IUPAC rules mentioned in above answers).\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-377, Paragraph - Problem)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'2','mcq','2,2,4,4-tetramethylpentane','4–ethyl–2–methylhexane','3,3-di-tert-butyl-2,2,4,4-tetramethylpentane','3,3-di-tert-butyl-2,2,4,4-tetramethylpropane','NEW','11-377','11-299',NULL),
(130338,'chemistry','How many covalent bonds are present in structure of 3-ethyl-2, 2–dimethylpentane, refer diagram?(n1313)','28','The compound is a saturated compound and 26 comes when we calculate all the bonds.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-378, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','35','28','22','18','NEW','11-378','11-300','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130338q.png'),
(130339,'chemistry','Choose the correct structure for the compound named 3,4,4,5-Tetramethylheptane, refer diagram?(n1314)','a','We can get a clarification by drawing the structure according to following steps:\n\n1. Draw the carbon chain.\n\n2. Number the carbon atoms.\n\n3. Attach the substituents on the respective carbon atoms.\n\n4. Satisfy the valency of carbon atoms by adding the required number of hydrogen atoms on each carbon.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-378, Paragraph - Problem) diagram(ne1304)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-378','11-300','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130339q.png'),
(130340,'chemistry','Choose the correct structure for the compound named 2,5-Dimethyhexane, refer diagram?(n1314)','b','We can get a clarification by drawing the structure according to following steps:\n\n1. Draw the carbon chain.\n\n2. Number the carbon atoms.\n\n3. Attach the substituents on the respective carbon atoms.\n\n4. Satisfy the valency of carbon atoms by adding the required number of hydrogen atoms on each carbon.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-378, Paragraph - Problem) diagram(ne1304)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-378','11-300','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130340q.png'),
(130341,'chemistry','Choose the correct structure for the compound named 3-Methylheptane, refer diagram?(n1314)','c','We can get a clarification by drawing the structure according to following steps:\n\n1. Draw the carbon chain.\n\n2. Number the carbon atoms.\n\n3. Attach the substituents on the respective carbon atoms.\n\n4. Satisfy the valency of carbon atoms by adding the required number of hydrogen atoms on each carbon.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-378, Paragraph - Problem) For eg refer to diagram(ne1305)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-378','11-300','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130341q.png'),
