(130346,'chemistry','Which catalyst/s catalyse hydrogenation reaction at room temperature?','Both a and b','Platinum and palladium catalyse the reaction at room temperature but relatively higher temperature and pressure are required with nickel catalysts.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-378, Paragraph - 5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Pt','Pd','Ni','Both a and b','NEW','11-378','11-300',NULL),
(130347,'chemistry','Which catalyst catalyse hydrogenation reaction under high pressure?','Ni','Platinum and palladium catalyse the reaction at room temperature but relatively higher temperature and pressure are required with nickel catalysts.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-378, Paragraph - 5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Pt','Pd','Ni','Zn','NEW','11-378','11-300',NULL),
(130348,'chemistry','Alkyl halides except ________ on reduction with zinc and dilute hydrochloric acid give alkanes.','Fluorides','Alkyl halides (except fluorides) on reduction with zinc and dilute hydrochloric acid give alkanes. \n\nAs fluorides are more reactive, they create several byproducts before being reduced, hence alkyl fluorides cannot be reduced using zinc and HCl. \n\nThat is, when HCl and fluorine combine, chlorine gas and HF are released.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-378, Paragraph - 6)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Chlorides','Fluorides','Bromides','Iodides','NEW','11-378','11-300',NULL),
(130349,'chemistry','The catalyst used in following reaction shown in diagram?(n1316)','Zn, H⁺','Alkyl halides (except fluorides) on reduction with zinc and dilute hydrochloric acid give alkanes. \n\nThe reaction show the reduction of 1-chloropropane with hydrogen. The reagent for the reaction are zinc and a dilute acid like HCl.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-378, Paragraph - 6)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','Pt/Pd/Ni','Zn, H⁺','dry ether','CaO/heat','NEW','11-378','11-300','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130349q.png'),
(130350,'chemistry','Which of the following does not give alkane on reduction with zinc and dilute hydrochloric acid?','CH₃-F','Alkyl halides (except fluorides) on reduction with zinc and dilute hydrochloric acid give alkanes. \n\nAs fluorides are more reactive, they create several byproducts before being reduced, hence alkyl fluorides cannot be reduced using zinc and HCl. \n\nThat is, when HCl and fluorine combine, chlorine gas and HF are released.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-378, Paragraph - 6)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'2','mcq','CH₃-F','CH₃-Cl','CH₃-Br','CH₃-I','NEW','11-378','11-300',NULL),
(130351,'chemistry','Which of the following is the byproduct of the reaction given in diagram?(n1319)','HCl','Alkyl halides (except fluorides) on reduction with zinc and dilute hydrochloric acid give alkanes. \n\nThe reaction gives hydrogen halide as a byproduct. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-378, Paragraph - 6)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','Cl₂','H₂','HCl','ZnCl₂','NEW','11-378','11-300','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130351q.png'),
(130352,'chemistry','Alkyl halides on treatment with sodium metal in dry ethereal (free from moisture) solution give higher alkanes. This reaction is known as','Wurtz reaction','Alkyl halides on treatment with sodium metal in dry ethereal (free from moisture) solution give higher alkanes. \n\nThis reaction is known as Wurtz reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-379, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Reimer Tiemann reaction','Wurtz reaction','Kolbe\'s reaction','Clemenson\'s reduction','NEW','11-379','11-301',NULL),
(130353,'chemistry','The catalyst used in following reaction shown in diagram?(n1317)','Dry ether','Alkyl halides on treatment with sodium metal in dry ethereal (free from moisture) solution give higher alkanes. \n\nThis reaction is known as Wurtz reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-379, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','Pt/Pd/Ni','Zn, H⁺','Dry ether','CaO/heat','NEW','11-379','11-301','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130353q.png'),
(130354,'chemistry','Which method of preparation is used to prepare higher alkanes?','Wurtz reaction','The Wurtz reaction is an organic chemical coupling process in which sodium metal reacts with two aryl halides in the presence of a dry ether solution to produce a higher cycloalkane and a molecule that contains both sodium and the halogen. \n\nWurtz reaction is used for the preparation of higher alkanes containing even number of carbon atoms.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-379, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Reimer Tiemann reaction','Kolbe\'s reaction','Wurtz reaction','Clemenson\'s reduction','NEW','11-379','11-301',NULL),
(130355,'chemistry','The catalyst used in following reaction shown in diagram?(n1318)','CaO/heat','Sodium salts of carboxylic acids on heating with soda lime (mixture of sodium hydroxide and calcium oxide) give alkanes containing one carbon atom less than the carboxylic acid.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-379, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','Pt/Pd/Ni','Zn, H⁺','dry ether','CaO/heat','NEW','11-379','11-301','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130355q.png'),
(130356,'chemistry','Sodium salt of which acid will be needed for the preparation of propane ?','Butanoic acid,','Sodium salts of carboxylic acids on heating with soda lime (mixture of sodium hydroxide and calcium oxide) give alkanes containing one carbon atom less than the carboxylic acid. \n\nIn order to prepare propane we will use butanoic acid. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-379, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'2','mcq','Propanoic acid','Sodium propanoate','Butanoic acid,','Sodium ethanoate','NEW','11-379','11-301',NULL),
(130357,'chemistry','The process of elimination of carbon dioxide from a carboxylic acid is known as?','decarboxylation','The process of elimination of carbon dioxide from a carboxylic acid is known as decarboxylation.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-379, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','fillup','de,car,bo,xy,la,ti,on,ol,in,ul',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-379','11-301',NULL),
(213313,'chemistry','What is the bond diissociation enthalpy of HBr ?','363 kJ/mol','The bond dissociation enthalpy of HBr is 363 kJ/mol\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 201 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','295 kJ/mol','363 kJ/mol','546 kJ/mol','399 kJ/mol','NEW','12-201',NULL,NULL),
(130358,'chemistry','Name the following reaction given in diagram?(n1320)','Kolbe\'s electrolysis','In Kolbe’s electrolytic method, an aqueous solution of sodium or potassium salt of a carboxylic acid on electrolysis gives alkane containing even number of carbon atoms at the anode.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-379, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','Wurtz reaction','Kolbe\'s electrolysis','Clemenson\'s reduction','Kolbe\'s reaction','NEW','11-379','11-301','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130358q.png'),
(130359,'chemistry','In kolbe\'s electrolysis the alkane is obtained at?','Anode','In Kolbe’s electrolytic method, an aqueous solution of sodium or potassium salt of a carboxylic acid on electrolysis gives alkane containing even number of carbon atoms at the anode.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-379, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Anode','Cathode','Electrolytic solution','All of the above','NEW','11-379','11-301',NULL),
(130360,'chemistry','Which gas is evolved at anode in Kolbe\'s electrolysis reaction?','carbon dioxide','Decarboxylation of intermediate free radical takes place to generate product reacting free radical & carbon dioxide.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-379, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','chlorine','carbon dioxide','water','dihydrogen','NEW','11-379','11-301',NULL),
(130361,'chemistry','In kolbe\'s electrolysis what is obtained at cathode?','dihydrogen','In Kolbe’s electrolytic method, an aqueous solution of sodium or potassium salt of a carboxylic acid on electrolysis gives alkane containing even number of carbon atoms at the anode and dihydrogen +NaOH at cathode.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-379, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','alkane','carbon dioxide','sodium halide','dihydrogen','NEW','11-379','11-301',NULL),
(130362,'chemistry','Which of the following product cannot be obtained through kolbe\'s electrolysis?','Methane','Methane cannot be prepared by this kolbe\'s electrolysis because product is formed from reaction of two free radical alkyl groups.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-379, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'2','mcq','Ethane','Propane','Methane','Butane','NEW','11-379','11-301',NULL),
(130363,'chemistry','Alkanes are almost non-polar molecules because','All of the above','Alkanes are almost non-polar molecules because of the covalent nature of C-C and C-H bonds and due to very little difference of electronegativity between carbon and hydrogen atoms.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-379, Paragraph - 5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Covalent nature of C-C and C-H bonds','Very little difference of electronegativity between C and H','Hydrophobic Nature','All of the above','NEW','11-379','11-301',NULL),
(130364,'chemistry','Alkanes possess _______ van der Waals forces.','weak','They possess weak van der Waals forces.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-379, Paragraph - 5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','fillup','st,ro,ng,we,ak',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-379','11-301',NULL),
(130365,'chemistry','Alkanes are colourless and ______','odourless','Alkanes are colourless and odourless.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-379, Paragraph - 5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','fillup','od,ou,r,le,ss,am,mo,ni,cal',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-379','11-301',NULL),
(130366,'chemistry','_______ and lower fractions of petroleum are also used for dry cleaning of clothes to remove grease stains','Petrol','Petrol and lower fractions of petroleum are also used for dry cleaning of clothes to remove grease stains.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-379, Paragraph - 5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','fillup','Pe,tr,ol,eum,gas',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-379','11-301',NULL),
(130367,'chemistry','What do you think about solubility of alkanes in water?','Least soluble.','Alkanes are not soluble in water, which is highly polar. Since the alkanes are obtained from petrol, they are nonpolar. \n\nThe two substances do not meet the criterion of solubility, namely, that “like dissolves like.”\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-380, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Least soluble.','Mostly soluble.','Sparingly soluble.','Soluble if nitrogen is present.','NEW','11-380','11-302',NULL),
(130368,'chemistry','The correct decreasing order of boiling point of alkane is?','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃>CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃>CH₃CH₂CH₃>CH₄','There is a steady increase in boiling point with increase in molecular mass. \n\nAs the number of carbon increase in chain, there is more area available for van der waal forces. \n\nTherefore, the longer the chain the higher is the boiling point. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-380, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','order','1,CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃','2,CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃','3,CH₃CH₂CH₃','4,CH₄','NEW','11-380','11-302',NULL),
(130369,'chemistry','The correct  order of boiling point of the following compounds is?','pentane>2-methylbutane>2,2-dimethylpropane','There is a steady increase in boiling point with increase in molecular mass. \n\nAs the number of carbon increase in chain, there is more area available for van der waal forces. \nTherefore, the longer the chain the higher is the boiling point. \n\nWith increase in number of branched chains, the molecule attains the shape of a sphere. \n\nThis results in smaller area of contact and therefore weak intermolecular forces between spherical molecules, which are overcome at relatively lower temperatures. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-380, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','order','1,pentane','2,2-methylbutane','3,22-dimethylpropane',NULL,'NEW','11-380','11-302',NULL),
(130370,'chemistry','Alkanes generally inert towards?','All of the above','Alkanes are generally inert towards acids, bases, oxidising and reducing agents.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-380, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Acid','Base','Redox agents','All of the above','NEW','11-380','11-302',NULL),
(130371,'chemistry','One or more hydrogen atoms of alkanes can be replaced by?','All of the above','One or more hydrogen atoms of alkanes can be replaced by halogens, nitro group and sulphonic acid group.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-380, Paragraph - 5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Halogens','Nitro group','Sulphonic group','All of the above','NEW','11-380','11-302',NULL),
(130372,'chemistry','The catalyst of the following reaction shown in diagram?(n1321)','Sunlight','Halogenation takes place either at higher temperature (573-773 K) or in the presence of diffused sunlight or ultraviolet light.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-380, Paragraph - 5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','Hydrogen','Sunlight','Air','Ether','NEW','11-380','11-302','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130372q.png'),
(130373,'chemistry','Reactions in which hydrogen atoms of alkanes are substituted are known as ______ reactions','Substitution','The reactions in which hydrogen atoms of alkanes are substituted are known as substitution reactions.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-380, Paragraph - 5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','fillup','Su,bs,ti,tu,ti,on,ol,io,nt',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-380','11-302',NULL),
(130374,'chemistry','What product is formed in the reaction given in the diagram?(n1322)','CCl₄','The chlorination of trichloromethane gives tetrachloromethane and HCl.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-380, Paragraph - 5) diagram(ne1306)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','CH₃Cl','CH₂Cl₂','CHCl₃','CCl₄','NEW','11-380','11-302','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130374q.png'),
(130375,'chemistry','The rate of reaction of alkanes with halogens is ?','F₂>Cl₂>Br₂>I₂','It is found that the rate of reaction of alkanes with halogens is  F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂. \n\nRate of replacement of hydrogens of alkanes is:\n\n3° > 2° > 1°.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-381, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','order','1,F₂','2,Cl₂','3,Br₂','4,I₂','NEW','11-381','11-303',NULL),
(130376,'chemistry','Rate of replacement of hydrogens of alkanes is?','3° > 2° > 1°','It is found that the rate of reaction of alkanes with halogens is  F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂.\n \nRate of replacement of hydrogens of alkanes is:\n\n3° > 2° > 1°.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-381, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','3° < 2° > 1°','3° < 2° < 1°','3° > 2° > 1°','3° = 2° < 1°','NEW','11-381','11-303',NULL),
(130377,'chemistry','_________ halogenation is too violent to be controlled.','Fluorination','Fluorination is too violent to be controlled. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-381, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Fluorination','Chlorination','Bromination','Iodination','NEW','11-381','11-303',NULL),
(130378,'chemistry','_________ halogenation is very slow and a reversible reaction.','Iodination','Iodination is very slow and a reversible reaction. \n\nIt can be carried out in the presence of oxidizing agents like HIO₃ or HNO₃.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-381, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Fluorination','Chlorination','Bromination','Iodination','NEW','11-381','11-303',NULL),
(130379,'chemistry','Iodination can be carried out in the presence of ______ agents','oxidising','Iodination is very slow and a reversible reaction. \n\nIt can be carried out in the presence of oxidizing agents like HIO₃ or HNO₃.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-381, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','fillup','ox,id,is,ing,re,duc',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-381','11-303',NULL),
(130380,'chemistry','Halogenation takes place through','Free radical mechanism','Halogenation is supposed to proceed via free radical chain mechanism involving three steps namely initiation, propagation and termination.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-381, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Nucleophile substitution mechanism','Electrophilic substitution mechanism','Nucleophile addition mechanism','Free radical mechanism','NEW','11-381','11-303',NULL),
(130381,'chemistry','Which of the following is an initiation step in halogenation, refer diagram?(n1323)','a','The reaction is initiated by homolysis of chlorine molecule in the presence of light or heat. \n\nThe Cl–Cl bond is weaker than the C–C and C–H bond and hence, is easiest to break.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-381, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-381','11-303','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130381q.png'),
(130382,'chemistry','Which of the following is an propagation step in halogenation, refer diagram?(n1323)','Both b and c','Chlorine free radical attacks the methane molecule and takes the reaction in the forward direction by breaking the C-H bond to generate methyl free radical with the formation of H-Cl.\n\nThe methyl radical thus obtained attacks the second molecule of chlorine to form CH₃ – Cl with the liberation of another chlorine free radical by homolysis of chlorine molecule.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-381, Paragraph - 4,5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','Only a','Only b','Both a and b','Both b and c','NEW','11-381','11-303','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130382q.png'),
(130383,'chemistry','Which of the following is an termination step in halogenation, refer diagram?(n1323)','d','The reaction stops after some time due to consumption of reactants.\n\nThough in, CH₃ – Cl, the one of the products is formed but free radicals are consumed and the chain is terminated.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-381, Paragraph - 7)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-381','11-303','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130383q.png'),
(130384,'chemistry','The reactants involved in general combustion equation for any alkane is represented as, refer diagram?(n1324)','d','Alkanes on heating in the presence of air or dioxygen are completely oxidized to carbon dioxide and water with the evolution of large amount of heat.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-381, Paragraph - 8) diagram(ne1307)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-381','11-303','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130384q.png'),
(130385,'chemistry','What is the product of the reaction shown in the diagram?(n1325)','Carbon black','During incomplete combustion of alkanes with insufficient amount of air or dioxygen, carbon black is formed which is used in the manufacture of ink, printer ink, black pigments and as filters.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-381, Paragraph - 9)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','Carbon dioxide','Carbon monoxide','Carbon black','Carbon','NEW','11-381','11-303','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130385q.png'),
(130386,'chemistry','During incomplete combustion of alkanes with insufficient amount of air or dioxygen _____ is formed','Carbon black','During incomplete combustion of alkanes with insufficient amount of air or dioxygen, carbon black is formed which is used in the manufacture of ink, printer ink, black pigments and as filters.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-381, Paragraph - 9)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Carbon dioxide','Carbon monoxide','Carbon black','Carbon','NEW','11-381','11-303',NULL),
(130387,'chemistry','The carbon black formed after incomplete combustion of alkanes is used in','All of the above','During incomplete combustion of alkanes with insufficient amount of air or dioxygen, carbon black is formed which is used in the manufacture of ink, printer ink, black pigments and as filters.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-381, Paragraph - 9)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Manufacture of ink','Printer ink','Black pigments','All of the above','NEW','11-381','11-303',NULL),
(130388,'chemistry','The product of the following reaction will be ______, refer diagram?(n1326)','Methanol','Alkanes on heating with a regulated supply of dioxygen or air at high pressure and in the presence of suitable catalysts give a variety of oxidation products which includes methanol, methanal, ethanoic acid and 2-Methylpropan-2-ol.  \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-382, Paragraph - 1) diagram(ne1308)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','Methanol','Methanal','Methanoic acid','Methene','NEW','11-382','11-304','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130388q.png'),
(213314,'chemistry','Halogens form many oxides with oxygen but most of them are ________.','unstable','Halogens form many oxides with oxygen but most of them are unstable. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 201 Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','reactive','unstable','stable','solid','NEW','12-201',NULL,NULL),
(130389,'chemistry','The catalyst for the reaction given in diagram?(n1327)','Mo₂O₃','Alkanes on heating with a regulated supply of dioxygen or air at high pressure and in the presence of suitable catalysts give a variety of oxidation products which includes methanol, methanal, ethanoic acid and 2-Methylpropan-2-ol. \n\nThe catalyst used for this oxidation is Mo₂O₃.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-382, Paragraph - 1) diagram(ne1309)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','KMnO₄','Mo₂O₃','Cr₂O₇','O₂','NEW','11-382','11-304','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130389q.png'),
(130390,'chemistry','The product of the following reaction will be ______, refer diagram?(n1328)','Ethanoic acid','Alkanes on heating with a regulated supply of dioxygen or air at high pressure and in the presence of suitable catalysts give a variety of oxidation products which includes methanol, methanal, ethanoic acid and 2-Methylpropan-2-ol. \n\nThe product of the following reaction is ethanoic acid.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-382, Paragraph - 1) diagram(ne1310)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','Ethane','Ethanol','Ethanal','Ethanoic acid','NEW','11-382','11-304','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130390q.png'),
(130391,'chemistry','The catalyst for the reaction given in diagram?(n1329)','KMnO₄','Ordinarily alkanes resist oxidation but alkanes having tertiary H atom can be oxidized to corresponding alcohols by potassium permanganate. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-382, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','Mo₂O₃','O₂','KMnO₄','Cr₂O₃','NEW','11-382','11-304','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130391q.png'),
(130392,'chemistry','The catalyst for the reaction given in diagram?(n1330)','AlCl₃/HCl','n-Alkanes on heating in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride and hydrogen chloride gas isomerise to branched chain alkanes.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-382, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','Na/HCl','Na/ Dry ether','AlCl₃/HCl','Fe/HCl','NEW','11-382','11-304','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130392q.png'),
(130393,'chemistry','______ products are generally not reported in organic reactions','Minor','Minor products are generally not reported in organic reactions.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-382, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','fillup','Mi,nor,Ma,jor',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-382','11-304',NULL),
(130394,'chemistry','What could be the possible catalyst for the reaction shown in the diagram?(n1331)','Cr₂O₃','n-Alkanes having six or more carbon atoms on heating to 773K at 10-20 atmospheric pressure in the presence of oxides of vanadium, molybdenum or chromium supported over alumina get dehydrogenated and cyclised to benzene and its homologues. \n\nThis reaction is known as aromatization or reforming.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-382, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','Cr₂O₇','O₂','KMnO₄','Cr₂O₃','NEW','11-382','11-304','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130394q.png'),
(130395,'chemistry','Alkanes with how many carbon undergo aromatisation on dehydrogenation?','6','n-Alkanes having six or more carbon atoms on heating to 773K at 10-20 atmospheric pressure in the presence of oxides of vanadium, molybdenum or chromium supported over alumina get dehydrogenated and cyclised to benzene and its homologues. \n\nThis reaction is known as aromatization or reforming.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-382, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','3','4','5','6','NEW','11-382','11-304',NULL),
(130396,'chemistry','What oxides are used of dehydrogenation of higher n-alkanes to undergo aromatisation?','All of the above','n-Alkanes having six or more carbon atoms on heating to 773K at 10-20 atmospheric pressure in the presence of oxides of vanadium, molybdenum or chromium supported over alumina get dehydrogenated and cyclised to benzene and its homologues. \n\nThis reaction is known as aromatization or reforming.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-382, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Chromium','Vanadium','Molybdenum','All of the above','NEW','11-382','11-304',NULL),
(130397,'chemistry','Which alkane do you suggest for preparation of toluene ?','n-heptane','Toluene (C₇H₈) is methyl derivative of benzene which can be synthesized using n-heptane.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-382, Paragraph - 5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'2','mcq','sec-hexane','n-heptane','n-hexane','Not possible','NEW','11-382','11-304',NULL),
(130398,'chemistry','Methane reacts with steam at 1273 K in the presence of nickel catalyst to form carbon _______ and dihydrogen.','Monoxide','Methane reacts with steam at 1273 K in the presence of nickel catalyst to form carbon monoxide and dihydrogen. \n\nThis method is used for industrial preparation of dihydrogen gas.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-382, Paragraph - 6)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'2','mcq','Black','Monoxide','Dioxide','Solid','NEW','11-382','11-304',NULL),
(130399,'chemistry','The catalyst for the reaction of methane with steam at 1273K is?','Ni','Methane reacts with steam at 1273 K in the presence of nickel catalyst to form carbon monoxide and dihydrogen. \n\nThis method is used for industrial preparation of dihydrogen gas.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-382, Paragraph - 6)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','Fe/HCl','Na/ Dry ether','AlCl₃/HCl','Ni','NEW','11-382','11-304',NULL),
(130521,'chemistry','Select the correct structure according to the IUPAC name 2-n-Propylpent-1-ene, refer diagram?(n1341)','c','For nomenclature of alkenes in IUPAC system, the longest chain of carbon atoms containing the double bond is selected. \n\nNumbering of the chain is done from the end which is nearer to the double bond. \n\nThe suffix ‘ene’ replaces ‘ane’ of alkanes.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-385, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-385','11-307','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130521q.png'),
(130522,'chemistry','Select the correct structure according to the IUPAC name 1,3,5,7 Octatetraene: diagram(n1341)','b','For nomenclature of alkenes in IUPAC system, the longest chain of carbon atoms containing the double bond is selected. \n\nNumbering of the chain is done from the end which is nearer to the double bond. \n\nThe suffix ‘ene’ replaces ‘ane’ of alkanes.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-385, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-385','11-307','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130522q.png'),
(130523,'chemistry','Calculate the number of sigma and pi bond in 4-Ethyl-2,6-dimethyl-dec-4-ene.','σ bonds : 41, π bond : 1','An aliphatic straight chain olefin\'s number of double bonds can be determined using the following formula:\n\nP=(2X−Y)/2+1, where X = no. of carbon atoms and Y = no. of hydrogen atoms.\n\nThe number of single bonds in aliphatic olefin straight chain can be determined by formula:\n\nA=3Y/2−2\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-385, Paragraph - example) diagram(ne1316)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','σ bonds : 33, π bonds : 2','σ bonds : 17, π bonds : 4','σ bonds : 23, π bond : 1','σ bonds : 41, π bond : 1','NEW','11-385','11-307',NULL),
(130524,'chemistry','Calculate the number of sigma and pi bond in  2,8-Dimethyl-3, 6-decadiene.','σ bonds : 33, π bonds : 2','An aliphatic straight chain olefin\'s number of double bonds can be determined using the following formula:\n\nP=(2X−Y)/2+1, where X = no. of carbon atoms and Y = no. of hydrogen atoms.\n\nThe number of single bonds in aliphatic olefin straight chain can be determined by formula:\n\nA=3Y/2−2\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-385, Paragraph - example) diagram(ne1313)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','σ bonds : 33, π bonds : 2','σ bonds : 17, π bonds : 4','σ bonds : 23, π bond : 1','σ bonds : 41, π bond : 1','NEW','11-385','11-307',NULL),
(130525,'chemistry','Calculate the number of sigma and pi bond in 1,3,5,7-octatetraene','σ bonds : 17, π bonds : 4','An aliphatic straight chain olefin\'s number of double bonds can be determined using the following formula:\n\nP=(2X−Y)/2+1, where X = no. of carbon atoms and Y = no. of hydrogen atoms.\n\nThe number of single bonds in aliphatic olefin straight chain can be determined by formula: \n\nA=3Y/2−2\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-385, Paragraph - example) diagram(ne1314)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','σ bonds : 33, π bonds : 2','σ bonds : 17, π bonds : 4','σ bonds : 23, π bond : 1','σ bonds : 41, π bond : 1','NEW','11-385','11-307',NULL),
(130526,'chemistry','Calculate the number of sigma and pi bond in 2-(n-propyl)pent-1-ene.','σ bonds : 23, π bond : 1','CH₂=C(CH₂-CH₂-CH₃)-CH₂-CH₂-CH₃\nThe single bonds (C-C and C-H) in the molecule are sigma bonds, while the double bond (C=C) is a pi bond. Therefore, we have:\n\n23 sigma bonds (10 C-C, 10 C-H, 2 C=C, and 1 C-H in the propyl group)\n1 pi bond (C=C)\nThus, there are 23 sigma bonds and 1 pi bond in 2-n-propylpent-1-en.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','σ bonds : 33, π bonds : 2','σ bonds : 17, π bonds : 4','σ bonds : 23, π bond : 1','σ bonds : 41, π bond : 1','NEW','11-385','11-307',NULL),
(130527,'chemistry','What isomerism/shown by alkenes','Both (a) and (b)','Alkenes show both structural isomerism and geometrical isomerism.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-385, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','mcq','Structural','Geometrical','Both (a) and (b)','Cis/trans','NEW','11-385','11-307',NULL),
(130528,'chemistry','But-1-ene and 2-methylprop-l-ene are the examples of','Chain isomers','These compounds have different carbon chains. \n\nHence, they are examples of chain isomers.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-386, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','mcq','Chain isomers','Position isomers','Functional isomers','Optical isomers','NEW','11-386','11-308',NULL),
(130529,'chemistry','Number of possible structural isomers of C₄H₈ is','3','Number of possible structural isomers of C₄H₈ is 3.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-385, Paragraph - last) diagram(ne1318)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','3','4','5','6','NEW','11-385','11-307',NULL),
(130530,'chemistry','Write possible no. of different structural isomers of alkenes corresponding to C₅H₁₀','5','Possible no. of different structural isomers of alkenes corresponding to C₅H₁₀ is 5.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-386, Paragraph - example) diagram(ne1317)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','4','5','6','7','NEW','11-386','11-308',NULL),
(130531,'chemistry','The type of isomerism shown in diagram is: diagram(n1342)','Geometrical isomers','In geometrical isomerism, doubly bonded carbon atoms have to satisfy the remaining two valences by joining with two atoms or groups. \n\nIf the two atoms or groups attached to each carbon atom are different, they can be represented by YX C = C XY like structure. \n\nYX C = C XY can be represented in space in the two ways shown in diagram above.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-386, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','Chain isomers','Position isomers','Geometrical isomers','Positional isomers','NEW','11-386','11-308','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130531q.png'),
(130532,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds will exhibit geometrical isomerism?','1-phenyl-2-butene','The compound 1-phenyl-1,2-butene exhibits geometrical isomerism. \n\nAs there is a common group attached to both the carbon forming double bond & other groups are different.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-386, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'2','mcq','1-phenyl-2-butene','2-phenyl-l-butene','3-phenyl-1-butene','1,1-diphenyl-1-propene','NEW','11-386','11-308',NULL),
(130533,'chemistry','Which of the following has higher dipole moment; cis-2-butene or trans-2-butene?','cis-2-butene','Cis form of alkene is found to be more polar than the trans form. \n\nFor example, dipole moment of cis-but-2-ene is 0.33 Debye, whereas, dipole moment of the trans form is almost zero or it can be said that trans-but-2-ene is non-polar.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-386, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'2','mcq','cis-2-butene','trans-2-butene','Both have same dipole moment','None of the above','NEW','11-386','11-308',NULL),
(130534,'chemistry','In the case of solids, it is observed that the trans isomer has ______ melting point than the cis form.','higher','Because in trans form there is symmetry & close packing takes place in solids.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-387, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'2','fillup','hi,gh,er,lo,wer',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-387','11-309',NULL),
(130535,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds will show cis-trans isomerism: diagram(n1343)','iii and iv','(iii) and (iv). In structures (i) and (ii), two identical groups are attached to one of the doubly bonded carbon atom. \n\nIn order to exhibit cis and trans configurations, the doubly bonded carbon atom should have two different groups attached.  \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-387, Paragraph - example)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','i and ii','i and iii','ii and iv','iii and iv','NEW','11-387','11-309','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130535q.png'),
(130536,'chemistry','Predict the catalyst of the reaction given in diagram(n1344)','Pd/C','Alkynes on partial reduction with calculated amount of dihydrogen in the presence of palladised charcoal partially deactivated with poisons like sulphur compounds or quinoline give alkenes.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-387, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','AlCl₃','Pd/C','Ni','Zn, H⁺','NEW','11-387','11-309','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130536q.png'),
(130537,'chemistry','Palladised charcoal partially deactivated with?','Both (a) and (b)','Palladised charcoal is partially deactivated with poisons like sulphur compounds or quinoline give alkenes.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-387, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','mcq','Sulphur compounds','Quinoline','Both (a) and (b)','CO','NEW','11-387','11-309',NULL),
(130538,'chemistry','Partially deactivated palladised charcoal is known as _________catalyst','Lindlar','Partially deactivated palladised charcoal is known as Lindlar’s catalyst.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-387, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','fillup','Li,nd,lar,Ra,ney',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-387','11-309',NULL),
(130539,'chemistry','Predict the catalyst of the given reaction. diagram(n1345)','Na/liq.NH₃','Alkenes reduced by Pd/C are having cis geometry. \n\nHowever, alkynes on reduction with sodium in liquid ammonia form trans alkenes.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-387, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','Zn, H⁺','Pd/C','Ni','Na/liq.NH₃','NEW','11-387','11-309','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130539q.png'),
(130540,'chemistry','Predict the catalyst of the given reaction. diagram(n1346)','Pd/C','Alkenes reduced by Pd/C are having cis geometry. \n\nHowever, alkynes on reduction with sodium in liquid ammonia form trans alkenes.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-387, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','Zn, H⁺','Pd/C','Ni','Na/liq.NH₃','NEW','11-387','11-309','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130540q.png'),
(130541,'chemistry','Predict the catalyst of the given reaction: diagram(n1347)','alc KOH','Alkyl halides (R-X) on heating with alcoholic potash (potassium hydroxide dissolved in alcohol) eliminate one molecule of halogen acid to form alkenes. \n\nThis reaction is known as dehydrohalogenation.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-388, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','Zn, HCl','alc KOH','CCl₄','conc H₂SO₄','NEW','11-388','11-310','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130541q.png'),
(130542,'chemistry','Removal of halogen acid is known as ________','dehydrohalogenation','Alkyl halides (R-X) on heating with alcoholic potash (potassium hydroxide dissolved in alcohol) eliminate one molecule of halogen acid to form alkenes. \n\nThis reaction is known as dehydrohalogenation ( removal of halogen acid). \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-388, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','fillup','de,hy,dr,oh,al,og,en,at,ion,br,om',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-388','11-310',NULL),
(130543,'chemistry','Dehydrohalogenation reaction is an example of','β-elimination','This is example of β-elimination reaction, since hydrogen atom is eliminated from the β carbon atom (carbon atom next to the carbon to which halogen is attached).\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-388, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'2','mcq','a-elimination','β-elimination','a-substitution','β-substitution','NEW','11-388','11-310',NULL),
(130544,'chemistry','The rate of reaction of dehydrohalogenation is determined by','Both (a) and (b)','Nature of halogen atom and the alkyl group determine rate of the reaction. \n\nIt is observed that for halogens, the rate is: iodine > bromine > chlorine, while for alkyl groups it is: tert > secondary > primary.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-388, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','mcq','Nature of halogen atom','Alkyl group','Both (a) and (b)','None of these','NEW','11-388','11-310',NULL),
(130545,'chemistry','The rate of reaction of halogen towards β-elimination reaction is','iodine > bromine > chlorine','Nature of halogen atom and the alkyl group determine rate of the reaction. \n\nIt is observed that for halogens, the rate is: iodine > bromine > chlorine, while for alkyl groups it is: tert > secondary > primary.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-388, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'2','mcq','chlorine > bromine > iodine','iodine > bromine > chlorine','bromine > iodine > chlorine','chlorine > iodine > bromine','NEW','11-388','11-310',NULL),
(130546,'chemistry','The rate of reaction of alkyl group towards β-elimination reaction is','tert > secondary > primary','Nature of halogen atom and the alkyl group determine rate of the reaction. \n\nIt is observed that for halogens, the rate is: iodine > bromine > chlorine, while for alkyl groups it is: tert > secondary > primary.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-388, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'2','mcq','primary>secondary>tert','secondary>primary>tert','tert > secondary > primary','all react with same rate','NEW','11-388','11-310',NULL),
(130547,'chemistry','Dihalides in which two halogen atoms are attached to two adjacent carbon atoms are known as _______ dihalides','vicinal','Dihalides in which two halogen atoms are attached to two adjacent carbon atoms are known as vicinal dihalides.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-388, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','fillup','vi,ci,nal,ge,m-',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-388','11-310',NULL),
(130548,'chemistry','Identify the omitted product in reaction shown in the diagram?(n1348)','ZnBr₂','Vicinal dihalides on treatment with zinc metal lose a molecule of ZnX₂ to form an alkene. \n\nThis reaction is known as dehalogenation.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-388, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','2ZnBr','2HBr','Br₂','ZnBr₂','NEW','11-388','11-310','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130548q.png'),
(130549,'chemistry','Predict the catalyst of the reaction given in diagram?(n1349)','conc H₂SO₄','Alcohols on heating with concentrated sulphuric acid form alkenes with the elimination of one water molecule. \n\nThis reaction is called acidic dehydration.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-388, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','Zn, H⁺','alc KOH','CCl₄','conc H₂SO₄','NEW','11-388','11-310','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130549q.png'),
(130550,'chemistry','Alcohols on heating with conc H₂SO₄  form alkenes with elimination of one water molecule. This reaction is also known as','Both (a) and (c)','Since a water molecule is eliminated from the alcohol molecule in the presence of an acid, this reaction is known as acidic dehydration of alcohols. \n\nThis reaction is also the example of β-elimination reaction since –OH group takes out one hydrogen atom from the β-carbon atom.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-388, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'2','mcq','β-elimination','Basic dehydration of alcohols','Acidic dehydration of alcohols','Both (a) and (c)','NEW','11-388','11-310',NULL),
(130551,'chemistry','Alkenes as a class resemble alkanes in physical properties, except','Both (a) and (b)','Alkenes as a class resemble alkanes in physical properties, except in types of isomerism and difference in polar nature.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-388, Paragraph - 5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','mcq','Types of Isomerism','Polar nature','Melting point order','Both (a) and (b)','NEW','11-388','11-310',NULL),
(130552,'chemistry','Liquid alkenes are with carbon chain of','4-17','The first three members are gases, the next fourteen are liquids and the higher ones are solids.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-388, Paragraph - 5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','mcq','1-14','4-17','4-14','1-17','NEW','11-388','11-310',NULL),
(130553,'chemistry','All other alkenes are colourless and odourless except','Ethene','Ethene is a colourless gas with a faint sweet smell. \n\nAll other alkenes are colourless and odourless.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-388, Paragraph - 5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'2','mcq','Ethene','Propene','Decene','Undecene','NEW','11-388','11-310',NULL),
(130554,'chemistry','The boiling point of alkenes','Decrease with branching','Like alkanes, straight chain alkenes have higher boiling point than isomeric branched chain compounds. \n\nThe branching lowers the boiling point of the isomer due to a decrease in interaction area.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-388, Paragraph - 5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'2','mcq','Increase with branching','Decrease with branching','Remains same with branching','Increase with decrease in carbon chain','NEW','11-388','11-310',NULL),
(130555,'chemistry','Which of the following is the correct reason for the addition reactions on alkenes?','Loosely held pi-electrons','Alkenes are the rich source of loosely held pi (π) electrons, due to which they show addition reactions in which the electrophiles add on to the carbon-carbon double bond to form the addition products.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-388, Paragraph - 6)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'2','mcq','Loosely held pi-electrons','Tightly held pi-electrons','Loosely held sigma-electrons','Tightly held sigma-electrons','NEW','11-388','11-310',NULL),
(130556,'chemistry','The structures of alkenes which on hydrogenation give 2-methyl butane, for options refer diagram?(n1381)','a','Alkenes add up one molecule of dihydrogen gas in the presence of finely divided nickel, palladium or platinum to form alkanes.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-388, Paragraph - 7)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-388','11-310','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130556q.png'),
(130557,'chemistry','Halogens which add up to alkene to form vicinal dihalides','Both (a) and (b)','Halogens like bromine or chlorine add up to alkene to form vicinal dihalides. \n\nHowever, iodine does show addition reaction under normal condition.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-388, Paragraph - 8)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','mcq','Chlorine','Bromine','Iodine','Both (a) and (b)','NEW','11-388','11-310',NULL),
(130558,'chemistry','Which halogen is used for test of unsaturation?','Bromine','The reddish orange colour of bromine solution in carbon tetrachloride is discharged when bromine adds up to an unsaturation site. \n\nThis reaction is used as a test for unsaturation.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-389, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'2','mcq','Fluorine','chlorine','Bromine','Iodine','NEW','11-389','11-311',NULL),
(130559,'chemistry','Addition of halogens to alkenes is an example of','Electrophilic addition reaction','Addition of halogens to alkenes is an example of electrophilic addition reaction involving cyclic halonium ion formation.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-389, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'2','mcq','Electrophilic addition reaction','Electrophilic substitution reaction','Nucleophilic addition reaction','nucleophilic substitution reaction','NEW','11-389','11-311',NULL),
(130560,'chemistry','Predict the catalyst of the reaction given in diagram?(n1350)','CCl₄','The reddish orange colour of bromine solution in carbon tetrachloride is discharged when bromine adds up to an unsaturation site. \n\nThis reaction is used as a test for unsaturation.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-389, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','Zn, H⁺','alc KOH','CCl₄','AlCl₃','NEW','11-389','11-311','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130560q.png'),
(130561,'chemistry','Predict the catalyst of the reaction given in diagram?(n1351)','No catalyst required','Chlorine does not require any kind of catalyst to form vicinal dihalides.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-389, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','Cl₂','AlCl₃','CCl₄','No catalyst required','NEW','11-389','11-311','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130561q.png'),
(130562,'chemistry','The correct order of reactivity of the hydrogen halides with alkenes is','HI>HBr>HCl','Hydrogen halides (HCl, HBr,HI) add up to alkenes to form alkyl halides. \n\nThe order of reactivity of the hydrogen halides is HI > HBr > HCl.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-389, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','HI>HBr>HCl','HCl>HBr>HI','HI > HCl > HBr','HBr>HCl>HI','NEW','11-389','11-311',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(130563,'chemistry','Addition of hydrogen halides is an example of','Electrophilic addition reaction','Like addition of halogens to alkenes, addition of hydrogen halides is also an example of electrophilic addition reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-389, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','mcq','Electrophilic addition reaction','Electrophilic substitution reaction','Nucleophilic addition reaction','Nucleophilic substitution reaction','NEW','11-389','11-311',NULL),
(130564,'chemistry','The rule which states that negative part of the addendum gets attached to that carbon atom which possesses less number of hydrogen atoms is called','Markovnikov’s rule','Markovnikov rule states that negative part of the addendum (adding molecule) gets attached to that carbon atom which possesses lesser number of hydrogen atoms. \n\nThus according to this rule, product I i.e., 2-bromopropane is expected.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-389, Paragraph - 5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','mcq','Saytzeff rule','Hofmann rule','Markovnikov’s rule','Anti-Markovnikov’s addition','NEW','11-389','11-311',NULL),
(130565,'chemistry','What will be the product of the following reaction given in diagram?(n1352)','b','Markovnikov rule states that negative part of the addendum (adding molecule) gets attached to that carbon atom which possesses lesser number of hydrogen atoms. \n\nThus according to this rule, product I i.e., 2-bromopropane is expected.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-389, Paragraph - 5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-389','11-311','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130565q.png'),
(130566,'chemistry','Addition of which hydrogen halide is based on anti-markovnikov\'s rule','HBr','In the presence of peroxide, addition of HBr to unsymmetrical alkenes like propene takes place contrary to the Markovnikov rule. \n\nThis happens only with HBr but not with HCl and Hl. \n\nThis addition reaction was observed by M.S. Kharash and F.R. Mayo in 1933 at the University of Chicago. \n\nThis reaction is known as peroxide or Kharash effect or addition reaction anti to Markovnikov rule.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-390, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'2','mcq','HF','HCl','HBr','HI','NEW','11-390','11-312',NULL),
(130567,'chemistry','The addition of HBr to 1-butene gives a mixture of products A, B and C shown in diagram?(n1380)','A and B as major and C as minor products','The alkene is unsymmetrical, hence will follow Markovnikov’s rule to give major product that is A and C is minor.\n\nSince, A contains a chiral carbon, it exists in two enantiomeric forms (A and B) which are mirror images of each other.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-389, Paragraph - 5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','A and B as major and C as minor products','B as major, A and C as minor products','B as minor, A and C as major products','A and B as minor and C as major products','NEW','11-389','11-311','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130567q.png'),
(130568,'chemistry','In the presence of peroxide, addition of HBr to unsymmetrical alkenes like propene takes place contrary to the Markovnikov rule is known as______','Kharash-effect','In the presence of peroxide, addition of HBr to unsymmetrical alkenes like propene takes place contrary to the Markovnikov rule. \n\nThis happens only with HBr but not with HCl and Hl. \n\nThis addition reaction was observed by M.S. Kharash and F.R. Mayo in 1933 at the University of Chicago. \n\nThis reaction is known as peroxide or Kharash effect or addition reaction anti to Markovnikov rule.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-390, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','fillup','Kh,ar,as,h-,ef,fe,ct,Ma,yo',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-390','11-312',NULL),
(130569,'chemistry','What will be the product of the following reaction given in diagram?(n1353)','a','In the presence of peroxide, addition of HBr to unsymmetrical alkenes like propene takes place contrary to the Markovnikov rule. \n\nThis happens only with HBr but not with HCl and Hl. \n\nThis addition reaction was observed by M.S. Kharash and F.R. Mayo in 1933 at the University of Chicago. \n\nThis reaction is known as peroxide or Kharash effect or addition reaction anti to Markovnikov rule.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-390, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-390','11-312','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130569q.png'),
(130570,'chemistry','Write IUPAC names of the products obtained by addition reactions of HBr to hex-1-ene in the absence of peroxide','2-bromohexane','In absence of peroxide, the HBr is added according the Markovnikov rule.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-390, Paragraph - example) diagram(ne1319)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','1-bromohexane','2-bromohexane','3-bromohexane','3-bromo,3-methyl-pentane','NEW','11-390','11-312',NULL),
(130571,'chemistry','Write IUPAC names of the products obtained by addition reactions of HBr to hex-1-ene in the presence of peroxide.','1-bromohexane','In presence of peroxide, the HBr is added according the anti-Markovnikov rule.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-390, Paragraph - example) diagram(ne1320)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','1-bromohexane','2-bromohexane','3-bromohexane','3-bromo,3-methyl-pentane','NEW','11-390','11-312',NULL),
(130572,'chemistry','The product obtained in the following reaction shown in diagram?(n1354)','c','Cold concentrated sulphuric acid adds to alkenes in accordance with Markovnikov rule to form alkyl hydrogen sulphate by the electrophilic addition reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-390, Paragraph - last)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-390','11-312','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130572q.png'),
(130573,'chemistry','Addition of water in presence of conc H₂SO₄ (few drops) to 2-methyl propene produces alcohols in accordance with','Markovnikov’s rule','In the presence of a few drops of concentrated sulphuric acid alkenes react with water to form alcohols, in accordance with the Markovnikov rule.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-391, Paragraph -1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','mcq','Anti-Markovnikov’s addition','Markovnikov’s rule','Saytzeff\'s rule','Hofmann rule','NEW','11-391','11-313',NULL),
(130574,'chemistry','Predict the catalyst of the reaction given in diagram?(n1355)','H₂O, H⁺','In the presence of a few drops of concentrated sulphuric acid alkenes react with water to form alcohols, in accordance with the Markovnikov rule.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-391, Paragraph -1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','H₂O, H⁺','alc KOH','CCl₄','conc H₂SO₄','NEW','11-391','11-313','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130574q.png'),
(130575,'chemistry','Aqueous solution of potassium permanganate is known as _______reagent','Baeyer\'s','Alkenes on reaction with cold, dilute, aqueous solution of potassium permanganate (Baeyer’s reagent) produce vicinal glycols. \n\nDecolorization of KMnO₄ solution is used as a test for unsaturation.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-391, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','fillup','Ba,ey,er\'s,Li,nd,lar',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-391','11-313',NULL),
(130576,'chemistry','Predict the catalyst of the reaction given in diagram?(n1356)','dil. KMnO₄','Alkenes on reaction with cold, dilute, aqueous solution of potassium permanganate (Baeyer’s reagent) produce vicinal glycols. \n\nDecolorization of KMnO₄ solution is used as a test for unsaturation.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-391, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','H₂O, H⁺','dil. KMnO₄','KMnO₄/H⁺','O₃','NEW','11-391','11-313','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130576q.png'),
(130577,'chemistry','Predict the catalyst of the reaction given in diagram?(n1357)','KMnO₄/H⁺','Acidic potassium permanganate or acidic potassium dichromate oxidises alkenes to ketones and/or acids depending upon the nature of the alkene and the experimental conditions.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-391, Paragraph -3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','H₂O, H⁺','dil. KMnO₄','KMnO₄/H⁺','O₃','NEW','11-391','11-313','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130577q.png'),
(130578,'chemistry','Which of the following is used for test of unsaturation?','All of the above','Alkenes on reaction with cold, dilute, aqueous solution of potassium permanganate (Baeyer’s reagent) produce vicinal glycols. \n\nDecolorization of KMnO₄ solution is used as a test for unsaturation.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-391, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'2','mcq','Ozone molecule','Potassium permanganate solution','Bromine water test','All of the above','NEW','11-391','11-313',NULL),
(130579,'chemistry','Consider the reaction given in the diagram?(n1358)','Propene and ethanal','Ozonolysis of alkenes involves the addition of ozone molecule to alkene to form ozonide, and then cleavage of the ozonide by Zn-H₂O to smaller molecules. \n\nThis reaction is highly useful in detecting the position of the double bond in alkenes or other unsaturated compounds.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-391, Paragraph -4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','Propene and methanal','Propane and ethanal','Propene and ethanol','Propene and ethanal','NEW','11-391','11-313','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130579q.png'),
(130580,'chemistry','The IUPAC names of the products obtained by the ozonolysis of 1-phenylbut-1-ene are','Propanal and benzaldehyde','Ozonolysis of alkenes involves the addition of ozone molecule to alkene to form ozonide, and then cleavage of the ozonide by Zn-H₂O to smaller molecules. \n\nThis reaction is highly useful in detecting the position of the double bond in alkenes or other unsaturated compounds.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-391, Paragraph -4) diagram(ne1332)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','Propanal and benzaldehyde','Propanoic acid and benzoic acid','Propanal and benzoic acid','Propanoic acid and benzaldehyde','NEW','11-391','11-313',NULL),
(130581,'chemistry','An alkene A contains three C—C, eight C—H sigma-bonds and one C—C pi-bond. A on ozonolysis gives two moles of an aldehyde of molar mass 44 u. Write IUPAC name of A.','but-2-ene','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-391, Paragraph -4) diagram(ne1333)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','prop-1-ene','but-2-ene','but-1-ene','prop-2-ene','NEW','11-391','11-313',NULL),
(130582,'chemistry','Which of the following is/are used for test of unsaturation?','All of the above','All among the options are used as test for unsaturation.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-391, Paragraph -4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'2','mcq','Bromine water test','Baeyer\'s test','Ozonolysis','All of the above','NEW','11-391','11-313',NULL),
(213315,'chemistry','Fluorine forms two oxides. However, among those, only ______ is thermally stable at 298 K.','OF₂','Fluorine forms two oxides. However, among those, only OF₂ is thermally stable at 298 K. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 201 Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','O₂F₂','OF₂','OF','O₂F','NEW','12-201',NULL,NULL),
(130583,'chemistry','Polythene is obtained by the combination of large number of ethene molecules at','high temperature, high pressure and presence of catalyst','Polythene is obtained by the combination of large number of ethene molecules at high temperature, high pressure and in the presence of a catalyst. \n\nThe large molecules thus obtained are called polymers. \n\nThis reaction is known as polymerisation.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-391, Paragraph -5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'2','mcq','high temperature, low pressure and presence of a catalyst','high temperature and low volume and presence of a catalyst','high temperature, high pressure and presence of catalyst',NULL,'NEW','11-391','11-313',NULL),
(130584,'chemistry','Which of the following is an example of polythene,refer diagram?(n1359)','c','The simple compounds from which polymers are formed are called monomers. \n\nThe second option is the monomer that is used to form polythene.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-392, Paragraph -1) diagram(ne1321)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-392','11-314','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130584q.png'),
(130720,'chemistry','The type of reaction shown by arenes','All of these','Arenes are characterised by electrophilic substitution reactions. \n\nHowever, under special conditions they can also undergo addition and oxidation reactions.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'1','mcq','Substitution','Addition','Oxidation','All of these','NEW','11-400','11-322',NULL),
(130721,'chemistry','Consider the reaction given below in diagram?(n1372)','a','A nitro group is introduced into benzene ring when benzene is heated with a mixture of concentrated nitric acid and concentrated sulphuric acid (nitrating mixture).\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-400','11-322','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130721q.png'),
(130722,'chemistry','Predict the omitted catalyst for the reaction given in the diagram?(n1373)','Any one of these','Arenes react with halogens in the presence of a Lewis acid like anhydrous FeCl₃, FeBr₃ or AlCl₃ to yield haloarenes.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'3','mcq','AlCl₃','FeCl₃','FeBr₃','Any one of these','NEW','11-400','11-322','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130722q.png'),
(130723,'chemistry','Predict the omitted product in the diagram?(n1374)','C₆H₅SO₃H','The replacement of a hydrogen atom by a sulphonic acid group in a ring is called sulphonation. \n\nIt is carried out by heating benzene with fuming sulphuric acid (oleum).\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -6)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'3','mcq','C₆H₅SO₃H','C₆H₅SO₂','C₆H₅OH','C₆H₅CHO','NEW','11-400','11-322','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130723q.png'),
(130724,'chemistry','Predict the omitted catalyst for the reaction given in the diagram?(n1375)','Anhyd. AlCl₃','When benzene is treated with an alkyl halide in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride, alkylbenene is formed.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -7)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'3','mcq','Anhyd. AlCl₃','FeCl₃','FeBr₃','HCl','NEW','11-400','11-322','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130724q.png'),
(130725,'chemistry','Acetophenone (C₆H₅COCH₃ ) is prepared from reaction of benzene (C₆H₆) with A in the presence of anhyd. AlCl₃.Here, A can be','Both (a) and (b)','The reaction of benzene with an acyl halide or acid anhydride in the presence of Lewis acids (AlCl3) yields acyl benzene.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-401, Paragraph -1) diagram(ne1328)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'3','mcq','Acetylchloride','Acetic anhydride','Ethanal','Both (a) and (b)','NEW','11-401','11-323',NULL),
(130726,'chemistry','Total number of Cl₂ atoms are required for the reaction given in diagram?(n1376)','6','Benzene on treatment with excess of chlorine in the presence of anhydrous AlCl₃ can be chlorinated to hexachlorobenzene (C₆Cl₆).\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-401, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'3','mcq','3','4','5','6','NEW','11-401','11-323','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130726q.png'),
(130727,'chemistry','During nitration of benzene, the attacking electrophile is','N0₂⁺','In the case of nitration, the electrophile, nitronium ion, N0₂⁺ is produced by transfer of a proton (from sulphuric acid) to nitric acid.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-401, Paragraph -5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'2','mcq','NO₃⁺','NO₂⁻','N0₂⁺','HN0₃','NEW','11-401','11-323',NULL),
(130728,'chemistry','In case of generation of nitronium ion sulphuric acid and nitric acid respectively serve as','acid,base','It is interesting to note that in the process of generation of nitronium ion, sulphuric acid serves as an acid and nitric acid as a base.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-401, Paragraph -6)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'2','mcq','base,acid','acid,base','acid,acid','acid,neutral','NEW','11-401','11-323',NULL),
(130729,'chemistry','Hybridisation of carbon atom in arenium ion','one sp³','Attack of electrophile results in the formation of σ-complex or arenium ion in which one of the carbon is sp³ hybridised.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-401, Paragraph -7)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'2','mcq','all sp²','two sp²','one sp³','two sp³','NEW','11-401','11-323',NULL),
(130730,'chemistry','What is the last step of Mechanism of electrophilic substitution reactions','Removal of proton from the carbocation intermediate','Electrophilic substitution reaction proceeds via 3 steps and the last step is removal of proton.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-402, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'2','mcq','Generation of electrophile','Attack of electrophile','Formation of carbocation intermediate','Removal of proton from the carbocation intermediate','NEW','11-402','11-324',NULL),
(130731,'chemistry','[AlCl₄]⁻ is used to remove proton from arenium ion in the reaction of','Both (a) and (b)','To restore the aromatic character, σ -complex releases proton from sp³ hybridised carbon on attack by [AlCl₄]⁻ (in case of halogenation, alkylation and acylation).\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-402, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'2','mcq','halogenation','acylation','nitration','Both (a) and (b)','NEW','11-402','11-324',NULL),
(130732,'chemistry','Total number of Cl₂ atoms are required for the reaction given in diagram?(n1377)','3','Under ultra-violet light, three chlorine molecules add to benzene to produce benzene hexachloride, C6H6Cl6 which is also called gammaxane.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-402, Paragraph -4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'3','mcq','3','4','5','6','NEW','11-402','11-324','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130732q.png'),
(130733,'chemistry','When benzene is heated in air, it burns with sooty flame producing X and Y, as shown in the given diagram?(n1378)','b','When heated in air, benzene burns with sooty flame producing CO2 and H2O.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-402, Paragraph -5) diagram(ne1329)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-402','11-324','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130733q.png'),
(130734,'chemistry','Phenol is o, p-directing in electrophilic substitution reaction. The directing influence is explained by','OH exerts +M effect','Phenol is resonance hybrid of following structures shown in.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-402, Paragraph -7) diagram(ne1330)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'3','mcq','Ph exerts +M effect','OH exerts -I effect','OH exerts +M effect','O exerts -I effect','NEW','11-402','11-324',NULL),
(130735,'chemistry','Chlorobenzene is o, p-directing in electrophilic substitution reaction. The directing influence is explained by','Cl exerts +M effect','In the case of aryl halides, halogens are moderately deactivating. \n\nBecause of their strong – I effect, overall electron density on benzene ring decreases. It makes further substitution difficult. \n\nHowever, due to resonance the electron density on o– and p– positions is greater than that at the m-position. \n\nHence, they are also o– and p – directing groups.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-403, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'3','mcq','Ph exerts +M effect','Cl exerts +I effect','Cl exerts +M effect','Cl exerts strong -I effect','NEW','11-403','11-325',NULL),
(130736,'chemistry','Which of the following deactivates the benzene ring towards electrophilic substitution and is o- & p- directing?','—Cl','Examples of activating and o-,p- directing groups are –NH₂, –NHR, –NHCOCH₃, –OCH₃, –CH₃, etc.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-403, Paragraph -1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'1','mcq','—Cl','— CHO','–NHR','All of these','NEW','11-403','11-325',NULL),
(130737,'chemistry','Which of the following set of functional groups is m-directing?','—NO₂, —CHO, —SO₃H, — COR','The groups which direct the incoming group to meta position are called meta directing groups. \n\nSome examples of meta directing groups are –NO₂, –CN, –CHO, –COR, –COOH, –COOR, –SO₃H, etc.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-403, Paragraph -3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'2','mcq','—NO₂ , —NH₂ ,—COOH, — COOR','—NO₂, —CHO, —SO₃H, — COR','—CN, —CHO,—NHCOCH₃ ,—COOR','— CN, —NH₂ ,—NHR, —OCH₃','NEW','11-403','11-325',NULL),
(130738,'chemistry','In nitrobenzene, the X attacks on comparatively electron rich meta-position resulting in Y substitution. Here, X and Y refer to','electrophile, meta','Nitro group reduces the electron density in the benzene ring due to its strong–I effect. \n\nNitrobenzene is a resonance hybrid of the following structures given in \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-403, Paragraph -4) diagram(ne1331)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'3','mcq','electrophile, meta','nucleophile, meta','deactivating group, para','activating group, meta','NEW','11-403','11-325',NULL),
(130739,'chemistry','Arrange the following set of compounds in order of their decreasing relative reactivity with an electrophile, E+: \n\n-chlorobenzene, \n\n-2,4-dinitrochlorobenzene, \n\n-p-nitrochlorobenzene','chlorobenzene > p-nitrochlorobenzene > 2, 4-dinitro chlorobenzene','Presence of electron releasing group (or activating group) increases the electron density in benzene nucleus. \n\nTherefore, electrophile will attack benzene nucleus easily. \n\nBut the presence of electron withdrawing group like —NO2 decreases the electron density in benzene ring. \n\nTherefore, electrophile will attack benzene nucleus with difficulty.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-403, Paragraph -4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'3','mcq','chlorobenzene > 2, 4-dinitrochlorobenzene > p-nitrochlorobenzene','chlorobenzene > p-nitrochlorobenzene > 2, 4-dinitro chlorobenzene','p-nitrochlorobenzene > chlorobenzene > 2, 4-dinitro chlorobenzene','2, 4-dinitrochlorobenzene > p-nitrochlorobenzene > chlorobenzene','NEW','11-403','11-325',NULL),
(130740,'chemistry','Which is not a carcinogen among the following structures shown in diagram?(n1379)','All are carcinogens','Benzene and polynuclear hydrocarbons containing more than two benzene rings fused together are toxic and said to possess cancer producing (carcinogenic) property.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-403, Paragraph -6)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','All are carcinogens','NEW','11-403','11-325','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130740q.png'),
(130822,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds will exhibit cis-trans (geometrical) isomerism?','2-Butene','Cis-trans isomerism is exhibited by compounds having C=C, C N and N=N groups, due to restricted rotation around the double bond. \n\nAmong the given options, only 2-butene exhibits geometrical isomerism.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-386, Paragraph - 2)\n(NEET 2009)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Butanol','2-Butyne','2-Buteno','2-Butene','NEW','11-386','11-308',NULL),
(130823,'chemistry','Guess the product A in the reaction shown in diagram?(n1392)','d','The reaction is expected to follow the Markovnikov\'s rule. \n\nHowever, the unexpected outcome may be explained by a carbocation rearrangement from the initial 2º-carbocation to a 3º-carbocation by a 1,2-shift of a hydrogen atom. \n\nThis shifting will give (d) as the major product,\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-389, Paragraph - 5) \n(NEET 2008) diagram(ne1340)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-389','11-311','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130823q.png'),
(130824,'chemistry','Which of the compound with molecular formula C₅H₁₀ yields acetone on ozonolysis?','2-methyl-2-butene','Upon ozonolysis, 2-methyl-2-butene undergoes oxidative cleavage to form two molecules of acetone (CH₃)₂C=O\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-331, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','3-methyl-1-butene','cyclopentane','2-methyl-1-butene','2-methyl-2-butene','NEW','11-391','11-313',NULL),
(130825,'chemistry','Which one of the following alkenes will react faster with H₂ under catalytic hydrogenation conditions,refer diagram?(n1393)','a','The relative rates of hydrogenation decrease with the increase of steric hindrance. \n\nMost stable the alkene, slowly it undergoes hydrogenation to give the product. \n\nLeast substituted alkene is less stable and more reactive.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-388, Paragraph - 7)\n(NEET 2005)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-388','11-310','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130825q.png'),
(130826,'chemistry','Reaction of HBr with propene in the presence of peroxide gives','n-propyl bromide.','In the presence of peroxide, addition of HBr to unsymmetrical alkenes like propene takes place contrary to the Markovnikov rule. \n\nThis happens only with HBr but not with HCl and Hl. \n\nThis addition reaction was observed by M.S. Kharash and F.R. Mayo in 1933 at the University of Chicago. \n\nThis reaction is known as peroxide or Kharash effect or addition reaction anti to Markovnikov rule.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-390, Paragraph - 1)\n(NEET 2004)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','isopropyl bromide','3-bromopropane','allyl bromide','n-propyl bromide.','NEW','11-390','11-312',NULL),
(213316,'chemistry','The correct order of stability of oxides of halogens','I > Cl > Br','The correct order of stability of oxides of halogens is I > Cl > Br\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 201 Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','I > Cl > Br','I < Cl < Br','I > Br > Cl','I < Br < Cl','NEW','12-201',NULL,NULL),
(130827,'chemistry','The reaction with NaIO₄ in the presence of KMnO₄ of the compound shown in diagram?(n1394)','b','Acidic potassium permanganate or acidic potassium dichromate oxidises alkenes to ketones and/or acids depending upon the nature of the alkene and the experimental conditions.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-391, Paragraph -3)\n(NEET 2003)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-391','11-313','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130827q.png'),
(130828,'chemistry','Geometrical isomers differ in','spatial arrangement of atoms','Geometrical isomers are those isomers which possess the same molecular and structural formula but differ in the arrangement of atoms or groups in space due to hindered rotation around the double bonded atoms.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-386, Paragraph - 2)\n(NEET 2002)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','spatial arrangement of atoms','position of atoms','spatial arrangement of bonds','length of carbon chain','NEW','11-386','11-308',NULL),
(130829,'chemistry','In preparation of alkene from alcohol using Al₂O₃ which is the effective factor?','Temperature','Temperature is an effective factor because at different temperature it forms different products.\n\nAt lower temperature, intermolecular dehydration takes place between two molecules of alcohol and ether will be formed.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-388, Paragraph - 4)\n(NEET 2001)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Porosity of Al₂O₃','Temperature','Concentration','Surface area of Al₂O₃','NEW','11-388','11-310',NULL),
(130830,'chemistry','Which reagent converts propene to 1-propanol?','B₂H₆, H₂O₂, OH–','Propene adds to diborane giving an addition product. \n\nThe addition compound on oxidation gives 1-propanol. \n\nHere addition of water takes place according to anti-Markownikoff ’s rule.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-391, Paragraph -1)\n(NEET 2000)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','H₂O, H₂SO₄','B₂H₆, H₂O₂, OH–','Hg(OAc)₂, NaBH₄/H₂O','Aq. KOH','NEW','11-391','11-313',NULL),
(130831,'chemistry','Which is maximum stable?','trans-2-butene','This is most stable as the repulsion between two methyl groups is least.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-386, Paragraph - 2)\n(NEET 2000)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1-butene','cis-2-butene','trans-2-butene','All have same stability','NEW','11-386','11-308',NULL),
(130832,'chemistry','2-Butene shows geometrical isomerism due to','restricted rotation about double bond','Geometrical isomers are those isomers which possess the same molecular and structural formula but differ in the arrangement of atoms or groups in space due to hindered rotation around the double bonded atoms.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-386, Paragraph - 2)\n(NEET 2000)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','restricted rotation about double bond','free rotation about double bond','free rotation about single bond','chiral carbon','NEW','11-386','11-308',NULL),
(130833,'chemistry','2-Bromopentane is heated with potassium ethoxide in ethanol. The major product obtained is','trans-2-pentene','The compound undergoes β-elimination to give 2-pentene. \n\nThe most stable stereoisomer will be trans-2-pentene as the bulky compounds will be maximum distance apart. \n\n(NEET 1998)\ndiagram(ne1342)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','trans-2-pentene','1-pentene','2-ethoxy pentane','2-cis-pentene.','NEW','11-388','11-310',NULL),
(130834,'chemistry','M and R are in the reaction given in diagram?(n1395)','d','The M is formed simply by adding the hypochlorous acid across the double bond. \n\nThe Cl in the compound can then be replaced by reacting it with a base, in this case NaHCO3 (baking soda). \n\nThe OH will substitute the Cl giving a diol. \n(NEET 1997)\ndiagram(ne1343)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-388','11-310','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130834q.png'),
(130835,'chemistry','The reaction, CH₂=CH–CH₃ + HBr → CH₃CHBr–CH₃ is','electrophilic addition','Like addition of halogens to alkenes, addition of hydrogen halides is also an example of electrophilic addition reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-389, Paragraph - 2)\n(NEET 1996)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','fillup','ele,ct,ro,ph,il,ic, ad,di,ti,on,nu,cl,eo',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-389','11-390',NULL),
(130836,'chemistry','Which of the following has zero dipole moment?','trans-2-butene','Cis form of alkene is found to be more polar than the trans form. \n\nFor example, dipole moment of cis-but-2-ene is 0.33 Debye, whereas, dipole moment of the trans form is almost zero or it can be said that trans-but-2-ene is non-polar.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-386, Paragraph - 4)\n(NEET 1996)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1-butene','2-methyl-1-propene','trans-2-butene','trans-4-butene','NEW','11-386','11-308',NULL),
(130837,'chemistry','One of the following which does not observe the anti-Markownikoff ’s addition of HBr, is','but-2-ene','In case of but-2-ene both double bonded carbons are identical. \n\nTherefore, it does not observe the anti-Markownikoff ’s addition of HBr.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-390, Paragraph - 1)\n(NEET 1994)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','pent-2-ene','propene','but-2-ene','but-1-ene.','NEW','11-390','11-312',NULL),
(130838,'chemistry','Reduction of 2-butyne with sodium in liquid ammonia gives predominantly','trans-2-butene','However, alkynes on reduction with sodium in liquid ammonia form trans alkenes.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-387, Paragraph - 4)\n(NEET 1993)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','cis-2-butene','no reaction','trans-2-butene','n-butane.','NEW','11-387','11-309',NULL),
(130839,'chemistry','The restricted rotation about carbon carbon double bond in 2-butene is due to','sideways overlap of two p-orbitals.','Restricted rotation is due to sideways overlap of two p-orbitals.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-386, Paragraph - 3)\n(NEET 1993)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','sideways overlap of two p-orbitals.','overlap of two sp² -hybridized orbitals','overlap of one p and one sp² -hybridized orbitals','sideways overlap of three p-orbitals.','NEW','11-386','11-308',NULL),
(130840,'chemistry','Which one of the following can exhibit cis-trans isomerism?','ClCH=CHCl','Cis-trans isomerism is exhibited by compounds having C=C, C N and N=N groups, due to restricted rotation around the double bond. \n\nAmong the given options, only dichloroethene exhibits geometrical isomerism.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-386, Paragraph - 2)\n(NEET 1989)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','CH₃–CHCl–COOH','H–C=C–Cl','ClCH=CHCl','ClCH₂–CH₂Cl','NEW','11-386','11-308',NULL),
(130841,'chemistry','The number of sigma(s) bonds present in the product A of the reaction given in diagram?(n1396)','21','The product is 1,3,5-trimethyl-benzene so it has 21 sigma bonds.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-396, Paragraph -2)\n(NEET 2019)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','9','18','21','24','NEW','11-396','11-318','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130841q.png'),
(130842,'chemistry','Which one is the correct order of acidity?','CH ≡CH> CH₃—C≡CH₂> CH₂=CH₂ > CH₃— CH₃','Acidic character increases with increasing s-character of the orbital.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-394, Paragraph -last)\n(NEET 2017)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CH₂=CH₂ > CH₃—CH = CH₂ > CH₃ —C≡CH > CH≡CH','CH ≡CH> CH₃—C≡CH₂> CH₂=CH₂ > CH₃— CH₃','CH≡CH> CH₂=CH₂ > CH₃—C≡CH₂> CH₃—CH₃','CH₃— CH₃ > CH₂=CH₂ > CH₃—C≡CH> CH≡CH','NEW','11-394','11-316',NULL),
(130843,'chemistry','Predict the correct intermediate and product in the following reaction given in diagram?(n1397)','c','The reactant undergoes hydrolysis according to the Markovnikov\'s rule. \n\nThe intermediate then tautomerize to ketone.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-395, Paragraph -13.68 reaction) \n(NEET 2007)\ndiagram(ne1344)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-395','11-317','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130843q.png'),
(130844,'chemistry','The pair of electrons in the given carbanion, CH₃-C≡C⁻, is present in which of the following orbitals?','sp','Triple bond carbon are sp hybridised so electron is present in sp orbital.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-393, Paragraph -2)\n(NEET-I 2016)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','sp','sp²','sp³','2p','NEW','11-393','11-315',NULL),
(130845,'chemistry','X and Y are in the reaction given in diagram?(n1398)','X = 1-butyne, Y = 3-hexyne','The (i) reagents in the reaction are the strong acid that abstract the acidic hydrogen. \n\nThe (ii) is used to add the carbon chain. \n\nThe triple bonds remain uneffected. \n\nThe reaction is carried out twice and each time the acidic hydrogen is replaced by the ethyl group.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-387, Paragraph - 4) \n(NEET-I 2016)\ndiagram(ne1345)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','X = 2-butyne, Y = 2-hexyne','X = 1-butyne, Y = 2-hexyne','X = 1-butyne, Y = 3-hexyne','X = 2-butyne, Y = 3-hexyne','NEW','11-387','11-309','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130845q.png'),
(130846,'chemistry','Which of the following organic compounds has same hybridization as its combustion product (CO₂)?','ethyne','Both of them have sp hybridisation.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-393, Paragraph -2)\n(NEET 2014)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','ethane','ethyne','ethene','ethanol','NEW','11-393','11-315',NULL),
(130847,'chemistry','Which of the following reagents will be able to distinguish between 1-butyne and 2-butyne?','NaNH₂','Terminal alkynes (1-butyne) react with NaNH₂ to form sodium acetylide and evolve hydrogen but 2-butyne do not.\n\n(Mains 2012)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','NaNH₂','O₂','HCl','Br₂','NEW','11-395','11-317',NULL),
(130848,'chemistry','The product P in the reaction given will not give which following test,refer diagram?(n1399)','Victor Meyer test','Acetaldehyde will be formed after tautomerization which does not give Victor Meyer test.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-395, Paragraph -13.68 reaction)\n(Karnataka NEET 2013)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Tollens’ reagent test','Brady’s reagent test','Victor Meyer test','Iodoform test.','NEW','11-395','11-317','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130848q.png'),
(130849,'chemistry','Considering the state of hybridization of carbon atoms, find out the molecule among the following which is linear?','CH₃—C≡C—CH₃','Compound have sp hybridisation which leads to linear structure.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-393, Paragraph -2)\n(NEET 2011)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','CH₃—CH=CH—CH₃','CH₃—C≡C—CH₃','CH₂=CH—CH₂—C≡CH','CH₃—CH₂—CH₂—CH₃','NEW','11-393','11-315',NULL),
(130850,'chemistry','Base strength of the following in order of - (i) H₃C-CH₂⁻ (ii)H₂C=CH⁻ (iii)H-C≡C⁻','(i) > (ii) > (iii)','The more s-character the more acidic.Conjugate base of stronger acid is weaker and vice-versa.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-394, Paragraph -last)\n(NEET 2008)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) > (iii) > (ii)','(i) > (ii) > (iii)','(ii) > (i) > (iii)','(iii) > (ii) > (i)','NEW','11-394','11-316',NULL),
(130851,'chemistry','Predict the product C obtained in the following reaction of 1-butyne, shown in diagram?(n13100)','c','According to Markownikoff\'s rule, during hydrohalogenation to unsymmetrical alkene, the negative part of the addendum adds to less hydrogenated (i.e. more substituted) carbon atom.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-395, Paragraph -2)\n(NEET 2007)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-395','11-317','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130851q.png'),
(130852,'chemistry','Products of the following reaction shown in diagram?(n13101)','b','On ozonolysis, higher alkynes form diketones which are further oxidised to dicarboxylic acid.\n\n(NEET 2005)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-395','11-317','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130852q.png'),
(130853,'chemistry','When CH₃CH₂CHCl₂ is treated with NaNH₂, the product formed is, refer diagram?(n13102)','b','Here NaNH₂ cause dehalogenation from the same terminal carbon as it is a gem-dihalide so alkyne will be formed correspondingly.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-394, Paragraph -1)\n(NEET 2002)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-394','11-316','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130853q.png'),
(130854,'chemistry','When acetylene is passed through dil.H₂SO₄ in the presence of HgSO₄, the compound formed is','acetaldehyde','Acetaldehyde will be formed after tautomerization.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-395, Paragraph -6)\n(NEET 1999)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','ketone','acetic acid','ether','acetaldehyde','NEW','11-395','11-317',NULL),
(130855,'chemistry','The cylindrical shape of an alkyne is due to','one sigma C – C and two pi C – C bonds','Electron cloud between two carbon atoms is cylindrically symmetrical about the internuclear axis. \n\nThus, ethyne is a linear molecule.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-393, Paragraph -2)\n(NEET 1997)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','two sigma C – C and one pi C – C bonds','one sigma C – C and two pi C – C bonds','three sigma C – C bonds','three pi C – C bonds.','NEW','11-393','11-315',NULL),
(130856,'chemistry','The reagent in the reaction given in diagram?(n13103)','c','A powerful base is needed to carry out second dehydrohalogenation reaction e.g., hot alcoholic KOH solution or alkoxide ion.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-394, Paragraph -1)\n(NEET 1993)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-394','11-316','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130856q.png'),
(130857,'chemistry','A compound is treated with NaNH₂ to give sodium salt. Identify the compound','ethyne','Alkynes react with strong bases like NaNH₂ to form sodium acetylide derivative known as acetylides.\n\n(NEET 1993)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','ethyne','benzene','ethene','ethane','NEW','11-394','11-316',NULL),
(130858,'chemistry','The shortest C–C bond distance is found in','acetylene','Shortest C– C distance (1.20 Å) is in acetylene.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-393, Paragraph -2)\n(NEET 1991)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','diamond','ethane','benzene','acetylene','NEW','11-393','11-315',NULL),
(130859,'chemistry','Acetylenic hydrogens are acidic because','sigma electron density of C – H bond in acetylene is nearer to carbon, which has 50% s-character','Acidic character increases with increasing s-character of the orbital.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-394, Paragraph -last)\n(NEET 1989)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','sigma electron density of C – H bond in acetylene is nearer to carbon, which has 50% s-character','acetylene has only open hydrogen in each carbon','acetylene contains least number of hydrogens among the possible hydrocarbons having two carbons','acetylene belongs to the class of alkynes with molecular formula,CₙH₂ₙ-₂','NEW','11-394','11-316',NULL),
(130860,'chemistry','Which is the most suitable reagent among the following to distinguish compound (3) from rest of the compounds,refer diagram?(n13104)','Ammoniacal silver nitrate','All the three reagents except ammoniacal AgNO₃ reacts with 1, 2 and 4 compounds. \n\nThe compound 3 possessing the terminal alkyne only reacts with ammoniacal AgNO3 and thus can be distinguished from 1, 2 and 4 compounds.\n\n(NEET 1989)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Bromine in carbon tetrachloride','Bromine in acetic acid','Alk.KMnO₄','Ammoniacal silver nitrate','NEW','11-394','11-316','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130860q.png'),
(130861,'chemistry','Among the following the reaction that proceeds through an electrophilic substitution is, refer diagram?(n13105)','b','The attacking species in the reaction given in option (c) is an electrophile i.e.,Cl⁺. \n\nTherefore, it is an electrophilic substitution reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -5)\n(NEET 2019)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-400','11-322','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130861q.png'),
(130862,'chemistry','Which of the following can be used as the halide component for Friedel–Crafts reaction?','Isopropyl chloride','Chlorobenzene, bromobenzene and chloroethene are not suitable halide components as C–X bond acquires some double bond character due to resonance of lone pair of electrons with p-bond.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -7)\n(NEET-II 2016)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Chlorobenzene','Bromobenzene','Chloroethene','Isopropyl chloride','NEW','11-400','11-322',NULL),
(130863,'chemistry','In which of the following molecules, all atoms are coplanar, refer diagram?(n13106)','a','Biphenyl is coplanar as all C-atoms are sp² hybridised.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-399, Paragraph -3)\n(NEET-II 2016)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-399','11-321','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130863q.png'),
(130864,'chemistry','In pyrrole the electron density is maximum on the position, refer diagram?(n13107)','2 and 5','Pyrrole has maximum electron density on 2 and 5. \n\nIt generally reacts with electrophiles at the C-2 or C-5 due to the highest degree of stability of the protonated intermediate.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-399, Paragraph -3)\n(NEET-II 2016)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','2 and 3','3 and 4','2 and 4','2 and 5','NEW','11-399','11-321','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130864q.png'),
(130865,'chemistry','The product P in the reaction given in diagram?(n13108)','c','The following reaction of cyclohexane and benzene in presence of hydrogen fluoride is an example of Friedel craft alkylation.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -7) \n(NEET-II 2016)\ndiagram(ne1346)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-400','11-322','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130865q.png'),
(130866,'chemistry','Consider the nitration of benzene using mixed conc.H₂SO₄ and HNO₃.If a large amount of KHSO₄ is added to the mixture, the rate of nitration will be','slower','If a large amount of KHSO₄ is added then conc. of HSO₄ – ions increases and the reaction will be shifted in backward direction hence, the rate of nitration will be slower.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -5)\n(NEET-I 2016)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','unchanged','doubled','faster','slower','NEW','11-400','11-322',NULL),
(130867,'chemistry','The oxidation of benzene by V₂O₅ in the presence of air produces','maleic anhydride','Maleic anhydride is produced when benzene is oxidised by V2O5 in the presence of air. \n\nPhenol results from partial benzene oxidation by V2O5 in the presence of air, whereas maleic anhydride results from full oxidation.\n(NEET 2015)\ndiagram(ne1347)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','maleic anhydride','benzoic acid','benzaldehyde','benzoic anhydride','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(130868,'chemistry','What products are formed when the following compound is treated with Br₂ in the presence of FeBr₃, refer diagram?(n13109)','c','—CH₃ group is o,p-directing. \n\nBecause of crowding, no substitution occurs at the carbon atom between the two —CH₃ groups in m-xylene, even though two —CH₃ groups activate that position.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -5)\n(NEET 2014)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-400','11-322','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130868q.png'),
(130869,'chemistry','Some meta-directing substituents in aromatic substitution are given. Which one is most deactivating?','— NO₂','— NO₂ is most deactivating due to – I and – M effect.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-403, Paragraph -3)\n(NEET 2013)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','— COOH','— NO₂','— CN','— SO₃H','NEW','11-403','11-325',NULL),
(130870,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds will not undergo Friedel-Crafts reaction easily?','Nitrobenzene','Nitrobenzene is strongly deactivated, hence will not undergo Friedel-Crafts reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-403, Paragraph -4)\n(NEET 2013)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Nitrobenzene','Toluene','Cumene','Xylene','NEW','11-403','11-325',NULL),
(130871,'chemistry','Among the following compounds the one that is most reactive towards electrophilic nitration is','toluene','As the +I effect increases reactivity towards electrophilic reactions increases and as –I or –M effect increases, reactivity towards electrophilic reactions decreases.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-403, Paragraph -4)\n(Karnataka NEET 2013)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','benzoic acid','nitrobenzene','toluene','benzene','NEW','11-403','11-325',NULL),
(130872,'chemistry','Which of the following chemical system is non aromatic,refer diagram?(n13110)','d','The molecules which do not satisfy Huckel rule or (4n + 2)pi-electron rule are said to be non-aromatic. \n\nThe compound (d) has total 4pi electrons.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-399, Paragraph -4)\n(NEET 1992, 2012)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-399','11-321','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130872q.png'),
(130873,'chemistry','The reaction of toluene with Cl₂ in presence of FeCl₃ gives X and reaction in presence of light gives Y. Thus, X and Y are','X = o- and p-chlorotoluene, Y = trichloromethyl benzene','The reaction of Cl₂, in presence of FeCl₃, with toluene yields a ring substitution product \n\nIn presence of sunlight, free radical reaction takes place. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -5)\n(NEET 2010)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','X = benzal chloride, Y = o-chlorotoluene','X = m-chlorotoluene, Y = p-chlorotoluene','X = o- and p-chlorotoluene, Y = trichloromethyl benzene','X = benzyl chloride, Y = m-chlorotoluene.','NEW','11-400','11-322',NULL),
(130874,'chemistry','Benzene reacts with CH₃Cl in the presence of anhydrous AlCl₃ to form','toluene','When benzene is treated with an alkyl halide in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride, alkylbenene is formed.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -7)\n(NEET 2009)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','chlorobenzene','benzyl chloride','xylene','toluene','NEW','11-400','11-322',NULL),
(130875,'chemistry','Nitrobenzene can be prepared from benzene by using a mixture of conc. HNO₃ and conc.H₂SO₄. In the mixture, nitric acid acts as a/an','base','It is interesting to note that in the process of generation of nitronium ion, sulphuric acid serves as an acid and nitric acid as a base.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-401, Paragraph -6)\n(NEET 2009)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','acid','base','catalyst','reducing agent.','NEW','11-401','11-323',NULL),
(130876,'chemistry','Which one of the following is most reactive towards electrophilic attack, refer diagram?(n13111)','a','Groups like, —Cl and —NO₂ shows –I effect. –I groups attached to the benzene ring decrease the electron density and hence less prone to electrophilic attack.\n\n —OH not only shows –I effect but also +M effect which predominates the –I character and electron density is increased in the benzene ring which facilitates electrophilic attack.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-402, Paragraph -7)\n(NEET 2008)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-402','11-324','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130876q.png'),
(130877,'chemistry','The order of decreasing reactivity towards an electrophilic reagent, for the following would be (i) chlorobenzene (ii) benzene (iii) toluene (iv) phenol','(iv) > (iii) > (ii) > (i)','Benzene having any activating group i.e., —OH, —R undergoes electrophilic substitution easily as compared to benzene itself. \n\nThus, toluene and phenol undergo electrophilic substitution easily. \n\nChlorine due to –I-effect deactivates the ring. So, it is difficult to carry out the electrophilic substitution in chlorobenzene.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-402, Paragraph -7)\n(NEET 2007)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(ii)>(iv)>(i)>(iii)','(iii) > (iv) > (ii) > (i)','(iv) > (iii) > (ii) > (i)','(i) > (ii) > (iii) > (iv)','NEW','11-402','11-324',NULL),
(130878,'chemistry','Using anhydrous AlCl₃ as catalyst, which one of the following reactions produces ethylbenzene (PhEt)?','ethene+benzene','The given reaction is following the friedel crafts alkylation react.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -7) \n(NEET 2004)\n diagram(ne1348)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','ethanol+benzene','propene+benzene','ethene+benzene','ethane+benzene','NEW','11-400','11-322',NULL),
(130879,'chemistry','Which one of the following is a free-radical substitution reaction, refer diagram?(n13112)','a','The chlorine forms a free radical on boiling which then replaces the hydrogen of methyl group.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -5) \n(NEET 2003)\ndiagram(ne1349)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-403','11-325','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130879q.png'),
(130880,'chemistry','The correct order of reactivity towards the electrophilic substitution of the compounds aniline (I), benzene (II) and nitrobenzene (III) is','I > II > III','Examples of activating and o-,p- directing groups are –NH₂, –NHR, –NHCOCH₃, –OCH₃, –CH₃, etc.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-403, Paragraph -1)\n(NEET 2003)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','III > II > I','II > III > I','I < II > III','I > II > III','NEW','11-403','11-325',NULL),
(130881,'chemistry','Increasing order of electrophilic substitution for following compounds, refer diagram?(n13113)','IV < I < II < III','Presence of electron releasing group (or activating group) increases the electron density in benzene nucleus. \n\nTherefore, electrophile will attack benzene nucleus easily. \n\nBut the presence of electron withdrawing group like —NO2 decreases the electron density in benzene ring. \n\nTherefore, electrophile will attack benzene nucleus with difficulty.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-403, Paragraph -4)\n(NEET 2000)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','IV < I < II < III','III < II < I < IV','I < IV < III < II','II < III < I < IV','NEW','11-403','11-325','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130881q.png'),
(130882,'chemistry','In Friedel-Crafts reaction, toluene can be prepared by','C₆H₆ + CH₃Cl','When benzene is treated with an alkyl halide in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride, alkylbenene is formed.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -7)\n(NEET 2000)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','C₆H₆ + CH₃Cl','C₆H₅Cl + CH₄','C₆H₆ + CH₂Cl₂','C₆H₆ + CH₃COCl','NEW','11-400','11-322',NULL),
(130883,'chemistry','In Friedel-Crafts alkylation, besides AlCl₃ the other reactants are','C₆H₆ + CH₃Cl','When benzene is treated with an alkyl halide in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride, alkylbenene is formed.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -7)\n(NEET 1999)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','C₆H₆ + CH₃Cl','C₆H₅Cl + CH₄','C₆H₆ + NH₃','C₆H₆ + CH₃COCl','NEW','11-400','11-322',NULL),
(130884,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds will be most easily attacked by an electrophile, refer diagram?(n13114)','a',': —OH, —Cl and —CH₃ groups in benzene are ortho, para directing groups. \n\nBut among these —OH group is strongly activating while —CH₃ is weakly activating and —Cl is deactivating. \n\nThus, phenol will be most easily attacked by an electrophile.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-403, Paragraph -4)\n(NEET 1998,1999)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-403','11-325','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130884q.png'),
(130885,'chemistry','Which one of these is not compatible with arenes?','Electrophilic additions','Arenes undergo electrophilic substitution reactions and are resistant to addition reactions, due to delocalisation of p-electrons. \n\nThese are also stabilized by resonance.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-402, Paragraph -2)\n(NEET 1998)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Electrophilic additions','Delocalisation of p-electrons','Greater stability','Resonance','NEW','11-402','11-324',NULL),
(130886,'chemistry','Among the following compounds (I-III) the correct order of reaction with electrophile is, refer diagram?(n13115)','I > II > III','In structure III, withdrawal of electrons by —NO₂ causes decrease in reaction rate while in structure I, there is electron releasing effect by —OCH₃ group which accelerates the reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-403, Paragraph -4)\n(NEET 1997)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','I > II > III','I = II > III','II > III > I','III < I < II','NEW','11-403','11-325','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130886q.png'),
(130887,'chemistry','The reactive species in the nitration of benzene is','NO₂⁺','In the case of nitration, the electrophile, nitronium ion, N0₂⁺ is produced by transfer of a proton (from sulphuric acid) to nitric acid.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-401, Paragraph -5)\n(NEET 1994)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','NO₃','HNO₃','NO₂⁺','NO₂⁻','NEW','11-401','11-323',NULL),
(130888,'chemistry','Select the true statement about benzene amongst the following.','There is cyclic delocalisation of p-electrons in benzene','Due to resonance all the C – C bonds in the benzene possess same nature and the resonating structures are obtained because of the delocalisation of p-electrons.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-399, Paragraph -3)\n(NEET 1992)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Because of unsaturation benzene easily undergoes addition','Because of unsaturation benzene easily undergoes addition','There is cyclic delocalisation of p-electrons in benzene','Monosubstitution of benzene gives three isomeric products','NEW','11-399','11-321',NULL),
(130889,'chemistry','Which is the correct symbol relating the two Kekule structures of benzene, refer diagram?(n13116)','d','Benzene shows Kekule structures which are resonating structures and these structures are separated by a double headed arrow (↔)\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-398, Paragraph -1)\n(NEET 1993)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-397','11-319','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130889q.png'),
(130890,'chemistry','Dihedral angle of least stable conformer of ethane is: Refer diagram(n13117)','0°','Dihedral angle (D.A.) of least stable conformer of ethane = 0°\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph -1)\n(NEET 2021)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','120°','180°','60°','0°','NEW','11-383','11-305','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130890q.png'),
(130891,'chemistry','The major product formed in dehydrohalogenation reaction of 2-bromopentane is pent-2-ene. This product formation is based on ?','Saytzeff\'s Rule','The major compound formed is pent-2-ene which is formed by the dehydrohalogenation/β-elimination reaction following the Saytzeff\'s rule. \n\nAccording to the rule the favoured result is an alkene with fewer hydrogen atoms on the double-bonded carbon. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-388, Paragraph -1) \n(NEET 2021) \ndiagram(ne13118)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Saytzeff\'s Rule','Hund\'s Rule','Hoffmann Rule','Huckel\'s Rule','NEW','11-388','11-310',NULL),
(130892,'chemistry','The major product of the following chemical reaction is: Refer diagram(n13119)','1','In presence of peroxide, the HBr is added according the anti-Markovnikov rule.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-390, Paragraph - example) \n(NEET 2021)\ndiagram(ne1320)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-390','11-312','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130892q.png'),
(130893,'chemistry','Consider the above reaction and identify the missing reagent/chemical. Refer diagram(n13120)','CaO','Sodium salts of carboxylic acids on heating with soda lime (mixture of sodium hydroxide and calcium oxide) give alkanes containing one carbon atom less than the carboxylic acid. \n\nThis process of elimination of carbon dioxide from a carboxylic acid is known as decarboxylation.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-379, Paragraph -2)\n(NEET 2021)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','B₂H₆','Red Phosphorus','CaO','DIBAL-H','NEW','11-399','11-321','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130893q.png'),
(130894,'chemistry','Which compound amongst the following is not an aromatic compound? Refer diagram(n13121)','4','Planar, cyclic, conjugated species containing (4n + 2)pi electrons will be aromatic in nature (n is an integer). \n\nStructure 1,2 and 3 are aromatic but 4 is not.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-399, Paragraph -4) \n(NEET 2022)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-399','11-321','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130894q.png'),
(130895,'chemistry','Which of the following sequence of reactions is suitable to synthesize chlorobenzene? Rrefer diagram(n13122)','1','Benzene reacts with chlorine in presence of anhydrous FeCl3 to give chlorobenzene according to the Friedel crafts alkylation.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-399, Paragraph -7) \n(NEET 2022)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-399','11-321','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130895q.png'),
(130896,'chemistry','The correct IUPAC name of the following compound is. Refer diagram(n13123)','1-bromo-5-chloro-4-methylhexan-3-ol','There are several sets of rules for IUPAC nomenclature. \n\n1 The longest continuous carbon chain should be found and named.\n\n2. List the names of the organisations that make up this chain.\n\n3. Starting at the end of the chain closest to a substituent group, sequentially number the chain.\n\n4. Indicate each substituent group\'s location with the relevant number and name.\n\n5. Put the name together by listing the groups alphabetically using the whole name. (e.g., cyclopropyl before isobutyl).\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph -5) \n(NEET 2022)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1-bromo-5-chloro-4-methylhexan-3-ol','6-bromo-2-chloro-4-methythexan-4-ol','1-bromo-4-methyl-5-chlorohexan-3-ol','6-bromo-4-methyl-2-chlorohexan-4-ol','NEW','11-374','11-296','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130896q.png'),
(130897,'chemistry','Compound X on reaction with O₃ followed by Zn/H₂Ogives formaldehyde and 2-methyl propanal as products.\nThe compound X is,','3-methylbut-1-ene','The given reaction is the reductive ozonolysis of an alkene.\n\nOzonolysis of alkenes involves the addition of ozone molecule to alkene to form ozonide, and then cleavage of the ozonide by Zn-H₂O to smaller molecules.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-391, Paragraph -4) \n(NEET 2022)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','3-methylbut-1-ene','2-methylbut-1-ene','2-methylbut-2-ene','pent-2-ene','NEW','11-391','11-313',NULL),
(130898,'chemistry','The Correct IUPAC name of the compound shown in diagram(130898Q)','4-ethyl-3-propylhex-1-ene','According to IUPAC nomenclature, the carbon chain containing a double bond is considered the parent chain. The position of the double bond is one. At the fourth position, the ethyl group is present and at the third position, the propyl group is present.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','3-ethyl-4-ethnylheptane','3-ethyl-4-propylhex-5-ene','3-(1-ethyl propyl)hex-1-ene','4-ethyl-3-propylhex-1-ene','NEW','11-374','11-296','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130898q.png'),
(130899,'chemistry','The Incorrect IUPAC among the following in the diagram(130899Q):','1','The name of the compound given in the first option is wrong because double bond gets higher priority than Br.In the IUPAC system of nomenclature, preference is given to multiple bonds than halogen substituents.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-374','11-296','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130899q.png'),
(130924,'chemistry','n-Propylbenzene can be obtained from: Refer diagram(n13130)','c','The reaction of benzene with an acyl halide or acid anhydride in the presence of Lewis acids (AlCl₃) yields acyl benzene. \n\nThe n-propylbenzene can not be obtained from 1-chloropropane due to the rearrangement of cation formed. \n\nThis does not happen in acylation. The carbonyl group is reduced to give the n-propylbenzene.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph - 10)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-400','11-322','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130924q.png'),
(130925,'chemistry','Carcinogenic hydrocarbons are produced by the incomplete combustion of:','tobacco, coal and petroleum','Benzene and polynuclear hydrocarbons containing more than two benzene rings fused together are toxic and said to possess cancer producing (carcinogenic) property. \n\nSuch polynuclear hydrocarbons are formed on incomplete combustion of organic materials like tobacco, coal and petroleum.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-403, Paragraph - 5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','biogas, methane and coal','plastic, wood and petroleum','natural gas, coal and kerosene','tobacco, coal and petroleum','NEW','11-403','11-325',NULL),
(130926,'chemistry','The IUPAC name of the given compound is: refer diagram(n13131)','1,2,5,6-dibenzathracene','Benzene and polynuclear hydrocarbons containing more than two benzene rings fused together are toxic and said to possess cancer producing (carcinogenic) property. \n\nSuch polynuclear hydrocarbons are formed on incomplete combustion of organic materials like tobacco, coal and petroleum.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-403, Paragraph -5 ) diagram (ne1352)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1,2,5-dibenzathracene','1,2-benzpyrene','1,2-benzathracene','1,2,5,6-dibenzathracene','NEW','11-403','11-325','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130926q.png'),
(130927,'chemistry','Given below are two statements.\n\nAssertion (A): Meta directing groups direct the incoming electrons to meta position which is electron rich.\n\nReason(R): The deactivating groups increase the electron density on meta position by resonating electrons on it.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct.','Meta directing group reduces the electron density in the benzene ring due to its strong–I effect. \n\nNitrobenzene is a resonance hybrid of the following structures. \n\nIn this case, the overall electron density on benzene ring decreases making further substitution difficult, therefore these groups are also called ‘deactivating groups’. \n\nThe electron density on o– and p– position is comparatively less than that at meta position. \n\nHence, the electrophile attacks on comparatively electron rich meta position resulting in meta substitution.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-403, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct.','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct.','NEW','11-403','11-325',NULL),
(130928,'chemistry','The ortho and para directing groups increase the electron density at ortho and para position due to its:','positive m effect','The +m effect is much stronger and prominent in ortho-para directing groups.\n \nAlthough there is some -I effect but it is negligible.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-402, Paragraph - 7)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','-I effect','decreasing the electron density at meta position','-m effect','positive m effect','NEW','11-402','11-324',NULL),
(130929,'chemistry','Chlorobenzene is produced by passing chlorine through','benzene in anhyd. FeBr₃','Arenes react with halogens in the presence of a Lewis acid like anhydrous FeCl₃, FeBr₃ or AlCl₃ to yield haloarenes.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph - 6)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','phenol at high temperature','toluene in acidic iron','benzene in anhyd. FeBr₃','bromobenzene in acidic H₂SO₄','NEW','11-400','11-322',NULL),
(130930,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a characteristic of aromatic hydrocarbons?','They are miscible in water','Aromatic hydrocarbons are nonpolar molecules and are usually colourless liquids or solids with a characteristic aroma. \n\nYou are also familiar with naphthalene balls which are used in toilets and for preservation of clothes because of unique smell of the compound and the moth repellent property. \n\nAromatic hydrocarbons are immiscible with water but are readily miscible with organic solvents. They burn with sooty flame.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','They are non-polar','Colourless liquid with characteristic aroma','They are planar','They are miscible in water','NEW','11-400','11-322',NULL),
(130931,'chemistry','Select the alkane structure that indicates the primary, secondary and tertiary carbons. Refer diagram(n13132)','c','Carbon that is bonded to another carbon is known as primary carbon (1°). \n\nCarbon connected to two additional carbons is referred to as secondary carbon (2°).\n \nCarbon bonded to three other carbons to form tertiary carbon (3°).\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-396, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-396','11-318',NULL),
(130932,'chemistry','What is the correct IUPAC name of the given compound? Refer diagram (n13133)','b','There are several sets of rules for IUPAC nomenclature. \n\n1 The longest continuous carbon chain should be found and named.\n\n2. List the names of the organisations that make up this chain.\n\n3. Starting at the end of the chain closest to a substituent group, sequentially number the chain.\n\n4. Indicate each substituent group\'s location with the relevant number and name.\n\n5. Put the name together by listing the groups alphabetically using the whole name. (e.g., cyclopropyl before isobutyl). \n\nThus the name of the given compound is 2-methylpentane.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 6)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-374','11-296','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130932q.png'),
(130933,'chemistry','The hydrocarbons are ______','all of these','Hydrocarbons are the important sources of energy.\n\nThe term ‘hydrocarbon’ is self-explanatory which means compounds of carbon and hydrogen only. \n\nHydrocarbons play a key role in our daily life. \n\nYou must be familiar with the terms ‘LPG’ and ‘CNG’ used as fuels.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-373, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','source of enegrgy','insoluble in water','compounds of carbon and hydrogen','all of these','NEW','11-373','11-295',NULL),
(130934,'chemistry','The common name of hydrocarbons are _______','written in parenthesis','Common names are given in parenthesis.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','written in bold letters','not helpful','written in parenthesis','written in italics','NEW','11-374','11-296',NULL),
(130935,'chemistry','How are the given structures related to each other? Refer diagram(n13134)','they all are chain isomers','Such structural isomers which differ in chain of carbon atoms are known as chain isomers.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-375, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','they all are metamers','they all are chain isomers','they all are functional group isomers','they all are position isomers','NEW','11-375','11-297','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130935q.png'),
(130936,'chemistry','Which of the following is not true about chain isomers?','they possess different molecular formula','Although structural isomers share the same molecular formula, their atoms are bonded differently. \n\nIn general, structural isomers show different physical and chemical characteristics. \n\nStereoisomers share the same functional groups and formula, but they differ in how those groups are arranged in space.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-375, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','they possess different properties','they possess different molecular formula','they possess same molecular formula','they have branched and straight chains','NEW','11-375','11-297',NULL),
(130937,'chemistry','How many members of alkane family are gases and liquids?','C₁ to C₄ are gases and C₅ to C₁₇ are liquids.','Due to the weak forces, the first four members, C₁ to C₄ are gases, C₅ to C₁₇ are liquids and those containing 18 carbon atoms or more are solids at 298 K.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-379, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','C₁ to C₄ are gases and C₅ to C₈ are liquids.','C₁ to C₅ are gases and C₆ to C₁₂ are liquids.','C₁ to C₅ are gases and C₆ to C₁₈ are liquids.','C₁ to C₄ are gases and C₅ to C₁₇ are liquids.','NEW','11-379','11-300',NULL),
(131016,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Toluene on Friedal Crafts methylation gives o - and p xylene.\n\nReason (R) CH3-group bonded to benzene ring increases electron\ndensity at o- and p- position.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','The reason for the formation of o- and p-xylene as major products is that the methyl group attached to the benzene ring increases the electron density at the ortho and para positions of the ring by the inductive effect.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-396','11-318',NULL),
(131017,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Nitration of benzene with nitric acid requires the use of concentrated sulphuric acid.\n\nReason (R) The mixture of concentrated sulphuric acid and concentrated nitric acid produces the electrophile, NO2+.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Nitration of benzene with nitric acid requires the use of concentrated sulfuric acid as a catalyst. The reason for this is that sulfuric acid acts as a dehydrating agent that removes water molecules from nitric acid, which helps to generate the nitronium ion (NO2+) electrophile. Nitronium ion is an important electrophile that is necessary for the nitration of benzene.\n\nThe reaction between concentrated sulfuric acid and concentrated nitric acid produces nitronium ion as follows:\n\nH2SO4 + HNO3 → HSO4- + H2NO3+\n\nH2NO3+ → NO2+ + H2O\n\nIn this reaction, sulfuric acid acts as a protonating agent that protonates nitric acid to form nitronium ion. The nitronium ion generated is a powerful electrophile that is responsible for the nitration of benzene.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-400','11-322',NULL),
(131018,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Among isomeric pentanes, 2, 2-dimethylpentane has highest boiling point.\n\nReason (R) Branching does not affect the boiling point.','Assertion is true but reason is false','2,2-Dimethylpentane (also known as neohexane) has the highest boiling point among the isomeric pentanes. The reason for this is that neohexane is the most symmetrical and compact of all the isomers. \n\nOn the other hand, branching does affect the boiling point of a molecule. As the number of branches in a molecule increases, the surface area of the molecule decreases, and the molecule becomes more compact. As a result, the intermolecular forces become weaker, which leads to a lower boiling point.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-375','11-297',NULL),
(131411,'chemistry','Assertion: Alkanes are used as fuels due to the evolution of a large amount of heat during their combustion.\n\nReason: Alkanes on heating in the presence of air or dioxygen are completely oxidized to carbon dioxide and water with the evolution of a large amount of heat.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','When alkanes are heated in the presence of air or dioxygen, they undergo complete combustion, which means they are completely oxidized to carbon dioxide and water with the evolution of a large amount of heat. This heat released during the combustion of alkanes makes them useful as fuels in various applications, such as in internal combustion engines, furnaces, and boilers.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-373','11-295',NULL),
(131412,'chemistry','Assertion: Alkanes having tertiary H atom can be oxidized to corresponding alcohols by potassium permanganate.\n\nReason: Alkanes ordinarily resist oxidation','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Alkanes are generally resistant to oxidation. However, alkanes having a tertiary hydrogen atom can be oxidized to corresponding alcohols by potassium permanganate. This is because the tertiary hydrogen atom is more easily oxidized due to the presence of electron-donating alkyl groups attached to the carbon atom bearing the hydrogen atom. Potassium permanganate is a strong oxidizing agent that can oxidize the tertiary hydrogen atom to a hydroxyl (-OH) group.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-391','11-313',NULL),
(131413,'chemistry','Assertion: Alkanes can undergo halogenation reaction in the presence of ultraviolet light or sunlight.\n\nReason: The reaction between alkanes and halogens is an example of an addition reaction.','Assertion is true but reason is false','The reaction between alkanes and halogens is exothermic and not endothermic. In the halogenation reaction, the halogen atom replaces one of the hydrogen atoms of the alkane, resulting in the formation of a halogenoalkane. \n\nThis is an example of a substitution reaction where one functional group is substituted by another. The reaction takes place in the presence of ultraviolet light or sunlight to provide the activation energy required to initiate the reaction.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-401','11-323',NULL),
(131414,'chemistry','Assertion: Methane reacts with steam at 1273 K in the presence of nickel catalyst .\n\nReason: This method is used for industrial preparation of dihydrogen gas.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Methane does react with steam at high temperature (1273 K) in the presence of a nickel catalyst to produce carbon monoxide and hydrogen gas. \n\nThis is known as steam reforming of methane, and it is indeed used on an industrial scale for the production of hydrogen gas.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-395','11-317',NULL),
(131415,'chemistry','Assertion: Alkenes can also undergo substitution reactions.\n\nReason: The carbon-carbon double bond in alkenes is a region of low electron density.','Both Assertion and reason are false','Alkenes cannot undergo substitution reactions, as the carbon-carbon double bond is not easily broken. \n\nThe carbon-carbon double bond is a region of high electron density. This makes alkenes reactive towards electrophiles and undergo addition reactions.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-395','11-317',NULL),
(131416,'chemistry','Assertion: Alkenes do not undergo substitution reactions.\n\nReason: Alkenes have a π bond which is stronger than a σ bond and thus substitution reactions are not favorable.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Alkenes cannot undergo substitution reactions, as the carbon-carbon double bond is not easily broken.\n\nThe π bond in alkenes is weaker than the σ bond and hence addition reactions are favored over substitution reactions.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-395','11-317',NULL),
(131417,'chemistry','Assertion: Alkanes can have infinite number of conformations by rotation around C-C single bonds.\n\nReason: Rotation around a C-C single bond  is hindered by a small energy barrier of 1-20 kJ mol–1 due to weak repulsive interaction between the adjacent bonds.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Alkanes have carbon-carbon sigma (σ) bonds which allow free rotation around C-C single bonds resulting in different spatial arrangements of atoms called conformations. However, this rotation is hindered by a small energy barrier called torsional strain due to weak repulsive interaction between the adjacent bonds.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-383','11-305',NULL),
(131418,'chemistry','Assertion: Alkanes show geometrical isomerism in their carbon-carbon double bond.\n\nReason: The carbon-carbon double bond in alkenes is formed by the overlap of one sp²hybridized orbital of each carbon atom with each other.','Both Assertion and reason are false','Alkenes, not alkanes, show geometrical isomerism in their carbon-carbon double bond. The carbon-carbon double bond in alkenes is formed by the overlap of one sp² hybridized orbital of each carbon atom with each other. Due to this, the carbon-carbon double bond is rigid and prevents free rotation, leading to the formation of two different isomers with different spatial arrangements of the substituent groups','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-386','11-308',NULL),
(131419,'chemistry','Assertion: The eclipsed conformation of ethane has more torsional strain than the staggered conformation.\n\nReason: In the eclipsed conformation, the hydrogen atoms on adjacent carbon atoms are closer to each other, leading to repulsive interactions','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','The eclipsed conformation of ethane has more torsional strain than the staggered conformation because in the eclipsed conformation, the hydrogen atoms on adjacent carbon atoms are closer to each other. This results in repulsive interactions between the electron clouds of the C-H bonds, which causes an increase in torsional strain.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-386','11-308',NULL),
(131420,'chemistry','Assertion: Sawhorse projection is a type of conformational projection used to represent the spatial arrangement of atoms in a molecule of ethane.\n\nReason: Sawhorse projection is a 2-dimensional representation of the 3-dimensional molecule of ethane viewed along the molecular axis.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Sawhorse projection is a type of conformational projection used to represent the spatial arrangement of atoms in a molecule of ethane. It is drawn by viewing the molecule along the C-C bond and projecting it onto the paper.\n\nSawhorse projection is a 2-dimensional representation of the 3-dimensional molecule of ethane viewed along the molecular axis.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-386','11-308',NULL),
(131421,'chemistry','Assertion: The nature of halogen atom and the alkyl group determines the rate of dehydrohalogenation reaction.\n\nReason: In dehydrohalogenation reaction, one molecule of halogen acid is eliminated from alkyl halides on heating with alcoholic potash to form alkenes.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','The nature of halogen atom and the alkyl group determines the rate of dehydrohalogenation reaction. The rate of the reaction is observed to be iodine > bromine > chlorine for halogens and tert > secondary > primary for alkyl groups. The reason for this is that the strength of the C-X bond decreases as we move down the halogen group due to the increase in atomic size, and thus the rate of reaction increases.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-381','11-303',NULL),
(131422,'chemistry','Assertion: Hydrogen atoms of ethyne attached to triply bonded carbon atom are acidic in nature.\n\nReason: Due to the maximum percentage of s character (50%), the sp2 hybridised orbitals of carbon atoms in ethyne molecules have highest electronegativity','Assertion is true but reason is false','Hydrogen atoms of ethyne attached to triply bonded carbon atom are acidic in nature.\n\nAs the percentage of s-character increases in hybridization, the electronegativity of the hybrid orbital also increases. The sp-hybridized orbitals have maximum s-character (50%) and are thus more electronegative than sp2 and sp3 hybridized orbitals. The high electronegativity of sp hybridized orbitals attracts the shared electron pair of the C-H bond towards it and makes the hydrogen attached to the triply bonded carbon atom more acidic.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(131423,'chemistry','Assertion: Thin film of polyacetylene can be used as electrodes in batteries.\n\nReason: These films are good conductors, lighter and cheaper than the metal conductors.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Polyacetylene is a type of polymer formed from acetylene monomers. It has a highly conjugated structure which gives it good electrical conductivity. Due to this property, thin films of polyacetylene can be used as electrodes in batteries, as stated in the assertion.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-392','11-314','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/131423q.png'),
(131424,'chemistry','Assertion: Aromatic hydrocarbons are non-polar molecules and are usually immiscible with water but readily miscible with organic solvents.\n\nReason: Aromatic hydrocarbons have a characteristic aroma and burn with a sooty flame.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Aromatic hydrocarbons have a ring of sp2 hybridized carbon atoms with delocalized pi electrons. Due to the symmetrical arrangement of the carbon atoms in the ring, the molecule is nonpolar. This nonpolarity makes them immiscible with water but they are readily miscible with organic solvents such as benzene, toluene, and chloroform. Aromatic hydrocarbons burn with a sooty flame due to the incomplete combustion of carbon','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(131425,'chemistry','Assertion: Benzene and polynuclear hydrocarbons containing more than two benzene rings fused together are toxic\n\nReason: Such polynuclear hydrocarbons are formed on incomplete combustion of organic materials like tobacco, coal and petroleum','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Benzene and polynuclear hydrocarbons containing more than two benzene rings fused together are toxic and possess cancer-producing properties. The reason given for this is that such polynuclear hydrocarbons are formed on incomplete combustion of organic materials like tobacco, coal, and petroleum. They enter the human body, undergo various biochemical reactions, damage DNA, and cause cancer.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-373','11-295',NULL),
(220106,'chemistry','S (s) + O₂ (g) → _____ (g)','SO₂','2 Mg (s) + O₂ (g) → 2 MgO (s) and S (s) + O₂ (g) → SO₂ (g) (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 263; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','fillup','S,O₂,S₂,O',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-263','11-235',NULL),
(220111,'chemistry','2 H₂S(g) + O₂ (g) → 2___ (s) + 2 H₂O (l)','S','2 H₂S(g) + O₂ (g) → 2 S (s) + 2 H₂O (l) (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','fillup','S,S₂',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220112,'chemistry','Oxidation does not only involve reactions adding oxygen but the elements that are','electronegative','As knowledge of chemists grew, it was natural to extend the term oxidation for reactions which do not involve oxygen but other electronegative elements. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','mcq','electronegative','electropositive','electroneutral','none of these','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220113,'chemistry','Mg (s) + F₂ (g) → ____ (s)','MgF₂','Mg (s) + F₂ (g) → MgF₂ (s) (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','fillup','Mg,F₂,Mg₂,F',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220114,'chemistry','Mg (s) + Cl₂ (g) → _____ (s)','MgCl₂','Mg (s) + Cl₂ (g) → MgCl₂ (s) (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','fillup','Mg,Cl₂,Mg₂,Cl₂',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220115,'chemistry','Mg (s) + S (s) → _____ (s)','MgS','Mg (s) + S (s) → MgS (s) (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','fillup','Mg,S,Mg₂,S₂',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220116,'chemistry','2K₄[Fe(CN)₆](aq) + H₂O₂ (aq) →2K₃[Fe(CN)₆](aq) + 2KOH (aq) Here the reactant iron compound is called Potassium _____','ferrocyanide','This reaction is interpreted as oxidation due to the removal of electropositive element potassium from potassium ferrocyanide before it changes to potassium ferricyanide. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'2','fillup','fe,rr,oc,ya,ni,de,ous,ic',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220118,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of Fe in K₄[Fe(CN)₆]','2+','2K₄[Fe(CN)₆](aq) + H₂O₂ (aq) →2K₃[Fe(CN)₆](aq) + 2KOH (aq) This reaction is interpreted as oxidation due to the removal of electropositive element potassium from potassium ferrocyanide before it changes to potassium ferricyanide. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','mcq','2+','3+','4+','1+','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220119,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of K in K₄[Fe(CN)₆]','1+','In K₄[Fe(CN)₆]: Let x be be the oxidation state of Potassium: \n\n4x + 2 +6×(−1)=0\n4x-4=0\nx=+1\n\n2K₄[Fe(CN)₆](aq) + H₂O₂ (aq) →2K₃[Fe(CN)₆](aq) + 2KOH (aq) This reaction is interpreted as oxidation due to the removal of electropositive element potassium from potassium ferrocyanide before it changes to potassium ferricyanide. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','mcq','2+','3+','4+','1+','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220120,'chemistry','2K₄[Fe(CN)₆](aq) + H₂O₂ (aq) →2K₃[Fe(CN)₆](aq) + 2KOH (aq) Which element undergoes oxidation in this reaction?','Fe','2K₄[Fe(CN)₆](aq) + H₂O₂ (aq) →2K₃[Fe(CN)₆](aq) + 2KOH (aq) This reaction is interpreted as oxidation due to the removal of electropositive element potassium from potassium ferrocyanide before it changes to potassium ferricyanide. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'2','mcq','K','Fe','H','C','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220121,'chemistry','2K₄[Fe(CN)₆](aq) + H₂O₂ (aq) →2K₃[Fe(CN)₆](aq) + 2KOH (aq) Which element undergoes neither oxidation nor reduction in this reaction?','K','The oxidation state of K (potassium) is +1 in both the reactants and the products. Therefore, it is not undergoing oxidation or reduction.\n\nThe oxidation state of O (oxygen) is -1 in H₂O₂ and -2 in KOH, which means it is undergoing reduction.\n\nThe oxidation state of Fe (iron) is +2 in K₄[Fe(CN)₆] and +3 in K₃[Fe(CN)₆], which means it is undergoing oxidation.\n\nThe oxidation state of C (carbon) is +4 in Fe(CN)₆⁴⁻ and +3 in Fe(CN)₆³⁻, which means it is undergoing reduction.\n\nTherefore, the element that undergoes neither oxidation nor reduction in this reaction is potassium (K).\n (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','mcq','K','Fe','H','C','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220122,'chemistry','2K₄[Fe(CN)₆](aq) + H₂O₂ (aq) →2K₃[Fe(CN)₆](aq) + 2KOH (aq) Which element undergoes reduction in this reaction?','O','2K₄[Fe(CN)₆](aq) + H₂O₂ (aq) →2K₃[Fe(CN)₆](aq) + 2KOH (aq) This reaction is interpreted as oxidation due to the removal of electropositive element potassium from potassium ferrocyanide before it changes to potassium ferricyanide. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','mcq','K','Fe','O','C','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220123,'chemistry','The term “oxidation” is defined as the addition of _____/electronegative element to a substance or removal of hydrogen/ electropositive element from a substance.','oxygen','the term “oxidation” is defined as the addition of oxygen/electronegative element to a substance or removal of hydrogen/ electropositive element from a substance. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','fillup','ox,yg,en,ide,hal',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220124,'chemistry','The term reduction has been broadened these days to include removal of ______/electronegative element from a substance or addition of hydrogen/ electropositive element to a substance.','oxygen','The term reduction has been broadened these days to include removal of oxygen/electronegative element from a substance or addition of hydrogen/ electropositive element to a substance. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','fillup','ox,yg,en,ide,hal',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
