(220129,'chemistry','2HgCl₂ (aq) + SnCl₂ (aq) → ____ (s)+SnCl₄ (aq)','Hg₂Cl₂','2HgCl₂ (aq) + SnCl₂ (aq) → Hg₂Cl₂ (s)+SnCl₄ (aq) (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','fillup','Hg₂,Cl₂,Cl,Cl₃',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220130,'chemistry','2HgCl₂ (aq) + SnCl₂ (aq) → Hg₂Cl₂ (s)+ ___ (aq)','SnCl₄','2HgCl₂ (aq) + SnCl₂ (aq) → Hg₂Cl₂ (s)+SnCl₄ (aq) (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','fillup','Sn,Cl₄,Cl₃,Sn₃',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(31405,'chemistry','Which of the following is the most acidic?','Cl₂O₇','The normal oxide formed by the element on extreme left is the most basic (ne.g., Na₂O), whereas that formed by the element on extreme right is the most acidic (ne.g., Cl₂O₇). \n\nOxides of elements in the centre are amphoteric (ne.g., Al₂O₃, As₂O₃) or neutral (ne.g., CO, NO, N₂O).\n\nAmphoteric oxides behave as acidic with bases and as basic with acids, whereas neutral oxides have no acidic or basic properties \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 94; Paragraph no. 2 )','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Periodic Trends and Chemical reactivity',NULL,'1','mcq','Cl₂O₇','Na₂O','CO₂','NO₂','NEW','11-094','11-094','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/31405q.png'),
(220131,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\nAssertion : The word “redox” was coined for this class of chemical reactions Reason : Oxidation and reduction always occur simultaneously in a reaction','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','It was soon realised that oxidation and reduction always occur simultaneously (as will be apparent by re-examining all the equations given above), hence, the word “redox” was coined for this class of chemical reactions (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The statement of the Assertion is false but the Reason is true','Both the statements are false','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220132,'chemistry','In the reaction given below, identify the species undergoing reduction : H₂S (g) + Cl₂ (g) → 2 HCl (g) + S (s)','Cl₂','H₂S is oxidised because a more electronegative element, chlorine is added to hydrogen (or a more electropositive element, hydrogen has been removed from S). Chlorine is reduced due to addition of hydrogen to it. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Problem 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','mcq','H₂S','Cl₂','HCl','S','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220133,'chemistry','In the reaction given below, identify the species undergoing oxidation : H₂S (g) + Cl₂ (g) → 2 HCl (g) + S (s)','H₂S','H₂S is oxidised because a more electronegative element, chlorine is added to hydrogen (or a more electropositive element, hydrogen has been removed from S). Chlorine is reduced due to addition of hydrogen to it. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Problem 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','mcq','H₂S','Cl₂','HCl','S','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220134,'chemistry','In the reaction given below, identify the species undergoing reduction : 3Fe₃O₄ (s) + 8 Al (s) → 9 Fe (s) + 4Al₂O₃ (s)','Fe₃O₄','Aluminium is oxidised because oxygen is added to it. Ferrous ferric oxide is reduced because oxygen has been removed from it. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Problem 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','mcq','Fe₃O₄','Al','Al₂O₃','Fe','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220135,'chemistry','In the reaction given below, identify the species undergoing oxidation : 3Fe₃O₄ (s) + 8 Al (s) → 9 Fe (s) + 4Al₂O₃ (s)','Al','Aluminium is oxidised because oxygen is added to it. Ferrous ferric oxide is reduced because oxygen has been removed from it. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Problem 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','mcq','Fe₃O₄','Al','Al₂O₃','Fe','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220136,'chemistry','In the reaction given below, identify the species undergoing reduction: 2 Na (s) + H₂ (g) → 2 NaH (s)','H₂','With the careful application of the concept of electronegativity only we may infer that sodium is oxidised and hydrogen is reduced. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Problem 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','mcq','Na','H₂','NaH','Cannot be determined','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220137,'chemistry','In the reaction given below, identify the species undergoing oxidation: 2 Na (s) + H₂ (g) → 2 NaH (s)','Na','With the careful application of the concept of electronegativity only we may infer that sodium is oxidised and hydrogen is reduced. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Problem 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','mcq','Na','H₂','NaH','Cannot be determined','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220201,'chemistry','2Na(s) + Cl₂(g) → 2____ (s)','NaCl','2Na(s) + Cl₂(g) → 2NaCl(s) (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265; Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','fillup','Na,Cl,NaO',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(220202,'chemistry','4Na(s) + O₂(g) → 2_____(s)','Na₂O','4Na(s) + O₂(g) → 2Na₂O(s) (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265; Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','fillup','Na₂O,Na,O₃',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(220203,'chemistry','2Na(s) + S(s) → ____(s)','Na₂S','2Na(s) + S(s) →NaCl(s) (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265; Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','fillup','Na₂,S,Na₂,',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(220204,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\nAssertion : Each of the partial steps of a redox reaction is called a half reaction, which explicitly shows involvement of electrons. Reason : Sum of the half reactions gives the overall reaction.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','Each of the above steps is called a half reaction, which explicitly shows involvement of electrons. Sum of the half reactions gives the overall reaction (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265; Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The statement of the Assertion is false but the Reason is true','Both the statements are false','NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(220205,'chemistry','Half reactions that involve loss of electrons are called _____ reactions.','oxidation','Half reactions that involve loss of electrons are called oxidation reactions. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265; Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','fillup','ox,id,at,io,n,yge,ide',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(220206,'chemistry','The half reactions that involve gain of electrons are called ______ reactions.','reduction','The half reactions that involve gain of electrons are called reduction reactions. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265; Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','fillup','re,du,ct,ion,ce,action',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(220207,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\n\nAssertion : Half reactions that involve loss of electrons are called oxidation reactions\n\nReason : Half reactions that involve gain of electrons are called reduction reactions.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','Reactions 8.12 to 8.14 suggest that half reactions that involve loss of electrons are called oxidation reactions. Similarly, the half reactions that involve gain of electrons are called reduction reactions (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265; Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The statement of the Assertion is false but the Reason is true','Both the statements are false','NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(220208,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\nAssertion : Half reactions that involve loss of electrons are called reduction reactions. Reason : Half reactions that involve gain of electrons are called oxidation reactions.','Both the statements are false','Reactions 8.12 to 8.14 suggest that half reactions that involve loss of electrons are called oxidation reactions. Similarly, the half reactions that involve gain of electrons are called reduction reactions (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265; Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The statement of the Assertion is false but the Reason is true','Both the statements are false','NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(220209,'chemistry','The new way of defining oxidation and reduction has been achieved only by establishing a correlation between the behaviour of species as per the ____ idea and their interplay in electron-transfer change.','classical','The new way of defining oxidation and reduction has been achieved only by establishing a correlation between the behaviour of species as per the classical idea and their interplay in electron-transfer change. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265; Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','fillup','cl,as,si,cal,li,cone',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(220210,'chemistry','Acceptor of electrons is called ___ agent','oxidising','Oxidising agent : Acceptor of electron(s). (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265; Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','fillup','ox,id,is,in,g,ide,ygen',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(220211,'chemistry','Donor of electrons is called ____ agent','reducing','Reducing agent : Donor of electron(s) (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265; Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','fillup','re,du,ci,ng,ac,tion',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(220212,'chemistry','Which entity gets oxidised in 2 Na(s) + H₂(g) → 2 NaH?','Na','This splitting of the reaction under examination into two half reactions automatically reveals that here sodium is oxidised and hydrogen is reduced, therefore, the complete reaction is a redox change (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 266; Problem 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','mcq','Na','H₂','NaH','Na⁺','NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(220213,'chemistry','Which entity gets reduced in 2 Na(s) + H₂(g) → 2 NaH?','H₂','This splitting of the reaction under examination into two half reactions automatically reveals that here sodium is oxidised and hydrogen is reduced, therefore, the complete reaction is a redox change (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 266; Problem 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','mcq','Na','H₂','NaH','Na⁺','NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(220214,'chemistry','What is the color of metallic copper?','Reddish','You may notice that the strip becomes coated with reddish metallic copper\nCu is Red because its band gap is 1.8-2.0 eV.\n (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 266; Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','mcq','Reddish','Bluish','Yellowish','Greenish','NEW','11-266','11-238',NULL),
(220215,'chemistry','What is the color of Copper Nitrate solution?','Blue','You may notice that the strip becomes coated with reddish metallic copper and the blue colour of the solution disappears. \nCopper(II) nitrate describes any member of the family of inorganic compounds with the formula Cu(NO3)2(H2O)x. The hydrates are blue solids. Anhydrous copper nitrate forms blue-green crystals and sublimes in a vacuum at 150-200 °C\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 266; Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','mcq','Red','Blue','Green','Yellow','NEW','11-266','11-238',NULL),
(220216,'chemistry','What is the color of Zinc Nitrate solution?','Colourless','Formation of Zn²⁺ ions among the products can easily be judged when the blue colour of the solution due to Cu²⁺ has disappeared. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 266; Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','mcq','Red','Blue','Green','Colourless','NEW','11-266','11-238',NULL),
(220217,'chemistry','What is the color of ZnS?','White','If hydrogen sulphide gas is passed through the colourless solution containing Zn²⁺ ions, appearance of white zinc sulphide, ZnS can be seen on making the solution alkaline with ammonia. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 266; Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','mcq','Black','White','Yellowish','Green','NEW','11-266','11-238',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(220218,'chemistry','Zn(s) + Cu²⁺ (aq) → Zn²⁺ (aq) + Cu(s). Which species gets oxidised?','Zn','In reaction (8.15), zinc has lost electrons to form Zn²⁺ and, therefore, zinc is oxidised. Evidently, now if zinc is oxidised, releasing electrons, something must be reduced, accepting the electrons lost by zinc. Copper ion is reduced by gaining electrons from the zinc. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 266; Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','mcq','Zn','Zn²⁺','Cu','Cu²⁺','NEW','11-266','11-238',NULL),
(220219,'chemistry','Zn(s) + Cu²⁺ (aq) → Zn²⁺ (aq) + Cu(s). Which species gets reduced?','Cu²⁺','In reaction (8.15), zinc has lost electrons to form Zn²⁺ and, therefore, zinc is oxidised. Evidently, now if zinc is oxidised, releasing electrons, something must be reduced, accepting the electrons lost by zinc. Copper ion is reduced by gaining electrons from the zinc. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 266; Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','mcq','Zn','Zn²⁺','Cu','Cu²⁺','NEW','11-266','11-238',NULL),
(220220,'chemistry','What is the color of CuS?','Black','No visible reaction is noticed and attempt to detect the presence of Cu2+ ions by passing H2S gas through the solution to produce the black colour of cupric sulphide, CuS, does not succeed. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 266; Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','mcq','Black','White','Yellowish','Green','NEW','11-266','11-238',NULL),
(220221,'chemistry','Zn(s) + Cu²⁺ (aq) → Zn²⁺ (aq) + Cu(s) In this reaction, state of equilibrium favours whom?','Products','We thus conclude that the state of equilibrium for the reaction (8.15) greatly favours the products over the reactants (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 266; Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','mcq','Reactants','Products','Acid','Base','NEW','11-266','11-238',NULL),
(220222,'chemistry','Cu(s) + 2Ag⁺(aq) → Cu²⁺(aq) + 2Ag(s). Which species gets oxidised?','Cu','Here, Cu(s) is oxidised to Cu²⁺(aq) and Ag⁺ (aq) is reduced to Ag(s). Equilibrium greatly favours the products Cu²⁺(aq) and Ag(s). (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 266; Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','mcq','Cu','Ag⁺','Cu²⁺','Ag','NEW','11-266','11-238',NULL),
(220223,'chemistry','Cu(s) + 2Ag⁺(aq) → Cu²⁺(aq) + 2Ag(s). Which species gets reduced?','Ag⁺','Here, Cu(s) is oxidised to Cu²⁺(aq) and Ag⁺ (aq) is reduced to Ag(s). Equilibrium greatly favours the products Cu²⁺(aq) and Ag(s). (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 266; Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','mcq','Cu','Ag⁺','Cu²⁺','Ag','NEW','11-266','11-238',NULL),
(220224,'chemistry','Cu(s) + 2Ag⁺(aq) → Cu²⁺(aq) + 2Ag(s).In this reaction, state of equilibrium favours whom?','Products','Here, Cu(s) is oxidised to Cu²⁺(aq) and Ag⁺ (aq) is reduced to Ag(s). Equilibrium greatly favours the products Cu²⁺(aq) and Ag(s). (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 266; Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','mcq','Reactants','Products','Acid','Base','NEW','11-266','11-238',NULL),
(220225,'chemistry','Co(s) + Ni²⁺(aq) → Co²⁺(aq) + Ni(s). Which species gets oxidised?','Co','This can be seen from the fact that oxidation state of Co goes from 0 to +2 (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 266; Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','mcq','Co','Co²⁺','Ni²⁺','Ni','NEW','11-266','11-238',NULL),
(220226,'chemistry','Co(s) + Ni²⁺(aq) → Co²⁺(aq) + Ni(s). Which species gets reduced?','Ni²⁺','This can be seen from the fact that oxidation state of Ni goes from +2 to 0 (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 266; Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','mcq','Co','Co²⁺','Ni²⁺','Ni','NEW','11-266','11-238',NULL),
(220227,'chemistry','Co(s) + Ni²⁺(aq) → Co²⁺(aq) + Ni(s). In this reaction, state of equilibrium favours whom?','Neither reactant nor product','At equilibrium, chemical tests reveal that both Ni²⁺(aq) and Co²⁺(aq) are present at moderate concentrations. In this case, neither the reactants [Co(s) and Ni²⁺(aq)] nor the products [Co²⁺(aq) and Ni (s)] are greatly favoured.','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','mcq','Reactants','Products','Acid','Neither reactant nor product','NEW','11-266','11-238',NULL),
(220228,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\nAssertion : The competition for release of electrons incidently reminds us of the competition for release of protons among acids. Reason : The similarity suggests that we might develop a table in which metals and their ions are listed on the basis of their tendency to release electrons just as we do in the case of acids to indicate the strength of the acids.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','This competition for release of electrons incidently reminds us of the competition for release of protons among acids. The similarity suggests that we might develop a table in which metals and their ions are listed on the basis of their tendency to release electrons just as we do in the case of acids to indicate the strength of the acids. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267; Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The statement of the Assertion is false but the Reason is true','Both the statements are false','NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(220229,'chemistry','What is the correct electron releasing order?','Zn>Cu>Ag','By comparison we have come to know that zinc releases electrons to copper and copper releases electrons to silver and, therefore, the electron releasing tendency of the metals is in the order: Zn>Cu>Ag. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267; Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','mcq','Zn>Cu>Ag','Zn>Ag>CU','Cu>Zn>Ag','Cu>Ag>Zn','NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(220230,'chemistry','The competition for electrons between various metals helps us to design a class of cells, named as _____ cells in which the chemical reactions become the source of electrical energy.','Galvanic','The competition for electrons between various metals helps us to design a class of cells, named as Galvanic cells in which the chemical reactions become the source of electrical energy. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267; Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','fillup','Ga,lv,an,ic,li,um,',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(220231,'chemistry','The competition for electrons between various metals helps us to design a class of cells, named as Galvanic cells in which the ____ reactions become the source of electrical energy.','chemical','The competition for electrons between various metals helps us to design a class of cells, named as Galvanic cells in which the chemical reactions become the source of electrical energy. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267; Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','fillup','ch,em,ic,al,lo,ri,ne',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(220232,'chemistry','The competition for electrons between various metals helps us to design a class of cells, named as Galvanic cells in which the chemical reactions become the source of _____ energy.','electrical','The competition for electrons between various metals helps us to design a class of cells, named as Galvanic cells in which the chemical reactions become the source of electrical energy. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267; Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','fillup','el,ec,tr,ic,al,ode',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(220233,'chemistry','ZnS can be seen on making the solution alkaline with ____.','ammonia','ZnS can be seen on making the solution alkaline with ammonia. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267; Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electron Transfer',NULL,'1','fillup','am,mo,nia,hy,dr,oxi,de',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(220318,'chemistry','Oxidation number of atom in K⁺ is ____','1','For ions composed of only one atom, the oxidation number is equal to the charge on the ion. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220319,'chemistry','Oxidation number of atom in Rb⁺ is ____','1','For ions composed of only one atom, the oxidation number is equal to the charge on the ion. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220320,'chemistry','Oxidation number of atom in Cs⁺ is ____','1','For ions composed of only one atom, the oxidation number is equal to the charge on the ion. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220321,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of aluminium in its compounds ?','3','Aluminium is regarded to have an oxidation number of +3 in all its compounds. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'1','mcq','3','4','-3','-4','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(2203220,'chemistry','The oxidation number of oxygen in most compounds is ___','-2','The oxidation number of oxygen in most compounds is –2 (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'1','mcq','-2','2','-4','-1','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220323,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of oxygen in H₂O₂','-1','In peroxides, each oxygen atom is assigned an oxidation number of –1 (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'1','mcq','-1','-2','-3','0','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220324,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of oxygen in Na₂O₂','-1','In peroxides, each oxygen atom is assigned an oxidation number of –1 (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'1','mcq','-1','-2','-3','0','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220325,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of oxygen in BaO₂','-1','In peroxides, each oxygen atom is assigned an oxidation number of –1 (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'1','mcq','-1','-2','-3','0','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220326,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of oxygen in peroxides','-1','In peroxides, each oxygen atom is assigned an oxidation number of –1 (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'1','mcq','-1','-2','-3','0','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220327,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of oxygen in KO₂','-1/2','In superoxides, each oxygen atom is assigned an oxidation number of –(½) (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'1','mcq','-1','-1/2','-3/4','-2','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220328,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of oxygen in superoxides','-1/2','In superoxides, each oxygen atom is assigned an oxidation number of –(½) (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'1','mcq','-1','-1/2','-3/4','-2','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220329,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of oxygen in RbO₂','-1/2','In superoxides, each oxygen atom is assigned an oxidation number of –(½) (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'1','mcq','-1','-1/2','-3/4','-2','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220330,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of oxygen in CsO₂','-1/2','In superoxides, each oxygen atom is assigned an oxidation number of –(½) (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'1','mcq','-1','-1/2','-3/4','-2','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220331,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of oxygen in OF₂','2','In such compounds e.g., oxygen difluoride (OF₂) and dioxygen difluoride (O₂F₂), the oxygen is assigned an oxidation number of +2 and +1, respectively. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'1','mcq','1','-1/2','-3/4','2','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220332,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of oxygen in O₂F₂','1','In such compounds e.g., oxygen difluoride (OF₂) and dioxygen difluoride (O₂F₂), the oxygen is assigned an oxidation number of +2 and +1, respectively. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'1','mcq','1','-1/2','-3/4','2','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220333,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of hydrogen in NaH','-1','The oxidation number of hydrogen is +1, except when it is bonded to metals in binary compounds (that is compounds containing two elements). (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'1','mcq','-1','-2','-3','1','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220334,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of hydrogen in LiH','-1','The oxidation number of hydrogen is +1, except when it is bonded to metals in binary compounds (that is compounds containing two elements). (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'1','mcq','-1','-2','-3','1','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
