(220349,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\nAssertion : Cl, Br, and I have an oxidation number of –1, when they occur as halide ions in their compounds. Reason : Chlorine, bromine and iodine when combined with oxygen, for example in oxoacids and oxoanions, have positive oxidation numbers','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','Cl, Br, and I have an oxidation number of –1, when they occur as halide ions in their compounds. Chlorine, bromine and iodine when combined with oxygen, for example in oxoacids and oxoanions, have positive oxidation numbers. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The statement of the Assertion is false but the Reason is true','Both the statements are false','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220350,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\nAssertion : The algebraic sum of the oxidation number of all the atoms in a compound must be zero. Reason : In polyatomic ion, the algebraic sum of all the oxidation numbers of atoms of the ion must equal the charge on the ion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The algebraic sum of the oxidation number of all the atoms in a compound must be zero. In polyatomic ion, the algebraic sum of all the oxidation numbers of atoms of the ion must equal the charge on the ion. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The statement of the Assertion is false but the Reason is true','Both the statements are false','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220351,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\nAssertion : In polyatomic ion, the algebraic sum of all the oxidation numbers of atoms of the ion must equal the charge on the ion. Reason :The algebraic sum of the oxidation number of all the atoms in a compound must be zero.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The algebraic sum of the oxidation number of all the atoms in a compound must be zero. In polyatomic ion, the algebraic sum of all the oxidation numbers of atoms of the ion must equal the charge on the ion. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The statement of the Assertion is false but the Reason is true','Both the statements are false','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220352,'chemistry','What is the sum of oxidation number of three oxygen atoms and one carbon atom in the carbonate ion','-2','The sum of oxidation number of three oxygen atoms and one carbon atom in the carbonate ion is -2 (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'1','mcq','-1','-2','1','2','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220353,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\nAssertion : metallic elements have positive oxidation numbers Reason : non metallic elements have positive or negative oxidation numbers','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','By the application of above rules, we can find out the oxidation number of the desired element in a molecule or in an ion. It is clear that the metallic elements have positive oxidation number and nonmetallic elements have positive or negative oxidation number (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The statement of the Assertion is false but the Reason is true','Both the statements are false','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(31406,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a neutral oxide?','NO₂','The normal oxide formed by the element on extreme left is the most basic (ne.g., Na₂O), whereas that formed by the element on extreme right is the most acidic (ne.g., Cl₂O₇). \n\nOxides of elements in the centre are amphoteric (ne.g., Al₂O₃, As₂O₃) or neutral (ne.g., CO, NO, N₂O).\n\nAmphoteric oxides behave as acidic with bases and as basic with acids, whereas neutral oxides have no acidic or basic properties \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 94; Paragraph no. 2 )','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Periodic Trends and Chemical reactivity',NULL,'1','mcq','CO','NO','NO₂','N₂O','NEW','11-094','11-094',NULL),
(220354,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\nAssertion : The highest value of oxidation number exhibited by an atom of an element generally increases across the period in the periodic table Reason: The highest oxidation number of a representative element is the group number for the first two groups and the group number minus 10 (following the long form of periodic table) for the other groups.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The atoms of transition elements usually display several positive oxidation states. The highest oxidation number of a representative element is the group number for the first two groups and the group number minus 10 (following the long form of periodic table) for the other groups. Thus, it implies that the highest value of oxidation number exhibited by an atom of an element generally increases across the period in the periodic table. In the third period, the highest value of oxidation number changes from 1 to 7 as indicated below in the compounds of the elements (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The statement of the Assertion is false but the Reason is true','Both the statements are false','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220355,'chemistry','In the third period, the highest value of oxidation number changes from','1-7','In the third period, the highest value of oxidation number changes from 1 to 7 as indicated below in the compounds of the elements. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'1','mcq','1-7','1-5','1-3','1-2','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
