(220434,'chemistry','Assertion : Chemical means are available to oxidise Cl⁻, Br⁻ and I⁻ but not F⁻ Reason : Fluorine is the strongest oxidising agent','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','Fluorine is the strongest oxidising agent; there is no way to convert F⁻ ions to F₂ by chemical means. The only way to achieve F₂ from F⁻ is to oxidise electrolytically (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 271; Paragraph 8.3.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Types of Redox Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The statement of the Assertion is true but the Reason is false','Both the statements are false','NEW','11-271','11-243',NULL),
(220435,'chemistry','Assertion : The only way to make F₂ from F⁻ is by electrolytic methods Reason : Fluorine is the strongest oxidising agent and chemical conversion is not possible','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','Fluorine is the strongest oxidising agent; there is no way to convert F⁻ ions to F₂ by chemical means. The only way to achieve F₂ from F⁻ is to oxidise electrolytically (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 271; Paragraph 8.3.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Types of Redox Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The statement of the Assertion is true but the Reason is false','Both the statements are false','NEW','11-271','11-243',NULL),
(220436,'chemistry','In a disproportionation reaction','an element in one oxidation state is simultaneously oxidised and reduced.','In a disproportionation reaction an element in one oxidation state is simultaneously oxidised and reduced.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272; 4. Disproportionation reactions)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Types of Redox Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','an element in one oxidation state is simultaneously oxidised and reduced.','two reactants, each containing the same element but with a different oxidation number, form a product in which the elements involved reach the same oxidation number.','other name of an ordinary redox reaction','an element is oxidised while reducing its isotope','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(220437,'chemistry','One of the reacting substances in a disproportionation reaction always contains an element that can exist in at least ____ oxidation states.','3','One of the reacting substances in a disproportionation reaction always contains an element that can exist in at least three oxidation states.','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Types of Redox Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','3','4','2','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(220438,'chemistry','Mark the false statement with respect to disproportionation reaction.','Formation of hydrogen peroxide from water and oxygen is an example.','The element in the form of reacting substance is in the intermediate oxidation state; and both higher and lower oxidation states of that element are formed in the reaction. The decomposition of hydrogen peroxide is a familiar example of the reaction, where oxygen experiences disproportionation. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272; 4. Disproportionation reactions)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Types of Redox Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','The element in the form of reacting substance is in the intermediate oxidation state.','Higher oxidation state of reactant is formed in product.','lower oxidation state of reactant is formed in product.','Formation of hydrogen peroxide from water and oxygen is an example.','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(220439,'chemistry','Mark the false statement.','Phosphorous undergo disproportionation in the acidic medium','Phosphorous, sulphur and chlorine undergo disproportionation in the alkaline. This departure shown by fluorine is not surprising for us as we know the limitation of fluorine that, being the most electronegative element, it cannot exhibit any positive oxidation state. This means that among halogens, fluorine does not show a disproportionation tendency. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272; 4. Disproportionation reactions)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Types of Redox Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Phosphorous undergo disproportionation in the acidic medium','Fluorine being the most electronegative element, it cannot exhibit any positive oxidation state.','sulphur undergo disproportionation in the alkaline medium','Chlorine undergo disproportionation in the alkaline medium','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(220440,'chemistry','Which halogen can’t undergo disproportionation?','F','This departure shown by fluorine is not surprising for us as we know the limitation of fluorine that, being the most electronegative element, it cannot exhibit any positive oxidation state. This means that among halogens, fluorine does not show a disproportionation tendency. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272; 4. Disproportionation reactions)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Types of Redox Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Cl','F','Br','I','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(220441,'chemistry','S₈ (s)+ OH⁻(aq) → (Give products)','S²⁻ (aq) + S₂O₃²⁻ (aq) + H₂O(l)','S₈ (s)+ 12OH⁻(aq) → 4S²⁻ (aq) + 2S₂O₃²⁻ (aq) + 6H₂O(l) (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272; 4. Disproportionation reactions)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Types of Redox Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','S²⁻ (aq) + S₂O₃²⁻ (aq) + H₂O(l)','S²⁻ (aq) + SO4²⁻ (aq) + H₂O(l)','S²⁻ (s) + 2S₂O₃²⁻ (aq)','4S²⁻ (aq) +6H₂O(l)','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(220442,'chemistry','P₄(s) + 3OH⁻ (aq)+ 3H₂O(l) → give products','PH₃(g) + 3H₂PO₂⁻ (aq)','P₄(s) + 3OH⁻ (aq)+ 3H₂O(l) → PH₃(g) + 3H₂PO₂⁻ (aq) (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272; 4. Disproportionation reactions)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Types of Redox Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','PH₃(g) + 3H₂PO₂⁻ (aq)','2PH₃(g) + 2H₂PO₃⁻ (aq)','3PH₃(g) + H₂PO₄⁻ (aq)','H₃PO₄','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(220443,'chemistry','Mark the false reaction.','F₂ + 2OH⁻ → FO⁻ + F⁻ + H₂O','It is of interest to mention here that whereas bromine and iodine follow the same trend as exhibited by chlorine in reaction (8.48).fluorine shows deviation from this behaviour when it reacts with alkali.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272; 4. Disproportionation reactions)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Types of Redox Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cl₂(g) + 2OH⁻ (aq) → ClO⁻ (aq) + Cl⁻ (aq) + H₂O (l)','Br₂ + 2OH⁻ (aq) → BrO⁻ (aq) + Br⁻ (aq) + H₂O (l)','I₂ + 2OH⁻ → lO⁻ + I⁻ + H₂O','F₂ + 2OH⁻ → FO⁻ + F⁻ + H₂O','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(220444,'chemistry','Which of the following is not formed in the following reaction?\n2F₂ + 2OH⁻ →','HOF','2F₂ + 2OH⁻ → 2F⁻ + OF₂ + H₂O\nIn all its compounds, fluorine has an\noxidation number of –1 \nFlourine is very electronegative and it cannot exist in positive oxidation states in its compounds . in oxyacids of halogens ,oxygen carries a negative charge while the halogen carries a positive charge and here flourine has +1 which is very unstable\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 260; Paragrahg 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Types of Redox Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','F⁻','OF₂','H₂O','HOF','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(220445,'chemistry','Classify the reaction\nN₂ + O₂ → 2NO','Combination','the compound nitric oxide is formed by the combination of the elemental substances, nitrogen and oxygen; therefore, this is an example of combination redox reactions.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272; Problem 2.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Types of Redox Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Combination','Decomposition','Displacement','Disproportionation','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(220446,'chemistry','Classify the reaction\n 2Pb(NO₃)₂(s) → 2PbO(s) + 4 NO₂ (g) + O₂(g)','Decomposition','involves the breaking down of lead nitrate into three components; therefore, this is categorised under decomposition redox reaction.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272; Problem 2.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Types of Redox Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Combination','Decomposition','Displacement','Disproportionation','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(220447,'chemistry','Classify the reaction\n NaH(s) + H₂O(l) → NaOH(aq) + H₂ (g)','Displacement','hydrogen of water has been displaced by hydride ion into dihydrogen gas. Therefore, this may be called as displacement redox reaction.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272; Problem 2.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Types of Redox Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Combination','Decomposition','Displacement','Disproportionation','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(220448,'chemistry','Classify the reaction\n 2NO₂ (g) + 2OH⁻ (aq) → NO₂⁻(aq) + NO₃⁻ (aq)+H₂O(l)','Disproportionation','Is an example of disproportionation redox reaction.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272; Problem 2.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Types of Redox Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Combination','Decomposition','Displacement','Disproportionation','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(220449,'chemistry','Which of the following species, do not show disproportionation reaction?','ClO₄⁻','Among the oxoanions of chlorine listed above, ClO₄⁻ does not disproportionate because in this oxoanion chlorine is present in its highest oxidation state that is, +7.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272; Problem 8.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Types of Redox Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','ClO⁻','ClO₂⁻','ClO₃⁻','ClO₄⁻','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(220450,'chemistry','ClO⁻ disproportionates to','Cl⁻ and ClO₃⁻','3ClO⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + ClO₃⁻ \n6ClO₂⁻→ 4ClO₃⁻ + 2Cl⁻\n4ClO₃⁻ → Cl⁻ + 3ClO₄⁻ \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272; Problem 8.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Types of Redox Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cl⁻ and ClO₃⁻','Cl⁻ and ClO4⁻','Cl⁻ and ClO4⁻','Cl₂ and ClO₃⁻','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(220451,'chemistry','ClO₂⁻ disproportionates to','Cl⁻ and ClO₃⁻','4ClO⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + ClO₃⁻ \n6ClO₂⁻→ 4ClO₃⁻ + 2Cl⁻\n4ClO₃⁻ → Cl⁻ + 3ClO₄⁻ \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272; Problem 8.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Types of Redox Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cl⁻ and ClO₃⁻','Cl⁻ and ClO₄⁻','Cl₂ and ClO₄⁻','Cl₂ and ClO₃⁻','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(220452,'chemistry','ClO₃⁻ disproportionates to','Cl⁻ and ClO₄⁻','5ClO⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + ClO₃⁻ \n6ClO₂⁻→ 4ClO₃⁻ + 2Cl⁻\n4ClO₃⁻ → Cl⁻ + 3ClO₄⁻ \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272; Problem 8.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Types of Redox Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cl⁻ and ClO₃⁻','Cl⁻ and ClO₄⁻','Cl₂ and ClO₄⁻','Cl₂ and ClO₃⁻','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(220606,'chemistry','How many of the following are true for redox balancing through oxidation number method?\n1. if the reaction is taking place in water, add H⁺ or OH⁻ ions to the expression on the appropriate side so that the total ionic charges of reactants and products are equal.\n2. If the reaction is carried out in acidic solution, use H⁺ ions in the equation.\n3. If in basic solution, use OH⁻ ions. \n4.Make the numbers of hydrogen atoms in the expression on the two sides equal by adding water molecules to the reactants or products.','4','Ascertain the involvement of ions if the reaction is taking place in water, add H⁺ or OH⁻ ions to the expression on the appropriate side so that the total ionic charges of reactants and products are equal. If the reaction is carried out in acidic solution, use H ions in the equation; if in basic solution, use OH⁻ ions.   Make the numbers of hydrogen atoms in the expression on the two sides equal by adding water (H2O) molecules to the reactants or products. Now, also check the number of oxygen atoms. If there are the same number of oxygen atoms in the reactants and products. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 274; Paragraph 8.3.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Redox Balancing',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-274','11-246',NULL),
(220607,'chemistry','How many of the following are false with respect to redox balancing through half reaction method? \n1. For reactions occurring in acidic medium, add H₂O to balance O atoms and H⁺ to balance H atoms. \n2. Add electrons to one side of the half reaction to balance the charges. If need be, make the number of electrons equal in the two half reactions by multiplying one or both half reactions by appropriate number. \n3.For the reaction in a basic medium, for each H⁺ ion, add an equal number of OH⁻ ions to both sides of the equation. Where H⁺ and OH⁻ appear on the same side of the equation, combine these to give H₂O.','0','Balance the atoms other than O and H in each half reaction individually.  For reactions occurring in acidic  medium, add H₂O to balance O atoms and H⁺ to balance H atoms. For the reaction in a basic medium, first balance the atoms as is done in acidic medium. Then for each H⁺ ion, add an equal number of OH⁻ ions to both sides of the equation. Where H⁺ and OH⁻ appear on the same side of the equation, combine these to give H₂O. \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 276; Paragraph 8.3.2(b))','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Redox Balancing',NULL,'3','mcq','0','1','2','3','NEW','11-274','11-246',NULL),
(220608,'chemistry','Give the value of x, y and z respectively.\nxCr₂O₇⁻ (aq) + ySO₃²⁻ + zH⁺ (aq) → (2x)Cr³⁺ (aq) + ySO₄²⁻ (aq) +(z/2)H₂O','1,3,8','Cr₂O₇⁻ (aq) + 3SO₃²⁻ + 8H⁺ (aq) → 2Cr³⁺ (aq) + 3SO₄²⁻ (aq) + 4H₂O\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 274; Problem 8.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Redox Balancing',NULL,'3','mcq','1,3,8','2,3,4','2,3,8','1,1,4','NEW','11-275','11-247',NULL),
(220609,'chemistry','Permanganate ion reacts with bromide ion in basic medium to give manganese dioxide and bromate ion.\nGive the coefficients of MnO₄⁻, BrO₃⁻ and OH⁻ respectively.','2,1,2','2MnO₄⁻(aq) + Br⁻ (aq) + H₂O(l) → 2MnO₂(s) + BrO₃⁻ (aq) + 2OH⁻ (aq) \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 275; Problem 8.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Redox Balancing',NULL,'3','mcq','2,1,2','2,1,1','2,1,4','1,2,4','NEW','11-275','11-247',NULL),
(220610,'chemistry','xFe²⁺ (aq) + (y/2)Cr₂O₇²⁻ (aq) + zH⁺ (aq) → xFe³⁺ (aq) +yCr³⁺ (aq) + (z/2)H₂O(l)\nGive x, y and z respectively.','6,2,14','(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 276; 8.3.2(b))\n6Fe²⁺ (aq) + Cr₂O₇²⁻ (aq) + 14H⁺ (aq) → 6 Fe³⁺ (aq) +2Cr³⁺ (aq) + 7H₂O(l)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Redox Balancing',NULL,'3','mcq','3,4,7','6,2,14','6,2,7','3,2,7','NEW','11-276','11-248',NULL),
