(220721,'chemistry','What is salt bridge?','a U-tube containing a solution of potassium chloride or ammonium nitrate usually solidified by boiling with agar agar and later cooling to a jelly like substance','(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 278; Paragraph 8.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'2','mcq','a U-tube containing a solution of potassium chloride or ammonium nitrate usually solidified by boiling with agar agar and later cooling to a jelly like substance','a U-tube containing a solution of potassium chloride or ammonium nitrate usually solidified by freezing with agar agar and later cooling to a jelly like substance','a metallic wire that completes the circuit internally.','a bridge that allows the two solutions in a Daniell cell to mix and form an electric contact.','NEW','11-278','11-250',NULL),
(220722,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement.','The electricity from solution in one beaker to solution in the other beaker flows by the migration of electrons through the salt bridge.','The electricity from solution in one beaker to solution in the other beaker flows by the migration of ions through the salt bridge. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 278; Paragraph 8.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'2','mcq','In a Daniell cell if switch is off no reaction takes place.','In a Daniell cell, the two redox couples are represented as Zn²⁺/Zn and Cu²⁺/Cu.','The transfer of electrons does not take place directly from Zn to Cu but through the metallic wire connecting the two rods.','The electricity from solution in one beaker to solution in the other beaker flows by the migration of electrons through the salt bridge.','NEW','11-278','11-250',NULL),
(220723,'chemistry','Mark the false statement.','A positive standard electrode potential means that the electrode is a weaker oxidising agent than standard hydrogen electrode.','(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 278; Paragraph 8.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'2','mcq','The flow of current in a Daniell cell is possible only if there is a potential difference between the copper and zinc rods.','Potential associated with each electrode is known as electrode potential.','By convention, the standard electrode potential of hydrogen electrode is 0.00 volts.','A positive standard electrode potential means that the electrode is a weaker oxidising agent than standard hydrogen electrode.','NEW','11-278','11-250',NULL),
(220724,'chemistry','What is standard electrode potential?','If the concentration of each species taking part in the electrode reaction is unity (if any gas appears in the electrode reaction, it is confined to 1 atmospheric pressure) and further the reaction is carried out at 298K, then the potential of each electrode is said to be the Standard Electrode Potential.','(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 278; Paragraph 8.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'2','mcq','If the concentration of each species taking part in the electrode reaction is unity (if any gas appears in the electrode reaction, it is confined to 1 atmospheric pressure) and further the reaction is carried out at 298K, then the potential of each electrode is said to be the Standard Electrode Potential.','If the concentration of each species taking part in the electrode reaction is unity (if any gas appears in the electrode reaction, it is confined to 1 Pascal pressure) and further the reaction is carried out at 298K, then the potential of each electrode is said to be the Standard Electrode Potential.','If the concentration of each species taking part in the electrode reaction is unity (if any gas appears in the electrode reaction, it is confined to 1 atmospheric pressure), then the potential of each electrode at any temperature(as it is temperature independent) is said to be the Standard Electrode Potential.','If the concentration of each species taking part in the electrode reaction is unity (if any gas appears in the electrode reaction, it is confined to 1 atmospheric pressure) and further the reaction is carried out at 298⁰C, then the potential of each electrode is said to be the Standard Electrode Potential.','NEW','11-278','11-250',NULL),
(220725,'chemistry','Mark the false statement.','Fluoride ion is the strongest reducing agent.','(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 279; Table 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'2','mcq','The electrode potential value for each electrode process is a measure of the relative tendency of the active species in the process to remain in the oxidised/reduced form.','Salt Bridge provides an electric contact between the two solutions without allowing them to mix with each other.','In a Daniell cell, zinc and copper rods are connected by a metallic wire with a provision for an ammeter and a switch.','Fluoride ion is the strongest reducing agent.','NEW','11-279','11-251',NULL),
(220726,'chemistry','Mark the correct statement.','A positive standard electrode potential means that the electrode is a weaker reducing agent than standard hydrogen electrode.','(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 278; Paragraph 8.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'2','mcq','A positive standard electrode potential means that the electrode is a weaker oxidising agent than standard hydrogen electrode.','A positive standard electrode potential means that the electrode is a weaker reducing agent than standard hydrogen electrode.','A negative standard electrode potential means that the electrode is a weaker reducing agent than standard hydrogen electrode.','A negative standard electrode potential means that the electrode is a stronger oxidising agent than standard hydrogen electrode.','NEW','11-279','11-251',NULL),
