(220811,'chemistry','Which of the following is redox reaction?','Both oxidation and reduction','A  a redox reaction is a reaction involving both oxidation and reduction simultaneously.\n(NEET 1997)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Evaporation of H₂O','Both oxidation and reduction','H₂SO₄ with NaOH','In atmosphere O₃ from O₂ by lightning','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220812,'chemistry','Without losing its concentration, ZnCl₂ solution cannot be kept in contact with ____.','Al','In electrochemical series, Al is placed above Zn and all other are present below Zn, That means, Al can displace Zinc from ZnCl₂ solution. Hence, it cannot keep in contact with Al. \n2Al + 3ZnCl₂ → 2AlCl₃ + 3Zn\n(NEET 1998)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Pb','Al','Au','Ag','NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(220813,'chemistry','What is the change in oxidation number of carbon in the following reaction? \nCH₄(g) + 4Cl₂(g) → CCl₄(l) + 4HCl(g)','-4 to +4','The oxidation number of carbon in CH₄ is –4 while the oxidation number of carbon in CCl₄ is +4. Thus, the change in oxidation number of carbon in the given reaction is from –4 to +4.\n(NEET 2020) diagram(e220813)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','+4 to + 4','0 to + 4','-4 to +4','0 to -4','NEW','11-269','11-241',NULL),
(220814,'chemistry','The correct structure of tribromo octaoxide is: \ndiagram(220814)','(1)','Option (1) is the correct structure of tribromo octaoxide.\n(NEET 2019)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(1)','(2)','(3)','(4)','NEW','11-269','11-241','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/220814q.png'),
(220815,'chemistry','Hot concentrated sulphuric acid is a moderately strong oxidising agent. Which of the following reactions does not show oxidising behaviour?','CaF₂ + H₂SO₄ → CaSO₄ + 2HF','CaF₂ + H₂SO₄ → CaSO₄ + 2HF\nIn this reaction oxidation number of Ca,H,S and O was not changed (which means it is not a redox reaction) hence, this reaction does not show oxidising behaviour.\n(NEET-II 2016)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Cu + 2H₂SO₄ → CuSO₄ + SO₂ + 2H₂O','S + 2H₂SO₄ → 3SO₂ + 2H₂O','C + 2H₂SO₄ → CO₂ + 2SO₂ + 2H₂O','CaF₂ + H₂SO₄ → CaSO₄ + 2HF','NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(220816,'chemistry','The pair of compounds that can exist together is ____.','FeCl₂, SnCl₂','FeCl₂ and SnCl₂ are both reducing agents with low oxidation numbers. Hence, neither can reduce other.\n[NEET (2014)]','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','FeCl₃, SnCl₂','HgCl₂, SnCl₂','FeCl₂, SnCl₂','FeCl₃, KI','NEW','11-264','11-235',NULL),
(220817,'chemistry','A mixture of potassium chlorate, oxalic acid and sulphuric acid is heated. During the reaction which element undergoes maximum change in the oxidation number?','Cl','Maximum change occurs in oxidation number occurs in case of chlorine, i.e., from +5 to –1.\n[NEET (2012)] diagram(e220817)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','S','H','Cl','C','NEW','11-264','11-235',NULL),
(220818,'chemistry','Oxidation numbers of P in PO₄³⁻, of S in SO₄²⁻ and that of Cr in Cr₂O₇²⁻ are respectively _____.','+5, +6 and +6','Let the oxidation number of P in PO₄³⁻ be x. Therefore, x + 4(–2) = –3 , x = +5 \nLet the oxidation number of S in SO₄²⁻ be y. Therefore,  y + 4(–2) = –2 , y = +6 \nLet the oxidation number of Cr in Cr₂O₇²⁻ be z. Therefore, 2z + 7(–2) = –2 ,  z = +6\n[NEET (2009)]','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','+3, +6 and +5','+5, +3 and +6','–3, +6 and +6','+5, +6 and +6','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220819,'chemistry','Number of moles of MnO⁴⁻ required to oxidise one mole of ferrous oxalate completely in acidic medium will be ____.','0.4 moles','In acidic medium MnO⁴⁻ oxidises ferrous oxalate as follows:\n2MnO⁴⁻ + 16H⁺ + 5C₂O₄²⁻ → 2Mn²⁺ + 10CO₂+ 8H₂O\nAccording to the equation, 5 moles of oxalate ions are oxidised by 2 moles of MnO⁴⁻. Therefore, number of moles of MnO⁴⁻ required to oxidise one mole of ferrous oxalate: 2/5 = 0.4 moles.\n[NEET (2008)]','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','7.5 moles','0.2 moles','0.6 moles','0.4 moles','NEW','11-275','11-247',NULL),
(220820,'chemistry','Which is the best description of the behaviour of bromine in the reaction given below?\nH₂O + Br₂ → HOBr + HBr','Both oxidised and reduced','H₂O+Br₂ → HOBr+HBr \nIn the above reaction, the oxidation number of Br in Br₂ is Zero. It increases from zero to +1 in HOBr, and decreases from zero to –1 in HBr. Thus, Br₂ is oxidised as well as reduced. Hence, it is a redox reaction.\n[NEET (2004)]','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Proton acceptor only','Both oxidised and reduced','Oxidised only','Reduced only','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220821,'chemistry','The oxidation states of sulphur in the anions SO₃²⁻, S₂O₄²⁻ and S₂O₆²⁻ follow the order ____.','1-S₂O₄²⁻,2-SO₃²⁻,3-S₂O₆²⁻','The oxidation number of sulphur in S₂O₄²⁻: 2x−8=−2 ⇒ x=+3\nThe oxidation number of sulphur in SO₃²⁻ : x−6=−2 ⇒ x=+4  \nThe oxidation number of sulphur in S₂O₆²⁻: 2x−12=−2 ⇒ x=+5\nThus, Oxidation states follows the order : S₂O₄²⁻ < SO₃²⁻ < S₂O₆²⁻\n[NEET (2003)]','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','order','1,S₂O₄²⁻','2, SO₃²⁻','3,S₂O₆²⁻',NULL,'NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220822,'chemistry','Oxidation state of Fe in Fe₃O₄ is ____.','8/3','Let the oxidation state of Fe in Fe₃O₄ be x: \n3x + 4(–2) = 0 ⇒ x = 8/3\nThus, oxidation state of Fe in Fe₃O₄ is 8/3.\n[NEET (1999)]','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','5/4','4/5','3/2','8/3','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220823,'chemistry','Reaction of sodium thiosulphate with iodine gives ____.','tetrathionate ion','2Na₂S₂O₃ + I₂ →  Na₂S₄O₆(Sodium tetrathionate)  +  2NaI \n[NEET (1996)]','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','tetrathionate ion','sulphide ion','sulphate ion','sulphite ion.','NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(220824,'chemistry','The oxide, which cannot act as a reducing agent is ____.','CO₂','CO₂ cannot act as a reducing agent as carbon cannot have an oxidation state of more than +4.\n[NEET (1995)]','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CO₂','ClO₂','NO₂','SO₂','NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(220825,'chemistry','Which substance is serving as a reducing agent in the following reaction?\n14H⁺ + Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 3Ni → 7H₂O + 2Cr³⁺ + 3Ni²⁺','Ni','14H⁺ + Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 3Ni → 7H₂O + 2Cr³⁺ + 3Ni²⁺\nSince the oxidation number of Ni increases from 0 to 2, therefore Ni acts as a reducing agent.\n([NEET (1994)]','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','H+','Cr₂O₇ ²⁻','H₂O','Ni','NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(220826,'chemistry','The oxidation state of I in H₄IO₆⁻ is','7','Let the Oxidation state of I in H₄IO₆ be x. The oxidation states of H and O are +1 and -2 respectively. Since the atom as whole is single negatively charged, following equation holds true:  (4 × 1) + x + (6 × – 2) = – 1  ⇒  x = 12-4-2  ⇒ x = + 7\n[NEET (1994)]','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','-1','7','5','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220827,'chemistry','Consider the change in oxidation state of bromine corresponding to different emf values as shown in the given diagram.\nThen the species undergoing disproportionation is _____ (220827)','HBrO','A disproportion reaction is a reaction in which same species is oxidised as well as reduced.\nE°cell should be positive for a reaction to be spontaneous. E°cell  for the disproportionation of HBrO.\nHBrO → Br₂ ; E° = 1.595 V, Standard reduction potential (cathode)\nHBrO → BrO₃⁻ ; E°₂ = –1.5 V, Standard oxidation potential (anode)\nE°cell = Standard reduction potential (cathode) -  Standard oxidation potential (anode) = 1.595 – 1.5 = 0.095V \n(NEET 2018)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','BrO³⁻','BrO⁴⁻','Br₂','HBrO','NEW','11-278','11-250','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/220827q.png'),
(220828,'chemistry','Which of the following reactions is the metal\ndisplacement reaction ? Choose the right option.','Cr₂O₃ + 2Al → Al₂O₃ + 2Cr','Aluminium is more electropositive than Cr, so it displaced chromium from Cr₂O₃.\nCr₂O₃ + 2Al  → Al₂O₃  + 2Cr\nReactions (a) and (d) are examples of decomposition reactions. Reactions (b) and (c) are displacement reactions, but (c) is not a metal displacement reaction.\n(NEET 2021)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','2KClO₃ → 2KCl + 3O₂','Cr₂O₃ + 2Al → Al₂O₃ + 2Cr','Fe + 2HCl → FeCl₂ + H₂O','2Pb(NO₃)₂   → 2PbO + 4NO₂  + O₂','NEW','11-270','11-242',NULL),
(220829,'chemistry','At 298 K, the standard electrode potentials of Cu²⁺/ Cu, Zn²⁺/ Zn, Fe²⁺/ Fe and Ag⁺/Ag are 0.34 V, –0.76 V, –0.44 V and 0.80 V, respectively. On the basis of standard electrode potential, predict which of the following reaction cannot occur?','2CuSO₄(aq) + 2Ag(s) → 2Cu(s) + Ag₂SO₄(aq)','E°cell should be positive for a reaction to be spontaneous.\nE°cell = E°cathode - E°anode \nFor CuSO₄(aq) + Zn(s) → ZnSO₄(aq) + Cu(s)\nE°cell = –0.44 V – (–0.76 V) = 0.32V\nFor CuSO₄(aq) + Fe(s) → FeSO₄(aq) + Cu(s)    \nE°cell = 0.80V−(−0.44V) = 1.24V\nFor  FeSO₄(aq) + Zn(s) → ZnSO₄(aq) + Fe(s) \nE°cell = 0.34V−(−0.76V) = 1.1V \nFor 2CuSO₄(aq) + 2Ag(s) → 2Cu(s) + Ag₂SO₄(aq) \nE°cell = 0.34V - 0.80V  = –0.46V (negative value means that the reaction will not be spontaneous) \n(NEET 2022)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CuSO₄(aq) + Zn(s) → ZnSO₄(aq) + Cu(s)','CuSO₄(aq) + Fe(s) → FeSO₄(aq) + Cu(s)','FeSO₄(aq) + Zn(s) → ZnSO₄(aq) + Fe(s)','2CuSO₄(aq) + 2Ag(s) → 2Cu(s) + Ag₂SO₄(aq)','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(220830,'chemistry','From the following identify the reaction having the top position in the EMF series according to their electrode potential.\n1- Mg2+ + 2e- ---> Mg\n2- Fe2+ + 2e- ---> Fe\n3- Au3+ + 3e- ---> Au\n4- K+ + e- ----> K','3',NULL,'Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(220831,'chemistry','What is the change in the oxidation number of carbon in the following reaction\nCH4 + 4Cl2 ----> CCl4 + 4HCl','-4 to +4',NULL,'Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','0 to +4','-4 to +4','0 to -4','+4 to -4','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
