(220844,'chemistry','The oxidation numbers of A,B and C are +2, +5 and -2 rsvp, then possible formula of the compound is','A3(BC4)2','The net charge on molecule is zero.','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','A2(BC2)2','A3(BC4)2','A2(BC3)2','A3(B2C)2','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220845,'chemistry','aZn + bNO3- + cH+ -----> dNH4+  +eH2O  + fZn2+\nIn the above reaction the correct values of a,b,c,d,e and f rsvp are:','a = 4, b = 1, c = 10, d = 1, e = 3, f = 4','STEP 1:  Skeleton equation :  \nZn + NO−3 → Zn+2   +   NH+4\nOxidation half reaction : \nZn → Zn+2 + 2e−\nReduction half reaction: \nNO−3 + 8e− → NH+4\nAll atoms are balanced except H and O.\n\nSTEP 2:  Balancing of charge: (Acidic medium)\nOxidation half reaction : \nZn → Zn+2  + 2e−\nReduction half reaction: \nNO−3 + 8e− +10H+  →  NH+4  \n\nSTEP 3: Balancing of O atom and H atom : \nReduction half reaction: \nNO−3  +   8e−  + 10H+  →  NH+4  +    3H2O\n\nSTEP 4: Balancing of electrons gained and lost : \nOxidation half reaction : \nZn → Zn+2 + 2e− ) × 4  ...................(I)\nReduction half reaction:\nNO−3 + 8e− + 10H+ → NH+4  +  3H2O)  ×  1...............(II) \n\nSTEP 4: Final reaction : \n4 Zn +  NO−3  + 10H+ → NH+4 + 3H2O +  4Zn2+ By comparing it with given equation we can say that , a = 4, b = 1 , c= 10 , d = 1 , e =3  and f = 4','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a = 2, b = 4, c = 6, d = 8, e = 4, f = 2','a = 1, b = 4, c = 10, d = 3, e = 1, f = 4','a = 4, b = 1, c = 10, d = 1, e = 3, f = 4','a = 10, b = 4, c = 1, d = 3, e = 4, f = 2','NEW','11-263','11-235',NULL),
(220846,'chemistry','The standard electrode potential (E°) values of Al⁺³/Al, Ag⁺/Ag, K⁺/K and Cr⁺³/Cr are –1.66 V, 0.80 V, –2.93 V and –0.79 V, respectively. \n\nThe correct decreasing order of reducing power of the metal is','K > Al > Cr > Ag','A higher tendency to lose electrons will show higher reducing power. Secondly, as the standard reduction electrode potential value increases the tendency of reducing power decreases.','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ag > Cr > Al > K','K > Al > Cr > Ag','K > Al > Ag > Cr','Al > K > Ag > Cr','NEW','11-278','11-250',NULL),
(220847,'chemistry','Disproportionation reactions are:\n(a) 2Cu+ -----> Cu2+ + Cu\n(b) 3MnO4-2  + 4H+  --------> 2MnO4-  + MnO2  +  2H2O\n(c) 2KMnO4   ---------->   K2MnO4   +   MnO2  + O2\n(d) 2MnO4-   +  3Mn2+   +     2H2O   -------> 5MnO2  +   4H+','(a) and (b) only','In disproportionation reactions, one substance is oxidized and that same substance gets reduced as well.\n\nIn the first reaction, Cu gets oxidized and Cu gets reduced. \nIn the second reaction, MnO42- gets oxidized into MnO4-. In the same reaction, MnO42- gets reduced into MnO2\n\nIn the third reaction, 2KMnO4 →  K2MnO4 + MnO2 + O2, Mn gets reduced from + 7 to +6 and +7 to +4, and O gets oxidised to -2 to 0. here two different element in a compound gets oxidised hence it is not a disproportionation reaction.\n\nIn the fourth reaction, Mn oxidation state is changes from + 7 to +4 and Mn2+ undergoes from +2 to +4. Hence, both oxidation reaction.','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(a) and (d) only','(a) and (b) only','(a), (b) and (c) only','(a), (c) and (d) only','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(220911,'chemistry','CuO(s) + H₂(g) → Cu(s) + H₂O(g)\nMark the correct statement about the reaction above.','Hydrogen is oxidised from 0 to +1. Whereas, Copper  is reduced from +2 to 0.','In this reaction the oxidation number of Cu decreased from +2 to 0 and the oxidation state of hydrogen is increased to +1.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 280; Exercise 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Copper is oxidised from 0 to +1. Whereas, Hydrogen is reduced from +2 to 0.','Hydrogen is oxidised from 0 to +1. Whereas, Copper  is reduced from +2 to 0.','Hydrogen is oxidised from 0 to +2. Whereas, Copper is reduced from 0 to +1.','This is not a redox reaction.','NEW','11-280','11-252',NULL),
(220912,'chemistry','Fe₂O₃(s)  + 3CO(g)  → 2Fe(s)  + 3CO₂(g)\nMark the correct statement about the reaction above.','Carbon in CO is oxidised from +2 to +4 in CO₂. Whereas, Fe i+n Fe₂O₃ is reduced to zero in Fe from +3.','In this reaction the oxidation number of Fe in Fe₂O₃ is decreased to zero in Fe from +3 and the oxidation number of carbon in CO is increased from +2 to +4 in  CO₂.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 280; Exercise 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Carbon in CO is oxidised from +2 to +4 in CO₂. Whereas, Fe i+n Fe₂O₃ is reduced to zero in Fe from +3.','Fe in Fe₂O₃ is oxidised from zero to +3. Whereas, Carbon in CO₂ is reduced from +4 to +2.','Carbon in CO is oxidised from +2 to +4 in CO₂. Whereas, Fe in Fe₂O₃ is reduced from +3 to +2.','This is not a redox reaction.','NEW','11-280','11-252',NULL),
(220913,'chemistry','4BCl₃(g)  +  3LiAlH₄(s)  → 2B₂H₆(g)  + 3 LiCl(s) +  3 AlCl₃(s)\nMark the correct statement about the reaction above.','Oxidation: The oxidation number of H increases from –1 in LiAlH₄ to +1 in B₂H₆ i.e., LiAlH₄ is oxidised to B₂H₆.\nReduction: The oxidation number of B decreases from +3 in BCl₃ to –3 in B₂H₆.','In this reaction, the oxidation number of B decreases from +3 in BCl₃ to –3 in B₂H₆. i.e., BCl₃ is reduced to B₂H₆. Also, the oxidation number of H increases from –1 in LiAlH₄ to +1 in B₂H₆ i.e., LiAlH₄ is oxidised to B₂H₆.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 280; Exercise 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Oxidation: The oxidation number of H increases from –1 in LiAlH₄ to +1 in B₂H₆ i.e., LiAlH₄ is oxidised to B₂H₆.\nReduction: The oxidation number of B decreases from +3 in BCl₃ to –3 in B₂H₆.','Oxidation: The oxidation number of B increases from -3 in BCl₃ to +3 in B₂H₆.\nReduction: The oxidation number of H decreases from +1 in LiAlH₄ to -1 in B₂H₆ i.e., LiAlH₄ is oxidised to B₂H₆.','Oxidation: The oxidation number of H increases from –1 in LiAlH₄ to +6 in B₂H₆ i.e., LiAlH₄ is oxidised to B₂H₆.\nReduction: The oxidation number of B  from +3 in BCl₃ to –6 in B₂H₆.','This is not a redox reaction.','NEW','11-280','11-252',NULL),
(220914,'chemistry','2K(s)  +  F₂(g)  →  2K⁺F⁻ (s)\nMark the correct statement about the reaction above.','The reaction is an example of a redox reaction.','In the formation of K⁺F⁻, K loses electron (oxidation) and F gains electron (reduction) Therefore, it is a redox reaction..\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 280; Exercise 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','The reaction is an example of a corrosion reaction.','The reaction is an example of a disproportionation reaction.','The reaction is an example of a redox reaction.','The reaction is an example of a metal displacement reaction.','NEW','11-280','11-252',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(220915,'chemistry','4NH₃(g) + 5O₂(g) → 4NO(g) + 6H₂O(g)\nMark the correct statement about the reaction above.','The reaction is an example of a displacement reaction.','4NH₃(g) + 5O₂(g) → 4NO(g) + 6H₂O(g)\nSince the H of NH₃ is displaced by oxygen, it is a displacement reaction.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 280; Exercise 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','The reaction is an example of a combination reaction.','The reaction is an example of a disproportionation reaction.','The reaction is an example of a displacement reaction.','The reaction is an example of a metal displacement reaction.','NEW','11-280','11-252',NULL),
(220916,'chemistry','Fluorine gives the following reaction with ice:  \nH₂O(s) + F₂ (g)→  HF(g)  + HOF(g)\nThe reaction fall under the category of _____.','Disproportionation Reaction','In this reaction oxidation number of F decreased from zero to −1 in HF, and increased to +1 in HOF. This shows that \ni. Fluorine is both reduced as well as oxidised. \nii. Fluorine exists in three oxidation states.\nhence, it is a disproportionation redox reaction.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 280; Exercise 8.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Combination  Reaction','Disproportionation Reaction','Metal Displacement Reaction','Non-metal Displacement Reaction','NEW','11-280','11-252',NULL),
(220917,'chemistry','The oxidation number of sulphur, chromium and nitrogen in H₂SO₅ , Cr₂O₇²⁻ and NO₃⁻ are ____ respectively.','+6,+6,+5','1.H₂SO₅\nLet x be the oxidation number of S. Hence, 2(+1)+x+5(−2)=0\nx=+8 \n+8 Oxidation state of S is not possible as S cannot have oxidation number more than 6. The fallacy is overcomed if we calculate the oxidation number from its structure HO−S(O₂)−O−O−H. Thus, −1+x+2(−2)+2(−1)+1=0\nx=+6\n2.Cr₂O₇²⁻\nLet x be the oxidation number of Cr in dichromate ion. Hence, 2x+7(−2)=−2\nx=+6\n3.NO₃⁻\nLet x be the oxidation number of N in nitrate ion.\nFrom the structure O⁻−N⁺(O)−O⁻. Hence, x+1(−1)+1(−2)+1(−2)=0\nx=+5\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 280; Exercise 8.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','+6,+5,+6','+5,+6,+5','+6,+5,+5','+6,+6,+5','NEW','11-280','11-252',NULL),
(220918,'chemistry','Match the following compounds with their respective formula.','Mercury(II) chloride-HgCl₂,Nickel(II) sulphate-NiSO₄,Tin(IV) oxide-SnO₂','The chemical formula of Mercury(II) chloride, Nickel(II) sulphate and Tin(IV) oxide is HgCl₂, NiSO₄, and SnO₂ respectively.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 280; Exercise 8.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','match','Mercury(II) chloride,HgCl₂','Nickel(II) sulphate,NiSO₄','Tin(IV) oxide,SnO₂',NULL,'NEW','11-280','11-252',NULL),
(220919,'chemistry','Match the following compounds with their respective formula.','Thallium(I) sulphate-Tl₂SO₄ , Iron (III) sulphate-Fe₂(SO₄)₃ , Chromium (III) oxide-Cr₂O₃','The chemical formula of Thallium(I) sulphate, Iron (III) sulphate and Chromium (III) oxide is Tl₂SO₄, Fe₂(SO₄)₃, and Cr₂O₃ respectively.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 280; Exercise 8.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','match','Thallium(I) sulphate,Tl₂SO₄','Iron (III) sulphate,Fe₂(SO₄)₃','Chromium (III) oxide,Cr₂O₃',NULL,'NEW','11-280','11-252',NULL),
(220920,'chemistry','Match the following compounds with their respective oxidation state of carbon atom.','C₆CI₆-+1, CH=CH-−1, CCl₄-+4, CH₄-−4','The oxidation number of carbon in C₆CI₆, CH=CH, CCl₄, CH₄ is +1,-1, +4 and -4 respectively.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 280; Exercise 8.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','match','C₆CI₆,+1','CH=CH,−1','CCl₄,+4','CH₄,−4','NEW','11-280','11-252',NULL),
(220921,'chemistry','Match the following compounds with their respective oxidation state of nitrogen atom.','N₂O₅-+5, N₂O₄-+4, N₂O₃-+3, NO-+2','The oxidation number of nitrogen in N₂O₅, N₂O, N₂O₃, NO is +5, +4, +3, and +2 respectively.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 280; Exercise 8.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','match','N₂O₅,+5','N₂O₄,+4','N₂O₃,+3','NO,+2','NEW','11-280','11-252',NULL),
(31409,'chemistry','Which elements are less electropositive than group 1 and 2 elements.','D block','D block elements are less electropositive than group 1 and 2 elements.\nElectropositive character decrease as we move from left to right in the periodic table. This is because of the increase in ionisation potential as we move along the period\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 95; Paragraph no. 1 )','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Periodic Trends and Chemical reactivity',NULL,'1','mcq','D block','P block','S block','F block','NEW','11-095','11-095',NULL),
(71517,'chemistry','What will be the correct order of vapour pressure of water, acetone and ether at 30°C? Given that among these compounds, water has maximum boiling point and ether has minimum boiling point.','Water < Acetone < Ether','Greater the boiling point, less is the vapour pressure. Hence, the correct order of vapour pressures will be:\nWater < acetone < ether.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Water < Ether < Acetone','Water < Acetone < Ether','Ether < Acetone < Water','Acetone < Ether < Water','NEW','11-194','11-170',NULL),
(220922,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R).\nAssertion: Sulphur dioxide can act as oxidising as well as reducing agents in its reactions, while Ozone acts only as an oxidant.\nReason: Sulphur can have oxidation states from +6 to -2. While Ozone can have oxidation number of zero or below.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The substance that may both increase or decrease its oxidation state can operate as both an oxidising and a reducing agent. Sulphur is in the +4 oxidation state in sulphur dioxide. When used as a reducing agent, it may raise its oxidation number to +6; when used as an oxidising agent, it can lower it to either 0 or -2. The oxidation number of oxygen is 0 in ozone. It can only decrease its oxidation number to -1 or -2 and cannot increase it. Hence, it can only act as an oxidant.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 280; Exercise 8.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','11-280','11-252',NULL),
(220923,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R).\nAssertion: Hydrogen peroxide can act as oxidising as well as reducing agents in its reactions, while nitric acid act only as an oxidant.\nReason: The minimum and maximum oxidation numbers of O in Hydrogen peroxide are -2 and 0 respectively. While nitric acid, N has oxidation number of +5 which can only be decreased.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','Some elements can increase or decrease its oxidation state can act as both oxidising and reducing agent. In hydrogen peroxide oxidation number of oxygen is -1. It can increase its oxidation number upto zero and can decrease its oxidation number to -2. While in nitric acid oxidation state of nitrogen is +5. It cannot increase its oxidation state because it is the maximum oxidation state of nitrate. It can only decrease its oxidation number.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 280; Exercise 8.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','11-280','11-252',NULL),
(220924,'chemistry','xCO₂ (g) + yH₂O (l)  →  C₆H₁₂O₆ (aq)  + 1/2yH₂O (l) + 6O₂ (g)\nGive the value of x and 1/2 y respectively.','6,6','6CO₂ (g) + 12H₂O (l)  →  C₆H₁₂O₆ (aq)  + 6H₂O (l) + 6O₂ (g)\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 281; Exercise 8.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','6,6','6,12','12,6','12,12','NEW','11-281','11-253',NULL),
(220925,'chemistry','AgF₂ is an unstable chemical compound. However, if produced, the chemical is a powerful oxidising agent. Mark the correct justification of this phenomenon.','All are correct','Silver in AgF₂ has an oxidation state of +2. However, +2 is an unstable oxidation state of silver. As a result, whenever AgF₂ is formed, silver rapidly accepts an electron to produce Ag⁺¹. This contributes to reducing the oxidation state of silver from +2 to a more stable +1. AgF₂ thus, serves as a strong oxidising agent.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 281; Exercise 8.10)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Silver in AgF₂ possess an oxidation state of +2 which is highly unstable.','Silver in AgF₂ reacts readily to achieve an oxidation state of +1.','Silver in AgF₂ tends to rapidly gain an electron.','All are correct','NEW','11-281','11-253',NULL),
(220926,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\nAssertion : C is a reducing agent, whereas O₂ is an oxidising agent. If an excess of C is burned in the presence of an inadequate quantity of O₂, CO is created; on the other hand, if C is burned in the presence of an excess of O₂, CO₂ is formed.\nReason : When an oxidising agent and a reducing agent react, the excess of the reducing agent will result in the formation of a compound with a lower oxidation state, and the excess of the oxidising agent will result in the formation of a compound with a higher oxidation state.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','Whenever a reaction between an oxidising agent and a reducing agent is carried out, a compound of lower oxidation state is formed if the reducing agent is in excess and a compound of higher oxidation state is formed if the oxidising agent is in excess. \nAs an example, C is a reducing agent while O₂ is an oxidising agent. When an excess of carbon is burned in a restricted supply of O₂, CO is generated with a C oxidation state of +2. If an excess of O₂ is used, the newly produced CO is oxidised to CO₂, with a C oxidation state of +4.\n2C(Excess) + O₂ →2C + 2O\nC + O₂(Excess)→CO₂ + 4\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 281; Exercise 8.11)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','11-281','11-253',NULL),
(220927,'chemistry','Although both alkaline potassium permanganate and acidic potassium permanganate are used as oxidants, alcoholic potassium permanganate is used as an oxidant in the production of benzoic acid from toluene. What might be the possible cause?','All are correct','Alcoholic potassium permanganate is used as an oxidant in the production of benzoic acid from toluene for the following reasons.\ni. The process itself produces OH⁻ ions in a neutral medium. As a result, the cost of introducing an acid or a base is lowered.\nii. Since both KMnO₄ and alcohol are polar, they are homogenous to one another. Because both toluene and alcohol are organic substances, they are also homogenous to one another. In a homogeneous media, reactions can occur at a quicker pace than in a heterogeneous medium. As a result, KMnO₄ and toluene react faster in alcohol. \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 281; Exercise 8.12(a))','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Because both KMnO₄ and alcohol are polar, they are homogenous.','Toluene and alcohol are also homogeneous to each other because both are organic compounds.','In a neutral medium, OH⁻ ions are produced in the reaction itself. As a result, the cost of adding an acid or a base can be reduced.','All are correct','NEW','11-281','11-253',NULL),
(220928,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R).\nAssertion : When concentrated sulphuric acid is introduced to an inorganic mixture including chloride, the result is colourless pungent smelling gas HCl, but when the mixture contains bromide, the result is red bromine vapour.\nReason : Bromine is less reactive than chlorine.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The reason (R) is not the correct explanation of assertion (A) because when concentrated H₂SO₄ is introduced to an inorganic mixture containing chloride, a pungent-smelling gas (HCl) is produced. Because HCl is a poor reducing agent, it cannot convert H₂SO₄ to SO₂.\n2NaCl + 2H₂SO₄ → 2NaHSO₄ + 2HCl (colorless pungent smelling gas)\nWhereas, When concentrated H₂SO₄ is introduced to an inorganic mixture containing bromide, HBr is formed initially. \n2NaBr + 2H₂SO₄ → 2NaHSO₄ + 2HBr\n HBr, being a powerful reducing agent, converts H₂SO₄ to SO₂ by the formation of red bromine vapour.\n2HBr + H₂SO₄ → Br₂ + SO₂ + 2H₂O\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 281; Exercise 8.12(b))','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','11-281','11-253',NULL),
(220930,'chemistry','Identify the substance oxidised, reduced, oxidising agent and reducing agent for the following reaction:\n2AgBr (s) + C₆H₆O₂(aq) → 2Ag(s) + 2HBr (aq) + C₆H₄O₂(aq)','Oxidised substance C₆H₆O₂,\nReduced substance AgBr,\nOxidising agent  AgBr,\nReducing agent C₆H₆O₂','2AgBr (s) + C₆H₆O₂(aq) → 2Ag(s) + 2HBr (aq) + C₆H₄O₂(aq)\nIn the reaction above, Ag⁺ is reduced and acts as oxidising agent. C₆H₆O₂ is oxidised because of dehydrogenation and it acts as reducing agent.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 281; Exercise 8.13)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Oxidised substance AgBr,\nReduced substance AgBr,\nOxidising agent  C₆H₆O₂,\nReducing agent C₆H₆O₂','Oxidised substance AgBr,\nReduced substance C₆H₆O₂,\nOxidising agent  C₆H₆O₂,\nReducing agent AgBr','Oxidised substance C₆H₆O₂,\nReduced substance AgBr,\nOxidising agent  AgBr,\nReducing agent C₆H₆O₂','Oxidised substance C₆H₆O₂,\nReduced substance C₆H₆O₂,\nOxidising agent  AgBr,\nReducing agent AgBr','NEW','11-281','11-253',NULL),
(220931,'chemistry','Identify the substance oxidised, reduced, oxidising agent and reducing agent for the following reaction:\nHCHO(l) + 2[Ag (NH₃)₂]⁺(aq) + 3OH⁻(aq) → 2Ag(s) +  HCOO⁻(aq) + 4NH₃(aq)  + 2H₂O(l)','Oxidised substance HCHO\nReduced substance [Ag (NH₃)₂]⁺ \nOxidising agent [Ag (NH₃)₂]⁺\nReducing agent HCHO','HCHO(l) + 2[Ag (NH₃)₂]⁺(aq) + 3OH⁻(aq) → 2Ag(s) +  HCOO⁻(aq) + 4NH₃(aq)  + 2H₂O(l)\nHCHO is oxidised to format and [Ag (NH₃)₂]⁺  is reduced to Ag. [Ag (NH₃)₂]⁺ is the oxidising agent and HCHO is reducing agent.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 281; Exercise 8.13)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Oxidised substance  HCHO\nReduced substance [Ag (NH₃)₂]⁺\nOxidising agent HCHO\nReducing agent [Ag (NH₃)₂]⁺','Oxidised substance HCHO\nReduced substance [Ag (NH₃)₂]⁺ \nOxidising agent [Ag (NH₃)₂]⁺\nReducing agent HCHO','Oxidised substance [Ag (NH₃)₂]⁺,\nReduced substance HCHO\nOxidising agent HCHO\nReducing agent [Ag (NH₃)₂]⁺','Oxidised substance [Ag (NH₃)₂]⁺,\nReduced substance HCHO,\nOxidising agent [Ag (NH₃)₂]⁺,\nReducing agent HCHO','NEW','11-281','11-253',NULL),
(220932,'chemistry','Identify the substance oxidised, reduced, oxidising agent and reducing agent for the following reaction:\nHCHO (l) + 2 Cu²⁺(aq) + 5 OH⁻(aq) → Cu₂O(s) + HCOO⁻(aq) + 3H₂O(l)','Oxidised substance HCHO,  \nReduced substance  Cu²⁺, \nOxidising agent  Cu²⁺,\nReducing agent HCHO','HCHO (l) + 2 Cu²⁺(aq) + 5 OH⁻(aq) → Cu₂O(s) + HCOO⁻(aq) + 3H₂O(l)\nHCHO is oxidised and Cu²⁺ is reduced.\nCu²⁺ is the oxidising agent and HCHO is the reducing agent.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 281; Exercise 8.13)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Oxidised substance HCHO,  \nReduced substance  Cu²⁺, \nOxidising agent  Cu²⁺,\nReducing agent HCHO','Oxidised substance  Cu²⁺,\nReduced substance HCHO,\nOxidising agent HCHO,\nReducing agent  Cu²⁺','Oxidised substance  Cu²⁺,\nReduced substance HCHO,\nOxidising agent  Cu²⁺,\nReducing agent HCHO','Oxidised substance HCHO,\nReduced substance   Cu²⁺,\nOxidising agent HCHO,\nReducing agent  Cu²⁺','NEW','11-281','11-253',NULL),
(220933,'chemistry','Identify the substance oxidised, reduced, oxidising agent and reducing agent for the following reaction:\nN₂H₄(l) + 2H₂O₂(l) → N₂(g) + 4H₂O(l)','Oxidised substance N₂H₄,  \nReduced substance H₂O₂,\nOxidising agent H₂O₂,\nReducing agent N₂H₄','N₂H₄(l) + 2H₂O₂(l) → N₂(g) + 4H₂O(l)\nN₂H₄ is oxidised and H₂O₂ is reduced.\nHence,H₂O₂ is an oxidising agent and N₂H₄ is a reducing agent.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 281; Exercise 8.13)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Oxidised substance H₂O₂,\nReduced substance N₂H₄,\nOxidising agent N₂H₄,\nReducing agent H₂O₂','Oxidised substance N₂H₄,\nReduced substance  H₂O₂,\nOxidising agent N₂H₄,\nReducing agent  H₂O₂','Oxidised substance N₂H₄,\nReduced substance N₂H₄,\nOxidising agent  H₂O₂,\nReducing agent  H₂O₂','Oxidised substance N₂H₄,  \nReduced substance H₂O₂,\nOxidising agent H₂O₂,\nReducing agent N₂H₄','NEW','11-281','11-253',NULL),
(220934,'chemistry','Identify the substance oxidised, reduced, oxidising agent and reducing agent for the following reaction:\nPb(s) + PbO₂(s) + 2H₂SO₄(aq) → 2PbSO₄(s) + 2H₂O(l)','Oxidised substance Pb,  \nReduced substance PbO₂,\nOxidising agent PbO₂,\nReducing agent Pb','Pb(s) + PbO₂(s) + 2H₂SO₄(aq) → 2PbSO₄(s) + 2H₂O(l)\nPb is oxidised and PbO₂ is reduced.\nPbO₂ is oxidising agent and Pb is reducing agent.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 281; Exercise 8.13)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Oxidised substance Pb,  \nReduced substance PbO₂,\nOxidising agent PbO₂,\nReducing agent Pb','Oxidised substance PbO₂,  \nReduced substance PbO₂,\nOxidising agent Pb,\nReducing agent Pb','Oxidised substance PbO₂,  \nReduced substance Pb,\nOxidising agent Pb,\nReducing agent PbO₂','Oxidised substance Pb,  \nReduced substance PbO₂,\nOxidising agent Pb,\nReducing agent PbO₂','NEW','11-281','11-253',NULL),
(213317,'chemistry','Match the following','Chlorine oxides-Highly explosives, ClO₂-Bleaching agent, Bromine oxides-Powerful oxidizing agents, I₂O₅-Estimation of CO','The correct match is Chlorine oxides-Highly explosives, ClO₂-Bleaching agent, Bromine oxides-Powerful oxidizing agents, I₂O₅-Estimation of CO\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 201 Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','match','Chlorine oxides,Highly explosives','ClO₂,Bleaching agent','Bromine oxides,Powerful oxidizing agents','I₂O₅,Estimation of CO','NEW','12-201',NULL,NULL),
(220935,'chemistry','Thiosulphate, being a reductant, gives different reactions with iodine and bromine.\n2S₂O₃²⁻ (aq) + I₂(s) → S₄O₆ ²⁻(aq) +  2I⁻(aq) \nS₂O₃²⁻(aq) + 2Br₂(l) + 5H₂O(l) → 2SO₄²⁻(aq) + 4Br ⁻(aq) + 10H⁺(aq)\nThe reason behind this deviation in behavior is:','Bromine is a stronger oxidising agent than Iodine.','The average oxidation number of S in S₂O₃²⁻ is +2. Being a stronger oxidising agent than I₂,\nBr₂ oxidises S₂O₃²⁻ to SO₄²⁻, in which the oxidation number of S is +6. However,  I₂ is a weak oxidising agent. Therefore, it\noxidises S₂O₃²⁻  to S₄O₆ ²⁻, in which the average oxidation number of S is only +2.5. As a result, Thiosulphate reacts differently with\niodine and bromine.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 281; Exercise 8.14)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Iodine is a stronger oxidising agent than Bromine.','Iodine is more electronegative than bromine.','Bromine is a stronger oxidising agent than Iodine.','Bromine is a stronger reducing agent than iodine.','NEW','11-281','11-253',NULL),
(220936,'chemistry','Mark the option that best justifies the following statement. \n"Among halogens, fluorine is the best oxidant and among hydrohalic compounds, hydroiodic acid is the best reductant."\na) Fluorine has the ability to oxidize other halogens to their ions; Whereas Cl, Br and I cannot convert Fluorine to Fluoride ion.\nb) The oxidizing power of halogens decreases in the order of I₂ < Br₂ < Cl₂ < F₂.\nc) Hydroiodic acid can easily reduce oxidising agents i.e., Sulphuric acid.','Both a and c','‣  F₂ has the ability to oxidise Cl₂, Br₂, and I₂  into their ions.  Contrarily , Cl₂, Br₂, and I₂ are unable to oxidise F⁻ to F₂. The oxidising power of halogens increases in the order of I₂ < Br₂ < Cl₂ < F₂. Fluorine is therefore the most effective halogen oxidant.\n‣  Among hydrohalic compounds, HI and HBr can reduce H₂SO₄ to SO₂, but HCl and HF cannot. Again, I⁻ can reduce Cu²⁺ to Cu⁺, but Br⁻ cannot. Hence, hydroiodic acid is the best reductant among hydrohalic compounds. Thus, the reducing power of hydrohalic acids increases in the order of HF < HCl < HBr < HI.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 281; Exercise 8.15)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both a and c','All of them','NEW','11-281','11-253',NULL),
(220937,'chemistry','XeO₆⁴⁻  is a part of compound Na₄XeO₆. What conclusion can you draw about Na₄XeO₆ by looking at the following equation.\nXeO₆⁴⁻(aq) + 2F⁻(aq) + 6H⁺(aq) → XeO₃(g)+ F₂(g) + 3H₂O(l)','Na₄XeO₆ is a stronger oxidising agent than F⁻.','The given reaction occurs because XeO₆⁴⁻ oxidises F⁻ and F⁻ reduces XeO₆⁴⁻.\nXeO₆⁴⁻(aq) + 2F⁻(aq) + 6H⁺(aq) → XeO₃(g)+ F₂(g) + 3H₂O(l)\nIn this reaction, the oxidation number (O.N.) of Xe decreases from +8 in XeO₆⁴⁻ to +6 in XeO₃ and the O.N. of F\nincreases from -1 in F⁻ to O in F2.\nHence, we can conclude that is a stronger oxidising agent than F⁻.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 281; Exercise 8.16)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Na₄XeO₆ is a stronger reducing agent than F⁻.','Na₄XeO₆ is a weaker oxidising agent than F⁻.','Na₄XeO₆ is a weaker reducing agent than F⁻.','Na₄XeO₆ is a stronger oxidising agent than F⁻.','NEW','11-281','11-253',NULL),
(220938,'chemistry','Consider the following two reactions.\n(i) C₆H₅CHO(l) + 2[Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺(aq) + 3OH⁻(aq) → C₆H₅COO⁻(aq) + 2Ag(s) + 4NH₃(aq) + 2H₂O(l)\n(ii) C₆H₅CHO(l) + 2Cu²⁺(aq) + 5OH⁻(aq) → No change observed.\nWhat inference do you draw about the behaviour of Ag⁺ and Cu²⁺ from these reactions?','Ag⁺ is a stronger oxidising agent than Cu²⁺.','In reaction (i) , Ag⁺ oxidises C₆H₅CHO to C₆H₅COO⁻, but in reaction (ii), Cu²⁺ cannot oxidise C₆H₅CHO.\nHence, we can say that Ag⁺ is a stronger oxidising agent than Cu²⁺.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 281; Exercise 8.17)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Ag⁺ is a stronger reducing agent than Cu²⁺.','Ag⁺ is a reducing agent but not an oxidisng agent.','Ag⁺ is a stronger oxidising agent than Cu²⁺.','Cu²⁺ is not an oxidising agent.','NEW','11-281','11-253',NULL),
(220939,'chemistry','Balance the following reaction by ion electron method.\nMnO₄⁻ (aq) + I⁻ (aq) →  MnO₂(s) + I₂(s) (in basic medium)','6I⁻(aq) + MnO₄⁻(aq) + 4H₂O (l) → 3I₂ (s) + MnO₂(s) + 8OH⁻','Step 1: The oxidation half reaction is: \nI⁻ →I₂\nThe reduction half reaction is:\nMnO₄⁻ →MnO₂\nStep 2: Balancing I in the oxidation half-reaction, we have: \n2I⁻⟶ I₂\nNow, to balance the charge, we add 2e⁻ to the RHS of the reaction: \n2I⁻ ⟶ I₂ + 2e⁻\nStep 3: In the reduction half reaction, the oxidation state of Mn has reduced from +7 to +4.\nThus, 3 electrons are added to the LHS of the reaction. \nMnO₄⁻ + 3e- ⟶ MnO₂ \nNow, to balance the charge, we add 4 OH⁻ ions to the RHS of the reaction as the reaction is taking place in a basic medium.\nMnO₄⁻ + 3e⁻ ⟶ MnO₂ + 4OH⁻\nStep 4: In this equation, there are 6 O atoms on the RHS and 4 O atoms on the LHS. Therefore, two water molecules are\nadded to the LHS. \nMnO₄⁻ + 2H₂O+ 3e⁻ ⟶ MnO₂ + 4OH⁻\nStep 5: Equalising the number of electrons by multiplying the oxidation half reaction by 3 and the reduction half reaction by 2,\nwe have: \n6I⁻ ⟶ 3I₂ + 6e⁻\n2MnO₄⁻ + 4H₂O+ 6e⁻ ⟶ 2MnO₂ + 8OH⁻\nStep 6: Adding the two half reactions, we have the net balanced redox reaction as:\n6I⁻(aq) + MnO₄⁻(aq) + 4H₂O (l) → 3I₂ (s) + MnO₂(s) + 8OH⁻\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 281; Exercise 8.18)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','6I⁻(aq) + 2MnO₄⁻(aq) + 2H₂O (l) → 3I₂ (s) + 2MnO₂(s) + 2OH⁻','6I⁻(aq) + 6MnO₄⁻(aq) + 4H₂O (l) → 3I₂ (s) + 6MnO₂(s) + 8OH⁻','4I⁻(aq) + MnO₄⁻(aq) + 2H₂O (l) → 2I₂ (s) + MnO₂(s) + 6OH⁻','6I⁻(aq) + MnO₄⁻(aq) + 4H₂O (l) → 3I₂ (s) + MnO₂(s) + 8OH⁻','NEW','11-281','11-253',NULL),
(220940,'chemistry','Balance the following reaction by ion electron method.\nMnO₄⁻(aq) + SO₂(g) →  Mn²⁺(aq) + HSO₄⁻(aq)  (in acidic solution)','2MnO₄⁻(aq) + 5SO₂(g) +2H₂O(l) + H⁺(aq) →  2Mn²⁺(aq) + 5HSO₄⁻(aq)','We have the oxidation half reaction as:\nSO₂ + 2H₂O →   HSO₄⁻ + 3H⁺ + 2e⁻\nAnd the reduction half reaction as:\nMnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O\nMultiplying the oxidation half reaction by 5 and the reduction half reaction by 2, and then by adding them, we have the\nnet balanced redox reaction as:\n2MnO₄⁻(aq) + 5SO₂(g) +2H₂O(l) + H⁺(aq) →  2Mn²⁺(aq) + 5HSO₄⁻(aq)\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 281; Exercise 8.18)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','2MnO₄⁻(aq) + 5SO₂(g) +2H₂O(l) + H⁺(aq) →  2Mn²⁺(aq) + 5HSO₄⁻(aq)','2MnO₄⁻(aq) + 5SO₂(g) + H₂O(l) + H⁺(aq) →  2Mn²⁺(aq) + 5HSO₄⁻(aq)','4MnO₄⁻(aq) + 6SO₂(g) +2H₂O(l) + H⁺(aq) → 4Mn²⁺(aq) + 6HSO₄⁻(aq)','MnO₄⁻(aq) + 5SO₂(g) +2H₂O(l) + H⁺(aq) →  Mn²⁺(aq) + 5HSO₄⁻(aq)','NEW','11-281','11-253',NULL),
(220941,'chemistry','Balance the following reaction by ion electron method.\nH₂O₂(aq) + Fe²⁺(aq) →  Fe³⁺(aq) + H₂O (l)  (in acidic solution)','H₂O₂(aq) + 2Fe²⁺(aq) +2H⁺(aq) →  2Fe³⁺(aq) + 2H₂O (l)','We have the oxidation half reaction as:\nFe²⁺→  Fe³⁺ + e⁻\nAnd the reduction half reaction as:\nH₂O₂ + 2H⁺ + e⁻→  2H₂O\nMultiplying the oxidation half reaction by 2 and then adding it to the reduction half reaction, we have the net balanced\nredox reaction as:\nH₂O₂(aq) + 2Fe²⁺(aq) +2H⁺(aq) →  2Fe³⁺(aq) + 2H₂O (l) \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 281; Exercise 8.18)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','4H₂O₂(aq) + 2Fe²⁺(aq) +2H⁺(aq) → 2Fe³⁺(aq) + 4H₂O (l)','H₂O₂(aq) + 2Fe²⁺(aq) + H⁺(aq) →  2Fe³⁺(aq) + 2H₂O (l)','H₂O₂(aq) + 2Fe²⁺(aq) +2H⁺(aq) →  2Fe³⁺(aq) + 2H₂O (l)','2H₂O₂(aq) + 2Fe²⁺(aq) +2H⁺(aq) →  2Fe³⁺(aq) + 4H₂O (l)','NEW','11-281','11-253',NULL),
(220942,'chemistry','Balance the following reaction by ion electron method.\nCr₂O₇²⁻+  SO₂(g) →  Cr³⁺(aq) + SO₄²⁻(aq) (in acidic solution)','Cr₂O₇²⁻(aq) +  3SO₂(g) + 2H⁺(aq) →  2Cr³⁺(aq) + 3SO₄²⁻(aq) + H₂O(l)','We have the oxidation half reaction as:\nSO₂ + 2H₂O →  SO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺ + 2e⁻\nAnd the reduction half reaction as:\nCr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O \nMultiplying the oxidation half reaction by 3 and then adding it to the reduction half reaction, we have the net balanced\nredox reaction as:\nCr₂O₇²⁻(aq) +  3SO₂(g) + 2H⁺(aq) →  2Cr³⁺(aq) + 3SO₄²⁻(aq) + H₂O(l) \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 281; Exercise 8.18)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','2Cr₂O₇²⁻(aq) +  3SO₂(g) + 2H+(aq) →  4Cr³⁺(aq) + 3SO₄²⁻(aq) + H₂O(l)','Cr₂O₇²⁻(aq) +  3SO₂(g) + 3H+(aq) →  2Cr³⁺(aq) + 3SO₄²⁻(aq) + 3H₂O(l)','Cr₂O₇²⁻(aq) +  3SO₂(g) + 2H+(aq) →  2Cr³⁺(aq) + 3SO₄²⁻(aq) + H₂O(l)','2Cr₂O₇²⁻(aq) + 6SO₂(g) + 2H+(aq) → 2Cr³⁺(aq) + 6SO₄²⁻(aq) + H₂O(l)','NEW','11-281','11-253',NULL),
(220943,'chemistry','Balance the following equations in basic medium by oxidation number methods.\nP₄(s) + OH⁻(aq)  →  PH₃(g) + HPO₂⁻(aq)','P₄(s) + 3OH⁻(aq) + 3H₂O →  PH₃(g) + 3H₂PO₂⁻(aq)','P₄(s) + OH⁻(aq)  →  PH₃(g) + HPO₂⁻(aq) \nThe oxidation half equation is: \nP₄  → H₂PO₂⁻\nThe O.N. and charge is balanced by adding 4 electrons and 8OH- as:\n P₄ + 8OH⁻ →  4H₂PO₂⁻ + 4e⁻\nThe reduction half equation is: \nP₄  → PH₃\nThe O.N. and charge is balanced by adding 12 electrons and 12OH- as:   \nP₄  + 12e⁻ → PH₃ + 12OH⁻\nThe O and H atoms are balanced by adding 12H₂O as: \nP₄  + 12H₂O  + 12e⁻ → PH₃ + 12OH⁻\nBy multiplying equation (i) with 3 and (ii) with 2 and then adding them, the balanced chemical equation can be\nobtained as:\nP₄(s) + 3OH⁻(aq) + 3H₂O →  PH₃(g) + 3H₂PO₂⁻(aq)\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 281; Exercise 8.19)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','2P₄(s) + 3OH⁻(aq) + 3H₂O →  2PH₃(g) + 3H₂PO₂⁻(aq)','2P₄(s) + 4OH⁻(aq) + 3H₂O →  2PH₃(g) + 4H₂PO₂⁻(aq)','P₄(s) + 3OH⁻(aq) + 3H₂O →  PH₃(g) + 3H₂PO₂⁻(aq)','P₄(s) + OH⁻(aq) + 3H₂O →  PH₃(g) + 3H₂PO₂⁻(aq)','NEW','11-281','11-253',NULL),
(220944,'chemistry','Balance the following equations in basic medium by oxidation number methods.\nN₂H₄(l) + ClO₃⁻(aq) → NO(g) + Cl⁻(g)','3N₂H₄(l) + 4ClO₃⁻(aq) → 6NO(g) + 4Cl⁻(g) + 6H₂O(l)','N₂H₄(l) + ClO₃⁻(aq) → NO(g) + Cl⁻(g)\nOxidation number method:  Total decrease in oxidation number of N = 2 × 4 = 8  \nTotal increase in oxidation number of Cl = 1 × 6 = 6  \nOn multiplying N₂H₄ with 3 and ClO₃⁻ with 4 to balance the increase and decrease in O.N., we get:  3N₂H₄ + 4ClO₃⁻→ NO + Cl⁻\nThe N and Cl atoms are balanced as: 3N₂H₄ + 4ClO₃⁻→ 6NO + 4Cl⁻\nThe O atoms are balanced by adding 6H₂O as: 3N₂H₄(l) + 4ClO₃⁻(aq) → 6NO(g) + 4Cl⁻(g) + 6H₂O(l)\nThis is the required balanced equation.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 281; Exercise 8.19)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','4N₂H₄(l) + 4ClO₃⁻(aq) → 8NO(g) + 4Cl⁻(g) + 6H₂O(l)','3N₂H₄(l) + 4ClO₃⁻(aq) → 6NO(g) + 4Cl⁻(g) + 6H₂O(l)','3N₂H₄(l) + ClO₃⁻(aq) → 6NO(g) + Cl⁻(g) + 6H₂O(l)','4N₂H₄(l) + 4ClO₃⁻(aq) → 4NO(g) + 4Cl⁻(g) + 4H₂O(l)','NEW','11-281','11-253',NULL),
(220945,'chemistry','Balance the following equations in basic medium by oxidation number methods.\nCl₂O₇(g) + H₂O₂(aq) →  ClO₂⁻(aq) + O₂(g) + H⁺','Cl₂O₇(g) + 4H₂O₂(aq) + 2OH⁻ →  2ClO₂⁻(aq) + 4O₂(g) + 5H₂O','Cl₂O₇(g) + H₂O₂(aq) →  ClO₂⁻(aq) + O₂(g) + H⁺\nOxidation number method:  Total decrease in oxidation number of Cl₂O₇ = 4 × 2 = 8  \nTotal increase in oxidation number of H₂O₂  = 2 × 1 = 2  \nBy multiplying H₂O₂  and O₂ with 4 to balance the increase and decrease in the oxidation number, we get:\nCl₂O₇ + 4H₂O₂  → ClO₂⁻ + 4O₂The Cl atoms are balanced as:\nCl₂O₇ + 4H₂O₂  → 2ClO₂⁻ + 4O₂\nThe O atoms are balanced by adding 3H₂O as:\nCl₂O₇ + 4H₂O₂  → 2ClO₂⁻ + 4O₂ + 3H₂O\nThe H atoms are balanced by adding 2OH⁻ and 2H₂O as:\nCl₂O₇(g) + 4H₂O₂(aq) + 2OH⁻ →  2ClO₂⁻(aq) + 4O₂(g) + 5H₂O\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 281; Exercise 8.19)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Cl₂O₇(g) + 4H₂O₂(aq) + 2OH⁻ →  2ClO₂⁻(aq) + 4O₂(g) + 5H₂O','Cl₂O₇(g) + 3H₂O₂(aq) + 2OH⁻ →  2ClO₂⁻(aq) + 3O₂(g) + 5H₂O','2Cl₂O₇(g) + 4H₂O₂(aq) + 2OH⁻ → 2ClO₂⁻(aq) + 7O₂(g) + 5H₂O','2Cl₂O₇(g) + 2H₂O₂(aq) + 2OH⁻ →  2ClO₂⁻(aq) + 2O₂(g) + 5H₂O','NEW','11-281','11-253',NULL),
(220946,'chemistry','What sorts of informations can you draw from the following reaction ? \n(CN)₂(g) + 2OH⁻(aq) → CN⁻(aq) + CNO⁻(aq) + H₂O(l)','All of them.','The oxidation number of C in (CN)₂ , CN⁻ and CNO⁻ is +3, +2 and +4 respectively.\nIt can be easily observed that the same compound is being reduced and oxidised simultaneously in the given\nequation. Reactions in which the same compound is reduced and oxidised is known as disproportionation reactions.\nThus, it can be said that the alkaline decomposition of cyanogen is an example of disproportionation reaction.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 283; Exercise 8.20)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','The same compound is being reduced and oxidised simultaneously.','The reaction shows alkaline decomposition of cyanogen','It is an example of disproportionation reaction.','All of them.','NEW','11-283','11-255',NULL),
(213318,'chemistry','The ionic character of the halides decreases in the order ______________ where M is a monovalent metal.','MF > MCl > MBr > MI','The ionic character of the halides decreases in the order MF > MCl > MBr > MIwhere M is a monovalent metal. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 202 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','MF > MCl > MBr > MI','MF > Ml > MBr > MCI','MI > MBr > MCl > MF','MF > MBr > MCl > MI','NEW','12-202',NULL,NULL),
(220947,'chemistry','The Mn³⁺ ion is unstable in solution and undergoes disproportionation to give Mn²⁺, MnO₂ , and H+ ion. \nChoose the write balanced ionic equation for this reaction.','2Mn³⁺ + 2H₂O → MnO₂ + Mn²⁺ + 4H⁺','This is the reaction\'s basic equation; \nMn³⁺ → Mn²⁺ + MnO₂ + H⁺\n‣ Oxidation half equation:\nMn³⁺ → MnO₂\nBalance O.N. by adding electrons,\nMn³⁺ → MnO₂ + e⁻\nBalance charge by adding 4 H⁺ ions\nBalance O atoms by adding 2H₂O\nMn³⁺ + 2H₂O → MnO₂ + 4 H⁺ + e⁻\nBalance O atoms by adding 2H₂O\nMn³⁺ + 2H₂O → MnO₂ + 4 H⁺ + e⁻ .......................(1)\n‣ Reduction half equation:\nMn³⁺→ Mn²⁺\nBalance O.N. by adding electrons:\nMn³⁺ + e⁻→ Mn²⁺ ..............(2)\nAdding Equation (1) and (2), the balanced equation for the disproportionation reaction is:\n2Mn³⁺ + 2H₂O → MnO₂ + Mn²⁺ + 4H⁺\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 283; Exercise 8.21)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','4Mn³⁺ + 2H₂O → 2MnO₂ + 2Mn²⁺ + 4H⁺','4Mn³⁺ + 4H₂O → 2MnO₂ + 2Mn²⁺ + 4H⁺','2Mn³⁺ + 2H₂O → MnO₂ + Mn²⁺ + 4H⁺','2Mn³⁺ + 2H₂O → MnO₂ + 2Mn²⁺ + 4H⁺','NEW','11-283','11-255',NULL),
(220948,'chemistry','Identify the element that exhibits only negative oxidation state.','F','F exhibits only negative oxidation state of -1.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 283; Exercise 8.22)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','F','I','Ne','Cs','NEW','11-283','11-255',NULL),
(220949,'chemistry','Identify the element that exhibits only positive oxidation state.','Cs','Cs exhibits positive oxidation state of +1.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 283; Exercise 8.22)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','F','I','Ne','Cs','NEW','11-283','11-255',NULL),
(220950,'chemistry','Identify the element that exhibits both positive and negative oxidation states.','I','Iodine exhibits both positive and negative oxidation states. It exhibits oxidation states of - 1, + 1, + 3, + 5, and + 7.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 283; Exercise 8.22)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','F','I','Ne','Cs','NEW','11-283','11-255',NULL),
(220951,'chemistry','Identify the element which exhibits neither the negative nor does the positive oxidation state.','Ne','The oxidation state of Ne is zero. It exhibits neither negative nor positive oxidation states.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 283; Exercise 8.22)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','F','I','Ne','Cs','NEW','11-283','11-255',NULL),
(220952,'chemistry','Although chlorine is used to purify drinking water, excess of it is dangerous. The excess of chlorine is removed by treating with sulphur dioxide. What would be the balanced equation for this redox change taking place in water?','Cl₂ + SO₂ + 2H₂O → 2Cl⁻ + SO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺','The balanced chemical equation for this redox change taking place under water is:\nCl₂ + SO₂ + 2H₂O → 2Cl⁻ + SO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 283; Exercise 8.23)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Cl₂ + SO₂ + 2H₂O → 2Cl⁻ + SO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺','2Cl₂ + H₂SO₄ + 2H₂O → 2Cl⁻ + SO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺','2Cl₂ + H₂SO₄ + 2H₂O → 4Cl⁻ + SO₄²⁻ + 4H₂O','Cl₂+ SO₂ + 2HCl → 3Cl⁻ + SO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺','NEW','11-283','11-255',NULL),
(220953,'chemistry','Some elements, for example P, Mn and Cu, can exist in oxidation states up to three. What type of redox reaction they are most likely to show?','Disproportionation Reaction','Disproportionation reactions are a special type of redox reactions. In a disproportionation reaction an element in one oxidation state is simultaneously oxidised and reduced. One of the reacting substances in a disproportionation reaction always contains an element that can exist in at least three oxidation states.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 283; Exercise 8.24)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Displacement Reaction','Combination Reaction','Disproportionation Reaction','Combustion Reaction','NEW','11-283','11-255',NULL),
(220954,'chemistry','The balanced chemical equation for Ostwald’s process for the manufacture of nitric acid is:\n4NH₃(g) + 5O₂(g) → 4NO(g) + 6H₂O(g)\nKeeping the above equation in mind, Calculate the maximum weight of nitric oxide that can be obtained starting only with 10.00 g. of ammonia and 20.00 g of oxygen ?','15g of NO.','The balanced chemical equation for Ostwald’s process is: \n4NH₃(g) + 5O₂(g) → 4NO(g) + 6H₂O(g)\nThe molar masses of ammonia and oxygen are 17 g/mol and 32 g/mol respectively.\n5 moles (160 g) of oxygen reacts with 4 moles (68 g) of ammonia.\n20 g of oxygen will react with (68×20)/160 =8.5 g of ammonia. Hence, oxygen is the limiting reagent.The molar mass of NO is 30 g/mol. 5 moles (160 g) of oxygen will produce 4 moles (120 g) of NO.\n20 g of oxygen will produce (120×20)/160 =15g of NO.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 283; Exercise 8.25)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','15g of NO.','150g of NO.','200g of NO.','20g of NO.','NEW','11-283','11-255',NULL),
(220955,'chemistry','Cu⁺ + e⁻ → Cu (Eᵒ = 0.52)\nAg⁺ + e⁻ → Ag (Eᵒ = 0.80)\nAg →  Ag⁺ + e⁻ (Eᵒ = –0.80)\nFe²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Fe (Eᵒ = –0.44)\nFe³⁺ + e⁻ → Fe²⁺ (Eᵒ =0.77)\nBr₂ + 2e⁻ → 2Br ⁻ (Eᵒ =1.09)\nUsing the standard electrode potentials, which reaction among the following is not feasible:','Ag(s) and Fe³⁺(aq)','Here, E° for the overall reaction is negative. Hence, the reaction between Ag and Fe³⁺ is not feasible.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 283; Exercise 8.26) diagram(e220958)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ag⁺(aq) and Cu(s)','Fe³⁺ (aq) and Cu(s)','Ag(s) and Fe³⁺(aq)','Br₂ (aq) and Fe²⁺(aq)','NEW','11-283','11-255',NULL),
(220956,'chemistry','Select the inaccurate option(s) that depict the following galvanic cell reaction:\nZn(s) + 2Ag⁺(aq) → Zn²⁺(aq) +2Ag(s)\n(a) Zn electrode is positively charged because at this electrode, Zn oxidizes to Zn²⁺.\n(b) Ions are the carriers of current in the cell.\n(c) The reaction at the Zn electrode can be represented as follows:\n Zn(s)→Zn²⁺(aq)+2e⁻\nAnd the reaction at Ag electrode can be represented as:\n2Ag⁺(aq)+2e⁻→2Ag(s)','Both a and b','‣ The statement (a) is inaccurate because Zn electrode is negatively charged because Zn oxidises to Zn²⁺ at this electrode and the releasing electrons accumulate on this electrode.\n‣ Statement (b) is also inaccurate as electrons are the carriers of current in the cell.\n‣ Statement (c) is true as the reaction at the Zn electrode can be represented as follows:\n Zn(s)→Zn²⁺(aq)+2e⁻\nAnd the reaction at Ag electrode can be represented as:\n2Ag⁺(aq)+2e⁻→2Ag(s)\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 283; Exercise 8.30)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both a and c','All of them.','NEW','11-283','11-255',NULL),
(213319,'chemistry','Which one is more covalent?','SbCl₅','SbCl₅ is the most covalent among the foowing.  Greater the positive charge smaller the cation greater the polarisation thus more the covalent character.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 202 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','SbCl₅','SnCl₄','SbCl₂','SnCl₂','NEW','12-202',NULL,NULL),
(220957,'chemistry','Electrolysis in an aqueous solution of AgNO₃ with platinum electrodes will result in?','At cathode: Ag⁺ + e⁻→ Ag \nAt anode: Ag → Ag⁺ + e⁻','AgNO₃ ionizes in aqueous solutions to form Ag⁺ and NO³⁻ ions.\nOn electrolysis, either Ag+ ions or H₂O molecules can be reduced at the cathode. But the reduction potential of Ag+ ions is higher than that of H₂O.\nAg⁺ + e⁻→ Ag (Eᵒ = 0.80V)\n2H₂O + 2e⁻→ H₂ + 2OH⁻ (Eᵒ = -0.83V)\nHence, Ag⁺  ions are reduced at the cathode. Similarly, Ag metal or H₂O molecules can be oxidised at the anode. But\nthe oxidation potential of Ag is higher than that of H₂O molecules.\nAg → Ag⁺ + e⁻ (Eᵒ = 0.80V)\n2H₂O → O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ (Eᵒ = -1.23V)\nTherefore, Ag metal gets oxidised at the anode.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 280; Exercise 8.27)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','At cathode: 2H₂O + 2e⁻→ H₂ + 2OH⁻\nAt anode: Ag → Ag⁺ + e⁻','At cathode: Ag⁺ + e⁻→ Ag \nAt anode: Ag → Ag⁺ + e⁻','At cathode: Ag⁺ + e⁻→ Ag \nAt anode: 2H₂O + 2e⁻→ H₂ + 2OH⁻','At cathode: 2H₂O → O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻  \nAt anode: Ag → Ag⁺ + e⁻','NEW','11-280','11-252',NULL),
(220958,'chemistry','What would be the products of electrolysis in an aqueous solution AgNO₃ with platinum electrodes?','Both a and b','Pt cannot be oxidised easily. Hence, at the anode, oxidation of water occurs to liberate O₂. At the cathode, Ag⁺ ions are reduced and get deposited.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 283; Exercise 8.27)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','H₂O will be oxidised at the anode, and Ag⁺ ions are reduced.','Pt cannot be oxidised easily. Hence O₂ will librate at anode, and Ag⁺ ions will be deposited at cathode.','Ag metal or H₂O molecules are oxidised at the cathode. Similarly, Ag⁺ ions can be oxidised at the anode.','Both a and b','NEW','11-283','11-255',NULL),
(220959,'chemistry','Electrolysis in an aqueous solution of CuCl₂  with platinum electrodes will result in?','At cathode: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu\nAt anode: 2H₂O → O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻','In aqueous solutions, CuCl₂ ionizes to give Cu²⁺ and Cl⁻ ions as: CuCl₂ → Cu²⁺ + 2Cl⁻\nOn electrolysis, either of Cu²⁺ ions or H₂O molecules can get reduced at the cathode. But the reduction potential of Cu²⁺ is more than that of H₂O molecules. \nCu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu  (Eᵒ = 2.34V)\n2H₂O + 2e⁻→ H₂ + 2OH⁻ (Eᵒ = -0.83V)\nHence, Cu²⁺ ions are reduced at the cathode and get deposited. Similarly, at the anode, either of Cl⁻ or H₂O is oxidized. The oxidation potential of H₂O is higher than that of Cl⁻. \n2Cl⁻→ Cl₂ + 2e⁻ (Eᵒ = -1.36V)\n2H₂O → O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ (Eᵒ = -1.23V)\nBut oxidation of H₂O molecules occurs at a lower electrode potential than that of Cl⁻ ions because of over-voltage\n(extra voltage required to liberate gas). As a result, Cl⁻ions are oxidized at the anode to liberate Cl₂ gas.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 283; Exercise 8.27)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','At cathode: 2Cl⁻→ Cl₂ + 2e⁻\nAt anode:  Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu','At cathode: Cu  → Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ \nAt anode: Cl₂ + 2e⁻ → 2Cl⁻','At cathode: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu\nAt anode: 2H₂O → O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻','At cathode: 2H₂O → O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻\nAt anode: 2Cl⁻→ Cl₂ + 2e⁻','NEW','11-283','11-255',NULL),
(220960,'chemistry','Electrolysis in an aqueous solution of H₂SO₄  with platinum electrodes will result in?','At cathode: 2H⁺+2e⁻→ H₂\nAt anode:  2H₂O(l) + 2e⁻ → H₂ + 2OH⁻','H₂SO₄ ionizes in aqueous solutions to give H⁺ and SO₄²⁻ ions: \nH₂SO₄ → 2H⁺+SO₄²⁻\nOn electrolysis, either of H⁺ ions or H₂O molecules can get reduced at the cathode. But the reduction potential of H⁺\nions is higher than that of H₂O molecules\n2H⁺+2e⁻→ H₂(g)\n2H₂O(l) + 2e⁻ → H₂ + 2OH⁻(g).\nHence, at the cathode, H+ ions are reduced to liberate H₂ gas. On the other hand, at the anode, either of SO₄²⁻ ions or H₂O molecules can get oxidised. But the oxidation of SO₄²⁻ involves breaking of more bonds than that of H₂O molecules. Hence, SO₄²⁻ ions have a lower oxidation potential than H₂O. Thus, H₂O is oxidised at the anode to liberate O₂ molecules.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 283; Exercise 8.27)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','At cathode: 2H⁺+2e⁻→ H₂\nAt anode:  2H₂O(l) + 2e⁻ → H₂ + 2OH⁻','At cathode: 2H⁺ → H₂ + 2e⁻\nAt anode: H₂O(l) + 2e⁻→ 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ + O₂','At cathode: H₂ → 2H⁺+2e⁻\nAt anode:  2H₂O → H₂ + 2OH⁻','At cathode:  H₂O → 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ + O₂\nAt anode: 2H⁺+2e⁻→ H₂','NEW','11-283','11-255',NULL),
(220961,'chemistry','A metal having stronger reducing power displaces another metal having weaker reducing power from its salt solution. Arrange the following metals in the order in which their salt solutions displace each other.\nAl, Cu, Fe, Mg and Zn.','Mg > Al > Zn > Fe > Cu','A metal having stronger reducing power displaces another metal having weaker reducing power from its salt solution.\nThe order of the increasing reducing power of the given metals is Cu < Fe < Zn < Al < Mg.\nHence, we can say that Mg can displace Al from its salt solution, but Al cannot displace Mg.\nThus, the order in which the given metals displace each other from the solution of their salts is given below:\n Mg > Al > Zn > Fe > Cu\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 283; Exercise 8.28)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Mg > Al > Zn > Fe > Cu','Zn > Fe > Mg > Al >Cu','Mg > Zn > Fe > Al > Cu','Fe > Cu > Mg > Al > Zn','NEW','11-283','11-255',NULL),
(220962,'chemistry','Select the option(s) from the following statements that are false with respect to " Displacement reactions",\n(a) Dihydrogen from acids may be produced by metals, which reaily react with steam.\n(b) In a Metal displacement reaction, the reducing metal is a better reducing agent than the one that is being reduced which evidently shows more capability to lose electrons as compared to the one that is reduced.\n(c) "Layer\'s test", used to identifying halide ions in the laboratory, is a Metal displacement reaction.','Both a and c','‣ Statement (a) is false as Metals, which don\'t react with steam, can make dihydrogen from acids. (Non-metal displacement reaction)\n‣ Statement (b) is true as in a Metal displacement reaction, the reducing metal is a better reducing agent than the one that is being reduced which evidently shows more capability to lose electrons as compared to the one that is reduced.\n‣ Statement (c) is also inaccurate because the \'Layer Test\' is used in the laboratory to identify Br⁻ and I⁻. Following the reaction taking place in Layer\'s test.\nCl₂ (g) + 2Br⁻ (aq) → 2Cl⁻ (aq) + Br₂ (l)\nCl₂ (g) + 2I⁻ (aq)  →  2Cl⁻ (aq) + I₂ (s)\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 271; Paragraph 8.3.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both a and c','All of them','NEW','11-271','11-243',NULL),
(213320,'chemistry','Flourine exibits only-','-1 oxidation state','Flourine exibits only -1 oxidation state\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 202 Example 7.16)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','1+ oxidation state','3+ oxidation state','5+ oxidation state','-1 oxidation state','NEW','12-202',NULL,NULL),
(220963,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R).\nAssertion: The displacement reactions of chlorine, bromine and iodine using fluorine are not generally carried out in aqueous solution.\nReason: Fluorine is so reactive that it can replace chloride, bromide and iodide ions in solution. In fact, fluorine is so reactive that it attacks water and displaces the oxygen of water.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','Fluorine is so reactive that it can replace chloride, bromide and iodide ions in solution. In fact, fluorine is so reactive that it attacks water and displaces the oxygen of water :\n2H₂O (l) + 2F₂ (g)  → 4HF(aq)  + O₂(g)\nIt is for this reason that the displacement reactions of chlorine, bromine and iodine using fluorine are not generally carried out in aqueous solution.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 271; Paragraph 8.3.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','11-271','11-243',NULL),
(220964,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R).\nAssertion : Cr₂O₇²⁻ ion oxidises the indicator material diphenylamine right after the equivalence point to give a strong blue colour, signalling the end point.\nReason :  Cr₂O₇²⁻ ion being a strong oxidising agent, acts as a self-indicator.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','The reason is false because  Cr₂O₇²⁻ cannot act as a self indicator. Cr₂O₇²⁻ only exhibits oxidising behaviour in acidic enviornment, hence it cannot be used as a self-indicator as its reduction product blocks the visual detection of the reaction\'s end product.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 277; Paragraph 8.3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','11-271','11-243',NULL),
(220965,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R).\nAssertion : The oxidation process is shown as a drop in electron density and the reduction process as an increase in electron density surrounding the atom(s) engaged in the reaction.\nReason : The idea of redox reactions has evolved throughout time. The transformation is still taking place.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct, as the idea of redox reactions has evolved throughout time. The transformation is still taking place. In fact, in more recent times, the oxidation process is shown as a drop in electron density and the reduction process as an increase in electron density surrounding the atom(s) engaged in the reaction. The assertion\'s justification is that changes in electron density result from electron gains and losses during the redox processes.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 277; Paragraph 8.3.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','11-271','11-243',NULL),
(220966,'chemistry','Choose the option(s) from the following assertions that are untrue with respect to the " Daniell Cell", \n(a) The transfer of electrons takes place through the migration of ions through the salt bridge.\n(b) The electricity from solution in one beaker to solution in the other beaker flows by the metallic wire connecting the two rods.\n(c)  The flow of current is possible only if there is a potential difference between the electrodes.','Both a and b','The statement (a) is false because the transfer of electrons takes place through the metallic wire connecting the two rods. \nWhereas, the statement (b) is false because the electricity from solution in one beaker to solution in the other beaker flows by the migration of ions through the salt bridge.\nThe statement (c) is true as the flow of current is possible only if there is a potential difference between the electrodes.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 278; Paragraph 8.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both c and d','none of them','NEW','11-278','11-250',NULL),
(220967,'chemistry','Choose the option(s) from the following assertions that are true with respect to the " Oxidation Number",\na) Metallic elements have a positive oxidation number, whereas nonmetallic elements have either a positive or negative oxidation number.\nb) In accordance with the long form of the periodic table, the greatest oxidation number of a representative element is the group number for the first two groups and the group number minus 10 for all subsequent groups.\nc) The maximum oxidation number shown by an atom of an element usually decreases across the periodic table.','Both a and b','Statement (a) and (b) are correct as metallic elements have a positive oxidation number, whereas nonmetallic elements have either a positive or negative oxidation number. And in accordance with the long form of the periodic table, the greatest oxidation number of a representative element is the group number for the first two groups and the group number minus 10 for all subsequent groups.\nThe statement (c) is false because the maximum oxidation number shown by an atom of an element usually increases across the periodic table.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both c and d','All of them','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(31410,'chemistry','Identify the incorrect statement:','Among transition metals (3d series), the change in atomic radii is much higher as compared to those of representative elements across the period.','Among transition metals (3d series), the change in atomic radii is much less as compared to those of representative elements across the period.\n\nThe electronegativity of any given element is not constant; it varies depending on the element to which it is bound.\n\nUnlike ionization enthalpy and electron gain enthalpy, electronegativity is not a measurable quantity.\n\nElectron affinity is defined as absolute zero. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 90(last paragraph), 91(Paragraph 2), 94(last paragraph); Paragraph no. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Periodic Trends and Chemical reactivity',NULL,'3','mcq','Electron affinity is defined as absolute zero.','Unlike ionization enthalpy and electron gain enthalpy, electronegativity is not a measureable quantity.','The electronegativity of any given element is not constant, it varies depending on the element to which it is bound.','Among transition metals (3d series), the change in atomic radii is much higher as compared to those of representative elements across the period.','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(220968,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R).\nAssertion : ClO₄⁻ oxoanion has chlorine in its maximum oxidation state, +7. Therefore , it does not disproportionate.\nReason :  In a disproportionation reaction, the element in the form of the reacting component is in an intermediate oxidation state, and the reaction creates both higher and lower oxidation states of that element.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','A disproportionation reaction always involves at least three possible oxidation states for one of the reacting substances. The element in the form of the reacting material is in an intermediate oxidation state, and both higher and lower oxidation states of that element are created throughout the reaction. ClO₄⁻ does not disproportionate because in this oxoanion chlorine is present in its highest oxidation state that is, +7.\nHence, Reason is the correct explanation of the given assertion.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272; Paragraph 4 & problem 8.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(220969,'chemistry','The synthesis of common bleaching agents is described by the given reaction.\nCl₂(g) + 2 OH⁻(aq)  →  ClO⁻ (aq) + Cl⁻ (aq) + H₂O (l)\nChoose the option that most accurately sums up the functioning of the bleaching agent.','The ClO⁻ ion oxidises the coloured stains of the substances to colourless compounds.','Cl₂(g) + 2 OH⁻(aq)  →  ClO⁻ (aq) + Cl⁻ (aq) + H₂O (l)\nThe reaction produces the hypochlorite ion (ClO⁻), which oxidises the coloured stains of the substances to colourless compounds.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272; Paragraph 4 & problem 8.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','The  Cl⁻ ion reduces the coloured stains of the substances to colourless compounds.','The  Cl⁻ ion oxidises the coloured stains of the substances to colourless compounds.','The ClO⁻ ion reduces the coloured stains of the substances to colourless compounds.','The ClO⁻ ion oxidises the coloured stains of the substances to colourless compounds.','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(220970,'chemistry','Choose the option(s) from the following assertions that are untrue in relation to the  " Oxidation Number Method",\na) To make the total ionic charges of the reactants and products equal, H⁺ ions are included to the equation if the reaction is occurring in an acidic solution.\nb) If two substances are reduced and nothing is oxidised, electrons e⁻ must be added to the expression on the appropriate side to balance the overall charges.\nc) If the reaction is occurring in a non-aqueous medium, ions will definitely be involved. To ensure that the total ionic charges of the reactants and products are equivalent, H⁺ or OH⁻ ions must be added to the formula on the relevant side.','Both b and c','• The statement (a) is accurate because, if the reaction is taking place in an acidic solution, H⁺  ions are added to the equation to make the total ionic charges of the reactants and products equal.\n• The statement (b) is false because if two substances are reduced and nothing is oxidised or vice-versa, something is wrong. Either the formulas of reactants or products are wrong or the oxidation numbers have not been assigned properly.\n• The statement (c) is false because if the reaction is occurring in aqueous medium,  H⁺ or OH⁻ ions must be added to the equation on the appropriate side in order to balance the total ionic charges of the reactants and products.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 274; Paragraph 8.3.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both c and d','All of them','NEW','11-274','11-246',NULL),
(220971,'chemistry','Choose the option(s) from the following assertions that are misleading in relation to the " Half Reaction Method", \na) In acidic medium reactions, OH⁻ is used to balance O atoms while H₂ is used to balance H atoms.\nb) The number of electrons in the oxidation half- reaction should be twice as much than the reduction half reaction; this may be accomplished by multiplying one or both halves by the appropriate number.\nc) In each half reaction, the atoms aside from H and O are separately balanced.','Both a and b','• Statement (a) is inaccurate because in acidic medium reactions, H₂O is used to balance O atoms while H⁺ is used to balance H atoms.\n• Statement (b) is also not true because the number of electrons in the each half- reaction should be equal; this may be accomplished by multiplying one or both halves by the appropriate number.\n• Whereas statement (c) is accurate as in each half reaction, the atoms aside from H and O are separately balanced. \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 276; Paragraph 8.3.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both a and c','All of them','NEW','11-276','11-248',NULL),
(220972,'chemistry','Choose the option(s) from the following assertions that are untrue in relation to the  " Titrations Based on Redox Reactions", \na) Iodine is a reagent used in redox titration that may also be applied as an indicator in a variety of alkyl halide processes.\nb) The titration technique can be used in redox systems to measure a reductant or oxidant strength using a redox sensitive indicator.\nc) Permanganate ion is a self indicator since it exhibits a pink hue at the point when the mole stoichiometry of the reductant and the oxidant is equal.','Both a and c','• Statement (a) is false because Iodine is a reagent used in redox titration, and its application is limited to reagents that can oxidise I⁻ ions.\n• Statement (b) is true because the titration technique can be used in redox systems to measure a reductant or oxidant strength using a redox sensitive indicator.\n• Statement (c) is also untrue since permanganate ion is a sensitive self indicator, exhibiting a pink colour at very low concentrations of 106 mol dm⁻³.\n The titration technique can be used in redox systems to measure a reductant or oxidant strength using a redox sensitive indicator.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 277; Paragraph 8.3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both a and c','All of them','NEW','11-277','11-249',NULL),
(213321,'chemistry','F₂ is more oxidizing than Cl₂ due to-\n\na. High hydration energy\nb. Low bond dissociation energy\nc. Low hydration energy\nd. High bond dissociation energy','Both a and b','F₂ is more oxidizing than Cl₂ due to high hydration energy and low bond dissociation energy\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 202 Intext question)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Both b and c','Both a and c','Both a and b','Both a and d','NEW','12-202',NULL,NULL),
(213322,'chemistry','The anomalous behaviour of  F is not due to -','High positiive electron gain enthalpy\'s','The anomalous behaviour of  F is due to smallest size, high electronegativity and compact electron cloud\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 202 Intext question)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Smallest size','High electronegativity','Compact electron cloud','High positiive electron gain enthalpy\'s','NEW','12-202',NULL,NULL),
(220973,'chemistry','Choose the option(s) from the following assertions that are accurate in relation to the  " Fractional Oxidation Number", \na) The chemical formula and structure are critical in calculating each element\'s true oxidation number.\nb) The element that is realised to have a fractional oxidation state exists in several oxidation states.\nc) The fractional oxidation number indicates that the element in that particular species exists in more than one whole number of oxidation states.','Both b and c','‣ Statement (a) is inaccurate because the fractional oxidation number is only the average oxidation number, and the actual oxidation number of an element in a specific species can only be determined by that species\' structure.\n‣ Statement (b) and (c) correctly state that the element with a fractional oxidation state occurs in several oxidation states. The fractional oxidation number shows that the element occurs in more than one whole number of oxidation states in that particular species.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 277; Paragraph 8.3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both a and c','All of them','NEW','11-277','11-249',NULL),
(221313,'chemistry','Which of the following reactions is a disproportionation reaction?','2 FeCl₃  + 3 H₂S → 6 HCl + 2 FeS + S','Explanation: Disproportionation reactions involve an element in a compound undergoing both oxidation and reduction simultaneously. In the given reaction, iron (Fe) in FeCl₃ is both oxidized and reduced, forming FeS (reduction) and Cl₂ (oxidation), respectively. Hence, it is a disproportionation reaction. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272;Paragraph 8.3.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exercise Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2 Na + Cl₂ → 2 NaCl','2 H₂ + O₂ → 2 H₂O','2 FeCl₃  + 3 H₂S → 6 HCl + 2 FeS + S','Cu + 2 AgNO₃ → Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2 Ag','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(221314,'chemistry','Which of the following species is getting reduced in the following reaction?                       2 KClO₃ → 2 KCl + 3 O₂','O₂','Explanation: In the given reaction, oxygen gas (O₂) is getting reduced as it gains electrons and its oxidation state decreases from 0 to -2, indicating reduction. KClO₃ is a reactant and its oxidation state remains unchanged, while KCl is a product and its oxidation state also remains unchanged. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265;Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exercise Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','KClO₃','KCl','O₂','None of the above','NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(221315,'chemistry','What is the reducing agent in the following reaction?                  2 KI + Cl₂ → 2 KCl + I₂','KI','Explanation: In the given reaction, potassium iodide (KI) acts as the reducing agent as it gets oxidized by losing electrons. It causes the reduction of chlorine (Cl₂) to chloride ions (Cl-) by transferring electrons to Cl₂, thereby undergoing oxidation itself. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265;Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exercise Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','KI','Cl₂','KCl','I₂','NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(221316,'chemistry','In the given reaction below identify the species undergoing oxidation                                    H₂S +Cl₂ (g) -> 2HCl(g) + S(s)','H₂S','H₂S is oxidised because a more electronegative element,chlorine is added to hydrogen (or a more electropositive element, hydrogen has been removed to from S). Chlorine is reduced due to addition of hydrogen to it. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265;Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exercise Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','H₂S','Cl₂','S','no species is oxidised','NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(221317,'chemistry','In the given reaction below identify the species undergoing oxidation.                           3Fe₃O₄(s) + 8 Al (s) ->9Fe(s) + 4Al₂O₃','Al','Aluminium is oxidised because oxygen is added to it . Ferrous ferric oxide is reduced because oxygen has been removed from it  (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265;Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exercise Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Fe₃O₄','Al','Fe','no species is oxidised','NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(221318,'chemistry','In the given reaction below identify the species undergoing oxidation.                                      2 Na(s) +H₂-> 2NaH(s)','Na','With the careful application of the concept of electronegativity only we may infer that sodium is oxidised and hydrogen is reduced . (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265;Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exercise Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Na','H₂','NaH','no species is oxidised','NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(221319,'chemistry','Which of the following species do not show disproportionation reaction','ClO₄⁻','ClO₄⁻  does not show disproportionation reaction because in this oxoanion chlorine is present in the highest oxidation state that is +7. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272;Paragraph 8.3.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exercise Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','ClO⁻','ClO₂⁻','ClO₃⁻','ClO₄⁻','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(221320,'chemistry','Identify the element that exhibit only negative oxidation state','F','F exhibit only negative oxidation state because of -1 because it has highest electronegativity (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267;Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exercise Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','F','Cs','Ne','I','NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(221321,'chemistry','Identify the element that exhibit only positive oxidation state','Cs','Cs exhibit positive oxidation state of +1 because it is a alkali metal atom (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267;Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exercise Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','F','Cs','Ne','I','NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(221322,'chemistry','Identify the element that exhibit both positive and negative  oxidation state','I','I exhibit both positive and negative oxidation state . It exhibit oxidation states of -1,+1,+3,+5 and +7 (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267;Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exercise Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','F','Cs','Ne','I','NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(221323,'chemistry','Identify the element that exhibit neither positive and negative  oxidation state','Ne','The oxidation state of Ne is zero .It can not exhibit negative or positive oxidation state because of its complete octate (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267;Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exercise Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','F','Cs','Ne','I','NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(221419,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a redox reaction','Calcium carbonate decomposition','CaCO₃->CaO + CO₂ in the above reaction oxidation state of calcium is +2,oxidation state of oxygen is -2 and oxidation state of carbon is -4 (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 263 ;Introduction )','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Burning of different types of fuels for obtaining energy for domestic,transport and other commercial purposes','Electrochemical process for extraction of highly reactive metals','Calcium carbonate decomposition','Manufacturing of chemical compounds like caustic soda','NEW','11-263','11-235',NULL),
(221420,'chemistry','Originally the term "oxidation "  was used to describe','Addition of oxygen to element or compound','The term "oxidation "  was used to describe addition of oxygen to element or compound(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 263 ; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Addition of electron','Addition of oxygen to element or compound','Removal of electrons from element or compound','Removal of oxygen from compound','NEW','11-263','11-235',NULL),
(221421,'chemistry','Choose the correct option for the following reaction       CH₄(g) +2O₂ (g) -> CO₂ (g) +2H₂O(l)','Methane is oxidised','CH₄(g) +2O₂ (g) -> CO₂ (g) +2H₂O(l) In the above reaction methane is converted to carbon dioxide and water due to addition of oxygen , here methane is getting oxidised owing to the addition of oxygen to it (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Methane is oxidised','Methane is reduced','It is not a redox reaction','Oxidation state of hydrogen changes','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(221422,'chemistry','Half reactions that involve gain of electrons are called','Reduction reaction','Eg: 2Na(s) ->2Na⁺ (g) + 2e⁻ In the above reaction Cl gains elctrons and becomes chlorine ion and gets reduced hence it is a reduction reaction (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265 ; Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Oxidation reactions','Reduction reaction','Displacement reaction','Disproportionation reaction','NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(221423,'chemistry','The oxidation of Magnesium with fluorine,chlorine,sulphur occurs according to following reactions :\n\na. Mg(s) + F₂(g) ->MgF₂ (s)	\n\nb. Mg(s) + Cl₂(g) ->MgCl₂ (s)	\n\nc. Mg(s) + S(s) ->MgS (s)	\n\nd. Mg(s) + F₂(g) ->MgF (s)','a,b,c','Mg undergoes oxidation with these elements. Removal of electrons and removal of electro positive element is also considered to be oxidative (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264 ; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b,c','a,b,d','b,c','a,c,d','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(221424,'chemistry','Half reactions that involve loss of electrons are called','Oxidation reactions','Eg: 2Na(s) ->2Na⁺ (g) + 2e⁻ In the above reaction there is a loss of electrons from sodium atom and it undergoes oxidation (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265 ; Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Oxidation reactions','Reduction reaction','Displacement reaction','Disproportionation reaction','NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(221425,'chemistry','In the set up for Daniel cells choose the correct statement :\n\na. Electrons are produced at anode        \n\nb. The circuit is completed inside cell by migration of ions through the salt bridge        \n\nc. Electrons are produced at anode        \n\nd. It may be noted that the direction of current is opposite to the direction of electron flow','a,b,d','As soon as switch is in the position in Daniel cell we observe                1. The transfer of electrons now does not take a place directly from Zn to Cu²⁺ but through  the metallic wire connecting the two rods as is apparent from the arrow which  indicates the flow of current.\n2. The electricity from solution in one beaker to solution in the other beaker flows by the migration of ions through the salt bridge. e We know that the flow of current is possible\nonly if there is a potential difference between the copper and zinc rods known as electrodes here.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 263 ; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b,c','a,b','b,c','a,b,d','NEW','11-263','11-235',NULL),
(221426,'chemistry','Which of the following is an example of a redox reaction?','Rusting of iron','Answer: d) Rusting of iron Explanation: Rusting of iron involves the oxidation of iron to form iron oxide, which is a redox reaction as it involves both oxidation and reduction reactions.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 263 ;Introduction )','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Burning of paper','Dissolving salt in water','Mixing oil and vinegar','Rusting of iron','NEW','11-263','11-235',NULL),
(221427,'chemistry','What is the reducing agent in the following reaction?     2AgNO3 + Cu → Cu(NO3)2 + 2Ag','Cu','Answer: a) Cu Explanation: In this reaction, Cu undergoes oxidation and loses electrons, thus acting as the reducing agent by reducing Ag+ ions to Ag atoms.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 269;Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Cu','AgNO3','Cu(NO3)2','Ag','NEW','11-269','11-241',NULL),
(221428,'chemistry','Which of the following is an example of a disproportionation reaction?','2Cu2O → 4Cu + O2','Answer: d) 2Cu2O → 4Cu + O2 Explanation: Disproportionation reaction occurs when a single species undergoes both oxidation and reduction simultaneously. In this case, Cu2O is getting reduced to Cu and oxidized to form O2.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272;Paragraph 8.3.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2Na + Cl2 → 2NaCl','2H2 + O2 → 2H2O','2Cl- → Cl2 + 2e-','2Cu2O → 4Cu + O2','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(221429,'chemistry','What is the oxidation number of chlorine in KClO3?','5','Answer: c) +5 Explanation: In KClO3, K has an oxidation number of +1, and O has an oxidation number of -2. Let the oxidation number of Cl be x. So, according to the rule that the sum of oxidation numbers in a compound is equal to the charge on the compound (which is 0 for KClO3), we can write the equation as +1 + x + (-2 x 3) = 0. Solving for x, we get x = +5.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 269;Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','-1','1','5','-5','NEW','11-269','11-241',NULL),
(221430,'chemistry','Which of the following is an example of a redox reaction used in a biological system?','Photosynthesis','Answer: c) Photosynthesis Explanation: Photosynthesis is a redox reaction that occurs in plants and some microorganisms, where carbon dioxide (CO2) is reduced to glucose (C6H12O6) using energy from sunlight. This process involves both oxidation and reduction reactions.','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Combustion of glucose','Neutralization of stomach acid','Photosynthesis','Electroplating','NEW','11-263','11-235',NULL),
(221431,'chemistry','Which of the following is used as a standard reducing agent in laboratory experiments?','H₂','Answer: c) H2 Explanation: Hydrogen gas (H2) is commonly used as a standard reducing agent in laboratory experiments, as it readily donates electrons to other species and undergoes reduction reactions.','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Cu','Fe','H₂','An','NEW','11-263','11-235',NULL),
(221432,'chemistry','Which of the following is not an application of redox reactions?','Neutralization reactions','Answer: d) Neutralization reactions Explanation: Neutralization reactions are not typically considered as redox reactions, as they do not involve electron transfer between species. Redox reactions are involved in applications such as batteries, corrosion prevention, and metabolism in living organisms.','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Batteries','Corrosion prevention','Metabolism in living organisms','Neutralization reactions','NEW','11-263','11-235',NULL),
(221433,'chemistry','Which of the following is a redox reaction that occurs during cellular respiration?','Glycolysis','Explanation: Glycolysis is a redox reaction that occurs during cellular respiration, where glucose is oxidized to produce ATP (adenosine triphosphate), NADH (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide), and pyruvate, involving both oxidation and reduction reactions.','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Photosynthesis','Fermentation','Glycolysis','Krebs cycle','NEW','11-263','11-235',NULL),
(221434,'chemistry','Which of the following is an example of a redox reaction used in the field of medicine?','Antioxidant activity','Explanation: Antioxidant activity is a redox reaction used in the field of medicine, where antioxidants act as reducing agents by donating electrons to neutralize harmful free radicals, preventing oxidative damage to cells and tissues.','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Neutralization of stomach acid','Combustion of drugs','Digestion of food','Antioxidant activity','NEW','11-263','11-235',NULL),
(150201,'chemistry','Solids that appear amorphous but have microcrystalline structure are called ________ ?','Polycrystalline solids','Solids that appear amorphous but have microcrystalline structure are called polycrystalline solids. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 4  Paragraph No.1 )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Classification of crystalline solids',NULL,'2','mcq','Monocrystalline solids','Bicrystalline solids','Multicrystalline solids','Polycrystalline solids','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150202,'chemistry','Which of the following often occurs in polycrysatlline condition?','Platinum','Metals often occurs in polycrystalline structure Pt, Ti etc. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 4  Paragraph No.1 )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Classification of crystalline solids',NULL,'2','mcq','Platinum','Iodine','Rubber','Glass','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150203,'chemistry','Which of the following is correct with reference to polycrystalline solids like metals?\n \n 1. Metallic sample may appear to be isotropic.\n \n 2. Single crystal of the sample also istropic.','1 is correct and 2 is incorrect.','In metals, the metalic sample may apper to be isotropic even through single crystal of sample is an istropic. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 4  Paragraph No.1 )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Classification of crystalline solids',NULL,'3','mcq','1 and 2 are correct','1 and 2 are incorrect.','1 is correct and 2 is incorrect.','1 is incorrect and 2 is correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150204,'chemistry','Which one of the following is best photovoltaic material?','Amorphous','Amorphous silicon is one of the best, photovoltaic material. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 4  Paragraph No.2 )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Classification of crystalline solids',NULL,'1','mcq','Amorphous','Crystalline','Polycrystaline','Semi-solid','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150205,'chemistry','Match the following.','Amorphous solids-pvc and cellophane,Crystalline solids-benzoic acid and copper','Crystalline solids have long-range order while amorphous solids have short-range order. Sodium chloride is an example of crystalline solid. Amorphous solids are also called as pseudo solids and super cooled liquids. For example glass, plastic and rubber.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 4  intext question )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Classification of crystalline solids',NULL,'3','match','Amorphous solids,pvc and cellophane','Crystalline solids,benzoic acid and copper',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150206,'chemistry','Most of the solid substances are ________ in nature.','Crystalline','Most of the solids substances are crystalline in nature. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 4  Paragraph No.3 )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Classification of crystalline solids',NULL,'1','mcq','Amorphous','Crystalline','Polycrystaline','Semi-solid','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150207,'chemistry','Match the following','Nonpolar molecular-argon,polar molecular-HCL,Hydrogen bonded-Water,ionic-potassium nitrate','Most of the solid substances are crystalline in nature. For example, all the metallic elements like iron, copper and silver; non-metallic elements like sulphur, phosphorus and iodine and compounds like sodium chloride, zinc sulphide and naphthalene form crystalline solids.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 4-5  Paragraph No.1 )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Classification of crystalline solids',NULL,'3','match','nonpolar molecular,argon','polar molecular,HCL','Hydrogen bonded,Water','ionic,potassium nitrate','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150208,'chemistry','Select the coorrect statement/s.\n \n (i) Amorphous solids have irregular shape.\n \n (ii) Crystalline solids melt at a sharp and characteristic temperature.\n \n (iii) Crystalline solids give plain smooth surface when cut.\n \n (iv) Amorphous solids have definite enthalpy of fusion.','i, ii and iii','Amorphous solids have irregular shape. They gradually soften over a range of temperature. When cut with a sharp edged tool, they cut into two pieces with irregular surfaces. They do not have definite enthalpy of fusion. Crystalline solids have definite characteristic geometrical shape. They  melt at a sharp and characteristic temperature. When cut with a sharp edged tool, they split into two pieces and the newly generated surfaces are plain and smooth. They have a definite and characteristic enthalpy of fusion.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 3 Paragraph 3, table 1.1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Classification of crystalline solids',NULL,'3','mcq','i and ii','ii, iii and iv','ii and iii','i, ii and iii','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150209,'chemistry','What kind of intermolcular forces are found in crystalline solids?','All of above','Crystalline solids can be classified in various ways. The method depends on the purpose in hand. Here, we will classify crystalline solids on the basis of nature of intermolecular forces or bonds that hold the constituent particles together. These are — (i) Van der waals forces; (ii) Ionic bonds; (iii) Covalent bonds; and (iv) Metallic bonds.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 4 Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Classification of crystalline solids',NULL,'2','mcq','Van der waals forces','Ionic bonds','Covalent bonds and metallic bonds','All of above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150210,'chemistry','Molecular solids can be subdivided into:','Polar, non-polar and hydrogen bonded molecular solids','Molecules are the constituent particles of molecular solids. These are further sub divided into the following categories: (i) Non polar molecular solids, (ii) Polar molecular solids, (iii) Hydrogen bonded molecular solids.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 4 Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Classification of crystalline solids',NULL,'2','mcq','Polar and non- polar molecular solids','Polar, non-polar and hydrogen bonded molecular solids','Dipole-dipole and van der waal molecular solids','Covalent and ionic molecular solids','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150211,'chemistry','Which of the following options are correct?\n \n a, Non-polar materials are held by low force\n \n b, They are non-conductor of electricity and are hard\n \n c, They have low melting Points\n \n d, They are usually in liquid or gaseous state at high temperature and pressure only','a and c','Non-polar materials are held by low force, They are non-conductor of electricty and are soft , They have low melting Points\nand  are usually in the liquid or gaseous state at normal temperature and pressure only (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 5  Paragraph No.1 )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Classification of crystalline solids',NULL,'2','mcq','a, b and c','a,c and d','a and c','All of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150212,'chemistry','Which of the following are volatile liquid or soft solids under room temperature and pressure?','H₂O','Hydrogen bonded molecular solids are volatile liquid or soft solids under room temperature and pressure. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 5  Paragraph No.2 )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Classification of crystalline solids',NULL,'2','mcq','H₂','I₂','SO₂','H₂O','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150213,'chemistry','Which of the following are/is incorrect\n \n 1. Ionic solids are electrical insulators in solid-state.\n \n 2. Ionic solids are electrical insulators in the molten state but conductors in dissolved form.','1 is correct and 2 is incorrect.','1 is correct and 2 is incorrect.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Classification of crystalline solids',NULL,'2','mcq','1 and 2 are correct','1 and 2 are incorrect.','1 is correct and 2 is incorrect.','1 is incorrect and 2 is correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150214,'chemistry','Which of the following solids are hard and brittle in nature?','NaCl','Ionic solids are genrally hard and brittle.  \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 5 Paragraph No.4 )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Classification of crystalline solids',NULL,'2','mcq','HCl','SO₂','NH₃','NaCl','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150215,'chemistry','Free electrons are responsible for which of the following features of metal?\n \n a. Colour\n \n b. Electrical resistance \n \n c. Thermal conductance\n \n d. Lustre','a,c and d','Free electrons is in metals are responsible for electrical and thermal conductance as well as colour and lustre. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 5 Paragraph No.5 )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Classification of crystalline solids',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b and d','a,b and c','a,c and d','a and d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150216,'chemistry','Which of the following is a gaint molecule?','Silicon carbide','Covalent solids are called gaint molecules. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 5 Paragraph No.6)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Classification of crystalline solids',NULL,'2','mcq','Silicon carbide','NaCl','NH₃','H₂O','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150217,'chemistry','Which of the following has extremly high melting point?','Covalent solids','Covalent solids has extremely high melting point and even decompose before melting. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 5 Paragraph No.6)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Classification of crystalline solids',NULL,'1','mcq','Molecular solids','Ionic solids','Metallic solids','Covalent solids','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150302,'chemistry','Which of the following is solid lubricant?','Graphite','Graphite has a layer structure and different layers can slide over one another, making it a good lubricant. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 6 Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Close Packed Structures',NULL,'1','mcq','Fluorocarbon','Modifier','Silicon','Graphite','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
