(150415,'chemistry','Centred unit cells are of______ types.','3','Centred unit cells are of three types. 1. End-centred unit cell, 2. Body-centred unit cell, 3. Face-centred unit cell\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 9 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150416,'chemistry','Body-centred unit cell has consitutent particles present at________.','Its body-centre and at its corners','Such a unit cell contains one constituent particle (atom, molecule or ion) at its body-centre besides the ones that are at its corners.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 9 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'3','mcq','Its body-centre','Corners only','Its body-centre and at its corners','Centre of each face','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150417,'chemistry','Face-centred unit cell has consitutent particles present at________.','Centre of each face and at its corners','Such a unit cell contains one constituent particle present at the centre of each face, besides the ones that are at its corners.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 9 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'3','mcq','Its body-centre','Corners only','Centre of each face and at its corners','Centre of each face','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150418,'chemistry','End-centred unit cell has consitutent particles present at________.','One constituent particle is present at the centre of any two opposite faces and at its corners','In such a unit cell, one constituent particle is present at the centre of any two opposite faces besides the ones present at its corners.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 9 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'3','mcq','One constituent particle is present at the centre of any two opposite faces','Corners only','One constituent particle is present at the centre of any two opposite faces and at its corners','Centre of each face','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150419,'chemistry','Which among the following represents each small sphere as only the centre of the particle occupying that position as per the given diagram?(n1507)','a','Each small sphere in Fig.(a) represents only the centre of the particle occupying that position and not its actual size. Such structures are called open structures.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 12 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','a and b','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/150419q.png'),
(150420,'chemistry','Which among the following shows the actual portions of different atoms present in a cubic unit cell as per the given diagram?(n1507)','c','Each small sphere in Fig.(a) represents only the centre of the particle occupying that position and not its actual size. Such structures are called open structures. Fig. (b) depicts space-filling representation of the unit cell with actual particle size and Fig. (c) shows the actual portions of different atoms present in a cubic unit cell.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 12 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','a and c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/150420q.png'),
(150421,'chemistry','What is the contribution of each corner atom to a unit cell?','1 / 8','Each corner atom is shared between 8 adjacent unit cells four in same layer, four in same layer and 4 in upper or lower layer, therefore net contribution of a corner atom to a unit cell is 1/8.  \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 12, paragraph no 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'1','mcq','1 / 2','1 / 4','1 / 8','1 / 16','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150422,'chemistry','What is the total no of atoms in one unit cell of primitive cubic unit cells?','1','Each cubic unit cell has 8 atoms on its corners, so total no of atoms in 1 unit cell is 8×1/8=1 atom. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 12, paragraph no 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','12','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150423,'chemistry','Which of the following is a space filling structure of primitive unit cell? diagram(n1504)','b','An image or physical depiction of a molecule with partial spheres of atoms that are linked together directly and have diameters proportional to those of the actual atoms is known as a space filling model.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 12-13)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','All of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/150423q.png'),
(150424,'chemistry','What is the total no of atoms in two unit cell of body centred cubic unit cells?','4','In BCC, the total no of atoms in 1 unit cell is 2. \n(i) 8 corners × 1/8 per corner atom = 1 atom.\n(ii) 1 body centre atom.\nTotal no of atoms in 2 unit cells of BCC = 2×2=4.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page  13; Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'2','mcq','1','4','8','16','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150425,'chemistry','What is the contribution of each corner atom at face centre?','1 / 2','Each atom at face centre is shared between 2 adjacent cells. so its contribution to each unit cell is 1/2. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page  13; Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'1','mcq','1 / 2','1 / 4','1 / 8','1 / 6','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150426,'chemistry','What is the total number of atoms in a face centred cubic unit cell?','4','A face-centred cubic (fcc) unit cell contains atoms at all the corners and at the centre of all the faces of the cube. Each atom located at the face-centre is shared between two adjacent unit cells and only 1/2 of each atom belongs to a unit cell. Thus, in a face-centred cubic (FCC) unit cell:\n(i) 8 corners atoms × 1/8 atom per unit cell = 8 x 1/8 = 1 atom.\n(ii) 6 face-centred atoms × 1/2 atom per unit cell = 6 × 1/2 = 3 atoms\nTotal number of atoms per unit cell = 4 atoms\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page  13; Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','4','12','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150427,'chemistry','What portion of an atom at body centre is shared with a neighbouring unit cell?','0%','An atom at body centre is not shared at all width a neighbouring unit cell. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page  13; Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'2','mcq','100%','50%','25%','0%','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150428,'chemistry','What is the number of atoms in a cubic unit cell, which has one atom on each corner and 2 atoms on each body diagonal?','9','Total no of body = 4 and each has 2 atom \nTotal no of atoms on the body diagonals = 4×2= 8\nTotal no of atoms on the corner= 8×1/8=1\nTotal atoms = 8+1= 9\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 12- 13)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'3','mcq','1','8','5','9','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150429,'chemistry','Potassium crystalline in body centred cubic lattice. What is the number of unit cells in 4g of potassium? ( At wt of k=3)','3.08 x 10²²','There will be eight atoms at corners of the cube and one atom at the body centre.\nNumber of atoms per unit cell = ( 8 × 1/8) = 2\nNumber of atoms in 4.0 g of potassium = 4/39 x 6.023 x 10²³\nNumber of unit cells in 4.0 g of potassium = 4/39 x 6.023 x 10²³/2 = 3.08 x 10²²\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 12- 13)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'3','mcq','0.77 x 10²²','1.54 x 10²²','3.08 x 10²²','6.16 x 10²²','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150430,'chemistry','Unit cell consists of a cube in which X atoms are present at corners and Y atoms at face centre, if Y atoms are missing from 2 corners of the unit cell, what is the formula of compound?','XY₂','X atoms are at the corners and Y atoms are at the face centres. Therefore, the given compound has an FCC lattice structure.\nIf two Y atoms are missing, contribution from corner atoms = 8 x 1/8 = 1.\nContribution of Y atoms at face centres = 1/2 x 4 = 2\nFormula of the compound = XY₂\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 12- 13)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'2','mcq','XY','XY₂','X₂Y','XY₄','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150431,'chemistry','What is the cordination number in one dimensional close packed structure ?','2','In 1-D closed packed structure, each atom is in contact with two of its neighbours thus CN is 2. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 14; Paragraph No 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150432,'chemistry','Choose the correct statements\n \n a. 2-D ABAb type packing is more efficient than 2-D AAA type.\n \n b. 2-D ABAB type packing has hexagonal voids.','a is correct but b is incorrect','In ABAB arrangements there is less void then AA type square packing in 2-D Making it more efficient, In 2-D ABAB type Packing, triangular voids are present.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 15; Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'3','mcq','Both statements are correct','a is correct but b is incorrect','a is incorrect but b is correct','Both statements are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150433,'chemistry','What is the percentage of occupied area in 2-D square close packing?','78%','The percentage of occupied area in 2-D square close packing is 78%.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 14; fig 1.18a)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'3','mcq','52%','68%','78%','90%','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150434,'chemistry','What is the percentage of occupied area in 2-D hexagonal close packing?','90%','The percentage of occupied area in 2-D hexagonal close packing is 90%.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 14; fig 1.18b)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'3','mcq','52%','68%','78%','90%','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150435,'chemistry','What is kind of void shown in diagram(n1505)','Terahedral void','When the centres of the four spheres are joined, a tertahedral is formed, hence the void is called tetrahedral void.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 14; fig 1.21a) diagram(ne1503)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'3','mcq','Upward Triangular void','Downward triangular void','Terahedral void','Octahedral void','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/150435q.png'),
(150436,'chemistry','What is kind of void shown in diagram(n1506)','Octahedral void','When the centres of the 6 spheres are joined, a octahedral is formed, hence the void is called octahedral void. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 14; fig 1.21a) diagram(ne1504)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'3','mcq','Upward Triangular void','Downward triangular void','Terahedral void','Octahedral void','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/150436q.png'),
(150437,'chemistry','What is no of octahedral voids per atom ccp?','1','If no of atoms in fcc is \'n\' then no of octahedral voids is also \'n\' so 1 octahedral void per atom. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 17; Pagraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150512,'chemistry','What does the given diagram depict? (n1509)','Open structure of a unit cell','A body-centred cubic (bcc) unit cell has an atom at each of its corners and also one atom at its body centre. Fig. depicts open structure.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 13 Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Electrtical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Open structure of a unit cell','Space-filling representation of the unit cell','Centre of the particle occupying that position','Actual portions of different atoms present in a cubic unit cell','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/150512q.png'),
(150513,'chemistry','Select the correct statement/s.\n \n (i) In solids, the constituent particles are close-packed.\n \n (ii) There are large vacuum spaces in solids.\n \n (iii) Solids are of 2 types.\n \n (iv) Thermal enegry is uniformly distributed in metallic solids.\n \n (v) Metals are highly malleable and ductile.','i, iii, iv and v','In solids, the constituent particles are close-packed, leaving the minimum vacant space. Let us consider the constituent particles as identical hard spheres and build up the three-dimensional structure in three steps.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 14 Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Electrtical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','i, ii, iii, iv and v','i, iii and v','i, iii, iv and v','Only v','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150514,'chemistry','Which of the following discription truely depicts the arrangment of solid particles in one dimension?','Arrangment of spheres in a row in which they are touching each other.','There is only one way of arranging spheres in a one-dimensional close packed structure, that is to arrange them in a row and touching each other.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 14 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Electrtical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Stacking (placing) the rows of close packed spheres exactly above one another.','Arrangment of spheres in a row in which they are touching each other.','Staggering of particles in a  manner such that its spheres fit in the depressions','Two rows of particles are aligned horizontally','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150515,'chemistry','In 2 dimensional packing, the two rows are stacked such that _______.','The second row are exactly above those of the first row.','Two dimensional close packed structure can be generated by stacking (placing) the rows of close packed spheres. The second row may be placed in contact with the first one such that the spheres of the second row are exactly above those of the first row. The spheres of the two rows are aligned horizontally as well as vertically.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 14 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Electrtical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','The second row are exactly above those of the first row.','Spheres in a row in which they are touching each other.','The spheres of the two rows are aligned only horizontally.','Each sphere is in contact with two of its neighbours.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150516,'chemistry','In 2 dimensional square close packing, each sphere is in contact with ______ of its neighbours.','4','In this arrangement, each sphere is in contact with four of its neighbours. Thus, the two dimensional coordination number is 4. Also, if the centres of these 4 immediate neighbouring spheres are joined, a square is formed. Hence this packing is called square close packing in two dimensions.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 15 Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Electrtical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','3','4','5','6','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150517,'chemistry','In two dimensional close packing, if the centres of 4 immediate neighbours are joined _______ is formed.','Square','In this arrangement, each sphere is in contact with four of its neighbours. Thus, the two dimensional coordination number is 4. Also, if the centres of these 4 immediate neighbouring spheres are joined, a square is formed. Hence this packing is called square close packing in two dimensions.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 15 Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Electrtical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Tetrahedral','Octahedral','Square','Hexagon','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150518,'chemistry','Which of the following close packing is most efficient?','Hexagonal close packing','In hexagonal close packing, there is less free space and this packing is more efficient than the square close packing. Each sphere is in contact with six of its neighbours and the two dimensional coordination number is 6.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 15 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Electrtical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Hexagonal close packing','Square close packing','Tetrahedral closes packing','Close packing in one dimension','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150519,'chemistry','The first and the thrid layer atoms in hexagonal closed packing are________.','Aligned to each other','The second row may be placed above the first one in a staggered manner such that its spheres fit in the depressions of the first row. If the arrangement of spheres in the first row is called ‘A’ type, the one in the second row is different and may be called ‘B’ type. When the third row is placed adjacent to the second in staggered manner, its spheres are aligned with those of the first layer. Hence this layer is also of ‘A’ type.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 15 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Electrtical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Aligned to each other','Diagonal to each other','Separated by a distance twice than adjacent layers','Separated by a distance 4 times than adjacent layers','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150520,'chemistry','All real structures are__________ structures.','3 dimensional','All real structures are three dimensional structures. They can be obtained by stacking two dimensional layers one above the other.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 15 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Electrtical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','1 dimensional','2 dimensional','3 dimensional','Hexagonal','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150711,'chemistry','Density of KBr is 2.75 g km³. The edge length of unit cells is 654pm. Find the type of unit cell of KBr (K=39 Br=80).','fcc','For KBr M= 39+80 =119 \na=654 x10⁻¹⁰cm  d= 2.75 cm³ \nd=Z x M/Nₐ x a³  or  z = d x Nₐ x a³/m on solving Z = 3.89 = 4. Hence it is fcc lattice. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 23)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'3','mcq','fcc','hcp','bcc','Simple cubic lattice','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150712,'chemistry','Choose the correct relation between radius (r) and site length in ccp.','a=2√2r','If r is the radius of the sphere, we find b = 4r = √2a or a = 4r/√2 = 2√2r.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 20)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'2','mcq','a=2√2r','r=2√2a','a=√6r','r=√6a','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150713,'chemistry','Choose the correct relation between radius (r) and site length in bcc.','r=√3/4 a','The length of the body diagonal c is equal to 4r, where r is the radius of the sphere (atom), as all the three spheres along the diagonal touch each other. Therefore, √3a = 4r, a = 4r/√3. Also we can write, r=√3/4 a\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 21)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'2','mcq','r=√3/4 a','a=√3/4 r','a=2√2r','r=2√2a','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150714,'chemistry','Defects in crystals occurs when crystallisation occurs at________','All of the above','Defects in crystals occur when crystallisation occurs at a fast or moderate rate but even when it occurs at an extremely slow rate. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 24 Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'2','mcq','Fast rate','Moderate rate','Extremely slow rate','All of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150715,'chemistry','Stoichiometric defect is also known as','Thermodynamic defect','Stoichiometric defect are also known as Intrinsic defect and thermodynamic defect. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 24 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'1','mcq','Frenkel defects','Impurity defect','Thermodynamic defect','Vacancy defects','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150716,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a type of point ?','Crystal defect','Point defect is of 3 types stoichiometric defect,non-soichiometric defect and Impurity defect.   \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 24 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'1','mcq','Stoichiometric defect','Non-stoichiometric defect','Impurity defect','Crystal defect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150717,'chemistry','Vacancy defect leads to which of the following ?','Decrease in density','When some of the lattice sites are vacant, the crystal is said to have vacancy defect. This results in decrease in density of the substance. This defect can also develop when a substance is heated.  \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 24 Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'1','mcq','Decrease in conductivity','Increase in area','Decrease in density','Increase in mass','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150718,'chemistry','Which of the following defect can develop when a substance is heated?','Vacancy defects','When some of the lattice sites are vacant, the crystal is said to have vacancy defect. This results in decrease in density of the substance. This defect can also develop when a substance is heated.  \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 24 Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'1','mcq','Interstitial defect','Vacancy defects','Frenkel defects','Schottkey defect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150719,'chemistry','Select the correct statement \n \n a. Interstitial defect increases the density of a substance. \n \n b. Ionic solids must always maintain electrical neutrality.','Both statements are correct','Interstitial defect increases the density of substance ionic solids must always maintain electrical neutrality  (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 24 Paragraph 6)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'2','mcq','Both statements are correct','a is correct but b is incorrect','a is incorrect but b is correct','Both statements are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150720,'chemistry','Frenkel defect is also known as ?','Dislocation deffect','Frenkel defect forms a vacancy site at original site and interstitial defect at new site hence also known as dislocation deffect.  \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 25 Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'1','mcq','Vacancy Defect','Schottky defect','Dislocation deffect','Interstitial defect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150721,'chemistry','Select the correct statement \n \n a. Frenkel defect is shown by an ionic substance in which there is a small difference in the size of ions.\n \n b. Frenkel defect is shown by ZnS due to small size of Zn⁺² and Ag⁺ ions.','a is incorrect but b is correct','Frenkel defect is shown by an ionic substance in which there is a larger difference in the size of ions. Frenkel defect is shown by ZnS due to small size of Zn⁺² and Ag⁺ ions.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 25 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'2','mcq','Both statements are correct','a is correct but b is incorrect','a is incorrect but b is correct','Both statements are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(213323,'chemistry','What is the largest source of F?','Sea water','The largest source of F is Sea water\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 202 Intext question)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Sea water','Sea weeds','Marine shells','Fresh water','NEW','12-202',NULL,NULL),
(150722,'chemistry','Select the correct statement','Frenkel does not change the density of solid','Frenkel defect a vacancy is created at its original site  and interstitial defect at its new site, the density of solids remains unchanged. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 25 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'2','mcq','Frenkel defect creates an interstitial defect at its original site','Frenkel defect creates a vacancy defect at its new site','Frenkel does not change the density of solid','Frenkel defect creates a schottkey defect at its new site','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150723,'chemistry','Schottky defect in ionic solids is basically a __________ defect.','Vacancy','Schottky defect is basically a vacancy defect in ionic solids in oder to maintain electrical neutrality equal number of cations and anions are missing. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 25 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'1','mcq','Vacancy','Interstitial','Dislocation','Frenkel','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150724,'chemistry','How many of the following defects lead to decrease in density vacancy defect, interstital defect, frenkel defect, schottky defect?','2','Both vacancy and shottky defects lead to decrease in density while interstital defects increase density and frenkel defect causes no change in density. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 24-25)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150725,'chemistry','What is the number of schottky pairs per cm³ in NaCl?','10⁶','In NaCl, there are approximately 10⁶ schottky pairs per cm³ at room temperature. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 25 Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'1','mcq','10⁶','10¹⁶','10⁸','10²²','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150726,'chemistry','How many ions are present in 1 cm³ of NaCl?','10²²','There are about 10²² ions in 1 cm³ of NaCl. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 25 Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'1','mcq','10⁶','10¹⁶','10⁸','10²²','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150727,'chemistry','What is the number of schottky defect per ion?','10⁻¹⁶','There is 1 Schottky defect per 10⁻¹⁶ ions. so 10⁻¹⁶ schottky defects per ion. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 25 Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'1','mcq','10⁶','10¹⁶','10⁻¹⁶','10²²','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150810,'chemistry','Semiconductors have electrical conductivities in the range from ______.','10⁻⁶ to 10⁴ ohm⁻¹ m⁻¹','Semiconductors have conductivities in the intermediate range from 10⁻⁶ to 10⁴ ohm⁻¹ m⁻¹. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 27 Paragraph 1 )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Magnetic properties',NULL,'1','mcq','10⁴ to 10⁷ ohm⁻¹ m⁻¹','10⁻²⁰ to 10⁻¹⁰ ohm⁻¹ m⁻¹','10⁻⁶ to 10⁴ ohm⁻¹ m⁻¹','10⁻⁴ to 10⁶ ohm⁻¹ m⁻¹','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150811,'chemistry','Conductors conduct electricity through movement of _____.','Electrons','A conductor may conduct electricity through movement of electrons or ions. Metallic conductors belong to the former category and electrolytes to the latter. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 27 Paragraph 2 )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Magnetic properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Atoms','Molecules','Electrons','Radicals','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150812,'chemistry','Select the correct statement.\n \n a. Conductivity of metals is independent of the number of valance electrons available. \n \n b. Atomic orbitals of metal atoms from molecular orbitals which are so close in energy to each other that they form a band.','a is incorrect but b is correct','Conductivity of metals depends on no of valance electron available per atom (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 27 Paragraph 2 )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Magnetic properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Both statements are correct','a is correct but b is incorrect','a is incorrect but b is correct','Both statements are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150813,'chemistry','If in a solid energy gap between valance and conduction bond is small then which of the following may be that solid?','Si','Semiconductors have small energy gap. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 27 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Magnetic properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Cu','Glass','Si','Ag','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150814,'chemistry','Forbidden zone is found in which of the following materials?','Glass','Forbidden zone is seen in insulators. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 27 fig 1.33(b))','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Magnetic properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Cu','Glass','Si','Ag','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150815,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is false?','N type semiconductor is electrically negative.','The option a statement is false. When pure semiconductor, which is also electrically neutral, is combined with impurity atoms, which are electrically neutral in and of itself, the p-type and n-type semi conductors are created. The resulting p- or n-type semiconductor is therefore electrically neutral. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 28 Paragraph 2 )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Magnetic properties',NULL,'1','mcq','N type semiconductor is electrically negative.','Silicon doped with electron-rich impurity forms n-type semiconductor.','The n-type semiconductor conducts electricity through the free electrons.','Elements of group 14 when dopped with elements of group 15 produce n-type semiconductors.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150816,'chemistry','Si on being doped with which of the following will form n type semic conductors?','Arsenic','Group 15 dopants form n type semiconductors.  \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 28 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Magnetic properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Carbon','gallium','Arsenic','Boron','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150817,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is true?','Place where the valence electron is missing is called electron hole or electron vacancy','Silicon or germanium can also be doped with a group 13 element like B, Al or Ga which contains only three valence electrons. The place where the fourth valence electron is missing is called electron hole or electron vacancy. An electron from a neighbouring atom can come and fill the electron hole, but in doing so it would leave an electron hole at its original position.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 28 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Magnetic properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Silicon or germanium can also be doped with a group 13 element give n-type semiconductors.','Place where the valence electron is missing is called electron hole or electron vacancy','In p-type semiconductor, the conductivity is through the electrons.','The n-type semiconductor conducts electricity through the holes.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150818,'chemistry','Ge on being doped with which of the following will form p-type semic conductors?','Thallium','Group 1 dopants form p type semiconductors. Silicon or germanium can also be doped with a group 13 element like B, Al or Ga which contains only three valence electrons. The place where the fourth valence electron is missing is called electron hole or electron vacancy. An electron from a neighbouring atom can come and fill the electron hole, but in doing so it would leave an electron hole at its original position. This type of semi conductors are called p-type semiconductors.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 28 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Magnetic properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Thallium','Carbon','Arsenic','Antimony','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150819,'chemistry','Which of the following is used as a rectifier?','Diode','Various combinations of n-type and p-type semiconductors are used for making electronic components. Diode is a combination of n-type and p-type semiconductors and is used as a rectifier.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 29 Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Magnetic properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Solar Cell','Diode','npn transistor','pnp transistor','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150820,'chemistry','Diode is a _______?','Combination of n and p type semiconductor','Various combinations of n-type and p-type semiconductors are used for making electronic components. Diode is a combination of n-type and p-type semiconductors and is used as a rectifier.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 29 Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Magnetic properties',NULL,'1','mcq','p-type semiconductor','n-type semiconductor','pnp semiconductor','Combination of n and p type semiconductor','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150912,'chemistry','Select the correct statement.\n \n a. Paramagnetic substances are strongly attracted in a magnetic field.\n \n b. Paramagnetic substances are magnetised in the same direction as external magnetic field.','a is incorrect but b is correct','Paramagnetic substances are weakly magnetic field. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 29 Paragraph 5)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both statements are correct','a is correct but b is incorrect','a is incorrect but b is correct','Both statements are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150913,'chemistry','How many of the following are paramagnetic substances?\n \n O₂+Cu⁺²,H₂O,C₆H₆,Cr⁺³,Fe⁺³,NaCl,MnO','4','Paramagnetism is due to presence of one or more unpaired electrons which are attracted by the magnetic field.Examples of paramagnetic substance O₂+Cu⁺²,Cr⁺³,Fe⁺³. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 29 Paragraph 5)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1','3','4','6','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150914,'chemistry','Which of the following substances is repelled by maganetic field?','NaCl','Diamagnetic substances are repelled by magnetic field. NaCl,C₆H₆ and water are some examples of such materials. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 30 Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Fe⁺³','Cr⁺³','NaCl','O₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150915,'chemistry','Diamagnetism is shown by substances having______.','No unpaired electron','Diamagnestism shown by substances having no unpaired elctrons. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 30 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Odd Number of unpaired Electrons','Even Number of unpaired','No unpaired electron','unpaired electrons','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150916,'chemistry','How many of the following substances are ferromagnetic?\n \n Fe,Co,Ni,Gd,CrO₂,CrO₃,MnO,Fe₃O₄','5','Examples of ferromagnetic substances are iron, cobalt, nickel, gadolinium and CrO₂. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 30 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','3','4','5','6','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150917,'chemistry','How many of the following substances are not ferromagnetic?\n \n Fe,Co,Ni,Gd,CrO₂,CrO₃,MnO,Fe₃O₄','3','Examples of ferromagnetic substances: Fe,Co,Ni,Gd,CrO₂.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 30 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','3','4','5','6','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150918,'chemistry','Which of the following substances does not have magnetic domains?','Diamagnetic','Domains are observed in ferromagnetic, antiferromagnetic and ferrimagnetic substances. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 30 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Feromagnetic','Diamagnetic','Antimagnetic','Ferrimagnetic','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150919,'chemistry','Given below are two statements.\n \n Assertion: Ferromagnetic substances can be permanently magnetised.\n \n Reasons: Ordering of domains persist even when the magnetic field is removed.\n \n In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Ferromagnetism: A few substances like iron, cobalt, nickel, gadolinium and CrO₂ are attracted very strongly by a magnetic field. Such substances are called ferromagnetic substances. Besides strong attractions, these substances can be permanently magnetised. In solid state, the metal ions of ferromagnetic substances are grouped together into small regions called domains. Thus, each domain acts as a tiny magnet. In an unmagnetised piece of a ferromagnetic substance the domains are randomly oriented and their magnetic moments get cancelled. When the substance is placed in a magnetic field all the domains get oriented in the direction of the magnetic field and a strong magnetic effect is produced. This ordering of domains persist even when the magnetic field is removed and the ferromagnetic substance becomes a permanent magnet.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 30 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false.','Assertion is false but eason is true.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151217,'chemistry','If a stands for the edge length of the cubic systems: simple cubic, body-centred cubic and face-centred cubic, then the ratio of radii of the spheres in these systems will be respectively','1/2a : √3/4a : 1/2√2a','For simple cubic : r = a/2\nFor body centred : r = a√3 / 4\nFor face-centred: r = a/2√2\nwhere a = edge length, r = radius.\nRatio of radii of the three will be = 1/2a : √3/4a : 1/2√2a\n(2008)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1/2a : √3/2a : √2/2a','1a : √3a : √2a','1/2a : √3/4a : 1/2√2a','1/2a : √3a : 1/√2a','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151218,'chemistry','The fraction of total volume occupied by the atoms present in a simple cube is','π/6','The packing fraction measures how much space an atom or atoms takes up in a unit cell. Edge length is twice as long as radius, as seen in the left portion of the image. The number of effective atom z = 8 x 1/8 =1\nPacking fraction for a cubic unit cell is given by\nf = Z x 4/3πr³/ a³ = π/6\n(2007)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','π/3√2','π/4√2','π/4','π/6','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151219,'chemistry','The pyknometric density of sodium chloride crystal is 2.165 × 10³ kg m⁻³ while its X-ray density is 2.178 × 10³ kg m⁻³. The fraction of unoccupied sites in sodium chloride crystal is','5.96 × 10⁻³','Molar volume from pyknometric density = M / 2.165 x 10³ m³. \nMolar volume from X-ray density = M / 2.178 x 10³ m³\nVolumn occupied = M / 10³ [ 1/ 2.165 - 1/ 2.178] m³\nFraction unoccupied = [ 0.013M x 10⁻³ / 2.165 x 2.178] / [M x 10⁻³/ 2.165] = 5.96 x 10⁻³\n(2003)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','5.96','5.96 × 10⁻²','5.96 × 10⁻¹','5.96 × 10⁻³','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151220,'chemistry','The edge length of face-centred unit cubic cells is 508 pm. If the radius of the cation is 110 pm, the radius of the anion is','144 pm','In the face–centred cubic lattice, the edge length of the unit cell, a = r + 2R + r, where r = Radius of cation, R = Radius of anion ⇒ 508 = 2 × 110 + 2R ⇒ R = 144 pm\n(1998)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','144 pm','398 pm','288 pm','618 pm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151221,'chemistry','Iron exhibits bcc structure at room temperature. Above 900°C, it transforms to fcc structure. The ratio of density of iron at room temperature to that at 900°C (assuming molar mass and atomic radii of iron remains constant with temperature) is','3√3/4√2','For bcc lattice : Z = 2, a = 4r / √3,\nFor fcc lattice : Z = 4, a = 2√2 r \ndᵣ.ₜ/ d₉₀₀°C = [ZxM / N a³]ᵦ𝒸𝒸/ [ZxM / N a³]f𝒸𝒸\nGiven, molar mass and atomic radii are constant = 3√3/4√2  \n(NEET 2018)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','√3/√2','4√3/3√2','3√3/4√2','1/2','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151222,'chemistry','CsBr crystallises in a body-centred cubic lattice. The unit cell length is 436.6 pm. Given that the atomic mass of Cs = 133 and that of Br = 80 amu and Avogadro number being 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹, the density of CsBr is','4.25 g/cm³','Density of CsBr = Z x M/ a³ x Nₐ = 1 x 213/ (436.6 x 10⁻¹⁰)³ x 6.022 x 10²³ = 4.25g/cm³ \n(2006)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','4.25 g/cm³','42.5 g/cm³','0.425 g/cm³','8.25 g/cm³','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151223,'chemistry','An element (atomic mass = 100 g/mol) having bcc structure has unit cell edge 400 pm. The density of element is','5.188 g/cm³','Cell edge = 400 pm; number of atoms in bcc (Z) = 2 and atomic mass = 100 g/mol. Since atomic mass is 100 g/mol, therefore mass of each atom (m) = 100/6.022 x 10²³ = 1.66 x 10⁻²³g. We know that volume of unit cell = (400 pm)³ = (64 × 10⁶) pm³ = 64 × 10⁻²⁴ cm³ and mass of unit cell = Z × m = 2 × (16.6 × 10⁻²³) = 33.2 × 10⁻²³ g. d = M / V = 5.188 g/cm³ \n(1996)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','7.289 g/cm³','2.144 g/cm³','10.376 g/cm³','5.188 g/cm³','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(151224,'chemistry','Formula of nickel oxide with metal deficiency defect in its crystal is Ni₀.₉₈O. The crystal contains Ni²⁺ and Ni³⁺ ions. The fraction of nickel existing as Ni²⁺ ions in the crystal is','0.96','Let the fraction of metal which exists as Ni2+ ion be x. Then the fraction of metal as Ni³⁺ = 0.98 – x \\ 2x + 3(0.98 – x) = 2 ⇒ 2x + 2.94 – 3x = 2 ⇒ x = 0.94\n(Odisha NEET 2019)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','0.96','0.04','0.5','0.3','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151225,'chemistry','The appearance of colour in solid alkali metal halides is generally due to','F-centres','F-centres are the sites where anions are missing and instead electrons are present. They are responsible for colours.\n(2006)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','interstitial positions','F-centres','Schottky defect','Frenkel defect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151226,'chemistry','Schottky defect in crystals is observed when','equal number of cations and anions are missing from the lattice.','In Schottky defect, equal no. of cations and anions are missing from the lattice. So, the crystal remains neutral. Such defect is more common in highly ionic compounds of similar cationic and anionic size, i.e. NaCl.\n(1998)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','density of the crystal is increased','unequal number of cations and anions are missing from the lattice','an ion leaves its normal site and occupies an interstitial site','equal number of cations and anions are missing from the lattice.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151227,'chemistry','Ionic solids, with Schottky defects, contain in their structure','equal number of cation and anion vacancies','When an atom is missing from its normal lattice site, a lattice vacancy is created. Such a defect, which involves equal number of cation and anion vacancies in the crystal lattice is called a Schottky defect.\n(1994)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','cation vacancies only','cation vacancies and interstitial cations','equal number of cation and anion vacancies','anion vacancies and interstitial anions.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151228,'chemistry','Which is the incorrect statement?','Frenkel defect is favoured in those ionic compounds in which sizes of cation and anions are almost equal.','Frenkel defect is favoured in those ionic compounds in which there is large difference in the size of cations and anions. Non-stoichiometric defects due to metal deficiency is shown by FeₓO where x = 0.93 to 0.96.\n(NEET 2017)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Density decreases in case of crystals with Schottky defect.','NaCl(s) is insulator, silicon is semiconductor, silver is conductor, quartz is piezoelectric crystal.','Frenkel defect is favoured in those ionic compounds in which sizes of cation and anions are almost equal.','FeO₀.₉₈ has non-stoichiometric metal deficiency defect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151229,'chemistry','With which one of the following elements silicon should be doped so as to give p-type of semiconductor?','Boron','If silicon is doped with any of the elements of group 13 (B, Al, Ga, In, Tl) of the periodic table, p-type of semiconductor will be obtained.\n(2008)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Selenium','Boron','Germanium','Arsenic','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151230,'chemistry','If NaCl is doped with 10⁻⁴ mol % of SrCl₂, the concentration of cation vacancies will be ( N = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)','6.02 × 10¹⁷ mol⁻¹','As each Sr²⁺ ion introduces one cation vacancy, therefore, concentration of cation vacancies = mole % of SrCl₂ added.\n∴ Concentration of cation vacancies = 10⁻⁴ mole% = 10⁻⁴/100 x 6.022 x 10²³ = 6.022 x 10¹⁷\n(2007)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','6.02 × 10¹⁶ mol⁻¹','6.02 × 10¹⁷ mol⁻¹','6.02 × 10¹⁴ mol⁻¹','6.02 × 10¹⁵ mol⁻¹','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151231,'chemistry','If we mix a pentavalent impurity in a crystal lattice of germanium, what type of semiconductor formation will occur?','n-type semiconductor','When an impurity atom with 5 valence electrons (as arsenic) is introduced in a germanium crystal, it replaces one of the germanium atoms. Four of the five\nvalence electrons of the impurity atom form covalent bonds with each valence electron of four germanium atoms and fifth valence electron becomes free to move in the crystal structure. This free electron acts as a charge carrier. Such as an impure germanium crystal is called n-type semiconductor because in it charge carriers are negative (free electrons).\n(1996)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','n-type semiconductor','p-type semiconductor','(a) and (b)','None of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151232,'chemistry','On doping Ge metal with a little of In or Ga, one gets','p-type semiconductor','p-type of semiconductors are produced (a) due to metal deficiency defects (b) by adding impurity containing less electrons (i.e. atoms of group 13). Ge belongs to Group 14 and In or Ga to Group 13. Hence on doping p-type semiconductor is obtained. This doping of Ge with In increase the electrical conductivity of the Ge crystal.\n(1993)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','p-type semiconductor','n-type semiconductor','insulator','rectifier.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(213401,'chemistry','Chlorine was discovered in 1774 by Scheele by the action of HCl on _______ .','MnO₂','Chlorine was discovered in 1774 by Scheele by the action of HCl on MnO₂ .\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 202 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Chlorine',NULL,'1','mcq','MnO₂','HNO₃','KCl','MnO','NEW','12-202',NULL,NULL),
(151233,'chemistry','Given below are two statements.\n \n Assertion: In a particular defect, an ionic solid is electrically neutral, even if few of its cations are missing from its unit cells.\n \n Reasons: In an ionic solid, Frenkel defect arises due to dislocation of cation from its lattice site to interstitial site, maintaining overall electrical neutrality.\n \n In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below: [NEET 2022]','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Frenkel defect forms a vacancy site at original site and interstitial defect at new site hence also known as dislocation deffect.  \n\n"Schottky defect is basically a vacancy defect in ionic solids in oder to maintain electrical neutrality equal number of cations and anions are missing. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 25 Paragraph 3)"','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and the reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct but reason is not correct.','Both assertion and reason are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151234,'chemistry','On the basis of their magnetic properties, substances can be classified into ______ categories','5','On the basis of their magnetic properties, substances can be classified into five categories: \n(i) paramagnetic \n(ii) diamagnetic \n(iii) ferromagnetic \n(iv) antiferromagnetic \n(v) ferrimagnetic.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 29 Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','2','3','4','5','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151235,'chemistry','A 3D structure of solids from two dimensional square closed packing layers can be obtained by______.','placing the second layer exactly over the first one','Three-dimensional close packing forms two-dimensional square close-packed layers: While placing the second square close-packed layer above the first we follow the same rule that was followed when one row was placed adjacent to the other. The second layer is placed over the first layer such that the spheres of the upper layer are exactly above those of the first layer.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 15 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','placing the second layer horizontally over the first one','placing the second layer exactly over the first one','placing the second layer diagonally over the first one','placing the second layer over the first one such that they are in the depressions of each other','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151236,'chemistry','In a given compound, the fraction of octahedral or tetrahedral voids occupied depends upon________.','chemical formula','All octahedral or tetrahedral voids are not occupied. In a given compound, the fraction of octahedral or tetrahedral voids that are occupied, depends upon the chemical formula of the compound.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 18 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','structural formula','chemical formula','emipirical formula','molecular formula','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151237,'chemistry','________ is the percentage of total space filled by the particles.','Packing efficiency','In whatever way the constituent particles (atoms, molecules or ions) are packed, there is always some free space in the form of voids. Packing efficiency is the percentage of total space filled by the particles.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 18 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Octahedral Voids','Tetrahedral Voids:','Packing efficiency','Bravais lattices','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151238,'chemistry','What kind of arrangment of particles is found in magnesium?','Covering tetrahedral voids','Tetrahedral voids of the second layer may be covered by the spheres of the third layer. In this case, the spheres of the third layer are exactly aligned with those of the first layer. Thus, the pattern of spheres is repeated in alternate layers. This pattern is often written as ABAB ....... pattern. This structure is called hexagonal close packed (hcp). This sort of arrangement of atoms is found in many metals like magnesium and zinc.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 17 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Covering octahedral voids','Covering tetrahedral voids','Close packing in one dimension','Close packing in two dimension','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151239,'chemistry','What is the value of r in the given diagram?(n1510)','r = a/2√2','If r is the radius of the sphere, we find b = 4r = √2a or a = 4r/√2 = 2√2r. We can also write r = a/2√2\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 20 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','r=√3/4 a','r=√3/4 a','r = a/2√2','r=2√2a','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/151239q.png'),
(151240,'chemistry','What is the value of r in the given diagram?(n1510)','r=√3/4 a','The length of the body diagonal c is equal to 4r, where r is the radius of the sphere (atom), as all the three spheres along the diagonal touch each other. Therefore, √3a = 4r, a = 4r/√3. Also we can write, r=√3/4 a\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 21 paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','r=√3/4 a','r=√1/4 a','r = a/2√3','r=2√2a','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/151240q.png'),
(151241,'chemistry','Which of the following are the characteristics of a crystal lattice?\n \n (a) Each point in a lattice is called lattice point or lattice site.\n \n (b) Each point in a crystal lattice represents one constituent particle.\n \n (c) Lattice points are joined by circular lines to bring out the geometry of the lattice.\n \n (d) Each point in a crystal lattice only represents ions.','a and b','Following are the characteristics of a crystal lattice:\n(a) Each point in a lattice is called lattice point or lattice site.\n(b) Each point in a crystal lattice represents one constituent particle which may be an atom, a molecule (group of atoms) or an ion.\n(c) Lattice points are joined by straight lines to bring out the geometry of the lattice.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 8 Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a and b','b','c and d','a, b, c and d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151242,'chemistry','How much of a spece lattice is required to specify the crystal completely?','Small portion of space lattice','We need only a small part of the space lattice of a crystal to spacify crystal completely. This small part is called unit cell.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 8 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','3/4 of the total space lattice','Half of the crystal','Small portion of space lattice','A crystal structure can not be specified completely.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151243,'chemistry','What kind of cell is chosen to represent the crystal completely?','Cell with prependicular sides of shortest length','Normally that cell is chosen which has perpendicular sides of shortest length and one can construct entire crystal by translational displacement of the unit cell in three dimensions. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 8 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cell with prependicular sides of shortest length','Cell with verticular sides of shortest length','Cell with perpendicular sides of longest length','Cell with verticular sides of longest length','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151244,'chemistry','Which of the following are the characteristics of a crystal lattice?\n \n (a) Each point in a lattice is called lattice point or lattice site.\n \n (b) Each point in a crystal lattice represents one constituent particle.\n \n (c) Lattice points are joined by circular lines to bring out the geometry of the lattice.\n \n (d) Each point in a crystal lattice only represents ions.','a and b','Following are the characteristics of a crystal lattice:\n(a) Each point in a lattice is called lattice point or lattice site.\n(b) Each point in a crystal lattice represents one constituent particle which may be an atom, a molecule (group of atoms) or an ion.\n(c) Lattice points are joined by straight lines to bring out the geometry of the lattice.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 8 Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a and b','b','c and d','a, b, c and d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151245,'chemistry','How much of a spece lattice is required to specify the crystal completely?','Small portion of space lattice','We need only a small part of the space lattice of a crystal to spacify crystal completely. This small part is called unit cell.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 8 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','3/4 of the total space lattice','Half of the crystal','Small portion of space lattice','A crystal structure can not be specified completely.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151246,'chemistry','What kind of cell is chosen to represent the crystal completely?','Cell with prependicular sides of shortest length','Normally that cell is chosen which has perpendicular sides of shortest length and one can construct entire crystal by translational displacement of the unit cell in three dimensions. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 8 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cell with prependicular sides of shortest length','Cell with verticular sides of shortest length','Cell with perpendicular sides of longest length','Cell with verticular sides of longest length','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151247,'chemistry','Solids can be classified as _______ on the basis of order of their constituent particles.','a and b','Solids can be classified as crystalline or amorphous on the basis of the nature of order present in the arrangement of their constituent particles.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 2 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Crystalline','Amorphous','Insulator','a and b','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151248,'chemistry','Select the correct statement/s.\n \n (i) Solids can be classified as crystalline or amorphous.\n \n (ii) A crystalline solid has definite characteristic geometrical shape.\n \n (iii) Crystal has disordered and non-repetitive pattern of particles in three dimensions.\n \n (iv) Crystals have short range order.','i and ii','Solids can be classified as crystalline or amorphous on the basis of the nature of order present in the arrangement of their constituent particles. A crystalline solid usually consists of a large number of small crystals, each of them having a definite characteristic geometrical shape. The arrangement of constituent particles (atoms, molecules or ions) in a crystal is ordered and repetitive in three dimensions. If we observe the pattern in one region of the crystal, we can predict accurately the position of particles in any other region of the crystal however far they may be from the place of observation. Thus, crystal has a long range order.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 2 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','i and ii','ii, iii and iv','ii and iii','i, iii and iv','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151249,'chemistry','Which of the following metal has repetitive particles pattern over short range?','quartz glass','Glass, rubber and many plastics do not form crystals when their liquids solidify on cooling. These are called amorphous solids. The term amorphous comes from the Greek word amorphos, meaning no form.The arrangement of constituent particles (atoms, molecules or ions) in such a solid has only short range order. In such an arrangement, a regular and periodically repeating pattern is observed over short distances only. In the case of amorphous quartz glass there is no long range order.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 2 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','quartz','quartz glass','sodium chloride','Cesium bromide','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151250,'chemistry','Given below are two statements.\n \n Assertion: Amorphous are called super coooled liquids.\n \n Reasons: Like liquids, amorphous solids have a tendency to flow, though very slowly.\n \n In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both assertion and reason are true and the reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Amorphous solids soften, melt and start flowing over a range of temperature and can be moulded and blown into various shapes. Amorphous solids have the same structural features as liquids and are conveniently regarded as extremely viscous liquids. Like liquids, amorphous solids have a tendency to flow, though very slowly. Therefore, sometimes these are called pseudo solids or super cooled liquids.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 3 Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion is correct but reason is not correct.','Both assertion and reason are true and the reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151251,'chemistry','Given below are two statements.\n \n Assertion: In amorphous solids, value of any physical property would be same along any direction.\n \n Reasons: There is no long range order in them and arrangement of particles is not definite along all the directions.\n \n In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both assertion and reason are true and the reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Amorphous solids are isotropic in nature. Their properties such as mechanical strength, refractive index and electrical conductivity, etc., are same in all directions. It is because there is no long range order in them and arrangement of particles is not definite along all the directions. Hence, the overall arrangement becomes equivalent in all directions. Therefore, value of any physical property would be same along any direction.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 3 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion is correct but reason is not correct.','Both assertion and reason are true and the reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(213402,'chemistry','Chlorine can be prepared by heating MnO₂ with-','Conc.HCl','By heating manganese dioxide with concentrated hydrochloric acid.\nMnO₂ + 4HCl → MnCl₂ + Cl₂ + 2H₂O\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 202 Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Chlorine',NULL,'1','mcq','Conc.HCl','Conc.HBr','Conc.HF','Conc.HI','NEW','12-202',NULL,NULL),
(151252,'chemistry','Given below are two statements.\n \n Assertion: In crystalline solids, value of any physical property would be different along any direction.\n \n Reasons: This arises from same arrangement of particles in different directions.\n \n In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Assertion is correct but reason is not correct.','Crystalline solids are anisotropic in nature, that is, some of their physical properties like electrical resistance or refractive index show different values when measured along different directions in the same crystals. This arises from different arrangement of particles in different directions.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 3 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion is correct but reason is not correct.','Both assertion and reason are true and the reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151318,'chemistry','Element ‘B’ forms ccp structure and ‘A’ occupies  half of the octahedral voids, while oxygen atoms  occupy all the tetrahedral voids. The structure of  bimetallic oxide is [JEE (Main)-2019]','AB₂O₄','Lattice formed by B(ccp) = 4\nA = 50% of octahedral voids = 2\nO = tetrahedral voids = 8\nFormula = AB₂O₄','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','A₂B₂O','AB₂O₄','A₄B₂O','A₂BO₄','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151319,'chemistry','The amorphous form of silica is [JEE (Main)-2019]','Kieselguhr','Quartz, tridymite and cristobalite are crystalline\n forms of silica.\nKieselguhr is an amorphous form of silica.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Quartz','Tridymite','Kieselguhr','Cristobalite','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151320,'chemistry','An element has a face-centred cubic (fcc) structure  with a cell edge of a. The distance between the  centres of two nearest tetrahedral voids in the  lattice is : [JEE (Main)-2019]','a/2','In FCC, tetrahedral voids are located on the body  diagonal at a distance of √3a/4 from the corner.\n\nTogether they form a smaller cube of edge length a/2','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','3\n/2 a','a/2','a','√2a','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151321,'chemistry','The ratio of number of atoms present in a simple  cubic, body centered cubic and face centered cubic  structure are, respectively [JEE (Main)-2019]','1 : 2 : 4','No. of atoms in\nSimple cubic unit cell = 8 × 1/8 = 1\nB.C.C. unit cell = 8 × 1/8 + 1×1 = 2\nFCC unit cell = 8 × 1/8 +( 1/2 × 6) = 4','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','4 : 2 : 3','4 : 2 : 1','8 : 1 : 6','1 : 2 : 4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151519,'chemistry','Which of the following point defects are shown by AgBr (s) crystals?','A & B','AgBr shows both Schottky and Frenkel defects. In AgBr, both Ag+ and Br ions are absent from the lattice causing Schottky defect. However, Ag+ ions are mobile so they have a tendency to move aside the lattice and trapped in interstitial site, hence cause Frenkel defect.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','A & B','B & C','A & C','B & D','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151520,'chemistry','To get a n-type semiconductor from silicon, it should be doped with a substance with valency','5','Impurity of higher group is doped to get n-type semiconductor. Thus, silicon (valency = 4) should be doped with the element with valency equal to 5.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','5','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151521,'chemistry','Which of the following is true about the charge acquired by p-type semiconductors?','Neutral','p-Type semiconductors are neutral but they conduct electricity through positive holes.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Positive','Neutral','Negative','Depends on concentration of p impurity','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151522,'chemistry','Graphite cannot be classified as','Ionic solid','Constituent units of graphite are carbon atoms, held together by covalent bonding in 2D network structure. Thus, it is not an ionic solid.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Conducting solid','Network solid','Covalent solid','Ionic solid','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151523,'chemistry','The lattice site in a pure crystal cannot be occupied by','Electron','Pure crystals have constituents i.e., atoms or molecules or ions as lattice points which are arranged in fixed stoichiometric ratio. Electron can occupy the lattice site only when there is imperfection in solid and not in a pure crystal.\nHence, existence of free electrons are not possible, it is possible on in case of imperfection in solid.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Molecule','Electron','Ion','Atom','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151524,'chemistry','Graphite is a good conductor of electricity due to the presence of','Free valence electrons','In graphite one carbon atom is attached to three other carbon atoms. One electron of carbon remains free. Due to this free valence electron graphite is an electrical conductor.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Free valence electrons','Lone pair of electrons','Cations','Anions','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151525,'chemistry','The sharp melting point of crystalline solids is due to','A regular arrangement of constituent particles observed over a long distance in the crystal lattice','A solid is said to be crystalline if the various constituent structural units (atoms, ions or molecules) of which the solid is made, are arranged in a definite geometrical pattern within the solid.\n\nThe type of forces in crystalline solids are of long range order due to which they have sharp melting point.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','A regular arrangement of constituent particles observed over a short distance in the crystal lattice .','A regular arrangement of constituent particles observed over a long distance in the crystal lattice','Same arrangement of constituent particles in different directions','Different arrangement of constituent particles in different directions','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151526,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is not true about amorphous solids ?','They are anisotropic in nature','Amorphous solids are isotropic because they show thermal and optical properties, same in all directions.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','On heating they may become crystalline at certain temperature','They may become crystalline on keeping for a long time','Amorphous solids can be moulded by heating','They are anisotropic in nature','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151527,'chemistry','Which of the following is true about the value of refractive index of quartz glass ?','Same in all directions','Since quartz glass is an amorphous solid having short range order of constitutents. Hence, value of refractive index is same in all directions, can be measured and not be equal to zero always.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Same in all directions','Different in different directions','Cannot be measured','Always zero','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151528,'chemistry','Which of the following condition favours the existence of a substance in the solid state?','Low temperature','At low temperature substance exists in solid state due to decrease in molecular motion which leads to strong cohesive forces i.e., forces which hold the constituent particles together.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Low temperature','High temperature','High thermal energy','Weak cohesive forces','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151529,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a characteristic of a crystalline solid ?','Isotropic nature','Anisotropy: Crystalline solids are anisotropic in nature, that is some of their physical properties like electrical resistance or refractive index show different values when measured along different directions in the same crystal. This arises from different arrangement of particles in different directions arrangement of particles along different directions.\n\nIsotropy: In case of amorphous substances, properties such as electrical conductivity, refractive index, thermal expansion, etc. are identical in all directions just as in case of gases or liquids. This property is called isotropy and the substances showing this property are called isotropic.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Definite and characteristic heat of fusion','Isotropic nature','A regular periodically repeated pattern of arrangement of constituent particles in the entire crystal','A true solid','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151530,'chemistry','Which of the following is an amorphous solid?','Quartz glass (SiO₂)','∙ An amorphous solid is a solid that has no ordered internal structure. \n∙ Examples of amorphous solids include glass, rubber, plastic, and gels.\n∙ Amorphous solids break into uneven pieces with irregular edges.\n∙ Graphite, alum, and silicon carbide are crystalline solids.\n\nHence, quartz glass (SiO₂ ) is an amorphous solid due to its short-range order of constituent particles.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Graphite','Quartz glass (SiO₂)','Chrome alum','Silicon carbide (SiC)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151531,'chemistry','Which of the following arrangement shows schematic alignment of magnetic moments of antiferromagnetic substances? Refer diagram (n1513)','d','The substances which have domain structure are oppositely oriented and cancel each other\'s magnetic fileld are known as antiferromagnetic substances.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a','b','c','d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151532,'chemistry','Iodine molecules are held in the crystals lattice by','London forces','The iodine molecules are a class of non-polar molecular solid in which constituents molecules are held together by London or dispersion forces. Their solids are soft and non-conductor of electricity.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','London forces','Dipole-dipole interactions','Covalent bonds','Coulombic forces','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151533,'chemistry','Which of the following is not the characteristic of ionic solids?','Very low value of electrical conductivity in the molten state','The ionic solids easily dissociated into its ions in the molten state and show high electrical conductivity. So, statement (a) is incorrect while ionic solids are anisotropic and brittle liked with a very strong force of interactions.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Very low value of electrical conductivity in the molten state','Brittle nature','Very strong forces of interactions','Anisotropic nature','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151534,'chemistry','Cations are present in the interstitial sites in','Frenkel defect','A Frenkel defect or dislocation defect is a type of defect in crystalline solids wherein an atom is displaced from its lattice position to an interstitial site, creating a vacancy at the original site and an interstitial defect at the new location without any changes in chemical properties.\n\nIn this defect, the cation is displaced from its lattice site to an interstitial site. Refer diagram(ne1516)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Frenkel defect','Schottky defect','Vacancy defect','Metal deficiency defect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151535,'chemistry','Schottky defect is observed in crystals when','Equal number of cations and anions are missing from the lattice','Schottky defect is observed when equal number of cations and anions are missing from the lattice. This defect changes the density as a pair of ions are missing from the lattice.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Some cations move from their lattice site to interstitial sites','Equal number of cations and anions are missing from the lattice','Some lattice sites are occupied by electrons','Some impurity is present in the lattice','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151536,'chemistry','The total number of tetrahedral voids in the face centered unit cell is','8','In face centered cubic unit cell (FCC), tetrahedral voids are present on body diagonals. There are two tetrahedral voids on each body diagonal at (1/4)th of the distance from each corner.\n\nSo, total number of tetrahedral voids = (no. of tetrahedral voids per body diagonal) x (no. of body diagonals) = 2 × 4 = 8.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','6','10','8','12','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151537,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is not true about the hexagonal close packing?','In this arrangement, spheres of the fourth layer are exactly aligned with those of the first layer.','Hexagonal close packing can be arranged by two layers\nA and B one over another which can be diagrammatically represented below\n\nHere, we can see easily that 1st layer and 4th layer are not exactly aligned. Thus, statement "In this arrangement, spheres of the fourth layer are exactly aligned with those of the first layer" is not correct while other three statements are true. Refer diagram(ne1517)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','The coordination number is 12','It has 74% packing efficiency','Tetrahedral voids of the second layer are covered by the spheres of the third layer','In this arrangement, spheres of the fourth layer are exactly aligned with those of the first layer.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151538,'chemistry','What is the coordination number in a square close packed structure in two dimensions?','4','Coordination number in a square closed packed structure in two dimensions is equal to 4','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','2','4','6','3','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151539,'chemistry','The correct order of the packing efficiency in different types of unit cells is…………','fcc > bcc > simple cubic','Packing efficiency in different types of unit cells correct order is fcc (74%) > bcc (68%) > simple cubic (52%)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','fcc < bcc < simple cubic','fcc > bcc > simple cubic','fcc < bcc > simple cubic','bcc < fcc > simple cubic','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151540,'chemistry','In the cubic close packing, the unit cell has','8 tetrahedral voids within the unit cells.','In the cubic close packing the unit cell has 8 tetrahedral voids within it and are located at each eight smaller cube of a unit cell.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','4 tetrahedral voids each of which is shared by four adjacent unit cells','4 tetrahedral voids within the unit cell','8 tetrahedral voids each of which is shared by four adjacent unit cells','8 tetrahedral voids within the unit cells.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151541,'chemistry','A perfect crystal of silicon (fig) is doped with some elements as given in the options. Which of these options show n-type semiconductors? (n1514)','a,c','When group 15 elements are doped into a perfect crystal, it leads to the formation of n-type semiconductor.\nHere, in (a) as (group 15, period 3) is doped to perfect Si-crystal and in (c) as (group 15, period 2) is doped to perfect Si-crystal.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,c','a,b','c,d','b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151542,'chemistry','Under the influence of electric field, which of the following statements are true about the movement of electrons and holes in a p-type semiconductor ?\n \n (a) Electron will move towards the positively charged plate through electron holes\n \n \n \n (b) Holes will appear to be moving towards the negatively charged plate\n \n \n \n (c) Both electrons and holes appear to move towards the positively charged plate\n \n \n \n (d) Movement of electrons is not related to the movement of holes','a,b','In the p-type semiconductor, the conductivity is due to the existence of a hole. When the electric field is applied to p-type semiconductor hole starts moving towards negatively charged plate and electron towards the positively charged plate.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151543,'chemistry','The value of magnetic moment is zero in the case of antiferromagnetic substances because the domains …\n \n \n \n (a) get oriented in the direction of the applied magnetic field\n \n \n \n (b) get oriented opposite to the direction of the applied magnetic field\n \n \n \n (c) are oppositely oriented with respect to each other without the application of magnetic field\n \n \n \n (d) cancel out each other’s magnetic moment','c,d','(c, d) In the case of antiferromagnetic substances, the magnetic moment becomes zero because the domains are oppositely oriented with respect to each other without the application of magnetic field which cancel out each other.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151418,'chemistry','A : Number of rectangular plane in a cubic crystal is 3.\n\nR : Rectangular planes passes through corner to corner of unit cell.','A is true but R is false.','Total rectangular planes are 3 and rectangular plane passes through opposite face.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151419,'chemistry','A : ccp is more efficient than hcp.\n\nR : Packing fraction is different in both cases.','Both A and R are false.','CCP and HCP both have same packing efficiency.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151420,'chemistry','A : Coordination number of both Na⁺ and Cl⁻ in NaCl is 6.\n\nR : Second coordination number of Cl⁻ in the NaCl unit is 12.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','The coordination number is defined as the number of ions or atoms surrounding a particular ion in a crystal lattice. In NaCl, each Na⁺ ion is surrounded by 6 Cl⁻ ions and each Cl⁻ ion is surrounded by 6 Na⁺ ions, which gives a coordination number of 6 for both Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions.\n\nThe second coordination number of Cl⁻ in the NaCl unit is 6, which is the same as the first coordination number. The second coordination number is defined as the number of ions or atoms surrounding a particular ion in the second shell, beyond the first coordination shell. Since NaCl has a simple cubic structure, the second coordination shell of Cl⁻ ions does not exist.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30113,'chemistry','Which of following elements is not placed in group VI of Mendeleev\'s periodic table?','Manganese','Manganses is not placed in group VI of Mendeleev\'s periodic table, as shown in the diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 77; diagram(ne0303)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Genesis of Periodic Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Manganese','Chromium','Molybdenum','Tungsten','NEW','11-076','11-076',NULL),
(30114,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about Dobereiner\'s triads is correct?','They were based on the observation that the atomic weight of the middle element was the average of the atomic weights of the other two elements','By 1829 Doberenier noted a similarity among the physical and chemical properties of several groups of three elements (Triads). \n\nIn each case, he noticed that the middle element of each of then Triads had an atomic weight about half way between the atomic weights of the other two,\n\nAlso the properties of the middle element were in between those of the other.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 75; Paragraph No. 2)(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 75; diagram(ne0304)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Genesis of Periodic Classification',NULL,'2','mcq','They were used to classify elements based on their electronegativity','They consisted of groups of two elements that had similar properties','They were arranged in order of increasing atomic number','They were based on the observation that the atomic weight of the middle element was the average of the atomic weights of the other two elements','NEW','11-075','11-075',NULL),
(30115,'chemistry','According to law of ocatves, every ______ element had properties similar to first element?','Eighth','Newlands arranged the elements in increasing order of their atomic weights and noted that every eighth element had properties similar to the first element.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 75; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Genesis of Periodic Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Eighth','Fourth','Third','Seventh','NEW','11-075','11-075',NULL),
(30116,'chemistry','Davy medal was awarded to','Newlands','Although his idea was not widely accepted at that time, he, for his work, was later awarded Davy Medal in 1887 by the Royal Society, London.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 75; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Genesis of Periodic Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Newlands','Meyer','Mendeleev','Moseley','NEW','11-075','11-075',NULL),
(30117,'chemistry','Melting point of Eka-Aluminium as predicted by Mendeleev was ___','Low','Melting point of Eka-Aluminium as predicted by Mendeleev was considered as low, as shown in the referred diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 76; diagram(ne0305)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Genesis of Periodic Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Low','High','Moderate','Standard','NEW','11-076','11-076',NULL),
(30118,'chemistry','Who fully recognized the significance of periodicity and used broader range of physical and chemical properties to classify the elements?','Mendeleev','Mendeleev’s system of classifying elements was more elaborate than that of Lothar Meyer’s. \n\nHe fully recognized the significance of periodicity and used broader range of physical and chemical properties to classify the elements.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 76; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Genesis of Periodic Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Moseley','Meyer','Mendeleev','Newlands','NEW','11-076','11-076',NULL),
(30119,'chemistry','Which scientist observed the change in length of repeating pattern while the development of periodic table?','Meyer','Lothar Meyer observed a change in length of that repeating pattern.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 75; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Genesis of Periodic Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Meyer','Doberenier','Newlands','Mendeleev','NEW','11-075','11-075',NULL),
(30120,'chemistry','Assertion: Mendeleev ignored the order of atomic weight and placed elements with similar properties together.\n\nReason: Mendeleev thought atomic mass measurement would be incorrect.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Mendeleev ignored the order of atomic weights, thinking that the atomic measurements might be incorrect, and place the elements with similar properties together. \n\nFor example, iodine with lower atomic weight than that of tellurium (Group VI) was placed in Group VII along with fluorine, chlorine, bromine because of similarities in properties. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 76; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Genesis of Periodic Classification',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','11-076','11-076',NULL),
(30121,'chemistry','Consider the following statements:\n\nStatement I: Mendeleev proposed that some elements were still undiscovered and left several gaps in his table.\n\nStatement II: Mendeleev primary aim was to arrange the elements of similar properties in similar groups.','Both statements are true.','At the same time, keeping his primary aim of arranging the elements of similar properties in the same group, he proposed that some of the elements were still undiscovered and, therefore, left several gaps in the table. \n\nFor example, both gallium and germanium were unknown at the time.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 76; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Genesis of Periodic Classification',NULL,'2','mcq','Only statement I is true.','Only statement II is true.','Both statements are false.','Both statements are true.','NEW','11-076','11-076',NULL),
(30122,'chemistry','Which scientist profound the law of octaves?','Newlands','John Alexander Newlands in 1865 profounded the Law of Octaves.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 75; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Genesis of Periodic Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Newlands','Meyer','Mendeleev','Moseley','NEW','11-075','11-075',NULL),
(30123,'chemistry','Who proposed that on arranging elements in the increasing order of their atomic weights, similarities appear in physical and chemical properties at regular intervals?','Mendeleev & Meyer','The Periodic Law, as we know it today owes its development to the Russian chemist, Dmitri Mendeleev (1834-1907) and the German chemist, Lothar Meyer (1830-1895). \n\nWorking independently, both the chemists in 1869 proposed that on arranging elements in the increasing order of their atomic weights, similarities appear in physical and chemical properties at regular intervals.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 75; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Genesis of Periodic Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Mendeleev & Meyer','Meyer & Moseley','Dobereiner & Newland','Moseley & Dobereiner','NEW','11-075','11-075',NULL),
(30301,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true?\n\na. The new element first gets temporary name, with symbol consisting of two letters.\n\nb. Official names of all elements have been announced by IUPAC.\n\nc. The permanent name might reflect the country in which the element was discovered, or pay tribute to a notable scientist.','Only b and c','The new element first gets a temporary name, with symbol consisting of three letters. Later permanent name and symbol are given by a vote of IUPAC representatives from each country. \n\nThe permanent name might reflect the country (or state of the country) in which the element was discovered, or pay tribute to a notable scientist. As of now, elements with atomic numbers up to 118 have been discovered. Official names of all elements have been announced by IUPAC.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 81; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Nomenclature of elements',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only a and c','Only b and c','All of these','NEW','11-081','11-081',NULL),
(30428,'chemistry','Assertion: The properties of an element have periodic dependence upon its atomic number.\n\nReason: The properties of an element have periodic dependence upon its atomic number beacuse it is also dependence on relative atomic mass.','Assertion is correct but reason is not the fact which support this statement.','Elements in the same vertical column or group have similar valence shell electronic configurations, the same number of electrons in the outer orbitals, and similar properties. \n\nFor example, the Group 1 elements (alkali metals) all have ns1 valence shell electronic configuration as shown in diagram \n\nThus it can be seen that the properties of an element have periodic dependence upon its atomic number and not on relative atomic mass. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 82; Paragraph No. 2; diagram(ne0318)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Assertion is correct but reason is not the fact which support this statement.','Assertion is incorrect but reason is  the fact which support this statement.','Assertion and reason both are true.','Assertion and reason both are false','NEW','11-082','11-082',NULL),
(30429,'chemistry','We can classify the elements into four blocks viz., s-block, p-block, d-block and f-block depending on the type of atomic orbitals that are________.','Being filled with electrons','The elements in a vertical column of the Periodic Table constitute a group or family and exhibit similar chemical behaviour. \n\nThis similarity arises because these elements have the same number and same distribution of electrons in their outermost orbitals. \n\nWe can classify the elements into four blocks viz., s-block, p-block, d-block and f-block depending on the type of atomic orbitals that are being filled with electrons. This is illustrated in diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 82 & 83; Paragraph No. 3; diagram(ne0319)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Have empty orbital','High transition state','Having protons','Being filled with electrons','NEW','11-082','11-082',NULL),
(30430,'chemistry','The elements having ns1 and ns2 outermost electronic configuration belong to the __Block Elements.','s','The elements of Group 1 (alkali metals) and Group 2 (alkaline earth metals) which have ns1 and ns2 outermost electronic configuration belong to the s-Block Elements.\n\nThis is also diagramatically explained in diagram\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 82 - 84; Paragraph No. 4; diagram(ne0319)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','p','s','d','f','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(31411,'chemistry','Assertion: There is high reactiviy seen in two extremes of the periodic table and lowest in the centre.\n\nReason: The ionization enthalpy of the extreme left element in a period is the highest and the electron gain enthalpy of the element on the extreme right is the least negative.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','The atomic and ionic radii, as we know, generally decrease in a period from left to right. As a consequence, the ionization enthalpies generally increase (with some exceptions as outlined in section 3.7.1(a)) and electron gain enthalpies become more negative across a period. In other words, the ionization enthalpy of the extreme left element in a period is the least and the electron gain enthalpy of the element on the extreme right is the highest negative (note : noble gases having completely filled shells have rather positive electron gain enthalpy values). This results into high chemical reactivity at the two extremes and the lowest in the centre. Thus, the maximum chemical reactivity at the extreme left (among alkali metals) is exhibited by the loss of an electron leading to the formation of a cation and at the extreme right (among halogens) shown by the gain of an electron forming an anion.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 94; Paragraph no. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Periodic Trends and Chemical reactivity',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct statement but reason is wrong statement.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion and reason both are wrong statements.','NEW','11-094','11-094',NULL),
(31412,'chemistry','How many of the following quantities will increase if we go down the group in the modern periodic table?\n\nA. Electronegativity\n\nB. Ionisation energy\n\nC. Atomic size\n\nD. Metallic nature\n\nE. Electron gain enthalpy','2','Atomic radius generally decreases across a period from left to right. \n\nAs shown in the diagram \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 91; diagram(ne0312)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Periodic Trends and Chemical reactivity',NULL,'3','mcq','3','2','1','4','NEW','11-091','11-091',NULL),
(31413,'chemistry','Assertion: Electronegativity increase along the period in the modern periodic table.\n\nReason: The attraction between the outer electrons and the nucleus increases as theatomic radius decreases in a period.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion.','The electronegativity be related to atomic radii, which tend to decrease across each period from left to right, but increase down each group. \n\nThe attraction between the outer (or valence) electrons and the nucleus increases as the atomic radius decreases in a period. \n\nThe electronegativity also increases. On the same account electronegativity values decrease with the increase in atomic radii down a group. The trend is similar to that of ionization enthalpy.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 91; Paragraph no. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Periodic Trends and Chemical reactivity',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion is wrong statement but reason is correct statement.','Assertion and reason both are wrong statements.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements but reason is not correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation for assertion.','NEW','11-091','11-091',NULL),
(31414,'chemistry','Predict the formula of compound formed by Silicon and fluorine.','SiF₄','Silicon is group 14 element with a valence of 4; bromine belongs to the Halogen family with a valence of 1. \n\nHence the formula of the compound formed would be SiF₄ .\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 92; Problem 3.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Periodic Trends and Chemical reactivity',NULL,'2','mcq','SiF','SiF₂','SiF₄','Si₄F','NEW','11-092','11-092',NULL),
(31415,'chemistry','A chemical reaction with water, Na2O is _____ oxide.','Basic','It is shown in the given diagram  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 94; Exercise 3.10; diagram(ne0325)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Periodic Trends and Chemical reactivity',NULL,'1','fillup','Bas,Ac,id,ic',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-094','11-094',NULL),
(31416,'chemistry','A chemical reaction with water, Cl2O7 is _____ oxide.','Acidic','It is shown in the given diagram  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 94; Exercise 3.10; diagram(ne0325)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Periodic Trends and Chemical reactivity',NULL,'1','fillup','Bas,Ac,id,ic',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-094','11-094',NULL),
(31417,'chemistry','Basic or acidic nature can be qualitatively tested with _________.','Litmus Paper','Basic or acidic nature can be qualitatively tested withlitmus paper. It is shown in the given diagram. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 94; Exercise 3.10) diagram(ne0325)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Periodic Trends and Chemical reactivity',NULL,'1','mcq','Chromatography','UV rays','Litmus Paper','Spectrometry','NEW','11-094','11-094',NULL),
(31418,'chemistry','Draw a chemical reaction of Na₂O with water:\n\nNa₂O + H₂O →','2NaOH','A chemical reaction of Na₂O with water is:  \n\nNa₂O + H₂O → 2NaOH as it is illustrated in the diagrams. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 94; Exercise 3.10) digrams(ne0325)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Periodic Trends and Chemical reactivity',NULL,'2','mcq','Na₂OH','2NaOH','NA₂O₂H₂','NAOH + H₂','NEW','11-094','11-094',NULL),
(31419,'chemistry','Draw a chemical reaction of Cl₂O₇ with water:\n\nCl₂O₇ + H₂O →','2HClO₄','A chemical reaction of Cl₂O₇ with water is:  \n\nCl₂O₇ + H₂O → 2HClO₄ as it is illustrated in the diagrams. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 94; Exercise 3.10) diagram(ne0325)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Periodic Trends and Chemical reactivity',NULL,'2','mcq','2HClO₂','HClO₂H','2HClO₄','ClH₂O','NEW','11-094','11-094',NULL),
(31420,'chemistry','The change in atomic radii is still ____ among inner-transition metals (4f series).','Smaller','The change in atomic radii is still smaller among inner-transition metals (4f series). \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 94; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Periodic Trends and Chemical reactivity',NULL,'1','fillup','La,rg,er,Sm,all',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-094','11-094',NULL),
(31421,'chemistry','The ionization enthalpies are intermediate between those of ___________.','s and p blocks','The ionization enthalpies are intermediate between those of s- and p-blocks.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 94 & 95; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Periodic Trends and Chemical reactivity',NULL,'1','mcq','f and d blocks','p and d blocks','s and d blocks','s and p blocks','NEW','11-094','11-094',NULL),
(31422,'chemistry','The metallic character _______ down the group.','increases','In a group, the increase in atomic and ionic radii with increase in atomic number generally results in a gradual decrease in ionization enthalpies and a regular decrease (with in some third period elements in electron gain enthalpies in the case of main group elements.\n\nThus, the metallic character increases down the group and non-metallic character decreases. \n\nThis trend can be related with their reducing and oxidizing property which you will learn later.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 95; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Periodic Trends and Chemical reactivity',NULL,'1','fillup','in,cre,as,es,de',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-095','11-095',NULL),
(31423,'chemistry','The non-metallic character ______ down the group.','decreases','In a group, the increase in atomic and ionic radii with increase in atomic number generally results in a gradual decrease in ionization enthalpies and a regular decrease (with in some third period elements in electron gain enthalpies in the case of main group elements.\n\nThus, the metallic character increases down the group and non-metallic character decreases.\n\nThis trend can be related with their reducing and oxidizing property which you will learn later.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 95; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Periodic Trends and Chemical reactivity',NULL,'1','fillup','in,cre,as,es,de',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-095','11-095',NULL),
(31501,'chemistry','Predict the binary compound formed by Element 71 and fluorine.','LuF₃','Predict the formulas of the stable binary compounds that would be formed by the combination of the following pairs of elements.\n\n(a) Lithium and oxygen (b) Magnesium and nitrogen (c) Aluminium and iodine (d) Silicon and oxygen (e) Phosphorus and fluorine (f) Element 71 and fluorine\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 98; Exercise 3.32)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'3','mcq','LaF₃','LuF₂','LuF₃','LaF₂','NEW','11-092','11-092',NULL),
(31502,'chemistry','Which one of the following statements is incorrect in relation to ionization enthalpy?','Removal of electron from orbitals bearing lower n value is easier than from orbital having higher n value.','Removal of electron from orbitals bearing lower n value is easier than from orbital having higher n value is the incorrect option from the given ones. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 98; Exercise 3.37)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'2','mcq','Ionization enthalpy increases for each successive electron.','Removal of electron from orbitals bearing lower n value is easier than from orbital having higher n value.','The greatest increase in ionization enthalpy is experienced on removal of electron from core noble gas configuration.','End of valence electrons is marked by a big jump in ionization enthalpy.','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31503,'chemistry','Which statement is true about second electron gain enthalpy of oxygen?','It is positive, hence process is endothermic.','Would you expect the second electron gain enthalpy of O as positive, more negative or less negative than the first? \n\nIt is positive, hence process is endothermic. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 97; Exercise 3.21)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'3','mcq','It is negative, but less in magnitude as compared to first electron gain enthalpy.','It is positive, hence process is endothermic.','It will exactly equal to first electron gain enthalpy.','It is negative, but more in magnitude as compared to first electron gain enthalpy.','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31504,'chemistry','Arrange in the decreasing order of reactivity:','F>Cl>Br>I','The increasing order of reactivity among group 1 elements is Li < Na < K < Rb <Cs whereas that among group 17 elements is F > CI > Br > I.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 97; Exercise 3.28)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'3','order','1,F','2,Cl','3,Br','4,I','NEW','11-094','11-094',NULL),
(31505,'chemistry','Which element has least ionisation enthalpy in group 13?','In','In element has least ionisation enthalpy in group 13 which is (579 KJmol⁻¹). \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 97; Exercise 3.19)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'2','mcq','Tl','Al','B','In','NEW','11-088','11-088',NULL),
(31506,'chemistry','Considering the elements B, Al, Mg, and K, the correct order of their metallic character is :','K > Mg > Al > B','Considering the elements B, Al, Mg, and K, the correct order of their metallic character is: K > Mg > Al > B. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 97; Exercise 3.38)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'3','order','1,K','2,Mg','3,Al','4,B','NEW','11-091','11-091',NULL),
(31507,'chemistry','Which element do you think would have been named by Lawrence Berkeley Laboratory?','Lawrencium','It is shown in the given diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 97; Exercise 3.7) diagram(ne0301)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'1','mcq','Lawrencium','Bohrium','Lutetium','Lanthanum','NEW','11-080','11-080',NULL),
(31508,'chemistry','In the modern periodic table, the period indicates the value of:','Principal quantum number','In the modern periodic table, the period indicates the value of principal quantum number.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 97; Exercise 3.33)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'2','mcq','Atomic number','Atomic mass','Principal quantum number','Azimuthal quantum number','NEW','11-078','11-078',NULL),
(31509,'chemistry','Which of the following statements related to the modern periodic table is incorrect?','The d-block has 8 columns, because a maximum of 8 electrons can occupy all the orbitals in a d-subshell.','The d-block has 8 columns, because a maximum of 8 electrons can occupy all the orbitals in a d-subshell. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 97; Exercise 3.34)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'2','mcq','The p-block has 6 columns, because a maximum of 6 electrons can occupy all the orbitals in a p-shell.','The d-block has 8 columns, because a maximum of 8 electrons can occupy all the orbitals in a d-subshell.','Each block contains a number of columns equal to the number of electrons that can occupy that subshell.','The block indicates value of azimuthal quantum number (l) for the last subshell that received electrons in building up the electronic configuration.','NEW','11-079','11-079',NULL),
(31510,'chemistry','Anything that influences the valence electrons will affect the chemistry of the element. Which one of the following factors does not affect the valence shell?','Nuclear mass','Anything that influences the valence electrons will affect the nuclear mass of the element. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 97; Exercise 3.35)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'2','mcq','Nuclear mass','Nuclear charge','Number of core electrons','Value of principal quantum number','NEW','11-092','11-092',NULL),
(31511,'chemistry','Write the atomic number of the element present in the third period and seventeenth group of the periodic table.','17','The atomic number of the element present in the third period and seventeenth group of the periodic table is 17 which is chlorine. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 97; Exercise 3.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'2','mcq','35','17','16','12','NEW','11-079','11-079',NULL),
(31512,'chemistry','Why do elements in the same group have similar physical and chemical properties?','Because of its atom\'s outermost electron shell.','Elements in the same group have similar physical and chemical properties because of its atom\'s outermost electron shell. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 97; Exercise 3.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'1','mcq','Because of its atom\'s outermost electron shell.','Both of them','None of them','Because of its atom\'s innermost electron shell','NEW','11-082','11-082',NULL),
(31513,'chemistry','How do atomic radius vary in a period and in a group?','Decreases across a period and increases down a group','Atomic radius vary in a period and in a group decreases across a period and increases down a group. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 97; Exercise 3.10)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'1','mcq','Decreases across a period and increases down a group','Both of them','None of them','Increases across a period and decreases down a group','NEW','11-085','11-085',NULL),
(31514,'chemistry','What do you understand by isoelectronic species?','Having same number of electrons','An isoelectronic species are atom or ions having same number of electrons. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 97; Exercise 3.11)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'1','mcq','Having same number of electrons','Having lowest number of electrons','Having different number of electrons','Having highest number of electrons','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31515,'chemistry','Name a species that will be isoelectronic with each of the following atoms or ions.','S²⁻ ion-(16 + 2 = 18 electrons), Cl⁻ ion-(17 + 1 = 18 electrons), Ca²⁺ ion-(20 – 2 = 18 electrons), K⁺ ion-(19 – 1 = 18 electrons)','Ions of different elements which have the same number of electrons but different magnitude of the nuclear charge are called isoelectronic ions. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 97; Exercise 3.11)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'2','match','S²⁻ ion,(16 + 2 = 18 electrons)','Ca²⁺ ion,(20 – 2 = 18 electrons)','Cl⁻ ion,(17 + 1 = 18 electrons)','K⁺ ion,(19 – 1 = 18 electrons)','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31516,'chemistry','Energy of an electron in the ground state of the hydrogen atom is –2.18×10⁻¹⁸J. Calculate the ionization enthalpy of atomic hydrogen in terms ofJ mol⁻¹.','13.13 xx 10^(5)` J/mole','Ionisation enthalpy of hydrogen atom `= 13.13 xx 10^(5)` J/mole. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 97; Exercise 3.15)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'3','mcq','13.13 xx 10^(5)` J/mole','18.13 xx 10^(5)` J/mole','18.18xx 10^(5)` J/mole','13.18 xx 10^(5)` J/mole','NEW','11-089','11-089',NULL),
(31517,'chemistry','Which of the following pairs of elements would have a more negative electron gain enthalpy?\n\n(i) O or F (ii) F or Cl','(ii) F or Cl','Cl and F will have more negative electron gain enthalpy because in going from O to F the atomic size decreases and the nuclear charge increases. \n\nBoth these factors make the value of electron gain enthalpy of fluorine more negative because they tend to increase the nuclear pull for the incoming electron. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 97; Exercise 3.20)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'2','mcq','Both of the pairs will remain same','(ii) F or Cl','(i) O or F','It isheoritically wrong statement regarding electron gain enthalpy','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31518,'chemistry','Mark the correct various factor(s) due to which the ionization enthalpy of the main group elements tends to decrease down a group?','All of these','Ionization enthalpy of the main group elements tends to decrease down the group due to the following factors such as atomic size, shielding or screening effect and nuclear charge. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 97; Exercise 3.18)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'2','mcq','Atomic size','Shielding or screening effect','Nuclear charge','All of these','NEW','11-088','11-088',NULL),
(31519,'chemistry','How would you react to the statement that the electronegativity of N on Pauling scale is 3.0 in all the nitrogen compounds?','No, This statement is false for Nitrogen compounds','Electronegativity of an element is a variable property. It is different in different compounds. \n\nHence, the statement which says that the electronegativity of N on Pauling scale is 3.0 in all nitrogen compounds is incorrect. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 97; Exercise 3.22)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'2','mcq','Yes, This statement is true for Nitrogen compounds','No, This statement is false for Nitrogen compounds','For all compounds other than Nitrogen','For all the Nitrogen and Oxygen compounds','NEW','11-091','11-091',NULL),
(31520,'chemistry','What is the basic difference between the terms electron gain enthalpy and electronegativity?','Property of bonded atom vs isolated atom','The basic difference between the terms electron gain enthalpy and electronegativity is property of bonded atom vs isolated atom, because Electron gain enthalpy is the tendency of an isolated gaseous atom to accept an additional electron to form a negative ion. \n\nElectronegativity is the tendency of the atom of an element in a chemical compound to attract a shared pair of electrons towards it in a covalent bond.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 97; Exercise 3.22)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'1','mcq','Property of bonded atom vs isolated atom','Property of bonded atom vs tripple bonded atom','Property of bonded atom vs double bonded atom','None of these','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31521,'chemistry','What would you expect the first ionization enthalpies for two isotopes of the same element?','Same','The ionisation energy for two isotopes of an element should be same. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 97; Exercise 3.25)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'1','mcq','Same','Different','Opposite','Inverse','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31522,'chemistry','Identify the group of element with five electrons in the outer subshell.','group 17','The outermost p subshell has five electrons in them. \n\nIn the periodic table this electronic configuration is shown by the halogen family which is belong to group 17 of the periodic table. \n\nTherefore the elements can be fluorine (2p5), chlorine (3p5), bromine (4p5), iodine (5p5). \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 97; Exercise 3.27)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'2','mcq','group 17','group 16','group 7','group 6','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(31523,'chemistry','Identify an element that would tend to lose two electrons:','Mg','The element that would tend to lose two electrons are; Nitrogen is an element with five electrons in its outershell, Magnesium is an element which tends to lose two electrons, Oxygen is an element that would tend to gain two electrons, Halogen Group, Zero group ( Group 18 - noble gases) and Silicon. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 97; Exercise 3.27)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'2','mcq','Cr','Zn','Mg','Zr','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(31524,'chemistry','Identify an element that would tend to gain two electrons:','Se','Chalcogens (group 16 elements) have the tendency to gain two electrons. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 97; Exercise 3.27)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'1','mcq','Se','He','Ne','Xe','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(31525,'chemistry','Identify the group having metal, non-metal, liquid as well as gas at the room temperature.','Group 17','The elements of the group 17 having metal, non-metal, liquid as well as gas at the room temperature.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 97; Exercise 3.27)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Exercises',NULL,'2','mcq','Group 17','Group 16','Group 7','Group 6','NEW','11-083','11-083',NULL),
