(31601,'chemistry','Identify the incorrect match in the following diagram(n0301)','D, iv','Because element (Z=110) is ununnilium and its IUPAC offical name is Darmstadtium. \n\n(NEET 2020)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','A, i','B, ii','C, iii','D, iv','NEW','11-080','11-080','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/31601q.png'),
(31602,'chemistry','The element Z=114 has been discovered recently. It will belong to which of the following family/group and electronic configuration?','Carbon family, [Rn] 5f¹⁴ 6d¹⁰ 7s² 7p²','The electronic configuration of the element with Z = 114 (flerovium) is [Rn] 5f 14 6d 10 7s 2 7p 2. \n\nHence, it belongs to carbon family which has the same outer electronic configuration. \n\n(NEET 2017)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Carbon family, [Rn] 5f¹⁴ 6d¹⁰ 7s² 7p²','Oxygen family, [Rn] 5f¹⁴  6d¹⁰ 7s² 7p⁴','Nitrogen family, [Rn] 5f¹⁴  6d¹⁰ 7s² 7p⁶','Halogen family, [Rn] 5f¹⁴  6d¹⁰ 7s² 7p⁵','NEW','11-081','11-081',NULL),
(31603,'chemistry','For the second period elements the correct increasing order of first ionization enthalpy is:','Li < B < Be < C < O < N < F < Ne','The correct order of first ionisation enthalpy is: Li<B<Be<C<O<N<F<Ne. \n\n(NEET 2019)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Li < B < Be < C < N < O < F < Ne','Li < B < Be < C < O < N < F < Ne','Li < Be < B < C < N < O < F < Ne','i < Be < B < C < O < N < F < Ne','NEW','11-088','11-088',NULL),
(31604,'chemistry','Which of the following oxides is most acidic in nature?','BeO','BeO is most acidic in nature as the acidity of oxides decreases with increases in the electropositive character of the central atom. \n\n(NEET 2018)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','MgO','CaO','BeO','BaO','NEW','11-094','11-094',NULL),
(31605,'chemistry','Match the elements:','Copper-Transition metal, Fluorine-Non-metal, Silicon-Metalloid, Cerium-Lanthanoid','Copper belongs to transition metal, Fluorine belongs to non-metal, Silicon belongs to metalloid and Cerium belongs to lanthanoid. \n\n(NEET 2020)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','match','Copper,Transition metal','Fluorine,Non-metal','Silicon, Metalloid','Cerium, Lanthanoid','NEW','11-079','11-079',NULL),
(31606,'chemistry','Match the following:','CO-Neutral, BaO-Basic, Al₂O₃-Amphoteric, Cl₂O₇-Acidic','CO is neutral in nature, BaO is basic in nature, Al₂O₃ is amphoteric in nature and Cl₂O₇ is acidic in nature.\n\n(NEET 2020)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','match','Cl₂O₇, Acidic','Al₂O₃, Amphoteric','BaO, Basic','CO, Neutral','NEW','11-094','11-094',NULL),
(31607,'chemistry','Match the oxides given below:','N₂O-Neutral, Na₂O-Basic, Al₂O₃-Amphoteric, Cl₂O₇-Acidic','N₂O is neutral in nature, Na₂O is basic in nature, Al₂O₃ is amphoteric in nature and Cl₂O₇ is acidic in nature. \n\n(NEET 2019)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','match','Cl₂O₇,Acidic','Al₂O₃,Amphoteric','Na₂O,Basic','N₂O,Neutral','NEW','11-094','11-094',NULL),
(31608,'chemistry','Zr(Z=40) and Hf(Z=72) have similar atomic and ionic radii because of:','Lanthanoid contraction','Due to lanthanoid contraction Zr and Hf has similar atomic and ionic radii. \n\n(NEET 2020)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Lanthanoid contraction','Diagonal relationship','Having similar chemical properties','Belonging to same group','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31609,'chemistry','The correct sequence of bond enthalpy of \' C−X\' bond is:','CH₃​−F>CH₃−Cl>CH₃​−Br>CH₃−I','The correct sequence of bond enthalpy of \' C−X\' bond is: CH3​−F>CH3−Cl>CH3​−Br>CH3−I  \n\n(NEET 2021)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','CH₃​−F>CH₃−Cl>CH₃​−Br>CH₃−I','CH₃−F<CH₃−Cl<CH₃−Br<CH₃−I','CH₃−F<CH₃−Cl>CH₃−Br<CH₃−I','CH₃−F>CH₃−Cl<CH₃−Br<CH₃−I','NEW','11-089','11-089',NULL),
(31610,'chemistry','Noble gases are named because of their inertness towards reactivity. Identify an incorrect statement about them.','Noble gases have very high melting and boiling points.','Noble gases have very low boiling points due to weak dispersion forces. \n\nIn comparison to other substances of comparable atomic and molecular weights, noble gases have exceptionally lowmelting and boiling points. \n\n(NEET 2021)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Noble gases have very high melting and boiling points.','Noble gases are sparingly soluble in water.','Noble gases have weak dispersion forces.','Noble gases have large positive values of electron gain enthalpy.','NEW','11-089','11-089',NULL),
(31611,'chemistry','The incorrect statement among the following is :','Most of the trivalent Lanthanoid ions are colorless in the solid state.','The statement is incorrect because most of the trivalent lanthanoid ions are coloured in the solid state. \n\n(NEET 2021)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Actinoid contraction is greater for element to element than Lanthanoid contraction.','Most of the trivalent Lanthanoid ions are colorless in the solid state.','Lanthanoids are good conductors of heat and electricity.','Actinoids are highly reactive metals, especially when finely divided','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31612,'chemistry','From the following pairs of ions which one is not an iso-electronic pair?','Fe²⁺,Mn²⁺','Isoectronic ions should have same number of electrons.Fe²⁺,Mn²⁺ ions do not have same numbers of electrons.\n\nThus these are not isoelectronic species. \n\n(NEET 2021)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Fe²⁺,Mn²⁺','O²⁻,F⁻','Na⁺,Mg²⁺','Mn²⁺,Fe³⁺','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31613,'chemistry','Identify the incorrect match. diagram(n0301)','(d), (iv)','Because element (Z=110) is ununnilium and its IUPAC offical name is Darmstadtium. \n\n(NEET 2020)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','(d), (iv)','(c), (iii)','(b), (ii)','(a), (i)','NEW','11-080','11-080','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/31613q.png'),
(31614,'chemistry','The IUPAC Name of an element with atomic number 119 is','ununennium','Hint: For one un is used and for 9 en is used.The atomic number is 119. For 1 un is used and for 9 enn is used. Hence, the symbol and the name respectively are uue and \nununennium  Reference: (Class-11)(NEET2022)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','ununoctium','ununennium','unnilennium','unununnium','NEW','11-080','11-080',NULL),
(31615,'chemistry','The element expected to form the largest ion to achieve the nearest noble gas configuration is','N','In isoelectronic species, as the negative charge increases, the size of the ion also increases. \nStep 1: \nThe noble gas configuration of a given element is as follows:\n\nNa when lose one electron and form Na+ then it obtained a noble gas configuration.\nO when gain two electrons and form O2- then it obtained a noble gas configuration. \nF when gain one electron and form F- then it obtained a noble gas configuration. \nN when gain three electrons and form N3- then it obtained a noble gas configuration. \n\nStep 2: \nNa+ , O2-, F-,  and N3- are isoelectronic species. In the case of isoelectronic species, as the negative charge increases, the size of the ion also increases. \n\nThus, N3- has the largest size and Na+ has the minimum size. \n\nThus, option fourth is the correct answer. Reference: (Class-11)(NEET2023)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Na','O','F','N','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31616,'chemistry','Among the following,The most characteristic oxidation states for lead and tin are respectively:','2 and 4','This is due to the inert pair effect, As we move down the group, the intervening d and f electrons shield the ns2 electrons poorly and hence the ns2 electrons are pushed inside towards the nucleus.  This results in the reluctance of ns electrons to participate in bond formation and this phenomenon are called the Inert pair effect. Thus, the tendency to show lower oxidation state increases. Hence the most characteristic oxidation state for lead and tin are +2, +4 respectively.\nAs lead lies at the bottom of the group, it shows +2 oxidation state generally.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','4 and 2','2 and 4','4 and 4','2 and 2','NEW','11-085','11-085',NULL),
(31617,'chemistry','The correct order of increasing electron affinity for the elements ,O,S,F and Cl:','O<S<F<Cl','3rd-period elements are having higher electron affinity than 2nd period elements,The electron gain enthalpy is seen to decrease on moving down a group and increases from moving left to right in a period.Due to the smaller size of the members of III period compared to the corresponding members of second period, they have higher electron gain enthalpy.Thus, the electron gain enthalpy of Cl and F (17 group) is higher as compared to O and S (16 group). Cl has higher electron gain enthalpy as compared to F, this is because due to the small size of F atom, the incoming electron experiences a greater force of repulsion.Similarly, the electron gain enthalpy of S is higher as compared to O due to its small size.Therefore, the correct order of electron gain enthalpy of given elements is  : O < S < F < Cl','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Cl<F<O<S','O<S<F<Cl','F<S<O<CL','S<O<CL<F','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31618,'chemistry','The correct order of least negative to most negative electron gain enthalpy for C,Ca,Al,F,and O:','Ca<Al<C<O<F','As the electronegativity increases the value of electron gain enthalpy became more negative.Electron gain enthalpy is defined as the amount of energy released when an electron is added to an isolated gaseous atom. During the addition of an electron, energy can either be released or absorbed.Electron gain enthalpy becomes more negative along the period. This is because the effective nuclear charge increases from left to right and it will be easier to add an electron to a smaller atom.\nAlso, it becomes less negative down the group because size of the atom increases and the added electron will be farther from the nucleus.So, the order of least negative to most negative electron gain enthalpy will be: Ca < Al < C < O < F .','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Al<Ca<O<C,F','Al<O<C<Ca<F','C<F<O<Al<Ca','Ca<Al<C<O<F','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31619,'chemistry','Which one of the following arrangements represents the correct order of increasing electron gain enthalpy with negative sign of the given atomic species:','Cl<F<S<O','Hint: Second-period element has less negative electron gain enthalpy than third-period element\nThe electron gain enthalpy value for the given element is as follows:\nO = -141 kJ mol-1 ; S = -200 kJ mol-1 ; F = -328 kJ mol-1; Cl = -349 kJ mol-1 From O to Cl the value of electron gain enthalpy decreases because with the negative sign, the more negative value will be little and the less negative value will be high. \nThus, the order of electron gain enthalpy (with a negative sign) is Cl < F < S < O. If an electron affinity order is asked then the order would be  O< S< F < Cl  (without a negative sign)\nThus, option fourth is the correct answer because we had to consider the negative sign','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','F<Cl<O<S','S<O<Cl<F','O<S<Cl<F','Cl<F<S<O','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31620,'chemistry','The value of electron gain enthalpy of Na+ if  IE₁  of Na = 5.1eV, is','-5.1eV','The ionisation energy is the energy require to remove an electron from an atom while electron gain enthalpy is the energy require to gain an electron from an atom. Thus, for same atom the value will be same but signs will be opposite.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','+10.2eV','-5.1eV','-10.2eV','+2.55eV','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31621,'chemistry','The first ionization potential of Be and B will be respectively:','8.8 and 6.6','Ionization energy depends on the electronic configuration The electronic configuration of Be and B are as follows:\nBe = Is² 2s²\nB= 1s² 2s² 2p¹\nIn Be the first electron will be removed from fully filled 2s orbital and in B electron is removed from the partially filled 2p orbital. Removing electrons from fully filled 2s orbital is difficult than 2p orbital. Hence, Be ionization energy is more than B.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','8.8 and 8.8','6.6 and 6.6','6.6 and 8.8','8.8 and 6.6','NEW','11-088','11-088',NULL),
(31622,'chemistry','Incorrect order among the following','Li<Be<B<C-1st IP','HINT: First ionisation potential of Be > B\nExplanation: \n\n1. On going down the group , the electronegativity of the central atom decreases , and the bond between the central atom and hydrogen becomes less polar , so the bond strength also decreases hence it becomes easier to lose a hydrogen atom thus increasing the acidity.\nThus, acidic character decreases as we move from NH3  to AsH3.\n2. Across a period the ionization potential increases from left to right due to increase in net nuclear charge of elements.\nSo it should be like C > B > Be > Li. But there is an exception in the case of Be and B the ionization potential of Be is higher than that of B.\nIt is because of the fact that electronic configuration of Be is 1s22s2 and that of B is 1s22s22p1 and as electron in p can be removed easily as compared to the electron from s in Be. This is because of fully filled s orbitals.\nSo less energy will be required in the case of B than in Be. So the Ionization potential of Be will be greater than that of B.\nThus , the correct order will be like C > Be > B > Li instead of like C > B > Be > Li.\n3.On moving along a period, the tendency to lose electrons decreases and thus, the metallic character decreases. While on moving down the group, basic character increases due to its increase in metallic character.\nSo, the basic character of oxide is : Al2O3 < MgO < Na2O < K2O\n4. Down the group ionic radius increases. So the correct order of ionic radius is : Li+ < Na+ < K+ < Cs+','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','NH₃<PH₃<AsH₃-Acidic strength','Li<Be<B<C-1st IP','Al₂O₃<MgO<Na₂O<K₂O-Basic','Li+<Na+<K+<Cs+-Ionic radius','NEW','11-088','11-088',NULL),
(31623,'chemistry','Among the elements Ca,Mg,P and Cl, the correct order of increasing atomic radii is','Cl<P<Mg<Ca','HINT: See the atomic radii trend along the period and down the group.\n\nStep 1:\n\nThe atomic radii increase down the group so, the atomic radii of Mg < Ca because they belong to the same group.\n\nStep 2:\n\nMg, Cl, and P belong to the same period, and along the period from left to right, the atomic radii decrease. So, the order is Cl < P < Mg. \n\nThe atomic radii value is as follows: \n\nCa = 197 pm\nMg = 160 pm \nP = 110 pm\nCl = 99 pm\n\nThus, the correct order of atomic radii will be Cl < P < Mg < Ca.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Cl<P<Mg<Ca','P<Cl<Ca<Mg','Ca<Mg<P<Cl','Mg<Ca<Cl<P','NEW','11-086','11-086',NULL),
(31624,'chemistry','The correct order of ionic radii is:','N3->Mg+2>Al+3','HINT: Size of neutral atom is more than cation and less than anion.\n\nExplanation:\n\nOption 1 is incorrect as positively charged cation cannot be larger than the neutral atom of the same element.\n\nOption 2 and 3 are incorrect as negative charges having the same number of electrons are bigger than positive or neutral ions/atoms.\n\nOption 4 is the correct answer.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','H->H+>H','Na+>F->O2-','F->O2->Na+','N3->Mg+2>Al+3','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31625,'chemistry','The ions O2-,F-,Na+,Mg+2,and Al+3 are isoelectronic, Their ionic radii show:','A Significant decrease from O2- to Al+3','In isoelectronic species, cation size is less than an anion size','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','A Significant increase from O2- to Al+3','A Significant decrease from O2- to Al+3','Increase from O2- to F- and decrease from Na+ to Al3+','Decrease from O2- to F- and increase from Na+ to Al+3','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31626,'chemistry','If an atom has electronic configuration 1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d3 , we will place it in group number:','Fifth','HINT: Given configuration is of Vanadium(V) Explanation: Electronic configuration of atom is 1s22s22p63s23p63d34s2 As the valence electrons are present in d subshell , the given element belongs to d-block. If the element is in the d block, then the number of the group can be determined by the formula:( number of electrons in (n−1)d subshell )+ (number of electrons in (n) s subshell). Group no. of given atom = 3+2 = 5 Vanadium is present in group 5.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Fifth','Fifteenth','Second','Third','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(31627,'chemistry','Magnesium reacts with an element (X) to form an ionic compound. If the ground state electronic configuration of (X) is 1s² 2s² 2p³,the simplest formula for this compound is.','Mg3X2','Here Mg present in +2 oxidation state and the element \'X\' found to be Halogen by configuration and as we know halogen contains -1 charge .So by this we derive ionic compound formulae formed as Mg3X2','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Mg2X3','MgX2','Mg2X','Mg3X2','NEW','11-092','11-092',NULL),
(31628,'chemistry','Match the following in diagram(31628Q)','4','Hint: Left to right across the period atomic size decrease Left to right across the period size of atom decreases because effective nuclear charge increases. The order of elements according to their atomic radii is as follows: B < C < N < O B = 88 pm C = 77 pm N = 74 pm O = 66 pm Hence, option fourth is the correct answer.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-085','11-085','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/31628q.png'),
(31629,'chemistry','The correct order of ionic radii is:','N3->Mg+2>Al+3','HINT: Size of neutral atom is more than cation and less than anion.\n\nExplanation:\n\nOption 1 is incorrect as positively charged cation cannot be larger than the neutral atom of the same element.\n\nOption 2 and 3 are incorrect as negative charges having the same number of electrons are bigger than positive or neutral ions/atoms.\n\nOption 4 is the correct answer.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','H->H+>H','Na+>F->O2-','F->O2->Na+','N3->Mg+2>Al+3','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31630,'chemistry','Ionic Radii are:','inverse proportion to the effective nuclear charge.','Ionic radii decreases as the effective nuclear charge increases.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','inverse proportion to the effective nuclear charge.','inverse proportion to the square of effective nuclear charge.','direct proportion to the screening effect.','direct proportion to the square of screening effect.','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31631,'chemistry','The Incorrect match among the following:','B<C<N<O(Increasing First Ionisation Enthalpy)','HINT: Second period ionization energy does not increases in regular manner. Explanation: For option (1) First ionisation energy is the energy required to remove an electron from outermost shell. Hence, correct order is B < C < O < N. For option (2) Electron gain enthalpy is the energy required to gain an electron in the outermost shell. Hence, the correct order is I < Br < F < Cl. For option (3) As we move down the group in alkali metal, metallic radius increases Li < Na < K < Rb. For option (4) In case of isoelectronic species, as positive charge decreases or negative charge increases the ionic size of the species increases and vice-versa Al3+ < Mg2+ < Na+ <F- .','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','B<C<N<O(Increasing First Ionisation Enthalpy)','I<Br<F<Cl(Increasing Electron gain Enthalpy)','Li<Na<K<Rb(increasing Metallic Radii)','Al+3<Mg+2<Na+<F-(Increasing Ionic Size)','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31632,'chemistry','The first Ionization Enthalpies of elements X and Y are 419KJ/mol and 590KJ/mol ,respectively and the second Ionization Enthalpies of element X  and Y are 3069KJ/mol and 1145KJ/mol,respectively.The correct statement is;','X is an Alkali metal and Y is an Alkaline earth metal','Hint: Ionization enthalpy of element depends on the electronic configuration. The first ionization enthalpies of element X and Y are 419 kJ mol–1 and 590 kJ mol–1 and the second ionization enthalpy of  of X and Y are 3069 kJ mol–1 and 1145 kJ mol–1. The energy gap between first and second ionization enthalpy for X is very high. Thus it indicate that X has one valence electron in its outermost shell and after losing one electron it attained a noble gas configuration.  Hence, X is a alkali metal. The energy gap between first and second ionization enthalpy for Y is not much high. Thus it indicate that Y has atleast two valence electrons in its outermost shell. Hence, Y  is a alkaline earth metal. Hence, option first is the correct answer.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','X is an Alkali metal and Y is an Alkaline earth metal','X is an Alkaline eart metal and Y is an Alkali metal','Both X and Y are Alkali metals','Both X and Y are Alkaline earth metals','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31633,'chemistry','The correct order of decreasing second ionisation enthalpy of Ti(22), V(23), Cr(24) and Mn(25) is-','Cr > Mn > V > Ti','"Electronic configuration of the given elements are- Cr : [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹ Mn : [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s² V : [Ar] 3d³ 4s² Ti : [Ar] 3d² 4s² In general, ionization potential (both 1st and 2nd) increases from left to right across the period due to increase in effective nuclear charge. On this basis, the second IP values should exhibit the trend :Mn > Cr > V > Ti . But the actual observed order is : Cr > Mn > V > Ti Practically, only chromium is exceptional and rest others show the normal trend. This exceptional behaviour of chromium is due to the stable configuration (3d⁵) that it achieves after the loss of first electron. (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 221; Paragraph 8.3.3) (AIPMT 2008)"','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Mn > Cr > Ti > V','Ti > V > Cr > Mn','Cr > Mn > V > Ti','V > Mn > Cr > Ti','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31634,'chemistry','"The formation of the oxide ion, O 2– (g), from oxygen atom requires first an exothermic and then an endothermic step as shown below: O (g) + e –  → O– (g) ; ∆ H = – 141 kJ mol –1 O – (g) + e –  → O2– (g); ∆ H = + 780 kJ mol –1 Thus process of formation of O 2– in gas phase is unfavourable even though O2– is isoelectronic with neon. It is due to the fact that,"','electron repulsion outweighs the stability gained by achieving noble gas configuration.','There is a lot of repulsion when similar charges approach each other as O–(g), and electron are both negatively charged. To add an  electron under such situation, the force of repulsion is to be overcome  by applying external energy.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','oxygen is more electronegative.','addition of electron in oxygen results in larger size of the ion.','electron repulsion outweighs the stability gained by achieving noble gas configuration.','O – ion has comparatively smaller size than oxygen atom.','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31635,'chemistry','Among the following electronic configurations, an atom that has the lowest Ionization Enthalpy is:','1s2 2s2 2p6 3s1','HINT: 1s2 2s2 2p6 3s1 has lowest ionisation enthalpy among given configurations. Explanation: Ionisation enthalpy depends on the force of attraction with which the electrons are held by the nucleus. Due to exceptionally high stability of fully and exactly half-filled p orbitals like p3 and p6, high IE is required. 2p6 is also strongly held since it lies close to the nucleus(n=2) but 3s1 lies farther than 2p orbital and also contains only one electron. Hence it will have the lowest value of IE.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','1s2 2s2 2p5','1s2 2s2 2p3','1s2 2s2 2p6 3s1','1s2 2s2 2p6','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31636,'chemistry','Among the following electronic configuration, the one having the highest ionisation enthalpy  is ?','[Ne]3s² 3p³','Electronic  configuration with the highest ionisation enthalpy is  [Ne]3s² 3p³ on moving down the group the ionisation enthalpy decreases . In a period on moving from left to right ionisation enthalpy increases.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','[Ne]3s²','[Ne]3s² 3p³','[Ne]3s² 3p²','[Ar]3d¹⁰ 4s² 4p³','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31637,'chemistry','The correct of 1st IE among the following elements Be,B,C,N,O is:','B<Be<C<O<N','Hint - I.E is directly proportional to stable electronic configuration and inversely proportional to size. The energy required to remove the most loosely bound electron from an isolated gaseous atom is called the ionization energy. The ionization potential decreases as the size of the atom decreases. Atoms with fully or partly filled orbitals have high ionization potential. But in the case of Be and B, and N/O, Be /N has higher ionization potential due to the stable configuration of 2s2 and 2p3 respectively. The ionization potential values of given elements are as follows: B = 801 kJ/mol Be = 899 kJ/mol C = 1086 kJ/mol O = 1314 kJ/mol N = 1402 kJ/mol Thus, option 1 is the correct answer.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','B<Be<C<O<N','B<Be<C<N<O','Be<B<C<N<O','B<Be<C<O<N','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31638,'chemistry','The correct of 1st IE for the given four elements is:','C<O<N<F','HINT: Nitrogen has half-filled electronic configuration. Explanation: As we move from left to right across a period, the ionization energy of elements increases. This is due to the decrease in the size of atoms across a period. The valence electrons get closer to the nucleus of an atom as we move from left to right due to increased nuclear charge. But the ionization energy of N is more than O because of stable half-filled electronic configuration i.e. 1s2 2s2 2p3. So, the correct order of ionization energy is:  C < O < N < F Thus, option 3 is the correct answer.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','C<N<F<O','C<N<O<F','C<O<N<F','C<F<N<O','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31639,'chemistry','Which of the following arrangements does not give the correct picture of the trends indicated against it?','F2>Cl2>Br2>I2:Bond Dissociation Energy','In general as the bond length increases, the bond dissociation energy decreases. But the bond dissociation energy of F2 is less than that of Cl2. Due to the exceptionally small size of the Fluorine and high interelectronic repulsions, the energy required to dissociate the FF bond is not the highest as expected and the following order is followed. Cl2> Br2> F2> I2','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','F2>Cl2>Br2>I2:Oxidising Power','F<Cl>Br>I:Electron Gain Enthalpy','F2>Cl2>Br2>I2:Bond Dissociation Energy','F>Cl>Br>I:Electronegativity','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31640,'chemistry','Among the following, the most characterstic oxidation states of Lead and Tin are, respectively:','2 ,4','HINT: This is due to the inert pair effect. Explanation: As we move down the group, the intervening d and f electrons shield the ns2 electrons poorly and hence the ns2 electrons are pushed inside towards the nucleus.  This results in the reluctance of ns electrons to participate in bond formation and this phenomenon are called the Inert pair effect. Thus, the tendency to show lower oxidation state increases. Hence the most characteristic oxidation state for lead and tin are +2, +4 respectively. As lead lies at the bottom of the group, it shows +2 oxidation state generally.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','4, 2','2 ,4','4, 4','2, 2','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31641,'chemistry','The species of Ar, K+ , Ca+2 contains the same number of electron. In which order do their radii increase:','Ca+2<K+<Ar','For isoelectronic species, ionic radii decreases with increase in positive charge. So, the correct increasing order of ionic radii is Ca2+ < K+ <Ar. size of Ar = 1.91 A size of K = 2.03A size of K+ = 1.38 A size of Ca = 1.74 A size of Ca+2 = 1.0 A','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Ar<K+<Ca+2','Ca+2<Ar<K+','Ca+2<K+<Ar','K+<Ar,Ca+2','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31642,'chemistry','The Correct order of decreasing Ionic radii among the following Isoelectronic Species is:','S-2>Cl->K+>Ca+2','When a cation is formed, there is a deficiency of electrons as the electrons are taken away from the outer shell. The remaining electrons are therefore, seen to experience a greater force of attraction by the nucleus and in turn are strongly attracted towards the nucleus. The electrons are held tightly by the electrons and the radius is reduced. But in case of an anion, the electrons are added to the outer shell and the number of electrons increases. Therefore the hold of the nucleus decreases and the ionic radius increases. Thus, the correct order of ionic radii is : S2-> Cl-> K+> Ca2+','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Ca+2>K+>S-2>Cl-','Cl->S-2>Ca+2>K+','S-2>Cl->K+>Ca+2','K+>Ca+2>Cl->S-2','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31643,'chemistry','Identify the correct order of size of the following:','Ca+2<K+<Ar<Cl-<S-2','Here note that all the species are isoelectronic with 18 electrons. For isoelectronic species, as atomic number increases,number of protons increases and effective nuclear charge for 18 electrons increases and thus size will follow the order as shown below: Ca2+ < K+< Ar < Cl- < S2-','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Ca+2<K+<Ar<S-2<Cl-','Ca+2<K+<Ar<Cl-<S-2','Ar<Ca+2<K+<Cl-<S-2','Ca+2Ar<K+<Cl-<S-2','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31644,'chemistry','Match the element in Column I with that in Column II in diagram(31644Q):','2','HINT: Cerium is an f - block element. Explanation: The correct answer is as follows: 1. Copper – Transition metal. 2. Fluorine – Non-metal. 3. Silicon – Metalloids. 4. Cerium – Lanthanoid.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-085','11-085','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/31644q.png'),
(31645,'chemistry','An electronic configuration that has maximum difference between II and III ionization potential is:','1s2 2s2 2p6 3s1','HINT:  For stable electronic configuration I.P. will be more. Explanation: After removal of 1 electronic the configurations will be : 1. 1s2 2s2 2p6 2. 1s2 2s2 2p6 3s1 3. 1s2 2s2 2p5 4. 1s2 2s2 2p4 We need to see the electronic configuration for which the difference is maximum in (II) and  (III) I.P. For second electronic configuration after removal of 2nd electron atom will get stable electronic configuration ie 1s2 2s2 2p6. Thus, very high energy will be required to remove the third electron. So, the difference between (II) and (III) ionisation potential will be maximum in this case.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','1s2 2s2 2p6 3s1','1s2 2s2 2p6 3s1','1s2 2s2 2p6','1s2 2s2 2p5','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31646,'chemistry','Be+2 is isoelectronic with which of the following ions:','Li+','Both have exactly 2 electrons in the 1s orbital. NCERT Reference: Page Number: 84, Paragraph: 3.7.1 (b)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','H+','Li+','Na+','Mg+2','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(31701,'chemistry','Consider the isoelectronic species, Na+, Mg2+, F– and O2–. The correct order of increasing length of their radii is _________.','Mg 2+ < Na+ < F– < O2–','Amongst the isoelectronic species the ionic radii decrease as the nuclear charge increases Mg 2+ < Na+ < F– < O2–','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','F- < O2– < Mg 2+ < Na +','Mg 2+ < Na+ < F– < O2–','O 2– < F– < Na+ < Mg2+','O 2– < F– < Mg2+ < Na +','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31702,'chemistry','Which of the following is not an actinoid?','Terbium (Z = 65)','Actinoids are elements with Z= 90 – 103. Therefore, Terbium (Z = 65) is not an actinoid. Terbium (Z = 65) is a lanthanoid. Tb: [Xe]4f95d06s2','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Curium (Z = 96)','Californium (Z = 98)','Uranium (Z = 92)','Terbium (Z = 65)','NEW','11-083','11-083',NULL),
(31703,'chemistry','The order of screening effect of electrons of s, p, d and/orbitals of a given shell of an atom on its outer shell electrons is','s>p> d>f','The screening effect of the orbitals follows the order of s >p > d>f as screening effect decreases from s-orbital to f-orbital in an atom on account of shape of the orbital.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','s>p> d>f','f> d> p> s','p < d < s <f','f> p> s> d','NEW','11-089','11-089',NULL),
(31704,'chemistry','The first ionization enthalpies of Na, Mg, Al and Si are in the order','Na < Mg > Al < Si','The electronic configurations of Na and Mg are:\nNa (11): [Ne] 3s1 and Mg (12): [Ne] 3s2 In both\n the atoms, the electron is to be removed from 3s-orbital but nuclear \ncharge in Na is less than Mg. Thus, ionisation energy of Na is less than\n Mg (Na < Mg).\n\nThe electronic configurations of Mg and Al are:\n\nMg: [Ne] 3s2; Al: [Ne] 3s2 3pl\n\nIn Mg, the electron is to be removed from 3s-orbital while in Al, \nit is to be removed from 3p-orbital. Since it is easier to remove an \nelectron from 3p-orbital in comparison to 3s-orbital, the ionization \nenthalpy of Mg is higher than Al (Mg > Al).\n\nThe electronic configurations of Al and Si are:\n\nAl (13): [Ne] 3s2 3p1 Si (14): [Ne] 3s2 3p2\n\nIn both the atoms, the electron is to be removed from 3p-orbital \nbut nuclear charge in Si is more than Al. Thus, ionisation enthalpy of \nAl is less than Si','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Na < Mg > Al < Si','Na>Mg>Al>Si','Na < Mg < Al < Si','Na > Mg > Al < Si','NEW','11-088','11-088','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/31704q.png'),
(31705,'chemistry','The electronic configuration of gadolinium (Atomic number 64) is','[Xe] 4f 7 5d1 6s2','The electronic configuration of La (Z = 57) is [Xe] 5dl 6s2.\n Therefore, further addition of electrons occurs in the lower energy \n4f-orbital till it is exactly half-filled at Eu (Z = 63) Thus, the \nelectronic configuration of Eu is [Xe] 4f7 6s2. Thereafter, addition of next electron does not occur in the more stable exactly half-filled 4f7 shell but occurs in the little higher energy 5d-orbital. Thus, the electronic configuration of Gd (Z = 64) is [Xe] 4f7 5dl 6s2.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','[Xe] 4f 3 5d5 6s2','[Xe] 4f 7 5d2 6s1','[Xe] 4f 7 5d1 6s2','[Xe] 4f 8 5d6 6s2','NEW','11-079','11-079',NULL),
(31706,'chemistry','The statement that is not correct for periodic classification of elements is','For transition elements, the 3d-orbitals are filled with electrons after 3p-orbitals and before 4s-orbitals','For transition elements, the 3d-orbitals are filled with electrons after\n 3p and 4s-orbitals and before 4p-orbitals. The order of filling the \norbitals is:\n\nIs 2s 2p 3s 3p 4s 3d4p 5s 4d 5p 6s …','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','The properties of elements are periodic function of their atomic numbers.','Non-metallic elements are less in number than metallic elements.','For transition elements, the 3d-orbitals are filled with electrons after 3p-orbitals and before 4s-orbitals','The first ionization enthalpies of elements generally increase with increase in atomic number as we go along a period.','NEW','11-081','11-081','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/31706q.png'),
(31707,'chemistry','Among halogens, the correct order of amount of energy released in electron gain (electron gain enthalpy) is','F < Cl > Br > I','Chlorine has higher electron gain enthalpy than fluorine. This is due to small size of fluorine atom, i.e., the electron density is high which resists the addition of an electron (F < Cl).\nIn general, electron gain enthalpy decreases as atomic size increases. Thus, electron gain enthalpy follows the order:\nCl > Br > I','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','F > Cl > Br > I','F < Cl < Br < I','F < Cl > Br > I','F < Cl < Br > I','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31708,'chemistry','The period number in the long form of the periodic table is equal to','maximum principal quantum number of any element of the period.','Since each period starts with the filling of electrons in a new principal quantum number, therefore, the period number in the long form of the periodic table refers to the maximum principal quantum number of  any element in the period.\nPeriod number = maximum n of any element\n(where, n = principal quantum number).','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','magnetic quantum number of any element of the period.','atomic number of any element of the period.','maximum principal quantum number of any element of the period.','maximum Azimuthal quantum number of any element of the period.','NEW','11-078','11-078','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/31708q.png'),
(31709,'chemistry','The elements in which electrons are progressively filled in 4f-orbital are called','lanthanoids.','n lanthanoids, the electrons are filled in 4f-orbitals','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','actinoids.','transition elements.','lanthanoids.','halogens.','NEW','11-078','11-078','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/31709q.png'),
(31710,'chemistry','Which of the following is the correct order of size of the given species?','I – > I > I +','Anion is bigger than the parent atom and cation is smaller than the parent atom.\n\nThus, I– > I > I+','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','I > I – > I +','I + > I – > I','I > I + > I –','I – > I > I +','NEW','11-087','11-087','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/31710q.png'),
(213403,'chemistry','What is the name of the reaction given below?\n4HCl  +  O₂ 🠖 2Cl₂ + 2H₂O in presence of CuCl₂','Deacon\'s process','Deacon’s process: By oxidation of hydrogen chloride gas by atmospheric oxygen in the presence of CuCl₂ (catalyst) at 723 K.\nHCl + O₂ → 2Cl₂ + 2H₂O (In the presence of CuCl₂)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 202 Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Chlorine',NULL,'2','mcq','Haber\'s process','Deacon\'s process','Kolbe\'s process','Contact process','NEW','12-202',NULL,NULL),
(31711,'chemistry','The formation of the oxide ion, O 2– (g), from oxygen atom requires first an\nexothermic and then an endothermic step as shown below:\nO (g) + e –  → O– (g) ; ∆ H = – 141 kJ mol –1\nO – (g) + e –  → O2– (g); ∆ H = + 780 kJ mol –1\nThus process of formation of O 2– in gas phase is unfavourable even though O2– is isoelectronic with neon. It is due to the fact that,','electron repulsion outweighs the stability gained by achieving noble gas configuration.','There is a lot of repulsion when similar charges approach each other as O–(g), and electron are both negatively charged. To add an  electron under such situation, the force of repulsion is to be overcome  by applying external energy.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','oxygen is more electronegative.','addition of electron in oxygen results in larger size of the ion.','electron repulsion outweighs the stability gained by achieving noble gas configuration.','O – ion has comparatively smaller size than oxygen atom.','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31712,'chemistry','The element with atomic number 57 belongs to','d-block','The element with atomic number 57 is Lanthanum (La). Lanthanum belongs to the d-block of the periodic table.\n This is because the electrons that occupy the f-orbitals of these elements play a significant role in their chemical and physical properties.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','s-block','p-block','d-block','f-block','NEW','11-079','11-079',NULL),
(31713,'chemistry','The last element of the p-block in 6th period is represented by the outermost electronic configuration','4f 14 5d10 6s2 6p6','The p-block elements are the elements in the periodic table that have their valence electrons in the p orbital of their outermost energy level. In the 6th period, the p-block elements include Sulfur (S), Selenium (Se), Tellurium (Te), and Polonium (Po), which have electron configurations of [Ne] 3s2 3p4, [Ar] 3d10 4s2 4p4, [Kr] 4d10 5s2 5p4, and [Xe] 4f14 5d10 6s2 6p4, respectively.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','7s2 7p6','5f14 6d10 7s2 7p0','4f 14 5d10 6s2 6p6','4f 14 5d10 6s 2 6p 4','NEW','11-079','11-079',NULL),
(31714,'chemistry','Which of the elements whose atomic numbers are given below,  cannot be accommodated in the present set up of the long form of the  periodic table?','126','The atomic numbers 107, 118, 126, and 102 correspond to the elements bohrium (Bh), ununoctium (Uuo), unbihexium (Unbihexium), and nobelium (No), respectively.\n\nCurrently, the long form of the periodic table includes elements up to atomic number 118 (Oganesson, Og), which was added in 2016. However, the element with atomic number 126 (Unbihexium) has not yet been synthesized or discovered, and its properties are unknown.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','107','118','126','102','NEW','11-079','11-079',NULL),
(31715,'chemistry','The electronic configuration of the element which is just above the element with atomic number 43 in the same group is________ .','1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 3d 5 4s2','The element just above Technetium in the same group is Ruthenium (Ru), which has an atomic number of 44. The electronic configuration of Ruthenium is:\n\n1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 3d6 4s2\n\nTherefore, the correct electronic configuration of the element just above the element with atomic number 43 (Technetium) in the same group is:\n\n1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 3d5 4s2\n\nThis corresponds to the electronic configuration of Ruthenium (Ru), which is the element just above Technetium in group 7 of the periodic table.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 3d 5 4s2','1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 3d 5 4s3 4p6','1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 3d 6 4s2','1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 3d 7 4s2','NEW','11-079','11-079',NULL),
(31716,'chemistry','The elements with atomic numbers 35, 53 and 85 are all ________','halogens','Halogens are a group of nonmetallic elements in the periodic table that have seven valence electrons, which make them highly reactive. The halogen group includes the elements fluorine (F), chlorine (Cl), bromine (Br), iodine (I), and astatine (At).\n\nThe element with atomic number 35 is chlorine (Cl), which is a halogen.\n\nThe element with atomic number 53 is iodine (I), which is also a halogen.\n\nThe element with atomic number 85 is astatine (At), which is the heaviest halogen and the rarest naturally occurring element on Earth.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','noble gases','halogens','heavy metals','light metals','NEW','11-079','11-079',NULL),
(31717,'chemistry','Electronic configurations of four elements A, B, C and D are given below :\n(A) 1s2 2s2 2p6                          \n\n(B) 1s2 2s2 2p4\n\n(C) 1s2 2s2 2p6 3s1                  \n\n(D) 1s2 2s2 2p5\nWhich of the following is the correct order of increasing tendency to gain electron :','A < C < B < D','A – Is2 2s2 2p6 – Noble gas configuration\nB -1s2 2s2 2p4 – 2 electrons short of stable configuration\nC – 1s2 2s2 2p6 3s1 – Requires one electron to complete 5-orbital\nD -1s2 2s2 2p5 – Requires one electron to attain noble gas configuration\nNoble gases have no tendency to gain electrons since all their  orbitals are completely filled. Thus, element A has the least electron  gain enthalpy.\nSince element D has one electron less and element B has two electrons less than the corresponding noble gas configuration, hence,  element D has the highest electron gain enthalpy followed by element B.Since, element C has one electron in the 5-orbital and hence needs  one more electron to complete it, therefore, electron gain enthalpy of C is less than that of element B. Combining all the facts given above, \nthe electron gain enthalpies of the four elements increase in the order A  < C < B < D.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','A < C < B < D','A < B < C < D','D < B < C < A','D < A < B < C','NEW','11-091','11-091',NULL),
(31718,'chemistry','Assertion (A) : Generally, ionisation enthalpy increases from left to right in a period.\n\nReason (R) : When successive electrons are added to the orbitals in the same principal quantum level, the shielding effect of inner core of electrons does not increase very much to compensate for the increased attraction of the electron to the nucleus','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation of assertion.','Ionisation enthalpy increases from left to right across the period due to decrease in atomic size.\n\nThe electrons present within the subshell has almost same effective nuclear charge.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion is correct statement and reason is wrong statement.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion and reason both are wrong statements.','Assertion is wrong statement and reason is correct statement.','NEW','11-089','11-089',NULL),
(213404,'chemistry','What does the electrolysis of Brine solution give?','Chlorine','Electrolytic process: Chlorine is obtained by the electrolysis of brine (concentrated NaCl solution). Chlorine is liberated at anode. It is also obtained as a by–product in many chemical industries.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 202 Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Chlorine',NULL,'1','mcq','Chlorine','Sodium','Hydrogen','Oxygen','NEW','12-202',NULL,NULL),
(31719,'chemistry','Assertion (A) : Boron has a smaller first ionisation enthalpy than beryllium.\n\nReason (R) : The penetration of a 2s electron to the nucleus is more than the 2p electron hence 2p electron is more shielded by the inner core of electrons than the 2s electrons','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation of assertion.','Boron (1s22s22p1) has less first ionisation enthalpy than beryllium (1s22s2) because beryllium has fully filled .s-subshell.\n\n2s-electrons are nearer to the nucleus as compared to 2s-electrons.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion is correct statement and reason is wrong statement.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion and reason both are wrong statements.','Assertion is wrong statement and reason is correct statement.','NEW','11-089','11-089',NULL),
(31720,'chemistry','Assertion (A) : Electron gain enthalpy becomes less negative as we go down a group.\n\nReason (R) : Size of the atom increases on going down the group and the added electron would be farther from the nucleus.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation of assertion.','On moving down the group, the electron gain enthalpy becomes less  negative because on moving down the group the atomic size increases and  the added electron lies away from the nucleus.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Assertion is correct statement and reason is wrong statement.','Assertion and reason both are correct statements and reason is correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion and reason both are wrong statements.','Assertion is wrong statement and reason is correct statement.','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31721,'chemistry','Match the correct atomic radius with the element','Be-111, O-66, C-77, B-88','The atomic radius is defined as one-half of the distance between the nuclei of two identical atoms that are bonded together.\n\nOut of the given options, the correct match of atomic radius with the element is:\n\nBe (beryllium) - 111 pm (picometers)\nO (oxygen) - 66 pm\nC (carbon) - 77 pm\nB (boron) - 88 pm','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','match','Be,111','C,77','O,66','B,88','NEW','11-086','11-086',NULL),
(31722,'chemistry','Which of the following elements can show covalency greater than 4?\n(i) Be\n(ii) P\n(iii) S\n(iv) B','ii,iii','P and S have d-orbitals in their valence shell and therefore, can \naccommodate more than 8. electrons in their respective valence shells. \nHence they show covalency more than 4.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i,ii','ii,iii','iii,iv','i.iv','NEW','11-092','11-092',NULL),
(31723,'chemistry','Those elements impart colour to the flame on heating in it, the atoms of which\nrequire low energy for the ionisation (i.e., absorb energy in the visible region\nof spectrum). The elements of which of the following groups will impart colour\nto the flame?\n(i) 2\n(ii) 13\n(iii) 1\n(iv) 17','i,iii','The elements of group 1 (alkali metals) and group 2 (alkaline earth \nmetals) have low ionization enthalpies. Therefore, they impart colour to\n flame.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i,ii','ii,iii','iii,iv','i,iii','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(31724,'chemistry','Which of the following sequences contain atomic numbers of only\nrepresentative elements?\n(i) 3, 33, 53, 87\n(ii) 2, 10, 22, 36\n(iii) 7, 17, 25, 37, 48\n(iv) 9, 35, 51, 88','i,iv','Elements of 5 and p-block elements are called representative elements. \nElements of f-block (Z=21 – 30; 39 – 48; 57 and 72 – 80; 89 and 104 – \n112) are called transition elements while those of f-block (with Z = \n58-71 and Z = 90 – 103) are called inner transition elements.\n\n(a) 3 – Group 1, 33 – group 15, 53 – group 17 and 87 – group 1.\n\n(d) 9 – Group 17, 35 – Group 17, 51 – Group 15, 88 – Group 2.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i,ii','ii,iii','iii,iv','i,iv','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(31725,'chemistry','Which of the following elements will gain one electron more readily in\ncomparison to other elements of their group?\n(i) S (g)\n(ii) Na (g)\n(iii) O (g)\n(iv) Cl (g)','i,iv','Chlorine has highest tendency to gain an electron and its electron gain \nenthalpy (-ve) is high. O and S belong to group 16 but S has larger \ntendency to accept electron.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','i,ii','ii,iii','iii,iv','i,iv','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31726,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Helium has the highest first ionisation enthalpy in the periodic table.\n(ii) Chlorine has less negative electron gain enthalpy than fluorine.\n(iii) Mercury and bromine are liquids at room temperature.\n(iv) In any period, atomic radius of alkali metal is the highest.','i,iii,iv','Chlorine has more negative electron gain enthalpy than fluorine due to bigger size and lesser electronic repulsion.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','i,ii,iii','ii,iii','iii,iv','i,iii,iv','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31727,'chemistry','Which of the following sets contain only isoelectronic ions?\n(i) Zn2+ , Ca2+, Ga 3+, Al3+\n(ii) K +, Ca2+, Sc3+, Cl –\n(iii) P3–, S2–, Cl– , K+\n(iv) Ti 4+, Ar, Cr 3+ , V5+','ii,iii','(a) Zn2+ (30 – 2 = 28), Ca2+ (20 – 2 = 18), Ga3+ (31-3= 28), Al3+ (13 – 3 = 10) are not isoelectronic.\n\n(b) K+ (19 – 1 = 18), Ca2+ (20 – 2 = 18), Sc3+ (21 – 3 = 18), Cl– (17 + 1 = 18) are isoelectronic.\n\n(c) P3- (15 + 3 = 18), S2- (16 + 2 = 18), Cl– (17 + 1 = 18), K+ (19 – 1 = 18) are isoelectronic.\n\n(d) Ti4+ (22 – 4 = 18), Ar (18), Cr3+ (24 – 3 = 21), V5+ (23 – 5 = 18) are not isoelectronic.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i,ii','ii,iii','iii,iv','i,iv','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31728,'chemistry','In which of the following options order of arrangement does not agree with\nthe variation of property indicated against it?\n(i) Al 3+ < Mg2+ < Na+ < F– (increasing ionic size)\n(ii) B < C < N < O (increasing first ionisation enthalpy)\n(iii) I < Br < Cl < F (increasing electron gain enthalpy)\n(iv) Li < Na < K < Rb (increasing metallic radius)','ii,iii','For increasing first ionization enthalpy, the order should be:\n\nB < C < O < N\n\nFor increasing electron gain enthalpy, the order should be:\n\nI < Br < F < Cl','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','i,ii','ii,iii','iii,iv','i,iv','NEW','11-088','11-088',NULL),
(31729,'chemistry','Which of the following have no unit?\n(i) Electronegativity\n(ii) Electron gain enthalpy\n(iii) Ionisation enthalpy\n(iv) Metallic character','i,iv','Electron gain enthalpy and ionization enthalpy have units of enthalpy.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i,ii','ii,iii','iii,iv','i,iv','NEW','11-089','11-089',NULL),
(31730,'chemistry','Ionic radii vary in\n(i) inverse proportion to the effective nuclear charge.\n(ii) inverse proportion to the square of effective nuclear charge.\n(iii) direct proportion to the screening effect.\n(iv) direct proportion to the square of screening effect.','iii,i','Ionic radii decreases as the effective nuclear charge increases.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','i,ii','ii,iii','iii,i','i,iv','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(31731,'chemistry','An element belongs to 3rd period and group-13 of the periodic table. Which of\nthe following properties will be shown by the element?\n(i) Good conductor of electricity\n(ii) Liquid, metallic\n(iii) Solid, metallic\n(iv) Solid, non metallic','iii,i','The element belonging to 3rd period and 13th group is aluminium which is a metal. Hence, it is solid, metallic and good conductor of electricity.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i,ii','ii,iii','iii,i','i,iv','NEW','11-085','11-085',NULL),
(31732,'chemistry','Choose the correct order of atomic radii of fluorine and neon (in pm) out of\nthe options given below','72, 160','Atomic radius decreases as we move from left to right in a period in the\n periodic table. Fluorine has the smallest atomic radius. As we move to \nneon\n\nin the same period, the atomic radius increases as it has van der Waals \nradius and van der Waals radii are bigger than covalent radii.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','72, 160','160, 160','72, 72','160, 72','NEW','11-086','11-086',NULL),
(31801,'chemistry','The correct decreasing order of positive electron\ngain enthalpy for the following inert gases\nHe, Ne, Kr, Xe','Ne > Kr > Xe > He','Correct order is Ne > Kr > Xe > He','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> JEE Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','He > Ne > Kr > Xe','He > Ne > Xe > Kr','He > Xe > Ne > Kr','Ne > Kr > Xe > He','NEW','11-090','11-090','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/31801q.png'),
(31802,'chemistry','Arrange the following elements in increasing order\nof metallic character\nSi, K, Mg and Be','Si < Be < Mg < K','Based on the electronegativity of the given\nelements, the correct increasing order of metallic\ncharacter is\nSi < Be < Mg < K','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> JEE Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Si < Mg < Be < K','Be < Mg < Si < K','Si < Be < Mg < K','K < Mg < Si < Be','NEW','11-095','11-095','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/31802q.png'),
(31803,'chemistry','A : Alkali metals show characteristic colour in\nreducing flame.\nR : They can be identified by flame test','Assertion is false and reason is true','Alkali metals show characteristic colour in oxidising\nflame.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> JEE Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Assertion is true and reason is false','Assertion is false and reason is true','Both assertion and reason are true reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true. But reason is not the correct explanation of assertion','NEW','11-095','11-095',NULL),
(31804,'chemistry','Which of the following order is correct regarding\nmagnitude of first electron gain enthalpy?','O < S','first electron gain enthalpy order : Cl > F > Br > I\nS > Se > Te > O','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> JEE Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cl < F','O < S','Te < O','S < Se','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31805,'chemistry','Which of the following option contains all the\nisoelectronic species?','S–2 , Cl-, K , Ca +2','S–2 , Cl-, K , Ca +2  all the species contain 18\nelectrons.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> JEE Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','N3–, O2–, F, Na','S–2 , Cl-, K , Ca +2','NH , CH3 4 5, PF , Na⁺','Ne, Na , F, N ⁻³','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31806,'chemistry','What is the correct orderr of electron gain enthalpy of Cl, F, Te, Po','Cl > F > Te > Po','Down the group electron affinity decreases. The size of fluorine atom is small. The addition of electron to small fluorine atom\nresults in electron-electron repulsions. Hence, fluorine has less electron affinity than chlorine.\nHence, the correct order is Cl > F > Te > Po','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> JEE Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','F > Cl > Te > Po','Cl > F > Te > Po','Te > Po > Cl > F','Po > Te > F > Cl','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31807,'chemistry','The ionic radii of K+, Na+, Al3+ and Mg2+ are in the order :','Al3+ < Mg2+ < Na+ < K+','Al3+, Mg2+ Na+ are isoelectronic ionic species. For monatomic ionic isoelectronic species as positive charge increases\nionic size decreases.\nThe order of size of Na+& K+ is  Na+ < K+\nIncreasing order of ionic radii Al3+ < Mg2+ < Na+ < K+','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> JEE Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Na+ < K+ < Mg2+ < Al3+','Al3+ < Mg2+ < K+ < Na+','Al3+ < Mg2+ < Na+ < K+','K+ < Al3+ < Mg2+ < Na+','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31808,'chemistry','The correct order of first ionisation enthalpy among following elements Be, B, C, N, O is','B < Be < C < O < N','The ionisation enthalpy increases as we go from left to right in a period, while it decreases as we come down a\ngroup. So according to this, the order will be Be < B < C < N < O .But there are two exceptions, in the case of Be and N because of the highly stable 2s² (completely filled orbital) and 2s²2p³ valence\nconfiguration. Due to this, the correct order is: B < Be < C < O < N','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> JEE Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','B < Be < C < O < N','B < Be < C < N < O','Be < B < C < N < O','Be < B < C < O < N','NEW','11-088','11-088',NULL),
(31809,'chemistry','Which one of the following will not give flame test?','Be','The electrons in beryllium and magnesium are too strongly bound to get excited by flame. Hence, these elements do not\nimpart any colour to the flame. The flame test for Ca, Sr and Ba is helpful in their detection in qualitative analysis and\nestimation by flame photometry','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> JEE Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Ca','Ba','Sr','Be','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(31810,'chemistry','The correct order of ionic size of N3−, Na+, F−, Mg2+  and O2− is','Mg2+ < Na+ < F− < O2− < N3−','These are all isoelectronic species. More the negative charge on the species, more is the size of that ion and more the\npositive charge on the species, less is the size of that ion.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> JEE Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Mg2+ > Na+ > F− > O2− < N3−','N3− < F− > O2− > Na+ > Mg2+','Mg2+ < Na+ < F− < O2− < N3−','N3− > O2− > F− > Na+ < Mg2+','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(31811,'chemistry','The isotopes of Hydrogen differ in the following property','Atomic mass','Isotopes are the atoms of the same element with different mass number. They have the same atomic number. So, number of\nprotons and electrons are same, Hence their electronic configuration are same, but number of neutrons are different.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> JEE Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Electronic configuration','Number of protons','Atomic number','Atomic mass','NEW','11-085','11-085',NULL),
(31812,'chemistry','Correct order of first ionisation energy for the elements with given electronic configuration is:\nI) 3s²\nII) 3s²3p¹\nIII) 3s²3p³\nIV) 3s²3p⁴','(III) > (IV) > (I) > (II)','Electronic configuration of Al = 1s22s22p63s23p1\nElectronic configuration of Mg = 1s22s22p63s2\nAl has one unpaired electron in its highest energy orbital (3p) , and Mg highest energy orbital (3s) has the paired electrons.\nIt is energetically favourable for all the electrons in an orbital to be paired, which means that breaking up this pair would require more energy.\nCompared 16th to group, 15 group have high ionisation energy. It is because of the stable half filled configuration of  the 15th group elements.\nP-3s² 3p³\nS-3s²  3p⁴\nTherefore P has more ionisation energy than S','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> JEE Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(III) > (IV) > (I) > (II)','(IV) > (III) > (II) > (I)','(I) > (II) > (III) > (IV)','(II) > (III) > (I) > (IV)','NEW','11-088','11-088',NULL),
(31813,'chemistry','The correct decreasing order for magnitude of electron gain enthalpy is:','S > Se > Te > O','Correct order of electron gain enthalpy is :-\nO < S > Se > Te\n⇒ S > Se > Te > O\n(Oxygen shows least electron gain enthalpy due to small size of atom)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> JEE Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','S > Se > Te > O','Te > Se > S > O','O > S > Se > Te','S > O > Se > Te','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(31814,'chemistry','Match the following electronic configuration with Hᵢₑvalues','1s²  2s²-899,1s² 2s² 2p¹-801,1s² 2s²  2p³-1400,1s² 2s² 2p⁴-1300','Order : B < Be < O < N','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> JEE Questions',NULL,'1','match','1s²  2s², 899','1s² 2s² 2p¹,801','1s² 2s²  2p³,1300','1s² 2s² 2p⁴,1400','NEW','11-088','11-088',NULL),
(31815,'chemistry','Within each pair of elements F & Cl, S & Se and Li & Na, \nrespectively, the elements that release more energy upon an electron \ngain are:','Cl, S and Li','Despite F being more electronegative than Cl, due to the small size \nof F, Cl would have a more negative value of electron gain enthalpy \nbecause of inter-electronic repulsions.\n\nAs we go down the group, the negative electron gain enthalpy decreases.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> JEE Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Cl, Se and Na','Cl, S and Li','Cl, S and Li','F, Se and Na','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(32001,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct regarding the chemical properties of Na2O and Cl2O7 with water?','Na2O reacts with water to form a basic solution, while Cl2O7 reacts with water to form an acidic solution.','When Na2O reacts with water, it forms NaOH which is a strong base and gives a basic solution.\nNa2O + H2O → 2NaOH\nOn the other hand, Cl2O7 reacts with water to form HClO4 which is a strong acid and gives an acidic solution.\nCl2O7 + H2O → 2HClO4\n(NCERT Chemistry Class 11, Page 91 Box Problem 3.10)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Box Questions and Intext Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Na2O reacts with water to form a basic solution, while Cl2O7 reacts with water to form an acidic solution.','Na2O reacts with water to form an acidic solution, while Cl2O7 reacts with water to form a basic solution.','Both Na2O and Cl2O7 react with water to form a basic solution.','Both Na2O and Cl2O7 react with water to form an acidic solution.','NEW','11-094','11-094',NULL),
(32002,'chemistry','Which of the following is a possible compound formed by Si and Br','SiBr4','Silicon is group 14 element with a valence of 4; bromine belongs to the halogen family with a valence of 1. Hence the formula of the compound formed would be SiBr4.(NCERT Chemistry Class 11, Page 89 Box Problem 3.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Box Questions and Intext Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','SiBr','SiBr2','SiBr3','SiBr4','NEW','11-092','11-092',NULL),
(32003,'chemistry','Which of the following is a possible compound formed by Aluminium and sulphur','Al2S3','Aluminium belongs to group 13 with a valence of 3; sulphur belongs to group 16 elements with a valence of. Hence, the formula of the compound formed would be Al2S3.(NCERT Chemistry Class 11, Page 89 Box Problem 3.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Box Questions and Intext Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Al2S3','Al3S8','Al4S3','Al2S6','NEW','11-092','11-092',NULL),
(32004,'chemistry','Select the Pair which will have the most\nnegative electron gain enthalpy and which\nthe least negative respectively?\nP, S, Cl, F.','Cl,P','Electron gain enthalpy generally becomes more negative across a period as we move from left to right. Within a group, electron gain enthalpy becomes less negative down a group. However, adding an electron to the 2p-orbital leads to greater repulsion\nthan adding an electron to the larger 3p-orbital. Hence the element with most negative electron gain enthalpy is chlorine; the one with the least negative electron gain enthalpy is phosphorus.\n(NCERT Chemistry Class 11, Page 87 Box Problem 3.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Box Questions and Intext Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Cl,F','Cl,P','F,S','F,P','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(32005,'chemistry','Which one of the following species will have the largest and the smallest size Respectively?\nMg, Mg2+, Al, Al3+ .','Mg ,Al3+','Atomic radii decrease across a period. Cations are smaller than their parent atoms. Among isoelectronic species, the one with the larger positive nuclear charge will have a smaller radius. Hence the largest species is Mg; the smallest one is Al3+.\n(NCERT Chemistry Class 11, Page 84 Box Problem 3.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Box Questions and Intext Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Mg,Al','Al,Al+3','Mg ,Al3+','Mg2+,Al3+','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(32006,'chemistry','Arrange the following elements in the increasing order of metallic character : Si, Be, Mg, Na, P.','P < Si <Be < Mg < Na.','Metallic character increases down a group and decreases along a period as we movefrom left to right. Hence the order of increasing metallic character is: P < Si < Be < Mg < Na.(NCERT Chemistry Class 11, Page 82 Box Problem 3.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Box Questions and Intext Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','P < Si <Be < Mg < Na.','Na >Mg >Si >Be >P','Si <P<Be < Mg < Na.','Na >Be >Mg> Si >P','NEW','11-095','11-095',NULL),
(32007,'chemistry','If the elements Z = 120  was  discovered. In which family will it be placed?','Alkaline Earth Metals','The element with Z = 120, will be placed in Group 2 (alkaline earth metals).\nUnbinilium is the name of the elements .The electronic configuration will be [ 1 s²  2 s²  2 p⁶ 3 s ² 3 p⁶ 4 s ² 3 d¹⁰ 4 p⁶  5 s ² 4 d¹⁰ 5 p⁶ 6 s² 4 f¹⁴ 5 d¹⁰ 6 p⁶ 7 s² 5 f¹⁴ 6 d¹⁰ 7 p 6 8 s² .(NCERT Chemistry Class 11, Page 81 Box Problem 3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Box Questions and Intext Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Carbon Family','Boron Family','Nitrogen Family','Alkaline Earth Metals','NEW','11-079','11-079',NULL),
(32008,'chemistry','If the elements Z = 117 was  discovered. In which family will it be placed?','Halogen Family','Element with Z= 117, would belong to the halogen family (Group 17) and the electronicconfiguration would be [Rn] 5f14 6d10 7s2 7p5 , (NCERT Chemistry Class 11, Page 84 Box Problem 3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Box Questions and Intext Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Nobel Gases','Alkali Metals','Halogen Family','Nitrogen Family','NEW','11-079','11-079',NULL),
(32009,'chemistry','What would be the symbol for the element with atomic number 120','Ubn','the roots for 1, 2 and 0 are un, bi and nil, respectively. Hence, the symbol and the name respectively are Ubn and unbinilium.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11, Page 78 Box Problem 3.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Box Questions and Intext Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ubn','Unu','Uub','Uut','NEW','11-081','11-081',NULL),
(32010,'chemistry','Which of the following statements justifies the presence of 18 elements in the 5th period of the Periodic Table?','The 5th period has the largest number of orbitals, which can accommodate 18 elements.','When n = 5, l = 0, 1, 2, 3. The order in which the energy of the available orbitals4d, 5s and 5p increases is 5s < 4d < 5p.The total number of orbitals available are 9. The maximum number of electrons thatcan be accommodated is 18; and therefore 18 elements are there in the 5thperiod (NCERT Chemistry Class 11, Page 79  Box Problem 3.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Box Questions and Intext Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','The 5th period has the largest number of orbitals, which can accommodate 18 elements.','The 5th period corresponds to the filling of the n=5 shell.','The 5th period elements have the same number of valence electrons.','The 5th period is the transition period from metals to nonmetals.','NEW','11-081','11-081',NULL),
(32011,'chemistry','Which of the following statements justifies the prediction of the first ionization enthalpy value for Al?','Al has a higher nuclear charge than Si, indicating a higher ionization enthalpy.','The first ionization enthalpy is the energy required to remove the most loosely held electron from an atom in the gaseous state. This means that it takes more energy to remove an electron from an atom with a higher nuclear charge because the attraction between the positively charged nucleus and the negatively charged electron is stronger.In the given question, the ionization enthalpy of Mg (737 kJ/mol) is higher than that of Na (496 kJ/mol) because Mg has a higher nuclear charge than Na. Similarly, Si has a higher nuclear charge than Al, so it is expected that the ionization enthalpy of Al will be higher than that of Si.Therefore, the correct option is C) Al has a higher nuclear charge than Si, indicating a higher ionization enthalpy.(NCERT Chemistry Class 11, Page 86 Box Problem 3.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> NCERT Box Questions and Intext Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Al has a smaller atomic radius than Si, indicating a higher ionization enthalpy.','Al is a metal, indicating a lower ionization enthalpy than Si.','Al has a higher nuclear charge than Si, indicating a higher ionization enthalpy.','Al has a half-filled p-orbital, indicating a lower ionization enthalpy than Si.','NEW','11-088','11-088',NULL),
(32101,'chemistry','Assertion: The number of elements known in 1865 was double that in 1800.\nReason: A significant increase in the number of elements was observed during this period due to the discovery of new elements.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The number of known elements increased significantly between 1800 and 1865 due to the discovery of new elements during this period. In 1800, only about 30 elements were known, while by 1865 the number of known elements had doubled to about 60.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-074','11-074',NULL),
(32102,'chemistry','Assertion: All the known elements are natural.\nReason: The recently discovered elements are man-made.','Assertion is is incorrect but Reason is correct.','While the majority of elements are naturally occurring, some elements have been synthesized through artificial nuclear reactions. These man-made elements are typically highly unstable and decay quickly, which makes them difficult to study and observe.\n\nThe first synthetic element, technetium (Tc), was synthesized in 1937, and since then, many other synthetic elements have been created in laboratories through various nuclear reactions.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is is incorrect but Reason is correct.','Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-074','11-074',NULL),
(32103,'chemistry','Assertion: It is easy to study the chemistry of all the known elements and their innumerable compounds individually.\nReason: Scientists have developed a systematic way to classify the elements to ease out the problem.','Assertion is is incorrect but Reason is correct.','While scientists have developed a systematic way to classify the elements into groups and periods in the periodic table, studying the chemistry of all known elements and their innumerable compounds individually is not an easy task.\n\nThere are more than 100 known elements, each with their unique chemical properties, and the number of possible compounds that can be formed with these elements is virtually infinite. Studying the chemistry of each element and its compounds individually requires extensive experimentation, analysis, and knowledge of chemistry.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is is incorrect but Reason is correct.','Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-074','11-074',NULL),
(32104,'chemistry','Assertion: The Law of Triads is considered a coincidence and dismissed.\nReason: The Law of Triads works only for a few elements.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Law of Triads was a classification system proposed by the German chemist Johann Wolfgang Döbereiner in 1829, which stated that certain groups of three elements had similar chemical and physical properties, and that the middle element\'s atomic weight was approximately equal to the average of the other two.\n\nHowever, the Law of Triads was later dismissed as a coincidence because it did not hold true for all elements, and it was difficult to find similar triads for many elements.','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-075','11-075',NULL),
(213405,'chemistry','Chlorine is a _________ gas with ___________ and suffocating odour.','greenish yellow, pungent','Chlorine is a greenish yellow gas with pungent and suffocating odour.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 203 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Chlorine',NULL,'1','mcq','blue, punget','greenish yellow, pungent','yellow, pungent','colourless, pungent*','NEW','12-203',NULL,NULL),
(32105,'chemistry','Assertion: A cylindrical table of elements was developed by A.E.B. de \nChancourtois in 1862.\nReason: The table arranged the then-known elements in order of \ndecreasing atomic weights and showed the periodic recurrence of \nproperties.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The cylindrical table of elements was developed by A.E.B. de Chancourtois in 1862, by arranging the then-known elements in order of increasing atomic weights.\nHence the assertion is true but reason is false','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The assertion is false, and the reason is true','NEW','11-075','11-075',NULL),
(32106,'chemistry','Assertion: Two successive ionisation enthalpy of argon are 56.8eV and 36.8eV respectively.                                     \n\nReason: Z eff of Ar is greater than Ar⁺','Assertion and reason are false','Successive ionisation enthalpy after the first ionisation should be greater but in assertion it is give than second ionisation energy is less( that is 36.8<56.8) hence it is false.    Z eff is the effective nuclear charge  it is more when the element is ionised ( Reference :NCERT Chemistry Class 11 Page 84 topic 3.7.1 c ionisation enthalpy para2','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion and reason are true but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-088','11-088',NULL),
(32107,'chemistry','Assertion: Electron affinity of F is greater than Cl.             \n\nReason : Ionisation potential of fluorine is less than chlorine','Assertion and reason are false','Electron affinity of fluorine is less than chlorine because of its small size.Ionisation potential of fluorine is more than chlorine because of its more effective nuclear charge .(Reference :NCERT Chemistry Class 11 Page 85 topic 3.7.1 c ionisation enthalpy Fig 3.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion and reason are true but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(32108,'chemistry','Assertion: Properties of Be is similar to that of Al.       \n\nReason:Both the elements belongs to the same group','Assertion is true but reason is false','Be and Al have similar properties because of the diagonal relationship between them,they don\'t belong to same group (Reference :NCERT Chemistry Class 11 Page 83. topic 3.7.1ionisation enthalpy table 3.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion and reason are true but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-093','11-093',NULL),
(32109,'chemistry','Assertion:Ionisation potential of first element in a period is minimum         \n\nReason: effective nuclear charge of the first element is minimum','Assertion is true but reason is false','Ionisation potential of first element in a period table is minimum because it has only 1 electron in its valence shell after loss of which the element will have stable noble gas configuration (Reference :NCERT Chemistry Class 11 Page 85 topic 3.7.1 c ionisation enthalpy Fig 3.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion and reason are true but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-089','11-089',NULL),
(32110,'chemistry','Assertion: Size of anion is less than the parent atom.       \n\nReason :Z eff of anion is greater than the parent atom','Assertion and reason are false','Size of anion is always more than the parent atom because it\'s Zeff decrease and atomic radius increases(Reference :NCERT Chemistry Class 11 Page 84 topic 3.7.1 B ionic radius para 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion and reason are true but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-088','11-088',NULL),
(32111,'chemistry','Assertion: Cl is the most electronegative element .       Reason: Cl has the highest tendency to lose electrons','Assertion is true but reason is false','Cl is the most electronegative element. Electronegativity is the ability of the element to gain electrons (Reference :NCERT Chemistry Class 11 Page 81 topic 3.6.2 The P Block elements para1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion and reason are true but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-091','11-091',NULL),
(32112,'chemistry','Assertion : Atomic radius increases on descending down the group                                                                               \n\nReason : On moving down the group Zeff increases','Assertion is true but reason is false','Atomic radius increases as we move down the group because Zeff decreases on moving down as the number of shells increases and also because of the screening effect (Reference :NCERT Chemistry Class 11 Page 83 topic 3.7.1 atomic radius para 1','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion and reason are true but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-086','11-086',NULL),
(32113,'chemistry','Assertion: Atomic size boron is larger than beryllium.         \n\nReason:No of shells is more in boron than beryllium','Assertion is false reason is true','Atomic size of boron is less than beryllium as we move along the period and both don\'t contain same number of shell(Reference :NCERT Chemistry Class 11 Page 83 topic 3.7.1 atomic radius para 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion and reason are true but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false reason is true','NEW','11-086','11-086',NULL),
(213406,'chemistry','Cl is about 2-5 times _______ than air.','heavier','Chlorine is about 2-5 times heavier than air.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 203 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Chlorine',NULL,'1','mcq','denser','heavier','lighter','combustible','NEW','12-203',NULL,NULL),
(32114,'chemistry','Assertion:First Ionisation energy of nitrogen is lower than oxygen.                                                                            \n\nReason: Across the period  effective nuclear charge decrease','Assertion and reason are false','Both assertion and reason are false because first ionisation energy of nitrogen is more than oxygen because of the half filled 2p³ configuration.Across the period effective nuclear charge increases as electrons are added to the same valence shell while number of protons keeps increasing inside the nucleus (Reference :NCERT Chemistry Class 11 Page 85 topic 3.7.1 c ionisation enthalpy Fig 3.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion and reason are true but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-089','11-089',NULL),
(32115,'chemistry','Assertion: Size of Ca⁺² is larger than K⁺.                          \n\nReason: Number of electrons in Ca⁺² is more than K⁺','Assertion and reason are false','Size of Ca⁺² is smaller than K⁺ because of more effective nuclear charge of calcium .Both of them contain same number of electrons (isoelectronic species)(Reference :NCERT Chemistry Class 11 Page 84 topic 3.7.1 B ionic radius para 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion and reason are true but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-087','11-087',NULL),
(32116,'chemistry','Assertion: chemical behaviour of the first member of a group of elements in the s and p block are different compared to their subsequent members.                                               \n\nReason: The anomalous behaviour is attributed to their small size, large charge/radius ratio and high electronegativity of elements','Assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion','chemical behaviour of the first member of a group of elements in the s and p block are different compared to their subsequent members. The anomalous behaviour is attributed to their small size, large charge/radius ratio and high electronegativity of elements ( Reference :NCERT Chemistry Class 11 Page 90 topic 3.7.2 B anomalous properties of second period elements para 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion and reason are true but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-093','11-093',NULL),
(32117,'chemistry','Assertion:electronegativity is not a measurable quantity .         \n\nReason: it is the ability of atom in a chemical compound to attract shared pair of electrons','Assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Electronegativity is not a measurable quantity because it is the ability of atom in a chemical compound to attract shared pair of electrons (Reference :NCERT Chemistry Class 11 Page 87 topic 3.7.1 e electronegativity  para1 )','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion and reason are true but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(32118,'chemistry','Assertion: Mendeleev periodic table was based on atomic masses.                                                                          \n\nReason: Modern periodic table arranges them in the order of their atomic numbers','Assertion and reason are true but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not correct explanation of assertion .(Reference :NCERT Chemistry Class 11 Page 92 summary para 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion and reason are true but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-078','11-078',NULL),
(32119,'chemistry','Assertion: Atomic number in a period are consecutive.           \n\nReason: atomic number in a group increases in pattern','Assertion and reason are true but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not correct explanation of assertion .The Periodic table is arranged from left to right so atomic number in period are consecutive and in group increases in a pattern( Reference :NCERT Chemistry Class 11 Page 92 summary para 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion and reason are true but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-081','11-081',NULL),
(32120,'chemistry','Assertion: elements of same group have similar chemical properties.                                                                          \n\nReason: elements of same group have similar valence shell electronic configuration','Assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Both Assertion and reason are true reason is correct explanation of assertion .Same distribution of electrons in the outer most shells ensures similar properties like ionisation energy and electron gain enthalpy(Reference :NCERT Chemistry Class 11 Page 92 summary para1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion and reason are true but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-082','11-082',NULL),
(32121,'chemistry','Assertion: hydrogen has unique position in the periodic table.         \n\nReason: hydrogen has 1 electron in the 1s orbital;','Assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Both Assertion and reason are true reason is correct explanation of assertion.Hydrogen is the only element in periodic with only one electron in its valence shell .It has certain properties similar to both alkali metals and halongens .Like alkali metals it has only one electron in its valence shell .Like halogens it has 1 electron less than noble gas configuration (Reference :NCERT Chemistry Class 11 Page 92 summary para 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion and reason are true but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-082','11-082',NULL),
(32122,'chemistry','Assertion:Metals comprise more than 78% of known elements.                                                                   \n\nReason:Non metals which are located in the top of periodic table are less than 20 in number.','Assertion and reason are true but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','Both Assertion and reason are true but reason is not correct explanation of assertion (Reference :NCERT Chemistry Class 11 Page 92 summary para1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion and reason are true but reason is not correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-085','11-085',NULL),
(300127,'chemistry','When drugs bind to enzyme other than active site, that site is called','Allosteric site','Some drugs do not bind to the enzyme’s active site. These bind to a different site of enzyme which is called  allosteric site. This binding of inhibitor at allosteric site changes the shape of the active site in such a way that substrate cannot recognise it.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 449; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Activator site','Allosteric site','Regular site','None of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300128,'chemistry','True about competitive inhibition of enzyme','drug and substrate compete for active site','Drugs compete with the natural substrate for their attachment on the active sites of enzymes. Such drugs are called  competitive inhibitors.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 448; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','mcq','drug and substrate compete for allosteric','drug and enzyme compete for active site','drug and substrate compete for active site','drug and Enzyme compete for allosteric','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300129,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is true when an enzyme is permanently blocked-','The body degrades the enzyme-inhibitor complex and Synthesises a new enzyme.','the enzyme is blocked permanently. The body then degrades the enzyme-inhibitor complex and synthesises the new enzyme.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 450; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','The body degrades the enzyme-inhibitor complex and enzyme is removed from inhibitor and available for reaction','The body degrades the enzyme-inhibitor complex and Synthesises a new enzyme.','Body reuse that Enzyme','Permanently Enzyme remains Blocked and in the body','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300130,'chemistry','Drugs bind to allosteric site:\n1. It does not change the shape of active site.\n2. It changes the shape of active site.\n3. Substrate cannot recognise the Active site','2 & 3','Some drugs do not bind to the enzyme’s active site. These bind to a different site of enzyme which is called  allosteric site. This binding of inhibitor at allosteric site changes the shape of the active site in such a way that substrate cannot recognise it.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 449; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'3','mcq','1 & 3','2 & 3','only 2','only 3','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300131,'chemistry','Actions of Drugs -','All of these','1. Block the binding site of enzyme 2. Inhibit the catalytic activity of enzyme. 3. Prevent the binding of the substrate. (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 448)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','block the binding site of enzyme','inhibit the catalytic activity of enzyme','prevent the binding of the substrate','All of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300132,'chemistry','If a Strong covalent bond is formed between enzyme and drug then.\n1. It can\'t break easily. \n2  It can break easily. \n3. Enzyme Block permanently. \n4. Body reuse that enzyme.\n5. Irreversible and blocked enzyme','1,3 & 5','If the bond formed between an enzyme and an inhibitor is a  strong covalent bond and cannot be broken easily. (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 448; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'3','mcq','1,4 & 2','1 & 2','2,3,4','1,3 & 5','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300133,'chemistry','Some drugs do not Bind to enzyme active site instead bind to different site of enzyme, this site is called','Allosteric site','Some drugs do not bind to the enzyme’s active site. These bind to a different site of enzyme which is called  allosteric site. This binding of inhibitor at allosteric site changes the shape of the active site in such a way that substrate cannot recognise it.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 449; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Activator site','Allosteric site','Regular site','Ionic site','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300134,'chemistry','Which type of forces by the substrate to the active site of enzyme-','All of these','Substrates bind to the active site of the enzyme through a variety of interactions such as ionic bonding, hydrogen bonding, van der Waals interaction or dipole-dipole interaction. (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 448; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','dipole-dipole interaction','van der Waals interaction','hydrogen bonding','All of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
