(300137,'chemistry','Which of the following is not true for drug receptors','receptor proteins is decomposed and destroyed after removal of chemical messenger','Receptors are proteins that are crucial to body’s communication process. Majority of these are embedded in cell membranes. Receptor proteins are embedded in the cell membrane in such a way that their small part possessing active site projects out of the surface of the membrane and opens on the outside region of the cell membrane . In the body, message between two neurons and that between neurons to muscles is communicated through certain chemicals. These chemicals, known as  chemical messengers  are received at the binding sites of receptor proteins. To accommodate a messenger, shape of the receptor  site  changes. This brings about the transfer of message into the cell. Thus, chemical messenger gives message to the cell without entering the cell.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 450; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','receptor proteins are embedded in the cell membrane.','the chemical known as chemical messengers are received at the binding sites of receptors.','the receptors show selectivity for one chemical messenger over other.','receptor proteins is decomposed and destroyed after removal of chemical messenger','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300138,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are incorrect about receptor proteins? \nI. Majority of receptor proteins are embedded in the cell membranes.\nII. The active site of receptor proteins opens on the inside region of the cell. \nIII. Chemical messengers are received at the binding sites of receptor proteins. \nIV. Shape of receptor changes during attachment of messenger.','II only','Receptors are proteins that are crucial to body’s communication process. Majority of these are embedded in cell membranes. Receptor proteins are embedded in the cell membrane in such a way that their small part possessing active site projects out of the surface of the membrane and opens on the outside region of the cell membrane . In the body, message between two neurons and that between neurons to muscles is communicated through certain chemicals. These chemicals, known as  chemical messengers  are received at the binding sites of receptor proteins. To accommodate a messenger, shape of the receptor  site  changes. This brings about the transfer of message into the cell. Thus, chemical messenger gives message to the cell without entering the cell.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 450; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'3','mcq','III AND IV','I AND II','II only','IV only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300139,'chemistry','Receptors are proteins that are crucial to body’s ________ process.','Communication','Receptors are proteins that are crucial to body’s communication process.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 450; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Enzymatic','Catalytic','Communication','Protein Synthesises','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300140,'chemistry','Receptors are embedded in ___________.','Cell membranes','Receptors are proteins that are crucial to body’s communication process. Majority of these are embedded in cell membranes. Receptor proteins are embedded in the cell membrane in such a way that their small part possessing active site projects out of the surface of the membrane and opens on the outside region of the cell membrane .(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 450; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Cytoplasm','Nuclear Membranes','Cell membranes','Nucleoplasm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300141,'chemistry','Active site projects out of the surface of the membrane and opens on the _________ region of the ___________membrane','Outside, Cell membrane','Receptors are proteins that are crucial to body’s communication process. Majority of these are embedded in cell membranes. Receptor proteins are embedded in the cell membrane in such a way that their small part possessing active site projects out of the surface of the membrane and opens on the outside region of the cell membrane .(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 450; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Outside, Nuclear Membranes','Outside, Cell membrane','Inside, Nuclear Membranes','Inside, Cell membranes','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300142,'chemistry','In the body, message between two neurons and that between neurons to muscles is communicated through certain chemicals. These chemicals, known as','Neurotransmitters','In the body, message between two neurons and that between neurons to muscles is communicated through certain chemicals. These chemicals, known as neurotransmitters, the chemical messengers.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 450; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Neurotransmitters','Hormones','Catalyst','Enzymes','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300143,'chemistry','Assertion: To accommodate a messenger, shape of the receptor  site  changes. This brings about the transfer of message into the cell. \nReason: chemical messenger gives message to the cell without entering the cell.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion is True  but the Reason is false','Both the Assertion and Reason are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300144,'chemistry','Correct order of Message transfer into the cell-\n\n(a) Receptor receiving chemical messenger.\n(b) Shape of the receptor changed after attachment of messenger\n(c) Receptor regains structure after removal of chemical messenger','ABC','(a) Receptor receiving chemical messenger (b) Shape of the receptor changed after attachment of messenger (c) Receptor regains structure after removal of chemical messenger.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 450; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'3','mcq','CAB','BAC','ABC','CBA','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300145,'chemistry','Assertion: Receptors show selectivity for one chemical messenger over the other.\nReason: because their binding sites have Similar shape, structure  and amino acid composition.','Assertion is True  but the Reason is false','receptors show selectivity for one chemical messenger over the other because their binding sites have different shape, structure  and amino acid composition.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 450; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion is True  but the Reason is false','Both the Assertion and Reason are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300146,'chemistry','Drugs that bind to the receptor site and inhibit its natural function are called  ___________.','Antagonists','Drugs that bind to the receptor site and inhibit its natural function are called  antagonists. These are useful when blocking of message is required. There are other types of drugs that mimic the natural messenger by switching  on  the receptor, these are called  agonists. These are useful when there is lack of natural chemical messenger.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 451; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Agonist','Antagonists','Analgesics','Antipyretic','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300147,'chemistry','Drugs that mimic the natural messenger by switching  on  the receptor, these are called __________.','Agonists','Drugs that mimic the natural messenger by switching  on  the receptor, these are called  agonists.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 451; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','fillup','Ant,Ago,ni,sts',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300148,'chemistry','Which type of drug useful when there is lack of natural chemical messenger.','Agonists','Drugs that mimic the natural messenger by switching  on  the receptor, these are called  agonists.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 451; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Agonists','Antagonists','Analgesics','Antipyretic','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300149,'chemistry','Which type of drug useful when blocking of message is required.','Antagonists','Drugs that bind to the receptor site and inhibit its natural function are called antagonists. These are useful when blocking of message is required. (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 451; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Agonist','Antagonists','Analgesics','Antipyretic','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300150,'chemistry','Drugs that bind to the receptor site and inhibits its natural functions are called (A). whereas the drugs that mimic the natural messenger by switching on the receptor are called (B).','antagonist and agonist','Drugs that mimic the natural messenger by switching  on  the receptor, these are called  agonists.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 451; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','antagonist and mimic drugs','agonist and mimic drugs','antagonist and agonist','agonist and antagonists','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
