(300714,'chemistry','Chloropicrin is obtained by the reaction of','nitric acid on chloroform.','When chloroform is treated with concentrated nitric acid, its hydrogen is replaced by nitro group.\nCHCl3 + HONO2 → CNO2Cl3 ( Chloropicrin)+ H2O\n                              (Reference NNET 2004)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Previous Year question',NULL,'1','mcq','steam on carbon tetrachloride','nitric acid on chlorobenzene','chlorine on picric acid','nitric acid on chloroform.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300715,'chemistry','Aspirin is an acetylation product of','o-hydroxybenzoic acid','Aspirin is acetyl salicylic acid, which is formed by acetylation of o-hydroxybenzoic acid.\n(Reference NCERT class 12 oage 445 paragraph 2 NEET 1998)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Previous Year question',NULL,'2','mcq','m-hydroxybenzoic acid','o-dihydroxybenzene','o-hydroxybenzoic acid','p-dihydroxybenzene.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300716,'chemistry','Which of the following can possibly be used as analgesic without causing addiction and mood modification?','N-Acetyl-para-aminophenol.','N-Acetyl-para-aminophenol (or paracetamol) is an antipyretic which can also be used as an analgesic to relieve pain without addition and mood modification.\n(Reference NCERT class 12 oage 445 paragraph 2 NEET 1997)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Previous Year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Diazepam','Tetrahydrocatinol','Morphine','N-Acetyl-para-aminophenol.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300717,'chemistry','Which one of the following statements is not true?','Ampicillin is a natural antibiotic.','Ampicillin is a modification of penicillin and thus is a synthetic antibiotic.\n(Reference NCERT class 12 oage 446 paragraph 2 NEET 1994)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Previous Year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Ampicillin is a natural antibiotic.','Aspirin is both analgesic and antipyretic.','Sulphadiazine is a synthetic antibacterial drug.','Some disinfectants can be used as antiseptics.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300718,'chemistry','Diazo coupling is useful to prepare some','dyes','Azo dyes are derived by coupling of a phenol adsorbed on the surface of a fabric with a diazonium salt. Dyes can be prepared by diazo coupling.\n(Reference NCERT class 12 oage 446 paragraph last NEET 1994)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Previous Year question',NULL,'1','mcq','pesticides','dyes','proteins','vitamins.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300719,'chemistry','Which of the following is a cationic detergent?','Cetyltrimethyl ammonium bromide','Cetyltrimethyl ammonium bromide is a cationic detergent.\n(Reference NCERT class 12 page 452 paragraph 2 NEET 2020)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Previous Year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Sodium lauryl sulphate','Sodium stearate','Cetyltrimethyl ammonium bromide','Sodium dodecylbenzene sulphonate','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300720,'chemistry','Given below are two statements:\n\nStatement I :\nAspirin and Paracetamol belong to the class of narcotic analgesics.\n\nStatement II :\nMorphine and Heroin are non-narcotic analgesics. \n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.','Both Statement I and Statement II are false.','Aspirin and paracetamol belongs to the class of non-narcotic analgesic.\n\nMorphine and heroin are narcotic analgesics.[NEET 2021]','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Previous Year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false.','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300810,'chemistry','Why are receptors proteins crucial to body communication process?\n \n a)they accept and transfer messages from the outside to inside the cell\n \n b)they accept and transfer messages from the inside to the outside the cell\n \n c)They transfer messages by binding with chemical messengers\n \n d)They are enabling chemical messengers to enter the cell.','a,c','Receptor proteins are placed in the cell membrane and they transfer messages when bind with chemical messengers,without them entering the cell\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 442 paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,d','b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300811,'chemistry','Which statement is true \n \n a)Receptor show selectivity for one chemical messanger\n \n b)all receptors enable all chemical messengers to sand a message to the cell\n \n c) Receptors are selective to chemical messangers based on shape , structure and aminoacids composition of their binding sites\n \n d)Receptors are selective to chemical messangers based on shape , structure and aminoacids composition of chemical messengers','a,c','Receptors show selectivity for one chemical messanger because their binding sites have different shape,structure and aminoacids composition .\n \n Reference NCERT class 12 page 442 paragraph 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,d','b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300812,'chemistry','After receptor received chemical messenger:\n \n a)shape of the receptor changed\n \n b)chemical messenger is travelling through the receptor\n \n c)receptor regains structure after the removal of chemical messenger\n \n d)Chemical messenger enters the cell after it exits the receptor','a,c','Transfer of the message:\n \n 1)Receptor receiving chemical messenger \n \n 2) Shape of the receptor changed after the attachmant\n \n 3)Receptor regain structure after the removal of chemical messenger\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 442 paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,d','b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300813,'chemistry','Antagonists:\n \n a)drugs that bind to the receptor site and catalyse its natural function\n \n b)drugs that bind to the receptor site and inhibit its natural function\n \n c)are blocking of message\n \n d)drugs used when the message can\'t be transfered itself','a,c','Antagonists are drugs that bind to the receptor site and inhibit its natural function(We use it to block the message)\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 443 paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,d','b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300814,'chemistry','When there is lack of natural chemical messages:\n \n a)We use drugs that mimic the natural messanger by switching on the receptor\n \n b)we use drug that catalyse the formation of needed chemical messanger\n \n c)we use agonists\n \n d)we use antagonists','a,c','Agonists are drugs that mimic the natural messanger by switching on the receptor and we are using them when there is lack of natural chemical messangers\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 443 paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,d','b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300815,'chemistry','In which way are chemical messenger and active site of receptor bonding?\n \n a)Active site changes its chape when messenger is bind\n \n b)strong covalent bond\n \n c)Shape of receptors active site adapt to chemical messenger\n \n d)they don\'t bond they come close enough to transfer the message','a,c','Transfer of the message:\n \n 1)Receptor receiving chemical messenger \n \n 2) Shape of the receptor changed after the attachmant\n \n 3)Receptor regain structure after the removal of chemical messenger\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 442 paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,d','b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300816,'chemistry','What chemicals were first used in treatment of acidity in stomach:\n \n a)sodium hydrogen carbonate\n \n b)histamine\n \n c)Al and Mg hydroxide\n \n d)Cimetidine','a,c','Until 1970 treatment of acidy in stomach was administration of antiacids (sodiumhydrogencarbonate , mixture of Al and Mg hydroxide)\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 443 paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,d','b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300817,'chemistry','What was the flaw of hydrogen carbonate in treatment of acidy in stomach?\n \n a)can make the stomach alkaline\n \n b)they killed good bacteria in stomach\n \n c)they trigger the production of more acids\n \n d)it changes pH above neutrality.','a,c','Hydrogencarbonate can make the stomach alkaline and trigger the production of even more acids.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 443 paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,d','b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300818,'chemistry','In which way metal hydroxide increase the pH above neutrality in stomach acidity treatment?\n \n a)It solubilize and reacts with hydrochloric acid to form aluminium chloride and water\n \n b)it triggers the production of natural antiacids in the stomach\n \n c)inhibits the action of pepsin \n \n d)they make stomach more alkaline','a,c','Aluminum hydroxide is a basic inorganic salt that acts by neutralizing hydrochloric acid in gastric secretions. Aluminum hydroxide is slowly solubilized in the stomach and reacts with hydrochloric acid to form aluminum chloride and water. It also inhibits the action of pepsin by increasing the pH and via adsorption.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 442 paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,d','b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300819,'chemistry','Which type of reaction is the reaction between aluminium hydroxide and hydrochloric acid in the stomach?\n \n a)neutralisation\n \n b)alkali-acid reaction\n \n c)acid-base reaction\n \n d)acid - alcohol reaction','a,c','Aluminium hydroxide is a base and hydrochloric acid is , naturaly,acid so it\'s acid-base neutralisation.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 443 paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,d','b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300820,'chemistry','Combination of which chemicals are included in the reaction that have positive impact in treatment of acidity of stomach?\n \n a)histamine\n \n b)cimetidine\n \n c)hydrochloric acid\n \n d)sodiumhydrogencarbonate','a,b','Cimeditine(Tegamet) prevent the interaction of histamine with the receptor present in the stomach wall.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 443 paragraph 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,d','a,b','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300821,'chemistry','Which statement is true:\n \n a)Tranquilizers relieve anxiety, stress, irritability or excitement by inducing a sense of well-being.\n \n b) Iproniazid is derivative of barbituric acid.\n \n c)chlordiazepoxide and meprobamate, are relatively mild tranquilizers suitable for relieving tension.','a,c','Tranquilizers are a class of chemical compounds used for the treatment of stress, and mild or even severe mental diseases. These relieve anxiety, stress, irritability or excitement by inducing a sense of well-being.\n \n Derivatives of barbituric acid viz., veronal, amytal, nembutal, luminal and seconal constitute an important class of tranquilizers. These derivatives are called barbiturates.\n \n Some tranquilizers namely, chlordiazepoxide and meprobamate, are relatively mild tranquilizers suitable for relieving tension.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 444 paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300822,'chemistry','Which statement is false \n \n a)Analgesics reduce or abolish pain although they may cause impairment of consciousness, mental confusion, incoordination or paralysis or some other disturbances of nervous system.\n \n b)Aspirin and paracetamol belong to the class of narcotic analgesics.\n \n c)Morphine produce sleep.','a,b','Analgesics reduce or abolish pain without causing impairment of consciousness, mental confusion, incoordination or paralysis or some other disturbances of nervous system.\n \n Aspirin and paracetamol belong to the class of non-narcotic analgesics. Aspirin is the most familiar example. Aspirin inhibits the synthesis of chemicals known as prostaglandins which stimulate inflammation in the tissue and cause pain.\n \n Morphine and many of its homologues, when administered in medicinal doses, relieve pain and produce sleep.\n \n (Reference NCERT Class 12 page 445 paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300823,'chemistry','Which statement is true \n \n a)Tranquilizers and analgesics affect the message transfer mechanism from nerve to receptor.\n \n b)If the level of noradrenaline is low for some reason,antidepressant drugs are required.\n \n c)Equanil is used in controlling depression and hypertension.','all are correct','Tranquilizers and analgesics are neurologically active drugs. These affect the message transfer mechanism from nerve to receptor.\n \n If the level of noradrenaline is low for some reason, then the signal-sending activity becomes low, and the person suffers from depression. In such situations, antidepressant drugs are required.\n \n Some tranquilizers namely, chlordiazepoxide and meprobamate, are relatively mild tranquilizers suitable for relieving tension. Equanil is used in controlling depression and hypertension.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 444 paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300824,'chemistry','Which statement is correct?\n \n a)Aspirin inhibits the synthesis of prostaglandins which stimulate inflammation in the tissue and cause pain.\n \n b)Opiates are obtained from the opium poppy.\n \n c)Morphine narcotics, n poisonous doses, these produce stupor, coma, convulsions and ultimately death.','All are correct','Aspirin inhibits the synthesis of chemicals known as prostaglandins which stimulate inflammation in the tissue and cause pain. These drugs are effective in relieving skeletal pain such as that due to arthritis.\n \n Morphine narcotics are sometimes referred to as opiates, since they are obtained from the opium poppy. \n \n These analgesics are chiefly used for the relief of postoperative pain, cardiac pain and pains of terminal cancer, and in child birth.\n \n Morphine and many of its homologues, when administered in medicinal doses, relieve pain and produce sleep. In poisonous doses, these produce stupor, coma, convulsions and ultimately death.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 445 paragraph last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300825,'chemistry','Which statement is true\n \n\n a) Heroine is morphine narcotic\n \n b)An antimicrobial tends only to destroy microbes such as bacteria (antibacterial drugs), fungi (antifungal agents), virus (antiviral agents).\n \n \n c)Antibiotics are used as drugs to treat infections because of their low toxicity for humans and animals.','a,c','An antimicrobial tends to destroy/prevent development or inhibit the pathogenic action of microbes such as bacteria (antibacterial drugs), fungi (antifungal agents), virus (antiviral agents), or other parasites (antiparasitic drugs) selectively.\n \n Antibiotics are used as drugs to treat infections because of their low toxicity for humans and animals.\n \n Heroine is a morphine drug.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 446 paragraph 1.2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300826,'chemistry','Which statement is false\n \n \n a)antibiotics were classified as chemical substances produced by microorganisms.\n \n \n b)An antibiotic now refers to a substance produced wholly or partly by chemical synthesis, which very high concentrations inhibits the growth or destroys microorganisms.\n \n c)arsphenamine is most effective treatment discovered for syphilis.','b,c','Antibiotics are used as drugs to treat infections because of their low toxicity for humans and animals. Initially antibiotics were classified as chemical substances produced by microorganisms (bacteria, fungi and molds) that inhibit the growth or even destroy microorganisms.\n \n \n \n An antibiotic now refers to a substance produced wholly or partly by chemical synthesis, which in low concentrations inhibits the growth or destroys microorganisms by intervening in their metabolic processes.\n \n \n \n Arsphenamine, known as salvarsan.was the first effective treatment discovered or syphilis. Although salvarsan is toxic to human beings, its effect on the bacteria, spirochete.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 446 paragraph last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300827,'chemistry','Which statement is correct:\n \n a)prontosil resembles in structure to the salvarsan.\n \n b)Antibiotics have only cidal (killing) effect .\n \n c)The range of bacteria or other microorganisms that are affected by a certain antibiotic is expressed as its spectrum of action.','a,c','Ehrlich succeeded in preparing the first effective antibacterial agent, prontosil, which resembles in structure to the compound, salvarsan.\n \n Antibiotics have either cidal (killing) effect or a static (inhibitory) effect on microbes.\n \n The range of bacteria or other microorganisms that are affected by a certain antibiotic is expressed as its spectrum of action.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 447 paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300828,'chemistry','Which statement is correct?\n \n a)Antibiotics which kill or inhibit a wide range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria are said to be narrow spectrum antibiotics.\n \n b)Penicillin G has a narrow spectrum.\n \n c) Chloramphenicol is a broad spectrum antibiotic.','b,c','Antibiotics which kill or inhibit a wide range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria are said to be broad spectrum antibiotics.\n \n Penicillin G has a narrow spectrum.\n \n Chloramphenicol, isolated in 1947, is a broad spectrum antibiotic. It is rapidly absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and hence can be given orally in case of typhoid, dysentery, acute fever...\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 447 paragraph last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300829,'chemistry','Which statements are not correct\n \n a)Ampicillin and Amoxycillin are synthetic modifications of penicillins.\n \n b)Those effective mainly against Gram-positive or Gram-negative bacteria are broad spectrum antibiotics.\n \n c)The antibiotic Vancomycin is supposed to be toxic towards certain strains of cancer cells.','b,c','Ampicillin and Amoxycillin are synthetic modifications of penicillins. These have broad spectrum.\n \n The antibiotic dysidazirine is supposed to be toxic towards certain strains of cancer cells.\n \n Those effective mainly against \n \n Gram-positive or Gram-negative bacteria are narrow spectrumantibiotics.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 447 paragraph last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300830,'chemistry','Which statement is true\n \n a)Boric acid in dilute aqueous solution is weak antiseptic for eyes.\n \n b)Chlorine in the concentration of 2 to 4 ppm is disinfectant.\n \n c)Antifertility drugs are used in controlling problems caused by population increase','a,c','The increased population has caused many social problems in terms of food resources, environmental issues, employment, etc. To control these problems, population is required to be controlled. This has lead to the concept of family planning. Antifertility drugs are of use in this direction.\n \n Chlorine in the concentration of 0.2 to 0.4 ppm in aqueous solution and sulphur dioxide in very low concentrations, are disinfectants.\n \n Boric acid in dilute aqueous solution is weak antiseptic for eyes.','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300831,'chemistry','Which statement is correct\n \n \n \n a)One percent solution of phenol is an antiseptic\n \n b)Bithionol (the compound is also called bithional) is added to soaps to impart antiseptic properties.\n \n c)Progesterone suppresses ovulation.','b,c','It is known that progesterone suppresses ovulation. Synthetic progesterone derivatives are more potent than progesterone.\n \n Bithionol (the compound is also called bithional) is added to soaps to impart antiseptic properties.\n \n 0.2 per cent solution of phenol is an antiseptic while its one percent solution is disinfectant.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 4478 paragraph 1,3)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300832,'chemistry','Which statement is correct :\n \n \n \n a)Dettol is a mixture of chloroxylenol and terpineol.\n \n b)Disinfectants are applied to inanimate objects such as floors, drainage system, instruments.\n \n c)The estrogen derivative which is used in combination with progesterone derivative is Norethindrone','a,b','Norethindrone is an example of synthetic progesterone derivative most widely used as antifertility drug. The estrogen derivative which is used in combination with progesterone derivative is ethynylestradiol (novestrol).\n \n Disinfectants are applied to inanimate objects such as floors, drainage system, instruments, etc. Same substances can act as an antiseptic as well as disinfectant by varying the concentration.\n \n Commonly used antiseptic, dettol is a mixture of chloroxylenol and terpineol.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 448 paragraph first and last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300833,'chemistry','Which statement is not correct\n \n a) Terpineol has two double bonds in its structure\n \n b)20-30 per cent solution of iodine in alcohol- water mixture is known a tincture of iodine.\n \n c)Synthetic progesterone derivatives are more potent than progesterone.','a,b','Terpineol has only one double bond in its structure.\n \n Iodine is a powerful antiseptic. Its 2-3 per cent solution in alcohol- water mixture is known as tincture of iodine.\n \n It is known that progesterone suppresses ovulation. Synthetic progesterone derivatives are more potent than progesterone. Norethindrone is an example of synthetic progesterone derivative\n \n (Reference: NCERT class 12 page 448 paragraph 1,last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300834,'chemistry','Which statement is correct\n \n a) Only nutritive suplements above all chemicals in food categories have nutritive value.\n \n b) Artificial sweeteners add to calorie intake.\n \n c)Aspartame is roughly 100 times as sweet as cane sugar.','a,c','Main categories of food additives are as follows: \n \n (i) Food colours \n \n (ii) Flavours and sweeteners \n \n (iii) Fat emulsifiers and stabilising agents \n \n (iv) Flour improvers - antistaling agents and bleaches \n \n (v) Antioxidants \n \n (vi) Preservatives \n \n (vii) Nutritional supplements such as minerals, vitamins and amino acids.\n \n Except for chemicals of category (vii), none of the above additives \n \n have nutritive value.\n \n Natural sweeteners, e.g., sucrose add to calorie intake and therefore many people prefer to use artificial sweeteners.\n \n Aspartame is the most successful and widely used artificial sweetener. It is roughly 100 times as sweet as cane sugar.\n \n \n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 449 paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300835,'chemistry','Which statement is not correct\n \n a)Saccharin is about 200 times as sweet as cane sugar.\n \n b)Alitame is the strongest artificial sweetener.\n \n c)Sucralose has two atoms of chlorine in its structure.','a,c','Saccharin, is the first popular artificial sweetening agent. It has been used as a sweetening agent ever since it was discovered in 1879. It is about 550 times as sweet as cane sugar.\n \n Alitame is high potency sweetener, although it is more stable than aspartame, the control of sweetness of food is difficult while using it. It is about 2000 times as sweet as cane sugar.\n \n Sucralose is trichloro derivative of sucrose.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 450 paraph 1,2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(213408,'chemistry','Chlorine reacts with hydrocarbons and gives substitution products with ________ hydrocarbons and addition products with _______ hydrocarbons.','saturated, unsaturated','Chlorine reacts with hydrocarbons and gives substitution products with saturated hydrocarbons and addition products with unsaturated hydrocarbons.\nCH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl (Conditions- UV light)\nC₂H₄ + Cl₂ → C₂H₄Cl₂ (Conditions- Room temperature)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 203 Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Chlorine',NULL,'2','mcq','saturated, unsaturated','unsaturated, saturated','saturated, saturated','unsaturated, unsaturated','NEW','12-203',NULL,NULL),
(300836,'chemistry','Which statement is true \n \n a)Aspartame is the weakest artificial sweetener.\n \n b)The most commonly used preservatives include is sodium benzoate,\n \n c)Soaps containing sodium salts are .formed by heating fat','All are correct','Aspartame is the most successful and widely used artificial sweetener. It is roughly 100 times as sweet as cane sugar.\n \n The most commonly used preservatives include table salt, sugar, vegetable oils and sodium benzoate, C6H5COONa.\n \n Soaps containing sodium salts are formed by heating fat (i.e., glyceryl ester of fatty acid) with aqueous sodium hydroxide solution.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 450 paragraph 1,3,last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300837,'chemistry','Which statement is correct.\n \n a)Soaps used for cleaning purpose are sodium or potassium salts of long chain fatty acids.\n \n b)Transparent soaps are made by dissolving the soap in ethanol and then evaporating the excess solvent.\n \n c)Laundry soaps contain fillers like sodium rosinate, sodium silicate, borax and sodium carbonate.','All are correct','Soaps used for cleaning purpose are sodium or potassium salts of long chain fatty acids. \n \n Transparent soaps are made by dissolving the soap in ethanol and then evaporating the excess solvent. \n \n Laundry soaps contain fillers like sodium rosinate, sodium silicate, borax and sodium carbonate.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 451 1.2.3)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300838,'chemistry','Which statement is not correct\n \n a)insoluble soaps are used as cleansing agent.\n \n b)Synthetic detergents are cleansing agents that contains soap.\n \n c)Soap chips are made by running a thin sheet of melted soap onto a cool cylinder and scraping off the soaps in small broken pieces.','a,b','Insoluble soaps separate as scum in water and are useless as cleansing agent. In fact these are hinderance to good washing, because the precipitate adheres onto the fibre of the cloth as gummy mass.\n \n Synthetic detergents are cleansing agents which have all the properties of soaps, but which actually do not contain any soap.\n \n Soap chips are made by running a thin sheet of melted soap onto a cool cylinder and scraping off the soaps in small broken pieces.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 451 paragraph 4,6)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300839,'chemistry','Which statement is correct \n \n a)Alkyl hydrogensulphates formed by treating long chain alcohols with concentrated sulphuric acid are neutralised with alkali to form anionic detergents.\n \n b)Cetyltrimethylammonium bromine is used as hair conditioners.\n \n c)Liquid dishwashing detergents are ionic type detergent.','a,b','Cetyltrimethylammoniumbromide is a popular cationic detergent and is used in hair conditioners.\n \n Liquid dishwashing detergents are non-ionic type of detergent.\n \n Anionic detergents are sodium salts of sulphonated long chain alcohols or hydrocarbons. Alkyl hydrogensulphates formed by treating long chain alcohols with concentrated sulphuric acid are neutralised with alkali to form anionic detergents.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 452 1,3,last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300840,'chemistry','Which statement is correct :\n \n a)Cationic detergents have germicidal properties\n \n b)Anionic detergents are also used in toothpastes.\n \n c)Cationic detergents are quaternary ammonium salts of amines with acetates, chlorides or bromides as anions.','all are correct','Cationic detergents are quarternary ammonium salts of amines with acetates, chlorides or bromides as anions. Cationic part possess a long hydrocarbon chain and a positive charge on nitrogen atom. Hence, these are called cationic detergents.\n \n In anionic detergents, the anionic part of the molecule is involved in the cleansing action. Sodium salts of alkylbenzenesulphonates are an important class of anionic detergents. They are mostly used for household work. Anionic detergents are also used in toothpastes.\n \n Cationic detergents have germicidal properties and are expensive, therefore, these are of limited use.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 452 paragraph 1,3,last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300841,'chemistry','Which statement is correct \n \n a)None ionic detergent is formed when stearic acid reacts with polyethyleneglycol.\n \n b)Main problem that appears in the use of detergents is slow degradation.\n \n c)In anionic detergents, the anionic part of the molecule is involved in the cleansing action.','All are correct','In anionic detergents, the anionic part of the molecule is involved in the cleansing action. Sodium salts of alkylbenzenesulphonates are an important class of anionic detergents.\n \n Main problem that appears in the use of detergents is that if their hydrocarbon chain is highly branched, then bacteria cannot degrade this easily. Slow degradation of detergents leads to their accumulation. Effluents containing such detergents reach the rivers, ponds, etc.\n \n In anionic detergents, the anionic part of the molecule is involved in the cleansing action. Sodium salts of alkylbenzenesulphonates are an important class of anionic detergents.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 452 paragraph 2,last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300842,'chemistry','Which statement is correct?\n \n \n \n a)antiseptics killing or arresting the growth of microorganisms is an example of classification based on pharmacological effect.\n \n b)histamine causing the inflammation of the body is example of classification on the basis on drug action\n \n c)analgesics having pain killing effect,is an example of classification based on drug action','a,b','This classification is based on pharmacological effect of the drugs. It is useful for doctors because it provides them the whole range of drugs available for the treatment of a particular type of problem. For example, analgesics have pain killing effect, antiseptics kill or arrest the growth of microorganisms.\n \n It is based on the action of a drug on a particular biochemical process. For example, all antihistamines inhibit the action of the compound, histamine which causes inflammation in the body.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 440 paragraph 1,2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(213409,'chemistry','Chlorine is a ___________ bleaching agent.','powerful','Chlorine water on standing loses its yellow colour due to the formation of HCl and HOCl. Hypochlorous acid (HOCl) so formed, gives nascent oxygen which is responsible for oxidising and bleaching properties of chlorine. Chlorine is a powerful bleaching agent; bleaching action is due to oxidation.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 203 Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Chlorine',NULL,'1','mcq','weak','mild','moderately weak','powerful','NEW','12-203',NULL,NULL),
(213410,'chemistry','What is the chemical formula of phosgene?','COCl₂','Chemical formula of phosgene is COCl₂\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 204 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Chlorine',NULL,'3','mcq','COI₂','COCl₂','COBr₂','COCl','NEW','12-204',NULL,NULL),
(300843,'chemistry','Which statement is correct?\n \n a)Enzymes are example, proteins which perform the role of biological catalysts in the body.\n \n b)The first function of an enzyme is to hold the substrate for a chemical reaction.\n \n c)The second function of an enzyme is to provide functional groups that will attack the substrate and carry out chemical reaction.','all are correct','In their catalytic activity, enzymes perform two major functions:\n \n The first function of an enzyme is to hold the substrate for a chemical reaction. Active sites of enzymes hold the substrate molecule in a suitable position, so that it can be attacked by the reagent effectively.Substrates bind to the active site of the enzyme through a variety of interactions such as ionic bonding, hydrogen bonding, van der Waals interaction or dipole-dipole interaction \n \n The second function of an enzyme is to provide functional groups that will attack the substrate and carry out chemical reaction.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 440 paragraph last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300844,'chemistry','Which statements are correct?\n \n a)Enzymes and receptors are proteins.\n \n b)Lipids are structural parts of the cell membrane, but carbohydrates are not.\n \n c)Substrates bind to the active site of the enzyme.','a,c','Proteins which perform the role of biological catalysts in the body are called enzymes, those which are crucial to communication system in the body are called receptors.Lipids and carbohydrates are structural parts of the cell membrane.\n \n Substrates bind to the active site of the enzyme through a variety of interactions such as ionic bonding, hydrogen bonding, van der Waals interaction or dipole-dipole interaction\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 440 paragraph last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300845,'chemistry','Which statements is correct?\n \n a)Drugs inhibit any activities of enzymes.\n \n b)Drugs that compete with the natural substrate for their attachment on the active sites of enzymes are called competitive inhibitors.\n \n c)Allosteric site is not a site on the enzyme','a,b','Drugs inhibit any of the above mentioned activities of enzymes. These can block the binding site of the enzyme and prevent the binding of substrate, or can inhibit the catalytic activity of the enzyme. Such drugs are called enzyme inhibitors.\n \n Drugs compete with the natural substrate for their attachment on the active sites of enzymes. Such drugs are called competitive inhibitors.\n \n Some drugs do not bind to the enzyme’s active site. These bind to a different site of enzyme which is called allosteric site.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 441 paragraph last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300846,'chemistry','Which statement is correct?\n \n a)Allosteric site changes the shape of the active site in such a way that substrate can recognise it.\n \n b)Receptors are embedded in cell membranes.\n \n c)Message between two neurons and that between neurons to muscles is communicated through chemical messengers.','b,c','Receptors are proteins that are crucial to body’s communication process. Majority of these are embedded in cell membranes.\n \n In the body, message between two neurons and that between neurons to muscles is communicated through certain chemicals. These chemicals, known as chemical messengers are received at the binding sites of receptor proteins.\n \n Some drugs do not bind to the enzyme’s active site. These bind to a different site of enzyme which is called allosteric site. This binding of inhibitor at allosteric site (Fig.16.3) changes the shape of the active site in such a way that substrate can- not recognise it.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 441 paragraph last, page 442 paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300847,'chemistry','Which statement is correct?\n \n a)Receptors small part possessing active site projects out of the surface of the membrane and opens on the inside region of the cell membrane.\n \n b)Chemical messengers are received at the binding sites of receptor proteins.\n \n c)Antagonists are useful when blocking of message is required.','b,c','Drugs that bind to the receptor site and inhibit its natural function are called antagonists. These are useful when blocking of message is required.\n \n In the body, message between two neurons and that between neurons to muscles is communicated through certain chemicals. These chemicals, known as chemical messengers are received at the binding sites of receptor proteins.\n \n Receptor proteins are embedded in the cell membrane in such a way that their small part possessing active site projects out of the surface of the membrane and opens on the outside region of the cell membrane\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 442 paragraph 2 page 443 paragraph 1','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300848,'chemistry','Which statement is correct?\n \n a)Excessive hydrogencarbonate can make the stomach alkaline\n \n b)Histamine stimulates the secretion of pepsin\n \n c)Histamine stimulates the secretion of hydrochloric acid','all are correct','Until 1970, only treatment for acidity was administration of antacids, such as sodium hydrogencarbonate or a mixture of aluminium and magnesium hydroxide. However, excessive hydrogencarbonate can make the stomach alkaline and trigger the production of even more acid.\n \n A major breakthrough in the treatment of hyperacidity came through the discovery according to which a chemical, histamine, stimulates the secretion of pepsin and hydrochloric acid in the stomach.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 443 paragraph 2,3)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300849,'chemistry','Which statement is correct?\n \n a)Cimetidine (Tegamet) was designed to inhibit the interaction of histamine with the receptors present in the stomach wall.\n \n b)Brompheniramine (Dimetapp) acts like antihistamine.\n \n c)Histamine is a potent vasodilator.','b,c','Histamine is a potent vasodilator. It has various functions. It contracts the smooth muscles in the bronchi and gut and relaxes other muscles, such as those in the walls of fine blood vessels.\n \n Synthetic drugs, brompheniramine (Dimetapp) and terfenadine (Seldane), act as antihistamines.\n \n Cimetidine (Tegamet) was designed to prevent the interaction of histamine with the receptors present in the stomach wall.\n \n (Reference : NCERT class 12 page 443 paragraph last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(213411,'chemistry','What is the chemical formula of tear gas?','CCl₃NO₂','Chemical formula of tear gas CCl₃NO₂\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 204 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Chlorine',NULL,'1','mcq','CClNO₂','CCl₃NO₂','CCl₃NO','Cl₃NO₂','NEW','12-204',NULL,NULL),
(213412,'chemistry','What is the chemical formula of mustard gas?','C₄H₈SCl₂','Chemical formula of mustard gas C₄H₈SCl₂\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 204 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Chlorine',NULL,'1','mcq','C₄H₈Cl₂','C₄H₈SCl₂','C₄H₈SCl','C₄H₈S','NEW','12-204',NULL,NULL),
(300850,'chemistry','Which statement is correct ?\n \n a)Brompheniramine (Dimetapp) and terfenadine (Seldane) are competitive inhibitors\n \n b)Tranquilizers affect the message transfer mechanism from nerve to receptor.\n \n c)Noradrenaline is one of the neurotransmitters that plays a role in mood changes.','all are correct','Synthetic drugs, brompheniramine (Dimetapp) and terfenadine (Seldane), act as antihistamines. They interfere with the natural action of histamine by competing with histamine for binding sites of receptor where histamine exerts its effect.\n \n Tranquilizers and analgesics are neurologically active drugs. These affect the message transfer mechanism from nerve to receptor.\n \n There are various types of tranquilizers. They function by different mechanisms. For example, noradrenaline is one of the neurotransmitters that plays a role in mood changes. If the level of noradrenaline is low for some reason, then the signal-sending activity becomes low, and the person suffers from depression.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 444 paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300851,'chemistry','Which statement is correct?\n \n a)Tranquilizers are a class of chemical compounds used for the treatment of stress.\n \n b)If the level of noradrenaline is low for some reason,antidepressant drugs are required.\n \n c)Chlordiazepoxide and meprobamate are antidepressants.','a,b','Tranquilizers are a class of chemical compounds used for the treatment of stress, and mild or even severe mental diseases. These relieve anxiety, stress, irritability or excitement by inducing a sense of well-being.\n \n noradrenaline is one of the neurotransmitters that plays a role in mood changes. If the level of noradrenaline is low for some reason, then the signal-sending activity becomes low, and the person suffers from depression. In such situations, antidepressant drugs are required.\n \n Some tranquilizers namely, chlordiazepoxide and meprobamate, are relatively mild tranquilizers suitable for relieving tension.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 444 paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300852,'chemistry','Which statement is correct?\n \n a) Equanil is mild tranquilizer suitable for relieving tension.\n \n b)Barbiturates are sleep producing agents.\n \n c)Antidepressant drugs inhibit the enzymes which catalyse the degradation of noradrenaline.','b,c','Equanil is used in controlling depression and hypertension.\n \n barbiturates. Barbiturates are hypnotic, i.e., sleep producing agents. Some other substances used as tranquilizers are valium and serotonin. antidepressant drugs are required. These drugs inhibit the enzymes which catalyse the degradation of noradrenaline. If the enzyme is inhibited, this important neurotransmitter is slowly metabolised and can activate its receptor for longer periods of time.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 444 paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300853,'chemistry','Which statement is true ?\n \n a)Both types of analgesics are addictive.\n \n b) Aspirin inhibits the synthesis prostaglandins.\n \n c)Antipyretics reduce fiver.','b,c','Analgesics are classified as follows: \n \n (i) Non-narcotic (non-addictive) analgesics \n \n (ii) Narcotic drugs\n \n Aspirin inhibits the synthesis of chemicals known as prostaglandins which stimulate inflammation in the tissue and cause pain.\n \n These drugs are effective in relieving skeletal pain such as that due to arthritis. These drugs have many other effects such as reducing fever (antipyretic) and preventing platelet coagulation.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 445 paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300854,'chemistry','Which statement is correct?\n \n a)Prostaglandins stimulate inflammation in the tissue and cause pain.\n \n b)Morphine relieves pain.\n \n c)Because of its anti blood clotting action, aspirin finds use in prevention of heart attacks.','all are correct','Aspirin inhibits the synthesis of chemicals known as prostaglandins which stimulate inflammation in the tissue and cause pain. These drugs are effective in relieving skeletal pain such as that due to arthritis. These drugs have many other effects such as reducing fever (antipyretic) and preventing platelet coagulation. Because of its anti blood clotting action, aspirin finds use in prevention of heart attacks.\n \n Morphine and many of its homologues, when administered in medicinal doses, relieve pain and produce sleep.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 445 paragraph last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300855,'chemistry','Which statement is correct \n \n a)Antibiotics, antiseptics and analgesics are antimicrobial drugs.\n \n b)Initially antibiotics were classified as chemical substances produced by microorganisms.\n \n c)Salvarsan is toxic to humans.','b,c','Antibiotics, antiseptics and disinfectants are antimicrobial drugs.\n \n Initially antibiotics were classified as chemical substances produced by microorganisms (bacteria, fungi and molds) that inhibit the growth or even destroy microorganisms.\n \n Although salvarsan is toxic to human beings, its effect on the bacteria, spirochete, which causes syphilis is much greater than on human beings.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 446 paragraph 1,3)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300856,'chemistry','Which statement is correct?\n \n a) Antibiotics are substance produced only partly by chemical synthesis.\n \n b)Salvarsan has two Arsenic atoms in its structure.\n \n c)There is similarity in structures of salvarsan and azodyes.','b,c','An antibiotic now refers to a substance produced wholly or partly by chemical synthesis, which in low concentrations inhibits the growth or destroys microorganisms by intervening in their metabolic processes.\n \n Ehrlich noted that there is similarity in structures of salvarsan and azodyes. The –As = As– linkage present in arsphenamine resembles the –N = N – linkage present in azodyes in the sense that arsenic atom is present in place of nitrogen.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 446 paragraph last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300857,'chemistry','Which statement is true?\n \n a)The first effective antibacterial agent is prontosil.\n \n b)Antibiotics have only cidal (killing) effect on microbes.\n \n c)There is Sulphur in penicilin','a,c','Antibiotics have either cidal (killing) effect or a static (inhibitory) effect on microbes.\n \n The first effective antibacterial agent, prontosil, which resembles in structure to the compound, salvarsan.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 447 paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','c only','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(71518,'chemistry','At 500 K , the equilibrium constant K꜀ , for the reaction is 5\n\n1/2 H₂(g) + 1/2 I₂(g) ⇌ HI(g) \n\nWhat would be the equilibrium constant K꜀ , for the reaction \n\n2 H₂(g) + 2 I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g)','0.04','If the given equation is multiple by 2, the equilibrium constant for new equation will be squared , and on reversing the reaction value of equilibrium constant is reciprocated.\nThus K = 5² = 25\nFor the required equation = 1/25 = 0.04','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','0.04','0.4','25','2.5','NEW','11-201','11-177',NULL),
(300858,'chemistry','Given bellow are two statements : one is labeled assertion (A) and the other is labeled Reason:\n \n A:Drugs are chemicals of low molecular masses.\n \n R:Drugs are always poisons','A is correct but R is not correct','Drugs are chemicals of low molecular masses (~100 – 500u). These interact with macromolecular targets and produce a biological response.\n \n If taken in doses higher than those recommended, most of the drugs used as medicines are potential poisons.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 439 paragraph last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','both A and R are correct but R isn\'t correct explanation of A','A is correct but R is not correct','R is correct but A isn\'t correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300859,'chemistry','Given bellow are two statements : one is labeled assertion (A) and the other is labeled Reason:\n \n A:Histamine which causes inflammation in the body.\n \n R:We can classify drugs based on its action.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','Classification is based on the action of a drug on a particular biochemical process. For example, all antihistamines inhibit the action of the compound, histamine which causes inflammation in the body. There are various ways in which action of histamines can be blocked.\n \n (Reference NCERT CLASS 12 page 440 paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','both A and R are correct but R isn\'t correct explanation of A','A is correct but R is not correct','R is correct but A isn\'t correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300860,'chemistry','Given bellow are two statements : one is labeled assertion (A) and the other is labeled Reason:\n \n A:Antiseptics kill or arrest the growth of microorganisms.\n \n R:Antiseptics are class of drug. The classification is based on pharmacological effect of the drugs.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','This classification is based on pharmacological effect of the drugs. It is useful for doctors because it provides them the whole range of drugs available for the treatment of a particular type of problem. For example, analgesics have pain killing effect, antiseptics kill or arrest the growth of microorganisms.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 440 paragraph first)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','both A and R are correct but R isn\'t correct explanation of A','A is correct but R is not correct','R is correct but A isn\'t correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300861,'chemistry','Given bellow are two statements : one is labeled assertion (A) and the other is labeled Reason:\n \n A:Enzymes catalyse the reaction.\n \n R:Active sites of enzymes hold the substrate molecule in a suitable position, so that it can be attacked by the reagent effectively.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','In their catalytic activity, enzymes perform two major functions:\n \n The first function of an enzyme is to hold the substrate for a chemical reaction. Active sites of enzymes hold the substrate molecule in a suitable position, so that it can be attacked by the reagent effectively.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 440 paragraph last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','both A and R are correct but R isn\'t correct explanation of A','A is correct but R is not correct','R is correct but A isn\'t correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300862,'chemistry','Given bellow are two statements : one is labeled assertion (A) and the other is labeled Reason:\n \n A: Drugs can only inhibit catalytic activity of the enzymes.\n \n R:Drugs inhibit activity of the enzymes by competing with substrate for the attachment on the active sites','R is correct but A isn\'t correct','Drugs inhibit any of the above mentioned activities of enzymes. These can block the binding site of the enzyme and prevent the binding of substrate, or can inhibit the catalytic activity of the enzyme. Such drugs are called enzyme inhibitors.\n \n Drugs compete with the natural substrate for their attachment on the active sites of enzymes. Such drugs are called competitive inhibitors.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 441 paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','both A and R are correct but R isn\'t correct explanation of A','A is correct but R is not correct','R is correct but A isn\'t correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300863,'chemistry','Given bellow are two statements : one is labeled assertion (A) and the other is labeled Reason:\n \n A:Binding of inhibitor at allosteric site changes the shape of the active site in such a way that substrate can- not recognise it.\n \n R: If the enzyme is blocked permanently,the body then degrades the enzyme-inhibitor complex and synthesises the new enzyme.','both A and R are correct but R isn\'t correct explanation of A','Some drugs do not bind to the enzyme’s active site. These bind to a different site of enzyme which is called allosteric site. This binding of inhibitor at allosteric site (Fig.16.3) changes the shape of the active site in such a way that substrate can- not recognise it.\n \n If the bond formed between an enzyme and an inhibitor is a strong covalent bond and cannot be broken easily, then the enzyme is blocked permanently. The body then degrades the enzyme-inhibitor complex and synthesises the new enzyme.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 441 paragraph last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','both A and R are correct but R isn\'t correct explanation of A','A is correct but R is not correct','R is correct but A isn\'t correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300864,'chemistry','Given bellow are two statements : one is labeled assertion (A) and the other is labeled Reason:\n \n A:Receptors are proteins that are crucial to body’s communication process.\n \n R:Receptor proteins possess active site ,that has one end bellow and one end on the surface of the membrane.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','Receptors are proteins that are crucial to body’s communication process. Majority of these are embedded in cell membranes.Receptor proteins are embedded in the cell membrane in such a way that their small part possessing active site projects out of the surface of the membrane and opens on the outside region of the cell membrane.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 442 paragraph last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','both A and R are correct but R isn\'t correct explanation of A','A is correct but R is not correct','R is correct but A isn\'t correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300865,'chemistry','Given bellow are two statements : one is labeled assertion (A) and the other is labeled Reason:\n \n A:Message between two neurons and that between neurons to muscles is communicated through chemical messengers.\n \n R:Chemical messenger gives message to the cell without entering the cell','both A and R are correct but R isn\'t correct explanation of A','In the body, message between two neurons and that between neurons to muscles is communicated through certain chemicals. These chemicals, known as chemical messengers are received at the binding sites of receptor proteins. To accommodate a messenger, shape of the receptor site changes.This brings about the transfer of message into the cell. Thus, chemical messenger gives message to the cell without entering the cell.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 442 paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','both A and R are correct but R isn\'t correct explanation of A','A is correct but R is not correct','R is correct but A isn\'t correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300866,'chemistry','Given bellow are two statements : one is labeled assertion (A) and the other is labeled Reason:\n \n A:Shape of the receptor changed after attachment of messenger,that it regains structure after removal of chemical messenger.\n \n R:Receptors show selectivity for one chemical messenger over the other because their binding sites have different shape.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','Receptor-messenger interaction:\n \n (a) Receptor receiving chemical messenger \n \n (b) Shape of the receptor changed after attachment of messenger \n \n (c) Receptor regains structure after removal of chemical messenger.\n \n There are a large number of different receptors in the body that interact with different chemical messengers. These receptors show selectivity for one chemical messenger over the other because their binding sites have different shape, structure and amino acid composition.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 442 paragraph last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','both A and R are correct but R isn\'t correct explanation of A','A is correct but R is not correct','R is correct but A isn\'t correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300867,'chemistry','Given bellow are two statements : one is labeled assertion (A) and the other is labeled Reason:\n \n A:Metal hydroxides are insoluble and do not increase the pH above neutrality of stomach,in stomach acidity treatment.\n \n R:Histamine stimulates the secretion of pepsin and hydrochloric acid in the stomach.','both A and R are correct but R isn\'t correct explanation of A','Excessive hydrogencarbonate can make the stomach alkaline and trigger the production of even more acid. Metal hydroxides are better alternatives because of being insoluble, these do not increase the pH above neutrality.\n \n A major breakthrough in the treatment of hyperacidity came through the discovery according to which a chemical, histamine, stimulates the secretion of pepsin and hydrochloric acid in the stomach.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 443 paragraph last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','both A and R are correct but R isn\'t correct explanation of A','A is correct but R is not correct','R is correct but A isn\'t correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300868,'chemistry','Given bellow are two statements : one is labeled assertion (A) and the other is labeled Reason:\n \n A:Histamine is a potent vasodilator.\n\n R:Histamine is responsible for the nasal congestion associated with common cold and allergic response to pollen.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','Histamine is a potent vasodilator. It has various functions. It contracts the smooth muscles in the bronchi and gut and relaxes other muscles, such as those in the walls of fine blood vessels. Histamine is also responsible for the nasal congestion associated with common cold and allergic response to pollen.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 443 paragraph last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','both A and R are correct but R isn\'t correct explanation of A','A is correct but R is not correct','R is correct but A isn\'t correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300869,'chemistry','Given bellow are two statements : one is labeled assertion (A) and the other is labeled Reason:\n \n A:Tranquilizers and analgesics are neurologically active drugs.\n \n R:Tranquilizers are a class of chemical compounds used for the treatment of stress, and mild or even severe mental diseases.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','Tranquilizers and analgesics are neurologically active drugs. These affect the message transfer mechanism from nerve to receptor. Tranquilizers are a class of chemical compounds used for the treatment of stress, and mild or even severe mental diseases. These relieve anxiety, stress, irritability or excitement by inducing a sense of well-being.\n \n (Reference NCERT class page 444 paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','both A and R are correct but R isn\'t correct explanation of A','A is correct but R is not correct','R is correct but A isn\'t correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300870,'chemistry','Given bellow are two statements : one is labeled assertion (A) and the other is labeled Reason:\n \n A:If the level of noradrenaline is low for some reason, then the signal-sending activity becomes low, and the person suffers from depression.\n \n R:Antidepressant drugs inhibit the enzymes which catalyse the degradation of noradrenaline.','both A and R are correct but R isn\'t correct explanation of A','Noradrenaline is one of the neurotransmitters that plays a role in mood changes. If the level of noradrenaline is low for some reason, then the signal-sending activity becomes low, and the person suffers from depression. In such situations, antidepressant drugs are required. These drugs inhibit the enzymes which catalyse the degradation of noradrenaline.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 444 paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','both A and R are correct but R isn\'t correct explanation of A','A is correct but R is not correct','R is correct but A isn\'t correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(71519,'chemistry','In which of the following reactions, the equilibrium remains unaffected on addition of small amount of argon at constant volume?','The equilibrium will remain unaffected in all three cases on addition of small amount of inert gas at constant volume.','The equilibrium will remain unaffected in all three cases on addition of small amount of inert gas at constant volume.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌2HI(g)','PCl₅(g) ⇌PCl₃(g) + Cl₂(g)','N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌2NH₃(g)','The equilibrium will remain unaffected in all three cases on addition of small amount of inert gas at constant volume.','NEW','11-211','11-187',NULL),
(300871,'chemistry','Given bellow are two statements : one is labeled assertion (A) and the other is labeled Reason:\n \n A:Morphine is narcotic analgesic.\n\n R:Morphine narcotics are sometimes referred to as opiates, and it is addictive.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','Morphine and many of its homologues, when administered in medicinal doses, relieve pain and produce sleep. In poisonous doses, these produce stupor, coma, convulsions and ultimately death. Morphine narcotics are sometimes referred to as opiates, since they are obtained from the opium poppy.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 445 paragraph last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','both A and R are correct but R isn\'t correct explanation of A','A is correct but R is not correct','R is correct but A isn\'t correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300872,'chemistry','Given bellow are two statements : one is labeled assertion (A) and the other is labeled Reason:\n \n A:Antibiotics are drugs that treat infections\n \n R:Antibiotics are chemical substances that inhibit the growth or even destroy microorganisms.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','Antibiotics are used as drugs to treat infections because of their low toxicity for humans and animals. Initially antibiotics were classified as chemical substances produced by microorganisms (bacteria, fungi and molds) that inhibit the growth or even destroy microorganisms.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 446 paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','both A and R are correct but R isn\'t correct explanation of A','A is correct but R is not correct','R is correct but A isn\'t correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300904,'chemistry','The most useful classification of drugs for medicinal chemists is ........','on the basis of molecular targets','The classification based on molecular targets is the most useful classification for medicinal chemists.(Reference: NCERT- Chemistry Class 12; Page 448; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','on the basis of chemical structure','on the basis of drug action','on the basis of molecular targets','on the basis of pharmacological effect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300905,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct?','Some tranquilizers function by inhibiting the enzymes which catalyse the degradation of noradrenaline','"Tranquilizers are a class of chemical compounds used for the treatment of stress, and mild or even severe mental diseases. These relieve anxiety, stress, irritability or excitement by inducing a sense of well-being. They form an essential component of sleeping pills.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 452 Para 2 )"','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Some tranquilizers function by inhibiting the enzymes which catalyse the degradation of noradrenaline','Tranquilizers are narcotic drugs','Tranquilizers are chemical compounds that do not affect the message transfer from nerve to receptor','Tranquilizers are chemical compounds that can relieve pain and fever','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300906,'chemistry','Salvarsan is arsenic containing drug which was first used for the treatment of ...........','syphilis','"Salvarsan is antibacterial earlier used in the treatment of syphilis and is no longer used as it was found to be toxic to humans,\n Some disinfectants are used as antiseptic at lower concentration."','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','syphilis','typhoid','meningitis','dysentery','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300907,'chemistry','A narrow spectrum antibiotic is active against ................','gram positive or gram negative bacteria','"Those antibiotics effective mainly against Gram-positive or Gram-negative bacteria are narrow spectrum antibiotics.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 455 Para 3 )"','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','gram positive or gram negative bacteria','gram negative bacteria only','single organism or one disease','both gram positive and gram negative bacteria','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300908,'chemistry','Compound which is added to soap to impart antiseptic properties is .............','bithional','Bithionol (the compound is also called bithional) is added to soaps to impart antiseptic properties. Iodine is a powerful antiseptic. Its 2-3 per cent solution in alcohol- water mixture is known as tincture of iodine. It is applied on wounds. (Reference NCERT class 12 page 448 paragraph 1 )','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','sodium laurylsulphate','sodium dodecylbenzenesulphonate','rosin','bithional','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300909,'chemistry','Equanil is ..........','tranquillizer','Some tranquilizers namely, chlordiazepoxide and meprobamate, are relatively mild tranquilizers suitable for relieving tension. Equanil is used in controlling depression and hypertension.\n(Reference NCERT class 12 page 444 paragraph 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','artificial sweetener','tranquillizer','antihistamine','antifertility drug','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300910,'chemistry','Which of the following enhances leathering property of soap?','Sodium rosinate','Shaving soaps contain glycerol to prevent rapid drying. A gum called, rosin is added while making them. It forms sodium rosinate which lathers well. (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 459 Para 2 )','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Sodium carbonate','Sodium rosinate','Sodium sterate','Trisodium phosphate','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300911,'chemistry','Glycerol is added to soap. It functions ............','to prevent rapid drying','Shaving soaps contain glycerol to prevent rapid drying.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 459 Para 2 )','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','as a filler','to increase leathering','to prevent rapid drying','to make soap granules','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300912,'chemistry','Which of the following is an example of liquid dishwashing detergent? Refer diagram(n3041)','b','Non-ionic detergents do not contain any ion in their constitution. One such detergent is formed when stearic acid reacts with polyethyleneglycol.Liquid dishwashing detergents are non-ionic type. Mechanism of cleansing action of this type of detergents is the same as that of soaps. These also remove grease and oil by micelle formation.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 460 Para 2 )','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/300912q.png'),
(300913,'chemistry','Polyethyleneglycols are used in the preparation of which type of detergents?','Non-ionic detergents','Non-ionic detergents do not contain any ion in their constitution. One such detergent is formed when stearic acid reacts with polyethyleneglycol.Liquid dishwashing detergents are non-ionic type. Mechanism of cleansing action of this type of detergents is the same as that of soaps. These also remove grease and oil by micelle formation.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 460 Para 2 )','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cationic detergents','Anionic detergents','Non-ionic detergents','Soaps','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300914,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a target molecule for drug function in body?','Vitamins','Drugs usually interact with biomolecules such as carbohydrates, lipids, proteins and nucleic acids. These are called target molecules or drug targets.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 448; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Carbohydrates','Lipids','Vitamins','Proteins','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300915,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is not true about enzyme inhibitors?','Generally a strong covalent bond is formed between an inhibitor and an enzyme','"If the enzyme-inhibitor bond is strong covalent bond that cannot be broken easily than the enzyme is blocked permanently . The body degrades enzyme-inhibitor complex and synthesises the new enzyme.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 441 paragraph 3)"','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Inhibit the catalytic activity of the enzyme','Prevent the binding of substrate','Generally a strong covalent bond is formed between an inhibitor and an enzyme','Inhibitors can be competitive or non-competitive','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300916,'chemistry','Which of the following chemicals can be added for sweetening of food items at cooking temperature and does not provide calories?','Sucralose','"Sucralose is trichloro derivative of sucrose. Its\nappearance and taste is like sugar. It is stable at cooking\ntemperature and it does not provide calories.(Reference NCERT class 12 page 449 paragraph last )"','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Sucrose','Glucose','Aspartame','Sucralose','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300917,'chemistry','Which of the following will not enhance nutritional value of food?','Artificial sweeteners','"Sucralose is trichloro derivative of sucrose. Its\nappearance and taste is like sugar. It is stable at cooking\ntemperature and it does not provide calories.(Reference NCERT class 12 page 449 paragraph last )"','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Minerals','Artificial sweeteners','Vitamins','Amino acids','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300918,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are incorrect about receptor proteins?\n\n(a) Majority of receptor proteins are embedded in the cell membranes\n\n(b) The active site of receptor proteins opens on the inside region of the cell\n\n(c) Chemical messengers are received at the binding sites of receptor proteins\n\n(d) Shape of receptor doesn’t change during attachment of messenger','b,d','Receptors are proteins that are crucial to body’s communication process. Majority of these are embedded in cell membranes. Receptor proteins are embedded in the cell membrane in such a way that their small part possessing active site projects out of the surface of the membrane and opens on the outside region of the cell membrane . \n\nIn the body, message between two neurons and that between neurons to muscles is communicated through certain chemicals. These chemicals, known as  chemical messengers  are received at the binding sites of receptor proteins. \n\nTo accommodate a messenger, shape of the receptor  site  changes. This brings about the transfer of message into the cell. \n\nThus, chemical messenger gives message to the cell without entering the cell.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 450; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','b,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300919,'chemistry','Which of the following are not used as food preservatives?\n\n(a) Table salt \n\n(b) Sodium hydrogencarbonate\n\n(c) Cane sugar \n\n(d) Benzoic acid','b,c','Food preservatives prevent spoilage of food due to prohibiting microbial growth. The most commonly used preservatives include table salt, sugar, vegetable oils and sodium benzoate, C6H5COONa. Sodium benzoate is used in limited quantities and is metabolised in the body. Salts of sorbic acid and propanoic acid are also used as preservatives. (Reference NCERT class 12 page 450 paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','b,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300920,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct about barbiturates?\n\n(a) Hypnotics or sleep producing agents\n\n(b) These are tranquilizers\n\n(c) Non-narcotic analgesics','a,b','Barbiturates are hypnotic, i.e.,\nsleep producing agents. \n\n"Derivatives of barbituric acid viz., veronal, amytal, nembutal, luminal and seconal constitute an important class of tranquilizers. These derivatives are called barbiturates. Barbiturates are hypnotic, i.e.,\nsleep producing agents. Some other substances used as tranquilizers\nare valium and serotonin.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 453 Para 1 )"','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,b,c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300921,'chemistry','Which of the following are sulpha drugs?\n\n(a) Sulphapyridine \n\n(b) Prontosil\n\n(c) Salvarsan \n\n(d) Nardil','a,b','In 1932, he succeeded in preparing the first effective antibacterial agent, prontosil, which resembles in structure to the compound, salvarsan. Soon it was discovered that in the body prontosil H.W. Florey and Alexander Fleming shared the Nobel prize for Medicine in 1945 for their independent contributions to the development of penicillin. is converted to a compound called sulphanilamide, which is the real active compound. Thus the sulpha drugs were discovered.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 455 Para 2 )','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300922,'chemistry','Which of the following are antidepressants?\n\n(a) Iproniazid \n\n(b) Phenelzine\n\n(c) Equanil \n\n(d) Salvarsan','a,b,c','Iproniazid, Equanil, Phenelzine,Chlordiazepoxide are the examples of Antidepressants. (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 452 Para 2 )','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,d','a,b,c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300923,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are incorrect about penicillin?\n\n(a) An antibacterial fungus\n\n(b) Ampicillin is its synthetic modification\n\n(c) It has bacteriostatic effect\n\n(d) It is a broad spectrum antibiotic','c,d','Antibiotics which kill or inhibit a wide range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria are said to be broad spectrum antibiotics. Those effective mainly against Gram-positive or Gram-negative bacteria are narrow spectrum antibiotics. If effective against a single organism or disease, they are referred to as limited spectrum antibiotics. Penicillin G has a narrow spectrum. \n\n Antibiotics which kill or inhibit a wide range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria are said to be broad spectrum antibiotics. Those effective mainly against Gram-positive or Gram-negative bacteria are narrow spectrum antibiotics. If effective against a single organism or disease, they are referred to as limited spectrum antibiotics. Penicillin G has a narrow spectrum. (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 455 Para 2 )','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300924,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds are administered as antacids?\n\n(a) Sodium carbonate \n\n(b) Sodium hydrogencarbonate\n\n(c) Aluminium carbonate \n\n(d) Magnesium hydroxide','b,d','Aluminium carbonate and magnesium hydroxide are also commonly used as antacids because they can react with stomach acid to form salts, which are less acidic and can alleviate symptoms of acid reflux and heartburn.\n\n\n Sodium carbonate is not used as an antacid because it is highly alkaline and can cause side effects such as nausea and vomiting. Sodium hydrogencarbonate, on the other hand, is mildly alkaline and can neutralize stomach acid, making it an effective antacid.','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300925,'chemistry','Amongst the following antihistamines, which are antacids?\n\na. Ranitidine\nb. Brompheniramine\nc. Terfenadine\nd. Cimetidine','a,d','Example of Antacids are : ranitidine (Zantac),cimetidine (Tegamet).(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 451; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300926,'chemistry','Veronal and luminal are derivatives of barbituric acid which are ............\n\na. tranquillizers \nb. non narcotic analgesic\nc. anti allergic\nd. neurologically active drugs','a,d','Derivatives of barbituric acid viz., veronal, amytal, nembutal, luminal and seconal constitute an important class of tranquilizers. These derivatives are called  barbiturates.  Barbiturates are hypnotic,  i.e., sleep producing agents. Some other substances used as tranquilizers are valium and serotonin.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 453 Para 1 )','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300927,'chemistry','Which of the following are anionic detergents?\n\n(a) Sodium salts of sulphonated long chain alcohol\n\n(b) Ester of stearic acid and polyethylene glycol\n\n(c) Quarternary ammonium salt of amine with acetate ion\n\n(d) Sodium salts of sulphonated long chain hydrocarbons','a,d','Cetyltrimethylammoniumbromide is a popular cationic detergent and is used in hair conditioners.\n \n Liquid dishwashing detergents are non-ionic type of detergent.\n \n Anionic detergents are sodium salts of sulphonated long chain alcohols or hydrocarbons. Alkyl hydrogensulphates formed by treating long chain alcohols with concentrated sulphuric acid are neutralised with alkali to form anionic detergents.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 452 1,3,last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300928,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\n\n(a) Cationic detergents have germicidal properties\n(b) Bacteria can degrade the detergents containing highly branched chains\n(c) Some synthetic detergents can give foam even in ice cold water\n(d) Synthetic detergents are not  soap','a,c,d','Cationic detergents are quarternary ammonium salts of amines with acetates, chlorides or bromides as anions. Cationic part possess a long hydrocarbon chain and a positive charge on nitrogen atom. Hence, these are called cationic detergents.\n \n In anionic detergents, the anionic part of the molecule is involved in the cleansing action. Sodium salts of alkylbenzenesulphonates are an important class of anionic detergents. They are mostly used for household work. Anionic detergents are also used in toothpastes.\n \n Cationic detergents have germicidal properties and are expensive, therefore, these are of limited use.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 452 paragraph 1,3,last)\n\nSynthetic detergents are cleansing agents which have all the properties of soaps, but which actually do not contain any soap.  These can be used both in soft and hard water as they give foam even in hard water. Some of the detergents give foam even in ice cold water.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 460 Para 1 )','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c,d','all of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300929,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Penicillin (G) is an antihistamine.\n\nReason (R) Penicillin (G) is effective against gram positive as well as gram negative bacteria.','Both Assertion and reason are false','Penicillin (G) is not an antihistamine, it is an antibiotic that is effective against a wide range of bacterial infections\nPenicillin is effective against some gram positive bacteria.','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300930,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Sulpha drug contain sulphonamide group.\n\nReason (R) Salvarsan is a sulpha drug.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Sulpha drugs, also known as sulfonamides, contain a sulphonamide group as an essential part of their chemical structure. This sulphonamide group is responsible for the antibacterial activity of these drugs by inhibiting the bacterial folic acid synthesis pathway.\n\nHowever, Salvarsan is not a sulpha drug. Salvarsan, also known as arsphenamine, is an arsenic-based drug used to treat syphilis.','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300931,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Receptors are crucial to body’s communication process.\n\nReason (R) Receptors are proteins.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Receptors are specialized proteins found on the surface or inside cells that are crucial to the body\'s communication process. They receive and transmit signals from hormones, neurotransmitters, and other signaling molecules, allowing cells to respond and adapt to changes in their environment.\n\nThe reason that receptors are proteins is also true. Receptors are typically made up of amino acids and have a specific three-dimensional structure that enables them to recognize and bind to their specific ligands or signaling molecules.\n\nHowever, while the reason is true, it does not provide a complete explanation for the assertion. While it is true that receptors are proteins, the fact that they are proteins alone does not fully explain why they are crucial to the body\'s communication process. The complex nature of receptor signaling and their role in cellular communication is what makes them crucial to the body\'s overall function.','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300932,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Enzymes have active sites that hold substrate molecule for a chemical reaction.\n\nReason (R) Drugs compete with natural substrate by attaching covalently to the active site of enzyme.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Some drugs do not bind to the enzyme’s active site.These bind to a different site of enzyme which is called allosteric site. This binding of inhibitor at allosteric site (Fig.16.3) changes the shape of the active site in such a way that substrate can- not recognise it. If the bond formed between an enzyme and an inhibitor is a strong covalent bond and cannot be broken easily, then the enzyme is blocked permanently. The body then degrades the enzyme-inhibitor complex and synthesises the new enzyme.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 448; Fig 16.3)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300933,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Chemical messengers are chemicals that enable communication of message between two neurons or between neurons and muscles.\n\nReason (R): Chemicals enter the cell through receptor.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Receptors are proteins that are crucial to body’s communication process. Majority of these are embedded in cell membranes. Receptor proteins are embedded in the cell membrane in such a way that their small part possessing active site projects out of the surface of the membrane and opens on the outside region of the cell membrane . In the body, message between two neurons and that between neurons to muscles is communicated through certain chemicals. These chemicals, known as  chemical messengers  are received at the binding sites of receptor proteins. To accommodate a messenger, shape of the receptor  site  changes. This brings about the transfer of message into the cell. Thus, chemical messenger gives message to the cell without entering the cell.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 450; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300934,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Transparent soaps are made by dissolving soaps in ethanol.\n\nReason (R) Ethanol makes things invisible.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Transparent soaps are made by dissolving regular soap in ethanol or glycerin to remove the impurities that cause the soap to become cloudy. So, the Assertion is partially true.\n\nHowever, the Reason "Ethanol makes things invisible" is false. Ethanol is a clear and colorless liquid, which means it can be seen, not invisible. The transparency of soaps made with ethanol is due to the solvent properties of ethanol, which helps to keep the soap clear and transparent.','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300935,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Sodium chloride is added to precipitate soap after saponification.\n\nReason (R) Hydrolysis of esters of long chain fatty acids by alkali produces soap in colloidal form.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Soaps containing sodium salts are formed by heating fat (i.e., glyceryl ester of fatty acid) with aqueous sodium hydroxide solution. This reaction is known as saponification.Saponification reaction, esters of fatty acids are hydrolysed and the soap obtained remains in colloidal form. It is precipitated from the solution by adding sodium chloride.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 459 Para 1 )','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300936,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Competitive inhibitors compete with natural substrate for their attachment on the active sites of enzymes.\n\nReason (R) In competitive inhibition, inhibitor binds to the allosteric site of the enzyme','Assertion is true but reason is false','Drugs inhibit any of the above mentioned activities of enzymes. These can block the binding site of the enzyme and prevent the binding of substrate, or can inhibit the catalytic activity of the enzyme. Such drugs are called enzyme inhibitors.\n \n Drugs compete with the natural substrate for their attachment on the active sites of enzymes. Such drugs are called competitive inhibitors.\n \n Some drugs do not bind to the enzyme’s active site. These bind to a different site of enzyme which is called allosteric site.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 441 paragraph last)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300937,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Chemical messenger gives message to the cell without entering the cell.\n\nReason (R) Chemical messenger is received at the binding site of receptor proteins.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Receptors are proteins that are crucial to body’s communication process. Majority of these are embedded in cell membranes. Receptor proteins are embedded in the cell membrane in such a way that their small part possessing active site projects out of the surface of the membrane and opens on the outside region of the cell membrane . In the body, message between two neurons and that between neurons to muscles is communicated through certain chemicals. These chemicals, known as  chemical messengers  are received at the binding sites of receptor proteins. To accommodate a messenger, shape of the receptor  site  changes. This brings about the transfer of message into the cell. Thus, chemical messenger gives message to the cell without entering the cell.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 450; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300938,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Receptor proteins show selectivity for one chemical messenger over the other.\n\nReason (R) Chemical messenger binds to the receptor site and inhibits its natural function.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Receptors are proteins that are crucial to body’s communication process. Majority of these are embedded in cell membranes. Receptor proteins are embedded in the cell membrane in such a way that their small part possessing active site projects out of the surface of the membrane and opens on the outside region of the cell membrane . In the body, message between two neurons and that between neurons to muscles is communicated through certain chemicals. These chemicals, known as  chemical messengers  are received at the binding sites of receptor proteins. To accommodate a messenger, shape of the receptor  site  changes. This brings about the transfer of message into the cell. Thus, chemical messenger gives message to the cell without entering the cell.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 450; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300939,'chemistry','Assertion (A) All chemicals added to food items are called food preservatives.\n\nReason (R) All these chemicals increase the nutritive value of the food','Both Assertion and reason are false','Food preservatives prevent spoilage of food due to prohibiting microbial growth. The most commonly used preservatives include table salt, sugar, vegetable oils and sodium benzoate, C6H5COONa. Sodium benzoate is used in limited quantities and is metabolised in the body. Salts of sorbic acid and propanoic acid are also used as preservatives. (Reference NCERT class 12 page 450 paragraph 2)\n\n"Main categories of food additives are as follows: \n \n (i) Food colours \n \n (ii) Flavours and sweeteners \n \n (iii) Fat emulsifiers and stabilising agents \n \n (iv) Flour improvers - antistaling agents and bleaches \n \n (v) Antioxidants \n \n (vi) Preservatives \n \n (vii) Nutritional supplements such as minerals, vitamins and amino acids.\n \n Except for chemicals of category (vii), none of the above additives \n \n have nutritive value.\n \n \n \n (Reference NCERT class 12 page 449 paragraph 1)"','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300940,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Artificial sweeteners are added to the food to control the intake of calories.\n\nReason (R) Most of the artificial sweeteners are inert and do not metabolise in the body.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','We require artificial sweetening agents as they are sweet in taste but they have no food value. They go directly through the digestive tract without being digested. It helps diabetic patients to eat sweet food without adding any extra sugar in their body.(Reference NCERT class 12 page 448 paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(60206,'chemistry','The presence of reactants in a closed vessel made of conducting material e.g., copper or steel is an example of a','Closed system','The presence of reactant in a closed vessel made of conducting material e.g., copper or Steel is an example of a closed vessel.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 155; Paragraph No.6.1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'1','mcq','Open system','Closed system','Isolated sytem','None of these','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(60217,'chemistry','The State of the thermodynamic system is described by its measurable or ----------- properties.','macroscopic','The State of a Thermodynamic system is described by its measurable or macroscopic properties. .(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 156; Paragraph No.6.1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'1','fillup','ma,cro,scop,ic,mi,cro,scop,ic',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-162','11-138',NULL),
(60218,'chemistry','Which variables called state functions or State variables?','P, V, T','Variables like P, V, T are called state functions or state variables because their values depend only in the state of the system and not how it is reached.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 156; Paragraph No.6.1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'2','mcq','P, V, T','P, T, R','P, V, R','Only P, V','NEW','11-162','11-138',NULL),
(60219,'chemistry','Which energy is the total energy of the system?','Internal energy','Internal energy is the total energy within a system. It is the sum of all types of energies ( chemical, electrical, mechanical or any other type) of the system and is a state function.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 155; Paragraph No.6.1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'1','mcq','Physical energy','Internal energy','Chemical energy','Kinetic energy','NEW','11-162','11-138',NULL),
(60220,'chemistry','A system changes From state X to Y with a change in internal energy measuring to 30 kj/mol, by a reversible path and returns from Y to X by an irreversible path. What will be the net change in internal energy?','zero','For a cyclic process, net change  internal energy is zero. change in internal energy does not depend on the path by which final stage is reached.         \nFor a given change in state, q and w can vary depending on how the change is carried\nout. However, q +w = ∆U will depend only on\ninitial and final state. It will be independent of\nthe way the change is carried out.If there is\nno transfer of energy as heat or as work\n(isolated system) i.e., if w = 0 and q = 0, then ∆ U = 0. \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 158; Paragraph No.6.1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'3','mcq','zero','25 kj','>25 kj','<25 kj','NEW','11-162','11-138',NULL),
(60221,'chemistry','What is the first law of thermodynamics for adiabatic change?','∆ U= U₂ - U₁= W(ad)','For adiabatic change, ∆ U= U₂ - U₁= W(ad)\nwhere, W(ad) is work done when no heat is exchanged i.e; = 0.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 156; Paragraph No.6.1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'1','mcq','∆ U= U₂ - U₁= W(ad)','∆U= U₂ - U₁ =q(ad)','∆U=  U₁ - U₂=q(ad)','∆U = U₁ - U₂= W(ad)','NEW','11-164','11-140',NULL),
(60222,'chemistry','When no work is done at constant volume ,∆U = ?','∆U = qv or ∆U = q','∆U= qv or ∆U= q , when no work is done at constant volume.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 157; Paragraph No.6.1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'1','mcq','∆U = 0','∆U = ∆V','∆U = ∆H','∆U = qv or ∆U = q','NEW','11-164','11-140',NULL),
(60223,'chemistry','A system absorbs 100 kj heat and does 50 kj work. What is the net change in internal energy of the system?','Increased by 50 kj','∆U = q + w, ∆U =. 100+ (-50) = 50 kj , w = -50  because work is done by the system.The positive sign expresses\nthat wad is positive when work is done on the\nsystem and the internal energy of system\nincreases. Similarly, if the work is done by the\nsystem,wad will be negative because internal\nenergy of the system decreases.\n (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 157; Paragraph No.6.1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'3','mcq','Decreased by 60 kj','Increased by 25 kj','Decreased by 50 kj','Increased by 50 kj','NEW','11-164','11-140',NULL),
(60224,'chemistry','What is the internal energy change of the system when 2.865 j of heat was supplied to a system at constant volume.It resulted in the increase in temperature of the system from 600 to 499.8 K.','2.865 j','At constant volume, ∆V = 0,    w = -P∆V = 0,   ∆U = q = 2.865 j\nLet us substitute w = – pex∆V\n\n, ∆U = q - pex∆V\n,If a process is carried out at constant volume\n(∆V = 0), then\n∆U = qV\nthe subscript V in q\nVdenotes that heat is\nsupplied at constant volume.  (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'2','mcq','- 3.645 j','- 2.865 j','2.865 j','3.645 j','NEW','11-164','11-140',NULL),
(60225,'chemistry','What will be the heat given by the system when 20 kj of work is done by the system andchange in internal energy is 10kj?','-10 kj','ΔU = Q - W \n\n10 kJ = Q - (-20 kJ)\n\nSimplifying the expression on the right side gives:\n\n10 kJ = Q + 20 kJ\n\nSubtracting 20 kJ from both sides gives:\n\nQ = -10 kJ\n\nThe negative sign for Q indicates that heat is extracted from the system\n\n (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 157; Paragraph No.6.1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'2','mcq','-20 kj','-10 kj','30 kj','- 25 kj','NEW','11-164','11-140',NULL),
(60226,'chemistry','Consider the following assertion and reason: \n \n Assertion : There is no change in internal energy in a cyclic process. \n \n Reason : Internal energy is a state function.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 156; Paragraph No.6.1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','11-164','11-140',NULL),
(60227,'chemistry','Express the change in internal energy of a system when No heat is absorbed by the system from the surroundings, but work (w) is done on the system. What type of wall does the system have ?','∆U = Wad, wall is adiabatic','We take a system\ncontaining some quantity of water in a thermos\nflask or in an insulated beaker. This would not allow exchange of heat between the system\nand surroundings through its boundary and\nwe call this type of system as adiabatic. .(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 156; Paragraph No.6.1.4) (NCERT Problem- 6.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'2','mcq','∆U = q, wall is adiabatic','∆U = -q, wall is adiabatic','∆U = q, wall is  non-adiabatic','∆U = Wad, wall is adiabatic','NEW','11-164','11-140',NULL),
(60228,'chemistry','Express the change in internal energy of a system when No work is done on the system, but q amount of heat is taken out from the system and given to the surroundings. What type of wall does the system have?','∆U = -q, thermally conducting walls','This exchange\nof energy, which is a result of temperature difference is called heat, q. Transfer of heat through\n thermally conducting walls instead of\n adiabatic walls..(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 157; Paragraph No.6.1.4) (NCERT Problem- 6.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Thermodynamics terms',NULL,'2','mcq','∆U = qv thermally conducting walls','∆U = -q, thermally conducting walls','∆U = q, thermally conducting walls','∆U = -q, thermally non conducting walls','NEW','11-164','11-140',NULL),
(60329,'chemistry','What is the work done in a free expansion of an ideal gas?','No work done','No work is done during free expansion of an ideal gas. \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 159; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'1','mcq','No work done','w = f.d','No for sometime','Yes for sometime','NEW','11-166','11-142',NULL),
(60330,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct:\n i. Free expansion is an isothermal process of an ideal gas.\n \n ii. In a free expansion there is a external pressure.\n \n iii. There is no temprature change in the free expansion of an ideal gas\n \n iv. In a free expansion there is no work done.','i, iii, and iv','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii not iii','i, iii, and iv','iv and ii not i','i,ii,iii,iv','NEW','11-166','11-142',NULL),
(60331,'chemistry','What is the work done and external pressure for isothermal expansion of an ideal gas into vacuum?','w = 0 and Pₑₓ = 0','For isothermal (T = constant ) expansion of an ideal gas into vacuum; w=0 since Pₑₓ = 0. Also joule\n determined experimentally that q = 0; therefore, ∆U = 0..(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'1','mcq','w = 0 and Pex = (Pᵢₙ + dp)','w = 0 and Pₑₓ = 0','w = 0 and Pₑₓ =(Pᵢₙ ± dp)','w = 0 and Pₑₓ = (Pᵢₙ - dp)','NEW','11-166','11-142',NULL),
(60332,'chemistry','What is value of q, for isothermal reversible change in free expansion of an ideal gas?','q = 2.303nRT log V₂/V₁','For isothermal reversible expansion\nq = -w nRT ln V₂/V₁ = 2.303 nRT log V₂/V₁(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'2','mcq','q = 2.303nRT log V₁/V₂','q = -2.303nRT log V₁/V₂','q = -2.303nRT log V₂/V₁','q = 2.303nRT log V₂/V₁','NEW','11-166','11-142',NULL),
(60333,'chemistry','For an isothermal irreversible expansion process, the value of q can be','q = -w = Pₑₓ (Vբ/Vᵢ)','"For isothermal irreversible expansion\nq = -w =  Pₑₓ (Vբ-Vᵢ) (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'1','mcq','q = -w = -Pex (Vᵢ/Vբ)','q = -w = Pₑₓ (Vբ/Vᵢ)','q = -w = -Pₑₓ (Vբ/Vᵢ)','q = -w = Pₑₓ (Vբ/Vᵢ)','NEW','11-166','11-142',NULL),
(60334,'chemistry','In an adiabatic expansion of ideal gas','w = ∆U','Since the system is insulated, heat is not allowed to enter or leave the system \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'1','mcq','w = ∆U','w = -∆U','∆U = 0','w = 0','NEW','11-166','11-142',NULL),
(60335,'chemistry','Calculate Q and W for the isothermal reversible expansion of 5 mole an ideal gas from an initial pressure of 5.0 bar to a final pressure of 0.5 bar at a constant temperature of 273 K respectively.','-26136J, 26136J','W = -2.303 nRT log(Pբ/Pi), where n = 5, R = 8.314 J/K/mol, T = 273 K, P = 5.0 to.0.5,\nW= -2.303 ×5× 8.314 × 273 ×log (5/0.5), W = -26136J. In first law of thermodynamics ∆U = Q + W, for isothermal ∆U = 0, Q = -W, Q = 26136J \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'2','mcq','26136J,-26136J','-26136J, 26136J','-31265J, 31265J','31265J, -31265J','NEW','11-166','11-142',NULL),
(60336,'chemistry','Calculate Q for the isothermal reversible expansion of 5 mole an ideal gas from an initial volume of 10 L to a final volume of 20 L at a constant temperature of 300 K respectively.','-8616.6 J','W = -2.303 nRT log(Vբ/Vᵢ), where n = 5, R = 8.314 J/K/mol, T = 300 K, P = 10 to 20,\nW= -2.303 ×5× 8.314 × 300 ×log (20/10), W = -8616.6J. In first law of thermodynamics ∆U = Q + W, for isothermal ∆U = 0, Q = -W, Q = 8616.6J \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'2','mcq','+8945 J','-8616.6 J','86456 J','-8945 J','NEW','11-166','11-142',NULL),
(60337,'chemistry','10 mol of an ideal gas at 500 k is expanded isothermally from an initial volume 1 L to 10 L. The ∆U for this process is','Zero','Changes in internal energy depends upon temprature. For isothermal ( constant temperature), the internal energy of the gas remains constant, so ∆E = 0. \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Work',NULL,'2','mcq','-3','3','Zero','-2','NEW','11-166','11-142',NULL),
(60425,'chemistry','In thermodynamics, a distinction is made between extensive properties and ----------------properties.','intensive','In thermodynamics, a distinction is made between extensive properties and intensive properties. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 162; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'1','fillup','in,ten,si,ve,no,nin,ten,si,ve',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-168','11-144',NULL),
(60442,'chemistry','What is value of heat capacity C, when heat is transferred from the system by 100J, initial temprature is 10°C and final temprature is 35°C?','-4J/k','q = C∆T , q = -100J ( heat is transferred from the system), ∆T = (Tf - Ti) \nC = q/∆T, C = -100J/(308K - 283K), C = -4J/k. .(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 162; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','4J/k','25J/k','-4J/k','-25J/k','NEW','11-168','11-144',NULL),
(60443,'chemistry','The molar heat capacity of water at constant pressure ,Cp is 100J/k/mol. When 20kJ of heat is supplied to 3kg water which is free to expand, the increase in temperature of water is','1.2004K','qm = mc∆T, qm = 20 kJ or 20000J, m = 3000g, c = 100 J/k/mol. \nNo. of moles = 3000/18 = 166.6 mol, ∆T = 20000/166.6 × 100 = 1.2004K .(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 163; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','1.2004K','-1.2004K','-1.002K','1.002K','NEW','11-168','11-144',NULL),
(60444,'chemistry','An ideal gas does work on its surroundings when it expands by 5.6 L against external pressure 5 atm. This work done is used to heat up 1 mole of water at 300K. What would be the final temprature of water in kelvin if specific heat for water is 4.184J/g/k?','300.371K','w = -Pex.dv, w = -5 ×5.6 = -28.0J. Because this work is used an raising the temperature of water, \nso work done is equal to the heat supplied i.e.,  w = m.Cs..∆T\nGiven that, m = 18g (=1 mole), Cs = 4.184J/g/K, q = +28.0J, ∆T = ?(Heat is given to water)\n∆T = q/Cs.m = 28.0/4.184 ×18 = 0.371  \n∴ Final temperature, Tf = Ti + ∆T = 300 + 0.371 = 300.371K .(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 163; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','-300.371K','298.37K','300.371K','-298.37K','NEW','11-168','11-144',NULL),
(60445,'chemistry','Calculate the number of kJ of heat necessary to raise the temperature of 60.0 g of aluminium from 35°C to 55°C. Molar heat capacity of Al is 24 J mol–1 K–1 .','1.07 j','q = m.c.∆T = 60.0/27 mol × 24 j/mol/K × 20K = 1.07 j .(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 163; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','1.07 j','-2.07 j','2.0769 j','-1.07 j','NEW','11-168','11-144',NULL),
(60446,'chemistry','Molar heat capacity of zinc is 195 J/k/mol.The heat necessary raise the temperature of 195g of zinc from 80°c to 40°c is','23400 J','q = m.c.∆T = 195/65 mol × 195j/mol/K × 40K = 23400 j .(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 163; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','-23400 J','20300 J','21400 J','23400 J','NEW','11-168','11-144',NULL),
(60447,'chemistry','What is the specific heat capacity and molar heat capacity for water?','4.18 J/g,75.24 J/mol','Specific heat capacity for H₂O = 4.18 J/g\nMolar heat capacity for H₂O = 4.18 × 18 = 75.24 J/mol','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','4.18 J/g,75.24 J/mol','3.18 J/g,73.24 J/mol','4.18 J/g,75.20 J/mol','4.10 J/g , 75.24 J/mol','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60448,'chemistry','Consider the following formulas: \n \n i. Cp - CV = R \n \n ii .Cp + R = Cv \n \n iii. Cp = Cv + R \n \n iv Cp∆T = Cv∆T + R∆T \n \n Which of the above formula is false?','only ii','We can write equation for heat, q\nat constant volume as qV\n= Cv∆T = ∆U\nat constant pressure as qp = Cp∆T =∆H\nOn putting the values of ∆H and ∆U,\nwe have\nCp∆T = Cv∆T + R∆T\nCp = Cv + R\n Cp - Cv = R\n \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 163; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','i, ii','only ii','iii, iv','ii, iii','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60449,'chemistry','The difference between Cpand Cv can be derived using the empirical relation H=U+pV. Calculate the difference between Cp and Cv for 100 mole of an ideal gas.','+418.4j','Cp - Cv = R\nfor 100 mol of a gas\nCp - Cv =nR\nCp - Cv = 100 × 4.184j = 418.4j (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 163; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','-4.184j','+4.184j','+418.4j','-418.4j','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60450,'chemistry','What is the degree of freedom, for monoatomic ideal gas ?','n = 3','Degree of freedom for monoatomic ideal gas is 3, n = 3','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','n = 3','n = -3','n = -2','n = 2','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60451,'chemistry','What is the value of Cv and Cp for monoatomic gas?','3/2R, 5/2R','for monoatomic ideal gas\nCv = n/2 R = 3/2 R\nand for Cp = Cv +R = 3/2R + R = 5/2R','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','7/2R, 5/2R','3/2R, 5/2R','5/2R, 3/2R','5/2R, 7/2R','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60452,'chemistry','What is the degree of freedom, for diatomic ideal gas ?','n = 5','Degree of freedom for diatomic ideal gas is 5, n = 5','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','n = 5','n = 5/2R','n = 7/2R','n = 7','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60453,'chemistry','What is the value of Cv and Cp for diatomic gas?','5/2R, 7/2R','for diatomic ideal gas\nCv = n/2 R = 5/2 R\nand for Cp = Cv +R = 5/2R + R = 7/2R','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','5/2R, 7/2R','7/2R, 5/2R','7/2R, 9/2R','9/2R, 7/2R','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60454,'chemistry','Equal volumes of two diatomic gases,A and B at same temperature and pressure are mixed.The ratio of specific heat (Cv/Cp) of the mixture will be?','0.714','Cp for diatomic ideal for = 7/2R and Cv for diatomic gas 5/2R  \nCv/Cp = 5/2R÷ 7/2R = 5/7 = 0.714 (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 163; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','1.4','1.66','0.33','0.714','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60455,'chemistry','What is the difference between Cp and Cv. When the heat capacity at constant volume is 60J/k/mol and the heat capacity at constant pressure is 100J/k/mol?','40J/k/mol','At constant pressure,Cp = 100J/k/mol\nAt constant volume,Cv = 60J/k/mol\nR = Cp - CV = 100 - 60 = 40J/k/mol (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 163; Paragraph No.6.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','40J/k/mol','-40J/k/mol','0','-30J/k/mol','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60456,'chemistry','Which experimental technique is used to measure energy changes associated with chemical or physical processes?','Calorimetry','We can measure energy changes associated\nwith chemical or physical processes by an\nexperimental technique called calorimetry. In\ncalorimetry, the process is carried out in a\nvessel called calorimeter, which is immersed\nin a known volume of a liquid. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 163; Paragraph No.6.3)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','Thermometer','Calorimetry','Thermometer and calorimeter','calories','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60457,'chemistry','What is the measurement of ∆H in calorimeter?','∆H = qp','The reaction is carried out in an insulated vessel ( calorimeter) directly and rise or fall in temprature\nis noted down depending upon whether the reaction is exothermic or endothermic. (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 164; Paragraph No.6.3)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'2','mcq','∆H = qv','∆H = 0','∆H = ∆U','∆H = qp','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60458,'chemistry','Consider the following assertion and reason: \n \n Assertion : ∆H for an exothermic reaction is negative and for an endothermic react is positive. \n \n Reason : Enthalpy is the extensive property.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 164; Paragraph No.6.3)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60459,'chemistry','1g of graphite is burnt in a bomb calorimeter in excess of oxygen at 298 K and 1 atmospheric pressure according to the equation C (graphite) + O₂(g) → CO₂(g) During the reaction, temperature rises from 298 K to 299 K. If the heat capacity of the bomb calorimeter is 20.7kJ/K, What is the enthalpy change for the above reaction at 298 K and 1 atm?','- 2.48 ×10² KJ mol⁻¹','q = CV × ∆T,  q = CV × ∆T = -20.7 KJ/k × (299 - 298)K = - 20.7 KJ\n Thus, ∆U  for the combustion of the 1g of graphite = - 20.7 KJk⁻¹\nfor combustion of 1 mol of graphite, = 12.0 g mol⁻¹ ×(-20.7KJ)/1gl = -2.48KJmol-1 (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 164; Paragraph No.6.3)(NCERT Problem 6.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'3','mcq','2.48 × 10² KJ','25.8 ×10⁻²KJ','- 2.48 ×10² KJ mol⁻¹','2 × 10⁻¹ KJ','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60460,'chemistry','What is the measurement of ∆U in bomb calorimetry?','∆U = C × ∆T × M/m','∆U = C × ∆T × M/m, where C = heat capacity, ∆T = change in temperature, M = molar mass, m = mass of substance','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpy',NULL,'1','mcq','∆U = C × ∆T × M/m','∆U = C × ∆T + M/m','∆U = C × ∆T × m/M','∆U = C × ∆V × M/m','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(60651,'chemistry','Calculate the approximate energy released from the following experiments 1000 mL of 2.0 M HCl with 1000 mL of 2.0 M NaOH is neutralizing and the temperature of the solution rises 5.0°C. Density of the solution is 2.0g/mL and specific heat of the solution is 4.184 J/g°C.','83680 J','Total volume of solution =1000+1000=2000 mL.\nThe density of the solution is 2 g/mL\nTotal mass of solution = Density × volume =2.0  g/mL ×2000 mL = 4000 g.\nApproximate energy released q= mass × specific heat of the solution × temperature change\nq=4000×4.184×5.0 = 83680 J\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 173; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','41840 J','21840 J','3184 J','83680 J','NEW','11-178','11-154',NULL),
(60652,'chemistry','What is the numerical value of enthalpy of neutralisation ? (in kJmol −1)','-57.3 kJ/mol','The reaction in which an acid and a base react to give a salt and water is called neutralization reaction.\nNeutralization reactions are exothermic in nature. The heat change when one gram equivalent of an\nacid is completely neutralised by a base or vice versa in dilute solution, is called heat of neutralization\nand it is −57.3kJmol −1 (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 173; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','-50.3 kJ/mol','-51.3 kJ/mol','-57.9 kJ/mol','-57.3 kJ/mol','NEW','11-178','11-154',NULL),
(60653,'chemistry','The lattice enthalpy of an ionic compound is the enthalpy change which occurs when one mole of an ionic compound dissociates into its ions in --------- state.','gaseous','The lattice enthalpy of an ionic compound is\nthe enthalpy change which occurs when one\nmole of an ionic compound dissociates into its\nions in gaseous state.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 173; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'1','fillup','ga,seo,us,li,qu,id',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-178','11-154',NULL),
(60654,'chemistry','Ionic compound is also called?','Solid compound','Ionic compound is also called solid compound.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Solid compound','Lattice compound','Liquid compound','Gaseous compound','NEW','11-178','11-154',NULL),
(60655,'chemistry','Which is correct formula to find lattice enthalpy?','∆Hf = S.E + I.E + D.E/2 + E.A + L.E','No direct method to find lattice enthalpy. Only Born Harber cycle to produce\nlattice energy.\n∆Hf = S.E + I.E + D.E/2 + E.A + L.E\nS.E = sublimation energy\nI.E = ionisation energy(loose electron)\nD.E = dissociation energy\nE.A = electron affinity(add electron)\nL.E = lattice energy\nfirstly change into gaseous state and then add and loose the electron.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 173; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','∆Hf = S.E + I.E + D.E/2 - E.A + L.E','∆Hf = S.E + I.E - D.E/2 - E.A - L.E','∆Hf = S.E - I.E + D.E/2 + E.A + L.E','∆Hf = S.E + I.E + D.E/2 + E.A + L.E','NEW','11-179','11-155',NULL),
(60656,'chemistry','Ionization energy and electron affinity are defined at?','Absolute zero','Ionization energy and electron affinity are defined at absolute zero\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 174; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Absolute non zero','losing and gaining electon','Absolute zero','lattice energy','NEW','11-179','11-155',NULL),
(60657,'chemistry','Lattice energy is inversely proportional to','1/size','Lattice energy is directly proportional to 1/size.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','1/size','size','1/charge','Ionization energy','NEW','11-179','11-155',NULL),
(60658,'chemistry','Lattice energy is directly proportional to','charge','Lattice energy is directly proportional to charge .','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','size','charge','Ionization energy','1/charge','NEW','11-179','11-155',NULL),
(60659,'chemistry','Calculate the lattice enthalpy of MgO from the following data at standard states- Enthalpy of sublimation of Mg = 15KJ/mole Ionisation energy of Mg⁺ = 737.7KJ/mole Ionisation energy of Mg⁺²= 1450.7KJ/mole \n Enthalpy of dissociation of Oxygen = 498KJ/ mole Electron gain enthalpy of O⁻= -142KJ/mole Electron gain enthalpy of O⁻²= 774.4KJ/mole Enthalpy of formation of MgO = -601.7 KJ/mole','3686.5 KJ/mol','According to the born harber cycle\n∆Hf = S.E + I.E1 + I.E2 + D.E/2 + E.A1+ E.A2 + L.E\n-601.7= 15 + 737.7 + 1450.7 + 249 - 142 + 774.4+ L.E    (D.E/2 = 498/2 = 249)\n-601.7 = 3226.8 - 142 + L.E\n-601.7 = 3084.8+ L.E\nL.E = 3084.8 + 601.7  = 3686.5 kJ/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 174; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','-3787.5 KJ/mol','3787.5 KJ/mol','3686.5 KJ/mol','-3686.5 KJ/mol','NEW','11-179','11-155',NULL),
(60660,'chemistry','Calculate the ionisation energy of KCl from the following data at standard states- Enthalpy of sublimation of K = 89KJ/mole Enthalpy of dissociation of Cl = 242KJ/ mole Electron gain enthalpy of Cl-= -348KJ/mole Lattice enthalpy of KCl = 715 kJ/mol Enthalpy of formation of KCl = -487KJ/mole','1064 kJ/mol','According to the born harber cycle\n∆Hf = S.E + I.E + D.E/2 + E.A1+L.E\n-487= 89 + I.E + 121 - 348 + 715    (D.E/2 = 242/2 = 121)\n-487 = 925 - 348 - I.E\n-487 = 577 - I.E\nI.E = 577 + 487 = 1064 kJ/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 174; Paragraph No.6.5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','-1185 kJ/mol','1064 kJ/mol','1185 kJ/mol','-1064 kJ/mol','NEW','11-179','11-155',NULL),
(60661,'chemistry','If radius is incresed then lattice energy -----------.','decreased','If radius is incresed then lattice energy decreased.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'1','fillup','de,cre,as,ed,in,cre,as,ed',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-179','11-155',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(60662,'chemistry','Which is the correct increasing order of lattice energy, when we move from top to bottom for NaCl, KCl, CsCl and MgCl2','MgCl2 > NaCl > KCl > CsCl','First see the charge on that compound\nNa+ or Mg+2\nMg+2 > Na+\nand by size Na+ > k+ > Cs  ( L.E is inversely proportional to size)\nwe move from top to bottom in a group the lattice energy will be decreased due to decrease in bond strength and increase in bond\nlength.\ncharge will dominating factor so,\nMgCl2 has more lattice energy than NaCl, KCl, CsCl because charge will dominating factor\nMgCl2 > NaF','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','MgCl2 < NaCl > KCl > CsCl','MgCl2 > NaCl < KCl < CsCl','MgCl2 < NaCl < KCl < CsCl','MgCl2 > NaCl > KCl > CsCl','NEW','11-179','11-155',NULL),
(60663,'chemistry','Which is the correct increasing order of lattice energy when we move from top to bottom in periodic table for LiCl, NaCl, KCl, RbCl and CsCl is','LiCl > NaCl > KCl > RbCl > CsCl','we move from top to bottom in a group the lattice energy will be decreased due to decrease in bond strength and increase in bond\nlength.So the correct order of lattice energy is .CsCl<RbCl<KCl<NaCl<LiCl.\nHere charge is same in all compound  Li+ and Cl- , Na+ and Cl- , K+ and Cl- , Rb+ and Cl- , Cs+ and Cl-\nso we see the size of the compound of S group because Cl is present in all compound.\nLiCl>NaCl>KCl>RbCl>CsCl.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','LiCl > NaCl > KCl > RbCl > CsCl','LiCl < NaCl < KCl < RbCl < CsCl','LiCl > NaCl < KCl < RbCl < CsCl','LiCl < NaCl > KCl > RbCl > CsCl','NEW','11-179','11-155',NULL),
(60664,'chemistry','Which is the correct increasing increasing order of lattice energy when we move from top to bottom in group for NaCl, NaF, KCl and RbCl is','RbCl < KCl < NaCl < NaF','We know that, lattice energy decreases as size of the ions in the compound increases. So, the correct order is\nRbCl < KCl < NaCl < NaF','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','RbCl > KCl < NaCl < NaF','RbCl > KCl > NaCl > NaF','RbCl < KCl < NaCl < NaF','RbCl < KCl < NaCl > NaF','NEW','11-179','11-155',NULL),
(60665,'chemistry','Which is the correct increasing order of lattice energy when we move from left to right in period for Na₂O, MgO, Al₂O₃ is','Al₂O₃ > MgO > Na₂O','we move from left to right in a period the lattice energy will be increased due to increased in Charge.\nlength.So the correct order of lattice energy is Al₂O₃ > MgO > Na₂O\nHere charge is different in all compound of 3rd period  Al2(+3 charge), Mg(+2 charge) and Na(+1 charge)\nCharge is dominating factor, so lattice energy is\nAl₂O₃ > MgO > Na₂O','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Al₂O₃ > MgO < Na₂O','Al₂O₃ > MgO > Na₂O','Al₂O₃ < MgO < Na₂O','None of the above','NEW','11-179','11-155',NULL),
(60666,'chemistry','Water requires heat for --------- .','evaporation','Water requires heat for evaporation.(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 165; Paragraph No.6.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Enthalpies for different types of reactions',NULL,'1','fillup','ev,apo,rati,on,su,bli,mati,on',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-179','11-155',NULL),
(60926,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct for the spontaneous adsorption of a gas?','∆S is negative and therefore, ∆H should be highly negative','Using Gibb\'s helmholtz equation\nΔG=ΔH−TΔS  \nΔS=−ve for adsorption because there is decrease in entropy as there is decrease in randomness\ndue to association between adsorbate and adsorbent.\nAs it is spontaneous, ΔG is negative and therefore, ΔH should be highly negative.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6) (NEET 2014)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','∆S is negative and therefore, ∆H should be highly positive','∆S is negative and therefore, ∆H should be highly negative','∆S is positive and therefore, ∆H should be highly positive','∆S is positive and therefore, ∆H should be highly negative','NEW','11-178','11-154','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60926q.png'),
(60927,'chemistry','A reaction having equal energies of activation for forward and reverse reactions has :','∆H = 0','Change in enthalpy for a reaction equal to change in activation energies.\n∆H = (Ea)f - (Ea)b = 0 \nHere, (Ea)f  denotes the activation energy for forward rereactio\n(Ea)b denotes the activation energy for backward reaction\nAccording to question,\n(Ea)f = (Ea)b\nTherefore, \nΔH=(Ea)f − (Ea)b = 0 \n∆H = 0 (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 165; Paragraph No.6.4) (NEET 2013)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','∆H = 0','∆S = 0','∆H = ∆G = ∆S = 0','∆G = 0','NEW','11-165','11-139',NULL),
(60928,'chemistry','The enthalpy of fusion of water is 1.435kcal.mol. The molar entropy change for the melting of ice at 0°C is:','5.260 cal/ (mol K)','∆S = ∆H/T\n∆Sfus =∆Hfus/Tfus\n= 1.435 ×10^3/273 = 5.26 cal/(mol K)\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6) (NEET 2012)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','21.04 cal/ (mol K)','5.260 cal/ (mol K)','10.52 cal/ (mol K)','0.526cal/ (mol K)','NEW','11-178','11-154',NULL),
(60929,'chemistry','For a sample of perfect gas when its pressure is changed isothermally from pi​ to pf​, the entropy change is given by:','ΔS=nRln(Pi/Pf)','For an ideal gas undergoing reversible expansion, when temperature change from Ti to Tf and\npressure change from Pi to Pf,\n∆S = nCp lnTf/Ti + nR lnPi/Pf\nfor isothermal process, Ti to Tf, so ln = 0\n∆S = nR lnPi/Pf\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6) (NEET - II 2016)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','ΔS=nRln(Pf/Pi)','ΔS=nRln(Pi/Pf)','ΔS=nRTln(Pf/Pi)','ΔS=RTln(Pi/Pf)','NEW','11-178','11-154',NULL),
(60930,'chemistry','2 moles of ideal gas at 27°C temperature is expanded reversibly from 2L to 20L. Find entropy change: ( R = 2cal/mol K)','9.2','ΔS (Entropy change) = 2.303 n R log₁₀​V1​/V2​​\n=2.303×2×2×log10 20/2\n\n= ​2.303×2×2×1\n\n=9.212cal \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6) (NEET 2002)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','0','92.1','9.2','4','NEW','11-178','11-154','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/60930q.png'),
(60931,'chemistry','The densities of graphite and diamond at 298 K are 2.25 and 3.31gcm−3, respectively. If the standard free energy difference (ΔG°) is equal to 1895 J mol−1 the pressure at which graphite will be transformed into diamond at 298 K is:','9.92 ×10⁸ pa','∆G° = -P∆V = work done\n∆V = (12/3.31 - 12/2.25) ×10^-3L = -1.17 × 10^-3 L\nΔG=1895J/mol                           \n−(−1.71×10^−3)×P×101.3 \nP = 1895/1.71×10^−3×101.3\nP = 10.93 × 10³ atm\nP = 11.08 ×10⁸ pa ≌ 9.92 × 10⁸ pa.    (∵1atm = 101325 pa)\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6) (NEET 2003)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','9.92 ×10⁵ pa','9.92 ×10⁸ pa','9.92 ×10⁶ pa','9.92 ×10⁷pa','NEW','11-178','11-154',NULL),
(60932,'chemistry','What is the entropy change (in JK−1 mol−1) when one mole of ice is converted into water at 0°C ? (The enthalpy change for the conversion of ice to liquid water is 6.0 KJ mol−1 at 0°C)','21.98','S = qrev/T\n= 6000/273 = 21.978 j/k/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6) (NEET 2003)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','20.13','2.013','2.198','21.98','NEW','11-178','11-154',NULL),
(60933,'chemistry','Considering entropy(s) as a thermodynamic parameter, the criterion for the spontaneity of any process is:','∆Ssystem + ∆Ssurrounding > 0','Entropy (S) is the measure of randomness or disorder of the molecules. It is an extensive property and a state function.\nA process is spontaneous if and only if the entropy of the universe increases.\nTherefore, for spontaneous process,\nΔStotal > 0 or ΔSsystem + ΔSsurrounding > 0 \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 177; Paragraph No.6.6) (NEET 2004)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','∆Ssystem + ∆Ssurrounding > 0','∆Ssurrounding > 0 only','∆Ssystem > 0 only','∆Ssystem - ∆Ssurrounding > 0','NEW','11-177','11-153',NULL),
(60934,'chemistry','For the gas phase reaction PCl₅(g) ------- PCl₃(g) + Cl₂(g) Which of the following conditions are correct ?','∆H > 0 and ∆S > 0','Gas phase reaction\nPCl₅(g) -------- PCl₃(g) + Cl₂(g)\n∆H= ∆E+∆ngRT, ∆ng= difference in no. of moles of gaseous products and reactants.\nsince ∆ng is +ve, hence ∆H is +ve\n∆ng=1+1−1=1> 0, hence ∆H > 0 &  ∆S= Sproduct - Sreactant  > 0 \n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 177; Paragraph No.6.6) (NEET 2008)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','∆H < 0 and ∆S < 0','∆H > 0 and ∆S > 0','∆H = 0 and ∆S < 0','∆H > 0 and ∆S < 0','NEW','11-177','11-153',NULL),
(60935,'chemistry','The values of ΔH and ΔS for the reaction, C(graphite) + CO₂(g)→2CO(g) are 170 kJ and 170J/K respectively. This reaction will be spontaneous at;','1110 K','For a spontaneous reaction, ∆G= Negative\nAnd at equilibrium ∆G=0 i.e ∆H=T∆S \nT = 170 × 10³/170 = 1000K\n∆G= -ve only when T∆S > ∆H, which is possible only when  T = 1110 K\n∆G = 170 × 10³ - (1110 × 170) = 18700 kJ\nThen reaction is spontaneous at T = 1110 K\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6) (NEET 2009)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','910 K','1110 K','510 K','410 K','NEW','11-178','11-154',NULL),
(60936,'chemistry','For vaporization of water at 1 atmospheric pressure, the values of ΔH and ΔS are 40.63kJmol−1 and108.8 JK−1mol−1 respectively. The temperature when Gibb\'s energy change (ΔG) for this transformation will be zero, is :','373.4 K','According to Gibb\'s equation, ΔG = ΔH - TΔS when ΔG = 0, ΔH = TΔS \nΔH = 40.63 kJ mol−1= 40.63 x 10³Jmol−1, ΔS = 108.8 JK−1mol−1\nT = ∆H/∆S = 40.63 × 10³/108.8 = 373.43 K\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6) (Mains 2010)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','273.4 K','373.4 K','393.4 K','293.4 K','NEW','11-178','11-154',NULL),
(60937,'chemistry','Standard entropies of X₂, Y₂ and XY₃ are 60, 40 and 50J/K/mol respectively. For the reaction 1/2X₂ + 3/2Y₂ ------ XY₃, ∆H = -30kJ at equilibrium, the temperature should be','750 K','In reaction 1/2X₂ + 3/2Y₂ ------ XY₃\n∆S° = 50 - 1/2 ×60 + 3/2 × 40 = - 40 J/K/mol\nAt equilibrium ∆G° = 0, ∆H° = T∆S° \nT = ∆H°/∆S° = -30 × 10³ J/mol/-40 J/k/mol\n=750 K\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6) (NEET 2010)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','750 K','1000 K','510 K','1250 K','NEW','11-178','11-154',NULL),
(60938,'chemistry','If the enthalpy change for the transition of liquid water steam is 30 kJmol−1 at 27∘C, the entropy change for the process would be :','100 J/mol/K','ΔG = ΔH - TΔS\n0 = ∆H - T∆S (∆G = 0 as transition of H2O(l) to H2O(v) is at equilibrium\n∆S = ∆H/T = 30 ×10³/300 = 100 J/mol/K\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6)(NEET 2011)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','10 J/mol/K','1.0 J/mol/K','100 J/mol/K','0.1 J/mol/K','NEW','11-178','11-154',NULL),
(60939,'chemistry','The enthalpy of fusion of water is 1.435kcal.mol. The molar entropy change for the melting of ice at 0°C is:','5.260cal/(mol K)','ΔS=ΔH/T\n∴ The molar entropy change for the melting of ice =  \n1.435/ 0+273 =5.26 × 10^-3 kcal/mol\n= 5.26 cal/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6)(NEET 2012)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','0.5260cal/(mol K)','21.04cal/(mol K)','10.52cal/(mol K)','5.260cal/(mol K)','NEW','11-178','11-154',NULL),
(60940,'chemistry','A reaction having equal energies of activation for forward and reverse reactions has','∆H = 0','∆H = (Ea)f - (Ea)b\nAccording to the question (Ea)f = (Ea)b\n∆H = 0 (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)(NEET 2013)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','∆H = 0','∆S = 0','∆G = 0','∆H = ∆S = ∆S = 0','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(60941,'chemistry','For a given reaction, ΔH=35.5kJmol⁻¹and ΔS=83.6kJmol⁻¹. The reaction is spontaneous at: (Assume that ΔH and ΔS do not vary with temperature)','T > 425 K','For a spontaneous reaction\n∆G < 0 i.e., ∆H - T∆S < 0, T > ∆H/∆S \nT > (35.5 ×1000/83.6) = 424.6 = 425 K\nT > 425K   (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 178; Paragraph No.6.6)(NEET 2017)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','all temperatures','T > 425 K','T > 298 K','T < 425 K','NEW','11-178','11-154',NULL),
(60942,'chemistry','Given that bond energies of H−H and Cl−Cl are 430 kJ mol⁻¹and 240 kJ mol⁻¹ respectively and ΔfH for HCl is −90 kJ mol⁻¹. Bond enthalpy of HCl is :','425 kJ/mol','1/2H₂ + 1/2Cl₂ = HCl\n∆rH = ∆rHr + ∆rHp - B.E.HCl\n-90 = 1/2 ×430 + 1/2 × 220 - B..E. HCl\nB.E. HCl = 215+120+901 = 425 kJ/mol\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 160; Paragraph No.6.2.2) (NEET 2007)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','425 kJ/mol','290 kJ/mol','245 kJ/mol','390 kJ/mol','NEW','11-160','11-136',NULL),
(60943,'chemistry','When 1 mole of a gas is heated at constant 1 volume, temperature is raised from 298 K to 308 K. Heat supplied to the gas is 500 J. Then, which statement is correct?','q = ∆E = 500, w = 0','ΔH=ΔE+PΔV\nWhen ΔV=0;w=0.\nAs ΔE=q+w,ΔE=q\nΔH=500 J, \nΔH=ΔE=500 J,q=500J,w=0.     (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)(NEET 2001)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','q = -w = 500, ∆E = 0','q = w = 500, ∆E = 0','q = ∆E = 500, w = 0','∆E = 0, q = w = -500','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(60944,'chemistry','For the reaction C₂H₅OH(l) + 3O₂(g) ---- 2CO₂(g) + 3H₂O(l) which one is true?','∆H = ∆E - RT','ΔH=ΔE+PΔV\nPV = nRT\nP∆V = ∆ngRT , ∆ng = 2 - 3 = -1\n∆H = ∆E + ∆ngRT \n∆H = ∆E - RT\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)(NEET 2000)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','∆H = ∆E - 2RT','∆H = ∆E - RT','∆H = ∆E + 2RT','∆H = ∆E + RT','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(60945,'chemistry','During an isothermal expansion of an ideal gas, its:','enthalpy remains unaffected','An isothermal process is change of a system in which the temprature remains constant. For an ideal gas\nduring an isothermal expansion the enthalpy, as well as internal energy, remains constant.\nDuring isothermal expansion of an ideal gas, ΔE = 0, ΔT=0 \nΔH=ΔE+Δ(PV) = ΔE + nRΔT ( PV = nR∆T)\n∆H = 0 + 0 = 0 \nchange in enthalpy is zero, means enthalpy remains same or unaffected.\n(Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 161; Paragraph No.6.2.2)(NEET 1994, 1991)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','internal energy increases','enthalpy decreases','enthalpy remains unaffected','enthalpy reduce to zero','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(60946,'chemistry','When 5 litres of a gas mixture of methane and propane is perfectly combusted at 0°C and 1 atmosphere, 16 litres of oxygen at the same temperature and pressure is consumed. The amount of heat released from this combustion in kJ (∆Hcomb (CH4) = 890 kJ mol–1, ∆Hcomb. (C3H8) = 2220 kJ mol–1) is','317','CH4 + 2O2 → CO2 + 2H2O\n \n C3H8 + 5O2 → 3CO2 + 4H2O\n \n No. of moles in gaseous mixture\n \n CH4 + C3H8 = 5/22.4= 0.22 moles\n \n \n \n No. of moles of O2 = 16/22.4= 0.71 moles\n \n \n \n Let x moles of CH4 is there in a gaseous mixture so, number of moles of C3H8 would be 0.22 – x. Then moles of O2 consumed,\n \n 2x + (0.22 – x)5 = 0.71 or x = 0.13\n \n Total amount of heat liberated = 0.13 × 890 + 0.09 × 2220 = 315.5 kJ\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 166 paragraph 3 NEET 2013)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','38','317','477','32','NEW','11-166','11-140',NULL),
(60947,'chemistry','The heat of combustion of carbon to CO2 is–393.5 kJ/mol. The heat released upon formation of 35.2 g of CO2 from carbon and oxygen gas is','– 315 kJ','C(s)+ O2(g) CO2(g), ∆H = – 393.5 kJ/mol⇒ Amount of heat released on formation of 44 g CO2 = 393.5 kJ\\ Amount of heat released on formation of 35.2 g of CO2 = - 393.5/44*35 2 . . = -314.8\n \n\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 167 paragraph 4 NEET 2015)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','315 kJ','– 3.15 kJ','– 630 kJ','– 315 kJ','NEW','11-167','11-141',NULL),
(60948,'chemistry','If enthalpies of formation for C2H4(g), CO2(g)andH2O(l)at 25°C and 1 atm pressure are 52, – 394 and – 286 kJ/mol respectively, then enthalpy of combustion of C2H4(g) will be','– 1412 kJ/mol','C2H4 + 3O2 → 2 CO2 + 2H2O\n \n ∆H° = ∆H°products – ∆H°reactants = 2 × (– 394) + 2 × (– 286) – (52 + 0) = – 1412 kJ/mol\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 170 paragraph 4 NEET 1995)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','141.2 kJ/mol','– 141.2 kJ/mol','1412 kJ/mol','– 1412 kJ/mol','NEW','11-170','11-144',NULL),
(60949,'chemistry','Given that \nC + O2 → CO2, ∆H° = – x kJ \n2CO + O2 → 2CO2, ∆H° = – y kJ\n \n The enthalpy of formation of carbon monoxide will be','(y − 2x)/2','C(s)+ O2(g) → CO2(g) ∆H° = – x kJ\n \n CO(g)+1/2O2(g) → CO2(g)∆H° = −y/2 kJ\n \n By subtracting equation (ii) from (i) we get,\n \n ∆H =-x-(-y/2)=(y-2x)/2\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 170 paragraph 4 NEET 1997)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','(y − 2x)/2','2x – y','y – 2x','(2x – y)/2','NEW','11-170','11-144',NULL),
(60950,'chemistry','In the reaction : \n\nS + 3/2O2 → SO3 + 2x kcal \n\nSO2 + 1/2 O2 → SO3 + y kcal, the heat of formation of SO2 is','2x – y','S + 3/2O2 → SO3 + 2x kcal\n \n SO2 +1/2O2 → SO3 + y kcal\n \n S + O2 → SO2 + (2x – y) kcal\n \n The heat of formation of SO2 is (2x – y) kcal/mole.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 173 paragraph 1 NEET 1999)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','x+y','2x – y','x-y','2x+y','NEW','11-173','11-149',NULL),
(60951,'chemistry','Enthalpy of CH4 + 1/2 O2 → CH3OH is negative. If enthalpy of combustion of CH4 and CH3OH are x and y respectively, then which relation is correct?','x > y','CH4 + 2O2 → CO2 + 2H2O, ∆H1 = –x\n \n CH3OH + 3/2O2 → CO2 + 2H2O, ∆H2 = –y\n \n CH4 +1/2O2 → CH3OH, ∆H3 = –ve\n \n –x – (–y) = –ve\n \n y – x = – ve. Hence, x > y.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 170 paragraph 4 NEET 2001)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','x > y','x = y','x < y','x ≥ y','NEW','11-170','11-144',NULL),
(60952,'chemistry','Heat of combustion ∆H for C(s) , H2(g) and CH4(g) are –94, –68 and –213 kcal/mol, then ∆H for C(s) + 2H2(g) → CH4(g) Is','–17 kcal','∆Hiv = ∆Hi+ ∆Hii – ∆Hiii= (–94 – 68 × 2 + 213) kcal = –17 kcal\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 170 paragraph 4 NEET 2002)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','–17 kcal','–170 kcal','–111 kcal','–85 kcal','NEW','11-170','11-144',NULL),
(60953,'chemistry','In an endothermic reaction, the value of ∆H is','POSITIVE','In endothermic reactions, energy of reactants is less than energy of products. Thus, ER < EP.\n \n ∆H = Ep– Er = +ve\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 167 paragraph 4 NEET 1999)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','POSITIVE','NEGATIVE','ZERO','CONSTANT','NEW','11-167','11-141',NULL),
(60954,'chemistry','Assume each reaction is carried out in an opencontainer. For which reaction will ∆H = ∆E?','H2(g)+ Br2(g) → 2HBr(g)','∆H = ∆E + ∆ngRT\n \n For H2(g)+ Br2(g) → 2HBr(g)\n \n ∆ng= 2 – (1 + 1) = 0. i.e. ∆H = ∆E\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 168 paragraph 1 NEET 2006)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','2CO(g)+ O2(g) → 2CO2(g)','H2(g)+ Br2(g) → 2HBr(g)','C(s)+ 2H2O(g) → 2H2(g)+ CO2(g)','PCl5(g) → PCl3(g)+ Cl2(g)','NEW','11-168','11-142',NULL),
(60955,'chemistry','Under isothermal conditions, a gas at 300 K expands from 0.1 L to 0.25 L against a constant external pressure of 2 bar. The work done by the gas is\n \n [Given that 1 L bar = 100 J]','-30J','Expansion of a gas against a constant external pressure is an irreversible process. The work done in an irreversible process\n \n = – Pext ∆V = – Pext (V2 – V1) = –2 (0.25 – 0.1)\n \n = – 2 × 0.15 L bar = – 0.30 × 100 J = – 30 J\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 167 paragraph 1 NEET 2019)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','30J','-30J','5J','25J','NEW','11-167','11-141',NULL),
(60956,'chemistry','For which one of the following equations is ∆H°reaction equal to ∆H°f for the product?','Xe(g)+ 2F2(g) → XeF4(g)','For (c), ∆H°reaction= ∆H°f(XeF4) – [∆H°f(Xe) + 2∆H°f(F2)]\n \n Enthalpies of formation of elementary substances Xe and F2 are taken as zero.\n \n Thus, ∆H°reaction = ∆H°f(XeF4)\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 173 paragraph 1 NEET 2003)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','N2(g)+ O3(g) → N2O3(g)','CH4(g)+ 2Cl2(g) → CH2Cl2(l) + 2HCl(g)','Xe(g)+ 2F2(g) → XeF4(g)','2CO(g)+ O2(g) → 2CO2(g)','NEW','11-173','11-149',NULL),
(60957,'chemistry','The correct option for free expansion of an ideal gas under adiabatic condition is','q = 0, ∆T = 0 and w = 0','For free expansion of an ideal gas, Pex = 0,w = –Pex∆V = 0\n \n For adiabatic process, q = 0 According to first law of thermodynamics, ∆U = q + w = 0\n \n As internal energy of an ideal gas is a function of\n \n temperature, ∆U = 0, ∆T = 0\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 167 paragraph 2 NEET 2020)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','q = 0, ∆T = 0 and w = 0','q = 0, ∆T < 0 and w > 0','q < 0, ∆T = 0 and w = 0','q > 0, ∆T > 0 and w > 0','NEW','11-167','11-141',NULL),
(60958,'chemistry','Which of the following is the correct equation?','∆U = ∆W - ∆Q','This is the mathematical relation of first law of thermodynamics. Here ∆U = change in internal energy; ∆Q = heat absorbed by the system and W = work done by the system.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 164 paragraph 5 NEET 1996)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','∆U = ∆W + ∆Q','∆U = ∆W - ∆Q','∆W = ∆DU + ∆Q','None of these','NEW','11-164','11-138',NULL),
(60959,'chemistry','In a closed insulated container a liquid is stirred with a paddle to increase the temperature, which of the following is true?','∆E = W ≠ 0, q = 0','The mathematical form of first law of thermodynamics : q = ∆E + W\n \n Since the system is closed and insulated, q = 0\n \n Paddle work is done on system. W ≠ 0.\n \n Temperature and hence internal energy of the system increases. ∆E ≠ 0.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 164 paragraph 5 NEET 2002)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','∆E = W ≠ 0, q = 0','∆E = W = q ≠ 0','∆E = 0, W = q ≠ 0','W = 0, ∆E = q ≠ 0','NEW','11-164','11-138',NULL),
(60960,'chemistry','Which of the following are not state functions?\n \n (I) q + w (II) q\n \n (III) w (IV) H – TS','(II) and (III)','State functions or state variables are those which depend only on the state of the system and not on how the state was reached.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 162 paragraph 3 NEET 2008)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','(I), (II) and (III)','(I) and (IV)','(II) and (III)','(II), (III) and (IV)','NEW','11-162','11-138',NULL),
(60961,'chemistry','Enthalpy change for the reaction,4H(g) → 2H2(g) is –869.6 kJ\n \n The dissociation energy of H – H bond is','434.8 kJ','The dissociation energy of H – H bond is\n \n 869.6/2=434 8 kJ\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 177 paragraph 3 NEET 2011)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','– 434.8 kJ','– 869.6 kJ','434.8 kJ','217.4 kJ','NEW','11-177','11-153',NULL),
(60962,'chemistry','Bond dissociation enthalpy of H2, Cl2 and HCl are 434, 242 and 431 kJ mol–1 respectively. Enthalpy of formation of HCl is','–93 kJ mol–1','For the given reaction, enthalpy of reaction can be calculated as\n \n ∆Hreaction = Σ(B.E)reactants – Σ(B.E)products= [B.E.(C=C) + B.E.(H–H) + 4 × B.E.(C–H)]– [B.E.(C–C) + 6 × B.E.(C–H)]= –120.02 kJ mol–1\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 171paragraph 2NEET 2008)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','–93 kJ mol–1','245 kJ mol–1','93 kJ mol–1','–245 kJ mol–1','NEW','11-171','11-145',NULL),
(60964,'chemistry','Consider the following reactions :\n \n (i) H(aq)+OH(aq)= H2O (l)∆H = –X1 kJ mol–1\n \n (ii) H2(g)+ 1/2O2(g)= H2O(l), ∆H = –X2 kJ mol–1\n \n (iii)CO2(g)+ H2(g)= CO(g)+ H2O(l),∆H = –X3 kJ mol–1\n \n (iv) C2H2(g)+ 5/2O2(g)= 2CO2(g)+ H2O(l),∆H = +X4 kJ mol–1\n \n Enthalpy of formation of H2O(l) is','–X2 kJ mol–1.','The amount of heat absorbed or releasedwhen 1 mole of a substance is directly obtained from its constituent elements is called the heat of formation or enthalpy of formation. Equation (i) represents neutralisation reaction, (iii) represents hydrogenation reaction and (iv) represents combustion reaction. Thus, enthalpy of formation of H2O(l) is –X2 kJ mol–1.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 174 paragraph 1 NEET 2007)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','X3 kJ mol–1','–X4 kJ mol–1','X1 kJ mol–1','–X2 kJ mol–1.','NEW','11-174','11-150',NULL),
(60965,'chemistry','If the bond energies of H–H, Br–Br, and H–Br are 433, 192 and 364 kJ mol–1 respectively, the ∆H° for the reaction H2(g)+ Br2(g) → 2HBr(g) is','–103 kJ','H – H + Br – Br → 2H – Br\n \n energy absorbed 433 + 192= 625\n \n energy reliesed 2 × 364 = 728\n \n Net energy released = 728 – 625 = 103 kJ\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 177 paragraph 2 NEET 2004)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','–261 kJ','103 kJ','261 kJ','–103 kJ','NEW','11-177','11-153',NULL),
(60966,'chemistry','For the reaction, 2Cl(g) → Cl2(g), the correct option is','∆rH < 0 and ∆rS < 0','In the reaction, 2Cl(g) Cl2(g), the randomness decreases as 2 moles of Cl(g) are converted to 1 mole of Cl2(g) , thus, ∆rS < 0.And this is an exothermic reaction, thus, ∆rH < 0.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 183 paragraph 1NEET 2020)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','∆rH > 0 and ∆rS > 0','∆rH > 0 and ∆rS < 0','∆rH < 0 and ∆rS > 0','∆rH < 0 and ∆rS < 0','NEW','11-183','11-159',NULL),
(60967,'chemistry','In which case change in entropy is negative?','2H(g) → H2(g)','If ∆ng< 0 then ∆S < 0\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 184 paragraph 2 NEET 2019)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','2H(g) → H2(g)','Evaporation of water','Expansion of a gas at constant temperature','Sublimation of solid to gas','NEW','11-184','11-160',NULL),
(60968,'chemistry','In which of the following reactions, standarreaction entropy change (∆S°) is positive and standard Gibbs energy change (∆G°) decreases sharply with increasing temperature?','C(graphite) +1/2O2(g) → CO(g)','C(graphite) +1/2O2(g) → CO(g)\n \n ∆ng = 1-1/2=1/2\n \n As amount of gaseous substance is increasing in the product side thus, ∆S is positive for this reaction.And we know that ∆G = ∆H – T∆S As ∆S is positive, thus increase in temperature will make the term (–T∆S) more negative and ∆G will decrease.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 184 paragraph 2 NEET 2012)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','C(graphite) +1/2O2(g) → CO(g)','C(g) +1/2O2(g) → CO(g)','C(graphite) +1/2O2(g) →1/2 CO2(g)','Mg(s)+1/2O2(g) → MgO(s)','NEW','11-184','11-160',NULL),
(60969,'chemistry','If the enthalpy change for the transition of liquid water to steam is 30 kJ mol–1 at 27°C, the entropy change for the process would be','100 J mol–1 K–1','We know that ∆G = ∆H – T∆S\n \n 0 = ∆H – T∆S\n \n ∆ S=∆ H/T = (30*10^3)/300=100J mol -1K-1\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 184 paragraph 2 NEET 2011)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','10 J mol–1 K–1','1.0 J mol–1 K–1','0.1 J mol–1 K–1','100 J mol–1 K–1','NEW','11-184','11-160',NULL),
(60970,'chemistry','Identify the correct statement for change of Gibbs’ energy for a system (∆Gsystem) at constant temperature and pressure.','If ∆Gsystem = 0, the system has attained equilibrium.','The criteria for spontaneity of a process in terms of ∆G is as follows :\n \n • If ∆G is negative, the process is spontaneous.\n \n • If ∆G is positive, the process does not occur in the forward direction. It may occur in the backward direction.\n \n • If ∆G is zero, the system is in equilibrium.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 184 paragraph 2 NEET 2006)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','If ∆Gsystem < 0, the process is not spontaneous.','If ∆Gsystem > 0, the process is spontaneous.','If ∆Gsystem = 0, the system has attained equilibrium.','If ∆Gsystem = 0, the system is still moving in a particular direction.','NEW','11-184','11-160',NULL),
(60971,'chemistry','The enthalpy and entropy change for the reaction: Br2(l)+ Cl2(g) → 2BrCl(g) are 30 kJ mol–1 and 105 J K–1 mol–1 respectively.\n \n The temperature at which the reaction will be in equilibrium is','285.7 K','∆ S=∆ H/T\n \n 105=(30/T)*1000 T= (30*1000)/105=285.7 K\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 183 paragraph 1 NEET 2006)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','300 K','285.7 K','273 K','450 K','NEW','11-183','11-159',NULL),
(60972,'chemistry','Which of the following pairs of a chemical reaction is certain to result in a spontaneous reaction?','Exothermic and increasing disorder','For spontaneous reaction, ∆ H = –ve, ∆ S = +ve. Spontaneity depends upon both critical minimum energy and maximum randomness disorderness.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 183 paragraph 1 NEET 2005)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Exothermic and increasing disorder','Exothermic and decreasing disorder','Endothermic and increasing disorder','Endothermic and decreasing disorder','NEW','11-183','11-159',NULL),
(60973,'chemistry','A reaction occurs spontaneously if','T∆S = ∆H and both ∆H and ∆S are +ve','∆G = ∆H – T∆S\n \n ∆G = –ve for spontaneous reaction.\n \n When ∆S = +ve, ∆H = +ve and T∆S > ∆H ⇒ ∆G = –ve\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 184 paragraph 2 NEET 2005)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','T∆S < ∆H and both ∆H and ∆S are +ve','T∆S > ∆H and ∆H is +ve and ∆S is –ve','T∆S > ∆H and both ∆H and ∆S are +ve','T∆S = ∆H and both ∆H and ∆S are +ve','NEW','11-184','11-160',NULL),
(60974,'chemistry','Unit of entropy is','J K–1 mol–1','Entropy change (∆S) is given by ∆S=qrev/T=[J K–1mol–1]\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 183 paragraph 1 NEET 2002)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','J K–1 mol–1','J mol–1','J–1K–1 mol–1','J K mol–1','NEW','11-184','11-160',NULL),
(60975,'chemistry','Identify the correct statement regarding entropy.','At absolute zero of temperature, entropy of a perfectly crystalline substance is taken to be zero.','The entropy of a substance increases with increase in temperature. However at absolute zero the entropy of a perfectly crystalline substance is taken as zero, which is also called as third law of thermodynamics.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 183 paragraph 1 NEET 1998)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','At absolute zero of temperature, the entropy ofall crystalline substances is taken to be zero.','At absolute zero of temperature, the entropy of a perfectly crystalline substance is +ve.','At absolute zero of temperature, entropy of a perfectly crystalline substance is taken to be zero.','At 0°C, the entropy of a perfectly crystalline substance is taken to be zero.','NEW','11-183','11-159',NULL),
(60976,'chemistry','If a system expands under adiabetic conditions then','Delta E decreases',NULL,'Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','temprature Increases','Delta E decreases','Delta E increases','None of the above','NEW','11-184','11-160',NULL),
(60977,'chemistry','The work done when 1 mole of gas expands reversibly and isothermally from a pressure of 5atm to 1 atm at 300 K is\nGiven ( log 5= 0.6989 and R= 8.314 Jk-1mol-1)','-4014.6',NULL,'Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','0 J','150J','4014.6','-4014.6','NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(60978,'chemistry','Which amongst the folloowing options is a correct relatrion between enthalpy change and change in internal energy\nA- delta H + delta U = delta nR\nB- delta H = delta U - delta ngRT\nC- delta H = delta U + delta ngRT\nD- delta H - Delta U = -delta ngRT','3',NULL,'Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-180','11-156',NULL),
(60979,'chemistry','Which of the following is true for a reaction in which all the reactant and products are liquid\n1- Delta H = Delta E\n2- Delta H= Delta W\n3- Delta H > Delta E\n4- None of the above','1',NULL,'Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-183','11-159',NULL),
(60980,'chemistry','Given the following reaction:\n4H----> 2H2\nThe enthalpy change for the reaction is -869.6KJ. The dissocioation energy for H-H bond is','+434.8KJ',NULL,'Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','-869.6KJ','+434.8KJ','+217.4KJ','-434.8KJ','NEW','11-174','11-150',NULL),
(60981,'chemistry','If for a certain reaction  ∆rH is 30KJmil-1 at 450K the value of  ∆rS(in Jk-1Mol-1) for whuich the same reaction will be spontaneous at the same temprature is','70',NULL,'Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','70','-33','33','-70','NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(60982,'chemistry','At 27. C latent heat of fusion of a compound is 2930 J/mol entropy change is','9.77J/mol K',NULL,'Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','9.77 J/mol K','10.77 J/mol K','9.07 J/mol K','0.977 J/mol K','NEW','11-182','11-158',NULL),
(61032,'chemistry','Select the incorrect statement:','∆rH° (electron gain enthalpy) =  A( electron affinity) – 5/2 RT','The value of Cp for each species (ionization and electron gain) is 5/2 R (CV = 3/2R) \n \n So, ∆rCp°= + 5/2 R (for ionization) \n \n ∆rCp° = – 5/2 R (for electron gain) \n \n Therefore, \n \n ∆rH° (ionization enthalpy)= E0 (ionization energy) + 5/2 RT \n \n ∆rH° (electron gain enthalpy) = – A( electron affinity) – 5/2 RT\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 179 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cp= + 5/2 R (for ionization enthalpy)','Cp = – 5/2 R (for electron gain enthalpy)','∆rH° (ionization enthalpy)= E0 (ionization energy) + 5/2 RT','∆rH° (electron gain enthalpy) =  A( electron affinity) – 5/2 RT','NEW','11-179','11-155',NULL),
(61033,'chemistry','Born-Haber cycle is used to determine:\n \n a)ionization enthalpy\n \n b)disociation enthalpy\n \n c)lattice energy','c only','Since it is impossible to determine lattice enthalpies directly by experiment, we use an indirect method where we construct an enthalpy diagram called a Born-Haber Cycle.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 179 paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b,c','b only','both b and c','c only','NEW','11-179','11-155',NULL),
(61034,'chemistry','In a tank of const volume,there is only air at the pressure of 4bar an the the temperature of 10 °C.How much does the air pressure change if the temperature (in degrees Celsius) rises by 50%?','7 kPa','p2/p1=T2/T1 p2=p1*(T2/T1)= 4.07bar\n \n t2= t1+0.5t1=15°C T2= t2+273.15K=288.15K\n \n \n \n ▲p = p2-p1=0,07 bar = 7kPa\n \n (Reference: NCERT chemistry class 11; Page 166; Paragraph No.6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','2000 Pa','7 kPa','10 kPa','1000 J/K','NEW','11-166','11-140',NULL),
(61035,'chemistry','Choose right statemant for the entalpy change of this reaction : CH4 (g) + Cl2 (g) = CHCl3 (t) + HCl(g), if we know enthalpy changes :\n \n 1) CH4 (g) + 2O2 (g) = CO2 (g) + 2H2O (t) ∆H1 = -920,04 kJ\n \n 2) CHCl3 (t) + 3/2 O2(g) = CO2 (g) + H2O (t) + HCl (g) ∆H2 = -686,50 kJ\n \n 3) H2 (g) + 1/2 O2 (g) = H2O (t) ∆H3 = -285,99 kJ\n \n                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     \n \n \n \n \n a) enthalpy is positive\n \n b)enthalpy is negative \n \n c) ∆H<920,04 kJ\n \n d)∆H>-686.15kJ','a,d','If we subtract the secound and the third from the first reaction,and after that we double the forth one and add it to the first also,we are going to get the reaction we are looking the enthalpy change for. So based on the Hesse\'s law we get : \n \n ∆H = ∆H1- ∆H2 - ∆H3+2∆H4 = -132.23 kJ\n \n (Reference: NCERT class 11 page 175 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,d','a,b','b,c','a,c','NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(61036,'chemistry','Choos correct statemant about the enthalpy change that is heppening when 1 mol of NH3 reacts with 1mol of HCl and then make NH4Cl , with enthalpy change of 176,43kJ,enthalpies of NH3 and HCl are -46,63 kJ/mol and -91,25 kJ/mol respectfully.\n \n \n \n a)is positive \n \n b)is negative\n \n c)∆ H1 < -176,23 kJ \n \n d) ∆ H1 > +176,23 kJ','b,c','1) NH3 (g) + HCl (g) → NH4Cl (g) ∆ H1 = -176,23 kJ\n \n 2) 1/2N2 (g) + 3/2H2 (g) → NH3 (g) ∆H2 = -45,63 kJ\n \n 3) 1/2H2 (g) + 1/2Cl2 (g) → HCl (g) ∆H2 = -91,25 kJ\n \n 1/2N2 (g) +2H2 + 1/2Cl2 (g) → NH4Cl \n \n ∆H = ∆H1 + ∆H2 + ∆H3 = -313.11 kJ/mol \n \n (Reference: NCERT class 11 page 175 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,d','a,b','b,c','a,c','NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(61037,'chemistry','Choose the right statmenat about the standard enthalpy of formation N2O,if we have standard enthalpy of formation of gaseus CO2 (-393,5kJ/mol),and standard enthalpy of overall reaction (-557,5 kJ/mol)','82 kJ','C(grafite) + 2 N2O(g) → CO2(g) + 2 N2(g)\n \n ΔHr ˚ = (ΔHf ˚(CO2(g)) + 2 · ΔHf ˚(N2(g))) – (ΔHf ˚(C(grafit)) + 2 · ΔHf ˚(N2O(g)))\n \n \n \n ΔHr˚ = ΔHf˚(CO2(g)) – 2 · ΔHf˚(N2O(g))\n \n ΔHf˚(N2O(g))= (ΔHf˚(CO2(g)) – ΔHr˚)/2=(-393,5-(-557,5))/2 = 82 kJ/mol\n \n (Reference: NCERT class 11 page 173 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','-802.2 kJ/mol','200.8 kJ','3kJ','82 kJ','NEW','11-173','11-149',NULL),
(61038,'chemistry','Choose the right statemant about the standard enthalpy change of burning CH4,if there are fiven standard enthalpy of formation of CO2(-393,5kJ/mol),H2O(= - 241,8 kJ/mol),CH4(- 74,9 kJ/mol):','-802.2 kJ/mol','CH4(g) + 2 O2(g) → CO2(g) + 2 H2O(g)\n \n ΔHr˚ = (ΔHf˚(CO2(g)) + 2 · ΔHf˚(H2O(g))) – (ΔHf˚(CH4(g)) + 2 · ΔHf˚(O2(g)))\n \n standard enthalpy of formation of chemical elements is always 0 , ΔHf˚(O2(g))=0:\n \n \n \n ΔHr˚=(-393,5 +2 · (-241,8)) – (-74,9)=-802,2 kJ/mol\n \n (Reference: NCERT class 11 page 173 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','-802.2 kJ/mol','200.8 kJ','3kJ','5.7 kJ','NEW','11-173','11-149',NULL),
(61039,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true? about standard enthalpy change of termic dissociation of NH4Cl,if there are given standard enthalpies of formation NH4Cl(-315,6 kJ/mol),NH3(- 46,0 kJ/mol),HC(- 92,5 kJ/mol) :\n \n a)ΔHr˚ = (ΔHf˚(NH3(g)) + ΔHf˚(HCl(g))) + ΔHf˚(NH4Cl(s))\n \n b)ΔHr˚ = (ΔHf˚(NH3(g)) + ΔHf˚(HCl(g))) – ΔHf˚(NH4Cl(s))\n \n c)ΔHr˚ =176,6 kJ/mol\n \n d)ΔHr˚ =-454,6 kJ/mol','b,c','NH4Cl(s) → NH3(g) + HCl(g)\n \n ΔHr˚ = (ΔHf˚(NH3(g)) + ΔHf˚(HCl(g))) – ΔHf˚(NH4Cl(s))=176,6 kJ/mol\n \n (Reference: NCERT class 11 page 170 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,d','a,b','b,c','a,c','NEW','11-180','11-156',NULL),
(61040,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\n \n \n \n A:Reaction of dissolving HCl in water is exothermic.\n \n R:Standard enthalpy change for this reaction is ΔHr˚ <0:','Both statement A and statement R are true and R is a correct explanation of statement A','HCl(g) + aq → HCl(aq)\n \n ΔHr˚ = (ΔHf˚(HCl(aq)) – ΔHf˚(HCl(g))\n \n ΔHr˚=-74,9 kJ/mol\n \n (Reference: NCERT class 11 page 180 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both statement A and statement R are true and R is a correct explanation of statement A','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','11-169','11-143',NULL),
(61041,'chemistry','How much heat is need to be add so the temperature of 14.4g of wather rises form 18°C to 60°C?(p=const Cp(H2O) = 4186 J/ K*kg)','2.5MJ','q= Cp· ΔT · m\n \n ΔT=T2 – T1= (60 + 273,15) – (18 + 273,15) = 333,15 – 291,15 = 42 K\n \n q = 4186 [J/K∙kg] · 42 [K] · 14.4 [kg] ≈ 2.5 MJ\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 169 paragraph 1st)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','200 kJ','300kJ','500 MJ','2.5MJ','NEW','11-169','11-143',NULL),
(61042,'chemistry','How much heat is need to be add so the temperature of 125 mol of wather rises form 20°C to 60°C?(p=const Cp(H2O) = 75.3 J/ K*kg)','376.5 kJ','q = Cp · ΔT · n\n \n ΔT=T2 – T1= (60 + 273,15) – (20 + 273,15) = 60 – 20 = 40 K\n \n Q = 75,3 [J/K∙mol] · 40 [K] · 125 [mol]= 376,5 kJ\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 169 paragraph 1st)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','200 kJ','300kJ','500 kJ','376.5 kJ','NEW','11-183','11-159',NULL),
(61043,'chemistry','In which pair the first substance has higher entropy?\n \n a) 1. 1mol Ar(g) at 25 K  ;2. 1mol Ar(g) at 20 K\n \n b) 1. 1mol H2O(g) at 8°C ; 2. 1mol H2O(l) at 8°C\n \n c) 1. 1mol HCL(g) at 25K ; 2. 1mol CCl4(g) at 25K\n \n d)1. 1mol of He(g) at 35K ; 2. 1mol of He(g) at 42K','a,b','Entropy is higher if its higher temperature , more chaotic agregate state and if the substance has more atoms .\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 183 paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,d','a,b','b,c','a,c','NEW','11-183','11-159',NULL),
(61044,'chemistry','In which of folowing reactions the entropy change is positive? \n \n a) H2O(l)=H2O(g)\n \n b)CaCO3(S)=CaO(s)+CO2(g)\n \n c)N2(g)+3H2(g)=2NH3(g)\n \n d)N2(g)+O2(g)=2NO(g)','a,b','Entropy is higher if its higher temperature , more chaotic agregate state and if the substance has more atoms .\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 183 paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,d','a,b','b,c','a,c','NEW','11-170','11-144',NULL),
(61045,'chemistry','How many mols of propane should be burned (p=const.) so that the change of enthalpy of tris reaction is 2500 kJ?\n \n ΔfH(CO2,g)= -393.5 kJ mol-1,ΔfH(H2O,l)= -285.84 kJ mol-1,ΔfH(C3H8,g)= -103.916 kJ mol\n \n a)more than 50\n \n b)less than 50\n \n c)more than 30\n \n d)less than 30','b,c','2500 = (3 * (-393.5) + 4 * (-285.84)) - n* ΔfH(C3H8,g)\n \n \n \n n * (-103,916)= -4823.86\n \n \n \n n= -4823.86/-103.916 = 46.42 mol\n \n (Reference: NCERT class 11 page 170 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,d','a,b','b,c','a,c','NEW','11-183','11-159',NULL),
(61046,'chemistry','In which of folowing reactions the entropy change is positive? \n \n \n \n a) O2(g) → 2 O(g)\n \n b) Br2(l) → Br2(g)\n \n c) N2(g,10 bar) → N2(g,1 bar)\n \n d)C(s,graphite) → C(s,diamond)','a,b,c','Entropy is higher if its higher temperature , more chaotic agregate state and if the substance has more atoms .\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 183 paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b,d','a,b,c','b,c,d','a,c,d','NEW','11-173','11-149',NULL),
(61047,'chemistry','Based on standard enthalpy of formation of CO2(g) (-393.5 kJ/mol) and standard enthalpy of overall reaction (-557.5 kJ/mol). We can say that standard enthalpy of formation N2O is:','82 kJ/mol','C(graphite) + 2 N2O(g) = CO2(g) + 2 N2(g)\n \n ΔHr° =ΔHf°(CO2(g)) + 2 ΔHf°(N2(g)) – (ΔHf°(C (grafit)) + 2ΔHf°(N2O(g)))\n \n \n \n - 2 ·ΔHf°(N2O(g)) = - 557,5 kJ/mol + 393,5 kJ/mol\n \n - 2 ·ΔHf°(N2O(g)) = -164 kJ/mol\n \n ΔHf °(N2O(g)) = 82 kJ/mol\n \n \n \n \n \n (Reference: NCERT class 11 page 173 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','82 kJ/mol','100 kJ/mol','2 kJ/mol','1000 kJ/mol','NEW','11-170','11-144',NULL),
(61048,'chemistry','Calculate the standard enthalpy change of burning methane ? ΔHf°(CO2) = -393,5kJ/mol, ΔHf°(H2O) = - 241,8 kJ/mol, ΔHf°(CH4) = - 74,9 kJ/mol.','-802 kJ/mol','The balanced chemical equation for the combustion of methane (CH4) is:\n\nCH4 + 2O2 -> CO2 + 2H2O\n\nThe enthalpy change for this reaction can be calculated using Hess\'s law, which states that the enthalpy change of a reaction is equal to the sum of the enthalpy changes of its individual steps.\n\nStep 1: Calculate the enthalpy change for the formation of one mole of CH4 from its constituent elements:\n\nC(graphite) + 2H2(g) -> CH4(g) ΔHf° = -74.9 kJ/mol\n\nStep 2: Calculate the enthalpy change for the combustion of one mole of CH4 to form one mole of CO2 and two moles of H2O:\n\nCH4(g) + 2O2(g) -> CO2(g) + 2H2O(l) ΔH° = ?\n\nTo calculate the enthalpy change for this step, we need to use the enthalpy of formation values for CO2 and H2O, as well as the enthalpy of formation of CH4 calculated in Step 1. We can use the following equation:\n\nΔH° = ΣΔHf°(products) - ΣΔHf°(reactants)\n\nwhere ΣΔHf° represents the sum of the standard enthalpies of formation for each product or reactant, with the coefficients of the balanced chemical equation taken into account.\n\nΔH° = [ΔHf°(CO2) + 2ΔHf°(H2O)] - ΔHf°(CH4)\nΔH° = [(-393.5 kJ/mol) + 2(-241.8 kJ/mol)] - (-74.9 kJ/mol)= -802.26 kJ/mol\n \n (Reference: NCERT class 11 page 170 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','-802 kJ/mol','-1073 kJ/mol','200 kJ/mol','-200 kJ/mol','NEW','11-185','11-161',NULL),
(61049,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true??\n a)When temperature of the system rises, motions of substance molecules become more vigorous and entropy increases.\n\n b)The second law of thermodynamics explains why spontaneous endothermic reactions are so common.\n\n c)If a reaction has a positive enthalpy change and positive entropy change, it can be spontaneous when T∆S is large enough to outweigh ∆H.\n\n d) If ∆G is positive (> 0), the process is spontaneous.','a,c','When temperature of the system rises, motions of substance molecules become more vigorous and entropy increases.\n\nThe second law of thermodynamics explains why spontaneous exothermic reactions are so common.\n\nIf a reaction has a positive enthalpy change and positive entropy change, it can be spontaneous when T∆S is large enough to outweigh ∆H.\n\n If ∆G is negative (< 0), the process is spontaneous.\n(Reference NCERT class  page 185 paragraphs 2,3,5,6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','b,d','NEW','11-183','11-159',NULL),
(61050,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true?\n\na)When a system is in equilibrium, the entropy is minimum.\n\nb)The greater the disorder in an isolated system, the higher is the entropy.\n\nc)Whenever heat is added to the system, it increases molecular motions causing increased randomness in the system.\n\nd)For a given substance, the crystalline solid state is the state of highest entropy (most ordered),','b,c','When a system is in equilibrium, the entropy is maximum.\n\nThe greater the disorder in an isolated system, the higher is the entropy.\n\nWhenever heat is added to the system, it increases molecular motions causing increased randomness in the system.\n\nFor a given substance, the crystalline solid state is the state of lowest entropy (most ordered).\n(Reference NCERT class 11 page 183 paragraph 1,2.3.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','b,d','NEW','11-175','11-151',NULL),
(61051,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true??\n \n \n \n a)In case of diatomic molecules, like dihydrogen , the enthalpy of atomization is also the bond dissociation enthalpy.\n \n b)the change in enthalpy depends of the path between initial state (reactants) and final state (products).\n \n c)If a reaction takes place in several steps then its standard reaction enthalpy is the sum of the standard enthalpies of the intermediate reactions','a,c','In case of diatomic molecules, like dihydrogen , the enthalpy of atomization is also the bond dissociation enthalpy.\nthe change in enthalpy is independent of the path between initial state (reactants) and final state (products).\nIf a reaction takes place in several steps then its standard reaction enthalpy is the sum of the standard enthalpies of the intermediate reactions\n(Reference NCERT class 11 page 175,177 pagarraph 5,2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','b,d','NEW','11-172','11-148',NULL),
(61052,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true? ?\n \n \n \n a)Sublimation is direct conversion of a solid into its vapour.\n \n b)The magnitude of the enthalpy change depends on the strength of the intermolecular interactions in the substance undergoing the phase transfomations.\n \n c)it requires more heat to vaporise 1 mol of acetone than it does to vaporize 1 mol of water.','a,b','Sublimation is direct conversion of a solid into its vapour.\nThe magnitude of the enthalpy change depends on the strength of the intermolecular interactions in the substance undergoing the phase transfomations.\nit requires less heat to vaporise 1 mol of acetone than it does to vaporize 1 mol of water.\n(Reference NCERT class 11 page 172 paragraph 1,2,3)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','b,d','NEW','11-171','11-145',NULL),
(61053,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true??\n \n a)Enthalpy of a reaction depends on the conditions under which a reaction is carried out,therefore is necessary that we must \n \n specify some standard conditions.\n \n b)Melting of a solid is endothermic, so all enthalpies of fusion are positive.\n \n c) Standard enthalpy of vaporation of water is negative','a,b','Enthalpy of a reaction depends on the conditions under which a reaction is carried out,therefore is necessary that we must\nspecify some standard conditions.\nMelting of a solid is endothermic, so all enthalpies of fusion are positive.\n\n Standard enthalpy of vaporation of water is + 40.79 kJ moI–1\n(Reference NCERT class 11 page171 paragraph 2,6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','b,d','NEW','11-168','11-142',NULL),
(61054,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true?\n \n \n \n a)Intensive property is a property whose value depends on the quantity or size of matter present in the system.\n \n b)Volume is an extensive property and temperature is an intensive property.\n \n c)Heat capacity is directly proportional to amount of substance.','b,c','Extensive property is a property whose value depends on the quantity or size of matter present in the system.\nVolume is an extensive property and temperature is an intensive property.\nHeat capacity is directly proportional to amount of substance.\n(Reference NCERT class 11 page 168 paragraph 1,2,5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','b,d','NEW','11-172','11-148',NULL),
(61055,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true?\n \n \n \n a)No work is done during free expansion of an ideal gas whether the process is reversible or irreversible.\n \n b) Free expansion for adiabatic irreversible change q = – w = pex(Vf– Vi )\n \n c)For isothermal (T = constant) expansion of an ideal gas into vacuum ∆U = 0','a,c','No work is done during free expansion of an ideal gas whether the process is reversible or irreversible.\n Free expansion for adiabatic irreversible change q = – w = pex(Vf– Vi )\nFor isothermal (T = constant) expansion of an ideal gas into vacuum ∆U = 0"\n(Reference NCERT class 11 page 166 paragraph 2,3)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','b,d','NEW','11-166','11-140',NULL),
(61056,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true?\n \n \n \n a)The energy of an isolated system is 0.\n \n b)Wall is adiabatic when there is no heat absorbed by the system from the surroundings, but work (w) is done on the system.\n \n c)It\'s closed system when w amount of work is done by the system and q amount of heat is supplied to the system.','b,c','The energy of an isolated system is constant.\nWall is  adiabatic when there is no heat absorbed by the system from the surroundings, but work (w) is done on the system.\nIt\'s closed system when w amount of work is done by the system and q amount of heat is supplied to the system.\n(Reference NCERT class 11 page 164 paragraph 2.4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','b,d','NEW','11-164','11-138',NULL),
(61057,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true?:\n \n \n \n a)The positive sign expresses that wad is positive when work is done by the system and the internal energy of system increases.\n \n b)By conventions of IUPAC in chemical thermodynamics. The q is positive, when heat is transferred from the surroundings to the system\n \n c)exchange of energy, which is a result of temperature difference is called heat, q.','b,c','The positive sign expresses that wad is positive when work is done on the system and the internal energy of system increases.\nBy conventions of IUPAC in chemical thermodynamics. The q is positive, when heat is transferred from the surroundings to the system\nThe exchange of energy, which is a result of temperature difference is called heat, q.\n\n(Reference NCERT class 11 page 163 paragraph 3,4,6)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','b,d','NEW','11-163','11-137',NULL),
(61058,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true? \n \n \n \n a)In a closed system, there is no exchange of energy or matter between the system and the surroundings\n \n b)internal energy is total energy of the system.\n \n c)We can change the state of the system by doing mechanical work','b,c','In an isolated system, there is no exchange of energy or matter between the system and the surroundings\ninternal energy is total energy of the system.\nWe can change the state of the system by doing mechanical work\n(Reference NCERT class 11 page 162 paragraph  1,4,5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','b,d','NEW','11-162','11-138',NULL),
(61059,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true? \n \n \n \n a)Internal energy of the sistem can change when heat passes into or out of the system,\n \n b) adiabatic system is the sistem where there is allowed exchange of heat between the system and surroundings through its boundary.\n \n c)The state of a thermodynamic system is described by its measurable or macroscopic (bulk) properties that are called state functions','a,c','Internal energy of the sistem can change when heat passes into or out of the system,\nadiabatic system is the sistem where there is not allowed exchange of heat between the system and surroundings through its boundary.\nThe state of a thermodynamic system is described by its measurable or macroscopic (bulk) properties that are called state functions\n(Reference NCERT page 162 paragraph 3,4,5)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','b,d','NEW','11-162','11-138',NULL),
(61060,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true? \n \n a)system in thermodynamics refers to that part of universe in which observations are made and remaining universe constitutes the surroundings.\n \n b)classify the systems according to the movements of matter and energy in or out of the system.\n \n c)The wall that separates the system from the surroundings is called boundary.','all of them','system in thermodynamics refers to that part of universe in which observations are made and remaining universe constitutes the surroundings.\nclassify the systems according to the movements of matter and energy in or out of the system.\nThe wall that separates the system from the surroundings is called boundary.\n(Reference NCERT class 11 page 162 paragraph 2,3,4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','all of them','NEW','11-162','11-138',NULL),
(61061,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true? \n \n a)The presence of reactants in an open beaker is an example of an open system.\n \n b)The presence of reactants in a closed vessel made of conducting material is an exaple of open system.\n \n c)The system may simply be defined by a set of Cartesian coordinates specifying a particular volume in space.','a,c','The presence of reactants in an open beaker is an example of an open system.\nThe presence of reactants in a closed vessel made of conducting material is an exaple of slosed system.\nThe system may simply be defined by a set of Cartesian coordinates specifying a particular volume in space.\n(Reference NCERT class 11 page 161 paragraph 4,6,7)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','b,d','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(61062,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true?\n \n a) Gibbs function is G = H + TS\n \n b)when liquid cristalise to solid entropy decreases\n \n c) most of the chemical reactions there are changes in both enthalpy and entropy.','b,c','Gibbs function is G = H – TS\nwhen liquid cristalise to solid entropy decreases\n most of the chemical reactions there are changes in both enthalpy and entropy.\n(Reference NCERT class 11 page 184 paragraph 1,2)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','b,d','NEW','11-184','11-160',NULL),
(61063,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true?\n \n a)increase in enthalpy of the surrounding is equal to increase in the enthalpy of the system.\n \n b)If a reaction has a positive enthalpy change and positive entropy change, it can be spontaneous when T∆S is large enough to outweigh ∆H.\n \n c)The entropy of any pure crystalline substance approaches zero as the temperature approaches absolute zero.','b,c','increase in enthalpy of the surrounding is equal to decrease in the enthalpy of the system.\nIf a reaction has a positive enthalpy change and positive entropy change, it can be spontaneous when T∆S is large enough to outweigh ∆H.\nThe entropy of any pure crystalline substance approaches zero as the temperature approaches absolute zero.\n(Reference NCERT class 11 page 185 paragraph  3,4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','b,d','NEW','11-185','11-161',NULL),
(61064,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true? \n \n a)If ∆rH° is negative,∆rS° is positive and ∆rG° is negative than Reaction spontaneous at all temperatures\n \n b)means that the reactions in both the directions should proceed with a increase in free energy\n \n c)For strongly endothermic reactions, the value of ∆rH° may be large and positive.','a,c','If ∆rH° is negative,∆rS° is positive and  ∆rG° is negative than Reaction spontaneous at all temperatures\nmeans that the reactions in both the directions should proceed with a decrease in free energy\nFor strongly endothermic reactions, the value of ∆rH° may be large and positive.\n(Reference NCERT class 11 page 186 paragraph 3,4)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','b,d','NEW','11-186','11-162',NULL),
(61131,'chemistry','Among the following, the set of parameters that  represents path functions, is [JEE (Main)-2019]','(B) and (C)','(A) q + w = \'U, state function\n(B) q, path function\n(C) w, path function\n(D) H – TS = G, state function','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','(A), (B) and (C)','(B) and (C)','(B), (C) and (D)','(A) and (D)','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(61132,'chemistry','During compression of a spring he work done is  10 kJ and 2 kJ escaped to the surroundings as  heat. The change in internal energy, ∆U (in kJ) is [JEE (Main)-2019]','8','w = 10 kJ\nq = – 2 kJ\n∆U = q + w = 10 – 2 = 8 kJ','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','12','-12','8','-8','NEW','11-181','11-157',NULL),
(61133,'chemistry','A process will be spontaneous at all temperatures  if : [JEE (Main)-2019]','ΔH < 0 and ΔS > 0','A reaction is spontaneous if ∆Gsys is negative.\n∆Gsys =∆Hsys – T∆Ssys\nA reaction will be spontaneous at all temperatures\n ,if ∆Hsys is negative and ∆Ssys = +ve','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','ΔH < 0 and ΔS > 0','ΔH > 0 and ΔS < 0','ΔH > 0 and ΔS > 0','ΔH < 0 and ΔS < 0','NEW','11-165','11-139',NULL),
(61134,'chemistry','The difference between ∆H and ∆U (∆H – ∆U), when the combustion of one mole of heptane(I) is  carried out at a temperature T, is equal to  [JEE (Main)-2019]','-4RT','C₇H₁₆(l) +  11O₂(g) → 7 CO₂(g) + 8H₂O(l)\n\n∆H – ∆U = ∆ng RT\n∆ng = –4\n∆H – ∆U = –4RT','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','–3RT','4RT','3RT','-4RT','NEW','11-161','11-137',NULL),
(61135,'chemistry','An ideal gas is allowed to expand from 1 L to10 L against a constant external pressure of  1 bar. The work done in kJ is [JEE (Main)-2019]','-0.9','w = –PV\n= –(1 bar) × (9 L)\n= –(10⁵ Pa) × (9 × 10⁻³) m³\n= –9 × 10² N-m\n= –900 J = –0.9 kJ','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','-9','-0.9','-2','10','NEW','11-164','11-138',NULL),
(61136,'chemistry','Enthalpy of sublimation of iodine is 24 cal/g at  200°C. If specific heat of I₂(s) and I₂(vap) are 0.055  and 0.031 cal/gK respectively, then enthalpy of  sublimation of iodine at 250°C in cal/g is  [JEE (Main)-2019]','22.8','I₂(s) → I₂(g) \n\n(∆H)T2 - (∆H)T1  = ∆Cₚ(T2 - T1)\n\n(∆₂₅₀ = (∆H)₂₀₀+ (0.031 – 0.055) 50\n        = 24 – 50 × 0.024 = 22.8','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','11.4','2.85','5.7','22.8','NEW','11-168','11-142',NULL),
(61137,'chemistry','The process with negative entropy change is  [JEE (Main)-2019]','Synthesis of ammonia from N₂\n and H₂','N₂\n(g) + 3H₂\n(g) → 2NH₃(g)\n→ Number of gaseous moles are decreasing\n→Change in entropy will be negative','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Sublimation of dry ice','Dissociation of CaSO₄\n(s) to CaO(s) and SO₃\n(g)','Synthesis of ammonia from N₂\n and H₂','Dissolution of iodine in water','NEW','11-169','11-143',NULL),
(61201,'chemistry','The net enthalpy change of a reaction is the amount of energy required to break all the bonds in reactant molecules minus amount of energy required to form all the bonds in the product molecules. What will be the enthalpy change for the following reaction?\nH₂(g) + Br₂(g)→2HBr(g)\nGiven that bond energy of H₂, Br₂ and HBr is 4.35 kJ /mol,192 kJ /mol and 368 kJ /mol respectively.','-109 KJ/mol','∆ᵣ\nH°  = Σ B.E. ( reactant) - Σ B.E. ( product)\n          = B.E. H₂\n + B.E. Br₂\n - 2×B.E. HBr\n          = 432 + 192 - ( 2 × 368)\n          =-109 KJ/mol','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','-109 KJ/mol','-218 KJ/mol','-100 KJ/mol','-227 KJ/mol','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(61202,'chemistry','If the combustion of 1 g of graphite produces 20.7 kJ of heat, what will be molar enthalpy change?','-2.48 x 10² kJ/mol','Molar enthalpy change for graphite (ΔH) = enthalpy change for 1 g x molar mass of C = -20.7×12 = -2.48 x 10² kJ/mol\nSince the sign of ΔH = -ve, it is an exothermic reaction.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','-2.48  kJ/mol','-2.48 x 10² kJ/mol','-248 x 10² kJ/mol','-2.48 x 10⁴ kJ/mol','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(61203,'chemistry','The difference between Cp and Cv can be derived using the empirical relation H = U + pV. Calculate the difference between Cp and Cv for 10 moles of an ideal gas.','83.14J','Given that, Cv = heat capacity at constant volume,\nCp = heat capacity at constant pressure Difference between Cp and Cv is equal to gas constant (R).\n.’. Cp – Cv = nR                                (where, n = no. of moles)\n= 10 x 8.314 = 83.14J','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','83.14J','83.14kJ','8314J','8.314J','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(61204,'chemistry','Expansion of a gas in vacuum is called free expansion. Calculate the work done and the change in internal energy when 1 litre of ideal gas expands isothermally into vacuum until its total volume is 5 litre.','0,0','During free expansion, external pressure is zero, so Work done, w = -PextΔV\n= -0(5 – 1) = 0\nSince the gas is expanding isothermally, therefore, q = 0\nΔU = q + w =0+0=0','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','0,0','0,1','1,0','1,1','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(61205,'chemistry','A sample of 1.0 mol of a monoatomic ideal gas is taken through a cyclic process of expansion and compression as shown in the figure. What will be the value of ΔH for the cycle as a whole? Refer diagram(n0604)','ΔH = 0','During cyclic process, change in internal energy is zero.\nΔU=0 and no work is said to be done, as system returns to the initial state.\nFor a steady state cyclic process at any given stage enthalpy is one single value however, at different stage it would vary. ΔH=0','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','ΔH = 0','ΔH = 1','ΔH ≠ 0','ΔH ≥ 0','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/61205q.png'),
(61206,'chemistry','The enthalpy of atomisation for the reaction CH₄(g) → C(g) + 4H(g) is 1665 kJ/mol. What is the bond energy of C – H bond?','416.2 kJ mol','CH₄→ C + 4H, ∆H= + 1665 kJ/mol\nBond energy of (C – H) bond = 1665/4 =416.2 kJ mol','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','416.2 kJ mol','416.2 J mol','4162 J mol','4162 kJ mol','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(61207,'chemistry','One mole of acetone requires less heat to vapourise than 1 mol of water. Which of the two liquids has higher enthalpy of vapourisation?','(∆Hr)water > (∆Hr)acetone','Due to weakforce of attraction between molecules, acetone requires less heat to vaporise. Hence, water has higher enthalpy of vaporisation.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','(∆Hr)water = (∆Hr)acetone','(∆Hr)water < (∆Hr)acetone','(∆Hr)water > (∆Hr)acetone','(∆Hr)water << (∆Hr)acetone','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(61208,'chemistry','Consider the following reaction between zinc and oxygen and choose the correct options from the options given below:\n\n2Zn(s) + 0₂(g) → 2ZnO(s); ∆H=-693.8 kJ/mol\n\n(i) The enthalpy of two moles ZnO is less than the total enthalpy of two moles of Zn and one mole of oxygen by 693.8 kJ.\n\n(ii) The enthalpy of two moles of ZnO is more than the total enthalpy of two moles of Zn and one mole of oxygen by 693.8 kJ.\n\n(iii) 8 kJ /mol energy is evolved in the reaction.\n\n(iv) 693.8 kJ/mol energy is absorbed in the reaction.','(i) & (ii)','∆բH = Σa∆բHº - Σb∆բHº\nThe reaction depicted above is exothermic in nature and hence the reaction enthalpy is more than the product enthalpy.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','(i) & (ii)','(i) & (iii)','(ii) & (iii)','All of them','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(61209,'chemistry','The spontaneity means, having the potential to proceed without the assistance of external agency. The processes which occur spontaneously are\n\n(a) flow of heat from colder to warmer body.\n\n(b) gas in a container contracting into one comer.\n\n(c) gas expanding to fill the available volume.\n\n(d) burning carbon in oxygen to give carbon dioxide.','C & D','Gas expands or diffuses in available space spontaneously, e.g., leakage of cooking gas gives smell of ethyl mercaptan spontaneously. Moreover, burning of carbon to C0₂ is also spontaneous.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','A & B','B & C','C & D','A & D','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(61210,'chemistry','In an exothermic reaction, heat is evolved, and system loses heat to the surroundings. For such system\n\n(a) qₚ will be negative   \n                            \n(b) ∆γHwill be negative\n\n(c) qₚ will be positive  \n                              \n(d) ∆γHwill be positive.','A & B','For an exothermic reaction, qₚ = -ve, ∆γH = -ve','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','A & B','B & C','C & D','A & D','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(61211,'chemistry','Thermodynamics mainly deals with\n\n(a) interrelation of various forms of energy and their transformation front one from to another.\n\n(b) energy changes in the processes which depend only on initial and final states of the microscopic system containing a few molecules.\n\n(c) how and at what rate these energy transformations are carried out.\n\n(d) the system in equilibrium state or moving from one equilibrium state to another equilibrium state.','A & D','Thermodynamics deals with interrelation of various forms of energy and their transformation into each other. It also deals with thermal or mechanical equilibrium. However, if does not tell anything about the rate of reaction.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','A & B','B & C','C & D','A & D','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(61212,'chemistry','Which of the following is not correct?','∆G is positive for a spontaneous reaction','∆G gives a criterion for spontaneity at constant pressure and temperature.\n\n(i) If ∆G is negative (< 0). the process is spontaneous.\n\n(ii) If ∆G is positiv e (> 0). the process is non-spontaneous.\n\n(iii) If ∆G is zero then reaction is at equilibrium.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','∆G is zero for a reversible reaction.','∆G is positive for a spontaneous reaction','∆G is negative tor a spontaneous reaction','∆G is positive for a non-spontaneous','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(61213,'chemistry','Enthalpy of sublimation of a substance is equal to','Enthalpy of fusion + enthalpy of vapourisation','Enthalpy of sublimation of a substance is equal to enthalpy of fusion + enthalpy of vapourisation.\nSublimation is direct conversion of solid to vapour, i.e., solid → vapour\nWriting in two steps, we have solid → liquid → vapour.\nSolid → liquid requires enthalpy of fusion.\nLiquid →vapour requires enthalpy of vapourisation','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Enthalpy of fusion + enthalpy of vapourisation','Enthalpy of fusion','Enthalpy of vapourisation','Twice the enthalpy of vapourisation','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(61214,'chemistry','The enthalpies of elements in their standard states are taken as zero. The enthalpy of formation of a compound','May be positive or negative','Heat of formation of a compound may be positive or negative, \n\ne.g.,C(s) + O₂ (g) →CO₂ (g); ∆H° = -392.5 KJ/mol\n\nN₂(g) + 1/2 O₂(g) →N₂O(g) ; ∆H° = +92 KJ/mol','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Is always negative','Is always positive','May be positive or negative','Is never negative','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(61215,'chemistry','Consider the reactions given below. On the basis of these reactions find out which of the algebraic relations given in options (a) to (d) is correct?\n\n(i) C(g) + 4H(g) → CH₄(g); ∆ᵣH= kJ/mol\n\n(ii) C(graphite, s) + 2H₂ (g) → CH₄(g); ∆ᵣH = y kJ/mol','x >y','x > y because same bonds are formed  in reactions (i) and (ii)  but bonds between reactant molecules are broken only in reaction (ii). As energy is absorbed when bonds are broken, energy released in reaction (i) is greater than that in reaction (ii)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','x = y','x = 2y','x >y','x< y','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(61216,'chemistry','On the basis of thermochemical equations (i), (ii) and (iii), find out which of the algebraic relationships given in options (a) to (d) is correct.\n\nC(graphite) + O₂(g) → CO₂(g); ∆ᵣH = x KJ/Mol\nC(graphite) + 1/2O₂(g) → CO(g); ∆ᵣH = y KJ/Mol\n\nCO(g) + 1/2O₂(g) → CO(g); ∆ᵣH = z KJ/Mol','x = y + z','C(graphite) + O₂\n\n(g) → CO₂\n\n(g); ∆ᵣ\nH = x KJ/Mol ------(I)\n\nC(graphite) + 1/2O₂\n\n(g) → CO\n(g); ∆ᵣ\nH = y KJ/Mol-----(II)\n\nSubtracting the equation (I) & (II) we get \n\nCO(g) + 1/2O₂\n\n(g) → CO\n(g); ∆ᵣ\nH = z KJ/Mol--------(III)\n\nEquation (III) obtained by subtracting (II) from (I)\n\nTherefore, z = x-y \nx = y + z','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','x = y + z','z = y + x','y = x + z','x = y + 2z','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(61217,'chemistry','The entropy change can be calculated by using the expression ∆S = q(rev) / T.  When water freezes in a glass beaker, choose the correct statement amongst the following:\n\nWhen water freezes in a glass beaker, choose the correct statement amongst the following:\n\n(a) ∆S(system) decreases but ∆S(surroundings) remains the same.\n\n(b) ∆S(system) increases but ∆S(surroundings) decreases.\n\n(C) ∆S(system) decreases but ∆S(surroundmgs) increases.\n\n(d) ∆S(system) decreases but ∆S(surroundings) also decreases.','c','During the process of freezing energy is released , which is absorbed by the surroundings.\n\n∆S(System) = -q(rev) / T\n∆S(Surrounding) = q(rev) / T\n\nTherefore the entropy of the system decreases and that surroundings is increases.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(61218,'chemistry','The pressure-volume work for an ideal gas can be calculated by using the expression.\n\nThe work can also be calculated from the pV plot by using the area under curve within the specified limits. When an ideal gas is compressed (a) reversibly or (b) irreversibly from Vi to Vf, choose the correct option. Refer diagram(n0605)','w (reversible) < w (irreversible)','w (reversible) < w (irreversible)\nArea under the curve is greater in irreversible compression than that of reversible compression. Refer diagram(ne0602)','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','w (reversible) = w (irreversible)','w (reversible) < w (irreversible)','w (reversible) > w (irreversible)','w (reversible) = w (irreversible) + pex. ∆V','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/61218q.png'),
(61219,'chemistry','In an adiabatic process, no transfer-of heat takes place between system and surroundings. Choose the correct option for free expansion of an ideal gas under adiabatic condition from the following.','q = 0 , ∆T = 0 , w=0','For free expansion w = 0 For adiabatic process q = 0 From first law of thermodynamics,\n∆U=q + w\n= 0 + 0 = 0\nSince there is no change of internal energy, hence temperature will also remain constant, i.e., ∆T = 0','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','q = 0 , ∆T ≠ 0 , w=0','q ≠ 0 , ∆T = 0 , w=0','q = 0 , ∆T = 0 , w=0','q = 0 , ∆T < 0 , w≠0','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(61220,'chemistry','Thermodynamics is not concerned about .','The rate at which a reaction proceeds.','Thermodynamics is not concerned with rate at which a reaction proceeds. The rate of reaction is dealt by kinetics.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Energy changes involved in a chemical reaction.','The extent to which a chemical reaction proceeds.','The rate at which a reaction proceeds.','The feasibility of chemical reaction.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(61221,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct?','The presence of reactants in a closed vessel made up of copper is an example of a closed system.','For a closed vessel made up of copper, no matter can be exchanged between the system and the surroundings but energy exchange can occur through its walls.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','The presence of reacting species in a covered beaker is an example of open system.','There is an exchange of energy as well as matter between the system and the surroundings in a closed system.','The presence of reactants in a closed vessel made up of copper is an example of a closed system.','The presence of reactants in a thermos flask or any other closed insulated vessel is an example of a closed system.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(61222,'chemistry','The state of gas can be described by quoting the relationship between','Pressure, volume, temperature, amount','State of a system can be described by state functions or state variables which are pressure, volume, temperature and amount of the gas (PV= nRT).','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Pressure, volume, temperature','Temperature, amount, pressure','Amount, volume, temperature','Pressure, volume, temperature, amount','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(61223,'chemistry','The volume of gas is reduced to half from its original volume. The specific heat will .','Remain constant','The specific heat of a substance is the heat required to raise the temperature of 1 gram of a substance by one degree (1 K or 1 °C). It is an intensive property and is independent of the volume of the substance','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Reduce to half','Be doubled','Remain constant','Increase four times','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(61224,'chemistry','During complete combustion of one mole of butane, 2658 kJ of heat is released. The thermochemical reaction for above change is','C₄H₁₀(g) + 13/2 O₂ → 4CO₂(g) + 5H₂O(l).   ∆꜀H = -2658 KJ/Mol','Exothermic reaction for combustion of one mole of butane is represented as:\nC₄H₁₀(g) + 13/2 O₂ → 4CO₂(g) + 5H₂O(l).   ∆꜀H = -2658 KJ/Mol','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','2C₄H₁₀(g) + 13 O₂ → 8CO₂(g) + 10H₂O(l).   ∆꜀H = -2658 KJ/Mol','C₄H₁₀(g) + 13/2 O₂ → 4CO₂(g) + 5H₂O(l).   ∆꜀H = -1329 KJ/Mol','C₄H₁₀(g) + 13/2 O₂ → 4CO₂(g) + 5H₂O(l).   ∆꜀H = -2658 KJ/Mol','C₄H₁₀(g) + 13/2 O₂ → 4CO₂(g) + 5H₂O(l).   ∆꜀H = +2658 KJ/Mol','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(61401,'chemistry','Calculate the entropy change in surroundings when 1.00 mol of H₂\nO is formed under standard conditions. ∆ᵣ\nH° = – 286 kJ/mol','959.73 J/Kmol','ΔHբ°[ H₂O(s) ] = 286 KJ/mol\n\nq(surroundings) = +286 KJ/mol\nΔS(surr) = q/298 = 286 × 1000/298 J/K mol  = 959.73 J/Kmol','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','959.73 J/Kmol','95973 J/Kmol','95.9 J/Kmol','454.36 J/K mol','NEW','11-189','11-141',NULL),
(61402,'chemistry','The equilibrium constant for a reaction is 10. What will be the value of ΔG° ? R = 8.314J /Kmol, T= 300 K','– 5744.14 J/mol','We know that ΔG° = – 2.303RT logK\n= – 2.303 x 8.314 x 300 x log10\n= – 5744.14 J/mol','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','– 5744.14 J/mol','– 574414 J/mol','– 57.4414 J/mol','– 574.414 J/mol','NEW','11-189','11-141',NULL),
(61403,'chemistry','For the reaction\n2A(g) + B(g) → 2D(g), ΔU° = -10.5 kJ and ΔS° = – 44.1 J/K\nCalculate ΔG° for the reaction ?','0.16 KJ/mol','ΔH° = ΔU° + Δn(g)RT\nΔU° = -10.5 KJ/mol , R = 8.314 J/K mol , Δn(g) = 2-(1+2) = -1\n\nΔH° = -10.5 - (1) × 8.314 × 10⁻³ × 298  = -12.98 KJ/mol\n\nΔG° = ΔH° - TΔS° = -12.98 -(298 × -44.1 × 10⁻³) = 0.16 KJ/mol\n\nSince ΔG° is positive , the reaction is not spontaneous.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','0.16 KJ/mol','0.16 J/mol','16 KJ/mol','0.32 KJ/mol','NEW','11-191','11-176',NULL),
(61404,'chemistry','For the reaction, 2Cl(g) → Cl₂(g), what are the signs of ΔH and ΔS?','-,-','ΔH is negative because bond energy is released and ΔS is negative because there is less randomness among the molecules than among the atoms.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','-,-','+,+','-,+','+,-','NEW','11-189','11-141',NULL),
(61405,'chemistry','For the reaction at 298 K, 2A + B → C,\nΔH = 400 kJ /mol and ΔS = 0.2 kJ /K mol At what temperature will the reaction become spontaneous considering ΔH and ΔS to be constant over the temperature range.','> 2000k','According to Gibbs Helmholtz equation, ΔG = ΔH – TΔS.\n\nAt equilibrium, ΔG = 0 \nT = ΔH/ΔS =400/0.2 = 2000 k\n\nThus reaction will be spontaneous at temperatures above 2000k [ Above 2000k ,ΔG = -ve ]','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','> 2000k','< 2000k','= 2000k','≤ 2000k','NEW','11-190','11-176',NULL),
(61406,'chemistry','For an isolated system, ΔU = 0, what will be ΔS?','ΔS > 0','When energy factor has no role to play, for the process to be spontaneous ΔS must be +ve i.e., ΔS > 0.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','ΔS > 0','ΔS < 0','ΔS = 0','ΔS ≤ 0','NEW','11-190','11-176',NULL),
(61407,'chemistry','Calculate the enthalpy change for the process\nCCl₄(g) → C(g) + 4Cl(g)\nand calculate bond enthalpy of C – Cl in CCl₄(g).\nΔvapH°(CCl₄) = 30.5 kJ /mol,\nΔբH°(CCl₄) = -135.5 kJ /mol,\nΔₐH°(C) = 715.0 kJ /mol, where ΔₐH° is enthalpy of atomisation ΔₐH°(Cl₂ = 242 kJ /mol)','326 kJ/mol','CCl₄(l) → CCl₄(g)  ; ΔvapH°(CCl₄) = 30.5 kJ /mol ----> (1)\n\nC(g) + 2Cl₂(g) → CCl₄(g) ; ΔբH°(CCl₄) = -135.5 kJ /mol -------> (2)\n\nC(s) → C(g) ; ΔₐH°(C) = 715.0 kJ /mol ------>(3)\n\nCl₂(g) → 2Cl(g) ; ΔₐH°(C) = 242.0 kJ /mol ------>(4)\n\nRequired equation is \n\nCCl₄(g) → C(g) + 4Cl(g) ; ∆H = ?\n\nFrom Hess law , \n\nEqn.(3) + 2 ×Eqn.(4) - Eqn.(1) - Eqn.(2) gives required equation.\n\n∆H = 715.0 + 2(242) - 30.5 - (-135.5) = 1304 kJ/mol\n\nBond enthalpy of C-Cl bond in CCl₄ (Average value) = 1304/4 = 326 KJ/mol.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','326 kJ/mol','326 J/mol','3.26 kJ/mol','32.6 kJ/mol','NEW','11-189','11-141',NULL),
(61408,'chemistry','Given: N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g); ∆ᵣH° = -92.4 kJ /mol. What is the standard enthalpy of formation of NH₃ gas?','-46.2 KJ/mol','N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g); ∆ᵣH° = -92.4 kJ /mol. \n\nStandard enthalpy of formation of NH₃ = -92.4/ 2 = 46.2 KJ/mol','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','-46.2 KJ/mol','-462 KJ/mol','-92.4 KJ/mol','-46.2 J/mol','NEW','11-189','11-141',NULL),
(61409,'chemistry','Enthalpies of formation of CO(g), CO₂(g), N₂O(g) and N₂O₄(g) are -110, – 393, 81 and 9.7 kJ /mol respectively. Find the value of ∆ᵣf for the reaction : N₂O₄(g) + 3CO(g) → N₂O(g) + 3CO₂(g)','-777.7 KJ/mol','N₂O₄(g) + 3CO(g) → N₂O(g) + 3CO₂(g) \n\nΔբH = [ ΔբH [N₂O(g)] + 3ΔբH[CO₂(g)] ] - [ΔբH[N₂O₄(g)] + 3×ΔբH[CO(g)]] = 81 + 3 × (-393) -[ 9.7 + 3(-110) ] = -777.7 KJ/mol','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','-777.7 KJ/mol','-747.7 KJ/mol','-888.8 KJ/mol','-141.4 KJ/mol','NEW','11-189','11-141',NULL),
(61410,'chemistry','Enthalpy of combustion of carbon to CO₂ is -393.5 kJ /mol. Calculate the heat released upon formation of 35.2 g of CO₂ from carbon and dioxygen gas.','-314.8 kJ','C + O₂ → CO₂; ΔT = -393.5 kJ\n∵ When 44 g of CO₂ is formed from carbon and dioxygen gas, heat released = 393.5 kJ\n∴ When 35.2 g of CO₂ is formed from carbon and dioxygen gas, heat released = 393.5 × 35.4 / 44 = 314.8 kJ \nThus, ΔH = -314.8 kJ','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','-314.8 kJ','-314.8 J','-3148 kJ','-3148 J','NEW','11-191','11-176',NULL),
(61411,'chemistry','Calculate the enthalpy change on freezing of 1.0 mol of water at 10.0°C to ice at -10.0°C. Δ(fus)H = 6.03 kJ /mol at 0°C.\nCₚ[H₂O(l)] = 75.3 J /mol K,\nCₚ[H₂O(s)] = 36.8 J /mol K','-7.151 KJ/ mol','The enthalpy change on freezing from 10°C to -10°C may expressed as\n\nLiquid (10°C) -∆H₁ → Liquid (0°C) -∆H₂→ Solid(0°C) -∆H₃→ Solid(-10°C)\n\n∆H₁ = nCₚ[ H₂O(l) ] × ∆T = 1 × 75.3 J/mol K × -10/T =  -753 J/mol = -0.753 KJ/mol\n\n∆H₂ = n(-∆(fus) H ) = -1 × 6.03  A= -6.03 KJ/mol\n\n∆H₃ = nCₚ[ H₂O(s) ] = -1 × 36.8 ×10 = -368 J/mol\n\n∆H = ∆H₁ + ∆H₂ + ∆H₃  = -0.753 KJ/mol -6.03 KJ/mol -368 J/mol = -7.151 KJ/mol','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','-7.151 KJ/ mol','-7.151 J/ mol','-7151 KJ/ mol','-7151 J/ mol','NEW','11-190','11-176',NULL),
(61412,'chemistry','Calculate the number of kJ of heat necessary to raise the temperature of 60.0 g of aluminium from 35°C to 55°C. Molar heat capacity of Al is 24 J /mol K','1.07kJ','Mass of Al = 60 g\nRise in temperature, ΔT = 55 - 35 = 20°C\nMolar heat capacity of Al = 24 J /mol K\nSpecific heat capacity of Al = 24/27 J/gK\nEnergy required = m x c x ΔT = 60×24/27×20 = 1066.67 J=1.068kJ or 1.07kJ','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','1.07kJ','1.7kJ','1.09kJ','17kJ','NEW','11-191','11-176',NULL),
(61413,'chemistry','In a process, 701 J of heat is absorbed by a system and 394 S of work is done by the system. What is the change in internal energy for the process?','307 J','Heat absorbed by the system (q) = 701 J\nWork done by the system (w) = -394 J\nAccording to first law of thermodynamics,\nΔU = q + w = 701 + (-394) = 701 – 394 = 307 J','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','307 J','1095 J','1195 J','317 J','NEW','11-190','11-176',NULL),
(61414,'chemistry','A reaction, A + B → C + D + q is found to have a positive entropy change. The reaction will be','Possible at any temperature','A + B → C + D + q, ΔS = +ve\nHere, ΔH = -ve\nΔG = ΔH – TΔS\nFor reaction to be spontaneous, ΔG should be -ve. As ΔH = -ve and ΔS is +ve, ΔG will be -ve at any temperature.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','Possible at high temperature','Possible only at low temperature','Not possible at any temperature','Possible at any temperature','NEW','11-190','11-176',NULL),
(61415,'chemistry','The enthalpy of combustion of methane, graphite and dihydrogen at 298 K are, -890.3 kJ /mol,-393.5 kJ /mol and-285.8 kJ /mol respectively. Enthalpy of formation of CH₄(g) will be','-74.8 kJ /mol','CH₄ +2O₂  → CO₂ + 2H₂0 ; ΔH = -890.3 kJ/mol -----(1)\n\nC + O₂ → CO₂ ; ΔH = -393.5 KJ/mol -----(2)\n\nH₂ + 1/2O₂ → H₂O ; ΔH = -285.8 KJ/mol  ------(3)\n\nAdd equation (2) & (3) and subtract equation (1) , we get \n\nC + 2H₂ → CH₄ \n\nΔH = -393.5 - 571.6 + 890.3 = -74.8 KJ/mol\n\nEnthalpy of formation of CH₄ is -74.8 KJ/mol','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','-74.8 kJ /mol','-52.27 kJ/mol','74.8 kJ /mol','52.26 kJ /mol','NEW','11-190','11-176',NULL),
(61416,'chemistry','ΔU° of combustion of methane is – X kJ/mol. The value of ΔH° is','<ΔU°','CH₄(g)+2O₂ → CO₂(g)+2H₂O(l)\nΔn = 1 – 3 = -2. \nΔH° = ΔU° + ΔnRT = -X- 2RT','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','= ΔU°','>ΔU°','<ΔU°','0','NEW','11-191','11-176',NULL),
(61417,'chemistry','The enthalpies of all elements in their standard states are','Zero','By convention, the standard enthalpy of formation of every element in its standard state is zero.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','Unity','Zero','<0','Different for each element','NEW','11-191','11-176',NULL),
(61418,'chemistry','For the process to occur under adiabatic conditions, the correct condition is','g = 0','Adiabatic system does not exchange heat with the surroundings.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','ΔT = 0','Δp = 0','g = 0','w = 0','NEW','11-189','11-141',NULL),
(61419,'chemistry','Choose the correct answer. A thermodynamic state function is a quantity','Whose value is independent of path','State function is a property of the system whose value depends only upon the state of the system and is independent of the path or the manner by which the state is reached.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','Used to determine heat changes','Whose value is independent of path','Used to determine pressure volume work','Whose value depends on temperature only','NEW','11-190','11-176',NULL),
(61501,'chemistry','Assertion:  Cdiamond → Cgraphite\nΔH and ΔU are same for this reaction.\nReason:  Entropy increases during the conversion of diamond to graphite.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','For the reaction, Δng\n = 0\n∴ ΔH = ΔU\nAlso, graphite is more disordered because of sp² hybridization and π electrons.\n⇒ ΔS is +ve','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-165','11-141',NULL),
(61502,'chemistry','Assertion:  Specific heat is an intensive property.\nReason:  Heat capacity is an extensive property.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','Specific heat C = q/mΔT\n\nq/m = extensive/ extensive = intensive \n\nHeat capacity, S = q /ΔT\n\n∵ q is extensive\n⇒ S is also extensive','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-169','11-145',NULL),
(61503,'chemistry','Assertion:  All reactions which are exothermic are spontaneous.\nReason:  All reactions in which entropy increases are spontaneous.','Both A and R are false','Not all exothermic reactions are spontaneous. Neither those reactions in which entropy increases. Only those\n reactions are spontaneous for which ΔG < 0.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-181','11-156',NULL),
(61504,'chemistry','Assertion:  Enthalpy of neutralisation of 1 equivalent each of HCl and H₂\nSO₄ with NaOH is same.\nReason:  Enthalpy of neutralisation is always the heat evolved when 1 mole acid is neutralised by a base.','A is true but R is false','1 eq of strong acid and strong base gives out – 57.1 kJ g/eq\nEnthalpy of neutralisation is heat evolved when 1 g equivalent of acid reacts with 1 g equivalent of base.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-179','11-155',NULL),
(61505,'chemistry','Assertion:  q and w are path function.\nReason:  q + w is a state function.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','q and w are path functions, while, q + w = ΔU is a state function and do not depend upon path.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-163','11-149',NULL),
(61506,'chemistry','Assertion:  Dissolution of sugar in water proceed via increases in entropy.\nReason:  Entropy decreases when egg is hard boiled.','A is true but R is false','Dissolution of sugar in water results in increase in entropy\n\nC₁₂H₂₂O₁₁ + H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆\n\nWhen egg is boiled, bonds are broken\n and egg attains a more disordered state\n⇒ ΔS = +ve\nAlso, egg is boiled ⇒ spontaneous process.\nSo, ΔG < 0 at 100°C\n∵ ΔH is +ve, ΔS must be +ve for ΔG to be –ve','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-182','11-158',NULL),
(61507,'chemistry','Assertion:  For an isolated system ΔG = –TΔStotal.\nReason:  For an isolated system q = 0.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','For an isolated system, ΔH = 0\n⇒ ΔG = – TΔStotal','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-181','11-156',NULL),
(61508,'chemistry','Assertion:  Combustion is an exothermic process.\nReason:  Combustion is a spontaneous process.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','Combustion is exothermic and also spontaneous.\nSince ΔG < 0.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-181','11-156',NULL),
(61509,'chemistry','Assertion:  Total enthalpy change of a multistep process is sum of ΔH1 + ΔH2 + ΔH3 + .....\nReason:  When heat is absorbed by the system, the sign of q is taken to be negative.','A is true but R is false','∵ ΔH is a state function, So for multistep process,\nΔH = ΔH₁\n + ΔH₂+ ΔH\n₃+ ……\nAlso, when heat is absorbed by the system, q is +ve.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-165','11-141',NULL),
(61510,'chemistry','Assertion:  Bond energy is equal to enthalpy of formation with negative sign.\nReason:  Bond energy is energy required to dissociate 1 mole single bond.','A is true but R is false','Bond energy is the energy required to dissociate 1 mole of bonds (can be single, double or triple) ⇒ Reason\n is false.\nNow, ΔHᵣ\n = (B.E.)reactants – (B.E.)products\nLets take an example, of H₂O\n\nΔHբ  = (B.E.)reactants – (B.E.)products\nΔHf\n = – (ΔHբ\n)\nreactants + (ΔH\nբ\nproducts\n\nComparing, (ΔHբ\n) = – B.E.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-164','11-140',NULL),
(61511,'chemistry','Assertion:  ΔH is positive for endothermic reactions.\nReason:  If total enthalpies of reactants and products are Hᵣ & Hₚ respectively then for an endothermic reaction\n Hᵣ < Hₚ.','A is true but R is false','If Hᵣ and Hₚ are total enthalpies of reactants and products respectively, then for an endothermic reaction\nHᵣ < Hₚ\nor, Hₚ – Hᵣ < 0\n⇒ ΔH < 0','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-164','11-140',NULL),
(61512,'chemistry','Assertion:  The energy of the universe is constant, whereas the entropy of the universe is continuously increasing.\nReason:  For spontaneous process ΔS > 0.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','For universe, energy is constant while ΔS > 0\nAlso, for a spontaneous process, ΔSuniverse > 0','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-182','11-158',NULL),
(61513,'chemistry','Assertion:  A non-spontaneous process becomes spontaneous when coupled with a suitable spontaneous reaction.\nReason:  The overall free energy of coupled spontaneous reaction is negative.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Overall free energy change for coupled spontaneous reaction is –ve.\nAlso, A non spontaneous reaction becomes spontaneous if coupled with another reaction so that\n Δ(Goverall) < 0.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-181','11-156',NULL),
(61514,'chemistry','Assertion:  An ideal crystal has more entropy than a real crystal.\nReason:  An ideal crystal has more disorder.','Both A and R are false','An ideal crystal has lesser entropy (more ordered) than a real crystal.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-182','11-158',NULL),
(61515,'chemistry','Assertion:  Work done in an irreversible isothermal process at constant volume is zero.\nReason:  Work is assigned negative sign during expansion and is assigned positive sign during compression.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','Work done at constant volume = 0\n∵ w = – pext ΔV\nAlso, according to 10 PAC convention,\nexpansion ⇒ w = –ve\ncompression ⇒ w = +ve.','Physical chemistry >> Thermodynamics >> Assertion and Reason',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','11-165','11-141',NULL),
(210634,'chemistry','Why are pentahalides of P, As, Sb and Bi are more covalent than the trihalides?','Due to high polarization power in 5+ oxidation state','Greater the oxidation state, more will be the polarization power, and according to Fajan\'s principle, more the polarization power, more will be the covalent character in pentahalides of P, As, Sb and Bi. Also central atom has 5+ oxidation while in trihalides central atom has 3+ oxidation state.\n(NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 174, Intext question)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Due to high polarization power in 5+ oxidation state','Due to low polarization power in 5+ oxidation state','Due to high polarization power in 3+ oxidation state','Due to low polarization power in 3+ oxidation state','NEW','12-174',NULL,NULL),
(210635,'chemistry','Which one is the strongest reducing agent?','BiH₃','Down group size increases, so among group 15 elements Bi has largest size and size compatibility with Hydrogen is smallest,so it is readily available in form of hydride and can add to other chemical species. Addition of hydrogen is reduction reaction\n(NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 174, Intext question)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','PH₃','AsH₃','SbH₃','BiH₃','NEW','12-174',NULL,NULL),
(210906,'chemistry','The presence of a lone pair of electrons on the nitrogen atom of the ammonia molecule makes ammonia a _________ .','Lewis base','The presence of a lone pair of electrons on the nitrogen atom of the ammonia molecule makes it a Lewis base. \n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 177, Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Ammonia - Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Lewis base','Lewis acid','Conjugate acid','Conjugate base','NEW','12-177',NULL,NULL),
(210907,'chemistry','Match the following:','Zn(OH)₂ (s)-White ppt, Fe₂O₃.xH₂O (s)-Brown ppt, [Cu(NH₃)₄]₂ (aq)-deep blue, [Ag(NH₃)₂]Cl (aq)-colourless','The correct match is Zn(OH)₂(s)-White ppt, Fe₂O₃.xH₂O(s)-Brown ppt, [Cu(NH₃)₄]₂(aq)-deep blue, [Ag(NH₃)₂]Cl(aq)-colourless\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 177, Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Ammonia - Properties',NULL,'3','match','Zn(OH)₂ (s),White ppt','Fe₂O₄.xH₂O (s),Brown ppt','[Cu(NH₃)₄]₂ (aq),deep blue','[Ag(NH₃)₂]Cl (aq),colourless','NEW','12-177',NULL,NULL),
(210908,'chemistry','Why does NH₃ act as lewis base?','Due to the presence of lone pair','According to lewis concept a base can donate electrons, since nitrogen has lone pair so most of nitrogenous compounds act as base\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 177, Eaxample 7.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Ammonia - Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Due to its high electronegativity','Due to the presence of lone pair','Due to its ability to release OH⁻ ions','Due to its ability to release H⁺  ions','NEW','12-177',NULL,NULL),
(211901,'chemistry','What are the oxidation states of Po?','–2, 2, 4, 6','The oxidation states of Po are -2, 2, 4, 6\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 186 Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Physical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','2, 4','–2, 2, 4, 6','–2, –1, 1, 2','2, 4, 2, 6','NEW','12-186',NULL,NULL),
(211902,'chemistry','The elements of Group-16 have _____ electrons in the outermost shell and have ___________ general electronic configuration.','six, ns²np⁴','The elements of Group-16 have six electrons in the outermost shell and have ns²np⁴ general electronic configuration.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 186 Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Physical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','six, ns²np³','six, ns²np⁴','four, ns²np⁴','five, ns²np³','NEW','12-186',NULL,NULL),
(211903,'chemistry','What is the electronegativity of S?','2.58','The electronegativity of Sulphur is 2.58.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 186 Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Physical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','2.58','3.5','2.55','2.53','NEW','12-186',NULL,NULL),
(211904,'chemistry','Which oxidation state is not shown by oxygen?','4','Since electronegativity of oxygen is very high, it shows only negative oxidation state as –2 except in the case of OF₂ where its oxidation state is +2. It shows -1 oxidation state in Peroxides.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 186 Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Physical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','2-','1-','2+','4','NEW','12-186',NULL,NULL),
(211905,'chemistry','Due to increase in the number of shells, how do atomic and ionic radii vary?','They increase from top to bottom in the group 16.','Due to increase in the number of shells, atomic and ionic radii increase from top to bottom in the group 16.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 186 Paragraph No. 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Physical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','They increase from top to bottom in the group 16.','They decrease from top to bottom in the group 16.','They increase from bottom to top in the group 16.','They remain constant from top to bottom in the group 16.','NEW','12-186',NULL,NULL),
(211906,'chemistry','What is the trend of ionisation enthalpy in Group-16 elements?','It decreases from top to bottom in the group 16.','Ionisation enthalpy decreases down the group. It is due to increase in size.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 187 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Physical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','It increases from top to bottom in the group 16.','It decreases from top to bottom in the group 16.','It decrease for first 4 elements, and then remains constant.','It remains constant from top to bottom in the group 16.','NEW','12-187',NULL,NULL),
(211907,'chemistry','The Ionization potenstial of group 16 elements is ________ than group 15 elements','lower','The Ionization potenstial of group 16 elements is lower than group 15 elements\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 187 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Physical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','lower','higher','sightly higher','extremely lower','NEW','12-187',NULL,NULL),
(211908,'chemistry','Because of the compact nature of oxygen atom, it has-','less negative electron gain enthalpy than sulphur.','Because of the compact nature of oxygen atom, it has less negative electron gain enthalpy than sulphur. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 187 Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Physical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','more negative electron gain enthalpy than sulphur.','less negative electron gain enthalpy than sulphur.','very high negative electron gain enthalpy than sulphur.','less positive electron gain enthalpy than sulphur.','NEW','12-187',NULL,NULL),
(211909,'chemistry','What is the trend of electron affinity from S to Po?','Gradually decrease down the group','Electron affinity gradually decreases from top to bottom.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 187 Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Physical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Gradually decrease down the group','Gradually Increase down the group','First increase then decrease','First decrease then increase','NEW','12-187',NULL,NULL),
(211910,'chemistry','Next to fluorine,  __________ has the highest electronegativity value amongst the elements.','oxygen','Next to fluorine, oxygen has the highest electronegativity value amongst the elements. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 187 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Physical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','sulphur','helium','oxygen','barium','NEW','12-187',NULL,NULL),
(211911,'chemistry','Which among the following is metal?','Polonium','Oxygen and sulphur are non-metals, selenium and tellurium metalloids, whereas polonium is a metal. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 187 Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Physical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Selenium','Tellurium','Sulphur','Polonium','NEW','12-187',NULL,NULL),
(211912,'chemistry','Match the following','Oxygen-Diatomic, Sulphur-Polyatomic, Paramagnetic-Oxygen, Diamagnetic-Sulphur','The correct match is Oxygen-Diatomic, Sulphur-Polyatomic, Paramagnetic-Oxygen, Diamagnetic-Sulphur\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 187 Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Physical Properties',NULL,'3','match','Oxygen,Diatomic','Sulphur,Polyatomic','Paramagnetic,Oxygen','Diamagnetic,Sulphur','NEW','12-187',NULL,NULL),
(211913,'chemistry','Why is there a large difference in melting and boiling points of Oxygen and sulphur?','It is due to there atomicity','The large difference between the melting and boiling points of oxygen and sulphur may be explained on the basis of their atomicity; oxygen exists as diatomic molecule (O₂) whereas sulphur exists as polyatomic molecule (S₈).\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 187 Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Physical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','It is due to there electronegativity','It is due to there Reactiviity','It is due to there size','It is due to there atomicity','NEW','12-187',NULL,NULL),
(212001,'chemistry','How does stability of -2 oxidation state in Group 16 vary?','It decreases from top to bottom in the group 16.','The stability of -2 oxidation state decreases down the group.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 187 Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','It increases from top to bottom in the group 16.','It decreases from top to bottom in the group 16.','It increases from top to bottom for the first 4 elements.','It remains constant from top to bottom in the group 16.','NEW','12-187',NULL,NULL),
(212002,'chemistry','In which of the following the oxidation state of Oxygen is +2?','OF₂','Let the oxidation state of Oxygen be x.\nThe oxidation state of Fl is -1\nTherefore for OF₂,\nx + 2(-1) = 0\nx = 2\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 187 Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','H₂O','CO₂','OF₂','NO₂','NEW','12-187',NULL,NULL),
(212003,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and Reason (R).\n\nAssertion : The stability of +4 oxidation state increase down the group in Group-16.\n\nReason : Inert pair effect increase down the group which prevents the loss of ns electrons.','Both assertion and reason are true and the reason is a correct explanation of the assertion','The stability of + 6 oxidation state decreases down the group and stability of + 4 oxidation state increases. This is due to inert pair effect in +4 oxidation state.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 187 Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and the reason is a correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true and the reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','12-187',NULL,NULL),
(212004,'chemistry','What kind of bonding is observed in +4 and +6 oxidation states?','Covalent','Bonding in +4 and +6 oxidation states is primarily covalent.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 187 Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Covalent','Ionic','Co-ordinate','Hydrogen','NEW','12-187',NULL,NULL),
(212005,'chemistry','Choose the wrong statement\n\na. The anomalous behaviour of oxygen is due to its small size and high electronegativity as compared to corresponding menbers of group\nb. H₂O is liquid while H₂S is gas due to hydrogen bonding which is present only in H₂O\nc. All members of group-16 have d -subshell\nd. Oxygen has the ability to expand its covalence beyond 2.','Only c and d','Oxygen, being a Group-16 element, doesn\'t have d-subshell and therefore it cannot expand its covalance more than 4. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 187 Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c and d','Only d','NEW','12-187',NULL,NULL),
(212006,'chemistry','Which type of hydrides are formed by elements of Group-16?','H₂E','Elements of group-16 form H₂E type of hydrides where E= O, S, Se, Te.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 188 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','H₃E','H₂E','H₆E','HE','NEW','12-188',NULL,NULL),
(212007,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statement about hydrides of group 16 (E= O, S, Se, Te).\n\na. The acidic character of hydrides of decrease down the group due to decrease in bond dissociation energy of H-E\n\nb. Thermal stability from decreases from H₂O to H₂Te.\n\nc. All hydrides are good reducing agents except H₂O.\n\nd. Reducing ability of hydrides increase down the group.','Only a','The acidic character of hydrides of increase down the group due to decrease in bond dissociation energy of H-E\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 188 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c and d','All statements are correct','NEW','12-188',NULL,NULL),
(212008,'chemistry','What is the enthalpy of dissociation in kJ/mol for H₂S?','347 kJ/mol','The enthalpy of dissociation of H₂S is 347 kJ/mol\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 188 Table 7.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','347 kJ/mol','463 kJ/mol','276 kJ/mol','238 kJ/mol','NEW','12-188',NULL,NULL),
(212009,'chemistry','Choose the wrong statement about oxides of group 16 (E= O,S,Se,Te,)\n\na. All these elements form oxides of the type EO₂ \nb. O₃ and SO₂ are geases whileSeO₂ is solid\nc. All oxides are good oxidizing agents except H₂O\nd. Oxidizing ability of oxides increase down the group','Only c','The correct statement is \'All the hydrides except water possess reducing property and this character increases from H₂S to H₂Te\'.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 188 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c','Only d','NEW','12-188',NULL,NULL),
(212010,'chemistry','SO₃ is ______ acidic than SO₂','more','SO₃ is more acidic than SO₂\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 188 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','less','more','slightly less','slightly more','NEW','12-188',NULL,NULL),
(212011,'chemistry','Choose the wrong statement regarding halides of group 16 elements (E= O, S, Se, Te)\n\na. All members form halides of the type EX₆, EX₄, EX₂.\nb. Among hexahalides, EF₆ are the only stable halides.\nc. All hexahalides are gaseous and have octahedral geometry.\nd. SF₆ is not stable due to steric factor','only d','Amongst hexahalides, hexafluorides are the only stable halides. All hexafluorides are gaseous in nature. They have octahedral structure. Sulphur hexafluoride, SF₆ is exceptionally stable for steric reasons.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 188 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','only a','only b','only c','only d','NEW','12-188',NULL,NULL),
(212012,'chemistry','In which order does the stabilty of halides decrease?','F⁻ > Cl⁻ > Br⁻ > I⁻','The stability of halides decrease in the order F⁻ > Cl⁻ > Br⁻ > I⁻\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 188 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','F⁻ > Cl⁻ > Br⁻ > I⁻','F⁻ < Cl⁻ < Br⁻ < I⁻','Cl⁻ > F⁻ > Br⁻ > I⁻','F⁻ > Cl⁻ < Br⁻ > I⁻','NEW','12-188',NULL,NULL),
(212013,'chemistry','What is the geometry of hexahalides of Group-16?','octahedral','All hexahalides are gaseous in nature. They have octahedral structure.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 188 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','tetrahedral','octahedral','trigonal planar','square planar','NEW','12-188',NULL,NULL),
(212014,'chemistry','Amongst tetrafluorides, SF₄ is a _____, SeF₄ a _____ and TeF₄ a _____.','gas, liquid, solid','Amongst tetrafluorides, SF₄ is a gas, SeF₄ a liquid and TeF₄ a solid. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 188 Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','solid, gas, liquid,','gas, solid, liquid','gas, solid, liquid','gas, liquid, solid','NEW','12-188',NULL,NULL),
(212015,'chemistry','All elements except _______ form dichlorides and dibromides.','oxygen','All elements except oxygen form dichlorides and dibromides. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 188 Paragraph No. 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','oxygen','sulphur','selenium','polonium','NEW','12-188',NULL,NULL),
(212016,'chemistry','Choose the wrong statement about dihalides of group-16.\n\na. Dichlorides and dibromides are formed by sp³ hybridization\nb. They have tetrahedral geometry.\nc. They undergo oxidation reactions.\nd. They are dimers of monohalides.','Only c','The correct statement is \'Dihalides undergo disproportionation reaction\'.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 188 Paragraph No. 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c','only d','NEW','12-188',NULL,NULL),
(212017,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and Reason (R)\n\nAssertion : H₂S is less acidic than H₂Te\n\nReason : The bond dissociation energy of H-S is larger than H-Te','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Due to the decrease in bond (E–H) dissociation enthalpy down the group, acidic character increases.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 189 Intext question)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','12-189',NULL,NULL),
(212018,'chemistry','What is the order of thermal stability of hydrides of group 16 elements?','H₂O > H₂S > H₂Se > H₂Te','The order of thermal stability of hydrides of group 16 elements is H₂O > H₂S > H₂Se > H₂Te.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 189 Intext question)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','H₂O > H₂S > H₂Se > H₂Te','H₂O < H₂S < H₂Se < H₂Te','H₂O > H₂S < H₂Se > H₂Te','H₂O < H₂S > H₂Se > H₂Te','NEW','12-189',NULL,NULL),
(212019,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason (R).\n\nAsserion : H₂O is liquid while H₂S is a gas.\n\nReason: Hydrogen bonding present in  H₂O and absent in  H₂S.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Because of small size and high electronegativity of oxygen, molecules of water are highly associated through hydrogen bonding resulting in its liquid state.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 189 Intext question)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','12-189',NULL,NULL),
(212101,'chemistry','Dioxygen can be obtained in labs by heating oxygen containing _______.','salts','Dioxygen can be obtained in the laboratory by heating oxygen containing salts such as chlorates, nitrates and permanganates.\n2KClO₃ + Heat → 2KCl + 3O₂\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 189 Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Dioxides - Preparation',NULL,'3','mcq','acids','salts','bases','alkalis','NEW','12-189',NULL,NULL),
(212102,'chemistry','Electrolysis of water leads to the release of-','hydrogen at cathode and oxygen at anode','Electrolysis of water leads to the release of hydrogen at the cathode and oxygen at the anode.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 189 Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Dioxides - Preparation',NULL,'2','mcq','hydrogen at anode and oxygen at cathode','hydrogen at cathode and oxygen at anode','hydronium ions at cathode and oxygen at anode','hydronium ions at anode and oxygen at cathode','NEW','12-189',NULL,NULL),
(212201,'chemistry','Dioxygen is a _________ and ________ gas.','colourless, odourless','Dioxygen is a colourless and odourless gas.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 189 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Dioxides - Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','pale blue, odourless','colourless, odourless','pale blue, toxic','colourless, toxic','NEW','12-189',NULL,NULL),
(212202,'chemistry','Solubility of dioxygen in water is to the extent of ________ in 100 cm³ water at 293 K.','3.08 cm³','Solubility of dioxygen in water is to the extent of 3.08 cm³ in 100 cm³ water at 293 K. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 189 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Dioxides - Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','3.08 cm³','3.5 cm³','3.9 cm³','3.01 cm³','NEW','12-189',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(212203,'chemistry','Dioxygen liquefies at ______ and freezes at _____.','90 K, 55 K','Dioxygen liquefies at 90 K and freezes at 55 K.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 189 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Dioxides - Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','293 K, 45 K','90 K, 55 K','116 K, 33 K','100 K, 50 K','NEW','12-189',NULL,NULL),
(212204,'chemistry','Oxygen atom has three stable isotopes: ______,_____ and _______.','¹⁶O,¹⁷O,¹⁸O','Oxygen atom has three stable isotopes: ¹⁶O,¹⁷O and ¹⁸O\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 189 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Dioxides - Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','¹⁶O, ¹⁷O, ²³O','¹⁶O,¹⁷O,¹⁸O','¹⁸O, ¹⁹O, ²³O','¹⁹O, ²¹O, ²³O','NEW','12-189',NULL,NULL),
(212205,'chemistry','What is the bond dissociation enthalpy of oxgyen-oxygen double bond?','493.4 kJ/mol','Bond dissociation enthalpy of oxgyen-oxygen double bond is 493.4 kJ/mol . \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 190 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Dioxides - Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','483.4 kJ/mol','490.4 kJ/mol','293.4 kJ/mol','493.4 kJ/mol','NEW','12-190',NULL,NULL),
(212206,'chemistry','Which of the following does not react with oxygen directly?','Pt','Dioxygen directly reacts with nearly all metals and non-metals except some metals ( e.g., Au, Pt) and some noble gases.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 190 Intext question)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Dioxides - Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Pt','Fe','Zn','Ti','NEW','12-190',NULL,NULL),
(212207,'chemistry','Complete the following reaction\n4Al + 3O₂ 🠆','2Al₂O₃','The complete reaction is - 4Al + 3O₂ 🠆 2Al₂O₃\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 190 Intext question)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Dioxides - Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','2Al₂O₃','Al₃O₂','AlO','AlO₂','NEW','12-190',NULL,NULL),
(212301,'chemistry','A binary compound of which element with another element is called oxide?','Oxygen','A binary compound of oxygen with another element is called oxide. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 190 Paragraph No. 3','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Simple Oxides',NULL,'1','mcq','Ozone','Oxygen','Oxoacid','Nacent oxygen','NEW','12-190',NULL,NULL),
(212302,'chemistry','What type of oxides are Pb₂O₄ and Fe₃O₄?','Mixed oxides','Pb₂O₄ and Fe₃O₄ are mixed oxides.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 190 Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Simple Oxides',NULL,'1','mcq','Simple oxides','Mixed oxides','Acidic oxides','Basic oxides','NEW','12-190',NULL,NULL),
(212303,'chemistry','What are the oxides which give only base with water known as?','Basic oxides','The oxides which give a base with water are known as basic oxides. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 190 Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Simple Oxides',NULL,'1','mcq','Acidic oxides','Basic oxides','Neutral oxides','Amphoteric oxides','NEW','12-190',NULL,NULL),
(212304,'chemistry','What does CaO combines with water to give?','Base','CaO combines with water to give  Ca(OH)₂. Ca(OH)₂ is a base.\nCaO + H₂O →  Ca(OH)₂\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 190 Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Simple Oxides',NULL,'1','mcq','Acid','Base','Neutral oxides','Amphoteric oxides','NEW','12-190',NULL,NULL),
(212305,'chemistry','Amphoteric oxides are-','acidic as well as basic in nature','Amphoteric oxides are acidic as well as basic in nature\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 191 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Simple Oxides',NULL,'3','mcq','acidic in nature','acidic as well as basic in nature','basic in nature','neutral in nature','NEW','12-191',NULL,NULL),
(212306,'chemistry','Match the following','Mn₂O₇-Acidic oxide, BaO-Basic oxide, Al₂O₃-Amphoteric oxide, MgO-Normal oxide','The correct match is Mn₂O₇-Acidic oxide, BaO-Basic oxide, Al₂O₃-Amphoteric oxide, MgO-Normal oxide\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 191 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Simple Oxides',NULL,'3','match','Mn₂O₇,Acidic oxide','BaO,Basic oxide','Al₂O₃,Amphoteric oxide','MgO,Normal oxide','NEW','12-191',NULL,NULL),
(212401,'chemistry','Ozone is an _________ form of oxygen.','allotropic','Ozone is an allotropic form of oxygen. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 191 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Ozone',NULL,'1','mcq','allotropic','isotopic','isotropic','isobaric','NEW','12-191',NULL,NULL),
(212402,'chemistry','The harmful concentration of ozone is above-','100 ppm','The harmful concentration of ozone is above 100 ppm\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 191 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Ozone',NULL,'1','mcq','100 ppm','80 ppm','20 ppm','200 ppm','NEW','12-191',NULL,NULL),
(212403,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and Reason (R).\n\nAssertion : Ozone is thermodynamically unstable as compared to oxygen.\n\nReason : Decomposition of ozone is exothermic and leads to increase in entropy','Both assertion and reason are true and the reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Ozone is thermodynamically unstable with respect to oxygen since its decomposition into oxygen results in the liberation of heat (∆H is negative) and an increase in entropy (∆S is positive). These two effects reinforce each other, resulting in large negative Gibbs energy change (∆G) for its conversion into oxygen.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 191 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Ozone',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and the reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true and the reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','12-191',NULL,NULL),
(212404,'chemistry','Ozone is a powerful ______.','oxidising agent','Ozone is a powerful oxidizing agent.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 191 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Ozone',NULL,'1','mcq','oxidising agent','reducing agent','colorising agent','catalyst','NEW','12-191',NULL,NULL),
(212405,'chemistry','In quantitative test of ozone,  it is reacted with an excess of KI solution buffered with borate liberated iodine is titrated against standard solution of-','Sodium thiosulphate','When ozone reacts with an excess of potassium iodide solution buffered with a borate buffer (pH 9.2), iodine is liberated which can be titrated against a standard solution of sodium thiosulphate. This is a quantitative method for estimating O3 gas.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 191 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Ozone',NULL,'1','mcq','Sodium thiosulphate','Sodium thiosulphite','Sodium thiosulphide','Sodium hydroxide','NEW','12-191',NULL,NULL),
(212406,'chemistry','Formation of ozone from oxygen is an ___________ process.','endothermic','Formation of ozone from oxygen is an endothermic process. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 191 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Ozone',NULL,'1','mcq','exothermic','endothermic','combustion','reduction','NEW','12-191',NULL,NULL),
(212407,'chemistry','What is the colour of pure ozone?','Pale blue','Pure ozone is a pale blue gas, dark blue liquid and violet-black solid.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 191 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Ozone',NULL,'2','mcq','Pale blue','Colourless','Dark blue','Violet-black','NEW','12-191',NULL,NULL),
(212408,'chemistry','Match the following','NO-Supersonic jet aeroplanes, Freons-Ozone, Ozone-Angular geometry, Manufacturing of KMnO₄-O₃ as oxidizng agent','The correct match is - NO-Supersonic jet aeroplanes, Freons-Ozone, Ozone-Angular geometry, Manufacturing of KMnO₄-O₃ as oxidizng agent\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 192 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Ozone',NULL,'3','match','NO,Supersonic jet aeroplanes','Freons,Ozone','Ozone,Angular geometry','Manufacturing of KMnO₄,O₃ as oxidizng agent','NEW','12-192',NULL,NULL),
(212501,'chemistry','Yellow rhombic (α-sulphur) and monoclinic (β -sulphur) are the ______________ of sulphur.','allotropes','Yellow rhombic (α-sulphur) and monoclinic (β -sulphur) are the allotropes of sulphur. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 192 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphur -Allotropic Forms',NULL,'1','mcq','allotropes','isotopes','isotropes','isobars','NEW','12-192',NULL,NULL),
(212502,'chemistry','What is the colour of α-sulphur?','Yellow','Rhombic sulphur (α-sulphur) is yellow in colour\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 192 Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphur -Allotropic Forms',NULL,'1','mcq','Brown','Black','Yellow','Red','NEW','12-192',NULL,NULL),
(212503,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statement about rhombic sulphur\n\na. Its crystals are formed by evaporating the solution of roll sulphur in CS₂\n\nb. It is soluble in water\n\nc. It is partially soluble in benzene,ether \n\nd. It is readily soluble in CS₂','Only b','Rhombic sulphur crystals are formed on evaporating the solution of roll sulphur in CS₂. It is insoluble in water but dissolves to some extent in benzene, alcohol and ether. It is readily soluble in CS₂.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 192 Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphur -Allotropic Forms',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c and d','All statemnets are correct','NEW','12-192',NULL,NULL),
(212504,'chemistry','What is the specific gravity of β-sulphur?','1.98','The specific gravity of β-sulphur is 1.98.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 192 Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphur -Allotropic Forms',NULL,'3','mcq','1.98','1.88','1.89','1.08','NEW','12-192',NULL,NULL),
(212505,'chemistry','Choose the wrong statement about β-sulphur\n\na. It is prepared by melting rhombic sulphur\n\nb. Its crystals are needle shaped\n\nc. It is stable above 369K and transforms to rhombic sulphur below it.\n\nd. At 369 K β-sulphur form is stable.','only d','β-sulphur form of sulphur is prepared by melting rhombic sulphur in a dish and cooling, till crust is formed. Two holes are made in the crust and the remaining liquid poured out. On removing the crust, colourless needle shaped crystals of β-sulphur are formed. \n\nIt is stable above 369 K and transforms into α-sulphur below it. Conversely, α-sulphur is stable below 369 K and transforms into β-sulphur above this. \n\nAt 369 K both the forms are stable. This temperature is called transition temperature\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 192 Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphur -Allotropic Forms',NULL,'2','mcq','only a','only b','only c','only d','NEW','12-192',NULL,NULL),
(212506,'chemistry','What is the temperature at which two allotropic forms are stable called?','Transition temprature','α-sulphur is stable below 369 K and transforms into β-sulphur above this. At 369 K both the forms are stable. This temperature is called transition temperature.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 192 Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphur -Allotropic Forms',NULL,'1','mcq','Melting temprature','Transition temprature','Freezing temprature','Stable temperature','NEW','12-192',NULL,NULL),
(212507,'chemistry','Which form of sulphur is paramagnetic?','S₂','In vapour state sulphur partly exists as S₂ molecule which has two unpaired electrons in the antibonding π* orbitals like O₂ and, hence, exhibits paramagnetism.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 192 Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphur -Allotropic Forms',NULL,'2','mcq','S₆','S₂','S₈','S','NEW','12-192',NULL,NULL),
(212601,'chemistry','Sulphur dioxide is formed together with a little _______sulphur trioxide when sulphur is burnt in air or oxygen.','6-8%','Sulphur dioxide is formed together with a little (6-8%) sulphur trioxide when sulphur is burnt in air or oxygen.\nS (s) + O₂ (g) → SO₂ (g)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 193 Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphur oxides',NULL,'2','mcq','6-8%','8-10%','3-6%','2-4%','NEW','12-193',NULL,NULL),
(212602,'chemistry','Sulphur dioxide is a ________ gas with ______ smell and is _______ soluble in water.','colourless, pungent, highly','Sulphur dioxide is a colourless gas with pungent smell and is highly soluble in water.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 193 Paragraph No. 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphur oxides',NULL,'3','mcq','yellow, pungent, highly','colourless, pungent, highly','colourless, no, highly','colourless, pungent, not','NEW','12-193',NULL,NULL),
(212603,'chemistry','What is formed when Sulphur dioxide  is passed through water?','Sulphurous acid','Sulphur dioxide, when passed through water, forms a solution of sulphurous acid.\nSO₂ (g) + H₂O (l) ⇄ H₂SO₃ (aq)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 193 Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphur oxides',NULL,'2','mcq','Sulphurous acid','Sulphuric acid','Pyrosulphurous acid','Pyrosulphuric acid','NEW','12-193',NULL,NULL),
(212604,'chemistry','In the reaction with ______ and ______, the behaviour of sulphur dioxide is very similar to that of carbon dioxide.','water, alkalies','In the reaction with water and alkalies the behaviour of sulphur dioxide is very similar to that of carbon dioxide. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 193 Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphur oxides',NULL,'2','mcq','water, alkanes','water, alkalies','HCl, alkalies','HCl, alkanes','NEW','12-193',NULL,NULL),
(212605,'chemistry','What is SO₂Cl₂ is the chemical formula?','Sulphuryl chloride','Sulphur dioxide reacts with chlorine in the presence of charcoal (which acts as a catalyst) to give sulphuryl chloride, SO₂Cl₂.\nSO₂ (g) + Cl₂ (g) → SO₂Cl₂ (l)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 194 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphur oxides',NULL,'2','mcq','Sulphuryl chlorate','Sulphuric chlorate','Sulphonic chloride','Sulphuryl chloride','NEW','12-194',NULL,NULL),
(212606,'chemistry','Which of the following is the use of sulphur dioxide?','Refining petroleum and sugar','Sulphur dioxide is used in refining petroleum and sugar\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 194 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphur oxides',NULL,'2','mcq','Manufacture of many metals, particularly steel','As a germicide, disinfectant and for sterilising water','Refining petroleum and sugar','For bleaching woodpulp','NEW','12-194',NULL,NULL),
(212607,'chemistry','SO₂ act as reducing agent and converts-','Fe(III) to Fe(II)','SO₂ act as reducing agent and convert Fe(III) to Fe(II)\n2Fe³⁺ + SO₂ + 2H₂O → 2Fe²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 194 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphur oxides',NULL,'2','mcq','Fe(II) to Fe(I)','Fe(III) to Fe(II)','Fe(III) to Fe(0)','Fe(II) to Fe(0)','NEW','12-194',NULL,NULL),
(212608,'chemistry','Why are the two S-O bonds in SO₂ are equal?','Due to resonance','The two S-O bonds in SO₂ are equal due to resonance.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 194 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphur oxides',NULL,'1','mcq','Due to conjugation','Due to resonance','Due to electromeric effect','Due to inductive effect','NEW','12-194',NULL,NULL),
(212701,'chemistry','What is x in H₂SₓO₆?','x = 2 to 5','x in H₂SₓO₆ is x = 2 to 5\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 194 Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Oxoacids of Sulphur',NULL,'3','mcq','x = 2 to 4','x = 3 to 6','x = 2 to 5','x = 2 to 6','NEW','12-194',NULL,NULL),
(212702,'chemistry','What is the chemical formula for peroxodisulphuric acid?','H₂S₂O₈','The chemical formula of Peroxodisulphuric acid is H₂S₂O₂\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 194 Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Oxoacids of Sulphur',NULL,'2','mcq','H₂S₂O₇','H₂S₂O₈','H₂SO₄','H₂SO₃','NEW','12-194',NULL,NULL),
(212703,'chemistry','Match the A, B, C, D with their respective name.\ndiagram(n2105)','A-Sulphurus acid, B-Sulphuric acid, C-Peroxodisulphuric acid, D-Oleum','The correct match is A-Sulphurus acid, B-Sulphuric acid, C-Peroxodisulphuric acid, D-Oleum\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 194 Paragraph No. 4)\ndiagram(ne2105)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Oxoacids of Sulphur',NULL,'3','match','A,Sulphurus acid','B,Sulphuric acid','C,Peroxodisulphuric acid','D,Oleum','NEW','12-194',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/match/212703q.png'),
(212801,'chemistry','What is the name of the process by which Sulphuric acid is manufactured?','Contact','Sulphuric acid is manufactured by theContact process.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 195 Paragraph No. 1','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphuric Acid - Manufacture',NULL,'1','mcq','Contact','Haber\'s','Deacon\'s','Electrolytic','NEW','12-195',NULL,NULL),
(212802,'chemistry','In contact process, what does absorption of SO₃ in H₂SO₄ gives ?','H₂S₂O₇','In contact process, third step involves absorption of SO₃ in H₂SO₄ gives oleum H₂S₂O₇.\nSO₃ + H₂SO₄ → H₂S₂O₇\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 195 Paragraph No. 1','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphuric Acid - Manufacture',NULL,'2','mcq','H₂S₂O₅','H₂S₂O₈','H₂S₂O₆','H₂S₂O₇','NEW','12-195',NULL,NULL),
(212803,'chemistry','The conversion of SO₂ to SO₃ by the reaction with oxygen is done in the presence of which catalyst?','V₂O₅','The conversion of SO₂ to SO₃ by the reaction with oxygen is done in the presence of a catalyst V₂O₅. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 195 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphuric Acid - Manufacture',NULL,'2','mcq','V₂O₅','H₂','SO₄','MnO₂','NEW','12-195',NULL,NULL),
(212804,'chemistry','The reaction 2SO₂ (g)+ O₂ (g)→ 2SO₃ (g) in presence of vanadium pentoxide is;','Exothermic, reversible and the forward reaction','The key step in the manufacture of H2SO4 is the catalytic oxidation of SO2 with O2 to give SO3 in the presence of V2O5 (catalyst).\n2SO₂ (g)+ O₂ (g)→ 2SO₃ (g)\nThe reaction is exothermic, reversible and the forward reaction leadsto a decrease in volume. Therefore, low temperature and high pressure are the favourable conditions for maximum yield.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 195 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphuric Acid - Manufacture',NULL,'3','mcq','Exothermic, reversible and the forward reaction','Exothermic, reversible and backward reaction','Endothermic, irreversible and forward reaction','Exothermic, irreversible and forward reaction','NEW','12-195',NULL,NULL),
(212805,'chemistry','What is the purity of sulphuric acid obtained by Contact process?','96-98% pure','The sulphuric acid obtained by Contact process is 96-98% pure.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 195 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphuric Acid - Manufacture',NULL,'2','mcq','92-94% pure','96-98% pure','87-92% pure','61-67% pure','NEW','12-195',NULL,NULL),
(212806,'chemistry','What is the specific gravity of Sulphuric acid at 298 K ?','1.84','Sulphuric acid is a colourless, dense, oily liquid with a specific gravity of 1.84 at 298 K.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 196 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphuric Acid - Manufacture',NULL,'3','mcq','1.16','1.48','1.84','1.68','NEW','12-196',NULL,NULL),
(212807,'chemistry','______ the value of dissociation constant (Ka), the _______ is the acid.','Greater, stronger','The larger value of Kₐ₁ ( Kₐ₁ >10) means that H₂SO₄ is largely dissociated into H⁺ and HSO₄⁻ .Greater the value of dissociation constant (Ka), the stronger is the acid. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 196 Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphuric Acid - Manufacture',NULL,'1','mcq','Greater, stronger','Greater, weaker','Smaller, stronger','None of these','NEW','12-196',NULL,NULL),
(212808,'chemistry','Match the A, B, C, D in diagram(n2106)','A-Arsenic purifier,B-Venedium pentoxide,C-Catalytic converter, D-Dust precipitator','The correct match is A-Arsenic purifier,B-Venedium pentoxide,C-Catalytic converter, D-Dust precipitator\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 196 Paragraph No. 5)\ndiagram(ne2106)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphuric Acid - Manufacture',NULL,'3','match','A,Arsenic purifier','B,Venedium pentoxide','C,Catalytic converter','D, Dust precipitator','NEW','12-196',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/match/212808q.png'),
(212901,'chemistry','Concentrated sulphuric acid is a strong _______ agent.','dehydrating','Concentrated sulphuric acid is a strong dehydrating agent. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 196 Paragraph No. 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphuric Acid - Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','reducing','oxidising','dehydrating','bleaching','NEW','12-196',NULL,NULL),
(212902,'chemistry','Hot concentrated sulphuric acid is a moderately strong _______ agent.','oxidising','Hot concentrated sulphuric acid is a moderately strong oxidising agent. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 196 Paragraph No. 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphuric Acid - Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','reducing','oxidising','dehydrating','bleaching','NEW','12-196',NULL,NULL),
(212903,'chemistry','Both metals and non-metals are _______ by _______ sulphuric acid.','oxidised, concentrated','Both metals and non-metals are oxidised by concentrated sulphuric acid, which is reduced to SO₂.\nCu + 2H₂SO₄ (conc.) → CuSO₄ + SO₂ + 2H₂O\nS + 2H₂SO₄ (conc.) → 3SO₂ + 2H₂O\nC + 2H₂SO₄ (conc.) → CO₂ + 2SO₂ + 2H₂O\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 196 Paragraph No. 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphuric Acid - Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','reduced, dilute','reduced, concentrated','oxidised, concentrated','oxidoised, dilute','NEW','12-196',NULL,NULL),
(212904,'chemistry','When H₂SO₄ is added to CaF₂ what does it form?','HF','When H₂SO₄ is added to CaF₂ it forms hydrogen fluoride\nCaF₂ + H₂SO₄.→  CaSO₄ + 2HF\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 197 Intext question)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphuric Acid - Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','HF','SF₆','SO₂','H₂S','NEW','12-197',NULL,NULL),
(212905,'chemistry','When SO₃ is passed through water what does it form?','H₂SO₄','When SO₃ is passed through water it forms H₂SO₄\nSO₃ + H₂O → H₂SO₄\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 197 Intext question)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphuric Acid - Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','SO₂','H₂SO₄','H₂S','Oleum','NEW','12-197',NULL,NULL),
(212906,'chemistry','In which the following area H₂SO₄ is not used?','Anti-chlor disinfectant','The following are the uses of Sulphuric acid-\n(a) petroleum refining \n(b) manufacture of pigments, paints and dyestuf intermediates \n(c) detergent industry \n(d) metallurgical applications (e.g.,cleansing metals before enameling, electroplating and galvanising\n(e) storage batteries \n(f) in the manufacture of nitrocellulose products\n(g) as a laboratory reagent.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 197 Intext question)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphuric Acid - Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Fertilizers','Pigment industry','Metallurgy','Anti-chlor disinfectant','NEW','12-197',NULL,NULL),
(212907,'chemistry','Which of the following is not the optimum condition for the manufacturing of H₂SO₄ ?','High temperature','The reaction is exothermic, reversible and the forward reaction leads to a decrease in volume. Therefore, low temperature and high pressure are the favourable conditions for maximum yield. But the temperature should not be very low otherwise rate of reaction will become slow.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 197 Intext question)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphuric Acid - Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Low Temprature','High pressure','High temperature','None of these','NEW','12-197',NULL,NULL),
(212908,'chemistry','Why is  Kₐ₁ > Kₐ₂ for sulphuric acid?','H₂SO₄ is largely dissociated into H⁺ and HSO₄⁻','H₂SO₄ + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + HSO₄⁻ Kₐ₁ = Very large (Kₐ₁ > 10)\nHSO₄⁻ + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + SO₄²⁻ Kₐ₂ = 1.2 x 10⁻²\nThe larger value of Kₐ₁ (Kₐ₁ >10) means that H₂SO₄  is largely dissociated into  H⁺ and HSO₄⁻. Greater the value of dissociation constant (Ka), the stronger is the acid.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 197 Intext question)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Sulphuric Acid - Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','HSO₄⁻ is more stable than SO₄²⁻','H₂SO₄ is largely dissociated into H⁺ and HSO₄⁻','H₂SO₄ is more stable than HSO₄⁻','H₂SO₄ is largely dissociated into H⁺ and SO₄²⁻','NEW','12-197',NULL,NULL),
(213001,'chemistry','What are Group-17 elements called?','Halogens','Group 17 elements are called halogens.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 197 Paragraph No. 6','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 17 Elements - Occurance',NULL,'1','mcq','Noble gases','Halogens','Actinoid elements','Chacogens','NEW','12-197',NULL,NULL),
