(214102,'chemistry','Xenon forms ______ binary fluorides.','three','Xenon forms three binary fluorides, XeF₂, XeF₄ and XeF₆ by the direct reaction of elements under appropriate experimental conditions.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 210 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 18 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','two','three','four','five','NEW','12-210',NULL,NULL),
(214103,'chemistry','XeF₂, XeF₄ and XeF₆ are powerful-','fluorinating agents','XeF₂, XeF₄ and XeF₆ are colourless crystalline solids and sublime readily at 298 K. They are powerful fluorinating agents. They are readily hydrolysed even by traces of water.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 210 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 18 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','bleaching agents','fluorinating agents','oxidising agents','reducing agents','NEW','12-210',NULL,NULL),
(214104,'chemistry','XeF₂, XeF₄ and XeF₆ are ________, ___________ and ___________ readily at 298 K.','colourless, crystalline solids, sublime','XeF₂, XeF₄ and XeF₆ are colourless crystalline solids and sublime readily at 298 K. They are powerful fluorinating agents. They are readily hydrolysed even by traces of water.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 210 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 18 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','colourless, liquids, sublime','colourless, crystalline solids, sublime','colourless, gases, sublime','coloured, gases, sublime','NEW','12-210',NULL,NULL),
(214105,'chemistry','Partial hydrolysis of XeF₆ gives _________.','oxyfluorides','Partial hydrolysis of XeF₆ gives oxyfluorides, XeOF₄ and XeO₂F₂ \nXeOF₄ + H₂O → XeOF₄ + 2HF\nXeO₂F₂ + 2H₂O → XeO₂F₂ + 4HF\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 210 Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 18 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','oxyfluorates','oxofluorates','oxyfluorides','oxofluorides','NEW','12-210',NULL,NULL),
(214107,'chemistry','XeO₃ is a colourless explosive_________ and has a ___________ molecular structure.','solid, pyramidal','XeO₃ is a colourless explosive solid and has a pyramidal molecular structure. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 210 Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 18 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','liquid, pyramidal','solid, pyramidal','gas, octahedral','solid, octahedral','NEW','12-210',NULL,NULL),
(214201,'chemistry','Urea reacts with water to form  A  which will decompose to form B. B when passed through Cu²⁺(aq), deep blue colour solution C is formed. What is the formula of C from the following?','[Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺','NH₂CONH₂ + H₂O → NH₂COONH₄ (A) → NH₃ (B) + CO₂\nCu²⁺ (aq) + 4NH₃ (B) → [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺ (C) (aq)\nTherefore, the molecular formula of C ia  [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 177 Paragraph No. 1)\n(NEET 2020)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CuSO₄','[Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺','Cu(OH)₂','CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂','NEW','12-177',NULL,NULL),
(214202,'chemistry','When copper is heated with conc. HNO₃ it produces-','Cu(NO₃)₂ and NO₂','When copper is heated with conc. HNO₃ it produces Cu(NO₃)₂ and NO₂\nCu + 4HNO₃(conc.) —→ Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2NO₂ + 2H₂O\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 180 Paragraph No. 1)\n(NEET-I 2016)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cu(NO₃)₂, NO and NO₂','Cu(NO₃)₂ and N₂O','Cu(NO₃)₂ and NO₂','Cu(NO₃)₂ and NO','NEW','12-180',NULL,NULL),
(214203,'chemistry','A compound ‘X’ upon reaction with H₂O produces a colourless gas ‘Y’ with rotten fish smell. Gas ‘Y’ is absorbed in a solution of CuSO₄ to give Cu₃P₂ as one of the products. Predict the compound ‘X’.','Ca₃P₂','Ca₃P₂ + 6H₂O  → 3Ca(OH)₂ + 2PH₃ (g)\n(X)                                            (Y) (Rotten fish smell)\n3CuSO₄ + 2PH₃ → Cu₃P₂ + 3H₂SO₄\n                   (Y)    (Copper phosphide)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 181 Paragraph No. 7.7)\n(Odisha NEET 2019)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Ca₃P₂','NH₄Cl','As₂O₃','Ca₃(PO₄)₂','NEW','12-181',NULL,NULL),
(214204,'chemistry','Identify the incorrect statement related to PCl₅ from the following :','PCl₅ molecule is non-reactive.','PCl₅ is a reactive gas as it easily provides Cl₂ gas.\n(NEET 2019)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 183 Paragraph No. 7.8.2)\ndiagram(ne2109)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','PCl₅ molecule is non-reactive.','Three equatorial P – Cl bonds make an angle of 120° with each other','Two axial P – Cl bonds make an angle of 180° with each other.','Axial P – Cl bonds are longer than equatorial P – Cl bonds','NEW','12-183',NULL,NULL),
(214205,'chemistry','Which of the following oxoacids of phosphorus has strongest reducing property?','H₃PO₂','Acids which contain P – H bonds have strong reducing properties. Among the given compounds, H3PO2 is the strongest reducing agent as it contains two P – H bonds.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 185 Paragraph No. 1)\n(Odisha NEET 2019)\ndiagram(ne2110)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','H₄P₂O₇','H₃PO₃','H₃PO₂','H₃PO₄','NEW','12-185',NULL,NULL),
(214206,'chemistry','Which is the correct statement for the given acids?','Phosphinic acid is a monoprotic acid while phosphonic acid is a diprotic acid.','Phosphinic acid is a monoprotic acid while phosphonic acid is a diprotic acid.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 185 Paragraph No. 2)\n(NEET-I 2016) \ndiagram(ne2111)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Phosphinic acid is a monoprotic acid while phosphonic acid is a diprotic acid.','Phosphinic acid is a diprotic acid while phosphonic acid is a monoprotic acid','Both are diprotic acids.','Both are triprotic acids','NEW','12-185',NULL,NULL),
(2000035072,'physics','A disc of radius $2\\,m$ and mass $100\\,kg$ rolls on a horizontal floor. Its centre of mass has speed of $20\\,cm/s$. How much work is needed to stop it?','A','Work required = change in kinetic energy<br>$k_{f}=0$<br>$k_{i}=\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} I \\omega^{2}=\\dfrac{3}{4} m v^{2}$<br>$ = \\frac{3}{4} \\times 100 \\times {\\left( {20 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}} \\right)^2} = 3\\,J$<br>$|\\Delta K E|=3 J=W$','Plane Motion of a Rigid Body (Translation and Rotation)','System of Particles and Rotational Motion','Medium',NULL,'$3\\,J$','$30\\,kJ$','$2\\,J$','$1\\,J$',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(214207,'chemistry','Strong reducing behaviour of H₃PO₂ is due to-','presence of one —OH group and two P—H bonds','All oxyacids of phosphorus which have P—H bonds act as strong reducing agents. H3PO2 has two P—H bonds hence, it acts as a strong reducing agent.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 185 Paragraph No. 2)\n(AIPMT 2015)\ndiagram(ne2111)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','high electron gain enthalpy of phosphorus','high oxidation state of phosphorus','presence of two —OH groups and one P—H bond','presence of one —OH group and two P—H bonds','NEW','12-185',NULL,NULL),
(214208,'chemistry','Which is the correct thermal stability order for H₂E (E = O, S, Se, Te and Po) ?','H₂Po < H₂Te < H₂Se < H₂S < H₂O','The thermal stability of hydrides decreases from H₂O to H₂Po. This is because as the size of atom E in H₂E increases, the bond H–E becomes weaker and thus, breaks on heating. Therefore, the correct order of thermal stability is H₂Po < H₂Te < H₂Se < H₂S < H₂O.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 188 Paragraph No. 2)\n(NEET 2019)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','H₂Se < H₂Te < H₂Po < H₂O < H₂S','H₂S < H₂O < H₂Se < H₂Te < H₂Po','H₂O < H₂S < H₂Se < H₂Te < H₂Po','H₂Po < H₂Te < H₂Se < H₂S < H₂O','NEW','12-188',NULL,NULL),
(214209,'chemistry','Acidity of diprotic acids in aqueous solutions increases in the order','H₂S < H₂Se < H₂Te','As the atomic size increases down the group, the bond length increases and the bond strength decreases and the cleavage of E–H bond becomes easier thus, more will be the acidity. Thus, the correct order is : H₂S < H₂Se < H₂Te.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 188 Paragraph No. 2)\n(NEET 2014)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','H₂S < H₂Se < H₂Te','H₂Se < H₂S < H₂Te','H₂Te < H₂S < H₂Se','H₂Se < H₂Te < H₂S','NEW','12-188',NULL,NULL),
(214210,'chemistry','Which of the following does not give oxygen on heating?','(NH₄)₂Cr₂O₇','(NH₄)₂Cr₂O₇ + ∆ → N₂ + Cr₂O₃ + 4H₂O\nZn(ClO₃)₂ + ∆ → ZnCl₂ + 3O₂\nKClO₃ + ∆ → KCl + 3/2O₂\n2K₂Cr₂O₇ + ∆ → 2K₂CrO₄ + Cr₂O₃ + 3/2O₂\n(NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','K₂Cr₂O₇','(NH₄)₂Cr₂O₇','KClO₃','Zn(ClO₃)₂','NEW','12-174',NULL,NULL),
(214211,'chemistry','Match the following :','CO-Neutral, BaO-Basic, Al₂O₃-Amphoteric, Cl₂O₇-Acidic','CO — neutral, BaO — basic, Al₂O₃ — amphoteric and Cl₂O₇ — acidic.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 190 Paragraph No. 7.12)\n(NEET 2020)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','match','CO,Neutral','BaO,Basic','Al₂O₃,Amphoteric','Cl₂O₇,Acidic','NEW','12-190',NULL,NULL),
(214212,'chemistry','Which of the following oxoacid of sulphur has — O — O — linkage','H₂S₂O₈, peroxodisulphuric acid','Peroxodisulphuric acid, H₂S₂O₈ has — O — O — linkage.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 194 Paragraph No. 7.16)\n(NEET 2020)\nRefer diagram(ne2112)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','H₂SO₃, sulphurous acid','H₂SO₄, sulphuric acid','H₂S₂O₈, peroxodisulphuric acid','H₂S₂O₇, pyrosulphuric acid','NEW','12-194',NULL,NULL),
(214213,'chemistry','Identify the correct formula of oleum from the following :','H₂S₂O₇','H₂S₂O₇  is the correct formula of Oleum\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 194 Paragraph No. 7.16)\n(Odisha NEET 2019)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','H₂S₂O₇','H₂SO₃','H₂SO₄','H₂S₂O₈','NEW','12-194',NULL,NULL),
(214214,'chemistry','In which pair of ions both the species contain S — S bond?','S₄O₆ ²⁻, S₂O₃ ²⁻','In S₄O₆ ²⁻ and S₂O₃ ²⁻ S-S bond is present\n(NEET 2017)\nRefer diagram(ne2113)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','S₄O₆ ²⁻, S₂O₃ ²⁻','S₂O₇ ²⁻, S₂O₈ ²⁻','S₄O6 ²⁻, S₂O₇ ²⁻','S₂O₇ ²⁻, S₂O₃ ²⁻','NEW','12-194',NULL,NULL),
(214215,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is not true for halogens?','All but fluorine show positive oxidation states.','All halogens show both positive and negative oxidation states while fluorine shows only negative oxidation state except +1 in HOF.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 199 Paragraph 7.18.8)\n(NEET 2018)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','All form monobasic oxyacids','All are oxidizing agents.','All but fluorine show positive oxidation states.','Chlorine has the highest electron-gain enthalpy.','NEW','12-199',NULL,NULL),
(214216,'chemistry','Which one of the following orders is correct for the bond dissociation enthalpy of halogen molecules?','Cl₂ > Br₂ > F₂ > I₂','The order of bond dissociation enthalpy is :\n                              Cl₂     >    Br₂    >        F₂     >     I₂\nB.E. (in kJ/mol)     242.6      192.8       158.8       151.1\nA reason for this anomaly is the relatively large electron-electron repulsion among the lone pairs in F₂ molecule where they are much closer to each other than in case of Cl₂.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 198 Table 7.8)\n(NEET-I 2016)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Br₂ > I₂ > F₂ > Cl₂','F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂','I₂ > Br₂ > Cl₂ > F₂','Cl₂ > Br₂ > F₂ > I₂','NEW','12-198',NULL,NULL),
(214217,'chemistry','Match the following :','Pure nitrogen-Sodium azide or Barium azide, Haber process-Ammonia, Contact process-Sulphuric acid, Deacon’s process-Chlorine','The correct match is Pure nitrogen-Sodium azide or Barium azide, Haber process-Ammonia, Contact process-Sulphuric acid, Deacon’s process-Chlorine\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 174 Paragraph 7.2)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 176 Paragraph 1)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 195 Paragraph 7.17)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 202 Paragraph 7.19)\n(NEET 2019)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','match','Pure nitrogen,Sodium azide or Barium azide','Haber process,Ammonia','Contact process,Sulphuric acid','Deacon’s process,Chlorine','NEW','12-202',NULL,NULL),
(214218,'chemistry','Among the following, the correct order of acidity is-','HClO < HClO₂ < HClO₃ < HClO₄','The acidic character of the oxoacids increases with increase in oxidation number of the halogen atom i.e., HClO < HClO₂ < HClO₃ < HClO₄. This can be explained on the basis of relative stability of the anions left after removal of a proton. Since the stability of the anion decreases in the order : ClO₄⁻ > ClO₃⁻ > ClO₂⁻ > ClO⁻, acid strength also decrease in the same order.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 205 Paragraph 7.21)\n(NEET-I 2016, 2007, 2005)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','HClO₂ < HClO < HClO₃ < HClO₄','HClO₄ < HClO₂ < HClO < HClO₃','HClO₃ < HClO₄ < HClO₂ < HClO','HClO < HClO₂ < HClO₃ < HClO₄','NEW','12-205',NULL,NULL),
(214219,'chemistry','Which of the statements given below is incorrect?','OF₂ is an oxide of fluorine.','OF₂ (oxygen difluoride) is a fluoride of oxygen because fluorine is more electronegative than oxygen.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 201 Paragraph 2)\n(NEET 2015)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','O₃ molecule is bent.','ONF is isoelectronic with O₂N⁻','OF₂ is an oxide of fluorine.','Cl₂O₇ is an anhydride of perchloric acid.','NEW','12-201',NULL,NULL),
(214220,'chemistry','In which of the following compounds, nitrogen exhibits highest oxidation state?','N₃H','N₂H₄ ⇒ 2x + 4 (+1) = 0\n⇒ 2x + 4 = 0\n⇒ x = –2\nNH₃ ⇒ x + 3(+1) = 0 ⇒ x = –3\nN₃H ⇒ 3x + 1(+1) = 0\n⇒ 3x + 1 = 0 ⇒ x = –1/3\nNH₂OH ⇒ x + 2 + 1(–2) + 1 = 0\n⇒ x + 1 = 0 ⇒ x = –1\nThus, highest oxidation state is –1/3.\n(AIPMT 2012)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','N₂H₄','NH₃','N₃H','NH₂OH','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(214221,'chemistry','Nitrogen forms N₂, but phosphorus does not form P₂, however, it converts P₄, reason is-','pπ – pπ bonding is weak','For strong p-bonding, pπ - pπ bonding should be strong. In case of P, due to larger size as compared to N-atom, pπ - pπ bonding is not so strong.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 172 Paragraph No. last)\n(AIPMT 2001)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','triple bond present between phosphorus atom','pπ – pπ bonding is weak','pπ – pπ bonding is strong','pπ – pπ bonding is strong','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(214222,'chemistry','Which of the following oxides is most acidic?','N₂O₅','Among N, P, As and Sb, the former has highest electronegativity (EN) so its oxide is most acidic. As the electronegativity value of element increases, the acidic character of the oxide also increases.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 173 Paragraph No. 3)\n(AIPMT 1999)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','As₂O₅','P₂O₅','N₂O₅','Sb₂O₅','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(214223,'chemistry','Which of the following has the highest dipole moment?','NH₃','Due to greater electronegativity of nitrogen, dipole moment for NH₃ is greater.\n(AIPMT 1997)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','SbH₃','AsH₃','NH₃','PH₃','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(214224,'chemistry','The basic character of hydrides of the V group elements decreases in the order-','NH₃ > PH₃ > AsH₃ > SbH₃','All the hydrides of group V elements have one lone pair of electrons on their central atom. Therefore, they can act as Lewis bases. The basic character of these hydrides decreases down the group.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 173 Paragraph No. 2)\n(AIPMT 1996)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','NH₃ > PH₃ > AsH₃ > SbH₃','SbH₃ > AsH₃ > PH₃ > NH₃','SbH₃ > PH₃ > AsH₃ > NH₃','NH₃ > SbH₃ > PH₃ > AsH₃','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(214225,'chemistry','Among the following oxides, the lowest acidic is-','As₄O₆','The acidic character of the oxides decreases with the decrease in the oxidation state and also decreases down the group.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 173 Paragraph No. 2)\n(AIPMT 1996)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','As₄O₆','As₄O₁₀','P₄O₆','P₄O₁₀','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(214226,'chemistry','Which of the following fluorides does not exist?','NF₅','Nitrogen cannot form pentahalides because it cannot expand its octet due to non-availability of d-orbitals.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 174 Paragraph No. 1)\n(AIPMT 1993)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','NF₅','PF₅','AsF₅','SbF₅','NEW','12-174',NULL,NULL),
(214227,'chemistry','Which one has the lowest boiling point?','PH₃','Boiling point of hydrides increases with increase in atomic number but ammonia has exceptionally high boiling point due to hydrogen bonding. Thus, the correct order of boiling point is, BiH₃ > SbH₃ > NH₃ > AsH₃ > PH₃\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 173 Table 7.2)\n(AIPMT 1989)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','NH₃','PH₃','AsH₃','SbH₃','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(214228,'chemistry','Number of electrons shared in the formation of nitrogen molecule is-','6','Nitrogen molecule is diatomic containing a triple bond between two N atoms, therefore, nitrogen molecule is formed by sharing six electrons.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 175 Paragraph 2)\n(AIPMT 1992)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','6','10','2','8','NEW','12-175',NULL,NULL),
(214229,'chemistry','Nitrogen is relatively inactive element because-','dissociation energy of its molecule is fairly high.','N₂ molecule contains triple bond between N atoms having very high dissociation energy (946 kJ mol⁻¹) due to which it is relatively inactive.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 175 Paragraph 2)\n(AIPMT 1992)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','its atom has a stable electronic configuration','it has low atomic radius','its electronegativity is fairly high','dissociation energy of its molecule is fairly high.','NEW','12-175',NULL,NULL),
(214230,'chemistry','Pure nitrogen is prepared in the laboratory by heating a mixture of-','NH₄Cl + NaNO₂','Pure nitrogen is prepared in the laboratory by heating a mixture of NH₄Cl + NaNO₂\nNH₄Cl + NaNO₂ → NH₄NO₂ + NaCl\nNH₄NO₂ + Heat → N₂ + 2H₂O\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 174 Paragraph 7.2)\n(AIPMT 1991)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','NH₄OH + NaCl','NH₄NO₃ + NaCl','NH₄Cl + NaOH','NH₄Cl + NaNO₂','NEW','12-174',NULL,NULL),
(214231,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is not correct for nitrogen?','d-orbitals are available for bonding.','In case of nitrogen, d-orbitals are not available for bonding. N : 1s² 2s² 2p³\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 172 Paragraph Last)\n(AIPMT 1990)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Its electronegativity is very high.','d-orbitals are available for bonding.','It is a typical non-metal.','Its molecular size is small.','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(214232,'chemistry','Aqueous solution of ammonia consists of-','NH₄⁺ and OH⁻','Aqueous solution of ammonia contains NH+4 and OH– ions.\nNH₃ + H₂O ⇄ NH₄⁺ + 4OH⁻\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 176 Paragraph 3)\n(AIPMT 1991)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','H⁺','OH⁻','NH₄⁺','NH₄⁺ and OH⁻','NEW','12-176',NULL,NULL),
(214233,'chemistry','Which of the following oxides of nitrogen is paramagnetic?','NO₂','NO₂ is paramagnetic due to the presence of one unpaired electron.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 178 Table 7.4)\n(AIPMT 1994)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','NO₂','N₂O₃','N₂O','N₂O₅','NEW','12-178',NULL,NULL),
(214234,'chemistry','Which of the following is a nitric acid anhydride?','N₂O₅','When two molecules of nitric acid undergoes heating, lose a water molecule to form an anhydride.\nH₂N₂O₆ → N₂O₅+ H₂O\nThus, N₂O₅ is nitric acid anhydride.\n(AIPMT 1988)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','NO','NO₂','N₂O₅','N₂O₃','NEW','12-178',NULL,NULL),
(214235,'chemistry','Zn gives H₂ gas with H₂SO₅ and HCl but not with HNO₃ because-','NO₃– is reduced in preference to hydronium ion.','Zinc is on the top position of hydrogen in electrochemical series. So, Zn displaces H₂ from dilute H₂SO₅ and HCl with liberation of H₂.\nZn + H₂SO₄ → ZnSO₄ + H₂\nOn the other hand, HNO₃ is one oxidising agent. Hydrogen obtained in the reaction is converted into H₂O.\n2Zn + 4HNO₃ → 2Zn(NO₃)₂ + 2H₂\n2HNO₃  →  H₂O + NO₂ + 3O₂\n2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O\n(AIPMT 2002)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Zn act as oxidising agent when react with HNO₃','HNO₃ is weaker acid than H₂SO₄ and HCl','in electrochemical series Zn is above hydrogen','NO₃– is reduced in preference to hydronium ion.','NEW','12-180',NULL,NULL),
(214236,'chemistry','Sugarcane on reaction with nitric acid gives-','(COOH)₂','C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁     +      18[O]     →   6(COOH)₂ + 5H₂O\nCane sugar     From HNO₃     Oxalic acid \n(AIPMT 1992)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CO₂ and SO₂','(COOH)₂','2HCOOH (two moles.','no reaction.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(214237,'chemistry','Which of the following phosphorus is the most reactive?','White phosphorus','White phosphorus has low ignition temperature so it is most reactive among all the allotropes.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 181 Paragraph 1)\n(AIPMT 1999)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Scarlet phosphorus','White phosphorus','Red phosphorus','Violet phosphorus','NEW','12-181',NULL,NULL),
(214238,'chemistry','Each of the following is true for white and red phosphorus except that they-','are both soluble in CS₂','Red phosphorus is insoluble in CS₂ and only white P is soluble in CS₂.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 181 Paragraph 2)\n(AIPMT 1989)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','are both soluble in CS₂','can be oxidised by heating in air','can be oxidised by heating in air','can be converted into one another','NEW','12-181',NULL,NULL),
(214239,'chemistry','PH₄I + NaOH forms-','PH₃','PH₄I + NaOH forms PH₃\nPH₄I + NaOH → NaI + PH₃ + H₁O\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 181 Paragraph 3)\n(AIPMT 1991)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','PH₃','NH₃','P₄O₆','P₄O₁₀','NEW','12-181',NULL,NULL),
(214240,'chemistry','PCl₃ reacts with water to form-','H₃PO₃, HCl','PCl₃ reacts with water to form H₃PO₃ and HCl\nPCl₃ + 3H₂O → H₃PO₃ + 3HCl\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 182 Paragraph 3)\n(AIPMT 1991)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','PH₃','H₃PO₃, HCl','POCl₃','H₃PO₄','NEW','12-182',NULL,NULL),
(214241,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is not valid for oxoacids of phosphorus?','Hypophosphorous acid is a diprotic acid.','Hypophosphorous acid is a monoprotic acid.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 184 Paragraph 7.9)\n(AIPMT 2012)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Orthophosphoric acid is used in the manufacture of triple superphosphate.','Hypophosphorous acid is a diprotic acid.','All oxoacids contain tetrahedral four coordinated phosphorus.','All oxoacids contain atleast one P O unit and one P—OH group.','NEW','12-184',NULL,NULL),
(214242,'chemistry','Oxidation states of P in H₄P₂O₅, H₄P₂O₆, H₄P₂O₇ are respectively-','3+, 4+, 5+','The oxidation state can be calculated as :\nH₄P₂O₅ : +4 + 2x + 5(– 2) = 0 ⇒ 2x – 6 = 0 ⇒ x = +3\nH₄P₂O₆ : +4 + 2x + 6(– 2) = 0 ⇒ 2x – 8 = 0 ⇒ x = +4\nH₄P₂O₇ : +4 + 2x + 7(– 2) = 0 ⇒ 2x – 10 = 0 ⇒ x = +5\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 184 Table 7.5)\n(AIPMT 2010)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','3+, 5+, 4+','5+, 3+, 4+','5+, 4+, 3+','3+, 4+, 5+','NEW','12-184',NULL,NULL),
(214243,'chemistry','How many bridging oxygen atoms are present in P₄O₁₀?','6','There are 6 bridging oxygen atoms are present in P₄O₁₀\n(Mains 2010)\nRefer diagram(ne2114)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','6','4','2','5','NEW','12-185',NULL,NULL),
(214244,'chemistry','The structural formula of hypophosphorous acid is\nRefer diagram(n2110)','C','The formula of hypophosphorous acid is H₃PO₂ as shown in (c). It is a monobasic acid.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 185 Fig 7.4)\n(AIPMT 1997)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','12-185',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/214244q.png'),
(214245,'chemistry','H₃PO₂ is the molecular formula of an acid of phosphorus. Its name and basicity respectively are-','hypophosphorous acid and one','H₃PO₂ is named as hypophosphorous acid. As it contains only one P—OH group, its basicity is one.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 184 Table 7.5)\n(AIPMT 1992)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','phosphorous acid and two','hypophosphorous acid and two','hypophosphorous acid and one','hypophosphoric acid and two.','NEW','12-184',NULL,NULL),
(214246,'chemistry','Which one of the following substance is used in the laboratory for fast drying of neutral gases?','Phosphorus pentoxide','P₂O₅ absorbs moisture much readily than anhydrous CaCl₂.\n(AIPMT 1992)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Phosphorus pentoxide','Active charcoal','Anhydrous calcium chloride','Na₃PO₄','NEW','12-184',NULL,NULL),
(214247,'chemistry','P₂O₅ is heated with water to give-','orthophosphoric acid.','P₂O₅ is heated with water to give orthophosphoric acid.\nP₂O₅ + 3H2O → 2H₃PO₄\n(AIPMT 1991)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','hypophosphorous acid','phosphorous acid','hypophosphoric acid','orthophosphoric acid.','NEW','12-184',NULL,NULL),
(214248,'chemistry','Basicity of orthophosphoric acid is-','3','Basicity of orthophosphoric acid is 3. Orthophosphoric acid, H₃PO₄ contains three P—OH groups and is therefore, tribasic.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 185 Paragraph 2)\n(AIPMT 1991)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','2','3','4','5','NEW','12-185',NULL,NULL),
(214249,'chemistry','When orthophosphoric acid is heated to 600°C, the product formed is-','H₃PO₃','On heating, it gives pyrophosphoric acid at 525 K and metaphosphoric acid at 875 K.\n2H₃PO₄ (heated at 525 K) → H₄P₂O₇ (heated at 875 K) → 2HPO₃\nOrthophosphoric                    Pyrophosphoric                       Metaphosphoric acid\n       acid                                         acid\n(AIPMT 1989)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','PH₃','P₂O₅','H₃PO₃','HPO₃','NEW','12-185',NULL,NULL),
(214250,'chemistry','Which of the following bonds has the highest energy?','S–S','Bond energy of S — S is exceptionally high due to its catenation tendency. B.E.(kJ/mol ) : \nO—O: 142 kJ/mol   S—S: 226 kJ/mol   Se—Se: 172 kJ/mol   Te—Te: 126 kJ/mol\n(AIPMT 1996)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','S–S','O–O','Se–Se','Te–Te','NEW','12-186',NULL,NULL),
(214251,'chemistry','Which would quickly absorb oxygen?','Alkaline solution of pyrogallol','Alkaline solution of pyrogallol absorbs oxygen quickly.\n(AIPMT 1991)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Alkaline solution of pyrogallol','Conc. H₂SO₄','Lime water','Alkaline solution of CuSO4','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(214252,'chemistry','Oxygen will directly react with each of the following elements except-','Cl','Chlorine does not react directly with oxygen.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 190 Paragraph 2)\n(AIPMT 1989)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','P','Cl','Na','S','NEW','12-190',NULL,NULL),
(214253,'chemistry','It is possible to obtain oxygen from air by fractional distillation because-','oxygen has higher b.pt. than nitrogen','Air is liquefied by making use of the Joule- Thomson effect (cooling by expansion of the gas). Water vapour and CO₂ are removed by solidification. The remaining constituents of liquid air i.e., liquid oxygen and liquid nitrogen are separated by means of fractional distillation as fractional distillation is a process of separation of mixture based on the difference in their boiling points. (b.pt. of O₂ =  183°C : b.pt. of N₂ = –195.8°C).\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 189 Paragraph 7.11)\n(AIPMT 1989)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','oxygen is in a different group of the periodic table from nitrogen','oxygen is more reactive than nitrogen','oxygen has higher b.pt. than nitrogen','oxygen has a lower density than nitrogen.','NEW','12-189',NULL,NULL),
(214254,'chemistry','The angular shape of ozone molecule (O₃) consists of-','2 sigma and 1 pi bond','The angular shape of ozone molecule (O₃) consists of 2 sigma and 1 pi bond\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 192 Paragraph 2)\n(AIPMT 2008)\nRefer diagram(ne2115)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1 sigma and 1 pi bond','2 sigma and 1 pi bond','1 sigma and 2 pi bond','2 sigma and 2 pi bond','NEW','12-192',NULL,NULL),
(214255,'chemistry','The gases respectively absorbed by alkaline pyrogallol and oil of cinnamon are-','O₂, O₃','Alkaline pyrogallol absorbs O₂ and oil of cinnamon absorbs O₃.\n(AIPMT 1989)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','O₃, CH₄','O₂, O₃','SO₂, CH₄','N₂O, O₃','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(214256,'chemistry','Nitrogen dioxide and sulphur dioxide have some properties in common. Which property is shown by one of these compounds, but not by the other?','Is used as a food preservative.','NO₂ is not used as a food preservative.\n(2015, Cancelled)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Is soluble in water.','Is used as a food preservative.','Forms ‘acid-rain’.','Is a reducing agent.','NEW','12-194',NULL,NULL),
(214257,'chemistry','Sulphur trioxide can be obtained by which of the  following reaction?','Fe₂(SO₄)₃ →','Fe₂(SO₄)₃ + heat → Fe₂O₃ + 3SO₃\n(AIPMT 2012)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CaSO₄ + C →','Fe₂(SO₄)₃ →','S + H₂SO₄ →','H₂SO₄ + PCl₅ →','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(214258,'chemistry','Oleum is-','fuming H₂SO₄','Pyrosulphuric acid or oleum (+6) is H₂S₂O₇ which is obtained by dissolving SO₃ and is called fuming sulphuric acid.\n(AIPMT 1991)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','castor oil','oil of vitriol','fuming H₂SO₄','none of these.','NEW','12-195',NULL,NULL),
(214259,'chemistry','Match List I (substances) with List II (processes) employed in the manufacture of the substances and select the correct option.','Sulphuric acid-Contact process, Steel-Bessemer’s process, Sodium hydroxide-Leblanc process, Ammonia-Haber’s process','The correct match is Sulphuric acid-Contact process, Steel-Bessemer’s process, Sodium hydroxide-Leblanc process, Ammonia-Haber’s process\n(Mains 2010)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','match','Sulphuric acid,Contact process','Steel,Bessemer’s process','Sodium hydroxide,Leblanc process','Ammonia,Haber’s process','NEW','12-195',NULL,NULL),
(214260,'chemistry','The variation of the boiling points of the hydrogen halides is in the order HF > HI > HBr > HCl. What explains the higher boiling point of hydrogen fluoride?','There is strong hydrogen bonding between HF molecules.','HF forms strong intermolecular H-bonding due to high electronegativity of F. Hence, the boiling point of HF is abnormally high. Boiling points of other hydrogen halides gradually increase from HCl to HI due to increase in size of halogen atoms from Cl to I which further increase the magnitude of van der Waals’ forces.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 201 Paragraph 2)\n(NEET 2015)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','There is strong hydrogen bonding between HF molecules.','The bond energy of HF molecules is greater than in other hydrogen halides.','The effect of nuclear shielding is much reduced in fluorine which polarises the HF molecule.','The electronegativity of fluorine is much higher than for other elements in the group.','NEW','12-201',NULL,NULL),
(214261,'chemistry','Among the following which is the strongest oxidising agent?','F₂','Standard reduction potentials of halogens are positive and decrease from fluorine to iodine. So, F₂ is the strongest oxidising agent.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 200 Paragraph last)\n(AIPMT 2009)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Br₂','I₂','CI₂','F₂','NEW','12-200',NULL,NULL),
(214262,'chemistry','Which one of the following arrangements does not give the correct picture of the trends indicated against it?','F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂ : Bond dissociation energy','In case of diatomic molecules (X₂) of halogens the bond dissociation energy decreases in the order : Cl₂ > Br₂ > F₂ > I₂. This is due to relatively large electron-electron repulsion among the lone pairs is F₂ than in case of Cl₂. The oxidising power, electronegativity and reactivity decrease in the order : F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂ Electron gain enthalpy of halogens follows the given order : Cl₂ > F₂ > Br₂ > I₂ The low value of electron gain enthalpy of fluorine is probably due to small size of fluorine atom.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 198 Table 7.8)\n(AIPMT 2008)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂ : Bond dissociation energy','F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂ : Electronegativity','F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂ : Oxidizing power','F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂ : Electron gain enthalpy','NEW','12-198',NULL,NULL),
(214263,'chemistry','Which one of the following orders is not in accordance with the property stated against it?','F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂ : Bond dissociation energy','X – X bond                                      F – F          Cl – Cl         Br – Br         I – I\nBond dissociation (kcal/mol)             38               57               45.5            35.6\nThe lower value of bond dissociation energy of fluorine is due to the high inter-electronic repulsions between non-bonding electrons in the 2p-orbitals of fluorine. As a result F – F bond is weaker in comparison to Cl – Cl and Br – Br bonds.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 198 Table 7.8)\n(AIPMT 2006)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂ : Bond dissociation energy','F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ >I ₂: Oxidising power','HI >HBr  > HCl > HF : Oxidising power','F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂ : Electronegativity','NEW','12-198',NULL,NULL),
(214264,'chemistry','Which statement is wrong?','Bond energy of F₂ > Cl₂','Due to more repulsion in between non-bonding electron pairs (2p) of two fluorines (due to small size of F-atom) in comparison to non bonding electron pairs (3p) in chlorine, the bond energy of F₂ is less than Cl₂.\nB.E. (F₂) = 158.8 kJ/mole and B.E. (Cl₂) = 242.6 kJ/mole\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 198 Table 7.8)\n(AIPMT 2000)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Bond energy of F₂ > Cl₂','Electronegativity of F > Cl','F is more oxidising than Cl','Electron affinity of Cl > F','NEW','12-198',NULL,NULL),
(214265,'chemistry','Which of the following has the greatest electron affinity?','CI','In general, the electron affinity decreases from top to bottom in a group. But in group 17, fluorine has lower electron affinity as compared to chlorine due to very small size of fluorine atom. As a result, there are strong interelectronic repulsions in the relatively small 2s orbitals of fluorine and thus, the incoming electron does not experience much attraction.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 198 Table 7.8)\n(AIPMT 1996)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','I','Br','F','CI','NEW','12-198',NULL,NULL),
(214266,'chemistry','Which of the following displaces Br₂ from an aqueous solution containing bromide ions?','Cl₂','Since chlorine is stronger oxidising agent than bromine, therefore it will displace bromine from an aqueous solution containing bromide ions.\nCl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂\n(AIPMT 1994)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','I₂','I₃⁻','Cl₂','Cl⁻','NEW','12-198',NULL,NULL),
(214267,'chemistry','Which of the following species has four lone pairs of electrons?','Cl⁻','Outer electronic configuration of Cl = 3s²3pₓ²3pᵧ²3p₂¹\nOuter electronic configuration of Cl⁻ = 3s²3pₓ²3pᵧ²3p₂²\ni.e., 4 lone pair of electrons\n(AIPMT 1993)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','I','O','Cl⁻','He','NEW','12-198',NULL,NULL),
(214268,'chemistry','When Cl₂ gas reacts with hot and concentrated sodium hydroxide solution, the oxidation number of chlorine changes from-','zero to –1 and zero to +5','3Cl₂ + 6NaOH (hot and conc.) → 5NaCl + NaClO₃ + 3H₂O\nOxidation state oof Chlorine in Cl₂ = 0\nOxidation state oof Chlorine in NaCl = -1\nOxidation state oof Chlorine in NaClO₃ = +5\nThis is an example of disproportionation reaction and oxidation state of chlorine changes from 0 to –1 and +5.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 203 Paragraph 4)\n(AIPMT 2012)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','zero to +1 and zero to –5','zero to –1 and zero to +5','zero to –1 and zero to +3','zero to +1 and zero to –3','NEW','12-203',NULL,NULL),
(214269,'chemistry','Which of the following is used in the preparation of chlorine?','Both MnO₂ and KMnO₄','MnO₂ + 4HCl → MnCl₂ + 2H₂O + Cl₂↑\n2KMnO₄ + 16HCl → 2KCl + 2MnCl₂ + 8H₂O + 5Cl₂↑\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 202 Paragraph 7.19)\n(AIPMT 1999)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both MnO₂ and KMnO₄','Only KMnO₄','Only MnO₂','Either MnO₂ or KMnO₄','NEW','12-202',NULL,NULL),
(214270,'chemistry','Which of the following elements is extracted commercially by the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of its compound?','Cl','Chlorine is obtained by the electrolysis of brine (concentrated NaCl solution). Chlorine is liberated at anode.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 202 Paragraph 7.19)\n(AIPMT 1993)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Cl','Br','Al','Na','NEW','12-202',NULL,NULL),
(214271,'chemistry','When chlorine is passed over dry slaked lime at room temperature, the main reaction product is-','CaOCl₂','With dry slaked lime it gives bleaching powder.\nCa(OH)₂ + Cl₂ → CaOCl₂ + H₂O\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 203 Paragraph 4)\n(AIPMT 1992)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ca(ClO₂)₂','CaCl₂','CaOCl₂','Ca(OCl)₂','NEW','12-203',NULL,NULL),
(214272,'chemistry','In the manufacture of bromine from sea water, the mother liquor containing bromides is treated with-','chlorine','Bromide in the mother liquor (containing MgBr₂) is oxidised to Br₂ by passing Cl₂ which is a stronger oxidising agent.\n(AIPMT 1992)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','carbon dioxide','chlorine','iodine','sulphur dioxide.','NEW','12-198',NULL,NULL),
(214273,'chemistry','The bleaching action of chlorine is due to','oxidation.','Bleaching action of chlorine is due to oxidation in presence of moisture. Bleaching effect is permanent.\nH₂O + Cl₂ → 2HCl + [O]\nColouring matter + [O] → Colourless matter\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 203 Paragraph 4)\n(AIPMT 1991)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','reduction','hydrogenation','chlorination','oxidation.','NEW','12-203',NULL,NULL),
(214274,'chemistry','Bleaching powder reacts with a few drops of conc. HCl to give-','chlorine','CaOCl₂ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + H₂O + Cl₂\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 203 Paragraph 4)\n(AIPMT 1989)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','chlorine','hypochlorous acid','calcium oxide','oxygen.','NEW','12-203',NULL,NULL),
(214275,'chemistry','Which of the statements given below is incorrect?','OF₂ is an oxide of fluorine.','OF₂ (oxygen difluoride) is a fluoride of oxygen because fluorine is more electronegative than oxygen.\n(NEET 2015)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','O₃ molecule is bent.','ONF is isoelectronic with O2N-','OF₂ is an oxide of fluorine.','Cl₂O₇ is an anhydride of perchloric acid.','NEW','12-201',NULL,NULL),
(214276,'chemistry','The correct order of increasing bond angles in the following species is-','ClO₂⁻ < Cl₂O < ClO₂','For ClO₂⁻  lower bond angle due to lone pair - lone pair repulsion\nFor Cl₂O High electronegativity of O than Cl so, bond pair is more closer to O atom so higher lp - bp repulsion.\nFor ClO₂ Highest bond angle due to lower lone pair - bond pair repulsion\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 205 Paragraph 7.21)\n(AIPMT 2010)\nRefer diagram(ne2115)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Cl₂O < ClO₂ < ClO₂⁻','ClO₂ < Cl₂O < ClO₂⁻','Cl₂O < ClO₂⁻ < ClO₂','ClO₂⁻ < Cl₂O < ClO₂','NEW','12-205',NULL,NULL),
(214277,'chemistry','Which one of the following oxides is expected to exhibit paramagnetic behaviour?','ClO₂','CO₂, SiO₂, SiO₂ : diamagnetic\nClO₂ : paramagnetic\n(AIPMT 2005)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CO₂','SiO₂','SiO₂','ClO₂','NEW','12-205',NULL,NULL),
(214278,'chemistry','Match the interhalogen compounds of column-I with the geometry in column-II and assign the correct code.','XX′-Linear, XX′₃-T-shape, XX′₅-Square pyramidal, XX′₇-Pentagonal bipyramidal','The correct match is XX′-Linear, XX′₃-T-shape, XX′₅-Square pyramidal, XX′₇-Pentagonal bipyramidal\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 206 Paragraph 7.22)\n(NEET 2017)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','match','XX′,Linear','XX′₃,T-shape','XX′₅,Square pyramidal','XX′₇,Pentagonal bipyramidal','NEW','12-206',NULL,NULL),
(214279,'chemistry','Match the Xenon compounds in Column-I with its structure in Column-II and assign the correct code.','XeF₄-square planar, XeF₆-distorted octahedral, XeOF₄-square pyramidal, XeO₃-pyramidal','The correct match is XeF₄-square planar, XeF₆-distorted octahedral, XeOF₄-square pyramidal, XeO₃-pyramidal\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 210 Fig 7.9)\n(NEET 2019, NEET-I 2016)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','match','XeF₄,square planar','XeF₆,distorted octahedral','XeOF₄,square pyramidal','XeO₃,pyramidal','NEW','12-210',NULL,NULL),
(214280,'chemistry','Identify the incorrect statement, regarding the molecule XeO₄.','XeO₄ molecule is square planar.','The incorrect statement is XeO₄ molecule is square planar. The structure of XeO₄ is tetrahedral\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 210 Paragraph b)\n(Karnataka NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','XeO₄ molecule is square planar.','There are four p⫪ - d⫪ bonds.','There are four sp3- p, sigma bonds.','XeO₄ molecule is tetrahedral.','NEW','12-210',NULL,NULL),
(214281,'chemistry','Which compound has planar structure?','XeF₄','In XeF₄ the ‘Xe’ atom is sp³d² hybridised, which contains two lone pair orbitals and four bond pair orbitals. Therefore, the shape of XeF4 molecule is square planar, with one lone pair orbital over and other below the plane.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 210 Fig 7.9)\n(AIPMT 2000)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','XeF₄','XeOF₂','XeO₂F₂','XeO₄','NEW','12-210',NULL,NULL),
(214282,'chemistry','Match List - I with List - II.','PCl₅-Trigonal bipyramidal, SF₆-Octahedral, BrF₅-Square pyramidal, BF₃-Trigonal planar','The correct match PCl₅-Trigonal bipyramidal, SF₆-Octahedral, BrF₅-Square pyramidal, BF₃-Trigonal planar\n(NEET 2021)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','match','PCl₅,Trigonal bipyramidal','SF₆,Octahedral','BrF₅,Square pyramidal','BF₃,Trigonal planar','NEW','12-206',NULL,NULL),
(214283,'chemistry','Statement I : Acid strength increases in the order given as HF << HCl << HBr << HI.\n\nStatement II : As the size of the elements F, Cl, Br, I increases down the group, the bond strength of HF, HCl, HBr and HI decreases and so the acid strength increases.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.','Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','Down the group size increases. Overlapping decreases, acidic strength increases.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 201 Paragraph 1)\n(NEET 2021)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','Both Statement I and Statement II are false.','Statement I is correct but statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true.','NEW','12-201',NULL,NULL),
(214284,'chemistry','In which one of the following arrangements the given sequence is not strictly according to the properties indicated against it','H₂O < H₂S < H₂Se < H₂Te ; increasing pKa values','H₂O < H₂S < H₂Se < H₂Te ; increasing pKa values. Down the group acidic strength increases So pKa value decreases.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 201 Paragraph 1)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 188 Paragraph 1)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 173 Paragraph 1)\n(NEET 2021)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','HF < HCl < HBr < HI ; Increasing acidic strength','H₂O < H₂S < H₂Se < H₂Te ; increasing pKa values','NH₃ < PH₃ < AsH₃ < SbH₃ ; increasing acidic character','CO₂ < SiO₂ < SnO₂ < PbO₂ ; increasing oxidising power','NEW','12-188',NULL,NULL),
(214285,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\n\nAssertion (A): ICI is more reactive than I₂.\n\nReason (R): I-CI bond is weaker than I-I bond.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','In general, interhalogen compounds are more reactive than halogens (except fluorine). This is because  X – X bond in interhalogens is weaker than X – X bond in halogens excepts F – F bond. Therefore  I–Cl is more reactive than I2 because of weaker I – Cl bond then I–I bond.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 206 Paragraph 7.22)\n(NEET 2022)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-206',NULL,NULL),
(214286,'chemistry','Given below are two statements\n\nStatement I : The boiling points of the following hydrides of group 16 elements increases in the order –H₂O < H₂S < H₂Se < H₂Te\n\nStatement II : The boiling points of these hydrides increase with increase in molar mass.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect','The boiling points of these hybrids not exactly increases with increase in molar mass. H₂O has maximum boiling point due to intermolecular hydrogen bonding.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 188 Paragraph 1)\n(NEET 2022)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect','Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct','NEW','12-188',NULL,NULL),
(214287,'chemistry','The correct order for boiling point of the following compound is','BiH3>SbH3> NH3> AsH3.> PH3','NEET2022','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','AsH3> PH3> NH3> SbH3> BiH3','BiH3>SbH3> NH3> AsH3.> PH3','NH3> PH3> AsH3> SbH3> BiH3','PH3> NH3>AsH3>SbH3>BiH3','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
