(214297,'chemistry','Which of the following reaction does not exhibit the oxidising behaviour of H2SO4?','CaF2+H2SO4-----> CaSO4+ 2HF','NEET2016','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cu + 2H2SO4 -----> CuSO4 + SO2 + 2H2O','3S + 2H2SO4 -----> 3SO2 + 2H2O','C+ 2H2SO4 ------> CO2 + 2SO2 + 2H2O','CaF2+H2SO4-----> CaSO4+ 2HF','NEW','12-196',NULL,NULL),
(214298,'chemistry','The correct order of bond dissociation enthalpy of halogen molecule is \n1- Cl2>Br2>F2>I2\n2- Br2>I2>F2>Cl2\n3- F2>Cl2>Br2>I2\n4- I2>Br2>Cl2>F2','1','NEET2016','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-198',NULL,NULL),
(214299,'chemistry','Wrong statement among the following is','Bond energy of F2> Cl2','AIPMT2000','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Bond energy of F2>Cl2','Electronegativity of F>Cl','F is more ixidizing then Cl','Electron affinity of Cl>F','NEW','12-198',NULL,NULL),
(214300,'chemistry','Correct statement among the following is','`The bond energy of F2 is less than Cl2','AIPMT2002','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Silicon exhibits 8 coordination numbers in it\'s compound','The bond energy of F2 is less then Cl2','Mn(III) oxidation state is more stable then Mn(II)  in an aqueous state','Elements of group 15 shows only +2 or +3 oxidation state','NEW','12-198',NULL,NULL),
(214301,'chemistry','Fluorine is a strong oxidizing agent then chlorine because\n1- F-F has a law enthalpy of dissociatrion\n2- Fluorine ion has high ionization enthalpy\n3- Electron gain enthalpy of fluorine is less then chlorine\n4- Fluorine has a very small size\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given','a and b only','NEET2022','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a and b only','a and c only','a and d only','b and c only','NEW','12-198',NULL,NULL),
(214302,'chemistry','Identify the incorrect statement about halogens','Chlorine has the highest electron gain enthalpy','NEET2018','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','All from monobasic oxyacids.','All are oxidizing agents.','All but fluorine shows positive oxidation state.','Chlorine has the highest electron gain enthalpy','NEW','12-198',NULL,NULL),
(214303,'chemistry','The reaction that is a part of large scale industrial preparation of nitric acid is: Refer diagram (214303Q)','2','The Ostwald process is a chemical process used for making nitric acid (HNO3). It is a mainstay of the modern chemical industry, and it provides the main raw material for the most common type of fertilizer production.\n \n(i) Ammonia is converted to nitric acid in 2 stages. It is oxidized by heating with oxygen in the presence of a catalyst such as Pt with 10% Rh, Pt metal on fused silica wool, Cu or Ni, to form NO and water (as steam).\nThis reaction is strongly exothermic, making it a useful heat source once initiated.\n (ii) Stage two encompasses two reactions and is carried out in an absorption apparatus containing water. Initially nitric oxide is oxidized again to yield nitrogen dioxide.\n\nThis gas is then readily absorbed by the water, yielding the desired product (nitric acid, albeit in a dilute form), while reducing a portion of it back to nitric oxide\n(NEET 2022)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/214303q.png'),
(214304,'chemistry','Which one of the following statements is not true?','HF is a stronger acid than HCl.','1. Iodine has the highest electrode potential value and thus is the weakest oxidising agent or the strongest reducing agent.\n2. Fluorine exhibits only -1 oxidation state whereas other halogens show +1, +3, +5 and +7 oxidation states also. Fluorine being the most electronegative element cannot have positive oxidation state.\n\nOther halogens have d orbitals, therefore can expand their octet.\n\n3. In Group 17 oxyacids, electron density is drawn away from the O atom as the electronegativity of the halogen increases. This in turn draws electron density away from the O–H bond and weakens it.\n\nThe weaker the O–H bond, the stronger the acid. Cl is more electronegative than Br so,  HOCl is stronger acid than HOBr.\n\n4. Amongst the haloacids, only HF is weak. Acid strength can be determined by the stability of the conjugate base (for most acids, the conjugate base is what you get when you remove H+ from the acid).\n\nStability of conjugate base : I- >Br- > Cl-  >F-\n\nAcidity order: HF < HCl < HBr < HI\n\nThus, HCl is stronger acid than HF','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Among halide ions, iodide is the most powerful reducing agent.','Fluorine is the only halogen that does not show a variable oxidation state.','HOCl is a stronger acid than HOBr.','HF is a stronger acid than HCl.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(214305,'chemistry','In the structure of ClF₃, the number of lone pairs of electrons on central atom \'Cl\' is:','2','Refer diagram (214305E)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(214306,'chemistry','Which one of the following reactions does not come under hydrolysis type reaction? Refer diagram (214306Q)','3','In hydrolysis type reaction, the oxidation state remains same. Reaction 3 is a redox reaction. (NEET 2020)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/214306q.png'),
(214307,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statement among the following regarding noble gases','Noble gases have very high melting and boiling points','The general electronic configuration of noble gas elements is ns2 np6 except He (i.e. 1s2).\n\nNoble gases have large positive electron gain enthalpy because the extra electron has to be placed in the next higher principal quantum energy level thereby producing a highly unstable electronic configuration.\n\nNoble gases only have London dispersion forces, so there\'s very little attraction between water and these noble gases. They still can dissolve, but only very little, because some water molecules will be able to form dipole-induced dipole attractions with the noble gas atom.\n\nBecause of weak dispersion forces melting point and boiling points of noble gases are low.\n\nThe melting and boiling point (respectively) of noble gas is as follows: \n\nNe= 24.6 K; Ar= 83.8 K; Kr=115.9 K; Xe=161.3 K; Rn=202 K\n\nHe=4.2 K; Ne=27.1 K; Ar=87.2 K; Kr=119.7 K; Xe=165.0 K; Rn=211 K','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Noble gases have weak dispersion forces','Noble gases have large positive values of electron gain enthalpy','Noble gases are sparingly soluble in water','Noble gases have very high melting and boiling points','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(214364,'chemistry','Lead nitrate when heated at 400°C decomposes to form Lead oxide and releases a brown gas X. X when cooled forms Y. Gas Y and nitrogen monoxide react together at 250 K to form blue coloured solid Z. This solid, Z, when tested with blue litmus paper turns it red. What are X, Y and Z respectively?','NO₂, N₂O₄, N₂O₃','Lead nitrate when heated at 673 K decomposes to form PbO, brown coloured NO₂ and O₂. \nPb(NO₃)₂ → PbO + NO₂ + O₂\nNO₂ when cooled forms N₂O₄. \n2NO₂ + cooled → N₂O₄ .\nNO and N₂O₄ react at 250 K to form Dinitrogen trioxide which is blue coloured acidic solid.  \n2NO + N₂O₄ → N₂O₃\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 178 Table 7.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','NO, NO₂, N₂O₃','N₂O, NO, N₂O₃','NO₂, N₂O, N₂O₄','NO₂, N₂O₄, N₂O₃','NEW','12-178',NULL,NULL),
(214365,'chemistry','X when reacts with NaNO₂ releases gas Y and when reacted with Ca(OH)₂ releases the gas Z which has pungent smell. Gas Y undergoes Haber\'s process to release the same gas Z. What is X?','NH₄Cl','In the laboratory, dinitrogen is prepared by treating an aqueous solution of ammonium chloride with sodium nitrite.\nNH₄Cl (X) + NaNO₂ → N₂ (Y) + 2H₂O + NaCl. \nOn a small scale ammonia is obtained from ammonium salts which decompose when treated with caustic soda or calcium hydroxide.\n2NH₄Cl (X) + Ca(OH)₂ → NH₃ (Z) + 2H₂O + CaCl₂  \nOn a large scale, ammonia is manufactured by Haber’s process.\nN₂ (Y)+ 3H₂ → 2NH₃ (Z)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 174 Paragraph No. 7.2)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 175 Paragraph No. 7.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','NH₄OH','NaNO₂','NaNO₃','NH₄Cl','NEW','12-175',NULL,NULL),
(214366,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\n\nAssertion (A) : NH₃ form hydrogen bond but PH₃ does not\n\nReason (R) : N has small size and high electronegativity.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Hydrogen bonding occurs when hydrogen is bound to a strongly electronegative element, i.e. oxygen, nitrogen, or fluorine,N has small atomic size and high electronegativity. Hence, NH₃ can form hydrogen bonds. P has large size and low electronegativity. Hence, PH₃ cannot form hydrogen bonds.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 172 Paragraph No. last)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(214367,'chemistry','Zinc sulphate gets precipitated to a white ppt and compound X, when it is reacted with aqueous solution of Ammonia. The compound X is then treated with Caustic soda.  This leads to the release of gas with pungent odour. What is the compound X?','(NH₄)₂SO₄','As a weak base, it precipitates the hydroxides (hydrated oxides in case of some metals) of many metals from their salt solutions.\nZnSO₄ + 2NH₄OH → Zn(OH)₂ + (NH₄)₂SO₄ (Compound X). \nOn a small scale ammonia is obtained from ammonium salts which  decompose when treated with caustic soda\n(NH₄)₂SO₄ (Compound X) + NaOH → 2NH₃ + 2H₂O + Na₂SO₄\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 176 Paragraph No. last)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 175 Paragraph No. 7.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','NH₃SO₄','(NH₄)SO₃','(NH₄)₂SO₂','(NH₄)₂SO₄','NEW','12-176',NULL,NULL),
(214368,'chemistry','In which Nitrogen oxide both the N-O bonds are not equal?','N₂O₃ and N₂O₅','In N₂O and NO, there is only one N-O bond. In NO₂, both the N-O bonds are equal as both of them participate equally in resonance. Likewise in N₂O₄ there are 4 N-O bonds and all of them are equal due to their participation in resonance. However in N₂O₃ there are 3 N-O bonds out of which on 2 N-O bonds participate in resonance. Therefore there are 2 bonds with partial double bond character and 1 bond with complete double bond character. In N₂O₅, there are 6 N-O bonds out of which 4 have partial double bond character due to resonance and 2 have single bond because of forming bridged structure with central oxygen. Thus N₂O₃ and N₂O₅ have different N-O bond lengths.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 178 Paragraph No. 7.4)\nRefer diagram(ne2120)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','N₂O₃ and N₂O₅','NO₂ and N₂O','N₂O₃ and N₂O','N₂O and N₂O₅','NEW','12-178',NULL,NULL),
(214369,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\n\nAssertion (A) : The value of HNH angle is higher than HPH, HAsH, and HSbH angles\n\nReason (R) : The bond pairs of electrons become more and more close to the central atom\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','Since the lone pair-bond pair repulsions are stronger then the bond pair-bond pair repulsion, therefore, in NH₃ the bond angle decreases from 109°28 to 107°8 .As we move from N to P to As to Sb, the electronegativity of the central atom goes on decreasing.As a result, bond pairs of electrons, lie away from the central atom.In other words, force of repulsion between the adjacent bond pairs goes on decreasing and consequently the bond angles keep on decreasing from NH₃ to SbH₃ Also there sp3 hybridisation in NH₃ and only s–p bonding between hydrogen and other elements of the group.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 173 Table 7.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(214370,'chemistry','Which is the correct order of Second and third ionisation enthalpies Group-15 elements?','N > P > As  > Bi > Sb','The correct order of second and third ionisation enthalpies of Group-15 elements is N > P > As  > Bi > Sb\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 171 Table 7.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','N > P > As  > Bi > Sb','N > P > As  > Sb > Bi','N > P > Bi  > P > Sb','P > N > Bi  > As > Sb','NEW','12-171',NULL,NULL),
(214308,'chemistry','Match first ionisation enthalpy with respective Group-15 element.','N-1402 kJ/mol, P-1012 kJ/mol, Sb-834, kJ/mol, Bi-703 kJ/mol','The correct match is N-1402 kJ/mol, P-1012 kJ/mol, Sb-834, kJ/mol, Bi-703 kJ/mol\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 171 Table 7.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','match','N,1402 kJ/mol','P,1012 kJ/mol','Sb,834 kJ/mol','Bi,703 kJ/mol','NEW','12-171',NULL,NULL),
(214309,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\n\nA. Group-15 elements show regular decrease in covalent radii from Bi to N \n\nB. They also show regular increase in ionic radii from N to Bi\n\nC. Arsenic has the highest melting point among group 15 elements.\n\nD. Boiling point of Antimony is more than bismuth','A, C and D','Group-15 elements show irregular pattern in their ionic radii. \nThe correct order of melting points of Group-15 elements is N < P < Bi < Sb < As, therefore Arsenic has the highest melting point. \nThe correct order of boiling points of Group-15 elements is N < P < As < Bi < Sb\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 171 Table 7.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','B, C and D','A, B and C','B, C and D','A, C and D','NEW','12-171',NULL,NULL),
(214310,'chemistry','Arrange the given Group-15 elements in correct order of their densities from lowest to highest.','N > As > Sb > Bi','The correct order of densities of Group-15 elements is N < P < As < Sb < Bi \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 171 Table 7.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'1','order','1,N','2,As','3,Sb','4,Bi','NEW','12-171',NULL,NULL),
(214311,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\n\nAssertion (A) : R₃P=O exists but R₃N=O does not\n\nReason (R) : Nitrogen cannot have covalency more than 4\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Nitrogen cannot have covalency more than 4. This is the restriction which comes in nitrogen(N) to expand its coordination number beyond four. Hence, R₃N=O does not exist. But P can show covalency of 5. Hence, R₃P=O exists. Nitrogen cannot form dπ−pπ bonds but phosphorous can form dπ−pπ bonds. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 172 Paragraph No. last)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(214312,'chemistry','Which of the following is the allotrope of Arsenic?','Grey ɑ-form','Grey ɑ-form is the allotrope of Arsenic.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 171 Table 7.1 Foot note)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Black ɑ-form','Yellow ɑ-form','White ɑ-form','Grey ɑ-form','NEW','12-171',NULL,NULL),
(214313,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are not incorrect about hydrides of Group-15?\n\nA.  Ammonia has the highest melting point while SbH₃ has the lowest\n\nB. Phosphine has lowest boiling point.\n\nC. E-H bond distance increases from NH₃ to SbH₃\n\nD. Formation of BiH₃ is an endothermic process.\n\nE. Dissociation of N-H bond releases maximum energy.','B, C, D','The trend of melting point of Group-15 is NH₃ > SbH₃ > AsH₃ > PH₃ and that of boiling point is BiH₃ > SbH₃ > NH₃ > AsH₃ > PH₃. E-H bond distance increases from NH₃ to SbH₃. Only formation of NH₃ is exothermic process. Dissociation of N-H bond absorbs maximum energy.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 173 Table 7.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A, B, D','A, C, D','A, B, C','B, C, D','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(214314,'chemistry','Which nitrogen oxide has N-O-N bond?','N₂O₅','N₂O₅ has N-O-N bond.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  178 Table 7.4) \nRefer diagram(ne2103)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','N₂O₃','N₂O₄','NO₂','N₂O₅','NEW','12-178',NULL,NULL),
(214315,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\n\nAssertion (A) : NH₃ is basic but BiH₃ is feebly basic\n\nReason (R) : Electron density is more on nitrogen than on bismuth\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both NH₃ and BiH₃ have lone pair of electrons on the central atom and hence should behave as Lewis bases. But NH₃ is much more basic than BiH₃. This can be explained on the basis of electron density on the central atom. Atomic size of N (70 pm) is much smaller than that of Bi (148 pm). Therefore the electron density on N−atom is much higher than that on Bi−atom. Consequently, the tendency of N in NH₃ to donote its pair of electrons is much higher than that of Bi in BiH₃. Thus, NH₃ is much more basic than BiH₃\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  173 Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(214316,'chemistry','How many P-O-P bonds are present in Cyclotrimetaphosphpric acid?','3','There are 3 P-O-P bonds are present in Cyclotrimetaphosphpric acid\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page 185 Fig 7.4) \nRefer diagram(ne2116)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','2','1','3','4','NEW','12-185',NULL,NULL),
(214317,'chemistry','Phosphorus pentachloride is hydrolysed to form compound A and HCl. A is further hydrolysed to form compound B. B is heated to form C, an oxoacid of phosphorus that has one P-O-P bond in its structure. What are A and C respectively?','POCI₃ and H₄P₂O₇','Phosphorus pentachloride hydrolysis to form POCl₃ (A) and on further hydrolysis to form Orthophosphoroc acid H₃PO₄ (B). \nPCl₅ + H₂O → POCI₃ + 2HCI\nPOCI₃ + 3H₂O → H₃PO₄ + 3HCI\nH₄P₂O₇ (C) is formed by heating orthophosphoric acid. \n2H₃PO₄ →  H₄P₂O₇ + H₂O\nPyrophosphoric acid has 1 POP bond in its structure. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 183 Paragraph 2)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  184 Table 7.5) \nRefer diagram(ne2117)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','POCI₃ and H₄P₂O₈','POCI₃ and H₄P₂O₅','POCI₃ and H₄P₂O₇','POCI₃ and H₄P₂O₆','NEW','12-184',NULL,NULL),
(214318,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\n\nAssertion (A) : There exists diatomic molecule of nitrogen but tetratomic molecule for phosphorus\n\nReason (R) : Nitrogen is more electronegative than Phosphorous.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','Nitrogen is smaller in size hence it forms multiple bonds with other nitrogen and exists as diatomic molecule. Whereas phosphorus atomic size is more so it can form single bond with other phosphorus atoms. Hence it exists as tetra atomic molecule.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 172 Paragraph last)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(214319,'chemistry','Which among the following nitrogen oxides is neutral?','N₂O','NO and N₂O are neutral oxides.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  178 Table 7.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','N₂O₃','NO₂','N₂O','N₂O₅','NEW','12-178',NULL,NULL),
(214320,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct about Nitrogen oxides? \n\nA. Among all the nitrogen oxides only N₂O₃ can be found in liquid state.\nB. N₂O₃ is blue in colour\nC. N₂O is found in solid forms\nD. NO₂ has linear shape','B only','Among all the nitrogen oxides only N₂O₄ can be found in liquid state. N₂O is found in gaseous forms.\nNO₂ has angular shape\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  178 Table 7.3) \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  178 Table 7.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A and C','B and D','B only','C only','NEW','12-178',NULL,NULL),
(214321,'chemistry','P₄O₁₀ when treated with HNO₃ forms oxoacid A, while on treating with water forms oxoacid B. What are A and B respectively?','HPO₃, H₃PO₄','P₄O₁₀ + H₂O form Orthophosphoric acid (H₃PO₄) . \nDuring the preparation of N₂O₅ Nitric acid is added to P₄O₁₀ and thus HPO₃ is also formed. \n4HNO₃ + P₄O₁₀ → 4HPO₃ + 2N₂O₅\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  178 Table 7.3) \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  184 Table 7.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','H₄P₂O₄, H₃PO₃','HPO₃, H₃PO₃','HPO₃, H₃PO₄','H₄P₂O7, H₃PO₄','NEW','12-184',NULL,NULL),
(214322,'chemistry','Which of the following is correct about bond lengths and bond angels of HNO₃? \nRefer diagram(n2111)','B > A ; Y > Z > X','B > A ; Y > Z > X is the correct answer\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  178 Table 7.4) \nRefer diagram(ne2118)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','B > A ; X > Y > Z','A > B ; X > Y > Z','B > A ; Y > X > Z','B > A ; Y > Z > X','NEW','12-178',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/214322q.png'),
(214323,'chemistry','Which of the following is correct about bond lengths and bond angels of N₂O₃? \nRefer diagram(n2112)','C > B > A ; Y > Z > X','C > B > A ; Y > Z > X is the correct answer\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  179 Paragraph last) \nRefer diagram(ne2119)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','C > B > A ; Y > Z > X','C > A > B ; Y > Z > X','A > B > C ; Y > Z > X','A > C > B ; Z > Y > X','NEW','12-179',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/214323q.png'),
(214324,'chemistry','A brown gas, which also acts as a typical odd molecule, decomposes to release dioxygen and a Colourless gas X . This gas further decomposes to release gas Y and dioxygen. Y is tasteless and non-toxic gas. Y when passed through compressor at 20 MPa with dihydrogen releases a gas Z which has pungent odour. Z when reacts with AgCl turns white ppt to Colourless mass. What are X, Y and White ppt respectively?','NO, N₂, [Ag(NH₃)₂]Cl','NO₂ is a brown gas and also acts as typical odd molecule.\n2NO₂ ⇄ 2NO (X) + O₂.  \nNO is a colourless gas   \nNO ⇄ N₂ (Y)+ O₂\nThe optimum conditions for the production of ammonia are a pressure of 200 × 10⁵ Pa (about 200 atm),\nN₂ (Y) + H₂ → 2NH₃ (Z)\nThe presence of a lone pair of electrons on the nitrogen atom of the ammonia molecule makes it a Lewis base. It donates the electron pair and forms linkage with metal ions\nAgCl + 2NH₃ (Z) → [Ag(NH₃)₂]Cl\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  179 Paragraph 3)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  175 Paragraph 2)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  176 Fig 7.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','N₂O, N₂, [Ag(NH₃)]Cl','NO, N₂O,  [Ag(NH₃)₃]Cl','N₂O₃, N₂,  [Ag(NH₃)₂]Cl','NO, N₂, [Ag(NH₃)₂]Cl','NEW','12-179',NULL,NULL),
(214325,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\n\nAssertion (A) : Nitrogen shows catenation properties less than phosphorus\n\nReason (R) : P-P bond enthalpy is high\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','N−N single bond is weak as nitrogen has small size and lone pairs on two N atoms repel each other. Phosphorous has larger atomic size and the lone pairs on P repel to smaller extent. Thus, the strength of P−P bond is greater than the strength of N−N bond. High bond enthalpy of P−P bond results in tendency for catenation.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  172 Paragraph Last)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(214326,'chemistry','State true or false.\n\nA. Halogens are strong oxidising agents.  \n\nB. Flourine forms only 1 oxoacid \n\nC. SO₂ is not an air pollutant','A. True B. True C. False','Halogens are strong oxidizing agents as they have strong tendency to accept electrons. The oxidizing strength decreases from fluorine to iodine.\nHOF due to small atomic size and high electronegativity. Fluorine cannot act as central atom in higher oxoacids.\nSulphur dioxide (SO₂) is an air pollutant made up of sulphur and oxygen atoms and is harmful to both plants and people. On dissolution in rain water, SO₂ produces acid rain.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  213 Question 7.23)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  213 Question 7.22)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  213 Question 7.24)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','A. False B. True C. False','A. False B. True C. False','A. True B. False C. True','A. True B. True C. False','NEW','12-205',NULL,NULL),
(214327,'chemistry','Give the formula of a noble gas specie isostructral with ICl₄⁻, IBr₂⁻ and BrO₃-⁻ respectively.','XeF₄, XeF₂, XeO₃','XeF₄ is isostructural with ICl₄⁻. It is square planar due to sp 3d2 hybridization which results in octahedral geometry with two lone pairs occupy opposite positions.\nXeF₂ is isostructural with IBr₂⁻ . It has a linear shape due to sp3d hybridization which results in trigonal bipyramidal geometry with 3 lone pairs in equatorial plane.\nXeO₃ is isostructural with BrO₃-⁻ . It has a pyramidal shape due to sp3 hybridisation with terahedral geometry in which one position is occupied by a lone pair.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  210)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','XeF₆, XeF₄, XeOF₄','XeF₄, XeF₂, XeO₃','XeF₂, XeF₄, XeO₃','XeF₄, XeF₆, XeO₂F₂','NEW','12-210',NULL,NULL),
(214328,'chemistry','Which one of the following doesn\'t exist?','NeF₂','As Xe and Ne both are inert gas, but Xe can form compound with Oxygen and Fluorine because of its large size. Ne is smaller in size and highly inert thus exist in atomic form only and do not form molecule with another atom, as it is highly nonreactive.Thus NeF2 does not exist.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  212 Question 7.37)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'1','mcq','XeOF₄','NeF₂','XeF₂','XeF₆','NEW','12-210',NULL,NULL),
(214329,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\n\nAssertion (A) : Electron gain enthalpy values for O → O⁻ and O → O²⁻ as –141 and 702 kJ/mol respectively, There is the formation of a large number of oxides having O²⁻ species and not O⁻\n\nReason (R) : The lattice energy of O²⁻ is much lower than the lattice energy of O⁻\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','Oxygen has negative first electron gain enthalpy and positive second ionization enthalpy . Based on this, we should expect the formation of oxides with O⁻ species and not with O²⁻  species. However, in reality, most oxides have O²⁻ ion due to following reasons:\n(i) O²⁻ has stable noble gas electronic configuration.\n(ii) The lattice energy of O²⁻  is much higher than the lattice energy of O⁻.This more than compensates the higher energy required for removal of second electron from O⁻ to form O²⁻ and makes O²⁻ ion stable.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  186 Table 7.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-186',NULL,NULL),
(214330,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\n\nAssertion (A) : Nitrogen forms hydrogen bonding while chlorine doesn\'t\n\nReason (R) : Chlorine is more electronegative than Nitrogen \n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','The chlorine atom is too large. Despite its electronegativity , the size of the atom is such that its electron density is too low to form hydrogen bonds. This is why Chlorine does not display hydrogen bonding while nitrogen does.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  213 Question 7.25)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-201',NULL,NULL),
(214331,'chemistry','How many statements are correct about electronegativities\n\nA. Antimony is more electronegative than Bismuth \n\nB. Nitrogen and Bromine are equally electronegative\n\nC. As and Te have nearly same electronegativity.\n\nD. F is the most electronegative element','3','Only statement A is incorrect. Antimony and Bismuth have same electronegativity. Nitrogen and Bromine are equally electronegative having value 3.0. As (2) and Te (2.01) have nearly same electronegativities.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 171 Table 7.1)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 186 Table 7.6)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 198 Table 7.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0','1','2','3','NEW','12-198',NULL,NULL),
(214332,'chemistry','How is the presence of SO₂ detected?','By it\'s decolourising property towards acidified KMnO₄ solution','5SO₂ + 2MnO⁴⁻ + 2H₂O → 5SO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺ + 2Mn²⁺\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  194 Question 7.22)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','By its colourless nature','By it\'s decolourising property towards acidified KMnO₄ solution','By it\'s decolourising property towards alkaine KMnO₄ solution','By its pungent odour','NEW','12-194',NULL,NULL),
(214333,'chemistry','The S-O-O-S bond and S-O-S bond are bond in A and B. What are A and B respectively?','Peroxodisulphuric acid, Pyrosulphuric acid.','The S-O-O-S bond and S-O-S bond are bond in Peroxodisulphuric acid (H₂S₂O₈) and Pyrosulphuric acid (H₂S₂O₇)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  194 Fig. 7.6)\nRefer diagram(ne2105)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Peroxomonosulphuric acid, Pyrosulphurous acid.','Peroxodisulphuric acid, Pyrosulphurous acid.','Peroxomonosulphuric acid, Pyrosulphuric acid.','Peroxodisulphuric acid, Pyrosulphuric acid.','NEW','12-194',NULL,NULL),
(214334,'chemistry','A black sulphide when treated with ozone becomes white. What is this white compound?','PbSO₄','Ozone oxidises Lead sulphide to lead sulphate. Lead sulphide is black coloured and lead sulphate is white. \nPbS + 4O₃ → PbSO₄ + 4O₂\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  191 Paragraph last)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','ZnSO₄','PbSO₄','CaSO₄','BaSO₄','NEW','12-191',NULL,NULL),
(214335,'chemistry','Industrially O₂ can be obtained by-','from liquification and fractional distillation of air','Industrially O₂ can be obtained from liquification and fractional distillation of air\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no  189 Paragraph 7.11)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','thermal decomposition of Ag₂O','heating KClO₃','from H₂O₂','from liquification and fractional distillation of air','NEW','12-189',NULL,NULL),
(214336,'chemistry','Ammonia can be dried by using','CaO','Ammonia can be dried by passing over quick line. \nCaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂\nCaCl₂ + 8NH₃ → CaCI₂.8NH₃\nP₂O₅ + NH₃ + H₂O → 2(NH4)₃PO4 \nH₂SO₄ + 2NH₃ → (NH4)₂SO₄','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CaO','Conc. H₂SO₄','P₂O₅','Anhydrous CaCl₂','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(214337,'chemistry','In which of the reactions product is correct?','C + 2H₂SO₄ (conc.) → CO₂ + 2SO₂ + 2H₂O','S₈ + 48HNO₃ → 8H₂SO₄ + 48NO₂ + 16H₂O\nP₄ + 20HNO₃ → 4H₃PO₄ + 20NO₂ + 4H₂O\nC + 2H₂SO₄ (conc.) → CO₂ + 2SO₂ + 2H₂O\nS + 2H₂SO₄ (conc.) → 3SO₂ + 2H₂O\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page 180 Paragraph 3)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page 196 Paragraph last)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','S₈ + 48HNO₃ → 8H₂SO₄ + 48NO + 16H₂O','P₄ + 20HNO₃ → 4H₃PO₄ + 20NO + 4H₂O','C + 2H₂SO₄ (conc.) → CO₂ + 2SO₂ + 2H₂O','S₆ + 2H₂SO₄ (conc.) → 3SO₂ + 2H₂O','NEW','12-196',NULL,NULL),
(214338,'chemistry','In which reaction one of the product is not a paramagnetic gas ?','Zn + Dil. HNO₃ →','3Cu + 8HNO₃ (dilute) → 3Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2NO + 4H₂O\nCu + 4HNO₃ (conc.) → Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2NO₂ + 2H₂O\n4Zn + 10HNO₃ (dilute) → 4 Zn(NO₃)₂ + 5H₂O + N₂O\nZn + 4HNO₃ (conc.) → Zn (NO₃)₂ + 2H₂O + 2NO₂\nNO and NO₂ are paramagnetic gases as they contain they have unpaired elecrons in their valence shells\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page 180 Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Cu + Dil HNO₃ →','Cu + Conc.HNO₃ →','Zn + Dil. HNO₃ →','Zn + Conc. HNO₃ →','NEW','12-180',NULL,NULL),
(214339,'chemistry','Which of the following has chair shaped structure?','S₆','S₆ has the chair-shaped structure.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 193 Fig 7.5)\nRefer diagram(ne2121)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','S₈','S₆','S₂','S₁₆','NEW','12-193',NULL,NULL),
(214340,'chemistry','Bleaching powder contains the salt of an oxoacid as one of is components. The anyhdride of that oxoacid is','Cl₂O','Bleaching powder is Ca(OCl)Cl. So, bleaching powder contains a salt of HOCl. Cl₂O is an anhydride of HOCl. \nCl₂O + H₂O ⇒ 2HOCl. Hence, option A is correct\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 203 Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Cl₂O','ClO₂','ClO','Cl₂O₃','NEW','12-203',NULL,NULL),
(214341,'chemistry','XeF₂ reacts with PF₅ to form-','[XeF]⁺[PF₆]⁻','PF₅ is a fluoride ion acceptor so it yields cationic species with xenon fluorides.\n[XeF₂] + [PF₅] → [XeF]⁺[PF₆]⁻\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 210 Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','[XeF₂]⁺[PF₅]⁻','[XeF]⁺[PF₆]⁻','[XeF₃]⁺[PF₄]⁻','[Xe]⁺[PF₇]⁻','NEW','12-210',NULL,NULL),
(214342,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\n \nAssertion (A) : HClO is stronger acid than HBrO\n\nReason (R) : Greater is the electronegativity of the halogen, greater will be the attraction of electron pair towards it and more easily H+ ions will release\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Cl is more electronegative (and smaller in size) than Br. Hence, the tendency of Cl to withdraw electrons from Cl-O-H bond is higher than the tendency of Br to withdraw electrons from Br-O-H bond.  This is because, the inductive effect of Cl is higher than the inductive effect of Br. Thus, O-H bond in HClO breaks more easily than H-O bond in HBrO. Hence, HClO is more acidic than HBrO.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page 205 Paragraph 7.21)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-205',NULL,NULL),
(214343,'chemistry','Which of the following is incorrect trend of property of hydrogen halides?','pKa value ; HF < HCI < HBr < HI','The correct order is pKa value ; HF > HCI > HBr > HI\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 201 Table 7.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Melting point ; HI > HF > HBr > HCI','Boiling point ; HF > HI > HBr > HCI','pKa value ; HF < HCI < HBr < HI','Dissociation enthalpy ;  HF > HCI > HBr > HI','NEW','12-201',NULL,NULL),
(214344,'chemistry','Which of the following is correct?','When F₂ is taken in excess with Cl₂, ClF₃ is formed','Equal volumes of Cl₂ and F₂ combine to give ClF \nCl₂ + F₂ → 2ClF \nEquimolar concentrations of I₂ and Cl₂ combine to give ICl \nI₂ + Cl₂ → 2ICl \nWhen F₂ is taken in excess with Br₂, BrF₅ is formed \nBr₂ + 5F₂ → 2BrF₅\nWhen F₂ is taken in excess with Cl₂, ClF₃ is formed\nCl₂ + 3F₂ → 2ClF₃\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 206 Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Equimolar of Cl₂ and F₂ combine to form ClF','Equal volume of I₂ and Cl₂ combine to form ICl','When Cl is taken in excess with Br₂, BrF₃ is formed','When F₂ is taken in excess with Cl₂, ClF₃ is formed','NEW','12-206',NULL,NULL),
