(214352,'chemistry','Which among the following properties of halogens doesn\'t follow a regular pattern throughout the group?','Bond dissociation enthalpy','Melting point, Boiling point and density increase down the group for halogens. However, irregular pattern is observed in case of bond dissociation enthalpy (Cl2 > Br2 > F2 > I2)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page 198 Table 7.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Melting point ; HI > HF > HBr > HCI','Boiling point','Density','Bond dissociation enthalpy','NEW','12-198',NULL,NULL),
(214353,'chemistry','Xe and F2 combine in X and Y ratios to form XeF4 and XeF6 respectively. What are X and Y ?','1:5, 1:20','Xe (g) + 2F₂ (g) → XeF₄(s)\n(1:5 ratio)\nXe (g) + 3F₂ (g) → XeF₆(s)\n(1:20 ratio)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page 210 Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1:2, 1:6','1:2, 1:3','1:4, 1:6','1:5, 1:20','NEW','12-210',NULL,NULL),
(214354,'chemistry','Which of the folowing is the common product when XeF2, XeF4 and XeF6 undergo complete hydrolysis?','HF','2XeF₂ (s) + 2H₂O(l) → 2Xe (g) + 4HF(aq) + O₂(g)\n6XeF₄ + 12H₂O → 4Xe + 2XeO₃ + 24HF + 3 O₂\nXeF₆ + 3H₂O → XeO₃ + 6HF\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page 210 Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','HF','O₂','Xe','XeO₃','NEW','12-210',NULL,NULL),
(214355,'chemistry','Which of the following reactions is wrong?','BrCl + H₂O → HBr + HOCl','All interhaogen compounds undergo hydrolysis giving halide ion derived from the smaller halogen and a hypohalite ( when XX′), halite ( when XX′₃), halate (when XX′₅) and perhalate (when XX′₇) anion derived from the larger halogen.\nXX\' + H₂O → HX\' + HOX\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page 207 Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','BrF + H₂O → HF + HOBr','ICl + H₂O → HCl + HOI','ICl₃ + H₂O  → HCl + HOI','BrCl + H₂O → HBr + HOCl','NEW','12-207',NULL,NULL),
(214356,'chemistry','Which of the following gas is heavier than air ?','CO₂','The molecular weights of the N₂, O₂, CO₂ and H₂ 28 u, 32 u, 44 u, 2u respectively So CO₂ is heavier than air (O₂)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','N₂','O₂','CO₂','H₂','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(214357,'chemistry','The correct statement regarding (a) HClO (b) HClO₂ (c) HClO₃ (d) HClO₄ is-','The number of lone paira of electrons on Cl in (b) and (c) together is three','There are 1, 2 and 3 Cl=O bonds in HCIO₂, HCIO₃ and HCIO₄ respectively. There are 3, 2, 1 and 0 lone pairs on Cl in HCIO, HCIO₂, HCIO₃ and HCIO₄ respectively. The strongest acid is HClO₄. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 205 Fig 7.8) \nRefer diagram(ne2122)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The number of Cl=O bonds in (b) and (c) together is two','The number of lone paira of electrons on Cl in (b) and (c) together is three','The hybridization of Cl in (d) is sp³','(a) is the strongest acid','NEW','12-205',NULL,NULL),
(214358,'chemistry','Which of the following reaction is wrong?','[XeF₂] + [PF₆] → [XeF]⁺[PF₆]⁻','The correct reactions are-\n[XeF₂] + [PF₅] → [XeF]⁺[PF₆]⁻\n[XeF₆] + [NaF] → [Na]⁺[PF₇]⁻\n[XeF₄] + [SbF₅] → [XeF₃]⁺[SbF₆]⁻\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 210 Pargraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','[XeF₂] + [PF₅] → [XeF]⁺[PF₆]⁻','[XeF₆] + [NaF] → [Na]⁺[PF₇]⁻','[XeF₄] + [SbF₅] → [XeF₃]⁺[SbF₆]⁻','[XeF₂] + [PF₆] → [XeF]⁺[PF₆]⁻','NEW','12-210',NULL,NULL),
