(214362,'chemistry','What can be used in place of Conc. HCl, to obtain Chlorine, by reacting with MnO₂?','Mixture of common salt and Conc. H₂SO₄','By heating manganese dioxide with concentrated hydrochloric acid.\nMnO₂ + 4HCl → MnCl₂ + Cl₂ + 2H₂O\nHowever, a mixture of common salt and concentrated H₂SO₄ is used in place of HCl.\n4NaCl + MnO₂ + 4H₂SO₄→ MnCl₂ + 4NaHSO₄ + 2H₂O + Cl₂\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page 202 Paragraph 7.19)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Mixture of common salt and Conc.HNO₃','Mixture of common salt and dil. HNO₃','Mixture of common salt and dil. H₂SO₄','Mixture of common salt and Conc. H₂SO₄','NEW','12-202',NULL,NULL),
(214363,'chemistry','Arrange the following temperatures in the increasing order\nA is the temperature at which H₂SO₄ plant is operated\nB is the temperature at which Dinitrogen combines with dihydrogen to form ammonia \nC is the temperature at which S₂ is dominant species \nD is the temperature of boiling point of He','D < A < B < C','The temperature at which H₂SO₄ plant is operated is 720 K\nThe temperature at which Dinitrogen combines with dihydrogen to form ammonia is 773 K \nThe temperature at which S₂ is dominant species is 1000 K\nThe temperature of boiling point of He is 4.2 K\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 193 Paragraph 1)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 175 Paragraph 3)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 195 Paragraph 4)\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 205 Paragraph 7.23.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','order','1,D','2,A','3,B','4,C','NEW','12-205',NULL,NULL),
(214401,'chemistry','On addition of conc. H2SO4 to a chloride salt, colourless fumes are evolved but in case of iodide salt, violet fumes come out. This is because','HI gets oxidised to I2','The reason why colourless fumes are evolved on addition of concentrated H2SO4 to chloride salt and violet fumes come out in case of iodide salt is due to the difference in the nature of the halides and the reaction that occurs when they are treated with H2SO4.When concentrated H2SO4 is added to a chloride salt, it reacts with the chloride ion to form HCl gas, which is a colourless gas.\n The reaction is as follows:NaCl + H2SO4 → NaHSO4 + HCl .The HCl gas formed on reaction with H2SO4 is a strong acid and can react with moisture in the air to form white fumes of hydrochloric acid.On the other hand, when concentrated H2SO4 is added to an iodide salt, it reacts with the iodide ion to form hydrogen iodide gas (HI), which is a violet coloured gas. The reaction is as follows:NaI + H2SO4 → NaHSO4 + HIThe hydrogen iodide gas formed on reaction with H2SO4 is a weak acid and gets oxidized to form iodine, which gives the violet fumes. The oxidation of HI to iodine is due to the strong oxidizing power of concentrated H2SO4, which acts as an oxidizing agent in this reaction.Reference: NCERT Chemistry Part II textbook for class 12, Chapter 7, p. 276.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','H2SO4 reduces HI to I2','HI is of violet colour','HI gets oxidised to I2','HI changes to HIO3','NEW','12-276',NULL,NULL),
(214402,'chemistry','In qualitative analysis when H2S is passed through an aqueous solution of salt acidified with dil. HCl, a black precipitate is obtained. On boiling the precipitate with dil. HNO3, it forms a solution of blue colour. Addition of excess of aqueous solution of ammonia to this solution gives _________.','deep blue solution of [Cu (NH3)4]2+','The given question is related to the qualitative analysis of a salt solution using hydrogen sulfide gas, dilute hydrochloric acid, dilute nitric acid, and ammonia.When hydrogen sulfide gas is passed through an aqueous solution of a salt acidified with dilute hydrochloric acid, a black precipitate is obtained. This black precipitate is due to the formation of insoluble sulfides of certain metals like lead, copper, silver, mercury, etc.On boiling the black precipitate with dilute nitric acid, it forms a blue-colored solution. This blue coloration is due to the formation of nitro complexes of metals like copper and lead.Addition of an excess of aqueous solution of ammonia to this blue solution gives a deep blue solution of copper tetrammine complex. This is because ammonia forms complex ions with metal ions like Cu2+ to form tetrammine copper complexes.References: NCERT Chemistry Part II textbook for class 12, Chapter 6, p. 242-243.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','deep blue precipitate of Cu (OH)2','deep blue solution of [Cu (NH3)4]2+','deep blue solution of Cu(NO3)2','deep blue solution of Cu(OH)2.Cu(NO3)2','NEW','12-243',NULL,NULL),
(214403,'chemistry','In a cyclotrimetaphosphoric acid molecule, how many single and double\nbonds are present?','3 double bonds; 12 single bonds','Cyclotrimetaphosphoric acid contains three double bonds and 9 single bonds','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','3 double bonds; 9 single bonds','6 double bonds; 6 single bonds','3 double bonds; 12 single bonds','Zero double bonds; 12 single bonds','NEW','12-185',NULL,NULL),
(214404,'chemistry','Which of the following elements can be involved in pπ–dπ bonding?','Phosphorus','pπ-dπ bonding is a type of covalent bonding in which both p orbitals and d orbitals of two atoms overlap and form a bond. This type of bonding is seen in compounds of phosphorus, such as phosphorus pentachloride (PCl5), phosphorus pentoxide (P4O10), etc. In these compounds, the central phosphorus atom has five unpaired electrons in its valence shell, which can hybridize to form five sp3d orbitals. Four of these sp3d orbitals are used to form four sigma bonds with chlorine/oxygen atoms, while the remaining sp3d orbital overlaps with the p orbital of the other phosphorus atom to form a pπ-dπ bond.On the other hand, carbon and nitrogen typically form covalent bonds through the overlap of p orbitals to form pi (π) bonds, whereas boron usually forms three covalent bonds with other atoms using its three valence electrons in its p orbital.Reference: NCERT Chemistry Part II textbook for class 12, Chapter 7, p. 268-270.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Carbon','Nitrogen','Phosphorus','Boron','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(214405,'chemistry','Which of the following pairs of ions are isoelectronic and isostructural?','CO32–, NO3–','CO32- and NO3-  are isoelectronic with 32 electrons and sp2 hybridisation\n\nhence trigonal planar structure.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','CO32–, NO3–','ClO3 – , CO3 2–','SO3 2–, NO3 –','ClO3– , SO3 2–','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(214406,'chemistry','Affinity for hydrogen decreases in the group from fluorine to iodine. Which of\nthe halogen acids should have highest bond dissociation enthalpy?','HF','HF On moving top to bottom HCl Size of halogen atom increases HBr H-X \nbond length increases HI Bond dissociation enthalpy decreases','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','HF','HCl','HBr','HI','NEW','12-201',NULL,NULL),
(214407,'chemistry','Which of the compounds will act as strongest reducing agent?','SbH3','SbH3  will act as strongest reducing agent due to minimum bond enthalpy.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','NH3','PH3','AsH3','SbH3','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(214408,'chemistry','On heating with concentrated NaOH solution in an inert atmosphere of CO2,\nwhite phosphorus gives a gas. Which of the following statement is incorrect\nabout the gas?','It is more basic than NH3.','he statement that is incorrect about the gas obtained on heating white phosphorus with concentrated NaOH solution in an inert atmosphere of CO2 is (c) It is more basic than NH3.White phosphorus (P4) reacts with concentrated NaOH solution in the presence of CO2 to give phosphine gas (PH3) along with sodium hypophosphite and sodium phosphate.The properties of phosphine gas are given below:\n(a) It is highly poisonous and has a smell like rotten fish.\n(b) Its solution in water decomposes in the presence of light.\n(c) It is less basic than NH3.\n(d) It is a reducing agent and can reduce metal ions to their respective metals.Option (c) is incorrect because NH3 (ammonia) is a weak base, and phosphine (PH3) is an even weaker base than NH3. Hence, the correct option is (d) It is less basic than NH3.Reference: NCERT Chemistry Part II textbook for class 12, Chapter 7, p. 289-290','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','It is highly poisonous and has smell like rotten fish.','It’s solution in water decomposes in the presence of light','It is more basic than NH3.','It is less basic than NH3.','NEW','12-182',NULL,NULL),
(214409,'chemistry','Which of the following acids forms three series of salts?','H3PO4','H3PO4 has 3 -OH groups \ni.e., has three ionisable H-atoms and hence forms three series of salts.\n These three possible series of salts of H3PO4 are as follows: .\n\nNaH2PO4, NaHPO4 and Na3PO4','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','H3PO2','H3BO3','H3PO4','H3PO3','NEW','12-185',NULL,NULL),
(214410,'chemistry','Strong reducing behaviour of H3PO2 is due to','Presence of one –OH group and two P–H bonds','H3PO2  is a monobasic acid. It acts as a reducing agent as it has two P – H bonds.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Low oxidation state of phosphorus','Presence of two –OH groups and one P–H bond','Presence of one –OH group and two P–H bonds','High electron gain enthalpy of phosphorus','NEW','12-185',NULL,NULL),
(214411,'chemistry','On heating lead nitrate forms oxides of nitrogen and lead. The oxides formed\nare ______.','NO2, PbO','2Pb(NO3)2 —> 2PbO + 4NO2 + O2','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','N2O, PbO','NO2, PbO','NO, PbO','NO, PbO2','NEW','12-178',NULL,NULL),
(214412,'chemistry','Which of the following elements does not show allotropy?','Nitrogen','Nitrogen does not show allotropy due to its weak N – N single bond.\n\nTherefore, ability of nitrogen to form polymeric structure or more than \none structure become less. Hence, nitrogen does not show allotropy.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Nitrogen','Bismuth','Antimony','Arsenic','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(214413,'chemistry','Maximum covalency of nitrogen is ______________.','4','Maximum covalency of nitrogen is four as it cannot extend its valency beyond four [ NH4, R4N] due to absence of vacant d orbitals.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','3','5','4','6','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(214414,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is wrong?','Single N–N bond is stronger than the single P–P bond.','Nitrogen forms pπ-pπ multiple bond and the bond strength is very high. Single N – N bond is weaker than single P – P bond.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Single N–N bond is stronger than the single P–P bond.','PH3 can act as a ligand in the formation of coordination compound\nwith transition elements.','NO2 is paramagnetic in nature.','Covalency of nitrogen in N2O5 is four','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(214415,'chemistry','A brown ring is formed in the ring test for NO3– ion. It is due to the formation of','[Fe(H2O)5 (NO)]2+','When freshly prepared solution of FeSO4 is added in a solution containing NO3 ion, it leads to formation of a brown coloured complex. This is known as brown ring test of nitrate.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','[Fe(H2O)5 (NO)]2+','FeSO4.NO2','[Fe(H2O)4(NO)2]2+','FeSO4.HNO3','NEW','12-180',NULL,NULL),
(214416,'chemistry','Elements of group-15 form compounds in +5 oxidation state. However, bismuth forms only one well characterised compound in +5 oxidation state.The compound is','BiF5','Bismuth commonly shows +3 oxidation state instead of +5 oxidation state due to inert pair effect. But Bi forms BiF5 compound due to high electronegativity and small size of fluorine atom.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Bi2O5','BiF5','BiCl5','Bi2S5','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(214417,'chemistry','On heating ammonium dichromate and barium azide separately we get','N2 in both cases','N2 in both cases.When ammonium dichromate is heated, it decomposes into chromium (III) oxide, nitrogen gas, and water vapour. The balanced chemical equation for the reaction is:\n(NH4)2Cr2O7(s) → Cr2O3(s) + N2(g) + 4H2O(g)\nBarium azide, on the other hand, decomposes on heating to form barium oxide, nitrogen gas, and some azides. However, these azides further decompose to form nitrogen gas. The overall reaction can be represented as:\n2Ba(N3)2(s) → 2BaO(s) + 3N2(g) + N2(g)\nTherefore, the correct option is (a) N2 in both cases.Reference: NCERT Chemistry Part II textbook for class 12, Chapter 7, p. 299-300.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','N2 in both cases','N2 with ammonium dichromate and NO with barium azide','N2O with ammonium dichromate and N2 with barium azide','N2O with ammonium dichromate and NO2 with barium azide','NEW','12-174',NULL,NULL),
(214418,'chemistry','In the preparation of HNO3, we get NO gas by catalytic oxidation of ammonia.\nThe moles of NO produced by the oxidation of two moles of NH3 will be ______','2','Two moles of NH3 will produce 2 moles of NO on catalytic oxidation of ammonia in preparation of nitric acid','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','2','3','4','6','NEW','12-179',NULL,NULL),
(214419,'chemistry','The oxidation state of central atom in the anion of compound NaH2PO2 will\nbe ______.','1+','The anion in the compound NaH2PO2 is H2PO2-, where phosphorus is the central atom. The oxidation state of the central atom (phosphorus) can be calculated as follows\n:Let x be the oxidation state of phosphorus.The oxidation state of hydrogen is +1, and there are two hydrogen atoms in the anion. \nTherefore, the total oxidation state of hydrogen is +2.The oxidation state of oxygen is -2, and there are two oxygen atoms in the anion. Therefore, the total oxidation state of oxygen is -4.The oxidation state of sodium (Na) is +1.\nUsing the oxidation state rules, we can write the following equation:(+1) + x + (-4) + (+2) = +1','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','3+','5+','1+','-3','NEW','12-184',NULL,NULL),
(214420,'chemistry','Which of the following is not tetrahedral in shape','SF4','SF4 trigonal bipyramidal structure.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','NH4+','SiCl4','SF4','SO4 2–','NEW','12-188',NULL,NULL),
(214421,'chemistry','Which of the following are peroxoacids of sulphur?','H2SO5 and H2S2O8',NULL,'Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','H2SO5 and H2S2O8','H2SO5 and H2S2O7','H2S2O7 and H2S2O8','H2S2O6 and H2S2O7','NEW','12-194',NULL,NULL),
(214422,'chemistry','Hot conc. H2SO4 acts as moderately strong oxidising agent. It oxidises both metals and nonmetals. Which of the following element is oxidised by conc. H2SO4 into two gaseous products?','C','oncentrated H2SO4 acts as a moderately strong oxidizing agent and can oxidize both metals and nonmetals.Out of the given options, the element that is oxidized by concentrated H2SO4 into two gaseous products is:(c) PhosphorusWhen phosphorus reacts with concentrated H2SO4, it is oxidized to phosphorus pentoxide (P4O10) and sulfur dioxide (SO2) gas.P4 + 10H2SO4 → 4H3PO4 + 10SO2 + 2H2OThe sulfur dioxide formed in the reaction with phosphorus can further react with more H2SO4 to form sulfur trioxide (SO3), which is also a gaseous product.Reference: NCERT Chemistry Part II textbook for class 12, Chapter 7, p. 232.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cu','S','C','Zn','NEW','12-196',NULL,NULL),
(214423,'chemistry','A black compound of manganese reacts with a halogen acid to give greenish yellow gas. When excess of this gas reacts with NH3 an unstable trihalide is formed. In this process the oxidation state of nitrogen changes from _________.','– 3 to +3','Mn02 + 4HCl -» MnCl2 + 2H2O + Cl\n\n(greenish yellow gas)\n\nWhen excess of Cl2 reacts with NH3 the products are NCl3 and HCl.\n\nNH3 + 3Cl2 —> NCl3 +3HCl\nO.S. (-3)    O.S.(+3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','– 3 to +3','– 3 to 0','– 3 to +5','0 to – 3','NEW','12-203',NULL,NULL),
(214424,'chemistry','In the preparation of compounds of Xe, Bartlett had taken O2+ Pt F6– as a base compound. This is because','both O2 and Xe have almost same ionisation enthalpy.','Bartlett had taken 02 Pt as a base compound because 02 and Xe both have  almost same ionization enthalpy. The ionization enthalpies of noble  gases are the highest in their respective periods due to their stable \nelectronic configurations.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','both O2 and Xe have same size.','both O2 and Xe have same electron gain enthalpy.','both O2 and Xe have almost same ionisation enthalpy.','both Xe and O2 are gases','NEW','12-209',NULL,NULL),
(214425,'chemistry','In solid state PCl5 is a _________.','ionic solid with [PCl4]+ tetrahedral and [PCl6]– octahedra','In the solid state, PCl5 is an ionic compound.Phosphorus pentachloride (PCl5) is a covalent molecule in the gaseous state and in nonpolar solvents such as benzene. However, in the solid state, PCl5 undergoes ionization to form PCl4+ and PCl6- ions, leading to the formation of an ionic crystal lattice.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','covalent solid','octahedral structure','ionic solid with [PCl6]+ octahedral and [PCl4]– tetrahedra','ionic solid with [PCl4]+ tetrahedral and [PCl6]– octahedra','NEW','12-183',NULL,NULL),
(214426,'chemistry','Reduction potentials of some ions are given below. Arrange them in decreasing\norder of oxidising power.\nClO4–   =1.19V       \nIO4– =1.65V           \nBrO4– =1.74V','BrO4– > IO4– > ClO4–','The higher the reduction potential, the higher is its tendency to get reduced. Hence, the order of oxidizing power is\n\nBrO4– > IO4– > CIO4–','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','ClO4– > IO4– > BrO4–','IO4– > BrO4– > ClO4–','BrO4– > IO4– > ClO4–','BrO4– > ClO4– > IO4–','NEW','12-201',NULL,NULL),
(214427,'chemistry','Which of the following is isoelectronic pair?','BrO2– , BrF2+','An isoelectronic pair is a pair of ions or atoms that have the same number of electrons.Out of the given options, the isoelectronic pair is:\n(b) BrO2–, BrF2+The BrO2– ion has a total of 34 electrons, while BrF2+ ion has 34 electrons as well. Therefore, they are isoelectronic.\nOption (a) ICl2, ClO2 - ICl2 has 19 electrons while ClO2 has 28 electrons, so they are not isoelectronic.\nOption (c) ClO2, BrF - ClO2 has 28 electrons while BrF has 20 electrons, so they are not isoelectronic.\nOption (d) CN–, O3 - CN– has 10 electrons while O3 has 18 electrons, so they are not isoelectronic.\nReference: NCERT Chemistry Part II textbook for class 12, Chapter 7, p. 242.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','ICl2, ClO2','BrO2– , BrF2+','ClO2, BrF','CN–, O3','NEW','12-206',NULL,NULL),
(214428,'chemistry','Assertion : N2 is less reactive than P4.\n\nReason : Nitrogen has more electron gain enthalpy than phosphorus.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','N2 is less reactive due to high bond dissociation energy. Its electron gain enthalpy is less than phosphorus.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(214429,'chemistry','Assertion : HNO3 makes iron passive.\n\nReason : HNO3 forms a protective layer of ferric nitrate on the surface\nof iron.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Passivity is attained by formation of a thin film of oxide on iron.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-180',NULL,NULL),
(214430,'chemistry','Assertion : HI cannot be prepared by the reaction of KI with concentrated H2SO4\n\nReason : HI has lowest H–X bond strength among halogen acids.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The Assertion given in the question is true. HI cannot be prepared by the reaction of KI with concentrated H2SO4. This is because the HI molecule is very unstable and easily decomposes into Hydrogen gas and Iodine. The reaction of KI with concentrated H2SO4 generates H2S gas due to the reduction of Sulphuric acid by Iodide ion. The Iodine generated from HI decomposition can also react with H2S gas to form Hydrogen Iodide, but the overall yield is very low.The Reason given in the question is also true. Among halogen acids, the H-X bond strength decreases in the order HF > HCl > HBr > HI, where X represents the halogen. This is because the size of the halogen atom increases down the group, leading to an increase in bond length and a decrease in bond strength. Since HI has the weakest H-X bond among halogen acids, it is the most reactive and the easiest to dissociate.Reference: NCERT Chemistry Part II textbook for class 12, Chapter 7, p. 269.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-201',NULL,NULL),
(214431,'chemistry','Assertion : Both rhombic and monoclinic sulphur exist as S8 but oxygen exists as O2.\n\nReason : Oxygen forms pπ – pπ multiple bond due to small size and small bond length but pπ – pπ bonding is not possible in sulphur.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','S exists as S8 but oxygen exists as O2 because oxygen forms pπ-pπ multiple bonds which is not possible in S.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-192',NULL,NULL),
(214432,'chemistry','Assertion : NaCl reacts with concentrated H2SO4 to give colourless fumes with pungent smell. But on adding MnO2 the fumes become greenish yellow.\n\nReason : MnO2 oxidises HCl to chlorine gas which is greenish yellow','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Colourless fumes of HCl become greenish yellow because MnO2 oxidises HCl to chlorine gas.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-203',NULL,NULL),
(214433,'chemistry','Assertion : SF6 cannot be hydrolysed but SF4 can be.\n\nReason : Six F atoms in SF6 prevent the attack of H2O on sulphur atom of SF6.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','SF6 is sterically protected due to presence of six F atoms around S which prevents the attack of H2O on SF6.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-188',NULL,NULL),
(214501,'chemistry','In XeF₂, XeF₄, XeF₆ the number of lone pairs on Xe are respectively [2002]','3,2,1','In XeF₂, Xe has two fluorine atoms attached to it, and each fluorine atom contributes one electron to form a covalent bond with xenon. Therefore, there are no lone pairs on Xe in XeF₂.\n\nIn XeF₄, Xe has four fluorine atoms attached to it, and each fluorine atom contributes one electron to form a covalent bond with xenon. Therefore, there are two lone pairs on Xe in XeF₄.\n\nIn XeF₆, Xe has six fluorine atoms attached to it, and each fluorine atom contributes one electron to form a covalent bond with xenon. Therefore, there is only one lone pair on Xe in XeF₆.\n\nSo, the correct answer is 3,2,1: XeF₂ has 0 lone pairs on Xe, XeF₄ has 2 lone pairs on Xe, and XeF₆ has 1 lone pair on Xe.\n\nRefer diagram(ne2123)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','2,3,1','1,2,3','4,1,2','3,2,1','NEW','12-210',NULL,NULL),
(214502,'chemistry','In case of nitrogen, NCl₃ is possible but not NCl₅ while in case of phosphorus, PCl₃ as well as PCl₅ are possible. It is due to [2002]','availability of vacat d orbitals in P but not in N','Electronic configuration of nitrogen (N) is 1s²2s²2p³, which means that it has no vacant d orbitals to accommodate the additional electrons required for the formation of NCl₅. On the other hand, phosphorus (P) has the electronic configuration of 1s²2s²2p⁶3s²3p³, which means that it has three vacant d orbitals that can accommodate additional electrons, allowing for the formation of PCl₅.\n\nTherefore, the availability of vacant d orbitals in P but not in N is the reason why P can form both PCl₃ and PCl₅, while N can only form NCl₃ but not NCl₅.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 165)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','availability of vacat d orbitals in P but not in N','lower electronegativity of P than N','lower tendency of H bond formation in  P than N','occurence of P in solid while N in gaseous state at room temperature','NEW','12-171',NULL,NULL),
(214503,'chemistry','Number of sigma bond in P₄O₁₀ is','16','The molecule P₄O₁₀ contains four phosphorus atoms and ten oxygen atoms. Each of the four phosphorus atoms is connected to three other phosphorus atoms via single covalent bonds, resulting in 12 sigma bonds. Each of the ten oxygen atoms is connected to one phosphorus atom via a single covalent bond, resulting in 10 sigma bonds.\n\nTherefore, the total number of sigma bonds in P₄O₁₀ is:\n\n12 (P-P bonds) + 10 (P-O bonds) = 16.\nRefer diagram(ne2124)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','6','7','17','16','NEW','12-184',NULL,NULL),
(214504,'chemistry','Oxidation number of Cl in CaOCl₂ is','+1 and -1 since it contains ClO- and Cl-','The oxidation number of an element in a compound is the charge it would have if all the shared electrons were assigned to the more electronegative atom in the bond.\n\nIn CaOCl2, the oxidation number of calcium (Ca) is +2 because it is a Group 2 element, and the oxidation number of oxygen (O) is -2 because it is more electronegative than calcium.\n\nTo determine the oxidation number of chlorine (Cl), we can use the fact that the sum of the oxidation numbers in a compound is equal to the overall charge of the compound. In this case, CaOCl2 is neutral, so the sum of the oxidation numbers must be zero.\n\nLet x be the oxidation number of Cl in CaOCl2. Then we have:\n\n+2 (from Ca) + (-2) (from O) + x (from Cl) = 0\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get:\n\nx = 0 - (+2) - (-2) = 0\n\nThis means that the oxidation number of Cl in CaOCl2 is zero.\n\nHowever, if we consider the polyatomic ion ClO- that is present in the compound, the oxidation number of Cl in ClO- is -1 (since oxygen has an oxidation number of -2). Therefore, there must be another Cl atom with an oxidation number of +1 in order to balance the overall charge of the compound. Hence, the oxidation number of Cl in CaOCl2 is both +1 and -1, due to the presence of both ClO- and Cl- ions in the compound.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 203 Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','0, since it contains Cl2','-1, since it contains Cl-','+1, since it contains ClO-','+1 and -1 since it contains ClO- and Cl-','NEW','12-205',NULL,NULL),
(214505,'chemistry','What may be expected to happen when phosphine gas is mixed with chlorine gas?','The mixture only cools down','When phosphine gas (PH3) is mixed with chlorine gas (Cl2), they react to form phosphorus trichloride (PCl3) and hydrogen chloride gas (HCl) as follows:\n\nPH3 + Cl2 → PCl3 + HCl\n\nThis is an exothermic reaction, which means that energy is released in the form of heat. However, the amount of heat released may not be enough to overcome the heat absorbed during the vaporization of the reactants.\n\nTherefore, when phosphine gas is mixed with chlorine gas, the mixture is expected to cool down due to the absorption of heat during the reaction','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','PCl₃ and HCl are formed and the mixture warms up','PCl₅ and HCl are formed and the mixture cools down','PH₃.Cl₂ is formed with warming up','The mixture only cools down','NEW','12-181',NULL,NULL),
(214506,'chemistry','Concentrated hydrochloric acid when kept in open air sometimes produces a cloud of white fumes. The explanation for it is that [2003)','oxygen in air reacts with the emitted HCl gas to form a cloud of chlorine gas','Reaction between HCl and oxygen (O2) can produce a cloud of chlorine (Cl2) gas. The balanced chemical equation for this reaction is:\n\n4HCl + O2 → 2H2O + 2Cl2\n\nIn the presence of oxygen, concentrated hydrochloric acid can decompose to form hydrogen chloride gas (HCl) and chlorine gas (Cl2). The Cl2 gas produced can react with moisture in the air to form a visible cloud of hydrochloric acid droplets.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','oxygen in air reacts with the emitted HCl gas to form a cloud of chlorine gas','strong affinity ofHCl gas for moisture in air\nresults in forming of droplets of liquid solution which appears like a cloudy smoke','due to strong affinity for water,\nconcentrated hydrochloric acid pulls\nmoisture of air towards itself This moisture\nforms droplets of water and hence the\ncloud.','concentrated hydrochloric acid emits\nstrongly smelling HCl gas all the time','NEW','12-202',NULL,NULL),
(214507,'chemistry','Which one of the following substances has the highest proton affinity ? [2003]','NH₃','Proton affinity refers to the tendency of a substance to attract and bond with a proton (H+). The higher the proton affinity of a substance, the more likely it is to accept a proton.\n\nAmong the given options, NH3 (ammonia) has the highest proton affinity. This is because it has a lone pair of electrons on the nitrogen atom, which can readily bond with a proton to form NH4+ (ammonium ion). This reaction is exothermic, releasing energy in the form of heat, which further indicates that NH3 has a high proton affinity.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','H₂S','NH₃','PH₃','H₂O','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(214508,'chemistry','Which among the following factors is the most important in making fluorine the strongest oxidizing halogen?[2004]','Bond dissociation energy','The strength of an oxidizing agent depends on its ability to accept electrons from other substances, which in turn depends on various factors such as ionization energy, electron affinity, and bond dissociation energy.\n\nAmong the given options, bond dissociation energy is the most important factor in making fluorine the strongest oxidizing halogen. Bond dissociation energy refers to the energy required to break a bond between two atoms. Fluorine has the highest bond dissociation energy among all the halogens, meaning that it requires the most energy to break the F-F bond. As a result, fluorine can easily remove electrons from other substances and act as a strong oxidizing agent.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','Hydration enthalpy','Ionization enthalpy','Electron affinity','Bond dissociation energy','NEW','12-200',NULL,NULL),
(214509,'chemistry','Excess of KI reacts with CuSO₄ solution and then Na₂SO₃ solution is added to it. Which of the statements is incorrect for this reaction ?[2004]','CuI₂ is formed','When excess KI reacts with CuSO4 solution, a red-brown precipitate of CuI is formed due to the following reaction:\n\n2KI + CuSO4 → CuI2 + K2SO4\n\nWhen Na2SO3 solution is added to the mixture, the following reaction takes place:\n\nCuI2 + Na2SO3 + H2O → Cu2I2 + Na2SO4 + 2H+ + SO32-\n\nIn this reaction, the CuI2 is reduced to Cu2I2 by Na2SO3, which acts as a reducing agent.\n\nTherefore, the incorrect statement for this reaction is: CuI2 is formed. CuI2 is not formed in this reaction. Instead, CuI is formed initially, which is then reduced to Cu2I2 by Na2SO3.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','NaS₂O₃ is oxidised','CuI₂ is formed','Cu₂I₂ is formed','Evolved I₂ is reduced','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(214510,'chemistry','Which one of the following statement regarding helium is incorrect?[2004]','It is used to fill gas balloons instead of\nhydrogen because it is lighter and noninflammable','Helium is actually used to fill gas balloons not because it is lighter, but because it is non-flammable and safer than hydrogen, which is highly flammable and can cause explosions. However, helium is not as light as hydrogen, so it is not as commonly used for filling balloons for applications like air travel or weather balloons.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','It is used to produce and sustain powerful\nsuperconducting magnets','It is used as a cryogenic agent for carrying\nout experiments at low temperatures','It is used to fill gas balloons instead of\nhydrogen because it is lighter and noninflammable','It is used in gas-cooled nuclear reactors','NEW','12-211',NULL,NULL),
(214511,'chemistry','The number of hydrogen atom(s) attached to phosphorus atom in hypophosphorous acid is [2005]','two','The number of hydrogen atom(s) attached to the phosphorus atom in hypophosphorous acid is 2.\n\nThe chemical formula of hypophosphorous acid is H3PO2. It contains one phosphorus atom, three hydrogen atoms, and two oxygen atoms. The two hydrogen atoms are directly attached to the phosphorus atom, while the third hydrogen atom is attached to one of the oxygen atoms. Therefore, there are two hydrogen atoms attached to the phosphorus atom in hypophosphorous acid.\nRefer diagram(ne2125)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','three','one','two','zero','NEW','12-185',NULL,NULL),
(214512,'chemistry','The correct order of the thermal stability of hydrogen halides (H-X) is [2005]','HF> HCl >HBr > HI','This is because the strength of the hydrogen-halogen bond decreases down the group as the size of the halogen atom increases. Therefore, the bond strength is weakest for HF and strongest for HI.\n\nAs a result, the thermal stability of the hydrogen halides follows the opposite trend, with HI being the most thermally stable and HF being the least thermally stable. This is because the weaker the bond, the easier it is to break apart upon heating.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','HI> HCl <HF> HBr','HCl<HF> HBr< HI','HF> HCl >HBr > HI','HI < HBr > HCl < HF','NEW','12-201',NULL,NULL),
(214513,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is true? [2006]','HNO3 is a stronger acid than HNO2','In aqueous solutions, acids donate hydrogen ions (H+) to water, forming hydronium ions (H3O+). The strength of an acid depends on how readily it donates H+ ions to water. The stronger the acid, the more readily it donates H+ ions to water.\n\nComparing HNO3 and HNO2, both are oxoacids of nitrogen with different oxidation states. HNO3 has a higher oxidation state (+5) than HNO2 (+3), and thus it can donate H+ ions more easily, making it a stronger acid. Therefore, HNO3 is a stronger acid than HNO2.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'3','mcq','HClO4 is a weaker acid than HClO3','HNO3 is a stronger acid than HNO2','H3PO3 is a stronger acid than H2SO3','In aqueous medium HF is a stronger acid than HCl','NEW','12-179',NULL,NULL),
(214514,'chemistry','The increasing order of the first ionization oxygen requires high temperature enthalpies of the elements B, P, Sand F (Lowest first) is [2006]','B<S<P<F','The trend of increasing ionization enthalpy from left to right and decreasing from top to bottom in a group is generally followed, but it can be affected by exceptions such as fully or half-filled orbitals. In this case, phosphorus has a stable half-filled electronic configuration, which makes its ionization enthalpy greater than that of sulfur. Thus, the correct order is B < S < P < F.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','B<P<S<F','B<S<P<F','F < S < P < B','P < S < B < F','NEW','12-200',NULL,NULL),
(214515,'chemistry','What products are expected from the disproportionation reaction of hypochlorous  acid? [2006]','HCl and HClO₃','The disproportionation reaction of hypochlorous acid (HClO) is given by:\n\n2HClO → HClO₃ + HCl\n\nSo, the products expected from the disproportionation reaction of hypochlorous acid are hydrogen chloride (HCl) and chloric acid (HClO₃).','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','HCl and Cl₂O','HCl and HClO₃','HClO₃ and Cl₂O','HClO₂ and HClO₄','NEW','12-205',NULL,NULL),
(214516,'chemistry','Identify the incorrect statement among the following. [2007]','Cl2 reacts with excess of NH3 to give N2 and HCl','Cl2 reacts with excess of NH3 to give N2 and HCl" is incorrect. In fact, when Cl2 reacts with excess NH3, it forms mainly nitrogen trichloride (NCl3) and ammonium chloride (NH4Cl) as products, along with some other minor products.\n\nThe correct reaction is:\n\n3Cl2 + 8NH3 → 6NH4Cl + NCl3\n\nThe reason for this reaction is that chlorine is a highly electronegative element and can easily attract electrons from other atoms. In this reaction, it attracts electrons from the nitrogen atoms in ammonia (NH3) and forms nitrogen trichloride (NCl3).','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'3','mcq','Br2 reacts with hot and strong NaOH solution to give NaBr and H2O','Ozone reacts with SO2 to give SO3','Silicon reacts with NaOH in the presence of air to give Na2SiO3 and H2O','Cl2 reacts with excess of NH3 to give N2 and HCl','NEW','12-203',NULL,NULL),
(214517,'chemistry','Regular use of the follwoing fertilizers increases the acidity of soil? [2007]','Ammonium sulphate','The regular use of ammonium sulfate as a fertilizer can increase the acidity of soil because ammonium ions (NH4+) released from ammonium sulfate fertilizer can undergo nitrification in the soil to form nitrate ions (NO3-) and hydrogen ions (H+). The hydrogen ions can then lower the soil pH, making it more acidic. In contrast, other fertilizers like potassium nitrate, urea, and superphosphate of lime do not have such an effect on soil acidity.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','Ammonium sulphate','Potassium nitrate','Urea','Superphosphate of lime','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(214518,'chemistry','Which one of the following reactions compounds is not feasible? [2009]','XeO3 + 6HF ----> XeF6 + 3H2O','XeO3 + 6HF → XeF6 + 3H2O is not feasible because XeO3 and HF are not strong enough to react with each other to form XeF6 and H2O. XeO3 is a powerful oxidizing agent and HF is a weak acid. Therefore, XeO3 can oxidize HF to form H2O and F2, but it cannot convert HF to XeF6. The actual reaction between XeO3 and HF is as follows:\n\nXeO3 + 6HF → XeOF4 + 2H2O + 4F2\n\nHere, XeO3 reacts with HF to form XeOF4, which is a volatile compound and can be further converted to XeF6 by reacting with excess fluorine gas. Therefore, the correct option is XeO3 + 6HF → XeF6 + 3H2O.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','3XeF4 + 6H2O ---> 2Xe + XeO3 + 12HF + 1.5O2','2XeF2 + 2H2O ----> 2 Xe + 4HF + O2','XeF6 + RbF -----> Rb[XeF7]','XeO3 + 6HF ----> XeF6 + 3H2O','NEW','12-210',NULL,NULL),
(214519,'chemistry','Which of the following has maximum number of lone pairs associated with Xe?','XeF2','XeF2 has the maximum number of lone pairs associated with Xe.\n\nIn XeF4, Xe is bonded to four fluoride atoms, leaving only two lone pair on Xe.\n\nIn XeF6, Xe is bonded to six fluoride atoms, leaving one lone pairs on Xe.\n\nIn XeO3, Xe is bonded to three oxygen atoms, leaving one lone pair on Xe.\n\nIn XeF2, Xe is bonded to two fluoride atoms, leaving three lone pairs on Xe.Refer diagram(ne2126)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','XeF4','XeF6','XeF2','XeO3','NEW','12-210',NULL,NULL),
(214520,'chemistry','The molecule having smallest bond angle is:','SbCl3','In general, the bond angle decreases as the lone pair on the central atom increases. The central atoms in NCl3, AsCl3, PCl3, and SbCl3 all have one lone pair. However, due to the larger size of the Sb atom, the bond angles in SbCl3 are the smallest among these molecules. Therefore, SbCl3 has the smallest bond angle among the given options.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','NCl3','AsCl3','SbCl3','PCl3','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(214521,'chemistry','Which among the following is the most reactive? [JEE M 2015]','ICl','ICl is the most reactive among the given options. This is because the reactivity of halogens decreases down the group due to the increasing size of the atoms and the decreasing electronegativity. Among ICl, Iodine is more electronegative than Chlorine, which makes the bond more polar and hence more reactive.\nthe interhalogen compounds are\ngenerally more reactive than halogens\n( except F 2), since the bond between two\ndissimilar electronegative elements is\nweaker than the bond between two similar\natoms i.e, X - X','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','I2','ICl','Cl2','Br2','NEW','12-207',NULL,NULL),
(214522,'chemistry','Assertion: Nitrogen and oxygen are the main components in the atmosphere but these do not react to form oxides of nitrogen\n\nReason: The reaction between nitrogen and oxygen requires high temperature [2015]','Both assertion and reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation for the assertion','Nitrogen and oxygen are the main components in the atmosphere, but they do not react to form oxides of nitrogen under normal conditions. This is because the reaction between nitrogen and oxygen is highly endothermic and requires a high temperature of around 2000°C to 2500°C, which is not present in the atmosphere','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','The assertion is incorrect, but the reason is correct','Both the assertion are incorrect','Both assertion and reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation for the assertion','Both assertion and reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation for the assertion','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(214523,'chemistry','Which one has the highest boiling point? [2015]','Xe','Xenon (Xe) has the highest boiling point among the given options. This is because Xe is the heaviest noble gas with the highest number of electrons, which leads to stronger London dispersion forces between its atoms. As a result, Xe requires more energy to overcome these attractive forces and transition into a gaseous state, resulting in a higher boiling point compared to other noble gases.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','Kr','Xe','He','Ne','NEW','12-208',NULL,NULL),
(214524,'chemistry','The pair in which phosphorus atoms have a formal oxidation state of +3 is: [2016]','Orthophosphorus and pyrophosphorus acids','In orthophosphorus acid (H3PO3), the oxidation state of each phosphorus atom is +3. In pyrophosphorus acid (H4P2O5), the average oxidation state of the two phosphorus atoms is also +3.\n\nIn hypophosphorus acid (H3PO2), each phosphorus atom has an oxidation state of +1, while in pyrophosphoric acid (H4P2O7), the average oxidation state of the two phosphorus atoms is +5.\nRefer diagram(ne2127)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','Orthophosphorus and hypophosphorus acids','Pyrophosphorus and pyrophosphoric acids','Orthophosphorus and pyrophosphorus acids','Pyrophosphorus and hypophosphoric acid','NEW','12-185',NULL,NULL),
(214525,'chemistry','The reaction of zinc and dilute and concentrated nitric acid, produces:','N₂O and NO₂','The reaction of zinc and dilute nitric acid produces nitrogen monoxide (NO) and water:\n\nZn + 4HNO3 (dilute) → Zn(NO3)2 + 2N₂O + 2H2O\n\nHowever, the reaction of zinc and concentrated nitric acid produces nitrogen dioxide (NO2), nitrogen monoxide (NO), and water:\n\n3Zn + 8HNO3 (concentrated) → 3Zn(NO3)2 + 2NO2 + 4H2O','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','NO and N₂O','NO₂ and N₂O','N₂O and NO₂','NO₂ and NO','NEW','12-179',NULL,NULL),
(214526,'chemistry','The products obtained when chlorine gas reacts with cold and dilute aqueous NaOH are:','Cl⁻ and ClO⁻','When chlorine gas reacts with cold and dilute aqueous NaOH are Cl⁻ and ClO⁻. The balanced chemical equation for the reaction is:\n\nCl2 + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaClO + H2O\n\nThe reaction involves the reduction of Cl2 to Cl⁻ and the oxidation of OH⁻ to ClO⁻.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','ClO⁻ and ClO₃⁻','ClO₂⁻ and ClO₃⁻','Cl⁻ and ClO⁻','Cl⁻ and ClO₂⁻','NEW','12-205',NULL,NULL),
(214527,'chemistry','The recommended concentration of flouride ion in drinking water is up to 1 ppm as flouride ion is required to make teeth enamel harder by converting [2Ca₃(PO₄)₂. Ca(OH)₂] to:','[2Ca3(PO4)2.CaF2]','Fluoride ions, when present in small concentrations in drinking water, can be beneficial for dental health as they can promote the remineralization of teeth enamel and make it more resistant to decay. The chemical reaction that occurs when fluoride ions are present in the enamel is as follows: 2Ca3(PO4)2 + Ca(OH)2 + 4F- → 2Ca3(PO4)2.CaF2 + 2H2O In this reaction, the fluoride ions replace some of the hydroxide ions in the hydroxyapatite (Ca3(PO4)2.Ca(OH)2) that makes up tooth enamel.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','CaF₂','CaF₂.Ca(OH)₂','[2Ca₃(PO4)₂.CaF₂]','[2(Ca₃(PO₄)).CaF₂]','NEW','12-197',NULL,NULL),
(214601,'chemistry','A: Ammonia is used to produce various nitrogenous fertilisers.\n\nR: Ammonia is the primary raw material used in the manufacture of nitrogenous fertilisers.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Ammonia is used to produce various nitrogenous fertilisers (ammonium nitrate, urea, ammonium phosphate and ammonium sulphate)," "Ammonia is the primary raw material used in the manufacture of nitrogenous fertilisers\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 172)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(214602,'chemistry','A: Nitric acid can be concentrated to 98% by mass through dehydration with concentrated H2SO4.\n\nR: Aqueous HNO3 can be concentrated by distillation up to ~68% by mass.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The aqueous HNO3 can be concentrated by distillation up to ~68% by mass.Further concentration to 98% can be achieved by dehydration with concentrated H2SO4. (Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 173)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(214603,'chemistry','A: Nitric acid is a strong oxidizing agent.\n\nR: Nitric acid attacks all metals and oxidizes non-metals and their compounds.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Concentrated nitric acid is a strong oxidising agent and attacks most metals except noble metals such as gold and platinum... Concentrated nitric acid also oxidises non–metals and their compounds."(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 174)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-174',NULL,NULL),
(214604,'chemistry','A: Nitric acid exists as a planar molecule in the gaseous state.\n\nR: Nitric acid behaves as a strong acid in aqueous solution.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','In the gaseous state, HNO3 exists as a planar molecule with the structure as shown. In aqueous solution, nitric acid behaves as a strong acid giving hydronium and nitrate ions.(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 174)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-174',NULL,NULL),
(214605,'chemistry','A: The brown ring test is used to detect the presence of nitrate ions in solution.\n\nR: The brown ring test involves the reduction of nitrates to nitric oxide by Fe2+ and the formation of a brown coloured complex with nitric oxide and Fe2+.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The familiar brown ring test for nitrates depends on the ability of Fe2+ to reduce nitrates to nitric oxide, which reacts with Fe2+ to form a brown coloured complex. The test is usually carried out by adding dilute ferrous sulphate solution to an aqueous solution containing nitrate ion, and then carefully adding concentrated sulphuric acid along the sides of the test tube. A brown ring at the interface between the solution and sulphuric acid layers indicates the presence of nitrate ion in solution."(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 175)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-175',NULL,NULL),
(214606,'chemistry','A: Nitric acid is used for the preparation of nitroglycerin, trinitrotoluene and other organic nitro compounds.\n\nR: Conc. Sulphuric acid is used in the pickling of stainless steel.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','The major use of nitric acid is in the manufacture of ammonium nitrate for fertilisers and other nitrates for use in explosives and pyrotechnics. It is also used for the preparation of nitroglycerin, trinitrotoluene and other organic nitro compounds. Other major uses are in the pickling of stainless steel, etching of metals and as an oxidiser in rocket fuels(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 175)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-175',NULL,NULL),
(214607,'chemistry','A: White phosphorus is more reactive than red phosphorus.\n\nR: White phosphorus has angular strain in the P4 molecule','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','White phosphorus is less stable and therefore, more reactive than the other solid phases under normal conditions because of angular strain in the P4 molecule where the angles are only 60°." and "Chemically, red phosphorus is much less reactive than white phosphorus."(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 175)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-175',NULL,NULL),
(214608,'chemistry','A: White phosphorus is soluble in carbon disulphide.\n\nR: Red phosphorus is poisonous and insoluble in water.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','White phosphorus is poisonous, insoluble in water but soluble in carbon disulphide" and "Red phosphorus is odourless, nonpoisonous and insoluble in water as well as in carbon disulphide."(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 175)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-175',NULL,NULL),
(214609,'chemistry','A: White phosphorus dissolves in boiling NaOH solution in an inert atmosphere giving PH3.\n\nR: White phosphorus glows in the dark due to chemiluminescence.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','White phosphorus dissolves in boiling NaOH solution in an inert atmosphere giving PH3." and "It is poisonous, insoluble in water but soluble in carbon disulphide and glows in dark (chemiluminescence)."(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 175)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-175',NULL,NULL),
(214610,'chemistry','A: Black phosphorus does not burn in air up to 673 K.\n\nR: Black phosphorus is polymeric, consisting of chains of P4 tetrahedra linked together','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Black phosphorus does not burn in air up to 673 K. Black phosphorus is polymeric, consisting of chains of P4 tetrahedra linked together in the manner "(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 175)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-175',NULL,NULL),
(214611,'chemistry','A: Phosphine gives phosphonium compounds with acids.\n\nR: Phosphine explodes in contact with traces of oxidizing agents like HNO3, Cl2 and Br2 vapours.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Phosphine is weakly basic and like ammonia, gives phosphonium\ncompounds with acids.\nPhosphine explodes in contact with traces of oxidizing agents like HNO3, Cl2, and Br2 vapours. (Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 176)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-176',NULL,NULL),
(214612,'chemistry','A: In oxoacids, all phosphorus atoms are tetrahedrally surrounded by other atoms.\n\nR: This arrangement is due to the fact that each phosphorus atom forms four covalent bonds with surrounding atoms.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','In oxoacids phosphorus is tetrahedrally surrounded by other atoms.The reason for this tetrahedral arrangement is explained by the statement "each phosphorus atom forms four covalent bonds with surrounding atoms."\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 180)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-180',NULL,NULL),
(214613,'chemistry','A: Hypophosphorous acid is a good reducing agent because it contains P–H bonds.\n\nR: The P–H bonds in hypophosphorous acid ionize to give H+ ions, which act as reducing agents.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','The acids which contain P–H bond have strong reducing properties.\nThus, hypophosphorous acid is a good reducing agent as it contains two P–H bonds and reduces, for example, AgNO3  to metallic silver. \n4 AgNO3 + 2H2O + H3PO2 → 4Ag + 4HNO3 + H3PO4\nThese P–H bonds are not ionisable to give H+  and do not play any role in basicity.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 180)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-180',NULL,NULL),
(214614,'chemistry','A: Oxoacids containing phosphorus with lower oxidation states do not contain both P–P and P–H bonds.\n\nR: This is because these acids contain either P–P or P–H bonds, but not both.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Oxoacids in which phosphorus has lower oxidation state (less than +5) contain, in addition to P=O and P–OH bonds, either P–P (e.g., in H4P2O6) or P–H (e.g., in H3PO2) bonds but not both.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 180)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-180',NULL,NULL),
(214615,'chemistry','A: Sulphates are the primary source of combined sulphur.\n\nR: Organic materials contain traces of sulphur.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Combined sulphur exists primarily as sulphates such as gypsum CaSO4.2H2O, epsom salt MgSO4.7H2O, baryte BaSO4 and sulphides such as galena PbS, zinc blende ZnS, copper pyrites CuFeS2. Organic materials such as eggs, proteins, garlic, onion, mustard, hair and wool contain sulphur."(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 180)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-180',NULL,NULL),
(214616,'chemistry','A: The metallic character of Group 16 elements increases from oxygen to polonium.\n\nR: Oxygen has a higher electronegativity value than polonium.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','"Within the group, electronegativity decreases with an increase in atomic number. This implies that the metallic character increases from oxygen to polonium. Next to fluorine, oxygen has the highest electronegativity value amongst the elements."(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 181)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-181',NULL,NULL),
(214617,'chemistry','A: The reducing property of dioxide decreases from SO2 to TeO2.\n\nR: SO2 is reducing while TeO2 is an oxidising agent.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Reducing property of dioxide decreases from SO2 to TeO2; SO2 is reducing while TeO2 is an oxidising agent."(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 183)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-183',NULL,NULL),
(214618,'chemistry','A: When a slow dry stream of oxygen is passed through a silent electrical discharge, conversion of oxygen to ozone (10%) occurs.\n\nR: The formation of ozone from oxygen is an endothermic process and requires a silent electrical discharge to prevent its decomposition.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','"When a slow dry stream of oxygen is passed through a silent electrical discharge, conversion of oxygen to ozone (10%) occurs." and "Since the formation of ozone from oxygen is an endothermic process, it is necessary to use a silent electrical discharge in its preparation to prevent its decomposition."(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 186)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-186',NULL,NULL),
(214619,'chemistry','A: Ozone acts as a powerful oxidising agent and oxidises lead sulphide to lead sulphate and iodide ions to iodine.\n\nR: Due to the ease with which it liberates atoms of nascent oxygen, it acts as a powerful oxidising agent.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','"Ozone acts as a powerful oxidising agent and oxidises lead sulphide to lead sulphate and iodide ions to iodine." and "Due to the ease with which it liberates atoms of nascent oxygen (O3 → O2 + O), it acts as a powerful oxidising agent."(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 186)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-186',NULL,NULL),
(214620,'chemistry','A: The stable form of sulphur at room temperature is rhombic sulphur.\n\nR: Rhombic sulphur transforms to monoclinic sulphur when heated above 269 K.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','The stable form at room temperature is rhombic sulphur, which transforms to monoclinic sulphur when heated above 369 K." "Conversely, α-sulphur is stable below 369 K and transforms into β-sulphur above this.(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 187)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-187',NULL,NULL),
(214621,'chemistry','A: Sulphur dioxide is a reducing agent in the presence of water.\n\nR: Sulphur dioxide converts iron(II) ions to iron(III) ions','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','When moist, sulphur dioxide behaves as a reducing agent. For example, it converts iron(III) ions to iron(II) ions and decolourises acidified potassium permanganate(VII) solution.(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 189)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-189',NULL,NULL),
(71506,'chemistry','When hydrochloric acid is added to cobalt nitrate solution at room temperature, the following reaction takes place and the reaction mixture becomes blue. On cooling the mixture, it becomes pink. On the basis of this information mark the correct answer.\n[Co(H₂0)₆]²⁺ (aq) + 4Cl⁻(aq)⇌ [CoCl₄]²⁻(aq) + 6H₂0(l)\n(Pink)                                                           (Blue)','∆H > 0 for the reaction','Since the reaction shifts to backward direction on cooling, this means that the backward reaction is exothermic reaction. Therefore, the forward reaction is endothermic reaction and ∆H > 0.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','∆H > 0 for the reaction','∆H < 0 for the reaction','∆H = 0 for the reaction','The sign of ∆H cannot be predicted on the basis of this information.','NEW','11-211','11-187','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/71506q.png'),
(214622,'chemistry','A: Sulphur dioxide is used in the refining of petroleum and sugar.\n\nR: Sulphuric acid, sodium hydrogen sulphite and calcium hydrogen sulphite are manufactured from sulphur dioxide.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Sulphur dioxide is used (i) in refining petroleum and sugar... (iii) as an anti-chlor, disinfectant and preservative. Sulphuric acid, sodium hydrogen sulphite and calcium hydrogen sulphite (industrial chemicals) are manufactured from sulphur dioxide.(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 189)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-189',NULL,NULL),
(214623,'chemistry','A: Sulphur dioxide reacts readily with sodium hydroxide solution.\n\nR: Sulphur dioxide reacts with chlorine in the presence of charcoal to give sulphonyl chloride.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','It reacts readily with sodium hydroxide solution, forming sodium sulphite, which then reacts with more sulphur dioxide to form sodium hydrogen sulphite... Sulphur dioxide reacts with chlorine in the presence of charcoal (which acts as a catalyst) to give sulphuryl chloride, SO2Cl2."(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 189)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-189',NULL,NULL),
(214624,'chemistry','A: Certain forms of terrestrial life contain iodine in their systems.\n\nR: Various seaweeds contain upto 0.5% of iodine.','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','Certain forms of marine life contain iodine in their systems." And it also gives an example, "various seaweeds, for example, contain upto 0.5% of iodine." (Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 192)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-192',NULL,NULL),
(214625,'chemistry','A: The relative oxidising power of halogens can be illustrated by their reactions with water.\n\nR: Fluorine oxidises water to oxygen, whereas chlorine and bromine react with water to form corresponding hydrohalic and hypohalous acids.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The relative oxidising power of halogens can further be illustrated by their reactions with water. Fluorine oxidises water to oxygen whereas chlorine and bromine react with water to form corresponding hydrohalic and hypohalous acids."(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 195)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-195',NULL,NULL),
(214626,'chemistry','A: The acidic strength of hydrohalic acids decreases down the group from HF to HI.\n\nR: The stability of hydrogen halides decreases down the group  in the order H-F > H-Cl > H-Br > H-I.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The acidic strength of these acids varies in the order: HF < HCl < HBr < HI. The stability of these halides decreases down the group due to decrease in bond (H–X) dissociation enthalpy in the order: H–F > H–Cl > H–Br > H–I."(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 195)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-195',NULL,NULL),
(214627,'chemistry','A: O2F2 oxidises plutonium to PuF6 and the reaction is used in removing plutonium as PuF6 from spent nuclear fuel.\n\nR: O2F2 is a strong fluorinating agent because of the higher electronegativity of fluorine than oxygen.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','O2F2 oxidises plutonium to PuF6 and the reaction is used in removing plutonium as PuF6 from spent nuclear fuel" and "Both are strong fluorinating agents. O2F2 oxidises plutonium to PuF6 and the reaction is used in removing plutonium as PuF6 from spent nuclear fuel. are essentially oxygen fluorides because of the higher electronegativity of fluorine than oxygen.(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 196)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-196',NULL,NULL),
(214628,'chemistry','A: Interhalogen compounds are more reactive than halogens (except fluorine).\n\nR: This is because X-X\' bond in interhalogens is weaker than X-X bond in halogens except F-F bond.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','"In general, interhalogen compounds are more reactive than halogens (except fluorine). This is because X–X′ bond in interhalogens is weaker than X–X bond in halogens except F–F bond." (Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 201)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-201',NULL,NULL),
(71507,'chemistry','The pH of neutral water at 25°C is 7.0. As the temperature increases, ionization of water increases, however, the concentration of H⁺ ions and OH⁻ ions are equal. What will be the pH of pure water at 60°C?','Less than 7.0','At 25°C, [H⁺] = [OH⁻] = 10⁷ and Kw = [H⁺] [OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴. On heating, Kw increases, i.e., [H⁺] [OH⁻] > 10⁻¹⁴ .As [H⁺] = [OH⁻], [H⁺]² > 10⁻¹⁴ or [H⁺] > 10⁻⁷ M or pH < 7.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','Equal to 7.0','Greater than 7.0','Less than 7.0','Equal to zero','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(214629,'chemistry','A: Helium is used to produce and sustain powerful superconducting magnets.\n\nR: Helium has a low boiling point and is used as a cryogenic agent for carrying out various experiments at low temperatures.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','"Liquid helium (b.p. 4.2 K) finds use as a cryogenic agent for carrying out various experiments at low temperatures. It is used to produce and sustain powerful superconducting magnets which form an essential part of modern NMR spectrometers and Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) systems for clinical diagnosis."(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page no. 205)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct statements, but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but reason is wrong statement','Assertion is wrong but reason is correct statement','NEW','12-205',NULL,NULL),
(214630,'chemistry','If chlorine gas is passed through hot NaOH solution, two changes are observed\nin the oxidation number of chlorine during the reaction. These are ________\nand _________.\n(i) 0 to +5\n\n(ii) 0 to +3\n\n(iii) 0 to –1\n\n(iv) 0 to +1','i','0 to +5','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i','ii,iii','iii,iv','iv,i','NEW','12-203',NULL,NULL),
(214631,'chemistry','Which of the following options are not in accordance with the property\nmentioned against them?\n(i) F2 > Cl2 > Br2 > I2                                         Oxidising power.\n\n(ii) MI > MBr > MCl > MF                                   Ionic character of metal halide.\n\n(iii) F2 > Cl2 > Br2 > I2                                       Bond dissociation enthalpy.\n\n(iv) HI < HBr < HCl < HF                                     Hydrogen-halogen bond strength.','ii,iii','Bond dissociation enthalpy order is Cl2 > Br2 > F2 > I2\n\nIonic character of metal halides increases as electronegativity of halogen increases\n\nMI < MBr < MCI < MF','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','i,ii','ii,iii','iii,iv','iv,i','NEW','12-201',NULL,NULL),
(214632,'chemistry','Which of the following is correct for P4 molecule of white phosphorus?\n(i) It has 6 lone pairs of electrons.\n\n(ii) It has six P–P single bonds.\n\n(iii) It has three P–P single bonds.\n\n(iv) It has four lone pairs of electrons.','ii,iv','P4molecule has total four lone pairs of electrons situated at each P-atom.\n\nIt has six P – P single bond.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i,ii','ii,iii','ii,iv','iv,i','NEW','12-181',NULL,NULL),
(214633,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Among halogens, radius ratio between iodine and fluorine is maximum.\n\n(ii) Leaving F—F bond, all halogens have weaker X—X bond than X—X\' bond in interhalogens.\n\n(iii) Among interhalogen compounds maximum number of atoms are present in iodine fluoride.\n\n(iv) Interhalogen compounds are more reactive than halogen compounds','iv,i,iii','Among halogens radius ratio between iodine and fluorine is maximum\n\nbecause iodine has maximum radius while fluorine has minimum radius. \nAlso, due to highest radius ratio maximum number of atoms are present in\n iodine fluoride.\n\nInterhalogen compounds are more reactive than halogen compounds because A\n – B bond of dissimilar halogen is weaker than A – A or B – B bond of \nhalogens.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','i,ii,iii','ii,iii,iv','ii,iv,iii','iv,i,iii','NEW','12-207',NULL,NULL),
(214634,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct for SO2 gas?\n(i) It acts as bleaching agent in moist conditions.\n\n(ii) It’s molecule has linear geometry.\n\n(iii) It’s dilute solution is used as disinfectant.\n\n(iv) It can be prepared by the reaction of dilute H2SO4 with metal sulphide.','i,iii','SO2 acts as a bleaching agent in presence of moisture. This is due to reducing nature of SO2\n\nSO2 + 2H2O ->• H2SO4 + 2H\n\nColoured matter + H –> colourless matter. Its dilute solution is used as disinfectant.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','i,iii','ii,iii','ii,iv','iv,i','NEW','12-194',NULL,NULL),
(214635,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\n(i) All the three N—O bond lengths in HNO3 are equal.\n\n(ii) All P—Cl bond lengths in PCl5 molecule in gaseous state are equal.\n\n(iii) P4 molecule in white phohsphorus have angular strain therefore white phosphorus is very reactive.\n\n(iv) PCl is ionic in solid state in which cation is tetrahedral and anion is octahedral.','iii,iv','All the three N – O bond lengths in HNO3 are not equal.\n\n(b) All P – Cl bond lengths in PCl5 molecule in gaseous state are not equal. Axial bond is longer than equatorial bond.\n\n(c) P4 molecule in white phosphorus have angular strain therefore white phosphorus is very reactive.\n\n(d) PCl5 is ionic in solid state in which cation is tetrahedral and anion is octahedral.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','i,ii','ii,iii','iii,iv','iv,i','NEW','12-183',NULL,NULL),
(214636,'chemistry','Which of the following orders are correct as per the properties mentioned against\neach?\n(i) As2O3 < SiO2 < P2O3 < SO2                     Acid strength.\n\n(ii) AsH3 < PH3 < NH3                                    Enthalpy of vapourisation.\n\n(iii) S < O < Cl < F                                             More negative electron gain enthalpy.\n\n(iv) H2O > H2S > H2Se > H2Te                      Thermal stability.','iv,i','Acidic strength of oxides in a group decreases down the group and increases along a period from left to right.\n\nThermal stability of hydrides of group 16 decreases down the group.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i,ii','ii,iii','iii,iv','iv,i','NEW','12-188',NULL,NULL),
(214637,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\n(i) S–S bond is present in H2S2O6\n.\n(ii) In peroxosulphuric acid (H2SO5) sulphur is in +6 oxidation state.\n\n(iii) Iron powder along with Al2O3 and K2O is used as a catalyst in the preparation of NH3 by Haber’s process.\n\n(iv) Change in enthalpy is positive for the preparation of SO3 by catalytic oxidation of SO2.','i,ii','S–S bond is present in H2S2O6\nIn peroxosulphuric acid (H2SO5) sulphur is in +6 oxidation state','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','i,ii','ii,iii','iii,iv','iv,i','NEW','12-194',NULL,NULL),
(214638,'chemistry','In which of the following reactions conc. H2SO4 is used as an oxidising reagent?\n(i) CaF2 + H2SO4 → CaSO4 + 2HF\n\n(ii) 2HI + H2SO4 → I2 + SO2 + 2H2O\n\n(iii) Cu + 2H2SO4 → CuSO4 + SO2 + 2H2O\n\n(iv) NaCl + H2SO4 → NaHSO4 + HCl','ii,iii','In the above given four reactions, (b) and (c) represent oxidizing behaviour of H2S04.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','i,ii','ii,iii','iii,iv','iv,i','NEW','12-196',NULL,NULL),
(214639,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true?\n(i) Only type of interactions between particles of noble gases are due to weak dispersion forces.\n\n(ii) Ionisation enthalpy of molecular oxygen is very close to that of xenon.\n\n(iii) Hydrolysis of XeF6 is a redox reaction.\n\n(iv) Xenon fluorides are not reactive.','i,ii','Weak dispersion forces are present between particles of noble gases. \nIonization enthalpy of molecular oxygen is very close to that of xenon.','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i,ii','ii,iii','iii,iv','iv,i','NEW','12-210',NULL,NULL),
