(201110,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\n\n\nA: Metals out of all elements,had a special part in antiquity . \n \nR: Different periods of human civilisation have been named after non-metals.','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','The history of civilisation is linked to the use of metals in antiquity in many ways. Different periods of early human civilisations have been named after metals. The skill of extraction of metals gave many metals and brought about several changes in the human society. It gave weapons, tools, ornaments, utensils, etc., and enriched the cultural life.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12,unit 6 , page 149 , paragraph first)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201111,'chemistry','Which metal was FIRST found in India? \n \n a)iron \n \n b)gold \n \n c)silver \n \n d)copper','copper','The first evidence of metal in Indian subcontinent comes from Mehrgarh in Baluchistan, where a small copper bead, dated to about 6000 BCE was found.\n(Reference : NCERT class 12, unit 6,page 149 paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','iron','gold','silver','copper','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201112,'chemistry','Chalcopyrite  \n \n a)is an ore of Cu \n \n b)is the type of copper \n \n c)oxidizes to malachite\n \n d)an ore of Fe','a,c','The ore for extraction of metal for making the objects was obtained from chalcopyrite ore deposits in Aravalli Hills. Collection of archaeological texts from copper-plates and rock-inscriptions have been compiled and published by the Archaeological Survey of India during the past century.\n(Reference : NCERT class 12,unit 6, page 150 , paragraph1)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201113,'chemistry','Where were silver and gold first found? \n \n a)Aravali Hills \n \n b)Harappaus \n \n c)Mehrgarh in Baluchistan \n \n d)river Sindhu','a,d','Harappans also used gold and silver, as well as their joint alloy electrum. Variety of ornaments such as pendants, bangles, beads and rings have been found in ceramic or bronze pots. Early gold and silver ornaments have been found from Indus Valley sites such as Mohenjodaro (3000 BCE).The river Sindhu was an important source of gold in ancient times. It is interesting that the availability of alluvial placer gold in the river Sindhu has been reported in modern times also.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12, unit 6,page 150 paragraph 2 and 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,c','a,b','a,d','b,c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201114,'chemistry','What feature of gold was driving force for the development of gold refining? \n \n a)colour \n \n b)solidity \n \n c)amount of impurity \n \n d)flexibility','a,c','The native gold has different colours depending upon the nature and amount of impurity present in it. It is likely that the different colours of native gold were a major driving force for the development of gold refining.\n(Reference : NCERT class 12,unit 6,page 150, paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','d,b','a,c','b,c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201115,'chemistry','When  was the first radioactive isotope found \n \n a)1800-1000 BCE \n \n b) 100-1200 BCE\n \n c)1800-1900 BCE \n \n d) 200-1500BCE','a only','Radiocarbon dating places them between BCE 1800 and 1000. The results of excavation indicate that the knowledge of iron smelting and manufacturing of iron artefacts was well known in Eastern Vindhyas and it was in use in the Central Ganga Plains, at least from the early 2nd millennium BCE.\n(Reference : NCERT class 12, unit 6,page 150 paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a only','a,b','d,b','d,a','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201116,'chemistry','In Which answer are the elements lined up by the percentige of presence in wrought iron?\n \n a)C,Si,Mn\n \n b)P,Ni,Cu\n \n c)Si,Mn,C\n \n d)Ni,Cu,P','a,b','The average composition (weight%) of the components present in the wrought iron of the pillar, besides iron, are 0.15% C, 0.05% Si, 0.05% Mn, 0.25% P, 0.005% Ni, 0.03% Cu and 0.02% N.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12,unit 6,page 151,paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','d,a','b,d','a,c','a,b','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201117,'chemistry','The elements mixed together during manufacture of steel by crucible process are:\n \n a)iron\n \n b)charcoal  \n \n c)gold\n \n d) glass','a,b,d','Iron smelting and the use of iron was especially established in South Indian megalithic cultures. The forging of wrought iron seems to have been at peak in India in the Ist millennium CE. Greek accounts report the manufacture of steel in India by crucible process. In this process, iron, charcoal and glass were mixed together in a crucible and heated until the iron melted and absorbed the carbon.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12,unit 6,page 151,paragraoh 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b,d','b,c,a','c,b,d','a,c,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201118,'chemistry','Which statemant is true \n \n a)Radiocarbon dating of charcoal from iron slag revealed evidence of continuous smelting in Khasi Hills of Meghalaya.\n \n b)India was the first country to master copper distillation.\n \n c)The remnants of former iron-ore excavation and iron manufacturing are visible even now in the landscape of Khasi Hills.\n \n d)The slag layer, which is dated to 353 BCE – CE 128, indicates that Khasi Hill region is the earliest iron smelting site studied in the entire region of North East India.','a,c,d','Radiocarbon dating of charcoal from iron slag revealed evidence of continuous smelting in Khasi Hills of Meghalaya. The slag layer, which is dated to 353 BCE – CE 128, indicates that Khasi Hill region is the earliest iron smelting site studied in the entire region of North East India. The remnants of former iron-ore excavation and iron manufacturing are visible even now in the landscape of Khasi Hills. British naturalists who visited Meghalaya in early 19th century described the iron industry that had developed in the upper part of the Khasi Hills. There is archaeological evidence of zinc production in Rajasthan mines at Zawar from the 6th or 5th BCE. India was the first country to master zinc distillation.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12,unit 6,page 151 paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,c,d','b,c,a','c,b,d','a,b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201119,'chemistry','Which statemant is true about iron bolcks?\n \n a)from iron blocks we can get wrought iron \n \n b)they are formed when iron ore is reduced to solid state\n \n c)they are useful because there is no need for purification,they are clean product \n \n d)When iron ore smelted in liquid state and cooled down again, porous free\niron blocks are formed.','a,b','When iron ore is reduced in solid state by using charcoal, porous iron blocks are formed. Therefore, reduced iron blocks are also called sponge iron blocks. Any useful product can be obtained from this material only after removing the porosity by hot forging. The iron so obtained is termed as wrought iron.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12, unit 6,page 151 , paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,d','c,b','a,b','b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201120,'chemistry','Downward distillation technique was used in producing: \n \n a)iron \n \n b)zinc \n \n c)mercury \n \n d)copper','b,c','There is archaeological evidence of zinc production in Rajasthan mines at Zawar from the 6th or 5th BCE. India was the first country to master zinc distillation. Due to low boiling point, zinc tends to vapourise while its ore is smelted. Pure zinc could be produced after a sophisticated ‘downward’ distillation technique in which the vapour was condensed in a lower container. This technique was also applied to mercury. Indian metallurgists were masters in this technique. This has been described in Sanskrit texts of 14th century.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12, unit 6,page 151,last paragraph)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,d','b,c','a,b','b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201121,'chemistry','Aluminium is a major component of\n \n a)Mica \n \n b)clay\n \n c)charcoal\n \n d)saphires','a,b,d','Among metals, aluminium is the most abundant. In fact, it is the third most abundant element in earth’s crust (8.3% approx. by weight). It is a major component of many igneous minerals including mica and clays.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12,unit 6,page 152 paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b,d','d,c,a','d,c,b','a,b,c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201122,'chemistry','The main impurity in spahires is: \n \n a)Cu\n \n b)Co \n \n c)Al\n \n d)Cl','b only','Gems ‘ruby’ and ‘sapphire’ have Cr and Co respectively as impurity.\n\n\n(Reference: NCERT class 12,unit 6, page 152,paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b,d','d,c,a','a only','b only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201123,'chemistry','Choose the answers where the elements are lined up by abundance in earth\'s crust.(from most to least abundant) \n \n a)Oxygen,Aluminium,Hydrogen \n \n b)Hydrogen,Oxygen,Iron \n \n c)Oxygen,Hydrogen,Iron,\n \n d)Oxygen,Aluminium,Iron,','a,d','O=46%,Al=8%,Fe=5%,H=01%\n(Reference : NCERT class 12,unit 6,page 152,paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','b,c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201124,'chemistry','There are many important procedures for concentration of ores. They are used depending on:\n\na)Physical properties of ores and gangue\n\nb)quantity of impurities in ores\n\nc)types of the chemical bonds in ores\n\nd)difference between specific gravities of the ore and\nthe gangue particles.','a,d','Removal of the unwanted materials (e.g., sand, clays, etc.) from the ore is known as concentration, dressing or benefaction.Concentration of ores involves several steps and selection of these steps depends upon the differences in physical properties of the compound of the metal present and that of the gangue.\n(Reference : NCERT class 12 unit 6 page 153 paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','b,c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201125,'chemistry','Which statemants about Hydraulic Washing is true\n \n a) it\'s based on differences in specific gravities of gangue particals and ore particles \n \n b)it\'s based differences in magnetic properties of gangue and ore \n \n c)in this process an upward stream of running water is used to wash the powdered ore.\n \n d)the process was invented by washerwoman','a,c','Hydraulic Washing is based on the difference between specific gravities of the ore and\nthe gangue particles.\n(Reference : NCERT class 12 unit 6 page 153 paragraph 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','b,c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201126,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\n\n\nA: Magnetic separation uses differences in magnetic properties of the ore components. \n \nR:The component that is more attracted by the magnetic roler remains attracted towatrds the belt and falls closer to it.','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','This is based on differences in magnetic properties of the ore components. If either the ore or the gangue is attracted towards magnetic field, then the separation is carried out by this method. For example iron ores are attracted towards magnet, hence, non–magnetic impurities can be separted from them using magnetic separation. The powdered ore is dropped over a conveyer belt which moves over a magnetic roller \nMagnetic substance remains attracted towards the belt and falls close to it.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12,unit 6, page 153paragraph last)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201127,'chemistry','What enhances non-wettability of the mineral particles in froth floatation method? \n \n a)collectors \n \n b)froth stabilisers \n \n c)fatty acids and pine oils\n \n d)depressants','a,c','Collectors (e.g., pine oils, fatty acids, xanthates, etc.) enhance non-\nwettability of the mineral particles and froth stabilisers (e.g., cresols, aniline) stabilise the froth.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12, unit 6, page 154 paragraph first)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,d','a,c','c,b','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201128,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\n\n\nA: Froth floatation process is concentration procedure that is based on keeping the minerals in the upper foam and separate tham from the ore. \n \nR: It was invented by washerman.','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','One can do wonders if he or she has a scientific temperament and is attentive to observations. A washerwoman had an innovative mind too. While washing a miner’s overalls, she noticed that sand and similar dirt fell to the bottom of the washtub. What was peculiar, the copper bearing compounds that had come to the clothes from the mines, were caught in the soapsuds and so they came to the top. One of her clients, Mrs. Carrie Everson was a chemist. The washerwoman told her experience to Mrs. Everson. The latter thought that the idea could be used for separating copper compounds from rocky and earth materials on large scale. This way an invention came up. At that time only those ores were used for extraction of copper, which contained large amounts of the metal. Invention of the Froth Floatation Method made copper mining profitable even from the low- grade ores. World production of copper soared and the metal became cheaper.  \n(Reference : NCERT class 12 unit 6 page 154 paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201129,'chemistry','Which statemants are true about process of concentration of Bauxite ?\n\n a)Concentration is carried out by heating the powdered ore\n \n b)Bauxite contains  copper as impurities\n \n c)Concentration of powdered ore is done with a concentrated solution of NaOH\n \n d)it\'s done on 573 – 623 K and 45 – 46 bar pressure.','a,c','Bauxite is the principal ore of aluminium. It usually contains SiO2,iron oxides and titanium oxide (TiO2) as impurities. Concentration is carried out by heating the powdered ore with a concentrated solution of NaOH at 473 – 523 K and 35 – 36 bar pressure. This process is called digestion.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12,unit 6, page 154 paragraph last)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','b,d','c,b','a,c','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201130,'chemistry','In leaching  Al2O3 and 2Na[Al(OH)4] are  in the form of \n \n a)solid,aqueous\n \n b)solid,molten\n \n c)solid,solid\n \n d)aquueous,solid','a','Al2O3(s) + 2NaOH(aq) + 3H2O(l) → 2Na[Al(OH)4](aq)\n(Reference  NCERT class 12 unit 6 page 154 paragraph last)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201131,'chemistry','Line up the steps in Leaching of alumina from bauxite\n \n a)SiO2 disolves forming sodium silicate \n \n b)seeding Al2O3 to the solution \n \n c)sodium silicate remains in the solution and hydrated alumina is heated \n \n d)heating the powered ore with concentrated solution','d,a,b,c','This way, Al2O3 is extracted out as sodium aluminate.The impurity, SiO2 too dissolves forming sodium silicate. Other impurities are left behind.\nAl2O3(s) + 2NaOH(aq) + 3H2O(l) → 2Na[Al(OH)4](aq)\nThe sodium aluminate present in solution is neutralised by passing CO2 gas and hydrated Al2O3 is precipitated. At this stage, small amount of freshly prepared sample of hydrated Al2O3 is added to the solution. This is called seeding. It induces the precipitation.\n2Na[Al(OH)4](aq) + 2CO2(g) → Al2O3.xH2O(s) + 2NaHCO3(aq) \nSodium silicate remains in the solution and hydrated alumina is filtered, dried and heated to give back pure Al2O3.\nAl2O3.xH2O(s) 1470 K Al2O3(s) + xH2O(g)\n(Reference NCERT class 12 unit 6 page 155 paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','d,a,c,b','b,c,a,d','d,a,b,c','c,a,b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201132,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is correct?\n \na.  In the metallurgy of Ag ,metal is leached with a solution of NaCN in the presence of air\n \nb. In the metallurgy of Au ,metal is leached with a solution of KCN in the presence of air \n\nc. In the metallurgy of Au ,metal is leached with a solution of NaOH in the presence of air \n\nd. In the metallurgy of silver ,metal is leached with a solution of KCN in the presence of O2','a,b,d','In the metallurgy of silver and gold, the respective metal is leached with a dilute solution of NaCN or KCN in the presence of air, which supplies O2.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 unit 6 page 155 paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b,d','a,c,d','b,c,a','b,d,c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201133,'chemistry','Find a and b:\n \n2PbS + a → 2PbO + b. \n \n a) a is O2, b is SO2\n \n b) a is O2, b is SO3\n \n c)a is SO2, b is SO3\n \n d)a is SO3, b is SO2','a','2PbS + 3O2 → 2PbO + 2SO2\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 unit 6 page 156 paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','a,b','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201134,'chemistry','For reaction to proceed ∆S should be: \n \n a)∆S<0 \n \n b)∆S>0 \n \n c)∆H > T∆S.\n \n d)∆H < T∆S.','b,d','When the value of ∆G is negative in equation 6.14, only then the reaction will proceed. ∆G can become negative in the following situations:\nIf ∆S is positive, on increasing the temperature (T), the value of T∆S increases so that ∆H< T∆S. In this situation ∆G will become negative on increasing temperature.\n(Reference : NCERT class 12 unit 6 page 156 paragraph last)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','a,c','c,d','b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201135,'chemistry','On the abscisse and ordinate  on Ellingham Diagram are:\n \n a) ∆G ,∆H \n \n b) ∆H ,T\n \n c) ∆G, T\n \n d) ∆S,∆H','c','The graphical representation of Gibbs energy was first used by H.J.T. Ellingham. This provides a sound basis for considering the choice of reducing agent in the reduction of oxides. This is known as Ellingham Diagram. Such diagrams help us in predicting the feasibility of thermal reduction of an ore.\n(Reference NCERT class 12 unit 6, page 157 fig 6,4)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201136,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\n\n\nA: In Blast furnace, in the upper part the temperature is lower and the Fe oxides coming from top are reduced to FeO \n \nR: The burning of Coke suplies most of the heat','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','A blast of hot airis blown from the bottom of the furnace by burning coke in the lower portion to give temperature upto about 2200K. The burning of coke, therefore, supplies most of the heat required in the process. The CO and heat move to the upper part of the furnace. In upper part, the temperature is lower and the iron oxides (Fe2O3 and Fe3O4) coming from the top are reduced in steps to FeO.\n(Reference NCERT clas 12 unit 6, page 160 paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201137,'chemistry','The Iron obtained in blast furnace : \n \n a) is called pig iron \n \n b) is purest form of iron \n \n c) is called wrought iron iron \n \n d) has many impurities','a,d','The iron obtained from Blast furnace contains about 4% carbon and many impurities in smaller amount (e.g., S, P, Si, Mn). This is known as pig iron.\n(Reference NCERT class 12 unit 6, page 161 paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,d','b,c','a,b','c,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201138,'chemistry','In hydrometallurgy Copper is treated with: \n \n a) acid \n \n b) bacteria\n \n c) Scrap iron \n \n d) heat','a,b','Copper is extracted by hydrometallurgy from low grade ores. It is leached out using acid or bacteria.\n(Reference NCERT class 12 unit 6, page 163 paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,d','b,c','a,b','c,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201139,'chemistry','In this reaction 2[Au(CN)2](aq) + Zn(s) → 2Au(s) + [Zn(CN)4]2(aq) what is the oxidising agent\n \n a) Zinc \n \n b) Au \n \n c) 2Au(CN)2](aq) \n \n d) Zn(CN)4]2(aq)','c only','In this reaction zinc acts as a reducing agent, 2Au(CN)2](aq) acts as oxidising agent\n(Reference NCERT class 12 unit 6, page 164 paragraph first)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','c only','a,c','c,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201140,'chemistry','Brass contain: \n \n  \n \n a) More Cu than Zn \n \n b) More Zn than Cu \n \n c) less than 50% Zn\n \n d) More than 50% Cu','a,c','Brass, (Cu 60%, Zn40%)\n(Reference NCERT class 12 unit 6, page 166 paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,d','b,c','a,c','c,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201141,'chemistry','Select the correct statement:\n\na. Zinc is used as coinage alloy\n\nb. Cu mixed with tin is tougher than the Cu itself\n\nc. Copper is used in water and steam pipes\n\nd. German Silver does not contains Ni','b,c','Copper is used for making wires used in electrical industry and for water and steam pipes. It is also used in several alloys that are rather tougher than the metal itself, e.g., brass (with zinc), bronze (with tin) and coinage alloy (with nickel).\n\ngerman silver (Cu 25-30%, Zn 25-30%, Ni 40–50%).\n\n(Reference NCERT class 12 unit 6, page 166 paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,d','b,c','a,c','c,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201142,'chemistry','German silver has: \n \n a)more Cu that Ni \n \n b)more Ni than Cu \n \n c)Tha same amaunts of Cu and Zn  \n \n d)More Cu than Zn','a,c','german silver (Cu 25-30%, Zn 25-30%, Ni 40–50%).\n(Reference : NCERT class 12 unit 6 page 166 paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,d','b,c','a,c','c,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201143,'chemistry','Which steel is used for making cutting tools and which for making cables:\n \n a)Chrome steel is used for cutting tools\n \n b)Alloy steel is used in cycles\n \n c) Stainless steels are used in crushing machines\n \n d)Chrome steel is used for crushing machines','a,d','Chrome steel is used for cutting tools and crushing machines, and stainless steel is used for cycles, automobiles, utensils, pens, etc.\n(Refferences: NCERT class 12 unit 6 page 166 paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','a,d','a,b','b,c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201144,'chemistry','When we say that it may be purified by fractional distillation , and that in combination with sulphur gives Sphalerite we are talking about which element and its ores are: \n \n a)Calamine\n \n b)Nickel \n \n c)Zinc \n \n d)Magnetite','a,c','Zinc blende or Sphalerite, ZnS, The metal may be purified by fractional distillation.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 unit 6 page 167 paragraph last)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,c','a,d','a,b','b,c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201222,'chemistry','In which of the following method of purification, metal is converted to its volatile compound which is decomposed to give pure metal?\n\n(a) Heating with stream of carbon monoxide\n(b) Heating with iodine\n(c) Liquation\n(d) Distillation','a,b','The method of purification in which a metal is converted into its volatile compound, which is then decomposed to obtain pure metal is known as the "vapour phase refining" method. This method is based on the principle that the metal is converted into a volatile compound, which can be purified by distillation and then decomposed to get pure metal.\n\nOut of the given options, option (a) "heating with a stream of carbon monoxide" is an example of vapour phase refining method. This method is used for refining metals such as nickel by the Mond process. In this process, nickel is heated with a stream of carbon monoxide to form volatile nickel carbonyl (Ni(CO)4), which is then decomposed at a higher temperature to obtain pure nickel.\n\nOption (b) "heating with iodine" is an example of the van Arkel method, which is used for refining metals such as zirconium and titanium. In this method, the metal is heated with iodine to form a volatile metal iodide, which is then decomposed to obtain pure metal.\n\nOption (c) "liquation" is a method used for the purification of metals such as tin and lead, where the impurities are removed by selective melting and solidification.\n\nOption (d) "distillation" is a general method used for the purification of liquids or volatile substances, and it is not specific to metal purification.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 165 , paragraph last)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201223,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\n\n(a) A depressant prevents certain type of particle to come to the froth.\n\n(b) Copper matte contains Cu2S and ZnS.\n\n(c) The solidified copper obtained from reverberatory furnace has blistered appearance due to evolution of SO2 during the extraction.\n\n(d) Zinc can be extracted by self-reduction.','a,c','Statement (a) is true. A depressant is a reagent that is added during froth floatation to prevent certain types of particles from coming to the froth. For example, in the separation of lead sulfide (PbS) and zinc sulfide (ZnS), sodium cyanide (NaCN) is added as a depressant to prevent ZnS from coming to the froth.\n\nStatement (b) is false. Copper matte is a mixture of copper sulfide (Cu2S) and iron sulfide (FeS), but it does not contain zinc sulfide (ZnS).\n\nStatement (c) is true. During the extraction of copper from its sulfide ore, the solidified copper obtained from the reverberatory furnace has a blistered appearance due to the evolution of sulfur dioxide (SO2) gas. This gas is formed as a result of the reaction between copper sulfide and oxygen.\n\nStatement (d) is false. Zinc cannot be extracted by self-reduction. Zinc is extracted from its ore, zinc blende (ZnS), by roasting the ore in the presence of excess air to form zinc oxide (ZnO). The zinc oxide is then reduced to zinc metal using carbon. The overall reaction can be represented as follows:\n\nZnS + 2O2 → 2ZnO + 2SO2\nZnO + C → Zn + CO2','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201224,'chemistry','In the extraction of chlorine from brine ............\n\n(a) ΔG for the overall reaction is negative.\n(b) ΔG for the overall reaction is positive.\n(c) ΔE for the overall reaction has negative value.\n(d) ΔE for the overall reaction has positive value.','b,c','The extraction of chlorine from brine involves electrolysis, where an electric current is passed through a brine solution (sodium chloride dissolved in water) to separate the sodium and chlorine ions. The overall reaction is as follows:\n\n2NaCl(aq) + 2H2O(l) → 2NaOH(aq) + H2(g) + Cl2(g)\n\nThe standard free energy change (ΔG°) for this reaction is positive, which means the reaction is non-spontaneous under standard conditions. However, the reaction can be made to occur by supplying electrical energy to the system. This also means that the standard potential (E°) of the reaction is negative, since ΔG° = -nFE°, where n is the number of electrons transferred and F is the Faraday constant.\n\nSo the correct answers are (b) ΔG for the overall reaction is positive and (c) ΔE for the overall reaction has negative value.','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201225,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Nickel can be purified by Mond’s process.\nReason (R): Ni (CO)4 is a volatile compound which decomposes at 460 K to give pure Ni.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Mond\'s process is a method for purifying nickel by converting it into a volatile complex, nickel tetracarbonyl (Ni(CO)4), which can then be decomposed to obtain pure nickel. The process involves the following steps:\n\nImpure nickel is heated with carbon monoxide gas to form nickel carbonyl (Ni(CO)4).\nThe nickel carbonyl is then decomposed at high temperatures (~460 K) to release pure nickel metal and carbon monoxide gas.\nThe assertion (A) is true because nickel can indeed be purified by Mond\'s process. The reason (R) is also true because Ni(CO)4 is a volatile compound that decomposes at ~460 K to give pure nickel metal and carbon monoxide gas. Therefore, reason (R) is a correct explanation of assertion (A).','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201226,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Zirconium can be purified by van Arkel method.\n\nReason (R): ZrI4 is volatile and decomposes at 1800K.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','The van Arkel method is a process used to purify certain metals, including zirconium. The method involves the formation and decomposition of a volatile metal iodide compound, which allows for the separation of impurities from the desired metal. The steps involved in the van Arkel method for zirconium purification are as follows:\n\nZirconium is heated with iodine to form zirconium tetraiodide (ZrI4).\nZirconium tetraiodide is heated to a high temperature (~1800 K) under reduced pressure, causing it to decompose into pure zirconium metal and iodine gas.\nThe zirconium metal is then collected and purified further by remelting and casting into ingots.\nThe assertion (A) is true because zirconium can indeed be purified by the van Arkel method. The reason (R) is also true because ZrI4 is a volatile compound that decomposes at a high temperature of ~1800 K, which allows for the extraction of pure zirconium metal. Therefore, reason (R) is a correct explanation of assertion (A).\n\nSo the correct answer is: Both assertion and reason are true, and reason is a correct explanation of assertion.','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201227,'chemistry','Assertion (A): Sulphide ores are concentrated by froth flotation method.\n\nReason (R): Cresols stabilise the froth in froth floatation method.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Sulphide ores are concentrated by the froth flotation method, which involves preferential wetting of the ore particles by oil and gangue particles by water. The froth stabilizer used in this method is usually cresols, which help to stabilize the froth and ensure that the ore particles are carried to the surface.\n\nThe assertion (A) is true because sulphide ores can indeed be concentrated by the froth flotation method. The reason (R) is also true because cresols are commonly used to stabilize the froth in this method. \n\nThe main reason for the extraction of metal using froth flotation is the formation of a stable froth, which carries the metal ore particles to the surface. The ore particles are then separated from the froth and further processed to extract the metal.','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201228,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Zone refining method is very useful for producing semiconductors.\n\nReason (R) Semiconductors are of high purity.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Zone refining is  a very useful method for producing high-purity semiconductors. In this method, a small region of a rod-shaped material, typically a metal or semiconductor, is melted using an external heat source. The molten zone is then moved along the rod, either by pulling the rod through a stationary heater or by moving the heater along the rod.\n\nThe impurities in the material are preferentially distributed in the molten zone, and as the zone moves along the rod, these impurities are removed from the region of interest. The solidified zone that is left behind is of higher purity than the original material, and this process can be repeated several times to achieve very high levels of purity.\n\nThe assertion (A) is true because zone refining is indeed a very useful method for producing semiconductors of high purity. The reason (R) is also true because semiconductors do indeed require high levels of purity to function effectively. \n\nThe main reason why zone refining is useful for producing high-purity semiconductors is that this method allows for precise control over the distribution of impurities in the material. This, in turn, allows for the removal of impurities from the material and the production of a highly pure final product.','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201416,'chemistry','A: Sodium aluminate is formed by heating bauxite with a concentrated solution of NaOH.\n\nR: Al2O3 is extracted out as sodium aluminate.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','in the leaching process, bauxite is heated with a concentrated solution of NaOH at 473 – 523 K and 35 – 36 bar pressure. This process is called digestion. Sodium aluminate is formed as a result of this process, and Al2O3 is extracted out as sodium aluminate.','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201417,'chemistry','A: Sulphide ores are usually converted to oxide before reduction.\n\nR: Oxides are easier to reduce than sulphide ores.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','To extract metal from concentrated ore, it must be converted to a form which is suitable for reduction to metal. Usually sulphide ores are converted to oxide before reduction because oxides are easier to reduce." (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 155)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201418,'chemistry','A: Ellingham diagram is used to predict the feasibility of the reduction of metal oxides.\n\nR: The temperature at which the slope of the Ellingham diagram changes indicates the melting point of the metal.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Ellingham diagram is a thermodynamic tool that helps in predicting the feasibility of reduction reactions involving metal oxides. \n"Each plot is a straight line and slopes upwards except when some change in phase (s→ l or l→ g) takes place. The temperature at which such change occurs, is indicated by an increase in the slope on positive side."\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 158)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201419,'chemistry','A: After concentration, mixture of oxide ores of iron is subjected to calcination/roasting to remove water, to decompose carbonates and to oxidise sulphides.\n\nR: Calcination/roasting is done to remove impurities from the oxide ores of iron before they are fed into a Blast furnace for reduction.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','the process of calcination/roasting which is done to remove water, carbonates, and oxidize sulphides from the mixture of oxide ores of iron. \n\nThe Reason statement is also correct as it explains the reason why calcination/roasting is done. It states that calcination/roasting is done to remove impurities from the oxide ores of iron before they are fed into a Blast furnace for reduction. \n\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 160)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201420,'chemistry','A: The iron obtained from the blast furnace contains about 4% carbon and many impurities in smaller amounts.\n\nR: Pig iron is different from cast iron and is made by melting pig iron with scrap iron and coke using hot air blast.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','"The iron obtained from Blast furnace contains about 4% carbon and many impurities in smaller amount (e.g., S, P, Si, Mn). This is known as pig iron. Cast iron is different from pig iron and is made by melting pig iron with scrap iron and coke using hot air blast. It has slightly lower carbon content (about 3%) and is extremely hard and brittle."\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 161)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201421,'chemistry','A: Limestone is added as a flux during the extraction of copper.\n\nR: Sulphur, silicon, and phosphorus are removed from the metal by passing it through rollers.','Assertion is true but the reason is false','"Limestone is added as a flux and sulphur, silicon, and phosphorus are oxidised and passed into the slag. The metal is removed and freed from the slag  by passing through rollers." \n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 161)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201422,'chemistry','A: Electrolytic refining is a method of obtaining pure metal from impure metal.\n\nR: In this method, the impure metal is made to act as cathode, and a strip of the same metal in pure form is used as anode.','Assertion is true but the reason is false','In this method, the impure metal is made to act as anode. A strip of the same metal in pure form is used as cathode.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 164)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201423,'chemistry','A: The Mond process is a method of refining nickel.\n\nR: In the Mond process, nickel is converted into a volatile complex named as nickel tetracarbonyl which is decomposed at higher temperature to obtain pure metal.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Mond Process for Refining Nickel: In this process, nickel is heated in a stream of carbon monoxide forming a volatile complex named as nickel tetracarbonyl. This complex is decomposed at higher temperature to obtain pure metal."\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 165)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201424,'chemistry','A: Aluminium foils are used as wrappers for food materials. The fine dust of the metal is used in paints and lacquers.\n\nR: Aluminium, being highly reactive, is also used in the extraction of chromium and manganese from their oxides.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Aluminium foils are used as wrappers for food materials. The fine\ndust of the metal is used in paints and lacquers. Aluminium, being\nhighly reactive, is also used in the extraction of chromium and\nmanganese from their oxides. Wires of aluminium are used as\nelectricity conductors. Alloys containing aluminium, being light,\nare very useful.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 166)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(70815,'chemistry','Compare the acidity of the following acids, HF,HCl,HBr & HI','HI>HBr>HCl>HF','(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 224 ; paragraph 6) diagram(ne0722)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Ionization of Acids and Bases',NULL,'2','mcq','HF>HCl>HBr>HI','HF>HBr>HI>HCl','HI>HBr>HF>HCl','HI>HBr>HCl>HF','NEW','11-224','11-200',NULL),
(70816,'chemistry','Evaluate the pH of the solution resulting on addition of 0.05M acetate ion to 0.05M acetic acid solution. Ka of acetic acid is 1.8x10⁻⁵','4.74','(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 225 ; paragraph 2) diagram(ne0723)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Ionization of Acids and Bases',NULL,'3','mcq','5.47','4.47','4.74','5.74','NEW','11-225','11-201',NULL),
(70817,'chemistry','Calculate the pH after 50.0 mL of 0.1 M ammonia solution is treated with 25.0 mL of 0.10M HCl. The disociation constant of ammonia, Kb = 1.77 × 10⁻⁵','pH = 9.24','(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 225; Problem 7.24) diagram(ne0724)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Ionization of Acids and Bases',NULL,'3','mcq','pH = 9.24','pH = 10.24','pH = 8.24','pH = 11.24','NEW','11-225','11-201',NULL),
(71101,'chemistry','What is Kc for the following equilibrium when the equilibrium concentration of each substance is: [SO₂ ]= 0.60M, [O₂ ] = 0.82M and [SO₃ ] = 1.90M ?\n2SO₂ (g) + O₂ (g) = 2SO₃ (g)','12.229','Kc = [SO₃]² / [SO₂]² [O₂]    so, Kc = (1.9)² / {(0.60)² x 0.82} , Kc = 12.229   \nExercise question - 7.2,(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 232)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Excercise questions',NULL,'1','mcq','13.229','12.229','25.229','50.229','NEW','11-200','11-176',NULL),
(71102,'chemistry','CaCO₃ (s) = CaO(s) + CO₂ (g); Kp = 167 at 1073 K, find Kc value.','1.9','CaCO₃ (s) = CaO(s) + CO₂\nn=(0+1)−0=1\nKc​=Kp/(RT)ⁿ\nKc​=167bar/{(0.0821Lbar/K)×(1073K)}​=1.9mol/L  ; Exercise question - 7.5 (ii),(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 232)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Excercise questions',NULL,'1','mcq','11.9','0.119','1.9','9.11','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71103,'chemistry','For the following equilibrium, Kc = 6.3 × 10¹⁴ at 1000 K NO (g) + O₃ (g) = NO₂ (g) + O₂  (g) both the forward and reverse reactions in the equilibrium are elementary imolecular reactions. What is Kc, for the reverse reaction ?','1.59 × 10⁻¹⁵','The value of the equilibrium constant for the reverse reaction is obtained from the reciprocal of the value of the equilibrium constant of the forward reaction.\nHence, Kc(reverse)​=1/Kc\nSubstitute values in the above expression.\nKc(reverse)​=1/(6.3×10¹⁴) =1.59×10−¹⁵  \nExercise question - 7.6,(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 232)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Excercise questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1.59 × 10⁻¹³','1.59 × 10⁻¹⁴','1.59 × 10⁻¹⁵','1.59 × 10⁻¹⁶','NEW','11-200','11-176',NULL),
(71104,'chemistry','Nitric oxide reacts with Br₂ and gives nitrosyl bromide as per reaction 2NO (g) + Br₂ (g) = 2NOBr (g). When 0.087 mol of NO and 0.0437 mol of Br₂ are mixed in a closed container at constant temperature, 0.0518 mol of NOBr is obtained at equilibrium. Calculate equilibrium amount of NO and Br₂.','0.0352mol of NO and 0.0178mol of Br₂','2NO(g)         +         Br₂(g)          =  2NOBr(g) \n initial        0.0870                       0.0437                   ----\n final        (0.087−2a)                  (0.0437−a)          2a\n\nGiven: 2a = 0.0518 \n∴a = 0.0259 \nAt equilibrium, NO = 0.0870−0.0518  = 0.0352 mole \n∴Br₂ = 0.0437−0.0259 = 0.0178 mole ;Exercise question - 7.9,(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 233)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Excercise questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.352mol of NO and 0.178mol of Br₂','0.0352mol of NO and 0.178mol of Br₂','0.352mol of NO and 0.0178mol of Br₂','0.0352mol of NO and 0.0178mol of Br₂','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71105,'chemistry','A sample of HI(g) is placed in flask at a pressure of 0.2 atm. At equilibrium the partial pressure of HI(g) is 0.04 atm. What is Kp for the given equilibrium ?\nGiven 2HI (g) = H₂ (g) + I₂ (g)','4','The equilibrium reaction is shown below.\n               2HI(g) =   H₂(g) +  I₂ (g)\nInitial       0.2             ---           ---\nfinal         0.2-2x         x             x\n But the equilibrium pressure of HI is 0.04 atm.\nSo, 0.2−2x=0.04\nHence, 2x=0.2−0.04=0.16 or x=0.08.\nThe expression for the equilibrium constant is Kp = {p(H₂)xp(I₂)}/ {p(HI)}² =  (0.08×0.08)/(0.04)² = 4  ;   Exercise question - 7.11,(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 233)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Excercise questions',NULL,'2','mcq','4','10.23','6.32','4.8','NEW','11-202','11-178','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/71105q.png'),
(71106,'chemistry','One mole of H₂O and one mole of CO are taken in 10 L vessel and heated to\n725 K. At equilibrium 40% of water (by mass) reacts with CO according to the\nequation,\nH₂O (g) + CO (g) = H₂ (g) + CO₂ (g). Calculate the equilibrium constant for the reaction.','0.44','Initial amount of H₂O = 1 mol = 18g. \nIf 40% of water by mass reacts with CO, then the amount of H₂O consumed =18×40100=7.2g=0.4mol \nSo, the amount of H₂O that remains equilibrium =1−0.4 = 0.6 mol \nAs per the reaction, 0.4 mol of H₂O reacts with equal amount of CO(g) and forms 0.4 mol of each of H₂ and CO₂. \nSo, in the equilibrium mixture, [H₂O] = 0.6 / 10 mol⋅L −¹= 0.06 mol⋅L−¹ \n[CO] = (1−0.4) / 10 mol⋅L−¹ = 0.06 mol⋅L−¹ \nand [H₂] = [CO₂] = 0.4 / 10 mol⋅L−¹= 0.04 mol⋅L−¹ \nhence, for the reaction \nKc=[H₂][CO₂] / [H₂O][CO]=(0.04×0.04)/(0.06×0.06) = 0.44;   Exercise question - 7.14,(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 233)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Excercise questions',NULL,'2','mcq','4.4','0.44','0.04','0.004','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71107,'chemistry','Kp = 0.04 atm at 899 K for the equilibrium shown below. What is the equilibrium concentration of C₂H₆ when it is placed in a flask at 4.0 atm pressure and allowedto come to equilibrium?\nC₂H₆ (g) = C₂H₄ (g) + H₂ (g)','3.62 atm','The equilibrium reaction is\n                  C₂H₆(g) = C₂​H₄ ​(g)+H₂​(g).\n        Initial       4             ---           ---\n        Final      4−x           x             x\nThe expression for the equilibrium constant is Kp​= (pC₂​H₄  x ​​pH₂ ) / p(C₂​H₆)​​​​​.\nSubstitute values in the above expression.\n0.04= x² / (4-x)​ or x = 0.38\n\nThus the pressure of ethane is, pC₂​H₆ ​​= 3.62atm.Exercise question - 7.17,(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 233)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Excercise questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.3 atm','3.00 atm','3.62 atm','0.362 atm','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(71108,'chemistry','A sample of pure PCl₅ was introduced into an evacuated vessel at 473 K. After equilibrium was attained, concentration of PCl₅ was found to be 0.5 × 10⁻¹ mol L⁻¹. If value of Kc is 8.3 × 10⁻³ , what are the concentrations of PCl₃ and Cl₂ at equilibrium? Given PCl₅ (g) = PCl₃ (g) + Cl₂(g).','0.02 mol L⁻¹ for both','Let x M be the equilibrium concentration of PCl₃​.\nThe equilibrium reaction is shown below.\nPCl₅ (g) = PCl₃ (g) + Cl₂(g)\nThe equilibrium concentrations of PCl₅​,PCl₃ and Cl2​ are 0.5×10⁻¹M, x and x respectively.\nThe expression of the equilibrium constant is Kc  = [PCl₃][Cl₂​]​ / [PCl₅].\nSubstitute values in the above expression.\n8.3×10⁻³=   x²​ / 0.5×10⁻¹\nx² = 4.15×10⁻⁴\nx  = 0.02M  ; Exercise question - 7.19,(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 234)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Excercise questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.2 mol L⁻¹ for both','0.02 mol L⁻¹ for both','0.002 mol L⁻¹ for both','0.0002 mol L⁻¹ for both','NEW','11-203','11-179',NULL),
(71109,'chemistry','C (s) + CO₂ (g) = 2CO (g). At 1127 K and 1 atm pressure, a gaseous mixture of CO and CO₂ in equilibrium with soild carbon has 90.55% CO by mass .Calculate Kc for this reaction at the above temperature.','0.153','Suppose the mixture has 100g as total mass.\n\nThe masses of CO and CO₂​ are 90.55g and 100−90.55 = 9.45 g respectively.\nThe number of moles of CO are 90.55/ 28 ​= 3.234.\nThe number of moles of CO₂​ are 9.45 / 44 ​= 0.215.\nThe mole fraction of CO is 3.234​ /(3.234 + 0.215 ) = 0.938.\nThe mole fraction of CO₂​ is 1−0.938 = 0.062.\n\nThe partial pressure of CO is the product of the mole fraction of CO and the total pressure.\nIt is 0.938×1 = 0.938 atm.\n\nThe partial pressure of CO₂ is 0.062 × 1=0.062 atm.\n\nThe expression for the equilibrium constant is: \nKp​= (pCO)²​​/pCO₂​​=(0.938)²​ / 0.062 = 14.19\nn ​= 2−1 = 1\nKc​=Kp​(RT)⁻ⁿ = 14.19×(0.0821×1127)⁻¹ = 0.153   ; Exercise question - 7.23,(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 234)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Excercise questions',NULL,'3','mcq','15.3','1.53','0.153','0.0153','NEW','11-204','11-180',NULL),
(71110,'chemistry','Which of the following reactions will get affected by increasing the pressure?\nI. 2H₂ (g) + CO (g) = CH₃OH (g) \nII. CaCO₃ (s) = CaO (s) + CO₂ (g)','both will be affected','According to Le Chatelier principle, if the difference between the total moles of gaseous reactants and pruducts is not zero, then change in pressure must affect either forward or backward reaction depending upon stoichiometry of the overall balanced reaction. Exercise question - 7.26,(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 235)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Excercise questions',NULL,'2','mcq','II will be affected','I will be affected','both will be affected','none will be affected','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71111,'chemistry','The value of Kc for the reaction 3O₂ (g) = 2O₃ (g) is 2.0 ×10⁻⁵⁰ at 25°C. If the equilibrium concentration of O₂ in air at 25°C is 1.6 ×10⁻², what is the concentration of O₃?','2.86 × 10⁻²⁸ M','The equilibrium constant expression is \nKc​=[O₃​]²​ / [O₂​]³\n2.0×10⁻⁵⁰ = [O₃​]²​ / (1.6 ×10⁻²)³\n[O₃]² = (2.0×10⁻⁵⁰)×(1.6 ×10⁻²)³ = 8.192×10⁻⁵⁶\nHence, the equilibrium concentration of ozone is\n[O₃] = 2.86×10⁻²⁸ M\nExercise question - 7.33,(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 236)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Excercise questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2.86 ×10^28  M','2.86 × 10⁻²⁷ M','2.86 × 10⁻²⁸ M','2.86 × 10⁻²⁹ M','NEW','11-200','11-176',NULL),
(71112,'chemistry','The pH of a sample of vinegar is 3.76. Calculate the concentration of hydrogen ion in it.','1.7x10⁻⁴ M','pH = -log [H⁺]\nlog [H⁺]  = -pH\n[H⁺] = antilog (-pH)\n= antilog(-3.76)\n= 1.74x10⁻⁴ M  ; Exercise question - 7.42,(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 236)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Excercise questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1.7x10⁻³ M','1.7x10⁻⁴ M','1.7x10⁻⁵ M','1.7x10⁻⁶ M','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71113,'chemistry','It has been found that the pH of a 0.01M solution of an organic acid is 4.15. Calculate the concentration of the anion, the ionization constant of the acid.','[A⁻ ] = 7.08 x10⁻⁵M, Ka = 5.08 × 10⁻⁷','pH=−log[H⁺]=4.15\n[H⁺]=antilog(−4.15)=7.08×10⁻⁵\n[A⁻]=[H⁺]=7.08×10⁻⁵\n\nThe concentration of undissociated acid is (0.01−0.000071) M = 0.009929 M.\n\nHA+H₂​O = H₃​O⁺+A⁻\n\nKa​=[H₃​O+][A⁻] / [HA] ​= (7.08×10⁻⁵)² / 0.009929 = 5.05×10⁻⁷  ; Exercise question - 7.47,(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 236)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Excercise questions',NULL,'2','mcq','[A⁻ ] = 7.08 x10⁻¹⁰M, Ka = 5.08 × 10⁻⁷','[A⁻ ] = 7.08 x10⁻²M, Ka = 5.08 × 10⁻⁷','[A⁻ ] = 7.08 x10⁻⁵M, Ka = 5.08 × 10⁻⁵','[A⁻ ] = 7.08 x10⁻⁵M, Ka = 5.08 × 10⁻⁷','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(71114,'chemistry','1mL of 13.6 M HCl is diluted with water to give 1 litre of solution. Calculate the pH .','1.87','Moles of HCl in the original solution = 0.001×13.6 = 0.0136 moles.\nNow concentration of HCl in 1 liter solution = 0.01361 = 0.0136 M\nConcentration of H⁺ = 0.0136 M\npH=−log[H⁺]=−log(0.0136)=1.87  ;  Exercise question - 7.49(d),(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 237)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Excercise questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1.87','1.27','1.67','1.77','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71115,'chemistry','The degree of ionization of a 0.1M bromoacetic acid solution is 0.132. Calculate the pH of the solution and the pKa of bromoacetic acid.','pH = 1.88, pKa = 2.70','[H⁺]=cα=0.1×0.132=0.0132M\npH=−log[H⁺]=−log0.0132=1.88\n\nThe acid dissociation constant is Ka ​= cα² / (1−α)​ = 0.1×(0.132)² / (1−0.132) ​= 2.01×10⁻³.\npKa ​= −logKa​ = −log(2.01×10⁻³)  =  2.7  ;  Exercise question - 7.50,(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 237)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Excercise questions',NULL,'2','mcq','pH = 3.88, pKa = 3.70','pH = 1.88, pKa = 2.70','pH = 2.88, pKa = 2.70','pH = 1.88, pKa = 3.70','NEW','11-220','11-196',NULL),
(71116,'chemistry','If 0.561 g of KOH is dissolved in water to give 200 mL of solution at 298 K. What is pH of that solution?','12.7','[KOH] = [K⁺ ]= [OH⁻] = (0.561×1000) / (56×200)​ = 0.050 M\n[H⁺] = Kw / [OH⁻] ​​= 10⁻¹⁴ / 0.05​= 2.0×10⁻¹³\npH = −log[H⁺] = −log(2.0×10⁻¹³ ) = 12.7   ; Exercise question - 7.57,(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 238)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Excercise questions',NULL,'2','mcq','12.7','12','11.7','11.5','NEW','11-222','11-198',NULL),
(71117,'chemistry','A 0.02 M solution of pyridinium hydrochloride has pH = 3.44. Calculate the ionization constant of pyridine.','1.5 × 10⁻⁹','pH=0.5pKw​−0.5pKb​−0.5logC\n\nSubstitute values in the above expression:\n\n3.44 = 0.5×14 − 0.5pKb − 0.5 log0.02\npKb ​= 8.818\nKb ​= 1.52×10⁻⁹   ;  Exercise question - 7.62,(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 237)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Excercise questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1.5 × 10⁻¹¹','1.5 × 10⁻¹⁰','1.5 × 10⁻⁹','1.5 × 10⁻⁸','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71118,'chemistry','Ionic product of water at 310 K is 2.7 × 10⁻¹⁴. What is the pH of neutral water at this temperature?','6.78','Kw​=[H⁺][OH−]=2.7×10⁻¹⁴=[H⁺]²\n\nAs, in neutral [H⁺]=[OH−]\n\n[H⁺]= (2.7×10⁻¹⁴)¹/²\n​       =1.643×10⁻⁷\npH = −log[H⁺] = −log(1.643×10⁻⁷) = 6.78  ;  Exercise question - 7.65,(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 238)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Excercise questions',NULL,'2','mcq','7','7.11','6.98','6.78','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71119,'chemistry','The solubility product constant of Ag₂CrO₄ and AgBr are 1.1 × 10⁻¹² and 5.0 × 10⁻¹³ respectively. Calculate the ratio of the molarities of their saturated solutions.','91.9','Silver chromate: Ag₂CrO₄  = 2Ag⁺ + CrO₄²⁻\n[Ag⁺] = 2s₁​, [CrO₄²⁻] ​ =s₁​\nKsp​=(2s₁)²(s₁)=4s³ = 1.1×10⁻¹²\ns₁​ = 6.5×10⁻⁵.....(1)\n\nSilver bromide: AgBr =  Ag⁺ + Br⁻\n[Ag⁺] = [Br⁻] = s₂\nKsp​ = (s₂​)×(s₂​) =  (s₂)²​ = 5.0×10⁻¹³\ns₂​ = 7.07×10⁻⁷......(2)\nDivide equation (1) by equation (2) to obtain the ratio of the molarities of saturated solutions:\n​s₁ / s₂​​ =  6.5×10⁻⁵ / 7.07×10⁻⁷ ​= 91.9  ;  Exercise question - 7.68,(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 238)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Excercise questions',NULL,'3','mcq','91.9','9.19','0.919','0.0919','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71120,'chemistry','Equal volumes of 0.002 M solutions of sodium iodate and cupric chlorate are mixed together. Will it lead to precipitation of copper iodate? (For cupric iodate Ksp = 7.4 × 10⁻⁸ ).','no precipitaion','After mixing, the concentration of NaIO₃​ is 0.002 / 2 ​= 0.001 M.\nAfter mixing, the concentration of Cu(ClO₃​)₂​ is 0.002​ / 2 = 0.001 M.\nNaIO₃  = Na⁺+IO₃⁻\n[IO₃⁻] = 0.001 M\nCu(ClO₃​) =  Cu²⁺ +2ClO₃⁻​\n[Cu²⁺] = 0.001 M\nThe ionic product of Cu(IO₃​)₂​ is\n[Cu²⁺][I⁻]² = 0.001×(0.001)² = 1×10⁻⁹\nAs the ionic product is less than the solubility product, no precipitation will occur. ;             Exercise question - 7.69,(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 238)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Excercise questions',NULL,'2','mcq','full precipitation','partial precipitation','no precipitaion','can\'t be predicted','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71121,'chemistry','What is the minimum volume of water required to dissolve 1g of calcium sulphate at 298 K? (For calcium sulphate, Ksp is 9.1 × 10⁻⁶).','2.43 litre of water','[Ca²⁺]=[SO₄²⁻​]=s \nKsp​ = [Ca²⁺][SO₄²⁻​] = s×s = s² = 9.1×10⁻⁶\ns = 0.003016 M\nThe molar  mass of CaSO₄​ is (40+32+64)g mol⁻¹  = 136g mol⁻¹.\nThe solubility in g L⁻¹ is (0.003017 × 136) g L⁻¹ = 0.410g L⁻¹.\nHence, 0.41 g dissolves in 1 L.\n1 g will dissolve in (1 / 0.41) L ​=2.43L.   ;  Exercise question - 7.72,(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 238)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Excercise questions',NULL,'3','mcq','243 litre of water','24.3 litre of water','2.43 litre of water','0.243 litre of water','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71122,'chemistry','In liquid-gas equilibrium, the pressure of vapours above the liquid is constant at','constant temperature','Vapour pressure is directly proportional to temperature. Increase in temperature will increase the vapour pressure.\nSo to keep vapour pressure constant, temperature should be constant.\nA pure liquid is placed inside a closed chamber at a particular temperature. After sometime the pressure inside the closed chamber becomes constant as  it gives rise to a constant vapour pressure because of an equilibrium in which the number of molecules leaving the liquid equals the number returning to liquid from the vapour state. We say that  the system has reached equilibrium state at this stage. It shoud be remembered that air is presnt inside the chamber at 1 atm pressure. (NCERT chemistry class 11; page 192 ; paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','constant temperature','low temperature','high temperature','none of these','NEW','11-192','11-168',NULL),
(71123,'chemistry','The equilibrium constants of the following are \nN₂+ 3H₂   ⇌  2NH₃; K1\nN₂ + O₂    ⇌  2NO; K₂ \nH₂ + ½O₂ ⇌  H₂0; K₃\nThe equilibrium constant (K) for the reaction  2NH₃ + 5/2O₂ ⇌  2NO + 3H₂O will be','K₂K₃³/K₁','N₂+ 3H₂   → 2NH₃; K1 on reversing the equation\n\n2NH₃ ⇌ N₂+ 3H₂  ;1/K1   ......1\n\nN₂ + O₂    ⇌  2NO; K₂  ........2\n\nH₂ + ½O₂ ⇌  H₂0; K₃ ; multiplying this equation throughout by 3 we get  \n3H₂ + 3/2O₂ ⇌  3H₂0; K₃³ ...3\n\nby adding all these 1 , 2 , and 3  2NH₃ + 5/2O₂ ⇌  2NO + 3H₂O ; K= K₂K₃³/K₁  ,which is the required anwer','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','K₂K₃³/K₁','K₂K₃/K₁','K₂³K₃/K₁','K₁K₃³/K₂','NEW','11-201','11-177',NULL),
(71124,'chemistry','If the equilibrium constant for N₂(g)+O₂(g) ⇌ 2NO (g)is K ,the equilibrium constant for 1/2N₂ (g)+ 1/2O₂(g) ⇌ NO(g) will be','K¹⸍²','Whenever a reaction is multiplied by any number the equilibrium constant becomes Kⁿ','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','1/2K','K','K₂','K¹⸍²','NEW','11-201','11-177',NULL),
(71125,'chemistry','Given that the equilibrium constant for the reaction 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g)  has a value of 278 at a particular temperature .What is the value for the equilibrium constant for the following reaction at the same temperature?\nSO₃(g) ⇌ SO₂(g) + 1/2O₂(g)','6 ×10⁻²','2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g) ;K =278 . For the reverse reaction \n2SO₃(g) ⇌ 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g)  ;K\' = 1/278 ......(1)\nFor the required reaction we can divide (1) by a value of 2 . Hence we get \nSO₃(g) ⇌ SO₂(g) + 1/2O₂(g) ;K\'\' = √K\' , that is √(1/278)\nOn solving we get \n√(1/278) = 0.0599 ≈ 6 ×10⁻²','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1.8 × 10⁻³','3.6 × 10⁻³','6 ×10⁻²','1.3 × 10⁻⁵','NEW','11-201','11-177',NULL),
(71126,'chemistry','Given the reaction between 2 gases represented by A₂ and B₂ to give the compound AB ; A₂(g) + B₂(g) ⇌ 2AB(g) . At equilibrium the concentrattion of A₂ = 3.0×10⁻³M, of B₂  = 4.2×10⁻³M ,of AB = 2.8×10⁻³M .If the reaction takes place in a sealed vessel at 527°C then the value of K  will be ?','0.62','Given A₂(g) + B₂(g) ⇌ 2AB(g) . For this reaction Kc = [AB]²/[A₂][B₂]\nSubstituting  A₂ = 3.0×10⁻³M,  B₂  = 4.2×10⁻³M , AB = 2.8×10⁻³M we get Kc = 0.62 , Which is the required solution','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','2','1.9','0.62','4.5','NEW','11-200','11-176',NULL),
(71127,'chemistry','For the reaction N₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2NO(g) , the equilibrium constant is K₁. The equilibrum constant is K₂  for the reaction 2NO(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g)  . What is the K for the reaction, NO₂(g) ⇌  1/2N₂(g) + O₂(g)?','(1/K₁K₂)¹⸍²','Given\nN₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2NO(g) ; K₁ .On reversing this reaction and multiplying by 1/2\nNO(g) ⇌ 1/2N₂(g) + 1/2O₂(g) ; K₁ \' =1/(K₁ )¹⸍² .......(i)\nSimilarly 2NO(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g) ; K₂ . On reversing the equation and multiplying by 1/2\nNO₂(g) ⇌ NO(g) + 1/2O₂(g) ;K₂\' = 1/(K₂)¹⸍² .....(ii)\nOn adding (i) and (ii) we get NO₂(g) ⇌  1/2N₂(g) + O₂(g) ; K\' = 1/(K₁ K₂)¹⸍²','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','1/2K₁K₂','1/4K₁K₂','(1/K₁K₂)¹⸍²','1/K₁K₂','NEW','11-201','11-177',NULL),
(71128,'chemistry','The dissociation constants for acetic acid and HCN at 25℃ are 1.5×10⁻⁵ and 4.5×10⁻¹⁰ respectievely the equilibrium constant for the equilibrium CN⁻ + CH₃COOH ⇌ HCN + CH₃COO⁻ would be','3.0 × 10⁴','CH₃COOH  ⇌ CH₃COO⁻ + H⁺ ; K₁=1.5×10⁻⁵....(i)\n\nHCN  ⇌ CN⁻ + H⁺  ;  K₂ = 4.5×10⁻¹⁰ , On reversing \nCN⁻ + H⁺ ⇌ HCN ; K₂\' =1/(4.5×10⁻¹⁰ ) ........(ii)\n\nOn adding (i) and (ii) we get \nCN⁻ + CH₃COOH ⇌ HCN + CH₃COO⁻ ; K\' = K₁×K₂\'\nThat is K\' = (1.5×10⁻⁵ )/(4.5×10⁻¹⁰ ) = 3.0 × 10⁵','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','3.0 × 10⁻⁵','3.0 × 10⁻⁴','3.0 × 10⁴','3.0 × 10⁵','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(71129,'chemistry','The value of equilibrium constant of the reaction HI(g) ⇌ 1/2H₂(g) + 1/2I₂ (g) is 8.0 .The equilibrium constant of the reaction H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g) will be:','1/64','HI(g) ⇌ 1/2H₂(g) + 1/2I₂ (g) ; K = 8\n\nOn reversing the reactio and multiplying by 2 we get the required equation\nH₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g) ;K\'= (1/K)² = (1/8)² = 1/64','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','16','1/8','1/64','1/63','NEW','11-201','11-177',NULL),
(71130,'chemistry','Equilibrium constants K₁ and K₂ for the following equilibria are related as?\nNO(g) + 1/2O₂(g) ⇌ NO₂(g) ;K₁  and \n2NO₂(g) ⇌  2NO(g) + O₂(g) ;K₂','K₂=1/K₁²','NO(g) + 1/2O₂(g) ⇌ NO₂(g) ;K₁ \nK₁ =P(NO₂)/P(NO)P(O₂)¹⸍² ....(I)\n\n2NO₂(g) ⇌  2NO(g) + O₂(g) ;K₂\nK₂ = P(NO)²P(O₂)/P(NO₂)²......(II)\n\nTaking square roots on both sides in (II) \n√K₂ = P(NO)P(O₂)¹⸍²/P(NO₂)\nIt is now clear that  1/√K₂ = K₁\nor K₂=1/K₁²','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','K₂=1/K₁²','K₂=K₁²','K₂=1/K₁','K₂=K₁/2','NEW','11-201','11-177',NULL),
(71131,'chemistry','If K₁ and K₂ are the respective equilibrium constants for the two reactions, XeF₆(g) + H₂O(g) → XeOF₄(g) + 2HF(g) and XeO₄(g) + XeF₆(g) → XeOF₄(g)+ XeO₃F₂(g) respectievely  then the equilibrium constant of the reaction,\nXeO₄(g) + 2HF(g) → XeO₃F₂ + H₂O(g) , will be','K₂/K₁','Given  \nXeF₆(g) + H₂O(g) → XeOF₄(g) + 2HF(g)  ; K₁ . On reversing the equation we have\nXeOF₄(g) + 2HF(g) → XeF₆(g) + H₂O(g)  ;K₁\' =1/K₁ ......(i)\n\nsimilarly XeO₄(g) + XeF₆(g) → XeOF₄(g)+ XeO₃F₂(g) ;K₂ ........(ii)\n\non adding (i) and (ii) we get the equation\nXeO₄(g) + 2HF(g) → XeO₃F₂ + H₂O(g)  K\' = K₁\' * K₂ = K₂/K₁','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','K₁/K₂','K₁×K₂','K₁/(K₂)²','K₂/K₁','NEW','11-201','11-177',NULL),
(71132,'chemistry','The equilibrium constant for the reaction N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g) is K, then the equilibrium constant for the equilibrium 2NH₃(g) ⇌ N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) is','1/K','For the reverse reaction equilibrium constant K\' = 1/K','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','√K','√(1/K)','1/K','1/K²','NEW','11-201','11-177',NULL),
(71133,'chemistry','K₁ and K₂ are equilibrium constants for reactions (i) and (ii) respectively.\nN₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2NO(g) ....(i) and\nNO(g) ⇌  1/2N₂(g) + 1/2O₂(g).....(ii) ; then','K₁=(1/K₂)²','N₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2NO(g) ;K₁ .....(I)\n\nNO(g) ⇌  1/2N₂(g) + 1/2O₂(g) ; K₂.......(II)\nBut if we reverse (II) and and multiply  by 2 we get (I) \nThat is K₁ = (1/K₂)²','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','K₁=(1/K₂)²','K₁=K₂²','K₁=1/K₂','K₁=(K₂)⁰','NEW','11-201','11-177',NULL),
(71134,'chemistry','The reaction, 2A(g) + B(g) ⇌ 3C(g) + D(g) is begun with the concentrations of A and B both at an initial value of 1.00 M. When equilibrium is reached, the concentration of D is measured and found to be 0.25 M. The value for the equilibrium constant for this reaction is given by the expression','[(0.75)³(0.25)] ÷ [(0.50)²(0.75)]','2A(g)       +          B(g) ⇌       3C(g)      +      D(g)\ninitial moles    :                 1                         1                0                  0\nmoles at equilibrium:    [1–(2×0.25)]    [1 – 0.25]     [3×0.25]            0.25\n                                     = 0.5                 = 0.75          = 0.75             = 0.25\n\nEquilibrium constant, Kc = [C]³[D]/[A]²[B]\n                                         =[0.25]³[0.75]/[0.5]²[0.75]\n                                         =[(0.75)³(0.25)] ÷ [(0.50)²(0.75)]','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','[(0.75)³(0.25)] ÷ [(1.00)²(1.00)]','[(0.75)³(0.25)] ÷ [(0.50)²(0.75)]','[(0.75)³(0.25)] ÷ [(0.50)²(0.25)]','[(0.75)³(0.25)] ÷ [(0.75)²(0.25)]','NEW','11-200','11-176',NULL),
(71135,'chemistry','The dissociation equilibrium of a gas AB₂ can be represented as : 2AB₂(g) ⇌ 2AB(g) + B₂(g) .The degree of dissociation is x and is small compared to 1. The expression relating the degree of dissociation (x) with equilibrium constant Kp and total pressure P is','(2Kp/P)¹⸍³','2AB₂(g) ⇌ 2AB(g) + B₂(g)\ninitially             2                0            0\nequilibrium      2-2x           2x          x\n\nKp= P[AB]²P[B₂]/P[AB₂]² …….(I)\n\nTotal moles at equilibrium =    2-2x+2x+x = 2+x\n\nPᵢ=TotalPressure  x Mole fraction of that gas\n\nP[AB] = P×(2-2x / 2+x)   ;P[B₂] =  P × (2x / 2+x)    ; P[AB₂] = P × (x / 2+x)  \n\nSubstituting these values in (I) we have \nx =(2Kp/P)¹⸍³','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','(2Kp/P)¹⸍²','(Kp/P)','(2Kp/P)','(2Kp/P)¹⸍³','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71136,'chemistry','The values of Kp₁and Kp₂ for the reactions,\nX ⇌  Y + Z ...(i)\nA ⇌ 2B ...(ii) ; are in the ratio 9 : 1. If degree of dissociation of X and A be equal, then total pressure at equilibrium (i) and (ii) are in the ratio','36:1','X      ⇌         Y     +     Z\ninitially           1                 0             0\nequilibrium    1-α              α             α\n\nKp₁= P[Y]P[Z]/P[X] \nKp₁= P[α]P[α ]/P[1-α]…….(I)\n\n                      A       ⇌       2B \ninitially           1                 0             \nequilibrium    1- α              2α \n  \nKp₂= P[B]²/P[A]         \nKp₂= P[2α ²/P[1-α]   ..........(II)    \nFrom (I) and(II) we get Kp₁/Kp₂ = 36:1','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','36:1','1:1','3:1','1:9','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71137,'chemistry','A 20 litre container at 400 K contains CO₂(g) at pressure 0.4 atm and an excess of SrO (neglect the volume of solid SrO). The volume of the container is now decreased by moving the movable piston fitted in the container. The maximum volume of the container, when pressure of CO₂ attains its maximum value, will be (Given that : SrCO₃(s)  ⇌  SrO(s) + CO₂(g) ,Kp = 1.6 atm)','5L','SrCO₃(s)  ⇌  SrO(s) + CO₂(g) \nKp= P[SrO]P[CO₂]/P[SrCO₃] .Since we can take partial pressures of solid = 1;\nKp= P[CO₂] =1.6 (given)\nLet the maximum volume of the container when pressure of CO2 is 1.6 atm be V L\nDuring the process, PV = constant ; 0.4 × 20 = 1.6 × V\nHence we get V = 5L','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','10L','4L','2L','5L','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71138,'chemistry','In which of the following equilibrium Kc and Kp are not equal?','2C(s) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2CO₂(g)','Kp and Kc are related as Kp = Kc (RT)Dng where Δng = difference in the no. of moles of products and reactants in the gaseous state.\nFor 2C(s) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2CO₂(g) ; Δng = 2-1=1','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','2NO(g) ⇌ N₂(g) + O₂(g)','SO₂(g) + NO₂(g)  ⇌ SO₃(g) + NO(g)','H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g)','2C(s) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2CO₂(g)','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71139,'chemistry','If the concentration of OH⁻ ions in the reaction Fe(OH)₃(s)  ⇌   Fe³⁺ (aq) + 3OH⁻(aq) is decreased by 1/4 times, then equilibrium concentration of Fe³+ will increase by','64 times','Fe(OH)₃(s)  ⇌   Fe³⁺ (aq) + 3OH⁻(aq) for this reaction \nKeq = [ Fe³⁺][OH⁻]³/[Fe(OH)₃] .But Fe(OH)₃ is a solid and hence its concentration is taken as unity .\nThere Keq =  [ Fe³⁺][OH⁻]³ \nThe concentration of OH⁻ ions  is decreased by 1/4 times . Keq has to remain a constant .There fore reaction takes place in the forward direction and according to the equation [ Fe³⁺] increases by 64 times','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','64 times','4 times','8 times','16 times','NEW','11-200','11-176',NULL),
(71140,'chemistry','Equilibrium constant Kp for following reaction MgCO₃(s) ⇌ MgO(s) + CO₂(g)','Kp = PCO2','Kp = PCO2 .Solids do not exert pressure, so their partial pressure is taken as unity.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','Kp = PCO2','Kp = PCO2 x ((PCO2 x PMgO)/PMgCO₃)','Kp =(PCO2 + PMgO)/PMgCO₃)','Kp =PMgCO₃/(PCO2 x PMgO)','NEW','11-203','11-179',NULL),
(71141,'chemistry','If the value of equilibrium constant for a particular reaction is 1.6 × 10¹², then at equilibrium the system will contain','mostly products','The value of K is high which means reaction proceeds almost to completion i.e., the system will contain mostly products.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','mostly products','similar amounts of reactants and products','all reactants','mostly reactants','NEW','11-205','11-181',NULL),
(71142,'chemistry','In Haber process, 30 litres of dihydrogen and 30 litres of dinitrogen were taken for reaction which yielded only 50% of the expected product. What will be the composition of gaseous mixture under the aforesaid condition in the end?','10 litres ammonia, 25 litres nitrogen, 15 litres hydrogen','N₂+ 3H₂   → 2NH₃\n      1      3            2     (stoichiometric ratio)\n\n      30L  30L         ?    (Taken 30L each of nitrogen and ammonia ; we can find the amount of ammonia formed by comparing the stoichiometry )\n\nHere it is clear that Hydrogen is limiting and should get consumed completely . Nitrogen is excess agent and is in excess amount by 20L and 20 L ammonia should be formed.\nBut in question it is also given that the reaction is only 50 % efficient . Hence 25L of Nitrogen ; 15 L of hydrogen and 10L of ammonia will be there at the end of the reaction','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','20 litres ammonia, 20 litres nitrogen, 20 litres hydrogen','10 litres ammonia, 25 litres nitrogen, 15 litres hydrogen','20 litres ammonia, 10 litres nitrogen, 30 litres hydrogen','20 litres ammonia, 25 litres nitrogen, 15 litres hydrogen','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(71143,'chemistry','The reaction quotient (Q) for the reaction N₂(g) + 3H₂(g)  ⇌  2NH₃(g) is given by Q=[NH₃]²/[N₂][H₂]³ The reaction will proceed from right to left if ,where Kc is the equilibrium constant.','Qc > Kc','N₂(g) + 3H₂(g)  ⇌  2NH₃(g) \nQc=[NH₃]²/[N₂][H₂]³ ;Kc=[NH₃]²/[N₂][H₂]³ \nIf Qc <Kc it means numerator of Qc is less than that Kc . that means less products are formed than at equilibrium and the reaction will take place in the forward direction to reach equilibrium. ; left to right.\nIf Qc >Kc it means numerator of Qc is less than that Kc . that means more products are formed than at equilibrium and the reaction will take place in th backward direction to reach equilibrium ; right to left.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','Qc = Kc','Qc < Kc','Qc > Kc','Qc = 0','NEW','11-206','11-182',NULL),
(71144,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct for a reversible process in a state of equilibrium?','ΔG° = –2.30 RT log K','ΔG = ΔG° + RT lnQ .........(i) \nIf the reaction is at equilibrium  ΔG = 0 and Q=Kc\nHence (i) becomes 0 = ΔG° + RT lnKc \nΔG° = -RT lnK \nΔG° = -2.303RTlogK','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','ΔG° = –2.30 RT log K','ΔG° = 2.30 RT log K','ΔG = –2.30 RT logK','ΔG = 2.30 RT log K','NEW','11-208','11-184',NULL),
(71145,'chemistry','Match List I (Equations) with List II (Type of processes) and select the correct option. diagram (n0702)','A - (iv), B - (i), C - (ii), D - (iii)','Kp > Q that means the reaction will favour in the forward direction or in other words the reaction is spontaneous.\nWhen ΔG° < RT ln Q, ΔG° is positive, reverse reaction is feasible, thus reaction is non spontaneous.\nKp = Q  the reaction is in equilibrium \nT>ΔH/ΔS     or  TΔS>ΔH . Here for the reaction to be spontaneous  ΔG° has to be negative this is possible only when the reaction is endothermic ΔH = +ve.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','A - (i), B - (ii), C - (iii), D - (iv)','A - (iii), B - (iv), C - (ii), D - (i)','A - (iv), B - (i), C - (ii), D - (iii)','A - (ii), B - (i), C - (iv), D - (iii)','NEW','11-208','11-184','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/71145q.png'),
(71146,'chemistry','Which one of the following conditions will favour maximum formation of the product in the reaction A₂(g) + B₂(g) ⇌ X₂(g); ΔᵣH = –X kJ ?','Low temperature and high pressure',';','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','Low temperature and high pressure','Low temperature and low pressure','High temperature and high pressure','High temperature and low pressure','NEW','11-211','11-187',NULL),
(71147,'chemistry','For the reversible reaction, N₂(g) + 3H₂(g)  ⇌  2NH₃(g) + heat ,The equilibrium shifts in forward direction','by increasing pressure and decreasing temperature.','In this reaction forward direction leads to a less no of gaseous products that is on the product side no of moles of gasoeus molecules are less than on the reactant side and hence oit leads to lowering of pressure in the forward direction also forward reaction is exothermic. Hence, according to Le Chatelier’s principle, at high pressure and low temperature, the given reversible reaction will shift in forward direction to form more product.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','by increasing the concentration of NH₃(g)','by decreasing the pressure','by decreasing the concentrations of N₂(g) and  H₂(g)','by increasing pressure and decreasing temperature.','NEW','11-211','11-187',NULL),
(71148,'chemistry','For a given exothermic reaction, Kp and K′p are the equilibrium constants at temperatures T₁ and T₂, respectively. Assuming that heat of reaction is constant in temperature range between  T₁ and T₂, it is readily observed that','Kp > K′p','Since the reaction is exothermic T₁ < T₂\nFrom equation we already know that log K′p/Kp = -ΔH/2.303R(1/T₂-1/T₁) .......(i)\n(1/T₂-1/T₁) will be negative since T₁ < T₂ .; also ΔH is negative \nlogK′p - logKp = negative\nlogK′p - logKp <0\n K′p<Kp','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','Kp > K′p','Kp < K′p','Kp = K′p','Kp =1/ K′p','NEW','11-211','11-187',NULL),
(71149,'chemistry','KMnO₄ can be prepared from K₂MnO₄ as per the reaction, 3MnO₄²⁻ + 2H₂O     2MnO₄⁻ + MnO₂ + 4OH⁻ The reaction can go to completion by removing OH⁻  ions by adding','CO₂','HCl and SO₂ are reducing agents. So, they can reduce MnO₄⁻ .\nCO₂ is neither oxidising nor reducing agent, it will provide only acidic medium. It can shift the reaction in forward direction and the reaction can go to completion.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','CO₂','SO₂','HCl','KOH','NEW','11-209','11-185',NULL),
(71150,'chemistry','The value of ΔH for the reaction X₂(g) + 4X₂(g) ⇌ 2XY₄(g) is less than zero.Formation of XY4₄g) will be favoured at','high pressure and low temperature.','X₂(g) + 4X₂(g) ⇌ 2XY₄(g) \nNo of gaseous molecules on the product side is less than the no of gaso=eous molecules on the reactant side . Or in other words Δng < 0. Therefore for forward reaction to be favoured the pressure should increase \nAlso given ΔH < 0 ; Which means exothermic and hence favoured at low temperature.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','high temperature and high pressure','low pressure and low temperature','high temperature and low pressure','high pressure and low temperature.','NEW','11-211','11-187',NULL),
(71151,'chemistry','CH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) ⇌ CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(l), ΔHr = –170.8 kJ mol–1. Which of the following statements is not true?','The equilibrium constant for the reaction is given by Kp =[CO₂]/[CH₄][O₂]','Since ΔHr is negative the reaction is exothermic.\nThe concentration of CO₂(g) and H₂O(l) wonnt be equal at equilibrium . It is clearly visible from the stoichiometric coefficients.\nAddition of CH₄(g) or O₂(g) at equilibrium will cause the reaction to be favoured in the forward direction and hence a shift to right \nAt equilibrium Kp =P[CO₂]/P[CH₄]P[O₂]²','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','The reaction is exothermic.','At equilibrium, the concentrations of CO₂(g) and H₂O(l) are not equal.','The equilibrium constant for the reaction is given by Kp =[CO₂]/[CH₄][O₂]','Addition of CH₄(g) or O₂(g) at equilibrium will cause a shift to the right.','NEW','11-199','11-175',NULL),
(71152,'chemistry','Reaction BaO₂ (s) ⇌ BaO(s) + O₂(g) ; ΔH = +ve. In equilibrium condition, pressure of O₂ depends on','increase temperature on equilibrium','More temperature would lead to more dissociation of BaO₂ to BaO and O₂ .Even though mass of BaO increases in dissociationit wont affect the pressure of Oxygen because BaO is solid. Similarly increase in mass of BaO₂ also wont affect the pressure of oxygen beacse it is also solid . Therefore increase in temperature is the only reason that can increses the pressure of oxygen.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','increase mass of BaO₂','increase mass of BaO','increase temperature on equilibrium','increase mass of BaO₂ and BaO both','NEW','11-211','11-187',NULL),
(71153,'chemistry','For any reversible reaction, if we increase concentration of the reactants, then effect on equilibrium constant','unchange','A + B  ⇌  C + D\nFor such a reaction Keq = [C][D]/[A][B] \nIncrease in concentration would only lead the reaction in forward direction but wont affecct the Keq','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','depends on amount of concentration','unchange','decrease','increase','NEW','11-209','11-185',NULL),
(71154,'chemistry','According to Le Chatelier’s principle, adding heat to a solid and liquid in equilibrium will cause the','amount of solid to decrease','Solid ⇌ Liquid : When temperature is increased more solid is converted to liquid and hence the amount of solid decreases .','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','temperature to increase','temperature to decrease','amount of liquid to decrease','amount of solid to decrease','NEW','11-209','11-185',NULL),
(71155,'chemistry','Which one of the following information can be obtained on the basis of Le Chatelier principle?','Shift in equilibrium position on changing value of a constraint','According to Le Chateliers Principle a object at equilibrium when subjected to any change in its physical parameters will shift its equilibrium so as to counteract the change imposed.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','Dissociation constant of a weak acid','Entropy change in a reaction','Equilibrium constant of a chemical reaction','Shift in equilibrium position on changing value of a constraint','NEW','11-209','11-185',NULL),
(71156,'chemistry','Aqueous solution of which of the following compounds is the best conductor of electric current?','Hydrochloric acid, HCl','HCl is a strong acid and it completely dissociates','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','Hydrochloric acid, HCl','Ammonia, NH₃','Fructose, C₆H₁₂O₆','Acetic acid, C₂H₄O₂','NEW','11-217','11-193',NULL),
(71157,'chemistry','Aqueous solution of acetic acid contains','CH₃COO⁻, H₃O⁺ and CH₃COOH','Acetic acid is a weak acid and is not completely dissociated hence some CH₃COOH are present along with CH₃COO⁻ and H₃O⁺ .Also H⁺ does not exist independently as it is highly reactive. hence B s the correct option .','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','CH₃COO⁻ and H⁺','CH₃COO⁻, H₃O⁺ and CH₃COOH','CH₃COO⁻ , H₃O⁺ and H⁺','CH₃COOH, CH₃COO⁻ and H⁺','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(71158,'chemistry','Conjugate base for Bronsted acids H₂O and HF are','OH⁻ and F⁻ , respectively.','For H₂O and HF the conjugate base are OH⁻ and F⁻ , respectively.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','H₃O⁺ and H₂F⁺, respectively','OH⁻ and H₂F⁺, respectively','H₃O⁺ and F⁻, respectively','OH⁻ and F⁻ , respectively.','NEW','11-215','11-191',NULL),
(71159,'chemistry','Which of the following cannot act both as Bronsted acid and as Bronsted base ?','HCl','Since HCl cannot accept proton it cannot act as a Lewis acid','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','HCO₃⁻','NH₃','HCl','HSO₄⁻','NEW','11-214','11-190',NULL),
(71160,'chemistry','Which of the following fluoro-compounds is most likely to behave as a Lewis base?','PF₃','BF₃ has incomplete octet .Hence it can act as a lewis acid.\nPF₃ has lone pair of electron in Phosphorous . Hence it can donate electron and therefore a Lewis base.\nCF₄ it has completely filled octet and is electron precise.\nSiF₄ the silicon has a vacant d orbital and can act as lewis acid by accomodating electron in d orbital.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','BF₃','PF₃','CF₄','SiF₄','NEW','11-216','11-192',NULL),
(71161,'chemistry','Which of these is least likely to act as a Lewis base?','BF₃','Boron trifluoride due to its incomplete octet acts as a Lewis acid by accepting electron and henceit is least likely to act as a lewis base.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','BF₃','PF₃','CO','F⁻','NEW','11-216','11-192',NULL),
(71162,'chemistry','Which is the strongest acid in the following?','HClO₄','Cl is more elextronegative than Sulphur . Also HClO₄ has the highest oxidation number of +7 . These factors make it a strong acid.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','HClO₄','H₂SO₃','H₂SO₄','HClO₃','NEW','11-217','11-193',NULL),
(71163,'chemistry','Which one of the following molecular hydrides acts as a Lewis acid?','B₂H₆','Compounds which have an incomplete octet (or is short of electrons) can acts as lewis acid . Since B₂H₆ has an incomplete octet around Boron it acts as aLewis acid.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','NH₃','H₂O a','B₂H₆','CH₄','NEW','11-216','11-192',NULL),
(71164,'chemistry','Which of the following molecules acts as a Lewis acid?','(CH₃)₃B','A compound which does not have sufficient electron acts as a lewis acid . Since (CH₃)₃B does not have octes around boron it acts as the lewis acid.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','(CH₃)₂O','(CH₃)₃P','(CH₃)₃N','(CH₃)₃B','NEW','11-216','11-192',NULL),
(71165,'chemistry','Which one of the following statements is not true?','HF is a stronger acid than HCl.','HF has a strong bond between hydrogen anf fluorine and also Fluorine is highly electronegative . Hence it does not donate proton and hence HCl is more acidic than HF','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','Among halide ions, iodide is the most powerful reducing agent.','Fluorine is the only halogen that does not show a variable oxidation state.','HOCl is a stronger acid than HOBr.','HF is a stronger acid than HCl.','NEW','11-217','11-193',NULL),
(71166,'chemistry','Which one of the following compounds is not a protonic acid?','B(OH)₃','B(OH)₃ in water forms a cordinated compund .\nThe reaction goes like B(OH)₃ + H₂O ⇌ [ B(OH)₄]⁻ + H⁺ .Here B(OH)₃ does not donate proton in turn it accepts hydroxyl ion hrom water . Hence it is not a protonic acid.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','B(OH)₃','PO(OH)₃','SO(OH)₂','SO₂(OH)₂','NEW','11-214','11-190',NULL),
(71167,'chemistry','In HS⁻, I⁻ , R-NH₂, NH₃ order of proton accepting tendency will be','R-NH₂ > NH₃ >HS⁻ > I⁻','Basicity of a substance is the tendency to accept a proton . These are decided upon by several factors\nAs the number of alkyl group increases , electron releasing tendencty increases .Also since R-NH₂ has a lone pair of electron on Nitrogen the electron releasing tendency intensify ,and hence it can accept a proton readily\nAs size increases basicity decreases and hence it has only less tendency to donate electron or in other words to accept the proton\nCoupling all these factors the correct order must be ;R-NH₂ > NH₃ >HS⁻ > I⁻','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','I⁻ > NH₃ > R-NH₂ > HS⁻','NH₃ >  R-NH₂ >HS⁻ >I⁻','R-NH₂ > NH₃ >HS⁻ > I⁻','HS⁻ > R-NH₂ >NH₃ >I⁻','NEW','11-217','11-193',NULL),
(71168,'chemistry','Conjugate acid of NH₂⁻ is','NH₃','Base + H⁺ = Conjugate acid\ntherefore NH₂⁻ + H⁺ = NH₃ , which is the conjugate acid','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','NH₄OH','NH₄⁺','NH₂⁻','NH₃','NEW','11-215','11-191',NULL),
(71169,'chemistry','Which compound is electron deficient?','BCl₃','In BCl₃ the central atom Boron is SP2 hybridised and hence it has only 6 electrons in the valence shell making it electron deficient..','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','BeCl₂','BCl₃','CCl₄','PCl₅','NEW','11-216','11-192',NULL),
(71170,'chemistry','The strongest conjugate base is','CH₃COOH','Weakest acid has the strongest conjugate base .CH₃COOH is the weakest acid among the HNO₃, HCl ,H₂SO₄ and hence it has the strongest conjugate base','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','SO₄²⁻','Cl⁻','NO₃⁻','CH₃COOH','NEW','11-217','11-193',NULL),
(71171,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a Lewis acid?','C₂H₄','C₂H₄ is an electron precise molecule and hence it is not a lewis acis. SiF₄ has vaccant d orbitals making it a lewis acid. BF₃ is also electron deficient since Boron has incomplete octet and FeCl₃ is also lewis acid because of incomplete octet of central atom.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','SiF₄','C₂H₄','BF₃','FeCl₃','NEW','11-216','11-192',NULL),
(71172,'chemistry','Repeated use of which one of the following fertilizers would increase the acidity of the soil?','Ammonium sulphate','Ammonium sulphate (NH₄)₂SO₄ is a salt of strong acid that is suphuric acid and a weak base ie NH₄OH . Therefore it is an acidic salt and hence prolonged use of this salt would make the soil acidic.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','Ammonium sulphate','Superphosphate of lime','Urea','Potassium nitrate','NEW','11-217','11-193',NULL),
(71173,'chemistry','Find out the solubility of Ni(OH)₂ in 0.1 M NaOH. Given that the ionic product of Ni(OH)₂ is 2 × 10⁻¹⁵.','2 × 10⁻¹³','Ni(OH)₂ ⇌ Ni²⁺ + 2OH⁻\n  s               s         2s\n\n NaOH ⇌    Na⁺    +      OH⁻\n  (0.1M)     (0.1M)       (0.1M)\n\n[OH⁻] =2s + 1 ≈ 1 (2s<<<<1)\n\nIonic product of Ni(OH)₂ = [Ni²⁺][OH⁻]²\n\n2 × 10⁻¹⁵.=s(0.1)²\ns=2 × 10⁻¹⁵/(0.1)² . Hence s = 2 × 10⁻¹³','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','2 × 10⁻¹³','2 × 10⁻⁸','1 × 10⁻¹³','1 × 10⁸','NEW','11-230','11-206',NULL),
(71174,'chemistry','The pH of 0.01 M NaOH(aq) solution will be','12','NaOH  ⇌       Na⁺       +     OH⁻\n [0.01M]      [0.01M]       [0.01M] \n[OH⁻] =  [0.01M]  = 1× 10⁻² \npOH = -log[OH⁻] = -log (1× 10⁻²) =2\npH = 14 - pOH = 12','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','7.01','2','12','9','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(2000035369,'physics','At what height above the surface of earth, the value of acceleration due to gravity would be half of its value on the surface of earth? (take, radius of the earth is $6400\\,km$ )','B','The ratio,<br>$\\frac{{g\'}}{g} = \\frac{{{R^2}}}{{{{(R + h)}^2}}} = \\frac{1}{2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow R + h = \\sqrt 2 R$<br>$ \\Rightarrow h = (\\sqrt 2  - 1)R$<br>$ \\Rightarrow h = (0.414) \\times 6400{\\rm{ (given) }}$<br>$\\therefore h = 2650\\,km$','Acceleration Due to Gravity of the Earth','Gravitation','Medium',NULL,'$2561\\,km$','$2650\\,km$','$3200\\,km$','$9800\\,km$',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(71175,'chemistry','Following solutions were prepared by mixing different volumes of NaOH and HCl of different concentrations :\n\nA. 60 mL M/10 HCl + 40 mL M/10 NaOH\n\nB. 55 mL M/10 + HCl 45 mL M/10 NaOH\n\nC. 75 mL M/5 HCl + 25 mL M/5 NaOH\n\nD. 100 mL M/5 HCl +100 mL M/5 NaOH\n\npH of which one of them will be equal to 1?','C','For acid base mixture\nif equivalence of acid > equivalence of base then the mixture is acidic .\nif equivalence of acid < equivalence of base then the mixture is basic.\nif equivalence of acid = equivalence of base then the mixture is neutral.\nFor finding that we need to find the difference of equivalence of acid and base\nGenerally N₁V₁ -N₂V₂ = NᵢVᵢ = (Valency factor x M₁)V₁ -(Valency factor x M₂)V₂ = NᵢVᵢ {Since normality = molarity  x valency factor)\nHere \nA. 60 mL M/10 HCl + 40 mL M/10 NaOH \nN₁V₁ -N₂V₂ = NᵢVᵢ ; =((1/10 x 0.06) - (1/10 x 0.04)) =Nᵢ x 0.1 \n Nᵢ = 0.002/0.1 = 0.02 = 2 x 10⁻²\npH = -log[H+] ; [H+] = Normality \nHence pH = -log 2 x 10⁻² = 1.698\n\nB. 55 mL M/10 + HCl 45 mL M/10 NaOH\nN₁V₁ -N₂V₂ = NᵢVᵢ ; =((1/10 x 0.055) - (1/10 x 0.045)) =Nᵢ x 0.1\n Nᵢ = 0.001/0.1 = 0.01 =10⁻²\npH = -log[H+] ; [H+] = Normality \nHence pH = -log 10⁻² = 2\n\nC. 75 mL M/5 HCl + 25 mL M/5 NaOH\nN₁V₁ -N₂V₂ = NᵢVᵢ ; =((1/5 x 0.075) - (1/5 x 0.025)) =Nᵢ x 0.1\n Nᵢ = 0.01/0.1 = 0.1 =10⁻¹\npH = -log[H+] ; [H+] = Normality \nHence pH = -log10⁻¹ = 1\n\nD.100 mL M/5 HCl +100 mL M/5 NaOH\nN₁V₁ -N₂V₂ = NᵢVᵢ ; =((1/5 x 0.1) - (1/5 x 0.1)) =Nᵢ x 0.1\n Nᵢ = 0/0.1 = 0\npH = 7 ; neutral .','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','B','A','D','C','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71176,'chemistry','The percentage of pyridine (C₅H₅N) that forms pyridinium ion (C₅H₅NH⁺ ) in a 0.10 M aqueous pyridine solution (Kb for C₅H₅N = 1.7 × 10⁻⁹) is','0.013%','C₅H₅N + H₂O ⇌ C₅H₅NH⁺ +OH-\nGiven 0.1M solution of pyridine and Kb for C₅H₅N = 1.7 × 10⁻⁹\nα = √(Kb/C)\n      √(1.7 × 10⁻⁹/0.1)=1.3 x 10⁻⁴ \nPercentage = 1.3 x 10⁻⁴  x 100 =0.013%','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','0.006%','0.013%','0.77%','1.6%','NEW','11-221','11-197',NULL),
(71177,'chemistry','What is the pH of the resulting solution when equal volumes of 0.1 M NaOH and 0.01 M HCl are mixed?','12.65','Let V volume of NaOH and HCl be mixed .\nwe know for a mixture of acid and base N₁V₁ -N₂V₂ = NᵢVᵢ and [H+] is given by resultant normality Nᵢ\nHere 0.1 M NaOH and 0.01 M HCl are mixed \n1 mole of a acid is completely neutralised by 1 mole of a base\nHence 0.01 M of HCl is completely nneutralised by 0.001M NaOH ;That means 0.09M NaOH is left \nHence \n0.1 x V - 0.01 x V = Nᵢ x (2V) \n0.09/2 = Nᵢ = 0.045 = [OH-] {since here base is excess and not acid therefore [OH-]}\npOH = -log[OH-] ;[OH-] = Nᵢ \nHence pOH = -log (45 x10⁻²) =1.35\npH = 14 - pOH = 14 - 1.35 = 12.65','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','2','7','1.04','12.65','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71178,'chemistry','Which of the following salts will give highest pH in water?','Na₂CO₃','Na₂CO₃ is a salt of strong base NaOH and weak acid H₂CO₃ . Hence the solution will be basic and pH >7','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','KCl','NaCl','Na₂CO₃','CuSO₄','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71179,'chemistry','Accumulation of lactic acid (HC₃H₅O₃), a monobasic acid in tissues leads to pain and a feeling of fatigue. In a 0.10 M aqueous solution, lactic acid is 3.7% dissociated. The value of dissociation constant, Ka, for this acid will be','1.4×10⁻⁴','α = √Kb/C \nKb = α² × C\nα×100 = 3.7%\nα = 0.037 ; C = 0.1 \nKb = (0.037) ² × 0.1 = 1.369 ×10⁻⁴','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','1.4 ×10⁻⁵','1.4×10⁻⁴','3.7×10⁻⁴','2.8×10⁻⁴','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(71180,'chemistry','At 100°C the Kw of water is 55 times its value at 25°C. What will be the pH of neutral solution? (log 55 = 1.74)','6.13','Kw = [H⁺][OH⁻] that is Kw = 1×10⁻¹⁴  25 degree celcius ; \nGiven at 100 degree celc Kw = 55 × 10⁻¹⁴  \nie Kw = [H⁺][OH⁻] or Kw = [H⁺]² \ntherefore  [H⁺]² = 55 × 10⁻¹⁴  \n[H⁺] = √55 × 10⁻¹⁴  \npH = -log[H⁺] = -log( √55 × 10⁻¹⁴)  \non solving we get 6.13','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','7','7.87','5.13','6.13','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71181,'chemistry','Equimolar solutions of the following substances were prepared separately. Which one of these will record the highest pH value?','BaCl₂','Among the 4 options for same acid HCl with different bases like Ba(OH)₂  Al(OH)₃, LiOH , Be(OH)₂  .Among these bases the strongest is Ba(OH)₂ . Therefore the right answer is BaCl₂','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','BaCl₂','AlCl₃','LiCl','BeCl₂','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71182,'chemistry','What is [H+] in mol/L of a solution that is 0.20 M in CH₃COONa and 0.10 M in CH₃COOH? (Ka for CH₃COOH = 1.8 × ×10⁻⁵)','9.0×10⁻⁶','CH₃COOH ⇌ CH₃COO⁻+ H+\nC – x                  x             x\nlikewise  CH₃COONa  ⇌ CH₃COO⁻    +    Na+\n                   0.2 M               0.2 M            0.2 M\nKa = [CH₃COO⁻][H+]/[CH₃COOH] ......(1)\nKa=1.8 × ×10⁻⁵ ; [CH₃COOH] = C-x ≈ 0.10 M ; [CH₃COO⁻] = 0.2 + x ≈ 0.2M\nSubstituting all these values  (1) we get the [H+] =9.0×10⁻⁶','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','3.5 ×10⁻⁴','1.1×10⁻⁵','1.8×10⁻⁵','9.0×10⁻⁶','NEW','11-227','11-203',NULL),
(71183,'chemistry','The ionization constant of ammonium hydroxide is 1.77 × 10–5 at 298 K. Hydrolysis constant of ammonium chloride is','5.65×10⁻¹⁰','NH₄Cl being a salt of strong acid and weak base, hydrolysis constant is given by the equation\nKh =Kw/Kb  ; \nKb = 1.77 × 10⁻⁵ ;Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴\nSubstituting them we get Kh = 5.65×10⁻¹⁰','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','6.5×10⁻¹²','5.65×10⁻¹³','5.65×10⁻¹²','5.65×10⁻¹⁰','NEW','11-226','11-202',NULL),
(71184,'chemistry','What is the [OH-] in the final solution prepared by mixing 20.0 mL of 0.050 M HCl with 30.0 mL of 0.10 M Ba(OH)₂?','0.10 M','HCl + Ba(OH)2 = BaCl2 + H2O','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','0.40 M','0.0050 M','0.12 M','0.10 M','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(71185,'chemistry','Equal volumes of three acid solutions of pH 3, 4 and 5 are mixed in a vessel. What will be the H+ ion concentration in the mixture?','3.7×10⁻⁴','M= M₁V₁ +M₂V₂+M₃V₃/V₁+V₂+V₃ .......(i)\nGiven pH as 3 , 4 , 5 respectievely for 3 Solutions . we can interfere from pH that [H+] are M₁=10⁻³ ,M₂V₂ =10⁻⁴ ,M₃=10⁻⁵ as pH= -log [H+] \ntherefore from (i) since V₁ = V₂ = V₃ = V and substituting the values of M₁ , M₂ , M₃ we get\nM =','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','3.7×10⁻³','1.11×10⁻³','1.11×10⁻⁴','3.7×10⁻⁴','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71186,'chemistry','A weak acid, HA, has a Ka of 1.00 ×10⁻⁵. If 0.100 mol of this acid is dissolved in one litre of water, the percentage of acid dissociated at equilibrium is closest to','1.00%','For a weak acid degree of dissociation is α = √(Ka/C)\nKa =1.00 ×10⁻⁵ ;C =0.100 mol\nSubstituting them we get α=','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','1.00%','99.90%','0.10%','99.00%','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(71187,'chemistry','Calculate the pOH of a solution at 25°C that contains 1 × 10⁻¹⁰ M of hydronium ions, i.e. H₃O+.','4','Given [H₃O+] = 1 × 10⁻¹⁰ M  ie pH = 10\nat 25°C, pH + pOH = pKw = 14 \nHence pOH = 4','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','4','9','1','7','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71188,'chemistry','The hydrogen ion concentration of a 10⁻⁸ M HCl aqueous solution at 298 K (Kw= 10⁻¹⁴) is','1.0525 × 10⁻⁷M','x','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1.0 × 10⁻⁸M','1.0 × 10⁻⁶ M','1.0525 × 10⁻⁷M','9.525 × 10⁻⁸M','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71189,'chemistry','At 25°C, the dissociation constant of a base, BOH, is 1.0 × 10⁻¹². The concentration of hydroxyl ions in 0.01 M aqueous solution of the base would be.','1.0 x10⁻⁷M','BOH   ⇌   B⁺ +  OH⁻\n  C            0         0 \n  C-Cɑ      Cɑ      Cɑ\n\n[OH⁻] = Cɑ  ;C =0.01 M  ; Kb =  1.0 × 10⁻¹².  ; ɑ = √Kb/C \n[OH⁻]  = C x √Kb/C = √Kb x C \nsubstituting we will get [OH⁻] = 1.0 x10⁻⁷M','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1.0 x10⁻⁵ M.','1.0 × 10⁻⁶ M','2.0 × 10⁻⁶ M','1.0 x10⁻⁷M','NEW','11-221','11-197',NULL),
(71190,'chemistry','Which has highest pH?','Na₂CO₃','NH₄Cl being a salt of strong acid and weak base , the salt will be acidic and pH < 7 \nCH₃COOK is salt of weak acid CH₃COOH and a strong alkali .CH₃COOK + H₂O ⇌ CH₃COOH + K⁺ + OH⁻ ; the solution is basic and pH nearly 8.8\nSodium carbonate (a salt of strong alkali and a weak acid) ; Na₂CO₃ + 2H₂O ⇌ 2Na ⁺ + 2OH⁻ + H₂CO₃ ; pH > 10\nNaNO₃ is a salt of strong base and strong acid, so pH of the solution will be 7.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','CH₃COOK','Na₂CO₃','NH₄Cl','NaNO₃','NEW','11-226','11-202',NULL),
(71191,'chemistry','Ionisation constant of CH₃COOH is 1.7 ×10⁻⁵  and concentration of H+  ions is 3.4 × 10⁻⁴  Then find out initial concentration of CH₃COOH molecules.','6.8×10⁻³','CH₃COOH ⇌ CH₃COO⁻+ H+\nKa = [ CH₃COO⁻][H+] / [CH₃COOH] ....(i)\nKa =1.7 ×10⁻⁵   ; [ H+] = [ CH₃COO⁻] =3.4 × 10⁻⁴    . Substituting these values in (i) we get [CH₃COOH] = 6.8×10⁻³','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','3.4× 10⁻⁴','3.4×10⁻³','6.8× 10⁻⁴','6.8×10⁻³','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(71192,'chemistry','Correct relation between dissociation constants of a dibasic acid is','Ka₁>Ka₂','H₂A ⇌ HA⁻ + H⁺  .....Ka₁\nHA⁻ ⇌  A⁻ + H⁺  ......Ka₂\nIn first step H⁺ comes from a neutral molecule while in second step it comes from a charged ion making the removal of H⁺  difficult . \nHence Ka₁>Ka₂','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','Ka₁=Ka₂','Ka₁>Ka₂','Ka₁<Ka₂','Ka₁=1/Ka₂','NEW','11-224','11-200',NULL),
(71193,'chemistry','Which statement is wrong about pH and H⁺ ?','Adding 1 N solution of CH₃COOH and 1 N solution of NaOH, pH will be seven.','After mixing 1 N solution of CH₃COOH (weak acid) and 1 N NaOH (strong base), the resulting solution will have free OH⁻ions since . Thus, pH will be greater than 7.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','pH of neutral water is not zero.','Adding 1 N solution of CH₃COOH and 1 N solution of NaOH, pH will be seven.','[H+] of dilute and hot H₂SO₄ is more than concentrated and cold H₂SO₄.','Mixing solution of CH₃COOH and HCl, pH will be less than 7.','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71194,'chemistry','The concentration of [H+] and concentration of [OH– ] of a 0.1 aqueous solution of 2% ionised weak acid is [ionic product of water = 1 × 10⁻¹⁴]','2 ×10⁻³ and 5 ×10⁻¹²','Kw = [H⁺][OH⁻] \n[H⁺] = Cɑ =  0.1 x 0.02 ( since given ionisation is 2 %)\n[H⁺] = 0.002 = 2 x 10⁻³ \nKw = [H⁺][OH⁻] \n[OH⁻] = Kw/[H⁺]  = 1 × 10⁻¹⁴ /  2 x 10⁻³  = 5 ×10⁻¹²','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','2 ×10⁻³ and 5 ×10⁻¹²','1 ×10⁻³ and 3 ×10⁻¹¹','0.02 ×10⁻³  and 5 ×10⁻¹¹','3 ×10⁻² and 4 ×10⁻¹³','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(71195,'chemistry','The hydride ion H⁻ is stronger base than its hydroxide ion OH⁻. Which of the following reaction will occur if sodium hydride (NaH) is dissolved in water?','H⁻ + H₂O ⇌ OH⁻ + H₂',NULL,'Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','H⁻ + H₂O ⇌ No reaction','H⁻ + H₂O ⇌ H₂O','H⁻ + H₂O ⇌ OH⁻ + H₂','None of these','NEW','11-217','11-193',NULL),
(71196,'chemistry','The ionic product of water at 25°C is 10⁻¹⁴ Its ionic product at 90°C will be,','1 x 10⁻¹²','Ionic product of wate is a direct consequence of temperature . Hence as temperature increases ionic product of water increases which gives (d) as correct option','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1 x 10⁻¹⁴','1 x 10⁻¹⁶','1 x 10⁻²⁰','1 x 10⁻¹²','NEW','11-217','11-193',NULL),
(71197,'chemistry','If α is dissociation constant, then the total number of moles for the reaction, 2HI → H₂ + I₂ will be','2','2HI →  H₂ +  I₂  \ninitially                     2          0      0\nafter dissociation    2-2α     α      α\n\ntherefore total no of moles after dissociation is  2-2α + α + α = 2','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','1-α','2','2-α','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(71198,'chemistry','The pH value of N/10 NaOH solution is','13','NaOH is a strong base and it completely dissociates into Na⁺ and OH⁻\nGiven N/10 NaOH that means   [OH⁻] =0.1M = 10⁻¹M\npOH = -log [OH⁻] = -log10⁻¹ = 1 \npH = 14 = pOH \n      = 14 - 1 = 13','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','12','13','10','11','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71199,'chemistry','The pH value of a 10M solution of HCl is','less than 0','Since HCl is a strong acid it completely ionises and the [H⁺] = [Cl⁻] = [HCl] =10M\npH = -log [H⁺] = -log10 = -1 \nHence pH is less than 0','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','equal to 1','equal to 2','less than 0','equal to 0','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71200,'chemistry','At 80°C, distilled water has [H₃O⁺] concentration equal to 1 × 10⁻⁶ mole/litre. The value of Kw at this temperature will be','1 x 10⁻¹²','Kw = [H⁺][OH⁻] \n [H⁺] = [OH⁻] =1 × 10⁻⁶ mole/litre.\nHence Kw = 1 × 10⁻⁶ x 1 × 10⁻⁶ =1 × 10⁻¹²','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1 x 10⁻¹²','1 x 10⁻¹⁵','1 x 10⁻⁶','1 x 10⁻⁹','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71201,'chemistry','0.1 M solution of which one of these substances will act basic?','Sodium borate','The salt sodium borate is made from strong base and weak acid . Therefore sodium borate is basic','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','Sodium borate','Ammonium chloride','Calcium nitrate','Sodium sulphate','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71202,'chemistry','The compound whose water solution has the highest pH is','Na₂CO₃','NaCl being the salt of a strong acid and a strong base is neutral . \nNH₄Cl and NaHCO₃ being the salts of strong acids have low pH . Na₂CO₃ is basic in nature and hence have highest pH','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','NaCl','NaHCO₃','Na₂CO₃','NH₄Cl','NEW','11-226','11-202',NULL),
(71203,'chemistry','Which will make basic buffer?','100 mL of 0.1 M HCl + 200 mL of 0.1 M NH₄OH','HCl +  NH₄OH  ⇌  NH₄Cl\n100 mL of 0.1 M HCl + 200 mL of 0.1 M NH₄OH\nThat is10 millimole of HCl and 20 millimole of NH₄OH\nit is clear that HCl is a limiting agent.\nsolution will contain NH₄OH(base) and NH₄Cl( salt of weak acid and strong base) . Hence the solution will be basic in nature','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','100 mL of 0.1 M HCl + 100 mL of 0.1 M NaOH','50 mL of 0.1 M NaOH + 25 mL of 0.1 M CH₃COOH','100 mL of 0.1 M CH₃COOH + 100 mL of 0.1 M NaOH','100 mL of 0.1 M HCl + 200 mL of 0.1 M NH₄OH','NEW','11-227','11-203',NULL),
(71204,'chemistry','Which one of the following pairs of solutions is not an acidic buffer?','HClO₄ and NaClO₄','Acidic buffer is a mixture of a weak acid and its\nsalt with a strong base. HClO4 is the strongest acid among the given.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','CH₃COOH and CH₃COONa','H₂CO₃ and Na₂CO₃','H₃PO₄and Na₃PO ₄','HClO₄ and NaClO₄','NEW','11-227','11-203',NULL),
(71205,'chemistry','The dissociation constant of a weak acid is 1 × 10⁻⁴.  In order to prepare a buffer solution with a pH = 5, the [Salt]/[Acid] ratio should be','10 : 1','pH = pKa + log [SALT]/[ACID]............(i)\n\n5 = -logKa + log[SALT]/[ACID]\n5 = -log (1 × 10⁻⁴) + log[SALT]/[ACID]\n5 = 4 + log[SALT]/[ACID]\n1 = log[SALT]/[ACID]       (since log10=1 we can equate)\n10 = [SALT]/[ACID]','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','4 : 5','10 : 1','5 : 4','1 : 10','NEW','11-227','11-203',NULL),
(71206,'chemistry','Buffer solutions have constant acidity and alkalinity because','these give unionised acid or base on reaction with added acid or alkali','these give unionised acid or base on reaction with added acid or alkali','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','these give unionised acid or base on reaction with added acid or alkali','acids and alkalies in these solutions are shielded from attack by other ions','they have large excess of H⁺ or OH– ions','they have fixed value of pH.','NEW','11-227','11-203',NULL),
(71207,'chemistry','A buffer solution is prepared in which the concentration of NH₃ is 0.30 M and the concentration of NH₄+ is 0.20 M. If the equilibrium constant, Kb for NH₃ equals 1.8 × 10⁻⁵, what is the pH of this solution? (log 2.7 = 0.43)','9.43','pOH = pKb + log [SALT]/[BASE]............(i)\nNH₄+ = 0.20 M which s the salt\nNH₃ = 0.30 M   which is the base\nKb = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵\nlog 2.7 = 0.43\nsubstituting them in (i) we get pOH =4.56\npH= 14 - pOH = 14 - 4.56 = 9.43','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','9.08','9.43','11.72','8.73','NEW','11-227','11-203',NULL),
(71208,'chemistry','In a buffer solution containing equal concentration of B⁻ and HB, the Kb for B⁻ is 10⁻¹⁰ The pH of buffer solution is','4','pOH = pKb + log [B⁻]/[HB]\nAlso given [B⁻] = [HB] =10⁻¹⁰ \nSubstituting we get pOH = pKb + log [10⁻¹⁰ ]/[10⁻¹⁰ ]\nthat is pOH = pKb\npKb = 10 since Kb = 10⁻¹⁰ (pKb = -logKb)\nHence pOH = 10 \npH= 14 - pOH = 14 - 10 = 4','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','10','7','6','4','NEW','11-227','11-203',NULL),
(71209,'chemistry','Which of the following pairs constitutes a buffer?','HNO₂ and NaNO₂','HCl HNO₃ are strong acid NaOH is a strong base and hence these are not used in buffer . HNO₂ (weak acid) and NaNO₂ (salt formed by the combination of a strong base NaOH)  is an example of acidic buffer.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','HCl and KCl','HNO₂ and NaNO₂','NaOH and NaCl','HNO₃ and NH₄NO₃','NEW','11-227','11-203',NULL),
(71210,'chemistry','The rapid change of pH near the stoichiometric point of an acid-base titration is the basis of indicator detection. pH of the solution is related to ratio of the concentrations of the conjugate acid (HIn) and base (In⁻) forms of the indicator by the expression','Log [In⁻]/[HIn]  =  pH- pKᵢₙ','Let us consider the formation of a salt of weak acid and strong base \nIn⁻ + H₂O ⇌ HIn + OH- \nHere Kh = [HIn ][OH- ]/[ In⁻ ]............(1)\n\nWe can also have HIn ⇌  In⁻ + H+ and H₂O ⇌ H+ + OH- \n\nKIn = [In⁻][H+]/HIn and Kw = [ H+][OH-]\n\nKw/KIn  = [OH-][HIn]/[ [In⁻]..........(2)\n\n(1) and (2) is same ie Kw/KIn  = Kh\n\n{Kw x [In⁻]} / {KIn x [HIn]} =[OH-]\n\nlog[OH-] = logKw - logKin + log[ [In⁻]/[HIn]\n\n-pOH = -pKw + pKin + log[ [In⁻]/[HIn]\n\npKw - pOH = pKin + log[ [In⁻]/[HIn]\n\npH = pKin + log[ [In⁻]/[HIn]        {pH + pOH = pKw }\n\nlog[ [In⁻]/[HIn] = pH - pKin','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','Log [In⁻]/[HIn] = pKᵢₙ -pH','Log [HIn]/[In⁻] = pKᵢₙ -pH','Log [HIn]/[In⁻] = pH- pKᵢₙ','Log [In⁻]/[HIn]  =  pH- pKᵢₙ','NEW','11-217','11-193',NULL),
(71211,'chemistry','Solution of 0.1 N NH₄OH and 0.1 N NH₄Cl has pH 9.25. Then find out pKb of NH₄OH.','4.75','According to Henderson equation, the pH of a basic buffer, pH = 14 – pKb -log [SALT]/[BASE]\npKb = 14 – pH - log [SALT]/[BASE]\npKb = 14 – 9.25 -log [0.1]/[0.1]\npKb = 14 – 9.25 = 4.75\npKb of NH₄OH = 4.75','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','9.25','4.75','3.75','8.25','NEW','11-226','11-202',NULL),
(71212,'chemistry','A physician wishes to prepare a buffer solution at pH = 3.85 that efficiently resists changes in pH yet contains only small concentration of the buffering agents. Which of the following weak acids together with its sodium salt would be best to use?','Acetoacetic acid (pKa = 3.58)','pH = pKa + log [SALT]/[BASE]; this is the equation for pH of an acidic buffer .\npH = 3.85 ;  pKa=2.58.\nSubstitute values in the above equation.\nHence, [salt]:[acid]=1:1','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','2, 5-Dihydroxybenzoic acid (pKa = 2.97)','Acetoacetic acid (pKa = 3.58)','m-Chlorobenzoic acid (pKa = 3.98)','p-Chlorocinnamic acid (pKa = 4.41)','NEW','11-227','11-203',NULL),
(71213,'chemistry','The pH value of blood does not appreciably change by a small addition of an acid or a base, because the blood','contains serum protein which acts as buffer','Blood maintains its pH by the buffer present in it that is','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','can be easily coagulated','contains iron as a part of the molecule','is a body fluid','contains serum protein which acts as buffer','NEW','11-226','11-202',NULL),
(71214,'chemistry','pH of a saturated solution of Ca(OH)₂ is 9. The solubility product (Ksp) of Ca(OH)₂s','0.5 × 10⁻¹⁵','pH= 9 Hence pOH = 14 – 9 = 5 (since pH +pOH = 14)\nTherefore [OH-] = 10⁻⁵ ( pOH = -log[OH-] )\nCa(OH)₂ ⇌ Ca²⁺     +   2OH-\n                     S               2S\n                    0.5x10⁻⁵     10⁻⁵\nKsp = [Ca²⁺] [OH-]²\n[Ca²⁺] = 0.5x10⁻⁵     [OH-] = 10⁻⁵ \nSubstituting them we get Ksp = 0.5 × 10⁻¹⁵','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','0.5× 10⁻¹⁰','0.5 × 10⁻¹⁵','0.25× 10⁻¹⁰','0.125 × 10⁻¹⁵','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71215,'chemistry','The molar solubility of CaF₂ (Ksp = 5.3 × 10⁻¹¹ ) in 0.1 M solution of NaF will be','5.3 ×  10⁻⁹ mol L–1','CaF₂ ⇌ Ca²⁺ + 2F⁻\n               S        2S  \nNaF ⇌ Na⁺ +     F-\n              0.1M    0.1M\n[Ca²⁺] = s ‘ [F⁻] = 2s+1 = 0.1 \nKsp = [Ca²⁺][F⁻] \nThat is  5.3 × 10⁻¹¹ = s x (0.1) ² = 5.3 ×  10⁻⁹ mol L–1 =s , solubility','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','5.3 × 10⁻¹¹ mol L–1','5.3 ×  10⁻⁸mol L–1','5.3 ×  10⁻⁹ mol L–1','5.3 x10⁻¹⁰ mol L–1','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71216,'chemistry','The solubility of BaSO4 in water is 2.42 × 10⁻³ g L–1 at 298 K. The value of its solubility product (Ksp) will be .(Given molar mass of BaSO4 = 233 g mol–1)','1.08 × 10⁻¹⁰ mol² L⁻²','Given solubility of BaSO4 =2.42 × 10⁻³ g L–1 =(2.42 × 10⁻³)/233 mol L–1=1.04 x10⁻⁵  mol/ L\nKsp  OF BaSO4 = [Ba²⁺] [SO₄²⁻] = s x s = s²\ns = 1.04 x10⁻⁵  mol/ L \nHence Ksp = 1.08 × 10⁻¹⁰ mol² L⁻²','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1.08 × 10⁻¹⁰ mol² L⁻²','1.08 × 10⁻¹² mol² L⁻²','1.08× 10⁻¹⁴ mol² L⁻²','1.08 × 10⁻⁸ mol² L⁻²','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71217,'chemistry','Concentration of the Ag+ ions in a saturated solution of Ag₂C₂O₄ is 2.2 × 10⁻⁴ mol L–1. Solubility product of Ag₂C₂O₄ is','5.3× 10⁻¹²','Ag₂C₂O₄ ⇌ 2Ag⁺ + C₂O₄ ²⁻\n                   2s           s \nKsp =  [Ag⁺]²[C₂O₄ ²⁻] = 4s³\n solubility of  [Ag⁺] =  2.2 × 10⁻⁴ mol L–1 that is 2s = 2.2 × 10⁻⁴ mol L–1 ; s = 1.1 × 10⁻⁴ mol L–1\nThen Ksp = 4 x (1.1 × 10⁻⁴ )³mol L–1 = 5.3× 10⁻¹²','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','2.66× 10⁻¹²','4.5×  10⁻¹¹','5.3× 10⁻¹²','2.42×  10⁻⁸','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71218,'chemistry','The solubility of AgCl(s) with solubility product 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁰ in 0.1 M NaCl solution would be','1.6x10⁻⁹','AgCl   ⇌   Ag⁺     +   Cl- . If the solubility of AgCl is s then \n\nAgCl   ⇌   Ag⁺     +   Cl-\n s              s            (s +  0.1)   (chlorine from NaCl also included)\n\nKsp = s(s + 0.1)\n1.6 × 10⁻¹⁰ =   s(s + 0.1)\nOn solving we get s=1.6x10⁻⁹','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','1.26x10⁻⁵','1.6x10⁻⁹','1.6x10⁻¹¹','zero','NEW','11-230','11-206',NULL),
(71219,'chemistry','MY and NY₃, two nearly insoluble salts, have the same Ksp values of 6.2 ×  10⁻¹³ at room temperature. Which statement would be true in regard to MY and NY₃?','The molar solubility of MY in water is less than that of NY₃.','For MY : Ksp = s₁² ; s₁ = √Ksp = √ 6.2 × 10⁻¹³ mol L–1 = 7.87 × 10⁻⁷ mol L–1\nFor  NY₃ : Ksp = (s₂)²(3s₂)³ ;s₂ = ∜(6.2 × 10⁻¹³/27) = 3.89 × 10⁻⁴ mol L–1\nHence, molar solubility of MY in water is less than that of NY₃.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','The salts MY and NY₃ are more soluble in 0.5 M KY than in pure water.','The addition of the salt of KY to solution of MY and NY₃ will have no effect on their solubilities.','The molar solubilities of MY and NY₃ in water are identical.','The molar solubility of MY in water is less than that of NY₃.','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71220,'chemistry','The Ksp of Ag₂CrO₄, AgCl, AgBr and AgI are respectively, 1.1 × × 10⁻¹², 1.8  × 10⁻¹⁰, 5.0 × 10⁻¹³, 8.3 × 10⁻¹⁷. Which one of the following salts will precipitate last if AgNO₃ solution is added to the solution containing equal moles of NaCl, NaBr, NaI and Na₂CrO₄?','Ag₂CrO₄','Ksp of Ag₂CrO₄, AgCl, AgBr and AgI are respectively, 1.1 × × 10⁻¹², 1.8  × 10⁻¹⁰, 5.0 × 10⁻¹³, 8.3 × 10⁻¹⁷ which is given . Let us assume the solubility is s . Then Ksp of Ag₂CrO₄, AgCl, AgBr and AgI in terms of solubility will be 4s³ , s² ,s² and s² respectievely .\nFor Ag₂CrO₄ = Ksp = 4s³ Hence s = 0.65 x 10⁻⁴ \nFor AgCl = Ksp =s² Hence s = 1.34 x 10⁻⁵ \nFor AgBr  = Ksp =s² Hence s = 0.71 x 10⁻⁶\nFor AgI  = Ksp =s² Hence s =  0.9 x 10⁻⁸\nThe highest value is for Ag₂CrO₄ . Hence its precipitation occurs at last ..','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','AgBr','Ag₂CrO₄','AgCl','AgI','NEW','11-230','11-206',NULL),
(71221,'chemistry','Using the Gibbs’ energy change, ΔG° = +63.3 kJ, for the following reaction, Ag₂CO₃(s)  ⇌  2Ag⁺ (aq)+ CO₃²⁻ (aq) the Ksp of Ag₂CO₃(s) in water at 25 °C is (R = 8.314 J K–1 mol–1)','8.0 x 10⁻¹²','ΔG° = +63.3 kJ = -2.303RTlogKsp \nΔG° =-2.303RTlogKsp\n+63.3 kJ  =-2.303 × 8.314 × 298logKsp\nlogKsp = (+63.3 × 10³ )/-2.303 × 8.314\n           = -11.09\nKsp = antilog(-11.09) =8.182 x 10⁻¹²','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','3.2 x 10⁻²⁶','8.0 x 10⁻¹²','2.9 x 10⁻³','7.9  x 10⁻²','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71222,'chemistry','The values of Ksp of CaCO₃ and CaC₂O₄ are 4.7 × 10⁻⁹ and 1.3 × 10⁻⁹ respectively at 25°C. If the mixture of these two is washed with water, what is the concentration of Ca²⁺ ions in water?','7.746× 10⁻⁵M','CaF₂ ⇌ Ca²⁺ + CO₃²⁻\n               y          y\n CaC₂O₄ ⇌ Ca²⁺ +  C₂O₄²⁻\n                     z           z\n[Ca²⁺] = x + y \nx(x+y) =  4.7 × 10⁻⁹  ......(1) \ny(x+y) = 1.3 × 10⁻⁹   .......(2)\n(1)/(2) gives x/y = 3.6\nx=3.6y\nx(x+y) =  4.7 × 10⁻⁹  and x=3.6y\nx² + xy =4.7 × 10⁻⁹ and x=3.6y . on solving we get x=1.38× 10⁻⁵\nsimilarly we can get y by substituting the value of x in (1)\nwe get y = 1.38× 10⁻⁵\n Also [Ca²⁺] = x + y =1.38× 10⁻⁵ +1.38× 10⁻⁵ = 7.746× 10⁻⁵M','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','5.831× 10⁻⁵M','6.856× 10⁻⁵M','3.606× 10⁻⁵M','7.746× 10⁻⁵M','NEW','11-230','11-206',NULL),
(71223,'chemistry','Identify the correct order of solubility in aqueous medium.','Na₂S > ZnS > CuS','Solubility of a salt is a result of the solubility product . Greater the solubility product greater is the solubility .From solubility product we can get the correct order of the solubility as Na₂S > ZnS > CuS\n.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','Na₂S > CuS > ZnS','Na₂S > ZnS > CuS','CuS > ZnS > Na₂S','ZnS > Na₂S > CuS','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71224,'chemistry','pH of a saturated solution of Ba(OH)₂ is 12. The value of solubility product (Ksp) of Ba(OH)₂ is','5.0× 10⁻⁷','pH = 12 ; -log[H+] = 12\nHence [H⁺] =10⁻¹⁴\nwe also know that  [OH-][H⁺] = 10⁻¹⁴\nTherefore [OH-] = 10⁻¹⁴/10⁻¹²   .\n[OH-] =10⁻²\nBa(OH)₂ ⇌ Ba²⁺     +   2OH-\n                     S               2S\n                    \nKsp = [Ba²⁺] [OH-]²    \n   2S=10⁻²\ns = 0.5 x 10⁻²\nKsp = 4S³        (s  x (2s)³)\n        =4 x (0.5 x 10⁻²)³=5.0× 10⁻⁷','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','3.3× 10⁻⁷','5.0× 10⁻⁷','4.0× 10⁻⁶','5.0× 10⁻⁶','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71225,'chemistry','In qualitative analysis, the metals of group I can be separated from other ions by precipitating them as chloride salts. A solution initially contains Ag⁺ and Pb²⁺ at a concentration of 0.10 M. Aqueous HCl is added to this solution until the Cl⁻ concentration is 0.10 M. What will the of Ag⁺ and Pb²⁺ be at equilibrium? ( Ksp for AgCl = 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰ ;Ksp for PbCl₂ = 1.7 × 10⁻⁵)','[Ag⁺] = 1.8 × 10⁻⁹ M, [Pb²⁺] = 1.7 × 10⁻³M','Ksp for AgCl = 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰\nKsp = [ Ag⁺][Cl-]  =1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰\n[ Ag⁺] = 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰/0.1 =[Ag⁺] = 1.8 × 10⁻⁹ \n\nKsp for PbCl₂ = 1.7 × 10⁻⁵\nKsp = [ Pb²⁺][Cl-]²  =1.7 × 10⁻⁵ \n[ Pb²⁺] =1.7 × 10⁻⁵ /(0.1)² = 1.7 × 10⁻³M','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','[Ag⁺] = 1.8 × 10⁻⁷ M, [Pb²⁺] = 1.7 × 10⁻⁶ M','[Ag⁺] = 1.8 × 10⁻¹¹ M, [Pb²⁺] = 8.5 × 10⁻⁵M','[Ag⁺] = 1.8 × 10⁻⁹ M, [Pb²⁺] = 1.7 × 10⁻³M','[Ag⁺] = 1.8 × 10⁻¹¹ M, [Pb²⁺] = 1.7 × 10⁻⁴M','NEW','11-230','11-206',NULL),
(71226,'chemistry','H₂S gas when passed through a solution of cations containing HCl precipitates the cations of second group of qualitative analysis but not those belonging to the fourth group. It is because','presence of HCl decreases the sulphide ion concentration','H₂S is passed in presence of HCl in the qualitative analysis of cations of second group. Due to common ion effect ionisaytion of H₂S  decreases (since H+ is a common ion in both H₂S and HCl ) and hence less sulphide ions are obtained . But these sulphide ions are sufficient enough for the precipitation of second group cations in form of their sulphides due to lower value of their Ksp .\nHere 4th group cations are not precipitated in similar way because their solubility product is high (Ksp)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','presence of HCl decreases the sulphide ion concentration','solubility product of group II sulphides is more than that of group IV sulphides','presence of HCl increases the sulphide ion concentration','sulphides of group IV cations are unstable in HCl.','NEW','11-230','11-206',NULL),
(71227,'chemistry','The solubility product of a sparingly soluble salt AX₂ is 3.2 × 10⁻¹¹ .Its solubility (in moles/L) is','2× 10⁻⁴','AX₂ ⇌   A²⁺     +   2X-\n              S             2S\nGiven Ksp = 3.2 × 10⁻¹¹ \n                  =[  A²⁺ ][X- ]²\n                  =s(2s)²=4s³\n4s³ = 3.2 × 10⁻¹¹ \ns= 2× 10⁻⁴','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','5.6× 10⁻⁶','3.1× 10⁻⁴','2× 10⁻⁴','4× 10⁻⁴','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71228,'chemistry','The solubility product of AgI at 25°C is 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁶ mol² L⁻¹. The solubility of AgI in 10⁻⁴  N solution of KI at 25°C is approximately (in mol L⁻¹)','1.0 × 10⁻¹²','KI   ⇌   K⁺     +   I-\nFor KI solution 1N= 1M\nKI   ⇌   K⁺     +   I-\n             10⁻⁴      10⁻⁴ \nAgI   ⇌   Ag⁺     +   I-\n               s            s + 10⁻⁴  (Since AgI is present in 1N KI solution)\nFor AgI Ksp = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁶  =  (s )(s + 10⁻⁴ ) =  10⁻⁴s + s²     (s² is very less compared to10⁻⁴s hence we neglect) \n1.0 × 10⁻¹⁶ = 10⁻⁴s \ns = 1.0 × 10⁻¹²','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1.0 × 10⁻¹⁶','1.0 × 10⁻¹²','1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰','1.0 × 10⁻⁸','NEW','11-230','11-206',NULL),
(71229,'chemistry','Solubility of MX₂ type electrolytes is 0.5 × 10⁻⁴ mol/lit., then find out Ksp of electrolytes.','5 x 10⁻¹³','Let the solubility of the salt be s\nMX₂ ⇌ M²⁺ + 2X⁻\n          s        2s\nKsp = 2s² \nGiven s = 0.5 × 10⁻⁴ mol/lit.\nHence Ksp =5 x 10⁻¹³','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','5 x 10⁻¹²','25 x 10⁻¹⁰','1 x 10⁻¹³','5 x 10⁻¹³','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71230,'chemistry','Solubility of M₂S salt is 3.5 × 10⁻⁶ then find out solubility product.','1.7 × 10⁻¹⁶','Let the solubility of the salt be s\nMX₂ ⇌ S²⁺ + 2M⁻\n          s        2s\nKsp = 4S³        (s  x (2s)²)\n\nGiven s = 3.5 × 10⁻⁶ mol/lit.\nHence Ksp =1.7 × 10⁻¹⁶','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1.7 × 10⁻⁶','1.7 × 10⁻¹⁶','1.7 × 10⁻¹⁸','1.7 x 10⁻¹²','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71231,'chemistry','The solubility of a saturated solution of calcium fluoride is 2 × 10⁻⁴ moles per litre. Its solubility product is','32 x 10⁻¹²','Let the solubility of the salt be s\nCaF₂ ⇌ Ca²⁺ + 2F⁻\n          s        2s\nKsp = 4S³        (s  x (2s)²)\nGiven s =  2 × 10⁻⁴ mol/lit.\nHence Ksp =32 x 10⁻¹²','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','22×  10⁻¹¹','14 × 10⁻⁴','2 x 10⁻¹²','32 x 10⁻¹²','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71232,'chemistry','The solubility product of CuS, Ag₂S and HgS are 10⁻³¹, 10⁻⁴⁴  and 10⁻⁵⁴  respectively. The solubilities of these sulphides are in the order','CuS > Ag₂S > HgS','Greater the solubility product greater is the solubility of that salt.Hence the order is CuS > Ag₂S > HgS','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','HgS > Ag₂S > CuS','CuS > Ag₂S > HgS','Ag₂S > CuS > HgS','Ag₂S > HgS > CuS','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71233,'chemistry','The solubility of AgCl will be minimum in','0.01M CaCl₂','As compared to other salts there are greater number of Cl⁻ ions in CaCl₂.Due to common ion effect the Le Chateliers principle applies and the reaction takes place in the backward direction suppressinng the ionisation of the salt and hence its solubility.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','0.01M CaCl₂','pure water','0.001M AgNO₃','0.01M NaCl','NEW','11-230','11-206',NULL),
(71234,'chemistry','Which one of the following is most soluble?','MnS (Ksp = 7 × 10⁻¹⁶)','Greater the solubility product greater is the solubility of that salt.Hence the order is MnS','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','Bi₂S₃ (Ksp = 1 × 10⁻⁷⁰)','Ag₂S (Ksp = 6 × 10⁻⁵¹)','CuS (Ksp = 8 × 10⁻³⁷)','MnS (Ksp = 7 × 10⁻¹⁶)','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71235,'chemistry','The pKb of dimethylamine and pKa of acetic acid are 3.27 and 4.77 respectively at T (K). The correct option for the pH of dimethylammonium acetate solution is','7.75','Dimethylammonium acetate is a salt of dimethylamine and acetic acid which are weak base and weak acid.\nHence pH of such kind of salt is given by the equation pH= 7 + 1/2pKa - 1/2pKb\npKb of dimethylamine is 3.27 and pKa of acetic acid is 4.77\nSubstituting the values we get pH = 7.75','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','8.5','5.5','7.75','6.25','NEW','11-223','11-199',NULL),
(71236,'chemistry','Hydrolysis of sucrose is given by the following reaction : Sucrose + H₂O Glucose + Fructose If the equilibrium constant (Kc) is 2 ×10¹³  at 300 K, the value of ΔrG° at the same temperature will be','–8.314 J mol–1K–1 × 300 K × ln(2 ×10¹³ )','ΔG = ΔG° + RTlnQ\nAt equilibrium ΔG = 0 and Q=Kc Hence ΔG° = - RTlnKc = –8.314 J mol–1K–1 × 300 K × ln(2 ×10¹³ )','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','–8.314 J mol–1K–1 × 300 K × ln(2 ×10¹³ )','8.314 J mol–1K–1 × 300 K × ln(2 ×10¹³ )','8.314 J mol–1K–1 × 300 K × ln(3 ×10¹³ )','–8.314 J mol–1K–1 × 300 K × ln(4 ×10¹³ )','NEW','11-208','11-184',NULL),
(71237,'chemistry','If A + B ⇋ C + D (Constant = K1) and E + F ⇌ G + H (Constant = K2), then what is the constant of reaction for C + D + E + F ⇒ product?','K1 * K2','To determine the equilibrium constant for the overall reaction, we need to combine the given equilibrium reactions in a way that yields the desired product. Here\'s how we can achieve this:\n\n Reverse the first reaction:\nC + D ⇋ A + B (Constant = 1/K1)\n\n Add the reversed first reaction to the second reaction:\nC + D + E + F ⇋ A + B + G + H (Constant = 1/K1 * K2)\n\n Remove common terms from both sides:\nC + D + E + F ⇒ G + H (Constant = 1/K1 * K2)\n\n Invert the constant:\nC + D + E + F ⇒ product (Constant = K1 * K2)\n\n Therefore, the equilibrium constant for the overall reaction C + D + E + F ⇒ product is K1 * K2.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','K1 * K2','K2 / K1','K1 / K2','None of these','NEW','11-201','11-177',NULL),
(71238,'chemistry','Consider the following reaction taking place in 1L capacity container at 300 K.\nA+B⇌C+D\nIf one mole each of A and B are present initially and at equilibrium 0.7 mol of C is formed, then the equilibrium constant \n(Kc)\n for the reaction is','5.4',NULL,'Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','9.7','1.2','6.7','5.4','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71239,'chemistry','Which of the following species behaves as a Lewis acid?','Boron trifluoride (BF3)','Analysis of options:\nStannous chloride (SnCl2): While Sn can accept a lone pair, its tendency is weaker compared to BF3. Additionally, SnCl2 readily undergoes hydrolysis in water, complicating its Lewis acid behavior.\nStannic chloride (SnCl4): Sn in SnCl4 has no empty orbitals and is surrounded by electronegative Cl atoms, making it an electron pair donor (Lewis base) rather than an acceptor.\nBoron trifluoride (BF3): BF3 has only three F atoms bonded to B, leaving an empty p orbital on B. This empty orbital readily accepts electron pairs, making it a strong Lewis acid.\n Therefore, based on the definition and analysis, only BF3 exhibits the characteristics of a Lewis acid.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','Stannous chloride (SnCl2)','Stannic chloride (SnCl4)','Boron trifluoride (BF3)','Both stannous chloride (SnCl2) and stannic chloride (SnCl4)','NEW','11-216','11-192',NULL),
(71240,'chemistry','Which one of the following orders correctly represents the increasing acid strengths of the given acids?\n\nHClO < HClO₂ < HClO₃\nHClO < HCl < HClO₃ < HClO₂\nHClO₂ < HClO₃ < HClO < HCl\nHClO₃ < HClO₂ < HClO < HCl','HClO < HClO₂ < HClO₃','More oxygen atoms in oxyacids of chlorine = more stable conjugate base = stronger acid. So, increasing order of acid strength is HClO < HClO₂ < HClO₃.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','HClO < HClO₂ < HClO₃','HClO < HCl < HClO₃ < HClO₂','HClO₂ < HClO₃ < HClO < HCl','HClO₃ < HClO₂ < HClO < HCl','NEW','11-224','11-200',NULL),
(71241,'chemistry','If 0.01 M acetic acid solution is 1% ionised, then pH of this acetic acid solution is :','4',NULL,'Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','3','2','4','1','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(71242,'chemistry','The salt solution that is basic in nature is:','4. Sodium acetate.','Sodium acetate wins the basicity race!\n\nAcetate (CH3COO⁻) readily donates protons (H⁺) from acetic acid (weak), raising OH⁻ from water and increasing pH.\nAmmonium salts release ammonium (NH4⁺), a weak acid that slightly lowers pH (not enough to overcome water\'s natural basicity).\nNitrate (NO3⁻) doesn\'t accept protons, leaving water\'s basicity untouched.\n Sodium acetate\'s strong conjugate base is the key to its basic crown.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1. Ammonium chloride.','2. Ammonium sulphate.','3. Ammonium nitrate.','4. Sodium acetate.','NEW','11-226','11-202',NULL),
(71243,'chemistry','The pH of the solution containing 50 mL each of 0.10 M sodium acetate and 0.01 M acetic acid is: [Given pKa of CH3COOH = 4.57]','5.57','Acetate readily donates protons, keeping pH near pKa (4.57) of its partner, acetic acid. So, pH hovers around 5.57, not lower (like option 1) or higher (like option 4).','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','2.57','5.57','3.57','4.57','NEW','11-227','11-203',NULL),
(71244,'chemistry','For the reaction 3 O₂(g) ⇌ 2 O₃(g) at 298 K, Kc = 3.0 × 10⁻⁵⁹. If the equilibrium concentration of O₂ is 0.040 M, what is the concentration of O₃ in M?\n\n1.2 × 10²¹\n4.38 × 10⁻³²\n1.9 × 10⁻⁶³\n2.4 × 10³¹','4.38 × 10⁻³²','O₂ eats itself to make O₃, but not efficiently (Kc tiny). We have excess O₂ (0.04 M), so Kc tells us O₃ won\'t be much either. Calculate using stoichiometry and square root (Kc has [O₃]²), and you\'ll land on 2. 4.38 × 10⁻³² M. Other options are way off due to Kc and initial [O₂]!','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','1.2 × 10²¹','4.38 × 10⁻³²','1.9 × 10⁻⁶³','2.4 × 10³¹','NEW','11-199','11-175',NULL),
(71301,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true regarding equilibrium \n\n(i)Phase transfer reactions are also examples of chemical equilibrium because there is a change  involved at the interphase\n\n(ii)In the starting of any reaction the rate of forward reaction is greater than rate of backward reaction provided concentration of products greater than concentration of reactants.\n\n(iii)The equilibrium is usually dynamic but at times it can become static.\n\n(iv)It is due to the static equilibrium that the rate of the reactants and products remain a constant .','Only (ii) is true','Phase transfer reactions are examples of physical equilibrium .\nIf the amount of reactant is greater than the amount of product the rate of forward reaction will be greater to attain equilibrium.\nThe equilibrium is always dynamic otherwise the concentration of the substance would not have remained the same. \nThe concentration of the substances at equilibrium remains unchanged as a consequence of its dynamic nature .(NCERT chemistry class 11 ; Equilibrium)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Only (i) and (ii) is true','(i) (ii) and (iv) are true (iii) is false','(i) (ii) (iii) and (iv) are true','Only (ii) is true','NEW','11-193','11-169',NULL),
(71302,'chemistry','Regarding ice and water equilibrium which statements are true ?\n\n(i)It is an example of solid liquid equilibrium .\n\n(ii)The rate of transfer of ice molecules into water is equal to the rate of transfer of water molecules into ice at equilibrium.\n\n(iii)At any point after equilibrium the ratio of mass of ice and water should remain a constant.','All are true','The equilibrium involving ice and water are examples of solid liquid equilibria. At interphase the rate of transfer of water molecules into ice and ice to water molecules are equal . Since the rates of the change taking place at equilibrium are equal the ratio of mass of the ice and water should remain a constant any time after equilibrium is obtained.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 195; solid liquid equilibrium)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Only (ii) is true','Only (i) is true','(ii) and (iii) are true','All are true','NEW','11-193','11-169',NULL),
(71303,'chemistry','Assertion: At a particular temperature and pressure the substance with more volatility will have a less boiling point .\n\nReason: The factors affecting the liquid evaporation does also includes the nature of the substance .','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is  the correct explanation for assertion','More the volatility of a substance higher is the vapor pressure . We know that a substance boils when the vapor pressure is equal to the atmospheric pressure. Higher is the volatility of a substance means it takes only less time for the vapor pressure to become equal to the atmospheric pressure . Hence lower will be  the boiling point since the atmospheric pressure is easily obtained.\nVolatility is a consequence of evaporation. Easily evaporable substance volatilizes fast . Therefore factors which affect evaporation like the nature of the liquid ,the amount of the liquid, and the temperature also affects volatility and in turn boiling point .(NCERT chemistry class 11; Equilibrium)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion is true reason is false','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is  the correct explanation for assertion','Assertion is false but reason is true','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation for assertion','NEW','11-194','11-170',NULL),
(71304,'chemistry','Assertion :Molar concentration of pure solid or liquid is never seen in the expression of the equilibrium constant of a reaction \n\nReason : The concentration of solids or liquids are often affected and does not  remain a constant','Assertion is true reason is false','Molar concentration of pure solids or liquids never appear in the expression of equilibrium constant because it is always a constant (independent of amount present)(NCERT chemistry class 11;Equilibrium)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation for assertion','Assertion is true reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is  the correct explanation for assertion','NEW','11-203','11-179',NULL),
(71305,'chemistry','Match the following diagram(n0701)','(i) –(c) ;(ii) – (b) ; (iii) – (a)','Kp = Kc(RT) ᵟⁿ\nWhen no of gaseous molecules on reactant and product side equals Δn = 0 and hence Kp = Kc (RT) ⁰ That is Kp=Kc.\n\nIn Habers Process N₂₍g₎+ 3H₂₍g₎⇌ 2NH₃₍g₎  ; Here Δn=-2 . Therefore Kp= Kc (RT)⁻² i.e. Kp = Kc/(RT) ²                                \n\nCaCO₃ ₍ₛ₎ ⇌ CaO ₍ₛ₎+ CO₂ ₍g₎   . Here Kp = P(CO₂) Since CaCO₃ and CaO are solids they do not have partial pressures \nFeO ₍ₛ₎+CO ₍g₎ ⇌ Fe ₍ₛ₎+ CO₂ ₍g₎    . Here  Kp =   P(CO₂)/P(CO) since FeO and Fe are solids they need not be considered .  \n\nC₍ₛ₎+ CO₂ ₍g₎ ⇌ 2CO ₍g₎   Here Kp =P(CO) ²/P(CO₂) Since C is solid it does not have partial pressure .\n(NCERT chemistry class 11; Equilibrium)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(i) –(b) ;(ii) – (a) ; (iii) – (c)','(i) –(c) ;(ii) – (b) ; (iii) – (a)','(i) –(a) ;(ii) – (b) ; (iii) – (c)','(i) –(c) ;(ii) – (a) ; (iii) – (a)','NEW','11-202','11-178','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/71305q.png'),
(71306,'chemistry','Assertion :If we add more of products on to equilibrium mixture faster is the rate of backward reaction at that instant untill it attains new equilibrium.\n\nReason : Equilibrium constant gives information about the rate of the reaction .','Assertion is true reason is false.','When concentration of product increaes according to Le Chateliers Principle the reaction shifts in the backward direction .The rate of the backward reaction will be faster than forward reaction untill the effect is nullified\nIt  is important to note that an equilibrium constant does not give any information about the rate at which the equilibrium is reached..(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 205 ; paragraph 3).','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is  the correct explanation for assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is true reason is false.','Assertion is false but reason is true.','NEW','11-205','11-181',NULL),
(71307,'chemistry','If we drop some radioactive sugar into a beaker of saturated normal sugar solution which of the following are true?\n\n(i)Radioactivity can be observed after sometime due to dynamic nature of equilibrium.\n\n(ii)We see radioactivity since there is an exchange of radioactive and non-radioactive sugar molecules at the interphase.\n\n(iii)Radioactivity is observed which stops after sometimes.\n\n(iv)No radioactivity is observed because we are adding radioactive sugar to saturated solution.\n\n(v)The exchange of radioactive and non radioactive sugar molecules takes place at interphase but the amount never attains a constant value.','(i) and (ii) are true (iii) (iv) and (v) are false','In a saturated solution, a dynamic equilibrium exits between the solute molecules in the solid state and in the solution: Sugar (solution) Sugar (solid), and the rate of dissolution of sugar = rate of crystallization of sugar. Equality of the two rates and dynamic nature of equilibrium has been confirmed with the help of radioactive sugar. If we drop some radioactive sugar into saturated solution of non-radioactive sugar, then after some time radioactivity is observed both in the solution and in the solid sugar. Initially there were no radioactive sugar molecules in the solution but due to dynamic nature of equilibrium there is exchange between the radioactive and non -radioactive sugar molecules between the two phases. The ratio of the radioactive to non- radioactive molecules in the solution increase till it attains a constant value.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 195 ; paragraph 1 and 2).','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(i),(ii) and (iii) are true (iv) and (v) are false','(i),(ii) and (iv) are true (iii) and (v) are false','(i) and (ii) are true (iii) (iv) and (v) are false','(i) and (v) are true (ii) (iii) and (iv) are false.','NEW','11-195','11-171',NULL),
(71308,'chemistry','Allotropic changes like C(graphite) ⇌ C(diamond) falls under which category of equilibrium ?.','Physical equilibrium','Allotropic changes are examples of physical equilibrium (NCERT chemistry class 11 ; Equilibrium)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Physical equilibrium','Chemical equilibrium','Ionic equilibrium','Allotropic changes don’t attain equilibrium','NEW','11-193','11-169',NULL),
(71309,'chemistry','Find out if the reverse reaction  for the reaction 2NO₍g₎ + O₂₍g₎ ⇌ 2NO₂₍g₎  is spontaneous or not.\nΔH = -120KJ ; ΔS = -150J at 298K','Non Spontaneous','ΔG = ΔH – TΔS \nTherefore ΔG = -120 X 10³ -(298 x -15 ) = -120KJ + 4.470KJ = -113.5KJ\nHence for reverse reaction ΔG = 113.5KJ i.e. reverse reaction is non spontaneous at this temperature . (NCERT chemistry class 11 ; Equilibrium)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Spontaneous','Non Spontaneous','Spontaneous above 298K','Cant be predicted','NEW','11-208','11-184',NULL),
(71310,'chemistry','With time for a reaction, what happens to the reaction quotient and the rate of the forward reaction','Increases and decreases respectively','The rate of the forward reaction decreases and the rate of the backward reaction increases with time so as to attain equilibrium and eventually they become equal. The value of the reaction quotient keeps on increasing until equilibrium as the amount of product keeps on increasing .(NCERT chemistry class 11 ; Equilibrium)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Increases and increases respectively','Increases and decreases respectively','Decreases and decreases respectively','Decreases and increases respectively','NEW','11-206','11-182',NULL),
(71311,'chemistry','Which of the following reactions can attain equilibrium? \n\n(i)Precipitation reactions\n\n(ii)Neutralization reactions \n\n(iii)Reactions in open vessels \n\n(iv)Haber’s process \n\n(v)Sublimation of ammonium chloride','(i) (ii) (iii) can’t attain equilibrium while (iv) and (v) can','Precipitation reactions, Neutralization reactions, reactions in open vessels cant attain equilibrium while phase change reaction , sublimation Haber’s process .etc. takes place in both forward and backward direction and can attain equilibrium.(NCERT chemistry class 11 ; Equilibrium)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(i) (ii) (iv) can attain equilibrium while (iii) and (v) can’t','(i) (iii) (iv) can attain equilibrium while (ii) and (v) can’t','(i) (ii)  can’t attain equilibrium while (iii) (iv) and (v) can’t','(i) (ii) (iii) can’t attain equilibrium while (iv) and (v) can','NEW','11-198','11-174',NULL),
(71312,'chemistry','The ratio of Kp/Kc for the following reaction is Ni ₍ₛ₎ + 4CO₍g₎  ⇌ Ni(CO)₄ ₍g₎','1/(RT)³','For this reaction Kc = [Ni(CO)₄]/[CO]⁴\n\nKp = Kc(RT) ᵟⁿ . Hence Kp = {[Ni(CO)₄]/[CO]⁴}(RT)⁻³ =  [Ni(CO)₄]/[CO]⁴(RT)³\n\nKp/Kc  =  {[Ni(CO)₄]/[CO]⁴(RT)³ }/[Ni(CO)₄]/[CO]⁴ = 1/(RT)³(NCERT chemistry class 11 ; Equilibrium)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1/ RT','RT','1/(RT)³','(RT)³','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71313,'chemistry','H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI ;Kc =57 at 700K  . What can we interfere from the value of Kc at this temperature?','Appreciable amount of both reactant and product present','If Kc > 10³ , products predominate over reactants, i.e., if Kc is very large, the reaction proceeds nearly to completion. If Kc < 10⁻³ , reactant predominate over products , i.e., if Kc is very small, the reaction proceeds rarely. If Kc between 10³  and 10⁻³ , appreciable concentration of both reactants and products are present.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 205 ; paragraph 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Products dominates reactants.','Reactant dominates products.','Appreciable amount of both reactant and product present','We cant interfere anything just by looking into the value of Kc.','NEW','11-205','11-181',NULL),
(71314,'chemistry','Keq = ₑ(–ΔG ° /RT)\nFor  the given relation between Gibbs free energy and equilibrium constant which is false?','If ΔG ° > 0  spontaneous reaction and K>1','• If ∆G ° < 0, then –∆G ° /RT is positive, and ₑ(–∆G ° /RT)   >1, making K >1, which implies a spontaneous reaction or the reaction which proceeds in the forward direction to such an extent that the products are present predominantly.\n• If ∆G 0 > 0, then –∆G ° /RT is negative, and ₑ(–∆G ° /RT) < 1, that is , K < 1, which implies a non-spontaneous reaction or a reaction which proceeds in the forward direction to such a small degree that only a very minute quantity of product is formed.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 208 ; paragraph 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','If ΔG ° < 0 forward reaction is favored','If ΔG ° > 0 non spontaneous reaction','If ΔG ° < 0 ;K>1 and more products are formed','If ΔG ° > 0  spontaneous reaction and K>1','NEW','11-208','11-184',NULL),
(71315,'chemistry','Assertion : In Haber’s process Le Chatelier’s principle helps in increasing the yield of the reaction.\n\nReason: In Haber’s process continuous removal of products maintains Qc at a value more than Kc.','Assertion is true reason is false','Contiuous removal of product favours the reaction in forward direction according to le chateliers principle because the equilibrium shifts towards right and at any time the value of Qc will be less than that of Kc and hence it increases the yield of reaction.(NCERT chemistry class 11; Equilibrium).','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Assertion is true reason is false','Both assertion and reason and true but reason is not the correct explanation for assertion','Assertion is false but reason is true','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation for assertion','NEW','11-209','11-185',NULL),
(71316,'chemistry','Value of Kc is independent of?\n\n(i)Catalyst \n\n(ii)Temperature \n\n(iii)Initial concentration\n\n(iv)Pressure\n\n(v)Direction in which equilibrium is attained','Independent on (i) (iii) (iv) and (v) but depends upon (ii)','The value of equilibrium constant is independent of initial concentrations of the reactants and products.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 205 ; paragraph 1)\nEquilibrium constant is temperature dependent having one unique value for a particular reaction represented by a balanced equation at a given temperature. (NCERT chemistry class 11; page 205 ; paragraph 1)\nAlso other factors like pressure , volume , concentration of the substance present in the mixture , direction in which the equilibrium is attained does not have any effect on equilibrium constant(NCERT chemistry class 11 ; Equilibrium)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Independent on (i) and (iii) but depends upon (ii) (iv) and (v)','Independent on (ii) (iii) and (iv) but depends upon (i) and (v)','Independent on (i) (iii) (iv) and (v) but depends upon (ii)','Independent on (i) and (iv) but depends upon (ii) (iii) and (v)','NEW','11-205','11-181',NULL),
(71317,'chemistry','For the following reactions how pressure changes affect the product formation?\n\n(i)H₂₍g₎ + I₂₍g₎ ⇌ 2HI₍g₎   \n\n(ii) SO₂₍g₎ + Cl₂₍g₎ ⇌ SO₂Cl₂₍g₎','Pressure does not affect the reaction (i) but favours forward reaction in the reaction (ii)','If the pressure increases the reaction shifts towards the direction in which the no of moles gaseous molecules is less . In first reaction the no of  gaseous molecules on either side of the reaction is same eand hence pressure does not have any effect here. In the reaction (ii) the no of gaseous moles on the product side is less than that of reactant side and hence equilibrium shifts from left to right favouring more product formation.(NCERT chemistry class 11 ; Equilibrium)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','No effect in both the reaction','Pressure does not affect the reaction (i) but favours forward reaction in the reaction (ii)','Pressure favours forward reaction in (i) and backward reaction in (ii)','Pressure increases the product by favouring the forward reaction in the reactions (i) and (ii)','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71318,'chemistry','In contacts process the oxidation of SO₂ to give SO₃  ΔH= -198KJ . For a high yield of sulphuric acid the favourable conditions are \n(2SO₂₍g₎ + O₂₍g₎ ⇌ 2SO₃₍g₎)','High pressure ; low temperature','For high yield of Sulphuric acid we need a large amount of SO₃ . Since in the reaction forward reaction is exothermic low temperature favours the forward reaction . \nAlso if pressure is increased the reaction shifts in the direction where number of gaseous molecules is less and in this case it is in the forward direction . Hence low temperature and high pressure favours high yield .(NCERT chemistry class 11 ; Equilibrium)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'1','mcq','High pressure ; low temperature','High pressure ; high temperature','Low pressure ;low temperature','Low pressure; high temperature','NEW','11-208','11-184',NULL),
(71319,'chemistry','Assertion : Catalyst does not affect the equilibrium constant .\n\nReason : Catalyst lowers the activation energy of forward and backward reaction .','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation for assertion','Catalyst does not affect equilibrium.\nCatalyst lowers the activation energy for the forward and reverse reactions by exactly the same amount. Catalyst does not affect the equilibrium composition of a reaction mixture. It does not appear in the balanced chemical equation or in the equilibrium constant expression.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 212 ; paragraph 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Assertion is true reason is false','Both assertion and reason and true but reason is not the correct explanation for assertion','Assertion is false but reason is true','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation for assertion','NEW','11-212','11-188',NULL),
(71320,'chemistry','A ₍g₎ + B ₍g₎⇌ C ₍g₎  + heat . In the equilibrium mixture for a high concentration of C , what should be the condition of temperature and pressure.','Low temperature and high pressure','Since the reaction is exothermic low temperature favours forward reaction and hence a high concentration of C  \nWhen presuure is increased the reaction shifts in the direction where no of gaseous molecules is less . Here Δn=-1 ie no of gaseous molecueles on the product side is less than that of the reactant side and hence the reaction takes place in the forward direction if pressure is increased.(NCERT chemistry class 11 ; Equilibrium)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Low temperature and high pressure','High pressure and high temperature','High temperature and low pressure','low pressure and low temperature','NEW','11-211','11-187',NULL),
(71321,'chemistry','Which among the following can act as a Lewis acid?','SnCl₄','CCl₄ is electron precise and it neither it can act as lewis acid or lewis base \nAgCl an ionic salt and it does not act as acid or base\nNH₃ has lone pair of electron and it act as a lewis base\nSnCl₄ has vacant d orbitals and it can accept a pair of electron and accommodate in the d orbital and hence it is a lewis acid.\n(NCERT chemistry class 11 ; Equilibrium)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CCl₄','AgCl','SnCl₄','NH₃','NEW','11-216','11-192',NULL),
(71322,'chemistry','Choose the correct relation between pH and acidity from the following','pH  ∝ 1/(Acidity)','As the value of pH increases acidity decreases and as the value of pH decreases acidity increases .(NCERT chemistry class 11 ; Equilibrium)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'1','mcq','pH ∝ acidity','pH ∝ 1/Acidity','pH ∝ (Acidity)','pH  ∝ 1/(Acidity)','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71323,'chemistry','Which of the following can act as both acid and base?','HC₂O₄⁻','For a substance to act as both acid and base it should have the ability to donate as well  as accept H⁺ . Among the following only HC₂O₄⁻ can both donate as well as accept H⁺.(NCERT chemistry class 11 ; Equilibrium)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','HC₂O₄⁻','H₃PO₄','PO₄³⁻','H₃PO₃','NEW','11-217','11-193',NULL),
(71324,'chemistry','Choose the correct statements out of the following.\n\n(i)        Molarity of pure water is 55.5M\n\n(ii)        pKw = pH – pOH\n\n(iii)        Kw is temperature independent\n\n(iv)        Kw = [H⁺][OH⁻] \n\n(v)        The change in  pH with temperature can’t be ignored','(ii) (iii) (v) are false (i) (iv)  are true','Molarity of pure water is 55.55M.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 217 ; paragraph 8)\n\npKw = pH + pOH. (NCERT chemistry class 11 ; Equilibrium)\n\nThe value of Kw is temperature dependent as it is an equilibrium constant.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 217 ; paragraph 9)\n\nIonic product of water is Kw = [H⁺][OH⁻].(NCERT chemistry class 11 ; Equilibrium)\n\npH scale is logarithmic, a change in pH by just one unit also means  change in [H+] by a factor of 10. Similarly, when the hydrogen ion concentration, [H+] changes by a factor of 100, the value of pH changes by 2 units. Now you can realize why the change in pH with temperature is often ignored.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 218 ; paragraph 8)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(i) (ii) (iii) are true (iv) and (v) are false','(i) (iii) (v) are true (iv) and (ii) are false','(ii) (iii) (v) are true (i) and (iv) are false','(ii) (iii) (v) are false (i) (iv)  are true','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71325,'chemistry','Calculate the pH for 10⁻⁴M Ba(OH) ₂','10','pH = -log[H⁺] . Also pH + pOH = 14\n\nGiven 10⁻⁴M Ba(OH)₂ \n\nFor strong bases i.e whose [OH⁻] is > 10⁻⁶ pOH = -log[OH⁻] = -log(10⁻⁴) = -(-4 x log[10]) = 4\n\npOH = 4 ; pH + pOH = 14 ;hence pH = 14 – pOH = 14 – 4 = 10 (NCERT class 11 chemistry ; Equilibrium)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','4','10','14','11','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71326,'chemistry','Ice + heat  ⇌ water . If more pressure is applied then which of the following is true','More water is formed','More pressure means more water will be formed hence forward reaction takes place.(NCERT class 11 chemistry ; Equilibrium)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'1','mcq','More ice is formed','More water is formed','More water will evaporate','No effect will be there','NEW','11-210','11-186',NULL),
(71327,'chemistry','Calculate the pH of 10⁻⁸ M aqueous HCl solution .','6.9','pH = -log[H⁺] . Also pH + pOH = 14\n\n If [H⁺] is < 10⁻⁶ that means the acid is diluted . Here we have to consider the concentration of [H⁺] in water also needs to considered ie [H⁺] = 10⁻⁸ +10⁻⁷= (10 x10⁻⁸)+ 10⁻⁸ = 11 x10⁻⁸\n\npH = -log[H⁺] = -log [11 x10⁻⁸] = -log11 + -log 10⁻⁸=  -log 10⁻⁸ +-log11 =8-log11  ie a value less than 8 and acidic .\n7 is neutral pH . and above 7 is basic . Therefore the option left with us 6.9. (NCERT class 11 chemistry ; Equilibrium)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','6.9','7.1','8','7.5','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71328,'chemistry','If the solubility product of Hg₂SO₄ = 7.4 x 10⁻⁷ Then what is concentration of Hg⁺ ions in it?','1.14x 10⁻²','Hg₂SO₄ ⇌ 2Hg⁺ + SO₄²⁻\n                    2S      S\nKsp=[Hg⁺]²[SO₄²⁻]\n      = (2s)²(s) = 4s³\nGiven 7.4 x 10⁻⁷ = 4s³\ns³ = 1.85 x 10⁻⁷\ns=0.57 x 10⁻² \nHg⁺ = 2s = 2 x (0.57 x 10⁻² ) = 1.14 x 10⁻² (NCERT class 11 chemistry ; Equilibrium)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.57 x 10⁻²','1.14x 10⁻²','1.85 x 10⁻⁷','2.5 x 10⁻⁷','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71329,'chemistry','Assertion : The solubilities of salts of weak acids like phosphates increases at lower pH .\n\nReason: At lower pH the concentration of anion decreases due to its protonation.','Both assertion and reason and true but reason is  the correct explanation for assertion','The solubility of salts of weak acids like phosphates increases at lower pH. This is because at lower pH the concentration of the anion decreases due to its protonation. This in turn increase the solubility of the salt so that Ksp=Qsp.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 230 ; paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion is true reason is false','Both assertion and reason and true but reason is  the correct explanation for assertion','Both assertion and reason and true but reason is not the correct explanation for assertion','Assertion is false reason is true','NEW','11-226','11-202',NULL),
(71509,'chemistry','Kₐ₁ , Kₐ₂ and Kₐ₃  are the respective ionization constant for the following reactions.\n\n    H₂S ⇌ H⁺ + HS⁻\n    HS⁻ ⇌ H⁺ + S²⁻\n    H₂S ⇌ 2H⁺ + S²⁻\nThe correct relationship between Kₐ₁ , Kₐ₂ and Kₐ₃ is','Kₐ₃ = Kₐ₂ × Kₐ₁','For the reaction ,H₂S ⇌ H⁺ + HS⁻\nKₐ₁ = [H⁺][HS⁻]/[H₂S]\n\nHS⁻ ⇌ H⁺ + S²⁻\nKₐ₂ =[H⁺][S²⁻]/[HS⁻] \n\nWhen the above two reaction are added, their equipment constant are multiplied.Thus\n\n Kₐ₃ = [H⁺]²[S²⁻]/[H₂S] = Kₐ₂ × Kₐ₁\n\nHence ,  Kₐ₃ = Kₐ₂ × Kₐ₁','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','Kₐ₃ = Kₐ₂ × Kₐ₁','Kₐ₃ = Kₐ₂ + Kₐ₁','Kₐ₃ = Kₐ₂ - Kₐ₁','Kₐ₃ = Kₐ₂ / Kₐ₁','NEW','11-201','11-177',NULL),
(71330,'chemistry','Which of the following are true . \n\n(i)        Solubility product of a salt is independent on temperature.\n\n(ii)       More is the strength of the HA bond less is its acidity \n\n(iii)       For a polyprotic acid the magnitude of higher order ionization constants are greater than the lower order ones\n\n(iv)       Substances like codeine , nicotine and quinine have weakly acidic property\n\n(v)        Solubility product is useful in purification of common salt','(i) and (iv) are wrong (ii) (iii) and (v) are correct','When strength of H-A bond decreases, that is, the energy required to break the bond decreases, HA becomes a stronger acid. (NCERT chemistry class 11; page 224 ; paragraph 5)\n\nAs temperature increases the solubility of any substance changes and hence the solubility product is definitely affected by temperature.(NCERT chemistry class 11; chapter equilibrium)\n\nIt can be seen that higher order ionization constants (Ka2 , Ka3) are smaller than the lower order ionization constant (Ka1) of a polyprotic acid.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 224 ; paragraph 2)\n\nMethylamine, codeine, quinine and nicotine all behave as very weak bases due to their very small Kb.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 221; paragraph 5)\n\nIf we take a saturated solution of sodium chloride and pass HCl gas through it, then sodium chloride is precipitated due to increased concentration (activity) of chloride ion available from the dissociation of HCl. Sodium chloride thus obtained is of very high purity and we can get rid of impurities like sodium and magnesium sulphates.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 230 ; paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Most challenging questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(iii) (ii) are wrong  (iv) and (v) are correct','(i) (ii) are wrong (iii) (iv) and (v) are correct','(i) and (iv) are wrong (ii) (iii) and (v) are correct','(i) (ii) are correct (iii) (iv) and (v) are wrong','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71401,'chemistry','The molar solubility of Cd(OH)₂ is 1.84 × 10⁻⁵ M  in water. The expected solubility of Cd(OH)₂ in a  buffer solution of pH = 12 is [JEE (Main)-2019]','2.49 × 10⁻¹⁰ M','Cd(OH)₂⇌ Cd²⁺ + 2OH⁻  \n                 s             2s\nAt equilibrium, Kₛₚ = S(2S)² = 4S³\n→ Kₛₚ = 4 × (1.84 × 10⁻⁵)³\nSolubility in buffer solution having pH = 12\n[OH]⁻ = 10⁻²\n\nCd(OH)₂⇌ Cd²⁺ + 2OH⁻  \n                 s\'        2s\' + 10⁻² ≈ 10⁻²\n\n→ Kₛₚ = 4 × (1.84 × 10⁻⁵)³ = s\'(10⁻²)²\ns⁻ = 2.49 × 10⁻¹⁵/10⁻⁴ = 2.49 × 10⁻¹⁰ M.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','1.84 × 10⁻⁹ M','6.23 × 10⁻¹¹ M','(2.49/1.84)10⁻⁹ M','2.49 × 10⁻¹⁰ M','NEW','11-227','11-203',NULL),
(71402,'chemistry','The INCORRECT match in the following is  [JEE (Main)-2019]','△G° < 0, K < 1','△G° = –RT lnK\n→ If K > 1 then △G° < 0\nIf K < 1 then △G° > 0\nIf K = 1 then △G° = 0','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','△G° = 0, K = 1','△G° < 0, K < 1','△G° > 0, K < 1','△G° < 0, K > 1','NEW','11-208','11-184',NULL),
(71403,'chemistry','In which one of the following equilibria,  KP ≠ KC? [JEE (Main)-2019]','2C(s) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2CO(g)','→ Kₚ = K꜀ .(RT)^△ng\n→ If △ng ≠ 0 then Kₚ ≠ K꜀\n2C(s) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2CO(g)\n△ng = + 1\nKₚ = K꜀.(RT)¹','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','2HI(g) ⇌ H₂(g) + I₂(g)','2NO(g) ⇌ N₂(g) + O₂(g)','NO₂(g) + SO₂(g) ⇌ NO(g) + SO₃(g)','2C(s) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2CO(g)','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71404,'chemistry','What is the molar solubility of Al(OH)₃ in 0.2 M  NaOH solution? Given that, solubility product of  Al(OH)₃ = 2.4 × 10⁻²⁴ [JEE (Main)-2019]','3 × 10⁻²²','Al(OH)₃ ⇌ Al³⁺ + 3OH⁻ , Kₛₚ = 2.4 × 10⁻²⁴ \n                s       0.2s+3s    ≈ 0.2\n\ns(0.2)³ = 2.4 × 10⁻²⁴\n\ns = 2.4 × 10⁻²⁴/8 × 10⁻³ = 3 × 10⁻²² mol/L','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','3 × 10⁻²²','12 × 10⁻²³','3 × 10⁻¹⁹','12 × 10⁻²¹','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71405,'chemistry','For the reaction,\n2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g),\n△H = –57.2 kJ /mol and K꜀ = 1.7 × 10¹⁶.\nWhich of the following statement is INCORRECT?','The equilibrium constant is large suggestive of\n reaction going to completion and so no\n catalyst is required.','2SO₂ (g) + O₂ (g)⇌ 2SO₂ (g) \nK꜀\n = 1.7 × 10¹⁶ i.e. reaction goes to completion.\nEquilibrium constant has no relation with catalyst.\nCatalyst only affects the rate with which a reaction\nproceeds.\nFor the given reaction, catalyst V₂O₅ is used to\n speed up the reaction (Contact process)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','The equilibrium constant is large suggestive of\n reaction going to completion and so no\n catalyst is required.','The addition of inert gas at constant volume will not affect the equilibrium constant.','The equilibrium will shift in forward direction as\n the pressure increases.','The equilibrium constant decreases as the\n temperature increases.','NEW','11-208','11-184',NULL),
(71406,'chemistry','The pH of a 0.02 M NH₄Cl solution will be [given  Kb(NH₄OH) = 10⁻⁵ and log2 = 0.301] [JEE (Main)-2019]','5.35','NH₄ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄OH + H⁺\n\n0.22-x     x          Kₕ = 10⁻¹⁴/10⁻⁵ = 10⁻⁹\n≈0.22\n\nKₕ = x²/0.02\n10⁻⁹×2×10⁻² = x²\nx = √20 × 10⁻⁶\npH = -log(√20 × 10⁻⁶)\npH = 5.35','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','2.65','5.35','4.35','4.65','NEW','11-227','11-203',NULL),
(71407,'chemistry','Consider the following statements\n\n(a) The pH of a mixture containing 400 mL of  0.1 M H₂SO₄ and 400 mL of 0.1 M NaOH will  be approximately 1.3.\n\n(b) Ionic product of water is temperature dependent.\n\n(c) A monobasic acid with Kₐ = 10⁻⁵ has a  pH = 5. The degree of dissociation of this acid  is 50%.\n\n(d) The Le Chatelier’s principle is not applicable to  common-ion effect.\n\nThe correct statements are [JEE (Main)-2019]','(a), (b) and (d)','→                   H₂SO₄ + NaOH → NaHSO₄ + H₂O\nInitial moles.    0.04       0.04\n                                                           0.04            0.04\nNaHSO₄ → Na⁺ + H⁺ + SO₄\n²⁻\n[H⁺] = 0.04/0.80 = 0.05M ; pH = 1.3\n\n→Ionic product of water increases with increase of\n temperature because ionisation of water is\n endothermic.\n\n→ HA ⇌ H⁺+ A⁻\nC(1 – α)  Cα Cα  pH = 5 & Ka = 10⁻⁵\n\n10⁻⁵ = (Cα²/1-α); C= 2 × 10⁻⁵ and α = 0.5','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','(a), (b) and (d)','(b) and (c)','(a) and (b)','(a), (b) and (c)','NEW','11-218','11-194','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/71407q.png'),
(71408,'chemistry','In an acid-base titration, 0.1 M HCl solution was  added to the NaOH solution of unknown strength.\n Which of the following correctly shows the change  of pH of the titration mixture in this experiment?  [JEE (Main)-2019]. Refer diagram(n0704)','A','The pH of NaOH is more than 7 and during the\n titration it decreases so graph (A) is correct.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','11-218','11-194','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/71408q.png'),
(71409,'chemistry','For the following reactions, equilibrium constants\nare given :\nS(s) + O₂(g) ⇌ SO₂(g) ; K₁ = 10⁵²\n2S(s) + 3O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₂(g) ; K₂ = 10¹²⁹\nThe equilibrium constant for the reaction,\n 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g)⇌ 2SO₃(g)  is\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','10²⁵','eqⁿ 1    S + O₂ → SO₂        K₁ = 10⁵²\neqⁿ 2    2S + 3O₂ → 2SO₃ K₂ = 10¹²⁹\neqⁿ 3    2SO₂ + O₂ → 2SO₃\neqⁿ 3 = eqⁿ 2 – 2 (eqⁿ 1)\n = 10¹²⁹/(10⁵²)² = 10²⁵','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','10²⁵','10¹⁵⁴','10¹⁸¹','10⁷⁷','NEW','11-201','11-177',NULL),
(71410,'chemistry','If solubility product of Zr₃(PO₄)₄ is denoted by Kₛₚ and its molar solubility is denoted by S, then which  of the following relation between S and Kₛₚ is  correct?','S = (Kₛₚ/6912)⅐','Zr₃(PO₄)₄ ⇌ 3Zr⁴⁺+ 4PO₄³⁻\n\nKₛₚ = [Zr⁴⁺]³[PO₄³⁻]⁴ = (3S)³(4S)⁴\nKₛₚ = 6912 S⁷\nS = (Kₛₚ/6912)⅐','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','S = (Kₛₚ/6912)⅐','S = (Kₛₚ/216)⅐','S = (Kₛₚ/144)⅙','S = (Kₛₚ/929)⅑','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71411,'chemistry','If Kₛₚ of Ag₂CO₃ is 8 × 10⁻¹², the molar solubility  of Ag₂CO₃ in 0.1 M AgNO₃ is [JEE (Main)-2019]','8 × 10⁻¹⁰ M','AgNO₃ → Ag⁺ + NO₃⁻\n               0.1     0.1\n\nAg₂CO₃ ⇌ Ag²⁺ + CO₃²⁻\n              0.1+2x     x     \n                ≈ 0.1\n\nKₛₚ = [Ag²⁺]² [CO₃]\n      =  (0.1)²x = 8 × 10⁻¹²\n0.01 x = 8 × 10⁻¹²\n x = 8 × 10⁻¹⁰ M','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','8 × 10⁻¹⁰ M','8 × 10⁻¹¹ M','8 × 10⁻¹² M','8 × 10⁻¹³ M','NEW','11-230','11-206',NULL),
(71412,'chemistry','In a chemical reaction,  A + 2B ⇌K⇌ 2C + D , the  initial concentration of B was 1.5 times of the  concentration of A, but the equilibrium  concentrations of A and B were found to be equal.  The equilibrium constant (K) for the aforesaid  chemical reaction is [JEE (Main)-2019','4','A   +   2B ⇌ 2C  +  D \nt = 0   2          3\nteq     2-x     3-2x     2x       x\n\nGiven, 3 – 2x = 2 – x\n→ x = 1\n→ [C] = 2, [D] = 1\n[A] = 1, [B] = 1\nK꜀\n = 2².1/1².1 = 4','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','1','4','1/4','16','NEW','11-199','11-175',NULL),
(71413,'chemistry','Two solids dissociate as follows\nINSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(71431,'chemistry','The equilibrium constant (K꜀) for the reaction\n N₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2NO(g) at temperature T is 4 × 10⁻⁴.\n The value of K꜀\n for the reaction,\n NO(g) → 1 /2 N₂(g) +  1/2 O₂(g) at the same temperature  is [AIEEE-2012]','50','According to the law of mass action, it is the proposition that the rate of the chemical reaction is directly proportional to the product of the activities or concentration of the reactants.\n\nEquation 1:\nN₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2NO(g)\n\nThe equilibrium constant  K꜀\n = [NO]²/ [N₂][O₂] = 4 × 10⁻⁴\n\nEquation 2:\nNO(g) → 1\n/2 N₂(g) + \n1\n/2 O₂(g)\n\nk\' = [N₂]½[O₂]½ / [NO] = 1 / √ 4 × 10⁻⁴ = 50\nk\' = 50','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','50','0.02','2.5 × 10²','4 × 10⁻⁴','NEW','11-201','11-177',NULL),
(71432,'chemistry','The pH of a 0.1 molar solution of the acid HQ is  3. The value of the ionization constant, Ka of this  acid is [AIEEE-2012]','1 × 10⁻⁵','pH = -log[H⁺] = 3\nHence , [H⁺] = 10⁻³\n\nThe concentration c of the solution is 0.1 M.\nThe relationship between the hydrogen ion concentration, the dissociation constant Kₐ and the concentration c is as given below.\n\n[H⁺] = √ C.Kₐ \n10⁻³ = √0.1 × Kₐ\nKₐ = 10⁻⁵','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','1 × 10⁻⁵','1 × 10⁻³','1 × 10⁻⁷','3 × 10⁻¹','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(71433,'chemistry','An acid HA ionises as  HA ⇌ H⁺ + A⁻.The pH of 1.0 M solution is 5. Its dissociation constant would be','1 × 10⁻¹⁰','[H⁺] = √Kₐ.C\n10⁻ᵖᴴ = √Kₐ.C\n10⁻⁵ = √Kₐ.C\n(10⁻⁵)² = Kₐ.C\nKₐ = 10⁻¹⁰/C\nKₐ = 1 × 10⁻¹⁰. ( C = 1M)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','1 × 10⁻¹⁰','1 × 10⁻⁵','5','5 × 10⁻⁸','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(71434,'chemistry','The Kₛₚ for Cr(OH)₃ is 1.6 × 10⁻³⁰. The molar  solubility of this compound in water is  [AIEEE-2011]','(1.6 × 10⁻³⁰/27)¼','Cr(OH)₃ ⇌Cr³⁺ + 3OH⁻\nKₛₚ = (Cr³⁺)(OH⁻)³\nKₛₚ = 27s⁴\ns =  (Kₛₚ/27)¼ = (1.6 × 10⁻³⁰/27)¼','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','1.6 × 10⁻³⁰/27','(1.6 × 10⁻³⁰)½','(1.6 × 10⁻³⁰)¼','(1.6 × 10⁻³⁰/27)¼','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71435,'chemistry','If 10⁻⁴ dm³ of water is introduced into a 1.0 dm³  flask at 300 K, how many moles of water are in the\n vapour phase when equilibrium is established?  (Given : Vapour pressure of H₂O at 300 K is  3170 Pa; R = 8.314 J /Kmol) [AIEEE-2010])','1.27 × 10⁻³ mol','PV = nRT\n3170 × 10⁻³ = n × 8.314 × 300\nn = 3170 × 10⁻³/8.314 × 300 = 1.27 × 10⁻³','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','1.27 × 10⁻³ mol','5.56 × 10⁻³ mol','1.53 × 10⁻² mol','4.46 × 10⁻² mol','NEW','11-194','11-170',NULL),
(71436,'chemistry','At 25°C, the solubility product of Mg(OH)₂ is  1.0 × 10⁻¹¹. At which pH, will Mg²⁺ ions start  precipitating in the form of Mg(OH)₂ from a solution of 0.001 M Mg²⁺ ions? [AIEEE-2010]','10','Kₛₚ = [Mg²⁺] [OH⁻]\n²\n1 × 10⁻¹¹ = 0.001 × [OH⁻\n]²\n→ [OH⁻] = 10⁻⁴ M\npOH = 4, pH = 10','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','8','9','10','11','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71437,'chemistry','Three reactions involving H₂PO₄⁻ are given below\n\n(i) H₃PO₄ + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + H₂PO₄⁻ \n(ii) H₂PO₄⁻ + H₂O → H₂PO₄²⁻  + H₃O⁺ \n(iii) H₂PO₄⁻ + OH⁻ → H₃PO₄ + O² \nIn which of the above does H₂PO₄⁻ act as an acid?','(ii) only','An acid is one which can accept a pair of e⁻ or donate H⁺.In(ii) only H₂PO₄⁻ acts as an acid by donating H⁺ to water.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','(i) only','(ii) only','(i) and (ii)','(iii) only','NEW','11-217','11-193',NULL),
(71438,'chemistry','Solubility product of silver bromide is 5.0 × 10⁻¹³.  The quantity of potassium bromide (molar mass taken  as 120 g /mol) to be added to 1 litre of 0.05 M  solution of silver nitrate to start the precipitation of  AgBr is [AIEEE-2010]','1.2 × 10⁻⁹ g','[Ag⁺] = 0.05 , [Br⁻] = xM\n\nKₛₚ = [Ag⁺][Br⁻]\n5 × 10⁻¹³ = 0.05 × x\nx = 5 × 10⁻¹³/ 0.05 = 10⁻¹¹\n\nSolubility of KBr is (120 Solubility of KBr is (120 × 10⁻¹¹) or 1.2 × 10⁻⁹ g/L','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','5.0 × 10⁻⁸ g','1.2 × 10⁻¹⁰ g','1.2 × 10⁻⁹ g','6.2 × 10⁻⁵ g','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71439,'chemistry','In aqueous solution the ionisation constants for  carbonic acid are\n K₁ = 4.2 × 10⁻⁷ and K₂ = 4.8 × 10⁻¹¹. Select the correct statement for a saturated 0.034 M solution (of the carbonic acid. [AIEEE-2010]','The concentrations of H⁺ and HCO₃⁻\n are\n approximately equal','Since K₂  < < K₁\n→\nConc. of H⁺ and HCO₃⁻ are approximately same.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','The concentrations of H⁺ and HCO₃⁻\n are\n approximately equal','The concentrations of CO₃²⁻\n are\n 0.034 M','The concentrations of H⁺ is double that of  CO₃²⁻','The concentration of CO₃²⁻\n  is greater than that\n of HCO₃⁻','NEW','11-224','11-200',NULL),
(71440,'chemistry','Solid Ba(NO₃)₂ is gradually dissolved in a  1.0 × 10⁻⁴ M Na₂CO₃ solution. At what  concentration of Ba²⁺ will a precipitate begin to  form? (Kₛₚ for BaCO₃ = 5.1 × 10⁻⁹) [AIEEE-2009]','5.1 × 10⁻⁵ M','[ CO²₃⁻ ] = 10⁻⁴ M\n\nKₛₚ(BaCO₃) = [Ba²⁺][CO²₃⁻]\n[Ba²⁺]          = Kₛₚ/[CO²₃⁻] = 5.1 × 10⁻⁹/10⁻⁴ = 5.1 × 10⁻⁵ M','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','5.1 × 10⁻⁵ M','8.1 × 10⁻⁸ M','8.1 × 10⁻⁷','4.1 × 10⁻⁵ M','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71501,'chemistry','We know that the relationship between K꜀ and Kₚ is Kₚ = K꜀(RT)^∆n .What would be the value of an for the reaction NH₄Cl(s) ⇆ NH₃(g) + HCl(g)?','2','The relationship between Kₚ and K꜀ is\nKₚ = K꜀ (RT)^∆n\nWhere ∆n = (number of moles of gaseous products) – (number of moles of gaseous reactants)\nFor the reaction,\nNH₄Cl(s) ⇆ NH₃(g) + HCl(g)\n∆n = 2 – 0 = 2','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','1','0.5','1.5','2','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71502,'chemistry','For the reaction H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌2Hl(g), the standard free energy is ∆G° >0  The equilibrium constant (K) would be','K<1','∆G° = -RTln K. ∆G° > 0 means ∆G° is +ve. This can be so only if In K is -ve, i.e., K< 1.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','K = 0','K > 1','K=1','K<1','NEW','11-208','11-184',NULL),
(71503,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a general characteristic of equilibria involving physical processes?','All the physical processes stop at equilibrium.','All the physical processes like melting of ice and freezing of water, etc., do not stop at equilibrium.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Equilibrium is possible only in a closed system at a given temperature.','All measurable properties of the system remain constant.','All the physical processes stop at equilibrium.','The opposing processes occur at the same rate and there is dynamic but stable condition.','NEW','11-196','11-172',NULL),
(71504,'chemistry','PCl₅, PCl₃ and Cl₂ are at equilibrium at 500 K in a closed container and their concentrations are 0.8 x 10⁻³ mol/L, 1.2 x 10⁻³ mol/L and 1.2 x 10⁻³ mol/L.The value of K꜀ for the reaction PCl₅(g) ⇌PCl₃(g) + Cl₂(g) will be','1.8 x 10⁻³ mol/L','PCl₅(g) ⇌PCl₃(g) + Cl₂(g)\n\nK꜀ = [PCl₃][Cl₂]/[PCl₅] = 1.2 x 10⁻³ × 1.2 x 10⁻³/0.8 x 10⁻³ = 1.8 x 10⁻³ mol/L','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','8 x 10⁻³ mol/L','1.8 x 10⁻³ mol/L','1.8 x 10³ mol/L','0.55 x 10⁴ mol/L','NEW','11-199','11-175',NULL),
(71505,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is incorrect?','The intensity of red colour increases when oxalic acid is added to a solution containing iron (III) nitrate and potassium thiocyanate','Fe³⁺+SCN⁻ ⇌ FeSCN²⁺(Red)\nWhen oxalic acid is added to a solution containing iron nitrate and potassium thiocyanate, oxalic acid reacts with Fe³⁺ ions to form a stable complex ion [Fe(C₂0₄)₃]³⁻, thus, decreasing the concentration of free Fe³⁺ ions which in mm decreases the intensity of red colour.\nFe³⁺+SCN⁻ ⇌ [FeSCN]²⁺ (Red)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','In equilibrium mixture of ice and water kept in perfectly insulated flask, mass of ice and water does not change with time.','The intensity of red colour increases when oxalic acid is added to a solution containing iron (III) nitrate and potassium thiocyanate','On addition of catalyst the equilibrium constant value is not affected.','Equilibrium constant for a reaction with negative △H value decreases as the temperature increases.','NEW','11-209','11-185',NULL),
(71520,'chemistry','For the reaction N₂0₄(g) ⇌2N0₂(g), the value of K is 50 at 400 K and 1700 at 500 K. Which of the following options is correct?\n(a) The reaction is endothermic.\n(b) The reaction is exothermic.\n(c) If NO₂(g) and N₂0₄(g) are mixed 400 K at partial pressures 20 bar and 2 bar respectively, more N₂0₄(g) will be formed.\n(d) The entropy of the system increases.','a,c & d','(a) As the value of K increases with increase of temperature and K = Kբ / K_b, this means that Kբ increases, i.e., forward reaction is favoured.\nHence, reaction is endothermic.\n(c)  At 400K , Q = P²_NO₂/P_N₂0₄ = (20)²/2 = 200. Thus Q>K . Equilibrium will shift backwards to form more N₂0₄.\n(d) As reaction is accompanied by increase in the number of moles , entropy increase.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b & c','a and b','only c','a,c & d','NEW','11-211','11-187',NULL),
(71521,'chemistry','At a particular temperature and atmospheric pressure, the solid and liquid phases of a pure substance can exist in equilibrium. Which of the following term defines this temperature? .\n(a) Normal melting point\n(b) Equilibrium temperature\n(c) Boiling point\n(d) Freezing point','a & d','These are normal melting point and freezing point since they are measured at atmospheric pressure.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b & c','a & d','a,b,c & d','only b','NEW','11-193','11-169',NULL),
(71701,'chemistry','The concentration of sulphide ion in 0.1 M HCl solution saturated with hydrogen sulphide is 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ M. If 10 mL of this solution is added to 5 mL of 0.04 M solution of FeSO₄, MnCl₂, ZnCl₂ and CaCl₂, in which solutions precipitation will take place ?\nGiven Kₛₚ for\nFeS = 6.3 × 10⁻¹⁸,\nMnS = 2.5 × 10⁻¹³,\nZnS = 1.6 × 10⁻²⁴ and\nCdS = 8.0 × 10⁻²⁷.','ZnS & CdS','Precipitation will take place in the solution for which ionic product is greater than solubility product. As 10 mL of solution containing S²⁻ ion is mixed with 5 mL of metal salt solution, after mixing\n\n[S²⁻]=1.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ x10/15= 6.67 × 10⁻²⁰\n[Fe²⁺] = [Mn²⁺] = [Zn²⁺] = [Cd²⁺]=(5/15)0.04 =1.33× 10⁻². \n\nHence the ionic product [M²⁺][S²⁻] = 1.33 x 10² x 6.67 x 10⁻²⁰ = 8.87 x 10⁻²² \nTherefore in ZnS (K = 1.6 x 10²⁴) and CdS (Ksp = 8.0 × 10⁻²⁷), ionic product exceeds solubility product. Hence, precipitation will take place','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','ZnS & CdS','ZnS & FeS','FeS & MnS','ZnS & MnS','NEW','11-230','11-206',NULL),
(71702,'chemistry','What is the minimum volume of water required to dissolve 1 g of calcium sulphate at 298 K. For calcium sulphate Kₛₚ = 9.1 × 10⁻⁶?','2.43 L','CaSO₄(s)  ⇌  Ca²⁺(aq) +SO₄²⁻(aq)\n\nIf S is the solubility of CaSO₄, in mol /L  then K sp =[Ca²⁺ ][SO₄²⁻]=S²\n\nor, S = √Ksp = √ 9.1 × 10⁻⁶ =3.02 × 10⁻³ mol /L = 3.02 × 10⁻³ ×  136gL = 0.411g / L \n\n(Molar mass of CaSO₄ = 136 g /mol)Thus, for dissolving 0.411 g, water required = 1 L For dissolving 1 g, 1 water required =1/0.411L= 2.43 L','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','2.43 L','3.43 L','1.43 L','4.43 L','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71703,'chemistry','What is the maximum concentration of equimolar solutions of ferrous sulphate and sodium sulphide so that when mixed in equal volumes, there is no precipitation of iron sulphide ? (For iron sulphide, Kₛₚ = 6.3 × 10⁻¹⁸).','5.02 × 10⁻⁹ mol/L','Let the concentration of each of FeSO₄ and Na₂S is x mol L. Then after mixing equal volumes:\n\n[FeSO₄]= (x/2)M ,  [Na₂S]=(x/2)M\n\nor [Fe²+]=(x/2)M and [S²] =(x/2) M \n\nFeS ⇌ Fe²⁺+S²⁻, Ksp = [Fe²⁺][S²⁻] \n6.3×10⁻¹⁸ =  (x/2)(x/2)\nx² = 4 × 6.3×10⁻¹⁸ × 25.2 × 10⁻¹⁸\nx=5.02 x 10⁻⁹ mol /L \nMaximum concentration of FeSO₄, and Na₂S which will not precipitate iron sulphide = 5.02 x 10⁻⁹ mol /L','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','5.02 × 10⁻⁹ mol/L','2.51 × 10⁻⁹ mol/L','5.02 × 10⁻¹⁹ mol/L','5.02 mol/L','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71704,'chemistry','The ionization constant of benzoic acid is 6.46 × 10⁻⁵ and Kₛₚ for silver benzoate is 2.5 × 10⁻¹³. How many times is silver benzoate more soluble in a buffer of pH 3.19 compared to its solubility in pure water ?','3.32 times','Suppose S is the molar solubility of silver benzoate in water , then  \nCHCOOAg    ⇌   CHCOO⁻(aq) + Ag⁺\nKsp = S² , S = √ 2.5 × 10⁻¹³ = 50 × 10⁻⁷ M\nIf the solubility of salt of weak acid of ionization constant Ka is S , then Ksp, Ka, S\' are related to each other at pH = 3.19 \n → [H] = 6.46 × 10⁻⁴\nKsp = S\'²[ Ka/(Ka + [H⁺] ]\nS\' = { 2.5 × 10⁻¹³/ [6.46 × 10⁻⁵/ (6.45 × 10⁻⁵ + 6.46 × 10⁻⁴)]}½\nS\' = ( 2.75 × 10⁻¹²)½ = 1.658 × 10⁻⁶ M\n\nThe ratio S\'/S = 1.658 × 10⁻⁶/5 × 10⁻⁷ = 3.32\nsilver benzoate is 3.32 times more soluble in buffer of pH 3.19 than in pure water.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','3.32 times','1.32 times','1.66 times','4.32 times','NEW','11-227','11-203',NULL),
(71705,'chemistry','Equal volumes of 0.002 M solutions of sodium iodate and copper chlorate are mixed together. Will it lead to the precipitation of copper iodate ? (For copper iodate Kₛₚ = 7.4 × 10⁻⁸).','No Precipitation','When equal volumes of sodium iodate and copper chlorate are mixed , the molar concentration of both the solutes would be reduce to half i.e. 0.001 M\n\nNaIO₃ → Na+ + IO₃⁻\n0.001 M         0.001M\n\nCu(ClO₂)₂ → Cu₂+ + 2ClO₃⁻\n0.01 M                         0.01M\n\n → After mixing [IO₃⁻] = [NaIO₃] = 0.001 M \nsolubility Equilibrium for Cooper iodate may be written as \nCu(IO₃)2      Cu2+(aq) + 2IO₃⁻(aq)\n\nIonic product of copper iodate= [Cu²+][IO3-]² = (0.001)(0.001)² = 1 × 10-⁹\nSince ionic product (1 × 10-⁹) is less than Ksp (7.4 × 10-⁸), therefore, no Precipitation will take place.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','No Precipitation','Precipitation will takes place','Given information is not enough','Minimum Precipitation only takes place','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71706,'chemistry','The solubility product constants of Ag₂CrO₄ and AgBr are 1.1 × 10⁻¹² and 5.0 × 10⁻¹³ respectively. Calculate the ratio of the molarities of their saturated solutions','91.94','Ksp(Ag2CrO4) > Ksp(AgBr)\n→ Ag2CrO4 is more soluble \n\nAg2CrO4     2Ag(aq) + CrO4²-\nKsp = 4S³ and S = [ 1.1 × 10-¹²/4] = 6.50 × 10-⁵ M\n\nAgBr Ag+(aq) + Br-(aq) ; Ksp = S\'²\n→ S\' = (5 × 10-¹³)½ = 7.07 × 10-⁷ M\n\nThe ratio of the molarities= S/S\' = 6.50 × 10-⁵/7.07 × 10-⁷ = 91.94','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','91.94','6/7','9194','7/6','NEW','11-230','11-206',NULL),
(71707,'chemistry','Calculate the pH of the resultant mixtures: 10 mL. of 0.2 M Ca(OH)₂ + 25 mL of 0.1 M HCl','12.63','10 mL. of 0.2 M Ca(OH)₂  = 10 × 0.2 milimoles = 2 millimoles of Ca(OH)₂\n25 mL of 0.1 M HCl = 25 × 0.1 millimoles = 2.5 millimoles of HCl\n\nCa(OH)₂ + 2 HCl → CaCl2 + 2H2O\n1 millimole of Ca(OH)₂ reacts with 2 millimoles of HCL  \n →2.5 millimoles of HCl will react with 1.25 millimoles of Ca(OH)₂\n\nCa(OH)₂ left = 2-1.25 = 0.75 millimoles (HCl is the limiting reactant)\nTotal volume of the solution= 10+25 ml=35ml\n\n→ Molarity of Ca(OH)₂ in the mixture solution= (0.75/35)M = 0.0214 M\n[OH-] = 2 × 0.0214 M = 0.0428 M = 4.28 × 10-²M\n\npOH = -log(4.28 × 10-²) = 2-0.6314 = 1.3686 ≈ 1.37\n\npH = 14-1.37 = 12.63.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','1.37','21','3.37','12.63','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71708,'chemistry','Calculate the pH of the resultant mixtures: 10 mL of 0.01 M H₂SO₄ + 10 mL of 0.01 M Ca(OH)₂','7','10 mL of 0.01 M H₂SO₄ = 10 × 0.01 millimole = 0.1 millimole \n10 mL of 0.01 M Ca(OH)₂ = 10 × 0.01 millimole= 0.1 millimole\n\nCa(OH)₂+ H₂SO₄ → CaSO₄ + 2H₂O\n1 mole of Ca(OH)₂ reacts with 1 mole of H₂SO₄.\n\n→ 0.1 millimole of Ca(OH)₂ will react completely with 0.1 millimole of H₂SO₄ , hence , solution will be neutral with pH = 7.0','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','7','12.63','1','5','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71709,'chemistry','Calculate the pH of the resultant mixtures: 10 mL of 0.1 M H₂SO₄ + 10 mL of 0.1 M KOH.','1.3','10 mL of 0.1 M H₂SO₄ = 1 millimole \n10 mL of 0.1 M KOH = 1 millimole \n2KOH + H₂SO₄→ K₂SO₄ + 2H₂O \n1 millimole of KOH will react with 0.5 millimole of H₂SO₄  \n→H₂SO₄ left=1-0.5-0.5 millimole\n\nVolume of reaction mixture = 10+ 10 = 20 mL . \nMolarity of H₂SO₄ , in the mixture solution = 0.5/20 solution =2.5 x 10⁻² M \npH = -log(5 x 10²)=2-0.699 = 1.3\n\n[H⁺] = 2×2.5 × 10⁻² = 5 × 10⁻²\npH = -log(5 × 10⁻²) = 2 - 0.699 = 1.3','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','1.3','7','1.7','1','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71710,'chemistry','The ionic product of water at 310 K is 2.7 × 10⁻¹⁴. What is the pH value of neutral water at this temperature ?','6.78','We know that \n[H⁺] = √Ka = √ 2.7 × 10⁻¹⁴ = 1.643 × 10⁻⁷M\npH = -log (1.643 × 10⁻⁷) = 7 - 0.2156 = 6.78','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','6.78','7.78','8.78','5.78','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71711,'chemistry','The ionisation constant of chloroacetic acid is 1.35 × 10⁻³. What will be the pH of 0.1 M acid solution ?','1.936','pH of acid solution \n[H⁺] = √Ka × C\nKa = 1.35 × 10⁻³ , C = 0.1 M \n[H⁺] = √1.35 × 10⁻³ × 0.1 = 1.16 ×10⁻²\n \npH = -log(1.16 ×10⁻²) = 2 - 0.064 = 1.936','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','7.935','6.165','1.936','5.165','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(71712,'chemistry','A 0.02 M solution of pyridinium hydrochloride has pH = 3.44. Calculate the ionisation constant of pyridine.','1.513 × 10⁻⁹','Pyridinium hydrochloride is a salt of weak salt and strong acid. ,then \npH = 7-1/2(logC + pKb)\n3.44 = 7 -1/2(log0.02 + pKb)\n-3.56 = -1/2(-1.7 + pKb)\npKb = 8.82\n\nKb = antilog (-8.82) = 1.513 × 10⁻⁹','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','1.513 × 10⁻⁹','1.513 × 10⁻³','1.513 × 10⁻⁷','1513','NEW','11-226','11-202',NULL),
(71713,'chemistry','The ionisation constant of nitrous acid is 4.5 × 10⁻⁴. Calculate the pH value of 0.04 M NaNO₂ solution.','7.975','Degree of hydrolysis,h = √Kw/Ka.C = √ 10⁻¹⁴/4.5 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.04 = 2.36 × 10⁻⁵\n\npH =1/2pKw + 1/2 pKa + 1/2logC\npH = 1/2 [ -log10⁻¹⁴+(-4.5 × 10⁻⁴) + log(4 × 10⁻²)] = 7.975','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','7.975','8.975','5.975','6.975','NEW','11-226','11-202',NULL),
(71714,'chemistry','The ionisation constant of nitrous acid is 4.5 × 10⁻⁴. Calculate degree of hydrolysis of 0.04 M NaNO₂ solution.','2.36 × 10⁻⁵','Degree of hydrolysis,h = √Kw/Ka.C = √ 10⁻¹⁴/4.5 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.04 = 2.36 × 10⁻⁵','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','2.36 × 10⁻⁵','2.36 × 10⁻³','2.36 × 10⁻²','236','NEW','11-226','11-202',NULL),
(71715,'chemistry','The pH of 0.1M solution of cyanic acid (HCNO) is 2.34. Calculate degree of dissociation in the solution of the acid.','0.0457','HCNO ⇌ H⁺ + CNO⁻\npH = 2.34 or log [H⁺] = -2.34\n[H⁺] = antilog(-2.34) = 4.57 × 10⁻³M\n\nKa = 4.57 × 10⁻³ × 4.57 × 10⁻³/0.1 = 2.09 × 10⁻⁴\n\nα = √Ka/C = √2.09 × 10⁻⁴/0.1 = 0.0457','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','0.0457','0.457','1.4','1','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(71716,'chemistry','The pH of 0.1M solution of cyanic acid (HCNO) is 2.34. Calculate ionisation constant of the acid ?','2.09 × 10⁻⁴','HCNO ⇌ H⁺ + CNO⁻\npH = 2.34 or log [H⁺] = -2.34\n[H⁺] = antilog(-2.34) = 4.57 × 10⁻³M\n\nKa = 4.57 × 10⁻³ × 4.57 × 10⁻³/0.1 = 2.09 × 10⁻⁴','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','2.09 × 10⁻⁴','2.09 × 10⁻³','209 × 10⁻⁴','209','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(71717,'chemistry','The ionisation constant of propionic acid is 1.32 × 10⁻⁵.What will be its degree of ionisation if the solution is 0.01 M in HCl also ?','1.32 × 10⁻⁴','Refer diagram (ne0730)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','1.32 × 10⁻⁴','1.32 × 10⁻³','1','1.32','NEW','11-225','11-201',NULL),
(71718,'chemistry','The ionisation constant of propionic acid is 1.32 × 10⁻⁵. Calculate the degree of ionisation of acid in its 0.05 M solution ?','1.62 × 10⁻²','Ka = 1.32 × 10⁻⁵ , C = 0.05 M\nα = √Ka/C = √1.32 × 10⁻⁵/0.05 = 1.62 × 10⁻².','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','1.62 × 10⁻²','1.62 × 10⁻³','2.62','2.62 × 10⁻²','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(71719,'chemistry','The ionisation constant of propionic acid is 1.32 × 10⁻⁵. Calculate the pH of acid in its 0.05 M solution ?','3.09','Ka = 1.32 × 10⁻⁵ , C = 0.05 M\nα = √Ka/C = √1.32 × 10⁻⁵/0.05 = 1.62 × 10⁻².\n[H⁺] = Cα = 1.62 × 10⁻² × 0.05 = 8.1 × 10⁻⁴\n\npH = -log(8.1 × 10⁻⁴) = 3.09','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','3.09','1.09','2.09','4.09','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(71720,'chemistry','The solubility of Sr(OH)₂ at 298 K is 19.23 g/L of solution. Calculate the concentrations of strontium ?','0.1581 M','Molar mass of Sr(OH)₂ = 87.6 + 34 = 121.6 g /mol \nSolubility of Sr(OH)₂, in moles /L = (19.23g /L )/(121.6g/mol) = 0.1581M\n\nAssuming complete dissociation,\n \nSr(OH)₂ →Sr²⁺ + 2OH⁻\n[Sr²⁺] = 0.1581 M','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','0.1581 M','0.151 M','1581 M','1 M','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71721,'chemistry','The solubility of Sr(OH)₂ at 298 K is 19.23 g/L of solution. Calculate the concentrations of hydroxyl ions ?','0.3162','Molar mass of Sr(OH)₂ = 87.6 + 34 = 121.6 g /mol \nSolubility of Sr(OH)₂, in moles /L = (19.23g /L )/(121.6g/mol) = 0.1581M\n\nAssuming complete dissociation,\n \nSr(OH)₂ →Sr²⁺ + 2OH⁻\n[Sr²⁺] = 0.1581 M\n2 × [2OH⁻] = 2 × 0.1581 M =0.3162','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','0.3162','3162','3.162','31625','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71722,'chemistry','The solubility of Sr(OH)₂ at 298 K is 19.23 g/L of solution. Calculate the pH of the solution.','13.5','Molar mass of Sr(OH)₂ = 87.6 + 34 = 121.6 g /mol \nSolubility of Sr(OH)₂, in moles /L = (19.23g /L )/(121.6g/mol) = 0.1581M\n\nAssuming complete dissociation,\n \nSr(OH)₂ →Sr²⁺ + 2OH⁻\n[Sr²⁺] = 0.1581 M\n2 × [2OH⁻] = 2 × 0.1581 M =0.3162\n\npOH = -log(0.3162) = 0.5\npH = 14-0.5 = 3.5','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','13.5','27','135','1','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71723,'chemistry','0.561 g KOH is dissolved in water to give 200 mL of solution at 298K. Calculate the concentration of potassium ?','0.05','Molar mass of KOH = 56.0 g /mol \nNo. of moles of KOH = 0.561 g /56.0 g /mol = = 0.01 mol \nand conc. of KOH = 0.01 /0.2 = 0.05 mol/L\n\nKOH(aq) → K⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq)\n[K⁺] = [OH⁻] = 0.05','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','0.05','0.5','5','500','NEW','11-212','11-188',NULL),
(71724,'chemistry','0.561 g KOH is dissolved in water to give 200 mL of solution at 298K. Calculate the concentration of hydrogen ?','2 × 10⁻¹³','Molar mass of KOH = 56.0 g /mol \nNo. of moles of KOH = 0.561 g /56.0 g /mol = = 0.01 mol \nand conc. of KOH = 0.01 /0.2 = 0.05 mol/L\n\nKOH(aq) → K⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq)\n[K⁺] = [OH⁻] = 0.05\n\n[H⁺] = Kw/[OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴/0.05 = 2 × 10⁻¹³','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','2 × 10⁻¹³','2 × 10¹³','2 × 10⁻³','2','NEW','11-212','11-188',NULL),
(71725,'chemistry','0.561 g KOH is dissolved in water to give 200 mL of solution at 298K. Calculate the concentration of hydroxyl ions.','0.05','Molar mass of KOH = 56.0 g /mol \nNo. of moles of KOH = 0.561 g /56.0 g /mol = = 0.01 mol \nand conc. of KOH = 0.01 /0.2 = 0.05 mol/L\n\nKOH(aq) → K⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq)\n[K⁺] = [OH⁻] = 0.05','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','0.05','0.5','5','500','NEW','11-217','11-193',NULL),
(71726,'chemistry','0.561 g KOH is dissolved in water to give 200 mL of solution at 298K. What is the pH of the solution ?','12.7','Molar mass of KOH = 56.0 g /mol \nNo. of moles of KOH = 0.561 g /56.0 g /mol = = 0.01 mol \nand conc. of KOH = 0.01 /0.2 = 0.05 mol/L\n\nKOH(aq) → K⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq)\n[K⁺] = [OH⁻] = 0.05\n\n[H⁺] = Kw/[OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴/0.05 = 2 × 10⁻¹³\n\npH = -log (2 × 10⁻¹³) = 12.70','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','12.7','11.7','1.3','1','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
