(71737,'chemistry','Calculate the pH of the following solutions : 2 g of TIOH dissolved in water to give 2 litre of solution.','11.65','Molar mass of TlOH = 221.4 g/mol\n\nn(TlOH) = 2/221.4 = 9.03 × 10⁻³ mol.\n\n[OH⁻] = [TlOH] = 9.03 × 10⁻³/2 = 4.51 × 10⁻³ M\n\npOH = -log(4.51 × 10⁻³) = 2.35\npH = 14-2.35 = 11.65','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','11.65','21.65','2.35','10.65','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71738,'chemistry','Calculate the pH of the following solutions : 0.3 g of Ca(OH)₂ dissolved in water to give 500 mL of solution.','12.21','n(Ca(OH)₂) = 0.3/74 = 4.05 × 10⁻³\n\n[OH⁻] = 2[(Ca(OH)₂] = (2 × 4.05 × 10⁻³ )/0.5 = 1.62 × 10⁻² M\n\npOH = -log(1.62 × 10⁻²) = 1.79\npH = 14-1.79 = 12.21.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','12.21','21.12','1221','2112','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71739,'chemistry','Calculate the pH of the following solutions : 0.3 g of NaOH dissolved in water to give 200 mL of solution.','12.57','n(NaOH) = 0.3/40 = 75 × 10⁻³ mol\n\n[OH⁻] = [NaOH] = 7.5 × 10⁻³/0.2L = 0.0375 M\npOH = -log[0.0375 M] = 1.43 \npH = 14-1.43\npH = 12.57','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','12.57','21.12','11.54','1.57','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71740,'chemistry','Calculate the pH of the following solutions : 1 mL of 13.6 M HCl diluted with water to give 1 litre of solution.','1.87','M1V1 = M2V2 \n13.6 × 1 = M × 1000\nM = 13.6/1000 = 0.0136 mol/L\n[H⁺] = [HCl] = 0.0136 mol/L\npH = -log(0.0136) = 1.87','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','1.87','2.87','3.87','0.87','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71741,'chemistry','Assuming complete dissociation, calculate the pH of the following solutions : 0.003 M HCl','2.52','HCl + aq → H⁺ + Cl⁻\n[H⁺] = [HCl] = 3 × 10⁻³M\npH = -log(3 × 10⁻³) = 2.52','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','2.52','1.52','11.48','12.48','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71742,'chemistry','Assuming complete dissociation, calculate the pH of the following solutions : 0.005 M NaOH','11.7','NaOH + aq → Na⁺ + OH⁻\n[OH⁻] = 5 × 10⁻³ M\n[H⁺] = 10⁻¹⁴/5 × 10⁻³ M = 2 × 10⁻¹² M\npH = -log(2 × 10⁻¹²) = 11.70','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','11.7','2.3','3.3','10.7','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71743,'chemistry','Assuming complete dissociation, calculate the pH of the following solutions :0.002 M HBr','2.7','HBr + aq → H⁺ + Br⁻\n[H⁺] = 2 × 10⁻³ M\npH = -log(2 × 10⁻³ M) = 2.70','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','2.7','11.3','11.7','2.3','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71744,'chemistry','Assuming complete dissociation, calculate the pH of the following solutions :0.002 M KOH.','11.3','KOH + aq → K⁺ + OH⁻\n[OH⁻] = 2 × 10⁻³ M\n\n[H⁺] = 10⁻¹⁴/2 × 10⁻³ = 5 × 10⁻¹²\npH = -log(5 × 10⁻¹²) = 11.30','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','11.3','2.7','11.5','2.3','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71745,'chemistry','It has been found that the pH of 0.01 M solution of organic acid is 4.15. Calculate the concentration of the anion.','7.08 × 10⁻⁵','pH = -log[H⁺]\n[H⁺] = antilog(4.15)\n[H⁺] = 7.08 × 10⁻⁵ M\nHA(aq) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + A⁻(aq)\n\n[H⁺]= [A⁻] = 7.08 × 10⁻⁵ M','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','7.08 × 10⁻³','7.08 × 10⁻⁵','708','7.08 × 10⁻¹⁴','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71746,'chemistry','It has been found that the pH of 0.01 M solution of organic acid is 4.15. Calculate the ionization constant of the acid  [[H⁺]= [A⁻] = 7.08 × 10⁻⁵ M]','5.05 × 10⁻⁷','Ka = Cα²/1-α = 0.01(7.08 × 10⁻³)²/1-7.08 × 10⁻³ = 5.05 × 10⁻⁷','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','5.05 × 10⁻⁷','5.05 × 10⁷','15 × 10⁻⁷','5','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(71747,'chemistry','It has been found that the pH of 0.01 M solution of organic acid is 4.15. Calculate the pKa. [Ka = 5.05 × 10⁻⁷]','6.29','pKa = -logKa = -log(5.05 × 10⁻⁷) = 6.29','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','6.29','7.29','8.71','9.71','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(71748,'chemistry','The ionization constant of acetic acid is 1.74 × 10⁻⁵. Calculate the degree of dissociation of acetic acid in 0.05 M solution.','0.0186','α = √Ka/C = √ 1.74 × 10⁻⁵/5 × 10⁻² = 0.0186','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','0.0186','0.086','0.86','0.186','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(71749,'chemistry','The ionization constant of acetic acid is 1.74 × 10⁻⁵. Calculate the concentration of acetate ions in solution. [α = 0.0186]','9.33 × 10⁻⁴','CH₃COOH ⇌ CH₃COO⁻ + H⁺\nKa = [CH₃COO⁻][H⁺]/[CH₃COOH] =[H⁺]² /[CH₃COOH]\n[H⁺] = √Ka[CH₃COOH] \n        = √ 1.74 × 10⁻⁵ × 5 × 10⁻² = 9.33 × 10⁻⁴\n[CH₃COO⁻] = [H⁺] = 9.33 × 10⁻⁴ M','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','9.33 × 10⁻⁴','9.33 × 10⁻⁵','8.33 × 10⁻⁴','9.33','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(71750,'chemistry','The ionization constant of acetic acid is 1.74 × 10⁻⁵. Calculate the pH of the solution.[α = 0.0186]','3.03','CH₃COOH ⇌ CH₃COO⁻ + H⁺\nKa = [CH₃COO⁻][H⁺]/[CH₃COOH] =[H⁺]² /[CH₃COOH]\n[H⁺] = √Ka[CH₃COOH] \n        = √ 1.74 × 10⁻⁵ × 5 × 10⁻² = 9.33 × 10⁻⁴\n[CH₃COO⁻] = [H⁺] = 9.33 × 10⁻⁴ M\n\npH = -log(H⁺) = -log(9.33 × 10⁻⁴) = 4-0.9699 = 3.03.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','3.03','6.06','4.04','5.05','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(71751,'chemistry','The first dissociation constant H₂S is 9.1 × 10⁻⁸. Calculate the concentration of HS⁻ ions in its 0.1 M solution.','9.5 × 10⁻⁵','H₂S⇌HS⁻ + H⁺ \nInitial              C        0        0\nEquilibrium.   C-Cα   Cα    Cα\nKa = [HS⁻][H⁺]/[H₂S] = Cα²/1-α =Cα² (1>>>a)\nand [HS⁻] = Cα = √KaC \n[HS⁻] = √ 9.1 × 10⁻⁸ × 0.1 = 9.1 × 10⁻⁵.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','9.1 × 10⁻⁸','9.5 × 10⁻⁵','8.1 × 10⁻⁸','8.1 × 10⁻⁷','NEW','11-224','11-200',NULL),
(71752,'chemistry','The ionisation constant of phenol is 1.0 x 10⁻¹⁰. What is the concentration of phenate ion in 0.05 M solution of phenol ?','2.33 × 10⁻⁶','C₆H₅OH ⇌C₆H₅O⁻ + H⁺\nInitial conc.         0.05M      0           0   \nAfter dislocation  0.05-x     x            x\nKa = x²/0.05-x = 1 × 10⁻¹⁰(Given)\n By simplify, x =2.33 × 10⁻⁶','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','2.33 × 10⁻⁶','2.33','233','2.33 × 10⁻⁹','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(71753,'chemistry','The ionisation constant of phenol is 1.0 x 10⁻¹⁰. What will be the degree of ionisation if the solution is also 0.01 M in sodium phenate ?','1 × 10⁻⁸','C₆H₅OH ⇌C₆H₅O⁻ + H⁺\nInitial conc.         0.05M      0           0   \nAfter dislocation  0.05-x     x            x\nIn the presence of 0.01 M of C₆H₅ONa, suppose x is the amount of phenol dissociated , then at equilibrium \n[C₆H₅OH] = 0.05-x ≈0.05\n[C₆H₅O⁻] = 0.01 + x ≈ 0.01M , [H⁺] = xM \nKa = 0.01 × x = 0.05 = 1 × 10⁻¹⁰(Given)\nx = 5× 10⁻¹⁰\nα = x/C = 5× 10⁻¹⁰/0.05 = 1 × 10⁻⁸','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','1 × 10⁻⁸','1 × 10⁻⁷','1','1 × 10⁻⁹','NEW','11-225','11-201',NULL),
(71754,'chemistry','The ionisation constants of HF, HCOOH and HCN at 298 K are 6.8 x 10⁻⁴, 1.8 × 10⁻⁴ and 4.8 × 10⁻⁹ respectively. Which of the ionisation constant of the corresponding congugate bases have more value ?','CN⁻','Kb = Kw/Ka \n\nFor F⁻ , Kb = 10⁻¹⁴/6.8 × 10⁻⁴ = 1.47 × 10⁻¹¹\nFor HCOO⁻ , Kb = 10⁻¹⁴/1.8 × 10⁻⁴ = 5.6 × 10⁻¹¹\nFor CN⁻ , Kb = 10⁻¹⁴/4.8 × 10⁻⁹ = 2.08 × 10⁻⁶(Highest)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','CN⁻','HCOO⁻','F⁻','All of them','NEW','11-222','11-198',NULL),
(71755,'chemistry','The pH of soft drink is 3.76. Calculate the concentration of hydrogen ions in it.','1.734 × 10⁻⁴ M','pH = -log[H⁺]\n[H⁺] = -antilog[pH] = antilog(-3.76) = 1.738 × 10⁻⁴M','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','1.734 × 10⁻⁴ M','1.734 × 10⁴ M','2.734 × 10⁻⁴ M','1734 × 10⁻⁴ M','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71756,'chemistry','The concentration of hydrogen ions in a sample of soft drink is 3.8 × 10⁻³ M. What is its pH value ?','2.42','[H⁺] = 3.8 × 10⁻³M \npH = -log[H⁺] = -log[3.8 × 10⁻³]\n      = 3log10- log3.8 = 3 - 0.5797\npH = 2.4203 ≈ 2.42.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','2.42','242','4.84','484','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71757,'chemistry','The reaction CO(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ CH₄(g) + H₂O(g) is at equilibrium at 1300 K in a 1L flask. It also contain 0.30 mol of CO, 0.10 mol of H₂ and 0.02 mol of H₂O and an unknown amount of CH₄ in the flask. Determine the concentration of CH₄ in the mixture. The equilibrium constant, Kc for the reaction at the given temperature is 3.90.','5.84 × 10⁻² mol/L','CO(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ CH₄(g) + H₂O(g)\nApplying law of chemical equilibrium,\nK꜀ = [CH₄][H₂O]/[H₂]³[CO] = [CH₄] 0.02/0.3(0.1)³ \n[CH₄] = 5.84 × 10⁻² mol/L.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','5.84 × 10⁻² mol/L','5.84 × 10² mol/L','5.84 × 10⁻⁴ mol/L','5.84 mol/L','NEW','11-199','11-175',NULL),
(2000031116,'physics','A spherical conductor of radius 10 $cm$ has  a charge of $3.2 \\times {10^{ - 7}}\\,C$ distributed uniformly. What is the magnitude of electric field at a point 15 $cm$ from the centre of the sphere?<br>$\\left( {\\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}} = 9 \\times {{10}^9}N{m^2}/{C^2}} \\right)$','A','$E = \\frac{{KQ}}{{{r^2}}} = \\frac{{9 \\times {{10}^9} \\times 3.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 7}}}}{{{{(15 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}})}^2}}}$<br>$E = 1.28 \\times {10^5}N/C$','Continuous Charge Distribution','Electric charges & Fields','Medium',NULL,'$1.28 \\times {10^5}N/C$','$1.28 \\times {10^6}N/C$','$1.28 \\times {10^7}N/C$','$1.28 \\times {10^4}N/C$',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(71758,'chemistry','The value of K꜀ for the reaction 3O₂(g) ⇌ 2O₃(g) is 2.0 × 1 x 10⁻⁵⁰ at 25°C. If equilibrium concentration of O₂ in air at 25°C is 1.6 × 10⁻², what is the concentration of O3 ?','2.864 × 10⁻²⁸ mol/L','3O₂(g) ⇌ 2O₃(g)\nK꜀ = [O₃]²/[O₂]³ , 2 × 10⁻⁵⁰ = [O₃]²/(1.6 × 10⁻²)³\n[O₃]² = 2 × 10⁻⁵⁰ × (1.6)³ × 10⁻⁶\n[O₃] = √2 × 10⁻⁵⁰ ×  (1.6)³ × 10⁻²⁸ = √3.2 × (1.6)² × 10⁻²⁸ \n[O₃] = 2.864 × 10⁻²⁸ mol/L','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','2.864 × 10⁻²⁸ mol/L','2.864 × 10⁻¹⁸ mol/L','5.664 × 10⁻²⁸ mol/L','2864 × 10⁻²⁸ mol/L','NEW','11-199','11-175',NULL),
(71759,'chemistry','Dihydrogen gas used in Haber’s process is produced by reacting methane from natural gas with high temperature steam. The first stage of two stage reaction involves the formation of CO and H2. In second stage, CO formed in first stage is reacted with more steam in water gas shift reaction.\nCO(g) + H₂O(g) ⇌ CO₂(g) + H₂(g)\nIf a reaction vessel at 400°C is charged with an equimolar mixture of CO and steam so that Pco = PH₂O = 4.0 bar, what will be the partial pressure of H₂ at equilibrium ? Kp = 0.1 at 400°C.','3.04 bar','CO(g) + H₂O(g) ⇌ CO₂(g) + H₂(g)\nInitial       4             4               0             0 \nAt eq (P) 4-p         4-p           p             p \nApplying law of chemical equilibrium, \n\nKₚ = P(CO₂).P(H₂)/P(CO).P(H₂O)\n10.1 = p²/(4-p)(4-p) = p/4-p = √10.1\np/4-p = 3.178. p = 3.04 bar','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','3.04 bar','60.8 bar','304 bar','30.4 bar','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71760,'chemistry','The equilibrium constant for the following reaction is 1.6 x 10⁵ at 1024 K.\nH₂(g) + Br₂(g) ⇌ 2HBr(g)\nFind the equilibrium pressure of all gases if 10.0 bar of HBr is introduced into a sealed container at 1024K.','10 bar','H₂(g) + Br₂(g) ⇌ 2HBr(g)\nFor the reaction, 2HBr(g) ⇌ H₂(g) + Br₂(g)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','10 bar','1 bar','100 bar','0.1 bar','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71761,'chemistry','Does the number of moles of reaction products increase, decrease or remain same when each of the following equilibria is subjected to a decrease in pressure by increasing the volume ?\n(a) PCl₅(g) ⇌ PCl₃(g) + Cl₂\n(b) CaO(s) + CO₂(g) ⇌ CaCO₃(s)\n(c) 3Fe(s) + 4H₂O(g) ⇌ Fe₃O₄(s) + 4H₂(g)','↑,↓,remains same','Applying Le Chatelier’s principle, on decreasing the pressure, equilibrium shifts to the direction in which pressure increases, i.e., number of moles of gaseous substances is more. Thus, moles of reaction products will increase in reaction (a), decrease in reaction (b) and remain same (np, = nr gaseous) in reaction (c).','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','↑,↓,remains same','↑,↑,remains same','↑,↓,↓','↑,↓,↑','NEW','11-210','11-186',NULL),
(71762,'chemistry','At 1127 K and 1 atmosphere pressure, a gaseous mixture of CO and CO₂ in equilibrium with solid carbon has 90.55% by mass.\nC(s) + CO₂(g) ⇌ 2CO(g)\nCalculate K꜀ for the reaction at the above temperature.','0.153','Lets the total mass of the mixture of CO and CO₂ is 100g , then CO = 90.55 g and CO₂ = 100-90.55 = 9.45g \n\nMoles of CO = 90.55/28 = 3.234\nMoles of CO₂ = 9.45/44 = 0.215\n\nMole fraction of CO = 3.234/(3.234+0.215) = 0.938\nMole fraction of CO₂ = 0.215/(3.234+0.215) = 0.062\n\n→ P(CO) = Mole fraction × Total pressure\n                 =  0.938 × 1atm = 0.938 atm\nP(CO₂) = 0.062 × 1atm = 0.062 atm\nKₚ for the reaction C(s) + CO₂(g) ⇌ 2CO(g)\n\nKₚ = P²(CO)/P(CO₂) = (0.938)²/0.062 = 14.19 \n\n△ng = 2-1 = 1 , Kₚ = K꜀(RT)^△ng\nK꜀ = Kₚ/(RT) = 14.19/0.0821 × 1127 = 0.153','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','0.153','0.0153','153','1.53','NEW','11-204','11-180',NULL),
(71763,'chemistry','Bromine monochloride (BrCl) decomposes into bromine and chlorine and reaches the equilibrium :\n2BrCl(g) ⇌ Br₂(g) + Cl₂(g)\nFor which K꜀ is 32 at 500 K. If initially pure BrCl is present at a concentration of 3.3× 10⁻³ mol /L, what is its molar concentration in the mixture at equilibrium ?','3 × 10⁻⁴ mol/L','2BrCl(g) ⇌ Br₂(g) + Cl₂(g)\nInitial.       3.3 × 10⁻³          0            0\nAt eq.      3.3 × 10⁻³ - x.      x/2         x/2\n\nK꜀ = (x/2)(x/2) / (3.3 × 10⁻³ - x)² = 32(Given) \nBy solving, x = 3 × 10⁻³\n\nAt equilibrium, [ BrCl ] = 3.3 × 10⁻³ - 3.0 × 10⁻³ = 3 × 10⁻⁴ mol/L','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','3 × 10⁻⁴ mol/L','3 × 10⁴ mol/L','6 × 10⁻⁴ mol/L','6 × 10⁴ mol/L','NEW','11-199','11-175',NULL),
(71764,'chemistry','Equilibrium constant K꜀ for the reaction, N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g) at 500 K is 0.061. At particular time, the analysis shows that the composition of the reaction mixture is : 3.0 mol /L of N₂ , 2.0 mol /L of H₂,0.50 mol /L of NH₃. Is the reaction at equilibrium ? If not, in which direction does the reaction tend to proceed to reach the equilibrium ?','Not in Equilibrium, Proceeds forward','Q꜀ = [NH₃]²/[N₂][H₂]³ = (0.5)²/3(0.2)³ = 0.0104\nGiven K꜀ = 0.061 \nAs Q꜀ ≠ K꜀ , reaction is not in equilibrium .\nAs Q꜀ < K꜀ , reaction will proceed in the forward reaction.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','Not in Equilibrium, Proceeds forward','Equilibrium','Not in Equilibrium, Proceeds Backwards','Equilibrium, Proceeds forward','NEW','11-206','11-182',NULL),
(71765,'chemistry','One of the reactions that takes place in producing steel from iron ore is the reduction of iron (II) oxide by carbon monoxide to give iron metal and CO₂\nFeO(s) + CO(g) ⇌ Fe(s) + CO₂(g); Kₚ = 0.265 atm at 1050 K\nWhat are the equilibrium partial pressures of CO and CO₂ at 1050 K if the initial pressures are :Pco = 1.4 atm and Pco₂ = 0.80 atm ?','1.739 & 0.461 atm','FeO(s) + CO(g) ⇌ Fe(s) + CO₂(g)\nInitial pressure  1.4 atm                             0.80 atm\nP at equilibrium 1.4 - p                              0.80+p\n→ At equilibrium, PCO₂ = (0.80+p)atm,\nPCO = (1.4-p)atm\nKₚ = P(CO₂)/P(CO) => 0.265 = 0.80 + p / 1.4-p \nBy solving, P = -0.339 atm\n\nP(CO)eq = 1.4 + 0.339 atm = 1.739 atm\nP(CO₂)eq = 1.4 - 0.339 atm = 0.461 atm','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','1.739 & 0.461 atm','1.739 & 1.739 atm','0.461 & 0.461 atm','0.461 & 1.739 atm','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71766,'chemistry','Kₚ = 0.04 atm at 898 K for the equilibrium shown below. What is the equilibrium concentration of C₂H6 when it is placed in a flask at 4 atm pressure and allowed to come to equilibrium.\nC₂H₆(g) ⇌ C₂H₄(g) + H₂(g)','3.62 atm','C₂H₆(g) ⇌ C₂H₄(g) + H₂(g)\nInitial pressure 4 atm         0              0\nAt eqi.                4-p              p              p \nApplying law of Equilibrium,\nKₚ = P(C₂H₆) × P(H₂) / P(C₂H₄) \n0.04 = p²/4-p\np² + 0.04p - 0.16 = 0\n→ p = [ - 0.04 ± √ 0.016 - 4(-0.16) ] / 2 = -0.04±0.80/2\nTaking positive value, p = 0.76/2 = 0.38 \n\n→ [C₂H₄]eq = 4-0.38 atm = 3.62 atm.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','3.62 atm','7.32 atm','362 atm','732 atm','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(2000031118,'physics','The dimensions $\\left[ {ML{T^{ - 2}}{A^{ - 2}}} \\right]$ belong to the','B','$\\left[ {ML{T^{ - 2}}{A^{ - 2}}} \\right] = $  Magnetic permeability','Dimensions','Units & Measurements','Medium',NULL,'self inductance','magnetic permeability','electric permittivity','magnetic flux',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(71767,'chemistry','What is the equilibrium concentration of each of the substances in the equilibrium when the initial concentration of ICl was 0.78 M ?\n2ICl(g) ⇌ I₂(g) + Cl₂(g) ; K꜀ = 0.14','0.446 M','2ICl(g) ⇌ I₂(g) + Cl₂(g) ;K꜀ = 0.14\nInitial mol conc.  0.78       0           0\nEq. mol conc.   0.78-2x     x            x\n\nApplying law of chemical equilibrium,\n\nK꜀ = [I₂][Cl₂]/[ICl]² => 0.14 = x²/(0.78-2x)²\nx = 0.292-0.748x \nx = 0.167\nHence at the equilibrium , [I₂] = [Cl₂] = 0.167 M\n[ICl] = 0.78 - 2(0.167) = 0.446 M','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','0.446 M','446 M','4.46 M','44.6 M','NEW','11-199','11-175',NULL),
(71768,'chemistry','At 700 K, the equilibrium constant for the reaction H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g) is 54.8. If 0.5 mol /L of HI(g) is present at equilibrium at 700 K, what are the concentration of H₂(g) and I₂(g) ? Assume that we initially started with HI(g) and allowed it to reach equilibrium at 700 K.','0.068 mol/L','2HI(g) ⇌H₂(g) + I₂(g)\nInitial conc.       a           0          0\nAt equilibrium.  0.5        x           x\n\nK꜀ = [H₂][I₂]/[HI]² or 1/54.8 = x²/(0.5)² \nx = 0.068 \n[H₂]=[I₂]=0.068.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','0.068 mol/L','0.68 mol/L','68 mol/L','0.0068 mol/L','NEW','11-199','11-175',NULL),
(71769,'chemistry','If 1 mole of H₂O and 1 mole of CO are taken in a 10 litre vessel and heated to 725 K, at equilibrium point 40 percent of water (by mass) reacts with carbon monoxide according to equation,\nH₂O(g) + CO(g) ⇌ H₂(g) + CO₂(g)\nCalculate the equilibrium constant for the reaction.','0.44','From the above reaction,\n                            H₂O(g) + CO(g) ⇌ H₂(g) + CO₂(g)\nintial no of moles.    1            1                 0          0\nAt equilibrium.          0.6        0.6             0.4      0.4\n→[H₂O] = 0.6/10 = 0.06 mol/L\n    [ CO ] = 0.6/10 =  0.06 mol/L\n    [H₂] = 0.4/10 = 0.04 mol/L and [CO₂] = 0.4/10 = 0.04 mol/L\n\nK꜀ = [H₂][CO₂]/[H₂O] [CO] = 0.04×0.04/0.06×0.06 = 0.44.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','0.44','0.88','0.11','0.66','NEW','11-199','11-175',NULL),
(71770,'chemistry','A mixture of 1.57 mol of N₂, 1.92 mol of H₂ and 8.13 mol of NH₃ is introduced into a 20 L reaction vessel at 500 K. At this temperature, the equilibrium constant K꜀ for the reaction given as follows :\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g) is 1.7 × 10⁻².\nIs this reaction at equilibrium ? If not, what is the direction of net reaction ?','Not in Equilibrium, Proceeds Backwards','The reaction is \nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g) \n\nQ꜀ = [NH₃]²/[N₂][H₂]³ = (8.13/20)²/[(1.57/20)(1.97/20)] = 2.38 × 10³\n\nAs Q꜀≠K꜀ , the reaction mixture is not in equilibrium.\nAs Q꜀= K꜀ , the net reaction will be in the backward reaction.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','Not in Equilibrium, Proceeds Backwards','Equilibrium','Not in Equilibrium, Proceeds Forward','Equilibrium, Proceeds Backwards','NEW','11-206','11-182',NULL),
(71771,'chemistry','A sample of HI(g) is placed in a flask at a pressure of 0.2 atm. At equilibrium partial pressure of HI(g) is 0.04 atm. What is Kₚ for the given equilibrium ?\n2HI(g) ⇌ H₂(g) + I₂(g)','4','2HI(g) ⇌ H₂(g) + I₂(g)\nInitial pressure      0.2 atm          0     0\npressure at eq.      0.04 atm      0.16  0.16\n                                                           2       2\n                                           = 0.08 atm. = 0.08 atm\nAccording to law of chemical equilibrium,\n\nKₚ = Pₕ₂ × Pₗ₂/Pₕₗ² = 0.08 × 0.08 / (0.04)² = 4\nKₚ = 4.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','4','3','2','1','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71772,'chemistry','At 450 K, Kₚ = 2.0 x 10¹⁰ bar for the given reaction at equilibrium.\n2SO₂(g)+ O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g)\nWhat is K꜀ at this temperature?','7.47 × 10¹¹ /M','Kₚ = K꜀(RT)^△ng  or K꜀ = Kₚ/(RT)^△ng\n\n△ng = 2-(2+1) = -1 , T = 450 k\nR = 0.083 bar L/K mol\n\nK꜀ = 2 × 10¹⁰/ (0.083 × 450)⁻¹\nK꜀ = 2 × 10¹⁰ ×0.083 × 450 = 7.47 × 10¹¹/M','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','7.47 × 10¹¹ /M','8.47 × 10¹¹ /M','9.47 × 10¹¹ /M','7.47 × 10²¹ /M','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71773,'chemistry','At a certain temperature and total pressure of 10⁵ Pa, iodine vapour contains 40% by volume of I atoms. I₂(g) ⇌ 2l(g)Calculate Kₚ for the equilibrium.','2.67 × 10⁴','I₂(g) ⇌ 2l\nIntial moles.                  1          0\nMoles at equilibrium   1-x      2x\n\nTotal moles at equilibrium = 1-x+2x = 1+x\nBut 2x/1+x = 0.4(given) ; x = 1/4\n\nSo mole fraction of I₂ = 1-(1/4)/1+(1/4) = 0.6\n\nMole fraction of I = 2 × (1/4) / 1+(1/4) = 0.4\n\nPₗ₁ = 0.4 × 10⁵ Pa\nPₗ₂ = 0.6 × 10⁵ Pa\n\nKₚ = Pₗ₁²/Pₗ₂² = (0.4)²(10⁵)²/(0.6)(10⁵) = 2.67 × 10⁴','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','2.67 × 10⁴','2.67 × 10⁶','267 × 10⁴','2.67 × 10¹²','NEW','11-202','11-178',NULL),
(71774,'chemistry','What is K꜀ for the following equilibrium when the equilibrium concentration of each substance is : [SO₂] = 0.60 M, [O₂] = 0.82 M and [SO₃] = 1.90 M\n2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g).','12.299 L/mol','2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g)\n\nApplying law of chemical equilibrium ,\n\nK꜀ = [SO₃]²/[SO₂]²[O₂] = (1.9)²/(0.6)²(0.82) = 12.299 L/mol','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','12.299 L/mol','11.299 L/mol','1.299 L/mol','122.99 L/mol','NEW','11-199','11-175',NULL),
(71801,'chemistry','A : At higher temperature, K_w of water remains unaltered.\nR : K_w is a constant.','Both A & R are false','At higher temperature, H₂O dissociates more and hence K_w value increases.\ni.e., K_w is dependant upon temperature.\n\nREFERENCE : NCERT XI page no - 217','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','11-217','11-193',NULL),
(71802,'chemistry','A : HCl, HNO₃ and H₂SO₄ are equally strong acids in water.\nR : Water is a stronger acid than alcohols.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','HCl, HNO₃ and H₂SO₄ are almost equally strong in water. They have close values of pKₐ.\nAlso, H₂O is stronger acid than alcohol.\n\nREFERENCE: NCERT XI page no -216 , 5th paragraph','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','11-217','11-193',NULL),
(71803,'chemistry','A : Increasing the concentration of H₂ will increase magnitude of equilibrium constant of the reaction\n H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI\nR : Value of K꜀ depends upon the concentration of reactants and products taken.','Both A & R are false','Value of K꜀ indeed depends upon concentration of products and reactants at equilibrium, not initial\n concentration. But in the given equation, increase in concentration of H₂ or I₂ or Hl will not affect the equilibrium\n constant\n\nREFERENCE: NCERT XI page no -210 , 3rd paragraph','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','11-210','11-186','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/71803q.png'),
(71804,'chemistry','A : Increasing the temperature, increases [H⁺] concentration in water.\nR : Water is acidic at higher temperature.','A is true but R is false','Upon increasing temperature, H⁺ concentration increases, but so does OH⁻ concentration. Overall, water\n remains neutral as [H⁺] = [OH⁻]\n\nREFERENCE : NCERT XI page no -211','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','11-211','11-187',NULL),
(71805,'chemistry','A : Solution of CH₃COONH₄ is a buffer solution.\nR : H⁺ ion added will be consumed by CH₃COO⁻ ion and OH⁻ ion added will be consumed by NH₄\n⁺ ion.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','CH₃COO⁻ NH₄\n⁺ is a buffer solution since.\n\nCH₃COO⁻ NH₄\n⁺  → CH₃COO⁻ ⇌CH₃COOH + NH₄\n⁺ ⇌NH₄\nOH\nWhen H+ is added,  CH₃COO⁻ takes up H⁺ ion and when OH⁻ is added. NH₄\n⁺ consume it.\n\nREFERENCE: NCERT XI page no -226','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','11-226','11-202',NULL),
(71806,'chemistry','A : Ksp is a constant value for any salt, at particular temperature.\nR : Solubility of any salt is constant at a particular temperature.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','At a particular temperature and solvent, Ksp is constant because solubility of salts depend upon temperature.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71807,'chemistry','A : H₃O⁺ ion from water is also taken in consideration while calculating the pH of very dilute solution (say\n concentration = 10⁻⁹ M) of acid.\nR : [H₃O⁺] from water is only available in very dilute solution of acid.','A is true but R is false','For very dilute solution of acid, dissociation of water is also taken into account.\nFor 10⁻⁹ acid.\nH⁺= 10⁻⁹(Due to acid) + 10⁻⁷(Due to water)\nThis H₃O⁺ ion due to water is present in all acidic solutions. At higher acid concentrations, we neglect this\n 10⁻⁷ M value for convenience in calculation.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71808,'chemistry','A : pH of 10⁻⁸ M HCl solution is approx 6.9.\nR : HCl is a strong acid.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','HCl is indeed a strong acid.\nFor 10⁻⁸ M solution of HCL\n\n[H⁺] = 10⁻⁸ + 10⁻⁷(Due to water) = 1.1 × 10⁻⁷\n\npH = -log₁₀ [H⁺]= 6.9','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71809,'chemistry','A : For equilibrium Ice⇌ Water on increasing temperature and pressure more of water will form.\nR : Forward reaction is endothermic and volume decreases on product side.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Heat + ice ⇌ water\n\nOn increasing pressure, volume decreases. For water, decrease in volume of water is less (∵ density increase)\n⇒ Concentration of ice increases with respect to water, hence equilibrium shifts towards right.\nAlso, reaction is endothermic, hence increase in temperature results in melting of ice.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','11-211','11-187',NULL),
(71810,'chemistry','A : At equilibrium concentration of the reactant and product does not change with time for a chemical reaction.\nR : The rate of reaction is zero at equilibrium.','A is true but R is false','The rate of forward reaction = rate of backward reaction\nThe above condition denotes equilibrium\nSo, the concentration of reactants and products remain constant and do not change with time.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','11-196','11-172',NULL),
(71811,'chemistry','A : pH of 0.1 M HCl solution is less than 0.1 M HCN solution.\nR : In equimolar solutions, the number of ionisable H⁺ present in HCl is less than present in HCN solution.','A is true but R is false','HCl is stronger acid than HCN\n∴ pH of HCl is lesser than pH of HCN when both are equimolar.\nNumber of ionisable H⁺ is 1 for both HCl and HCN','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','11-224','11-200',NULL),
(71812,'chemistry','A : A catalyst does not alter the equilibrium constant of a reaction.\nR : A catalyst does not alter the position of chemical equilibrium.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A catalyst neither alters position of chemical equilibrium, nor does it alter the equilibrium constant. It just\n facilitates the reaction.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','11-212','11-188',NULL),
(71813,'chemistry','A : pH of equimolar solution of NH₄Cl and NH₄OH does not change when small amount of HCl is added to it.\nR : pOH of above solution is equal to pKb of the buffer.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','Equimolar solution of NH₄Cl and NH₄OH acts as buffer. So when HCl is added in small amount, it resists\n change in pH.\n\nAgain, applying Henderson-Hasselbalch equation, pOH = pKb + log(salt/base)\n∴ [salt] = [base]\n∴ pOH = pKb','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','11-226','11-202',NULL),
(71814,'chemistry','A : The reaction 2NO(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g) is favoured in the forward direction with increase of pressure.\nR : The above reaction is exothermic','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','The given reaction is exothermic. When pressure increases, equilibrium shifts towards the side of lower number\n of gaseous moles.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','11-210','11-186',NULL),
(71815,'chemistry','A : pH of 1 M NaCl solution is 7 at 25ºC.\nR : pH of this solution decreases when it is diluted 100 times.','A is true but R is false','When a solution is diluted, there would not be any effect, just the solubility of NaCl would increase.\nThis is because NaCl is neutral in H₂O as it is made up of a strong acid and a strong base.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','11-226','11-202',NULL),
(71816,'chemistry','A : CO₂ is a Lewis acid.\nR : H₂SO₄ is Arrhenius acid as well as Bronsted acid.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','H₂SO₄ is Arrhenius as well as Bronsted acid since if releases H⁺ ions.\nAlso, CO₂ is a Lewis acid because of oxygen atoms which are highly electronegative.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','11-216','11-192',NULL),
(71817,'chemistry','A : For H₂CO₃ Kₐ₁< Kₐ₂\nR : H₂CO₃ is weaker acid than HCO₃\n⁻','Both A & R are false','For any acid, Kₐ\n₁> Kₐ₂ i.e., H₂CO₃\nwould be a stronger acid than HCO₃\n⁻ due to electrostatic force of\n attraction in the latter','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','11-224','11-200',NULL),
(71818,'chemistry','A : pH of mixture of 0.1 M HCN and 0.05 M NaOH is less than 7.\nR : HCN is a weak acid and NaOH is a strong base.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','HCN + NaOH → NaCN + H₂\nO\n\nt=1 , Meq    0.1V    0.05V        0\nFinal , Meq  0.05V   -               0.05 \n\nResulting solution will be acidic buffer so pH < 7','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(71819,'chemistry','A : Solubility of AgCl is more in NH₃ than in pure water.\nR : Common ion effect decreases the concentration of Cl⁻ when NH₃ is added to 0.1 M AgCl solution.','A is true but R is false','Solubility of AgCl is more in NH₃  than in pure water because it forms a complex.\n\nAgCl + NH₃ → [ Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺Cl⁻\nThere are no common ions in a solution containing AgCl and NH₃','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','11-230','11-206',NULL),
(71820,'chemistry','A : Precipitate formation takes place when Kᵢₚ > Kₛₚ.\nR : Kᵢₚ = Kₛₚ for a saturated solution.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','Precipitate is formed only when ionic product > solubility product.\nFor a saturated solution, Kᵢₚ = Kₛₚ','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Assertion-Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(41901,'chemistry','Which of the following, set of molecules will have  zero dipole moment?','Boron trifluoride, beryllium difluoride, carbon dioxide, 1, 4-dichlorobenzene','BF₃ is symmetrical molecule, any symmetry present leads to 0 dipole moment BeF₂ has linear geometry both bond moments are in opposite direction so resultant dipole moment = zero CO₂ being linear has zero dipole moment 1,4-dichlorobenzene, both Cl are at opposite position to each other so have bond moment in opposite direction so resultant dipole moment=0 \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry, Class 11 page no.111) (NEET 2020)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Ammonia, beryllium difluoride, water, 1, 4-dichlorobenzene','Boron trifluoride, hydrogen fluoride, carbon dioxide, 1, 3-dichlorobenzene','Nitrogen trifluoride, beryllium difluoride, water, 1, 3-dichlorobenzene','Boron trifluoride, beryllium difluoride, carbon dioxide, 1, 4-dichlorobenzene','NEW','11-134','11-134',NULL),
(41902,'chemistry','Which of the following is the correct order of dipole moment ?','BF₃ < NF₃ < NH₃< H₂O','BF₃ is symmetrical molecule, any symmetry present leads to 0 dipole moment NF₃ three bond moments and one orbital moment (due to lone pair) are in almost opposite direction cancel the dipole moment of each other, due to trigonal pyramidal geometry they are not perfectly opposite so resultant dipole moment is almost zero(0.23) but not zero NH₃ three bond moments and one orbital moment are all in same direction so resultant dipole moment has significant value(1.47) In water Two orbiral moments and two bond moments are in same direction so has even more dipole moment than NH₃ as oxygen is more electronegative. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry, Class 11 page no.111) (Odisha NEET 2019)p','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','NH₃ < BF₃ < NF₃ < H₂O','BF₃ < NF₃ < NH₃< H₂O','BF₃ < NH₃ < NF₃ < H₂O','H₂O < NF₃< NH₃ < BF₃','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41903,'chemistry','The species, having bond angles of 120° is:','BCl₃','BCl₃ having three sigma bonds mean three hybrid orbital so sp² hybridized sp² hybridization has three hybrid orbitals and trigonal planar geometry and 120⁰ angle (NEET 2017)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','ClF₃','NCl₃','BCl₃','PH₃','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41904,'chemistry','Consider the molecules CH₄, NH₃ and H₂O. Which of the given statements is false?','The H — O — H bond angle in H₂O is larger than the H — C — H bond angle in CH₄.','H-O-H bond angle is smaller due to lone-pair -lone-pair repulsion, water molecule tends to arrange in a way to overcome this repulsion so it do reduction in bond angle 104.5⁰ more repulsion smaller will be bond angle CH₄ only have bond pair -bond repulsuion which are easily minimized by taking angle of 109.5⁰. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry, Class 11 page no.114) (NEET-I 2016)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','The H — O — H bond angle in H₂O is smaller than the H — N — H bond angle in NH₃.','The H — C — H bond angle in CH₄ is larger than the H — N — H bond angle in NH₃.','The H — C — H bond angle in CH₄, the H — N — H bond angle in NH₃, and the H — O — H bond angle in H₂O are all greater than 90°','The H — O — H bond angle in H₂O is larger than the H — C — H bond angle in CH₄.','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(2000031142,'physics','Taking into account of the significant figures, what is the value of 9.99 $m$-0.0099 $m$?','A','In subtration the number of decimal places in the result should be equal to the number of decimal places of that term in the 	operation which contain lesser number of decimal places.<br>The answer should have three siginificant figures','Significant Figures and Rounding off Numbers','Units & Measurements','Medium',NULL,'$9.98\\,m$','$9.980\\,m$','$9.9\\,m$','$9.9801\\,m$',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(41905,'chemistry','Which of the following molecules has the maximum dipole moment?','NH₃','NF₃ three bond moments and one orbital moment (due to lone pair) are in almost opposite direction cancel the dipole moment of each other, due to trigonal pyramidal geometry they are not perfectly oposite so resultant dipole moment is almost zero(0.23) but not zero NH₃ three bond moments and one orbital moment are all in same direction so resultant dipole moment has significant value(1.47) CO₂ has linear geometry so 𝝁=zero CH₄ has symmetry so 𝝁 is also zero for symmetrical molecules. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry, Class 11 page no.111) (NEET 2014)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','CO₂','CH₄','NH₃','NF₃','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(41906,'chemistry','In the structure of ClF₃, the number of lone pairs of electrons on central atom ‘Cl’ is:','Two','Cl present in seventh group having three lone pairs and can make only one bond but in ClF₃ Cl is making three bond by using one lone pair out of three so it has two lone pairs left. (NEET 2018)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','One','Two','Four','Three','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(41907,'chemistry','Predict the correct order among the following:','lone pair - lone pair > lone pair - bond pair > bond pair - bond pair','lp-lp repulsion are maximum as lone pairs occupy large space as compared bond pairs because lone pairs are under the hold of only one nucleus while bond pairs lie between two nuclei ,more space more will be repulsion. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 113; Paragraph 2) (NEET-I 2016)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','bond pair - bond pair > lone pair - bond pair > lone pair - lone pair','lone pair - bond pair > bond pair - bond pair > lone pair - lone pair','lone pair - lone pair > lone pair - bond pair > bond pair - bond pair','lone pair - lone pair > bond pair - bond pair > lone pair - bond pair','NEW','11-113','11-113',NULL),
(41908,'chemistry','Which of the following species contains equal number of σ and  𝞹-bonds?','XeO₄','(CN)₂ 3σ 4𝞹, CH₂(CN)₂ 6σ 4𝞹, HCO₃⁻ 4σ 1𝞹, XeO₄ 4σ 4𝞹. (NEET 2015)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','(CN)₂','(CH)₂(CN)₂','HCO₃ ⁻','XeO₄','NEW','11-113','11-113',NULL),
(41909,'chemistry','Which one of the following molecules contains no 𝞹- bond?','H₂O','O=S=O  2𝞹 bond\n\nO=C=O  2𝞹 bond\n\nO=N-O⁻ 1𝞹 bond\n\nH-O-H no 𝞹 bond (NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','SO₂','NO₂','CO₂','H₂O','NEW','11-113','11-113',NULL),
(41910,'chemistry','Which of the following pairs of compounds is isoelectronic and isostructural?','None of these','TeI₂ and XeF₂ have 158 and 72 electrons respectively so not isoelectronic and have bent and linear geometry so also not isostructural IBr₂⁻and XeF₂ have 124 and 72 electrons respectively so not isoelectronic and have linear  geometry so also isostructural IF₃ and XeF₂ have 80 and 72 electrons respectively so not isoelectronic and have T-shaped and linear geometry so also not isostructural. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 116) (NEET 2017)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','TeI₂, XeF₂','IBr₂ ⁻ , XeF₂','IF₃, XeF₂','None of these','NEW','11-116','11-116',NULL),
(41911,'chemistry','The hybridizations of atomic orbitals of nitrogen in NO₂⁺ , NO₃⁻ and NH₄⁺ respectively are:','sp, sp² and sp³','NO₂⁺, O=N⁺=O having two sigma bonds which is only possible in sp, NO₃⁻ Three sigma bonds possible in sp² hybridization, NH₄⁺ Four sigma bonds possible in sp³ hybridization. \n\nNote: Number of sigma bonds is equal to number of hybrid orbitals. \n\nIf lone pair present this also involve in hybridization so number of sigma bonds + number of lone pairs = Hybrid orbitals. (NEET-II 2016)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','sp, sp³ and sp²','sp², sp³ and sp','sp, sp² and sp³','sp² , sp and sp³','NEW','11-116','11-116',NULL),
(41912,'chemistry','Which of the following pairs of ions is isoelectronic and isostructural?','CO₃²⁻, NO₃⁻','CO₃²⁻ \nC =  6 electrons\nO = 8×3 = 24 electrons\nnegative charges = 2\n6 + 24 + 2 = 32 electrons\n\nNO₃⁻\nN = 7 electrons\nO = 8×3 = 24 electrons\nnegative charge = 1\n7 + 24 + 1 = 32 electrons\n\nso CO₃²⁻, NO₃⁻ isoelectronic\n(NEET-II 2016)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','CO₃²⁻, NO₃⁻','ClO₃⁻ , CO₃²⁻','SO₃²⁻, NO₃⁻','ClO₃⁻ , SO₃²⁻','NEW','11-116','11-116',NULL),
(41913,'chemistry','The correct geometry and hybridization for XeF₄ are:','Octahedral, sp³d²','Number of sigma bonds + number of lone pairs = Hybrid orbitals, XeF₄ making four sigma bonds and have two lone pairs , 4 + 2 = 6 hybrid orbitals, this is possible in sp³d² with octahedral geometry \nIt is octahedral in geometry while its shape will be square planar.\n\n(NEET-II 2016)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Octahedral, sp³d²','Trigonal bipyramidal, sp³ d','Planar triangle, sp³ d³','Square planar, sp³d² .','NEW','11-116','11-116',NULL),
(41914,'chemistry','Among the following, which one is a wrong statement?','SeF₄ and CH₄ have same shape.','SeF₄ and CH₄ do not have same shape\n\nSeF₄  4 sigma and one lone pair so 5 hybrid orbitals (sp³d)\n\nCH₄ only four sigma bonds present so 4 hybrid orbitals(sp³)\n\nboth undergoing different hybridization so will not have same shape\n(NEET-II 2016)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','PH₅ and BiCl₅ do not exist.','p𝛑-d𝛑 bonds are present in SO₂.','SeF₄ and CH₄ have same shape.','I₃⁺ has bent geometry','NEW','11-116','11-116',NULL),
(41915,'chemistry','In which of the following pairs, both the species are not isostructural?','XeF₄, XeO₄','XeF₄, 4 sigma bonds, two lone pairs so will have 6 hybrid orbitals and undergo sp³d² hybridization due to lone pair it will have square planar structure instead of ocxtahedral XeO₄ four sigma bond but n lone pair so 4 hybrid orbitals it needs which is possible in sp³ hybridization will have tetrahedral structure (NEET 2015)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Diamond, Silicon carbide','NH₃, PH₃','XeF₄, XeO₄','SiCl₄, PCl₄⁺','NEW','11-116','11-116',NULL),
(41916,'chemistry','Maximum bond angle at nitrogen is present in which of the following?','NO₂⁺','NO₂⁺ sp hybridized 180°  bond angle\n\nNO₃⁻ sp² hybridized 120° bond angle\n\nNO₂  sp² hybridized 134° bond angle \n\nNO₂⁻ sp² hybridized  115°  bond angle\n(NEET 2015)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','NO₂⁺','NO₃⁻','NO₂','NO₂⁻','NEW','11-116','11-116',NULL),
(41917,'chemistry','Which one of the following species has planar triangular shape?','NO₃⁻','NO₃⁻ sp² hybridized 120° bond angle trigonal planar shape. (NEET 2014)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','N₃','NO₃⁻','NO₂⁻','CO₂','NEW','11-116','11-116',NULL),
(41918,'chemistry','XeF₂ is isostructural with:','ICl₂⁻','ICl₂⁻ two sigma bonds and 3 lone pairs present so 5 hybrid orbitals required which is possible in sp³d hybridization XeF₂ two sigma bonds and 3 lone pairs present so 5 hybrid orbitals required which is possible in sp³d hybridization both have same hybridization so isostructural. (NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','SbCl₃','BaCl₂','TeF₂','ICl₂⁻','NEW','11-116','11-116',NULL),
(41919,'chemistry','Which of the following is a polar molecule?','SF₄','Polarity depends on electronegativity difference and dipole moment all have zero dipole moment except SF₄ so it is polar. (NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','SiF₄','XeF₄','BF₃','SF₄','NEW','11-116','11-116',NULL),
(41920,'chemistry','The outer orbitals of C in ethene molecule can be considered to be hybridized to give three equivalent sp² orbitals. The total number of sigma (σ) and pi (𝛑) bonds in ethene molecule is:','5 sigma (σ) and 1 pi (𝛑) bonds','H₂C= CH₂ each C making three sigma bonds two with H and one common sigma bond with other carbon so total 5 sigma bonds present both carbon making one pi bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 123; Paragraph 1)  (Karnataka NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','3 sigma (σ) and 2 pi (𝛑) bonds','4 sigma (σ) and 1 pi (𝛑) bonds','5 sigma (σ) and 1 pi (𝛑) bonds','1 sigma (σ) and 2 pi (𝛑) bonds.','NEW','11-123','11-123',NULL),
(41921,'chemistry','In which of the following pairs both the species have sp³ hybridization?','NF₃, H₂O','NF₃, three sigma bonds with F and has one lone pair so total 4 hybrid orbitals it needs which is possible in sp³ H₂O 2 sigma bonds with H and has 2 lone pair so total 4 hybrid orbitals it needs which is possible in sp³ both has sp³ hybridization. \n\nReference : ( NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 122; Paragraph 1)  (Karnataka NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','SiF4, BeH₂','NF₃, H₂O','NF₃, BF₃','H₂S, BF₃','NEW','11-123','11-123',NULL),
(41922,'chemistry','Consider the following species : CN⁺, CN⁻ , NO and CN. Which one of these will have the highest bond order?','CN⁻','NO = 7 + 8 elctrons = 15 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(𝛑*2pₓ)¹=(𝛑*2pᵧ)⁰\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2(Nᵦ-Nₐ)\n                           1/2(10-5)   = 2.5\n\nCN⁻  =  6  + 8  = 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2(Nᵦ-Nₐ)\n                           1/2(10-4)   = 3\n\nCN⁺  =   6 +  6  = 12 electrons\n\nσ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2(Nᵦ-Nₐ)\n\n                           1/2(8-4)   =   2\n\nCN  = 6 + 7 = 13 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)¹\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ)\n                           1/2(9-4)   =   2.5\n\nCN⁻   has highest bond order\n\n(NEET 2018)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','NO','CN⁻','CN⁺','CN','NEW','11-122','11-122',NULL),
(41923,'chemistry','Which one of the following pairs of species have the same bond order?','CN⁻, CO','CN⁻  =  6  + 8  = 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                           1/2(10-4)   =   3\n\nCO = 6 + 8 = 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                           1/2(10-4)   =   3\n(NEET 2017)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','O₂, NO⁺','CN⁻, CO','N₂, O₂⁻','CO, NO','NEW','11-122','11-122',NULL),
(41924,'chemistry','Which of the following is paramagnetic?','O₂⁻','O + O⁻  = 8 + 9 = 17 electrons\n\nσ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2p)²(𝛑*2pₓ)²=(𝛑*2pᵧ)¹\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (10-7) = 1.5\n\nCO = 6 + 8 = 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                           1/2(10-4)   =   3\n\nCN⁻  =  6  + 8  = 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                           1/2(10-4)   =   3\n\nNO = 7 + 7 elctrons = 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2p)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                           1/2(10-4)   =   3\n\nO₂⁻ have unpaired electron while all other has paired electron\nparamagnetic behaviour is related to unpiared electron\n(NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','CN⁻','NO⁺','CO','O₂⁻','NEW','11-122','11-122',NULL),
(41925,'chemistry','The pair of species that has the same bond order in the following is:','CO, NO⁺','"O + O  = 8 + 8 = 16 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2p)²(𝛑*2pₓ)¹=(𝛑*2pᵧ)¹\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (10-6) = 2\n\nN + N  = 7+ 7= 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2p)²(σ2p𝓏)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (10-4) = 3\n\nCO = 6 + 8 = 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                           1/2(10-4)   =   3\n\nCN⁻  =  6  + 8  = 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                           1/2(10-4)   =   3\n\nNO⁺  = 7 + 7 elctrons = 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2p)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                           1/2(10-4)   =   3\n\n(Karnataka NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','CO, NO⁺','NO⁻ , CN⁻','O₂, N₂','O₂, B₂','NEW','11-122','11-122',NULL),
(41926,'chemistry','In which of the following ionization processes the bond energy increases and the magnetic behaviour changes from paramagnetic to diamagnetic?','NO → NO⁺','O + O  = 8 + 8 = 16 electrons\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(𝛑*2pₓ)¹=(𝛑*2pᵧ)¹\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (10-6) = 2\nParamagnetic\nO + O⁺  = 8 + 7 = 15 electrons\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(𝛑*2pₓ)¹\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (10-5) = 2.5\nParamagnetic\nN + N  = 7+ 7= 14 electrons\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)²\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (10-4) = 3\nDiamagnetic\nN + N⁺  = 7+ 6= 13 electrons\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)¹\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (9-4) = 2.5\nParamagnetic\nC + C = 6+ 6= 12 electrons\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (8-4) = 2\nDiamagnetic\nC + C⁺ = 6+ 5= 11 electrons\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)¹\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (7-4) = 1.5\nParamagnetic\nNO = 7 + 8 elctrons = 15 electrons\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(𝛑*2pₓ)¹=(𝛑*2pᵧ)⁰\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                           1/2(10-5)   =   2.5\nParamagnetic\nNO⁺ = 7 + 7 elctrons = 14 electrons\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                           1/2(10-4)   =   3\nDiamagnetic\nNO → NO⁺ bond order increase,bond energy increase magnetic behav-\nour changes\n\n(Karnataka NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','O₂ → O₂⁺','C₂ → C₂⁺','NO → NO⁺','N₂ → N₂⁺','NEW','11-122','11-122',NULL),
(41927,'chemistry','Identify a molecule which does not exist:','He₂','He-He = (σ1s)²(σ*1s)²\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                           1/2(2-2)   =   0  no bond will be formed\n(NEET 2020)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','He₂','Li₂','C₂','O₂','NEW','11-122','11-122',NULL),
(41928,'chemistry','Which of the following diatomic molecular species has only pi bonds according to Molecular Orbital Theory?','C₂','"O + O  = 8 + 8 = 16 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(𝛑*2pₓ)¹=(𝛑*2pᵧ)¹\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (10-6) = 2\nparamagnetic\n\nN + N  = 7+ 7= 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ)\n                             1/2 (10-4) = 3\nDiamagnetic\n\nC + C = 6+ 6= 12 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ)\n                             1/2 (8-4) = 2\nDiamagnetic\n\nBe + Be = 4+ 4= 8 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)¹=(𝛑2pᵧ)¹\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (8-4) = 2\nParamagnetic   \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130) (NEET 2019)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Be₂','O₂','N₂','C₂','NEW','11-130','11-130',NULL),
(41929,'chemistry','Which of the following is paramagnetic?','O₂','"O + O  = 8 + 8 = 16 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(𝛑*2pₓ)¹=(𝛑*2pᵧ)¹\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (10-6) = 2\nparamagnetic\n\nN + N  = 7+ 7= 14 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²(σ2p𝓏)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (10-4) = 3\nDiamagnetic\n\nC + C = 6+ 6= 12 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)²=(𝛑2pᵧ)²\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (8-4) = 2\nDiamagnetic\n\nBe + Be = 4+ 4= 8 electrons\n\n(σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(𝛑2pₓ)¹=(𝛑2pᵧ)¹\n\nBond order (b.o) = 1/2( Nᵦ-Nₐ )\n                             1/2 (8-4) = 2\n\nParamagnetic\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130) \n\n(NEET 2019)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','N₂','H₂','Li₂','O₂','NEW','11-130','11-130',NULL),
(41930,'chemistry','Decreasing order of stability of O₂, O₂ ⁻ , O₂ ⁺ and O₂ ²⁻ is:','O₂⁺ > O₂ > O₂ ⁻ > O₂ ²⁻','Stability is directly proportional to bond order\n\nO₂⁺  bond order = 2.5\nO₂  bond order = 2\nO₂ ⁻ bond order 1.5\nO₂ ²⁻ bond order 1 \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130) \n(NEET 2015)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','O₂²⁻ > O₂ ⁻ > O₂ > O₂⁺','O₂ > O₂⁺ > O₂ ²⁻ > O₂ ⁻','O₂ – > O₂ ²– > O₂ ⁺> O₂','O₂⁺ > O₂ > O₂ ⁻ > O₂ ²⁻','NEW','11-130','11-130',NULL),
(41931,'chemistry','The correct bond order in the following species is:','O₂ ⁻ < O₂ ⁺ < O₂ ²⁺','Stability is directly proportional to bond order\n\nO₂⁺  bond order = 2.5\nO₂  bond order = 2\nO₂ ⁻ bond order 1.5\nO₂ ²⁻ bond order 1 \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 130)  (NEET 2015)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','O₂ ⁺ < O₂ ⁻ < O₂ ²⁺','O₂ ⁻ < O₂ ⁺ < O₂ ²⁺','O₂ ²⁺ < O₂ ⁺ < O₂ ⁻','O₂ ²⁺ < O₂ ⁻ < O₂ ⁺','NEW','11-130','11-130',NULL),
(41932,'chemistry','Which one of the following compounds shows the presence of intramolecular hydrogen bond?','Cellulose','H₂O₂, HCN and conc. CH₃COOH form intermolecular hydrogen bonding while cellulose has intramolecular hydrogen bonding. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 132) (NEET-II 2016)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >>  Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','H₂O₂','HCN','Cellulose','Concentrated acetic acid','NEW','11-132','11-132',NULL),
(41933,'chemistry','C-O bond length is minimum in:','CO','C-O bond length is minimum in CO because isoelectronic has the same bond order according to molecular orbital theory. (NEET2020)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','CO','CO2','CO₃²⁻','HCOO–','NEW','11-107','11-107',NULL),
(41934,'chemistry','Molecules are held together in a crystal by:','Van der Waal’s attraction','Molecules in a crystalline structure are held together by the weak Van der Waal\'s interaction. (NEET 2020)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Van der Waal’s attraction','hydrogen bond','electrostatic attraction','dipole-dipole attraction','NEW','11-107','11-107',NULL),
(41935,'chemistry','Which of the molecules does not have a permanent dipole moment?','CS₂','The structure of CS₂ is linear and therefore it does not have permanent dipole moment. \n\nIt is represented as S = C = S. (NEET 2016)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','CS₂','H₂S','SO₂','SO₃','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(41936,'chemistry','the most preferred structure with lowest energy for SO3 is : - diagram(41936Q)','1','HINT: Structure with least formal charge is most stable.\n\nExplanation:\n\nMost preferred structure of SO3 with lowest energy is the one that contains maximum number of covalent bonds.\n\nAlso , formal charges also tells about the lowest energy structure from a number of possible Lewis structures for a given molecule. \n\nThus, the structure with more number of covalent bonds or smallest formal charges on the atoms will be most stable.','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-104','11-104','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/41936q.png'),
(41937,'chemistry','in NO3-1 ion, the number of bond pair and lone pair on nitrogen atoms are respectively :','4,0','The nitrogen form four bonds. One double bond, one coordinate bond and one single bond. All five electron of nitrogen is used up. Hence it is left with zero lone pair.','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','2,2','3,1','1,3','4,0','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(41938,'chemistry','among the folowing, how many species dont have eight electrons in outermost shell ? NH3 , AlCl3, BeCl2, CCl4','3','Hint: Phosphorus has 5 electrons in its outermost shell.\n\nNitrogen has 5 electrons in its outermost shell and it forms three sigma bonds with three hydrogen atoms. Thus, nitrogen has 8 electrons in its outermost shell in NH3.\n\nAluminum has 3 electrons in its outermost shell and it forms three sigma bonds with three chlorine atoms. Thus, aluminum has total 6 electrons in its outermost shell in AlCl3.\n\nBeryllium has 2 electrons in its outermost shell and it forms two sigma bonds with two chlorine atoms. Thus, beryllium has a total of 4 electrons in its outermost shell in BeCl2.\n\nCarbon has 4 electrons in its outermost shell and it forms four sigma bonds with four chlorine atoms. Thus, carbon has a total of 8 electrons in its outermost shell in CCl4.\n\nPhosphorus has 5 electrons in its outermost shell and it forms five sigma bonds with five chlorine atoms. Thus, phosphorus has a total of 10 electrons in its outermost shell in PCl5. \n\nThus, a total of three species NOT have eight electrons around the central atom in its outermost shell.','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','3','2','4','NEW','11-121','11-121',NULL),
(41939,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement. - diagram(41939Q) :','1','Hint: For isoelectronic species as negative charge increases, size also increases.\n\nOn moving from left to right, the atomic radius decreases across the period. This happens due to the increase in the effective nuclear charge. Whereas, on moving down the group, the atomic radius increases because of the increase in the number of shell. Amongst the isoelectronic species, ionic radius increases in with increasing negative charge in anions and decreases with the increasing positive charge in the cations.','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(41940,'chemistry','which of the following is not true about Carbonate ion : - diagram(41940Q)',NULL,'Hint: In \nC\nO\n2\n−\n3\n, all the bond lengths are equal \n\nThe carbonate ion shows resonance. Due to resonance, all the CO bonds get a partial double bond character, and because of it, all CO bonds have equal bond lengths.  \n\nThe \nC\nO\n2\n−\n3\n ion has three sigma bonds and one pie bond. The carbon form three sigma bonds hence, the hybridization of carbon is sp2 .','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-110','11-110','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/41940q.png'),
(41941,'chemistry','H2O is dipolar, BeF2 is not, why ? - diagram(41941Q)','3','HINT: H2O is angular and BeF2 is linear.\n\nExplanation: \n\nH2O is sp3 hybridised with angular shape. Due to this angular shape , bond dipole do not cancel each other .Thus , it is dipolar in nature.\n\nBeF2 is sp hybridised which is linear in shape. F being more electronegative than Be, attracts the electron density towards itself. \n\nSo , bond dipole will cancel each other and it will be non- polar.','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-114','11-114','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/41941q.png'),
(41942,'chemistry','in regular octahedral molecule, MX6, th number of X-M-X bonds at 180 degree are :','3','HINT: Three X−M−X bonds are at 180∘ in octahedral structure.\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn octahedral structure MX6, the six hybrid orbitals (sp3d2) are directed towards the corners of a regular octahedral with an angle of 90∘ .\n\nAccording to following structure of MX6, the number of X−M−X bonds at 180∘ must be three.','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','2','6','4','3','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(41943,'chemistry','identify the wrong pair ;- diagram(41943Q)','a','Hint = PCl5 - Trigonal bipyramidal, sp3d','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','b','c','d','a','NEW','11-114','11-114','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/41943q.png'),
(41944,'chemistry','Match the following: - diagram(41944Q)','2','Hint:  For octahedral, trigonal bipyramidal, and tetrahedral, the coordination number is 6, 5 and 4 respectively\n\nThe hybridization for the tetrahedral molecule is sp3 and the coordination number is 4. \nThe hybridization for the square planar molecule is dsp2 and the coordination number is 4.\nThe hybridization for the trigonal bipyramidal molecule is sp3d and the coordination number is 5.\nThe hybridization for the octahedral molecule is sp3d2 and the coordination number is 6.','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-114','11-114','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/41944q.png'),
(41945,'chemistry','identify the correct order of bond angle in the following compound / species ; - diagram(41945Q)','1','Hint: The bond angle depends on the hybridization of the central atom and the interaction between the bond pair and lone pair.\n\nStep 1: \n\nThe bond angle depends on the hybridization of the central atom. If the hybridization is the same then the bond angle depends on the lone pair and bond pair repulsion, as the number of lone pairs increases bond angle decreases.\n\nThe hybridization of carbon in CO2 is sp and the bond angle is 180o. \n\nThe hybridization of N in NH3, N in NH4+, and O in H2O is sp3. In water, two lone pairs are present hence it has a minimum bond angle. In an NH3 molecule, one lone pair is present hence, its bond angle is more than water but less than NH4+. \n\nStep 2: \n\nCO2 bond angle is 180o\nNH4+ bond angle is approx. 109o\nNH3 bond angle is approx. 107o\nH2O bond angle is approx. 105o\n\nHence, an option first is the correct answer.','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-108','11-108','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/41945q.png'),
(41946,'chemistry','in which of the following species , central atom is sp2 hybridized ? BF3, NH2-, NO2-, H2O','BF3,NO2-','HINT: Hybridisation = number of sigma bonds + no of lone pairs.\n\nExplanation:\n\nCentral atom is sp2 hybridized in BF3 and \nN\nO\n−\n2\n.','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','NO2- , NH2-','NH2-, H2O','NO2-,H2O','BF3,NO2-','NEW','11-120','11-120',NULL),
(41947,'chemistry','correct state of hybridization of C2,C3,C4,and C5 in following hydrocarbon : - diagram(41947Q)  is in the sequence - diagram(41947Q-2)','1','Hint: Hybridisation is the sum of lone pair and sigma bond central atom formed.\n\nTo find out the hybridization, count the number of \nσ\n bonds. Pi bonds (double and triple bonds are not considered to find hybridization). \n\n\nIf the number of \nσ\n bonds = 2; \nsp hybridization \n\n\nIf the number of \nσ\n bonds = 3; \nsp2 hybridization \n\n\nIf the number of \nσ\n bonds = 4; \nsp3 hybridization.','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-120','11-120','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/41947q.png'),
(41948,'chemistry','increasing order of bond length ? - diagram(41948Q)','4','diagram(41948E) As we know that , Bond order  \nα\n1\nB\no\nn\nd\nl\ne\nn\ng\nt\nh\n\n\nOrder of bond length :  NO+< NO < NO- < \nO\n−\n2','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-109','11-109','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/41948q.png'),
(41949,'chemistry','the correct order of dissociation energy of N2 and N2+ is :','N2>N2+','Step 1:\n\nCalculate the bond order of N2 and \nN\n+\n2\n as follows:\n\nThe electronic configuration of N2 is as follows:\n\n(σ1s)2 (σ∗1s)2 (σ2s)2 (σ∗2s)2 (π2px)2 (π2py)2 (σ2pz)2\n\nBond order = ​nb−na2\n​\n\n​\nB\no\nn\nd\n \no\nr\nd\ne\nr\n=\n10\n−\n4\n2\n​\n\n= 3.\n\nThe electronic configuration of \nN\n+\n2\n is as follows:\n\n(σ1s)2 (σ∗1s)2 (σ2s)2 (σ∗2s)2 (π2px)2 (π2py)2 (σ2pz)1\n\nBond order = ​\nn\nb\n−\nn\na\n2\n​\n\n​\nB\no\nn\nd\n \no\nr\nd\ne\nr\n=\n9\n−\n4\n2\n​\n\n= 2.5\n\nStep 2:\n\nDissociation energy is directly proportional to bond order.\n\nN2 has higher bond order than N2+ hence, dissociation energy of N2 is higher than N2+ .\n\ndissociation energy of the nitrogen molecule is 9·756 eV.\ndissociation energy of the nitrogen molecule cation =7.76 eV/molecule','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','N2>N2+','N2=N2+','N2 < N2+','none of these','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(2000031304,'physics','Three charges $q_{1},-q_{1}$ and $q_{2}$ are placed as shown in figure, $S$ is a Gaussian surface. Electric field at any point on $S$ is','D','The electric field at any point on $S$ (Gaussian surface) is due to all the charges but the flux through the $S$ is due to only charges $q_{2}$ and $-q_{1}$ inside the Gaussian surface.','Applications of Gauss\'s Law','Electric charges & Fields','Medium',NULL,'Due to $q_{2}$ only','Uniform at all points','Zero at all the points','Due to all the charges',NULL,NULL,NULL,'physics/12_31304_question_1.png'),
(41950,'chemistry','A species that does not follow paramagnetism :','CO','HINT: Paramagnetic species contains unpaired electrons.\n\nExplanation: \n\nParamagnetic materials are materials that tend to get weakly magnetized in the direction of the magnetizing field when placed in a magnetic field. \n\nParamagnetic materials have a permanent dipole moment or permanent magnetic moment. They contains unpaired electrons.\n\nFrom the MO diagram of CO , we can see that it doesn\'t have any unpaired electron. So, it is diamagentic in nature.','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','N2+','O2-','CO','none of these','NEW','11-129','11-129',NULL),
(41951,'chemistry','four diatomic species are listed below, identify the correct order in which the bond order is increasing in them: - diagram(41951Q)','3','HINT: Use MOT\n\nFor C22−, total electron = 14\nElectronic configuration:\nKKσ2s2σ*2s2π\n2\np\nx\n2\nπ\n2\np\ny\n2\nσ\n2\np\nz\n2\n\nB\no\nn\nd\n \no\nr\nd\ne\nr\n \n=\n \nN\nb\n−\nN\na\n2\n \n=\n \n8\n−\n2\n2\n \n=\n \n3\n\nF\no\nr\n \nN\nO\n,\n \nt\no\nt\na\nl\n \ne\nl\ne\nc\nt\nr\no\nn\n \n=\n \n5\n \n+\n6\n \n=\n \n11\n \nE\nl\ne\nc\nt\nr\no\nn\ni\nc\n \nc\no\nn\nf\ni\ng\nu\nr\na\nt\ni\no\nn\n \n:\n\nK\nK\nσ\n2\ns\n2\nσ\n*\n2\ns\n2\nσ\n2\np\nz\n2\nπ\n2\np\nx\n2\nπ\n2\np\ny\n2\nπ\n2\np\nx\n1\nB\no\nn\nd\n \no\nr\nd\ne\nr\n \n=\n \n(\n8\n−\n3\n)\n2\n \n=\n \n2\n.\n5\nF\no\nr\n \nO\n2\n−\n \n,\n \nt\no\nt\na\nl\n \ne\nl\ne\nc\nt\nr\no\nn\n \n=\n \n17\n\nE\nl\ne\nc\nt\nr\no\nn\ni\nc\n \nc\no\nn\nf\ni\ng\nu\nr\na\nt\ni\no\nn\n \n:\n\nK\nK\nσ\n(\n2\ns\n2\n)\nσ\n*\n(\n2\ns\n2\n)\nσ\n(\n2\np\nz\n)\n2\nπ\n(\n2\np\nx\n)\n2\nπ\n(\n2\np\ny\n)\n2\nπ\n*\n(\n2\np\nx\n)\n1\n \nB\n.\nO\n \n=\n \n8\n−\n5\n2\n \n=\n \n1\n.\n5\n\nF\no\nr\n \nH\ne\n2\n+\n \n,\n \nt\no\nt\na\nl\n \ne\nl\ne\nc\nt\nr\no\nn\n \n=\n \n3\n\nE\nl\ne\nc\nt\nr\no\nn\ni\nc\n \nc\no\nn\nf\ni\ng\nu\nr\na\nt\ni\no\nn\n \n:\n\n \nσ\n1\ns\n2\nσ\n*\n1\ns\n1\n\nB\n.\nO\n.\n=\n \n2\n−\n1\n2\n \n=\n \n0\n.\n5\nSo, the order of Bond order is \nH\ne\n2\n+\n \n<\nO\n2\n−\n \n<\n \nN\nO\n \n<\n  \nC\n2\n2\n−','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-125','11-125','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/41951q.png'),
(41952,'chemistry','which of the following two ions from the list given below has same geometry that is explained by same hybridization of orbitals : NO2-, NO3-,NH2-,NH4-,SCN-.','NO2-,NO3-','NO-2 = 2σ + 1 lp = 3, ie, sp2 hybridization \nNO3- = 3σ, i.e. sp2 hybridization \nNH2- = 2σ + 2lp = 4, ie, sp3 hybridization \nNH+4 = 4σ =4, ie, sp3 hybridization\n\nSCN- : C has 2 sigma bonds and 2 pi bonds and hence has sp hybridisation.\nTherefore, out of the given options, only NO-2 and NO3-have the same hybridization.','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','NH4-,NO3-','SCN-,NH2-','NO2-,NH2-','NO2-,NO3-','NEW','11-120','11-120',NULL),
(41953,'chemistry','which of the following species has linear shape ?','NO2+','HINT: NO2+ is linear in shape.\n\nExplanation:\n\nDue to sp-hybridisation of N in NO2+ ,it has linear shape as shown below: \nO=N+=O','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','NO2-','SO2','NO2+','O3','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(41954,'chemistry','the potential energy (y) curve for H2 formation as a function of internuclear distance(x)  of the H atoms as shown below - diagram(41954Q) . The bond energy of H2 is ; - diagram(41951Q-2)','1','As per the given curve bond energy is the amount of energy is released during the bond formation is\ni.e. = Final - Initial = b - a\n\nPotential energy of two H atoms at infinite distance = a\n\nPotential energy of two H atoms at distance equal to bond length = b\n\nBE = (b – a)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-130','11-130','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/41954q.png'),
(41955,'chemistry','if the main axis of diatomic molecule is z , then molecular orbital Px and Py overlap to form which of the following orbital :','no bond will form','HINT: The three p orbitals are all at right angles to each other.\n\nExplanation: \n\nIf main axis of a molecule is z, then px and py can\'t form any bond as there is no overlap between these orbitals. Both px and py  are perpendicular to each other.\n\npx can form π bond with other px orbital and py can form π bond with other py orbtial.','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','pie molecular orbital','sigma molecular orbital','delta molecular orbital','no bond will form','NEW','11-119','11-119',NULL),
(41956,'chemistry','among the CaH2,BeH2, BaH2 the order of ionic character is : - diagram(41956Q)','1','Ionic character ∝ size of cation \nDown the group, size of the cation increases due to which ionic character of its hydride decreases. Since the order of ionic size of Be, Ca and Ba is Be<Ca<Ba, so the ionic character of the hydrides will be BeH2 <CaH2 <BaH2','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-112','11-112','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/41956q.png'),
(41957,'chemistry','BF3 is a planar and an electron deficient compound. Hybridization and number of electrons around the central atom, respectively are:','sp2 and 6','1 bond pair means two electrons.\n3σ bond= sp2\nThe number of bond pairs is three. One bond pair needs two electrons. So, the number of electrons for the three bond pairs is 6.','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','sp2 and 6','sp2 and 8','sp3 and 6','sp3 and 8','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(41958,'chemistry','The bond angle is maximum in','NH ₄⁺','lone pair-lone pair or lone pair-bond pair repulsion decreases the bond angle. Refer diagram (41958E)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','NH₃','NH ₄⁺','PCl₃','SCl₂','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(41959,'chemistry','Match the following. Refer diagram (41959Q)','2','1. The hybridization of nitrogen in NH3 is sp3 . The molecular geometry is tetrahedral and the shape of NH3 is trigonal bipyramidal because nitrogen contains a lone pair. \n2. The hybridization of chlorine in ClF3 is sp3d. The molecular geometry is trigonal bipyramidal and the shape of ClF3 is T-shape.\n3. The hybridization of P in PCl5 is sp3d. The molecular geometry and shape both is trigonal bipyramidal. \n4. The hybridization of Br in BrF5 is sp3d2. The molecular geometry is octahedral and shape is square pyramidal because Br contains one lone pair. \nHence, option second is the correct answer.','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-120','11-120','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/41959q.png'),
(41960,'chemistry','The incorrect statements among the following for sigma and pi- bonds formed between two carbon atoms is: Refer diagram (41960Q)','2','. A sigma bond is stronger than a pi-bond. This is because the sigma bond is formed by axial overlap and the pi bond is formed by lateral overlap. Thus the extent of overlap is greater in sigma bond formation than in pi bond formation.\n\n2. Bond energy of the sigma bond is higher than that of the pi bond as a sigma bond is stronger than a pi bond. Hence, option second is the wrong statement.\n\n3. Free rotation of atoms about a sigma bond is allowed but not in the case of a pi-bond. When the carbon atoms of the sigma bond are rotated, the axial overlap is not affected. but in the case of pi bonds, the lateral overlap will be affected.\n\n4. A sigma bond determines the direction between carbon atoms, but a pi-bond has no primary effect in this regard. A pi bond is present only when a sigma bond is already present.','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-120','11-120','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/41960q.png'),
(41961,'chemistry','In which of the following molecules, all atoms are coplanar. Refer diagram (41961Q)','1','Here, all carbons are sp2-hybridised in this molecule so all atoms are coplanar .\n\nBut in other molecules, sp3 hybridised carbon atoms are also present . In those molecules , all atoms are not coplanar.','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-120','11-120','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/41961q.png'),
(41962,'chemistry','What is the total number of sigma and pi bonds in pent-2-en-4yne is:','10 sigma and 3 pi-bonds','There are 10 sigma bonds and 3 pi bonds in pent-2-en-4-yne. (NEET-2019)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','13 sigma and no pi-bonds','10 sigma and 3 pi-bonds','8 sigma and 5 pi-bonds','11 sigma and 2 pi-bonds','NEW','11-120','11-120',NULL),
(41963,'chemistry','Which among the following is an incorrect statement. Refer diagram (41963Q)','1','Refer diagram (41963E)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/41963q.png'),
(42001,'chemistry','Isostructural species are those which have the same shape and hybridisation. Among the given species identify the isostructural pairs.','[BF₄⁻ and NH ₄⁺ ]','(a) NF₃, is pyramidal vhereas BF₃ is planar triangular.\n(b) BF₄⁻  and NH₄⁺ ion both are tetrahedral and sp³  hybridisation.\n(c) BCI ₃ is triangular planar whereas BrCl₃   is T shaped\n( d) NH₃  is pyramidal whereas NO₃⁻   is triangular planar. (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 108 table 4.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','[NF₃ and BF₃ ]','[BF₄⁻ and NH ₄⁺ ]','[BCl₃ and BrCl₃ ]','[NH₃ and NO₃⁻ ]','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(42002,'chemistry','Polarity in a molecule and hence the dipole moment depends primarily on electronegativity of the constituent atoms and shape of a molecule.\nWhich of the following has the highest dipole moment?','H₂O','CO, being symmetrical has zero dipole moment\nAmong HI, SO₂ and H₂O dipole moment is highest for H₂O as in it the central atom contains 2 lone pairs (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 108 Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CO₂','HI','H₂O','SO₂','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(42003,'chemistry','The types of hybrid orbitals of nitrogen in NO₂⁺ , NO₃⁻ and NH₄⁺ respectively are expected to be','sp ,sp² and sp³','The type of hybrid orbital of nitrogen can be decided by using VSEPR theory counting bond pairs and lone pairs.                                                   NO₂⁺ = 2bp + 0 lp =>liner =sp hybridised.                                             NO₃⁻ = 3bp + 0 lp => sp² hybridised.          NH₄⁺  =  4bp + 0 lp => sp³ hybridised (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 117 Paragraph 1 in topic 4.6.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','sp , sp³ and sp²','sp ,sp² and sp³','sp², sp and sp³','sp², sp³ and sp','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(42004,'chemistry','Hydrogen bonds are formed in many compounds e.g.,  The boiling point of such compounds depends to a large extent on the strength of hydrogen bond and the number of hydrogen bonds. The correct decreasing order of the boiling points of above compounds is :','H₂O>HF>NH₃','Explanation: The factors that affect the strength of hydrogen bond are electronegativity, size of atom and number of hydrogen bonds per mol. Usually, F is the most electronegative element, but, in H₂O, there are more H-bonds, and their size is smaller than N. (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 128 Paragraph 1 in topic 4.9.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','HF>H₂O>NH₃','H₂O>HF>NH₃','NH₃ > HF >H₂O','NH₃ >H₂O>HF','NEW','11-128','11-128',NULL),
(42005,'chemistry','In PO₄³⁻ ion the formal charge on the oxygen atom of P-O bond is','-0.75','In PO₄³⁻,formal charge on O atom of P-O bond =total charge /number of O atoms =-3/4 = -0.75 (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 100 Paragraph 1 in topic 4.1.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','-1','-0.75','0.75','NEW','11-100','11-100',NULL),
(42006,'chemistry','In NO₃⁻  ion, the number of bond pairs and lone pairs of electrons on\nnitrogen atom are','4, 0','To solve this question, we must know  the structure of NO₃⁻ ion .\nThen, count the bond pairs and lone pair electron  on nitrogen\nAns. (d) In N atom, number  of valence electrons = 5\nDue to the presence of one negative charge, number of valence electrons = 5 +1 = 6\none O atom forms two  bond and two O atom shared wth two electrons of N atom .Thus 3 O atoms shared with 8e⁻ of N atom .Therefore number of bond pair is 4 and number of lone pair is zero (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 117 Paragraph 1 in topic 4.6.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2,2','3,1','1,3','4, 0','NEW','11-117','11-117',NULL),
(42007,'chemistry','Which of the following species has tetrahedral geometry?','BH₄⁻','BH₄⁻ => four bond pairs + 0 lone pairs  =>sp³ =>tetrahedral geometry           NH₂⁻ =>V - shape                                      CO₃²⁻=>triangular planar.                           H₃O⁺ =>pyramidal (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 110 table 4.6,4.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','BH₄⁻','NH₂⁻','CO₃²⁻','H₃O⁺','NEW','11-110','11-110',NULL),
(42008,'chemistry','Which of the molecule /ion out of the following does not contain unpaired electrons','O₂²⁻','The electronic configuration of O₂ is           Y O₂ = (σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pᵧ²=𝛑2pₓ² (𝛑*2pᵧ¹=𝛑*2pₓ¹). So after addition of electrons there will be no unpaired electrons (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 125 Paragraph 1 in topic 4.7.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','N₂⁺','O₂','O₂²⁻','B₂','NEW','11-125','11-125',NULL),
(42009,'chemistry','Which of the molecule /ion out of the following all bonds are not equal','C₂H₄','XeF₄ => square planar (all bonds are equal).                                                  BF₄⁻ => tetrahedral (all bonds are equal).                                                   C₂ H₂ => C DOUBLE BOND C is not equal to C-H  thus in this molecule all bonds are not equal (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 110 table 4.6,4.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','XeF₄','BF₄⁻','C₂H₄','SiF₄','NEW','11-110','11-110',NULL),
(42010,'chemistry','In which of the following substance will hydrogen bond be strongest','H₂O','HCl ,HI,H₂S does not form hydrogen bond . Only H₂O molecules forms 4 hydrogen bonding (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 128 Paragraph 1 in topic 4.9.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','HCl','H₂O','HI','H₂S','NEW','11-128','11-128',NULL),
(42011,'chemistry','If the electronic configuration of an element is 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d² 4s², the four electron involved in chemical bond formation will be','3d²,4s²','The given electronic configuration shows that an element is vanadium (Z- 22). It belongs to d-block of the periodic table. in transition elements i.e. -block elements electrons of ns and (n - 1)d subshell take part in bond formation.(Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 125 Paragraph 1 in topic 4.7.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','3p⁶','3p⁶,4s²','3p⁶ , 3d²','3d²,4s²','NEW','11-125','11-125',NULL),
(42012,'chemistry','Which of the following angle corresponds to sp² hybridisation?','120','For sp² hybridisation the geometry is generally triangular planar ,Thus bond angle is 120 degree Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 117 Paragraph 2 in topic 4.6.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','90','120','180','109','NEW','11-117','11-117',NULL),
(42013,'chemistry','The electronic configurations of A ,B,C are given below.             \n\nA: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶                            \n\nB: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p³                \n\nC: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁵ \n\nStable form of A may be represented by','A','The given  electronic configuration shows that A represents noble gas  because the octet is complete.It is neon which has 10 atomic number (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 125 Paragraph 1 in topic 4.7.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','A','A₂','A₃','A₄','NEW','11--125','11--125',NULL),
(42014,'chemistry','The electronic configurations of A ,B,C are given below.             \n\nA: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶                            \n\nB: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p³                \n\nC: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁵  \n\nStable form of C may be represented by','C₂','The electronic configuration of C represent chiorine. Its stable form is dichlorine (Cl₂ )(Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 125 Paragraph 1 in topic 4.7.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','C','C₂','C₃','C₄','NEW','11--125','11--125',NULL),
(42015,'chemistry','The electronic configurations of A ,B,C are given below.             \n\nA: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶                            \n\nB: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p³                \n\nC: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁵ \n\nThe molecular formula for the compound formed by B and C will be','BC₃','The bond between B and C will be covalent . Both B and C are non metal atoms.B represents Phosphorus and C represents Chlorine . Stable compound formed is PCl₃ (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 125 Paragraph 1 in topic 4.7.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','BC','B₂C','BC₂','BC₃','NEW','11--125','11--125',NULL),
(42016,'chemistry','The electronic configurations of A ,B,C are given below.             \n\nA: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶                            \n\nB: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p³                \n\nC: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁵  \n\nThe bond between B and C will be','Covalent','The bond between B and C will be covalent . Both B and C are non metal atoms.B represents Phosphorus and C represents Chlorine .(Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 125 Paragraph 1 in topic 4.7.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ionic','Covalent','Hydrogen','Co-ordinate','NEW','11--125','11--125',NULL),
(42017,'chemistry','Which of the following options represents the correct bond order?','O₂⁻<O₂<O₂⁺','The electronic configuration of O₂ is            O₂ = (σ1s)²(σ*1s)²(σ2s)²(σ*2s)²(σ2p𝓏)²(𝛑2pᵧ²=𝛑2pₓ² (𝛑*2pᵧ¹=𝛑*2pₓ¹). So after addition of electrons there will be no unpaired electrons bond order = 1/2(Nb - Nl )= 1/2(10-6) =2(Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 105 Paragraph 1 in topic 4.3.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','O₂⁻<O₂<O₂⁺','O₂⁻>O₂>O₂⁺','O₂⁻ > O₂< O₂⁺','O₂⁻ < O₂> O₂⁺','NEW','11-105','11-105',NULL),
(42018,'chemistry','Electronic configuration of the outer most shell of the most electronegative element is','2s² 2p⁵','2s² 2p⁵-fluorine                                       3s² 3p⁵-chlorine                                       4s² 4p⁵ -bromine                                    5s² 5p⁵-iodine(Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 125 Paragraph 1 in topic 4.7.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','2s² 2p⁵','3s² 3p⁵','4s² 4p⁵','5s² 5p⁵','NEW','11-125','11-125',NULL),
(42019,'chemistry','Among the following electronic configuration, the one having the highest ionisation enthalpy  is ?','[Ne]3s² 3p³','Electronic  configuration with the highest ionisation enthalpy is  [Ne]3s² 3p³ on moving down the group the ionisation enthalpy decreases . In a period on moving from left to right ionisation enthalpy increases.','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','[Ne]3s²','[Ne]3s² 3p³','[Ne]3s² 3p²','[Ar]3d¹⁰ 4s² 4p³','NEW','11-106','11-106',NULL),
(42020,'chemistry','Which of the following have identical bond order?          \n\na)CN⁻.                                                                                    \nB)NO⁺                                                                             \nC)O₂⁻.                                                                               \nD)O₂²⁻','a,b','CN⁻ and NO⁺ because of same number of electrons have same bond order.','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,c','a,b','a,d','b,c','NEW','11-109','11-109',NULL),
(42021,'chemistry','Which of the following Attain the linear structure?          \na.BeCl₂         \nb.NCO⁺         \nc.NO₂        \nd.CS₂','a,d','BeCl₂ and CS₂ both are linear .however NO₂ is angular with bond angle 132 ° and bond length 1.20 A °','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,d','a,c','a,b','b,c','NEW','11-107','11-107',NULL),
(42022,'chemistry','CO is isoelectronic with','NO⁺','Isoelectronic species are those which have same number of electrons but different nuclear charge','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','NO⁺','N₃','SnCl₂','NO₂⁻','NEW','11-109','11-109',NULL);
