(42257,'chemistry','What is the order of polarity of the following bond? \n\n(i) C-H \n(ii) F-H \n(iii) Br-H\n(iv) Na-I \n(v) K-F \n(vi) Li-Cl','K-F > Li-Cl > F-H > Na-I > Br-H','Higher the value of electronegativity difference. Higher will be the Polar nature of the compound (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 107 Paragraph 4.3.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Li-Cl > K-F > F-H > Br-H > C-H >Na-I','K-F > Li-Cl > F-H > Br-H > Na-I >C-H','K-F > F-H > Li-C1 > Br-H > Na-I >C-H','K-F > Li-Cl > F-H > Na-I > Br-H','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(42258,'chemistry','Which of the following structures of hydrazoic acid molecule is not expected to be canonical form?        \n1. HN=N⁺ =N.    \n2.HN-N⁺ ≡ N⁻     \n3.H—N⁺ ≡ N⁺ —N²⁻','III only','Like charges cannot decide on adjacent atom. Hence option three cannot be expected as a canonical form for hydrazoic acid (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 105 Paragraph 1 of 4.3.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','I only','II only','Both I and II','III only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(42259,'chemistry','The charge/size ratio of a cation determinesitspolarizing power. Which one of the following sequences represents the increasing order of the polarizing power of the cationic species, K⁺,Ca²⁺,Mg²⁺,Be²⁺','K⁺ < Ca²⁺ < Mg²⁺ < Be²⁺','Higher charge and smaller size of cations increases polarisation, As a result  size of the given cation decreases as K⁺ > Ca²⁺ > Mg²⁺ > Be²⁺.hence polarising power increases as K⁺ < Ca²⁺ < Mg²⁺ < Be²⁺ (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 108 Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ca²⁺ < Mg²⁺ < Be²⁺ < K⁺','Mg²⁺ < Be²⁺ < K⁺ < Ca²⁺','Be²⁺ < K⁺ < Ca²⁺< Mg²⁺','K⁺ < Ca²⁺ < Mg²⁺ < Be²⁺','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(42260,'chemistry','Arrange the following compounds in order of increasing dipole moment : \n\n(I) Toluene \n(II) m-Dichlorobenzene\n(III) o-Dichlorobenzene \n(IV) p-Dichlorobenzene','III>II>I>IV','o,m, P derivatives have α = 60° ,120 ° ,180° respectively, p - derivative has zero dipole moment .Dipole moment of m-dichlorobenzene is more than toluene.  (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 108 Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','III>II>IV>I','III>II>I>IV','II>III>I>IV','III>I>II>IV','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(42261,'chemistry','If the resultant dipole moment of water is 1.85 D, then what is its value in terms of C m?','6.16 x 10-3? Cm','I D = 3.33 x 10⁻³⁰ C m.                      1.85 D = 3.33 x 10⁻³⁰ C m  x 1.85               = 6.16 x 10⁻³⁰ (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 107 Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','6.07 x 10-3? C m','6.16 x 10-3? Cm','6.08 x 10-29 C m','6.16 x 10-28 Cm','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(42262,'chemistry','Which of the following molecules has highest dipole moment?','NH ₃','BF₃ is triangular play, Naan and B₂H₆ is a dimer of triangular plane of molecule BH ₃ that for both of these have zero dipole moment, NH ₃ and NF₃, on the other hand, have a structure and does have the dipole moment (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 108 Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','BF₃','NH ₃','NF ₃','B₂H₆','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(42263,'chemistry','According to Fajans\' rules, covalent bonding is favoured by','small cation and large anion','Covalent bonding is favoured by small cation and Large anion (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 108 Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','small cation and large anion','small cation and small anion','large cation and large anion','large cation and small anion.','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(42264,'chemistry','The correct order of decreasing polarity is','HF > H₂O > SO₂ > NH₃','Electronegativity difference in              HF = 4-2.1 = 1.9                               SO₂=3.5-2.5 = 1                                 H₂O = 3.5-2.1=1.4                             ammonia= 0-2.1= .9 electronegativity difference decreases the percentage ionic character decreases accordingly thus the correct order is HF > H₂O > SO₂ > NH₃ (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 107 Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','HF > SO₂ > H₂O > NH₃','HF > H₂O > SO₂ > NH₃','HF > NH₃ < SO₂ > H₂O','H₂O < NH₃ > SO₂ > HF','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(42265,'chemistry','Which bond angle  would result in the maximum dipole moment for the triatomic molecule XY₂ as shown below?','90','The dipole moment of to dipole is inclined at angle theta is given by the equation, μ = √ X² +Y² +2XYcosθ as the angle increases from 90° to 180°. The value of cos θ becomes more and more negative. Hence, resultant decreases thus dipole moment is maximum when θ is equal to 90°.(Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 109 Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','90','120','150','180','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(42266,'chemistry','The interatomic distances in H ₂ and Cl ₂ molecules are 74 and 198 pm respectively. The bond length of HCl is','136 pm','Interatomic distance in H₂ molecule= 74 pm and interatomic distance in Cl₂ = 198 pm.                                      Atoms linked by σ-bond covalent     radius (r)= Interatomic distance/2.Therefore covalent radius of hydrogen =I/2 =198/2 =99 pm.  Therefore bond length is 99+ 37 = 136 pm (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 103 Paragraph 4.3.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','124 pm','248 pm','272 pm','136 pm','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(42267,'chemistry','Which of the following molecules has highest bond energy ?','C-C','The bond energies of F -F, C-C,N-N,O-O bonds  are 33, 80, 39,34.2 kJ per mole respectively. Therefore molecule of C-C has the highest bond energy. (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 104 Paragraph 4.3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','F-F','N-N','C-C','O—O','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(42268,'chemistry','In PO ion, the formal charge on each oxygen atom and P-O bond order respectively are','- 0.75, + 1.25','Here, three units negative charge is shared for four atoms. Therefore, formal charge =-3/4 =0.75. Bond order of P—O of bond = total number of bond between two atoms in all structure /total number of resonating structures                                              =2+1+1+1/4 =5/4 =1.25 (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 100 .Paragraph 4.1.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','-1.0, + 0.75','- 0.75, + 1.25','- 0.75, +1.0','- 0.75, + 0.75','NEW','11-104','11-104',NULL),
(42269,'chemistry','Carbon tetrachloride has no dipole moment, because','it is symmetrical tetrahedral structure','Due to symmetrical tetrahedral structure, the dipole moment of 1 C – Cl born is CCL₄ is cancelled by opposite C – Cl bond thus a molecule has a polar bond but zero dipole moment.((Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 107 Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','similar sizes of carbon and chlorine atoms','it is symmetrical tetrahedral structure','it is planar structure','none of the above.','NEW','11-111','11-111',NULL),
(42270,'chemistry','The bond length between hybridised carbon atom and carbon atom is minimum in','Propyne','Small of the atom stronger is the born and greater the bond association energy. There for the bond C – D has the greatest energy or smallest atoms. (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 104 Paragraph 4.3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','propene','Propyne','propane','Butane','NEW','11-108','11-108',NULL),
(42271,'chemistry','The boiling  point  of heavy water is?','99 degree Celsius','Heavy water is D₂O .It does not have hydrogen bonding ,therefore its boiling point is slightly less than that of water(Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 127. Paragraph 4.9.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','108 degree Celsius','101.4 degree Celsius','99 degree Celsius','110 degree Celsius','NEW','11-131','11-131',NULL),
(42272,'chemistry','Methanol and ethanol are miscible in water due to','hydrogen bonding character','Methane and ethanol form hydrogen bond with water and thus miscible in water (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 127. Paragraph 4.9.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','covalent character','hydrogen bonding character','oxygen bonding character','none of the above.','NEW','11-131','11-131',NULL),
(42273,'chemistry','Water (H₂O) is liquid while hydrogen sulphide (H₂S) is a gas because','water molecules associate through hydrogen bonding.','Water molecules are associated through intermolecular hydrogen bonding and thus exist in liquid form whereas in H₂S  no hydrogen bond exist hence it is a gas  (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 127. Paragraph 4.9.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','water has higher molecular weight','hydrogen sulphide is a weak acid','sulphur has high electronegativity than oxygen','water molecules associate through hydrogen bonding.','NEW','11-131','11-131',NULL),
(42274,'chemistry','The pair likely to form the strongest hydrogen bonding is','HCOOH and CH₃ COOH','Dimerisation occurs only in carboxylic acid which indicate strong hydrogen bonding (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 127. Paragraph 4.9.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','H₂O₂ and H₂O','HCOOH and CH₃ COOH','CH₃ COOH and CH₃COO CH₃','SiH₄ and SiCl₄','NEW','11-132','11-132',NULL),
(42275,'chemistry','The boiling point is not affected due to hydrogen bonding in','hydrogen chloride.','HCl does not undergo hydrogen bonding, and its boiling point is not affected by hydrogen bond (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 127. Paragraph 4.9.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','water','ammonia','methyl alcohol','hydrogen chloride.','NEW','11-132','11-132',NULL),
(42276,'chemistry','The crystal lattice of ice is mostly formed by','covalent as well as H-bonds.','Crystal lattice of ice is mostly found by covalent as well as hydrogen bond (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 127. Paragraph 4.9.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','ionic forces','covalent bond','intramolecular H-bonds','covalent as well as H-bonds.','NEW','11-131','11-131',NULL),
(42277,'chemistry','What is the dominant intermolecular force or bond that must be overcome in converting liquid CH3OH to a gas?','Hydrogen bonding','Methanol can undergo intermolecular association through hydrogen bonding as the -OH  is highly polarised. As a result in order to convert liquid CH₃OH  to gaseous  state, the strong hydrogen bonds must be broken. (Reference : NCERT Class 11 part 1 page 127. Paragraph 4.9.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Dipole-dipole interaction','Covalent bonds','London dispersion force','Hydrogen bonding','NEW','11-131','11-131',NULL),
(140519,'chemistry','Which of the following is not the reason of  "Taj Mahal deformity and marble discoloration" ?','Low levels of sulphur and nitrogen oxides in the air.','The air around the city of Agra, where the Taj Mahal is located, contains fairly high levels of sulphur and nitrogen oxides. It is mainly due to a large number of industries and power plants around the area. Use of poor quality of coal, kerosene and firewood as fuel for domestic purposes add up to this problem. The resulting acid rain reacts with marble, CaCO₃ of Taj Mahal causing damage to this wonderful monument that has attracted people from around the world. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 410; Taj Mahal and Acid Rain)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Acid Rain',NULL,'1','mcq','Use of poor quality of coal and kerosene as fuel in around the area.','Low levels of sulphur and nitrogen oxides in the air.','Large number of industries and power plants around the area.','Acid rain formation.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140520,'chemistry','What city does not belong to the Taj Trapezium?','Kanpur','The ‘Taj Trapezium’ is an area that includes the towns of Agra, Firozabad, Mathura and Bharatpur. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 410; Taj Mahal and Acid Rain)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Acid Rain',NULL,'1','mcq','Firozabad','Mathura','Kanpur','Bharatpur','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140521,'chemistry','Which of the following fuel will be used to prevent the historical monument from disfiguring under the action plan?','Natural gas','The Government of India announced an action plan in early 1995 to prevent the disfiguring of this historical monument. Under this plan more than 2000 polluting industries lying inside the trapezium would switch over to the use of natural gas or liquefied petroleum gas instead of coal or oil. A new natural gas pipeline would bring more than half a million cubic metres of natural gas a day to this area.  People living in the city will also be encouraged to use liquefied petroleum gas in place of coal, kerosene or firewood. Vehicles plying on highways in the vicinity of Taj would be encouraged to use low sulphur content diesel.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 410; Taj Mahal and Acid Rain)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Acid Rain',NULL,'1','mcq','Coal','Firewood','Kerosene','Natural gas','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140807,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: Dynamic equilibrium exists between the production and decomposition of ozone molecules.\n\nReason: Ozone is thermodynamically stable and decomposes to molecular oxygen.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Ozone is thermodynamically unstable and decomposes to molecular oxygen. Thus, a dynamic equilibrium exists between the production and decomposition of ozone molecules.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Stratospheric Pollution',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140808,'chemistry','The main reason behind ozone layer depletion is due to release of:','CFCs','The main reason of ozone layer depletion is believed to be the release of chlorofluorocarbon compounds (CFCs), also known as freons.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Stratospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','CFCs','CO₂','O₂','HFCs','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140809,'chemistry','Freons is another name for:','CFCs','The main reason of ozone layer depletion is believed to be the release of chlorofluorocarbon compounds (CFCs), also known as freons.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Stratospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','CFCs','CO₂','NO','HFCs','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140810,'chemistry','Which among the following are the properties of freons?\nA. Highly reactive\nB. Non-flammable\nC. Non toxic \nD. Inorganic molecules','Both B and C','Chlorofluorocarbon compounds (CFCs), also known as freons, are nonreactive, non flammable, non toxic organic molecules.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Stratospheric Pollution',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and B','Both B and C','Both C and D','Both A and D','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140811,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: CFCs are used in refrigerators, air conditioners, in the production of plastic foam and by the electronic industry for cleaning computer parts.\n\nReason: CFCs are non-reactive, non-flammable, non toxic organic molecules.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Chlorofluorocarbons are nonreactive, non flammable, non toxic organic molecules, therefore used in refrigerators, air conditioners, in the production of plastic foam and by the electronic industry for cleaning computer parts etc. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Stratospheric Pollution',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140812,'chemistry','Molecule CF₂Cl₂ when broken down by strong UV radiation will give which type of radical?','Cl•','Once CFCs are released in the atmosphere, they mix with the normal atmospheric gases and eventually reach the stratosphere. In stratosphere, they get broken down by powerful UV radiations, releasing chlorine free radical.\nCF₂Cl₂ (g)  →   Cl• (g)  + C•F₂Cl (g) \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Stratospheric Pollution',NULL,'2','mcq','Cl•','F•','Br•','I•','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140813,'chemistry','Consider the following reactions.\nCl• + O₃ → A + B \nA + O → Cl• + B,\nA is a free radical, and B is a paramagnetic gas; in this case, A and B are:','ClO• and O₂','Once CFCs are released in the atmosphere, they mix with the normal atmospheric gases and eventually reach the stratosphere. In stratosphere, they get broken down by powerful UV radiations, releasing chlorine free  radical.\nCF₂Cl₂ (g)  →   Cl• (g)  + C•F₂Cl (g) \nThe chlorine radical then react with stratospheric ozone to form chlorine monoxide radicals and molecular oxygen.Reaction of chlorine monoxide radical with atomic oxygen produces more chlorine radicals.\nCl• (g) + O₃ (g) → ClO• (g) + O₂(g)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Stratospheric Pollution',NULL,'2','mcq','Cl• and O₂','ClO• and O₂','ClO₂• and Cl•','O and ClO₂•','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140814,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: CFCs are transporting agents for continuously generating chlorine radicals into the stratosphere and damaging the ozone layer.\n\nReason: The chlorine radicals are continuously regenerated and cause the breakdown of ozone.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Once CFCs are released in the atmosphere, they mix with the normal atmospheric gases and eventually reach the stratosphere. In stratosphere, they get broken down by powerful UV radiations, releasing chlorine free  radical.\nCF₂Cl₂ (g)  →   Cl• (g)  + C•F₂Cl (g) \nThe chlorine radical then react with stratospheric ozone to form chlorine monoxide radicals and molecular oxygen.Reaction of chlorine monoxide radical with atomic oxygen produces more chlorine radicals.\nCl• (g) + O₃ (g) → ClO• (g) + O₂(g)\nReaction of chlorine monoxide radical with atomic oxygen produces more chlorine radicals. \nClO• (g) + O (g) → Cl•(g) + O₂(g)\nThe chlorine radicals are continuously regenerated and cause the breakdown of ozone. Thus, CFCs are transporting agents for continuously generating chlorine radicals into the stratosphere and damaging the ozone layer.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Stratospheric Pollution',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140910,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: UV radiations also cause damage to fish production.\n\nReason: UV radiation cause killing of many phytoplanktons which affect aquatic food chain.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','UV radiations lead to ageing of skin, cataract, sunburn, skin cancer, killing of many phytoplanktons, damage to fish productivity etc.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 414; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> The Ozone Hole',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140911,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statement with respect to effect of UV radiations?','UV restores paints and fibres, causing them to fade slower.','It has also been reported that plant proteins get easily affected by UV radiations which leads to the harmful mutation of cells. It also increases evaporation of surface water through the stomata of the leaves and decreases the moisture content of the soil. Increase in UV radiations damage paints and fibres, causing them to fade faster.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 414; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> The Ozone Hole',NULL,'2','mcq','Plant proteins get easily affected by UV radiations.','It decreases moisture content of soil by increasing the transpiration.','UV restores paints and fibres, causing them to fade slower.','Causes harmful cell mutations.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141006,'chemistry','In cold water, dissolved oxygen (DO) can reach a concentration up to _____  ppm.','10','In cold water, dissolved oxygen (DO) can reach a concentration up to 10 ppm.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 414; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Water pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','10','100','200,000','10000','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141007,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: When organic matter decomposes in water, even a small amount can reduce the water\'s dissolved oxygen.\n\nReason: The large population of bacteria consumes oxygen dissolved in water.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The large population of bacteria decomposes organic matter present in water. They consume oxygen dissolved in water. The amount of oxygen that water can hold in the solution is limited. In cold water, dissolved oxygen (DO) can reach a concentration up to 10 ppm (parts per million), whereas oxygen in air is about 200,000 ppm. That is why even a moderate amount of organic matter when decomposes in water can deplete the water of its dissolved oxygen.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 414; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Water pollution',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/141007q.png'),
(141008,'chemistry','Choose the correct statements from the following.\nA. Water pollution originates from human activities.\nB. Excessive phytoplankton growth within water is also a cause of water pollution.\nC. The amount of oxygen that water can hold in the solution is extremely high.','Both A and B','Excessive phytoplankton growth within water is also a cause of water pollution. These wastes are biodegradable. The large population of bacteria decomposes organic matter present in water. They consume oxygen dissolved in water. The amount of oxygen that water can hold in the solution is limited.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 414; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Water pollution',NULL,'2','mcq','Only A','Both A and B','Both B and C','A, B , and C','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141009,'chemistry','If the concentration of dissolved oxygen of water is below _____ ppm, the growth of fish gets inhibited.','6 ppm','If the concentration of dissolved oxygen of water is below 6 ppm, the growth of fish gets inhibited.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 415; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Water pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','6 ppm','10 ppm','250 ppm','100 ppm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141010,'chemistry','Which of the following options allows oxygen to enter water?','Through atmosphere.','Oxygen reaches water either through atmosphere or from the process of photosynthesis carried out by many aquatic green plants during daylight.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 415; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Water pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Oxidation of organic matter by microbes.','Respiration by plants.','Predation by zooplanktons.','Through atmosphere.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141011,'chemistry','The amount of oxygen required by bacteria to break down the organic matter present in a certain volume of a sample of water, is called BOD. BOD is an abbreviation for:','Biological Oxygen Demand','The amount of oxygen required by bacteria to break down the organic matter present in a certain volume of a sample of water, is called Biochemical Oxygen Demand (BOD).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 415; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Water pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Biological Oxygen Demand','Biological Oxygen Disturbance','Bacterial Oxygen Demand','Biological Oxygen Distress','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141012,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is true if a lake near the city has a reported BOD value of 4 ppm?','Water in lake in clean and can be used for domestic purposes.','Clean water would have BOD value of less than 5 ppm whereas highly polluted water could have a BOD value of 17 ppm or more.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 415; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Water pollution',NULL,'2','mcq','Water in lake is highly polluted and cannot be used for domestic purposes.','Water in lake in clean and can be used for domestic purposes.','Water in lake is moderately polluted, but can be used for washing purposes.','Water in lake is highly toxic and is a biohazard.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141013,'chemistry','If too much of organic matter is added to water, then which of the following is expected to happen?','Oxygen-dependent aquatic life will die.','If too much of organic matter is added to water, all the available oxygen is used up. This causes oxygen dependent aquatic life to die. Thus, anaerobic bacteria (which do not require oxygen) begin to break down the organic waste and produce chemicals that have a foul smell and are harmful to human health. Aerobic (oxygen requiring) bacteria degrade these organic wastes and keep the water depleted in dissolved oxygen.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 415; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Water pollution',NULL,'2','mcq','All the organisms would die.','Oxygen-dependent aquatic life will die.','Anaerobic bacteria will die soon.','Aerobic bacteria will be able to break down the organic waste.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141014,'chemistry','Which of the following heavy metals is not a chemical pollutant?','Iron','Water soluble inorganic chemicals that include heavy metals such as cadmium, mercury, nickel etc constitute an important class of pollutants. Although an excess of iron is undesirable, it is not a contaminant. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 415; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Water pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Nickel','Mercury','Cadmium','Iron','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141015,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: Heavy metals such as cadmium, mercury, nickel are dangerous to humans.\n\nReason: Human body cannot excrete heavy metals and they lead to damage to kidneys, liver etc.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Heavy metals such as cadmium, mercury, nickel are dangerous to humans because our body cannot excrete them. Over the time, it crosses the tolerance limit. These metals then can damage kidneys, central nervous system, liver etc.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 415; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Water pollution',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141016,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a chemical source of water pollution?','Fruits and vegetable wastes dumped in water.','Acids (like sulphuric acid) from mine drainage and salts from many different sources including raw salt used to melt snow and ice in the colder climates (sodium and calcium chloride) are water soluble chemical pollutants.The organic chemicals are another group of substances that are found in polluted water. Petroleum products pollute many sources of water e.g., major oil spills in oceans. Other organic substances with serious impacts are the pesticides that drift down from sprays or runoff from lands.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 415; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Water pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Acids from mine drainage and salts from a variety of sources, including raw salt, which is used to melt snow and ice in colder climates.','Fruits and vegetable wastes dumped in water.','Oil spills in ocean.','Pesticides that drift down from sprays or runoff from lands.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141017,'chemistry','PCBs (polychlorinated biphenyls) are not employed in:','Purifier for drinking water.','Various industrial chemicals like polychlorinated biphenyls, (PCBs)  which are used as cleansing solvent, detergents and fertilizers add to the list of water pollutants. PCBs are suspected to be carcinogenic. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 415; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Water pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Cleansing solvent','Detergents','Purifier for drinking water.','Fertilizers','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141018,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: The use of biodegradable detergents creates problem aquatic life forms.\n\nReason: The bacteria responsible for degrading biodegradable detergent feed on it and grow rapidly. While growing, they may use up all the oxygen dissolved in water.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Nowadays most of the detergents available are biodegradable. However, their use can create other problems. The bacteria responsible for degrading biodegradable detergent feed on it and grow rapidly. While growing, they may use up all the oxygen dissolved in water. The lack of oxygen kills all other forms of aquatic life such as fish and plants.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 415; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Water pollution',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141019,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: Bloom-infested water inhibits the growth of other living organisms in the water body.\n\nReason: The addition of phosphates in water enhances algae growth and algae covers the water surface reducing oxygen concentration.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The addition of phosphates in water enhances algae growth. Such profuse growth of algae, covers the water surface and reduces the oxygen concentration in water. This leads to anaerobic conditions, commonly with accumulation of obnoxious decay and animal death.  Thus, bloom-infested water inhibits the growth of other living organisms in the water body. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 415; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Water pollution',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141020,'chemistry','The process through which nutrient-rich water bodies support a dense plant population, killing animal life by depriving it of oxygen, is known as:','Eutrophication','The process in which nutrient enriched water bodies  support a dense plant population, which kills animal life by depriving it of oxygen and results in subsequent loss of biodiversity is known as Eutrophication.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 415; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Water pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Eutrophication','Biological Oxygen Demand','Favism','Biomagnification','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/141020q.png'),
(141021,'chemistry','Match the following pollutants with their sources.','Organic waste-animal excreta,Sediments-Strip mining,Pesticides-chemicals used for killing of fungi and insects,Heat- Coolant water','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 414; Table 14.1) diagram(ne1402)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Water pollution',NULL,'3','match','Organic waste,animal excreta','Sediments,Strip mining','Pesticides,chemicals used for killing of fungi and insects','Heat, Coolant water','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141601,'chemistry','The liquefied gas that is used in dry cleaning along with a suitable detergents is:','CO₂','Earlier, tetrachloroethene (Cl₂C=CCl₂) was used as a solvent for dry cleaning. The chemical contaminated groundwater and is also thought to be carcinogenic. The process using this compound is now being replaced by a process, where liquefied carbon dioxide, with a suitable detergent is used. Replacement of halogenated solvent with liquid CO₂ will do less damage to groundwater.\n(NEET 2019)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','CO₂','Water gas','Petroleum gas','NO₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141602,'chemistry','Among the following, the one that is not a greenhouse gas is:','Sulphur dioxide','Besides carbon dioxide, other greenhouse gases are methane, water vapours, nitrous oxide, CFCs and ozone. \n(NEET 2019)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Nitrous oxide','Methane','Ozone','Sulphur dioxide','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141603,'chemistry','Which oxide of nitrogen is not a common pollutant introduced into atmosphere both due to natural and human activity?','N₂O₅','Dinitrogen pentoxide (N₂O₅) is a rarely occurring  pollutant of nitrogen oxide. Dinitrogen pentoxide is an unstable and toxic oxidiser. It attracts NOₓ gases in the atmosphere, which cause ozone layer depletion.\n(NEET 2018)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','N₂O₅','NO₂','NO','N₂O','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141604,'chemistry','Which of the following is a sink for CO?','Microorganisms present in soil','Microorganisms present in the soil consume atmospheric CO.\n(NEET 2017)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Microorganisms present in soil','Hemoglobin','Oceans','Plants','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141605,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a common component of Photochemical Smog?','Chlorofluorocarbons','Photochemical smog is a combination of air pollutants that have been chemically changed into more toxic chemicals as a result of exposure to sunlight. The primary contributors to photochemical smog are nitrogen oxides, volatile organic compounds (VOCs), tropospheric ozone, and PAN (peroxyacetyl nitrate).\n(AIPMT 2014)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Ozone','Acrolein','Peroxyacetyl nitrate','Chlorofluorocarbons','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141606,'chemistry','Which of the following is not correct about carbon monoxide?','The carboxyhemoglobin (haemoglobin bound to CO) is less stable than oxyhaemoglobin.','The carboxyhemoglobin is about 300 times more stable than oxyhaemoglobin.\n(NEET 2020)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','It forms carboxyhemoglobin.','It reduces oxygen carrying ability of blood.','The carboxyhemoglobin (haemoglobin bound to CO) is less stable than oxyhaemoglobin.','It is produced due to incomplete combustion.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141607,'chemistry','Which one of the following statements regarding photochemical smog is not correct?','Photochemical smog does not cause irritation in eyes and throat.','Photochemical smog causes irritation in eyes and throat.\n(2012)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Carbon monoxide does not play any role in photochemical smog formation.','Photochemical smog is an oxidising agent in character.','Photochemical smog is formed through photochemical reaction involving solar energy.','Photochemical smog does not cause irritation in eyes and throat.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141608,'chemistry','Which one of the following is responsible for depletion of the ozone layer in the upper strata of the atmosphere?','Freons','Chlorofluorocarbons such as freon-11 (CFCl₃) and freon-12 (CF₂Cl₂) emitted as propellants in aerosol spray cans, refrigerators, fire fighting reagents etc. are stable compounds and chemically inert. They do not react with any substance with which they come in contact and thus float through the atmosphere unchanged and eventually enter the stratosphere. There they absorb UV radiations and break down liberating free atomic chlorine which causes decomposition of ozone. This results in the depletion of the ozone layer.\n(2004) diagram(ne1407)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Polyhalogens','Ferrocene','Fullerenes','Freons','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141609,'chemistry','About 20 km above the earth, there is an ozone layer. Which one of the following statements about ozone and ozone layer is true?','It is beneficial to us as it stops U.V. radiation.','Ozone layer is very beneficial to us, because it stops harmful ultraviolet radiations to reach the earth.\n (1995)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','It is beneficial to us as it stops U.V. radiation.','Conversion of O₃ to O₂ is an endothermic reaction.','Ozone is a triatomic linear molecule.','It is harmful as it stops useful radiation.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141610,'chemistry','Which one of the following statements is not true?','When the pH of rain water is higher than 6.5, it is called acid rain.','When pH of rain water drops below 5.6 it is called acid rain.\n(Karnataka NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Clean water would have a BOD value of 5 ppm.','Fluoride deficiency in drinking water is harmful. Soluble fluoride is often used to bring its concentration upto 1 ppm.','When the pH of rain water is higher than 6.5, it is called acid rain.','Dissolved Oxygen (DO) in cold water can reach a concentration upto 10 ppm.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141611,'chemistry','Which one of the following statement is not true?','Concentration of DO below 6 ppm is good for the growth of fish.','Fish dies in water bodies polluted by sewage due to decrease in dissolved oxygen (D.O). \n(2011)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','pH of drinking water should be between  5.5 – 9.5.','Concentration of DO below 6 ppm is good for the growth of fish.','Clean water would have a BOD value of less than 5 ppm.','Oxides of sulphur, nitrogen and carbon, are the most widespread air pollutant.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141612,'chemistry','Green chemistry means such reactions which:','reduce the use and production of hazardous chemicals.','Green chemistry is the design, development, and implementation of chemical products and processes to reduce or eliminate the use and generation of substances hazardous to human health and the environment. Green chemistry also refers to the redesign of chemical products and processes with the goal of reducing or eliminating any negative environmental or health effects.\n (2008)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','are related to the depletion of ozone layer','study the reactions in plants','produce colour during reactions','reduce the use and production of hazardous chemicals.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141613,'chemistry','Match List-I with List-II and choose the correct answer from the options given below. \ndiagram(n1408)','(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)','(NEET 2021) diagram(ne1409)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)','(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)','(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)','(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/141613q.png'),
(141614,'chemistry','The pollution due to oxides of sulphur gets enhanced due to the presence of:\n(a) particulate matter\n(b) ozone\n(c) hydrocarbons\n(d) hydrogen peroxide\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.','(a), (b), (d) only','Presence of particulate matter in polluted air catalyzes the oxidation of SO₂ to SO₃. \ni.e., 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2SO₃(g)\nThis reaction can also be promoted by O₃ and H₂O₂, as\nSO₂(g) + O₃(g) → SO₃(g) + O₂(g) \nSO₂(g) + H₂O₂(l) → H₂SO₄(aq)\n(NEET 2022)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(a), (d) only','(a), (b), (d) only','(b), (c), (d) only','(a), (c), (d) only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(171239,'chemistry','Given below are two statements:\n\nAssertion (A): The conductivity of a solution decreases with dilution\n\nReason (R): On dilution, the number of ions per unit volume increases\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','The conductivity of a solution is the conductance of ions present in a unit volume of the solution.\n \nOn dilution, the number of ions per unit volume decreases. Hence the conductivity decreases.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 85; Example No. 3.7)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-085','12-051',NULL),
(141701,'chemistry','"Carbon monoxide is poisonous, whereas carbon dioxide is not." Choose the best probable explanation for this phenomenon.','CO forms a more stable complex with haemoglobin than the oxygen-hemoglobin complex.','Carbon monoxide is poisonous because it can form a more stable complex with haemoglobin (carboxyhemoglobin) than the oxygen-hemoglobin complex. A carboxyhemoglobin concentration of 3-4% reduces the oxygen-carrying capacity of blood. This causes headaches, blurred vision, anxiety, and cardiovascular problems. A higher level of concentration may possibly result in death. In comparison to carbon monoxide, carbon dioxide is not as toxic. Only at really high concentrations it becomes dangerous.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 421; Exercise 14.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CO creates a stable bond with oxygen, forcing the O₂ bonds to break.','CO forms a more stable complex with haemoglobin than the oxygen-hemoglobin complex.','CO interferes with the glucose carrying capacity of blood.','CO immediately attacks the lungs, making breathing impossible.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141702,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: The statues and monuments are damaged by acid rain.\n\nReason: Acid rain gives the following reaction with marble: \nCaCO₃ + H₂SO₄  →  CaSO₄ + H₂O  +  CO₂','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','‣ Acid rain is caused by a variety of human activities that release sulphur and nitrogen oxides into the atmosphere. These oxides get oxidised and subsequently react with water vapour to generate acids. i.e., 2SO₂ (g) + O₂ (g) + 2H₂O (l) → 2H₂SO₄(aq)\n4NO₂ (g) + O₂ (g) + 2H₂O (l) → 4HNO₃ (aq)\n‣ Acid rain reacts with the marble of monuments and statues: \nCaCO₃ + H₂SO₄  →  CaSO₄ + H₂O  +  CO₂\nThus, causing them to lose their brilliance and colour and eventually disintegrate.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 421; Exercise 14.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141703,'chemistry','Mark the reaction that does not takes place in the formation of photochemical smog.','Cl• (g) + O₃(g) → ClO•(g) + O₂(g)','The following reactions take place during the formation of photochemical smog:\n‣ In the presence of sunlight, nitrogen dioxide is converted to nitric oxide by the following reaction:\nNO₂(g) → NO(g) + O(g) \n‣ The nascent oxygen produced in the above reaction combines with oxygen O2 to produce ozone in a reversible reaction as follows:\nO(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ O₃ (g) \n‣ Ozone combines with nitric oxide to produce nitrogen dioxide and oxygen gas. Following is the reaction:\nO₃(g) + NO(g) → NO₂(g) + O₂ (g) \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 421; Exercise 14.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','O₃(g) + NO(g) → NO₂(g) + O₂ (g)','Cl• (g) + O₃(g) → ClO•(g) + O₂(g)','O(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ O₃ (g)','NO₂(g) → NO(g) + O(g)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141704,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about "Photochemical smog control" are true?\n\na. The primary precursors of photochemical smog can be reduced by controlling the secondary precursors.\n\nb. Automobile exhaust appears to be the primary source of pollution.\n\nc. The use of catalytic converters in automobiles can avoid the emission of nitrogen oxide and hydrocarbons.\n\nd. Photochemical pollution can be reduced by planting CO₂-metabolizing plants.','Both b and c','The following methods are used to control the formation of photochemical smog:\na. Statement (a) is incorrect as controlling the primary precursors of photochemical smog, such as NO₂ and hydrocarbons, as well as the secondary precursors, such as ozone and PAN, will automatically eliminate photochemical smog.\nb. Statement (b) is correct as automobile exhaust appears to be the primary source of pollution.\nc. Statement (c) is correct because the use of catalytic converters in automobiles can avoid the emission of nitrogen oxide and hydrocarbons.  \nd. Statement (d) is incorrect because plants that can metabolise nitrogen oxide (NO, NO₂), such as Pinus, Juniperus, Quercus, Pyrus, and Vitis, could aid in reducing photochemical smog.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 421; Exercise 14.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both a and c','Both c and d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141705,'chemistry','Choose the false answer for \'Green chemistry\':\n\na. Green chemistry is a cost-effective technique which involves reduction in material, energy consumption and waste generation.\n\nb. The solution to agricultural pollution is to stop the traditional development process.\n\nc. During a synthesis, starting materials must be carefully chosen so that they can react and yield additional byproducts.\n\nd. To reduce environmental pollution, byproducts generated during a process should be used effectively.','Both b and c','a. Statement (a) is correct as Green chemistry is a cost effective approach which involves reduction in material, energy consumption and waste generation.\nb. Statement (b) is false because the solution to agricultural pollution does not lie in stopping the process of development that has been set in; but to discover methods, which would help in the reduction of deterioration of the environment.\nc. Statement (c) is false too because during a synthesis, care must be taken to choose starting materials that can be converted into end products with yield approximately upto 100 per cent.\nd. Statement (d) is correct as the byproducts generated during a process, if not used gainfully, add to the environmental pollution.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page; 419 & 420, paragraph; 3,4 & 2,3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both c and d','Both a and c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141706,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: Metathesis is used in the production of revolutionary environmentally friendly polymers.\n\nReason: Metathesis minimizes potentially harmful waste by manufacturing wisely.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Metathesis is a way to rearrange groups of atoms within molecules.The metathesis has enormous commercial potential in the pharmaceutical, biotechnology, and food industries. It is also used in the production of ground-breaking environmentally friendly polymers. \nThis is a significant step forward for \'green chemistry,\' as it reduces potentially harmful waste through wiser production. Metathesis illustrates the importance of applying basic science for the benefit of man, society, and the environment.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page; 419, paragraph; "Nobel goes to green chemists")','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141707,'chemistry','How can an individual contribute towards a better quality of our environment and human life?','Set up a compost tin in home and replace fertilizers by producing manure from it.','Individually, you can help to improve the quality of the environment and human existence by adopting the following rules:\na. Always set up a compost tin in your garden or any other place in your home and use it to produce manure for your plants to reduce the use of fertilizers.\nb. Use a cloth bag and avoid asking for plastic carry bags when you buy groceries, vegetables or any other item.  \nc. See that all newspapers, glass, aluminum and other items in your area are recycled. \nd. Use public transport to reduce automobile effects. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 420; Paragraph \'Think it over\')','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Set up a compost tin in home and replace fertilizers by producing manure from it.','Use plastic bags to carry groceries, vegetables or any other item.','Dump all newspapers, glass, aluminum and other items in your area\'s landfill.','Use personal cars and vehicles.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141708,'chemistry','Which of the following ways to reduce pollution on an individual level are correct?\n\na. Use chemical fertilizers in gardens rather than compost.\n\nb. Use pesticides like DDT, and malathion at home.\n\nc. Add a few crystals of potassium permanganate (KMnO₄) or bleaching powder to your home\'s water tank.\n\nd. Notify the press or the pollution control board\'s office about the polluted water body in your neighbourhood.','Both c and d','Following are the measures one can take to limit the release of pollutants into the environment.\na. Use compost in gardens rather than chemical fertilizers.\nb. Avoid using pesticides like DDT, malathion, and others at home instead use dried neem leaves to keep insects at bay.\nc. Add a few crystals of potassium permanganate (KMnO₄) or bleaching powder to your home\'s water tank.\nd. Notify the press or the pollution control board\'s office about the polluted water body in your neighbourhood.\nHence, statements (a) and (b) are false.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 416; Paragraph \'Activity 2\')','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both a and b','Both c and d','Both a and c','Both b and d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141709,'chemistry','Select the incorrect option(s) from the statements about "Environmental pollution":\n\na. Environmental contamination is the result of unfavourable changes in our environment.\n\nb. The most significant form of environmental contamination is food pollution.\n\nc. Degradable pollutants include substances such as DDT, plastics, and nuclear waste.\n\nd. Pollutants cannot naturally exit the environment.','b, c and d','a. Statement (a) is correct as environmental pollution is the effect of undesirable changes in our surroundings that have harmful effects on plants, animals and human beings. \nb. Statement (b) is incorrect because an average person requires 12-15 times more air than food. Therefore, even small amounts of air pollutants become significant when compared to similar levels in foods.\nc. Statement (c) is incorrect too because pollutants that degrade slowly include dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane (DDT), plastic materials, heavy metals, numerous chemicals, nuclear waste, and others.\nd. Statement (c) is incorrect too as pollutants are compounds that are present in higher concentrations than in natural abundance and are formed as a result of human activity or natural events.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 421; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only c','a, b and c','Both c and d','b, c and d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141710,'chemistry','Pollution from gaseous and particulate pollutants happens in the:','Troposphere','Atmospheric pollution is generally studied as tropospheric and stratospheric pollution.\n▸ Tropospheric pollution occurs due to the presence of undesirable solid or gaseous particles in the air. \n▸ Stratospheric pollution occurs due to depletion of the ozone layer (ozone hole) by certain compounds like oxides of nitrogen, chlorofluorocarbons(CFCs).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 407; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Troposphere','Stratosphere','Mesosphere','Exosphere','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141711,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: Particulate matter increases sulphur trioxide concentrations in the atmosphere.\n\nReason: Ultra violet rays catalyze the oxidation of SO₂ to SO₃.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Uncatalysed oxidation of sulphur dioxide is slow. However, the presence of particulate matter in polluted air catalyses the oxidation of sulphur dioxide to sulphur trioxide. \n2SO₂ (g) +O₂ (g) → 2SO₃ (g) \nThe reaction can also be promoted by ozone and hydrogen peroxide. \nSO₂ (g) +O₃ (g) → SO₃ (g) + O₂ (g) \nSO₂ (g)  + H₂O₂ (l) → H₂SO₄ (aq)\nHence the assertion is true, but the reason is false.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 407; Paragraph 8)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141712,'chemistry','Choose the correct option for the given statements.\na. When lightning strikes at high altitudes, N₂ and O₂ combine to generate nitrogen oxides.\n\nb. When fossil fuel is used in an automobile engine, N₂ and O₂ combine to produce considerable amounts of nitric oxide (NO) and nitrogen dioxide (NO₂).\n\nc. When nitric oxide combines with dioxygen in the troposphere, the rate of NO₂ generation increases.\n\nd. At room temperature, dinitrogen and dioxygen combine to produce NO.','Both a and b','▸Dinitrogen and dioxygen are the main constituents of air. These gases do not react with each other at a normal temperature. \n▸At high altitudes when lightning strikes, they combine to form oxides of nitrogen. \n▸In an automobile engine, (at high temperature) when fossil fuel is burnt, dinitrogen and dioxygen combine to yield significant quantities of nitric oxide (NO) and nitrogen dioxide (NO₂). \n▸Rate of production of NO₂ is faster when nitric oxide reacts with ozone in the stratosphere.\nHence, Statements (c) and (d) are incorrect.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 407; Paragraph 9, 10)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both a and b','Both c and d','Both a and c','Both b and d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(171240,'chemistry','Which one is the way to determine the ᴧ°ₘ value of water?','Using Kohlrausch law','We can determine ᴧ°ₘ by using Kohlrauschs law.The law states that limiting molar conductivity of an electrolyte can be represented as the sum of the individual contributions of the anion and cation of the electrolyte.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 83)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Using Kohlrausch law','Using Galvanic cell','Using Nernst equation','All of these','NEW','12-083','12-049',NULL),
(141713,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: Particulate pollutants bigger than 5 microns are likely to lodge in the nasal passage, whilst particles of about 10 micron enter into lungs easily.\n\nReason: The effect of particulate pollutants are largely dependent on the nature of particle.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','‣ The effect of particulate pollutants are mainly dependent on the particle size. \n‣ Particulate pollutants bigger than 5 microns are likely to lodge in the nasal passage, whereas particles of about 10 micron enter into lungs easily.\nHence, the assertion is true, but the reason is false.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 411; Paragraph 7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141714,'chemistry','Mark the false statement regarding DDT.','DDT is a biodegradable insecticide.','‣ During World War II, DDT was found to be of great use in the control of malaria and other insect-borne diseases. \n‣ After the war, DDT was put to use in agriculture to control the damages caused by insects, rodents, weeds and various crop diseases. \n‣ Because DDT is non biodegradable and has adverse effects, its use has been banned in India.\n‣ Repeated use of similar pesticides causes insect resistance.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page ; Paragraph )','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','DDT can control insect borne diseases.','DDT is both a pesticide and a herbicide.','DDT is a biodegradable insecticide.','Repeated use of DDT caused insect resistance.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141715,'chemistry','Choose the correct option(s) from the following statements regarding " Particulate pollutants":\na. Particulate pollutants are found in vehicle emissions, fire smoke particles, dust particles, and ash from industries.\nb. Some of the viable particulate particles produce allergic reactions in humans.\nc. Mercury, a particulate pollutant, interferes with the development and maturation of red blood cells.\nd. Non-viable particles are classified depending on their shape and structure.','Both a and b','a. Statement (a) is correct as particulate pollutants are found in vehicle emissions, fire smoke particles, dust particles, and ash from industries.\nb.  Statement (b) is correct too as bacteria, fungus, moulds, algae, and other viable particles are minute living creatures that are dispersed in the atmosphere. Some fungus found in the air cause allergic reactions in humans.\nc. Statement (c) is incorrect because lead, a particle pollutant, interferes with red blood cell formation and maturation.\nd. Statement (d) is also incorrect because non-viable particles are classified according to their nature and size.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 411; Paragraph 2,3 & 8)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both a and c','Both b and d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141716,'chemistry','Given below are two statements.\nStatement I: Instead of alum, tamarind seed kernel powder should be used to treat wastewater.\nStatement II: Alum has been found to increase toxic ions in treated water, which can cause disease. \nChoose the best answer based on the statements above.','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','The powdered kernel of tamarind seeds has been discovered to be an efficient substance for cleaning municipal and industrial waste water. It is a non-toxic, biodegradable, and low-cost substance. Typically, this powder is dumped as agricultural waste. The current approach is to treat such water with alum. Alum has been discovered to increase harmful ions in treated water, which can cause sickness.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 420; Paragraph 8)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.','Statement I is correct, and Statement II is incorrect.','Statement I is incorrect, and Statement II is correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141717,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: Fish productivity suffers as a result of ozone layer depletion.\n\nReason: Increased UV exposure causes the death of phytoplanktons.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Phytoplanktons die as a result of increased UV exposure. The dissolved organic stuff in water is decomposed by a massive population of bacteria. They feed on dissolved oxygen in water. The amount of oxygen that water can hold in solution is limited. As a result, fish productivity decreases. Hence , ozone depletion is responsible for the fall in fish productivity.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 414; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141718,'chemistry','Mark the reaction that is not involved in ozone layer depletion.','3CH₄ + 2O₃   →  3CH₂=O + 3H₂O','When ultraviolet rays react with oxygen molecules (O2), they produce ozone (O3) and atomic oxygen (O).\nhν + O₂ → 2O•\nO₂ + O• ⇌ O₃\nThe formation and breakdown of ozone are in dynamic equilibrium. The interruption of this equilibrium causes ozone depletion.\nChlorofluorocarbons combine with atmospheric gases and rise to the stratosphere, where UV radiation degrade them into chlorine free radicals. These chlorine free radicals react with ozone to produce oxygen and ClO• free radicals, which then react with atomic oxygen to generate more chlorine free radicals.\nCF₂Cl₂ → Cl• + C•F₂Cl\nCl• + O₃ → ClO• + O₂\nClO• + O• → Cl• + O₂\n As a result, the ozone layer is damaged by the constant decomposition of ozone in the stratosphere.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 421; Exercise 14.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CF₂Cl₂ (g) → Cl•(g)  + C•F₂Cl (g)','Cl• (g) + O₃ (g) → ClO• (g) + O₂ (g)','3CH₄ + 2O₃   →  3CH₂=O + 3H₂O','ClO• (g) + O(g) → Cl•(g) + O₂(g)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141719,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the "Consequences of ozone depletion".\na. As the ozone layer depleted, more UV radiation filters into the thermosphere.\nb. UV rays easily affect animal proteins, resulting in dangerous cell mutations.\nc. Increased UV radiation damages paints and fibres, causing them to fade more quickly.\nd. UV radiation causes skin ageing, cataracts, sunburn, and skin cancer.','Both c and d','a. As the ozone layer depletes, more UV radiation filters into the troposphere.\nb. UV radiations easily affect plant proteins, causing harmful cell mutations.\nc. Increased UV radiation damages paints and fibres, causing them to fade more quickly.\nd. UV radiation causes skin ageing, cataracts, sunburn, and skin cancer.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 421; Exercise 14.10)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both a and b','Both c and d','Both a and c','Both b and d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141720,'chemistry','Match the following water pollutants to their sources.','Sediments-Soil erosion by agriculture and strip mining, Radioactive substances-Mining of uranium containing minerals, Plant nutrients-Chemical fertilizers, Organic wastes-Decaying animals and plants','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 421; Exercise 14.11) diagram(ne1410)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','match','Sediments,Soil erosion by agriculture and strip mining','Radioactive substances,Mining of uranium containing minerals','Plant nutrients,Chemical fertilizers','Organic wastes,Decaying animals and plants','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141721,'chemistry','Choose the correct option. \n\na. The most abundant greenhouse gases on Earth are CO₂, CH₄, O₃, CFCs, and water vapour\n\nb. The atmosphere warms up as a result of radiation absorption\n\nc. greenhouse gases are harmful as they make  the Earth\'s temperature unsustainable to life..','a,b','The most abundant greenhouse gases on Earth are CO₂, CH₄, O₃, CFCs, and water vapour. These gases exist close to the Earth\'s surface. They capture solar energy emitted back from the Earth\'s surface. The atmosphere warms up as a result of radiation absorption. Hence , greenhouse gases are critical for maintaining the Earth\'s temperature in order to sustain life.\nIn the absence of greenhouse gases, the average temperature of the planet will drop significantly, making it uninhabitable. Life on Earth would be impossible as a result.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 421; Exercise 14.17)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','all of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141722,'chemistry','Mark the option that is not involved in the water pollution.','Freons','Some major water pollutants are microorganisms, plant nutrients, toxic heavy metals and pesticides. Freons also known as chlorofluorocarbons are responsible for ozone layer depletion.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 414; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Fertilizers','Freons','Organic wastes','Pesticides','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141723,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: Greenhouse gases cause climate change, respiratory diseases, and air pollution, yet they are necessary for life on Earth.\n\nReason: Greenhouse gases absorb solar energy, maintaining the Earth\'s temperature stable.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The most abundant greenhouse gases on Earth are CO₂, CH₄, O₃, CFCs, and water vapour. These gases exist close to the Earth\'s surface. They capture solar energy emitted back from the Earth\'s surface. The atmosphere warms up as a result of radiation absorption. Hence , greenhouse gases are critical for maintaining the Earth\'s temperature in order to sustain life.\nIn the absence of greenhouse gases, the average temperature of the planet will drop significantly, making it uninhabitable. Life on Earth would be impossible as a result.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 421; Exercise 14.17)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141724,'chemistry','Assume you\'ve been tasked with investigating the reason of a large fish death in a lake. You found no harmful substances in the water sample, but you did find a lot of phytoplankton. How would you deduce the cause of this phenomenon?','Phytoplanktons depleted the oxygen in the water.','Water has a finite amount of dissolved oxygen. The abundance of phytoplanktons depletes this oxygen. The bacteria that decompose phytoplankton need a lot of oxygen. They utilize dissolved oxygen. Fish growth and activity usually require 5-6 ppm of dissolved oxygen. The BOD level in the water falls below 6 ppm, which restricts fish growth and causes significant fish kill.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 421; Exercise 14.18)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Phytoplanktons increased the water\'s BOD level below 5 ppm.','Phytoplanktons decreased the dissolved organic matter.','Phytoplanktons depleted the oxygen in the water.','The phytoplanktons themselves are hazardous to fish.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141725,'chemistry','Given below are two statements.\nStatement I:  Plastic, glass, metal scraps, and so on should be recycled.\nStatement II:  Biodegradable waste decomposes aerobically and anaerobically into manure\nIn light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options below.','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Domestic waste can be classified into two types based on its composition: biodegradable and non-biodegradable. Biodegradable waste, such as leaves and rotten food, should be disposed of in landfills, where it decomposes aerobically and anaerobically into manure. Non-biodegradable waste (that cannot be degraded) such as plastic, glass, metal scraps, and so on should be recycled.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 421; Exercise 14.19)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.','Statement I is correct, and Statement II is incorrect.','Statement I is incorrect, and Statement II is correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141726,'chemistry','Assume a farmer attempted to build a compost producing pit for his agricultural field. After a few days, the pit acquired a terrible odour and flies. Where did the farmer commit a mistake?','He did not covered the pit properly.','The decomposition of organic materials occurs with the passage of time. Composting involves burying organic wastes beneath layers of dirt. Microorganisms such as bacteria and fungi can then decompose these organic wastes.\n▸ Covering the composite pit will help to reduce the odour\n▸ Flies are prevented from entering the composting pit in this manner.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 421; Exercise 14.20)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','He did not burned the waste properly.','He added degradable waste in the pit.','He did not added disinfectants in the pit.','He did not covered the pit properly.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(171241,'chemistry','One of the simplest electrolytic cells consists of two copper strips dipping in an aqueous solution of copper _______','sulphate','One of the simplest electrolytic cells consists of two copper strips dipping in an aqueous solution of copper sulphate.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 85; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','fillup','ro,sul,xide,pha,te,hyd',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-085','12-051',NULL),
(171242,'chemistry','During electrolysis of a copper cell, which one is true?\n\na) Copper metal is deposited on the anode\n\nb) At the anode, Cu is converted into Cu²⁺ ions\n\nc) Copper is dissolved at anode','Only b and c','Copper metal is deposited on the cathode. \n\nAt the anode, copper is converted into Cu²⁺ ions by the reaction:\n\nCu(s) → Cu²⁺(s) + 2e–\n\nThus copper is dissolved (oxidized) at the anode and deposited (reduced) at the cathode\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 85; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Only a and b','Only b and c','Only a and c','All of these','NEW','12-085','12-051',NULL),
(141727,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: During a synthesis, it is critical to select starting materials that can be transformed into end products with a yield of up to 100%.\n\nReason: If reactants in a chemical process are fully converted into useful eco - friendly products utilising an eco friendly medium, no chemical pollutants are introduced into the environment.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','If reactants in a chemical process are fully transformed into useful environment friendly products utilising an eco friendly medium, no chemical pollutants are introduced into the environment. \nDuring synthesis, care must be taken to select starting materials that can be transformed into end products with yields of up to 100%. This can be accomplished by establishing optimal synthesis conditions.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 420; Paragraph 2,3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141728,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement.\na. In cold water, dissolved oxygen (DO) can reach a concentration up to 10,000 ppm.\nb. Dumped leaves, grass, and other organic matter have no impact on water quality.\nc. Dissolved oxygen concentration in water is critical for aquatic life.\nd. The amount of BOD in the water is a measure of the amount of organic material in the water, expressed in terms of how much oxygen is required to break it down biologically.','Both a and b','a. Statement (a) is incorrect because in cold water, dissolved oxygen (DO) can reach a concentration up to 10 ppm.\nb. Statement (b) is incorrect too because organic matter, such as leaves, grass, and rubbish, is another major water pollutant.\nc. Statement (c) is correct as dissolved oxygen concentration in water is critical for aquatic life.\nd. Statement (d) is also correct as the amount of BOD in the water is a measure of the amount of organic material in the water, expressed in terms of how much oxygen is required to break it down biologically.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 414-415; Paragraph 6,7-1,3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both a and c','Both b and d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141729,'chemistry','Given below are two statements.\n\nStatement I: Fertilizers indirectly cause anaerobic conditions in water.\n\nStatement II: Algae growth in water covers the water\'s surface and reduces its oxygen concentration.\n\nChoose the best answer based on the statements above','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Fertilizers contain phosphates as additives. The addition of phosphates in water enhances algae growth. Such profuse growth of algae, covers the water surface and reduces the oxygen concentration in water. This leads to anaerobic conditions, commonly with accumulation of obnoxious decay and animal death. Hence, Both statements are correct.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 415; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.','Statement I is correct, and Statement II is incorrect.','Statement I is incorrect, and Statement II is correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141730,'chemistry','Fluoride ions are necessary to harden tooth enamel by converting [3(Ca₃(PO₄)₂.Ca(OH)₂] to:','[3(Ca₃(PO₄)₂.CaF₂]','The F⁻ ions make the enamel on teeth much harder by converting hydroxyapatite, [3(Ca₃(PO₄)₂.Ca(OH)₂], the enamel on the surface of the teeth, into much harder fluorapatite, [3(Ca₃(PO₄)₂.CaF₂].\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 415; Paragraph 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','[3(Ca₃(PO₄)₂.CaOCl₂]','[3(Ca₃(PO₄)₂.CaF₂]','[3(Ca₃(PO₄)₂.CaCl₂]','CaF₄','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141731,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statement regarding "lead in drinking water".','Excess of lead in drinking water causes blue baby syndrome.','Drinking water gets contaminated with lead when lead pipes are used for transportation of water. The prescribed upper limit concentration of lead in drinking water is about 50 ppb. Lead can damage kidney, liver, reproductive system etc.\nExcess nitrate in drinking water can cause methemoglobinemia (\'blue baby\' syndrome). Hence, option (d) is incorrect.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page ; Paragraph )','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','When lead pipes transport water, they contaminate it with lead.','The maximum allowed concentration of lead in drinking water is 50 ppb.','Lead can damage kidney, liver, reproductive system etc.','Excess of lead in drinking water causes blue baby syndrome.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141732,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect option(s) from the following statements regarding " Pesticides ":\na. Chlorinated organic toxins are more biodegradable and less persistent.\nb. Herbicides are less persistent as compared to organophosphate.\nc. Aldrin and Dieldrin were introduced in the market by pesticide industry to replace DDT as insects become resistant to DDT.\nd. Due to its adverse effect, Orango phosphates has been banned in India.','Both a and d','a. Organophosphates and carbamates, which are less persistent or more biodegradable than chlorinated organic toxins, have been introduced the market as a solution to its high persistence.\nb. Most herbicides are toxic to mammals but are not as persistent as organo-chlorides. These chemicals decompose in a few months.\nc. Aldrin and Dieldrin were introduced in the market by pesticide industry to replace DDT as insects become resistant to DDT.\nd. Due to its adverse effect, DDT has been banned in India.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 417; Paragraph 1,2 & 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both c and d','Both a and d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141733,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: High persistent pesticides are transported from lower to higher trophic levels via the food chain.\n\nReason: Organic pesticides are insoluble in water and nonbiodegradable.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Pesticides are basically synthetic toxic chemicals with ecological implications. The majority of organic toxins are insoluble in water and nonbiodegradable. As a result, these highly persistent pesticides are transported from lower to higher trophic levels via the food chain. Toxin concentrations in higher animals rise over time, causing major metabolic and physiological problems.\nHence, The assertion and the reason are both correct, and the reason explains the assertion correctly.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 417; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141734,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statement for "controlling soil pollution."','Household and biological waste should be disposed in rural areas directly.','Following are the ways to reduce soil pollution\n‣ A suitable way for controlling the soil pollution is to avoid the direct addition of pollutants to the soil And also wastes should undergo proper treatment. \n‣ Non-biodegradable waste should be recycled rather than buried in the soil.\n‣ Excessive usage of insecticides and pesticides should be avoided.\n‣ Household waste, biological waste, and chemical waste should be incinerated. Incineration significantly reduces waste volume.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 421; Exercise 14.14)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Before disposal, waste should be carefully treated and recycled.','Household and biological waste should be disposed in rural areas directly.','Excessive usage of insecticides and pesticides should be avoided.','Non-biodegradable waste should be recycled rather than buried in the soil.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141735,'chemistry','Which of the following gases does not contribute to the greenhouse effect?','Oxygen','The gases which are responsible for the greenhouse effect are:\ni. Carbon dioxide CO₂\n​ii. Methane CH₄\niii. Water H₂O\niv. Nitrous oxide NO\nv. Ozone O₃\n​vii. Chlorofluorocarbons CFCs\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 421; Exercise 14.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Oxygen','Carbon dioxide','Nitrous oxide','​Chlorofluorocarbons','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141736,'chemistry','Mark the correct option regarding "Tropospheric pollution".\na. Tropospheric pollution occurs in the lowest layer of the atmosphere.\nb. Carbon monoxide is a carcinogenic tropospheric pollutant.\nc. Hydrocarbons are poisonous because they react with haemoglobin in the blood, resulting in death.\nd. Carbon dioxide is poisonous in nature.','Both a and d','a. Tropospheric pollution occurs when undesired substances are found in the lowest layer of the atmosphere. The major gaseous pollutants are sulphur oxides, nitrogen oxides, carbon oxides, and hydrocarbons. \nb. Hydrocarbons are carcinogenic, and their byproducts are significant pollutants. \nc. Carbon monoxide is toxic in nature because it combines with haemoglobin in the blood, causing death. \nd. Despite the fact that carbon dioxide is not poisonous in nature, it contributes to global warming by trapping reflected IR photons. This causes the Earth\'s atmosphere to warm, resulting in the melting of icebergs and glaciers.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 421; Exercise 14.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both a and d','Both c and d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141737,'chemistry','Which of the following is a herbicide?','Sodium arsenite','‣  Pesticides are man made, poisonous compounds that are used in agriculture to control insect, rodent, weed, and crop disease damage. For example, DDT, Carbamate, and Organophosphate.\n‣  Herbicides are chemicals that are used to manage weeds. For example sodium chlorate (NaCIO₃) and sodium arsenite (Na₃AsO₃)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 421; Exercise 14.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','DDT','Sodium arsenite','Carbamates','Organophosphates','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141801,'chemistry','Identify the incorrect statement from the following:\n [AIEEE-2011]','Ozone absorbs infrared radiation','Ozone absorbs infrared radiation(Fatual information)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Ozone absorbs infrared radiation','Oxides of nitrogen in the atmosphere can\n cause the depletion of ozone layer','Ozone absorbs the intense ultraviolet radiation\n of the sun','Depletion of ozone layer is because of its\n chemical reactions with chlorofluoro alkanes','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141802,'chemistry','The gas leaked from a storage tank of the Union\n Carbide plant in Bhopal gas tragedy was\n [JEE (Main)-2013]','Methylisocyanate','Methylisocynate (CH₃NCO) a deadly poisonous\n gas leaked from a storage tank of the Union\n Carbide Plant in Bhopal gas tragedy.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Methylisocyanate','Methylamine','Ammonia','Phosgene','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141803,'chemistry','The concentration of fluoride, lead, nitrate and iron\n in a water sample from an underground lake was\n found to be 1000 ppb, 40 ppb, 100 ppm and 0.2\nppm, respectively. This water is unsuitable for\n drinking due to high concentration of','Nitrate','The maximum limit of nitrate in drinking water is\n 50 ppm.\nExcess nitrate in drinking water can cause blue\n baby syndrome.\nThe prescribed limit for fluoride is 10 ppm.\nThe prescribed limit for lead is 50 ppb.\nThe prescribed limit for iron is 0.2 ppm.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Lead','Nitrate','Iron','Fluoride','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141804,'chemistry','A water sample has ppm level concentration of\n following anions\nF⁻ = 10; SO₄²⁻ = 100; NO₃⁻ = 50\nThe anion/anions that make/makes the water\n sample unsuitable for drinking is/are\n[JEE (Main)-2017]','Only F⁻','Permissible limit of F ⁻ in drinking water is upto 1\nppm. Excess concentration of F⁻ > 10 ppm\n causes decay of bones.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Only F⁻','Only SO₄\n²⁻','Only NO₃\n⁻','Both SO₄\n²⁻ and NO₃\n⁻','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141805,'chemistry','The recommended concentration of fluoride ion in\n drinking water is up to 1 ppm as fluoride ion is\n required to make teeth enamel harder by converting\n [3Ca₃(PO₄)\n₂.Ca(OH)₂] to [JEE (Main)-2018]','CF₂Cl₂','F⁻  ions make the teeth enamel harder by\n converting\n\n[3Ca₃(PO₄)₂.Ca(OH)₂](Hydroxyapatite) to [3Ca₃(PO₄)₂.CaF₂](Fluorapatite)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','H₃CCOOONO₂','CH₃CHCHO','CF₂Cl₂','O₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141806,'chemistry','Which of the following conditions in drinking water\n causes methemoglobinemia? [JEE (Main)-2019]','> 50 ppm of nitrate','Methemoglobinemia is caused by drinking water\n which is contaminated with nitrate.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','> 50 ppm of nitrate','> 50 ppm of lead','> 50 ppm of chloride','> 100 ppm of sulphate','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141807,'chemistry','Water filled in two glasses A and B have BOD\n values of 10 and 20, respectively. The correct\n statement regarding them, is [JEE (Main)-2019]','B is more polluted than A','As B.O.D. increase, level of pollution in water\n increases.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and B are suitable for drinking','A is suitable for drinking, whereas B is not','B is more polluted than A','A is more polluted than B','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141808,'chemistry','The reaction that is NOT involved in the ozone\n layer depletion mechanism in the stratosphere is\n [JEE (Main)-2019] Refer diagram(n1410)','3','CH₄ + 2O₃ → 3CH₂\n=O + 3H₂\nO not involved in\n the ozone layer.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141809,'chemistry','The concentration of dissolved oxygen (DO) in cold\n water can go upto: [JEE (Main)-2019]','10 ppm','DO in cold water can go upto 10 ppm','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','14 ppm','16 ppm','10 ppm','8 ppm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141810,'chemistry','Peroxyacetyl nitrate (PAN), an eye irritant is\n produced by [JEE (Main)-2019]','Photochemical smog','P.A.N is produced by Photochemical smog.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Classical smog','Acid rain','Organic waste','Photochemical smog','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141811,'chemistry','Taj Mahal is being slowly disfigured and\n discoloured. This is primarily due to\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','Acid rain','Taj Mahal is being slowly disfigured and discoloured\n due to Acid Rain.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Acid rain','Water pollution','Global warming','Soil pollution','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141812,'chemistry','The higher concentration of which gas in air can\n cause stiffness of flower buds? [JEE (Main)-2019]','SO₂','High concentration of SO₂ leads to stiffness of\n flower buds.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','SO₂','CO','CO₂','NO₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(141813,'chemistry','Water samples with BOD values of 4 ppm and\n 18 ppm, respectively, are [JEE (Main)-2019]','Clean and Highly polluted','Clean water have BOD value of less than 5 ppm\n whereas highly polluted water could have BOD\n value of 17 ppm or more.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Clean and Highly polluted','Clean and Clean','Highly polluted and Clean','Highly polluted and Highly polluted','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141814,'chemistry','The molecule that has minimum/no role in the\n formation of photochemical smog, is\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','NO','NO, O₃ and HCHO are involved in the formation\n photochemical smog.\nN₂ has no role in photochemical smog','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','NO','CH₂\n=O','O₃','N₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141815,'chemistry','The compound that is NOT a common component\n of photochemical smog is [JEE (Main)-2019]','CF₂Cl₂','CF₂Cl₂ is not a common component of\n photochemical smog.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','H₃CCOOONO₂','CH₂\n=CHCHO','CF₂Cl₂','O₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141816,'chemistry','The upper stratosphere consisting of the ozone\n layer protects us from the sun’s radiation that falls\n in the wavelength region of [JEE (Main)-2019]','200 – 315 nm','Ozone layer protects from ultra violet radiation.\n→ Wavelength range lies in 200 – 315 nm','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','200 – 315 nm','600 – 750 nm','400 – 550 nm','0.8 – 1.5 nm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141817,'chemistry','Which is wrong with respect to our responsibility as\n a human being to protect our environment?\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','Using plastic bags','Use of plastic bags is hazardous to our environment','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Using plastic bags','Restricting the use of vehicles','Avoiding the use of floodlighted facilities','Setting up compost tin in gardens','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141818,'chemistry','Assertion : Ozone is destroyed by CFCs in the\n upper stratosphere.\nReason : Ozone holes increase the amount of UV\n radiation reaching the earth. [JEE (Main)-2019]','Assertion and reason are correct, but the\n reason is not the explanation for the assertion','Refer diagram(ne1411)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion and reason are both correct, and the\n reason is the correct explanation for the\n assertion.','Assertion is false, but the reason is correct.','Assertion and reason are correct, but the\n reason is not the explanation for the assertion','Assertion and reason are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141819,'chemistry','The maximum prescribed concentration of copper\n in drinking water is : [JEE (Main)-2019]','3 ppm','Maximum prescribed concentration of Cu in\n drinking water is 3 ppm.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','3 ppm','0.05 ppm','0.5 ppm','5 ppm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141820,'chemistry','Excessive release of CO₂ into the atmosphere\n results in [JEE (Main)-2019]','Global warming','CO₂ causes global warming.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Depletion of ozone','Polar vortex','Formation of smog','Global warming','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141821,'chemistry','The layer of atmosphere between 10 km to 50 km\n above the sea level is called as\n[JEE (Main)-2019]','Stratosphere','Between 10-50 km above sea level lies\n stratosphere.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Stratosphere','Mesosphere','Thermosphere','Troposphere','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141822,'chemistry','The regions of the atmosphere, where clouds form\n and where we live, respectively, are :\n[JEE (Main)-2019]','Troposphere and Troposphere','The lowest region of atmosphere in which human\n beings live is troposphere. It extends up to a height\n of 10 km from sea level. Clouds are also formed in\n this layer.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Troposphere and Troposphere','Stratosphere and Troposphere','Troposphere and Stratosphere','Stratosphere and Stratosphere','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141823,'chemistry','Air pollution that occurs in sunlight is\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','Oxidising smog','Air pollution caused by sunlight is photochemical\n smog and it is oxidising.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Fog','Oxidising smog','Acid rain','Reducing smog','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141824,'chemistry','The correct set of species responsible for the\n photochemical smog is : [JEE (Main)-2019]','NO, NO₂, O₃ and hydrocarbons','Photochemical smog contains oxides of nitrogen,\n ozone and hydrocarbons.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','CO₂, NO₂, SO₂ and hydrocarbons','N₂, O₂, O₃ and hydrocarbons','NO, NO₂, O₃ and hydrocarbons','N₂, NO₂ and hydrocarbons','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141825,'chemistry','The primary pollutant that leads to photochemical\n smog is [JEE (Main)-2019]','Nitrogen oxides','In photochemical smog :\nPrimary pollutants → NO₂, hydrocarbons\nSecondary pollutants → Ozone, acrolein','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Nitrogen oxides','Sulphur dioxide','Ozone','Acrolein','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141826,'chemistry','The pH of rain water, is approximately\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','5.6','pH of rain water is around 5.6.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','7','7.5','5.6','6.5','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141827,'chemistry','A water sample has ppm level concentration of the\n following metals: Fe = 0.2 ; Mn = 5.0 ; Cu = 3.0 ;\nZn = 5.0. The metal that makes the water sample\n unsuitable for drinking is: [JEE (Main)-2019]','Mn','Prescribed level of Mn is 0.5 ppm. So water sample\n containing Mn = 5ppm is water unsuitable for\n drinking.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','Cu','Mn','Zn','Fe','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141901,'chemistry','Which of the following gases is not a greenhouse gas?','CO','The gases which absorb solar energy near the earth’s surface and then radiate it back to the earth are called greenhouse gases.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> NCERT Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','CO','O₃','CH₄','H₂O','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2000031681,'physics','A uniform electric field ${E=3 \\times 10^{5} N C^{-1}}$ is acting along the positive Y -axis. The electric flux through a rectangle of area ${10 {~cm} \\times 30 {~cm}}$ whose plane is parallel to the ${{Z}-{X}}$ plane is','B','Electric flux is given by ${\\phi=\\vec{E} \\cdot \\vec{A}}$<br>${\\therefore \\phi=3 \\times 10^{5} \\times 300 \\times 10^{-4}}$<br>$ = 9 \\times {10^3}\\,\\,Vm$','Electric Flux through a plane surface','Electric charges & Fields','Medium',NULL,'${12 \\times 10^{3} {Vm}}$','${9 \\times 10^{3} {Vm}}$','${15 \\times 10^{3} {Vm}}$','${18 \\times 10^{3} {Vm}}$',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141902,'chemistry','Photochemical smog occurs in warm, dry and sunny climate. One of the following is not amongst the components of photochemical smog, identify it.','SO₂','The smog which is formed in the presence of sunlight is called photochemical smog. This occurs in the months of summer when NO₂ and hydrocarbons are present in large amounts in the atmosphere. Concentration of O₃, PAN, aldehydes and ketones increases up in the atmosphere. SO₂ is not responsible for photochemical smog','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> NCERT Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','NO₂','O₃','SO₂','Unsaturated hydrocarbon','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141903,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is not true about classical smog?','Its main components are produced by the action of sunlight on emissions of automobiles and factories.','Classical smog is initiated by a mixture of SO₂, particulates and high humidity in the atmosphere in cold conditions. A fog of H₂SO₄ droplets formed condenses on the particulates to form the smog. It is of reducing nature. The gases released by automobiles and factories are not responsible for classical smog.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> NCERT Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Its main components are produced by the action of sunlight on emissions of automobiles and factories.','Produced in cold and humid climate.','It contains compounds of reducing nature.','It contains smoke, fog and sulphur dioxide.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141904,'chemistry','Biochemical Oxygen Demand, (BOD) is a measure of organic material present in water. BOD value less than 5 ppm indicates a water sample to be','Rich in dissolved oxygen','The total amount of oxygen consumed by micro-organisms (bacteria) in decomposing organic matter present in certain volume of a sample of water is called Biochemical Oxygen Demand (BOD) of the water.\n\nWater is considered to be pure if it has BOD less than 5 ppm, whereas highly polluted water has BOD more than 17 ppm. Thus, the water having BOD less than 5 ppm is rich in dissolved oxygen.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> NCERT Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Rich in dissolved oxygen','Poor in dissolved oxygen.','Highly polluted','Not suitable for aquatic life.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141905,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is wrong?','Ozone is not responsible for greenhouse effect.','Ozone is also one of the greenhouse gases.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> NCERT Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Ozone is not responsible for greenhouse effect.','Ozone can oxidize sulphur dioxide present in the atmosphere to sulphur trioxide.','Ozone hole is thinning of ozone layer present in stratosphere.','Ozone is produced in upper stratosphere by the action of UV rays on oxygen.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141906,'chemistry','Sewage containing organic waste should not be disposed in water bodies because it causes major water pollution. Fishes in such a polluted water die because of','Decrease in the amount of dissolved oxygen in water.','Organic waste consumes oxygen and therefore, dissolved oxygen in water decreases and fish in such polluted water die','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> NCERT Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Large number of mosquitoes.','Increase in the amount of dissolved oxygen.','Decrease in the amount of dissolved oxygen in water.','Clogging of gills by mud.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141907,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about photochemical smog is wrong?','It has low concentration of oxidizing agent.','Photochemical smog is oxidizing in nature because it contains large concentration of oxidizing agents NO₂ and O₃.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> NCERT Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','It has high concentration of oxidizing agents','It has low concentration of oxidizing agent.','It can be controlled by controlling the release of NO₂, hydrocarbons, ozone etc.','Plantation of some plants like pinus helps in controlling photochemical','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141908,'chemistry','The gaseous envelope around the earth is known as atmosphere. The lowest layer of this is extended up to 10 km from sea level. This layer is','Troposphere','The atmosphere is divided into four major regions:\n(i) Troposphere (ii) Stratosphere  (iii) Mesosphere and (iv) Thermosphere Troposphere is the lowest region of the atmosphere.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> NCERT Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Stratosphere','Troposphere','Mesosphere','Hydrosphere','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141909,'chemistry','Dinitrogen and dioxygen are main constituents of air but these do not react with each other to form oxides of nitrogen because____________ .','The reaction is endothermic and requires very high temperature.','Major compounds of atmosphere are dinitrogen, dioxygen and water vapour.\nN₂ = 78.08%, O₂ = 20.95%\nBoth dinitrogen and dioxygen do not react with each other as nitrogen is an inactive gas. The triple bond in N₂ is very stable and its dissociation energy is very high. Both react with each other at very high temperature.\nN₂(g) + O₂(g) ---3000°C--->  2NO(g)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> NCERT Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','The reaction is endothermic and requires very high temperature.','The reaction can be initiated only in presence of a catalyst','Oxides of nitrogen are unstable','N₂ and O₂ are unreactive','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141910,'chemistry','The pollutants which come directly in the air from source are called primary pollutants. Primary’pollutants are sometimes converted into secondary pollutants. Which of the following belongs to secondary air pollutants?','Peroxyacetyl nitrate','Primary pollutants are directly emitted from fossil fuels, and also factories, These includes oxides of sulphur, carbon, methane. When the primary pollutants react with other components present in the atmosphere, secondary pollutants are formed. Smog, Peroxyacetyl nitrate are the examples of secondary air pollutants.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> NCERT Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','CO','Hydrocarbon','Peroxyacetyl nitrate','NO','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141911,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct?','Ozone hole is thinning of the ozone layer of the stratosphere at some places.','Ozone hole is thinning of the ozone layer of the stratosphere at some places.\n\nThe ozone hole is the depletion of the ozone layer. It is thinning of the ozone layer of the stratosphere at some places.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> NCERT Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Ozone hole means vanishing the ozone layer around the earth completely.','Ozone hole is a hole formed in the stratosphere from which ozone oozes out.','Ozone hole is thinning of the ozone layer of the stratosphere at some places.','Ozone hole is a hole formed in the troposphere from which ozone oozes out.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141912,'chemistry','Which of the following practices will not come under green chemistry?','Using plastic cans for neatly storing substances.','Plastic is non-biodegradable polymer. Hence, it does not come under green chemistry. Green chemistry includes processes which lead to minimum pollution and less harm to the environment','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> NCERT Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','If possible, making use of soap made of vegetable oils instead of using synthetic detergents.','Using H₂O₂ for bleaching purpose instead of using chlorine based bleaching agents','Using bicycle for traveling small distances instead of using petrol/diesel based vehicles','Using plastic cans for neatly storing substances.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141913,'chemistry','Which of the following conditions shows the polluted environment?\n(a) pH of rain water is 5.6.\n(b) Amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere is 0.03%.\n(c) Biochemical oxygen demand is 10 ppm.\n(d) Eutrophication','c,d','Polluted water may contain nutrients for the growth of algae, which covers the water surface and reduces the oxygen concentration in water. This leads to anaerobic condition, accumulation of obnoxious decay and animal death. This is process of eutrophication.\n\nThe amount of oxygen required by bacteria to break down the organic matter present in a certain volume of sample of water is called Biochemical Oxygen Demand. Clean water would have BOD value of 5 ppm whereas highly polluted could have BOD value of 17 ppm or more.\n\nNormally rain water has pH of 6 due to H+ ion formed by reaction of rain water with carbon dioxide in the atmosphere. When the pH of the rain water drops below 5.6, it is called acid rain.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> NCERT Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','c,d','a,b','a,c','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141914,'chemistry','Phosphate containing fertilizers cause water pollution. Addition of such compounds in water bodies causes\n(a) enhanced growth of algae\n(b) decrease in amount of dissolved oxygen in water\n(c) deposition of calcium phosphate\n(d) increase in fish population','a,b','Fertilizers containing phosphate present in water help in the excessive growth of aquatic plants and algae. Micro-organisms which decompose these plants consume oxygen. As a result, the amount of dissolved oxygen in the water decreases.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> NCERT Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','c,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141915,'chemistry','The acids present in acid rain are________ .\n(a) Peroxyacetylnitrate                        \n(b) H₂CO₃\n(c) HNO₃                                                  \n(d) H₂SO₄','b,c,d','CO₂ is slightly soluble in water forming carbonic acid.\n CO₂ + H₂O → H₂CO₃\nThe oxides of nitrogen undergo oxidation reaction followed by reaction with water vapours to form nitric acid.\n\n2NO + 0₂→ 2N0₂\n2NO₂ + H₂O → HNO₃  + HNO₂\n\nThe oxidation of S0₂ into S0₃ occurs in the presence of dust particles or metal ions. The S0₃ then reacts with water vapours to form H₂SO₄.\n\n2S0₂ + 0₂ →[ Dust particles or Metal ions] 2S0₃\n\nS0₃ + H₂O → H₂SO₄','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> NCERT Exemplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b,c','b,c,d','a,c,d','only b','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141916,'chemistry','The consequences of global warming may be .\n(a) increase in average temperature of the earth\n(b) melting of Himalayan Glaciers –\n(c) increased biochemical oxygen demand\n(d) eutrophication','a,b','The rate at which solar radiation is reaching the earth is constant but the amount of CO₂ in the air is increasing. Consequently, the heat radiated back to the earth will increase and the temperature of the earth surface will also increase.\nThis increase in temperature will disturb the thermal balance on the earth and could cause glacier  and ice caps to melt.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> NCERT Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','a,d','c,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(142001,'chemistry','A : Global warming is due to Greenhouse Effect.\nR : Greenhouse gas is CH₄.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','Assertion is true greenhouse effect causes global warming. The reason is also true, CH₄ is a greenhouse gas\n but it is not the explanation of assertion.\n\nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11 : Page no 400, 5th paragraph','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(142002,'chemistry','A : Ozone depletion potential is maximum in CFC\'s.\nR : CFC looses active chlorine.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','CFCs causes depletion of ozone layer by releasing chlorine free radical which reacts with ozone and CFCs\n are most active in depleting ozone layer. Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct\n explanation.\n\nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11 : Page no 400,5th paragraph','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(142003,'chemistry','A : Photochemical smog is also known as oxidising smog.\nR : Photochemical smog is chiefly composed of O₃ , PAN. etc.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Photochemical smog is an oxidizing smog because it contains ozone (O₃) and NO₂ which are oxidizing agents.\n\nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11 : Page no 403 , 8th paragraph','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(142004,'chemistry','A : Value of COD is greater than BOD.\nR : Used oxygen is demanded by some chemicals for oxidation of inorganic and organic pollutants.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','BOD contains only oxygen used for biodegradable pollutants whereas COD includes both biodegradable and\n non-biodegradable pollutants. hence, both assetion and reason are true.\n\nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11 : Page no 407, 3rd paragraph.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(142005,'chemistry','A : Productivity of eutrophic lake is maximum.\nR : Eutrophication is mainly due to addition of SO₄\n²⁻ and heavy metals.','A is true but R is false','Productivity of eutrophic lake is maximum due to the presence of excess of nutrients but eutrophication is not\n mainly due to the SO₄\n²⁻ ion and heavy metals. Eutrophication is mainly due to the fertilizer which run off into\n lakes, stream, rivers.\n\nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11 : Page no 406, 5th paragraph.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(142006,'chemistry','A : Ozone is destroyed by solar radiation in the upper stratosphere.\nR : Thinning of the ozone layer allows excessive UV radiations to reach the surface of the earth.','A is false but reason is true','Ozone is produced in the stratosphere as UV rays(solar radiations) act on  O₂ molecules. But, the major reason for ozone depletion is 𝐶𝐹𝐶𝑠 (chlorofluorocarbons). The 𝐶𝐹𝐶𝑠 in the upper stratosphere react with ozone and break the molecule which results in ozone depletion.\nThe ozone layer protects us from the harmful UV radiationthat comes from the sun. Due to its depletion, the amount of UV radiation reaching the earth increases. UV rays filter into the troposphere and cause skin cancer, cataract, sunburn, etc. It also damages fish productivity.\n\nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11 :Page no 400,5th paragraph','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(142007,'chemistry','A : Excessive use of chlorinated synthetic pesticides causes soil and water pollution.\nR : Such pesticides are non- biodegradable.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is the correct explanation of assertion. Insecticides, pesticides and herbicides cause soil and water pollution. They are non-biodegradable\n\nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11 : Page no 408, 8th paragraph.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(142008,'chemistry','A : If BOD level of water in reservoir is less than 5 ppm it is not polluted.\nR : High biological oxygen demand means the low activity of bacteria in water.','Both A & R are false','If the BOD level of water in the reservoir is less then 5 ppm it is not polluted.Reason: High biological oxygen demand means a low activity of bacteria in water which is incorrect.\nBOD indicates the amount of oxygen required by bacteria to break down organic matter present in a certain volume of a sample of water. Therefore, a low BOD is an indication of good quality water, while a high BOD indicates polluted water. Dissolved oxygen (DO) is consumed by bacteria when large amounts of organic matter from sewage or other discharges are present in the water.\n\nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11 :NCERT class 11 : Page no 407, 3rd paragraph.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(142009,'chemistry','A : Greenhouse effect was observed in houses used to grow plants and these are made of green glass.\nR : Greenhouse name has been given because glasshouses are made of green glass.','A is true but R is false','In cold places, sunlight required to grow plants which are less. Hence, plants are kept in a glass house so that sunlight enters in the green house to heat up the soil and plants. The warm soil and plants emit infrared radiations which are partially absorbed and partially reflected by the glass. This phenomenon of absorption is called greenhouse effect.\n\nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11 : Page no 400, 5th paragraph.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(142010,'chemistry','A : The pH of acid rain is less than 5.6.\nR : Carbon dioxide present in the atmosphere dissolves in rainwater and forms carbonic acid.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','Rain water has a pH of 5.6 due to the presence of H+ ions formed by the reaction of rain water with carbon dioxide present in the atmosphere. When the pH of the rain water drops below 5.6, it is called acid rain. Hence, the given assertion is the correct statement.The reaction is involved in the formation of carbonic acid.Hence, from the above analysis it is concluded that  both A and R are correct but R is not the correct explanation of A.\n\nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11 : Page no 401, 3rd paragraph.','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(142011,'chemistry','A : Carbon dioxide is one of the important greenhouse gases.\nR : It is largely produced by the respiratory function of animals and plants.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','The increased amount of Carbon dioxide in the air is mainly responsible for global warming. So, it is a major green house gas. It is mainly produced by respiratory function of animals and plants (in dark, in absence of photosynthesis, plants also produce carbon dioxide).\n\nREFERENCE: NCERT class 11 : Page no 400,3rd paragraph','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but reason is true','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160430,'chemistry','H₂S, a toxic gas with rotten egg like smell, is used for the qualitative analysis. if the solubility of H₂S in water at STP is 0.195 m, calculate henry’s law constant.','282','Solubility of H₂S in water is given to be 0.195 m, \nMoles of water = 1000/18 = 55.55 mol\nSo x(H₂S) = 0.195 /0.195+55.55 = 0.0035 \nKₕ = p(H₂S)/x(H₂S) = 0.987/0.0035 = 282 bar. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-41 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'2','mcq','282','280','287','272','NEW','12-041','12-007',NULL),
(160431,'chemistry','Henry’s law constant for CO2 in water is 1.67x10⁸ Pa at 298 K. calculate the quantity of CO2 in 500 ml of soda water when packed under 2.5 atm CO2 pressure at 298 K.','1.848','x(H₂O) = p/Kₕ = 2.53 x 10⁵/1.67x10⁸  = 1.52 x 10⁻³, Taking density of soda water = water, mass of water = 1 x 500 = 500gm, moles of water = 500/18 = 27.78, moles of CO₂ = mole fraction( H₂O) x moles of H₂O = 0.042mol, mass of CO₂ = 1.848gm. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-41 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'2','mcq','1.848','1.52','2.53','1.454','NEW','12-041','12-007',NULL),
(160432,'chemistry','The partial pressure of ethane over a solution containing 6.56 × 10⁻³ g of ethane is 1 bar. if the solution contains 5.00 × 10⁻² g of ethane, then what shall be the partial pressure of the gas?','7.62','Using Henry\'s Law, 6.56 x 10⁻³ = k x 1\np = m/k => 5.00 × 10⁻²/6.56 × 10⁻³ g = 7.62 bar. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-41 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'2','mcq','6.56','7.62','6.45','6.33','NEW','12-041','12-007',NULL),
(160433,'chemistry','Henry’s law constant for the molality of methane in benzene at 298 K is 4.27 × 10⁵ mm Hg. calculate the solubility of methane in benzene at 298 K under 760 mm Hg','178 x 10⁻⁵','p = K x C => C= 760/4.27 x 10⁵ =178 x 10⁻⁵ = solubility of methane in benzene. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-41 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'2','mcq','167 x 10⁻⁵','187 x 10 ⁻⁵','178 x 10⁻⁵','176 x 10⁻⁵','NEW','12-041','12-007',NULL),
(160434,'chemistry','If N₂ gas is bubbled through water at 293 K, how many millimoles of N₂ gas would dissolve in 1 litre of water?Assume that N₂ exerts a partial pressure of 0.987 bar. given that Henry’s law constant for N₂ at 293 K is 76.48 kbar','0.716','x (Nitrogen) = p (Nitrogen)/K = 0.987/76480 = 1.29 x 10⁻⁵, As 1 litre of water contains 55.5 mol of it, therefore if n represents. number of moles of N₂ in solution, x (Nitrogen) = 1.29 x 10⁻⁵ = n/55.5, n = 0.716mmol.\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-41 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'2','mcq','0.726','0.555','0.786','0.716','NEW','12-041','12-007',NULL),
(160613,'chemistry','________  are binary mixtures having the same composition in liquid and vapour phase and boil at a constant temperature','Azeotropes','Some liquids on mixing, form azeotropes which are binary mixtures having the same composition in liquid and vapour phase and boil at a constant temperature. In such cases, it is not possible to separate the components by fractional distillation. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-48 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','Azeotropes','sol','fog','Smog','NEW','12-048','12-014',NULL),
(160614,'chemistry','_________ solution is not possible to separate the components by fractional distillation','Azeotropes','Some liquids on mixing, form azeotropes which are binary mixtures having the same composition in liquid and vapour phase and boil at a constant temperature. In such cases, it is not possible to separate the components by fractional distillation. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-48 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','Azeotropes','sol','fog','Smog','NEW','12-048','12-014',NULL),
(160615,'chemistry','Azeotrope are the solutions which show a large positive deviation from raoult’s law form _______ boiling Point','Minimum','The solutions which show a large positive deviation from Raoult’s law form Minimum boiling are azeotrope. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-48 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','Minimum','Maximum','high','in respect on these','NEW','12-048','12-014',NULL),
(160616,'chemistry','Azeotrope are the solutions that show large negative deviation from raoult’s law form ______ boiling  at a specific composition','Maximum','The solutions that show large negative deviation from Raoult’s law form Maximum boiling azeotrope at a specific composition. Nitric acid and water is an example of this class of azeotrope. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-49 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','Minimum','Maximum','High','irrespective of these','NEW','12-049','12-015',NULL),
(160617,'chemistry','Which one of the following is not correct for an ideal solution?','change volume of mixing is not 0','The enthalpy & volume of mixing of the pure components to form the solution is zero. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','it must obey Raoult’s law','change enthalpy of mixing = change volume of mixing','change volume of mixing = 0','change volume of mixing is not 0','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160618,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is/are true for an ideal solution?','No heat is absorbed or evolved when the components are mixed','The solutions which obey Raoult’s law over the entire range of concentration are known as ideal solutions. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','Intermolecular attraction forces between A—A and B—B types are not  equal to A—B type','Volume of solution is not equal to sum of volume of two component','No heat is absorbed or evolved when the components are mixed','Do not follow Raoult\'s law','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160619,'chemistry','Solution of bromoethane and chloroethane','Obeys Raoult’s law over the entire range of concentration','A perfectly ideal solution is rare but some solutions are nearly ideal in behaviour. Solution of n-hexane and n-heptane, bromoethane and chloroethane, benzene and toluene, etc. fall into this category. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 5)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','Obeys Raoult’s law over the entire range of concentration','is a non-ideal solution','change volume of mixing is not 0','Do not follow Raoult\'s law','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160620,'chemistry','True statement about non-ideal solutions is','A—A or B—B type interactions >A—B type interactions','When a solution does not obey Raoult’s law over the entire range of concentration, then it is called non-ideal solution. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 6)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','do obey Raoult’s law over entire range of concentration','A—A or B—B type interactions >A—B type interactions','A—A or B—B type interactions = A —B type interactions','No heat is absorbed or evolved when the components are mixed','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160621,'chemistry','What does the following represented by diagram(n1604)','Positive deviation from Raoult’s law','In case of positive deviation from Raoult’s law, A-B interactions are weaker than those between A-A or B-B. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 7)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'3','mcq','ideal solution','Positive deviation from Raoult’s law','Negative deviation from Raoult’s law','No deviation','NEW','12-047','12-013','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/160621q.png'),
(160622,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is/are true for the diagram(n1605)','Vapour pressure of the solution decreases','In case of negative deviations from Raoult’s law, the intermolecular attractive forces between A-A and B-B are weaker than those between A-B. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-48 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'3','mcq','Vapour pressure of the solution decreases','The escaping tendency of molecule increases for each component','Solution shows positive deviation from Raoult’s law','Solution shows no deviation from Raoult’s law','NEW','12-048','12-014','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/160622q.png'),
(160623,'chemistry','The solution formed by adding carbon disulphide to acetone, shows positive deviation from raoult’s law. it is because','The dipolar interactions between solute-solvent molecules are weaker than the respective interactions among the solute-solute and solvent-solvent molecules','In case of positive deviation from Raoult’s law, A-B interactions are weaker than those between A-A or B-B. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 7)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','The dipolar interactions between solute-solvent molecules are stronger than the respective interactions among the solute-solute and solvent-solvent molecules','All the dipolar interactions among solute-solvent, solute-solute and solvent-solvent are same','The dipolar interactions between solute-solvent molecules are weaker than the respective interactions among the solute-solute and solvent-solvent molecules','Only solute-solvent interactions are involved in the reaction','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160624,'chemistry','Arrange the following in the increasing order of their solubility in n-Octane based on solute-solvent interactions.','KCl < CH₃OH < CH₃CN < cyclohexane','In case of negative deviations from Raoult’s law, the intermolecular attractive forces between A-A and B-B are weaker than those between A-B. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-48 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'3','mcq','KCl < CH₃CN < CH₃OH < cyclohexane','KCl < cyclohexane < CH₃CN< CH₃CN','KCl < cyclohexane < CH₃OH < CH₃CN','KCl < CH₃OH < CH₃CN < cyclohexane','NEW','12-048','12-014',NULL),
(160625,'chemistry','The solution which show large positive deviation from raoult’s law form','Minimum boiling azeotrope at a specific composition','The solutions which show a large positive deviation from Raoult’s law form Minimum boiling azeotrope. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-48 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','Maximum boiling azeotrope at a specific composition','Maximum freezing azeotrope at a specific composition','Minimum boiling azeotrope at a specific composition','Minimum freezing azeotrope at a specific composition','NEW','12-048','12-014',NULL),
(160626,'chemistry','Solution of azeotropic nitric acid mixture contain','68% HNO₃ ,32% H₂O by mass','The solutions that show large negative deviation from Raoult’s law form Maximum boiling azeotrope at a specific composition. Nitric acid and water is an example of this class of azeotrope. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-49 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','2% HNO₃ ,68% H₂O by mass','68% HNO₃ ,32% H₂O by mass','50% HNO₃ ,50% H₂O by mass','30% H₂O, 70% HNO₃ by mass','NEW','12-049','12-015',NULL),
(160627,'chemistry','The solution that forms Maximum boiling azeotrope is','acetone - chloroform','The solutions that show large negative deviation from Raoult’s law form Maximum boiling azeotrope at a specific composition. Nitric acid and water is an example of this class of azeotrope. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-49 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','benzene - toluene','carbon disulphide - acetone','acetone - chloroform','n-hexane - n-heptane','NEW','12-049','12-015',NULL),
(160628,'chemistry','Which of the following azeotropic solutions has the boiling point less than the boiling point of its constituents molecules?','CH₃CH₂OHand CH₃COCH₃','The solutions which show a large positive deviation from Raoult’s law form Minimum boiling azeotrope. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-48 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','CHCl₃ and CH₃COCH₃','CS₂ and CH₃COCH₃','CH₃CH₂OHand CH₃COCH₃','CH₃CHOandCS₂','NEW','12-048','12-014',NULL),
(160629,'chemistry','Among the following, the azeotropic mixture is','CCl₄ + CHCl₃','Some liquids on mixing, form azeotropes which are binary mixtures having the same composition in liquid and vapour phase and boil at a constant temperature. In such cases, it is not possible to separate the components by fractional distillation. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-48 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','CCl₄ + CHCl₃','C₆H₁₄ + C₇H₁₆','C₂H₅Br + C₂H₅Cl','chlorobenzene + bromobenzene','NEW','12-048','12-014',NULL),
(160630,'chemistry','An aqueous solution of 2% non-volatile solute exerts a pressure of 1.004 bar at the normal boiling point of the solvent. what is the molar mass of the solute?','41.35','Given that of aq. solution. This means 2 g of non-volatile solute in 98 g of H₂O, Also the vapour of water at normal boiling point = 1.013 bar, Using Raoult\'s law : M₂ = 2 x 18 x 1.013/98 x 0.009 = 41.35g/mol.\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 5)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','53.41','31.45','41.35','35.14','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160631,'chemistry','Benzene and toluene form ideal solution over the entire range of composition. the vapour pressure of pure benzene and toluene at 300 K are 50.71 mm Hg and 32.06 mm Hg respectively. calculate the mole fraction of benzene in vapour phase if 80 g of benzene is mixed with 100 g of toluene.','0.6','No. of moles of benzene = 80/78 = 1.026 mol, the no. of moles of toluene = 100/92 = 1.087, mole fraction of benzene = 1.026 /1.026 + 1.087 = 0.486, mole fraction of toluene = 1-0.486 = 0.514, Pₜₒₜₐₗ = 50.71 x 0.486 + 32.06 x 0.514 = 41.13 mm Hg => Hence mole fraction of benzene in vapour phase is given by = 24.65/41.13 = 0.60. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 5)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.6','0.514','0.486','1.026','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160714,'chemistry','The correct statement about semipermeable membrane is','It appears as a continuous sheet or film but actually it is porous','These membranes can be of animal or vegetable origin and these occur naturally such as pig’s bladder or parchment or can be synthetic such as cellophane. These membranes appear to be continuous sheets or films, yet they contain a network of submicroscopic holes or pores. Small solvent molecules, like water, can pass through these holes but the passage of bigger molecules like solute is hindered. Membranes having this kind of properties are known as semipermeable membranes (SPM). \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-54 Paragraph - last)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'2','mcq','It appears as a continuous sheet or film but actually it is porous','Only small solute molecules can pass through it','it is always natural, i.e. of animal or vegetable origin','it is always natural','NEW','12-054','12-020',NULL),
(160715,'chemistry','On the basis of the information given above select the correct option. \n\ni. semipermeable membrane contains network of submicroscopic holes or pores. \n\nii. semipermeable membrane appears to be continuous sheets or films. \n\niii. solvent molecules cannot pass through the holes of semipermeable membrane but solute molecules can pass.','I & II only','These membranes can be of animal or vegetable origin and these occur naturally such as pig’s bladder or parchment or can be synthetic such as cellophane. These membranes appear to be continuous sheets or films, yet they contain a network of submicroscopic holes or pores. Small solvent molecules, like water, can pass through these holes but the passage of bigger molecules like solute is hindered. Membranes having this kind of properties are known as semipermeable membranes (SPM). \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-54 Paragraph - last)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Only II','I & II only','Only III','i,ii and iii','NEW','12-054','12-020',NULL),
(160716,'chemistry','Osmosis is the process of movement of solvent particles','From the pure solvent to the solution','Assume that only solvent molecules can pass through these semipermeable membranes. If this membrane is placed between the solvent and solution, the solvent molecules will flow through the membrane from pure solvent to the solution. This process of flow of the solvent is called osmosis. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-54 Paragraph - 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'2','mcq','From the solution of higher concentration to the solution of lower concentration','Flow  of solute respect of concentration','From the pure solvent to the solution','From the solution to the pure solvent','NEW','12-054','12-020',NULL),
(160717,'chemistry','Select the correct statement.','Osmotic pressure is related to molar mass of the solute','The Osmotic pressure of a solution is the excess pressure that must be applied to a solution to prevent osmosis, i.e., to stop the passage of solvent molecules through a semipermeable membrane into the solution Osmotic pressure is a colligative property as it depends on the number of solute molecules and not on their identity. For dilute solutions, it has been found experimentally that Osmotic pressure is proportional to the molarity, C of the solution at a given temperature T.  \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-55 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Osmotic pressure is not the excess pressure applied on solution side to stop osmosis','Osmotic pressure is inversely proportional to the molarity of solution at a given temperature','Osmotic pressure is related to molar mass of the solute','Osmotic pressure is related to molar mass of the solvent','NEW','12-055','12-021',NULL),
(160718,'chemistry','At a given temperature, Osmotic pressure of the concentrated solution of a substance','Is higher than that of the dilute solution','K oc C, so more the concentration, higher is the Osmotic pressure. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-55 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Is higher than that of the dilute solution','is lower than that of the dilute solution','same as that of the dilute solution','cannot be compared with Osmotic pressure of dilute solution','NEW','12-055','12-021',NULL),
(160719,'chemistry','0.1 m NaCl and 0.005 m BaCl₂ solutions are separated by a semipermeable membrane in a container. for this system, choose the correct answer.','Water flows from BaCl₂ solution to NaCl solution','The Osmotic pressure of a solution is the excess pressure that must be applied to a solution to prevent osmosis, i.e., to stop the passage of solvent molecules through a semipermeable membrane into the solution Osmotic pressure is a colligative property as it depends on the number of solute molecules and not on their identity. For dilute solutions, it has been found experimentally that Osmotic pressure is proportional to the molarity, C of the solution at a given temperature T.  \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-55 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','There is no movement of any solution across the membrane','Water flows from BaCl₂ solution to NaCl solution','Water flows from NaCl solution to BaCl₂ solution','Osmotic pressure of 0.1 M NaCl is lower than that of BaCl₂ (assume complete dissociation)','NEW','12-055','12-021',NULL),
(160720,'chemistry','Osmotic pressure method is used to determine molar mass of protein, macromolecules like polymers, biomolecules etc., because','Osmotic pressure is measured at room temperature','Measurement of Osmotic pressure provides another method of determining molar masses of solutes. This method is widely used to determine molar masses of proteins, polymers and other macromolecules. The Osmotic pressure method has the advantage over other methods as pressure measurement is around the room temperature and the molarity of the solution is used instead of molality.  \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-55 Paragraph - last)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Osmotic pressure is measured at room temperature','Osmotic pressure depends upon molality','these are very big molecules and have poor solubility','Osmotic pressure can be measured at any temperature','NEW','12-055','12-021',NULL),
(160721,'chemistry','Osmotic pressure present in the fluid inside the blood cell is equivalent to','0.9% (m/V) NaCl solution','If we place the cells in a solution containing more than 0.9% (mass/volume) sodium chloride, water will flow out of the cells and they would shrink. Such a solution is called hypertonic. If the salt concentration is less than 0.9% (mass/volume), the solution is said to be hypotonic. In this case, water will flow into the cells if placed in this solution and they would swell. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-56 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'2','mcq','0.9% (m/V) NaCl solution','less than 0.9 % (m/V) NaCl solution','more than 0.9 % (tn/V) NaCl solution','0.9% (m/V) Na₂SO₄ solution','NEW','12-056','12-022',NULL),
(160722,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true about Osmotic pressure? \n\ni. flow of solvent from solution across a semipermeable membrane can be stopped, if some extra pressure is applied on solution. \n\nii. it is the pressure that stops the flow of solvent towards solution. \n\niii. it is the pressure that allow flows of solvent across the semipermeable membrane.','Both I and II','The Osmotic pressure of a solution is the excess pressure that must be applied to a solution to prevent osmosis, i.e., to stop the passage of solvent molecules through a semipermeable membrane into the solution Osmotic pressure is a colligative property as it depends on the number of solute molecules and not on their identity. For dilute solutions, it has been found experimentally that Osmotic pressure is proportional to the molarity, C of the solution at a given temperature T.  \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-55 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Both I and II','Both II and III','Both I and III','I, II and III','NEW','12-055','12-021',NULL),
(160723,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following \n\nAssertion (A) when a solution is separated from the pure solvent by a semipermeable membrane, the solvent molecules pass through it from pure solvent side to the solution side. \n\nReason (R) diffusion of solvent occurs from a region of high concentration solution to a region of low concentration solution. \n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:"','Both A and R are correct, R is not the correct explanation of A','The Osmotic pressure of a solution is the excess pressure that must be applied to a solution to prevent osmosis, i.e., to stop the passage of solvent molecules through a semipermeable membrane into the solution Osmotic pressure is a colligative property as it depends on the number of solute molecules and not on their identity. For dilute solutions, it has been found experimentally that Osmotic pressure is proportional to the molarity, C of the solution at a given temperature T.  \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-55 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are correct, R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are correct, R is not the correct explanation of A','A is correct, R is incorrect','A is incorrect, R is correct.','NEW','12-055','12-021',NULL),
(160724,'chemistry','A 6% solution of urea is isotonic with','1 M solution of glucose','Thus, 18 g of glucose is present in 100 mL of solution. In other words, 1 M solution of glucose (18 g in 100 mL) is isotonic with 6% solution of urea.  \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-55 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'2','mcq','1 M solution of BCl₃','1 M solution of H2CO3','1 M solution of NaCl','1 M solution of glucose','NEW','12-055','12-021',NULL),
(160725,'chemistry','Solutions A, B, C and d are respectively 0.1M Glucose, 0.05 M NaCl, 0.05 M BaCl₂ and 0.1 M AlCl₃ . which one of the following pairs is isotonic?','A and B','Isotonic solutions have same molar concentration of solute particles in solution. Molar concentration of solute particles in solution are 0.1 M in glucose, 2x 0.05 M in NaCl, 3 x 0.05 in BaCl₂ and 4 x 0.1 in AlCl₃ . Therefore, 0.1 M glucose and 0.05 M NaCl solutions are isotonic. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-56 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'2','mcq','C and B','A and B','A and C','A and D','NEW','12-056','12-022',NULL),
(160726,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following \n\nAssertion (A) people taking a lot of salty food experience the puffiness or swelling, called edema.\n\nReason (R) there is water retention in tissue cells and intercellular spaces because of osmosis \n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:"','Both A and R are correct, R is the correct explanation of A.','When placed in water containing less than 0.9% (mass/ volume) salt, blood cells swell due to flow of water in them by osmosis. People taking a lot of salt or salty food experience water retention in tissue cells and intercellular spaces because of osmosis. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-56 Paragraph - last)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are correct, R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is correct, R is incorrect','A is incorrect, R is correct.','NEW','12-056','12-022',NULL),
(160727,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following \n\nAssertion (A) high blood pressure patients are advised to take the Minimum quantity of salt. \n\nReason (R) from salt 4Na  and Cl” ion concentration increases in the body fluid which may rupture the blood cells. \n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below','Both A and R are correct, R is the correct explanation of A.','When placed in water containing less than 0.9% (mass/ volume) salt, blood cells swell due to flow of water in them by osmosis. People taking a lot of salt or salty food experience water retention in tissue cells and intercellular spaces because of osmosis. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-56 Paragraph - last)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are correct, R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is correct, R is incorrect','A is incorrect, R is correct.','NEW','12-056','12-022',NULL),
(160728,'chemistry','Reverse osmosis is a process','in which solvent moves from solution of higher concentration to solution of lower concentration','The direction of osmosis can be reversed if a pressure larger than the Osmotic pressure is applied to the solution side. That is, now the pure solvent flows out of the solution through the semipermeable membrane. This phenomenon is called reverse osmosis. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-57 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'2','mcq','in which applied pressure to the solution side, is smaller than the Osmotic pressure','in which solvent moves from solution of lower concentration to solution of lower concentration','which is not used for desalination of seawater','in which solvent moves from solution of higher concentration to solution of lower concentration','NEW','12-057','12-023',NULL),
(160729,'chemistry','Boiling point of water at 750 mm Hg is 99.63°C. how much sucrose is to be added to 500 g of water such that it boils at 100°C?','121.67','DTᵦ = Kᵦ x 1000 x w₂/M₂ x w₁ => w₂ = 0.37 x 342 x 500/0.52 x 1000 = 121.67g.  Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-53 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'2','mcq','120','121.67','122.67','126','NEW','12-053','12-019',NULL),
(160730,'chemistry','Calculate the mass of ascorbic acid (vitamin C, C₆H₈O₆ ) to be dissolved in 75 g of acetic acid to lower its melting point by 1.5°C. k𝒻 = 3.9 k kg/mol .','5.08','DTᵦ = Kᵦ x 1000 x w₂/M₂ x w₁ => w₂ = 1.5 x 176  x 75/3.9 x 1000 = 5.08g.  Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-53 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'2','mcq','5.08','6','3','1.5','NEW','12-053','12-019',NULL),
(160731,'chemistry','Calculate the Osmotic pressure in pascals exerted by a solution prepared by dissolving 1.0 g of polymer of molar mass 185,000 in 450 ml of water at 37°C.','30.98','Osmotic pressure = pi = n/V RT => pi =8.314 x 10³ x 310 / 185000 x 0.45 = 30.98 Pa. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-55 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'1','mcq','28','29.98','30.98','32','NEW','12-055','12-021',NULL),
(160732,'chemistry','At 300 K, 36 g of glucose present in a litre of its solution has an Osmotic pressure of 4.98 bar. if the Osmotic pressure of the solution is 1.52 bars at the same temperature, what would be its concentration?','0.061','According to the question pi₁/C₁=pi₂/C₂ => C₂ = 1.52 x 36/4.98 x 180 = 0.061M. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-55 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'2','mcq','0.036','0.061','1.56','0.56','NEW','12-055','12-021',NULL),
(160733,'chemistry','18 g of glucose, C₆H₁₂O₆ , is dissolved in 1 kg of water in a saucepan. at what temperature will water boil at 1.013 bar? k for water is 0.52 k kg/mol .','373.202','DTᵦ = Kᵦ x m = 0.52 K kg/mol × 0.1 mol/kg = 0.052 K, Since water boils at 373.15 K at 1.013 bar pressure, Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-53 Paragraph - 4) Boiling point of solution will be 373.15 + 0.052 = 373.202 K','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'2','mcq','373.202','373.15','373','374','NEW','12-053','12-019',NULL),
(160734,'chemistry','The boiling point of benzene is 353.23 K. when 1.80 g of a non-volatile solute was dissolved in 90 g of benzene, the boiling point is raised to 354.11 K. calculate the molar mass of the solute. kb for benzene is 2.53 kg/mol','58','The boiling point of benzene is 353.23 K. when 1.80 g of a non-volatile solute was dissolved in 90 g of benzene, the boiling point is raised to 354.11 K. calculate the molar mass of the solute. kb for benzene is 2.53 K kg/mol','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'2','mcq','58','38','25.3','27.8','NEW','12-051','12-017',NULL),
(160735,'chemistry','1.00 g of a nonelectrolyte solute dissolved in 50 g of benzene lowered the freezing point of benzene by 0.40 K. the freezing point depression constant of benzene is 5.12 kg/mol. find the molar mass of the solute.','256','M₂ = 5.12 x 1 x 1000/0.40 x 50 = 256g/mol. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-53 Paragraph - 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'2','mcq','250','512','756','256','NEW','12-053','12-019',NULL),
(160736,'chemistry','200 cm³ of an aqueous solution of a protein contains 1.26 g of the protein. the Osmotic pressure of such a solution at 300 K is found to be 2.57 × 10⁻³ bar. calculate the molar mass of the protein.','61','The various quantities known to us are as follows: Π = 2.57 x 10⁻³, V = 200 cm³ = 0.200L, M₂ = 1.26 x 0.083 x 300/2.57 x 10⁻³ x 0.200 = 61.022g/mol. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-55 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'2','mcq','61','200','300','82','NEW','12-055','12-021',NULL),
(160737,'chemistry','Colligative properties are observed when (a) a non-volatile solid is dissolved in a volatile liquid (b) a non-volatile liquid is dissolved in an another volatile liquid(c) a gas is dissolved in a non-volatile liquid(d) a volatile liquid is dissolved in an another volatile liquid','a,b','Colligative Properties are observed when a non-volatile solid or liquid is dissolved in some other volatile liquid\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-49 )','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,b,f','NEW','12-049','12-015',NULL),
(160738,'chemistry','Aerosol are colloids which contain \n(a) A solid dispersed in a liquid \n(b) A solid dispersed in a gas \n(c) A liquid dispersed in a gas \n(d) A liquid in another liquid','Both b and c','A colloidal dispersion of solid or liquid particles in a gas is aerosol. For example, smoke or fog.\nA colloidal system in which the dispersed phase is composed of either solid or liquid particles and in which the dispersion medium is some gas, usually aerosol. For example, the spray of fine particles of a substance such as paint, asthma medication or insecticide that is released from an aerosol can.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Both b and c','Only b','All of them','a,b & d','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160739,'chemistry','9.9 g NaNO₃, dissolved in 940 g of water find molality?','0.124 m','Mass of water = 940 g Mass of NaNO₃ = 9.9g Formula Mass of NaNO₃, 23+ 14+ 48 = 85 g mole⁻¹ Using Molarity relation Molality = moles of solute/kg of solvent. moles of solute/ Molar mass of solute x 1000/ mass of solvent in gram =9.9/85 x 1000/940= 0.124 m','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','0.124 m','0.156 m','0.134 m','0.213 m','NEW','12-039','12-005',NULL),
(160740,'chemistry','What are the freezing point of a solution prepared by dissolving 13.3g of ethylene glycol in 100g of water.','F.P=-3.99°C','Mass of Solute (ethylene glycol) = W₂ = 13.3g Molar mass of Solute = 62 g mole⁻¹ Mass of solvent (water) W, 100g Molar mass of solvent (water H₂O) = 18g /mole Molal F. P constant, K, for water = 1.86°C /Mol. Freezing point of Solution = Delta T = Kf x W₂ x 1000 / M₂ x W₁= 1.86x 13.3 x 1000 / 62 x 100 = 3.99 °C Freezing point of pure water = 0°C Freezing point of solution = T₂ = T₁ - deltaT₁= =-3.99°C','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','F.P=-3.99°C','F.P=-2.99°C','F.P=-1.99°C','F.P=-4.99°C','NEW','12-053','12-019',NULL),
(160741,'chemistry','What are the boiling points of a solution prepared by dissolving 13.3g of ethylene glycol in 100g of water','B.P = 101.115°C','Mass of Solute (ethylene glycol) = W₂ = 13.3g Molar mass of Solute = 62 g mole⁻¹ Mass of solvent (water) W, 100g Molar mass of solvent (water H₂O) = 18g mole⁻¹  Mol. Elevation B. P constant. K, for water = 0.52°C Boiling point of Solution = delta T = Kb x W2 x 1000 / M2 x W1= 0.52 x 13.3 x 1000 / 62 x 100 = 1.115 °C Boiling point of pure water = 100 °C Boiling point of solution = T₂ = T1 + deltaT1 = =100 + 1.116 °C','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','B.P = 101.115°C','B.P = 103.115°C','B.P = 102.115°C','B.P = 104.115°C','NEW','12-051','12-017',NULL),
(160742,'chemistry','Calculate the amount of ice that will separate out on cooling a solution containing 50 g of ethylene glycol in 200 g water to –9.3°C. (Kf for water = 1.86 K kg mol–1).','38.71 g.','w = 50 g, ΔTf = 9.3°C Kf = 1.86 K kg mol⁻¹ m = 62 g .W =  Tf = Kf × w × 1000 /m × W =9.3 = 1.86 × 50 × d × 1000 / 62× 50=W = 161.29g Hence amount of ice separated from 200 g water = 200 – 161.29 = 38.71 g.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','38.71 g.','39.34 g','37.23 g','37.67 g','NEW','12-053','12-019',NULL),
(160743,'chemistry','Maximum freezing point will be for 1 molal solution, assuming equal ionization in each case:','[Fe(H₂O)₃Cl₃].3H₂O','As it will give least number of ions so it will have maximum boiling point.\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-52 )','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','[Fe(H₂O)₆] Cl₃','[Fe(H₂O)₅Cl] Cl₂.H₂O','[Fe(H₂O)₄Cl₂]Cl.2H₂O','[Fe(H₂O)₃Cl₃].3H₂O','NEW','12-052','12-018',NULL),
(160744,'chemistry','The azeotropic mixture of water and ethanol boils at 78.15°C. When this mixture is distilled, it is possible to obtain:','Neither H₂O nor C2H5OH in their pure state','The azeotropic mixture cannot be separated into individual components as both the components boil at the same temperature.\n(NCERT Class-12 Page-48 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Pure H₂O','Pure C2H5OH','Pure H₂O as well as pure C2H5OH','Neither H₂O nor C2H5OH in their pure state','NEW','12-048','12-014',NULL),
(160745,'chemistry','The temperature at which 10% aqueous solution of glucose will show the osmotic pressure of 14 atm is:','307.3 K','P =  nRT/V So, T = PV/Rn = 14 × 100 × 10⁻⁸ × 180 / 0.082 × 10 = 307.3 K','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','34 K','307.3°C','307.3 K','34°C','NEW','12-055','12-021',NULL),
(160746,'chemistry','For an aqueous solution, freezing point is – 0.186°C.Elevation of the boiling point of the same solution is (Kf = 1.86°  kg/mol and Kb = 0.512°  kg/mol )','0.0512°','ΔTb = mKb, ΔTf = mKf ΔTb=Kb/Kf  x ΔTf  =0.512/ 1.86 × 0.186= 0.0512°C','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','0.186°','0.0512°','0.196°','0.176°','NEW','12-052','12-018',NULL),
(160747,'chemistry','The depression in freezing point of 0.01 M aqueous solution of urea, sodium chloride and sodium sulphate is in the ratio:','0.04309027778','Number of ions formed Urea : NaCl : Na₂SO₄ =0.01 mole : 0.01 × 2 mole : 0.01 × 3 mole=1:2:3=Thus, ΔTf is also in the same ratio. (ΔTf α i, when molality is constant)\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-52 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','0.04237268519','0.04309027778','0.04310185185','0.08475694444','NEW','12-052','12-018',NULL),
(160748,'chemistry','Raoult\'s law was given by','French chemist, Francois Marie Raoult','Vapour Pressure of Liquid-Liquid Solutions\nReference : (NCERT Class 12 Page - 43 )','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','French chemist, Francois Marie Raoult','German chemist, Francois Marte Raoult','English chemist, Francois Marte Raoult','Russian chemist, Francois Marte Raoult','NEW','12-043','12-009',NULL),
(160749,'chemistry','When did French chemist, Francois Marte Raoult gave Raoult law','1886','Vapour Pressure of Liquid-Liquid Solutions,\nReference : (NCERT Class 12 Page - 43','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','1885','1886','1887','1888','NEW','12-043','12-009',NULL),
(160750,'chemistry','1 Bar is equal to how many atmosphere.','0.9871','As 1 bar = 1/1.013 atm','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','0.9871','0.9881','0.9891','0.9861','NEW','12-050','12-016',NULL),
(160751,'chemistry','Elevation constant unit is','K kg mol⁻¹','Elevation of  Boiling Point \nReference : (NCERT Class 12 Page - 52)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','K kg mol⁻¹','kg mol⁻¹','K kg mol','kg mol⁻¹','NEW','12-052','12-018',NULL),
(160914,'chemistry','Vapour pressure of chloroform (CHCl₃) and dichloromethane (CH₂Cl₂) at 25°C are 200 mm Hg and 41.5 mm Hg respectively. vapour pressure of the solution obtained by mixing 25.5 g of CHCl₃ and 40 g of CH₂Cl₂ at the same temperature will be (molecular mass of CHCl₃ = 119.5 u and molecular  mass of CH₂Cl₂ = 85 u)','347.9 mm Hg','Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 1) diagram(ne1609)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','173.9 mm Hg','250 mm Hg','347.9 mm Hg','185.12 mm Hg','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160915,'chemistry','A solution has a 1 : 4 mole ratio of pentane to hexane. the vapour pressures of the pure hydrocarbons at 20 °C are 440 mm Hg for pentane and 120 mm Hg for hexane. the mole fraction of pentane in the vapour phase would be','0.478','nC₅H₁₂/nC₆H₁₄ = 1/4 ⇒ x(C₅H₁₂) = 1/5, x(C₆H₁₄) = 4/5, p° = p°C₅H₁₂ = 440 mm Hg;  p°C₆H₁₄=120 mm Hg, Ptotal = p°C₅H₁₂ x x(C₅H₁₂) + p°C₆H₁₄ x x(C₆H₁)₄ = 88 +96 = 184 mm Hg, By Dalton’s law, pC₅H₁₂ =  X\'C₅H₁₂ x Ptotal,(x′ C₅H₁₂ mole fraction of pentane above the solution.) pC₅H₁₂ = 440 x 1/5 = 88, X\'(C₅H₁₂) = 88/184 = 0.478 . Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','0.549','0.786','0.108','0.478','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160916,'chemistry','The vapour pressure of two liquids p and q are 80 and 60 torr, respectively. the total vapour pressure of solution obtained by mixing 3 mole of p and 2 mol of q would be','72 torr','By Raoult’s Law, Pₜ = p°ₚ X°ₚ + pᵩ° Xᵩ = 80 x 3/5 + 60 x 2/5 = 72. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','72 torr','20 torr','66 torr','80 torr','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160917,'chemistry','The mixture which shows positive deviation from raoult’s law is','ethanol + acetone','Mixture of ethanol and acetone shows positive deviation from Raoult’s law. In pure ethanol, molecules are hydrogen bonded. On adding acetone, its molecules get in between the host molecules and break some of the hydrogen bonds between them. Due to weakening of interactions, the solution shows positive deviation from Raoult’s law. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-48 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','ethanol + acetone','benzene + toluene','acetone + chloroform','chloroethane + bromoethane.','NEW','12-048','12-014',NULL),
(160918,'chemistry','For an ideal solution, the correct option is','Dₘᵢₓ H = 0 at constant T and P','For an ideal solution, DiffmixH = 0 and DiffmixV = 0 at constant T and P. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','DₘᵢₓG = 0 at constant T and P','DₘᵢₓS = 0 at constant T and P','Dₘᵢₓ V ≠ 0 at constant T and P','Dₘᵢₓ H = 0 at constant T and P','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160919,'chemistry','The mixture that forms Maximum boiling azeotrope is','water + nitric acid','Maximum boiling azeotropes are formed by those solutions which show negative deviations from Raoult’s law. H₂O and HNO3 mixture shows negative deviations.  Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-49 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','heptane + Octane','water + nitric acid','ethanol + water','acetone + carbon disulphide.','NEW','12-049','12-015',NULL),
(160920,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct regarding a solution of two components A and B exhibiting positive deviation from ideal behaviour?','Intermolecular attractive forces between A-A and B-B are stronger than those between A-B','In case of positive deviation from Raoult’s law, A-B interactions are weaker than those between A-A or B-B. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 7)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','Intermolecular attractive forces between A-A and B-B are stronger than those between A-B','∆mix H = 0 at constant T and P.','∆mix V = 0 at constant T and P','Intermolecular attractive forces between A-A and B-B are equal to those between A-B','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160921,'chemistry','Which one of the following is incorrect for ideal solution?','DGₘᵢₓ = 0','The enthalpy & volume of mixing of the pure components to form the solution is zero. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','DHₘᵢₓ = 0','DUₘᵢₓ = 0','dP = Pₒᵦₛ – P𝒸ₐₗ by Raoult’s law = 0','DGₘᵢₓ = 0','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160922,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about the composition of the vapour over an ideal 1 : 1 molar mixture of benzene and toluene is correct? assume that the temperature is constant at 25°C. (given, vapour pressure data at 25°C, benzene = 12.8 KPa, toluene = 3.85 KPa','The vapour will contain a higher percentage of benzene','For an ideal 1 : 1 molar mixture of benzene and toluene, Benzene=1/2 & x Toluene =1/2, Benzene = 1/2p"Benzene = 1/2 x 12.8KPa = 6.4KPa, pToluene = 1/2 x 3.85KPa=1.925KPa. Thus, the vapour will contain a high percentage of  benzene as the partial vapour pressure of benzene is  higher as compared to that of toluene. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','association','equimolar','The vapour will contain a higher percentage of benzene','The vapour will contain a higher percentage of toluene','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160923,'chemistry','Which condition is not satisfied by an ideal solution?','DₘᵢₓS = 0','For an ideal solution : Entropy change (DₘᵢₓS) on mixing ≠ 0. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','DₘᵢₓV = 0','DₘᵢₓS = 0','Obeyance to Raoult’s Law','DₘᵢₓH = 0','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160924,'chemistry','A solution of acetone in ethanol','shows a positive deviation from Raoult’s law','In case of positive deviation from Raoult’s law, A-B interactions are weaker than those between A-A or B-B. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 7)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','obeys Raoult’s law','shows a negative deviation from Raoult’s law','shows a positive deviation from Raoult’s law','behaves like a near ideal solution','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160925,'chemistry','A solution containing components a and b follows raoult’s law','A - B attraction force remains same as A - A and B - B','Raoult’s law is valid for ideal solutions only. The element of non-ideality enters into the picture when the molecules of the solute and solvent affect each others intermolecular forces. A solution containing components of A and B behaves as ideal solution when A – B attraction force remains same as A – A and B – B attraction forces. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','A - B attraction force is greater than A - A and B - B','A - B attraction force is less than A - A and B - B','A - B attraction force remains same as A - A and B - B','volume of solution is different from sum of volume of solute and solvent.','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160926,'chemistry','All form ideal solution except','C₂H₅I and C₂H₅OH','Because C₂H₅I and C₂H₅OH are dissimilar liquids. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 6)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','C₆H₅Cl and C₆H₅Br','C₆H₆ and C₆H₅CH3','C₂H₆ and C₂H₅I','C₂H₅I and C₂H₅OH','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160927,'chemistry','An ideal solution is formed when its components','have both the above characteristics','The enthalpy & volume of mixing of the pure components to form the solution is zero. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','have no volume change on mixing','have no enthalpy change on mixing','have both the above characteristics','have high solubility.','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160928,'chemistry','The freezing point depression constant (k𝒻) of benzene is 5.12 Kkg mol⁻¹. the freezing point depression for the solution of molality 0.078 m containing a non-electrolyte solute in benzene is (rounded off upto two decimal places)','0.40 K','The freezing point depression constant (k𝒻) of benzene is 5.12 K kg mol⁻¹. the freezing point depression for the solution of molality 0.078 m containing a non-electrolyte solute in benzene is (rounded off upto two decimal places)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','0.20 K','0.40 K','0.60 K','0.80 K','NEW','12-052','12-018',NULL),
(160929,'chemistry','If molality of the dilute solution is doubled, the value of molal depression constant (Kf ) will be','unchanged','The value of molal depression constant, Kf is constant for a particular solvent, thus, it will be unchanged when molality of the dilute solution is doubled. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-52 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','halved','tripled','unchanged','doubled.','NEW','12-052','12-018',NULL),
(160930,'chemistry','At 100°C the vapour pressure of a solution of 6.5 g of a solute in 100 g water is 732 mm. if kᵦ = 0.52, the boiling point of this solution will be','101 °C','Given : Wᵦ = 6.5 g, Wₐ = 100 g, ps = 732 mm, Kᵦ = 0.52, T°ᵦ = 100°C, p° = 760 mm, p"-ps/p" = N₂/n1 => N₂ = 28 x 100/760 x 18 = 0.2046mol, T − 100°C = 0.52 x 0.2046 x 1000/100 = 1.06 => Tᵦ = 1.06+100 = 101C.  Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-53 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','100 °C','104 °C','101 °C','102 °C','NEW','12-053','12-019',NULL),
(160931,'chemistry','200 ml of an aqueous solution of a protein contains its 1.26 g. the Osmotic pressure of this solution at 300 K is found to be 2.57 × 10⁻³ bar. the molar mass of protein will be (r = 0.083 l bar/mol/k)','61038 g/mol','Here, pV = nRT, M = wRT/pi.V = 1.26 x 0.083 x 300/2.57 x 10⁻³ x 0.2 = 61038 g/mol. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-55 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','122044 g/mol','51022 g/mol','31011 g/mol','61038 g/mol','NEW','12-055','12-021',NULL),
(160932,'chemistry','A solution of sucrose (molar mass = 342 g mol⁻¹) has been prepared by dissolving 68.5 g of sucrose in 1000 g of water. the freezing point of the solution obtained will be (k𝒻 for water = 1.86 kg mol⁻¹)','– 0.372 °C','Here, DT𝒻 = K𝒻 m, m = wᵦ/Mᵦ x 1000/Wₐ = 68.5/342 = 0.2, T𝒻 = 0 – 0.372°C = – 0.372°C. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-53 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','– 0.372 °C','– 0.520 °C','0.372 °C','0.520 °C','NEW','12-053','12-019',NULL),
(160933,'chemistry','During osmosis, flow of water through a semipermeable membrane is','from both sides of semipermeable membrane with unequal flow rates.','Assume that only solvent molecules can pass through these semipermeable membranes. If this membrane is placed between the solvent and solution, the solvent molecules will flow through the membrane from pure solvent to the solution. This process of flow of the solvent is called osmosis. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-54 Paragraph - 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','from solution having lower concentration only','from solution having higher concentration only','from both sides of semipermeable membrane with equal flow rates','from both sides of semipermeable membrane with unequal flow rates.','NEW','12-054','12-020',NULL),
(160934,'chemistry','A solution of sucrose (molar mass = 342 g mol⁻¹) has been prepared by dissolving 68.5 g of sucrose in 1000 g of water. the freezing point of the solution obtained will be (k𝒻 for water = 1.86K kg mol⁻¹)','0.4 K','MB = 1000/WA x Kf x wB/DTf, DTf = 1000/51.2 x 5.12/250 x 1 =0.4K. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-53 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','0.3 K','0.4 K','0.5 K','0.6 K','NEW','12-053','12-019',NULL),
(160935,'chemistry','A solution containing 10 g per dm³ of urea (molecular mass = 60 g/mol) is isotonic with a 5% solution of a non-volatile solute. the molecular mass of this non-volatile solute is','300 g/mol','Molar concentration of urea = 10/60 dm⁻³, Molar concentration of non-volatile solution = 50/MB dm⁻³ => For isotonic solutions Molecular mass = 50 x 60/10 = 300g mol⁻¹. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-56 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','200 g/mol','250 g/mol','300 g/mol','350 g/mol','NEW','12-056','12-022',NULL),
(160936,'chemistry','A solution of urea (mol. mass 56 g mol⁻¹) boils at 100.18°C at the atmospheric pressure. if Kf and kb for water are 1.86 and 0.512K kg mol⁻¹ respectively, the above solution will freeze at','– 0.654°C','DTf/DTb = Kf/Kb, DTf = 1.86 x 0.18/0.512 = 0.654, Freezing point of urea in water = –0.654°C. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-53 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','0.654°C','– 0.654°C','6.54°C','0.654°C','NEW','12-053','12-019',NULL),
(160937,'chemistry','A solution contains non-volatile solute of molecular mass M2. which of the following can be used to calculate the molecular mass of solute in terms of Osmotic pressure?','M₂ = {m₂/V} x RT/pi','For dilute solution pi = n/ V x RT => pi x V = m₂/M₂ RT => M₂ = m₂RT/pi x V. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-53 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','M₂ = {m₂/pi} x RTV','M₂ = {m₂/V} x RT/pi','M₂ = {m₂/V} x RTpi','M₂ = {m₂/V} x pi/RT','NEW','12-053','12-019',NULL),
(160938,'chemistry','Pure water can be obtained from sea water by','reverse osmosis','The direction of osmosis can be reversed if a pressure larger than the Osmotic pressure is applied to the solution side. That is, now the pure solvent flows out of the solution through the semipermeable membrane. This phenomenon is called reverse osmosis. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-57 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','centrifugation','plasmolysis','reverse osmosis','sedimentation','NEW','12-057','12-023',NULL),
(160939,'chemistry','From the colligative properties of solution, which one is the best method for the determination of molecular weight of proteins and polymers','Osmotic pressure','Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-53 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','Osmotic pressure','Lowering in vapour pressure','Lowering in freezing point','Elevation in boiling point','NEW','12-053','12-019',NULL),
(160940,'chemistry','The vapour pressure of benzene at a certain temperature is 640 mm Hg. a non-volatile and non-electrolyte solid, weighing 2.175 g is added to 39.08 of benzene. the vapour pressure of the solution is 600 mm  Hg. what is the molecular weight of solid substance?','69.5','From Raoult’s law p"-ps/p" = wᵦ x Mₐ/ Wₐ x Mᵦ => 640-600/640 = 21.75 x 78/ 39.08 x Mᵦ = 69.5. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','69.5','79.5','59.5','49.5','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160941,'chemistry','If 0.15 g of a solute, dissolved in 15 g of solvent, is boiled at a temperature higher by 0.216°C, than that of the pure solvent. the molecular weight of the substance (molal elevation constant for the solvent is 2.16°C) is','100','Mb = 1000 x 2.16 x 0.15/0.216 x 15 = 100. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-51 Paragraph - last)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1.01','100','1000','10.1','NEW','12-051','12-017',NULL),
(160942,'chemistry','A 5% solution of cane sugar (mol. wt. = 342) is isotonic with 1% solution of a substance x. the molecular weight of x is','68.4','For isotonic solutions, C1 = C2, W1/M1V1 = W2/M2V2 => 5/342 x 0.1 = 1/M2 x 0.1, M2 = 342/5 = 68.4. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-55 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','68.4','171.2','34.2','136.8','NEW','12-055','12-021',NULL),
(160943,'chemistry','The vapour pressure of a solvent decreased by 10 mm of mercury when a non-volatile solute was added to the solvent. the mole fraction of the solute in the solution is 0.2. what should be the mole fraction of the solvent if the decrease in the vapour pressure is to be 20 mm of mercury?','0.6','From Raoult’s law p"-ps/p" = x₂ => 10/p"=0.2 => 50 mm Hg, mole fraction of solute = 20/50 = 0.4, mole fraction of solvent = 1 – 0.4 = 0.6. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','0.4','0.6','0.3','0.2','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160944,'chemistry','The vapour pressure of CCl₄ at 25°C is 143 mm Hg. if 0.5 g of a non-volatile solute (mol. weight = 65) is dissolved in 100 g CCl₄, the vapour pressure of the solution will be','141.43 mm Hg','Vapour pressure of pure solvent (p°ₐ) = 143 mm Hg, weight of solute (wᵦ) = 0.5 g, weight of solvent (Wₐ) = 100 g, molecular weight of solute (Mᵦ) = 65 and molecular weight of solvent (Mₐ) = 154., p"-ps/p" = wᵦ x Mₐ/wₐ x Mᵦ => 143-ps/143 = 0.5 x 154/65 x 100 = 141.43 mm Hg = ps. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','199.34 mm Hg','143.99 mm Hg','141.43 mm Hg','94.39 mm Hg','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160945,'chemistry','The relationship between Osmotic pressure at 273 K when 10 g glucose (p₁), 10 g urea (p₂), and 10 g sucrose (p₃) are dissolved in 250 ml of water is','p₂ > p₁ > p₃','The Osmotic pressure is a colligative property and it depends upon the number of moles of a solute. Since n₂ > n₁ > n₃(n=moles), therefore p₂ > p₁ > p₃. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-55 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','p₂ > p₁ > p₃','p₁ > p₂ > p₃','p₃ > p₁ > p₂','p₂ > p₃ > p₁','NEW','12-055','12-021',NULL),
(160946,'chemistry','According to raoult’s law, the relative lowering of vapour pressure for a solution is equal to','mole fraction of solute','According to Raoult’s law, p = p° x XA. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-49 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','mole fraction of solute','moles of solvent.','moles of solute','mole fraction of solvent','NEW','12-049','12-015',NULL),
(160947,'chemistry','If 0.1 M solution of glucose and 0.1 M solution of urea are placed on two sides of the semipermeable membrane to equal heights, then it will be correct to say that','there will be no net movement across the membrane','There is no net movement of the solvent through the semipermeable membrane between two solutions of equal concentration. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-55 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','there will be no net movement across the membrane','glucose will flow towards glucose solution','urea will flow towards glucose solution','water will flow from urea solution to glucose.','NEW','12-055','12-021',NULL),
(160948,'chemistry','Which one is a colligative property?','Osmotic pressure','(1) relative lowering of vapour pressure of the solvent (2) depression of freezing point of the solvent (3) elevation of boiling point of the solvent and (4) Osmotic pressure of the solution. All these properties depend on the number of solute particles irrespective of their nature relative to the total number of particles present in the solution. Such properties are called colligative properties. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-49 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','Boiling point','Melting point','Osmotic pressure','Freezing point','NEW','12-049','12-015',NULL),
(160949,'chemistry','Blood cells retain their normal shape in solution which are','isotonic to blood','Blood cells neither swell or shrink in isotonic solution. The solutions having same Osmotic pressure are called isotonic solutions. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-56 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','hypertonic to blood','isotonic to blood','equinormal to blood.','hypotonic to blood','NEW','12-056','12-022',NULL),
(160950,'chemistry','The relative lowering of the vapour pressure is equal to the ratio between the number of','solute molecules to the total molecules in the solution','Quantitatively, the extent to which a solute is dissociated or associated can be expressed by van’t Hoff factor i. This factor has been defined as ratio of normal molar mass to experimentally determined molar mass or as the ratio of observed colligative property to the Calculated colligative property. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-61 Paragraph - Summary)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','solvent molecules to the total molecules in the solution','solute molecules to the total molecules in the solution','solvent molecules to the total number of ions of the solute','solute molecules to the solvent molecules','NEW','12-049','12-015',NULL),
(160951,'chemistry','TheVan\'t Hoff factor (i) for a dilute aqueous solution of the strong electrolyte barium hydroxide is','3','Quantitatively, the extent to which a solute is dissociated or associated can be expressed by van’t Hoff factor i. This factor has been defined as ratio of normal molar mass to experimentally determined molar mass or as the ratio of observed colligative property to the Calculated colligative property. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-61 Paragraph - Summary)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','0','1','2','3','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(160952,'chemistry','The boiling point of 0.2 mol kg⁻¹ solution of x in water is greater than equimolal solution of y in water. which one of the following statements is true in this case?','X is undergoing dissociation in water','Elevation in boiling point and depression in freezing point, both are the colligative properties, i.e. depend only upon the number of particles present in solution. In other words, as the number of particles increases, boiling point increases but freezing point decreases. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-53 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','Molecular mass of X is less than the molecular mass of Y','Y is undergoing dissociation in water while X undergoes no change','X is undergoing dissociation in water','Molecular mass of X is greater than the molecular mass of Y','NEW','12-053','12-019',NULL),
(160953,'chemistry','Of the following 0.10 m aqueous solutions, which one will exhibit the largest freezing point depression?','Al₂(SO₄)₃','Quantitatively, the extent to which a solute is dissociated or associated can be expressed by van’t Hoff factor i. This factor has been defined as ratio of normal molar mass to experimentally determined molar mass or as the ratio of observed colligative property to the Calculated colligative property. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-61 Paragraph - Summary)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','KCl','C₆H₁₂O₆','Al₂(SO₄)₃','K₂SO₄','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(160954,'chemistry','TheVan\'t Hoff factor i for a compound which undergoes dissociation in one solvent and association in other solvent is respectively','greater than one and less than one','Quantitatively, the extent to which a solute is dissociated or associated can be expressed by van’t Hoff factor i. This factor has been defined as ratio of normal molar mass to experimentally determined molar mass or as the ratio of observed colligative property to the Calculated colligative property. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-61 Paragraph - Summary)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','less than one and greater than one','less than one and less than one','greater than one and less than one','greater than one and greater than one.','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(160955,'chemistry','The freezing point depression constant for water is –1.86 °C m⁻¹. if 5.00 g Na₂SO₄ is dissolved in 45.0 g H₂O, the freezing point is changed by –3.82 °C. calculate the Van\'t Hoff factor for Na₂SO₄','2.63','We know that, DTf = i x f x wB/MB x 1000/WA => i = 3.82 x 142 x 45/1.86 x 5 x 1000 = 2.63. Reference (NCERT Class-12 Page-58 Paragraph - 5)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1.11','2.63','3.11','3.13','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(160956,'chemistry','A 0.1 molal aqueous solution of a weak acid is 30% ionized. if Kf for water is 1.86°C/m, the freezing point of the solution will be','– 0.24 °C','i for weak acid is 1 + a. Here a is degree of dissociation i.e., 30/100 = 0.3,  i = 1 + a = 1 + 0.3 = 1.3 =>DTf = i x Kf x m = 1.3 x 1.86 x 0.1 = 0.24 => Tf = 0-0.24= -0.24. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-58 Paragraph - 5)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','– 0.18 °C','– 0.30 °C','– 0.54 °C','– 0.24 °C','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(160957,'chemistry','An aqueous solution is 1.00 molal in KI. which change will cause the vapour pressure of the solution to increase?','Addition of water','Addition of water to an aqueous solution of KI causes the concentration of the solution to decrease thereby increasing the vapour pressure. In the other three options, the electrolytes undergo ionization, which leads to lowering of vapour pressure. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-61 Paragraph - Summary)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','Addition of NaCl','Addition of 1.00 molal KI','Addition of water','Addition of Na₂SO₄','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(160958,'chemistry','A 0.0020 m aqueous solution of an ionic compound [CO(NH₃)₅(NO₂)]Cl freezes at –0.00732 °C. number of moles of ions which 1 mol of ionic compound produces on being dissolved in water will be (Kf = –1.86 °C/m)','2','Applying, DTf = i x Kf x m => 0.00732 = i × 1.86 × 0.002 => i = 2. Reference (NCERT Class-12 Page-58 Paragraph - 5)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','3','1','4','2','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(160959,'chemistry','0.5 molal aqueous solution of a weak acid (HX) is 20% ionised. if Kf for water is 1.86 kg mol⁻¹, the lowering in freezing point of the solution is','1.12 K','i = 1 + a = 1 + 0.2 = 1.2 => DTf = i x Kf x m => = 1.2 × 1.86 × 0.5 = 1.116 K ≈ 1.12 K. Reference (NCERT Class-12 Page-58 Paragraph - 5)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','0.56 K','1.12 K','–0.56 K','-1.12 K','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(160960,'chemistry','Which of the following 0.10 m aqueous solution will have the lowest freezing point?','Al₂(SO₄)₃','Quantitatively, the extent to which a solute is dissociated or associated can be expressed by van’t Hoff factor i. This factor has been defined as ratio of normal molar mass to experimentally determined molar mass or as the ratio of observed colligative property to the Calculated colligative property. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-61 Paragraph - Summary)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','KI','C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁','Al₂(SO₄)₃','C₅H₁₀O₅','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(160961,'chemistry','Which of the following salts has the same value of Van\'t Hoff factor (i) as that of K₃[Fe(CN)₆]?','Al(NO₃)₃','Quantitatively, the extent to which a solute is dissociated or associated can be expressed by van’t Hoff factor i. This factor has been defined as ratio of normal molar mass to experimentally determined molar mass or as the ratio of observed colligative property to the Calculated colligative property. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-61 Paragraph - Summary)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','Na₂SO₄','Al(NO₃)₃','Al₂(SO₄)₃','NaCl','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(160962,'chemistry','At 25°C, the highest Osmotic pressure is exhibited by 0.1 m solution of','CaCl₂','Measurement of Osmotic pressure provides another method of determining molar masses of solutes. This method is widely used to determine molar masses of proteins, polymers and other macromolecules. The Osmotic pressure method has the advantage over other methods as pressure measurement is around the room temperature and the molarity of the solution is used instead of molality.  Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-55 Paragraph - last)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','glucose','KCl','CaCl₂','urea','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(160963,'chemistry','Which of the following aqueous solution has Minimum freezing point?','0.01 m NaCl','Depression in freezing point is a colligative property (i.e. depends only upon the number of particles of solutes). Thus, the compound which produces Maximum number of ions will have the lowest freezing point. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-58 Paragraph - 5)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','0.01 m NaCl','0.005 m MgSO₄','0.005 m C₂H₅OH','0.005 m MgI₂','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(160964,'chemistry','The following solutions were prepared by dissolving 10 g of glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆) in 250 ml of water (P₁), 10 g of urea (CH₄N₂O) in 250 ml of water (P₂) and 10 g of sucrose (C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁) in 250 ml of water (P₃). The right option for the decreasing order of osmotic pressure of these solutions','P₂>P₁>P₃','We know π= iCRT\nFor Glucose = P₁ = 1 × (10/180) × R × T = 0.055RT\nFor Urea = P₂ = 1 ×(10/60) × R × T = 0.166RT\nFor Sucrose = P₃ = 1 ×(10/342) × R × T = 0.029RT\nHence , P₂>P₁>P₃','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','P₂>P₁>P₃','P₁>P₂>P₃','P₂>P₃>P₁','P₃>P₁>P₂','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(2000031685,'physics','The total electric flux through a closed spherical surface of radius \' ${r}$ \' enclosing an electric dipole of dipole moment ${2 a q}$ is (Give ${\\varepsilon_{0}=}$ permittivity of free space)','A','Using Gauss\'s law ${\\phi=\\oint \\vec{E} \\cdot d \\vec{A}=\\dfrac{q_{\\text {in }}}{\\varepsilon_{0}}}$<br>As net charge is zero, so ${\\phi=}$ zero .','Electric flux through a closed surface and Gauss’s Law','Electric charges & Fields','Medium',NULL,'Zero','${\\dfrac{q}{\\varepsilon_{0}}}$','${\\dfrac{2 q}{\\varepsilon_{0}}}$','${\\dfrac{8 \\pi r^{2} q}{\\varepsilon_{0}}}$',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160965,'chemistry','Which one is not correct mathematical equation for Dalton’s Law of partial pressure? Here p = total pressure of gaseous mixture','Pᵢ=XᵢPᵢ° ;where Xᵢ= mole fraction of ith gas in gaseous mixture ; Pᵢ° = pressure of ith gas in pure state','1)According to Dalton’s law of partial pressures, the total pressure ( ptotal ) over the solution phase in the container will be the sum of the partial pressures of the components of the solution.  Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-44 Paragraph - 3)\n\n2)P = P₁ + P₂ + P₃\n\n3)Also, Pᵢ=XᵢP ; P=Total pressure ,  Xᵢ= mole fraction of  i th gas in gaseous mixture.\n\n4)P = P₁ + P₂ + P₃ = P = n₁(RT/V) + n₂(RT/V) + n₃(RT/V)  ( since PV=nRT)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','P = P₁ + P₂ + P₃','P = n₁(RT/V) + n₂(RT/V) + n₃(RT/V)','Pᵢ=XᵢP ; P=Total pressure ,  Xᵢ= mole fraction of ith gas in gaseous mixture','Pᵢ=XᵢPᵢ° ;where Xᵢ= mole fraction of ith gas in gaseous mixture ; Pᵢ° = pressure of ith gas in pure state','NEW','12-044','12-010',NULL),
(160966,'chemistry','The temperature dependent term among the following is:','molarity','Molarity and normality are temperature dependent because they involve volume of solutions. Volume is dependent on temperature.\n\nMolarity(M)=Moles of solute/ Volume of solution (in L)\n\nMolality, mole fraction and weight percentage do not depend on temperature because they doesnot have volume term in their formula.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','molality','molarity','mole fraction','weight percentage','NEW','12-039','12-005',NULL),
(160967,'chemistry','given below are two statements one is assertion one is reason ASSERTION:helium is used to dilute oxygen in diving apparatus REASON:;helium has a solubility in oxygen.  choose the correct statement:','A is true R is false','When the divers come toward the surface of the water, the pressure gradually decreases. This releases the dissolved gases and leads to the formation of bubbles of nitrogen in the blood. This blocks capillaries and creates a medical condition known as bends, which are painful and dangerous to life. \n\nTo avoid bends, as well as, the toxic effects of high concentrations of nitrogen in the blood, the tanks used by scuba divers are filled with air diluted with helium (11.7% helium, 56.2% nitrogen, and 32.1% oxygen).\n\nThus, Assertion is correct. The reason is wrong. Helium is added to dilute oxygen in the diving apparatus because helium is less soluble in blood than nitrogen. \nThus, option fourth is the correct answer.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','A is false R is true','both A and R are true and R is correct explanation of A','both A  and R are true and R is the not correct explanation of A','A is true R is false','NEW','12-043','12-009',NULL),
(160968,'chemistry','the ideal solution indicates:','attraction force remains the same in A-A and B-B','At molecular level, ideal behaviour of the solutions can be explained by considering two components A and B. In pure components, the intermolecular attractive interactions will be of types A-A and B-B, whereas in the binary solutions in addition to these two interactions, A-B type of interactions will also be present.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','A-B force of attraction is greater than A-A and B-B','A-B force of attraction is lesser than A-A and B-B','attraction force remains the same in A-A and B-B','the volume of solution is different from the sum of solution of solute and solvent .','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160969,'chemistry','the correct value of vapour pressure of a solution at 45 degree celcius with benzene to octane in a molar ratio 3:2...','336mm of hg','Hint: \nP\n=\nP\no\nX\nStep 1:\n\nCalculate the partial pressure of benzene and octane is as follows:\n\nP\n(\nbenzene\n)\n=\nP\no\nX\n\nP\n(\nbenzene\n)\n=\n280\n×\n3\n5\n\nP\n(\nbenzene\n)\n \n=\n \n168\n \natm\n\nP\n(\noctane\n)\n=\n420\n×\n2\n5\n\nP\n(\noctane\n)\n \n=\n \n168\n \natm\nStep 2:\n\nCalculate the total pressure as follows:\n\nP(total) = P(benzene) + P(octane)\n\nP(total) = 168 +168\n\nP(total) = 336 atm','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','336mm of hg','350 mm of hg','160mm of hg','168mm of hg','NEW','12-044','12-010',NULL),
(160970,'chemistry','If  8g of non electrolyte solute is mixed in 114g on n-octane to reduce its vapour pressure to 80%, the molar mass of solute in g/mol is?','40','Hint: \nP\n0\n−\nP\ns\nP\n0\n=\nX\nsolute\nStep 1:\n\nSince moles of solute is very low, so it is a dilute solution,\n\nP\n0\n−\nP\ns\nP\n0\n=\nn\nsolute\nn\nsolvent\nStep 2:\n\nLet P0 = 100, V.P reduced to 80%, \n∴\nP\ns\n=\n80\n100\n−\n80\n100\n=\n8\n/\nm\n114\n/\n114\n\nm\n=\n40','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','40','60','80','20','NEW','12-050','12-016',NULL),
(160971,'chemistry','if one mole of sugar is dissolved in three moles of water at 298K .the relative lowering of vapour pressure is :','0.25','Hint: \nΔ\nP\nP\no\n=\nX\ns\no\nl\nu\nt\ne\n\n\nThe formula for relative lowering of vapor pressure is as follows: \n\nΔ\nP\nP\no\n=\nX\ns\no\nl\nu\nt\ne\n\n\nCalculate the value of relative lowering of vapor pressure as follows:\n\nΔ\nP\nP\no\n=\nn\nu\nm\nb\ne\nr\n \no\nf\n \nm\no\nl\ne\n \no\nf\n \ns\nu\ng\na\nr\nn\nu\nm\nb\ne\nr\n \no\nf\n \nm\no\nl\ne\n \no\nf\n \ns\nu\ng\na\nr\n+\nn\nu\nm\nb\ne\nr\n \no\nf\n \nm\no\nl\ne\n \no\nf\n \nw\na\nt\ne\nr\n\nΔ\nP\nP\no\n=\n1\n1\n+\n3\n\nΔ\nP\nP\no\n= 0.25','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','0.25','0.15','0.5','0.33','NEW','12-049','12-015',NULL),
(160972,'chemistry','in 1 molal solution that contain 0.5 moles solute there is:','500g of solvent','Hint: Molal is the unit of molality\n\nThe formula of molality is as follows:\n\nM\no\nl\na\nl\ni\nt\ny\n=\nn\nu\nm\nb\ne\nr\n \no\nf\n \nm\no\nl\ne\ns\n \no\nf\n \ns\no\nl\nu\nt\ne\na\nm\no\nu\nn\nt\n \no\nf\n \ns\no\nl\nv\ne\nn\nt\n(\nk\ng\n)\n\nThe value of molality is 1 molal.\n\n1\n=\n0.5\n \nm\no\nl\na\nm\no\nu\nn\nt\n \no\nf\n \ns\no\nl\nv\ne\nn\nt\n(\nk\ng\n)\n\namount of solvent = 0.5 kg or 500 g','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1000g of solvent','500ml of solvent','500g of solvent','100ml of solvent','NEW','12-039','12-005',NULL),
(160973,'chemistry','following solutions are prepared dissolving 10g of glucose in 250ml of water (P1),10g urea in 250ml water (P2), 10g sucrose in 250ml of water (P3).the decreasing order of osmotic pressure of these solutions is :','P2>P1>P3','Hint: \nπ\n=\nCRT\nThe formula of osmotic pressure is as follows:\n\nπ\n=\nCST\n=\nn\nv\nST\n=\nm\nM\n×\n1\nv\nST\nmVST is the same for all 3 solutions.\n\nm = given mass\n\nV = volume\n\nS = solution constant\n\nT = temperature\n\nSo, the higher the molecular mass, the lower will be the osmotic pressure. Hence, osmotic pressure is inversely proportional to the molecular weight of the substance at constant, m, V, S, and T.\n\nThe decreasing order of osmotic pressure for the given solutions is \nP\n2\n>\nP\n1\n>\nP\n3\nurea\n \n=\n \n60\n \n(\nP\n2\n)\n\nglucose\n \n=\n \n180\n \n(\nP\n1\n)\n\nSucrose\n \n=\n \n342\n(\nP\n3\n)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','P2> P3>P1','P3>P1>P2','P2>P1>P3','P1>P2>P3','NEW','12-055','12-021',NULL),
(160975,'chemistry','1g of non electrolyte solute (molar mass 250g/mol) is dissolved in 51.2g of benzene .if the freezing point depression constant,kf of benzene is 5.12mol-1 kg K , the freezing point of benzene will be lowered by :','0.4K','Hint:\nΔ\nT\nf\n=\nK\nf\n×\nW\n1\n×\n1000\nW\n2\n×\nM\n1\nExplanation:\nStep 1:\n\nΔ\nT\nf\n=\nK\nf\n×\nW\n1\n×\n1000\nW\n2\n×\nM\n1\nW1 = Weight of the solute\n\nW2 = weight of solvent\n\nM1 = Molar mass of solute\n\nKf = Freezing point depression constant\n\nStep 2:\n\nNow\n,\n \nΔ\nT\nf\n=\n5\n.\n12\n×\n1\n×\n1000\n51\n.\n2\n×\n250\n\nΔ\nT\nf\n=\n0\n.\n4\n \nK','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','0.4K','0.3K','0.5K','0.2K','NEW','12-052','12-018',NULL),
(160976,'chemistry','isotonic solutions must have the same','osmotic pressure','Hint: \nπ\n1\n=\nπ\n2\nIsotonic solutions have same osmotic pressure\n\nπ\n1\n \n=\n \nC\n1\nST\n\nπ\n2\n \n=\n \nC\n2\nST\n\nso\n \nat\n \nsame\n \ntemp\n,\nif\n \nC\n1\n \n=\n  \nC\n2\n \nπ\n1\n \n=\nπ\n2\nTwo solutions having the same osmotic pressure across a semipermeable membrane is referred to as an isotonic solution','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','vapour pressure','freezing temperature','osmotic pressure','boiling temperature','NEW','12-056','12-022',NULL),
(160977,'chemistry','For an ideal solution, the non-zero value will be for:\n(NEET - 2015)','ΔS𝖒𝖎𝖝','For an ideal solution, Δ Smix = 0\nwhereas ΔVmixing = 0, ΔHmixing = 0 .\nThe entropy change Δ S = ΔQ/T (= Δ H/T at constant pressure) only for reversible processes. For irreversible processes such as mixing ΔS > ΔQ/T (= ΔН/T at constant pressure).\n\nThus for mixing of ideal gases or ideal solutions ΔQ = 0 but ΔS > 0.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','ΔH𝖒𝖎𝖝','ΔS𝖒𝖎𝖝','Δ V𝖒𝖎𝖝','Δ P = Pobserved — PRaoult','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160978,'chemistry','The incorrect statement among the following for an ideal solution is:\n(NEET - 2016)','ΔG𝖒𝖎𝖝 = 0','The solutions which obey Raoult\'s law over the entire range of concentration are known as ideal solutions. The ideal solutions have two other important properties.\n\nThe enthalpy of mixing of the pure components to form the solution is zero and the volume of mixing is also zero, i.e…,\n\nΔHmix = 0, ΔVmix = 0\nFor a spontaneous reaction, the value is negative. Hence, ΔGmix = 0, is incorrect.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','ΔH𝖒𝖎𝖝 = 0','ΔU𝖒𝖎𝖝 = 0','ΔP = Pobs - P(calculated by Raoult’s law) = 0','ΔG𝖒𝖎𝖝 = 0','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160979,'chemistry','The correct statement regarding a solution of two components A and B exhibiting positive deviation from ideal behavior is -\n(NEET - 2019)','Intermolecular attractive force between A-A and B-B are stronger than those between A-B','In case of positive deviation, the intermolecular force of attraction between solute particle and solvent particle is more than solute-solvent particle.\n\nHere, solute particle and solvent particle has higher tendency to get vapourised because intermolecular force is weak.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','Intermolecular attractive force between A-A and B-B are stronger than those between A-B','ΔmixH = 0 at constant T and P','Δmix V = 0 at constant T and P','Intermolecular attractive forces between A-A and B-B are equal to those between A-B','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160980,'chemistry','The electrolyte having the same value of Van\'t Hoff factor (i) as that of Al₂(SO₄)₃ (if all are 100% ionized) is:\n(NEET- 2015)','K₄[Fe(CN)₆]','Step 1:\nCalculate the value of i for Al₂ (S0₄)₃ as follows:\nFor the equation,\nAl₂ (S0₄)₃ →2Al³⁺ + 3S0₄² ⁻\nValue of Van\'t Hoff factor, i = 5\nStep 2:\nCalculate the value of i for other compounds given in the options as follows:\n(a) K₂SO₄ → 2K⁺  + S0₄² ⁻\nValue of i=3\n(b) K₃[Fe(CN)₆] → 3K⁺  + [Fe(CN)]₆³-\nValue of i = 4\n(c) Al(NO₃)₃ →Al³⁺ + 3 NO₃-\nValue of i=4\n(d) K₄[Fe(CN)₆] →4K⁺ + [Fe(CN)]₆³-\nValue of i=5\nHence, K₄[Fe(CN)₆]  has the same value of i as that of Al₂(SO₄)₃.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','K₂SO₄','К₃[Fe(CN)₆]','Al(NO₃)₃','K₄[Fe(CN)₆]','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(161101,'chemistry','A solution containing 62 g ethylene glycol in\n 250 g water is cooled to –10°C. If Kբ\n  for water is\n 1.86 K kg /mol, the amount of water (in g)\n separated as ice is [JEE (Main)-2019]','64','∆Tf = Kf × m\n10 = 1.86 ×( 62 × 1000/62 × Wa)\nWa = 1.86 × 1000/10 = 186 g\nGiven amount of H₂O is 250 g\n→ The amount of water separated as ice\n= 250 – 186 = 64 g','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','64','32','16','48','NEW','12-052','12-018',NULL),
(161102,'chemistry','A solution is prepared by dissolving 0.6 g of urea\n (molar mass = 60 g /mol) and 1.8 g of glucose\n (molar mass = 180 g /mol)) in 100 mL of water at\n 27°C. The osmotic pressure of the solution is\n (R = 0.08206 L atm /K mol) [JEE (Main)-2019]','4.92 atm','Osmotic pressure (S) = CRT\nSolute : urea and glucose\n→  S = (C₁ + C₂) RT = [ (0.6/60×0.1) + (1.8/180×0.1) ] × 0.0821 × 300 = 4.926 atm','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','1.64 atm','2.46 atm','8.2 atm','4.92 atm','NEW','12-055','12-021',NULL),
(161103,'chemistry','1 g of a non-volatile non-electrolyte solute is\n dissolved in 100 g of two different solvents A and\n B whose ebullioscopic constants are in the ratio of\n 1 : 5. The ratio of the elevation in their boiling\n points, ∆Tb(A)/∆Tb(B) = ? [JEE (Main)-2019]','1 : 5','∆Tb = kb × m\n(Kb)A/(kb)B = 1/5\n\n(∆Tb)A/(∆Tb)B = (Kb)A/(kb)B = 1/5','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','1 : 5','10 : 1','5 : 1','1 : 0.2','NEW','12-051','12-017',NULL),
(161104,'chemistry','At room temperature, a dilute solution of urea is\n prepared by dissolving 0.60 g of urea in 360 g of\n water. If the vapour pressure of pure water at this\n temperature is 35 mmHg, lowering of vapour pressure\n will be : (molar mass of urea = 60 g /mol)\n[JEE (Main)-2019]','0.017 mmHg','Relative lowering of VP is given by\n\nP°B - PB/P°B = Xa = Na/Na + Nb ≈ Na / Nb\nP°B - PB/35  = 0.16 × 18/60 × 360 = 1/2000\n\nOn solving, ∆PB = P°B - PB = 0.017','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','0.031 mmHg','0.017 mmHg','0.028 mmHg','0.027 mmHg','NEW','12-049','12-015',NULL),
(161105,'chemistry','Molal depression constant for a solvent is\n 4.0 K kg /mol. The depression in the freezing\n point of the solvent for 0.03 mol /kg solution of\n K₂SO₄ is\n (Assume complete dissociation of the electrolyte)','0.36 K','K₂SO₄ → 2K⁺ + SO₄²⁻\ni (van’t Hoff Factor) = 3\n→ ∆Tf = iKf\nm\n= 3 × 4 × 0.03\n= 0.36 K','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','0.36 K','0.18 K','0.12 K','0.24 K','NEW','12-052','12-018',NULL),
(161106,'chemistry','The osmotic pressure of a dilute solution of an\n ionic compound XY in water is four times that of a\n solution of 0.01 M BaCl₂ in water. Assuming\n complete dissociation of the given ionic compounds\n in water, the concentration of XY (in mol /L) in\n solution is [JEE (Main)-2019]','6 × 10⁻²','πXY = 4π(BaCl₂)\n→ 2[XY] = 4 ×(0.01)× 3\n[XY] = 0.06 = 6 × 10⁻² mol/L','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','16 × 10⁻⁴','4 × 10⁻⁴','6 × 10⁻²','4 × 10⁻²','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(161107,'chemistry','Liquid ‘M’ and liquid ‘N’ form an ideal solution. The\n vapour pressures of pure liquids ‘M’ and ‘N’ are\n 450 and 700 mmHg, respectively, at the same\n temperature. Then correct statement is\n(xₘ = Mole fraction of ‘M’ in solution;\nxₙ = Mole fraction of ‘N’ in solution;\nyₘ = Mole fraction of ‘M’ in vapour phase;\nyₙ = Mole fraction of ‘N’ in vapour phase)','xₘ/xₙ > yₘ/yₙ','P°m = 450mm Hg , P°n = 700 mmHg\nPm = P°mXm = YmPt\nP°m = Ym(Pt)/Xm\n\nSimilarly, P°n = Yn(Pt)/ Xn \n\nGiven , P°m < P°n\n\nYm/Xm < Yn/Xn\n\nxₘ/xₙ > yₘ/yₙ','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','xₘ/xₙ = yₘ/yₙ','xₘ/xₙ > yₘ/yₙ','xₘ/xₙ < yₘ/yₙ','(xₘ - yₘ)   <   (xₙ - yₙ)','NEW','12-044','12-010',NULL),
(161108,'chemistry','For the solution of the gases w, x, y and z in water\n at 298 K, the Henrys law constants (Kₕ) are 0.5,\n2, 35 and 40 kbar, respectively. The correct plot for\n the given data is : [JEE (Main)-2019] Refer diagram(n1609)','C','Refer diagram(ne1610)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','12-041','12-007','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/161108q.png'),
(161109,'chemistry','The vapour pressures of pure liquids A and B are\n 400 and 600 mmHg, respectively at 298 K. On\n mixing the two liquids, the sum of their initial\n volumes is equal to the volume of the final mixture.\n The mole fraction of liquid B is 0.5 in the mixture.\n The vapour pressure of the final solution, the mole\n fractions of components A and B in vapour phase,\nrespectively are [JEE (Main)-2019]','500 mmHg, 0.4, 0.6','P = XbP°b + XaP°a = 0.5 × 600  + 0.5×400 = 300+200 = 500\nPb = Yb Ptotal \nYb = Pb/Ptotal = 300/500 = 3/5 = 0.6\nYa = Pa/Ptotal = 200/500 = 2/5 = 0.4','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','500 mmHg, 0.4, 0.6','500 mmHg, 0.5, 0.5','450 mmHg, 0.4, 0.6','450 mmHg, 0.5, 0.5','NEW','12-044','12-010',NULL),
(161110,'chemistry','Molecules of benzoic acid (C₆H₅COOH) dimerise in\n benzene. ‘w’ g of the acid dissolved in 30 g of\n benzene shows a depression in freezing point\n equal to 2 K. If the percentage association of the\n acid to form dimer in the solution is 80, then w is\n (Given that Kf\n = 5 K kg /mol, Molar mass of\n benzoic acid = 122 g /mol) [JEE (Main)-2019]','2.4 g','Refer diagram(ne1611)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','1.5 g','2.4 g','1.8 g','1.0 g','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(161111,'chemistry','Freezing point of a 4% aqueous solution of X is\n equal to freezing point of 12% aqueous solution of\n Y. If molecular weight of X is A, then molecular\n weight of Y is [JEE (Main)-2019]','3A','4/Mx = 12/My \nMy = 3Mx \nMy = 3A\n(Since density of solutions are not given therefore\n assuming molality to be equal to molarity and given\n % as % W/V)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','2A','3A','A','4A','NEW','12-052','12-018',NULL),
(161112,'chemistry','K₂HgI₄ is 40% ionised in aqueous solution. The value\n of its van’t Hoff factor (i) is [JEE (Main)-2019]','1.8','K₂HgI₄ ⇌ 2K⁺ + [HgI₄]²⁻\nn = 3\nα = i-1/n-1 \n0.4 = i -1/3-1 \ni = 1.8','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','1.6','2','2.2','1.8','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(161113,'chemistry','The freezing point of a diluted milk sample is found\n to be –0.2°C, while it should have been –0.5°C for\n pure milk. How much water has been added to\n pure milk to make the diluted sample?\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','3 cups of water and 2 cups of pure milk','Freezing point of diluted milk = – 0.2°C\n\n , ∆Tf\' = 0.2°C\nFreezing point of pure milk = – 0.5°C\n , ∆Tf\n = 0.5°C\n\n∆Tf/∆Tf\' = Kfm/Kfm\'\n0.5/0.2 = w1\'/w1 = 5/2\n\n→ 2 cups of pure milk mixed with 3 cups of water\n overall 5 cups of diluted milk.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','3 cups of water and 2 cups of pure milk','1 cup of water and 2 cups of pure milk','2 cups of water to 3 cups of pure milk','1 cup of water to 3 cups of pure milk','NEW','12-053','12-019',NULL),
(161114,'chemistry','Elevation in the boiling point for 1 molal solution of\n glucose is 2 K. The depression in the freezing\n point for 2 molal solution of glucose in the same\n solvent is 2 K. The relation between Kb and Kf\n  is\n[JEE (Main)-2019]','Kb = 2 Kf','∆Tb = KbM → Kb(1) = 2 → Kb = 2 K/m\n∆Tf = KfM → Kf(2) = 2 → Kb = 1 K/m\n\nkf = 0.5kb , kb = 2 kf','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Kb = 0.5 Kf','Kb = 2 Kf','Kb = 1.5 Kf','Kb = Kf','NEW','12-053','12-019',NULL),
(161115,'chemistry','Liquids A and B form an ideal solution in the entire\n composition range. At 350 K, the vapor pressures\n of pure A and pure B are 7 × 10³ Pa and 12 × 10³ Pa, respectively. The composition of the vapor in\n equilibrium with a solution containing 40 mole\n percent of A at this temperature is\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','xA = 0.28; xB = 0.72','P°A = 7 × 10³\nP°B = 12 × 10³\nXa = 0.4\nXb = 0.6\n→ P = (7 × 0.4 + 12 × 0.6 ) × 10³ = 10⁴\nPA = 2.8 × 10³, PB = 7.2 × 10³\n→ YA = 0.28, YB = 0.72','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','xA = 0.76; xB = 0.24','xA = 0.37; xB = 0.63','xA = 0.28; xB = 0.72','xA = 0.4; xB = 0.6','NEW','12-045','12-011',NULL),
(161116,'chemistry','Which one of the following statements regarding\n Henry\'s law is not correct? [JEE (Main)-2019]','Higher the value of KH at a given pressure,\n higher is the solubility of the gas in the liquids.','Solubility decreases with the increase in value of KH.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Different gases have different KH (Henry\'s law\n constant) values at the same temperature','The value of KH increases with increase of\n temperature and KH is function of the nature of\n the gas','The partial pressure of the gas in vapour phase\n is proportional to the mole fraction of the gas\n in the solution','Higher the value of KH at a given pressure,\n higher is the solubility of the gas in the liquids.','NEW','12-041','12-007',NULL),
(161117,'chemistry','The freezing point of benzene decreases by 0.45ºC\n when 0.2 g of acetic acid is added to 20 g of\n benzene. If acetic acid associates to form a dimer\n in benzene, percentage association of acetic acid\n in benzene will be (Kf\n for benzene = 5.12 K kg\n/mol) [JEE (Main)-2017]','94.60%','Refer diagram(ne1612)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','74.60%','94.60%','64.60%','80.40%','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(161118,'chemistry','18 g glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆) is added to 178.2 g water.\n The vapor pressure of water (in torr) for this\n aqueous solution is [JEE (Main)-2016]','752.4','P° - Ps / Ps = nsolute / nsolvent = (18/180)/178.2/18 = 18/17.82 \n\nAt normal boiling point of water V.P. = pº = 760 torr\n760- Ps / Ps = 18/1782 \nps = 752.4 torr','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','76','752.4','759','7.6','NEW','12-049','12-015',NULL),
(161119,'chemistry','The vapour pressure of acetone at 20°C is 185 torr.\n When 1.2 g of a non-volatile substance was\n dissolved in 100 g of acetone at 20°C, its vapour\n pressure was 183 torr. The molar mass (g /mol)\n of the substance is [JEE (Main)-2015]','64','Vapour pressure of pure acetone P° = 185 torr A \nVapour pressure of solution, PS = 183 torr\nMolar mass of solvent, MA = 58 g/mole\nas we know P°A - Ps/Ps = nb / na\n\n185-183/183 = Wb × Ma / Mb × Wa\n2/183 = 1.2 × 58 / Mb × 100\nMb = 63.68 g/mole','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','32','64','128','488','NEW','12-049','12-015',NULL),
(161120,'chemistry','Consider separate solutions of 0.500 M\n C₂H₅OH(aq), 0.100 M Mg₃(PO₄)₂(aq), 0.250 M\n KBr(aq) and 0.125 M Na₃PO₄(aq) at 25°C. Which\n statement is true about these solutions, assuming\n all salts to be strong electrolytes?\n [JEE (Main)-2014]','They all have the same osmotic pressure.','π =  iCRT\nπC₂H₅OH = 1 × 0.500 × R × T =  0.5RT\nπMg₃(PO₄)₂ = 5 × 0.100 × R × T = 0.5RT\nπKBr = 2 × 0.250 × R × T = 0.5RT\nπNa₃PO₄ = 4 × 0.125 × R × T  =0.5RT','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','They all have the same osmotic pressure.','0.100 M Mg₃(PO₄)₂(aq) has the highest\n osmotic pressure.','0.125 M Na₃PO₄(aq) has the highest osmotic\n pressure.','0.500 M C₂H₅OH(aq) has the highest osmotic\n pressure.','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(161121,'chemistry','Kf for water is 1.86 K kg /mol. If your automobile\n radiator holds 1.0 kg of water, how many grams of\n ethylene glycol (C₂H₆O₂) must you add to get the\n freezing point of the solution lowered to – 2.8°C?\n [AIEEE-2012]','93 g','Refer diagram(ne1613)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','93 g','39 g','27 g','72 g','NEW','12-052','12-018',NULL),
(161122,'chemistry','A 5% solution of cane sugar (molar mass 342 is\n isotonic with 1% of a solution of an unknown\n solute. The molar mass of unknown solute in g/mol\n is [AIEEE-2011]','68.4','For isotonic solution\nπ1 = π2\nC1 = C2 \nW1/M1V1 = W2/M2V2\n5/342 ×100 = 1 / M2 × 100\nM2 = 68.4','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','34.2','136.2','171.2','68.4','NEW','12-056','12-022',NULL),
(161123,'chemistry','On mixing, heptane and octane from an ideal\n solution. At 373 K, the vapour pressures of the two\n liquid components (heptane and octane) are 105\nkPa and 45 kPa respectively. Vapour pressure of\n the solution obtained by mixing 25.0 g of heptane\n and 35 g of octane will be (molar mass of heptane\n = 100 g /mol and of octane = 114 g /mol)\n [AIEEE-2010]','72.0 kPa','P = XaP°a + XbP°b\nn(heptane) = 25/100 = 0.25\nn(octane) = 35/114 = 0.307\np = 0.25/(0.25 + 0.307)105 + 0.307/(0.25 + 0.307)45 = 47.127 + 24.84 = 71.96\n ≈ 72 kPa','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','144.5 kPa','72.0 kPa','36.1 kPa','96.2 kPa','NEW','12-044','12-010',NULL),
(161124,'chemistry','If sodium sulphate is considered to be completely\n dissociated into cations and anions in aqueous\n solution, the change in freezing point of water (∆Tf\n),\nwhen 0.01 mol of sodium sulphate is dissolved in\n 1 kg of water, is (Kf\n = 1.86 K kg /mol)\n [AIEEE-2010]','0.0558 K','∆Tf = i Kf\nm\ni for Na₂SO₄ is 3(100% ionisation)\n∆Tf = 3 × 1.86 × (0.01/1) = 0.0558 K','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','0.0186 K','0.0372 K','0.0558 K','0.0744 K','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(161125,'chemistry','Two liquids X and Y from an ideal solution. At 300\nK, vapour pressure of the solution containing 1 mol\n of X and 3 mol of Y is 550 mmHg. At the same\n temperature, if 1 mol of Y is further added to this\n solution, vapour pressure of the solution increases\n by 10 mmHg. Vapour pressure (in mmHg) of X and\n Y in their pure states will be, respectively\n [AIEEE-2009]','400 and 600','Let V. P. of pure X = x\nand V. P. of pure Y = y\nThen, x/4 + 3y/4 = 550 → (1) and \nx/5 = 4y/5 = 560 → (2)\nSolving (i) and (ii), we get\nx = 400 mm\n and y = 600 mm','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','300 and 400','400 and 600','500 and 600','200 and 300','NEW','12-044','12-010',NULL),
(161126,'chemistry','A binary liquid solution is prepared by mixing\n n-heptane and ethanol. Which one of the following\n statements is correct regarding the behaviour of the\n solution? [AIEEE-2009]','The solution is non-ideal, showing +ve deviation\n from Raoult\'s Law','Ethanol has H-Bonding, n-heptane tries to break the\n H-bonds of ethanol, hence, V.P. increases. Such a\n solution shows positive deviation from Raoult\'s Law.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','The solution is non-ideal, showing +ve deviation\n from Raoult\'s Law','The solution is non-ideal, showing –ve deviation\n from Raoult\'s Law','n-heptane shows +ve deviation while ethanol\n shows –ve deviation from Raoult\'s Law','The solution formed is an ideal solution','NEW','12-048','12-014',NULL),
(161201,'chemistry','Which of the following unit is useful in relating concentration of solution with its vapour pressure?','Mole fraction','Mole fraction is useful in’relating vapour pressure with concentration of solution. According to Raoult’s law, the partial vapour pressure of each component in the solution is directly proportional to its mole fraction.\nA is one component.\nPa ∝ Xa \nXa = na / na + nb','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Mole fraction','Parts per million','Mass percentage','Molality','NEW','12-044','12-010',NULL),
(161202,'chemistry','On dissolving sugar in water at room temperature solution feels cool to touch. Under which of the following case dissolution of sugar will be most rapid?','Powdered sugar in hot water','Since the solution is cool to touch, the dissolution is endothermic. Therefore, high temperature will favour dissolution. Further, powdered sugar has large surface area and is favourable for dissolution','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Sugar crystals in cold water','Sugar crystals in hot water','Powdered sugar in cold water','Powdered sugar in hot water','NEW','12-040','12-006',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(161203,'chemistry','At equilibrium the rate of dissolution of a solid solute in a volatile liquid solvent is','equal to the rate of crystallisation','At equilibrium the rate of dissolution of solid solute is equal to rate of crystallisation.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','less than the rate of crystallisation','greater than the rate of crystallisation','equal to the rate of crystallisation','zero','NEW','12-040','12-006',NULL),
(161204,'chemistry','A beaker contains a solution of substance ‘A’. Precipitation of substance ‘A’ takes place when small amount of ‘A’ is added to the solution. The solution is','supersaturated','When small amount of solute is added to its solution and it does not dissolve and get precipitated then this solution is supersaturated solution.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','saturated','supersaturated','unsaturated','concentrated','NEW','12-040','12-006',NULL),
(161205,'chemistry','Maximum amount of a solid solute that can be dissolved in a specified amount of a given liquid solvent does not depend upon','pressure','Solubility of a solid in liquid does not depend on pressure because solid is practically incompressible.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','temperature','nature of solute','pressure','nature of solvent','NEW','12-040','12-006',NULL),
(161206,'chemistry','Low concentration of oxygen in the blood and tissues of people living at high altitude is due to','low atmospheric pressure','At high altitudes the atmospheric pressure is less but the body temperature remains same hence concentration of oxygen in the air as well as blood is less','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','low temperature','low atmospheric pressure','high atmospheric pressure','both low temperature and high atmospheric pressure','NEW','12-043','12-009',NULL),
(161207,'chemistry','Considering the formation, breaking and strength of hydrogen bond, predict which of the following mixture will show a positive deviation from Raoulf s law?','Methanol and acetone','Mixture of Methanol and acetone shows positive deviation because methanol-methanol and acetone-acetone interactions are more than methanol-acetone. The more number of hydrogen bonds are broken the less number of new H-bonds are formed','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Methanol and acetone','Chloroform and acetone','Nitric acid and water','Phenol and aniline','NEW','12-048','12-014',NULL),
(161208,'chemistry','Colligative properties depend on','the number of solute particles in solution','Colligative properties depend upon the number of solute particles in the solution and independent of its nature.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','the nature of the solute particles dissolved in solution','the number of solute particles in solution','the physical properties of the solute particles dissolved in solution','the nature of solvent particles','NEW','12-049','12-015',NULL),
(161209,'chemistry','Which of the following aqueous solution should have the highest boiling point?','1.0MNa₂SO₄','1.0 M Na₂SO₄ since it furnishes maximum number of ions (2Na+ + SO₄–).','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','l.OMNaOH','1.0MNa₂SO₄','1.0MNH₄NO₃','l.OMKNO₃','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(161210,'chemistry','The unit of ebullioscopic constant is','K kg /mol or K /molality','Kb = ∆Tb/m = K/ mol/kg or K/ Molality \nThe unit of ebullioscopic constant is K kg /mol or K /molality.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','K kg /mol or K /molality','mol /K kg  or molality/K','Kg / mol K or K / molality','k mol / kg or k ( molality)','NEW','12-051','12-017',NULL),
(161211,'chemistry','In comparison to a 0.01 M solution of glucose, the depression in freezing point of a 0.01 M MgCl2 solution is :','about three times','0.01 M solution of glucose does not ionize while 0.01 M MgCl₂ solution furnishes 3 ions (Mg²⁺ + 2Cl⁻) in the solution, hence the value of colligative property for MgCl₂ solution is about 3 times.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','the same','about twice','about three times','about six times','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(161212,'chemistry','An unripe mango placed in a concentrated salt solution to prepare pickle, shrivels because','it loses water due to osmosis','Water starts moving out of mango (lower concentration) to the salt solution (higher concentration) due to osmosis.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','it gains water due to osmosis','it loses water due to reverse osmosis','it gains water due to reverse osmosis','it loses water due to osmosis','NEW','12-056','12-022',NULL),
(161213,'chemistry','At a given temperature, osmotic pressure of a concentrated solution of a substance','is higher than that of a dilute solution','π = CRT , where C is concentration of the solution. So, the higher the concentration of solution at given temperature the higher will be the osmotic pressure.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','is higher than that of a dilute solution','is lower than that of a dilute solution','is same as that of a dilute solution','cannot be compared with osmotic pressure of dilute solution','NEW','12-055','12-021',NULL),
(161214,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is false?','Two different solutions of sucrose of same molality-prepared in different solvents will have the same depression in freezing point.','Value of A is different for different solvents. Thus, according to relation, ∆Tf = Kfm even for same molal solutions depression in freezing point will be different.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','Two different solutions of sucrose of same molality-prepared in different solvents will have the same depression in freezing point.','The osmotic pressure of a solution is given by the equation 3.14(pi) = CRT (where C is the molarity of the solution).','Decreasing order of osmotic pressure for 0.01 M aqueous solutions of barium chloride, potassium chloride, acetic acid and sucrose is\nBaCl₂> KCl > CH₂COOH > sucrose','According to Raoult’s law, the vapour pressure exerted by a volatile component of a solution is directly proportional to its mole fraction in the solution.','NEW','12-053','12-019',NULL),
(161215,'chemistry','The value of van’t Hoff factor.s for KCl, NaCl and K2SO4, respectively, are','2, 2 and 3','KCl (K⁺ + Cl⁻ ) and NaCl (Na⁺ + Cl⁻ ) ionize to give 2 ions and K₂SO₄(2K⁺ + SO₄⁻) ionizes to give 3 ions thus, van’t Hoff factors for KCl, NaCl and K₂SO₄ are 2, 2 and 3 respectively.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','2, 2 and 2','2, 2 and 3','1, 1, and 2','1, 1 and 1','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(161216,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is false?','In reverse osmosis, solvent molecules move through a semipermeable membrane from a region of lower concentration of solute to a region of higher concentration.','In reverse osmosis, solvent molecules move through a semipermeable membrane from higher concentration of solute to lower concentration.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Units of atmospheric pressure and osmotic pressure are the same.','In reverse osmosis, solvent molecules move through a semipermeable membrane from a region of lower concentration of solute to a region of higher concentration.','The value of molal depression constant depends on nature of solvent.','Relative lowering of vapour pressure, is a dimensionless quantity.','NEW','12-057','12-023',NULL),
(161217,'chemistry','Value of Henry’s constant KH','increases with increase in temperature','Value of Henry’s constant increases with increase in temperature','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','increases with increase in temperature','decreases with increase in temperature','remains constant','first increases then decreases','NEW','12-041','12-007',NULL),
(161218,'chemistry','The value of Henry’s constant KH is','greater for gases with lower solubility','The higher the value of KH, the lower is the solubility of gas in the liquid.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','greater for gases with higher solubility','greater for gases with lower solubility','constant for all gases','not related to the solubility of gases','NEW','12-041','12-007',NULL),
(161219,'chemistry','Consider the figure and mark the correct option. Refer diagram(n1610)','Water will move from side (B) to side (A) if a pressure greater than osmotic pressure is applied on piston (B).','Water will move from side 3 (concentrated sodium chloride solution) to side A (fresh water) if a pressure greater than osmotic pressure is applied on piston B.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Water will move from side (A) to side (B) if a pressure lower than osmotic pressure is applied on piston (B).','Water will move from side (B) to side (A) if a pressure greater than osmotic pressure is applied on piston (B).','Water will move from side (B) to side (A) if a pressure equal to osmotic pressure is applied on piston (B).','Water will move from side (A) to side (B) if pressure equal to osmotic pressure is applied on piston (A).','NEW','12-057','12-023','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/161219q.png'),
(161220,'chemistry','We have three aqueous solutions of NaCl labelled as ‘A’, ‘B’ and ‘C’ with concentrations 0.1 M, 0.01 M and 0.001 M, respectively. The value of van’t Hoff factor for these solutions will be in the order','iA=iB =iC','The value of van’t Hoff’s factor will be iA=iB =iC due to complete dissociation of strong electrolyte (NaCl) in dilute solutions. On complete dissociation value of i for NaCl is 2.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','iA<iB <iC','iA>iB >iC','iA=iB =iC','iA<iB >iC','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(161221,'chemistry','On the basis of information given below mark the correct option. Information:\n(I) In bromoethane and chloroethane mixture intermolecular interactions of A-A and B-B type are nearly same as A-B type interactions.\n(II) In ethanol and acetone mixture A-A or B-B type intermolecular interactions are stronger than A-B type interactions.\n(III) In chloroform and acetone mixture A-A or B-B type intermolecular interactions are weaker than A-B type interactions.','Solution (I) will follow Raoult’s law.','Solution A will follow Raoult’s law due to nearly same interactions between A-A, B-B and A-B. The solution formed will be nearly ideal.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Solution (II) and (III) will follow Raoult’s law.','Solution (I) will follow Raoult’s law.','Solution (II) will show negative deviation from Raoult’s law.','Solution (III) will show positive deviation from Raoult’s law.','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(161222,'chemistry','Two beakers of capacity 500 mL were taken. One of these beakers, labelled “A”, was filled with 400 mL water whereas’the beaker labelled “B” was filled with 400 mL of 2 M solution of NaCl. At the same temperature both the beakers were placed in closed containers of same material and same capacity as shown in figure.\nAt a given temperature, which of the following statement is correct about the vapour pressure of pure water and that of NaCl solution. Refer diagram(n1611)','Vapour pressure in container (A) is more than that in container (B).','Since NaCl is a non-volatile solute, it will reduce vapour pressure of the solution. Hence, vapour pressure of pure water in container A is more than in container B.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Vapour pressure in container (A) is more than that in container (B).','Vapour pressure in container (A) is less than that in container (B).','Vapour pressure is equal in both the containers.','Vapour pressure in container (B) is twice the vapour pressure in container (A).','NEW','12-049','12-015','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/161222q.png'),
(161223,'chemistry','If two liquids A and B form minimum boiling azeotrope at some specific composiiion’hien','A-B interactions are weaker than those between A-A or B-B.','If A-B interactions < A-A or B-B the vapour pressure will be more and the result will be positive deviation. The solutions which show positive deviation form minimum boiling azeotropes.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','A-B interactions are stronger than those between A-A or B-B.','vapour pressure of solution increases because more number of molecules of liquids A and B can escape from the solution.','vapour pressure of solution decreases because less number of molecules of only one of the liquids escape from the solution.','A-B interactions are weaker than those between A-A or B-B.','NEW','12-048','12-014',NULL),
(161224,'chemistry','4 L of 0.02 M aqueous solution of NaCl was diluted by adding one litre of water. The molality of the resultant solution is','0.016','M = n/V =  0.02 = n/4 or n = 0.08\nm = n / Mass of water in Kg = 0.08/5 = 0.016','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','0.004','0.008','0.012','0.016','NEW','12-039','12-005',NULL),
(161225,'chemistry','On the basis of information given below, mark the correct option. Information: On adding acetone to methanol some of the hydrogen bonds between methanol molecules break.','At specific composition methanol-acetone mixture will form minimum boiling azeotrope and will show positive deviation from Raoulf s law.','At specific composition methanol-acetone mixture will show positive deviation from Raoulf s law as it has lesser interactions than methanol- methanol and acetone-acetone interactions. Hence it forms minimum boiling azeotrope.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','At specific composition methanol-acetone mixture will form minimum boiling azeotrope and will show positive deviation from Raoulf s law.','At specific composition methanol-acetone mixture forms maximum boiling azeotrope and will show positive deviation from Raoulf s law.','At specific composition methanol-acetone mixture will form minimum boiling azeotrope and will show-negative deviation from Raoult’s law.','At specific composition methanol-acetone mixture will form maximum boiling azeotrope and will show negative deviation from Raoulf s law.','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(161226,'chemistry','KH value for Ar(g),CO₂(g), HCHO(g) and CH₄(g) are 40.39, 1.67, 1.83 x 10.5 and 0.413 respectively. \nArrange these gases in the order of their increasing solubility.','Ar <  CO₂ < CH₄< HCHO','The higher the value of KH, the lower is the solubility of the gas in the liquid. Hence the order of increasing solubility of the gases will be Ar <  CO₂ < CH₄< HCHO','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Ar > CO₂ > CH₄< HCHO','HCHO< Ar <  CO₂ < CH₄','Ar <  CO₂ < CH₄< HCHO','Ar <  CH₄ < CO₂ < < HCHO','NEW','12-041','12-007',NULL),
(161227,'chemistry','Which of the following factor(s) affect the solubility of a gaseous solute in the fixed volume of liquid solvent?\n(i) Nature of solute (ii) Temperature (iii) Pressure\n(a) (i) and (iii) at’constant T \n(b) (i) and (ii) at constant P\n(c) (ii) and (iii) only \n(d) (iii) only','a,b','At constant temperature, the solubility of a gaseous solute in liquid depends on nature of solute and pressure. At constant pressure, solubility is dependent upon nature of solute and temperature.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','NEW','12-041','12-007',NULL),
(161228,'chemistry','Intermolecular forces between two benzene molecules are nearly of same strength as those between two toluene molecules. For a mixture of benzene and toluene, which of the following are not true?\na. ∆mix H = Zero\nb. ∆mix V = Zero\nc. This will form minimum boiling azeotrope\nd. This will not form ideal solution','c,d','For an ideal solution, and benzene and toluene will form an ideal solution. Also, ideal solutions do not form minimum boiling azeotropes.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(161229,'chemistry','Relative lowering of vapour pressure is a colligative property because\n(a) it depends on the concentration of a non-electrolyte solute in solution and does not depend on the nature of the solute molecules.\n(b) it depends on number of particles of electrolyte solute in solution and does not depend on the nature of the solute particles.\n(c) it depends on the concentration of a non-electrolyte solute in solution as well as on the nature of the solute molecules.\n(d) it depends on the concentration of an electrolyte or a non-electrolyte solute in solution as well as on the nature of solute molecules','a,b','Relative lowering of vapour pressure depends on concentration of non¬electrolyte solute in solution and number of particles of electrolyte solute. It does not depend on nature of solute in both conditions','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','NEW','12-049','12-015',NULL),
(161230,'chemistry','van’t Hoff factor i is given by the expression\na) i = Normal molar mass / Abnormal molar mass\nb) i = Abnormal molar mass/Normal molar mass\nc) i =  Observed colligative property/Calculated colligative property\nd) i =  Calculated colligative property/Observed colligative property','a,c','i = Normal molar mass / Abnormal molar mass or Observed colligative property/Calculated colligative property','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,d','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(161231,'chemistry','Isotonic solutions must have the same\n(a) solute \n(b) density\n(c) elevation in boiling point (d) depression in freezing point','c,d','π = CRT or π = (n/V)RT\nIsotonic solutions must have same osmotic pressure at a given temperature hence must have same volume and number of moles i.e., same molar concentration. Thus, the isotonic solutions have same elevation in boiling point, and depression in freezing point.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','c,d','NEW','12-056','12-022',NULL),
(161232,'chemistry','Which of the following binary mixtures will have same composition in liquid and vapour phase?\n(a) Benzene-Toluene \n(b) Water-Nitric acid\n(c) Water-Ethanol \n(d) 6-Hexane-n-Heptane','b,c','Non-ideal solution mixtures with same composition in liquid and vapour phase are azeotropes. Water-nitric acid has maximum boiling azeotrope and water-ethanol has a minimum boiling azeotrope.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','c,d','NEW','12-049','12-015',NULL),
(161233,'chemistry','In isotonic solutions\n(a) solute and solvent both are same\n(b) osmotic pressure is same\n(c) solute and solvent may or may not be same\n(d) solute is always same solvent may be different','b,c','For isotonic solutions osmotic pressure is same, solute or solvent may not be same.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','c,d','NEW','12-056','12-022',NULL),
(161234,'chemistry','For a binary ideal liquid solution, the variation in total vapour pressure versus composition of solution it given by which of the curves? Refer diagram(n1612)','a,d','Depending on the vapour pressure of the pure component 1 and 2 total vapour pressure over the solution decreases or increases with the increase of their mole fraction of component 1.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,d','NEW','12-047','12-013','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/161234q.png'),
(161235,'chemistry','Colligative properties are observed when\n(a) a non-volatile solid is dissolved in a volatile liquid\n(b) a non-volatile liquid is dissolved in an another volatile liquid\n(c) a gas is dissolved in a non-volatile liquid\n(d) a volatile liquid is dissolved in an another volatile liquid','a,b','Colligative properties are observed when a non-volatile solid or liquid are dissolved in a volatile liquid.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,d','NEW','12-049','12-015',NULL),
(161301,'chemistry','Determine the osmotic pressure of a solution prepared by dissolving 25 mg of K₂SO₄ in 2 litre of water at 25°C, assuming that it is completely dissociated. (C.B.S.E. 2013)','5.23 × 10⁻³ atm','Step I. Calculation of Van’t Hoff factor (i)\nK₂SO₄ dissociates in water as : Refer diagram(ne1614)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','5.23 × 10⁻³ atm','5.23 × 10³ atm','5.23  atm','5.23 ×10⁶ atm','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(161302,'chemistry','Determine the amount of CaCl₂ (i = 2.47) dissolved in 2.5 litre of water such that its osmotic pressure is 0.75 atm at 27°C.','3.42g','We know that,\nπ = i RT\n⇒ π = i RT\n⇒ w = ?\nGiven,\nπ = 0.75 atm\nV = 2.5L\ni = 2.47\nT = (27 + 273)K = 300K\nR = 0.0821 L atm /Kmol\nMolar mass of CaCl₂(M) = 1 × 40 + 2 × 35.5 = 111 g /mol \nTherefore, w = 0.75 × 111 × 2.5 / (2.47 × 0.0821 × 300) = 3.42 g\nHence the required amount of CaCl₂ is 3.42g','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','3.42g','2.42g','4.42g','5.42g','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(161303,'chemistry','The air is a mixture of a number of gases. The major components are oxygen and nitrogen with an approximate proportion of 20% is to 79% by volume at 298 K. The water is in equilibrium with air at a pressure of 10 atm. At 298 K if Henry’s law constants for oxygen and nitrogen are 3.30 x 10⁷ mm and 6.51 x 10⁷ mm respectively, calculate the composition of Oxygen in water.','4.65 × 10⁻⁵','Percentage of oxygen (O₂) in air = 20%\nAlso, it is given that water is in equilibrium with air at a total pressure of 10 atm that is, (10 × 760) mm = 7600 mm\nTherefore, partial pressure of oxygen,\nPO₂ = 20/100 x 7600 mm\n= 1520 mm Hg\nNow, according to Henry’s law,\np = KH.χ\nFor oxygen: X(PO₂) = PO₂ /Kh = 1520/3.33 × 10⁷ = 4.65 × 10⁻⁵','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','4.65 × 10⁻⁴','4.65 × 10⁻⁵','4.65 × 10⁻³','4.65 × 10⁻⁷','NEW','12-041','12-007',NULL),
(161304,'chemistry','The air is a mixture of a number of gases. The major components are oxygen and nitrogen with an approximate proportion of 20% is to 79% by volume at 298 K. The water is in equilibrium with air at a pressure of 10 atm. At 298 K if Henry’s law constants for oxygen and nitrogen are 3.30 x 10⁷ mm and 6.51 x 10⁷ mm respectively, calculate the composition of Nitrogen in water.','9.22 × 10⁻⁵','Percentage of nitrogen (N₂) in air = 79%\nAlso, it is given that water is in equilibrium with air at a total pressure of 10 atm that is, (10 × 760) mm = 7600 mm\nNow, according to Henry’s law,\np = KH.χ\nFor oxygen: X(PN₂) = PN₂ /Kh = 6004/6.51 × 10⁷ = 9.22 × 10⁻⁵','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','9.22 × 10⁻⁵','9.22 × 10⁻⁴','9.22 × 10⁻³','9.22','NEW','12-041','12-007',NULL),
(161305,'chemistry','Benzene and toluene form ideal solution over the entire range of composition. The vapour pressure of pure benzene and toluene at 300 K are 50.71 mm Hg and 32.06 mm Hg respectively. Calculate the mole fraction of benzene in vapour phase if 80g of benzene is mixed with 100g of toluene.','0.6','Refer diagram(ne1615)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','0.6','0.5','0.1','0.3','NEW','12-045','12-011',NULL),
