(161317,'chemistry','If the density of lake water is 1.25 g m/L, and it contains 92 g of Na⁺ ions per kg of water, calculate the molality of Na⁺ ions in the lake. (C.B.S.E. Outside Delhi 2008)','4m','Molality of Na⁺ ions(m) = No of moles of Na⁺ ions / Mass of water in Kg = (92/23)/1kg = 4mol/kg = 4m','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','4m','5m','1m','2m','NEW','12-039','12-005',NULL),
(161318,'chemistry','Amongst the following compounds, identify which are insoluble in water?\n(i) phenol\n(ii) toluene\n(iii) formic acid\n(iv) ethylene glycol\n(v) chloroform\n(vi) pentanol','ii & v','(i) Phenol (having polar – OH group) – Partially soluble.\n(ii) Toluene (non-polar) – Insoluble.\n(iii) Formic acid (form hydrogen bonds with water molecules) – Highly soluble.\n(iv) Ethylene glycol (form hydrogen bonds with water molecules) Highly soluble.\n(v) Chloroform (non-polar)- Insoluble.\n(vi) Pentanol (having polar -OH) – Partially soluble.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','i, ii , iii & vi','iv & v','ii & v','iii, iv & vi','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(161319,'chemistry','Based on solute solvent interactions, arrange the following in order of increasing solubility in n-octane. Cyclohexane, KCl, CH₃OH, CH₃CN.','KCl < CH₃OH < CH₃CN < cyclohexane','n-octane (C₈H₁₈) is a non-polar liquid and solubility is governed by the principle that like dissolve like. Keeping this in view, the increasing order of solubility of different solutes is:\nKCl < CH₃OH < CH₃CN < cyclohexane.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','KCl < CH₃OH < CH₃CN < cyclohexane','KCl > CH₃OH > CH₃CN > cyclohexane','KCl > CH₃OH = CH₃CN < cyclohexane','KCl < CH₃OH < CH₃CN > cyclohexane','NEW','12-040','12-006',NULL),
