(161415,'chemistry','A : CuSO₄.5H₂O is a solution of liquid in solid.\nR : Solution is a homogeneous mixture.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','CuSO₄.5H₂O , 5 water molecule is co-ordinated to CuSO4.\nSolution is always homogenious mixture.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','12-035','12-001',NULL),
(161416,'chemistry','A : 1 M solution and 1 molal solution contain same mass of solute.\nR : 1 M and 1 m aqueous solution are equally concentrated.','A is true but R is false','1 molar aqueous solution is less concentrated than 1 molar.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','12-039','12-005',NULL),
(161417,'chemistry','A : Boiling point of water at higher altitude is lower than 100°C.\nR : Boiling point is a colligative property.','A is true but R is false','Yes, external pressure is lower than 1 atm and elevation in boiling point is a colligative property not boiling\n point.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','12-049','12-015',NULL),
(161418,'chemistry','A : van’t Hoff factor for benzoic acid in aqueous medium is 2 assuming complete ionisation.\nR : van’t Hoff factor for 100% ionised solute equal to the number of ions produces.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Refer diagram(ne1639)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A & R are false','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(170209,'chemistry','With reference to the Daniell cell, when Eₑₓₜ > 1.1 V\n(i) Electrons flow from Cu to Zn and current flows from Zn to Cu.\n(ii) Zinc is deposited at the zinc electrode and copper dissolves at the copper electrode.','Both statements are correct','Current flows opposite direction of electron flow. So electrons flow from Cu rod to Zn rod hence current flows from Zn to Cu. Zinc is deposited at the zinc electrode and copper dissolves at the copper electrode.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 66; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrochemical Cells',NULL,'2','mcq','Only statement (i) is correct','Only statement (ii) is correct','Both statements are incorrect','Both statements are correct','NEW','12-066','12-032',NULL),
(170210,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R).\nAssertion: Electrolytic cell uses electrical energy to carry non-spontaneous chemical reactions\nReason: Chemical energy of a spontaneous redox reaction can be converted into electrical energy','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','The electrolytic cell is a device for using electrical energy to carry non-spontaneous chemical reactions. After reaching a cell potential of 1.1 V, any further increase in the external potential of a galvanic cell again starts the reaction but in the opposite direction.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 66; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrochemical Cells',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW','12-066','12-032',NULL),
(170617,'chemistry','As the electrons enter at one end and go out through the other end, the composition of the metallic conductor remains _____.','unchanged','As the electrons enter at one end and go out through the other end, the composition of the metallic conductor remains unchanged.\n \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 77; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'1','fillup','un,chan,ged,cha,ng,ed',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-077','12-043',NULL),
(170618,'chemistry','Even very pure water has small amounts of hydrogen and ____ ions (10⁻⁷M) which lend it very low conductivity (3.5 × 10⁻⁵S m⁻¹).','hydroxyl','Even very pure water has small amounts of hydrogen and hydroxyl ions (~10⁻⁷M) which lend it very low conductivity (3.5 × 10⁻⁵ S m⁻¹).\n \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 77; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','Na⁺','Mg⁺²','hydroxyl','hydronium','NEW','12-077','12-043',NULL),
(170619,'chemistry','When electrolytes are dissolved in water, they furnish their own ions in the solution hence its conductivity ______.','increases','When electrolytes are dissolved in water, they furnish their own ions in the solution hence its conductivity also increases.\n  \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 77; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','increases','decreases','remains same','first increases then decreases','NEW','12-077','12-043',NULL),
(170620,'chemistry','The conductance of electricity by ions present in the solutions is called electrolytic or ____ conductance.','ionic','The conductance of electricity by ions present in the solutions is called electrolytic or ionic conductance. \n \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 77; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'1','fillup','io,nic,cov,ale,nt',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-077','12-043',NULL),
(170621,'chemistry','The conductivity of electrolytic (ionic) solutions depends on:\n\n(i) the nature of the electrolyte added\n\n(ii) size of the ions produced and their solvation\n\n(iii) the nature of the solvent and its viscosity\n\n(iv) volume of the electrolyte\n\n(v) temperature (it increases with the increase of temperature).','(i), (ii), (iii) & (v) only','The conductivity of electrolytic (ionic) solutions depends on:\n\n(i) the nature of the electrolyte added\n\n(ii) size of the ions produced and their solvation\n\n(iii) the nature of the solvent and its viscosity\n\n(iv) concentration of the electrolyte\n\n(v) temperature (it increases with the increase of temperature). \n  \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 77; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii) & (v) only','(i), (ii) & (iii) only','(i), (ii), (iv) & (v) only','(i), (ii), (iii) & (v) only','NEW','12-077','12-043',NULL),
(170622,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion and Reason:\n\nAssertion : The electrical resistance of any object increases with increase in its length\n\nReason : Electrical resistance of any object increases with increase in its area of cross section','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Electrical resistance of any object decreases with increase in its area of cross section\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 75; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW','12-075','12-041',NULL),
(170623,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion and Reason.\n\nAssertion : The conductivity of electrolytic solutions increases with increase in temperature\n\nReason : Electronic conductance decreases with increase in temperature','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','The conductivity of electrolytic solutions increases with an increase in temperature as ionization increases\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 77; Paragraph 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW','12-077','12-043',NULL),
(170624,'chemistry','What is the order of conductivity S/m of these conductors?','Ag > Cu > Au >Na','Conductivity values for the given materials are:\n\nSodium 2.1×10³\n\nCopper 5.9×10³\n\nSilver 6.2×10³\n\nGold 4.5×10³\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 76; Table No. 3.2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'3','order','1,Ag','2,Cu','3,Au','4,Na','NEW','12-076','12-042',NULL),
(170711,'chemistry','What is the order of limiting molar conductivity of these ions in water?','H⁺ > Ca⁺² > Mg⁺² > K⁺','Limiting molar conductivity of these ions are:\n\nH⁺ 349.6 \n\nK⁺ 73.5 \n\nCa⁺² 119.0 \n\nMg⁺²= 106.0\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 83; Table No. 3.4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of the Conductivity of Ionic Solutions',NULL,'3','order','1,H⁺','2,Ca⁺²','3,Mg⁺²','4,K⁺','NEW','12-083','12-049',NULL),
(170813,'chemistry','The molar conductivity of 0.025 mol/L methanoic acid is 46.1 S cm²/ mol. \n\nCalculate its degree of dissociation and dissociation constant. \n\nGiven  Λ ⁰ (H⁺) = 349.6 S cm²/ mol and Λ ⁰  (HCOO⁻) = 54.6 S cm²/ mol.','0.114 and 3.67 x 10⁻⁴ mol/L','We are given,\n\n Λ ⁰  for (H⁺)=349.6 S cm² /mol\n\n Λ ⁰  for (HCOO ⁻) = 54.6 S cm ² /mol\n\n Λ ⁰ₘ (HCOOH)=  Λ ⁰ (H⁺) +  Λ ⁰  (HCOO⁻ )\n\n=349.6+54.6=404.2 S cm² /mol\n\nDegree of dissociation (α)= Λₘ /  Λ ⁰ ₘ = 46.1/404.2 =0.114\n\nCalculation of dissociation constant :- \n\nHCOOH⇌HCOO⁻ + H⁺ \n\n      C             0              0\n\n C−α             Cα          Cα\n\nK= Ca² /1−α =  0.025×(0.114) ²/ 1−0.114 =3.67×10⁻⁴  \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 85; Example 3.9)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Variation of Conductivity and Molar Conductivity with Concentration',NULL,'3','mcq','0.114 and 36.7 x 10⁻⁴ mol/L','0.114 and 367 x 10⁻⁴ mol/L','0.15 and 0.367 x 10⁻⁴ mol/L','0.114 and 3.67 x 10⁻⁴ mol/L','NEW','12-085','12-051',NULL),
(170814,'chemistry','What is the degree of dissociation at infinite dilution?','1','At infinite dilution (concentration c tends to zero) electrolyte dissociates completely (degree of dissociation =1),\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 83; Paragraph 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Variation of Conductivity and Molar Conductivity with Concentration',NULL,'2','mcq','0.5','1','0','0.25','NEW','12-083','12-049',NULL),
(170815,'chemistry','At any concentration c, the degree of dissociation (α ) is related to molar conductivity Λ ₘ at that concentration, and limiting molar conductivity Λ ⁰ₘ is expressed by which of the following relations?','α  = Λ ₘ  / Λ ⁰ₘ','At any concentration c, the degree of dissociation (α ) is related to molar conductivity Λ ₘ at that concentration, and limiting molar conductivity Λ ⁰ₘ is expressed by:\n\nα  = Λ ₘ  / Λ ⁰ₘ \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 84; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Variation of Conductivity and Molar Conductivity with Concentration',NULL,'1','mcq','α  = Λ ⁰ₘ','α  = Λ ₘ','α  = Λ ₘ  / Λ ⁰ₘ','α  = Λ ₘ  x Λ ⁰ₘ','NEW','12-084','12-050',NULL),
(2000031767,'physics','Which of the follwoing phenomena support the wave theory of light?<br>(a) Scattering<br>(b) Interference<br>(c) Diffraction<br>(d) Velocity of light in a denser medium is less than the velocity of light in the rarer medium','C','Except scattering, all other phenomena support wave nature of light.','Miscellaneous Problems on Wave Optics','Wave Optics','Easy',NULL,'$a,b,c$','$a,b,d$','$b,c,d$','$a,c,d$',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(170816,'chemistry','The conductivity of 0.20 M solution of KCl at 298 K is 0.0248S/cm . \n\nCalculate its molar conductivity.','124 S cm²/mol','k (Conductivity) =0.0248 S/cm\n\nC (molarity) =0.20 M\n\nT (temperature) =298 K\n\n Λ ₘ =? (molar conductivity)\n\n Λ ₘ =  1000 x k/C \n\n Therefore  Λ ₘ =    124 S cm²/mol\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 93; Exercise No. 3.8)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Variation of Conductivity and Molar Conductivity with Concentration',NULL,'2','mcq','124 S cm²/mol','0.124 S cm²/mol','1.24 S cm²/mol','1224 S cm²/mol','NEW','12-079','12-045',NULL),
(170817,'chemistry','The resistance of a conductivity cell containing 0.001 M KCI solution at 298 K is 1500 Ω \n\nWhat is the cell constant if conductivity of 0.001 M KCI solution at 298 K is 0.146 x 10⁻³ S/cm?','0.219/cm','cell constant = conductivity x resistance \n\n= 0.146 x 10⁻³ S/cm x 1500\n\n= 0.219/cm\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 93; Exercise No. 3.9)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Variation of Conductivity and Molar Conductivity with Concentration',NULL,'2','mcq','219/cm','0.219/cm','2.19/cm','21.9/cm','NEW','12-079','12-045',NULL),
(170818,'chemistry','Conductivity of 0.00241 M acetic acid is 7.896 x 10⁻⁵ S/cm. \n\nCalculate its molar conductivity. \n\nIf Λ ⁰ₘ, for acetic acid is 390.5 S cm²/ mol, what is its dissociation constant?','1.86 x 10⁻⁵','Given, k = 7.896 x 10⁻⁵ S/cm\n\nC =  0.00241 M/L\n\n Λ ₘ = k x 1000/C \n\n= 32.76 cm\n\nα =  Λ ₘ/Λ ⁰ₘ = 8.4 x 10⁻²\n\nK = Cα ²/1 - α \n\n= 1.86 x 10⁻⁵\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 94; Exercise No. 3.11)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Variation of Conductivity and Molar Conductivity with Concentration',NULL,'2','mcq','1.86 x 10⁻⁵','186 x 10⁻⁵','18.6 x 10⁻⁵','0.186 x 10⁻⁵','NEW','12-084','12-050',NULL),
(170819,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion and Reason.\n\nAssertion : Molar conductivity increases with decrease in concentration\n\nReason : Conductivity always decreases with decrease in concentration','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Molar conductivity increases with decrease in concentration. \n\nDecrease in k (conductivity) on dilution of a solution is more than compensated by increase in its volume.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 81; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Variation of Conductivity and Molar Conductivity with Concentration',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW','12-081','12-047',NULL),
(170820,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion and Reason.\n\nAssertion: Kohlrausch\'s law helps to find the molar conductivity of weak electrolytes at infinite dilution\n\nReason : Molar conductivity of weak electrolytes at infinite dilution cannot be determined experimentally','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','At very low concentrations the conductivity of the solution is so low that it cannot be measured accurately, hence kohlrausch\'s law of independent migration ions is used.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 83; Paragraph 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Variation of Conductivity and Molar Conductivity with Concentration',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW','12-083','12-049',NULL),
(170908,'chemistry','How many moles of the electron are required for the reduction of one mole of silver ions?','One','One mole of the electron is required for the reduction of one mole of silver ions.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 86; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'1','mcq','One','Two','Three','Zero','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(170909,'chemistry','One Faraday is equal to','96500 C mol⁻¹','One Faraday is approximately equal to 96500 C mol⁻¹\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 86; Paragraph 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'2','mcq','96500 C mol⁻¹','0.0821 C mol⁻¹','8.314 C mol⁻¹','6.023 X 10²³ C mol⁻¹','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(170910,'chemistry','In commercial production of metals, current as high as  ___ thousands amperes are used that amounts to about 0.518 F per second.','fifty','In commercial production of metals, current as high as 50,000 amperes are used that amounts to about 0.518 F per second.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 86; Paragraph 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'1','fillup','fif,ty,ni,ne,ty',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(170911,'chemistry','A solution of CuSO₄ is electrolysed for 10 minutes with a current of 1.5 amperes. \n\nWhat is the mass of copper deposited at the cathode?','0.2938 g','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 86; Example No. 3.10)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'2','mcq','2938 g','0.2938 g','29.38 g','293.8 g','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(170912,'chemistry','What do products of electrolysis depend on?\n\na) Nature of material used\n\nb) Type of electrodes\n\nc) Density of the solution','Only a and b','Products of electrolysis depend on the nature of the material being electrolyzed and the type of electrodes being used.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 86; Paragraph 5)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and b','Only b and c','Only a and c','All of these','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(170913,'chemistry','______ electrode acts only as source or sink for electrons','Inert','If the electrode is inert (e.g., platinum or gold), it does not participate in the chemical reaction and acts only as source or sink for electrons.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 86; Paragraph 5)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'1','fillup','In,ert,re,ac,tive',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(170914,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct?\n\na. The products of electrolysis depend on the different oxidizing and reducing species present in the electrolytic cell and their standard electrode potentials.\n\nb. Some of the electrochemical processes although feasible, are so slow kinetically that at lower voltages these do not seem to take place and extra potential (called overpotential) has to be applied, which makes the such process more difficult to occur.\n\nc. The products of electrolysis do not depend on the density of the solution.','All of these','The products of electrolysis depend on the different oxidizing and reducing species present in the electrolytic cell and their standard electrode potentials. \n\nMoreover, some of the electrochemical processes although feasible, are so slow kinetically that at lower voltages these do not seem to take place and extra potential (called overpotential) has to be applied, which makes the such process more difficult to occur.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 87; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'3','mcq','Only a and b','Only b and c','Only a and c','All of these','NEW','12-087','12-053',NULL),
(170915,'chemistry','What are the products of electrolysis when we use molten NaCl ?','Sodium metal and chlorine gas','If we use molten NaCl, the products of electrolysis are sodium metal and Chlorine gas.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 87; Paragraph 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'1','mcq','Oxygen and hydrogen gas','Sodium metal and chlorine gas','Both of these','None of these','NEW','12-087','12-053',NULL),
(170916,'chemistry','What are the products of electrolysis when we use aqueous NaCl solution?','NaOH, chlorine gas and hydrogen gas','The products of electrolysis when we use aqueous NaCl solution are NaOH, chlorine gas, and hydrogen gas. \n\nThe reaction with a higher value of E⁻ is preferred.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 87; Paragraph 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'1','mcq','Na metal and chlorine gas','NaOH, chlorine gas and hydrogen gas','Both of these','None of these','NEW','12-087','12-053',NULL),
(170917,'chemistry','What are the products obtained at anode on electrolysis of dilute sulphuric acid using platinum electrode?','Oxygen','During the electrolysis of  dilute sulphuric acid, the following processes are possible at the anode:\n\n2H₂O(l ) → O₂(g) + 4H⁺(aq) + 4e⁻\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 87; Paragraph 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'1','mcq','Hydrogen','Oxygen','Marsh gas','None of these','NEW','12-087','12-053',NULL),
(170918,'chemistry','What are the product obtained at anode on electrolysis of highly concentrated sulphuric acid using platinum electrodes?','S₂O₈⁻² ions','At higher concentrations of sulphuric acid, the following reaction preferred: \n\n2SO₄ ⁻² (aq) → S₂O₈ ⁻² (aq) + 2e⁻\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 88; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'1','mcq','Hydrogen gas','Oxygen gas','S₂O₈⁻² ions','None of these','NEW','12-088','12-054',NULL),
(170919,'chemistry','Consider the reaction: Cr₂O₇⁻² + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr⁺³  + 7H₂O\n\nWhat is the quantity of electricity in coulombs needed to reduce 1 mol of Cr₂O₇⁻²?','5.79×10⁵ C','The valency factor for Chromium is 6. \n\nApplying faraday\'s first law-\n\nQ/F = moles x 6\n\nQ=1×6×96500\n\nQ = 5.79×10⁵ C\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 88; Example 3.12)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'2','mcq','579×10⁵ C','5.79×10⁵ C','57.9×10⁵ C','0.579×10⁵ C','NEW','12-088','12-054',NULL),
(170920,'chemistry','How much charge is required for the following reduction:\n\n1 mole of Al⁺³  to Al','289500 C','The electrode reaction is  Al⁺³ + 3e →Al\n\n∴ Quantity of charge required for reduction of 1 mol of   Al⁺³ =3F=3 x 96500 C=289500C\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 93; Exercise No. 3.12)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'2','mcq','289500 C','28950.0 C','289.500 C','28.9500 C','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(170921,'chemistry','How much charge is required for the following reduction:\n\n1 mole of Cu⁺² to Cu','193000 C','The electrode reaction is Cu⁺² + 2e⁻ → Cu\n\n∴ Quantity of charge required for reduction of 1 mol of Cu⁺²=2F=2 x 96500=193000 C\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 93; Exercise No. 3.12)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'2','mcq','289500C','193000 C','28950.0C','19300 C','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(170922,'chemistry','How much charge is required for the following reduction;\n\n1 mole MnO₄⁻ to Mn⁺²','482500 C','The electrode reaction is MnO₄⁻  → Mn⁺²\n\ni.e., Mn⁺⁷ + 5e⁻ → Mn⁺².\n\n∴ Quantity of charge required = 5F\n\n=5 x 96500 C=482500\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 93; Exercise No. 3.12)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'2','mcq','193000 C','289500C','482500 C','48250 C','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(170923,'chemistry','How much electricity in terms of Faraday is required to produce:\n\n20·0 g of Ca from molten CaCl₂','1 F','Ca⁺² +2e⁻  →Ca\n\n1 mole (40 g) of Ca will require 2 F of electricity.\n\n20 g (0.5 mole) of Ca will require 1 F of electricity\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 93; Exercise No. 3.13)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'2','mcq','1 F','2 F','3 F','4 F','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(170924,'chemistry','How much electricity in terms of Faraday is required to produce:\n\n40·0 g of Al from molten Al₂O₃','4.4 F','Al⁺³ + 3e⁻  → Al\n\n1 mole (27 g) of Al will require 3 moles of electrons.\n\n40 g of Al will require = 3 x 40 / 27 = 4.4 F\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 93; Exercise No. 3.13)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'2','mcq','6 F','3 F','4 F','4.4 F','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(170925,'chemistry','How much electricity is required in coulomb for the oxidation of 1 mol of H₂O to O₂','193000 C','H₂O  → 1/2 O₂ + 2H⁺² +2e⁻\n\nOxidation of one mole of water will require 2×96500=193000 C\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 93; Exercise No. 3.14)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'2','mcq','19300 C','289500C','4825000 C','193000 C','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(170926,'chemistry','How much electricity is required in coulomb for the oxidation of 1 mol of FeO to Fe₂O₃','96500 C','Fe⁺²   → Fe⁺³ +1e⁻\n\nOxidation of one mole of Fe⁺² will require 1×96500=96500 C.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 93; Exercise No. 3.14','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'2','mcq','965000 C','96500 C','19300 C','289500 C','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(170927,'chemistry','If a current of 0.5 ampere flows through a metallic wire for 2 hours, then how many electrons would flow through the wire?','2.22 x 10²²','Q=It \n\nWhere Q= no. of electron\n\nI= current\n\nt= time taken\n\nQ=0.5A×72005\n\n=3600C\n\nAs 96487C=6.023×10²³  no. of electron\n\n3600C = 6.023 x 10²³ x 3600/96487 = 2.22 x 10²² no. of electrons\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 88; Example 3.10)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'2','mcq','22.2 x 10²²','222 x 10²²','2.22 x 10²²','0.222 x 10²²','NEW','12-088','12-054',NULL),
(170928,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion and Reason.\n\nAssertion: In electrolysis, the quantity of electricity needed for depositing 1 mole of silver is different from that required for 1 mole of Cu\n\nReason : Molecular weight of silver and copper are different','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','1 mole of electrons are needed to deposit 1 mole of Ag while 2 mole for Cu, It depends on the charge of the ions\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 86; Paragraph 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(170929,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion and Reason.\n\nAssertion: In electrolysis of aqueous NaCl the product obtained is hydrogen gas\n\nReason : Gases are liberated faster than metals','Assertion is true but Reason is false','The reaction with higher electrode potential is preferred hence instead of Na⁺ getting reduced to sodium metal, H⁺ gets reduced\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 87; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW','12-087','12-053',NULL),
(171013,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct?\n\na. The water vapours produced during the reaction in hydogren-oxygen fuel cell in Apollo space programme were condensed and added to the drinking water supply for the astronauts\n\nb. In hydrogen-oxygen fuel cell, hydrogen and oxygen are bubbled through porous carbon electrodes into concentrated aqueous sodium hydroxide solution.','Both and b','The water vapours produced during the reaction were condensed and added to the drinking water supply for the astronauts. \n\nIn the cell, hydrogen and oxygen are bubbled through porous carbon electrodes into concentrated aqueous sodium hydroxide solution.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 90; Paragraph 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Both and b','None of these','NEW','12-090','12-056',NULL),
(171014,'chemistry','Which of the following catalysts are used in hydrogen-oxygen fuel cell?','Pt and Pd','Catalysts like finely divided platinum or palladium metal are incorporated into the electrodes for increasing the rate of electrode reactions.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 90; Paragraph 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'1','mcq','Pt and Pd','Ni and Cd','Ni and He','He and Ar','NEW','12-090','12-056',NULL),
(171015,'chemistry','What is the efficiency of fuel cells?','70 %','Fuel cells produce electricity with an efficiency of about 70 % compared to thermal plants whose efficiency is about 40%.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 90; Paragraph 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'1','mcq','70 %','40 %','100 %','50 %','NEW','12-090','12-056',NULL),
(171016,'chemistry','What is the efficiency of thermal plants?','40 %','Fuel cells produce electricity with an efficiency of about 70 % compared to thermal plants whose efficiency is about 40%.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 91; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'1','mcq','70 %','40 %','100 %','50 %','NEW','12-091','12-057',NULL),
(171017,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct?\n\na. Better catalysts and electrolytes increase the efficiency of fuel cells\n\nb. Fuel cells are pollution-free\n\nc. Fuel cells are the alternative of energy sources','All of these','There has been tremendous progress in the development of new electrode materials, better catalysts, and electrolytes for increasing the efficiency of fuel cells. \n\nThese have been used in automobiles on an experimental basis. \n\nFuel cells are pollution free and in view of their future importance, a variety of fuel cells have been fabricated and tried.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 91; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'3','mcq','Only a and b','Only b and c','Only a and c','All of these','NEW','12-091','12-057',NULL),
(171018,'chemistry','Which of the following is an example of corrosion?','All of these','Corrosion slowly coats the surfaces of metallic objects with oxides or other salts of the metal. \n\nThe rusting of iron, tarnishing of silver, and development of green coating on copper and bronze are some examples of corrosion.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 91; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'1','mcq','The rusting of iron','Tarnishing of silver','Development of green coating on copper','All of these','NEW','12-091','12-057',NULL),
(171019,'chemistry','The chemistry of corrosion is quite complex but it may be considered essentially as an _____ phenomenon.','electrochemical','The chemistry of corrosion is quite complex but it may be considered essentially as an electrochemical phenomenon.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 91; Paragraph 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'1','fillup','el,ec,tro,che,mical,ph,ys,ical',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-091','12-057',NULL),
(171020,'chemistry','Which of the following is rust?','Fe₂O₃.xH₂O','Hydrated ferric oxide (Fe₂O₃.xH₂O)\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 91; Paragraph 5)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'1','mcq','Fe₃O₄.xH₂O','Fe₂O₃.xH₂O','Both of these','None of these','NEW','12-091','12-057',NULL),
(171021,'chemistry','Which of the following methods are used to prevent corrosion?','All of these','One of the simplest methods of preventing corrosion is to prevent the surface of the metallic object to come in contact with the atmosphere. \n\nThis can be done by covering the surface with paint or by some chemicals (e.g. bisphenol). \n\nAnother simple method is to cover the surface with other metals (Sn, Zn, etc.) that are inert or react to save the object. \n\nAn electrochemical method is to provide a sacrificial electrode of another metal (like Mg, Zn, etc.) that corrodes itself but saves the object.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 91; Paragraph 6)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'2','mcq','Painting','Cover the surface by other metals','Provide a sacrificial electrode of another metal','All of these','NEW','12-091','12-057',NULL),
(171022,'chemistry','Which of the following metals are used to cover a surface to prevent corrosion?','Zn','To prevent corrosion, simple method is to cover the surface by other metals (Sn, Zn, etc.)\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 91; Paragraph 6)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'1','mcq','Zn','Ca','Na','K','NEW','12-091','12-057',NULL),
(171023,'chemistry','Which of the following metals are used as sacrificial electrodes to  prevent corrosion?','Mg','An electrochemical method is to provide a sacrificial electrode of another metal (like Mg, Zn, etc.) which corrodes itself but saves the object\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 91; Paragraph 6)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'1','mcq','Na','Ca','Mg','Sn','NEW','12-091','12-057',NULL),
(171024,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion and Reason.\n\nAssertion: In mercury cell, the cell potential is approximately 1.35 V and remains constant in its life\n\nReason : The overall reaction in mercury cell is represented as \n\nZn(Hg) + HgO (s) → ZnO (s) + HgO (l)','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Overall reaction does not involve any ion in solution whose concentration can change during its life time\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 89; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW','12-089','12-055',NULL),
(171105,'chemistry','Standard electrode potential for Sn⁺⁴/Sn⁺² couple is +0.15 V and that for the Cr⁺³/Cr couple is –0.74 V. \n\nThese two couples in their standard state are connected to make a cell. \n\nThe cell potential will be','0.89 V','E°cell = E°cathode – E°anode\n\n= 0.15 – (– 0.74) = 0.15 + 0.74 = 0.89 V\n\n(NEET 2011)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.89 V','1.19 V','0.18 V','1.83 V','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171153,'chemistry','When CuSO₄ is electrolysed using platinum electrodes,','copper is liberated at cathode, oxygen at anode','CuSO₄ ⇌ Cu⁺² + SO₄⁻²\n\nH₂O ⇌ H⁺ + OH ⁻\n\nAt cathode: Cu⁺² + 2e⁻ → Cu\n\nAt anode: 4OH⁻  → 2H₂O + O₂ + 4e⁻\n \n(NEET 1993)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','copper is liberated at cathode, sulphur at anode','copper is liberated at cathode, oxygen at anode','sulphur is liberated at cathode, oxygen at anode','oxygen is liberated at cathode, copper at anode','NEW','12-087','12-053',NULL),
(171154,'chemistry','On electrolysis of dilute sulphuric acid using platinum electrodes, the product obtained at the anode will be','oxygen','During electrolysis of dilute sulphuric acid, product obtained at anode will be oxygen.\n\nAt anode: 4OH⁻  → 2H₂O + O₂ + 4e⁻ \n\n(NEET 1992)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','hydrogen','oxygen','hydrogen sulphide','sulphur dioxide','NEW','12-087','12-053',NULL),
(171155,'chemistry','A device that converts energy of combustion of fuels like hydrogen and methane, directly into electrical energy is known as','fuel cell','Fuel cell converts energy of combustion of fuels like hydrogen and methane, directly into electrical energy.\n\n(NEET 2015)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','dynamo','Ni-Cd cell','fuel cell','electrolytic cell','NEW','12-090','12-056',NULL),
(171156,'chemistry','The efficiency of a fuel cell is given by','ΔG/ΔH','The thermal efficiency, η of a fuel conversion device is the amount of useful energy produced relative to the change in enthalpy, ΔH between the product and feed streams\n\nη =useful energy /Δ H\n\nIn an ideal case of an electrochemical converter, such as a fuel cell, the change in Gibbs free energy, ΔG of the reaction is available as useful electric energy at that temperature of the conversion\n\nHence, η =  ΔG/ΔH\n\n(NEET 2007)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','ΔG/ΔS','ΔG/ΔH','ΔS/ΔH','ΔH/ΔG','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(171157,'chemistry','Zinc can be coated on iron to produce galvanized iron but the reverse is not possible. It is because','zinc has higher negative electrode potential than iron','Reduction potential values of E° Zn⁺²/Zn = –0.76 V \n\nand E° Fe⁺²/Fe = -0.44 V\n\nThus, due to the higher negative electrode potential value of zinc than iron, iron cannot be coated on zinc.\n\n(NEET-II 2016)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','zinc is lighter than iron','zinc has lower melting point than iron','zinc has lower negative electrode potential than iron','zinc has higher negative electrode potential than iron','NEW','12-091','12-057',NULL),
(171158,'chemistry','The most convenient method to protect the bottom of ship made of iron is','white tin plating','The most convenient method to protect the bottom of the ship made of iron is white tin plating preventing the build up of barnacles\n\n(NEET 2001)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','coating it with red lead oxide','white tin plating','connecting it with Mg bloc','connecting it with Pb block','NEW','12-091','12-057',NULL),
(171159,'chemistry','To protect iron against corrosion, the most durable metal plating on it is','zinc plating','To protect iron against corrosion, the most durable metal plating on it is zinc plating.\n\n(NEET 1994)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','copper plating','zinc plating','nickel plating','tin plating','NEW','12-091','12-057',NULL),
(171161,'chemistry','The molar conductivity of 0.007 M acetic acid is 20 S cm² mol⁻¹. \n\nWhat is the dissociation constant of acetic acid? \n\nChoose the correct option.\n\nᴧ⁰ₕ+ = 350 S cm² mol⁻¹\n\nᴧ⁰CH₃COO⁻ = 50 S cm² mol⁻¹','1.75 × 10⁻⁵ mol L⁻¹','ᴧ⁰ₘ₍Cₕ₃Cₒₒₕ₎ = ᴧ⁰ₘ₍ₕ+₎ + ᴧ⁰ₘ₍Cₕ₃Cₒₒ-₎ =350 + 50 = 400 Scm²mol⁻¹\n\nα = ᴧᶜₘ/ᴧ⁰ₘ = 20/400 = 5 x 10⁻²\n\nKₐ(CH₃COOH) = Cα² = 0.007 x (5x 10⁻²)² = 1.75 x 10⁻⁵ mol L⁻¹\n\n(NEET 2021)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2.75 × 10⁻⁴ mol L⁻¹','1.75 × 10⁻⁴ mol L⁻¹','1.75 × 10⁻⁵ mol L⁻¹','2.75 × 10⁻⁵ mol L⁻¹','NEW','12-084','12-050',NULL),
(171162,'chemistry','At 298 K, the standard electrode potentials of Cu²⁺ / Cu, Zn²⁺ / Zn, Fe²⁺ / Fe and Ag⁺ / Ag are 0.34 V, –0.76 V, –0.44 V, and 0.80 V, respectively. \n\nOn the basis of standard electrode potential, predict which of the following reaction cannot occur?','2CuSO₄(aq) + 2Ag(s) → 2Cu(s) + Ag₂SO₄(aq)','According to the electrochemical series, more reactive element can displace the less reactive element .\nOrder of reactivity: Zn > Fe > Cu > Ag\n\nTherefore, Ag being less reactive cannot displace Cu from CuSO₄ solution. \n(NEET 2022)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CuSO₄(aq) + Zn(s) → ZnSO₄(aq) + Cu(s)','CuSO₄(aq) + Fe(s) → FeSO₄(aq) + Cu(s)','FeSO₄(aq) + Zn(s) → ZnSO₄(aq) + Fe(s)','2CuSO₄(aq) + 2Ag(s) → 2Cu(s) + Ag₂SO₄(aq)','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171163,'chemistry','Match the following.','Li-electrochemical cells, Na-coolant in fast breeder reactors, KOH-absorbent for carbon dioxide, Cs-photoelectric cell','• Cs is used in the photoelectric cell due to its low ionisation enthalpy\n\n• KOH used to adsorb CO₂ and changes into K₂CO₃\n\n• Liquid sodium metal is used as a coolant in fast breeder nuclear reactor\n\n• Lithium is used in electrochemical cells\n\n(NEET 2022)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','match','Li,electrochemical cells','Na,coolant in fast breeder reactors','KOH,absorbent for carbon dioxide','Cs,photoelectric cell','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(171164,'chemistry','Given below are half-cell reactions:\n\nMnO₄⁻+ 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O\n\nE⁰(Mn²⁺/MnO₄⁻) = -1.51 V\n\n H₂O → 1/2 O₂ + 2H⁺ + 2e⁻\n\nE⁰(O₂/H₂O) = +1.223 V\n\nWill the permanganate ion, MnO₄⁻liberate O₂ from water in the presence of an acid?','Yes, because E⁰𝒸ₑₗₗ = + 0.287 V','• MnO₄⁻+ 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O ........(i)\n\n  E⁰(MnO₄⁻/Mn²⁺) = -E⁰(Mn²⁺/MnO₄⁻) = 1.51 V\n\n• H₂O → 1/2 O₂ + 2H⁺ + 2e⁻.........(ii)\n\nE⁰(O₂/H₂O) = 1.223 V\n\nUsing 2x(i) + 5x(ii), net cell reaction is \n\n2MnO₄⁻ + 6H⁺ → 2Mn²⁺ + 5/2O₂ + 3H₂O\n\nE⁰𝒸ₑₗₗ = E⁰𝒸 - E⁰ₐ = E⁰(MnO₄⁻/Mn²⁺) - E⁰(O₂/H₂O) = 1.51 - 1.223 = 0.287 V\n\nSince, E⁰𝒸ₑₗₗ > 0, therefore net cell reaction is spontaneous and so MnO₄⁻ liberates O₂ from H₂O in presence of an acid\n\n(NEET 2022)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Yes, because E⁰𝒸ₑₗₗ = + 0.287 V','No, because E⁰𝒸ₑₗₗ = – 0.287 V','Yes, because E⁰𝒸ₑₗₗ = + 2.733 V','No, because E⁰𝒸ₑₗₗ = − 2.733 V','NEW','12-072','12-038',NULL),
(171165,'chemistry','Find the emf of the cell in which the following reaction takes place at 298 K\n\nNi(s) + 2Ag⁺ (0.001M) → Ni²⁺ (0.001M) + 2Ag(s)\n\n(Given that, E⁰𝒸ₑₗₗ = 10.5 V, 2.303RT/F = 0.059 at 298 K)','10.4115 V','(NEET 2022) \ndiagram(ne1709)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','10.4115 V','1.385 V','0.9615 V','1.05 V','NEW','12-072','12-038',NULL),
(171166,'chemistry','Limiting molar conductivity of NH₄OH [i.e., ᴧ°ₘ(NH₄OH)] is equal to','ᴧ°ₘ(NH₄Cl) + ᴧ°ₘ(NaOH) – ᴧ°ₘ(NaCl)','ᴧ°ₘ(NH₄OH) = ᴧ°ₘ(NH₄Cl) + ᴧ°ₘ(NaOH) – ᴧ°ₘ(NaCl)\n\n(NEET 2012)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','ᴧ°ₘ(NH₄Cl) + ᴧ°ₘ(NaCl) – ᴧ°ₘ(NaOH)','ᴧ°ₘ(NaOH) + ᴧ°ₘ(NaCl) – ᴧ°ₘ(NH₄Cl)','ᴧ°ₘ(NH₄OH) + ᴧ°ₘ(NH₄Cl) – ᴧ°ₘ(HCl)','ᴧ°ₘ(NH₄Cl) + ᴧ°ₘ(NaOH) – ᴧ°ₘ(NaCl)','NEW','12-083','12-049',NULL),
(171167,'chemistry','Which of the following expressions correctly represents the equivalent conductance at infinite dilution of Al₂(SO₄)₃? \n\nGiven that ᴧ°(Al³⁺) and ᴧ°(SO₄²⁻) are equivalent conductances at infinite dilution of the respective ions.','ᴧ°(Al³⁺) + ᴧ°(SO₄²⁻)','At infinite dilution, when dissociation is complete, each ion makes a definite contribution toward molar conductance of the electrolyte irrespective of the nature of the other ion with which it is associated.\n\nHence, ᴧ°Al₂(SO₄)₃ = ᴧ°(Al³⁺) + ᴧ°(SO₄²⁻)\n\n(NEET 2010)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','2ᴧ°(Al³⁺) + 3ᴧ°(SO₄²⁻)','ᴧ°(Al³⁺) + ᴧ°(SO₄²⁻)','[ᴧ°(Al³⁺) + ᴧ°(SO₄²⁻)] x 6','1/3 ᴧ°(Al³⁺) + 1/2 ᴧ°(SO₄²⁻)','NEW','12-083','12-049',NULL),
(171168,'chemistry','The concentration of Zinc chloride solution will change when it is placed in a container which is made of :','Al','In electrochemical series, Al is below Zn. Hence, Al have tendency to reduce Zn2+ into Zn and it self oxidised into Al3+.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Al','Cu','Ag','None','NEW','12-070','12-036',NULL),
(171169,'chemistry','The molar conductance of NaCl, HCl, CH3COOH, at infinite dilution are 126, 45,426, 16, and 91 S cm/mol .the molar conductance of CH3COOH at infine dilution will be:','390.71 S cm2/mol','Step 1:\n\nFrom Kohlrausch Law,\n\nΛ\no\nm\n(\nHCl\n)\n=\nΛ\no\nm\n(\nH\n+\n)\n \n+\n \nΛ\no\nm\n(\nCl\n−\n)\n=\n426\n.\n16\n    \n.\n.\n.\n.\n1\n\nΛ\no\nm\n(\nNaCl\n)\n=\nΛ\no\nm\n(\nNa\n+\n)\n \n+\n \nΛ\no\nm\n(\nCl\n−\n)\n=\n126\n.\n45\n  \n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\n2\n\nΛ\no\nm\n(\nCH\n3\nCOONa\n)\n=\nΛ\no\nm\n(\nNa\n+\n)\n \n+\n \nΛ\no\nm\n(\nCH\n3\nCOO\n−\n)\n=\n91\n.\n0\n  \n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\n3\nStep 2:\n\nAdd 1 and 3 and subtract 2 and find the value of molar conductance of CH3COOH at infinite dilution .\n\nΛ\no\nm\n(\nCH\n3\nCOOH\n)\n=\nΛ\no\nm\n(\nH\n+\n)\n \n+\n \nΛ\no\nm\n(\nCl\n−\n)\n+\n \nΛ\no\nm\n(\nNa\n+\n)\n \n+\n \nΛ\no\nm\n(\nCH\n3\nCOO\n−\n)\n−\n{\nΛ\no\nm\n(\nNa\n+\n)\n}\n \n+\n \nΛ\no\nm\n(\nCl\n−\n)\n\n=\nΛ\no\nm\n(\nH\n+\n)\n \n+\n \nΛ\no\nm\n(\nCH\n3\nCOO\n−\n)\n\n=\n \n426\n.\n16\n \n+\n \n91\n.\n0\n \n−\n \n126\n.\n45\n\n=\n390\n.\n71\n \nS\n \ncm\n2\n \nmol\n−\n1','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','698.28 S cm2 /mol','540.48 S cm2/mol','201.28 S cm2/mol','390.71 S cm2/mol','NEW','12-083','12-049',NULL),
(171170,'chemistry','Λ°m for NaCl, HCl and NaA are 126.4, 425.9 and\n100.5 S cm²/mol, respectively. If the conductivity\nof 0.001 M HA is 5 × 10⁻⁵ S /cm, degree of\ndissociation of HA is [JEE (Main)-2019]','0.1233','Hint: \nα\n=\nΛ\nm\nΛ\no\nm\n\n\nStep 1: \n\nThe formula for the degree of dissociation is as follows:\n\nα\n=\nΛ\nm\nΛ\no\nm\n\n\nFirst, calculate the value of \nΛ\nm\n is as follows: \n\n∧\nm\n=\n1000\n×\nk\nC\n\n\n∧\nm\n=\n1000\n×\n4.95\n×\n10\n−\n5\n0.001028\n\n=\n48.15\n\n\nStep 2: \n\nCalculate the value \n∧\no\nm\n  for acetic acid is as follows: \n\n∧\no\nm\n(\nC\nH\n3\nC\nO\nO\nH\n)\n=\n∧\no\nm\n(\nC\nH\n3\nC\nO\nO\nN\na\n)\n+\n∧\no\nm\n(\nH\nC\nl\n)\n−\n∧\no\nm\n(\nN\na\nC\nl\n)\n\n=\n91.05\n+\n425.9\n−\n126.4\n\n=\n390.5\n\n\nCalculate the value of the degree of dissociation is as follows:\n\nα\n=\n48.15\n390.5\n\n= 0.1233','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.01233','0.1233','1','1.233','NEW','12-084','12-050',NULL),
(171171,'chemistry','the number of electrons delivered at cathode during electrolysis by a current of 1 ampere in 60 seconds is( charge on electron is 1.602×10 −19 coulombs.','3.75x10^20','3. From Faraday\'s first law of electrolysis, \n\nW/E = it/96500   ......... (i)\n\nGiven i = 1A; t=60s\n\nPutting these values in eq. (i) we get \n\nW/E = 1x60/96500\n\nor W/E = 6/9650 = Number of mole of electrons\n\nNumber of electrons = 6/9650 x 6.022x 1023 = 3.75 x 1020','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','6x10^23','6x10^20','3.75x10^20','7.48 x 10^23','NEW','12-085','12-051',NULL),
(171172,'chemistry','three cells with their Eo(cell)  values are given below: a) FeI Fe+2 II Fe+3 I Fe = 0.404  B) FeI Fe+2 II Fe+3 I Fe+2 I pt = 1.211  C) Fe I Fe+3 II Fe+3 IFe +2 I Pt= 0.807  the standard GIbbs free energy values for three celss are respectively,( F represent the charge on 1 mole of electrons)','-2.424F,-2.422F,-2.421F','Hint: \nΔ\nG\no\n=\n−\nn\nF\nE\no\nc\ne\nl\nl\n\n\n(a) \n\nThe reaction is as follows:\n\nFe → Fe2+ + 2e- \nFe3+ + 3e- → Fe \n\nThe number of electrons transferred is 6. \nΔ\nG\no\n=\n−\n6\n×\nF\n×\n0.404\n \n= -2.424 V \n\n(b) \n\nThe reaction is as follows:\n\nFe → Fe2+ + 2e- \nFe3+ + 1e- → Fe2+  \n\nThe number of electrons transferred is 2. \n \nΔ\nG\no\n=\n−\n2\n×\nF\n×\n1.211\n \n\n= -2.422 V\n\n(c) \nFe → Fe3+ + 3e- \nFe3+ + 1e- → Fe2+ \n\nThe number of electrons transferred is 3.\n\nΔ\nG\no\n=\n−\n3\n×\nF\n×\n0.807\n \n=-2.421 V','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','-1.212F , -1.211F, -0.807F','+2.424F,+2.422F,+2.421F','-0.808F,-2.422F,-2.421F','-2.424F,-2.422F,-2.421F','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171173,'chemistry','the emf of a daniel cell  at 298K is E1 ZnI ZNSO4 (0.01M) II CuSO4 (1.00 M) I Cu . when the concentration of ZnSO4 is 1 M and of CuSO4 is 0.01 M ,the emf is changed to E2. the correct relationship between E1 and E2 is :','E1>E2','HINT: \nE\n=\nE\n0\nc\ne\nl\nl\n−\n0.059\n2\nl\no\ng\n[\nZ\nn\n+\n2\n]\n[\nC\nu\n+\n2\n]\n\n\nExplanation: \n\nSTEP 1:\nCell representation : Zn|ZnSO4(0.01 M) || CuSO4(1.0 M)|Cu\n\nReaction at Anode : \nZ\nn\n(\ns\n)\n→\nZ\nn\n+\n2\n(\na\nq\n)\n+\n2\ne\n−\n\nReaction at Cathode: \nC\nu\n+\n2\n(\na\nq\n)\n+\n2\ne\n−\n→\nC\nu\n(\ns\n)\n\n\nSo, Nernst equation will be : \nE\n=\nE\n0\nc\ne\nl\nl\n−\n0.059\n2\nl\no\ng\n[\nZ\nn\n+\n2\n]\n[\nC\nu\n+\n2\n]\n\n\nSTEP 2 :\n\n⇒ \nE\n1\n=\nE\n0\nc\ne\nl\nl\n−\n0.059\n2\nl\no\ng\n0.01\n1\n\n\n⇒\nE\n1\n=\nE\n0\nc\ne\nl\nl\n+\n0.059\n .............(I)\n\nSimilarly ; \nE\n2\n=\nE\n0\nc\ne\nl\nl\n−\n0.059\n2\nl\no\ng\n1\n0.01\n\n\n⇒ \nE\n2\n=\nE\n0\nc\ne\nl\nl\n−\n0.059\n ............(II)\n\nFrom (I) and (II) we can say that E1 > E2','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','E1>E2','E1<E2','E1=E2','E2=0 ≠E1','NEW','12-072','12-038',NULL),
(171174,'chemistry','in a typical fuel cell ,the reactants R and products P are :','R= H2 g ,O2g , P= H2O (I)','HINT: A fuel cell uses the chemical energy of hydrogen or other fuels to cleanly and efficiently produce electricity. If hydrogen is the fuel, the only products are electricity, water, and heat.\n\nExplanation:\n\nH\n2\n \n⇋\n \n2\nH\nΘ\n \n+\n2\ne\n−\n\n2\nH\nΘ\n \n+\n \n2\nΘ\nO\nH\n \n⇋\n \n2\nH\n2\nO\n\nH\n2\n \n+\n \n2\nO\nH\n \n⇋\n \n2\nH\n2\nO\n \n+\n \n2\ne\n−\n\nA\nt\n \nc\na\nt\nh\no\nd\ne\n,\n \nO\n2\n \ng\ne\nt\ns\n \nr\ne\nd\nu\nc\ne\nd\n \nt\no\n \nΘ\nO\nH\n\ni\n.\ne\n.\n,\n \nO\n2\n \n+\n \n2\nH\n2\nO\n \n+\n \n4\ne\n−\n⇋\n \n4\nΘ\nO\nH\n\nH\ne\nn\nc\ne\n,\n \nt\nh\ne\n \nn\ne\nt\n \nr\ne\na\nc\nt\ni\no\nn\n \ni\ns\n \n2\nH\n2\n \n+\n \nO\n2\n⇋\n \n2\nH\n2\nO\nIn a typical fuel cell\nReactants = H2, O2\nProducts = H2O','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','R= H2 g ,O2g , P= H2O2 (I)','R= H2 g ,O2g , P= H2O (I)','R= H2 g ,O2g , Cl2 g , P=  HClO4 (aq)','R= H2 g ,N2g , P= NH3(aq)','NEW','12-090','12-056',NULL),
(171175,'chemistry','the conductivity of a centimolar solution of KCL at 25degree celcius is 0.210 /ohm/cm and resistance of cell containing solution at 25 degree celcius is 60ohm .the value of cell comstant is:','1.26/cm','Hint: k = \n1\nR\n×\nx\n\n\nStep 1: \n\nThe formula of conductivity is as follows:\n\nk = Cx or = \n1\nR\n×\nx\n\n\nThe given values are as follows:\n\nk =  0.0210 ohm-1 cm-1\nR = 60 ohm\n\nStep 2: \n\nCalculate the value of the cell constant as follows:\n\nx = kR\n= 0.0210 × 60 \n= 1.26 cm-1\n\nThus, option 4th is the correct answer.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','3.34/cm','1.34/cm','3.38/cm','1.26/cm','NEW','12-079','12-045',NULL),
(171176,'chemistry','Equivalent conductances of Ba²⁺ and Cl⁻ ions are 127 and 76 ohm⁻¹ cm⁻¹ eq⁻¹ respectively.\n \nEquivalent conductance of BaCl₂ at infinite dilution is','139.5','λ∞ = 1/n+ λ+∞ + 1/n- λ-∞\n\nSo, λ∞ (BaCl₂) = 1/2 x λ∞(Ba²⁺) + 1/1 λ∞ (Cl⁻)\n\n                          = 1/2 x 127 + 1 x 76 = 139.5\n\n(NEET 2000)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','139.5','101.5','203','279','NEW','12-083','12-049',NULL),
(171177,'chemistry','The number of electrons delivered at the cathode during electrolysis by a current of 1 ampere in 60 seconds is\n \n(charge on electron = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C)','3.75 × 10²⁰','Q = I × t\n\nQ = 1 × 60 = 60 C\n\nNow, 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C ≡ 1 electron\n\nSo, 60 C ≡ 60/(1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ ) = 3.75 × 10²⁰ electrons\n\n(NEET-II 2016)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','6 × 10²³','6 × 10²⁰','3.75 × 10²⁰','7.48 × 10²³','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171212,'chemistry','In a galvanic cell, what exists between the two electrodes?','Potential difference','There exists a potential difference between the two electrodes and as soon as the switch is in the on position the electrons flow from negative electrode to positive electrode. \n\nThe direction of current flow is opposite to that of electron flow.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 67; Paragraph No.4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Zinc metal','Copper metal','Sulphate ions','Potential difference','NEW','12-067','12-033',NULL),
(171213,'chemistry','Given below are two statements:\n\nAssertion (A): Cell reaction of copper and silver electrode represented as Cu(s)|Cu²⁺(aq)||Ag⁺(aq)|Ag(s)\n\nReason (R): Silver electrode acts as an anode and copper electrode acts as a cathode\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','Half-cell reactions:\n\nCathode (reduction): 2Ag⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ → 2Ag(s) ---(i)\n\nAnode (oxidation): Cu(s) → Cu²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ ---(ii)\n\nIt can be seen that the sum of (i) and (ii) leads to overall reaction in the cell and that silver electrode acts as a cathode and copper electrode acts as an anode. \n\nThe cell can be represented as:\n\nCu(s)|Cu²⁺(aq)||Ag⁺(aq)|Ag(s)\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 67; Paragraph No.2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-067','12-033',NULL),
(171214,'chemistry','We have E𝒸ₑₗₗ = Eᵣᵢ₉ₕₜ – Eₗₑfₜ = EAg⁺|Ag– ECu²⁺|Cu\n\nWhat can you conclude from this equation?','E𝒸ₑₗₗ = Eᵣᵢ₉ₕₜ-Eₗₑfₜ','From the cell reaction, Cu(s)|Cu²⁺(aq)||Ag⁺(aq)|Ag(s) we have \n\nE𝒸ₑₗₗ = Eᵣᵢ₉ₕₜ-Eₗₑfₜ = EAg+|Ag – ECu²⁺|Cu\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 68; Paragraph No.2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','E𝒸ₑₗₗ = Eₗₑfₜ-Eᵣᵢ₉ₕₜ','E𝒸ₑₗₗ = Eᵣᵢ₉ₕₜ+Eₗₑfₜ','E𝒸ₑₗₗ = Eᵣᵢ₉ₕₜ-Eₗₑfₜ','E𝒸ₑₗₗ = 0','NEW','12-068','12-034',NULL),
(171215,'chemistry','Choose the correct options for the standard hydrogen electrode:\n\na) The pressure of hydrogen gas is 1 bar\n\nb) The concentration of hydrogen ion in the solution is 1 molar\n\nc) The pressure of hydrogen gas is 14.7 psi','All are correct','In the standard hydrogen electrode, the pressure of hydrogen gas is 1 bar=14.7 psi and the concentration of hydrogen ion in the solution is 1 molar.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 68; Paragraph No.4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Only a and b','Only a and c','Only b and c','All are correct','NEW','12-068','12-034',NULL),
(171216,'chemistry','From where the pure hydrogen gas is bubbled in the standard hydrogen electrode?','From the acidic solution where the electrode is dipped','The standard hydrogen electrode consists of a platinum electrode coated with platinum black.\n \nThe electrode is dipped in an acidic solution and pure hydrogen gas is bubbled through it.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 68; Paragraph No.4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','From the basic solution where the electrode is dipped','Pure hydrogen is bubbled from the environment','From the acidic solution where the electrode is dipped','All of the above','NEW','12-068','12-034',NULL),
(171217,'chemistry','If the concentrations of the oxidized and the reduced forms of the species in the right-hand half-cell are unity, then which of the following will be equal to the cell potential?','Standard electrode potential','If the concentrations of the oxidized and the reduced forms of the species in the right-hand half-cell are unity, then the cell potential is equal to the standard electrode potential.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 69; Paragraph No.1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Standard hydrogen potential','Standard electrode potential','Standard cell emf','None of these','NEW','12-069','12-035',NULL),
(171218,'chemistry','Given below are two statements:\n\nAssertion (A): In nitric acid, Cu is oxidized by nitrate ion and not by hydrogen ion.\n\nReason (R): Hydrogen ions cannot oxidize Cu\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Cu does not dissolve in HCl. \n\nIn nitric acid it is oxidised by nitrate ion and not by hydrogen ion.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 69; Paragraph No.2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-069','12-035',NULL),
(171219,'chemistry','What do you call the electrode representing \n\nPt(s)|H₂(g)| H⁺(aq)?','Hydrogen electrode','Hydrogen electrode representing as Pt(s)|H₂(g)| H⁺(aq)\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 69; Paragraph No.3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Bromine electrode','Hydrogen electrode','Galvanic electrode','Battery','NEW','12-069','12-035',NULL),
(171220,'chemistry','Which one is the half-cell reaction of Bromine electrode?','½ Br₂(aq) + e⁻ → Br⁻(aq)','Bromine electrode: \n\nPt(s)|Br₂(aq)| Br–(aq)\n\nHalf-cell reaction: \n\n½ Br₂(aq) + e⁻ → Br⁻(aq)\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 69; Paragraph No.3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','HBr(aq) + e⁻ → Br ⁻(aq)','HBr(aq) + e⁻ → Br ⁻ + H⁺(aq)','½ Br₂(aq) + e⁻ → Br⁻(aq)','½ H₂(aq) + e⁻ → H⁺(aq)','NEW','12-069','12-035',NULL),
(171221,'chemistry','Given below are two statements of a system Mg²⁺|Mg:\n\nAssertion (A): The standard electrode potential of the system Mg²⁺|Mg can be measured with respect to the standard hydrogen electrode\n\nReason (R): The cell, consisting of Mg | MgSO₄ (aq 1 M) as the cathode and the standard hydrogen electrode as the anode\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','The standard electrode potential of the system Mg²⁺|Mg can be measured with respect to the standard hydrogen electrode, a cell, consisting of Mg | MgSO₄ (aq 1 M) as the anode and the standard hydrogen electrode as the cathode.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 70; Example No.3.1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-070','12-036',NULL),
(171222,'chemistry','Match the following.','Fe²⁺ can oxidise Br₂, Cl₂ and I₂\n\nMg²⁺ can reduce Na⁺,K⁺ and Ca⁺\n\nO₂⁻ can oxidise Co³⁺, F₂ and Au³⁺','Oxidizing agents from Table 3.1:   \n\nF₂> Co³⁺> Au³⁺> Cl₂> O₂> Br₂> I₂> Fe²⁺> Mg²⁺> Na⁺> Ca²⁺> K⁺\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 71; Table 3.1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','match','Fe²⁺,can oxidise Br₂, Cl₂ and I₂','Mg²⁺,can reduce Na⁺,K⁺ and Ca⁺','O₂,can oxidise Co³⁺, F₂ and Au³⁺',NULL,'NEW','12-071','12-037',NULL),
(171223,'chemistry','Given below are two statements of the equation: \n\nE𝒸ₑₗₗ=E⁰𝒸ₑₗₗ - RT/2F ln (Zn²⁺/Cu²⁺)\n\nAssertion (A): It can be seen that E𝒸ₑₗₗ depends on the concentration of both Cu²⁺ and Zn²⁺ ions\n\nReason (R): E𝒸ₑₗₗ increases with an increase in the concentration of Cu²⁺ ions and a decrease in the concentration of Zn²⁺ ions\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','It can be seen that E(cell) depends on the concentration of both Cu²⁺ and Zn²⁺ ions. \n\nIt increases with an increase in the concentration of Cu²⁺ ions and a decrease in the concentration of Zn²⁺ ions.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 72; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-072','12-038',NULL),
(171224,'chemistry','What will be the Nernst equation for the following reaction?\n\naA + bB → cC + dD','E𝒸ₑₗₗ = E⁰𝒸ₑₗₗ-RT/nF ln ([C]ᶜ[D]ᵈ) / ([A]ᵃ[B]ᵇ)','For the reaction aA + bB → cC + dD; Nernst equation can be written as:\n\nE𝒸ₑₗₗ = E⁰𝒸ₑₗₗ-RT/nF ln (Q)\n\nE𝒸ₑₗₗ = E⁰𝒸ₑₗₗ-RT/nF ln ([C]ᶜ[D]ᵈ) / ([A]ᵃ[B]ᵇ)\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 72; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','E𝒸ₑₗₗ = E⁰𝒸ₑₗₗ-RT/nF ln ([C]ᶜ[D]ᵈ) / ([A]ᵃ[B]ᵇ)','E𝒸ₑₗₗ = E⁰𝒸ₑₗₗ-RT/nF ln ([A]ᵃ[B]ᵇ / ([C]ᶜ[D]ᵈ))','E𝒸ₑₗₗ = E⁰𝒸ₑₗₗ+RT/nF ln ([C]ᶜ[D]ᵈ) / ([A]ᵃ[B]ᵇ)','E𝒸ₑₗₗ = -RT/nF ln ([C]ᶜ[D]ᵈ) / ([A]ᵃ[B]ᵇ)','NEW','12-072','12-038',NULL),
(171225,'chemistry','Which one is the correct option for the following equation?\n\nE⁰𝒸ₑₗₗ= 2.303 RT/nF log K𝒸\n\na) It gives a relationship between the equilibrium constant of the reaction and the standard potential of the cell in which that reaction takes place\n\nb) It gives a relationship between the conductivity of the solution and the standard potential of the cell in which that reaction takes place\n\nc) It is derived from the Nernst equation','Only a and c','This equation is derived from the Nernst equation and it gives a relationship between the equilibrium constant of the reaction and the standard potential of the cell in which that reaction takes place.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 73; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and b','Only b and c','Only a and c','All of the above','NEW','12-073','12-039',NULL),
(171226,'chemistry','Electrical work done in one second is equal to electrical potential multiplied by the total _______ passed.','charge','Electrical work done in one second is equal to electrical potential multiplied by the total charge passed.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 74; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','fillup','cha,cur,rge,rent',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171227,'chemistry','Match the following regarding the equation \n\nΔᵣG = – nFE₍𝒸ₑₗₗ₎','nF-is the amount of charge passed, ΔᵣG-is an extensive thermodynamic property, E₍𝒸ₑₗₗ₎-is an intensive parameter','The reversible work done by a galvanic cell is equal to a decrease in its Gibbs energy and therefore if the emf of the cell is E and nF is the amount of charge passed and ΔᵣG is the Gibbs energy of the reaction,\nthen ΔᵣG  =  –  nFE₍𝒸ₑₗₗ₎\n\nHere, E₍𝒸ₑₗₗ₎ is an intensive parameter but ΔrG is an extensive thermodynamic property and the value depends on n\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 74; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','match','nF,is the amount of charge passed','ΔᵣG,is an extensive thermodynamic property','E₍𝒸ₑₗₗ₎,is an intensive parameter',NULL,'NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171228,'chemistry','The resistivity of a substance is its ________','resistance','The resistivity for a substance is its resistance\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 75; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','fillup','con,res,duct,ist,ivi,an,ty,ce',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-075','12-041',NULL),
(171229,'chemistry','For measuring the resistance of an ionic solution, we face two problems:\n\nProblem (A): Passing direct current (DC) changes the composition of the solution.\n\nProblem (B): A solution cannot be connected to the bridge like a metallic wire or other solid conductors.\n\nWhich one is the correct option from the following?','Both (A) and (B) are correct','For measuring the resistance of an ionic solution we face two problems. \n\nFirstly, passing direct current (DC) changes the composition of the solution. \n\nSecondly, a solution cannot be connected to the bridge like a metallic wire or other solid conductors. \n\nThe first difficulty is resolved by using an alternating current (AC) source of power. \n\nThe second problem is solved by using a specially designed vessel called a conductivity cell.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 77; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Problem (A) is resolved by using an alternating current (AC) source of power','Problem (B) is solved by using a specially designed vessel called conductivity cell','Both (A) and (B) are correct','Both (A) and (B) are incorrect','NEW','12-077','12-043',NULL),
(171230,'chemistry','Normally, conductivity κ is expressed in which one of the following?','S cm⁻¹','The SI units of conductivity are S m⁻¹ but quite often, k is expressed in S cm⁻¹. \n\nThe conductivity of a material in S m⁻¹ is its conductance when it is 1 m long and its area of cross section is 1 m². \n\nIt may be noted that 1 S cm⁻¹ = 100 S m⁻¹\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 75; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','S cm⁻¹','S m⁻¹','S m','S cm','NEW','12-075','12-041',NULL),
(171231,'chemistry','The magnitude of conductivity depends on which of the following?','All of these','The magnitude of conductivity varies a great deal and depends on the nature of the material.\n\nIt also depends on the temperature and pressure at which the measurements are made.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 76; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Nature of the material','Temperature','Pressure','All of these','NEW','12-076','12-042',NULL),
(171232,'chemistry','Given below are two statements:\n\nAssertion (A): Passage of direct current through an ionic solution over a prolonged period can lead to a change in its composition\n\nReason (R): It happens due to electrochemical reactions\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','The passage of direct current through an ionic solution over a prolonged period can lead to a change in its composition due to electrochemical reactions.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 77; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-077','12-043',NULL),
(171233,'chemistry','Once the cell constant is determined, we can use it for a solution to measure which one of the following?','resistance & conductivity','Once the cell constant is determined, we can use it for measuring the resistance or conductivity of any solution.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 78; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','resistance & conductivity','concentration & conductivity','resistance & temperature','temperature & conductivity','NEW','12-078','12-044',NULL),
(171234,'chemistry','How will you calculate R₂ from the arrangement for measurement of resistance of a solution of an electrolyte as shown in the diagram? (n1709)','R₂=R₁R₄/R₃','Unknown resistance, R₂=R₁R₄/R₃\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 78; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','R₂=R₁R₃/R₄','R₂=R₃R₄/R₁','R₂=R₁/R₄R₃','R₂=R₁R₄/R₃','NEW','12-078','12-044','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/171234q.png'),
(171235,'chemistry','For strong electrolytes, Λₘ is represented as \n\nΛₘ = Λ⁰ₘ - A c¹/²\n\nIf we plot Λₘ against c¹/²,\n\nwhich one will be the slope?','-A','For strong electrolytes, Λₘ increases slowly with dilution and can be represented by the equation: \n\nΛₘ = Λ⁰ₘ - A c¹/²\n\nIt can be seen that if we plot Λₘ against c¹/², we obtain a straight line with an intercept equal to Λ⁰ₘ and slope equal to ‘–A’.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 81; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','A','-A','-Ac¹/²','Λ⁰ₘ','NEW','12-081','12-047',NULL),
(171236,'chemistry','Match the following electrolytes with the type they have:','NaCl-1-1, CaCl₂-2-1, MgSO₄-2-2','NaCl, CaCl₂, and MgSO₄ are known as 1-1, 2-1, and 2-2 electrolytes respectively.\n\nNaCl⟶Na + +Cl⁻ \n              1   :    1 \nHence 1−1 electrolyte.\n\nMgCl₂ ⟶ Mg²⁺ + 2Cl⁻ \n                    2   :  1 \nHence 2−1 electrolyte.\n\nCaSO₄ ⟶ Ca²⁺ + SO₄⁻² \n                     2   :   2 \nHence 2−2 electrolyte.\n\nCharge on cation and anion decode electrolyte type.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 81; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','match','NaCl,1-1','CaCl₂,2-1','MgSO₄,2-2',NULL,'NEW','12-081','12-047',NULL),
(171237,'chemistry','A plot of Λₘ (y-axis) and c¹/² (x-axis) is shown in the diagram.\n\nDetermine the values of Λₘ⁰ and A for KCl. \n\nRefer below diagram(n1708)','150.0 S cm² mol⁻¹ and 87.46 S cm² mol⁻¹/(mol/L⁻¹)¹/²','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 82; Example No. 3.6)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','147.0 S cm² mol⁻¹ and 87.46 S cm² mol⁻¹/(mol/L⁻¹)¹/²','149.8.0 S cm² mol⁻¹ and 87.46 S cm² mol⁻¹/(mol/L⁻¹)¹/²','150.0 S cm² mol⁻¹ and 77.46 S cm² mol⁻¹/(mol/L⁻¹)¹/²','150.0 S cm² mol⁻¹ and 87.46 S cm² mol⁻¹/(mol/L⁻¹)¹/²','NEW','12-082','12-048','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/171237q.png'),
(171238,'chemistry','The limiting molar conductivity for sodium chloride is given by the equation shown in the diagram. \n\nWhich one is the correct option? \n\na) Λ°ₘ is the limiting molar conductivity of NaCl\n\nb) λ⁰+ is the number of cations\n\nc) λ⁰- is the limiting molar conductivity of anions\n\nRefer below diagram(n1706)','Only a and c','Λ°ₘ is the limiting molar conductivity of NaCl. \nλ⁰+ is the limiting molar conductivity of cations and λ⁰- is the limiting molar conductivity of anions; whereas v+ is the number of cations and v- is the number of anions. \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 83; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and b','Only b and c','Only a and c','All of a, b and c','NEW','12-083','12-049','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/171238q.png'),
(2000032027,'physics','According to Cartesian sign convention, in ray optics','A','According to Cartesian sign convention, the distances measured in the same direction as the incident light are taken as positive and those measured in the direction opposite 	to the direction of incident light are taken as negative','Miscellaneous Problems on Ray Optics and Optical Instruments ','Ray Optics And Optical Instruments','Easy',NULL,'All distances in the direction of incident ray are taken positive','All distances are taken  negative','All distances in the direction of incident ray are taken negative','All distances are taken positive',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(171243,'chemistry','Given below are two statements about copper purification:\n\nAssertion (A): The impure copper is made of an anode that dissolves on a passing current and pure copper is deposited at the cathode\n\nReason (R): This is the basis for an industrial process in which impure copper is converted into copper of high purity\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Copper is dissolved (oxidised) at anode and deposited (reduced) at cathode. \n\nThis is the basis for an industrial process in which impure copper is converted into copper of high purity. \n\nThe impure copper is made of an anode that dissolves on passing current and pure copper is deposited at the cathode.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 85; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-085','12-051',NULL),
(171244,'chemistry','Which one was not available during Faraday’s times?','constant current sources','There were no constant current sources available during Faraday’s times.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 86; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','constant current sources','clocks','temperature measuring tools','voltage measuring tools','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171245,'chemistry','Given below are two statements during Faraday\'s times:\n\nAssertion (A): The general practice was to put a hydrogen electrode for determining the quantity of electricity passed from the amount of metal deposited or consumed\n\nReason (R): There were no constant current sources available during Faraday’s times\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','There were no constant current sources available during Faraday’s times. \n\nThe general practice was to put a coulometer (a standard electrolytic cell) for determining the quantity of electricity passed from the amount of metal (generally silver or copper) deposited or consumed.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 86; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171246,'chemistry','What is the charge on one electron?','1.6021× 10⁻¹⁹ C','We know that charge on one electron is equal to 1.6021× 10⁻¹⁹ C\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 86; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','6.023× 10⁻¹⁹ C','6.023× 10²³ C','96500 C','1.6021× 10⁻¹⁹ C','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171247,'chemistry','The charge passed through the _________ cell during electrolysis is equal to the product of current in amperes and time in seconds.','electrolytic','The charge passed through the electrolytic cell during electrolysis is equal to the product of current in amperes and time in seconds\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 86; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','fillup','ele,ys,ctr,is,ol,yt,ic',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(171248,'chemistry','Given below are two statements of electrolysis:\n\nAssertion (A): Products of electrolysis participate in the electrode reaction\n\nReason (R): The electrode is reactive\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Products of electrolysis depend on the nature of the material being electrolyzed and the type of electrodes being used. \n\nIf the electrode is inert (e.g., platinum or gold), it does not participate in the chemical reaction and acts only as a source or sink for electrons. \n\nOn the other hand, if the electrode is reactive, it participates in the electrode reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 86; Paragraph 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171249,'chemistry','From the above reactions shown in the diagram, which one is the correct option? \n\na) Reaction A is in the cathode\n\nb) Reaction A is in the anode\n\nc) Reaction B is in the cathode\n\nd) Reaction B is in  the anode\n\nRefer diagram(n1702)','Only a and d','Cathode: H₂O(l ) + e⁻ → ½ H₂(g) + OH⁻ (aq)\n\nAnode: Cl⁻ (aq) → ½ Cl₂(g) + e⁻\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 87; Paragraph 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and d','Only b and c','Only a and c','Only c and d','NEW','12-087','12-053','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/171249q.png'),
(171250,'chemistry','If a current of 0.5 ampere flows through a metallic wire for 2 hours, then how many electrons would flow through the wire?','2.25 X 10²³','I = 0.5 A\n\nt = 2 hours = 2 × 60 × 60 s = 7200 s\n\nThus, Q = It\n\n= 0.5 A × 7200 s = 3600 C\n\nWe know that 96487 C = 6.023 X 10²³ number of electrons.\n\n3600 C = 6.023 X 10²³ X 3600/96487 = 2.25 X 10²³ number of electrons\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 88; Example No. 3.1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2.25 X 10¹⁹','4.5 X 10²³','1.125 X 10²³','2.25 X 10²³','NEW','12-088','12-054',NULL),
(171251,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true for the batteries? \n\na) Primary batteries are rechargeable.\n\nb) The reaction occurs only once at secondary batteries.\n\nc) The dry cell is a primary battery.\n\nd) On charging the Lead storage battery, the reaction is reversed.','Only (c) and (d)','a) In the primary batteries, the reaction occurs only once, and after use over a period of time battery becomes dead and cannot be reused again. So it can’t be recharged which is not the correct answer.\n\nb) A secondary cell after use can be recharged by passing a current through it in the opposite direction so that it can be used again. It is a rechargeable battery and can be used many times. So it is not the correct answer. \n\nc) The most familiar example of a primary battery is a dry cell. So this option is the correct answer. \n\nd) On charging the Lead storage battery the reaction is reversed and PbSO₄(s) on the anode and cathode are converted into Pb and PbO₂, respectively. So this option is correct.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 88; Paragraph No.4, Page No. 89; Paragraph No.2,)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Only (c) and (d)','Only (a) and (b)','Only (b) and (c)','All the options except (d)','NEW','12-088','12-054',NULL),
(171252,'chemistry','What properties should batteries have for practical use?','all of these','For a battery to be of practical use it should be reasonably light, and compact and its voltage should not vary appreciably during its use.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 88; Paragraph No.2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','reasonably light','compact','voltage should not vary appreciably during its use','all of these','NEW','12-088','12-054',NULL),
(171253,'chemistry','Which of the following option is true for the types of batteries?\n\na) Primary batteries\n\nb) Secondary batteries\n\nc) Tertiary batteries','Only a and c','There are mainly two types of batteries:\n\nOne is primary batteries and another is secondary batteries\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 88; Paragraph No.2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Only a and b','Only b and c','Only a and c','All of these','NEW','12-088','12-054',NULL),
(171254,'chemistry','Match the following.','Dry cell-1.5V, Mercury cell-1.35 V, Galvanic cell-1.1 V','A dry cell has a potential of 1.5 V\n\nMercury cell potential is approximately 1.35 V\n\nThe galvanic cell potential is 1.1 V\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 88; Paragraph No.4; Page No. 89; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','match','Dry cell,1.5V','Mercury cell,1.35V','Galvanic cell,1.1V',NULL,'NEW','12-088','12-054',NULL),
(171255,'chemistry','Which one is the accurate option for the commercial dry cell?\n\na) In the reaction at cathode, manganese is reduced from the + 4 oxidation state to the +3 state\n\nb) Ammonia produced in the reaction forms a complex with Zn²⁺ to give [Zn (NH₃)₄]²⁺\n\nc) Consists of a graphite (carbon) cathode in a zinc container; the latter acts as the anode','All of these','In the reaction at the cathode, manganese is reduced from the + 4 oxidation state to the +3 state. \n\nAmmonia produced in the reaction forms a complex with Zn²⁺ to give [Zn (NH₃)₄]²⁺. \n\nThis cell consists of a graphite (carbon) cathode in a zinc container; the latter acts as the anode.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 88; Paragraph No.4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and b','Only b and c','Only a and c','All of these','NEW','12-088','12-054',NULL),
(171256,'chemistry','Commonly used cells are mercury cells. \n\nIn there, the reducing agent is ________','zinc','Commonly used cells are mercury cells. \n\nIn there, the reducing agent is zinc and the oxidizing agent is mercury (II) oxide.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 89; Paragraph No.1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','fillup','cop,zin,mer,per,cu,ry,(II),c',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-089','12-055',NULL),
(171257,'chemistry','Two cathode reactions of different cells are given.\n\nCathode reaction A: MnO₂+ NH₄⁺+ e⁻→ MnO(OH) + NH₃\n\nCathode reaction B: HgO + H₂O + 2e⁻ → Hg(l) + 2OH⁻\n\nWhich one is the correct option?\n\na) Cathode reaction A occurs in dry cell\n\nb) Cathode reaction B occurs in mercury cell','Both a and b','Cathode reaction of dry cell: MnO₂+ NH₄⁺+ e⁻→ MnO(OH) + NH₃\n\nCathode reaction of mercury cell: HgO + H₂O + 2e⁻ → Hg(l) + 2OH⁻\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 89; Paragraph No.1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Both a and b','None of these','NEW','12-089','12-055',NULL),
(171258,'chemistry','Which cell can undergo a large number of discharging and charging cycles?','A secondary cell','A good secondary cell can undergo a large number of discharging and charging cycles.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 89; Paragraph No.2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','A primary cell','A secondary cell','A galvanic cell','All of the above','NEW','12-089','12-055',NULL),
(171259,'chemistry','In the lead storage battery, which one is used as cathode?','PbO₂','The most important secondary cell is the lead storage battery commonly used in automobiles and inverters.\n \nIt consists of a lead anode and a grid of lead packed with lead dioxide (PbO₂) as a cathode.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 89; Paragraph No.2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','PbO₂','PbO','Pb₂O','Pb','NEW','12-089','12-055',NULL),
(171260,'chemistry','Given below are two statements:\n\nAssertion (A): On charging the battery the reaction is reversed and PbSO₄(s) on the anode and cathode is converted into Pb and PbO₂, respectively.\n\nReason (R): A secondary cell after use can be recharged by passing a current through it in the opposite direction so that it can be used again.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','A secondary cell after use can be recharged by passing a current through it in the opposite direction so that it can be used again. \n\nOn charging the battery the reaction is reversed and PbSO₄(s) on the anode, and cathode is converted into Pb and PbO₂, respectively.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 89; Paragraph No.3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-089','12-055',NULL),
(171261,'chemistry','Which one is the overall cell reaction of lead storage battery consisting of cathode and anode reaction?','Pb(s) + PbO₂(s) + 2H₂SO₄(aq) → 2PbSO₄(s) + 2H₂O(l)','The overall cell reaction of lead storage battery consists of cathode and anode reaction is:\n\nPb(s) + PbO₂(s) + 2H₂SO₄(aq) → 2PbSO₄(s) + 2H₂O(l)\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 89; Paragraph No.3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Pb(s) + PbO(s) + 2H₂SO₄(aq) → 2PbSO₄(s) + 2H₂O(l)','Pb(s) + PbO₂(s) + 2H₂SO₄(aq) → 2PbO(s) + 2H₂O(l)','Pb(s) + PbO₂(s) + 2H₂SO₄(aq) → 2PbSO₄(s)','Pb(s) + PbO₂(s) + 2H₂SO₄(aq) → 2PbSO₄(s) + 2H₂O(l)','NEW','12-089','12-055',NULL),
(171262,'chemistry','Which one do anode and cathode both produce in a lead storage battery?','PbSO₄','The cell reactions when the battery is in use are given below:\n\nAnode: Pb(s) + SO₄²⁻(aq) → PbSO₄(s) + 2e⁻\n\nCathode: PbO₂(s) + SO₄²⁻(aq) + 4H⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ → PbSO₄ (s) + 2H₂O (l)\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 89; Paragraph No.3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','PbSO₄','PbO','Pb','PbCO₃','NEW','12-089','12-055',NULL),
(171263,'chemistry','Which one is the overall cell reaction of a rechargeable nickel-cadmium cell?','Cd (s) + 2Ni(OH)₃ (s) → CdO (s) + 2Ni(OH)₂ (s) + H₂O (l )','Cd (s) + 2Ni(OH)₃ (s) → CdO (s) + 2Ni(OH)₂ (s) + H₂O (l)\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 90; Paragraph No.1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cd (s) + 2Ni(OH)₂ (s) → CdO (s) + 2Ni(OH)₂ (s) + H₂O (l )','Cd (s) + 2Ni(OH)₃ (s) → Cd(OH)₂ (s) + 2Ni(OH)₂ (s) + H₂O (l )','Cd (s) + 2Ni(OH)₃ (s) → CdO (s) + 2Ni(OH)₃ (s) + H₂O (l )','Cd (s) + 2Ni(OH)₃ (s) → CdO (s) + 2Ni(OH)₂ (s) + H₂O (l )','NEW','12-090','12-056',NULL),
(171264,'chemistry','Which one is the correction option?\n\na) Galvanic cell directly converts chemical energy into electricity and is highly efficient\n\nb) It is not possible to make such cells in which reactants are fed continuously to the electrodes and products are removed continuously from the electrolyte compartment','Only a','We know that a galvanic cell directly converts chemical energy into electricity and is highly efficient. \n\nIt is now possible to make such cells in which reactants are fed continuously to the electrodes and products are removed continuously from the electrolyte compartment.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 90; Paragraph No.2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Only a','Only b','Both a and b','None of these','NEW','12-091','12-057',NULL),
(171265,'chemistry','Which one is the correct option for a fuel cell shown in the diagram? \n\na) Overall reaction is 2H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2H₂O(l)\n\nb) Total 4 electrons are transferred in the cell reaction\n\nc) O₂(g) + 2H₂O(l) + 2e⁻→ 2OH⁻(aq) is cathode reaction\n\nRefer below diagram(n1701)','Only a and b','Cathode: O₂(g) + 2H₂O(l) + 4e–→ 4OH⁻(aq)\n\nAnode: 2H₂ (g) + 4OH⁻(aq) → 4H₂O(l) + 4e⁻ (so 4 electrons transferred)\n\nOverall reaction being:\n\n2H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2H₂O(l)\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 90; Paragraph No.4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and b','Only b and c','Only a and c','All of the above','NEW','12-090','12-056',NULL),
(171266,'chemistry','Which one of the following causes enormous damage to buildings, bridges, ships, and to all objects made of metals, especially iron?','Corrosion','Corrosion causes enormous damage to buildings, bridges, ships, and to all objects made of metals, especially iron. \n\nWe lose crores of rupees every year on account of corrosion.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 91; Paragraph No.2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Batteries','Corrosion','Electrolytic cell','Conductivity','NEW','12-091','12-057',NULL),
(171267,'chemistry','Electrons released at an anodic spot move through the metal and go to another spot on the metal and reduce oxygen in the presence of which ion?','H⁺','Electrons released at an anodic spot move through the metal and go to another spot on the metal and reduce oxygen in the presence of H⁺\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 91; Paragraph No.3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','O²⁻','Fe²⁺','Fe³⁺','H⁺','NEW','12-091','12-057',NULL),
(171268,'chemistry','Given below are two statements of cathode reaction during corrosion:\n\nAssertion (A): H⁺ is believed to be available from H₂CO₃\n\nReason (R): H₂CO₃ formed due to the dissolution of carbon monoxide from the air into water\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','Electrons released at an anodic spot move through the metal and go to another spot on the metal and reduce oxygen in the presence of H⁺ \n\n(which is believed to be available from H₂CO₃ formed due to the dissolution of carbon dioxide from the air into water. Hydrogen ions in water may also be available due to the dissolution of other acidic oxides from the atmosphere).\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 91; Paragraph No.4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-091','12-057',NULL),
(171269,'chemistry','Which one is the overall reaction of corrosion?','2Fe(s) + O₂(g) + 4H⁺(aq) → 2Fe²⁺(aq) + 2H₂O(l)','The overall reaction:\n\n2Fe(s) + O₂(g) + 4H⁺(aq) → 2Fe²⁺(aq) + 2H₂O(l)      E𝒸ₑₗₗ =1.67 V\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 91; Paragraph No.4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','2FeO(s) + O₂(g) + 4H⁺(aq) → 2Fe²⁺(aq) + 2H₂O(l)','2Fe(s) + O₂(g) + 4H⁺(aq) → 2Fe³⁺(aq) + 2H₂O(l)','2Fe(s) + O₂(g) + 4H⁺(aq) → 2Fe²⁺(aq) + O²⁻','2Fe(s) + O₂(g) + 4H⁺(aq) → 2Fe²⁺(aq) + 2H₂O(l)','NEW','12-091','12-057',NULL),
(171270,'chemistry','What materials other than hydrogen can be used as fuels in fuel cells? \nChoose the correct option.\n\na) methane\nb) zinc\nc) methanol','Only a and c','Methane and methanol can be used as fuels in fuel cells.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 92; Example No.3.14)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Only a and b','Only b and c','Only a and c','All of the above','NEW','12-090','12-056',NULL),
(171271,'chemistry','Predict the correct option as examples of fossil fuels which are the main sources of energy that are driving our economy.\n\na) coal\nb) oil\nc) gas','All of these','At present, the main source of energy that is driving our economy is fossil fuels such as coal, oil, and gas.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 92; Paragraph No.1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Only a and b','Only b and c','Only a and c','All of these','NEW','12-090','12-056',NULL),
(171272,'chemistry','Given below are two statements of energy consumption:\n\nAssertion (A): As more people on the planet aspire to improve their standard of living, their energy requirement will increase\n\nReason (R): The per capita consumption of energy used is a measure of development\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','As more people on the planet aspire to improve their standard of living, their energy requirements will increase.\n \nThe per capita consumption of energy used is a measure of development.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 92; Paragraph No.1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-092','12-058',NULL),
(171273,'chemistry','Given below are two statements about the Greenhouse Effect:\n\nAssertion (A): A rise in the temperature of the Earth’s surface, causes polar ice to melt and ocean levels to rise.\n\nReason (R): We are already aware that carbon dioxide produced by the combustion of fossil fuels is resulting in the Greenhouse Effect.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','We are already aware that carbon dioxide produced by the combustion of fossil fuels is resulting in the Greenhouse Effect. \n\nThis is leading to a rise in the temperature of the Earth’s surface, causing polar ice to melt and ocean levels to rise.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 92; Paragraph No.1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-092','12-058',NULL),
(171274,'chemistry','Which one is not due to the Greenhouse Effect?\n\na) Flooding of low-lying areas\nb) Earthquake\nc) Heavy rain\nd) The Maldives facing total submergence','Only b and c','The greenhouse effect is leading to a rise in the temperature of the Earth’s surface, causing polar ice to melt and ocean levels to rise. \n\nThis will flood low-lying areas along the coast and some island nations such as the Maldives face total submergence.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 92; Paragraph No.1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Only a and c','Only b and c','Only c and d','Only b','NEW','12-092','12-058',NULL),
(171275,'chemistry','To avoid the Greenhouse Effect, we need to limit our use of ________ fuels.','carbonaceous','To avoid such a catastrophe (The greenhouse Effect), we need to limit our use of carbonaceous fuels.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 92; Paragraph No.1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','fillup','car,bio,bon,ac,eo,us',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-092','12-058',NULL),
(171276,'chemistry','What are the advantages of using Hydrogen as fuel?\n\na) Hydrogen provides an ideal alternative as its combustion results in water only.\n\nb) Hydrogen can be used as a renewable and non-polluting source of energy.\n\nc) Easy to store.','Only a and b','Hydrogen provides an ideal alternative as its combustion results in water only. \n\nHydrogen production must come from splitting water using solar energy. \n\nTherefore, hydrogen can be used as a renewable and non-polluting source of energy.\n \nThis is the vision of the Hydrogen Economy.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 92; Paragraph No.1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Only a and b','Only b and c','Only a and c','All of these','NEW','12-092','12-058',NULL),
(171277,'chemistry','Depict the galvanic cell in which the reaction.\n\nZn(s)+2Ag⁺(aq) → Zn²⁺(aq)+2Ag(s) takes place. \n\nChoose the correct option from the following:\n\na) The negatively charged electrode is the zinc electrode. It acts as an anode.\n\nb) In the external circuit, the current will flow from zinc to silver.\n\nc) Oxidation at anode: Zn(s)→Zn²⁺(aq)+2e⁻','Only a and c','For the galvanic cell reaction:\n\nZn(s)+2Ag⁺(aq) → Zn²⁺(aq)+2Ag(s) \n\na) The negatively charged electrode is the zinc electrode. It acts as an anode.\n\nb) In the external circuit, the current will flow from silver to zinc.\n\nc) Oxidation at anode: Zn(s)→Zn²⁺(aq)+2e⁻\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 93; Example No.3.3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and b','Only b and c','Only a and c','All of these','NEW','12-068','12-034',NULL),
(171278,'chemistry','Predict the products of electrolysis in each of the following:\n\n(i) An aqueous solution of AgNO₃ with silver electrodes.\n\n(ii) An aqueous solution of AgNO₃ with platinum electrodes.\n\nWhich one is the correct option for anode reaction?\n\na) Product of (i) is Ag⁺ \n\nb) Product of (II) is Ag⁺ \n\nc) Product of (ii) is O₂','Only a and c','(i) An aqueous solution of AgNO₃ with silver electrodes.\n\nAt cathode: Silver ions have lower discharge potential than hydrogen ions. Hence, silver ions will be deposited in preference to hydrogen ions.\n\nAt anode: Silver anode will dissolve to form silver ions in the solution.\n\nAg→Ag⁺ + e⁻\n\n(ii) An aqueous solution of AgNO₃ with platinum electrodes.\n\nAt cathode: Silver ions have lower discharge potential than hydrogen ions. Hence, silver ions will be deposited in preference to hydrogen ions.\n\nAt anode: Hydroxide ions having lower discharge potential will be discharged in preference to nitrate ions. \n\nHydroxide ions will decompose to give oxygen.\n\n4OH⁻(aq)→2H₂O(l)+O₂(g)+4e⁻\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 94; Example No.3.18)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and b','Only b and c','Only a and c','All of these','NEW','12-087','12-053',NULL),
(171279,'chemistry','Predict the products of electrolysis in each of the following:\n\n(i) A dilute solution of H₂SO₄ with platinum electrodes.\n\n(ii) An aqueous solution of CuCl₂ with platinum electrodes.\n\nWhich one is the correct option for cathode reaction?\n\na) Product of (i) is H₂ gas\n\nb) Product of (i) is O₂\n\nc) Product of (ii) is Cu','Only a and c','(i) A dilute solution of H₂SO₄ with platinum electrodes.\n\nAt cathode: 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂(g)\n\nAt anode: Hydroxide ions having lower discharge potential will be discharged in preference to sulphate ions. \n\nHydroxide ions will decompose to give oxygen.\n\n4OH⁻(aq)→2H₂O(l)+O₂(g)+4e⁻\n\n\n(ii) An aqueous solution of CuCl₂ with platinum electrodes\n\nAt cathode: Cupric ions will be reduced in preference to protons\n\nCu²⁺ +2e⁻ →Cu\n\nAt anode: Chloride ions will be oxidized in preference to hydroxide ions\n\n2Cl⁻ →Cl₂ +2e⁻\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 94; Example No.3.18)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and b','Only b and c','Only a and c','All of these','NEW','12-087','12-053',NULL),
(171303,'chemistry','Given :\nCo³⁺ + e⁻ → Co²⁺ ; E° = +1.81 V\nPb⁴⁺ + 2e⁻ → Pb²⁺ ; E° = +1.67 V\nCe⁴⁺ + e⁻ → Ce³⁺ ; E° = +1.61 V\nBi³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Bi ; E° = +0.20 V\nOxidizing power of the species will increase in the\norder : [JEE (Main)-2019]','Bi³⁺ < Ce⁴⁺  < Pb⁴⁺  < Co³⁺','Greater the reduction potential, greater is the\noxidising power.\nSo, Bi³⁺ < Ce⁴⁺  < Pb⁴⁺  < Co³⁺','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','Co³⁺ < Ce⁴⁺  < Bi³⁺ < Pb⁴⁺','Co³⁺ < Pb⁴⁺  < Ce⁴⁺  < Bi³⁺','Ce⁴⁺  < Pb⁴⁺  < Bi³⁺ < Co³⁺','Bi³⁺ < Ce⁴⁺  < Pb⁴⁺  < Co³⁺','NEW','12-070','12-036',NULL),
(171314,'chemistry','If the standard electrode potential for a cell is 2 V\nat 300 K, the equilibrium constant (K) for the\n reaction Zn(s) + Cu²⁺(aq) ⇌ Zn²⁺(aq) + Cu(s) at 300 K is approximately\n(R = 8 J/Kmol, F = 96000 C/mol)','e¹⁶⁰','lnK = nFE°cell / RT\nlnK = 2 × 96000 × 2 / 8 × 300 = 160\nK = e¹⁶⁰','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','e³²⁰','e¹⁶⁰','e⁻¹⁶⁰','e⁻⁸⁰','NEW','12-073','12-039',NULL),
(171501,'chemistry','Predict the products of electrolysis in each of the following : An aqueous solution of AgNO₃ with platinum electrodes.','OH & O₂','At cathode: Ag⁺ ions have lower discharge potential than H⁺ ions. Hence, Ag⁺ ions will be deposited as Ag in preference to H⁺ ions.\n\nAt anode: As anode is not attackable, out of OH⁻ and NO, ions, OH⁻ ions have lower discharge potential. Hence, OH⁻ ions will be discharged in preference to NO₃⁻ ions, which then decompose to give out O₂\n\nOH⁻(aq) → OH + e⁻\n4OH → 2H₂O(l) + O₂(g)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','OH & O₂','O₂ & O₂','OH & OH','Cu & Ag⁺','NEW','12-087','12-053',NULL),
(171502,'chemistry','Predict the products of electrolysis in each of the following : A dilute solution of H₂SO₄ with platinum electrodes.','H₂ & O₂','H₂SO₄(aq) → 2H⁺(aq) + SO₄²⁻(aq)\nH₂O ⇌ H⁺ + OH⁻\n\nAt cathode: H⁺ + e⁻ → H\nH + H → H₂(g)\n\nAt anode : OH⁻  → OH + e⁻\n4OH → 2H₂O + O₂(g)\n\nThus , H₂ gas is liberated at the cathode and O₂ gas at the anode.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','H₂ & O₂','H₂ & H₂','O₂ & O₂','OH & OH','NEW','12-087','12-053',NULL),
(171503,'chemistry','Predict the products of electrolysis in each of the following : An aqueous solution of CuCl₂ with platinum electrodes.','Cu & Cl₂','CuCl₂(s) + aq  → Cu²⁺(aq) + 2Cl⁻(aq)\nH₂O ⇌ H⁺ + OH⁻\nAt cathode: Cu²⁺ ions will be reduced in preference to H⁺ ions and copper will be deposited at cathode.\n\nCu²⁺ +2e⁻ → Cu\n\nAt anode: Cl⁻ ions will be discharged in preference to OH⁻ ions which remains in solution.\n\nCl⁻ → Cl +e⁻ \nCl+Cl → Cl₂(g)\n\nThus, Cu will be deposited on the cathode and Cl₂ gas will be liberated at the anode.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','Cu & Cl₂','Cu & Cl','Cl₂ & Cl₂','Cu & Cu','NEW','12-087','12-053',NULL),
(171504,'chemistry','Using the standard electrode potentials given in the table, predict if the reaction between the following is feasible.\n(a) Fe³⁺(aq) and I⁻(aq)\n(b) Ag⁺(aq) and Cu(s)\n(c) Fe³⁺(aq) and Br⁻(aq)\n(d) Ag(s) and Fe³⁺(aq)\n(e) Br₂(aq) and Fe²⁺(aq).','a,b & c','A particular reaction can be feasible if e.m.f. of the cell based on the E° values is positive. Keeping this in mind, let us predict the feasibility of the reactions. Refer diagram(ne1719)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b & c','a,b, c & d','a,b','only c & d','NEW','12-069','12-035','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/171504q.png'),
(171505,'chemistry','Three electrolytic cells A, B, C containing solutions of ZnSO₄, AgNO₃ and CuSO₄, respectively are connected in series. A steady current of 1.5 amperes was passed through them until 1.45 g of silver deposited at the cathode of call B. How long did the current flow?','863.73 seconds','Given: I = 1.5A , W = 1.45g of Ag ,t=?,E=108,n = 1\n\nUsing Faraday\'s 1st law of electrolysis W = ZIt or, W = E/(nF) × It\n\nt = 1.45×96500 /1.5×108  = 863.73 seconds','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','863.73 seconds','3.73 seconds','73 seconds','8633 seconds','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171506,'chemistry','Three electrolytic cells A, B, C containing solutions of ZnSO₄, AgNO₃ and CuSO₄, respectively are connected in series. A steady current of 1.5 amperes was passed through them until 45 g of silver deposited at the cathode of call B. What mass of copper and zinc were deposited?','0.426g & 0.438g','Refer diagram(ne1720)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','0.426g & 0.438g','0.426g & 0.426g','0.438g & 0.438g','1g & 48g','NEW','12-086','12-052','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/171506q.png'),
(171507,'chemistry','A solution of Ni(N0₃)₂ is electrolyzed between platinum electrodes using a current of 5 amperes for 20 minutes. What mass of Ni is deposited at the cathode?','1.825 g','Quantity of electricity passed =(5A) × (20 × 60 sec)=6000C\nThus, 2F, i.e., 2 x 96500C of charge deposit = 1 mole of Ni=58.7 g .\n→6000 C of charge will deposite = 58.7 × 6000/2 × 96500 = 1.825 g of Ni','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','1.825 g','1825 g','25 g','18245 g','NEW','12-086','12-052','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/171507q.png'),
(171508,'chemistry','How much electricity is required in coulomb for the oxidation of 1 mol of H₂O to O₂?','193000C','The electrode reaction for 1 mol of H₂O is\nH₂O → H₂ + 1/2O₂\n\nO²⁻ → 1/2O₂ + 2e⁻\nQuantity of electricity required = 2F = 2 × 96500C = 193000C','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','19300 C','193000C','9650 C','96500 C','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171509,'chemistry','How much electricity is required in coulomb for the oxidation of  1 mol of FeO to Fe₂O₃','96500 C','The electrode reaction for 1 mol of FeO is\n\nFeO + 1/2O₂ → 1/2 Fe₂O₃ i.e. Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ + e⁻\n→ Quantity of electricity required = 1F=96500 C','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','96500 C','9650 C','96.500 C','965000 C','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171510,'chemistry','How much electricity in terms of Faraday is required to produce 20 g of Ca from molten CaCl₂','2F & 1F','Ca² +  2e → Ca(s) \n          2F       40g\n\nTo produce 40 g of CaCl₂, charge needed = 2F\n\nTo produce 20 g of CaCl₂, charge needed = 1F','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','2F & 1F','1F & 1F','1F & 2F','2F & 2F','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171511,'chemistry','How much electricity in terms of Faraday is required to produce 40.0 g of Al from molten Al₂O₃ ?','6F & 4.44 F','Al₂O₃ + 6e⁻ → 2Al + 3O²⁻\n            6F    (2×27)g\nTo produce 54 g of Al, charge needed = 6F\n\nTo produce 40 g of Al, charge needed = (40/54) × 6 =  4.44 F','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','6F & 4.44 F','6F & 6 F','4.44F & 4.44 F','2F & 1F','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171512,'chemistry','How much charge is required for the following reductions 1 mol of Al³⁺ to Al?','289500 C','The electrode reaction is Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ ——> Al\n∴ Quantity of charge required for reduction of 1 mol of Al³⁺=3F=3 x 96500C=289500C.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','289500 C','28950 C','2895000 C','28.9500 C','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171513,'chemistry','How much charge is required for the following reductions 1 mol of Cu²⁺ to Cu ?','193000 C','The electrode reaction is Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ ——–> Cu\n∴ Quantity of charge required for reduction of 1 mol of Cu²⁺=2F=2 x 96500=193000 C.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','193000 C','19300 C','19.3000 C','1930000 C','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171514,'chemistry','How much charge is required for the following reductions 1 mol of MnO₄- to Mn²⁺?','4825000 C','The electrode reaction is MnO₄- ———-> Mn²⁺.\ni.e., Mn⁷⁺ + 5e- –——-> Mn²⁺.\n∴ Quantity of charge required = 5F\n=5 x 96500 C=4825000.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','4825000 C','4825 C','48.25000 C','482500 C','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171515,'chemistry','Conductivity of 0.00241 M acetic acid is 7.896 x 10⁻⁵ S /cm. Calculate its molar conductivity.','32.76 S cm²/mol','Λᶜₘ = k × 1000 / molarity = 7.896 x 10⁻⁵ × 1000 / 0.00241 = 32.76 S cm²/mol','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','32.76 S cm²/mol','3276 S cm²/mol','327.6 S cm²/mol','32.76 S m²/mol','NEW','12-079','12-045',NULL),
(171516,'chemistry','Conductivity of 0.00241 M acetic acid is 7.896 x 10⁻⁵ S /cm. If Λ⁰ₘ, for acetic acid is 390.5 S cm² /mol, what is its dissociation constant? [Λᶜₘ = 32.76 S cm²/mol]','1.86 × 10⁻⁵','α = Λᶜₘ/Λ⁰ₘ = 32.76/390.5 = 8.4 × 10⁻² \nKa = cα²/1-α = 0.00241 (8.4 × 10⁻² )² / 1-8.4 × 10⁻² = 1.86 × 10⁻⁵','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','1.86 × 10⁻⁵','1.86 × 10⁵','1.86 × 10³','1.8','NEW','12-083','12-049',NULL),
(171517,'chemistry','The resistance of a conductivity cell containing 0.001 M KCI solution at 298 K is 1500 Ω .What is the cell constant if conductivity of 0.001 M KCI solution at 298 K is 0.146 x 10⁻³ S /cm?','0.219/cm','Cell constant= Conductivity/ Conductance= Conductivity × Resistance = 0.146 x 10⁻³ × 1500 = 0.219/cm','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','0.219/cm','219/cm','2.19/cm','21.9/cm','NEW','12-079','12-045',NULL),
(171518,'chemistry','The conductivity of 0.20 M solution of KCl at 298 K is 0.0248 S /cm. Calculate its molar conductivity.','124 S cm²/mol','Λₘ = k × 1000/molarity= 0.0248 × 1000/0.2 = 124 S cm²/mol.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','124 S cm²/mol','84 S cm²/mol','144 S cm²/mol','244 S cm²/mol','NEW','12-079','12-045',NULL),
(171519,'chemistry','In the button cells widely used in watches and other devices the following reaction takes place: Refer diagram(n1726)','1.104V & -2.13 × 10⁵ J','Refer diagram(ne1721)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','1.104V & -2.13 × 10⁵ J','-2.13 × 10⁵ J & 1.104V','-2.13 × 10⁵ J','1 V & 2 J','NEW','12-074','12-040','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/171519q.png'),
(171520,'chemistry','Calculate the standard cell potentials of the galvanic cells in which the following reactions take place. Refer diagram(n1727)','3.17 × 10³⁴','Refer diagram(ne1722)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','3.17 × 10³⁴','3.17 × 10²²','8.17 × 10³⁴','3.17 × 10⁴²','NEW','12-069','12-035','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/171520q.png'),
(171521,'chemistry','Calculate the standard cell potentials of the galvanic cells in which the following reactions take place. Refer diagram(n1728)','3.22','Refer diagram(ne1723)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','3.22','4.15','4.72','2.22','NEW','12-069','12-035',NULL),
(171522,'chemistry','Depict the galvanic cell in which the reaction\nZn(s) + 2Ag⁺(aq) —-> 7M²⁺(aq) + 2Ag (s) takes place. Further show:\nWhich of the electrode is negatively charged?','Zn','Anode, i. e, zinc electrode will be negatively charged.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','Zn','Ag','Zn & Ag','None of them','NEW','12-068','12-034',NULL),
(171523,'chemistry','Depict the galvanic cell in which the reaction\nZn(s) + 2Ag⁺(aq) —-> 7M²⁺(aq) + 2Ag (s) takes place. Further show: The carriers of the current in the cell.','The current will flow from silver to copper in the external circuit','The current will flow from silver to copper in the external circuit','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','The current will flow from silver to copper in the internal circuit','The current will flow from Cu to Ag in the external circuit','The current won\'t flow in the external circuit','The current will flow from silver to copper in the external circuit','NEW','12-068','12-034',NULL),
(171524,'chemistry','Depict the galvanic cell in which the reaction\nZn(s) + 2Ag⁺(aq) —-> 7M²⁺(aq) + 2Ag (s) takes place. Further show: Individual reaction at each electrode. What\'s its products??','Ag & Zn²⁺','At anode: Zn(s) ——–> Zn²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻\nAt cathode: 2Ag⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ ——–> 2Ag(s)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','Ag & Zn²⁺','Ag⁺ & Zn²⁺','Ag & Zn','Ag²⁺ & Zn','NEW','12-068','12-034',NULL),
(171525,'chemistry','Given the standard electrode potentials, K⁺/K=-2. 93 V, Ag⁺/Ag = 0.80 V, Hg²⁺/Hg =0.79V, Mg²⁺/Mg=-2.37V, Cr³⁺/Cr=0.74V.\nArrange these metals in their increasing order of reducing power.','Ag<Hg<Cr<Mg<K','Higher the oxidation potential more easily it is oxidized and hence greater is the reducing power. Thus, increasing order of reducing power will be Ag<Hg<Cr<Mg<K.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','Ag<Hg<Cr<Mg<K','Ag>Hg<Cr<Mg<K','Ag<Hg<Cr>Mg<K','Ag>Hg>Cr>Mg<K','NEW','12-070','12-036',NULL),
(171526,'chemistry','Arrange the following metals in the order in which they displace each other from their salts.\nAl, Cu, Fe, Mg and Zn','Mg, Al, Zn, Fe, Cu','Mg, Al, Zn, Fe, Cu','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','Mg, Al, Zn, Fe, Cu','Fe, Al, Zn, Mg, Cu','Mg, Cu, Zn, Fe, Al','Fe, Al, Zn, Mg, Cu','NEW','12-071','12-037',NULL),
(190124,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion(A) :The air becomes dry in the presence of silica gel.\nReason(R) : Silica gel adsorbs the water molecules.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The air becomes dry in the presence of silica gel because the water molecules get adsorbed on the surface of the gel.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph 5.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is false but the Reason is true.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190125,'chemistry','The process of removing an adsorbed substance from a surface on which it is adsorbed is called ______ .','Desorption','The process of removing an adsorbed substance from a surface on which it is adsorbed is called desorption.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph 5.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','Adsorption','Absorption','Desorption','Sorption','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190126,'chemistry','Which statement is true about adsorption :','The substance is concentrated only at the surface and does not penetrate through the surface to the bulk of the adsorbent.','In adsorption, the substance is concentrated only at the surface and does not penetrate through the surface to the bulk of the adsorbent.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph 5.1.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'2','mcq','The substance is concentrated only at the surface and does not penetrate through the surface to the bulk of the adsorbent.','The substance is uniformly distributed throughout the bulk of the solid.','The substance is unevenly distributed on surface of solid.','The surface of solid is  porous.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190127,'chemistry','Which statement is true :\na. The dipping of chalk in ink is both adsorption and absorption phenomena.\nb. Water vapours get absorbed on calcium chloride.\nc. Water vapours get absorbed on silica gel.\nd. Oil on the surface of water is an adsorption process.','a, b and d','a. When a chalk stick is dipped in ink, the surface retains the colour of the ink due to adsorption of coloured molecules while the solvent of the ink goes deeper into the stick due to absorption. On breaking the chalk stick, it is found to be white from inside. \nb. A distinction can be made between absorption and adsorption by taking an example of water vapour. Water vapours are absorbed by anhydrous calcium chloride.\nc. Water vapours are adsorbed by silica gel.\nd. Oil in water is also an example of adsorption.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph 5.1.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'2','mcq','a, b and c','a, b and d','b, c and d','c, d and a','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190128,'chemistry','Match the following.','Water vapours on Calcium Chloride-Absorption, Water vapours on Silica gel-Adsorption, Oil in water-Adsorption, Sponge soaked in water-Sorption','a. In absorption, the substance is uniformly distributed throughout the bulk of the solid. e.g., Water vapours are absorbed by anhydrous calcium chloride.\nb. In adsorption, the substance is concentrated only at the surface. e.g., Water vapours are adsorbed by silica gel.\nc. In adsorption, the substance is concentrated only at the surface. e.g., Oil in water is adsorption.\nd. Both adsorption and absorption take place simultaneously. e.g., Sponge soaked in water is sorption. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph 5.1.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','match','Water vapours on Calcium Chloride,Absorption','Water vapours on Silica gel,Adsorption','Oil in water,Adsorption','Sponge soaked in water,Sorption','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190129,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : In adsorption the concentration of the adsorbate increases only at the surface of the adsorbent.\nReason : In absorption the concentration is uniform throughout the bulk of the solid.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','In adsorption the concentration of the adsorbate increases only at the surface of the adsorbent, while in absorption the concentration is uniform throughout the bulk of the solid.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph 5.1.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is false but the Reason is true.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190130,'chemistry','Which among the following statement is true?','Both adsorption and absorption can take place simultaneously.','Adsorption and absorption can both occur at the same time. The term sorption is used to describe both the processes.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph 5.1.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'2','mcq','Both adsorption and absorption can take place simultaneously.','Adsorption and absorption can never take place simultaneously.','Adsorption and absorption can take place one after other.','Adsorption is always irreversible.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190131,'chemistry','When both adsorption and absorption take place simultaneously, it is known as _____.','Sorption','Adsorption and absorption can both occur at the same time. The term sorption is used to describe both the processes.\nIn adsorption the concentration of the adsorbate increases only at the surface of the adsorbent, while in absorption the concentration is uniform throughout the bulk of the solid.\n\nAdsorption and absorption can both occur at the same time.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph 5.1.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','Sorption','Desorption','Adsorption','Absorption','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190132,'chemistry','Which among the following statement(s) is not true?','Adsorption arises due to the fact that the surface particles of the adsorbent are in the same environment as the particles inside the bulk.','Adsorption arises due to the fact that the surface particles of the adsorbent are not in the same environment as the particles inside the bulk. \nIn absorption the concentration is uniform throughout the bulk of the solid.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph 5.1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'2','mcq','Adsorption arises due to the fact that the surface particles of the adsorbent are not in the same environment as the particles inside the bulk.','Adsorption arises due to the fact that the surface particles of the adsorbent are in the same environment as the particles inside the bulk.','In absorption the concentration is uniform throughout the bulk of the solid.','Adsorption and absorption can both occur at the same time.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190133,'chemistry','The extent of adsorption _____ with the increase of surface area per unit mass of the adsorbent at a given temperature and pressure.','Increases','At a given temperature and pressure, the amount of adsorption increases as the adsorbent surface area per unit mass increases.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.125, paragraph 5.1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','Increases','Decreases','Remains constant','Remains inconstant','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190134,'chemistry','During adsorption, there is always a _____ in residual forces of the surface.','decrease','During adsorption, there is always a decrease in surface residual forces, i.e., a decrease in surface energy, which appears as heat.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.125, Paragraph 5.1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','increase','decrease','outage','shutdown','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190135,'chemistry','Which of the following processes does not occur at the interface of phases?','Homogeneous catalysis','Many important phenomena, noticeable amongst these being corrosion, electrode processes, heterogeneous catalysis, dissolution and crystallisation occur at interfaces. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12, page no. 123, Paragraph \'Introduction\')','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','Crystallization','Heterogeneous catalysis','Homogeneous catalysis','Corrosion','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190136,'chemistry','Consider the given reaction. What conclusions can you make about the catalyst?\ndiagram(n1901)','Catalyst and products are in the same phases.','Heterogeneous catalysis refers to the catalytic reaction in which the reactants and catalyst are in different phases. In the given reaction, one of the reactants is in liquid state and the other in gaseous state while the catalyst is in the solid state. Hence, the catalyst is heterogeneous in nature.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.131. Paragraph 5.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','No reaction occurs','The catalyst is homogeneous in nature.','Catalyst and reactants are in the same phases.','Catalyst and products are in the same phases.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/190136q.png'),
(190137,'chemistry','Surface phenomena called \'Sorption\' is defines the phenomenon :','When both adsorption and absorption take place simultaneously.','Adsorption and absorption can both occur at the same time. The term sorption is used to describe both the processes.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph 5.1.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','The process of uniform distribution of particles throughout the body.','The process of retention of particles at surface.','When both adsorption and absorption take place simultaneously.','Separation of adsorbent and adsorbent.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190138,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\n\nAssertion : Residual attractive forces of the adsorbate are responsible for attracting the adsorbent particles on its surface. \nReason : Residual forces are balanced forces.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','The adsorbent residual attractive forces are responsible for attracting the adsorbate particles on its surface.\n\nInside the adsorbent all the forces acting between the particles are mutually balanced but on the surface the particles are not surrounded by atoms\nor molecules of their kind on all sides, and hence they possess unbalanced\nor residual attractive forces\n\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.125. Paragraph 5.1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190139,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is true?','The extent of adsorption increases with the increase of surface area per unit mass of the adsorbent at a given temperature and pressure.','The extent of adsorption increases with the increase of surface area per unit mass of the adsorbent at a given temperature and pressure.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.125, paragraph 5.1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'2','mcq','The extent of adsorption decrease with the increase of surface area per unit mass of the adsorbent at a given temperature and pressure.','The extent of adsorption increases with the increase of surface area per unit mass of the adsorbent at a given temperature and pressure.','The extent of adsorption increases with the decrease of surface area per unit mass of the adsorbent at a given temperature and pressure.','The extent of adsorption remains constant with the decrease of surface area per unit mass of the adsorbent at a given temperature and pressure.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190140,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : During adsorption, there is always a decrease in residual forces of the surface.\nReason : During adsorption Entropy of system decreases.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','During adsorption, the surface\'s residual forces always diminish, resulting in a drop in surface energy, which appears as heat. When a gas is adsorbed, its molecules\' freedom of movement is restricted. This results in a decrease in the entropy of the gas after adsorption. Adsorption is consequently accompanied with a drop in the system\'s enthalpy as well as entropy.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.125, paragraph 5.1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190141,'chemistry','Adsorption is :\na. Endothermic Process\nb. Exothermic Process\nc. Irreversible Process \nd. Reversible Process','b, c and d','During adsorption, there is always a drop in surface residual forces, i.e., a decrease in surface energy, which appears as heat. As a result, adsorption is always an exothermic process. Adsorption is classified into two categories, Which : \ni. Physisorption of a gas by a solid is usually reversible. \nii. Chemisorption is usually irreversible in nature because it includes compound synthesis. \nAdsorption is hence capable of being both reversible and irreversible.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.125, paragraph 5.1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','a, b and c','b, c and d','a, c and d','a, b and d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190142,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : The entropy of the gas after adsorption decreases.\nReason : When a gas is adsorbed, the freedom of movement of its molecules become restricted.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','When a gas is adsorbed, its molecules can no longer move as freely. This results in a decrease in the entropy of the gas after adsorption, i.e., entropy ∆S is negative.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.125, paragraph 5.1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190143,'chemistry','At the equilibrium position in the process of adsorption ______.','∆H = T∆S','As adsorption progresses, ∆H gets less and less negative until it equals T∆S and ∆G equals zero. Equilibrium is attained at this point. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.125, paragraph 5.1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'2','mcq','∆H > 0','∆H = T∆S','∆H > T∆S','∆H < T∆S','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190144,'chemistry','Condition for spontaneous adsorption process are.\ni. Gibbs free energy must be negative. \nii. Enthalpy of system must be positive.\niii. Entropy of system must be negative.\niv. Gibbs free energy must be positive.','Both i and iii','Adsorption is always an exothermic reaction. In other words, the adsorption enthalpy ∆H is always negative. When a gas is adsorbed, its molecules can no longer move as freely. This results in a decrease in the entropy of the gas after adsorption, i.e., entropy ∆S is negative. The thermodynamic condition for a spontaneous process is that Gibbs free energy ∆G must be negative at constant temperature and pressure.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.125, paragraph 5.1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both i and ii','Both ii and iii','Both iii and iv','Both i and iii','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190145,'chemistry','As the adsorption proceeds ∆H becomes _____.','less & less negative','As adsorption progresses, ∆H gets less and less negative until it equals T∆S and ∆G equals zero. Equilibrium is attained at this point. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.125, paragraph 5.1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','more and more negative','less & less negative','Remains constant','increase initially, then decrease','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190146,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : As the adsorption proceeds ∆H becomes less and less negative ultimately ∆H becomes equal to T∆S and ∆G becomes zero.\nReason : Extent of adsorption is inversely proportional to critical temperature.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','As adsorption progresses, ∆H gets less and less negative until it equals T∆S and ∆G equals zero. Equilibrium is attained at this point.\nThe extent of adsorption increases with the increase of surface area per unit mass of the adsorbent at a given temperature and pressure. Hence, The statement of reason is false.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.125, paragraph 5.1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190147,'chemistry','Adsorption cannot be accompanied if there is :','an increase in entropy of system','Adsorption is always an exothermic reaction. In other words, the adsorption enthalpy ∆H is always negative. When a gas is adsorbed, its molecules can no longer move as freely. This results in a decrease in the entropy of the gas after adsorption, i.e., entropy ∆S is negative. The thermodynamic condition for a spontaneous process is that Gibbs free energy ∆G must be negative at constant temperature and pressure.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.125, paragraph 5.1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','a decrease in entropy of system','a decrease in enthalpy of system','entropy ∆S is negative','an increase in entropy of system','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190148,'chemistry','Which of the following is not accurate for "Physical Adsorption" ?','Enthalpy of adsorption is high (80-240 kJ mol⁻¹) in this case.','The properties of "Physical Adsorption" are as follows :\ni. It depends on the nature of gas. More easily liquefiable gases are adsorbed readily.\nii. Enthalpy of adsorption is low (20-40 kJ mol⁻¹)in this case.\niii. No appreciable activation energy is needed.\niv. It depends on the surface area. It increases with an increase of surface area.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.126-127, Table 5.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','It depends on the nature of gas.','Enthalpy of adsorption is high (80-240 kJ mol⁻¹) in this case.','No appreciable activation energy is needed.','It increases with an increase of surface area.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190149,'chemistry','There are mainly ____ type of adsorption.','Two','There are mainly two types of adsorption of gases on solids.\ni. Physical adsorption or physisorption.\nii. Chemical adsorption or chemisorption.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.125, paragraph 5.1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','One','Two','Three','Four','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190150,'chemistry','Which among the following characteristics is associated with " Adsorption"?','ΔG, ΔH and ΔS all are negative.','Adsorption is always an exothermic reaction. In other words, the adsorption enthalpy ∆H is always negative. When a gas is adsorbed, its molecules can no longer move as freely. This results in a decrease in the entropy of the gas after adsorption, i.e., entropy ∆S is negative. The thermodynamic condition for a spontaneous process is that Gibbs free energy ∆G must be negative at constant temperature and pressure.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.125, paragraph 5.1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','ΔG is negative but ΔH and ΔS are positive.','ΔG, ΔH and ΔS all are negative.','ΔG and ΔS are negative but ΔH is positive.','ΔG is negative but ΔH andΔS are positive.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190151,'chemistry','Mark the options that are correct about "Physical adsorption" :\ni. Extent of adsorption decrease with an increase in temperature.\nii. As temperature rises, the extent of adsorption decreases initially and subsequently increases.\niii. Reversible Process.\niv. A high rate of enthalpy of adsorption.\nv. High activation energy is required.\nvi. Directly proportional to surface area.','i, iii and vi','The properties of "Physical Adsorption" are as follows :\ni. Statement (i) is true as low temperature is favourable for adsorption. It decreases with increase of temperature.\nii. Statement (ii) is false as the extent of adsorption decreases with increase of temperature. \niii. Statement (iii) is true as it is reversible in nature. \niv. Statement (iv) is false as the enthalpy of adsorption is low (20-40 kJ mol⁻¹)in this case.\nv. Statement (v) is false too as no appreciable activation energy is needed\nvi. Statement (vi) is true as it depends on the surface area. It increases with an increase of surface area. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.126-127, Table 5.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','ii, iv and vi','i, iii and iv','i, iii and vi','ii, iii and iv','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190152,'chemistry','Mark the options that are correct about "Chemical Adsorption" :\ni. Extent of adsorption decrease with rise in temperature.\nii. High temperature is favourable for adsorption.\niii. Reversible Process.\niv. High enthalpy of adsorption.\nv. Activation energy involved.\nvi. Also known as Freundlich adsorption.','ii,iv and v','The properties of "Chemical Adsorption" are as follows :\ni. Statement (i) is incorrect as temperature favours chemical adsorption.\nii. Statement (ii) is true as high temperature is favourable for adsorption. It increases with the increase of temperature.\niii. Statement (iii) is incorrect as chemical adsorption is irreversible. \niv. Statement (iv) is true as enthalpy of adsorption is high (80-240 kJ mol⁻¹) in this case.\nv. Statement (v) is also true as high activation energy is sometimes needed.\nvi. Statement (vi) is incorrect because the other name for chemical adsorption is activated adsorption.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.126-127, Table 5.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','ii, iii and v','i, iii and vi','ii,iv and v','ii, iv and vi','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190153,'chemistry','Accumulation of gas on the surface of a solid occurs on account of weak van der Waals’ forces, the adsorption is termed as ______.','Physisorption','If accumulation of gas on the surface of a solid occurs on account of weak van der Waals’ forces, the adsorption is termed as physical adsorption or physisorption. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 125; Paragraph 5.1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','Chemisorption','Physisorption','Sorption','Desorption','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190154,'chemistry','When the gas molecules or atoms are held to the solid surface by chemical bonds, the adsorption is termed ______.','Chemisorption','When the gas molecules or atoms are held to the solid surface by chemical bonds, the adsorption is termed chemical adsorption or chemisorption. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 125; Paragraph 5.1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','Physisorption','Chemisorption','Desorption','Sorption','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190155,'chemistry','High Activation energy involves in _____.','Chemisorption','Enthalpy of chemisorption is high (80-240 kJ mol⁻¹) as it involves chemical bond formation.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 126; Paragraph 5.1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','Physisorption','Sorption','Desorption','Chemisorption','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190156,'chemistry','Strong covalent and ionic bonds are involved in _____.','Chemisorption','When the gas molecules or atoms are held to the solid surface by chemical bonds, the adsorption is termed chemical adsorption or chemisorption. The chemical bonds may be covalent or ionic in nature. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 125; Paragraph 5.1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','Desorption','Chemisorption','Physisorption','Sorption','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190157,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Dihydrogen adsorption on nickel begins as physisorption and subsequently progress to chemisorption.\nReadon : Van der Waals forces first adsorb dihydrogen on nickel. Hydrogen molecules then dissociate to generate hydrogen atoms, which are retained on the surface by chemisorption.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Physical adsorption at low temperature may pass into chemisorption as the temperature is increased. For example, dihydrogen is first adsorbed on nickel by van der Waals’ forces. Molecules of hydrogen then dissociate to form hydrogen atoms which are held on the surface by chemisorption.  \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 125; Paragraph 5.1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190158,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Lack of specificity shown in physisorption toward a particular gas.\nReason : Van Der waals forces are universal in nature.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Characteristics of physisorption\n(i) Lack of specificity : Because Van der Waals forces are universal, a given adsorbent surface does not exhibit any preference for a specific gas.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 125; Paragraph 5.1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190159,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : 1gm of activated charcoal adsorb more Sulphur Dioxide (critical temperature 630K) than Methane (critical temperature 190K).\nReason : The extent of adsorption is inversely proportional to critical temperature.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','The amount of gas adsorbed by a solid depends on the nature of gas. In general, easily liquefiable gases (i.e., with higher critical temperatures) are readily adsorbed as Van der Waals forces are stronger near the critical temperatures. Thus, 1g of activated charcoal adsorbs more sulphur dioxide (critical temperature 630K), than methane (critical temperature 190K). \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 125; Paragraph 5.1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190160,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Easily liquefiable gases do not adsorb easily.\nReason : Easily liquefiable gases have high critical temperature as van der Waals’ forces are weaker near the critical temperature.','Both statements are false.','The amount of gas adsorbed by a solid depends on the nature of gas. In general, easily liquefiable gases (i.e., with higher critical temperatures) are readily adsorbed as van der Waals’ forces are stronger near the critical temperatures. Thus, 1g of activated charcoal adsorbs more sulphur dioxide (critical temperature 630K), than methane (critical temperature 190K).\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 125; Paragraph 5.1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190161,'chemistry','Absorption of gas is increased when the pressure is _____ as the Volume of gas ______ , Respectively;.','increased, decreases','Physical adsorption of a gas by a solid is generally reversible. Thus, \nSolid + Gas ⇌ Gas/Solid + Heat  \nMore of gas is adsorbed when pressure is increased as the volume of the gas decreases (Le–Chateliers’s  principle) and the gas can be removed by decreasing pressure.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 126; Paragraph 5.1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'2','mcq','decreased, decreases','increased, decreases','increased, increases','decreased, increases','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190162,'chemistry','The extent of adsorption increase with ______ of surface area of ______ .','increase, adsorbent','➣ Surface area of adsorbent : The extent of adsorption increases with the increase of surface area of the adsorbent.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 126; Paragraph 5.1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'2','mcq','increase, adsorbate','increase, adsorbent','decrease, adsorbent','decrease, adsorbate','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190163,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Porous materials have large surface area are good adsorbent. \nReason : The extent of adsorption increase with increase of surface area.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','➣ Surface area of adsorbent : The extent of adsorption increases with the increase of surface area of the adsorbent. Thus, finely divided metals and porous substances having large surface areas are good adsorbents.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 126; Paragraph 5.1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190164,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Enthalpy of adsorption is high in physical adsorption.\nReason : Because the attraction between gas molecule and solid surface is only due to covalent and ionic forces.','Both statements are false.','➣ Enthalpy of adsorption : No doubt, physical adsorption is an exothermic process but its enthalpy of adsorption is quite low (20 - 40 kJ mol⁻¹). This is because the attraction between gas molecules and solid surface is only due to weak van der Waals’ forces.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 126; Paragraph 5.1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190165,'chemistry','Consider the following -\n1. Lack of specificity\n2. Highly specific\n3. Reversible \n4. Irreversible \n5. Low enthalpy of adsorption.\n6. High enthalpy of adsorption \n7. Extent of adsorption increase with increase of surface area of adsorbent.\nWhich of the above statements are True for Physisorption?','1,3,5 and 7','The properties of "Physical Adsorption" are as follows :\n1. It arises because of van der Waals’ forces. \n2. It is not specific in nature. \n3. It is reversible in nature. \n4. It depends on the nature of gas. More easily liquefiable gases are adsorbed readily.\n5. Enthalpy of adsorption is low (20 - 40 kJ mol⁻¹)in this case.\n6. Low temperature is favourable for adsorption. It decreases with increase of temperature. \n7. No appreciable activation energy is needed. \n8. It depends on the surface area. It increases with an increase of surface area. \n9. It results into multimolecular layers on adsorbent surface under high pressure.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.126-127, Table 5.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','2,4,6 and 7','2,5,6 and 7','1,3,5 and 7','1,4,5 and 7','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190166,'chemistry','Chemisorption involves compound formation which requires ______ .','high activation energy','Characteristics of Chemisorption\n➣ Irreversibility : As chemisorption involves compound formation, it is usually irreversible in nature. Chemisorption is also an exothermic process but the process is very slow at low temperatures on account of high energy of activation. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, Page no. 126, Paragraph 5.1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','high activation energy','no activation energy','low activation energy','no chemical bond formation take place','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190167,'chemistry','Oxygen is adsorbed on metals by virtue of oxide formation and hydrogen is adsorbed by transition metals due to hydride formation. The Phenomenon is called  ______ .','Chemisorption','Characteristics of chemisorption\n➣ High specificity : Chemisorption is highly specific and it will only occur if there is some possibility of chemical bonding between adsorbent and adsorbate. For example, oxygen is adsorbed on metals by virtue of oxide formation and hydrogen is adsorbed by transition metals due to hydride formation.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, Page no. 126, Paragraph 5.1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','Physisorption','Chemisorption','Absorption','Emulsion','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190168,'chemistry','Which among the following statements(s) is true for Chemisorption? \n1. High temperature is favourable for adsorption. It increases with the increase of temperature. \n2. High activation energy is sometimes needed. \n3. It also depends on the surface area. It too increases with an increase of surface area. \n4. It results into unimolecular layer.\n5. It results into multimolecular layers on adsorbent surface under high pressure.\n6. Low temperature is favourable for adsorption. It decreases with increase of temperature.','1,2,3 and 4','The properties of "Chemical Adsorption" are as follows :\n1. It is caused by chemical bond formation. \n2. It is highly specific in nature. \n3. It is irreversible. \n4. It also depends on the nature \n5. Enthalpy of adsorption is high (80-240 kJ mol⁻¹) in this case.\n6. High temperature is favourable for adsorption. It increases with the increase of temperature. \n7. High activation energy is sometimes needed. \n8. It also depends on the surface area. It too increases with an increase of surface area. \n9. It results into unimolecular layer.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, Page no. 126-127, Table 5.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','1,2,3 and 4','1,2,3 and 5','Both 1 and 5','Both 1 and 4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190169,'chemistry','The correct option representing a Freundlich adsorption isotherm is ______ .','x/m=kp⁰.⁴','Freundlich adsorption isotherm is  \nx/m = k.p¹/ⁿ (n > 1)\nIn it, value of 1/n lies in between 0 to 1. So, correct option is\nx/m = k.p⁰.⁸\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, Page no.127, Paragraph 5.1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','x/m=kp⁻⁰.⁴','x/m=kp⁵.⁵','x/m=kp⁰.⁴','x/m=kp⁻¹⁰','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190170,'chemistry','In Freundlich adsorption isotherm, the value of n is ______ .','n > 1','Freundlich adsorption isotherm is :\nx/m = k.p¹/ⁿ (n > 1)\nThe factor 1/n can have values between 0 and 1 (probable range 0.1 to 0.5).\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, Page no.127, Paragraph 5.1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','n < 1','n = 0','n > 1','n = 2.314','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190171,'chemistry','The variation in the amount of gas adsorbed by the adsorbent with  ______ at constant ______ can be expressed by means of a curve termed as adsorption isotherm.','pressure, temperature','The variation in the amount of gas adsorbed by the adsorbent with pressure at constant temperature can be expressed by means of a curve termed as adsorption isotherm.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, Page no.127, Paragraph 5.1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'2','mcq','volume, temperature','temperature, pressure','pressure, temperature','volume,pressure','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190172,'chemistry','The accurate relation of Freundlich isotherm is ______ .','x/m = k.p¹/ⁿ (n > 1)','Freundlich adsorption isotherm is :\nx/m = k.p¹/ⁿ (n > 1)\nwhere x is the mass of the gas adsorbed on mass m of the adsorbent at pressure P, k and n are constants which depend on the nature of the adsorbent and the gas at a particular temperature.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, Page no.127, Paragraph 5.1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','x/m = k.p⁻¹/ⁿ (n > 1)','x/m = k.p¹/n (n < 1)','x/m = k.p¹/ⁿ (n > 1)','x/m = k.p⁻¹/¹⁰ (n > 1)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190173,'chemistry','x/m = k.p¹/n Where x and m are ______  respectively.','x is the mass of gas adsorbed on mass m of Adsorbent','Freundlich adsorption isotherm is :\nx/m = k.p¹/ⁿ (n > 1)\nwhere x is the mass of the gas adsorbed on mass m of the adsorbent at pressure P, k and n are constants which depend on the nature of the adsorbent and the gas at a particular temperature.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, Page no.127, Paragraph 5.1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','m is the mass of gas absorbed on mass x of Adsorbent','x is the mass of gas adsorbed on mass m of Adsorbent','x is the pressure applied on the gas adsorbed on mass m of Adsorbent','x is the temperature of the gas adsorbed on mass m of Adsorbent','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190174,'chemistry','x/m = k.p¹/n , n depends upon the','gas at a particular temperature','Freundlich adsorption isotherm is :\nx/m = k.p¹/ⁿ (n > 1)\nwhere x is the mass of the gas adsorbed on mass m of the adsorbent at pressure P, k and n are constants which depend on the nature of the adsorbent and the gas at a particular temperature.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, Page no.127, Paragraph 5.1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','nature of the absorbent','gas at a particular temperature','moles of the gas','pressure of the gas','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190175,'chemistry','Adsorption isotherms approach toward saturation at _____ .','high pressure','Adsorption isotherms are often represented by a curve that plots the mass of the gas adsorbed per grams of adsorbent against pressure. These graphs show that at a constant pressure, physical adsorption decreases as temperature rises. At high pressure, these curves seem to approach saturation.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, Page no.127, Paragraph 5.1.4) diagram(ne1909)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','low pressure','low temperature','high pressure','high temperature','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190176,'chemistry','log x/m = log k + 1/n log p \nThe slope of this expression is ______ .','1/n','log x/m = log k + 1/n log p \nThe validity of the Freundlich isotherm can be demonstrated by graphing log x/m on the y-axis (ordinate) and log p on the x-axis (abscissa). If the result is a straight line, the Freundlich isotherm is valid; otherwise, it is not. The slope of the first straight line yields the value n.The intercept on the y-axis gives the value of log k.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, Page no.127, Paragraph 5.1.4) diagram(ne1910)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','p/k','log 1/n','1/n','log p/k','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190177,'chemistry','The factor 1/n can have values between :','0 - 1','The factor 1/n can have values between 0 and 1 (probable range 0.1 to 0.5).\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, Page no.127, Paragraph 5.1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','0 - 1','0 - 5','(-1) - 0','0.5 - 1.5','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190178,'chemistry','Physical adsorption of a gas by a material is usually reversible. Which of the following equations is valid for this process :','Solid + Gas ⇌ Gas/Solid + Heat','Physical adsorption of a gas by a solid is generally reversible. Thus, \nSolid + Gas ⇌ Gas/Solid + Heat\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, Page no.126, Paragraph 5.1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'2','mcq','Solid + Gas ⇌ Gas/liquid + Heat','Solid + Gas ⇌ Gas/Solid + Heat','Solid + Gas ⇌ Solid + Heat','Solid + Gas ⇌ Aerosol + Heat','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190179,'chemistry','Which is not correct regarding the adsorption of a gas on surface of a solid?','On increasing temperature, adsorption increases continuously.','In the case of physical adsorption, the extent of adsorption (x/m) decreases with increasing temperature, whereas in the case of chemical adsorption, the extent of adsorption (x/m) increases first, reaching a maximum, and then decreasing.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, Page no. 126, Paragraph 5.1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','On increasing temperature, adsorption increases continuously.','Enthalpy and entropy changes are negative.','It is a reversible reaction.','Adsorption is more for some specific substance.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190180,'chemistry','The Langmuir adsorption isotherm is deduced using the assumption :','the adsorption sites are equivalent in their \nability to adsorb the particles.','The Langmuir adsorption isotherm is based on the idea that every adsorption site is equivalent and that a particle\'s ability to bind there is irrespective of whether nearby sites are occupied or not.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'2','mcq','the adsorption sites are equivalent in their \nability to adsorb the particles.','the heat of adsorption varies with coverage.','the adsorbed molecules interact with each other.','the adsorption takes place in multilayers.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190181,'chemistry','What happen when acetic acid in water is shaken with charcoal?','The concentration of acid decreases','When a solution of acetic acid in water is shaken with charcoal, a part of the acid is adsorbed by the charcoal and the concentration of the acid decreases in the solution.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 128 ; Paragraph 5.1.5)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','pH increases','The concentration of acid decreases','The concentration of acid increases','The colour of the solution changes','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190182,'chemistry','Why Acetic Acid concentration decrease when it is shaken with charcoal?','Acid is adsorbed by the charcoal','When a solution of acetic acid in water is shaken with charcoal, a part of the acid is adsorbed by the charcoal and the concentration of the acid decreases in the solution.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 128 ; Paragraph 5.1.5)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','Acid is absorbed by the charcoal','Charcoal disassociate acetic acid into carbide and water','Acid is adsorbed by the charcoal','Acetic acid dissociates charcoal into carbide and water','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190183,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Litmus solution when shaken with charcoal it become colorless.\nReason : Charcoal act as an adsorbent.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','When a solution of acetic acid in water is shaken with charcoal, a part of the acid is adsorbed by the charcoal and the concentration of the acid decreases in the solution. Similarly, the litmus solution when shaken with charcoal becomes colourless.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 128 ; Paragraph 5.1.5)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190184,'chemistry','The precipitate of Mg(OH)² attain blue colour when precipitated in presence of magneson reagent. This is due to :','Adsorption of magneson','The precipitate of Mg(OH)₂ attains blue colour when precipitated in presence of magneson reagent. The colour is due to adsorption of magneson. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 128 ; Paragraph 5.1.5)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','Basicity of solution decrease','Adsorption of magneson','Basicity of solution increase','Absorption of magneson','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190185,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is true about adsorption in solution?\n(i) The extent of adsorption increase with an increase in temperature as it get more kinetic energy in solution. \n(ii) The extent of adsorption increases with an increase of surface area\nof the adsorbent.\n(iii) The extent of adsorption depends on the concentration of the solvent\nin solution.\n(iv) The extent of adsorption depends on the nature of the adsorbent\nand the adsorbate.','Both ii & iv','The following observations have been made in the case of adsorption from solution phase :\n(i) The extent of adsorption decreases with an increase in temperature. \n(ii) The extent of adsorption increases with an increase of surface area of the adsorbent. \n(iii) The extent of adsorption depends on the concentration of the solute in solution. \n(iv) The extent of adsorption depends on the nature of the adsorbent and the adsorbate.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 128 ; Paragraph 5.1.5)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'2','mcq','Both iii & iv','Both i & iii','Both ii & iv','ii, iii & iv','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190186,'chemistry','Expression of adsorption from solution is :','x/m = k.C¹/ⁿ (n > 1)','The precise mechanism of adsorption from solution is not known. Freundlich’s equation approximately describes the behaviour of adsorption from solution with a difference that instead of pressure, concentration of the solution is taken into account, i.e.,\nx/m = k.C¹/ⁿ (n > 1)\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 128 ; Paragraph 5.1.5)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','x/m = k.Cn  (n > 1)','x/m = k.C (n > 1)','x/m = k.p¹/n (n > 1)','x/m = k.C¹/ⁿ (n > 1)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190187,'chemistry','log x/m = log k + 1/n log C   \nPlotting x/m against log C gives :','a straight line','log x/m = log k + 1/n log C   \nPlotting x/m against log C, a straight line is obtained which shows the validity of Freundlich isotherm. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 128 ; Paragraph 5.1.5)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','a slope','a curve','a straight line','a downward curve','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190188,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Charcoal absorb traces of air during vacuum creation.\nReason : Because extent of adsorption increase with increase of surface area of the charcoal.','Both statements are false.','An application of adsorption is the production of high vacuum. The remaining traces of air can be adsorbed by charcoal from a vessel evacuated by a vacuum pump to give a very high vacuum.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 128 ; Paragraph 5.1.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190189,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\n\nAssertion : It is recommended to use gas mask with activated charcoal for breathing in coal mines.\n\nReason : Charcoal in gas mask absorb the poisonous gases.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','An application of adsorption control of humidity. Silica and aluminium gels are used as adsorbents for removing moisture and controlling humidity.\n\n Gas masks: Gas mask (a device which consists of activated charcoal\nor mixture of adsorbents) is usually used for breathing in coal\nmines to adsorb poisonous gases.\n\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 129 ; Paragraph 5.1.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190190,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Silica Gel sachets comes with supplement tablet and medicines.\nReason : Silica Gel act as a moisture adsorbent.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','An application of adsorption control of humidity. Silica and aluminium gels are used as adsorbents for removing moisture and controlling humidity.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 129 ; Paragraph 5.1.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190191,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Adsorption of reactants on the solid surface of the catalyst increases the rate of reaction.\nReason : Catalyst provides functional group to substrate.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Mechanism of catalysis : Reactants diffuse to the surface of the catalyst. Reactant molecules adsorb on the surface of the catalyst. Chemical reaction occurs on the catalyst’s surface through formation of an intermediate. Reaction products detach from the catalyst surface, and thereby, making the surface available again for more reaction to occur.\nThe statement of reason is false because catalyst remains unchanged in mass and chemical composition at the end of the reaction and does not provide any functional group to the substrate.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 131 ; Paragraph 5.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190192,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : A mixture of noble gases can be separated by adsorption on coconut charcoal at different temperatures.\nReason : Because difference in degree of adsorption due to difference in critical temperature.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Separation of inert gases : Due to the difference in degree of adsorption\nof gases by charcoal, a mixture of noble gases can be separated by\nadsorption on coconut charcoal at different temperatures.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 129 ; Paragraph 5.1.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190193,'chemistry','Animal charcoal removes colours of solutions by :','Adsorbing coloured impurities','Animal charcoal removes colours of solutions by adsorbing coloured impurities.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 129 ; Paragraph 5.1.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','Adsorbing coloured impurities','Absorbing coloured impurities','Absorbing colourless impurities','Adsorbing colourless impurities','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190194,'chemistry','Which among the following statement(s) is true regarding " Applications of Adsorption" :\n1. Curing disease by killing germs.\n2. In froth floatation low grade sulphide Ores are separated using silica.\n3. Act as adsorption indicator.\n4. Chromatographic analysis','1,3 and 4','Applications of Adsorption :\n1. In curing diseases : A number of drugs are used to kill germs by getting adsorbed on them. \n2. Froth floatation process : A low grade sulphide ore is concentrated by separating it from silica and other earthy matter by this method using pine oil and frothing agent. \n3. Adsorption indicators : Surfaces of certain precipitates such as silver halides have the property of adsorbing some dyes like eosin, fluorescein, etc. and thereby producing a characteristic colour at the end point. \n4. Chromatographic analysis : Chromatographic analysis based on the phenomenon of adsorption finds a number of applications in analytical and industrial fields.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 129 ; Paragraph 5.1.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','2,3 and 4','1,2 and 3','1,3 and 4','3,2 and 4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190195,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Powdered substances are more effective adsorbents than their crystalline forms.\nReason : Powdered substance have high surface area.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The extent of adsorption increases with the increase of surface area per unit mass of the adsorbent at a given temperature and pressure. Powdered and porous substances have a high surface area. Hence, Powdered substances are more effective adsorbents than their crystalline forms.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 129 ; Intext questions 5.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190196,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Physisorption decrease with increase in temperature.\nReason : Physisorption is an exothermic','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Since the adsorption process is exothermic, the physical adsorption occurs readily at low temperature and decreases with increasing temperature (Le–Chateliers’s  principle).\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 126; Paragraph 5.1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190197,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Chemical adsorption of molecules on surface require activation energy.\nReason : Because the bond of the adsorbed molecule are broken.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Chemisorption is also an exothermic process but the process is very slow at low temperatures on account of high energy of activation. As chemisorption involves compound formation, Covalent or ionic bond formation requires significant amount of energy. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 129; Paragraph 5.1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190324,'chemistry','Insecticide spray is an example of _____.','Aerosols','Insecticide spray is an example of aerosol colloidal system of liquid dispersed in gas.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136; Table 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Aerosols','Emulsions','Foam','Gels','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
