(190330,'chemistry','Milk is an example of _____.','Emulsions','Milk is an example of emulsion colloidal system of liquid in liquid.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136; Table 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Aerosols','Emulsions','Foam','Gels','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190331,'chemistry','Hair cream is an example of _____.','Emulsions','Hair cream is an example of emulsion colloidal system of liquid in liquid.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136; Table 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Aerosols','Emulsions','Foam','Gels','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190332,'chemistry','Butter is an example of _____.','Emulsions','Butter is an example of emulsion colloidal system of liquid in liquid.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136; Table 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Aerosols','Emulsions','Foam','Gels','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190333,'chemistry','Jelly is an example of _____.','Gels','Butter is an example of gel colloidal system of liquid in solid.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136; Table 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Aerosols','Emulsions','Foam','Gels','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190334,'chemistry','The number of phases in Colloidal system are _____.','2','A colloid is a heterogeneous system in which one substance is dispersed (dispersed phase) as very fine particles in another substance called dispersion medium.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136; Paragraph 5.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','2','3','4','1','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190335,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : A gas mixed with another gas doesn\'t form a Colloid.\nReason : A gas mixed with another gas form a homogenous mixture.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','A gas mixed with another gas forms a homogeneous mixture and hence is not a colloidal system.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136; Paragraph 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190336,'chemistry','Firefighting foams used at emergency airplane landings are _____.','Colloid','Firefighting foams, used at emergency airplane landings are colloidal systems.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 137; Paragraph 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Suspension','Colloid','Solution','Water','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190337,'chemistry','Most biological fluids are :','solids dispersed in water.','Most biological fluids are aqueous sols (solids dispersed in water).\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 137; Paragraph 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','solid dispersed in gas.','liquid dispersed in gas.','liquid dispersed in solid.','solids dispersed in water.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190338,'chemistry','Within a typical cell, proteins and nucleic acids are ______ sized particles dispersed in an aqueous solution of ions and\nsmall molecules.','Colloidal','Within a typical cell, proteins and nucleic acids are colloidal-sized particles dispersed in an aqueous solution of ions and small molecules.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 137; Paragraph 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Suspension','Colloidal','Solution','Dispersion','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190339,'chemistry','The most common colloids are :','sols, gels and emulsions.','Colloids are classified into several types, including Solid sol, Sol Aerosol, Gel, Emulsion, Aerosol, Solid sol, and Foam. The most common are sols (solids in liquids), gels (liquids in solids), and emulsions (liquids in liquids).\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 137; Paragraph 5.4.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','sols, gels and foams.','sols, gels and emulsions.','aerosols, gels and emulsions.','solid sols, foams and emulsions.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190340,'chemistry','If the dispersion medium is water the sol is called :','Both a and b','If the dispersion medium is water, the sol is called aquasol or hydrosol.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 137; Paragraph 5.4.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Aquasol','Hydrosol','alcosol','Both a and b','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190418,'chemistry','Which of the following is an example of Association colloids.','Soap','A common example of an associated colloid is soap.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 138; Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Soap','Platinum sol','starch, cellulose','proteins,nylon','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190419,'chemistry','Which of the following colloid contain both lyophilic and lyophobic parts?','Associated colloids','Associated colloids have both lyophobic and lyophilic parts. Micelles may contain as many as 100 molecules or more.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 138; Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Multimolecular colloids.','Macromolecular colloids.','Associated colloids','Emulsions','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190420,'chemistry','Soap is sodium or potassium salt of a  ______ .','higher fatty acid','Soap is sodium or potassium salt of a higher fatty acid and may be represented as RCOO–Na⁺. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 138; Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','higher alcohol','higher fatty acid','lower fatty acid','higher hydrocarbon chain','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190421,'chemistry','Sodium stearate (CH₃(CH₂)₁₆COO–Na⁺) is a major component of _____.','Bar soap','Sodium stearate (CH₃(CH₂)₁₆COO–Na⁺) is a major component of many bar soaps.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 138; Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Face soap','Detergent','Bar soap','laundry soap','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190422,'chemistry','At the critical micelle concentration (CMC) the surfactant molecules :','associate','Some chemicals function as regular strong electrolytes at low concentrations but display colloidal behaviour at greater concentrations due to aggregation formation. The aggregated particles thus formed are called micelles. As a result, they are often referred to as associated colloids.\nThe concentration of surfactants in a solution above which micelles form is known as the critical Micelle Concentration (CMC). Surfactants in solution adsorb to surfaces below the CMC, decreasing surface tension. The vast majority of added surfactant will form micelles above the CMC.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 138; Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','dissociate','associate','dissolve','become completely soluble','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190423,'chemistry','In soap, the a higher fatty acid part consists of :','non-polar tail & a polar head.','Soap is sodium or potassium salt of a higher fatty acid and may be represented as RCOO–Na⁺. When dissolved in water, it dissociates into RCOO and Na⁺ ions. The RCOO⁻ ions, however, consist of two parts — a long hydrocarbon chain R (also called non-polar ‘tail’) which is hydrophobic (water repelling), and a polar group COO⁻ (also called polar-ionic ‘head’), which is hydrophilic (water loving).\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 138; Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','polar tail & a non-polar head.','both head and tail are non-polar.','both head and tail are polar.','non-polar tail & a polar head.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190424,'chemistry','In soap, the a higher fatty acid part has a long hydrocarbon chain R which is _____ and a polar group  COO⁻  which is ______.','hydrophobic, hydrophilic','Soap is sodium or potassium salt of a higher fatty acid and may be represented as RCOO–Na⁺. When dissolved in water, it dissociates into RCOO and Na⁺ ions. The RCOO⁻ ions, however, consist of two parts — a long hydrocarbon chain R (also called non-polar ‘tail’) which is hydrophobic (water repelling), and a polar group COO⁻ (also called polar-ionic ‘head’), which is hydrophilic (water loving).\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 138; Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'2','mcq','hydrophobic, hydrophilic','both hydrophobic','hydrophilic, hydrophobic','neutral, hydrophilic','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190425,'chemistry','The soap molecule has a ______ head.','hydrophilic','Soap is sodium or potassium salt of a higher fatty acid and may be represented as RCOO–Na⁺. When dissolved in water, it dissociates into RCOO and Na⁺ ions. The RCOO⁻ ions, however, consist of two parts — a long hydrocarbon chain R (also called non-polar ‘tail’) which is hydrophobic (water repelling), and a polar group COO⁻ (also called polar-ionic ‘head’), which is hydrophilic (water loving).\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 138; Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','hydrophilic','hydrophobic','neutral','amphipathic','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190426,'chemistry','The soap molecule has a ______ tail.','hydrophobic','Soap is sodium or potassium salt of a higher fatty acid and may be represented as RCOO–Na⁺. When dissolved in water, it dissociates into RCOO and Na⁺ ions. The RCOO⁻ ions, however, consist of two parts — a long hydrocarbon chain R (also called non-polar ‘tail’) which is hydrophobic (water repelling), and a polar group COO⁻ (also called polar-ionic ‘head’), which is hydrophilic (water loving).\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 138; Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','hydrophobic','hydrophilic','neutral','amphipathic','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190427,'chemistry','Which of the following is a detergent?','Sodium lauryl sulphate','In the case of detergents, such as sodium lauryl sulphate, CH₃(CH₂)₁₁SO₄Na⁺, the polar group, along with the lengthy hydrocarbon chain, is -SO₄⁻.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 139; Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'2','mcq','Sodium lauryl sulphate','Sodium stearate','Sodium benzoate','Cocamidopropyl betaine','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190428,'chemistry','Which part of soap molecule attached to dirt or oil droplet?','hydrophobic part of the stearate ions','The cleansing action of soap is due to the fact that soap molecules form micelle around the oil droplet in such a way that hydrophobic part of the stearate ions is in the oil droplet and hydrophilic part projects out of the grease droplet like the bristles.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 139; Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','hydrophobic part of the stearate ions','hydrophilic part of the stearate ions','polar part of the stearate ions','the head of the stearate ions','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190429,'chemistry','Soap helps in ______ and washing away\nof oils and fats.','emulsification','The cleansing action of soap is due to the fact that soap molecules form micelle around the oil droplet in such a way that hydrophobic part of the stearate ions is in the oil droplet and hydrophilic part projects out of the grease droplet like the bristles. Since the polar groups can interact with water, the oil droplet surrounded by stearate ions is now pulled in water and removed from the dirty surface. Thus soap helps in emulsification and washing away of oils and fats.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 139; Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','colloid','emulsification','suspension','solution','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190430,'chemistry','Which of the following metal sols, cannot be prepared by Bredig\'s arc method?','K','Electrical disintegration or Bredig’s Arc  method involves dispersion as well as condensation. Colloidal sols of metals such as gold, silver, platinum, etc., can be prepared by this method. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 139; Paragraph 5.4.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Ag','K','Au','Pt','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190431,'chemistry','A dispersion of AgCl in water is ______.','Hydrophobic sol.','Electrical disintegration or Bredig’s Arc  method involves dispersion as well as condensation. Colloidal sols of metals such as gold, silver, platinum, etc., can be prepared by this method. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 139; Paragraph 5.4.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Hydrophilic colloid','An emulsion','An alcosol','Hydrophobic sol.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190432,'chemistry','Which of the following does not constitute irreversible colloidal system in water as dispersion medium?','Starch solution','Clay, platinum and Fe(OH)₃ are irreversible because colloidal particles, as they cannot be removed from the dispersion medium. \nColloidal sols directly formed by mixing substances like gum, gelatine, starch, rubber, etc., with a suitable liquid are called lyophilic sols. These sols are also called reversible sols. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 137; Paragraph 5.4.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Clay','Platinum','Fe(OH)₃','Starch solution','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190433,'chemistry','Hydrophilic colloids are stable due to :','layer of dispersion medium on their particles.','A key feature of lyophilic sols is that if the dispersion media is separated from the dispersed phase (for example, via evaporation), the sol can be recreated by simply mixing with the dispersion medium. This is why these sols are also known as reversible sols. Moreover, these sols are highly stable and do not coagulate quickly.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 137; Paragraph 5.4.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','negative charged particles.','large size of particles.','small size of particles.','layer of dispersion medium on their particles.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190434,'chemistry','Which of the following is a hydrophilic\ncolloidal sol?','Starch sol.','The term \'lyophilic\' means \'loving liquids.\' Lyophilic sols are colloidal sols that are formed immediately by mixing substances such as gum, gelatine, starch, rubber, and others with a suitable liquid (the dispersion medium).\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 137; Paragraph 5.4.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'2','mcq','Barium sulphate sol.','Arsenious sulphide sol.','Starch sol.','Silver iodide solution','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190435,'chemistry','Peptisation is a process of :','Dispersing precipitate into colloidal sols','Peptization may be defined as the process of converting a precipitate into colloidal sol by shaking it with dispersion medium in the presence of a small amount of electrolyte. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 140; Paragraph 5.4.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Precipitation of colloidal particles','Purification of colloids','Dispersing precipitate into colloidal sols','Movement of colloidal particles in the electrical field','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190436,'chemistry','Which of the following methods is not used to prepare colloidal dispersions :','Halogenation','Colloidal dispersions can be prepared by chemical reactions leading to formation of molecules by double decomposition, oxidation, reduction or hydrolysis. These molecules then aggregate leading to formation of sols.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 139; Paragraph 5.4.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Double decomposition','Oxidation','Reduction','Halogenation','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190437,'chemistry','As₂O₃ + 3H₂S → As₂S₃ (sol) + 3H₂O\nThis reaction is a ______ reaction.','Double decomposition','Colloidal dispersions can be prepared by chemical reactions leading to formation of molecules by double decomposition, oxidation, reduction or hydrolysis. These molecules then aggregate leading to formation of sols.\nAs₂O₃ + 3H₂S → As₂S₃ (sol) + 3H₂O   (Double decomposition)\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 139; Paragraph 5.4.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Double decomposition','Oxidation','Hydrolysis','Reduction','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190438,'chemistry','SO₂ + 2H₂S → 3S (sol) + 2H₂O\nThis reaction is a ______ reaction.','Oxidation','Colloidal dispersions can be prepared by chemical reactions leading to formation of molecules by double decomposition, oxidation, reduction or hydrolysis. These molecules then aggregate leading to formation of sols.\nSO₂ + 2H₂S → 3S (sol) + 2H₂O  (Oxidation)\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 139; Paragraph 5.4.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Double decomposition','Oxidation','Hydrolysis','Reduction','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190439,'chemistry','2AuCl₃ + 3 HCHO + 3H₂O → 2Au (sol) + 3HCOOH + 6HCl\nThis reaction is a ______ reaction.','Reduction','Colloidal dispersions can be prepared by chemical reactions leading to formation of molecules by double decomposition, oxidation, reduction or hydrolysis. These molecules then aggregate leading to formation of sols.\n2AuCl₃ + 3 HCHO + 3H₂O → 2Au (sol) + 3HCOOH + 6HCl   (Reduction)\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 139; Paragraph 5.4.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Double decomposition','Oxidation','Hydrolysis','Reduction','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190440,'chemistry','FeCl₃ + 3H₂O → Fe(OH)₃ (sol) + 3HCl\nThis reaction is a ______ reaction.','Hydrolysis','Colloidal dispersions can be prepared by chemical reactions leading to formation of molecules by double decomposition, oxidation, reduction or hydrolysis. These molecules then aggregate leading to formation of sols.\nFeCl₃ + 3H₂O → Fe(OH)₃ (sol) + 3HCl  (Hydrolysis)\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 139; Paragraph 5.4.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Double decomposition','Oxidation','Hydrolysis','Reduction','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190441,'chemistry','Electrical disintegration or Bredig’s Arc method involves :','Dispersion as well as\ncondensation','Electrical disintegration or Bredig’s Arc method involves dispersion as well as condensation. Colloidal sols of metals such as gold, silver, platinum, etc., can be prepared by this method. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 139; Paragraph 5.4.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Dispersion','Condensation','Oxidation','Dispersion as well as\ncondensation','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190442,'chemistry','In which method vaporization of metal take place during colloid formation?','Electrical disintegration','Electrical disintegration or Bredig’s Arc  method This process involves dispersion as well as condensation. Colloidal sols of metals such as gold, silver, platinum, etc., can be prepared by this method. In this method, electric arc is struck between electrodes of the metal immersed in the dispersion medium. The intense heat produced evaporates the metal, which then condenses to form particles of colloidal size. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 139; Paragraph 5.4.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Electrical disintegration','Peptization','Oxidation','Hydrolysis','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190443,'chemistry','The method that converts a precipitate into colloidal sol by shaking it in the presence of a little amount of electrolyte is known as :','Peptization','Peptization may be defined as the process of converting a precipitate into colloidal sol by shaking it with dispersion medium in the presence of a small amount of electrolyte. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 140; Paragraph 5.4.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Precipitation','Peptization','Coagulation','Conversation','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190444,'chemistry','The electrolyte used for Peptization purpose\nis called :','Peptizing agent','Peptization may be defined as the process of converting a precipitate into colloidal sol by shaking it with dispersion medium in the presence of a small amount of electrolyte. The electrolyte used for this purpose is called peptizing agent. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 140; Paragraph 5.4.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Colloidal agent','Precipitating agent','Peptizing agent','Coagulating agent','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190445,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Peptization is commonly used to convert a freshly formed precipitate into a colloidal sol.\nReason : Peptization results from the addition of common ions.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Peptization is the process of shaking a freshly precipitated substance into a colloidal solution while using the suitable electrolyte. Therefore, it is obvious from the definition that peptization is carried on by the addition of the proper electrolyte while using specific ions, not by any common ions.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 140; Paragraph 5.4.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190446,'chemistry','The condition(s) for Tyndall effect are :','The refractive indices of the dispersed phase and the dispersion medium differ greatly in magnitude.','Tyndall effect is observed only when the following two conditions are satisfied.\n(i) The diameter of the dispersed particles is not much smaller than the wavelength of the light used; and\n(ii) The refractive indices of the dispersed phase and the dispersion medium differ greatly in magnitude.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 141; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'2','mcq','The diameter of the dispersed particles is equal to than the wavelength of the light used.','The diameter of the dispersed particles is smaller than the wavelength of the light used.','The refractive indices of the dispersed phase and the dispersion medium differ greatly in magnitude.','The refractive indices of the dispersed phase and the dispersion medium have the same magnitude.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190447,'chemistry','Tyndall effect is used to distinguish between :','colloidal and true solution.','Tyndall effect is used to distinguish between a colloidal and true solution. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 141; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','colloidal and true solution.','colloidal and suspension.','two different colloid.','suspension and true solution.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190448,'chemistry','Ultramicroscope is based on :','Tyndall effect','In 1903, Zsigmondy used the Tyndall effect to create the ultramicroscope. An powerful beam of light is focused on a colloidal solution contained in a glass vessel. The focus of the light is then observed using a microscope at right angles to the beam. Individual colloidal particles look as bright stars on a dark background. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 141; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Tyndall effect','Filtration Slit','Diffraction','Reflection','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190449,'chemistry','Who discovered Ultramicroscope :','Zsigmondy','In 1903, Zsigmondy used the Tyndall effect to create the ultramicroscope. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 141; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Zsigmondy','Graham','Parkinson','Robert brown','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190450,'chemistry','Choose the correct option for "Ultramicroscope Disadvantages" :','No information about the size and\nshape of colloidal particles.','In an Ultramicroscope, a powerful beam of light is focused on a colloidal solution contained in a glass vessel. The focus of the light is then observed using a microscope at right angles to the beam. Individual colloidal particles look as bright stars on a dark background. An ultramicroscope only sees the light that is reflected by colloidal particles, not the actual particles themselves. As a result, an ultramicroscope cannot give information regarding the size and shape of colloidal particles.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 141; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','No information about Density of colloidal particles.','No information about the colour of colloidal particles.','No information about the size and\nshape of colloidal particles.','unable to detect the light reflected by colloidal particles.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190451,'chemistry','The random-motion of colloidal particles in the dispersion medium is referred to as :','Brownian movement','When colloidal solutions are viewed using a powerful ultramicroscope, the colloidal particles appear to move in a continuous zig-zag motion over the entire field of vision. This motion was first observed by the British botanist, Robert Brown, and is known as Brownian movement.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 142; Paragraph 5.4.6 (iv))','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Adsorption','Brownian movement','Coagulation','Tyndall effect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190452,'chemistry','Milk represents colloidal solution of :','Fat dispersed in water','There are two types of emulsions.\n(i) Oil dispersed in water (O/W type) and\n(ii) Water dispersed in oil (W/O type).\nIn the first system, water acts as dispersion medium. Examples of this type of emulsion are milk and vanishing cream. In milk, liquid fat is dispersed in water. Vanishing cream is an oil in water (O in W) type emulsion.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 145; Paragraph 5.5)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Fat dispersed in water','Water dispersed in fat','Fat dispersed in oil','Oil dispersed in water','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190453,'chemistry','What is observed when a beam of light is passed through a colloidal sol?','light gets scattered','When a light beam is passed through a colloidal solution, scattering of light is observed. This is referred as the Tyndall effect. This light scattering illuminates the beam\'s path in the colloidal solution.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 148; Exercise 5.15 (i))','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','light gets reflected','light gets absorbed','light gets scattered','light undergoes reflection','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190454,'chemistry','The presence of colloidal particles of dust in-air imparts blue colour to the sky. This is due to :','Scattering of light','Dust particles along with water suspended in air scatter blue light which reaches our eyes and the sky looks blue to us. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146; Paragraph 5.6(i))','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Absorption of light','Reflection of light','Refraction of light','Scattering of light','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190455,'chemistry','A sol has positively charged colloidal particles. Which of the following solutions is required in lowest concentration for coagulation :','K₃[Fe(CN)₆]','A negative ion causes the precipitation of positively charged sol and vice versa. In the coagulation of a positive sol, the flocculating power is in the order : [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ > PO₄³⁻ > SO₄²⁻ > Cl⁻. Hence, K₃[Fe(CN)₆] will be required in lowest concentration for coagulation.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 144; Paragraph (v))','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','NaCl','K₃[Fe(CN)₆]','ZnCl₂','Na₂SO₄','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190456,'chemistry','The minimum concentration of an electrolyte in millimoles per litre required to cause coagulation of a sol is called its ______.','Coagulating value','The minimum concentration of an electrolyte in millimoles per litre required to cause precipitation of a sol in two hours is called coagulating value. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 144; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Coagulating value','Protective value','Gold number','Critical value','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190457,'chemistry','Which of following processes best describes the purification of muddy water by addition of alum?','Coagulation','The water obtained from natural sources often contains suspended impurities. Alum is added to such water to coagulate the suspended impurities and make water fit for drinking purposes.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146; Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Absorption','Adsorption','Dialysis','Coagulation','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190458,'chemistry','An arsenious sulphide sol carries a negative charge. The maximum precipitating power for this sol is possessed by ______.','AICI₃','The greater the valence of the flocculating ion added, the greater its power to precipitate. This is known as the Hardy-Schulze rule. The flocculating power of a negative sol in coagulation is as follows : Al⁺³ > Ba⁺² > Na⁺. Hence, AICI₃ will have the maximum precipitating power.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 144, Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','K₂SO₄','Na₃PO₄','CaCl₂','AICI₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190459,'chemistry','Gold number gives the indication of :','Protective power of a colloid','The protective power of lyophilic colloids is expressed in terms of gold number, a concept coined by Zsigmondy. The gold number is the number of protective colloid in milligrammes that prevents 10 millilitres of red gold solution from coagulating when 1 millilitre of a sodium chloride solution at a 10 percent concentration is added to it.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'2','mcq','Gm-molecules of gold per 100ml of colloidal solution','Percentage of gold in the suspension','Charge on the colloids','Protective power of a colloid','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190460,'chemistry','Which of the following will have the highest coagulating power for As₂S₃ colloid?','Al³⁺','The greater the valence of the flocculating ion added, the greater its power to precipitate. This is known as the Hardy-Schulze rule. The flocculating power of a negative sol in coagulation is as follows : Al⁺³ > Ba⁺² > Na⁺. Hence, AICI₃ will have the maximum precipitating power.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 144, Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Phosphate ion','Sulphate ion','Al³⁺','Na⁺','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190461,'chemistry','Which among the following options is not used for the coagulation of the lyophobic sols :','Adding detergent','The coagulation of the lyophobic sols can be carried out in the following ways :\n(i) By electrophoresis : The colloidal particles move towards oppositely charged electrodes, get discharged and precipitated.\n(ii) By mixing two oppositely charged sols.\n(iii) By boiling.\n(iv) By persistent dialysis.\n(v) By addition of electrolytes.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 144; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Boiling','Adding oppositely charged','Adding electrolyte','Adding detergent','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190463,'chemistry','In which of the following states, the particle size be greater than 300 mμ?','Suspension','True Solution - less than 1nm, \nColloidal solution - 1 to1000nm,\nSuspension more than 1000 nm.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136; Paragraph 5.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Colloidal','Suspension','True Solution','dispersed particles','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190464,'chemistry','The protective power of lyophilic sol is','Expressed in terms of gold number','The gold number is the number of protective colloid in milligrammes that prevents 10 millilitres of red gold solution from coagulating when 1 millilitre of a sodium chloride solution at a 10 percent concentration is added to it. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12).','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Dependent on the size of colloidal particles','Expressed in terms of gold number','Expressed by x/m','Directly proportional to the magnitude of charge on it','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190465,'chemistry','Brownian motion is due to','Impact of molecules of the dispersion medium on the colloidal particles','The Brownian movement has been explained to be due to the unbalanced bombardment of the particles by the molecules of the dispersion medium. The Brownian movement has a stirring effect which does not permit the particles to settle and thus, is responsible for the stability of sols.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 142; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'2','mcq','Temperature fluctuation within the liquid phase','Attraction and repulsion between charges on the colloidal particle','Impact of molecules of the dispersion medium on the colloidal particles','Convective currents','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190466,'chemistry','Colloidal particles of soap sol in water are :','Negatively charged','Soap has a cleansing action because soap molecules form micelles around the oil droplet in such a way that the hydrophobic part of the stearate ions are in the oil droplet and the hydrophilic part extends out of the grease droplet like bristles. Because the polar groups may interact with water, the oil droplet surrounded by stearate ions is now drawn in by water and removed from the contaminated surface. As a result, soap helps in the emulsification and removal of oils and fats. The negatively charged sheath that surrounds the globules prevents them from coming together and creating aggregates.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 139; paragraph 5.4.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Positively charged','Negatively charged','Neutral','Unpredictable','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190467,'chemistry','Tyndall effect is shown by','Colloidal solution','Colloid and suspension show Tyndall effect.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 141; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Colloidal solution','Osmotic solution','Hypotonic Solution','Hypertonic Solution','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(190468,'chemistry','Which of the following is not heterogeneous :','True Solution','Colloid and suspension are heterogeneous, so is emulsion. True solution is homogeneous in nature.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136; Paragraph 5.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Emulsion','Suspension','True Solution','Colloidal sol','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190469,'chemistry','Brownian movement was first observed by the British botanist,','Robert Brown','Brownian motion was first observed by the British botanist, Robert Brown.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 142; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Zsigmondy','Robert Brown','Graham','Parkinson','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190470,'chemistry','Brownian motion is independent of the nature of :','colloid','Brownian motion is independent of the nature of the colloid but depends on the size of the particles and viscosity of the solution.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 142; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','size of the particles','colloid','viscosity','solution','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190471,'chemistry','Brownian motion depends on the :','size of the particles','Brownian motion is independent of the nature of the colloid but depends on the size of the particles and viscosity of the solution.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 142; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','size of the particles','nature of colloid','stability of colloid','Nature of the dispersed phase','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190472,'chemistry','Brownian motion conditions for faster motion are :','small size and less viscosity','Brownian motion is independent of the nature of the colloid but depends on the size of the particles and viscosity of the solution. Smaller the size and lesser the viscosity, faster is the motion.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 142; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','small size and high viscosity','small size and less viscosity','large size and high viscosity','large size and low viscosity','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(181318,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is not correct for the catalyst?','It is a substance that does not change the equilibrium constant of a reaction.','(a) It catalyses the forward and’backward reactions to the same extent as it decreases energy of activation hence, increases the rate of both the reactions.\n(b) Since reaction quotient is ‘the relation between concentration of reactants and products. Hence, catalyst does not alter Gibbs free energy as it is related reaction quotient. Thus, Gibbs free energy does not change during the reaction when catalyst is added to it.\n(c) It does not alter equilibrium of reaction as equilibrium constant is also concentration dependent term.\n(d) It provides an alternate mechanism by reducing activation energy between reactants and product.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','It catalyses the forward and backward reactions to the same extent.','It alters AG of the reaction.','It is a substance that does not change the equilibrium constant of a reaction.','It provides an alternate mechanism by reducing activation energy between reactants and products.','NEW','12-116','12-082',NULL),
(190473,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : The presence of equal and similar charges on colloidal particles is largely responsible in providing stability to the colloidal solution.\nReason : The repulsive forces between charged particles having same charge prevent them from coalescing or aggregating.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The presence of equal and similar charges on colloidal particles is largely responsible in providing stability to the colloidal solution, because the repulsive forces between charged particles having same charge prevent them from coalescing or aggregating when they come closer to one another.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 142; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190474,'chemistry','The sol particles acquire positive or negative charge by ______ of positive or negative ions.','Preferential Adsorption','The sol particles acquire positive or negative charge by preferential adsorption of positive or negative ions. When two or more ions are present in the dispersion medium, preferential adsorption of the ion common to the colloidal particle usually takes place.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 142-143; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Preferential Absorption','Preferential Adsorption','Isotherm Adsorption','Isotherm Absorption','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190475,'chemistry','The correct statement about Helmholtz electrical double layer is:\na. It is made up of two layers of identical charges surrounding colloidal sol.\nb. It is made up of two layers of opposite charges that surround the colloidal sol.\nc. The first layer of ions is diffused, whereas the second layer of ions is fixed.\nd. The potential difference between the fixed layer and the diffused layer is referred to as the zeta potential.','Both b and d','The combination of the two layers of opposite charges around the colloidal particle is called Helmholtz electrical double layer. According to modern views, the first layer of ions is firmly held and is termed fixed layer while the second layer is mobile which is termed diffused layer. Because charge separation is a seat of potential, charges of opposite signs on the fixed and diffused parts of the double layer result in a difference in potential between these layers in the same way that potential difference develops in a capacitor. This potential difference between the fixed layer and the diffused layer of opposite charges is called the electrokinetic potential or zeta potential.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 143; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'2','mcq','Both a and b','Both c and d','Both b and d','Both b and c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190476,'chemistry','The potential difference between the fixed layer and the diffused layer of opposite charges is called _______.','Zeta potential','Because charge separation is a seat of potential, charges of opposite signs on the fixed and diffused parts of the double layer result in a difference in potential between these layers in the same way that potential difference develops in a capacitor. This potential difference between the fixed layer and the diffused layer of opposite charges is called the electrokinetic potential or zeta potential.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 143; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'2','mcq','Electromagnetic potential','Zeta potential','Layer Potential','Fixed potential','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190477,'chemistry','A & B in the picture are :\ndiagram(n1903)','Fixed layer, Diffused layer','The first layer (A) of ions is firmly held and is termed fixed layer while the second layer (B) is mobile which is termed diffused layer.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 143; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'2','mcq','Fixed layer, Diffused layer','Diffused layer, Fixed layer','Both A and B are Diffused layer','Electrical layer, Diffused layer','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/190477q.png'),
(190478,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Double layer helps in stability of colloid.\nReason : Particles attract each other and maintain stability.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','If two particles of an insoluble material (precipitate) do not have double layers they can come close enough and attractive van der Waals forces pull them together. When particles possess double layer, the overall effect is that particles repel each other at large distances of separation. This repulsion prevents their close approach. They remain dispersed and colloid is stabilised.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 143; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190479,'chemistry','Addition of oppositely charged ion to sol  does not results in :','expansion of the diffused double layer','The addition of more electrolytes to sol suppresses the diffused double layer and reduces the zeta potential. This decreases the electrostatic repulsion between particles to a large extent and colloid precipitates. That is why colloid is particularly sensitive to oppositely charged ions.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 143; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'2','mcq','decrease the electrostatic repulsion between particles to a larger extent','suppression of diffused double layer','reduction of zeta potential','expansion of the diffused double layer','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190480,'chemistry','The existence of charge on colloidal particles is confirmed by :','electrophoresis.','The existence of charge on colloidal particles is confirmed by electrophoresis experiment.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 143; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','electrodialysis.','electrophoresis.','Zeta potential.','precipitation.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190481,'chemistry','The movement of colloidal particle under an applied electric potential is called :','electrophoresis.','The movement of colloidal particles under an applied electric potential is called electrophoresis.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 143; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','electrodialysis.','electrophoresis.','Zeta potential.','precipitation.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(181319,'chemistry','The value of rate constant of a pseudo first order reaction','depends on the concentration of reactants present in excess','The value of rate constant of a pseudo first order reaction depends on the concentration of reactants present in excess','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','depends on the concentration of reactants present in small amount','depends on the concentration of reactants present in excess','is independent of the concentration of reactants','depends only on temperature','NEW','12-112','12-078',NULL),
(190482,'chemistry','The coagulation of the lyophobic sols cannot be carried out by :','Electroosmosis','The coagulation of the lyophobic sols can be\ncarried out in the following ways :\n(i) By electrophoresis : The colloidal particles move towards oppositely charged electrodes, get discharged and precipitated.\n(ii) By mixing two oppositely charged sols.\n(iii) By boiling.\n(iv) By persistent dialysis.\n(v) By addition of electrolytes.\nThe coagulation of the lyophobic sols cannot be carried out by an electroosmosis.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 143; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Electrophoresis','By mixing oppositely charged particles','Persistent Dialysis','Electroosmosis','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190483,'chemistry','Greater the valence of the flocculating ion added, the greater is its power to cause precipitation. This is known as :','Hardy-Schulze rule.','The greater the valence of the flocculating ion added, the greater is its power to cause precipitation. This is known as Hardy-Schulze rule.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 144; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'2','mcq','Gold no.','Hardy-Schulze rule.','Coagulation Power','Freundlich isotherm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190484,'chemistry','Which among the following will have the highest coagulating power for a ferric hydroxide solution','K₃[Fe(CN)₆]','A negative ion causes the precipitation of positively charged sol and vice versa. Ferric Hydroxide sol being positive in nature requires negatively charged iron with high magnitude to coagulate. In the coagulation of a positive sol, the flocculating power is in the order : [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ > PO₄³⁻ > SO₄²⁻ > Cl⁻. Hence, K₃[Fe(CN)₆] will have the highest power of  coagulation.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 144; Paragraph (v))','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','NaCl','BaCl₂','K₂CrO₄','K₃[Fe(CN)₆]','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190485,'chemistry','A metal sulphide sol can be flocculated by one of the following :','Al³⁺','A negative ion causes the precipitation of positively charged sol and vice versa. Negatively charged metal sulphide sol can be coagulated by positive ions (Al³⁺).\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 144; Paragraph (v))','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','[Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻','Al³⁺','SO₄²⁻','BeCl₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190486,'chemistry','Ferric chloride is applied to stop bleeding because :','Fe³⁺ ions coagulates blood, which is a negatively charged sol','Blood is a colloidal solution of an albuminoid substance. The styptic action of alum and ferric chloride solution is due to coagulation of blood forming a clot which stops further bleeding. Fe³⁺  ions coagulate negatively charged blood solution.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146; Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Fe³⁺ ions coagulates blood, which is a negatively charged sol','Fe³⁺ ions coagulates blood, which is a positively charged sol','CI⁻ ions coagulates blood, which is a negatively charged sol','CI⁻ ions coagulates blood, which is a positively charged sol','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190487,'chemistry','A colloidal solution is subjected to an electrical field. The particles move towards anode. The coagulation of same sol is studied using NaCl, BaCl₂ and AICI₃ solutions. Their order of coagulating power should be :','AICI₃> BaCl₂> NaCl','The Hardy-Schulze rule states that the greater the valence of the flocculating ion supplied, the greater its ability to precipitate. The coagulating power of a solution is determined by the valency or charge of the ion in the solution, and it increases as the valency of the ion increases. Aluminium (Al) in aluminium chloride (AICI₃) has a valency of 3, barium (Ba) in barium chloride (BaCl₂) has a valency of 2, and sodium (Na) in sodium chloride (NaCl) has a valency of 1.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 144; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','NaCl > BaCl₂> AICl₃','BaCl₂>AICI₃> NaCl','AICI₃> BaCl₂> NaCl','BaCl₂> NaCl > AICI₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190488,'chemistry','Which of the following method is used for sol destruction :','Addition of an electrolyte','When excess of an electrolyte is added, the colloidal particles are precipitated. The reason is that colloids interact with ions carrying charge opposite to that present on themselves. This causes neutralisation leading to their coagulation. Thus, the sol would be destroyed.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 144; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Condensation','Dialysis','Diffusion through animal membrane','Addition of an electrolyte','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190489,'chemistry','Number of millimoles of electrolyte required to coagulate one litre of colloidal solution is called :','coagulating value','The minimum concentration of an electrolyte in millimoles per litre required to cause precipitation of a sol in two hours is called coagulating value. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 144; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','coagulating value','coagulating number','Golds number','precipitation number','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190490,'chemistry','The ability of an ion to bring about coagulation of a given colloidal solution depends upon :','Both magnitude and sign of charge','The ability of an ion to cause coagulation of a certain colloid is determined by both the magnitude and sign of its charge. Even in small amounts, ions of opposite charge and larger magnitude can cause coagulation.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 144; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Its size of ion','The sign of charge','The magnitude of charge','Both magnitude and sign of charge','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190491,'chemistry','Flocculation value is expressed in terms of','Millimole/litre','The minimum concentration of an electrolyte in millimoles per litre required to cause precipitation of a sol in two hours is called coagulation/Flocculation value. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 144; Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Millimole/litre','Mole/litre','Grams/litre','Mole/millilitre','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190492,'chemistry','Which of the following has largest protecting power :','Gelatin (Gold no.-0.01)','Protective power is inversely proportional to Gold number.  \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Gelatin (Gold no.-0.01)','Dextrin (Gold no.- 15)','Potato starch (Gold no.-25)','Albumin (Gold no-0.25)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190493,'chemistry','The coagulation of 10cm³ of Gold sol is completely prevented by addition of 0.025g of starch to it. The gold number of starch is :','25','Number of milligrams of protective colloid required to prevent the correlation of 10 ml of standard red gold sol on adding 1 ml of 10% NaCl electrolyte is called gold number. \nGold number is the weight of protective colloid in milligrams which prevents the coagulation of 10cm³ of gold sol\n∴ Gold number of starch = 0.025×1000 = 25(mg)\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','0.025','0.25','2.5','25','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190494,'chemistry','Gelatin does not protects :','Starch sol','Gelatin being lyophilic in nature it protect lyophobic colloid.   \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Gold sol','As₂S₃ Sol','Fe(OH)₃Sol','Starch sol','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190495,'chemistry','Gold number is minimum in case of :','Gelatin','Gelatin has the lowest gold number (i.e.,  range 0.005−0.01).\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Gelatin','Egg albumin','Gum arabic','Starch','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190496,'chemistry','The gold numbers of four protective colloids O.P.Q and R are 0.005, 0.01, 0.1 and 0.5 respectively. The decreasing order of their protective power is :','O,P,Q,R','Lower the gold number, higher will be its protective power. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'3','mcq','R,Q,P,O','O,P,Q,R','P,Q,R,O','Q,R,O,P','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190497,'chemistry','Factors which are responsible for the stability of lyophilic sols :','charge and solvation of the colloidal particles','There are two factors which are responsible for the stability of lyophilic sols. These factors are the charge and solvation of the colloidal particles. When these two factors are removed, a lyophilic sol can be coagulated.\n-Reference : NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 145; Paragraph (Coagulation of lyophilic sols))','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','stability and charge of Colloidal particles','viscosity and solvation of Colloidal particles','charge and solvation of the colloidal particles','stability and size of the colloidal particles','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190498,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Lyophilic sols are more stable than lyophobic sols.\nReason :  This is due to the fact that lyophobic colloids are extensively solvated.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Lyophilic sols are more stable than lyophobic sols. This is due to the fact that lyophilic colloids are extensively solvated, i.e., colloidal particles are covered by a sheath of the liquid in which they are dispersed.\n-Reference : NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 145; Paragraph (Protection of colloids))','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190526,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Colloidal medicine are less effective.\nReason : Colloidal medicines usually have smaller surface area.','Both statements are false.','Colloidal medicines are more effective because they have large surface area and are therefore easily assimilated.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190527,'chemistry','Tanin has _____ charges.','Negative','Hide has positively charged particles, whereas tannin contains negatively charged colloidal particles\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 147, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'1','mcq','Positive','Negative','Neutral','polar','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190528,'chemistry','Animal hide has _____ charges.','Positive','Hide has positively charged particles, whereas tannin contains negatively charged colloidal particles\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 147, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'1','mcq','Positive','Negative','Neutral','polar','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190529,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Hardening of leather takes place due to mutual coagulation of tannin and animal hide.\nReason : Both are colloid and contain similar charges.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Animal hides are colloidal in nature. When a hide, which has positively charged particles, is soaked in tannin, which contains negatively charged colloidal particles, mutual coagulation takes place. This results in the hardening of leather. This process is termed as tanning.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 147, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190530,'chemistry','Which salts are used in place of tannin?','Chromium salts','Chromium salts are also used in place of tannin.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 147, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'1','mcq','Sodium salts','Chromium salts','Potassium salts','Calcium salts','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190531,'chemistry','Which of the following used in Photographic film :','AgBr','Photographic plates or films are prepared by coating an emulsion of the light sensitive silver bromide in gelatin over glass plates or celluloid films.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 147, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'1','mcq','AgBr','AgCl','AgOH','AgI','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190532,'chemistry','Latex is a colloidal solution of rubber particles which has ______ charge.','Negative','Latex is a colloidal solution of rubber particles which are negatively charged. Rubber is obtained by coagulation of latex.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 147, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'1','mcq','Positive','Negative','Neutral','Polar','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190533,'chemistry','After Coagulation of Latex we get :','Rubber','Latex is a colloidal solution of rubber particles which are negatively charged. Rubber is obtained by coagulation of latex.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 147, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'1','mcq','Emulsion','Foam','Rubber','Tanin','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190534,'chemistry','How many of the following are Colloids?     \nPaints, inks, synthetic plastics, rubber,\ngraphite lubricants, cement.','7','Paints, inks, synthetic plastics, rubber, graphite lubricants, cement, etc., are all colloidal solutions.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 147, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'2','mcq','5','6','7','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190622,'chemistry','Which among the following method is commonly used method for destruction of colloid?','By adding electrolyte','By adding electrolytes the colloidal particles are precipitated. The electrolytes neutralise the charge of colloids leading to their coagulation and thus, destroy the colloid.\n(NEET 2000)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Dialysis','Condensation','Filtration by animal membrane','By adding electrolyte','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190623,'chemistry','At the critical micelle concentration (CMC) the surfactant molecules :','associate','The soap concentration at which micelles (spherical colloid molecules) first appear is called as critical micelle concentration (CMC). At this condition, the surfactant molecules associate with each other.\n(NEET 1998)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','associate','Dissociate','Decompose','become completely soluble.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190624,'chemistry','The ability of anion, to bring about coagulation of a given colloid, depends upon :','both magnitude and charge','Both magnitude of charge and nature of charge effect coagulation of a given colloid. Greater the magnitude of the charge, quicker will be the coagulation.\n(NEET 1997)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','magnitude of the charge','both magnitude and charge','its charge only','sign of the charge alone','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190625,'chemistry','When a few typical solutes are separated by a particular selective membrane such as protein particles, blood corpuscles, this process is called :','dialysis','Dialysis is the process of separating the particles of colloids from the particles of crystalloids by means of diffusion through a selective membrane placed in water.\n(NEET 1996)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','transpiration','endosmosis','dialysis','diffusion','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190626,'chemistry','The right option for the statement "Tyndall effect is exhibited by", is :','Starch solution','Tyndall effect is exhibited by colloidal solutions. Starch solution is a colloidal solution.\n(NEET 2021)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','NaCl solution','Glucose solution','Starch solution','Urea solution','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190627,'chemistry','Which of the following p-V curve represents maximum work done?\ndiagram(n1905)','-2','The area under the \'p-V\' graph can be used to calculate the work done by any thermodynamic process. As can be observed, option \'2\' covers the most area.\n(NEET 2022) diagram(ne1916)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','-1','-2','-3','-4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/190627q.png'),
(190628,'chemistry','The incorrect statement regarding enzymes is :','Enzymes are polysaccharides.','Enzymes are complex nitrogenous organic compounds which are produced by living plants and animals. They are protein molecules of high molecular mass. They are not polysaccharides.\n(NEET 2022)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Enzymes are biocatalysts.','Like chemical catalysts enzymes reduce the activation energy of bio processes.','Enzymes are polysaccharides.','Enzymes are very specific for a particular reaction and substrate.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190629,'chemistry','Given below are two statements.\n \n Statement I : The lead chamber process involves the oxidation of sulphur dioxide with dioxygen in the presence of nitrogen oxides as the catalyst.\n \n Statement II : The catalytic reaction that is taking place here is heterogeneous.\n \n In light of above statements choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.','Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect.','Homogeneous catalysis occurs when the reactants and catalyst are in the same phase (i.e., liquid or gas). In the lead chamber process, sulphur dioxide is oxidised to sulphur trioxide with dioxygen in the presence of nitrogen oxides as the catalyst. The reactants, sulphur dioxide and oxygen, and the catalyst, nitric oxide, are all in the same phase. Hence, this catalytic reaction is said to be homogeneous catalysis.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 130, Paragraph 5.2.1) diagram(ne1917)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.','Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect.','Statement I is incorrect and Statement II is correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190710,'chemistry','Sand in water suspension settles over time, while starch in water colloid remains dispersed. Which of the following options best supports the stated assertion?','The size of the particles contributes to the different behaviour of suspension and colloid.','Suspensions have particle sizes of more than 1000 nm, whereas colloids have particle sizes ranging from 1-1000 nm. The small size of colloids allows them to remain suspended. Because of this, larger sand particles have a tendency to settle, but smaller starch particles maintain dispersion.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136, Paragraph 5.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','The type of solvent contributes to the different behaviour of suspension and colloid.','External conditions i.e, temperature and pH contribute to the different behaviour of suspension and colloid.','The quantity of solute contributes to the different behaviour of suspension and colloid.','The size of the particles contributes to the different behaviour of suspension and colloid.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190711,'chemistry','Select the correct option(s) from the following statements explaining " solid catalysts",\n \n a. The reactants must get adsorbed adequately firm on to the catalyst to become active.\n \n b. The reactants must form an irreversible ionic bond with the surface of the catalyst.\n \n c. Multiple reactants can be catalysed by a single catalyst.\n \n d. A catalyst has the ability to control a reaction to yield a certain product when other products are attainable.','Both a and d','a. Statement (a) is correct as the reactants must get adsorbed adequately strong on to the catalyst to become active.\nb. Statement (b) is incorrect as the reactants must not be so strongly adsorbed that they become immobilised, leaving no space on the catalyst\'s surface for other reactants to adsorb.\nc. Statement (b) is also incorrect as a substance that serves as a catalyst in one reaction may fail to catalyse another.\nd. Statement (d) is correct as a catalyst\'s selectivity is its ability to direct a reaction to produce a specific product when multiple products are possible under the same reaction conditions.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 132, Paragraph 5.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both a and b','Both c and d','Both a and d','Both b and c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190712,'chemistry','A student noticed that when solution x was placed in the dark and facing the light, it seemed clear. However, when he saw the same solution at right angles to the passage of light, i.e., the path of the beam is illuminated by a bluish light, he was surprised. What was the result of his observations? (Hint : Tyndall effect)','The solution x was a colloid.','When studied in the direction of light, colloidal solutions appear clear; but, when observed from a direction at right angles to the direction of the light beam, they exhibit a mild to intense opalescence, i.e., the path of the beam is lighted by a bluish light. This effect, the Tyndall effect occurs when colloidal particles scatter light in all directions in space. This scattering of light illuminates the path of the beam in the colloidal dispersion.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 141, Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','The solution x was a suspension.','The solution x was simply distilled water.','The solution x was a colloid.','The solution x was a true solution.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190713,'chemistry','Select the correct option(s) from the following statements explaining " Tyndall effect" :\n \n a. Tyndall cone refers to the light source use to observe Tyndall effect. \n \n b. Individual colloid particles can be detected using a microscope and the Tyndall effect.\n \n c. The Tyndall effect is only observed in colloidal fluids.\n \n d. The colour of colloidal solution changes depending on how the light is received by the observer.','Both b and d','a. Statement (a) is false as Tyndall cone refers to the bright light cone (luminous path of the beam of light). \nb. Statement (b) is correct. Zsigmondy used the Tyndall effect to create the ultramicroscope in 1903. A powerful light beam is focused on a colloidal solution held in a glass vessel. A microscope at right angles to the beam is then used to examine the light\'s focus. Individual colloidal particles stand out against a dark background as bright stars.\nc. Statement (c) is incorrect as Tyndall effect is applicable to all types of liquid. For example Tyndall effect can be seen during film projection in a cinema hall due to light scattering by dust and smoke particles.\nd. Statement (d) is also correct as the colour of colloidal solution changes depending on how the light is received by the observer. For example, a mixture of milk and water appears blue when viewed by the reflected light and red when viewed by the transmitted light.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 141, Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both a and b','Both c and d','Both b and d','Both a and c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190714,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n \n Assertion(A) : The finest gold sol is red in colour; as particle size rises, it appears purple, then blue, and eventually golden.\n \n Reason(R) : Colloidal solution\'s colour varies depending on how the observer receives the light.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Colloidal solution\'s colour varies depending on how the observer receives the light. Finest gold sol, for example, is red in colour; as particle size increases, it turns purple, then blue, and eventually golden.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 142, Paragraph 5.4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is correct, but the reason is not correct','Both assertion and reason are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190715,'chemistry','Match the following common sols according to the type of charge present on their particles.','Hydrated metallic oxides such as Al₂O₃.xH₂O-Positively charged, Metals like copper and silver-Negatively charged, Metallic sulphides such as As₂S₃-Negatively charged, Haemoglobin-Positively charged,','A list of some common sols with the nature of charge on their particles is given in the table.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 142, Paragraph 5.4.6 (table)) diagram(ne1918)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','match','Hydrated metallic oxides such as Al₂O₃.xH₂O,Positively charged','Metals like copper and silver,Negatively charged','Metallic sulphides such as As₂S₃,Negatively charged','Haemoglobin,Positively charged','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190716,'chemistry','Match the following common sols according to the type of charge present on their particles.','Basic dye stuffs-Positively charged, Sols of gum and gelatin-Positively charged, Basic dye stuffs-Negatively charged, Oxides such as TiO₂ sol-Negatively charged,','A list of some common sols with the nature of charge on their particles is given in the table.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 142, Paragraph 5.4.6 (table)) diagram(ne1919)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','match','Basic dye stuffs,Positively charged','Sols of gum and gelatin,Positively charged','Basic dye stuffs,Negatively charged','Oxides such as TiO₂ sol,Negatively charged','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190717,'chemistry','Choose the correct option(s) regarding "Brownian movement".\n \n a. The Brownian motion is impacted by the colloid\'s viscosity and size.\n \n b. Brownian motion is determined by the nature of the colloid.\n \n c. The larger the size and the lower the viscosity, the faster the motion.\n \n d. The colloidal sols\' stability is caused by Brownian motion.','Both a and d','a. Statement (a) is true as the Brownian movement is influenced by particle size and solution viscosity. \nb. Statement (b) is incorrect as Brownian motion is independent of the nature of the colloid. \nc. Statement (b) is also incorrect as the faster the motion, the smaller the size and the lower the viscosity.  \nd. Statement (d) is true as this motion provides a stirring action that prevents particles from settling and is hence responsible for the stability of sols.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 142, Paragraph (iv))','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both a and b','Both c and d','Both b and c','Both a and d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190718,'chemistry','Select the correct option(s) from the following statements explaining " Micelles" :\n \n a. Stearate ions remain at the water\'s surface if the required micelle concentration is not obtained.\n \n b. Ionic micelles can contain up to 100 stearate ions.\n \n c. Soap is a micelle of its own.\n \n d. Stearate ions form a spherical shape in an ionic micelle, with their COO⁻ ions pointing towards the centre and the hydrocarbon chains part remaining on the sphere\'s surface.','Both a and b','a. Statement (a) is correct as stearate ions remain on the surface of water at low concentrations of soap.\nb. Statement (b) is also correct as ionic micelles can contain up to 100 stearate ions.\nc. Statement (c) is false as soap is not a micelle on its own. However, when dissolved in water, it dissociates into RCOO⁻ and Na⁺ ions, which form micelles.\nd. Statement (d) is false too as stearate ions form a spherical shape in a micelle, with their hydrocarbon chains (hydrophobic) pointing towards the centre and the COO⁻  (hydrophilic) part remaining on the sphere\'s surface.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 138-139, Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both a and d','Both a and c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(181320,'chemistry','Consider the reaction A —> B. The concentration of both the reactants and the products varies exponentially with time. Which of the following figures correctly describes the change in concentration of reactants and products with time? Refer diagram(n1847)','b','The concentration of reactants decreases with time and concentration of products increases with time. Refer diagram (ne1855)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-101','12-067','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181320q.png'),
(181321,'chemistry','Rate law cannot be determined from balanced chemical equation if\n(a) reverse reaction is involved\n(b) it is an elementary reaction\n(c) it is a sequence of elementary reactions\n(d) any of the reactants is in excess','a,c,d','Rate law can be determined from balanced chemical equation if it is an elementary reaction.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,c,d','a,d','a,b','only b','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(190719,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n \n Assertion : The Hardy-Schulze law should be modified to account for the polarising power of the flocculating ion.\n \n Reason : The larger the ion, the stronger its polarising power.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','According to Hardy-Schulze law, "the greater the valency of the flocculating ion added, the greater its ability to cause precipitation."\nWe know that the smaller the ion, the stronger its polarising power. Hence , the Hardy-Schulze law can be modified based on the polarising power of the flocculating ion.  The modified Hardy-Schulze law can be stated as "the more the polarising strength of the flocculating ion added, the greater its power to cause precipitation." \nHence, the assertion is correct, but the reason is not.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 147, Intext question 5.7)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190720,'chemistry','It is necessary to wash the precipitate with water before estimating it quantitatively. The underlying cause of this is :','The ions that combine to form the precipitate become adsorbed on the precipitate\'s surface.','When a substance precipitates, some of the ions that combine to produce the precipitate get adsorbed on the precipitate\'s surface. Therefore, in order to get rid of these adsorbed ions or other impurities, it becomes necessary to wash the precipitate before quantifying it.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 147, Intext question 5.8)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','The precipitates are contaminated by the electrolyte used for precipitation.','The ions that combine to form the precipitate become adsorbed on the precipitate\'s surface.','The precipitates adsorb oxides like CO₂, NO₂ from the air.','The precipitate is unstable and requires H⁺ from water.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190721,'chemistry','Which of the following methods are used to increase the activation energy of the adsorbent?\n \n a. By removing the gases adsorbed.\n \n b. By adding a catalyst to increase the adsorption.\n \n c. By washing the surface with acid to smoothen it.\n \n d. By making the surface of adsorbent rough.','Both a and d','The only way to increase the activation of an adsorbent is to increase its surface area, which can be achieved using one of the following ways:\n(i) By removing the adsorbed gases.\n(ii) Break the adsorbent into small pieces.\n(iii) By making the surface of adsorbent rough.\nHence, methods (b) and (c) are not applicable.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 148, Exercise 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both a and b','Both c and d','Both b and c','Both a and d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190722,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n \n Assertion : Powdered substances (adsorbent) have a higher capacity for adsorption.\n \n Reason : Finely divided particles have a larger surface area per mass.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Adsorption is a surface phenomena, hence it is directly proportional to the surface area. A finely divided substance has a larger surface area. The greater the surface area of the adsorbent, the greater its absorbent power. For this reason, an absorbent with finely divided surface is more effective.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 148, Exercise 5.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190723,'chemistry','Given below are two statements\n \n Statement I : Adsorption isotherm, also called Freundlich adsorption isotherm defines the relation between the amount of gas adsorbed by the adsorbent with pressure at a constant temperature. \n \n Statement II : The the variation of the extent of adsorption (x/m) with pressure (P) at a given temperature can be mathematically represented by the expression x/m = k x P¹/ⁿ , while x is the mass of adsorbate and m is the mass of adsorbent. \n \n In light of above statements choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.','Statement I is incorrect and Statement II is correct.','The adsorption isotherm is a curve that defines the relation between the amount of gas adsorbed by the adsorbent with pressure at a constant temperature. Whereas, The Freundlich adsorption isotherm is the mathematical representation for the variation of the extent of adsorption (x/m) with pressure (P) at a given temperature. \nx/m = k x P¹/ⁿ , \nwhile x is the mass of adsorbate and m is the mass of adsorbent. Hence, Statement I is incorrect and Statement II is correct.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 148, Exercise 5.5)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.','Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect.','Statement I is incorrect and Statement II is correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190724,'chemistry','What kind of catalysis can be explained using adsorption theory?','Heterogeneous catalysis','Adsorption theory is given for heterogeneous catalyst. Adsorption of a gas on a solid surface is an example. In heterogeneous catalysis, molecules first become adsorbed on the solid catalyst\'s surface before reacting.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 148, Exercise 5.7)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Enzyme catalysis','Homogeneous catalysis','Heterogeneous catalysis','All types of catalysis','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190725,'chemistry','Given below are two statements\n \n Statement I : The adsorbent\'s surface particles are unstable during adsorption, and when the adsorbate is adsorbed on the surface, the adsorbent\'s energy reduces, resulting in heat evolution.\n \n Statement II : Chemisorption, unlike physiosorption, is never exothermic.\n \n In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.','Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect.','Adsorption is an exothermic reaction because the particles on the adsorbent\'s surface are very unstable, and as the adsorbate adsorbs to the adsorbent\'s surface, the energy of the adsorbent reduces, releasing heat.\nThus, statement (II) is incorrect since adsorption, whether chemical or physical, is always exothermic.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 148, Exercise 5.8)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.','Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect.','Statement I is incorrect, and Statement II is correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190726,'chemistry','Match the following colloidal systems with respect to their physical states of the dispersed phase and dispersion medium.','Aerosol-liquid dispersed in gas, Foam-gas dispersed in liquid, Emulsion-liquid dispersed in liquid, Solid sol-gas dispersed in solid','Colloids can be classified on the basis of physical states of components i.e., on the basis of physical states of the dispersed phase and dispersion medium.\ni. Aerosol is a liquid dispersed in gas. \nii. Foam is a gas dispersed in liquid, \niii. Emulsion is a liquid dispersed in liquid, \niv. Solid solis a gas dispersed in solid \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 148, Exercise 5.9)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','match','Aerosol,liquid dispersed in gas','Foam,gas dispersed in liquid','Emulsion,liquid dispersed in liquid','Solid sol,gas dispersed in solid','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190727,'chemistry','According to the Freundlich isotherm, at a constant temperature and intermediate pressure range, value of (x/m) is :','(x/m) is proportional to a fractional power of pressure.','The Freundlich adsorption isotherm explains the effect of pressure. \n(i) At lower range of pressure, (x/m) is directly proportional to the applied pressure. x/m ∝ p\n(ii)  At higher range of pressure, (x/m) is independent of applied pressure.  x/m ∝ p⁰\n(iii) At intermediate pressure range, (x/m) is proportional to a fractional power of pressure, i.e., x/m ∝ p¹/ⁿ  where, 1/n is a fraction. Its value may be 0 and 1\n Reference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 148, Exercise 5.10)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(x/m) is inversely proportional to the applied pressure','(x/m) is directly proportional to the applied pressure','(x/m) is proportional to a fractional power of pressure.','(x/m) is independent of applied pressure','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190728,'chemistry','Match the following sols with their respective category depending upon the nature of interaction between the dispersed phase and the dispersion medium','Arsenious sulfide-lyophobic sol, Rubber-lyophilic sols, Gelatin-lyophilic sols, Ferric hydroxide-lyophobic sol','Lyophilic (liquid-loving) sols are colloidal sols that are formed directly by mixing substances such as rubber, gum, gelatine, starch, etc., with a suitable liquid (the dispersion medium).\nSubstances such as metals, their sulphides etc., do not form colloidal sols when simply mixed with the dispersion medium. These sols are known as lyophobic (liquid-hating) sols.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 148, Exercise 5.11)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','match','Arsenious sulfide,lyophobic sol','Rubber,lyophilic sols','Gelatin,lyophilic sols','Ferric hydroxide,lyophobic sol','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190729,'chemistry','Given below are two statements\n \n Statement I : Micelles exhibit colloidal behaviour only above a certain temperature, known as kraft\'s temperature, and a certain concentration, known as Critical Micelle Concentration (CMC).\n \n Statement II : Associated colloids behave as strong electrolytes at lower concentrations but at higher concentrations behave as colloids due to the formation of aggregates.\n \n In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Associated colloids behave as strong electrolytes at lower concentrations but at higher concentrations behave as colloids due to the formation of aggregates, called micelles. They display colloidal behaviour only above a certain temperature, known as kraft\'s temperature, and a certain concentration, known as Critical Micelle Concentration (CMC). Soaps and synthetic detergents are two examples.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 148, Exercise 5.12)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.','Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect.','Statement I is incorrect, and Statement II is correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190730,'chemistry','Suppose you add NaCl, an electrolyte to hydrated ferric oxide sol. The result would be','Particles of ferric oxide sol are positively charged. Thus, they get coagulated in the presence of negatively charge Cl⁻  ions.','When NaCl is introduced to ferric oxide sol, it produces Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions. Ferric oxide sol particles are positively charged. As a result, in the presence of negatively charged Cl⁻ ions, they coagulate.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 148, Exercise 5.15)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Particles of ferric oxide sol are negatively charged. Thus, they form a lyophobic sol on addition of NaCl.','Particles of ferric oxide sol are positively charged. Thus, they get coagulated in the presence of negatively charge Cl⁻  ions.','Particles of ferric oxide sol are positively charged. Thus, form a micelle at a critical temperature.','Particles of NaCl sol are positively charged. Thus, they form a lyophilic sol in the presence of positively charge ferric ions.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190731,'chemistry','What happens when electricity is passed through a colloidal solution ?\n \n a. Colloidal particles begin to migrate toward an oppositely charged electrode. \n \n b. Colloidals particles move away from the oppositely charged electrode.\n \n c. Colloidals particles lose charge at the electrode and become coagulated.\n \n d. Colloidals particles are uncharged, under the influence of electric current the begin to move but do not coagulate.','Both a and c','When an electric current is passed through a sol, colloidal particles begin to move towards an oppositely charged electrode, where they lose their charge and get coagulated. \nThis can be confirmed by electrophoresis experiment. Colloidal particles move towards one of the electrodes when an electric potential is applied across two Pt electrodes dipped in a colloidal solution. Electrophoresis is the movement of colloidal particles under an applied electric potential. Hence, the statements (b) and (d) are false.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 148, Exercise 5.15)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both a and b','Both c and d','Both a and c','Both b and d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190732,'chemistry','Select the correct option(s) from the following statements that explain "Catalysis by Zeolites":\n \n a. They are aluminonitrate having a microporous structure and an Al - O - N framework.\n \n b. The reactions that occur in zeolites are not dependent on the size and form of the reactant and product molecules, but rather on the pores and cavities of the zeolites.\n \n c. Zeolites are commonly used as catalysts in the petrochemical industry for hydrocarbon cracking and isomerisation. They are also used to remove permanent water hardness.\n \n d. ZSM - 5 is a catalyst used in the dehydration of alcohols to gasoline.','Both c and d','a. They are aluminosilicates having a microporous structure and an Al - O - Si framework.\nb. The size and form of the reactant and product molecules, as well as the pores and cavities of the zeolites, influence the reactions that occur in them.\nc. Zeolites are commonly used as catalysts in the petrochemical industry for hydrocarbon cracking and isomerisation. They are also used to remove permanent water hardness.\nd. ZSM - 5 is a catalyst used in the dehydration of alcohols to gasoline.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 148, Exercise 5.21)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both c and d','Both a and c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190733,'chemistry','Given below are two statements\n \n Statement I : Zeolites have pore sizes ranging from 260-740pm and react with only molecules of the same size.\n \n Statement II : Shape-selective catalysis refers to a catalytic reaction that is dependent on the structure of the pores of the catalyst as well as the size of the molecules of the reactant and the product.\n \n In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Shape-selective catalysis refers to a catalytic reaction that is dependent on the structure of the pores of the catalyst as well as the size of the molecules of the reactant and the product. Catalysis using zeolites, for example, is known as shape selective catalysis. Zeolites have pores with sizes ranging from 260 to 740pm. As a result, molecules with pore sizes greater than this cannot enter the zeolite and undergo the reaction.\nHence, both statement I and statement II are correct.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 148, Exercise 5.22)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.','Statement I is incorrect, and Statement II is correct.','Statement I is correct, and Statement II is incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190734,'chemistry','Choose the appropriate option(s) from the following statements that explain " Dialysis":\n \n a. When a colloidal solution is placed in water and an electric current is introduced, the colloidal solution\'s sol particles pass through the membrane and migrate to electrodes with opposite charges.\n \n b. Dialysis can be performed using membranes such as animal membrane (bladder), parchment paper, or cellophane sheet.\n \n c. If the dissolved component in the impure colloidal solution is just an electrolyte, dialysis can be sped up by providing an electric field.\n \n d. Dialysis is the removal of a dissolved substance from a colloidal solution via diffusion through a suitable membrane.','b, c and d','a. Statement (a) is incorrect because colloidal particles are unable to pass through the dialysis membrane. Other ions and contaminants are removed through the dialysis membrane.\nb. Statement (b) is correct as dialysis can be performed using membranes such as animal membrane (bladder), parchment paper, or cellophane sheet.\nc. Statement (c) is also accurate because if the dissolved component in the impure colloidal solution is only an electrolyte, dialysis can be sped up by applying an electric field.\nd. Statement (d) is also accurate as dialysis is the removal of a dissolved substance from a colloidal solution via diffusion through a suitable membrane.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 140 & 148, Exercise 5.23)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a, b and c','b, c and d','a, c and d','a, b and d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190735,'chemistry','Sort the following sols according to their dispersion medium.','Alcosol-colloidal sol of cellulose nitrate in ethanol, Hydrosol-starch sol, Aerosol-fog, Hydrosol-gold sol','(i) Alcosol: An alcosol is a colloidal solution with alcohol as the dispersion medium and a solid component as the dispersed phase.\nAn alcosol is, for example, colloidal sol of cellulose nitrate in ethyl alcohol.\n(ii) Aerosol: An aerosol is a colloidal solution with a gas as the dispersion medium and a solid as the dispersed phase.\nFor instance: fog. \n(iii) Hydrosol: A hydrosol is a colloidal solution with water as the dispersion medium and a solid as the dispersed phase.\nFor instance, starch sol or gold sol.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 148, Exercise 5.26)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','match','Alcosol,colloidal sol of cellulose nitrate in ethanol','Hydrosol,starch sol','Aerosol,fog','Hydrosol,gold sol','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190736,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n \n Assertion : Colloid is not a substance but a state of substance.\n \n Reason : It is the size of the matter\'s particles, or the state in which the material exists. It is in the colloidal state if its size is between 1 and 1000 nm.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','"Colloid is not a substance but a state of substance".\nColloid is not a substance but a state of substance is a correct statement since some compounds that are crystalloids under one set of conditions can be colloids under another. For example, NaCl is a crystalloid in aqueous medium but behaves as a colloid when mixed with benzene. It is the size of the matter\'s particles, or the state in which the material exists. It is in the colloidal state if its size is between 1 and 1000 nm.\nHence, Reason (R) is the correct explanation of assertion (A).\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 148, Exercise 5.27)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Statement I is correct, and Statement II is incorrect.','Statement I is incorrect, and Statement II is correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190737,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect answer from the following explanations of "Rain" :\n \n a. Pure rain water is a suspension since the particles of water scatter light.\n \n b. Condensation in the high atmosphere causes tiny water droplets suspended in the air to combine and size up until they fall as rain.\n \n c. Rain falls when two oppositely charged clouds collide.\n \n d. Artificial rain can be produced by dispersing a charge-carrying substance similar to those observed on clouds from an airplane.','Both a and d','a. Statement (a) is false as pure rain water is a colloid because the water particles are not visible enough to scatter light and display the Tyndall Effect.\nb. Statement (b) is correct as clouds are aerosols composed of tiny water droplets suspended in air. Because of condensation in the high atmosphere, colloidal droplets of water become larger and larger in size until they fall as rain.\nc. Statement (c) is also correct as rainfall can occur when two oppositely charged clouds meet.\nd. Statement (d) is false as it is feasible to create artificial rain by throwing electrified sand or spraying a sol containing a charge opposite to the one on clouds from an aeroplane.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both a and b','Both c and d','Both a and d','Both b and c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190738,'chemistry','Assume a student added equal volumes of acetic acid solutions of two different concentrations to equivalent amounts of charcoal in two separate flasks. He intends to test the adsorption of various concentrations of acetic acid solutions. Which approach can he take?','Freundlich’s equation x/m = k.C¹/ⁿ','Freundlich’s equation approximately describes the behaviour of adsorption from solution with a difference that instead of pressure, concentration of the solution is taken into account, i.e., \nx/m = k.C¹/ⁿ\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 128, Paragraph 5.1.5)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Freundlich’s equation x/m = k.C¹/ⁿ','Freundlich’s equation x/m = k.p¹/ⁿ','Freundlich’s equation log x/m = log k + 1/n log p','Langmuir adsorption θ = Kp/1+Kp','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190739,'chemistry','Choose the appropriate option(s) from the following statements that explain "Helmholtz electrical double layer":\n \n a. Additional electrolytes inhibit the diffusion of the double layer.\n \n b. If two double layers come close enough, attractive van der Waals forces would draw them together.\n \n c. The development of potential difference in a capacitor is similar to how potential difference develops between Helmholtz electrical double layers.\n \n d. A colloid is especially sensitive to ions with positive charges.','Both a and c','a. Statement (c) is correct as the addition of more electrolytes to sol inhibits the diffused double layer and reduces the zeta potential.\nb. Statement (b) is incorrect because if two particles of an insoluble material do not have double layers, attractive van der Waals forces will pull them together. When particles have a double layer, the overall effect is that particles repel each other across long distances.\nc. Statement (c) is correct as the charges of opposite signs on the fixed and diffused parts of the double layer result in a difference in potential between these layers in the same way that potential difference is created in a capacitor.\nd. Statement (d) is incorrect as the colloid is particularly sensitive to oppositely charged ions.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 143, Paragraph (b))','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both a and b','Both c and d','Both a and c','Both b and d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190740,'chemistry','Given below are two statements\n \n Statement I : Soaps have a critical micelle concentration (CMC) of 10⁻⁴ to 10⁻³ mol L-1, and these colloids revert back to individual ions when diluted.\n \n Statement II : Soap sols are highly stable and, in many ways, resemble true solutions.\n \n In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.','Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect.','Statement I is correct because soaps are associated colloids that, when diluted, revert back to individual ions. Soaps have CMCs ranging from 10⁻⁴ to 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹. Statement II, on the other hand, is incorrect since macromolecular colloids are highly stable and, in many ways, resemble true solutions, whereas soap is not a macromolecular colloid.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 138, Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.','Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect.','Statement I is incorrect, and Statement II is correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190741,'chemistry','Which of the following methods is used to transform simple filter paper into ultra-filter paper?','by soaking the filter paper in a collodion solution, hardening it with formaldehyde, and finally drying it.','The filter paper can be made into ultra-filter paper by soaking it in a collodion solution, hardening it with formaldehyde, and finally drying it.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 140, Paragraph 5.4.5)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','by soaking the solution in ether, hardening it with formaldehyde and finally drying it.','by soaking the filter paper in a collodion solution, hardening it with formaldehyde, and finally drying it.','by soaking the filter paper in a sulphuric acid solution, hardening it with 3% alum solution and sun drying it.','by soaking the filter paper in liquid nitrogen, hardening it with nitrocellulose and sun drying it.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190742,'chemistry','Which among the following diagram reactions are correct?(n1908)','Both 1 and 4','Because Ni converts CO and H₂ to methane and water, whereas Cu/Zn-Cr₂O₃ converts CO and H₂ to methanol, reactions (2) and \n(3) are incorrect.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 132, Paragraph 5.2.2) diagram(ne1920)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both 1 and 2','Both 2 and 3','Both 3 and 4','Both 1 and 4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/190742q.png'),
(190811,'chemistry','Among the colloids cheese (C), milk (M) and\n smoke (S), the correct combination of the\n dispersed phase and dispersion medium,\n respectively is [JEE (Main)-2019]','C : liquid in solid; M : liquid in liquid; S : solid\n in gas','Refer diagram(ne1923)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','C : solid in liquid; M : liquid in liquid; S : gas\n in solid','C : liquid in solid; M : liquid in solid; S : solid\n in gas','C : liquid in solid; M : liquid in liquid; S : solid\n in gas','C : solid in liquid; M : solid in liquid; S : solid\n in gas','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190812,'chemistry','An example of solid sol is [JEE (Main)-2019]','Gem stones','Gem stones are solid sol','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Butter','Hair cream','Paint','Gem stones','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190813,'chemistry','Haemoglobin and gold sol are examples of\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','Positively and negatively charged','Haemoglobin →  Positive sol\nMetal → Negative sol','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Negatively charged sols','Positively charged sols','Positively and negatively charged','Negatively and positively charged sols,\n respectively','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190814,'chemistry','For coagulation of arsenious sulphide sol, which\n one of the following salt solution will be most\n effective? [JEE (Main)-2019]','AlCl₃','Arsenious sulphide sol is negatively charged, so\n according to Hardy-Schulze rule the cation which is\n in more oxidation state will be most effective.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','Na₃PO₄','AlCl₃','NaCl','BaCl₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190815,'chemistry','Adsorption of a gas follows Freundlich adsorption\n isotherm. In the given plot, x is the mass of the gas\n adsorbed on mass m of the adsorbent at pressure\np. \nx\n/m is proportional to.... refer diagram(n1911)','P½','In Freundlich adsorption of a gas on the surface of\n solid, the extent of adsorption(x/m) is related to\n pressure of gas (P) as\n\nx/m = kP¹/ⁿ \nOr log x/m = logK + (1/n) log P\nThe slope of plot of log x/m versus log P = 2/4 = 1/2 \n→ x/m ∝ P½','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'3','mcq','P²','P','P¼','P½','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/190815q.png'),
(190816,'chemistry','The Tyndall effect is observed only when following\n conditions are satisfied [JEE (Main)-2017]\n\n(a) The diameter of the dispersed particles is\n much smaller than the wavelength of the light\n used.\n(b) The diameter of the dispersed particle is not\n much smaller than the wavelength of the light\n used\n(c) The refractive indices of the dispersed phase\n and dispersion medium are almost similar in\n magnitude\n(d) The refractive indices of the dispersed phase\n and dispersion medium differ greatly in\n magnitude','(b) and (d)','For Tyndall effect refractive index of dispersion phase\n and dispersion medium must differ significantly.\n Secondly, size of dispersed phase should not differ\n much from wavelength used.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','(a) and (c)','(b) and (c)','(a) and (d)','(b) and (d)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190817,'chemistry','For a linear plot of log x/m \'versus log p in a\n Freundlich adsorption isotherm, which of the\n following statements is correct? (k and n are\n constants) [JEE (Main)-2016]','Only 1\n/n appears as the slope','According to Freundlich adsorption isother : refer diagram(ne1924)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'3','mcq','1/2 appears as the intercept','Only 1\n/n appears as the slope','log (1/n) appears as the intercept','Both k and \n1\n/n appear in the slope term','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190818,'chemistry','3 g of activated charcoal was added to 50 mL of\n acetic acid solution (0.06N) in a flask. After an hour\n it was filtered and the strength of the filtrate was\n found to be 0.042 N. The amount of acetic acid\n adsorbed (per gram of charcoal) is\n [JEE (Main)-2015]','18 mg','Number of moles of acetic acid adsorbed = ( 0.06 × [50/1000] - 0.042 × [50/1000] ) = 0.9/1000 moles.\n\nWeight of acetic acid adsorbed = 0.9 × 60 mg\n= 54 mg\nHence, the amount of acetic acid adsorbed per g of\n charcoal = 54/3 = 18mg.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','18 mg','36 mg','42 mg','54 mg','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190819,'chemistry','The coagulating power of electrolytes having ions\n Na⁺, Al³⁺ and Ba²⁺ for arsenic sulphide sol\n increases in the order [JEE (Main)-2013]','Na⁺ < Ba²⁺ < Al³⁺','The coagulating power of cation to cause\n coagulation of arsenic sulphide sol increases with\n the increase of valency of the cation.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','Al³⁺ < Ba²⁺ < Na⁺','Na⁺ < Ba²⁺ < Al³⁺','Ba²⁺ < Na⁺ < Al³⁺','Al³⁺ < Na⁺ < Ba²⁺','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190820,'chemistry','According to Freundlich adsorption isotherm, which\n of the following is correct? [AIEEE-2012]','All the above are correct for different ranges of\n pressure',NULL,'Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','x/m ∝ p¹','x/m ∝ P¹/ⁿ','x/m ∝ p⁰','All the above are correct for different ranges of\n pressure','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190821,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is incorrect\n regarding physissorptions? [AIEEE-2009]','Enthalpy of adsorption (∆H adsorption) is low and\n positive','Physisorption is an exothermic process with ∆H =\n–20 kJ/mol','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','More easily liquefiable gases are adsorbed\n readily','Under high pressure it results into multi\n molecular layer on adsorbent surface','Enthalpy of adsorption (∆H adsorption) is low and\n positive','It occurs because of van der Waal’s forces','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190907,'chemistry','Which one of the following is not applicable to the phenomenon of adsorption?','∆H > 0','Adsorption is an exothermic process. So, ∆H > 0','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','∆H > 0','∆G < 0','∆S < 0','∆H < 0','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190908,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a favourable condition for physical adsorption?','High Temperature','Physical adsorption is favoured at low temperature.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','High pressure','Negative ∆H','Higher critical temperature of adsorbate','High Temperature','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190909,'chemistry','Physical adsorption of a gaseous species may change to chemical adsorption with','increase in temperature','On increasing temperature physisorption changes to chemisorption. As the temperature increases, energy of activation of adsorbate particles increases which leads to formation of chemical bond between adsorbate and adsorbent. Hence, physisorption transform into chemisorption.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','decrease in temperature','increase in temperature','increase in surface area of adsorbent','decrease in surface area of adsorbent','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190911,'chemistry','Which of the following is an example of absorption?','Water on calcium chloride','Calcium chloride absorbs water. Other examples show adsorption.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Water on silica gel','Water on calcium chloride','Hydrogen on finely divided nickel','Oxygen on metal surface','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190912,'chemistry','On the basis of data given below predict which of the following gases shows least adsorption on a definite amount of charcoal? Refer diagram(n1912)','d','The lesser the value of critical temperature of gases the lesser will be the extent of adsorption. Here H₂ has lowest value of critical temperature, i.e., 33.\nHence, hydrogen gas shows least adsorption on a definite amount of charcoal.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/190912q.png'),
(190913,'chemistry','In which of the following reactions heterogeneous catalysis involved?\n(i) 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g)–>2SO₂(g)\n(ii) 2SO₂(g)–> 2SO₃(g)\n(iii) N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) –>2NH₃(g)\n(iv) CH₃COOCH₃(l) + H₂O(l) HCI —> CH₃COOH(aq) + CH₃OH(aq)','(a) (ii), (iii)','(a) In reaction (ii) and reaction (iii), catalysts are in solid state, and reactants and products are gases','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','(a) (ii), (iii)','(b) (ii), (iii), (iv)','(c) (i), (ii), (iii)','(d) (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190914,'chemistry','At high concentration of soap in water, soap behaves as','associated colloid','At low concentration soap behaves as strong electrolyte but at higher concentration it behaves as colloid due to formation of aggregates called micelles. These are known as associated colloids.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','molecular colloid','associated colloid','macromolecular colloid','lyophilic colloid','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190915,'chemistry','Which of the following will show Tyndall effect?','Aqueous solution of soap above critical micelle concentration.','Aqueous solution of soap above critical micelle concentration leads to the formation of colloidal solution. Tyndall effect is a characteristic of colloidal solution in which colloidal particles show a coloured appearance when sunlight is passes through it and seen perpendicularly.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Aqueous solution of soap below critical micelle concentration.','Aqueous solution of soap above critical micelle concentration.','Aqueous solution of sodium chloride.','Aqueous solution of sugar.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190916,'chemistry','Method by which lyophobic sol can be protected?','By addition of lyophilic sol','Lyophobic sol can be protected by adding lyophilic sol which is known as protective colloid.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','By addition of oppositely charged sol','By addition of an electrolyte','By addition of lyophilic sol','By boiling','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190917,'chemistry','Freshly prepared precipitate sometimes gets converted to colloidal solution by','peptisation','Peptisation is the process of converting freshly prepared precipitate into colloid.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','coagulation','electrolysis','diffusion','peptisation','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190918,'chemistry','Which of the following electrolytes will have maximum coagulating value for Ag⁺/Ag sol ?','Na₃PO₄','According to Hardy-Schulze law, the greater the charge on anion, the greater will be its coagulating power.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','Na₂S','Na₃PO₄','Na₂SO₄','NaCl','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190919,'chemistry','A colloidal system having a solid substance as a dispersed phase and a liquid as a dispersion medium is classified as','sol','It is called sol.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','solid sol','gel','emulsion','sol','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190920,'chemistry','The values of colligative properties of colloidal solution are of small order in comparison to those shown by true solutions of same concentration because colloidal particles','are comparatively less in number','Colloidal particles are large in size and less in number.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','exhibit enormous surface area','remain suspended in the dispersion medium','form lyophilic colloids','are comparatively less in number','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190921,'chemistry','Arrange the following diagrams in the correct sequence of steps involved in the mechanism of catalysis, in accordance with modem adsorption theory. Refer diagram(n1913)','b','Correct sequence is i —» iii -» ii —»iv -> v\nEach transformation denotes a meaningful process as follows\ni —»adsorption of A and B on surface\niii —»ii interaction between A and B to form intermediate\nii —»iv starting desorption of A-B\niv —» v complete desorption. Refer diagram(ne1926)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/190921q.png'),
(190922,'chemistry','Which of the following process is responsible for the formation of delta at a place where rivers meet the sea?','Coagulation','A delta is formed at a place where rivers meet the sea due to the process of setting down of colloidal particles. The ions which are present in sea water are responsible for coagulation.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Emulsification','Colloid formation','Coagulation','Peptisation','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190923,'chemistry','Which of the following curves is in accordance with Freundlich adsorption isotherm?','iii','Refer diagram(ne1927)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','i','ii','iii','iv','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190924,'chemistry','Which of the following process is not responsible for the presence of electric charge on the sol particles?','Absorption of ionic species from solution','Absorption of ionic species from solution is not responsible for the presence of electric charge on the sol particles. Charge on the sol particles is due to\n(i) electrons capture by sol particles during electro dispersion of metal.\n(ii) preferential adsorption of ionic species from solution.\n(iii) formation of Helmholtz electrical double layer.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Electron capture by sol particles','Adsorption of ionic species from solution','Formation of Helmholtz electrical double layer','Absorption of ionic species from solution','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190925,'chemistry','Which of the following phenomenon is applicable to the process shown in the figure? Refer diagram(n1916)','b','Colour is adsorbed on animal charcoal, hence sugar solution which comes out from the column is colourless.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/190925q.png'),
(190926,'chemistry','Which of the following options are correct?\n(a) Micelle formation by soap in aqueous solution is possible at all temperatures.\n(b) Micelle formation by soap in aqueous solution occurs above a particular concentration.\n(c) On dilution of soap solution micelles may revert to individual ions.\n(d) Soap solution behaves as a normal strong electrolyte at all concentrations.','b,c','Micelle formation is possible only above critical micelle concentration and on dilution the micelles may form electrolytes.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','b,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190927,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct about solid catalyst?\n(a) Same reactants may give different product by using different catalysts.\n(b) Catalyst does not change AH of reaction.\n(c) Catalyst is required in large quantities to catalyse reactions.\n(d) Catalytic activity of a solid catalyst does not depend upon the strength of chemisorption.','a,b','Same reactants may give different products by using different catalysts as different catalysis have different specific functions to mould the reaction towards specific product, e.g., starting with H₂ and CO, and using different catalysts, we get different products. Refer diagram(ne1928)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','b,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190928,'chemistry','Freundlich adsorption isotherm is given by the expression x/m=KP1/n. Which of the following conclusions can be drawn from this expression? Refer diagram(n1917)','a,c','Refer diagram(ne1929)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','b,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/190928q.png'),
(190929,'chemistry','H₂ gas is adsorbed on activated charcoal to a very little extent in comparison to easily liquefiable gases due to\n(a) very strong van der Waals interaction\n(b) very weak van der Waals forces\n(c) very low critical temperature\n(d) very high critical temperature.','b,c','Due to weak van der Waals forces and very low critical temperature, H2 is adsorbed to a limited extent.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','b,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190930,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Mixing two oppositely charged sols neutralizes their charges and stabilizes the colloid.\n(b) Presence of equal and similar charges on colloidal particles provides stability to the colloids.\n(c) Any amount of dispersed liquid can be added to emulsion without destabilizing it.\n(d) Brownian movement stabilizes sols.','b,d','Presence of equal and similar charges on colloidal particles provides stability to colloids as repulsive forces between charge particles having same charge prevent them from colliding when they come closer to each other.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','b,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190931,'chemistry','An emulsion cannot be broken by and\n(a) heating\n(b) adding more amount of dispersion medium\n(c) freezing\n(d) adding emulsifying agent','b,d','An emulsion cannot be broken by adding more amount of dispersion medium and emulsifying agent. It can be broken by heating, freezing, etc.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','b,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190932,'chemistry','Which of the following substances will precipitate the negatively charged emulsions?\n(a) KCl (b) Glucose\n(c) Urea (d) NaCl','a,d','KCl and NaCl contain K⁺ and Na⁺ which will precipitate negatively charged emulsions.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','b,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190933,'chemistry','Which of the following colloids cannot be coagulated easily?\n(a) Lyophobic colloids (b) Irreversible colloids\n(c) Reversible colloids (d) Lyophilic colloids','c,d','Lyophilic colloids (liquid loving colloids) which are also known as reversible colloid cannot be coagulated easily. The stability of these colloids are due to\n(i) Charge on colloidal particles and\n(ii) Solvation of colloidal particles.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','b,d','c,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190934,'chemistry','What happens when a lyophilic sol is added to a lyophobic sol?\n(a) Lyophobic sol is protected.\n(b) Lyophilic sol is protected.\n(c) Film of lyophilic sol is formed over lyophobic sol.\n(d) Film of lyophobic sol is formed over lyophilic sol.','a,c','Lyophobic sols are protected due to formation of film of lyophobic sol.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','c,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190935,'chemistry','Which phenomenon occurs when an electric field is applied to a-colloidal solution and electrophoresis is prevented?\n(a) Reverse osmosis takes place.\n(b) Electroosmosis takes place.\n(c) Dispersion medium begins to move.\n(d) Dispersion medium becomes stationary.','b,c','The dispersion medium begins to move in an electric field when electrophoresis is prevented. This is known as electroosmosis.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190936,'chemistry','In a reaction, catalyst changes \n(a) physically (b) qualitatively\n(c) chemically (d) quantitatively','a,b','In a reaction catalyst changes physically and qualitatively as it is unaltered during the reaction and remain quantitatively intact after completion of reaction, and does not change chemically.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190937,'chemistry','Which of the following phenomenon occurs when a chalk stick is dipped in ink?\n(a) Adsorption of coloured substance\n(b) Adsorption of solvent\n(c) Absorption and adsorption both of solvent\n(d) Absorption of solvent.','a,d','Both adsorption and absorption take place. Coloured substance is adsorbed while solvent is absorbed.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
