(231005,'chemistry','What would be color of the compound [CoCl(NH₃)₅]⁺², if it absorbs yellow light?','Violet','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 259; Table 9.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Colour in Coordination Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Red','Violet','Yellow','Yellow-orange','NEW','12-259','12-133',NULL),
(231006,'chemistry','Assertion: [Ti(H₂O)₆]⁺³ appears violet in colour.\nReason: Light corresponding to the energy of blue-green region is absorbed by the complex, it would\nexcite the electron from t₂𝓰 level to the e𝓰 level.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','[Ti(H₂O)₆]⁺³, which is violet in colour. This is an octahedral complex\nwhere the single electron (Ti3+ is a 3d1 system) in the metal d orbital is\nin the t2g level in the ground state of the complex. The next higher state\navailable for the electron is the empty eg level.Light corresponding to the energy of blue-green region is absorbed by the complex, it would\nexcite the electron from t₂𝓰 level to the e𝓰 level.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 259; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Colour in Coordination Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are false.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','NEW','12-259','12-133',NULL),
(231007,'chemistry','Assertion: [Ti(H₂O)₆]Cl₃ on heating becomes colourless.\nReason: In absence of ligand crystal field splitting does not occur.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','It is important to note that in the absence of ligand,\ncrystal field splitting does not occur and hence the\nsubstance is colourless. For\nexample, removal of water\nfrom [Ti(H2O)6]Cl3 on heating\nrenders it colourless.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 260; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Colour in Coordination Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are false.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','NEW','12-260','12-134',NULL),
(231008,'chemistry','What is the colour of anhydrous CuSO₄?','White','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 260; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Colour in Coordination Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Blue','Red','White','Green','NEW','12-260','12-134',NULL),
(231009,'chemistry','In an experiment nickel(II) chloride is dissolved in water, then didentate ligand, ethane-1,2-diamine(en) is progressively added in the\nmolar ratios. What result is expected?','Change in color from pale blue to violet','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 260; Paragraph 1) diagram(ne2303)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Colour in Coordination Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','Change in color from pale blue to violet','Change in color from violet to pale blue.','No change in colour is observed.','It is a explosive reaction, involving huge amount of release of heat.','NEW','12-260','12-134',NULL),
(231010,'chemistry','Which d-block element is cause for the colour of ruby?','Cr','Ruby is aluminium oxide (Al2O3) containing about 0.5-1% Cr3+ ions (d3), which are\nrandomly distributed in positions normally occupied by Al3+. We may view\nthese chromium(III) species as octahedral chromium(III) complexes incorporated\ninto the alumina lattice; d–d transitions at these centres give rise to the colour.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 260; Colour of Some gemstones)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Colour in Coordination Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Cu','Cr','Co','Fe','NEW','12-260','12-134',NULL),
(231011,'chemistry','In emerald, Cr⁺³ occupy ____ sites in mineral beryl.','octahedral','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 261; Colour of Some gemstones)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Colour in Coordination Compounds',NULL,'1','fillup','oct,ah,ed,ral',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-261','12-135',NULL),
(231012,'chemistry','Which of the following could not be explained by CFT?','Covalent character of bonding between the ligand and the central\natom','The crystal field model is successful in explaining the formation, structures, colour and magnetic properties of coordination compounds\nto a large extent. However, from the assumptions that the ligands are point charges, it follows that anionic ligands should exert the greatest\nsplitting effect. The anionic ligands actually are found at the low end of the spectrochemical series. Further, it does not take into account\nthe covalent character of bonding between the ligand and the central\natom.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 261; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Colour in Coordination Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','Color of coordination compounds','Sructure of coordination compounds','Magnetic properties of coordination compounds','Covalent character of bonding between the ligand and the central\natom','NEW','12-261','12-135',NULL),
(231013,'chemistry','Assertion: According crystal field theory anionic ligands should exert greatest splitting effect.\nReason: Anionic ligands are actuallly found at the low end of the spectrochemical series.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','The crystal field model is successful in explaining the formation, structures, colour and magnetic properties of coordination compounds\nto a large extent. However, from the assumptions that the ligands are point charges, it follows that anionic ligands should exert the greatest\nsplitting effect. The anionic ligands actually are found at the low end of the spectrochemical series. Further, it does not take into account\nthe covalent character of bonding between the ligand and the central\natom.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 261; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Colour in Coordination Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are false.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','NEW','12-261','12-135',NULL),
(231014,'chemistry','Assertion: [Ni(NH₃)₆]⁺² is an outer orbital complex.\nReason: In [Ni(NH₃)₆]⁺², Ni is in +2 oxidation state and the hybridisation involved is sp³d².','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 261; Intext Question 9.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Colour in Coordination Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are false.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','NEW','12-261','12-135',NULL),
(231015,'chemistry','Predict the numbe of unpaired electrons in [Pt(CN)₄]²⁻.','0','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 261; Intext Question 9.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Colour in Coordination Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','2','0','1','4','NEW','12-261','12-135',NULL),
(231101,'chemistry','What is the shape of pentacarbonyliron(0)?','trigonal bipyramidal','Tetracarbonylnickel(0) is tetrahedral, pentacarbonyliron(0) is trigonalbipyramidal while\nhexacarbonyl chromium(0) is octahedral.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 261; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Bonding in Metal Carbonyls',NULL,'1','mcq','trigonal bipyramidal','square pyramidal','octahedral','pentagonal planar','NEW','12-261','12-135',NULL),
(231106,'chemistry','Which statement is true for metal-carbon bond in metal carbonyls?','The metal to ligand bonding creates a synergic effect which strengthens the\nbond between CO and the metal.','The metal-carbon bond in metal carbonyls\npossess both s and p character. The M–C s bond is formed by the donation of lone pair of electrons\non the carbonyl carbon into a vacant orbital of the metal. The M–C p bond is formed by the\ndonation of a pair of electrons from a filled d orbital of metal into the vacant antibonding p* orbital of\ncarbon monoxide. The metal to ligand bonding\ncreates a synergic effect which strengthens the\nbond between CO and the metal(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 262; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Bonding in Metal Carbonyls',NULL,'2','mcq','The metal to ligand bonding creates a synergic effect which strengthens the\nbond between CO and the metal.','The M–C 𝛑 bond is formed by the donation of a pair of electrons from a filled d orbital of metal into the vacant bonding 𝛑 orbital of\ncarbon monoxide.','The metal-carbon bond in metal carbonyls\npossess only 𝛑 character.','The M–C σ bond is formed by the donation of lone pair of electrons on the carbonyl carbon into a vacant  anntibonding orbital of\nthe metal.','NEW','12-262','12-136',NULL),
(231207,'chemistry','Which of the following are coordination compounds of great biological importance?','All of these','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 263; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Importance and Application of Coordination Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Chlorophyll','Carboxypeptidase','Haemoglobin','All of these','NEW','12-263','12-137',NULL),
(231208,'chemistry','Which metal is found in Wilkinson catalyst?','Rh','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 263; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Importance and Application of Coordination Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Rh','Re','Ru','Rd','NEW','12-263','12-137',NULL),
(231209,'chemistry','Which of the following is used to treat lead poisoning?','EDTA','There is growing interest in the use of chelate therapy in medicinal\nchemistry. An example is the treatment of problems caused by the\npresence of metals in toxic proportions in plant/animal systems.\nThus, excess of copper and iron are removed by the chelating ligands\nD–penicillamine and desferrioxime B via the formation of coordination\ncompounds. EDTA is used in the treatment of lead poisoning.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 263; Paragraph 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Importance and Application of Coordination Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','DMG','EDTA','D–penicillamine','cis–platin','NEW','12-263','12-137',NULL),
(231210,'chemistry','Which of the folllowing chelating ligands are used to remove excess copper and iron?','D–penicillamine','There is growing interest in the use of chelate therapy in medicinal\nchemistry. An example is the treatment of problems caused by the\npresence of metals in toxic proportions in plant/animal systems.\nThus, excess of copper and iron are removed by the chelating ligands\nD–penicillamine and desferrioxime B via the formation of coordination\ncompounds. EDTA is used in the treatment of lead poisoning.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 263; Paragraph 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Importance and Application of Coordination Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','DMG','EDTA','D–penicillamine','cis–platin','NEW','12-263','12-137',NULL),
(231211,'chemistry','Which of the following is anti-cancer compound?','cis-platin','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 263; Paragraph 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Importance and Application of Coordination Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','DMG','EDTA','D–penicillamine','cis-platin','NEW','12-263','12-137',NULL),
(231212,'chemistry','Which solution fixes the developed film in black and white photography?','Hypo','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 263; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Importance and Application of Coordination Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Hypo','Potassium permangante','Iodine solution','All of these','NEW','12-263','12-137',NULL),
(231213,'chemistry','Which of the following complex help smooth and even electroplating with gold and silver?','[Ag(CN)₂]⁻','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 263; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Importance and Application of Coordination Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','[Ag(Cl)₂]⁻','[Ag(CN)₂]⁻','[Ag(NCS)₂]⁻','[Ag(en)₂]','NEW','12-263','12-137',NULL),
(231376,'chemistry','The complex that will exhibit maximum ionic conductivity in aqueous solution is','K4[Fe(CN)6]','Ionic conductivity is directly proportional to the number of ions.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','K4[Fe(CN)6]','[Co(NH3)6]Cl3','[Cu(NH3)4]Cl2','[Ni(Co)4]','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231377,'chemistry','An excess of AgNO3 is added in 100ml of a 0.01 M solution of dichlorotetraaquachromium(III)chloride. The no. of moles of AgCl would be precipitated would be\n1. 0.002\n2. 0.003\n3. 0.01\n4. 0.001','0.001','One mole of Cl- will react with AgNO3 to give one mole of AgCl.\nThe given complex is [Cr(H2O)4Cl2]+Cl-,hence only one ionizable Cl- is present and will be precipitated. \n0.01 M means 0.01 mol per 1000 ml of solution.\n100 ml(given) sol will have   (0.01/1000)×100 = 0.001mol of complex \nHence,0.001 mol of AgCl will be precipitated','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','0.002','0.003','0.01','0.001','NEW','12-245','12-119',NULL),
(231378,'chemistry','Ethylene DIaminetetraacetate (EDTA) ion is','Hexadentate ligand with four "O" and two "N" donor atoms','EDTA is a hexadentate ligand. EDTA is a hexadentate ligand with four "O"  atoms and two " N" as donor atoms.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','Bidentate ligand with two "N" donor atoms','Tridentate Ligand with three "N" donor atoms','Hexadentate ligand with four "O" and two "N" donor atoms','Unidentate ligand','NEW','12-262','12-136',NULL),
(231379,'chemistry','[Co(NH3)4(NO2)2]Cl exhibits:','linkage isomerism, ionization isomerism and geometrical isomerism','The compound [Co(NH3)4(NO2)2]Cl exhibits following type of isomerism: \n(i) LINKAGE ISOMERISM \n[Co(NH3)4(NO2)2]Cl and \n[Co(NH3)4(ONO)2]Cl \n(ii) IONISATION ISOMERISM \n[Co(NH3)4(NO)2Cl]NO2 and \n[Co(NH3)4(NO2)2]Cl \n(iii) GEOMETRICAL ISOMERISM','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','linkage isomerism, ionization isomerism and optical isomerism','linkage isomerism, ionization isomerism and geometrical isomerism','geometrical isomerism, ionization isomerism and optical isomerism','linkage isomerism, geometrical isomerism and optical isomerism','NEW','12-253','12-125',NULL),
(231350,'chemistry','CFSE (in the octahedral field) will be maximum in: (Atomic number Co = 27 )','[Co(CN)6]3-','Stronger the field of the ligand, more the magnitude of Δoct','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','[Co(H2O)6]3+','[Co(NH3)6]3+','[Co(CN)6]3-','[Co(C2O4)3]3-','NEW','12-257','12-131',NULL),
(231351,'chemistry','The CFSE for [CoCl6]4- is 18000cm-1. The CFSE for [CoCl4]2- will be','8000 cm-1','[CoCl4]−2 is tetrahedral whereas [CoCl6]-4 is octahedral.\nΔt = (4/9)×Δ0\nΔt = (4/9)×18000 = 8000 cm−1','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','MCQ','6000 cm-1','16000 cm-1','18000 cm-1','8000 cm-1','NEW','12-257','12-131',NULL),
(231352,'chemistry','Which among the following is a paramagnetic complex?','[CoBr4]-2','If the central atom has an unpaired electron then the complex is paramagnetic','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','MCQ','[Co(NH36]+3','[Pt(en)(Cl2)]','[CoBr4]-2','Mo(CO)6','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231353,'chemistry','The atomic numbers of Cr and Fe are respectively 24 and 26. Among the following the one that is paramagnetic with the spin of electrons is','[Cr(NH3)6]3+','CO and CN- are strong field ligands(SFL)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','MCQ','CrCO6','FeCO5','[Fe(CN6)]4-','[Cr(NH3)6]3+','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231354,'chemistry','Among the given complex, the complex having the highest paramagnetic behaviour is (At No. Ti = 22, Cr = 24, Co = 27, Zn = 30)','[Cr(NH3)6]3+','More the number of unpaired electrons , more will be paramagnetic behaviour.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','MCQ','[Zn(NH3)6]2+','[Ti(NH3)6]3+','[Cr(NH3)6]3+','[Co(NH3)6]3+','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231355,'chemistry','The coordination compound that will give maximum number of isomers is:','[Cr(SCN)2(NH3)4]2+','1. [Co(NH3)4Cl2] has two geometrical isomers that are cis and trans form. It has no optical isomers due to a plane of symmetry. \n2. [Ni(en)(NH3)4]2+ will show optical isomerism.\n3. [Ni(C2O4)(en)2] will show optical isomerism. \n4.[Cr(SCN)2(NH3)4]2+ shows geometrical isomerism in cis and trans form as well as linkage isomerism due to presence of thiocyanate or SCN−. SCN− is an ambidentate ligand so it can coordinate with either sulphur or nitrogen atom. Hence [Cr(SCN)2(NH3)4]2+ will have maximum number of isomers.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','MCQ','[Co(NH3)4Cl2]','[Ni(en)(NH3)4]2','[Ni(C2O4)(en)2]','[Cr(SCN)2(NH3)4]2+','NEW','12-253','12-125',NULL),
(231356,'chemistry','Which one of the following is expected to exhibit optical isomerism?','cis-[Co(en)2Cl2]','When compound mirror image is non-superimposable then it is optically active. cis-[Pt(NH3)2Cl2]  and trans−[Pt(NH3)2Cl2] has a plane of symmetry, thus, they are optically inactive. trans−[Co(en)2Cl2] is also optically inactive because it has plane of symmetry. But in the case of cis-[Co(en)2Cl2] no element of symmetry is present. The mirror image of cis-[Co(en)2Cl2] is non-superimposable hence it is optically active.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','MCQ','cis-[Pt(NH3)2Cl2]','cis-[Co(en)2Cl2]','trans−[Co(en)2Cl2]','trans−[Pt(NH3)2Cl2]','NEW','12-252','12-124',NULL),
(231357,'chemistry','Which of the following does not exhibit optical isomerism?','[Co(NH3)3Cl3]','Optical isomerism is exhibited by only those complexes in which plane of symmetry is absent. Hence, due to the presence of symmetry elements optical isomerism is absent in [Co(NH3)3Cl3].','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','MCQ','[Co(en)2Cl2]+','[Co(NH3)3Cl3]','[Co(en)Cl2(NH3)2]+','[Co(en)3]+3','NEW','12-252','12-124',NULL),
(231358,'chemistry','An ion among the following that has a magnetic moment of 2.84 BM is: \n(At No. Ni=28, Ti=22, Cr= 24, Co = 27)','Ni2+','μ = √n(n+2)  BM \nμ = 2.84 BM \nAfter solving this we get n = 2 So, only Ni2+ has 2 unpaired electrons as its atomic number is 28 and configuration is 3d8.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','MCQ','Ni2+','Ti3+','Cr2+','Co2+','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231359,'chemistry','In [Cr(NH3)6](Br)3, number of unpaired electrons in Cr','3','In [Cr(NH3)6]Br3, let oxidation state of Cr is x ⇒ x+ 6(0) = +3  \nThus it shows the configuration t2^3 eg^0.\nd1,d2, d3 , d8 ,d9 ,d10 configuration is the configuration that do not get affected whether the ligand is a strong field ligand or a weak field ligand. Hence, the number  of unpaired electrons will be 3.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','MCQ','4','3','1','2','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231360,'chemistry','The incoorect option among the following is','Ni(CO)4 Tetrahedral and Paramagnetic','Ni(CO)4 is sp3 hyberdised and Tetrahedral & Diamagnetic in nature\n[Ni(CN)4]2- is dsp2 hyberdised and sqaure planar & Diamagnetic in nature\n[NiCl4]2- is sp3 hyberdised and Tetrahedral & paramagnetic in nature','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','MCQ','Ni(CO)4 Tetrahedral and Paramagnetic','[Ni(CN)4]2- Square Planar and Diamagnetic','Ni(CO)4 Tetrahedral and Diamagnetic','[Ni(Cl)4]2- Tetrahedral and Paramagnetic','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(181322,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are applicable to a balanced chemical equation of an elementary reaction?\n(a) Order is same as molecularity\n(b) Order is less than the molecularity\n(c) Order is greater than the molecularity\n(d) Molecularity can never be zero','a,d','For an elementary reaction, the order and molecularity are same. Molecularity of a reaction can never be zero.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,d','a,c,d','a,b','c,d','NEW','12-104','12-070',NULL),
(231361,'chemistry','The correct order of spin-only magnetic moment for the given complexes is:','[MnCl6]3- > [Co(H 2O)6]2+ > [Fe(CN)6]3-','Spin-only magnetic moment depends on the number of unpaired electron. Spin only formula to calculate the magnetic moment of transition metal ions is \nμ =√n(n+2) BM where n = no. of unpaired electrons. Thus it depends on the number of unpaired electrons as well as the nature of ligands. Strong field ligand can pair up the electrons but weak field ligands can\'t pair the electrons. CN- is a strong field ligand.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','MCQ','[Co(H2O)6]2+ > [MnCl6]3- > [Fe(CN)6]3-','[Fe(CN)6]3- > [Co(H2O)6]2+ > [MnCl8]3-','[MnCl6]3- > [Fe(CN)6]3- > [Co(H2O)6]2+','[MnCl6]3- > [Co(H 2O)6]2+ > [Fe(CN)6]3-','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231362,'chemistry','A magnetic moment of 1.73 BM will be shown by which one of the following:','[Cu(NH3)4]2+','For magnetic moment of 1.73, n=1 which is possible in Cu+2 3d9','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','MCQ','[Ni(CN)4]2-','TiCl4','[COCl6]4','[Cu(NH3)4]2+','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231363,'chemistry','[Cr(H2O)6]Cl3 (At. No. of Cr = 24 ) has a magnetic moment of 3.83 BM. The correct distribution of 3d electrons in the chromium of the complex is','3d1zy, 3d1xy, 3d1xz','complex [Cr(H2O)6]Cl3 is octahedral and its lower energy set is t2g after splitting. Hence unpaired electrons will first occupy a lower energy t2g set of orbitals which is formed by dxy dyz dzx','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','MCQ','(3dx2-y2)^1, (3dz2)^1, (3dxy)^1','(3dxy)^1, (3dx2 -y2)^1, (3dyz)^1','3d1zy, 3d1xy, 3d1xz','(3dxy)^1 ,(3dyz)^1 ,(3dz2)^1','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231364,'chemistry','Considering H2O as a weak ligand, the number of unpaired electrons in [Mn(H2O)6]2+ will be\n(Mn = 25 Atomic No.)','5','STEP 1: In [Mn(H2O)6]2+, let oxidation state of Mn is x, So, x + 6(0) = +2 ⇒ x = +2 \nSTEP 2: The electronic configuration of Mn is [Ar] 3d5 4s2. So, Mn2+ will have electronic configuration [Ar]3d5. As water is a weak field ligand , Mn+2 has total number of unpaired elctrons are 5.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','MCQ','5','2','4','3','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231365,'chemistry','Match List 1 and 2\nList1 (Complexes)                                                                                             List2 (Type of Isomer)\na. [Co(NH3)5NO2]Cl2 and [Co(NH3)5ONO]Cl2                                        I. Ionisaton Isomerism\nb. [Cr(NH3)6 [Co(CN)6] and [Cr(CN)6], [Co(NH3)6]                                  II. Coordination Isomerism\nc. [Co(NH3)5SO4]Br and [Co(NH3)5Br]SO4                                             III. Linkage Isomerism\nd. [Cr(H2O)6]Cl3 and  [Cr(H2O)5Cl]Cl2.H2O                                             IV. Solvate Isomerism','a-III, b-II, c-I, d-IV','1. [Co(NH3)5NO2]Cl2 and [Co(NH3)5ONO]Cl2 is an example of linkage isomerism. Linkage isomerism arises in a coordination compound containing an ambidentate ligand and NO2- is an ambidentate ligand. \n2. [Cr(NH3)6] [Co(CN)6] and [Cr(CN)6][Co(NH3)6] is an example of Coordination Isomerism. This type of isomerism arises from the interchange of ligands between cationic and anionic entities of different metal ions present in a complex. \n3. [Co(NH3)5(SO4)]Br and [Co(NH3)5Br]SO4 is an example of ionization isomerism. In this isomerism counter ion in a complex salt is itself a potential ligand and can displace a ligand it can then become the counter ion. \n4. [Cr(H2O)6]Cl3 and [Cr(H2O)5Cl]Cl2.H2O is an example of solvate isomerism. This form of isomerism is also known as ‘hydrate isomerism\'. In this type of isomerism, compounds have the same formula but differ only in the number of water molecules of crystallization. Hence, option third is the correct answer.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','MCQ','a-III, b-I, c-II, d-IV','a-II, b-III, c-IV, d-I','a-III, b-II, c-I, d-IV','a-IV, b-III, c-II, d-I','NEW','12-253','12-125',NULL),
(231366,'chemistry','The existance of two different coloured complexes with the composition of [Co(NH3)4Cl2]+ is due to','Geometrical Isomerism','Geometrical isomerism is exhibited by the complexes of [MA4B2] type.The complex [Co(NH3)4Cl2]+ is a [MA4B2] type complex and therefore it exhibits geometrical isomerism.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','MCQ','Linkage Isomerism','Geometrical Isomerism','Coordination Isomerism','Ionisation Isomerism','NEW','12-252','12-124',NULL),
(231367,'chemistry','The number of isomers of [Pt(NH3)4] [CuCl4]','4','Isomerism arises from the interchange of ligands between cationic and anionic entities of different metal ions present in a complex.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','MCQ','2','3','4','5','NEW','12-252','12-124',NULL),
(231368,'chemistry','The complexes [Co(NH3)6] [Cr(CN)6] and [Cr(NH3)6] [Co(CN)6] show','Coordination Isomerism','Co-ordination Isomers. Both the cation and anion of the complex must be complex ions in order to exhibit coordination isomerism and exchange of ligands takes place between the cation and anion.\nThus, the complex [Co(NH3)6][Cr(CN)6] and [Cr(NH3)6][Co(CN)6] are the examples of co-ordination isomerisms.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','MCQ','Ionisation Isomerism','Coordination Isomerism','Geometrical Isomerism','Linkage Isomerism','NEW','12-253','12-125',NULL),
(231369,'chemistry','The correct statement, among the following regarding [Mn(CN)6]3-','It is d2sp3 hyberdised and octahedral','The oxidization state of Mn in the complex [Mn(CN)6]3− is +3. Electronic configuration of  Mn+3 → 3d4\nAs the CN- is a strong ligand, it causes the pairing of electrons in the complex. \nThe coordination number of Mn is 6, therefore an octahedral complex is formed.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','MCQ','It is sp3d2 hyberdised and tetrahedral','It is d2sp3 hyberdised and octahedral','It is dsp2 hyberdised and square planar','It is sp3d2 hyberdised and octahedral','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231370,'chemistry','Among [Ni(CO)4], [Ni(CN)4]2- and [Ni(Cl)4]2- species the hyberdisation states of Ni atom are respectively \n(Atomic No. Ni = 28)','sp3, dsp2, sp3','[Ni(CO)4]	is sp3 hyberdised and haveTetrahedral\n[Ni(CN)4]2- is dsp2	hyberdised and have Square planar\n[Ni(Cl)4]2- is sp3 hyberdised and have Tetrahedral','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','MCQ','sp3, dsp2, sp3','sp3, sp3, dsp2','dsp2, sp3, sp3','sp3, dsp2, dsp2','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231371,'chemistry','of the following complex ions which is diamagnetic in nature','[Ni(CN)4]2-','[Ni(CN)4]2− all the electrons are paired in the complex, therefore, it is diamagnetic in nature','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','MCQ','[Ni(CN)4]2-','[CuCl4]2-','[CoF6]3-','[NiCl4]2-','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231372,'chemistry','Which one of the following is an outer orbital complex and exhibits paramagnetic behaviour','[Ni(NH3)6]2+','An orbital possessing unpaired d-orbital has the ability to exhibit paramagnetic behaviour.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','MCQ','[Ni(NH3)6]2+','[Zn(NH3)6]2+','[Cr(NH3)6]3+','[Co(NH3)6]3+','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231373,'chemistry','The number of unpaired electrons in the complex ion [CoF6]3- is \n(Atomic No. Co = 27)','4','The hybridization of Co3+ is sp3d2 . In [CoF6]3–, Co contains three unpaired electrons. Hence, option second is the correct answer.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','MCQ','2','3','4','0','NEW','12-256','12-130',NULL),
(231374,'chemistry','Which of the following complexes has the highest paramagnetic behaviour?\n(where gly = glycine, en = ethylenediamine, ox = oxalate ion and bpy = bipyridyl moieties)\n(At no: Ti= 22, V = 23 Fe = 26 Co = 27 )','[Co(OX)2(OH)2]','Greater the number of unpaired electrons, the larger is the paramagnetic character.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','[V(gly)2(OH)2(NH3)2]+','[Fe(en)(bpy)(NH3)2]2+','[Co(OX)2(OH)2]','[Ti(NH3)6]3+','NEW','12-256','12-130',NULL),
(231375,'chemistry','A diagmagnetic compound among the following is','[Ni(CN)4]2-','If the central atom has a zero unpaired electron then the complex is paramagnetic','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','[Co(F6)]3-','[Ni(CN)4]2-','[NiCl4]2-','[Fe(CN)6]3-','NEW','12-256','12-130',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(231380,'chemistry','The compound that give a pair of Enantiomorphsis:(en=NH2CH2CH2NH)','[Co(en)2CI2]CI','HINT:[Co(en)2Cl2]Cl will give a pair of enantiomorphs. Explanation: Enantiomorphs or enantiomers are a pair of compounds which are non superimposable mirror images of each other. The example is [Co(en)2Cl2]+  Dichlorobis [ethylene diamine] cobalt (III) can occur as enantiomorphs','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','[Co(NH3)4CI2]NO2','[Cr(NH3)6][Co(CN)6]','[Co(en)2CI2]CI','[Pt(NH3)4][PtCI6]','NEW','12-252','12-124',NULL),
(231381,'chemistry','"The d-electron configurations of Cr²⁺, Mn²⁺, Fe²⁺ and Ni²⁺ and 3d⁴, 3d⁵, 3d⁶ and 3d⁸ respectively. Which one of the following aqua complexes will exhibit the minimum paramagnetic behaviour? (At. No. Cr= 24, Mn = 25, Fe = 26, Ni = 28)"','[Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺','"Minimum paramagnetic behaviour is shown by the complex with minimum number of unpaired electrons. Mn²⁺(23)= 3d⁵–5unpaired electrons Fe²⁺(24)= 3d⁶–4 unpaired electrons Ni²⁺(26)= 3d⁸– 2 unpaired electrons Cr²⁺(22)= 3d⁴ –4 unpaired electrons So, [Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺ will exhibit minimum paramagnetic behaviour as it has the least number of unpaired electrons i.e. 2"','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','[Mn(H₂O)₆]²⁺','[Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺','[Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺','[Cr(H₂O)₆]²⁺','NEW','12-256','12-130',NULL),
(231424,'chemistry','The CFSE for octahedral [CoCl₆]⁻⁴ is 9000 cm⁻1. The CFSE for tetrahedral [CoCl₄]⁻² will be','4000','Solution is given below in diagram(ne2306)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','8000','4000','16,000','18,000','NEW','12-257','12-131',NULL),
(231425,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a chelating agent?\na. Glycinato\nb. Thiosulphato\nc. Oxalato\nd. Ethane-1,2 diamine','a only','When a di- or polydentate ligand uses its two or more donor\natoms simultaneously to bind a single metal ion, it is said to be a\nchelating ligand\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 247; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b,c','a,d','d','NEW','12-263','12-137',NULL),
(231426,'chemistry','Which of the following outer orbital complexes has same number of unpaired  electrons?\na. [MnCl₆]⁻³\nb. [FeF₆]⁻³\nc. [CoF₆]⁻³','a,c','Solution is given below in diagram(ne2307)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','none of them','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231427,'chemistry','Which of the following complexes are heteropleptic according to the given diagram(n2302)','b,d','Complexes in which a metal is bound to only one kind of donor\ngroups is called as homoleptic complex, whereas when more than one type of ligand are present in complex,  it is called heteroleptic complex.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 248; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,d','a,b,c','all of the above','NEW','12-255','12-129','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/231427q.png'),
(231428,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: Linkage isomerism arises in coordination compounds containing bidentate ligand\n\nReason: Ambidentate ligand has two different donor atoms','The assertion is false, but the reason is true.','Linkage isomerism arises in a coordination compound containing ambidentate ligand. \n\nA simple example is provided by complexes containing the thiocyanate ligand, NCS–, which may bind through the nitrogen to give M–NCS or through sulphur to give M–SCN.\n\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 253; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is false, but the reason is true.','Both statements are false.','NEW','12-253','12-125',NULL),
(231429,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: Toxic metal ions are removed by the chelating ligands.\n\nReason: Chelate complexes tend to be more stable.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','There is growing interest in the use of chelate therapy in medicinal chemistry. An example is the treatment of problems caused by the presence of metals in toxic proportions in plant/animal systems. Thus, excess of copper and iron are removed by the chelating ligands D–penicillamine and desferrioxime B via the formation of coordination compounds. EDTA is used in the treatment of lead poisoning.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 263; Paragraph 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is false, but the reason is true.','Both statements are false.','NEW','12-263','12-137',NULL),
(231430,'chemistry','Match the coordination compounds with the central metal atoms as given in diagram(n2303)','A⟶(2), B⟶(3), C⟶(1)','Chlorophyll, hemoglobin, and Vit. B12 are coordination compounds of Mg, Fe and Co respectively. Coordination compounds also find many applications in electroplating, textile dyeing, and medicinal chemistry. (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 244; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A⟶(2), B⟶(1), C⟶(3)','A⟶(3), B⟶(1), C⟶(2)','A⟶(2), B⟶(3), C⟶(1)','A⟶(2), B⟶(1), C⟶(3)','NEW','12-263','12-137','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/231430q.png'),
(231501,'chemistry','The coordination numbers of Co and Al in\n [Co(Cl)(en)₂]Cl and K₃[AI(C₂O₄)₃], respectively, are\n (en = ethane-1, 2-diamine) [JEE (Main)-2019]','5 and 6','[Co(Cl)(en)₂]Cl\nCl⁻ - monodentate ligand\nen - bidentate ligand\n→ Co-ordination No. of Co = (2 × 2) + 1 = 5\nK₃[Al(C₂O₄)₃\n]\nC₂O²⁻₄ - bidentate ligand\n→ Co-ordination No. of Al = 2 × 3 = 6','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','3 and 3','5 and 3','5 and 6','6 and 6','NEW','12-248','12-122',NULL),
(231502,'chemistry','Complete removal of both the axial ligands (along\n the z-axis) from an octahedral complex leads to\n which of the following splitting patterns? (relative\n orbital energies not on scale).\n[JEE (Main)-2019] refer diagram(n2308)','2','The field becomes square planar and the order of\n energy is dₓ²₋ᵧ² > dₓᵧ > d_z² > d_zx = d_yz.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231503,'chemistry','The complex ion that will lose its crystal field\n stabilization energy upon oxidation of its metal to\n +3 state is','[Fe(phen)₃]\n\n²⁺','Refer diagram(ne2308)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','[Ni(phen)₃]\n\n²⁺','[Co(phen)₃]\n²⁺','[Zn(phen)₃]\n²⁺','[Fe(phen)₃]\n\n²⁺','NEW','12-257','12-131',NULL),
(231504,'chemistry','The crystal field stabilization energy (CFSE) of\n [Fe(H₂O)₆]Cl₂ and K₂[NiCl₄], respectively, are\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','–0.4∆₀ and –0.8∆ₜ','[Fe(H₂O)₆\n\n²⁺  t_2g⁴ e_g²         CFSE = –0.4∆₀\n[NiCl₄]²⁻        e⁴ t₂⁴                  CFSE = –0.8∆ₜ','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','–2.4∆₀ and –1.2∆ₜ','–0.6∆₀ and –0.8∆ₜ','–0.4∆₀ and –0.8∆ₜ','–0.4∆₀ and –1.2∆ₜ','NEW','12-257','12-131',NULL),
(231505,'chemistry','The INCORRECT statement is [JEE (Main)-2019]','The gemstone, ruby, has Cr³+ ions occupying\n the octahedral sites of beryl.','Ruby is aluminium oxide (Al₂O₃) containing about\n 0.5 – 1% Cr³⁺ ions which are randomly distributed\n in the position normally occupied by Al³⁺ ions.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','The spin-only magnetic moments of [Fe(H₂O)₆]\n²+\n and [Cr(H₂O)₆]\n²+ are nearly similar.','The gemstone, ruby, has Cr³+ ions occupying\n the octahedral sites of beryl.','The spin-only magnetic moment of\n [Ni(NH₃)\n₄(H₂O)₂]\n²+ is 2.83 BM.','The color of [CoCl(NH₃)₅]\n²+ is violet as it\n absorbs the yellow light.','NEW','12-261','12-135',NULL),
(231506,'chemistry','Three complexes, [CoCl(NH₃)₅]³⁺(l),\n [Co(NH₃)\n₅H₂O]³⁺(ll) and [Co(NH₃)₆]\n³⁺(lll) absorb light\n in the visible region. The correct order of the\n wavelength of light absorbed by them is :\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','(II) > (I) > (III)','In a co-ordination compound, the strong field ligand\n causes higher splitting of the d-orbitals. Wavelength\n of the energy absorbed by the co-ordination\n compound is inversely proportional to ligand field\n strength of the given co-ordination compound. The\n decreasing order of ligand field strength is NH₃ >\nH₂O > Cl. Therefore decreasing order of wavelength absorbed is (I) > (II) > (III).','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','(I) > (II) > (III)','(II) > (I) > (III)','(III) > (I) > (II)','(III) > (II) > (I)','NEW','12-260','12-134',NULL),
(231507,'chemistry','The species that can have a trans-isomer is :\n (en = ethane-1, 2-diamine, ox = oxalate)\n[JEE (Main)-2019]','[Pt(en)₂Cl₂]²\n⁺','Refer diagram(ne2309)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','[Zn(en)Cl₂]','[Pt(en)Cl₂]','[Cr(en)₂(ox)]⁺','[Pt(en)₂Cl₂]²\n⁺','NEW','12-252','12-124',NULL),
(231508,'chemistry','The maximum possible denticities of a ligand given\n below towards a common transition and inner-\ntransition metal ion, respectively, are\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','6 and 8','The maximum possible denticities of the given ligand\n towards transition metal ion is 6 and towards inner\n transition metal ion is 8.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','6 and 8','8 and 6','8 and 8','6 and 6','NEW','12-248','12-122',NULL),
(231509,'chemistry','The correct statements among I to III are\n(I) Valence bond theory cannot explain the color\n exhibited by transition metal complexes.\n(II) Valence bond theory can predict quantitatively\n the magnetic properties of transition metal\ncomplexes.\n(III) Valence bond theory cannot distinguish ligands\n as weak and strong field ones.\n[JEE (Main)-2019]','(I) and (III) only','Valence bond theory cannot predict quantitatively\n the magnetic properties of transition metal complex.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','(II) and (III) only','(I), (II) and (III)','(I) and (II) only','(I) and (III) only','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231510,'chemistry','The degenerate orbitals of [Cr(H₂O)₆]³⁺ are\n[JEE (Main)-2019]','dxz and dyz','Cr³⁺ has d³ configuration and forms an octahedral\n inner orbitals complex.The set of degenerate orbitals are (dxy, dyz and dxz)\n and (d_x²-y² and  d_z²)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','dxz and dyz','d_x²-y² and dxy','dz² and dxz','dyz and dz²','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231511,'chemistry','The one that will show optical activity is\n (en = ethane-1,2-diamine) [JEE (Main)-2019] Refer diagram(n2309)','c','It has has no plane of symmetry or centre of symmetry,\nhence it is optically active. Refer diagram(ne2310)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-252','12-124',NULL),
(231512,'chemistry','The number of water molecule(s) not coordinated to\n copper ion directly in CuSO₄.5H₂O, is\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','1','In CuSO₄.5H₂O, four H₂O molecules are directly\n coordinated to the central metal ion while one H₂O molecule is hydrogen bonded.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','4','1','2','3','NEW','12-248','12-122',NULL),
(231513,'chemistry','The calculated spin-only magnetic moments (BM) of\n the anionic and cationic species of [Fe(H₂O)₆]₂ and\n [Fe(CN)₆], respectively, are [JEE (Main)-2019]','0 and 4.9','Refer diagram(ne2311)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','2.84 and 5.92','4.9 and 0','0 and 5.92','0 and 4.9','NEW','12-256','12-130',NULL),
(231514,'chemistry','The compound that inhibits the growth of tumors is\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','cis-[Pt(Cl)₂(NH₃)\n₂]','Refer diagram(ne2312)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','cis-[Pt(Cl)₂(NH₃)\n₂]','trans-[Pt(Cl)₂(NH₃)\n₂]','cis-[Pd(Cl)₂(NH₃)\n₂]','trans-[Pd(Cl)₂(NH₃)\n₂]','NEW','12-263','12-137',NULL),
(231515,'chemistry','The following ligand is [JEE (Main)-2019]','Tetradentate','It has four lone pairs.\nDenticity is 4.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Tetradentate','Tridentate','Bidentate','Hexadentate','NEW','12-248','12-122',NULL),
(231516,'chemistry','The correct order of the spin-only magnetic moment\n of metal ions in the following low-spin complexes,\n [V(CN)₆]⁴⁻, [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻, [Ru(NH₃)₆]³⁺  and\n [Cr(NH₃)\n₆]\n²⁺ , is [JEE (Main)-2019]','V²\n⁺ > Cr²\n⁺ > Ru³\n⁺ > Fe²\n⁺','Refer diagram(ne2313)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','V²\n⁺ > Cr²\n⁺ > Ru³\n⁺ > Fe²\n⁺','Cr²\n⁺ > V²\n⁺ > Ru²\n⁺ > Fe²\n⁺','V²\n⁺ > Ru³⁺ > Cr²\n⁺ > Fe²\n⁺','Cr²\n⁺ > Ru³⁺ > Fe²\n⁺ > V²\n⁺','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231517,'chemistry','The magnetic moment of an octahedral homoleptic\n Mn(II) complex is 5.9 BM. The suitable ligand for\n this complex is [JEE (Main)-2019]','NCS⁻','Electronic configuration of Mn²\n⁺ is\n Mn²\n⁺ : 3d⁵\n\nIt has 5 unpaired electrons which corresponds to\n magnetic moment of √35 = 5.9 BM. This shows that\n the homoleptic complex of Mn²\n⁺ has only weak field\n ligands and that is NCS⁻. The remaining three\n ligands are strong field ligands.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','CO','Ethylenediamine','NCS⁻','CN⁻','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231518,'chemistry','Mn₂(CO)₁₀ is an organometallic compound due to\n the presence of [JEE (Main)-2019]','Mn – C bond','It is organometallic compound due to presence of\n Mn – C bond.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','Mn – C bond','Mn – Mn bond','Mn – O bond','C – O bond','NEW','12-262','12-136',NULL),
(231519,'chemistry','The pair of metal ions that can give a spin only\n magnetic moment of 3.9 BM for the complex\n [M(H₂O)₆]Cl₂, is [JEE (Main)-2019]','V²\n⁺ and Co²\n⁺','μ = 3.9 BM\nSo, the central metal ion has 3 unpaired electrons.\n→ Configuration is either d³ or d⁷ as H₂O is a weak\n field ligand.\nV²\n⁺ has d³ configuration.\nCo²\n⁺ has d⁷ configuration.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','V²\n⁺ and Co²\n⁺','Co²\n⁺ and Fe²\n⁺','V²\n⁺ and Fe²\n⁺','Cr²\n⁺ and Mn²\n⁺','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231520,'chemistry','The metal d-orbitals that are directly facing the\n ligands in K₃[Co(CN)₆] are [JEE (Main)-2019]','dx² – y² and dz²','K₃[Co(CN)₆]\nDuring splitting in octahederal co-ordination entities,\n dx²– y² and dz² orbitals point towards the direction\n of ligands (i.e. they experience more repulsion and\n their energy is raised)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','dxy, dxz and dyz','dxz, dyz and dz²','dx² – y² and dz²','dxy and dx² – y²','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231521,'chemistry','The coordination number of Th in\n K₄[Th(C₂O₄)\n₄(OH₂\n)₂] is [JEE (Main)-2019]','10','K₄[Th(C₂O₄)\n₄(OH₂\n)₂] \nC₂O₄²⁻ is bidentate ligand and H2O is monodentate\n ligand.\n→ Co-ordination no. of Th = 2 × 4 + 2 = 10','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','10','6','14','8','NEW','12-248','12-122',NULL),
(231522,'chemistry','The number of bridging CO ligand(s) and Co-Co\n bond(s) in Co₂(CO)₈, respectively are\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','2 and 1','Refer diagram(ne2314)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','2 and 1','0 and 2','2 and 0','4 and 0','NEW','12-262','12-136',NULL),
(231523,'chemistry','The difference in the number of unpaired electrons\n of a metal ion in its high spin and low-spin\n octahedral complexes is two. The metal ion is\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','Co²\n⁺','Refer diagram(ne2315)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','Ni²\n⁺','Mn²\n⁺','Fe²\n⁺','Co²\n⁺','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231524,'chemistry','A reaction of cobalt (III) chloride and\n ethylenediamine in a 1 : 2 mole ratio generates\n two isomeric products A (violet coloured) and B\n (green coloured). A can show optical activity, but,\n B is optically inactive. What type of isomers does\n A and B represent? [JEE (Main)-2019]','Geometrical isomers','[CoCl₆]\n³⁻ + 2(en)→  [CoCl₂(en)₂]\n\n(1 : 2 mole ratio)              (cis-trans isomer)\nA - optically active (cis-isomer)\nB - optically inactive (trans isomer)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','Ionisation isomers','Coordination isomers','Geometrical isomers','Linkage isomers','NEW','12-252','12-124',NULL),
(231525,'chemistry','The electrolytes usually used in the electroplating\n of gold and silver, respectively, are\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','[Au(CN)₂]\n⁻ and [Ag(CN)₂]\n⁻','[Au(CN)₂]⁻ and [Ag(CN)₂]⁻ are used in the\n electroplating of Au and Ag respectively.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','[Au(CN)₂]⁻ and [AgCl₂]\n⁻','[Au(NH₃)\n₂]\n+ and [Ag(CN)₂]\n⁻','[Au(CN)₂]\n⁻ and [Ag(CN)₂]\n⁻','[Au(OH)₄]\n⁻ and [Ag(OH)₂]\n⁻','NEW','12-262','12-136',NULL),
