(231527,'chemistry','Wilkinson catalyst is (Et = C₂H₅)\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','[(Ph₃P)₃RhCl]','Wilkinson\'s catalyst is [Rh(PPh₃)₃]Cl','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','[(Ph₃P)₃IrCl]','[(Ph₃P)₃RhCl]','[(Et₃P)₃IrCl]','[(Et₃P)₃RhCl]','NEW','12-263','12-137',NULL),
(231528,'chemistry','The complex that has highest crystal field splitting\n energy (∆), is [JEE (Main)-2019]','K₃[Co(CN)₆]','For the same metal ion, greater the co-ordination\n number and greater the strength of the\n ligands, greater is the value of crystal field splitting\n energy\n : ⁻CN > NH₃ > H₂O > Cl⁻','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','[Co(NH₃)\n₅Cl]Cl₂','K₂[CoCl₄]','K₃[Co(CN)₆]','[Co(NH₃)\n₅(H₂O)]Cl₃','NEW','12-257','12-131',NULL),
(231529,'chemistry','Homoleptic octahedral complexes of a metal ion\n `M³⁺’ with three monodentate ligands L₁, L₂ and L₃\n absorb wavelengths in the region of green, blue\n and red respectively. The increasing order of the\n ligand strength is [JEE (Main)-2019]','L₃ < L₁ < L₂','Greater the energy or lesser the wavelength of light\n absorbed, greater is the ligand strength\n.\nEnergy : \nBlue > Green > Red\nSo, ligand strength : L₂ > L₁ > L₃','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','L₁ < L₂ < L₃','L₃ < L₂ < L₁','L₃ < L₁ < L₂','L₂ < L₁ < L₃','NEW','12-260','12-134',NULL),
(231530,'chemistry','Two complexes [Cr(H₂O)₆]Cl₃ (A) and [Cr(NH₃)\n₆]Cl₃\n(B) are violet and yellow coloured, respectively. The\n incorrect statement regarding them is\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','0 values of (A) and (B) are calculated from\n the energies of violet and yellow light,\n respectively','Refer diagram(ne2317)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','0 values of (A) and (B) are calculated from\n the energies of violet and yellow light,\n respectively','Both are paramagnetic with three unpaired\n electrons','0 value for (A) is less than that of (B)','Both absorb energies corresponding to their\n complementary colors','NEW','12-260','12-134',NULL),
(231531,'chemistry','Consider the following reaction and statements\n: [Co(NH₃)₄Br₂]\n⁺ + Br⁻ → [Co(NH₃)₃Br₃] + NH₃\n\n(I) Two isomers are produced if the reactant\n complex ion is a cis-isomer\n(II) Two isomers are produced if the reactant\n complex ion is a trans-isomer.\n(III) Only one isomer is produced if the reactant\n complex ion is a trans-isomer.\n(IV) Only one isomer is produced if the reactant\n complex ion is a cis-isomer.\nThe correct statements are:','(I) and (III)','From the given image it is clear that two isomers are produced if the reactant complexion is a cis-isomer, as well as only one isomer, is produced if the reactant complex is a trans-isomer.\n\nStatement (I) and (III) are correct. Refer diagram(ne2318)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','(I) and (II)','(I) and (III)','(III) and (IV)','(II) and (IV)','NEW','12-252','12-124',NULL),
(231532,'chemistry','The oxidation states ofINSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(51415,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about root-mean-square speed is/are correct? \n \n a. Root-mean-square speed is the speed of a gas molecule that has the average kinetic energy of the gas. \n \n b. Root-mean-square speed is directly proportional to the temperature of the gas. \n \n c. Root-mean-square speed is inversely proportional to the molar mass of the gas.','all of them','Root-mean-square speed is defined as the speed of a gas molecule that has the average kinetic energy of the gas. It is directly proportional to the temperature of the gas, as predicted by the kinetic theory of gases. It is also inversely proportional to the square root of the molar mass of the gas, since heavier molecules move more slowly than lighter molecules.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 148 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','all of them','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51416,'chemistry','What is the ideal gas equation? \n \n a. PV = nRT \n \n b. P = nRT/V \n \n c. V = nRT/P','only a','The ideal gas equation, also known as the general gas equation, is PV = nRT, where P is the pressure, V is the volume, n is the number of moles, R is the gas constant, and T is the absolute temperature.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 145 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only a','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/51416q.png'),
(51417,'chemistry','What is the gas constant in the ideal gas equation? \n \n a. 0.0821 L·atm/mol·K \n \n b. 8.31 J/mol·K \n \n c. 8.314 J/mol·K','all of them','The gas constant can be expressed in different units depending on the application. The two most common units are L·atm/mol·K and J/mol·K, which are numerically equivalent but differ in their physical meaning.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 145 paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','all of them','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51418,'chemistry','What is the significance of the ideal gas equation? \n \n a. It describes the behavior of real gases under all conditions. \n \n b. It provides a theoretical framework for understanding the macroscopic properties of gases. \n \n c. It allows us to calculate the pressure, volume, temperature, or amount of gas in a system from the other variables.','only c','The ideal gas equation is a mathematical relationship that allows us to relate the pressure, volume, temperature, or amount of gas in a system, given the values of the other variables. It is based on the assumptions of the kinetic-molecular theory, which describes the behavior of ideal gases but may not apply to real gases under all conditions.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 145 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only c','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51419,'chemistry','What is the standard molar volume of a gas at STP? \n \n a. 22.4 L/mol \n \n b. 24.5 L/mol \n \n c. 25.0 L/mol','only a','The standard molar volume of a gas at STP (standard temperature and pressure) is 22.4 L/mol. STP is defined as a temperature of 273.15 K (0 °C) and a pressure of 1 atm (101.3 kPa).\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 144 paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only a','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51420,'chemistry','What is the relationship between the molar mass of a gas and its density at STP? \n \n a. The higher the molar mass, the higher the density. \n \n b. The lower the molar mass, the higher the density. \n \n c. There is no correlation between molar mass and density.','only a','The density of a gas at STP is directly proportional to its molar mass. This is because at a fixed temperature and pressure, the same number of gas molecules occupy the same volume, but heavier molecules have more mass per unit volume than lighter molecules.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 146 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only a','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51421,'chemistry','What is the relationship between the temperature and the volume of an ideal gas at constant pressure? \n \n a. V ∝ T \n \n b. V ∝ 1/T \n \n c. V = constant','only a','According to Gay-Lussac\'s law, the volume of an ideal gas at constant pressure is directly proportional to its temperature, provided that the pressure remains constant. This relationship can be expressed mathematically as V/T = constant or V1/T1 = V2/T2.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 142 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only a','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51422,'chemistry','What is the relationship between the pressure and the volume of an ideal gas at constant temperature? \n \n a. P ∝ V \n \n b. P ∝ 1/V \n \n c. P = constant','only b','According to Boyle\'s law, the pressure of an ideal gas at constant temperature is inversely proportional to its volume, provided that the temperature remains constant. This relationship can be expressed mathematically as PV = constant or P\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 140 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,b','only b','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51423,'chemistry','In the following question a statement of Assertion (A) followed by a statement of Reason (R) is given. Choose the correct option out of the choices given below in each question\n \n Assertion (A): At higher altitudes, the boiling point of a liquid decreases.\n \n Reason (R): At higher altitudes, the atmospheric pressure decreases.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','At high altitudes atmospheric pressure is low. Therefore liquids at high altitudes boil at lower temperatures in comparison to that at sea level. Since water boils at low temperature on hills, the pressure cooker is used for cooking food. In hospitals surgical instruments are sterilized in autoclaves in which boiling point of water is increased by increasing the pressure above the atmospheric pressure by using a weight covering the vent.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 155 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51424,'chemistry','n the following question a statement of Assertion (A) followed by a statement of Reason (R) is given. Choose the correct option out of the choices given below in each question\n \n Assertion (A): Gases are compressible while liquids and solids are not.\n \n Reason (R): The particles in gases have more space between them compared to liquids and solids.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','The gaseous state is characterized by the following physical properties. \n \n • Gases are highly compressible. \n \n • Gases exert pressure equally in all directions. \n \n • Gases have much lower density than the solids and liquids. \n \n • The volume and the shape of gases are not fixed. These assume volume and shape of the container. \n \n • Gases mix evenly and completely in all proportions without any mechanical aid.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 140 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51425,'chemistry','In the following question a statement of Assertion (A) followed by a statement of Reason (R) is given. Choose the correct option out of the choices given below in each question\n \n Assertion (A): Boyle\'s law states that the volume of a gas is inversely proportional to its pressure, keeping temperature constant.\n \n Reason (R): As the pressure on a gas increases, the volume of the gas decreases, and vice versa.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','On the basis of his experiments, Robert Boyle reached to the conclusion that at constant temperature, the pressure of a fixed amount (i.e., number of moles n) of gas varies inversely with its volume. This is known as Boyle’s law.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 140 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51426,'chemistry','In the following question a statement of Assertion (A) followed by a statement of Reason (R) is given. Choose the correct option out of the choices given below in each question\n \n Assertion (A): Real gases do not behave ideally at high pressures and low temperatures. \n \n Reason (R): At high pressures and low temperatures, the volume of gas molecules becomes significant compared to the total volume of the gas, and the intermolecular forces become significant.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Real gases follow gaseous laws only under certain specific conditions when forces of interaction are practically negligible. In all other situations these deviate from ideal behaviour. You will learn about the deviations later in this unit.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 145 paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51427,'chemistry','In the following question a statement of Assertion (A) followed by a statement of Reason (R) is given. Choose the correct option out of the choices given below in each question\n \n Assertion (A): The ideal gas law only applies to gases at low pressures and high temperatures.\n \n Reason (R): The ideal gas law assumes that gas molecules have negligible volume and do not interact with each other, which is not true at high pressures and low temperatures.','A is true but R is false','A gas that follows Boyle’s law, Charles’ law and Avogadro law strictly is called an ideal gas. Such a gas is hypothetical. It is assumed that intermolecular forces are not present between the molecules of an ideal gas. Real gases follow these laws only under certain specific conditions when forces of interaction are practically negligible. In all other situations these deviate from ideal behaviour. You will learn about the deviations later in this unit.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 145 paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but B is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51428,'chemistry','In the following question a statement of Assertion (A) followed by a statement of Reason (R) is given. Choose the correct option out of the choices given below in each question\n \n Assertion (A): The van der Waals equation accounts for the non-ideal behavior of real gases.\n \n Reason (R): The van der Waals equation adds correction factors to the ideal gas law to account for the volume and intermolecular forces of gas molecules.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','The repulsive forces cause the molecules to behave as small but impenetrable spheres. The volume occupied by the molecules also becomes significant because instead of moving in volume V, these are now restricted to volume (V–nb) where nb is approximately the total volume occupied by the molecules themselves. Here, b is a constant. Having taken into account the corrections for pressure and volume, we can rewrite equation as (p+an²/V²)(V-nb)=nRT that is known as van der Waals equation.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 151 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51429,'chemistry','In the following question a statement of Assertion (A) followed by a statement of Reason (R) is given. Choose the correct option out of the choices given below in each question\n \n Assertion (A): The pressure of a gas decreases as its volume increases, according to Charles\'s law.\n \n Reason (R): At constant pressure, the volume of a gas increases as its temperature increases, according to Charles\'s law.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Charles’ law, which states that pressure remaining constant, the volume of a fixed mass of a gas is directly proportional to its absolute temperature.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 143 paragraph 1st)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51430,'chemistry','In the following question a statement of Assertion (A) followed by a statement of Reason (R) is given. Choose the correct option out of the choices given below in each question\n \n Assertion (A): The density of a gas increases as its temperature increases.\n \n Reason (R): At constant pressure, the volume of a gas increases as its temperature increases, according to the ideal gas law.','A is true but R is false','Gases become denser at high pressure. A relationship can be obtained between density\n \n and pressure of a gas by using Boyle’s law.By definition, density ‘d’ is related to the\n \n mass ‘m’ and the volume ‘V’ by the relation d=m/V\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 141 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51431,'chemistry','In the following question a statement of Assertion (A) followed by a statement of Reason (R) is given. Choose the correct option out of the choices given below in each question\n \n Assertion (A): The kinetic molecular theory explains the behavior of gases.\n \n Reason (R): The kinetic molecular theory assumes that gas molecules are in constant random motion and collide elastically with each other and the walls of the container.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','One of the assumption of kinetic molar theory is :\n \n Particles of a gas are always in constant and random motion. If the particles were at rest and occupied fixed positions, then a gas would have had a fixed shape which is not observed.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 149 paragraph 5)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51432,'chemistry','In the following question a statement of Assertion (A) followed by a statement of Reason (R) is given. Choose the correct option out of the choices given below in each question\n \n Assertion (A): The ideal gas law assumes that gas molecules have negligible volume and do not interact with each other.\n \n Reason (R): The assumptions of the ideal gas law are based on the kinetic molecular theory and the behavior of gases under certain conditions.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','The laws (e.g., Boyle’s law, Charles’ law etc.) which are concise statements of experimental facts observed in the laboratory by the scientists. Conducting careful experiments is an important aspect of scientific method and it tells us how the particular system is behaving under different conditions.A theory is a model (i.e., a mental picture) that enables us to better understand our observations. The theory that attempts to elucidate the behaviour of gases is known as kinetic molecular theory.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 149 paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51433,'chemistry','In the following question a statement of Assertion (A) followed by a statement of Reason (R) is given. Choose the correct option out of the choices given below in each question\n \n Assertion (A): The temperature of a gas affects its average kinetic energy, according to the kinetic molecular theory. \n \n Reason (R): The kinetic molecular theory assumes that the average kinetic energy of gas molecules is proportional to their temperature.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','If a molecule has variable speed, then it must have a variable kinetic energy. Under these circumstances, we can talk only about average kinetic energy. In kinetic theory, it is assumed that average kinetic energy of the gas molecules is directly proportional to the absolute temperature. It is seen that on heating a gas at constant volume, the pressure increases. On heating the gas, kinetic energy of the particles increases and these strike the walls of the container more frequently, thus, exerting more pressure.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 149 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51434,'chemistry','In the following question a statement of Assertion (A) followed by a statement of Reason (R) is given. Choose the correct option out of the choices given below in each question\n \n Assertion (A): The molar volume of a gas decreases as its pressure increases, according to Avogadro\'s law. \n \n Reason (R): Avogadro\'s law states that the volume of a gas is directly proportional to the number of moles of gas molecules, at constant temperature and pressure.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Avogadro\'s law states that equal volumes of all gases under the same conditions of temperature and pressure contain equal number of molecules. This means that as long as the temperature and pressure remain constant, the volume depends upon number of molecules of the gas or in other words amount of the gas.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 144 paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51435,'chemistry','In the following question a statement of Assertion (A) followed by a statement of Reason (R) is given. Choose the correct option out of the choices given below in each question\n \n Assertion (A): The root-mean-square speed of gas molecules is proportional to the square root of their temperature, according to the kinetic molecular theory. \n \n Reason (R): The kinetic molecular theory assumes that the root-mean-square speed of gas molecules is proportional to the square root of their average kinetic energy.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','The mean square speed is the direct measure of the average kinetic energy of gas molecules. If we take the square root of the mean of the square of speeds then we get a value of speed which is different from most probable speed and average speed. This speed is called root mean square speed.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 148 paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51436,'chemistry','In the following question a statement of Assertion (A) followed by a statement of Reason (R) is given. Choose the correct option out of the choices given below in each question\n \n Assertion (A): The pressure of a gas is proportional to the number of collisions between its molecules and the walls of the container. \n \n Reason (R): The pressure of a gas is defined as the force per unit area exerted by the gas molecules on the walls of the container..','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Collisions of gas molecules are perfectly elastic. This means that total energy of molecules before and after the collision remains same. There may be exchange of energy between colliding molecules, their individual energies may change, but the sum of their energies remains constant. If there were loss of kinetic energy, the motion of molecules will stop and gases will settle down. This is contrary to what is actually observed.\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 151 paragraph 7)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(180105,'chemistry','Thermodynamics tells about the____ of a reaction whereas chemical kinetics tells about the ____ of a reaction','Feasiblity Rate','The feasibility of the reaction can be predicted by thermodynamics and rate of reaction can be predicted by chemical kinetics(Reference:  NCERT 12th chemistry ; Page 95 ; Paragraph 5)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Introduction',NULL,'1','fillup','Fe,a,si,bli,ty R,at,e',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-095','12-061',NULL),
(180106,'chemistry','Which of the following factors affect the rate of reaction?','All of the above','(Reference:  NCERT 12th chemistry ; Page 96 ; Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Concentration','Temperature and pressure','Catalyst','All of the above','NEW','12-096','12-062',NULL),
(180107,'chemistry','Select the correct statements among the following:','All of the above','(Reference:  NCERT 12th chemistry ; Page 96 ; Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Introduction',NULL,'3','mcq','By mixing of aq. AgNO₃ and aq. NaCl, precipitation of AgCl occurs instantaneously.','Rusting of iron in presence of air and moisture is very slow process.','Hydrolysis of starch proceed with a moderate speed .','All of the above','NEW','12-096','12-062',NULL),
(180108,'chemistry','"Diamond is forever".What does that statement means-','Conversion of diamond into graphite is slow enough to predict at all.','(Reference:  NCERT 12th chemistry ; Page 95 ; Paragraph 5)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Diamond is so stable that it never disappeares.','Conversion of diamond into graphite is slow enough to predict at all.','Diamond is most stable isotope of carbon.','Statement is incorrect','NEW','12-095','12-062',NULL),
(180109,'chemistry','Select the correct statements among the following:','All of the above','(Reference:  NCERT 12th chemistry ; Page 96 ; Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Introduction',NULL,'3','mcq','At the macroscopic level, we are interested in amounts reacted or formed and the rates of their consumption or\nformation.','At the molecular level, the reaction mechanisms involving orientation and energy of molecules undergoing collisions.','Kinetic studies  help us to determine the speed or rate of a chemical reaction and conditions by which the reaction rates can be altered.','All of the above','NEW','12-096','12-062',NULL),
(180212,'chemistry','The decomposition of N2O5 in CCl4 at 318K has been studied by monitoring the concentration of N2O5 in the solution. Initially the concentration of N2O5 is 2.33 mol L–1 and after 184 minutes, it is reduced to 2.08 mol L–1. The reaction takes place according to the equation\n2 N₂O₅ (g) → 4 NO₂ (g) + O₂ (g)\nCalculate the average rate of this reaction in terms of hours, minutes and seconds. What is the rate of production of NO₂ during this period?','2.72 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹min⁻¹','(Reference:  NCERT 12th chemistry ; Page 100 ; Example 4.2)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Rate of reaction',NULL,'3','mcq','2.72 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹min⁻¹','3.72 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹min⁻¹','4.72 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹min⁻¹','5.72 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹min⁻¹','NEW','12-100','12-066',NULL),
(180511,'chemistry','Select the second order reaction:','Both a and c','(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 104; paragraph 4, 5)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Molecularity of a reaction',NULL,'2','mcq','KClO₃ + FeSO₄ + H₂SO₄ → KCl + Fe₂(SO₄)₃ + 3H₂O','2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O +O₂','2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O +O₂ (In presense of I⁻ in alkaline medium)','Both a and c','NEW','12-104','12-069',NULL),
(180616,'chemistry','Assertion: Half life of zero order reaction, t₁/₂ = [R]₀/3k.                                                                                                                             Reason: Half life of zero order reaction is directly proportional to rate constant.','Both assertion and reason are false.','Half life of zero order reaction, t₁/₂ = [R]₀/2k.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     Half life of zero order reaction is inversely proportional to rate constant.(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 110; Paragraph 1,2)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Integrated rate equations',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-110','12-076',NULL),
(180617,'chemistry','Half life of first order reaction is','0.693/k','(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 110; Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Integrated rate equations',NULL,'1','mcq','k/0.693','0.693/k','0.693k','2.303k','NEW','12-110','12-076',NULL),
(180618,'chemistry','Assertion: For first order reaction, half life period is constant.                                                                                                                    Reason: Half life is independent of initial concentration of the reacting species for first order reaction.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Half life of first order reaction, half life period is constant because it is independent of initial concentration of the reacting species.(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 111; Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Integrated rate equations',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-111','12-076',NULL),
(180619,'chemistry','Assertion: For zero order reaction t₁/₂ ∝ [R]₀.                                                                                                                                           Reason: For first order reaction t₁/₂ is independent of [R]₀.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 111; Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Integrated rate equations',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(180620,'chemistry','A first order reaction is found to have a rate constant, k = 5.5 × 10⁻¹⁴s⁻¹. Find the half life of the reaction.','1.26 × 10¹³s','(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 111; Example 4.7)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Integrated rate equations',NULL,'2','mcq','1.26 × 10¹³s','0.26 × 10¹³s','3.26 × 10¹³s','2.26 × 10¹³s','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(180621,'chemistry','CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O→CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH (in acidic medium) is example of','Pseudo first order reaction','(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 112; Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Integrated rate equations',NULL,'1','mcq','First order reaction','Second order reaction','Pseudo first order reaction','Zero order reaction','NEW','12-112','12-078',NULL),
(180622,'chemistry','Inversion of cane sugar is example of','Pseudo first order reaction','(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 112; Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Integrated rate equations',NULL,'1','mcq','First order reaction','Second order reaction','Pseudo first order reaction','Zero order reaction','NEW','12-112','12-078',NULL),
(180623,'chemistry','A first order reaction has a rate constant 1.15 × 10⁻³s⁻¹ . How long will 5 g of\nthis reactant take to reduce to 3 g?','444s','t = 2.303/k log [R₀]/[R] = 2.303 / 1.15× 10⁻³ log (5/3). = 2.00 × 10³ log (1.667) = 2 × 0.2219 = 444s','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Integrated rate equations',NULL,'2','mcq','444s','400s','300s','500s','NEW','12-112','12-078',NULL),
(180624,'chemistry','Select the correct statements among the following:','All of the above','(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 106; Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Integrated rate equations',NULL,'2','mcq','Zero order reactions are relatively uncommon reactions.','Enzyme catalysed reactions are examples of zero order reaction.','Reactions which occur on metal surfaces are examples of zero order reaction.','All of the above','NEW','12-106','12-072',NULL),
(180625,'chemistry','Select the correct statements among the following:','All of the above','(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 112; Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Integrated rate equations',NULL,'3','mcq','Many reactions which obey first order rate law although they are higher order reactions.','The hydrolysis of ethyl acetate is a second order reaction and concentration of both ethyl acetate and water affect the rate of the reaction.','When water is taken in large excess for hydrolysis of ethyl acetate, therefore, concentration of water is not altered much during the reaction.','All of the above','NEW','12-112','12-078',NULL),
(180626,'chemistry','The rate constant of the reaction A→Product is 0.6×10⁻³ mole L⁻¹s⁻¹ If the concentration\nof A is 5 M, then the concentration of product after 20 minutes will be:','0.72 M','Solution: The rate constant has a unit mole per second which indicates zero order reaction.\nx = kt, So we can use the formula\n[B]=[A]0​(1−e^−kt)\nWhere:\n\n[A]0​ is the initial concentration of A\nk is the rate constant of the reaction\nt is the time elapsed\n[B] is concentration of product\nSubstituting the given values, we get:\n[B]=5×(1−e^−0.6×10−3×20×60)≈0.72M\nTherefore, the concentration of product after 20 minutes is approximately 0.72 M','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Integrated rate equations',NULL,'3','mcq','1.38 M','0.92 M','0.72 M','0.23 M','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(180714,'chemistry','Assertion: Action of the catalyst can be explained by intermediate complex theory.                                                                                    Reason: A catalyst participates in a chemical reaction by forming temporary bonds with the reactants resulting in an intermediate complex has transitory existence and decomposes to yield products and the catalyst.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','The action of the catalyst can be explained by intermediate complex theory. According to this theory, a catalyst participates in a chemical reaction by forming temporary bonds with the reactants resulting in an intermediate complex. This has a transistor existence and decomposes to yield products and the catalyst.(Reference NCERT Class 12; Page 116; Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Temperature dependence of the rate of a reaction',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-116','12-082',NULL),
(180715,'chemistry','Assertion: Catalyst catalyses the spontaneous reactions but does not catalyse non - spontaneous reaction.                                                          Reason: A catalyst does not alter Gibbs energy, ∆G of a reaction.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','A Catalyst catalyses the spontaneous reactions but does not catalyse non - spontaneous reactions as it does not alter Gibbs energy.(Reference NCERT Class 12; Page 116; Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Temperature dependence of the rate of a reaction',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-116','12-082',NULL),
(180716,'chemistry','Assertion: A catalyst does not change the equilibrium constant of reaction.                                                                                                Reason: A catalyst catalyses the forward as well as the backward reactions to the same extent.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','A catalyst does not change the equilibrium constant of the reaction because it catalyses the forward as well as backward reactions to the same extend so that the equilibrium state remains same but is reached earlier.( Reference NCERT Class 12; Page 116; Paragraph 4).','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Temperature dependence of the rate of a reaction',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-116','12-082',NULL),
(180717,'chemistry','Who used statistics to predict the behaviour of large number of molecules.','Both a and b','(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 113; Paragraph 5)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Temperature dependence of the rate of a reaction',NULL,'2','mcq','Ludwig Boltzmann','James Clark Maxwell','William Lewis','Both a and b','NEW','12-113','12-079',NULL),
(180718,'chemistry','Select the correct statements among the following about energy distribution curve:','All of the above','(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 113; paragraph 5; page 114; paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Temperature dependence of the rate of a reaction',NULL,'3','mcq','The peak of the distribution curve showing energies among gaseous molecules corresponds to most probable kinetic energy.','When the temperature is raised, the maximum of the curve moves to the higher energy value.','The area under the curve must be constant since total probability must be one at all times.','All of the above','NEW','12-113','12-079',NULL),
(181024,'chemistry','2A → B + C, It would be a zero order reaction when','The rate of reaction remains same at any concentration of A','It Would be Zero Order Reaction, when the Rate is independent of the Reactant Concentration.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','The rate of reaction is proportional to square of concentration of A','The rate of reaction remains same at any concentration of A','The rate remains unchanged at any concentration of B and C','The rate of reaction doubles if concentration of B is increased to double','NEW','12-105','12-071',NULL),
(181025,'chemistry','For the reaction; 2N₂O₅ → 4NO₂ + O₂ rate and rate constant are 1.02 × 10⁻⁴molL⁻¹s⁻¹ and 3.4 × 10⁻⁵ sec⁻¹ respectively, then concentration of N₂O₅ at that time will be','3','Rate of the reaction is given by,\n\nRate = k[N₂O₅]                                                                                                                                                  \n [N₂O₅] = Rate/k = 1.02 × 10⁻⁴/3.4 × 10⁻⁵ sec⁻¹ = 3 molL⁻¹','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1.732','3','1.02 × 10⁻⁴','3.4 × 10⁵','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(181324,'chemistry','For a complex reaction\n(a) order of overall reaction is same as molecularity of the slowest step\n(b) order of overall reaction is less than the molecularity of the slowest step\n(c) order of overall reaction is greater than molecularity of the slowest step\n(d) molecularity of the slowest step is never zero or non-integer .','a,d','(a) For a complex reaction, order of overall reaction = molecularity of slowest step.\nAs rate of overall reaction depends upon total number of molecules involved in slowest step of the reaction. Hence, molecularity of the slowest step is equal to order of overall reaction.\n(b) Since the completion of any chemical reaction is not possible in the absence of reactants. Hence, slowest step of any chemical reaction must\ncontain at least one reactant. Thus, molecularity’of the slowest step is never zero or non-integer.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,d','a,b','b,c','b,d','NEW','12-104','12-070',NULL),
(181026,'chemistry','The experimental data for the reaction,\n2A + B₂ → 2AB is ..... The rate equation for the above data is.....  Refer diagram(n1816)','rate = k [B₂]','From an examination of above data, it is clear that when the concentration of B₂ is doubled, the rate is doubled. Hence, the order of reaction with respect to B₂ is one.\nFurther, when concentration of A is doubled, the rate remains unaltered. So, order of reaction with respect to A is zero.\nThe probable rate law for the reaction will be                                                                                                                    \n -dx/dt = k[B₂][A]⁰ = k[B₂]                                                                                                                                                \n Alternatively, Rate =k[B₂]α                                                                                                                                                           \n 1.6x10⁻⁴ = k[0.5]α                                                                                                                                                                        \n 3.2x10⁻⁴ = k[1]α                                                                                                                                                        \n On dividing we get, α=1                                                                                                                                           \n Rate = k[A]⁰[B₂]¹ = k[B₂]¹','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','rate = k [A]²[B]²','rate = k [A]²[B]','rate = k [B₂]','rate = k [B₂]²','NEW','12-101','12-067','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181026q.png'),
(181027,'chemistry','The given reaction,\n2FeCl₃ + SnCl₂ → 2FeCl₂ + SnCl₄ is an example of','Third order reaction','For the third order reaction, the general reaction can be written as,\nA+B+C → Products                                                                                                                                                   \n r = k[A]²[B]¹                                                                                                                                                                             \n r = k[FeCl₃]²[SnCl₂]','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Third order reaction','First order reaction','Second order reaction','None of these','NEW','12-102','12-068',NULL),
(181028,'chemistry','The data for the reaction A + B → C,                                                                                                               \n The rate law corresponds to the above data is..... diagram(n1817)','rate = k[B]³','In Exp 1 and 3 (Keeping the Conc of [B] same), when the conc of [A] is doubled, there was no change in the rate.So, the Order wrt [A] is \'0\'.\n\nIn Exp 2 and 4 (Keeping the Conc of [A] same), when the conc of [B] is doubled, the rate was increased 8 times. So, the Order wrt [B] is \'3\'                              \n r=k[B]³','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','rate = k[A][B]³','rate = k[A]²[B]²','rate = k[B]³','rate = k[B]⁴','NEW','12-101','12-067','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181028q.png'),
(181029,'chemistry','The rate constant for a first order reaction is4.606 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹. The time required to reduce 2.0 g of the reactant to 0.2 g is','500sec','t = 2.303/k log [R₀]/[R] = 2.303 / 4.606 × 10⁻³ log (2/0.2). = 500sec','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','100sec','200sec','500sec','1000sec','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181030,'chemistry','If the rate constant for a first order reaction is k, the time (t) required for the completion of 99% of the reaction is given by','t = 4.606/k','First order rate constant is given as,\nk= 2.303/t log[A]₀/[A]                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           99% completed reaction,\nk= 2.303/t log100/1 = 2.303/t log10²                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       = 2.303/t 2log10                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           =2 x 2.303/t = 4.606/t                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 t = 4.606/k     ​','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','t = 4.606/k','t = 2.303/k','t = 0.693/k','t = 6.909/k','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181031,'chemistry','A first order reaction has a rate constant of 2.303 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹. The time required for 40 g of this reactant to reduce to 10 g will be [Given that log₁₀2 = 0.3010]','602s','For a first order reaction; t ₁/₂= 0.693/k\n​ t ₁/₂= 0.693/2.303 × 10⁻³ = 301s                                          \n The time required for 40g of reactant to reduce to 10g                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  t₇₅% = 2 x t ₁/₂ = 2 x 301 = 602s','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','301s','602s','2000s','230.3s','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181032,'chemistry','The correct difference between first and second order reactions is that','The half-life of a first-order reaction does not depend on reactant concentrations; the half-life of a second-order reaction does depend on reactant concentrations','For first order reaction, t₁/₂ = 0.693/k\nwhich is independent of initial concentration of reactant                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               \nFor second order reaction, t₁/₂ = 1/[A₀]k','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','The rate of a first-order reaction does not depend on reactant concentrations; the rate of a second-order reaction does depend on reactant concentrations','The half-life of a first-order reaction does not depend on reactant concentrations; the half-life of a second-order reaction does depend on reactant concentrations','A first-order reaction can be catalysed; a second order reaction cannot be catalysed','The rate of a first-order reaction does depend on reactant concentrations; the rate of a second order reaction does not depend on reactant concentrations','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181033,'chemistry','When initial concentration of the reactant is doubled, the half-life period of a zero order reaction','Is doubled','When initial concentration of the reactant is doubled, the half-life period of a zero order reaction is doubled.\nThe half-life period of a zero order reaction is given by the expression                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   t₁/₂ = [A₀]/2k                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      The initial concentration of the reactant is doubled.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       [A₀]\' = 2[A₀]...(i)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         t₁/₂\'= [A₀]\'/2k....(ii)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Substitute equation (1) in equation (2)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      t₁/₂\'= 2[A₀]/2k                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    t₁/₂\'= 2t₁/₂                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           The half-life period of a zero order reaction is doubled.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Is halved','Is doubled','Is tripled','Remains unchanged.','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181034,'chemistry','A first order reaction has a specific reaction rate of 10⁻² sec⁻¹. How much time will it take for 20 g of the reactant to reduce to 5 g','138.6 sec','t = 2.303/k log(a/a-x)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         t = 2.303/10⁻² log(20/5)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      t=138.6 sec\nHence, the time required will be 138.6 sec.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','138.6 sec','346.5 sec','693.0 sec','238.6 sec','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181035,'chemistry','The rate of first-order reaction is 0.04 mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹ at 10 seconds and 0.03 mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹ at 20 seconds after initiation of the reaction. The half-life period of the reaction is','24.1s','K = 2.303/t log([A]₀/[A])                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  K = 2.303/10 log(0.04/0.03)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   K = 2.303/10 log(4/3)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           K = 2.303 x 0.123/10                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          K=0.0285 t₁/₂ = 0.693/K                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        t₁/₂ = 0.693/0.0285 = 24.1s','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','44.1s','54.1s','24.1s','34.1s','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181036,'chemistry','When initial concentration of a reactant is doubled in a reaction, its half-life period is not affected. The order of the reaction is','First','When initial concentration of a reactant is doubled in a reaction, its half-life period is not affected. The order of the reaction is 1. The half-life period t₁/₂  of the first-order reaction is independent of the initial concentration of the reactant.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Second','More than zero but less than first','Zero','First','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181037,'chemistry','A reaction is 50% complete in 2 hours and 75% complete in 4 hours. The order of reaction is','1','The reaction goes to 50% completion in first 2 hours. If the initial concentration is A, it becomes A/2 in 2 hours. \nThen, in the next 2 hours, it comes A/4 from A/2. This means it again reduced by half in 2 hours. \nThis means the half-life of the reaction is 2 hours which is constant. Half-life is constant in the first order reaction. Hence, the order of the reaction is 1.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1','0','2','3','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181038,'chemistry','The half-life of a substance in a certain enzyme catalysed reaction is 138 s. The time required for the concentration of the substance to fall from 1.28 mg L⁻¹ to 0.04 mg L⁻¹ is','690s','Enzyme catalysed reactions are initially follow first order kinetics when concentration decreases 1.28 mgL ⁻¹ to 0.04 mgL⁻¹.                                                                                                                           1.28 mgL⁻¹/  0.04 mgL⁻¹ = 32 = 2⁵                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      Then five half life completed.\nNumber of half lives =5\n\nSo, times required =5×138=690s\n\nThe time required for the concentration of the substance to fall from 1.28 mgL⁻¹ to 0.04 mgL⁻¹.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','414s','552s','690s','276s','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181039,'chemistry','Half-life period of a first order reaction is 1386 seconds. The specific rate constant of the reaction is','0.5 x 10⁻³s⁻¹','Half-life of a first-order reaction = 0.693/K                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    where,\n K is the rate constant or the specific rate constant.\n\nGiven half-life = 1386 sec.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         0.693/K = 1386                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  K = 0.693/1386 = 0.5 x 10⁻³s⁻¹','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','5 x 10⁻²s⁻¹','5 x 10⁻³s⁻¹','0.5 x 10⁻²s⁻¹','0.5 x 10⁻³s⁻¹','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181040,'chemistry','If 60% of a first order reaction was completed in 60 minutes, 50% of the same reaction would be completed in approximately (log 4 = 0.60, log 5 = 0.69)','45 minutes','Rate of a general 1ˢᵗ order reaction.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       2.303log[A]ₜ/[A]₀ = -kt                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           \n​Given : 60% of reaction completes in 60 minutes.\n 2.303log 0.4[A]₀/[A]₀ = -60k                   \n 2.303log0.4=−60k​\nk = -2.303log0.4/60  \nUsing the value of k                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        2.303log 0.5[A]₀/[A]₀ = -xk                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          2.303log0.5=−x(-2.303log0.4/60)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                60log0.5/log0.4 = x                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     x = 60(log5−1)/(log4 -1)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    x = 60(0.69 − 1)/(0.60 - 1)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  x = 60×0.31/0.4                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    x = 45min\n​','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','45 minutes','60 minutes','40 minutes','50 minutes','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181041,'chemistry','In a first-order reaction, A → B, if k is rate constant and initial concentration of the reactant A is 0.5 M, then the half-life is','ln2/k','For a first-order reaction,\nA→B\n\nRate = k[A]\n\nInitial concentration=0.5MWe know that the rate constant of a first order reaction has only time unit. It has no concentration unit, which means that numerical value of k for a first order reaction is independent of concentration unit.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          t₁/₂ = ln2/k = 0.693/k = 2.303/klog2','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','log2/k','log2/k√0.5','ln2/k','0.693/0.5k','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181042,'chemistry','For a first order reaction A → B the reaction rate at reactant concentration of 0.01 M is found to be 2.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹. The half-life period of the reaction is','347s','For first order reaction,\nA⟶B\nRate =k×[A]\n\nGiven, rate =2.0×10⁻⁵ mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     [A] = Conc. of A = 0.01 M                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               So, 2.0×10⁻⁵ = k × 0.01                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           k = 2.0×10⁻⁵/0.01 s⁻¹                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    k = 2.0 x 10⁻³ s⁻¹                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            For first order reaction,                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          t₁/₂ = 0.693/k = 0.693/2.0 x 10⁻³ = 346.5 = 347s','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','30s','300s','347s','220s','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181043,'chemistry','The rate of a first order reaction is 1.5 × 10⁻² mol L⁻¹ min⁻¹ at 0.5 M concentration of the reactant. The half-life of the reaction is','23.1 min','For the first-order reaction,                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             dx/dt = k[A] & t₁/₂ = 0.693/k                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Given:                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  dx/dt = 1.5 × 10⁻²                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 [A]=0.5M\n\nk=3×10⁻²                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       t₁/₂ = 0.693/3 x 10⁻² = 23.1min','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','23.1 min','0.383 min','8.73 min','7.53 min','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181044,'chemistry','The reaction A ⟶ B follows first order kinetics. The time taken for 0.8 mole of A to produce 0.6 mole of B is 1 hour. What is the time taken for conversion of 0.9 mole of A to produce 0.675 mole of B ?','1h','k= 2.303/t log[A]₀/[A]                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               k= 2.303/1 log[0.8]/[0.2] .....(i)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              Remaining moles of A=0.9−0.675\n=0.225                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                k= 2.303/t₁ log[0.9]/[0.225].....(ii)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       From eq. (i) and (ii)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           2.303/1 log[0.8]/[0.2] = 2.303/t₁ log[0.9]/[0.225]                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                t₁ = 1h','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1h','0.5h','2h','0.25h','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181045,'chemistry','For a first-order reaction, the half-life period is independent of','Initial concentration','For a first-order reaction, the half-life is independent of initial concentrations of reactants.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Initial concentration','First power of final concentration','Cube root of initial concentration','Square root of final concentration.','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181046,'chemistry','For a reaction, activation energy Ea = 0 and the rate constant at 200 K is 1.6 × 10⁶ s⁻¹. The rate constant at 400 K will be\n[Given that gas constant R = 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹]','1.6 × 10⁶ s⁻¹','log k₂/k₁ = Ea/2.303R[T₂ - T₁ /T₁T₂].                                                                                                                                                                                                   \n Ea = 0                                                                                                                                                            \n log k₂/k₁ = 0                                                                                                                                                  \n k₂/k₁ = 10⁰ = 1                                                                                                                                                \n k₁ = k₂                                                                                                                                                      \n k₂ = 1.6 × 10⁶ s⁻¹ at 400K','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','3.2 × 10⁴ s⁻¹','1.6 × 10⁶ s⁻¹','1.6 × 10³ s⁻¹','3.2 × 10⁶ s⁻¹','NEW','12-112','12-078',NULL),
(181047,'chemistry','The addition of a catalyst during a chemical reaction alters which of the following quantities?','Activation energy','The addition of a catalyst during a chemical reaction alters activation energy. The catalyst provides an alternate pathway of lower activation energy.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Enthalpy','Activation energy','Entropy','Internal energy','NEW','12-113','12-079',NULL),
(181048,'chemistry','The activation energy of a reaction can be determined from the slope of which of the following graphs?','lnK vs 1/T','The Arrhenius equation is lnk = lnA-Ea/RT                                                                                                                                \n Thus, the activation energy of a reaction can be determined from the slope of which of the graph lnK vs 1/T                                                           \n Slope = -Ea/R                                                                                                                                                          \n Hence, Ea = −slope×R','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','lnK vs 1/T','T/lnk vs 1/T','lnK vs T','lnK/T vs T','NEW','12-113','12-079',NULL),
(181049,'chemistry','What is the activation energy for a reaction if its rate doubles when the temperature is raised from 20 °C to 35 °C?','34.7 kJ mol–1','log(k/k₀) = Ea/2.303R(1/T₀-1/T)                                                                                                                                       \n Substituting the values                                                                                                                                                \n log(2) = Ea/2.303x8.314(1/293-1/308)                                                                                                                                      \n Ea = 34.7 kJ mol⁻¹','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','34.7 kJ mol–1','15.1 kJ mol–1','342 kJ mol–1','269 kJ mol–1','NEW','12-113','12-079',NULL),
(181050,'chemistry','In a zero-order reaction, for every 10 °C rise of temperature, the rate is doubled. If the temperature is increased from 10 °C to 100 °C, the rate of the reaction will become','512 times','For rise in temperature, n=1                                                                                                                                           \n Therefore rate =2ⁿ=2¹                                                                                                                                                  \n When temperature is increased from 10⁰C to 100⁰C change in temperature =100−10=90⁰C therefore n=9 therefore, rate =2⁹=512 times','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','256 times','512 times','64 times','128 times','NEW','12-113','12-079',NULL),
(181051,'chemistry','Activation energy (Ea) and rate constants (k₁ and k₂) of a chemical reaction at two different temperatures (T₁ and T₂) are related by','ln(k₂/k₁) = -Ea/R(1/T₂-1/T₁)','ln(k₂/k₁) = -Ea/R(1/T₂-1/T₁)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','ln(k₂/k₁) = -Ea/R(1/T₂-1/T₁)','ln(k₂/k₁) = Ea/R(1/T₂-1/T₁)','ln(k₂/k₁) = -Ea/R(1/T₂+1/T₁)','ln(k₂/k₁) = Ea/R(1/T₂-1/T₁)','NEW','12-113','12-079',NULL),
(181052,'chemistry','The rate of the reaction, 2NO + Cl₂→ 2NOCl is given by the rate equation, rate = k[NO]²[Cl₂]. The value of the rate constant can be increased by','Increasing the temperature','Rate constant is only affected by the temperature, it does not get affected by the increase in concentration of NO and Cl₂.Hence the answer is increasing the temperature','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Increasing the temperature','Increasing the concentration of NO','Increasing the concentration of the Cl₂','Doing all of these','NEW','12-113','12-079',NULL),
(181053,'chemistry','The rate constants k₁ and k₂ for two different reactions are 10¹⁶·e–²⁰⁰⁰/ᵀ and 10¹⁵ · e–¹⁰⁰⁰/ᵀ, respectively. The temperature at which k₁ = k₂ is','1000K/2.303','k₁ = 10¹⁶·e–²⁰⁰⁰/ᵀ                                                                                                                                                     \n k₂ = 10¹⁵ · e–¹⁰⁰⁰/ᵀ                                                                                                                                              \n By taking log on both of them                                                                                                                                           \n logk₁ = log10¹⁶ - 2000t/2.303                                                                                                                                          \n logk₂ = log10¹⁵ - 1000k/2.303                                                                                                                                    \n Now, k₁ = k₂, Hence from equations T = 1000t/2.303','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','2000 K','1000 K','1000K/2.303','2000K/2.303','NEW','12-114','12-080',NULL),
(181054,'chemistry','The temperature dependence of rate constant (k) of a chemical reaction is written in terms of Arrhenius equation, k = A×e–ᴱ*/ᴿᵀ. Activation energy (E*) of the reaction can be calculated by plotting','logk vs 1/T','k=Ae⁻ᴱᵃ/ᴿᵀ                                                                                                                                                        \n logk = logA-Ea/RT                                                                                                                                            \nby plotting\nlog k vs 1/T                                                                                                                                                      \n its slope will give Ea/R by that we can calculate Ea','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','k vs T','k vs 1/logT','logk vs 1/T','logk vs 1/logT','NEW','12-114','12-080',NULL),
(181055,'chemistry','The activation energy for a simple chemical reaction A ⇌ B is Ea in forward direction. The activation energy for reverse reaction','Can be less than or more than Ea','As we know,\nΔH = Ea₍𝒻₎ − Ea₍ᵦ₎;  if ΔH=−ve,Ea₍ᵦ₎ > Ea₍𝒻₎                                                                                                                             \n if ΔH=+ve, Ea₍ᵦ₎ < Ea₍𝒻₎','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Is negative of Ea','Is always less than Ea','Can be less than or more than Ea','Is always double of Ea','NEW','12-114','12-080',NULL),
(181056,'chemistry','When a biochemical reaction is carried out in laboratory, outside the human body in absence of enzyme, then rate of reaction obtained is 10⁻⁶ times, the activation energy of reaction in the presence of enzyme is','Different from Ea obtained in laboratory','When a biochemical reaction is carried out in the laboratory in the absence of an enzyme, the rate of the reaction obtained is much slower than the rate of the same reaction in the presence of an enzyme. This is because enzymes lower the activation energy required for the reaction to occur, which speeds up the reaction. Thus, it can be concluded that the activation energy of the reaction in the presence of the enzyme would be different from the activation energy obtained in the laboratory in the absence of the enzyme','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','6/RT','P is required','Different from Ea obtained in laboratory','Can’t say anything','NEW','12-114','12-080',NULL),
(181057,'chemistry','How enzymes increases the rate of reactions?','By lowering activation energy','By lowering activation energy','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','By lowering activation energy','By increasing activation energy','By changing equilibrium constant','By forming enzyme substrate complex','NEW','12-114','12-080',NULL),
(181058,'chemistry','Activation energy of a chemical reaction can be determined by','Evaluating rate constants at two different temperatures','Activation energy of a chemical reaction can be determined by evaluating rate constants at two different temperature','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Evaluating rate constants at two different temperatures','Evaluating velocities of reaction at two different temperatures','Evaluating rate constant at standard temperature','Changing concentration of reactants','NEW','12-114','12-080',NULL),
(181059,'chemistry','By the action of enzymes, the rate of biochemical reaction','Either (a) or (c).','Enzymes are the very efficient catalyst for the enzymatic biochemical reaction and Enzymes speed up the reaction by providing lower activation energy.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Does not change','Increases','Decreases','Either (a) or (c).','NEW','12-115','12-081',NULL),
(181060,'chemistry','An increase in the concentration of the reactants of a reaction leads to change in','Collision frequency.','The number of collisions that takes place per second per unit volume of the reaction mixture is known as collision frequency (Z)\n\nAs the concentration of reactants increases the number of reactant molecules per unit volume increases which increases the collision frequency.\n\nZ∝ number of reactant molecules per unit volume(Reference:  NCERT 12th ; Page 107 ; Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Activation energy','Heat of reaction','Threshold energy','Collision frequency.','NEW','12-116','12-082',NULL),
(181061,'chemistry','For a chemical reaction 4A+3B−>6C+9D Rate of formation of C is 6×10 −2 mol L −1 s −1 and rate of disappearance of A is 4×10 −2 molL −1 s −1 . The rate of reaction and amount of B consumed in interval of 10 seconds, respectively will be:','1×10 −2 mol L −1 s −1 and 30×10 −2 mol L −1','Hint: − 1 4 d [ A ] d t = − 1 3 d [ B ] d t = 1 6 d [ C ] d t = 1 9 d [ D ] d t Step 1: The rate of reaction for 4A + 3B → 6C + 9D is as follows: − 1 4 d [ A ] d t = − 1 3 d [ B ] d t = 1 6 d [ C ] d t = 1 9 d [ D ] d t The given value are as follows: rate of formation of C( d [ C ] d t ) = 6 × 10–2 mol L–1 s–1 rate of disappearance of A( − d [ A ] d t ) =  4 × 10–2 mol L–1 s–1 Step 2: Calculate the rate of reaction as follows: Rate of reaction = 1 6 d [ C ] d t = 6 × 10 − 2 6 Rate of reaction = 1 × 10–2 mol L–1 s–1 Calculate the amount of B consumed in interval of 10 seconds as follows: Rate of reaction = − 1 3 d [ B ] d t 30 × 10–2 mol L–1 s–1 = − d [ B ] d t Hence, option one is the correct answer.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1×10 −2 mol L −1 s −1 and 30×10 −2 mol L −1','10×10 −2 mol L −1 s −1 and 10×10 −2 mol L −1','1×10 −2 mol L −1 s −1 and 10×10 −2 mol L −1','10×10−2 mol L−1 s−1 and 30×10−2 mol L−1','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(2000110659,'physics','In a meter bridge experiment, the balance point from left is  $37.5\\,cm.$  The ratio of the right gap resistance to the left gap resistance is','A','Position of balanced point from left,<br>${l_1} = 37.5\\,cm$<br>As we know that in balanced meter bridge,<br>$\\dfrac{l}{100-l}=\\dfrac{R_{1}}{R_{2}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\dfrac{37.5}{62.5}=\\dfrac{R_{1}}{R_{2}}=0.6=\\dfrac{6}{10}$<br>or $\\quad R_{2} / R_{1}=\\dfrac{10}{6}=\\dfrac{5}{3}$','Electrical Instruments','Current Electricity','Easy',NULL,'$5 / 3$','$8 / 5$','$4 / 5$','$3 / 2$',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(181062,'chemistry','The rate of the reaction, 2NO + Cl2 → 2NOCl is given by the rate equation, Rate = k[NO]2[Cl2] . The value of the rate constant can be increased by','Increase the temprature','HINT: Rate constant is only temperature dependent. Explanation: The rate constant may also be expressed using the Arrhenius Equation: k = Ae-Ea/RT It depends only on the temperature and not on the concentration of reactants and products. By increasing temperatue rate constant will also increase.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Increasing the concerntration of NO','Increase the concerntration of Cl2','Increase the temprature','All of the above','NEW','12-112','12-078',NULL),
(181063,'chemistry','The incorrect order regarding the order of reaction is:','The order is always a whole number','HINT: Order of reaction calculate experimentally. Explanation: If the rate of a reaction is given by k[A]x[B]y then the order of the reaction is x+y. This order can be a positive, negative number , zero, or even fractional. It can be determined only experimentally and does not depend on the stoichiometric coefficients of reactants. Hence, option 3rd is the answer','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Order is not influenced by stoichiometric coefficient of the reactants','Order of the reaction is the sum of power to the concerntration terms of reactants to express the rate of reaction','The order is always a whole number','Order can be determined by experiments only','NEW','12-102','12-068',NULL),
(181064,'chemistry','For the Reaction 2A + B → C, rates=K[A]2.If the concerntration of the reactant is doubled refer to diagram(181064Q):','b and d only','Hint: With respect to A, the order is second and the rate increases four-time when concentration became double The rate law of the reaction is as follows: R = k[A]2 The order of reaction with respect to A is 2. If the concentration of reactant is doubled then the rate increases four times. R\' = k[2A]2 = 4R Hence, statement one is incorrect. The fourth statement is correct. The rate of reaction increases four times. The rate constant will remain unchanged, however rate of reaction is directly proportional to the rate constant because the rate constant only depends on temperature and does not depends on the concentration of reactant. Hence, statement two is correct. The third statement is incorrect because the rate constant will not change. Thus, option third is the correct answer.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a and c only','a and b only','b and d only','c and d only','NEW','12-102','12-068','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181064q.png'),
(181065,'chemistry','If at a given instant, for the reaction 2N₂O₅ → 4NO₂ + O₂ rate and rate constant are 1.02x10-4 and 3.4x10-5 per sec respectively , then the concerntration of N2O5 at that time will be:','3.0 mol/ltr','HINT: The order of the reaction is 1 Explanation: Step 1: The rate of the reaction for the given reaction is as follows: R = k [ N 2 O 5 ] The reaction is first order. Step 2: R = k [ N 2 O 5 ] [ N 2 O 5 ] = R k [ N 2 O 5 ] = 1 . 02 × 10 − 4 3 . 4 × 10 − 5 [ N 2 O 5 ] = 3 M','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1.732 mol/ltr','3.0 mol/ltr','1.02x10-4 mol/ltr','3.4x105 mol/ltr','NEW','12-099','12-065',NULL),
(181066,'chemistry','If the concerntration of a solution is changed from 0.2 to 0.4, then what will be rate and rate constant.The reaction is of first order and rate consatnt is K=1x10-6:','4x10-7 ; 1x10-6','Refer to Ncert First order kinetics section','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','2x10-7 ; 1x10-6','1x10-7 ; 1x10+6','4x10-7 ; 1x10-6','2x10-3 ; 1x10-3','NEW','12-106','12-072',NULL),
(181067,'chemistry','For a first order reaction A →product, initial concerntration of A is 0.1 , which becomes 0.001 after 5 minutes .Rate constant for the reaction in per min is:','0.9212','Hint: k = 2.303 t l o g [ A o ] [ A ] The formula of first-order reaction is as follows: k = 2.303 t l o g [ A o ] [ A ] Calculate the value of the rate constant as follows: k = 2.303 5 l o g 0.1 0.001 k = 2.303 5 × 2 × l o g 10 = 0.92 min-1','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.2303','1.3818','0.9212','0.4606','NEW','12-107','12-073',NULL),
(181068,'chemistry','The correct statement among the following in diagram(181068Q):','2','For first order, t1/2 is independent of initial concentration For second order T ­1/2 = 1/K a = 1/K[A]o For second order reaction, t1/2 depends on initial concentration [A]o.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-111','12-077','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181068q.png'),
(181069,'chemistry','When the initial concerntration of of a reactant is doubled in a reaction , it\'s half-life period is not affected .The order of reaction will be:','1','Explanation: For a first order reaction, t1/2 = 0 . 693 k As half life period is independent of initial concentration of a reactant, it is not affected by doubling the initial concentration.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','0','1','1.5','2','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181070,'chemistry','The radioisotope , tritium has a half life of 12.3 years. If the initial amount of tritium is 32mg , how many miligram of it  would remain after 49.2 years?','2mg','Hint: The reaction follows a first-order reaction Step 1: Calculate the value of the rate constant is as follows: For first-order reaction, T 1 2 is 0 . 693 k . T 1 / 2 = 0 . 693 k k = 0 . 693 12 . 3 k = 0 . 056 year − 1 Step 2: Calculate the amount of tritium after 49.2 years is as follows: k = 1 t ln [ A o ] [ A ] 0 . 056 = 1 49 . 2 ln [ 32 ] [ A ] 49 . 2 × 0 . 056 = ln [ 32 ] [ A ] 2 . 8 = ln [ 32 ] [ A ] e 2 . 8 = [ 32 ] [ A ] [ A ] = 32 16 = 2 mg Hence, the answer is option 2.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1mg','2mg','4mg','8mg','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181071,'chemistry','If the half-life of a substance is 77days and then it\'s decay constant (1/days) will be:','0.009','hint: Use t1/2 = 0.693 k Explanation: STEP 1:  Radioactive decays follow first order kinetics. t 1 / 2 = 0.693 k STEP 2: Given, half life = 77 days k = 0.693 77days = 0.009 days−1','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.9','0.09','0.009','0.013','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181072,'chemistry','The half-life for a zero-order reaction having 0.02M initial concretration of reactant in 100 sec.The rate constant (in mol/ltr*sec) for the reaction is:','1x10-4','Hint - For zero order reaction, half life is directly proportional to the initial reaction. ( t 1 / 2 ) z e r o = [ A ] 0 2 K 100 s = 0 . 02 M 2 K K = 0 . 02 M 2 × 100 = 1 × 10 − 4 m o l L − 1 s − 1','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1x10-4','2x10-4','2x10-3','1x10-2','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181073,'chemistry','When the initial concerntration of of a reactant is doubled, the half-life period for a zero-order reaction is:','is doubled','In zero order reaction t1/2 is directly proportional to concerntration','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','is halved','is doubled','is tripled','remains unchanged','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181074,'chemistry','Half-life of a radioactive sample is 4 days.After 16 days what quantity of matter remain unchanged:','1/16','Refer to ncert half life section','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1/4','1/8','1/16','1/32','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181075,'chemistry','For a reaction A →B ,enthalpy of reaction is -4.2KJ/mol and enthalpy of activation is +9.6KJ/mol. The correct potential energy profile for the reaction is:','4','Hint: Reaction is exothermic The enthalpy of the reaction is − 4 . 2 kJ mol − 1 . It indicates that energy is released during the reaction and the reaction is exothermic. For an exothermic reaction, the energy of the reactant is higher than the energy of the product. Hence, option 4th is the correct answer.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-113','12-079',NULL),
(181076,'chemistry','For an endothermic reaction , the energy of activation is Ea and the enthalpy of reaction is ΔH(both of these in KJ/mol).The minimum value of Ea will be:','More than ΔH','HINT: The energy of reactants is less than that of the products in an endothermic reaction. Explanation: For an endothermic reaction, the potential energy diagram is given by Where Ea = activation energy of forwarding reaction Ea = activation energy of backward reaction Δ H = enthalpy of the reaction And from the above diagram, it is clear that E a = E a + Δ H Thus, E a > Δ H','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Less than ΔH','Equal to ΔH','More than ΔH','Equal to zero','NEW','12-116','12-082',NULL),
(181077,'chemistry','The plot of lnK vs 1/T for the following reaction 2N₂O₅ → 4NO₂ + O₂ gives a straight line with the slope of line equal to -1x10+4K.The activation energy for the reaction in J/mol is:(Given R=8.3J/K*mol)','8.3x10+4','Hint: l n K = l n A − E a R T Step 1: The Arrhenius equation is as follows: l n K = l n A − E a R T The slope is equal to − E a R . Step 2: Calculate the value of Ea value is as follows: − E a R = − 1.0 × 10 4 K Ea = 1.0 × 10 4 × 8.3 = 8.3 × 10 4 J m o l − 1','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','4.0x10+2','4.0x10-2','8.3x10-4','8.3x10+4','NEW','12-113','12-079',NULL),
(181078,'chemistry','The incorrect among the following in the diagram(181078Q) is:','1','Both the forward and the backward reactions can be speeded up with the same rate in the presence of a catalyst. A catalyst will decrease the activation energies of the forward and the backward reactions to the same extent. Therefore, there is no effect on the equilibrium constant in the presence of a catalyst at any given temperature.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-116','12-082','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181078q.png'),
(181079,'chemistry','A reaction having equal energies of activation for forward and reverse reaction has:','ΔH=0','Enthalpy change is the difference between the activation energies of forward and backward reactions. If they are the same then the enthalpy change is zero. ΔH = (Ea)forward – (Ea)backward If (Ea)forward = (Ea)backward, then ΔH=0. Activation energy didn\'t tell about the spontaneity of the reaction and cannot tell about the entropy change in the reaction. The formula of Δ G is as follows: Δ G = Δ H − T Δ S If the value of Δ H zero but it is not compulsory that Δ G and Δ S is also zero. Thus, option third can\'t be the answer.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','ΔG=0','ΔH=0','ΔG=ΔH=ΔS=0','ΔS=0','NEW','12-116','12-082',NULL),
(181080,'chemistry','Given below are two statements:one labelled as Assertion(A) and the other is albelled as Reason(R) in the diagram(181080Q):','2','Hint: The minimum kinetic energy that a molecule must possess in order to bring about an effective collision between two reactant molecules is considered a hard sphere and leads to a chemical reaction. In general, activation energy cannot be zero because at zero activation every collision will give the product. This situation is not possible in most cases. Thus, the assertion is wrong. The minimum extra amount of energy absorbed by reactant molecules so that their energy becomes equal to threshold value, is called activation energy.  It is a correct statement. Note: For some free radical reactions activation energy is zero(not given in NCERT and beyond the scope of students) hence, this question has some controversy. Some educational resources\' answer keys mention that option third is the answer. To obtain the most reliable answer, it is advisable to refer to the official answer key provided by the National Testing Agency (NTA).','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','beyond ncert as per nta',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181080q.png'),
(181081,'chemistry','For a certain reaction , thee rate=K[A]2[B] , when final concerntration of A is tripled  kepping the concerntration of B constant, the initial rate would be:','increase by a factor of nine','Hint: Compare the two rate condition The formula for the rate is as follows: rate(R) = k [ A ] 2 [ B ] The initial concentration of A is tripled keeping the concentration of B constant. The new rate equation is as follows: R\' = k [ 3 A ] 2 [ B ] R\' = 9 k [ A ] 2 [ B ] R\'= 9R The initial rate would be increased by a factor of nine. Thus, option 4th is the correct answer.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','increase by a factor of three','decrease by a factor of nine','increase by a factor of six','increase by a factor of nine','NEW','12-099','12-064',NULL),
(181082,'chemistry','The half-life of a first order reaction is 2000 years. If the concerntration after 8000 yearsis 0.02M , then the initial concerntration is:','0.32M','Hint: T 1 2 = 0.693 k Step 1: First, calculate the number of half-life as follows: Total time = n × half-life time period 8000 = n × 2000 n = 4. n is the number of half-life. Step 2: N = No( 1 2 )n 0.02= No( 1 2 )4 No = 0.32 g Hence, option second is the correct answer.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.16M','0.32M','0.08M','0.04M','NEW','12-110','12-076',NULL),
(181083,'chemistry','A 300-gm radioactive sample has a half-life of 3 hours.After 18 hours remaining quantity will be:','4.68gm','Refer to ncert half life section','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','4.68gm','2.34gm','3.34gm','9.37gm','NEW','12-110','12-076',NULL),
(181084,'chemistry','The slope of Arrhenius plot(lnK vs 1/T) the first order reaction is -5x10+3K. The value of Ea of the reaction is:[Given R=8.314J/mol*K]','41.5 KJ/mol','Hint: lnK = lnA − E a RT Step 1: The Arrhenius equation is as follows: lnK = lnA − E a RT Here, - E a R is the slope. Step 2: The value of the slope is − 5 × 10 3 K . Calculate the value of activation energy is as follows: − 5 × 10 3 K = - E a R 5 × 10 3 K × 8.314 J mol-1 K-1 = Ea Ea = 41570 J mol-1 or 41.57 kJ mol-1','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','166 KJ/mol','-83 KJ/mol','41.5 KJ/mol','83 KJ/mol','NEW','12-113','12-079',NULL),
(181085,'chemistry','The given graph is a representation of the kinetics of a reaction refer to diagram(181085Q):','4','Hint: Rate = k for zero order, and t1/2 = = 0.693 k for first order The given graph is as follows: The rate law for zero order is as follows: Rate = k[A]0 Rate = k Rate does not depend on the concentration of reactant. Hence, zero-order (y=rate and x=concentration) give above graph. The formula of t1/2 for first order reaction is as follows: t1/2 = = 0.693 k t1/2 does not depend on the concentration of reactant. Hence, the graph between t1/2 and concentration is constant. Hence, option fourth is correct.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-111','12-077','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181085q.png'),
(181115,'chemistry','Which of the following is not an example of\n heterogeneous catalytic reaction?\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','Combustion of coal','C + O₂ →  CO₂ No catalyst\nOstwald process :\n 4NH₃ + 5O₂ —Pt/Rh→ 4NO₂+ 6H₂O\nHaber’s process\n N₂ + 3H₂ —Fe→ 2NH₃','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','Combustion of coal','Ostwald’s process','Hydrogenation of vegetable oils','Haber’s process','NEW','12-116','12-082',NULL),
(181116,'chemistry','For an elementary chemical reaction,','2k₁ [A₂] – 2k₋₁ [A]²','Refer diagram(ne1830)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','k₁ [A₂] + k₋₁ [A]²','2k₁ [A₂] – 2k₋₁ [A]²','2k₁ [A₂] – k₋₁ [A]²','k₁ [A₂] – k₋₁ [A]²','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(181117,'chemistry','If a reaction follows the Arrhenius equation, the plot\n Ink vs 1/RT gives straight line with a gradient\n (–y) unit. The energy required to activate the\n reactant is [JEE (Main)-2019]','y unit','K = Ae^-E /RT\nlnK = lnA - Ea/RT\nFor Ink versus 1/RT, slope = -y\n– y = – Ea\nEa = y','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','yR unit','y/R unit','–y unit','y unit','NEW','12-113','12-079',NULL),
(181118,'chemistry','The reaction 2X → B is a zeroth order reaction. If\n the initial concentration of X is 0.2 M, the half-life\n is 6 h. When the initial concentration of X is 0.5\nM, the time required to reach its final\n concentration of 0.2 M will be [JEE (Main)-2019]','18.0 h','For the reaction 2X o B, follow zeroth order\nRate equation is\n2 Kt = [A]₀ – [A]\nFor the half-life\n2kt = [A]₀/2\nk = 0.2 / 2 × 2 × 6 = 1/20 M / hr\n→ Time required to reach from 0.5 M to 0.2 M\n2 Kt = [A]₀ – [A]\nt = (0.5 – 0.2) × 60\n= 18 hour','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','12.0 h','7.2 h','9.0 h','18.0 h','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181119,'chemistry','Decomposition of X exhibits a rate constant of\n 0.05 Pg/year. How many years are required for the\n decomposition of 5 Pg of X into 2.5 Pg? [JEE (Main)-2019]','50','Rate constant of decomposition of X = 0.05 µg/year\n. From unit of rate constant, it is clear that the\n decomposition follows zero order kinetics.\nFor zero order kinetics,\n[X] = [X]₀  – kt\nt = 5 - 2.5 /0.05 = 50 years','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','40','20','50','25','NEW','12-103','12-069',NULL),
(181120,'chemistry','For a reaction, consider the plot of In k versus 1/\n T given in the figure. If the rate constant of this\n reaction at 400 K is 10⁻⁵ /s, then the rate\n constant at 500 K is.... Refer diagram(n1835)','10⁻⁴ /s','lnK= lnA - Ea/RT\nSlope = Ea/R = 4606K\nln(k₂/k₁) = Ea/R ( T₂ - T₁ / T₁T₂) = 4606 × 100 / (400 × 500) = 2.303\n\nlog(k₂/k₁) = 1 \nk₂/k₁ = 10\nk₂ = 10k₁\n = 10⁻⁵ × 10 = 10⁻⁴ /s','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','4 × 10⁻⁴ /s','10⁻⁶ /s','2 × 10⁻⁴ /s','10⁻⁴ /s','NEW','12-113','12-079','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181120q.png'),
(181121,'chemistry','For the reaction 2A + B → C, the values of initial\n rate at different reactant concentrations are given in\n the table below. The rate law for the reaction is...... Refer diagram(n1836)','Rate = k[A][B]²','2A + B → P\nRate = k[A]ˣ [B]ʸ\nExp-1, 0.045 = k[0.05]ˣ [0.05]ʸ …(i)\nExp-2, 0.090 = k[0.1]ˣ\n [0.05]ʸ …(ii)\nExp-3, 0.72 = k[0.2]ˣ [0.1]ʸ…(iii)\nDivide equation (i) by equation (ii)\n0.045/0.090 = (1/2)ˣ → x = 1\nDivide equation (i) by equation (iii)\n0.045/0.72 = (0.05/0.1)ʸ(0.02/0.1)\n(1/2)² = (1/2)ʸ , y = 2\nRate law = k[A]¹ [B]²','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','Rate = k[A]²[B]²','Rate = k[A][B]','Rate = k[A]²[B]','Rate = k[A][B]²','NEW','12-101','12-067','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181121q.png'),
(181122,'chemistry','For a reaction scheme A —k₁→ B —k₂→ C ,if the\n rate of formation of B is set to be zero then the\n concentration of B is given by : [JEE (Main)-2019]','(k₁/k₂)A','A —k₁→ B —k₂→ C\nd[B]/dt = k₁A = k₂B = 0\nB = (k₁/k₂)A','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','k₁k₂[A]','(k₁-k₂)A','(k₁/k₂)A','(k₁+k₂)A','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(181123,'chemistry','The given plots represent the variation of the\n concentration of a reactant R with time for two\n different reactions (i) and (ii). The respective orders\n of the reactions are.... Refer diagram(n1837)','1, 0','Graph-(i) : In[Reactant] vs time is linear\n.Hence, 1st order\nGraph-(ii) : [Reactant] vs time is linear\n.Hence, zero order','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','0, 1','1, 1','1, 0','0, 2','NEW','12-102','12-068','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181123q.png'),
(181124,'chemistry','Consider the given plot of enthalpy of the following\n the reaction between A and B.\nA+B → C+D \nIdentify the incorrect statement. Refer diagram(n1838)','Activation enthalpy to form C is 5 kJ /mol less than that to form D','Activation enthalpy to form C is 5 kJ more than\n that to form D.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','Activation enthalpy to form C is 5 kJ /mol less than that to form D','D is kinetically stable product','Formation of A and B from C has highest\n enthalpy of activation','C is the thermodynamically stable product','NEW','12-113','12-079','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181124q.png'),
(181125,'chemistry','A bacterial infection in an internal wound grows as\n N꜀(t) = N₀exp(t), where the time t is in hours. A\n dose of antibiotic, taken orally, needs 1 hour to\n reach the wound. Once it reaches there, the\n bacterial population goes down as dN\n/dt\n = – 5N².\nWhat will be the plot of N₀\n/N\n vs. t after 1 hour ?\n [JEE (Main)-2019] Refer diagram(n1839)','3','When drug is administered bacterial growth is\n given by dN/dt = -5N²\nN₀/Nₜ = 1 +5tN₀. Thus N₀/Nₜ increases linearly\n with t.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181125q.png'),
(181126,'chemistry','For the reaction of H₂ with I₂, the rate constant\n is 2.5 × 10⁻⁴ dm³ /mol s at 327°C and\n 1.0 dm³ /mol s at 527°C. The activation energy\n for the reaction, in kJ /mol is :\n (R = 8.314 J /K mol) [JEE (Main)-2019]','166','log(k₂/k₁) = (Ea/2.303R)(1/T₁ -1/T₂)\nlog (1/2.5 × 10⁻⁴) = Ea/8.314×2.303 (1/600 - 1/800)\nEa ≈ 166 kJ/mol','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','150','59','72','166','NEW','12-113','12-079',NULL),
(181127,'chemistry','In the following reaction; xA → yB\nlog₁₀[-d(A)/dt ] = log₁₀[d(B)/dt] + 0.3010\n‘A’ and ‘B’ respectively can be [JEE (Main)-2019]','C₂H₄ and C₄H₈','xA →yB\n- dA/xdt = dB/ydt \n-dA/dt = dB/dt(x/y)\nlog[-dA/dt] = log[dB/dt] + log(x/y)\nx/y = 2\nThe reaction is of type 2A → B.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','C₂H₄ and C₄H₈','N₂O₄ and NO₂','n-Butane and Iso-butane','C₂H₂ and C₆H₆','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(181128,'chemistry','NO₂ required for a reaction is produced by the\n decomposition of N₂O₅ in CCI₄ as per the equation,\n2N₂O₅(g) → 4 NO₂(g) + O₂(g).\nThe initial concentration of N₂O₅ is 3.00 mol /L\n and it is 2.75 mol /L after 30 minutes. The rate\n of formation of NO₂ is [JEE (Main)-2019]','1.667 × 10⁻² mol /Lmin','Refer diagram(ne1831)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','1.667 × 10⁻² mol /Lmin','4.167 × 10⁻³ mol /Lmin','8.333 × 10⁻³ mol /L min','2.083 × 10⁻³ mol /Lmin','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(181218,'chemistry','Calculate the half-life of the first order reaction from their rate constants given below: 4 /year','0.173 year','Half life period of a first order reaction is given by t₁/₂ = 0.693/k , hence t₁/₂ = 0.693/4 = 0.173 year','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Exercises',NULL,'1','mcq','0.173 year','173 year','1.73 year','17.3 year','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181219,'chemistry','The half-life for radioactive decay of ¹⁴C is 5730 years. An archaeological artifact containing wood had only 80% of the ¹⁴C found in a living tree. Estimate the age of the sample.','1845 years','Refer diagram(ne1837)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Exercises',NULL,'2','mcq','1845 years','18.45 years','1.845 years','184.5 years','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181220,'chemistry','The rate of a reaction quadruples when the temperature changes from 293 K to 313 K. Calculate the energy of activation of the reaction assuming that it does not change with temperature.','52.864 kJ','Refer diagram(ne1850)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Exercises',NULL,'2','mcq','52.864 kJ','52864 kJ','52864 J','5286.4 J','NEW','12-113','12-079',NULL),
(181309,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is not correct about order of a reaction?','The order of a reaction is always equal to the sum of the stoichiometric coefficients of reactants in the balanced chemical equation for a reaction.','Order of reaction is equal to the sum of powers of concentration of the reactants in rate law expression.\nFor any chemical reaction , The answer is the option (iii). The summation of powers of the reactant\'s concentration is used for determining the order of reaction. The order of a reaction is always equal to the sum of the stoichiometric coefficients of reacting species in the balanced chemical equation.\nOrder of reaction can be a fraction also. Order of reaction is not always equal to sum of the stoichiometric coefficients of reactants in the balanced chemical equation. For a reaction it may or may not be equal to sum of stoichiometric coefficient of reactants.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','The order of a reaction can be a fractional number.','Order of a reaction is experimentally determined quantity.','The order of a reaction is always equal to the sum of the stoichiometric coefficients of reactants in the balanced chemical equation for a reaction.','The order of a reaction is the sum of the powers of molar concentration of the reactants in the rate law expression','NEW','12-102','12-068',NULL),
(181310,'chemistry','Consider the graph given below. Which of the following option does not show instantaneous rate of reaction at 40th second? Refer diagram(n1844)','V₄-V₂/50-30','Does not show instantaneous rate of reaction at 40th second.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','V₅-V₂/50-30','V₄-V₂/50-30','V₃-V₂/40-30','V₃-V₁/40-20','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181310q.png'),
(181311,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is correct?','The rate of a reaction decreases with passage of time as the concentration of reactants decreases.','The rate of a reaction depends upon the concentration of reactants.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','The rate of a reaction decreases with passage of time as the concentration of reactants decreases.','The rate of a reaction is same at any time during the reaction.','The rate of a reaction is independent of temperature change.','The rate of a reaction decreases with increase in concentration of reactant(s).','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(181312,'chemistry','Which of the following expressions is correct for the rate of reaction given below? Refer diagram(n1845)','c','Rate law expression for the above equation can be written as refer diagram(ne1854)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-101','12-067','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181312q.png'),
(181313,'chemistry','Which of the following graph represents exothermic reaction?Refer diagram(n1846)','a','The answer is the option (i). In an exothermic reaction the reaction activation energy of product should be higher than that of reactant whereas the enthalpy of reactant should be greater than that of products.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-113','12-079','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181313q.png'),
(181314,'chemistry','Rate law for the reaction ‘B’ is doubled, keeping the concentration of ‘A’ constant, the value of rate constant will be','the same','Rate constant of a reaction does not depend upon concentrations of the reactants.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','the same','doubled','quadrupled','halved','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(181315,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is incorrect about the collision theory of chemical reaction?','Collision of atoms or molecules possessing sufficient threshold energy results into the product formation.','According the postulates of collision theory there are the following necessary conditions for any reaction to be occur:\n(i) Molecule should collide with sufficient threshold energy.\n(ii) Their orientation must be proper.\n(iii) The collision must be effective.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','It considers reacting molecules or atoms to be hard spheres and ignores their structural features.','Number of effective collisions determines the rate of a reaction.','Collision of atoms or molecules possessing sufficient threshold energy results into the product formation.','Molecules should collide with sufficient threshold energy and proper orientation for the collision to be effective.','NEW','12-113','12-079',NULL),
(181316,'chemistry','A first order reaction is 50% completed in 1.26×10¹⁴ s. How much time would it take for 100% completion?','infinite','Reaction would be 100% complete only after infinite time which cannot be calculated.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','1.26 x 10¹⁵ s','2.52×10¹⁴ s','2.52 x 10²⁸ s','infinite','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181317,'chemistry','Compounds ‘A’ and ‘B’ react according to the following chemical equation.\nConcentration of either ‘A’ or ‘B’ were changed keeping the concentrations of one of the reactants constant and rates were measured as a function of initial concentration. The following results were obtained. Choose the correct option for the rate equations for this reaction. Refer diagram(n1846)','b','Rate=K[A]ˣ[B]ʸ  \nWhen concentration of B is doubled keeping the concentration of A constant, the rate of formation of C increases by a factor of four. This indicates that the rate of reactions depends upon the square of concentration of B. When concentration of A is doubled, the rate of formation of C also doubles from the initial value. This shows that the rate depends on first power of concentration of A. Hence Rate= K[A]ˣ[B]ʸ.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-101','12-067','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181317q.png'),
(181325,'chemistry','At high pressure the following reaction is zero order.\nWhich of the following options are correct for this reaction?\n(a) Rate of reaction = Rate constant\n(b) Rate of the reaction depends on concentration of ammonia.\n(c) Rate of decomposition of ammonia will remain constant until ammonia disappears completely\n(d) Further increase in pressure will change the rate of reaction. Refer diagram(n1848)','a,c,d','For a zero order reaction, the rate of reaction is independent of concentration of ammonia.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,c,d','only b','a,b,c','only d','NEW','12-105','12-071','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181325q.png'),
(181327,'chemistry','According to Maxwell Boltzmann distribution of energy\n(a) the fraction of molecules with most probable kinetic energy decreases at higher temperatures\n(b) the fraction of molecules with most probable kinetic energy increases at higher temperatures\n(c) most probable kinetic energy increases at higher temperatures\n(d) most probable kinetic energy decreases at higher temperatures','a,c','Distribution of kinetic energy may be described by plotting a graph of fraction of molecules versus kinetic energy.\nKinetic energy of maximum fraction of molecule is known as most probable kinetic energy. It is important to note that with increase of temperature, peak shifts forward but downward. This means that with increase of temperature\n(i) most probable kinetic energy increases.\n(ii) the fractions of molecules possessing most probable kinetic energy decreases.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,c','a,d','a,b','a,c,d','NEW','12-113','12-079',NULL),
(181328,'chemistry','In the graph showing Maxwell Boltzmann distribution of energy,\n(a) area under the curve must not change with increase in temperature\n(b) area under the curve increases with increase in temperature\n(c) area under the curve decreases with increase in temperature\n(d) with increase in temperature curve broadens and shifts to the right hand side','a,d','Area under the curve remains same with increase in temperature and with increase in temperature curve broadens and shifts to the right-hand side.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,d','a,b','c,d','b,d','NEW','12-112','12-078',NULL),
(181329,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are in accordance with the Arrhenius equation?\n(a) The rate of a reaction increases with increase in temperature.\n(b) The rate of reaction increases with decrease in activation energy.\n(c) The rate constant decreases exponentially with increase in temperature.\n(d) The rate of reaction decreases with decrease in activation energy.','a,b','The rate of reaction increases with temperature. It becomes double with every 10° change. It increases with decrease in activation energy.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,d','b,c','a,c,d','NEW','12-112','12-078',NULL),
(181330,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statements.\n(a) Catalyst provides an alternative pathway to reaction mechanism.\n(b) Catalyst raises the activation energy.\n(c) Catalyst lower the activation energy.\n(d) Catalyst alters enthapy change of the reaction.','b,d','(i) As the catalyst is added to the reaction medium rate of reaction increases by decreasing activation energy of molecule. Hence, it follows an alternative pathway.\n(ii) Catalyst does not change the enthapy change of reaction. Energy of ‘ reactant and product remain same in both catalysed and  uncatalysed .\nreaction.Hence, (a) and (c) are incorrect statements.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,c','a,b','b,d','a,d','NEW','12-119','12-082',NULL),
(181331,'chemistry','Which of the following graphs are correct for a zero order reaction? Refer diagram(n1850)','a,d','If we plot [R] against t we get a straight line with slope = -k and intercept equal to[R]₀ from the equation\n[R] = -kt + [R]₀\nThe rate is directly proportional to the zero power of concentration in zero order reaction.\nRate = -d[R]/dt = k[R]₀','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,d','a,b','b,c','b,d','NEW','12-105','12-071','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181331q.png'),
(181332,'chemistry','Which of the following graphs are correct for a first order reaction? Refer diagram(n1851)','a,d','Refer diagram(ne1858)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,d','a,b','b,c','b,d','NEW','12-106','12-072','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181332q.png'),
(80425,'chemistry','Which metal ion (M⁺³) among the following has smallest ionic radius?','Cr','Cr⁺³ has the smallest ionic radius and the value is 62 pm.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 220; Table 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','Mn','Sc','Ti','Cr','NEW','12-220','12-093',NULL),
(80426,'chemistry','Arrange the following in increasing order of their M⁺³ ionic sizes.','Ni<Cr<Mn<Ti','Ni < Cr < Mn < Ti is the increasing order of their M⁺³ ionic sizes.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 220; Table 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'3','order','1,Ni','2,Cr','3,Mn','4,Ti','NEW','12-220','12-093',NULL),
(80427,'chemistry','Which element from 3d series has smallest M⁺² and M⁺³ ionic size?','Ni','Ni has smallest M⁺² and M⁺³ ionic size.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 220; Table 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','Cu','Cr','Zn','Ni','NEW','12-220','12-093',NULL),
(80428,'chemistry','Which ion among the following is strongest oxdising agent?','Co⁺³','Co⁺³ is the strongest oxidising agent because it has the positive SRP value of 1.97 for M³⁺/M²⁺, which means it is stable in +2 state than in +3 state.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 220; Table 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Co⁺³','Fe⁺³','V⁺³','Cr⁺³','NEW','12-220','12-093',NULL),
(80429,'chemistry','Which metal among the following has positive value of  Electrode potential M⁺³/M⁺²?','Mn','Mn, Fe and Co have positive value of  Electrode potential M⁺³/M⁺²\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 220; Table 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','Mn','Ti','V','Cr','NEW','12-220','12-093',NULL),
(80430,'chemistry','Conversion from M⁺² to M is thermodynamically most favourable for which ion?','Ti⁺²','Ti⁺² has the most negative SRP value (-1.63). Therefore, it is favourable for Ti to stay in 0 oxidation state than in +2 oxidation state.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 220; Table 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','V⁺²','Ti⁺²','Cr⁺²','Mn⁺²','NEW','12-220','12-093',NULL),
(80431,'chemistry','Which element among the following has positive value of electrode potential M⁺²/ M?','Cu','Cu has positive value of electrode potential M⁺²/ M and its value is +0.34.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 220; Table 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Cu','Fe','Co','Zn','NEW','12-220','12-093',NULL),
(80432,'chemistry','Arrange the following ions according to their increasing oxidising power.','V⁺³<Fe⁺³<Mn⁺³<Co⁺³','V⁺³ < Fe⁺³ < Mn⁺³ < Co⁺³ is the order of increasing oxidising power. This is because the SRP values for V⁺³/V²⁺,  Fe⁺³/Fe²⁺,  Mn⁺³/Mn²⁺, Co⁺³/Co²⁺ are -0.26, +0.77, +1.57, +1.97. Greater the SRP value, better the oxidising agent.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 220; Table 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'3','order','1,V⁺³','2,Fe⁺³','3,Mn⁺³','4,Co⁺³','NEW','12-220','12-093',NULL),
(80433,'chemistry','Arrange the following according to their increasing density.','V<Zn<Mn<Ni','V < Zn < Mn < Ni is the order of their increasing density with values for V, Zn, Mn, and Mn 6.07 gcm⁻³, 7.1 gcm⁻³, 7.21 gcm⁻³, 8.9 gcm⁻³ respectively.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 220; Table 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'3','order','1,V','2,Zn','3,Mn','4,Ni','NEW','12-220','12-093',NULL),
(80434,'chemistry','Which element from 3d series has minimum value of density?','Sc','Scandium has the minimum density of 3.43 gcm⁻³.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 220; Table 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','Sc','V','Zn','Mn','NEW','12-220','12-093',NULL),
(80435,'chemistry','Assertion: Amongst elements of 3d series Zinc has lowest value of enthalpy of atomisation.\n\nReason: Zinc has fully filled d-orbitals and poor interatomic metallic bonding.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','In the formation of metallic bonds, no eletrons from 3d-orbitals are involved in case of zinc, while in all other metals of the 3d series, electrons from the d-orbitals are always involved in the formation of metallic bonds.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 221; Intext Question 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-221','12-094',NULL),
(80436,'chemistry','A pair among the following have nearly the same atomic radii. (Numbers in the parenthesis are atomic numbers).','Zr (40) and Hf (72)','Zr (atomic radii 160 pm) and Hf (atomic radii 158 pm) have nearly same atomic radii because of the lanthanoid contration. As a result, the elements of second and third transition series have almost similar atomic radii. \n\nThe atomic size of other elements are as follows: \nTi = 147 pm\nNb = 207 pm\nTa = 220 pm\n\nHence, option third is the correct option.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','Ti (22) and Zr (40)','Zr (40) and Nb (41)','Zr (40) and Hf (72)','Zr (40) and Ta (73)','NEW','12-220','12-094',NULL),
(80437,'chemistry','Zr (Z = 40) and Hf (Z = 72) have similar atomic and ionic radii because of:','Lanthanoid contraction','The electronic configuration of Zr is \n1s²2s²2p⁶3s²3p⁶3d¹⁰4s²4p⁶4d²5s² and Hf is 1s²2s²2p⁶3s²3p⁶3d¹⁰4s²4p⁶4d²5s²5p⁶4f¹⁴5d²6s². Due to poor shielding effect of 4f orbital in Hf, the effective nuclear charge increases and size of Hf decreases.\n\nZr size is 160 pm and Hf is 159 pm and this is due to lanthanoid contraction.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Lanthanoid contraction','Having similar chemical properties','Belonging to the same group','Diagonal relationship','NEW','12-220','12-094',NULL),
(80438,'chemistry','Which one of the following ions has electronic configuration [Ar 3d]⁶? \n(At. no: Mn = 25, Fe = 26, Co = 27, Ni = 28)','Co³⁺','The electronic configuration of given ions is-\nNi³⁺ (28) = [Ar] 3d⁷\nMn³⁺ (25) = [Ar] 3d⁴\nFe³⁺ (26) = [Ar] 3d⁵\nCo³⁺ (27) = [Ar] 3d⁶\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 220; Paragraph 8.2 Table left)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','Ni³⁺','Mn³⁺','Fe³⁺','Co³⁺','NEW','12-220','12-094',NULL),
(80439,'chemistry','Which one of the following does not correctly represent the correct order of the proper indicated against it?','Ti < V < Cr < Mn; increasing melting point','(1) As the number of unpaired electrons increases, the magnetic moment will also increase. So, the given order is correct.\n(2) Along the 3d series melting point increases up to the Cr than decreases. So, the correct order of melting point will be: Mn<Ti<V<Cr\n(3) The value of second ionisation energy generally increases along a period when we move from left to right in a period. But Cr⁺ has 3d⁵ configuration which is more stable. Thus, second IE of Cr will be more than Mn: The correct order of second IE is Ti < V < Mn < Cr\n(4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Ti³⁺< V³⁺< Cr³⁺< Mn³⁺; increasing magnetic moment','Ti < V < Cr < Mn; increasing melting point','Ti < V < Mn < Cr; increasing 2nd ionization enthalpy','Ti < V < Cr < Mn; increasing number of oxidation states','NEW','12-221','12-094',NULL),
(80440,'chemistry','Four successive members of the first-row transition elements are listed below with their atomic numbers. Which one of them is expected to have the highest third ionization enthalpy:','Manganese (Z = 25)','25Mn = 3d⁵ 4s² INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(81521,'chemistry','Sodium dichromate is ____ soluble than/to potassium dichromate.','more','Sodium dichromate is more soluble than potassium dichromate.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','more','less','equally','slightly less','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81522,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is true?','The oxidation state of chromium in chromate and dichromate is the same.','Orange crystals of potassium dichromate crystallise out. The chromates and dichromates are interconvertible in aqueous solution depending upon pH of the solution. The oxidation state of chromium in chromate and dichromate is the same.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Potassium dichromate is purple in color.','The oxidation state of chromium in chromate and dichromate is the same.','The chromates and dichromates are not interconvertible','Sodium dichromate is less soluble than potassium dichromate.','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(2000112373,'physics','In a hydrogen sample, if the atoms are excited to states with principal quantum number 20, then the number of different wavelengths which may be observed in the spectrum is','C','When atom are excited from ground state to excited state with $n=20$, then number of spectral line possible are given by<br>$N=\\dfrac{n(n-1)}{2}$<br>[where, ${n}=$ principal quantum number]<br>$\\therefore N=\\dfrac{20(20-1)}{2}=190$<br>So, the correct option is (3).','The Line Spectra of the Hydrogen Atom ','Atoms','Medium',NULL,'100','140','190','230',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(81523,'chemistry','Assertion: Potassium dichromate is prepared by treating sodium dichromate with potasssium chloride.\n\nReason: Sodium dichromate is more soluble than potassium dichromate.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Sodium dichromate is more soluble than potassium dichromate. The latter is therefore, prepared by treating the solution of sodium dichromate with potassium chloride.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81524,'chemistry','The Cr-O-Cr bond angle in dicrhomate ion is about-','126°','The Cr-O-Cr bond angle in dicrhomate ion is about 126°\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','109°','104°','126°','90°','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81525,'chemistry','What is correct about the structure of dichromate ion? \ndiagram(n0803)','The oxidation of chromium is +6','The dichromate ion consists of two tetrahedra sharing one corner with Cr–O–Cr bond angle of 126°. All bond angles are not same \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'3','mcq','It consist of two tetrahedrals sharing three corners.','The oxidation of chromium is +6','All bond lenghts are equal.','All bond angles are  same','NEW','12-231','12-106','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/81525q.png'),
(81526,'chemistry','Which of the following is not true for chromate and dichromate?','Chromate ion solution is orange while dichromate is yellow.','Orange crystals of potassium dichromate crystallise out. The chromates and dichromates are interconvertible in aqueous solution depending upon pH of the solution. The oxidation state of chromium in chromate and dichromate is the same.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Both ions have same oxidation state of chromium.','All bond lengths are equal in chromate but not in dichromate.','Chromate ion solution is orange while dichromate is yellow.','Dichromate ion solution is orange while chromate is yellow.','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81527,'chemistry','Which of the following are not true?\nA. Potassium dichromate is used as secondary standard in volumetric analysis.\nB. Sodium and potassium dichromates are strong oxidising agents.\nC. Sodium dichromate is more soluble than potassium dichromate.\nD. Acidified potassium dichromate oxidises iodides to iodates.','A and D','Sodium and potassium dichromates are strong oxidising agents; the sodium salt has a greater solubility in water and is extensively used as an oxidising agent in organic chemistry. Potassium dichromate is used as a primary standard in volumetric analysis. Acidified potassium dichromate will oxidise iodides to iodine,\nThus, statements  A and D are incorrect \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','A and C','B and C','A and C','A and D','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81528,'chemistry','Acidified potassium dichromate oxidises iodides to-','Iodine','Acidified potassium dichromate will oxidise iodides to iodine.\n6I⁻ → 3I₂+ 6e⁻\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','Iodine','Iodates','Iodites','Periodate','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81529,'chemistry','Which of the following convertion is not carried out by potassium dichromate?','Oxidation of iodides to iodates','Acidified potassium dichromate will oxidise iodides to iodine, sulphides to sulphur, tin(II) to tin(IV) and iron(II) salts to iron(III).\n6I⁻ → 3I₂+ 6e⁻\n3Sn²⁺ → 3Sn⁴⁺ + 6e⁻\n3H₂S → 6H⁺ + 3S + 6e⁻\n6Fe²⁺ → 6Fe³⁺ + 6e⁻\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Oxidation of Sn(II) to Sn(IV)','Oxidation of Fe(II) to Fe(III)','Oxidation of sulphides to sulphur','Oxidation of iodides to iodates','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81530,'chemistry','How many moles of acid (protons) are required for action of 1 mole of dichromate?','14','14 moles of acid (protons) are required for action of 1 mole of dichromate\nCr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ + 6Fe²⁺  → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O (EV= 1.33V)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','14','7','28','4','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81531,'chemistry','1 mole dichromate can oxidise how many moles of Fe(II)?','6','1 mole dichromate can oxidise 6 moles of Fe(II)\nCr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ →  6Fe³⁺ + 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','2','6','3','10','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81532,'chemistry','Which of the following oxidising agent is used for preparation of potassium permanganate?','KNO₃','Potassium permanganate is prepared by fusion of MnO₂ with an alkali metal hydroxide and an oxidising agent like KNO₃.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','KNO₃','H₂S','SO₂','NH₃','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81533,'chemistry','What is the product obtained by fusion of MnO₂ with alkali and oxidising agent?','K₂MnO₄','Potassium permanganate is prepared by fusion of MnO₂ with an alkali metal hydroxide and an oxidising agent like KNO₃. This produces the dark green K₂MnO₄ which disproportionates in a neutral or acidic solution to give permanganate.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','KMnO₄','K₂MnO₄','KMnO₃','MnO₃','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81534,'chemistry','What is the color of potassium manganate ?','Dark green','Potassium manganate is Dark green in color.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','Purple','Dark green','Blue','Yellow','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81535,'chemistry','Managanate ion ________ in a neutral or acidic medium to give Permanaganate ions.','disproportionates','Potassium permanganate is prepared by fusion of MnO₂  with an alkali metal hydroxide and an oxidising agent like KNO₃. This produces the dark green K₂MnO₄ which disproportionates in a neutral or acidic solution to give permanganate.\n\n2MnO₂ + 4KOH + O₂ → 2K₂MnO₄ + 2H₂O\n3MnO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺ → 2MnO⁴⁻ + MnO₂ + 2H₂O\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','disproportionates','oxidises','reduses','decomposes','NEW','12-231','12-106',NULL),
(81536,'chemistry','Managanate ion disproportionate in acidic medium to give permanaganate ions and another product X. X contains Mn, What is X?','MnO₂','Potassium permanganate is prepared by fusion of MnO₂  with an alkali metal hydroxide and an oxidising agent like KNO₃. This produces the dark green K₂MnO₄ which disproportionates in a neutral or acidic solution to give permanganate.\n\n2MnO₂ + 4KOH + O₂ → 2K₂MnO₄ + 2H₂O\n\n3MnO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺ → 2MnO⁴⁻ + MnO₂ + 2H₂O \n\nTherefore X is MnO₂\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','MnO₂','K₂MnO₂','MnO₃','Mn(OH)₂','NEW','12-232','12-107',NULL),
(81537,'chemistry','What is commercial method for preparation of Permanganate ion?','Electrolytic oxidation of Mn(VI)','Commercially permanganate is prepared by the alkaline oxidative fusion of MnO₂ followed by the electrolytic oxidation of manganate (Vl).\n\n1. Fused with KOH, oxidised with air or KNO₃\n    2MnO₂   → MnO₄²⁻ (manganate ion)\n\n2. Electrolytic oxidation in alkaline solution\n     MnO₄²⁻ (manganate ion) → MnO₄⁻ (permanganate ion)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','Oxidation of Mn(VI) using O₃.','Electrolytic oxidation of Mn(VI)','Oxidation of Mn(VI) using KNO₃.','Oxidation of Mn(VI) using peroxodisulphate.','NEW','12-232','12-107',NULL),
(81538,'chemistry','What is lab method for preparation of Permanganate ion?','Oxidation of Mn(VI) using peroxodisulphate.','In the laboratory, a manganese (II) ion salt is oxidised by peroxodisulphate to permanganate.\n2Mn²⁺ + 5S₂O₈²⁻ + 8H₂O → 2MnO₄⁻ + 10SO₄²⁻ + 16H⁺\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 233; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','Oxidation of Mn(VI) using O₃.','Electrolytic oxidation of Mn(VI)','Oxidation of Mn(VI) using KNO₃.','Oxidation of Mn(VI) using peroxodisulphate.','NEW','12-232','12-107',NULL),
(81539,'chemistry','What is the color of potassium permanganate ?','Dark purple','Potassium permanganate forms dark purple (almost black) crystals\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 233; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','Dark green','Dark purple','Yellow','Blue','NEW','12-232','12-107',NULL),
(81540,'chemistry','Which compound is isostrutural with potassium permanaganate?','Potassium perchlorate','Potassium permanganate forms dark purple (almost black) crystals which are isostructural with those of KClO₄.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Potassium perchlorate','Potassium chloride','Sodium chlorate','Potassium bromide','NEW','12-232','12-107',NULL),
(81541,'chemistry','Which is false for potassium permanganate?','Its salt is highly water soluble.','Potassium permanganate forms dark purple (almost black) crystals which are isostructural with those of KClO₄. The salt is not very soluble in water (6.4 g/100 g of water at 293 K), but when heated it decomposes at 513 K.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','It has almost black crystals.','Its salt is highly water soluble.','It decomposes at 513K.','Upon decomposition it gives oxygen gas.','NEW','12-232','12-107',NULL),
(81542,'chemistry','What is true for potassium permanganate?','It is diamagnetic in nature.','It has two physical properties of considerable interest: its intense colour and its diamagnetism along with temperature-dependent weak\nparamagnetism.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','It has color because of d-d transition.','It is paramagnetic in nature.','It is diamagnetic in nature.','temperature-independent weak paramagnetism','NEW','12-232','12-107',NULL),
(81543,'chemistry','Select the incorrect option of potassium permanganate and potassium manganate?','The green manganate is paramagnetic because of 2 unpaired electrons','The manganate and permanganate ions are tetrahedral; the p-bonding takes place by overlap of p orbitals of oxygen with d orbitals of manganese. The green manganate is paramagnetic because of one unpaired electron but the permanganate is diamagnetic due to the absence of unpaired electron.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','The manganate and permanganate ions are tetrahedral','There is presence of π bonds in both the compounds.','Manganate is paramagnetic while permanganate is diamagnetic.','The green manganate is paramagnetic because of 2 unpaired electrons','NEW','12-232','12-107',NULL),
(81544,'chemistry','Acidified permanganate solution oxidises oxalates to-','Carbon dioxide','Acidified permanganate solution oxidises oxalates to carbon dioxide,\n5C₂O₄²⁻ → 10CO₂ + 10e⁻\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','Carbon monoxide','Carbon dioxide','Carbon suboxide','Oxygen','NEW','12-232','12-107',NULL),
(81545,'chemistry','What is the color the structure shown? \ndiagram(n0804)','Green','The structure is manganate ion and it is Green in color.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'3','mcq','Yellow','Purple','Green','Orange','NEW','12-232','12-107','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/81545q.png'),
(81546,'chemistry','What are the mole of hydronium ions required for oxidising action of potassium permanganate in neutral medium?','4','The reaction of Permanganate in Neutral medium is\nMnO₄⁻ + 4H⁺ + 3e⁻ → MnO₂ + 2H₂O\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','0','4','8','14','NEW','12-232','12-107',NULL),
(81547,'chemistry','In which medium does potassium permanganate act as strongest oxidising agent?','Neutral','Potassium permanganate act as strongest oxidising agent in Neutral medium\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Neutral','Basic','Acidic','Same in basic and neutral medium','NEW','12-232','12-107',NULL),
(81548,'chemistry','Assertion: Permanganate at [H⁺] = 1 should oxidise water but in practice the reaction is extremely slow unless either manganese(ll) ions are present or the temperature is raised.\n\nReason: Mn(II) acts as catalyst for the reaction or when the temperature is raised activation energy decreases.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','We can very well see that the hydrogen ion concentration of the solution plays an important part in influencing the reaction. Although many reactions can be understood by consideration of redox potential, kinetics of the reaction is also an important factor. Permanganate at [H⁺] = 1 should oxidise water but in practice the reaction is extremely slow unless either manganese(ll) ions are present or the temperature is raised.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-232','12-107',NULL),
(81549,'chemistry','When Fe(II) is oxidised to Fe(III) by permanganate what change in color is observed?','Green to Yellow','Fe²⁺ ion (green) is converted to Fe³⁺ (yellow) by potassium permanganate.\n5Fe²⁺ + MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O + 5Fe³⁺\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Green to Yellow','Yellow to Green','Blue to Green','Purple to pink','NEW','12-232','12-107',NULL),
(81550,'chemistry','In acidic solution potassium permanganate converts iodides into-','Iodine','In acidic solution Iodine is liberated from potassium iodide :\n10I⁻ + 2MnO₄⁻ + 16H⁺ → 2Mn²⁺ + 8H₂O + 5I₂\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','Iodine','Iodates','Iodites','Periodate','NEW','12-233','12-108',NULL),
(81551,'chemistry','Hydrogen sulphide is oxidised by potassium permanganate in acidic solution to give-','Sulphur','In acidic solution, Hydrogen sulphide is oxidised, sulphur being precipitated:\nH₂S →  2H⁺ + S²⁻\n5S²⁻ + 2MnO₄⁻ + 16H⁺ →  2Mn²⁺ + 8H₂O + 5S\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 234; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','Sulphate','Sulphur','Sulphur dioxide','Sulphite','NEW','12-233','12-108',NULL),
(81552,'chemistry','Sulphurous acid is oxidised by potassium permanganate in acidic solution to give-','Sulphates','In acidic solution, Sulphurous acid or sulphite is oxidised to a sulphate or sulphuric acid:\n5SO₃²⁻ + 2MnO₄⁻ + 6H⁺ →  2Mn²⁺ + 3H₂O + 5SO₄\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 234; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','Sulphates','Sulphur','Sulphur dioxide','Sulphite','NEW','12-233','12-108',NULL),
(81553,'chemistry','In neutral medium, potassium permanganate oxidises iodide to-','Iodates','In neutral medium, the oxidation of iodide to iodate takes place\n2MnO₄⁻+ H₂O + I⁻ → 2MnO₂ + 2OH⁻ + IO₃⁻\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 234; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Iodine','Iodates','Iodites','Periodate','NEW','12-233','12-108',NULL),
(81554,'chemistry','In neutral medium, potassium permanganate oxidises thiosulphate to-','Sulphate','In neutral medium, (b) Thiosulphate is oxidised almost quantitatively to sulphate:\n8MnO₄⁻ + 3S₂O₃²⁻ + H₂O →  8MnO₂ + 6SO₄²⁻ + 2OH⁻\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 234; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','Sulphate','sulphur','sulphur dioxide','Sulphite','NEW','12-233','12-108',NULL),
(81555,'chemistry','Manganous salt is oxidised to MnO₂ by potassium permanganate in neutral medium in presence of ZnO. What is the role of ZnO?','Used as a catalyst','Manganous salt is oxidised to MnO₂; the presence of zinc sulphate or zinc oxide catalyses the oxidation:\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 234; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'2','mcq','Used as a catalyst','Used as oxdising agent','Used as reducing agent','Used as flux','NEW','12-233','12-108',NULL),
(81556,'chemistry','Assertion: Permanganate titrations in presence of hydrochloric acid will give unsatisfactory results.\n\nReason: Hydrochloric acid is oxidised to chlorine gas, by potassium permanganate. \n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Permanganate titrations in presence of hydrochloric acid are unsatisfactory since hydrochloric acid is oxidised to chlorine.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 234; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-233','12-108',NULL),
(81557,'chemistry','What among the following is not the use of potassium permanganate?','As an oxidant for preparation of many azo compounds.','Besides its use in analytical chemistry, potassium permanganate is used as a favourite oxidant in preparative organic chemistry. Its uses for the bleaching of wool, cotton, silk and other textile fibres and for the decolourisation of oils are also dependent on its strong oxidising power.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 234; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Oxides and Oxoanions of Metals',NULL,'1','mcq','Used as a favourite oxidant in preparative organic chemistry.','Used in analytical chemistry.','Bleaching of wool, cotton and silk.','As an oxidant for preparation of many azo compounds.','NEW','12-233','12-108',NULL),
(81601,'chemistry','Which of the following is general symbol for lanthanoids?','Ln','Because lanthanum closely resembles the lanthanoids, it is usually included in any discussion of the lanthanoids for which the general symbol Ln is often used.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 234; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Inner Transition Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','La','Ln','Lr','Lt','NEW','12-233','12-108',NULL),
(81602,'chemistry','Which group of elements resemble one another more closely?','Lanthanoids','The lanthanoids resemble one another more closely than do the members of ordinary transition elements in any series.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 234; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Inner Transition Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Lanthanoids','3d series','4d series','5d series','NEW','12-233','12-108',NULL),
(81603,'chemistry','Lanthanoids have how many stable oxidation states?','1','Lanthanoids have only one stable oxidation state and their chemistry provides an excellent opportunity to examine the effect of small changes in size and nuclear charge along a series of otherwise similar elements.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 234; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Inner Transition Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','1','3','4','2','NEW','12-233','12-108',NULL),
(81604,'chemistry','Chemistry of actinoids is complex because-\nA. They have wide of range of oxidation states present.\nB. They are radioactive.\nC. They are abundantly present.','Both A and B','The chemistry of the actinoids is, much more complicated. The complication arises partly owing to the occurrence of a wide range of oxidation states in these elements and partly because their radioactivity creates special problems in their study;\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 234; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Inner Transition Elements',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and C','Both B and C','Both A and B','Only B','NEW','12-233','12-108',NULL),
(81701,'chemistry','What is the most stable oxidation state of Lanthanoids?','3+','The electronic configurations of all the tripositive ions (the most stable oxidation state of all the lanthanoids) are of the form 4fⁿ (n = 1 to 14 with increasing atomic number).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Electronic Configuration and Atomic Size',NULL,'1','mcq','2+','3+','4+','6+','NEW','12-234','12-109',NULL),
(81702,'chemistry','Which is true of electronic configuration of Lanthanoids?','These elements have electronic configuration with 6s² common but with variable occupancy of 4f level','It may be noted that atoms of Lanthanoids elements have electronic configuration with 6s² common but with variable occupancy of 4f level. However, the electronic configurations of all the tripositive ions (the most stable oxidation state of all the lanthanoids) are of the form 4fⁿ (n = 1 to 14 with increasing atomic number).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Electronic Configuration and Atomic Size',NULL,'2','mcq','These elements have electronic configuration with 6s² common but with variable occupancy of 4f level','Most stable oxidation is +4.','All tripositive ions have single electron in 6s orbital.','The electronic configurations of all the tripositive ions (the most stable oxidation state of all the lanthanoids) are of the form 4fⁿ (n = 0 to 14 with increasing atomic number).','NEW','12-234','12-109',NULL),
(81703,'chemistry','What are the number electrons in f-subshell of Sm?','6','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Table 8.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Electronic Configuration and Atomic Size',NULL,'1','mcq','4','7','6','8','NEW','12-234','12-109',NULL),
(81704,'chemistry','What are the number electrons in f-subshell of Ho³⁺?','10','Atomic no. of Ho is 67 and electronic configuration (Xe) 4f¹¹ 6s² . Ho³⁺ has 10 electrons in f-subshell and has electronic configuration (Xe) 4f¹⁰\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Table 8.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Electronic Configuration and Atomic Size',NULL,'2','mcq','10','14','8','7','NEW','12-234','12-109',NULL),
(81705,'chemistry','What is electronic configuration of La²⁺?','5d¹','The electronic configuration of La²⁺ is [Xe] 5d¹\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Table 8.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Electronic Configuration and Atomic Size',NULL,'2','mcq','6s¹','5d¹','4f¹','6p¹','NEW','12-234','12-109',NULL),
(81706,'chemistry','Which two elements have half-filled f-subshell?','Gd and Eu','The electronic configuration is given by\nZ = 64  Gd : [Xe] 4f⁷ 5d¹ 6s²\nZ = 63  Eu : [Xe] 4f⁷ 6s²\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Table 8.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Electronic Configuration and Atomic Size',NULL,'1','mcq','Gd and Ho','Eu and Gd','Eu and Ho','Gd and Eu','NEW','12-234','12-109',NULL),
(81707,'chemistry','Wihich of the following elements have single electron in 5d orbital?','Lu and Gd','The electronic configuration is given by\nZ = 64  Gd : [Xe] 4f⁷ 5d¹ 6s²\nZ = 71  Gd : [Xe] 4f¹⁴ 5d¹ 6s²\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Table 8.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Electronic Configuration and Atomic Size',NULL,'2','mcq','Ho and Pm','Tb and Dy','Lu and Gd','Tm and Yb','NEW','12-234','12-109',NULL),
(81708,'chemistry','Which of the following lanthanoid has the largest atomic radius?','Eu','La has the atomic radius 187 pm\nEr has the atomic radius 175 pm\nCe has the atomic radius 183 pm\nEu  has the largest atomic radius, 199 pm\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Table 8.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Electronic Configuration and Atomic Size',NULL,'2','mcq','La','Eu','Er','Ce','NEW','12-234','12-109',NULL),
(81709,'chemistry','Arrange the following in decreasing order of atomic radius?','Eu>Ce>Pr>Sm','Eu (199 pm) > Ce (183 pm) > Pr (181 pm) >  Sm (180 pm) is the correct decreasing order of atomic radius\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Table 8.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Electronic Configuration and Atomic Size',NULL,'3','order','1,Eu','2,Ce','3,Pr','4,Sm','NEW','12-234','12-109',NULL),
(81710,'chemistry','Arrange the following ions in order of their increasing radius?','Dy³⁺<Eu³⁺<Pm³⁺<Ce³⁺','Dy³⁺ (91 pm) < Eu³⁺ (95 pm) < Pm³⁺ (101 pm) < Ce³⁺ (103 pm) is the correct increasing order of atomic radius\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Table 8.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Electronic Configuration and Atomic Size',NULL,'3','order','1,Dy³⁺','2,Eu³⁺','3,Pm³⁺','4,Ce³⁺','NEW','12-234','12-109',NULL),
(81711,'chemistry','Which element has atomic number 60?','Nd','Neodymium (Nd) has atomic number 60\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Table 8.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Electronic Configuration and Atomic Size',NULL,'1','mcq','Er','Lu','Nd','Pr','NEW','12-234','12-109',NULL),
(81712,'chemistry','What is the atomic number of Tb?','65','The atomic number of Tb is 65\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Table 8.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Electronic Configuration and Atomic Size',NULL,'1','mcq','66','65','67','68','NEW','12-234','12-109',NULL),
(81713,'chemistry','The overall decrease in atomic and ionic radii from lanthanum to lutetium  is a unique feature called-','Lanthanoid contraction','The overall decrease in atomic and ionic radii from lanthanum to lutetium  is a unique feature called Lanthanoid contraction.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Electronic Configuration and Atomic Size',NULL,'1','mcq','Lanthanoid contraction','Actinoid contraction','Transition metal variation','Transition contraction','NEW','12-234','12-109',NULL),
(81714,'chemistry','The shielding of one 4 f electron by another is  ____ than one d electron by another with the _______ in nuclear charge along the series.','less,  increase','The shielding of one 4 f electron by another is less than one d electron by another with the increase in nuclear charge along the series. There is fairly regular decrease in the sizes with increasing atomic number.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Electronic Configuration and Atomic Size',NULL,'1','mcq','less,  increase','less,  decrease','more,  increase','more, decrease','NEW','12-234','12-109',NULL),
(81715,'chemistry','Assertion: It is difficult to seperate Hf and Zr and they occur together in nature.\n\nReason: Lanthanoid contraction causes the radius of Zr and Hf to be almost identical.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','The cumulative effect of the contraction of the lanthanoid series, known as lanthanoid contraction, causes the radii of the members of the third transition series to be very similar to those of the corresponding members of the second series. The almost identical radii of Zr (160 pm) and Hf (159 pm), a consequence of the lanthanoid contraction, account for their occurrence together in nature and for the difficulty faced in their separation.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Electronic Configuration and Atomic Size',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-234','12-109',NULL),
(81716,'chemistry','Which is not true for lanthanoid contraction?','The overall decrease in atomic and ionic radii from lanthanum to Cerium is called lanthanoid contraction','The overall decrease in atomic and ionic radii from lanthanum to lutetium (the lanthanoid contraction) is a unique feature in the chemistry of the lanthanoids. It has far reaching consequences in the chemistry of the third transition series of the elements.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Electronic Configuration and Atomic Size',NULL,'2','mcq','It has far reaching consequences in the chemistry of the third transition series of the elements.','It is caused due to imperfect shielding by 4f electron.','There is fairly regular decrease in sizes of increasing atomic size.','The overall decrease in atomic and ionic radii from lanthanum to Cerium is called lanthanoid contraction','NEW','12-234','12-109',NULL),
(81801,'chemistry','Assertion: Occasionally +2 and +4 ions in solution or in solid compounds are also obtained for lanthanoids.\n\nReason: Irregularity arises mainly due to extra stability of half-filled or filled f subhsell.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','In the lanthanoids, La(II) and Ln(III) compounds are predominant species. However, occasionally +2 and +4 ions in solution or in solid compounds are also obtained. This irregularity (as in ionisation enthalpies) arises mainly from the extra stability of empty, half-filled\nor filled f subshell.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Oxidation States',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(81802,'chemistry','Which of the following is strong oxidant?','Ce(IV)','The formation of Ce(IV) is favoured by its noble gas configuration, but it is a strong oxidant reverting to the common +3 state. The Eo value for Ce⁴⁺/ Ce³⁺ is + 1.74 V which suggests that it can oxidise water.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','Ce(IV)','Ce(III)','Sm(III)','Eu(II)','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(81803,'chemistry','Assertion: Ce(IV) is a good analytical reagent.\n\nReason: Ce(IV) reaction rate with water is very slow while being good oxidant.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','The formation of Ce(IV) is favoured by its noble gas configuration, but it is a strong oxidant reverting to the common +3 state. The Eo value for Ce⁴⁺/ Ce³⁺ is + 1.74 V which suggests that it can oxidise water.However, the reaction rate is very slow and hence Ce(IV) is a good analytical reagent.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Oxidation States',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(81804,'chemistry','Which of the following exhibit +4 oxidation states but in oxides only?','Pr, Nd, Tb, Dy','Pr, Nd, Tb and Dy exhibit +4 oxidation states but in oxide MO₂ only\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','Ce, Pr, Tb, Dy','Pr, Nd, Tb, Dy','Dy, Ho, Tb, Pr','Pm, Ce, Sm, Pr','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(81805,'chemistry','Which of the following lanthanoid forms MO₂ oxide?','Nd','Pr, Nd, Tb and Dy exhibit +4 oxidation states but in oxide MO₂ only\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','Ho','Sm','U','Nd','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(81806,'chemistry','Which of the following is reducing agent?','Eu(II)','Eu⁺² is formed by losing the two s electrons and its f⁷ configuration accounts for the formation of this ion. However, Eu⁺²  is a strong reducing agent changing to the common +3 state.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','Ce(IV)','Ce(III)','Sm(III)','Eu(II)','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(81807,'chemistry','What accounts for formation of formation Eu⁺²?','Half-filled f-subshell','Eu⁺² is formed by losing the two s electrons and its f⁷ configuration accounts for the formation of this ion. However, Eu⁺²  is a strong reducing agent changing to the common +3 state.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','Filled f-subshell','Half-filled f-subshell','Half-filled d-subshell','Smaller atomic radius','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(81808,'chemistry','Which element has both +2 and +3 oxidation state?','Sm','The behaviour of samarium is very much like europium, exhibiting both +2 and +3 oxidation states.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','Tb','Sm','Ce','Nd','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(81809,'chemistry','What is the nature of Tb (IV)?','Oxidant','Tb (IV) acts as Oxidising agent.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Oxidation States',NULL,'1','mcq','Reductant','Oxidant','Hydrating agent','Dehydrating agent','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(81810,'chemistry','Which pair of ions act as reductant?','Ce(IV) and Yb(II)','Ce(IV) and Yb(II) ions act as reductant\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Lanthanoid Oxidation States',NULL,'2','mcq','Eu(II) and Ce(IV)','Ce(IV) and Yb(II)','Tb(IV) and Yb(II)','Ce(III) and Eu(II)','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(81901,'chemistry','Which of the following is/are true for lanthanoids?\nA. All lanthanoids are silvery white soft metals.\nB.The hardness decreases with increasing atomic number.\nC. Samarium has melting point of 1623 K.','Only A and C','All the lanthanoids are silvery white soft metals and tarnish rapidly in air. The hardness increases with increasing atomic number, samarium being steel hard. Their melting points range between 1000 to 1200 K but samarium melts at 1623 K.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Lanthanoids',NULL,'2','mcq','Only A','Only A and B','Only A and C','All A, B and C','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(81902,'chemistry','Which lanthanoid is exception to range of melting point 1000-1200K?','Sm','Lanthanoids melting points range between 1000 to 1200 K but samarium melts at 1623 K.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Lanthanoids',NULL,'2','mcq','La','Lu','Sm','Nd','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(81903,'chemistry','How many are true for properties of lanthanoids?\nA. Many trivalent lanthanoid ions are coloured both in the solid state and in aqueous solutions.\nB. They have typical metallic structure and are good conductors of heat and electricity.\nC. Density and other properties change smoothly except for Eu and Yb and occasionally for Sm and Tm.\nD. Colour of these ions may be attributed to the presence of f electrons.','4','They have typical metallic structure and are good conductors of heat and electricity. Density and other properties change smoothly except for Eu and Yb and occasionally for Sm and Tm. Many trivalent lanthanoid ions are coloured both in the solid state and in aqueous solutions. Colour of these ions may be attributed to the presence of f electrons.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Lanthanoids',NULL,'2','mcq','1','3','2','4','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(81904,'chemistry','Which of the following does not show color?','Lu⁺³','Lu⁺³ does not show color because of absence of unpaired electrons in f subshell\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Lanthanoids',NULL,'2','mcq','Ce⁺³','Sm⁺³','Lu⁺³','Dy⁺³','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(81905,'chemistry','What is the reason for the color of lanthanoids ions?','f-f transition','Colour of the ions may be attributed to the presence of f electrons. However, absorption bands are narrow, probably because of the excitation within f level.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Lanthanoids',NULL,'2','mcq','f-f transition','d-d transition','charge transfer','back-bonding','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(81906,'chemistry','Which the following ion is paramagnetic?','Ce⁺³','The lanthanoid ions other than the f⁰ type (La³⁺ and Ce⁴⁺) and the f¹⁴ type (Yb²⁺ and Lu³⁺) are all paramagnetic.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Lanthanoids',NULL,'2','mcq','Ce⁺³','Lu⁺³','La⁺³','Ce⁺⁴','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(81907,'chemistry','Which ion is paramagnetic?','Sm⁺³','The lanthanoid ions other than the f⁰ type (La³⁺ and Ce⁴⁺) and the f¹⁴ type (Yb²⁺ and Lu³⁺) are all paramagnetic.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Lanthanoids',NULL,'1','mcq','Ce⁺⁴','Lu⁺³','La⁺³','Sm⁺³','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(81910,'chemistry','Second ionisation energy of lanthanoids is comparable with-','Calcium','The first ionisation enthalpies of the lanthanoids are around 600 kJ/mol, the second about 1200 kJ/mol comparable with those of calcium\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Lanthanoids',NULL,'1','mcq','Calcium','Neon','Chlorine','Sodium','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(81911,'chemistry','Which elements is expected to abnormally low value of third ionisation energy?','Gd','A detailed discussion of the variation of the third ionisation enthalpies indicates that the exchange enthalpy considerations (as in 3d orbitals of the first transition series), appear to impart a certain degree of stability to empty, half-filled and completely filled orbitals f level. This is indicated from the abnormally low value of the third ionisation enthalpy of lanthanum, gadolinium and lutetium.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Lanthanoids',NULL,'2','mcq','Ce','Tb','Tm','Gd','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(81912,'chemistry','Assertion: Lanthanum, gadolinium and lutetium have abnormally low value of third ionisation enthalpy.\n\nReason: Exchange enthaply considerations appear to impart certain degree of stability to empty, half-filled and completely filled orbitals f level.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','A detailed discussion of the variation of the third ionisation enthalpies indicates that the exchange enthalpy considerations (as in 3d orbitals of the first transition series), appear to impart a certain degree of stability to empty, half-filled and completely filled orbitals f level. This is indicated from the abnormally low value of the third ionisation enthalpy of lanthanum, gadolinium and lutetium.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Lanthanoids',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(81913,'chemistry','In chemical behaviou of lanthanoids, in general, the earlier members of the series are quite reactive similar to ___.','Calcium','In their chemical behaviour, in general, the earlier members of the series are quite reactive similar to calcium but, with increasing atomic number, they behave more like aluminium.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Lanthanoids',NULL,'1','mcq','luminium','Calcium','Sodium','DIoxygen','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(81914,'chemistry','Which lanthanoid is exception to range of Electrode potential -2.2 V to -2.4 V?','Eu','Values for EV for the half-reaction are in the range of –2.2 to –2.4 V except for Eu for which the value is – 2.0 V.\nLn³⁺(aq) + 3e⁻ → Ln(s)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 237; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Lanthanoids',NULL,'2','mcq','Ce','Eu','Yb','Sm','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(81915,'chemistry','When lanthanoid metal are heated with carbon which type of carbide is not formed?','LnC','Only 3 types of carbide are formed i.e Ln₃C, Ln₂C₃ n LnC₂\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 237; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Lanthanoids',NULL,'1','mcq','Ln₃C','Ln₂C₃','LnC₂','LnC','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(81916,'chemistry','Which of the following are false for lanthanoids and their compounds?\nA. The hydroxides are not definite compounds, just hydrated oxides.\nB. They liberate hydrogen from dilute acids and burn in halogens to form halides.\nC. They are basic like alkaline earth metal oxides and hydroxides.\nD. They form MO₃ type of oxides.','A and D','They liberate hydrogen from dilute acids and burn in halogens to form halides. They form oxides M₂O₃ and hydroxides M(OH)₃. The hydroxides are definite compounds, not just hydrated oxides. They are basic like alkaline earth metal oxides and hydroxides.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 237; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Lanthanoids',NULL,'2','mcq','A and D','B and D','A and B','A and C','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(81917,'chemistry','What compound is formed when Ln is heated with nitrogen?','LnN','LnN is formed when Ln is heated with nitrogen\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 237; Figure 8.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Lanthanoids',NULL,'1','mcq','Ln₂N₃','Ln₂N','LnN','Ln₃N','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(81918,'chemistry','Which carbide is formed when Ln is heated with carbon at 2773 K?','LnC₂','LnC₂ is formed when Ln is heated with carbon at 2773 K\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 237; Figure 8.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Lanthanoids',NULL,'2','mcq','Ln₃C','Ln₂C₃','LnC₂','LnC₃','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(81919,'chemistry','Which gas is released when lanthanoids react with water?','Dihydrogen','Dihydrogen is released when lanthanoids react with water\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 237; Figure 8.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Lanthanoids',NULL,'1','mcq','DIoxygen','Dihydrogen','Hydrogen peroxide','Nascent oxygen','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(81920,'chemistry','The best single use of lanthanoids is','Production of alloy steel','The best single use of the lanthanoids is for the production of alloy steels for plates and pipes. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 237; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Lanthanoids',NULL,'1','mcq','Production of alloy steel','Produce bullets','In X-ray screen','Petrol cracking','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(81921,'chemistry','What is the percentage of lanthanoid metal in mischmetal?','95%','A well known alloy is mischmetall which consists of a lanthanoid metal (~ 95%) and iron (~ 5%) and traces of S, C, Ca and Al. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 237; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Lanthanoids',NULL,'1','mcq','5%','95%','60%','34%','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(81922,'chemistry','What is the use of Misch metal in Mg-based alloy?','Produce bullets','A good deal of mischmetall is used in Mg-based alloy to produce bullets, shell and lighter flint.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 237; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Lanthanoids',NULL,'1','mcq','Production of alloy steel','Produce bullets','In X-ray screen','Petrol cracking','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(81923,'chemistry','What is the mixed oxides of lanthanoids?','Petrol cracking','Mixed oxides of lanthanoids are employed as catalysts in petroleum cracking. Some individual Ln oxides are used as phosphors in television screens and similar fluorescing surfaces.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 237; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Lanthanoids',NULL,'1','mcq','Production of alloy steel','Produce bullets','In X-ray screen','Petrol cracking','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(82001,'chemistry','Choose the odd with repect to actinoids.','Tb','Terbium Tb is a Lanthanioid\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 237; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Electronic Configuration and Ionic Size',NULL,'1','mcq','Tb','Np','Am','No','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82002,'chemistry','Which actinoids does not has electrons in f-subshell?','Th','Thorium has atomic no. 90 and the outer electronic configuration is 6d² 7s². Thus there are no electrons in f subshell\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 237; Table 8.10)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Electronic Configuration and Ionic Size',NULL,'2','mcq','U','Pu','Np','Th','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82003,'chemistry','What is the eletronic configuration of Th⁺³?','5f¹','Thorium has atomic no. 90 and the outer electronic configuration is 6d² 7s². For Th⁺³ the electronic configuration is 5f¹\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 237; Table 8.10)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Electronic Configuration and Ionic Size',NULL,'2','mcq','6s¹','5f¹','7s¹','6d¹','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82004,'chemistry','Arrange the following in the increasing order of their ionic radi?','Cf⁺³<Cm⁺³<Pu⁺³<U⁺³','Cf⁺³ (98 pm) < Cm⁺³ (99 pm) < Pu⁺³ (100 pm) < U⁺³ (103 pm) is the correct order of ionic radi.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 237; Table 8.10)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Electronic Configuration and Ionic Size',NULL,'3','order','1,Cf⁺³','2,Cm⁺³','3,Pu⁺³','4,U⁺³','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82005,'chemistry','Which of the following elements have half-filled f-subshell?','Am and Cm','The outer electronic configuration of Am is 5f⁷ 7s² and Cm is 5s⁷ 6d¹ 7s²\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 237; Table 8.10)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Electronic Configuration and Ionic Size',NULL,'2','mcq','Am and No','Fm and Cm','Th and Pu','Am and Cm','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82006,'chemistry','Arrange the following in decreasing order of their ionic radius?','Th⁺⁴>Np⁺⁴>Bk⁺⁴>Cf⁺⁴','Th⁺⁴ (99 pm) > Np⁺⁴ (92 pm) > Bk⁺⁴ (87 pm) > Cf⁺⁴ (86 pm) is the correct order of ionic radi \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 237; Table 8.10)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Electronic Configuration and Ionic Size',NULL,'3','order','1,Th⁺⁴','2,Np⁺⁴','3,Bk⁺⁴','4,Cf⁺⁴','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82007,'chemistry','What is outer electronic configuration of Cm?','5f⁷ 6d¹ 7s²','The outer electronic configuration of Cm is 5f⁷ 6d¹ 7s²\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 237; Table 8.10)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Electronic Configuration and Ionic Size',NULL,'2','mcq','5f⁷ 7s²','5f⁷ 6d¹ 7s²','5f⁷ 6d²','5f⁷ 6d¹','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82008,'chemistry','Which of the following has maximum unpaired electrons?','Bk⁺⁴','Bk⁺⁴ having outer electronic configuration as 5f⁷ has the maximum unpaired electrons in its f subshell among the following options.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 237; Table 8.10)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Electronic Configuration and Ionic Size',NULL,'2','mcq','Bk⁺⁴','Pu⁺⁴','Am⁺⁴','No⁺⁴','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82009,'chemistry','What is symbol for element with atomic number 99?','Es','Eienstenium is the element with atomic number 99 has symbol Es.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 237; Table 8.10)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Electronic Configuration and Ionic Size',NULL,'1','mcq','Eu','Es','Er','U','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82010,'chemistry','At which element does actinioid series end?','Lr','Element with atomic number 103 is the last member of Actinoid series. Lawrencium (Lr) has atomic number 103\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 237; Table 8.10)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Electronic Configuration and Ionic Size',NULL,'1','mcq','Es','Md','Lr','No','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82011,'chemistry','The actinoids are radioactive elements and the ___ members have relatively ____ half-lives.','earlier, long','The actinoids are radioactive elements and the earlier members have relatively long half-lives, the latter ones have half-life values ranging from a day to 3 minutes for lawrencium (Z =103).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 238; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Electronic Configuration and Ionic Size',NULL,'1','mcq','earlier, long','earlier, short','latter, long','latter, short','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82012,'chemistry','From which element onwards electrons are filled to 5f orbital?','Pa','The fourteen electrons are formally added to 5f, though not in thorium (Z = 90) but from Pa onwards the 5f orbitals are complete at element 103.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 238; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Electronic Configuration and Ionic Size',NULL,'2','mcq','Th','Pa','U','Np','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82013,'chemistry','Assertion: 5f electrons can participate in bonding to a far greater extent.\n\nReason: 5f orbitals does not resemble the 4f orbitals in their angular part of wave function.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Although the 5f orbitals resemble the 4f orbitals in their angular part of the wave-function, they are not as buried as 4f orbitals and hence 5f electrons can participate in bonding to a far greater extent.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 238; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Electronic Configuration and Ionic Size',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82014,'chemistry','There is a gradual ____ in the size of atoms or M³⁺ ions across the actinoid series.','decrease, M³⁺','There is a gradual  in the size of atoms or M³⁺ ions across the actinoid series. This may be referred to as the actinoid contraction (like lanthanoid contraction).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 238; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Electronic Configuration and Ionic Size',NULL,'1','mcq','increase, M²⁺','decrease, M²⁺','increase, M³⁺','decrease, M³⁺','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82101,'chemistry','Actinoid contraction is ___ from element to element as compared to lanthanoid contraction.','greater','Actinoid contraction (like lanthanoid contraction) is, however, greater from element to element in this series resulting from poor shielding by 5f electrons.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 238; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Oxidation states',NULL,'1','mcq','greater','lesser','same','slightly lesser','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82102,'chemistry','Assertion: There is greater range of oxidation states in actinoids.\n\nReason The energies of 5f, 6d and 7s are comparable.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','There is a greater range of oxidation states, which is in part attributed to the fact that the 5f, 6d and 7s levels are of comparable energies.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 238; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Oxidation states',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82103,'chemistry','Which oxidation is shown by actinoids in general?','3+','Actinoids, in general, show +3 oxidation state.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 238; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Oxidation states',NULL,'1','mcq','2+','4+','6+','3+','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82104,'chemistry','The elements, in the ___ half of the actinoid series frequently exhibit _____ oxidation states.','first, higher','The elements, in the first half of the actinoid series frequently exhibit higher oxidation states.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 238; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Oxidation states',NULL,'1','mcq','first, lower','second, higher','first, higher','second, lower','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82105,'chemistry','Which of the following is false?','The distribution of oxidation states is same for former and later actinoids','The actinoids resemble the lanthanoids in having more compounds in +3 state than in the +4 state. However, +3 and +4 ions tend to hydrolyse. Because the distribution of oxidation states among the actinoids is so uneven and so different for the former and later elements, it is unsatisfactory to review their chemistry in terms of oxidation states.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 238; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Oxidation states',NULL,'2','mcq','The actinoids resemble the lanthanoids in having more compounds in +3 state than in the +4 state.','The +3 and +4 ions of actinoids tend to hydrolyse.','It is unsatisfactory to review actinoids chemistry in terms of oxidation states.','The distribution of oxidation states is same for former and later actinoids','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82106,'chemistry','Which elements have highest number of possible oxidation states?','Np and Pu','Neptunium (Np) and Plutonium (Pu)  have 5 oxidation states i e +3, +4, +5, +6, +7\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 238; Table 8.11)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Oxidation states',NULL,'2','mcq','Np and Pu','U and Pu','Cm and Am','Am and Pa','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82107,'chemistry','Which of the following elements does not show +3 oxidation state?','Th','Thorium doesn\'t show +3 oxidation state. All the other elements in the options show +3 oxidation state\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 238; Table 8.11)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Oxidation states',NULL,'2','mcq','U','Md','Lr','Th','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82108,'chemistry','Which oxidation states are shown by Am?','3+, 4+, 5+, 6+','Americium (Am) shows +3, +4, +5, +6 oxidation states\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 238; Table 8.11)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Oxidation states',NULL,'1','mcq','3+ and 4+ only','3+ only','3+, 4+ and 5+ only','3+, 4+, 5+, 6+','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82109,'chemistry','Which oxidation states are shown by Cm?','3+ and 4+ only','Curium (Cm) shows 3+ and 4+ oxidation states only\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 238; Table 8.11)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Oxidation states',NULL,'1','mcq','3+ and 4+ only','3+ only','3+, 4+ and 5+ only','3+, 4+, 5+, 6+','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82110,'chemistry','Which element among the following does not show +6 oxidation state?','Md','Mendelevium (Md) shows only +3 oxidation state\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 238; Table 8.11)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Oxidation states',NULL,'1','mcq','U','Pu','Np','Md','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82111,'chemistry','What is the highest oxidation state shown by Uranium?','6+','The highest oxidation state shown by Uranium is +6\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 238; Table 8.11)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Oxidation states',NULL,'1','mcq','4+','7+','6+','3+','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82112,'chemistry','What is the highest oxidation state shown by Bk?','4+','The highest oxidation state shown by Berkelium is +4\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 238; Table 8.11)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Actinoids Oxidation states',NULL,'1','mcq','4+','7+','6+','3+','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82201,'chemistry','How many are true for actinoids?\nA. The actinoid metals are all silvery in appearance but display a variety of structures.\nB. The structural variability is obtained due to irregularities in metallic radii which are far greater than in lanthanoids.\nC. The actinoids are highly reactive metals, especially when finely divided.\nD. The magnetic properties of the actinoids are more complex than those of the lanthanoids.','4','The actinoid metals are all silvery in appearance but display a variety of structures. The structural variability is obtained due to irregularities in metallic radii which are far greater than in lanthanoids.The actinoids are highly reactive metals, especially when finely divided.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 238; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Actinoids',NULL,'2','mcq','2','1','4','3','NEW','12-239','12-113',NULL),
(82202,'chemistry','Action of boiling water on actinoids gives','mixture of oxides and hydride','Action of boiling water on actinoids gives mixture of oxides and hydride \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 239; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Actinoids',NULL,'1','mcq','mixture of oxides and hydride','only oxide','only hydride','hydroxides','NEW','12-239','12-113',NULL),
(82203,'chemistry','Which statement is incorrect about actinoids?','Combination with most non-metals does not takes place at moderate temperature.','Combination with most non metals takes place at moderate temperatures. Hydrochloric acid attacks all metals but most are slightly affected by nitric acid owing to the formation of protective oxide layers; alkalies have no action.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 239; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Actinoids',NULL,'2','mcq','Hydrochloric acid attacks all metals.','Most are slightly affected by nitric acid owing to the formation of protective oxide layers.','Alkalies have no action on them.','Combination with most non-metals does not takes place at moderate temperature.','NEW','12-239','12-113',NULL),
(82204,'chemistry','Although the variation in the magnetic susceptibility of the actinoids with the number of unpaired 5f electrons is roughly parallel to the corresponding results for the lanthanoids, the ___ have _____ values.','latter, higher','Although the variation in the magnetic susceptibility of the actinoids with the number of unpaired 5f electrons is roughly parallel to the corresponding results for the lanthanoids, the latter have higher values.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 239; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Actinoids',NULL,'1','mcq','former, lower','latter, higher','former, higher','latter, lower','NEW','12-239','12-113',NULL),
(82205,'chemistry','Ionisation enthalpy of ____ actinoids are _____ than for the early lanthanoids.','former, lower','It is evident from the behaviour of the actinoids that the ionisation enthalpies of the early actinoids, though not accurately known, but are lower than for the early lanthanoids.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 239; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Actinoids',NULL,'1','mcq','former, lower','latter, higher','former, higher','latter, lower','NEW','12-239','12-113',NULL),
(82206,'chemistry','Assertion: Ionisation enthalpy of early actinoids are lower than for the early lanthanoids.\n\nReason: 5f electrons are more effectively shielded from nucleur charge than 4f electrons of corresponding lanthanoid.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','It is evident from the behaviour of the actinoids that the ionisation enthalpies of the early actinoids, though not accurately known, but are lower than for the early lanthanoids. This is quite reasonable since it is to be expected that when 5f orbitals are beginning to be occupied, they will penetrate less into the inner core of electrons. The 5f electrons, will therefore, be more effectively shielded from the nuclear charge than the 4f electrons of the corresponding lanthanoids.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 239; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Actinoids',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are false.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','NEW','12-239','12-113',NULL),
(82207,'chemistry','A comparison of the actinoids with the lanthanoids, reveals that behaviour similar to that of the lanthanoids is not evident until the _____of the actinoid series.','second half','A comparison of the actinoids with the lanthanoids, reveals that behaviour similar to that of the lanthanoids is not evident until the second half of the actinoid series.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 239; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Actinoids',NULL,'1','mcq','second half','first half','last half','middle','NEW','12-239','12-113',NULL),
(82208,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statement.','The lanthanoid contraction is more important because the chemistry of elements preceeding the actinoids are much less known at the present time.','Even the early actinoids resemble the lanthanoids in showing close similarities with each other and in gradual variation in properties which do not entail change in oxidation state. The lanthanoid and actinoid contractions, have extended effects on the sizes, and therefore, the properties of the elements succeeding them in their respective periods. The lanthanoid contraction is more important because the chemistry of elements succeeding the actinoids are much less known at the present time.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 239; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Actinoids',NULL,'2','mcq','The lanthanoid contraction is more important because the chemistry of elements succeeding the actinoids are much less known at the present time.','Even the early actinoids resemble the lanthanoids in showing close similarities with each other and in gradual variation in properties which do not entail change in oxidation state.','The lanthanoid and actinoid contractions, have extended effects on the sizes, and therefore, the properties of the elements succeeding them in their respective periods.','The lanthanoid contraction is more important because the chemistry of elements preceeding the actinoids are much less known at the present time.','NEW','12-239','12-113',NULL),
(82209,'chemistry','Assertion: Actinoid contraction is greater from element to element than lanthanoid contraction.\n\nReason: 5f electrons themselves provide poor shielding from element to element in the series.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','The 5f electrons are more effectively shielded from nuclear charge. In other words the 5f electrons themselves provide poor shielding from element to element in the series.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 239; Intext Question 8.10)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> General Characterisitics of Actinoids',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-239','12-113',NULL),
(82301,'chemistry','Which of the following is used in pigment industry?','TiO','TiO is used in pigment industry\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 239; Paragraph 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Some Applications of d- and f-Block Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','TiO','MnO₂','Zn','V₂O₅','NEW','12-240','12-114',NULL),
(82302,'chemistry','Which of the following is used in dry battery cells?','MnO₂','MnO₂ is used in dry battery cells.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 239; Paragraph 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Some Applications of d- and f-Block Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','TiO','MnO₂','TiCl₄','V₂O₅','NEW','12-240','12-114',NULL),
(82303,'chemistry','Which of the elements/compound is not used in battery industry?','Ne','The battery industry also requires Zn and Ni/Cd. MnO₂ is used in dry battery cells.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 239; Paragraph 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Some Applications of d- and f-Block Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Ni and Cd','MnO₂','Zn','Ne','NEW','12-240','12-114',NULL),
(82304,'chemistry','Which group\'s element are used as coinage metals?','11','The elements of Group 11 are still worthy of being called the coinage metals.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 239; Paragraph 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Some Applications of d- and f-Block Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','10','11','9','8','NEW','12-240','12-114',NULL),
(82305,'chemistry','The \'silver\' UK coins are alloy of','Cu and Ni','The ‘silver’ UK coins are a Cu/Ni alloy.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 240; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Some Applications of d- and f-Block Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Ni and Cd','Cu and Ni','Ag and Au','Ag and Fe','NEW','12-240','12-114',NULL),
(82306,'chemistry','Which of the following is active component of Ziegler catalysts?','TiCl₄','TiCl₄ with Al(CH₃)₃ forms the basis of the Ziegler catalysts used to manufacture polyethylene (polythene).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 240; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Some Applications of d- and f-Block Elements',NULL,'2','mcq','TiO','MnO₂','TiCl₄','V₂O₅','NEW','12-240','12-114',NULL),
(82307,'chemistry','Which of the following catalyses oxidation of sulphur dioxide for production of sulphuric acids?','V₂O₅','V₂O₅ catalyses the oxidation of SO₂ in the manufacture of sulphuric acid.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 240; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Some Applications of d- and f-Block Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','TiO','MnO₂','Zn','V₂O₅','NEW','12-240','12-114',NULL),
(82308,'chemistry','Which catalyst is used in Haber process?','Fe','Iron catalysts are used in the Haber process for the production of ammonia from N₂/H₂ mixtures.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 240; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Some Applications of d- and f-Block Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Fe','Ni','Zn','V₂O₅','NEW','12-240','12-114',NULL),
(82309,'chemistry','Which complexes are used for the polymerisation of alkynes and other organic compounds such as benzene?','Ni','Nickel complexes are useful in the polymerisation of alkynes and other organic compounds such as benzene.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 240; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Some Applications of d- and f-Block Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Fe','Ni','Zn','V₂O₅','NEW','12-240','12-114',NULL),
(82310,'chemistry','Match the following with their respective uses.','AgBr-Photographic industry, PdCl₂- Wacker process, Ni-Hydrogenation, TiCl₄-Polyethene','Nickel catalysts enable the hydrogenation of fats to proceed. In the Wacker process the oxidation of ethyne to ethanal is catalysed by PdCl₂ .The photographic industry relies on the special light-sensitive properties of AgBr.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 240; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Some Applications of d- and f-Block Elements',NULL,'3','match','AgBr,Photographic industry','PdCl₂,Wacker process','TiCl₄,Polyethene','Ni,Hydrogenation','NEW','12-240','12-114',NULL),
(82401,'chemistry','Identify the incorrect statement.','The oxidation states of chromium in CrO₄²⁻ and Cr₂O₇²⁻are not the same.','The oxidation states of Cr in CrO₄²⁻ and Cr₂O₇²⁻are same i.e +6\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 218; Paragraph 8.3)\n(NEET 2020)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','The transition metals and their compounds are known for their catalytic activity due to their ability to adopt multiple oxidation states and to form complexes.','Interstitial compounds are those that are formed when small atoms like H,C or N are trapped inside the crystal lattices of metals.','The oxidation states of chromium in CrO₄²⁻ and Cr₂O₇²⁻are not the same.','Cr²⁺ (d⁴) is a stronger reducing agent than Fe²⁺(d⁶) in water.','NEW',NULL,'12-096',NULL),
(82402,'chemistry','Photographic films and plates have an essential ingredient of-','silver bromide','AgBr is highly photosensitive and is used as an ingredient for photographic films and plates.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 240; Paragraph 1)\n(AIPMT 1989)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','silver nitrate','silver bromide','sodium chloride','oleic acid.','NEW','12-239','12-113',NULL),
(82403,'chemistry','When neutral or faintly alkaline KMnO₄ is treated with potassium iodide, iodide ion is converted into \'X\'. \'X\' is-','IO₃⁻','In neutral or faintly alkaline solutions :\n2MnO₄⁻ + H₂O + I⁻ → 2MnO₂ + 2OH⁻ + IO₃⁻\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 234; Paragraph 2)\n(Odisha NEET 2019)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','IO⁻','IO₄⁻','IO₃⁻','I₂','NEW','12-234','12-108',NULL),
(82404,'chemistry','The manganate and permanganate ions are tetrahedral due to-','the π-bonding involves overlap of p-orbitals of oxygen with d-orbitals of manganese','In manganate and permanganate ions, p-bonding takes place by overlap of p-orbitals of oxygen with d-orbitals of manganese.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 233; Paragraph 3)\n(NEET 2019)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','the π-bonding involves overlap of d-orbitals of oxygen with d-orbitals of manganese','the π-bonding involves overlap of p-orbitals of oxygen with d-orbitals of manganese','there is no π-bonding','the π-bonding involves overlap of p-orbitals of oxygen with p-orbitals of manganese','NEW','12-233','12-107',NULL),
(82405,'chemistry','Which one of the following ions exhibits d-d transition and paramagnetism as well?','MnO₄²⁻','In MnO₄⁻ , Mn⁺⁷ (n=0) diamagnetic\nIn MnO₄²⁻,  Mn⁺⁶ (n=1)  paramagnetic\nIn CrO₄²⁻ , Cr⁺⁶ (n=0) diamagnetic\nIn Cr₂O₇²⁻,  Cr⁺⁶  (n=0) diamagnetic\nIn MnO₄²⁻, one unpaired electron (n) is present in d-orbital so, d-d transition is possible.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 233; Paragraph 3)\n(NEET 2018)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','MnO₄⁻','MnO₄²⁻','CrO₄²⁻','Cr₂O₇²⁻','NEW','12-227','12-101',NULL),
(82406,'chemistry','The reason for greater range of oxidation states in actinoids is attributed to-','5f, 6d and 7s levels having comparable energies','Actinoids have a greater range of oxidation states due to comparable energies of 5f, 6d and 7s orbitals. Hence, all their electrons can take part in bond formation.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 238; Paragraph 8.6.3 )\n(NEET 2017)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','actinoid contraction','5f, 6d and 7s levels having comparable energies','the radioactive nature of actinoids.','4f and 5d levels being close in energies','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82407,'chemistry','Which one of the following statements related to lanthanons is incorrect?','All the lanthanons are much more reactive than aluminium.','The first few members of the lanthanoid series are quite reactive, almost like calcium. However, with increasing atomic number, their behaviour becomes similar to that of aluminium.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235; Paragraph 8.5.3  8.5.4)\n(NEET-II 2016)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Europium shows +2 oxidation state.','The basicity decreases as the ionic radius decreases from Pr to Lu.','All the lanthanons are much more reactive than aluminium.','Ce(+4) solutions are widely used as oxidizing agent in volumetric analysis.','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(82408,'chemistry','Which one of the following statements is correct when SO₂ is passed through acidified K₂Cr₂O₇ solution?','Green Cr₂(SO₄)₃ is formed.','K₂Cr₂O₇ + H₂SO₄ + 3SO₂ → K₂SO₄+ Cr₂(SO₄)₃ (Green) + H₂O\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 2)\n(NEET-I 2016)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','SO₂ is reduced.','Green Cr₂(SO₄)₃ is formed.','The solution turns blue.','The solution is decolourised.','NEW','-',NULL,NULL),
(82409,'chemistry','Gadolinium belongs to 4f series. Its atomic number is 64. Which of the following is the correct electronic configuration of gadolinium?','[Xe] 4f⁷ 5d¹ 6s²','Gd has electronic configuration [Xe] 4f⁷ 5d¹ 6s².\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Table 8.9)\n(NEET 2015, AIPMT 1997)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','[Xe] 4f⁷ 5d¹ 6s²','[Xe] 4f⁹ 5s¹','[Xe] 4f⁸ 6d²','[Xe] 4f⁶ 5d² 6s²','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(82410,'chemistry','Because of lanthanoid contraction, which of the following pairs of elements have nearly same atomic radii? (Numbers in the parenthesis are atomic numbers)','Zr and Hf have about the same radius','Zr and Hf have nearly same radii due to lanthanoid contraction.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 219; Paragraph 8.3.2)\n(NEET 2015)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Zr and Hf have about the same radius','Zr and Zn have the same oxidation states','Zr and Y have about the same radius','Zr and Nb have similar oxidation state.','NEW','12-219','12-093',NULL),
(82411,'chemistry','Magnetic moment 2.84 B.M. is given by','Ni⁺²','μ = ✓n(n+2)  [Where n is no.of unpaired electrons]\n2.84 = [✓n(n+2)] . By solving it. we get n = 2 and Ni⁺² has 2 unpaired electrons in d-orbitals\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 228; Table 8.7)\n(NEET 2015)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ni⁺²','Mn⁺²','Cr⁺³','Ti⁺³','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(82412,'chemistry','Reason of lanthanoid contraction is-','negligible screening effect of ‘f ’-orbitals','Due to poor shielding effect of 4f-orbitals, nucleus will exert a strong attraction and size of atom or ion goes on decreasing as move in the series with increase in atomic number.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 219; Paragraph 8.3.2)\n(NEET 2014)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','increasing nuclear charge','negligible screening effect of ‘f ’-orbitals','decreasing nuclear charge','decreasing screening effect.','NEW','12-219','12-093',NULL),
(82413,'chemistry','Which of the following lanthanoid ions is diamagnetic?','Yb⁺²','Electronic configuration of the given ions are-\nSm⁺² (Z=62) - [Xe]4f⁶\nEu⁺² (Z=63) - [Xe]4f⁷\nCe⁺² (Z=58) - [Xe]4f²\nYb⁺² (Z=70) - [Xe]4f¹⁴\nOnly Yb⁺² is diamagnetic as it has no unpaired electrons. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Table 8.9)\n(NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Yb⁺²','Eu⁺²','Ce⁺²','Sm⁺²','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(82414,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about the interstitial compounds is incorrect?','They are chemically reactive.','Intersitital compounds are generally chemically inert.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 230; Paragraph 8.3.13)\n(NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','They are much harder than the pure metal.','They have higher melting points than the pure metal.','They are chemically reactive.','They retain metallic conductivity.','NEW','12-230','12-104',NULL),
(82415,'chemistry','Sc (Z = 21) is a transition element but Zn (Z = 30) is not because-','in case of Sc, 3d orbitals are partially filled but in Zn these are filled','Sc (Z = 21) has incompletely filled 3d-orbitals in its ground state (3d¹), it is considered as a transition element but Zn (Z = 30) has completely filled d-orbitals (3d¹⁰) in its ground state and its common oxidation state (+2), thus, it is not considered as a transition element.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 215; Paragraph 3)\n(Karnataka NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','both Sc³⁺ and Zn²⁺ ions are colourless and form white compounds','in case of Sc, 3d orbitals are partially filled but in Zn these are filled','last electron is assumed to be added to 4s level in case of Zn','both Sc and Zn do not exhibit variable oxidation states','NEW','12-215','12-089',NULL),
(82416,'chemistry','Which of the following ions has electronic configuration [Ar] 3d⁶?\n(At. nos. Mn = 25, Fe = 26, Co = 27, Ni = 28)','Co³⁺','The electronic configuration of the given ions is:\nNi³⁺ : [Ar] 3d⁷ 4s⁰\nMn³⁺ : [Ar] 3d⁴ 4s⁰\nFe³⁺ : [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s⁰\nCo³⁺ : [Ar] 3d⁶ 4s⁰ \nThus, Co³⁺ is the ion with the desired configuration.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 216; Table 8.1)\n(AIPMT 2010)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Ni³⁺','Mn³⁺','Fe³⁺','Co³⁺','NEW','12-216','12-090',NULL),
(82417,'chemistry','Among the following series of transition metal ions, the one where all metal ions have 3d² electronic configuration is-\n[At. nos. Ti = 22, V = 23, Cr = 24, Mn = 25]','Ti²⁺, V³⁺, Cr⁴⁺, Mn⁵⁺','₂₂Ti = [Ar]  3d² 4s² = Ti²⁺ = 3d² \n₂₃V = [Ar] 3d³ 4s² = V³⁺ = 3d² \n₂₄Cr = [Ar]  3d⁴ 4s² = Cr⁴⁺ = 3d² \n₂₅Mn = [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s² =, Mn⁵⁺ = 3d² \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 216; Table 8.2)\n(AIPMT 2004)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ti³⁺, V²⁺, Cr³⁺, Mn⁴⁺','Ti⁺, V⁴⁺, Cr⁶⁺, Mn⁷⁺','Ti⁴⁺, V³⁺, Cr²⁺, Mn³⁺','Ti²⁺, V³⁺, Cr⁴⁺, Mn⁵⁺','NEW','12-216','12-090',NULL),
(82418,'chemistry','Which of the following configuration is correct for iron?','1s², 2s², 2p⁶, 3s², 3p⁶, 4s², 3d⁶','(1s², 2s², 2p⁶, 3s², 3p⁶, 4s², 3d⁶ ) is the correct  configuration  for iron.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 216; Table 8.1)\n(AIPMT 1999)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1s², 2s², 2p⁶, 3s², 3p⁶, 4s², 3d⁷','1s², 2s², 2p⁶, 3s², 3p⁶, 4s², 3d⁵','1s², 2s², 2p⁶, 3s², 3p⁶','1s², 2s², 2p⁶, 3s², 3p⁶, 4s², 3d⁶','NEW','12-216','12-090',NULL),
(82419,'chemistry','Which of the following has more unpaird d-electrons?','Fe²⁺','The configuration for  N³⁺ : (1s², 2s², 2p³ ) . Thus, 0 unpaired electrons in d orbital\nThe configuration for  Fe²⁺ : (1s², 2s², 2p⁶, 3s², 3p⁶, 4s², 3d⁶ ). Thus, 4 unpaired electrons in d orbital\nThe configuration for  Zn⁺ : (1s², 2s², 2p⁶, 3s², 3p⁶, 4s¹, 3d¹⁰ ). Thus, 0 unpaired electrons in d orbital\nThe configuration for  Cu⁺ : (1s², 2s², 2p⁶, 3s², 3p⁶, 4s⁰, 3d¹⁰ ). Thus, 0 unpaired electrons in d orbital\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 216; Table 8.1)\n(AIPMT 1999)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','N³⁺','Fe²⁺','Zn⁺','Cu⁺','NEW','12-216','12-090',NULL),
(82420,'chemistry','The electronic configuration of transition elements is exhibited by-','ns² (n – 1)d¹⁻¹⁰','The electronic configuration of transition elements is exhibited by ns² (n – 1)d¹⁻¹⁰ \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 216; Table 8.1)\n(AIPMT 1996)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','ns¹','ns² np⁵','ns² (n – 1)d¹⁻¹⁰','ns² (n – 1)d¹⁰','NEW','12-216','12-090',NULL),
(82421,'chemistry','The electronic configurations of four elements are given. Which element does not belong to the same family as others?','[Ne] 3s² 3p⁵','[Ne] 3s² 3p⁵ is the electronic configuration of a p-block element whereas other configurations are those of d-block elements.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 216; Table 8.1)\n(AIPMT 1989)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','[Xe] 4f¹⁴ 5d¹⁰ 6s²','[Kr] 4d¹⁰ 5s²','[Ne] 3s² 3p⁵','[Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s²','NEW','12-216','12-090',NULL),
(82422,'chemistry','Match the metal ions given in Column-I with the spin magnetic moments of the ions given in Column-II and assign the correct code :','Co³⁺-✓24 B.M, Cr³⁺-✓15 B.M, Fe³⁺-✓35 B.M, Ni²⁺-✓8 B.M','The correct match is Co³⁺ - ✓24 B.M, Cr³⁺ - ✓15 B.M, Fe³⁺ - ✓35 B.M, Ni²⁺ - ✓8 B.M\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 228; Table 8.7)\n(NEET 2018)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','match','Co³⁺,✓24 B.M','Cr³⁺,✓15 B.M','Fe³⁺,✓35 B.M','Ni²⁺,✓8 B.M','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(82423,'chemistry','Which of the following processes does not involve oxidation of iron?','Formation of Fe(CO)₅ from Fe.','Oxidation number of Fe in Fe(CO)₅ is zero.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 222; Paragraph 8.3.4)\n(NEET 2015 cancelled)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Formation of Fe(CO)₅ from Fe.','Liberation of H₂ from steam by iron at high temperature.','Rusting of iron sheets.','Decolourisation of blue CuSO₄ solution by iron.','NEW','12-222','12-096',NULL),
(82424,'chemistry','Identify the alloy containing a non-metal as a constituent in it.','Steel','Invar ⇒ Ni(metal) + Fe(metal)\nSteel ⇒ C(non-metal) + Fe(metal)\nBell ⇒ Cu(metal) + Sn(metal) + Fe(metal)\nBronze ⇒ Cu(metal) + Sn(metal)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 230; Paragraph  8.3.14)\n(NEET 2012)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Invar','Steel','Bell metal','Bronze','NEW','12-230','12-104',NULL),
(82425,'chemistry','The catalytic activity of transition metals and their compounds is ascribed mainly to-','their ability to adopt variable oxidation states','The transition metals and their compounds are known for their catalytic activity. This activity is ascribed to their ability to adopt multiple oxidation states and to form complexes.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 230; Paragraph 8.3.12)\n(Mains 2012)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','their magnetic behaviour','their unfilled d-orbitals','their ability to adopt variable oxidation states','their chemical reactivity.','NEW','12-230','12-104',NULL),
(82426,'chemistry','Which one of the following does not correctly represent the correct order of the property indicate against it?','Ti < V < Cr < Mn : increasing melting points','Element : Ti < V < Cr < Mn.  No. of oxidation states : +3 +4 +5 +6 . Hence, given order is correct.\nMelting point order : Mn (1245°C) < Ti (1668°C) < Cr (1875°C) < V (1900°C) .\n2nd ionisation enthalpy order: Ti (1309 kJ/mol) < V (1414 kJ/mol) < Mn (1509 kJ/mol ) < Cr (1592 kJ/mol)\nMagnetic moment (m) = μ = ✓n(n + 2) \nFor Ti³⁺  n=1  μ = ✓1(1 + 2) = ✓3\nFor V³⁺   n=2  μ = ✓2(2 + 2) = ✓8\nFor Cr³⁺   n=3  μ = ✓3(3 + 2) = ✓15\nFor Mn³⁺  n=4  μ = ✓4(4 + 2) = ✓24 Therefore, Ti³⁺ < V³⁺ < Cr³⁺ < Mn³⁺ is the correct increasing magnetic moment order\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 216; Table 8.1)\n(Mains 2012)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Ti < V < Cr < Mn; increasing number of oxidation states','Ti³⁺ < V³⁺ < Cr³⁺ < Mn³⁺ : increasing magnetic moment','Ti < V < Cr < Mn : increasing melting points','Ti < V < Mn < Cr : increasing 2nd ionization enthalpy','NEW','12-218','12-092',NULL),
(82427,'chemistry','Four successive members of the first series of the transition metals are listed below. For which one of them the standard potential [E°(M²⁺/M)] value has a positive sign?','Cu (Z = 29)','Element               Co          Ni         Cu         Fe\n[E°(M²⁺/M)] (V)   – 0.28     –0.25     +0.34     –0.44\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 220; Table 8.2)\n(Mains 2012)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Co (Z = 27)','Ni (Z = 28)','Cu (Z = 29)','Fe (Z = 26)','NEW','12-224','12-098',NULL),
(82428,'chemistry','For the four successive transition elements (Cr, Mn, Fe and Co), the stability of +2 oxidation state will be there in which of the following order?\n(At. nos. Cr = 24, Mn = 25, Fe = 26, Co = 27)','Mn > Fe > Cr > Co','Spin correlation and exchange energy gives an electronic configuration a special stability which is greatest for half-filled electronic configurations. Mn²⁺ (d⁵) gets stabilisation due to half-filled configuration. In Fe²⁺ (d⁶) the placing of one extra electron in a subshell destabilises. Placing of 2 electrons in Co²⁺ (d⁷) destabilises it more. Cr²⁺ (d⁴) has one vacant subshell. Fe²⁺ gets more stabilisation compared to Cr²⁺ through exchange energy. So, the order is as follows : Mn > Fe > Cr > Co.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 222; Paragraph 8.3.4)\n(Mains 2011)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Mn > Fe > Cr > Co','Fe > Mn > Co > Cr','Co > Mn > Fe > Cr','Cr > Mn > Co > Fe','NEW','12-222','12-096',NULL),
(82429,'chemistry','Which of the following ions will exhibit colour in aqueous solutions?','Ti³⁺ (Z = 22)','Ions which have unpaired electrons exhibit colour in aqueous solution. Ti³⁺ has an outer electronic configuration of 4s⁰ 3d¹ , i.e., 1 unpaired electron. Thus, its solution will be coloured. Others are colourless due to empty or completely filled outermost orbitals.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 229; Paragraph 8.3.10)\n(AIPMT 2010)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','La³⁺ (Z = 57)','Ti³⁺ (Z = 22)','Lu³⁺ (Z = 71)','Sc³⁺ (Z = 21)','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(82430,'chemistry','Which of the following pairs has the same size?','Zr⁴⁺, Hf⁴⁺','Zr⁴⁺, Hf⁴⁺ belong to group IVB. But, Hf⁴⁺ has same size as Zr⁴⁺, due to the addition of 14 lanthanide elements before it in which electrons are added into the f-subshell which poorly shield the outer electrons and contraction in size occurs.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 219;Paragraph 8.3.2)\n(AIPMT 2010)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Fe²⁺, Ni²⁺','Zr⁴⁺, Ti⁴⁺','Zr⁴⁺, Hf⁴⁺','Zn²⁺, Hf⁴⁺','NEW','12-219','12-093',NULL),
(82431,'chemistry','Which one of the elements with the following outer orbital configurations may exhibit the largest number of oxidation states?','3d⁵ 4s²','Greater the number of valence electrons, more will be the number of oxidation states exhibited by the element. \n3d⁵ 4s¹,can show a maximum of 6 oxidation states.\n3d⁵ 4s² can show a maximum of 7 oxidation states.\n3d² 4s² can show a maximum of 4 oxidation states.\n3d³ 4s² can show a maximum of 5 oxidation states.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 222; Paragraph 8.3.4)\n(AIPMT 2009)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','3d⁵ 4s¹','3d⁵ 4s²','3d² 4s²','3d³ 4s²','NEW','12-222','12-096',NULL),
(82432,'chemistry','The correct order of decreasing second ionisation enthalpy of Ti(22), V(23), Cr(24) and Mn(25) is-','Cr > Mn > V > Ti','Electronic configuration of the given elements are-\nCr : [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹\nMn : [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s²\nV : [Ar] 3d³ 4s²\nTi : [Ar] 3d² 4s²\nIn general, ionization potential (both 1st and 2nd) increases from left to right across the period due to increase in effective nuclear charge. On this basis, the second IP values should exhibit the trend :Mn > Cr > V > Ti . But the actual observed order is : Cr > Mn > V > Ti Practically, only chromium is exceptional and rest others show the normal trend. This exceptional behaviour of chromium is due to the stable configuration (3d⁵) that it achieves after the loss of first electron.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 221; Paragraph 8.3.3)\n(AIPMT 2008)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Mn > Cr > Ti > V','Ti > V > Cr > Mn','Cr > Mn > V > Ti','V > Mn > Cr > Ti','NEW','12-221','12-095',NULL),
(82433,'chemistry','In which of the following pairs are both the ions coloured in aqueous solution?\n(At. no. : Sc = 21, Ti = 22, Ni = 28, Cu = 29,Co = 27)','Ni²⁺, Ti³⁺','Sc : [Ar] 3d¹ 4s²    Sc³⁺ : [Ar]    Colourless \nTi : [Ar] 3d² 4s²      Ti³⁺ : [Ar] 3d¹ Coloured\nNi : [Ar] 3d⁸ 4s²     Ni²⁺ : [Ar] 3d⁸  Coloured\nCu : [Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹  Cu⁺ : [Ar] 3d¹⁰ Colourless\nCo : [Ar] 3d⁷ 4s²   Co²⁺ : [Ar] 3d⁷ Coloured \nTi³⁺, Ni²⁺, Co²⁺ are coloured due to presence of unpaired ellectrons.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 229; Paragraph 8.3.10)\n(AIPMT 2006)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ni²⁺, Cu⁺','Ni²⁺, Ti³⁺','Sc³⁺, Ti³⁺','Sc³⁺, Co²⁺','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(82434,'chemistry','Four successive members of the first row transition elements are listed below with their atomic numbers. Which one of them is expected to have the highest third ionisation enthalpy?','Manganese (Z = 25)','I.E₃ (Mn)    >      I.E₃ (Cr)    >     I.E₃ (Fe)      >      I.E₃ (V)\n3260 kJ/mol        2990 kJ/mol     2962 kJ/mol       2833 kJ/mol\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 221; Paragraph 8.3.3)\n(AIPMT 2005)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Vanadium (Z = 23)','Chromium (Z = 24)','Manganese (Z = 25)','Iron (Z = 26)','NEW','12-221','12-095',NULL),
(82435,'chemistry','The aqueous solution containing which one of the following ions will be colourless?\n(Atomic number : Sc = 21, Fe = 26,Ti = 22, Mn = 25)','Sc³⁺','If the transition metal ion has unpaired electron then it shows colour.\nSc³⁺ : [Ar] \nTi³⁺ : [Ar] 3d¹\nMn²⁺ : [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s⁰\nFe²⁺ : [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹\nSc³⁺ does not contain unpaired electron, hence it will not undergo d – d transition and do not show colour.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 229; Paragraph 8.3.10)\n(AIPMT 2005)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Sc³⁺','Fe²⁺','Mn²⁺','Ti³⁺','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(82436,'chemistry','The catalytic activity of  transition metals is due to:','Variable oxidation states','The transition elements, on account of their variable valency, are able to form unstable intermediate compounds very readily.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 230; Paragraph 8.3.12)\n(AIPMT 2003)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','High enthalpy of atomization','Paramagnetic behaviour','Colour of hydrated ions','Variable oxidation states','NEW','12-230','12-104',NULL),
(82437,'chemistry','The basic character of the transition metal monoxides follows the order-\n(Atomic no’s. Ti = 22, V = 23, Cr = 24, Fe = 26)','TiO > VO > CrO > FeO','The order of basicity of transition metal monoxides is, TiO > VO > CrO > FeO.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 226; Table 8.6)\n(AIPMT 2003)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','VO > CrO > TiO > FeO','CrO > VO > FeO > TiO','TiO > FeO > VO > CrO','TiO > VO > CrO > FeO','NEW','12-226','12-100',NULL),
(82438,'chemistry','Which of the following shows maximum number of oxidation states?','Mn','Each of the element in group III B to VII B can show the maximum oxidation state equal to its group number. Mn is in group seven shows a maximum oxidation state of +7 in KMnO₄\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 222; Paragraph 8.3.4)\n(AIPMT 2002, 2000, 1994)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cr','Fe','Mn','V','NEW','12-222','12-096',NULL),
(82439,'chemistry','Which ion is colourless?','Sc³⁺','The general electronic configuration is given by Sc : [Ar] 3d¹ 4s² . In Sc³⁺ : [Ar] there is no unpaired ‘d’ electrons, therefore it is colourless in its solution.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 229; Paragraph 8.3.10)\n(AIPMT 2000)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cr⁴⁺','Sc³⁺','Ti³⁺','V³⁺','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(82440,'chemistry','Bell metal is an alloy of-','Cu + Sn','Bell metal ⇒ Cu = 80%, Sn = 20% . It is used for making bells, utensils, etc.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 230; Paragraph 8.3.14)\n(AIPMT 1999)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Cu + Zn','Cu + Sn','Cu + Pb','Cu + Ni','NEW','12-230','12-104',NULL),
(82441,'chemistry','In which of the following compounds transition metal has zero oxidation state?','[Fe(CO)₅]','In iron carbonyl, the oxidation number of ‘Fe’ is zero.\n[Fe(CO)₅]: x + 5 × 0 = 0 ⇒ x = 0\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 216; Paragraph 8.3.4)\n(AIPMT 1999)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','NOClO₄','NH₂NH₂','CrO₅','[Fe(CO)₅]','NEW','12-223','12-097',NULL),
(82442,'chemistry','Which one of the following ionic species will impart colour to an aqueous solution?','Cr³⁺','Cr³⁺ ion has three unpaired electrons in its valence shell, so it imparts colour to an aqueous solution.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 229; Paragraph 8.3.10)\n(AIPMT 1998)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Zn²⁺','Cu⁺','Ti⁴⁺','Cr³⁺','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(82443,'chemistry','A transition element X has a configuration [Ar] 3d⁴ in its +3 oxidation state. Its atomic number is-','25','The metal atom will have three more electrons. Therefore, the atomic number of the metal = 18 + 4 + 3 = 25\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 216; Paragraph 8.3.4)\n(AIPMT 1996)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','22','19','25','26','NEW','12-216','12-090',NULL),
(82444,'chemistry','The mercury is the only metal which is liquid at 0°C. This is due to its-','high ionization energy','Very high ionisation energy of Hg makes it difficult for electrons to participate in metallic bonding.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 218; Paragraph 8.3.1)\n(AIPMT 1995)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','high vapour pressure','weak metallic bond','high ionization energy','both (b) and (c).','NEW','12-218','12-092',NULL),
(82445,'chemistry','Amongst the TiF₆²⁻ CoF₆³⁻, Cu₂Cl₂, NiCl₄²⁻, which are the colourless species? (Atomic number of Ti = 22, Co = 27, Cu = 29, Ni = 28)','TiF₆²⁻,  Cu₂Cl₂,','In TiF₆²⁻, titanium is in +4 oxidation state. In Cu₂Cl₂, , the copper is in +1 state. Thus, in both cases, transition from one d-orbital to other is not possible.\nTi : [Ar] 3d² 4s²  Ti⁴⁺ : [Ar] 3d⁰ \nCu : [Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹  Cu⁺ : [Ar] 3d¹⁰ \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 229; Table 8.3.10)\n(AIPMT 1995)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CoF₆³⁻, NiCl₄²⁻','TiF₆²⁻,  Cu₂Cl₂,','NiCl₄²⁻,  Cu₂Cl₂,','CoF₆³⁻,  TiF₆²⁻','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(82446,'chemistry','Name the gas that can readily decolourise acidified KMnO₄ solution.','SO₂','SO₂ readily decolourises pink violet colour of acidified KMnO₄ solution.\n2KMnO₄ + 5SO₂ + 2H₂O → K₂SO₄ + 2MnSO₄ + 2H₂SO₄\nPink violet                                         (Colourless)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 233; Paragraph last)\n(NEET 2017)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','SO₂','NO₂','P₂O₅','CO₂','NEW','12-233','12-233',NULL),
(82447,'chemistry','Assuming complete ionisation, same moles of which of the following compounds will require the least amount of acidified KMnO₄  for complete oxidation?','FeSO₄','KMnO₄ (Mn⁷⁺) changes to Mn²⁺ i.e., number of electrons involved per mole of KMnO₄ is 5.\na) FeSO₃\nFe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ (No. of e⁻s involved = 1)\nSO₃³⁻  →  SO₄²⁻ (No. of e⁻s involved = 2) Total number of e⁻s involved = 1 + 2 = 3\nb) FeC₂O₄\nC₂O₄²⁻→ 2CO₂ (No. of e⁻s involved = 2)\nFe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ (No. of e⁻s involved = 1) Total number of e⁻s involved = 1 + 2 = 3\nc) Fe(NO₂)₂\nFe²⁺ → Fe³⁺  (No. of e⁻s involved = 1)\n2NO₂⁻ → 2NO₃⁻ (No. of e⁻s involved = 4) Total number of e⁻s involved = 1 + 4 = 5\nd) FeSO₄\nFe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ (No. of e⁻s involved = 1)\nTotal number of e⁻s involved = 1\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 233; Paragraph 2, 3 )\n(AIPMT 2015)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','FeSO₃','FeC₂O₄','Fe(NO₂)₂','FeSO₄','NEW','12-233','12-107',NULL),
(82448,'chemistry','The reaction of aqueous KMnO₄ with H₂O₂ in acidic conditions gives-','Mn²⁺ and O₂','Hydrogen peroxide is oxidised to H₂O and O₂.\n2KMnO₄ + 3H₂SO₄ + 5H₂O₂ → K₂SO₄ + 2MnSO₄ + 8H₂O + 5O₂\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph of potassium permangante)\n(AIPMT 2014)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Mn⁴⁺ and O₂','Mn²⁺ and O₂','Mn²⁺ and O₃','Mn⁴⁺ and MnO₂','NEW','12-232','12-106',NULL),
(82449,'chemistry','Which of the statements is not true?','Na₂Cr₂O₇ is preferred over K₂Cr₂O₇ in volumetric analysis.','Potassium dichromate is preferred over sodium dichromate in volumetric analysis, primarily because the latter is hygroscopic nature and therefore, accurate weighing is not possible in normal atmosphere.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 1,2,3)\n(AIPMT 2012)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','On passing H₂S through acidified K₂Cr₂O₇ solution, a milky colour is observed.','Na₂Cr₂O₇ is preferred over K₂Cr₂O₇ in volumetric analysis.','K₂Cr₂O₇ solution in acidic medium is orange.','K₂Cr₂O₇ solution becomes yellow on increasing the pH beyond 7.','NEW','12-232','12-106',NULL),
(82450,'chemistry','Acidified K₂Cr₂O₇ solution turns green when Na₂SO₃ is added to it. This is due to the formation of-','Cr₂(SO₄)₃','K₂Cr₂O₇ + 3Na₂SO₃ + 4H₂SO₄ → 3Na₂SO₄ + K₂SO₄ + Cr₂(SO₄)₃ + 4H₂O\nCr₂O₇²⁻ + 3SO₃²⁻ + 8H⁺ → 3SO₄²⁻ + 2Cr³⁺+ 4H₂O\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 1,2,3)\n(AIPMT 2011)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Cr₂(SO₄)₃','CrO₄²⁻','Cr₂(SO₃)₃','CrSO₄','NEW','12-232','12-106',NULL),
(82451,'chemistry','The number of moles of KMnO₄ reduced by one mole of KI in alkaline medium is-','two','In alkaline medium : 2KMnO₄ + KI + H₂O → 2KOH + 2MnO₂ + KIO₃\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 216; Table 8.1)\n(AIPMT 2005)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','one','two','five','one-fifth','NEW','12-216','12-090',NULL),
(82452,'chemistry','K₂Cr₂O₇ on heating with aqueous NaOH gives-','CrO₄²⁻','K₂Cr₂O₇ + 2NaOH → K₂CrO₄ + Na₂CrO₄ + H₂O\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 216; Table 8.1)\n(AIPMT 1997)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cr₂O₇²⁻','Cr(OH)₂','CrO₄²⁻','Cr(OH)₃','NEW','12-216','12-090',NULL),
(82453,'chemistry','KMnO₄ reacts with oxalic acid according to the equation\n2MnO₄⁻ + 5C₂O₄²⁻ + 16H⁺ → 2Mn²⁺ + 10CO₂ + 8H₂O\nHere 20 mL of 0.1 M KMnO₄ is equivalent to-','50 mL of 0.1 M C₂H₂O₄','2MnO₄⁻ + 5C₂O₄²⁻ + 16H⁺ → 2Mn²⁺ + 10CO₂ + 8H₂O\n2 moles of MnO₄⁻ ≡ 5 moles of C₂O₄²⁻\n20 mL of 0.1 M KMnO₄ = 2 mmol of KMnO₄\nAlso, 50 mL of 0.1 M C₂H₂O₄ ≡ 5 mmol of C₂O₄²⁻\nTherefore, these are equivalent.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 216; Table 8.1)\n(AIPMT 1996)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','50 mL of 0.5 M C₂H₂O₄','20 mL of 0.1 M C₂H₂O₄','20 mL of 0.5 M C₂H₂O₄','50 mL of 0.1 M C₂H₂O₄','NEW','12-234','12-108',NULL),
(82454,'chemistry','The oxidation state of Cr in K₂Cr₂O₇ is-','6+','Let, oxidation state of Cr in K₂Cr₂O₇ is x. Then,\n2 + 2x – 14 = 0\n⇒ 2x = 12    x = +6\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 216; Table 8.1)\n(AIPMT 1998)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','3+','6+','7+','5+','NEW','12-216','12-090',NULL),
(82455,'chemistry','The electronic configurations of Eu (Atomic No.63), Gd (Atomic No. 64) and Tb (Atomic No. 65) are-','[Xe] 4f⁷ 6s², [Xe] 4f⁷ 5d¹ 6s² , [Xe] 4f⁹ 6s²','The electronic configurations are-\nEu (Atomic No.63) - [Xe] 4f⁷ 6s²,\nGd (Atomic No. 64) -  [Xe] 4f⁷ 5d¹ 6s² \nTb (Atomic No. 65) -  [Xe] 4f⁹ 6s²\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Table 8.9)\n(NEET-I 2016)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','[Xe] 4f⁶ 5d¹ 6s² ,[Xe] 4f⁷ 5d¹ 6s², [Xe] 4f⁸ 5d¹ 6s²','[Xe] 4f⁷ 6s², [Xe] 4f⁷ 5d¹ 6s² , [Xe] 4f⁹ 6s²','[Xe] 4f⁷ 6s², [Xe] 4f⁸ 6s² , [Xe] 4f⁸ 5d¹ 6s²','[Xe] 4f⁶ 5d¹ 6s², [Xe] 4f⁷ 5d¹ 6s² , [Xe] 4f⁹ 6s²','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(82456,'chemistry','The incorrect statement among the following is-','Most of the trivalent Lanthanoid ions are colorless in the solid state','Most of the trivalent lanthanoid ions are coloured in the solid state.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Paragraph 8.5.4)\n(NEET 2021)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Actinioid contraction is greater from element to element than lanthanoids\' contraction.','Most of the trivalent Lanthanoid ions are colorless in the solid state','Lanthanoids are good conductors of heat and electricity.','Actinoids are highly reactive metals, especially when finely divided.','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(82457,'chemistry','Zr (Z =40) and Hf (Z =72) have similar atomic and ionic Zr (Z =40) and Hf (Z =72) have similar atomic and ionic radii because of :','lanthanoid contraction','Due to lanthanoid contraction Zr and Hf has similar atomic and ionic radii.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 219; Paragraph 8.3.2)\n(NEET 2021)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','belonging to same group','diagonal relationship','lanthanoid contraction','having similar chemical properties','NEW','12-219','12-093',NULL),
(82458,'chemistry','Gadolinium has a low value of third ionisation enthalpy because of-','high exchange enthalpy','Electronic configuration of Gadolinium- Gd :— [Xe] 4f⁷ 5d¹ 6s². In case of 3rd ionisation enthalpy electron will be removed from 5d and resultant configuration will be [Xe]4f⁷ that is stable electronic configuration as it will have high exchange energy, hence less energy will be required to remove 3rd electron.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235; Paragraph 8.5.2)\n(NEET 2022)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','small size','high exchange enthalpy','high electronegativity','high basic character','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(82459,'chemistry','Which of the following oxidation states is the most common among the lanthanoids?','3','The common stable oxidation state of all the lanthanoids is +3. The oxidation state of +2 and +4 are also exhibited by some of the elements. These oxidation states are only stable in those cases where stable 4f⁰, 4f⁷or 4f¹⁴ configurations are achieved.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235; Paragraph 8.5.3)\n(Mains 2010)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','4','2','5','3','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(82460,'chemistry','Identify the incorrect statement among the following :','As a result of lanthanoid contraction, the properties of 4d series of the transition elements have no similarities with the 5d series of elements.','In each vertical column of transition elements, the elements of second and third transition series resemble each other more closely than the elements of first and second transition series on account of lanthanide contraction. Hence, the properties of elements of 4d series of the transition elements resemble with the properties of the elements of 5d series of the transition elements.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 219; Paragraph 8.3.2)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235; Paragraph 8.5.2)\n(AIPMT  2007)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Lanthanoid contraction is the accumulation of successive shrinkages.','As a result of lanthanoid contraction, the properties of 4d series of the transition elements have no similarities with the 5d series of elements.','Shielding power of 4f electrons is quite weak.','There is a decrease in the radii of the atoms or ions as one proceeds from La to Lu.','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(82461,'chemistry','Lanthanoids are-','14 elements in the sixth period (atomic number = 58 to 71) that are filling the 4f sublevel','As sixth period can accommodate only 18 elements in the table, 14 members of 4f series (atomic number 58 to 71) are separately accommodated in a horizontal row below the periodic table. These are called as lanthanides.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 234; Paragraph 8.5)\n(AIPMT  2004)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','14 elements in the sixth period (atomic no. 90 to 103) that are filling 4f sublevel','14 elements in the seventh period (atomic number = 90 to 103) that are filling 5f sublevel','14 elements in the sixth period (atomic number = 58 to 71) that are filling the 4f sublevel','14 elements in the seventh period (atomic number = 58 to 71) that are filling 4f sublevel.','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(82462,'chemistry','The correct order of ionic radii of Y³⁺, La³⁺, Eu³⁺ and Lu³⁺ is (At. nos. Y = 39, La = 57, Eu = 63, Lu = 71)','Y³⁺ < Lu³⁺ < Eu³⁺ < La³⁺','On going from La³⁺ to Lu³⁺, the ionic radius shrinks from 1.15 Å to 0.93 Å (lanthanide contraction). The radius of La³⁺ is also larger than that of Y³⁺ ion which lies immediately above it in periodic table.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235; Paragraph 8.5.2)\n(AIPMT 2003)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Y³⁺ < La³⁺ < Eu³⁺ < Lu³⁺','Y³⁺ < Lu³⁺ < Eu³⁺ < La³⁺','Lu³⁺ < Eu³⁺ < La³⁺ < Y³⁺','La³⁺ < Eu³⁺ < Lu³⁺ < Y³⁺','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(82463,'chemistry','General electronic configuration of lanthanides is-','(n – 2) f¹⁻¹⁴  (n – 1) s²p⁶ d⁰⁻¹   ns²','The general electronic structure of lanthanides is, (n – 2) f¹⁻¹⁴  (n – 1) s²p⁶ d⁰⁻¹   ns²\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235; Paragraph 8.5.1)\n(AIPMT 2002)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(n – 2) f¹⁻¹⁴  (n – 1) s²p⁶ d⁰⁻¹   ns²','(n – 2) f¹⁰⁻¹⁴  (n – 1) d⁰⁻¹   ns²','(n – 2) f⁰⁻¹⁴  (n – 1) d¹⁰   ns²','(n – 2) d⁰⁻¹  (n – 1) f¹⁻¹⁴   ns²','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(82464,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is not correct?','La(OH)₃ is less basic than Lu(OH)₃.','La(OH)₃ is more basic than Lu(OH)₃. In lanthanides, the basic character of hydroxides decreases as the ionic radius decreases.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Paragraph 8.5.4)\n(AIPMT 2001)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','La(OH)₃ is less basic than Lu(OH)₃.','In lanthanide series ionic radius of Ln³⁺ ion decreases.','La is actually an element of transition series rather lanthanides.','Atomic radius of Zn and Hf are same because of lanthanide contraction.','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(82465,'chemistry','The lanthanide contraction is responsible for the fact that-','Zr and Hf have about the same radius','Due to lanthanide contraction, the elements of second and third transition series i.e., Zr and Hf resemble more with each other than the elements of first and second transition series.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235; Paragraph 8.5.2)\n(AIPMT 1997)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Zr and Hf have about the same radius','Zr and Zn have the same oxidation state','Zr and Y have about the same radius','Zr and Nb have similar oxidation state.','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(82466,'chemistry','Which of the following statements concerning lanthanide elements is false?','Ionic radii of trivalent lanthanides steadily increases with increase in the atomic number.','Ionic radii of trivalent lanthanides decreases with increase in atomic number.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Paragraph 8.5.4)\n(AIPMT 1994)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','All lanthanides are highly dense metals.','More characteristic oxidation state of lanthanide elements is +3.','Lanthanides are separated from one another by ion exchange method.','Ionic radii of trivalent lanthanides steadily increases with increase in the atomic number.','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(82467,'chemistry','Which of the following exhibits only +3 oxidation state?','Ac','U exhibits + 3, + 4, + 5, + 6\nTh exhibits + 3, + 4 ; Ac exhibits + 3 only\nPa exhibits + 3, + 4, + 5\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 238; Table 8.11)\n(Mains 2012)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','U','Th','Ac','Pa','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82468,'chemistry','More number of oxidation states are exhibited by the actinoids than by the lanthanoids. The main reason for this is-','lesser energy difference between 5f and 6d orbitals than that between 4f and 5d orbitals','The 5f-orbitals extend into space beyond the 6s and 6p-orbitals and participate in bonding. This is in direct contrast to the lanthanides where the 4f-orbitals are buried deep inside in the atom, totally shielded by outer orbitals and thus, unable to take part in bonding.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 238; Paragraph 8.6.3)\n(AIPMT 2005, 2006)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','more active nature of the actinoids','more energy difference between 5f and 6d orbitals than that between 4f and 5d orbitals','lesser energy difference between 5f and 6d orbitals than that between 4f and 5d orbitals','greater metallic character of the lanthanoids than that of the corresponding actinoids.','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82469,'chemistry','Which one of the following elements shows maximum number of different oxidation states in its compounds?','Am','‘La’ forms compounds in which its oxidation no. is +3.‘Eu’ and ‘Gd’ exhibit +2 as well as +3 oxidation states and not higher than that, due to stable (f⁷) configuration. whereas ‘Am’ exhibits the oxidation states +3, +4, +5, +6, etc. due to extremely large size and low ionisation energy.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 238; Paragraph 8.6.3)\n(AIPMT 1998)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Gd','La','Eu','Am','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82470,'chemistry','When calomel reacts with NH₄OH, we get-','HgNH₂Cl','When calomel reacts with NH4OH, it turns black due to the formation of a mixture of mercury and ammonium basic mercury (II) chloride.\nHg₂Cl₂ + 2NH₄OH → NH₄Cl + 2H₂O + Hg + HgNH₂Cl\n(AIPMT 1996)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Hg₂O','HgO','HgNH₂Cl','NH₂–Hg–Hg–Cl','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(82471,'chemistry','Which of the following elements is responsible for oxidation of water to O₂ in biological processes?','Mn','Water oxidation is catalyzed by a manganese-containing cofactor contained in photosystem II known as the oxygen-evolving complex. Manganese is an important cofactor, and calcium and chloride are also required for the reaction to occur.\n(AIPMT 1997)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cu','Fe','Mo','Mn','NEW','12-239','12-113',NULL),
(82472,'chemistry','HgCl₂ and I₂ both when dissolved in water containing I⁻ ions, the pair of species formed is-','HgI₄²⁻, I₃⁻','HgCl₂  +  4I⁻  → HgI₄²⁻ + 2CI⁻\nI₂ + I⁻ → I₃⁻\n(NEET 2017)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','HgI₂, I⁻','HgI₄²⁻, I₃⁻','Hg₂I₂, I⁻','HgI₂, I₃⁻','NEW','12-230','12-104',NULL),
(82473,'chemistry','Match the catalyst with the process :','V₂O₅-Oxidation of SO₂ in the manufacture of H₂SO₄, TiCl₄ +Al(CH₃)₃-Polymerisation of ethylene, PdCl₂-The oxidation of ethyne to ethanal, Nickel complexes-Polymerisation of alkynes','The correct match of catalyst with the process is-V₂O₅ - Oxidation of SO₂ in the manufacture of H₂SO₄, TiCl₄ +Al(CH₃)₃ - Polymerisation of ethylene, PdCl₂ - The oxidation of ethyne to ethanal, Nickel complexes, - Polymerisation of alkynes\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 240; Paragraph 1)\n(Odisha NEET 2019)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','match','V₂O₅,Oxidation of SO₂ in the manufacture of H₂SO₄','TiCl₄ +Al(CH₃)₃,Polymerisation of ethylene','PdCl₂,The oxidation of ethyne to ethanal','Nickel complexes,Polymerisation of alkynes','NEW','12-240','12-114',NULL),
(82474,'chemistry','Identify the incorrect statement.','The oxidation states of chromium in CrO₄²⁻ and Cr₂O₇²⁻ are not the same.','The oxidation state of chromium  in CrO₄²⁻ and Cr₂O₇²⁻ are the same i.e. +6','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','The transition metals and their compounds are known for their catalytic activity due to their ability to adopt multiple oxidation states and to form complexes','Interstital compounds are those that are forned when small atoms like H, C or N are trapped inside the crystal lattices of metals','The oxidation states of chromium in CrO₄²⁻ and Cr₂O₇²⁻ are not the same.','Cr²⁺(d⁴) is a stronger reducing agent than Fe²⁺(d⁶) in water','NEW','12-222','12-096',NULL),
(82475,'chemistry','Match the following aspects with the respective metal in the diagram(82475Q)','(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)','The metal which reveals a maximum number of oxidation state → Mn\nexhibits oxidation states from +2 to +7. The common oxidation states are +2, +4, and +7, but the less common +3, +5, and +6 states are easily prepared.\n\nThe metal although placed in 3d block is considered not as a transition element is →→ Zn\nZinc, Cadmium, Mercury are regarded as pseudo-transition elements. ... \n\nThe metal which does not exhibit variable oxidation state is →→ Sc (only +3)\nThe metal which in +1 oxidation state in aqueous solution undergoes disproportionation is →→ Cu\n2Cu⁺ ----- >   Cu + Cu²⁺','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)','(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)','(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)','(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)','NEW','12-223','12-097','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/82475q.png'),
(82476,'chemistry','Match the statements given in the list I with the corresponding element given in list II in the diagram:(82476Q)','(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)','1. The elements which are not considered transition elements are zinc, cadmium, and mercury. This element belongs to group 12 and have a full d¹⁰ configuration in their ground state as well as in their common oxidation states and hence, are not regarded as transition metals.\n\n2. Element which exhibits +3 oxidation state only is scandium. The electronic configuration of Sc is [Ar]3d¹4s². It can lose 3 electrons and obtain a Nobel gas configuration. \n\n3. Mn is the element in the 3d transition series which shows the maximum number of oxidation states. Mn can show +2, +3, +4, +5, +6, and +7.\n\n4. Cr is a strong reducing agent in its +2 oxidation state because its configuration changes from d⁴ to d³ , the latter having a half-filled t₂g. \n\nThus, option 4 is the correct answer.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)','(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)','(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)(i)','(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)','NEW','12-233','12-107','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/82476q.png'),
(82477,'chemistry','A set of ions among the following that have a 3d² eletronic configuration is:','Ti²⁺, V³⁺, Cr⁴⁺, Mn⁵⁺','1. The electronic configuration of Ti(Atomic number = 22) is: [Ar]3d² 4s²\n\nThus, Ti⁺² has configuration [Ar] 3d²\n\n2. The electronic configuration of V(Atomic no. = 23) is : [Ar]3d³ 4s²\n\nThus, V³⁺ has [Ar]3d² configuration.\n\n3. The electronic configuration of Cr(Atomic no. = 24) is : [Ar] 3d⁴ 4s²\n\nSo, Cr⁺⁴ has [Ar]3d² configuration.\n\n4. The electronic configuration of Mn (Atomic no. = 25) is : [Ar]3d⁵ 4s²\n\nSo, Mn⁺⁵ has [Ar]3d² configuration.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Ti⁺, V⁴⁺, Cr⁶⁺, Mn⁷⁺','Ti⁴⁺, V³⁺, Cr ²⁺, Mn³⁺','Ti²⁺, V³⁺, Cr⁴⁺, Mn⁵⁺','Ti³⁺, V²⁺, Cr³⁺, Mn⁴⁺','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(82478,'chemistry','Which of the following is a coloured and paramagnetic compound?','CuF₂','Generally transition element shows colour due to d-d transition. For d-d transition unpaired electron should present. Other reasons also are there for colour of compounds, like MLCT, LMCT etc. Other compounds are also coloured but not paramagnetic. Hence ,  CuF₂ is coloured and paramagnetic.\n\nOther compounds are also coloured but not paramagnetic. See diagram(82478E)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CuF₂','K₂Cr₂O₇','KMnO₄','K₄[Fe(CN)₆]','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(82479,'chemistry','Ion that gives color with water is:','Cr³⁺','1. Cu⁺ has [Ar]3d¹⁰ electronic configuration. It has fully filled electronic configuration so it won\'t give colour with water.\n2. Cr⁺3 has [Ar]3d³ electronic configuration. It has 3 unpaired electrons resulting in d-d transition. So Cr⁺³ will give colour with water. \n3. Electronic configuration of Na⁺ is [He]2s² 2p⁶. Because of fully filled electronic configuration it will not impart any colour with water.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cu⁺','Cr³⁺','Na⁺','None of the above','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(82480,'chemistry','A pair among the following have nearly the same atomic radii:','Zr (40) and Hf (72)','Zr (atomic radii 160 pm) and Hf (atomic radii 158 pm) have nearly same atomic radii because of the lanthanoid contration. As a result ,the elements of second and third transition series have almost similar atomic radii.\n\nThe atomic size of other elements are as follows:\nTi = 147 pm\nNb = 207 pm\nTa = 220 pm\n\nHence, option third is the correct option.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ti (22) and zr (40)','zr (40) and Nb (41)','Zr (40) and Hf (72)','Zr (40) and Ta (73)','NEW','12-219','12-93',NULL),
(82481,'chemistry','Zinc can be coated on iron to produce galvanized iron but the reverse is not possible because:      \n\n(NEET-2016)','Zinc has higher negative electrode potential than iron','Zinc has higher negative electrode potential than iron  :\n\nSTEP 1  The metal with more negative standard reduction potential, have higher tendency to get oxidised.       "Reduction potential values of E° Zn⁺²/Zn = –0.76 V \n\nand E° Fe⁺²/Fe = -0.44 V\nIn galvanized iron, Zn has higher negative reduction potential (i.e. less RP or more OP) Zn lose electrons because of more\n\ntendency to oxidise.\n\nSTEP 2: \n\nThus, Zn works as anode and protects iron from rusting by making iron as cathode.\n\nThe reverse is not possible because even if the zinc is coated with iron means iron will be in contact with moisture, zinc will get\n\noxidized first due to high negative reduction potential.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Zinc is lighter than iron','Zinc has lower melting point than iron','Zinc has lower negative electrode potential than iron','Zinc has higher negative electrode potential than iron','NEW','12-224','12-098',NULL),
(82482,'chemistry','The stability of Cu²⁺ is greater than that of Cu⁺ salts in an aqueous solution due to:\n (NEET 2023)','Hydration energy','Many copper (I) compounds are unstable in aqueous solutions and undergo disproportionation. The reaction is as follows:\n\n2Cu⁺ (aq) → Cu²⁺ (aq) + Cu (s)\n \n\nThe stability of Cu²⁺ (aq) rather than Cu⁺(aq) is due to the much more negative ∆hydH⁰ of Cu²⁺(aq) than Cu⁺, which more than compensates for the second ionisation enthalpy of Cu.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Second ionisation enthalpy','First ionisation enthalpy','Enthalpy of atomization','Hydration energy','NEW','12-224','12-098',NULL),
(82483,'chemistry','Zn gives H₂ gas with H₂SO₅ and HCl but not with HNO₃ because-','NO₃– is reduced in preference to hydronium ion.','Zinc is on the top position of hydrogen in electrochemical series. So, Zn displaces H₂ from dilute H₂SO₅ and HCl with liberation of H₂.\nZn + H₂SO₄ → ZnSO₄ + H₂\nOn the other hand, HNO₃ is one oxidising agent. Hydrogen obtained in the reaction is converted into H₂O.\n2Zn + 4HNO₃ → 2Zn(NO₃)₂ + 2H₂\n2HNO₃  →  H₂O + NO₂ + 3O₂\n2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O\n(AIPMT 2002)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Zn act as oxidising agent when react with HNO₃','HNO₃ is weaker acid than H₂SO₄ and HCl','in electrochemical series Zn is above hydrogen','NO₃– is reduced in preference to hydronium ion.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(82484,'chemistry','Given below are two statements - diagram(82484Q) choose the correct option :\n(NEET-2022)','I is incorrect but II is correct','Cr²⁺ is oxidising and Mn³⁺is reducing in nature- this is a false statement. \nSc³⁺ compounds are repelled by the applied magnetic field- this is a true statement.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both I and II are correct','Both I and II are incorrect','I is correct but II is incorrect','I is incorrect but II is correct','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(82485,'chemistry','Match List-I with List-II. Diagram(82485Q)','(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)','1. In [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻,  Fe oxidation state is +3. The CN- ligand is a strong field ligand thus pairing of electrons takes place. The configuration is t₂g⁵ eg⁰. It has one unpaired electron. Thus, the value of the magnetic moment is 1.73 BM.\n2. In [Fe(H2O)₆]³⁺, Fe oxidation state is +3. The H2O ligand is not a strong field ligand thus pairing of electrons does not take place. The configuration is t₂g³ eg². It has 5 unpaired electrons. Thus, the value of the magnetic moment is 5.92 BM.\n3. In [Fe(CN)6)₆]⁴⁻, Fe oxidation state is +2. The CN- ligand is a strong field ligand thus pairing of electrons takes place. The configuration is t₂g⁶ eg⁰. It has zero unpaired electrons. Thus, the value of the magnetic moment is zero BM.\n4. In [Fe(H2O) )₆]²⁺ Fe oxidation state is +2. The H2O ligand is not a strong field ligand thus pairing of electrons does not take place. The configuration is t₂g⁴ eg². It has 4 unpaired electrons. Thus, the value of the magnetic moment is 4.90 BM.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)','(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)','(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)','(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(82486,'chemistry','The d-electron configurations of Cr²⁺, Mn²⁺, Fe²⁺ and Ni²⁺ and 3d⁴, 3d⁵, 3d⁶ and 3d⁸ respectively. Which one of the following aqua complexes will exhibit the minimum paramagnetic behaviour? \n(At. No. Cr= 24, Mn = 25, Fe = 26, Ni = 28)','[Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺','Minimum paramagnetic behaviour is shown by the complex with minimum number of unpaired electrons. \n\nMn²⁺(23)= 3d⁵–5unpaired electrons \nFe²⁺(24)= 3d⁶–4 unpaired electrons \nNi²⁺(26)= 3d⁸– 2 unpaired electrons \nCr²⁺(22)= 3d⁴ –4 unpaired electrons \n\nSo, [Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺ will exhibit minimum paramagnetic behaviour as it has the least number of unpaired electrons i.e. 2','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','[Mn(H₂O)₆]²⁺','[Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺','[Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺','[Cr(H₂O)₆]²⁺','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(82487,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is INCORRECT? Diagram(82487Q)\n(NEET-2023)','C and D only','1. All the transition metals except scandium form MO oxides which are ionic. It is a correct statement. Scandium forms Sc₂O₃ oxide only. \n\n2. The highest oxidation number corresponding to the group number on transition metal oxides is attained in Sc₂O₃ to Mn₂O₇. The statement is correct. The scandium group number is 3 and it forms an oxide in which the oxidation state is +3.  In Mn₂O₇ , the Mn group is 7, and its oxidation state is +7. \n\n3. Basic character increases from V₂O₃ to V₂O₄ to V₂O₅. It is a wrong statement.  In vanadium, there is a gradual change from the basic V₂O₃ to less basic V₂O₄ and to amphoteric V₂O₅. \n\n4. V₂O₄ dissolves in acids to give VO²⁺ salts. Hence, D is the wrong statement. \n\n5. CrO is basic but Cr₂O₃ is amphoteric. It is a correct statement.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','B and C only','A and E only','B and D only','C and D only','NEW','12-226','12-100',NULL),
(82488,'chemistry','FeCr₂O₄ when reacts with Na₂CO₃ gives the product:\n(AIPMT-1999)','Na₂CrO₄','Fusion of chromite ore (FeCr₂O₄) with sodium or potassium carbonate in free access of air. The reaction with sodium carbonate occurs as follows:\n\n4FeCr₂O₄ + 8Na₂CO₃ + 7O₂ → 8Na₂CrO₄ + 2Fe₂O₃ + 8CO₂','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Na₂CrO₄','Na₂Cr₂O₇','Fe₃O₄','FeO','NEW','12-231','12-105',NULL),
(82489,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is false? Refer to Diagram(82489Q)\n(AIPMT-2012)','2','1. On passing H₂S through acidified K₂Cr₂O₇ solution, a milky colour is observed(because of S).\n\nThe reaction is: K₂Cr₂O₇  + 4H₂SO₄ + 3H₂S → K₂SO₄ +  Cr₂(SO₄)₃ + H₂O + 3S\n\n2. Sodium dichromate, Na₂Cr₂O₇ is hygroscopic in nature. Therefore, it can not be used in volumetric analysis. \n\n3. When KOH solution is added to potassium dichromate, the color of the solution becomes yellow because dichromate ions react with KOH and convert into chromate ions, which have yellow color.\n\nReactions involved are:\n2CrO₄²⁻ + 2H⁺ → Cr₂O₇²⁻ + H₂O\nCr₂O₇²⁻ -+2OH⁻ → 2Cr₂O₄²⁻ + H₂O','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-234','12-108',NULL),
(82490,'chemistry','KMnO₄ can be prepared from K₂MnO₄ as per the reaction, 3MnO₄²⁻ + 2H₂O     2MnO₄⁻ + MnO₂ + 4OH⁻ The reaction can go to completion by removing OH⁻  ions by adding','CO₂','HCl and SO₂ are reducing agents. So, they can reduce MnO₄⁻ .\nCO₂ is neither oxidising nor reducing agent, it will provide only acidic medium. It can shift the reaction in forward direction and the reaction can go to completion.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CO₂','SO₂','HCl','KOH','NEW','12-233','12-107',NULL),
(82491,'chemistry','In the neutral or faintly alkaline medium, KMnO₄ oxidises iodide into iodate. The change in oxidation state of manganese in this reaction is from:\n(NEET - 2022)','From +7 to +4','The reaction of KMnO₄\n with iodide ion is as follows:\n2MnO₄⁻ + H₂O + I⁻→> 2MnO₂+ 2OH⁻+ IO₃⁻\nThe oxidation state of K in KMnO, is +7 and in MnO2 is +4.\nHence, the change in the oxidation state of manganese in this reaction is from +7 to +4.\nThus, option second is the correct answer.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','From +6 to +5','From +7 to +4','From +6 to +4','From +7 to +3',NULL,'12-233','12-107',NULL),
(82492,'chemistry','Lanthanoid\'s most common oxidation state is:','3','In lanthanides (At no. of elements 58 to 71) the electronic configuration of three outermost shelts are (n−2)f¹⁻¹⁴(n−1)d⁰⁻¹ns²\nLanthanides show variable oxidation state like +2,+3,+4 and so on. But the most common oxidation state among these is +3.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2','5','3','4','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(82493,'chemistry','The incorrect statement among the following is :','Most of the trivalent Lanthanoid ions are colorless in the solid state.','The statement is incorrect because most of the trivalent lanthanoid ions are coloured in the solid state. \n\n(NEET 2021)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Actinoid contraction is greater for element to element than Lanthanoid contraction.','Most of the trivalent Lanthanoid ions are colorless in the solid state.','Lanthanoids are good conductors of heat and electricity.','Actinoids are highly reactive metals, especially when finely divided','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(82494,'chemistry','The decrease in size from left to right in the actinoid series is greater and more gradual than that in the lacthanoid series due to:\n(NEET- 2022)','5f orbitals have poor shielding effect','5f orbitals are bigger than 4f orbitals, in other words, 5f orbitals are more diffused thereby having the poor shielding effect. Hence have a more effective nuclear charge thereby having a small size.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','4f orbitals are penultimate','4f orbitals have greater shielding effect','5f orbitals have poor shielding effect','5f  orbitals have greater shielding effect','NEW','12-220','12-094',NULL),
(82495,'chemistry','The outer electronic configuration of Gd: (At. No. 64).\n(NEET-2013)','4f⁷ 5d¹ 6s²','"The electronic configuration of ₆₄Gd is:\n\n  ₆₄Gd: [Xe] 4f⁷ 5d¹ 6s² ."\n\nReference; Karnataka NEET; 2013','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','4f⁵ 5d⁴ 6s¹','4f⁷ 5d¹ 6s²','4f³ 5d⁵ 6s²','4f⁴ 5d⁵ 6s¹','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(82496,'chemistry','When copper is heated with conc. HNO₃ it produces-','Cu(NO₃)₂ and NO₂','When copper is heated with conc. HNO₃ it produces Cu(NO₃)₂ and NO₂\nCu + 4HNO₃(conc.) —→ Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2NO₂ + 2H₂O\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 180 Paragraph No. 1)\n(NEET-I 2016)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cu(NO₃)₂, NO and NO₂','Cu(NO₃)₂ and N₂O','Cu(NO₃)₂ and NO₂','Cu(NO₃)₂ and NO','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(82497,'chemistry','Diamagnetic lanthanoid ion among the following is:\n(At nos. Ce = 58, Sm = 62, Eu = 63, Yb = 70)\n(AIPMT – 2013)','Yb²⁺','Lanthanide ion with no unpaired electron is diamagnetic in nature. This is possible in two ways\n1. When no electron is present in f orbital i.e f⁰\n2. Completely filled f orbital i.e f¹⁴\nElectronic configuration:\nYb²⁺:[Xe14f¹⁴\nDiamagnetic, since no unpaired electron.\nHence, the correct option is the third option.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Sm²⁺','Eu²⁺','Yb²⁺','Ce²⁺','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(82498,'chemistry','The correct order of ionic radii of Y³⁺, La³⁺, Eu³⁺, and Lu³⁺ is:\n(Atomic nos. Y = 39, La = 57, Eu = 63, Lu = 71)\n(AIPMT – 2003)','Y³⁺ < Lu³⁺ < Eu³⁺ < La³⁺','Left to right along the period atomic size decreases. The atomic number of given ions is Y³⁺, La³⁺, Eu³⁺, and Lu³⁺ 39, 57, 63 and 71.\nY is yttrium. It is a d block element. The d block element is smaller in size than the f block element. Hence the correct order of ionic radii is Y³⁺ < Lu³⁺ < Eu³⁺ < La³⁺.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Y³⁺ < La³⁺ < Eu³⁺ < Lu³⁺','Y³⁺ < Lu³⁺ < Eu³⁺ < La³⁺','Lu³⁺ < Eu³⁺ < La³⁺ < Y³⁺','La³⁺ < Eu³⁺ < Lu³⁺ < Y³⁺','NEW','12-220','12-096',NULL),
(82499,'chemistry','Actinoids exhibit more number of oxidation states than lanthanoids. It is because of:\n(AIPMT - 2006, 2011)','The lesser energy difference between 5f and 6d orbitals than that between 4f and 5d orbitals.','Because the energy difference between 5f and 6d orbitals is lesser than that of 4f and 5d orbitals, so, more oxidation states are exhibited by the actinoids than by the lanthanoids.\nThe general electronic configuration of actinoids may be written as [Rn]5f¹⁻¹⁴6d⁰-17s²\nHence, option third is the correct option.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','The greater metallic character of the lanthanoids than that of the corresponding actinoids.','More energy difference between 5f and 6d orbitals than that between 4f and 5d orbitals.','The lesser energy difference between 5f and 6d orbitals than that between 4f and 5d orbitals.','More active nature of the actinoids.','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82501,'chemistry','The correct electronic configuration of the following ions Cu⁺, Lu²⁺, Th⁴⁺ are','[Ar] 3d¹⁰ ; [Xe] 4f¹⁴ 5d¹ ; [Rn] 5f⁰','The correct electronic configuration of  Cu⁺ ; [Ar] 3d¹⁰ ,  Lu²⁺ ; [Xe] 4f¹⁴ 5d¹ , Th⁴⁺ ; [Rn] 5f⁰\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 241; Problem 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','[Xe] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹ ; [Xe] 4f¹⁴ 5d¹ ; [Rn] 5f⁰','[Ar] 3d⁹ ; [Xe] 4f¹⁴ 6s¹ ; [Rn] 5f²','[Ar] 3d¹⁰ ; [Xe] 4f¹⁴ 5d¹ ; [Rn] 5f⁰','[Xe] 3d¹⁰ ; [Xe] 4f¹⁴ 5s¹ ; [Rn] 5f⁰','NEW','12-220','12-094',NULL),
(82502,'chemistry','Why are Mn²⁺ compounds more stable than Fe²⁺ towards oxidation to their +3 state?','Because of half-filled d-orbital in Mn in +2 oxidation state','The electronic configuration of Mn²+ is [Ar] 3d⁵ and that of Fe²+ is [Ar] 3d⁶. Half-filled orbitals are more stable than other incomplete orbitals. Therefore, Mn²⁺ compounds more stable than Fe²⁺ towards oxidation to their +3 state.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 241; Problem 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Because of half-filled d-orbital in Fe in +2 oxidation state','Because of half-filled d-orbital in Mn in +2 oxidation state','Because Mn²⁺/Mn has low standard reduction potential.','Because Fe²⁺/Fe has lower standard reduction potential.','NEW','12-220','12-094',NULL),
(82503,'chemistry','Which among the following oxidation state doesn\'t exist in ground state for first transition series?','3d⁴','3d⁴ oxidation state doesn\'t exist in ground state for first transition series.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 241; Table 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','3d⁴','3d⁵','3d³','3d²','NEW','12-222','12-096',NULL),
(82504,'chemistry','Name the oxometal anions of the first series of the transition metals in which the metal doesn\'t exhibits the oxidation state equal to its group number.','VO₄⁺','The group oxidation state of V is +5, Cr is +6 and that of Mn is +7. However, in VO₄⁺ the oxidation state of V is +9.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Table 8.4.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','VO₃⁻','CrO₄²⁻','MnO₄⁻','VO₄⁺','NEW','12-231','12-105',NULL),
(82505,'chemistry','Assertion: Transition metals and many of their compounds show paramagnetic behaviour.\n\nReason: Unpaired electrons in s-orbital is majorly responsible for paramagnetism\n\nChoose the correct option.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Paramagnetism arises from the presence of unpaired electrons, each such electron having a magnetic moment associated with its spin angular momentum and orbital angular momentum. Although the unpaired electrons in s orbital contribute to the paramagnetism but this fact is not majorly responsible for showing such magnetic behaviour. d-orbitals are majorly responsible for showing paramagnetism.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 228; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(82506,'chemistry','Assertion: The enthalpies of atomisation of the transition metals are high.\n\nReason:  Large number of unpaired electrons are present in their atoms.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','The maxima at about the middle of each series indicate that one unpaired electron per d orbital is particularly favourable for strong interatomic interaction.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 218; Paragraph last )','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-221','12-095',NULL),
(82507,'chemistry','What is the effect of increasing pH on a solution of Potassium dichromate?','The solution turns yellow.','On increasing pH basicity of the solution increases. Dichromate ion changes to Chromate ion in basic solution. The colour of Chromate ion is yellow. Therefore, on increasing pH of the solution colour changes to yellow. \nCr₂O₇²⁻ + 2OH⁻ → 2CrO₄⁻ + H₂O\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The solution turns purple.','The solution turns orange.','The solution turns dark green.','The solution turns yellow.','NEW','12-231','12-105',NULL),
(82508,'chemistry','For M²⁺/M and M³⁺/M²⁺ systems the E V values for some metals are as follows: \nCr²⁺/Cr    -0.9V          Cr³⁺/Cr²⁺ -0.4 V\nMn²⁺/Mn  -1.2V         Mn³⁺/Mn²⁺ +1.5 V\nFe²⁺/Fe   -0.4V           Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺ +0.8 V\nUse this data to comment upon: Why is Fe³⁺ stable in acid solution as compared to that of Cr³⁺ or Mn³⁺?','Mn³⁺ oxidises H₂O to O₂','While Fe³⁺ is stable, Mn³⁺ is not stable in acid solution because Mn³⁺ oxidises H₂O to O₂. \nReaction of Mn³⁺ with H₂O is spontaneous as Eo value of Mn³⁺ +e⁻ →Mn²⁺ Eo=1.50 is higher than the Eo  value of O₂ +4H⁺ +4e⁻→2H₂O Eo=1.23\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 224, 225; Paragraph 8.3.5, 8.3.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Fe³⁺ oxidises H₂O to O₂','O₂ oxidises Mn²⁺ to Mn³⁺','O₂ oxidises both Mn²⁺ and Fe²⁺','Mn³⁺ oxidises H₂O to O₂','NEW','12-224','12-098',NULL),
(82509,'chemistry','For M²⁺/M and M³⁺/M²⁺ systems the E V values for some metals are as follows: \nCr²⁺/Cr    -0.9V          Cr³⁺/Cr²⁺ -0.4 V\nMn²⁺/Mn  -1.2V         Mn³⁺/Mn²⁺ +1.5 V\nFe²⁺/Fe   -0.4V           Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺ +0.8 V\nUse this data to comment upon: What is the ease with which iron can be oxidised as compared to a similar process for either chromium or manganese metal.?','Mn > Cr > Fe','The reduction potentials for the given pairs increase in the following order.\n\nMn²⁺ / Mn < Cr²⁺ / Cr < Fe²⁺/Fe\n\nReduction potential for Mn²⁺/Mn is most negative and therefore, it will be most easily oxidised \nSo, the oxidation of Fe to Fe²⁺ is not as easy as the oxidation of Cr to Cr²⁺ and the oxidation of Mn to Mn²⁺. Thus, these metals can be arranged in the increasing order of their ability to get oxidised as: Fe < Cr < Mn\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 224, 225; Paragraph 8.3.5, 8.3.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Mn > Cr > Fe²⁺','Fe²⁺ > Cr > Mn','Fe > Cr > Mn','Mn > Cr > Fe','NEW','12-224','12-098',NULL),
(82510,'chemistry','Which of the following statements with regard to the chemistry of actinoids with that of the lanthanoids are correct?\n(i)  Actinoids have variable occupancy at 5f and 6d subshells\n(ii) For both-Lanthanoids and actinoids the common oxidation state is 3+.\n(iii) Lanthanoid contraction is greater from element to element than Actinoid contraction.','(ii) and (iii)','The correct statement is -" Actinoid contraction is greater from element to element than Lanthanoid contraction. "\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235 ; Paragraph 8.5.1, 8.5.2, 8.5.3)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 238 ; Paragraph 8.6.1, 8.6.2, 8.6.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(ii) and (iii)','(i) and (iii)','(i) (ii) and (iii)','(i) only','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(82511,'chemistry','Which among the following statements are incorrect? \nA. Lanthanum and other Lanthanioids are hard, silver metals\nB. Their principle oxidation state is +4. \nC. They react with water to give solutions giving +2 oxidation states. \nD. The hardness of Lanthanioids increases with increasing atomic number.','A, B and C only','The correct statements are -\nA. Lanthanum and other Lanthanioids are soft, silvery white metals\nB. Their principle oxidation state is +3 \nC. They react with water to give solutions giving +3 oxidation states. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 240 ; Paragraph 5th of Summary )\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236 ; Paragraph 8.5.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','A, B and C only','B and C only','B only','A and B only','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(82512,'chemistry','Assertion: Cobalt(II) is stable in aqueous solution but in the presence of complexing reagents it is easily oxidised.\n\nReason: Third Ionisation Energy of Cobalt is high.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Co (II) is stable in aqueous solutions however, in the presence of strong field complexing is agents, it is oxidised to Co (III). Although the 3rd ionisation energy for Co is high, but the higher amount of crystal field stabilisation field ligands overcomes this ionisation energy.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 224; Paragraph 8.3.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(82513,'chemistry','Assertion: The d¹ configuration is very unstable in ions.\n\nReason: There is availability of enough hydration energy.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','The ions in d configuration tend to lose one more electrons to get into stable d⁰ configuration. Also, the hydration or lattice energy is more than sufficient to remove the only electron present in the d-orbital of these ions. Therefore, they act as reducing agents.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 227; Paragraph 8.3.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-227','12-111',NULL),
(82514,'chemistry','Which one of these is the most stable in aqueous solution?','Cr³⁺','Unpaired electrons in Mn³⁺ = 4 In Cr³⁺ = 3 In V³⁺ = 2 In Ti³⁺ = 1 . Since half-filled configurations are more stable than others, Cr³⁺ is most stable among the following.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 222; Paragraph 8.3.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cr³⁺','Mn³⁺','V³⁺','Ti³⁺','NEW','12-222','12-096',NULL),
(82515,'chemistry','Assertion: The lowest oxide of transition metal is basic, the highest is amphoteric/acidic.\n\nReason: In lowest oxidation state electrons in metal ion are involved in Bonding.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','The lowest oxide of transition metal is basic because the metal has low oxidation state whereas, the highest oxide is acidic or amphoteric due to highest oxidation state. In the low oxidation state, some of the valence electrons of metal ion are not involved in bonding. Hence, it can donate electrons and behave as base. In higher oxidation state, valence e⁻s are involved in bonding and are not available, Instead, effective nuclear charge is high. Hence, it can accept e⁻s and hence behave as acid.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 8.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-231','12-105',NULL),
(82516,'chemistry','Assertion: A transition metal exhibits highest oxidation state in oxides and fluorides.\n\nReason: Oxygen and flourine have smaller sizes, higher ionic charges and vacant orbitals. \n\nChoose the correct option.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Transition metals form large no. of complex compounds because of their smaller size, high ionic charges and availability of vacant d-orbitals\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 222; Paragraph 8.3.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-222','12-096',NULL),
(82517,'chemistry','Assertion: The highest oxidation state is exhibited in oxoanions of a metal.\n\nReason: Oxygen ion is both very electronegative and extremely small.\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','A metal\'s maximum oxidation state can only be found in its oxide or fluoride. Oxygen and fluoride ions are both very electronegative and extremely small.\nDue to these properties, they may oxidize the metal to its greatest oxidation state.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 8.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-231','12-105',NULL),
(82518,'chemistry','Indicate the steps in the preparation of K₂Cr₂O₇ from chromite ore in correct order from first to last.\n1) Fusion of Chromite ore with sodium carbonate.  \n2) Acidification of sodium Chromate with Sulphuric acid. \n3) Crystallization of sodium dichromate. \n4) Treatment of sodium dichromate with Potassium chloride.','1→3→2→4','The correct order of the steps in the preparation of K₂Cr₂O₇ from chromite ore is-\n1. Fusion of Chromite ore with sodium carbonate.  \n       4FeCr₂O₄ + 8Na₂CO₃ + 7O₂ → 8Na₂CrO₄ + 2Fe₂O₃ + 8CO₂\n2. Crystallization of sodium dichromate. \n3. Acidification of sodium Chromate with Sulphuric acid. \n       2Na₂CrO₄ + 2H⁺ → Na₂Cr₂O₇ + 2Na⁺ + H₂O\n4. Treatment of sodium dichromate with Potassium chloride.\n        Na₂Cr₂O₇ + 2KCl → K₂Cr₂O₇ + 2NaCl\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 8.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','4→2→3→1','1→2→4→3','1→3→2→4','3→2→1→4','NEW','12-231','12-105',NULL),
(82519,'chemistry','Indicate the steps in the preparation of KMnO₄ from pyrolusite ore in correct order from last.to first\n1) Disproportionation of Manganate ion in acidic medium.\n2) Formation of dark purple crystals.\n3) Formation of dark green compound\n4) Fusion of MnO₂ with alkali hydroxide and oxidising agent.','2→1→3→4','The correct order of the steps in the preparation of KMnO₄ from pyrolusite ore from first to last.to first is-\n1) Fusion of MnO₂ with alkali hydroxide and oxidising agent. \n    2MnO₂ + 4KOH + O₂ → 2K₂MnO₄ + 2H₂O\n2) Formation of dark green compound. \n3) Disproportionation of Manganate ion in acidic medium. \n    3MnO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺ → 2MnO₄⁻ + MnO₂ + 2H₂O\n4) Formation of dark purple crystals.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 8.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','4→1→3→2','1→2→3→4','4→3→1→2','2→1→3→4','NEW','12-232','12-106',NULL),
(82520,'chemistry','How would you expect the five 3d orbitals to be occupied for these hydrated ions (octahedral)?','Cu²⁺-t₂g⁶ eg³, V²⁺-t₂g³, Fe²⁺-t₂g⁴ eg², Fe³⁺-t₂g³ eg²','The electronic configurations are given by- Cu²⁺ : 3d⁹ V²⁺ : 3d³ Fe²⁺ : 3d⁶ Fe³⁺ : 3d⁵ The correct match is Cu²⁺ - t₂g⁶ eg³, V²⁺ - t₂g³, Fe²⁺ - t₂g⁴ eg², Fe³⁺ - t₂g³ eg²\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 243; Problem 8.36)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'2','match','Cu²⁺,t₂g⁶ eg³','V²⁺,t₂g³','Fe²⁺,t₂g⁴ eg²','Fe³⁺,t₂g³ eg²','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(82521,'chemistry','What can be inferred from the magnetic moment values of the following complex species?\nExample Magnetic      Moment (BM)\nK₄[Mn(CN)₆)                   2.2','Hybridisation of Mn is d²sp³.','For magnetic moment 2.2 B.M , we solve 2.2 = [√n(n+2)] \nBy solving the equation we get n =1. Therefore, 1 unpaired electron is present in Mn. \nSince, CN⁻ is a strong field ligand, electrons start filling in d orbitals first and then s and p orbitals and forms low spin complexes. The structure for co-ordination number 6 is octahedral. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 243; Problem 8.38)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Two unpaired electrons are present in d orbitals.','The structure of the complex is tetrahedral.','CN⁻ is a weak field ligand','Hybridisation of Mn is d²sp³.','NEW','12-228','12-112',NULL),
(82522,'chemistry','Which among the following possesses amphoteric behaviour?','V₂O₅','Only V₂O₅ possesses amphoteric behaviour. All the other oxides are basic. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Paragraph 8.4.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','V₂O₃','V₂O₅','CrO','V₂O4','NEW','12-231','12-105',NULL),
(82523,'chemistry','A purple compound X,  in the presence of Zinc. sulphate and zinc oxide, oxidises a pink compound Y to form compound Z. Z when treated with alkali metal hydroxide and an oxidising agent KNO3 forms dark green compound. What is the compound Y here ?','A salt','MnO₂ reacts with alkali metal hydroxide and KNO₃ to give dark green compound Potassium Manganate. Therefore, Z is MnO₂. \n2MnO₂ + 4KOH + O₂ → 2K₂MnO₄ + 2H₂O\nPotassium permanganate oxidises Mangannous salt to MnO₂ in the presence of Zinc. sulphate and zinc oxide . \n2MnO₄⁻ + 3Mn²⁺ + 2H₂O ——> 5MnO₂ + 4H⁺\nManganous salt are Mn²⁺ type of salts and colour of Mn²⁺ is pink. Therefore, compound Y is a salt which is pink.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232 Paragraph last)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 233 Point 2(c))','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A halide','A nitrate','An oxide','A salt','NEW','12-232','12-106',NULL),
(82524,'chemistry','Iodide when reacts with acidic Potassium permanganate form X and with Potassium dichromate forms Y. What are X ad Y respectively?','Iodine, Iodine','In acid solutions: Iodine is liberated from potassium iodide :\n10I⁻ + 2MnO₄⁻ + 16H⁺ → 2Mn²⁺ + 8H₂O + 5I₂\n\nAcidified potassium dichromate will oxidise iodides to iodine,\n6I⁻ → 3I₂ + 6e⁻\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232 Paragraph 4)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 233 Point 1(a))','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Iodine, Iodate ion','Iodate ion, Iodine','Iodate ion, Iodate ion','Iodine, Iodine','NEW','12-232','12-106',NULL),
(82525,'chemistry','The oxidation state of Chromate ion is A. It changes to oxidation state B when kept in acidic solution. On further treating it with acid the oxidation state changes to C. What are oxidation states A, B and C respectively?','6+, 6+, 3+','Oxidation state of Chromate is 6+. It remains same when kept in acidic solution. So A and B both are 6+. \n2CrO₄²⁻ + 2H⁺ → Cr₂O₇²⁻ + H₂O\nOn further treating it with acidic solution dichromate ion changes to Cr³⁺. Thus, oxidation state C is 3+.\nCr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','6+, 3+, 3+','3+, 6+, 6+','6+, 3+, 2+','6+, 6+, 3+','NEW','12-232','12-106',NULL),
(82526,'chemistry','Which of the following is not repelled by magnetic field?','Yb³⁺','The lanthanoid ions other than the f⁰ type (La³⁺ and Ce⁴⁺) and the f¹⁴ type (Yb²⁺ and Lu³⁺) are all paramagnetic. Yb³⁺ too is paramagnetic and hence is attracted by magnetic field.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 236; Paragraph 8.5.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','La³⁺','Ce⁴⁺','Lu³⁺','Yb³⁺','NEW','12-236','12-106',NULL),
(82527,'chemistry','Which among the following graphs show regular pattern of the trend?','Trends in ionic radii of Lanthanioids','Only the graph of Trends in ionic radii of Lanthanioids shows regular pattern.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 218; Fig 8.1)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 219;  Fig 8.2)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 220; Fig 8.3)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Fig 8.6)\ndiagram(ne0804)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Trends in melting point of transition elements','Trends in atomic radii of transition elements','Trends in ionic radii of Lanthanioids','Trends in atomisation of transition elements','NEW','12-218','12-092',NULL),
(82528,'chemistry','Among which of the following elements the observed value of standard potential is not equal to its calculated value ?','Cobalt','Among the following options, only Cobalt has it\'s calculated value less than it\'s observed value. All the other options have their calculated value nearly equal to their observed value. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 231; Fig 8.4)\ndiagram(ne0805)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Chromium','Cobalt','Titanium','Vanadium','NEW','12-224','12-098',NULL),
(82529,'chemistry','Which trihalide of Titanium is not found?','Triflouride','Except triflourides, all the other trihalides are formed by Ti.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 225; Table 8.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Trichloride','Tribromide','Triflouride','Triiodide','NEW','12-225','12-099',NULL),
(82530,'chemistry','Which of the following element has maximum composition in misch metal?','Ce','Cerium is a Lanthanoid metal. pipes. A well known alloy is mischmetall which consists of a lanthanoid metal (~ 95%) and iron (~ 5%) and traces of S, C, Ca and Al. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 237; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','La','Ce','Lu','Fe','NEW','12-237','12-111',NULL),
(82531,'chemistry','Which of the following nitrates will leave behind the metal on strong heating?','Silver nitrate','Oxides of 3d metals -\n\nThe ability of oxygen to stabilise the highest oxidation number in the oxides of \'d\' block elements \nMetal oxides / Nitrates lying below Hg in Electrochemical series decompose to form metal \n\n2AgNO₃ ------2Ag (s) + O₂+ 2NO₂(g)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 225; Table 8.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Copper nitrate','Silver nitrate','Manganese nitrate','Ferric nitrate','NEW','12-225','12-099',NULL),
(82532,'chemistry','In which of the following oxyanion all X—O bonds are not equivalent? X is central metal.','CrO₇²⁻','In CrO₇²⁻, all X—O bonds are not equivalent. The Cr-O bond of Cr-O-Cr is 179 pm which is greater than the other Cr-O bond which is 163 pm. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 232,  Page 233)\ndiagram(ne0809)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','MnO₄⁻','CrO₄²⁻','MnO₄²⁻','CrO₇²⁻','NEW','12-232','12-106',NULL),
(82533,'chemistry','When Cu²⁺ solution reacts with the excess of KI, then which option is incorrect?','CuI₂ is formed','The reaction is given by 2Cu²⁺ + 4KI --- Cu₂I₂ + I₂ Thus it can be inferred that CuI₂ is not formed.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 226; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Insoluble Cu₂I₂ is obtained','I₂ is liberated','CuI₂ is formed','It is a redox reaction between Cu²⁺ and I⁻','NEW','12-226','12-100',NULL),
(82534,'chemistry','Cr²⁺ is reducing in nature because-','In Cr²⁺, configuration changes from d⁴ to d³ to achieve half filled t₂g','Cr²⁺ is strongly reducing in nature. It has a d⁴ configuration. While acting as a reducing agent, it gets oxidized to Cr³⁺ (electronic configuration, d³) which is a half filled t₂g configuration- a stable configuration\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 227; Paragraph 8.3.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','In Cr²⁺, configuration changes from d⁴ to d³ to achieve half filled t₂g','Cr²⁺ gain an electron to achieve d⁵ configuration','Cr²⁺ give an electron to achieve d⁵ configuration','In Cr²⁺, configuration changes from d⁴ to d³ to achieve half filled d-subshell','NEW','12-227','12-101',NULL),
(82535,'chemistry','Which of the following is not correct?','Cu + dil H₂SO₄ →  CuSO₄ + H₂','Of the first series, with the exception of copper, all the metals are relatively reactive. The metals of the first series with the exception of copper are relatively more reactive and are oxidised by 1M H+, though the actual rate at which these metals react with oxidising agents like hydrogen ion (H+) is sometimes slow.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 225; Table 8.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Zn + dil H₂SO₄ →  ZnSO₄ + H₂','Cu + dil H₂SO₄ →  CuSO₄ + H₂','CuSO₄ + KI →  K₂SO₄ + Cu₂I₂ + I₂','Na₂Cr₂O₄ + H₂SO₄ →   Na₂SO₄ + Na₂Cr₂O₇','NEW','12-225','12-099',NULL),
(82536,'chemistry','X + S₂O₈²⁻  → Y + SO₄²⁻. In the above reaction, what X and Y can\'t be ?','NO₃⁻ → NO₂⁻','All the below reactions occur in nature\nFe²⁺ + S₂O₈²⁻  → Fe³⁺ + SO₄²⁻.\nMn²⁺ + S₂O₈²⁻  → MnO₄⁻ + SO₄²⁻.\nI⁻ + S₂O₈²⁻  →  I₂ + SO₄²⁻.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 230; Paragraph 8.3.12 )','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺','Mn²⁺ → MnO₄⁻','I⁻ → I₂','NO₃⁻ → NO₂⁻','NEW','12-230','12-104',NULL),
(82537,'chemistry','Which metal of the first transition series is not reactive?','Copper','Of the first series, with the exception of copper, all the metals are relatively reactive. The metals of the first series with the exception of copper are relatively more reactive and are oxidised by 1M H+, though the actual rate at which these metals react with oxidising agents like hydrogen ion (H+) is sometimes slow.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 240; Paragraph Summary)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 227; Paragraph 8.3.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Copper','Iron','Scandium','Vanadium','NEW','12-240','12-110',NULL),
(82538,'chemistry','Arrange the following ions in increasing order of their ionic radii','Yb²⁺ < Tm²⁺ < Eu²⁺ < Sm²⁺','The correct order is Yb²⁺ < Tm²⁺ < Eu²⁺ < Sm²⁺\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235;  Fig 8.6)\ndiagram(ne0804)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Sm²⁺ < Eu²⁺ < Tm²⁺ < Yb²⁺','Tm²⁺ < Yb²⁺ < Sm²⁺ < Eu²⁺','Eu²⁺ < Sm²⁺ < Yb²⁺ < Tm²⁺','Yb²⁺ < Tm²⁺ < Eu²⁺ < Sm²⁺','NEW','12-235','12-105',NULL),
(82539,'chemistry','Which of the following reaction is not correct?','Ln + O₂ → Ln₂O','The correct reaction is Ln + O₂ → Ln₂O₃.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235;  Fig 8.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ln + N₂ → LnN','Ln + S → Ln₂S₃','Ln + X₂ → LnX₃','Ln + O₂ → Ln₂O','NEW','12-235','12-105',NULL),
(82540,'chemistry','Which among the following ions is not violet in colour?','Cr²⁺','Cr²⁺ is blue in colour while all the other ions are violet in color\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235;  Table 8.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Cr²⁺','V²⁺','Cr³⁺','Mn³⁺','NEW','12-235','12-105',NULL),
(82541,'chemistry','Borides of which among the following approach the hardness of diamond?','Interstitial compounds','Interstitial compounds are very hard, some borides approach diamond in hardness.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 235;  Paragraph 8.3.13)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Lanthanioids\' compounds','Interstitial compounds','Actinoids\' compounds','Transition compounds','NEW','12-235','12-105',NULL),
(82542,'chemistry','Which among the following statements are incorrect?\n A. Zn has the highest first Ionisation enthalpy.  \nB. Copper has the highest second Ionisation enthalpy. \nC. For Fe²⁺/Fe the standard reduction potential is positive. \nD. The enthalpy of atomisation of V is greater than Ti.','C','Zinc has the highest first Ionisation enthalpy while Sc has the lowest. Copper has the highest second Ionisation enthalpy. For M²⁺/M type, only Cu²⁺/Cu has positive standard reduction potential. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 220;  Table 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','12-220','12-094',NULL),
(82543,'chemistry','Which among the following statements are not incorrect?\nA. The trend in steady increase in second Ionisation enthalpy breaks for the formation of Cu³⁺ ion. \nB. The successive increase of second and third IE is higher than the first IE along the first transition series. \nC Cr and Cu have unusually high third Ionisation enthalpy \nD. It is difficult to obtain oxidation state greater than two for Nickel.','B and D','The trend of steady increase in third ionisation enthalpy breaks for the formation of Fe³⁺. In this case, ion has d⁵ configuration.The first ionisation enthalpy, in general, increases, but the magnitude of the increase in the second and third ionisation enthalpies.The dominant term is the second ionisation enthalpy which shows unusually high values for Cr and Cu where M⁺ ions have the d⁵ and d¹⁰ configurations respectively. the high values for third ionisation enthalpies of copper, nickel and zinc indicate why it is difficult to obtain oxidation state greater than two for these elements.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 221, 222;  Paragraph 8.3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','B and D','A and C','B and C','A and B','NEW','12-221','12-095',NULL),
(82544,'chemistry','Which among the following statements are correct? \nA. The 7+ oxidation state is not observed in simple halides in Mn. \nB. Except VF₅, other halides of V undergo hydrolysis to form VOX₄. \nC Ferrates decompose to Fe₃O₄ and O₂ in alkaline medium. \nD. Zn forms halides in +2 oxidation state only.','A and D','The +7 state for Mn is not represented in simple halides but MnO₃F is known, and beyond Mn no metal has a trihalide except FeX₃ and CoF₃ Although V⁺⁵ is represented only by VF₅, the other halides, however, undergo hydrolysis to give oxohalides, VOX₃.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 225, 226;  Paragraph 8.3.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A and B','B and C','A and D','B and D','NEW','12-225','12-099',NULL),
(82545,'chemistry','Select the incorrect statement.','Ce(III) is a good analytical reagent.','Ce(IV) is a good analytical reagent.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 221, 235;  Paragraph 8.5.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Most challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Lanthanioids have only one stable oxidation state.','Ce(III) is a good analytical reagent.','Absorption bands are narrow in Lanthanioids','La, Gd and Lu have abnormally low third Ionisation enthalpy','NEW','12-221','12-095',NULL),
(82546,'chemistry','Electronic configuration of a transition element X in +3 oxidation state is\n[Ar]3d5. What is its atomic number?','26','The electronic configuration of the transition element X in the +3 oxidation state is [Ar]3d5. Since the atomic number represents the total number of protons in the nucleus of an atom, we can find the atomic number of X by adding up the number of electrons in the 3d and 4s subshells, as well as the three electrons lost to form the +3 oxidation state.\n\nThus, the atomic number of X can be calculated as follows:\n\nAtomic number of X = number of electrons in 3d subshell + number of electrons in 4s subshell + number of electrons lost in oxidation state\n\nAtomic number of X = 18 + 5 + 3 = 26\n\nTherefore, the atomic number of the transition element X is 26.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','25','24','27','26','NEW','12-220','12-094',NULL),
(82547,'chemistry','The electronic configuration of Cu(II) is 3 9 d where as that of Cu(I) is 3 10 d .\nWhich of the following is correct?','Cu(II) is more stable','The electronic configuration of Cu(I) is [Ar] 3d10, while the electronic configuration of Cu(II) is [Ar] 3d9. This means that Cu(I) has a completely filled 3d subshell, which is a stable configuration, while Cu(II) has an incomplete 3d subshell, which is less stable.\n\nHowever, despite having an incomplete 3d subshell, Cu(II) is more stable than Cu(I). This is because Cu(II) has a greater effective nuclear charge, as it holds 17 electrons instead of 18 in Cu(I). This stronger attractive force between the positively charged nucleus and the 17 electrons in Cu(II) makes it more stable than Cu(I), even though Cu(I) has a stable 3d10 configuration.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is "Cu(II) is more stable."','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Cu(II) is more stable','Stability of Cu(I) and Cu(II) depends on nature of copper salts','Cu(I) and Cu(II) are equally stable','Cu(II) is less stable','NEW','12-221','12-095',NULL),
(82548,'chemistry','Which of these elements will have highest density?','Cu','The density of an element is dependent on its mass and volume, and for metals, it is typically higher for those with smaller atomic radii and higher atomic masses. As we move from left to right across a period in the periodic table, the metallic radius decreases while the atomic mass increases.\n\nAmong the given elements, Cu belongs to the right side of the periodic table in the transition metal series. It has the smallest atomic radius and highest atomic mass, which makes it the most dense element among the options provided. Its density is 89 g/cm3, which is higher than the densities of Fe, Co, and Ni.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is "Cu."','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Fe','Cu','Ni','Co','NEW','12-220','12-094',NULL),
(82549,'chemistry','Generally, transition elements form coloured salts due to the presence of\nunpaired electrons. Which of the following compounds will be coloured in\nsolid state?','CuF2','The color of transition metal compounds is often due to the presence of unpaired d electrons, which can absorb light in the visible region of the electromagnetic spectrum.\n\nAmong the given options, CuF2 is the compound that is most likely to be colored in the solid state. The copper ion in CuF2 has a +2 oxidation state and one unpaired d electron, which can absorb light in the visible region of the spectrum and give rise to a characteristic color.\n\nAg2SO4 and ZnF2 are not transition metal compounds and do not have unpaired d electrons, so they are not likely to be colored in the solid state. Cu2Cl2 is a transition metal compound, but it has a Cu(I) ion with a filled d subshell, so it is not likely to be colored.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is "CuF2."\n\n\n\n\nThe color of transition metal compounds is often due to the presence of unpaired d electrons, which can absorb light in the visible region of the electromagnetic spectrum.\n\nAmong the given options, CuF2 is the compound that is most likely to be colored in the solid state. The copper ion in CuF2 has a +2 oxidation state and one unpaired d electron, which can absorb light in the visible region of the spectrum and give rise to a characteristic color.\n\nAg2SO4 and ZnF2 are not transition metal compounds and do not have unpaired d electrons, so they are not likely to be colored in the solid state. Cu2Cl2 is a transition metal compound, but it has a Cu(I) ion with a filled d subshell, so it is not likely to be colored.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is "CuF2."','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Ag2SO4','Cu2Cl2','ZnF2','CuF2','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(82550,'chemistry','On addition of small amount of KMnO4 to concentratedH2SO4 , a green oily\ncompound is obtained which is highly explosive in nature. Identify the\ncompound from the following','Mn2O7','When a small amount of KMnO4 is added to concentrated H2SO4, a green oily compound is formed, which is highly explosive in nature. The compound formed in this reaction is Mn2O7.\n\nThe reaction can be represented as follows:\n\n2KMnO4 + 3H2SO4 → Mn2O7 + K2SO4 + 3H2O\n\nManganese heptoxide (Mn2O7) is a green oily liquid that is highly explosive and unstable. It is a powerful oxidizing agent and should be handled with extreme care.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is "Mn2O7."','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Mn2O7','Mn2O3','MnSO4','MnO2','NEW','12-231','12-105',NULL),
(82551,'chemistry','The magnetic nature of elements depends on the presence of unpaired\nelectrons. Identify the configuration of transition element, which shows\nhighest magnetic moment.','3d5','The magnetic properties of transition elements depend on the presence of unpaired electrons in their d orbitals. The greater the number of unpaired electrons, the higher the magnetic moment of the element. \n\nThe formula for calculating the magnetic moment (m) of an element with n unpaired electrons is given by:\n\nm = √n(n+2) BM\n\nwhere BM is the Bohr magneton. This formula takes into account the spin of the electrons, which can be either up or down.\n\nFor the 3d5 configuration, the number of unpaired electrons is 5. Therefore, its magnetic moment can be calculated as:\n\nm = √5(5+2) BM\n\nm = √35 BM\n\nm ≈ 5.92 BM\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is "3d5" with a magnetic moment of approximately 5.92 BM','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','3d7','3d2','3d8','3d5','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(82552,'chemistry','Which of the following oxidation state is common for all lanthanoids?','3+','The most common oxidation state for lanthanoids is +3. This is because the outermost electron configuration of lanthanoids is (n-2)f1-14 (n-1)d0-1 ns2, and they have a strong tendency to lose their three ns and (n-1)d electrons to achieve a stable f0-14 configuration. Some lanthanoids can also show other oxidation states, such as +2 and +4, but these are less common.\n\nExamples of lanthanoids with stable +2, +3, and +4 oxidation states are:\n\nEu2+ ([Xe]4f7)\nGd3+ ([Xe]4f7)\nCe4+ ([Xe]4f0)\nTb4+ ([Xe]4f7)\nTherefore, the correct answer is "+3".','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','2+','5+','4+','3+','NEW','12-235','12-105',NULL),
(82553,'chemistry','When KMnO4 solution is added to oxalic acid solution, the decolourisation\nis slow in the beginning but becomes instantaneous after some time because','Mn2+ acts as autocatalyst','Mn2+ acts as an autocatalyst. When KMnO4 is added to oxalic acid solution, MnO4- ion is reduced to Mn2+ ion by oxalic acid, and oxalic acid is oxidized to CO2. Mn2+ ion acts as a catalyst, promoting the reaction between KMnO4 and oxalic acid by oxidizing more oxalic acid to CO2. As the concentration of Mn2+ ion increases due to the progress of the reaction, it accelerates the reaction rate further, leading to instantaneous decolourisation of the solution.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','CO2 is formed as the product','Mn2+ acts as autocatalyst','MnO4- catalyses the reaction','reaction is exothermic','NEW','12-234','12-104',NULL),
(82554,'chemistry','There are 14 elements in actinoid series. Which of the following\nelements does not belong to this series?','Tm','The actinoid series consists of 14 elements with atomic numbers ranging from 90 to 103. These elements are named after the first member of the series, actinium (Ac). Thulium (Tm), on the other hand, belongs to the lanthanoid series, which consists of the 15 elements with atomic numbers ranging from 57 to 71 (sometimes 72). These elements are named after the first member of the series, lanthanum (La). Therefore, Tm does not belong to the actinoid series.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','U','Fm','Tm','Np','NEW','12-237','12-107',NULL),
(82555,'chemistry','KMnO4 acts as an oxidising agent in acidic medium. The number of moles\nof KMnO4 that will be needed to react with one mole of sulphide ions in\nacidic solution is','2/5','When KMnO4 acts as an oxidising agent in acidic medium, it reacts with sulfide ions (S2-) according to the following balanced equation:\n\nH2SO4(aq) + KMnO4(aq) + 5S2-(aq) → K2SO4(aq) + MnSO4(aq) + 5SO4^2-(aq) + 8H2O(l)\n\nFrom the balanced equation, we can see that 2 moles of KMnO4 react with 5 moles of S2-. Therefore, the number of moles of KMnO4 needed to react with 1 mole of S2- is:\n\n(2 moles KMnO4) / (5 moles S2-) = 0.4 moles KMnO4 / 1 mole S2-\n\nSo, the answer is 2/5.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','2/5','1/5','4/5','3/5','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(82556,'chemistry','Which of the following is amphoteric oxide?','V2O5,Cr2O3','V2O5 and Cr2O3 are amphoteric oxides because they can react with both acids and bases. V2O5 can react with strong acids to form vanadyl ions (VO2+) and with strong bases to form salts such as NaVO3. Similarly, Cr2O3 can react with acids to form chromic ions (Cr3+) and with bases to form chromates such as Na2CrO4.\n\nThe other options are:\n\nMn2O7: This is a highly acidic oxide and is not amphoteric.\nCrO3: This is an acidic oxide and reacts with bases but not with acids.\nCrO: This is a basic oxide and is not amphoteric.\nV2O4: This is a basic oxide and is not amphoteric.\nIt is worth noting that in general, lower oxides of transition metals tend to be basic, while higher oxides tend to be acidic. However, some oxides, such as V2O5 and Cr2O3, can exhibit amphoteric behavior.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','V2O5,Cr2O3','V2O5, V2O4','CrO, V2O5','Mn2O7, CrO3','NEW','12-231','12-105',NULL),
(82557,'chemistry','Gadolinium belongs to 4f series. Its atomic number is 64. Which of the\nfollowing is the correct electronic configuration of gadolinium?','[Xe]4f⁷5d¹6s²','Gadolinium (Gd) has an atomic number of 64 and belongs to the 4f series of the periodic table. The electronic configuration of Gd is [Xe]4f⁷5d¹6s².\n\nIn the ground state of a Gd atom, the 4f subshell is partially filled with 7 electrons, the 5d subshell is partially filled with 1 electron, and the 6s subshell is filled with 2 electrons. The presence of partially filled subshells in the inner transition metals like Gd gives rise to their unique chemical and physical properties.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','[Xe]4f⁷5d¹6s²','[Xe]4f⁹6s²','[Xe]4f⁸6d²','[Xe]4f⁷5d²6s²','NEW','12-236','12-106',NULL),
(82558,'chemistry','Interstitial compounds are formed when small atoms are trapped inside\nthe crystal lattice of metals. Which of the following is not the\ncharacteristic property of interstitial compounds?','The are chemically very reactive','Interstitial compounds are formed when small atoms are trapped inside the crystal\nlattice of metals. Some of their important characteristics are as follows\n(i) They are very hard and rigid.\n(ii) They have high melting point which are higher than those of the pure metals.\n(iii) They show conductivity like that of the pure metal.\n(iv) They acquire chemical inertness','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','They have high melting points in comparison to pure metals','The are chemically very reactive','They retain metallic conductivity','They are very hard','NEW','12-230','12-104',NULL),
(82559,'chemistry','The magnetic moment is associated with its spin angular momentum and\norbital angular momentum. Spin only magnetic moment value of Cr 3+ ion\nis','3.87 BM','The electronic configuration of Cr3+ ion is [Ar] 3d3 and it has 3 unpaired electrons. The spin-only magnetic moment (μs) can be calculated using the formula:\n\nμs = √(n(n+2)) BM\n\nwhere n is the total number of unpaired electrons.\n\nFor Cr3+ ion, n = 3, so μs = √(3(3+2)) = √15 ≈ 3.87 BM\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 3.87 BM.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','2.87 BM','3.57 BM','3.47 BM','3.87 BM','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(82560,'chemistry','KMnO4 acts as an oxidising agent in alkaline medium. When alkaline\nKMnO4 is treated with KI, iodide ion is oxidised to ……… .','IO₃⁻','When KMnO4 acts as an oxidizing agent in alkaline medium, it converts iodide ion (I⁻) to iodate ion (IO₃⁻). The overall reaction is:\n\n2MnO₄⁻ + 10I⁻ + 6H₂O → 2MnO₂ + 5IO₃⁻ + 12OH⁻\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is IO₃⁻.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','I2','IO₄⁻','IO₃⁻','IO-','NEW','12-234','12-104',NULL),
(82561,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is not correct?','Copper liberates hydrogen from acids','"Copper liberates hydrogen from acids" is not correct. Copper is not reactive enough to liberate hydrogen from acids, and therefore does not react with them. The other statements are correct:\n\n"In its higher oxidation states, manganese forms stable compounds with oxygen and fluorine"\n"Mn3+ and Co3+ are oxidising agents in aqueous solution"\n"Ti2+ and Cr2+ are reducing agents in aqueous solution"','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Copper liberates hydrogen from acids','Ti2+ and Cr2+ are reducing agents in aqueous solution','Mn3+ and Co3+ are oxidising agents in aqueous solution','In its higher oxidation states, manganese forms stable compounds with oxygen\nand fluorine','NEW','12-232','12-102',NULL),
(82562,'chemistry','When acidified K2Cr2O7 solution is added to Sn 2+ salt then Sn 2+ changes\nto','Sn4+','When acidified K2Cr2O7 solution is added to Sn 2+ salt, Sn2+ changes to Sn4+ because K2Cr2O7 acts as an oxidizing agent in acidic medium. The following redox reaction takes place:\n\nSn2+ + 2H+ + Cr2O7 2- → Sn4+ + Cr3+ + 3H2O\n\nHere, K2Cr2O7 is reduced from its +6 oxidation state to +3 oxidation state, while Sn2+ is oxidized from +2 oxidation state to +4 oxidation state. Therefore, the correct answer is Sn4+.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Sn','Sn+','Sn4+','Sn3+','NEW','12-232','12-102',NULL),
(82563,'chemistry','Highest oxidation state of manganese in fluoride is +4 (MnF4) but\nhighest oxidation state in oxides is +7(Mn2O7) because','in covalent compounds, fluorine can form single bond only while oxygen forms double bond','This is due to the difference in their electronegativities and atomic sizes. Fluorine is more electronegative than oxygen and has a smaller atomic size, so it can form only a single bond with manganese. On the other hand, oxygen has a larger atomic size and can form a double bond with manganese, allowing it to stabilize higher oxidation states.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','fluorine is more electronegative than oxygen','in covalent compounds, fluorine can form single bond only while oxygen forms double bond','fluorine stabilises lower oxidation state','fluorine does not possess d orbitals','NEW','12-226','112-100',NULL),
(82564,'chemistry','Although zirconium belongs to 4d transition series and hafniun to 5d\ntransition series even then they show similar physical and chemical\nproperties because ............. .','both have similar atomic radius','Zirconium (Zr) and Hafnium (Hf) belong to different transition series, but they exhibit similar physical and chemical properties because they have similar atomic radii. The similarity in their atomic radii is due to the lanthanide contraction, which causes a decrease in the size of the atoms and ions of the elements in the fifth period of the periodic table. As a result, the difference in the atomic radii between Zr and Hf is very small, and they both have similar chemical properties.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','both belong to d-block','both belong to the same group of the Periodic Table','both have similar atomic radius','both have same number of electrons','NEW','12-220','12-094',NULL),
(82565,'chemistry','Why is HCl not used to make the medium acidic in oxidation reactions of\nKMnO4 in acidic medium?','KMnO4 oxidisesHCl into Cl2 which is also an oxidising agent','HCl is not used to make the medium acidic in oxidation reactions of KMnO4 in acidic medium because KMnO4 oxidizes HCl into Cl2, which is also an oxidizing agent. This reaction can lead to undesired side reactions and make it difficult to control the reaction conditions.\n\nIn acidic medium, KMnO4 acts as a strong oxidizing agent, and it can oxidize a wide range of organic and inorganic compounds. It is commonly used in analytical chemistry and organic chemistry for the oxidation of organic compounds, such as alcohols, aldehydes, and ketones.\n\nTo create an acidic medium for KMnO4 oxidation reactions, sulfuric acid (H2SO4) is typically used instead of HCl. H2SO4 is a strong acid that does not react with KMnO4 and provides a stable acidic environment for the oxidation reactions. Additionally, the use of H2SO4 prevents the formation of Cl2, which can interfere with the reaction and produce undesirable results.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Both HCl and KMnO4 act as oxidising agents','KMnO4 acts as a reducing agent in the presence ofHCl','KMnO4 is a weaker oxidising agent thanHCl','KMnO4 oxidisesHCl into Cl2 which is also an oxidising agent','NEW','12-232','12-106',NULL),
(82566,'chemistry','Generally transition elements and their salts are coloured due to the\npresence of unpaired electrons in metal ions. Which of the following\ncompounds are coloured?\n\na. KMnO4\nb. Ce(SO4)2\nc. TiCl4\nd. Cu2Cl2','a,b','KMnO4 is a highly colored compound due to the presence of charge transfer bands in its spectrum, which is not caused by the presence of unpaired electrons in metal ions.\n\nSimilarly, Ce(SO4)2 is yellow colored due to the presence of charge transfer bands, and not due to f-f transitions. The oxidation state of cerium in Ce(SO4)2 is +4 with 4f0 electronic configuration.\n\nTiCl4 and Cu2Cl2 are not colored due to the absence of unpaired electrons in their metal ions. TiCl4 has a d0 configuration, while Cu2Cl2 has a d10 configuration, which means that all the d electrons are paired and there are no unpaired electrons to cause absorption of light in the visible region.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,d','c,d','b,c','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(82567,'chemistry','Transition elements show magnetic moment due to spin and orbital\nmotion of electrons. Which of the following metallic ions have almost\nsame spin only magnetic moment?\n\na. Co2+\nb. Cr2+\nc. Mn2+\nd. Cr3+','a,d','The magnetic moment of transition metal ions is due to the presence of unpaired electrons in their d orbitals. The number of unpaired electrons determines the magnitude of the magnetic moment. In this case, Co2+ and Cr3+ have almost the same spin-only magnetic moment because they both have three unpaired electrons.\n\nThe electronic configuration of Co2+ is [Ar] 3d7, and it has three unpaired electrons in its d orbitals. The electronic configuration of Cr3+ is [Ar] 3d3, and it also has three unpaired electrons in its d orbitals.\n\nOn the other hand, Cr2+ and Mn2+ have four and five unpaired electrons, respectively, due to their electronic configurations [Ar] 3d4 and [Ar] 3d5. Therefore, their spin-only magnetic moments are not almost the same as those of Co2+ and Cr3+.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,d','c,d','b,c','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(82568,'chemistry','In the form of dichromate, Cr(VI) is a strong oxidising agent in acidic medium but Mo(VI) in MoO3 and W(VI) in WO3 are not because\n\n(a) Cr (VI) is more stable thanMo(VI) and W(VI).\n\n(b)Mo (VI) and W (VI) are more stable than Cr(VI).\n\n(c) Higher oxidation states of heavier members of group-6 of transition series are more stable.\n\n(d) Lower oxidation states of heavier members of group-6 of transition series are more stable.','b,c','The stability of an oxidation state depends on the electronic configuration and size of the atom. In the case of group 6 elements, as we move down the group, the size of the atom increases and the effective nuclear charge experienced by the outermost electrons decreases. This leads to an increase in the stability of higher oxidation states, as the larger atoms can accommodate more electrons in their outermost orbitals.\n\nTherefore, Mo(VI) and W(VI) are more stable than Cr(VI) because they are larger in size and have a greater ability to stabilize the higher oxidation state. As a result, MoO3 and WO3 are not strong oxidizing agents in acidic medium compared to dichromate (Cr(VI)), which is highly stable due to the presence of two chromium atoms in the ion.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,d','c,d','b,c','NEW','12-223','12-097',NULL),
(82569,'chemistry','Which of the following actinoids show oxidation states upto +7?\n\na. Am\nb. Pu\nc. U\nd. Np','b,d','The actinoids or actinides are the elements in the f-block of the periodic table, which includes the elements from atomic number 89 to 103. These elements have the ability to show a wide range of oxidation states due to the presence of partially filled 5f orbitals.\n\nOut of the given options, Plutonium (Pu) and Neptunium (Np) are the actinoids that can show oxidation states up to +7. Pu and Np have partially filled 5f orbitals that can accommodate up to seven electrons, allowing them to exhibit a range of oxidation states from +3 to +7. On the other hand, Americium (Am) and Uranium (U) can show oxidation states up to +6 as they have partially filled 5f orbitals that can accommodate up to six electrons.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,d','b,d','b,c','NEW','12-237','12-107',NULL),
(82570,'chemistry','Which of the following actinoids have one electron in 6d orbital?\n(a) U (Atomic number. 92) \n(b) Np (Atomic number. 93)\n(c) Pu (Atomic number. 94) \n(d) Am (Atomic number. 95)','a,b','The general electronic configuration of actinoids is indeed (n-2)f(n-1)d1-10ns2, where n represents the principal quantum number.\n\nFor the specific case of U (atomic number 92) and Np (atomic number 93), their electronic configurations are:\n\nU: [Rn] 5f3 6d1 7s2\nNp: [Rn] 5f4 6d1 7s2\nAs you can see, both U and Np have one electron in the 6d orbital, while the remaining electrons are distributed among the 5f and 7s orbitals.\n\nOn the other hand, Pu (atomic number 94) and Am (atomic number 95) have their 6d orbitals fully occupied, as shown by their electronic configurations:\n\nPu: [Rn] 5f6 6d0 7s2\nAm: [Rn] 5f7 6d0 7s2','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,d','b,d','b,c','NEW','12-237','12-107',NULL),
(82571,'chemistry','Which of the following lanthanoids show +2 oxidation state besides the characteristic oxidation state +3 of lanthanoids?\n\n(a) Ce \n(b) Eu \n(c) Yb \n(d) Ho','b,c','The characteristic oxidation state of lanthanoids is indeed +3, but some of them can also exhibit other oxidation states.\n\nThe electronic configurations and possible oxidation states of the lanthanoids in the options are:\n\n(a) Cerium (Ce, atomic number 58) - [Xe] 4f15 5d1 6s2 - can have oxidation states of +3 and +4\n(b) Europium (Eu, atomic number 63) - [Xe] 4f7 6s2 - can have oxidation states of +2 and +3\n(c) Ytterbium (Yb, atomic number 70) - [Xe] 4f14 6s2 - can have oxidation states of +2 and +3\n(d) Holmium (Ho, atomic number 67) - [Xe] 4f11 6s2 - can have oxidation state of +3\n\nTherefore, the lanthanoids that can show an oxidation state of +2 in addition to the characteristic +3 oxidation state are Eu and Yb.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,d','b,d','b,c','NEW','12-236','12-106',NULL),
(82572,'chemistry','Which of the following ions show higher spin only magnetic moment value?\n(a) Ti3+ \n(b)Mn2+\n(c) Fe2+ \n(d) Co3+','b,c','The magnetic moment of a transition metal ion depends on the number of unpaired electrons in its d-orbitals. When these electrons are paired, they cancel each other\'s spin and contribute less to the magnetic moment. On the other hand, when there are unpaired electrons, they add up their spin and create a higher magnetic moment.\n\nIn the given options, Ti3+ has only one d-electron and is a high-spin configuration with a single unpaired electron. Mn2+ has five d-electrons and can have either a high-spin or low-spin configuration, depending on the strength of the crystal field. Fe2+ has four d-electrons and can also have either high-spin or low-spin configuration. Co3+ has six d-electrons and is a low-spin configuration, where all the electrons are paired up in the lower t2g orbitals.\n\nThe crystal field splitting energy (CFSE) is the energy difference between the lower t2g and higher eg orbitals in a crystal field. When the CFSE is high, as in Co3+, it becomes energetically unfavorable for electrons to occupy the higher eg orbitals and they pair up in the lower t2g orbitals. This results in a low-spin configuration with no unpaired electrons and a lower magnetic moment.\n\nIn contrast, when the CFSE is low, as in Fe2+ and Mn2+, the electrons prefer to occupy the higher eg orbitals, resulting in a high-spin configuration with unpaired electrons and a higher magnetic moment.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is (b) Mn2+ and (c) Fe2+.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,d','b,d','b,c','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(82573,'chemistry','Transition elements form binary compounds with halogens. Which of the following elements will form MF3 type compounds?\n(a) Cr \n(b) Co \n(c) Cu \n(d) Ni','a,b','The oxidation states of transition elements can range from +1 to +8, allowing them to form a wide variety of binary compounds with halogens. When a transition element forms a binary compound with a halogen, the oxidation state of the transition element is often related to the group number of the halogen.\n\nIn the case of MF3 compounds, the transition element has an oxidation state of +3, and the halogen is in group 17 (the halogens). Among the given options, only Cr and Co have stable +3 oxidation states, which allow them to form MF3 type compounds with halogens. On the other hand, Cu and Ni do not have stable +3 oxidation states and thus do not form CuF3 and NiF3.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is (a) Cr and (b) Co.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,d','b,d','b,c','NEW','12-225','12-099',NULL),
(82574,'chemistry','Which of the following will not act as oxidising agents?\n\n(a) CrO3 \n(b)MoO3 \n(c) WO3 \n(d) CrO₄²⁻','b,c','The correct answer is (b) MoO3 and (c) WO3. Both molybdenum and tungsten can have very high oxidation states, which generally makes them good oxidizing agents. However, in the given options, Mo and W are already in their highest oxidation state, and there is no lower oxidation state available to show stability. Therefore, they will not act as oxidizing agents.\n\nOn the other hand, CrO3 and CrO4^2- both have chromium in the +6 oxidation state, which is a highly oxidizing state. Therefore, they can act as oxidizing agents.\n\nIn summary, the species that will not act as oxidizing agents are those that are already in their highest oxidation state and do not show stability in lower oxidation states.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,d','b,d','b,c','NEW','12-227','12-101',NULL),
(82575,'chemistry','Although +3 is the characteristic oxidation state for lanthanoids but cerium also shows +4 oxidation state because\n(a) it has variable ionisation enthalpy\n(b) it has a tendency to attain noble gas configuration\n(c) it has a tendency to attain f0 configuration\n(d) it resembles Pb4+','b,c','Cerium is a member of the lanthanoid series, and the characteristic oxidation state for the lanthanoids is +3. However, cerium is unique in that it also exhibits a +4 oxidation state. This is due to the fact that cerium has a partially filled 4f orbital, which makes it more prone to exhibiting variable oxidation states than other lanthanoids.\n\nThe electronic configuration of cerium is [Xe] 4f^1 5d^1 6s^2, and when it loses four electrons, it can achieve a noble gas configuration of [Xe]. In addition, it can achieve an f0 configuration, which is energetically favorable due to the stability of completely filled or completely empty f-orbitals. Therefore, cerium has a tendency to attain noble gas configuration and attain f^0 configuration, which allows it to exhibit a +4 oxidation state.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is (b) it has a tendency to attain noble gas configuration and (c) it has a tendency to attain f0 configurati','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,d','b,d','b,c','NEW','12-239','12-109',NULL),
(82576,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Cu 2+ iodide is not known.\n\nReason (R) Cu 2+ oxidises I- to iodine.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Copper(II) ion (Cu2+) is a strong oxidizing agent, which means it can oxidize other substances by accepting electrons from them. In the case of Cu2+ iodide, Cu2+ can oxidize I- ions to iodine (I2). This means that Cu2+ iodide cannot exist because Cu2+ ions will react with iodide ions (I-) to form iodine (I2) instead.\n\nTherefore, both the assertion and reason are true, and the reason is a correct explanation of the assertion. The inability of Cu2+ iodide to exist is due to the oxidizing properties of Cu2+ ions that allow them to convert iodide ions to iodine.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are false.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','NEW','12-226','12-100',NULL),
(82577,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Separation of Zr and Hf is difficult.\n\nReason (R) Because Zr andHf lie in the same group of the Periodic Table.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion (A) is true, but the reason (R) is not a correct explanation of the assertion.\n\nThe separation of Zr and Hf is indeed difficult due to their almost identical ionic radii caused by the lanthanoid contraction. This contraction occurs because the 4f electrons are not very effective at shielding the increasing nuclear charge from the outermost 5d electrons. As a result, there is very little difference in the sizes of Zr4+ and Hf4+ ions, making it difficult to separate them using conventional methods.\n\nTherefore, the assertion is true, but the reason given is not a correct explanation for it.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are false.','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','NEW','12-220','12-094',NULL),
(82578,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Actinoids form relatively less stable complexes as compared to lanthanoids.\n\nReason (R) Actinoids can utilise their 5f orbitals alongwith 6d orbitals in\nbonding but lanthanoids do not use their 4f orbital for bonding.','Assertion is false but Reason is true','The stability of complexes formed by actinoids and lanthanoids is not solely dependent on the utilization of f orbitals in bonding. Both actinoids and lanthanoids can use their f orbitals in bonding, but the extent of their involvement depends on various factors such as the size of the metal ion, the charge on the ion, and the nature of the ligands.\n\nThe reason given in the statement is true as lanthanoids generally use their 4f orbitals for shielding and do not participate in bonding to a significant extent. On the other hand, actinoids have relatively more diffuse 5f orbitals that participate in bonding, which can lead to the formation of relatively less stable complexes.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is false but Reason is true','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82579,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Cu cannot liberate hydrogen from acids.\n\nReason (R) Because it has positive electrode potential.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Cu can not liberate hydrogen from acids because it has positive electrode potential.\nMetals having negative value of electrode potential liberateH2 gas.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is false but Reason is true','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','NEW','12-225','12-099',NULL),
(82580,'chemistry','Assertion (A) The highest oxidation state of osmium is +8 .\n\nReason (R) Osmium is a 5d-block element.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','The highest oxidation state of osmium is +8, and the reason for this is its ability to expand its octet by using all 8 valence electrons (2 from 6s and 6 from 5d)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Ncert Exemplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is false but Reason is true','Assertion is true and reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','NEW','12-222','12-096',NULL),
(82581,'chemistry','Most common oxidation states of Ce (cerium) are [2002]','3+,4+','Cerium is a rare earth element with atomic number 58. It has four valence electrons and can form several oxidation states, but the most common ones are +3 and +4.\n\nThe +3 oxidation state is the most stable and common oxidation state of Cerium. It is formed by losing three electrons from the outermost 5d and 6s orbitals. Cerium in the +3 oxidation state is a strong reducing agent and can be easily oxidized to the +4 oxidation state.\n\nThe +4 oxidation state is less stable than the +3 state, but it is still a common oxidation state of Cerium. It is formed by losing four electrons from the outermost 5d and 6s orbitals. Cerium in the +4 oxidation state is a good oxidizing agent and can be easily reduced to the +3 oxidation state.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 3+,4+.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','2+,3+','3+,5+','3+,4+','2+,4+','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(82582,'chemistry','Arrange in increasing order of their ionic radii . Refer diagram(82582Q)','a','The lanthanide contraction is a phenomenon that occurs in the lanthanide series, where there is a gradual decrease in the atomic and ionic radii of the elements from La to Lu. This is due to the increasing nuclear charge that attracts the electrons towards the nucleus, making the atomic and ionic radii smaller.\n\nIn the given options, the correct order in atomic size due to lanthanide contraction is La³⁺ > Ce³⁺ > Pr³⁺ > Nd³⁺ > Pm³⁺ > Sm³⁺ > Eu³⁺ > Gd³⁺ > Tb³⁺ > Dy³⁺ > Ho³⁺ > Er³⁺ > Tm³⁺ > Yb³⁺ > Lu³⁺.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a','d','c','b','NEW','12-219','12-093','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/82582q.png'),
(82583,'chemistry','Which of the following ions has the maximum magnetic moment? [2002]','Mn+2','The magnetic moment of an ion depends on the number of unpaired electrons present in its d-orbitals. The higher the number of unpaired electrons, the higher is the magnetic moment.\n\nOut of the given options, the Mn²⁺ ion has the maximum number of unpaired electrons.\n\nMn²⁺ ion has 5 unpaired electrons (3d⁵) and a magnetic moment of 5.9 BM.\nFe²⁺ ion has 4 unpaired electrons (3d⁶) and a magnetic moment of 4.9 BM.\nTi²⁺ ion has 2 unpaired electrons (3d²) and a magnetic moment of 1.75 BM.\nCr²⁺ ion has 3 unpaired electrons (3d⁴) and a magnetic moment of 3.87 BM.\nTherefore, the correct answer is Mn²⁺.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','Mn+2','Cr2+','Ti2+','Fe2+','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(82584,'chemistry','The most stable ion is. Refer diagram(n0806)','c','The stability of a complex ion depends on various factors, such as the size of the ligands, the charge on the metal ion, and the nature of the ligands. In general, the stronger the bond between the metal ion and the ligands, the more stable the complex ion.\n\nOut of the given options, [Fe(CN)6)]³⁻ is the most stable ion because CN⁻ is a strong ligand that forms a covalent bond with the Fe³⁺ ion, due to its high electronegativity and small size. This results in a high stability constant for the [Fe(CN)6)]³⁻ ion.\n\nOn the other hand, [Fe(OH)3)]³⁻ and [Fe(8iO)6)]³⁻ are less stable because OH⁻ and H₂O ligands are weaker than CN⁻, and they form weaker bonds with the Fe³⁺ ion.\n\n[Fe(Cl)6)]³⁻ is also less stable because Cl⁻ is a weaker ligand than CN⁻, and it forms weaker bonds with the Fe³⁺ ion.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is option (c) [Fe(CN)6)]³⁻.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a','d','c','b','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(82585,'chemistry','When KMnO4 acts as an oxidising agent and ultimately forms [MnO4]-, MnO2, Mn2O3, Mn2+ then the no. of electrons transferred in each case respectively is','1,3,4,5','In each case, the oxidation state of manganese changes. The number of electrons transferred can be determined by calculating the change in the oxidation state of manganese.\n\nKMnO4 → [MnO4]⁻: In this reaction, the oxidation state of manganese changes from +7 to +6. Hence, one electron is transferred.\nKMnO4 → MnO2: In this reaction, the oxidation state of manganese changes from +7 to +4. Hence, three electrons are transferred.\nKMnO4 → Mn2O3: In this reaction, the oxidation state of manganese changes from +7 to +3. Hence, four electrons are transferred.\nKMnO4 → Mn²⁺: In this reaction, the oxidation state of manganese changes from +7 to +2. Hence, five electrons are transferred.\nTherefore, the correct answer is 1, 3, 4, 5.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','4,3,1,5','3,5,7,1','1,3,4,5','1,5,3,7','NEW','12-232','12-106',NULL),
(82586,'chemistry','The radius of La3+ is 1.06 nm. Which one of the following will be closest to the radius of Lu3+','0.85 nm','The radius of La³⁺ is 1.06 nm, and we need to find the closest value to the radius of Lu³⁺.\n\nThe lanthanide series shows a regular decrease in size of atoms and ions as we move from La³⁺ to Lu³⁺. This decrease in size is known as the lanthanide contraction. The contraction arises due to an increase in the effective nuclear charge (due to the addition of electrons to the same principal quantum shell) which attracts the electrons towards the nucleus, leading to a reduction in the size of the atom/ion.\n\nTherefore, we can expect the radius of Lu³⁺ to be smaller than that of La³⁺. The option closest to this expectation is 0.85 nm.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','1.4 nm','1.6 nm','0.85 nm','1.06 nm','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(82587,'chemistry','Ammonia forms the complex ion [Cu(NH3)4]2+ with copper ions in alkaline solutions but not in acidic solutions. What is the reason for it? [2003]','In acidic solutions protons coordinate with ammonia molecule forming NH4+ ions and NH3 molecules are available','The reason why ammonia forms the complex ion [Cu(NH3)4]2+ with copper ions in alkaline solutions but not in acidic solutions is that in acidic solutions, protons (H+) coordinate with ammonia (NH3) molecules, forming NH4+ ions, which are not able to form the complex with copper ions. The complex formation requires uncoordinated NH3 molecules to bond with copper ions.\n\nOn the other hand, in alkaline solutions, excess OH- ions are present, which can abstract the protons from NH3 molecules, making them available for complex formation with copper ions. The reaction can be represented as follows:\n\nCu2+ + 4NH3 + 2OH- → [Cu(NH3)4]2+ + 2H2O','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','In acidic solutions protons coordinate with ammonia molecule forming NH4+ ions and NH3 molecules are available','In acidic solutions hydrations protects copper ions','Copper hydroxide is an amphoteric substance','In alkaline solutions insoluble Cu(OH)2 is precipitated which is soluble in excess of any alkali','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(82588,'chemistry','A red solid is insoluble in water. However it\nbecomes soluble if some KI is added to water.\nHeating the red solid in a test tube results in\nliberation of some violet coloured fumes and\ndroplets of a metal appear on the cooler parts of\nthe test tube. The red solid is [2003]','HgI2','HgI2 is insoluble in water, but it becomes soluble in the presence of potassium iodide (KI) due to the formation of the soluble complex ion [HgI4]2-.\n\nOn heating HgI2, it decomposes to form mercury and iodine vapors. The violet color of the fumes indicates the presence of iodine vapors, while the droplets of mercury condensing on the cooler parts of the test tube indicate the presence of mercury.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','HgI2','(NH4)2Cr2O7','Pb3O4','HgO','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(82589,'chemistry','A reduction in atomic size with increase in atomic\nnumber is a characteristic of elements of\n[2003)','f block','The reduction in atomic size with increase in atomic number is known as the "lanthanide contraction," which is a characteristic of the f-block elements.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','d block','high atomic masses','radioactive series','f block','NEW','12-220','12-094',NULL),
(82590,'chemistry','What would happen when a solution of\npotassium chromate is treated with an excess of\ndilute nitric acid? [2003)','Cr2O₇²⁻ and H2O are formed','When a solution of potassium chromate (K2CrO4) is treated with an excess of dilute nitric acid (HNO3), the following reaction takes place:\n\nK2CrO4 + 4HNO3 → 2CrO3 + 2KNO3 + 2H2O\n\nThe orange-yellow potassium chromate is converted into orange-red chromium trioxide (CrO3), which is a strong oxidizing agent. The chromium trioxide then reacts with water to form chromic acid (H2CrO4), which further reacts with nitric acid to form chromium(VI) nitrate (Cr(NO3)6) and water:\n\nCrO3 + H2O → H2CrO4\nH2CrO4 + 6HNO3 → Cr(NO3)6 + 4H2O\n\nThus, the final products of the reaction are Cr2O₇²⁻ and H2O.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','Cr2O₇²⁻ and H2O are formed','Cr3+ and Cr2O₇²⁻ are formed','Cr2O₄²⁻ is oxidised to +7 state of Cr','Cr2O₄²⁻ is reduced to +3 state of Cr','NEW','12-232','12-106',NULL),
(82591,'chemistry','Which one of the following nitrates will leave\nbehind a metal on strong heating ? [2003)','Silver nitrate','When silver nitrate is heated strongly, it decomposes to form silver metal, nitrogen dioxide and oxygen. The chemical equation for the reaction is:\n\n2AgNO3(s) → 2Ag(s) + 2NO2(g) + O2(g)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','Copper nitrate','Ferric nitrate','Silver nitrate','Manganese nitrate','NEW','12-233','12-107',NULL),
(82592,'chemistry','Of the following outer electronic configurations of atoms, the highest oxidation state is achieved by which one of them? [2004]','(n-1)d5ns2','The highest oxidation state is generally achieved when the atom has an incomplete d subshell. Among the given options, the outer electronic configuration of (n-1)d5ns2 has an incomplete d subshell (with five electrons), which can participate in oxidation reactions. Therefore, the highest oxidation state is achieved by (n-1)d5ns2.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','(n-1)d3ns2','(n-1)d5ns2','(n-1)d8ns2','(n-1)d5ns1','NEW','12-220','12-094',NULL),
(82593,'chemistry','The soldiers ofNapolean army while at Alps during freezing winter suffered a serious problem as regards to the tin buttons of their uniforms. White metallic tin buttons got converted to grey power. This transformation is related to\n[2004]','a change in the crystalline structure of tin','The transformation of white metallic tin buttons to grey powder is related to a change in the crystalline structure of tin. This phenomenon is known as tin pest or tin disease. At very low temperatures, below 13.2 °C, tin undergoes a transformation from its normal form, called white tin or beta-tin, to a brittle, powdery form called gray tin or alpha-tin. This transformation is due to a change in the crystal structure of tin from a metallic, cubic structure to a non-metallic, tetragonal structure. The transformation is spontaneous and irreversible, and can cause the destruction of tin objects.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a change in the partial pressure ofoxygen in the air','an interaction with water vapour contained in the humid air','an interaction with nitrogen of the air at very low temperature','a change in the crystalline structure of tin','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(82594,'chemistry','Among the properties (a) reducing (b) oxidising (c) complexing, the set of properties shown by CN-ion towards metal species is [2004]','c,a','CN- ion can act as a strong ligand to form coordination compounds with metal ions, so it exhibits complexing properties.\n\nCN- ion can also act as a reducing agent because it has a high affinity for the metal ions and can form stable complexes with them. In the process, it gains an electron, which corresponds to reduction.\n\nHowever, CN- ion is not an oxidizing agent, so it does not exhibit oxidizing properties.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','c,a','a,b,c','a,b','b,c','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(82595,'chemistry','Cerium (Z = 58) is an important member of the lanthanoids. Which of the following statements about cerium is incorrect? [2004]','The +4 oxidation state of cerium is not known in solutions','The +4 oxidation state of cerium is commonly known and stable in solutions','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','The +4 oxidation state of cerium is not known in solutions','Cerium (IV) acts as an oxidizing agent','The common oxidation states of cerium are +3and+4','The + 3 oxidation state of cerium is more stable than the +4 oxidation state','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82596,'chemistry','The correct order of magnetic moments (spin only values in B.M.) among is','c','The correct order of magnetic moments (spin only values in B.M.) is:\n\n(c) [MnCl4]2- > [CoCl4]2- > [Fe(CN)6]4-\n\nThis is because the magnetic moment is related to the number of unpaired electrons in the complex ion. The complex with more unpaired electrons will have a larger magnetic moment.\n\nIn option (a), [Fe(CN)6]4- has the largest number of unpaired electrons, but the order of [MnCl4]2- and [CoCl4]2- is reversed.\n\nIn option (b), the order of [MnCl4]2- and [Fe(CN)6]4- is reversed compared to option (c), and [CoCl4]2- has the same position as in option (a).\n\nIn option (d), [Fe(CN)6]4- has the largest magnetic moment, but the order of [CoCl4]2- and [MnCl4]2- is reversed compared to option (c).\n\nTherefore, option (c) is the correct answer.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a','d','c','b','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(82597,'chemistry','Heating mixture of Cu2O and Cu2S wil give','Cu+ SO2','Heating a mixture of Cu2O and Cu2S results in the formation of copper and sulfur dioxide gas. This can be represented by the following equation:\n\n2Cu2O + 2Cu2S → 6Cu + SO2','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','Cu2SO3','Cu+ SO2','Cu+ SO3','CuO+CuS','NEW','-',NULL,NULL),
(82598,'chemistry','The oxidation state of chromium in the final product formed by the reaction between Kl and acidified potassium dichromate solution is: [2005]','3+','When acidified potassium dichromate (K2Cr2O7) is reacted with KI in an acidic medium, the following redox reaction takes place:\n\nK2Cr2O7 + 6KI + 7H2SO4 → 3I2 + Cr2(SO4)3 + 4K2SO4 + 7H2O\n\nIn this reaction, the K2Cr2O7 is reduced to Cr2(SO4)3 and the I^- ion is oxidized to I2. The iodine molecules combine to form a deep violet solution.\n\nThe overall reaction can be represented as:\n\n3H+ + Cr2O72- + 6I^- → 3I2 + 2Cr3+ + 7H2O\n\nTherefore, the oxidation state of chromium in the final product formed by the reaction between KI and acidified potassium dichromate solution is +3.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','3+','4+','6+','2+','NEW','12-236','12-110',NULL),
(82599,'chemistry','Calomel (Hg2Cl2) on reaction with ammonium hydroxide gives [2005]','HgNH2Cl','The reaction between calomel (Hg2Cl2) and ammonium hydroxide (NH4OH) is a double displacement reaction, which can be represented as follows:\n\nHg2Cl2 + 2NH4OH → HgNH2Cl + NH4Cl + H2O\n\nIn this reaction, the ammonium hydroxide reacts with the calomel to form a complex salt, ammoniated mercury chloride (HgNH2Cl), along with ammonium chloride (NH4Cl) and water (H2O) as byproducts.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','HgO','HgNH2Cl','NH2-Hg-Hg-Cl','Hg2O','NEW','-',NULL,NULL),
(82600,'chemistry','The lanthanide contraction is responsible for the fact that [2005]','Zr and Hf have about the same radius','The lanthanide contraction refers to the steady decrease in the atomic and ionic radii of the elements with increasing atomic number that occurs as one moves across the lanthanide series. As a result of this, the 4f electrons are not effective in shielding the increasing nuclear charge from the outer electrons, resulting in a relatively constant atomic size for the elements following the lanthanides. This means that the atomic radii of Zr and Hf, which are located immediately after the lanthanides, are very similar.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','Zr and Zn have the same oxidation state','Zr and Y have about the same radius','Zr and Nb have similar oxidation state','Zr and Hf have about the same radius','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(82601,'chemistry','The value of the \'spin only\' magnetic moment for one of the following configurations is 2.84 BM. The correct one is [2005]','d 4 (in strong ligand fields)','The \'spin only\' magnetic moment is given by the formula µ = √n(n+2) BM, where n is the number of unpaired electrons.\n\nFor d4 in a strong ligand field, there are two unpaired electrons, so the magnetic moment is given by:\n\nµ = √2(2+2) BM = √8 BM = 2.83 BM (approx.)\n\nTherefore, the correct option is d4 (in strong ligand fields) with a magnetic moment of approximately 2.83 BM.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','d 5 (in strong ligand field)','d 4 (in strong ligand fields)','d 4 (in weak ligand fields)','d3 (in weak as well as in strong fields)','NEW','12-228','12-102',NULL),
(82602,'chemistry','Which of the following factors may be regarded as the main cause of lanthanide contraction? [2005]','Poor shielding of one of 4f electron by\nanother in the subshell','The lanthanide contraction refers to the phenomenon where the atomic radii of the elements in the lanthanide series decrease gradually as the atomic number increases. This is primarily due to the effective shielding of one of the 4f electrons by another in the subshell, which results in a decrease in the effective nuclear charge experienced by the outer electrons and a decrease in atomic size.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'3','mcq','Greater shielding of 5d electrons by 4f electrons','Poor shielding of one of 4f electron by\nanother in the subshell','Effective shielding of one of 4f electrons\nby another in the subshell','Poorer shielding of 5d electrons by 4f\nelectrons','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(82603,'chemistry','A metal, M forms chlorides in its +2 and +4 oxidation states. Which of the following statements about these chlorides is correct? [2006]','MCl2 is more ionic than MCl4','The +2 oxidation state is more stable for metals of Groups 1 and 2, whereas the +4 oxidation state is more stable for the transition metals. Therefore, it is likely that the metal M is a transition metal. In general, the chlorides of metals in their +2 oxidation state are more ionic than those in their +4 oxidation state. This is because in the +2 oxidation state, the metal ion has fewer electrons and is smaller, so it has a higher charge density, which makes it more attractive to the chloride ions. In addition, the MCl2 chlorides are typically less hydrolyzed than the MCl4 chlorides, less volatile, and less soluble in anhydrous ethanol.\n\nTherefore, the correct statement about the chlorides of the metal M is that MCl2 is more ionic than MCl4.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','MCl2 is more ionic than MCl4','MCl2 is more soluble in anhydrous ethanol than MCl4','MCl2 is more volatile than MCl4','MCl2 is more easily hydrolysed than MCl4','NEW','12-222','12-096',NULL),
(82604,'chemistry','Lanthanoid contraction is caused due to [2006]','the imperfect shielding on outer electrons by 4felectrons from the nuclear charge','The lanthanoid contraction is caused by the imperfect shielding of outer electrons by 4f electrons from the nuclear charge. As we move from Ce to Lu, the number of electrons in the 4f subshell remains the same (14), but the atomic radius decreases due to an increase in effective nuclear charge, which is not fully shielded by the 4f electrons. This results in a contraction of the 4f subshell, which in turn affects the properties of the elements.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','the same effective nuclear charge from Ce to Lu','the appreciable shielding on outer electrons by Sd electrons from the nuclear charge','the appreciable shielding on outer electrons by 4f electrons from the nuclear charge','the imperfect shielding on outer electrons by 4felectrons from the nuclear charge','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(82605,'chemistry','The "spin-only" magnetic moment [in units of Bohr magneton, (μB)] of Ni2+ in aqueous solution would be (At. No. Ni = 28) [2006]','2.84','The "spin-only" magnetic moment (in units of Bohr magneton, μB) of a transition metal ion is given by the formula:\n\n√n(n+2) BM\n\nwhere n is the number of unpaired electrons.\n\nIn the case of Ni2+, the electronic configuration is: 1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 3d8.\n\nSince there are 2 unpaired electrons in the 3d subshell, n = 2.\n\nSubstituting this value in the formula, we get:\n\n√2(2+2) = 2.83 ≈ 2.84 BM.\n\nTherefore, the "spin-only" magnetic moment of Ni2+ in aqueous solution is 2.84 BM.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','6','4.9','2.84','1.73','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(82606,'chemistry','The stability of dihalides of Si, Ge, Sn and Pb increases steadily in the sequence.','d','The inert pair effect and the trend in stability of dihalides for group 14 elements. As we go down the group, the stability of the lower oxidation state (+2 for group 14 elements) increases due to the increasing size of the atoms, which makes it easier for the valence shell electrons to participate in bonding. This trend is reflected in the order of stability of the dihalides, with the higher oxidation states becoming relatively less stable as we go down the group, leading to the correct order of SiX2 < GeX2 < SnX2 < PbX2.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a','d','c','b','NEW','12-222','12-096',NULL),
(82607,'chemistry','Identify the incorrect statement among the following: [2007]','4f and 5f orbitals are equally shielded.','The shielding effect of the inner electrons is less effective for the 5f orbitals compared to the 4f orbitals. This is because the 5f orbitals are more diffuse and farther away from the nucleus, which results in poor shielding of the outer electrons from the nuclear charge.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','4f and 5f orbitals are equally shielded.','The chemistry of various lanthanoids is very similar.','La and Lu have partially filled d-orbitals and no other partially filled orbitals.','d-Block elements show irregular and erratic chemical properties among themselves.','NEW','12-219','12-093',NULL),
(82608,'chemistry','The actinoids exhibit more number of oxidation states in general than the lanthanoids. This is because [2007]','the 5f orbitals extend further from the\nnucleus than the 4f orbitals','The 5f orbitals of the actinoids extend further from the nucleus than the 4f orbitals of the lanthanoids. This makes the 5f electrons of the actinoids more available for bonding, leading to a greater variety of oxidation states compared to the lanthanoids.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','the 5f orbitals extend further from the\nnucleus than the 4f orbitals','the actinoids are more reactive than the lanthanoids','there is a similarity between 4f and 5f orbitals in their angular part of the wave function','the 5f orbitals are more buried than the 4f\norbitals','NEW','12-234','12-108',NULL),
(82609,'chemistry','Larger number of oxidation states are exhibited\nby the actinoids than those by the lanthanoids,\nthe main reason being [2008]','lesser energy difference between 5f and\n6d than between 4f and 5d orbitals','Both actinoids and lanthanoids have partially filled f-orbitals, which give rise to their unique properties. However, actinoids also have partially filled d-orbitals in addition to f-orbitals, while lanthanoids do not.\n\nThe energy difference between the 5f and 6d orbitals in actinoids is smaller than the energy difference between the 4f and 5d orbitals in lanthanoids. This means that it is easier for actinoids to remove electrons from both the 5f and 6d orbitals, leading to a larger number of possible oxidation states. In contrast, lanthanoids can only remove electrons from their partially filled f-orbitals, limiting the number of possible oxidation states.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','4f orbitals more diffused than the 5f orbitals','more reactive nature of the actionids than\nthe lanthanoids','more energy difference between 5f and 6d\nthan between 4 f and 5d orbitals','lesser energy difference between 5f and\n6d than between 4f and 5d orbitals','NEW','12-234','12-108',NULL),
(82610,'chemistry','Amount of oxalic acid present in a solution can\nbe determined by its titration with KMnO 4\nsolution in the presence of H2SO 4• The titration\ngives unsatisfactory result when carried out in\nthe presence ofHCl, because HCl [2008]','reduces permanganate to Mn2+','Hydrochloric acid (HCl) does not react with oxalic acid in a titration with potassium permanganate (KMnO4) solution. However, HCl reacts with permanganate to produce chlorine gas, which interferes with the titration and can lead to unsatisfactory results. This is why oxalic acid titrations are typically carried out in the presence of sulfuric acid (H2SO4) instead of hydrochloric acid.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','gets oxidised by oxalic acid to chlorine','Oxidises oxalic acid to carbon doxide and\nwater','reduces permanganate to Mn2+','furnishes H+ ions in addition to those from\noxalic acd','NEW','12-233','12-107',NULL),
(82611,'chemistry','Knowing that the chemistry of lanthanoids(Ln)\nis dominated by its+ 3 oxidation state, which of\nthe following statements is incorrect? [2009]','Ln (III) compounds are generally\ncolourless.','Ln(III) compounds are actually known to exhibit a wide range of colors. The color of Ln(III) compounds depends on various factors, such as the nature of the ligands coordinated to the Ln(III) ion, the oxidation state of the ion, and the crystal field splitting of the complex. For example, Ce(III) compounds are often pale yellow or colorless, while Pr(III) compounds are usually green, and Nd(III) compounds are typically purple. Dy(III) compounds are known to exhibit a range of colors, including pink, purple, and blue. Therefore, the statement that Ln(III) compounds are generally colorless is incorrect','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','The ionic size of Ln(III)decrease in general\nwith increasing atomic number','Because of the large size of the Ln (III) ions\nthe bonding in its compounds is\npredominantly ionic in character.','Ln (III) hydroxide are mainly basic in character','Ln (III) compounds are generally\ncolourless.','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(82612,'chemistry','Iron exhibits+ 2 and+ 3 oxidation states. Which of the following statements about iron is incorrect? [2012]','Ferrous compounds are more easily\nhydrolysed than the corresponding ferric\ncompounds.','Ferric compounds are more easily hydrolyzed than ferrous compounds. This is because in ferric compounds, the iron ion has a higher charge and a smaller size, which leads to stronger electrostatic interactions with water molecules and easier hydrolysis. Ferrous compounds, on the other hand, have a lower charge and a larger size, which results in weaker interactions with water molecules and less hydrolysis.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','Ferrous oxide is more basic in nature than\nthe ferric oxide.','Ferrous compounds are more easily\nhydrolysed than the corresponding ferric\ncompounds.','Ferrous compounds are less volatile than\nthe corresponding ferric compounds.','Ferrous compounds are relatively more\nionic than the corresponding ferric\ncompounds','NEW','12-219','12-093',NULL),
(82613,'chemistry','Which of the following arrangements does not represent the correct order of the property stated against it?\n\n(a) v2+ < Cr2+ < Mn2+ < Fe2+: paramagnetic behaviour\n\n(b) Sc < Ti < Cr < Mn: number of oxidation states\n\n(c) Co3+ < Fe3+ < Cr3+ < Sc3+: stability in aqueous solution\n\n(d) Ni2+ < Ca2+ < Fe2+ < Mn2+: ionic size','a','(a) v2+ < Cr2+ < Mn2+ < Fe2+: paramagnetic behaviour\n\nParamagnetism is the phenomenon where substances are weakly attracted by a magnetic field. This attraction is due to the presence of unpaired electrons in the atoms or ions of the substance. Among the given ions, vanadium(II), chromium(II), manganese(II), and iron(II) have unpaired electrons, and they exhibit paramagnetic behavior.\n\nThe correct order of the property stated in option (a) should be v2+ < Cr2+ < Mn2+ < Fe2+: ferromagnetic or paramagnetic behavior. This is because the number of unpaired electrons in the ions increases in the same order, and therefore, their magnetic behavior should also increase.\n\n(b) Ni2+ < Ca2+ < Fe2+ < Mn2+: ionic size\n\nThe ionic size of an ion is determined by the number of electrons and the effective nuclear charge that it possesses. Among the given ions, Ca2+ has the smallest ionic size, while Mn2+ has the largest ionic size.\n\nThe correct order of the property stated in option (b) is Ni2+ < Ca2+ < Fe2+ < Mn2+: ionic size. This is because the ionic radius of an ion decreases as the nuclear charge of the ion increases.\n\n(c) Co3+ < Fe3+ < Cr3+ < Sc3+: stability in aqueous solution\n\nThe stability of an ion in aqueous solution depends on its ability to form coordination complexes with water molecules. Among the given ions, Sc3+ has the highest charge density and the smallest size, which makes it more stable in aqueous solution.\n\nThe correct order of the property stated in option (c) is Co3+ < Fe3+ < Cr3+ < Sc3+: stability in aqueous solution. This is because the stability of an ion in aqueous solution decreases as the ionic radius of the ion increases.\n\n(d) Sc < Ti < Cr < Mn: number of oxidation states\n\nThe number of oxidation states that an element can exhibit depends on the number of valence electrons that it has. Among the given elements, manganese has the highest number of valence electrons, and therefore, it can exhibit the highest number of oxidation states.\n\nThe correct order of the property stated in option (d) is Sc < Ti < Cr < Mn: number of oxidation states. This is because the number of valence electrons in the elements increases in the same order, and therefore, their ability to exhibit multiple oxidation states should also increase.\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the arrangement in option (a) does not represent the correct order of the property stated against it.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a','d','c','b','NEW','12-222','12-096',NULL),
(82614,'chemistry','Match the catalyst to the correct processes. Refer diagram(n0810)','d','(i) Wacker process: It is a chemical reaction for the conversion of ethylene to acetaldehyde in the presence of a palladium(II) chloride catalyst, PdCl2. Therefore, the catalyst PdCl2 is matched with process (i).\n\n(ii) Ziegler-Natta polymerization: It is a process for the polymerization of olefins, such as ethylene, using a catalyst containing a transition metal compound, such as titanium tetrachloride, TiCl4. Therefore, the catalyst TiCl4 is matched with process (ii).\n\n(iii) Contact process: It is a process for the production of sulfuric acid from sulfur dioxide using a vanadium(V) oxide catalyst, V2O5. Therefore, the catalyst V2O5 is matched with process (iii).\n\n(iv) Deacon\'s process: It is a chemical process for the oxidation of hydrogen chloride to produce chlorine in the presence of a copper(II) chloride catalyst, CuCl2. Therefore, the catalyst CuCl2 is matched with process (iv).\n\nHence, the correct match is (A) - (ii), (B) - (i), (C) - (iv), (D) - (iii).','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a','d','c','b','NEW','12-240','12-114','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/82614q.png'),
(82615,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds is metallic and ferromagnetic?','CrO2','Out of the given compounds, only CrO2 is metallic and ferromagnetic. This is due to the presence of unpaired d-electrons in the chromium (Cr) ions. The electronic configuration of Cr in CrO2 is [Ar]3d⁴ 4s², which has four unpaired electrons in the 3d orbital.\n\nIn contrast, VO2, MnO2, and TiO2 are not metallic and ferromagnetic. VO2 is a semiconductor, MnO2 is an insulator, and TiO2 is a semiconductor or insulator depending on the crystal structure.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','VO2','CrO2','TiO2','MnO2','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(0,'chemistry','A : FeO is basic in character.\nR : Oxides of Transition metals are basic when metal is in lower oxidation state.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both are correct and correct explanation (fact).','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A & R are false','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','NEW','12-231','12-105',NULL),
(82702,'chemistry','A : Ti (IV) complexes are white.\nR : Ti (IV) has no electrons in d subshell.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Ti⁴⁺ → 3d⁰ → no d-d transition → no colour.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A & R are false','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','NEW','12-231','12-105',NULL),
(82703,'chemistry','A : Ce⁴⁺ is a good oxidizing agent.\nR : Sm²⁺ is a good reducing agent.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','Both the statements are true.\nBut, reason is not the correct explanation for oxidizing character of Ce⁴⁺.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A & R are false','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(82704,'chemistry','A : Equivalent mass of K₂Cr₂O₇ when it acts as an oxidizing agent in acidic medium is M/6.\nR : During reduction, oxidation number of chromium changes from +6 to +3.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','In acidic medium, n-factor of K₂Cr₂O₇ is 6\n\nEquivalent mass = M/6','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A & R are false','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','NEW','12-232','12-106',NULL),
(82705,'chemistry','A : All Cr–O bond length in K₂Cr₂O₇ are equal.\nR : Both the Cr are present in dsp² hybrid state.','Both A & R are false','In K₂Cr₂O₇, some Cr–O bonds have double bond and some are single bond, so bond lengths are different and\n Cr is sp³ hybridized.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A & R are false','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','NEW','-',NULL,NULL),
(82706,'chemistry','A : FeCl₃ reacts with KCNS to give blood red colour.\nR : FeCl₃ reacts with KCNS to form potassium ferro-ferricyanide.','A is true but R is false','FeCl₃(Yellow) + KCNS → Fe(CNS)₃(Blood red\n ferric thiocyanate) + 3KCl','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A & R are false','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','NEW','-',NULL,NULL),
(82707,'chemistry','A : La(OH)₃ is less basic than Lu(OH)₃.\nR : Basic character of hydroxides of lanthanides increase on moving from La³⁺ to Lu³⁺.','Both A & R are false','As we go right in a period the basic nature of hydroxides decreases.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A & R are false','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','NEW','12-231','12-105',NULL),
(82708,'chemistry','A : KMnO₄ is purple in colour due to charge transfer.\nR : In MnO₄\n-, Mn is in + 7 oxidation state and thus has no electron present in d orbitals.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','KMnO₄ is purple.\nIn Mn⁷⁺ no any d electron, so d-d transition is not possible. Hence charge transfer.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A & R are false','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','NEW','12-233','12-107',NULL),
(82709,'chemistry','A : CuSO₄.5H₂O on heating to 250°C losses all the five H₂O molecules and becomes anhydrous.\nR : All the five H₂O molecules are co-ordinated to the central Cu²⁺ ion.','A is true but R is false','CuSO₄.5H₂O → CuSO₄(anhydrous) + 5H₂O\nAll the 5H₂O molecules are not bonded to the central atom.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A & R are false','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','NEW','coordination compound q I think',NULL,NULL),
(82710,'chemistry','A : Cr₂O₇\n-² is orange in colour.\nR : It is due to the presence of unpaired electrons in d-subshell of Cr.','A is true but R is false','Cr₂O₇\n-² → orange colour → true\nHere Cr is in 6+ → 3d⁰ 4s⁰ → no any unpaired electron in d-subshell.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A & R are false','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','NEW','12-233','12-107',NULL),
(82711,'chemistry','A : Mn shows exceptional M.P. in 3d series.\nR : Its outer configuration is 4s² 3d⁵.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Mn shows exceptional MP. Due to its very stable configuration i.e. 3d⁵ 4s².','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A & R are false','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','NEW','12-218','12-092',NULL),
(82712,'chemistry','A : I.E. of 5d elements > 4d elements in general.\nR : It is due to lanthanide contraction.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','I.E. 5d elements > I.E. 4d elements due to lanthanide contraction.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A & R are false','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','NEW','12-219','12-093',NULL),
(82713,'chemistry','A : There is very less gap between the value of radii of 4d and 5d elements.\nR : Size of 5d is more than 4d subshell.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','The gap between radii of 4d and 5d elements is very less due to lanthanide contraction and size of 5d is\n greater than size of 4d → True.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A & R are false','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','NEW','12-219','12-093',NULL),
(82714,'chemistry','A : Mn₂O\n₇\n is acidic in nature.\nR : Mn has +7 oxidation state.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Mn₂O\n₇\n acidic in nature → True. In 7+ state Mn has high tendency to accept electron.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A & R are false','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','NEW','12-231','12-105',NULL),
(82715,'chemistry','A : NiCl₂ is more stable than PtCl₂.\nR : K₂PtCl₆ is more stable than K₂PtCl₆.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','NiCl₂ is more stable than PtCl₂ → True\nK₂PtCl₆ is more stable than K₂PtCl₆ → True\n , Reason is CFSE.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A & R are false','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','NEW','-',NULL,NULL),
(82716,'chemistry','A : Cr₂O₇\n-² becomes equilibrium with CrO₄-² at PH > 5.\nR : Cr₂O₇\n-² is tetrahedral having Cr – O – Cr angle 109º28\'.','Both A & R are false','CrO₄-² ⇌pH=4  ⇌Cr₂O₇\n-² \nIn K₂Cr₂O₇\n the Cr–O–Cr angle is 126°.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A & R are false','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(82717,'chemistry','A : In CrO₅ oxidation state of Cr is +10.\nR : Cr₂O₇\n-² (aq) is yellow in colour.','Both A & R are false','In CrO₅, oxidation state of Cr is 6+.\nCr₂O₇\n-² (aq) is orange in colour.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A & R are false','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','NEW','12-229','12-103',NULL),
(82718,'chemistry','A : Cu²⁺ is the only ion (M²⁺) which has positive Eºred (M²⁺/M) in 3d series.\nR : Cu has lower hydration enthalpy as comparision to its I.E. and ∆Hatm.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both the statements are correct with proper explanation.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A & R are false','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','NEW','12-225','12-099',NULL),
(82719,'chemistry','A : Hg is a liquid transition metal.\nR : It has strong metallic bonding.','Both A & R are false','Hg is not a transition metal and there is no any strong metallic bonding that is why it is liquid.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A & R are false','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','NEW','12-218','12-092',NULL),
(82720,'chemistry','A : Lanthanides have +3 as most common oxidation state.\nR : Electrons of 4f in lanthanides rarely participate in bonding.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Lanthanides, most commonly shows 3+ oxidation state because the 4f electrons do not participate in bonding.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A & R are false','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(82801,'chemistry','The main reason for a larger number of oxidation states exhibited by the actinoids than the corresponding lanthanoids is:\n(AIPMT - 2005)','Lesser energy difference between 5f and 6d orbitals than between 4f and 5d orbitals','Due to lesser energy difference between the 5f and 6d orbital of actinoids, more electrons can participate in bond formation, so more oxidation states are exhibited by actinoids.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous year questions part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Larger atomic size of actinoids than the lanthanoids','More energy difference between 5f and 6d orbitals than between 4f and 5d orbitals','Lesser energy difference between 5f and 6d orbitals than between 4f and 5d orbitals','Greater reactive nature of the actinoids than the lanthanoids','NEW','12-238','12-112',NULL),
(82802,'chemistry','Identify the incorrect statement from the following:\n(NEET - 2020)','Lanthanoids reveal only +3 oxidation state.','Lanthanoids shows general oxidation state +3 but some elements can shows +2 as\nwell as +4.\nCe (Z=58),\nPr (Z=59),\nNd (2=60),\nTb (2=65)\nand Dy (Z=66)\nshows +4 oxidation state\nNd (Z=60),\nSm (Z=62), Eu (Z=63),\nTm (Z=69)\nand Yb (Z=70)\nshows +2 oxidation state.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous year questions part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Zirconium and Hafnium have identical radii of 160 pm and 159 pm, respectively as a consequence of lanthanoid contraction.','Lanthanoids reveal only +3 oxidation state.','The lanthanoid ions other than the f° type and the f14 type are all paramagnetic.','The overall decrease in atomic and ionic radii from lanthanum to lutetium is called lanthanoid contraction.','NEW','12-235','12-109',NULL),
(82803,'chemistry','Which of the following elements can combine with chlorine to form more than one binary compound?\n(AIPMT - 2004)','Fe','STEP 1:\nA binary compound is one made of two different elements. These can be one of each element such as CuCI₂ or FeO. There can also be several of each element such as Fe₂0₃, or SnBr₄.\n\nSTEP 2:\nMetals that have variable oxidation numbers can form more than one type of binary compound like Fe shows the oxidation state +2 and +3. Hence it forms two types of binary compounds. e.g., FeCl₂, FeCl₃\n\nSTEP 3:\nZn and Ca shows onlv a +2 oxidation state and K shows onlv a +1 oxidation state.','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Previous year questions part 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Zn','K','Ca','Fe','NEW','12-225','12-099',NULL),
(310109,'chemistry','In Duma\'s method for the estimation of nitrogen the sample is heated with copper oxide and the gas evolved is passed over','Copper gauze','Dumas method for estimation of nitrogen: Known quantity of nitrogen-containing organic compound is heated with copper oxide in an atmosphere of carbon dioxide to yield free nitrogen in addition to carbon dioxide and water. Traces of nitrogen oxides formed if any are reduced to nitrogen by passing the gaseous mixture over a heated copper gauze. Nitrogen is collected over an aqueous solution of potassium hydroxide. Volume of nitrogen produced is measured at room temperature and atmospheric pressure.','PRINCIPLES RELATED TO PRACTICAL \n CHEMISTRY>>QUANTITATIVE ANALYSIS>>DUMAS METHOD',NULL,'2','MCQ','Nickel','Copper oxide','Copper gauze','None of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(310110,'chemistry','In Duma\'s method for determining the nitrogen content of an organic compound, the nitrogen content is determined in the form of','Gaseous dinitrogen','any nitrogen present in the compound is converted into oxides of nitrogen','PRINCIPLES RELATED TO PRACTICAL \n CHEMISTRY>>QUANTITATIVE ANALYSIS>>DUMAS METHOD',NULL,'1','MCQ','Gaseous Ammonia','NaCN','Gaseous dinitrogen','ammonium sulfate','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(310210,'chemistry','Kjeldahl\'s trap is the part of','Kjeldahl\'s distillation','The Kjeldahl method was introduced in 1883 and consists of three main steps: sample digestion, distillation, and ammonia determination','PRINCIPLES RELATED TO PRACTICAL \n CHEMISTRY>>QUANTITATIVE ANALYSIS>>KJELDAHL\'S METHOD',NULL,'1','MCQ','Kjeldahl\'s distillation','Kjeldahl\'s sublimation','Kjeldahl\'s sedimentation','Kjeldahl\'s filtration','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(310211,'chemistry','Kjeldahl\'s method can be used for the estimation of nitrogen in:','ethylamine.','Kjeldahl method is not suitable for compounds containing nitrogen in azo and nitro groups or in rings (quinoline, pyridine, etc.). In these cases, the nitrogen cannot be converted to ammonium sulphate by following the Kjeldahl method. Therefore','PRINCIPLES RELATED TO PRACTICAL \nCHEMISTRY>>QUANTITATIVE ANALYSIS>>KJELDAHL\'S METHOD',NULL,'2','MCQ','pyridine.','azobenzene.','nitrobenzene.','ethylamine.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(310304,'chemistry','Sodium extract is added to nitric acid and boiled for the test of halogens before adding silver nitrate to:','convert sodium cyanide and sodium\n sulphide to Hydrogen cyanide and\n hydrogen sulphide which are volatile.','If sodium cyanide and sodium sulphide are present, they react\n with silver nitrate solution to give precipitates, which interferes \n with the test.','PRINCIPLES RELATED TO PRACTICAL \n CHEMISTRY>>QUANTITATIVE ANALYSIS>>CARIUS METHOD',NULL,'1','MCQ','neutralise alkaline solution of \n sodium fusion extract','convert sodium cyanide and sodium\n sulphide to Hydrogen cyanide and\n hydrogen sulphide which are volatile.','convert sodium cyanide and \n sodium sulphide into \n sodium thiocyanate.','get white precipitate of silver \n cyanide and black precipitate \n of silver sulfide .','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(310305,'chemistry','In the estimation of sulphur, the organic compound on treating with concentrated nitric acid , is converted to','Sulphuric acid','During the estimation of sulphur present in the organic \n compound, a known mass of an organic compound is heated in \n a Carius tube with fuming nitric acid.','PRINCIPLES RELATED TO PRACTICAL \n CHEMISTRY>>QUANTITATIVE ANALYSIS>>CARIUS METHOD',NULL,'1','MCQ','Sulfur dioxide','Hydrogen sulfide','Sulphuric acid','Sulfur trioxide','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(310306,'chemistry','In the estimation of sulphur, which reagent is heated along with organic compound in a Carius tube?','Sodium peroxide','To estimate sulphur a known mass of an organic compound is\n heated in a Carius tube with sodium peroxide or fuming nitric \n acid. Sulphur present in the compound is oxidised to sulphuric \n acid. It is precipitated as barium sulphate by adding excess of \n barium chloride solution in water. The precipitate is filtered,\n washed, dried and weighed. The percentage of sulphur can be \n calculated from the mass of barium sulphate.','PRINCIPLES RELATED TO PRACTICAL \n CHEMISTRY>>QUANTITATIVE ANALYSIS>>CARIUS METHOD',NULL,'2','MCQ','Sodium Peroxide','Dilute nitric acid','Dilute sulphuric acid','Sodium hydroxide','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(310308,'chemistry','In Carius method, halogen containing organic compound is heated with fuming nitric acid in the presence of:','AgNO3','In Carius method, halogen containing organic compound is heated with fuming nitric acid in the presence of Silver nitrate. The role of Lunar caustic (AgNO 3) is to distinguish halide ions by giving different colour of precipitation in the product.','PRINCIPLES RELATED TO PRACTICAL \nCHEMISTRY>>QUANTITATIVE ANALYSIS>>CARIUS METHOD',NULL,'1','MCQ','HNO3','AgNO3','CuSO4','BaSO4','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
